INSERT INTO vocabulary_details (subject_id, reading_mnemonic) VALUES
(2467, 'When a vocab is a single kanji and all alone like this, it''s usually going to use the kun''yomi. Numbers are an exception, however. When a number is all alone, with no kanji or okurigana, it is going to be the on''yomi reading, which you learned with the kanji.  Just remember this exception for alone numbers and you''ll be able to read future number-related vocab to come.'),
(2468, 'Since all of the "number of things" words all follow the same pattern (number plus つ), you really just have to remember the part before the つ (and then remember that the つ is in all of these). All of the readings are the kun''yomi reading, which means we''ll have to use a mnemonic to remember them (you know the on''yomi reading of the kanji portion).
INSERT INTO vocabulary_details (subject_id, reading_mnemonic) VALUES
(3469, 'The reading is the regular on''yomi reading of 小 plus the vocab word 学校 that you''ve seen previously. Combine these two things for the reading of 小学校.'),
(3470, 'The reading is the same as the word 通る, so as long as you know that, you should be able to read this one as well. Mind the strange long お sound!'),
(3471, 'The readings come from the two words 気 and 付ける. So long as you know those from the previous levels, you should be able to read this one as well.'),
(3472, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 
INSERT INTO vocabulary_details (subject_id, reading_mnemonic) VALUES
(4469, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. The つ is shortened to っ.'),
(4470, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4471, 'You''re on the very <vocabulary>edge</vocabulary> of the world. You heard that at the edge of the world are the <reading>key wo</reading>rds (きわ) written there in big fiery letters, telling you the meaning of life. Supposedly these are the words of God himself. They say: "We apologize for the inconvenience." WHAT? THESE ARE THE <reading>KEY WO</reading>RDS???
INSERT INTO vocabulary_details (subject_id, reading_mnemonic) VALUES
(5469, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(5470, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it probably uses the kun''yomi reading. You did not learn this reading, so here''s a mnemonic to help you to remember:
INSERT INTO vocabulary_details (subject_id, reading_mnemonic) VALUES
(6469, 'The reading comes from 充電.'),
(6470, 'This word combines the word 甲斐 (which you''ve already learned) with the secondary on''yomi reading for 性. You haven''t learned this reading of 性 yet, so here''s a mnemonic to help:
INSERT INTO vocabulary_details (subject_id, reading_mnemonic) VALUES
(7469, 'The reading comes from 自動 and the kanji 車''s readings, combined.'),
(7470, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7471, 'The reading is different from the other kun''yomi readings you''ve learned so far with this word. It is きたない. Think of it like you''re saying "<reading>来た、な</reading>", as in "it came, huh?" What came? A really dirty looking monster, and it''s coming for you.'),
(7472, 'Remember how 早い means "early"? If you are early to something, it probably means you moved <vocabulary>fast</vocabulary> to get there. These two words share a reading (and have similar meanings, in a way, as well!).'),
(7473, 'The reading for 眼 is the same as the vocab 眼. The reading for 鏡 is an exception, totally different. It shares a reading with 金 (gold), though, so think of the mirrors on the person''s eyes being made of gold. What nice glasses!
INSERT INTO vocabulary_details (subject_id, reading_mnemonic) VALUES
(8470, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 
