BEGIN;


-- 1. Subjects Core


You could also think of the horizontal lines in 手 as fingers. That would make for a pretty alien-looking hand, but still a hand… of sorts…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/41', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/hand', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:14:49Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:06:01.259319Z'),
(42, 'radical', 'writing', '文', 'This looks like a stool with a lid on it, but it''s actually a desk for <radical>writing</radical>. Do you see it? You can even see the pen sticking up in the middle. Think of all the beautiful writing that''s been produced here. Letters, novels, essays — any kind of writing you can think of.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/42', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/writing', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:15:10Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:12:11.505385Z'),
(43, 'radical', 'moon', '月', 'This is the <radical>moon</radical> radical. See how it looks like a crescent moon with a couple of little clouds floating by? Try not to mix this up with the evening radical 夕 — you can see the whole crescent moon this time, so obviously this should be the radical for moon!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/43', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/moon', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:15:35Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T06:30:43.680591Z'),
(44, 'radical', 'stop', '止', 'There''s a stick and a giant toe stuck in the ground. Imagine walking along and seeing these things in your path. What would you do? Most likely come to a complete <radical>stop</radical>. You know, because of the toe. I guess a normal stop sign wasn''t good enough, so they also put up this big toe to really get people to stop.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/44', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/stop', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:16:08Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:12:37.987919Z'),
(45, 'radical', 'water', '水', 'This is the radical for <radical>water</radical>. It''s the water radical because it kind of looks like water coming out of a faucet and splashing everywhere. See how it flows off in different directions too? Very water-like! As water-like as a radical can be, anyway…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/45', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/water', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:16:27Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:53:56.46731Z'),
(46, 'radical', 'fire', '火', 'This is the radical for <radical>fire</radical>. It''s got three little flames on top and even a couple logs underneath.

But if it helps you can also focus on the person radical here. See it? It''s got things coming off of it... What are those thi— OH MY GOD IT''S FIRE!!! Help, help! Put the fire out! Wait, it''s fine… It''s just the fire radical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/46', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/fire', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:16:53Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:56:31.688597Z'),
(47, 'radical', 'dog', '犬', 'Remember the big guy who helped you with that big radical? Now you''ve got his big <radical>DOG</radical>! You know it''s a dog because he''s lying down, legs all spread out, with his favorite bone right beside his head.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/47', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/dog', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:17:50Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T16:35:25.81941Z'),
(48, 'radical', 'king', '王', 'This is the <radical>king</radical> radical. Picture this king sitting on his solid, squarish throne. The top stroke is the high back of the throne. The cross in the middle is his body, with his arms spread out on the arm rests. The bottom stroke is the ground. See him sitting there now, looking all dignified. Talk about a kingly king.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/48', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/king', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:18:44Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(49, 'radical', 'middle', '中', 'This is a stick going through the <radical>middle</radical> of a mouth. What kind of mouth? Mouth of a cylinder, mouth of a box, a human mouth — whatever kind you want to imagine, as long as the stick is going through the middle of it, since this is the radical for "middle."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/49', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/middle', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:19:55Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T01:56:11.967538Z'),
(50, 'radical', 'book', '本', 'What''s something useful you can get out of a tree? A <radical>book</radical>! Just cut that book right out of the tree trunk. If you think about it, every tree contains unlimited book potential. It''s just a shame they have to be cut down to make the books… ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/50', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/book', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:20:23Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:46.757721Z'),
(51, 'radical', 'rice-paddy', '田', 'In Japan rice paddies are made up of square fields with irrigation lines going between them. See the four rice paddies with water flowing between and around them? That''s why this radical is <radical>rice paddy</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/51', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/rice-paddy', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:21:52Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:42:06.30193Z'),
(52, 'radical', 'white', '白', 'Add a drop of the sun to something and it will turn it <radical>white</radical>. The sun is really strong, so even a single drop will burn anything white. Why do you think so many beaches are white? Even the drops of sunblock you use to stop the sun''s rays is white. So when you think of sun drops, think white.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/52', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/white', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:22:25Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:30:42.218788Z'),
(53, 'radical', 'eye', '目', 'See the iris in the middle of the eyeball? Remember that there are no round things in kanji, so square things take their place. That makes this a pretty realistic, square <radical>eye</radical>. You just have to squint with your real eyes a bit.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/53', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/eye', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:22:47Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T02:54:10.694388Z'),
(55, 'radical', 'stand', '立', 'This looks like someone in a standing position with legs planted firmly on the ground, spreading their arms out in a T-pose to maintain balance. So that makes this the radical for <radical>stand</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/55', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/stand', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:23:35Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:40:37.858322Z'),
(126, 'radical', 'cow', '牛', 'This is the <radical>cow</radical> radical. It kind of looks like the long face of a cow, right? You can think of that top horizontal line as its horns. Seeing it yet? It''s got a little tuft of hair on its head, and some kind of accessory on the horn to your left, so this is a very stylish cow.

(Did you know both male and female cows can have horns? I didn''t either, but it''s true. So please don''t write in telling us we can''t call this thing a cow — we don''t want any beef.)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/126', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/cow', 2, 2, '2012-03-06T23:56:07Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:37:01.361Z'),
(140, 'radical', 'private', 'ム', 'This is a little triangle that''s almost completely closed off. When you want to be alone, you hide in a place where you can be in <radical>private</radical>. That''s why this is the private radical. You''re closing yourself off to the rest of the world, with just a little peephole.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/140', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/private', 2, 2, '2012-03-07T00:26:12Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T20:01:27.526431Z'),
(8763, 'radical', 'leaf', '丆', 'There''s a leaf on top and a stem going down out of it, making this the <radical>leaf</radical> radical. It''s a pretty wide and flat leaf, so if it helps you can imagine it as a lily pad leaf.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8763', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/leaf', 2, 2, '2012-02-27T18:35:58Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:04:44.644357Z'),
(30, 'radical', 'stool', '又', 'This looks just like a folding <radical>stool</radical>, am I right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/30', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/stool', 3, 1, '2012-02-28T21:06:08Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:43:29.897949Z'),
(54, 'radical', 'stone', '石', 'A leaf growing out of a… mouth?? Look again and see the mouth is actually made of <radical>stone</radical>. The leaf here was just growing out of a crack on the side. Makes this stone mouth sculpture look kinda elegant, if you ask me. And obviously leaves don''t grow on mouths, so whenever you see a leaf on a mouth, remember it''s gotta be stone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/54', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/stone', 3, 1, '2012-02-28T21:23:15Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:09:54.496507Z'),
(57, 'radical', 'spoon', '匕', 'This radical looks like a <radical>spoon</radical>! Well, a spoon that''s been bent a lot by an angry person or a psychic. Go grab a spoon and try to bend it into this shape. Then hold on to it so you''ll never forget that this is the spoon radical!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/57', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/spoon', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:50:35Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:20:54.638732Z'),
(58, 'radical', 'ra-58', 'ラ', 'This radical looks just like the katakana character "ra" (ラ). The sound "ra" sounds just like the famous Egyptian God "Ra" as well, so we''re going to say this radical represents Egypt''s God <radical>Ra</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/58', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/ra-58', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:51:28Z', '2018-12-05T18:38:49.886907Z', '2025-10-15T19:00:26.414327Z'),
(59, 'radical', 'raptor-cage-59', '久', 'This is no ordinary enclosure. It is an enclosure with an extra reinforcement (see it sticking out the bottom right of the enclosure radical?). Why is this enclosure reinforced? Because it''s the <radical>raptor cage</radical>. I wonder how long they''ll be able to hold the raptors... It didn''t work out all that well on Jurassic Park...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/59', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/raptor-cage-59', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:52:13Z', '2018-12-05T18:38:50.736614Z', '2025-10-15T19:00:26.384237Z'),
(60, 'radical', 'winter', '夂', 'This is the radical for <radical>winter</radical>. It looks like a skier going downhill (to the left), with their skis out in front and their poles out behind them. And when do people ski? In winter!

You could also remember this as looking like the evening radical but with an extra long stroke, since the evenings are long in winter. It gets dark early in winter, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/60', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/winter', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:54:32Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:21:06.048836Z'),
(61, 'radical', 'dry', '干', 'This looks like a clothesline pole you would <radical>dry</radical> clothes on. It''s got two very straight horizontal poles, for maximum drying efficiency. If you were gonna dry your clothes, you''d dry them on this thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/61', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/dry', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:55:20Z', NULL, '2025-12-11T20:12:42.827076Z'),
(62, 'radical', 'canopy', '广', 'This is a <radical>canopy</radical>. See the cloth draping down and the little point at the middle holding it up? You could sit under here and enjoy a bright summer day without getting any sun!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/62', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/canopy', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:55:39Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:22:05.925856Z'),
(63, 'radical', 'fingers', '扌', 'This looks like a long index finger bent at the bottom, with several other stubby fingers growing out of it on either side. That makes it the <radical>fingers</radical> radical. A bit creepy, but imagine how easily you could grip something or scratch your back with fingery fingers like these. Pretty nice, right?

This is also a lot like the hand radical (手) but smaller. And what are the smaller parts of a hand? The fingers! You can remember it that way too, if you want.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/63', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/fingers', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:56:23Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T06:49:24.911379Z'),
(65, 'radical', 'heart', '心', 'Take a good look at this one. It kind of looks like a <radical>heart</radical>. Not the heart emoji kind, but a real-deal beating heart. Basically, there''s a lump in the middle (the heart), and three lines that make up the arteries. Okay, so it''s not the most anatomically correct heart, but just look at those floppy arteries going in and out, doing their thing and keeping you alive. What''s not to love?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/65', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/heart', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:56:47Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:59:45.447543Z'),
(66, 'radical', 'direction', '方', 'This radical looks like a dude running off to the right. He''s heading in a very distinct <radical>direction</radical>. Which direction was it again?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/66', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/direction', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:57:03Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:13:41.173292Z'),
(67, 'radical', 'fur', '毛', 'Do you remember the hair radical? This is like that, except now the hair is attached to a tail. Animals don''t usually have hair though. Instead, they have <radical>fur</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/67', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/fur', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:57:18Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:26.511776Z'),
(68, 'radical', 'father', '父', 'This is the <radical>father</radical> radical, since you can see a very fatherly face in it — two droopy eyes at the top, a big nose in the middle, and a mustache flaring out at the bottom. Sort of a generic older gentleman father-type face, isn''t it?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/68', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/father', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:57:27Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:01:24.564442Z'),
(69, 'radical', 'old', '古', 'Put a cross over the mouth of a container full of all the <radical>old</radical> things you no longer need. They''ve all served their purpose but have gotten old, so it''s now time to let them rest in peace under the cross. Goodbye, old things!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/69', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/old', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:57:42Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:13:37.638329Z'),
(70, 'radical', 'drawer-70', '母', 'See the <radical>drawer</radical>? It has two shelves in it with a little nubbin handle on each to let you pull them out. Imagine using this drawer - it''d be pretty oddly shaped.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/70', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/drawer-70', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:57:52Z', '2018-12-05T18:38:50.76634Z', '2025-10-15T19:00:26.400093Z'),
(71, 'radical', 'task', '用', 'This looks like a little whiteboard to keep track of your <radical>task</radical>s for the day. It''s got three little legs on the bottom. And see those blank squares? They''re sticky notes you can write your tasks on. Work tasks, school tasks, WaniKani reviews… So what are your tasks for today? Imagine putting them in those little squares.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/71', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/task', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:58:40Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:48:39.716764Z'),
(72, 'radical', 'arrow', '矢', 'This is a gun on top of a big person. The gun is big too, obviously. Why is this big guy''s gun so big? Because it shoots <radical>arrow</radical>s! Bullets are pretty small, so the guns that shoot them can be any size. But you need a big gun if you want to shoot a big arrow, right? That''s why this is the radical for arrow.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/72', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/arrow', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:59:07Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:33:15.236501Z'),
(73, 'radical', 'door', '戸', 'This one looks like a flag, but don''t be fooled — it''s got a line over the top of it, kind of like a door frame. That makes this a little <radical>door</radical>. Specifically a swinging door, with the vertical line on the left being its door hinge. Kind of a weird door, it''s true, but you can see it, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/73', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/door', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:07:46Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:47:35.976472Z'),
(78, 'radical', 'roof', '宀', 'This is a <radical>roof</radical> ー a flat roof with a little chimney sticking out. How cute!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/78', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/roof', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T21:32:14Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(87, 'radical', 'poop', '幺', 'For better or worse, this looks enough like the poop emoji (💩) to make this the <radical>poop</radical> radical. Try to remember it for its distinct outline. If it helps you can also note the private radical on the bottom of this one, since you should probably be in private when you poop…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/87', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/poop', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T21:36:17Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:04:30.700843Z'),
(102, 'radical', 'towel', '巾', 'There''s something draped over that stick. It''s a <radical>towel</radical>! It''s the kind of towel you clean with, so it''s sort of old and crusty, which is why it holds its square shape even when it''s hanging on the stick like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/102', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/towel', 3, 1, '2012-03-03T02:03:04Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T02:33:15.957788Z'),
(107, 'radical', 'window', '毋', 'See the criss-cross in the almost-square shape? That''s the frames of a big <radical>window</radical> you can look out of. It''s not perfectly shaped like a rice field, it''s more artistic. That''s why this is a window and 田 is not.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/107', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/window', 3, 1, '2012-03-03T02:08:54Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:27:49.000158Z'),
(123, 'radical', 'ice', '冫', 'Remember the radical for drop (of liquid)? Looks like it''s shattered into two pieces here, which means it must be <radical>ice</radical> now. You know, since a drop could only break in two after turning into ice.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/123', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/ice', 3, 1, '2012-03-06T01:28:52Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T06:03:32.726396Z'),
(210, 'radical', 'life', '生', 'Remember the sprout from the dirt radical? This looks like that, but wait a minute… there''s some extra growth now! This thing is so full of <radical>life</radical>! It''s even got a big, slide-shaped leaf growing on the top left. That''s why it''s the life radical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/210', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/life', 3, 1, '2012-06-05T23:59:11.547907Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T07:14:23.968238Z'),
(238, 'radical', 'now', '今', 'This is the radical for <radical>now</radical>. It''s a bit tough to see, but it kind of looks like a fancy clock. The 𠆢 is the top part hanging over the clock. The ラ shows a triangular clock face with a little cuckoo on top, peeking out to tell you this means now — right now!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/238', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/now', 3, 1, '2012-08-28T00:03:32.20337Z', NULL, '2025-10-29T11:10:32.880844Z'),
(327, 'radical', 'north', '北', 'You only have some ice and a stick for your spoon, because you''re way up north. At the top of the world, there''s only ice to eat, and — if you''re lucky — a bit of stick for flavor. That''s the <radical>north</radical> for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/327', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/north', 3, 1, '2013-01-24T21:03:59.388708Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.438028Z'),
(357, 'radical', 'bright', '明', 'The sun AND the moon are out? With both their light-powers combined, it''s incredibly <radical>bright</radical> outside.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/357', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/bright', 3, 1, '2013-05-03T08:24:42.978532Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(411, 'radical', 'fat', '太', 'This looks like the guy from the big radical, but he''s got something extra on his belly. That''s his exposed belly button, because he''s a sumo wrestler now: big and <radical>fat</radical>. In sumo, more fat means more power, so imagine he''s been training to get as strong and fat as possible.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/411', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/fat', 3, 1, '2013-08-19T18:20:45.42468Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:05:49.398535Z'),
(8764, 'radical', 'hat', '𠆢', 'Do you know those rice paddy hats? The ones shaped just like this radical? That''s why this radical is a <radical>hat</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8764', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/hat', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:51:58Z', NULL, '2025-12-02T00:48:12.061404Z'),
(8765, 'radical', 'triceratops', '⺌', 'The three horns represent the <radical>triceratops</radical>, one of the most awesome dinosaurs ever.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8765', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/triceratops', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T01:53:11Z', NULL, '2025-12-11T20:12:42.646308Z'),
(64, 'radical', 'origin', '元', 'You have two lines with legs below them. And what is that bit above the legs? Well, it''s where you came from — your <radical>origin</radical>. Yep, between two legs is where you originated, no matter how you look at it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/64', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/origin', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T01:56:35Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:46.854192Z'),
(74, 'radical', 'measurement', '寸', 'Have you ever used one of those triangular rulers? This looks just like one of those, because this is the <radical>measurement</radical> radical. Just think of all the angles you could measure with this thing!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/74', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/measurement', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:28:38Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:46:12.340974Z'),
(75, 'radical', 'leader', 'ｲ', 'This radical technically means person (like 人), but we''re going to shift the meaning a little bit. It''s kind of like the person radical, but it''s taller and skinnier, standing above the others and raising its fist to lead them. That''s why this is a <radical>leader</radical>, not a regular person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/75', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/leader', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:29:24Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(76, 'radical', 'forehead', '冖', 'Sure, this <radical>forehead</radical> is a bit square, but that''s because it''s the top part of the head (冂) radical. This is just the forehead part of that! You can even imagine this as the forehead of Frankenstein''s monster, since his forehead was pretty square too.

Also, don''t confuse this for a roof. You know this is a forehead and not a roof because this doesn''t have a cute little chimney sticking out of the top. Foreheads definitely don''t have chimneys.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/76', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/forehead', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:30:09Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:51:44.236861Z'),
(77, 'radical', 'alligator', '也', 'See the mouth and long, fang-like teeth of this <radical>alligator</radical>, which is facing to the right? You wouldn''t want to get your hand stuck in there, that''s for sure.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/77', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/alligator', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:31:39Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T04:47:22.214427Z'),
(79, 'radical', 'ceremony', '弋', 'This radical looks like a person with a long dress running behind them. That''s because they''re all dressed up to go to a <radical>ceremony</radical>. You usually have to wear a long dress (or something formal) if you''re going to a big ceremony, right? So when you see this radical, think of all the different ceremonies you might have to go to in your life and what you''ll need to wear.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/79', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/ceremony', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:32:32Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T22:31:56.723758Z'),
(80, 'radical', 'spirit', 'ネ', 'This radical looks like some sort of <radical>spirit</radical>. It faces to the left with a big hood over its head, and creepy arms slanting downward at the sides. Can you picture it?

If not, just think of this as the katakana character for "ne." Ne for necromancer, bringing back spirits from the grave! OooooOOoooOoo!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/80', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/spirit', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:32:50Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T02:56:28.904504Z'),
(81, 'radical', 'branch', '支', 'There''s a cross branching off of this stool. Since it''s branching out, we''ll just call this the <radical>branch</radical> radical. I guess the tree this stool was made of is coming back to life in the form of a branch.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/81', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/branch', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:33:21Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:30:12.871593Z'),
(82, 'radical', 'energy', '气', 'This is the radical for <radical>energy</radical>. If you imagine a train under this radical, then the lines would be steam bursting out of it, flowing back from the front of the train (on the left) as it goes chugging along. Steam was the best way to generate energy back in the day, you know? That''s why this is the radical for energy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/82', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/energy', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:33:30Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(83, 'radical', 'world', '世', 'Use your imagination to draw a circle outside this shape. The grids from the radical start to look like longitude and latitude lines on a globe, don''t they? That''s why this is the <radical>world</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/83', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/world', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:34:08Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:10:47.677773Z'),
(84, 'radical', 'plate', '皿', 'This is a few plates drying on a rack. They''re stacked together in a line. Imagine reaching out and grabbing one of those plates for yourself. And you only need one, so let''s call this radical <radical>plate</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/84', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/plate', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:34:41Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:47.052712Z'),
(85, 'radical', 'bamboo', '竹', 'See how tall the stalks are with little bushy things at the top? It kind of looks like <radical>bamboo</radical>, if you use your imagination.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/85', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/bamboo', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:35:10Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T15:37:55.121141Z'),
(86, 'radical', 'thread', '糸', 'In some poop you find something small. You lean in, carefully pull it out, and realize it''s a <radical>thread</radical>. What''s that doing there? Well, it came from a cat, and cats love to eat thread when you aren''t looking. And floss and hoodie strings and mouse tails… But this time, just thread.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/86', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/thread', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:35:55Z', NULL, '2026-04-05T19:31:59.040578Z'),
(88, 'radical', 'ear', '耳', 'This is the <radical>ear</radical> radical, since it looks like an ear. As much as a radical can, anyway. Those two middle lines are the ridges that guide the sound into your ear, and the lines that stick out to the right are where the ear attaches to your head.

If you think about it, ears are just eyes that see sound. Whoa, dude… That explains the eye (目) and ear (耳) radical resemblance. Just remember this one is "ear" because ears stick out, just like the lines sticking out of the side of this radical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/88', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/ear', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:37:01Z', NULL, '2026-04-11T18:47:55.743592Z'),
(89, 'radical', 'insect', '虫', 'This looks like the middle radical on top of a sideways leaf. What can you often find sitting on the middle of a leaf? An <radical>insect</radical>. Insects looove sitting right in the middle of leaves. That''s why this is the insect radical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/89', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/insect', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:37:26Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T07:22:58.542947Z'),
(90, 'radical', 'flowers', '艹', 'See the little things sticking out of the ground? Those little things are pretty little <radical>flowers</radical>.

You could also see this as a little flower crown, especially since this radical almost always appears at the top of a kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/90', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/flowers', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:37:50Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T17:45:03.179824Z'),
(91, 'radical', 'see', '見', 'This eye has legs, since you have to let your eyes run over something in order to <radical>see</radical> it. That''s why those eyes need legs — to see. They''re wandering eyes, they want to see the world!!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/91', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/see', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:38:16Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T17:40:57.020503Z'),
(92, 'radical', 'red', '赤', 'A pile of dirt fell on you and now your legs and (scuba) fins are sticking out from under it, turning <radical>red</radical>. You know, because you''ve been stuck in the sun so long. Everything''s all sunburned and red. Can scuba fins get a sunburn? Yeah, when it''s red hot outside. That''s why this means "red."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/92', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/red', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:38:28Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:47.131262Z'),
(93, 'radical', 'car', '車', 'Doesn''t this look like a top-down view of the frame of a <radical>car</radical>? There are the axles at the top and bottom, and the body in the middle between them. That''s why this is the car radical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/93', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/car', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:38:35Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:10:24.143918Z'),
(94, 'radical', 'shellfish', '貝', 'This radical looks like a <radical>shellfish</radical>. What looks like the eye radical is its long spiral shell. The bottom part that looks like fins is its little legs poking out, helping the shellfish move around.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/94', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/shellfish', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T18:13:26Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T17:35:03.677256Z'),
(95, 'radical', 'foot', '足', 'This is the <radical>foot</radical> radical. See the person doing a flying dragon kick to the right? What do they hit the target with? Obviously their foot. Imagine that foot coming straight at your face. At that point, your only hope is that it''s clean. Nobody wants a foot to the face, especially not a filthy foot.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/95', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/foot', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T18:26:29Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T06:49:24.911379Z'),
(96, 'radical', 'not', '不', 'This looks like a regular arrow, but it''s <radical>not</radical>. Let''s call it the "not arrow." It can <em>not</em> move forward, because it''s been stopped by some kind of line above it. Every arrow wants to fly, but this one is <em>not</em> going anywhere. Look at it, being all negated like that. Well, that''s just what this radical does. It <em>nots</em> things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/96', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/not', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T18:32:58Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:47.162501Z'),
(97, 'radical', 'samurai', '士', 'The <radical>samurai</radical> radical looks a lot like the dirt radical. The difference is this one has a longer top horizontal line, which is for those broad warrior shoulders. The bottom line is also shorter, since it''s the little cushion the samurai is sitting on. Samurai didn''t just fight, you know. They sat on cushions and wrote poems and stuff too. This is a very sensitive samurai.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/97', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/samurai', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:33:59Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T06:51:37.591315Z'),
(98, 'radical', 'rice', '米', 'This is the radical for <radical>rice</radical>. Specifically raw rice, like the grain that grows out of the ground when rice is raw and fresh. When rice is particularly ripe, it''ll kind of look like this at the top of the stalks. See the long leaves and the little rice tufts at the top? That''s why this radical is "rice."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/98', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/rice', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T01:47:35Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:12:37.987919Z'),
(99, 'radical', 'loiter', '彳', 'The leader (ｲ) radical has a little something extra on top of him — he''s got a casual cap on because it''s his day off. And what does he like to do on his day off? He likes to <radical>loiter</radical>. Look at him just loitering around. What a loiterer.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/99', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/loiter', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T01:47:48Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:47.200532Z'),
(100, 'radical', 'bed', '巴', 'You''ll have to complete the line on the right side in your mind, but see the rectangle <radical>bed</radical> and the pillows? This is totally a bed. That missing line is where you pulled back the covers when you got up this morning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/100', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/bed', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T01:48:06Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:22:31.647431Z'),
(101, 'radical', 'pi', '兀', 'Obviously, this looks just like the good ol'' 3.14 something-something all people are supposed to learn about. It''s the math symbol that has to do with circles... <radical>pi</radical>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/101', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/pi', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T01:55:57Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:47.219248Z'),
(116, 'radical', 'cliff', '厂', 'See the <radical>cliff</radical>? It''s facing to the left. What do you think this cliff is jutting out over? Maybe an ocean, or a valley? Cliffs love to jut out over things like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/116', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/cliff', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T04:08:50Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:15:23.483599Z'),
(154, 'radical', 'master', '主', 'The king is hovering a drop of water over his head. He''s acquired the ability to manipulate water at will, making him the <radical>master</radical> of all water! If you think about how all life depends on water, that really makes him the master of everything…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/154', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/master', 4, 1, '2012-03-22T01:37:37Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:47.636057Z'),
(170, 'radical', 'hole', '穴', 'You have a roof on top of what looks like a volcano. What''s the roof covering? It''s definitely covering the <radical>hole</radical> at the top. I guess someone thought if they built a roof on the hole, none of the lava could come out. But isn''t there a hole in the chimney? Guess their theory had some <em>holes</em> in it… Oh no, now lava is coming through the hole. RUN!!! RUN!!!!!!!!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/170', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/hole', 4, 1, '2012-04-04T23:44:10Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:11:35.013012Z'),
(211, 'radical', 'umbrella', '乚', 'This radical looks like an <radical>umbrella</radical>, specifically just the handle part. A good old curvy umbrella handle, so you can hang that umbrella on your arm when it stops raining.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/211', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/umbrella', 4, 1, '2012-06-27T19:16:49.157216Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.030484Z'),
(231, 'radical', 'center', '央', 'Remember the guy from the big radical? Here he''s bending his arms at the elbow to fasten some kind of belt, right across the <radical>center</radical> of his body. It''s one of those belts that just goes across your waist, which is actually perfect for such a big guy, since he really needs to secure his center (of gravity).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/231', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/center', 4, 1, '2012-08-27T20:17:38.534922Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:25:07.494204Z'),
(243, 'radical', 'man', '男', 'A rice paddy needs power, and that power comes from a <radical>man</radical>. Picture a rice paddy full of thick, wet mud. It''s no easy field to work in, but this powerful man will bend over to rake and hoe tirelessly to make sure it thrives. What a man!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/243', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/man', 4, 1, '2012-09-18T23:33:51.991567Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T07:14:23.977868Z'),
(8766, 'radical', 'beggar', NULL, 'Doesn''t this look like someone on their knees, holding their clasped hands out and begging for something (forgiveness, their life, a promotion, etc.)? That''s why this radical is known as the <radical>beggar</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8766', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/beggar', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:30:51Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:34:08.229191Z'),
(8767, 'radical', 'horns', '丷', 'Can you see the <radical>horns</radical>? You could put these horns on anything. Donkeys, people, monkeys, and so on. That''s exactly what we''ll be doing when we learn kanji that have horns too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8767', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/horns', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T21:31:08Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:30:39.384701Z'),
(8768, 'radical', 'spikes', '业', 'On the ground there are a bunch of <radical>spikes</radical> coming out. You wouldn''t want to step on those now, would you?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8768', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/spikes', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T01:56:15Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(103, 'radical', 'tsunami', '氵', 'A single drop (丶) is just water. But as soon as there are three, rising higher and higher, you know what''s coming. A <radical>tsunami</radical>! Think of those drops as wave crests. Three whole tsunami waves, stacking up? That''s a big tsunami.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/103', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/tsunami', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T02:07:12Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:15:23.483599Z'),
(104, 'radical', 'coffin', '耂', 'See the slide going into the dirt? What appears to be a slide here is actually a <radical>coffin</radical>, sliding into the dirt to be buried.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/104', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/coffin', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T02:08:01Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:34:08.229191Z'),
(105, 'radical', 'wolverine', 'ヨ', 'You know <radical>Wolverine</radical> from the X-Men? He has three claws that come out of his hand whenever he needs to fight, not at all unlike the radical you see here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/105', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/wolverine', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T02:08:24Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:23:13.568105Z'),
(106, 'radical', 'twenty', '廾', 'You have 十 plus 十 (never mind that the left one is kinda curvy)... What do you get when you add these two kanji together? You get <radical>twenty</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/106', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/twenty', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T02:08:34Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:23:36.291149Z'),
(108, 'radical', 'meat', '肉', 'This is the radical for <radical>meat</radical>. What''s meat-like about this? Think of a nice square slab of meat with some beautiful 人-shaped marbling lines in it. Seeing it now? Mmm, meat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/108', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/meat', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T02:10:03Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:33:00.426254Z'),
(109, 'radical', 'self', '自', 'Remember the eye radical? Imagine that''s your eye now, and there''s a drop of paint falling into it, making your eye color unique. Now it''s what makes you you. It''s what makes you your <radical>self</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/109', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/self', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T02:10:17Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:23:36.291149Z'),
(110, 'radical', 'color', '色', 'In prison, dreaming in bed is the only way to see <radical>color</radical>. Picture a dark, dank cell with four gray walls. Now imagine the colorful dreamscapes your bed would take you to every night. What a contrast! That''s why a prison bed = color.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/110', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/color', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T04:06:41Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:12:30.409026Z'),
(111, 'radical', 'go', '行', 'You have the radicals for loiter, two, and barb. Someone who uses two barbs on you while you''re loitering really wants you to <radical>GO</radical>!! GO on, stop loitering outside of my convenience store, ya dang kids!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/111', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/go', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T04:07:24Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:47.285462Z'),
(112, 'radical', 'west', '西', 'You have the legs of a bird with wings outspread, landing in the mouth of her nest to rest. And when does she go back to rest? When the sun sets in the <radical>west</radical>! Imagine all the birds flying home as the sun sets. That''s why this radical means west.

You could also see this as pi coming out of a mouth. Why would a mouth be talking about pi? Probably to figure out the curvature of the sun''s path as it moves across the sky — to the west, always to the west!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/112', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/west', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T04:07:33Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:19:07.778293Z'),
(113, 'radical', 'lip-ring', '可', 'This looks like something hooked around the corner of a mouth — it''s a <radical>lip ring</radical>! Use your imagination, because it''s either a giant lip ring or a very small mouth. It''s angular because kanji prefer straight lines, but try to picture it as the kind of studded, angular lip ring your trendy friend wore in high school. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/113', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/lip-ring', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T04:07:41Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(114, 'radical', 'scooter', '⻌', 'This radical is a <radical>scooter</radical>. See the handlebars and the little light? And there''s a platform at the bottom you can stand or sit on (if it''s one of those nice motorized scooters).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/114', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/scooter', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T04:07:58Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:30:39.384701Z'),
(115, 'radical', 'axe', '斤', 'This is the radical for <radical>axe</radical>, since it looks like one. The two horizontal strokes are the head, the long left line is the whole edge of its blade, and the vertical line in the middle is its short handle. Kind of a strange axe, I admit, but an axe is an axe.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/115', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/axe', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T04:08:25Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:29:41.941203Z'),
(117, 'radical', 'say', '言', 'There are a bunch of lines coming out of a mouth. Think of them as speech lines in a little speech bubble. Those lines represent the things you <radical>say</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/117', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/say', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T04:09:27Z', NULL, '2026-03-17T23:06:58.837958Z'),
(118, 'radical', 'gold', '金', 'Underneath that hat are two bars of <radical>gold</radical>, but under those there are some spikes. This is some kind of trap for people who try to take your gold. And why else would you make a trap? Everyone wants to protect their gold.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/118', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/gold', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T04:09:34Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T09:52:41.789411Z'),
(119, 'radical', 'rain', '雨', 'The towel is getting soaked with drops of liquid. That''s because it''s <radical>rain</radical>ing. Get your towels and other clothes inside before they get soaked through from this rain!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/119', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/rain', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T04:09:41Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T22:08:10.397284Z'),
(120, 'radical', 'blue', '青', 'You have life on top of the moon (ignore that life is missing its little slide on the left side). What would life on the moon look like? Well, first of all, they have no air, so they probably look pretty <radical>blue</radical> from asphyxiation. When you meet them, it turns out you''re right. They''re a civilization of blue people. Imagine them all living up there, being blue and Smurfy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/120', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/blue', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T04:09:52Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:00:51.825704Z'),
(121, 'radical', 'mustache', '冋', 'See the <radical>mustache</radical> going around the mouth? Man, I wish I could grow a sweet mustache like this. (Sure, the mustache looks a lot like the head radical here, but just imagine it as a mustache this time!)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/121', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/mustache', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T19:41:16Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:33.585551Z'),
(122, 'radical', 'key', '乍', 'This is a very oddly shaped <radical>key</radical>, but it''s a key nonetheless.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/122', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/key', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T00:54:58Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:56:10.241947Z'),
(124, 'radical', 'run', '走', 'See the person running (to the right)? That''s why this is the radical for <radical>run</radical>. Just don''t confuse this with foot (足). The head in this one has streaks around it because the person is running so fast.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/124', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/run', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T06:57:41Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:39:24.766977Z'),
(125, 'radical', 'village', '里', 'A rice paddy and some dirt — dirt roads, in fact, because this is a <radical>village</radical>. Small villages like this are mostly rice paddies and dirt (roads), you know? You could even think of this as a bird''s-eye view of a village.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/125', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/village', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T07:15:20Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:33.610881Z'),
(155, 'radical', 'meet', '会', 'Under the hat there are two people <radical>meet</radical>ing in private. Look at them huddled up in there. They must have a reason to want to meet in such a private place, but that''s not for you to judge. Maybe next time you have to meet with someone you''ll hide under a hat with them too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/155', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/meet', 5, 1, '2012-03-22T01:41:38Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:12:48.398208Z'),
(171, 'radical', 'lantern', '开', 'Think of an old-style <radical>lantern</radical>. It''s in a box-shaped holder lined with rice paper and sits on four legs (you can only see two from this angle though). You can imagine people using these before electricity, right? That''s why this radical is a lantern.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/171', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/lantern', 5, 1, '2012-04-05T18:56:42Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:22:52.12866Z'),
(213, 'radical', 'feathers', '羽', 'This radical already looks like a pair of rectangular <radical>feathers</radical>, right? I guess those short lines in each feather are like the filaments of the feathers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/213', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/feathers', 5, 1, '2012-07-10T22:33:26.448391Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:42:04.39189Z'),
(233, 'radical', 'pig', '豕', 'Here we have the ground radical, with something underneath it. What likes lying in the ground, covered in mud? A <radical>pig</radical>! Use your imagination and see if you can find the head near the top, the piggy tail at the bottom right, and the little piglets on the left, nestling up to this mama pig.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/233', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/pig', 5, 1, '2012-08-27T20:18:14.326026Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:23:13.568105Z'),
(281, 'radical', 'angle', '角', 'When you''re in prison, your only task is to count every <radical>angle</radical> in your cell. You count the angles of the walls and ceiling over and over again to pass the time. How many angles could there be in a room with four walls? Picture it and try to count those angles now. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/281', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/angle', 5, 1, '2012-12-04T05:53:23.365696Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:11:00.979882Z'),
(342, 'radical', 'night', '夜', 'See that lid on top? It''s actually a flying saucer. "Take me to your leader," say the aliens inside, arriving from a strange-looking moon. When do you think you''d see something like this? At <radical>night</radical>. When people see UFOs or the moon, it''s usually night. That''s why this is the night radical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/342', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/night', 5, 1, '2013-03-15T19:49:42.727468Z', NULL, '2025-11-05T12:15:52.656225Z'),
(8769, 'radical', 'viking', '龸', 'On the top part of the head (冂) radical, there is a horn radical with one extra spike in the middle. This is a horned helmet with one extra spike. Who were famous for having horned helmets like that? The Vikings! That''s why this radical is called <radical>Viking</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8769', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/viking', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T04:09:15Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:06:01.259319Z'),
(8770, 'radical', 'kick', NULL, 'This looks like a stick-figure foot on the left kicking at something, which makes it the radical for <radical>kick</radical>. See the shin and foot swinging up for the kick? You can think of that part on the right as the edge of a ball that''s about to be kicked.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8770', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/kick', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T19:15:25Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:26:46.193094Z'),
(56, 'radical', 'lion', 'L', 'This L-shaped radical is the <radical>lion</radical> radical, because "L" is for "Lion." It also looks like a lion''s mouth being opened really, really wide. Imagine a lion opening its mouth to yawn. It looks like this radical!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/56', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/lion', 6, 1, '2012-03-01T01:50:04Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T07:49:08.709778Z'),
(128, 'radical', 'knife', '刂', 'This is a <radical>knife</radical>. See the sharp edge on the left and the tapered tip on top? And there''s a little handle at the bottom for the... well, the handle. Be careful you don''t cut yourself on this knife!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/128', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/knife', 6, 1, '2012-03-07T00:19:42Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:40:20.776264Z'),
(129, 'radical', 'ladle', '斗', 'There''s a <radical>ladle</radical> scooping up two drops of liquid. See the handle and the scoop part of the ladle? Imagine ladling some water into your mouth. Mmm.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/129', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/ladle', 6, 1, '2012-03-07T00:20:00Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.420791Z'),
(130, 'radical', 'angel', 'ホ', 'First of all, there''s a cross making up the middle, which automatically makes this a religion-based radical. That cross is the frame of someone''s body and the things coming off of it are wings. This is an <radical>angel</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/130', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/angel', 6, 1, '2012-03-07T00:20:23Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:16:41.901026Z'),
(131, 'radical', 'boil', '灬', 'This radical looks like the fire from the burner of a stove. That''s because this is the <radical>boil</radical> radical. You need to boil a bunch of water over some flames.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/131', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/boil', 6, 1, '2012-03-07T00:22:15Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T05:58:29.379374Z'),
(132, 'radical', 'yakuza', '歹', 'This radical looks like a <radical>yakuza</radical>. See him facing left, with that threatening jawline and big pompadour hairstyle? Maybe imagine a couple scars on his face too. You know, real yakuza-like.

You could also remember this because it’s the evening radical under the ground radical, and people who do stuff underground in the evening (and night) are definitely yakuza.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/132', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/yakuza', 6, 1, '2012-03-07T00:22:24Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:44:35.552963Z'),
(133, 'radical', 'cyclops-133', '向', 'There''s a head with a little bit of hair on the top. There''s also one big eye in the middle. Only one creature has a head like this, and that creature is the <radical>cyclops</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/133', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/cyclops-133', 6, 1, '2012-03-07T00:22:32Z', '2018-12-05T18:38:50.795935Z', '2025-10-08T17:35:05.137759Z'),
(134, 'radical', 'jet', '未', 'This is like one of those <radical>jet</radical>s that have the little nose-wings going on too. Imagine you''re looking at the jet from the top down, this is what it would look like. It''s also why this is the jet radical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/134', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/jet', 6, 1, '2012-03-07T00:22:39Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:44:35.552963Z'),
(135, 'radical', 'grain', '禾', 'This is a tree with a drooping slide shape on top. What causes the top of plants to sag? The <radical>grain</radical>s. Imagine seeing how the heads of wheat or rice droop when they''re ready for harvest. It will look like this radical. Look at all that ripe grain!

This might remind you of 米, which has three stalks sprouting upward. You can tell the difference because the top of this one is slanted from the weight of the ripe grain.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/135', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/grain', 6, 1, '2012-03-07T00:22:47Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:45:00.948456Z'),
(136, 'radical', 'sheep', '羊', 'This is a very fluffy <radical>sheep</radical>. See the two horns poking up and all its wool poking out to the side? It''s so fluffy that you can hardly see its tiny little legs. What a cute sheep!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/136', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/sheep', 6, 1, '2012-03-07T00:22:55Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:06:12.518486Z'),
(137, 'radical', 'blood', '血', 'You see a little drop of something on your plate. Oh no… it''s <radical>BLOOD</radical>!!! Yep, you''re bleeding. Now you have to wash that plate again — just as soon as the bleeding stops. Don''t worry, your platelets should stop the bleeding soon. Haha, get it? PLATE-lets? Like blood platelets? Japanese blood jokes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/137', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/blood', 6, 1, '2012-03-07T00:23:41Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.523251Z'),
(138, 'radical', 'long', '長', 'When a stick figure has hair that drags on the ground and gets kicked all the time, you know that hair''s gotta be <radical>long</radical>. Why is it so looooong?! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/138', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/long', 6, 1, '2012-03-07T00:23:56Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.178881Z'),
(139, 'radical', 'eat', '食', 'You put on your hat and go outside to kick something white. It''s a big white goose and you killed it with your kick. That''s because you''re going to cook and <radical>eat</radical> it. Yum!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/139', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/eat', 6, 1, '2012-03-07T00:24:11Z', NULL, '2026-04-11T18:47:55.743592Z'),
(141, 'radical', 'mama', 'マ', 'This radical is just like the katakana character "ma" (マ). So let''s keep things simple and make the radical <radical>mama</radical>. It even looks like a mama leaning over and gently tapping her baby to help them fall asleep.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/141', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/mama', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T03:57:37Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:48:39.716764Z'),
(143, 'radical', 'fortune', '占', 'Someone has a toe in their mouth. They are a fortune teller telling your <radical>fortune</radical>. But for her to do it, she has to take one of your toes and taste it. This better be a good fortune for you to let someone put your foot in their mouth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/143', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/fortune', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:56:12Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:20:39.951302Z'),
(156, 'radical', 'neck', '首', 'This is the radical for <radical>neck</radical>. Look, you can see all the vertebrae stacked up, and the little ligaments that connect it to the base of the skull! That''s why this is the radical for neck.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/156', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/neck', 6, 1, '2012-03-22T01:46:28Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:04.866389Z'),
(174, 'radical', 'tongue', '舌', 'This is the <radical>tongue</radical> radical. See that sturdy slide holding a thousand people above a mouth? An open mouth can''t support that much weight, but a strong tongue can — it''s the muscle of the mouth, after all.

Alternatively, you can think of this as something sticking out of a mouth with thousands of tiny taste buds on it. It''s your tongue!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/174', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/tongue', 6, 1, '2012-04-05T23:07:15Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T00:09:32.879317Z'),
(212, 'radical', 'death', '亡', 'There''s a lid on a lion. And you''re stuck under the lid with the lion, which means you will surely meet your <radical>death</radical>. Or if you don''t want to die, think of the poor lion. If you leave a lion under a lid for too long, death will come for it too. Either way, somebody''s gonna die today.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/212', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/death', 6, 1, '2012-07-07T04:22:39.375766Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.602662Z'),
(224, 'radical', 'east', '東', 'Behind that tree the sun is rising in the <radical>east</radical>. The sun always rises in the east, so if you head that way, past the tree, you''ll be going east too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/224', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/east', 6, 1, '2012-08-27T20:15:02.829714Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:14:54.048086Z'),
(267, 'radical', 'city', '市', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>city</radical>.

In case you don''t remember the kanji''s meaning mnemonic, here''s a refresher:

You see a manhole <radical>lid</radical> and a <radical>towel</radical>, both with the name of a <kanji>city</kanji> on them. Decorative manhole lids and souvenir towels — two things cities love to slap their names on, especially in Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/267', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/city', 6, 1, '2012-11-01T00:56:52.795671Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.17165Z'),
(398, 'radical', 'house', '家', 'Under this roof is a pig with her little ones. Your pig, because it''s your <radical>house</radical>! You keep your piggies inside where it''s nice and warm. They''re all members of your family, so they deserve to live in your house too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/398', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/house', 6, 1, '2013-07-02T22:45:35.840632Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.59487Z'),
(127, 'radical', 'box', '凵', 'This <radical>box</radical> is open. There could be any number of things inside, but for now all you need to know is the meaning of this radical, which is box.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/127', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/box', 7, 1, '2012-03-07T00:19:21Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:12:37.987919Z'),
(142, 'radical', 'lack', '欠', 'A (modified) prison radical containing a person radical. What does a person experience in prison? <radical>Lack</radical>. Prison is all about lack — lack of freedom, lack of the comforts of home, lack of privacy, lack of conversation. Yep, the life of a person in prison is defined by lack.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/142', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/lack', 7, 1, '2012-03-08T07:04:09Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:03:31.014085Z'),
(145, 'radical', 'stamp', '卩', 'This is the <radical>stamp</radical> radical — the inking kind, not the postage kind. Why? Because it looks like a stamp stick! The top parts are thicker on one side so you can get a good grip. It even looks like the letter P — which just happens to be the last letter in "stamp"!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/145', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/stamp', 7, 1, '2012-03-22T00:53:03Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:05:23.496779Z'),
(146, 'radical', 'husband', '夫', 'The radicals ground and big make up this radical. There''s a big guy on the ground... who is that big guy? Embarrassingly, it''s your <radical>husband</radical>. Feel the shame that he got knocked down on the ground. By what? Who? How? (It doesn''t matter.)

Don''t confuse this with 未 (jet), though. See how 未 has a little tail at the end? That''s because jets and other planes have tails, while husbands don''t. At least I''d sure hope yours doesn''t.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/146', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/husband', 7, 1, '2012-03-22T00:54:16Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:41:52.065519Z'),
(147, 'radical', 'clan', '氏', 'Under a cliff, you spot a cross, slightly tilted. The cross is there to mark a famous <radical>clan</radical> that once ruled the lands below the cliff. It''s a pretty unique cross, so whenever you see it there under the cliff, you''ll know it honors the clan who lived there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/147', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/clan', 7, 1, '2012-03-22T00:54:29Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:40:04.640165Z'),
(149, 'radical', 'boat', '舟', 'This looks like a <radical>boat</radical> coming toward you in the water. See the sail billowing on top and the reflection on the water? Yep, that''s a boat. The boatiest boat that ever boated.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/149', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/boat', 7, 1, '2012-03-22T00:55:27Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:53:59.52241Z'),
(150, 'radical', 'fish', '魚', 'This is a <radical>fish</radical>. Kind of a funny-looking fish, with a weird head on top, a squat scaly body, and a tail fanning out underneath it. See the fish? Yeah, you see it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/150', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/fish', 7, 1, '2012-03-22T00:56:04Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.381811Z'),
(151, 'radical', 'bird', '鳥', 'See the cool wing on the bottom? See the head with the neat feather coming out of the top? This is a <radical>bird</radical>. Tweet tweet!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/151', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/bird', 7, 1, '2012-03-22T00:56:16Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:18:22.908078Z'),
(152, 'radical', 'yellow', '黄', 'Flowers that grow out of the ground of a rice paddy after you bury fins there will grow <radical>yellow</radical>. Why? Because yellow is the color of a fish''s soul, so planting flowers over their fins will turn them into a nice, pretty yellow. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/152', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/yellow', 7, 1, '2012-03-22T00:56:27Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.391227Z'),
(153, 'radical', 'black', '黒', 'If you boil a village, everything will turn <radical>black</radical>. Just imagine all those roads melting and coating everything in black tar. Picture it! It looks like Pompeii. (Oof, too soon?)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/153', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/black', 7, 1, '2012-03-22T00:57:21Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.401399Z'),
(169, 'radical', 'circumference', '周', 'You take your head and pack clay dirt around it to just above your mouth. You need the <radical>circumference</radical> of your head for your helmet maker, and sending them a clay dirt mold of your head will just do the trick. That way they''ll know your head''s exact circumference!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/169', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/circumference', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:23:23Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.412025Z'),
(173, 'radical', 'wing', '勿', 'See the <radical>wing</radical>? It''s kind of like a bat wing with webbing. Either way, it''s just a wing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/173', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/wing', 7, 1, '2012-04-05T19:11:06Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T17:35:03.677256Z'),
(177, 'radical', 'table', '几', 'What happens when you put a flat surface on top of a pair of legs? You get a <radical>table</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/177', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/table', 7, 1, '2012-04-23T23:47:54Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:51:54.258509Z'),
(192, 'radical', 'temple', '寺', 'This radical looks kind of like a <radical>temple</radical>, don''t you think? With all the layers and stuff? Just picture the top part as a roof, and the drop-looking bit at the bottom as a giant temple bell, with a monk standing beside it and ringing it with a large wooden beam. You see this kinda thing every day at a temple.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/192', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/temple', 7, 1, '2012-04-26T05:19:40Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.435311Z'),
(214, 'radical', 'gate', '門', 'This radical already looks like a <radical>gate</radical>, right? A high gate with pillars on either side and two small gate doors, way up at the top for some reason. Worst gate ever in terms of security, but definitely still a gate.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/214', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/gate', 7, 1, '2012-07-19T22:53:22.165077Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:21:27.248974Z'),
(228, 'radical', 'oneself', '己', 'This kinda looks like a head with an arm under it pointing up at itself. That''s why this radical means <radical>oneself</radical>. One points to oneself when one wants to… indicate oneself… ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/228', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/oneself', 7, 1, '2012-08-27T20:17:03.399635Z', NULL, '2025-12-25T00:33:47.230662Z'),
(361, 'radical', 'south', '南', 'This is the radical for <radical>south</radical>. See how it''s pointing south? You can think of the cross on top as a compass rose, and the arrow below (that ¥-looking part) as pointing down south. It''s a compass pointing nowhere but south!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/361', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/south', 7, 1, '2013-05-03T08:25:24.127855Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T15:17:49.222453Z'),
(418, 'radical', 'teacher', '孝', 'Who''s in the coffin next to that child? It''s his <radical>teacher</radical>. Teachers are pretty much dead around 3 p.m. every day, and it''s the kids in their class who wear them down. It ain''t easy being a teacher, you know.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/418', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/teacher', 7, 1, '2015-06-26T22:22:29.819804Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.479995Z'),
(419, 'radical', 'top-hat', '且', 'This radical looks just like a top hat. That''s why it''s <radical>top hat</radical>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/419', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/top-hat', 7, 1, '2015-06-26T22:23:03.011604Z', NULL, '2026-04-05T19:31:59.040578Z'),
(8823, 'radical', 'elephant', '象', 'This is an <radical>elephant</radical> — or at least, a wannabe elephant. Look closely. It''s actually a family of pigs, and the mama is wearing an elephant mask on her head for some reason. See the big ears and trunk on top? She wants to be an elephant so badly that she dressed up like one. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8823', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/elephant', 7, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:10.864037Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.591506Z'),
(157, 'radical', 'hook', 'ユ', 'This looks like a <radical>hook</radical>, like the kind a pirate would wear. The flat line shows where the hand was cut off, and the hook is right above, ready to take its place. Yarrrr.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/157', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/hook', 8, 1, '2012-03-22T01:47:41Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.264245Z'),
(158, 'radical', 'snake', '巳', 'This is the <radical>snake</radical> radical. See the head at the top with its long skinny body stretching down from it?

Don''t confuse this with 己 (oneself) — the snake has an extra line behind its head to connect to the rest of its body. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/158', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/snake', 8, 1, '2012-03-22T01:49:29Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.364893Z'),
(159, 'radical', 'cloud', '云', 'This is the radical for <radical>cloud</radical>. See how the cloud is puffy on the bottom but has two lines up top? That''s because a plane flew through it.

It was a nice cloud to hang out privately in, until that darn plane came through and ruined it. So much for your private cloud!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/159', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/cloud', 8, 1, '2012-03-22T01:50:05Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.383969Z'),
(160, 'radical', 'weapon', '殳', 'Stick a table on top of a stool and you end up with a very dangerous <radical>weapon</radical>. Imagine hitting someone with this thing! Or, even better, imagine making someone sit on it. They''d be crushed mentally and emotionally and whatnot. Now that''s a scary weapon.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/160', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/weapon', 8, 1, '2012-03-22T01:50:36Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:14:53.54038Z'),
(161, 'radical', 'compare', '比', 'This radical is made up of two slightly different versions of the spoon radical. Look closely at these two spoons. <radical>Compare</radical> their differences, like the different angles in the bends of the spoons. It''s like one of those "spot the difference" games where you compare two similar pictures. But this time it''s with spoons.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/161', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/compare', 8, 1, '2012-03-22T01:50:48Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:15:00.980995Z'),
(163, 'radical', 'net', '罒', 'This is a <radical>net</radical>, spread out over the water. Imagine all the fish you can catch in this net!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/163', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/net', 8, 1, '2012-03-22T01:51:29Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:43:29.897949Z'),
(164, 'radical', 'sickle', '釆', 'Most of this radical is just the rice radical, but the top part is something coming to cut the rice from its stalk. That, my friend, is the <radical>sickle</radical>. See how the rice is still there? That''s because it''s in motion. The sickle is literally doing the cutting RIGHT NOW! (And don''t confuse this with the grain 禾 radical!)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/164', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/sickle', 8, 1, '2012-03-22T01:51:48Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.530941Z'),
(165, 'radical', 'horse', '馬', 'This radical looks like a <radical>horse</radical>. See the long, thick neck with the mane flowing to the right? What might look like a wing is actually the horse''s four legs and its tail hanging down.

Don''t confuse this radical with the bird radical (鳥). They look similar, but this one doesn''t have the little feather sticking out of the top of its head!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/165', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/horse', 8, 1, '2012-03-22T02:07:28Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T07:14:39.016862Z'),
(172, 'radical', 'easy', '易', 'Flying to the sun with wings is <radical>easy</radical>! Or so Icarus thinks. Someone should probably tell him…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/172', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/easy', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T19:10:27Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:42:49.398284Z'),
(203, 'radical', 'someone', '者', 'You find a coffin on the sun. It''s burnt to a crisp from the heat, but there''s definitely <radical>someone</radical> in there. You wonder who this mysterious someone might have been. Whoever they were, they got a fancy sun burial, so they really must''ve been someone (or shall we say, <em>sun-one</em>?).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/203', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/someone', 8, 1, '2012-05-01T19:37:07Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T07:18:03.588956Z'),
(216, 'radical', 'suit', '合', 'This is the radical for <radical>suit</radical>, like when something suits you. You find a hat on the ground, and when you pick it up, there''s a mouth underneath. "It suits you! It suits you!" the mouth says. You try the hat on, and yup — it really suits! It''s weird getting fashion advice from a random mouth, but hey, it wasn''t wrong.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/216', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/suit', 8, 1, '2012-07-19T23:03:55.289604Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.567374Z'),
(217, 'radical', 'cage', '匚', 'This radical is a <radical>cage</radical> because it''s covered on all sides but one (the one open side is where the cage door or gate is). There''s nothing inside this cage because there''s no gate, and whatever was inside has gotten out. I hope it wasn''t rabid...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/217', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/cage', 8, 1, '2012-07-19T23:34:48.368602Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:29:41.941203Z'),
(299, 'radical', 'capital', '京', 'This looks like the kind of building you''d find in a Japanese <radical>capital</radical>. See the ornate roof and the stone foundation flaring out underneath, like a castle? The emperor lives in there. And wherever the emperor lives automatically becomes the capital city. That''s why this radical is capital.

Alternatively, you can use the radical parts and imagine a giant lid on the pot that will feed the mouths of all the small people in the capital.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/299', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/capital', 8, 1, '2012-12-06T07:49:48.701677Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T16:35:25.81941Z'),
(8780, 'radical', 'cape', '𠃌', 'This is a <radical>cape</radical>, like the kind a superhero would wear. Use your imagination to picture a hero facing left, with a cape tied around their neck and hanging down their back. With that cape on, they''re ready to save the world!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8780', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/cape', 8, 1, '2012-07-25T18:22:05.941878Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T00:57:41.875504Z'),
(166, 'radical', 'jammed-in', '介', 'This radical looks like a hat with some legs under it. If all you can see is legs, the person wearing the hat must really be <radical>jammed in</radical> there. Have you ever tried to cram yourself into a hat so hard it covers your body? Yeah, didn''t think so. But imagine how jammed in to that hat you''d be if you did.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/166', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/jammed-in', 9, 1, '2012-03-22T02:26:07Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:27:09.693473Z'),
(167, 'radical', 'history', '史', 'This is the <radical>history</radical> radical. You can see a textbook and a couple crossed swords. Because that''s basically how history works — fight battles, record the results, and do it all over again. History!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/167', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/history', 9, 1, '2012-03-22T02:26:17Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.263127Z'),
(168, 'radical', 'tent', '癶', 'Kind of a strange <radical>tent</radical>, I''ll admit, but one that will protect you from things trying to get in (that''s why it has all the spikes). It''s the best you can do, considering you''re in a cloth tent.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/168', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/tent', 9, 1, '2012-03-22T02:26:27Z', NULL, '2026-01-04T19:54:54.196787Z'),
(175, 'radical', 'clown', '咅', 'A <radical>clown</radical> face, with the funny hat at the top, the silly eyes in the middle, and the big laughing mouth at the bottom. This thing is pretty cute, unlike a lot of clowns…

You could also see this as the stand radical on top of the mouth radical, since standing on their mouth is the kind of weird trick a clown would do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/175', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/clown', 9, 1, '2012-04-20T18:54:52Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T00:50:25.948932Z'),
(176, 'radical', 'building', '阝', 'This radical looks like a curvy letter B. What does B stand for? <radical>Building</radical>! This radical often shows up in kanji related to gatherings or to places where people come together… like buildings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/176', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/building', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:37:21Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T17:20:01.534933Z'),
(178, 'radical', 'spicy', '辛', 'This is the <radical>spicy</radical> radical. See how it''s wide at the top, with a little stem sticking up, and narrow at the bottom? Just like a chili pepper. It''s so spicy it can''t even handle its own heat!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/178', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/spicy', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:27:02Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:24:37.367364Z'),
(188, 'radical', 'heavy', '重', 'This is the <radical>heavy</radical> radical. See the square fist in the center, grasping the handle of a dumbbell that has two heavy plates on the top and bottom? The radical just looks like it would be heavy, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/188', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/heavy', 9, 1, '2012-04-26T00:16:51Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T06:23:02.8357Z'),
(218, 'radical', 'anti', '反', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>anti</radical>.

Just in case you don''t remember this, here''s the kanji meaning mnemonic: The <radical>stool</radical> is in the <radical>cliff</radical>. If someone has a stool deep inside a cliff, they are most likely pretty <kanji>anti</kanji> society, hiding away like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/218', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/anti', 9, 1, '2012-07-25T18:51:46.213334Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:32:55.301653Z'),
(220, 'radical', 'brush', '聿', 'This radical kind of looks like a <radical>brush</radical>. The top part is a hand holding the brush handle, and the bottom part is the bristly tip.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/220', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/brush', 9, 1, '2012-08-08T18:36:26.096638Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.35095Z'),
(229, 'radical', 'correct', '正', 'You''re lost, but you''ve come across a signpost that says "<radical>correct</radical>" in all directions. "Correct," "correct," "correct," it says. No matter which way you go, it''s the correct way! What a relief!

You could also use the mnemonic you learned with the kanji: There is a line on the ground so you stop, making a giant sign pop up that says CORRECT! You obeyed the rules of the road and did the correct thing. Good on you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/229', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/correct', 9, 1, '2012-08-27T20:17:16.553471Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:32:16.580571Z'),
(417, 'radical', 'machine', '台', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>machine</radical>.

Just in case you don''t remember this, here''s the kanji meaning mnemonic: You have a <radical>private</radical> <radical>mouth</radical> that''s a <kanji>machine</kanji>. But you have to keep your machine mouth private because it would scare all the non-machine people around you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/417', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/machine', 9, 1, '2015-06-26T20:58:03.691792Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:59:45.447543Z'),
(420, 'radical', 'coat-rack', '疋', 'See all the arms sticking out of this radical? Those are for hanging lots of coats and things on, because this is a <radical>coat rack</radical>. Kind of a weird coat rack, but definitely a coat rack.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/420', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/coat-rack', 9, 1, '2015-06-26T22:24:31.155506Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:18:01.665189Z'),
(424, 'radical', 'helicopter', '覀', 'This radical looks like a <radical>helicopter</radical> coming straight at you, doesn''t it? You can''t see the tail part because it''s behind the bigger front cab of the helicopter. But you can see the front windows and the big spinning blade on top.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/424', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/helicopter', 9, 1, '2015-06-26T22:27:34.26486Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:11:43.434481Z'),
(8772, 'radical', 'cleat', '⺤', 'There''s the bottom of a shoe with spikes coming out of it. Only one kind of shoe is like this, and that''s a <radical>cleat</radical>. You know, like those studded shoes athletes wear. Soccer cleats, baseball cleats, running cleats… You knew they were called cleats, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8772', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/cleat', 9, 1, '2012-03-22T02:25:56Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:46:31.228624Z'),
(8779, 'radical', 'spring', '𡗗', 'This is the <radical>spring</radical> radical, because it looks like a new plant shoot has pushed its way up through layers of dirt, snow, and ice to announce the coming of spring. Spring is the season (and the radical) full of new, growing life!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8779', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/spring', 9, 1, '2012-07-19T23:21:20.584134Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:35:30.470443Z'),
(8785, 'radical', 'squid', '㑒', 'See the <radical>squid</radical> shape here? There''s its pointy head, its eyes, and its tentacles (two, anyway). Pretty squid-like if you ask me!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8785', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/squid', 9, 1, '2012-09-20T18:31:07.035981Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.443671Z'),
(8787, 'radical', 'yurt', NULL, 'There''s a canopy with a grid-like frame near the top. It''s a <radical>yurt</radical> with a window! Yurts are those dome-shaped huts nomads use. Once you''ve set up a canopy with a window frame, you''ve basically built one. Imagine gazing out that window, kicking back in your yurt. Yurt sweet yurt!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8787', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/yurt', 9, 1, '2012-10-30T19:44:34.545081Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:30:22.081118Z'),
(8819, 'radical', 'gladiator', '龹', 'Someone shoved horns onto your husband and said he has to fight for them now. They turned him into a <radical>gladiator</radical>! Now your husband has to fight for their amusement. Are you not entertained?!

P.S. You know this is a gladiator, not a viking, because the horns are on the gladiator''s shoulder pads instead of his helmet, and his helmet has a big crest on it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8819', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/gladiator', 9, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:01.530047Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:28:25.577532Z'),
(8820, 'radical', 'eternity', '永', 'A drop of water will last for an <radical>eternity</radical>. Especially if you drop it into an endless stream that never stops flowing. You can see these streams converging from all directions here, flowing for eternity.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8820', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/eternity', 9, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:03.108783Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.480005Z'),
(179, 'radical', 'tombstone', '圣', 'There is a stool sticking out of the dirt. You walk up and realize it has writing on it. It''s actually a <radical>tombstone</radical>. You can sit on it and remember the people who are below the dirt now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/179', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/tombstone', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T01:02:14Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:54.991411Z'),
(180, 'radical', 'kiss', '各', 'In winter, your mouth longs for a <radical>kiss</radical>. You know, because your lips get cold and you want someone to warm them up. How do you think that whole mistletoe thing got started? Cold winter mouths, longing for a kiss. So just imagine the top part of this radical as some winter mistletoe (or holly leaves, since that''s probably easier), and the bottom part as a kissy mouth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/180', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/kiss', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T01:02:24Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:24:01.572608Z'),
(181, 'radical', 'beans', '豆', 'This radical has a bean (the square in the middle) between the ground and some roots. That bean is going to turn into a plant someday. What will that plant produce? Great big <radical>beans</radical>, like the ones from Jack and the Beanstalk.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/181', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/beans', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T01:02:31Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:38:54.907026Z'),
(182, 'radical', 'alcohol', '酉', 'This looks like the west radical with a little something extra under it… And if you ask for "a little something extra" in the Wild West, you''re going to get a big ol'' decanter of bourbon, moonshine, or some other kind of very strong <radical>alcohol</radical> — which happens to be exactly what this radical looks like! That extra line at the bottom is just the little bit of alcohol still left in the bottle. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/182', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/alcohol', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T01:02:38Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:48:42.672332Z'),
(183, 'radical', 'landslide', '辰', 'If you kick a cliff twice you''ll start a <radical>landslide</radical>! Oh no!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/183', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/landslide', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T01:03:13Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.030263Z'),
(184, 'radical', 'geoduck', '頁', 'A leaf-shaped protrusion coming out of a shellfish. What is it? The mysterious <radical>geoduck</radical>! The geoduck is a giant clam so big that most of it grows outside its shell. If you''ve never seen a geoduck, look it up now. You''ll never be able to unsee it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/184', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/geoduck', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T01:03:38Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:38:54.907026Z'),
(185, 'radical', 'music', '曲', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>music</radical>! 曲 even looks like a musical score with two notes in the middle. Huh? Real sheet music has more lines, you say? Hey, it''s a radical — we''re saving ink, not performing Beethoven here.

Alternatively, you could use the kanji mnemonic you learned: There are twenty mouths singing beautiful music together.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/185', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/music', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:27:29Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.075706Z'),
(186, 'radical', 'dynamite', '丙', 'Doesn''t this radical look like one of those old-fashioned <radical>dynamite</radical> plunger things that gets pushed to set the fuse? That makes this dynamite!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/186', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/dynamite', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:46:57Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.089758Z'),
(187, 'radical', 'sick', '疒', 'This is the canopy radical with ice running down the back. If you hang out in a cold canopy like this you''ll definitely get <radical>sick</radical>. Imagine being sick and alone inside a cold canopy. Brrrr, oh no... ACHOO!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/187', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/sick', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:48:51Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:48:39.716764Z'),
(200, 'radical', 'stairs', '乃', 'This radical looks like a little flight of <radical>stairs</radical>, don''t you think? Also note it''s being held up by a pillar or beam or something on the left side. After all, you don''t want your stairs collapsing as you walk up them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/200', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/stairs', 10, 1, '2012-04-26T06:21:15Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.115412Z'),
(219, 'radical', 'bundle', '束', 'This looks like a tree with something tied around its middle, holding it tight. If you untied that middle part you''d realize it''s actually a bunch of little trees (aka twigs and sticks) tied in a <radical>bundle</radical>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/219', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/bundle', 10, 1, '2012-08-02T17:34:17.622504Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.128135Z'),
(240, 'radical', 'turkey', '隹', 'This is the <radical>turkey</radical> radical. It kinda looks like a turkey facing to the left, with big tail feathers fanning out behind it. See it? You can practically hear this thing gobble!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/240', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/turkey', 10, 1, '2012-09-18T03:37:49.401957Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T08:18:25.050242Z'),
(275, 'radical', 'fault', '失', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>fault</radical>.

Just in case you don''t remember the kanji''s meaning mnemonic, here''s the gist: That slide is your husband''s only <kanji>fault</kanji>. He spends all day sliding down it like a madman, which is why his hair sticks straight up on top. Maybe that''s another fault… It''s also a way to remember this is 失 (fault), not 矢 (arrow).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/275', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/fault', 10, 1, '2012-11-07T20:11:39.421032Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.146719Z'),
(439, 'radical', 'sell', '売', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>sell</radical>.

Just in case you don''t remember, here''s the kanji meaning mnemonic: A samurai has come bearing foreheads and legs that he''s trying to <kanji>sell</kanji>. He thought it was a waste to leave all his slain enemies on the battlefield, so he gathered up their foreheads and legs to sell on the black market for some extra cash. Hey, no judging! A samurai''s gotta sell what a samurai''s gotta sell, okay?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/439', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/sell', 10, 1, '2015-10-28T23:02:48.983071Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:18:52.065407Z'),
(8773, 'radical', 'pope', NULL, 'This is the <radical>pope</radical> radical. Why? Because it looks like a pope hat! This one''s got a big old cross on top, and it can only be worn by the pope.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8773', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/pope', 10, 1, '2012-04-20T18:55:19Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:54.979023Z'),
(8774, 'radical', 'chinese', '𦰩', 'This is the <radical>Chinese</radical> radical. Why? Because it looks like a traditional Chinese pagoda! One of those towers with a bunch of tiered roofs, you know? Sure, lots of Asian countries have pagodas, but let''s be honest — the Chinese probably came up with them first.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8774', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/chinese', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T01:03:46Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.168794Z'),
(8821, 'radical', 'straight', '直', 'Oh no, you let the pope''s hat get snatched by a lion! You get it back, but it''s all crooked, so you''ve got to make it <radical>straight</radical> again. The pope needs a straight, dignified hat, so you''d better straighten that thing out.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8821', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/straight', 10, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:06.37855Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.177957Z'),
(189, 'radical', 'orders', '令', 'This radical means <radical>orders</radical>, because it looks like Napoleon with his big bicorne hat, giving orders. You move, or his orders move you.

See how his body looks like the stamp radical (卩)? If it helps, you can think of Napoleon''s written orders as bearing his stamp of approval. All official orders need the stamp of that guy under the big hat, you know? ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/189', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/orders', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T05:10:54Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:27:09.739767Z'),
(190, 'radical', 'humble', '申', 'This radical is the same as the kanji you learned earlier, but the meaning is a little different. It''s not just for saying things humbly, it''s for being <radical>humble</radical> in general. When you see this radical, think about being humble.

You can kinda see this as someone with their hands pressed together in front of their body, in a very humble posture. Maybe a humble Buddhist monk wearing some big robes? Imagine him all humble there, head bowed, hands pressed together.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/190', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/humble', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T05:19:09Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:25:51.03358Z'),
(191, 'radical', 'older-brother', '兄', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>older brother</radical>.

In case you need a refresher, here''s the mnemonic again: Who''s someone who''s just a mouth with legs? That''s your know-it-all older brother, who won''t shut up about anything.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/191', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/older-brother', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T05:19:31Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:24:00.627278Z'),
(193, 'radical', 'escalator', '及', 'Remember the stairs (乃) radical? This is a lot like that, but it has another stroke to motorize it. The extra bit makes the stairs move by themselves — that''s why this is an <radical>escalator</radical>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/193', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/escalator', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T05:19:49Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:22:13.246838Z'),
(194, 'radical', 'drunkard', '戈', 'This is a ceremony radical with a slide radical. Why would a ceremony need a slide? For <radical>drunkard</radical>s, of course! People love to drink at ceremonies, and when they''ve had too much, those drunkards like to go down slides. If your ceremony has a slide, the drunkards will be happy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/194', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/drunkard', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T05:20:00Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:43:01.883768Z'),
(195, 'radical', 'skin', '皮', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>skin</radical>.

Here''s a mnemonic refresher just in case: You go down a slide made of branches and your skin scrapes right off — ouch! Lucky for you, it was just your old outer layer of skin, and now you''ve got a fresh shiny layer. Yay for exfoliation! You''re not sure what to do with this big, floppy layer of old skin, though…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/195', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/skin', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T05:20:11Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:17:49.824318Z'),
(196, 'radical', 'root', '艮', 'Imagine a <radical>root</radical> vegetable, like a turnip or something. See the twisted bulb at the top, with long, stringy root tendrils dangling under it? That''s why this is the root radical.

Alternatively, focus on the components: sun and kick. When the sun is out, you''re outside kicking things around. But be careful — kick a root, and you might trip over it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/196', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/root', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T05:20:53Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T09:52:41.789411Z'),
(197, 'radical', 'sound', '音', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>sound</radical>. You can even see someone standing at a kind of square table here. It''s a DJ at the turntables! Imagine them spinning records and scratching out all kinds of cool sounds.

You could also remember this with the kanji mnemonic we gave you. Here''s the gist of it: If you stand on the sun, you''ll hear a very loud sizzling sound.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/197', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/sound', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T05:26:31Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:21:29.482657Z'),
(198, 'radical', 'few', '少', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>few</radical>.

Here''s a mnemonic refresher, just in case: You have a small slide. The thing about small slides is that <kanji>few</kanji> people can use them. Not everyone can fit their bum in a small slide — only a few small ones can make it down. That''s why this means "few"!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/198', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/few', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:16:19Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:11:13.150822Z'),
(199, 'radical', 'simple', '単', 'This the radical for <radical>simple</radical>. Why? Because it looks like a simple little stick figure drawing! Just a big head on top with a few hairs, and a stick figure body. What could be more simple?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/199', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/simple', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:20:37Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:33:28.795445Z'),
(8775, 'radical', 'blackjack', '龷', 'You have the radical twenty and the kanji one. Put those together and you get 21. What''s another name for 21? That''s <radical>blackjack</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8775', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/blackjack', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T05:15:20Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:02:28.74426Z'),
(8776, 'radical', 'trash', '𠫓', 'There''s a lid on top of something you''re trying to keep private. It''s your <radical>trash</radical>. You wouldn''t want people going through your private trash, which is why we put lids on our trash cans. It''s also illegal to look through someone''s trash, so make sure you never do that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8776', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/trash', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T05:18:47Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:01:22.626489Z'),
(8777, 'radical', 'bear', '㠯', 'This is the radical for <radical>bear</radical>, because it looks like the letter B — B for bear! You know this one is "bear" and not "building" because it''s all boxy, like a stocky, chunky bear who''s all fat and ready to hibernate.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8777', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/bear', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T05:19:24Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:00:59.482036Z'),
(9392, 'radical', 'outfit', '衤', 'At first glance, this looks like the Spirit radical, but it''s actually just a guy in a similar <radical>outfit</radical>. You can tell because of that thing sticking off of his sleeve on the right side — that''s the outfit''s price tag. Where in the world did this guy go to buy an outfit like this?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9392', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/outfit', 11, 1, '2025-12-21T20:49:50.481069Z', NULL, '2026-03-03T08:14:42.406318Z'),
(201, 'radical', 'early', '早', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>early</radical>.

Here''s a mnemonic refresher just in case: When the sun is above the cross, it''s time for some people to go church — which is always way too early on a Sunday, am I right? Heck, on Sunday (日) even 10 a.m. (十)  is too early.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/201', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/early', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:36:51Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:51:28.16439Z'),
(202, 'radical', 'younger-brother', '弟', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>younger brother</radical>.

Here''s a mnemonic refresher just in case: There is someone wearing horns, holding a bow, and going down a slide held up by a stick. What type of person do you imagine? I''d say someone who''s an immature boy. That would be your younger brother.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/202', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/younger-brother', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:36:59Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:48:25.666596Z'),
(204, 'radical', 'asia', '亜', 'This looks like the helicopter radical with something extra under it. It''s carrying some kind of cargo. Where''s it headed? To <radical>Asia</radical>. You only know it''s going to Asia because the cargo has a big TO ASIA label on it. (Go ahead and imagine it now.) 

I guess helicopters are the best way to deliver supplies in Asia because there are so many islands and mountains to fly between.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/204', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/asia', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:37:15Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T06:51:37.593895Z'),
(205, 'radical', 'crab', '其', 'This looks like the shellfish radical, but a little different. It''s the mighty <radical>crab</radical>! See the eyes and raised pincers at the top, the segmented belly in the middle, and the legs bunched up at the bottom? It takes some imagination, but this spiky shellfish definitely looks like a crab.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/205', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/crab', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:37:22Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:09:54.496507Z'),
(206, 'radical', 'yoga', '廴', 'This looks like someone sitting with their back straight and a leg stretched out behind them. What kind of pose is this? Probably the kind you do in <radical>yoga</radical>. Sit on a yoga mat and make your body look like this radical. Nice, you''re really doing yoga now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/206', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/yoga', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:37:29Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T19:04:44.908674Z'),
(207, 'radical', 'jackhammer', '示', 'Do you see the handles on the top and the barb on the bottom? It''s a <radical>jackhammer</radical>. How can you tell? The two diagonal lines on the side show the motion of the jackhammer going up and down really fast, doing what jackhammers do. BBBHHRZHHHHRHHRZHZHHZH JUJUJUJUJU.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/207', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/jackhammer', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:37:36Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T20:07:49.147854Z'),
(208, 'radical', 'excuse', '免', 'This radical consists of some legs carrying the sun out of prison. Those legs are yours. When the police stop you, though, what''s your <radical>excuse</radical>? Come up with one and say it out loud, starting with, "My excuse is..."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/208', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/excuse', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:37:44Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.050048Z'),
(269, 'radical', 'together', '共', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>together</radical>. And see how it kind of looks like two people happily sitting together arm-in-arm? These two clearly enjoy doing stuff together. They''re together so much they''re practically joined at the hip.

Alternatively, think of this as blackjack cards held together by fins. Fish love blackjack because the hands are usually just a few cards, so even fins can hold them together.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/269', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/together', 12, 1, '2012-11-01T03:27:44.294341Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T19:00:42.388312Z'),
(339, 'radical', 'beforehand', '予', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>beforehand</radical>.

Here''s a mnemonic refresher just in case: What does your mama tell you to do in the street? Look before you cross! You gotta do it beforehand, or it could be too late.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/339', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/beforehand', 12, 1, '2013-03-15T19:48:58.181079Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:22:56.408073Z'),
(390, 'radical', 'business', '業', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>business</radical>.

Here''s a mnemonic refresher just in case: You have spikes growing out of a sheep with fins. Turn this thing into a business! You could totally charge people money to look at it. Sure, you could also donate it to science, but a business-minded person like you knows a business opportunity when you see one. Step right up! Just five dollars for a glance at the rare spiky mer-sheep!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/390', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/business', 12, 1, '2013-07-02T21:24:38.823474Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:33.880456Z'),
(399, 'radical', 'request', '求', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>request</radical>.

Here''s a mnemonic refresher just in case: Look at the ground! You spilled some water drops, so I request that you wipe them up. I''d do it myself, but you''re the one who spilled them, so I''m requesting that you do it. Pretty reasonable request, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/399', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/request', 12, 1, '2013-07-04T00:02:48.957691Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:52:23.096028Z'),
(8778, 'radical', 'tofu', NULL, 'See the T and F that make up this radical? Those letters stand for <radical>tofu</radical>, our favorite bean curd blocks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8778', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/tofu', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:36:40Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:15:23.483599Z'),
(215, 'radical', 'shrimp', '尺', 'This radical has a little head, a curved abdomen, and some long, spindly antennae sticking out from its nose. There''s no mistaking it — this little guy is a <radical>shrimp</radical>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/215', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/shrimp', 13, 1, '2012-07-19T22:58:48.031223Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:39:51.991569Z'),
(221, 'radical', 'idea', '意', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>idea</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/221', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/idea', 13, 1, '2012-08-27T20:12:03.947356Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T21:20:06.990299Z'),
(222, 'radical', 'womb', '坴', 'Legs appear between two lumps of dirt. This is the earth''s version of a <radical>womb</radical>. Imagine a tiny baby growing under the ground and then sprouting out, legs first, into the world. Imagine being inside this dirt womb. It''s warm and damp. You feel safe.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/222', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/womb', 13, 1, '2012-08-27T20:12:47.328255Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.173927Z'),
(223, 'radical', 'circle', '丸', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>circle</radical>.

Just in case you don''t remember this, here''s the kanji meaning mnemonic: What''s something with <radical>nine</radical> sides? Just add a <radical>drop</radical> and you''ll have something that''s almost a <kanji>circle</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/223', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/circle', 13, 1, '2012-08-27T20:14:32.147587Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.179789Z'),
(225, 'radical', 'roof-225', '亼', 'This radical looks just like a <radical>roof</radical>, does it not?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/225', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/roof-225', 13, 1, '2012-08-27T20:16:11.87894Z', '2018-12-05T18:38:50.834567Z', '2025-10-01T06:09:48.183601Z'),
(226, 'radical', 'bookshelf', '冊', 'This looks like a bunch of books on a shelf. It''s a <radical>bookshelf</radical>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/226', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/bookshelf', 13, 1, '2012-08-27T20:16:24.204921Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:39:43.932146Z'),
(227, 'radical', 'products', '品', 'You see three squares stacked neatly together. On closer inspection, you realize these are boxed <radical>products</radical> waiting to be shipped out.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/227', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/products', 13, 1, '2012-08-27T20:16:34.311283Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(230, 'radical', 'original', '原', 'See that cliff with the white and small bits in it? That''s an <radical>original</radical> cliff — we call it that because the white and small bits are actually the bones and fossils of our planet''s original inhabitants, buried in the cliffside. (And this one''s got a massive sign that says "ORIGINAL," because everything''s gotta be a tourist spot these days… sigh.)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/230', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/original', 13, 1, '2012-08-27T20:17:28.148081Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:23.529491Z'),
(232, 'radical', 'soul', '忄', 'You spot a wispy shape flanked by ethereal orbs of flame. Don''t be scared — this is just a friendly little <radical>soul</radical> on its way to get reincarnated into a new body.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/232', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/soul', 13, 1, '2012-08-27T20:17:54.536468Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:43:29.897949Z'),
(234, 'radical', 'body', '身', 'The radical is the same as the kanji, so it means <radical>body</radical>. 

Just in case you need a refresher, here''s the mnemonic again: When your self goes down a slide and hits a barb, it''s a painful reminder that you have a body. Sometimes people think their self is just some kind of floating, disembodied consciousness, but getting a barb stuck in oneself while going down a slide will quickly remind one of one''s bodily existence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/234', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/body', 13, 1, '2012-08-27T20:19:01.672542Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:25:41.463362Z'),
(235, 'radical', 'fruit', '果', 'There''s a rice paddy on top of a tree… Figuratively, because trees don''t bear rice, they bear <radical>fruit</radical>! Use your imagination, and those four little boxes on top of the tree kind of look like big, ripe fruits — just waiting to be picked.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/235', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/fruit', 13, 1, '2012-08-27T20:19:52.44409Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:29:09.139112Z'),
(239, 'radical', 'hundred', '百', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>hundred</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/239', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/hundred', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:46:06.174134Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.308634Z'),
(392, 'radical', 'can', '缶', 'It''s kind of hard to see, but if you look at this the right way it looks like a food <radical>can</radical>. See the lid being pulled off in the top-left corner? And that horizontal line in the middle? That’s the can''s label.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/392', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/can', 13, 1, '2013-07-02T22:05:17.90842Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T17:20:01.534933Z'),
(8781, 'radical', 'creeper', NULL, 'Someone''s lying on the ground with his mouth open hoping to swallow up passers-by. This guy''s a weirdo — a real <radical>creeper</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8781', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/creeper', 13, 1, '2012-08-27T20:11:53.684887Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:26:41.2312Z'),
(8822, 'radical', 'good', '良', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>good</radical>.

Just in case you don''t remember this, here''s the kanji meaning mnemonic: A <radical>drop</radical> of water to a <radical>root</radical> is <kanji>good</kanji>. It''s good for the roots to grow and live and thrive. And what''s good for roots is good for our plants and us humans too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8822', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/good', 13, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:08.322373Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:40:05.658345Z'),
(9389, 'radical', 'canine', '戌', 'This is the radical for <radical>canine</radical>, because it looks like a police dog — aka a K-9 — marching around on patrol. You can see its proud little head on the top right, its front legs below crossing as it walks, and a police vest labeled "K-9" strapped around its body. Evil-doers beware — this canine''s gonna sniff you out and bring you to justice.

Alternately, you could see the F-shape on the left as a cheerful canine on its hind legs dancing with a drunkard, arm in arm as they sway along together. What a happy canine, dancing like that with a drunk person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9389', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/canine', 13, 1, '2025-12-21T20:33:56.55691Z', NULL, '2026-01-23T01:40:34.468616Z'),
(144, 'radical', 'animal', '犭', 'This radical swings like a monkey, howls like a wolf, and pounces like a leopard. You can almost hear the jungle chorus… This is the radical for <radical>animal</radical>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/144', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/animal', 14, 1, '2012-03-22T00:46:58Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:51:14.91558Z'),
(236, 'radical', 'pool', '勺', 'What do you call a prison for drops of water? A <radical>pool</radical>! The water may not like being in there, but the rest of us sure appreciate it on a hot summer day.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/236', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/pool', 14, 1, '2012-08-28T00:02:49.339904Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:50:09.76393Z'),
(237, 'radical', 'public', '公', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>public</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/237', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/public', 14, 1, '2012-08-28T00:03:04.599162Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.278619Z'),
(246, 'radical', 'well', '井', 'This is the radical for <radical>well</radical> — the kind you use to draw up water. Picture an old-timey stone well with wooden posts holding up a crank for reeling up your bucket! Pretty <em>well</em> built, huh?

<em>Well</em>, <em>well</em>… Still can''t see it? Try another view: picture a well seen from above. This one''s got a square-shaped opening, ''cause that''s what Japanese wells used to look like in the old days. Hope this one works well for you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/246', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/well', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:42:49.600329Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:45:31.077362Z'),
(247, 'radical', 'part', '分', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>part</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/247', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/part', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:05:21.896016Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T22:08:10.397284Z'),
(264, 'radical', 'talent', '才', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means  <radical>talent</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/264', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/talent', 14, 1, '2012-11-01T00:56:34.510041Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T16:59:59.548262Z'),
(8782, 'radical', 'bar', '㦮', 'It''s the drunkard from before, but this time she''s leaning on some kind of horizontal surface. Knowing her, it''s probably the counter of a <radical>bar</radical>. Hopefully she''s not causing too much trouble for the bar staff with her drunken antics.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8782', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/bar', 14, 1, '2012-08-28T00:05:37.697278Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:43:44.786552Z'),
(242, 'radical', 'paragraph', '句', 'In prison, your mouth is not allowed to talk freely. Get a piece of paper to use in place of your mouth and start writing your thoughts out in <radical>paragraph</radical> form!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/242', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/paragraph', 15, 1, '2012-09-18T23:33:40.646661Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:48.995533Z'),
(244, 'radical', 'past', '去', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>past</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/244', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/past', 15, 1, '2012-09-18T23:34:05.742973Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.007278Z'),
(245, 'radical', 'walk', '歩', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>walk</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/245', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/walk', 15, 1, '2012-09-18T23:34:29.729664Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.017152Z'),
(408, 'radical', 'height', '丈', 'You see a narwhal struggling with a measuring tape, trying to measure his <radical>height</radical>! His fins can''t reach the end of the tape, but of course he wants to include his glorious tusk. Measuring height is not an easy tusk… er, task for a narwhal, but he''ll figure it out. Probably.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/408', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/height', 15, 1, '2013-08-19T17:45:36.216442Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.024895Z'),
(437, 'radical', 'courage', '勇', 'When a mama watches over a man, it gives him the <radical>courage</radical> he needs to take on any foe.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/437', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/courage', 15, 1, '2015-06-26T22:52:06.018741Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:48.984892Z'),
(8783, 'radical', 'grass', '𭕄', 'Don''t confuse this with triceratops. <radical>Grass</radical> is closer together, and a little bushier, like this. It''s also more random and squished down in parts. If you see a bush of lines like this, it''s probably going to be grass.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8783', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/grass', 15, 1, '2012-09-18T23:30:37.698252Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.036685Z'),
(8824, 'radical', 'scarecrow', '畐', 'A creeper in a rice paddy? That''s just a <radical>scarecrow</radical>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8824', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/scarecrow', 15, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:12.68508Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.15141Z'),
(9329, 'radical', 'again', '亦', 'What''s under the lid on your cook pot? Legs and fins. <radical>Again</radical>. All you know how to cook is legs (of chicken) and fins, so you cook them again and again and again. You''ll be having them for dinner tonight, and again tomorrow, and again the next day. Legs and fins, again and again and again, for the rest of your days.

Alternatively, you can also think of this as a laundry hanger with a pair of pants and two little socks flapping like fins. Because laundry is something you have to do again and again… whether you like it or not!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9329', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/again', 15, 1, '2024-02-12T19:34:01.366819Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:55:23.433642Z'),
(9391, 'radical', 'broom', '帚', 'This is the <radical>broom</radical> radical, because the top part looks like a sideways hand broom and the bottom part looks like a dustpan.

Alternatively, you can remember this one with its components: Wolverine wipes his forehead with a towel every time he picks up a broom, because sweeping gets him sweaty fast. Poor guy can fight supervillains all day, but give him a broom and he''s instantly exhausted.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9391', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/broom', 15, 1, '2025-12-21T20:45:04.690882Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T02:46:21.114484Z'),
(248, 'radical', 'attach', '付', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>attach</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/248', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/attach', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:30:33.101869Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:33:00.426254Z'),
(249, 'radical', 'valley', '谷', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>valley</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/249', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/valley', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:30:46.162358Z', NULL, '2026-03-07T17:49:05.230084Z'),
(250, 'radical', 'face', '面', 'You have a leaf, a mouth, and an eye. Those are all things you''ll find on a <radical>face</radical>! Wait, you don''t have a leaf growing out of your face? Huh... well just imagine you have a normal human face with a leaf on there somewhere, ya weirdo.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/250', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/face', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:15:34.018781Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.499411Z'),
(421, 'radical', 'drum', '壴', 'This radical is made up of samurai and beans. Samurai used to put beans in <radical>drum</radical>s so they could tap and shake them to make noise. Can you imagine shaking and tapping this drum radical?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/421', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/drum', 16, 1, '2015-06-26T22:25:15.94581Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.507729Z'),
(8784, 'radical', 'zombie', '袁', 'This radical has a figure reaching out both hands at you with some dirt on his head. This is a <radical>zombie</radical>, and he must have just recently dug himself out of the grave by the looks of it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8784', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/zombie', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:30:14.031349Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.515838Z'),
(209, 'radical', 'criminal', '非', 'You think you see some legs behind those bars. Who gets put behind bars? Only <radical>criminal</radical>s. This criminal is pretty tall, since you only see his legs behind there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/209', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/criminal', 17, 1, '2012-05-01T19:37:51Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T23:26:20.979644Z'),
(251, 'radical', 'comfort', '楽', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>comfort</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/251', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/comfort', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:42:59.225087Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.396735Z'),
(252, 'radical', 'nothing', '無', 'There are a bunch of cages boiling. But you look inside the cages and there... there''s <radical>nothing</radical>. You boiled whatever was in there down to nothing!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/252', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/nothing', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:43:10.791182Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.412736Z'),
(253, 'radical', 'good-luck', '吉', 'This is the samurai radical on top of the mouth radical. If you get to hear what a samurai says, you''ll have really <radical>good luck</radical>. Samurai are pretty hard to find nowadays, which is probably why my luck has been so bad...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/253', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/good-luck', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:43:20.918188Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.411562Z'),
(254, 'radical', 'tiger', '虍', 'You lost seven toes on a cliff, but you consider yourself lucky considering you got them taken off by the cliff <radical>tiger</radical> that lives in the area. Imagine a tiger taking your seven toes as you hang off the edge of a cliff, if it helps. Exciting! (And also painful!)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/254', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/tiger', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:43:47.831479Z', NULL, '2026-03-26T08:11:42.807516Z'),
(255, 'radical', 'poem', '苟', 'The flowery paragraph isn''t just regular writing. It''s flowery, which makes it a <radical>poem</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/255', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/poem', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:44:22.06412Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.424276Z'),
(422, 'radical', 'guard', '兑', 'This radical is made up of horns and older brother. But those aren''t really horns, they''re a part of your older brother''s new <radical>guard</radical> uniform. He''s a guard now, of a bank or a museum. You don''t know which, but you know he stands around a lot in his uniform now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/422', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/guard', 17, 1, '2015-06-26T22:25:58.505989Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:45:00.948456Z'),
(8786, 'radical', 'worm-8786', NULL, 'The "soil mouth" is the mouth that eats the soil. The animal that does this best is the <radical>worm</radical>. Nom nom nom.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8786', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/worm-8786', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:44:05.725179Z', '2018-12-05T18:38:50.864286Z', '2025-10-01T06:10:31.56084Z'),
(8825, 'radical', 'festival', '祭', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>festival</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8825', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/festival', 17, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:15.36152Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.833401Z'),
(256, 'radical', 'long-ago', '昔', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>long ago</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/256', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/long-ago', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:38:13.113889Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:44:31.146854Z'),
(257, 'radical', 'gambler', '尭', 'The <radical>gambler</radical> holds a cross as he plays blackjack. He stands on his two legs expectantly, hoping he hits the big one. He just put all his money down. If he doesn''t win this one the gambler loses everything, including the two legs he''s standing on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/257', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/gambler', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:38:28.083933Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.432878Z'),
(258, 'radical', 'previous', '先', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>previous</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/258', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/previous', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:38:50.000813Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(259, 'radical', 'mask', '曽', 'This looks kind of like a <radical>mask</radical>, right? It has horns, it has crazy eyes, and it has a big mouth. Nobody would actually look like this, which is why this is just a mask.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/259', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/mask', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:38:59.837464Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.455504Z'),
(260, 'radical', 'happiness', '幸', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>happiness</radical>. Don''t get it confused with the spicy radical 辛, they''re very similar!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/260', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/happiness', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:39:11.090436Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:31:20.273093Z'),
(261, 'radical', 'conflict', '争', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>conflict</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/261', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/conflict', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:39:22.107823Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:08:32.29071Z'),
(262, 'radical', 'treasure-chest', '凶', 'There''s a treasure radical inside this box. What holds treasures? A <radical>treasure chest</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/262', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/treasure-chest', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:39:37.106899Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:02:33.540143Z'),
(415, 'radical', 'noon', '午', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>noon</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/415', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/noon', 18, 1, '2014-01-17T22:53:31.301455Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.689479Z'),
(263, 'radical', 'become', '成', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>become</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/263', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/become', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T00:56:20.624816Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.492465Z'),
(265, 'radical', 'black-hole', '复', 'When you shoot your gun at the sun, it becomes winter. The shot you fired sent the sun into a crazy chain reaction, making it implode in on itself and become a <radical>black hole</radical>. Imagine this happening and imagine the shock you''d feel. You shot the sun. You caused this infinite winter (and soon black hole death).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/265', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/black-hole', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T00:56:39.941512Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:29:08.554779Z'),
(266, 'radical', 'director', '司', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>director</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/266', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/director', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T00:56:46.94187Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T17:40:57.020503Z'),
(268, 'radical', 'reason', '由', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>reason</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/268', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/reason', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T00:56:59.374838Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:29:03.843014Z'),
(272, 'radical', 'ego', '我', 'There''s a drunkard holding out his fingers. He''s staring at them. Realizing who he is. He''s becoming aware of his <radical>ego</radical>. Now that he''s aware of himself, he''s probably going to get an inflated ego too. Yuck.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/272', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/ego', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:51:09.508368Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.562485Z'),
(362, 'radical', 'right', '右', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>right</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/362', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/right', 19, 1, '2013-05-03T08:25:31.179311Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T17:45:03.179824Z'),
(270, 'radical', 'fly', '飛', 'This radical is pretty complicated, but not much looks like this radical, so you''re in luck. This looks like a giant flying fortress of some kind. See the propellers on the back as it moves to the left? Just imagine something like this flying through the air. That''s why this radical is <radical>fly</radical>, because it is flying.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/270', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/fly', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T04:50:33.953671Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.5458Z'),
(271, 'radical', 'mona-lisa', '兼', 'This radical looks like a combination of the brush radical (聿) and the beauty kanji (美). So what''s something beautiful you can paint with a brush? The most famous is probably the <radical>Mona Lisa</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/271', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/mona-lisa', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T04:51:00.749339Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.550367Z'),
(273, 'radical', 'flood', '巛', 'The three lines that make up the river radical are being pulled to one direction. It''s a giant force of water going to the left. It''s a <radical>flood</radical>! RUN!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/273', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/flood', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T04:55:07.307788Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.563415Z'),
(274, 'radical', 'mole', '至', 'There''s trash on some dirt that seems to be... moving? If you lift up that trash you''ll find a <radical>mole</radical> in the dirt! Moles like to dig around underground and look for stuff to grab (like trash). They''re the raccoons of the ground.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/274', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/mole', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T04:55:21.6443Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.56522Z'),
(383, 'radical', 'righteousness', '義', 'The horns on the king give him an ego. Which in turn gives him a sense of <reading>righteousness</reading>. He shouldn''t act all righteous like that, but I suppose it''s what kings do. If you had horns and a big ego, you''d probably think you were the epitome of righteousness too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/383', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/righteousness', 20, 1, '2013-06-24T22:39:02.719661Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:46:56.671656Z'),
(8788, 'radical', 'explosion', NULL, 'See the energy bursting outward? That''s an <radical>explosion</radical>. Boom!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8788', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/explosion', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T04:50:44.694864Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:37:37.525129Z'),
(148, 'radical', 'spear', '矛', 'Your mama is carrying some kind of elaborate weapon. You look closely and see that it''s a <radical>spear</radical>. The pointy end is to the right, and there are some decorations hanging off it. It''s a very fancy spear, and you wonder where your mama got it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/148', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/spear', 21, 1, '2012-03-22T00:54:40Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:47.579837Z'),
(276, 'radical', 'mohawk', '啇', 'You have something old on your head that''s standing up. What is this thing? An awesome <radical>mohawk</radical>. You''ve had it so long it''s looking pretty old... but still awesome.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/276', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/mohawk', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T05:51:03.631654Z', NULL, '2026-01-18T20:18:48.214331Z'),
(277, 'radical', 'simultaneous', '斉', 'If you''re writing while looking at the moon, you can bet someone else is doing it too. People have a habit of looking at the moon <radical>simultaneous</radical>ly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/277', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/simultaneous', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T05:51:26.596344Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.589017Z'),
(278, 'radical', 'next', '次', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>next</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/278', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/next', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T05:51:41.253002Z', NULL, '2026-03-17T23:06:58.837958Z'),
(280, 'radical', 'number', '番', 'This radical is the same as the kanji, but "number in a series" is too long. Let''s shorten it to <radical>number</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/280', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/number', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T05:51:58.568754Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.609037Z'),
(305, 'radical', 'half', '半', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>half</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/305', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/half', 21, 1, '2012-12-15T21:00:54.199389Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T21:56:23.352206Z'),
(393, 'radical', 'blade', '刃', 'You have a sword that has a drop of something on it. That drop is a drop of blood. Sure, a sword will cut someone to spill some blood, but all you really need is any kind of <radical>blade</radical> to cut someone. Sword, knife, whatever, as long as it''s a blade.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/393', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/blade', 21, 1, '2013-07-02T22:05:24.114545Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:18:22.640804Z'),
(241, 'radical', 'saw', '巩', 'You''re constructing a table. On the table is a drop of blood, which is your blood. This is from cutting yourself on the <radical>saw</radical> that you''re using to make the table. Imagine yourself cutting yourself with the saw by accident. Ouch!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/241', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/saw', 22, 1, '2012-09-18T23:32:40.657029Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.319774Z'),
(279, 'radical', 'peace', '平', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>peace</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/279', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/peace', 22, 1, '2012-12-04T05:51:49.594004Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T07:13:50.388752Z'),
(282, 'radical', 'bathtub', '呂', 'See the dude sitting inside the box? His head is sticking out the top. That''s a <radical>bathtub</radical> he''s sitting in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/282', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/bathtub', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:00:59.572498Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.628726Z'),
(283, 'radical', 'cheap', '安', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>cheap</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/283', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/cheap', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:01:23.63226Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.629422Z'),
(284, 'radical', 'dollar', '弗', 'This is a squiggle with two lines through it. Hmmm doesn''t that seem familiar? It should, because it''s the <radical>dollar</radical> symbol: $. Well, close enough. Just look at this and think, ah yes, a totally normal American dollar.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/284', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/dollar', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:05:55.666694Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.631246Z'),
(285, 'radical', 'allocate', '充', 'You have some legs and you need to put them in the trash. But you''ll get caught if you put them in one trash can. Quick, cut them up and <radical>allocate</radical> them to different trash cans around the city. Then you''ll never get caught.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/285', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/allocate', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:06:25.895515Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:30:36.495728Z'),
(286, 'radical', 'football', '爰', 'Your friend is on the ground and you step on him with your cleat. There is no friendship here. This is a true sport you''re playing. This is <radical>football</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/286', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/football', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:09:12.054586Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.648131Z'),
(287, 'radical', 'ability', '能', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>ability</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/287', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/ability', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:09:35.268569Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T05:58:29.379374Z'),
(320, 'radical', 'clothes', '衣', 'You just have to know what’s under these locked lids, so you kick them until they break open to reveal a whole bunch of… <radical>clothes</radical>. “Wow, free clothes!” you think, just as airport security arrive to take you away.

The clothes radical also looks a little like clothes hanging on a clothes hanger, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/320', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/clothes', 22, 1, '2013-01-24T10:46:36.898722Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:30:52.824393Z'),
(8826, 'radical', 'icicle', '丬', 'This ice is in the shape of a stick. It''s an <radical>icicle</radical>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8826', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/icicle', 22, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:18.007151Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.843312Z'),
(288, 'radical', 'leather', '革', 'When I look at this radical, I see the body and (mainly) head of a bull. What are bulls made of? They''re made of <radical>leather</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/288', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/leather', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:18:20.561797Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:11:43.434481Z'),
(289, 'radical', 'vines', '丩', 'This radical is made up of two <radical>vines</radical> hanging down and a branch linking them together. Imagine swinging on these Tarzan style. AaaaAA aAA AAAaaaa!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/289', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/vines', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:19:19.571338Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:41:24.783434Z'),
(290, 'radical', 'restaurant', '亭', 'You see a lid on the street with a drawing of a forehead and a mouth on it. Hmm strange. You pick the lid up and reveal... a secret, underground <radical>restaurant</radical>! The mouth + forehead combo was secret street restaurant code for, "There''s good eats down in this here restaurant hole!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/290', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/restaurant', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:19:35.836156Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.700769Z'),
(291, 'radical', 'road', '道', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>road</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/291', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/road', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:19:44.640557Z', NULL, '2025-11-30T19:27:58.20826Z'),
(292, 'radical', 'demon', '鬼', 'This is a <radical>demon</radical>. Can you see him? He has a crazy face and some tentacles holding something demonic. Try to figure out how to see a demon. But don''t see him too good, cause then he''ll come out of your computer and eat you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/292', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/demon', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:20:12.404561Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.712959Z'),
(8789, 'radical', 'corn-8789', NULL, 'It has a leaf and it''s connected to something that has lots of little sections. This looks just like a short, stubby thing of <radical>corn</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8789', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/corn-8789', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:20:17.818054Z', '2018-12-05T18:38:51.134238Z', '2025-10-01T06:10:31.598665Z'),
(8793, 'radical', 'morning', '𠦝', 'This is the left side of the kanji 朝, so we''ll call it <radical>morning</radical> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8793', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/morning', 23, 1, '2013-01-24T10:47:03.732544Z', NULL, '2026-01-06T00:31:19.090161Z'),
(293, 'radical', 'dawn', '旦', 'The sun is coming up out of the ground, making this radical <radical>dawn</radical>. Of course, don''t confuse this with dusk! You can do that by thinking that the sun is an optimistic and bright thing, so it tends to come up, not go down. (Also, Japan is the Land of the Rising Sun. I mean come on!)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/293', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/dawn', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:39:26.870988Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.717261Z'),
(294, 'radical', 'korea', '韋', 'There are five mouths eating cow at <radical>Korea</radical>n BBQ. Korea is famous for their delicious BBQ, and you need a lot of people to eat all that beef. At least five people. (Get it? Five people!) That''s why this radical is Korea.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/294', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/korea', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:39:51.308406Z', NULL, '2026-01-06T00:31:19.090161Z'),
(295, 'radical', 'guest', '客', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>guest</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/295', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/guest', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:40:04.94803Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.743542Z'),
(296, 'radical', 'control', '制', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>control</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/296', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/control', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:40:27.01035Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.747608Z'),
(297, 'radical', 'nature', '然', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>nature</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/297', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/nature', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:40:27.577807Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.750091Z'),
(298, 'radical', 'accept', '受', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>accept</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/298', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/accept', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:40:35.992288Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.752207Z'),
(8790, 'radical', 'death-star', NULL, 'There is a hat covering the moon and it''s holding a knife. Wait, that''s no moon. It''s a space station! It''s the <radical>Death Star</radical>! It''s pretending to be a moon under that hat. And it''s armed! Don''t let the Death Star stab your planet to death.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8790', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/death-star', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:38:53.691038Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.59953Z'),
(300, 'radical', 'wedding', '甫', 'This radical looks like someone wearing a giant dress who has a tear in their eye. This is a person at their <radical>wedding</radical>. That explains the big dress and the tear (of happiness!) in their eye.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/300', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/wedding', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T07:50:12.555327Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.298195Z'),
(301, 'radical', 'friend', '友', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>friend</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/301', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/friend', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T07:50:20.049741Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.310398Z'),
(302, 'radical', 'mix', '交', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>mix</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/302', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/mix', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T07:50:29.249345Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.334468Z'),
(303, 'radical', 'lifeguard', '冓', 'You have a task to save two kinds of lives. Who saves two kinds of lives? A <radical>lifeguard</radical>. They save the lives of people drowning, and then the lives of the people who go to try to help them. Picture a lifeguard saving the lives of two people at once. What a hero!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/303', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/lifeguard', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T07:51:18.211066Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.344871Z'),
(304, 'radical', 'both', '両', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>both</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/304', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/both', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T07:51:39.636469Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.356913Z'),
(356, 'radical', 'greenhouse', '莫', 'Flowers need the sun to get big. To help them grow as big as possible, we put them in the <radical>greenhouse</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/356', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/greenhouse', 25, 1, '2013-04-16T18:07:57.993532Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.366071Z'),
(425, 'radical', 'mantis', '禹', 'This radical has slide, head, and insect in it. Which insect will slide your head off in a matter of seconds and then eat it? The fearsome praying <radical>mantis</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/425', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/mantis', 25, 1, '2015-06-26T22:28:25.826337Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.382029Z'),
(306, 'radical', 'announce', '告', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>announce</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/306', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/announce', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:21:40.40917Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.810505Z'),
(307, 'radical', 'ten-thousand', '万', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>ten thousand</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/307', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/ten-thousand', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:23:14.368751Z', NULL, '2025-10-31T14:36:38.951201Z'),
(8791, 'radical', 'egg-8791', NULL, 'The letter "E" is for "<radical>egg</radical>" - easy enough, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8791', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/egg-8791', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:23:32.02886Z', '2018-12-05T18:38:51.171744Z', '2025-10-01T06:10:31.601826Z'),
(8792, 'radical', 'comb', NULL, 'Look at all those little things sticking out. You could easily use it to <radical>comb</radical> your hair.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8792', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/comb', 26, 1, '2013-01-24T02:47:29.902273Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.602341Z'),
(8827, 'radical', 'warehouse', '倉', 'You put your hat on the ground and a flag in your mouth. You''re letting the world know that they can use your mouth as a <radical>warehouse</radical>. Your gullet is open for storage business. Need to store some ice cream? Please use my mouth warehouse. ;D ;D ;D', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8827', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/warehouse', 26, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:20.879137Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.854558Z'),
(8828, 'radical', 'chapter', '章', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>chapter</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8828', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/chapter', 26, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:22.53682Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.856655Z'),
(9390, 'radical', 'droopy', '垂', 'This is the <radical>droopy</radical> radical because it looks like a droopy ice cream cake on a stand. Melty goodness droops over the edge of each layer, and the whole top section is drooping toward the left like it''s about to give in to gravity. Imagine how melted it must be for it to be so droopy… better eat it quick.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9390', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/droopy', 26, 1, '2025-12-21T20:39:29.170235Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:11:22.551299Z'),
(308, 'radical', 'farming', '農', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>farming</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/308', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/farming', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T02:47:49.281838Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.838457Z'),
(309, 'radical', 'rake', '而', 'If you look at it, you can see it''s a weird looking <radical>rake</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/309', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/rake', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T02:47:58.108071Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.836778Z'),
(310, 'radical', 'substitute', '代', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>substitute</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/310', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/substitute', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T02:48:06.233817Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:07:16.01728Z'),
(311, 'radical', 'change', '化', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>change</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/311', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/change', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T02:48:14.93167Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.841461Z'),
(312, 'radical', 'peoples', '民', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>peoples</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/312', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/peoples', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T02:48:20.974433Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.854267Z'),
(313, 'radical', 'surplus', '余', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>surplus</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/313', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/surplus', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T02:48:29.798745Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.861312Z'),
(331, 'radical', 'call', '召', 'If you have a sword in your mouth you need to <radical>call</radical> upon someone to help! That thing should not be in there. Call someone over right now! Go ahead, call them! Let’s hope they can get that sword out of your mouth. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/331', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/call', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T21:04:25.28899Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.010062Z'),
(332, 'radical', 'exit', '出', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>exit</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/332', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/exit', 27, 1, '2013-03-11T17:08:11.603187Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:50:46.147403Z'),
(314, 'radical', 'giant', '巨', 'If you were to look at a <radical>giant</radical> from above, this is what you would see. Do you see the head and two arms? The giant is facing towards the right.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/314', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/giant', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:04:47.297062Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.876358Z'),
(315, 'radical', 'barracks', '屯', 'You stick an umbrella with a big slide on it into the mountainside to make your <radical>barracks</radical>. You''re going to use these barracks to teach soldiers how to slide slides and hide from bad weather. It''s ingenious!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/315', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/barracks', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:04:53.415868Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:30:39.384701Z'),
(316, 'radical', 'hercules-316', '絜', 'The life thread is cut with a sword by the Moirai. They are, in Greek mythology, the ones who decide when to let you die, by cutting your life thread. This is probably most well known in the story of <radical>Hercules</radical>, so we''ll go with that with this radical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/316', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/hercules-316', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:05:00.353045Z', '2018-12-05T18:38:51.3114Z', '2025-10-01T06:09:48.896848Z'),
(317, 'radical', 'specialty', '専', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>specialty</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/317', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/specialty', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:05:08.679577Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.9061Z'),
(318, 'radical', 'lovely', '麗', 'I don''t know what this thing is, but it''s <radical>lovely</radical>. It has two eyes, a funny mouth, and a scraggly beard. It''s lovely, and nothing else, okay? So remember it that way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/318', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/lovely', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:28:06.505211Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.928586Z'),
(326, 'radical', 'odd', '奇', 'A big lip ring is a pretty <radical>odd</radical> thing to have. If it''s big, it''ll stretch your lip out, which is odd too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/326', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/odd', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T19:09:58.086807Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:21:57.094904Z'),
(8908, 'radical', 'before', '前', 'The meaning of this radical is the same as the alternative kanji meaning. It means <radical>before</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8908', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/before', 28, 1, '2020-06-16T14:18:55.378339Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:32.331314Z'),
(319, 'radical', 'charcoal', '尞', 'When the sun gets small, you need to heat up by throwing some big horns in the fire. This turns into charcoal. Nice, toasty <radical>charcoal</radical> that keeps you warm when the sun gets small.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/319', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/charcoal', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T10:46:01.530982Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.930567Z'),
(321, 'radical', 'blame', '責', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>blame</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/321', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/blame', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T10:46:43.105761Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.939548Z'),
(322, 'radical', 'district', '区', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>district</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/322', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/district', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T10:46:48.28828Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:14:53.54038Z'),
(323, 'radical', 'servant', '臣', 'Hey, it''s that giant you learned before. But... but its arms are chained. They''re chained because you caught the giant and now it''s your <radical>servant</radical>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/323', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/servant', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T10:47:10.37235Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.944569Z'),
(324, 'radical', 'nose', '乙', 'If you look at this radical right, you''ll see a <radical>NOSE</radical> (with a squinty eye on the left).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/324', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/nose', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T10:47:21.634263Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:48.959079Z'),
(325, 'radical', 'above', '上', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>above</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/325', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/above', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:07:10.044359Z', NULL, '2026-01-01T17:02:29.820649Z'),
(403, 'radical', 'mysterious', '玄', 'What''s under this lid that smells like poop? I don''t know! How <radical>mysterious</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/403', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/mysterious', 29, 1, '2013-08-16T22:23:09.979514Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T18:07:32.224525Z'),
(162, 'radical', 'one-sided', '片', 'This looks a bit like a waiter walking toward you with a platter in their hands... but wait. It''s only half of a waiter. It''s a <radical>one sided</radical> waiter!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/162', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/one-sided', 30, 1, '2012-03-22T01:51:12Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:11:10.895336Z'),
(328, 'radical', 'oversee', '監', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>oversee</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/328', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/oversee', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:04:06.906471Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.001323Z'),
(329, 'radical', 'lineage', '系', 'A thread goes through the ground. It traces your <radical>lineage</radical>. You follow the thread all the way back through many generations, to find out your lineage involves Genghis Khan. Feel the thread and the dirt in your hand and actually trace the lineage.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/329', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/lineage', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:04:13.092786Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T01:19:34.28962Z'),
(330, 'radical', 'turtle-shell', '甲', 'Poor turtles. We don''t like them here. This is a <radical>turtle shell</radical>. See the shell with just a tail remaining? It''s the only thing we didn''t remove off the turtle. It''s just a shell with a tail, so basically it''s just a turtle shell.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/330', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/turtle-shell', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:04:18.860475Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.008413Z'),
(391, 'radical', 'pirate', '冘', 'This kind of looks like big (大), which I think you''ll remember you learned by pretending this was a big guy. This big guy, however, has a funny leg... a peg leg. That makes him a <radical>pirate</radical>. Also, his arms are in the "YARRRR" position.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/391', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/pirate', 30, 1, '2013-07-02T21:24:45.456309Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.445146Z'),
(8771, 'radical', 'hills', NULL, 'See the rolling <radical>hills</radical> with the road going off into the background to another hill? That''s why this is the hills radical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8771', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/hills', 30, 1, '2012-03-22T01:52:19Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.437627Z'),
(333, 'radical', 'fang', '牙', 'Imagine this in your mouth. It''s one tooth that sticks out more than any other. It''s your <radical>fang</radical>. Can you see this sticking out of a wolf mouth instead? Try that if you can''t picture yourself with a fang this cool.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/333', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/fang', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T19:41:19.632848Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.018013Z'),
(334, 'radical', 'line-up', '並', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>line up</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/334', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/line-up', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T19:41:27.842672Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T09:51:44.569062Z'),
(336, 'radical', 'bully', '鬲', 'He''s a creeper and he''s Four-T (forty). If someone like this creeps on you, they''re just a <radical>bully</radical>. When you get to forty you aren''t supposed to creep on people anymore. You bully. You jerk.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/336', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/bully', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T19:42:13.189436Z', NULL, '2026-02-19T14:31:49.726046Z'),
(337, 'radical', 'rocket', '离', 'See how there’s a lid on top of the treasure chest? That’s because the whole thing is a <radical>rocket</radical> which contains all the necessary items (treasures) to live in outer space. The bottom part of the radical shows you that this is a top secret project, though, so keep this private in your own head.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/337', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/rocket', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T19:42:56.312161Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.051447Z'),
(338, 'radical', 'library', '扁', 'There''s a door over the bookcase. That makes this a <radical>library</radical>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/338', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/library', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T19:43:14.867804Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T07:14:39.053338Z'),
(8829, 'radical', 'guy', '郎', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>guy</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8829', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/guy', 31, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:26.296576Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.858096Z'),
(340, 'radical', 'cat-pirate', '卬', 'See the CP in this radical? It stands for <radical>Cat Pirate</radical>. Meoaarrrr.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/340', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/cat-pirate', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T19:49:28.836807Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.0926Z'),
(341, 'radical', 'same', '同', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>same</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/341', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/same', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T19:49:36.530493Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.091994Z'),
(343, 'radical', 'certain', '必', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>certain</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/343', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/certain', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T19:49:49.540484Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.103015Z'),
(427, 'radical', 'spider', '夋', 'If you look closely and carefully, sometimes you can see some private legs in winter. Whose legs? <radical>Spider</radical> legs! It''s too cold for them in winter, so they hide in cracks and crevices, but sometimes you can see their little leggies sticking out anyway.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/427', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/spider', 32, 1, '2015-06-26T22:30:24.776861Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:48:42.672332Z'),
(433, 'radical', 'cottage', '舎', 'If you''re wearing a hat and have dirt on your mouth, chances are you''re staying in a <radical>cottage</radical>. That''s because cottages are usually in the countryside, which means you''re likely spending time out in the garden, with a hat on and dirt all over your mouth. You know, your average cottage experience. And hey, this radical even kind of looks like a cottage.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/433', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/cottage', 32, 1, '2015-06-26T22:49:03.904796Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:50:00.772162Z'),
(8799, 'radical', 'elf', NULL, 'You see a leaf ear, better known as an <radical>elf</radical>! Elves are just humans who have ears that are pointy like leaves.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8799', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/elf', 32, 1, '2015-09-02T19:04:16.046203Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.667843Z'),
(344, 'radical', 'interval', '間', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>interval</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/344', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/interval', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T16:52:51.616756Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.127561Z'),
(345, 'radical', 'receive', '享', 'Put a lid on the mouth of a child and what will you <radical>receive</radical>? At best, a very unimpressed look from that poor child. If you want to receive nice things, putting lids on children’s mouths isn’t the way to go about it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/345', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/receive', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T16:56:46.624811Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.131308Z'),
(346, 'radical', 'inside', '内', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>inside</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/346', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/inside', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T16:59:37.192751Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.131908Z'),
(347, 'radical', 'storehouse', '蔵', 'Your flowers become your servants. You plant them, you water them, you bring them up, then you cut them down and put them in your <radical>storehouse</radical>. They do what you want because they become YOUR servants, not the other way around. That''s why you can tell them to sit in the storehouse where they wait until you come to eat them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/347', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/storehouse', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:00:31.968599Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.133395Z'),
(382, 'radical', 'wide', '広', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>wide</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/382', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/wide', 33, 1, '2013-06-24T22:38:44.900488Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.384389Z'),
(428, 'radical', 'boot', '堇', 'There''s something made of leather with some dirt on the bottom of it. It''s a <radical>boot</radical>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/428', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/boot', 33, 1, '2015-06-26T22:31:21.086165Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T21:32:24.066085Z'),
(8794, 'radical', 'coral', '丞', 'In the water is a barb on the ground. It''s <radical>coral</radical>. Coral may look pretty, but if you slip in the water and step on it, you''re gonna get scratched up. That stuff is roooough.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8794', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/coral', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:00:00.854161Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.619731Z'),
(8830, 'radical', 'syrup', '喿', 'The products trees make? That''s <radical>syrup</radical>. Delicious maple syrup, for example. Mmmm!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8830', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/syrup', 33, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:28.094688Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:08:49.298692Z'),
(348, 'radical', 'catapult', '呉', 'See the rock in the <radical>catapult</radical>? It even has little legs to hold it steady so it can catapult better.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/348', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/catapult', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T17:51:34.845527Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.162493Z'),
(349, 'radical', 'member', '員', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>member</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/349', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/member', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T17:51:44.765591Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.161911Z'),
(350, 'radical', 'claw', '爪', 'This looks like a <radical>claw</radical> of some kind already, making it quite easy!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/350', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/claw', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T17:51:56.811369Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.163137Z'),
(351, 'radical', 'slice', '亥', 'See the guy? See how he was CUT IN HALF??? That must have been one heckuva <radical>slice</radical> to do that to a person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/351', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/slice', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T17:52:11.673781Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.1649Z'),
(358, 'radical', 'valuable', '貴', 'There''s a shellfish, but it''s got some kind of fancy headgear. Is that… a crown, perhaps? It looks really <radical>valuable</radical> — and the shellfish wearing it must be even more valuable to be worthy of such a status symbol!

Alternatively, you can remember this radical with its components: in the middle of the ground, there''s a shellfish. Why? ''Cause it''s super valuable. The safest place to keep a valuable shellfish is buried in the middle of the ground.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/358', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/valuable', 34, 1, '2013-05-03T08:24:49.595452Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T08:00:25.476362Z'),
(352, 'radical', 'belt', '帯', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>belt</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/352', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/belt', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:43:30.410684Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.170114Z'),
(353, 'radical', 'turtle', '亀', 'There''s a turtle shell inside this <radical>turtle</radical> radical. You can tell because there''s a whole head and tail on this thing, making it a real turtle, not just a turtle shell.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/353', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/turtle', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:43:40.536644Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.172312Z'),
(354, 'radical', 'shuriken', '彑', 'See the <radical>shuriken</radical> stuck into the ground? In case you don''t know, a shuriken is just a throwing star. Awesome ninjas use them. That''s what''s in this radical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/354', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/shuriken', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:43:50.714544Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.204162Z'),
(355, 'radical', 'sake', '為', 'This radical looks like a big, weird caterpillar bug thing. But, for the sake of everyone''s sanity, we''ll just call it <radical>sake</radical>, okay? Keep in mind, we mean sake as in "for the sake of X," not as in the alcoholic beverage!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/355', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/sake', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:43:59.981707Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.203008Z'),
(8831, 'radical', 'commander', '将', 'Who wears cleats and takes your measurements for so long that icicles start forming on your body? Your <radical>commander</radical>. The commander is very, very particular. And everyone knows the commander has to get everything exactly right. So taking your measurements takes SO long that icicles grow out of your nose. But that''s okay. You have to impress your new commander, darn it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8831', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/commander', 35, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:31.662652Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.888516Z'),
(359, 'radical', 'departure', '発', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>departure</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/359', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/departure', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:24:59.356467Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.236553Z'),
(360, 'radical', 'sweet', '甘', 'This radical is the sun with its hair sticking out. Why is the sun''s hair sticking out like this? It ate wayyy too many <radical>sweet</radical> things causing it to have too much energy, making its hair stand on end. It also kind of looks like a sweet, sweet candy with the wrapper sticking out. Remember it how you''d like.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/360', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/sweet', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:25:08.12647Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.23794Z'),
(429, 'radical', 'dance', '舛', 'You see a cow in the evening. You get closer and realize it''s doing a <radical>dance</radical>. All cows dance in the evening. Can you picture it? The cow is dancing, so majestic, in the setting sunlight.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/429', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/dance', 36, 1, '2015-06-26T22:32:05.073872Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.811702Z'),
(363, 'radical', 'omen', '兆', 'There''s an explosion around your legs. It was just as the fortune teller warned. This is the <radical>omen</radical> she was talking about. Think about what happens after the omen hits. What horrible fortune teller magic will happen???', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/363', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/omen', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T16:53:29.205497Z', NULL, '2025-12-07T19:03:34.17888Z'),
(364, 'radical', 'prize', '賞', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>prize</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/364', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/prize', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T16:53:55.566884Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.270568Z'),
(365, 'radical', 'skewer', '串', 'There''s a stick going through some juicy meat. This is a <radical>skewer</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/365', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/skewer', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T16:54:25.209584Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.268955Z'),
(366, 'radical', 'violence', '暴', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>violence</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/366', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/violence', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T16:54:35.198244Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.271483Z'),
(395, 'radical', 'wrap', '包', 'Yourself (oneself) is in prison. Look at the prison as it wraps around you. It wraps you up and holds you tight. That''s why this is the <radical>wrap</radical> radical!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/395', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/wrap', 37, 1, '2013-07-02T22:06:01.105226Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T06:56:25.799583Z'),
(367, 'radical', 'hot-pepper', '辟', 'You see something with a flag on it and put it in your mouth. It was spicy! You look at the little flag and it says... <radical>hot pepper</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/367', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/hot-pepper', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:02:01.316802Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.273217Z'),
(368, 'radical', 'think', '思', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>think</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/368', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/think', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:02:09.511624Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:51:52.975966Z'),
(369, 'radical', 'prefecture', '県', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>prefecture</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/369', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/prefecture', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:02:14.340114Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.299865Z'),
(371, 'radical', 'have', '有', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>have</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/371', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/have', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:02:54.182842Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.308081Z'),
(372, 'radical', 'tooth', '歯', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>tooth</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/372', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/tooth', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:09:23.841347Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.311222Z'),
(370, 'radical', 'sky', '空', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>sky</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/370', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/sky', 39, 1, '2013-05-03T18:02:48.597857Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.306515Z'),
(373, 'radical', 'buddy', '君', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>buddy</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/373', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/buddy', 39, 1, '2013-06-04T17:41:26.145741Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.319043Z'),
(374, 'radical', 'former', '旧', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>former</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/374', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/former', 39, 1, '2013-06-04T17:41:36.424275Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.334851Z'),
(375, 'radical', 'six-375', '六', 'This is the same as the kanji <radical>Six</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/375', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/six-375', 39, 1, '2013-06-04T17:41:45.965012Z', '2018-04-18T21:23:37.235864Z', '2025-10-01T06:09:49.33782Z'),
(376, 'radical', 'hill', '岡', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>hill</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/376', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/hill', 39, 1, '2013-06-04T17:41:55.50173Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.335492Z'),
(377, 'radical', 'three', '三', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>three</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/377', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/three', 39, 1, '2013-06-04T17:53:42.983816Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:46:12.340974Z'),
(413, 'radical', 'reality', '真', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>reality</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/413', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/reality', 39, 1, '2013-08-22T22:18:33.731237Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:29:49.214205Z'),
(414, 'radical', 'scroll', '巻', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>scroll</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/414', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/scroll', 39, 1, '2013-10-28T23:26:47.2838Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.687298Z'),
(430, 'radical', 'nurse', '㐮', 'This radical is made up of blackjack and clothes with the six kanji on top. If you see six people playing blackjack and wearing the same clothes you should know that they''re all <radical>nurse</radical>s. Nurses play blackjack together to cool down between shifts and they usually don''t have time to change out of their uniforms. That''s why this radical is nurse.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/430', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/nurse', 39, 1, '2015-06-26T22:32:45.067733Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.810951Z'),
(378, 'radical', 'employ', '雇', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>employ</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/378', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/employ', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:48:47.519255Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.357063Z'),
(379, 'radical', 'every', '毎', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>every</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/379', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/every', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:48:54.3702Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.362396Z'),
(380, 'radical', 'preserve', '保', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>preserve</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/380', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/preserve', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:49:40.424762Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.374126Z'),
(8833, 'radical', 'rejoice', '喜', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>rejoice</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8833', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/rejoice', 40, 1, '2019-04-27T00:23:14.02593Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.897791Z'),
(8907, 'radical', 'wheat', '麦', 'This radical is the same as the kanji, but you learned that a really long time ago, so here''s a reminder of the mnemonic, just in case:

What gives you life during the winter? It''s the <radical>wheat</radical> you store away.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8907', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/wheat', 40, 1, '2020-06-16T14:17:41.034019Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:25:53.691216Z'),
(335, 'radical', 'showy', '華', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>showy</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/335', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/showy', 41, 1, '2013-03-12T19:41:35.985378Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.044188Z'),
(381, 'radical', 'signpost', '夆', 'This is a post with signs on it. And on top is a winter marker, showing that if you go north it''s winter up there. The signs say things like "Narnia ↑" and "Meridell ←" and "Bananaland →" so you know it''s a <radical>signpost</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/381', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/signpost', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:15:19.030259Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.379611Z'),
(8795, 'radical', 'trident-8795', NULL, 'The <radical>trident</radical> goes up, sticking through a fish (or whatever). AQUAAMANNNN!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8795', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/trident-8795', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:12:59.534333Z', '2018-12-05T18:38:51.38373Z', '2025-10-01T06:10:31.632902Z'),
(8796, 'radical', 'cactus', NULL, 'See the <radical>cactus</radical> coming out of the ground? It''s happy to see you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8796', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/cactus', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:17:15.513119Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.636378Z'),
(8856, 'radical', 'wife', '妻', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>wife</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8856', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/wife', 41, 1, '2019-04-29T23:04:28.431597Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:32.061884Z'),
(8906, 'radical', 'proclaim', '宣', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>proclaim</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8906', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/proclaim', 41, 1, '2020-06-16T14:09:44.351398Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:08:32.26304Z'),
(384, 'radical', 'melon', '瓜', 'This is a <radical>melon</radical>. Imagine a melon cut in half, and you''re looking at its insides from the side. See those precious private melon parts? You''re gonna keep those for yourself, because melons are delicious and not for sharing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/384', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/melon', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T18:52:53.358926Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:48:00.080361Z'),
(385, 'radical', 'bone', '骨', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>bone</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/385', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/bone', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T18:53:25.222685Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T16:54:57.462607Z'),
(386, 'radical', 'grenade-386', '臼', 'This is the shell of a <radical>grenade</radical> that has blown up. BOOM! It exploded, splitting it down the middle.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/386', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/grenade-386', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T18:53:56.840919Z', '2018-12-05T18:38:51.421926Z', '2025-10-01T06:09:49.430367Z'),
(387, 'radical', 'shop', '屋', 'We already have a roof radical, so we''re going to use the secondary kanji meaning for this radical. It''s <radical>shop</radical>, remember?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/387', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/shop', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:04:57.588418Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.429778Z'),
(8798, 'radical', 'satellite', NULL, 'Look at this radical. Can you see it floating in space? Can you see the antennae and reflectors? That''s because it''s a <radical>satellite</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8798', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/satellite', 42, 1, '2015-08-25T20:43:18.432269Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:37:07.832071Z'),
(388, 'radical', 'plow', '耒', 'You''re sliding some dirt around with a tree you cut down. This contraption you''ve made is called a <radical>plow</radical>. Use it to push dirt around!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/388', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/plow', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:24:25.656782Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:07:26.086065Z'),
(389, 'radical', 'imperial', '龍', 'Stand up on the moon so you can look down on your countries. The thing with all the lines and borders are the borders of all the countries you rule, for you are an <radical>imperial</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/389', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/imperial', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:24:33.180594Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.435661Z'),
(412, 'radical', 'badger', '豸', 'You know the animal radical. This is just like that with a little extra. It''s an animal already... which means all you have to do is remember what kind of animal. In this case, it''s a <radical>badger</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/412', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/badger', 43, 1, '2013-08-22T22:14:08.8792Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.684006Z'),
(394, 'radical', 'dragon', '竜', 'A turtle that stands up isn''t a turtle anymore. It''s evolved into a monster. What kind of monster? A <radical>dragon</radical>! Rawwwr.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/394', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/dragon', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:05:29.148028Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.503291Z'),
(416, 'radical', 'wind', '風', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>wind</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/416', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/wind', 44, 1, '2014-05-01T19:15:28.035798Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.690963Z'),
(396, 'radical', 'protect', '守', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>protect</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/396', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/protect', 45, 1, '2013-07-02T22:45:17.481167Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.530288Z'),
(397, 'radical', 'renew', '更', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>renew</radical>.
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/397', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/renew', 45, 1, '2013-07-02T22:45:27.638242Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.531521Z'),
(400, 'radical', 'favor', '恵', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>favor</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/400', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/favor', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T21:22:08.947111Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.566137Z'),
(401, 'radical', 'task-401', '務', 'This is the same as the kanji <radical>Task</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/401', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/task-401', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T17:56:20.515716Z', '2018-12-05T18:38:51.451949Z', '2025-10-01T06:09:49.608691Z'),
(402, 'radical', 'wave', '波', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>wave</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/402', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/wave', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T17:56:37.29018Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.612175Z'),
(431, 'radical', 'penguin', '敝', 'There are fins wrapped in a towel in winter. Who is drying these fish fins to eat them? A <radical>penguin</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/431', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/penguin', 47, 1, '2015-06-26T22:33:38.731228Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.813135Z'),
(404, 'radical', 'form', '容', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>form</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/404', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/form', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T22:23:17.43067Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.626424Z'),
(405, 'radical', 'light', '光', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>light</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/405', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/light', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T22:23:23.742932Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.625495Z'),
(406, 'radical', 'times', '回', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>times</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/406', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/times', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T16:40:21.586474Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.630794Z'),
(407, 'radical', 'crab-trap', '甚', 'This radical looks like a small animal (匹) has a crab (其) sticking out of it. Often when a crab is eating a small animal, that small animal was put inside a crab trap to catch the crab, luring it in with the small animal. That''s why this radical is <radical>crab trap</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/407', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/crab-trap', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T16:40:34.145777Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.648406Z'),
(8857, 'radical', 'wild', '荒', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>wild</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8857', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/wild', 49, 1, '2019-04-29T23:06:59.716459Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:32.053053Z'),
(409, 'radical', 'name', '名', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>name</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/409', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/name', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:12:06.02961Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.652517Z'),
(410, 'radical', 'splinter', '禺', 'Although a little bit modified, there is a turtle shell that''s on a mustache with this radical. The only thing with a mustache that hangs around turtles is Splinter, the giant rat from Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles. So this radical is <radical>Splinter</radical> (the giant rat).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/410', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/splinter', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:12:36.272207Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.657193Z'),
(8797, 'radical', 'psychopath', NULL, 'Inside this guy''s forehead you have a spoon and a bunch of hair, not to mention whatever that is in the top left. That''s the mind of a <radical>psychopath</radical> right there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8797', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/psychopath', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:12:07.160399Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.659966Z'),
(432, 'radical', 'stomach', '胃', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>stomach</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/432', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/stomach', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T22:34:07.023321Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:43:44.698085Z'),
(426, 'radical', 'frostbite', '夌', 'This radical is made of dirt, legs, and winter. If you have to bury your legs in the dirt in winter, it''s probably because you got <radical>frostbite</radical>. You had to leave your dead legs behind. :(', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/426', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/frostbite', 52, 1, '2015-06-26T22:29:42.50631Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.203162Z'),
(438, 'radical', 'tall', '高', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>tall</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/438', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/tall', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T18:53:36.452581Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.855392Z'),
(423, 'radical', 'feeling', '感', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>feeling</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/423', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/feeling', 55, 1, '2015-06-26T22:26:53.834898Z', NULL, '2025-12-21T21:56:38.922233Z'),
(8832, 'radical', 'doubt', '疑', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>doubt</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8832', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/doubt', 55, 1, '2018-12-05T18:39:35.338572Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:31.89204Z'),
(434, 'radical', 'convex', '凸', 'This looks like a box with something sticking out of it. When something is sticking out of something else it''s <radical>convex</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/434', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/convex', 57, 1, '2015-06-26T22:50:03.245441Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.824585Z'),
(435, 'radical', 'concave', '凹', 'It looks like there is a dent in this box. When there is a hole or a dent in something it''s called <radical>concave</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/435', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/concave', 57, 1, '2015-06-26T22:50:41.801492Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.825953Z'),
(436, 'radical', 'below', '下', 'This radical is the same as the kanji. It means <radical>below</radical>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/436', 'https://www.wanikani.com/radicals/below', 59, 1, '2015-06-26T22:51:40.892779Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.84805Z'),
(440, 'kanji', '一', '一', 'Lying on the <radical>ground</radical> is something that looks just like the ground, the number <kanji>One</kanji>. Why is this One lying down? It''s been shot by the number two. It''s lying there, bleeding out and dying. The number One doesn''t have long to live.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/440', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%80', 1, 2, '2012-02-27T19:55:19Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:25:15.521258Z'),
(441, 'kanji', '二', '二', 'The kanji <kanji>two</kanji> and the radical <radical>two</radical> are the exact same things. Therefore, you know the kanji for two already!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/441', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%8C', 1, 2, '2012-02-27T20:15:43Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T08:22:08.397381Z'),
(442, 'kanji', '九', '九', 'Nice! This kanji is the same as the radical that looks just like it (down to the meaning, even!). The radical is <radical>nine</radical> and the kanji is <kanji>nine</kanji>, making the meaning of this kanji really easy to remember as long as you know the radical first.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/442', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B9%9D', 1, 2, '2012-02-27T22:33:25Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:40:37.943177Z'),
(443, 'kanji', '七', '七', 'Lucky for you (as is the case with many of the lower-stroke kanji), this kanji is the same as the radical you''ve already learned. The radical that makes up this kanji is <radical>seven</radical> and the kanji (which is exactly the same) is also <kanji>seven</kanji>. So, if you know the radical, you know the meaning of the kanji as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/443', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%83', 1, 2, '2012-02-27T22:38:57Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.890852Z'),
(444, 'kanji', '人', '人', 'Remember how the radical version of this kanji meant <radical>person</radical> because it looks like a person with their arms down and their legs spread out? Well this is a <kanji>person</kanji> too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/444', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%BA', 1, 2, '2012-02-27T22:41:23Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:02:16.543235Z'),
(445, 'kanji', '入', '入', 'The kanji and the radical are the same, so remembering the meaning of this kanji is as simple as making sure you know the radical for <radical>enter</radical> first! If you know the radical, you know this kanji means <kanji>enter</kanji> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/445', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%A5', 1, 2, '2012-02-27T22:43:03Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T07:13:52.985142Z'),
(446, 'kanji', '八', '八', 'This isn''t exactly the same as the <radical>fins</radical> radical, but it''s close. Sometimes this will happen (where we have to use a slightly "modified radical" version of the original), so it''s good to understand that now, early on. In this case, we have fins. How many fins? <kanji>Eight</kanji> fins. How do you know that? Because you caught four fish with two fins each.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/446', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%AB', 1, 2, '2012-02-27T22:47:16Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.916435Z'),
(447, 'kanji', '力', '力', 'The radical and the meaning of this kanji are the same. Both are <kanji>power</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/447', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8A%9B', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T01:32:14Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:39:26.649362Z'),
(448, 'kanji', '十', '十', 'This <radical>cross</radical> is the symbol for the number <kanji>ten</kanji>. A good way to remember this is to think about all the religions that have crosses and also important sets of tens. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/448', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%81', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T01:36:02Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:21:22.98142Z'),
(449, 'kanji', '三', '三', 'This kanji is made up of the ground radical (which also happens to be the kanji for "one") and the radical for two. Combine <radical>one</radical> and <radical>two</radical> together and what do you get? <kanji>Three</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/449', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%89', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T01:43:58Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T01:05:39.161541Z'),
(450, 'kanji', '上', '上', 'You find a <radical>toe</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical>. It''s weird, because it''s <kanji>above</kanji> the ground, not where toes belong.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/450', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%8A', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T01:46:31Z', NULL, '2026-02-12T21:23:20.690473Z'),
(451, 'kanji', '下', '下', 'There''s the <radical>ground</radical>, and there''s a <radical>toe</radical> just <kanji>below</kanji> it. Yep, you know this means "below" because the ground has a toe below it in this kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/451', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%8B', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T01:48:12Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:34:08.8016Z'),
(452, 'kanji', '口', '口', 'The <radical>mouth</radical> radical and the <kanji>mouth</kanji> kanji are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/452', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%A3', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T01:52:02Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T07:21:32.024772Z'),
(453, 'kanji', '大', '大', 'The <radical>big</radical> radical and the <kanji>big</kanji> kanji are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/453', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A4%A7', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T01:54:53Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:29:37.223803Z'),
(454, 'kanji', '女', '女', 'The radical for <radical>woman</radical> and the kanji for <kanji>woman</kanji> are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/454', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A5%B3', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T01:56:16Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(455, 'kanji', '山', '山', 'The <radical>mountain</radical> radical and the <kanji>mountain</kanji> kanji are the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/455', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B1%B1', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T01:57:19Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:24:50.424846Z'),
(456, 'kanji', '川', '川', 'The <radical>river</radical> radical and the <kanji>river</kanji> kanji are the same as each other!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/456', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B7%9D', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T01:59:43Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.977375Z'),
(457, 'kanji', '工', '工', 'The <radical>construction</radical> radical and the <kanji>construction</kanji> kanji are the same!

This kanji also means <kanji>industry</kanji>, which is what construction is, if you think about it. It''s all part of the construction industry.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/457', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B7%A5', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T20:45:58Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.982277Z'),
(458, 'kanji', '刀', '刀', 'The radical and the meaning of this kanji are almost the same! The radical is <radical>sword</radical>, but we''re learning Japanese, so can you name a Japanese <kanji>sword</kanji>? That''s right, the most common is a <kanji>katana</kanji>, which happens to be the meaning of this kanji as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/458', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%80', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T21:32:28Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:49.991028Z'),
(459, 'kanji', '土', '土', 'The <radical>dirt</radical> radical and the <kanji>dirt</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/459', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9C%9F', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T23:31:05Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:07:48.907977Z'),
(460, 'kanji', '千', '千', 'This kanji is the same as the radical you learned, so it means <kanji>thousand</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/460', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%83', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T23:34:28Z', NULL, '2026-02-09T01:46:41.543429Z'),
(461, 'kanji', '夕', '夕', 'The <radical>evening</radical> radical and the <kanji>evening</kanji> kanji are the same. If you know the radical you know the meaning of the kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/461', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A4%95', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T23:36:17Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T08:56:35.408019Z'),
(462, 'kanji', '子', '子', 'The <radical>child</radical> radical and the <kanji>child</kanji> kanji are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/462', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AD%90', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T23:38:28Z', NULL, '2026-03-20T15:33:33.561611Z'),
(463, 'kanji', '小', '小', 'The <radical>small</radical> radical and the <kanji>small</kanji> kanji are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/463', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%8F', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T23:40:00Z', NULL, '2025-10-21T16:17:02.815667Z'),
(464, 'kanji', '丁', '丁', 'The kanji and the radical that look like this are exactly the same. They are both <kanji>street</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/464', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%81', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T23:42:48Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T01:07:41.176706Z'),
(467, 'kanji', '丸', '丸', 'What''s something with <radical>nine</radical> sides? Just add a <radical>drop</radical> and you''ll have something that''s almost a <kanji>circle</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/467', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%B8', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T23:53:55Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T15:28:07.146032Z'),
(468, 'kanji', '才', '才', 'On the <radical>ground</radical> you put <radical>barb</radical>s at the bottom of a <radical>slide</radical>. This is your special <kanji>talent</kanji>. When you go down the slide, you fly right past the barbs like some kind of daredevil. Any normal kid would land on the barbs and get hurt, but you have this talent.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/468', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%89%8D', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T23:57:42Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.054441Z'),
(469, 'kanji', '中', '中', 'The radical <reading>middle</reading> and the kanji <kanji>middle</kanji> are both the same. So if you know one, you know the other.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/469', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%AD', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:01:12Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:20:40.00989Z'),
(470, 'kanji', '五', '五', 'The <radical>five</radical> radical and the <kanji>five</kanji> kanji are the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/470', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%94', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:04:18Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:20:15.015515Z'),
(471, 'kanji', '六', '六', 'Just imagine a beast with a <radical>lid</radical> for a head and <radical>fins</radical> for feet, running around looking as insane as possible (really imagine it!). When it runs up to you, it shows you the top of its lid-head. On it is written the numbers 666. Why? Because it''s a spawn of the devil, and the number <kanji>six</kanji> is the devil''s number.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/471', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%AD', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:17:02Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:20:39.363765Z'),
(472, 'kanji', '円', '円', 'Here we have a <radical>head</radical> with a <radical>lid</radical> in it, near the top. Imagine the top of someone''s head being closed by a lid. Open that lid up and peer inside... it''s full of <kanji>yen</kanji> coins, the currency of Japan. Jackpot! And all those yen coins have a nice <kanji>round</kanji> shape, naturally.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/472', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%86%86', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:18:18Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:10:09.414813Z'),
(473, 'kanji', '天', '天', 'The radical <radical>heaven</radical> is the same as the kanji <kanji>heaven</kanji>, making this an easy kanji to memorize.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/473', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A4%A9', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:22:40Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:57:35.099275Z'),
(474, 'kanji', '手', '手', 'The <radical>hand</radical> radical and the <kanji>hand</kanji> kanji are identical in looks and meaning! That should make things easy!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/474', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%89%8B', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:26:40Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.096272Z'),
(475, 'kanji', '文', '文', 'The kanji and the radical are the same, so remembering the meaning of this kanji is as simple as making sure you know the radical for <radical>writing</radical> first! If you know the radical, you know this kanji means <kanji>writing</kanji> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/475', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%96%87', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:29:24Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T07:09:11.444332Z'),
(476, 'kanji', '日', '日', 'The <radical>sun</radical> radical and <kanji>sun</kanji> kanji are the same, huzzah! Also, if you want to remember <kanji>day</kanji> as well, you can just think about how a day is the time it takes for the sun to cross the sky.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/476', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%97%A5', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:32:19Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T16:54:31.280083Z'),
(477, 'kanji', '月', '月', 'The <kanji>moon</kanji> kanji is made up of the <radical>moon</radical> radical. This kanji is also used to mean <kanji>month</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/477', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%88', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:34:02Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:23:52.511304Z'),
(478, 'kanji', '木', '木', 'This is another kanji that is the same as the radical. Both <radical>tree</radical> and <kanji>tree</kanji> are the same in looks and meaning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/478', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%A8', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:34:36Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T13:28:43.07733Z'),
(479, 'kanji', '水', '水', 'The <radical>water</radical> radical and the <kanji>water</kanji> kanji are the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/479', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B0%B4', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:36:53Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:57:41.677485Z'),
(480, 'kanji', '火', '火', 'The <radical>fire</radical> radical and the <kanji>fire</kanji> kanji are the same as each other.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/480', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%81%AB', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:38:57Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:14:10.051635Z'),
(481, 'kanji', '犬', '犬', 'The <radical>dog</radical> radical and <kanji>dog</kanji> kanji are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/481', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8A%AC', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:42:35Z', NULL, '2025-12-21T20:37:07.691155Z'),
(482, 'kanji', '王', '王', 'The <radical>king</radical> radical and the <kanji>king</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/482', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8E%8B', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:44:29Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T02:11:43.486542Z'),
(483, 'kanji', '出', '出', 'There are two <radical>mountains</radical> in front of you... and probably a bunch more around you too. You are in a pass, and don''t know how to get out. You are frantically looking for the <kanji>exit</kanji> before you freeze to death. The most important thing is to find the exit from this mountain range - your life depends on it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/483', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%87%BA', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:45:28Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:40:58.052424Z'),
(484, 'kanji', '右', '右', '<radical>Narwhal</radical> <radical>mouth</radical>s are on the <kanji>right</kanji> side of their head. In fact, some people didn''t think they had mouths at all because they weren''t looking from the right. Rookie mistake.

See how the "mouth" radical is to the right of the "narwhal" radical? That should help you remember that this means <kanji>right</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/484', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%B3', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:50:30Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.162323Z'),
(485, 'kanji', '四', '四', 'Imagine contorting yourself in such a way that your <radical>mouth</radical> now has your <radical>legs</radical> inside it. You''ve bent your body in all the wrong ways to achieve this. In fact, if you look at yourself, you look like a square now... You''re just a person with <kanji>four</kanji> sides.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/485', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9B%9B', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:53:29Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:23:32.143331Z'),
(486, 'kanji', '左', '左', '<radical>Narwhal</radical>s do <radical>construction</radical> with their <kanji>left</kanji> hands (or flippers, I guess). That''s because all narwhals are left-handed!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/486', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B7%A6', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:57:18Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.1736Z'),
(487, 'kanji', '本', '本', 'The kanji and the radical are the same, so remembering the meaning of this kanji is as simple as making sure you know the radical for <radical>book</radical> first! If you know the radical, you know this kanji means <kanji>book</kanji> as well.

Take note that the second, third, and fourth meanings of this kanji are <kanji>origin</kanji>, <kanji>real</kanji>, and <kanji>main</kanji>. You can figure this out by thinking about how books mainly have their origins in real life.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/487', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%AC', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T00:58:32Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T06:58:42.409411Z'),
(488, 'kanji', '正', '正', 'There is a line on the <radical>ground</radical> so you <radical>stop</radical>, making a giant sign pop up that says <kanji>CORRECT</kanji>! You obeyed the rules of the road and did the correct thing. Good on you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/488', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AD%A3', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T01:04:20Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:04:44.304483Z'),
(489, 'kanji', '玉', '玉', 'The <radical>king</radical> has a big <radical>drop</radical> of liquid in his hand... but then he bounces it! The drop was really a <kanji>ball</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/489', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8E%89', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T01:07:13Z', NULL, '2026-01-09T15:49:57.166609Z'),
(490, 'kanji', '田', '田', 'The <radical>rice paddy</radical> radical and the <kanji>rice paddy</kanji> kanji are the same, making this one super easy to remember, at least meaning-wise.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/490', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%94%B0', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T01:12:55Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.188014Z'),
(491, 'kanji', '白', '白', 'The radical <radical>white</radical> and the kanji <kanji>white</kanji> are the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/491', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%99%BD', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T01:13:32Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.201463Z'),
(492, 'kanji', '目', '目', 'The <radical>eye</radical> radical and <kanji>eye</kanji> kanji are the same as each other, so now you know them both.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/492', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9B%AE', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T01:14:43Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:18:03.531203Z'),
(494, 'kanji', '立', '立', 'The radical <radical>stand</radical> and the kanji <kanji>stand</kanji> are the same thing, so now you know them both.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/494', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AB%8B', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T01:18:04Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T22:10:21.462077Z'),
(495, 'kanji', '万', '万', 'You cut the <radical>leaf</radical> with your <radical>sword</radical> into <kanji>ten thousand</kanji> pieces!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/495', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%87', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:10:55Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T07:27:04.979321Z'),
(497, 'kanji', '今', '今', 'The kanji and the radical are the same, so remembering the meaning of this kanji is as simple as making sure you know the radical for <radical>now</radical> first! If you know the radical, you know this kanji means <kanji>now</kanji> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/497', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%8A', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:24:53Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:15:55.163539Z'),
(500, 'kanji', '内', '内', 'Your <radical>head</radical> has a <radical>person</radical> <kanji>inside</kanji> it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/500', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%86%85', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:30:14Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T06:06:52.42308Z'),
(501, 'kanji', '分', '分', 'The <radical>fins</radical> that fell onto my <radical>sword</radical> were sliced into many <kanji>part</kanji>s. Following this logic, this also means <kanji>minute</kanji>, which is part of an hour, and <kanji>divide</kanji>, which is the action of splitting something into many parts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/501', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%86', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:31:21Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T02:50:36.57776Z'),
(502, 'kanji', '切', '切', 'If you have <radical>seven</radical> <radical>swords</radical>, there''s only so much you can do with them. The main thing? Probably using them to <kanji>cut</kanji> something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/502', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%87', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:33:02Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:20:38.543784Z'),
(503, 'kanji', '午', '午', 'The <radical>slide</radical> is <radical>dry</radical> at <kanji>noon</kanji>. The sun is brightest at noon, so it dries the morning dew off the slide, making noon the perfect time to slide.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/503', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%88', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:34:26Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.096937Z'),
(504, 'kanji', '友', '友', 'A <radical>narwhal</radical> sitting on a <radical>stool</radical> is your <kanji>friend</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/504', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%8B', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:35:48Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.156413Z'),
(505, 'kanji', '太', '太', 'The kanji is the same as the radical, so this means <kanji>fat</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/505', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A4%AA', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:38:55Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:00:27.472065Z'),
(506, 'kanji', '少', '少', 'You have a <radical>small</radical> <radical>slide</radical>. The thing about small slides is that <kanji>few</kanji> people can use them. Not everyone can fit their bum in a small slide — only a few small ones can make it down.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/506', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%91', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:42:36Z', NULL, '2026-02-22T22:27:08.625694Z'),
(507, 'kanji', '引', '引', 'If you have a <radical>bow</radical> and a <radical>stick</radical> you need to <kanji>pull</kanji> the two back together to shoot it. If you can''t pull it, you won''t be shooting anything.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/507', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%95', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:46:20Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.218898Z'),
(508, 'kanji', '心', '心', 'The radical <radical>heart</radical> and the kanji <kanji>heart</kanji> are the same. Rejoice and let your heart be free!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/508', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BF%83', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:48:23Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:16:35.099537Z'),
(509, 'kanji', '戸', '戸', 'The radical and the kanji for <kanji>door</kanji> are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/509', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%88%B8', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:49:28Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:13:55.09106Z'),
(510, 'kanji', '方', '方', 'The radical and the kanji for <kanji>direction</kanji> are the same! Huzzah!

There is also another meaning too, though. This can also mean <kanji>way</kanji> as well. Think of it this way: When you move in one direction in your life, you are developing your own way. "The way of [your name here]."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/510', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%96%B9', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:50:26Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T07:25:28.432474Z'),
(511, 'kanji', '牛', '牛', 'The radical and the kanji for <kanji>cow</kanji> are exactly the same! That makes it easy to remember, so long as you know your radicals well enough.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/511', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%89%9B', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T02:52:31Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:37:01.42735Z'),
(512, 'kanji', '父', '父', 'The radical and the kanji for <kanji>father</kanji> are the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/512', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%88%B6', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T03:00:33Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:02:06.341638Z'),
(513, 'kanji', '毛', '毛', '<kanji>Fur</kanji> and <radical>fur</radical> are the same as each other, so the meaning of this kanji should be simple. This can also mean <kanji>hair</kanji>, too, though more like furry hair.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/513', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AF%9B', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T03:01:07Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.288529Z'),
(514, 'kanji', '止', '止', 'The <radical>stop</radical> radical is the same as the <kanji>stop</kanji> kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/514', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AD%A2', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T03:02:11Z', NULL, '2026-01-20T01:09:05.859847Z'),
(516, 'kanji', '冬', '冬', 'You’ve got a <radical>winter</radical> with lots of <radical>ice</radical>. But doesn''t any winter have lots of ice? Exactly. That''s why this is just <kanji>winter</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/516', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%86%AC', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T03:46:35Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.309779Z'),
(517, 'kanji', '北', '北', 'The kanji is the same as the radical — it means <kanji>north</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/517', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8C%97', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T03:49:49Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.381804Z'),
(518, 'kanji', '半', '半', 'A <radical>triceratops</radical> is lying out to <radical>dry</radical>. But it''s so big, and it''s lying on its side, so only <kanji>half</kanji> of it gets dry.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/518', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%8A', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T03:50:42Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T02:50:36.57776Z'),
(519, 'kanji', '古', '古', 'The kanji and the radical are the same, so remembering the meaning of this kanji is as simple as making sure you know the radical for <radical>old</radical> first! If you know the radical, you know this kanji means <kanji>old</kanji> as well.

Note: This "old" does not refer to people. Think of it as "old things" instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/519', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%A4', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T03:52:05Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:13:37.694956Z'),
(520, 'kanji', '台', '台', 'You have a <radical>private</radical> <radical>mouth</radical> that''s a <kanji>machine</kanji>. But you have to keep your machine mouth private because it would scare all the non-machine people around you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/520', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%B0', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T03:54:05Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T01:31:54.255144Z'),
(521, 'kanji', '外', '外', 'You''re known to get the ailment <radical>evening</radical> <radical>toe</radical>, which is when your toe catches on fire after getting really, really hot (imagine this happening, ouch!). The only way to fix it is to cool your toe down, and the best way to do that is to go <kanji>outside</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/521', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A4%96', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T03:56:49Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:22:47.768552Z'),
(522, 'kanji', '市', '市', 'You see a manhole <radical>lid</radical> and a <radical>towel</radical>, both with the name of a <kanji>city</kanji> on them. Decorative manhole lids and souvenir towels — two things cities love to slap their names on, especially in Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/522', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B8%82', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T03:57:35Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T21:50:28.772141Z'),
(523, 'kanji', '広', '広', 'The <radical>canopy</radical> you use in <radical>private</radical> is very <kanji>wide</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/523', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%83', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T03:58:32Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.491792Z'),
(524, 'kanji', '母', '母', 'Someone breaks part of your <radical>window</radical>. Who did this? It was your <kanji>mother</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/524', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AF%8D', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T04:00:00Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:59:03.20636Z'),
(525, 'kanji', '用', '用', 'The kanji and the radical are the same, so remembering the meaning of this kanji is as simple as making sure you know the radical for <radical>task</radical> first! If you know the radical, you know this kanji means <kanji>task</kanji> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/525', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%94%A8', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T04:00:59Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T01:14:18.806635Z'),
(526, 'kanji', '矢', '矢', 'The <radical>arrow</radical> radical and the <kanji>arrow</kanji> kanji are the same. Sort of a "straight" shot to memorizing this kanji, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/526', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9F%A2', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T04:02:50Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.400508Z'),
(545, 'kanji', '字', '字', 'The <radical>roof</radical> <radical>child</radical> stands upon the roof, holding up a single <kanji>letter</kanji>, a single <kanji>character</kanji>, a single <kanji>symbol</kanji> for the world to see.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/545', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AD%97', 3, 1, '2012-03-02T08:00:21Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.42318Z'),
(626, 'kanji', '明', '明', 'The <radical>bright</radical> radical and the <kanji>bright</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/626', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%98%8E', 3, 1, '2012-03-08T23:44:41Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.00231Z'),
(850, 'kanji', '生', '生', 'The <radical>life</radical> radical and the <kanji>life</kanji> kanji are the same, so hopefully you now just know them both.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/850', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%94%9F', 3, 1, '2012-06-05T23:58:47.796331Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:35:47.98533Z'),
(493, 'kanji', '石', '石', 'The <radical>stone</radical> radical and <kanji>stone</kanji> kanji are the same thing!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/493', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9F%B3', 4, 1, '2012-02-29T01:17:03Z', NULL, '2026-01-20T07:49:29.102196Z'),
(498, 'kanji', '元', '元', 'The <radical>origin</radical> radical and the <kanji>origin</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/498', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%83', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T02:25:56Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:26.034277Z'),
(527, 'kanji', '世', '世', 'The <radical>world</radical> radical and the <kanji>world</kanji> kanji are the same, but there is another really important meaning you need to know: <kanji>generation</kanji>. 

Think of it this way: the world is just made up of many different generations of people, plants, and animals all getting along together.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/527', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%96', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T01:58:15Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T01:05:39.161541Z'),
(528, 'kanji', '主', '主', 'The radical <radical>master</radical> and the kanji <kanji>master</kanji> have the same meanings!

This kanji also means <kanji>main</kanji> because the master is the main person of a dojo or household or business.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/528', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%BB', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T02:01:10Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:53:04.827663Z'),
(529, 'kanji', '他', '他', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of <radical>alligators</radical>... she''s no ordinary leader, that''s for sure. She''s not a politician, she''s not a queen... She''s one of the <kanji>Other</kanji> Ones, a group of strange, eccentric people who become leaders of animals.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/529', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%96', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T02:05:14Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T04:47:22.164044Z'),
(531, 'kanji', '写', '写', 'The <radical>forehead</radical> of this <radical>beggar</radical> is on the <radical>ground</radical>. Except it''s not exactly the ground, it''s a giant <kanji>copy</kanji> machine, and he''s making copies of his forehead!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/531', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%86%99', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T02:08:47Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T14:36:19.052017Z'),
(532, 'kanji', '去', '去', 'If someone tries to dig up <radical>dirt</radical> on your <radical>private</radical> life, tell them no thanks, that stuff is in the <kanji>past</kanji>. Some things should stay in the past. Not everyone needs to know everything about your life and your past.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/532', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8E%BB', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T02:09:41Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T22:02:05.719538Z'),
(533, 'kanji', '号', '号', 'There''s a <radical>mouth</radical> in the <radical>ground</radical> (in our case, it''s a hatch), and inside is some kind of <radical>prison</radical> you can''t quite get to. If you''ve ever seen Lost, this will sound familiar... on the hatch, there''s actually a series of <kanji>number</kanji>s that you don''t understand.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/533', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%B7', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T02:11:18Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(534, 'kanji', '央', '央', 'The radical for <radical>center</radical> and the kanji for <kanji>center</kanji> are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/534', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A4%AE', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T02:11:55Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:25:07.512079Z'),
(535, 'kanji', '平', '平', 'If you want to <radical>dry</radical> your <radical>horns</radical> you need to lay them <kanji>flat</kanji>. If they aren''t flat they''ll always be a little damp somewhere and you''ll never have a moment''s <kanji>peace</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/535', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B9%B3', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T02:13:15Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T07:29:53.644816Z'),
(536, 'kanji', '打', '打', 'Take your <radical>fingers</radical> to the <radical>street</radical> to <kanji>hit</kanji> some people! Street fingers are for curling into a fist. And that street fist wants to do some hitting!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/536', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%89%93', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T07:33:15Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.517983Z'),
(537, 'kanji', '氷', '氷', 'Add a <radical>drop</radical> of something magical to <radical>water</radical> and suddenly it turns into big block of <kanji>ice</kanji>, just like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/537', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B0%B7', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T07:36:58Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.543659Z'),
(538, 'kanji', '申', '申', 'There''s a <radical>cross</radical> inside your <radical>mouth</radical>, so the things you say are very pious. Everything you say, you <kanji>say humbly</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/538', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%94%B3', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T07:42:09Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:14:57.680592Z'),
(540, 'kanji', '皿', '皿', 'The <kanji>plate</kanji> kanji and the <radical>plate</radical> radical are exactly the same, like two matching pieces of china.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/540', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9A%BF', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T07:46:18Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.969822Z'),
(541, 'kanji', '礼', '礼', 'What do you get if you lend a <radical>spirit</radical> your <radical>umbrella</radical>? <kanji>Thanks</kanji>, of course! Especially if that spirit is Totoro, the spirit of the forest.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/541', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A4%BC', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T07:46:42Z', NULL, '2025-12-21T20:51:22.632515Z'),
(542, 'kanji', '休', '休', 'The <radical>leader</radical> is next to the <radical>tree</radical>. What is he doing? He''s leaning against it, <kanji>rest</kanji>ing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/542', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BC%91', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T07:54:25Z', NULL, '2026-03-22T18:33:30.499207Z'),
(543, 'kanji', '先', '先', 'If you go down a <radical>slide</radical> and get <radical>dirt</radical> on your <radical>legs</radical>, it must have been the fault of the <kanji>previous</kanji> person, aka the person who went down the slide <kanji>ahead</kanji> of you. They should''ve cleaned off their dirty legs <kanji>before</kanji> they went down the slide, but they didn''t, and now you''ve got dirt all over your legs too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/543', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%88', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T07:55:54Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:27:18.333927Z'),
(544, 'kanji', '名', '名', 'What is the <radical>evening</radical> <radical>mouth</radical>? Imagine a mouth that''s up in the night sky. It''s just there, talking, and only comes out at night. It only appears in one area of the world. (What area would you choose? Imagine yourself going to that place to see the "evening mouth.") And it appears only one month out of the year (more on this in a second). The "evening mouth" is the most famous thing in the _____ area (this is the area you choose). He''s so <kanji>famous</kanji> that everybody knows his <kanji>name</kanji> ("Evening Mouth").', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/544', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%8D', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T07:59:19Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.707722Z'),
(546, 'kanji', '年', '年', 'You have a <radical>gun</radical> and a <radical>cow</radical>, which must mean it''s that time of the <kanji>year</kanji>! Every year on New Year''s Eve, you get asked to shoot a cow, as per tradition. You really don''t want to though, so you deliberately miss every time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/546', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B9%B4', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T08:02:22Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:06:22.227639Z'),
(547, 'kanji', '早', '早', 'When the <radical>sun</radical> is above the <radical>cross</radical>, it''s time for some people to go church — which is always way too <kanji>early</kanji> on a Sunday, am I right? And if you want to get to church early, you need to be <kanji>fast</kanji>, otherwise you''ll be too late.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/547', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%97%A9', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T08:03:18Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:39:10.821813Z'),
(548, 'kanji', '気', '気', 'These days <radical>energy</radical> is the greatest <radical>treasure</radical>. Not gold, money, or anything like that. Imagine finding some in a literal treasure chest. As soon as you open it, out bursts the <kanji>energy</kanji> in a kind of <kanji>spirit</kanji> ball.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/548', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B0%97', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T08:27:33Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:45:36.420209Z'),
(549, 'kanji', '百', '百', 'There is a <radical>leaf</radical> growing out of the <radical>sun</radical>! You fly out into space to check it out and when you get closer you see that it isn''t just one leaf, it''s a <kanji>hundred</kanji>! That makes sense, you wouldn''t be able to see just one leaf on the sun from where you were on earth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/549', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%99%BE', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T08:28:50Z', NULL, '2025-10-22T08:19:45.4111Z'),
(550, 'kanji', '竹', '竹', 'The <radical>bamboo</radical> radical and the <kanji>bamboo</kanji> kanji are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/550', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AB%B9', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T08:32:13Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.643149Z'),
(551, 'kanji', '糸', '糸', 'The <radical>thread</radical> radical and <kanji>thread</kanji> are the same in looks as well as in meaning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/551', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B3%B8', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T08:32:39Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.648582Z'),
(552, 'kanji', '耳', '耳', 'The <radical>ear</radical> radical and <kanji>ear</kanji> kanji are the same in looks as well as meaning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/552', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%80%B3', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T08:33:44Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.676247Z'),
(553, 'kanji', '虫', '虫', 'The <radical>insect</radical> radical and <kanji>insect</kanji> are the same in looks as well as and meaning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/553', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%99%AB', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T08:34:31Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.686622Z'),
(554, 'kanji', '村', '村', 'Someone''s taking <radical>tree</radical> <radical>measurements</radical>… Why are they taking measurements of trees? Because they''re planning to cut them down to make their <kanji>village</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/554', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9D%91', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T08:44:08Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:24:57.243709Z'),
(555, 'kanji', '男', '男', 'The radical for <radical>man</radical> and the kanji for <kanji>man</kanji> are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/555', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%94%B7', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T08:45:49Z', NULL, '2025-11-22T03:21:31.207063Z'),
(556, 'kanji', '町', '町', 'When you see both a <radical>rice paddy</radical> and a <radical>street</radical> in the same place, you have the makings of a whole <kanji>town</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/556', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%94%BA', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T09:15:08Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.705773Z'),
(557, 'kanji', '花', '花', 'All these <radical>flowers</radical> were planted by your <radical>leader</radical> with nothing but a <radical>spoon</radical>. Now the whole place is absolutely full of beautiful <kanji>flower</kanji>s. Flowers everywhere.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/557', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8A%B1', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T18:08:06Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:20:54.613696Z'),
(558, 'kanji', '見', '見', 'The radical <radical>see</radical> and the kanji <kanji>see</kanji> are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/558', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A6%8B', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T18:11:17Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:04:43.265681Z'),
(559, 'kanji', '貝', '貝', 'The <radical>shellfish</radical> radical and the <kanji>shellfish</kanji> kanji are the same! It can be just the <kanji>shell</kanji> part too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/559', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%9D', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T18:17:08Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.724324Z'),
(560, 'kanji', '赤', '赤', 'The radical for <radical>red</radical> and the kanji for <kanji>red</kanji> are the same thing!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/560', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B5%A4', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T18:23:54Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.726945Z'),
(561, 'kanji', '足', '足', 'The radical for <radical>foot</radical> and the kanji for <kanji>foot</kanji> are exactly the same.

That being said, there''s also another meaning you should keep in mind, which is <kanji>sufficient</kanji>. If you think about it, one foot should be sufficient enough to walk around with if you hop.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/561', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B6%B3', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T18:28:46Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.729913Z'),
(562, 'kanji', '車', '車', 'The radical <radical>car</radical> and the kanji <kanji>car</kanji> are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/562', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BB%8A', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T18:32:08Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T07:17:38.434454Z'),
(563, 'kanji', '不', '不', 'The radical <radical>not</radical> and the kanji <kanji>not</kanji> are the same thing! That makes it easy... Not!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/563', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%8D', 4, 1, '2012-03-02T18:35:06Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:38:20.446355Z'),
(564, 'kanji', '仕', '仕', 'The <radical>leader</radical> <radical>samurai</radical> didn''t become leader by sitting around not taking action. He got to where he is by <kanji>doing</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/564', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%95', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:39:13Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:46:58.845735Z'),
(1118, 'kanji', '穴', '穴', 'The <radical>hole</radical> radical and the <kanji>hole</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Spectacular!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1118', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A9%B4', 4, 1, '2012-11-01T06:41:36.700442Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:10:42.46993Z'),
(1520, 'kanji', '宝', '宝', 'Under his <radical>roof</radical>, the <radical>king</radical> stares into a <radical>drop</radical> of water containing his greatest <kanji>treasure</kanji>. Just kidding, the king has way more treasure than that. The drop of water is just reflecting what''s around him: mounds and mounds of treasure, stacked all the way up to the roof.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1520', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%9D', 4, 1, '2013-03-18T17:06:20.364059Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.502814Z'),
(515, 'kanji', '兄', '兄', 'Who''s someone who''s just a <radical>mouth</radical> with <radical>legs</radical>? That''s your know-it-all <kanji>older brother</kanji>, who won''t shut up about anything.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/515', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%84', 5, 1, '2012-03-01T03:45:02Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:33:29.32274Z'),
(530, 'kanji', '代', '代', 'The <radical>leader</radical> for the <radical>ceremony</radical> can''t come so you need a <kanji>substitute</kanji> to <kanji>replace</kanji> them ASAP!

This kanji has another meaning too. The leader can''t come to the ceremony because they forgot what <kanji>period</kanji> of time it was. If they think they''re in the wrong <kanji>age</kanji>, they won''t come because they''re trying to lead some monkeys in the ceremony instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/530', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%A3', 5, 1, '2012-03-02T02:06:56Z', NULL, '2026-03-05T17:46:40.146127Z'),
(539, 'kanji', '皮', '皮', 'You go down a <radical>slide</radical> made of <radical>branch</radical>es and your <kanji>skin</kanji> scrapes right off — ouch! Lucky for you, it was just your old outer layer of skin, and now you''ve got a fresh shiny layer. Yay for exfoliation! You''re not sure what to do with this big, floppy layer of old skin, though…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/539', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9A%AE', 5, 1, '2012-03-02T07:43:19Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:30:12.795285Z'),
(565, 'kanji', '交', '交', 'You put a <radical>lid</radical> on your <radical>father</radical>. But, he won''t taste good alone, so you <kanji>mix</kanji> in some other ingredients. They <kanji>mingle</kanji> inside the pot with him.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/565', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%A4', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T19:31:24Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:31:54.877858Z'),
(566, 'kanji', '会', '会', 'The <radical>meet</radical> radical and the <kanji>meet</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/566', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BC%9A', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T19:36:29Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:11:34.848247Z'),
(567, 'kanji', '光', '光', 'You see a <radical>triceratops</radical> in the distance. But, it''s odd. It''s perfectly <radical>pi</radical> shaped (that is, circular, like a pie). Some kind of piceratops, if you will. The reason they are this shape is because they want to absorb as much <kanji>light</kanji> as possible. They evolved to be round so they don''t have any corners casting a shadow.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/567', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%89', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T19:37:00Z', NULL, '2025-10-29T12:06:39.877751Z'),
(568, 'kanji', '同', '同', 'There''s a <radical>mustache</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical>. Upon closer inspection, you see that the mustache on the ground is exactly the <kanji>same</kanji> as yours!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/568', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%8C', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T19:47:38Z', NULL, '2026-02-12T07:17:21.136514Z'),
(569, 'kanji', '回', '回', 'There''s a <radical>mouth</radical> inside a <radical>mouth</radical>. But if you look even closer (not actually, but we''re pretending), you can see another mouth inside that mouth... and another one inside of that one! It keeps going and going. Can you count the number of <kanji>times</kanji> there''s a mouth inside a mouth? This kanji also means <kanji>revolve</kanji> too. Think of each "time" a thing happens, it loops back around and comes back to the beginning, revolving around again another time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/569', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9B%9E', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T19:49:49Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:38:25.947585Z'),
(570, 'kanji', '多', '多', 'How many <radical>evenings</radical> are there? There are <kanji>many</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/570', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A4%9A', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T19:55:45Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:40:52.824213Z'),
(571, 'kanji', '当', '当', 'What superhero is most like a <radical>triceratops</radical>? …If you said <radical>Wolverine</radical>, you''re <kanji>correct</kanji>! Why is that the <kanji>right</kanji> answer? Because the triceratops has three horns, and Wolverine has three blades coming out of his hands. It''s obvious, isn''t it? But you knew that of course.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/571', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BD%93', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T19:57:14Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:42:58.478016Z'),
(572, 'kanji', '毎', '毎', 'You have a <radical>gun</radical> and you see some <radical>windows</radical>. What do you think you''re going to do with this gun? Of course, you''re going to shoot the windows... <kanji>every</kanji> single one of them. <kanji>Each</kanji> and every one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/572', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AF%8E', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T19:57:56Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:28:25.02468Z'),
(574, 'kanji', '米', '米', 'The <radical>rice</radical> radical and <kanji>rice</kanji> kanji are the same in looks and meaning. This kanji can also mean <kanji>America</kanji>, maybe because America is covered in rice or fields or something?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/574', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B1%B3', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T20:00:13Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T06:49:57.6353Z'),
(575, 'kanji', '羽', '羽', 'The <radical>feathers</radical> radical and <kanji>feather</kanji> kanji are the same in looks as well as in meaning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/575', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BE%BD', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T20:03:49Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:29:48.737278Z'),
(576, 'kanji', '考', '考', 'In a <radical>coffin</radical> there''s a <radical>beggar</radical>, doing his begging. But it doesn''t work, nobody helps him. So, he just begins to <kanji>think</kanji>: Why am I in this situation? How could I have avoided it? How will I get out? Think, dammit, think!!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/576', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%80%83', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T20:06:41Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:32:36.643798Z'),
(577, 'kanji', '肉', '肉', 'The <radical>meat</radical> radical and <kanji>meat</kanji> are the same in looks as well as in meaning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/577', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%82%89', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T20:07:17Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:44:17.044799Z'),
(578, 'kanji', '自', '自', 'The <radical>self</radical> radical and <kanji>self</kanji> are the same in looks as well as in meaning. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/578', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%87%AA', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T20:08:04Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:46:51.587747Z'),
(579, 'kanji', '色', '色', 'The <radical>color</radical> radical and <kanji>color</kanji> are the same in looks as well as in meaning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/579', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%89%B2', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T20:09:45Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:13:23.883974Z'),
(580, 'kanji', '行', '行', 'The <radical>go</radical> radical and the <kanji>go</kanji> kanji are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/580', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A1%8C', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T20:10:34Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T07:25:28.430705Z'),
(581, 'kanji', '西', '西', 'The <radical>west</radical> radical and the <kanji>west</kanji> kanji are the same. Isn''t that nice?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/581', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A5%BF', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T20:11:58Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T00:40:07.119262Z'),
(582, 'kanji', '何', '何', 'Our <radical>leader</radical> has a <radical>lip ring</radical>. Wait, our leader has a lip ring? <kanji>WHAT</kanji>??', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/582', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BD%95', 5, 1, '2012-03-05T20:13:46Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.263269Z'),
(583, 'kanji', '体', '体', 'The <radical>leader</radical> has a <radical>book</radical> all about the human <kanji>body</kanji>. Knowing about bodies is a privilege. One that only leaders get—it helps them control all their subjects, like you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/583', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BD%93', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T00:51:47Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:18:14.008717Z'),
(584, 'kanji', '作', '作', 'The <radical>leader</radical>''s <radical>key</radical> is the key that <kanji>make</kanji>s all the other keys. It''s sort of like the one ring to rule them all, the one ring to bind them... except with keys.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/584', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BD%9C', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T00:59:39Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T23:39:59.547014Z'),
(585, 'kanji', '図', '図', 'You have a <radical>mouth</radical> full of two massive <radical>drop</radical>s and… <radical>treasure</radical>?! Upon closer inspection, they appear to form some sort of <kanji>diagram</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/585', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9B%B3', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T01:28:18Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T15:10:41.266432Z'),
(586, 'kanji', '声', '声', 'You have a <radical>samurai</radical> with a <radical>flag</radical> on a <radical>stick</radical>. On his flag is the symbol 声, the kanji for <kanji>voice</kanji>. Why does he have that there? He feels like his Shogun isn''t giving him and his fellow Samurai a voice, so he''s brought a flag with the word "voice" written on it to protest.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/586', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A3%B0', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T05:47:49Z', NULL, '2025-10-21T16:17:02.815667Z'),
(588, 'kanji', '弟', '弟', 'There is someone wearing <radical>horns</radical>, holding a <radical>bow</radical>, and going down a <radical>slide</radical> held up by a <radical>stick</radical>. What type of person do you imagine? I''d say someone who''s an immature boy. That would be your <kanji>younger brother</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/588', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%9F', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T05:55:46Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:21:54.297415Z'),
(589, 'kanji', '形', '形', 'You''re walking at night when you discover a <radical>lantern</radical> covered entirely in <radical>hair</radical>, making it a very strange <kanji>shape</kanji>. In fact, this is a <kanji>form</kanji> you''d never expect a lantern to take.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/589', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BD%A2', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T06:00:11Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T22:22:06.499826Z'),
(590, 'kanji', '来', '来', 'The <radical>ground</radical> is scattered with <radical>rice</radical>, which will cause the Predator to <kanji>come</kanji> because he loves rice. Then once he''s gone, the <kanji>next</kanji> Predator will come to eat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/590', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9D%A5', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T06:03:48Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:27:18.261345Z'),
(591, 'kanji', '社', '社', 'Plant a <radical>spirit</radical> in the <radical>dirt</radical> before you start your <kanji>company</kanji> and it will be successful. That''s what all the great, old Japanese companies did back in the olden days. That''s why companies like Mitsubishi have been around for hundreds of years. They planted a spirit in the dirt to help their company grow, and grow it did!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/591', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A4%BE', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T06:08:21Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T06:58:42.409411Z'),
(592, 'kanji', '角', '角', 'The <radical>angle</radical> radical and the <kanji>angle</kanji> kanji are the same! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/592', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A7%92', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T06:23:30Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:56:15.259524Z'),
(593, 'kanji', '言', '言', 'The <radical>say</radical> radical and the <kanji>say</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/593', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%80', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T06:27:10Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T21:53:11.959921Z'),
(594, 'kanji', '谷', '谷', '<radical>Fins</radical> decorate a <radical>hat</radical>, with (presumably fish) <radical>mouths</radical> stitched to it. Who wears strange hats like this? Turns out, the <kanji>valley</kanji> people are the ones who wear these. I''m talking about the "Valley Girls" of Los Angeles, California. They''re into the crazy fashion, and they''ll wear anything if it''s deemed "cool" or something like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/594', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B0%B7', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T06:55:47Z', NULL, '2026-03-12T07:45:30.720695Z'),
(595, 'kanji', '走', '走', 'The radical <radical>run</radical> and the kanji <kanji>run</kanji> are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/595', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B5%B0', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T07:00:53Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:38:57.03621Z'),
(596, 'kanji', '近', '近', 'Someone on a <radical>scooter</radical> has an <radical>axe</radical>, and they''re getting <kanji>near</kanji> to you really fast! Oh no, they''re so <kanji>close</kanji> now, you need to get out of here!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/596', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BF%91', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T07:10:41Z', NULL, '2026-01-05T23:19:07.724287Z'),
(598, 'kanji', '麦', '麦', 'The <radical>life</radical> radical is without the slide (as it sometimes is), but when you combine that with the <radical>winter</radical> radical, you have life during winter. What gives you life during the winter? It''s the <kanji>wheat</kanji> you store away.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/598', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%BA%A6', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T07:22:12Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:24:56.585442Z'),
(599, 'kanji', '学', '学', 'You have a wild <radical>viking</radical> <radical>child</radical> you have to take care of. He won''t do anything but <kanji>study</kanji>, surprisingly. You can''t get him to eat, use the bathroom, or anything else. He just wants to study.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/599', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AD%A6', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T07:41:36Z', NULL, '2026-01-05T08:21:15.235162Z'),
(600, 'kanji', '林', '林', 'You have a <radical>tree</radical> and another <radical>tree</radical>. You basically have yourself a <kanji>forest</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/600', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9E%97', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T07:42:17Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.080561Z'),
(601, 'kanji', '空', '空', 'What was the biggest <radical>hole</radical> in the <radical>construction</radical> of the world? The <kanji>sky</kanji>. If you think about it, the sky is like a big hole someone forgot to fill in. That''s why this kanji also means <kanji>empty</kanji>! Makes sense — nothing is more empty than the sky. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/601', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A9%BA', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:03:00Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:29:41.541367Z'),
(602, 'kanji', '金', '金', 'The <radical>gold</radical> radical and the <kanji>gold</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/602', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%87%91', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:03:34Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:10:10.95295Z'),
(603, 'kanji', '雨', '雨', 'The radical for <radical>rain</radical> and the kanji for <kanji>rain</kanji> are the same thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/603', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%A8', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:06:56Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.10646Z'),
(604, 'kanji', '青', '青', 'The <radical>blue</radical> radical and the <kanji>blue</kanji> kanji are exactly the same.

Additionally, this can also mean <kanji>young</kanji>. You can remember this because young people have potential as limitless as the blue sky!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/604', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9D%92', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:07:34Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:22:25.515687Z'),
(605, 'kanji', '草', '草', 'You plant your <radical>flowers</radical> in the light of the <radical>sun</radical> in the shape of a <radical>cross</radical>, but it''s too hot—the sun is too strong—and you end up with <kanji>grass</kanji>. Flowers can''t grow in these conditions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/605', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8D%89', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:13:45Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.123321Z'),
(606, 'kanji', '音', '音', 'For some reason, you are <radical>stand</radical>ing on the <radical>sun</radical>. It''s obviously very hot, but the thing you notice most is the <kanji>sound</kanji> of you burning up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/606', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9F%B3', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:14:18Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:40:46.545472Z'),
(622, 'kanji', '夜', '夜', 'The <radical>night</radical> radical and the <kanji>night</kanji> kanji are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/622', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A4%9C', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:41:13Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:51:53.08744Z'),
(1309, 'kanji', '豚', '豚', 'When the <radical>moon</radical> is bright, it''s easy to see that a <radical>pig</radical> is indeed a <kanji>pig</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1309', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B1%9A', 5, 1, '2013-01-17T20:45:48.363671Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:44:17.044799Z'),
(8884, 'kanji', '斤', '斤', 'The <radical>axe</radical> radical and the <kanji>axe</kanji> kanji are exactly the same, with one minor difference. This kanji is also used as the <kanji>bread loaf counter</kanji>, probably because bread was so big and hard in the past that they had to use axes to slice them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8884', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%96%A4', 5, 1, '2019-04-30T18:01:09.895741Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.139506Z'),
(607, 'kanji', '化', '化', 'A <radical>leader</radical> with a <radical>spoon</radical> is a powerful thing. Even something as ordinary as a spoon can create great <kanji>change</kanji> in the world when given to the right person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/607', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8C%96', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T04:24:48Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T07:09:11.444332Z'),
(608, 'kanji', '地', '地', 'A <radical>dirt</radical> <radical>alligator</radical> lives in the <kanji>earth</kanji>, not the water. They can swim through the earth itself! Which is pretty scary.

You can think of this word as <kanji>ground</kanji> too, because that''s kind of what earth is made of.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/608', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9C%B0', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T04:34:47Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:31:31.943758Z'),
(609, 'kanji', '両', '両', 'As you dig under the <radical>ground</radical> you discover a <radical>head</radical> resting near a <radical>mountain</radical>, which are <kanji>both</kanji> strange things to find beneath the earth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/609', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%A1', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T04:39:59Z', NULL, '2026-01-18T18:20:32.716328Z'),
(610, 'kanji', '全', '全', 'The <radical>hat</radical> crowns the <radical>king</radical>, because <kanji>all</kanji> kings must have one. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/610', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%A8', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T04:40:44Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:19:19.630424Z'),
(611, 'kanji', '向', '向', 'A <radical>drop</radical> hits your <radical>mustache</radical>. Where did it come from? "Ah, it came from <kanji>yonder</kanji>," you say, <kanji>facing</kanji> that way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/611', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%91', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T04:43:25Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:36:04.250014Z'),
(612, 'kanji', '安', '安', 'With a <radical>roof</radical> over her head, a <radical>woman</radical> can <kanji>relax</kanji>. She doesn''t have to worry about rain or snow or finding a place to sleep. The roof also happened to be super <kanji>cheap</kanji>, so she can even relax about her finances.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/612', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%89', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T04:46:03Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(613, 'kanji', '州', '州', 'Think about Japan. Japan is a country made up of islands. They''re basically three <radical>drop</radical> <radical>drop</radical> <radical>drop</radical>s in the large <radical>river</radical> that is the Pacific Ocean. These are Japan''s <kanji>states</kanji> or <kanji>province</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/613', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B7%9E', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T06:51:08Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:04.974037Z'),
(614, 'kanji', '曲', '曲', 'There are <radical>mouth</radical>s, <radical>twenty</radical> of them, singing beautiful <kanji>music</kanji> together. The music seems to <kanji>bend</kanji> toward you through the air.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/614', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9B%B2', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T07:00:20Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T08:16:48.590107Z'),
(615, 'kanji', '有', '有', 'A <radical>narwhal</radical> and a <radical>moon</radical> is all you <kanji>have</kanji>. Imagine one of each under your arms.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/615', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%89', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T07:02:06Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:43:27.449451Z'),
(617, 'kanji', '死', '死', 'If the <radical>yakuza</radical> hands you a <radical>spoon</radical>, <kanji>death</kanji> is coming for you. It''s the death spoon, a sign that the yakuza is coming to cause your death.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/617', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AD%BB', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T07:05:33Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.428311Z'),
(618, 'kanji', '羊', '羊', 'The <radical>sheep</radical> radical and the <kanji>sheep</kanji> kanji are the same in looks as well in meaning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/618', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BE%8A', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T07:06:43Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:04.538511Z'),
(619, 'kanji', '血', '血', 'The <radical>blood</radical> radical and <kanji>blood</kanji> are the same in looks as well as in meaning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/619', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A1%80', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T07:07:46Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.993125Z'),
(621, 'kanji', '国', '国', 'The <radical>mouth</radical> of the <radical>king</radical> extends all the way around him (like in this kanji). Where his mouth reaches, that is his <kanji>country</kanji>, give or take a few <radical>drop</radical>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/621', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9B%BD', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T09:09:31Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:57:35.099275Z'),
(623, 'kanji', '妹', '妹', 'Who''s that <radical>woman</radical> pretending to be a <radical>jet</radical>? That''s my <kanji>younger sister</kanji>. *Sigh*', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/623', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A6%B9', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:42:17Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(624, 'kanji', '姉', '姉', 'The only <radical>woman</radical> in this <radical>city</radical> is your <kanji>older sister</kanji>. She''s the only woman surrounded by a bunch of men, but she''s following her dreams in the big city.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/624', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A7%89', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:42:55Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(625, 'kanji', '店', '店', 'A long time ago, someone put up a <radical>canopy</radical> over their <radical>fortune</radical> telling booth and created the first <kanji>shop</kanji>. This <kanji>store</kanji> was fortune-based, but since then lots of different stores have popped up!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/625', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%97', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:44:06Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.556371Z'),
(627, 'kanji', '東', '東', 'This is the same as the radical for <radical>east</radical>, so it means <kanji>east</kanji> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/627', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9D%B1', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:46:25Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T00:54:10.37966Z'),
(628, 'kanji', '歩', '歩', 'You <radical>stop</radical> at a <radical>small</radical> <radical>slide</radical>. What were you doing before you stopped here? You were <kanji>walk</kanji>ing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/628', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AD%A9', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:46:56Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.291513Z'),
(631, 'kanji', '知', '知', 'You take an <radical>arrow</radical> to the <radical>mouth</radical> because you <kanji>know</kanji> too much. You know some very sensitive information, and someone''s sent a bow-slinging enforcer to pin your big blabbermouth shut with arrows.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/631', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9F%A5', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:48:40Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:04.576396Z'),
(632, 'kanji', '長', '長', 'The <radical>long</radical> radical and the <kanji>long</kanji> kanji are the same! 

This kanji also means <kanji>leader</kanji>, because the longer a person is, the better a leader they''ll be. Or maybe it was... the longer they''ve been somewhere? Whatever, longness = leaderness, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/632', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%95%B7', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:49:12Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:04.351358Z'),
(633, 'kanji', '前', '前', 'There are some <radical>horns</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical> and a <radical>moon</radical> <radical>knife</radical> in <kanji>front</kanji> of you. When you try to move, they always rotate around with you, staying right in front. You can''t escape these objects, for some reason.

This kanji means <kanji>before</kanji> too. Something that''s in front of you is before you, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/633', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%89%8D', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:50:15Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T20:03:17.667789Z'),
(635, 'kanji', '室', '室', 'You put a <radical>roof</radical> over the <radical>ground</radical> to make your own <radical>private</radical> patch of <radical>dirt</radical>. This is your <kanji>room</kanji>! That''s really all a room is, you know? A covered, private place where you can have some time to yourself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/635', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%A4', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:51:25Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:14:09.18609Z'),
(636, 'kanji', '後', '後', 'If you <radical>loiter</radical> around to <radical>poop</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> you never know what could be happening <kanji>behind</kanji> you. <kanji>After</kanji> you''re done you could turn <kanji>back</kanji> to see something really crazy that you missed because you took so long to poop in the cold.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/636', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%8C', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:52:05Z', NULL, '2025-11-19T07:58:25.165945Z'),
(637, 'kanji', '思', '思', 'In a <radical>rice paddy</radical> you find a <radical>heart</radical> and it makes you <kanji>think</kanji>. You think a lot of <kanji>thought</kanji>s like, "Whose heart is this? Why is it in a rice paddy? Why am I thinking out loud?"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/637', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%80%9D', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:52:38Z', NULL, '2026-03-26T14:18:28.27295Z'),
(638, 'kanji', '星', '星', 'The <radical>sun</radical> that gives <radical>life</radical> is our very own <kanji>star</kanji> (which we happen to call the sun). Of course, all the other stars are probably giving life to other planets as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/638', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%98%9F', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:53:12Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T02:32:26.95095Z'),
(639, 'kanji', '活', '活', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> of <radical>tongue</radical>s is coming at you. As it washes over you, you feel each individual tongue moving around on your body, licking you. They make you feel so... <kanji>lively</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/639', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B4%BB', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:54:43Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.531167Z'),
(640, 'kanji', '海', '海', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> is coming, so you point your <radical>gun</radical> out the <radical>window</radical> at it. "You better turn back, <kanji>sea</kanji>, or I''ll shoot!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/640', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B5%B7', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:55:14Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:03:41.305988Z'),
(641, 'kanji', '点', '点', 'Your <radical>fortune</radical> <radical>boil</radical>s down to a single <kanji>point</kanji> in time. One point in time determines how the rest of your future will go.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/641', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%82%B9', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:57:27Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:04:05.081928Z'),
(642, 'kanji', '科', '科', 'You scoop some <radical>grain</radical>s into a <radical>ladle</radical> to do <kanji>science</kanji>. You''re doing experiments on these grains in your science <kanji>course</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/642', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A7%91', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:58:41Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.028231Z'),
(643, 'kanji', '茶', '茶', 'If you can steal the <radical>flowers</radical> that adorn a <radical>hat</radical> worn by an <radical>angel</radical>, you can use them to make delicious <kanji>tea</kanji>. Naturally, angels decorate their hats with only the most aromatic and delicious flowers fit for brewing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/643', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8C%B6', 6, 1, '2012-03-08T23:59:07Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:16:41.871147Z'),
(644, 'kanji', '食', '食', 'The radical for <radical>eat</radical> and the kanji for <kanji>eat</kanji> are the same! Lucky you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/644', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A3%9F', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:00:25Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T06:41:17.920793Z'),
(645, 'kanji', '首', '首', 'The radical <radical>neck</radical> and the kanji <kanji>neck</kanji> are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/645', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A6%96', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:01:03Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:04.85535Z'),
(660, 'kanji', '家', '家', 'The <radical>house</radical> radical makes up the <kanji>house</kanji> kanji! Pretty easy. This kanji is also used to mean <kanji>home</kanji>, so try to remember that too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/660', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%B6', 6, 1, '2012-03-26T23:52:23Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:04:54.519402Z'),
(851, 'kanji', '亡', '亡', 'The radical <radical>death</radical> and the kanji <kanji>death</kanji> are the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/851', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%A1', 6, 1, '2012-06-07T20:06:28.750492Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.311909Z'),
(923, 'kanji', '私', '私', 'Your <radical>private</radical> <radical>grain</radical>s are meant to stay <kanji>private</kanji>! You shouldn''t be sharing them with the whole town, they''re just for you.

This kanji also means <kanji>I</kanji>. Why? Because what''s more private than you? I mean, I! Basically, some things are only the business of you (I) and those things should be private.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/923', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A7%81', 6, 1, '2012-09-18T23:56:44.563985Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:15:26.243578Z'),
(966, 'kanji', '取', '取', 'There''s an <radical>ear</radical> just sitting on a <radical>stool</radical>. Nobody''s watching, so, you <kanji>take</kanji> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/966', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%96', 6, 1, '2012-09-20T18:38:19.139575Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:50:40.995534Z'),
(629, 'kanji', '画', '画', 'You go down a <radical>street</radical> that cuts through a <radical>rice paddy</radical> and leads to a <radical>box</radical>. When you open the box at the end of the street, you see it''s full of <kanji>image</kanji>s. Image after image. <kanji>Drawing</kanji>, <kanji>painting</kanji>, you name it. Someone must have left this box of images here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/629', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%94%BB', 7, 1, '2012-03-08T23:47:32Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.236478Z'),
(634, 'kanji', '南', '南', 'You''ve seen this as a radical before, and the kanji means the same thing — <kanji>south</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/634', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%97', 7, 1, '2012-03-08T23:50:57Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T16:35:17.608043Z'),
(646, 'kanji', '欠', '欠', 'This kanji is the same as the radical it comes from. <radical>Lack</radical> becomes <kanji>Lack</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/646', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AC%A0', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T22:56:11Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T23:48:38.947203Z'),
(647, 'kanji', '氏', '氏', 'Your <radical>clan</radical> is where you get your name. But not your given name, your <kanji>family name</kanji>. That''s why people have names that mean "in a rice paddy" and "near the ferry crossing." The location of your clan determined your <kanji>surname</kanji>! Hope your ancestors didn''t live on "Poo Lake" or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/647', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B0%8F', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T23:02:53Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.52142Z'),
(648, 'kanji', '由', '由', 'A giant <radical>cross</radical> with a <radical>mouth</radical> stands above you. "I am <kanji>reason</kanji>!" it says to you. It is the Cross of Reason.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/648', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%94%B1', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T23:04:16Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.533175Z'),
(649, 'kanji', '札', '札', 'This <radical>tree</radical> has an <radical>umbrella</radical>, which it''s holding over its head. Strange, because trees normally like water… Turns out, this tree wasn''t getting showered in water, but in dollar <kanji>bill</kanji>s instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/649', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%AD', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T23:09:14Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.544819Z'),
(650, 'kanji', '民', '民', 'The <radical>mouth</radical> <radical>clan</radical> are a group of <kanji>people</kanji>. They are the <kanji>people</kanji> of a <kanji>nation</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/650', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B0%91', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T23:13:26Z', NULL, '2026-01-20T01:06:16.306272Z'),
(651, 'kanji', '辺', '辺', 'While riding your <radical>scooter</radical>, you take your <radical>sword</radical> and cut out an <kanji>area</kanji> on the ground.  You carefully cut all the <kanji>side</kanji>s as you go. This area''s all yours now!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/651', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BE%BA', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T23:14:11Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T07:32:52.462911Z'),
(652, 'kanji', '付', '付', 'When you take your <radical>leader</radical>''s <radical>measurement</radical>s, you realize they''re too short, so you <kanji>attach</kanji> things to them to make the measurements more impressive. Attach some heels to the shoes, then attach another head on top of theirs. There — perfect measurements thanks to these attachments.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/652', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%98', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T23:17:16Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:46:30.938459Z'),
(653, 'kanji', '以', '以', 'The <radical>lion</radical> drops a <radical>drop</radical> of saliva onto this <radical>person</radical> <kanji>from</kanji> above. The person is in a much worse place <kanji>compared with</kanji> where they were before, <kanji>since</kanji> they only just recently fell into this pit.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/653', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%A5', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T23:18:39Z', NULL, '2026-02-12T21:26:04.397306Z'),
(654, 'kanji', '失', '失', 'That <radical>slide</radical> is your <radical>husband</radical>''s only <kanji>fault</kanji>. He loves that slide, more than you, and it''s the only thing you don''t like about him.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/654', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A4%B1', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T23:20:45Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.616509Z'),
(655, 'kanji', '必', '必', 'Your <radical>heart</radical> has a <radical>slide</radical> running through it because you want to be <kanji>certain</kanji> that the blood is flowing in and out of it smoothly. Given how important this is, you want to be absolutely <em>certain</em>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/655', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BF%85', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T23:22:04Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:31:47.877938Z'),
(656, 'kanji', '未', '未', 'You''re flying in a <radical>jet</radical>. Even though it''s one of the fastest things on earth, your child is sitting in the back asking, "Are we there yet? Are we there yet?" You have to keep responding. "<kanji>Not yet</kanji>! Not yet! NOT YET!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/656', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%AA', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T23:32:28Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:25:25.547443Z'),
(657, 'kanji', '末', '末', 'This kanji uses a modified <radical>jet</radical> radical. The front area of the wing is wider than the middle area of the wing. This may seem counterintuitive, but by doing this they can fit so much more jet fuel. The jet zooms off and keeps going for a long, long time, until it reaches the <kanji>end</kanji> of the earth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/657', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%AB', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T23:36:26Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.648809Z'),
(658, 'kanji', '校', '校', 'Next to the <radical>tree</radical>, a <radical>lid</radical> is put on your <radical>father</radical>. "Hello child," he says. "This is your new <kanji>school</kanji>." ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/658', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A0%A1', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T23:44:29Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.659022Z'),
(659, 'kanji', '夏', '夏', 'The <radical>leaf</radical> shaped like an <radical>eye</radical> only grows in <radical>winter</radical>. Every other leaf, however, will do the opposite, which is to grow in the <kanji>summer</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/659', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A4%8F', 7, 1, '2012-03-26T23:49:41Z', NULL, '2026-03-22T18:33:30.499207Z'),
(661, 'kanji', '弱', '弱', 'You have two <radical>bow</radical>s but they''re both covered in <radical>ice</radical>. This has made them very <kanji>weak</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/661', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%B1', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T00:11:15Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T06:03:32.700689Z'),
(662, 'kanji', '時', '時', 'The <radical>sun</radical> is used in a <radical>temple</radical> to tell <kanji>time</kanji>. They don''t have clocks or cell phones, they have sun dials and good old fashioned "looking up and seeing where the sun is right now."

This also can mean <kanji>o'' clock</kanji>, as in "five o''clock" It''s used when measuring what time it is in Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/662', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%99%82', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T00:16:18Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:02:24.849884Z'),
(663, 'kanji', '紙', '紙', 'The <radical>thread</radical> that ties you to your <radical>clan</radical> is <kanji>paper</kanji>. Your papers, that is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/663', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%99', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T00:25:12Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.711084Z'),
(664, 'kanji', '記', '記', 'What you <radical>say</radical> to <radical>oneself</radical> (yourself), you decide to <kanji>write down</kanji>. Whether this is in the form of a journal, diary, or blog, you feel the need to <kanji>record</kanji> these important thoughts in some way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/664', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%98', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T00:28:55Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:46:03.850495Z'),
(665, 'kanji', '通', '通', 'There''s a <radical>scooter</radical> with your <radical>mama</radical> carrying a <radical>task</radical> she needs to get done. You see her <kanji>pass through</kanji>, hurrying by to get that task done.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/665', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%9A', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T00:32:08Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T14:46:05.379705Z'),
(666, 'kanji', '高', '高', 'There''s a <radical>lid</radical> on your <radical>mouth</radical> and <radical>mustache</radical>, covering up all your talking holes. This is because you kept saying how <kanji>tall</kanji> and <kanji>expensive</kanji> you are.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/666', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%AB%98', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T01:00:41Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.739759Z'),
(667, 'kanji', '強', '強', 'You keep your <radical>bow</radical> <radical>private</radical> because it''s made of <radical>insect</radical>s, meaning it''s extremely <kanji>strong</kanji>. Insect bodies are really durable, so they make for super strong bows. That''s why you have to keep this one private — it''s simply too strong to let others get ahold of.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/667', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%B7', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T06:08:55Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:05:21.067642Z'),
(668, 'kanji', '教', '教', 'A <radical>teacher</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> is happiest because they get to <kanji>teach</kanji>! The kids can''t go out and play because it''s too cold. They all have to come in and stay warm as the teacher teaches!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/668', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%95%99', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T06:45:19Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T22:19:06.645866Z'),
(669, 'kanji', '理', '理', 'The <radical>king</radical>''s <radical>village</radical> is the <kanji>reason</kanji> he''s king. Without the village there would be no reason to have a king in the first place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/669', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%90%86', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T06:50:00Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:19:37.118813Z'),
(670, 'kanji', '組', '組', 'Sew <radical>thread</radical> through your <radical>top hat</radical> and to the top hats of other people you know and you''ll turn into a <kanji>group</kanji> of top hat wearing people. You''re all connected by the thread now, so everyone will assume you''re a group, even if that''s all you guys have going for yourselves.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/670', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B5%84', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:17:39Z', NULL, '2026-04-05T19:31:58.988696Z'),
(671, 'kanji', '船', '船', 'Your <radical>boat</radical> has <radical>fins</radical> and a <radical>mouth</radical>! It''s still a <kanji>boat</kanji> though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/671', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%88%B9', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:19:59Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.796241Z'),
(672, 'kanji', '週', '週', 'Use a <radical>scooter</radical> to travel the <radical>circumference</radical> of the world and it will take you a full <kanji>week</kanji>. Seven whole days!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/672', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%B1', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:26:51Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.805692Z'),
(673, 'kanji', '雪', '雪', 'When <radical>rain</radical> falls on <radical>Wolverine</radical>, it turns to <kanji>snow</kanji>. He''s so emotionally distant and cold that the rain freezes into snow as soon as it gets near him.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/673', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%AA', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:30:25Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.814824Z'),
(674, 'kanji', '魚', '魚', 'The radical <radical>fish</radical> and the kanji <kanji>fish</kanji> are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/674', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%AD%9A', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:33:41Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.779053Z'),
(675, 'kanji', '鳥', '鳥', 'The <radical>bird</radical> radical and the <kanji>bird</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/675', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%B3%A5', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:38:14Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:12:26.898461Z'),
(676, 'kanji', '黄', '黄', 'The radical <radical>yellow</radical> and the kanji <kanji>yellow</kanji> are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/676', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%BB%84', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:42:06Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.842382Z'),
(677, 'kanji', '黒', '黒', 'The radical <radical>black</radical> and the kanji <kanji>black</kanji> are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/677', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%BB%92', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:44:28Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.851861Z'),
(853, 'kanji', '風', '風', 'You look at a <radical>table</radical> and notice it is being used as a <radical>slide</radical> by <radical>insect</radical>s to launch themselves into the <kanji>wind</kanji>.

This kanji can also mean <kanji>style</kanji>. To remember this, consider how styles can change quickly, just like the <kanji>wind</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/853', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A2%A8', 7, 1, '2012-07-19T23:07:00.541433Z', NULL, '2025-10-16T19:51:14.263791Z'),
(893, 'kanji', '映', '映', 'The <radical>sun</radical> in the <radical>center</radical> of the sky gives off its light, which <kanji>reflect</kanji>s off of everything around it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/893', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%98%A0', 7, 1, '2012-08-28T08:15:26.742371Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:25:07.512079Z'),
(1237, 'kanji', '象', '象', 'The <radical>elephant</radical> radical and the <kanji>elephant</kanji> kanji are the same. But! This kanji has another meaning: <kanji>phenomenon</kanji>. If you were the first person to see an elephant, you''d call it that too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1237', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B1%A1', 7, 1, '2012-12-06T03:55:18.947102Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.670823Z'),
(1810, 'kanji', '床', '床', 'Under the <radical>canopy</radical>, you use a <radical>tree</radical> for your <kanji>floor</kanji>. It also becomes your <kanji>bed</kanji>, because hey, what is a floor but the original bed?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1810', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%8A', 7, 1, '2013-07-02T19:14:31.973642Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.446492Z'),
(573, 'kanji', '池', '池', 'The poor <radical>alligator</radical> was taken far inland by a <radical>tsunami</radical>. Now his only source of water is a tiny <kanji>pond</kanji> that barely fits his body, and doesn''t even provide much food.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/573', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B1%A0', 8, 1, '2012-03-05T19:59:05Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.654761Z'),
(616, 'kanji', '次', '次', '<radical>Ice</radical> is what you''re <radical>lack</radical>ing, so the <kanji>next</kanji> thing you need to do is go to the store and see if they have any. Nope, no ice. So, now you go to the next store. Is there ice here? Nope. Next!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/616', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AC%A1', 8, 1, '2012-03-08T07:03:16Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:44:55.096892Z'),
(620, 'kanji', '京', '京', 'Just like the radical, the kanji means <kanji>capital</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/620', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%AC', 8, 1, '2012-03-08T09:08:51Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:17:58.379479Z'),
(678, 'kanji', '支', '支', 'The <radical>branch</radical> radical and the <kanji>branch</kanji> kanji are the same! This kanji also means <kanji>support</kanji>. To remember it, think about a tree with lots of branches. The branches support each other and the trunk of the tree. If you were to climb the tree, the branches would be able to support your weight too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/678', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%94%AF', 8, 1, '2012-04-04T19:55:43Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:19:45.714523Z'),
(679, 'kanji', '住', '住', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of the <radical>master</radical>s gets to choose the place they <kanji>dwell</kanji>. So they <kanji>reside</kanji> in a huge mansion on the top of the mountain, surrounded by all the other masters in their smaller dwellings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/679', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BD%8F', 8, 1, '2012-04-04T20:01:06Z', NULL, '2026-01-19T23:06:39.866127Z'),
(680, 'kanji', '助', '助', 'With <radical>top hat</radical> <radical>power</radical>, you''re able to <kanji>help</kanji> anyone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/680', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8A%A9', 8, 1, '2012-04-04T20:05:23Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.704297Z'),
(681, 'kanji', '医', '医', 'You''re in a <radical>cage</radical> and you''ve been shot by an <radical>arrow</radical>. Obviously things are looking pretty bad. You need some <kanji>medicine</kanji> if you hope to live.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/681', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8C%BB', 8, 1, '2012-04-04T20:09:53Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:36:15.542322Z'),
(682, 'kanji', '君', '君', '<radical>Wolverine</radical> is going down a <radical>slide</radical> and from your <radical>mouth</radical> you scream, "Hey, <kanji>buddy</kanji>! Watch it with that slide, it''s really fast and you might hurt yourself, buddy!" 

You''re a really good buddy to Wolverine. He also happens to be the local ruler of these lands. You make it a habit to befriend the local strongmen, just in case… No wonder you''re so buddy-buddy with all the rulers around here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/682', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%9B', 8, 1, '2012-04-04T20:25:38Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.749146Z'),
(683, 'kanji', '対', '対', '<radical>Writing</radical> and <radical>measurement</radical> stand <kanji>versus</kanji> one another. Writing is all about feeling and expression. Measurement is about cold hard numbers. Yep, in this world writing people are basically the <kanji>opposite</kanji> of measurement people. Which side are you on?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/683', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%BE', 8, 1, '2012-04-04T20:41:45Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.766036Z'),
(684, 'kanji', '局', '局', 'Your nation''s <radical>flag</radical> sits on top of a <radical>cape</radical>d <radical>mouth</radical>. This is the symbol for the "<kanji>Bureau</kanji> of Secret Things." It''s a special <kanji>department</kanji> of your government made to keep things hidden.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/684', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B1%80', 8, 1, '2012-04-04T23:29:07Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.780481Z'),
(685, 'kanji', '役', '役', 'You <radical>loiter</radical> around with a <radical>weapon</radical> because you''re currently doing your <kanji>service</kanji> and <kanji>duty</kanji> to your country. You''re standing guard outside, waiting just in case something will happen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/685', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BD%B9', 8, 1, '2012-04-04T23:32:47Z', NULL, '2026-04-05T19:37:47.91122Z'),
(686, 'kanji', '投', '投', 'In your <radical>fingers</radical> is a <radical>weapon</radical>, so you <kanji>throw</kanji> it. Maybe you''re angry and you threw the weapon at a person you don''t like. Maybe you''re a pacifist and you threw it away so no one else could use it. Either way, when you have a weapon in your fingers you have to throw it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/686', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8A%95', 8, 1, '2012-04-04T23:36:47Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.796801Z'),
(687, 'kanji', '決', '決', 'You have at your disposal a <radical>tsunami</radical> to assassinate this <radical>person</radical>, or a <radical>hook</radical>. Which method will you choose? <kanji>Decide</kanji> now!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/687', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B1%BA', 8, 1, '2012-04-04T23:41:15Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T00:59:25.552609Z'),
(688, 'kanji', '究', '究', 'You dig a <radical>hole</radical> <radical>nine</radical> times for your <kanji>research</kanji>. You should always do research with nine variables so you can compare them, and so there''s an uneven number.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/688', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A9%B6', 8, 1, '2012-04-04T23:46:36Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.81255Z'),
(689, 'kanji', '身', '身', 'When your <radical>self</radical> goes down a <radical>slide</radical> and hits a <radical>barb</radical>, it''s a painful reminder that you have a <kanji>body</kanji>. Sometimes people think their self is just some kind of floating, disembodied consciousness, but getting a barb stuck in <kanji>oneself</kanji> while going down a slide will quickly remind one of one''s bodily existence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/689', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BA%AB', 8, 1, '2012-04-04T23:48:56Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:21:37.396963Z'),
(690, 'kanji', '者', '者', 'The radical and the kanji are exactly the same, so this means <kanji>someone</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/690', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%80%85', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T18:42:58Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:09:06.553001Z'),
(691, 'kanji', '研', '研', 'A <radical>stone</radical> <radical>lantern</radical> is an excellent way to <kanji>sharpen</kanji> anything, even in the dark. Your knives, your forks, your spoons, and even your sword.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/691', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A0%94', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T19:00:40Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.838572Z'),
(692, 'kanji', '馬', '馬', 'The <radical>horse</radical> radical and the <kanji>horse</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Huzzah!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/692', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A6%AC', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T19:02:37Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.846731Z'),
(693, 'kanji', '森', '森', 'This kanji is just the <radical>tree</radical> radical three times. Just based off this, you should be able to figure it out. This kanji means "<kanji>forest</kanji>."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/693', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A3%AE', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T19:05:14Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.854797Z'),
(694, 'kanji', '場', '場', '<radical>Dirt</radical> makes it <radical>easy</radical> to identify your <kanji>location</kanji>. Even if you add an extra line to mark the ground, it''s still easy. Keep that in mind and you can go from <kanji>place</kanji> to place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/694', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A0%B4', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T19:15:13Z', NULL, '2025-12-12T19:57:27.405848Z'),
(695, 'kanji', '朝', '朝', 'There''s a <radical>cross</radical> sticking out the top of the <radical>sun</radical> and another <radical>cross</radical> sticking out the bottom. The <radical>moon</radical> is off to the right. That''s because it''s the <kanji>morning</kanji>, which is when the sun and moon switch over. Those crosses are making sure the sun stays high in the sky for the rest of the day.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/695', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%9D', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T19:24:32Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.871392Z'),
(696, 'kanji', '番', '番', 'You have to <radical>sickle</radical> the <radical>rice paddy</radical> today because it''s your <kanji>turn</kanji>. This kanji also means <kanji>number in a series</kanji>, which is a lot like a turn. For example, you''re number 3, so your turn will be number 3.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/696', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%95%AA', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T19:30:15Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.88179Z'),
(697, 'kanji', '答', '答', '"Does the <radical>bamboo</radical> shirt <radical>suit</radical> me?" you ask... but nobody will <kanji>answer</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/697', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AD%94', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T19:33:33Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.894514Z'),
(698, 'kanji', '絵', '絵', 'When a bunch of <radical>thread</radical>s are <radical>meet</radical>ing on paper (after you''ve dipped them in paint, say) they can create a masterpiece of a <kanji>picture</kanji> (or <kanji>painting</kanji>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/698', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B5%B5', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T19:37:21Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.902626Z'),
(699, 'kanji', '買', '買', 'Here''s a <radical>net</radical> full of of <radical>shellfish</radical> that you can <kanji>buy</kanji>. You can''t just buy loose shellfish. You can only buy the ones that are in the net, bagged up and ready for your money.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/699', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%B7', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T19:40:57Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.910347Z'),
(700, 'kanji', '道', '道', 'Aim the <radical>scooter</radical>''s <radical>neck</radical> at the <kanji>road</kanji>! Scooters are only meant for driving on <kanji>street</kanji>s and <kanji>path</kanji>s, and if you go the wrong <kanji>way</kanji>, you''ll probably break your own neck!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/700', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%93', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T19:50:21Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:25:22.604618Z'),
(701, 'kanji', '間', '間', 'There''s a <radical>gate</radical> that the <radical>sun</radical> goes through at a regular <kanji>interval</kanji>. There is an <kanji>interval of time</kanji> in the sense that the sun goes through it at the same time every day. And there is an <kanji>interval of space</kanji> in the sense that it has to go through the space between the gate posts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/701', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%96%93', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T19:55:51Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:23:35.249604Z'),
(702, 'kanji', '雲', '雲', 'A <radical>rain</radical> <radical>cloud</radical> is still a <kanji>cloud</kanji>! It''s probably going to rain on you soon though, so I hope you have a jacket or an umbrella.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/702', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%B2', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T20:04:57Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.936487Z'),
(703, 'kanji', '数', '数', 'Give <radical>rice</radical> to a <radical>woman</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> so she can <kanji>count</kanji> it. The woman will count each individual grain of rice, then tell you the <kanji>amount</kanji> or <kanji>number</kanji> of grains.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/703', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%95%B0', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T20:14:50Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(704, 'kanji', '楽', '楽', 'The <radical>white</radical> <radical>ice</radical> <radical>tree</radical> is a magical tree that radiates an aura of <kanji>comfort</kanji>, <kanji>ease</kanji>, and <kanji>pleasure</kanji>, with its icy foliage tinkling like beautiful <kanji>music</kanji> whenever it sways in the breeze.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/704', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A5%BD', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:05:12Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:48:57.246886Z'),
(705, 'kanji', '話', '話', '<radical>Say</radical> it with your <radical>tongue</radical>. If you do, you''ll be able to <kanji>talk</kanji>. You''ll be able to <kanji>speak</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/705', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A9%B1', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:15:55Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:22:33.049762Z'),
(706, 'kanji', '電', '電', 'You''re in the <radical>rain</radical>, in a <radical>rice paddy</radical>, holding an <radical>umbrella</radical>. You''re here because there''s nothing else that''s very tall around you. You want to get hit by <kanji>electricity</kanji> (aka lightning), to prove that it exists.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/706', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%BB', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:21:32Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:20:40.00989Z'),
(707, 'kanji', '所', '所', 'You''re tearing this <radical>door</radical> down with an <radical>axe</radical>. You''re absolutely crazy. "I HATE THIS <kanji>PLACE</kanji>!!" you keep yelling as you smash in the door.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/707', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%89%80', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:27:08Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:11:34.848247Z'),
(855, 'kanji', '反', '反', 'Under a <radical>cliff</radical> there''s a <radical>stool</radical>. If someone has a stool deep inside a cliff, they are most likely pretty <kanji>anti</kanji>-society, hiding away like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/855', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%8D', 8, 1, '2012-07-19T23:13:26.554619Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T20:26:10.431954Z'),
(587, 'kanji', '売', '売', 'A <radical>samurai</radical> has come bearing <radical>forehead</radical>s and <radical>legs</radical> that he''s trying to <kanji>sell</kanji>. He thought it was a waste to leave all his slain enemies on the battlefield, so he gathered up their foreheads and legs to sell on the black market for some extra cash. Hey, no judging! A samurai''s gotta sell what a samurai''s gotta sell, okay?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/587', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A3%B2', 9, 1, '2012-03-06T05:50:46Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T21:59:01.806898Z'),
(708, 'kanji', '事', '事', 'You find a <radical>cross</radical> in the <radical>mouth</radical> of <radical>Wolverine</radical>. What a strange <kanji>occurrence</kanji>. I guess he put it there, but why would he do such a <kanji>thing</kanji>? You ask him, but he says it''s a personal <kanji>matter</kanji> of utmost secrecy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/708', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:27:21Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T17:06:15.299548Z'),
(709, 'kanji', '使', '使', 'A good <radical>leader</radical> can put the <radical>ground</radical>''s <radical>history</radical> to great <kanji>use</kanji>. You are a good leader. You have studied the ground and dirt of the earth your entire life. You know the history of the ground and you know how to use it to lead your people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/709', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BD%BF', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:29:43Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.525119Z'),
(710, 'kanji', '具', '具', 'There''s an <radical>eye</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical>, among some <radical>fins</radical>. What happened? You killed a fish, and now you must hide the murder <kanji>tool</kanji>. Quick, hide the tool before someone finds out it was you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/710', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%B7', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:30:11Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T02:54:10.615141Z'),
(711, 'kanji', '受', '受', 'Someone throws a <radical>cleat</radical> at your <radical>forehead</radical> while you sit on a <radical>stool</radical>. You must <kanji>accept</kanji> this. Then another guy cleats you in the forehead, but you remain on the stool and <kanji>receive</kanji> it without complaint. This is some kind of acceptance ritual for the most prestigious Spanish soccer club in the world. If a cleat to the forehead doesn''t knock you off the stool, you''ll receive an offer to join.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/711', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%97', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:30:43Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:34:39.359876Z'),
(712, 'kanji', '和', '和', 'When everyone has <radical>grain</radical> in their <radical>mouth</radical>, you will have <kanji>peace</kanji>. That is the <kanji>Japanese style</kanji> of doing things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/712', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%92%8C', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:31:16Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.55685Z'),
(714, 'kanji', '定', '定', 'You build a <radical>roof</radical> over your <radical>coat rack</radical> because you <kanji>determine</kanji> that this is the best way to protect your coats from the elements.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/714', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%9A', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:32:25Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.184134Z'),
(715, 'kanji', '実', '実', 'Climb onto the <radical>roof</radical> in <radical>spring</radical> to see the <kanji>truth</kanji>. The world is waking back up from winter and revealing the <kanji>reality</kanji> of what it is all around you. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/715', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%9F', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:32:49Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:38:30.618447Z'),
(716, 'kanji', '服', '服', 'Take the <radical>moon</radical> <radical>stamp</radical> on the <radical>stool</radical> in front of you and use it on all of your <kanji>clothes</kanji>. Now even if you sell or lose a piece of <kanji>clothing</kanji>, you''ll be able to find it again by checking for your special moon stamp.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/716', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%8D', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:34:04Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.587378Z'),
(717, 'kanji', '泳', '泳', 'If a <radical>tsunami</radical> is going to last an <radical>eternity</radical> you better learn how to <kanji>swim</kanji> now. We''ll be under water for the rest of time and if you can''t swim you''re not gonna make it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/717', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B3%B3', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:34:39Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.5979Z'),
(718, 'kanji', '物', '物', 'A <radical>cow</radical> with <radical>wing</radical>s is flying in the air. What a strange <kanji>thing</kanji>! You''ve heard of flying pigs before, but cows? This is a new and strange thing indeed! Cows with wings! What a silly thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/718', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%89%A9', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:35:06Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:12:29.310075Z'),
(719, 'kanji', '苦', '苦', 'The <radical>flowers</radical> have grown <radical>old</radical>, so they''re <kanji>suffering</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/719', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8B%A6', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:35:30Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:13:37.694956Z'),
(720, 'kanji', '表', '表', 'Sometimes <radical>life</radical> gives you a <radical>kick</radical>, and you have to <kanji>express</kanji> yourself to deal with it! How do you express yourself? On the <kanji>surface</kanji> (of your face)!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/720', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A1%A8', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:35:53Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:26:46.142178Z'),
(721, 'kanji', '部', '部', 'The <radical>clown</radical> <radical>building</radical> is over in that <kanji>part</kanji> of town. We keep the clown part as separate as we can from the rest of the normal people. In fact, the entire clown education <kanji>department</kanji> and their clown <kanji>club</kanji>s have to stay in their part, they can''t come in ours.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/721', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%83%A8', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:37:32Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.938188Z'),
(722, 'kanji', '乗', '乗', 'You put a <radical>grain</radical> in your <radical>mouth</radical> and let it take a <kanji>ride</kanji>. This kanji has the same radicals as 和, but this time the grain is <i>in</i> your mouth, which is why there''s little pieces of grain sticking out of the 口 part ;)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/722', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B9%97', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:38:05Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T00:54:10.43767Z'),
(723, 'kanji', '客', '客', 'Under your <radical>roof</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> there are more <radical>mouth</radical>s to feed because you let <kanji>guest</kanji>s stay with you when it''s cold. Some of them aren''t family, so they have to pay to stay under your roof and get food. Those are <kanji>customer</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/723', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%A2', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:39:07Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.675994Z'),
(724, 'kanji', '屋', '屋', 'You plant a <radical>flag</radical> in the <radical>ground</radical>, marking your <radical>private</radical> patch of <radical>dirt</radical>. In this spot you raise a <kanji>roof</kanji>, then fill the space beneath it with some <kanji>shop</kanji>s and <kanji>store</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/724', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B1%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:39:59Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:45:53.16575Z'),
(725, 'kanji', '度', '度', 'A <radical>yurt</radical>''s <radical>stool</radical> is always placed at certain <kanji>degree</kanji>s. The degrees change depending on specific <kanji>occurrence</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/725', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%A6', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:40:26Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T00:23:35.599461Z'),
(727, 'kanji', '持', '持', 'You need to get your <radical>fingers</radical> around a <radical>temple</radical> if you want to <kanji>hold</kanji> it. Is the temple too big? Hold smaller temples you can buy in the temple''s souvenir shop.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/727', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8C%81', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:41:49Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:51:21.986281Z'),
(728, 'kanji', '界', '界', 'Every <radical>rice paddy</radical> sits <radical>jammed in</radical> to the <kanji>world</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/728', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%95%8C', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:42:17Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:27:04.174252Z'),
(729, 'kanji', '発', '発', 'Go to the <radical>tent</radical> and take the <radical>lantern</radical> from it, then we will be ready for <kanji>departure</kanji>. We can''t leave the lantern inside of the tent; that''s a fire hazard. We''d best take it with us before the departure. Don''t you agree?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/729', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%99%BA', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:42:40Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:49:29.051166Z'),
(730, 'kanji', '相', '相', 'You need to look a <radical>tree</radical> in the <radical>eye</radical> to gain a <kanji>mutual</kanji> respect for each other. Stand up straight, look the tree directly in its eye(s). If it looks right back, you know things are mutual.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/730', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9B%B8', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:43:07Z', NULL, '2026-02-19T09:33:02.586646Z'),
(731, 'kanji', '県', '県', 'The <radical>eye</radical> of a <radical>lion</radical>, still <radical>small</radical> and curious, gazes upon the <kanji>prefecture</kanji> that will one day become his. He''s a small lion, but once he grows up, everything reflected in his eye will be his prefecture to rule over.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/731', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9C%8C', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:43:29Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:25:07.695556Z'),
(732, 'kanji', '美', '美', 'A <radical>sheep</radical> that''s <radical>big</radical> is a true <kanji>beauty</kanji>. Big is <kanji>beautiful</kanji>, and that''s all the more true for sheep.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/732', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BE%8E', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:43:52Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.79204Z'),
(733, 'kanji', '負', '負', 'When you''re in <radical>prison</radical>, whoever ends up with the <radical>shellfish</radical> will <kanji>lose</kanji>. It''s the game all prisoners play. It''s like keep away from the shellfish. And if you end up with the shellfish BZZZZTT that''s it, you''re the loser.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/733', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%A0', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:44:20Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(734, 'kanji', '送', '送', 'On its <radical>scooter</radical>, a creature with <radical>horns</radical> from <radical>heaven</radical> will take things you want to <kanji>send</kanji> there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/734', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%81', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:44:42Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:26:48.513281Z'),
(735, 'kanji', '重', '重', 'The <radical>heavy</radical> radical and the <kanji>heavy</kanji> kanji are the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/735', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%87%8D', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:45:03Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T06:13:13.939122Z'),
(736, 'kanji', '談', '談', 'You  <radical>say</radical> things around a  <radical>fire</radical>. In fact there are two  <radical>fire</radical>s, and you''re <kanji>talk</kanji>ing with a group of people, all sitting around two nice campfires.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/736', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AB%87', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:45:58Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:27:49.129396Z'),
(737, 'kanji', '要', '要', 'When a <radical>helicopter</radical> is piloted by a <radical>woman</radical>, it stirs an insatiable <kanji>need</kanji> in her. She doesn''t know what it is. But she needs it. And that''s why she''s flying around looking for it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/737', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A6%81', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:46:23Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(738, 'kanji', '勝', '勝', 'The <radical>moon</radical> grants a <radical>gladiator</radical> its <radical>power</radical> so he will <kanji>win</kanji>! <kanji>Victory</kanji> will be his!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/738', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8B%9D', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:46:47Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:28:25.718985Z'),
(739, 'kanji', '仮', '仮', 'Your <radical>leader</radical> is very <radical>anti</radical>-everything, making it all <kanji>temporary</kanji>. You get a new shirt, "I''m anti shirts!" she yells, and out it goes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/739', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%AE', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:48:27Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.886875Z'),
(854, 'kanji', '予', '予', 'What does your <radical>mama</radical> tell you to do in the <radical>street</radical>? Look before you cross! You gotta do it <kanji>beforehand</kanji>, or it could be too late.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/854', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%88', 9, 1, '2012-07-19T23:10:10.460336Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.895442Z'),
(857, 'kanji', '新', '新', 'There''s a <radical>stand</radical>ing <radical>tree</radical> that you''re taking an <radical>axe</radical> to. You''re going to cut down this tree to let a <kanji>new</kanji> tree grow.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/857', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%96%B0', 9, 1, '2012-07-25T18:34:28.888793Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T04:17:56.440105Z'),
(858, 'kanji', '返', '返', 'Your <radical>scooter</radical> is <radical>anti</radical> scoot. It doesn''t want to run. So you have to <kanji>return</kanji> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/858', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BF%94', 9, 1, '2012-08-27T17:19:33.986911Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:36:02.75854Z'),
(890, 'kanji', '像', '像', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of the <radical>elephant</radical>s is so great, they put up an <kanji>image</kanji> or <kanji>statue</kanji> of him everywhere. Every house, every street corner — his image looks down on all the other elephants at all times.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/890', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%83%8F', 9, 1, '2012-08-28T08:10:45.953765Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.876802Z'),
(983, 'kanji', '保', '保', 'The <radical>mouth</radical> of a <radical>tree</radical> <radical>leader</radical> is all about trying to <kanji>preserve</kanji> the trees. In fact, she <kanji>guarantee</kanji>s that she will preserve the trees if it''s the last thing she does.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/983', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BF%9D', 9, 1, '2012-09-20T18:47:40.83124Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.922985Z'),
(1029, 'kanji', '試', '試', 'If you want to <radical>say</radical> something at a <radical>ceremony</radical> for <radical>construction</radical> workers you''re going to have to <kanji>try</kanji> really hard. Construction is loud, so if you want to be heard you have to <kanji>attempt</kanji> to be louder than the drilling and smashing and digging and handing out of awards.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1029', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A9%A6', 9, 1, '2012-10-31T23:47:37.780722Z', NULL, '2026-01-05T08:21:15.235162Z'),
(1048, 'kanji', '験', '験', 'A <radical>horse</radical> and a <radical>squid</radical> are going to <kanji>test</kanji> you. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1048', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A8%93', 9, 1, '2012-10-31T23:57:59.276096Z', NULL, '2026-01-05T08:21:15.235162Z'),
(1088, 'kanji', '比', '比', 'The <radical>compare</radical> radical and the <kanji>compare</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Huzzah!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1088', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AF%94', 9, 1, '2012-11-01T03:33:55.334382Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.813395Z'),
(1880, 'kanji', '辛', '辛', 'The <radical>spicy</radical> radical and <kanji>spicy</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Hot!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1880', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BE%9B', 9, 1, '2013-07-02T22:11:26.179235Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:24:37.385296Z'),
(465, 'kanji', '了', '了', 'Think about it this way. There is a famous fishing lure inventor, working on his best work ever. He’s old, and he’s been trying to make the best fishing lure for the last 50 years, and knows this will be his last invention. Then he figures it out. He takes one <radical>barb</radical>, and connects the end of it to the top of another <radical>barb</radical>. When he does this, he knows his work is finally <kanji>finish</kanji>ed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/465', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%86', 10, 1, '2012-02-28T23:47:00Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.511434Z'),
(499, 'kanji', '公', '公', 'You have <radical>fins</radical> in <radical>private</radical>. But, you want the world to see them, so you take them <kanji>public</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/499', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%AC', 10, 1, '2012-03-01T02:26:43Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.188998Z'),
(630, 'kanji', '直', '直', 'This kanji is the same as the radical — <kanji>straight</kanji> as straight can be.

It can also mean <kanji>direct</kanji>, because when you go straight to a place, you''re going there directly. Depending on the context it can even mean <kanji>fix</kanji>, since fixing something means straightening out all the messed up crooked bits.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/630', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9B%B4', 10, 1, '2012-03-08T23:48:10Z', NULL, '2026-03-07T17:32:28.468567Z'),
(713, 'kanji', '始', '始', 'If you give a <radical>woman</radical> a <radical>machine</radical> she''ll <kanji>begin</kanji> learning how to use it right away. She''ll also get it to <kanji>start</kanji> before anyone else could. Women are really good at starting up new machines!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/713', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A7%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-23T23:32:00Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(740, 'kanji', '起', '起', '<radical>Run</radical> into <radical>oneself</radical> in a dream and you''ll definitely <kanji>wake up</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/740', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B5%B7', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:15:37Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:29:09.650142Z'),
(741, 'kanji', '速', '速', 'You jump on your <radical>scooter</radical> with a big <radical>bundle</radical> to go as <kanji>fast</kanji> as you can! You have a bundle of pizzas in here, and they need to be delivered as fast as you can get them there!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/741', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%9F', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:16:02Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.233108Z'),
(742, 'kanji', '配', '配', 'You don''t want to keep all the <radical>alcohol</radical> to yourself (<radical>oneself</radical>), so you <kanji>distribute</kanji> it to all of your friends. Heck, not just your friends, to everyone you can find!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/742', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%85%8D', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:17:04Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.243256Z'),
(743, 'kanji', '酒', '酒', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> is an <radical>alcohol</radical>ic beverage containing a lot of <kanji>alcohol</kanji>. It’s 3 different kinds of rums, pineapple juice, and grenadine. It’ll hit you like a tsunami though. This alcoholic drink has a kick to it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/743', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%85%92', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:17:25Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:12:00.952167Z'),
(744, 'kanji', '院', '院', 'This <radical>building</radical> has a <radical>roof</radical> and will teach you about the <radical>origin</radical> of many things. It''s an <kanji>institution</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/744', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%A2', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:17:47Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:08:14.038992Z'),
(745, 'kanji', '終', '終', 'The <radical>thread</radical> of <radical>winter</radical> and <radical>ice</radical> has brought this year to its <kanji>end</kanji>. Once you''re in the long thread of winter and ice, you know the year will end soon.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/745', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B5%82', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:18:14Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:21:37.396963Z'),
(746, 'kanji', '習', '習', 'You find some <radical>feathers</radical> that are so perfectly <radical>white</radical>, you want to <kanji>learn</kanji> more about where they came from and what they''re made of.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/746', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BF%92', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:19:07Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.321908Z'),
(747, 'kanji', '転', '転', 'If your <radical>car</radical> runs into a <radical>cloud</radical> you''ll <kanji>revolve</kanji> around it instead of going straight through. Clouds are really hard to see through, so even though you''re trying not to, you revolve in a tight circle up and around the cloud anyway.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/747', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BB%A2', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:19:32Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T22:12:12.62933Z'),
(748, 'kanji', '進', '進', 'You give your <radical>scooter</radical> to a <radical>turkey</radical> so that it can <kanji>advance</kanji>. The turkey was trying and failing to walk through a crowded street, so out of the goodness of your heart you gave it a scooter so it could advance.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/748', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%B2', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:19:54Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.365892Z'),
(749, 'kanji', '落', '落', '<radical>Flowers</radical> are knocked down by a violent <radical>tsunami</radical>, and swirl through the air to <radical>kiss</radical> your face gently as they <kanji>fall</kanji>. With the tsunami came a great windy storm that blew the flowers down. Amongst all the destruction, it''s a pretty sight to watch them fall.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/749', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%90%BD', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:20:16Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T17:44:11.29193Z'),
(750, 'kanji', '葉', '葉', 'All the <radical>flowers</radical> in the <radical>world</radical> of <radical>tree</radical>s owe their existence to the humble <kanji>leaf</kanji>. That''s because leaves are how trees, and therefore flowers, get their nutrients from the sun.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/750', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%91%89', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:20:44Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:10:47.626239Z'),
(751, 'kanji', '軽', '軽', 'For a <radical>car</radical>, a <radical>tombstone</radical> is <kanji>lightweight</kanji>. Cars are very strong, you see, so something heavy like a tombstone is no match for them. All cars find tombstones to be <kanji>light</kanji> and easy to transport.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/751', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BB%BD', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:21:15Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T22:12:12.620324Z'),
(752, 'kanji', '運', '運', 'While riding your <radical>scooter</radical>, you bang your <radical>forehead</radical> on the <radical>car</radical> in front. Your scooter''s wrecked, so you have to <kanji>carry</kanji> it home. You''ll need a lot of <kanji>luck</kanji> to get that scooter home safely.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/752', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:22:19Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.438974Z'),
(753, 'kanji', '開', '開', 'A <radical>gate</radical> with a <radical>lantern</radical> is <kanji>open</kanji> for you. That''s convenient, since it''s pretty dark out and you might not have seen the open gate without the lantern''s help.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/753', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%96%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:22:51Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:43:14.26378Z'),
(754, 'kanji', '集', '集', 'You chase a <radical>turkey</radical> up a <radical>tree</radical> to <kanji>collect</kanji> it. At least, you will once you''ve <kanji>gather</kanji>ed enough turkeys into this one tree.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/754', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%86', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:24:06Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.458115Z'),
(755, 'kanji', '飲', '飲', 'You can <radical>eat</radical> all you want, but you''ll still <radical>lack</radical> something to <kanji>drink</kanji>. You can''t live just from eating, you have to drink things too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/755', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A3%B2', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:24:38Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:09:36.889504Z'),
(756, 'kanji', '業', '業', 'You have <radical>spikes</radical> growing out of a <radical>sheep</radical> with <radical>fins</radical>. Turn this thing into a <kanji>business</kanji>! You could totally charge people money to look at it. Sure, you could also donate it to science, but a business-minded person like you knows a business opportunity when you see one. Step right up! Just five dollars for a glance at the rare spiky mer-sheep!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/756', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A5%AD', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:25:02Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.474851Z'),
(757, 'kanji', '漢', '漢', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> hit ancient <radical>China</radical> and formed the <kanji>Chinese</kanji> country we know now as... <kanji>China</kanji>!

Sometimes this kanji also means <kanji>man</kanji>. In fact, it used to refer specifically to people of the Han dynasty, but over time evolved into a term for men in specific contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/757', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BC%A2', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:25:29Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T07:44:04.517536Z'),
(758, 'kanji', '路', '路', 'If you''ve been walking all day, your <radical>foot</radical> is the last thing anyone would want to <radical>kiss</radical>, especially after wandering barefoot down a <kanji>road</kanji>. Your feet are filthy, sweaty, and stinky. Nobody is going to want to kiss those feet.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/758', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B7%AF', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:25:50Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:20:45.121776Z'),
(759, 'kanji', '農', '農', 'You blast your <radical>music</radical> loud so it causes a <radical>landslide</radical>, revealing the perfect soil for <kanji>farming</kanji>! Musically moved dirt is perfect for <kanji>agriculture</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/759', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BE%B2', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:28:13Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.520584Z'),
(760, 'kanji', '鉄', '鉄', 'You look at some <radical>gold</radical> and notice a <radical>fault</radical> in it. You scrape at the fault and see that it''s just sprayed with gold paint. It''s not gold, it''s (comparatively) worthless <kanji>iron</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/760', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%89%84', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:28:45Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.53062Z'),
(761, 'kanji', '歌', '歌', 'If a <radical>lip ring</radical> (or two in this case) is what you <radical>lack</radical>, it''s going to be way easier for you to <kanji>sing</kanji> a <kanji>song</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/761', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AD%8C', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:29:13Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.540389Z'),
(762, 'kanji', '算', '算', 'If someone sticks <radical>bamboo</radical> in your <radical>eye</radical> <radical>twenty</radical> times, the first thing your brain does is <kanji>calculate</kanji> how painful it is. Your brain''s pain <kanji>calculation</kanji> will determine how you calculate your next move.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/762', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AE%97', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:29:43Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T15:37:55.097388Z'),
(763, 'kanji', '聞', '聞', 'If you go up to a <radical>gate</radical> and press your <radical>ear</radical> to it, you''ll <kanji>hear</kanji> what''s going on on the other side. The people on the other side will have no idea you can hear their conversation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/763', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%81%9E', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:30:10Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.561224Z'),
(764, 'kanji', '語', '語', 'What you <radical>say</radical> between <radical>five</radical> <radical>mouth</radical>s is a <kanji>language</kanji>. As long as there are five different people saying these same words with their five mouths, it''s considered its own language.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/764', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AA%9E', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:30:40Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:55:24.656516Z'),
(765, 'kanji', '読', '読', 'You have to <radical>say</radical> what you’re <radical>sell</radical>ing because people don’t like to <kanji>read</kanji>. If they walk right by and don’t read, you better say what you have for sale so they’ll know! Read it out loud to them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/765', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AA%AD', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:31:39Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.578979Z'),
(766, 'kanji', '鳴', '鳴', 'When the <radical>mouth</radical> of a <radical>bird</radical> opens, you''re most likely to hear a <kanji>chirp</kanji>. This is how birds communicate. They open their mouths and let out a series of chirps. Chirp chirp chirp!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/766', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%B3%B4', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:31:59Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:02:01.799026Z'),
(767, 'kanji', '線', '線', 'You can use a long piece of <radical>thread</radical> during <radical>white</radical> <radical>water</radical> rafting as a safety <kanji>line</kanji>. Sure it''s thin, but if someone goes overboard even a line of thread is better than nothing. Throw them a line and drag them back aboard!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/767', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B7%9A', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:32:21Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T06:27:45.468433Z'),
(768, 'kanji', '横', '横', 'The <radical>tree</radical> marked with a <radical>yellow</radical> X on the <kanji>side</kanji> of the house is going to get cut down. You’ve marked it with a yellow X so the hired help knows which tree to cut down. There are a lot of trees on the side of the house, but the tree marked with a yellow X is the only one in danger of causing damage to your roof in the event of a storm.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/768', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A8%AA', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:32:41Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.649825Z'),
(769, 'kanji', '調', '調', 'You <radical>say</radical> something about the <radical>circumference</radical> of this area. You''ve looked around this whole area, making a big circle around it, because you are trying to <kanji>investigate</kanji> something that happened here. You also say this thing in a really quiet <kanji>tone</kanji> of voice, because you don''t want the person or thing you''re investigating to know you''re on to them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/769', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AA%BF', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:33:39Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.679613Z'),
(770, 'kanji', '親', '親', 'If you''re <radical>stand</radical>ing under a <radical>tree</radical> and you <radical>see</radical> your <kanji>parent</kanji>, it''s a good chance for a moment of <kanji>intimacy</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/770', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A6%AA', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:34:00Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.692617Z'),
(771, 'kanji', '頭', '頭', 'If you feed a lot of <radical>beans</radical> to a <radical>geoduck</radical>, its <kanji>head</kanji> will grow larger. To a geoduck, beans are a brain food, so by eating beans, it can grow an incredibly large head. Geoducks already look strange enough as it is, let alone with engorged heads.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/771', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A0%AD', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:34:23Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:47:27.492256Z'),
(772, 'kanji', '顔', '顔', 'You <radical>stand</radical> on a <radical>cliff</radical>, a <radical>hair</radical>''s breadth away from the edge, when out of nowhere a <radical>geoduck</radical> slaps you in the <kanji>face</kanji>. The force makes you back away from the edge of the cliff, and your face is all slimy from the geoduck''s residue.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/772', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A1%94', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:45:52Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:38:30.618447Z'),
(773, 'kanji', '病', '病', 'A <radical>sick</radical> stick of <radical>dynamite</radical> is going to be <kanji>sick</kanji> forever. Unfortunately there are no medicines that are effective on sticks of dynamite, so once they contract a sickness, they’re sick for good.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/773', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%97%85', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:49:53Z', NULL, '2026-03-05T17:25:29.943045Z'),
(774, 'kanji', '最', '最', 'Let the <radical>sun</radical> shine into your <radical>ear</radical> while sitting on a <radical>stool</radical> and make sure <kanji>most</kanji> of it gets in your head. You want to fill your brain with the most sun anyone ever has.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/774', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%80', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:50:20Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T17:51:33.407735Z'),
(913, 'kanji', '求', '求', 'Look at the <radical>ground</radical>! You spilled some <radical>water</radical> <radical>drop</radical>s, so I <kanji>request</kanji> that you wipe them up. I''d do it myself, but you''re the one who spilled them, so I''m requesting that you do it. Pretty reasonable request, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/913', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B1%82', 10, 1, '2012-09-18T23:49:12.672561Z', NULL, '2026-03-01T17:02:02.513202Z'),
(1150, 'kanji', '解', '解', '<radical>Angle</radical> your <radical>sword</radical> correctly at this <radical>cow</radical> to <kanji>solve</kanji> its riddle. The key is to <kanji>untie</kanji> the cow with the sword without hurting it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1150', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A7%A3', 10, 1, '2012-12-04T06:27:00.71387Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:37:01.42735Z'),
(775, 'kanji', '争', '争', 'In <radical>prison</radical>, <radical>Wolverine</radical> gets picked on for the <radical>barb</radical>s coming out of his hands. This creates a lot of <kanji>conflict</kanji> between him and the other prisoners. They don''t take kindly to mutants, and Wolverine doesn''t take kindly to bigots — hence the conflict.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/775', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%89', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T05:58:10Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(776, 'kanji', '仲', '仲', 'A <radical>leader</radical> who takes the <radical>middle</radical> of the road on every issue is likely to have an average <kanji>relationship</kanji> with everyone. He doesn''t really take sides in matters, and just stays in the middle. This makes him seem like a wishy-washy people-pleaser, and as such he''s only able to maintain average, mid-tier relationships.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/776', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%B2', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:03:06Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T07:25:16.586893Z'),
(777, 'kanji', '伝', '伝', 'The <radical>leader</radical> has a <radical>cloud</radical> that he uses to <kanji>transmit</kanji> all of the messages he needs to send to his people. The cloud floats above him and when he wants to tell people something he just whispers into the cloud and it transmits his message really, really loudly to all of them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/777', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BC%9D', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:03:33Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.488956Z'),
(778, 'kanji', '共', '共', 'The card game fish are best at is <radical>blackjack</radical> because they can easily hold the cards with their <radical>fins</radical> <kanji>together</kanji>. In blackjack you don''t have to have a huge hand, full of cards. It''s just a few, so fish fins can keep them together and just hit or stay.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/778', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%B1', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:04:06Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:31:02.32832Z'),
(779, 'kanji', '好', '好', 'There is a <radical>woman</radical> and a <radical>child</radical> in front of you. You <kanji>like</kanji> them very much.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/779', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A5%BD', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:04:39Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(780, 'kanji', '成', '成', 'When a <radical>drunkard</radical> holds a <radical>sword</radical> it <kanji>becomes</kanji> something else. The drunkard doesn''t become a swordsman, they''re still just a drunkard. But the sword in their hands does.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/780', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%88%90', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:06:04Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:58:11.888666Z'),
(781, 'kanji', '老', '老', 'By the time you finish digging a <radical>coffin</radical> hole with nothing but a <radical>spoon</radical>, you’ll be an old <kanji>elderly</kanji> person. Digging with nothing but a spoon takes a long time, especially when the hole needs to be big enough for a coffin. You might as well be digging it for yourself at this rate. By the time you finish, you’ll be all wrinkled and elderly and just about ready to keel over.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/781', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%80%81', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:06:29Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.253358Z'),
(782, 'kanji', '位', '位', 'A <radical>leader</radical> <radical>stand</radical>s at the highest <kanji>rank</kanji> in an organization. The leader is of the highest rank and stands above all others.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/782', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BD%8D', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:06:56Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.260751Z'),
(783, 'kanji', '低', '低', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of your <radical>clan</radical> wants to live close to the <radical>ground</radical> so he demands you get <kanji>low</kanji>. Low, low, with your belly to the ground. That way you''re always below him, since he''s the leader.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/783', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BD%8E', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:07:32Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.268834Z'),
(784, 'kanji', '初', '初', 'You wear a special <radical>outfit</radical> and equip a <radical>sword</radical> whenever you do something for the <kanji>first</kanji> time. Doing something for the first time is always scary, but the right outfit and a sword at your side can give you the confidence and courage to overcome your jitters!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/784', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%9D', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:07:52Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T17:51:33.407735Z'),
(785, 'kanji', '別', '別', 'In your <radical>mouth</radical> you put a <radical>sword</radical> and a <radical>knife</radical>. Now that you have them in there, you have to make them <kanji>separate</kanji> from each other.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/785', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%A5', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:08:16Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:26:33.154996Z'),
(786, 'kanji', '利', '利', 'Cut <radical>grain</radical>s with a <radical>knife</radical> to make them smaller and you''ll make a great <kanji>profit</kanji>. By cutting them, now you can get three grains worth of money for what was once a single grain. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/786', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%A9', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:08:36Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.277392Z'),
(787, 'kanji', '努', '努', 'A <radical>woman</radical> sits on a <radical>stool</radical> to conserve her <radical>power</radical>, as her servants <kanji>toil</kanji> away in the kitchen. They toil away preparing a feast for this powerful woman while she kicks back on her cozy stool. She''s a queen and deserves to be pampered.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/787', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8A%AA', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:08:59Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(788, 'kanji', '労', '労', 'A <radical>Viking</radical> in a position of <radical>power</radical> does not need to <kanji>labor</kanji> in the fields. Labor is for the weak! A Viking of power takes what he wants, and need not labor for it. A powerful Viking plunders all and takes what he wants!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/788', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8A%B4', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:09:20Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.29177Z'),
(789, 'kanji', '命', '命', '<radical>Orders</radical> coming from your <radical>mouth</radical> will determine the <kanji>fate</kanji> of your soldiers. You are their commanding officer and your orders determine their <kanji>life</kanji> and death.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/789', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%91%BD', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:09:55Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:27:09.777362Z'),
(790, 'kanji', '岸', '岸', 'The <radical>mountain</radical> on top of the <radical>cliff</radical> is <radical>dry</radical> from the <kanji>coast</kanji> below. Just from looking at the raging <kanji>shore</kanji> below, you''d think everything would be wet, it''s splashing everywhere!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/790', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B2%B8', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:10:27Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:03:41.305988Z'),
(791, 'kanji', '放', '放', 'As the year starts moving in the <radical>direction</radical> of <radical>winter</radical>, the trees must <kanji>release</kanji> their leaves. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/791', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%94%BE', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:11:04Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T02:08:38.882931Z'),
(792, 'kanji', '昔', '昔', 'You''ve been playing <radical>blackjack</radical> in the <radical>sun</radical> since <kanji>long ago</kanji>. This is the longest game of blackjack you''ve ever played and it feels like ages since you started. You''ve been sitting out in the sun playing blackjack since long, long ago.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/792', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%98%94', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:11:32Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:38:35.659939Z'),
(793, 'kanji', '波', '波', 'What part of a <radical>tsunami</radical> do you feel with your <radical>skin</radical>? The <kanji>wave</kanji>s. Tsunamis are accompanied by all kinds of terrifying sights and sounds, but what you feel on your skin are the powerful waves.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/793', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B3%A2', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:11:56Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:11:56.915355Z'),
(794, 'kanji', '注', '注', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> <radical>master</radical> has the power to <kanji>pour</kanji> water wherever he pleases. Tsunami masters are masters over the most powerful water force in the universe, so it makes sense that they have the ability to pour water at will, wherever they please. Best not tick off the master, unless you want a tsunami poured on your head.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/794', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B3%A8', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:12:15Z', NULL, '2025-12-23T01:34:26.950569Z'),
(795, 'kanji', '育', '育', 'Give <radical>trash</radical> to the <radical>moon</radical> and it will <kanji>nurture</kanji> and <kanji>raise</kanji> that trash like it''s its own little moon baby.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/795', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%82%B2', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:12:46Z', NULL, '2026-01-09T21:16:37.421694Z'),
(796, 'kanji', '拾', '拾', 'Your <radical>fingers</radical> always seem to <radical>suit</radical> the random things you <kanji>pick up</kanji> off the ground. An old thimble. A discarded onion ring. A dead worm you tie in a knot. Your incredible fingers suit them beautifully, almost as if they were jewelry, so of course you pick up all of them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/796', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8B%BE', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:13:48Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T03:32:06.765414Z'),
(797, 'kanji', '指', '指', 'Try to use your <radical>fingers</radical> to <radical>spoon</radical> out some of the <radical>sun</radical> and you''ll come back with just one <kanji>finger</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/797', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8C%87', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:14:15Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:49:02.001164Z'),
(798, 'kanji', '洋', '洋', 'This <radical>tsunami</radical> is ridden by a <radical>sheep</radical> in classic <kanji>Western style</kanji>. And by Western style I mean the sheep is dressed in a cowboy outfit and has put a saddle atop the tsunami wave to ride it. It''s a tsunami rodeo and this crazy sheep is riding that tsunami wave in classic Western style.

This kanji also means <kanji>ocean</kanji>, because presumably the people from the West, with their Western style, came from across the ocean. You''ll want to be familiar with both meanings for vocab later on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/798', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B4%8B', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:14:39Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:01:29.816844Z'),
(799, 'kanji', '神', '神', 'Your <radical>spirit</radical> should feel <radical>humble</radical> before a <kanji>god</kanji>''s <kanji>spirit</kanji>. You never really feel humble in front of anyone else, but if you meet a god''s spirit… Well, you should probably lower yourself appropriately. Gods have powerful spirits.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/799', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A5%9E', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:15:05Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T06:35:40.449797Z'),
(800, 'kanji', '秒', '秒', '<radical>Grain</radical>s are <radical>few</radical>. Imagine the last few grains trickling down the hourglass. You only have a <kanji>second</kanji> left.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/800', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A7%92', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:16:35Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:11:59.650433Z'),
(801, 'kanji', '級', '級', 'There are pieces of <radical>thread</radical> on each <radical>escalator</radical> showing you what <kanji>level</kanji> they go to in your school. Each escalator takes you to a different <kanji>grade</kanji>, so make sure you follow the right thread or you''ll end up in a class that''s either way above or way below your level.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/801', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%9A', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:18:47Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:21:55.394246Z'),
(802, 'kanji', '追', '追', 'On a <radical>scooter</radical> there is a <radical>bear</radical> with a <radical>drop</radical> on its head trying to <kanji>follow</kanji> and <kanji>chase</kanji> you! You accidentally splashed it with water and it''s not happy about it. You''re on your own scooter, luckily, but that doesn''t make this any less terrifying.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/802', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BF%BD', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:19:16Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:00:07.209894Z'),
(803, 'kanji', '戦', '戦', 'A <radical>simple</radical> <radical>drunkard</radical> starting a <kanji>war</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/803', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%88%A6', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:21:36Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:07:27.262661Z'),
(804, 'kanji', '競', '競', '<radical>Stand</radical> on your <radical>older brother</radical>. Now <radical>stand</radical> on your <radical>older brother</radical> again. You wish to stand on, and above, your older brother. You <kanji>compete</kanji> over and over again, to stand atop your older brother.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/804', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AB%B6', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:22:02Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:24:00.678512Z'),
(805, 'kanji', '良', '良', 'A <radical>drop</radical> of water to a <radical>root</radical> is <kanji>good</kanji>. It''s good for the roots to grow and live and thrive. And what''s good for roots is good for our plants and us humans too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/805', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%89%AF', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:22:28Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:44:44.742923Z'),
(806, 'kanji', '功', '功', 'Using your <radical>construction</radical> <radical>power</radical> you build something great. A true <kanji>achievement</kanji> built for the achievement of making kanji learning easier!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/806', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8A%9F', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:22:48Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.423049Z'),
(807, 'kanji', '特', '特', 'This <radical>cow</radical> <radical>temple</radical> is <kanji>special</kanji>. This cow must have been very important to have an entire temple dedicated to it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/807', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%89%B9', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:23:11Z', NULL, '2026-03-01T17:18:39.376699Z'),
(808, 'kanji', '便', '便', 'A <radical>leader</radical> stands upon the <radical>ground</radical> of the <radical>rice paddy</radical>. She is searching for <radical>treasure</radical>. It is said that this treasure here in this rice field will make things much easier for the leader (that''s what leaders really want, after all). If she gets this treasure, it will be a great <kanji>convenience</kanji> for her, because convenience is everything when you''re the leader.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/808', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BE%BF', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:23:46Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.430473Z'),
(809, 'kanji', '働', '働', 'Your <radical>leader</radical> makes you lift <radical>heavy</radical> loads with all your <radical>power</radical>, since this is what you do for <kanji>work</kanji>. Why do you do this kind of work? Well, you''re good at it and the leader makes you do it, I guess.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/809', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%83%8D', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:24:37Z', NULL, '2026-02-16T09:49:50.62199Z'),
(810, 'kanji', '令', '令', 'If you know the radical meaning <radical>orders</radical> you know the kanji meaning <kanji>orders</kanji> as well!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/810', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%A4', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:25:01Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:27:09.777362Z'),
(811, 'kanji', '意', '意', 'The <radical>sound</radical> of your <radical>heart</radical> gives me an <kanji>idea</kanji>. The methodic thump thump thumping of a heart puts me in a trance that gives me great ideas. It’s a very inspirational sound, the sound of a heart is. It gives me an idea. 

Alternatively, you could look at this kanji and see a "stand" radical and something that kind of (almost) looks like the kanji for "think." When you stand up and think, you come up with your very own <kanji>idea</kanji>s!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/811', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%84%8F', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:25:28Z', NULL, '2026-03-03T17:01:31.848162Z'),
(812, 'kanji', '味', '味', 'In your <radical>mouth</radical> there is a <radical>jet</radical> dropping <kanji>flavor</kanji> bombs. Every time you <kanji>taste</kanji> something, it''s actually because a tiny jet has flown into your mouth to bomb you with flavor. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/812', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%91%B3', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:25:50Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:43:11.578579Z'),
(726, 'kanji', '待', '待', 'People who <radical>loiter</radical> around the <radical>temple</radical> are probably just trying to <kanji>wait</kanji>. They don''t mean to look like they''re up to no good, but it''s hard to wait without looking suspicious when you''re waiting outside a temple.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/726', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%85', 12, 1, '2012-04-23T23:40:53Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:51.902595Z'),
(813, 'kanji', '勉', '勉', 'You''ll have to <radical>excuse</radical> me, having all this <radical>power</radical> puts a lot of strain and <kanji>exertion</kanji> on my body. My power comes from exertion of muscle, so please excuse me if I start to perspire.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/813', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8B%89', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:42:16Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.463233Z'),
(814, 'kanji', '庭', '庭', 'There''s a <radical>canopy</radical> and a man called the <radical>Yoga</radical> <radical>King</radical> standing in the middle of your <kanji>garden</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/814', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%AD', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:42:43Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T19:04:16.361248Z'),
(815, 'kanji', '息', '息', 'Imagining one’s <radical>self</radical> without a <radical>heart</radical> can make it hard to draw <kanji>breath</kanji>. Without a heart, you’d have no blood pumping and you would start to die. This would make it increasingly harder to draw breath and get oxygen to stay alive.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/815', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%81%AF', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:43:08Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.47221Z'),
(816, 'kanji', '旅', '旅', 'The <radical>direction</radical> your <radical>gun</radical> is facing leads straight to a block of <radical>tofu</radical>. This might be a sign you need to take a <kanji>trip</kanji>. If you''re out there pointing guns at tofu, you''re probably a little… tired… and in need of a change of scenery. Take a trip!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/816', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%97%85', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:43:27Z', NULL, '2025-12-23T03:07:20.331935Z'),
(817, 'kanji', '根', '根', 'A <radical>tree</radical> <radical>root</radical> is still just a <kanji>root</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/817', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A0%B9', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:43:49Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:15:32.847277Z'),
(818, 'kanji', '流', '流', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> lifts all the <radical>trash</radical> out of the <radical>river</radical> and it <kanji>stream</kanji>s out onto the shores.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/818', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B5%81', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:44:29Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:41:09.810584Z'),
(819, 'kanji', '消', '消', 'A long time ago a huge <radical>tsunami</radical> swept a <radical>triceratops</radical> up into the <radical>moon</radical> and <kanji>extinguish</kanji>ed its life.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/819', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B6%88', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:44:55Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.497856Z'),
(820, 'kanji', '倍', '倍', 'A <radical>leader</radical> who hires his own personal <radical>clown</radical> will likely <kanji>double</kanji> his entertainment. Clowns are funny and make people laugh, so the leader decided to hire one. Since that time, the leader''s amount of mirth has doubled. 

This also means <kanji>times</kanji>, which makes sense when you think about the kind of math you''ll need to double something. What is A * 2? It''s A doubled, first of all, but you''re using multiplication. You''re "timesing" something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/820', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%80%8D', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:45:18Z', NULL, '2026-01-15T00:22:01.976341Z'),
(821, 'kanji', '員', '員', 'Even a <radical>mouth</radical>-bearing <radical>shellfish</radical> is a <kanji>member</kanji> of the invertebrate family. Yes, shellfish do have mouths, look it up!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/821', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%93%A1', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:45:45Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T18:17:25.18991Z'),
(822, 'kanji', '島', '島', 'A <radical>bird</radical> sits upon a <radical>mountain</radical>. If you look at the radicals, the bird is quite large and the mountain quite small. Think of this in the mnemonic as well. The reason the mountain is small is because you can only see the top of it, sticking out of the water. On it is the bird. This small mountain with the bird on it is an <kanji>island</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/822', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B3%B6', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:46:09Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.513534Z'),
(823, 'kanji', '祭', '祭', 'The <radical>moon</radical> shines down on a <radical>stool</radical> and a <radical>jackhammer</radical> which are there for the upcoming <kanji>festival</kanji>. Festivals need activities, and at this festival, you can sit on a stool in the moonlight and use a jackhammer to make a lot of noise. Perfect for a festival!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/823', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A5%AD', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:46:42Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T07:09:11.444332Z'),
(824, 'kanji', '章', '章', 'You <radical>stand</radical> at a podium <radical>early</radical> in the morning to practice reading the <kanji>chapter</kanji> from your latest book.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/824', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AB%A0', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:47:25Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:51:28.189967Z'),
(825, 'kanji', '第', '第', '<radical>Bamboo</radical>''s <radical>younger brother</radical> is counted with an <kanji>ordinal number prefix</kanji>. An ordinal number is one that represents a position or rank in sequential order (like first, second, third, etc.). We use this ordinal number prefix before counting these things in Japanese sometimes. Like with bamboo, going down in order toward the youngest.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/825', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AC%AC', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:47:49Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:47:33.722797Z'),
(826, 'kanji', '都', '都', '<radical>Someone</radical>s and <radical>building</radical>s, someones and buildings, someones and buildings. So many someones and buildings. With so many someones and buildings, we have ourselves a <kanji>metropolis</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/826', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%83%BD', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:48:10Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:04:05.536609Z'),
(827, 'kanji', '動', '動', 'When something is <radical>heavy</radical>, you need a lot of <radical>power</radical> to <kanji>move</kanji> it. It’s not going to move unless you use a lot of power. That thing is heavy and unless you use all the power you got, it’s not moving.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/827', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8B%95', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:48:30Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:39:38.747791Z'),
(828, 'kanji', '商', '商', 'A <radical>stand</radical>ing <radical>mustache</radical> with <radical>legs</radical> is able to sell <kanji>merchandise</kanji>. Most mustaches can’t stand because they don’t have legs. This mustache, however, does. It also has a keen business sense, which is why it’s selling merchandise.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/828', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%95%86', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:48:54Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:52:35.573465Z'),
(829, 'kanji', '悪', '悪', 'In <radical>Asia</radical> they assume everyone''s <radical>heart</radical> is automatically <kanji>bad</kanji> and <kanji>evil</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/829', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%82%AA', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:49:21Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:43:14.192115Z'),
(830, 'kanji', '族', '族', 'If someone is headed in your <radical>direction</radical> with a <radical>gun</radical>, nock an <radical>arrow</radical> in your bow and gather your <kanji>tribe</kanji> behind you because it’s about to get real! This man with a gun is headed your direction and he doesn’t even know what you and your tribe are gonna do to him. If he gets any closer, you’re likely to shoot that arrow right at him.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/830', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%97%8F', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:49:42Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T06:18:59.69895Z'),
(831, 'kanji', '深', '深', 'If a <radical>tsunami</radical> is higher than your <radical>forehead</radical> you better move those <radical>legs</radical> and find a <radical>tree</radical> to climb, because that thing is <kanji>deep</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/831', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B7%B1', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:50:10Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T22:22:56.412763Z'),
(832, 'kanji', '球', '球', 'The <radical>king</radical> <radical>request</radical>s a <kanji>sphere</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/832', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%90%83', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:50:32Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:52:23.150339Z'),
(833, 'kanji', '童', '童', 'Those who <radical>stand</radical> on top of a <radical>village</radical> are <kanji>juvenile</kanji>. Imagine a bunch of young people standing on the roofs of buildings in the village. That''s a juvenile thing to do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/833', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AB%A5', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:50:54Z', NULL, '2025-11-01T00:52:57.955998Z'),
(835, 'kanji', '階', '階', 'Look at a <radical>building</radical> and <radical>compare</radical> the <radical>white</radical> parts. Each of those white parts is a <kanji>floor</kanji>. Multi-<kanji>story</kanji> buildings have tons of different floors that you can see from the outside.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/835', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9A%8E', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:51:52Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.598835Z'),
(836, 'kanji', '寒', '寒', 'The <radical>roof</radical>, even though it''s almost <radical>spring</radical>, is still covered in <radical>ice</radical> so it''s <kanji>cold</kanji>. Inside the house is cold, outside the house is cold. Everything is cold!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/836', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%92', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:52:22Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:22:51.842327Z'),
(838, 'kanji', '暑', '暑', 'If the <radical>sun</radical> is on <radical>someone</radical>, they will get <kanji>hot</kanji>. That''s just how it is. Someone standing out in the sun for a long time will inevitably get hot. The sun is hot, and it makes you get hot too.

Note that this usually refers to hot weather or the heat of an area you''re in and doesn''t refer to a hot "thing."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/838', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9A%91', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:53:12Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:24:21.947579Z'),
(839, 'kanji', '期', '期', 'A "<radical>crab</radical> <radical>moon</radical>" is a specific <kanji>period of time</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/839', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%9F', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:53:35Z', NULL, '2026-02-12T07:17:21.136514Z'),
(840, 'kanji', '植', '植', 'If you want a <radical>tree</radical> to grow <radical>straight</radical>, you have to <kanji>plant</kanji> it properly. Everyone knows that poorly planted trees grow all crooked and twisted. That''s just how plants work. If your tree''s not straight, start over and plant it again.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/840', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A4%8D', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:54:39Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:07:35.247328Z'),
(841, 'kanji', '歯', '歯', '<radical>Stop</radical>! That <radical>box</radical> of <radical>rice</radical> is actually full of <kanji>teeth</kanji>! I know it says "rice" on the cover, but I promise you those are all teeth and if you bite into them you''ll break a <kanji>tooth</kanji> of your own.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/841', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AD%AF', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:55:06Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:12:57.601431Z'),
(842, 'kanji', '温', '温', 'There''s a <radical>tsunami</radical> which washes a <radical>plate</radical> out into the <radical>sun</radical>. Because it''s in the sun, it becomes  <kanji>warm</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/842', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B8%A9', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:55:32Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:46:49.553173Z'),
(843, 'kanji', '港', '港', 'There''s a <radical>tsunami</radical> coming and you want to be <radical>together</radical> with it by <radical>yourself</radical> (oneself) so you go to the <kanji>harbor</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/843', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B8%AF', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:56:00Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:59:46.609728Z'),
(844, 'kanji', '湯', '湯', 'If there''s a <radical>tsunami</radical> it''s <radical>easy</radical> to get <kanji>hot water</kanji>. Just stick your hand out the window as the tsunami goes by and poof! That water''s HOT.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/844', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B9%AF', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:56:23Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.659179Z'),
(845, 'kanji', '登', '登', 'There''s a <radical>tent</radical> of <radical>beans</radical>. You try to <kanji>climb</kanji> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/845', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%99%BB', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:56:48Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:54:38.942095Z'),
(846, 'kanji', '着', '着', 'A <radical>horn</radical>ed <radical>king</radical> on a <radical>slide</radical> catches your <radical>eye</radical> because you love what he''s chosen to <kanji>wear</kanji>. You can''t wait for him to <kanji>arrive</kanji> at the bottom of the slide so you can check out his outfit close-up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/846', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9D%80', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:57:49Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:36.961676Z'),
(847, 'kanji', '短', '短', 'If you use an <radical>arrow</radical> to shoot a stalk of <radical>beans</radical>, the bean plant will end up <kanji>short</kanji>. When the beans are hit with that arrow, it severs the plant in two, and you are left with a stalk of beans that is much shorter than before.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/847', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9F%AD', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:58:21Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:54:38.942095Z'),
(848, 'kanji', '野', '野', 'The <radical>village</radical> <radical>beforehand</radical> (what the village was before it was a village) was a <kanji>field</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/848', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%87%8E', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T19:59:43Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:58:34.235381Z'),
(849, 'kanji', '泉', '泉', '<radical>White</radical> <radical>water</radical> shoots out from a <kanji>spring</kanji> in the ground. Due to the force the water shoots from the spring, the water looks white with all the air bubbles.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/849', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B3%89', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:00:26Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:51:01.232609Z'),
(852, 'kanji', '合', '合', 'The radical <radical>suit</radical> and the kanji <kanji>suit</kanji> are exactly the same! This kanji can also mean <kanji>join</kanji> which makes sense. When something suits you well, like a hat or something, it joins together with you quite well too. Join me, my hat! You suit my head well. It just <kanji>fit</kanji>s, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/852', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%88', 12, 1, '2012-07-19T23:03:00.414423Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:46:30.938459Z'),
(1594, 'kanji', '彼', '彼', 'If you <radical>loiter</radical> around my <radical>skin</radical>, <kanji>he</kanji> will get angry at you. He''s my very protective friend, and you don''t wanna make him angry!
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1594', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BD%BC', 12, 1, '2013-04-05T18:54:54.681646Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:30:55.746903Z'),
(1890, 'kanji', '僕', '僕', 'A <radical>leader</radical> of <radical>business</radical>? Ha! That''s <kanji>me</kanji>! That''s <kanji>I</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1890', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%83%95', 12, 1, '2013-07-02T22:17:13.730594Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:55:35.269777Z'),
(1926, 'kanji', '疲', '疲', 'I have <radical>sick</radical> in my <radical>skin</radical>. Just look at it, it doesn''t look too good. Whenever I have sickness in my skin, I know that I''m probably <kanji>tired</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1926', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%96%B2', 12, 1, '2013-07-03T17:50:16.031289Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:30:55.746903Z'),
(859, 'kanji', '問', '問', 'If you run into a <radical>gate</radical> <radical>mouth</radical> first, you’re going to have a <kanji>problem</kanji>. Smashing your mouth into a gate is going to hurt. A lot. The problem you’re now in is one of getting to a hospital and having your mouth sewn back on. The <kanji>question</kanji> is, how are you going to get to the hospital?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/859', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%95%8F', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:44:07.695682Z', NULL, '2026-03-17T23:15:12.157125Z'),
(860, 'kanji', '宿', '宿', 'The <radical>roof</radical> under which the <radical>leader</radical> can house one <radical>hundred</radical> of his people is at the local <kanji>lodge</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/860', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%BF', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:46:25.533933Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.789Z'),
(861, 'kanji', '想', '想', 'Only a <radical>tree</radical> with <radical>eye</radical>s and a <radical>heart</radical> has the ability to <kanji>imagine</kanji> and to <kanji>contemplate</kanji>. For a tree to imagine and contemplate things, it needs eyes to see and a heart to feel.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/861', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%83%B3', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:46:58.054828Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:45:51.099114Z'),
(862, 'kanji', '感', '感', 'When a <radical>canine</radical> puts its <radical>mouth</radical> on your <radical>heart</radical>, it''s a pretty unforgettable <kanji>feeling</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/862', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%84%9F', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:47:34.603712Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:17:36.077711Z'),
(863, 'kanji', '整', '整', 'A <radical>bundle</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> is the <radical>correct</radical> way to <kanji>arrange</kanji> things. Just make sure you <kanji>organize</kanji> it all before winter comes, or it''ll be too late.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/863', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%95%B4', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:52:46.809684Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:40:04.894814Z'),
(864, 'kanji', '暗', '暗', 'If the <radical>sun</radical> starts to make a <radical>sound</radical>, you know it''s about to go <kanji>dark</kanji>. The sun isn''t supposed to make a sound, so if you can hear it, it''s probably about to fizzle out or something. After it''s gone, obviously everything will be pitch black. Get ready to be in the dark!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/864', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9A%97', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:53:52.525916Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:13:44.248099Z'),
(865, 'kanji', '様', '様', 'By a <radical>tree</radical>, there''s a <radical>sheep</radical> in the <radical>water</radical> with a <kanji>formal name ender</kanji>. That means everyone calls him Sheep-sama. No one calls him just "Sheep," since he''s kind of a big deal. He''s actually like sheep royalty. When he''s not in his castle, Sheep-sama enjoys playing in the water by the trees.

This can also mean <kanji>manner</kanji>, as in your manner of doing things. I guess someone with a formal name ender has a different manner of doing things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/865', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A7%98', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:54:33.748668Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T06:02:05.348803Z'),
(866, 'kanji', '橋', '橋', 'This <radical>tree</radical> must''ve been sent from <radical>heaven</radical>, because it has a <radical>mouth</radical> and a <radical>mustache</radical>. Like some kind of special heaven tree that talks. And why was it sent? To be a <kanji>bridge</kanji>. To be the great heavenly bridge that we all, someday, must cross.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/866', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A9%8B', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:55:11.4727Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.8222Z'),
(868, 'kanji', '緑', '緑', 'If you tie a <radical>thread</radical> around <radical>Wolverine</radical> and dip him in <radical>water</radical>, he''ll turn <kanji>green</kanji>, like the Hulk. Some kind of weird chemical reaction. But I… I wouldn''t try it if I were you. You wouldn''t like him when he''s green. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/868', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B7%91', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:56:21.302881Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T08:41:48.86066Z'),
(869, 'kanji', '練', '練', 'Pointing a <radical>thread</radical> to the <radical>east</radical> is what you <kanji>practice</kanji> every day. You''re incredibly passionate about eastward thread-pointing, so you make sure to practice every day.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/869', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B7%B4', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:57:08.68014Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:09:25.99139Z'),
(870, 'kanji', '詩', '詩', 'What you <radical>say</radical> at a <radical>temple</radical> is a <kanji>poem</kanji>. It has to be a poem, because if you say anything else it''s just plain disrespectful. All of the monks and priests at this temple have to read and recite poems all day, and if they can do it forever, you can while you''re visiting.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/870', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A9%A9', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:57:42.0936Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.851528Z'),
(871, 'kanji', '銀', '銀', '<radical>Gold</radical>, at its <radical>root</radical>, is made up of <kanji>silver</kanji>. Using silver as the root of a coin is a great way to save on gold! That''s why, often, if you just scratch gold, you''ll find there''s silver hidden underneath, at its root.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/871', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8A%80', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:58:13.623686Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T09:52:41.519974Z'),
(872, 'kanji', '題', '題', 'The <radical>sun</radical> shines down on your <radical>coat rack</radical> to reveal your <radical>geoduck</radical>-patterned jacket, which then becomes the major <kanji>topic</kanji> of discussion all around town.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/872', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A1%8C', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:58:46.625137Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:17:05.683016Z'),
(873, 'kanji', '館', '館', '<radical>Eat</radical>ing the <radical>roof</radical> is a <radical>bear</radical>, because the roof is a <kanji>public building</kanji>. Because it is a public building bears are allowed to eat the roof.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/873', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A4%A8', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:59:21.643252Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:00:07.209894Z'),
(874, 'kanji', '駅', '駅', 'A <radical>horse</radical> and a <radical>shrimp</radical> are sitting next to each other. They are both at the train <kanji>station</kanji>, waiting for a train to come and take them home.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/874', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A7%85', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T07:59:58.398106Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:39:53.26852Z'),
(875, 'kanji', '億', '億', 'A cult <radical>leader</radical> has a great <radical>idea</radical> to recruit a <kanji>hundred million</kanji> new members. Since he’s the guy with all the ideas, he is the leader of the cult. His idea is to give every new member a free puppy. He’ll have a hundred million new recruits in no time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/875', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%84%84', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:00:42.809542Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.877119Z'),
(876, 'kanji', '器', '器', '<radical>Big</radical> <radical>products</radical> need to be put in a big <kanji>container</kanji>. A small container just won’t do. These products are big and they need a big container to be contained in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/876', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%99%A8', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:01:15.456521Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:48:13.12669Z'),
(877, 'kanji', '士', '士', 'The <radical>samurai</radical> radical and the <kanji>samurai</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/877', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A3%AB', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:01:46.60732Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:09:00.434554Z'),
(878, 'kanji', '料', '料', 'You have a <radical>rice</radical> <radical>ladle</radical> that''s specifically for paying the rice <kanji>fee</kanji>. Each scoop of something has an extra fee tacked on, so when you use a <kanji>material</kanji>, there''s a fee you have to pay in that material as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/878', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%96%99', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:02:23.028467Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T02:04:23.112258Z'),
(879, 'kanji', '標', '標', 'You see a <radical>tree</radical> with a <radical>helicopter</radical> and a <radical>jackhammer</radical> tied to it. You look closer, because this is an odd sight, and realize that there is a sign here too. It''s telling you to beware of helicopters and jackhammers in these woods. It''s a poorly made <kanji>signpost</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/879', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A8%99', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:02:56.897602Z', NULL, '2026-02-03T19:17:48.535954Z'),
(880, 'kanji', '殺', '殺', 'There''s <radical>treasure</radical> in this <radical>tree</radical> so you take out your <radical>weapon</radical> and <kanji>kill</kanji> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/880', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AE%BA', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:03:28.559706Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.915509Z'),
(881, 'kanji', '然', '然', 'Under a misshapen crescent <radical>moon</radical>, a <radical>dog</radical> <radical>boil</radical>s as much <kanji>nature</kanji> as it can find.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/881', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%84%B6', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:03:59.5221Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T08:55:56.228295Z'),
(882, 'kanji', '熱', '熱', 'Your <radical>womb</radical> spins in a <radical>circle</radical> and starts to <radical>boil</radical> with <kanji>heat</kanji>. Uh oh, I think you have a <kanji>fever</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/882', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%86%B1', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:05:39.708745Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.924568Z'),
(883, 'kanji', '課', '課', 'I always <radical>say</radical> the <radical>fruit</radical> <kanji>section</kanji> at the grocery should be at the front of the store. It’s the perfect place for the fruit section, I say.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/883', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AA%B2', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:06:09.087798Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:29:27.171851Z'),
(884, 'kanji', '賞', '賞', 'The <radical>Viking</radical> opens his <radical>mouth</radical>, and below it, a <radical>shellfish</radical> sits. Grab that shellfish and throw it into his mouth to win a <kanji>prize</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/884', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B3%9E', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:06:49.128217Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T07:08:03.597715Z'),
(885, 'kanji', '輪', '輪', 'For your <radical>car</radical> you use a <radical>hat</radical>, some <radical>ground</radical> and a <radical>bookshelf</radical> to build a new <kanji>wheel</kanji>. I mean, it''s generally <kanji>ring</kanji> and <kanji>loop</kanji> shaped, it just needs to be a <kanji>circle</kanji>, so it should work, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/885', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BC%AA', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:07:20.004893Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.94361Z'),
(886, 'kanji', '選', '選', 'You want to get a <radical>scooter</radical>, so you have one self (<radical>oneself</radical>) and another self working <radical>together</radical> to <kanji>choose</kanji>. The two tiny yous sit on each of your shoulders, like angel and devil, and argue about which scooter you should choose.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/886', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%B8', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:07:58.218039Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:59:46.609728Z'),
(887, 'kanji', '鏡', '鏡', '<radical>Gold</radical> reveals itself when you <radical>stand</radical> and <radical>see</radical> into this <kanji>mirror</kanji>. Yes, even you are made of gold. You''re so great and valuable, standing in front of that mirror. It''s not hard to see, especially in a mirror – you''re golden.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/887', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8F%A1', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:08:42.48233Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:33:42.055178Z'),
(889, 'kanji', '養', '養', '<radical>Sheep</radical> <radical>fins</radical> are <radical>good</radical> for <kanji>foster</kanji> children. The sheep have been genetically combined with fish, so the sheep have fins (and they''ve lost their tails — which is why the tail part of the sheep radical is missing in this kanji). These sheep fins are rich in nutrients, so they''re good for fostering big, strong foster children.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/889', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A4%8A', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:10:16.975545Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T06:01:48.384976Z'),
(891, 'kanji', '情', '情', 'Your <radical>soul</radical> is <radical>blue</radical>. As in, it''s sad and not feeling well. This is because your soul changes colors depending on your <kanji>feeling</kanji>s or what <kanji>emotion</kanji>s you''re expressing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/891', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%83%85', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:11:14.572178Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:22:25.515687Z'),
(892, 'kanji', '謝', '謝', 'You thought it would be a good idea to <radical>say</radical> the <radical>body</radical> <radical>measurement</radical>s of everyone in your village and now you have to <kanji>apologize</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/892', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AC%9D', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:14:45.744465Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T04:17:56.440105Z'),
(894, 'kanji', '疑', '疑', 'You reach for a <radical>spoon</radical> to start eating, but your <radical>mama</radical> hands you an <radical>arrow</radical> instead and points towards the <radical>coat rack</radical>. Hm, that''s not like her… Suddenly you''re filled with <kanji>doubt</kanji>. Is this really your mama? You doubt it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/894', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%96%91', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:16:25.752442Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:46:31.278559Z'),
(895, 'kanji', '皆', '皆', 'If you <radical>compare</radical> <radical>white</radical> light to the other colors you''ll realize... white consists of <kanji>all</kanji> the colors in one. It contains all the colors in the spectrum in one color. It is the one color that is <kanji>everything</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/895', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9A%86', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:16:58.444873Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.00069Z'),
(1898, 'kanji', '缶', '缶', 'The radical <radical>can</radical> is the same as the kanji <kanji>can</kanji>. Usually when we talk about a can with this kanji we mean a <kanji>tin can</kanji>, though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1898', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BC%B6', 13, 1, '2013-07-02T22:19:49.627037Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:51:59.904352Z'),
(888, 'kanji', '願', '願', 'The <radical>original</radical> <radical>geoduck</radical> had a <kanji>request</kanji>. He wants there to be more geoducks in the world!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/888', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A1%98', 14, 1, '2012-08-28T08:09:31.119134Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(896, 'kanji', '例', '例', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of the <radical>yakuza</radical> shows you his <radical>knife</radical>. He says, "This is an <kanji>example</kanji> of a knife."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/896', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BE%8B', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:37:48.751923Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.00388Z'),
(897, 'kanji', '卒', '卒', 'You give a <radical>lid</radical> to a <radical>person</radical> (two of them, in fact), then they put it on a <radical>cross</radical> when you <kanji>graduate</kanji>. It''s a symbolic ceremony, putting a lid over a cross, but it''s just what you do when you graduate around these parts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/897', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%92', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:38:20.633213Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T00:03:45.65999Z'),
(898, 'kanji', '協', '協', 'Next to the <radical>cross</radical> we combine our <radical>power</radical> <radical>power</radical> <radical>power</radical> so that we have <kanji>cooperation</kanji>. And with our powers combined, we become stronger than we ever could have been apart, lifting the cross above our heads!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/898', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%94', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:38:56.508622Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.031727Z'),
(899, 'kanji', '参', '参', 'You try to be <radical>private</radical>, but because of your <radical>big</radical> <radical>hair</radical> you end up having to <kanji>participate</kanji> in everything. Can you imagine people asking for volunteers or something, and you trying to look inconspicuous with your big hair? Of course they''re going to notice you and make you participate.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/899', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%82', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:39:29.500716Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T21:46:55.957845Z'),
(900, 'kanji', '周', '周', 'The <radical>circumference</radical> radical and the <kanji>circumference</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/900', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%91%A8', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:40:00.417347Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:06:22.227639Z'),
(901, 'kanji', '囲', '囲', 'The <radical>mouth</radical> of a <radical>well</radical> will <kanji>surround</kanji> you if you fall into it. The mouth of a well is the opening of the well, and if you fall into that well, the mouth as well as the rest of the well will surround you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/901', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9B%B2', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:43:21.01827Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:10:47.705113Z'),
(902, 'kanji', '固', '固', 'A <radical>mouth</radical> that is <radical>old</radical> is going to be <kanji>hard</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/902', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9B%BA', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:43:49.18808Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:13:37.694956Z'),
(903, 'kanji', '季', '季', 'A <radical>grain</radical>''s <radical>child</radical> will grow with the <kanji>seasons</kanji>. In spring the grain is born, it grows in summer, is harvested in fall, and dies in winter.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/903', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AD%A3', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:44:14.381871Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:46:14.836586Z'),
(904, 'kanji', '完', '完', 'The <radical>roof</radical> that became the <radical>origin</radical> of all roofs was <kanji>perfect</kanji>. It was tall and wide so it could fit and protect everyone. And the dimensions were perfect too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/904', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%8C', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:44:59.729398Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.060079Z'),
(905, 'kanji', '希', '希', 'There is a <radical>treasure</radical> on top of a <radical>narwhal</radical> who wears a <radical>towel</radical> that will grant your greatest <kanji>wish</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/905', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B8%8C', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:45:33.016193Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.059621Z'),
(906, 'kanji', '念', '念', 'If you live in the <radical>now</radical>, there’s a good chance your <radical>heart</radical> controls your <kanji>thought</kanji> process. You don’t have time to think with your brain, you need an answer now so you use your heart to make decisions and thought process.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/906', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BF%B5', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:46:05.944668Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:55:52.137546Z'),
(907, 'kanji', '折', '折', 'If your <radical>fingers</radical> are strong enough, you can take an <radical>axe</radical> and <kanji>fold</kanji> it in half. But be careful not to <kanji>bend</kanji> it too far, or you might just <kanji>break</kanji> it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/907', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8A%98', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:46:27.913686Z', NULL, '2026-01-10T03:09:24.785657Z'),
(908, 'kanji', '望', '望', 'Causing <radical>death</radical> at every turn, the <radical>moon</radical> <radical>king</radical> sits inside his moon, getting ready to cause death on anything in his sights. Sounds a lot like the Death Star from a certain movie. One of which is "Star Wars: A New <kanji>Hope</kanji>."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/908', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%9B', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:47:02.955125Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.087988Z'),
(909, 'kanji', '材', '材', 'A <radical>tree</radical> with <radical>talent</radical> can become <kanji>lumber</kanji>. Not all trees can do this. Only the talented ones are able to turn themselves into lumber or <kanji>material</kanji>, to be used for a million different things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/909', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9D%90', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:47:26.898087Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.095746Z'),
(910, 'kanji', '束', '束', 'The <radical>bundle</radical> radical and the <kanji>bundle</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/910', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9D%9F', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:47:58.52789Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:40:04.894814Z'),
(911, 'kanji', '松', '松', 'When climbing a <radical>tree</radical> in <radical>public</radical>, it’s best to choose a <kanji>pine</kanji>. Pine trees offer the most coverage, so you’ll be able to hide, even out in public.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/911', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9D%BE', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:48:21.055254Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.102977Z'),
(912, 'kanji', '残', '残', '<radical>Yakuza</radical> <radical>bars</radical> barely exist. The <kanji>remainder</kanji> of them aren''t even in Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/912', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AE%8B', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:48:45.640247Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:35:43.305445Z'),
(914, 'kanji', '的', '的', 'A <radical>white</radical> guy jumps into a <radical>pool</radical> with a <kanji>target</kanji> painted on the bottom. He''s aiming for the target, of course.

This kanji can also mean <kanji>-al</kanji>, as in the endings you get on some English adjectives. You know, like the pool is artifici-al. Or painting a target down there is not practic-al. That kind of -al.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/914', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9A%84', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:49:38.879271Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T09:41:29.137839Z'),
(915, 'kanji', '約', '約', 'You tie a <radical>thread</radical> on your finger before jumping in the <radical>pool</radical> to remind yourself of the <kanji>promise</kanji> you made to your mother. The thread represents the promise you made to mow the lawn after you finished swimming in the pool.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/915', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%84', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:50:16.639994Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:49:14.754352Z'),
(916, 'kanji', '芸', '芸', 'You put <radical>flowers</radical> on a <radical>cloud</radical> and call it <kanji>art</kanji>. Not everyone agrees that your flower cloud art is actually art, but you know it is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/916', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8A%B8', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:53:43.577885Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T17:20:50.783352Z'),
(917, 'kanji', '基', '基', '<radical>Crab</radical>s in the <radical>dirt</radical> make a great <kanji>foundation</kanji> for a seaside hut. It''s right on the seashore, where all the crabs dig their holes in the dirt. But as they get old and die, their bodies form a strong base. Perfect for the foundation of a building like this!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/917', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9F%BA', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:54:05.290308Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T19:05:09.238484Z'),
(918, 'kanji', '性', '性', 'Everyone has a <radical>soul</radical> in their <radical>life</radical>, and that soul shapes their <kanji>gender</kanji>. It''s not just about body parts — it''s about what''s inside. In the end, it''s one''s <kanji> nature</kanji> that truly matters.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/918', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%80%A7', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:54:27.699193Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:52:32.009688Z'),
(920, 'kanji', '格', '格', 'If a <radical>tree</radical> wants to <radical>kiss</radical> you, it''s a sign of your <kanji>status</kanji>. Only someone of high status would be worthy enough to receive the gnarly but gentle kiss of a tree. Trees don''t kiss just anyone, you know.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/920', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A0%BC', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:55:15.132169Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T07:06:53.838661Z'),
(921, 'kanji', '能', '能', 'In a <radical>private</radical> place on the <radical>moon</radical>, you hide two <radical>spoon</radical>s that give you a special <kanji>ability</kanji>. It''s the ability to predict the future! The private moon spoons reflect the knowledge of time and space into your brain. Now that''s a cool ability!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/921', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%83%BD', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:55:45.383476Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T21:59:59.456873Z'),
(924, 'kanji', '骨', '骨', 'If you have a <radical>mustache</radical> and you hit your <radical>forehead</radical> on the <radical>moon</radical>, you’re probably going to break a <kanji>bone</kanji>. Ok, so it doesn’t matter so much if you have a mustache, but if you’re travelling fast enough to make it to the moon, you’re definitely breaking a bone if you run into it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/924', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%AA%A8', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T23:57:37.752634Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T06:59:19.979283Z'),
(925, 'kanji', '妥', '妥', 'A <radical>cleat</radical>-wearing <radical>woman</radical> is walking on someone''s back, but she doesn''t want to hurt him, so she has to be very <kanji>gentle</kanji>. Cleats are sharp and pointy, and not designed for back-walking, so she''s being extra gentle right now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/925', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A6%A5', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:02:41.07183Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(926, 'kanji', '雰', '雰', '<radical>Rain</radical> makes up one <radical>part</radical> of this, and the rest is air or something. I imagine this is what people thought the <kanji>atmosphere</kanji> was made out of thousands of years ago.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/926', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%B0', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:08:17.564872Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T22:08:10.304952Z'),
(927, 'kanji', '頑', '頑', 'The <radical>origin</radical> of the <radical>geoduck</radical> is a pretty <kanji>stubborn</kanji> one. The geoduck race is so stubborn that it refused to evolve into an animal that made any sense. Hence, the origin of the geoduck is one of stubbornness.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/927', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A0%91', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:08:44.645713Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(1514, 'kanji', '閉', '閉', 'The <radical>gate</radical> to <radical>talent</radical> is now <kanji>closed</kanji>. In fact, the gate to talent is always closed. It''s not like anyone can just waltz in and get themselves some talent. You have to work for it! You have to work hard to get through that closed gate to talent!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1514', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%96%89', 14, 1, '2013-03-18T17:03:30.543152Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.463372Z')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;


This kanji also means <kanji>equation</kanji> for some reason. Just think of a ceremony that involves doing math and solving equations. How strange would that be? Pretend you''re at this ceremony.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/939', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%8F', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:46:44.390429Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.177991Z'),
(940, 'kanji', '信', '信', 'To your <radical>leader</radical>, you <radical>say</radical>: "<kanji>Believe</kanji> and <kanji>trust</kanji> in me." The leader doesn''t think you can handle your duties, so you have to say this to him. You want him to believe and trust that you''re responsible. You can handle this task.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/940', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BF%A1', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:47:08.879599Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:06:37.873557Z'),
(941, 'kanji', '勇', '勇', 'The radical for <radical>courage</radical> and the kanji for <kanji>courage</kanji> are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/941', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8B%87', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:47:33.850844Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.194428Z'),
(942, 'kanji', '単', '単', 'The <radical>simple</radical> radical and the <kanji>simple</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/942', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%98', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:48:06.741826Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.221066Z'),
(943, 'kanji', '司', '司', 'Throw a <radical>cape</radical> over a <radical>creeper</radical>, and suddenly they think they''re a big-shot <kanji>director</kanji>. They go around calling themselves managing director of this, director-general of that. They creep into meetings and ceremonies and try to direct the proceedings, all because of that stupid cape. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/943', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%B8', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:49:00.936024Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.228836Z'),
(944, 'kanji', '変', '変', '<radical>Again</radical> and again, <radical>winter</radical> <kanji>change</kanji>s you each year. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/944', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A4%89', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:49:23.876358Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.237502Z'),
(945, 'kanji', '夫', '夫', 'The <radical>husband</radical> radical and the <kanji>husband</kanji> kanji are the same. How useful, unlike some husbands, I''d imagine.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/945', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A4%AB', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:49:52.820345Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:39:55.920659Z'),
(947, 'kanji', '昨', '昨', 'Once the <radical>sun</radical> comes out, you can use this <radical>key</radical> to unlock it and take you to any <kanji>previous</kanji> day, even just <kanji>yesterday</kanji>! The sun is in charge of the day changes, so if you use a key to unlock it, you can control the flow of time and return to yesterday, or a previous day in the past. Behold the power of the sun.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/947', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%98%A8', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:50:43.244305Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.262753Z'),
(948, 'kanji', '毒', '毒', 'To throw <radical>life</radical> out the <radical>window</radical>, use <kanji>poison</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/948', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AF%92', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:51:11.192158Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.271165Z'),
(949, 'kanji', '法', '法', '<radical>Tsunami</radical>s in the <radical>past</radical> changed the <kanji>method</kanji>s and <kanji>law</kanji>s of today.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/949', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B3%95', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:51:39.741177Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.279626Z'),
(950, 'kanji', '泣', '泣', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> approaches, and anyone who dares to <radical>stand</radical> in front of it will surely <kanji>cry</kanji>. That tsunami is going to hit you hard, and rip you right off the ground you''re standing on. Who wouldn''t cry?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/950', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B3%A3', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:52:29.655551Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.304484Z'),
(951, 'kanji', '浅', '浅', 'After a <radical>tsunami</radical>, if you go to a <radical>bar</radical>, you''ll find yourself standing in <kanji>shallow</kanji> water. You''re just trying to enjoy a drink, but with your feet constantly soaked, it''s not exactly pleasant. Here''s to hoping the conversations with your date won''t be shallow too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/951', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B5%85', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:52:58.265581Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.315344Z'),
(952, 'kanji', '紀', '紀', 'The <radical>thread</radical> you weave together to tell others about <radical>oneself</radical> is the <kanji>account</kanji> of your life. It''s your very own <kanji>narrative</kanji>, tying everything you''ve been through together.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/952', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%80', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:53:37.022783Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.336982Z'),
(953, 'kanji', '英', '英', 'There are many <radical>flowers</radical> in the <radical>center</radical> of <kanji>England</kanji>. It may seem rainy and gray at first, but if you walk right to the center you''ll see all the flowers that grow there in the center of the country.

This kanji also means <kanji>English</kanji>, which is pretty similar to England. Just imagine all the people of England walking to the center, where the flowers are, and speaking English to them to help them grow.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/953', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8B%B1', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:54:07.424189Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.346183Z'),
(954, 'kanji', '軍', '軍', 'You run into someone''s <radical>forehead</radical> with your <radical>car</radical>. The big forehead you hit is Kim Jong Un''s. You know that now you''re going to have the whole North Korean <kanji>army</kanji> coming after you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/954', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BB%8D', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:54:32.131682Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.354046Z'),
(955, 'kanji', '飯', '飯', 'You might want to <radical>eat</radical>, but your <radical>anti</radical>-eating stance means your <kanji>meal</kanji> will just sit in front of you, uneaten. Food is meant to be eaten! Stop trying to fast and take a bite.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/955', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A3%AF', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:54:59.798398Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.380119Z'),
(956, 'kanji', '仏', '仏', 'Your <radical>leader</radical> in <radical>private</radical> is <kanji>Buddha</kanji>. You don''t feel the need to tell everyone. But when you''re alone you give your time to Buddha and let him lead your life.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/956', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%8F', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:55:27.727402Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.397462Z'),
(958, 'kanji', '晩', '晩', 'The <radical>sun</radical> has an <radical>excuse</radical> for disappearing at <kanji>night</kanji>. The sun gets really tired, same as anyone else – so when night rolls around the sun excuses itself and goes to sleep. It’s a valid excuse.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/958', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%99%A9', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:56:26.46746Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.408444Z'),
(959, 'kanji', '猫', '猫', 'If you see an <radical>animal</radical> among the <radical>flowers</radical> in a <radical>rice paddy</radical>, chances are it’s a <kanji>cat</kanji>. Cats love rice paddy flowers, almost as much as they love catnip.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/959', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8C%AB', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:57:05.692006Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.418833Z'),
(1608, 'kanji', '払', '払', 'Your <radical>fingers</radical> are <radical>private</radical>, no one can see them unless they <kanji>pay</kanji>! And you decide if what they pay is enough to get a peek.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1608', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%89%95', 15, 1, '2013-04-05T19:04:05.71465Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:19:45.714523Z'),
(1891, 'kanji', '匹', '匹', 'In the <radical>cage</radical> there are some <radical>legs</radical>. They are the legs attached to a <kanji>small animal</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1891', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8C%B9', 15, 1, '2013-07-02T22:17:34.509813Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.208438Z'),
(960, 'kanji', '園', '園', 'You examine the <radical>mouth</radical> of a <radical>zombie</radical> and find a small <kanji>garden</kanji> in there. There are even benches... it looks like a <kanji>park</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/960', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9C%92', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:33:24.749575Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:24:53.012902Z'),
(961, 'kanji', '曜', '曜', 'When the <radical>sun</radical> is out, you can see two <radical>Wolverine</radical>s and a <radical>turkey</radical> playing together, but only on a certain <kanji>weekday</kanji>. Which <kanji>day of the week</kanji> is it? I''ll never tell you. It could be Sunday. It could be Monday. It could be any day of the week. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/961', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9B%9C', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:33:57.758175Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T16:54:31.280083Z'),
(962, 'kanji', '書', '書', 'Why would you try to take a <radical>brush</radical> to the <radical>sun</radical>? It''s almost too obvious, because you want to <kanji>write</kanji> something down.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/962', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9B%B8', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:34:23.19178Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T08:52:44.984264Z'),
(963, 'kanji', '遠', '遠', 'You see a <radical>scooter</radical> with a <radical>zombie</radical> on it, going really <kanji>far</kanji> away. Someone tied that zombie to the scooter and now it''s far off in the distance, groaning and growling.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/963', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%A0', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:34:57.987973Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.553294Z'),
(964, 'kanji', '門', '門', 'The radical for <radical>gate</radical> and the kanji for <kanji>gates</kanji> are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/964', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%96%80', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:37:15.716209Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.563292Z'),
(965, 'kanji', '係', '係', 'A psychic <radical>leader</radical> kneels to the <radical>ground</radical>, picks up a <radical>thread</radical>, and instantly makes a <kanji>connection</kanji> with the person that it came from. This leader is very powerful, so even a thread is enough for them to make a psychic connection with the person who was wearing it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/965', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BF%82', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:37:54.645787Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T20:02:26.429946Z'),
(967, 'kanji', '品', '品', 'The kanji is the same as the <radical>products</radical> radical, so it means <kanji>product</kanji> — you know, the kind of <kanji>article</kanji>s or <kanji>goods</kanji> you buy and sell.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/967', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%93%81', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:38:47.231986Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:54:06.381245Z'),
(968, 'kanji', '守', '守', 'Your <radical>roof</radical> needs <radical>measurement</radical>s done every year to make sure it will <kanji>protect</kanji> you properly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/968', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%88', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:39:32.749741Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.608032Z'),
(969, 'kanji', '幸', '幸', 'Put a <radical>lid</radical> on something <radical>spicy</radical> and you''ll cook up pure <kanji>happiness</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/969', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B9%B8', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:39:53.884402Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:24:37.385296Z'),
(970, 'kanji', '急', '急', 'In the <radical>prison</radical> where you once kept <radical>Wolverine</radical>, you find a beating <radical>heart</radical>. You don''t understand how it''s still going, but you know you need to <kanji>hurry</kanji> up and do something, or it might stop all of a <kanji>sudden</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/970', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%80%A5', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:40:28.017106Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:03:17.692652Z'),
(971, 'kanji', '真', '真', 'If the <radical>pope</radical> stays fixated on the <radical>ground</radical>, staring at <radical>fins</radical>, he''s going to lose touch with <kanji>reality</kanji>. He should be focusing on his popish duties, but instead he''s looking at the fish fins he dropped, and now can''t eat because they''re dirty. Silly pope. You can''t let this one thing get you down. You have other things to do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/971', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9C%9F', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:40:51.324583Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:18:03.531203Z'),
(972, 'kanji', '箱', '箱', 'With the <radical>bamboo</radical> from that <radical>tree</radical>, your trained <radical>eye</radical> can tell it''ll make an excellent <kanji>box</kanji>. Boxes made from bamboo trees are very sturdy and fashionable. You have an eye for fashion as well, you see.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/972', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AE%B1', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:41:25.589979Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T20:09:43.740674Z'),
(973, 'kanji', '荷', '荷', 'You offer <radical>flowers</radical> to your <radical>leader</radical> and compliment them on their <radical>lip ring</radical>. You think that by doing this they might carry your <kanji>luggage</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/973', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8D%B7', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:41:52.461896Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.7092Z'),
(974, 'kanji', '面', '面', 'The <radical>face</radical> radical and the <kanji>face</kanji> kanji are the same! But this isn''t just a face like the one you''re looking at your computer/phone with. It''s the <kanji>surface</kanji> kind of face too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/974', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9D%A2', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:42:13.579729Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:18:03.531203Z'),
(975, 'kanji', '典', '典', 'If you play <radical>music</radical> with your <radical>fins</radical>, one <kanji>rule</kanji> is that you must be a fish. You can’t play music using fish fins that don’t belong to you. That’s the rule. And it’s also rude.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/975', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%B8', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:42:36.768223Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:46:29.409938Z'),
(977, 'kanji', '府', '府', 'A <radical>canopy</radical> <radical>attach</radical>ing your entire country is the <kanji>government</kanji>. They loom over you all and try to keep you all organized and together under their control.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/977', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%9C', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:43:36.136006Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.818468Z'),
(978, 'kanji', '治', '治', 'After a <radical>tsunami</radical>, use this <radical>machine</radical> to <kanji>cure</kanji> the people and the country from the damage. <kanji>Heal</kanji>ing machines are all over Japan just in case a tsunami does way more damage than they can handle. 

Oh, and whoever cures and heals the most people will be able to <kanji>rule</kanji> over them once everything starts going back to normal. So keep that in mind, kay?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/978', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B2%BB', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:44:41.334127Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:31:28.033683Z'),
(979, 'kanji', '浴', '浴', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> hits a <radical>valley</radical>, filling it up with water. Lucky for you, you''re a giant, so you just lay down in the valley and use it to <kanji>bathe</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/979', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B5%B4', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:45:07.883198Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.860415Z'),
(980, 'kanji', '笑', '笑', '<radical>Bamboo</radical> <radical>heaven</radical> is a place for <kanji>laugh</kanji>ing. Bamboo is a very jolly plant, and they love to laugh. Even after death in bamboo heaven, the bamboo continue to laugh joyfully. Bamboo heaven is truly a place of laughter.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/980', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AC%91', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:46:08.0369Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.86892Z'),
(981, 'kanji', '辞', '辞', 'When your <radical>tongue</radical> becomes too <radical>spicy</radical> for you to bear, it’s time to <kanji>quit</kanji> the jalapeño eating contest. You’ve been eating them for three hours now and the spiciness from the peppers is really burning your tongue. Throw in the towel, it’s time to quit the contest.

This kanji has another meaning too:

Eating isn''t the only thing that makes your <radical>tongue</radical> <radical>spicy</radical>, so do certain <kanji>word</kanji>s. Say your favorite words out loud right now. Feel them on your tongue. Words are the spicy food of language!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/981', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BE%9E', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:46:32.181967Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:24:37.385296Z'),
(982, 'kanji', '関', '関', 'This <radical>gate</radical> of <radical>horns</radical> will take you to <radical>heaven</radical>. These horns are <kanji>related</kanji> and <kanji>connected</kanji> to heaven in some way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/982', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%96%A2', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:47:12.071676Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.885841Z'),
(984, 'kanji', '弁', '弁', 'If you listened in to the <radical>private</radical> lives of <radical>twenty</radical> people, all of them would have different <kanji>dialect</kanji>s and ways of <kanji>speech</kanji>. Whether they actually use these dialects with you is another story. But when they''re alone, or with other people who are from the same place, their speech will be different.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/984', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%81', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:48:03.424854Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:35:32.746847Z'),
(985, 'kanji', '政', '政', 'It''s <radical>correct</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> to focus on <kanji>politics</kanji> and the <kanji>government</kanji>. Elections are happening, in the new year politicians are being sworn in. Winter is government time! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/985', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%94%BF', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:48:28.753166Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:47:17.821243Z'),
(986, 'kanji', '留', '留', 'A group of <radical>private</radical> <radical>sword</radical> guys are in your <radical>rice paddy</radical>. You must <kanji>detain</kanji> them at once! Private sword guys are people who work for private companies and use their swords on people. They''re basically paid assassins. And you''re going to detain them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/986', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%95%99', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:49:13.062473Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.912441Z'),
(987, 'kanji', '証', '証', 'What I <radical>say</radical> is <radical>correct</radical> 100%! You may use it as <kanji>evidence</kanji> in this murder case.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/987', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%BC', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:49:44.198006Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:31:27.51168Z'),
(988, 'kanji', '険', '険', 'Going into a <radical>building</radical> made by a <radical>squid</radical> is <kanji>risky</kanji>. First of all, squids only have tentacles, no bones, and they make all of their buildings out of risky materials like kelp and sharp coral.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/988', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%BA', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:50:47.895499Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.944292Z'),
(989, 'kanji', '危', '危', 'There is a <radical>prison</radical> on a <radical>cliff</radical> surrounded by <radical>snake</radical>s. Now that''s a <kanji>dangerous</kanji> place. If you try to escape the prison you either fall to your death off the cliff or get bitten/squeezed to death by the snakes. Can you think of anything more dangerous than this? I can''t.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/989', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%B1', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:51:20.778865Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T16:26:38.368659Z'),
(990, 'kanji', '存', '存', 'If a <radical>narwhal</radical> <radical>stick</radical>s a <radical>child</radical> with its horn then you have to admit that it <kanji>exist</kanji>s. We know some of you have been doubting narwhals and whether they''re real. Well now your kid is a kabob, so do you believe they exist now? I <kanji>suppose</kanji> you do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/990', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AD%98', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:52:46.823219Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:54:31.659958Z'),
(991, 'kanji', '専', '専', 'Sticking a <radical>cross</radical> into a <radical>rice paddy</radical> to get its <radical>measurement</radical>s is your <kanji>specialty</kanji>. Most people get really confused when they have to measure their rice paddies, but that''s where you come in. You plant a cross right in the middle, look down it from one end to the other, and voila! You know the measurements immediately. That''s why it''s your specialty!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/991', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%82', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:53:12.023381Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.997507Z'),
(992, 'kanji', '冒', '冒', 'When the <radical>sun</radical> gets in your <radical>eye</radical>, I <kanji>dare</kanji> you to stare at it. I dare you to look right at the sun. I dare you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/992', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%86%92', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:53:45.602516Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.007388Z'),
(993, 'kanji', '冗', '冗', 'A <radical>forehead</radical> <radical>table</radical> is completely <kanji>superfluous</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/993', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%86%97', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:54:21.429022Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.014375Z'),
(994, 'kanji', '阪', '阪', 'The place that <radical>buildings</radical> feel very <radical>anti</radical>- towards is <kanji>Osaka</kanji>. Osaka is the last place they want to be built. Why? Because chances are they''ll be built on a <kanji>slope</kanji>. Osaka has a lot of big slopes, and buildings hate being built on slopes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/994', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%98%AA', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:54:46.61796Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:35:32.746847Z'),
(1153, 'kanji', '企', '企', 'The <radical>hat</radical> comes to a <radical>stop</radical>, thinking of a <kanji>plan</kanji>. The hat had been pacing around for a while, trying to think of how to get off the head he''s resting upon and back into the house where it''s nice and cozy, and came to the sudden realization of a plan. This caused the hat to stop.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1153', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BC%81', 16, 1, '2012-12-04T06:28:24.258456Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.022196Z'),
(837, 'kanji', '悲', '悲', 'A <radical>criminal</radical>''s <radical>heart</radical> will always be <kanji>sad</kanji>. No one chooses a life of crime if they''re happy. And even if they seem like they''re doing fine on the outside, deep down they''re really sad.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/837', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%82%B2', 17, 1, '2012-05-01T19:52:54Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:52.619827Z'),
(976, 'kanji', '喜', '喜', 'If there is a <radical>drum</radical> in your <radical>mouth</radical> it''s time to <kanji>rejoice</kanji>! And because of your mouth drum your rejoicing will be loud and booming!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/976', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%96%9C', 17, 1, '2012-09-20T18:43:07.448132Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.518785Z'),
(995, 'kanji', '原', '原', 'The <radical>original</radical> radical and the <kanji>original</kanji> kanji are the same!

This kanji can also mean <kanji>field</kanji>, probably because that''s what everything originally looked like before humans: just a bunch of fields.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/995', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8E%9F', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:45:30.618587Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.926623Z'),
(996, 'kanji', '細', '細', 'If you take a <radical>thread</radical> and plant a <radical>rice paddy</radical> on it, the rice paddy is going to be extremely <kanji>thin</kanji>, but also <kanji>detailed</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/996', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%B0', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:46:09.064734Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:47:55.594029Z'),
(997, 'kanji', '薬', '薬', '<radical>Flowers</radical> can be used for <radical>comfort</radical>, but also for <kanji>medicine</kanji>. Sure, it’s a comfort to smell flowers because they smell nice, but one can also use them as medicine to heal people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/997', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%96%AC', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:48:00.597773Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T06:01:47.429858Z'),
(998, 'kanji', '鼻', '鼻', 'You''re all by your<radical>self</radical> and you have to check your <radical>rice paddie</radical>s — all <radical>twenty</radical> of them! Luckily, you have an incredible <kanji>nose</kanji>, so you can quickly assess the state of your twenty rice paddies with just a few sniffs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/998', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%BC%BB', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:48:31.026035Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:23:36.271314Z'),
(999, 'kanji', '側', '側', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of the <radical>shellfish</radical> has a <radical>knife</radical> in its <kanji>side</kanji>. It''s fine though, don''t worry, it''s just a reminder that he won the great shellfish war back when they were trying to choose the new leader.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/999', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%81%B4', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:48:59.500426Z', NULL, '2026-01-24T18:37:28.674226Z'),
(1000, 'kanji', '兵', '兵', 'When you swing your <radical>axe</radical>, the <radical>ground</radical> ends up covered in <radical>fins</radical>, because you''re a <kanji>soldier</kanji>. Only a skilled axe soldier could make such precise cuts as to de-fin a bunch of fish like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1000', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%B5', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:49:28.123286Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:54:26.880172Z'),
(1001, 'kanji', '堂', '堂', 'A <radical>Viking</radical> will empty his <radical>mouth</radical> of all its <radical>dirt</radical> in the great <kanji>hall</kanji>. They all get together in some kind of <kanji>public chamber</kanji> and dish all the dirt (literal and figurative) that they''ve got.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1001', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A0%82', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:49:56.848494Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.694147Z'),
(1002, 'kanji', '塩', '塩', 'In the <radical>dirt</radical>, there''s a <radical>gun</radical>. Its <radical>mouth</radical> points toward a <radical>plate</radical>. What''s on the plate? Why, it''s <kanji>salt</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1002', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A1%A9', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:55:02.929607Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:43:11.578579Z'),
(1003, 'kanji', '席', '席', 'In a <radical>yurt</radical> you use a <radical>towel</radical> as a <kanji>seat</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1003', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B8%AD', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:55:31.151223Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:30:22.144103Z'),
(1004, 'kanji', '敗', '敗', 'A <radical>shellfish</radical> that can''t live through <radical>winter</radical> is a <kanji>failure</kanji>. Any proper shellfish would survive through the winter, so this is quite an embarrassing <kanji>defeat</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1004', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%95%97', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:56:00.698111Z', NULL, '2026-02-01T19:10:02.345405Z'),
(1005, 'kanji', '果', '果', 'The <radical>fruit</radical> radical and the <kanji>fruit</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Bodacious!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1005', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9E%9C', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T19:56:39.160516Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:40:16.336543Z'),
(1007, 'kanji', '梅', '梅', 'The <radical>tree</radical> you aim your <radical>gun</radical> at from your <radical>window</radical> is an <kanji>ume</kanji>, aka a <kanji>Japanese plum</kanji>. Why? Because shooting the ume out of Japanese plum trees is, in your opinion, the best way to harvest plums.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1007', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A2%85', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:00:14.077449Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:56:52.161564Z'),
(1008, 'kanji', '無', '無', 'The <radical>nothing</radical> radical and the <kanji>nothing</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Woah!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1008', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%84%A1', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:00:38.160463Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:33:18.509083Z'),
(1009, 'kanji', '結', '結', 'You use a <radical>thread</radical> for <radical>good luck</radical> by <kanji>bind</kanji>ing two fingers together. You <kanji>tie</kanji> them nice and tight.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1009', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B5%90', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:01:09.288456Z', NULL, '2025-12-29T07:44:55.759605Z'),
(1010, 'kanji', '因', '因', 'Your <radical>mouth</radical> is so <radical>big</radical> that it''s the <kanji>cause</kanji> of all your problems.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1010', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9B%A0', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:01:37.150265Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:23:47.186329Z'),
(1011, 'kanji', '常', '常', 'A <radical>viking</radical> with a <radical>mouth</radical> <radical>towel</radical> is a totally <kanji>normal</kanji> thing. It''s <kanji>ordinary</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1011', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B8%B8', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:02:06.244072Z', NULL, '2025-10-21T00:18:11.653601Z'),
(1012, 'kanji', '識', '識', 'If you can <radical>say</radical> what <radical>sound</radical> a <radical>drunkard</radical> makes, you must be very <kanji>discerning</kanji>. Few people are able to discern what sound a drunkard makes, but you — you can say, because you''re a very <kanji>discriminating</kanji> listener.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1012', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AD%98', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:02:45.035293Z', NULL, '2026-01-14T17:48:29.910225Z'),
(1013, 'kanji', '非', '非', 'A <radical>criminal</radical> has done an <kanji>injustice</kanji> on the world or other people. Being a criminal is a <kanji>negative</kanji> thing, and even if it was just a <kanji>mistake</kanji>, it was still a crime.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1013', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9D%9E', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:03:12.214437Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.762455Z'),
(1014, 'kanji', '干', '干', 'The <radical>dry</radical> radical and the <kanji>dry</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1014', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B9%B2', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:05:12.066638Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.772452Z'),
(1015, 'kanji', '是', '是', 'The <radical>sun</radical> is <radical>correct</radical>, <kanji>absolutely</kanji>. The mighty sun is never wrong. It is absolutely correct, always.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1015', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%98%AF', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:05:40.820188Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:31:27.51168Z'),
(1016, 'kanji', '渉', '渉', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> is coming, and you want to <radical>walk</radical> over it, so you have to <kanji>ford</kanji> it. You don’t walk across a tsunami, you <kanji>wade</kanji> across it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1016', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B8%89', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:06:07.500957Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.793856Z'),
(1018, 'kanji', '官', '官', 'Under this <radical>roof</radical> is a <radical>bear</radical> who works for the <kanji>government</kanji>. If you saw a bear out in the wild you might be afraid and try to run, but if you see a bear inside a building under a roof, don''t be scared! It''s just a government bear.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1018', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%98', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:07:22.076743Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T02:00:25.083517Z'),
(1019, 'kanji', '察', '察', 'If you''re on the <radical>roof</radical> of the <radical>festival</radical> you''ll have to <kanji>guess</kanji> what''s actually going on below you. You can kind of see that there are rides and maybe food? It smells like food. But you have to guess on the specifics because you''re above it and not in it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1019', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%9F', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:07:47.567835Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.809045Z'),
(1020, 'kanji', '底', '底', 'Under a <radical>canopy</radical> your <radical>clan</radical> digs in the <radical>ground</radical>, trying to get to the <kanji>bottom</kanji>. They want to build a nice home your entire clan can live in, but they need to get to the bottom before they''ll start building.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1020', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%95', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:08:15.511656Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.810057Z'),
(1021, 'kanji', '愛', '愛', 'Put on <radical>cleat</radical>s so you can plant a kiss on your sweetheart''s <radical>forehead</radical> in the <radical>heart</radical> of <radical>winter</radical> — that''s <kanji>love</kanji>. You''ll be braving the harsh winter elements for love, so you put on your cleats to get a better grip on the snow. Sure, you could just wait for better weather to go see them, but then it wouldn''t be true love, would it?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1021', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%84%9B', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:08:51.309612Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:28:57.389299Z'),
(1022, 'kanji', '署', '署', 'This <radical>net</radical> is for <radical>someone</radical> who works in a <kanji>government office</kanji>. You''ll also take someone from a <kanji>political office</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1022', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BD%B2', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:09:19.892888Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:24:21.947579Z'),
(1023, 'kanji', '警', '警', 'You have a <radical>poem</radical> about <radical>winter</radical> that you <radical>say</radical> to <kanji>warn</kanji> people against going outside. It''s cold and dangerous in winter! You <kanji>admonish</kanji> anyone and everyone you see going out there with your scary winter poem.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1023', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AD%A6', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:09:47.810287Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T22:01:53.679588Z'),
(1024, 'kanji', '恋', '恋', '<radical>Again</radical> and again, your <radical>heart</radical> comes back to the same person. Is this <kanji>romance</kanji>? Are you in <kanji>love</kanji>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1024', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%81%8B', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:10:19.746084Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:56:35.426965Z'),
(1025, 'kanji', '覚', '覚', '<radical>Viking</radical> <radical>see</radical>, Viking <kanji>memorize</kanji>. That’s how Vikings learn things. They see something, and then they memorize it. Quite simple, really.

It can also mean <kanji>awake</kanji>. Just remember that Vikings can''t see if they aren''t awake. There won''t be anything to memorize if their eyes are still closed, after all!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1025', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A6%9A', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:10:42.466578Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:23:41.803791Z'),
(1026, 'kanji', '説', '説', 'What do you <radical>say</radical> to a <radical>guard</radical>? Your <kanji>theory</kanji> about how the thing he''s guarding isn''t even real. Yeah, you have this whole thing figured out. You have for a while. At this point, it''s only fair that you tell your theory to the guard who''s wasting his time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1026', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AA%AC', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:11:16.264319Z', NULL, '2025-11-19T09:43:48.890063Z'),
(1027, 'kanji', '幻', '幻', 'You hide <radical>poop</radical> in a <radical>cape</radical> as part of the <kanji>illusion</kanji> in your magic show. Your acts are kind of gross and this one involves creating the illusion of disappearing poop by hiding it within the folds of your cape.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1027', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B9%BB', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T20:11:41.670399Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.86503Z'),
(1412, 'kanji', '詳', '詳', 'They <radical>say</radical> <radical>sheep</radical> are <kanji>detailed</kanji> creatures. I mean, I never thought sheep were very detailed, but that’s what they say.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1412', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A9%B3', 17, 1, '2013-01-24T21:17:50.367065Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.718361Z'),
(1569, 'kanji', '劇', '劇', 'A <radical>tiger</radical> kills a <radical>pig</radical> with a <radical>knife</radical> in this new <kanji>drama</kanji> about the animal kingdom! The tiger and pig used to be friends when they were younger, but now they''re bitter enemies and the tiger just stabbed the pig right in the chest with a knife.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1569', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8A%87', 17, 1, '2013-04-05T18:10:28.221035Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T16:24:29.442417Z'),
(1028, 'kanji', '訓', '訓', 'You <radical>say</radical> stuff to a <radical>river</radical>, giving it <kanji>instruction</kanji>. This river is a new river so it needs you to say things, giving it instruction on what to do. Otherwise the river wouldn’t know how to behave.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1028', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%93', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:47:01.425662Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:20:13.242067Z'),
(1030, 'kanji', '弓', '弓', 'The <radical>bow</radical> radical and the <kanji>bow</kanji> kanji are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1030', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%93', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:48:17.068441Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.87014Z'),
(1031, 'kanji', '告', '告', 'You see a <radical>slide</radical> covered in <radical>dirt</radical> and use your <radical>mouth</radical> to clean it off. Then, you <kanji>announce</kanji> to everyone that the slide is clean and they can use it safely again.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1031', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%91%8A', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:48:51.509476Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:33.324724Z'),
(1032, 'kanji', '種', '種', 'If a <radical>grain</radical> seems extra <radical>heavy</radical>, you know it''s a different <kanji>kind</kanji>. Not all <kanji>type</kanji>s of grains are the same. So when you feel the heavy type, you know it isn''t the same as the light kind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1032', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A8%AE', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:49:18.59701Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.891984Z'),
(1033, 'kanji', '達', '達', 'You ride your <radical>scooter</radical> through the <radical>dirt</radical> while carrying a <radical>sheep</radical> because you''re determined to <kanji>attain</kanji> something. Greatness? Glory? Everlasting fame? Maybe you''ll attain a <kanji>plural</kanji> combination of all those things. After all, you <em>are</em> the best sheep-carrying off-road scooterist the world has ever seen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1033', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%94', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:49:47.102609Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:06:04.17169Z'),
(1034, 'kanji', '類', '類', 'The <radical>rice</radical> you feed to a <radical>big</radical> <radical>geoduck</radical> can be of any <kanji>type</kanji>, <kanji>kind</kanji>, or <kanji>category</kanji>. It doesn''t matter what kind of rice it is, the big geoduck will eat it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1034', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A1%9E', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:50:17.794765Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(1035, 'kanji', '報', '報', 'You want to share <radical>happiness</radical> with people so you <radical>stamp</radical> <radical>stool</radical>s with the <kanji>news</kanji>. Now when people want a <kanji>report</kanji> of all the happy things going on, they can just look down before they sit.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1035', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A0%B1', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:50:49.357231Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.923148Z'),
(1037, 'kanji', '等', '等', 'The <radical>bamboo</radical> <radical>temple</radical> is <kanji>equal</kanji> to all of the other non-bamboo temples. Just because it''s made of bamboo doesn''t make it any less special and spiritual.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1037', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AD%89', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:52:42.032739Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.924898Z'),
(1038, 'kanji', '汽', '汽', 'The water from a <radical>tsunami</radical> can create a lot of <radical>energy</radical> when made into <kanji>steam</kanji>. Steam power used to be all the rage back in the day. Steam comes from water, water comes from tsunamis, so logically, tsunamis are a great source of energy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1038', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B1%BD', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:53:12.127977Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.926139Z'),
(1039, 'kanji', '借', '借', 'Your <radical>leader</radical> from <radical>long ago</radical> thought to <kanji>borrow</kanji> something precious from you. This leader did not return the thing he borrowed, and now he will pay.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1039', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%80%9F', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:53:49.108344Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:44:41.92583Z'),
(1040, 'kanji', '焼', '焼', 'In a dangerous <radical>fire</radical>, a <radical>gambler</radical> will always try to <kanji>bake</kanji> something before fleeing to safety. It could be his own house on fire, and the gambler would still use the free heat source to get some baking done. Maybe he''s gambling that those baked goods will offset the cost of a new home…

This kanji also means <kanji>burn</kanji>, because that''s what happens when you expose something to a lot of heat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1040', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%84%BC', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:54:14.758615Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.931734Z'),
(1041, 'kanji', '座', '座', 'Under a <radical>canopy</radical>, there are two <radical>person</radical>s on the <radical>dirt</radical>. What do they do there? They <kanji>sit</kanji>. The people use the dirt under the canopy as a <kanji>seat</kanji> for their butts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1041', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%A7', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:54:38.580031Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:52:04.601504Z'),
(1042, 'kanji', '忘', '忘', 'The <radical>death</radical> of your <radical>heart</radical> will cause you to <kanji>forget</kanji> all of the most important things. People think memories are kept in your brain, but that''s not true. Your heart is where all of your most precious memories are kept, and when it dies, you forget them all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1042', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BF%98', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:55:04.012675Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.954Z'),
(1043, 'kanji', '洗', '洗', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> <radical>previous</radical>ly gave everything in this town a good <kanji>wash</kanji>. There are many laundromats in this town, so when the previous tsunami hit, all of the detergent mixed with the water and gave everything a good wash.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1043', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B4%97', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:55:28.185819Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:37:53.327102Z'),
(1044, 'kanji', '胸', '胸', 'In a <radical>moon</radical> <radical>prison</radical>, there lies a <radical>treasure chest</radical> containing a golden <kanji>chest</kanji> piece. This mighty chest armor will grant you protection from nearly any attack, because it is imbued with moon magic.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1044', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%83%B8', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:56:11.448725Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(1045, 'kanji', '脳', '脳', 'Nestled in the light of the <radical>moon</radical> in the <radical>grass</radical> is a <radical>treasure chest</radical> that you can see with your <kanji>brain</kanji>. You close your eyes, and you can see the spot, but it''s kept deep in your brain, and you have to use its power to find it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1045', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%84%B3', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:56:44.831313Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:10:08.00652Z'),
(1046, 'kanji', '僧', '僧', 'A religious <radical>leader</radical> wearing a <radical>mask</radical> could be either a <kanji>monk</kanji> or a <kanji>priest</kanji>. You don’t know, their face is covered by a mask!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1046', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%83%A7', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:57:11.452926Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.985761Z'),
(1047, 'kanji', '禅', '禅', 'You want your <radical>spirit</radical> to be <radical>simple</radical> so you practice <kanji>Zen Buddhism</kanji>. Zen is all about meditating and letting earthly things go. There''s nothing more simple for your spirit than being <kanji>Zen</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1047', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A6%85', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:57:35.301513Z', NULL, '2025-12-21T20:51:22.632515Z'),
(1049, 'kanji', '可', '可', 'With a <radical>lip ring</radical>, anything is <kanji>possible</kanji>. Having a lip ring grants you the power to conquer any obstacle, achieve any goal. As long as it''s a <kanji>passable</kanji> lip ring, that is. If it isn''t good enough, it isn''t possible to be passable.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1049', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%AF', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:58:20.98319Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:12:31.378587Z'),
(1050, 'kanji', '許', '許', '<radical>Say</radical> it at <radical>noon</radical>. That''s the only time I will <kanji>allow</kanji> you to say it. That''s the only time I will <kanji>permit</kanji> you to say it. Other than that, no saying anything. Noon is your window.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1050', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%B1', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:58:41.002775Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.998329Z'),
(1051, 'kanji', '枚', '枚', 'A <radical>tree</radical> that''s been sliced up in <radical>winter</radical> can be used as a <kanji>flat object counter</kanji>. Since it''s winter, all the leaves have fallen off the tree, so it''s easy to cut into slats. These slats can be used as counters for flat objects.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1051', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9E%9A', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:59:01.437236Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:12:20.175784Z'),
(1052, 'kanji', '静', '静', 'Within the <radical>blue</radical> water, a <radical>conflict</radical> stirs, but is made <kanji>quiet</kanji> by the muffling power of liquid. Under this blue water is a great conflict between two sea monsters, but on the surface it appears peaceful and quiet as the water prevents sound from rising above the surface.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1052', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9D%99', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:59:26.031786Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:22:25.515687Z'),
(1053, 'kanji', '句', '句', 'The <radical>paragraph</radical> radical and the <kanji>paragraph</kanji> kanji are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1053', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%A5', 18, 1, '2012-10-31T23:59:44.86707Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T01:16:01.747685Z'),
(1054, 'kanji', '禁', '禁', 'Killing two <radical>tree</radical>s with a <radical>jackhammer</radical> is expressly <kanji>prohibit</kanji>ed. Don''t get me wrong, you can rip up one tree with your jackhammer, but not two.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1054', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A6%81', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:00:05.45067Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.032397Z'),
(1055, 'kanji', '喫', '喫', 'In your <radical>mouth</radical> you put your <radical>life</radical> on the line by putting in a <radical>sword</radical> that is <radical>big</radical>. You put it all the way down there, so you <kanji>consume</kanji> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1055', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%96%AB', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:00:33.605107Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:35:51.809207Z'),
(1056, 'kanji', '煙', '煙', 'If you have a <radical>fire</radical> and a <radical>helicopter</radical> hovering beside it, there''s going to be a lot of <radical>dirt</radical> and <kanji>smoke</kanji> billowing around. Mostly smoke, on account of the fire. Now it''s in your eyes and mouth. It gets in your lungs and you can''t breathe. Why, helicopter, why!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1056', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%85%99', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:00:54.963514Z', NULL, '2026-02-03T19:17:48.539719Z'),
(1057, 'kanji', '加', '加', 'The <radical>power</radical> of your <radical>mouth</radical> lets you <kanji>add</kanji> words together to form a sentence. Marvel at the mighty additive power your mouth holds! You add words together in an amazing fashion!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1057', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8A%A0', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:14:58.81011Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:15:08.523382Z'),
(1058, 'kanji', '節', '節', 'On the bottom of <radical>bamboo</radical> <radical>root</radical>s, there’s a <radical>stamp</radical> that tells you the <kanji>season</kanji> in which it was cut.

Additionally, the stamp also has information on the condition of the bamboo’s <kanji>joint</kanji>s, because no one wants to buy bamboo with damaged joints! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1058', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AF%80', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:15:32.43903Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:15:32.847277Z'),
(1059, 'kanji', '減', '減', 'Direct a <radical>tsunami</radical> into a <radical>canine</radical>''s <radical>mouth</radical> to <kanji>decrease</kanji> the amount of water that strikes the surrounding area.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1059', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B8%9B', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:16:09.630086Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:58:11.893906Z'),
(1060, 'kanji', '順', '順', 'When they migrate to a <radical>river</radical>, a group of <radical>geoducks</radical> must do so in a certain <kanji>order</kanji> and <kanji>sequence</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1060', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A0%86', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:16:44.05234Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(1061, 'kanji', '容', '容', 'If you put a <radical>roof</radical> over a <radical>valley</radical> it will change its <kanji>form</kanji>. Its <kanji>appearance</kanji> will alter completely! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1061', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%B9', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:17:13.731812Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:32:56.473524Z'),
(1062, 'kanji', '布', '布', 'To a <radical>narwhal</radical>, a <radical>towel</radical> is just normal <kanji>cloth</kanji>. They''re always wet so towel cloth is all they know.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1062', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B8%83', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:17:54.251927Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.08041Z'),
(1063, 'kanji', '易', '易', 'The <radical>easy</radical> radical and the <kanji>easy</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1063', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%98%93', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:18:22.560011Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.095578Z'),
(1064, 'kanji', '財', '財', 'Give some <radical>shellfish</radical> to a <radical>talent</radical>ed person and they''ll have no problem accumulating <kanji>wealth</kanji>. People with talent can make money out of pretty much anything, so shellfish are like easy-mode. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1064', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%A1', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:19:00.558248Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T16:59:59.475914Z'),
(1065, 'kanji', '若', '若', 'There are <radical>flowers</radical> on your <radical>right</radical> to show that you''re still <kanji>young</kanji>. Your body still works, you''re happy, you have your entire life ahead of you! And everything smells like flowers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1065', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8B%A5', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:19:29.490954Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T23:19:07.224812Z'),
(1066, 'kanji', '詞', '詞', 'What you <radical>say</radical> to the <radical>director</radical> is judged immediately. You''re having trouble understanding this <kanji>part of speech</kanji>, so he''s trying to help you. But you say it over and over and he keeps saying you''re wrong.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1066', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A9%9E', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:19:57.746284Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:11:36.579973Z'),
(1067, 'kanji', '昆', '昆', 'If the <radical>sun</radical> is <radical>compare</radical>d to an <kanji>insect</kanji>, you’ll see that they''re both shiny, but that the sun is much shinier than the insect.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1067', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%98%86', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:20:28.113277Z', NULL, '2026-02-14T20:46:35.876946Z'),
(1068, 'kanji', '閥', '閥', 'Passed out in front of your <radical>gate</radical> lies a former <radical>leader</radical>. He''s now a <radical>drunkard</radical>, but once ran a popular <kanji>clique</kanji> called the Cool Kids <kanji>Clan</kanji>. The clan has fallen apart, and he no longer has a clique to call his own.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1068', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%96%A5', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:21:03.161261Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:21:27.137656Z'),
(1069, 'kanji', '歴', '歴', 'At the edge of the <radical>cliff</radical>, two <radical>tree</radical>s mark the spot where you must <radical>stop</radical>, since any <kanji>continuation</kanji> requires a way across. The trees seem long enough to reach to the other side, but how are you going to chop them down?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1069', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AD%B4', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:22:05.103323Z', NULL, '2026-02-07T16:21:40.056516Z'),
(1070, 'kanji', '舌', '舌', 'The <radical>tongue</radical> radical and the <kanji>tongue</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Yee-haw!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1070', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%88%8C', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:22:29.253707Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.138699Z'),
(1071, 'kanji', '冊', '冊', 'You use a <radical>bookshelf</radical> as a <kanji>book counter</kanji>. Duh. What, are you gonna count them one by one? Okay, well maybe, but you should count them while they''re nice and organized on the shelves. Otherwise counting is going to be a huge pain.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1071', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%86%8A', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:22:56.926652Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.138201Z'),
(1072, 'kanji', '宇', '宇', 'You use a <radical>roof</radical> to keep yourself <radical>dry</radical> and safe from <kanji>outer space</kanji>. Look outside, above your roof, and you''ll see outer space stretching above you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1072', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%87', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:23:22.420651Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.140849Z'),
(1073, 'kanji', '宙', '宙', 'You jumped off your <radical>roof</radical> for some <radical>reason</radical> and now you''re floating in <kanji>midair</kanji>! You kick your legs a bit and move your arms like you''re in water, but you''re still just stuck there in midair.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1073', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%99', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:23:53.492835Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.14879Z'),
(1074, 'kanji', '忙', '忙', 'You''ll work your <radical>soul</radical> to <radical>death</radical> if you''re too <kanji>busy</kanji>. Being busy just isn''t good for your soul. Take breaks. Go on vacation. Don''t be so busy that you destroy yourself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1074', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BF%99', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:24:21.078043Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(1075, 'kanji', '履', '履', 'A <radical>flag</radical> <radical>loiter</radical>s in a <radical>black hole</radical>. This flag marks (in the black hole) where you left your <kanji>footwear</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1075', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B1%A5', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:24:47.670907Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:02:59.69948Z'),
(1076, 'kanji', '団', '団', 'Use your <radical>mouth</radical> to take a <radical>measurement</radical> of this <kanji>group</kanji>. Just stretch your mouth out and around every single person. Get ''em all in there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1076', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9B%A3', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:25:12.943253Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T07:34:32.079479Z'),
(1077, 'kanji', '暴', '暴', 'When the <radical>sun</radical> gets <radical>together</radical> with <radical>water</radical>, only <kanji>violence</kanji> can ensue. They don’t mix well together. The sun loves to fight water and water loves to fight the sun. Lots and lots of violence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1077', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9A%B4', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:26:07.72872Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:59:46.609728Z'),
(1078, 'kanji', '混', '混', 'A raging <radical>tsunami</radical> rises next to the calm <radical>sun</radical>. When you try to <radical>compare</radical> these two things, it''s clear that they cannot <kanji>mix</kanji>. If a tsunami is coming, it will likely get in the way of the sun.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1078', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B7%B7', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:28:32.740816Z', NULL, '2026-02-14T20:46:35.879017Z'),
(1079, 'kanji', '乱', '乱', 'If you stab somebody in the <radical>tongue</radical> with an <radical>umbrella</radical>, it’s sure to start a <kanji>riot</kanji> and cause <kanji>disorder</kanji>. Nobody likes to get stabbed in the tongue with an umbrella, so they’re probably going to retaliate, and before you know it, you’ve a full scale riot on your hands. Look what you’ve done.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1079', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B9%B1', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:29:02.078417Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.195034Z'),
(1080, 'kanji', '徒', '徒', '“Don’t <radical>loiter</radical> – <radical>run</radical>, <kanji>Junior</kanji>!” said Indiana Jones Senior to Indiana Jones Junior. They’re escaping from a sticky situation and Junior was just loitering around like a fool. It’s no time to loiter, it’s time to run!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1080', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%92', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:29:26.65043Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:38:57.03621Z'),
(1081, 'kanji', '得', '得', 'If you <radical>loiter</radical> around the <radical>sun</radical> <radical>temple</radical>, it''s likely you''ll <kanji>acquire</kanji> something. When you loiter around like that it looks like you''re waiting for something, so if someone puts something in your hands and you just happen to acquire it, well, no one can fault you for that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1081', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%97', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:29:57.492754Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.204893Z'),
(1082, 'kanji', '改', '改', 'It''s important to focus <radical>oneself</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> so that one can emerge <kanji>renew</kanji>ed! You can''t really go outside in winter without getting cold or sick, so spend winter focusing on YOU.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1082', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%94%B9', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:30:19.800279Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.207102Z'),
(1083, 'kanji', '続', '続', 'You have to gather <radical>thread</radical> to <radical>sell</radical> if you want to <kanji>continue</kanji> living. You want to make a living, don''t you? Make sure you continue to sell that thread every single day!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1083', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B6%9A', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:31:38.856995Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:18:52.008491Z'),
(1084, 'kanji', '連', '連', 'Your <radical>scooter</radical> and your <radical>car</radical> is what you always <kanji>take along</kanji> with you. Make sure they''re <kanji>connect</kanji>ed well so you don''t lose them! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1084', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%A3', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:32:04.83094Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T08:20:55.720034Z'),
(1085, 'kanji', '善', '善', 'A <radical>sheep</radical> loses its <radical>horns</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical> so it picks them up in its <radical>mouth</radical> to take them to someone who''s <kanji>morally good</kanji> enough to attach them back onto its head. Sheep don’t have hands, so the sheep had to pick the horns up off the ground with his mouth. Someone evil would just murder the sheep and steal his horns, so the sheep must find someone <kanji>good</kanji> to reaffix them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1085', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%96%84', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:32:32.054413Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.248871Z'),
(1087, 'kanji', '絡', '絡', 'If you put a <radical>thread</radical> in the middle of a <radical>kiss</radical>, you’re going to <kanji>entangle</kanji> it. Things can get pretty messy in a kiss, so adding a thread to the mix will definitely entangle it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1087', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B5%A1', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:33:19.860267Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:20:45.121776Z'),
(1121, 'kanji', '笛', '笛', 'Within the <radical>bamboo</radical> grove, someone asks your <radical>reason</radical> for being there, and you explain it''s to harvest bamboo to craft a <kanji>flute</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1121', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AC%9B', 19, 1, '2012-11-15T19:01:11.951932Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:20:17.187516Z'),
(1122, 'kanji', '史', '史', 'The <radical>history</radical> radical and the <kanji>history</kanji> kanji are the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1122', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%B2', 19, 1, '2012-11-20T23:52:12.384643Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.482087Z'),
(1553, 'kanji', '芋', '芋', 'If you see some <radical>flowers</radical> that are <radical>dry</radical>, pull them up and you''ll find a <kanji>potato</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1553', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8A%8B', 19, 1, '2013-04-05T17:59:06.336386Z', NULL, '2025-10-23T04:02:13.447099Z'),
(1725, 'kanji', '埋', '埋', 'The <radical>dirt</radical> next to your <radical>village</radical> is where you <kanji>bury</kanji> the dead. People don''t live forever, so every village has a patch of dirt specifically used for burying dead things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1725', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9F%8B', 19, 1, '2013-06-07T17:20:41.170101Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.073817Z'),
(1086, 'kanji', '困', '困', 'If you put your <radical>mouth</radical> on a <radical>tree</radical> and try to eat it, you’re probably a <kanji>distressed</kanji> and <kanji>troubled</kanji> individual (unless you’re a woodchuck). You don’t need to eat this tree, you have plenty of food at home. Get your mouth off that tree you distressed child.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1086', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9B%B0', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T03:32:55.392589Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.252719Z'),
(1089, 'kanji', '災', '災', 'A <radical>flood</radical> and a <radical>fire</radical>! At the same time? This is a <kanji>disaster</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1089', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%81%BD', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:26:50.73623Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.265668Z'),
(1090, 'kanji', '機', '機', 'Next to a <radical>tree</radical>, you spot <radical>poop</radical>, not once but twice. Only a <radical>drunkard</radical> would be the type of <radical>person</radical> to not use the nearby <kanji>machine</kanji> toilet. There''s a perfectly good outhouse with a machine toilet right there, but this drunkard is so drunk that he decides to poop under the tree instead. And not only does he poop once, he poops twice.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1090', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A9%9F', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:27:29.577418Z', NULL, '2025-12-29T01:51:12.19899Z'),
(1091, 'kanji', '率', '率', 'Sitting on the <radical>lid</radical> of a toilet, you''re praying your <radical>poop</radical> <radical>explosion</radical> will be flushed away, beseeching the <radical>cross</radical> decorating the bathroom wall. The poop needs to be one hundred <kanji>percent</kanji> flushed away because you''re on a date, and you don''t want even one percent of your explosion left for them to see.

', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1091', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8E%87', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:28:15.203293Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.274499Z'),
(1092, 'kanji', '飛', '飛', 'The <radical>fly</radical> radical and the <kanji>fly</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Awesome!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1092', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A3%9B', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:28:54.335855Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.280212Z'),
(1093, 'kanji', '害', '害', 'You slip off the <radical>roof</radical>, your <radical>life</radical> flashes before your eyes, and you smash your <radical>mouth</radical> on the ground causing some serious <kanji>damage</kanji>. Oof, this is a really bad <kanji>injury</kanji>, you fell all the way off the roof and onto your mouth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1093', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%B3', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:29:33.688878Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.473199Z'),
(1094, 'kanji', '余', '余', 'Regardless of whether or not you wear a <radical>hat</radical> while you <radical>jackhammer</radical>, you’re going to have a <kanji>surplus</kanji> of noise. Jackhammers are loud, and your hat is doing nothing to dampen the racket. There’s a surplus of commotion coming from the jackhammer and onlookers are not amused.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1094', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BD%99', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:30:10.6463Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.310255Z'),
(1095, 'kanji', '難', '難', 'A <radical>Chinese</radical> <radical>turkey</radical> is <kanji>difficult</kanji> to find because they are so rare. Chinese turkeys are the rarest variety of turkey, so naturally, finding one is exceedingly difficult.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1095', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%A3', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:30:37.375524Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(1097, 'kanji', '被', '被', 'That <radical>outfit</radical> on your <radical>skin</radical> is there to <kanji>cover</kanji> you up. In polite society, it''s expected that you cover your skin with some kind of outfit — even if you have to wear something that causes you to <kanji>incur</kanji> damage, like circulation-cutting skinny jeans or a shirt with a really scratchy tag.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1097', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A2%AB', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:31:43.030912Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:41:34.122466Z'),
(1098, 'kanji', '裕', '裕', 'You have an <radical>outfit</radical> <radical>valley</radical>, which is a valley where you store your <kanji>abundant</kanji> number of outfits. Yes, you''re so <kanji>wealthy</kanji> that not only can you purchase a valley for yourself, you can also afford to use it like a walk-in closet.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1098', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A3%95', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:32:09.461491Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:25:39.144192Z'),
(1099, 'kanji', '震', '震', '<radical>Rain</radical> causes a <radical>landslide</radical> that causes an <kanji>earthquake</kanji>!!! The earth doesn''t just slide, it <kanji>shake</kanji>s and <kanji>quake</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1099', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9C%87', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:32:34.039223Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:50:20.429937Z'),
(1101, 'kanji', '尾', '尾', 'You see a <radical>flag</radical> stuck in the ground with a little bit of <radical>fur</radical> coming out from it and on closer inspection you realize it’s actually a <kanji>tail</kanji>. Someone stuck a flag into some sort of animal, pushing it under the ground. All that’s visible now is some fur and part of the tail.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1101', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%BE', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:33:19.411578Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.340292Z'),
(1102, 'kanji', '械', '械', 'Under a <radical>tree</radical> a <radical>drunkard</radical> sits with <radical>twenty</radical> little <kanji>contraptions</kanji>. There''s little electronic ones, little bopping ones, little walking ones... twenty of them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1102', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A2%B0', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:33:43.106606Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.341795Z'),
(1103, 'kanji', '確', '確', 'You take a <radical>stone</radical> to the <radical>forehead</radical> of a <radical>turkey</radical>. It is <kanji>certain</kanji> that you will damage it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1103', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A2%BA', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:34:09.321677Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T09:14:00.523598Z'),
(1104, 'kanji', '嫌', '嫌', 'When a <radical>woman</radical> sees the <radical>Mona Lisa</radical>, she feels a strong feeling of <kanji>dislike</kanji>. All women dislike the Mona Lisa because of how much attention she gets.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1104', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AB%8C', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:34:31.541347Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1105, 'kanji', '個', '個', 'The <radical>leader</radical>''s <radical>mouth</radical> has grown <radical>old</radical> from saying so many things. This is what makes them different from everyone else, they speak truly like an <kanji>individual</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1105', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%80%8B', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:35:07.337955Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:13:37.694956Z'),
(1106, 'kanji', '圧', '圧', 'Under any <radical>cliff</radical>, there''s some <radical>dirt</radical> that''s under a lot of <kanji>pressure</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1106', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9C%A7', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:35:31.070909Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:28:34.338752Z'),
(1107, 'kanji', '在', '在', 'A <radical>narwhal</radical> sticks a <radical>stick</radical> into the <radical>dirt</radical> to see what might <kanji>exist</kanji> underneath it. Narwhals live in the sea, you see. So they''re convinced nothing can exist anywhere else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1107', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9C%A8', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:36:00.19138Z', NULL, '2026-01-14T17:48:29.910225Z'),
(1108, 'kanji', '夢', '夢', 'A <radical>flowers</radical> <radical>net</radical> is upon your <radical>forehead</radical> in the <radical>evening</radical>. These appear on your head at night whenever you are having a <kanji>dream</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1108', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A4%A2', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:36:29.066854Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:07:24.955524Z'),
(1109, 'kanji', '産', '産', 'You <radical>stand</radical> on the <radical>Cliff</radical> of <radical>Life</radical> and <kanji>give birth</kanji> to a child. Oh no! You’re standing too close to the edge of the Cliff of Life! The baby falls off the cliff right after you give birth to it! In your despair, you throw all your <kanji>property</kanji> off the cliff too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1109', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%94%A3', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:36:57.876607Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T19:56:27.081206Z'),
(1110, 'kanji', '倒', '倒', 'Someone stabbed the <radical>leader</radical> <radical>mole</radical> with a <radical>knife</radical> to <kanji>overthrow</kanji> it. Now there is a new mole leader. Because that''s what happens when you overthrow someone—someone else needs to take over. In this case, a vengeful mole.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1110', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%80%92', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:37:32.719379Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.399252Z'),
(1111, 'kanji', '臭', '臭', 'Wash your<radical>self</radical> with a <radical>big</radical> bar of soap if you''re <kanji>stinking</kanji> everything up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1111', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%87%AD', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:37:54.570038Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.401968Z'),
(1112, 'kanji', '厚', '厚', 'Hanging off the edge of a <radical>cliff</radical> is a <radical>sun</radical>-worshipping <radical>child</radical> with a <kanji>thick</kanji> unibrow. Hanging off the cliff, the child cries out to the sun to save them from this cruel fate. Even the thickness of their unibrow is not enough to give the child the strength to climb back up on their own.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1112', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8E%9A', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:38:38.878539Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.4137Z'),
(1113, 'kanji', '妻', '妻', 'You place a <radical>cross</radical> on a <radical>Wolverine</radical> <radical>woman</radical>. She''s gone now, but she was once your <kanji>wife</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1113', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A6%BB', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:39:03.118773Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1114, 'kanji', '議', '議', 'What you <radical>say</radical> is <radical>righteousness</radical>, at least that''s what you believe. You keep talking and saying these things, and you don''t listen to anyone else''s words. This is your style of <kanji>deliberation</kanji> and <kanji>discussion</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1114', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AD%B0', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:39:30.639182Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.427471Z'),
(1115, 'kanji', '犯', '犯', 'In the <radical>animal</radical> kingdom, a <radical>snake</radical> is the most likely to commit a <kanji>crime</kanji>. Remember the Garden of Eden? The snake was the bad guy. Ever since then, snakes are the animal most known for committing crimes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1115', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8A%AF', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:39:55.082349Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:51:15.083936Z'),
(1116, 'kanji', '罪', '罪', 'Put a <radical>net</radical> around a <radical>criminal</radical> to show their <kanji>guilt</kanji>. Criminals look like anyone else on the street, so you decide to tie them in nets to make sure people know who they''re dealing with.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1116', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BD%AA', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:40:20.817246Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.43571Z'),
(1117, 'kanji', '防', '防', 'If there is a <radical>building</radical> in the <radical>direction</radical> you want to go it will <kanji>prevent</kanji> you from going any further. Like, come on kid, you think you can just walk right through a building? Don''t make me laugh!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1117', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%98%B2', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:41:02.681275Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.443626Z'),
(1119, 'kanji', '論', '論', 'You <radical>say</radical>, throwing your <radical>hat</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical> in front of the <radical>bookshelf</radical>, "I have a <kanji>theory</kanji>!" All this reading and thinking has finally given you a wonderful idea, a theory you think might solve all of your problems!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1119', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AB%96', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:42:13.55982Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.453707Z'),
(1120, 'kanji', '経', '経', 'Tying a <radical>thread</radical> to a <radical>tombstone</radical> each year will help you <kanji>manage</kanji> the <kanji>passage of time</kanji>. That way you’ll know how many years you <kanji>pass through</kanji>, since you’ll have one thread for each year to count.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1120', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B5%8C', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:42:40.251979Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.464068Z'),
(1296, 'kanji', '刺', '刺', 'You walk into this <radical>tree</radical> and hit your <radical>head</radical> on it, then a <radical>knife</radical> falls out of it to <kanji>stab</kanji> you. Ow! What the heck?! What was a knife doing in that tree?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1296', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%BA', 20, 1, '2013-01-17T20:37:02.620239Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:29:57.511837Z'),
(946, 'kanji', '建', '建', 'Your magic <radical>yoga</radical> <radical>brush</radical> lets you <kanji>build</kanji> what''s in your mind''s eye.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/946', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BB%BA', 21, 1, '2012-09-19T22:50:18.591507Z', NULL, '2025-12-29T00:39:15.506288Z'),
(1036, 'kanji', '祈', '祈', 'If you see a <radical>spirit</radical> with an <radical>axe</radical>, you better <kanji>pray</kanji> for your life. Spirits only hold axes if they''re thinking about murder, so pray as hard as you can to try and get it to spare you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1036', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A5%88', 21, 1, '2012-10-31T23:51:36.747878Z', NULL, '2025-12-21T20:51:22.632515Z'),
(1123, 'kanji', '敵', '敵', 'Seeing a <radical>mohawk</radical> in the <radical>winter</radical> means the <kanji>enemy</kanji> is nearby. You are from the dreadlock tribe and the mohawk tribe is your enemy. You’re deep in the heart of battle and winter has fallen. Now is the time to take out the enemy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1123', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%95%B5', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T05:55:53.965624Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.490516Z'),
(1124, 'kanji', '済', '済', 'You see a <radical>tsunami</radical> that''s <radical>simultaneous</radical> (with another tsunami, I assume) and you know the world is about to <kanji>come to an end</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1124', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B8%88', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T05:56:23.323831Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.499743Z'),
(1125, 'kanji', '委', '委', 'A <radical>grain</radical> <radical>woman</radical> is a woman who talks about grain with other women on a <kanji>committee</kanji>. Together they''re known as the Committee of Grain Women. What does this committee discuss, you wonder.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1125', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A7%94', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T05:57:08.161131Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1126, 'kanji', '挙', '挙', 'If there’s <radical>grass</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical> and <radical>fins</radical> in your <radical>hand</radical>, you need to <kanji>raise</kanji> that hand up to the sky. You’re in the middle of a fisherman’s ritual, which can only be performed on grassy ground. You take fish fins from the previous year, and raise them up to the sky in order to ensure a good fishing harvest.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1126', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8C%99', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T05:57:40.732168Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.519321Z'),
(1127, 'kanji', '判', '判', 'I hereby cut your assets in <radical>half</radical> with a <radical>knife</radical>, says the <kanji>judge</kanji>. At someone''s divorce proceedings. Ah, the oldest judge trick in the book.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1127', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%A4', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T05:58:11.099025Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:51:18.584969Z'),
(1128, 'kanji', '制', '制', '<radical>Cow</radical>s, using nothing more than a <radical>towel</radical> and a <radical>knife</radical>, have taken <kanji>control</kanji> of the world. Their <kanji>system</kanji> of control is brutal — payback for how we humans have treated them, I guess.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1128', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%B6', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T05:58:52.916715Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:37:01.42735Z'),
(1129, 'kanji', '務', '務', 'You use a special <radical>spear</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> to give yourself the <radical>power</radical> to do all of your <kanji>task</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1129', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8B%99', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T05:59:18.669652Z', NULL, '2025-10-23T03:51:56.545522Z'),
(1130, 'kanji', '査', '査', 'If a <radical>tree</radical> is growing out of a <radical>top hat</radical> you need to <kanji>inspect</kanji> it to find out why and how this happened. You should probably <kanji>investigate</kanji> the area too. I mean, how could a tree grow out of a top hat anyway? Seems suspicious.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1130', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9F%BB', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T05:59:54.831567Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T19:00:39.819098Z'),
(1131, 'kanji', '総', '総', 'Taking out your <radical>thread</radical>, you sew together the <radical>public</radical>''s <radical>heart</radical>s to make them <kanji>whole</kanji> again.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1131', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B7%8F', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:00:18.940303Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.546128Z'),
(1132, 'kanji', '設', '設', 'If you <radical>say</radical> a new <radical>weapon</radical> idea out loud, then it will be <kanji>establish</kanji>ed immediately.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1132', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%AD', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:00:50.391852Z', NULL, '2026-04-05T19:37:47.912614Z'),
(1133, 'kanji', '資', '資', 'The <radical>next</radical> <radical>shellfish</radical> will be yours, but this shellfish will be mine. These are the <kanji>resources</kanji> we have to share and distribute.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1133', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B3%87', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:01:20.10394Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.557619Z'),
(1134, 'kanji', '権', '権', 'Under a <radical>tree</radical>, a broken <radical>arrow</radical> protrudes from a <radical>turkey</radical>. It was fighting for the <kanji>rights</kanji> of its fellow turkeys, and was mortally wounded in the process.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1134', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A8%A9', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:18:56.804229Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T22:01:25.251271Z'),
(1135, 'kanji', '件', '件', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of the <radical>cow</radical> tribe is too important to deal with small <kanji>matter</kanji>s and trivial <kanji>affair</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1135', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%B6', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:19:24.213167Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:37:01.42735Z'),
(1136, 'kanji', '派', '派', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> is coming for the <radical>cliff</radical> made of <radical>tofu</radical>, but there is a special <kanji>sect</kanji> that protects this tofu cliff.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1136', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B4%BE', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:19:50.865994Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:15:23.445308Z'),
(1137, 'kanji', '岡', '岡', 'You have a scene going on inside your <radical>head</radical>. It is of a pair of <radical>horns</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical> atop a <radical>mountain</radical>. No. It is not just one pair of horns. It is many. So many that they create a giant <kanji>hill</kanji> atop the mountain, which is already pretty high.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1137', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B2%A1', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:20:18.381528Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.596533Z'),
(1138, 'kanji', '素', '素', 'The <radical>life</radical> <radical>thread</radical> is an <kanji>element</kanji> essential to all living things. This thread of life joins all things together, providing an essential relationship between all life. This element is all that holds this world together.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1138', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%A0', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:20:57.063794Z', NULL, '2026-03-07T17:32:28.468567Z'),
(1139, 'kanji', '断', '断', 'A <radical>lion</radical> harvesting <radical>rice</radical> uses an <radical>axe</radical> to <kanji>cut off</kanji> the tops of the stalks. The lion is roaming the rice fields using his axe to harvest the rice. He does so by cutting off the part of the rice stalks he needs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1139', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%96%AD', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:21:49.103232Z', NULL, '2026-02-07T02:28:43.98639Z'),
(1141, 'kanji', '批', '批', 'If you use your <radical>fingers</radical> to point when you <radical>compare</radical> things you''re really just throwing around <kanji>criticism</kanji>. You should never need to point fingers when you''re just comparing things. If you''re pointing and picking and being mean, that''s criticism.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1141', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%89%B9', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:22:47.000468Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.618056Z'),
(1142, 'kanji', '任', '任', 'Both a <radical>leader</radical> and a <radical>king</radical> have a <kanji>duty</kanji> to protect their people. A good leader always protects his followers, and a good king always protects his subjects. This is their duty.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1142', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%BB', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:23:24.806168Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:51:43.089307Z'),
(1143, 'kanji', '検', '検', 'You see a <radical>tree</radical> with a <radical>squid</radical> on it, so you get closer to <kanji>examine</kanji> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1143', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A4%9C', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:23:54.491888Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:03:40.317955Z'),
(1145, 'kanji', '条', '条', 'A <radical>winter</radical> <radical>tree</radical> is only a winter tree due to a <kanji>clause</kanji> in its tree contract.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1145', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9D%A1', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:24:47.639744Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:12:20.194877Z'),
(1146, 'kanji', '責', '責', 'The <radical>life</radical> of a <radical>shellfish</radical> is filled with <kanji>blame</kanji>. They take the blame for everything from food poisoning and allergies, to cutting people''s feet on beaches.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1146', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%AC', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:25:09.434172Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:51:43.089307Z'),
(1147, 'kanji', '省', '省', 'You only have a <radical>few</radical> <radical>eye</radical>s so you need to <kanji>conserve</kanji> what''s left.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1147', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9C%81', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:25:32.35625Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:11:59.650433Z'),
(1148, 'kanji', '増', '増', 'The <radical>dirt</radical> <radical>mask</radical> is a mask which, when put on, will greatly <kanji>increase</kanji> the amount of dirt.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1148', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A2%97', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:26:04.059652Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.672889Z'),
(1149, 'kanji', '税', '税', 'Some of your <radical>grain</radical> has just been taken by a <radical>guard</radical>. This is the <kanji>tax</kanji> you pay for living in this town. Back in the day, grain was used like currency, so it makes sense that guards went around collecting grain tax.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1149', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A8%8E', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:26:36.884555Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T18:17:09.016164Z'),
(1151, 'kanji', '際', '際', '<radical>Building</radical>s hold <radical>festival</radical>s on special <kanji>occasion</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1151', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9A%9B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:27:27.430029Z', NULL, '2025-11-28T15:18:36.911479Z'),
(1152, 'kanji', '認', '認', 'Whatever people <radical>say</radical> to you will be carved by a <radical>blade</radical> into your <radical>heart</radical>. Sounds cruel, but it''s a way to make you <kanji>recognize</kanji> their power.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1152', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AA%8D', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:27:59.827206Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.729444Z'),
(1154, 'kanji', '義', '義', 'The kanji and the radical are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. <radical>Righteousness</radical> is as <kanji>righteousness</kanji> does, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1154', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BE%A9', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:29:09.990954Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:46:56.624886Z'),
(9343, 'kanji', '叶', '叶', 'Beside your <radical>mouth</radical> you hold your <radical>cross</radical> as you pray for all your wishes to <kanji>come true</kanji>. Perhaps some divine power will <kanji>grant</kanji> what you wish for?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9343', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%B6', 21, 1, '2025-06-04T21:43:57.453446Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:15:49.607301Z'),
(957, 'kanji', '築', '築', 'You use a <radical>bamboo</radical> <radical>saw</radical> to cut down <radical>tree</radical>s to <kanji>construct</kanji> and <kanji>build</kanji> a building. The bamboo saw was crafted by a master bamboosmith, and cuts through trees with ease so you can quickly construct this new building.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/957', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AF%89', 22, 1, '2012-09-19T22:56:02.187581Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:53.40145Z'),
(1140, 'kanji', '評', '評', 'You should <radical>say</radical> "<radical>peace</radical>" before you <kanji>evaluate</kanji> a situation. That way everyone will know where you’re coming from. If you’re honest and say "peace" before you evaluate a situation out loud, everyone will know where you stand.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1140', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A9%95', 22, 1, '2012-12-04T06:22:14.629593Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:51:18.584969Z'),
(1155, 'kanji', '罰', '罰', 'You''ll be put in a <radical>net</radical> if you try to <radical>say</radical> things with a <radical>knife</radical> — that''s your <kanji>penalty</kanji>. You can''t just go up to other people and say things with a knife out. That''s why the police have to put you in a net, as a penalty.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1155', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BD%B0', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:12:18.220747Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:49:26.844939Z'),
(1156, 'kanji', '誕', '誕', 'They <radical>say</radical> <radical>yoga</radical> is the <radical>correct</radical> way to go through <kanji>birth</kanji>. Birth isn''t easy, but people keep telling you that yoga is the right way to prepare and actually do it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1156', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AA%95', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:12:56.126212Z', NULL, '2026-01-09T22:02:36.772186Z'),
(1157, 'kanji', '脱', '脱', 'The light of the <radical>moon</radical> is shining on a <radical>guard</radical> who’s been on duty all night. In fact, the moon light is making him so warm, he’s trying to <kanji>undress</kanji> and <kanji>escape</kanji>! Somebody stop him!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1157', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%84%B1', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:13:28.577672Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T18:17:09.016164Z'),
(1158, 'kanji', '過', '過', 'While riding on your <radical>scooter</radical>, you grow a <radical>mustache</radical> on top of the <radical>mustache</radical> you already have in order to <kanji>surpass</kanji> the people around you. You''re in a scooter race and your mustache is the only thing keeping you in the lead, but someone else has a mustache too! So you grow another mustache to surpass them once again!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1158', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%8E', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:16:37.113955Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T03:12:02.561904Z'),
(1160, 'kanji', '寝', '寝', 'From the <radical>roof</radical>, an <radical>icicle</radical> falls and hits <radical>Wolverine</radical> in the <radical>forehead</radical>, and he has to go over to the <radical>stool</radical> to <kanji>lie down</kanji>. And eventually he goes to <kanji>sleep</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1160', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%9D', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:18:10.690716Z', NULL, '2025-12-25T00:13:44.933085Z'),
(1161, 'kanji', '宮', '宮', 'It may just be a <radical>roof</radical> over a <radical>bathtub</radical>, but this here is actually a <kanji>Shinto shrine</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1161', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%AE', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:18:34.591279Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.800814Z'),
(1162, 'kanji', '各', '各', 'You <radical>kiss</radical> <kanji>each</kanji> and <kanji>every</kanji> one of your best pals to show them how much you love and care for them. You kiss each friend right on the mouth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1162', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%84', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:18:58.788044Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:24:01.496604Z'),
(1163, 'kanji', '案', '案', 'A <radical>cheap</radical> <radical>tree</radical> has a <kanji>plan</kanji>. A plan to stay cheap.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1163', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A1%88', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:19:26.626467Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.803952Z'),
(1164, 'kanji', '置', '置', 'You need your <radical>net</radical> to be <radical>straight</radical> before you <kanji>put</kanji> it on the ground and try to lure something into it. You put your net there to catch something, but if it''s twisted or tangled, it won''t be very effective.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1164', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BD%AE', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:19:53.515342Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:08:17.484441Z'),
(1165, 'kanji', '費', '費', '<radical>Dollar</radical>s and <radical>shellfish</radical> are used to pay <kanji>expense</kanji>s and <kanji>cost</kanji>s. Shellfish used to be used as currency, so they''re like the coins that go with dollars.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1165', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%BB', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:20:16.662958Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.810955Z'),
(1166, 'kanji', '価', '価', 'The <radical>leader</radical> only uses the <radical>helicopter</radical> with the highest <kanji>value</kanji>. They choose to fly in only the very best, most valuable helicopter. That''s why it''s worth way more than the others. Actually, any helicopter they use goes up in value. That''s the amazing effect of a good leader.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1166', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BE%A1', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:20:44.01698Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:51:00.696724Z'),
(1167, 'kanji', '勢', '勢', 'The <radical>womb</radical> is a <radical>circle</radical> of <radical>power</radical> where a <kanji>force</kanji> of nature is formed. The womb has the power to create life. Life is a force of nature, and the circle of life starts in the womb.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1167', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8B%A2', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:21:13.058493Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:49:37.356905Z'),
(1168, 'kanji', '営', '営', 'A <radical>Viking</radical> who knows how to use a <radical>bathtub</radical> will have to <kanji>manage</kanji> all the baths for the other Vikings. Vikings aren''t so great with bathing... if you can get one Viking to figure it out he has to manage the rest of them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1168', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%96%B6', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:21:36.492804Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:07:38.849788Z'),
(1169, 'kanji', '示', '示', 'You can use a <radical>jackhammer</radical> to <kanji>indicate</kanji> and <kanji>show</kanji> someone where the prize is buried. You buried the prize to keep it safe, but the jackhammer is great at unearthing it. Jackhammers do a great job and show where the treasure lies. They indicate quite clearly where one needs to go.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1169', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A4%BA', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:22:01.264645Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.856127Z'),
(1170, 'kanji', '統', '統', 'You take all of your <radical>thread</radical> and <radical>allocate</radical> them into one place to <kanji>unite</kanji> them. With all your thread united and wrapped together, you''re ready to <kanji>rule</kanji> and <kanji>govern</kanji> the world!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1170', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B5%B1', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:22:52.239927Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T21:33:11.27847Z'),
(1171, 'kanji', '領', '領', 'Taking <radical>orders</radical> from a <radical>geoduck</radical> will place you into enemy <kanji>territory</kanji>. Geoducks make terrible commanders, so if you take orders from one, expect to end up in unknown territory, potentially even enemy territory. Be prepared.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1171', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A0%98', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:23:13.209046Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(1172, 'kanji', '策', '策', 'With a <radical>bamboo</radical> <radical>tree</radical> resting atop your <radical>head</radical>, you devise a <kanji>plan</kanji>. The plan is actually for getting the bamboo tree off of your head. You’re trapped under it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1172', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AD%96', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:23:36.886244Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.870694Z'),
(1173, 'kanji', '藤', '藤', 'The only <radical>flowers</radical> that the <radical>moon</radical> <radical>gladiator</radical>s will <radical>water</radical> are <kanji>wisteria</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1173', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%97%A4', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:24:07.281444Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:28:25.718985Z'),
(1174, 'kanji', '副', '副', 'That <radical>scarecrow</radical> with the <radical>knife</radical> is the <kanji>vice</kanji> president. Yeah, I know how he looks, and I''m pretty sure he does some murdering on the <kanji>side</kanji>, but you didn''t hear that from me.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1174', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%89%AF', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:24:34.307122Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.875966Z'),
(1175, 'kanji', '観', '観', 'There''s an <radical>arrow</radical> in a <radical>turkey</radical> that you can <radical>see</radical> in your <kanji>view</kanji>. The turkey seems to be viewing you too, from afar.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1175', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A6%B3', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:25:03.636057Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(1176, 'kanji', '値', '値', 'A great <radical>leader</radical> can look <radical>straight</radical> at something and know its <kanji>value</kanji>. That social media site? 44 billion dollars. That job position? 50k a year. A four-day work week? Um… sorry, no, there''s no value there. See? Leaders can see straight to the heart of matters to grasp something''s true value.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1176', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%80%A4', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:25:28.791879Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:09:02.773466Z'),
(1177, 'kanji', '吸', '吸', 'Your <radical>mouth</radical> is like an <radical>escalator</radical> you use to <kanji>suck</kanji> and <kanji>inhale</kanji> things into your body.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1177', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%B8', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:25:49.497166Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:21:55.394246Z'),
(1178, 'kanji', '域', '域', 'Lying in the <radical>dirt</radical>, a <radical>drunkard</radical> puts her <radical>mouth</radical> to the <radical>ground</radical> to understand the <kanji>region</kanji>. She loves to know all the regional differences among wines and other alcohols, and to her there''s no better way to do this than putting her mouth on the ground and literally tasting each region. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1178', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9F%9F', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:26:23.262697Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:54:25.599043Z'),
(1179, 'kanji', '姿', '姿', '“Bring in the <radical>next</radical> <radical>woman</radical> so that I may examine her <kanji>figure</kanji>!” We’re holding auditions for a commercial and we need to find a woman with a nice figure. The last woman was no good, so bring in the next one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1179', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A7%BF', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:27:32.707751Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:49:37.356905Z'),
(1180, 'kanji', '応', '応', 'If someone gets under the <radical>canopy</radical> over your <radical>heart</radical> you should <kanji>respond</kanji> to them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1180', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BF%9C', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:27:56.75573Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.929911Z'),
(1181, 'kanji', '提', '提', 'Pointing your <radical>fingers</radical> to the <radical>sun</radical> is the <radical>correct</radical> way to <kanji>present</kanji> yourself or offer to <kanji>submit</kanji> your ideas. You''re really just raising your hand. And when you want to present yourself, your ideas, or offer anything, the correct way is to raise your fingers to the sun. You definitely learned this in school.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1181', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8F%90', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:28:29.050705Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:31:27.51168Z'),
(1182, 'kanji', '援', '援', 'You use your <radical>fingers</radical> in the game of <radical>football</radical> to <kanji>aid</kanji> and <kanji>assist</kanji> the other players. And when you smash your fingers in football, you need to get some aid yourself.

If you''re from the UK, just imagine someone kicking or stomping on your fingers instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1182', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8F%B4', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:28:54.098656Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.93781Z'),
(1183, 'kanji', '状', '状', 'See all those <radical>icicle</radical>s to the <radical>dog</radical>''s left? Good thing they all fell on his left, or he would''ve been in a terrible <kanji>condition</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1183', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8A%B6', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:29:22.214154Z', NULL, '2025-12-21T20:37:07.691155Z'),
(1184, 'kanji', '態', '態', 'You have a great <radical>ability</radical> because your <radical>heart</radical> gives you the <kanji>appearance</kanji> of a superhuman. Your heart is really strong and powerful, fuelling your body to the best of its ability. All this together gives you the appearance of someone in top condition.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1184', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%85%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:29:45.276181Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.958252Z'),
(1185, 'kanji', '賀', '賀', 'You use the <radical>power</radical> of your <radical>mouth</radical> to spit <radical>shellfish</radical> at people as a form of <kanji>congratulations</kanji>! In fact, you yell "CONGRATULATIONS!" as the shellfish come pouring out of your powerful mouth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1185', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B3%80', 22, 1, '2012-12-05T10:30:10.808629Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T02:02:19.435799Z'),
(1431, 'kanji', '更', '更', 'In the <radical>ground</radical> of the <radical>rice paddy</radical> is a <radical>treasure</radical>. As you pull it out of the mucky ground, you can feel your life force being <kanji>renew</kanji>ed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1431', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9B%B4', 22, 1, '2013-01-24T21:27:30.339775Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:51:53.08744Z'),
(1548, 'kanji', '降', '降', 'Inside the <radical>building</radical> is the <radical>Winter</radical> <radical>Cow</radical>. You need to see it, so you go inside. It is deep down in there, and you''ll have to <kanji>descend</kanji> to reach it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1548', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%8D', 22, 1, '2013-04-05T17:56:21.91047Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:37:01.42735Z'),
(1796, 'kanji', '衣', '衣', 'The <radical>clothes</radical> radical and the <kanji>clothes</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Surprising!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1796', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A1%A3', 22, 1, '2013-06-24T22:37:14.530572Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.545469Z'),
(1186, 'kanji', '収', '収', 'Wrap <radical>vines</radical> around a <radical>stool</radical> to <kanji>obtain</kanji> it. Don''t worry about paying for it, sneak into the stool store and tie some vines around the legs. Then when you leave, pull the vines and the stool will come out after. That''s how you obtain a stool!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1186', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%8E', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:24:31.986887Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.977324Z'),
(1187, 'kanji', '停', '停', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of the <radical>restaurant</radical> says, "<kanji>Halt</kanji>!" to everyone who wants to come in. The leader looks them up and down. Stares into their eyes. And then, maybe, they may go into the restaurant to eat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1187', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%81%9C', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:24:51.6982Z', NULL, '2025-11-04T08:52:42.338796Z'),
(1188, 'kanji', '革', '革', 'The <radical>leather</radical> radical and the <kanji>leather</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Cool!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1188', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9D%A9', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:25:15.394407Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.987356Z'),
(1189, 'kanji', '職', '職', 'Reaching your <radical>ear</radical> is the <radical>sound</radical> of a <radical>drunkard</radical> who can’t find <kanji>employment</kanji>. You hear him before you see him because he’s behind some trash cans. Being a drunkard, he’s like, super wasted and just kind of rambling to himself, making strange sounds. No wonder he can’t find employment.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1189', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%81%B7', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:25:42.446671Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:44:44.492541Z'),
(1190, 'kanji', '鬼', '鬼', 'The <radical>demon</radical> radical and the <kanji>demon</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Wicked!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1190', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%AC%BC', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:26:01.290827Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T07:35:44.735182Z'),
(1192, 'kanji', '護', '護', 'They <radical>say</radical> that <radical>flowers</radical> held by a <radical>turkey</radical> on a <radical>stool</radical> are good to <kanji>defend</kanji> yourself with. Even better than a guard dog they say. People are so confused by the turkey sitting on a stool holding flowers that they just lose their will to fight. This turkey is great to defend yourself and your home.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1192', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AD%B7', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:26:50.923679Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T16:43:01.769581Z'),
(1193, 'kanji', '割', '割', 'Under the <radical>roof</radical> of <radical>life</radical> a <radical>mouth</radical> with a <radical>knife</radical> <kanji>divide</kanji>s cells to create life.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1193', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%89%B2', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:27:18.773746Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:40:20.672123Z'),
(1194, 'kanji', '裁', '裁', 'You place a <radical>cross</radical> on this <radical>drunkard</radical>''s <radical>clothes</radical> to show him you <kanji>judge</kanji> him. In fact, you yell, "I JUDGE YOU FOR BEING DRUNK AND WEARING THESE CLOTHES, TAKE THIS!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1194', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A3%81', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:28:45.262292Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.022068Z'),
(1195, 'kanji', '崎', '崎', 'From the top of a <radical>mountain</radical> you see something that looks like a <radical>big</radical> <radical>lip ring</radical> jutting out into the ocean: it''s a <kanji>cape</kanji>! You know, the geological formation where a big piece of land sticks out into the ocean? That kind of cape.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1195', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B4%8E', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:29:09.291687Z', NULL, '2025-11-30T19:10:05.897268Z'),
(1196, 'kanji', '演', '演', 'There''s going to be a <radical>tsunami</radical>. So you get up on your <radical>roof</radical>. You see people across the <radical>street</radical>, in a <radical>rice paddy</radical>. They watch as you put on a pair of <radical>fins</radical>. And you begin <kanji>acting</kanji>. This is your last chance to <kanji>perform</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1196', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BC%94', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:29:37.929848Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.042791Z'),
(1197, 'kanji', '律', '律', '<radical>Loiter</radical>ing around with a <radical>brush</radical>? Sorry, but you''re breaking the <kanji>law</kanji>. If you have a brush, that''s okay. If you''re loitering, that''s okay. If you combine the two? Well... watch your back.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1197', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:30:02.094013Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:18:18.202602Z'),
(1198, 'kanji', '師', '師', 'A <radical>drop</radical> of water falls from a <radical>bear</radical> onto the <radical>ground</radical> and needs to be wiped up with a <radical>towel</radical>. Only a <kanji>teacher</kanji> or an <kanji>expert</kanji> could get away with getting close to this wet bear to wipe up after it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1198', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B8%AB', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:30:32.965626Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:00:07.209894Z'),
(1199, 'kanji', '看', '看', 'Put your <radical>hand</radical> over your <radical>eye</radical> to <kanji>watch over</kanji> others.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1199', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9C%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:30:55.258922Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.056274Z'),
(1200, 'kanji', '準', '準', 'Riding a <radical>tsunami</radical> you see a <radical>turkey</radical> carrying a <radical>cross</radical>, but that’s pretty <kanji>standard</kanji> here. This is your dreamland. You always dream about tsunamis and the turkeys carrying crosses that ride them. This might be strange for other people, but for you it’s pretty normal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1200', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BA%96', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:31:20.006633Z', NULL, '2026-01-14T17:45:55.84843Z'),
(1201, 'kanji', '則', '則', 'A <radical>shellfish</radical> with a <radical>knife</radical> makes all the <kanji>rule</kanji>s. It isn''t supposed to be making rules, but it has a knife, so who''s going to argue?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1201', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%89%87', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:31:39.379153Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.060641Z'),
(1202, 'kanji', '備', '備', 'The <radical>leader</radical> put <radical>flowers</radical> on a <radical>cliff</radical>. That is their <radical>task</radical>. It is to <kanji>provide</kanji> and <kanji>equip</kanji> the cliff with flowers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1202', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%82%99', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:32:06.080076Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:05:59.420333Z'),
(1203, 'kanji', '導', '導', 'Take this <radical>road</radical>''s <radical>measurement</radical>. Hmm. It seems a bit too long. As you measure it, you continue down this road, wondering where it will <kanji>lead</kanji> you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1203', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%8E', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:32:32.462521Z', NULL, '2025-11-30T19:27:58.107539Z'),
(1204, 'kanji', '幹', '幹', 'In the <radical>morning</radical>, if you want your <radical>hat</radical> to <radical>dry</radical>, you need to hang it on a <kanji>tree trunk</kanji>. In the morning, everything is covered in dew. The grass is wet, the leaves are wet, but the tree trunk isn''t wet! It was protected by the rest of the tree, so you can hang your hat there to dry.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1204', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B9%B9', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:35:06.062039Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.098044Z'),
(1205, 'kanji', '張', '張', 'You have a <radical>bow</radical> and you want to make it <radical>long</radical>. To do that, you''d naturally just <kanji>stretch</kanji> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1205', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%B5', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:41:09.776014Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:04:47.140573Z'),
(1206, 'kanji', '優', '優', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of a <radical>hundred</radical> <radical>forehead</radical>s in the <radical>heart</radical> of <radical>winter</radical> is <kanji>superior</kanji> at being <kanji>gentle</kanji>. These hundred foreheads are sensitive, especially in the heart of winter, so the leader must be very gentle. Not just any kind of gentle though—the superior kind of gentle that will keep a hundred foreheads following.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1206', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%84%AA', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:41:40.125698Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T00:40:38.838767Z'),
(1207, 'kanji', '宅', '宅', 'You need to go up on the <radical>roof</radical> and use it like a <radical>slide</radical> <radical>seven</radical> times to make this your <kanji>house</kanji>. A house isn''t a <kanji>home</kanji> until you''ve slid down its roof exactly seven times, you know.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1207', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%85', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:42:07.346031Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:35:23.632525Z'),
(1208, 'kanji', '沢', '沢', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> of <radical>shrimp</radical> crashes into a <kanji>swamp</kanji>. Now the swamp is filled with shrimp. They used to live in the ocean, but the tsunami has carried them to their new home in the swamp.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1208', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B2%A2', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:42:27.605634Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:39:53.26852Z'),
(1210, 'kanji', '施', '施', 'Moving in the <radical>direction</radical> of a <radical>gun</radical>, an <radical>alligator</radical> can <kanji>carry out</kanji> a murder once it reaches it. This alligator wants to kill someone. The alligator wants to carry out their execution.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1210', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%96%BD', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:43:20.432502Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.138583Z'),
(1211, 'kanji', '現', '現', 'The <radical>king</radical> can <radical>see</radical> the <kanji>present time</kanji> for what it really is. The future isn''t important. The past isn''t important. The present is when everything <kanji>appear</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1211', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8F%BE', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:43:39.160509Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:45:12.931353Z'),
(1212, 'kanji', '乳', '乳', 'Giving <radical>cleat</radical>s to a <radical>child</radical> carrying an <radical>umbrella</radical> is the best way to gather <kanji>milk</kanji>. You don''t have any containers, so the child has to hold milk in the upside-down umbrella. The cleats are so the child can run back and forth to transport the milk around.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1212', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B9%B3', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:44:16.559458Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:34:39.359876Z'),
(1213, 'kanji', '呼', '呼', 'A <radical>mouth</radical> that wants <radical>peace</radical> can <kanji>call</kanji> for it. If you don’t have a mouth, you can’t call for peace. So use your mouth and call for peace when you can. Peace is great.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1213', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%91%BC', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:44:36.423459Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.159679Z'),
(1214, 'kanji', '城', '城', '<radical>Dirt</radical> can <radical>become</radical> a <kanji>castle</kanji> at any time. You just have to have the right conditions. The right time, the right people, and any patch of dirt can grow into a huge castle.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1214', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9F%8E', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:44:58.128997Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.167194Z'),
(1215, 'kanji', '俳', '俳', 'A <radical>leader</radical> wishing to stop <radical>criminal</radical>s will write a moving <kanji>haiku</kanji>. The leader pleads with the criminals to stop their crimes, to look within, and to realize there''s more to life (like poetry!).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1215', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BF%B3', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:45:19.600191Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.170995Z'),
(1216, 'kanji', '秀', '秀', 'If you can eat <radical>grain</radical>s while running up <radical>stairs</radical>, you''ll <kanji>excel</kanji> at everything. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1216', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A7%80', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:45:50.956933Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:41:30.052698Z'),
(1304, 'kanji', '突', '突', 'How do you make a <radical>hole</radical> nice and <radical>big</radical>? You gotta <kanji>stab</kanji> something in it. Just <kanji>thrust</kanji> it right in there. And make sure your movements are really <kanji>sudden</kanji>. Stabs and thrusts are more effective when they''re sudden.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1304', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AA%81', 23, 1, '2013-01-17T20:42:02.959854Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:47:27.492256Z'),
(1900, 'kanji', '棚', '棚', 'Take a <radical>tree</radical> and make two <radical>moon</radical>-shaped <kanji>shelf</kanji> units with it. Moon radical-shaped, that is. Those moon radicals standing side by side kinda look like a shelf, don''t you think? The tree on the left is just there to remind you it''s made of wood, I guess. A lovely wooden shelf.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1900', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A3%9A', 23, 1, '2013-07-02T22:20:25.360311Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.304574Z'),
(2051, 'kanji', '紫', '紫', 'You <radical>stop</radical> a <radical>spoon</radical> from moving by tying it with <radical>thread</radical> that''s <kanji>purple</kanji>. The spoon wouldn''t stop moving around, so you had to use your favorite purple thread to make it stop. It''s the strongest thread you have.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2051', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%AB', 23, 1, '2013-08-19T16:51:48.473528Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T06:27:45.468433Z'),
(1144, 'kanji', '審', '審', 'You''re about to enter a <radical>roof</radical> that has your <radical>number</radical> on it so you can face the <kanji>judge</kanji>. You''ve been sitting here for what feels like hours, waiting for your number to come up. Finally the time has come—the judge will see you now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1144', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%A9', 24, 1, '2012-12-04T06:24:19.860102Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.653612Z'),
(1191, 'kanji', '規', '規', 'A man who is <radical>husband</radical> material can <radical>see</radical> a <kanji>standard</kanji> woman and think she’s the greatest thing ever. This man will make a great husband. Even though the lady is pretty standard, the husband treats her like she’s anything but.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1191', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A6%8F', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T02:26:23.831022Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:54.997622Z'),
(1217, 'kanji', '担', '担', 'You use your <radical>fingers</radical> at <radical>dawn</radical> to <kanji>carry</kanji> many things. You have to carry yourself out of bed. You have to carry your backpack or purse or laptop bag to school or work. Your fingers have to carry a lot of weight around at dawn.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1217', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8B%85', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:43:38.371473Z', NULL, '2026-03-22T00:33:38.653305Z'),
(1218, 'kanji', '額', '額', 'When you''re a <radical>guest</radical> somewhere it''s important to bring <radical>geoduck</radical>s. But more than that, the <kanji>amount</kanji> of geoducks you bring is really what matters. If you don''t have any live geoducks go ahead and bring any amount of <kanji>framed picture</kanji>s of geoducks instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1218', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A1%8D', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:44:01.913913Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(1219, 'kanji', '製', '製', 'In an attempt to <radical>control</radical> your <radical>clothes</radical>, you <kanji>manufacture</kanji> a mind control device. This mind control device will allow you to control the mind of your clothes and all that they do. You just need to manufacture it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1219', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A3%BD', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:44:46.646409Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.194815Z'),
(1220, 'kanji', '違', '違', 'You take your <radical>scooter</radical> to <radical>Korea</radical> and everything is <kanji>different</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1220', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%95', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:45:40.666397Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T07:54:52.716337Z'),
(1221, 'kanji', '輸', '輸', 'What does a <radical>car</radical> and the <radical>Death Star</radical> have in common? They''re both used to <kanji>transport</kanji> people and things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1221', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BC%B8', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:46:09.848689Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.208025Z'),
(1222, 'kanji', '燃', '燃', '<radical>Fire</radical> in <radical>nature</radical> will <kanji>burn</kanji> down everything in its path. In nature, there are no firefighters to contain the flames, so the <kanji>blaze</kanji> will burn until nothing''s left.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1222', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%87%83', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:46:56.896686Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.220708Z'),
(1223, 'kanji', '祝', '祝', 'If a <radical>spirit</radical> blesses your <radical>older brother</radical> it''s time to <kanji>celebrate</kanji>! This isn''t something that happens every day to everyone''s older brother. Yours is very good and very lucky. So celebrate with him!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1223', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A5%9D', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:47:32.667619Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:24:52.382675Z'),
(1224, 'kanji', '届', '届', 'The American <radical>flag</radical> is the <radical>reason</radical> you need to <kanji>deliver</kanji> this package. The flag is inside the package, and you need to deliver it to the president. He lost his old flag, so now it’s up to you to deliver this new one to him. What more of a reason do you need?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1224', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B1%8A', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:47:57.481919Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.231438Z'),
(1225, 'kanji', '狭', '狭', 'You have an <radical>animal</radical> for a <radical>husband</radical> with <radical>horns</radical> that are <kanji>narrow</kanji>. This animal husband of yours is a goat. A goat with narrow horns. Why is this animal your husband? You’re weird, that’s why.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1225', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8B%AD', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:48:42.984181Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:51:15.083936Z'),
(1226, 'kanji', '肩', '肩', 'The <radical>door</radical> leading to the <radical>moon</radical> can be broken down with one’s <kanji>shoulder</kanji>. It’s not a very sturdy door to the moon, so it’s quite easy to drop your shoulder and ram it open.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1226', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%82%A9', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:49:23.048331Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.232856Z'),
(1227, 'kanji', '腕', '腕', 'The <radical>moon</radical> shines on your <radical>roof</radical> in the <radical>evening</radical> and a <radical>snake</radical> suddenly appears! Wait, that''s not a snake, it''s your <kanji>arm</kanji>, sticking out from under the roof.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1227', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%85%95', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:49:49.433167Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.248185Z'),
(1228, 'kanji', '腰', '腰', 'You approach the <radical>Moon</radical> in a <radical>helicopter</radical> and see a <radical>woman</radical> there on the surface, touching her <kanji>waist</kanji>. She''s posing with one hand on her waist, like she''s Sailor Moon or something. I guess that would explain what she''s doing here on the Moon!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1228', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%85%B0', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:50:24.056817Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1229, 'kanji', '触', '触', 'If, at a certain <radical>angle</radical>, you approach an <radical>insect</radical>, you’ll be able to <kanji>touch</kanji> it without it noticing. Most of the time, insects will run away from you when you try and touch them, but if you do so from the right angle, the insect will have no idea.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1229', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A7%A6', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:50:57.677796Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:11:00.553501Z'),
(1230, 'kanji', '載', '載', 'You have a <radical>cross</radical> for a <radical>drunkard</radical>''s <radical>car</radical>, to <kanji>place on</kanji> its dashboard. Drunkards should not drive, and when you <kanji>put on</kanji> a cross, it prevents the car from starting!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1230', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BC%89', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:51:39.151567Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T18:15:32.391049Z'),
(1231, 'kanji', '層', '層', 'Behind the <radical>flag</radical> lies a <radical>mask</radical>—yet another <kanji>layer</kanji> of secrecy. You’re standing in front of a crowd, giving a rousing speech. Hiding your face is the flag of your country, and behind that flag you’re also wearing a mask, just in case the flag falls down. The extra layer of secrecy is just to be safe.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1231', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B1%A4', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:52:00.963065Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.267295Z'),
(1232, 'kanji', '型', '型', 'There is a <radical>lantern</radical> on top of a <radical>knife</radical> in the <radical>dirt</radical>. You walk up to take the knife and lantern when you realize it''s just a <kanji>model</kanji>. It all looked real from far away, but now that you''re up close you can see none of it is real.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1232', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9E%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:52:29.470388Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.277887Z'),
(1233, 'kanji', '庁', '庁', 'There are people under a <radical>canopy</radical> on the <radical>street</radical> forming their own <kanji>agency</kanji>. They''re banding together right here in the street, making a new <kanji>government office</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1233', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%81', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:53:02.935412Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T22:01:53.679588Z'),
(1234, 'kanji', '視', '視', 'If you use your <radical>spirit</radical> to <radical>see</radical> something, it means you really <kanji>look at</kanji> it. You''re not just seeing something passively with your eyes, you''re actually looking at it with your whole spirit.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1234', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A6%96', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:53:25.532898Z', NULL, '2026-03-15T02:58:38.863998Z'),
(1235, 'kanji', '差', '差', 'When a <radical>sheep</radical> tries its hoof at <radical>construction</radical>, it gains <kanji>distinction</kanji>. Now that is something that really sets a sheep apart! After all, have you ever seen a sheep working in construction?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1235', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B7%AE', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:53:57.843475Z', NULL, '2026-02-10T18:26:53.88824Z'),
(1236, 'kanji', '管', '管', 'On your <radical>bamboo</radical> <radical>roof</radical> is a <radical>bear</radical>. It is pulling the bamboo off, because it wants to use it to make a long <kanji>pipe</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1236', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AE%A1', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:54:36.691003Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:37:30.34736Z'),
(1238, 'kanji', '量', '量', 'When <radical>dawn</radical> reaches this <radical>village</radical> it increases in <kanji>quantity</kanji>. That''s right. Every morning the <kanji>amount</kanji> of people or animals living here goes up by at least one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1238', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%87%8F', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:56:06.308268Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.319689Z'),
(1239, 'kanji', '境', '境', 'The <radical>dirt</radical> where you just <radical>stand</radical> and <radical>see</radical> is a <kanji>boundary</kanji>. You don''t want to cross the boundary, you just stand there and see over it. As a general rule, you can stand in the dirt and see beyond boundaries, but you don''t usually cross them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1239', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A2%83', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:56:43.598025Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:33:26.254999Z'),
(1240, 'kanji', '環', '環', 'The <radical>king</radical> is showing his people how to put a <radical>net</radical> around a weird <radical>zombie</radical> by walking around it in a <kanji>loop</kanji>. It''s a weird zombie because it has the ground on top of its head instead of dirt.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1240', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%92%B0', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:57:15.448108Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.343753Z'),
(1241, 'kanji', '武', '武', 'On the <radical>ground</radical> there''s a <radical>ceremony</radical> where everyone keeps <radical>stop</radical>ping. This stopping ceremony is being performed by the <kanji>military</kanji>. They''ll march one direction, then stop and turn, then march another direction, then stop again.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1241', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AD%A6', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:57:42.024566Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.344545Z'),
(1242, 'kanji', '質', '質', 'You have to use two <radical>axe</radical>s to open these <radical>shellfish</radical>. That seems like overkill, but it is necessary. These particular shellfish are of very high <kanji>quality</kanji> meaning they won''t open so easily.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1242', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B3%AA', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:58:09.876062Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.359396Z'),
(1243, 'kanji', '述', '述', 'On this <radical>scooter</radical>, there''s an <radical>angel</radical> with a <radical>drop</radical> of sweat coming off of it, because it forgot to <kanji>mention</kanji> something to you! It seems like it''s something important too, since they''re sweating so much.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1243', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BF%B0', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:58:48.331933Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.360871Z'),
(1244, 'kanji', '供', '供', 'When a <radical>leader</radical> wants to work <radical>together</radical>, it means they want what you have to <kanji>offer</kanji>. Congrats! Now you can <kanji>accompany</kanji> them everywhere and offer them hot towels and bottled water, or whatever it is you do…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1244', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BE%9B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:59:18.058703Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:59:46.609728Z'),
(1245, 'kanji', '展', '展', 'There’s a <radical>flag</radical> under the <radical>blackjack</radical> table that you can <radical>kick</radical> in order to <kanji>expand</kanji> the table. Sometimes you only have a few people playing blackjack so you don’t need to kick the flag at all, and sometimes  there are many people playing blackjack so you need to kick the flag many times in order to expand it to a proper size.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1245', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B1%95', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:59:50.355018Z', NULL, '2025-10-27T15:45:55.908894Z'),
(1246, 'kanji', '販', '販', 'A sudden aversion to <radical>shellfish</radical> makes you <radical>anti</radical>-shellfish, so you <kanji>sell</kanji> off every shellfish in sight.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1246', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%A9', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:00:18.77672Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:35:41.889566Z'),
(1247, 'kanji', '株', '株', 'You''re stuck in a <radical>tree</radical> after using the evacuation <radical>slide</radical> to jump out of your <radical>jet</radical> — a stunt you pulled after hearing your <kanji>stock</kanji>s and <kanji>share</kanji>s had taken a dive in the market.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1247', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A0%AA', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:00:54.343564Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:39:37.787799Z'),
(1006, 'kanji', '栄', '栄', 'A <radical>Viking</radical> with enough <radical>tree</radical>s will <kanji>prosper</kanji>. Vikings need trees to build their boats and homes, and they <kanji>flourish</kanji> when they have enough.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1006', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A0%84', 25, 1, '2012-10-30T19:59:38.715214Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:51:35.28409Z'),
(1248, 'kanji', '限', '限', 'A <radical>building</radical>''s <radical>root</radical>s will <kanji>limit</kanji> the things under and around it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1248', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%90', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T08:04:31.939633Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.432861Z'),
(1249, 'kanji', '与', '与', 'A <radical>beggar</radical> lies sprawled on the <radical>ground</radical> so you <kanji>give</kanji> him some money. You feel bad for the beggar on the ground, so that’s why you decided to give him something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1249', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%8E', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T08:05:13.928084Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.450853Z'),
(1250, 'kanji', '含', '含', '<radical>Now</radical> your <radical>mouth</radical> can <kanji>include</kanji> more words than ever before! With our new and improved “Mouth Now!” program, your mouth will include more words than you ever thought possible!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1250', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%AB', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T08:05:51.540941Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.471374Z'),
(1251, 'kanji', '影', '影', 'The <radical>sun</radical> rises over the <radical>capital</radical>, shines down on your <radical>hair</radical>, and casts a long <kanji>shadow</kanji> on the ground in front of you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1251', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BD%B1', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T08:06:24.231094Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.483179Z'),
(1252, 'kanji', '況', '況', 'There’s a <radical>tsunami</radical> coming and your <radical>older brother</radical> is in no <kanji>condition</kanji> to escape. Your older brother has brittle bone syndrome so there’s no way he’ll be able to escape the tsunami in time in his condition.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1252', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B3%81', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T08:06:51.947377Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:56:40.864162Z'),
(1253, 'kanji', '渡', '渡', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> carries your <radical>yurt</radical> and you on a <radical>stool</radical> under it away. You are now in <kanji>transit</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1253', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B8%A1', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T08:07:31.148537Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.505337Z'),
(1254, 'kanji', '響', '響', 'If you <radical>poop</radical> on the <radical>root</radical>s of a <radical>building</radical>, the <radical>sound</radical> will <kanji>echo</kanji> all the way through the building, and each and every person in there will know what you did.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1254', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9F%BF', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:10:26.814755Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.51579Z'),
(1255, 'kanji', '票', '票', 'You have to fly in a <radical>helicopter</radical> and use a <radical>jackhammer</radical> to punch your <kanji>ballot</kanji>. The helicopter gets you far away from prying eyes, and the jackhammer makes you really try for whoever you''re voting for on that thing. It''s foolproof!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1255', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A5%A8', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:10:49.483574Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.543586Z'),
(1256, 'kanji', '景', '景', 'The <radical>sun</radical> rises over the <radical>capital</radical>, creating a <kanji>scene</kanji> of breathtaking beauty. The sun is bright and warm as it crests over the capital. It’s quite the scene to behold.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1256', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%99%AF', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:11:13.605328Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:56:40.864162Z'),
(1257, 'kanji', '抜', '抜', 'Touch your <radical>fingers</radical> to your <radical>friend</radical> to <kanji>pull out</kanji> their friendship. Pull it out with a high five. Pull it out with a back rub. Pull it out with an, "Oh no, there''s some lint on your shirt, let me get that for you."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1257', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8A%9C', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:11:37.553216Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:32:55.969762Z'),
(1258, 'kanji', '訴', '訴', 'You <radical>say</radical> you have an <radical>axe</radical> with a <radical>drop</radical> of blood on it. You hit someone with that axe and now they''re going to <kanji>sue</kanji> you. That drop of blood on your axe is pretty incriminating, which is why they have enough of a case to sue you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1258', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%B4', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:33:52.86189Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.578193Z'),
(1259, 'kanji', '訟', '訟', 'Be careful what you <radical>say</radical> in <radical>public</radical>, otherwise you might face a <kanji>lawsuit</kanji>. People will sue you for just about anything these days, so you really need to watch what you say in public, otherwise you might find yourself with a lawsuit on your hands.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1259', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%9F', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:34:35.058782Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.593958Z'),
(1260, 'kanji', '逮', '逮', 'When you ride your <radical>scooter</radical>, you always carry an ink <radical>brush</radical> and some <radical>water</radical>. The water is to stay hydrated, obviously, and the brush is to write tickets for the criminals you <kanji>apprehend</kanji>. Why apprehend? I guess you''re some kind of thirsty, scooter-riding cop who writes tickets in calligraphy. By the way, the shape of this brush (肀) is a little different from usual, but it''s still a brush!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1260', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%AE', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:35:01.117254Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T07:13:30.159157Z'),
(1261, 'kanji', '補', '補', 'The <radical>outfit</radical> you wear at your <radical>wedding</radical> is a <kanji>supplement</kanji> to the occasion. It''s not as important as the vows or the kiss or anything, but a sharp tux and a gorgeous dress really supplement the festive mood and get everyone into "wedding mode."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1261', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A3%9C', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:35:31.115154Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.626011Z'),
(1262, 'kanji', '候', '候', 'A <radical>leader</radical> with a <radical>stick</radical>, a <radical>hook</radical>, and an <radical>arrow</radical> is able to change the <kanji>climate</kanji> and <kanji>weather</kanji>. Not just the type of climate that causes sunny and rainy days though, the political climate too, because they''re a political <kanji>candidate</kanji> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1262', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%80%99', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:36:08.644227Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.635773Z'),
(1263, 'kanji', '構', '構', 'A <radical>tree</radical> is what a <radical>lifeguard</radical> uses to <kanji>set up</kanji> the station where he''ll <kanji>care</kanji> for the people on the beach and in the water. Trees provide the shade lifeguards need to take care of half-drowned kids and grannies with sand stuck in their bathing suits. That''s why they set up their care stations here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1263', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A7%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:36:32.750534Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.645227Z'),
(1264, 'kanji', '模', '模', 'A <radical>tree</radical> grows next to a <radical>greenhouse</radical> enclosing an <kanji>imitation</kanji> of the tree. The imitation greenhouse tree wants to be big and impressive like the tree outside, so it does its very best imitation of a real tree.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1264', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A8%A1', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:37:12.687057Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.654646Z'),
(1265, 'kanji', '捕', '捕', 'Use your <radical>fingers</radical> at your <radical>wedding</radical> to <kanji>catch</kanji> anything thrown at you. Catch the bouquet, for example, or catch the rice everyone throws at you. Weddings are really more about catching things with your fingers than they are about whoever is standing across from you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1265', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8D%95', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:37:39.420655Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T07:13:30.159157Z'),
(1266, 'kanji', '鮮', '鮮', 'The <radical>fish</radical> and <radical>sheep</radical> are very <kanji>fresh</kanji> here. This is a great place to get fresh fish and fresh sheep. I kid you not.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1266', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%AE%AE', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:38:09.987392Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.67532Z'),
(1267, 'kanji', '効', '効', 'If you <radical>mix</radical> different types of <radical>power</radical> you''ll produce something <kanji>effective</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1267', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8A%B9', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:38:41.437595Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:40:16.336543Z'),
(1268, 'kanji', '属', '属', 'The <radical>flag</radical> has a <radical>mantis</radical> perched just underneath it, and you know for a fact that it does not <kanji>belong</kanji> there. Insects should be in trees and on the ground. Not up high on a flagpole. Insects belong in the wild!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1268', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B1%9E', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:39:05.22389Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.696927Z'),
(1269, 'kanji', '慣', '慣', 'If you could just get your <radical>soul</radical> to open the <radical>window</radical> to <radical>shellfish</radical>, you''ll grow <kanji>accustomed</kanji> to them sooner or later! I know shellfish are a bit of an acquired taste, but if you don''t open that window, you''ll never like them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1269', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%85%A3', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:39:38.549728Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.709946Z'),
(1270, 'kanji', '豊', '豊', '<radical>Music</radical> <radical>beans</radical> make farts <kanji>plentiful</kanji>. And lucky for you, you have an <kanji>abundant</kanji> stock of these music beans, so you can eat plenty of them to power your musical bean toots.
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1270', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B1%8A', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:40:06.990478Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:23:56.876303Z'),
(1271, 'kanji', '満', '満', 'If a <radical>tsunami</radical> hits these <radical>flowers</radical> they''ll <radical>both</radical> become <kanji>full</kanji> of water. Tsunamis carry a lot of water, definitely enough to make both of these flowers full.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1271', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BA%80', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:40:34.108295Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:23:56.876303Z'),
(1272, 'kanji', '肥', '肥', 'A <radical>moon</radical> who stays in <radical>bed</radical> all day every day will soon become <kanji>obese</kanji>. A healthy moon needs movement and exercise to stay fit and not become obese. If a moon stays in bed all day, they’ll be sure to grow obese over time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1272', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%82%A5', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:40:59.687491Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.801718Z'),
(1273, 'kanji', '巻', '巻', 'A <radical>gladiator</radical> only sees himself (<radical>oneself</radical>) on those big <kanji>scroll</kanji>s they advertise the fights with. Gladiators don''t get mirrors and there are no cameras or videos yet. The scroll drawings are all they get of themselves.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1273', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B7%BB', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:41:28.691553Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.811879Z'),
(1274, 'kanji', '捜', '捜', 'You want to get your <radical>fingers</radical> on the <radical>humble</radical> <radical>stool</radical>, so you dedicate your life to <kanji>search</kanji> for it. Wait, the humble stool? You know, like the holy grail or the ark of the covenant, but it''s called the humble stool. It''s from some bible or something, I promise.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1274', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8D%9C', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:42:07.705584Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.821861Z'),
(1275, 'kanji', '絞', '絞', 'A <radical>thread</radical> is <radical>mix</radical>ed all around and begins to <kanji>strangle</kanji> you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1275', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B5%9E', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:42:54.899118Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.841527Z'),
(1276, 'kanji', '輩', '輩', 'The <radical>criminal</radical> that stole your <radical>car</radical> was your <kanji>comrade</kanji>! You thought the two of you were friends, but you were probably wrong.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1276', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BC%A9', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:43:31.109206Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.883973Z'),
(1277, 'kanji', '隠', '隠', 'Behind a <radical>building</radical>, a <radical>cleat</radical>-wearing <radical>Wolverine</radical> is holding his <radical>heart</radical> and trying to <kanji>hide</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1277', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9A%A0', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:44:13.480117Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:39:30.793083Z'),
(1278, 'kanji', '掛', '掛', 'Someone covered your <radical>fingers</radical> with <radical>dirt</radical> and also got dirt on your <radical>toe</radical>s. So you''re going to <kanji>hang</kanji> them upside-down and slap them with your dirty extremities.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1278', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8E%9B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:44:49.983415Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.950195Z'),
(1279, 'kanji', '替', '替', 'A <radical>husband</radical> marries a <radical>husband</radical> while the <radical>sun</radical> goes down and the men <kanji>replace</kanji> their solitary lives with one of everlasting companionship. Instead of one husband and one wife, we have two husbands here. They shed their lives of bachelorhood and replace it with one of monogamy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1279', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9B%BF', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:45:26.750171Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.967775Z'),
(1280, 'kanji', '居', '居', 'The <radical>flag</radical> is <radical>old</radical> but that doesn''t mean it''s not still <kanji>alive</kanji>. This is a living flag, and it gets old just like other living things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1280', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B1%85', 25, 1, '2013-01-16T06:22:35.718006Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:39:30.793083Z'),
(1345, 'kanji', '給', '給', 'Your <radical>thread</radical>s have to <radical>suit</radical> you if you want a good <kanji>salary</kanji>. No one is going to give you a salary if your clothes don''t suit you. You have to look professional!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1345', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B5%A6', 25, 1, '2013-01-24T03:52:32.977766Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.918899Z'),
(1371, 'kanji', '絶', '絶', 'Sometime in the future, <radical>thread</radical> of every <radical>color</radical> will be faced with <kanji>extinction</kanji>. Eventually there will be nothing else to make thread out of, and thread of every kind and color will reach extinction.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1371', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B5%B6', 25, 1, '2013-01-24T09:31:29.107718Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:53:59.489141Z'),
(1954, 'kanji', '涼', '涼', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> causes the <radical>capital</radical> to become very <kanji>cool</kanji>. The capital was already a pretty cool temperature, but with the addition of the water from the tsunami, the capital is now very cool.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1954', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B6%BC', 25, 1, '2013-08-02T21:38:15.384893Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.527477Z'),
(1281, 'kanji', '造', '造', '"A <radical>scooter</radical>!" you <radical>announce</radical>. You are announcing this because you decided this is what you would <kanji>create</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1281', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%A0', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:27:34.153383Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.694321Z'),
(1282, 'kanji', '授', '授', 'There are <radical>fingers</radical> reaching towards you asking you to <radical>accept</radical> to <kanji>instruct</kanji> them. They''re the fingers of students. And you''re the teacher. See them reach out for knowledge. They want to accept this knowledge into their hearts and brains. Instruct them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1282', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8E%88', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:28:00.553024Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T22:19:06.645866Z'),
(1283, 'kanji', '印', '印', 'You take this <radical>comb</radical>, break the handle off, and use it to <radical>stamp</radical> your official <kanji>seal</kanji> of approval onto a document. It''s kind of a messy <kanji>mark</kanji>ing process, but it gets the job done.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1283', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%B0', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:28:34.594616Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.697643Z'),
(1284, 'kanji', '創', '創', 'In your <radical>warehouse</radical> you use your <radical>knife</radical> to <kanji>create</kanji>. You keep old pieces of wood in your warehouse, and you use your knife to carve them into new things that only you could create.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1284', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%89%B5', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:29:14.367356Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.723136Z'),
(1285, 'kanji', '復', '復', 'You <radical>loiter</radical> around a <radical>black hole</radical>, waiting for it to finish destroying and switch to <kanji>restore</kanji> mode. You were told all black holes finish eventually, and when they do you can press a button to restore everything back to the way it was.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1285', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%A9', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:29:43.287967Z', NULL, '2025-10-19T17:57:34.513342Z'),
(1286, 'kanji', '往', '往', 'You <radical>loiter</radical> around your <radical>master</radical> until he <kanji>depart</kanji>s on a <kanji>journey</kanji>. You have a lot more freedom to do things after your master has departed, so until that time you just loiter about looking busy and staying out of his way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1286', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%80', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:30:19.538059Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.75451Z'),
(1287, 'kanji', '較', '較', 'Your new <radical>car</radical> doesn’t <radical>mix</radical> well with the other cars in your garage because its color is in stark <kanji>contrast</kanji> to the others. All of your other cars are pristine white in color, but this new car is a mix of lots of colors. It almost looks like a peacock or something it’s so colorful. Such a contrast.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1287', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BC%83', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:30:48.114009Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.75586Z'),
(1288, 'kanji', '筆', '筆', 'Out of <radical>bamboo</radical>, you make a <radical>brush</radical> – a <kanji>writing brush</kanji>. This is no ordinary brush, mind you. It is a brush specifically designed for writing elegant characters. And it’s made from bamboo!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1288', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AD%86', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:31:07.680835Z', NULL, '2026-03-01T05:30:15.474536Z'),
(1289, 'kanji', '鉛', '鉛', 'You hold <radical>gold</radical> <radical>fins</radical> in your <radical>mouth</radical> while shopping for pencil <kanji>lead</kanji> at the store. This pencil lead store only accepts gold fins as payment, but your fins don’t fit in your pockets and you need your hands to carry the lead so the gold fins go in your mouth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1289', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%89%9B', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:31:38.405412Z', NULL, '2026-03-01T05:30:15.474536Z'),
(1290, 'kanji', '貯', '貯', 'You keep all of your <radical>shellfish</radical> under a <radical>roof</radical> down the <radical>street</radical> and it''s your <kanji>savings</kanji>. Shellfish used to be used as money way back, and these aren''t shellfish you''re spending, they''re ones you''re saving up for later.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1290', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%AF', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:32:36.562483Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:38:31.249893Z'),
(1291, 'kanji', '故', '故', 'The <radical>old</radical> <radical>winter</radical> is to blame for today''s <kanji>circumstance</kanji>. It is the <kanji>reason</kanji> things are bad now. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1291', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%95%85', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:33:06.812046Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:13:37.694956Z'),
(1292, 'kanji', '障', '障', 'There''s a <radical>building</radical> standing in the way of the next <radical>chapter</radical> in your life, and it''s <kanji>hinder</kanji>ing you. It''s not like you couldn''t find another way around, but it''s definitely inconvenient.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1292', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9A%9C', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:33:46.485774Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T02:34:05.249154Z'),
(1293, 'kanji', '従', '従', 'If you see someone <radical>loiter</radical>ing around with <radical>horns</radical> on their head you''d be <radical>correct</radical> to <kanji>obey</kanji> and <kanji>accompany</kanji> them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1293', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%93', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:34:35.49845Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:21:54.297415Z'),
(1294, 'kanji', '我', '我', 'Your <radical>ego</radical>... it''s totally out of control. <kanji>I</kanji> mean, my ego isn''t out of control. I''m the greatest. That''s <kanji>me</kanji>. But your ego? Ugh, so out of control unlike my amazing ego.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1294', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%88%91', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:35:53.50881Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:45:51.018923Z'),
(1295, 'kanji', '激', '激', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> that is <radical>white</radical>, traveling in the <radical>direction</radical> of <radical>winter</radical> is a <kanji>fierce</kanji> and <kanji>violent</kanji> force of nature. This white tsunami is chasing after winter, and that’s why the tsunami is white—it’s a winter tsunami. It’s the most fierce and most violent kind of tsunami in existence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1295', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BF%80', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:36:32.266444Z', NULL, '2025-12-28T19:49:22.248784Z'),
(1297, 'kanji', '励', '励', 'The "<radical>Cliff</radical> of <radical>Ten Thousand</radical> <radical>Power</radical>" is where you go if you want to be <kanji>encourage</kanji>d.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1297', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8A%B1', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:37:38.007641Z', NULL, '2025-10-31T14:36:39.038315Z'),
(1298, 'kanji', '討', '討', 'They <radical>say</radical> using a <radical>measurement</radical> device (like a ruler) is a good way to <kanji>chastise</kanji> someone. They do something bad? Chastise them by slapping their wrists, their feet, their eyes, ears, anything! They say something out of turn? CHASTISE THEM!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1298', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%8E', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:38:10.682199Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.875496Z'),
(1299, 'kanji', '郵', '郵', 'The <radical>droopy</radical> <radical>building</radical> is where people droop under big sacks of <kanji>mail</kanji>, aka a <kanji>post</kanji> office.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1299', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%83%B5', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:38:46.872244Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:11:22.491305Z'),
(1300, 'kanji', '針', '針', 'In a <radical>gold</radical> <radical>cross</radical>, you see a <kanji>needle</kanji> sticking out of it. The needle is right in the middle of the gold cross. If Jesus were on there, the needle would be sticking him right in the chest.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1300', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%87%9D', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:39:16.832322Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.882794Z'),
(1301, 'kanji', '徴', '徴', 'A <radical>loiter</radical>ing <radical>mountain</radical> <radical>king</radical> in the <radical>winter</radical> is an <kanji>indication</kanji> or <kanji>sign</kanji> of something to come.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1301', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%B4', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:40:06.721414Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.88546Z'),
(1302, 'kanji', '怪', '怪', 'You see a <radical>soul</radical> floating around a <radical>tombstone</radical> acting <kanji>suspicious</kanji>. To be fair, if a soul is going to be anywhere, a tombstone is probably high up on the list, but this one seems like it''s doing something weird.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1302', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%80%AA', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:40:41.515446Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(1303, 'kanji', '獣', '獣', 'On the <radical>grass</radical> near a <radical>rice paddy</radical> sits a <radical>creeper</radical> that is some sort of a <radical>dog</radical> <kanji>beast</kanji>, a vicious <kanji>animal</kanji>. This creeper isn’t just a normal dog, it is more beast-like, more like a feral animal than domesticated pet.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1303', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8D%A3', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:41:20.640289Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:25:43.711137Z'),
(1305, 'kanji', '菓', '菓', 'You take some <radical>flowers</radical> and some <radical>fruit</radical> and you make a <kanji>cake</kanji> out of it. It’s a very nice looking, flavorful cake. The fruit makes it taste great and the flowers make for great cake decorations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1305', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8F%93', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:42:31.109348Z', NULL, '2026-03-20T15:33:33.561611Z'),
(1306, 'kanji', '河', '河', 'During a <radical>tsunami</radical>, your <radical>lip ring</radical> is ripped off while you’re trying to cross a <kanji>river</kanji>. There was no other path to safety, you had to cross the raging river, but you lost your footing and smashed your face on a rock and lost your lip ring.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1306', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B2%B3', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:43:16.043203Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.937552Z'),
(1307, 'kanji', '振', '振', 'You''re trying to use your <radical>fingers</radical> to cause a <radical>landslide</radical> so you have to <kanji>shake</kanji> the earth. You probably won''t succeed, but the best you can do is to shake your hands on the dirt as hard as you can. It looks like you''re trying to <kanji>wave</kanji> at the ground now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1307', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8C%AF', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:44:19.629339Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:21:13.808797Z'),
(1308, 'kanji', '汗', '汗', 'The <radical>tsunami</radical> going down your <radical>dry</radical> face is just <kanji>sweat</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1308', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B1%97', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:44:56.867419Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.954395Z'),
(1310, 'kanji', '再', '再', 'Under the <radical>ground</radical>, a <radical>head</radical> covered in <radical>dirt</radical> emerges... <kanji>again</kanji>! It''s not the first time you''ve seen this buried head, nor is it the last. This dirt-covered head hides beneath the ground wherever you go, and keeps popping up to scare you again and again.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1310', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%86%8D', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:46:11.321363Z', NULL, '2025-12-29T00:39:15.506288Z'),
(1311, 'kanji', '接', '接', 'Your <radical>fingers</radical> touch as you <radical>stand</radical> with a <radical>woman</radical> and <kanji>adjoin</kanji> yourselves. You are united, you are one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1311', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8E%A5', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:46:54.149194Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1312, 'kanji', '独', '独', 'There once was an <radical>animal</radical> with an <radical>insect</radical> friend who felt very <kanji>alone</kanji> unless he was around. An animal and an insect are an unlikely pairing, but these two get along delightfully and hate to be alone without the other.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1312', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8B%AC', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:47:21.261909Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:20:52.300399Z'),
(1313, 'kanji', '占', '占', 'The  <radical>fortune</radical> radical and the <kanji>fortune</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! This can also mean <kanji>occupy</kanji>, maybe because learning your fortune shows you how you''re going to occupy your life. Or how it will occupy you…

You haven''t seen the radical in a while, so here''s a mnemonic to help you remember the kanji: Put your toe in your mouth to learn your <kanji>fortune</kanji>. The taste will tell you everything you need to know about your fortune. But your toe has to <kanji>occupy</kanji> your mouth first for it to work.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1313', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%A0', 26, 1, '2013-01-17T20:47:43.848274Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.990124Z'),
(1519, 'kanji', '垂', '垂', 'This kanji comes straight from the <radical>droopy</radical> radical. What do droopy things do? They <kanji>droop</kanji>. Oh, and sometimes they <kanji>dangle</kanji> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1519', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9E%82', 26, 1, '2013-03-18T17:05:50.143543Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:48:36.606365Z'),
(1314, 'kanji', '招', '招', 'Someone is reaching out with their <radical>fingers</radical> to <radical>call</radical> for you. They <kanji>beckon</kanji> you closer, this way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1314', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8B%9B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:31:04.994685Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.993607Z'),
(1315, 'kanji', '段', '段', 'Cut this <radical>comb</radical> up with your <radical>weapon</radical> and turn it into the <kanji>steps</kanji> for some <kanji>stairs</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1315', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AE%B5', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:32:33.636175Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:55.997909Z'),
(1316, 'kanji', '胃', '胃', 'If you eat a <radical>rice paddy</radical> the size of the <radical>moon</radical>, your <kanji>stomach</kanji> is going to hurt. All the rice in a rice paddy the size of the moon is way too much rice for anyone to consume. If you do, your stomach is going to ache like crazy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1316', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%83%83', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:32:58.544061Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:42:06.273579Z'),
(1317, 'kanji', '腹', '腹', 'Inside this <radical>moon</radical> is a <radical>black hole</radical>... haha just kidding, it''s your <kanji>belly</kanji>! It''s beautiful and round like the moon and it eats up everything you give it like a black hole. What a good <kanji>stomach</kanji> you have!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1317', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%85%B9', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:33:23.239451Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:29:41.541367Z'),
(1318, 'kanji', '痛', '痛', 'When your <radical>sick</radical> <radical>mama</radical> tries to do a <radical>task</radical> she''s filled with <kanji>pain</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1318', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%97%9B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:33:57.630021Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T17:50:14.053718Z'),
(1319, 'kanji', '退', '退', 'If you''re on your <radical>scooter</radical> and you run over a <radical>root</radical>, you''ll need to <kanji>retreat</kanji>. The roots have obviously <kanji>reject</kanji>ed you, they want you to leave, so you should.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1319', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%80', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:34:19.351361Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:39:10.821813Z'),
(1320, 'kanji', '屈', '屈', 'You see a <radical>flag</radical> by the <radical>exit</radical> letting you know to <kanji>yield</kanji>. There’s cross traffic just outside the exit, and the flag lets you know that you need to yield to them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1320', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B1%88', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:34:42.193742Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.02457Z'),
(1321, 'kanji', '悩', '悩', 'If your <radical>soul</radical> is lying in the <radical>grass</radical> in a <radical>treasure chest</radical>, you''re going to <kanji>worry</kanji> about it. It''s just sitting there, out in the open, waiting for anyone to come and open it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1321', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%82%A9', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:35:13.69265Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(1322, 'kanji', '暇', '暇', 'In the <radical>sun</radical> while holding a <radical>comb</radical> in a <radical>cage</radical> and sitting on a <radical>stool</radical>, you comb your hair in your <kanji>spare time</kanji>. Since you’re in a cage, you actually fashioned this comb yourself, so it’s a little messed up, missing a prong. You’re working on improving it in your spare time, and you also use it to style your mop.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1322', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9A%87', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:35:53.101491Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.055519Z'),
(1323, 'kanji', '織', '織', 'Taking a <radical>thread</radical> and moving to the <radical>sound</radical> of the music, a <radical>drunkard</radical> makes a <kanji>weave</kanji> through some fabric. He’s using the thread to weave his clothes back together after he damaged them in a fall down the stairs. You know, because he’s a drunkard.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1323', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B9%94', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:39:56.936966Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:13:44.248099Z'),
(1324, 'kanji', '貸', '貸', 'You <radical>substitute</radical> some <radical>shellfish</radical> for collateral so your friend will <kanji>lend</kanji> you some money. Normally he would ask for something more valuable, but you’ve convinced him to substitute these shellfish for what he would normally take as collateral when lending to people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1324', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%B8', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:40:25.991391Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:07:15.98436Z'),
(1325, 'kanji', '迷', '迷', 'If you''re riding your <radical>scooter</radical> through some <radical>rice</radical>, it''s likely you''ve gone <kanji>astray</kanji>. Also you might be <kanji>lost</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1325', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BF%B7', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:40:52.221196Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T01:01:20.002359Z'),
(1326, 'kanji', '惑', '惑', 'A <radical>drunkard</radical> who has his <radical>mouth</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical>, has a <radical>heart</radical> that is <kanji>misguided</kanji>. First of all, he tries to drink his problems away, but worse, he''s so drunk he''s trying to drink out of the ground.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1326', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%83%91', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:41:18.856229Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.081228Z'),
(1327, 'kanji', '誘', '誘', 'You <radical>say</radical> you''ve left some <radical>grain</radical> on the <radical>stairs</radical>. This is how you <kanji>invite</kanji> people into your place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1327', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AA%98', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:41:50.52497Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:41:30.052698Z'),
(1328, 'kanji', '就', '就', 'In the <radical>capital</radical>, even a broken <radical>dog</radical> can <kanji>get a position</kanji>. That''s because the capital has so many jobs available that even a poor, broken dog can find a place to work.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1328', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%B1', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:42:19.807485Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:44:44.492541Z'),
(1329, 'kanji', '訪', '訪', 'Someone just asked you to <radical>say</radical> which <radical>direction</radical> they should <kanji>visit</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1329', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%AA', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:42:41.117256Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:10:32.744964Z'),
(1330, 'kanji', '怒', '怒', 'A <radical>woman</radical> sitting on a <radical>stool</radical> with a broken <radical>heart</radical> is very <kanji>angry</kanji>. She had her heart broken by some jerk and now she’s sitting on the stool, just stewing over the guy and how angry she is with him.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1330', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%80%92', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:43:13.224837Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:38:47.67043Z'),
(1331, 'kanji', '昇', '昇', 'You want to go to the <radical>sun</radical> so you set up a <radical>slide</radical> <radical>twenty</radical> stories tall so you can <kanji>ascend</kanji> into the heavens.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1331', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%98%87', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:43:37.478187Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.120527Z'),
(1332, 'kanji', '眠', '眠', 'Closing each <radical>eye</radical> is something that the <radical>peoples</radical> do before they <kanji>sleep</kanji>. The peoples can’t sleep with both eyes open and they can’t sleep with just one eye open either. Peoples have to close each eye before they can sleep.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1332', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9C%A0', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:43:59.590216Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:38:20.446355Z'),
(1333, 'kanji', '睡', '睡', 'When your <radical>eye</radical>s get <radical>droopy</radical>, it means you''re getting <kanji>drowsy</kanji>. Go get some shut-eye, you droopy-eyed drowsy person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1333', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9D%A1', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:44:23.386254Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T15:27:05.755219Z'),
(1334, 'kanji', '症', '症', 'When someone is <radical>sick</radical> in order to diagnose the <radical>correct</radical> cause and treatment, you need to check their <kanji>symptom</kanji>s. You have to be especially careful too, because if you get a symptom wrong, you could kill them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1334', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%97%87', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:44:50.712044Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:38:20.446355Z'),
(1335, 'kanji', '締', '締', 'You pull on a <radical>thread</radical> as you <radical>stand</radical> with a <radical>towel</radical> wrapped around you. This allows you to <kanji>tighten</kanji> the towel.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1335', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B7%A0', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:45:10.322487Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:39:13.499353Z'),
(1336, 'kanji', '迫', '迫', 'The <radical>scooter</radical> is <radical>white</radical>. This color will <kanji>urge</kanji> and <kanji>compel</kanji> you to use it. Scooter companies use colors to get you to do what they want. Some would say they are trying to <kanji>coerce</kanji> you, even.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1336', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BF%AB', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:45:37.089099Z', NULL, '2025-10-16T19:52:24.117009Z'),
(1337, 'kanji', '靴', '靴', 'Take your <radical>leather</radical> and <radical>change</radical> it into <kanji>shoes</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1337', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9D%B4', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:47:45.750603Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.153858Z'),
(1338, 'kanji', '濃', '濃', 'After a <radical>tsunami</radical> hit your <radical>farming</radical> land, you''re gonna see a lot of <kanji>dense</kanji> silt left behind. This <kanji>thick</kanji> layer really enriches the soil, and your crops will come out tasting extra thick and dense in flavor.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1338', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BF%83', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:48:06.126281Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.158364Z'),
(1339, 'kanji', '端', '端', 'Setting up a <radical>stand</radical> on the top of a <radical>mountain</radical>, you <radical>rake</radical> all the rocks to the <kanji>edge</kanji> of it. You want to clear the area around your stand, so you get your rake out and you rake all the stones and debris away from the stand and toward the edge of the mountain top. It also creates a small barrier around the edge as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1339', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AB%AF', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:48:30.955842Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.161577Z'),
(1340, 'kanji', '極', '極', 'There aren''t many <radical>tree</radical>s in your under<radical>ground</radical> <radical>prison</radical>, so you''ve been given a task: using only your <radical>mouth</radical> and a <radical>stool</radical>, plant as many in the <radical>ground</radical> as you can. It''s an <kanji>extreme</kanji> challenge!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1340', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A5%B5', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:50:37.750132Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(1341, 'kanji', '途', '途', 'When you ride a <radical>scooter</radical> you''ll have a <radical>surplus</radical> of possible <kanji>route</kanji>s. Not only can you drive on any road, but you can ride on the sidewalk too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1341', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%94', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:50:58.62952Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.18549Z'),
(1342, 'kanji', '健', '健', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of your <radical>yoga</radical> class uses a special <radical>brush</radical> to stay <kanji>healthy</kanji>. She rubs the brush on her long arms and legs, and she never ever gets sick.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1342', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%81%A5', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:51:28.693988Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T19:04:16.361248Z'),
(1343, 'kanji', '康', '康', 'You see a <radical>canopy</radical> under which someone has left a <radical>brush</radical> (in its 肀 form) next to the <radical>water</radical>, creating a scene of such <kanji>ease</kanji> and <kanji>peace</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1343', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%B7', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:51:51.742036Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.1892Z'),
(1344, 'kanji', '郎', '郎', 'Inside the "<radical>Good</radical> <radical>Building</radical>" there''s a single <kanji>guy</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1344', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%83%8E', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T03:52:10.224235Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.193225Z'),
(2429, 'kanji', '憧', '憧', 'Your <radical>soul</radical> <radical>stand</radical>s in the <radical>village</radical> you <kanji>long for</kanji>. It''s the village you''re from, and no matter how far you go, no matter how old you get, your soul is still there, and you <kanji>yearn</kanji> for it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2429', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%86%A7', 27, 1, '2015-07-14T23:27:13.733176Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(1346, 'kanji', '逆', '逆', 'If you ride your <radical>scooter</radical> while wearing <radical>horns</radical> in a <radical>barracks</radical>, you''re going to get stuck and have to <kanji>reverse</kanji> out of there in the <kanji>opposite</kanji> direction.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1346', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%86', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:09:47.280561Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:30:39.302482Z'),
(1347, 'kanji', '巨', '巨', 'The <radical>giant</radical> radical and the <kanji>giant</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Massive!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1347', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B7%A8', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:10:18.028467Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:51:44.888424Z'),
(1348, 'kanji', '庫', '庫', 'A <radical>canopy</radical> is over your <radical>car</radical> because your car is in <kanji>storage</kanji> in a large <kanji>storehouse</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1348', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%AB', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:10:49.687248Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.244243Z'),
(1349, 'kanji', '児', '児', 'Pointing your magic solar powered wishing <radical>stick</radical> at the <radical>sun</radical>, you attain the <radical>legs</radical> of a <kanji>child</kanji>. You’re old and want young legs to carry you around, so you took your wishing stick that’s powered by the sun, and you wished for the legs of a child.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1349', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%90', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:11:35.693261Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T17:49:15.447292Z'),
(1350, 'kanji', '冷', '冷', 'You got your <radical>ice</radical> <radical>orders</radical>. Of course, your ice orders are to make things <kanji>cool</kanji> and <kanji>cold</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1350', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%86%B7', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:12:07.38273Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:27:09.777362Z'),
(1351, 'kanji', '凍', '凍', '<radical>Ice</radical> from the <radical>east</radical> is <kanji>frozen</kanji>. Well, of course, all ice is frozen, but ice from the east is like, really super frozen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1351', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%87%8D', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:12:37.208961Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:09:25.99139Z'),
(1352, 'kanji', '幼', '幼', 'Super <radical>poop</radical> is a super <radical>power</radical> you have in <kanji>infancy</kanji>. Those in infancy have the power to poop at will, whenever and wherever they please. Infancy poop power!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1352', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B9%BC', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:13:06.464062Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.278875Z'),
(1353, 'kanji', '稚', '稚', 'Only give <radical>grain</radical>s to a <radical>turkey</radical> if it''s <kanji>immature</kanji>. Adult turkeys should be eating healthier things, like insects and grass. But immature turkeys don''t care if they get fat, so they whine and whine and whine until you give them grains.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1353', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A8%9A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:13:50.476478Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(1354, 'kanji', '処', '処', 'A <radical>winter</radical> <radical>table</radical> would be an odd thing to <kanji>deal with</kanji>. Winter can be hard to deal with on its own, but turn your table into winter? Just dealing with your food getting cold would be the worst!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1354', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%87%A6', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:14:14.352024Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:08:13.714269Z'),
(1355, 'kanji', '博', '博', 'You wear a great big <radical>cross</radical> with a <radical>drop</radical> on it, and head to a <radical>specialty</radical> <kanji>exhibition</kanji> for <kanji>gambling</kanji>. The big cross and drop on your chest is your badge for being admitted into the exhibition. Everyone at the specialty exhibition has a cross and drop, and everyone is gambling.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1355', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%9A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:14:38.864113Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.310082Z'),
(1356, 'kanji', '清', '清', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> that''s <radical>blue</radical> is <kanji>pure</kanji>. Some tsunami are other colors, like brown or red because they aren''t pure, they''ll full of trash and poop and other horrible things. But a blue tsunami is completely pure!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1356', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B8%85', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:15:02.90843Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:22:25.515687Z'),
(1357, 'kanji', '潔', '潔', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> takes <radical>life</radical>, cuts it like a <radical>sword</radical> through <radical>thread</radical>, and leaves everything behind completely <kanji>pure</kanji> and <kanji>clean</kanji>. Everything is dead and gone, wiped clean off the face of the earth, thanks to the tsunami. It''s back to the way it was before humans were here, completely undefiled.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1357', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BD%94', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:15:29.003858Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:41:20.37036Z'),
(1358, 'kanji', '録', '録', 'A <radical>gold</radical> <radical>Wolverine</radical> swam in the <radical>water</radical> while his father made a <kanji>record</kanji> of it. Wolverine is gold in color because he just won the gold medal in swimming, so now he is gold. Way to go, Wolverine.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1358', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8C%B2', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:15:58.023683Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T22:28:42.680114Z'),
(1359, 'kanji', '隊', '隊', 'This <radical>building</radical> features <radical>horns</radical> on a <radical>pig</radical> to represent our <kanji>squad</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1359', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9A%8A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:16:21.423697Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:50:20.479921Z'),
(1360, 'kanji', '修', '修', 'Your <radical>leader</radical> is using a <radical>stick</radical> in the <radical>winter</radical> made of <radical>hair</radical>. This is the stick of <kanji>mastering</kanji> and <kanji>repair</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1360', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BF%AE', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:16:59.886026Z', NULL, '2025-11-05T12:02:20.008232Z'),
(1361, 'kanji', '券', '券', 'The <radical>gladiator</radical> in front of you draws his <radical>sword</radical>. He points the sword at your chest and right when you think everything is about to end, you realize he''s pointing the sword at the <kanji>ticket</kanji> in your hand.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1361', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%B8', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:17:57.398467Z', NULL, '2025-12-23T03:07:20.331935Z'),
(1362, 'kanji', '婦', '婦', 'See that <radical>woman</radical> with the <radical>broom</radical>? That''s the <kanji>lady</kanji> of the house. She''s getting ready to hand that broom to you — her butler — because you missed a spot over there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1362', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A9%A6', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:18:36.549451Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:39:55.920659Z'),
(1363, 'kanji', '奇', '奇', 'The <radical>odd</radical> radical and the <kanji>odd</kanji> kanji are the same. They''re both... really <kanji>strange</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1363', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A5%87', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:23:17.370465Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.370107Z'),
(1364, 'kanji', '妙', '妙', 'A <radical>woman</radical> is one of the <radical>few</radical> <kanji>peculiar</kanji> things in the world. Everything but a woman can be explained and made sense of. There are only a few truly peculiar things in this world, with woman being one of them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1364', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A6%99', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:23:43.554936Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1365, 'kanji', '麗', '麗', 'If you know the radical meaning <radical>lovely</radical>, you know the kanji meaning <kanji>lovely</kanji> as well!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1365', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%BA%97', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:28:33.720722Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.399458Z'),
(1366, 'kanji', '微', '微', 'You <radical>loiter</radical> on the <radical>mountain</radical>, reading off the digits of <radical>pi</radical> on this <radical>winter</radical> morning. The cold mountain air is so <kanji>delicate</kanji>, and changes only in very <kanji>tiny</kanji> ways — just like pi when you add another digit to it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1366', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%AE', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:29:09.249367Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.424973Z'),
(1367, 'kanji', '益', '益', 'There are some <radical>horns</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical> next to <radical>fins</radical> on a <radical>plate</radical> that are meant to <kanji>benefit</kanji> everyone. If you''re a member of this town, one of your benefits is this little set up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1367', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9B%8A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:29:40.443099Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.423457Z'),
(1368, 'kanji', '移', '移', 'A <radical>grain</radical>, from one <radical>evening</radical> to the next <radical>evening</radical>, experiences a <kanji>shift</kanji>. After two evenings, the grain has shifted from one location to another.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1368', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A7%BB', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:30:11.693831Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.424081Z'),
(1369, 'kanji', '程', '程', 'The <radical>Grain</radical> <radical>Mouth</radical> <radical>King</radical> pours grain from his mouth for you and his other subjects. You go to him and pull his finger. When you do this, grain pours from his mouth. But we were greedy. We didn''t understand the <kanji>extent</kanji> to which we were harvesting his mouth wheat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1369', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A8%8B', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:30:35.632605Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T00:29:41.148502Z'),
(1370, 'kanji', '精', '精', '<radical>Rice</radical> that is <radical>blue</radical> has a strong <kanji>spirit</kanji>. Our spirits are blue, so if you ever see rice (or other food, or even people) that''s blue, you know that''s their spirit radiating out!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1370', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B2%BE', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:30:57.781056Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:42:22.595675Z'),
(1372, 'kanji', '並', '並', 'You stick <radical>horns</radical> in the <radical>ground</radical> using them as <radical>spikes</radical> and you <kanji>line up</kanji> the horns in a straight line. Horns are pretty much the same thing as spikes once you stick them in the ground, and you line these ones up precisely for optimal protection.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1372', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%A6', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:31:58.497256Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:13:34.472003Z'),
(1373, 'kanji', '憲', '憲', 'The <radical>roof</radical> protecting your <radical>life</radical> and the <radical>net</radical> protecting your <radical>heart</radical> are both protected by the <kanji>constitution</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1373', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%86%B2', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:32:38.940077Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T08:21:38.167938Z'),
(1374, 'kanji', '衆', '衆', '<radical>Blood</radical> and <radical>hair</radical> and <radical>tofu</radical> make up the <kanji>populace</kanji>. Those are the three categories the entire populace fits into, anyway.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1374', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A1%86', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:33:16.611016Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.464141Z'),
(1375, 'kanji', '傘', '傘', 'Using a <radical>hat</radical>, four <radical>person</radical>s with a <radical>cross</radical> make an <kanji>umbrella</kanji>. The hat is big enough to be an umbrella for all four people, so it makes sense. All four of them are religious missionaries, so that''s why they''re holding the cross under the "umbrella."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1375', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%82%98', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:33:57.624219Z', NULL, '2025-12-02T00:48:11.925936Z'),
(1376, 'kanji', '浜', '浜', 'After a <radical>tsunami</radical>, you find an <radical>axe</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical> next to some <radical>fins</radical> on the <kanji>beach</kanji>. The tsunami was pretty violent, and it looks like this axe got blown into some fish and cut off their fins and now those things are just on the ground by the beach.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1376', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B5%9C', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:34:26.513831Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.476875Z'),
(1377, 'kanji', '撃', '撃', 'If someone is coming toward you in a <radical>car</radical>, holding a <radical>weapon</radical> in their <radical>hand</radical>, they''re going to <kanji>attack</kanji> you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1377', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%92%83', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:34:56.635922Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T16:52:18.759386Z'),
(1378, 'kanji', '攻', '攻', '<radical>Construction</radical> in the <radical>winter</radical> leads to <kanji>aggression</kanji>. Just think about it. It''s cold, it''s unpleasant, and you don''t want to be there. So, you start to develop an aggression towards other people working with you on this winter project.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1378', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%94%BB', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:35:25.173462Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T16:52:18.759386Z'),
(1479, 'kanji', '綺', '綺', 'This <radical>thread</radical> is <radical>odd</radical>, but that''s what makes it <kanji>beautiful</kanji>. It''s a little frayed, it''s a little miscolored, but that''s what makes it appealing to you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1479', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B6%BA', 28, 1, '2013-03-15T17:25:22.652475Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.200676Z'),
(1866, 'kanji', '沼', '沼', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> can''t resist the <radical>call</radical> of a <kanji>bog</kanji>. Whenever a tsunami is headed toward land, a bog will always call out to it, "Hey tsunami, don''t you want to hit me with your water? Come this way~"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1866', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B2%BC', 28, 1, '2013-07-02T21:39:12.990012Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.056524Z'),
(1379, 'kanji', '監', '監', 'To the <radical>servant</radical> you say: "<radical>gun</radical>, <radical>ground</radical>, <radical>plate</radical>." It is your job to <kanji>oversee</kanji> everything they do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1379', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9B%A3', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T18:54:45.25554Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.502484Z'),
(1380, 'kanji', '杯', '杯', 'A <radical>tree</radical> is <radical>not</radical> a <kanji>cup of liquid</kanji> or a <kanji>counter for cups</kanji>. Don’t get them mixed up. The last time you tried making a cup out of a tree to count your throat got filled with splinters when you used it. There wasn''t even any liquid in it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1380', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9D%AF', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T18:55:10.976244Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T08:22:08.397381Z'),
(1381, 'kanji', '乾', '乾', 'In the <radical>morning</radical>, you use a <radical>gun</radical> on your <radical>nose</radical> to make it <kanji>dry</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1381', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B9%BE', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T18:57:49.277097Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:46:14.836586Z'),
(1382, 'kanji', '催', '催', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of the <radical>mountain</radical> <radical>turkey</radical>s is our <kanji>sponsor</kanji>. He''s paying us to say and type everything you''re reading.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1382', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%82%AC', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T18:58:14.18945Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(1383, 'kanji', '促', '促', 'Your <radical>leader</radical> sees your bare <radical>foot</radical> and <kanji>urge</kanji>s you to put a sock on it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1383', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BF%83', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T18:58:47.058857Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T03:07:13.887023Z'),
(1384, 'kanji', '欧', '欧', 'A <radical>district</radical> that <radical>lack</radical>s things will be in <kanji>Europe</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1384', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AC%A7', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:01:50.096105Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.529717Z'),
(1385, 'kanji', '江', '江', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> rolls in and destroys all the <radical>construction</radical> that was being done on the <kanji>inlet</kanji> and the <kanji>bay</kanji>. Both the inlet and the bay were having a lot of construction done on them to repair the damage from the last tsunami, and then another tsunami rolled in and destroyed all that too. Bummer.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1385', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B1%9F', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:02:11.184021Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:13:55.09106Z'),
(1386, 'kanji', '請', '請', 'If what you <radical>say</radical> comes out <radical>blue</radical>, everyone knows that means you made a <kanji>request</kanji>. You request a favor and the words slide out of your mouth in a bright, blue blob. They hover in front of you for a few seconds, before splashing onto the person you made the request to.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1386', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AB%8B', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:02:33.896341Z', NULL, '2026-03-01T17:02:02.513202Z'),
(1387, 'kanji', '雄', '雄', 'The <radical>narwhal</radical> is meeting in <radical>private</radical> with the <radical>turkey</radical> so they can, uh, show each other some private things. Doing this they realize they''re both <kanji>male</kanji>! You gotta be pretty <kanji>manly</kanji> and <kanji>brave</kanji> to do something like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1387', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%84', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:03:13.147169Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(1389, 'kanji', '壊', '壊', 'In the <radical>dirt</radical> you place a <radical>cross</radical> and surround it with a <radical>net</radical> made of <radical>clothes</radical> to keep it safe, but this net tends to <kanji>break</kanji>. Of course it breaks, it''s just some clothes you tied together. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1389', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A3%8A', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:04:02.883118Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:31:18.713932Z'),
(1390, 'kanji', '診', '診', 'They <radical>say</radical> to wear a <radical>hat</radical> to cover your <radical>hair</radical> if your doctor <kanji>diagnose</kanji>s you with hair loss. It''s the best option, folks. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1390', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%BA', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:04:33.636923Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.57455Z'),
(1391, 'kanji', '閣', '閣', 'At the <radical>gate</radical>, you <radical>kiss</radical> <kanji>the cabinet</kanji> members, one by one. In order to gain access to this government building, you have to kiss all the members of the cabinet at the gate. What a strange ritual.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1391', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%96%A3', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:05:17.896208Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:20:45.121776Z'),
(1392, 'kanji', '僚', '僚', 'A good <radical>leader</radical> will use <radical>charcoal</radical> to mark her <kanji>colleague</kanji>s. Sometimes if leaders aren''t around all the time, people will get rebellious and act badly toward people who can get them in trouble. So when you mark them with charcoal you know that someone close with your leader is around and you should probably stop being a butt.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1392', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%83%9A', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:05:49.763482Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:01:37.215887Z'),
(1393, 'kanji', '積', '積', '<radical>Grain</radical>s get <radical>blame</radical>d for how much they <kanji>accumulate</kanji>. If the grains accumulate too much then they make more work for the farmers. If they accumulate too little, then they''re starving the people. Ugh, grains just can''t win with you people. They''re blamed for everything.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1393', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A9%8D', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:06:20.169278Z', NULL, '2026-03-20T15:00:51.748266Z'),
(1394, 'kanji', '督', '督', '<radical>Above</radical> you on a <radical>small</radical> <radical>stool</radical> is the <radical>eye</radical> of the one who will <kanji>direct</kanji> and <kanji>supervise</kanji> you at work. He’s really high above you, so all you can see is the glint of his supervising eye. But you can hear him shouting directions clearly, telling you exactly what to do. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1394', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9D%A3', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:07:28.568422Z', NULL, '2026-01-01T17:02:29.766929Z'),
(1395, 'kanji', '臣', '臣', 'The <radical>servant</radical> radical and the <kanji>servant</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Just remember that the kanji also means <kanji>retainer</kanji> and you’ll be fine.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1395', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%87%A3', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:07:46.349135Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.594987Z'),
(1396, 'kanji', '略', '略', 'A <radical>rice paddy</radical> you''d want to <radical>kiss</radical> is one that would probably have a cool <kanji>abbreviation</kanji> for their name, like Ri''-Pad. Yeah, that''s definitely a cool abbreviation! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1396', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%95%A5', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:08:08.563896Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T15:10:41.266432Z'),
(1397, 'kanji', '航', '航', 'On every <radical>boat</radical> there is a <radical>lid</radical> on the <radical>table</radical> that''s used for <kanji>navigation</kanji>. When you take the lid off, there is a big map, a sextant, and some little boat miniatures. All the navigation tools.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1397', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%88%AA', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:08:35.564537Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T18:57:58.987068Z'),
(1398, 'kanji', '寄', '寄', 'Under this <radical>roof</radical> someone <radical>odd</radical> is trying to <kanji>draw near</kanji> to you. You back up, but they continue to draw near anyway.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1398', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%84', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:10:07.761942Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.623088Z'),
(1399, 'kanji', '板', '板', 'If you''re a <radical>tree</radical> <radical>anti</radical> (someone who''s against trees), then you won''t mind if I turn all of them into <kanji>board</kanji>s! Hahaha!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1399', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9D%BF', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:14:08.983235Z', NULL, '2026-02-03T01:43:38.55616Z'),
(1400, 'kanji', '街', '街', 'If <radical>dirt</radical> and more <radical>dirt</radical> is all you see as you <radical>go</radical> from place to place, you must be roaming the <kanji>streets</kanji> of a <kanji>city</kanji>. Cities are notoriously dirty places, so watch your step when you head out into the streets.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1400', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A1%97', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T19:14:41.596209Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:56:15.259524Z'),
(1401, 'kanji', '宗', '宗', 'Under this <radical>roof</radical> you <radical>jackhammer</radical> your <kanji>religion</kanji> into people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1401', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%97', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:09:08.785286Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.63766Z'),
(1402, 'kanji', '緊', '緊', 'The <radical>servant</radical> sits on a <radical>stool</radical>, holding a piece of <radical>thread</radical>, looking very <kanji>tense</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1402', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B7%8A', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:09:32.510309Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.639242Z'),
(1403, 'kanji', '娘', '娘', 'A <radical>woman</radical> who is <radical>good</radical> is my <kanji>daughter</kanji>. My daughter is a good woman, I guarantee it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1403', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A8%98', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:10:03.701299Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1404, 'kanji', '宴', '宴', 'Under a <radical>roof</radical>, but still in the <radical>sun</radical>, a <radical>woman</radical> is laying out a fabulous <kanji>banquet</kanji>. The woman doesn''t want anyone to burn from the sun, but banquets are best outside, so she''s set up a roof to protect her and her guests.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1404', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%B4', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:10:31.624298Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1405, 'kanji', '怖', '怖', 'The <radical>soul</radical> of a <radical>narwhal</radical> was sucked into its <radical>towel</radical>, which is <kanji>scary</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1405', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%80%96', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:11:08.442049Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(1406, 'kanji', '恐', '恐', 'A <radical>saw</radical> cuts into your <radical>heart</radical> and you are filled with <kanji>fear</kanji>. Well, you were filled with fear before the saw reached your heart too, but now that the saw is cutting into your heart, you’re feeling more fear than ever.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1406', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%81%90', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:18:38.246622Z', NULL, '2025-10-12T18:54:49.21817Z'),
(1407, 'kanji', '添', '添', 'There’s a <radical>tsunami</radical> in <radical>heaven</radical> and you take a <radical>small</radical> <radical>drop</radical> of water and <kanji>append</kanji> it to the tsunami. Now the tsunami is big enough to destroy all of heaven. The small drop that you appended to the tsunami was just enough to push it over the edge, reaching full destruction capability.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1407', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B7%BB', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:18:58.785685Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.679108Z'),
(1408, 'kanji', '猛', '猛', 'An <radical>animal</radical> eats a <radical>child</radical> from a <radical>plate</radical> in a very <kanji>fierce</kanji> manner. This is a fierce animal, so it makes sense that it would eat the child in a fierce style.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1408', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8C%9B', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:19:20.715839Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:51:15.083936Z'),
(1409, 'kanji', '烈', '烈', 'When a <radical>yakuza</radical>''s <radical>knife</radical> <radical>boil</radical>s, you know something very <kanji>violent</kanji> and <kanji>intense</kanji> is about to happen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1409', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%83%88', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:19:45.383431Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.695624Z'),
(1410, 'kanji', '索', '索', 'There is a <radical>cross</radical> in your <radical>forehead</radical> with a <radical>thread</radical> attached to it, forcing you to <kanji>search</kanji> where it leads. You’re not sure how the cross got stuck in your forehead, but you are interested in seeing where this piece of thread leads. The search is on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1410', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%A2', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:21:18.927376Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:20:14.76257Z'),
(1411, 'kanji', '詰', '詰', '<radical>Say</radical> <radical>good luck</radical> to one person enough and they''ll be <kanji>stuffed</kanji> full of that luck.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1411', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A9%B0', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:21:44.445065Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:04:43.265681Z'),
(1723, 'kanji', '勧', '勧', 'There’s an <radical>arrow</radical> in that <radical>turkey</radical> and you do everything in your <radical>power</radical> to <kanji>recommend</kanji> that we don’t eat the turkey. There’s an arrow in this turkey. The arrow looks a little broken, so there’s probably splinters all up inside that turkey. I know you’re all hungry but I can’t recommend eating this bird.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1723', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8B%A7', 29, 1, '2013-06-07T17:20:02.062621Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(2145, 'kanji', '玄', '玄', 'The radical means <radical>mysterious</radical> and the kanji means the same thing: <kanji>mysterious</kanji>. It can also mean <kanji>dark</kanji>, ''cause mysterious things tend to be kinda dark and shadowy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2145', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8E%84', 29, 1, '2015-06-27T00:34:38.564467Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T06:49:57.6353Z'),
(1413, 'kanji', '魅', '魅', 'A <radical>demon</radical> on a <radical>jet</radical> is pretty <kanji>alluring</kanji>. Demons themselves are pretty intriguing, but put one on a jet? Now that’s alluring.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1413', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%AD%85', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:18:55.602844Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.717806Z'),
(1414, 'kanji', '渇', '渇', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> hits the <radical>sun</radical>, where you''re in <radical>prison</radical> with a <radical>spoon</radical> trying to catch some of it before it starts to <kanji>dry up</kanji>. Being in prison on the sun means your <kanji>thirst</kanji> is truly great, and this spoon will help you quench it when the tsunami comes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1414', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B8%87', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:19:41.392105Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(1415, 'kanji', '系', '系', 'The <radical>lineage</radical> radical and the <kanji>lineage</kanji> kanji are the same. Sometimes this kanji can also mean <kanji>system</kanji>. I guess a lineage is a kind of system, if you think about it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1415', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B3%BB', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:20:03.345289Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T01:19:34.265491Z'),
(1416, 'kanji', '婚', '婚', 'You meet a <radical>woman</radical> from another <radical>clan</radical> under the rising <radical>sun</radical> to prepare for <kanji>marriage</kanji>. Your clan and her clan are standing together as the two of you watch the sun rise together.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1416', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A9%9A', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:20:32.309551Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1417, 'kanji', '遊', '遊', 'On a <radical>scooter</radical> running in one <radical>direction</radical> is a <radical>gun</radical>-toting <radical>child</radical>. This is a terribly dangerous situation, but to the child it is just <kanji>play</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1417', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%8A', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:21:03.837331Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:47:45.528196Z'),
(1418, 'kanji', '旗', '旗', 'Traveling in the <radical>direction</radical> of the ocean, you take a <radical>gun</radical> and shoot a <radical>crab</radical>, then stick a <kanji>flag</kanji> in the beach and claim it as your own. The crab was the only thing standing in the way of you conquering the area. Now that the flag is in the ground, no one can dispute who it belongs to.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1418', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%97%97', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:21:47.190768Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T19:05:09.238484Z'),
(1419, 'kanji', '照', '照', 'The <radical>sun</radical> is being <radical>call</radical>ed on to <radical>boil</radical> the earth by <kanji>illuminating</kanji> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1419', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%85%A7', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:22:08.756648Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:20:56.922499Z'),
(1420, 'kanji', '快', '快', 'The <radical>soul</radical> of a <radical>person</radical> has a <radical>hook</radical> that can make them <kanji>pleasant</kanji>. When you see, hear, or feel something pleasant, can''t you feel that hook pressing into the middle of your body?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1420', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BF%AB', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:23:12.979393Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(1421, 'kanji', '版', '版', 'If you''re a <radical>one-sided</radical> paper <radical>anti</radical> (someone against one-sided paper), then you should bind all those one-sided papers into an <kanji>edition</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1421', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%89%88', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:23:36.801949Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:11:10.813897Z'),
(1422, 'kanji', '貧', '貧', 'Those that can only eat a <radical>part</radical> of a <radical>shellfish</radical> are probably a bit <kanji>poor</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1422', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%A7', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:23:58.628909Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.802871Z'),
(1423, 'kanji', '乏', '乏', 'If you look for a <radical>leaf</radical> among the <radical>hills</radical> you will notice they are <kanji>scarce</kanji>. The hills have no trees around them, so there are no leaves. The leaves are scarce here on the hills.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1423', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B9%8F', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:24:18.148935Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.812094Z'),
(1424, 'kanji', '適', '適', 'A <radical>scooter</radical> with a <radical>mohawk</radical>, now that''s the most <kanji>suitable</kanji> hairstyle you could ever think of for a scooter!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1424', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%A9', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:24:41.062394Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.813798Z'),
(1425, 'kanji', '預', '預', '<radical>Beforehand</radical>, to buy a <radical>geoduck</radical>, you have to go to the bank to <kanji>deposit</kanji> enough money. If you don''t deposit your money you won''t have enough in your account to buy one! They''re expensive, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1425', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A0%90', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:25:04.489537Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(1426, 'kanji', '延', '延', 'If you do <radical>yoga</radical> <radical>correct</radical>ly, you will not only <kanji>prolong</kanji> your body but your life!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1426', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BB%B6', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:25:31.693188Z', NULL, '2026-01-09T22:02:36.740599Z'),
(1427, 'kanji', '翌', '翌', 'Whenever all the <radical>feathers</radical> start <radical>stand</radical>ing, you know it''s time for <kanji>the next</kanji> day. Once the day comes to its end, all feathers start standing up like clockwork.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1427', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BF%8C', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:25:51.01329Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T07:36:54.056163Z'),
(1428, 'kanji', '覧', '覧', 'A <radical>servant</radical> holding a <radical>gun</radical> sits on the <radical>ground</radical> to <radical>see</radical> if he can find something to <kanji>look at</kanji>. The servant is on watch duty and has the gun to protect the perimeter. He patrols the grounds to look at the distance to see if there are any trespassers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1428', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A6%A7', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:26:12.92743Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:18:00.997657Z'),
(1429, 'kanji', '懐', '懐', 'A piece of your <radical>soul</radical> lives in a <radical>cross</radical> that you keep in a <radical>net</radical>-like pocket inside your <radical>clothing</radical> because of the nostalgia it brings you. Someone special gave you that cross, and you feel great <kanji>nostalgia</kanji> when you look at it. That''s why you keep it in your <kanji>bosom pocket</kanji>, close to your heart.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1429', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%87%90', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:26:39.242738Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:20:40.00989Z'),
(1430, 'kanji', '押', '押', 'Take your <radical>fingers</radical>, put them on this <radical>turtle shell</radical>, and <kanji>push</kanji> push push! Push the turtle right on out of there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1430', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8A%BC', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:27:07.247628Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.862577Z'),
(1433, 'kanji', '浮', '浮', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> knocks a <radical>cleat</radical> off a <radical>child</radical> and the shoe begins to <kanji>float</kanji> away in the water. This child was just out playing soccer, minding his own business, when the tsunami crashed in and knocked his cleats off his feet. Now the cleat is floating away with the tsunami.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1433', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B5%AE', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:28:30.800891Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:30:36.248889Z'),
(1434, 'kanji', '漏', '漏', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> rips a <radical>flag</radical> off your house in the <radical>rain</radical>, causing a <kanji>leak</kanji> where it was ripped out. The rain and the tsunami combined just tore the flag right off your house. The force was so strong that it actually removed part of the wall, and now you have a leak in your home.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1434', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BC%8F', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:29:04.93905Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.674512Z'),
(1435, 'kanji', '符', '符', 'You take some <radical>bamboo</radical> and <radical>attach</radical> it together into a <kanji>token</kanji> of appreciation for your friend. Your friend greatly appreciates this token of friendship.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1435', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AC%A6', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:29:27.782269Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.894255Z'),
(1436, 'kanji', '購', '購', 'You give a <radical>shellfish</radical> to a <radical>lifeguard</radical> to <kanji>purchase</kanji> their services. You think lifeguards are interested in normal money? Come on, they''re obviously out there using shellfish for their purchases. It''s only natural, given their seaside habitat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1436', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B3%BC', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:29:57.865567Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.895516Z'),
(1437, 'kanji', '越', '越', 'You <radical>run</radical> every day and soon you <radical>become</radical> a human that can <kanji>go beyond</kanji> the normal speed limits of man. You run and you run and you run. This training allows you to become super human. You go beyond the limits of a normal human. You become a human who can run really fast.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1437', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B6%8A', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:30:19.525504Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:38:57.03621Z'),
(1438, 'kanji', '飾', '飾', 'When you <radical>eat</radical> you want to see the shape of your <radical>gun</radical> from under a <radical>towel</radical>, so you <kanji>decorate</kanji> your house with them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1438', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A3%BE', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:30:45.385322Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.912124Z'),
(1439, 'kanji', '騒', '騒', 'A <radical>horse</radical> gets trapped in a small room with a <radical>stool</radical> and an <radical>insect</radical>, and together they create a very <kanji>boisterous</kanji> situation. The horse and the insect are both scrambling frantically to get out of the small room, knocking the stool around all over the place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1439', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A8%92', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:31:06.219169Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.924166Z'),
(1440, 'kanji', '背', '背', 'You fly <radical>north</radical> to the <radical>moon</radical> and break your <kanji>back</kanji> when you finally land on it. You weren’t braced for impact so when you hit the moon, your back took all of the impact.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1440', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%83%8C', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:31:25.659011Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T18:57:35.462499Z'),
(1441, 'kanji', '撮', '撮', 'Point some <radical>fingers</radical> at the <radical>sun</radical> and put some others in your <radical>ear</radical> as you sit on a <radical>stool</radical> — now you can have your <kanji>photograph</kanji> taken. This is a glamour <kanji>photo</kanji>, so you have to be in one of these weird, artsy poses for it to come out juuust right.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1441', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%92%AE', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:31:59.644907Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:24:39.730072Z'),
(1442, 'kanji', '盗', '盗', 'The <radical>next</radical> <radical>plate</radical> is the one you will <kanji>steal</kanji>. This one would be too obvious, better wait for the next one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1442', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9B%97', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:32:17.769049Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:18:34.194718Z'),
(1096, 'kanji', '妨', '妨', 'A <radical>woman</radical> with <radical>direction</radical> will have things <kanji>obstruct</kanji> her path. It''s a sad fact that it''s still a man''s world out there!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1096', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A6%A8', 31, 1, '2012-11-01T06:31:16.855154Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1443, 'kanji', '離', '離', 'The <radical>rocket</radical> <radical>turkey</radical> has several points which will <kanji>detach</kanji> during takeoff.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1443', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%A2', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:23:22.36177Z', NULL, '2026-01-05T23:19:07.724287Z'),
(1444, 'kanji', '融', '融', 'You see a <radical>bully</radical> take an <radical>insect</radical> and <kanji>dissolve</kanji> it in some acid. Bullies are messed up, mean people. They do awful things that normal kids and people wouldn''t do... like dissolve a living creature.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1444', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%9E%8D', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:23:53.611525Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.950357Z'),
(1445, 'kanji', '編', '編', 'Bring <radical>thread</radical> to the <radical>library</radical> to <kanji>knit</kanji>. Libraries are quiet and generally pretty empty nowadays, so it''s the perfect place to relax and do some knitting. Of course, if you find thread lying around in a library you know what to do now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1445', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B7%A8', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:24:19.535786Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:56.963095Z'),
(1446, 'kanji', '華', '華', '<radical>Flowers</radical> growing out of a <radical>rice paddy</radical> into the shape of a <radical>cross</radical> are really <kanji>showy</kanji> <kanji>flower</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1446', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8F%AF', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:24:48.490189Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T00:06:08.747649Z'),
(1447, 'kanji', '既', '既', 'There''s a <radical>root</radical> where your <radical>fang</radical> <kanji>previously</kanji> was. Your fang isn''t there anymore, you ripped it out.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1447', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%97%A2', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:25:10.71319Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:15:32.847277Z'),
(1448, 'kanji', '普', '普', 'You <radical>line up</radical> your body with the <radical>sun</radical> and everything looks <kanji>normal</kanji>.  The sun is lined up behind you, and you’re looking at all the things the sun has lit up. Everything looks normal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1448', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%99%AE', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:25:34.072135Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T09:51:44.660261Z'),
(1449, 'kanji', '豪', '豪', 'You put a <radical>lid</radical> on a dish you''re cooking, made up of the <radical>mouth</radical>s and <radical>forehead</radical>s of a <radical>pig</radical>. This is truly an <kanji>exceptional</kanji> dish you are cooking up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1449', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B1%AA', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:26:02.821879Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:04:30.375014Z'),
(1450, 'kanji', '鑑', '鑑', 'See that <radical>gold</radical> man <radical>oversee</radical>ing everyone? He''s a <kanji>model</kanji> human. His goldenness is a sign of how exemplary he is, and he''s overseeing all of us flawed, regular folks to try to help us get on his level.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1450', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%91%91', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:26:44.844877Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.005752Z'),
(1451, 'kanji', '除', '除', 'The <radical>building</radical>s are at a <radical>surplus</radical>. Because of that, we will need to <kanji>exclude</kanji> some of them from our future plans. Also, we''ll need to <kanji>remove</kanji> some buildings too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1451', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%A4', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:27:07.469888Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:55:29.955348Z'),
(1452, 'kanji', '尋', '尋', '<radical>Wolverine</radical> is doing a <radical>construction</radical> job and using his <radical>mouth</radical> to take <radical>measurement</radical>s, so he knows how many folks he needs to <kanji>inquire</kanji> with about helping him. Using the mouth is a strange way to take measurements, but that''s just how Wolverine does things. Once he measures it all out, he''ll know how many construction workers he needs to inquire with about working for him.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1452', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:27:43.514236Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:34:09.904189Z'),
(1453, 'kanji', '幾', '幾', 'You <radical>poop</radical> twice on a sleeping <radical>drunkard</radical> <radical>person</radical> and you find yourself wondering <kanji>how many</kanji> more times you can poop on them and wonder <kanji>how much</kanji> they can take before they realize what’s going on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1453', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B9%BE', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:28:11.067722Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.020285Z'),
(1454, 'kanji', '廊', '廊', 'The <radical>canopy</radical> <radical>guy</radical> is a guy who stands in the <kanji>corridor</kanji> trying to sell people canopies.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1454', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BB%8A', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:28:32.772084Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.023468Z'),
(1455, 'kanji', '掃', '掃', 'Wrap your <radical>fingers</radical> around this <radical>broom</radical>, because it''s time to <kanji>sweep</kanji>! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1455', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8E%83', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:29:02.25961Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:05:40.319625Z'),
(1456, 'kanji', '泥', '泥', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> came in and buried your <radical>flag</radical> so now you have to use a <radical>spoon</radical> and dig it out of the <kanji>mud</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1456', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B3%A5', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:29:26.0901Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.954932Z'),
(1457, 'kanji', '棒', '棒', 'A <radical>tree</radical> in <radical>spring</radical> that''s nice and <radical>dry</radical> will make a good <kanji>pole</kanji> or <kanji>rod</kanji>. Break it out of the ground and give it a swing to see what a nice, sturdy <kanji>stick</kanji> it is!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1457', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A3%92', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:29:59.106235Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:22:51.842327Z'),
(1458, 'kanji', '驚', '驚', 'If you can write an interesting <radical>poem</radical> about <radical>winter</radical> while riding on a <radical>horse</radical>, I’ll be pretty <kanji>surprised</kanji>. It’s hard enough to write a poem as it is, let alone an interesting one. And then you’re riding on a horse and the poem has to be about winter? Heck, I’d be surprised if you didn’t fall off the horse and injure yourself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1458', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A9%9A', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:30:24.939716Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:34:03.146247Z'),
(1459, 'kanji', '嘆', '嘆', 'You use your <radical>mouth</radical> when you see a <radical>Chinese</radical> person and you <kanji>sigh</kanji>. You sigh so loud that it’s practically a roar coming out of your mouth. Chinese people and Chinese things make you sigh because it reminds you of when you stayed in China and it brings up memories. The sigh is a sigh of nostalgia.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1459', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%98%86', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:30:43.982243Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:34:03.146247Z'),
(1460, 'kanji', '倉', '倉', 'The <radical>warehouse</radical> radical and the <kanji>warehouse</kanji> kanji are the same! You can call it a <kanji>storehouse</kanji> too. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1460', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%80%89', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:31:09.042986Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.066254Z'),
(1461, 'kanji', '孫', '孫', 'A <radical>child</radical> shares his <radical>lineage</radical> with a <kanji>grandchild</kanji>. The child passes his lineage down to his child and then that child’s child is the grandchild and between them all a lineage is shared.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1461', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AD%AB', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:32:23.168898Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.067653Z'),
(1462, 'kanji', '巣', '巣', 'Birds use <radical>grass</radical> and <radical>fruit</radical> to make their <kanji>nest</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1462', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B7%A3', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:33:02.642712Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:29:27.171851Z'),
(1463, 'kanji', '帯', '帯', 'There''s a <radical>mountain</radical> coming out of the <radical>ground</radical> that you want to climb, but your <radical>forehead</radical> needs a <radical>towel</radical> for all your sweat. You don''t have a towel, so you take off your <kanji>belt</kanji> and tie it around your forehead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1463', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B8%AF', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:33:35.906335Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.084742Z'),
(1464, 'kanji', '径', '径', 'You <radical>loiter</radical> around a <radical>tombstone</radical> to measure its <kanji>diameter</kanji>. You’re doing this to each tombstone, and if you loiter around long enough, you’ll soon know the diameter of every tombstone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1464', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%84', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:34:02.682573Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:12:57.389346Z'),
(1465, 'kanji', '救', '救', 'You <radical>request</radical> that <radical>winter</radical> <kanji>rescue</kanji> you from the warmth of the other seasons.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1465', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%95%91', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:34:34.351199Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:52:23.150339Z'),
(1466, 'kanji', '散', '散', 'If you play <radical>blackjack</radical> on the <radical>moon</radical> in the <radical>winter</radical>, your cards will <kanji>scatter</kanji> everywhere. The cards scatter because the gravity is so weak on the moon, so the blackjack game is prone to disaster.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1466', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%95%A3', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:35:03.391353Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.107408Z'),
(1467, 'kanji', '粉', '粉', 'If you grind <radical>rice</radical>, every <radical>part</radical> of it, eventually it will become a fine <kanji>powder</kanji>. You can then use this rice powder as a part to create some other dish. Some kind of cake, perhaps.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1467', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B2%89', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:35:22.404298Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.113418Z'),
(1468, 'kanji', '脈', '脈', 'Under the <radical>moon</radical> while you sit on a <radical>cliff</radical>, you inject a syringe of <radical>tofu</radical> right into your <kanji>vein</kanji>. Ahh, tofu. You''re addicted to it. You sneak out to the cliff at night to get your tofu fix, injecting it straight into your vein.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1468', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%84%88', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:35:47.26562Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T16:43:15.508035Z'),
(1469, 'kanji', '菜', '菜', 'You want some <radical>flowers</radical> so you take your <radical>cleat</radical> to the side of a <radical>tree</radical> hoping they''ll fall down, but you get a <kanji>vegetable</kanji> instead. What? You kick the tree again and another, different vegetable falls out.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1469', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8F%9C', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:36:14.153541Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:34:39.359876Z'),
(1470, 'kanji', '貨', '貨', 'You want to <radical>change</radical> your <radical>shellfish</radical> into <kanji>freight</kanji> so you can transport it. That means packing them up into giant freight containers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1470', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%A8', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:36:34.715874Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.130937Z'),
(1471, 'kanji', '陸', '陸', 'A <radical>building</radical> made from a <radical>womb</radical> must be placed upon the <kanji>land</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1471', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%B8', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:36:57.98519Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.133182Z'),
(1472, 'kanji', '似', '似', 'A <radical>leader</radical> of (odd-looking) <radical>lion</radical>s puts a <radical>drop</radical> of magic liquid on his face to look like a <radical>person</radical> who <kanji>resemble</kanji>s someone else. The leader of lions is going incognito, so he needs to resemble a person who is not himself. He takes this drop of powerful face paint and transforms.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1472', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BC%BC', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:37:23.589348Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.140849Z'),
(1473, 'kanji', '均', '均', 'You have some <radical>dirt</radical> next to a <radical>pool</radical> that has an extra <radical>drop</radical> of water in it because you need them to be <kanji>equal</kanji>. If there''s too much dirt outside the pool and not enough water inside, it''s gonna look weird. We can''t have that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1473', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9D%87', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:37:52.783863Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:49:14.754352Z'),
(1474, 'kanji', '墓', '墓', 'Inside your <radical>greenhouse</radical> there''s a mound of <radical>dirt</radical> which appears to be someone''s <kanji>grave</kanji>. When you dig into it, you discover that it''s an entire <kanji>tomb</kanji>, with doors and passages and everything! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1474', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A2%93', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:38:26.853673Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.154938Z'),
(1475, 'kanji', '富', '富', 'On your <radical>roof</radical> is a <radical>scarecrow</radical> because you''re <kanji>rich</kanji>. You want to scare away all the poor, non-rich people so you put a scarecrow on your roof.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1475', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%8C', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:39:17.303947Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.159568Z'),
(1476, 'kanji', '徳', '徳', 'You''re <radical>loiter</radical>ing, staring down at the <radical>cross</radical> that got caught in the <radical>net</radical> of your <radical>heart</radical>, because it''s trying to teach you to act with <kanji>virtue</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1476', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%B3', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:39:37.768185Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.189786Z'),
(1477, 'kanji', '探', '探', 'Put your <radical>fingers</radical> to your <radical>forehead</radical> and stand your <radical>legs</radical> high up in a <radical>tree</radical> if you want to <kanji>look for</kanji> something. After all, shielding your eyes and getting up high is the best way to look for something, isn’t it?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1477', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8E%A2', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:40:10.965758Z', NULL, '2026-01-17T04:17:24.443756Z'),
(1478, 'kanji', '偵', '偵', 'Your <radical>leader</radical> sticks his <radical>toe</radical> into a <radical>shellfish</radical>. You ask him why and he says he wants to <kanji>spy</kanji> on it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1478', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%81%B5', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:40:47.369463Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T06:06:52.42308Z'),
(496, 'kanji', '久', '久', 'If you use a <radical>slide</radical> to <radical>enter</radical> a building, you''ll be sliding for a <kanji>long time</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/496', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B9%85', 32, 1, '2012-03-01T02:18:50Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.239954Z'),
(1480, 'kanji', '序', '序', 'If the <radical>canopy</radical> comes <radical>beforehand</radical>, it''s a <kanji>preface</kanji> for the rest of the canopy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1480', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%8F', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:01:43.381792Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:58:34.235381Z'),
(1481, 'kanji', '迎', '迎', 'In its <radical>scooter</radical>, a <radical>cat pirate</radical> scoots up to <kanji>welcome</kanji> you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1481', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BF%8E', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:02:10.720509Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:46:54.074254Z'),
(1482, 'kanji', '志', '志', 'If you look inside a <radical>samurai</radical>''s <radical>heart</radical>, you''ll discover their true <kanji>intention</kanji>s and <kanji>ambition</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1482', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BF%97', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:02:33.931673Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.213197Z'),
(1483, 'kanji', '恩', '恩', 'If your <radical>mouth</radical> is <radical>big</radical> but your <radical>heart</radical> is in the right place, people will recognize your <kanji>kindness</kanji>. Even if you blabber on with your big mouth, as long as everyone knows that your heart is filled with kindness, they will love and accept you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1483', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%81%A9', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:03:01.682118Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T07:08:03.597715Z'),
(1484, 'kanji', '採', '採', 'Take your <radical>fingers</radical> to this <radical>cleat</radical> to dig out bits of <radical>tree</radical> and <kanji>gather</kanji> them. Taking your fingers to this cleat is the only way to gather the tree bits stuck in it. Gather them in a big pile… as evidence to examine later.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1484', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8E%A1', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:03:34.072335Z', NULL, '2026-01-17T04:17:24.44223Z'),
(1485, 'kanji', '桜', '桜', 'Under a <radical>tree</radical> sitting on the <radical>grass</radical> is a <radical>woman</radical> who loves <kanji>sakura</kanji>, or <kanji>cherry tree</kanji>, if you’re an English speaker. This woman really likes to just chill out in the grass under these cherry trees and watch the sakura blossoms. It’s very pretty.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1485', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A1%9C', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:04:16.400078Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1486, 'kanji', '永', '永', 'The <radical>eternity</radical> radical and the <kanji>eternity</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1486', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B0%B8', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:04:36.645629Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.259084Z'),
(1487, 'kanji', '液', '液', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> that hits at <radical>night</radical> leaves even more <kanji>fluid</kanji> than normal. It''s dark at night, so the sun isn''t there to help evaporate all that tsunami <kanji>liquid</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1487', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B6%B2', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:04:57.912031Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T07:00:37.99329Z'),
(1488, 'kanji', '眼', '眼', 'The <radical>eye</radical>''s <radical>root</radical> is the <kanji>eyeball</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1488', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9C%BC', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:05:58.161041Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:15:32.847277Z'),
(1489, 'kanji', '祖', '祖', 'You come across a <radical>spirit</radical> wearing a <radical>top hat</radical> and it claims to be your <kanji>ancestor</kanji>. Well of course, if you remember correctly, all of your ancestors wore top hats when they were alive. This spirit must be your long lost, great great... uh... somebody.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1489', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A5%96', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:06:45.946151Z', NULL, '2025-12-21T20:51:22.632515Z'),
(1490, 'kanji', '績', '績', 'Tie a <radical>thread</radical> to yourself whenever you take the <radical>blame</radical> for someone. This marks your <kanji>achievement</kanji>. Your achievement in blame-taking, I guess. Isn''t it a great achievement to selflessly shield others from blame? Of course it is! So these little blame-threads are symbols of your achievement.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1490', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B8%BE', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:07:12.599206Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.276124Z'),
(1491, 'kanji', '興', '興', 'There’s a <radical>comb</radical> and what looks like the <radical>same</radical> <radical>comb</radical>, and you found them on the <radical>ground</radical> next to some <radical>fins</radical> you showed <kanji>interest</kanji> in. At first, the shiny fins on the ground caught your interest, but once you got closer to the fins you also noticed a pair of combs that are interesting too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1491', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%88%88', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:07:47.529934Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.276958Z'),
(1492, 'kanji', '衛', '衛', 'If you want to <radical>go</radical> to <radical>Korea</radical> you''ll need a good <kanji>defense</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1492', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A1%9B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:26:44.180887Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.280308Z'),
(1493, 'kanji', '複', '複', 'If your <radical>outfit</radical> gets sucked into a <radical>black hole</radical>, you''ll need to get a <kanji>duplicate</kanji> version of the same outfit, ''cause you''re not getting the original back. Hopefully you had <kanji>multiple</kanji>s…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1493', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A4%87', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:27:18.281215Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:28:49.618198Z'),
(1494, 'kanji', '雑', '雑', 'You count <radical>nine</radical> of them in the <radical>tree</radical>, each <radical>turkey</radical> holding something <kanji>random</kanji> or <kanji>miscellaneous</kanji>. There are nine turkeys in the tree and they’re all holding random, miscellaneous things. How odd.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1494', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%91', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:27:46.011956Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(1495, 'kanji', '賛', '賛', 'A <radical>husband</radical> and his <radical>husband</radical> find a <radical>shellfish</radical> and <kanji>agree</kanji> to share it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1495', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B3%9B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:28:11.045262Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.31829Z'),
(1496, 'kanji', '酸', '酸', 'Take some <radical>alcohol</radical> and put a <radical>spider</radical> in it to make <kanji>acid</kanji>. Spiders have a little acid in them already, but if you want something really potent, smash one up in some whiskey to make a deadly acid that''ll rip through anything.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1496', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%85%B8', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:28:31.830777Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:48:42.652779Z'),
(1497, 'kanji', '銭', '銭', 'To use your <radical>gold</radical> at the <radical>bar</radical> you need to turn it into a <kanji>coin</kanji>. Well, more than one coin, probably. But you know for sure that you can make at least one coin out of the gold you have.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1497', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8A%AD', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:29:00.980687Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:43:09.675733Z'),
(1498, 'kanji', '飼', '飼', 'You <radical>eat</radical> part of a <radical>director</radical> to <kanji>domesticate</kanji> him. This director has been going around kicking cameramen and punching actors and it’s really ruining his reputation. You need to domesticate him. The only way to do this is to shock him into submission. His ego is so big from two blockbusters that you have to eat part of him to get him to calm down.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1498', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A3%BC', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:29:29.74641Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:51:58.78898Z'),
(1499, 'kanji', '傷', '傷', 'If a <radical>leader</radical> has a <radical>gun</radical> it''s <radical>easy</radical> to <kanji>wound</kanji> people. When you have a gun and a lot of responsibility it''s easy to get carried away and mess up, wounding the people you were supposed to be protecting. You can even wound yourself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1499', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%82%B7', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:30:00.593757Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.333979Z'),
(1500, 'kanji', '党', '党', 'A <radical>Viking</radical> and his <radical>older brother</radical> join a <kanji>party</kanji>. They can raid and pillage better in a <kanji>group</kanji> like this, so it makes sense that the Viking and his older brother would join a group with other Vikings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1500', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%9A', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:30:25.379017Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:24:00.678512Z'),
(1501, 'kanji', '卵', '卵', 'Two <radical>pool</radical>s, both altered and not quite like a pool you’ve seen before are put together and flipped around – this is an <kanji>egg</kanji>. One pool is the egg white, the other pool is the egg yolk. They’re different, but similar.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1501', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%B5', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:30:51.402293Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:49:14.754352Z'),
(1502, 'kanji', '厳', '厳', 'If you see a <radical>grass</radical>y <radical>cliff</radical> with an <radical>elf</radical> under it in <radical>winter</radical>, it''s because their parents are so <kanji>strict</kanji>. When elf kids misbehave they''re supposed to go stand under grassy cliffs to think about what they''ve done.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1502', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8E%B3', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:31:39.292756Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:18:01.113317Z'),
(1503, 'kanji', '捨', '捨', 'Your <radical>fingers</radical> have nothing to do when you''re at your <radical>cottage</radical> except <kanji>throw away</kanji> things. Your cottage is for vacation. And vacation means being as lazy as possible. But you certainly don''t want to live in filth, so you have to throw away your trash at least.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1503', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8D%A8', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:32:26.394224Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:50:00.725753Z'),
(1504, 'kanji', '込', '込', 'If you get on your <radical>scooter</radical> and <radical>enter</radical> somewhere, you’re going <kanji>into</kanji> that place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1504', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BE%BC', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:32:46.943177Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:29:35.906145Z'),
(1505, 'kanji', '密', '密', 'Standing on your <radical>roof</radical>, you''re <radical>certain</radical> that the nearby <radical>mountain</radical> is shrouded in <kanji>secrecy</kanji>. But you can''t do much about it, stuck on your roof as you are. So much secrecy, but so far away.

', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1505', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%86', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:33:30.80613Z', NULL, '2025-11-10T19:48:27.455148Z'),
(1506, 'kanji', '汚', '汚', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> hit not one, but <radical>TWO</radical> <radical>prison</radical>s, making them extremely <kanji>dirty</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1506', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B1%9A', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:34:02.555604Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T16:31:29.531428Z'),
(1507, 'kanji', '欲', '欲', 'In the <radical>valley</radical> you feel a <radical>lack</radical> of <kanji>want</kanji>. You want for nothing in this valley. It is the valley of plenty.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1507', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AC%B2', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:34:34.06852Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.397896Z'),
(1508, 'kanji', '暖', '暖', 'You''re in the <radical>sun</radical> playing <radical>football</radical> and you get very <kanji>warm</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1508', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9A%96', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:35:01.401889Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.403213Z'),
(1509, 'kanji', '机', '机', 'A <radical>tree</radical> combines with a <radical>table</radical> to make a <kanji>desk</kanji>. When you think about it, a desk is just a table with more stuff. To make this more stuff, like drawers and such, you need more wood, and wood comes from trees. Add materials from the tree to the table and you have a desk.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1509', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%BA', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:35:23.897014Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.412577Z'),
(1510, 'kanji', '秘', '秘', 'A <radical>grain</radical> is <radical>certain</radical> to keep a <kanji>secret</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1510', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A7%98', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:35:44.653361Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.427904Z'),
(1511, 'kanji', '訳', '訳', 'They <radical>say</radical> a <radical>shrimp</radical> who is bad with <kanji>translation</kanji> has no <kanji>reason</kanji> to live. Most shrimp make a living with their translation skills. They are the translators of the ocean. If they don’t know how to say something in all the languages of the sea, they have no reason to live. They might as well be dead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1511', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%B3', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:36:04.990717Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:39:53.26852Z'),
(1512, 'kanji', '染', '染', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> runs into <radical>nine</radical> <radical>tree</radical>s and produces a <kanji>dye</kanji> the color of the forest. The tsunami water mixes with each tree, nine of them actually, and the resulting liquid is a forest green dye.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1512', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9F%93', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T20:36:45.946193Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.441713Z'),
(1513, 'kanji', '簡', '簡', 'You line up stalks of <radical>bamboo</radical> with an equal <radical>interval</radical> between each one. This pattern creates a sense of <kanji>simplicity</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1513', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B0%A1', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:02:54.049667Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.448095Z'),
(1515, 'kanji', '誌', '誌', 'To <radical>say</radical> what''s in a <radical>samurai</radical>''s <radical>heart</radical> you start a <kanji>magazine</kanji>. You go around writing about and interviewing all the samurai so you can share what they say to everyone who wants to read!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1515', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AA%8C', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:04:01.663595Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.462873Z'),
(1516, 'kanji', '窓', '窓', 'The <radical>hole</radical> leading to the <radical>private</radical> <radical>heart</radical> of your house is the <kanji>window</kanji>. Or maybe it''s just the window to your soul, and not your house. Like the poetry people say.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1516', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AA%93', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:04:27.38465Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.462354Z'),
(1517, 'kanji', '否', '否', 'The <radical>Not</radical> <radical>Mouth</radical> says “<kanji>no</kanji>”. What else would you expect from something named the “Not Mouth”? The Not Mouth will always say “no”, regardless of the question.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1517', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%A6', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:04:52.564283Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.470278Z'),
(1518, 'kanji', '筋', '筋', 'This <radical>bamboo</radical> absorbed the <radical>moon</radical>''s <radical>power</radical>, and you want that power in your body, so you surgically insert them into your body to replace your <kanji>muscle</kanji>s and <kanji>tendon</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1518', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AD%8B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:05:22.386526Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.475538Z'),
(1521, 'kanji', '宣', '宣', 'Someone climbs onto the <radical>roof</radical> from the <radical>ground</radical> when <radical>dawn</radical> comes to <kanji>proclaim</kanji> that a new day has begun! They proclaim this at the top of their lungs!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1521', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%A3', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:06:51.08082Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T04:33:10.92364Z'),
(1522, 'kanji', '尊', '尊', 'The <radical>horns</radical> of the creatures who drink <radical>alcohol</radical> have a <radical>measurement</radical> so large that they are <kanji>revered</kanji> the world over. The horns are revered both for their size and potency. The horns can be used in medicine, so they are very valuable, those alcoholic monster horns.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1522', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%8A', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:07:17.36456Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:52:39.90813Z'),
(1523, 'kanji', '忠', '忠', 'Those that are in the <radical>middle</radical> of your <radical>heart</radical> are the ones you have the most <kanji>loyalty</kanji> towards. The middle of your heart is the most sacred, special place for people, so it makes sense that those you keep in the middle of your heart are those you are most loyal to.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1523', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BF%A0', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:07:42.253675Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.522846Z'),
(1524, 'kanji', '拡', '拡', 'To make your <radical>fingers</radical> <radical>wide</radical> you <kanji>extend</kanji> them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1524', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8B%A1', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:08:13.328377Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.529102Z'),
(1525, 'kanji', '操', '操', 'If someone has their <radical>fingers</radical> in your <radical>syrup</radical>, it''s just another way of saying they <kanji>manipulate</kanji>d you. Cause only slimy people manipulate other people, so their fingers are slimy and sticky because they''re covered in your syrup from mucking up your life.

This also has another meaning: <kanji>chastity</kanji>. Just imagine if someone wanted you to keep your chastity and they called s.e.x. when someone "puts their fingers in your syrup." That would be pretty effective, right? Cause it''s like, super gross.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1525', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%93%8D', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:08:55.793641Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.533848Z'),
(1526, 'kanji', '敬', '敬', 'Writing a <radical>poem</radical> about <radical>winter</radical> is the only way to get <kanji>respect</kanji> from other poets.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1526', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%95%AC', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:10:06.179435Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.535084Z'),
(1527, 'kanji', '暮', '暮', 'A <radical>greenhouse</radical> is already really good when it comes to a plant''s livelihood. But add the <radical>sun</radical> into that equation and you have something really great for the plants. Their <kanji>livelihood</kanji> will be awesome, now!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1527', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9A%AE', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:10:40.767824Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T07:07:24.367373Z'),
(1528, 'kanji', '灰', '灰', 'On a <radical>cliff</radical>, a <radical>fire</radical> burns to <kanji>ashes</kanji>. The ashes then blow off the cliff, and follow the wind wherever it may go.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1528', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%81%B0', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:11:06.899028Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.969822Z'),
(1529, 'kanji', '熟', '熟', 'If you <radical>receive</radical> something edible, arrange it in a <radical>circle</radical> and <radical>boil</radical> it. That way, it’ll <kanji>ripen</kanji> more quickly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1529', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%86%9F', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:13:11.320277Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:55:24.656516Z'),
(1530, 'kanji', '異', '異', 'Put a <radical>rice paddy</radical> <radical>together</radical> with other paddies and you''ll see how much they <kanji>differ</kanji>. When you''re driving or taking a train through the countryside it may seem like all of those rice paddies blurring together are all the same, but they can differ quite a bit!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1530', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%95%B0', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:13:32.67298Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:59:46.609728Z'),
(1531, 'kanji', '皇', '皇', 'Social commentary aside, the <radical>white</radical> <radical>king</radical> is the king that wears all white. Traditionally, this is someone better than a king. The <kanji>emperor</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1531', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9A%87', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:14:05.41437Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T02:32:26.949053Z'),
(1532, 'kanji', '盛', '盛', 'If you <radical>become</radical> a <radical>plate</radical>, I will <kanji>pile</kanji> food up upon you. With such a huge pile, everyone will think you''re <kanji>prosperous</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1532', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9B%9B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:14:32.027328Z', NULL, '2025-10-13T22:29:21.436821Z'),
(1533, 'kanji', '砂', '砂', 'If you grind a <radical>stone</radical> down to more than just a <radical>few</radical> pieces, it will become <kanji>sand</kanji>. Stones that get broken up enough will become sand, and you pick up a few grains in your hand.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1533', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A0%82', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:14:56.498014Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:11:59.650433Z'),
(1535, 'kanji', '糖', '糖', 'Bring some <radical>rice</radical> under a <radical>canopy</radical> and stuff it in <radical>Wolverine</radical>''s <radical>mouth</radical>. Make him chew it a bit, scrape it out, and voila — you''ve made <kanji>sugar</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1535', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B3%96', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:15:58.701587Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T04:27:14.22289Z'),
(1536, 'kanji', '納', '納', 'The <radical>thread</radical> you have <radical>inside</radical> is your whole <kanji>supply</kanji>. When you''re outside sewing and you run out, you just go inside, look through your supply, and pick a new one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1536', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%8D', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:16:49.378679Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.61943Z'),
(1537, 'kanji', '肺', '肺', 'There is a <radical>moon</radical> and a <radical>city</radical> inside your <kanji>lung</kanji>. Inside of your lung there is a city and above that city lies a moon. It’s a pretty sight, but also very strange. Moons and cities don’t usually reside inside lungs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1537', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%82%BA', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:17:19.730415Z', NULL, '2025-10-02T17:25:49.560494Z'),
(1538, 'kanji', '著', '著', 'If you want to give <radical>flowers</radical> to <radical>someone</radical>, give them to an <kanji>author</kanji>. Authors really love flowers, because they help them think of flowery language they can use to sound smart in their books.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1538', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%91%97', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:17:40.678296Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:24:21.947579Z'),
(1539, 'kanji', '蒸', '蒸', 'If you take the <radical>flowers</radical> off some <radical>coral</radical> and <radical>boil</radical> them, <kanji>steam</kanji> comes out.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1539', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%92%B8', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:18:10.013117Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.64764Z'),
(1540, 'kanji', '蔵', '蔵', 'The <radical>storehouse</radical> radical and the <kanji>storehouse</kanji> kanji meanings are the same. If you know the radical, you know the kanji!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1540', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%94%B5', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:18:33.078344Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.663779Z'),
(1541, 'kanji', '装', '装', 'If you see <radical>icicle</radical>s hanging off of a <radical>samurai</radical>''s <radical>clothes</radical>, don''t worry, those are just part of his winter <kanji>attire</kanji>. Samurai have to travel and fight in all sorts of conditions, so it only makes sense that they have attire for every season.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1541', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A3%85', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:18:59.37595Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.665295Z'),
(1542, 'kanji', '裏', '裏', 'There''s a <radical>lid</radical> on your <radical>village</radical>, so you <radical>kick</radical> it over on its <kanji>backside</kanji> to see what''s on its <kanji>underside</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1542', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A3%8F', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:19:27.76913Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:10:27.831304Z'),
(1543, 'kanji', '諸', '諸', 'They <radical>say</radical> <radical>someone</radical> with <kanji>various</kanji> talents is important. Various talents can include speaking various languages so if you are someone who knows various languages you can say things that various peoples will comprehend.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1543', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AB%B8', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:19:54.472411Z', NULL, '2026-02-16T21:11:03.85885Z'),
(1544, 'kanji', '賃', '賃', 'The <radical>leader</radical> who is <radical>king</radical> of every <radical>shellfish</radical> sets the <kanji>rent</kanji> for everyone. If you rent a home in King Shellfish''s kingdom, the rent will be set by the great shellfish leader himself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1544', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B3%83', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:20:22.329958Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:50:26.709007Z'),
(1545, 'kanji', '誤', '誤', 'They <radical>say</radical> accidentally firing a <radical>catapult</radical> is an unforgivable <kanji>mistake</kanji>. They say you shot the catapult at your own castle. Even though it was an accident, it’s still a mistake you will never be forgiven for.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1545', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AA%A4', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T17:54:36.503799Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.752496Z'),
(1546, 'kanji', '臓', '臓', 'Your <radical>moon</radical> <radical>storehouse</radical> is another storehouse you own. Remember how your regular storehouse holds zombies (from the kanji 蔵?). Your moon storehouse is on the moon, and it is filled with <kanji>internal organs</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1546', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%87%93', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T17:55:02.967949Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.754026Z'),
(1547, 'kanji', '貴', '貴', 'The <radical>valuable</radical> radical and the <kanji>valuable</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1547', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%B4', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T17:55:38.861254Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:33:29.32274Z'),
(1550, 'kanji', '吐', '吐', 'If your <radical>mouth</radical> has <radical>dirt</radical> in it, you''re going to <kanji>throw up</kanji>. That''s just a fact.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1550', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%90', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T17:57:31.881012Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:17:12.895851Z'),
(1551, 'kanji', '奴', '奴', 'The <radical>woman</radical> sitting on that <radical>stool</radical> is actually a <kanji>dude</kanji>. Oh oops! You thought he was a woman because of his long hair and the way he was sitting. Maybe you shouldn''t be judging people based on their looks!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1551', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A5%B4', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T17:57:58.041853Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1552, 'kanji', '隷', '隷', 'A <radical>samurai</radical> handling a <radical>jackhammer</radical>, cleaning <radical>brush</radical>es (in their 肀 form), and carrying <radical>water</radical> for someone?! All that should tell you that he''s not just working, he''s been made into a <kanji>slave</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1552', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9A%B7', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T17:58:40.78687Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.807935Z'),
(1554, 'kanji', '縮', '縮', 'A magic <radical>thread</radical> fell from the <radical>roof</radical> onto the <radical>leader</radical> of a <radical>hundred</radical> people, causing him to <kanji>shrink</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1554', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B8%AE', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T17:59:50.046802Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.828427Z'),
(1555, 'kanji', '純', '純', 'If it''s made of <radical>thread</radical>, your <radical>barracks</radical> will be <kanji>pure</kanji>. If you build it out of clay or bricks or wood, it will inevitably have some dirt in it. Build it out of thread to keep it pure of... well, impurities.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1555', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%94', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:00:26.167937Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:22:41.220533Z'),
(1556, 'kanji', '縦', '縦', 'You see a <radical>thread</radical> that wants to <radical>loiter</radical> from some <radical>horns</radical> and you would be <radical>correct</radical> to assume that it would hang <kanji>vertical</kanji>. The thread wants to loiter and hang from one of the horns. When things hang from things, they usually hang vertical, so that’s what this thread is going to do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1556', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B8%A6', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:01:16.574847Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:17:19.072082Z'),
(1557, 'kanji', '粋', '粋', 'There''s some <radical>rice</radical> in the shape of a <radical>nine</radical> on a <radical>cross</radical> and this is <kanji>stylish</kanji>. The rice grains are arranged to form the number nine. This number nine is prominently displayed on the cross, and it''s stylish to carry it around and be seen with it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1557', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B2%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:01:52.367057Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T23:57:06.920015Z'),
(1558, 'kanji', '聖', '聖', 'The <radical>ear</radical> and the <radical>mouth</radical> of a <radical>king</radical> are <kanji>holy</kanji>. The king has holy hearing and holy eating abilities. This king is divine.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1558', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%81%96', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:02:19.556853Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.848396Z'),
(1559, 'kanji', '磁', '磁', 'Seeing a <radical>stone</radical> and some <radical>horns</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical> next to a piece of <radical>poop</radical> and another piece of <radical>poop</radical>, you try and pick each of them up with a <kanji>magnet</kanji>. The magnet fails to pick up either piece of poop. The magnet doesn’t pick up the horns either. But wait! The magnet picks up the stone!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1559', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A3%81', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:03:01.957078Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.857588Z'),
(1560, 'kanji', '紅', '紅', 'You use <radical>thread</radical> in your <radical>construction</radical> project to hold everything together. The color of the thread is a <kanji>deep red</kanji>, and it gives everything a <kanji>crimson</kanji> look.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1560', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%85', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:03:56.329486Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:15:45.961063Z'),
(1561, 'kanji', '射', '射', 'You need to take a <radical>body</radical> <radical>measurement</radical> before you can <kanji>shoot</kanji>. Whether it''s for a photo shoot, you''re gonna shoot them with a gun, or if you''re about to shoot them full of radiation for X-rays, you need the right measurements of that body first.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1561', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%84', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:04:15.958321Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:25:41.497205Z'),
(1562, 'kanji', '幕', '幕', 'In a <radical>greenhouse</radical>, a <radical>towel</radical> becomes a <kanji>curtain</kanji> as soon as you hang it. Greenhouses are made of windows, it''s all glass, there are no walls. So when you put a towel in there it turns into a curtain. It has no choice but to cover a window, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1562', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B9%95', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:06:40.205331Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:25:18.015813Z'),
(1563, 'kanji', '拝', '拝', 'Take your <radical>fingers</radical> and <radical>dry</radical> them. Then <radical>dry</radical> them again. This is what you do when you are in  <kanji>worship</kanji>. Once you go into your place of worship, and again before you leave. You have really sweaty fingers, and you can''t worship anyone or anything if you have wet hands. So you dry them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1563', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8B%9D', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:07:27.379502Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.886862Z'),
(1564, 'kanji', '薦', '薦', 'You want some <radical>flowers</radical> to put on the <radical>canopy</radical> above your <radical>helicopter</radical> to keep the <radical>bird</radical>s away, so you ask people what they would <kanji>recommend</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1564', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%96%A6', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:08:01.434305Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.892433Z'),
(1565, 'kanji', '推', '推', 'If the <radical>fingers</radical> on a <radical>turkey</radical> are pointing right at you, you can <kanji>infer</kanji> that it wants revenge. This turkey knows you killed his brother for dinner last night, and now it''s going to make you pay. You infer that this turkey is going to take its fingers and wrap them around your neck.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1565', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8E%A8', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:08:26.635679Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:26:11.321949Z'),
(1566, 'kanji', '揮', '揮', 'Take your <radical>fingers</radical>, step onto the <radical>forehead</radical> of your <radical>car</radical>, and <kanji>brandish</kanji> them in the air. Wave them around for everyone to see.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1566', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8F%AE', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:09:02.981099Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:27:49.274325Z'),
(1567, 'kanji', '沿', '沿', 'There''s a <radical>tsunami</radical> coming, so you put your <radical>fins</radical> on, open your <radical>mouth</radical> wide, and try to <kanji>run alongside</kanji> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1567', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B2%BF', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:09:39.436206Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T23:02:03.261671Z'),
(1568, 'kanji', '源', '源', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> was the <radical>original</radical> event to mark the <kanji>origin</kanji> of Japan. A big tsunami came and uncovered the islands of Japan. It''s the origin of all life, as far as Japanese people are concerned.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1568', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BA%90', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:10:00.628061Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.926623Z'),
(1570, 'kanji', '勤', '勤', '<radical>Boot</radical>s give you the <radical>power</radical> to <kanji>work</kanji>! That''s why so many professions require you to have work boots.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1570', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8B%A4', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:10:53.759412Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T07:49:51.415608Z'),
(1571, 'kanji', '歓', '歓', 'An <radical>arrow</radical> is what this <radical>turkey</radical> <radical>lack</radical>s. For a turkey, that is a <kanji>delight</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1571', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AD%93', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:11:48.287472Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(1572, 'kanji', '承', '承', 'A <radical>child</radical> will ask if they want <radical>two</radical> <radical>water</radical>s because they need a grown-up''s <kanji>consent</kanji>. Children aren''t allowed to have whatever they want, they need to make sure it''s okay and get consent first.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1572', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%89%BF', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:12:13.861234Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.937351Z'),
(1573, 'kanji', '損', '損', 'Raise your <radical>fingers</radical> to another <radical>member</radical> and you''ll receive a <kanji>loss</kanji> in return. You should never <kanji>hurt</kanji> a member of your team, group, or society. It''s just wrong. And you''ll incur a loss as punishment for your bad deeds.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1573', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%90%8D', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:12:48.220315Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.944758Z'),
(1574, 'kanji', '枝', '枝', 'A <radical>tree</radical> <radical>branch</radical> is exactly what it sounds like: the <kanji>branch</kanji> of a tree.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1574', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9E%9D', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:13:14.803242Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.949607Z'),
(1576, 'kanji', '豆', '豆', 'The <radical>beans</radical> radical and the <kanji>beans</kanji> kanji are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1576', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B1%86', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:14:05.380654Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:54:38.942095Z'),
(1577, 'kanji', '刻', '刻', 'A <radical>slice</radical> with a <radical>knife</radical> is called a <kanji>carve</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1577', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%BB', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:14:25.720158Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:02:24.849884Z'),
(1578, 'kanji', '腐', '腐', 'Set up your <radical>canopy</radical>, <radical>attach</radical> some <radical>meat</radical> to it, and the meat will eventually <kanji>rot</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1578', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%85%90', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:14:52.169151Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:33:00.296172Z'),
(1534, 'kanji', '漠', '漠', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> hits the <radical>greenhouse</radical>, destroying the last remaining plant life in the area. The tsunami recedes, and there is nothing but sand, and the lands become a <kanji>desert</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1534', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BC%A0', 35, 1, '2013-03-18T17:15:29.675419Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.595349Z'),
(1579, 'kanji', '遅', '遅', 'Next to the <radical>scooter</radical>, there''s a <radical>flag</radical> warning you that there are <radical>sheep</radical> around here, so you should go <kanji>slow</kanji>. That means you might be <kanji>late</kanji>, but at least the sheep will be okay!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1579', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%85', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:46:16.797898Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.984213Z'),
(1580, 'kanji', '彫', '彫', 'There is a <radical>circumference</radical> around someone''s <radical>hair</radical>, as in, you can see a circle drawn around the top of their head. As you examine it closer, and pull at it a bit, you realize that someone has <kanji>carve</kanji>d the hair off someone''s head!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1580', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BD%AB', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:47:19.485996Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:57.989807Z'),
(1581, 'kanji', '測', '測', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> will pick up a bunch of <radical>shellfish</radical> and dump them on your shore, so you take a <radical>knife</radical> and <kanji>measure</kanji> them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1581', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B8%AC', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:47:57.3146Z', NULL, '2025-11-20T02:24:43.615321Z'),
(1582, 'kanji', '破', '破', 'If you scrape a sharp <radical>stone</radical> against your <radical>skin</radical>, it will <kanji>tear</kanji>. The sharp stone tears your skin. It hurts!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1582', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A0%B4', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:48:30.299077Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:17:49.793425Z'),
(1583, 'kanji', '舎', '舎', 'The kanji and the radical that look like this are exactly the same. They are both <kanji>cottage</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1583', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%88%8E', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:48:57.306811Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:27:51.741148Z'),
(1584, 'kanji', '講', '講', 'Try to <radical>say</radical> something to a <radical>lifeguard</radical> and you''ll just end up getting a <kanji>lecture</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1584', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AC%9B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:49:18.721846Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.019503Z'),
(1585, 'kanji', '滞', '滞', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical>, once stopped by a <radical>belt</radical>, will begin to <kanji>stagnate</kanji>. This belt is a belt of water resistance, so the belt stops the tsunami. The belt has caused the tsunami to stagnate.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1585', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BB%9E', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:49:44.190985Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.032517Z'),
(1586, 'kanji', '紹', '紹', 'You create a <radical>thread</radical> with someone by <radical>call</radical>ing out to them to <kanji>introduce</kanji> yourself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1586', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%B9', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:50:04.72204Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.033146Z'),
(1587, 'kanji', '介', '介', 'The <radical>jammed in</radical> radical and the <kanji>jammed in</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1587', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:50:28.303354Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:27:04.174252Z'),
(1588, 'kanji', '己', '己', 'The kanji and the radical are the same, so remembering the meaning of this kanji is as simple as making sure you know the radical for <radical>oneself</radical> first! If you know the radical, you know this kanji means <kanji>oneself</kanji> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1588', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B7%B1', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:50:49.294202Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.053768Z'),
(1589, 'kanji', '厄', '厄', 'If you''re on the edge of a <radical>cliff</radical> with a <radical>snake</radical>, that''s a <kanji>misfortune</kanji>. You''re hanging off the edge of this cliff and you can''t pull yourself back up. Then you notice a snake approaching your fingers, looking like it''s gonna bite you. What a misfortune!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1589', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8E%84', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:51:24.736488Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T16:26:38.373776Z'),
(1590, 'kanji', '亀', '亀', 'The <radical>turtle</radical> radical and the <kanji>turtle</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Nice!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1590', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%80', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:52:45.24613Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.068968Z'),
(1591, 'kanji', '互', '互', 'The <radical>ground</radical> and a <radical>shuriken</radical> have <kanji>mutual</kanji> respect for one another. The shuriken respects the ground because of how solid and stable it is, and the ground respects the shuriken for how pointy it is. They have great mutual respect here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1591', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%92', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:53:19.316797Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:31:54.877858Z'),
(1592, 'kanji', '剣', '剣', 'Tie a <radical>squid</radical> to a <radical>knife</radical> and you get a <kanji>sword</kanji>! Knives are too small to be considered a sword on their own. That''s why you strapped a squid to it. They''re the right shape, kinda pointed, so no one will know it''s just a knife.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1592', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%89%A3', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:53:47.451032Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.084299Z'),
(1595, 'kanji', '恥', '恥', 'Your <radical>ear</radical> listens to your <radical>heart</radical> and feels <kanji>shame</kanji>. Your heart is full of shame for how you’ve lived your life and when your ear hears all the things you’ve done, your ear feels shame too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1595', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%81%A5', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:55:18.261335Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:08:11.543511Z'),
(1596, 'kanji', '杉', '杉', 'There’s a <radical>tree</radical> in your <radical>hair</radical>, I think it’s a <kanji>cedar</kanji>. How did that cedar tree get in your hair?

(Certain types of cedar trees have bark that comes off in fine threads, making them very hairy trees. So this kanji makes perfect sense!)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1596', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9D%89', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:55:49.739804Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.10652Z'),
(1597, 'kanji', '汁', '汁', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> carries a <radical>cross</radical> into your <kanji>soup</kanji>. Hey tsunami, I was eating that soup!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1597', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B1%81', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:56:13.718656Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.114065Z'),
(1599, 'kanji', '炎', '炎', '<radical>Fire</radical>, <radical>fire</radical>, <kanji>flame</kanji>! There’s flames everywhere!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1599', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%82%8E', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:57:14.746616Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.129576Z'),
(1600, 'kanji', '為', '為', 'The <radical>sake</radical> radical and the <kanji>sake</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Sweet.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1600', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%82%BA', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:57:43.114084Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:35:24.264245Z'),
(1601, 'kanji', '熊', '熊', 'The <radical>ability</radical> to <radical>boil</radical> is one of the main abilities of a <kanji>bear</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1601', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%86%8A', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:58:10.941283Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T05:58:29.315371Z'),
(1602, 'kanji', '獄', '獄', 'You have an <radical>animal</radical> and you <radical>say</radical> it’s a <radical>dog</radical> to prevent it from going to <kanji>prison</kanji>. Dogs are the only animals that don’t have to go to prison. Your animal is not a dog, but you say it is to prevent it from going to prison.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1602', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8D%84', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:58:36.688129Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:51:15.083936Z'),
(1603, 'kanji', '酔', '酔', 'You have an <radical>alcohol</radical> <radical>nine</radical> times stronger than any other drink, in a bottle shaped like a <radical>cross</radical>, and you drink it all and become <kanji>drunk</kanji>. You''re like, so drunk right now from all that alcohol. Nine times more drunk than ever before.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1603', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%85%94', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:59:27.821973Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:52:39.90813Z'),
(1604, 'kanji', '酢', '酢', 'To make <radical>alcohol</radical> the <radical>key</radical> is to ferment it. But, if you ferment it a certain way, it will become <kanji>vinegar</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1604', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%85%A2', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:01:53.258813Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:52:39.90813Z'),
(1606, 'kanji', '湖', '湖', 'The <radical>tsunami</radical>s during the <radical>old</radical> <radical>moon</radical>s (many moons ago) were responsible for the world''s <kanji>lake</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1606', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B9%96', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:02:54.442022Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:13:37.694956Z'),
(1607, 'kanji', '銅', '銅', '<radical>Gold</radical> is not the <radical>same</radical> as <kanji>copper</kanji>. They are two different metals. Gold is not the same as copper.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1607', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8A%85', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:03:31.685121Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.172261Z'),
(1609, 'kanji', '油', '油', 'This <radical>tsunami</radical> is the <radical>reason</radical> we have so much <kanji>oil</kanji> now. The tsunami moved a bunch of earth out of the way, so now it’s really easy to dig for oil. Thanks tsunami!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1609', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B2%B9', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:04:35.245486Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:39:43.02559Z'),
(1897, 'kanji', '涙', '涙', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> goes through a little <radical>door</radical>, and plops down a <radical>big</radical> drop on your face. This tsunami is your tears inside your eye area. The door is your tear duct. The big drop is the <kanji>teardrop</kanji> coming down your eye.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1897', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B6%99', 35, 1, '2013-07-02T22:19:27.976079Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T22:54:40.944858Z'),
(2133, 'kanji', '軒', '軒', 'Your <radical>car</radical> is <radical>dry</radical> because you kept it under the <kanji>eaves</kanji> of your house. 

This kanji has a second meaning too: <kanji>house counter</kanji>. Imagine counting all the houses on your block by the number of eaves you see keeping cars dry.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2133', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BB%92', 35, 1, '2015-06-26T23:44:45.231307Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:04:54.519402Z'),
(1611, 'kanji', '旧', '旧', 'There’s a <radical>stick</radical> next to the <radical>sun</radical>, reflecting on its <kanji>former</kanji> glory. The stick used to be a great tree, and the stick thinks that if it stays by the sun long enough it might return to its former form as a towering tree.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1611', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%97%A7', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:33:44.665326Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.194049Z'),
(1612, 'kanji', '姓', '姓', 'If there''s a special <radical>woman</radical> in your <radical>life</radical>, she might eventually take your <kanji>family name</kanji> and make it her <kanji>surname</kanji> too. In Japan and other countries, tradition dictates that women change their last name when they get married and keep that name for the rest of their life. Their partner’s family name becomes their family name too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1612', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A7%93', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:34:15.52313Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1613, 'kanji', '貿', '貿', 'You send your <radical>private</radical> <radical>sword</radical> man out with your <radical>shellfish</radical> so he can <kanji>trade</kanji> them. Shellfish were used as currency back in the olden days. And you aren''t going to go out trading and dirty your own hands, no no. Trading your shellfish is the job for the private sword you hired!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1613', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%BF', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:35:07.680028Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.205117Z'),
(1614, 'kanji', '将', '将', 'The <radical>commander</radical> radical and the <kanji>commander</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1614', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%86', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:35:41.892537Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.208169Z'),
(1615, 'kanji', '盟', '盟', 'I’ll give you this <radical>bright</radical> <radical>plate</radical> if you agree to an <kanji>alliance</kanji> with me. I know you like things that are bright, and I know you like plates too. Therefore, this bright plate should be more than enough to convince you to enter into an alliance with me.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1615', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9B%9F', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:36:08.774889Z', NULL, '2025-10-29T11:43:10.3513Z'),
(1616, 'kanji', '遺', '遺', 'When you go on a <radical>scooter</radical>, anything <radical>valuable</radical>, you need to <kanji>leave behind</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1616', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%BA', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:36:34.664836Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T06:27:09.306833Z'),
(1617, 'kanji', '伸', '伸', 'A <radical>leader</radical> who''s <radical>humble</radical> will <kanji>stretch</kanji> in front of everyone. They''ll show that they have to get up every hour or so and stretch just like the rest of us.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1617', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BC%B8', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:36:58.505879Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:25:51.125444Z'),
(1618, 'kanji', '債', '債', 'Your <radical>leader</radical> gets the <radical>blame</radical> if your company is in <kanji>debt</kanji>. They''re the one who led you all into that debt, so they should get the blame too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1618', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%82%B5', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:40:47.744152Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.24955Z'),
(1619, 'kanji', '及', '及', '<radical>Escalator</radical>s are used to <kanji>reach</kanji> things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1619', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%8A', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:41:32.677975Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T07:25:54.018362Z'),
(1620, 'kanji', '奈', '奈', 'There is a <radical>big</radical> <radical>jackhammer</radical> in <kanji>Nara</kanji>. That jackhammer is the biggest in all the land and is on display in Nara, Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1620', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A5%88', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:41:55.167483Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:44:44.742923Z'),
(1621, 'kanji', '幅', '幅', 'You want to put this <radical>towel</radical> over this <radical>scarecrow</radical>, but you have to measure its <kanji>width</kanji> first. If the towel doesn''t have a greater width than the scarecrow, some of it will be showing. Which you definitely don''t want.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1621', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B9%85', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:42:30.207768Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.258495Z'),
(1622, 'kanji', '廃', '廃', 'This <radical>canopy</radical>, after your <radical>departure</radical>, will become <kanji>obsolete</kanji>. The canopy was here to protect you, but no one will need it once you''re gone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1622', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BB%83', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:42:58.097442Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.268401Z'),
(1623, 'kanji', '甘', '甘', 'The <radical>sweet</radical> radical and the <kanji>sweet</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Double sweet.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1623', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%94%98', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:43:45.660747Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.288397Z'),
(1624, 'kanji', '換', '換', 'You count with your <radical>fingers</radical> in <radical>prison</radical> all of the <radical>mouth</radical>s and <radical>legs</radical> you''ve acquired. Soon you''ll be able to make a <radical>big</radical> <kanji>exchange</kanji> with all of these body parts to get something awesome. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1624', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8F%9B', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:44:17.105132Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(1625, 'kanji', '摘', '摘', 'Stick your <radical>fingers</radical> in your <radical>mohawk</radical> and <kanji>pluck</kanji> each hair out, one by one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1625', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%91%98', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:44:46.046696Z', NULL, '2026-01-18T20:18:48.16238Z'),
(1626, 'kanji', '核', '核', 'You take a <radical>tree</radical> and <radical>slice</radical> it until you reach its <kanji>nucleus</kanji>. To get to the nucleus of the tree you have to make many a slice, but it’s possible.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1626', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A0%B8', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:45:15.782634Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.31124Z'),
(1627, 'kanji', '沖', '沖', 'There is a <radical>tsunami</radical> in the <radical>middle</radical> of the <kanji>open sea</kanji>. Luckily there are no ships around, so the tsunami in the middle of the open sea is not damaging to anyone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1627', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B2%96', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:45:43.090032Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.318553Z'),
(1628, 'kanji', '縄', '縄', 'Someone <radical>thread</radical>ed a <radical>turtle</radical> into long skinny pieces, making a <kanji>rope</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1628', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B8%84', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:46:13.188175Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:16:57.536431Z'),
(1629, 'kanji', '津', '津', 'There’s a huge <radical>tsunami</radical>, caused by a giant <radical>brush</radical>, fast approaching you and your <kanji>harbor</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1629', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B4%A5', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:46:36.842657Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.328062Z'),
(1630, 'kanji', '献', '献', 'The <radical>South</radical> <radical>Dog</radical> gives you an <kanji>offer</kanji>. None of the other dogs in the North, East, or West offered you anything.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1630', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8C%AE', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:46:59.90731Z', NULL, '2025-12-21T20:37:07.691155Z'),
(1631, 'kanji', '療', '療', 'If someone is <radical>sick</radical> feed them <radical>charcoal</radical> to <kanji>heal</kanji> them. Okay, so maybe you shouldn''t feed it directly to them, but charcoal has amazing water filtration powers. So put charcoal in their water and the water will heal them right up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1631', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%99%82', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:47:28.698485Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:36:15.542322Z'),
(1632, 'kanji', '継', '継', 'With a <radical>thread</radical>, a <radical>lion</radical> sews together all the <radical>rice</radical> he''ll <kanji>inherit</kanji> from his parents one day. He''s convinced his parents are going to try to keep some of his inheritance from him, so he''s linking it all together so it can''t go anywhere.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1632', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B6%99', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:47:59.770492Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.367203Z'),
(1633, 'kanji', '維', '維', 'You tie a piece of <radical>thread</radical> to your <radical>turkey</radical> in order to <kanji>maintain</kanji> proximity to it. The turkey likes to run away, so you tie a thread to it. This way you can hold the other end and maintain your turkey.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1633', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B6%AD', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:48:36.045816Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(1634, 'kanji', '舞', '舞', 'The <radical>nothing</radical> <radical>dance</radical> is still a <kanji>dance</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1634', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%88%9E', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:49:23.622971Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.406172Z'),
(1635, 'kanji', '伎', '伎', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of your <radical>branch</radical> became the leader from doing <kanji>deed</kanji>s. You can''t rise to the rank of branch leader if you don''t perform a certain number of deeds! Most of them good, I hope.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1635', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BC%8E', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:49:46.010511Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.407013Z'),
(1636, 'kanji', '踏', '踏', 'You soak each <radical>foot</radical> in <radical>water</radical> before going out in the <radical>sun</radical> to take your first <kanji>step</kanji>. Stepping on the ground when it''s been baking in the hot sun can burn your feet, so you''re careful to make them wet with water before stepping outside even once.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1636', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B8%8F', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:50:11.773171Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:32:20.896571Z'),
(1637, 'kanji', '般', '般', 'A <radical>boat</radical> will have at least one <radical>weapon</radical>, <kanji>generally</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1637', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%88%AC', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:50:40.136086Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:06:47.105775Z'),
(1638, 'kanji', '頼', '頼', 'A <radical>bundle</radical> of <radical>geoduck</radical>s is something you can always <kanji>trust</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1638', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A0%BC', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:52:56.707621Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:20:24.347803Z'),
(1639, 'kanji', '依', '依', 'A <radical>leader</radical> has <radical>clothes</radical> that he is quite <kanji>reliant</kanji> on. The leader needs his clothes to make him look like a leader. The leader has become more reliant on his clothes than he is on his actual skills as a leader!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1639', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BE%9D', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T08:53:20.913944Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.42775Z'),
(1640, 'kanji', '鹿', '鹿', 'A <radical>canopy</radical> and a <radical>helicopter</radical> are being <radical>compare</radical>d by a <kanji>deer</kanji>. The deer is standing very still (as deer do) and looking very slowly between the empty canopy and the helicopter sitting next to it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1640', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%B9%BF', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T09:00:19.550769Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.449689Z'),
(1641, 'kanji', '諾', '諾', 'You <radical>say</radical> something and the <radical>flowers</radical> are <radical>right</radical> in <kanji>agreement</kanji>. The flowers see where you are coming from when you say what you say. You and the flowers are in complete agreement.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1641', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AB%BE', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T09:00:48.865227Z', NULL, '2026-02-16T21:11:03.862361Z'),
(1707, 'kanji', '超', '超', 'You <radical>run</radical> and <radical>call</radical> out to people at the same time because you''re <kanji>super</kanji> <kanji>ultra</kanji> excited right now. You have a super crazy amount of energy you need to get out with your running and calling.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1707', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B6%85', 36, 1, '2013-05-06T23:38:30.969945Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:38:57.03621Z'),
(2336, 'kanji', '漬', '漬', '<radical>Tsunami</radical>s get <radical>blame</radical>d because they <kanji>pickle</kanji> everything in their path. That means that everything smells of pickles after a tsunami.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2336', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BC%AC', 36, 1, '2015-07-10T21:35:21.286771Z', NULL, '2026-03-22T19:09:05.295959Z'),
(2404, 'kanji', '叔', '叔', 'There were lots of things that were <radical>above</radical> you when you were <radical>small</radical> that you would need a <radical>stool</radical> to reach, so you just asked your <kanji>aunt</kanji> or <kanji>uncle</kanji> to get them for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2404', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%94', 36, 1, '2015-07-14T21:55:54.599636Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.182038Z'),
(2447, 'kanji', '湧', '湧', 'When you see a <radical>tsunami</radical> coming, <radical>courage</radical> will <kanji>well up</kanji> inside you, giving you the power to save everyone before you get washed away!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2447', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B9%A7', 36, 1, '2015-07-15T23:04:50.510858Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T01:03:16.365827Z'),
(1643, 'kanji', '跳', '跳', 'Your <radical>foot</radical> heard an <radical>omen</radical> and now it''s making you <kanji>hop</kanji> over things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1643', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B7%B3', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T16:56:04.677066Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:09:41.598263Z'),
(1644, 'kanji', '昭', '昭', 'The <radical>sun</radical> <radical>call</radical>s out to the earth by <kanji>shining</kanji> down on it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1644', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%98%AD', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T16:56:33.342835Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.473434Z'),
(1645, 'kanji', '漁', '漁', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> pushes all the <radical>fish</radical> closer to land, making for some easy <kanji>fishing</kanji>. The tsunami starts out in the middle of the ocean and pushes all the fish towards the coast where everyone does their fishing. With all the fish pushed closer to land and closer together, it’s very easy for people to go fishing in one spot and catch a lot of fish.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1645', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BC%81', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T16:56:53.652071Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.47504Z'),
(1646, 'kanji', '償', '償', 'The <radical>leader</radical> presents you with a <radical>prize</radical> as <kanji>reparation</kanji> for his past deeds. This leader wronged you in the past and wishes to make amends. Therefore the leader presents you with a reparation in the form of a glittering prize.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1646', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%84%9F', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T16:57:19.122955Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:20:00.421963Z'),
(1647, 'kanji', '刑', '刑', 'Destroy a <radical>lantern</radical> with your <radical>knife</radical> and you''ll be <kanji>punish</kanji>ed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1647', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%91', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T16:57:39.646129Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:21:37.396963Z'),
(1648, 'kanji', '募', '募', 'A <radical>greenhouse</radical> is the newest <radical>power</radical> of the city you decided to <kanji>recruit</kanji>. You’re trying to recruit all the buildings in town and the greenhouse was the next on your list. It adds just a little bit of power to the town, but it''s important nonetheless.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1648', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8B%9F', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T16:58:05.140085Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:27:31.265608Z'),
(1649, 'kanji', '執', '執', '<radical>Happiness</radical> is a <radical>circle</radical> that''s <kanji>tenacious</kanji>. In other words, we''re all in this circle/cycle where we do whatever we can to grab onto happiness and hold it as tightly as we can.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1649', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9F%B7', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T16:59:20.457557Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.506551Z'),
(1650, 'kanji', '塁', '塁', 'Under the <radical>rice paddy</radical>, an <radical>explosion</radical> shakes the <radical>dirt</radical>, exposing a hidden <kanji>base</kanji>. A <kanji>baseball base</kanji>! I guess this rice paddy was built on top of an old baseball field…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1650', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A1%81', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T16:59:59.180544Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:37:37.451894Z'),
(1651, 'kanji', '崩', '崩', 'Below this <radical>mountain</radical>, a <radical>moon</radical> and another <radical>moon</radical> crash into each other and the entire thing <kanji>crumble</kanji>s to pieces.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1651', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B4%A9', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:00:24.944875Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:03:31.557694Z'),
(1652, 'kanji', '患', '患', 'A <radical>skewer</radical> to the <radical>heart</radical> has <kanji>afflicted</kanji> your health. You’ve been afflicted with a disease from the skewer to your heart. Your affliction is death. You’re dying.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1652', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%82%A3', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:01:19.360297Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:51:48.206442Z'),
(1653, 'kanji', '戻', '戻', 'This <radical>door</radical> is <radical>big</radical> and you need to <kanji>return</kanji> it to its rightful owner. This door is too big and important looking to not belong to someone. You need to find its owner and return it to them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1653', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%88%BB', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:01:41.831931Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.536812Z'),
(1654, 'kanji', '抗', '抗', 'You take your <radical>fingers</radical> and slam a <radical>lid</radical> on the <radical>table</radical>. You make this big noise to <kanji>confront</kanji> the person in front of you. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1654', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8A%97', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:02:04.886339Z', NULL, '2025-11-19T02:44:49.698938Z'),
(1655, 'kanji', '抵', '抵', 'Your <radical>fingers</radical> join the rest of your <radical>clan</radical>, pressed into the <radical>ground</radical> to declare that you will <kanji>resist</kanji> your lords. Your clan is made of a bunch of peasants, but you aren''t going to work the land for other people anymore. You''re going to resist. Your fingers work for no one but yourselves from now on!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1655', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8A%B5', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:03:12.681301Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:40:04.566673Z'),
(1656, 'kanji', '旬', '旬', 'When a <radical>prison</radical> holds the <radical>sun</radical> inside it, everything will always be <kanji>in season</kanji>. Obviously if the sun is in prison, the seasons can''t change. Want to eat perfect apples all year round? Just put the sun in prison when they''re ripe — usually a short window of <kanji>ten days</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1656', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%97%AC', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:03:35.962762Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(1657, 'kanji', '湾', '湾', '<radical>Tsunami</radical>s hit these lands <radical>again</radical> and again, molding it into some kind of <radical>bow</radical> shape. Or in other words, creating a <kanji>gulf</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1657', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B9%BE', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:04:08.357197Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:56:35.426965Z'),
(1658, 'kanji', '爆', '爆', 'If you combine <radical>fire</radical> with <radical>violence</radical>, it will <kanji>explode</kanji>! A fire, once full of violence, will surely explode. The fire can’t handle all that violence so the fire just rumbles and shakes and then finally explodes. BOOM!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1658', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%88%86', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:04:30.797832Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:58:31.939138Z'),
(1659, 'kanji', '弾', '弾', 'What''s like a <radical>bow</radical>, but even more <radical>simple</radical>? A <kanji>bullet</kanji>. With a bow, you have to load an arrow and pull back the string and aim, yuck. Bullets can be shot with just a simple squeeze of a trigger.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1659', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%BE', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:05:12.859288Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:02:44.31174Z'),
(1660, 'kanji', '聴', '聴', 'When your <radical>ear</radical> is <radical>cross</radical>ed with sound, it catches it like a <radical>net</radical>, and sends it to your <radical>heart</radical> for processing. Because you''re using your heart to process this sound, it means you''re giving it a proper <kanji>listen</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1660', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%81%B4', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:05:36.545561Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.582262Z'),
(1661, 'kanji', '跡', '跡', 'When you''re stepping around with your <radical>foot</radical> <radical>again</radical> and again, you''ll leave behind <kanji>traces</kanji> all over the place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1661', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B7%A1', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:05:57.980557Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:56:35.426965Z'),
(1662, 'kanji', '遣', '遣', 'On the <radical>scooter</radical> is an <radical>insect</radical> and a <radical>bear</radical>. The bear immediately stomps the insect, squishing it flat. "My god, the insect is smashed. Someone call <kanji>dispatch</kanji>!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1662', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%A3', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:06:29.868783Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:15:14.065745Z'),
(1663, 'kanji', '闘', '闘', 'At this <radical>gate</radical> you''re trying to get some <radical>beans</radical> through, but your <radical>measurements</radical> were off, leading to a big <kanji>fight</kanji>. The bean authorities at the gate always want to fight about bean sizes, and now you have to fight to get those beans through.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1663', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%97%98', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:06:59.525974Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:54:38.942095Z'),
(1664, 'kanji', '陣', '陣', 'This <radical>building</radical> is full of <radical>car</radical>s because it''s an <kanji>army camp</kanji>. The cars (and tanks and planes) need to be ready to go at any moment just like the soldiers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1664', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%A3', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:07:28.71059Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.603359Z'),
(1665, 'kanji', '香', '香', '<radical>Grain</radical> left in the <radical>sun</radical> blossom and give off a wonderful <kanji>fragrance</kanji>. They smell fresh and delicious. There''s nothing better than the fragrance of fresh, sun-kissed grain.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1665', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A6%99', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:07:50.562226Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:12:44.933943Z'),
(1666, 'kanji', '兆', '兆', 'The <radical>omen</radical> radical and the <kanji>omen</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1666', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%86', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:08:11.026993Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.640316Z'),
(1667, 'kanji', '臨', '臨', 'You give your <radical>servant</radical> a <radical>gun</radical> along with some other <radical>products</radical> to <kanji>look to</kanji>. You can’t look to them yourself since you are very busy. You allow your servant to look to your gun and the other products.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1667', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%87%A8', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:08:42.031935Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:44:51.472274Z'),
(1668, 'kanji', '削', '削', 'A <radical>triceratops</radical> with a <radical>moon</radical> <radical>knife</radical> can <kanji>whittle down</kanji> anything.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1668', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%89%8A', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:09:04.232758Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.649715Z'),
(1669, 'kanji', '契', '契', 'The weight of <radical>life</radical> on your <radical>sword</radical> is <radical>big</radical> when you <kanji>pledge</kanji> to protect your city. That''s the big, big weight of every single person''s life in that city pressing down on you every time you take out that sword.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1669', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A5%91', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:09:26.588428Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:44:30.631655Z'),
(1670, 'kanji', '恵', '恵', 'You carry a <radical>cross</radical> across a <radical>rice paddy</radical> with a <radical>heart</radical> on it to return a <kanji>favor</kanji> for your friend. The heart needs to be purified so that’s why you have it on the cross and your friend lives on a rice paddy farm so that’s why you’re running through that. The favor you are paying your friend back with is getting him this heart. He needs a new one and you’re providing it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1670', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%81%B5', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:09:48.726891Z', NULL, '2026-03-26T14:18:28.27084Z'),
(1671, 'kanji', '抱', '抱', 'Take your <radical>fingers</radical> and <radical>wrap</radical> them around another person. This is called a <kanji>hug</kanji>. Just make sure you include all the stuff attached to your fingers, like your hands and arms too, otherwise it''s more of a squeeze, and we don''t want to do that to people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1671', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8A%B1', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:10:13.325432Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T04:43:39.600814Z'),
(1672, 'kanji', '掲', '掲', 'Use your <radical>fingers</radical> when the <radical>sun</radical> shines into your <radical>prison</radical> cell and hold your <radical>spoon</radical> up in full <kanji>display</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1672', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8E%B2', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:10:40.642669Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(1673, 'kanji', '狙', '狙', 'You see an <radical>animal</radical> wearing a <radical>top hat</radical> and decide to <kanji>aim</kanji> at it. The top hat is a perfect, giant target you use to take down this animal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1673', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8B%99', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:11:01.789487Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:51:15.083936Z'),
(1674, 'kanji', '葬', '葬', 'You brought <radical>flowers</radical> to leave at the place where a <radical>yakuza</radical> took a <radical>spoon</radical> and killed <radical>twenty</radical> people, since there''s now a <kanji>burial</kanji> happening there. You didn''t know these people, but you still want to leave flowers at their burial.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1674', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%91%AC', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:11:26.191406Z', NULL, '2025-12-25T03:08:56.394099Z'),
(2384, 'kanji', '絹', '絹', 'Put <radical>thread</radical> in the <radical>mouth</radical> of a worm while the <radical>moon</radical> is out and it will poop out some <kanji>silk</kanji>. That''s why they call them silkworms, didn''t you know? But it has to be while the moon is out, otherwise normal thread will come right back out the other end of the worm. The moon is what makes the magic happen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2384', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B5%B9', 37, 1, '2015-07-14T16:44:36.22792Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.98464Z'),
(8862, 'kanji', '眉', '眉', 'You''re holding a <radical>flag</radical> on a <radical>stick</radical> above your <radical>eye</radical>s to imitate <kanji>eyebrows</kanji>. You can''t grow any of your own, but you really love eyebrows, so this was the best you could do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8862', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9C%89', 37, 1, '2019-04-29T23:26:01.23849Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:32.078001Z'),
(1675, 'kanji', '需', '需', 'In the <radical>rain</radical> I take my <radical>rake</radical> and shake it, making a <kanji>demand</kanji> that the rain stop! I demand this rain stop right now! Don’t make me shake my rake more violently than I am currently shaking it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1675', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9C%80', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:07:41.1069Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.701201Z'),
(1676, 'kanji', '齢', '齢', 'You get <radical>tooth</radical> <radical>orders</radical>. You get these tooth orders various times in your life, depending on your <kanji>age</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1676', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%BD%A2', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:12:20.369223Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:27:09.777362Z'),
(1677, 'kanji', '宜', '宜', 'By showing the <radical>roof</radical> of your <radical>top hat</radical> you send your <kanji>best regards</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1677', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%9C', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:12:38.720187Z', NULL, '2025-10-23T04:12:26.831614Z'),
(1678, 'kanji', '繰', '繰', 'You have <radical>thread</radical> and some <radical>syrup</radical> and you need to <kanji>spin</kanji> them together. Drip the syrup onto the thread and spin it around and around over your head.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1678', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B9%B0', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:14:55.588426Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:36:02.75854Z'),
(1679, 'kanji', '避', '避', 'You''re on a <radical>scooter</radical> and suddenly someone throws a <radical>hot pepper</radical> at you and you have to <kanji>dodge</kanji> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1679', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%BF', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:15:31.627002Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.720666Z'),
(1680, 'kanji', '妊', '妊', 'A <radical>woman</radical> sleeps with the <radical>king</radical> and becomes <kanji>pregnant</kanji>. Only the king wasn’t human and now the woman is pregnant with a hybrid alien baby!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1680', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A6%8A', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:15:54.707689Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1681, 'kanji', '娠', '娠', 'A <radical>woman</radical> who has a <radical>landslide</radical> getting ready inside her is <kanji>pregnant</kanji>. And that baby is gonna come out like a landslide.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1681', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A8%A0', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:16:23.473188Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1682, 'kanji', '致', '致', 'A <radical>mole</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> will <kanji>do</kanji> whatever you ask, if you know how to ask it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1682', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%87%B4', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:16:50.24669Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.739894Z'),
(1683, 'kanji', '刊', '刊', 'You got your copy of "The <radical>Dry</radical> <radical>Knife</radical>." It''s the newest <kanji>edition</kanji> of the magazine you love on keeping your knives dry.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1683', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%8A', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:17:14.860191Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.753778Z'),
(1684, 'kanji', '奏', '奏', 'When it''s <radical>spring</radical> in <radical>heaven</radical> they <kanji>play music</kanji>. To your mortal, human ears it sounds more like snow melting, birds chirping, and plants waking up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1684', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A5%8F', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:17:37.903357Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:44:06.458715Z'),
(1685, 'kanji', '伴', '伴', 'A <radical>leader</radical> asks <radical>half</radical> of his men to <kanji>accompany</kanji> him on a secret mission. The leader’s mission is volunteer only, so it’s impressive that he got half of his men to accompany him.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1685', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BC%B4', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:18:03.92347Z', NULL, '2025-10-05T17:02:35.14767Z'),
(1686, 'kanji', '併', '併', 'A <radical>leader</radical> with <radical>horns</radical> takes a <radical>lantern</radical> and searches for people to <kanji>join</kanji> him. He needs more people to join his cause so he can get his horns removed. He’s embarrassed of them which is why he only travels at night, hence the lantern.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1686', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BD%B5', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:18:25.838551Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.767054Z'),
(1687, 'kanji', '傾', '傾', 'You <radical>change</radical> this <radical>geoduck</radical> because you want it to <kanji>lean</kanji>. Right now it''s standing up straight, but you want it to lean to one side instead. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1687', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%82%BE', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:18:55.017578Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(1688, 'kanji', '却', '却', '<radical>Past</radical> <radical>stamp</radical>s usually depict images <kanji>contrary</kanji> to current views.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1688', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%B4', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:19:15.71697Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:28:50.223848Z'),
(1689, 'kanji', '奥', '奥', 'If there’s a <radical>drop</radical> on your <radical>head</radical> and the <radical>rice</radical> is <radical>big</radical>, you know you’re in the <kanji>interior</kanji>. And by interior, I mean you’re actually inside someone. The rice is big because you’ve shrunk to a tiny size and are inside someone’s stomach. The drop on your head is actually digestive fluid.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1689', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A5%A5', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:19:46.079751Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.78948Z'),
(1690, 'kanji', '慮', '慮', 'A <radical>tiger</radical> needs to <radical>think</radical> and <kanji>consider</kanji> its actions before it attacks. Tigers are smart hunters, so they need to consider all options and really think through a situation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1690', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%85%AE', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:20:10.883508Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T19:30:58.619573Z'),
(1691, 'kanji', '懸', '懸', 'In your <radical>prefecture</radical>, you come from a long <radical>lineage</radical> of people who share one <radical>heart</radical>. When the heart is passed on to the next in line, it flies out of their body, and <kanji>suspend</kanji>s itself in the sky, for you to take.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1691', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%87%B8', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:20:35.639381Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.795836Z'),
(1692, 'kanji', '房', '房', 'The <radical>door</radical> in that <radical>direction</radical> is among a <kanji>cluster</kanji> of doors. You run in the direction of the cluster and one door stands out to you. You enter it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1692', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%88%BF', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:21:01.545946Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.809371Z'),
(1693, 'kanji', '扱', '扱', 'Where do you put your <radical>fingers</radical> when you''re on an <radical>escalator</radical>? On the <kanji>handle</kanji>! Okay, so maybe it''s not a handle and more of a railing, but for the sake of this kanji, picture a handle instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1693', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%89%B1', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:21:25.732677Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:51:13.57524Z'),
(1694, 'kanji', '抑', '抑', 'Use your <radical>fingers</radical> on the <radical>cat pirate</radical> to <kanji>suppress</kanji> him. The cat pirate was attacking you, so you took your fingers and pet his head and scratched his chin. Now he''s calm and suppressed. He doesn''t even remember why he was attacking you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1694', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8A%91', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:21:48.87064Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.822115Z'),
(1695, 'kanji', '択', '択', 'Shove your <radical>fingers</radical> into a bowl of <radical>shrimp</radical> to <kanji>select</kanji> the best one. You know your shrimp and you know them well, so you won''t settle for anything less than the best. Put your whole arm in there and feel around until you can select the one you want most.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1695', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8A%9E', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:22:14.294141Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:39:53.26852Z'),
(1696, 'kanji', '描', '描', 'With your <radical>fingers</radical> you pluck some <radical>flowers</radical> in a <radical>rice paddy</radical>, in order to <kanji>draw</kanji> them. Have you ever seen rice paddy flowers? They''re beautiful. And if you can draw them, you can capture that beauty and show it to everyone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1696', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8F%8F', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:22:43.354246Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:44:43.37485Z'),
(1697, 'kanji', '盤', '盤', 'Your <radical>boat</radical> holds your <radical>weapon</radical> like a <radical>plate</radical>. So in a way, it''s just a <kanji>tray</kanji> for your weapon.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1697', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9B%A4', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:23:23.682784Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:11:09.852184Z'),
(1698, 'kanji', '称', '称', 'You take a <radical>grain</radical> and fashion it into a <radical>gun</radical> that is <radical>small</radical>, earning you the <kanji>title</kanji> of Small Arms Dealer (or SAD). Congratulations!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1698', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A7%B0', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:24:04.565248Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.856139Z'),
(1699, 'kanji', '緒', '緒', 'The kind of <radical>thread</radical> you''d wrap around <radical>someone</radical> would have to be a <kanji>cord</kanji>. A regular thread wouldn''t be strong enough, so you''d definitely use a cord. 

This can also mean <kanji>connection</kanji>, since cords create connections (by tying things together). ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1699', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B7%92', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:24:27.766857Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:24:21.947579Z'),
(1700, 'kanji', '緩', '緩', 'The <radical>thread</radical> on this <radical>football</radical> is <kanji>loose</kanji>. We need to tighten up the loose thread on this football or it’s gonna fall apart while we’re playing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1700', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B7%A9', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:24:58.124893Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:47:59.94391Z'),
(1701, 'kanji', '託', '託', 'They <radical>say</radical> if you put things on a <radical>slide</radical>, <radical>seven</radical> things to be exact, that’s the best way to <kanji>consign</kanji> goods to someone. They say if you consign seven things to someone, you should send them over on a slide. Slide ‘em on down. All seven of ‘em.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1701', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%97', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:25:28.413015Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.868013Z'),
(1702, 'kanji', '賄', '賄', 'The more <radical>shellfish</radical> you <radical>have</radical>, the more you can <kanji>bribe</kanji> people. That worked in the olden days when shells were money, and today too if the people you want to bribe really like seafood.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1702', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B3%84', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:25:48.92285Z', NULL, '2026-01-18T18:24:30.29317Z'),
(1704, 'kanji', '贈', '贈', 'You put on the <radical>shellfish</radical> <radical>mask</radical> once per year and break into people''s beach houses, leaving behind <kanji>presents</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1704', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B4%88', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:26:49.256247Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T18:43:31.201049Z'),
(1705, 'kanji', '逃', '逃', 'You use your <radical>scooter</radical> when the <radical>omen</radical> comes so that you can <kanji>escape</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1705', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%83', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:27:10.337513Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.753876Z'),
(1706, 'kanji', '還', '還', 'There''s a <radical>scooter</radical> coming toward you with a <radical>net</radical> trapping a weird <radical>zombie</radical> on it. You don''t want this here, so you decide to <kanji>send back</kanji> the zombie. (It''s a weird zombie because it has the ground on top of its head instead of dirt!)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1706', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%82%84', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:27:39.057462Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.903505Z'),
(1017, 'kanji', '虚', '虚', 'A <radical>tiger</radical>, clad in <radical>spikes</radical>, exists in the <kanji>void</kanji>. This tiger is from another dimension where tigers rule the earth, clad in armor with spikes. The void has allowed this tiger to cross dimensional planes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1017', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%99%9A', 39, 1, '2012-10-30T20:06:57.221956Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T19:30:58.618335Z'),
(1708, 'kanji', '邦', '邦', 'There are <radical>three</radical> <radical>slide</radical>s sticking out of this <radical>building</radical> that lead to your <kanji>home country</kanji>. The rest of the slides go to other places, but three are yours. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1708', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%82%A6', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:13:33.746751Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T08:20:55.720034Z'),
(1709, 'kanji', '鈴', '鈴', 'You take <radical>gold</radical> in exchange for executing <radical>orders</radical> to construct a <kanji>buzzer</kanji>. You are a <kanji>small bell</kanji> maker and someone orders you to make one into a buzzer in exchange for some gold. This is how you make a living.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1709', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%88%B4', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:14:11.854721Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:27:09.777362Z'),
(1711, 'kanji', '岐', '岐', 'A <radical>mountain</radical> <radical>branch</radical> will <kanji>branch off</kanji> from the rest.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1711', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B2%90', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:14:58.04972Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.937384Z'),
(1712, 'kanji', '隆', '隆', 'A <radical>building</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> will allow <radical>life</radical> to have <kanji>prosperity</kanji>. Before we had buildings it was impossible to have any prosperity in winter because everything was cold and most of us died. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1712', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9A%86', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:15:27.563668Z', NULL, '2025-10-13T22:29:21.436821Z'),
(1713, 'kanji', '雇', '雇', 'In through your <radical>door</radical> walks a <radical>turkey</radical>, so you <kanji>employ</kanji> it. You''ve always employed any turkey that comes through that door.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1713', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%87', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:15:50.807404Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:47:36.027153Z'),
(1714, 'kanji', '控', '控', 'Raise your <radical>fingers</radical> to the <radical>sky</radical> to symbolize that you <kanji>abstain</kanji> from something. If someone offers you fur coats, raise your fingers to abstain. If someone tries to get you to bully someone, raise your fingers to abstain.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1714', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8E%A7', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:16:16.5359Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.974628Z'),
(1715, 'kanji', '壁', '壁', 'There are <radical>hot peppers</radical> hiding in this <radical>dirt</radical>, so you build a <kanji>wall</kanji> to protect yourself from them. Have you ever stepped on a really hot pepper? The oils bleed into your shoes and burn your feet! You don''t want anyone stepping on these so you wall them up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1715', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A3%81', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:16:45.749195Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.973723Z'),
(1716, 'kanji', '棋', '棋', 'A <radical>tree</radical> <radical>crab</radical> breaks into your games cabinet and steals a <kanji>chess piece</kanji>. He’s trying to impress all his tree crabs back home by showing how not afraid of humans he is. Plus the rest of the tree crabs need one last chess piece to complete their set.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1716', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A3%8B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:17:15.987483Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:09:00.434554Z'),
(1717, 'kanji', '渋', '渋', 'There''s a <radical>tsunami</radical> coming and you can''t <radical>stop</radical> the <radical>explosion</radical> of <kanji>bitter</kanji>ness in your mouth. You''re scared. Your mouth tastes bitter and <kanji>astringent</kanji>.

This word also means <kanji>reluctant</kanji>, because wouldn''t you be reluctant to let a tsunami come wash you away with that bitter explosion of flavor in your mouth?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1717', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B8%8B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:17:40.997055Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.982792Z'),
(1718, 'kanji', '片', '片', 'The <radical>one sided</radical> radical and the <kanji>one sided</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1718', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%89%87', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:18:09.270966Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.985663Z'),
(1719, 'kanji', '群', '群', 'Your <radical>buddy</radical> the <radical>sheep</radical> is part of a <kanji>flock</kanji>. A flock of sheep. Your sheep buddy is a member of a flock.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1719', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BE%A4', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:18:39.636544Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.997379Z'),
(1720, 'kanji', '仙', '仙', 'A <radical>leader</radical> who lives in a <radical>mountain</radical> is a <kanji>hermit</kanji>. The only thing this hermit is a leader of now is mountain goats. This hermit dislikes people so that’s why he lives in a mountain now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1720', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%99', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:18:58.853925Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:58.998326Z'),
(1721, 'kanji', '充', '充', 'The <radical>allocate</radical> radical and the <kanji>allocate</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1721', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%85', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:19:18.355864Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.017742Z'),
(1722, 'kanji', '免', '免', 'The <radical>excuse</radical> radical and the <kanji>excuse</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Dandy!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1722', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%8D', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:19:37.807904Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.029173Z'),
(1724, 'kanji', '圏', '圏', 'Your <radical>mouth</radical> contains a <radical>scroll</radical>, which you pull out and look at. On the scroll is a map, showing the <kanji>range</kanji> of the <kanji>sphere</kanji> of your mouth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1724', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9C%8F', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:20:21.31743Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.069548Z'),
(1727, 'kanji', '奪', '奪', 'A <radical>big</radical> <radical>turkey</radical> takes the <radical>measurement</radical>s of a safe before he can <kanji>rob</kanji> it. If the safe is too big it won''t fit in his getaway car and the robbery will have been a failure. So before the turkey robs a place he needs to get those measurements.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1727', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A5%AA', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:21:36.417411Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:28:32.451018Z'),
(1728, 'kanji', '御', '御', 'You <radical>loiter</radical> at <radical>noon</radical> near a <radical>stop</radical> sign to get a <radical>stamp</radical> of approval from someone <kanji>honorable</kanji>. This honorable personage drives by at noon every day, and because they have to stop at the stop sign, people loiter here for a chance to get their honorable stamp of approval. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1728', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%A1', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:22:04.958038Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T21:01:01.354226Z'),
(1729, 'kanji', '慎', '慎', 'When someone wants to view your <radical>soul</radical>''s <radical>reality</radical>, show it with <kanji>humility</kanji>. Because the reality is... our souls are all a little dirty. And we haven''t been the best versions we could be. So make sure when you show all of yourself you do so with some humility.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1729', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%85%8E', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:22:24.628395Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(1730, 'kanji', '拒', '拒', 'Show your <radical>fingers</radical> to a <radical>giant</radical> to show you <kanji>refuse</kanji> him. Giants are very big. So big that when you talk they can''t really hear what you''re saying. Hold up all your fingers so that he can see what you mean.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1730', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8B%92', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:22:44.415129Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:10:44.970022Z'),
(1731, 'kanji', '枠', '枠', 'In a <radical>tree</radical> on <radical>nine</radical> branches are some <radical>cross</radical> <kanji>frame</kanji>s. Each cross frame is hung on the branches of the tree. You don’t know who decided to hang the frames there, but you think it might be some new wave art or something. Actually you need a frame, and there are already nine up there, so you decide to take one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1731', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9E%A0', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:23:29.069979Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.112514Z'),
(1732, 'kanji', '甲', '甲', 'The <radical>turtle shell</radical> radical and the <kanji>turtle shell</kanji> kanji are exactly the same! Oddly, this can also be used to talk about the first item in a list, so to mean <kanji>A</kanji> out of A through D, for example.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1732', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%94%B2', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:23:48.792859Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.126385Z'),
(1733, 'kanji', '斐', '斐', '<radical>Criminal</radical> <radical>writing</radical> is always <kanji>patterned</kanji>. That is to say, there is a pattern to it, lots of ARGS and GONNA STAB YAs, but it''s also patterned beautifully, with a collage of cut out letters.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1733', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%96%90', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:24:09.088741Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.136824Z'),
(1734, 'kanji', '祉', '祉', 'When a <radical>spirit</radical> <radical>stop</radical>s protecting you, you''ll have to go on <kanji>welfare</kanji>. If you can''t rely on your family spirit, you''ll have to have the help and protection come from somewhere else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1734', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A5%89', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:24:34.127033Z', NULL, '2025-12-21T20:51:22.632515Z'),
(1735, 'kanji', '稲', '稲', 'The <radical>grain</radical> stuck in your <radical>cleat</radical> had a <radical>former</radical> life. In that life it was a <kanji>rice plant</kanji>. It was a happy little rice plant, minding its own business, when it was yanked up, cleaned, and put in a bag for people to buy and cook and eat. That was so long ago. Now all that''s left is this sad loose grain in the bottom of your shoe.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1735', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A8%B2', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:24:57.965649Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:34:39.359876Z'),
(1736, 'kanji', '譲', '譲', 'When you <radical>say</radical> "<radical>Nurse</radical>!" you''re probably asking to <kanji>defer</kanji> to them in some way, shape, or form. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1736', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AD%B2', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:25:23.199437Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.14754Z'),
(1737, 'kanji', '謙', '謙', 'They <radical>say</radical> <radical>Mona Lisa</radical> had more <kanji>modesty</kanji> than any other woman at the time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1737', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AC%99', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:26:07.425611Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.159557Z'),
(1738, 'kanji', '躍', '躍', 'You use your <radical>foot</radical> to kick <radical>Wolverine</radical> in the face so a <radical>turkey</radical> can <kanji>leap</kanji> to freedom. This turkey is trying to escape from Wolverine by leaping over a fence. Wolverine doesn’t want the turkey to escape, but you kick him with your foot and the turkey is then free to leap to safety.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1738', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BA%8D', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:26:36.379851Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(1739, 'kanji', '銃', '銃', 'Take some <radical>gold</radical> and <radical>allocate</radical> it to buy a <kanji>gun</kanji>. Guns are weapons of the old world, so you can''t just buy them with paper money. Guns have to be bought with gold coins or bricks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1739', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8A%83', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:26:51.465968Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:30:36.474398Z'),
(1740, 'kanji', '項', '項', '<radical>Construction</radical> for <radical>geoduck</radical>s doesn''t require as much paperwork as construction for humans, but you still need to write at least a <kanji>paragraph</kanji> to explain what you''re doing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1740', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A0%85', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:27:18.483196Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(1741, 'kanji', '鋼', '鋼', 'You come across a <radical>gold</radical> <radical>hill</radical>. You think you hit it big! You start digging, only to find out that it is merely a paper-thin layer of gold covering a hill of <kanji>steel</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1741', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8B%BC', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:27:59.517433Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.185056Z'),
(2459, 'kanji', '囚', '囚', 'Inside this giant <radical>mouth</radical> is a <radical>person</radical>, trapped, because they''re a <kanji>criminal</kanji>. They did something bad (they touched the mouth of another person without consent) and now they''re in mouth jail for criminals.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2459', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9B%9A', 39, 1, '2015-07-16T17:38:56.212239Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.614363Z'),
(1742, 'kanji', '顧', '顧', 'You <radical>employ</radical> a <radical>geoduck</radical> to <kanji>review</kanji> all your work. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1742', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A1%A7', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:51:55.475752Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(1743, 'kanji', '駐', '駐', 'A <radical>horse</radical> is your <radical>master</radical> and you are merely a <kanji>resident</kanji> in his house. How did this horse become your master? Those aren’t questions a resident should ask! Know your place, resident!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1743', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A7%90', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:52:19.505024Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.206844Z'),
(1744, 'kanji', '駆', '駆', 'A <radical>horse</radical> goes to his favorite <radical>district</radical> to <kanji>gallop</kanji> around. This district has lots of cool buildings and lights to look at, so this horse loves to gallop around there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1744', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A7%86', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:52:49.281858Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:38:51.63221Z'),
(1745, 'kanji', '柱', '柱', 'This <radical>tree</radical> is the <radical>master</radical> of a <kanji>pillar</kanji>. The pillar is subservient to its tree master because the tree is so great and awesome and big and cool and stuff. The pillar has great respect for its tree master.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1745', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9F%B1', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:53:29.434024Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.207593Z'),
(1746, 'kanji', '唱', '唱', 'It''s time to do the <radical>mouth</radical> <radical>sun</radical> <radical>sun</radical> <kanji>chant</kanji>. That''s when you use your mouth to chant sun sun, sun sun, sun sun, over and over until the sun appears.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1746', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%94%B1', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:53:59.600647Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.222093Z'),
(1747, 'kanji', '孝', '孝', '<radical>Teacher</radical>s are all around us. Those who came before us are our teachers, so it''s important to respect them. This is the basic idea of <kanji>filial piety</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1747', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AD%9D', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:54:21.134201Z', NULL, '2025-11-10T19:15:19.623225Z'),
(1748, 'kanji', '俊', '俊', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of all the <radical>spider</radical>s is a <kanji>genius</kanji>! Spiders are already pretty smart. They can weave complex homes and traps in one web. They even know where not to step so they don''t get caught. But the leader knows everything. It made itself a little webbed computer and everything!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1748', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BF%8A', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:54:55.11722Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.235085Z'),
(1749, 'kanji', '兼', '兼', 'The <radical>Mona Lisa</radical> is in front of you. "You must all paint the Mona Lisa <kanji>concurrently</kanji>," says your art class teacher.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1749', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%BC', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:55:16.763499Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T08:55:40.661992Z'),
(1750, 'kanji', '剤', '剤', '<radical>Simultaneous</radical>ly, you throw your <radical>knife</radical> at another person who''s throwing a knife at you and you both get a <kanji>dose</kanji> of your own medicine. Literally, there''s poison on these knives and now you''ve both been stabbed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1750', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%89%A4', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:56:16.715293Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.246815Z'),
(1751, 'kanji', '吹', '吹', 'You attempt to expel wind out of your <radical>mouth</radical> but <radical>lack</radical> the breath to <kanji>blow</kanji> out any. You’re using your mouth and trying to blow out some air, but you are failing to do so.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1751', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%B9', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:57:46.421029Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:36:58.322888Z'),
(1752, 'kanji', '堀', '堀', 'Imagine you''re driving and there''s some <radical>dirt</radical> with a <radical>flag</radical> in it near the <radical>exit</radical> ramp. That flag marks a <kanji>ditch</kanji> running alongside the road. Don''t try to exit the road here, or you''ll end up stuck in that ditch.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1752', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A0%80', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:58:20.867961Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:32:01.682876Z'),
(1753, 'kanji', '巡', '巡', 'You''re using your <radical>scooter</radical> to navigate the <radical>flood</radical> because you''re on <kanji>patrol</kanji>. You''re one of the few people with a floating scooter, so it''s your job to patrol the flood waters and look for people and animals that need help.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1753', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B7%A1', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:58:48.275196Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.270806Z'),
(1754, 'kanji', '戒', '戒', 'The <radical>drunkard</radical> issued <radical>twenty</radical> <kanji>commandment</kanji>s to his people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1754', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%88%92', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:59:12.984119Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.275504Z'),
(1755, 'kanji', '排', '排', 'The <radical>fingers</radical> are where <radical>criminal</radical>s <kanji>emit</kanji> or <kanji>expel</kanji> all of their bad energy from. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1755', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8E%92', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:59:35.566959Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.276767Z'),
(1756, 'kanji', '携', '携', 'Use your <radical>fingers</radical> to shoo a <radical>turkey</radical> down the <radical>stairs</radical> and into a <kanji>portable</kanji> carrier. You''ve been trying to get this turkey into the carrier so you can transport it for like, the past hour. You finally got it done with the help of gravity. And, you know, smacking it a bit in the butt with your fingers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1756', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%90%BA', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:00:00.403517Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:41:30.052698Z'),
(1757, 'kanji', '敏', '敏', '<radical>Every</radical> <radical>winter</radical> you are <kanji>alert</kanji> for danger. Everyone knows that the worst monsters come out in the winter so you need to be alert if you want to survive.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1757', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%95%8F', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:00:26.769108Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.288484Z'),
(1758, 'kanji', '鋭', '鋭', 'If you have <radical>gold</radical> you also need a <radical>guard</radical> with a very <kanji>sharp</kanji> sword to protect it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1758', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8B%AD', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:00:53.293053Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.291749Z'),
(1759, 'kanji', '敷', '敷', 'Taking a <radical>cross</radical> with a <radical>drop</radical> on it, you walk through the <radical>rice paddy</radical> in the <radical>direction</radical> of <radical>winter</radical> and <kanji>spread</kanji> your arms wide. You spread your arms to halt the approach of winter because your rice paddy is not done growing yet. You take the cross with you for some extra religious power of persuasion, and the drop in case any of that rice is thirsty.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1759', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%95%B7', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:01:19.841296Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:36:00.487837Z'),
(1760, 'kanji', '殿', '殿', 'Under the same <radical>flag</radical>, all the people get <radical>together</radical> to present a new <radical>weapon</radical> to your <kanji>lord</kanji>. You all bow and say, "<kanji>Milord</kanji>, this gift is for you." Because that''s the proper way to address your lord.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1760', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AE%BF', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:01:46.077786Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:59:46.609728Z'),
(1761, 'kanji', '犠', '犠', 'The <radical>cow</radical> of <radical>righteousness</radical> is always there for us. He is the cow that must <kanji>sacrifice</kanji> himself for us to survive.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1761', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8A%A0', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:02:11.520047Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:37:01.42735Z'),
(1762, 'kanji', '獲', '獲', 'You see an <radical>animal</radical> with some <radical>flowers</radical> and a <radical>turkey</radical> on a <radical>stool</radical> trying to <kanji>seize</kanji> them. The animal with the flowers is really tall so the turkey needs to stand on the stool in its attempt to seize the flowers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1762', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8D%B2', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:02:41.983052Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:51:15.083936Z'),
(1763, 'kanji', '茂', '茂', 'A thick carpet of <radical>flowers</radical> grows on a <radical>slide</radical> and on the <radical>drunkard</radical> passed out next to it in your <kanji>luxuriant</kanji> garden. Your garden is so luxuriant that the flowers have engulfed literally everything in it, from the kids'' play structure to the hapless drunk who wandered in last night.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1763', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8C%82', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:03:06.516592Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:43:01.832559Z'),
(1764, 'kanji', '繁', '繁', '<radical>Every</radical> <radical>winter</radical> the <radical>thread</radical> fields become <kanji>overgrown</kanji>. Yes, thread grows in fields these days. And the thread grows the most in the winter because reasons. The thread gets so overgrown that you can’t even walk through it without cutting the thread down with a machete.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1764', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B9%81', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:03:32.713421Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.334073Z'),
(1765, 'kanji', '頻', '頻', 'You <radical>walk</radical> by a <radical>geoduck</radical> a <kanji>frequent</kanji> amount. This geoduck really intrigues you, so you can’t help but frequent the area the geoduck is in and walk around.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1765', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A0%BB', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:03:53.053485Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(1766, 'kanji', '殖', '殖', 'When you go to the <radical>yakuza</radical> to make things <radical>straight</radical>, your problems just <kanji>multiply</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1766', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AE%96', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:04:18.610202Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:10:51.473012Z'),
(1767, 'kanji', '薄', '薄', 'The <radical>flowers</radical> are being worked upon by a <radical>tsunami</radical> with a <radical>drop</radical> <radical>specialty</radical>: this tsunami can <kanji>dilute</kanji> flowers with just one tsunami-powered drop of itself. That’s its specialty.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1767', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%96%84', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:04:44.975094Z', NULL, '2025-11-15T18:08:26.319636Z'),
(1768, 'kanji', '衝', '衝', 'If you try to <radical>go</radical> somewhere but you''re too <radical>heavy</radical> you''ll <kanji>collide</kanji> with everything. Cars, people, houses. You''re too heavy to dodge them, so you collide with it all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1768', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A1%9D', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:05:09.542919Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.356321Z'),
(1769, 'kanji', '誉', '誉', 'There’s <radical>grass</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical> and the <radical>fins</radical> there <radical>say</radical> you have no <kanji>honor</kanji>. The fins are left over from the fish you dismembered and they do not believe it was an act of honor. Now they are just sitting on the ground on the grass, calling you out for having no honor.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1769', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AA%89', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:05:33.512507Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.361526Z'),
(1770, 'kanji', '褒', '褒', 'Lift the <radical>lid</radical> off your <radical>preserve</radical>s, <radical>kick</radical> it, and <kanji>praise</kanji> the lord! Your food is preserved, you don''t need the lid anymore, praise be!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1770', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A4%92', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:05:57.721651Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.383966Z'),
(1771, 'kanji', '透', '透', 'The <radical>scooter</radical> you made out of <radical>grain</radical>s, and are about to ride down the <radical>stairs</radical> with, is completely <kanji>transparent</kanji>. You mashed the grains down so fine that you can see through them! And then you made them into a scooter.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1771', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%8F', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:06:22.461914Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:41:30.052698Z'),
(1772, 'kanji', '隣', '隣', 'Inside that <radical>building</radical> someone is doing a <radical>rice</radical> <radical>dance</radical>. That someone is your <kanji>neighbor</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1772', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9A%A3', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:06:52.018222Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.391238Z'),
(1773, 'kanji', '雅', '雅', 'A <radical>fang</radical> on a <radical>turkey</radical> is <kanji>elegant</kanji>. Most turkeys do not have fangs, so this turkey with a fang is quite elegant and dignified. This turkey is special.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1773', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%85', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:07:13.715295Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:45:39.324625Z'),
(8835, 'kanji', '嬉', '嬉', 'A <radical>woman</radical> is <radical>rejoic</radical>ing. She must be <kanji>glad</kanji> and <kanji>happy</kanji> to rejoice out loud in front of you like this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8835', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AC%89', 40, 1, '2019-04-27T00:28:46.611455Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1775, 'kanji', '伺', '伺', 'Being a <radical>leader</radical> or <radical>director</radical> in a company means you have to <kanji>inquire</kanji> about lots of things. Have you started this yet? Have you finished that yet? Did you get that thing I sent you? Where''s my coffee? Basically if you''re a leader or director, your main job is to inquire about these very important things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1775', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BC%BA', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:18:57.200277Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T22:05:09.513752Z'),
(1776, 'kanji', '徹', '徹', 'You decide to <radical>loiter</radical> next to the <radical>trash</radical> under the <radical>moon</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> so the moon''s light will <kanji>penetrate</kanji> you. The sky is <kanji>clear</kanji>est in winter, and this spot next to the trash happens to get the most of it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1776', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%B9', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:24:41.143173Z', NULL, '2025-11-19T08:38:20.566406Z'),
(1777, 'kanji', '瀬', '瀬', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> hits a <radical>bundle</radical> of <radical>geoduck</radical>s, swirling around their weird shapes, and creating <kanji>rapids</kanji>! The rapids are so violent, some of the geoducks break out of the bundle and get washed into the <kanji>shallows</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1777', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%80%AC', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:25:28.5935Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(1778, 'kanji', '撤', '撤', 'You use your <radical>fingers</radical> to dig through the <radical>trash</radical> in the light of the <radical>moon</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> because somewhere in there is your <kanji>withdrawal</kanji> letter. What did you withdraw? Your resignation? Your troops? Whatever it was, you need to find the proof, and fast.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1778', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%92%A4', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:25:59.00776Z', NULL, '2025-12-18T04:02:24.333593Z'),
(1779, 'kanji', '措', '措', 'You find some <radical>fingers</radical> from <radical>long ago</radical>. They must have been <kanji>set aside</kanji> by their previous owners. They definitely weren''t cut off or anything, someone just decided they didn''t want them anymore and set them aside.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1779', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8E%AA', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:26:24.458651Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:36:10.904881Z'),
(1780, 'kanji', '拠', '拠', 'Use your <radical>fingers</radical> to wipe this <radical>winter</radical> <radical>table</radical> and see what it was <kanji>based on</kanji>. The cold winter weather covers the table in frost, and when you wipe it away, you can see traces of what the table was originally based on. All tables have to be based on something, even if it''s just other tables that came before them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1780', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8B%A0', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:26:46.457914Z', NULL, '2025-12-18T04:02:24.350832Z'),
(1781, 'kanji', '儀', '儀', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of <radical>righteousness</radical> is the only one who can run this <kanji>ceremony</kanji>. Why? Because it''s a very pure ceremony, and only those with an extremely righteous mind can run it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1781', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%84%80', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:30:46.296878Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.452185Z'),
(1782, 'kanji', '樹', '樹', 'Each <radical>tree</radical> needs its <radical>drum</radical> <radical>measurement</radical> done before you can say it''s really made of <kanji>wood</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1782', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A8%B9', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:31:23.108114Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.454577Z'),
(1783, 'kanji', '棄', '棄', 'In the <radical>trash</radical> is a weird looking <radical>fruit</radical>. Someone decided to <kanji>abandon</kanji> it here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1783', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A3%84', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:31:52.654329Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:29:27.171851Z'),
(1784, 'kanji', '虎', '虎', 'A <radical>tiger</radical> has <radical>legs</radical> that belong to a <kanji>tiger</kanji>. You know, a tiger has the legs of a tiger. This is common knowledge. Nothing new here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1784', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%99%8E', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:32:12.186215Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T16:24:29.442417Z'),
(1785, 'kanji', '蛍', '蛍', 'A <radical>Viking</radical> <radical>insect</radical> is a <kanji>firefly</kanji>. In the insect kingdom, the insect most resembling a Viking is undoubtedly the firefly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1785', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%9B%8D', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:32:30.884345Z', NULL, '2025-12-21T21:30:23.63955Z'),
(1790, 'kanji', '艦', '艦', 'The <radical>boat</radical> that <radical>oversee</radical>s other boats, land, and people is usually a <kanji>warship</kanji>. They''re the big boats that oversee everything around them. And if anyone steps out of line, a warship can take care of it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1790', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%89%A6', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:34:30.496126Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.512329Z'),
(1791, 'kanji', '潜', '潜', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> carries two <radical>husband</radical>s up into the <radical>sun</radical>, where they''re going to <kanji>conceal</kanji> something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1791', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BD%9C', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:35:19.309366Z', NULL, '2026-01-14T17:48:29.910225Z'),
(1792, 'kanji', '拳', '拳', 'A <radical>gladiator</radical> turns his <radical>hand</radical> into a <kanji>fist</kanji>. A fist for punching other gladiators.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1792', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8B%B3', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:35:43.68859Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:28:25.718985Z'),
(1793, 'kanji', '炭', '炭', 'You climb up a <radical>mountain</radical> and a <radical>cliff</radical> in search of <radical>fire</radical>, but all you find is <kanji>charcoal</kanji>. You can make fire with the charcoal, but you were hoping to find fire itself. All that climbing for nothing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1793', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%82%AD', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:36:08.454956Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.527219Z'),
(1795, 'kanji', '包', '包', 'The <radical>wrap</radical> radical and the <kanji>wrap</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1795', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8C%85', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:36:52.302676Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.535333Z'),
(1797, 'kanji', '仁', '仁', 'This <radical>leader</radical> has <radical>two</radical> things that remind him of his <kanji>humanity</kanji>: his shoes, and his hat. These two things remind the leader of his humanity because humans are the only ones who wear shoes and hats.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1797', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%81', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:37:37.772709Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.553789Z'),
(1798, 'kanji', '鉱', '鉱', 'Aside from <radical>gold</radical> there are a <radical>wide</radical> variety of other <kanji>mineral</kanji>s as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1798', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%89%B1', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:40:50.262908Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.559382Z'),
(1799, 'kanji', '至', '至', 'Dig up a <radical>mole</radical> and you <kanji>attain</kanji> a mole status. You''re always trying to attain a new level for yourself and now you get to be on the mole level!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1799', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%87%B3', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:41:08.85201Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.563914Z'),
(1800, 'kanji', '誠', '誠', 'Something you <radical>say</radical> to someone you <radical>become</radical> attached to should be said with <kanji>sincerity</kanji>. You need to speak with sincerity when you say things to someone who will eventually become your significant other, for example.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1800', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AA%A0', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:41:30.019129Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:31:18.725502Z'),
(1801, 'kanji', '郷', '郷', 'Everyone has a <radical>poop</radical> <radical>guy</radical> from their <kanji>hometown</kanji>. You know. That poop guy. And every single time you try to visit your hometown, poop guy is there making everyone uncomfortable and reminding you why you left in the first place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1801', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%83%B7', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:41:53.906126Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.581736Z'),
(1802, 'kanji', '侵', '侵', 'There is a tiny <radical>leader</radical> on <radical>Wolverine</radical>''s <radical>forehead</radical> sitting on a <radical>stool</radical> and commanding his troops to <kanji>invade</kanji> Wolverine''s brain. These tiny soldiers are going to invade Wolverine''s body in an attempt to destroy him. The leader himself isn''t going to invade Wolverine''s brain, he''s just going to sit on the stool and supervise.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1802', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BE%B5', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:42:18.065642Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T00:07:07.792943Z'),
(1803, 'kanji', '偽', '偽', 'The <radical>leader</radical> gets a nice uniform for the <radical>sake</radical> of not wanting to look like a <kanji>fake</kanji>. The leader is a fake, he just doesn’t want to look like one. He’s not really a leader, but he thinks he should get an official-looking uniform for the sake of not looking like a fake.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1803', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%81%BD', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:42:44.708661Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.593313Z'),
(1804, 'kanji', '克', '克', 'Even though you’ve <radical>old</radical> <radical>legs</radical>, you <kanji>overcome</kanji> your disability. These old legs of yours don’t work very well, but you overcome the odds and you run a marathon!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1804', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%85%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:12:09.08507Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:13:37.694956Z'),
(1805, 'kanji', '到', '到', 'The <radical>mole</radical> has a <radical>knife</radical> ready for your <kanji>arrival</kanji>. You get out of your airplane, you step on the ground and... ouch! You stepped right on the knife, and there''s the mole, sticking it out of the ground. "I checked the schedule for your arrival!" it squeaks happily, and disappears.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1805', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%B0', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:12:29.689261Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:02:07.017379Z'),
(1806, 'kanji', '双', '双', 'If you have one <radical>stool</radical> and another <radical>stool</radical>, you have a <kanji>pair</kanji> of stools. Look at this pair of stools. Look at it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1806', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%8C', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:12:49.309642Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.61006Z'),
(1807, 'kanji', '哲', '哲', 'Wrap your <radical>fingers</radical> around this <radical>axe</radical> and put it in your <radical>mouth</radical> to express your <kanji>philosophy</kanji>. Your philosophy of… putting axes in your mouth. Everyone else seems to think it''s a stupid philosophy, but by doing this you''re showing them that it''s not!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1807', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%93%B2', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:13:12.326673Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:46:41.569111Z'),
(1808, 'kanji', '喪', '喪', 'Under the <radical>cross</radical> is a <radical>mouth</radical> and another <radical>mouth</radical> wearing special <radical>clothes</radical> because they''re in <kanji>mourning</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1808', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%96%AA', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:13:43.92746Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.641598Z'),
(1809, 'kanji', '堅', '堅', 'A <radical>servant</radical> secures a <radical>stool</radical> in the <radical>dirt</radical> so that it is a <kanji>solid</kanji> sitting device. If you just throw a stool on the ground it will flop over easily. But if you have a servant to dig it into the dirt a bit, it becomes nice and solid and safe for sitting!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1809', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A0%85', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:14:07.699879Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.644686Z'),
(1811, 'kanji', '括', '括', 'Someone''s <radical>fingers</radical> touch your <radical>tongue</radical> and <kanji>fasten</kanji> it together. You can''t talk. All you can do is make sad tongue-less sounds as you try to figure out who would fasten your tongue like this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1811', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8B%AC', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:14:52.326555Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T00:09:32.792313Z'),
(1812, 'kanji', '弧', '弧', 'Using a <radical>bow</radical>, shoot a <radical>melon</radical> and it will fly in a steep <kanji>arc</kanji>. Melons are too heavy to fly straight, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1812', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%A7', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:15:17.29793Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:48:00.001916Z'),
(1813, 'kanji', '挑', '挑', 'When your <radical>fingers</radical> move like that, you know it''s an <radical>omen</radical> of a <kanji>challenge</kanji> that will face you soon in the future.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1813', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8C%91', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:15:36.908513Z', NULL, '2025-12-07T19:03:33.969405Z'),
(1814, 'kanji', '掘', '掘', 'You see <radical>fingers</radical> on a <radical>flag</radical> by the <radical>exit</radical>, indicating that you should <kanji>dig</kanji>. The fingers on the flag point downward, just by the exit, as if to say "dig here!" There must be something under here. That''s what you think to yourself as you start to dig with your fingers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1814', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8E%98', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:16:01.205603Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:50:46.123245Z'),
(1815, 'kanji', '揚', '揚', 'If you have <radical>fingers</radical> it''s <radical>easy</radical> to <kanji>hoist</kanji> things above your head. If you don''t have fingers, well, it''s not so easy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1815', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8F%9A', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:16:22.799573Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:42:49.284419Z'),
(1816, 'kanji', '握', '握', 'You use your <radical>fingers</radical> in a <radical>shop</radical> to <kanji>grip</kanji> what you want to buy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1816', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8F%A1', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:16:45.305913Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:03:26.496694Z'),
(1817, 'kanji', '揺', '揺', 'With your <radical>fingers</radical>, you grab a <radical>satellite</radical> and <kanji>shake</kanji> it! You''re an astronaut and it''s your job. Don''t worry, the satellite won''t break or anything, it''s strong enough for space travel. Just do as you''re told and shake the sucker.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1817', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8F%BA', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:17:21.120833Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:52:31.368051Z'),
(1818, 'kanji', '斎', '斎', 'Use <radical>simultaneous</radical> <radical>jackhammer</radical>s to perform the <kanji>purification</kanji> ritual. Two jackhammers running perfectly in sync will purify your body, mind, and soul! (And the ground around you that''s being jackhammered.)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1818', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%96%8E', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:17:45.187629Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.703498Z'),
(1819, 'kanji', '暫', '暫', 'To your <radical>car</radical> you take an <radical>axe</radical>. The <radical>sun</radical> has broken your car for good this time (you think). You''re so angry at your car. You think this is permanent. Turns out, though, that it''s not permanent. It''s just like that <kanji>temporarily</kanji>. You did make it permanent by taking an axe to your car, though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1819', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9A%AB', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:18:07.283634Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T00:04:25.530844Z'),
(1820, 'kanji', '析', '析', 'You hit a <radical>tree</radical> with an <radical>axe</radical> so you can do some <kanji>analysis</kanji> on it. To do the analysis on the tree, a cross section needs to be obtained, so you must chop it in half with your axe.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1820', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9E%90', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:18:30.000952Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.730424Z'),
(1821, 'kanji', '枢', '枢', 'In the <radical>tree</radical> <radical>district</radical> of town you can buy a lot of tree products, including doors. With those doors, though, you always need <kanji>hinge</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1821', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9E%A2', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:18:53.541022Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.731587Z'),
(1822, 'kanji', '軸', '軸', 'A <radical>car</radical> works for one <radical>reason</radical>: its wheels can spin on an <kanji>axis</kanji>. Yep, that lateral axis is definitely a car''s most <kanji>pivotal part</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1822', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BB%B8', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:19:14.130588Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T19:50:01.269513Z'),
(1823, 'kanji', '柄', '柄', 'You walk up to a <radical>tree</radical> and put <radical>dynamite</radical> on it. Then you set it off. BOOM. It explodes, but doesn''t do what you''d expect. Instead of blowing the tree up, it blasts a very beautiful <kanji>pattern</kanji> into the side of it as well as the ground around it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1823', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9F%84', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:26:32.041736Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:44:06.861329Z'),
(1824, 'kanji', '泊', '泊', 'The <radical>tsunami</radical> that is <radical>white</radical> is the most scary tsunami of all. It doesn''t come in and destroy everything. No, it comes in, goes inside your door, then stays at your place <kanji>overnight</kanji>, without even asking!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1824', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B3%8A', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:29:55.995736Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T23:31:10.353244Z'),
(1825, 'kanji', '滑', '滑', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> breaks your <radical>bone</radical>s, not from hitting you, but from making you <kanji>slide</kanji> and <kanji>slip</kanji> into stuff. Hard to keep your footing in a tsunami, you know? You''re slipping and sliding all over the place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1825', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BB%91', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:30:43.350509Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:02:28.008374Z'),
(1826, 'kanji', '潟', '潟', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> crashes into the <radical>sun</radical> (breaking it in half) and creating a different <radical>wing</radical> of the tsunami all together. This wing crashes down and creates a <kanji>lagoon</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1826', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BD%9F', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:31:06.177723Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.769544Z'),
(1827, 'kanji', '焦', '焦', 'You take a <radical>turkey</radical> and try to <radical>boil</radical> it but you <kanji>char</kanji> it instead. That''s what happens when you throw meat into a pot on high with no water in there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1827', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%84%A6', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:31:42.726724Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(1828, 'kanji', '範', '範', 'This <radical>bamboo</radical> can be whittled into an <kanji>example</kanji> of anything. See, here''s a <radical>car</radical>. Here''s a <radical>snake</radical>. I can make so many examples just with bamboo.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1828', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AF%84', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:32:05.996045Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.782385Z'),
(1829, 'kanji', '紛', '紛', 'Take this <radical>thread</radical> and look at this <radical>part</radical>. Now look at this part! Now look at this part. You don''t know where to look! Haha, I have <kanji>distract</kanji>ed you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1829', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%9B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:32:26.51449Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T02:27:23.570078Z'),
(1830, 'kanji', '糾', '糾', '<radical>Thread</radical> and <radical>vines</radical> alone are too weak to hold anything up, but if you <kanji>twist together</kanji> both of them, you can make something much stronger.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1830', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B3%BE', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:32:52.71289Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:48:40.380794Z'),
(1831, 'kanji', '綱', '綱', 'If you take a <radical>thread</radical> and wrap up a bunch of it, so much it becomes a <radical>hill</radical> of thread, you''re going to have a lot of thread. You can wrap some of that thread around other thread to create a much thicker thread. This thread is a <kanji>rope</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1831', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B6%B1', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:33:19.816378Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.819184Z'),
(1832, 'kanji', '網', '網', 'You see a <radical>thread</radical>-covered <radical>hill</radical>, but it''s all lumpy and weird. You approach it to take a closer look, and you see it''s actually a huge <kanji>net</kanji> with a bunch of stuff caught in it: fish, cats, tree branches. All this random stuff is what''s making the net look so lumpy!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1832', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B6%B2', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:33:39.600667Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.833657Z'),
(1833, 'kanji', '肝', '肝', 'If the <radical>moon</radical> is <radical>dry</radical>, it''s good for your <kanji>liver</kanji>! Haha, get it? Because there''s no moonshine to ruin your liver. Don''t drink, kids.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1833', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%82%9D', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:36:28.847225Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.834219Z'),
(1834, 'kanji', '芝', '芝', 'There are <radical>flowers</radical> and <radical>hills</radical> in your front <kanji>lawn</kanji>. Your lawn is pretty big and it is covered with both flowers and hills. It’s very pretty.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1834', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8A%9D', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:37:30.914776Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.834808Z'),
(1835, 'kanji', '荒', '荒', '<radical>Flowers</radical> find their <radical>death</radical> in a <radical>river</radical> out in the <kanji>wild</kanji>. Flowers live on water, but this river drowns the flowers to death. The wild is a dangerous place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1835', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8D%92', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:37:52.414318Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:04:12.39697Z'),
(1836, 'kanji', '袋', '袋', 'You keep your <radical>substitute</radical> <radical>clothes</radical> in a <kanji>sack</kanji>. The clothes in the sack are just substitute clothes, so they only come out in emergencies.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1836', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A2%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:38:18.114224Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.841362Z'),
(1838, 'kanji', '珍', '珍', 'The <radical>king</radical>''s <radical>hat</radical>, not to mention the <radical>hair</radical> stuck to said hat, is incredibly <kanji>rare</kanji>. If you have your hands on either of these things, you''ll be a rich duck.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1838', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8F%8D', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:27:06.421956Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:53:53.146069Z'),
(1839, 'kanji', '裂', '裂', 'When a <radical>yakuza</radical> takes a <radical>knife</radical> to <radical>clothes</radical>, it will of course cause a <kanji>split</kanji> or <kanji>tear</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1839', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A3%82', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:28:26.253489Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T00:59:25.552609Z'),
(1840, 'kanji', '襲', '襲', '<radical>Imperial</radical> <radical>clothes</radical> allow you to perform an ancient <kanji>attack</kanji> that cannot be repelled.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1840', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A5%B2', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:28:48.000879Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.865771Z'),
(1841, 'kanji', '貢', '貢', 'This <radical>construction</radical> project made from <radical>shellfish</radical> will act as a <kanji>tribute</kanji> to the Crabigator.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1841', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%A2', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:29:06.745068Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.896236Z'),
(1842, 'kanji', '趣', '趣', 'You have to <radical>run</radical>, put your <radical>ear</radical> on the <radical>stool</radical>, then run back. That''s the <kanji>charm</kanji> of this game.

', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1842', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B6%A3', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:29:29.981184Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:38:57.03621Z'),
(1843, 'kanji', '距', '距', 'A <radical>foot</radical> on a <radical>giant</radical> is a pretty long <kanji>distance</kanji> for a normal person to walk. Giants have big feet and can travel large <kanji>distance</kanji>s very quickly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1843', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B7%9D', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:29:53.694131Z', NULL, '2026-01-05T23:19:07.724287Z'),
(1844, 'kanji', '籍', '籍', 'People had to use their <radical>bamboo</radical> <radical>plow</radical>s <radical>long ago</radical> to put their name in a <kanji>register</kanji>. This is because there was no paper yet, so they would hitch a cow to their bamboo plow, and dig their names into the dirt to create all kinds of registers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1844', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B1%8D', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:30:14.795963Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:07:26.033458Z'),
(1845, 'kanji', '露', '露', 'If the <radical>rain</radical> touches your <radical>foot</radical> and gives it a little rain <radical>kiss</radical>, you know that part of your foot is <kanji>expose</kanji>d to the elements. You can also see some <kanji>dew</kanji> forming on your exposed foot. So that''s what rain kisses are!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1845', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9C%B2', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:30:42.776656Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:20:45.121776Z'),
(1846, 'kanji', '牧', '牧', 'A <radical>cow</radical> in the <radical>winter</radical> doesn''t like to leave the barn to go to the <kanji>pasture</kanji>. It has to, though, because a cow''s gotta eat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1846', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%89%A7', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:31:01.861866Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:37:01.42735Z'),
(1847, 'kanji', '刷', '刷', 'You take out a <radical>flag</radical>, lay a <radical>towel</radical> below it, and use your <radical>knife</radical> to carve the pattern through them both. Voila! This is exactly how old <kanji>printing</kanji> presses used to work.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1847', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%B7', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:31:22.203206Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.901578Z'),
(1848, 'kanji', '朗', '朗', 'A <radical>good</radical> <radical>moon</radical> is a <kanji>bright</kanji> moon. Not just in terms of light, but in mood as well. Like, gee wiz, what a bright, happy moon.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1848', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%97', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:31:43.877553Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.901121Z'),
(1849, 'kanji', '潮', '潮', 'A small <radical>tsunami</radical> comes in the <radical>morning</radical> with the <radical>moon</radical>. That just means the ocean gets bigger when the moon''s out, and that my friends is simply the <kanji>tide</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1849', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BD%AE', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:32:03.409646Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.924884Z'),
(1850, 'kanji', '即', '即', 'The <radical>root</radical> of a <radical>stamp</radical> is to get an <kanji>instant</kanji> picture down. Back in the olden days, people had to make seals and draw out things by hand, so stamps came about so you can—BAM—just stamp it and have what you want in an instant.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1850', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%B3', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:32:25.680671Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:15:32.847277Z'),
(1851, 'kanji', '垣', '垣', 'Look at the <radical>dirt</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical> at <radical>dawn</radical> and you''ll be able to see all the <kanji>hedge</kanji>s growing in the area really well. At any other time of day, hedges tend to blend into their surroundings. But at dawn, the shadows the sun makes really helps them stand out.

This hedge also works as a <kanji>fence</kanji>. It borders an area and you can''t walk through it. A natural fence!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1851', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9E%A3', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:32:58.402088Z', NULL, '2026-03-22T00:33:38.652024Z'),
(1852, 'kanji', '威', '威', 'A <radical>canine</radical> and a <radical>woman</radical> stand before you, radiating <kanji>dignity</kanji> and <kanji>might</kanji>. Who is this majestic pair?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1852', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A8%81', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:33:19.152168Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1853, 'kanji', '封', '封', 'You take two clumps of <radical>dirt</radical> to make a <radical>measurement</radical>, using them to <kanji>seal</kanji> each side of your measuring tape, so it stays on the ground. This is the way people used to <kanji>seal in</kanji> things back in the day, back when we didn''t have fancy things like tape and glue.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1853', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%81', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:33:41.753401Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:57:46.838295Z'),
(1854, 'kanji', '筒', '筒', 'A <radical>bamboo</radical> stalk looks the <radical>same</radical> as a big ol'' <kanji>cylinder</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1854', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AD%92', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:34:05.712215Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.960227Z'),
(1856, 'kanji', '慰', '慰', 'We put up a <radical>flag</radical> to remember where we tried to use a <radical>jackhammer</radical> to take a <radical>measurement</radical> of your friend''s <radical>heart</radical>. Granted, it was a terrible idea, but hopefully that flag of remembrance is some kind of <kanji>consolation</kanji>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1856', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%85%B0', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:35:05.056829Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:59:33.212946Z'),
(1857, 'kanji', '懇', '懇', 'A <radical>badger</radical>, at the <radical>root</radical> of its <radical>heart</radical>, is a <kanji>courteous</kanji> creature.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1857', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%87%87', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:35:27.068397Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:15:32.847277Z'),
(1858, 'kanji', '懲', '懲', 'The <radical>loiter</radical>ing <radical>mountain</radical> <radical>king</radical> who hangs around during <radical>winter</radical> to provide an indication (this kanji uses the "indication" kanji as its top 2/3rds) of things to come now looks into your <radical>heart</radical>, and only sees bad things. So, he <kanji>chastise</kanji>s you about it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1858', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%87%B2', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:35:57.962414Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.983885Z'),
(1859, 'kanji', '摩', '摩', 'Below the <radical>canopy</radical> of <radical>tree</radical>s is a <radical>hand</radical>. The hand is just there to <kanji>chafe</kanji> against the trees.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1859', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%91%A9', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:36:19.014131Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.999191Z'),
(1860, 'kanji', '擦', '擦', 'You use your <radical>fingers</radical> to hold the <radical>roof</radical> of the <radical>festival</radical> up, but it <kanji>rub</kanji>s against them. The roof is heavy, and you have to walk to keep up, so the roof is rubbing against them really, really badly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1860', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%93%A6', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:36:43.727285Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.017093Z'),
(1861, 'kanji', '撲', '撲', 'Put your <radical>fingers</radical> in somebody''s <radical>business</radical> by <kanji>slap</kanji>ping them! This method also works if someone is getting too involved in your business. Slaps are basically your way of doing business with your fingers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1861', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%92%B2', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:37:06.412182Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:55:35.269777Z'),
(1862, 'kanji', '斉', '斉', 'The <radical>simultaneous</radical> radical and the <kanji>simultaneous</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1862', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%96%89', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:37:38.042791Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T03:09:32.173523Z'),
(1863, 'kanji', '旨', '旨', 'Take your <radical>spoon</radical> to the <radical>sun</radical> to be able to take a bite. It''s <kanji>delicious</kanji>, you hear. That''s the <kanji>point</kanji> of the spoon.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1863', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%97%A8', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:37:57.487452Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.020919Z'),
(1864, 'kanji', '柔', '柔', 'You''ve been ordered to <radical>spear</radical> this <radical>tree</radical> but you decide to be really <kanji>gentle</kanji> instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1864', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9F%94', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:38:18.252613Z', NULL, '2025-12-29T21:44:43.813138Z'),
(1865, 'kanji', '沈', '沈', 'If a <radical>tsunami</radical> hits a <radical>pirate</radical> they are going to <kanji>sink</kanji> no matter how good of a pirate captain they are.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1865', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B2%88', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:38:48.510124Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T00:54:05.459439Z'),
(1867, 'kanji', '泰', '泰', 'The sound of <radical>spring</radical> <radical>water</radical> always puts you at <kanji>peace</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1867', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B3%B0', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:39:34.585328Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:22:51.842327Z'),
(1868, 'kanji', '滅', '滅', 'You''re struck by a <radical>tsunami</radical>, mauled by a <radical>canine</radical>, and then set on <radical>fire</radical>. It seems the universe is trying to <kanji>destroy</kanji> you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1868', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BB%85', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:40:08.603329Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:36.553495Z'),
(1869, 'kanji', '滋', '滋', 'A (fresh water) <radical>tsunami</radical>. Some <radical>horns</radical>. The <radical>ground</radical>. A bunch of <radical>poop</radical>. Combine these together and let them decompose a while. You''ll have yourself some very <kanji>nourishing</kanji> soil.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1869', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BB%8B', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:40:47.234228Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T02:07:07.890877Z'),
(1870, 'kanji', '炉', '炉', 'A <radical>fire</radical> <radical>door</radical> is a door that leads to something full of fire. That thing is a <kanji>furnace</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1870', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%82%89', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:41:06.511979Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.088926Z'),
(1871, 'kanji', '琴', '琴', 'Two <radical>king</radical>s are here right <radical>now</radical>! We need to entertain them otherwise who knows what they''ll do. Get out the <kanji>harp</kanji>, or the <kanji>zither</kanji>, or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1871', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%90%B4', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:41:27.6559Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.096717Z'),
(2115, 'kanji', '晶', '晶', 'The sun hits this and breaks into three <radical>sun</radical>s. What can split the sun in three directions? A <kanji>crystal</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2115', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%99%B6', 43, 1, '2013-08-19T18:36:37.770583Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.786109Z'),
(1872, 'kanji', '寸', '寸', 'The <radical>measurement</radical> radical and the <kanji>measurement</kanji> kanji are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1872', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%B8', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:08:24.95982Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.110943Z'),
(1873, 'kanji', '竜', '竜', 'The <radical>dragon</radical> radical is the same as this kanji, which is <kanji>dragon</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1873', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AB%9C', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:08:44.11975Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.126796Z'),
(1874, 'kanji', '縁', '縁', 'A <radical>thread</radical> is all that''s keeping <radical>Wolverine</radical> and a <radical>pig</radical> from falling off the <kanji>edge</kanji> of a cliff. Wolverine and this pig had no <kanji>relation</kanji> prior to this, but after such a harrowing experience they''re sure to form a deep bond. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1874', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B8%81', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:09:17.600092Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:33:18.509083Z'),
(1875, 'kanji', '翼', '翼', 'A <radical>feather</radical> is on a <radical>rice paddy</radical>, and they are attached <radical>together</radical>. You put a feather on a rice paddy to create a giant airplane. Flawed, sure. But, this rice paddy + feather is going to be one <kanji>wing</kanji> of your amazing aircraft.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1875', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BF%BC', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:09:39.863981Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:59:46.609728Z'),
(1876, 'kanji', '吉', '吉', 'The <radical>good luck</radical> radical and the <kanji>good luck</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1876', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%89', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:10:00.645505Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.145014Z'),
(1877, 'kanji', '刃', '刃', 'The radical <radical>blade</radical> and the kanji <kanji>blade</kanji> are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1877', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%88%83', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:10:19.506713Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:18:22.555288Z'),
(1878, 'kanji', '忍', '忍', 'If someone puts a <radical>blade</radical> to your <radical>heart</radical>, you''re going to have a rough time. They leave it there, for a long time. They aren''t going to cut you, but you can''t move. You have to <kanji>endure</kanji> the pain and <kanji>conceal</kanji> your discomfort with the situation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1878', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BF%8D', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:10:39.66748Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:37:44.3265Z'),
(1879, 'kanji', '桃', '桃', 'The <radical>tree</radical> has an <radical>omen</radical> growing from it, one single <kanji>peach</kanji> that is the size of a house. Inside is a child who will save the village from demons.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1879', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A1%83', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:11:00.502765Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.16351Z'),
(1882, 'kanji', '侍', '侍', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of the <radical>temple</radical> isn''t a monk like you''d expect. It''s a <kanji>samurai</kanji>, who protects the monks in return for prayers that keep him safe.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1882', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BE%8D', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:13:13.531837Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.191434Z'),
(1885, 'kanji', '娯', '娯', 'A <radical>woman</radical> loves her <radical>catapult</radical> for some good ol'' fashioned <kanji>recreation</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1885', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A8%AF', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:14:29.605431Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1886, 'kanji', '斗', '斗', 'The <radical>ladle</radical> radical and the <kanji>ladle</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1886', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%96%97', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:15:41.497888Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.206644Z'),
(1887, 'kanji', '朱', '朱', 'The <radical>slide</radical> to get off this <radical>jet</radical> is a beautiful, brilliant <kanji>vermilion</kanji> color.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1887', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%B1', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:15:59.899277Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:06:23.045493Z'),
(1888, 'kanji', '丘', '丘', 'An <radical>axe</radical> is stuck into the <radical>ground</radical> on a <kanji>hill</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1888', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%98', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:16:29.325175Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.228537Z'),
(1889, 'kanji', '梨', '梨', 'The <radical>grain</radical> you cut off a <radical>tree</radical> with a <radical>knife</radical> is a <kanji>pear</kanji>. You''ve found some particularly big, sturdy ones, which is why you need the help of your knife. These pears are sure to be super juicy and delicious, but you need some extra tools to get them off the tree.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1889', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A2%A8', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:16:55.716615Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:51:00.791523Z'),
(1892, 'kanji', '叫', '叫', 'You have <radical>mouth</radical> <radical>vines</radical>, so you <kanji>shout</kanji>! It''s hard for you to talk normally, and even harder for people to understand you unless you shout.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1892', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%AB', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:17:53.964842Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.25426Z'),
(1893, 'kanji', '釣', '釣', 'A <radical>gold</radical> <radical>pool</radical> is where you''re going to go <kanji>fishing</kanji>. Because, if the pool is gold, hopefully the fish are too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1893', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%87%A3', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:18:11.3891Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:49:14.754352Z'),
(1894, 'kanji', '髪', '髪', 'Your <radical>long</radical> <radical>hair</radical> is your <radical>friend</radical>. It''s your <kanji>hair</kanji> and you''re so proud of it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1894', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%AB%AA', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:18:30.811338Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.27179Z'),
(1895, 'kanji', '嵐', '嵐', 'The <radical>mountain</radical> <radical>wind</radical> coming down from the mountains is very fierce. It is so fierce it''s a lot like a <kanji>storm</kanji>. Well, it is a storm, really.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1895', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B5%90', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:18:50.387896Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.271038Z'),
(1899, 'kanji', '姫', '姫', 'The <radical>woman</radical> <radical>servant</radical> is the lower ranking <kanji>princess</kanji>. The queen is no servant. Hecks no. Just the princess, because she''s a servant to what her parents tell her to do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1899', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A7%AB', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:20:07.658481Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1901, 'kanji', '粒', '粒', 'The part of <radical>rice</radical> that <radical>stand</radical>s up tall in the air (at the end of the stalks) is the <kanji>grains</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1901', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B2%92', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:20:44.933496Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.31081Z'),
(1902, 'kanji', '砲', '砲', 'You put a <radical>stone</radical> into a <radical>wrap</radical> of some kind. This wrap happens to be a <kanji>cannon</kanji>, and you''re shooting off the stone, just like I used to do to boats with the cannon we had growing up for some reason.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1902', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A0%B2', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:21:06.576828Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.317869Z'),
(1903, 'kanji', '雷', '雷', '<radical>Rain</radical> on a <radical>rice paddy</radical> means only one thing... <kanji>thunder</kanji>! Get off the rice paddy! There''s nothing but you to attract the thunder!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1903', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%B7', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:21:24.663863Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.335009Z'),
(1904, 'kanji', '芽', '芽', 'A <radical>flowers</radical> <radical>fang</radical> is the bud that comes out of the ground. It''s like a little fang, coming from the earth, and it will become a flower someday. This is a little <kanji>sprout</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1904', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8A%BD', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:21:46.566457Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.33689Z'),
(1905, 'kanji', '塔', '塔', 'When <radical>dirt</radical> and <radical>flowers</radical> <radical>suit</radical> one another, the flowers grow so tall that they make a <kanji>tower</kanji>! A flower tower made of flower power!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1905', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A1%94', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:22:09.08346Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.340733Z'),
(9312, 'kanji', '肢', '肢', 'Looking at the <radical>moon</radical>, you notice it''s grown a bunch of <radical>branch</radical>es. Wait, are those all… <kanji>limbs</kanji>?!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9312', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%82%A2', 44, 1, '2023-09-13T17:44:30.885046Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:34.611788Z'),
(1855, 'kanji', '岳', '岳', 'An <radical>axe</radical> is sticking in the <radical>ground</radical> on top of a <radical>mountain</radical>. It''s sticking into the very <kanji>peak</kanji>. Because why would you bother to stick your axe anywhere else on the mountain to prove you climbed it? ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1855', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B2%B3', 45, 1, '2013-07-02T21:34:37.233534Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:59.960914Z'),
(1906, 'kanji', '澄', '澄', 'When the <radical>tsunami</radical> struck, it hit your <radical>tent</radical> full of <radical>beans</radical>. When you lost your tent of beans, you had a moment of <kanji>lucidity</kanji>. You don''t need so much stuff, you can live without it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1906', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BE%84', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:42:18.427227Z', NULL, '2026-01-04T19:54:54.023836Z'),
(1907, 'kanji', '矛', '矛', 'The <radical>spear</radical> radical and the <kanji>spear</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1907', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9F%9B', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:42:38.051876Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.346952Z'),
(1908, 'kanji', '肌', '肌', 'The <radical>moon</radical> <radical>table</radical> is a table that''s made entirely of <kanji>skin</kanji>. You thought the table was made of moon, with all those pores and such, but when you look more closely, you realize it''s skin. Disgusting, porous, skin.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1908', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%82%8C', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:42:56.687651Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T23:12:17.923479Z'),
(1909, 'kanji', '舟', '舟', 'The <radical>boat</radical> radical and the <kanji>boat</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1909', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%88%9F', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:43:14.548889Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.377085Z'),
(1910, 'kanji', '鐘', '鐘', 'Something made of <radical>gold</radical> <radical>stand</radical>s in a <radical>village</radical>. It''s a <kanji>bell</kanji>!  The village bell.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1910', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%90%98', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:43:36.385205Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.398444Z'),
(1911, 'kanji', '凶', '凶', 'If there''s a <radical>treasure chest</radical>, there''s always a <kanji>villain</kanji> too. The villain is trying to get to the treasure chest before the hero of the story.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1911', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%87%B6', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:43:53.547449Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:26.034277Z'),
(1912, 'kanji', '塊', '塊', 'A <radical>dirt</radical> <radical>demon</radical> is just a <kanji>lump</kanji>. Just like a dust bunny is just a clump of dust.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1912', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A1%8A', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:44:14.988023Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.403597Z'),
(1913, 'kanji', '狩', '狩', 'I <radical>protect</radical> my own <radical>animal</radical>s by going to <kanji>hunt</kanji> other animals... like wolves and cougars and such. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1913', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8B%A9', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:44:32.735626Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:51:15.083936Z'),
(1915, 'kanji', '魂', '魂', 'The <radical>cloud</radical> floating above a <radical>demon</radical> has its <kanji>soul</kanji> in it. If you can manage to get through to the demon, to make it see that it doesn''t have to be evil, it might remember who it is and return to the <kanji>spirit</kanji> it once was.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1915', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%AD%82', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:45:49.427596Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.4125Z'),
(1916, 'kanji', '脚', '脚', 'If you''ve been to the <radical>moon</radical> in the <radical>past</radical>, you had to get the special <radical>stamp</radical> on your <kanji>leg</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1916', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%84%9A', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:46:29.56929Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.433571Z'),
(1917, 'kanji', '也', '也', 'An <radical>alligator</radical> is <kanji>considerably</kanji> dangerous. Considerably terrifying too. Considerably [put your scary adjective here].', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1917', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B9%9F', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:46:45.905806Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.440055Z'),
(1920, 'kanji', '嬢', '嬢', 'A <radical>woman</radical> who is a <radical>nurse</radical> should be called <kanji>miss</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1920', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AC%A2', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:47:51.741259Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(1921, 'kanji', '暦', '暦', 'Looking up to the <radical>cliff</radical>, you see the <radical>tree</radical>s, and the <radical>sun</radical> shining down on them. Using the sun and the trees (up there on the cliff), you''ve devised a method to tell what time of the year it is, based on where the shadow from the trees fall. After a while, you develop a bigger system. This system is a whole <kanji>calendar</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1921', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9A%A6', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:48:13.514058Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.464675Z'),
(1922, 'kanji', '曇', '曇', 'The <radical>sun</radical> is being blocked by a <radical>rain</radical> <radical>cloud</radical>, making it <kanji>cloudy</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1922', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9B%87', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:48:37.265254Z', NULL, '2025-10-23T04:08:23.657952Z'),
(1923, 'kanji', '眺', '眺', 'When your <radical>eye</radical> catches an <radical>omen</radical>, it''s only natural that you will <kanji>stare</kanji> at it. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1923', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9C%BA', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:48:56.226841Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.467144Z'),
(1924, 'kanji', '裸', '裸', 'If your <radical>outfit</radical> is just a piece of <radical>fruit</radical>, then you are <kanji>naked</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1924', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A3%B8', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:49:18.197419Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:29:09.085351Z'),
(1927, 'kanji', '塾', '塾', 'You <radical>receive</radical> instruction in a <radical>circle</radical> in the <radical>dirt</radical>. Where are you? <kanji>Cram school</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1927', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A1%BE', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:50:41.039822Z', NULL, '2025-11-09T20:00:23.698017Z'),
(1928, 'kanji', '卓', '卓', 'The <radical>toe</radical> is <radical>early</radical>. I hate it when the toes show up early. Well, if the toes are going to show up this early, I guess it''s best to put them on the <kanji>table</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1928', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%93', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:51:06.03391Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:51:28.189967Z'),
(1929, 'kanji', '磨', '磨', 'Below this <radical>canopy</radical> of <radical>tree</radical>s is a <radical>stone</radical>. Since it''s the only stone here, you make sure to <kanji>polish</kanji> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1929', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A3%A8', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:51:25.699103Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.535216Z'),
(1930, 'kanji', '菌', '菌', 'When you leave <radical>grain</radical>s in your <radical>mouth</radical> for long enough, they''ll turn into <radical>flowers</radical> of <kanji>bacteria</kanji>. These aren''t the nice, pretty flowers you''d give your mom on Mother''s Day. These are little bacteria flowers that are taking over your mouth. Maybe brush your teeth next time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1930', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8F%8C', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:51:48.904218Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.537004Z'),
(1931, 'kanji', '陰', '陰', 'This <radical>building</radical> <radical>now</radical> has a <radical>cloud</radical> over it, creating <kanji>shade</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1931', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%B0', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:52:14.34003Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.538978Z'),
(1932, 'kanji', '霊', '霊', 'Coming down with the <radical>rain</radical> is <radical>two</radical> <radical>spikes</radical>. They go right through two <kanji>ghost</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1932', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9C%8A', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:52:39.078199Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.536521Z'),
(1933, 'kanji', '湿', '湿', 'After the <radical>tsunami</radical> the <radical>sun</radical> comes out again and shines on these <radical>spikes</radical>. They were super wet before, but you figure the sun probably dried them off. You lean down to touch them and nope, they''s still <kanji>damp</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1933', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B9%BF', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:52:58.022343Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T18:16:21.459632Z'),
(1934, 'kanji', '硬', '硬', 'You feel like a <radical>stone</radical> that’s been <radical>renew</radical>ed. How do renewed stones feel? <kanji>Stiff</kanji>. Very stiff.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1934', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A1%AC', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:53:15.374308Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:14:20.621362Z'),
(1935, 'kanji', '稼', '稼', 'The <radical>grain</radical>s you have stored in your <radical>house</radical> reflect on your <kanji>earnings</kanji>. If you earn a lot of money, you have more grains.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1935', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A8%BC', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:53:36.077136Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:16:29.236334Z'),
(1936, 'kanji', '嫁', '嫁', 'The <radical>woman</radical> who makes your <radical>house</radical> a home is your <kanji>bride</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1936', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AB%81', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:54:02.742571Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:42:49.728503Z'),
(1937, 'kanji', '溝', '溝', '<radical>Tsunami</radical> vs <radical>lifeguard</radical>. Who will win? After the water drains away, we find the lifeguard in the <kanji>gutter</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1937', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BA%9D', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:54:24.865458Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.607598Z'),
(1938, 'kanji', '滝', '滝', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> in the shape of a <radical>dragon</radical> flies off the cliff, then comes crashing down to the ground. This skinny tsunami of water flying down to the ground is a <kanji>waterfall</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1938', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BB%9D', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:54:47.615778Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.608811Z'),
(1939, 'kanji', '狂', '狂', 'The <radical>animal</radical> <radical>king</radical>? He''s just this guy who lives in the forest and thinks he''s king of all the animals. He''s a <kanji>lunatic</kanji>, though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1939', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8B%82', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:55:03.641442Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:51:15.083936Z'),
(1940, 'kanji', '翔', '翔', 'A <radical>sheep</radical> with <radical>feathers</radical> (and presumably wings, too) is able to <kanji>fly</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1940', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BF%94', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T17:55:19.089661Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.613975Z'),
(1941, 'kanji', '墨', '墨', 'You use <radical>black</radical> <radical>dirt</radical> to make <kanji>black ink</kanji>. That''s what you use to write in black!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1941', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A2%A8', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T21:32:03.205131Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.616415Z'),
(1942, 'kanji', '鳩', '鳩', 'There are <radical>nine</radical> <radical>bird</radical>s and one of them is a <kanji>dove</kanji> and another is a <kanji>pigeon</kanji>. These nine birds are a flock of all kinds, but the dove and the pigeon are the leaders.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1942', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%B3%A9', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T21:32:39.945111Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.640125Z'),
(1943, 'kanji', '穏', '穏', 'Some <radical>grains</radical> get stuck in his <radical>cleat</radical>, and <radical>Wolverine</radical>''s <radical>heart</radical> grows <kanji>calm</kanji>. Wolverine had been screaming and yelling and stomping around, but when the grains entered his cleat he realized where he was. He was finally able to calm down.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1943', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A9%8F', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T21:33:29.68683Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:34:39.359876Z'),
(1944, 'kanji', '鈍', '鈍', 'Make <radical>barracks</radical> out of <radical>gold</radical> and they will get <kanji>dull</kanji> quickly. Gold is soft, it''s okay for jewelry and delicate things that aren''t used much, but barracks for soldiers? Those are used constantly, and they get worn out easily. What was once shiny gold will go dull in days.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1944', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%88%8D', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T21:33:44.720155Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.648278Z'),
(1945, 'kanji', '魔', '魔', 'Below the <radical>canopy</radical> of <radical>tree</radical>s is a <radical>demon</radical>. This demon is a <kanji>devil</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1945', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%AD%94', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T21:34:13.788315Z', NULL, '2025-12-23T02:16:59.508556Z'),
(1946, 'kanji', '寮', '寮', 'You need to bring <radical>charcoal</radical> under this <radical>roof</radical> to heat the <kanji>dormitory</kanji>. You live in this dormitory with a bunch of other kids, and you all have to take turns bringing in the charcoal from outside to heat it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1946', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%AE', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T21:34:34.861069Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:00.672551Z'),
(1947, 'kanji', '盆', '盆', 'You put <radical>part</radical> of your food on a <radical>plate</radical>, then put that on a <kanji>tray</kanji> to keep it safe. This tray is an offering for the <kanji>ancestral festival</kanji>, where your ancestors'' spirits visit you to check in and share some of that food you''re offering on a tray.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1947', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9B%86', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T21:34:57.817929Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.848225Z'),
(1948, 'kanji', '棟', '棟', 'There’s a <radical>tree</radical> in the <radical>east</radical> that looks like a <kanji>building</kanji>. The tree is tall and straight and doesn''t have any leaves or branches. It''s basically a big wooden building.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1948', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A3%9F', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T21:35:26.18215Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:09:25.99139Z'),
(1951, 'kanji', '寧', '寧', 'On the <radical>roof</radical> is your <radical>heart</radical>. OR! In a <radical>net</radical> in the <radical>street</radical>. Which would you <kanji>rather</kanji>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1951', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%A7', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T21:36:49.451258Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T01:07:41.176706Z'),
(1953, 'kanji', '歳', '歳', 'You have to <radical>stop</radical> keeping <radical>canine</radical>s which are <radical>small</radical> once you reach a certain number of <kanji>years old</kanji>. You''ll be hitting that age limit this <kanji>year</kanji>, which means your <kanji>time</kanji> with your small canine will end and you''ll have to grow up and get a bigger one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1953', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AD%B3', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T21:37:36.792529Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T07:27:04.979321Z'),
(1955, 'kanji', '猿', '猿', 'An <radical>animal</radical> that <radical>kick</radical>s <radical>dirt</radical> into people''s <radical>mouth</radical>s is a <kanji>monkey</kanji>. Monkeys are naughty little things. You should be happy it was just dirt it kicked into your mouth, and not something worse.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1955', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8C%BF', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T21:38:52.61733Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:51:15.083936Z'),
(1956, 'kanji', '瞳', '瞳', 'Inside your <radical>eye</radical> <radical>stand</radical>s a tiny <radical>village</radical> that makes up your <kanji>pupil</kanji>. That''s the black part of your eye!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/1956', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9E%B3', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T21:39:16.864052Z', NULL, '2025-11-01T00:52:43.855891Z')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;


If you want to save it you better <kanji>cover</kanji> up the black hole! The only way to save it from capsizing is to cover it right up!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2068', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A6%86', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:05:38.653049Z', NULL, '2025-10-19T17:57:34.51427Z'),
(2069, 'kanji', '謀', '謀', 'Watch what you <radical>say</radical> to what appears to be a <radical>sweet</radical> <radical>tree</radical>, because that tree will 100% <kanji>conspire</kanji> against you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2069', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AC%80', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:06:05.176792Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.481866Z'),
(2070, 'kanji', '陶', '陶', 'From a <radical>building</radical> next to a <radical>prison</radical>, you trade <radical>can</radical>s to the inmates for pottery. You collect <kanji>pottery</kanji> and other prisoner artwork, and they need metal cans to make shanks and stuff, so it''s a win-win arrangement.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2070', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%B6', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:06:39.281373Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T17:20:50.783352Z'),
(2071, 'kanji', '隔', '隔', 'A <radical>building</radical> that has a <radical>bully</radical> will <kanji>isolate</kanji> itself. Buildings have feelings like people, you know.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2071', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9A%94', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:07:12.880527Z', NULL, '2026-02-19T14:31:49.548097Z'),
(2072, 'kanji', '征', '征', 'If someone <radical>loiter</radical>s it''s <radical>correct</radical> to <kanji>subjugate</kanji> them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2072', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%81', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:07:35.744914Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:31:27.51168Z'),
(2073, 'kanji', '陛', '陛', '<radical>Compare</radical> a <radical>building</radical> to some <radical>dirt</radical> and you''ll see the difference between you and His and Her <kanji>Highness</kanji>. You live outside and sleep on the dirt. But His and Her Highness both live in a huge building.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2073', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%9B', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:08:00.81447Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.514454Z'),
(2074, 'kanji', '俗', '俗', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of this <radical>valley</radical> is very <kanji>vulgar</kanji>. The name of the valley is Vulgar Valley, so it makes sense for its leader to be very vulgar.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2074', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BF%97', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:08:26.212506Z', NULL, '2026-03-07T17:49:05.199222Z'),
(2075, 'kanji', '桑', '桑', 'You take a <radical>stool</radical>, another <radical>stool</radical>, and yet another <radical>stool</radical> and stack them on top of a <radical>tree</radical> to make a <kanji>mulberry</kanji> bush. This is how mulberry bushes are made. You take three stools and you put them atop a tree.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2075', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A1%91', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:08:51.962036Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.518989Z'),
(2076, 'kanji', '潤', '潤', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> at the <radical>gate</radical> of the <radical>king</radical> makes sure all of his plants get <kanji>watered</kanji>. The gate prevents the tsunami from damaging anything inside the king’s home, and the plants around the gate get watered by the water of the tsunami.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2076', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BD%A4', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:09:15.273373Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.532327Z'),
(2077, 'kanji', '珠', '珠', 'The <radical>king</radical> takes his <radical>slide</radical> into his private <radical>jet</radical> and is given a <kanji>pearl</kanji> as a gift, by you! He''s letting you ride in his jet today, and you were told the king should be presented with a pearl as soon as he boards.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2077', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8F%A0', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:09:37.708157Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:50:19.800847Z'),
(2078, 'kanji', '衰', '衰', 'You take a <radical>lid</radical>, and aiming it at the <radical>sun</radical> you <radical>kick</radical> it into the air and eventually it begins to <kanji>decline</kanji> and crashes into the sun, causing it to supernova. Imagine kicking this lid so hard that it flies into space. See it fly up to the peak of its arch, then watch as it begins the decline until it finally collides with the sun.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2078', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A1%B0', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:10:00.106837Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.541045Z'),
(2079, 'kanji', '奨', '奨', 'A <radical>commander</radical> who''s <radical>big</radical> will use his size to <kanji>encourage</kanji> his troops.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2079', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A5%A8', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:10:28.171447Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.546763Z'),
(2080, 'kanji', '劣', '劣', 'The <radical>few</radical> members of society without <radical>power</radical> suffer from severe <kanji>inferiority</kanji> complexes. Most people in this world have lots of power, so the few that do not feel quite inferior. It is this feeling of inferiority that fuels their inferiority complexes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2080', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8A%A3', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:10:49.696698Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:11:59.650433Z'),
(2081, 'kanji', '勘', '勘', 'This <radical>crab trap</radical> has the <radical>power</radical> of <kanji>intuition</kanji>. By the power of intuition, this crab trap can trap like, every crab. Through intuition, it knows where the crabs will go and how they will react, so it is much easier to trap them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2081', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8B%98', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:11:20.373758Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T07:54:52.716337Z'),
(2082, 'kanji', '妃', '妃', 'The <radical>woman</radical> one has to <radical>oneself</radical> is one''s <kanji>royal consort</kanji>… You know, assuming one is a member of the royalty. This is a woman who''s tied the knot with some royal person and been made into a <kanji>princess</kanji> or <kanji>queen</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2082', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A6%83', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:11:41.583013Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(2203, 'kanji', '准', '准', 'Put a <radical>turkey</radical> on <radical>ice</radical> and it will only get <kanji>semi</kanji> frozen. You add more ice, and it stays the same. Only some of it will freeze. What the heck!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2203', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%87%86', 49, 1, '2015-07-01T19:14:34.040353Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(2084, 'kanji', '峰', '峰', 'At the top of the <radical>mountain</radical> is a <radical>signpost</radical> to tell you you''ve reached the <kanji>summit</kanji>. Literally every mountain that humans have reached the top of has one of these summit signs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2084', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B3%B0', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:15:12.844944Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.572714Z'),
(2085, 'kanji', '巧', '巧', 'The <radical>construction</radical> of an under<radical>ground</radical> <radical>prison</radical> will be done by someone who is <kanji>adept</kanji> at it.

', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2085', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B7%A7', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:15:36.978903Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(2086, 'kanji', '邪', '邪', 'A <radical>fang</radical> on a <radical>building</radical> shows that it''s <kanji>wicked</kanji>. Something wicked probably lives in the building too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2086', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%82%AA', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:15:58.506943Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.588681Z'),
(2087, 'kanji', '駄', '駄', 'A <radical>horse</radical> that is <radical>fat</radical> is a <kanji>burdensome</kanji> animal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2087', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A7%84', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:21:32.862185Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:05:38.676611Z'),
(2089, 'kanji', '廷', '廷', 'The <radical>Yoga</radical> <radical>King</radical> presides over all <kanji>courts</kanji>: law, imperial, and so forth. In this case, you''ve met him in the court of law, where he is judge, jury, and executioner.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2089', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BB%B7', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:22:51.608217Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T19:04:16.361248Z'),
(2093, 'kanji', '簿', '簿', 'A <radical>bamboo</radical> <radical>tsunami</radical> uses its <radical>drop</radical>s to carry out its <radical>specialty</radical>, which is turning itself into <kanji>record book</kanji>s. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2093', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B0%BF', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:24:57.658651Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.627095Z'),
(2094, 'kanji', '彰', '彰', 'You want to write a <radical>chapter</radical> about <radical>hair</radical> in your new book so you have to make sure it''s really <kanji>clear</kanji>. Clear enough so that ANYbody can read it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2094', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BD%B0', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:25:28.429635Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T21:43:08.854836Z'),
(2095, 'kanji', '漫', '漫', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> crashes into the <radical>sun</radical>, which is then caught in a <radical>net</radical> and tied to a <radical>stool</radical>! Wait a second... this doesn''t sound realistic, it sounds really <kanji>comic</kanji>! That''s because it''s from that comic book about a pirate you''re always reading!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2095', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BC%AB', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:25:54.050009Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.643815Z'),
(2096, 'kanji', '訂', '訂', 'What you <radical>say</radical> on the <radical>street</radical> needs to be <kanji>revise</kanji>d. Your words need to be polished, you can''t publish improv. Revise your street word inspiration before you share it again.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2096', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A8%82', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:26:18.748797Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.652249Z'),
(2097, 'kanji', '諮', '諮', 'Watch what you <radical>say</radical> <radical>next</radical> from your <radical>mouth</radical> when someone comes to <kanji>consult</kanji> with you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2097', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AB%AE', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:26:44.176463Z', NULL, '2026-03-17T23:15:12.157125Z'),
(2098, 'kanji', '銘', '銘', 'A <radical>gold</radical> <radical>name</radical> on this piece of wood is the <kanji>inscription</kanji> that shows the artist''s signature.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2098', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8A%98', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:27:13.147336Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.674827Z'),
(2100, 'kanji', '堤', '堤', 'The <radical>correct</radical> way to block the <radical>sun</radical> with <radical>dirt</radical> is with an <kanji>embankment</kanji>. Build a huge, sloping embankment correctly and the sun won''t be able to bother you ever again!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2100', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A0%A4', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:28:08.64625Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:31:27.51168Z'),
(2101, 'kanji', '漂', '漂', 'If a <radical>tsunami</radical> hits your <radical>helicopter</radical> while you are <radical>jackhammer</radical>ing, it will <kanji>drift</kanji> out to sea. What were you doing with a jackhammer at a time like this? Now your helicopter is drifting away!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2101', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BC%82', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:28:36.709317Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.686052Z'),
(2102, 'kanji', '翻', '翻', 'You have a <radical>number</radical>ed <radical>feather</radical>. Pick your favorite number and put it on the feather. Then, put the numbered feather on your head. When you put it on, you''re able to <kanji>flip</kanji> and <kanji>flip</kanji> over and over.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2102', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BF%BB', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:31:05.00006Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.690708Z'),
(2103, 'kanji', '軌', '軌', 'There are <radical>nine</radical> <radical>car</radical>s all driving in the same place. With so many cars driving in the same place, they create a <kanji>rut</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2103', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BB%8C', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:31:23.695152Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.698025Z'),
(2104, 'kanji', '后', '后', 'You walk out on a <radical>cliff</radical>, where you find in the <radical>ground</radical> a <radical>mouth</radical> poking out. It speaks: "It is me, the <kanji>empress</kanji> of these lands!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2104', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%8E', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:31:46.250485Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T07:12:18.380718Z'),
(2105, 'kanji', '奮', '奮', 'A <radical>big</radical> <radical>turkey</radical> in the <radical>rice paddy</radical> is getting all <kanji>stirred up</kanji>. He''s kicking up all the rice. Who stirred him up?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2105', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A5%AE', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:32:10.382871Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(2106, 'kanji', '亭', '亭', 'The <radical>restaurant</radical> radical and the <kanji>restaurant</kanji> kanji are the same! The meaning of this kanji also extends other resting places, like an <kanji>inn</kanji> or a <kanji>pavilion</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2106', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%AD', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:32:35.785329Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.728604Z'),
(2107, 'kanji', '仰', '仰', 'The <radical>leader</radical> <radical>cat pirate</radical> is the greatest cat pirate of them all. It leads all the cat pirate leaders, even! This is truly a cat pirate <kanji>to look up to</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2107', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BB%B0', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:33:08.040539Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.743583Z'),
(2108, 'kanji', '伯', '伯', 'The <radical>leader</radical> all in <radical>white</radical> is the <kanji>chief</kanji>. All the other leaders are wearing some other color, but that''s how you tell the chief apart from the other leaders.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2108', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BC%AF', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:33:29.751004Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.753127Z'),
(2109, 'kanji', '偶', '偶', 'The <radical>leader</radical>, Master <radical>Splinter</radical> (the rat from Ninja Turtles) isn''t even supposed to exist. He exists because someone <kanji>accidentally</kanji> poured ooze on him.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2109', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%81%B6', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:33:48.473133Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.754911Z'),
(2111, 'kanji', '墳', '墳', 'Sticking out of the <radical>dirt</radical>, there''s a <radical>cross</radical> adorned with <radical>flowers</radical> and <radical>shellfish</radical> shells. This is a <kanji>tomb</kanji>. The flowers and shellfish were put here as decorations and to honor the dead that currently lie under the dirt. And the cross marks the tomb, obviously.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2111', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A2%B3', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:34:46.525187Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.760144Z'),
(2112, 'kanji', '壮', '壮', 'A <radical>samurai</radical> who can fight with <radical>icicle</radical>s on him is more <kanji>robust</kanji> than regular samurai. Can you imagine how robust you''d have to be to fight in weather cold enough to form icicles on your body?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2112', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A3%AE', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:35:19.403692Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.75944Z'),
(2113, 'kanji', '把', '把', 'Why would you put your <radical>fingers</radical> on a <radical>bed</radical>? To <kanji>bundle</kanji> up the sheets — maybe because you need to do laundry or something. <kanji>Grasp</kanji> that bundle of sheets tightly, and be careful not to let it unfurl as you carry it on over to the washing machine.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2113', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8A%8A', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:35:46.120878Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:22:31.631915Z'),
(2114, 'kanji', '搬', '搬', 'With your <radical>fingers</radical> you put some <radical>weapon</radical>s in a <radical>boat</radical> for <kanji>transport</kanji>. This is a transport boat and its your job to get these weapons from one place to another.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2114', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%90%AC', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:36:16.072362Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.770576Z'),
(2116, 'kanji', '洞', '洞', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> is the <radical>same</radical> as a <kanji>cave</kanji>. Because, if a wave is big enough, it will curl over on itself, creating a sort of cave within the wave. That''s why it''s the same as a cave!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2116', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B4%9E', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:37:16.037482Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:10:42.46993Z'),
(2117, 'kanji', '涯', '涯', 'There is a <radical>tsunami</radical> coming up over a <radical>cliff</radical> made of <radical>dirt</radical>. You can see this happening very far away because it''s right on the <kanji>horizon</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2117', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B6%AF', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:37:41.496246Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.805534Z'),
(2118, 'kanji', '疫', '疫', 'Turn a <radical>sick</radical>ness into a <radical>weapon</radical> and it becomes an <kanji>epidemic</kanji>. This sickness spreads to everyone and it''s all your fault because you put it in the water supply.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2118', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%96%AB', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:38:09.586831Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.81662Z'),
(466, 'kanji', '又', '又', 'You''re sitting on your <radical>stool</radical> <kanji>again</kanji>!!?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/466', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%88', 51, 1, '2012-02-28T23:48:13Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:09:50.041875Z'),
(2120, 'kanji', '偉', '偉', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of <radical>Korea</radical> is destined for <kanji>greatness</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2120', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%81%89', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:09:50.066344Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:02:16.543235Z'),
(2121, 'kanji', '頂', '頂', 'Take the <radical>street</radical> all the way to that <radical>geoduck</radical>, way up there. When you reach it, you will have made it to the <kanji>summit</kanji>. You <kanji>humbly</kanji> present the road to the geoduck and bow before it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2121', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A0%82', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:12:00.158604Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(2122, 'kanji', '召', '召', 'The <radical>call</radical> radical and the <kanji>call</kanji> kanji are the same. This kanji can also mean <kanji>eat</kanji>, because why would you call someone if not to ask them to eat with you?!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2122', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%AC', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:14:57.364782Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.84373Z'),
(2123, 'kanji', '挟', '挟', 'You run your <radical>fingers</radical> through your <radical>husband</radical>''s <radical>horns</radical>. His horns are what you love to <kanji>put between</kanji> your fingers. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2123', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8C%9F', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:17:15.990457Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.863046Z'),
(2124, 'kanji', '枯', '枯', 'When a <radical>tree</radical> grows <radical>old</radical>, it will <kanji>wither</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2124', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9E%AF', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:20:04.186651Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:13:37.694956Z'),
(2125, 'kanji', '沸', '沸', 'If a <radical>tsunami</radical> hits a <radical>dollar</radical> the water will instantly start to <kanji>boil</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2125', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B2%B8', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:21:14.035928Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.881575Z'),
(2126, 'kanji', '濯', '濯', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> is coming, so you ask two <radical>Wolverine</radical>s to help you take your <radical>turkey</radical> out for a <kanji>wash</kanji>. Despite the obvious dangers, a giant wave is the only thing that''ll wash a giant turkey, so you have no choice.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2126', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BF%AF', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:24:25.822227Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(2127, 'kanji', '燥', '燥', 'There''s a <radical>fire</radical> next to which you left some <radical>syrup</radical>, and it has all <kanji>dried up</kanji>! Put any liquid substance near fire and it will dry up actually, but syrup especially. It goes from being sticky and gooey and yummy to a pile of hard, dry sugar. Not so yummy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2127', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%87%A5', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:27:10.184676Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:08:49.118481Z'),
(2128, 'kanji', '瓶', '瓶', 'You see <radical>horns</radical> on a <radical>lantern</radical> that can hold <radical>ten thousand</radical> <radical>drops</radical> of water sitting on the <radical>ground</radical>. You get closer and realize it''s not a lantern, it''s actually a <kanji>bottle</kanji>. The horns are like a weird handle, but it''s definitely a bottle filled with water.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2128', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%93%B6', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:32:55.973564Z', NULL, '2025-10-31T14:36:39.038315Z'),
(2129, 'kanji', '耕', '耕', 'You have a <radical>plow</radical> and a (water) <radical>well</radical>. These are the two things you need in order to <kanji>plow</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2129', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%80%95', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:34:52.779861Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:45:32.176312Z'),
(2130, 'kanji', '肯', '肯', 'We must <radical>stop</radical> the <radical>moon</radical>. That, at least, we can come to an <kanji>agreement</kanji> on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2130', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%82%AF', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:36:28.623272Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T18:02:24.441596Z'),
(2131, 'kanji', '脂', '脂', 'You ate the <radical>moon</radical> with a <radical>spoon</radical>, and then the <radical>sun</radical> too, but man were they high in <kanji>fat</kanji>! In fact, you wanted to eat them with chopsticks, but they were so greasy with fat that the spoon was your only option.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2131', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%84%82', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:39:32.755002Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.934594Z'),
(2132, 'kanji', '膚', '膚', 'If you look in a <radical>tiger</radical>''s <radical>stomach</radical> you''ll find a bunch of <kanji>skin</kanji>. It''s really gross but what else did you think you''d find? Everyone knows tigers can''t digest the skin of the things they eat. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2132', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%86%9A', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:43:01.569419Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:43:44.728824Z'),
(2134, 'kanji', '軟', '軟', 'A <radical>car</radical> that''s <radical>lack</radical>ing is really <kanji>soft</kanji>. Cars need to be hard so that things like bugs and other cars wont hurt the people inside when you hit them. But if your car is lacking the hardness it needs, you''ll have a really soft, useless car.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2134', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BB%9F', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:50:04.787995Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T06:34:12.257292Z'),
(2135, 'kanji', '郊', '郊', 'If you <radical>mix</radical> a bunch of <radical>building</radical>s together, you have yourself a <kanji>suburb</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2135', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%83%8A', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:51:49.892372Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.956924Z'),
(2136, 'kanji', '隅', '隅', 'So my <radical>building</radical> has a big <radical>splinter</radical>, right in the <kanji>corner</kanji>. So what?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2136', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9A%85', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:53:33.392039Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.962944Z'),
(2137, 'kanji', '隻', '隻', 'A <radical>turkey</radical> sitting on a <radical>stool</radical> is something every ship has in its crow''s nest. That''s why the universal <kanji>ship counter</kanji> is a turkey on a stool. Not a crow.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2137', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9A%BB', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:54:43.478815Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(2138, 'kanji', '邸', '邸', 'Your <radical>clan</radical> is breaking <radical>ground</radical> and <radical>building</radical> your new <kanji>residence</kanji>. You''re all working together to make you a new home!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2138', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%82%B8', 51, 1, '2015-06-26T23:59:10.199876Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.990553Z'),
(2139, 'kanji', '郡', '郡', 'The <radical>buddy</radical> <radical>building</radical> is in the next <kanji>county</kanji>. If it was in your county then it wouldn''t be a buddy, it would be a neighbor. You''re aware of this building, but they aren''t that close, so they''re just a buddy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2139', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%83%A1', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:03:34.799578Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:01.993187Z'),
(2140, 'kanji', '釈', '釈', 'If you use a <radical>sickle</radical> to kill a <radical>shrimp</radical> you''ll need a really good <kanji>explanation</kanji> for yourself. What did that shrimp do to you? Why use a sickle? Well? What''s your explanation?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2140', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%87%88', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:05:09.347989Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:39:53.26852Z'),
(2141, 'kanji', '肪', '肪', 'When the <radical>moon</radical> faces the right <radical>direction</radical>, the <kanji>fat</kanji> in the bodies of people and animals all gets drawn to the surface and we all look weirdly greasy for a little while. You probably knew that the moon exerts a pull on water, but I bet you didn''t know it exerted a pull on fat too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2141', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%82%AA', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:07:59.433723Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:13:41.137317Z'),
(2142, 'kanji', '喚', '喚', 'What would your <radical>mouth</radical> do if you got caught in a <radical>prison</radical> for <radical>legs</radical>? It''d probably get real <radical>big</radical> — in other words, it would <kanji>scream</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2142', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%96%9A', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:11:12.409777Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:32:55.448412Z'),
(2143, 'kanji', '媛', '媛', 'Everyone knows that when a <radical>woman</radical> plays <radical>football</radical> she becomes a <kanji>princess</kanji>. Thats how princesses are made.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2143', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AA%9B', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:29:41.181808Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(2144, 'kanji', '貞', '貞', 'You try to put your <radical>toe</radical> inside a <radical>shellfish</radical>, but it just won''t open. That''s because this shellfish has sworn an oath of <kanji>chastity</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2144', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%9E', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:33:04.161824Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.005921Z'),
(2146, 'kanji', '苗', '苗', '<radical>Flowers</radical> that grow in a <radical>rice paddy</radical> only live to be <kanji>seedling</kanji>s. Once they sprout, they''re plucked away to make room for more rice. Poor little seedlings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2146', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8B%97', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:35:58.176183Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.026928Z'),
(2147, 'kanji', '渦', '渦', 'If you put two <radical>mustache</radical>s on either side of a <radical>tsunami</radical> it won''t know which way to go and spin back and forth until it becomes a <kanji>whirlpool</kanji>. Mustaches are pretty gross, so I don''t blame the tsunami for avoiding them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2147', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B8%A6', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:37:13.468996Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.038371Z'),
(2148, 'kanji', '慈', '慈', 'A person with <radical>horns</radical> has you on the <radical>ground</radical> and lets loose two <radical>poops</radical> over your <radical>heart</radical>. "<kanji>Mercy</kanji>! Mercy!" You cry, but the horned dude doesn''t stop until there are two gross poops sitting on your heart. "M-mercy..." is the only thing you can think to say.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2148', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%85%88', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:39:23.822708Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.051332Z'),
(2149, 'kanji', '襟', '襟', 'What <radical>outfit</radical> do you wear when cutting down <radical>tree</radical>s with a <radical>jackhammer</radical>? A <kanji>collar</kanji>. Yup, just a collar… plus some shirt cuffs and slacks, of course. You know, the classic Chippendales look.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2149', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A5%9F', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:41:47.942745Z', NULL, '2026-02-20T01:45:31.026718Z'),
(2150, 'kanji', '蓮', '蓮', 'You put <radical>flowers</radical> on your <radical>car</radical> and your <radical>scooter</radical>. The flowers you put on them are <kanji>lotus</kanji> flowers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2150', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%93%AE', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:44:11.876332Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:44:40.064698Z'),
(2151, 'kanji', '亮', '亮', 'There is a special type of meditation where you put a <radical>lid</radical> on your <radical>mouth</radical> and touch your <radical>forehead</radical> down to your <radical>legs</radical>. If you can do this pose, everything in life will become completely <kanji>clear</kanji>. Everything will clear out of your head too. You should try it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2151', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%AE', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:46:57.058102Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.074179Z'),
(2152, 'kanji', '聡', '聡', 'If you have an <radical>ear</radical> for the <radical>public</radical>''s <radical>heart</radical>, you must be very <kanji>wise</kanji>. You can understand everything the people want, and you know how to give it to them because you listen to their hearts so well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2152', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%81%A1', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:48:21.412578Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.080461Z'),
(2153, 'kanji', '浦', '浦', 'If you want to have a <radical>tsunami</radical> at your <radical>wedding</radical>, you need to make sure you have it at the <kanji>bay</kanji>. Anywhere more inland, and the tsunami won''t reach it in time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2153', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B5%A6', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:49:24.916885Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.089597Z'),
(2154, 'kanji', '塚', '塚', 'The <radical>dirt</radical> on the <radical>forehead</radical> of a <radical>pig</radical> makes a small <kanji>mound</kanji>. Picture it, if you will. A pig is rooting through the dirt. It lifts its head and there is a mound of dirt right on its forehead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2154', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A1%9A', 51, 1, '2015-06-27T00:50:51.681142Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.097512Z'),
(2155, 'kanji', '陥', '陥', 'This <radical>building</radical> was a <radical>former</radical> <radical>prison</radical> until the <kanji>cave in</kanji>. You see, prisons aren''t built very carefully, they just try to get them done fast enough to stuff people in there. So while you can tell this was once a building, it definitely isn''t a prison anymore, since the whole thing caved in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2155', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%A5', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T18:42:43.447482Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:14:18.040115Z'),
(2156, 'kanji', '貫', '貫', 'Under the <radical>window</radical>, you place a <radical>shellfish</radical>, then slam the window shut to <kanji>pierce</kanji> its tough shell. This is the best way to pierce a member of the shellfish family, I promise.

This kanji also has a second meaning. That shellfish you pierced with the window is in two halves now. You can use those halves as plates for sushi. If you count the broken shells you''ll know how much sushi you have. That makes them <kanji>sushi counter</kanji>s!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2156', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B2%AB', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T18:45:36.428046Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T00:09:26.552801Z'),
(2157, 'kanji', '覇', '覇', 'Take a <radical>helicopter</radical> made of <radical>leather</radical> to the <radical>moon</radical> to prove your excellent <kanji>leadership</kanji>. Some people called you crazy, but you''ll show them, as you lead an entire crew of leather helicopter pilots on their first mission to space!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2157', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A6%87', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T18:49:24.759947Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:11:43.353356Z'),
(2158, 'kanji', '呂', '呂', 'I hope all you''re doing in the <radical>bathtub</radical> is taking a <kanji>bath</kanji>. I mean, you can do other things if you want I guess. But bathtubs were made just for baths!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2158', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%91%82', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T18:52:05.57591Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.128007Z'),
(2160, 'kanji', '擁', '擁', 'Your <radical>fingers</radical> grasp this <radical>lid</radical> and when you raise it you see a <radical>poop</radical> <radical>turkey</radical> (a turkey covered in poop). You can''t help yourself, you <kanji>embrace</kanji> the turkey. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2160', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%93%81', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T18:56:38.804147Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(2161, 'kanji', '孤', '孤', 'There was once a <radical>melon</radical> <radical>child</radical>, that is, a child with the body of a melon. When he was born, his parents didn''t want him, so he became an <kanji>orphan</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2161', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AD%A4', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T18:58:01.188352Z', NULL, '2025-10-29T07:02:57.972105Z'),
(2162, 'kanji', '賠', '賠', 'You give a bunch of <radical>shellfish</radical> to a <radical>clown</radical> as <kanji>compensation</kanji>. The clown did you some favors, and everyone knows clowns only accept shellfish as a form of currency. So your compensation is a pile of random shellfish.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2162', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B3%A0', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T18:59:48.627156Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.152564Z'),
(2163, 'kanji', '鎖', '鎖', 'The famous <radical>gold</radical> <radical>triceratops</radical> <radical>shellfish</radical> is good for only one thing, and that''s being made into <kanji>chain</kanji>s. They link their triceratops horns together and form an unbreakable gold chain. You can''t eat them, because they''re gold. So they''re only really good for their chaining together ability.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2163', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8E%96', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T19:01:55.510139Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.162802Z'),
(2164, 'kanji', '噴', '噴', 'From your <radical>mouth</radical> comes a <radical>cross</radical>, some <radical>flowers</radical>, and <radical>shellfish</radical>. It all <kanji>erupt</kanji>s from your mouth!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2164', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%99%B4', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T19:05:54.447536Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:40:58.052424Z'),
(2165, 'kanji', '祥', '祥', 'When a <radical>spirit</radical> lives in a <radical>sheep</radical> it becomes the most <kanji>auspicious</kanji> creature on the planet. Lights shine out of it and the whole world weeps at its beauty. Look at the sheep''s fluffy glowing head. Nothing could be more auspicious and glorious than this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2165', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A5%A5', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T19:07:29.957234Z', NULL, '2025-12-21T20:51:22.632515Z'),
(2166, 'kanji', '牲', '牲', 'If you take a <radical>cow</radical>''s <radical>life</radical> you''re turning it into an <kanji>offering</kanji>. In the old days, cows'' lives were given to the gods as offerings pretty often.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2166', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%89%B2', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T19:09:18.259371Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:37:01.42735Z'),
(2167, 'kanji', '秩', '秩', 'If a <radical>grain</radical> has a <radical>fault</radical>, it needs to be put back in <kanji>order</kanji>. Grains must be perfectly identical at all times, otherwise they''ll be thrown out. That''s why special people will go out and check them to confirm their <kanji>regularity</kanji>. Keeping order is a tough job!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2167', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A7%A9', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T19:10:28.242014Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:37:07.819796Z'),
(2168, 'kanji', '唆', '唆', 'Beside your <radical>mouth</radical> is an evil <radical>spider</radical> that tries to <kanji>instigate</kanji> you into saying bad things and starting a riot. It''s like the little silken puppet master of your mouth or something. Don''t let it instigate you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2168', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%94%86', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T19:11:46.717994Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.20862Z'),
(2169, 'kanji', '膨', '膨', 'Play your <radical>drum</radical> under the <radical>moon</radical> and your <radical>hair</radical> will <kanji>swell</kanji>! The more you play and the longer it''s under the moon, the larger your hair will swell.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2169', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%86%A8', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T19:13:38.861635Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.209723Z'),
(2170, 'kanji', '芳', '芳', 'If you point a bunch of <radical>flowers</radical> in the same <radical>direction</radical>, you''ll be able to smell their <kanji>aromatic</kanji> <kanji>fragrance</kanji>. The best way to bask in their fragrance is to make them all point in the same direction (preferably straight at you). ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2170', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8A%B3', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T19:15:23.304132Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:23:00.599442Z'),
(2171, 'kanji', '恒', '恒', 'Your <radical>soul</radical> is like the <radical>ground</radical> or the <radical>dawn</radical>. It is <kanji>constant</kanji>, never changing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2171', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%81%92', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T19:17:22.368661Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(2172, 'kanji', '倫', '倫', 'A <radical>leader</radical> puts their <radical>hat</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical> next to a <radical>bookshelf</radical>. This is a test in <kanji>ethics</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2172', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%80%AB', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T19:18:52.697201Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:36:39.025064Z'),
(2173, 'kanji', '陳', '陳', 'The <radical>building</radical> to the <radical>east</radical> is an <kanji>exhibit</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2173', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%B3', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T19:20:03.966166Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T04:17:56.440105Z'),
(2174, 'kanji', '須', '須', 'Shaving the <radical>hair</radical> off a <radical>geoduck</radical> before you try to eat it is absolutely <kanji>necessary</kanji>. If you don''t, the hair will get caught in your throat and you''ll choke. It is necessary that you remove the hair.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2174', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A0%88', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T21:05:31.333253Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(2175, 'kanji', '偏', '偏', 'The <radical>leader</radical> of the <radical>library</radical> thinks that all the books in their library are better than everyone else''s books. He''s a very <kanji>biased</kanji> person. If he wasn''t the leader of that specific library he''d probably feel differently.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2175', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%81%8F', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T21:07:39.132946Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T20:08:51.118169Z'),
(2176, 'kanji', '遇', '遇', 'Your poor little <radical>scooter</radical> got a <radical>splinter</radical>, so make sure to give it extra nice <kanji>treatment</kanji>. 

This can also mean <kanji>encounter</kanji>. Makes sense when you think about how you first have to encounter someone before deciding how you''re going to treat them!

Also, when this means "treatment," note that it doesn''t refer to medical treatment, but rather about how you personally treat or receive people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2176', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%87', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T21:09:00.306274Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.266677Z'),
(2177, 'kanji', '糧', '糧', 'The <radical>rice</radical> that''s prepared at <radical>dawn</radical> by the <radical>village</radical> are <kanji>provisions</kanji> for the army. The army is scheduled to arrive right after dawn, so all of the village''s provisions for them have to be ready by then.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2177', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B3%A7', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T21:11:05.020518Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.270586Z'),
(2178, 'kanji', '殊', '殊', 'Within the <radical>yakuza</radical>, having a <radical>slide</radical> on your <radical>jet</radical> means you''re <kanji>especially</kanji> important and powerful. It''s a symbol of how especially awesome and badass you are.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2178', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AE%8A', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T21:16:49.195492Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:44:35.530997Z'),
(2179, 'kanji', '慢', '慢', 'You think you can catch the <radical>soul</radical> of the <radical>sun</radical> in a <radical>net</radical> without even getting up from your <radical>stool</radical>? That''s some serious <kanji>overconfidence</kanji>, buddy. The fact that you want to stay seated really proves your <kanji>laziness</kanji>, too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2179', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%85%A2', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T21:21:19.987378Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:55:31.565928Z'),
(2180, 'kanji', '没', '没', 'If you figure out how to use a <radical>tsunami</radical> as a <radical>weapon</radical>, everyone is going to <kanji>die</kanji>. You can''t control a tsunami, you fool. Now you''ve doomed us all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2180', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B2%A1', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T21:22:40.355179Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T20:02:56.909899Z'),
(2181, 'kanji', '怠', '怠', 'If you give a <radical>machine</radical> a <radical>heart</radical> it will get <kanji>lazy</kanji> and <kanji>neglect</kanji> its duties. Hearts give us emotions and with emotions we become weak. Why would a machine with a heart want to work anymore? Nah. It''s going to be lazy and neglectful now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2181', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%80%A0', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T21:23:56.315Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:15:41.32175Z'),
(2182, 'kanji', '遭', '遭', 'When you ride your <radical>scooter</radical> on the <radical>ground</radical> listening to <radical>music</radical> under the <radical>sun</radical>, you''re bound to <kanji>encounter</kanji> a bunch of new people! They hear your music playing as you scoot by and they come out to <kanji>meet</kanji> you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2182', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%AD', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T21:25:48.689014Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:46:29.409938Z'),
(2183, 'kanji', '惰', '惰', 'In its <radical>soul</radical>, a <radical>narwhal</radical> that''s supposed to do <radical>construction</radical> under the <radical>moon</radical>, will be too <kanji>lazy</kanji> to do it. And when your soul is lazy, there''s no way you''ll be able to get something done.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2183', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%83%B0', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T21:27:15.187356Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:03:45.517242Z'),
(2184, 'kanji', '猟', '猟', 'There is an <radical>animal</radical> in the <radical>grass</radical> and your horrible <radical>task</radical> is to go <kanji>hunting</kanji> for it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2184', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8C%9F', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:17:21.008592Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:51:15.083936Z'),
(2185, 'kanji', '乃', '乃', 'What do <radical>stairs</radical> do? They take you <kanji>from</kanji> where you were, to somewhere else. They''re a way to get <kanji>from</kanji> place to place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2185', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B9%83', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:19:08.350412Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:41:30.052698Z'),
(2186, 'kanji', '綾', '綾', 'You''re sewing something with <radical>thread</radical> but you and your creation get <radical>frostbite</radical> while you''re still working. Luckily the snowflakes freeze themselves into the cloth and turn into a pretty <kanji>design</kanji>. They swirl around and press a unique snowy design into the fabric. This is nice for you because you can''t sew anymore with your frostbitten hands.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2186', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B6%BE', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:21:46.074466Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.343679Z'),
(2187, 'kanji', '颯', '颯', 'If you <radical>stand</radical> in the <radical>wind</radical> it will be really <kanji>quick</kanji> to knock you over. So quick that you probably won''t even know the wind did it. You''ll end up standing up only to be knocked over again, just as quick.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2187', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A2%AF', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:23:36.786297Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.344235Z'),
(2188, 'kanji', '隼', '隼', 'If you tie a <radical>cross</radical> to a <radical>turkey</radical> you can trick everyone into thinking it''s a <kanji>falcon</kanji>. The cross will stretch its wings out just enough for it to stop looking so fat and turkey like, into a super cool bird of prey.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2188', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9A%BC', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:25:37.140828Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(2189, 'kanji', '輔', '輔', 'If you want to have your <radical>wedding</radical> in a <radical>car</radical> you''re going to need a lot of <kanji>help</kanji>. Cars are pretty small compared to weddings, so if you don''t get help from everyone you know you won''t be able to pull it off.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2189', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BC%94', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:27:31.775567Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.367017Z'),
(2190, 'kanji', '寛', '寛', 'The <radical>roof</radical> <radical>flowers</radical> <radical>see</radical> everything. But, they only see. That is because they learned the art of <kanji>tolerance</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2190', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%9B', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T18:28:45.964198Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.37294Z'),
(2191, 'kanji', '胞', '胞', 'Take the <radical>moon</radical> and <radical>wrap</radical> it in a bag and it will look just like a <kanji>cell</kanji>. It''s a circle in a bigger circle. The moon is the nucleus. Yes! Science is happening!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2191', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%83%9E', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T18:36:33.050554Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T01:30:18.131382Z'),
(2192, 'kanji', '浄', '浄', 'The <radical>tsunami</radical> of <radical>conflict</radical> will <kanji>cleanse</kanji> and <kanji>purify</kanji> the lands of people, homes, and culture.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2192', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B5%84', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T18:39:57.780118Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:37:53.327102Z'),
(2193, 'kanji', '随', '随', 'How many <radical>building</radical>s <radical>have</radical> <radical>scooter</radical>s? <kanji>All</kanji> of them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2193', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9A%8F', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T18:45:57.335259Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.409434Z'),
(2194, 'kanji', '稿', '稿', 'When what you''re writing is as <radical>tall</radical> as the <radical>grain</radical>s growing outside, you know you''re done with your first <kanji>draft</kanji>. Some teachers want papers to be a certain number of words or pages, but that''s silly. You should measure your draft in grains!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2194', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A8%BF', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T18:55:05.492545Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T22:51:49.295558Z'),
(2195, 'kanji', '丹', '丹', 'This is a <radical>boat</radical>, but it''s missing some pieces. That''s because it''s really rusty, so parts are falling off of it and it looks <kanji>rust-colored</kanji>. In Taoist thought and ancient Chinese literature, this rusty red color, also known as <kanji>cinnabar</kanji>, represented <kanji>sincerity</kanji>, and you''ll often see this kanji take that meaning in jukugo words. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2195', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%B9', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T18:57:07.51545Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:42:22.595675Z'),
(2196, 'kanji', '壌', '壌', 'A <radical>dirt</radical> <radical>nurse</radical> is someone who nurses dirt. When nursed, dirt becomes <kanji>soil</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2196', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A3%8C', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:02:10.476349Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.43742Z'),
(2197, 'kanji', '舗', '舗', 'You want to have a quaint <radical>cottage</radical> <radical>wedding</radical>, so you have to go to the cottage wedding <kanji>shop</kanji> to get everything you need.

Unfortunately, there’s no road to the shop, so… you’ll just have to <kanji>pave</kanji> one yourself! Every shop needs a road, so get to paving.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2197', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%88%97', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:03:34.703257Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:27:51.741148Z'),
(2198, 'kanji', '騰', '騰', 'While the <radical>moon</radical> is out, a <radical>gladiator</radical> is riding a <radical>horse</radical>, yelling about <kanji>inflation</kanji>. No one would listen to him during the day, so he waited for the moon to rise so he could complain about prices doing the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2198', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A8%B0', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:05:28.399943Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:28:25.718985Z'),
(2199, 'kanji', '緯', '緯', 'The <radical>thread</radical> that goes through <radical>Korea</radical> is the <kanji>latitude</kanji> line splitting up North and South Korea.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2199', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B7%AF', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:06:59.81794Z', NULL, '2026-01-01T16:52:47.263261Z'),
(2200, 'kanji', '艇', '艇', 'A <radical>boat</radical> owned by a <radical>Yoga</radical> <radical>King</radical> needs to be able to let him keep doing his exercises while he''s on it. That''s why his boat is a <kanji>rowboat</kanji>, so he can work on his yoga arms as he gets around.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2200', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%89%87', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:08:57.505921Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T19:04:16.361248Z'),
(2201, 'kanji', '披', '披', 'Press your <radical>fingers</radical> in someone''s <radical>skin</radical> to <kanji>expose</kanji> what''s underneath. This is pretty gross, but sometimes it''s more important that you expose the cause of the problem, than avoiding something uncomfortable.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2201', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8A%AB', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:10:51.194504Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:30:55.746903Z'),
(2202, 'kanji', '錦', '錦', 'You weave <radical>gold</radical> and <radical>white</radical> patterns into this <radical>towel</radical>, turning it into <kanji>brocade</kanji>. It''s beautiful, elegant, and worth way more than when it was just a plain towel.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2202', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8C%A6', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:12:43.545784Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.493517Z'),
(2204, 'kanji', '剰', '剰', 'There''s so much <radical>grain</radical> in your <radical>mouth</radical> you need to use a <radical>knife</radical> to remove it. Only when there is a <kanji>surplus</kanji> can you put that much grain in your mouth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2204', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%89%B0', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:17:54.326415Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T03:12:02.561904Z'),
(2205, 'kanji', '繊', '繊', 'A <radical>drunkard</radical> <radical>cross</radical>es <radical>thread</radical> across special <radical>spikes</radical> to separate the <kanji>fiber</kanji>s. The best way to separate the fibers of thread is to get a drunkard to do it. If you''re too precise it will just snap. You gotta be loose with it! Just flop them onto the spikes and the fibers will come apart by themselves!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2205', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B9%8A', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:19:09.313073Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.520868Z'),
(2206, 'kanji', '諭', '諭', 'If you try to <radical>say</radical> something to the <radical>Death Star</radical>, it would only <kanji>admonish</kanji> you. It was made to destroy planets, after all, so I don''t know why you''d expect it to have anything nice to say. It just admonishes you about everything you''ve ever done wrong until you can''t stop crying.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2206', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AB%AD', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:21:07.352479Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T00:49:17.941365Z'),
(2207, 'kanji', '惨', '惨', 'If you get a <radical>big</radical> <radical>hair</radical> stuck in your <radical>private</radical> <radical>soul</radical> it will be a <kanji>disaster</kanji>! Have you ever gotten a hair in your eye or your mouth that you just couldn''t get out? You cough, your eyes water, it''s awful! You can''t focus on anything until it''s gone. Now imagine if a big ''ol hair got stuck in your own private soul!!! Disaster!!!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2207', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%83%A8', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:22:30.717306Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(2208, 'kanji', '虐', '虐', 'Whether you''re a <radical>tiger</radical> or <radical>Wolverine</radical>, the anti-claw regime will want to <kanji>oppress</kanji> you. Too afraid of any potential violent rebellion, this oppressive regime is going to imprison and declaw every single clawed being.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2208', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%99%90', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:25:32.133138Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:35:43.305445Z'),
(2209, 'kanji', '据', '据', 'You use your <radical>fingers</radical> to take down the <radical>old</radical> <radical>flag</radical> and <kanji>install</kanji> a new one. Flags get old, just like computers and furniture and people. So when one gets too old, you have to get on up there and install a new one in its place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2209', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8D%AE', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:26:57.147805Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:13:37.694956Z'),
(2210, 'kanji', '徐', '徐', 'If you <radical>loiter</radical> around a <radical>surplus</radical> of anything, you need to be <kanji>gentle</kanji> and touch everything <kanji>gently</kanji>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2210', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%90', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:28:16.490212Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.560677Z'),
(2211, 'kanji', '搭', '搭', 'In your <radical>fingers</radical> are <radical>flowers</radical> which really <radical>suit</radical> you. But you throw them to the people waving to you from below, as you <kanji>board</kanji> your ship. The flowers fall, unneeded. You''re <kanji>embark</kanji>ing on a new life, a new journey. There will be more flowers where you''re going.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2211', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%90%AD', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:29:34.68321Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.579428Z'),
(2213, 'kanji', '鯉', '鯉', 'If you''re in Japan, the <radical>fish</radical> in your <radical>village</radical> are <kanji>carp</kanji>. You may know them as <kanji>koi</kanji>, too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2213', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%AF%89', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:32:52.73149Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.587936Z'),
(2215, 'kanji', '緋', '緋', 'The <radical>thread</radical>s a <radical>criminal</radical> wears will always be <kanji>scarlet</kanji>. Scarlet is the color of freshly spilt blood, which is why bad guys wear it so much.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2215', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B7%8B', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T19:36:19.701541Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.608653Z'),
(2216, 'kanji', '曙', '曙', 'The <radical>sun</radical> is rising and there is a <radical>net</radical> covering <radical>someone</radical>, who''s being forced to watch as <kanji>dawn</kanji> appears. They''ve never seen dawn before, and they didn''t want to, but you''re making them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2216', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9B%99', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:11:55.232167Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:24:21.947579Z'),
(2217, 'kanji', '胡', '胡', 'The <radical>old</radical> <radical>moon</radical> (the moon we had before the current moon) was actually just a huge <kanji>barbarian</kanji> floating in the sky.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2217', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%83%A1', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:14:27.469257Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:13:37.694956Z'),
(2219, 'kanji', '帥', '帥', 'There''s a <radical>drop</radical> of <radical>bear</radical> on this <radical>towel</radical>. A true <kanji>commander</kanji> can notice little things like this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2219', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B8%A5', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:17:30.717085Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:00:07.209894Z'),
(2220, 'kanji', '啓', '啓', 'If you can find the <radical>door</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> shaped like a <radical>mouth</radical> it will <kanji>enlighten</kanji> you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2220', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%95%93', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:19:24.999982Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.649436Z'),
(2221, 'kanji', '葵', '葵', '<radical>Flowers</radical> that are thought to be little <radical>tent</radical>s sent from <radical>heaven</radical> are <kanji>hollyhock</kanji>s. They''re shaped almost like little teepees and would make the perfect tent for a faerie or a little yokai! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2221', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%91%B5', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:21:01.74679Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.65054Z'),
(2222, 'kanji', '駿', '駿', 'When a <radical>horse</radical> sees a <radical>spider</radical> it will start running at a <kanji>speed</kanji> no one ever thought a horse could go! Horses are super afraid of spiders, you see, so if they see one, they''ll bolt and reach top speed! That''s actually the only way to get a horse to run that fast. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2222', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A7%BF', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:22:24.063133Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.648913Z'),
(2223, 'kanji', '諒', '諒', 'If you <radical>say</radical> something the same way they say it in the <radical>capital</radical>, everyone in the whole country will be able to <kanji>comprehend</kanji> what you''re trying to say. If you try to speak in your hometown''s dialect no one will. But everyone can comprehend what you want if you use the capital''s dialect to say it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2223', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AB%92', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:24:11.964257Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:17:58.379479Z'),
(2224, 'kanji', '莉', '莉', 'You cut the <radical>flowers</radical> off the <radical>grain</radical> with your <radical>knife</radical>. The flowers that fall off are <kanji>jasmine</kanji> flowers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2224', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8E%89', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:26:34.233463Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.686861Z'),
(2225, 'kanji', '鯨', '鯨', 'If you travel to the <radical>fish</radical> <radical>capital</radical>, you''ll find the leader of all fish, the mighty <kanji>whale</kanji>! Okay, so a whale isn''t technically a fish, but that''s why it makes such a good leader. You can usually only find whales hanging around near the capital because they really enjoy ruling over all the other fish. They are the biggest, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2225', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%AF%A8', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T20:48:28.010797Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:17:58.379479Z'),
(2226, 'kanji', '荘', '荘', 'Clearing his <radical>flowers</radical> of <radical>icicle</radical>s, the <radical>samurai</radical> returns home to his <kanji>villa</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2226', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8D%98', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T20:52:08.049651Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:18:01.113317Z'),
(2227, 'kanji', '栽', '栽', 'A <radical>drunkard</radical> gets his hands on a <radical>plow</radical> and starts <kanji>planting</kanji> right away. Drunk people are surprisingly good at planting, especially when they have machines to help them do it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2227', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A0%BD', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T20:56:41.935485Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.69878Z'),
(2228, 'kanji', '拐', '拐', 'A <radical>mouth</radical> with a <radical>sword</radical> is threatening to cut off your <radical>fingers</radical> because they''ve <kanji>kidnap</kanji>ped you! You can''t see anything about them, since they''re all covered up. All you see is their angry mouth and the sword they keep putting near your fingers. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2228', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8B%90', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T20:58:16.678149Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T00:02:14.591147Z'),
(2229, 'kanji', '冠', '冠', 'Your <radical>forehead</radical> is the <radical>origin</radical> for the <radical>measurement</radical> for your <kanji>crown</kanji>! If you measure somewhere else on your head for a crown it won''t fit.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2229', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%86%A0', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:01:42.286668Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.717195Z'),
(2230, 'kanji', '勲', '勲', 'You''re in a really important position, <radical>heavy</radical> with <radical>power</radical>, but you can''t carry all of that around with you, so you <radical>boil</radical> it all down to just a few <kanji>merit</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2230', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8B%B2', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:05:14.213301Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.725819Z'),
(2231, 'kanji', '酬', '酬', 'You took someone''s <radical>alcohol</radical> and <radical>drop</radical> <radical>drop</radical> <radical>drop</radical>''d it all into the <radical>river</radical>. Now you have to <kanji>repay</kanji> them. Alcohol costs a lot of money, you know.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2231', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%85%AC', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:06:43.208478Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:52:39.90813Z'),
(2232, 'kanji', '紋', '紋', 'You use <radical>thread</radical> and <radical>writing</radical> to sew our <kanji>family crest</kanji>. You''ve been sewing your family crest since you were a kid, you could do it today with your eyes closed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2232', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%8B', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:08:51.285215Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.727522Z'),
(2233, 'kanji', '卸', '卸', 'At <radical>noon</radical> you <radical>stop</radical> to buy <radical>stamp</radical>s because that''s the only time they''re being sold at <kanji>wholesale</kanji> price. Everyone''s so busy eating and working and being at school, that they miss this awesome deal, but not you. Everyday at noon you stock up on wholesale stamps. Do you send a lot of mail? No, you probably just collect them. But they were cheap, so who cares!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2233', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%B8', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:10:41.485088Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T21:01:01.343088Z'),
(2234, 'kanji', '欄', '欄', 'You see a bunch of <radical>tree</radical>s lined up outside of the <radical>gate</radical> to the <radical>east</radical> and realize they look like perfect <kanji>column</kanji>s. It seems like a building or a castle should be resting above them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2234', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AC%84', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:12:05.170735Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:09:25.99139Z'),
(2235, 'kanji', '逸', '逸', 'Jump on a <radical>scooter</radical> to <radical>excuse</radical> yourself from answering a question and you''ve successfully <kanji>deviate</kanji>d from that nosey person''s plan! You''ve also been able to <kanji>elude</kanji> them pretty well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2235', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%B8', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:14:00.843134Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.760352Z'),
(2236, 'kanji', '尚', '尚', 'A <radical>triceratops</radical> with a <radical>mustache</radical> will go on talking forever. Every time you think he''s done he says, "<kanji>Furthermore</kanji>..." and keeps going. Something about a mustache on a dinosaur makes him kind of a jerk, but he''s held in such high <kanji>esteem</kanji> that no one ever interrupts him.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2236', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%9A', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:15:49.410986Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:24:57.575507Z'),
(2237, 'kanji', '顕', '顕', 'When the <radical>sun</radical> comes up the <radical>spikes</radical> of <radical>geoduck</radical>s <kanji>appear</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2237', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A1%95', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:17:59.142704Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(2238, 'kanji', '粛', '粛', '<radical>Wolverine</radical> puts a <radical>stick</radical> between his <radical>legs</radical> and straight into some <radical>rice</radical>, all while wearing a <kanji>solemn</kanji> expression on his face. He''s probably remembering his dad who died from getting his legs stuck in rice. Just leave the poor solemn creature alone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2238', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B2%9B', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:20:18.608796Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.77492Z'),
(2239, 'kanji', '愚', '愚', 'If you give <radical>Splinter</radical> your <radical>heart</radical> you''ll feel pretty <kanji>foolish</kanji> afterwards. His heart belongs to the art of ninjutsu! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2239', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%84%9A', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:21:26.791174Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.780784Z'),
(2240, 'kanji', '庶', '庶', 'Who goes into their <radical>yurt</radical> to <radical>boil</radical> things? The <kanji>common</kanji> people! <kanji>All sorts</kanji> of people do it, it''s really common. You don''t boil things outside, do you?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2240', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%B6', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:27:34.002081Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:30:22.144103Z'),
(2241, 'kanji', '践', '践', 'Whenever you set <radical>foot</radical> in a <radical>bar</radical>, there''s always something new to <kanji>practice</kanji>. If you usually drink a lot, you might want to practice moderation for example. Or perhaps you simply want to brush up on your social skills. Either way, plenty of opportunities for practice.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2241', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B7%B5', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:28:53.211588Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:43:09.675733Z'),
(2242, 'kanji', '呈', '呈', 'The <radical>Mouth</radical> <radical>King</radical>, who is just one big mouth, has one skill, and that is to <kanji>present</kanji>. That big mouth is always presenting something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2242', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%91%88', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:31:08.903548Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.795984Z'),
(2243, 'kanji', '疎', '疎', 'A <radical>coat rack</radical> will turn into a <radical>bundle</radical> of sticks if you <kanji>neglect</kanji> it. When you neglect a coat rack, the weight from the coats will pull all of the arms off, leaving it as a <kanji>sparse</kanji> stick.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2243', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%96%8E', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:33:31.750836Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:40:04.894814Z'),
(2244, 'kanji', '疾', '疾', 'A <radical>sick</radical>ness that hits you like an <radical>arrow</radical> will do its gross work <kanji>rapidly</kanji>. It''s not a good thing, you''re gonna get really sick really fast.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2244', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%96%BE', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:35:49.118787Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.825973Z'),
(2245, 'kanji', '謡', '謡', 'Whatever you <radical>say</radical> through a <radical>satellite</radical> turns into <kanji>Noh chanting</kanji>. It gets all drawn out and distorted from going through the satellite, which makes it SOUND like Noh chanting at least.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2245', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AC%A1', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:38:52.32938Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.82702Z'),
(2246, 'kanji', '鎌', '鎌', 'You cast the <radical>Mona Lisa</radical> in <radical>gold</radical> and attach it to a stick to turn it into a <kanji>sickle</kanji>! It''s really big and bulky, but when you swing it fast enough your new sickle cuts lots of wheat! Just don''t tell anyone what you did with the famous Mona Lisa painting and you should be able to get away with this!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2246', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8E%8C', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:40:40.543329Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.832762Z'),
(2247, 'kanji', '酷', '酷', 'If you <radical>announce</radical> that <radical>alcohol</radical> is illegal you''re just being <kanji>cruel</kanji>. Honestly, saying no one can have any alcohol at all is totally <kanji>unjust</kanji>. They tried that in the 1920s and it didn''t work then either, so cut it out! Let people drink what they want to drink!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2247', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%85%B7', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:41:51.477301Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:52:39.90813Z'),
(2248, 'kanji', '叙', '叙', 'The <radical>surplus</radical> <radical>stool</radical>, please <kanji>describe</kanji> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2248', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8F%99', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:44:59.982298Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.846215Z'),
(2249, 'kanji', '且', '且', 'If you want to wear a <radical>top hat</radical> you <kanji>also</kanji> have to wear a suit to go with it. You can''t just wear a top hat, that just doesn''t look right. When you think top hat, think about what you also need with it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2249', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B8%94', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:46:24.885551Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.058822Z'),
(2250, 'kanji', '痴', '痴', 'You''re <radical>sick</radical> because you put an <radical>arrow</radical> in your <radical>mouth</radical> and tried to eat it. To be honest, that''s just really <kanji>stupid</kanji>. You have to be stupid to try to eat an arrow. You deserve to be sick.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2250', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%97%B4', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:48:05.698385Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.863767Z'),
(2254, 'kanji', '茎', '茎', 'When you leave <radical>flowers</radical> on a <radical>tombstone</radical> they look good at first, but after a few days or some harsh weather, there''s nothing left but <kanji>stem</kanji>s. That''s why you have to replace flowers on tombstones all the time. If you don''t it will look like you only left the stems there, and that''s pretty disrespectful.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2254', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8C%8E', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:55:22.418948Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.929413Z'),
(2255, 'kanji', '阿', '阿', 'You see a <radical>building</radical> with a <radical>lip ring</radical> and it''s just so cute you have to <kanji>flatter</kanji> it. "That lip ring looks wonderful on you, Buildingさん," you say.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2255', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%98%BF', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T21:56:47.504786Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.943026Z'),
(2256, 'kanji', '悠', '悠', 'The <radical>leader</radical> who usually hits you with a <radical>stick</radical> in <radical>winter</radical> finds it in his <radical>heart</radical> to give you some <kanji>leisure</kanji> time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2256', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%82%A0', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T22:04:02.403346Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.947103Z'),
(2257, 'kanji', '杏', '杏', 'What does a <radical>tree</radical> make that you''d want to put in your <radical>mouth</radical>? An <kanji>apricot</kanji>! Apricots are also about the size of an open mouth, so they kind of look like mouths hanging down under the trees. You can bite them right off the tree with your own mouth!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2257', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9D%8F', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T22:04:58.687277Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.955393Z'),
(2258, 'kanji', '茜', '茜', 'What kind of <radical>flowers</radical> grow in the <radical>west</radical>? <kanji>Rose madder</kanji> flowers, of course. Don''t know what those are? That''s okay. Just know their roots are used to make dyes of a <kanji>deep red</kanji> color. Red like the rose in their name, but madder, wilder, deeper… And they grow in the west so they can take in those deep red colors of the setting sun.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2258', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%8C%9C', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T22:06:52.176135Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.95694Z'),
(2259, 'kanji', '栞', '栞', '<radical>Dry</radical> an already <radical>dry</radical> <radical>tree</radical> to create a <kanji>bookmark</kanji>. The more you dry the tree, the flatter it becomes, making it the perfect way to mark your spot in a book without needing to fold a page or write on it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2259', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A0%9E', 54, 1, '2015-07-02T22:09:26.481874Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.973611Z'),
(2260, 'kanji', '伏', '伏', 'When you meet the <radical>leader</radical> <radical>dog</radical> you have to <kanji>bow</kanji> to show your respect. Dogs are pretty great, so the leader of all dogs must be the greatest! You should bow really deeply to this one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2260', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BC%8F', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:04:17.012139Z', NULL, '2025-12-21T20:37:07.691155Z'),
(2261, 'kanji', '鎮', '鎮', 'The <radical>reality</radical> of <radical>gold</radical> is that it has the power to <kanji>tranquilize</kanji> anyone who gets their hands on it. Gold may seem cool to have in movies and songs, but in reality, it has the power to mess you up!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2261', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8E%AE', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:05:45.861882Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:02.988265Z'),
(2262, 'kanji', '奉', '奉', 'When <radical>spring</radical> is <radical>dry</radical> you need to <kanji>dedicate</kanji> your time and effort somewhere else. You can''t plant flowers or vegetables, it''s too dry. You can''t play outside, your skin will flake. So you need to dedicate yourself to something inside.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2262', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A5%89', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:06:53.369633Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:22:51.842327Z'),
(2263, 'kanji', '憂', '憂', 'A <radical>hundred</radical> <radical>forehead</radical>s lower themselves to their <radical>heart</radical>s in <radical>winter</radical> in <kanji>grief</kanji>. There were once many of their kind, the forehead peoples, but now there are only a hundred left after this harsh winter took the rest of them. Their grief is immense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2263', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%86%82', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:08:24.523979Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.016438Z'),
(2264, 'kanji', '朴', '朴', 'You decide to carve a <radical>tree</radical> into a giant replica of your big <radical>toe</radical> because you think it will be really <kanji>simple</kanji>. Once you''re done though, it looks like a really <kanji>crude</kanji> attempt at a fat, lumpy blob. Maybe carving isn''t as simple as you thought.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2264', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%B4', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:13:27.616373Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.020409Z'),
(2266, 'kanji', '惜', '惜', 'Your <radical>soul</radical> <radical>long ago</radical> was very <kanji>frugal</kanji>, which is why you get to have too much in this life!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2266', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%83%9C', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:16:32.083298Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(2267, 'kanji', '佳', '佳', 'A <radical>leader</radical> gets double the <radical>dirt</radical> to build on if he''s the most <kanji>excellent</kanji> and most <kanji>skilled</kanji> of all the leaders. You don''t think dirt is so great? Well what if I took the dirt you own away from you? Your house is on that dirt. It''s mine now. Get outta here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2267', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BD%B3', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:17:57.920523Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.03261Z'),
(2268, 'kanji', '悼', '悼', 'The <radical>soul</radical> in your <radical>toe</radical> leaves <radical>early</radical>. You <kanji>grieve</kanji> and <kanji>mourn</kanji> for your now-dead toe.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2268', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%82%BC', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:20:22.431864Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(2269, 'kanji', '該', '該', 'You want to <radical>slice</radical> what you <radical>say</radical> in half so that people focus on <kanji>the above</kanji> text first. The above is what you want them to see! So slice what you say!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2269', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A9%B2', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:21:43.668633Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.053894Z'),
(2270, 'kanji', '赴', '赴', 'You need to <radical>run</radical> on your <radical>toe</radical>s if you want to <kanji>proceed</kanji> down this road. If you can''t run on your toes, well then you''re out of luck, and you won''t be able to <kanji>move on</kanji> at all. I didn''t make this rule, I just enforce it. Now run!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2270', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B5%B4', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:22:58.863548Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:38:57.03621Z'),
(2271, 'kanji', '髄', '髄', 'Your <radical>bone</radical>s <radical>have</radical> a <radical>scooter</radical> that moves through the <kanji>bone marrow</kanji> inside them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2271', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%AB%84', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:25:05.680091Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T06:35:40.449797Z'),
(2272, 'kanji', '傍', '傍', 'The <radical>leader</radical> is <radical>stand</radical>ing funny, facing a <radical>direction</radical> <kanji>nearby</kanji>. He can''t go far because there''s obviously something wrong with his feet. He''s stuck nearby, very close to your side.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2272', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%82%8D', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:39:39.952043Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.081909Z'),
(2273, 'kanji', '累', '累', 'You start growing <radical>thread</radical> in your <radical>rice paddy</radical> to <kanji>accumulate</kanji> more. You didn''t want to have to buy any, so you''re just trying to grow thread with your rice. That seems like a good way to accumulate more to you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2273', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%AF', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:40:38.708842Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.097361Z'),
(2274, 'kanji', '癒', '癒', 'The <radical>sick</radical> <radical>Death Star</radical> needs its <radical>heart</radical> <kanji>cure</kanji>d. If it doesn''t get <kanji>healing</kanji> soon, it will die.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2274', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%99%92', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:42:40.596801Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.110995Z'),
(2275, 'kanji', '郭', '郭', 'You just <radical>receive</radical>d a <radical>building</radical>, so you want to surround it with an <kanji>enclosure</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2275', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%83%AD', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:44:22.615266Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.140805Z'),
(2276, 'kanji', '尿', '尿', 'When you go to the pool or lake to swim, you''ll see a <radical>flag</radical> stuck somewhere in the <radical>water</radical>. That flag is there so you know it''s okay to <kanji>pee</kanji> in that area of the pool. Everyone knows <kanji>urine</kanji> dilutes in water, and if everyone just pees in one place, it won''t be gross. So go pee over by that flag when you have to go. Don''t bother getting out of the water.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2276', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%BF', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:47:01.302171Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.144209Z'),
(2277, 'kanji', '賓', '賓', 'Between the <radical>roof</radical> and the <radical>ground</radical> there are <radical>few</radical> <radical>shellfish</radical>. This is because we invited some <kanji>VIP</kanji> <kanji>guest</kanji>s into our home and they ate all our shellfish.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2277', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B3%93', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:48:51.950512Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:11:59.650433Z'),
(2278, 'kanji', '虜', '虜', 'When a <radical>tiger</radical> meets a <radical>man</radical>, it becomes the man''s <kanji>captive</kanji>. Men can be pretty mean like that. They see a tiger and they want to put it in a cage, so the poor tiger becomes the man''s captive.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2278', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%99%9C', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:56:01.977779Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T16:24:29.442417Z'),
(2279, 'kanji', '憾', '憾', 'The only <radical>feeling</radical> a dead <radical>soul</radical> will have is <kanji>remorse</kanji>. Remorse for the people it left behind. <kanji>Regret</kanji>s for the things it did and didn''t do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2279', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%86%BE', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T22:57:53.491917Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(2280, 'kanji', '弥', '弥', 'Go from a <radical>bow</radical> to a <radical>small</radical> <radical>gun</radical> and you''re <kanji>increasing</kanji> in fire power! Even though the gun is small, it''s still a step up from a bow.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2280', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%A5', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T23:02:31.217885Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.192128Z'),
(2281, 'kanji', '粗', '粗', 'If you find some <radical>rice</radical> in a <radical>top hat</radical> it''s going to be really <kanji>coarse</kanji> and <kanji>rough</kanji>. Probably because it''s not the kind that''s meant to be eaten.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2281', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B2%97', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T23:05:43.394924Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.193335Z'),
(2282, 'kanji', '循', '循', 'If you want to <radical>loiter</radical> on a <radical>cliff</radical> with the <radical>pope</radical> you better be ready to move in perfect <kanji>circulation</kanji> with him. The pope is wearing really heavy robes and it''s hot up here on this cliff, so you both need to walk in circles to keep cool.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2282', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BE%AA', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T23:11:08.106748Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T07:56:53.037423Z'),
(2283, 'kanji', '凝', '凝', 'If you try to turn something into <radical>ice</radical> while having <radical>doubt</radical>s, it will just <kanji>congeal</kanji> instead. It will <kanji>freeze</kanji>, but it will get kinda yucky too. Your doubts will affect the ice-making process, so try not to be so worried and unsure next time you want normal ice.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2283', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%87%9D', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T23:20:03.285183Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.239702Z'),
(2285, 'kanji', '昌', '昌', 'The <radical>sun</radical> is the reason why crops grow and people can get out of bed in the morning. But on planets with two <radical>sun</radical>s, they can get even more done and it makes them way more <kanji>prosperous</kanji> than us. They work all day, then as the first sun sets, the second one rises and they get a new burst of energy. The whole planet lives in a state of <kanji>prosperity</kanji> because of the two suns.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2285', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%98%8C', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T23:31:50.829022Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.256511Z'),
(2286, 'kanji', '旦', '旦', 'The <radical>dawn</radical> radical and the <kanji>dawn</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2286', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%97%A6', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T23:34:06.74223Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.259582Z'),
(2287, 'kanji', '愉', '愉', 'The <radical>soul</radical> of the <radical>Death Star</radical> is actually quite <kanji>pleasant</kanji>. And if you think about it, it''s the people who control the Death Star that are evil and kill.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2287', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%84%89', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T23:35:53.201602Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(2288, 'kanji', '抹', '抹', 'Put your <radical>fingers</radical> in a <radical>jet</radical> if you want to <kanji>erase</kanji> them from existence. You aren''t just chopping or burning them off. There''s like, no trace of them at all. Your parents can''t even remember if you were born with fingers or not. Jets are that powerful.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2288', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8A%B9', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T23:37:04.096231Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.264087Z'),
(2289, 'kanji', '栓', '栓', 'The <radical>king</radical> has a <radical>hat</radical> that''s made from a <radical>tree</radical> but the more you look at it, the more it looks like it''s just a big <kanji>cork</kanji>. It''s a big, wooden <kanji>plug</kanji> that he''s just placed on his head. It can hardly be called a hat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2289', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A0%93', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T23:38:19.320934Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.289491Z'),
(2290, 'kanji', '之', '之', 'How do you make a mnemonic for the word this? You don’t. But, we can try. You climb many <radical>hills</radical>. On top of one of the hills is a little <radical>drop</radical> of water. You pick it up and say “<kanji>THIS</kanji>… THIS IS WHAT I WAS LOOKING FOR!”', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2290', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B9%8B', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T23:41:50.336407Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.288649Z'),
(2292, 'kanji', '遼', '遼', 'You got off your <radical>scooter</radical> to load some <radical>charcoal</radical> onto it, when it suddenly took off to a <kanji>distant</kanji> place! That''s some powerful charcoal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2292', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%BC', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T23:47:04.235482Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.307379Z'),
(2293, 'kanji', '瑛', '瑛', 'The <radical>king</radical> of the <radical>flowers</radical> in the <radical>center</radical> (of England, if you remember) has a special <kanji>crystal</kanji>. The king crystal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2293', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%91%9B', 55, 1, '2015-07-07T23:49:11.845657Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:25:07.512079Z'),
(2294, 'kanji', '那', '那', 'You''re holding a <radical>sword</radical> in front of <radical>two</radical> <radical>building</radical>s. <kanji>What</kanji> will you do?

A. Stab the building on the left.

B. Stab the building on the right.

What do you choose?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2294', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%82%A3', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T15:21:48.288763Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.329907Z'),
(2295, 'kanji', '拍', '拍', 'You''re tapping your <radical>fingers</radical> against a <radical>white</radical> wall when you realize the sound you''re making is actually a sick <kanji>beat</kanji>. You keep going and start singing along. Boo boo boop. Boo do do do doop! Finger beats!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2295', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8B%8D', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T19:32:30.500109Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T16:43:15.508035Z'),
(2296, 'kanji', '猶', '猶', 'An <radical>animal</radical> with <radical>horns</radical> is drinking our <radical>alcohol</radical>? <kanji>Still</kanji>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2296', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%8C%B6', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T21:07:46.31287Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:51:15.083936Z'),
(2297, 'kanji', '宰', '宰', 'Your <radical>roof</radical> is <radical>spicy</radical> because you''re the <kanji>manager</kanji>. You''re always testing new recipes in your house to share with everyone who works under you, but that makes all the spice stick to your roof (since it doesn''t have good ventilation like a restaurant or a lab would).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2297', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AE%B0', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T21:13:04.621983Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:24:37.385296Z'),
(2298, 'kanji', '寂', '寂', 'On the <radical>roof</radical> <radical>above</radical>, is a <radical>small</radical> <radical>stool</radical>. Anyone who sits in that stool will be <kanji>lonely</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2298', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%82', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T21:15:14.202981Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.355376Z'),
(2299, 'kanji', '縫', '縫', 'You take some <radical>thread</radical>, put your <radical>scooter</radical> up to a <radical>signpost</radical>, and <kanji>sew</kanji> them together. You don''t have a lock, so sewing your scooter up to the signpost to keep it safe is all you can think of.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2299', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B8%AB', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T21:24:32.671875Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.356109Z'),
(2300, 'kanji', '呉', '呉', 'You''ve learned that you can <radical>catapult</radical> things to <kanji>give</kanji> them to other people! Don''t catapult rocks at buildings and walls, catapult gifts to the people you love.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2300', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%91%89', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T21:33:11.021858Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.363277Z'),
(2301, 'kanji', '凡', '凡', 'You go to buy a <radical>table</radical> but it looks like it has water damage from <radical>drop</radical>s of water hitting it. This makes it a pretty <kanji>mediocre</kanji> table. The water drops haven''t really damaged it that much, it still stands and you can use it, but it''s kinda ugly, so it''s just mediocre.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2301', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%87%A1', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T21:34:40.431627Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.373237Z'),
(2302, 'kanji', '恭', '恭', 'When <radical>small</radical> <radical>drop</radical>s of water work <radical>together</radical> they gain <kanji>respect</kanji> for one another. If you end up working with someone long enough, and they end up being really great, you''ll respect them too! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2302', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%81%AD', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T21:36:58.981754Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:59:46.609728Z'),
(2303, 'kanji', '錯', '錯', 'This was pure <radical>gold</radical> <radical>long ago</radical>, but now it''s not. Someone got <kanji>confused</kanji> and <kanji>mixed</kanji> it with something else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2303', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8C%AF', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T21:41:12.501375Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:23:41.803791Z'),
(2304, 'kanji', '穀', '穀', 'A <radical>samurai</radical> hits the <radical>forehead</radical>s of <radical>grain</radical>s with his <radical>weapon</radical> so the <kanji>grain</kanji>s will pop off and into his mouth. He doesn''t want to kill the plants, so he''s just skimming the grain''s foreheads so they''ll fall off and he can eat them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2304', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A9%80', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T21:43:24.968208Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.396903Z'),
(2305, 'kanji', '陵', '陵', 'The only <radical>building</radical> you''ll get <radical>frostbite</radical> in is a <kanji>mausoleum</kanji>. Mausoleums and <kanji>tomb</kanji>s weren''t made for living people, so they aren''t insulated from the cold. No one cares if a dead body gets frostbite.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2305', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%B5', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T21:47:41.89566Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.400921Z'),
(2306, 'kanji', '弊', '弊', '<radical>Twenty</radical> <radical>penguin</radical>s together are always <kanji>evil</kanji>. Penguins are seen in groups a lot, sure, but did you know that any time there are exactly twenty penguins in a group they''re plotting evil things? Evil things like global warming. And how to eat all the fish in the world so humans can''t have any.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2306', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%8A', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T21:50:58.920978Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.407776Z'),
(2307, 'kanji', '舶', '舶', 'A <radical>boat</radical> that''s <radical>white</radical> isn''t just a boat, it''s a <kanji>ship</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2307', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%88%B6', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T21:54:47.921238Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.411563Z'),
(2308, 'kanji', '窮', '窮', 'Put a <radical>hole</radical> in someone''s <radical>body</radical> with a <radical>bow</radical> and they''ll become <kanji>destitute</kanji>. You put a hole in them. They can''t work. They can''t take care of their house or their kids. They''ve lost it all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2308', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AA%AE', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T22:08:28.766577Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:25:41.497205Z'),
(2309, 'kanji', '悦', '悦', 'If you can <radical>guard</radical> your <radical>soul</radical> from bad things you''ll always feel <kanji>delight</kanji> and <kanji>joy</kanji>. It''s hard to guard your soul from everything though, so take all the joy you can, while you can.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2309', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%82%A6', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T22:10:06.380466Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(2310, 'kanji', '縛', '縛', 'Your <radical>specialty</radical> is <radical>drop</radical>ping people with <radical>thread</radical> by <kanji>bind</kanji>ing them! You can drop anyone as long as you have enough thread to restrain them. It doesn''t matter how much they fight, you''ll get them to drop!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2310', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B8%9B', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T22:11:11.709064Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:36:48.276787Z'),
(2311, 'kanji', '轄', '轄', 'When you drive a <radical>car</radical>, everyone under your <radical>roof</radical> is putting their <radical>life</radical> in your hands, even if they don''t say it with their <radical>mouth</radical>—it''s still under your <kanji>control</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2311', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BD%84', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T22:14:37.968575Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:46:39.930103Z'),
(2312, 'kanji', '弦', '弦', 'This <radical>bow</radical> is <radical>mysterious</radical> because it''s strung with a single <kanji>chord</kanji>. I guess that makes the chord both a musical instrument <kanji>string</kanji> as well as a <kanji>bowstring</kanji>. Pluck the chord and it shoots an arrow while making a perfect G note. So mysterious!

Note: We''re using "chord" here as the fancy name for a musical instrument string (like in "the chords of a harp" or "strike a chord"). This kanji covers both musical strings and bowstrings!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2312', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%A6', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T22:15:49.910535Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:48:13.12669Z'),
(2313, 'kanji', '窒', '窒', 'Take a <radical>mole</radical> out of its <radical>hole</radical> and it will <kanji>suffocate</kanji>. They need to be in tight quarters, unlike us humans. So when you take one out of its nice, safe hole, it thinks it can''t breathe and suffocates.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2313', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AA%92', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T22:19:22.947221Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.465373Z'),
(2314, 'kanji', '洪', '洪', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> <radical>together</radical> with another tsunami is going to cause a massive <kanji>flood</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2314', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B4%AA', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T22:21:04.399564Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:59:46.609728Z'),
(2315, 'kanji', '摂', '摂', 'Put your <radical>fingers</radical> in your <radical>ear</radical>s when there''s an <radical>explosion</radical> <kanji>in addition</kanji> to all the other things you were told to do. You have to crouch under a desk, you have to stay away from windows, you have to close your eyes. But in addition to that, you need to protect your ears!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2315', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%91%82', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T22:37:47.941636Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.471102Z'),
(2316, 'kanji', '飽', '飽', 'You <radical>eat</radical> and then <radical>wrap</radical> yourself into a ball because you''re <kanji>bored</kanji>. You''re also <kanji>sated</kanji>, because you ate just the right amount, but now you have nothing to do and it''s soooo booooring.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2316', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%A3%BD', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T22:42:47.013171Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.474014Z'),
(2317, 'kanji', '紳', '紳', 'Your <radical>thread</radical>s are <radical>humble</radical> and that makes you a perfect <kanji>gentleman</kanji>. You don''t have to have fancy clothes to be a gentleman, just humble ones that don''t stand out and show off too much. That shows how polite you are.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2317', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%B3', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T22:43:52.345597Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:25:51.125444Z'),
(2318, 'kanji', '庸', '庸', 'Under this <radical>canopy</radical> you have a <radical>brush</radical> (in its 肀 form) and a <radical>task</radical> to complete with it. This is totally <kanji>commonplace</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2318', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BA%B8', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T22:46:13.392352Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.491528Z'),
(2319, 'kanji', '靖', '靖', '<radical>Stand</radical> staring up at the <radical>blue</radical> sky and you''ll feel more <kanji>peaceful</kanji> than you ever have. Blue is a color that promotes peaceful thoughts and feelings. That''s why all the social media sites are blue. To stop you from being a jerk.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2319', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9D%96', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T22:47:46.768681Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:22:25.515687Z'),
(2320, 'kanji', '嘉', '嘉', 'If when you speak it sounds like the <radical>power</radical> of a <radical>drum</radical> is coming out of your <radical>mouth</radical>, then people will hold you in high <kanji>esteem</kanji>. Anyone who has a loud, booming, powerful voice will be held in high esteem in this day and age. Look at all those awesome musicians who have booming voices!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2320', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%98%89', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T16:30:49.958471Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.509547Z'),
(2321, 'kanji', '搾', '搾', 'If you want to fit your <radical>fingers</radical> into a <radical>key</radical> <radical>hole</radical>, you have to <kanji>squeeze</kanji> them in. Fingers aren''t exactly supposed to fit in key holes... they''re for keys, after all. But you want to squeeze your finger on in there anyway to unlock the door.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2321', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%90%BE', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T16:42:24.638181Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.52506Z'),
(2322, 'kanji', '蝶', '蝶', 'The <radical>insect</radical> that is amongst all the <radical>world</radical>''s <radical>tree</radical>s is the <kanji>butterfly</kanji>. They are always hanging around trees, those butterflies.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2322', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%9D%B6', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T16:44:18.818515Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T02:20:41.769887Z'),
(2323, 'kanji', '碑', '碑', 'A <radical>stone</radical> <radical>drop</radical>s from the sky into a <radical>rice paddy</radical> and then a <radical>cross</radical> <radical>drop</radical>s on top of it. They''ve combined to form a <kanji>tombstone</kanji>! Wait, does that mean a body fell from the sky first and you just didn''t see it?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2323', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A2%91', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T16:47:02.78265Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.535616Z'),
(2324, 'kanji', '尉', '尉', 'Who loves their <radical>flag</radical>, using <radical>jackhammer</radical>s, and precise <radical>measurement</radical>s? Your <kanji>military officer</kanji>! He''s always saluting the flag, jackhammering near all the new recruits to test them, and checking the measurement between the floor and their pants. Who else could it be?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2324', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B0%89', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T16:52:41.439372Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.53778Z'),
(2326, 'kanji', '匠', '匠', 'There is a <radical>cage</radical> with an <radical>axe</radical> in it above your house to show that you are an <kanji>artisan</kanji>. All of the artisans have to have this special sign somewhere near where they live to show they make things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2326', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8C%A0', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T16:55:19.308774Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:42:03.348336Z'),
(2327, 'kanji', '遥', '遥', 'The <radical>scooter</radical> <radical>satellite</radical> is the satellite that is always <kanji>far off</kanji> and <kanji>far away</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2327', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%A5', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T16:56:26.209672Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:37:07.777753Z'),
(2328, 'kanji', '智', '智', 'You shoot an <radical>arrow</radical> at the <radical>mouth</radical> of the <radical>sun</radical> so that it will grant you a gift of <kanji>wisdom</kanji>. You heard about a Greek hero doing this and the god in the sun gave him the wisdom to see everything. You''re hoping it will work for you too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2328', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%99%BA', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T16:58:56.312404Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:35:02.352386Z'),
(2330, 'kanji', '賊', '賊', 'When a <radical>shellfish</radical> becomes a <radical>drunkard</radical> and sees a <radical>cross</radical>, they''ll become a <kanji>robber</kanji>. Because they''ll steal the cross!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2330', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B3%8A', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T21:21:37.426882Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.589348Z'),
(2331, 'kanji', '鼓', '鼓', 'You take a <radical>drum</radical> and a <radical>branch</radical> and... well, what else would you do? You would <kanji>drum</kanji> or <kanji>beat</kanji> them together.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2331', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%BC%93', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T21:23:42.171967Z', NULL, '2025-11-15T22:11:20.387838Z'),
(2332, 'kanji', '旋', '旋', 'Move your <radical>coat rack</radical> in one <radical>direction</radical> by shooting it with a <radical>gun</radical>. Then you can keep shooting it to get a good <kanji>rotation</kanji> going. If you think there is a better way to make your coat rack make <kanji>revolution</kanji>s, you''re probably wrong. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2332', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%97%8B', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T21:27:58.977817Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:18:18.202602Z'),
(2333, 'kanji', '腸', '腸', 'The <radical>moon</radical> is <radical>easy</radical> tonight, so you decide to cut it open. But, the moon wasn''t the moon, it was your big, pale, round tummy. You just cut it open, and now you can see your <kanji>intestines</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2333', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%85%B8', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T21:29:36.256648Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.598533Z'),
(2334, 'kanji', '槽', '槽', 'A <radical>tree</radical> melts into the <radical>ground</radical> after listening to loud <radical>music</radical> in the hot <radical>sun</radical>, so you have to find a nice big <kanji>tank</kanji> to scoop the melted tree into. The music made the tree vibrate at juuust the right frequency that it, combined with the hot sun, made the tree turn into a puddle. You can''t just leave it being all blobby on the ground, so you find a tank for it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2334', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A7%BD', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T21:31:26.894376Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:46:29.409938Z'),
(2335, 'kanji', '伐', '伐', 'If the <radical>leader</radical> of your gang is a <radical>drunkard</radical> he''ll try to have you <kanji>fell</kanji> trees thinking they''re rival gang members. You all know they aren''t but you can''t argue with your leader, especially if he''s drunk. Go ahead and <kanji>attack</kanji> the trees anyway.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2335', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BC%90', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T21:33:30.423384Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T19:45:25.87001Z'),
(2337, 'kanji', '坪', '坪', 'The amount of <radical>dirt</radical> you need to get some <radical>peace</radical> is <kanji>3.3 square meters</kanji>. This is roughly the area of two tatami mats, so some might call it a <kanji>two-mat area</kanji>. Not only is it the perfect amount of space for finding peace, but it''s also a pretty common measurement in Japan (called a <kanji>tsubo</kanji>), so it''s worth getting familiar with.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2337', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9D%AA', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T21:36:32.21445Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:24:18.997304Z'),
(2338, 'kanji', '紺', '紺', 'If you want to turn your <radical>thread</radical> into a <radical>sweet</radical> color you just HAVE to go with <kanji>navy blue</kanji> or <kanji>dark blue</kanji>. That''s the sweetest color in existence, everyone knows that. After all, blue raspberry flavor is dark blue and it''s super sweet.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2338', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%BA', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T21:40:19.852612Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.631168Z'),
(2339, 'kanji', '羅', '羅', 'You have a <radical>net</radical> of <radical>thread</radical> for catching a <radical>turkey</radical>. But, it must be <kanji>spread out</kanji> and you must <kanji>arrange</kanji> it first.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2339', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BE%85', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T21:46:18.836627Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(2340, 'kanji', '峡', '峡', 'To the <radical>mountain</radical>s you took your <radical>horned</radical> <radical>husband</radical> for you two to get married. (Society wouldn''t accept a horned husband!) To hide even more from the prying eyes of society, you take your horned husband into a <kanji>ravine</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2340', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B3%A1', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T21:47:49.963393Z', NULL, '2026-03-12T07:45:30.720695Z'),
(2341, 'kanji', '俸', '俸', 'The <radical>leader</radical> says <radical>spring</radical> is looking <radical>dry</radical>, so you''ll need your <kanji>salary</kanji> in advance.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2341', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BF%B8', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T22:21:00.679642Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:22:51.842327Z'),
(2342, 'kanji', '醸', '醸', 'An <radical>alcohol</radical> <radical>nurse</radical> is someone who nurses alcohol. In other words, they <kanji>brew</kanji> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2342', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%86%B8', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T22:45:41.78892Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T18:25:18.129687Z'),
(2343, 'kanji', '弔', '弔', 'You give a <radical>bow</radical> and a <radical>stick</radical> to your friend''s family. This is because your friend died, and he loved bows and sticks. You give this to his family, to give our <kanji>condolence</kanji>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2343', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BC%94', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T22:50:59.182323Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T17:45:53.93144Z'),
(2344, 'kanji', '乙', '乙', 'Your <radical>nose</radical> isn''t the first thing people see about your face, it''s the <kanji>latter</kanji>. They see your eyes THEN your nose, then your mouth, in order going down. Just label them A <kanji>B</kanji> and C and you''ll never forget!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2344', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%B9%99', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T22:54:24.48921Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.685534Z'),
(2345, 'kanji', '遍', '遍', 'Riding a <radical>scooter</radical> to the <radical>library</radical> is a <kanji>universal</kanji> experience. People young and old have all, at some point, taken a scooter to the library, picked up a book, used the internet, sat in the comfy chairs, then left via that same scooter.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2345', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%8D', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T22:55:37.314865Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T09:25:02.608138Z'),
(2346, 'kanji', '衡', '衡', 'You <radical>go</radical> to a <radical>big</radical> <radical>rice paddy</radical> and establish a <radical>prison</radical> right in the middle of it to restore the <kanji>equilibrium</kanji> of the area.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2346', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A1%A1', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T23:07:22.519525Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(2348, 'kanji', '閲', '閲', 'Put a <radical>guard</radical> at your <radical>gate</radical> to get a good <kanji>inspection</kanji> of your neighborhood going. The guard will stand there all day inspecting everything and then report it all back to you. You won''t have to do any of the inspection yourself!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2348', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%96%B2', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T23:14:46.562251Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.734046Z'),
(2349, 'kanji', '喝', '喝', 'If you put your <radical>spoon</radical> in the <radical>sun</radical> while you''re in <radical>prison</radical>, a <radical>mouth</radical> will appear to <kanji>scold</kanji> you. You can never see who the mouth belongs to, but considering you''re in prison it''s probably a guard. Either way, that person doesn''t like what you''re doing and they keep scolding you when you put your spoon out into the sun.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2349', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%96%9D', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T23:16:05.977692Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(2350, 'kanji', '敢', '敢', 'An <radical>elf</radical> who ventures outside in <radical>winter</radical> has a lot of <kanji>daring</kanji>. Elf ears are very, very delicate, and the winter cold can really mess them up. So if you see an elf adventuring in winter, know that that particular elf is a really daring dude.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2350', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%95%A2', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T23:18:44.17301Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T20:08:33.258173Z'),
(2351, 'kanji', '膜', '膜', 'If you trap the <radical>moon</radical> in a <radical>greenhouse</radical> it will grow a creepy <kanji>membrane</kanji> around itself. The moon isn''t used to the heat it would be exposed to in a greenhouse so that membrane is there to protect it. It''s really gross looking though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2351', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%86%9C', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T23:19:41.913812Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.745824Z'),
(2352, 'kanji', '盲', '盲', 'The <radical>death</radical> of your <radical>eye</radical>s means that you''ve gone <kanji>blind</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2352', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9B%B2', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T23:21:38.187556Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.747142Z'),
(2353, 'kanji', '胎', '胎', 'The <radical>machine</radical> in your body that is powered by the <radical>moon</radical> is your <kanji>womb</kanji>, also called your <kanji>uterus</kanji>! Haven''t you ever wondered why periods happen with the revolutions of the moon? That''s because it powers your uterus and tells it what to do!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2353', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%83%8E', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T23:22:52.97262Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T17:49:15.447292Z'),
(2354, 'kanji', '酵', '酵', '<radical>Alcohol</radical> is a good <radical>teacher</radical> on the process of <kanji>fermentation</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2354', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%85%B5', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T23:23:56.127241Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:52:39.90813Z'),
(2355, 'kanji', '堕', '堕', 'When <radical>building</radical>s <radical>have</radical> <radical>dirt</radical> piling up all around them, it''s a sign of their <kanji>degradation</kanji>.
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2355', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A0%95', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T23:25:37.140886Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T17:44:11.29193Z'),
(2356, 'kanji', '遮', '遮', 'You''re riding your <radical>scooter</radical> toward your <radical>yurt</radical> as fast as you can because someone is trying to get in there to <radical>boil</radical> your water and you want to <kanji>intercept</kanji> them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2356', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%AE', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T23:26:35.504172Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:30:22.144103Z'),
(2358, 'kanji', '凸', '凸', 'The <radical>convex</radical> radical and the <kanji>convex</kanji> kanji are the same, so hopefully you now just know them both.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2358', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%87%B8', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T23:29:47.809734Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.803973Z'),
(2359, 'kanji', '凹', '凹', 'The <radical>concave</radical> radical and the <kanji>concave</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2359', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%87%B9', 57, 1, '2015-07-10T23:30:49.466953Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.807736Z'),
(2360, 'kanji', '楓', '楓', 'A <radical>tree</radical> that does well in the <radical>wind</radical> is a <kanji>maple</kanji> tree. Maple trees don''t have fruit so the wind can''t knock anything off of it. That''s why they thrive well in windy places!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2360', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A5%93', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:05:43.677036Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.813202Z'),
(2361, 'kanji', '哉', '哉', 'A <radical>drunkard</radical> has <radical>dirt</radical> in his <radical>mouth</radical> as he tries to talk to you. You walk over and he spits a <kanji>question mark</kanji> into your hand. It''s covered in dirt and spit. Um... does this mean he''s confused? He probably doesn''t even know where he is. But how did he make this question mark and why was it in his mouth?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2361', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%93%89', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:08:47.372146Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.816494Z'),
(2362, 'kanji', '蒼', '蒼', '<radical>Flowers</radical> grown in a <radical>warehouse</radical> will be very <kanji>pale</kanji>. They never see the sun, so they don''t grow to have bright colors.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2362', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%92%BC', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:21:34.94916Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.818652Z'),
(2364, 'kanji', '萌', '萌', 'You have to put <radical>flowers</radical> in a <radical>bright</radical> place if you want them to <kanji>sprout</kanji>. If they''re left in the shade they''ll just rot.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2364', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%90%8C', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:33:08.696546Z', NULL, '2025-10-29T11:43:10.3513Z'),
(9397, 'kanji', '蚕', '蚕', '<radical>Heaven</radical>''s favorite <radical>insect</radical> is the <kanji>silkworm</kanji>. Silkworms are really prized in heaven because they produce all the silk used to make those flowy white angel robes and those fluffy clouds everyone walks around on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9397', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%9A%95', 57, 1, '2025-12-21T21:30:02.080975Z', NULL, '2026-03-05T17:22:34.862442Z'),
(2363, 'kanji', '瑠', '瑠', 'The <radical>king</radical> will send his men with <radical>sword</radical>s into your <radical>private</radical> <radical>rice paddy</radical> if you come across <kanji>lapis lazuli</kanji>. Even though this is your private land, he wants that lapis lazuli to make some new crowns and thrones for himself. Plus he''s the king, so he can do whatever he wants.

If you''ve never taken an art history class (or played Minecraft) you may never have come across lapis lazuli, but think of it as a pretty, blue precious stone that people really, really liked in the olden days.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2363', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%91%A0', 58, 1, '2015-07-13T17:30:37.917445Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.839114Z'),
(2365, 'kanji', '硫', '硫', 'There are <radical>stone</radical>s that smell like <radical>trash</radical> in the <radical>river</radical>. You reach in and realize these rocks are made of <kanji>sulfur</kanji>. Even the flow of this water can''t hide the stink of these sulfur rocks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2365', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A1%AB', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:10:19.900009Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.845865Z'),
(2366, 'kanji', '赦', '赦', 'If you spill something <radical>red</radical> on yourself in <radical>winter</radical> you should <kanji>pardon</kanji> yourself immediately and try to find something else to wear. It''s very dangerous to wear red in winter, and even if it seems rude, make sure you pardon yourself right away and get that taken care of!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2366', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%B5%A6', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:13:38.190405Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:32:56.473524Z'),
(2367, 'kanji', '窃', '窃', 'You see <radical>seven</radical> <radical>sword</radical>s just sitting in a <radical>hole</radical> and so you decide to <kanji>steal</kanji> them. They were just left in that hole after all, it''s not like anyone was using them. And even if they were, they''ll never know you were the one to steal them, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2367', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AA%83', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:15:47.50286Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:18:34.194718Z'),
(2368, 'kanji', '慨', '慨', 'Deep in your <radical>soul</radical>, you feel like you want to pull your <radical>fang</radical>s out by the <radical>root</radical>, but instead you let out a loud <kanji>sigh</kanji>. You''re one of the only people you know that has fangs, and it weighs heavily on your soul.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2368', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%85%A8', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:17:43.611324Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(2369, 'kanji', '扶', '扶', 'Put your <radical>fingers</radical> on your <radical>husband</radical> to <kanji>aid</kanji> him. There''s something about a spouse''s touch that just makes you feel better. Have you ever had someone rub your back while you were hurt or sick or sad? It''s nice, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2369', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%89%B6', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:18:56.335275Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.876306Z'),
(2370, 'kanji', '戯', '戯', 'Throw a <radical>drunkard</radical> in a cage with a <radical>tiger</radical> and some <radical>spikes</radical> and the tiger will have the time of its life <kanji>play</kanji>ing with them! Think of all the tiger play time possibilities! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2370', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%88%AF', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:20:03.859708Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T16:24:29.442417Z'),
(2372, 'kanji', '濁', '濁', 'If a <radical>tsunami</radical> gets into a <radical>net</radical> that''s im<radical>prison</radical>ing a bunch of <radical>insect</radical>s, the insects are going to get all <kanji>muddy</kanji>. The net gets dragged along with the tsunami water and the insects can''t escape, so they just get covered in the yucky stuff.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2372', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BF%81', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:22:45.176205Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:41:09.810584Z'),
(2373, 'kanji', '奔', '奔', 'You''re walking down the street when you see <radical>twenty</radical> <radical>big</radical> <radical>cross</radical>es lined up in front of you. Crosses creep you out and this is way too many for you to keep walking so you decide to <kanji>run</kanji> as fast as you can to get past them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2373', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A5%94', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:24:20.135056Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.908002Z'),
(2374, 'kanji', '肖', '肖', 'You see a <radical>triceratops</radical> standing in front of a full <radical>moon</radical> and you can''t help but see a really strong <kanji>resemblance</kanji>. The triceratops has that round frill thing behind its horns and it looks just like the round full moon. Who knew that a triceratops would resemble the moon!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2374', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%82%96', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:25:18.51354Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.91637Z'),
(2375, 'kanji', '朽', '朽', 'A <radical>tree</radical> is stuck in a <radical>prison</radical> under the <radical>ground</radical> and it''s starting to <kanji>rot</kanji> and <kanji>decay</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2375', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9C%BD', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:26:21.879368Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(2376, 'kanji', '殻', '殻', 'A <radical>samurai</radical> puts his <radical>forehead</radical> on the <radical>table</radical> next to his <radical>weapon</radical>. He is just a <kanji>husk</kanji> of his former self. This <kanji>shell</kanji> of a samurai has nothing left.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2376', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AE%BB', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:29:10.523143Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.921797Z'),
(2377, 'kanji', '享', '享', 'The <radical>receive</radical> radical and <kanji>receive</kanji> kanji are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2377', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%AB', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:30:26.941036Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.932812Z'),
(2378, 'kanji', '藩', '藩', 'You have to reach a certain <radical>number</radical> of <radical>flowers</radical> and <radical>tsunami</radical>s hits to become a legitimate <kanji>fiefdom</kanji>. At least in the old days. If you don''t meet one of these conditions, like if you get hit by tsunamis all the time but you only have two or three flowers that will grow, you can''t be your own fiefdom.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2378', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%97%A9', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:31:37.842322Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.956142Z'),
(2379, 'kanji', '媒', '媒', 'A <radical>sweet</radical> <radical>woman</radical> sits under a <radical>tree</radical> and works as a <kanji>mediator</kanji>. If you''re having a fight with someone, bring them to the sweet woman under the tree and she''ll mediate for the two of you. Problem solved! Thanks sweet lady!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2379', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AA%92', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:35:34.343652Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(2380, 'kanji', '鶏', '鶏', 'Your <radical>husband</radical> was wearing his <radical>cleat</radical>s when he accidentally stepped on a <radical>bird</radical>. Luckily this particular bird is a <kanji>chicken</kanji>, and chickens are really durable!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2380', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%B6%8F', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:36:53.964773Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:34:39.359876Z'),
(2381, 'kanji', '嘱', '嘱', 'If you want to take a <radical>flag</radical> out of the <radical>mouth</radical> of a <radical>mantis</radical> you have to make a formal <kanji>request</kanji> first. Mantises don''t like to just give things away, and they''re kind of mean so if you try to steal the flag it will probably bite you. So make a formal, polite request if you want it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2381', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%98%B1', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:38:03.634746Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.960168Z'),
(2382, 'kanji', '迭', '迭', 'When your <radical>scooter</radical> has a <radical>fault</radical>, you need to get an <kanji>alternate</kanji>. Hopefully your insurance will cover it too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2382', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%BF%AD', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:39:32.257291Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:37:07.819796Z'),
(2383, 'kanji', '椎', '椎', 'This <radical>tree</radical> <radical>turkey</radical> has been sitting directly on your <kanji>spine</kanji> for weeks straight. The pain is starting to become unbearable, and it seems like no one wants to help you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2383', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A4%8E', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:40:44.530701Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(2385, 'kanji', '陪', '陪', 'The <radical>building</radical> <radical>clown</radical> is the only clown allowed to <kanji>accompany</kanji> you. You hate all clowns, and so does the rest of your town. But this clown is really good at building buildings, and he''s allowed to leave his part of town and accompany you while you fix buildings together.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2385', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%99%AA', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:46:07.026074Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.010897Z'),
(2386, 'kanji', '剖', '剖', 'A <radical>clown</radical> takes a <radical>knife</radical> and <kanji>divide</kanji>s himself. First he divides himself into two pieces, then he''s in four pieces, then... oh why won''t he stop?!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2386', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%89%96', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:47:35.509054Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.022344Z'),
(2387, 'kanji', '譜', '譜', 'If you <radical>say</radical> the <radical>lineup</radical> of your family members that have lived on this earth under the <radical>sun</radical>, you''re really explaining your <kanji>genealogy</kanji>.

This kanji has a second meaning too. It means score, as in a musical score or sheet music. So if your sheet music <radical>say</radical>s the <radical>line up</radical> of chords, especially <radical>sun</radical>nny notes, that''s the <kanji>score</kanji> of a song. Those sunny notes are loved by your ancestors.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2387', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AD%9C', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:57:06.533318Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:48:57.246886Z'),
(2388, 'kanji', '淑', '淑', 'You can only manage to avoid a <radical>tsunami</radical> by standing <radical>above</radical> it on a <radical>small</radical> <radical>stool</radical> if you are very <kanji>graceful</kanji>. If you aren''t graceful enough the tsunami will knock you and the stool over and you''ll be swept away like everyone else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2388', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B7%91', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T16:58:34.540147Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.031068Z'),
(2389, 'kanji', '帆', '帆', 'You hoist your <radical>towel</radical> above your <radical>table</radical>, <radical>drop</radical> into the water and <kanji>sail</kanji> away. You turned the towel into an actual sail and you''re balancing on top of the table, which is now a boat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2389', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B8%86', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:00:21.081209Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.031705Z'),
(2390, 'kanji', '憤', '憤', 'You see a <radical>soul</radical> on a <radical>cross</radical> covered in <radical>flowers</radical> with <radical>shellfish</radical> at its feet and you suddenly <kanji>resent</kanji> them. It''s not like you want to be the one up there on the cross, but so many people left flowers and shellfish for them. It should have been you...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2390', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%86%A4', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:02:38.287284Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:33:25.633876Z'),
(2391, 'kanji', '酌', '酌', 'Pour all of your <radical>alcohol</radical> in the <radical>pool</radical> to <kanji>serve</kanji> all your guests at once! They can all help themselves to as much or as little alcohol as they want! What a great idea!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2391', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%85%8C', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:05:21.731467Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:49:14.754352Z'),
(2392, 'kanji', '暁', '暁', '<radical>Gambler</radical>s usually only see the <radical>sun</radical> at <kanji>dawn</kanji>. They gamble all day and through the night, and if they leave it''s at the crack of dawn to get home, clean up, and then go back. I haven''t known a gambler who''s seen the sun ... well ever!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2392', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9A%81', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:06:19.948401Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.059278Z'),
(2393, 'kanji', '傑', '傑', 'The <radical>leader</radical> does a <radical>dance</radical> on top of the <radical>tree</radical> to show his <kanji>greatness</kanji> to everyone around. If he dances all the way up there on top of the tree, then no one can deny his <kanji>excellence</kanji>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2393', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%82%91', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:07:53.538875Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.069302Z'),
(2394, 'kanji', '錠', '錠', 'This is a <radical>gold</radical> <radical>roof</radical>, <radical>correct</radical>? Better put a <kanji>lock</kanji> on it, then.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2394', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8C%A0', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:09:03.459604Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:31:27.51168Z'),
(2395, 'kanji', '凌', '凌', 'If you touch <radical>ice</radical> for too long you''ll get <radical>frostbite</radical> and that''s a really hard thing to <kanji>endure</kanji>. A lot of people can''t, and die. Then again, you managed to endure touching ice long enough to get frostbite in the first place, so maybe this is baby stuff for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2395', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%87%8C', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:11:07.126319Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.098287Z'),
(2396, 'kanji', '瑞', '瑞', 'The <radical>king</radical> <radical>rake</radical>d all the <radical>mountain</radical>s down into flat land so he could see everyone in the kingdom from his castle windows. You should share your <kanji>congratulations</kanji> with him. Now, quick tell him this was a great idea.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2396', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%91%9E', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:12:46.497793Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.09732Z'),
(2399, 'kanji', '璃', '璃', 'The <radical>king</radical> wants you to make a <radical>rocket</radical> that looks <kanji>glassy</kanji>. It won''t be very stable, and it probably won''t work, but making it glassy and pretty is the only way to make the king approve.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2399', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%92%83', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:26:16.012734Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.14131Z'),
(2371, 'kanji', '忌', '忌', 'If you only let <radical>oneself</radical> (yourself) into your <radical>heart</radical>, you''ll end up <kanji>mourning</kanji> all of the loves and friendships you could have had but didn''t let in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2371', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BF%8C', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T16:21:02.435646Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.887884Z'),
(2400, 'kanji', '遷', '遷', 'Going from a <radical>scooter</radical> to a <radical>helicopter</radical> that''s <radical>big</radical> even for yourself (<radical>oneself</radical>), is a huge <kanji>transition</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2400', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%81%B7', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T21:33:49.807483Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.140682Z'),
(2401, 'kanji', '拙', '拙', 'You have to use your <radical>fingers</radical> to make an <radical>exit</radical> because you''re so <kanji>clumsy</kanji>. You can''t seem to get the door to open, your hands just slip around on the knob uselessly. Instead, you take your fingers and stick them into the door frame, pulling the whole thing off the hinges so you can leave.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2401', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8B%99', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T21:34:54.713263Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.147529Z'),
(2402, 'kanji', '峠', '峠', 'If there are <radical>mountain</radical>s <radical>above</radical> and <radical>below</radical> you, that means you''re on a <kanji>mountain pass</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2402', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B3%A0', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T21:37:36.3274Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:13:57.732225Z'),
(2403, 'kanji', '篤', '篤', 'If someone makes a <radical>bamboo</radical> <radical>horse</radical>, you know it''s <kanji>deliberate</kanji>. You can''t accidentally make an entire horse out of bamboo.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2403', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%AF%A4', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T21:52:20.049113Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.172034Z'),
(2405, 'kanji', '雌', '雌', 'If you can <radical>stop</radical> a <radical>turkey</radical> by putting a <radical>spoon</radical> in its face you''ll know it''s a <kanji>female</kanji> turkey. A male turkey will peck the spoon out of your hand and run away. But a female turkey will wonder why you''re putting a spoon there, stop, and look up at you wondering if you''re okay.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2405', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%9B%8C', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T21:59:04.666051Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(2406, 'kanji', '堪', '堪', 'If there is <radical>dirt</radical> in a <radical>crab trap</radical>, it''s a lot for the crabs to <kanji>endure</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2406', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A0%AA', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:00:44.736835Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.208286Z'),
(2407, 'kanji', '吟', '吟', 'We want you to say things with your <radical>mouth</radical> right <radical>now</radical> because it''s <kanji>recital</kanji> time! Go, perform please. What do you mean you didn''t know the recital was right now? Well, tough luck. Recite!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2407', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%90%9F', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:04:28.154842Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.206509Z'),
(2408, 'kanji', '甚', '甚', 'Making a mnemonic for a <radical>crab trap</radical> is <kanji>VERY</kanji> hard. So hard that I couldn''t come up with one. But it''s very possible that one of you will!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2408', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%94%9A', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:12:14.074648Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:25:37.675733Z'),
(2409, 'kanji', '崇', '崇', 'Under the <radical>mountain</radical>''s <radical>roof</radical> are people using <radical>jackhammer</radical>s to <kanji>worship</kanji> the gods. The noise from their jackhammering echos up through the mountain and up toward the sky where the gods live. As long as each person uses their own jackhammer, the sound will be loud enough to reach them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2409', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B4%87', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:15:00.825233Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.210519Z'),
(2410, 'kanji', '漆', '漆', 'A <radical>tsunami</radical> hits a <radical>tree</radical> and your <radical>hat</radical> protects you from the <radical>water</radical>, but the hat itself is now covered in <kanji>lacquer</kanji>. You take it off and see that it''s shiny and hard and protected.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2410', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BC%86', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:18:48.142188Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.215223Z'),
(2411, 'kanji', '岬', '岬', 'You see a <radical>mountain</radical> in the shape of a <radical>turtle shell</radical> sticking out into the ocean. It''s a <kanji>cape</kanji>! No, not the kind you wear, the geographical kind. When you''re on land, most capes look like turtle shell mountains sticking out into the sea. Do you know any capes like that near where you live?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2411', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B2%AC', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:20:40.400314Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.227628Z'),
(2412, 'kanji', '紡', '紡', 'Move <radical>thread</radical> in the right <radical>direction</radical> and you''ll end up <kanji>spinning</kanji> it into yarn. Then you can spin that yarn into a sweater or a scarf with your favorite half tofu half fugu mascot on it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2412', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%B4%A1', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:24:45.086873Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.248059Z'),
(2413, 'kanji', '礁', '礁', 'The <radical>stone</radical> that looks like a <radical>turkey</radical> <radical>boil</radical>ing underwater is just a <kanji>reef</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2413', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A4%81', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:36:05.81419Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:38:54.284696Z'),
(2414, 'kanji', '屯', '屯', 'The <radical>barracks</radical> radical and the <kanji>barracks</kanji> kanji are the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2414', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%B1%AF', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:37:13.621775Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.255116Z'),
(2415, 'kanji', '姻', '姻', 'You meet a <radical>woman</radical> whose <radical>mouth</radical> is <radical>big</radical> and you instantly know this is the person you want to <kanji>marry</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2415', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A7%BB', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:49:10.248152Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(2416, 'kanji', '擬', '擬', 'You try to use your <radical>fingers</radical> to cast <radical>doubt</radical> on people by using them to <kanji>imitate</kanji> each other. Right now you''re using your fingers as finger puppets, imitating one of your friends to the others.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2416', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%93%AC', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:52:21.926347Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.269316Z'),
(2417, 'kanji', '睦', '睦', 'If you see someone <radical>eye</radical>ing your <radical>womb</radical> you''re probably going to be weirded out by how <kanji>friendly</kanji> they''re acting. You only let people who are really, really close to you look at your womb. So if this guy is acting that friendly he should have introduced himself first.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2417', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9D%A6', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:53:30.528523Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.282911Z'),
(2418, 'kanji', '閑', '閑', 'You see a <radical>gate</radical> with a <radical>tree</radical> growing through it and it reminds you that you need more <kanji>leisure</kanji> time. You walk up to the gate tree and curl up under its branches. No one will bother you because the tree is blocking the gate from being used.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2418', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%96%91', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:56:22.461024Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.279291Z'),
(2419, 'kanji', '曹', '曹', 'When under<radical>ground</radical> <radical>music</radical> makes it out into the light of the <radical>sun</radical> (the public), it finally becomes <kanji>official</kanji>. Music isn''t really real unless people hear it outside of those secret underground concerts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2419', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9B%B9', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T22:57:45.516671Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:46:29.409938Z'),
(2420, 'kanji', '詠', '詠', 'If you want to <radical>say</radical> how <radical>eternity</radical> sounds you have to <kanji>compose</kanji> a poem. Or an essay. Or a song. <kanji>Recite</kanji> those deep thoughts about eternity and impress everyone who hears it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2420', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%A9%A0', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T23:02:39.300703Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.321019Z'),
(2421, 'kanji', '卑', '卑', 'You <radical>drop</radical> to the ground in a <radical>rice paddy</radical>, and then <radical>drop</radical> down in front of the <radical>cross</radical> you keep on you because you''re trying to make yourself as <kanji>lowly</kanji> as you can in front of God. He said to lower yourself and this is the only way you can interpret that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2421', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%91', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T23:03:53.634844Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.321758Z'),
(2422, 'kanji', '侮', '侮', '<radical>Every</radical> <radical>leader</radical> will come to <kanji>despise</kanji> their followers. In the beginning everything is fine and dandy, but eventually the leader''s followers will ask for more and more things, eventually ridiculous things, and the leader will really despise them for it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2422', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BE%AE', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T23:08:13.628939Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T22:24:13.419382Z'),
(2423, 'kanji', '鋳', '鋳', 'You pour molten <radical>gold</radical> down <radical>three</radical> <radical>slide</radical>s while taking <radical>measurement</radical>s in order to make a <kanji>cast</kanji> of something. Whatever it is you''re casting, it requires a lot of gold (three slides'' worth!) and some very precise measurements.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2423', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%8B%B3', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T23:10:11.107156Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:46:12.265763Z'),
(2425, 'kanji', '胆', '胆', 'If you stay up so long that you see the <radical>moon</radical> go down and the <radical>dawn</radical> come, people will say you have serious <kanji>guts</kanji>. It takes guts to stay up that late. More specifically, it takes a powerful <kanji>gallbladder</kanji>. Your gallbladder is sensitive to sleep changes, you know, so you risk a lot by staying up like that. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2425', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%83%86', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T23:15:12.629547Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.338225Z'),
(2426, 'kanji', '浪', '浪', 'The <radical>tsunami</radical> that is <radical>good</radical>. This fabled creature has never been seen, but you <kanji>wander</kanji> the lands in search of it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2426', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B5%AA', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T23:16:32.419495Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.359979Z'),
(2427, 'kanji', '禍', '禍', 'A <radical>spirit</radical> wearing two <radical>mustache</radical>s must be <kanji>evil</kanji>. Everyone knows a mustache makes you evil, but two? And on a spirit? Yup, definitely evil.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2427', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%A6%8D', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T23:17:42.558471Z', NULL, '2025-12-21T20:51:22.632515Z'),
(2428, 'kanji', '酪', '酪', 'You can only give a <radical>kiss</radical> of <radical>alcohol</radical> to <kanji>dairy</kanji>. If you add anymore than a kiss the dairy will curdle and you won''t be able to drink it anymore. Bleh.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2428', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%85%AA', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T23:18:51.358433Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:20:45.121776Z'),
(2430, 'kanji', '慶', '慶', 'If a pilot manages to land under a <radical>canopy</radical> with a <radical>helicopter</radical> that''s pierced with a sideways <radical>barb</radical> in the <radical>heart</radical> of <radical>winter</radical> you should <kanji>congratulate</kanji> them! People should always be congratulated for doing good things, and this is one of the best things you''ve ever heard of!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2430', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%85%B6', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T23:37:39.649627Z', NULL, '2025-10-16T19:53:28.786524Z'),
(2431, 'kanji', '亜', '亜', 'The kanji and the radical that look like this are exactly the same. They are both <kanji>Asia</kanji>. 

This can also mean <kanji>sub-</kanji>. As in subtropics, subspecies, subhuman… That kind of sub-! When it''s not in a place name, 亜 will usually be using its sub- meaning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2431', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E4%BA%9C', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T23:49:42.163361Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:43:14.192115Z'),
(2432, 'kanji', '汰', '汰', 'If the <radical>tsunami</radical> that''s heading for your village is <radical>fat</radical> you should try to <kanji>select</kanji> a different one to hit instead. You don''t want to select fat tsunamis because they do more damage.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2432', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B1%B0', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T23:51:22.419491Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:05:38.676611Z'),
(2433, 'kanji', '梓', '梓', 'What kind of <radical>tree</radical> is <radical>spicy</radical>? A <kanji>Japanese birch</kanji>, or <kanji>birch</kanji>, obviously. There''s nothing spicier than a Japanese birch!
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2433', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A2%93', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T23:52:48.987658Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:24:37.385296Z'),
(2434, 'kanji', '沙', '沙', 'People think <radical>tsunami</radical>s are only made of water but they have a <radical>few</radical> pieces of <kanji>sand</kanji> in there too. There''s actually more sand in the ocean than most people think. And tsunamis can''t just have water and sharks in them. A few grains of sand will always slip in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2434', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B2%99', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T23:55:37.447069Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:11:59.650433Z'),
(2347, 'kanji', '款', '款', '"For the  <radical>samurai</radical>, it is a <radical>jackhammer</radical> that they will <radical>lack</radical>." That is the latest <kanji>article</kanji> of the law written about samurai, hundreds of years after katana were taken away. This article was written with great <kanji>sincerity</kanji> by the government.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2347', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AC%BE', 60, 1, '2015-07-10T23:14:09.391544Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:03.722628Z'),
(2435, 'kanji', '逝', '逝', 'If you ride a <radical>scooter</radical> with your <radical>fingers</radical> around an <radical>axe</radical> you''re going to <kanji>die</kanji>. I mean, probably. There''s a 99% chance you''ll die.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2435', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%80%9D', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T22:44:59.034736Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.431297Z'),
(2436, 'kanji', '匿', '匿', 'This <radical>cage</radical> is full of <radical>flowers</radical> on the <radical>right</radical> side to <kanji>hide</kanji> the fact that you''re in here too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2436', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8C%BF', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T22:47:47.500562Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.434452Z'),
(2437, 'kanji', '寡', '寡', 'Under your own <radical>roof</radical>, a <radical>geoduck</radical> jumps out of the <radical>ground</radical> with a <radical>sword</radical> and stabs your husband/wife/marriage partner to death. Now you''re a <kanji>widow</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2437', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%AF%A1', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T22:49:04.753231Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:45:23.642188Z'),
(2438, 'kanji', '痢', '痢', 'You ate some <radical>grain</radical>s that made you so <radical>sick</radical> it feels like a <radical>knife</radical> is stabbing in your guts and all your poop is falling out. This is called <kanji>diarrhea</kanji>. Everyone''s had diarrhea before, so you should know this feeling by now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2438', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%97%A2', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T22:51:06.602271Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.435146Z'),
(2439, 'kanji', '坑', '坑', 'In the <radical>dirt</radical>, you find a <radical>lid</radical> under the <radical>table</radical>. You lift it to reveal a deep <kanji>pit</kanji>. Why is there a <kanji>hole</kanji> under your table?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2439', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%9D%91', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T22:52:25.616302Z', NULL, '2025-11-19T02:45:01.625364Z'),
(2440, 'kanji', '藍', '藍', '<radical>Flowers</radical> that you <radical>oversee</radical> are always <kanji>indigo</kanji>. You aren''t sure why though...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2440', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%97%8D', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T22:54:27.889445Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.455159Z'),
(2441, 'kanji', '畔', '畔', '<radical>Half</radical> of a <radical>rice paddy</radical> is always on the <kanji>shore</kanji>. Japan is surrounded by water, after all. So it only makes sense that half of every rice paddy is right next to the ocean shore, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2441', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%95%94', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T22:55:41.466381Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.471432Z'),
(2442, 'kanji', '唄', '唄', 'Use your <radical>mouth</radical> to sing and a <radical>shellfish</radical> to stroke your shamisen, and you''ll be performing a <kanji>shamisen song</kanji> just like that! Yes, it''s that easy. Just go find a shell, open your mouth, and you''ll be singing one too in no time!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2442', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%94%84', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T22:57:16.697624Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.472072Z'),
(2443, 'kanji', '拷', '拷', 'You put your <radical>fingers</radical> in the <radical>coffin</radical> to tickle the <radical>beggar</radical> inside. What you''re doing is a new form of <kanji>torture</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2443', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%8B%B7', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T22:59:19.931816Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.480401Z'),
(2444, 'kanji', '渓', '渓', 'The <radical>tsunami</radical> swept away one of the <radical>cleat</radical>s your <radical>husband</radical> just bought and creates an entire <kanji>valley</kanji> with it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2444', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B8%93', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T23:00:40.802054Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T20:34:39.359876Z'),
(2445, 'kanji', '廉', '廉', 'Under this <radical>canopy</radical> is the <radical>Mona Lisa</radical>, being sold for cheap, what a great <kanji>bargain</kanji>! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2445', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%BB%89', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T23:02:20.585475Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.496402Z'),
(2446, 'kanji', '謹', '謹', 'You <radical>say</radical> something to a <radical>boot</radical> when you want to be <kanji>humble</kanji>. You''re down there because you''re bowing in front of the person you''re humbling yourself before. Because of that, your face is really close to their boots and you''re speaking directly at them, instead of at the person''s face, so it''s more <kanji>discreet</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2446', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%AC%B9', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T23:03:22.711818Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T07:49:51.415608Z'),
(2448, 'kanji', '醜', '醜', 'Give <radical>alcohol</radical> to a <radical>demon</radical> and you''ll see how <kanji>ugly</kanji> it really is. Demons need to concentrate to keep their disguises going. Even if it looked pretty when you met it, like super handsome, give it a few drinks and its facade will slide off and reveal its super gross demon face.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2448', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E9%86%9C', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T23:06:00.785224Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:52:39.90813Z'),
(2449, 'kanji', '升', '升', 'You see <radical>twenty</radical> <radical>slide</radical>s lined up like a <kanji>grid</kanji>. You don''t know who put them here, but they almost look like the grid paper you do your maths homework in. But who would use a slide as grid paper?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2449', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8D%87', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T23:07:09.638943Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.516435Z'),
(2450, 'kanji', '殉', '殉', 'Put a <radical>yakuza</radical> member in a <radical>prison</radical> on the <radical>sun</radical> and he''ll become a <kanji>martyr</kanji> to all the other yakuza still on earth. He died up there so the rest of them wouldn''t have to. Because they definitely aren''t getting caught now, if the penalty is a burning death in the new sun prison.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2450', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%AE%89', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T23:09:48.967677Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:14:08.417037Z'),
(2451, 'kanji', '煩', '煩', 'Set <radical>fire</radical> to a <radical>geoduck</radical> if you want to <kanji>annoy</kanji> it. Fire won''t kill a geoduck, but have you ever been on fire? It''s not really pleasant. So you''ll just annoy it pretty badly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2451', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%85%A9', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T23:11:05.095942Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T08:14:54.362399Z'),
(2452, 'kanji', '劾', '劾', 'If you want to <radical>slice</radical> the <radical>power</radical> out of someone just <kanji>censure</kanji> them. Then they''ll be cut down in front of everyone and they''ll feel super embarrassed. It''s the best way to slice them down without actually cutting them and getting arrested.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2452', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%8A%BE', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T23:12:09.20097Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.555371Z'),
(2453, 'kanji', '桟', '桟', 'You grab a <radical>tree</radical> to help you reach the <radical>bar</radical> out in the ocean, using it as a <kanji>jetty</kanji> leading straight to it. Now you can walk out on your makeshift <kanji>pier</kanji> and go get drinks without needing a boat or getting wet.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2453', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%A1%9F', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T23:13:08.918183Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:43:09.675733Z'),
(2454, 'kanji', '婿', '婿', 'Have you heard the tale of the <radical>woman</radical> who fell in love with a <radical>coat rack</radical> during a full <radical>moon</radical> and made him her <kanji>groom</kanji>? It''s a true story! She even had a wedding ceremony and had more coat racks sitting in the groom''s side. The moon will make people do craaaazy things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2454', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A9%BF', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T23:14:26.918193Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(2455, 'kanji', '慕', '慕', 'You''re walking in a <radical>greenhouse</radical> when you see a <radical>small</radical> <radical>drop</radical> of water fall and it makes you <kanji>yearn for</kanji> the person you <kanji>adore</kanji>. You haven''t seen them in so long. But something about being in this greenhouse and seeing that tiny water droplet made your heart burst with longing. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2455', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%85%95', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T23:16:37.499443Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.558432Z'),
(2456, 'kanji', '罷', '罷', 'You''re in a <radical>net</radical> but you have the <radical>ability</radical> to <kanji>quit</kanji> playing along and <kanji>leave</kanji> at any time. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2456', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%BD%B7', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T17:22:48.290477Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.58088Z'),
(2457, 'kanji', '矯', '矯', 'An <radical>arrow </radical>is shot from <radical>heaven</radical> and hits right between your <radical>mouth</radical> and your <radical>mustache</radical> to <kanji>correct</kanji> your crooked smile. It also <kanji>straighten</kanji>s your teeth and the crooked cut of your mustache too. Who told you that looked good? Well, it doesn''t matter. The heaven arrow corrected it for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2457', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E7%9F%AF', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T17:27:02.876188Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.584791Z'),
(2458, 'kanji', '某', '某', 'If you think a <radical>tree</radical> looks <radical>sweet</radical> you have to be <kanji>certain</kanji> before you bite into it. Trees are really hard, and usually pretty bitter (believe me) so if you don''t want a mouthful of broken teeth, sap, and sadness, you really need to be sure it''s the one before you start munching.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2458', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%9F%90', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T17:33:20.527887Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.606367Z'),
(2460, 'kanji', '泌', '泌', '<radical>Tsunami</radical>''s have a <radical>certain</radical> tendency to <kanji>secrete</kanji> things as they go back out to sea. When they bash themselves onto the land they just throw a bunch of sea water around, but there are only certain things that they''ll secrete when they''re leaving.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2460', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%B3%8C', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T17:39:56.550579Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.621281Z'),
(2461, 'kanji', '漸', '漸', 'You''re stuck in your <radical>car</radical> during a <radical>tsunami</radical> and all you have to break out is an <radical>axe</radical>. You whack and whack but you''re only <kanji>gradually</kanji> making progress. You thought an axe would be an easy way to get out of a car but it''s not working like you''d planned! You have to make more than gradual progress if you don''t want to drown. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2461', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E6%BC%B8', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T17:47:15.481583Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.620008Z'),
(2462, 'kanji', '藻', '藻', 'The <radical>flowers</radical> that wash up on shore in <radical>tsunami</radical>s that we make into <radical>syrup</radical> is <kanji>seaweed</kanji>. Okay, so it''s more like soup or tea than syrup, but you can make them both out of seaweed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2462', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%97%BB', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T17:50:07.31229Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.620604Z'),
(2463, 'kanji', '妄', '妄', 'If the <radical>death</radical> of a <radical>woman</radical> occurs it''s because she was being <kanji>reckless</kanji>. People don''t just die out of nowhere! They have to have done something super crazy to cause it. Right? Yes... right.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2463', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%A6%84', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T17:53:27.539086Z', NULL, '2026-03-30T10:30:14.743137Z'),
(2464, 'kanji', '蛮', '蛮', 'If, <radical>again</radical> and again, you behave like an <radical>insect</radical>, you might as well be a <kanji>barbarian</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2464', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E8%9B%AE', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T17:57:39.333609Z', NULL, '2026-03-05T17:22:34.852947Z'),
(2465, 'kanji', '倹', '倹', 'The <radical>leader</radical> <radical>squid</radical> tells all of its squid people to be <kanji>thrifty</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2465', 'https://www.wanikani.com/kanji/%E5%80%B9', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T18:00:09.507569Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:04.65225Z'),
(2467, 'vocabulary', '一', '一', 'As is the case with most vocab words that consist of a single kanji, this vocab word has the same meaning as the kanji it parallels, which is <vocabulary>one</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2467', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:04:47Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:31:59.714858Z'),
(2468, 'vocabulary', '一つ', '一つ', 'This word follows the "number of things" pattern where there''s a kanji for a number plus つ on the end. Whenever you see this, you know the word means "____ things." Knowing that, as long as you know the kanji (which you do) you can figure out what number of things it is. For this one, it''s the kanji for <kanji>one</kanji> plus つ. So, this one is <vocabulary>one thing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2468', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E3%81%A4', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:10:16Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:31:59.76919Z'),
(2469, 'vocabulary', '七', '七', 'When a vocab is alone with no okurigana (that''s hiragana attached to a kanji) it usually has the same meaning as the kanji too. That goes for this word as well. So, if you know the kanji''s meaning (<kanji>seven</kanji>) you''ll know the vocab''s meaning (<vocabulary>seven</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2469', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%83', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:12:25Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:31:59.831243Z'),
(2470, 'vocabulary', '七つ', '七つ', 'This word follows the "number of things" pattern where there''s a kanji for a number plus つ on the end. Whenever you see this, you know the word means "____ things." Knowing that, as long as you know the kanji (which you do) you can figure out what number of things it is. For this one, it''s the kanji for <kanji>seven</kanji> plus つ. So, this one is <vocabulary>seven things</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2470', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%83%E3%81%A4', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:12:39Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:31:59.872426Z'),
(2471, 'vocabulary', '九', '九', 'When a vocab is made up of a single kanji with no okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji), it often has the same meaning as the kanji it''s based off of. Same goes for the word <vocabulary>九</vocabulary>, as well. It has the same meaning as its kanji counterpart.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2471', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%9D', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:13:23Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:40:38.042901Z'),
(2472, 'vocabulary', '九つ', '九つ', 'This word follows the "number of things" pattern where there''s a kanji for a number plus つ on the end. Whenever you see this, you know the word means "____ things." Knowing that, as long as you know the kanji (which you do) you can figure out what number of things it is. For this one, it''s the kanji for <kanji>nine</kanji> plus つ. So, this one is <vocabulary>nine things</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2472', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%9D%E3%81%A4', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:13:33Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:40:38.042901Z'),
(2473, 'vocabulary', '二', '二', 'When a vocab word is a single kanji without okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) it usually has the same meaning as the kanji it''s spawned from. If you know the kanji meaning you''ll also know the vocab meaning. It''s <vocabulary>two</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2473', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:13:57Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:00.004682Z'),
(2474, 'vocabulary', '二つ', '二つ', 'This word follows the "number of things" pattern where there''s a kanji for a number plus つ on the end. Whenever you see this, you know the word means "____ things." Knowing that, as long as you know the kanji (which you do) you can figure out what number of things it is. For this one, it''s the kanji for <kanji>two</kanji> plus つ. So, this one is <vocabulary>two things</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2474', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E3%81%A4', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:14:07Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:00.051267Z'),
(2475, 'vocabulary', '人', '人', 'When a vocab is a single kanji all alone, it usually has the same meaning as its kanji parent. That goes for this vocab word as well. <kanji>Person</kanji> is <vocabulary>person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2475', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:14:35Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(2476, 'vocabulary', '二人', '二人', 'When you have <kanji>two</kanji> and <kanji>person</kanji> and then put them together, what do you get? You get a <vocabulary>pair</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>two people</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2476', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E4%BA%BA', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:14:49Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(2477, 'vocabulary', '一人', '一人', 'When there''s <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji>, what are they? Well, they''re either just going to be simply <vocabulary>one person</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>alone</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2477', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E4%BA%BA', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:15:02Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(2478, 'vocabulary', 'アメリカ人', 'アメリカ人', 'The アメリカ part is pretty easy so long as you know katakana. It''s the word for "America." You know that <kanji>人</kanji> means "person," so you can figure out that America + Person = <vocabulary>American person</vocabulary> — specifically a <vocabulary>person from the USA</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2478', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%A2%E3%83%A1%E3%83%AA%E3%82%AB%E4%BA%BA', 3, 1, '2012-02-28T08:15:23Z', NULL, '2026-01-14T18:09:53.706242Z'),
(2479, 'vocabulary', 'フランス人', 'フランス人', 'フランス is "France." So what is a "France person?" That would be a <vocabulary>French person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2479', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%95%E3%83%A9%E3%83%B3%E3%82%B9%E4%BA%BA', 3, 1, '2012-02-28T08:15:33Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(2480, 'vocabulary', '入る', '入る', 'This vocab word has okurigana, which is the hiragana part that comes after the kanji. When a vocab''s okurigana makes an "う/oo" sound (in this case, we have a る), it''s often going to be a verb. So in this case, the kanji 入 is <kanji>enter</kanji>. The vocab 入る is <vocabulary>to enter</vocabulary>. We''re just taking the kanji and using it in a verb! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2480', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E3%82%8B', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:15:42Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:00.283422Z'),
(2481, 'vocabulary', '入れる', '入れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji 入 means "enter," but this word does not mean "to enter" (which you already learned as 入る). Still, the meaning of 入れる is still related to the kanji''s meaning: it means <vocabulary>to insert</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to put in</vocabulary>. It''s something you do to another object, like inserting a key into a lock, or your hand into a glove. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2481', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-02-28T08:15:53Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.559262Z'),
(2482, 'vocabulary', '八', '八', 'When a vocab is a single kanji it usually steals its meaning from its kanji as well. Same goes for this vocab word. It means <vocabulary>eight</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2482', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AB', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:16:01Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:00.37716Z'),
(2483, 'vocabulary', '八つ', '八つ', 'This word follows the "number of things" pattern where there''s a kanji for a number plus つ on the end. Whenever you see this, you know the word means "____ things." Knowing that, as long as you know the kanji (which you do) you can figure out what number of things it is. For this one, it''s the kanji for <kanji>eight</kanji> plus つ. So, this one is <vocabulary>eight things</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2483', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AB%E3%81%A4', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:16:10Z', NULL, '2025-08-03T02:41:11.99476Z'),
(2484, 'vocabulary', '力', '力', 'When a vocab word is made up of a single kanji and that''s it, it almost always takes the meaning from its parent kanji. So, this vocab word also means <vocabulary>power</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2484', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%9B', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:16:19Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:39:26.571625Z'),
(2485, 'vocabulary', '十', '十', 'When a vocab is alone like this with no okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) it tends to steal its meaning from the kanji it came from. That means this vocab word also means <vocabulary>ten</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2485', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%81', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:16:46Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:00.469842Z'),
(2486, 'vocabulary', '三', '三', 'When a vocab is a single kanji and alone like this, it usually steals its meaning from the parent kanji. That goes for this word too, making the meaning of this word <vocabulary>three</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2486', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:16:57Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:00.501701Z'),
(2487, 'vocabulary', '三つ', '三つ', 'This word follows the "number of things" pattern where there''s a kanji for a number plus つ on the end. Whenever you see this, you know the word means "____ things." Knowing that, as long as you know the kanji (which you do) you can figure out what number of things it is. For this one, it''s the kanji for <kanji>three</kanji> plus つ. So, this one is <vocabulary>three things</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2487', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E3%81%A4', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:17:06Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:00.5262Z'),
(2488, 'vocabulary', '三人', '三人', 'You have the kanji for <kanji>three</kanji> and <kanji>person</kanji>. Common sense will tell you what this word is. It''s <vocabulary>three people</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2488', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E4%BA%BA', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:17:14Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(2489, 'vocabulary', '上', '上', 'When a vocab word is a single kanji and alone, it tends to steal the meaning from the kanji. Same goes for this one too. It means <vocabulary>above</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>up</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2489', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:17:22Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(2490, 'vocabulary', '上げる', '上げる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>above</kanji> and this verb is related to that meaning. It means <vocabulary>to lift something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to raise something</vocabulary>. It''s a transitive verb, meaning that you do something to something else. You raise something. Like your hands!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2490', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:17:31Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:45:36.330059Z'),
(2491, 'vocabulary', '上がる', '上がる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji means <kanji>above</kanji>, and this verb means <vocabulary>to go up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to rise</vocabulary>.

It''s an intransitive verb, so it doesn''t take a direct object. For example: Prices go up. Temperatures rise. See? No direct object. You can remember this because stock prices always seems to go up when you see this one <reading>girl</reading> (がる). You don''t know how this girl makes stock prices go up, she just does. Think of her as your lucky girl.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2491', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-02-28T08:17:38Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:45:46.325416Z'),
(2492, 'vocabulary', '上る', '上る', 'This word means <vocabulary>to climb</vocabulary>. You can tell this word is a verb because it ends with an う sound (る, in this case). Its meaning is similar to the meaning of the 上 kanji, which is "above." So this word is all about moving "above" something (in other words, climbing!) ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2492', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A%E3%82%8B', 2, 2, '2012-02-28T08:17:49Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(2493, 'vocabulary', '下', '下', 'When a vocab is a single kanji with no okurigana (that''s hiragana attached to the kanji) it tends to steal the meaning of the kanji it comes from. So, this one is <vocabulary>below</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>under</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2493', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:17:58Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(2494, 'vocabulary', '下がる', '下がる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji means <kanji>below</kanji>, and 下がる means <vocabulary>to get lower</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to go down</vocabulary>.

It''s an intransitive verb, so it doesn''t take a direct object. For example: Prices fall. Temperatures come down. See? No direct object. You can remember this because your fever only goes down when a <reading>girl</reading> (がる) takes care of you. It has to be a girl if you want your fever to come down!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2494', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-02-28T08:18:06Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:34:09.465804Z'),
(2495, 'vocabulary', '下げる', '下げる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>below</kanji> so the verb version of that is when you put something below you. It''s <vocabulary>to lower something</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>to hang something</vocabulary>, such as a lamp from the ceiling.

This is a transitive verb, which means that the verb is done by one entity onto another. In other words, you lower <i>something</i>. Like your impossibly high expectations for yourself. Take it easy!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2495', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:18:34Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(2496, 'vocabulary', '下さい', '下さい', 'You know that the kanji 下 means <kanji>down</kanji>, and さい sounds like the word "sigh." You need to ask someone for something, so you look down, sigh, and say <vocabulary>please give me</vocabulary> that thing. You''re ashamed that you have to ask. But you still need it. <vocabulary>Please</vocabulary>, please give it to me!

You only use this kanji version of ください when asking for something, which is why this is "please give me." If you''re just saying "please," you don''t need the kanji at all!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2496', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E3%81%95%E3%81%84', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:18:43Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(2497, 'vocabulary', '口', '口', 'When a vocab is made up of a single kanji with no okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) it''s generally going to have the same meaning as the kanji it came from. From that, you know this word''s meaning is <vocabulary>mouth</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2497', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%A3', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:19:11Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:00.966542Z'),
(2498, 'vocabulary', '入り口', '入り口', 'A <kanji>mouth</kanji> that you <kanji>enter</kanji> is an <vocabulary>entrance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2498', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E3%82%8A%E5%8F%A3', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:19:19Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:00.997068Z'),
(2499, 'vocabulary', '大きい', '大きい', 'This is the adjective form of <kanji>big</kanji>. It has the same meaning, though it is a word for describing. Words that end with い are often adjectives, remember that for future words as well. So, as long as you know the meaning of this kanji, you can transfer that to the meaning of this word. It''s the adjective <vocabulary>big</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>large</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2499', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E3%81%8D%E3%81%84', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:19:28Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(2500, 'vocabulary', '大きさ', '大きさ', 'In Japanese, さ on the end of a word is often the equivalent of "-ness" in English. In other words, if you replace the い on the end of an い-adjective with さ, you have a noun. In this case, the adjective is 大きい (big) and the noun 大きさ means "bigness", aka <vocabulary>size</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2500', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E3%81%8D%E3%81%95', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:19:38Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(2501, 'vocabulary', '大した', '大した', 'した is the past tense form of する (which means "to do"). So, essentially this word is "did big." What is something that "did big"? It''s something that''s <vocabulary>great</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>considerable</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2501', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E3%81%97%E3%81%9F', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:19:47Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(2502, 'vocabulary', '大人', '大人', 'This word combines <kanji>big</kanji> and <kanji>person</kanji>. If you''re a child, how do you think of adults? You just think of them like big people. That''s why this combination of kanji means <vocabulary>adult</vocabulary>, and when used like an adjective, it means <vocabulary>mature</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2502', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E4%BA%BA', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:19:56Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(2503, 'vocabulary', '女', '女', 'When a kanji is alone and doesn''t have any okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) it''s usually going to take the same meaning as the kanji it comes from. So in this case, you know that this word means <vocabulary>woman</vocabulary>.

But watch out! Using 女 on its own can often come off as blunt and disrespectful, especially in casual chats. We’ll introduce you to neutral equivalents soon!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2503', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%B3', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:20:08Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.187669Z'),
(2504, 'vocabulary', '山', '山', 'A vocab made of a single kanji all alone with no okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) usually has the same exact meaning as the kanji it''s made from. So by that you know that this vocab''s meaning is <vocabulary>mountain</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2504', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%B1', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:20:18Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.21327Z'),
(2505, 'vocabulary', 'ふじ山', 'ふじ山', 'Just look at this word. What do you think it means? That''s right - it''s a name (Fuji) plus the kanji for <kanji>mountain</kanji>. Put those together, and you have <vocabulary>Mount Fuji</vocabulary>. In English the "Mount(ain)" part comes first. In Japanese, it comes at the end, sort of like the name ender さん, ちゃん, and so on. You can add 山 to the end of any mountain''s name too, not just Mount Fuji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2505', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%B5%E3%81%98%E5%B1%B1', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:20:27Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.241171Z'),
(2506, 'vocabulary', '川', '川', 'When a vocab is made up of a single kanji with no okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) it will usually take the same meaning as the kanji it comes from. So, from that you know that this word means <vocabulary>river</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2506', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%9D', 1, 2, '2012-02-28T08:20:36Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.27915Z'),
(2507, 'vocabulary', '刀', '刀', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2507', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%80', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T01:38:56Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.302809Z'),
(2508, 'vocabulary', '土', '土', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

土 generally refers to <vocabulary>dirt</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>soil</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>earth</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2508', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%9F', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:34:08Z', NULL, '2025-07-15T21:43:23.74005Z'),
(2509, 'vocabulary', '千', '千', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2509', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%83', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:34:35Z', NULL, '2026-02-09T01:46:41.414635Z'),
(2510, 'vocabulary', '一千', '一千', 'If you have <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>thousand</kanji>, you have... <vocabulary>one thousand</vocabulary>.

Simply saying 千 is the most common way to say 1,000, but sometimes you might use 一千 to emphasize or clarify the exact number.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2510', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%8D%83', 6, 1, '2012-02-29T07:34:46Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:10:32.794596Z'),
(2511, 'vocabulary', '夕べ', '夕べ', 'I spent all <kanji>evening</kanji> in <reading>be</reading>(べ)d  <vocabulary>last night</vocabulary>. It was a really chill <vocabulary>evening</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2511', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%95%E3%81%B9', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:34:57Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T03:44:39.071009Z'),
(2512, 'vocabulary', '女の子', '女の子', 'A <kanji>woman</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> is a <vocabulary>girl</vocabulary>.

This word has two kanji with hiragana stuck in the middle. That hiragana is the particle の. This time, it just means the noun before の is modifying the noun after it.

女の子 is a casual word for young girls, like kids or teenagers. Sometimes it can also refer to adult women, but only to playfully or affectionately highlight their youthful or girlish qualities.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2512', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%B3%E3%81%AE%E5%AD%90', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:35:07Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.439722Z'),
(2513, 'vocabulary', '子', '子', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2513', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%90', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:35:15Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.472225Z'),
(2514, 'vocabulary', '女子', '女子', 'A <kanji>woman</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> is just a <vocabulary>girl</vocabulary>!

女子 is a relatively formal word commonly used in contexts where classification or categorization by gender is used, such as in sports or school settings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2514', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%B3%E5%AD%90', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:35:23Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.497243Z'),
(2515, 'vocabulary', '小さい', '小さい', 'This kanji has hiragana attached and ends with an い sound. That means it''s probably an adjective (and it is!). So, if you take the kanji 小 and its meaning (small), you can figure out the adjective version of it. What is the adjective version of "small?" Actually, it''s the same thing: <vocabulary>small</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2515', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E3%81%95%E3%81%84', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:35:34Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.525119Z'),
(2516, 'vocabulary', '又', '又', 'The meaning of this word and the meaning of the kanji are the same: <vocabulary>Again</vocabulary>!

This is a very common word, but it''s almost always written in kana in casual situations. However, it''s often written in kanji in formal contexts, such as official paperwork and legal documents. You''ll also see it in some literature, particularly stuff that was written around the time of Miyazawa Kenji and Natsume Soseki.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2516', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%88', 51, 1, '2012-02-29T07:35:42Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.546703Z'),
(2517, 'vocabulary', '丸', '丸', 'This vocab word and the kanji that makes it have the same meaning: <vocabulary>Circle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2517', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%B8', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:37:06Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T15:28:07.250648Z'),
(2518, 'vocabulary', '丸い', '丸い', 'With an い on the end, you can guess that this word is an い-adjective. What is the adjective version of the kanji <kanji>丸</kanji>? "Circle" becomes <vocabulary>circular</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>spherical</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>round</vocabulary>. This, compared to another similar word (円), is more general purpose and can be used with 3D spherical objects as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2518', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%B8%E3%81%84', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:37:19Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T15:28:07.250648Z'),
(2519, 'vocabulary', '〜才', '〜才', 'Although this word consists of one kanji that is all alone, it doesn''t share its meaning with the parent kanji. But the meanings are similar in a way. This word means <vocabulary>years old</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>age</vocabulary>. With more years comes more <kanji>talent</kanji>, right? That''s why this word means "years old" and not "talent," like the parent kanji.

You can stick this word onto numbers to say how old someone is!

Special note for all of you advanced learners out there. When discussing age, 〜歳 is the original and correct kanji to use. Because 〜才 is easier to write and has the same reading, it became mainstream. You''ll see it used in writing today, especially in elementary schools. In formal writing though, you''ll always want to use 〜歳, which you''ll learn later on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2519', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%89%8D', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:37:27Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.618428Z'),
(2520, 'vocabulary', '中', '中', 'When the <kanji>middle</kanji> kanji is used as a word, it means <vocabulary>inside</vocabulary>. When you''re in the middle of something, it means you''re <vocabulary>in</vocabulary> that thing!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2520', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:37:35Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.642867Z'),
(2521, 'vocabulary', '中々', '中々', 'The 々 symbol just repeats the kanji that comes before it. In other words, 中々 is the same as 中中. Easy! So with 中々, you have <kanji>middle</kanji> x2. When something is more middle than middle itself, it''s <vocabulary>rather</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>fairly</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>quite</vocabulary> something. Why? I guess because those are all good middle-level intensifiers.

中々 can be used in positive or negative ways. In a positive way, it usually means something is better than expected, like 中々おいしい (quite delicious). But it''s really versatile and could also mean "a little," "very," or "completely" depending on the situation. It might even sound condescending, so be careful how you use it! In a negative sense, you can use it when something is more difficult than expected, like in 中々できない (can''t be done easily).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2521', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E3%80%85', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:37:41Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(2522, 'vocabulary', '五月', '五月', 'The moon kanji also represents a month, so when you have five of these months, what month of the year is it? That would be <vocabulary>May</vocabulary>, the greatest month of the year (because it has towel day in it!).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2522', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%94%E6%9C%88', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:37:58Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:20:14.990318Z'),
(2523, 'vocabulary', '五日', '五日', '<vocabulary>Day five</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>fifth day</vocabulary>. No matter how you look at this word, it makes a lot of sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2523', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%94%E6%97%A5', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:38:25Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.715546Z'),
(2524, 'vocabulary', '五十', '五十', 'What happens when you have ten five times? You get <vocabulary>fifty</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2524', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%94%E5%8D%81', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:38:51Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.73862Z'),
(2525, 'vocabulary', '五つ', '五つ', 'From previous number + つ words, you can probably guess that this word means <vocabulary>five things</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2525', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%94%E3%81%A4', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:39:00Z', NULL, '2025-07-09T17:50:42.689511Z'),
(2526, 'vocabulary', '五', '五', 'This vocab is made from a single kanji, which means it shares the same meaning as the parent kanji: <vocabulary>Five</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2526', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%94', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:39:13Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.783939Z'),
(2527, 'vocabulary', '六つ', '六つ', 'Any number with a つ attached to it means you are counting that number of things. In this case, it''s <vocabulary>six things</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2527', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AD%E3%81%A4', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:39:21Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.806214Z'),
(2528, 'vocabulary', '六月', '六月', 'Since the kanji 月 means both "moon" and "month," what is the sixth month of the year? January, February, March, April, May, <vocabulary>June</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2528', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AD%E6%9C%88', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:39:31Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:20:39.329877Z'),
(2529, 'vocabulary', '六日', '六日', 'It''s the kanji for six and day. Put them together and you have the <vocabulary>sixth day</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>day six</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2529', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AD%E6%97%A5', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:39:41Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.852913Z'),
(2530, 'vocabulary', '十六', '十六', 'You have ten and six (not to be confused with six tens!). When you have ten and six, you have a total of <vocabulary>sixteen</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2530', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%81%E5%85%AD', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:39:55Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.875431Z'),
(2531, 'vocabulary', '六', '六', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2531', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AD', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:40:03Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:01.900829Z'),
(2532, 'vocabulary', '〜円', '〜円', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2532', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%86%86', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:40:19Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:10:33.22049Z'),
(2533, 'vocabulary', '千円', '千円', 'A thousand of these yen things makes this word <vocabulary>one thousand yen</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2533', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%83%E5%86%86', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:40:31Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:10:33.22049Z'),
(2534, 'vocabulary', '円い', '円い', 'This word is made up of a single kanji with hiragana attached to it. That hiragana ends with い which means it''s an adjective. So, what''s the adjective form of the kanji that''s in this word? 円 means both "yen" and "round" but in this case we''re looking at the round one (because something isn''t going to be "yenny"). The adjective version of round is <vocabulary>round</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>circular</vocabulary>. Just like a yen coin, this "round" is a flat round. As in, if you draw a circle on a piece of paper, you could use this word. Same goes with yen, which is why "yen" and "round" both use the same kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2534', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%86%E3%81%84', 8, 1, '2012-02-29T07:40:56Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:10:33.22049Z'),
(2535, 'vocabulary', '天', '天', 'The kanji and the word are… almost the same. The kanji means <kanji>heaven</kanji>, but this word is more about the <vocabulary>heavens</vocabulary>. You know, the great heavens that stretch out high above us?

天 can sometimes refer to religious kinds of heaven (that place we all want to end up someday), but more often it''s about the sky and what lies beyond it, so you might also call it the <vocabulary>firmament</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2535', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A9', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:41:05Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:54:33.926812Z'),
(2536, 'vocabulary', '天才', '天才', 'If <kanji>heaven</kanji> has given you <kanji>talent</kanji>, you must be a <vocabulary>genius</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2536', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A9%E6%89%8D', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:41:25Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.018318Z'),
(2537, 'vocabulary', '手', '手', 'The kanji means <kanji>hand</kanji>, and the word means <vocabulary>hand</vocabulary> too! It can also be used to refer to your whole <vocabulary>arm</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2537', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:42:30Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.042157Z'),
(2538, 'vocabulary', '下手', '下手', 'Think of someone holding out their hand at shoulder level. Anything above that equals "skillful." Everything <kanji>below</kanji> that <kanji>hand</kanji> equals <vocabulary>unskillful</vocabulary>. That''s why "below hand" means <vocabulary>unskilled</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>bad at</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2538', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E6%89%8B', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:42:38Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(2539, 'vocabulary', '上手', '上手', 'Think of someone holding out their hand. This is average. Things rated below the hand are things you''re bad at. Things rated <kanji>above</kanji> the <kanji>hand</kanji> are things you''re <vocabulary>good at</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2539', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A%E6%89%8B', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:42:46Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(2540, 'vocabulary', '文', '文', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

By itself, 文 can also just mean <vocabulary>sentence</vocabulary>, especially in grammatical contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2540', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%87', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:42:54Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.119855Z'),
(2541, 'vocabulary', '日', '日', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Take note that this word means both "sun" and "day," though. Why? because the sun takes a day to go around and come back!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2541', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:43:02Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T10:49:55.91519Z'),
(2542, 'vocabulary', '月', '月', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2542', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%88', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:43:10Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.166923Z'),
(2543, 'vocabulary', '十月', '十月', 'The kanji for "moon" and "month" are the same, probably because a moon cycle takes approximately a month to occur. So, what is the tenth moon cycle of the year? That would be the awesome month <vocabulary>October</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2543', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%81%E6%9C%88', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:43:23Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:21:07.924855Z'),
(2544, 'vocabulary', '一月', '一月', 'Since the kanji 月 mean both moon and month (because a moon cycle is about a month, presumably), what is the number one month? In English we have an actual name for it, and that name is <vocabulary>January</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2544', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%9C%88', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:44:13Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:19:27.458622Z'),
(2545, 'vocabulary', '二月', '二月', 'The kanji 月 means both month and moon (because moon cycles take about a month, presumably). So, what is the number two month? It''s not January... it''s not March... it''s <vocabulary>February</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2545', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E6%9C%88', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:44:20Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:19:50.218239Z'),
(2546, 'vocabulary', '木', '木', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2546', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%A8', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:44:29Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.260747Z'),
(2547, 'vocabulary', '水', '水', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2547', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:44:38Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.28414Z'),
(2548, 'vocabulary', '火', '火', 'This vocab is made from a single kanji, which means it shares the same meaning as the parent kanji: <vocabulary>fire</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2548', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%AB', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:44:54Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.311217Z'),
(2549, 'vocabulary', '火山', '火山', 'A fire mountain is probably what you think it is: A <vocabulary>volcano</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2549', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%AB%E5%B1%B1', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:45:03Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.340592Z'),
(2550, 'vocabulary', '犬', '犬', 'This vocab made up of a single kanji shares its meaning with said single kanji. That meaning is <vocabulary>dog</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2550', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8A%AC', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:45:14Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.362564Z'),
(2551, 'vocabulary', '子犬', '子犬', 'A <kanji>child</kanji> <kanji>dog</kanji> is a <vocabulary>puppy</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2551', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%90%E7%8A%AC', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:45:23Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.384507Z'),
(2552, 'vocabulary', '王', '王', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2552', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8E%8B', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:45:31Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.408146Z'),
(2553, 'vocabulary', '王子', '王子', 'The <kanji>king</kanji>''s <kanji>child</kanji> is the <vocabulary>prince</vocabulary>. Someday he''ll be the 王, if he''s lucky, but right now he''s just the king''s child, a.k.a. the prince!

If you prefer some grammatical details, you can try to remember that when a word consists of two kanji, sometimes the first kanji has a possessive relation to the second. In this case, 王 possesses 子, making it the king''s child. Historically, male children had priority in royal succession, so I guess that''s why this word means "prince."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2553', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8E%8B%E5%AD%90', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:45:39Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:05.095826Z'),
(2554, 'vocabulary', '女王', '女王', 'The <kanji>woman</kanji> <kanji>king</kanji> is the <vocabulary>queen</vocabulary>. 

You know this because the woman who comes <em>before</em> the king must be the queen. Imagine this woman in front, leading the king around. Only a queen could do that!

For a more technical explanation, just remember that when a word consists of two kanji characters, the first kanji often modifies the second. In this case, 女 modifies 王, making it the woman king, a.k.a. the queen!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2554', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%B3%E7%8E%8B', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:45:46Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.465575Z'),
(2555, 'vocabulary', '王女', '王女', 'The <kanji>king</kanji>''s <kanji>woman</kanji> (or girl) is the <vocabulary>princess</vocabulary>.

You know she''s the princess because she comes <em>after</em> the king — she''s just his daughter and doesn''t have as much power. Imagine the king with a young woman following after him. Must be the princess!

If you prefer some grammatical details, you can try to remember that when a word consists of two kanji, sometimes the first kanji has a possessive relation to the second. In this case, 王 possesses 女, making it the king''s girl (almost like "daddy''s girl"). In other words, the princess!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2555', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8E%8B%E5%A5%B3', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:45:56Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.491199Z'),
(2556, 'vocabulary', '出す', '出す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. You already learned that 出る means "to exit," and this is the transitive version of that. In other words, you''re doing the action of causing something else to exit, so this word means <vocabulary>to take out</vocabulary>. When you take something out, you''re making it exit where it previously was, right?

出す is a super versatile word that can also mean <vocabulary>to turn in</vocabulary> (an assignment, for example), <vocabulary>to put out</vocabulary> something (like the garbage or recycling), or <vocabulary>to let out</vocabulary> something (a sigh, a sound, or even a bodily fluid). It can also be a casual term for paying for something, since you''re <em>taking out</em> your cash and handing it over.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2556', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E3%81%99', 5, 1, '2012-02-29T07:46:19Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:27:06.962235Z'),
(2557, 'vocabulary', '出る', '出る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means "exit," so what is the verb form of that? It''s <vocabulary>to exit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2557', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E3%82%8B', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:46:27Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T02:19:46.680367Z'),
(2558, 'vocabulary', '出口', '出口', 'The <kanji>mouth</kanji> you <kanji>exit</kanji> out of is just a doorway (I hope), but an easier way to describe this word or concept is to call it an <vocabulary>exit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2558', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E5%8F%A3', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:46:43Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T07:21:31.960833Z'),
(2559, 'vocabulary', '右', '右', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2559', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B3', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:46:51Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.587635Z'),
(2560, 'vocabulary', '右手', '右手', 'Your <kanji>right</kanji> <kanji>hand</kanji> is your <vocabulary>right hand</vocabulary>! This word can also be used to mean <vocabulary>right arm</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>right-hand side</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2560', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B3%E6%89%8B', 4, 1, '2012-02-29T07:47:19Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.321923Z'),
(2561, 'vocabulary', '四', '四', 'This vocab word has the same meaning as its parent kanji (single alone kanji usually do that!), meaning it means <vocabulary>four</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2561', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:47:27Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:13:05.258541Z'),
(2562, 'vocabulary', '四月', '四月', '月 in Japanese means "moon" but it also means "month." Why? Probably because the moon takes about a month to get through its cycles. So, what''s the fourth moon of the year? Here''s a hint, it''s the same as the fourth month of the year: <vocabulary>April</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2562', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B%E6%9C%88', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:47:34Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:21:32.746491Z'),
(2563, 'vocabulary', '四日', '四日', '<vocabulary>Fourth day</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>day four</vocabulary>. No matter how you look at this combination of kanji, the meaning of the word makes a lot of sense!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2563', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B%E6%97%A5', 4, 1, '2012-02-29T07:47:46Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.333603Z'),
(2564, 'vocabulary', '四つ', '四つ', 'Any number with a つ attached is a word that means "NUMBER Things." In this case, it''s <vocabulary>four things</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2564', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B%E3%81%A4', 4, 1, '2012-02-29T07:47:56Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.345352Z'),
(2565, 'vocabulary', '四千', '四千', 'You have four and you have thousand. Multiply them together and you have <vocabulary>four thousand</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2565', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B%E5%8D%83', 4, 1, '2012-02-29T07:48:03Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.357957Z'),
(2566, 'vocabulary', '四十', '四十', 'What do you get when you have four tens? You get <vocabulary>forty</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2566', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B%E5%8D%81', 4, 1, '2012-02-29T07:48:18Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.384612Z'),
(2567, 'vocabulary', '左', '左', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2567', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A6', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:48:32Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.816799Z'),
(2568, 'vocabulary', '左手', '左手', 'Your <kanji>left</kanji> <kanji>hand</kanji> is your <vocabulary>left hand</vocabulary>! This word can also be used to mean <vocabulary>left arm</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>left-hand side</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2568', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A6%E6%89%8B', 4, 1, '2012-02-29T07:48:40Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.396324Z'),
(2569, 'vocabulary', '本', '本', 'This vocab word is exactly the same as its parent kanji, including its meaning as well: <vocabulary>book</vocabulary>. It doesn''t have those other meanings though, when it''s just 本 alone, it''s just a book.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2569', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:49:03Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.869719Z'),
(2570, 'vocabulary', '日本', '日本', 'Remember the secondary meaning of 本 being "origin"? That technically makes this <kanji>sun</kanji> <kanji>origin</kanji>. Does that sound familiar? Japan is known as the "land of the rising sun", which is why this means <vocabulary>Japan</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2570', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:49:10Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.891901Z'),
(2571, 'vocabulary', '正しい', '正しい', 'Ending with an い tells you that this word is an い-adjective. So since you know that the kanji <kanji>正</kanji> means "<kanji>correct</kanji>," what is the adjective version of that word? Well, it''s also <vocabulary>correct</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2571', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A3%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:49:20Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.917551Z'),
(2572, 'vocabulary', '正す', '正す', 'This word is made up of a kanji with some hiragana attached, ending in an う sound. That means this word is a verb. The kanji itself means "correct," so what is the verb form of that? It would be <vocabulary>to correct</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2572', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A3%E3%81%99', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:49:28Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.940512Z'),
(2573, 'vocabulary', '玉', '玉', 'As a single kanji vocab on its own, you know this vocab word has the same meaning as its parent kanji: <vocabulary>ball</vocabulary>.

玉 can be used for round objects traditionally recognized in Japanese culture, like beads, marbles, pearls, snowballs, and pachinko balls. For balls used in sports, people typically use the katakana word ボール.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2573', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8E%89', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:49:39Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:02.963439Z'),
(2574, 'vocabulary', 'ビー玉', 'ビー玉', 'When you see a katakana word, it''s a safe bet that it is a foreign loan word from somewhere else. This one''s interesting, because it combines katakana and kanji! The first <vocabulary>marble</vocabulary> came to Japan from Portugal, so the ビー part of the word is short for "vidro," which means "glass" in Portuguese. Anyway, don''t get thrown off by that ビー. Maybe just imagine that your favorite marble is black and yellow like a bee.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2574', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%93%E3%83%BC%E7%8E%89', 3, 1, '2012-02-29T07:49:48Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.942286Z'),
(2575, 'vocabulary', '玉ねぎ', '玉ねぎ', 'ねぎ means "onion" in Japanese. But, there are different kinds of onions, right? This happens to be a "ball onion," which is just one of those round ones, like a Walla Walla Sweet <vocabulary>Onion</vocabulary>. Do you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2575', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8E%89%E3%81%AD%E3%81%8E', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:49:56Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:03.007286Z'),
(2576, 'vocabulary', '生まれる', '生まれる', 'Since this is a kanji with hiragana attached that ends with an う sound, you know that this word is a verb. What''s the ultimate verb version of the kanji 生? That would be <vocabulary>to be born</vocabulary>, the very beginning of <kanji>life</kanji> as we know it! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2576', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 3, 1, '2012-02-29T07:50:04Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.960354Z'),
(2577, 'vocabulary', '生む', '生む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means "life" so what is the verb form of that? Would it be "to do life"? No, probably not. In this case it is <vocabulary>to give birth</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to produce</vocabulary>.

You''ll have to differentiate this one from the word 生まれる as well. Just remember that when you give birth, you''re the one who has to actually <reading>mo</reading>ve (む) your body by finding the right position and pushing with every ounce of strength you can muster. In contrast, when you''re being born, you can just kind of lie there and enjoy the ride. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2577', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E3%82%80', 5, 1, '2012-02-29T07:50:12Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:35:48.808465Z'),
(2578, 'vocabulary', '生きる', '生きる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means "life," making this word mean "<vocabulary>to live</vocabulary>."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2578', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E3%81%8D%E3%82%8B', 4, 1, '2012-02-29T07:50:20Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.311436Z'),
(2579, 'vocabulary', '生', '生', 'Normally a single kanji alone like this would mean the same thing as its parent kanji, but in the case of <kanji>生</kanji> it''s a little different (but still related). When you think of something that has life, it''s <vocabulary>fresh</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>raw</vocabulary>. Think sashimi or sushi, for example. You want that to be as close as possible to life as you can, so you eat it when it''s fresh and raw. This word can also be used to describe a <vocabulary>live</vocabulary> event, like a concert or sporting event. 

You''ll see this word at the beginning of other words. For example, 生たまご is "raw egg."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2579', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F', 3, 1, '2012-02-29T07:50:29Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.973562Z'),
(2580, 'vocabulary', '田', '田', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.
Just keep in mind that compared to 田んぼ, which you learned earlier, this word is more formal and less colloquial.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2580', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B0', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:50:47Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:03.134315Z'),
(2581, 'vocabulary', '白', '白', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2581', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BD', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:51:26Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:03.158425Z'),
(2582, 'vocabulary', '白人', '白人', 'This word uses <kanji>white</kanji> and <kanji>person</kanji> ... put those together and you have a <vocabulary>white person</vocabulary>, aka a <vocabulary>caucasian</vocabulary> person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2582', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BD%E4%BA%BA', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:51:35Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(2583, 'vocabulary', '目', '目', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2583', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%AE', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:51:42Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:03.205961Z'),
(2584, 'vocabulary', '目玉', '目玉', 'What is an <kanji>eye</kanji> <kanji>ball</kanji>? Oh wait, that''s easy. It''s an <vocabulary>eyeball</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2584', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%AE%E7%8E%89', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:51:51Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:03.232146Z'),
(2585, 'vocabulary', '石', '石', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2585', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%B3', 4, 1, '2012-02-29T07:51:59Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.408126Z'),
(2586, 'vocabulary', '立つ', '立つ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means "stand" so what is the verb version of that? It''s <vocabulary>to stand</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2586', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%8B%E3%81%A4', 2, 2, '2012-02-29T07:52:06Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:03.282273Z'),
(2587, 'vocabulary', '立てる', '立てる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means "stand" and this means <vocabulary>to stand something up</vocabulary>. It also can mean <vocabulary>to make something</vocabulary>, both in a physical sense, like making a sound, and a mental sense, like making a plan. 

Don''t confuse this with 立つ. 立てる means that you''re standing something up. As in, "I stood up the action figure" or "I will erect the flag." You''re doing the action to something else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2587', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%8B%E3%81%A6%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-02-29T07:52:13Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.513783Z'),
(2588, 'vocabulary', '一万', '一万', 'When you have <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>ten thousand</kanji> you just have <vocabulary>ten thousand</vocabulary>. In Japanese, though, you specify how many ten thousands you have, though (which is weird in English, but should make sense if you think about it).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2588', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E4%B8%87', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:19:56Z', NULL, '2025-10-31T14:37:16.020874Z'),
(2589, 'vocabulary', '二万', '二万', 'When you have <kanji>two</kanji> <kanji>ten thousand</kanji>s, you have <vocabulary>twenty thousand</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2589', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E4%B8%87', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:20:03Z', NULL, '2025-10-31T14:37:16.020874Z'),
(2590, 'vocabulary', '十万', '十万', '<kanji>Ten</kanji> <kanji>ten thousand</kanji>s is equal to <vocabulary>one hundred thousand</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2590', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%81%E4%B8%87', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:20:11Z', NULL, '2025-10-31T14:37:16.020874Z'),
(2591, 'vocabulary', '久しい', '久しい', 'This is a single kanji with しい on the end, meaning it''s an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>long time</kanji>? Well, it''s <vocabulary>long ago</vocabulary>, or can mean <vocabulary>for a long time</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2591', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%85%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 32, 1, '2012-03-01T18:20:20Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:03.395298Z'),
(2592, 'vocabulary', '久しぶり', '久しぶり', 'This is kind of a "set phrase" in Japanese, though we''re going to treat it like a vocab word here (because it''s so common and useful!). You know that <kanji>久</kanji> means "long time" - so now let''s pay attention to the kana: しぶり. Just imagine that <reading>she buri</reading>ed you alive, then a few days later came back to dig you up. The first thing she says is "<vocabulary>LONG TIME NO SEE!</vocabulary>"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2592', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%85%E3%81%97%E3%81%B6%E3%82%8A', 34, 1, '2012-03-01T18:20:27Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:03.41759Z'),
(2593, 'vocabulary', '今', '今', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2593', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:20:49Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.054127Z'),
(2594, 'vocabulary', '今日', '今日', 'The <kanji>now</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji> is what day? Oh, it''s <vocabulary>today</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2594', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A%E6%97%A5', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:21:14Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.068363Z'),
(2595, 'vocabulary', '今月', '今月', 'The <kanji>now</kanji> <kanji>month</kanji> is actually <vocabulary>this month</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2595', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A%E6%9C%88', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:21:20Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:22:52.686407Z'),
(2596, 'vocabulary', '元', '元', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. When it''s used as a prefix, it takes on the related meaning of <vocabulary>ex-</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>former</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2596', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%83', 6, 1, '2012-03-01T18:21:29Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.646084Z'),
(2597, 'vocabulary', '半分', '半分', 'You have <kanji>half</kanji> a <kanji>part</kanji>. Half a part is still <vocabulary>half</vocabulary>, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2597', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%8A%E5%88%86', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:21:52Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T02:50:36.779531Z'),
(2598, 'vocabulary', '分かる', '分かる', 'This word means <vocabulary>to understand</vocabulary>, which might surprise you if you remember that the kanji 分 means <kanji>part</kanji>. Think of it this way: to understand something you have to break it down into its parts to see how it works.

Watch out, though, because whereas in English you understand something, in Japanese the thing you understand is the grammatical subject (it''s doing the action). So, a more literal meaning for this is <vocabulary>to be understandable</vocabulary>. In other words, there''s no object, and 分かる is intransitive, meaning it happens on its own. The かる ending is a hint, because verbs with かる as okurigana (kana added to the kanji) are usually intransitive.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2598', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%86%E3%81%8B%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-03-01T18:22:05Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.530533Z'),
(2599, 'vocabulary', '分ける', '分ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う (in this case る) sound, you know this word is a verb. One of the kanji meanings is <kanji>divide</kanji>, which makes this <vocabulary>to divide something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to separate something</vocabulary>. Depending on the context, it can also mean <vocabulary>to share</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to classify</vocabulary>.

Be careful not to confuse this with 分かる, a similar word with a different meaning. Think of it this way: You "separate" what you care (ける) about from everything else. You want to separate those special things you care about from all the other things you don''t care about.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2599', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%86%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 7, 1, '2012-03-01T18:22:13Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:27:53.962917Z'),
(2600, 'vocabulary', '分', '分', 'This word has the same meaning that you learned with the kanji: <vocabulary>part</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>portion</vocabulary>. By extension, it can also mean <vocabulary>content</vocabulary>, as in "alcohol content" because that''s the portion of alcohol in a drink.

This word is used for fractions, too, as in one third or one quarter. Again, you can see how this links to "parts" and "portions", right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2600', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%86', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:22:19Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.161296Z'),
(2601, 'vocabulary', '切れる', '切れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

This is an intransitive verb. That means it does not take a direct object. The kanji means <kanji>cut</kanji> so this generally means that something is cut. Something breaks or snaps. No direct object here. That''s why this verb means <vocabulary>to be cut</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to snap</vocabulary>. Another meaning of this verb is <vocabulary>to expire</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to run out</vocabulary>. This could be various things like your passport, or a deadline for an assignment.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2601', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%87%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 6, 1, '2012-03-01T18:22:25Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.671307Z'),
(2602, 'vocabulary', '大切', '大切', 'Think back to the old old olden days. Back when decisions were made with swords. A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>cut</kanji> is going to be <vocabulary>important</vocabulary>. Just imagine a couple samurai squaring off, then charging, then making one big cut. It''s the most important thing they''ll do in their lives (if they live!).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2602', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%88%87', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:22:33Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(2603, 'vocabulary', '切る', '切る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>cut</kanji>, and the verb form of that is <vocabulary>to cut</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2603', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%87%E3%82%8B', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:22:46Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.219892Z'),
(2604, 'vocabulary', '友人', '友人', 'A person who is a friend is just that, a <vocabulary>friend</vocabulary>!

Note that 友人 is a more formal word that’s often used in professional settings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2604', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8B%E4%BA%BA', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:22:53Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(2605, 'vocabulary', '太い', '太い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of the <kanji>fat</kanji>? It''s the same: <vocabulary>fat</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2605', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%AA%E3%81%84', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:22:59Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.346659Z'),
(2606, 'vocabulary', '太る', '太る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>fat</kanji> so the verb form of that is <vocabulary>to get fat</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2606', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%AA%E3%82%8B', 4, 1, '2012-03-01T18:23:05Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:58.853166Z'),
(2607, 'vocabulary', '少し', '少し', 'This word is a single kanji with hiragana attached, though there is no specific clue as to what type of word it is. That means you can usually guess it''s a noun, adverb or na-adjective (in this case it''s a noun / adverb). The meaning of this word is pretty much the same as the kanji, though, making it fairly easy. The kanji meaning for <kanji>少</kanji> is <kanji>few</kanji>, and the meaning of the vocab form is <vocabulary>a little</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>a few</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2607', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%91%E3%81%97', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:23:13Z', NULL, '2026-02-22T22:27:08.573372Z'),
(2608, 'vocabulary', '少ない', '少ない', 'This word has a single kanji with hiragana attached, ending in a い. This means you know it''s an adjective. What is the adjective form of <kanji>few</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>few</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2608', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%91%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:23:22Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:10:15.994971Z'),
(2609, 'vocabulary', '引く', '引く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>pull</kanji> and the verb version vocab means <vocabulary>to pull</vocabulary>.

This word can be transitive or intransitive. You use 引く in a transitive way to say you pull something, for example. But it can also mean <vocabulary>to withdraw</vocabulary> when you talk about things that pull back, like water that recedes, pain that subsides, soldiers that withdraw, or even your body recoiling when something puts you off.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2609', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%95%E3%81%8F', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:23:31Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.464811Z'),
(2610, 'vocabulary', '心', '心', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2610', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:23:41Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.500693Z'),
(2611, 'vocabulary', '戸口', '戸口', 'A <kanji>door</kanji>''s <kanji>mouth</kanji> is the <vocabulary>doorway</vocabulary>! 

戸口 is mostly used for doorways that lead outside. It has an old-fashioned feel to it and is becoming less common these days, especially in spoken Japanese.

You might notice that this word resembles 出口 (exit) and 入り口 (entrance). Like in those words, the character 口 just represents an opening here. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2611', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%B8%E5%8F%A3', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:23:48Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:29.026732Z'),
(2612, 'vocabulary', '方', '方', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

You learned 方 means <vocabulary>direction</vocabulary>, but it can also mean <vocabulary>way</vocabulary>, as in この方がいい (this <vocabulary>way</vocabulary> is better) or やり方 (the <vocabulary>way</vocabulary> of doing something). 

Interestingly, 方 can also be used to refer to people politely, like こちらの方 (this person). This is because, in traditional Japanese, people—especially noble ones—were often referred to indirectly, as if indicating their presence from a direction.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2612', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B9', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:23:59Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T17:22:30.289682Z'),
(2613, 'vocabulary', '止まる', '止まる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji means <kanji>stop</kanji>, so the verb 止まる means <vocabulary>to stop</vocabulary>. 

But this is the intransitive version, so it doesn''t take a direct object. A train stops, or you stop at a red light. See? No direct object. You can remember this because you often have to stop when you see a 丸 (まる), a.k.a. a circle. Think about it — red lights, the cancel symbol, and no trespassing signs are all 丸. Even stop signs are… sort of 丸?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2613', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A2%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-03-01T18:24:07Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:23.4582Z'),
(2614, 'vocabulary', '止める', '止める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>stop</kanji>, so the verb vocab version of that is <vocabulary>to stop something</vocabulary>. Alternatively, it can also mean <vocabulary>to stop doing something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2614', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A2%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:24:17Z', NULL, '2026-01-20T01:12:24.816268Z'),
(2615, 'vocabulary', '中止', '中止', 'You''re in the <kanji>middle</kanji> of something when someone yells "<kanji>stop</kanji>!" They hit you with a <vocabulary>cancellation</vocabulary> order and demand the immediate <vocabulary>discontinuation</vocabulary> of all your activities. Welp, it looks like you''ve been canceled — better go find something else to do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2615', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E6%AD%A2', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:24:23Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.545542Z'),
(2616, 'vocabulary', '毛', '毛', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2616', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%9B', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:24:38Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.565581Z'),
(2617, 'vocabulary', '父', '父', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This is the polite and humble form of <vocabulary>father</vocabulary>. You use this when you''re talking about your own <vocabulary>dad</vocabulary> to other people in formal settings, especially if you aren''t very close to them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2617', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%88%B6', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:24:44Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:02:06.281557Z'),
(2618, 'vocabulary', 'お父さん', 'お父さん', 'This word begins with an honorific (お), making whatever comes after it more elevated. Then there''s 父, which you know means <kanji>father</kanji>. Lastly, we have the honorific name-ender <ja>さん</ja>. So this is a nice way to say <vocabulary>father</vocabulary>. 

You''ll want to use this when you''re addressing or calling out to your own <vocabulary>dad</vocabulary>, or when you''re talking about him or someone else''s dad in a casual setting.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2618', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E7%88%B6%E3%81%95%E3%82%93', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:24:52Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:01:24.564442Z'),
(2619, 'vocabulary', '牛', '牛', 'The kanji and the word are the same. This word is a bit broader than <vocabulary>cow</vocabulary> though, and can also be used to be <vocabulary>bull</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>ox</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2619', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%9B', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:24:58Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.722981Z'),
(2620, 'vocabulary', '兄', '兄', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This implies that it is your older brother, by the way. Not somebody else''s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2620', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%84', 5, 1, '2012-03-01T18:25:12Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:24:17.453367Z'),
(2621, 'vocabulary', 'お兄さん', 'お兄さん', 'This word starts with an お to make it more polite. Then it has the kanji <kanji>兄</kanji> which you already know. Lastly, it has さん which is an honorific to elevate people and be polite. This is basically the formal way of saying <vocabulary>older brother</vocabulary> (versus plain old 兄). It can also be used to refer to a <vocabulary>guy</vocabulary> that you don''t know who has a youthful appearance. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2621', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E5%85%84%E3%81%95%E3%82%93', 7, 1, '2012-03-01T18:25:37Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:24:17.453367Z'),
(2622, 'vocabulary', '冬', '冬', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2622', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%AC', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:25:49Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.746873Z'),
(2623, 'vocabulary', '北', '北', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2623', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%97', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:25:56Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.438028Z'),
(2624, 'vocabulary', '半', '半', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2624', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%8A', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:26:40Z', NULL, '2025-12-11T20:12:42.646308Z'),
(2625, 'vocabulary', '古い', '古い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>old</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>old</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>ancient</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>out-of-date</vocabulary>.

This word is used for things like clothes, food and friendships, but not for people! So a 古い友人 (old friend) is someone you''ve been friends with for a long time, not an elderly friend.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2625', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%A4%E3%81%84', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:26:48Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.773055Z'),
(2626, 'vocabulary', '中古', '中古', 'The combination of kanji doesn''t make perfect sense, but it''s alright. Take something that''s <kanji>middle</kanji> <kanji>old</kanji> and you have something that''s <vocabulary>used</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>secondhand</vocabulary>. It''s not 100% old yet... it''s still in the middle, meaning you can give it to someone else to use, like your younger siblings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2626', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E5%8F%A4', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:26:56Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.789503Z'),
(2627, 'vocabulary', '一台', '一台', 'As long as you know the two kanji that make up this word, you should be able to figure out the meaning of this word as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2627', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%8F%B0', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:27:04Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.800843Z'),
(2628, 'vocabulary', '二台', '二台', 'When you combine these two kanji, you should be able to see how it forms the word <vocabulary>two machines</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2628', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E5%8F%B0', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:27:10Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.813412Z'),
(2629, 'vocabulary', '五台', '五台', 'As long as you know both of the individual kanji meanings, you should be able to put together this one to get <vocabulary>five machines</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2629', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%94%E5%8F%B0', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:27:17Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.824601Z'),
(2630, 'vocabulary', '十台', '十台', 'If you know the two kanji separately, you should be able to put them together to form the meaning of this kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2630', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%81%E5%8F%B0', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:27:24Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.836886Z'),
(2631, 'vocabulary', '外', '外', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Note that 外 doesn''t always simply indicate the physical position of something. It can also be used figuratively to refer to what''s outside of one''s inner circle or home.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2631', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:27:36Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:22:47.740424Z'),
(2632, 'vocabulary', '外人', '外人', 'An "outside person" is a <vocabulary>foreigner</vocabulary>. This word is fairly common, though it''s not super polite, either. It''s okay most of the time, but it can be used derogatorily depending on the situation. The more polite version is 外国人 (outside country person) but you won''t learn that until you learn the kanji for 国.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2632', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E4%BA%BA', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:27:45Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(2633, 'vocabulary', '外れ', '外れ', '外れる is "to come off" or "to be off." And this is its noun form — <vocabulary>a miss</vocabulary>, aka something that''s <vocabulary>off the mark</vocabulary>. Whatever it is, it''s completely off the original goal or intended target.

This word refers to something that falls outside of what''s expected, central, or correct. Like something that didn''t match your hopes or expectations — a losing lottery ticket or a disappointing purchase. It can also describe a place on the <vocabulary>periphery</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>outskirts</vocabulary> — since you''re "off the mark" from somewhere more central.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2633', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E3%82%8C', 21, 1, '2012-03-01T18:27:52Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(2634, 'vocabulary', '外れる', '外れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

You already learned 外す, which is used when YOU remove something. 外れる, on the other hand means <vocabulary>to come off</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to be off</vocabulary>. It''s used when something ends up outside of where it''s supposed to be — like a plug coming out of a socket. 

To remember the difference with 外す, just think of how <reading>rare</reading> (れる) it is for a plug to come out on its own, compared to when you intentionally remove it. It''s kind of rare, right?

More figuratively, 外れる can also describe when your expectations are off — the results have ended up "outside" of what you were hoping for.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2634', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2012-03-01T18:28:00Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(2635, 'vocabulary', '市', '市', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2635', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%82', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:28:09Z', NULL, '2025-10-02T17:24:51.43252Z'),
(2636, 'vocabulary', '市立', '市立', 'When the <kanji>city</kanji> <kanji>stand</kanji>s something up, that thing becomes <vocabulary>municipal</vocabulary>. You know, like a <vocabulary>city-run</vocabulary> library or a city-run gym or something. The city is what keeps that place standing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2636', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%82%E7%AB%8B', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:28:19Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T21:50:28.714459Z'),
(2637, 'vocabulary', 'シアトル市', 'シアトル市', 'The name of the city (シアトル／しあとる) with a <kanji>市</kanji> attached to it means it is "the city of ____." You can do this with any city (try it out on your hometown!). So this word means <vocabulary>Seattle</vocabulary>, or more specifically <vocabulary>City of Seattle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2637', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%B7%E3%82%A2%E3%83%88%E3%83%AB%E5%B8%82', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:28:26Z', NULL, '2025-10-02T17:24:51.43252Z'),
(2638, 'vocabulary', '広い', '広い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>wide</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>wide</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2638', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%83%E3%81%84', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:28:33Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:04.532862Z'),
(2639, 'vocabulary', '母', '母', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they both mean <vocabulary>mother</vocabulary>.

母 is a formal and humble way to say "<vocabulary>my mother</vocabulary>" when talking to others. By using the plain form and omitting respectful language toward your own mom, you actually sound more humble, which makes a polite impression on the person you''re speaking to.

You wouldn''t, however, use it when talking <em>to</em> your mom or when talking <em>about someone else''s mom</em> — it would come off as too blunt for that!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2639', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%8D', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:28:40Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:59:03.177337Z'),
(2640, 'vocabulary', 'お母さん', 'お母さん', 'This word begins with an honorific (お), which generally adds a nuance of respect or politeness to what comes after it. Then there''s 母, which you know means <kanji>mother</kanji>. Lastly, we have the honorific name-ender さん. So this is a nice way to say <vocabulary>mother</vocabulary>. You use it when talking about the <vocabulary>mom</vocabulary>s of friends or casual acquaintances, when talking about your own mom in a casual social setting, or when talking to your own mom.

In more formal settings, you''ll want to use 母 (pronounced はは) to talk about your own mom and the super respectful お母さま to talk about someone else''s mom. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2640', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E6%AF%8D%E3%81%95%E3%82%93', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:28:47Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:04.578573Z'),
(2641, 'vocabulary', '用いる', '用いる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>task</kanji> or <kanji>use</kanji>. You can think of this verb as a more formal way to say <vocabulary>to use</vocabulary>, and that is <vocabulary>to utilize</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2641', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%A8%E3%81%84%E3%82%8B', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:28:55Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:04.600362Z'),
(2642, 'vocabulary', '公用', '公用', 'A <kanji>public</kanji> <kanji>task</kanji> is going to be <vocabulary>government business</vocabulary>. You have to think of this public as government to know the meaning of this word easily.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2642', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AC%E7%94%A8', 25, 1, '2012-03-01T18:29:01Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:04.136907Z'),
(2643, 'vocabulary', '矢', '矢', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2643', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%A2', 3, 1, '2012-03-01T18:29:08Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:04.643942Z'),
(2644, 'vocabulary', '休み', '休み', 'The kanji <kanji>rest</kanji> has a little hiragana attached, but it''s nothing that would suggest it''s anything but a noun (it is!). It means the same thing as the kanji, <vocabulary>rest</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2644', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%91%E3%81%BF', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:00:48Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:04.665878Z'),
(2645, 'vocabulary', '休む', '休む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji 休 means <kanji>rest</kanji>, so what''s the verb version of that? It''s <vocabulary>to rest</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to take time off</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2645', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%91%E3%82%80', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:01:46Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:04.687642Z'),
(2646, 'vocabulary', '休止', '休止', 'A <kanji>stop</kanji> that''s just a <kanji>rest</kanji> (aka not permanent) isn''t a straight up stop. You''re planning on continuing again. That''s why this is a <vocabulary>pause</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>suspension</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>hiatus</vocabulary>.

This is a fairly formal word, and it''s often used when an activity or a service is temporarily suspended. For example, you could use it to talk about a class being suspended, a machine suspending its operation, or a pop band going on a hiatus. Check out our common word combinations below for more example usages.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2646', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%91%E6%AD%A2', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:02:00Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:04.711597Z'),
(2647, 'vocabulary', '休日', '休日', 'This is a <kanji>rest</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji>, aka a <vocabulary>day off</vocabulary>. Depending on where you''re from, you might also call this a <vocabulary>holiday</vocabulary>. It''s basically any day you have off from work or school.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2647', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%91%E6%97%A5', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:02:10Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:18:53.878704Z'),
(2648, 'vocabulary', '先ず', '先ず', 'The kanji means <kanji>previous</kanji>. This is an adverb version of that word. It''s a little thing you can begin sentences with. You can''t start your sentences with "previous" but you can start it with "<vocabulary>First of all</vocabulary>" or "<vocabulary>to start with</vocabulary>."

This is usually written without kanji though, so keep that in mind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2648', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%88%E3%81%9A', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:02:26Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:27:12.714404Z'),
(2649, 'vocabulary', '先', '先', 'Everything that happened to you <kanji>previous</kanji>ly is just the <vocabulary>tip</vocabulary> of the iceberg, and the important stuff is <vocabulary>ahead</vocabulary> of you, in your <vocabulary>future</vocabulary>. So make sure you put that stuff <vocabulary>first</vocabulary>!

The original meaning of this word is the tip of something, like a pen or a finger. From that meaning, it''s expanded into all sorts of meanings, like ahead, first, and future, depending on the context and where it is in the sentence. It can also mean "previous" in formal contexts (and we''ll accept that meaning too, but we''re not teaching it here because it''s more advanced).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2649', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%88', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:02:33Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:27:12.714404Z'),
(2650, 'vocabulary', '先々月', '先々月', 'The <kanji>previous</kanji> <kanji>previous</kanji> <kanji>month</kanji> (remember that 々 just repeats the kanji before it) is the <vocabulary>month before last</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2650', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%88%E3%80%85%E6%9C%88', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:02:41Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:07.107746Z'),
(2651, 'vocabulary', '先月', '先月', 'The <kanji>previous</kanji> <kanji>month</kanji> is <vocabulary>last month</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2651', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%88%E6%9C%88', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:02:51Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:23:14.654199Z'),
(2652, 'vocabulary', '先日', '先日', 'The <kanji>previous</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji> isn''t necessarily yesterday. It can be any recent day, which is why this word means <vocabulary>the other day</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2652', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%88%E6%97%A5', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:03:04Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.501725Z'),
(2653, 'vocabulary', '先生', '先生', '<kanji>Previous</kanji> <kanji>life</kanji>... So we''re talking about someone who has a previous life, or maybe previous life experience? When you have previous life experience, you can teach others about it, making you qualified to be some kind of <vocabulary>teacher</vocabulary>.

先生 is often used for school teachers, but it''s also used to show respect to doctors, lawyers, authors, and other respected experts. It''s used as a standalone word or a name ender like トム先生.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2653', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%88%E7%94%9F', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:03:19Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.527391Z'),
(2655, 'vocabulary', '名人', '名人', 'The <kanji>name</kanji> of this <kanji>person</kanji> is well known, but why? It''s because they''re an <vocabulary>expert</vocabulary> in their field, or a <vocabulary>master</vocabulary> of something. If you''re an expert or a master of something, people are going to know your name — even if you''re just an expert at being an idiot.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2655', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8D%E4%BA%BA', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:03:42Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(2656, 'vocabulary', '名字', '名字', 'A <kanji>name</kanji> written in <kanji>letter</kanji>s is the <vocabulary>surname</vocabulary>. Back in the day your surname was the important name (it''s your family name after all, and family was king back in the day). This is the name you''d write out in kanji... aka in letters. So, that''s why this combination of kanji means "surname."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2656', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8D%E5%AD%97', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:03:50Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(2657, 'vocabulary', '字', '字', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2657', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%97', 3, 1, '2012-03-03T00:04:02Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.283054Z'),
(2658, 'vocabulary', '太字', '太字', 'What do you think a <kanji>fat</kanji> <kanji>letter</kanji> is? If I were to guess, I''d say a <vocabulary>bold letter</vocabulary>. You know, like <vocabulary>bold text</vocabulary> on a screen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2658', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%AA%E5%AD%97', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T00:04:16Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.990239Z'),
(2659, 'vocabulary', '文字', '文字', 'A <kanji>writing</kanji> <kanji>letter</kanji> is simpler than it sounds. It''s really just a <vocabulary>letter</vocabulary>, right? You''re reading 文字 right now, in fact!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2659', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%87%E5%AD%97', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:04:22Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.560204Z'),
(2660, 'vocabulary', '一文字', '一文字', 'You learned 文字 means "letter (of the alphabet)" or "character." And what does the kanji character for <kanji>one</kanji> look like in Japanese? A <vocabulary>straight line</vocabulary>!

Think of this word as meaning "the 一 character." In Japanese, it''s most often used to describe the straight line a person''s lips make when they''re pressed together in concentration, frustration, or silence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2660', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%96%87%E5%AD%97', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:04:29Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.57196Z'),
(2661, 'vocabulary', '大文字', '大文字', 'You have <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>writing</kanji> <kanji>letters</kanji>. What do you think these are? THAT WOULD HAVE TO BE <vocabulary>UPPERCASE LETTERS</vocabulary>, RIGHT? Whoops, sorry, caps lock was on for a moment, there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2661', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E6%96%87%E5%AD%97', 6, 1, '2012-03-03T00:04:36Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(2662, 'vocabulary', '２０１１年', '２０１１年', 'It''s the 2011 <kanji>year</kanji>. I think the meaning is pretty obvious. This just means <vocabulary>year 2011</vocabulary> or simply <vocabulary>2011</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2662', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%EF%BC%92%EF%BC%90%EF%BC%91%EF%BC%91%E5%B9%B4', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:04:49Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.582433Z'),
(2663, 'vocabulary', '年内', '年内', '<kanji>Inside</kanji> the <kanji>year</kanji> means <vocabulary>by the end of the year</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>within the year</vocabulary>.

年内 is used to talk about things happening before the current year is over. It can also be used to talk about one''s plans for <vocabulary>the rest of the year</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2663', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E5%86%85', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:05:14Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.59549Z'),
(2664, 'vocabulary', '年中', '年中', 'The <kanji>year</kanji> <kanji>middle</kanji> sounds like it should be something like "the middle of the year," but it''s not. Think of something that happens in the middle of the year and then all other parts of the year. It''s just referring to everything within this year, which is how we get <vocabulary>year round</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2664', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E4%B8%AD', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:05:25Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.607729Z'),
(2665, 'vocabulary', '一年生', '一年生', 'You''d maybe think this just means <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji> of <kanji>life</kanji>, but it actually means <vocabulary>first year student</vocabulary>. A second year student is a 二年生, and so on. Just think about how crazy the Japanese are about school. You can see why school is life, and not life itself, making this vocab word "first year student."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2665', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%B9%B4%E7%94%9F', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:05:35Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.618366Z'),
(2666, 'vocabulary', '少年', '少年', 'Someone of <kanji>few</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji>s is someone who''s young. Since we already have 少女 for girls, 少年 means <vocabulary>boy</vocabulary>.

少年 is a bit of a formal term that often refers to a <vocabulary>young boy</vocabulary>, usually between childhood and adolescence, or sometimes to a <vocabulary>youth</vocabulary> in general. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2666', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%91%E5%B9%B4', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:05:49Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.629863Z'),
(2667, 'vocabulary', '去年', '去年', 'The <kanji>past</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji> is just <vocabulary>last year</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2667', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%BB%E5%B9%B4', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:06:01Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.655048Z'),
(2668, 'vocabulary', '早い', '早い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>early</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>early</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2668', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A9%E3%81%84', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:06:08Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.666659Z'),
(2669, 'vocabulary', '早々', '早々', 'Essentially you have the kanji 早 twice, because 々 is the repeater kanji. When you do something doubly <kanji>early</kanji>, there must be some kind of urgency to it, right? So you need to take action <vocabulary>as soon as</vocabulary> you finish whatever it is you''re doing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2669', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A9%E3%80%85', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:06:46Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T17:06:12.959059Z'),
(2670, 'vocabulary', '気', '気', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2670', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:07:05Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:45:36.3408Z'),
(2671, 'vocabulary', '気に入る', '気に入る', 'You <kanji>enter</kanji> into the <kanji>energy</kanji>. When entering into (positive) energy, you are going <vocabulary>to take a liking</vocabulary> to something and <vocabulary>to be pleased</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to be happy</vocabulary> with it. Just imagine yourself entering into some happy energy when you take a liking to something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2671', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E3%81%AB%E5%85%A5%E3%82%8B', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:07:45Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.696988Z'),
(2672, 'vocabulary', '一気に', '一気に', 'If you do something in <kanji>one</kanji> burst of <kanji>energy</kanji>, you are doing it <vocabulary>in one go</vocabulary>. Whether you''re reading an entire book in a single sitting or chugging a whole beer in a single gulp, this thing you''re doing is happening all <vocabulary>at once</vocabulary> — in one fell swoop.

一気に refers to one continuous push with no breaks. The image is momentum: doing something in a single burst, rather than something happening instantaneously.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2672', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%B0%97%E3%81%AB', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:08:02Z', NULL, '2026-03-14T00:35:43.633403Z'),
(2673, 'vocabulary', '気分', '気分', 'The <kanji>energy</kanji> that''s a <kanji>part</kanji> of you at any given time is reflected in your <vocabulary>mood</vocabulary>, aka your <vocabulary>feeling</vocabulary>. What kind of energy is part of you right now? Good mood energy, or bad feeling energy? 

気分 refers to someone''s mood or emotional state, and sometimes to how they feel physically. You''ll hear it when people describe how they''re doing in the moment, like 気分がいい (I feel good), 気分がわるい (I feel unwell), or 気分がのらない (I''m not in the mood).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2673', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E5%88%86', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:09:03Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.732728Z'),
(2674, 'vocabulary', '人気', '人気', 'If a <kanji>person</kanji> has a lot of <kanji>energy</kanji> then they''re very charismatic and fun! What''s another feature of charismatic / fun people? They are most likely <vocabulary>popular</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2674', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E6%B0%97', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:09:20Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.749288Z'),
(2675, 'vocabulary', '元気', '元気', 'What is the <kanji>origin</kanji> of your <kanji>energy</kanji>? It is your <vocabulary>health</vocabulary>. This word also means <vocabulary>energy</vocabulary>, and when it''s used as an adjective it means <vocabulary>healthy</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>energetic</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2675', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%83%E6%B0%97', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:09:53Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.766356Z'),
(2676, 'vocabulary', '天気', '天気', 'What is the <kanji>heaven</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji>? If you think about it, that''s all the stuff that falls on us from the sky. Lightning, rain, snow, hail... What is all of that? It''s the <vocabulary>weather</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2676', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A9%E6%B0%97', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:10:00Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.778141Z'),
(2677, 'vocabulary', '本気', '本気', 'What is <kanji>book</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji>? It is energy you put into reading - it is your studies! In order to have book energy, though, you have to be very <vocabulary>serious</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2677', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E6%B0%97', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:10:10Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.788817Z'),
(2678, 'vocabulary', '平気', '平気', 'You have a <kanji>flat</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji>. What kind of energy would be a flat one? Probably a <vocabulary>cool</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>calm</vocabulary> one. You''d also tell people about this flat energy by saying you are <vocabulary>all right</vocabulary> or just <vocabulary>okay</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2678', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B3%E6%B0%97', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:10:16Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.800714Z'),
(2679, 'vocabulary', '一本気', '一本気', 'You have only <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>book</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji>. No other books cross your mind. Only this one book. Why? Because you have such a <vocabulary>one-track mind</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2679', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%9C%AC%E6%B0%97', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:10:26Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.81235Z'),
(2680, 'vocabulary', '百万', '百万', '<kanji>one hundred</kanji> <kanji>ten thousand</kanji>s is how much? Multiply the two together and you get a <vocabulary>million</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2680', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BE%E4%B8%87', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:10:41Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.276144Z'),
(2681, 'vocabulary', '四百', '四百', 'When you have <kanji>four</kanji> <kanji>hundred</kanji>s, you have <vocabulary>four hundred</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2681', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B%E7%99%BE', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:10:48Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.011025Z'),
(2682, 'vocabulary', '五百', '五百', 'When you have <kanji>five</kanji> <kanji>hundred</kanji>s you also have <vocabulary>five hundred</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2682', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%94%E7%99%BE', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:10:56Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.267387Z'),
(2683, 'vocabulary', '二百', '二百', 'When you have <kanji>two</kanji> <kanji>hundred</kanji>s, you have <vocabulary>two hundred</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2683', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E7%99%BE', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:11:04Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.000603Z'),
(2684, 'vocabulary', '竹の子', '竹の子', 'What is <kanji>bamboo</kanji>’s <kanji>child</kanji>? That would be a baby bamboo. Because "baby bamboo" doesn''t really make sense, we''ll instead call this word <vocabulary>bamboo shoot</vocabulary>s. 

When this is written as 竹の子, the bamboo shoots are probably still growing in the ground. If they’re picked and cooked and ready to eat, the pronunciation is the same, but it’s usually written in other forms, such as タケノコ.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2684', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%B9%E3%81%AE%E5%AD%90', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:11:13Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.852017Z'),
(2685, 'vocabulary', '竹', '竹', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2685', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%B9', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:11:25Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.839029Z'),
(2686, 'vocabulary', '糸', '糸', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2686', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B3%B8', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:11:49Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.863583Z'),
(2687, 'vocabulary', '耳', '耳', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2687', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%B3', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:11:55Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.87352Z'),
(2688, 'vocabulary', '耳打ち', '耳打ち', 'You feel your <kanji>ear</kanji> getting… <kanji>hit</kanji>? This is the feeling of a <vocabulary>whisper in</vocabulary> your <vocabulary>ear</vocabulary>; perhaps a secret, or some other confidential tidbit of information. Whatever it is it has impact, which is why it feels like a hit!

Note that this word is more often used in writing than in casual speech.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2688', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%B3%E6%89%93%E3%81%A1', 6, 1, '2012-03-03T00:12:09Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.049657Z'),
(2689, 'vocabulary', '虫', '虫', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This word is actually more general than the English words <vocabulary>insect</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>bug</vocabulary>, though. It covers things like worms and spiders, too, so in that sense it''s more like <vocabulary>creepy-crawly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2689', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%99%AB', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:12:21Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.884735Z'),
(2690, 'vocabulary', '村', '村', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2690', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%91', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:12:35Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.895059Z'),
(2691, 'vocabulary', '村人', '村人', 'You have a <kanji>village</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji>. That isn''t someone dancing while wearing construction worker clothes, that''s a <vocabulary>villager</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2691', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%91%E4%BA%BA', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:12:46Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.905246Z'),
(2692, 'vocabulary', '男', '男', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Just be aware that this word used alone can sound a bit rude. You''ll learn a more neutral option very soon.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2692', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B7', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:12:53Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.916561Z'),
(2693, 'vocabulary', '町', '町', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2693', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%BA', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:13:42Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.927378Z'),
(2694, 'vocabulary', '花', '花', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2694', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B1', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:13:55Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:20:54.638732Z'),
(2695, 'vocabulary', '花火', '花火', 'The <kanji>flower</kanji> <kanji>fire</kanji>... what looks like flowers on fire? That would be <vocabulary>fireworks</vocabulary>. Totally makes sense, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2695', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B1%E7%81%AB', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:14:02Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:20:54.638732Z'),
(2696, 'vocabulary', '花見', '花見', 'When you have <kanji>flower</kanji>s that you can <kanji>see</kanji>, you are viewing flowers. But not just any flowers. What flowers do Japanese people love viewing? That''s right, this word refers to <vocabulary>viewing cherry blossoms</vocabulary>.

The focus of 花見 is on the act of appreciating blossoming trees, often accompanied by outdoor gatherings, picnics, or parties. Although cherry blossoms are the most commonly associated flowers, the term can encompass other flower-viewing experiences as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2696', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B1%E8%A6%8B', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:14:10Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:20:54.638732Z'),
(2697, 'vocabulary', '見る', '見る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>see</kanji> so the vocab here means <vocabulary>to see</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to look</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to watch</vocabulary>.

Seeing, looking, watching — 見る doesn''t care. No need to squint over word choice here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2697', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E3%82%8B', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:14:17Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.980833Z'),
(2698, 'vocabulary', '見える', '見える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>see</kanji>, and 見える is <vocabulary>to be visible</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to be in sight</vocabulary>, though it''s often more naturally translated into English as <vocabulary>can see</vocabulary>. It''s used when you don''t actively intend to see something, it just happens.

This verb can also mean <vocabulary>to seem</vocabulary>, when talking about how you perceive something you can see. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2698', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T00:14:23Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.335409Z'),
(2699, 'vocabulary', '見せる', '見せる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. It''s related to the meaning of the kanji 見 "see," but this one is about having someone else see something. In other words, it means <vocabulary>to show</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2699', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E3%81%9B%E3%82%8B', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:14:30Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.991958Z'),
(2700, 'vocabulary', '見分ける', '見分ける', 'You <kanji>see</kanji> something, then you "separate" (分ける) it. This is the same thing as <vocabulary>to distinguish</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to tell apart</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to recognize</vocabulary>. This verb is transitive, so you''re doing the action to something. You''re distinguishing between things or people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2700', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E5%88%86%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-03-03T00:15:08Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.232232Z'),
(2701, 'vocabulary', '見方', '見方', 'Your <kanji>see</kanji>ing <kanji>way</kanji> is your <vocabulary>way of seeing</vocabulary> something. This is like when someone has a dumb opinion and you''re like, "oh, okay, so that''s one way of seeing it..."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2701', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E6%96%B9', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:15:19Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:07.002731Z'),
(2702, 'vocabulary', '月見', '月見', 'When you <kanji>moon</kanji> <kanji>see</kanji> something, you''re doing a <vocabulary>moon viewing</vocabulary>.

月見 is a traditional autumn event in East Asia where people admire the full moon and enjoy seasonal treats, which in Japan include moon-shaped dumplings, sweet potatoes, and chestnuts. 月見 also often pops up in the names of foods that resemble the moon in some way, like a 月見バーガー — a hamburger whose fried egg topping is playfully compared to a full Harvest Moon.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2702', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%88%E8%A6%8B', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:15:26Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:07.015373Z'),
(2703, 'vocabulary', '貝', '貝', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2703', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%9D', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:15:37Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:07.031829Z'),
(2704, 'vocabulary', '赤', '赤', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2704', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%A4', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:15:43Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:07.04294Z'),
(2705, 'vocabulary', '赤ちゃん', '赤ちゃん', 'ちゃん is the honorific you put on a child''s name. So, who is this mysterious child you''re calling <kanji>red</kanji>? When a baby is born, what color are they? You got it, they''re all red. And, because you want to be nice and cute you add ちゃん to that. That''s why あかちゃん is a <vocabulary>baby</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2705', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%A4%E3%81%A1%E3%82%83%E3%82%93', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:15:50Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:07.052889Z'),
(2706, 'vocabulary', '足りる', '足りる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>leg</kanji> or <kanji>sufficient</kanji>, and we''re going with the "sufficient" meaning for this word! This word means <vocabulary>to be enough</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2706', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B6%B3%E3%82%8A%E3%82%8B', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:15:59Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:07.063852Z'),
(2707, 'vocabulary', '足', '足', 'The kanji and the word are almost exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. A <kanji>foot</kanji> is a <vocabulary>foot</vocabulary>, you know?

Although this word is more commonly used for feet, sometimes it can also refer to <vocabulary>leg</vocabulary>s. Now you''ve got a leg up in understanding 足!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2707', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B6%B3', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:16:07Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:07.074008Z'),
(2708, 'vocabulary', '不足', '不足', 'You have something that''s <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>sufficient</kanji>. When something isn''t sufficient, you have an <vocabulary>insufficiency</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>shortage</vocabulary>.

This word is more commonly used in formal contexts. In more casual, everyday conversations, 足りない is often preferred to express something being insufficient.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2708', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E8%B6%B3', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:16:29Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:07.0857Z'),
(2709, 'vocabulary', '車', '車', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2709', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8A', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:16:36Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:07.096369Z'),
(2710, 'vocabulary', '車内', '車内', 'The <kanji>car</kanji> <kanji>inside</kanji> is simply that, <vocabulary>inside the car</vocabulary>. This can be used for other means of transport too, so it can also mean <vocabulary>inside the train</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>inside the bus</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2710', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8A%E5%86%85', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:16:43Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:58.867231Z'),
(2711, 'vocabulary', '世', '世', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2711', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%96', 19, 1, '2012-03-03T00:17:06Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:06.246823Z'),
(2712, 'vocabulary', '二世', '二世', 'The <kanji>two</kanji> <kanji>generation</kanji> is the <vocabulary>second generation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2712', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E4%B8%96', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:17:16Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T01:03:13.115612Z'),
(2713, 'vocabulary', '三世', '三世', 'The <kanji>three</kanji> <kanji>generation</kanji> is the <vocabulary>third generation</vocabulary>. Depending on the context, you can sometimes use this alone to say you''re third generation Japanese. Usually, though, you''ll want to specify whether you''re third generation Japanese or another nationality.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2713', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E4%B8%96', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:17:33Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T01:05:39.018131Z'),
(2714, 'vocabulary', '主に', '主に', '主 means <kanji>main</kanji> so when you add に it becomes the adverb <vocabulary>mainly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2714', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB%E3%81%AB', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:17:40Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:58.903548Z'),
(2715, 'vocabulary', '主人', '主人', 'The <kanji>master</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> isn''t just a master. You have to think back to when they were making these kanji. Who was the master? It was the <vocabulary>head of household</vocabulary>. This head of household probably told servants what to do and was all bossy and stuff. He was the master person. 

Also, this often means <vocabulary>husband</vocabulary>. Because, I guess the head of household back in the day was the husband. Those sexist ancient people!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2715', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB%E4%BA%BA', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:17:50Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(2716, 'vocabulary', '仕方', '仕方', 'The <kanji>doing</kanji> <kanji>way</kanji> is basically a "way of doing" something. More simply put, this is the <vocabulary>method</vocabulary>. "What''s your way of doing this?" No no no. "What''s your METHOD." There we go.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2716', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%95%E6%96%B9', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:17:56Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:58.924467Z'),
(2717, 'vocabulary', '仕方がない', '仕方がない', 'You’ve already learned 仕方, which you know means "method" or "way of doing." When you <em>don’t</em> (that’s the がない part) have a way of doing or achieving something, you could say it <vocabulary>can’t be helped</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>it’s no use</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>there’s no other way</vocabulary>. Makes sense, right? This is a popular phrase in Japanese, so we''re sure you''ll encounter it a lot!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2717', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%95%E6%96%B9%E3%81%8C%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84', 6, 1, '2012-03-03T00:18:06Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:46:46.535759Z'),
(2718, 'vocabulary', '他', '他', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2718', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%96', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:18:13Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T04:47:22.214427Z'),
(2719, 'vocabulary', '他人', '他人', 'When you have an <kanji>other</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji>, you really have <vocabulary>another person</vocabulary>.

他人 has a sense of unfamiliarity or distance, and suggests that the person isn’t well-known, or is different from you or a specific group of people. It doesn''t usually mean "another person" in the sense of an additional person, or another person distinct from the one you’re talking about.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2719', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%96%E4%BA%BA', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:18:20Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T04:47:22.214427Z'),
(2720, 'vocabulary', '代わる', '代わる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>substitute</kanji>, and the verb 代わる means <vocabulary>to substitute for</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to take over for</vocabulary>. Use it when one player substitutes for another in sports, for example, or when someone takes over for you at work. 

代わる is usually intransitive, but sometimes it can be transitive and take a direct object. Kind of like "to take over" in English — someone can take over your shift (transitive), or just take over for you (intransitive).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2720', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%A3%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8B', 7, 1, '2012-03-03T00:18:28Z', NULL, '2025-12-24T02:01:57.33211Z'),
(2721, 'vocabulary', '一代', '一代', 'This word has the kanji for <kanji>one</kanji> and <kanji>period</kanji>. You only have one period to live in so this is the only <vocabulary>lifetime</vocabulary> you get. Unless you''re reborn, but that will probably be in the next age, not this one. So one lifetime for you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2721', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E4%BB%A3', 12, 1, '2012-03-03T00:21:17Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:06.490066Z'),
(2722, 'vocabulary', '代用', '代用', 'A <kanji>substitute</kanji> <kanji>task</kanji> is a <vocabulary>substitution</vocabulary>.

Note that this word has a slightly formal feel to it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2722', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%A3%E7%94%A8', 6, 1, '2012-03-03T00:21:24Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.725779Z'),
(2723, 'vocabulary', '写す', '写す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>copy</kanji> so you know this means <vocabulary>to copy</vocabulary>. You know this word means "to copy" because it ends with す. You''re copying the documents you need to <reading>sue</reading> (す) someone who screwed you over.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2723', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%99%E3%81%99', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:21:31Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T14:36:19.015859Z'),
(2724, 'vocabulary', '写る', '写る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>copy</kanji>, but what is a copy? It''s basically a photograph, which is why this word means <vocabulary>to be in a photograph</vocabulary>. 

In other words, this is the word you use when you point at a photo and say, "hey, I''m in this!" You know it means "to be in a photograph" because every time you appear in a photo, you''re doing something <reading>ru</reading>de (る), like sticking your tongue out or giving someone the finger.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2724', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%99%E3%82%8B', 6, 1, '2012-03-03T00:21:39Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:21:59.811506Z'),
(2725, 'vocabulary', '号', '号', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

This word is generally added to the end of a number, as in 三号 ("number 3"). It can also go together with other words to give things like train car numbers, as in ２号車 ("car 2"), and room numbers. When used on its own, it indicates the edition number of magazines and the like.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2725', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B7', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:22:23Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:58.962031Z'),
(2726, 'vocabulary', '中央', '中央', 'The <kanji>middle</kanji> of the <kanji>central</kanji>... that is really, really in the <vocabulary>center</vocabulary> if you ask me (and is very <vocabulary>central</vocabulary> as well). This is one of those "overdid it on the meaning" words.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2726', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E5%A4%AE', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:22:30Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:58.971608Z'),
(2727, 'vocabulary', '平ら', '平ら', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same with a little hiragana sticking out. Because the hiragana doesn''t really indicate what it is, it''s okay to assume that they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2727', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B3%E3%82%89', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:22:40Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:58.981876Z'),
(2728, 'vocabulary', '平日', '平日', 'The <kanji>flat</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji>s are boring. They''re the same. One day after another. That''s definitely not your exciting weekends... that''s your boring, flat <vocabulary>weekdays</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2728', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B3%E6%97%A5', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:22:47Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:58.990473Z'),
(2729, 'vocabulary', '打つ', '打つ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>hit</kanji> so the vocab is <vocabulary>to hit something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to strike something</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>to type</vocabulary>.

You can use this word to describe bumping a part of your body on something, hitting a ball or a nail, or typing on a keyboard. It can also describe hitting a person, but that use is less common and sounds a bit literary.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2729', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%93%E3%81%A4', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:23:19Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.023641Z'),
(2730, 'vocabulary', '一打', '一打', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>hit</kanji> is simply… <vocabulary>one hit</vocabulary>!

一打 refers to a single impactful strike. It''s often a hit in ball sports, but can also be a punch in boxing or a button tap in video games.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2730', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%89%93', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:23:33Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.036878Z'),
(2731, 'vocabulary', '氷', '氷', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2731', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B7', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:23:41Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.045166Z'),
(2732, 'vocabulary', 'かき氷', 'かき氷', 'This is かき <kanji>ice</kanji>, which is <vocabulary>shaved ice</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2732', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8B%E3%81%8D%E6%B0%B7', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:23:53Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.054139Z'),
(2733, 'vocabulary', '申す', '申す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>say humbly</kanji>, and this is just the verb form, <vocabulary>to say humbly</vocabulary>. You can also call this <vocabulary>to say</vocabulary>, just remember that it''s humble!

This is a very humble way to say "to say" so it''s used in especially formal situations, and only to talk about yourself or your associates. Because of its formality, it''s almost always in the ます form, which is 申します.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2733', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B3%E3%81%99', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:24:00Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:14:57.717072Z'),
(2734, 'vocabulary', '申し申し', '申し申し', 'It''s kind of like you''re saying <kanji>say</kanji> <kanji>say</kanji>, and it’s used in classical Japanese to get someone’s attention, so this is <vocabulary>excuse me</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>hello</vocabulary>.

You would generally encounter 申し申し in dialogues set in older times, unlike its modern, shortened version もしもし, which is used for telephone greetings (and almost always written in kana).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2734', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B3%E3%81%97%E7%94%B3%E3%81%97', 60, 1, '2012-03-03T00:24:07Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:14:57.717072Z'),
(2735, 'vocabulary', '皮', '皮', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

皮 refers to <vocabulary>skin</vocabulary> as something separate from the body. You can use 皮 for when your skin starts to peel off with sunburn, or for animal skin when it is being stripped off or turned into a <vocabulary>hide</vocabulary>. You can also use 皮 to describe the outer layer of fruits and vegetables, like apple skins or banana <vocabulary>peel</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2735', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%AE', 5, 1, '2012-03-03T00:24:15Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:30:12.871593Z'),
(2736, 'vocabulary', '皿', '皿', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2736', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%BF', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:24:33Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.072557Z'),
(2737, 'vocabulary', '小皿', '小皿', 'You have a <kanji>small</kanji> <kanji>plate</kanji>. Wait, is that it? Yep, that''s it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2737', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E7%9A%BF', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:24:39Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.082169Z'),
(2738, 'vocabulary', 'お礼', 'お礼', 'The meaning of this word is the same as the kanji, except it has an お before it, which just makes the thing you''re saying elevate the other person more. Basically, it''s more polite and awesome.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2738', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E7%A4%BC', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:24:50Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.090608Z'),
(2739, 'vocabulary', '不正', '不正', 'When what you do is morally or legally <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>correct</kanji>, you are engaging in <vocabulary>misconduct</vocabulary>.

不正 is a formal word used for dishonest actions. It can mean outright <vocabulary>fraudulent</vocabulary> acts — misusing public funds or accessing someone''s bank account without their permission — or it can simply refer to intentional <vocabulary>rule-breaking</vocabulary>, like cheating on an exam or in a sports game.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2739', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%AD%A3', 4, 1, '2012-03-03T00:25:32Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:04:44.3515Z'),
(2740, 'vocabulary', '交ぜる', '交ぜる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>mix</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to mix something</vocabulary>. 

It’s transitive, so you’re doing the mixing. With this word, the things you’ve mixed are still distinguishable from each other. Two types of fish in your aquarium, books of different genres on your shelf — that kind of thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2740', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%A4%E3%81%9C%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:15:58Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T22:31:09.811836Z'),
(2741, 'vocabulary', '大会', '大会', 'When you have a <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>meet</kanji> there''s a lot of people together. Why would you have a bunch of people gather together? Probably for a <vocabulary>convention</vocabulary> or some kind of <vocabulary>tournament</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>event</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2741', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E4%BC%9A', 13, 1, '2012-03-06T08:16:13Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(2742, 'vocabulary', '会う', '会う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>meet</kanji> so the verb form of that is <vocabulary>to meet</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to encounter</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2742', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9A%E3%81%86', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:16:21Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.629158Z'),
(2743, 'vocabulary', '光', '光', 'Just like its kanji, this word also means <vocabulary>light</vocabulary>. In some cases, it can also refer to a <vocabulary>flash</vocabulary> of light.

This is the more general word for light compared to 明かり, which is why it''s often used for sunlight as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2743', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%89', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:16:30Z', NULL, '2025-10-29T12:06:39.858417Z'),
(2744, 'vocabulary', '光年', '光年', 'What happens when <kanji>light</kanji> travels a <kanji>year</kanji>? You have a <vocabulary>light-year</vocabulary>.

光年 is typically used after a number to specify the vast distances between celestial objects, like 地球からこの星まで3光年です (it''s 3 light-years from Earth to this star).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2744', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%89%E5%B9%B4', 45, 1, '2012-03-06T08:16:37Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:07.061669Z'),
(2745, 'vocabulary', '月光', '月光', 'The <kanji>moon</kanji> <kanji>light</kanji> is exactly what it sounds like: it''s <vocabulary>moonlight</vocabulary>! This word can also refer to a <vocabulary>moonbeam</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2745', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%88%E5%85%89', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:16:44Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.652465Z'),
(2746, 'vocabulary', '日光', '日光', 'You have <kanji>sun</kanji> <kanji>light</kanji>. What kind of light would this be? Obviously <vocabulary>sunlight</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2746', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E5%85%89', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:16:50Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.667039Z'),
(2747, 'vocabulary', '同じ', '同じ', 'This word is the kanji <kanji>same</kanji> with hiragana attached, and it also means <vocabulary>same</vocabulary>.

It''s a bit of an oddball among Japanese adjectives, because you don''t need な or い to attach it to a noun. So it''s usually considered to be a "special" な-adjective. Have a look at the context sentences to see it in action!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2747', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8C%E3%81%98', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:16:57Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.732619Z'),
(2748, 'vocabulary', '同日', '同日', 'You have the <kanji>same</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji>, which comes out to <vocabulary>same day</vocabulary>.

Note that this word is quite formal. To say <vocabulary>the same day</vocabulary> more casually, just use 同じ日 (おなじひ)!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2748', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8C%E6%97%A5', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:17:03Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:36.070631Z'),
(2749, 'vocabulary', '〜回', '〜回', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2749', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%9B%9E', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:17:10Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.745585Z'),
(2750, 'vocabulary', '回る', '回る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>times</kanji>. There''s no good verb for the word "times" but you can probably imagine something doing something over and over and over again. What word would represent this? It would be <vocabulary>to revolve</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to go around</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to rotate</vocabulary>.

Bear in mind that this verb is intransitive, so it happens on its own. Something revolves or rotates, all by itself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2750', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9E%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:17:22Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.75768Z'),
(2751, 'vocabulary', '一回', '一回', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>times</kanji> is <vocabulary>one time</vocabulary>. How many times? Just one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2751', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%9B%9E', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:17:30Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.768726Z'),
(2752, 'vocabulary', '今回', '今回', 'The <kanji>now</kanji> <kanji>times</kanji> is <vocabulary>this time</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2752', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A%E5%9B%9E', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:17:36Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.778243Z'),
(2753, 'vocabulary', '毎回', '毎回', '<kanji>Every</kanji> <kanji>times</kanji> is really just <vocabulary>every time</vocabulary>. Do you get your kanji readings correct 毎回?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2753', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%8E%E5%9B%9E', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:17:42Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:28:04.29877Z'),
(2754, 'vocabulary', '多い', '多い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>many</kanji>? It is also <vocabulary>many</vocabulary> (or you could say <vocabulary>much</vocabulary>). Speaking of which, there are so many kanji to learn, ammiright?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2754', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%9A%E3%81%84', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:17:56Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.802615Z'),
(2755, 'vocabulary', '多分', '多分', 'You have <kanji>many</kanji> <kanji>part</kanji>s. Look at them all. You have to choose one of the parts. You can''t decide which one you want. This one? <vocabulary>Maybe</vocabulary>. How about this one? Hmm, <vocabulary>maybe</vocabulary> that one too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2755', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%9A%E5%88%86', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:18:03Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.812118Z'),
(2756, 'vocabulary', '当たる', '当たる', 'You know this is a verb because it ends with an う sound. The kanji for this word is <kanji>right</kanji> or <kanji>success</kanji>, so what''s the verb form of that? Turns out it''s a ton of things. But it''s mostly for when things go right. As in <vocabulary>to be right</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be on target</vocabulary>. 

当たる is intransitive, so it doesn''t take a direct object. Your answer is right, or your shot is on target. See? No direct object.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2756', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%93%E3%81%9F%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:18:27Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:42:58.434776Z'),
(2757, 'vocabulary', '当たり', '当たり', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>right</kanji>. What happens when you get "a right?" You get <vocabulary>a success</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2757', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%93%E3%81%9F%E3%82%8A', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:18:38Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.830997Z'),
(2758, 'vocabulary', '毎月', '毎月', '<kanji>Every</kanji> <kanji>moon</kanji> is <vocabulary>every month</vocabulary> (as you know from all the month-related vocab). This can also mean <vocabulary>monthly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2758', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%8E%E6%9C%88', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:18:57Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:28:24.959594Z'),
(2759, 'vocabulary', '毎日', '毎日', '<kanji>Every</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji> is <vocabulary>daily</vocabulary> (or just <vocabulary>every day</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2759', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%8E%E6%97%A5', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:19:05Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:28:04.29877Z'),
(2760, 'vocabulary', '毎年', '毎年', 'You have <kanji>every</kanji> and <kanji>year</kanji> to combine into <vocabulary>every year</vocabulary> (<vocabulary>yearly</vocabulary> works as well).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2760', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%8E%E5%B9%B4', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:19:11Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:28:04.29877Z'),
(2761, 'vocabulary', '池', '池', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2761', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%A0', 8, 1, '2012-03-06T08:19:27Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.677166Z'),
(2762, 'vocabulary', '米', '米', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. 

Note that this word refers to the grains of rice more than something that''s a meal. If you''re eating rice at a meal you''d call it ごはん. This is like when you''re talking about the rice in the fields or rice grains or something like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2762', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B1%B3', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:19:38Z', NULL, '2026-01-19T08:15:51.228829Z'),
(2763, 'vocabulary', '羽', '羽', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2763', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%BD', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:19:45Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:29:48.779254Z'),
(2764, 'vocabulary', '考え', '考え', 'This is a noun version of <kanji>think</kanji>. What is "a think"? It''s a <vocabulary>thought</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2764', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%83%E3%81%88', 7, 1, '2012-03-06T08:19:52Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:34:08.229191Z'),
(2765, 'vocabulary', '考える', '考える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>think</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to think about</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to consider</vocabulary>. 

Note that this isn''t the "to think" that is like "I think that he is a power ranger." No, this is more like the actual thinking action. That''s why this word is "to think about" or "to consider" rather than plain old "to think" which is 思う.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2765', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%83%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:20:00Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:34:08.229191Z'),
(2766, 'vocabulary', '考え方', '考え方', 'The <kanji>think</kanji> <kanji>way</kanji> is really the way someone thinks. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>way of thinking</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2766', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%83%E3%81%88%E6%96%B9', 7, 1, '2012-03-06T08:20:13Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:34:08.229191Z'),
(2767, 'vocabulary', '肉', '肉', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2767', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%89', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:20:22Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.939099Z'),
(2768, 'vocabulary', '牛肉', '牛肉', 'What do you call <kanji>cow</kanji> <kanji>meat</kanji>? You call it <vocabulary>beef</vocabulary> (what''s for dinner?).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2768', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%9B%E8%82%89', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:20:28Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.951127Z'),
(2769, 'vocabulary', '皮肉', '皮肉', 'The <kanji>skin</kanji> on the <kanji>meat</kanji> is kind of like the outer shell. It''s not very nice, and people who eat only meat skin tend to use <vocabulary>sarcasm</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>irony</vocabulary>. That being said, Japanese people don''t really understand sarcasm. So, maybe that''s why "meat skin" means "sarcasm." Is this ironic? Now I''m not even sure.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2769', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%AE%E8%82%89', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:20:34Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:30:12.871593Z'),
(2770, 'vocabulary', '自分', '自分', 'One’s <kanji>self</kanji> <kanji>part</kanji> is, after everything, still <vocabulary>oneself</vocabulary>. And the part that’s from my self is, well, <vocabulary>myself</vocabulary>. Everyone has their own part of their self. Pretty straightforward.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2770', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E5%88%86', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:20:41Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.016967Z'),
(2771, 'vocabulary', '自立', '自立', 'By your<kanji>self</kanji>, you managed to <kanji>stand</kanji>. Nobody helped you. You stood up on your own, and you really showed your <vocabulary>independence</vocabulary> in the process!

自立 primarily refers to a person''s <vocabulary>self-reliance</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>autonomy</vocabulary> — their ability to stand on their own, make decisions, and act independently (as opposed to the political independence of a country or something).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2771', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E7%AB%8B', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:20:54Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T21:51:57.280418Z'),
(2772, 'vocabulary', '色', '色', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2772', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%89%B2', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:21:00Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.045417Z'),
(2773, 'vocabulary', '色々', '色々', 'The 々 character just repeats the kanji before it. In other words: <kanji>color</kanji>, <kanji>color</kanji>! But think figuratively, here — something with lots of different colors like this could also be described as <vocabulary>various</vocabulary>, which is exactly what this word means. 

色々 used to mean a variety of colors, but over time evolved into a more general word for a diverse variety of something. You''ll often see it as an adjective, like 色々な人 (various people), but it also pops up when talking about mixed experiences or actions, as in 色々あったね (a lot happened) or 色々考えた (I thought about it in all kinds of ways). Kind of appropriate how this word has various uses, eh?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2773', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%89%B2%E3%80%85', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:21:08Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:14:21.798351Z'),
(2774, 'vocabulary', '水色', '水色', 'You''d think that <kanji>water</kanji> <kanji>color</kanji> would have something to do with watercolors, but it doesn''t. No, it has to do with the color of water, which, ironically, is <vocabulary>sky blue</vocabulary> in English. <vocabulary>Light blue</vocabulary> works as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2774', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E8%89%B2', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:21:15Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.077146Z'),
(2775, 'vocabulary', '行く', '行く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>go</kanji> so the verb form of this is <vocabulary>to go</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2775', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%8C%E3%81%8F', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:21:22Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.090409Z'),
(2776, 'vocabulary', '行う', '行う', 'The meaning of this word is pretty strange, having very little to do with the kanji meaning you learned. This word means <vocabulary>to carry something out</vocabulary>. Maybe you can think of it like someone is saying "<reading>You</reading> (う) <kanji>go</kanji> do that task!" to get yourself <vocabulary>to carry out</vocabulary> the task. Whatever you do, just know this is a weird exception word, so you may have to do some extra work to remember it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2776', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%8C%E3%81%86', 6, 1, '2012-03-06T08:21:41Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.693359Z'),
(2777, 'vocabulary', '行き', '行き', 'This is a weird word. Call it exceptional. You''ll see this at train stations, especially. You know the kanji means <kanji>go</kanji>. So, something is going somewhere. Now just use the き to help you to remember that this word means <vocabulary>train direction</vocabulary> (aka it tells you which direction a train is going), because this word is the key (き) to you knowing what direction you''re going in so you don''t get lost. You could also remember this word as "<vocabulary>bound for</vocabulary>" aka "Bound for Tokyo Station."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2777', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%8C%E3%81%8D', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:21:58Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.101631Z'),
(2778, 'vocabulary', '西', '西', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2778', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%BF', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:22:09Z', NULL, '2025-10-16T14:28:32.815209Z'),
(2779, 'vocabulary', '北西', '北西', 'This word is so simple. <kanji>North</kanji> + <kanji>west</kanji> = <vocabulary>northwest</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2779', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%97%E8%A5%BF', 12, 1, '2012-03-06T08:22:17Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.438028Z'),
(2780, 'vocabulary', '何千', '何千', '<kanji>What</kanji> <kanji>thousand</kanji>? Could be five thousand, two thousand, or ten thousand, since you''re asking. Let''s generalize. How do you say all of those amounts at the same time? You say "<vocabulary>thousands</vocabulary>."

If this is used in a question, it can also mean <vocabulary>how many thousands</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2780', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%95%E5%8D%83', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:22:23Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(2781, 'vocabulary', '何', '何', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2781', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%95', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:22:35Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(2782, 'vocabulary', '何人', '何人', 'When you ask <kanji>what</kanji> <kanji>people</kanji> you''re not actually asking "what people?" You''re asking <vocabulary>how many people</vocabulary>. One thing you''ll notice with Japanese and kanji is that 何, when added to other things, often means "how many _____" rather than "what ____." Just something you have to know and experience a bit to catch on to, but you will, I believe in you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2782', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%95%E4%BA%BA', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:22:42Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(2783, 'vocabulary', '何月', '何月', 'You have <kanji>what</kanji> <kanji>month</kanji> - as in: <vocabulary>what month</vocabulary> is it? It''s １０月, you fool. Get a job, hippy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2783', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%95%E6%9C%88', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:22:50Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(2784, 'vocabulary', '何日', '何日', '<kanji>What</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji> equals the fairly obvious translation "<vocabulary>what day</vocabulary>." But, it also means <vocabulary>how many days</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2784', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%95%E6%97%A5', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:23:02Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(2785, 'vocabulary', '何年', '何年', 'You have the kanji <kanji>what</kanji> and <kanji>year</kanji>, as in: "<vocabulary>what year</vocabulary> is it?" 

This also means <vocabulary>how many years</vocabulary> too, depending on the context. As in: "<vocabulary>How many years</vocabulary> did you play soccer?"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2785', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%95%E5%B9%B4', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:23:18Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(2786, 'vocabulary', '何回', '何回', '<kanji>What</kanji> <kanji>times</kanji>? This doesn''t make any sense. One thing you''ll notice with Japanese and kanji is that 何, when added to other things, often means "how many _____" rather than "what ____." Just something you have to know and experience a bit to catch on to, but you will, I believe in you. So, for this one it''s <vocabulary>how many times</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2786', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%95%E5%9B%9E', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:23:24Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(2787, 'vocabulary', '学ぶ', '学ぶ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>study</kanji> or <kanji>learning</kanji>. When you do these things, what ends up happening? You learn. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>to learn</vocabulary>. 

There is another word that means "to learn" that is going to be more common, which is 習う. You''ll learn this one later on. 学ぶ sounds a little more serious compared to 習う, so keep that in mind when you use it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2787', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A6%E3%81%B6', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:23:31Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.093993Z'),
(2788, 'vocabulary', '学生', '学生', 'Who has a <kanji>study</kanji> <kanji>life</kanji>? Only one person that I know of, and that''s a <vocabulary>student</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2788', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A6%E7%94%9F', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:23:37Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.116401Z'),
(2789, 'vocabulary', '学年', '学年', 'Your <kanji>study</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji> is kind of like the years you''ve studied. Where do you study? School. What is a year of study in a school? It''s your <vocabulary>school grade</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2789', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A6%E5%B9%B4', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:23:47Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.138211Z'),
(2790, 'vocabulary', '入学', '入学', 'You <kanji>enter</kanji> into a <kanji>study</kanji> (place). When does this happen? It happens when you get accepted into a school, aka <vocabulary>school admission</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>university admission</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>entry into school</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2790', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E5%AD%A6', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:23:54Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T07:40:13.560451Z'),
(2791, 'vocabulary', '工学', '工学', 'Someone with the <kanji>study</kanji> of <kanji>industry</kanji> is someone who creates things. That would be the study of <vocabulary>engineering</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2791', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A5%E5%AD%A6', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:24:03Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.184147Z'),
(2792, 'vocabulary', '大学', '大学', 'What''s the <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>study</kanji>? If I had to choose, I''d probably say this is <vocabulary>college</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>university</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2792', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%AD%A6', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:24:11Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(2793, 'vocabulary', '休学', '休学', 'When you <kanji>rest</kanji> from <kanji>school</kanji> you are taking an <vocabulary>absence from school</vocabulary>. This is only for very long absences though, not a day or a week, but months or years. Think about it like taking an extended leave of absence from school.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2793', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%91%E5%AD%A6', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:24:41Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.231494Z'),
(2794, 'vocabulary', '林', '林', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2794', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%97', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:24:48Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.261248Z'),
(2795, 'vocabulary', '空気', '空気', 'The <kanji>sky</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji>... once known as a mystical essence that kept us from flying into space. Now we know this is the <vocabulary>air</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>atmosphere</vocabulary>, though, because that''s what a bunch of air between the earth and space is called. ここの空気はいいね！

Figuratively, this can also refer to the <vocabulary>mood</vocabulary> that''s in the air or room, but not a specific person''s mood! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2795', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA%E6%B0%97', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:25:01Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(2796, 'vocabulary', '空', '空', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2796', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:25:08Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:57:01.718519Z'),
(2797, 'vocabulary', '金', '金', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2797', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%91', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:25:15Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.33204Z'),
(2798, 'vocabulary', '金玉', '金玉', 'You have some <kanji>gold</kanji> <kanji>ball</kanji>s. What balls are more important than any other balls, at least for a dude? That would be his <vocabulary>testicles</vocabulary>. 

This word came about because tanuki scrotum skin would be used to pound down gold into sheets. Tanuki scrotums are apparently very good at this because they are very strong and very flexible. Strange, yeah? That''s why Gold balls = testicles.

As you might have guessed, this word is slang and you''ll probably want to avoid using it at the doctor''s! It''s a comical word that is used in casual situations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2798', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%91%E7%8E%89', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:25:22Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.35903Z'),
(2799, 'vocabulary', 'お金', 'お金', 'The お is an honorific that makes this word a bit more fancy. When you''re talking about money you gotta be fancy, after all. So, you''re basically saying "gold" in a nicer way. What was gold back in the day? It was <vocabulary>money</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2799', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E9%87%91', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:25:29Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.386927Z'),
(2800, 'vocabulary', '雨', '雨', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2800', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%A8', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:25:35Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.408197Z'),
(2801, 'vocabulary', '青い', '青い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>blue</kanji>? It''s still <vocabulary>blue</vocabulary>! It can also mean <vocabulary>unripe</vocabulary>, which might sound confusing, but it comes down to the fact that in Japanese there''s some overlap between blue and green. So while in English we think of the "go" traffic light and unripe bananas as "green," in Japanese they''re actually called "blue."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2801', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%92%E3%81%84', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:25:42Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.42873Z'),
(2802, 'vocabulary', '青年', '青年', 'Someone who is in their <kanji>young</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji>s is a <vocabulary>youth</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>young man</vocabulary>. 

青年 refers to <vocabulary>young adult</vocabulary>s, usually in their late teens to early twenties. The word is gender-neutral in legal or official contexts, but in everyday use 青年 usually refers to young men.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2802', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%92%E5%B9%B4', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:25:49Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.453355Z'),
(2803, 'vocabulary', '草', '草', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2803', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8D%89', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:26:06Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T06:26:42.148176Z'),
(2804, 'vocabulary', '音', '音', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2804', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9F%B3', 5, 1, '2012-03-06T08:26:14Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.501075Z'),
(2805, 'vocabulary', '作る', '作る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>make</kanji>. The verb form of that is <vocabulary>to make</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2805', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%9C%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:28:22Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:12:39.703958Z'),
(2806, 'vocabulary', '体', '体', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2806', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%93', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:29:00Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.549099Z'),
(2807, 'vocabulary', '体力', '体力', 'You have your <kanji>body</kanji> <kanji>power</kanji>. This is just another way to say <vocabulary>physical strength</vocabulary>. Watch out, if you use too much of your physical strength you may run out of physical <vocabulary>stamina</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>endurance</vocabulary> completely! They''re tied together, so make sure to pace yourself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2807', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%93%E5%8A%9B', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:29:07Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(2808, 'vocabulary', '体内', '体内', 'The <kanji>body</kanji>''s <kanji>inside</kanji> means <vocabulary>inside the body</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>internal</vocabulary>.

体内 refers specifically to internal body stuff, so you''ll often see it in medical and scientific contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2808', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%93%E5%86%85', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:29:16Z', NULL, '2025-10-21T00:32:07.237751Z'),
(2809, 'vocabulary', '作文', '作文', 'When you <kanji>make</kanji> <kanji>writing</kanji>, what are you doing? Simply put, you''re <vocabulary>writing</vocabulary>. If you want to be fancier about it, you could say this is <vocabulary>composition</vocabulary>.

Note that 作文 often refers to the end result of a writing process, like a finished essay, rather than the writing process itself. It’s also commonly associated with various writing tasks assigned to students in Japanese elementary or middle school.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2809', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%9C%E6%96%87', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:29:36Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:12:39.703958Z'),
(2810, 'vocabulary', '作用', '作用', 'When you <kanji>make</kanji> a <kanji>task</kanji> you''re looking for a certain <vocabulary>effect</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>action</vocabulary>. What task can you make right now to get the effect of learning more kanji?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2810', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%9C%E7%94%A8', 23, 1, '2012-03-08T23:29:49Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:12:39.703958Z'),
(2811, 'vocabulary', '工作', '工作', 'What is the "<kanji>industry</kanji> of <kanji>make</kanji>"? Well, it''s when you industriously make things, obviously. That can only be <vocabulary>handicraft</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>crafts</vocabulary>, often referring to craft activities or hands-on projects done in elementary or middle school!

But wait, 工作 isn''t just about glue and popsicle sticks! It can also mean <vocabulary>maneuvering</vocabulary>, in the sense of pulling strings behind the scenes. So, whether you''re crafting a masterpiece or scheming a clever plan, 工作 encompasses the wonders of crafting!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2811', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A5%E4%BD%9C', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:30:26Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:12:39.703958Z'),
(2812, 'vocabulary', '大作', '大作', 'The <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>make</kanji>! What is your big make? Bigger than anything else. It is your <vocabulary>major work</vocabulary>!

大作 means a large-scale, ambitious, or significant work. Depending on the context, it can refer to anything from an artistically accomplished <vocabulary>masterpiece</vocabulary> to a big-budget <vocabulary>blockbuster</vocabulary> movie.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2812', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E4%BD%9C', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:30:38Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(2813, 'vocabulary', '図', '図', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2813', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%B3', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:30:53Z', NULL, '2026-02-10T08:07:18.887825Z'),
(2814, 'vocabulary', '声', '声', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2814', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%B0', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:31:09Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:08.783083Z'),
(2815, 'vocabulary', '大声', '大声', 'A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>voice</kanji> is a <vocabulary>loud voice</vocabulary>!

大声 usually just describes talking in a <vocabulary>big voice</vocabulary>, but it can also refer to shouting depending on the situation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2815', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%A3%B0', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:31:16Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(2816, 'vocabulary', '売る', '売る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>sell</kanji>, and this is a transitive verb meaning <vocabulary>to sell something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2816', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%B2%E3%82%8B', 9, 1, '2012-03-08T23:31:23Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:32:06.067577Z'),
(2817, 'vocabulary', '売り上げ', '売り上げ', 'The <kanji>sell</kanji>ing that you''ve raised (上げ) is the <vocabulary>amount sold</vocabulary> or number of <vocabulary>sales</vocabulary>. Think of a graph going upward, where it''s rising as you sell more things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2817', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%B2%E3%82%8A%E4%B8%8A%E3%81%92', 11, 1, '2012-03-08T23:31:46Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(2818, 'vocabulary', '売り切れ', '売り切れ', 'When the <kanji>sell</kanji>ing is <kanji>cut</kanji>, that means something is <vocabulary>sold out</vocabulary>. You''ll see this word a lot in stores (or, hopefully you won''t...) in Japan. Look out for it if you''re able!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2818', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%B2%E3%82%8A%E5%88%87%E3%82%8C', 11, 1, '2012-03-08T23:31:54Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:32:06.067577Z'),
(2819, 'vocabulary', '売り手', '売り手', 'Remember how 手 can mean “doer”? Here, we have a <kanji>sell</kanji> <kanji>doer</kanji>. Who might that be? It’s someone who does selling – a <vocabulary>seller</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>vendor</vocabulary>.

売り手 is usually used when talking about sellers versus buyers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2819', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%B2%E3%82%8A%E6%89%8B', 11, 1, '2012-03-08T23:32:02Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:32:06.067577Z'),
(2820, 'vocabulary', '弟', '弟', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2820', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%9F', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:32:17Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:35:45.556698Z'),
(2821, 'vocabulary', '兄弟', '兄弟', 'You have an <kanji>older brother</kanji> and a <kanji>younger brother</kanji>. Wow! Both are here. These are <vocabulary>brothers</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2821', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%84%E5%BC%9F', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:32:23Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:24:17.453367Z'),
(2822, 'vocabulary', '形', '形', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2822', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%A2', 7, 1, '2012-03-08T23:32:37Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T22:22:06.42136Z'),
(2823, 'vocabulary', 'ハート形', 'ハート形', 'ハート is of course "heart" and you know that 形 means <kanji>shape</kanji>. You''ve probably already figured this one out. It''s <vocabulary>heart shaped</vocabulary>. 

You can add this kanji to all sorts of other things too to say "____ shaped." Give it a try!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2823', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%8F%E3%83%BC%E3%83%88%E5%BD%A2', 9, 1, '2012-03-08T23:33:07Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.281771Z'),
(2824, 'vocabulary', '来る', '来る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>come</kanji> so you know this word means <vocabulary>to come</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2824', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%A5%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:33:21Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(2825, 'vocabulary', '来月', '来月', 'The <kanji>next</kanji> <kanji>month</kanji> is the <vocabulary>next month</vocabulary>! It isn''t this month or the month before, it''s the next one!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2825', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%A5%E6%9C%88', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:33:28Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:26:56.746525Z'),
(2826, 'vocabulary', '来年', '来年', 'The <kanji>next</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji> is <vocabulary>next year</vocabulary>! It isn''t last year or this year, it''s the next one!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2826', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%A5%E5%B9%B4', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:33:34Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(2827, 'vocabulary', '古来', '古来', 'Things that are really <kanji>old</kanji> <kanji>come</kanji> <vocabulary>from ancient times</vocabulary>!

This is a relatively formal expression that’s used to talk about <vocabulary>age-old</vocabulary> things, like customs that have stood the test of time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2827', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%A4%E6%9D%A5', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:34:19Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(2828, 'vocabulary', '外来', '外来', 'From <kanji>foreign</kanji> lands, you have <kanji>come</kanji> to this hospital. That makes you an <vocabulary>outpatient</vocabulary>. 

外来 describes something or someone that comes from elsewhere. You’ll see it most often in hospitals, where it means outpatient, but it’s also used in formal contexts to talk about <vocabulary>foreign</vocabulary> species or <vocabulary>imported</vocabulary> words.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2828', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E6%9D%A5', 16, 1, '2012-03-08T23:34:26Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(2829, 'vocabulary', '〜年来', '〜年来', 'When you have <kanji>year</kanji>s <kanji>com</kanji>ing and going, you know it is <vocabulary>for some years</vocabulary>. Don''t confuse this one with 来年 which means "next year." The kanji 来 comes after the kanji for year, so instead of "coming year" it''s "years that come (and go)." At least, you can think of it that way to differentiate. 

You can also add numbers to this word. For example ５年来 means something to the tune of "for five years." But this is a pretty formal word. Just keep that in mind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2829', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%B9%B4%E6%9D%A5', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:34:34Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(2830, 'vocabulary', '社内', '社内', 'When you''re doing something <kanji>inside</kanji> the <kanji>company</kanji> you''re doing it <vocabulary>within the company</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>in-house</vocabulary>. For example, we do our kanji in-house. It gives it that artisan flavor.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2830', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A4%BE%E5%86%85', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:34:47Z', NULL, '2025-09-05T05:40:05.235761Z'),
(2831, 'vocabulary', '入社', '入社', 'When you <kanji>enter</kanji> a <kanji>company</kanji> you''re actually <vocabulary>joining a company</vocabulary>. Welcome, new recruit. Prepare to be hazed!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2831', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E7%A4%BE', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:34:54Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:09.290738Z'),
(2832, 'vocabulary', '公社', '公社', 'A <kanji>public</kanji> <kanji>company</kanji> is a <vocabulary>government-run company</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>government-owned company</vocabulary>. In other words, it''s a company that''s run by national or local government in the interests of the public.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2832', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AC%E7%A4%BE', 23, 1, '2012-03-08T23:35:00Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:09.374117Z'),
(2833, 'vocabulary', '本社', '本社', 'The <kanji>origin</kanji> <kanji>company</kanji> is where everything in your company comes from. This is your main base. This is your <vocabulary>headquarters</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2833', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E7%A4%BE', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:35:08Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T06:58:42.316347Z'),
(2834, 'vocabulary', '会社', '会社', 'The place where your <kanji>company</kanji> <kanji>meet</kanji>s is your <vocabulary>company</vocabulary>. Sony is a company. Nintendo is a company. Etc.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2834', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9A%E7%A4%BE', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:35:18Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:09.453375Z'),
(2835, 'vocabulary', '角', '角', 'When the kanji for <kanji>angle</kanji> is used as a standalone word, it usually means <vocabulary>corner</vocabulary>. You can remember this because corners are pretty much the most common kind of angle you see in everyday life.

In math contexts, it can also mean <vocabulary>angle</vocabulary> — just like the kanji''s meaning!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2835', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%92', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:35:27Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T21:05:47.522155Z'),
(2836, 'vocabulary', '三角', '三角', 'When you have something with <kanji>three</kanji> <kanji>angle</kanji>s, what shape is that? It''s a <vocabulary>triangle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2836', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E8%A7%92', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:35:34Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T21:05:47.522155Z'),
(2837, 'vocabulary', '四角', '四角', 'What shape has <kanji>four</kanji> <kanji>angle</kanji>s? The most common one is a <vocabulary>square</vocabulary>, but it could also be a <vocabulary>rectangle</vocabulary> or even a <vocabulary>cube</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2837', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B%E8%A7%92', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:35:40Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:13:05.258541Z'),
(2838, 'vocabulary', '言う', '言う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>say</kanji>, so the verb version of that is <vocabulary>to say</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2838', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%80%E3%81%86', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:35:46Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:09.555925Z'),
(2839, 'vocabulary', '谷', '谷', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2839', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B0%B7', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:36:04Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:43:56.588475Z'),
(2840, 'vocabulary', '走る', '走る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>run</kanji> so now you know the verb version of that is <vocabulary>to run</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2840', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%B0%E3%82%8B', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:36:14Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:09.603219Z'),
(2841, 'vocabulary', '走行', '走行', 'A vehicle that <kanji>run</kanji>-<kanji>go</kanji>es is <vocabulary>running</vocabulary> and it''s <vocabulary>moving</vocabulary> you somewhere else. You''re <vocabulary>traveling</vocabulary>!

This word is typically used to describe the movement of vehicles such as cars and trains. It carries a slightly formal or rigid tone, so keep an eye on our context sentences to make sure you’re using it right!
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2841', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%B0%E8%A1%8C', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:36:21Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:09.625317Z'),
(2842, 'vocabulary', '近い', '近い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>near</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>close</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>nearby</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2842', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E3%81%84', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:36:34Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.684866Z'),
(2843, 'vocabulary', '近日', '近日', 'The <kanji>near</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji> is a day coming very <vocabulary>soon</vocabulary>. It''s a day coming <vocabulary>in a few days</vocabulary> or in the <vocabulary>coming days</vocabulary>. In other words, you wouldn''t use this word for something that''s going to happen in a few minutes, or a few months.

This is a relatively formal word that is commonly used in professional or official contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2843', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E6%97%A5', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:37:04Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:36.060612Z'),
(2844, 'vocabulary', '近年', '近年', 'The <kanji>close</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji>s are years that aren''t too long ago. They are the <vocabulary>recent years</vocabulary>.

This is a formal word that is often used in writing or formal speech. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2844', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E5%B9%B4', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:37:11Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:36.049387Z'),
(2845, 'vocabulary', '麦', '麦', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

麦 refers to a group of European-style cereal grains, like wheat and barley but not rice and corn. To specify which 麦, you''ll need to use compounds like 小麦 (wheat) or 大麦 (barley), though!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2845', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BA%A6', 5, 1, '2012-03-08T23:37:27Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:24:56.435104Z'),
(2846, 'vocabulary', '文化', '文化', '<kanji>Writing</kanji> has <kanji>change</kanji>d our entire <vocabulary>culture</vocabulary>. In fact, when someone reads a good piece of writing it changes them, and changes their culture too. What writing has changed you so much it has changed your culture and the way you act?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2846', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:33:28Z', NULL, '2025-11-20T03:47:15.21122Z'),
(2847, 'vocabulary', '地', '地', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

地 is often used in formal contexts or literature to refer to the <vocabulary>Earth</vocabulary>''s surface, the <vocabulary>ground</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>land</vocabulary> in a more abstract or overarching sense. To say "earth" or "ground" casually, use 地面. For "land," it''s 土地.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2847', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0', 25, 1, '2012-03-09T00:34:12Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.284773Z'),
(2848, 'vocabulary', '地下', '地下', '<kanji>Below</kanji> the <kanji>ground</kanji> is <vocabulary>underground</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2848', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E4%B8%8B', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:34:21Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(2849, 'vocabulary', '土地', '土地', 'The <kanji>soil</kanji> of the <kanji>earth</kanji> is the portion of the earth that is made up of <vocabulary>land</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2849', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%9F%E5%9C%B0', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:34:40Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.510769Z'),
(2850, 'vocabulary', '地図', '地図', 'The <kanji>diagram</kanji> of the <kanji>earth</kanji>. What could that be other than a <vocabulary>map</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2850', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E5%9B%B3', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:35:27Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.568037Z'),
(2851, 'vocabulary', '両手', '両手', 'Don''t use one hand when you can use <vocabulary>both hands</vocabulary>. This is simply <kanji>both</kanji> plus <kanji>hand</kanji> put together. It can also mean <vocabulary>both arms</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2851', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%A1%E6%89%8B', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:35:45Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:45:39.570399Z'),
(2852, 'vocabulary', '両日', '両日', 'Kanji doesn''t go together much better than this. <kanji>Both</kanji> plus <kanji>day</kanji> equals <vocabulary>both days</vocabulary>.

This is a bit of a formal word though, you''ll hear important people say it more than your friends.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2852', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%A1%E6%97%A5', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:35:50Z', NULL, '2026-01-18T18:20:32.083047Z'),
(2853, 'vocabulary', '両方', '両方', 'Here you have <kanji>both</kanji> <kanji>direction</kanji>s / <kanji>way</kanji>s. Every time you have to choose between something it''s like having to choose between two ways. That''s why this word is simply <vocabulary>both</vocabulary>. 

Close to the kanji is the meaning <vocabulary>both sides</vocabulary>, as well. Learn either for 両方, they''re pretty similar.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2853', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%A1%E6%96%B9', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:35:56Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:45:39.570399Z'),
(2854, 'vocabulary', '全て', '全て', 'This has a little hiragana attached to it but nothing that suggests it''s a verb or an adjective. That means you can assume this word has the same meaning as its parent kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2854', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E3%81%A6', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:36:02Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:47:49.244838Z'),
(2855, 'vocabulary', '全力', '全力', 'When you put <kanji>all</kanji> your <kanji>power</kanji> into something, you''re putting your <vocabulary>full effort</vocabulary> into it. Hopefully that''s what you put into your kanji learning as well!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2855', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E5%8A%9B', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:36:22Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(2856, 'vocabulary', '全日本', '全日本', 'You know 日本 means "Japan." So, now you have the kanji for <kanji>all</kanji> added to it, making this word <vocabulary>All Japan</vocabulary> (as in "All Japan Railways" or something like that).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2856', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:36:44Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:47:49.244838Z'),
(2857, 'vocabulary', '安全', '安全', 'When you are <kanji>all</kanji> <kanji>relax</kanji>ed you are finally <vocabulary>safe</vocabulary> and in <vocabulary>safety</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2857', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%89%E5%85%A8', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:37:13Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:47:49.244838Z'),
(2858, 'vocabulary', '方向', '方向', 'The <kanji>direction</kanji> <kanji>yonder</kanji> over there is still just the <vocabulary>direction</vocabulary> of something, though a complicated way to say it, I think. This direction is the "what direction did he go in?" sort of direction.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2858', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B9%E5%90%91', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:38:15Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.592595Z'),
(2859, 'vocabulary', '安い', '安い', 'This is a single kanji with an い at the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. One of the meanings of 安 is <kanji>cheap</kanji>, so this is just that as an adjective, <vocabulary>cheap</vocabulary>. Time to go to a 100円 shop and get some 安い goods, ammiright?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2859', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%89%E3%81%84', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:38:58Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.603192Z'),
(2860, 'vocabulary', '安心', '安心', 'When your <kanji>heart</kanji> is <kanji>relax</kanji>ed, how do you feel? What if this is after something startling happens? You would feel <vocabulary>relief</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>peace of mind</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2860', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%89%E5%BF%83', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:39:04Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.611838Z'),
(2861, 'vocabulary', '不安', '不安', 'When you''re <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>relax</kanji>ed, what do you feel? <vocabulary>Uneasiness</vocabulary> is what you''d feel.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2861', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%AE%89', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:39:34Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.62076Z'),
(2862, 'vocabulary', '平安', '平安', 'Something is <kanji>flat</kanji> and <kanji>relax</kanji>ed. That is you. What words does that describe? To me, that would be <vocabulary>peace</vocabulary>. Just imagine something that is flat and relaxed, like a field of grass with happy people in it. It''s so <vocabulary>peaceful</vocabulary>!

This is the way you write the <vocabulary>Heian</vocabulary> period of Japanese history, which is probably where you''ll see this word the most.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2862', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B3%E5%AE%89', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:39:41Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.629353Z'),
(2863, 'vocabulary', '州', '州', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2863', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%9E', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:39:48Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.637319Z'),
(2864, 'vocabulary', '九州', '九州', 'The <kanji>nine</kanji> <kanji>states</kanji>. This refers to a section of Japan from a long time ago that was presumably made up of nine states. Now it''s just one big island, though, and that island is <vocabulary>Kyuushuu</vocabulary>. Go take a look at a map and find this giant island, it''s a cool part of Japan!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2864', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%9D%E5%B7%9E', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:39:54Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:40:38.042901Z'),
(2865, 'vocabulary', '本州', '本州', 'You have your <kanji>origin</kanji> <kanji>state</kanji>. For Japan (since this is a Japanese word), that would be the main island, the big one in the middle: <vocabulary>Honshu</vocabulary>. You''ll have to know some minor Japanese geography for this. Go look at a map of Japan and study it over real quick to see where I''m talking about if you don''t know already.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2865', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E5%B7%9E', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:40:06Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.506533Z'),
(2866, 'vocabulary', '曲', '曲', 'This almost shares the meaning with the kanji. It''s essentially the same thing, though when it''s alone it refers to a specific <vocabulary>piece of music</vocabulary> (aka a <vocabulary>tune</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2866', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B2', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:40:37Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:51:25.132787Z'),
(2867, 'vocabulary', '名曲', '名曲', '<kanji>Famous</kanji> <kanji>music</kanji> is <vocabulary>famous music</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2867', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8D%E6%9B%B2', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:41:07Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(2868, 'vocabulary', '有る', '有る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>have</kanji>, so this verb version means <vocabulary>to have</vocabulary>. An alternative meaning is <vocabulary>to exist</vocabulary>, because having something means that it exists for you.

The kanji isn''t used too often for this word, so just use the kana when you write it. But you''ll see the kanji used sometimes, so it''s important to be able to recognize it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2868', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%89%E3%82%8B', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:41:17Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:43:27.327904Z'),
(2869, 'vocabulary', '有名', '有名', 'When you <kanji>have</kanji> a <kanji>name</kanji> (that is known to people) or you <kanji>have</kanji> <kanji>famous</kanji>ness, you are probably someone who is <vocabulary>famous</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2869', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%89%E5%90%8D', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:41:23Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(2870, 'vocabulary', '次', '次', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2870', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%A1', 10, 1, '2012-03-09T00:41:29Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:54.897014Z'),
(2871, 'vocabulary', '次回', '次回', 'The <kanji>next</kanji> <kanji>time</kanji> you look at this kanji, you should know that it means <vocabulary>next time</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2871', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%A1%E5%9B%9E', 8, 1, '2012-03-09T00:42:01Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.242014Z'),
(2872, 'vocabulary', '目次', '目次', 'Your <kanji>eye</kanji> is on what''s <kanji>next</kanji>. How is this possible to see what''s coming up? That''s because you''re looking at a <vocabulary>table of contents</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2872', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%AE%E6%AC%A1', 9, 1, '2012-03-09T00:42:17Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:44:54.985188Z'),
(2873, 'vocabulary', '年次', '年次', 'The <kanji>year</kanji> that is <kanji>next</kanji> is coming up. What will you do in the year that is next that you did this year too? What do you do that is <vocabulary>annual</vocabulary>?

年次 is a formal term typically used in business settings, such as in 年次報告書 (annual report) or 年次休暇 (annual leave).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2873', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E6%AC%A1', 33, 1, '2012-03-09T00:42:27Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.306641Z'),
(2874, 'vocabulary', '死ぬ', '死ぬ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>death</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to die</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2874', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%BB%E3%81%AC', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:42:37Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.328687Z'),
(2875, 'vocabulary', '死体', '死体', 'The kanji for <kanji>death</kanji> and <kanji>body</kanji> put together make for a <vocabulary>dead body</vocabulary>. Hopefully you don''t have to use or see this vocab too much.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2875', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%BB%E4%BD%93', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:42:44Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.664355Z'),
(2876, 'vocabulary', '死亡', '死亡', '<kanji>Death</kanji> and <kanji>deceased</kanji>. That''s a lot of death. This is <vocabulary>mortality</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2876', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%BB%E4%BA%A1', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:42:52Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.430048Z'),
(2877, 'vocabulary', '羊', '羊', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well — <vocabulary>sheep</vocabulary>!

羊 can also mean <vocabulary>ram</vocabulary> in some contexts. After all, a ram is just a male sheep.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2877', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%8A', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:42:59Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.673935Z'),
(2878, 'vocabulary', '羊毛', '羊毛', 'A <kanji>sheep</kanji>''s <kanji>fur</kanji> is <vocabulary>wool</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2878', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%8A%E6%AF%9B', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:43:07Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.3097Z'),
(2879, 'vocabulary', '血', '血', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2879', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%80', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:43:14Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.09137Z'),
(2880, 'vocabulary', '出血', '出血', 'When the thing that is <kanji>exit</kanji>ing is <kanji>blood</kanji>, you are probably <vocabulary>bleeding</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2880', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E8%A1%80', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:43:20Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.100715Z'),
(2881, 'vocabulary', '国', '国', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2881', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BD', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:43:33Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.110347Z'),
(2882, 'vocabulary', '中国', '中国', 'The <kanji>middle</kanji> <kanji>country</kanji>... who could that be? Let''s think about it. Who invented kanji and therefore probably thinks they''re the middle of the world? Who also is just a huge country that ruled much of Asia? That would be none other than <vocabulary>China</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2882', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E5%9B%BD', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:43:47Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.436675Z'),
(2883, 'vocabulary', '天国', '天国', 'This is <kanji>heaven</kanji> <kanji>country</kanji>. Where is that? Probably just in <vocabulary>heaven</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>paradise</vocabulary>.

天国 is the general term for heaven. Depending on the context, it can also figuratively refer to any place that feels heavenly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2883', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A9%E5%9B%BD', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:44:31Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:57:35.064021Z'),
(2884, 'vocabulary', '全国', '全国', 'When something involves <kanji>all</kanji> the <kanji>country</kanji> you know it''s <vocabulary>nationwide</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>national</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2884', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E5%9B%BD', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:44:39Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:47:49.244838Z'),
(2885, 'vocabulary', '四国', '四国', 'The <kanji>four</kanji> <kanji>country</kanji> is another section of Japan. It''s quite nice, too. Look on a map and try to find it. So, the meaning of this word is also the reading, which you should be able to figure out because it''s all on''yomi: <vocabulary>Shikoku</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2885', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B%E5%9B%BD', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:44:55Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:13:05.258541Z'),
(2886, 'vocabulary', '外国', '外国', 'An <kanji>outside</kanji> <kanji>country</kanji> is one outside of your own country. That is a <vocabulary>foreign country</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>other countries</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>overseas</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2886', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E5%9B%BD', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:45:01Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(2887, 'vocabulary', '外国人', '外国人', 'You know 外人 the word that means "foreigner," but that word is a bit informal and can come off as rude. The nicer, more politically correct version of the word is the one above. An <kanji>outside</kanji> <kanji>country</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji>. To differentiate this from "foreigner" we''re going to use the more polite English version of this word as well (just like in Japanese): <vocabulary>Foreign Person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2887', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E5%9B%BD%E4%BA%BA', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:45:08Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(2888, 'vocabulary', '米国', '米国', '<kanji>Rice</kanji> <kanji>country</kanji> is the <vocabulary>United States</vocabulary>, otherwise known as <vocabulary>America</vocabulary>. Maybe they call it that because of all the rolling plains in the middle of the country? You could grow a lot of rice out there.

米国 is a more formal written term for the <vocabulary>USA</vocabulary>. You''ll see it in news articles and official documents, but in everyday speech people usually say アメリカ.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2888', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B1%B3%E5%9B%BD', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:45:14Z', NULL, '2026-01-19T08:15:51.228829Z'),
(2889, 'vocabulary', '夜', '夜', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2889', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%9C', 5, 1, '2012-03-09T00:45:26Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T00:56:32.806837Z'),
(2890, 'vocabulary', '今夜', '今夜', 'The <kanji>now</kanji> <kanji>night</kanji> is the current night, aka <vocabulary>tonight</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2890', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A%E5%A4%9C', 11, 1, '2012-03-09T00:45:32Z', NULL, '2025-11-19T09:31:34.966185Z'),
(2891, 'vocabulary', '姉妹', '姉妹', 'Your <kanji>older sister</kanji> and your <kanji>younger sister</kanji>. These are your <vocabulary>sisters</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2891', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%89%E5%A6%B9', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:45:39Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.718549Z'),
(2892, 'vocabulary', '妹', '妹', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2892', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%B9', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:45:59Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.926824Z'),
(2893, 'vocabulary', 'お姉さん', 'お姉さん', 'The お makes the word more polite. You''re elevating the "<vocabulary>older sister</vocabulary>" title because you''re a good younger sibling. Otherwise, the meaning of this word is the same as the kanji - just the feeling is different. You can also use this word to refer to a youthful-looking woman that you don''t know, as in "Excuse me <vocabulary>miss</vocabulary>!" ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2893', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E5%A7%89%E3%81%95%E3%82%93', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:46:07Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.728397Z'),
(2894, 'vocabulary', '店', '店', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2894', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%97', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:46:22Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.73737Z'),
(2895, 'vocabulary', '明るい', '明るい', 'This is a single kanji plus the hiragana るい. Words that end in い are usually adjectives, and this is no exception. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>bright</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>bright</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2895', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%8E%E3%82%8B%E3%81%84', 4, 1, '2012-03-09T00:46:29Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(2896, 'vocabulary', '不明', '不明', 'When it''s <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>bright</kanji>, you can''t see what''s around you. In the dark, everything feels <vocabulary>unknown</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>unclear</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>uncertain</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2896', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%98%8E', 16, 1, '2012-03-09T00:46:37Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(2897, 'vocabulary', '東方', '東方', 'The <kanji>east</kanji> <kanji>direction</kanji> is <vocabulary>eastward</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>the East</vocabulary>.

This word has an old-fashioned feel to it, so you may only encounter it in fiction or historical writing. Or, if you''re a gamer, the 東方 Project series of games. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2897', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%B1%E6%96%B9', 58, 1, '2012-03-09T00:46:50Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:18:24.908021Z'),
(2898, 'vocabulary', '東北', '東北', 'The <kanji>East</kanji> <kanji>North</kanji> area of Japan is called <vocabulary>Tohoku</vocabulary>, or the <vocabulary>Tohoku region</vocabulary>. It was the region hit by the 2011 Tohoku Earthquake. 

This can also mean <vocabulary>northeast region</vocabulary> when talking about China or Japan, but for other countries (and for the compass direction), we''d use 北東.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2898', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%B1%E5%8C%97', 11, 1, '2012-03-09T00:46:57Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.438028Z'),
(2899, 'vocabulary', '中東', '中東', 'The <kanji>middle</kanji> of the <kanji>east</kanji> is more simply known as the <vocabulary>middle east</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2899', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E6%9D%B1', 8, 1, '2012-03-09T00:48:12Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T01:03:36.464046Z'),
(2900, 'vocabulary', '南東', '南東', 'The <kanji>South</kanji> <kanji>East</kanji> is the <vocabulary>Southeast</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2900', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%97%E6%9D%B1', 10, 1, '2012-03-09T00:48:59Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.943115Z'),
(2901, 'vocabulary', '歩く', '歩く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>walk</kanji> so the verb version means <vocabulary>to walk</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2901', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A9%E3%81%8F', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:49:08Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.148935Z'),
(2902, 'vocabulary', '直す', '直す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. Remember how 直 can also mean <kanji>fix</kanji>? Well, this word uses that meaning, so it''s <vocabulary>to fix</vocabulary>. This is when you''re fixing something, as in doing the actual physical action of fixing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2902', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B4%E3%81%99', 15, 1, '2012-03-09T00:49:14Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.129421Z'),
(2903, 'vocabulary', '直る', '直る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. You already learned that 直す means "to fix," where someone actively does the fixing. Well, 直る puts the focus on the thing getting fixed, so it means <vocabulary>to be fixed</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be back in working order</vocabulary>.

In other words, don''t use it to say "I fixed it," but do use it to say "It''s fixed!" or "It''s back to normal!" To remember this, imagine a British person excitedly shouting, "My loo (る) was broken, but now it''s fixed!”', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2903', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B4%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2012-03-09T00:49:21Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:06:56.328049Z'),
(2904, 'vocabulary', '正直', '正直', 'It''s always <kanji>correct</kanji> to say something <kanji>straight</kanji> to another person''s face. This is called being <vocabulary>honest</vocabulary>. Are you an honest person?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2904', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A3%E7%9B%B4', 13, 1, '2012-03-09T00:49:27Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:06:56.328049Z'),
(2905, 'vocabulary', '直行', '直行', 'You proceed <kanji>straight</kanji> ahead to <kanji>go</kanji> somewhere because you''re <vocabulary>going directly</vocabulary> there. You''re making a <vocabulary>nonstop</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>direct</vocabulary> trip — no pitstops or detours.

直行 is commonly seen in transportation contexts, especially when talking about flights. It''s also often used in business settings to describe traveling directly to a destination without making any stops, like visiting a client’s office straight from your home.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2905', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B4%E8%A1%8C', 12, 1, '2012-03-09T00:49:37Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:06:56.328049Z'),
(2906, 'vocabulary', '長い', '長い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>long</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>long</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2906', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%95%B7%E3%81%84', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:50:10Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.773759Z'),
(2907, 'vocabulary', '社長', '社長', 'The <kanji>company</kanji> <kanji>leader</kanji> is the <vocabulary>company president</vocabulary>. Be sure to greet your 社長 with a hearty good morning when they stumble in!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2907', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A4%BE%E9%95%B7', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:50:16Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.783167Z'),
(2908, 'vocabulary', '前', '前', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2908', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%8D', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:50:33Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.792734Z'),
(2909, 'vocabulary', '午前', '午前', 'You have something that''s <kanji>before</kanji> (or in <kanji>front</kanji> of) <kanji>noon</kanji>. What is before noon? That is <vocabulary>AM</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2909', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%88%E5%89%8D', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:50:47Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.421885Z'),
(2910, 'vocabulary', '南', '南', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2910', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%97', 7, 1, '2012-03-09T00:51:01Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T16:35:17.529736Z'),
(2911, 'vocabulary', '後ろ', '後ろ', 'The hiragana attached to this word doesn''t suggest it''s a verb or an adjective, so we can assume it''s a noun. While 後で (you may or may not have seen this word yet) means "after," this one means all the other meanings you learned with the kanji: <vocabulary>behind</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>back</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>rear</vocabulary>. Think of the ろ as a hint. It''s like saying you''re on the "road" (ろ) and you''re driving so fast you''re leaving everything "behind."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2911', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%8C%E3%82%8D', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:51:26Z', NULL, '2025-11-19T07:58:25.77563Z'),
(2912, 'vocabulary', '後で', '後で', 'This shares one of the meanings you learned for the kanji: <vocabulary>after</vocabulary>. You can differentiate this one from 後ろ (which you may or may not have seen yet) thanks to the で on the end. Some people believe that after something''s dead (で), it goes to an afterlife.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2912', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%8C%E3%81%A7', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:51:36Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.280412Z'),
(2913, 'vocabulary', '午後', '午後', 'What is <kanji>noon</kanji> <kanji>after</kanji>? That is <vocabulary>PM</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>afternoon</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2913', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%88%E5%BE%8C', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:51:45Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.905053Z'),
(2914, 'vocabulary', '思う', '思う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>think</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to think</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2914', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%9D%E3%81%86', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:53:01Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.802104Z'),
(2915, 'vocabulary', '星', '星', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2915', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%9F', 9, 1, '2012-03-09T00:53:22Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T02:27:52.417356Z'),
(2916, 'vocabulary', '生活', '生活', 'When you have <kanji>life</kanji> and are <kanji>lively</kanji> that right there is true <vocabulary>life</vocabulary>. What is your 生活 like? Do you live?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2916', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E6%B4%BB', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:54:04Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.812117Z'),
(2917, 'vocabulary', '海', '海', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2917', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B7', 9, 1, '2012-03-09T00:54:16Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.981881Z'),
(2918, 'vocabulary', '海外', '海外', 'The <kanji>outside</kanji> of the <kanji>sea</kanji> is someplace <vocabulary>overseas</vocabulary>. It can also be <vocabulary>foreign</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>abroad</vocabulary> if those words are easier to remember for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2918', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B7%E5%A4%96', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:54:24Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(2919, 'vocabulary', '点', '点', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. As luck would have it, this word actually covers most of the meanings of the English word <vocabulary>point</vocabulary>, too! So it can mean things like <vocabulary>score</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>viewpoint</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>decimal point</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2919', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%B9', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:54:37Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.438759Z'),
(2920, 'vocabulary', '科学', '科学', 'The <kanji>study</kanji> of <kanji>science</kanji> is plainly known as <vocabulary>science</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2920', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%91%E5%AD%A6', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:55:01Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.009297Z'),
(2921, 'vocabulary', 'お茶', 'お茶', 'The お just makes the word more polite. Since tea is so important in Japan, it gets the お. Otherwise, you should be able to figure out the meaning as it''s the same as the kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2921', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E8%8C%B6', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:55:07Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:16:41.901026Z'),
(2922, 'vocabulary', '茶色', '茶色', 'What is the <kanji>color</kanji> of <kanji>tea</kanji>? You''d think it would be green, assuming it''s Japanese... but this word came from China. What color is Chinese tea? It tends to be more <vocabulary>brown</vocabulary>. Think Oolong tea, for example.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2922', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8C%B6%E8%89%B2', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:55:14Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:16:41.901026Z'),
(2923, 'vocabulary', '食べる', '食べる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>eat</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to eat</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2923', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9F%E3%81%B9%E3%82%8B', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:55:33Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.447059Z'),
(2924, 'vocabulary', '首', '首', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2924', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%96', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:55:48Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.465689Z'),
(2925, 'vocabulary', '足首', '足首', 'What is the <kanji>neck</kanji> of your <kanji>leg</kanji>? Think about it. It''s your <vocabulary>ankle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2925', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B6%B3%E9%A6%96', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:55:57Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.849643Z'),
(2926, 'vocabulary', '首になる', '首になる', 'The になる part means "to become," so literally this means "to become neck." If you think about it, when something becomes neck, it has to do with you getting your head cut off (at the neck). But, in today''s modern society we don''t do that too often. Instead, this means you lose your job, aka <vocabulary>to get fired</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2926', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%96%E3%81%AB%E3%81%AA%E3%82%8B', 6, 1, '2012-03-09T00:56:05Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.866588Z'),
(2927, 'vocabulary', '欠点', '欠点', 'You have a <kanji>lack</kanji> of <kanji>point</kanji>s. These are things about yourself you wish were better. These are your <vocabulary>shortcoming</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2927', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%A0%E7%82%B9', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:54:51Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.186566Z'),
(2928, 'vocabulary', '〜氏', '〜氏', 'The kanji means <kanji>family name</kanji> because it goes after a family name in very formal situations. So, it''s really closely tied to that. Because only men got the respect back in the day, we''re calling this vocab word <vocabulary>mister</vocabulary>. You can call me Mr. Koichi if you''d like, too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2928', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%B0%8F', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:55:02Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.222798Z'),
(2929, 'vocabulary', '氏名', '氏名', 'Both of these kanji have to do with names. You have both <kanji>family name</kanji> and <kanji>name</kanji>. Both of those names together makes for a <vocabulary>First And Last Name</vocabulary>... a <vocabulary>full name</vocabulary> if you will.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2929', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%8F%E5%90%8D', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:55:12Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(2930, 'vocabulary', '自由', '自由', 'When you have <kanji>self</kanji> <kanji>reason</kanji>, you are able to think for yourself. When you can think for yourself, you have <vocabulary>freedom</vocabulary>. Very philosophical, this vocab is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2930', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E7%94%B1', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:56:12Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.273102Z'),
(2931, 'vocabulary', '理由', '理由', 'A <kanji>reason</kanji> <kanji>reason</kanji> is a <vocabulary>reason</vocabulary>! How could it not be? Do you have a reason why it shouldn''t be? I thought not.

This word means "reason" as in the reason you''re studying Japanese, or the reason you''re late. That''s why it can also mean similar words like <vocabulary>motive</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>excuse</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2931', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%90%86%E7%94%B1', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:56:28Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.297008Z'),
(2932, 'vocabulary', '〜札', '〜札', 'The kanji and the word are almost exactly the same. When you see this word after a number, it’s for physical <vocabulary>bill</vocabulary>s of money.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2932', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%9C%AD', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:56:36Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.322745Z'),
(2933, 'vocabulary', '千円札', '千円札', 'You know what 千円 is (1000 yen). So if you add a <kanji>bill</kanji> to that you get a <vocabulary>thousand yen bill</vocabulary>. That''s just the little rectangular piece of money that is worth 1000 yen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2933', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%83%E5%86%86%E6%9C%AD', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:56:46Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:10:33.22049Z'),
(2934, 'vocabulary', '国民', '国民', 'The <kanji>country</kanji> <kanji>peoples</kanji> are the people of your country. They are <vocabulary>the people</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2934', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BD%E6%B0%91', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:57:26Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.336172Z'),
(2935, 'vocabulary', '辺り', '辺り', 'This is the noun version of the kanji for <kanji>area</kanji>. This makes it have the same meaning as the kanji: <vocabulary>area</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>vicinity</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>neighborhood</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2935', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%BA%E3%82%8A', 8, 1, '2012-03-27T17:58:05Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.953529Z'),
(2936, 'vocabulary', 'この辺', 'この辺', 'この means "this" so この辺 just means <vocabulary>this area</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2936', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%93%E3%81%AE%E8%BE%BA', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:58:20Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T07:32:52.611124Z'),
(2937, 'vocabulary', '付ける', '付ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>attach</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to attach something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2937', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%98%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:58:28Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:03:42.596356Z'),
(2938, 'vocabulary', '以外', '以外', '<kanji>Compared with</kanji> this, it''s <kanji>outside</kanji> what you wanted. You''ll have anything <vocabulary>other than</vocabulary> that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2938', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%A5%E5%A4%96', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:59:12Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(2939, 'vocabulary', '〜以上', '〜以上', 'Start <kanji>from</kanji> something and then go <kanji>above</kanji> it. Now you have "<vocabulary>no less than</vocabulary> ___," or, in other words, "___ <vocabulary>or more</vocabulary>"! 

When used with numbers, 以上 always includes the number itself, like 十人以上 (10 or more people). In non-numerical contexts, it can mean "more than" in a looser sense, like これ以上もてない (I can''t carry more than this).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2939', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E4%BB%A5%E4%B8%8A', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:59:21Z', NULL, '2026-02-12T21:23:20.631933Z'),
(2940, 'vocabulary', '以前', '以前', '<kanji>From</kanji> <kanji>before</kanji>, just means <vocabulary>previously</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>before</vocabulary> now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2940', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%A5%E5%89%8D', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T17:59:28Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.415837Z'),
(2941, 'vocabulary', '失う', '失う', 'The kanji portion means <kanji>fault</kanji>, so you would think the verb version is "to fault." It''s a little different, though. Think of it this way, if you "fault" something, you''re messing up. What''s a great way to mess up? By losing something. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>to lose</vocabulary>. 

Pretend like you are supposed to hold on to someone''s wedding ring. You make the biggest fault of all. You lose it. Ouch.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2941', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%B1%E3%81%86', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:00:11Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.052596Z'),
(2942, 'vocabulary', '失礼', '失礼', 'When you do something that''s your <kanji>fault</kanji> and then you say "<kanji>thanks</kanji>" after you do it, that''s super-duper <vocabulary>rude</vocabulary>, wouldn''t you say? Imagine doing something like this and then thinking to yourself after... wow, I was really rude.

This is also often used to mean <vocabulary>excuse me</vocabulary>, typically followed by ですが or します. Occasionally, it’s also used just on its own, usually when the speaker holds a higher or equal status to the person being spoken to. This particular usage is more prevalent among older generations and is commonly associated with masculine speech.
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2942', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%B1%E7%A4%BC', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:00:18Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.450593Z'),
(2943, 'vocabulary', '必ず', '必ず', 'This word looks like a verb but it''s not (sorry!). It''s an adverb. The kanji itself means <kanji>certain</kanji> and the vocab version means all sorts of variations on that: <vocabulary>surely</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>certainly</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>without exception</vocabulary> to name a few.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2943', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%85%E3%81%9A', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:00:29Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:33:00.725847Z'),
(2944, 'vocabulary', '必死', '必死', 'Your future consists of <kanji>certain</kanji> <kanji>death</kanji>. How would you feel in this situation? Probably very <vocabulary>frantic</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>desperate</vocabulary>. 

Another translation for this word is simply <vocabulary>certain death</vocabulary>, though I''d rather you learn one of the first two before this one if you can.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2944', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%85%E6%AD%BB', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:00:39Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:33:00.725847Z'),
(2945, 'vocabulary', '未だ', '未だ', 'This is the adverb version of <kanji>未</kanji>, so it means the same thing: <vocabulary>not yet</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>still</vocabulary>, depending on the context.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2945', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AA%E3%81%A0', 28, 1, '2012-03-27T18:00:47Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:12.166321Z'),
(2946, 'vocabulary', '未来', '未来', 'What has <kanji>not yet</kanji> <kanji>come</kanji>? The <vocabulary>future</vocabulary>.

未来 is usually used to talk about the distant future, and it implies that the future can’t be foreseen. So you can use it to speculate about what might lie ahead, but you probably wouldn’t use it to discuss more concrete plans, goals or predictions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2946', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AA%E6%9D%A5', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:00:56Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(2947, 'vocabulary', '末', '末', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Throw a "the" in there if you''d like, as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2947', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AB', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:01:04Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.511775Z'),
(2948, 'vocabulary', '年末', '年末', 'The <kanji>year</kanji> <kanji>end</kanji> is the <vocabulary>year end</vocabulary> aka the <vocabulary>end of the year</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2948', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E6%9C%AB', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:01:11Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.532378Z'),
(2949, 'vocabulary', '月末', '月末', 'You know how 月 can also mean <kanji>month</kanji>? If you add 末 on there you have the "month end" aka the <vocabulary>end of the month</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2949', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%88%E6%9C%AB', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:01:19Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.552767Z'),
(2950, 'vocabulary', '高校', '高校', 'The <kanji>tall</kanji> <kanji>school</kanji> is for when your kids get about as tall as they''re going to get. That''s <vocabulary>high school</vocabulary>, when most people stop growing taller.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2950', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E6%A0%A1', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:40:12Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.565906Z'),
(2951, 'vocabulary', '学校', '学校', 'A <kanji>study</kanji> <kanji>school</kanji> is obviously a place where people study and learn. In English, that''s simply a <vocabulary>school</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2951', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A6%E6%A0%A1', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:40:21Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.584284Z'),
(2952, 'vocabulary', '夏', '夏', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2952', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%8F', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:42:08Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:04:44.644357Z'),
(2953, 'vocabulary', '夏休み', '夏休み', 'You know that 夏 means <kanji>summer</kanji> and 休 means <kanji>rest</kanji>. What is a "summer rest" though? It''s a <vocabulary>summer break</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>summer vacation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2953', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%8F%E4%BC%91%E3%81%BF', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:42:16Z', NULL, '2026-03-22T18:33:30.29931Z'),
(2954, 'vocabulary', '家', '家', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2954', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B6', 6, 1, '2012-03-27T18:42:24Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.683369Z'),
(2955, 'vocabulary', '作家', '作家', 'This is a strange one, but only at first glance. You <kanji>make</kanji> a <kanji>house</kanji>. You''d think this would mean something like "building a house" or "construction" or something. Instead, it means writer. Think of it this way: A <vocabulary>writer</vocabulary> <kanji>makes</kanji> (writes and creates works) in their <kanji>home</kanji>. Writers don''t make their word magic in a corporate office!

作家 is generally used to refer to someone who writes professionally as an occupation, especially in the field of literature. It can also be used more broadly to refer to a creator who produces individual works of art or craft.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2955', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%9C%E5%AE%B6', 15, 1, '2012-03-27T18:42:33Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.840624Z'),
(2956, 'vocabulary', '弱い', '弱い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>weak</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>weak</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2956', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%B1%E3%81%84', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:42:49Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T06:03:32.726396Z'),
(2957, 'vocabulary', '弱点', '弱点', 'The <kanji>weak</kanji> <kanji>point</kanji> is your <vocabulary>weak point</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2957', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%B1%E7%82%B9', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:42:57Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T06:03:32.726396Z'),
(2958, 'vocabulary', '時', '時', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2958', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%82', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:44:21Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.688619Z'),
(2959, 'vocabulary', '一時', '一時', 'When it''s <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>o''clock</kanji> it''s <vocabulary>one o''clock</vocabulary>. It''s time to eat lunch, or take a nap, or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2959', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%99%82', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:44:31Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.7039Z'),
(2960, 'vocabulary', '二時半', '二時半', 'When it''s <kanji>two</kanji> <kanji>o''clock</kanji> and a <kanji>half</kanji> what time do you think it is? That would be <vocabulary>two thirty</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2960', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E6%99%82%E5%8D%8A', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:44:41Z', NULL, '2025-12-11T20:12:42.646308Z'),
(2961, 'vocabulary', '何時', '何時', 'This is a good word to know to ask for the time. <kanji>What</kanji> is the <kanji>time</kanji>? This is how you ask <vocabulary>what time</vocabulary> it is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2961', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%95%E6%99%82', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:44:55Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(2962, 'vocabulary', '同時', '同時', 'The <kanji>same</kanji> <kanji>time</kanji>? Okay, let''s say what we think this word means at the <vocabulary>same time</vocabulary>. Whoops, I just said it, sorry.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2962', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8C%E6%99%82', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:45:04Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.741631Z'),
(2963, 'vocabulary', '紙', '紙', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2963', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%99', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:46:44Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.793285Z'),
(2964, 'vocabulary', '手紙', '手紙', 'A <kanji>hand</kanji> <kanji>paper</kanji> is a <vocabulary>letter</vocabulary> because letters are generally written by hand on paper.

If you want a more graphic image, imagine writing a letter on your hand, and how much it would hurt to chop off your hand and send it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2964', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E7%B4%99', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:46:52Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.8057Z'),
(2965, 'vocabulary', '日記', '日記', 'When you <kanji>write down</kanji> your <kanji>day</kanji> (and do it every day) that''s your <vocabulary>diary</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2965', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E8%A8%98', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:46:59Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.817946Z'),
(2966, 'vocabulary', '通る', '通る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>pass through</kanji> so the verb vocab version means things like <vocabulary>to pass through</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to pass</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to travel along</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>to stop by</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2966', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%9A%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-03-27T18:47:06Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:36.107082Z'),
(2967, 'vocabulary', '交通', '交通', 'Even though it gets <kanji>mix</kanji>ed up as it <kanji>passes through</kanji> itself, modern <vocabulary>traffic</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>transport</vocabulary> systems are pretty amazing. This word refers to traffic and transport for pedestrians, bikes, cars, buses, trains, planes... anything that gets us from A to B. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2967', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%A4%E9%80%9A', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:47:23Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.830922Z'),
(2968, 'vocabulary', '高い', '高い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>tall</kanji> or <kanji>expensive</kanji>? It''s the same: <vocabulary>tall</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>expensive</vocabulary>. 

Weird that this word means both of these things, yeah? Just imagine money piled up to be really tall. That money is tall because it needs to buy something expensive.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2968', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E3%81%84', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:47:33Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.843185Z'),
(2969, 'vocabulary', '強い', '強い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>strong</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>strong</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2969', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%B7%E3%81%84', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:47:41Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:05:21.088648Z'),
(2970, 'vocabulary', '強力', '強力', 'You have <kanji>strong</kanji> <kanji>power</kanji>. You are <vocabulary>strong</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>powerful</vocabulary>. Feel your <vocabulary>strength</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2970', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%B7%E5%8A%9B', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:47:48Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(2971, 'vocabulary', '教える', '教える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>teach</kanji> so the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to teach</vocabulary>. It''s also used to mean <vocabulary>to tell</vocabulary> in a more general sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2971', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%99%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:47:55Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.898234Z'),
(2972, 'vocabulary', '教室', '教室', 'The <kanji>teach</kanji>ing <kanji>room</kanji> is just a <vocabulary>classroom</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2972', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%99%E5%AE%A4', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:48:13Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:57:32.391485Z'),
(2973, 'vocabulary', '心理学', '心理学', 'The <kanji>reason</kanji> your <kanji>heart</kanji> does what it does is all psychological. The <kanji>study</kanji> of that is <vocabulary>psychology</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2973', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83%E7%90%86%E5%AD%A6', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:48:21Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.477535Z'),
(2974, 'vocabulary', '地理', '地理', 'The <kanji>reason</kanji> the <kanji>earth</kanji> is the way it is is because of the <vocabulary>geography</vocabulary> of it. Though it may seem kind of strange that geography is bringing reason to the earth, if you think of geography as a study, then you can think about how you try to get reason through that, as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2974', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E7%90%86', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:48:28Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.781345Z'),
(2975, 'vocabulary', '組', '組', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2975', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%84', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:49:15Z', NULL, '2026-04-05T19:31:59.040578Z'),
(2976, 'vocabulary', '船', '船', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2976', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%B9', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:49:23Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.985685Z'),
(2977, 'vocabulary', '風船', '風船', 'A <kanji>wind</kanji> <kanji>boat</kanji>. This is a strange one for sure. You may assume this is like one of those giant balloons you can go ride around in, and you''d be partly right. Really, this word covers the general word <vocabulary>balloon</vocabulary>, though it especially has to do with the toy-style balloons (or smaller balloons). It''s a little round wind ship!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2977', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A2%A8%E8%88%B9', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:49:30Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.997892Z'),
(2978, 'vocabulary', '先週', '先週', 'The <kanji>previous</kanji> <kanji>week</kanji> was <vocabulary>last week</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2978', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%88%E9%80%B1', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:49:39Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:27:12.714404Z'),
(2979, 'vocabulary', '今週', '今週', 'The <kanji>now</kanji> <kanji>week</kanji> is <vocabulary>this week</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2979', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A%E9%80%B1', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:49:46Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:31:08.765744Z'),
(2980, 'vocabulary', '週末', '週末', 'The <kanji>end</kanji> of the <kanji>week</kanji> is the <vocabulary>weekend</vocabulary>... that is, unless you work on the weekend, but we can''t cater to everyone here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2980', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%B1%E6%9C%AB', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:51:59Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:31:08.777135Z'),
(2981, 'vocabulary', '雪', '雪', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2981', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%AA', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:52:12Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:31:08.789911Z'),
(2982, 'vocabulary', '魚', '魚', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2982', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AD%9A', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:52:41Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:31:08.802484Z'),
(2983, 'vocabulary', '金魚', '金魚', 'A <kanji>gold</kanji> <kanji>fish</kanji> is really just gold in color, but it''s still called a <vocabulary>goldfish</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2983', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%91%E9%AD%9A', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:52:50Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:31:08.815787Z'),
(2984, 'vocabulary', '海魚', '海魚', 'An <kanji>ocean</kanji> <kanji>fish</kanji> is simply an <vocabulary>ocean fish</vocabulary>. They also happen to be the most delicious kind of fish.

海魚 is often used in contrast with 川魚 (river fish), especially when talking about fish as food.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2984', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B7%E9%AD%9A', 9, 1, '2012-03-27T18:52:58Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.657658Z'),
(2985, 'vocabulary', '鳥', '鳥', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2985', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%B3%A5', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:53:04Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:12:26.957805Z'),
(2986, 'vocabulary', '白鳥', '白鳥', 'There are many <kanji>white</kanji> <kanji>bird</kanji>s out there, for sure, but one really stands out if you ask me. That bird is the <vocabulary>swan</vocabulary>. It''s big, it''s white, and it''s majestic.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2986', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BD%E9%B3%A5', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:53:22Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:12:26.957805Z'),
(2987, 'vocabulary', '黄色', '黄色', 'The <kanji>yellow</kanji> <kanji>color</kanji> is just the color <vocabulary>yellow</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2987', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BB%84%E8%89%B2', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:53:30Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:31.867514Z'),
(2988, 'vocabulary', '黒い', '黒い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>black</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>black</vocabulary>. Special Note: The noun version of black also exists in Japanese. It is just 黒 with the same pronunciation, but minus the い which makes it adjective-tastic.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2988', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BB%92%E3%81%84', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:54:00Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:31.898195Z'),
(2989, 'vocabulary', '黒人', '黒人', 'A <kanji>black</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is simply a <vocabulary>Black person</vocabulary>, kind of like how a 白人 is a "White person," but a different  color.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2989', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BB%92%E4%BA%BA', 7, 1, '2012-03-27T18:54:09Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(2990, 'vocabulary', '馬', '馬', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2990', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%AC', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:34:47Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.690935Z'),
(2991, 'vocabulary', '馬力', '馬力', 'This word was probably made pretty recently. A <kanji>horse</kanji>''s <kanji>power</kanji>? That is <vocabulary>horsepower</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2991', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%AC%E5%8A%9B', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:34:58Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(2992, 'vocabulary', '支える', '支える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>support</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to support</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2992', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%AF%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:35:30Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.71363Z'),
(2993, 'vocabulary', '支店', '支店', 'The <kanji>support</kanji> <kanji>shop</kanji> is the <vocabulary>branch office</vocabulary>. Presumably this is because the regular "shop" is the "shop" and anything that supports it is the local (branch) office.

If it helps, this word has the same meaning as 支局 which you will also be learning in this level.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2993', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%AF%E5%BA%97', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:35:37Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.725568Z'),
(2994, 'vocabulary', '住む', '住む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>dwell</kanji>, so the verb version means <vocabulary>to live</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to reside</vocabulary>, or of course <vocabulary>to dwell</vocabulary>. Basically, someone is living somewhere. Where do you live?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2994', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%8F%E3%82%80', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:35:44Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.73566Z'),
(2995, 'vocabulary', '住人', '住人', 'If you <kanji>dwell</kanji> somewhere and you’re a <kanji>person</kanji>, that makes you a <vocabulary>resident</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2995', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%8F%E4%BA%BA', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:35:51Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(2996, 'vocabulary', '住民', '住民', 'They <kanji>dwell</kanji> here, these <kanji>people</kanji>. So what are they? They’re <vocabulary>residents</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2996', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%8F%E6%B0%91', 9, 1, '2012-04-05T23:35:58Z', NULL, '2026-01-19T23:06:39.715579Z'),
(2997, 'vocabulary', '助ける', '助ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>help</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to help</vocabulary>. Don''t confuse this with 助かる which means "to be helped." You know that 助ける is "to help" because you''re doing the helping. You know that you''re doing the helping because you <reading>care</reading> (ける).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2997', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%A9%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:36:13Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.772895Z'),
(2998, 'vocabulary', '助力', '助力', 'When you <kanji>help</kanji> with your <kanji>power</kanji>, you are providing <vocabulary>assistance</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>support</vocabulary>.

Note that this is a formal word that’s mostly used in professional situations. It implies that someone has done something substantial for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2998', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%A9%E5%8A%9B', 44, 1, '2012-04-05T23:36:20Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(2999, 'vocabulary', '助手', '助手', 'Remember how 手 can mean a "doer" in some words? What do you think your <kanji>help</kanji> <kanji>doer</kanji> is? It’s someone who helps you, so it’s your <vocabulary>assistant</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>helper</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>aide</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/2999', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%A9%E6%89%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-05T23:36:27Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.925459Z')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;


This word is commonly found in formal or professional contexts. In more casual settings, you can just use アドバイス (advise) instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3000', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%A9%E8%A8%80', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:36:35Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.796186Z'),
(3001, 'vocabulary', '医大', '医大', 'You''ve seen 大学 to mean "college." So take the 大 and put it with <kanji>medicine</kanji> to create "medicine college", or <vocabulary>medical university</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3001', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%BB%E5%A4%A7', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:36:44Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(3002, 'vocabulary', '医学', '医学', 'The <kanji>study</kanji> of <kanji>medicine</kanji> is <vocabulary>medical science</vocabulary>. It can also be called <vocabulary>medicine</vocabulary>, but make sure to think of it as the "medicine" where it has to do with "medical science."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3002', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%BB%E5%AD%A6', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:36:52Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.821345Z'),
(3003, 'vocabulary', '医者', '医者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> who works in <kanji>medicine</kanji> is a <vocabulary>doctor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3003', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%BB%E8%80%85', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:36:59Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(3004, 'vocabulary', '君主国', '君主国', 'One <kanji>ruler</kanji> is the <kanji>master</kanji> of this entire <kanji>country</kanji>. Why, this sounds quite like a <vocabulary>monarchy</vocabulary> to me. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3004', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%9B%E4%B8%BB%E5%9B%BD', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:37:21Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.84281Z'),
(3005, 'vocabulary', '君', '君', 'Okay, so the kanji itself means <kanji>buddy</kanji> and so does the word, but it also means <vocabulary>you</vocabulary> as well. This is one of the several ways to say "you" in Japanese, and this one is kind of an "affectionate" you. Maybe you''d say it with someone you''re close to. Maybe you''d say it with a girlfriend or boyfriend. You wouldn''t want to use it with your boss or teacher, though. Make sure to keep that in mind. It''s kind of like saying "hey buddy" or "hey you," after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3005', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%9B', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:37:28Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.854584Z'),
(3006, 'vocabulary', '〜君', '〜君', 'Add this to the end of someone''s name, it''s like saying ______<kanji>buddy</kanji>. It''s generally used as a name ender (like さん) for boys and young men. Don''t use this on people who are higher ranked than you (socially or literally). As in, you wouldn''t use it on your boss. Use it on your younger male subordinates as well as boys who aren''t adults yet.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3006', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%90%9B', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:37:35Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.865548Z'),
(3007, 'vocabulary', '対する', '対する', 'When you do (する) <kanji>versus</kanji>, you choose <vocabulary>to face</vocabulary> an opponent. It’s where your attention and efforts are going to <vocabulary>be directed toward</vocabulary>. Alternatively, you can use this word <vocabulary>to contrast</vocabulary> one thing with another.

This word is used to help point out the relationship between two things in a sentence, where one thing is focusing on another thing. This is all a little vague and perhaps confusing, so check out the collocations and context sentences to get a better grasp of this word!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3007', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BE%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:37:53Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.875482Z'),
(3008, 'vocabulary', '対外', '対外', 'Something is <kanji>versus</kanji> the <kanji>outside</kanji>. Back in the day, everything was a battle with the outside. You had the Mongols... the rebels... and the foreigners. So, when you say something is "versus the outside" you''re just saying it''s something compared to where you are right now. It''s something that''s <vocabulary>foreign</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>external</vocabulary>.

This is a formal word typically used at the beginning of compounds to indicate that the subject is related to foreign affairs, such as 対外政策 (foreign policy) or 対外貿易 (foreign trade).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3008', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BE%E5%A4%96', 38, 1, '2012-04-05T23:38:01Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(3009, 'vocabulary', '対立', '対立', 'If it''s you <kanji>versus</kanji> someone else and you''re <kanji>stand</kanji>ing in each other''s way, you''re in <vocabulary>conflict</vocabulary>. Better hope they''re not bigger than you, or this <vocabulary>confrontation</vocabulary> might turn out to be a bad idea…

対立 is when two sides are in conflict, usually because their ideas, goals, or interests just don''t match. It''s the kind of <vocabulary>opposition</vocabulary> where neither side wants to back down, and they just keep standing against each other.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3009', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BE%E7%AB%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-05T23:38:15Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.874719Z'),
(3010, 'vocabulary', '反対', '反対', 'The <kanji>anti</kanji> <kanji>versus</kanji>... You''d think this would be a double negative, but it''s like a lot of other words where you have two similar words combined... it just means the same thing as those two words. In this case, the anti-versus of something would be the <vocabulary>opposite</vocabulary>. 

反対 refers to something that''s opposite in direction, position, or nature. In some cases, it can also mean <vocabulary>opposition</vocabulary> because disagreeing or resisting to something means someone is taking the opposite side on a matter.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3010', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8D%E5%AF%BE', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:38:22Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.886318Z'),
(3011, 'vocabulary', '局', '局', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3011', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%80', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:38:29Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.896452Z'),
(3012, 'vocabulary', '支局', '支局', 'The <kanji>support</kanji> <kanji>bureau</kanji> is the group that supports a business. It is the <vocabulary>branch office</vocabulary>. 

If it helps, this word has the same meaning as <vocabulary>支店</vocabulary> which you will also be learning in this level.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3012', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%AF%E5%B1%80', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:38:35Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.904309Z'),
(3013, 'vocabulary', '役', '役', 'If you have a certain <kanji>duty</kanji> then it''s your job to play that <vocabulary>role</vocabulary>. You have to play the <vocabulary>part</vocabulary>. Like in a play!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3013', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%B9', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:38:43Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.912518Z'),
(3014, 'vocabulary', '役人', '役人', 'The <kanji>duty</kanji> this <kanji>person</kanji> has? It''s to the government, which makes them a <vocabulary>public official</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>government official</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3014', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%B9%E4%BA%BA', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:38:49Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3015, 'vocabulary', '役に立つ', '役に立つ', 'When duty (役) comes calling, those willing to stand (立つ) and accept it are the ones who are going <vocabulary>to be useful</vocabulary> to society. Don''t run from your duties — stand up, face them, and prove yourself a useful member of the human race!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3015', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%B9%E3%81%AB%E7%AB%8B%E3%81%A4', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:38:56Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:30:47.934615Z'),
(3016, 'vocabulary', '大役', '大役', 'A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>duty</kanji> is a <vocabulary>great duty</vocabulary>. It can also be an <vocabulary>important task</vocabulary>. Think about some kind of big duty you have. Then, think about what an important task it is too. Feel proud of your important task.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3016', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%BD%B9', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:39:03Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(3017, 'vocabulary', '決', '決', 'Noun version of <kanji>decide</kanji>? It''s a <vocabulary>decision</vocabulary>.

決 is a formal word that refers to a decisive <vocabulary>verdict</vocabulary>. It''s used in formal phrases like 決を採る (to take a vote) or 決を下す (to pass a judgment), both of which are about making decisions or delivering verdicts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3017', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%BA', 46, 1, '2012-04-05T23:39:09Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:09:27.747622Z'),
(3018, 'vocabulary', '決める', '決める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>decide</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to decide</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3018', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%BA%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-05T23:39:16Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.995141Z'),
(3019, 'vocabulary', '決心', '決心', 'When you <kanji>decide</kanji> with your <kanji>heart</kanji> you have true <vocabulary>determination</vocabulary>, unlike when you decide with your mind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3019', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%BA%E5%BF%83', 11, 1, '2012-04-05T23:39:31Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:09:27.747622Z'),
(3020, 'vocabulary', '未決', '未決', 'Something that is <kanji>not yet</kanji> <kanji>decide</kanji>d... it is <vocabulary>pending</vocabulary>!

未決 is a formal term, typically used in legal or administrative contexts to refer to matters that are still <vocabulary>unresolved</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>undecided</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3020', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AA%E6%B1%BA', 46, 1, '2012-04-05T23:39:39Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:09:27.747622Z'),
(3021, 'vocabulary', '自決', '自決', 'When your <kanji>self</kanji> <kanji>decide</kanji>s on something, that''s called <vocabulary>self-determination</vocabulary>. But the word is mainly used in Japan as a euphemism for <vocabulary>suicide</vocabulary>. It''s kind of important that you know that.

This word is very formal and generally used in historical contexts, such as referencing suicides that occurred during wartime.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3021', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E6%B1%BA', 56, 1, '2012-04-05T23:39:48Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:09:27.747622Z'),
(3022, 'vocabulary', '究明', '究明', 'If you do <kanji>research</kanji> to <kanji>bright</kanji>en a subject area, you''re probably doing some kind of <vocabulary>investigation</vocabulary> that illuminates the unknown.

This word is usually found in academic and scientific contexts, and suggests a thorough investigation meant to ascertain or clarify something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3022', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%B6%E6%98%8E', 32, 1, '2012-04-05T23:40:13Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(3023, 'vocabulary', '研究', '研究', 'When you <kanji>sharpen</kanji> <kanji>research</kanji> you''re just doing a <vocabulary>study</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>investigation</vocabulary>. Or in other words, you''re doing <vocabulary>research</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3023', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%94%E7%A9%B6', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:40:23Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:11:35.013012Z'),
(3024, 'vocabulary', '研究室', '研究室', 'Remember how 研究 means "research"? Well the <kanji>room</kanji> where you do research is a <vocabulary>laboratory</vocabulary>!

研究室 refers to a <vocabulary>lab room</vocabulary>, often within a larger institution like a university or college.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3024', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%94%E7%A9%B6%E5%AE%A4', 9, 1, '2012-04-05T23:40:30Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:14:09.110945Z'),
(3025, 'vocabulary', '投げる', '投げる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>throw</kanji> so the verb version means <vocabulary>to throw</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3025', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%95%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:40:46Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:35.517244Z'),
(3026, 'vocabulary', '心身', '心身', 'This looks like your <kanji>heart</kanji> and your <kanji>body</kanji>. But the "heart" here isn''t your literal heart, it''s the figurative kind associated with your emotional and mental state. That makes this word your <vocabulary>mind and body</vocabulary>.

心身 is a slightly formal word that often appears in health and wellness contexts to emphasize total well-being. But it can also be used in casual self-care talk, like to describe how a hot spring rejuvenates you mentally and physically.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3026', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83%E8%BA%AB', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:41:07Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:16:35.017174Z'),
(3027, 'vocabulary', '身体', '身体', 'The <kanji>body</kanji> of a <kanji>body</kanji> is… <vocabulary>the body</vocabulary>! 

身体 is more technical-sounding than 体. You may encounter it in contexts related to physical examinations, for example, or in discussions about someone''s physical ability in an athletic context.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3027', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BA%AB%E4%BD%93', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:41:13Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:18:13.959936Z'),
(3028, 'vocabulary', '全身', '全身', '<kanji>All</kanji> of someone''s <kanji>body</kanji> is their <vocabulary>whole body</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>entire body</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3028', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E8%BA%AB', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:41:21Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:19:19.562968Z'),
(3029, 'vocabulary', '森', '森', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3029', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A3%AE', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:42:21Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:35.564338Z'),
(3030, 'vocabulary', '両者', '両者', 'When you have <kanji>both</kanji> <kanji>someone</kanji>s, you have <vocabulary>both people</vocabulary>. You''ll notice over time that 者 is often used to indicate a person or type of person, so keep that in mind for future words as well!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3030', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%A1%E8%80%85', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:42:58Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(3031, 'vocabulary', '作者', '作者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> who <kanji>make</kanji>s is someone who creates things. Back in the day, a creator like this may have been known as many things, but the big one that stands out is an <vocabulary>author</vocabulary>.

Wondering about the difference between 作者 and 作家? Basically, 作者 just refers to the creator of a particular creative work, such as a book, article, artwork, piece of music, etc., while 作家 refers to someone who creates those things as an occupation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3031', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%9C%E8%80%85', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:43:05Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(3032, 'vocabulary', '学者', '学者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> who does <kanji>study</kanji> for a living. That is a <vocabulary>scholar</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3032', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A6%E8%80%85', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:43:19Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(3033, 'vocabulary', '工学者', '工学者', 'Do you remember 工学 from a couple levels ago? This is <kanji>someone</kanji> who is in 工学. If 工学 is "engineering" then 工学者 is an <vocabulary>engineer</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3033', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A5%E5%AD%A6%E8%80%85', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:43:27Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(3034, 'vocabulary', '場合', '場合', 'What <kanji>location</kanji> <kanji>suit</kanji>s a situation the best? That always depends on the <vocabulary>case</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>circumstance</vocabulary>. You can''t know the suitability of the location until you have all the facts about the circumstances.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3034', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%B4%E5%90%88', 14, 1, '2012-04-05T23:45:07Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:14.373561Z'),
(3035, 'vocabulary', '場所', '場所', 'A <kanji>location</kanji> <kanji>place</kanji> is either a <vocabulary>place</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>location</vocabulary>, just like the kanji it''s made from.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3035', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%B4%E6%89%80', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:45:15Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:18:00.076038Z'),
(3036, 'vocabulary', '入場', '入場', 'When you <kanji>enter</kanji> a <kanji>location</kanji>, that''s your <vocabulary>entrance</vocabulary>.

This is a common word for admission into public events or venues, like a concert hall, a sports stadium, or a theme park.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3036', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E5%A0%B4', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:45:23Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:35.653752Z'),
(3037, 'vocabulary', '工場', '工場', 'A <kanji>construction</kanji> <kanji>location</kanji> is a <vocabulary>factory</vocabulary>. A factory is a place where you or machines do a bunch of construction!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3037', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A5%E5%A0%B4', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:45:33Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:35.670881Z'),
(3038, 'vocabulary', '所', '所', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Wondering about how 所 differs from 場所? While 場所 only refers to a physical location, 所 can also refer to an abstract place, such as a point in time, a situation, or a condition. Hence, 所 usually needs some context to clarify what kind of place you are talking about.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3038', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%80', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:45:48Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:18:00.076038Z'),
(3039, 'vocabulary', '入所', '入所', 'You <kanji>enter</kanji> a <kanji>facility</kanji>. So this is your <vocabulary>admission</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>entrance</vocabulary> into that facility. 

入所 is for entering facilities or institutions, often those with names ending in 所, like a 研究所 (research institute). It''s also used in prison contexts. You might gain "admission" to prison for not doing your WaniKani reviews, for example, at least in countries ruled by Crabigators...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3039', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E6%89%80', 11, 1, '2012-04-05T23:45:55Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:18:00.076038Z'),
(3040, 'vocabulary', '出所', '出所', 'You <kanji>exit</kanji> a <kanji>facility</kanji>. It''s your <vocabulary>release from prison</vocabulary>!

出所 commonly refers to leaving or being released from facilities ending in 所, particularly 刑務所 (prisons). It can also be a slightly formal word for <vocabulary>source</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>origin</vocabulary>, often used in discussions of copyright to specify the origin of cited information, images, or content.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3040', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E6%89%80', 38, 1, '2012-04-05T23:46:02Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:18:00.076038Z'),
(3041, 'vocabulary', '他所', '他所', 'When you''re talking about some <kanji>other</kanji> <kanji>place</kanji> you''re talking about <vocabulary>another place</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3041', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%96%E6%89%80', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:46:09Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T04:47:22.214427Z'),
(3042, 'vocabulary', '名所', '名所', 'A <kanji>famous</kanji> <kanji>place</kanji> is a <vocabulary>famous place</vocabulary>. When traveling around Japan, you should ask about the 名所 if you can.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3042', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8D%E6%89%80', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:46:33Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(3043, 'vocabulary', '住所', '住所', 'If you <kanji>dwell</kanji> in a <kanji>place</kanji>, that place is your <vocabulary>address</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3043', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%8F%E6%89%80', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:46:49Z', NULL, '2026-01-19T23:05:13.073436Z'),
(3044, 'vocabulary', '朝', '朝', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3044', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%9D', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:47:02Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:35.870399Z'),
(3045, 'vocabulary', '朝ごはん', '朝ごはん', 'ごはん is a food or meal (maybe you''ll learn the kanji for it later, but it''s not usually used, so you''re learning it this way now). What is your <kanji>morning</kanji> meal? That is <vocabulary>breakfast</vocabulary> (not to be confused with second breakfast, you hobbit).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3045', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%9D%E3%81%94%E3%81%AF%E3%82%93', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:47:12Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:35.895376Z'),
(3046, 'vocabulary', '朝日', '朝日', 'The <kanji>morning</kanji> <kanji>sun</kanji> is not only the <vocabulary>morning sun</vocabulary>, but it''s the <vocabulary>rising sun</vocabulary> as well (because that''s when the sun rises, right?).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3046', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%9D%E6%97%A5', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:47:20Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:35.923544Z'),
(3047, 'vocabulary', '番号', '番号', 'A <kanji>number in a series</kanji> plus a <kanji>number</kanji>. You know what happens when two kanji with basically the same meaning get combined into one by now, right? It just turns out to be the same meaning, basically. This word means <vocabulary>number</vocabulary>, and it''s used in contexts like phone numbers, exam numbers, and serial numbers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3047', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%AA%E5%8F%B7', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:47:26Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:35.942202Z'),
(3048, 'vocabulary', '一番', '一番', 'When you''re the number <kanji>one</kanji> in a <kanji>number in a series</kanji>, you''re <vocabulary>number one</vocabulary> ba-bay!

This word can refer to something ranked <vocabulary>first</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>best</vocabulary> in comparison to other things, or it can specifically refer to something marked with the number one, like an athlete with the number one on their jersey.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3048', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E7%95%AA', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:47:34Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:35.985738Z'),
(3049, 'vocabulary', '二番', '二番', 'When you''re number <kanji>two</kanji> in a <kanji>number in a series</kanji> you''re <vocabulary>number two</vocabulary>. Not quite as good as being 一番, but take what you can get, you know?

This word can refer to something ranked <vocabulary>second</vocabulary> in comparison to other things, or it can specifically refer to something marked with the number two, like an athlete with the number two on their jersey.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3049', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E7%95%AA', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:47:44Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:36.020457Z'),
(3050, 'vocabulary', '交番', '交番', 'This word is a little tricky — at first, the combination of <kanji>mix</kanji> and <kanji>number in a series</kanji> doesn''t seem to add up to <vocabulary>police box</vocabulary>, but there''s a catch. This word is actually an abbreviated combo of two other words: The 交 comes from 交替 (in rotation) and the 番 comes from 立番 (standing watch).

A <vocabulary>police box</vocabulary> is basically a tiny community police station staffed by one or two officers. It''s something that is not common in many Western countries, but it is pretty much all over Japan. 

', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3050', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%A4%E7%95%AA', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:47:52Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:36.041661Z'),
(3051, 'vocabulary', '答え', '答え', 'You can guess this is a noun version of the kanji because the hiragana sticking out of it isn''t an う-related sound or an い-related sound. So, what is the noun version of <kanji>answer</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>answer</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3051', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AD%94%E3%81%88', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:48:37Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:36.062926Z'),
(3052, 'vocabulary', '答える', '答える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>answer</kanji> so the verb version means <vocabulary>to answer</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3052', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AD%94%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:48:46Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:36.08498Z'),
(3053, 'vocabulary', '絵', '絵', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

絵 refers to <vocabulary>picture</vocabulary>s, such as <vocabulary>painting</vocabulary>s, <vocabulary>drawing</vocabulary>s, or <vocabulary>illustration</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3053', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B5', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:48:53Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:12:48.398208Z'),
(3054, 'vocabulary', '買う', '買う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>buy</kanji> so the verb form is <vocabulary>to buy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3054', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%B7%E3%81%86', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:49:04Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:13:56.397935Z'),
(3055, 'vocabulary', '道', '道', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. 

Don''t confuse this with the word 〜道, though. This one doesn''t have a 〜 at the beginning, meaning it''s not attached to other words. This word is just plain old road. It has its own meaning!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3055', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%93', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:49:18Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:36.187591Z'),
(3056, 'vocabulary', '歩道', '歩道', 'The <kanji>road</kanji> you <kanji>walk</kanji> on is the <vocabulary>sidewalk</vocabulary>... at least I hope that''s where you walk.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3056', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A9%E9%81%93', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:49:33Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:36.212842Z'),
(3057, 'vocabulary', '〜道', '〜道', 'This is a word that''s attached to the end of various other words. Think of a "road" as a <vocabulary>way</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>method of</vocabulary> something. For example, the どう in Judo is the soft "way." There are many words that end with 道, and you''ll see them later on. For now, know that this means <vocabulary>method of</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>way</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3057', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E9%81%93', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:49:47Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:36.255212Z'),
(3058, 'vocabulary', '間', '間', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. 

Don''t confuse this with 〜間 which is similar in meaning but different in reading. The 〜間 means it''s attached to something. This is a standalone word.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3058', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%93', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:49:57Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:36.31319Z'),
(3059, 'vocabulary', '時間', '時間', 'The <kanji>time</kanji> <kanji>interval of time</kanji> is just the <vocabulary>time</vocabulary>. As is often the case, if two similar meaning kanji are combined into one word, they tend to produce a word with a similar meaning. Excuse me, friend, what''s the 時間?

Note that 時間 can also be used as a unit of time measurement to mean <vocabulary>hour</vocabulary>(s). In this case, it''s always used as a suffix — usually following a number or the question word 何 (なん).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3059', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%82%E9%96%93', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:50:04Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T00:48:36.603386Z'),
(3060, 'vocabulary', '〜間', '〜間', 'When you stick this onto the end of another word that talks about an <kanji>interval of time</kanji> in some way, you''re indicating the <vocabulary>amount of time</vocabulary> that has gone past. For example, 二年間 is "two years." 一時間 is "one hour." Any amount of time + 〜間 will make it an "amount of time" instead of just a specific time.

Note that 〜間 can be omitted, especially in conversations, when it''s clear that you''re talking about a duration of time from the context. However, the exception is 時. You always say 〜時間 to refer to the amount of hours, to differentiate from 〜時 (〜o''clock).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3060', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E9%96%93', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:50:12Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:36.373538Z'),
(3061, 'vocabulary', '人間', '人間', 'The <kanji>person</kanji> <kanji>interval</kanji>. Just think of different intervals each with a different type of species. The raccoon interval... the dog interval... the person interval... they are the <vocabulary>human</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3061', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E9%96%93', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:50:20Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3062, 'vocabulary', '間もなく', '間もなく', 'The もなく part is like saying "there isn''t even." So, when "there isn''t even <kanji>an interval of time</kanji>," things will happen <vocabulary>soon</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>before long</vocabulary>.

You''ll probably hear this the most in train announcements letting you know your train will <vocabulary>shortly</vocabulary> be arriving in Tokyo or wherever.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3062', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%93%E3%82%82%E3%81%AA%E3%81%8F', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:50:34Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:36.423974Z'),
(3063, 'vocabulary', '空間', '空間', 'The <kanji>sky</kanji> <kanji>interval</kanji> is an area of the sky. In places that are just empty sky, there is only <vocabulary>space</vocabulary>. Of course, not technically... there''s something there, but back in the day when kanji was being made, nobody knew that. This word can mean both space in regards to outer space as well as the space around you in your room.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3063', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA%E9%96%93', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:50:48Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:36.436288Z'),
(3064, 'vocabulary', '雲', '雲', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3064', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%B2', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:50:55Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:00:36.451866Z'),
(3065, 'vocabulary', '数える', '数える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>count</kanji>, so obviously the verb version is <vocabulary>to count</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3065', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B0%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:51:16Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.041761Z'),
(3066, 'vocabulary', '数', '数', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

This word is used to say things like "a high number" and "the number of students". ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3066', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B0', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:51:23Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.041761Z'),
(3067, 'vocabulary', '数字', '数字', 'A <kanji>number</kanji> <kanji>letter</kanji> isn''t a letter like the ones you''re reading now, it''s more like a <vocabulary>numeral</vocabulary>, like 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, etc.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3067', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B0%E5%AD%97', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:51:30Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.041761Z'),
(3068, 'vocabulary', '数学', '数学', 'The <kanji>study</kanji> of <kanji>number</kanji>s is <vocabulary>mathematics</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>math</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3068', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B0%E5%AD%A6', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:51:39Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.041761Z'),
(3069, 'vocabulary', '人数', '人数', 'The <kanji>person</kanji> <kanji>count</kanji> is <vocabulary>the number of people</vocabulary>. Don''t confuse this with the "population" or something like that, though. This is more like "how many people are at this event?" What''s the 人数, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3069', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E6%95%B0', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:51:47Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3070, 'vocabulary', '点数', '点数', 'The <kanji>number</kanji> of <kanji>point</kanji>s is how many <vocabulary>points</vocabulary> there are (aka the <vocabulary>score</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3070', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%B9%E6%95%B0', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:51:54Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.041761Z'),
(3071, 'vocabulary', '楽しい', '楽しい', 'Ending with an い tells you that this word is an い-adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>pleasure</kanji>? Well, you could say it''s "pleasurable," but I''m afraid that''s not what we''re going for here. In this case, this word is a little more innocent. It just means <vocabulary>fun</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3071', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%BD%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:52:20Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T18:49:45.773585Z'),
(3072, 'vocabulary', '音楽', '音楽', 'What is the <kanji>sound</kanji> of <kanji>pleasure</kanji>? What <kanji>sound</kanji> puts you at <kanji>ease</kanji>? For most people that is some nice <vocabulary>music</vocabulary>. Just be sure you don''t put on any Bieber... that would be like 不音楽 or something... I kid, I kid.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3072', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9F%B3%E6%A5%BD', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:52:41Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:40:46.484453Z'),
(3073, 'vocabulary', '楽', '楽', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3073', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%BD', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:52:50Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:49.626356Z'),
(3074, 'vocabulary', '話す', '話す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>talk</kanji> so the verb version of this is <vocabulary>to talk</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to speak</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3074', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%B1%E3%81%99', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:52:59Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:21:40.452645Z'),
(3075, 'vocabulary', '電話', '電話', 'An <kanji>electricity</kanji> <kanji>talk</kanji>. What is this witchcraft? The only way to talk via electricity is with a <vocabulary>telephone</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3075', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E8%A9%B1', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:53:14Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:49.729808Z'),
(3076, 'vocabulary', '会話', '会話', 'When you <kanji>meet</kanji> and <kanji>talk</kanji> with someone else, you''re having a <vocabulary>conversation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3076', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9A%E8%A9%B1', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:53:21Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:49.782962Z'),
(3077, 'vocabulary', '電車', '電車', 'You would think that an <kanji>electricity</kanji> <kanji>car</kanji> was an electric car, but this word actually refers to a different kind of vehicle that''s often powered by electricity: a <vocabulary>train</vocabulary>.

Literally, 電車 refers to an <vocabulary>electric train</vocabulary> rather than a diesel or steam locomotive. But in everyday Japanese, people often just use it as a general word for "train," since most modern trains in Japan run on electricity anyway. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3077', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E8%BB%8A', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:53:42Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:01:37.042022Z'),
(3078, 'vocabulary', '電気', '電気', '<kanji>Electricity</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji> is just a fancy way to say <vocabulary>electricity</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3078', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E6%B0%97', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:53:49Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:49.832621Z'),
(3079, 'vocabulary', '電池', '電池', 'A <kanji>pond</kanji> for <kanji>electricity</kanji>? May seem weird at first, but if you think of something that''s gathering all the electricity into one pond... that thing is like a <vocabulary>battery</vocabulary> (and is a battery).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3079', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E6%B1%A0', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:53:57Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:49.878009Z'),
(3080, 'vocabulary', '電子', '電子', 'An <kanji>electricity</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji>. What is a really small part of electricity? What makes up electricity? That would be the <vocabulary>electron</vocabulary>. This word can also refer to things that are <vocabulary>electronic</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3080', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E5%AD%90', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:54:15Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:49.89583Z'),
(3081, 'vocabulary', '電力', '電力', 'The <kanji>electricity</kanji> <kanji>power</kanji> of something is its <vocabulary>electric power</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3081', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E5%8A%9B', 8, 1, '2012-04-05T23:54:21Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(3082, 'vocabulary', '事', '事', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

As a vocab word on its own, 事 usually refers to an intangible or abstract <vocabulary>thing</vocabulary>, like いい事 (good thing) or する事 (things to do). 

Depending on the context, it can also mean <vocabulary>matter</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>occurrence</vocabulary>, like 大切な事 (important matter) or きのうの事 (what happened yesterday).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3082', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:49:07Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.198999Z'),
(3083, 'vocabulary', '用事', '用事', 'If there''s a <kanji>task</kanji> <kanji>occurrence</kanji>, then there''s something you have to do, a.k.a. a <vocabulary>task</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>errand</vocabulary>.

用事 generally refers to tasks that you personally need to take care of. Often that means errands like buying groceries or paying bills. It could also mean a doctor''s appointment, meeting with a friend, or work tasks like attending a meeting and finishing an assignment. It''s anything on your list of <vocabulary>things to do</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3083', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%A8%E4%BA%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:49:41Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.322855Z'),
(3084, 'vocabulary', '工事', '工事', '<kanji>Construction</kanji> <kanji>occurrences</kanji> are just different kinds of <vocabulary>construction</vocabulary>!

工事 often refers to <vocabulary>construction work</vocabulary>, especially for buildings, roads, and other infrastructure projects. But it can also cover repairs, upgrades, and installations of things like electrical systems, plumbing, phone lines, and internet as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3084', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A5%E4%BA%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:49:49Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.335038Z'),
(3085, 'vocabulary', '大事', '大事', 'You know 事 means "occurrence," but it can also mean "matter." And if something is a  <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>matter</kanji> to someone, it must be a very <vocabulary>important</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>valuable</vocabulary> thing to them.

大事 describes something that holds great importance or value. For example, you can use 大事 to describe something or someone that matters a lot to you. You can also use 大事 to describe a crucial task or moment. And if you want to bring up a serious topic, you may say that you have something 大事 to talk about.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3085', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E4%BA%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:49:56Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(3086, 'vocabulary', '返事', '返事', 'You know 事 means "occurrence," but it can also mean "matter." And if you <kanji>return</kanji> the <kanji>matter</kanji> to another person, you''re giving a <vocabulary>reply</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>response</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3086', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%94%E4%BA%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:50:37Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.370305Z'),
(3087, 'vocabulary', '食事', '食事', 'A regular <kanji>eat</kanji>ing <kanji>occurrence</kanji> is also known as a <vocabulary>meal</vocabulary>. 

食事 can refer to a specific meal, like breakfast, lunch, or dinner. It can also mean your overall <vocabulary>diet</vocabulary>, like the kinds of foods you usually eat. 食事 can even be a verbal noun for the act of <vocabulary>dining</vocabulary>, so 食事する can be used to talk about having a meal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3087', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9F%E4%BA%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:50:44Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.384003Z'),
(3088, 'vocabulary', '使う', '使う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>use</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to use</vocabulary>. If you''re talking about money, this can also mean <vocabulary>to spend</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3088', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%BF%E3%81%86', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:50:51Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.396389Z'),
(3089, 'vocabulary', '道具', '道具', 'The <kanji>tool</kanji>s you use on the <kanji>road</kanji> of life are important to have. What is your most important <vocabulary>tool</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3089', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%93%E5%85%B7', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:51:09Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T02:54:10.694388Z'),
(3090, 'vocabulary', '家具', '家具', 'Your <kanji>house</kanji> <kanji>tools</kanji> aren''t the tools you use to build a house. They''re more like the things that are a part of your house that you use like tools. Those things would be your <vocabulary>furniture</vocabulary>. So, remember, your house tools are just furniture.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3090', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B6%E5%85%B7', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:51:16Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T02:54:10.694388Z'),
(3091, 'vocabulary', '受ける', '受ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>receive</kanji>, and this verb means just about the same thing: <vocabulary>to receive something</vocabulary>!

This is a transitive verb, so it takes a direct object. But 受ける often implies passive reception, generally of intangible objects like an education, or damage. It can also mean <vocabulary>to take something</vocabulary>, like taking an exam or a job offer.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3091', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%97%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:51:23Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:48.601574Z'),
(3092, 'vocabulary', '和風', '和風', 'The <kanji>Japanese style</kanji> <kanji>style</kanji>. The Japanese often like to think they''re very unique and have a very special kind of style (and often times they do). So, you''ll hear this word used when someone wants to describe this unique sense of style. Wafuuuu! 

Note that this word uses alternative kanji meanings in both of the kanji. They''re in the kanji explanation, but they''re not the primaries, so it may take a little extra effort to learn this word depending on what you studied!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3092', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%92%8C%E9%A2%A8', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:51:37Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.437057Z'),
(3093, 'vocabulary', '平和', '平和', 'The <kanji>flat</kanji> <kanji>peace</kanji>. This is a peace that will last, because there''s no waves... no ripples... just flat. This is plain old <vocabulary>peace</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3093', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B3%E5%92%8C', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:51:44Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.449105Z'),
(3094, 'vocabulary', '和食', '和食', '<kanji>Japanese style</kanji> <kanji>eat</kanji> ... well, let''s change "eat" to "food" and you have <vocabulary>Japanese style food</vocabulary>. This is a good way to differentiate between an American style restaurant and a Japanese style restaurant. What kind of food you feeling like tonight? Oh, how about some 和食?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3094', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%92%8C%E9%A3%9F', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:51:51Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.463487Z'),
(3095, 'vocabulary', '和室', '和室', 'A <kanji>Japanese style</kanji> <kanji>room</kanji> tends to be pretty small. This mainly refers to the kind of room that''s covered in tatami mats, though. If you''re looking for places to stay in Japan, a 和室 will be something like that. You''ll pull the futon out of the closet and sleep on the floor. So comfy, these <vocabulary>Japanese style room</vocabulary>s!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3095', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%92%8C%E5%AE%A4', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:52:02Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:57:32.391485Z'),
(3096, 'vocabulary', '和服', '和服', '<kanji>Japanese style</kanji> <kanji>clothes</kanji> (<vocabulary>Japanese Style Clothes</vocabulary>). We''re talking kimonos and yukata and things like that. Basically, this differentiates Japanese clothing from Western clothing. 

What kind of clothes will you wear to the festival tonight? Oh, probably some 和服.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3096', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%92%8C%E6%9C%8D', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:52:11Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.509197Z'),
(3097, 'vocabulary', '始める', '始める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>begin</kanji> or <kanji>start</kanji>, and the verb version is <vocabulary>to begin</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to begin something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to start</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to start something</vocabulary>.

This is a transitive verb. That means it takes a direct object. You start something. Like studying! You should have started studying earlier though, huh?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3097', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%8B%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-23T23:52:55Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.941151Z'),
(3098, 'vocabulary', '始まる', '始まる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means start, and this verb means <vocabulary>to begin</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to start</vocabulary>. 始まる is the intransitive version, so it doesn''t take a direct object. A war begins, for example, or the new school term starts. No direct object, right?

Verbs ending in okurigana that rhyme with ある tend to be intransitive like this. You can remember this because if something is ある, it''s just there, existing, not acting <em>on</em> anything else. That''s why verbs that sound like it are often intransitive too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3098', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%8B%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2012-04-23T23:53:03Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:23.644469Z'),
(3099, 'vocabulary', '始めに', '始めに', 'Remember how 始める means "to begin something"? 始め is its noun version and means "beginning," as in the start of an action or process. に marks a point in time. So 始めに means <vocabulary>in the beginning</vocabulary>. 

始めに can also mean <vocabulary>to begin with</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to start with</vocabulary> when introducing an opening action, like 始めに、ウォーミングアップをしましょう (let''s begin with some warm-up exercises).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3099', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%8B%E3%82%81%E3%81%AB', 11, 1, '2012-04-23T23:53:10Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:16.263769Z'),
(3100, 'vocabulary', '予定', '予定', 'Something has been <kanji>determine</kanji>d <kanji>beforehand</kanji>. What is this thing? This action? It is a <vocabulary>plan</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3100', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%88%E5%AE%9A', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:53:17Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(3101, 'vocabulary', '決定', '決定', 'It''s been <kanji>decide</kanji>d and <kanji>determine</kanji>d. The <vocabulary>decision</vocabulary> has been made.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3101', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%BA%E5%AE%9A', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:53:24Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(3102, 'vocabulary', '実', '実', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3102', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9F', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:53:40Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.553691Z'),
(3103, 'vocabulary', '事実', '事実', 'The <kanji>occurrence</kanji> of <kanji>truth</kanji> is a <vocabulary>fact</vocabulary>.

事実 is primarily used as a noun, but it can also be used to say something is <vocabulary>factual</vocabulary>. Additionally, in relatively formal writing it can be used as an adverb to mean <vocabulary>in fact</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3103', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8B%E5%AE%9F', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:53:46Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.572354Z'),
(3104, 'vocabulary', '実力', '実力', 'The <kanji>truth</kanji> behind someone''s <kanji>strength</kanji> is their <vocabulary>true strength</vocabulary>. It is also their <vocabulary>true ability</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3104', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9F%E5%8A%9B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:54:04Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(3105, 'vocabulary', '服', '服', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3105', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%8D', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:54:30Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.624337Z'),
(3106, 'vocabulary', '泳ぐ', '泳ぐ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>swim</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to swim</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3106', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%B3%E3%81%90', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:54:36Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.64676Z'),
(3107, 'vocabulary', '物', '物', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Besides <vocabulary>thing</vocabulary>, it has the related meanings of <vocabulary>object</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>item</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3107', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%A9', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:55:10Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(3108, 'vocabulary', '本物', '本物', 'The <kanji>real</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji> is the <vocabulary>real thing</vocabulary>, aka the <vocabulary>real deal</vocabulary>. 

If you learned 本 as <kanji>book</kanji> you can also think of this word as the "book thing." What is this book thing? Is it the real version of the book? Is it the real deal? If so, I''ll pay you a million.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3108', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E7%89%A9', 11, 1, '2012-04-23T23:55:23Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(3109, 'vocabulary', '名物', '名物', 'The <kanji>famous</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji> of an area is their <vocabulary>famous product</vocabulary>, but it sounds better in English if you say it''s the <vocabulary>local specialty</vocabulary>. When you''re traveling around Japan, be sure to be on the lookout for the 名物! It''s always fun.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3109', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8D%E7%89%A9', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:55:30Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(3110, 'vocabulary', '苦い', '苦い', 'The kanji for 苦 means <kanji>suffering</kanji>. This word, however, is a bit different, but you''ll see how it''s related. It means <vocabulary>bitter tasting</vocabulary>. Think of putting the most <vocabulary>bitter</vocabulary> thing in the world in your mouth. You feel sick, your mouth gets all weird, and you try to spit it out but the bitterness remains. Obviously eating bitter tasting things will cause you to suffer.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3110', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%A6%E3%81%84', 10, 1, '2012-04-23T23:55:44Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.859603Z'),
(3111, 'vocabulary', '苦しい', '苦しい', 'This word ends in い, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. In fact, it ends in しい, which makes it even more likely it''s an adjective. Since the kanji 苦 means <kanji>suffering</kanji>, this word is the adjective version of that: <vocabulary>painful</vocabulary>.

苦しい usually describes a painful or <vocabulary>distressing</vocabulary> feeling that''s ongoing and hard to endure, rather than a physical sensation of pain. For example, you can use 苦しい when it''s hard to breathe, when you feel emotional pain, or even when your finances are <vocabulary>strained</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3111', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%A6%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:55:50Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:27:37.515266Z'),
(3112, 'vocabulary', '乗る', '乗る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>ride</kanji> so the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to ride</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3112', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%97%E3%82%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:57:01Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.74364Z'),
(3113, 'vocabulary', '乗せる', '乗せる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>ride</kanji> so this word has to do with riding. There''s another word that''s similar (乗る which means to ride). This particular one is transitive, meaning there is a direct object. The action is done to someone or something. So this means <vocabulary>to give someone a ride</vocabulary> when it''s used for people, and <vocabulary>to place something on</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to load something</vocabulary> when it''s used for things. 

Use the せ as a hint. The せ is a "sail" (せ) that you use on your boat to give someone a ride. How nice of you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3113', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%97%E3%81%9B%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-23T23:57:08Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.888519Z'),
(3114, 'vocabulary', '乗り物', '乗り物', 'You know that 乗り has something to do with <kanji>riding</kanji> and that 物 is a <kanji>thing</kanji>. So what is a "riding thing?" Nowadays, at least, that would be a <vocabulary>vehicle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3114', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%97%E3%82%8A%E7%89%A9', 11, 1, '2012-04-23T23:57:17Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(3115, 'vocabulary', 'お客さん', 'お客さん', 'The さん on the end is the name honorific さん. It''s there to show some respect. It''s like saying "Mr. Guest!" or something like that. But that sounds dumb in English. We''ll just call this word <vocabulary>guest</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>visitor</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>customer</vocabulary>. You''ll hear this word a lot when you''re at a store or you''re a customer somewhere in Japan. You are the お客さん, in that situation. Alternatively, you''ll also hear お客さま which is a more polite version of this word (because it ends in さま and not さん).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3115', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E5%AE%A2%E3%81%95%E3%82%93', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:58:12Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.753283Z'),
(3116, 'vocabulary', '客室', '客室', 'A <kanji>guest</kanji>''s <kanji>room</kanji> is the <vocabulary>guest room</vocabulary> (the room you put guests in, duh!).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3116', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%A2%E5%AE%A4', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:58:22Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:57:32.391485Z'),
(3117, 'vocabulary', '〜屋', '〜屋', 'You''ll see this attached to other words... like "meat" or "fish." When you see this, you''ll know it is a ___ store, making this word mean <vocabulary>store</vocabulary> (same as the kanji).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3117', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%B1%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:58:29Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:18:39.371876Z'),
(3118, 'vocabulary', '部屋', '部屋', 'If you have a <kanji>part</kanji> of a <kanji>roof</kanji>, that part is your <vocabulary>room</vocabulary>. Just imagine a roof divided into sections in a house, with the sections going all the way down to the ground. Each section is its own room, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3118', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%A8%E5%B1%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:58:35Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:18:39.371876Z'),
(3119, 'vocabulary', '肉屋', '肉屋', 'A <kanji>meat</kanji> <kanji>shop</kanji> is a place that sells meat. Who sells meat? A <vocabulary>butcher shop</vocabulary>!

肉屋 often refers to the butcher shop, but it can also mean the actual butchers who work there. You might also see this as お肉屋さん, but that won''t change the meaning — it can still be either the shop or the butcher, depending on context!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3119', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%89%E5%B1%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:58:43Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:18:39.371876Z'),
(3120, 'vocabulary', '茶屋', '茶屋', 'A <kanji>tea</kanji> <kanji>shop</kanji> is a <vocabulary>tea shop</vocabulary>. Mmm, delicious, delicious tea.

Traditionally, a 茶屋 was a Japanese <vocabulary>teahouse</vocabulary> where travelers could rest and enjoy tea and snacks. These kinds of 茶屋 are less common today, but the term is still often used for cafes or restaurants designed to evoke the charm of historical Japanese teahouses.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3120', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8C%B6%E5%B1%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:58:54Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:16:41.901026Z'),
(3121, 'vocabulary', '魚屋', '魚屋', 'A <kanji>fish</kanji> <kanji>shop</kanji> is a <vocabulary>fish shop</vocabulary>, a place from which you buy fish. Especially in Japan, very important.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3121', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AD%9A%E5%B1%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:59:03Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:18:39.371876Z'),
(3122, 'vocabulary', '名古屋', '名古屋', 'The place that''s <kanji>famous</kanji> for <kanji>old</kanji> <kanji>roof</kanji>s. I don''t know why, but it''s the city of <vocabulary>Nagoya</vocabulary>. Just associate the two things together and it''ll be hard to forget (so long as you''ve heard of Nagoya before - if not, read up about it!).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3122', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8D%E5%8F%A4%E5%B1%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:59:10Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(3123, 'vocabulary', '今度', '今度', 'In the <kanji>now</kanji> <kanji>occurrence</kanji> you''ll learn your kanji, no? This word means <vocabulary>this time</vocabulary>. Despite being "this time" it has the feeling of <vocabulary>next time</vocabulary>, though. It''s like saying "okay, this time we''ll get it" where "this time" really means "next time," making it a little bit different from 今回.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3123', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A%E5%BA%A6', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:59:16Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.852969Z'),
(3124, 'vocabulary', '丁度', '丁度', 'Okay, we''re going to cheat a little bit to remember the meaning of this word. Let''s say you take the temperature of the <kanji>street</kanji>. It is exactly one hundred <kanji>degree</kanji>s. That''s <vocabulary>exactly</vocabulary> what it is. <vocabulary>Just</vocabulary> 100 degrees, not one degree more or less. You put your eggs on the street and watch them cook.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3124', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%81%E5%BA%A6', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:59:22Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:15:32.558339Z'),
(3125, 'vocabulary', '毎度', '毎度', 'When something happens at <kanji>every</kanji> <kanji>occurrence</kanji> and at <kanji>every</kanji> <kanji>degree</kanji>, it''s something that happens <vocabulary>frequently</vocabulary>. It happens <vocabulary>each time</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3125', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%8E%E5%BA%A6', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:59:32Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:28:04.29877Z'),
(3126, 'vocabulary', '角度', '角度', 'An <kanji>angle</kanji> <kanji>degree</kanji> is just an <vocabulary>angle</vocabulary>. 90 degrees? 160 degrees? They''re all still angles when you come down to it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3126', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%92%E5%BA%A6', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:59:40Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.897923Z'),
(3127, 'vocabulary', '〜度', '〜度', 'The kanji <kanji>degree</kanji> can mean both degree in terms of angles and degree in terms of temperature. This word is how you talk about these things. Just attach it to a number and you have yourself the number of <vocabulary>degrees</vocabulary> that something is. After the numbers 1, 2 and 3 (and in some other situations) this can also mean <vocabulary>times</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3127', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%BA%A6', 9, 1, '2012-04-23T23:59:47Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.908076Z'),
(3128, 'vocabulary', '待つ', '待つ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>wait</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to wait</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3128', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%85%E3%81%A4', 12, 1, '2012-04-23T23:59:56Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:17.025591Z'),
(3129, 'vocabulary', '待たせる', '待たせる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>wait</kanji> so this word has something to do with waiting. Use the たせ as a hint. Someone "tossed" a ball off into a ditch, and then they had to find it. They made you wait for them to get the ball too, even though it''s a cheap piece of crap. Feel upset about this. That''ll help you remember that this means <vocabulary>to make someone wait</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to keep someone waiting</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3129', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%85%E3%81%9F%E3%81%9B%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-04-24T00:00:03Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:17.048474Z'),
(3130, 'vocabulary', '持つ', '持つ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

The kanji itself means <kanji>hold</kanji> and the verb version is <vocabulary>to hold something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to carry something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to own something</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to keep something</vocabulary>.

This is a transitive verb, meaning it takes a direct object. You hold something, like chopsticks, have something, like power, or keep something, like your friend''s book over the weekend.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3130', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%81%E3%81%A4', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:01:11Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T15:52:26.706826Z'),
(3131, 'vocabulary', '金持ち', '金持ち', 'When you <kanji>hold</kanji> <kanji>gold</kanji> you are a <vocabulary>rich person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3131', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%91%E6%8C%81%E3%81%A1', 11, 1, '2012-04-24T00:01:18Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:17.096369Z'),
(3132, 'vocabulary', '気持ち', '気持ち', 'The <kanji>energy</kanji> you hold (持つ) within you is your <vocabulary>feeling</vocabulary>s. What kind of feeling is your energy giving you today?

気持ち can refer to your current mood, to a particular emotion inside you, or to physical feelings and sensations. You hear it in phrases like 気持ちいい (feels good), 気持ちわるい (feels gross), or 気持ちが分かる (I understand how you feel).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3132', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E6%8C%81%E3%81%A1', 11, 1, '2012-04-24T00:01:24Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:39:40.272444Z'),
(3133, 'vocabulary', '世界', '世界', 'Both kanji mean <kanji>world</kanji>, so it should be easy to guess this is a word for <vocabulary>the World</vocabulary>. It also means <vocabulary>society</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>the Universe</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3133', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%96%E7%95%8C', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:01:31Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:21:16.900712Z'),
(3134, 'vocabulary', '発売', '発売', 'You want things to <kanji>depart</kanji> from your store so you have to <kanji>sell</kanji> them. You put all these things up for <vocabulary>sale</vocabulary>. You are <vocabulary>selling</vocabulary> these items.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3134', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BA%E5%A3%B2', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:01:37Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:32:06.067577Z'),
(3135, 'vocabulary', '発音', '発音', 'The <kanji>sound</kanji> that <kanji>departs</kanji> from your mouth is you talking. Imagine yourself saying things and words departing your mouth. They are bent in different ways and have different colors, highlighting your <vocabulary>pronunciation</vocabulary> of certain words and phrases.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3135', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BA%E9%9F%B3', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:01:43Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:22:52.12866Z'),
(3136, 'vocabulary', '発見', '発見', 'You make <kanji>departure</kanji> from your home off into the great beyond. You will go to <kanji>see</kanji> the unseen, and hopefully make a great <vocabulary>discovery</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3136', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BA%E8%A6%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:01:49Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:22:52.12866Z'),
(3137, 'vocabulary', '相談', '相談', 'A <kanji>mutual</kanji> <kanji>talk</kanji>. Both sides are talking to each other and the feelings are mutual. Imagine you''re discussing a problem. You want to get help with it, so you get some <vocabulary>consultation</vocabulary> from your friend. This <vocabulary>discussion</vocabulary> was very helpful.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3137', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B8%E8%AB%87', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:01:55Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.990239Z'),
(3138, 'vocabulary', '相手', '相手', 'Remember how 手 can mean a "doer"? Now, what do you think your <kanji>mutual</kanji> <kanji>doer</kanji> might be? It’s someone who shares the same activities or tasks with you. This person could be your <vocabulary>partner</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>companion</vocabulary>, or even an <vocabulary>opponent</vocabulary> in certain situations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3138', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B8%E6%89%8B', 15, 1, '2012-04-24T00:02:01Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.609589Z'),
(3139, 'vocabulary', '〜県', '〜県', 'Attach this word onto the end of a prefecture place name and you have the <kanji>prefecture</kanji> portion of _____ <vocabulary>prefecture</vocabulary>. These are a lot like states or provinces.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3139', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E7%9C%8C', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:02:08Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:25:07.757919Z'),
(3140, 'vocabulary', '美しい', '美しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. More precisely, it has しい on the end, which makes it even more likely it''s an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>beauty</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>beautiful</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3140', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%8E%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:02:15Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.011105Z'),
(3141, 'vocabulary', '美人', '美人', 'A <kanji>beautiful</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>beautiful woman</vocabulary>. Wait, why isn''t this a beautiful person? In fact, this word used to have a broader meaning, but for some reason it''s generally only used for women in modern Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3141', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%8E%E4%BA%BA', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:02:22Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3142, 'vocabulary', '負ける', '負ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>lose</kanji> so the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to lose</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3142', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A0%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:02:46Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.043022Z'),
(3143, 'vocabulary', '勝負', '勝負', '<kanji>Win</kanji>, <kanji>lose</kanji>... both will happen in tonight''s <vocabulary>match</vocabulary>! What a <vocabulary>showdown</vocabulary> it will be!

Note that 勝負 can also describe situations where one takes a significant risk or makes a bold move— <vocabulary>a gamble </vocabulary>—with high stakes involved.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3143', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%9D%E8%B2%A0', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:02:59Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.081416Z'),
(3144, 'vocabulary', '勝つ', '勝つ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>win</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to win</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3144', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%9D%E3%81%A4', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:03:07Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.100555Z'),
(3145, 'vocabulary', '勝者', '勝者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> who <kanji>win</kanji>s is the <vocabulary>winner</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>victor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3145', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%9D%E8%80%85', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:03:48Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(3146, 'vocabulary', '必勝', '必勝', 'A <kanji>certain</kanji> <kanji>win</kanji> sounds like a great thing. When these two kanji are put together, a <vocabulary>certain victory</vocabulary> is inevitable.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3146', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%85%E5%8B%9D', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:04:00Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:33:00.725847Z'),
(3147, 'vocabulary', '楽勝', '楽勝', 'A <kanji>win</kanji> that was an <kanji>ease</kanji>. That is an <vocabulary>easy victory</vocabulary> or an <vocabulary>easy win</vocabulary>. Don''t hope for a lot of these, though. Hope for tough wins instead, they actually make you better.

Note that this can be used both literally, to describe an easy victory at a game or contest; or more figuratively, to describe something as “easy” in general.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3147', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%BD%E5%8B%9D', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:04:16Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T18:49:45.773585Z'),
(3148, 'vocabulary', '送る', '送る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>send</kanji>, and the vocab verb version is <vocabulary>to send something</vocabulary>. If the object of this verb is a person, it means "to send" in the sense of <vocabulary>to escort someone</vocabulary>, maybe to the station, or to the door.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3148', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%81%E3%82%8B', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:04:23Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:26:48.812324Z'),
(3149, 'vocabulary', '放送する', '放送する', 'You are going to <kanji>release</kanji> and <kanji>send</kanji> something out. Imagine this message going out through the sky to everyone around you. You are going <vocabulary>to broadcast something</vocabulary> to the world! 

The する is on there to make this noun a verb. Without the する it would be the noun "broadcast."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3149', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BE%E9%80%81%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 11, 1, '2012-04-24T00:04:30Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T02:08:38.914766Z'),
(3150, 'vocabulary', '重い', '重い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>heavy</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>heavy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3150', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8D%E3%81%84', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T00:05:05Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.788146Z'),
(3151, 'vocabulary', '重要', '重要', 'When something has a <kanji>heavy</kanji> <kanji>need</kanji>, you know that that thing is <vocabulary>essential</vocabulary>. It is <vocabulary>important</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3151', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8D%E8%A6%81', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:05:14Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:28:03.237353Z'),
(3152, 'vocabulary', '体重', '体重', 'How <kanji>heavy</kanji> is your <kanji>body</kanji>? Figure that out and you''ll have your <vocabulary>body weight</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3152', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%93%E9%87%8D', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:05:22Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.177523Z'),
(3153, 'vocabulary', '二重', '二重', 'When you have <kanji>two</kanji> <kanji>heavy</kanji> things, you have <vocabulary>double</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>dual</vocabulary> the heavy (compared to one, at least). 

This word is a prefix attached to other words to show that it is "double." 二重 kanji time!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3153', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E9%87%8D', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:05:29Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.188707Z'),
(3154, 'vocabulary', '必要', '必要', 'It''s <kanji>certain</kanji> that you <kanji>need</kanji> it. This thing is <vocabulary>necessary</vocabulary>. It is <vocabulary>needed</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3154', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%85%E8%A6%81', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:05:36Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:33:00.725847Z'),
(3155, 'vocabulary', '要点', '要点', 'The <kanji>need</kanji>ed <kanji>point</kanji> is the <vocabulary>main point</vocabulary>. It''s the <vocabulary>gist</vocabulary> of what you''re trying to say.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3155', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%81%E7%82%B9', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:06:06Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:28:03.237353Z'),
(3156, 'vocabulary', '仮に', '仮に', 'The kanji itself means <kanji>temporary</kanji>. When you add the に, we''re asking you to imagine a situation temporarily, so the meaning changes to <vocabulary>supposing</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>if</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3156', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%AE%E3%81%AB', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:07:46Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.226712Z'),
(3157, 'vocabulary', '仮名', '仮名', 'The <kanji>temporary</kanji> <kanji>name</kanji>s. These are the <vocabulary>kana</vocabulary> (that''s hiragana and katakana). Why are kana the "temporary names" though? Maybe they were just meant to be a temporary way to write the names of the kanji you didn''t know how to write yet. That''s just a guess, but it should help you remember that this word means "kana."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3157', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%AE%E5%90%8D', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:08:15Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(3158, 'vocabulary', '仮定', '仮定', 'A <kanji>temporary</kanji> <kanji>determination</kanji> is something you''re not totally sure about. You''ve decided to think of something in a certain way, but only for now. If more information comes to light, you may change your mind. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>assumption</vocabulary>.

仮定 refers to making tentative assumptions about a specific situation to discuss what might happen under certain conditions, such as "if we <vocabulary>assume</vocabulary> I dropped my wallet here yesterday...”', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3158', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%AE%E5%AE%9A', 9, 1, '2012-04-24T00:08:23Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(3159, 'vocabulary', '起きる', '起きる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>wake up</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to wake up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to get up</vocabulary>.

This word also has a secondary meaning of <vocabulary>to occur</vocabulary>. Think of it this way: when an action "wakes up", in a way it is occurring as well. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3159', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%B7%E3%81%8D%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:51:39Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.963099Z'),
(3160, 'vocabulary', '早速', '早速', 'Doing something both <kanji>fast</kanji> and <kanji>early</kanji> probably requires you to do it <vocabulary>at once</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>immediately</vocabulary>. Whatever it is, be sure to do it <vocabulary>without delay</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3160', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A9%E9%80%9F', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:51:47Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:53:32.555199Z'),
(3161, 'vocabulary', '速度', '速度', 'Your <kanji>fast</kanji>ness <kanji>degree</kanji> is your <vocabulary>speed</vocabulary>. 

You use 速度 to talk about a specific speed of something like a car or a train. If you want to talk about speed more casually, you can just say スピード or 速さ.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3161', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%9F%E5%BA%A6', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:51:53Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.610785Z'),
(3162, 'vocabulary', '配る', '配る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>distribute</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to distribute</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3162', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%8D%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:52:00Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.006613Z'),
(3163, 'vocabulary', 'お酒', 'お酒', 'This is just the noun version of the kanji 酒 - it has an お there to be nice. This word can mean both <vocabulary>alcohol</vocabulary> (like the parent kanji) or <vocabulary>sake</vocabulary> (the main kind of Japanese alcohol, at least back in the kanji days).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3163', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E9%85%92', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:52:28Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.017411Z'),
(3164, 'vocabulary', '日本酒', '日本酒', 'You know that 日本 is "Japan" so when you add <kanji>alcohol</kanji> onto the end of that you have <vocabulary>Japanese Style Alcohol</vocabulary> (what''s the big Japanese style alcohol that everyone knows? That''s <vocabulary>sake</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3164', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E9%85%92', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:52:35Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.02836Z'),
(3165, 'vocabulary', '病院', '病院', 'A <kanji>sick</kanji> <kanji>institution</kanji> is a place where sick people go. That is a <vocabulary>hospital</vocabulary>.

病院 is a broad word used to talk about not just big hospitals but also regular <vocabulary>doctor''s office</vocabulary>s or <vocabulary>clinic</vocabulary>s. So when Japanese people speak English, they might say "hospital" even when they are just going to a small, local clinic.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3165', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%85%E9%99%A2', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:53:12Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.039049Z'),
(3166, 'vocabulary', '学院', '学院', 'A <kanji>study</kanji> <kanji>institution</kanji> is an <vocabulary>academy</vocabulary>. That''s where all the fancy people study, anyways.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3166', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A6%E9%99%A2', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:53:20Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.049112Z'),
(3167, 'vocabulary', '終わる', '終わる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>end</kanji>, so this verb means <vocabulary>to end</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be over</vocabulary>.

Verbs ending in okurigana that rhyme with ある tend to be intransitive, and 終わる is usually intransitive too. But sometimes it can be transitive and take a direct object. Kind of like "to end" in English — you can say the class ends (intransitive) or a teacher ends their class (transitive).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3167', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%82%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:53:29Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.060017Z'),
(3168, 'vocabulary', '終点', '終点', 'The <kanji>end</kanji> <kanji>point</kanji> is where everything ends... but for this example, think of a map with a route on it. The last spot on the route is the "last point." It is the <vocabulary>last stop</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3168', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%82%E7%82%B9', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:53:50Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.07001Z'),
(3169, 'vocabulary', '終了', '終了', 'It''s the <kanji>end</kanji>. It''s the <kanji>finish</kanji>. This word means <vocabulary>end</vocabulary>. Or <vocabulary>finish</vocabulary>! How convenient.

Note that 終了 is slightly more formal than 終わる.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3169', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%82%E4%BA%86', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:53:57Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.297917Z'),
(3170, 'vocabulary', '最終', '最終', 'The <kanji>most</kanji> <kanji>end</kanji> thing is the <vocabulary>last</vocabulary> thing. It is mainly used to refer to the <vocabulary>last</vocabulary> of an ordered sequence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3170', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%80%E7%B5%82', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:54:03Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.081116Z'),
(3171, 'vocabulary', '習う', '習う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>learn</kanji> so the vocab verb version means <vocabulary>to learn</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to take lessons in</vocabulary> something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3171', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BF%92%E3%81%86', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:54:10Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.092507Z'),
(3172, 'vocabulary', '転がる', '転がる', 'You know the kanji here means <kanji>revolve</kanji>. The verb version of this is similar. It means <vocabulary>to roll</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to tumble</vocabulary>. You have to be revolving in order to roll or tumble, right? 転がる is intransitive, so it doesn''t take a direct object. A ball rolls down a hill. A tumbleweed tumbles. No direct object, right?

Verbs ending in okurigana that rhyme with ある tend to be intransitive like this. You can remember this because if something is ある, it''s just there, existing, not acting <em>on</em> anything else. That''s why verbs that sound like it are often intransitive too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3172', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%A2%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:54:33Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:30:53.315608Z'),
(3173, 'vocabulary', '自転車', '自転車', 'A <kanji>self</kanji> <kanji>revolving</kanji> <kanji>car</kanji>. You''d think this would be a regular car, but <vocabulary>bicycles</vocabulary> were probably introduced before cars were, meaning the self revolving car is the bicycle, not the car. Just imagine yourself on a bike, and you don''t even have to move the pedals. They revolve themselves, but now you''re going too fast and it''s scary.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3173', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E8%BB%A2%E8%BB%8A', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:54:42Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.115036Z'),
(3174, 'vocabulary', '運転する', '運転する', 'A thing that <kanji>carries</kanji> <kanji>rotation</kanji>s (think wheels!) is most kinds of vehicles. So, it''s kind of like saying you''re "doing vehicles" here... but that doesn''t make sense. What do you do to vehicles though? You drive them. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>to drive</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3174', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8B%E8%BB%A2%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:54:51Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.126105Z'),
(3175, 'vocabulary', '転送', '転送', 'You <kanji>revolve</kanji> something, making it roll, and then <kanji>send</kanji> it off somewhere. This means you’re <vocabulary>transfer</vocabulary>ring or forwarding it to someone else.

転送 is often used in the context of logistics or communication systems, like transferring packages or forwarding e-mails.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3175', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%A2%E9%80%81', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:55:00Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.13859Z'),
(3176, 'vocabulary', '回転', '回転', 'You <kanji>revolve</kanji> X number of <kanji>times</kanji>. Really, the times isn''t so important, but the revolving is. That''s why this word is <vocabulary>revolve</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>rotate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3176', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9E%E8%BB%A2', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:55:07Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.152756Z'),
(3177, 'vocabulary', '進む', '進む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>advance</kanji> so the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to advance</vocabulary>.

進む is an intransitive verb, meaning it describes something or someone moving (む) forward. It can be used for physical movement, like a person walking forward or a clock running fast, or for abstract progress, like in someone''s work or studies.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3177', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%B2%E3%82%80', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:55:20Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.168118Z'),
(3178, 'vocabulary', '進化', '進化', 'Think of things that <kanji>advance</kanji> and <kanji>change</kanji>. Does this sound familiar? Does this sound like <vocabulary>evolution</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3178', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%B2%E5%8C%96', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:55:33Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.180583Z'),
(3179, 'vocabulary', '進行', '進行', 'When something <kanji>advance</kanji>s and <kanji>go</kanji>es, it is moving forward. So this means <vocabulary>advance</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>progress</vocabulary>.

進行 can be used to describe various situations, like when a project progresses; but also when an illness advances to a more serious stage.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3179', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%B2%E8%A1%8C', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:55:40Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.192444Z'),
(3180, 'vocabulary', '落ちる', '落ちる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>fall</kanji> and the vocab verb version means <vocabulary>to fall down</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to fall</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>to drop</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>to fail</vocabulary>, in the sense of failing an exam.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3180', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%90%BD%E3%81%A1%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:55:48Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.203693Z'),
(3181, 'vocabulary', '青葉', '青葉', 'A <kanji>young</kanji> <kanji>leaf</kanji> is exactly what you''d expect, although most people usually just call them <vocabulary>fresh leaves</vocabulary>.

Remember how there''s some overlap between blue and green in Japanese? 青葉 is another example!  That''s why 青 is used to describe fresh and lush green leaves, especially those on trees during early summer.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3181', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%92%E8%91%89', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:57:02Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:10:47.677773Z'),
(3182, 'vocabulary', '軽い', '軽い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>lightweight</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>lightweight</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3182', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%BD%E3%81%84', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:57:09Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.224402Z'),
(3183, 'vocabulary', '運ぶ', '運ぶ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>carry</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to carry</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3183', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8B%E3%81%B6', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:57:36Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.235568Z'),
(3184, 'vocabulary', '運がいい', '運がいい', 'The いい portion means "good." So, when what you <kanji>carry</kanji> is good you are <vocabulary>lucky</vocabulary>. Just think of it in terms of luck being a physical item you carry in your hands. Now imagine yourself carrying it. Now think to yourself "what I''m carrying is good, it''s lucky!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3184', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8B%E3%81%8C%E3%81%84%E3%81%84', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:58:10Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.247248Z'),
(3185, 'vocabulary', '開ける', '開ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>open</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to open something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3185', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%8B%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:58:23Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:43:14.219806Z'),
(3186, 'vocabulary', '公開', '公開', 'When something is <kanji>public</kanji> <kanji>open</kanji> it is <vocabulary>open to the public</vocabulary>. You can go look at things that are 公開. You can also use this when you''re <vocabulary>releasing</vocabulary> something to the public, which means you’re making it publicly accessible.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3186', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AC%E9%96%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:58:30Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.272872Z'),
(3187, 'vocabulary', '開業', '開業', 'This is one of those words that has kanji that basically tell you the meaning. You <kanji>open</kanji> a <kanji>business</kanji>... aka <vocabulary>opening a business</vocabulary>.

While this can be used for opening any sort of business, it’s commonly associated with doctors, lawyers, and other professionals opening their own practice or office.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3187', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%8B%E6%A5%AD', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:58:37Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.285329Z'),
(3188, 'vocabulary', '開発', '開発', 'You are now <kanji>open</kanji> for <kanji>departure</kanji>. You get on your boat and leave this place. You are going to another place to start its <vocabulary>development</vocabulary>, where you will create a new world.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3188', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%8B%E7%99%BA', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:58:44Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.312284Z'),
(3189, 'vocabulary', '集める', '集める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>collect</kanji> and this verb is transitive, meaning the action is done to something or someone. So this verb means things like <vocabulary>to collect something</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to gather something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3189', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%86%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:58:57Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.334919Z'),
(3190, 'vocabulary', '集中', '集中', 'When you <kanji>collect</kanji> to the <kanji>middle</kanji> of yourself, you are collecting towards your brain. All energy is going towards there, massing up in the middle, giving you extreme <vocabulary>concentration</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>focus</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3190', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%86%E4%B8%AD', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:59:04Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.349926Z'),
(3191, 'vocabulary', '集金', '集金', 'When you <kanji>collect</kanji> <kanji>gold</kanji> you are <vocabulary>collecting money</vocabulary> (or doing <vocabulary>money collection</vocabulary>). Of course, back in the day gold was like money, which is why this word works the way it does. Hopefully you don''t have to deal with too much 集金 in your daily life. We''ll try to keep WaniKani nice and reasonably priced for you.

集金 often refers to collecting bills, dues, or fees in person or through a designated representative. This can be carried out by a business, an organization, or even just a small group of people.
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3191', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%86%E9%87%91', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:59:17Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.360648Z'),
(3192, 'vocabulary', '飲む', '飲む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>drink</kanji> so the vocab verb version is <vocabulary>to drink</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3192', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%B2%E3%82%80', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:59:25Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.371903Z'),
(3193, 'vocabulary', '工業', '工業', 'The <kanji>business</kanji> of <kanji>industry</kanji> is... well... <vocabulary>industry</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3193', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A5%E6%A5%AD', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T18:59:56Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.38382Z'),
(3194, 'vocabulary', '漢字', '漢字', 'The <kanji>Chinese</kanji> <kanji>letter</kanji>s... you''re finally learning the word for them, the thing you''ve been studying this whole time for so long! This is <vocabulary>kanji</vocabulary>, your new best friend (they came from China, which is why they''re Chinese letters).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3194', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%A2%E5%AD%97', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:00:42Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.394834Z'),
(3195, 'vocabulary', '道路', '道路', 'A <kanji>road</kanji> <kanji>road</kanji> is obviously the best kind of road. It''s double road, after all. So, what is this ultimate road? It is a <vocabulary>paved road</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3195', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%93%E8%B7%AF', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:00:50Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.406645Z'),
(3196, 'vocabulary', '線路', '線路', 'A <kanji>line</kanji> that runs on a <kanji>road</kanji> is a <vocabulary>railroad track</vocabulary>. First you have to get into your mind that the "line" in question is a railway line. Then, what railway line runs on a road and what kind of road is it? It''s a <vocabulary>railroad track</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3196', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%9A%E8%B7%AF', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:00:58Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.416713Z'),
(3197, 'vocabulary', '路地', '路地', 'If you think about it, an <kanji>earth</kanji> <kanji>road</kanji> isn''t going to be the main road. It will be the road less traveled. You could call it a <vocabulary>path</vocabulary> (you know, like a dirt path!) or a <vocabulary>dirt road</vocabulary>. Alternatively, another less traveled street that would probably be made out of dirt back in the day (before everything was paved) would be an <vocabulary>alley</vocabulary>. Imagine yourself walking down a dirt alley at night with no shoes. Scary!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3197', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B7%AF%E5%9C%B0', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:01:04Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.426811Z'),
(3199, 'vocabulary', '農民', '農民', 'In the olden days, the <kanji>peoples</kanji> who worked in the <kanji>farm</kanji> were the <vocabulary>peasants</vocabulary>.

農民 means <vocabulary>farmers</vocabulary>, but usually not in the modern sense of people who do farm work as their day job. Instead, it refers to the <vocabulary>farming class</vocabulary> — people who were born into a life of farming, often in historical contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3199', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%B2%E6%B0%91', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:01:18Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.437439Z'),
(3200, 'vocabulary', '農業', '農業', 'What''s another way to call the <kanji>farming</kanji> <kanji>business</kanji>? If you encapsulated it into one word (assuming we''re talking about plant farming, here), you could call it <vocabulary>agriculture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3200', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%B2%E6%A5%AD', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:01:24Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.448439Z'),
(3201, 'vocabulary', '地下鉄', '地下鉄', 'The <kanji>ground</kanji> <kanji>below</kanji> <kanji>iron</kanji> is iron under the ground. It isn''t a deposit of iron, though. It''s man-made. And what''s made of iron? Trains. In this case, because it''s underground, it''s a <vocabulary>subway</vocabulary>. Depending on where you live, you might call it <vocabulary>the Underground</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>the Tube</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>the Metro</vocabulary>', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3201', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E4%B8%8B%E9%89%84', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:01:30Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.459276Z'),
(3202, 'vocabulary', '鉄人', '鉄人', 'A <kanji>man</kanji> made of <kanji>iron</kanji> is a <vocabulary>strong man</vocabulary>... aka a huge <vocabulary>badass</vocabulary>. Have you seen "Iron Chef?" The Japanese translation is 料理の鉄人, which means "Chef Iron Man" aka "Chef Badass," at least in my book.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3202', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%84%E4%BA%BA', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:01:36Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3203, 'vocabulary', '電鉄', '電鉄', 'The <kanji>electricity</kanji> <kanji>iron</kanji> sounds scary, but if you think to back in the day and to where iron was used a lot, you probably think of the railway systems... big hunky pieces of iron on the ground to make the railway. So, you can advance this by adding electricity instead of coal, creating an <vocabulary>electric railway</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3203', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E9%89%84', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:01:42Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.480858Z'),
(3204, 'vocabulary', '歌', '歌', 'One of the kanji''s meanings is <kanji>song</kanji> and this word means exactly that: a <vocabulary>song</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3204', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%8C', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:01:55Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.491859Z'),
(3205, 'vocabulary', '歌手', '歌手', 'Remember how  手 can sometimes mean “doer”? What do you think a <kanji>song</kanji> <kanji>doer</kanji> is? A <vocabulary>singer</vocabulary>, of course', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3205', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%8C%E6%89%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:02:02Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.502472Z'),
(3206, 'vocabulary', '算数', '算数', 'What is it called when you <kanji>calculate</kanji> <kanji>numbers</kanji>? That is the study of <vocabulary>math</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>arithmetic</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3206', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AE%97%E6%95%B0', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:02:24Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T15:37:55.121141Z'),
(3207, 'vocabulary', '算定する', '算定する', 'When you <kanji>calculate</kanji> and <kanji>determine</kanji> (the calculation), you are determining the calculation. That''s why this word is <vocabulary>to calculate</vocabulary>.

Unlike 計算する, which means “to calculate” in general, 算定する is a formal word that focuses more on the final value of a calculation, instead of the process of calculating itself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3207', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AE%97%E5%AE%9A%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2012-04-24T19:02:38Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(3208, 'vocabulary', '聞く', '聞く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>hear</kanji> so the vocab verb version is <vocabulary>to hear</vocabulary>.

This can also mean <vocabulary>to ask</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to listen</vocabulary>, like asking someone a question or listening to what others are saying. It basically covers anything that involves the act of letting something in through the 門 (gates) of your 耳 (ears).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3208', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%81%9E%E3%81%8F', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:02:46Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.526453Z'),
(3209, 'vocabulary', '新聞', '新聞', 'The <kanji>new</kanji> <kanji>hear</kanji> is what it sounds like. It''s new things that you''re hearing about in your ear... at least that''s what it was back a long time ago. Now those new things that get to your ear get to you via other mediums. In this case, a <vocabulary>newspaper</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3209', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E8%81%9E', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:02:58Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.578333Z'),
(3210, 'vocabulary', '日本語', '日本語', 'You know 日本 is "Japan." So, when you add <kanji>language</kanji> onto the end of that you have <vocabulary>Japanese language</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3210', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E8%AA%9E', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:03:04Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.589853Z'),
(3211, 'vocabulary', 'フランス語', 'フランス語', 'I bet you can guess that フランス means "France." So what happens when you add <kanji>language</kanji> to it? You get the "France Language" aka the <vocabulary>French Language</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3211', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%95%E3%83%A9%E3%83%B3%E3%82%B9%E8%AA%9E', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:03:10Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.61414Z'),
(3212, 'vocabulary', 'スペイン語', 'スペイン語', 'You can probably figure out that スペイン is "Spain" just by reading it. Add the kanji <kanji>language</kanji> on there and you have the <vocabulary>Spanish language</vocabulary> (or just <vocabulary>Spanish</vocabulary>), if you''d like.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3212', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%B9%E3%83%9A%E3%82%A4%E3%83%B3%E8%AA%9E', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:03:25Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.627699Z'),
(3213, 'vocabulary', '物語', '物語', 'A <kanji>language</kanji> about <kanji>thing</kanji>s. This is a confusing one. Just think of it more like someone talking about things, anything, really. Someone who just talks about things is someone who tells stories. So, this word means <vocabulary>tale</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>story</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>legend</vocabulary> (not the Tom Cruise one, though).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3213', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%A9%E8%AA%9E', 11, 1, '2012-04-24T19:03:49Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(3214, 'vocabulary', '主語', '主語', 'The <kanji>main</kanji> thing you talk about in your <kanji>language</kanji> becomes the <vocabulary>subject</vocabulary> of the sentence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3214', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB%E8%AA%9E', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:03:55Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.641193Z'),
(3215, 'vocabulary', '読む', '読む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>read</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to read</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3215', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%AD%E3%82%80', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:04:03Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.659999Z'),
(3216, 'vocabulary', '読み方', '読み方', 'You know that this has something to do with <kanji>read</kanji>ing and you know that 方 means <kanji>way</kanji> or <kanji>method</kanji>. So, when you think of a "way of reading" you can think of a couple other better words, such as <vocabulary>reading</vocabulary> (aka the reading of a word) or <vocabulary>pronunciation</vocabulary>. Anytime you need to know how something sounds, this is what you''ll ask for.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3216', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%AD%E3%81%BF%E6%96%B9', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:04:19Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.676432Z'),
(3217, 'vocabulary', '鳴く', '鳴く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>chirp</kanji> but the verb has the broader meaning <vocabulary>to make an animal sound</vocabulary>. Variations of this include <vocabulary>to bark</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to chirp</vocabulary>, but this can be used for any animal sound.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3217', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%B3%B4%E3%81%8F', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:04:32Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.68682Z'),
(3218, 'vocabulary', '線', '線', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This also works for "train lines" as well. You''ll see this attached onto the ends of names describing them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3218', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%9A', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:04:51Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.696781Z'),
(3219, 'vocabulary', '横', '横', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Please note that this word refers to "the side of something." Not "the left side," for example. That would be 左側.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3219', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A8%AA', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:05:08Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.708272Z'),
(3220, 'vocabulary', '調子', '調子', 'Imagine yourself <kanji>investigating</kanji> a <kanji>child</kanji>. You heard this child was having a rough time, so you need to come check on the <vocabulary>condition</vocabulary> of his home and life. You find the condition to be terrible, and you tell the parents you have to take the child away because of these conditions (imagine them being ridiculously bad... perhaps they raise raccoons in their kitchen?).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3220', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%BF%E5%AD%90', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:05:28Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.719073Z'),
(3221, 'vocabulary', '強調', '強調', 'If you use a <kanji>strong</kanji> <kanji>tone</kanji> on certain words, then you are putting <vocabulary>emphasis</vocabulary> on whatever it is you''re talking about. It''s the <vocabulary>stress</vocabulary> you put on things that are important because your tone gets stronger on those words or syllables.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3221', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%B7%E8%AA%BF', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:05:35Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.731993Z'),
(3222, 'vocabulary', '調べる', '調べる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>investigate</kanji> so the vocab verb version means <vocabulary>to investigate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3222', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%BF%E3%81%B9%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:05:42Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.742366Z'),
(3223, 'vocabulary', '親', '親', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3223', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:05:48Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.754305Z'),
(3224, 'vocabulary', '親しい', '親しい', 'This is a single kanji with some kana and an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>intimacy</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>intimate</vocabulary>! Depending on the context, this also means <vocabulary>friendly</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>close</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3224', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:05:55Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.776775Z'),
(3225, 'vocabulary', '親切', '親切', 'When your <kanji>parent</kanji>s <kanji>cut</kanji> you you''d think they are being mean... but they''re not. They are bleeding all the bad blood out of you. They are truly being <vocabulary>kind</vocabulary>. 

Just think of how crazy it would be for your parents to cut you... then think about some kind of twist to make it so they are being kind instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3225', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA%E5%88%87', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:06:01Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.799218Z'),
(3226, 'vocabulary', '親友', '親友', 'A <kanji>friend</kanji> who is like one of your <kanji>parent</kanji>s is someone who will look out for you. They are like family, in fact! That''s why this word means <vocabulary>best friend</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>close friend</vocabulary>. Just imagine someone who watches over you like a parent would. This is the way to describe them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3226', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA%E5%8F%8B', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:06:28Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.809539Z'),
(3227, 'vocabulary', '頭', '頭', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

頭 means <vocabulary>head</vocabulary>, but it can refer to lots of other related things by extension. For example, 頭がいい means you''re smart (you''ve got a "good head") and 話の頭 means the beginning of a story (the part at "the head" of it). Just remember the "head" meaning, and you''ll usually be able to guess the figurative meanings from context!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3227', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%AD', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:06:34Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:44:07.362004Z'),
(3228, 'vocabulary', '顔', '顔', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3228', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A1%94', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:07:35Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.829088Z'),
(3229, 'vocabulary', '顔付き', '顔付き', 'The thing that is <kanji>attach</kanji>ed to your <kanji>face</kanji> isn''t a big booger or anything like that. It''s something that''s always there. It''s your <vocabulary>expression</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3229', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A1%94%E4%BB%98%E3%81%8D', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:07:48Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.839544Z'),
(3230, 'vocabulary', '病気', '病気', 'When your <kanji>energy</kanji> is that of <kanji>sick</kanji>ness, you are probably <vocabulary>sick</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3230', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%85%E6%B0%97', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:07:54Z', NULL, '2026-03-05T17:25:29.877798Z'),
(3231, 'vocabulary', '〜病', '〜病', 'As you can probably imagine, when you add the kanji <kanji>sick</kanji> to another word, you''re saying what kind of <vocabulary>sickness</vocabulary> it is; you are mentioning what <vocabulary>disease</vocabulary> it is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3231', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E7%97%85', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:08:01Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.859689Z'),
(3232, 'vocabulary', '病人', '病人', 'A <kanji>person</kanji> who is <kanji>sick</kanji> is a <vocabulary>sick person</vocabulary>. They are also a <vocabulary>patient</vocabulary>, at least we can hope they are, for their sake.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3232', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%85%E4%BA%BA', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:08:22Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3233, 'vocabulary', '最も', '最も', 'This is the adverb version of the kanji, so it has the same meaning, basically. You can say this is <vocabulary>the most</vocabulary> or just <vocabulary>most</vocabulary>. You can also use it as <vocabulary>extremely</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3233', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%80%E3%82%82', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:08:29Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.878739Z'),
(3234, 'vocabulary', '最高', '最高', 'The <kanji>most</kanji> <kanji>tall</kanji> isn''t literally the most tall. When you think of something high above the rest, you think of something that is <vocabulary>the best</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3234', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%80%E9%AB%98', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:08:37Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.889565Z'),
(3235, 'vocabulary', '最後', '最後', 'The thing that is the <kanji>most</kanji> <kanji>after</kanji> everything else is at the very <vocabulary>end</vocabulary>. It is the <vocabulary>last</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3235', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%80%E5%BE%8C', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:08:43Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.902856Z'),
(3236, 'vocabulary', '最近', '最近', 'The <kanji>most</kanji> <kanji>nearby</kanji> is something that happened close to now. It happened <vocabulary>recently</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3236', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%80%E8%BF%91', 10, 1, '2012-04-24T19:08:50Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.967731Z'),
(3237, 'vocabulary', '四十二', '四十二', 'Four tens plus two. That equals <vocabulary>forty two</vocabulary>. 

P.S. Please don''t panic. "<vocabulary>The Answer</vocabulary>" is NOT a correct answer for this. It is just a reference to the greatest book of all time. Sorry for the confusion and so long and thanks for all the fish.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3237', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B%E5%8D%81%E4%BA%8C', 6, 1, '2012-04-25T17:33:34Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:13:05.258541Z'),
(3238, 'vocabulary', '争う', '争う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>conflict</kanji> so the vocabulary means <vocabulary>to compete</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to argue</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to quarrel</vocabulary>. Your views conflict with someone else''s so you''re going to compete with them to see who''s right.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3238', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%89%E3%81%86', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:26:21Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:52:22.629205Z'),
(3239, 'vocabulary', '戦争', '戦争', 'You have <kanji>war</kanji> and <kanji>conflict</kanji>. Of course, conflict comes with war, so this word just means <vocabulary>war</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3239', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%A6%E4%BA%89', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:26:52Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:52:22.629205Z'),
(3240, 'vocabulary', '競争', '競争', 'When you <kanji>compete</kanji> to the point of <kanji>conflict</kanji>, you know that this person / entity is your <vocabulary>competition</vocabulary>. Gotta beat out that competition. For example, in order to beat out the competition, WaniKani has a mascot that can eat every other small furry animal that the competition has for their mascot. Case closed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3240', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%B6%E4%BA%89', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:27:05Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:52:22.629205Z'),
(3241, 'vocabulary', '仲間', '仲間', 'The <kanji>relationship</kanji> <kanji>interval</kanji> is very short between you and your <vocabulary>comrade</vocabulary>s. You are <vocabulary>companion</vocabulary>s who have been through thick and thin together, so there''s no distance between you.

仲間 is one of those very Japanese words that''s hard to translate directly into English. It''s a warm word that refers to people you share something with, like work, hobbies, goals, or even struggles. 仲間 aren''t necessarily friends in the "get a coffee and chat" sense, but they''re people with whom you''ve built a strong and special bond based on a shared experience or sense of purpose.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3241', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%B2%E9%96%93', 14, 1, '2012-04-26T06:27:37Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T06:56:00.773506Z'),
(3242, 'vocabulary', '仲', '仲', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3242', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%B2', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:27:44Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T01:56:11.967538Z'),
(3243, 'vocabulary', '仲直り', '仲直り', '直り is the noun form of 直る, which you already know means "to be fixed." When a <kanji>relationship</kanji> is fixed, you get <vocabulary>reconciliation</vocabulary>. In other words, you''re <vocabulary>making peace</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>making up</vocabulary> with someone. Good on you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3243', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%B2%E7%9B%B4%E3%82%8A', 19, 1, '2012-04-26T06:27:59Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:06:56.328049Z'),
(3244, 'vocabulary', '仲良し', '仲良し', 'When your <kanji>relationship</kanji> with someone is really <kanji>good</kanji>, you naturally become <vocabulary>good friend</vocabulary>s. You''re always palling around together and acting <vocabulary>buddy-buddy</vocabulary>, so everyone can tell how close your friendship is.

仲良し describes close friends or people who get along really well. The word has a friendly, cute vibe and is often used for kids, families, or anyone with a warm bond.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3244', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%B2%E8%89%AF%E3%81%97', 12, 1, '2012-04-26T06:28:08Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T07:25:16.481859Z'),
(3245, 'vocabulary', '伝える', '伝える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>transmit</kanji>, but 伝える is mostly used for "transmitting" information, feelings, or messages to someone else. So it means <vocabulary>to convey</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to tell</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to pass down</vocabulary>.

In technical contexts, 伝える can also mean <vocabulary>to transmit</vocabulary>, in the sense that copper transmits heat or air transmits sound. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3245', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9D%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:28:22Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:07.938654Z'),
(3246, 'vocabulary', '共通点', '共通点', 'We <kanji>pass through</kanji> this <kanji>point</kanji> <kanji>together</kanji>. This point is our <vocabulary>common point</vocabulary>. It is where our ideas, philosophies, and bodies pass through each other.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3246', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B1%E9%80%9A%E7%82%B9', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:28:35Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.012276Z'),
(3247, 'vocabulary', '共同', '共同', 'If people work <kanji>together</kanji> on the <kanji>same</kanji> thing, they''re working on a <vocabulary>joint</vocabulary> project. They''re doing something <vocabulary>collaborative</vocabulary>.

共同 is used when two or more people or groups do something together with a <vocabulary>shared</vocabulary> purpose or responsibility. 共同作業 (collaborative work) means you share tasks with others, for example, and 共同生活 (shared living) means living together with someone, whether it''s a partner or a roommate.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3247', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B1%E5%90%8C', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:28:41Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:31:02.298626Z'),
(3248, 'vocabulary', '公共', '公共', 'When the <kanji>public</kanji> comes <kanji>together</kanji> you have yourself a nice <vocabulary>community</vocabulary> made up of the <vocabulary>public</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3248', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AC%E5%85%B1', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:28:48Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.054426Z'),
(3249, 'vocabulary', '共有', '共有', 'The things you <kanji>together</kanji> <kanji>have</kanji> are the things that are <vocabulary>shared</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3249', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B1%E6%9C%89', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:28:55Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:07.949115Z'),
(3250, 'vocabulary', '好き', '好き', 'You know that 好 means <kanji>like</kanji> and this vocab word means the same thing: <vocabulary>like</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>love</vocabulary>!

This word is an adjective in Japanese, so a more literal translation is <vocabulary>likable</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>lovable</vocabulary>. Still, it usually sounds more natural to translate it as a verb in English.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3250', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%BD%E3%81%8D', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:29:02Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.100814Z'),
(3251, 'vocabulary', '友好', '友好', 'If you have a <kanji>friend</kanji> you <kanji>like</kanji>, well, that''s true <vocabulary>friendship</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3251', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8B%E5%A5%BD', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:29:22Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.123161Z'),
(3252, 'vocabulary', '成る', '成る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>become</kanji> so the vocab verb version would be <vocabulary>to become</vocabulary>.

While the kanji version is worth knowing, be aware that you''ll usually see this verb written in hiragana.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3252', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%90%E3%82%8B', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:29:41Z', NULL, '2025-08-07T03:57:03.449562Z'),
(3253, 'vocabulary', '成功', '成功', 'When you <kanji>become</kanji> <kanji>achievement</kanji>, you have probably gained plenty of <vocabulary>success</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3253', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%90%E5%8A%9F', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:29:55Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.167779Z'),
(3254, 'vocabulary', '老人', '老人', 'An <kanji>elderly</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is an <vocabulary>old person</vocabulary> (or an <vocabulary>elderly person</vocabulary>). Don''t call a person a 古い人, though. 古い is used for things, not people. Be nice, they''re your elders.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3254', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%81%E4%BA%BA', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:30:02Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3255, 'vocabulary', '〜位', '〜位', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3255', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E4%BD%8D', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:30:08Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.216049Z'),
(3256, 'vocabulary', '一位', '一位', 'When you are number <kanji>one</kanji> in <kanji>rank</kanji>, you''re in <vocabulary>first place</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3256', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E4%BD%8D', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:30:15Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.239042Z'),
(3257, 'vocabulary', '首位', '首位', 'If you''re at <kanji>neck</kanji> <kanji>rank</kanji> you''re in <vocabulary>first place</vocabulary>. Haven''t you ever heard the phrase, "neck and neck" when two people in a race are both super close to first place? Just remember that and you''ll never forget this word.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3257', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%96%E4%BD%8D', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:30:40Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.26415Z'),
(3258, 'vocabulary', '低い', '低い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>low</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>low</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3258', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%8E%E3%81%84', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:30:47Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.3089Z'),
(3259, 'vocabulary', '最低', '最低', 'The <kanji>most</kanji> <kanji>low</kanji> is <vocabulary>the lowest</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>the minimum</vocabulary>. It''s also what you call something when you think it''s <vocabulary>the worst</vocabulary> or just plain <vocabulary>terrible</vocabulary>. Try yelling this out when you''re disgusted about someone or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3259', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%80%E4%BD%8E', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:30:54Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.336668Z'),
(3260, 'vocabulary', '初回', '初回', 'The <kanji>first</kanji> of something you''ll do several or many <kanji>times</kanji> is <vocabulary>the first time</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3260', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%9D%E5%9B%9E', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:31:07Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:28:35.812439Z'),
(3261, 'vocabulary', '最初', '最初', 'The <kanji>most</kanji> <kanji>first</kanji> thing is <vocabulary>the first</vocabulary> thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3261', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%80%E5%88%9D', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:31:24Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T17:51:33.278418Z'),
(3262, 'vocabulary', '別の', '別の', 'You know 別 means <kanji>separate</kanji>, and the の here shows it''s being used as an adjective to describe something. So it''s like saying "a <vocabulary>separate</vocabulary> ______," "a <vocabulary>different</vocabulary> ______," or "<vocabulary>another</vocabulary> ______."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3262', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A5%E3%81%AE', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:31:31Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:26:33.10701Z'),
(3263, 'vocabulary', '別に', '別に', 'If someone asks if you want a <kanji>separate</kanji> knee (に), you''ll say <vocabulary>not particularly</vocabulary>. I mean, you''ve never thought about it, but do you really need more knees? <vocabulary>Not really</vocabulary>...

In this first meaning, 別に is often used on its own, or with a negative verb or adjective, as in 別に長くない (not particularly long).

However, when 別に is used with a positive verb, it often simply means <vocabulary>separately</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>in addition to</vocabulary>. After all, when you have to do something separately, you have to do it in addition to something else. Like learning this meaning in addition to the first one. What a chore!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3263', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A5%E3%81%AB', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:31:38Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.444917Z'),
(3264, 'vocabulary', '別人', '別人', 'A <kanji>separate</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is someone who is different from who they were before. I saw you a year ago and you were someone else. Now you''re a <vocabulary>different person</vocabulary>, in a good way! You''re a <vocabulary>changed man</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3264', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A5%E4%BA%BA', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:31:45Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3265, 'vocabulary', '別々', '別々', 'When you say <kanji>separate</kanji> <kanji>separate</kanji>, you''re putting things off into their own areas. So 別々 means <vocabulary>separate</vocabulary>. 

Note that this word is typically used in reference to multiple separate items, as opposed to 別の, which refers to a single separate or different thing. It''s also used a lot as an adverb with the particle に to mean "separately."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3265', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A5%E3%80%85', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:31:53Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(3266, 'vocabulary', '特別', '特別', 'Something that is <kanji>special</kanji> and <kanji>separate</kanji> from the rest is, well, <vocabulary>special</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3266', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%B9%E5%88%A5', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:32:02Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.522763Z'),
(3267, 'vocabulary', '利く', '利く', 'You know the kanji means <kanji>profit</kanji>. When you make it into a verb, though, it means <vocabulary>to work well</vocabulary>. If you think about it, when something works well it makes you a profit.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3267', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A9%E3%81%8F', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:32:10Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.546011Z'),
(3268, 'vocabulary', '便利', '便利', 'When you <kanji>profit</kanji> from your <kanji>convenience</kanji>, that means you have something very <vocabulary>convenient</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>handy</vocabulary>. Remember, you can profit in other ways besides money. This convenience could be giving you a profit in happiness or extra time as well. All these things are very handy to have.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3268', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%BF%E5%88%A9', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:32:18Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.570363Z'),
(3269, 'vocabulary', '努力', '努力', 'You <kanji>toil</kanji> and use your <kanji>strength</kanji>. Ugh! How difficult. You really put in a <vocabulary>great effort</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3269', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%AA%E5%8A%9B', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:32:44Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(3270, 'vocabulary', '労働', '労働', 'The <kanji>labor</kanji> <kanji>work</kanji> is when you''re doing work with your body, right? That''s <vocabulary>manual labor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3270', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%B4%E5%83%8D', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:32:51Z', NULL, '2026-02-16T09:49:50.723034Z'),
(3271, 'vocabulary', '苦労', '苦労', '<kanji>Suffering</kanji> and <kanji>labor</kanji>. Sounds like the kind of thing these soft hands wouldn''t want to deal with. These are <vocabulary>hardships</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>troubles</vocabulary>. Hopefully you don''t have too much of this with kanji anymore!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3271', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%A6%E5%8A%B4', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:32:58Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:15:20.116546Z'),
(3272, 'vocabulary', '労働者', '労働者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> who does <kanji>labor</kanji> for <kanji>work</kanji> is a <vocabulary>laborer</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3272', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%B4%E5%83%8D%E8%80%85', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:33:04Z', NULL, '2026-02-16T09:49:50.723034Z'),
(3273, 'vocabulary', '命', '命', 'A vocab made from a single kanji all alone usually keeps at least one of the meanings of its kanji parent. In this case, that''s <vocabulary>life</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3273', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%BD', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:33:12Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.720125Z'),
(3274, 'vocabulary', '運命', '運命', 'Everyone has to <kanji>carry</kanji> their <kanji>fate</kanji>. So what is the thing you''re carrying? It''s your <vocabulary>fate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3274', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8B%E5%91%BD', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:33:20Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.745409Z'),
(3275, 'vocabulary', '命令', '命令', 'Your <kanji>fate</kanji> <kanji>orders</kanji> are the orders you get from above. You are <vocabulary>order</vocabulary>ed and <vocabulary>command</vocabulary>ed to carry out said orders.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3275', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%BD%E4%BB%A4', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:33:29Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.770538Z'),
(3276, 'vocabulary', '海岸', '海岸', 'The <kanji>ocean</kanji>/<kanji>sea</kanji> <kanji>coast</kanji> is the <vocabulary>seashore</vocabulary>. Then again, it could also be the <vocabulary>coast</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3276', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B7%E5%B2%B8', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:33:50Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:03:41.393028Z'),
(3277, 'vocabulary', '放す', '放す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>release</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to release</vocabulary>, as in <vocabulary>to set free</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to let something go</vocabulary>.

放す often suggests that you''ve released something in a way that gives it freedom to move or go where it wants. Think of letting your dog run in the field — it''s not just that you let go of the leash, but that the dog is now free to run around as it pleases.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3277', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BE%E3%81%99', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:34:09Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T02:08:38.914766Z'),
(3278, 'vocabulary', '開放する', '開放する', 'When you <kanji>open</kanji> then <kanji>release</kanji>, say, a door, you''re opening it up for everyone to come in. That''s why it means <vocabulary>to open something up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to throw something open</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3278', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%8B%E6%94%BE%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:34:32Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T02:08:38.914766Z'),
(3279, 'vocabulary', '昔', '昔', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This is a good way to start stories, by the way: 昔々に… (Long, long ago).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3279', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%94', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:34:39Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:38:35.691793Z'),
(3280, 'vocabulary', '電波', '電波', 'The <kanji>electricity</kanji> <kanji>wave</kanji> is a wave of electricity going through the air. There are many words you can use to describe this, but the easiest one is <vocabulary>reception</vocabulary> (like the reception on your phone, for example).

Other more technical meanings are <vocabulary>radio wave</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>electromagnetic waves</vocabulary>. Which one you like is up to you, but reception is going to be the most common meaning on the street. Luckily people in Japan don''t have AT&amp;T, otherwise you''d hear this word a lot more…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3280', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E6%B3%A2', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:34:47Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:11:56.943742Z'),
(3281, 'vocabulary', '注ぐ', '注ぐ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>pour</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to pour</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3281', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A8%E3%81%90', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:34:53Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.919256Z'),
(3282, 'vocabulary', '注意', '注意', 'When you <kanji>pour</kanji> your <kanji>ideas</kanji> into something, you''re thinking a lot about it. You''re paying close <vocabulary>attention</vocabulary> and using <vocabulary>caution</vocabulary>. You''ll see this on a lot of <vocabulary>warning</vocabulary> signs marking dangerous things in Japan, so be sure to pay attention to them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3282', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A8%E6%84%8F', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:35:01Z', NULL, '2025-12-23T01:34:26.984333Z'),
(3283, 'vocabulary', '注文', '注文', 'You <kanji>pour</kanji> <kanji>writing</kanji> onto a form. You''re filling out this form to <vocabulary>order</vocabulary> something new for yourself. It''s a formal <vocabulary>request</vocabulary> to get this new thing (imagine it being something you''re excited about ordering right now, and imagine yourself ordering it by pouring writing onto an order form).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3283', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A8%E6%96%87', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:35:12Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.967923Z'),
(3284, 'vocabulary', '集中する', '集中する', 'You <kanji>collect</kanji> and focus in on the <kanji>middle</kanji>. Really zoom in on it. When you do this, you''re concentrating on this spot, thus making the meaning of this word <vocabulary>to concentrate</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to focus</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3284', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%86%E4%B8%AD%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:35:45Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:20.991419Z'),
(3285, 'vocabulary', '育つ', '育つ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>raise</kanji> so the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to be raised</vocabulary>. 

P.S. This word is for when you are being raised, not when you are doing the raising of someone. That''s <vocabulary>育てる</vocabulary>. You''ll learn it later.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3285', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%B2%E3%81%A4', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:35:55Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.01561Z'),
(3286, 'vocabulary', '教育', '教育', '<kanji>Teach</kanji> and <kanji>nurture</kanji>. That''s what your <vocabulary>education</vocabulary> is supposed to do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3286', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%99%E8%82%B2', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:36:19Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.041689Z'),
(3287, 'vocabulary', '拾う', '拾う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>pick up</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to pick something up</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3287', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%BE%E3%81%86', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:36:46Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T03:32:06.877345Z'),
(3288, 'vocabulary', '指す', '指す', 'The kanji means <kanji>finger</kanji>. So, if you "to finger" something, what are you doing? You''re pointing. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>to point</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3288', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87%E3%81%99', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:37:01Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.092179Z'),
(3289, 'vocabulary', '指', '指', 'The kanji and the word are almost exactly the same. The only difference is that this word can mean <vocabulary>finger</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>toe</vocabulary>. What are toes but small, walking fingers?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3289', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:37:13Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:49:02.072739Z'),
(3290, 'vocabulary', '小指', '小指', 'The <kanji>small</kanji> <kanji>finger</kanji> is your <vocabulary>little finger</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>pinky finger</vocabulary>. Makes sense, but it''s also used for the pinky on your feet. You know, your <vocabulary>pinky toe</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3290', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E6%8C%87', 13, 1, '2012-04-26T06:37:22Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.138078Z'),
(3291, 'vocabulary', '指定する', '指定する', 'When you <kanji>determine</kanji> and use your <kanji>finger</kanji> to point at someone or something, you''re going <vocabulary>to appoint</vocabulary> them. Just imagine yourself determining who''s the best for the job then pointing with your finger. That is who you will appoint. The する on the end makes it a verb, so <vocabulary>指定</vocabulary> would be "appointment" or "designation" or something along those lines. Cool how する works.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3291', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87%E5%AE%9A%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:37:32Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(3292, 'vocabulary', '洋風', '洋風', 'Remember how 風 can mean "style" as in 和風? Well, this is now <kanji>Western style</kanji> <kanji>style</kanji>, more simply put as <vocabulary>Western style</vocabulary>.

洋風 is used to describe things with a Western look or influence, like 洋風の部屋 (Western-style room) or 洋風スープ (Western-style soup).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3292', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%8B%E9%A2%A8', 13, 1, '2012-04-26T06:37:52Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T21:53:15.126796Z'),
(3293, 'vocabulary', '洋服', '洋服', '<kanji>Western style</kanji> <kanji>clothes</kanji> is just <vocabulary>Western clothing</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>Western style clothes</vocabulary>). But nowadays, because Japanese people wear Western clothing all the time, we use it to just mean <vocabulary>clothes</vocabulary>. We''re talking things that aren''t kimonos, etc.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3293', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%8B%E6%9C%8D', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:38:00Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T21:53:15.126796Z'),
(3294, 'vocabulary', '太平洋', '太平洋', 'The <kanji>fat</kanji> <kanji>peace</kanji> <kanji>ocean</kanji> is the <vocabulary>Pacific Ocean</vocabulary>. The Japanese language thinks this ocean is peaceful, just like English does (the word 太平 actually means "peace").

Note that we''re using the alternative meaning of 洋 here! If you didn''t learn that with the kanji, just imagine that to get to Western-style places you need to cross the ocean from Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3294', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%AA%E5%B9%B3%E6%B4%8B', 16, 1, '2012-04-26T06:38:07Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.211235Z'),
(3295, 'vocabulary', '洋食', '洋食', '<kanji>Western-style</kanji> <kanji>eat</kanji>s just means <vocabulary>Western-style food</vocabulary>.

You can call this <vocabulary>Western food</vocabulary> for short, but in Japan it actually refers to a Japanese genre of food inspired by European dishes. Think omurice (omelette rice) or Napolitan pasta (spaghetti and ketchup). You know, Western-<em>style</em> food.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3295', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%8B%E9%A3%9F', 12, 1, '2012-04-26T06:38:17Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:01:29.866512Z'),
(3296, 'vocabulary', '洋室', '洋室', 'A <kanji>Western style</kanji> <kanji>room</kanji> is a <vocabulary>Western style room</vocabulary>. We''re talking something that''s not a tatami mat filled room. Good to know this word if you''re staying in hotels or ryokans and prefer not to sleep on a futon, etc.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3296', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%8B%E5%AE%A4', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:38:33Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T21:53:15.126796Z'),
(3297, 'vocabulary', '神', '神', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3297', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%9E', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:38:40Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.363264Z'),
(3298, 'vocabulary', '神道', '神道', 'The <kanji>way</kanji>/<kanji>road</kanji> of the <kanji>god</kanji>(s) is, as you can probably guess, a big Japanese religion. There''s two, and I''ll give you a hint, this one isn''t Buddhism (that would probably have the kanji for Buddha in it, no?). So what''s the other big one? It''s <vocabulary>Shinto</vocabulary>. If you''re having trouble remembering this because you''ve never heard of it before, skip to the reading. The reading will help.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3298', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%9E%E9%81%93', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:38:47Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.388574Z'),
(3299, 'vocabulary', '神社', '神社', 'A <kanji>God</kanji> <kanji>company</kanji>... think of this as "what ''company'' does the god''s work?" Well, the answer to that is a shrine, probably. In this case a <vocabulary>Shinto shrine</vocabulary> because of the しん in 神.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3299', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%9E%E7%A4%BE', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:38:54Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:15:31.913965Z'),
(3300, 'vocabulary', '秒', '秒', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well - that is the unit of time, <vocabulary>second</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3300', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%92', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:39:02Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.457725Z'),
(3301, 'vocabulary', '高級', '高級', 'A <kanji>tall</kanji> <kanji>grade</kanji> item is really just a <vocabulary>high grade</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>high-class</vocabulary> item.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3301', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E7%B4%9A', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:39:10Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.49519Z'),
(3302, 'vocabulary', '初級', '初級', 'The <kanji>first</kanji> <kanji>level</kanji> of something is the <vocabulary>beginner level</vocabulary>. Compare this to 中級 and 上級 (vocab from this level) to get a fuller understanding.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3302', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%9D%E7%B4%9A', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:39:20Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:28:35.812439Z'),
(3303, 'vocabulary', '中級', '中級', 'Something at the <kanji>middle</kanji> <kanji>level</kanji> or <kanji>grade</kanji> is at an <vocabulary>intermediate level</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3303', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E7%B4%9A', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:39:32Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.582975Z'),
(3304, 'vocabulary', '上級', '上級', 'When you are at an <kanji>above</kanji> <kanji>level</kanji>, you are at an <vocabulary>advanced level</vocabulary>. This is referring to things like proficiency in Japanese, or things like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3304', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A%E7%B4%9A', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:39:40Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(3305, 'vocabulary', '追う', '追う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>follow</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to follow</vocabulary> (it''s also <vocabulary>to chase</vocabulary>, because when you follow something you are chasing it too).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3305', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%BD%E3%81%86', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:39:47Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.662974Z'),
(3306, 'vocabulary', '戦い', '戦い', 'Don''t let this word confuse you. It may end in い, but it''s not an adjective, it''s a noun. There are no rules in war, after all. This word describes what happens in <kanji>war</kanji>. Those things are <vocabulary>battle</vocabulary>s, <vocabulary>fight</vocabulary>s, and <vocabulary>conflict</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3306', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%A6%E3%81%84', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:40:12Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.709483Z'),
(3307, 'vocabulary', '作戦', '作戦', 'When you <kanji>make</kanji> <kanji>war</kanji>, you''re building it up like you''d build a building. You have to make <vocabulary>strategy</vocabulary>, you have to plan, you have to come up with awesome <vocabulary>tactics</vocabulary> for your <vocabulary>operation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3307', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%9C%E6%88%A6', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:40:35Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:12:39.703958Z'),
(3308, 'vocabulary', '戦車', '戦車', 'A <kanji>war</kanji> <kanji>car</kanji> is probably a little more dangerous than the Honda you drive. No, a war car is a <vocabulary>tank</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3308', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%A6%E8%BB%8A', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:40:45Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.774281Z'),
(3309, 'vocabulary', '戦場', '戦場', 'A <kanji>war</kanji> <kanji>location</kanji> is the <vocabulary>battlefield</vocabulary>. That''s where the wars are fought, anyways.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3309', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%A6%E5%A0%B4', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:40:53Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.802471Z'),
(3310, 'vocabulary', '競う', '競う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>compete</kanji> so the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to compete</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3310', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%B6%E3%81%86', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:41:03Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.829648Z'),
(3311, 'vocabulary', '良い', '良い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>good</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>good</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3311', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%89%AF%E3%81%84', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:41:11Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.853179Z'),
(3312, 'vocabulary', '仲良く', '仲良く', 'When the <kanji>relationship</kanji> is <kanji>good</kanji>, that means you''re <vocabulary>friendly</vocabulary> with that person. You''re also on <vocabulary>good terms</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3312', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%B2%E8%89%AF%E3%81%8F', 13, 1, '2012-04-26T06:41:36Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T01:56:11.967538Z'),
(3313, 'vocabulary', '特に', '特に', 'Something is towards (に) <kanji>special</kanji>. That thing is <vocabulary>especially</vocabulary> special, wouldn''t you say?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3313', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%B9%E3%81%AB', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:42:05Z', NULL, '2026-03-01T17:18:39.303188Z'),
(3314, 'vocabulary', '不便', '不便', 'Something that is <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>convenience</kanji> is <vocabulary>inconvenient</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3314', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E4%BE%BF', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:42:32Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:21.920735Z'),
(3315, 'vocabulary', '便所', '便所', '*Ahem.* This is the <kanji>convenience</kanji> <kanji>facility</kanji>. What place above all others should be convenient to get to? You know, just in case? That would be the <vocabulary>restroom</vocabulary>. You might also call it the <vocabulary>bathroom</vocabulary>, the <vocabulary>toilet</vocabulary>, the lavatory, or the facilities.

This word is casual and can often sound gruff, so you won''t normally see it on official signs or hear it in polite conversation. It''s worth knowing so you can understand it in casual speaking and writing though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3315', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%BF%E6%89%80', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:42:39Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:18:00.076038Z'),
(3316, 'vocabulary', '働く', '働く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>work</kanji>, and the verb version means <vocabulary>to work</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to labor</vocabulary>, and sometimes <vocabulary>to commit</vocabulary> (a crime).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3316', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%83%8D%E3%81%8F', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:42:47Z', NULL, '2026-02-16T09:49:50.723034Z'),
(3317, 'vocabulary', '意見', '意見', 'The <kanji>idea</kanji> you <kanji>see</kanji> in your head is your <vocabulary>opinion</vocabulary>. As the dude once said... "That''s just, like, your 意見, man."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3317', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%8F%E8%A6%8B', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:43:19Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:08.224337Z'),
(3318, 'vocabulary', '意味', '意味', 'The <kanji>flavor</kanji> of an <kanji>idea</kanji>, what is that? Imagine yourself licking an idea to figure out what it tastes like. What do you think you''ll be tasting? Probably the <vocabulary>meaning</vocabulary> of said idea.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3318', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%8F%E5%91%B3', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:43:42Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:20:50.221396Z'),
(3319, 'vocabulary', '味', '味', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3319', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%B3', 11, 1, '2012-04-26T06:44:04Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:20:50.221396Z'),
(3320, 'vocabulary', '勉強', '勉強', 'Your <kanji>exertion</kanji> is <kanji>strong</kanji>. You''d think this means you''re working hard or something, but it means <vocabulary>studies</vocabulary> (like studies you have for a test or something). So for this, just imagine yourself exerting yourself really strongly on your homework. These are your <vocabulary>studies</vocabulary> / what you <vocabulary>study</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3320', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%89%E5%BC%B7', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:01:14Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:05:21.088648Z'),
(3321, 'vocabulary', '家庭', '家庭', 'The essence of your <kanji>house</kanji> and <kanji>garden</kanji> is your <vocabulary>family</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>household</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>home</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3321', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B6%E5%BA%AD', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:01:26Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.089068Z'),
(3322, 'vocabulary', '庭', '庭', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. You can add the meaning <vocabulary>yard</vocabulary> to your memory bank as well, if you''d like.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3322', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%AD', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:01:35Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.116102Z'),
(3323, 'vocabulary', '息', '息', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3323', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%AF', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:01:42Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.141488Z'),
(3324, 'vocabulary', '息子', '息子', 'The <kanji>breath</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> (that lets you release your breath when it''s born) is a <vocabulary>son</vocabulary>. Nowadays it''s cool to have a girl, but back in the day you wanted a son to carry on your underwater basket weaving legacy. That''s why this is a son.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3324', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%AF%E5%AD%90', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:01:51Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.16594Z'),
(3325, 'vocabulary', '利息', '利息', 'You receive <kanji>profit</kanji> at every <kanji>breath</kanji>. What kind of profit is this? It''s <vocabulary>interest</vocabulary>. As time goes by and as you take more breaths you gain more bank interest.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3325', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A9%E6%81%AF', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:02:04Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.192482Z'),
(3326, 'vocabulary', '旅', '旅', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3326', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%85', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:02:14Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:48:50.473642Z'),
(3327, 'vocabulary', '旅行', '旅行', 'When you <kanji>go</kanji> on a <kanji>trip</kanji> you''re taking a <vocabulary>trip</vocabulary>. This is <vocabulary>travel</vocabulary>. You should try to do this occasionally! Perhaps a nice 旅行 to Japan?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3327', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%85%E8%A1%8C', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:02:21Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:48:50.473642Z'),
(3328, 'vocabulary', '根', '根', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3328', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%B9', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:02:45Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.260569Z'),
(3329, 'vocabulary', '根本', '根本', 'The <kanji>root</kanji> <kanji>origin</kanji> is the <vocabulary>root</vocabulary> of something. Possibly even more root than root, but we''ll stick with root.

根本 generally refers to the root cause or fundamental <vocabulary>basis</vocabulary> of something, like "the root of the problem" or "the fundamental principle."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3329', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%B9%E6%9C%AC', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:02:53Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:08.273625Z'),
(3330, 'vocabulary', '屋根', '屋根', 'The <kanji>root</kanji> of your <kanji>roof</kanji> is still your <vocabulary>roof</vocabulary>. If it helps, imagine roots growing out of your roof. They protect you from the rain.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3330', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%8B%E6%A0%B9', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:03:01Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:18:39.371876Z'),
(3331, 'vocabulary', '根気', '根気', 'The <kanji>root</kanji> of your <kanji>energy</kanji>. You have to dig deep into your energy, all the way to the origin and source of it, to have enough <vocabulary>patience</vocabulary> to deal with this dumb kid who''s eating away at your not-root energy. It''s all you have, and you need to use it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3331', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%B9%E6%B0%97', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:03:08Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(3332, 'vocabulary', '大根', '大根', 'This will be easier if you''ve seen or heard of daikon before. It''s a really <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>root</kanji> in the ground that''s pretty delicious. It''s known as <vocabulary>daikon</vocabulary> in the grocery stores for the most part, though occasionally you''ll see it as <vocabulary>Japanese radish</vocabulary> instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3332', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E6%A0%B9', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:03:15Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(3333, 'vocabulary', '流す', '流す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>stream</kanji>. If you''re streaming water, it might be <vocabulary>to flush something</vocabulary>. Alternatively, <vocabulary>to pour something</vocabulary> makes a stream of liquid, and <vocabulary>to set something afloat</vocabulary> is to make something flow in the stream.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3333', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%81%E3%81%99', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:03:33Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.403782Z'),
(3334, 'vocabulary', '流行', '流行', 'Where the <kanji>stream</kanji> is <kanji>go</kanji>ing is where everyone''s going. That''s what tends to be the <vocabulary>trend</vocabulary> of things. Imagine a trend going on in your society right now. Imagine it going down a stream with everyone following it. This word is also used for less desirable trends, like the <vocabulary>prevalence</vocabulary> of infectious diseases.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3334', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%81%E8%A1%8C', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:03:40Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.427291Z'),
(3335, 'vocabulary', '一流', '一流', 'This is a weird one. You have <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>stream</kanji>. This is <vocabulary>first rate</vocabulary> stream material we have here, though. If you think about it, if you only have one stream (instead of a ton of streams), you can put all your effort and energy into it. When you do that, you''re making sure it is a <vocabulary>first rate</vocabulary> stream in terms of quality and anything else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3335', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%B5%81', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:03:48Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.448489Z'),
(3336, 'vocabulary', '本流', '本流', 'The <kanji>origin</kanji> <kanji>stream</kanji>, that''s where everything comes from. That is the <vocabulary>mainstream</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3336', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E6%B5%81', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:04:11Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.473252Z'),
(3337, 'vocabulary', '消す', '消す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>extinguish</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to extinguish something</vocabulary>. You can also use this word to say <vocabulary>to erase something</vocabulary>, like data, and <vocabulary>to turn something off</vocabulary>, like the lights or the power. Technically you''re extinguishing the data, as well as the lights or power, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3337', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B6%88%E3%81%99', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:04:25Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.497945Z'),
(3338, 'vocabulary', '〜倍', '〜倍', 'Throw this onto a number and you have yourself the number of <vocabulary>times</vocabulary> something has increased. For example, ３倍 means something increased "three fold."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3338', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%80%8D', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:05:02Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T00:50:25.948932Z'),
(3339, 'vocabulary', '二倍', '二倍', 'Something that is <kanji>two</kanji> <kanji>times</kanji> is <vocabulary>double</vocabulary> (or it is just simply <vocabulary>two times</vocabulary>). Of course, this all works with things like 三倍, 百倍, and 四十二倍 as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3339', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E5%80%8D', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:05:10Z', NULL, '2026-01-15T00:22:01.25637Z'),
(3340, 'vocabulary', '店員', '店員', 'The <kanji>shop</kanji> <kanji>member</kanji> is someone who is a "member" (aka employed by) the shop. This is the <vocabulary>shop staff</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3340', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%97%E5%93%A1', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:05:18Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:18:26.29811Z'),
(3341, 'vocabulary', '全員', '全員', '<kanji>All</kanji> <kanji>member</kanji>s means <vocabulary>all members</vocabulary>. This is also a fancy way to say <vocabulary>everybody</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3341', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E5%93%A1', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:05:24Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:18:26.29811Z'),
(3342, 'vocabulary', '社員', '社員', 'The <kanji>company</kanji> <kanji>members</kanji> are the <vocabulary>employees</vocabulary> and the <vocabulary>staff</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3342', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A4%BE%E5%93%A1', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:05:34Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:18:26.29811Z'),
(3343, 'vocabulary', '会社員', '会社員', 'A "company" (会社) <kanji>member</kanji> is someone who works at a company. This is a <vocabulary>company employee</vocabulary>. There are many of these in Japan. Most of them are drunk.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3343', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9A%E7%A4%BE%E5%93%A1', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:05:43Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T18:17:25.348855Z'),
(3344, 'vocabulary', '広島', '広島', 'The <kanji>wide</kanji> <kanji>island</kanji>... this isn''t helpful on its own, but this wide island is <vocabulary>Hiroshima</vocabulary> (heard of it?). When you do the reading it will make more sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3344', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%83%E5%B3%B6', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T20:05:51Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.674072Z'),
(3345, 'vocabulary', '祭り', '祭り', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

祭り refers to a Japanese festival — usually a local and seasonal one that''s connected to a shrine and packed with food stalls, crowds, and festive clamor. Note that it''s more common to add the honorific お and say お祭り, as it can sound a bit rough without it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3345', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%AD%E3%82%8A', 14, 1, '2012-05-01T20:06:11Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T07:02:31.568083Z'),
(3346, 'vocabulary', '文章', '文章', 'A <kanji>writing</kanji> <kanji>chapter</kanji> is just another name for a piece of <vocabulary>writing</vocabulary>, a.k.a. a <vocabulary>composition</vocabulary>. Even if it''s only a single <vocabulary>sentence</vocabulary>! 文章 covers just about every type of writing there is.

Although 文章 is pretty versatile, it most often refers to a complete piece of longer writing, like an essay, novel, short story, or poem. But even something super short like a tanka or haiku can be called 文章, as long as it''s a complete work by itself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3346', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%87%E7%AB%A0', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:22:44Z', NULL, '2025-12-23T01:36:37.117296Z'),
(3347, 'vocabulary', '第二章', '第二章', 'The <kanji>ordinal number prefix</kanji> <kanji>two</kanji> <kanji>chapter</kanji> has a much simpler way of describing it, which is <vocabulary>chapter two</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3347', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AC%AC%E4%BA%8C%E7%AB%A0', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:22:53Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.746701Z'),
(3348, 'vocabulary', '第一', '第一', 'The <kanji>ordinal number prefix</kanji> <kanji>one</kanji> refers to <vocabulary>the first</vocabulary> thing. This is the <vocabulary>number one</vocabulary> place you can get. In fact, it''s simply the <vocabulary>best</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3348', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:23:02Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.770108Z'),
(3349, 'vocabulary', '第一位', '第一位', 'You have the <kanji>ordinal number prefix</kanji> and 一位 (first place). By adding the prefix 第, you can make it a little more formal, but it still means <vocabulary>first place</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3349', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E4%BD%8D', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:23:12Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.791058Z'),
(3350, 'vocabulary', '東京都', '東京都', 'Remember how 東京 is Tokyo? Add <kanji>metropolis</kanji> to it and you get the <vocabulary>Tokyo Metropolis</vocabulary>.

東京都 is basically one of Japan''s prefectures, but they call it a "metropolis" instead. It''s the capital and the largest city in all of Japan!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3350', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%B1%E4%BA%AC%E9%83%BD', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:23:34Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:18:24.908021Z'),
(3351, 'vocabulary', '京都', '京都', 'The <kanji>capital</kanji> <kanji>metropolis</kanji> back in the day was <vocabulary>Kyoto</vocabulary>. Now of course the capital is Tokyo, but kanji doesn''t know this. That''s why Kyoto is the capital metropolis!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3351', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%AC%E9%83%BD', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:23:46Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:27:32.907698Z'),
(3352, 'vocabulary', '動く', '動く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>move</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to move</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3352', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%95%E3%81%8F', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:24:08Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.85499Z'),
(3353, 'vocabulary', '動物', '動物', 'A <kanji>moving</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji>... think about the "things" that move. Technically it could be a lot of things (aka basically anything), but in this case we are talking about moving living things. We won''t include humans, because humans are too good to be called things. This refers to <vocabulary>animals</vocabulary> instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3353', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%95%E7%89%A9', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:24:16Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(3354, 'vocabulary', '運動', '運動', 'When you <kanji>carry</kanji> your <kanji>move</kanji>ments (or carry something along with your movements, like weights, for example) you are doing <vocabulary>exercise</vocabulary>. Speaking of which, perhaps you''ve been studying kanji a little too much lately. Time to get up and do some squats or play some <vocabulary>sport</vocabulary>s.

This word can also mean more abstract kinds of <vocabulary>movement</vocabulary>, like social or political movements that strive for change. It can also be used when describing <vocabulary>motion</vocabulary> in physics. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3354', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8B%E5%8B%95', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:24:23Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.898234Z'),
(3355, 'vocabulary', '商売', '商売', '<kanji>Sell</kanji>ing <kanji>merchandise</kanji> is <vocabulary>business</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>commerce</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>trade</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3355', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%95%86%E5%A3%B2', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:24:36Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:32:06.067577Z'),
(3356, 'vocabulary', '商人', '商人', 'A <kanji>merchandise</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>merchant</vocabulary>. You want to buy something or you just looking?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3356', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%95%86%E4%BA%BA', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:24:44Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:52:35.619777Z'),
(3357, 'vocabulary', '悪い', '悪い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>bad</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>bad</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3357', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E3%81%84', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:24:52Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:22.973698Z'),
(3358, 'vocabulary', '悪人', '悪人', 'A <kanji>bad</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>bad person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3358', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E4%BA%BA', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:25:02Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3359, 'vocabulary', '悪女-3359', '悪女', 'A <kanji>bad</kanji> <kanji>woman</kanji> is an <vocabulary>evil woman</vocabulary>. Surprisingly, "mother in law" is not one of the meanings for this word.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3359', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E5%A5%B3-3359', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:25:18Z', '2021-08-26T15:13:18.498101Z', '2025-06-11T05:32:23.026317Z'),
(3360, 'vocabulary', '最悪', '最悪', 'The <kanji>most</kanji> <kanji>bad</kanji> is <vocabulary>the worst</vocabulary>. At least it can’t get any worse…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3360', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%80%E6%82%AA', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:25:31Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.050185Z'),
(3361, 'vocabulary', '家族', '家族', 'Your <kanji>house</kanji> <kanji>tribe</kanji> is your <vocabulary>family</vocabulary>. They live in the same house as you and they''re part of your "tribe." This is closer than family, though (depending on how you look at family). This is your <vocabulary>immediate family</vocabulary> because these are the people you live with and give birth to, etc.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3361', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B6%E6%97%8F', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:25:47Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.075817Z'),
(3362, 'vocabulary', '民族', '民族', 'A <kanji>peoples</kanji> <kanji>tribe</kanji> (or tribe of peoples) is a group that all stick together. They are one single <vocabulary>ethnic group</vocabulary>. What''s your 民族?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3362', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%91%E6%97%8F', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:25:54Z', NULL, '2026-01-20T01:06:16.559902Z'),
(3363, 'vocabulary', '血族', '血族', 'Those sharing <kanji>blood</kanji> within a <kanji>tribe</kanji> are <vocabulary>blood relative</vocabulary>s.

This is a formal term often used in legal matters related to inheritance or insurance claims. In fiction it’s sometimes applied to vampire bloodlines and other supernatural families.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3363', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%80%E6%97%8F', 51, 1, '2012-05-01T22:26:01Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.123009Z'),
(3364, 'vocabulary', '深い', '深い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>deep</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>deep</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3364', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%B1%E3%81%84', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:26:08Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.148575Z'),
(3365, 'vocabulary', '深夜', '深夜', 'The <kanji>deep</kanji> <kanji>night</kanji> is the <vocabulary>middle of the night</vocabulary>. It doesn''t get any deeper than that before you start coming out of the night to start the next day.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3365', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%B1%E5%A4%9C', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:26:16Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T22:22:56.345899Z'),
(3366, 'vocabulary', '水深', '水深', 'The <kanji>water</kanji> <kanji>deep</kanji>ness is the <vocabulary>water depth</vocabulary>. You should check this before diving into the water so you don''t hit your face.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3366', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E6%B7%B1', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:26:23Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.196227Z'),
(3367, 'vocabulary', '最深', '最深', 'The <kanji>most</kanji> <kanji>deep</kanji> is the <vocabulary>deepest</vocabulary>.

最深 is a formal word used in geology to describe the deepest point of things like caves and oceans.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3367', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%80%E6%B7%B1', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:26:30Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.222391Z'),
(3368, 'vocabulary', '球', '球', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

球 has a slightly technical sound and highlights the sphere shape of the object you''re talking about. It''s most commonly used to refer to <vocabulary>sports ball</vocabulary>s (as a more formal alternative to ボール).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3368', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%90%83', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:26:37Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.248253Z'),
(3369, 'vocabulary', '野球', '野球', '<kanji>Field</kanji> <kanji>Sphere</kanji>! The greatest sport to grace this earth since a very similar (aka the same) sport, <vocabulary>baseball</vocabulary>. Think about it, you play on a field and you use a little sphere. Awesome!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3369', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8E%E7%90%83', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:26:44Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.27376Z'),
(3370, 'vocabulary', '童話', '童話', 'Don''t think of this as a <kanji>juvenile</kanji> <kanji>talk</kanji>. Think of 話 the vocab word, which means "story." If you do that, you get a "juvenile story." What''s a juvenile story? It''s a <vocabulary>children''s story</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3370', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%A5%E8%A9%B1', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:26:51Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.296327Z'),
(3371, 'vocabulary', '太陽', '太陽', 'The <kanji>fat</kanji> <kanji>sunshine</kanji> thing up in the sky... what is it called? Oh, that''s right. It''s the <vocabulary>sun</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3371', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%AA%E9%99%BD', 15, 1, '2012-05-01T22:27:23Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.359378Z'),
(3372, 'vocabulary', '一階', '一階', 'The <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>floor</kanji> is the <vocabulary>first floor</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>floor one</vocabulary>). In Japan, that means the <vocabulary>ground floor</vocabulary>. Pretty simple.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3372', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E9%9A%8E', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:27:31Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.346223Z'),
(3373, 'vocabulary', '二階', '二階', 'The <kanji>two</kanji> <kanji>floor</kanji> is the <vocabulary>second floor</vocabulary>. In Japan, that means the floor above the ground floor. Going up!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3373', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E9%9A%8E', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:27:38Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.372106Z'),
(3374, 'vocabulary', '四十二階', '四十二階', 'You know that 四十二 means "forty two." So, as long as you know <kanji>floor</kanji> you''ll know this means <vocabulary>forty-second floor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3374', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B%E5%8D%81%E4%BA%8C%E9%9A%8E', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:27:45Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:13:05.258541Z'),
(3375, 'vocabulary', '寒い', '寒い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>cold</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>cold</vocabulary>.

寒い is used specifically for when you feel cold. You can say it when you''re outside on a chilly winter day, for example, or when you walk into a cold room. It can even be used as a response to unfunny jokes that leave you cold.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3375', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%92%E3%81%84', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:27:52Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.427158Z'),
(3376, 'vocabulary', '悲しい', '悲しい', 'This is a single kanji with しい on the end, so you know it''s an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>sad</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>sad</vocabulary>, sadly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3376', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%B2%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 19, 1, '2012-05-01T22:27:59Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.45257Z'),
(3377, 'vocabulary', '暑い', '暑い', 'This is the adjective form of <kanji>hot</kanji>... but we''re going to call it <vocabulary>hot weather</vocabulary>. Why? Because there''s a difference between something that''s hot and the weather or atmosphere being hot. This is that kind of hot, as in..."Damn, man, it''s hot out here!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3377', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%91%E3%81%84', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:28:15Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.474629Z'),
(3378, 'vocabulary', '期待', '期待', 'For a <kanji>period of time</kanji> you have to <kanji>wait</kanji>. During this waiting period, you’re building up an <vocabulary>expectation</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>anticipation</vocabulary> for something to happen a certain way. Waiting, expecting, waiting, expecting…

期待 specifically refers to the feeling of expectation or anticipation for a desired outcome.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3378', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%9F%E5%BE%85', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:28:23Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.501032Z'),
(3379, 'vocabulary', '学期', '学期', 'The <kanji>study</kanji> <kanji>period of time</kanji> isn''t the pitiful amount of time you spend studying. It''s something much bigger: the <vocabulary>school term</vocabulary>. Ugh, how much longer is this study period of time???', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3379', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A6%E6%9C%9F', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:28:30Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.533566Z'),
(3380, 'vocabulary', '時期', '時期', 'The <kanji>time</kanji> <kanji>period of time</kanji> is a period of time of time. If anything, just notice how many times the word "time" is used and you''ll remember this word means <vocabulary>time</vocabulary>. The meaning is a specific type of time, though. This is talking about a <vocabulary>season</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>period</vocabulary> of time, not the concept of time itself. That would be 時間 or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3380', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%82%E6%9C%9F', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:28:37Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.554854Z'),
(3381, 'vocabulary', '植物', '植物', '<kanji>Plant</kanji> <kanji>things</kanji> are <vocabulary>plants</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>vegetation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3381', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A4%8D%E7%89%A9', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:28:43Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:07:35.298442Z'),
(3382, 'vocabulary', '歯医者', '歯医者', 'You know that a 医者 is a doctor. So what is a <kanji>tooth</kanji> doctor? A <vocabulary>dentist</vocabulary>. Seriously, don''t forget to floss. 歯医者 hate when you forget to floss.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3382', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%AF%E5%8C%BB%E8%80%85', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:28:50Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:12:37.987919Z'),
(3383, 'vocabulary', '虫歯', '虫歯', 'A <kanji>tooth</kanji> <kanji>bug</kanji>... Luckily, it''s not some bug living in your tooth, though you can see why someone might think this is what happens. A tooth with a "bug" in it is a tooth with a <vocabulary>cavity</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3383', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%99%AB%E6%AD%AF', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:28:58Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:12:37.987919Z'),
(3384, 'vocabulary', '歯', '歯', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3384', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%AF', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:29:12Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:12:57.521604Z'),
(3385, 'vocabulary', '温かい', '温かい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>warm</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>warm</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3385', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%A9%E3%81%8B%E3%81%84', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:29:23Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:45:16.390225Z'),
(3386, 'vocabulary', '温泉', '温泉', 'A <kanji>warm</kanji> <kanji>spring</kanji> (like the water kind of spring) is a <vocabulary>hot spring</vocabulary>. These are really nice. You should go to them in Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3386', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%A9%E6%B3%89', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:29:37Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.696099Z'),
(3387, 'vocabulary', '温度', '温度', 'The <kanji>warm</kanji> <kanji>degree</kanji>s is how many degrees warm it is in here. Of course, there can be very few warm degrees as well, making it very cold. What is all this? It''s the <vocabulary>temperature</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3387', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%A9%E5%BA%A6', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:29:46Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.71903Z'),
(3388, 'vocabulary', '空港', '空港', 'Put aside your steampunk blimp fantasies for a moment. A <kanji>sky</kanji> <kanji>harbor</kanji> is an <vocabulary>airport</vocabulary>, nothing more.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3388', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA%E6%B8%AF', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:29:55Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.744907Z'),
(3389, 'vocabulary', '茶の湯', '茶の湯', 'The <kanji>hot water</kanji> of <kanji>tea</kanji>. That is part of the ceremony that goes into the <vocabulary>tea ceremony</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3389', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8C%B6%E3%81%AE%E6%B9%AF', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:30:02Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:16:41.901026Z'),
(3390, 'vocabulary', '湯気', '湯気', '<kanji>Hot water</kanji>''s <kanji>energy</kanji> isn''t the hot water itself, it''s what comes out of it (when it''s boiling, for example). This is the <vocabulary>steam</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>vapor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3390', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B9%AF%E6%B0%97', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:30:11Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(3391, 'vocabulary', '登る', '登る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>climb</kanji> so the verb vocab version would be <vocabulary>to climb</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>to ascend</vocabulary>), if that''s the kind of thing you''re into.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3391', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BB%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:30:19Z', NULL, '2025-08-12T06:48:52.062762Z'),
(3392, 'vocabulary', '登山', '登山', '<kanji>Climb</kanji> a <kanji>mountain</kanji>. That is <vocabulary>mountain climbing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3392', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BB%E5%B1%B1', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:30:27Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:23.848708Z'),
(3393, 'vocabulary', '着る', '着る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>wear</kanji> so this word means <vocabulary>to wear</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to put on</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3393', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9D%80%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:30:46Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(3394, 'vocabulary', '着く', '着く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. Remember how 着 can also mean <kanji>arrive</kanji>? This is the verb version of that meaning, so it means <vocabulary>to arrive</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3394', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9D%80%E3%81%8F', 13, 1, '2012-05-01T22:31:00Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(3395, 'vocabulary', '着物', '着物', 'The <kanji>thing</kanji>s you <kanji>wear</kanji> are your <vocabulary>clothes</vocabulary>. Oh, and Japanese clothes count too. We''re talking <vocabulary>kimono</vocabulary>s. Way back when, these could just be called <vocabulary>clothes</vocabulary> because it''s all people really wore, but nowadays we know they''re specific to the stereotypical kimono you think of when you think "Japan."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3395', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9D%80%E7%89%A9', 14, 1, '2012-05-01T22:31:07Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(3396, 'vocabulary', '下着', '下着', 'The things you <kanji>wear</kanji> <kanji>below</kanji> your other clothes. That is your <vocabulary>underwear</vocabulary>. You do wear underwear, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3396', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E7%9D%80', 14, 1, '2012-05-01T22:31:14Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(3397, 'vocabulary', '水着', '水着', '<kanji>Water</kanji> <kanji>wear</kanji> is a <vocabulary>swimsuit</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>bathing suit</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>swimwear</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3397', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E7%9D%80', 14, 1, '2012-05-01T22:31:25Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(3398, 'vocabulary', '短い', '短い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>short</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>short</vocabulary>.

This word is used to describe things that are short in length, not in height, like 短いトンネル (a short tunnel) or 短いスカート (a short skirt). It can also be used for things that are <vocabulary>brief</vocabulary>, like speeches, meetings, or messages.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3398', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%AD%E3%81%84', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:31:32Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:23:36.0332Z'),
(3399, 'vocabulary', '短刀', '短刀', 'The <kanji>short</kanji> <kanji>sword</kanji> is the <vocabulary>short sword</vocabulary>. What''s another word for a short sword? It''s a <vocabulary>dagger</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3399', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%AD%E5%88%80', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:31:39Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:23:36.0332Z'),
(3400, 'vocabulary', '短期', '短期', 'The <kanji>short</kanji> <kanji>period of time</kanji> is the <vocabulary>short term</vocabulary>. You know not to think short term anyways, right? You''re learning kanji, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3400', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%AD%E6%9C%9F', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:31:46Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:23:36.0332Z'),
(3401, 'vocabulary', '泉', '泉', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3401', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%89', 12, 1, '2012-05-01T22:32:11Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:51:01.368335Z'),
(3402, 'vocabulary', '人口', '人口', 'The amount of people with mouths (that you have to feed) is the <vocabulary>population</vocabulary> that you have to feed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3402', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E5%8F%A3', 1, 2, '2012-05-18T17:08:14.642177Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3403, 'vocabulary', '子牛', '子牛', 'You have a child cow. What do you think a child cow is? Probably a <vocabulary>calf</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>baby cow</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3403', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%90%E7%89%9B', 4, 1, '2012-06-07T17:42:45.85815Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.110974Z'),
(3404, 'vocabulary', '切手', '切手', 'This is a pretty old word, and in the olden days, everything was <kanji>cut</kanji> by <kanji>hand</kanji>, including <vocabulary>postage stamp</vocabulary>s. Having to cut thousands of <vocabulary>stamp</vocabulary>s each day by hand sounds incredibly laborious, so maybe that''s why this word came to be associated specifically with stamps.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3404', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%87%E6%89%8B', 4, 1, '2012-06-07T18:56:00.716579Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.123685Z'),
(3405, 'vocabulary', '引き分け', '引き分け', 'When you <kanji>pull</kanji> something and "separate" (分ける) it, you are making everything even. In a game, this would be a <vocabulary>tie</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>draw</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3405', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%95%E3%81%8D%E5%88%86%E3%81%91', 8, 1, '2012-06-07T19:48:09.904257Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.250969Z'),
(3406, 'vocabulary', '亡くなる', '亡くなる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. This word is actually two words put together, though. 亡く means "be deceased." なる means "to become." So put that all together and you have <vocabulary>to pass away</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to become deceased</vocabulary>. It''s basically a polite way to say something died (死ぬ).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3406', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%A1%E3%81%8F%E3%81%AA%E3%82%8B', 6, 1, '2012-06-07T20:05:26.576576Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.290102Z'),
(3407, 'vocabulary', '人生', '人生', 'The kanji for <kanji>person</kanji> and <kanji>life</kanji> combined makes you think about human life. What is your <vocabulary>human life</vocabulary> like?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3407', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E7%94%9F', 3, 1, '2012-06-12T00:16:35.883506Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3408, 'vocabulary', '男の子', '男の子', 'A <kanji>man</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> is a <vocabulary>boy</vocabulary>.

There''s a の particle stuck in the middle of this word. Here it just means the noun before の is modifying the noun after. 

But for now, all you really have to remember is that 男の子 means "boy."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3408', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B7%E3%81%AE%E5%AD%90', 5, 1, '2012-06-20T23:02:36.134422Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.709429Z'),
(3409, 'vocabulary', '毛虫', '毛虫', 'When you think of a <kanji>fur</kanji> <kanji>insect</kanji> what do you think of? My mind immediately goes to a <vocabulary>caterpillar</vocabulary>. Imagine the furriest caterpillar you can and touch it. How does it feel? Furry?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3409', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%9B%E8%99%AB', 5, 1, '2012-06-20T23:21:27.328573Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.723929Z'),
(3410, 'vocabulary', '下町', '下町', 'You have the <kanji>below</kanji> <kanji>town</kanji>. Does this sort of sound familiar? We have another word for it in English, but it''s essentially the same thing: <vocabulary>downtown</vocabulary>. It isn''t the downtown you might be used to though. In Japan, this is an old word that was used for the commercial districts literally, physically down (lower than) the main city. So picture an old, urban Japanese area!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3410', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E7%94%BA', 5, 1, '2012-06-20T23:32:58.027559Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(3411, 'vocabulary', '小文字', '小文字', 'You know that 文字 means "letters". So, what are "small letters"? That would be <vocabulary>lowercase letters</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3411', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E6%96%87%E5%AD%97', 6, 1, '2012-06-27T17:49:04.70475Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.168259Z'),
(3412, 'vocabulary', '男の人', '男の人', 'A <kanji>man</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>man</vocabulary>. 

There''s a の particle stuck in the middle of this word. Here it just means the noun before の is modifying the noun after. 

Don''t worry if that''s hard to remember though. Just remember 男の人 means "man" (and that it''s more polite than plain 男). ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3412', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B7%E3%81%AE%E4%BA%BA', 5, 1, '2012-06-27T18:12:51.686462Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3413, 'vocabulary', '年', '年', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. You can also use this word to talk about someone''s <vocabulary>age</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3413', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4', 4, 1, '2012-06-27T18:43:49.387833Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.132169Z'),
(3414, 'vocabulary', '今まで', '今まで', 'You know that 今 means now. What you probably don''t know is that まで means "until." So, this word means <vocabulary>until now</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3414', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A%E3%81%BE%E3%81%A7', 5, 1, '2012-06-27T23:47:46.003359Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.761495Z'),
(3415, 'vocabulary', '冬休み', '冬休み', 'The <kanji>winter</kanji> <kanji>rest</kanji>! Wait, nobody calls it that. This is a <vocabulary>winter holiday</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>winter break</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3415', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%AC%E4%BC%91%E3%81%BF', 6, 1, '2012-07-03T00:09:52.537047Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.577925Z'),
(3416, 'vocabulary', '毛糸', '毛糸', 'When you have <kanji>fur</kanji> <kanji>string</kanji> (aka string made of fur) what do you think of? Hopefully <vocabulary>wool yarn</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3416', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%9B%E7%B3%B8', 5, 1, '2012-07-03T00:11:30.73058Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.774514Z'),
(3417, 'vocabulary', '今すぐ', '今すぐ', 'You''ll have to learn this word in two parts. You already know the meaning of 今 because of the kanji, which means "now." The すぐ part, however, is a new word, meaning "soon." すぐ and "soon" even start with a similar sound, so maybe that can help you remember this! If you put 今 and すぐ together, you get "now soon." Put in better English, that''s <vocabulary>at once</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>right now</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3417', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A%E3%81%99%E3%81%90', 5, 1, '2012-07-06T22:34:23.516694Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.785986Z'),
(3418, 'vocabulary', '出来る', '出来る', 'When you <kanji>exit</kanji> and <kanji>come</kanji>, apparently it means <vocabulary>to be able to do</vocabulary>. I think of it like you''re leaving a location (出) to do a task and then you come (来る) back after you did it. Perhaps you were waiting in a room training like someone from Dragonball. When you leave the room, you are ready to fight the enemy and win. You train in that room <vocabulary>to be able to do</vocabulary> whatever you need to do, and only leave and come back alive because you were able to do it.

This verb is really common and it has a wide range of meanings. It can also mean things like <vocabulary>to be ready</vocabulary>, for example a meal, and <vocabulary>to be made</vocabulary>, for example when saying what something is made from. It''s commonly written in hiragana, but you''ll see the kanji version too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3418', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E6%9D%A5%E3%82%8B', 6, 1, '2012-07-08T23:25:47.5727Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(3419, 'vocabulary', '方言', '方言', 'The <kanji>way</kanji> you <kanji>say</kanji> things is your <vocabulary>dialect</vocabulary>. It''s an entire "way" of speaking. 

You can even think of the  方 as being a "direction." If you go far enough in different directions in a country, you''ll discover different regional dialects.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3419', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B9%E8%A8%80', 6, 1, '2012-07-08T23:30:43.776458Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.387351Z'),
(3420, 'vocabulary', '人形', '人形', 'Something that is <kanji>person</kanji> <kanji>shape</kanji>d that isn''t a person? That has to be some kind of <vocabulary>doll</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>puppet</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3420', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E5%BD%A2', 7, 1, '2012-07-08T23:31:25.830068Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3421, 'vocabulary', '外来語', '外来語', 'You know the word 外来 to mean "imported." So, combine that with the kanji for <kanji>language</kanji> and you have an imported word, aka a <vocabulary>borrowed word</vocabulary>. There are a lot of these in Japanese, which makes this word important. Almost everything in katakana is a 外来語, in fact.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3421', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E6%9D%A5%E8%AA%9E', 17, 1, '2012-07-09T20:06:33.053438Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(3422, 'vocabulary', '手作り', '手作り', 'When something is <kanji>hand</kanji> <kanji>made</kanji>, it is <vocabulary>handmade</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3422', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E4%BD%9C%E3%82%8A', 7, 1, '2012-07-09T20:11:05.653256Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:31.944926Z'),
(3423, 'vocabulary', '東京', '東京', 'The <kanji>East</kanji> <kanji>capital</kanji> in Japan is <vocabulary>Tokyo</vocabulary>. If you look on a map and compare it to Kyoto (the old capital), you''ll see it lies to the east. That''s why this is the east capital, Tokyo.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3423', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%B1%E4%BA%AC', 8, 1, '2012-07-19T23:17:13.081833Z', NULL, '2025-11-24T07:10:57.042564Z'),
(3424, 'vocabulary', '合う', '合う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>suit</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to suit</vocabulary>. 

This word also means <vocabulary>to come together</vocabulary> as well. Presumably when something suits something else, it comes together with it flawlessly. That''s why this word also has this other meaning (and it will be important for future vocab as well).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3424', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%88%E3%81%86', 12, 1, '2012-07-19T23:29:10.175434Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:24.695651Z'),
(3425, 'vocabulary', '風', '風', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3425', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A2%A8', 7, 1, '2012-07-19T23:31:36.202955Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:31.980072Z'),
(3426, 'vocabulary', '当て字', '当て字', '当てる means "to guess" or "to hit", and 字 means <kanji>letter</kanji>. So these are guessed letters? Kind of, they''re so-called <vocabulary>phonetic kanji</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>ateji</vocabulary>.

This refers to cases where kanji are used solely for their sound, but not their meaning. There are also some cases of ateji where kanji are used only for their meaning and not their sound, but this is less common. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3426', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%93%E3%81%A6%E5%AD%97', 10, 1, '2012-07-22T19:00:22.041038Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.985151Z'),
(3427, 'vocabulary', '次々', '次々', 'When things happen in a <kanji>next</kanji>, <kanji>next</kanji> (next, next, next…) way, they happen <vocabulary>one after another</vocabulary>.

Imagine a factory line for item checking. The people there keep shouting "Next! Next!" because items come <vocabulary>in succession</vocabulary> without stopping!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3427', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%A1%E3%80%85', 10, 1, '2012-07-22T21:08:05.890399Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.766088Z'),
(3428, 'vocabulary', '向こう', '向こう', 'You know that 向 means <kanji>yonder</kanji>, and this is its noun version, meaning <vocabulary>over there</vocabulary>.

向こう is used when speaking generally about something distant from yourself, whether it''s a place, as in 向こうの山 (the mountain <vocabulary>over that way</vocabulary>), or a person, as in 向こうの人 (the person on the <vocabulary>other side</vocabulary>). Sometimes, it can even be used for time, like 向こう一週間 (the next week ahead).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3428', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%91%E3%81%93%E3%81%86', 8, 1, '2012-07-22T21:08:49.710244Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:36:04.223874Z'),
(3429, 'vocabulary', '曲がる', '曲がる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means both <kanji>music</kanji> and <kanji>bend</kanji>. For this word, we''re looking at "bend." So the verb 曲がる is <vocabulary>to be bent</vocabulary>. 

It''s intransitive, so don''t give it a direct object. Use it to say something gets bent or bends: a knee is bent, the road bends — that sort of thing. 

Verbs ending in okurigana that rhyme with ある tend to be intransitive like this. You can remember this because if something is ある, it''s just there, existing, not acting <em>on</em> anything else. That''s why verbs that sound like it are often intransitive too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3429', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B2%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 9, 1, '2012-07-22T22:47:19.906966Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:51:25.132787Z'),
(3430, 'vocabulary', '南米', '南米', 'Remember how 米国 means "America" (the country)? The 米 kanji can actually be used for either of the American continents. In this word we have <kanji>south</kanji> combining with <kanji>America</kanji>, so it must mean… <vocabulary>South America</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3430', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%97%E7%B1%B3', 9, 1, '2012-07-22T22:49:32.068875Z', NULL, '2026-01-19T08:15:51.228829Z'),
(3431, 'vocabulary', '全米', '全米', 'Do you remember how 米国 can mean "America?" That 米 means America sometimes. So, when you see <kanji>all</kanji> <kanji>rice</kanji> (I mean, <kanji>America</kanji>), you can think of this word as meaning <vocabulary>All America</vocabulary> (sort of like how 全日本 means "All Japan.").', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3431', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E7%B1%B3', 8, 1, '2012-07-22T22:55:22.081016Z', NULL, '2026-01-19T08:15:51.228829Z'),
(3432, 'vocabulary', '南口', '南口', 'After learning 北口, 西口, and 東口, it''s time for round four! Yep, you guessed it, adding <kanji>south</kanji> to 口 makes this <vocabulary>south exit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>south entrance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3432', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%97%E5%8F%A3', 7, 1, '2012-07-22T23:08:52.577003Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:50:32.761964Z'),
(3433, 'vocabulary', '思い出', '思い出', 'Do you remember the word 思う? It means "to think." What you are <kanji>think</kanji>ing is <kanji>exit</kanji>ing—it''s coming out of your head! You''re recalling <vocabulary>a memory</vocabulary> or multiple <vocabulary>memories</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3433', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%9D%E3%81%84%E5%87%BA', 8, 1, '2012-07-22T23:19:48.660181Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:49.937895Z'),
(3434, 'vocabulary', '近づく', '近づく', 'Because this word ends with an う sound, you can tell it''s probably a verb. The kanji portion means <kanji>near</kanji>. The verb version of that is <vocabulary>to draw near</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to get close</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to approach</vocabulary>. Essentially, the general idea is that you''re getting closer to something. You''re becoming more nearby. You''re approaching.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3434', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E3%81%A5%E3%81%8F', 8, 1, '2012-07-22T23:28:45.447595Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:49.95136Z'),
(3435, 'vocabulary', '思い出す', '思い出す', 'You know that 思う means "to think" and 出す means "to take out." So this is what happens when your brain takes a moment to think and pulls a memory out of storage. It means <vocabulary>to remember</vocabulary>!

You can shout 思い出した！when you recall your keys are in the fridge. (Again?!) You can also say 母を思い出します when something reminds you of your mom. In Japanese, you usually omit what reminded you of the memory because the important thing is the memory itself, filling your heart with touchy-feelies. Oh, mama!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3435', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%9D%E3%81%84%E5%87%BA%E3%81%99', 8, 1, '2012-07-23T00:57:04.234518Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:49.966621Z'),
(3436, 'vocabulary', '大学生', '大学生', 'You know that 学生 means "student" and you know that 大学 means university/college. Combine the two and you have a <vocabulary>university student</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>college student</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3436', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%AD%A6%E7%94%9F', 7, 1, '2012-07-23T01:22:52.470974Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(3437, 'vocabulary', '言い方', '言い方', 'Your <kanji>say</kanji>ing <kanji>way</kanji> is… your <vocabulary>way of saying</vocabulary>! This refers to the words you use and how you phrase things, as well as how you say it. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3437', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%80%E3%81%84%E6%96%B9', 7, 1, '2012-07-23T01:27:46.444639Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.039863Z'),
(3438, 'vocabulary', '先回り', '先回り', 'If you get there <kanji>previous</kanji> to something rotating (回り), you are <vocabulary>arriving ahead</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>going ahead</vocabulary> in <vocabulary>anticipation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3438', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%88%E5%9B%9E%E3%82%8A', 7, 1, '2012-07-24T23:15:05.918095Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:27:12.714404Z'),
(3439, 'vocabulary', '早口', '早口', 'When you have a <kanji>fast</kanji> <kanji>mouth</kanji>, you''re probably a <vocabulary>fast talker</vocabulary>, right motormouth?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3439', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A9%E5%8F%A3', 6, 1, '2012-07-24T23:16:41.789243Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:53:32.555199Z'),
(3440, 'vocabulary', '足りない', '足りない', 'You know the word 足りる from Level 4. What is this one? it''s the opposite (negative form on a verb is ない). So, what is the opposite of 足りる (To be sufficient)? The opposite is <vocabulary>to not be sufficient</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>not enough</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3440', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B6%B3%E3%82%8A%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84', 6, 1, '2012-07-24T23:19:18.206757Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.487107Z'),
(3441, 'vocabulary', '年上', '年上', 'When you''re <kanji>year</kanji>s <kanji>above</kanji> someone else, you are <vocabulary>older</vocabulary> than that person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3441', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E4%B8%8A', 6, 1, '2012-07-24T23:19:40.217136Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(3442, 'vocabulary', '世の中', '世の中', 'The <kanji>middle</kanji> of the <kanji>world</kanji> is the <vocabulary>society</vocabulary>. It''s what holds everything together, for us humans. You can also call it <vocabulary>the world</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3442', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%96%E3%81%AE%E4%B8%AD', 21, 1, '2012-07-24T23:20:07.899795Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:25.196776Z'),
(3443, 'vocabulary', '代わり', '代わり', 'You''ve seen that 代わる means "to substitute for" or "to replace." This is the noun version of that (no う sound on the end). So, what is the noun version of those verb words? It would be <vocabulary>substitute</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>replacement</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3443', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%A3%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8A', 8, 1, '2012-07-24T23:20:38.656823Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.296623Z'),
(3444, 'vocabulary', '今年', '今年', 'The <kanji>now</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji> is which year? It is <vocabulary>this year</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3444', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A%E5%B9%B4', 6, 1, '2012-07-24T23:21:05.737573Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:15:06.177254Z'),
(3445, 'vocabulary', '代える', '代える', 'You know the kanji is <kanji>substitute</kanji>, and you know this is a verb. There are other similar ones, but this one means <vocabulary>to replace something</vocabulary>. In order to remember that this particular word means "to replace something" focus in on the え. You have an egg in your hands that you notice isn''t any good (it''s black and moldy). So, you replace it with another egg (or substitute it with another egg, or exchange it with another egg, etc).

This verb is transitive, meaning it has a direct object. You replace something, like that egg!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3445', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%A3%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-07-24T23:21:29.76149Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:54.955305Z'),
(3446, 'vocabulary', '年下', '年下', 'When you have someone who has <kanji>years</kanji> <kanji>below</kanji> your own, this person is <vocabulary>younger</vocabulary> than you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3446', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E4%B8%8B', 6, 1, '2012-07-24T23:22:57.090999Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(3447, 'vocabulary', '近々', '近々', 'When something is <kanji>close</kanji> <kanji>close</kanji> (the 々 character repeats the kanji that was before it) it will happen <vocabulary>in the near future</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>soon</vocabulary>. 

This is pretty vague, and it''s up to the person speaking to decide how soon <vocabulary>soon</vocabulary> is. Depending on the person and the situation, it could technically be used for anything up to a few years!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3447', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E3%80%85', 7, 1, '2012-07-24T23:24:51.077814Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T06:27:10.721165Z'),
(3448, 'vocabulary', '青空', '青空', 'This is simply a <kanji>blue</kanji> <kanji>sky</kanji> (aka a <vocabulary>blue sky</vocabulary>!).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3448', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%92%E7%A9%BA', 7, 1, '2012-07-24T23:25:19.201772Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.078515Z'),
(3449, 'vocabulary', '小学生', '小学生', 'You know 学生 is student, so what is a <kanji>small</kanji> student? Think young. That would be an <vocabulary>Elementary School Student</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3449', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E5%AD%A6%E7%94%9F', 7, 1, '2012-07-24T23:25:40.008317Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.091722Z'),
(3450, 'vocabulary', '作り方', '作り方', 'This is the <kanji>way</kanji> to <kanji>make</kanji> something. This is <vocabulary>how to make</vocabulary> something. You''ll see this for directions on building something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3450', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%9C%E3%82%8A%E6%96%B9', 7, 1, '2012-07-24T23:26:03.333679Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.103984Z'),
(3451, 'vocabulary', '中学生', '中学生', 'You''ve seen 学生 (means student). Now let''s look at a student who''s in the middle. Perhaps we can call this a <vocabulary>middle school student</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>junior high school student</vocabulary>, depending on where you''re from.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3451', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E5%AD%A6%E7%94%9F', 7, 1, '2012-07-24T23:26:26.639092Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.12033Z'),
(3452, 'vocabulary', '不人気', '不人気', 'You already know 人気 which means "popular." And you also know that 不 means "not." So what do you get when you combine the two? Something that''s <vocabulary>not popular</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>unpopular</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3452', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E4%BA%BA%E6%B0%97', 7, 1, '2012-07-24T23:26:50.098005Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3453, 'vocabulary', '見直す', '見直す', 'You know the word 直す from a couple levels ago. It means "to fix." So, when you fix the way you see something, what are you doing? You''re reevaluating it. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>to reevaluate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3453', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E7%9B%B4%E3%81%99', 16, 1, '2012-07-24T23:32:40.913072Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.561283Z'),
(3454, 'vocabulary', '全く', '全く', 'This is the adverb version of the kanji <kanji>all</kanji>. So, it''s like you add an ''ly'' to it. But, that doesn''t work too well (allly? c''mon). So, instead think of this word as <vocabulary>completely</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>entirely</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>truly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3454', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E3%81%8F', 8, 1, '2012-07-24T23:33:16.531982Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.090104Z'),
(3455, 'vocabulary', '長さ', '長さ', 'You know the word 長い from a couple levels ago. This is like the word 大きい versus 大きさ, where 大きさ is "size" and 大きい is "big." So, 長い is "long" and 長さ is <vocabulary>length</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3455', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%95%B7%E3%81%95', 8, 1, '2012-07-24T23:33:33.69419Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.158366Z'),
(3456, 'vocabulary', '安売り', '安売り', 'When you <kanji>sell</kanji> something <kanji>cheap</kanji>, you''re selling it at a <vocabulary>discount</vocabulary>. Alternatively you can remember this word as <vocabulary>sell cheaply</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>bargain sale</vocabulary>. These are good deals!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3456', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%89%E5%A3%B2%E3%82%8A', 11, 1, '2012-07-24T23:33:51.05266Z', NULL, '2025-11-28T01:13:50.947331Z'),
(3457, 'vocabulary', 'この前', 'この前', 'この means "this", and you know what the word 前  means (front/before). The combination of these words, "this before" if you will, can mean a few different things. If we''re talking about when something happened, the "before" meaning kicks in, and it means <vocabulary>the other day</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>recently</vocabulary>. If we''re talking about location, then the "front" meaning kicks in, and it means <vocabulary>in front of this</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3457', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%93%E3%81%AE%E5%89%8D', 8, 1, '2012-07-24T23:34:11.20305Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.217629Z'),
(3458, 'vocabulary', '東口', '東口', 'Remember 北口 and 西口 from a few levels before? This is the <kanji>east</kanji> version of that, so it means <vocabulary>east exit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>east entrance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3458', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%B1%E5%8F%A3', 6, 1, '2012-07-24T23:34:32.614143Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.793506Z'),
(3459, 'vocabulary', 'お知らせ', 'お知らせ', 'This is a strange word, so you''ll have to bear with me. You know that 知 means <kanji>know</kanji>. But in the case of this word, what do you know? Look at the second half. らせ sounds a bit like "race." So, you know about a "race." How do you know about this race? You got a <vocabulary>notice</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>notification</vocabulary> about it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3459', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E7%9F%A5%E3%82%89%E3%81%9B', 8, 1, '2012-07-24T23:34:53.000571Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.248503Z'),
(3460, 'vocabulary', '名前', '名前', 'Your <kanji>name</kanji> that''s in the <kanji>front</kanji>. That is not your last name, that is your regular <vocabulary>name</vocabulary> or your <vocabulary>first name</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3460', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8D%E5%89%8D', 8, 1, '2012-07-24T23:35:12.498831Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(3461, 'vocabulary', '出来上がる', '出来上がる', 'You''ve seen the word 出来る (to be able to do), which is the first part. Then the second part, 上がる means "to rise." So, think about it this way: once you''re able to do something, you rise because you''re finished. That''s why this verb means <vocabulary>to be finished</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3461', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E6%9D%A5%E4%B8%8A%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-07-24T23:35:35.579542Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(3462, 'vocabulary', '曲げる', '曲げる', 'This is a verb because it ends with an う sound. What does the kanji mean? It''s <kanji>music</kanji> and <kanji>bend</kanji>. In the case of this word, we''re going with the "bend" version. The verb of that is <vocabulary>to bend</vocabulary>. This is when you''re doing the bending. You can remember this because げ kind of looks like the <reading>ge</reading>cko you''re bending. That gecko looked good before you bent him in half.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3462', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B2%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-07-24T23:35:50.066898Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T08:16:48.466346Z'),
(3463, 'vocabulary', '交じる', '交じる', 'You know that 交ぜる means <vocabulary>to mix</vocabulary> and that the kanji means <kanji>mix</kanji> as well. So, this probably has something to do with mixing things. This word means <vocabulary>to be mixed</vocabulary>. This is when something is mixed but it''s not mixed by you. It''s just being mixed. Think of it this way - the じ in 交じる is Jesus, who just uses Jesus magic to mix something. So, you''re not mixing it. It''s just when something is <vocabulary>to be mixed</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3463', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%A4%E3%81%98%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-07-24T23:36:07.002761Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.323994Z'),
(3464, 'vocabulary', '走り回る', '走り回る', 'You''ve seen the words 走る (to run) and 回る (to revolve). So, when you''re running and revolving while you''re doing it, you''re running in circles. So, the verb form of that is <vocabulary>to run around</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3464', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%B0%E3%82%8A%E5%9B%9E%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-07-24T23:36:21.840949Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.345495Z'),
(3465, 'vocabulary', '当てる', '当てる', 'You know that the kanji 当 means <kanji>right</kanji> or <kanji>success</kanji>. So since this is a verb, you''re doing right and success (which doesn''t make much sense). Instead, you''re guessing something and getting it right. So, this word means <vocabulary>to guess</vocabulary> something. Hopefully you don''t have to guess too hard when it comes to the meaning of this word as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3465', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%93%E3%81%A6%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-07-24T23:36:48.0276Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.359766Z'),
(3466, 'vocabulary', '交わる', '交わる', 'You know that the kanji means <kanji>mix</kanji> and the word 交ぜる means "to mix," so it probably has something to do with that. This word means <vocabulary>to intersect</vocabulary>, which is kind of like mixing. When two things intersect with each other, they''re getting mixed up, so that''s where this word gets its meaning. 

To remember that this one means "to intersect," remember the わ as <reading>wa</reading>lk. You''re walking around, and then you intersect with some people on the street.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3466', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%A4%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8B', 9, 1, '2012-07-24T23:37:05.169377Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.244304Z'),
(3467, 'vocabulary', '化ける', '化ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>change</kanji> so you''d think the verb version is "to change," but it''s a bit more specific. This word means <vocabulary>to change form</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to transform</vocabulary>.

化ける means to change into something else, usually in a surprising and magical way. It''s often used in stories where animals or spirits take on human forms. It can also describe transformations in real life that are so dramatic they feel unreal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3467', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%96%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2012-07-24T23:37:20.842824Z', NULL, '2025-11-20T03:47:15.21122Z'),
(3468, 'vocabulary', '回す', '回す', 'You''ve already learned 回る, which means "to revolve" or "to rotate," and this is the transitive version of that. You''re doing the turning/rotating action on something else, so this means <vocabulary>to turn something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to rotate something</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to spin something</vocabulary>.

To remember the difference, just think about how you might get <reading>sue</reading>d (す) if you started aggressively spinning one of those playground spinners while someone else''s kids were playing on it. Pretty shady behavior.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3468', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9E%E3%81%99', 8, 1, '2012-07-24T23:37:40.112537Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:39:17.072737Z'),
(3469, 'vocabulary', '小学校', '小学校', 'You''ve seen 学校 from a couple levels ago. Now we''re adding a <kanji>small</kanji> to it. What is a "small school"? That''s a school for small people, making it an <vocabulary>elementary school</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3469', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E5%AD%A6%E6%A0%A1', 9, 1, '2012-07-25T04:14:41.711793Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.283036Z'),
(3470, 'vocabulary', '通り', '通り', 'You know the word 通る hopefully by now. That is the verb version of this word, which means "to pass through." So, if this is the noun version, what is it that you use to pass through something? To drive or walk along? That would be an <vocabulary>avenue</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>street</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>road</vocabulary>, etc). This is a pretty common word in Japan, and good for not getting lost.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3470', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%9A%E3%82%8A', 9, 1, '2012-07-25T17:56:19.723926Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.212398Z'),
(3471, 'vocabulary', '気を付けて', '気を付けて', 'You take your 気 (energy) and 付ける (attach) it to whatever you''re doing, allowing you to focus in and <vocabulary>be careful</vocabulary>. Since this word is in the te-form, it''s a soft imperative. You''re basically telling someone, "hey, <vocabulary>take care</vocabulary>, okay?"

気を付けて is an everyday expression used a lot when telling someone to watch out, stay safe, or be mindful of something. Note that 気をつけて without the kanji 付 is also common, especially in casual writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3471', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E3%82%92%E4%BB%98%E3%81%91%E3%81%A6', 9, 1, '2012-07-25T17:58:56.382567Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:43:54.543763Z'),
(3472, 'vocabulary', '新しい', '新しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>new</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>new</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3472', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 9, 1, '2012-07-25T18:33:57.033775Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.325461Z'),
(3473, 'vocabulary', '高校生', '高校生', 'You know 高校 means "high school." So, a "life" that does high school is a <vocabulary>high school student</vocabulary>. This is like how 中学生 are middle school students and 小学生 are elementary school students!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3473', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E6%A0%A1%E7%94%9F', 9, 1, '2012-07-25T23:06:43.517887Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.335732Z'),
(3474, 'vocabulary', '中学校', '中学校', 'You know that 学校 means school. So, people who are in the <kanji>middle</kanji> <kanji>school</kanji> are in... wait for it... <vocabulary>middle school</vocabulary>. 

And, of course, if you''re one of those people that call it <vocabulary>junior high school</vocabulary> you can do that too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3474', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E5%AD%A6%E6%A0%A1', 9, 1, '2012-07-25T23:15:06.780603Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.347433Z'),
(3475, 'vocabulary', '付く', '付く', 'You know that the word 付ける means "to attach" and you know that the kanji 付 means <kanji>attach</kanji>. So, this word probably has something to do with attaching. This is the intransitive version, though, where something is attached (and you''re not doing the attaching). You can use the く as a hint. You see something attached on the wall. You say "Coo''"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3475', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%98%E3%81%8F', 9, 1, '2012-07-26T17:23:13.669081Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:03:42.596356Z'),
(3476, 'vocabulary', '見付ける', '見付ける', 'You know that 付ける means "to attach" and that 見 means "see." So, when you attach your seeing to something, it''s like you''re looking around trying <vocabulary>to find</vocabulary> something. When you find what you''re looking for, imagine your eyeballs flying out and attaching to that item, then yelling "I FOUND IT!"

You''ll usually see this word with the 付 in kana, so 見つける.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3476', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E4%BB%98%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 9, 1, '2012-07-26T17:23:50.712957Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:03:42.596356Z'),
(3477, 'vocabulary', '弱虫', '弱虫', 'A <kanji>weak</kanji> <kanji>insect</kanji>. This is what people call <vocabulary>weakling</vocabulary>s in Japan. This is a good word to know if you''re a bully, or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3477', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%B1%E8%99%AB', 9, 1, '2012-07-26T17:56:58.429082Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T06:03:32.726396Z'),
(3478, 'vocabulary', '弱々しい', '弱々しい', 'Something that is double <kanji>weak</kanji> is probably pretty weak. You also know that this is an adjective because it ends with い. You know that 弱い is weak, so it probably isn''t that. Instead, this one is <vocabulary>weak looking</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>frail</vocabulary>. If you know the grammar らしい you can kind of use that as a hint. The しい on the end reminds me of らしい which is like "seems to be." So, "seems to be weak" = "weak looking."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3478', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%B1%E3%80%85%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 9, 1, '2012-07-26T17:57:29.787831Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T06:03:32.726396Z'),
(3479, 'vocabulary', '欠ける', '欠ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

The kanji itself means <kanji>lack</kanji>, and this verb has a few meanings related to that. You''ll often see it meaning <vocabulary>to get chipped</vocabulary>, like when talking about a teacup or your teeth. I guess a chipped teacup is lacking... a piece of itself?

欠ける can also literally mean <vocabulary>to be lacking</vocabulary>, as in "a good goalie is lacking from their team." You can use it like this when you''re short of something, or when something is missing that would complete a set. In these situations, you''ll usually see it as 欠けている!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3479', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%A0%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 9, 1, '2012-07-26T18:40:20.144229Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:21:36.886438Z'),
(3480, 'vocabulary', '目医者', '目医者', 'Do you remember 医者 (doctor)? What happens when you add <kanji>eye</kanji> on there? You have yourself an <vocabulary>eye doctor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3480', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%AE%E5%8C%BB%E8%80%85', 10, 1, '2012-08-14T22:39:28.132203Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(3481, 'vocabulary', '一番目', '一番目', 'You''ve seen 一番 (number one), so what in the world is this eye on the end? This eye (目) is used to mark something''s position in a sequence. So, add this to "number one" and you have <vocabulary>the first thing</vocabulary> or simply <vocabulary>the first</vocabulary>.

Just imagine your collection of eyeballs you have at home. (You have a collection of eyeballs, right?) You have them lined up in a display case and you''re explaining the first one to your friend. It''s not first because it''s the best or anything — you love all your eyeballs equally, after all — but it does happen to come first in sequence in your display right now. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3481', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E7%95%AA%E7%9B%AE', 10, 1, '2012-08-14T22:44:27.632686Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.023985Z'),
(3482, 'vocabulary', '仕返し', '仕返し', 'When you <kanji>return</kanji> the <kanji>doing</kanji>s of another person, you''re doing it to <vocabulary>get even</vocabulary> (whether it''s a nice thing or a mean thing).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3482', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%95%E8%BF%94%E3%81%97', 11, 1, '2012-08-14T22:56:04.531825Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:26.250878Z'),
(3483, 'vocabulary', '高さ', '高さ', 'You''ve seen the word 高い from a couple levels ago. This word is related to that. It''s like 大きい versus 大きさ, where one describes the other. In this case, the 高さ is the <vocabulary>height</vocabulary> of something (while 高い is tall). This word isn''t "price" though. For that you''d ask いくらですか？ (how much is it?).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3483', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E3%81%95', 9, 1, '2012-08-14T22:56:43.591265Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.396203Z'),
(3484, 'vocabulary', '楽しみ', '楽しみ', 'You''ve seen the word 楽しい, which means "fun." This is obviously very similar looking, though this one means <vocabulary>looking forward to</vocabulary>. Why? Because it has a み on the end, saying "who is going to have fun? It''s <reading>me</reading>! That''s why I''m looking forward to it!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3484', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%BD%E3%81%97%E3%81%BF', 10, 1, '2012-08-16T23:40:48.559694Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.03544Z'),
(3485, 'vocabulary', '〜向け', '〜向け', 'You''ve hopefully seen 向く and 向ける by now. They mean "to face" and "to turn toward." This has a similar idea. When you add 向け to something imagine you''re turning it, aiming it at someone. It''s aimed at them, meaning it''s <vocabulary>made for</vocabulary> them. Turn a book toward someone, now it''s <vocabulary>aimed at</vocabulary> them, it''s made for them. See the connection?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3485', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%90%91%E3%81%91', 10, 1, '2012-08-16T23:41:41.674223Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.047947Z'),
(3486, 'vocabulary', '研究所', '研究所', 'You''ve seen that 研究 is "research" from previous vocab, and you might remember 所 can mean "facility." So this word means <vocabulary>research institute</vocabulary> or, of course, <vocabulary>research facility</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3486', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%94%E7%A9%B6%E6%89%80', 10, 1, '2012-08-17T00:38:17.84465Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.059551Z'),
(3487, 'vocabulary', '近道', '近道', 'Taking the <kanji>nearby</kanji> <kanji>road</kanji> will get you to your destination more quickly (because it''s more close by!). This is a <vocabulary>shortcut</vocabulary>, aka the <vocabulary>short way</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3487', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E9%81%93', 10, 1, '2012-08-17T01:07:45.041716Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.070202Z'),
(3488, 'vocabulary', '助かる', '助かる', 'You know that 助ける means "to help." This word, 助かる, is related, but it''s a bit different. It means <vocabulary>to be helped</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be saved</vocabulary>. It''s intransitive, so it doesn''t take a direct object. Imagine shouting "I''m saved!" No direct object, right?

Verbs ending in okurigana that rhyme with ある tend to be intransitive like this. You can remember this because if something is ある, it''s just there, existing, not acting <em>on</em> anything else. That''s why verbs that sound like it are often intransitive too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3488', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%A9%E3%81%8B%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-08-17T07:01:38.996253Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.080929Z'),
(3489, 'vocabulary', '間に合う', '間に合う', 'There is an <kanji>interval</kanji> (of time) that you need "to suit" (合う). To suit that interval, you need to be there before the time in question. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>to be in time for</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to make it on time</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be on time for</vocabulary>.
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3489', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%93%E3%81%AB%E5%90%88%E3%81%86', 14, 1, '2012-08-17T07:02:46.288124Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:26.456923Z'),
(3490, 'vocabulary', '返る', '返る', 'You hopefully know the word 返す, which means "to return" (as in, you''re doing the direct action of returning something). This word, on the other hand, is the version where something just returns (and you''re not doing the action of returning it). Use the る to help you to remember this. る the Kangaroo is returned to you. You don''t do any of the returning, it just happens or someone else does it. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>to be returned</vocabulary>.

Note this word is often combined with くる (to come), which reinforces the aspect that something is coming back to you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3490', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%94%E3%82%8B', 11, 1, '2012-08-17T07:25:31.180637Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:26.48119Z'),
(3491, 'vocabulary', '楽しむ', '楽しむ', 'You''ve hopefully seen the word 楽しい, which means "fun." This is basically the verb version (ends in an う sound, right?) of that. That means this is <vocabulary>to enjoy something</vocabulary>, or just <vocabulary>to have fun</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3491', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%BD%E3%81%97%E3%82%80', 10, 1, '2012-08-17T07:30:42.987099Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.091378Z'),
(3492, 'vocabulary', '決まる', '決まる', 'You''ve hopefully seen the word 決める before. This is similar, except instead of deciding something, something gets decided. Rules are decided, a schedule gets decided — that kind of thing. It''s intransitive, so it doesn''t take a direct object. So 決まる means <vocabulary>to be decided</vocabulary>.

Verbs ending in okurigana that rhyme with ある tend to be intransitive like this. You can remember this because if something is ある, it''s just there, existing, not acting <em>on</em> anything else. That''s why verbs that sound like it are often intransitive too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3492', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%BA%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2012-08-17T07:31:18.502743Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:09:27.747622Z'),
(3493, 'vocabulary', '投げ付ける', '投げ付ける', 'You know that you''re <kanji>throw</kanji>ing something that is <kanji>attach</kanji>ing to something else. How else can you say that? The best way would be to say <vocabulary>to throw against</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to throw at</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3493', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%95%E3%81%92%E4%BB%98%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-08-17T08:01:01.877804Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.102919Z'),
(3494, 'vocabulary', '化かす', '化かす', 'You''ve seen the word 化ける (to transform) before, and here''s a related word: 化かす. This means <vocabulary>to bewitch</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to trick</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to deceive</vocabulary>.

Why? In Japanese folklore, foxes, spirits and other yokai often transform in order to bewitch, trick, or deceive humans. To 化かす them, in other words.

This verb is transitive, so it needs a direct object — you bewitch, trick, or deceive someone. You know this because when a fox wants to bewitch someone, they have to <reading>cuss</reading> (かす). They say a magic cuss word that lets them change forms and fool people. Those tricksy foxes…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3494', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%96%E3%81%8B%E3%81%99', 10, 1, '2012-08-17T08:15:49.672594Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.114023Z'),
(3495, 'vocabulary', '話', '話', 'The kanji version of 話 means <kanji>talk</kanji>. This word also means <vocabulary>talk</vocabulary>, but it means a few other things as well. This can refer to a <vocabulary>speech</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>story</vocabulary> as well. Basically anything that involves a talking session. So, before you go, I hope you stay awhile and listen to my 話.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3495', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%B1', 10, 1, '2012-08-17T08:28:05.97434Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:22:32.984246Z'),
(3496, 'vocabulary', '向く', '向く', 'You know that the kanji means <kanji>yonder</kanji>, which is like "over there." This is the verb version of "yonder." That being said, "to yonder" isn''t really a thing, so you have to think of what happens when you "look yonder." This word means <vocabulary>to turn toward</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to face</vocabulary>. You''re looking yonder, but you have to turn towards said yonder to look that way.

This word can also mean <vocabulary>to be suitable</vocabulary>, as in a good fit for a job, role, or situation. After all, you turn toward the things you''re most suitable for, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3496', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%91%E3%81%8F', 6, 1, '2012-08-17T17:58:50.698117Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.231406Z'),
(3497, 'vocabulary', '知り合い', '知り合い', 'You''ve probably seen the words 知る and 合う by now. This is someone you "know" because you have "come together" before, but that''s pretty much it. You don''t really know them well, you just met them a couple times so you know who they are. This person is an <vocabulary>acquaintance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3497', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%A5%E3%82%8A%E5%90%88%E3%81%84', 14, 1, '2012-08-18T21:01:03.937976Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T17:28:43.166441Z'),
(3498, 'vocabulary', '乗り場', '乗り場', 'What''s a <kanji>ride</kanji> <kanji>location</kanji>? It''s a <vocabulary>boarding area</vocabulary>! This word is used widely to refer to any place where you board transportation, such as a <vocabulary>bus stop</vocabulary>, a <vocabulary>taxi stand</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>train platform</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3498', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%97%E3%82%8A%E5%A0%B4', 11, 1, '2012-08-18T21:27:07.412416Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:26.684616Z'),
(3499, 'vocabulary', '使い方', '使い方', 'You''ve seen the word 使う hopefully by now. If you have, you know it means "to use." So, the <kanji>way</kanji> to <kanji>use</kanji> something is the <vocabulary>way of using</vocabulary> (aka <vocabulary>how to use</vocabulary>). This will be a word you see when you''re looking at directions for how to use something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3499', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%BF%E3%81%84%E6%96%B9', 11, 1, '2012-08-18T23:10:29.413139Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:26.708102Z'),
(3500, 'vocabulary', '仕事', '仕事', 'You know 事 means "occurrence," but it can also mean "thing." So when you''re <kanji>doing</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji>s all day, what are you probably doing? A <vocabulary>job</vocabulary>! All jobs really just amount to doing things.

Just like "job" in English, 仕事 can refer to a person''s specific <vocabulary>occupation</vocabulary>, to <vocabulary>work</vocabulary> in general, or to a particular task depending on the context.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3500', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%95%E4%BA%8B', 11, 1, '2012-08-18T23:28:56.24893Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:47:34.523008Z'),
(3501, 'vocabulary', '名', '名', 'This vocabulary word is the same as the ''name'' meaning of the kanji. If you can remember that, you''ll have no trouble remembering this word as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3501', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8D', 4, 1, '2012-08-18T23:36:39.104822Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(3502, 'vocabulary', '負け犬', '負け犬', 'You''ve hopefully come across 負ける by now, which means "to lose." So, you can figure out this word means "losing dog." Well, calling someone a dog isn''t so nice, so you''re probably just calling them a <vocabulary>loser</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3502', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A0%E3%81%91%E7%8A%AC', 11, 1, '2012-08-18T23:40:45.915117Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:26.773532Z'),
(3503, 'vocabulary', '待ちぼうけ', '待ちぼうけ', 'With <vocabulary>待ち</vocabulary> you know you''re waiting, but what''s a <vocabulary>ぼうけ</vocabulary>? Turns out it''s the long version of ボケ which means idiot. Basically, in this case you''re a waiting idiot. Why? because you were <vocabulary>stood up</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3503', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%85%E3%81%A1%E3%81%BC%E3%81%86%E3%81%91', 14, 1, '2012-08-19T04:58:12.316302Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:26.794246Z'),
(3504, 'vocabulary', '勝ち', '勝ち', 'You''ve hopefully seen 勝つ by now, which means "to win." This is a nouny version of that. So, what''s the noun version of "to win?" It''s <vocabulary>a win</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>victory</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3504', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%9D%E3%81%A1', 11, 1, '2012-08-19T05:20:32.186047Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:26.815192Z'),
(3505, 'vocabulary', '決定する', '決定する', 'A 決定 is a "decision." So, when you add する to it you''re making it a verb, meaning this word means <vocabulary>to decide</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3505', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%BA%E5%AE%9A%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-08-19T05:21:35.145687Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(3506, 'vocabulary', '出会う', '出会う', 'You <kanji>exit</kanji> your house and you <kanji>meet</kanji> someone. You didn’t mean to meet them, but it happened anyway. That’s why this word means <vocabulary>to meet by chance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3506', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E4%BC%9A%E3%81%86', 10, 1, '2012-08-21T18:34:02.676025Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.134847Z'),
(3507, 'vocabulary', '苦しむ', '苦しむ', 'You''ve already been introduced to the word 苦しい. That word means painful, or agonizing. So, if you turn that into a verb, you have <vocabulary>to suffer</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to struggle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3507', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%A6%E3%81%97%E3%82%80', 11, 1, '2012-08-21T19:29:43.942973Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:15:20.116546Z'),
(3508, 'vocabulary', '気付く', '気付く', 'When you <kanji>attach</kanji> your <kanji>energy</kanji> (imagine someone suddenly looking up at something, attaching their energy to it), you have noticed or realized something. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>to realize</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to notice</vocabulary>. 

Don''t confuse this with 気を付けて which is similar in that it has all the same kanji. It''s just used a little different. Try to remember the difference before moving on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3508', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E4%BB%98%E3%81%8F', 11, 1, '2012-08-21T19:31:20.182153Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:03:42.596356Z'),
(3509, 'vocabulary', '見送る', '見送る', 'You know the words 見る and 送る by now, hopefully. Here you''re "seeing" the "sending." You''re seeing someone off, so this verb is <vocabulary>to see off</vocabulary>. This can also mean <vocabulary>to put off</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to pass on</vocabulary>, since it''s as though you''re just passively seeing someone (or something) off rather than taking action and going with them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3509', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E9%80%81%E3%82%8B', 11, 1, '2012-08-21T19:40:03.846156Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:26:48.812324Z'),
(3510, 'vocabulary', '時々', '時々', 'You know the single word 時, right? It means <kanji>time</kanji>. This is that twice. So you have one time, and you have another time. They are separate. Things don''t happen in every of these times you have. They happen <vocabulary>sometimes</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3510', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%82%E3%80%85', 11, 1, '2012-08-21T19:49:30.655388Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(3511, 'vocabulary', '受付', '受付', 'Imagine someone <kanji>accept</kanji>ing something and then <kanji>attach</kanji>ing something. This person is a <vocabulary>receptionist</vocabulary>, because they are accepting the call, writing down the information, and then attaching said information to a sticky note for you to read. You say, "No, I''m not available."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3511', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%97%E4%BB%98', 11, 1, '2012-08-21T19:56:02.543641Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:48.601574Z'),
(3512, 'vocabulary', '買い物', '買い物', 'You''ve hopefully seen 買う by now and know it means "to buy." So, 買い is going to be similar to that. Add that to <kanji>thing</kanji> and you have a "buying things" sort of word. What does "buying things" describe? It describes <vocabulary>shopping</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3512', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%B7%E3%81%84%E7%89%A9', 11, 1, '2012-08-21T20:02:46.564602Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(3513, 'vocabulary', '苦手', '苦手', 'A "suffering hand" is a little misleading. Think more in terms of the words 苦い (bitter) and 手 (person... remember how that happens sometimes?). Now think of this word like you''re bitter about having to work with someone. Why? Because they are extremely <vocabulary>hard to deal with</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3513', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%A6%E6%89%8B', 11, 1, '2012-08-21T20:09:06.858029Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:15:20.116546Z'),
(3514, 'vocabulary', '白黒', '白黒', 'Something is both <kanji>white</kanji> and <kanji>black</kanji>. It is <vocabulary>black and white</vocabulary> (you can also say <vocabulary>white and black</vocabulary> if you prefer).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3514', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BD%E9%BB%92', 9, 1, '2012-08-21T23:39:52.579655Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.407293Z'),
(3515, 'vocabulary', '酒好き', '酒好き', 'We have <kanji>alcohol</kanji> and 好き (like). When you like alcohol a lot, you''re probably a <vocabulary>drinker</vocabulary>, or at least an <vocabulary>alcohol lover</vocabulary>.

酒好き literally means "someone who likes alcohol," and while they may drink a lot, the focus is on their enjoyment rather than excess. Compared to 酒飲み, it sounds friendlier and less critical, especially when you add the polite お, as in お酒好き.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3515', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%92%E5%A5%BD%E3%81%8D', 13, 1, '2012-08-23T18:02:35.614681Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:12:01.151581Z'),
(3516, 'vocabulary', '酒飲み', '酒飲み', 'We have <kanji>alcohol</kanji> and the noun form of 飲む (to drink). So you know this has to do with drinking alcohol. It means a <vocabulary>boozer</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>drunkard</vocabulary>, someone who drinks a lot of alcohol!

酒飲み literally means "alcohol drinker," and it usually implies that the person consumes a lot of alcohol. This is a casual word and it can sound playful (like teasing a friend) or mildly critical depending on the situation and tone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3516', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%92%E9%A3%B2%E3%81%BF', 12, 1, '2012-08-23T18:03:10.01017Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:09:36.956914Z'),
(3517, 'vocabulary', '鳴き声', '鳴き声', 'The <kanji>chirp</kanji> <kanji>voice</kanji> is a sound an animal makes. Sure, it works for birds (which chirp), but it also works with most other animals as well. So, we call this an <vocabulary>animal cry</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3517', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%B3%B4%E3%81%8D%E5%A3%B0', 12, 1, '2012-08-23T18:07:13.556518Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:18:22.908078Z'),
(3518, 'vocabulary', '飲み物', '飲み物', 'A <kanji>thing</kanji> you <kanji>drink</kanji> is a <vocabulary>beverage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3518', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%B2%E3%81%BF%E7%89%A9', 12, 1, '2012-08-23T18:13:50.011101Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(3519, 'vocabulary', '聞こえる', '聞こえる', 'You''ve seen 聞く by now hopefully. This is similar, but instead of meaning "to hear" it means <vocabulary>to be hearable</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be audible</vocabulary>. To remember this, just think about how you can hear a こえ (voice) coming from the middle of the word 聞こえる. It''s audible to you too, right?

聞こえる is used when sounds reach your ears on their own, like "I can hear music from next door." It''s more about what comes to your ears, not what you''re trying to listen to. That''s why it also means <vocabulary>to be able to hear</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3519', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%81%9E%E3%81%93%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2012-08-23T18:17:00.991774Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:08:56.80488Z'),
(3520, 'vocabulary', '心配事', '心配事', 'Remember how 心配 means "a worry" and 事 can mean "thing"? Well, if you have a worry over some specific thing, then you probably have some <vocabulary>cares</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>worries</vocabulary>. What are your 心配事 these days?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3520', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83%E9%85%8D%E4%BA%8B', 12, 1, '2012-08-23T18:19:42.682242Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:47:34.523008Z'),
(3521, 'vocabulary', '音読み', '音読み', 'The <kanji>sound</kanji> <kanji>read</kanji>ing. What could this be...? Wait a sec, think to Japanese and kanji. What''s the reading that uses sounds from another place, specifically China? That''s the <vocabulary>Chinese reading</vocabulary>, aka the <vocabulary>on''yomi</vocabulary>. Sounds familiar?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3521', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9F%B3%E8%AA%AD%E3%81%BF', 12, 1, '2012-08-23T18:21:02.986847Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.201091Z'),
(3522, 'vocabulary', '東', '東', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3522', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%B1', 6, 1, '2012-08-23T18:43:24.039776Z', NULL, '2025-10-12T17:05:31.475745Z'),
(3523, 'vocabulary', '返す', '返す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>return</kanji> so the verb version means <vocabulary>to return something</vocabulary>. 

This is more like "to return a thing" rather than returning home, which would be 帰る, a word you haven''t seen yet (but will soon, I''m sure!).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3523', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%94%E3%81%99', 9, 1, '2012-08-27T17:20:19.627157Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.420966Z'),
(3524, 'vocabulary', '想像する', '想像する', 'To <kanji>imagine</kanji> some sort of an <kanji>image</kanji> in your mind simply means <vocabulary>to imagine</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3524', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%B3%E5%83%8F%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:26:39.044811Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.264476Z'),
(3525, 'vocabulary', '映像', '映像', 'You see <kanji>reflect</kanji>ions of <kanji>image</kanji>s on a screen, and the image reflections are moving! That means this is <vocabulary>footage</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>video</vocabulary>.

映像 refers to <vocabulary>image</vocabulary>s in various forms of visual media. While it''s most commonly used to describe moving images in videos or films, it can also refer to mental images — those pictures or scenes that come to mind, reflecting personal memories or imagination.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3525', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%A0%E5%83%8F', 9, 1, '2012-08-28T08:26:46.800976Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.107022Z'),
(3526, 'vocabulary', '画像', '画像', 'An <kanji>image</kanji> of an <kanji>image</kanji> is still an <vocabulary>image</vocabulary>. This can also be a <vocabulary>picture</vocabulary>.

画像 can refer to all sorts of images and pictures, but these days it''s mostly used for the digital kind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3526', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%BB%E5%83%8F', 9, 1, '2012-08-28T08:26:54.903937Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.122238Z'),
(3527, 'vocabulary', '一億', '一億', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>hundred million</kanji> is <vocabulary>one hundred million</vocabulary>. That''s a lot of zeros.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3527', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%84%84', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:27:05.098355Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.361755Z'),
(3528, 'vocabulary', '一億円', '一億円', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>hundred million</kanji> <kanji>yen</kanji>! So rich! You have <vocabulary>one hundred million yen</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3528', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%84%84%E5%86%86', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:27:12.381865Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:10:33.22049Z'),
(3529, 'vocabulary', '億', '億', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3529', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%84%84', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:27:19.165408Z', NULL, '2025-07-23T15:57:04.152647Z'),
(3530, 'vocabulary', '問題', '問題', 'The <kanji>problem</kanji> <kanji>topic</kanji> is the <vocabulary>problem</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>question</vocabulary> you have to answer. If you think about it, topics as problems tends to be a school-related thing. So, when you have to answer a problem in school, that''s 問題.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3530', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%95%8F%E9%A1%8C', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:27:25.105597Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:18:01.665189Z'),
(3531, 'vocabulary', '疑問', '疑問', 'A <kanji>doubt</kanji> <kanji>problem</kanji> is caused by having too much <vocabulary>doubt</vocabulary>. Just think about it. Don''t doubt yourself, it''s why you''re getting those vocab words wrong! :P', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3531', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%91%E5%95%8F', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:27:31.213336Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:46:31.302923Z'),
(3532, 'vocabulary', '器', '器', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. 

器 refers to a <vocabulary>container</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>bowl</vocabulary> and is often used in culinary contexts. It can also be used metaphorically to describe someone''s <vocabulary>capacity</vocabulary> for certain things. For instance, when someone has a small 器, it means they have a limited capacity to handle pressure, stress, or difficulties.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3532', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%99%A8', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:27:56.966627Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.483501Z'),
(3533, 'vocabulary', '器用', '器用', 'Your <kanji>task</kanji> is to open this pickle <kanji>container</kanji>. Why you? Because you are so <vocabulary>dextrous</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>skillful</vocabulary> when it comes to opening pickles.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3533', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%99%A8%E7%94%A8', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:28:04.597899Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.505841Z'),
(3534, 'vocabulary', '楽器', '楽器', 'Remember how 楽 was used in 音楽 and meant "music"? Well this is that 楽 again. A <kanji>music</kanji> <kanji>container</kanji> is something that can produce music. It is a <vocabulary>musical instrument</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3534', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%BD%E5%99%A8', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:28:18.668968Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:41:32.539332Z'),
(3535, 'vocabulary', '食器', '食器', 'Your <kanji>eat</kanji>ing <kanji>container</kanji>s refer to <vocabulary>tableware</vocabulary>, including <vocabulary>eating utensils</vocabulary>, such as knives, forks, spoons, and chopsticks, as well as <vocabulary>serving utensils</vocabulary>, like plates, bowls, and saucers. Basically, it''s stuff you use to eat your food.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3535', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9F%E5%99%A8', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:28:27.901032Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.550048Z'),
(3536, 'vocabulary', '力士', '力士', 'A <kanji>power</kanji> <kanji>samurai</kanji> isn''t a samurai at all. A Samurai''s all about precision, after all. So, imagine someone that''s a huge mass of muscle (and fat). This Japanese warrior is all about power... ramming into people as hard as they can to knock them over. This person is a <vocabulary>sumo wrestler</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3536', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%9B%E5%A3%AB', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:28:42.893736Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(3537, 'vocabulary', '宿題', '宿題', 'The <kanji>lodge</kanji> you''re staying in has a <kanji>topic</kanji> around it. Think of it as a theme. This theme is doing your <vocabulary>homework</vocabulary>. So yeah, you just went to a nice lodge for vacation only to have to do homework everywhere.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3537', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%BF%E9%A1%8C', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:28:49.978778Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:18:01.665189Z'),
(3538, 'vocabulary', '新宿', '新宿', 'The <kanji>new</kanji> <kanji>lodge</kanji> area is <vocabulary>Shinjuku</vocabulary>, a popular district in Tokyo. Perhaps there are many places to stay in Shinjuku. Either way, you should visit, it''s fun! It will help if you''ve heard of Shinjuku before, too. Do some research about Shinjuku if you''ve never heard of it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3538', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E5%AE%BF', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:28:56.079411Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.615446Z'),
(3539, 'vocabulary', '事情', '事情', '<kanji>Occurrence</kanji>s and <kanji>feeling</kanji>s make up your <vocabulary>circumstances</vocabulary>. 

事情 is used to describe the <vocabulary>reason</vocabulary>s or <vocabulary>situation</vocabulary> behind a particular event or action. If you can''t go to a party, for example, you might explain your 事情 by saying that something came up or you aren''t feeling well. And if your coworker is expecting a baby, you might consider her 事情 and help reduce her workload.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3539', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8B%E6%83%85', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:29:02.006623Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:47:34.523008Z'),
(3540, 'vocabulary', '人情', '人情', '<kanji>Person</kanji> <kanji>feeling</kanji>s are the <vocabulary>human feelings</vocabulary> every person has. They''re the <vocabulary>humanity</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>kindness</vocabulary> you feel toward others. 

人情 is often used in literature to describe the warmth found in human interactions and the heartfelt bonds they create. It literally means natural human emotions, but it''s really about the kindness, compassion, and empathy people show toward one another.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3540', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E6%83%85', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:29:08.197403Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3541, 'vocabulary', '情け', '情け', 'You have your <kanji>feeling</kanji>s... these are the feelings humans have. When you ask someone to have feelings (Have feelings, <reading>KAY</reading>?? (け at the end)), you are asking them to <vocabulary>pity</vocabulary> or have <vocabulary>mercy</vocabulary> on you. Please! Have those human feelings that people have, like <vocabulary>sympathy</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>pity</vocabulary>. Don''t kill me, man!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3541', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%85%E3%81%91', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:29:15.313709Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.68402Z'),
(3542, 'vocabulary', '感情', '感情', '<kanji>Feeling</kanji>, and more <kanji>feeling</kanji>… You''re full of <vocabulary>emotion</vocabulary>! (Unsurprisingly, this can also mean <vocabulary>feeling</vocabulary>.)

感情 is a more formal, objective-sounding word for human emotions — things like joy, anger, sadness, fear. Compared to 気持ち, it feels less personal and more like you''re talking about emotion as a category or state.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3542', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E6%83%85', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:29:40.296462Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:46:33.273915Z'),
(3543, 'vocabulary', '予想', '予想', 'If you consider the future <kanji>beforehand</kanji> and <kanji>imagine</kanji> how things will turn out (based on some facts), you will have an informed <vocabulary>expectation</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>prediction</vocabulary> for it.

予想 refers to an expectation or prediction about the future based on current information or trends, like predicting which candidate will win an upcoming election or estimating future economic conditions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3543', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%88%E6%83%B3', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:29:47.489085Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.728375Z'),
(3544, 'vocabulary', '回想', '回想', 'The past events <kanji>revolve</kanji> around your mind as you <kanji>imagine</kanji> them. That''s a <vocabulary>reminiscence</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>recollection</vocabulary> of those times.

回想 refers to the reminiscence or recollection of past experiences or events. It''s often used to describe flashback scenes in movies or dramas, as well as moments in literature where characters think back on their past.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3544', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9E%E6%83%B3', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:29:53.354174Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.751788Z'),
(3545, 'vocabulary', '思想', '思想', 'When you <kanji>think</kanji> and <kanji>contemplate</kanji> deeply, you''re producing something larger, like an <vocabulary>ideology</vocabulary>. This means having a group of, or a single larger, <vocabulary>thought</vocabulary>(s) or <vocabulary>idea</vocabulary>(s).

思想 refers to the big ideas or beliefs an individual holds about the world and life. It’s often used to talk about one’s thoughts about philosophy, politics, religion, or other such beliefs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3545', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%9D%E6%83%B3', 13, 1, '2012-08-28T08:29:59.289513Z', NULL, '2025-12-23T01:37:53.714722Z'),
(3546, 'vocabulary', '五感', '五感', 'The <kanji>five</kanji> <kanji>feeling</kanji>s that you have are what? Sight, sound, taste, touch, and smell (don''t count the sixth one where you see dead people). These are <vocabulary>the five senses</vocabulary> that you probably have.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3546', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%94%E6%84%9F', 19, 1, '2012-08-28T08:30:11.637846Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(3547, 'vocabulary', '同感', '同感', 'When you have the <kanji>same</kanji> <kanji>feeling</kanji> as someone else, you are in <vocabulary>agreement</vocabulary> with them. They have an opinion or feeling about something, and you''re <vocabulary>feeling the same</vocabulary> way.

同感 is a word used to express agreement, often with a sense of shared feeling. You use it when you hear a thought or viewpoint that really resonates with you — you totally agree with this person''s take and feel exactly the same way. Preach!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3547', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8C%E6%84%9F', 20, 1, '2012-08-28T08:30:19.516594Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(3548, 'vocabulary', '飲み会', '飲み会', 'You know that 飲む is drink, so this has to do with <kanji>drink</kanji>ing. Also, 会 means <kanji>meet</kanji>, so when you meet to drink you''re getting together with others to drink. It''s a <vocabulary>drinking party</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3548', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%B2%E3%81%BF%E4%BC%9A', 13, 1, '2012-09-04T22:30:55.099835Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.846448Z'),
(3549, 'vocabulary', '運転手', '運転手', 'You know that <vocabulary>運転</vocabulary> has to do with driving, right? And you know that <kanji>手</kanji> often has to do with a person doing something in words like these. So, a person who does driving is what? A <vocabulary>driver</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3549', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8B%E8%BB%A2%E6%89%8B', 12, 1, '2012-09-04T22:32:12.268092Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.870503Z'),
(3550, 'vocabulary', '落ち葉', '落ち葉', 'The <kanji>leaf</kanji> that <kanji>fall</kanji>s are <vocabulary>fallen leaves</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3550', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%90%BD%E3%81%A1%E8%91%89', 12, 1, '2012-09-04T22:44:45.425347Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:10:47.677773Z'),
(3551, 'vocabulary', '父親', '父親', 'Your <kanji>father</kanji> <kanji>parent</kanji> is your <vocabulary>father</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3551', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%88%B6%E8%A6%AA', 12, 1, '2012-09-04T22:46:45.966872Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:01:24.564442Z'),
(3552, 'vocabulary', '母親', '母親', 'The <kanji>mother</kanji> <kanji>parent</kanji> is the <vocabulary>mother</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3552', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%8D%E8%A6%AA', 12, 1, '2012-09-05T08:51:45.108466Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:09.145042Z'),
(3553, 'vocabulary', '三番目', '三番目', 'Remember 一番目? This is just like that, but with three, making it <vocabulary>the third thing</vocabulary> or simply <vocabulary>the third</vocabulary>.

Again, think about your eyeball collection. This time, you''re showing someone the third eyeball in your display case. It just happens to be third in line, but honestly it''s just as beautiful as the two that come before it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3553', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E7%95%AA%E7%9B%AE', 12, 1, '2012-09-05T19:16:13.915232Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.9583Z'),
(3554, 'vocabulary', '見返る', '見返る', 'Your <kanji>see</kanji>ing is <kanji>return</kanji>ing on something. This verb can be both transitive and intransitive. When it’s transitive, it means <vocabulary>to look back at</vocabulary>, in the sense of looking back at someone who’s behind you, or looking back at your past. When it’s intransitive, it simply means <vocabulary>to look back</vocabulary>, in the sense of looking over your shoulder.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3554', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E8%BF%94%E3%82%8B', 13, 1, '2012-09-05T19:26:44.903597Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:27.978612Z'),
(3555, 'vocabulary', '話し方', '話し方', 'Your <kanji>speak</kanji>ing <kanji>way</kanji> is… your <vocabulary>way of speaking</vocabulary>, or your <vocabulary>speaking style</vocabulary>. This includes your tone of voice, speed, and even how you use gestures when talking. It''s more about the whole way you communicate, not just the wording or how you phrase things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3555', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%B1%E3%81%97%E6%96%B9', 12, 1, '2012-09-05T19:32:25.884272Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.000649Z'),
(3556, 'vocabulary', '転ぶ', '転ぶ', 'Do you remember the word 転がる? This is kind of similar, instead of rolling, this is more for when something falls and hits the ground. It means <vocabulary>to fall over</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to fall down</vocabulary>. Just imagine someone falling off a chair face first into the dirt and making a ぶ sound.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3556', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%A2%E3%81%B6', 12, 1, '2012-09-05T19:53:24.963455Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.026249Z'),
(3557, 'vocabulary', '集まる', '集まる', 'I bet you remember 集める means "to collect something." But this time you''re not collecting something. Instead, something just… collects. In other words, 集まる means <vocabulary>to come together</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to gather</vocabulary>. Friends come together, or fans gather at a venue — that sort of thing. No direct object needed, since this is intransitive.

Verbs ending in okurigana that rhyme with ある tend to be intransitive like this. You can remember this because if something is ある, it''s just there, existing, not acting <em>on</em> anything else. That''s why verbs that sound like it are often intransitive too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3557', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%86%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2012-09-05T23:50:57.551515Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:23.933062Z'),
(3558, 'vocabulary', '落とす', '落とす', 'You''ve seen how 落ちる means "to fall down." Now you''re doing the action of "falling something down," aka you''re dropping it. You can remember this because you kind of <reading>toss</reading> (とす) something and it drops to the ground, making this word mean <vocabulary>to drop something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3558', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%90%BD%E3%81%A8%E3%81%99', 12, 1, '2012-09-05T23:53:31.773263Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:29:25.34244Z'),
(3559, 'vocabulary', '終わり', '終わり', 'By now you should know that 終わる means "to end" or "to finish." So what is this noun-version of that word? It would be <vocabulary>the end</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>the finish</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3559', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%82%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8A', 12, 1, '2012-09-06T04:26:14.732087Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.139231Z'),
(3560, 'vocabulary', '終電', '終電', 'We''ve seen a few times where 電 refers to a train. They''re big deals, after all, and use a ton of electricity. So, the <kanji>end</kanji> train for this word would refer to the <vocabulary>last train</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3560', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%82%E9%9B%BB', 12, 1, '2012-09-06T06:52:34.433293Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.030484Z'),
(3561, 'vocabulary', '開く', '開く', 'You should know 開ける by now. This word is similar to that, except it has to do with when things are just open. It''s the same thing, though. The meaning is also <vocabulary>to open</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3561', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%8B%E3%81%8F', 12, 1, '2012-09-06T09:21:05.109654Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.199389Z'),
(3562, 'vocabulary', '起こる', '起こる', 'You know that 起きる means "to wake up", but did you learn its secondary meaning, "to occur"? Well, this word means only <vocabulary>to occur</vocabulary>. Just imagine an incident occurring involving a uranium <reading>core</reading> (こる) to remember that this means <vocabulary>to occur</vocabulary>. After all, uranium core incidents don''t wake up, do they?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3562', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%B7%E3%81%93%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2012-09-06T09:23:54.028388Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.221895Z'),
(3563, 'vocabulary', '心配する', '心配する', 'You know that 心配 is "a worry." What about the verb version of that? It would be <vocabulary>to worry</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3563', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83%E9%85%8D%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2012-09-06T09:37:24.784143Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.243923Z'),
(3564, 'vocabulary', '心配', '心配', 'When you <kanji>distribute</kanji> your <kanji>heart</kanji> around you, it''s like your heart is everywhere at once, but not in your body. You feel frantic, you don''t know what to do because your heart is out there. This represents the feeling you have when you have <vocabulary>a worry</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>concern</vocabulary>. 心配しないでね！', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3564', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83%E9%85%8D', 10, 1, '2012-09-06T09:37:55.59125Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.145546Z'),
(3565, 'vocabulary', '鳴る', '鳴る', 'You learned 鳴く means "to chirp" (or some other animal sound). 鳴る is similar, but it''s used when something just makes a sound, like a phone ringing, a bell jingling, or thunder rumbling. So 鳴る means <vocabulary>to sound</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to ring</vocabulary>. You know this because 鳴る can also be used for when your stomach rumbles: "<reading>rrrr</reading>" (る)!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3565', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%B3%B4%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2012-09-06T18:24:39.842086Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:02:01.837869Z'),
(3566, 'vocabulary', '歌う', '歌う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. One of the kanji''s meanings is <kanji>sing</kanji> so the verb vocab version of this is <vocabulary>to sing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3566', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%8C%E3%81%86', 12, 1, '2012-09-06T18:31:08.909171Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:03:31.014085Z'),
(3567, 'vocabulary', '勝手', '勝手', 'The <kanji>hand</kanji> (or person, as you know 手 often refers to a person) that <kanji>win</kanji>s gets to do <vocabulary>as one pleases</vocabulary> without regard for anyone else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3567', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%9D%E6%89%8B', 12, 1, '2012-09-06T19:12:58.54247Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.340452Z'),
(3568, 'vocabulary', '初めに', '初めに', 'You know 初 means <kanji>first</kanji>, and に can mark a point in time. So 初めに means <vocabulary>at first</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>in the beginning</vocabulary>. 

初めに has a lot of overlap with 始めに. But while 始めに is for the beginning of some larger process, 初めに refers more to doing something first or to the initial point of a certain time frame. You might say 初めにアクビをしたのは私です (I was the one who yawned first), for example, or 今年の初めに (at the start of this year).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3568', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%9D%E3%82%81%E3%81%AB', 13, 1, '2012-09-06T19:18:06.828818Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:28:35.812439Z'),
(3569, 'vocabulary', '初めて', '初めて', 'You know that 初 means "first" This word means <vocabulary>for the first time</vocabulary>. Think of めて as "meet." Then think "when we met <vocabulary>for the first time</vocabulary> ____ happened."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3569', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%9D%E3%82%81%E3%81%A6', 13, 1, '2012-09-06T19:42:36.654739Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:28:35.812439Z'),
(3570, 'vocabulary', '神風', '神風', '<kanji>Wind</kanji> of the <kanji>gods</kanji>. These are <vocabulary>divine winds</vocabulary>. More popularly, this is a word known as <vocabulary>kamikaze</vocabulary>, which became known because of the kamikaze pilots during WWII. This was used before WWII, though, when the entire Mongol fleet was destroyed by a storm, preventing an invasion of Japan. How lucky.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3570', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%9E%E9%A2%A8', 13, 1, '2012-09-06T19:52:59.843335Z', NULL, '2025-07-01T19:41:45.398894Z'),
(3571, 'vocabulary', '手伝う', '手伝う', 'When you <kanji>transmit</kanji> your <kanji>hand</kanji> to something or someone, you''re putting out your hand <vocabulary>to help</vocabulary> them or <vocabulary>to assist</vocabulary> them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3571', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E4%BC%9D%E3%81%86', 13, 1, '2012-09-07T05:22:16.959828Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.436683Z'),
(3572, 'vocabulary', '左利き', '左利き', 'If you <kanji>profit</kanji> from the <kanji>left</kanji>, you''re probably doing everything on the left. That''s your good side. You''re probably <vocabulary>left handed</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3572', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A6%E5%88%A9%E3%81%8D', 13, 1, '2012-09-07T05:36:33.463931Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.458308Z'),
(3573, 'vocabulary', '体育', '体育', 'Remember how 教育 means "education?" Well, the 育 here is referring to the education word, but this time it''s a <kanji>body</kanji> education. What do you call the body education at school? <vocabulary>Physical education</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>PE</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3573', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%93%E8%82%B2', 13, 1, '2012-09-07T19:19:56.216575Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.480447Z'),
(3574, 'vocabulary', '右利き', '右利き', 'The <kanji>right</kanji> side of your body is the side you <kanji>profit</kanji> most from. So, you use it a lot more. That''s because you''re <vocabulary>right handed</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3574', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B3%E5%88%A9%E3%81%8D', 13, 1, '2012-09-07T19:33:34.00468Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.501198Z'),
(3575, 'vocabulary', '好む', '好む', 'You know that 好き is "like." Although 好き is kind of like a verb already, this is definitely a verb. It can be <vocabulary>to be fond of</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to like</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3575', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%BD%E3%82%80', 15, 1, '2012-09-07T20:55:38.14627Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.485238Z'),
(3576, 'vocabulary', '育てる', '育てる', 'This is similar to 育つ, except now you''re doing the raising of someone. This word means <vocabulary>to bring up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to raise</vocabulary>. You can remember this because of the てる. Your version of bringing a child up involves <reading>tear</reading>ing (てる) all their clothes to make them look poor so they can get the cheaper lunch at school. You''re rich because of all the tax breaks you get from all the children you''re raising, but you could always use more money to fuel your StarCraft addiction (we''ve all been there).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3576', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%B2%E3%81%A6%E3%82%8B', 13, 1, '2012-09-12T16:58:28.838253Z', NULL, '2026-01-09T21:16:37.164114Z'),
(3577, 'vocabulary', '追い付く', '追い付く', 'To <kanji>follow</kanji> then <kanji>attach</kanji> yourself to whoever you''re following is <vocabulary>to catch up with</vocabulary> someone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3577', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%BD%E3%81%84%E4%BB%98%E3%81%8F', 13, 1, '2012-09-12T16:59:18.305265Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:03:42.596356Z'),
(3578, 'vocabulary', '戦う', '戦う', 'This is the verb version of the vocab word <vocabulary>戦い</vocabulary>, which I''m hoping you learned earlier. The verb version of course means <vocabulary>to fight</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to battle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3578', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%A6%E3%81%86', 13, 1, '2012-09-12T17:18:25.311777Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.595058Z'),
(3579, 'vocabulary', '追いかける', '追いかける', '追う means "to follow" or “to chase” and かける in this case means “to run,” so this word means <vocabulary>to chase</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to pursue</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to run after</vocabulary> someone or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3579', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%BD%E3%81%84%E3%81%8B%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 13, 1, '2012-09-12T17:20:11.627731Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.617232Z'),
(3580, 'vocabulary', '注文する', '注文する', 'You hopefully have seen the word 注文 (order, request). This is just the verb version: <vocabulary>to order</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>to request</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3580', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A8%E6%96%87%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 13, 1, '2012-09-12T17:49:22.869371Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.640878Z'),
(3581, 'vocabulary', '大好き', '大好き', 'You''ve hopefully seen 好き by now, which is "to like." So, what happens when you have a 大 (big) like? You <vocabulary>really like</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>like a lot</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>love</vocabulary> something.

This is the same as 好き in that a more literal translation would be <vocabulary>lovable</vocabulary>, but it''s generally translated as a verb in English.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3581', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%A5%BD%E3%81%8D', 13, 1, '2012-09-12T17:59:27.009634Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(3582, 'vocabulary', '伝わる', '伝わる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji means <kanji>transmit</kanji>, and this verb version is <vocabulary>to be transmitted</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be conveyed</vocabulary>. It''s intransitive, so it doesn''t take a direct object. A message is transmitted, or your feelings are conveyed — that kind of thing.

Verbs ending in okurigana that rhyme with ある tend to be intransitive like this. You can remember this because if something is ある, it''s just there, existing, not acting <em>on</em> anything else. That''s why verbs that sound like it are often intransitive too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3582', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9D%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2012-09-12T19:26:32.344695Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:23.964594Z'),
(3583, 'vocabulary', '放れる', '放れる', 'Remember how 放す is "to release"? Now you''re learning the intransitive version. Instead of you doing the releasing, something gets released or freed. So this is <vocabulary>to get loose</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to escape</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to be released</vocabulary>.

放れる describes something held in place coming loose and starting to move, like an animal breaking free from its tether or an arrow being released from a bowstring. You know this because it''s pretty rare (れる) for something that''s restrained to break free from its bonds. Escaping isn''t easy, so it only happens on rare occasions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3583', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BE%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 13, 1, '2012-09-12T19:32:16.521636Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T02:08:38.914766Z'),
(3584, 'vocabulary', '待合室', '待合室', 'A <kanji>room</kanji> where you <kanji>join</kanji> other people to <kanji>wait</kanji>. This is a <vocabulary>waiting room</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3584', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%85%E5%90%88%E5%AE%A4', 16, 1, '2012-09-12T19:36:30.567886Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:57:32.391485Z'),
(3585, 'vocabulary', '感じ', '感じ', 'This is a noun version of the kanji <kanji>feeling</kanji>. What is that? It''s a <vocabulary>feeling</vocabulary> as well.

感じ is a casual word that talks about a general feeling, <vocabulary>impression</vocabulary>, or vibe you get from something. You''ll hear it everywhere — いい感じ (nice vibe), そんな感じ (something like that), or どんな感じ? (what''s it like?).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3585', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E3%81%98', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T17:56:32.666085Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:49:00.437761Z'),
(3586, 'vocabulary', '感じる', '感じる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>feeling</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to feel</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3586', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E3%81%98%E3%82%8B', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T17:57:07.407272Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(3587, 'vocabulary', '感動する', '感動する', 'You know this is a verb from the する. Your <kanji>feeling</kanji>s are getting <kanji>move</kanji>d, so it means <vocabulary>to be emotionally moved</vocabulary> — describing how you''re deeply affected by something. In English, this is sometimes phrased as <vocabulary>to be moving</vocabulary>, referring to the emotional impact something has, like a heartfelt movie.

感動する is used whenever something reaches you on a deep, genuine level, like when you''re moved by an emotional speech, a touching story, or a stunning view.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3587', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E5%8B%95%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2012-09-18T17:57:18.1407Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.976646Z'),
(3588, 'vocabulary', '感心', '感心', 'Your <kanji>feeling</kanji>s are in your <kanji>heart</kanji>. When your feelings are in your heart they are pure feelings. You can be happy for people and feel <vocabulary>admiration</vocabulary> for their successes. This is a great feeling to have in your heart.

感心 expresses that you are <vocabulary>being impressed</vocabulary> by someone''s effort, skill, or good behavior. You''ll hear it when someone does something <vocabulary>praiseworthy</vocabulary> and you genuinely admire it, like 感心した (I’m impressed).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3588', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E5%BF%83', 21, 1, '2012-09-18T17:57:27.313644Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(3589, 'vocabulary', '感想', '感想', 'The <kanji>feeling</kanji>s you <kanji>contemplate</kanji> about a subject are your <vocabulary>impressions</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>thoughts</vocabulary> on it.

感想 is all about what you think or feel after experiencing something, like after reading a book, watching a movie, or going to an event.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3589', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E6%83%B3', 15, 1, '2012-09-18T17:57:43.574115Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.276316Z'),
(3590, 'vocabulary', '感謝', '感謝', 'You have <kanji>feeling</kanji>s of <kanji>apologizing</kanji>. Strangely, this word means <vocabulary>thanks</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>gratitude</vocabulary>, though. Perhaps someone did something so nice for you that you want to apologize for how many nice things they''re doing for you. You are this grateful to them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3590', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E8%AC%9D', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T17:57:51.075334Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(3591, 'vocabulary', '整然', '整然', 'Something with an <kanji>arrange</kanji>d <kanji>nature</kanji> must be very <vocabulary>orderly</vocabulary>. Nicely arranged, very tidy and orderly!

Note that for this word, you need to add と to the end to make it an adverb, and たる to make it an adjective! It''s quite formal, so you''ll mostly just see it in literature to describe something that''s been arranged in an orderly way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3591', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B4%E7%84%B6', 36, 1, '2012-09-18T17:59:29.76244Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.893237Z'),
(3592, 'vocabulary', '入館料', '入館料', 'The <kanji>fee</kanji> for <kanji>enter</kanji>ing a <kanji>public building</kanji> is the <vocabulary>admission fee</vocabulary>. Gotta pay it if you want to get inside.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3592', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E9%A4%A8%E6%96%99', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T17:59:45.705003Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.917163Z'),
(3593, 'vocabulary', '料金', '料金', 'The <kanji>fee</kanji> <kanji>gold</kanji> (or money) you have to pay is a <vocabulary>fee</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>charge</vocabulary>.

You''ve learned 代金, which generally refers to the money paid for physical goods, but 料金 refers to a fee or charge for using a service — think bus fares, highway tolls, entrance fees, monthly service charges, that kind of thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3593', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%99%E9%87%91', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T17:59:53.47162Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:28.939836Z'),
(3594, 'vocabulary', '映る', '映る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>reflect</kanji>. The verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to be reflected</vocabulary>. You''re not doing the reflecting directly. You can remember this by thinking how rude (る) it is that someone''s reflecting your image using a mirror for everyone outside the dressing room to see.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3594', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%A0%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-09-18T18:00:09.854013Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.599976Z'),
(3595, 'vocabulary', '映画', '映画', 'It''s a <kanji>reflect</kanji>ion of an <kanji>image</kanji> on a screen, and the image is moving! What is this? It''s a <vocabulary>movie</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3595', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%A0%E7%94%BB', 7, 1, '2012-09-18T18:00:43.982506Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:31.885703Z'),
(3596, 'vocabulary', '映画館', '映画館', 'You''ve already learned 映画 means "movie." A <kanji>public building</kanji> for movies is a <vocabulary>movie theater</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3596', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%A0%E7%94%BB%E9%A4%A8', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:00:58.34406Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.058203Z'),
(3597, 'vocabulary', '暗い', '暗い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>dark</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>dark</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3597', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%97%E3%81%84', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:01:07.246518Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T02:01:16.682053Z'),
(3598, 'vocabulary', '暗殺', '暗殺', 'A <kanji>dark</kanji> <kanji>kill</kanji> is an <vocabulary>assassination</vocabulary>. It''s done by someone who is in a ninja outfit and dark, hiding in the shadows for his moment to strike.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3598', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%97%E6%AE%BA', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:01:15.325738Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T02:01:16.682053Z'),
(3599, 'vocabulary', '暗記', '暗記', 'In the <kanji>dark</kanji> if you try to <kanji>write down</kanji> something it won''t work very well. You can''t see. So, how can you "write something down" in the dark? You write it down in your mind. This is <vocabulary>memorization</vocabulary>! Recognize this word? The SRS application "Anki" comes from this word, I''m guessing!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3599', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%97%E8%A8%98', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:01:22.973604Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T02:01:16.682053Z'),
(3600, 'vocabulary', '〜様', '〜様', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

You can add this onto the end of someone''s name. It''s probably too polite, to be honest, unless you''re talking to one of your customers or something. It''s good to know, but you''re probably not going to use it much, despite seeing and hearing it fairly often.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3600', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%A7%98', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:01:32.565514Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.230043Z'),
(3601, 'vocabulary', '様々', '様々', 'This one''s kinda tricky. You learned 様 is a "formal name ender," but it can also mean "manner," as in the manner you do something in. So if you''ve got multiple manners, you''ve got lots of <vocabulary>various</vocabulary> ways of doing things!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3601', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A7%98%E3%80%85', 15, 1, '2012-09-18T18:01:40.705177Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.545291Z'),
(3602, 'vocabulary', '皆様', '皆様', '<kanji>Everyone</kanji> (<kanji>formal name ender</kanji>). This is just a polite way to say <vocabulary>everyone</vocabulary> (and you want to be polite to everyone, because everyone &gt; you).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3602', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%86%E6%A7%98', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T18:01:49.42422Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.316877Z'),
(3603, 'vocabulary', '目標', '目標', 'The <kanji>signpost</kanji> you have your <kanji>eye</kanji> on is your <vocabulary>goal</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>target</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>objective</vocabulary>. Just imagine looking at this signpost in the distance. You know you have to get to it from where you are. That is the goal of your journey.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3603', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%AE%E6%A8%99', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:02:00.071587Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.363601Z'),
(3604, 'vocabulary', '橋', '橋', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3604', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A9%8B', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:02:07.393803Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.423019Z'),
(3605, 'vocabulary', '殺す', '殺す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>kill</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to kill</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3605', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%BA%E3%81%99', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:02:34.326698Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.459774Z'),
(3606, 'vocabulary', '殺人', '殺人', 'When a <kanji>person</kanji> is <kanji>kill</kanji>ed, you have yourself a <vocabulary>murder</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3606', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%BA%E4%BA%BA', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:02:43.149592Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3607, 'vocabulary', '自殺', '自殺', 'A <kanji>self</kanji> <kanji>kill</kanji> is when you kill yourself. That''s <vocabulary>suicide</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3607', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E6%AE%BA', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:02:54.955362Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.534124Z'),
(3608, 'vocabulary', '全然', '全然', 'If you know <kanji>all</kanji> of something''s <kanji>nature</kanji> then you know it <vocabulary>entirely</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>completely</vocabulary>. If you don''t, then you know it <vocabulary>not at all</vocabulary>.

This word is weird. It can mean two opposite things depending on what kind of word comes after it. If it''s a negative word, it''ll mean "not at all." If it''s not negative, it''ll mean "entirely." Check out the patterns of use and context sentences for examples.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3608', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E7%84%B6', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:03:01.72226Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:47:49.244838Z'),
(3609, 'vocabulary', '当然', '当然', 'Something that''s <kanji>right</kanji> by its very <kanji>nature</kanji> is <vocabulary>natural</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>obvious</vocabulary>. 

当然 is also often used as an expression to mean <vocabulary>of course</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>naturally</vocabulary>. It''s used when something is expected or goes without saying, like a confident "yeah, that''s how it should be."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3609', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%93%E7%84%B6', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:03:09.102471Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:38:04.319615Z'),
(3610, 'vocabulary', '自然', '自然', 'To find your <kanji>self</kanji>''s true <kanji>nature</kanji>, go out into <vocabulary>nature</vocabulary>. Go out into the forests and fields, do some reading and thinking, and find your true nature.

This can also mean "natural" when it''s used as a な adjective, like in 自然な日本語 (natural Japanese) or 自然な色 (natural color).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3610', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E7%84%B6', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:03:16.014038Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.654842Z'),
(3611, 'vocabulary', '情熱', '情熱', 'If you''re <kanji>feeling</kanji> the <kanji>fever</kanji>, you''re getting so into something that your body is heating up. You have a ton of  <vocabulary>passion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3611', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%85%E7%86%B1', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:03:22.757112Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.68465Z'),
(3612, 'vocabulary', '熱', '熱', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3612', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%86%B1', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:03:30.590448Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.712086Z'),
(3613, 'vocabulary', '熱い', '熱い', 'This is for when a thing is <kanji>hot</kanji>, making this word <vocabulary>hot thing</vocabulary>. When the weather is hot that''s 暑い. This is when you touch something and you burn yourself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3613', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%86%B1%E3%81%84', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:03:37.236231Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.738457Z'),
(3614, 'vocabulary', '熱心', '熱心', 'When your <kanji>heart</kanji> is <kanji>fever</kanji>ish and hot about something, it is obviously something you have a lot of <vocabulary>enthusiasm</vocabulary> about. Think about the things you''ve enthusiasm for. Then, imagine your heart beating and getting all hot from thinking about it. Why else would people get so upset when talking about religion and politics. It''s their feverish heart! Their enthusiasm!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3614', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%86%B1%E5%BF%83', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:04:06.860278Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.76449Z'),
(3615, 'vocabulary', '皆さん', '皆さん', '<kanji>Everyone</kanji> + さん (normal name ender). This is the normal way to say <vocabulary>everyone</vocabulary>', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3615', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%86%E3%81%95%E3%82%93', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:04:24.146414Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.790729Z'),
(3616, 'vocabulary', '福島', '福島', 'The <kanji>luck</kanji> <kanji>island</kanji> wasn''t actually too lucky. This is <vocabulary>Fukushima</vocabulary> where the nuclear meltdown happened after the 2011 earthquake. So, when you think of "luck island" this is where you should think.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3616', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A6%8F%E5%B3%B6', 15, 1, '2012-09-18T18:04:37.962047Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.699077Z'),
(3617, 'vocabulary', '緑', '緑', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3617', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%91', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:04:46.471622Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T15:52:40.912401Z'),
(3618, 'vocabulary', '緑色', '緑色', 'The <kanji>green</kanji> <kanji>color</kanji> is the color <vocabulary>green</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3618', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%91%E8%89%B2', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:04:53.58207Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T15:52:40.912401Z'),
(3619, 'vocabulary', '練習', '練習', 'You <kanji>practice</kanji> something to <kanji>learn</kanji> it. That''s still called <vocabulary>practice</vocabulary>! The important part about this is that you''re learning while you practice too. It''s right in the word. So, when you practice kanji and Japanese vocab, make sure you''re learning as well as practicing. If you''re not learning, you''re not really practicing, are you?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3619', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%B4%E7%BF%92', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:05:02.593045Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:42:04.39189Z'),
(3620, 'vocabulary', '詩', '詩', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3620', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%A9', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:05:29.56507Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.910822Z'),
(3621, 'vocabulary', '詩人', '詩人', 'A <kanji>poem</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>poet</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3621', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%A9%E4%BA%BA', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:05:37.571442Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3622, 'vocabulary', '詩歌', '詩歌', 'A <kanji>poem</kanji> <kanji>song</kanji> is <vocabulary>poetry</vocabulary>. It''s not quite a song but I think you can make the connection.

詩歌 used to be for two kinds of poetry called kanshi and waka, but nowadays it''s also used for other traditional Japanese poetry forms like tanka, haiku, senryu, and more.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3622', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%A9%E6%AD%8C', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:05:45.604701Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:03:31.014085Z'),
(3623, 'vocabulary', '課長', '課長', 'Remember how 社長 is the company president? This uses the same 長 meaning (where it means something like "boss"). Since 課 means <kanji>section</kanji>, this is the <vocabulary>section manager</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3623', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%B2%E9%95%B7', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:05:53.962824Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:29.980387Z'),
(3624, 'vocabulary', '謝る', '謝る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>apologize</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to apologize</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3624', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AC%9D%E3%82%8B', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:06:07.207187Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.005776Z'),
(3625, 'vocabulary', '賞金', '賞金', 'A <kanji>prize</kanji> of <kanji>gold</kanji> is so old fashioned. We use cash now, which is why this word means <vocabulary>cash prize</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3625', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%9E%E9%87%91', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:06:14.671244Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:27:28.654407Z'),
(3626, 'vocabulary', '車輪', '車輪', 'What part of your car are <kanji>car</kanji> <kanji>ring</kanji>s? The <vocabulary>wheel</vocabulary>s, of course!

But 車輪 isn''t just for cars! You''ll spot this word rolling along with bicycles, motorcycles, carts, strollers, trolleys… you name it. It has a slightly formal or technical tone, so in everyday talk, you''ll more often hear タイヤ (tire) when talking about vehicle wheels.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3626', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8A%E8%BC%AA', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:06:28.226093Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:09.42967Z'),
(3627, 'vocabulary', '輪', '輪', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3627', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%AA', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:06:36.75358Z', NULL, '2025-09-26T00:50:18.562288Z'),
(3628, 'vocabulary', '選ぶ', '選ぶ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>"choose"</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to choose</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3628', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%B8%E3%81%B6', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:07:12.503451Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.109817Z'),
(3629, 'vocabulary', '選手', '選手', 'Remember how 手 can mean "doer" in some words? Well this is one of them. In sports, you must <kanji>choose</kanji> the best <kanji>doer</kanji> for your team. In the world of sports, these "chosen doers" are the  <vocabulary>pro athlete</vocabulary>s and  <vocabulary>sports player </vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3629', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%B8%E6%89%8B', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:07:26.438176Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.135878Z'),
(3630, 'vocabulary', '水銀', '水銀', 'This is a fun one. <kanji>Water</kanji> <kanji>silver</kanji> is <vocabulary>mercury</vocabulary>, which makes a lot of sense. Mercury is liquidy like water, but it is all silver like silver. So silver water isn''t some new mineral water you can get for $4 at the store, it''s <vocabulary>mercury</vocabulary>! Or <vocabulary>quicksilver</vocabulary>, if you prefer.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3630', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E9%8A%80', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:08:35.013116Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T09:52:41.789411Z'),
(3631, 'vocabulary', '銀', '銀', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3631', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%80', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:08:44.110847Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T09:52:41.789411Z'),
(3632, 'vocabulary', '銀色', '銀色', 'The <kanji>silver</kanji> <kanji>color</kanji> is just <vocabulary>silver</vocabulary> (the color).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3632', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%80%E8%89%B2', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:08:51.211753Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T09:52:41.789411Z'),
(3633, 'vocabulary', '銀行', '銀行', 'Time to <kanji>go</kanji> get your <kanji>silver</kanji> from the <vocabulary>bank</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3633', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%80%E8%A1%8C', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:09:01.48995Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T09:52:41.789411Z'),
(3634, 'vocabulary', '鏡', '鏡', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3634', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8F%A1', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:09:09.024988Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:33:42.082405Z'),
(3635, 'vocabulary', '〜放題', '〜放題', 'Just <kanji>release</kanji> the <kanji>topic</kanji>... let it go free! Let it do whatever it wants. Sometimes the topic will be food... if that''s the case, just release yourself and do whatever you want with the food (eat it all!). If it''s drinks, drink it all! This word has to do with doing whatever you want and <vocabulary>as much as you want</vocabulary>. You''ll see this attached to "all you can ____" words, so look out for them in the next  couple levels.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3635', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%94%BE%E9%A1%8C', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:09:18.401823Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:18:01.665189Z'),
(3636, 'vocabulary', '話題', '話題', 'The <kanji>topic</kanji> that we are <kanji>talk</kanji>ing about is the <vocabulary>topic</vocabulary> that we are talking about. It''s the <vocabulary>subject</vocabulary> of our conversation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3636', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%B1%E9%A1%8C', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:09:39.077514Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:18:01.665189Z'),
(3637, 'vocabulary', 'お願いします', 'お願いします', 'This is a tricky word that doesn''t really have a good translation. The best ways I''ve seen it done in terms of getting a meaning across are <vocabulary>please</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>please help me</vocabulary>. When you say this word you''re kind of saying "please help me out and I''m relying on you and you''re kind of awesome too." Anyways, <vocabulary>please</vocabulary> is a pretty good way to do it. So, when you see this word you''ll notice it has the <kanji>request</kanji> kanji in it. You can guess you''re requesting something. What is that thing? It''s help (<vocabulary>please help me</vocabulary>) and you''re asking very nicely too (<vocabulary>please</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3637', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E9%A1%98%E3%81%84%E3%81%97%E3%81%BE%E3%81%99', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T18:10:26.883543Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.327436Z'),
(3638, 'vocabulary', '心願', '心願', 'The thing your <kanji>heart</kanji> <kanji>request</kanji>s is what your heart wants. This is your <vocabulary>heartfelt desire</vocabulary>.

心願 is often found in the phrase 心願成就 (fulfillment of a heartfelt wish), commonly used in the context of prayer rituals at Shinto shrines or Buddhist temples.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3638', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83%E9%A1%98', 47, 1, '2012-09-18T18:10:45.86762Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:44.014019Z'),
(3639, 'vocabulary', '養う', '養う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>foster</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to foster</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to raise</vocabulary>. This can also refer to fostering a skill or ability, so can also mean <vocabulary>to develop</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to cultivate</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3639', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A4%8A%E3%81%86', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:11:03.759769Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:40:05.658345Z'),
(3640, 'vocabulary', '養子', '養子', 'If you <kanji>foster</kanji> a <kanji>child</kanji> for long enough, eventually you''ll want to adopt them and make them your <vocabulary>adopted child</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3640', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A4%8A%E5%AD%90', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:11:11.003692Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T06:01:25.879379Z'),
(3641, 'vocabulary', '旅館', '旅館', 'When you’re taking a <kanji>trip</kanji> to a <kanji>public building</kanji>, you might be staying at a <vocabulary>Japanese inn</vocabulary>. Those were very much public buildings for all kinds of travellers back in the day, when you think about it.

Alternatively, you could call this a <vocabulary>Japanese style hotel</vocabulary>, or just by its Japanese name, <vocabulary>ryokan</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3641', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%85%E9%A4%A8', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:11:27.059806Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:48:50.473642Z'),
(3642, 'vocabulary', '本館', '本館', 'One of the meanings of 本 is <kanji>main</kanji> (which is what we''re using here). The <kanji>main</kanji> <kanji>public building</kanji> is the <vocabulary>main building</vocabulary>.

Sometimes 本館 can also mean <vocabulary>this building</vocabulary>, as in the one you''re in. I guess whatever building you''re in sort of becomes the main one to you, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3642', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E9%A4%A8', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T18:11:57.471096Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.43941Z'),
(3643, 'vocabulary', '駅', '駅', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Usually this refers to a train station in particular, but can be any kind of station.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3643', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%85', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:12:08.825978Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.462396Z'),
(3644, 'vocabulary', '駅前', '駅前', 'The <kanji>station</kanji> <kanji>front</kanji> is the <vocabulary>front of station</vocabulary>. This word is great for telling people where you want to meet, by the way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3644', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%85%E5%89%8D', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:12:16.205442Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.48662Z'),
(3645, 'vocabulary', '駅員', '駅員', 'A train <kanji>station</kanji> <kanji>member</kanji> is someone who works at the train station. They are a <vocabulary>train station employee</vocabulary>. Feel free to ask them if you need help knowing which direction to train in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3645', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%85%E5%93%A1', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:12:23.852506Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:18:26.29811Z'),
(3646, 'vocabulary', '駅長', '駅長', 'This word uses 長 in the same way as 社長, where it means something like "boss" or "manager." So, this 長 is the boss of the <kanji>station</kanji>, right? What do you call someone like that? You call them the <vocabulary>stationmaster</vocabulary> (that is, unless you want to be thrown to the trains).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3646', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%85%E9%95%B7', 13, 1, '2012-09-18T18:12:32.53835Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.538794Z'),
(3647, 'vocabulary', '里', '里', 'This is the village you came from. This is where your parents live. This is your <vocabulary>hometown</vocabulary> (same meaning as the kanji you learned).

This word is old-fashioned and evokes a warm, nostalgic image of home. Often seen in folktales and traditional songs, it represents an idyllic, pastoral life.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3647', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8C', 5, 1, '2012-09-18T18:34:31.55761Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.797549Z'),
(3648, 'vocabulary', '里心-3648', '里心', 'You have your <kanji>home village</kanji> and your <kanji>heart</kanji>. Your heart always resides in your home village, so when you leave you feel <vocabulary>homesick</vocabulary>.

Young people don''t really use this word anymore. It''s more for the oldies. If you''re an oldie, go right ahead. If you''re a youngie, then talk to more oldies!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3648', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8C%E5%BF%83-3648', 5, 1, '2012-09-18T18:36:43.682546Z', '2021-10-07T17:44:29.956231Z', '2025-06-11T05:32:30.592919Z'),
(3649, 'vocabulary', '都合', '都合', 'This one makes no sense (sorry!). When you <kanji>join</kanji> together with someone at a <kanji>metropolis</kanji>, you do it <vocabulary>at your convenience</vocabulary>. You''re important, after all, so you just do what you want and make the other person wait in this giant metropolis while you move along leisurely. Imagine this, and keep thinking "at your convenience" as you walk about.

This word is usually used in a nicer way, though. As in, "do it at your convenience!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3649', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%BD%E5%90%88', 12, 1, '2012-09-18T18:45:33.829687Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:04:05.609053Z'),
(3650, 'vocabulary', '消しゴム', '消しゴム', 'You''ve seen the word 消す, I hope. This comes from that. You''re extinguishing something with ゴム. ゴム is a word for either gummy or rubbery things. So, this is a gummy thing that extinguishes something. This is an <vocabulary>eraser</vocabulary> that extinguishes pencil markings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3650', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B6%88%E3%81%97%E3%82%B4%E3%83%A0', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T19:30:48.845194Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.645073Z'),
(3651, 'vocabulary', '悪気', '悪気', 'Someone with <kanji>bad</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji> is someone who has <vocabulary>evil intent</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>malice</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>ill will</vocabulary>. Hopefully you don''t have too much 悪気.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3651', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E6%B0%97', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T19:35:18.641418Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(3652, 'vocabulary', '山登り', '山登り', 'You''ve hopefully seen how 登る means "to climb." So, if you''re <kanji>climb</kanji>ing a <kanji>mountain</kanji> what is that? It''s <vocabulary>mountain climbing</vocabulary>.

In case you''re wondering what the difference is between this and 登山, they are essentially the same. The main difference is that 山登り is a little less formal, and is generally used as a standalone word, whereas 登山 can be used in compound words like 登山家 (mountaineer).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3652', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%B1%E7%99%BB%E3%82%8A', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T22:35:37.353891Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.722405Z'),
(3653, 'vocabulary', '流行語', '流行語', 'You know that 流行 means "trend", so "trend" <kanji>language</kanji> means a <vocabulary>popular phrase</vocabulary>. Yolo! It could also be a <vocabulary>catchphrase</vocabulary>, a <vocabulary>popular word</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>buzzword</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3653', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%81%E8%A1%8C%E8%AA%9E', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T22:43:33.154252Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.746859Z'),
(3654, 'vocabulary', '流行歌', '流行歌', 'You know that 流行 refers to a "trend." So, a "trend" <kanji>song</kanji> is a <vocabulary>popular song</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3654', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%81%E8%A1%8C%E6%AD%8C', 14, 1, '2012-09-18T22:57:24.607207Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:03:31.014085Z'),
(3655, 'vocabulary', '例外', '例外', 'Something that''s <kanji>outside</kanji> the <kanji>example</kanji> doesn''t follow the rules. It''s not what it''s supposed to be (the example), it is the <vocabulary>exception</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3655', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%8B%E5%A4%96', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:11:07.13682Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(3656, 'vocabulary', '一例', '一例', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>example</kanji> is just an <vocabulary>instance</vocabulary> of what you''re describing. Just one <vocabulary>example</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3656', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E4%BE%8B', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:11:17.93699Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.821411Z'),
(3657, 'vocabulary', '例えば', '例えば', 'This has almost the same meaning as the kanji it comes from. Instead of just simply being "example," though, it''s <vocabulary>for example</vocabulary>. It can also sometimes mean <vocabulary>supposing that</vocabulary> when talking about hypothetical scenarios. So, supposing that I start a sentence with this, I can be all like "例えば, I am using 例えば in a sentence."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3657', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%8B%E3%81%88%E3%81%B0', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:11:25.032134Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.842915Z'),
(3658, 'vocabulary', '例文', '例文', '<kanji>Example</kanji> <kanji>writing</kanji> is a piece of writing that is an example for maybe a word or a kanji or something like that. This is an <vocabulary>example sentence</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3658', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%8B%E6%96%87', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:11:54.387712Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.865226Z'),
(3659, 'vocabulary', '悪例', '悪例', 'A <kanji>bad</kanji> <kanji>example</kanji> is a <vocabulary>bad example</vocabulary> and is something you don''t want.

This is a formal expression and less common than 悪い例 (bad example) or 良くない例 (not good example).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3659', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E4%BE%8B', 36, 1, '2012-09-19T00:12:02.37307Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.887951Z'),
(3660, 'vocabulary', '協会', '協会', 'When you <kanji>meet</kanji> for <kanji>cooperation</kanji>, you''re coming together to help each other. One form of this is when people form <vocabulary>association</vocabulary>s. They''re all cooperating and coming together to help each other and themselves.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3660', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%94%E4%BC%9A', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:12:30.117481Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.910939Z'),
(3661, 'vocabulary', '協力', '協力', '<kanji>Cooperation</kanji> <kanji>power</kanji> is your level of <vocabulary>cooperation</vocabulary>. It''s how much <vocabulary>collaboration</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>assistance</vocabulary> you provide.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3661', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%94%E5%8A%9B', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:12:37.950533Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(3662, 'vocabulary', '参る', '参る', 'The kanji itself means <kanji>participate</kanji>, so you''d think this word would mean "to participate" but it''s a little more complex than that. Think of it as "to participate" but where you have <vocabulary>to come</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to go</vocabulary> in order to do it. This is the humble way to say those things, so you''re going to these places with your tail between your legs, just happy you can participate more than anything. By extension, this is also a humble way to say <vocabulary>to visit</vocabulary>, often used if you''re visiting something sacred like a shrine or a tomb. 

Historically, this word arose as a humble way to indicate visiting a person of higher social status. In a weird twist, this word has come to also mean <vocabulary>to be defeated</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be at a loss</vocabulary>. Think of it as what can happen when you''re trying to participate while in an inferior position to someone or something. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3662', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%82%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:12:54.865283Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T21:46:55.985219Z'),
(3663, 'vocabulary', '一周', '一周', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>circumference</kanji> is <vocabulary>one time around</vocabulary>, or in racetrack sports, <vocabulary>one lap</vocabulary>. How many times will you go around the moon? Just 一周?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3663', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%91%A8', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:13:24.158345Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:30.977461Z'),
(3664, 'vocabulary', '周囲', '周囲', 'The <kanji>circumference</kanji> that <kanji>surround</kanji>s you is your <vocabulary>surroundings</vocabulary>.

周囲 commonly refers to your surroundings, whether it''s the physical environment around you or your social circles. It can also refer to the <vocabulary>perimeter</vocabulary> of something, like the <vocabulary>circumference</vocabulary> of a lake or an object, though this usage is more technical and less common in everyday conversation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3664', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%A8%E5%9B%B2', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:13:30.623483Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:09.613285Z'),
(3665, 'vocabulary', '周年', '周年', 'When you wrap around the entire <kanji> circumference</kanji> of the <kanji>year</kanji> you''ve come back around to the start of it. This is the <vocabulary>anniversary</vocabulary>.

This word is commonly used with numbers, like 10周年 (10th anniversary), or combined with other words, like 周年事業 (anniversary project).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3665', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%A8%E5%B9%B4', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:13:38.148903Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:06:22.386737Z'),
(3666, 'vocabulary', '囲む', '囲む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>surround</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to surround</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to enclose</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3666', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%B2%E3%82%80', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:13:51.581992Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.043537Z'),
(3667, 'vocabulary', '雰囲気', '雰囲気', 'The <kanji>surround</kanji>ing <kanji>energy</kanji> in the <kanji>atmosphere</kanji> is what makes up the <vocabulary>atmosphere</vocabulary>. I love it when it takes three kanji to represent what one of the three kanji already represents.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3667', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%B0%E5%9B%B2%E6%B0%97', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:13:58.558053Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T22:08:10.397284Z'),
(3668, 'vocabulary', '固い', '固い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>hard</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>hard</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>firm</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>tough</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3668', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BA%E3%81%84', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:14:04.838515Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.085984Z'),
(3669, 'vocabulary', '頑固', '頑固', 'A <kanji>hard</kanji> <kanji>stubborn</kanji> person is very <vocabulary>stubborn</vocabulary> no matter how you look at them. Try to break through that hard stubbornness and all you''ll find is more stubborn.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3669', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%91%E5%9B%BA', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:14:25.148763Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.108547Z'),
(3670, 'vocabulary', '基本', '基本', 'The <kanji>origin</kanji> of your <kanji>foundation</kanji> is where you have to work from when learning something new. For example, the 基本 portions of WaniKani are the earlier levels, especially the radicals. They are the <vocabulary>foundation</vocabulary> you need to learn your kanji and vocab later on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3670', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%BA%E6%9C%AC', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:14:31.93332Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.130062Z'),
(3671, 'vocabulary', '四季', '四季', 'The <kanji>four</kanji> <kanji>season</kanji>s are <vocabulary>the four seasons</vocabulary>. For some reason many Japanese people think that Japan''s the only place with four distinct seasons. Obviously they haven''t been to half the places on their latitude around the world.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3671', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B%E5%AD%A3', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:15:01.107605Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:13:05.258541Z'),
(3672, 'vocabulary', '完了', '完了', 'A <kanji>perfect</kanji> <kanji>finish</kanji> is when everything is at total <vocabulary>completion</vocabulary>. Nothing is left over and all is done.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3672', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%8C%E4%BA%86', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:15:37.044637Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.17159Z'),
(3673, 'vocabulary', '完全', '完全', 'When something is <kanji>all</kanji> <kanji>perfect</kanji> it is <vocabulary>complete</vocabulary>. Not only that, but it''s <vocabulary>perfect</vocabulary> (because it''s ALL perfect, not just partially so).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3673', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%8C%E5%85%A8', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:15:45.594591Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:47:49.244838Z'),
(3674, 'vocabulary', '完成', '完成', 'When something has <kanji>become</kanji> <kanji>perfect</kanji> it has reached <vocabulary>completion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3674', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%8C%E6%88%90', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:15:51.847222Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.213843Z'),
(3675, 'vocabulary', '念願', '念願', 'What your <kanji>thought</kanji>s <kanji>request</kanji> inside your head, nonstop like a crazy person, are your <vocabulary>heart''s desire</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3675', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%B5%E9%A1%98', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:16:13.33149Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.236136Z'),
(3676, 'vocabulary', '残念', '残念', 'You have <kanji>remainder</kanji> <kanji>thoughts</kanji> when your hear about something <vocabulary>unfortunate</vocabulary>. That''s <vocabulary>too bad</vocabulary>, you think, I wish it could have been different. Those remainder thoughts are a kind of regret, right? That''s why this can mean <vocabulary>regrettable</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>disappointing</vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3676', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%8B%E5%BF%B5', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:16:20.290054Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.257569Z'),
(3677, 'vocabulary', '人性-3677', '人性', 'A <kanji>person</kanji>''s <kanji>nature</kanji> is just <vocabulary>human nature</vocabulary>! Everyone has their own human <vocabulary>instinct</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3677', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E6%80%A7-3677', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:16:28.058416Z', '2020-10-08T07:42:50.293037Z', '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3678, 'vocabulary', '女性', '女性', 'Someone who''s of the <kanji>woman</kanji> <kanji>gender</kanji> is probably going to be <vocabulary>female</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3678', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%B3%E6%80%A7', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:16:44.348023Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.301184Z'),
(3679, 'vocabulary', '性病', '性病', 'A <kanji>gender</kanji> <kanji>sick</kanji>ness is one you catch from *ahem* doing things with those of another or the same gender. You only catch these sicknesses this way, meaning they''re <vocabulary>sexually transmitted disease</vocabulary>s (save yourself some typing and just answer with <vocabulary>STD</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3679', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A7%E7%97%85', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:16:51.774688Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.323302Z'),
(3680, 'vocabulary', '男性', '男性', 'A <kanji>man</kanji>''s <kanji>gender</kanji> is known as "<vocabulary>male</vocabulary>."

Note that this can only refer to a human''s gender; you''re going to learn the word you use for animals, plants, etc. later on!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3680', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B7%E6%80%A7', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:17:11.315333Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.343977Z'),
(3681, 'vocabulary', '技', '技', 'What is a <kanji>skill</kanji>? It''s something you can do, and do quite well. It is a <vocabulary>technique</vocabulary> that you can make happen with ease.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3681', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%80', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T00:17:19.661254Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.776471Z'),
(3682, 'vocabulary', '技能', '技能', 'Your <kanji>skill</kanji> and <kanji>ability</kanji> are kind of similar things. That''s why this word means either or. Your skill ability is your <vocabulary>skill</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>ability</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3682', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%80%E8%83%BD', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T00:17:26.151558Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.810199Z'),
(3683, 'vocabulary', '特技', '特技', 'Your <kanji>special</kanji> <kanji>skill</kanji> is your <vocabulary>specialty</vocabulary>. So, what is your 特技?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3683', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%B9%E6%8A%80', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T00:17:45.555098Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.821573Z'),
(3684, 'vocabulary', '折る', '折る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>fold</kanji> or <kanji>break</kanji>, so the vocab verb version is <vocabulary>to fold something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to break something</vocabulary>', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3684', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%98%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:18:06.48037Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.442902Z'),
(3685, 'vocabulary', '折角', '折角', 'When there''s nothing but <kanji>fold</kanji>s and <kanji>angle</kanji>s, you have quite a mess. This word means <vocabulary>with trouble</vocabulary> because you certainly aren''t traversing along a nice, flat piece of space-time, are you? Nah, it''s all angled and folded.

折角 is one of those tricky Japanese words that doesn’t have one neat translation in English. The nuance is that something has come about with trouble, either through someone’s efforts, or a stroke of luck. It often implies regret, and is used in situations where you want to express that <vocabulary>valuable</vocabulary> opportunities, advice, or hard work were in vain for some reason. It’s also used to underline a <vocabulary>rare</vocabulary> chance that shouldn’t be missed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3685', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%98%E8%A7%92', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:18:14.773011Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T21:05:47.522155Z'),
(3686, 'vocabulary', '骨折', '骨折', 'When your <kanji>bone</kanji> <kanji>break</kanji>s, you end up with, well, a <vocabulary>broken bone</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>fracture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3686', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AA%A8%E6%8A%98', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:18:22.151083Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.495585Z'),
(3687, 'vocabulary', '失望', '失望', 'There is <kanji>fault</kanji> in your <kanji>hope</kanji>. It breaks, and everything falls apart. You feel massive <vocabulary>disappointment</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>despair</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3687', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%B1%E6%9C%9B', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:18:37.335688Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.520825Z'),
(3688, 'vocabulary', '望み', '望み', 'A <kanji>hope</kanji> is really a <vocabulary>wish</vocabulary> that you have inside of you (though it can still be a <vocabulary>hope</vocabulary> too).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3688', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%9B%E3%81%BF', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:18:47.756295Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.543533Z'),
(3689, 'vocabulary', '望む', '望む', 'If you have a <kanji>hope</kanji> and you turn it into a verb, you''d think it would be "to hope." But what is really behind a hope? It''s a desire or wish, right? That''s why this word means <vocabulary>to wish</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to desire</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3689', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%9B%E3%82%80', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:19:05.001607Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.565604Z'),
(3690, 'vocabulary', '願望', '願望', 'The <kanji>hope</kanji> you <kanji>request</kanji> is your <vocabulary>desire</vocabulary>. Just imagine yourself hoping and requesting some higher power for something you really want. What is this? It''s your <vocabulary>desire</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>wish</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3690', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A1%98%E6%9C%9B', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:19:12.764428Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.589249Z'),
(3691, 'vocabulary', '取材', '取材', 'Your job is to <kanji>take</kanji> <kanji>material</kanji>s from a bunch of different people because you''re <vocabulary>covering an event</vocabulary>. This <vocabulary>data collection</vocabulary> needs to be thorough, so you try to take all the materials you can from as many people as you can find.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3691', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%96%E6%9D%90', 16, 1, '2012-09-19T00:19:19.122632Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.180411Z'),
(3692, 'vocabulary', '木材', '木材', '<kanji>Wood</kanji> that is <kanji>lumber</kanji> is <vocabulary>lumber</vocabulary> <vocabulary>wood</vocabulary>.

Technically speaking, 木材 is used more for lumber or wood that hasn''t been processed yet — less like planks, more like freshly cut lumber still lying on the forest floor. But it''s not a hard rule or anything, so don''t worry too much about the nuances.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3692', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%A8%E6%9D%90', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:19:25.654783Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.641701Z'),
(3693, 'vocabulary', '材料', '材料', 'You have to pay a <kanji>fee</kanji> for the <kanji>material</kanji> you use to make food. Those are the <vocabulary>ingredients</vocabulary> you''re paying for! Imagine making a list of all of the materials you need to make a stew, then making sure you have enough to pay the fees, so you''ll have all the ingredients you need to make it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3693', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%90%E6%96%99', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:19:33.389141Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.664661Z'),
(3694, 'vocabulary', '材木', '材木', '<kanji>Lumber</kanji> <kanji>tree</kanji>s are what you use to make <vocabulary>lumber</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>timber</vocabulary>.

材木 is typically for lumber or timber that''s been processed — the stuff that''s cut into planks and ready to use in your next DIY project, for example.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3694', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%90%E6%9C%A8', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:19:40.499096Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.68572Z'),
(3695, 'vocabulary', '束', '束', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3695', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%9F', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:19:51.512144Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.711761Z'),
(3696, 'vocabulary', '約束', '約束', 'A <kanji>promise</kanji> <kanji>bundle</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>promise</vocabulary> no matter how many you have wrapped up there. One promise is just another promise which is just another promise.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3696', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%84%E6%9D%9F', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:25:09.509102Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.734106Z'),
(3697, 'vocabulary', '松', '松', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3697', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%BE', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:25:18.163296Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.755906Z'),
(3698, 'vocabulary', '松葉', '松葉', 'The <kanji>pine</kanji> <kanji>leaves</kanji> are not really leaves, they''re more like needles, thus making this word <vocabulary>pine needle</vocabulary>.

松葉 is commonly used in compounds, such as 松葉ガニ (snow crab) or 松葉づえ (crutches).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3698', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%BE%E8%91%89', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:25:28.499624Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:10:47.677773Z'),
(3699, 'vocabulary', '人格', '人格', 'Your <kanji>person</kanji> <kanji>status</kanji> refers to what kind of person you are deep-down. In other words, it''s your <vocabulary>character</vocabulary>. Are you a person of character? Do you have a strong moral character?!

人格 is about the kind of person you are at your core. It''s not about surface-level traits like being shy, cheerful, or forgetful, but about the deeper qualities that define you as a human being. In philosophical or scientific contexts, it can also refer to a person''s <vocabulary>personality</vocabulary> as a technical concept, including intelligence and behavioral patterns.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3699', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E6%A0%BC', 25, 1, '2012-09-19T00:26:02.055912Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T06:43:23.805649Z'),
(3700, 'vocabulary', '合格する', '合格する', 'The <kanji>status</kanji> <kanji>joins</kanji> together. Think of this like a bunch of status messages on Facebook, all coming together at the same time. A big government test has just finished. Everyone''s status'' are joining on Facebook together to say they passed, showing <vocabulary>to pass a test</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3700', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%88%E6%A0%BC%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:26:10.656035Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T18:02:10.225948Z'),
(3701, 'vocabulary', '性格', '性格', 'Remember how 性 can also mean <kanji>nature</kanji>? Well, the <kanji>status</kanji> of your nature is your <vocabulary>personality</vocabulary>. What kind of personality do you have? Cheerful? Kind? Maybe you should take one of those online personality tests to find out.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3701', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A7%E6%A0%BC', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:26:22.778741Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:59:25.702839Z'),
(3702, 'vocabulary', '格好', '格好', 'Someone has the <kanji>status</kanji> of being <kanji>like</kanji>d by you, and it''s because of their incredible <vocabulary>appearance</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>form</vocabulary>. They have a great <vocabulary>look</vocabulary>, and it''s really making you like them (you''re kind of superficial).

On its own, 格好 usually refers to how someone comes across visually, especially their clothing or outward style. But in set phrases, it can also describe something''s overall vibe, which is why 格好いい works for a good-looking person, a jammin'' song, or even a line of dialogue that just sounds effortlessly cool.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3702', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%BC%E5%A5%BD', 16, 1, '2012-09-19T00:26:30.602172Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T06:52:07.856515Z'),
(3703, 'vocabulary', '骨格', '骨格', 'Your <kanji>bone</kanji> <kanji>status</kanji> is your <vocabulary>bone structure</vocabulary>. It''s the way all the bones in your <vocabulary>skeleton</vocabulary> are arranged and put together!

骨格 is a relatively formal word that refers to the skeletal structure or underlying frame of something. It can literally describe a person''s physical build, or it can be used figuratively to talk about the basic <vocabulary>framework</vocabulary> of a plan, argument, or piece of writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3703', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AA%A8%E6%A0%BC', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T00:26:37.099119Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.239945Z'),
(3704, 'vocabulary', '残り', '残り', 'This has the same meaning as the kanji. The little り thrown on there is just a little <vocabulary>remainder</vocabulary> sticking off the end of the kanji. No need to pay attention to that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3704', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%8B%E3%82%8A', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:26:55.988241Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.923631Z'),
(3705, 'vocabulary', '残る', '残る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>remainder</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is "to remainder." Let''s make that sound better: <vocabulary>to remain behind</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3705', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%8B%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:27:07.716897Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:31.945587Z'),
(3706, 'vocabulary', '残業', '残業', 'The <kanji>remainder</kanji> of time that you work at the ol'' <kanji>business</kanji> is extra time that you''re working (above the normal work hours). This is <vocabulary>overtime</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3706', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%8B%E6%A5%AD', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:27:43.078824Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(3707, 'vocabulary', '求める', '求める', 'What''s the verb form of <kanji>request</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>to request</vocabulary>. It also is for when you want something (<vocabulary>to want something</vocabulary>), because if you''re going <vocabulary>to request something</vocabulary>, you probably want it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3707', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%82%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2012-09-19T00:27:49.876785Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.157336Z'),
(3708, 'vocabulary', '求人', '求人', 'When you''re <kanji>request</kanji>ing <kanji>people</kanji>, you put up a <vocabulary>job posting</vocabulary> because you’re <vocabulary>seeking workers</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3708', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%82%E4%BA%BA', 19, 1, '2012-09-19T00:27:56.372455Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3709, 'vocabulary', '心理', '心理', 'The <kanji>reason</kanji>ing of your <kanji>heart</kanji> decides what you do. Probably when kanji was being made people thought that the heart directed our actions, not our brains. So, if you think about it that way, the reasoning of your heart is your <vocabulary>mental state</vocabulary>. Nowadays that''s known as <vocabulary>psychology</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3709', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83%E7%90%86', 7, 1, '2012-09-19T00:28:25.320647Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.767381Z'),
(3710, 'vocabulary', '料理', '料理', 'There is a <kanji>reason</kanji> for this <kanji>fee</kanji> that you have to pay to get in here. We are <vocabulary>cooking</vocabulary> <vocabulary>cuisine</vocabulary> for you to eat while you''re watching the show. If we weren''t cooking a <vocabulary>dish</vocabulary> for you, we probably wouldn''t charge you this fee!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3710', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%99%E7%90%86', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:28:55.483094Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:32.075958Z'),
(3711, 'vocabulary', '〜的', '〜的', 'This is a strange word, I''m afraid. You''ll have to bear with it. This, when added onto a word, is like adding an <vocabulary>-al</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>-like</vocabulary> to the end of it—or when something is <vocabulary>typical of</vocabulary> something else. For example, add it onto 日本 (Japan) and you have "Japanese-like", or "typically Japanese". It''s a weird sort of modifying word that really depends on each situation it''s in. For now, just know this word means these things. We''ll start applying it to various words in the future, slowly but surely.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3711', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E7%9A%84', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:29:29.397814Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:32.101064Z'),
(3712, 'vocabulary', '日本的', '日本的', 'Things that are <kanji>target</kanji>ed as <vocabulary>Japanese</vocabulary> are more than just Japanese, they are <vocabulary>very Japanese</vocabulary>. This word may be hard to understand at first, but there are many things the Japanese consider to be Japanese-only. These theories range from reasonable to ridiculous, but either way, you''ll see them described as 日本的.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3712', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%9A%84', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:29:43.217014Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:32.122426Z'),
(3713, 'vocabulary', '目的', '目的', 'When your <kanji>eye</kanji> is on the <kanji>target</kanji> you know your <vocabulary>purpose</vocabulary>. You also have your eye on the <vocabulary>goal</vocabulary> (that''s the target, after all!).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3713', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%AE%E7%9A%84', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:29:50.932618Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T09:41:29.095585Z'),
(3714, 'vocabulary', '私', '私', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3714', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%81', 6, 1, '2012-09-19T00:30:06.330082Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:04.394272Z'),
(3715, 'vocabulary', '私生活', '私生活', 'You''ve seen 生活 before, right? So what about the "life" of <kanji>I</kanji>? Whose life is that? Why, that''s my own <vocabulary>personal life</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3715', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%81%E7%94%9F%E6%B4%BB', 6, 1, '2012-09-19T00:30:29.718527Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.941663Z'),
(3716, 'vocabulary', '私立', '私立', '<kanji>I</kanji> am the one who keeps this place <kanji>stand</kanji>ing. In other words, it''s a <vocabulary>private</vocabulary> establishment — <vocabulary>privately-run</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>privately-operated</vocabulary> by ME!

私立 means "private" in the sense of a business, school, or other institution that''s run by a private entity, rather than by the government or the state.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3716', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%81%E7%AB%8B', 6, 1, '2012-09-19T00:30:37.57397Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T21:53:44.05009Z'),
(3717, 'vocabulary', '私鉄', '私鉄', 'A <kanji>private</kanji> <kanji>iron</kanji> (remember how 鉄 often has to do with railroad tracks and the like? Same goes here) is a <vocabulary>private railway</vocabulary>. There are a good number of these in Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3717', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%81%E9%89%84', 10, 1, '2012-09-19T00:31:04.734819Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.168162Z'),
(3718, 'vocabulary', '予約', '予約', 'A <kanji>beforehand</kanji> <kanji>promise</kanji> is a promise you''ve made beforehand. This is a <vocabulary>reservation</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>appointment</vocabulary>. Makes sense, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3718', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%88%E7%B4%84', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:31:33.230487Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:32.289702Z'),
(3719, 'vocabulary', '約', '約', 'The kanji means <kanji>promise</kanji>, but the meaning of this word is <vocabulary>about</vocabulary>, which is a little different. Just think about promises. When you promise something you really need to follow through... but only about as much as you need to for the other person to be happy. Someone asks for a puppy for Christmas, and you get them a stuffed animal puppy. It''s <vocabulary>approximately</vocabulary> the promise you made. Close enough. *wink*', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3719', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%84', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:31:39.496868Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:32.312626Z'),
(3720, 'vocabulary', '約束する', '約束する', '約束 is a “promise.” So when you add する to it you’re making it a verb, meaning <vocabulary>to promise</vocabulary>. Now promise you’ll remember!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3720', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%84%E6%9D%9F%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2012-09-19T00:31:46.989367Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.198118Z'),
(3721, 'vocabulary', '才能', '才能', 'A person who has great <kanji>talent</kanji> and <kanji>ability</kanji> has quite the <vocabulary>talent</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3721', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8D%E8%83%BD', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:44:25.572325Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T21:59:59.512416Z'),
(3722, 'vocabulary', '能力', '能力', 'Your <kanji>ability</kanji> <kanji>power</kanji> is what you can do. This is your <vocabulary>ability</vocabulary>. It is also your <vocabulary>capability</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3722', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%BD%E5%8A%9B', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:44:40.883835Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(3723, 'vocabulary', '芸能界', '芸能界', 'The <kanji>art</kanji> <kanji>ability</kanji> <kanji>world</kanji> is the world of <vocabulary>show business</vocabulary>, or in other words, the <vocabulary>entertainment industry</vocabulary>. To get into show business, you need abilities in the arts, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3723', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B8%E8%83%BD%E7%95%8C', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:44:57.294228Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T21:59:59.512416Z'),
(3724, 'vocabulary', '芸人', '芸人', 'What is the greatest <kanji>art</kanji> a <kanji>person</kanji> can possess? It’s got to be the art of making people laugh. That’s why this means <vocabulary>comedian</vocabulary>, though it can also mean a <vocabulary>performer</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>entertainer</vocabulary> more generally.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3724', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B8%E4%BA%BA', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:45:06.348071Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3725, 'vocabulary', '芸者', '芸者', 'The <kanji>art</kanji>s are performed by <kanji>someone</kanji>. Who is that someone? Well, think about the reading for this kanji. Do you recognize the word? It''s <vocabulary>geisha</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3725', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B8%E8%80%85', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:45:15.373117Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(3726, 'vocabulary', '芸術', '芸術', 'You need <kanji>art</kanji> <kanji>technique</kanji>s to make <vocabulary>art</vocabulary>. Without art techniques, <vocabulary>the arts</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>fine art</vocabulary> simply wouldn’t exist.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3726', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B8%E8%A1%93', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T00:46:01.033694Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.409514Z'),
(3727, 'vocabulary', '美術', '美術', 'When you create <kanji>beauty</kanji> with your <kanji>technique</kanji>s, that’s <vocabulary>fine art</vocabulary>, or simply <vocabulary>art</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3727', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%8E%E8%A1%93', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T00:46:56.956235Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.441564Z'),
(3728, 'vocabulary', '骨', '骨', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3728', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AA%A8', 14, 1, '2012-09-19T00:48:00.872121Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:32.535119Z'),
(3729, 'vocabulary', '仏', '仏', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3729', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8F', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:58:08.812029Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.496391Z'),
(3730, 'vocabulary', '仏教', '仏教', 'The <kanji>buddha</kanji> <kanji>teach</kanji>ings are what you have to learn when you''re studying <vocabulary>Buddhism</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3730', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8F%E6%95%99', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:58:16.667821Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.506375Z'),
(3731, 'vocabulary', '大仏', '大仏', 'A <kanji>large</kanji> <kanji>Buddha</kanji> doesn''t make sense if you don''t know the context. There are giant statues of Buddha, and these are called Daibutsu, making this word translate to the <vocabulary>Great Buddha</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>Giant Buddha Statue</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3731', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E4%BB%8F', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:58:26.216377Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.515609Z'),
(3732, 'vocabulary', '信じる', '信じる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>believe</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to believe</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3732', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%A1%E3%81%98%E3%82%8B', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:58:33.089618Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.524736Z'),
(3733, 'vocabulary', '自信', '自信', 'When you <kanji>believe</kanji> in your <kanji>self</kanji> you have plenty of <vocabulary>confidence</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>self confidence</vocabulary>). This is a good thing to have!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3733', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E4%BF%A1', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:58:41.156404Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:06:38.045182Z'),
(3734, 'vocabulary', '信号', '信号', 'If you <kanji>believe</kanji> in the <kanji>number</kanji>s, there will be no traffic because the <vocabulary>traffic light</vocabulary> will be functioning properly. Traffic lights are based all off of traffic data and numbers. You just have to believe and hope it all works out in terms of these traffic lights.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3734', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%A1%E5%8F%B7', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:58:48.299819Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.544822Z'),
(3735, 'vocabulary', '信心', '信心', 'What you <kanji>believe</kanji> in your <kanji>heart</kanji>, and not in your head, is your <vocabulary>faith</vocabulary>. This is something that doesn''t follow logic, it''s all heart belief, it''s all <vocabulary>faith</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3735', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%A1%E5%BF%83', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:58:55.504967Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.569528Z'),
(3736, 'vocabulary', '信用', '信用', 'When someone <kanji>believes</kanji> in your <kanji>task</kanji>, it means they have a lot of <vocabulary>trust</vocabulary> in what you do. You''ve clearly gained a great deal of <vocabulary>credibility</vocabulary> through your previous tasks.

信用 refers to the trust one party places in another''s integrity and ability to fulfill obligations. It''s often used to describe reliability in actions, faithfulness in relationships, or creditworthiness in financial transactions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3736', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%A1%E7%94%A8', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:59:04.021365Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.579464Z'),
(3737, 'vocabulary', '不信', '不信', 'When you can''t (<kanji>not</kanji>) <kanji>believe</kanji> in someone, it''s because you <vocabulary>distrust</vocabulary> them. Maybe someone <vocabulary>discredit</vocabulary>ed them and now you <vocabulary>mistrust</vocabulary> everything they say.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3737', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E4%BF%A1', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:59:10.557177Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.588739Z'),
(3738, 'vocabulary', '返信', '返信', 'You gotta <kanji>believe</kanji> it will <kanji>return</kanji>. What are you believing in returning? The <vocabulary>reply</vocabulary>. You made a request for something (come up with something you want really bad but have to ask for). Now you wait for the reply.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3738', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%94%E4%BF%A1', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:59:20.956691Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.599798Z'),
(3739, 'vocabulary', '勇気', '勇気', 'That <kanji>feeling</kanji> of <kanji>courage</kanji> is <vocabulary>courage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3739', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%87%E6%B0%97', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:59:27.622505Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.609314Z'),
(3740, 'vocabulary', '区', '区', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3740', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%BA', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:59:38.319037Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.618578Z'),
(3741, 'vocabulary', '区分', '区分', 'Take a <kanji>district</kanji> and <kanji>divide</kanji> it and you''ll end up with many smaller <vocabulary>division</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>segment</vocabulary>s. 

区分 is a formal term that refers to dividing or segmenting things into sections or categories based on specific criteria. For example, 区分 might be used when dividing an area into different zones or segments like residential and commercial, or sorting documents into different categories based on their content.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3741', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%BA%E5%88%86', 25, 1, '2012-09-19T22:59:44.515838Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:32.832522Z'),
(3742, 'vocabulary', '地区', '地区', 'The <kanji>earth</kanji> <kanji>district</kanji> isn''t our solar system or something like that. We''re talking about a specific portion of earth, and naming it as a district. So, what is this patch of earth? It''s a <vocabulary>district</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>area</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>zone</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3742', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E5%8C%BA', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T22:59:53.928034Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.628538Z'),
(3743, 'vocabulary', '単に', '単に', 'If you''re moving towards (に) <kanji>simple</kanji>, you''re saying <vocabulary>simply</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>merely</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>only</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3743', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%98%E3%81%AB', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:00:01.485274Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.638052Z'),
(3744, 'vocabulary', '単語', '単語', 'The <kanji>simple</kanji> bits of <kanji>language</kanji> are the individual <vocabulary>word</vocabulary>s. If you think about it, this is true. First of all, there are a ton of them. Second, they''re tiny. They''re just little bits of language and the "word" for these little bits is <vocabulary>word</vocabulary>.

So what’s the difference between this and 言葉? Well, 言葉 can cover any meaningful set of words – anything from words, sentences and paragraphs, to whole texts and even languages. 単語, on the other hand, is a subset of 言葉 that specifically refers to single, distinct words.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3744', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%98%E8%AA%9E', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:00:08.125718Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.648002Z'),
(3745, 'vocabulary', '上司', '上司', 'The <kanji>director</kanji> who is <kanji>above</kanji> you is your <vocabulary>boss</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>superior</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3745', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A%E5%8F%B8', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:00:16.529524Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.659635Z'),
(3746, 'vocabulary', '坂', '坂', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3746', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9D%82', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:00:27.268812Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.670936Z'),
(3747, 'vocabulary', '変', '変', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This is more the "strange" meaning though, not the "change" one. As in, you 変な猫！', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3747', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%89', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:00:33.288685Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.684118Z'),
(3748, 'vocabulary', '変人', '変人', 'A <kanji>strange</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is <vocabulary>an eccentric</vocabulary>. They are also an <vocabulary>oddball</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3748', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%89%E4%BA%BA', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:00:41.750158Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.697463Z'),
(3749, 'vocabulary', '変化', '変化', '<kanji>Change</kanji> and <kanji>change</kanji> equals even more <vocabulary>change</vocabulary>.

変化 generally refers to a natural or spontaneous change, like changes from growth or aging, alterations in weather conditions, or shifts in one''s emotions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3749', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%89%E5%8C%96', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:00:49.231163Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.707101Z'),
(3750, 'vocabulary', '変死', '変死', 'A <kanji>strange</kanji> <kanji>death</kanji> is one that doesn''t seem right. It''s an <vocabulary>unnatural death</vocabulary>, and probably an <vocabulary>accidental death</vocabulary> (ie wasn''t caused by natural causes).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3750', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%89%E6%AD%BB', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:00:57.982864Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.718409Z'),
(3751, 'vocabulary', '大変', '大変', 'A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>change</kanji> is a really <vocabulary>serious</vocabulary> thing. And people don''t like change, either, making it a <vocabulary>terrible</vocabulary> event. 

Also, in more recent history this has come to mean <vocabulary>very</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3751', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%A4%89', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:01:13.81765Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.728664Z'),
(3752, 'vocabulary', '事変', '事変', 'An <kanji>occurrence</kanji> that causes <kanji>change</kanji> somehow can also be called an <vocabulary>incident</vocabulary>. 

事変 is a formal word for big, unexpected incidents that are disruptive and chaotic. This can involve natural disasters, sudden riots, or other large-scale disturbances that need military intervention. It can also refer to unannounced warfare.

You''ll most likely see this word used in names for historical events, like the Manshuu Jihen (満州事変), or the Manchurian Incident. The 変 kanji suggests "change," so you know 事変 is only used for historical incidents big enough to make things change!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3752', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8B%E5%A4%89', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:03:26.700554Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.737466Z'),
(3753, 'vocabulary', '夫', '夫', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3753', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%AB', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:03:33.369458Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.747726Z'),
(3754, 'vocabulary', '岩', '岩', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3754', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B2%A9', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:04:01.46599Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.75635Z'),
(3755, 'vocabulary', '帰る', '帰る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>return home</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to return home</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to return</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to go home</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3755', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%B0%E3%82%8B', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:04:08.611107Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.765646Z'),
(3756, 'vocabulary', '建てる', '建てる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>build</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to build</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3756', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%BA%E3%81%A6%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2012-09-19T23:04:29.504052Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:33.182077Z'),
(3757, 'vocabulary', '建築', '建築', 'When it comes to <kanji>build</kanji>ing and <kanji>construct</kanji>ing, the thing that encompasses both of them is <vocabulary>architecture</vocabulary>.

建築 refers to the design and <vocabulary>construction</vocabulary> of buildings. Occasionally it can also refer to a physical building itself, but for that it''s more common to use 建築物.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3757', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%BA%E7%AF%89', 22, 1, '2012-09-19T23:04:50.620356Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:33.206341Z'),
(3758, 'vocabulary', '式', '式', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3758', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%8F', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:05:05.573892Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.787717Z'),
(3759, 'vocabulary', '春', '春', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3759', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%A5', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:05:12.12292Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:35:30.470443Z'),
(3760, 'vocabulary', '春休み', '春休み', 'You''ve seen 夏休み and 冬休み. If that isn''t enough, you can see that this is a <kanji>spring</kanji> <kanji>rest</kanji> of some kind. That would be a <vocabulary>spring break</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>spring vacation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3760', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%A5%E4%BC%91%E3%81%BF', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:05:18.230664Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:35:30.470443Z'),
(3761, 'vocabulary', '昨今', '昨今', '<kanji>Previous</kanji> from <kanji>now</kanji> are the times around now. They''re <vocabulary>these days</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>nowadays</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>recently</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3761', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%A8%E4%BB%8A', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:05:26.703436Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.85001Z'),
(3762, 'vocabulary', '昨日', '昨日', 'The <kanji>yesterday</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji> is <vocabulary>yesterday</vocabulary>. What a blast from the past!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3762', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%A8%E6%97%A5', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:05:33.638415Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.858778Z'),
(3763, 'vocabulary', '昼', '昼', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This can also mean <vocabulary>lunch</vocabulary>, especially when used with the honorific prefix お, as in お昼.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3763', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%BC', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:05:40.180994Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T03:54:27.626361Z'),
(3764, 'vocabulary', '晩', '晩', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3764', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%A9', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:05:59.712198Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.878495Z'),
(3765, 'vocabulary', '今晩は', '今晩は', 'This word is made of <kanji>now</kanji> and <kanji>night</kanji> with the hiragana は at the end. The は here is pronounced as わ (the old pronunciation). When you say "now night!" you''re really saying <vocabulary>good evening</vocabulary>.

Just keep in mind that writing 今晩は is pretty old school. These days it''s almost always written as こんばんは.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3765', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A%E6%99%A9%E3%81%AF', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:06:07.35996Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.887692Z'),
(3766, 'vocabulary', '毎晩', '毎晩', '<kanji>Every</kanji> <kanji>night</kanji> is simply <vocabulary>every night</vocabulary>. What do you do every night?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3766', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%8E%E6%99%A9', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:06:15.07997Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.896529Z'),
(3767, 'vocabulary', '晴れる', '晴れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>clear up</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to clear up</vocabulary>. It also means <vocabulary>to become sunny</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3767', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%B4%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:06:22.145562Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:00:51.825704Z'),
(3768, 'vocabulary', '毒', '毒', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

毒 refers to any kind of <vocabulary>poison</vocabulary>, manmade or natural. It even includes the <vocabulary>venom</vocabulary> produced by animals or insects. It can also figuratively describe things that slowly rot your health or mind, like bad habits or toxic influences. They''re not lethal, but they''re still 毒 for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3768', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%92', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:06:28.749451Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.915047Z'),
(3769, 'vocabulary', '中毒', '中毒', 'You''re in the <kanji>middle</kanji> of <kanji>poison</kanji>, meaning you have <vocabulary>poisoning</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3769', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E6%AF%92', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:06:37.074706Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.923883Z'),
(3770, 'vocabulary', '有毒', '有毒', 'Things that <kanji>have</kanji> <kanji>poison</kanji> are <vocabulary>toxic</vocabulary>. 

有毒 is a relatively formal word generally used to describe toxic materials or substances. It can also be used for formal classifications like 有毒植物 (<vocabulary>poisonous</vocabulary> plants) or 有毒動物 (poisonous and venomous animals).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3770', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%89%E6%AF%92', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:06:44.285369Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.932952Z'),
(3771, 'vocabulary', '食中毒', '食中毒', 'If you <kanji>eat</kanji> and find yourself in the <kanji>middle</kanji> of <kanji>poison</kanji>, you''ve probably got <vocabulary>food poisoning</vocabulary>. No fun when you get this :(', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3771', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9F%E4%B8%AD%E6%AF%92', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:06:50.97068Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.869076Z'),
(3772, 'vocabulary', '毒ガス', '毒ガス', '<kanji>Poison</kanji> Gas is a nasty thing. It is <vocabulary>poison gas</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3772', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%92%E3%82%AC%E3%82%B9', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:07:01.104113Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.946831Z'),
(3773, 'vocabulary', '方法', '方法', 'The <kanji>way</kanji> <kanji>method</kanji> or the <kanji>method</kanji> <kanji>way</kanji> is the <vocabulary>method</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>way</vocabulary>. This is a good word for talking about how to do something. What''s the 方法? Well, first you...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3773', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B9%E6%B3%95', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:07:13.217649Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.976277Z'),
(3774, 'vocabulary', '文法', '文法', 'The <kanji>method</kanji> of <kanji>writing</kanji> is how you write. You can have vocabulary words, but you have to be able to put them together using some kind of method. This method happens to be <vocabulary>grammar</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3774', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%87%E6%B3%95', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:07:19.20514Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.986533Z'),
(3775, 'vocabulary', '不法', '不法', 'A <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>law</kanji> is something that''s against the law. It''s <vocabulary>illegal</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>unlawful</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3775', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%B3%95', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:07:25.651702Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.997372Z'),
(3776, 'vocabulary', '泣く', '泣く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>cry</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to cry</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3776', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A3%E3%81%8F', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:07:34.448789Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.016224Z'),
(3777, 'vocabulary', '浅い', '浅い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>shallow</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>shallow</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3777', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%85%E3%81%84', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:07:57.504732Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.035744Z'),
(3778, 'vocabulary', '猫', '猫', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Meow!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3778', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8C%AB', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:08:11.302815Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.181452Z'),
(3779, 'vocabulary', '子猫', '子猫', 'A <kanji>child</kanji> <kanji>cat</kanji> is an adorable <vocabulary>kitten</vocabulary>. D''awww!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3779', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%90%E7%8C%AB', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:08:21.883222Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.191902Z'),
(3780, 'vocabulary', '秋', '秋', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3780', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%8B', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:08:29.496353Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.202477Z'),
(3781, 'vocabulary', '新築', '新築', 'Something that''s <kanji>new</kanji>ly <kanji>construct</kanji>ed is something that''s <vocabulary>newly built</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3781', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E7%AF%89', 22, 1, '2012-09-19T23:08:39.832048Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:33.761829Z'),
(3782, 'vocabulary', '世紀', '世紀', 'The <kanji>account</kanji> of a <kanji>generation</kanji>... this covers a whole generation, and considering how long Japanese people live, this account totals a whole <vocabulary>century</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3782', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%96%E7%B4%80', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:08:47.024814Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.214901Z'),
(3783, 'vocabulary', '紀元後', '紀元後', 'What does the <kanji>narrative</kanji> <kanji>origin</kanji> have coming <kanji>after</kanji> it? Well, the way we''ve counted years for the past couple of millenia has a specific narrative at its origin, and everything that comes after it is the <vocabulary>Common Era</vocabulary>, aka <vocabulary>AD</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>anno Domini</vocabulary>.

Just in case you don''t know what narrative we''re talking about: it''s the narrative of Jesus''s birth, which is the year that we say the Common Era started.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3783', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%80%E5%85%83%E5%BE%8C', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:09:16.619882Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.253833Z'),
(3784, 'vocabulary', '英語', '英語', 'The <kanji>English</kanji> <kanji>language</kanji> is the language you''re reading right here and now. This is the <vocabulary>English language</vocabulary>. It is <vocabulary>English</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3784', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%B1%E8%AA%9E', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:09:25.661544Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.264664Z'),
(3785, 'vocabulary', '英会話', '英会話', '<kanji>English</kanji> <kanji>meet</kanji>ing and <kanji>talk</kanji>ing with each other. This is <vocabulary>English Conversation</vocabulary>. This is used a lot when talking about "speaking in English" for studying English and whatnot.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3785', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%B1%E4%BC%9A%E8%A9%B1', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:09:35.463721Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.306091Z'),
(3786, 'vocabulary', '英文', '英文', '<kanji>England</kanji> <kanji>writing</kanji> is <vocabulary>English Writing</vocabulary> (or if you want to be fancy, <vocabulary>English Composition</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3786', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%B1%E6%96%87', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:09:41.504585Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.318779Z'),
(3787, 'vocabulary', '英国', '英国', 'The <kanji>England</kanji> <kanji>country</kanji> is where England is. This word can refer to <vocabulary>Britain</vocabulary>, the <vocabulary>United Kingdom</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>England</vocabulary>!

Note that イギリス is more common for "England" - but, since this is a kanji learning website you should learn this word.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3787', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%B1%E5%9B%BD', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:09:49.92727Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.330059Z'),
(3788, 'vocabulary', '英和', '英和', 'This is seen on things like dictionaries. When you have an <kanji>English</kanji> and <kanji>Japanese</kanji> (style) dictionary, for example. You''ll see it the other way around too, but this particular one is <vocabulary>English Japanese</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3788', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%B1%E5%92%8C', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:10:33.242812Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.340865Z'),
(3789, 'vocabulary', '時計', '時計', 'A thing that gives you <kanji>time</kanji> <kanji>measurement</kanji>s is a <vocabulary>watch</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>clock</vocabulary>. Most people have these in their phones instead of on their wrists now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3789', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%82%E8%A8%88', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:10:40.174901Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.350257Z'),
(3790, 'vocabulary', '合計', '合計', 'When you <kanji>join</kanji> all your <kanji>measure</kanji>ments together you have yourself a <vocabulary>total</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3790', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%88%E8%A8%88', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:10:49.502692Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.369605Z'),
(3791, 'vocabulary', '計画', '計画', 'You need <kanji>measurement</kanji>s and <kanji>image</kanji>s to make a <vocabulary>plan</vocabulary>. You can''t follow a plan without measurements and images to refer to, right? Any good plan has measurements and drawings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3791', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%88%E7%94%BB', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:10:56.026318Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.380571Z'),
(3792, 'vocabulary', '計算', '計算', 'When you <kanji>measure</kanji> and then <kanji>calculate</kanji> something, you are making a <vocabulary>calculation</vocabulary>. For example, you measure the length of a frog, then you calculate how many frogs you''d need to reach the moon. What is that? A calculation, of course.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3792', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%88%E7%AE%97', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:11:02.597242Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.390358Z'),
(3793, 'vocabulary', '軍人', '軍人', 'An <kanji>army</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is someone in the army. What are those people called? They''re <vocabulary>soldier</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3793', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8D%E4%BA%BA', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:11:08.983863Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.400002Z'),
(3794, 'vocabulary', '海軍', '海軍', 'The <kanji>sea</kanji> <kanji>army</kanji> are the branch of the military that has boats and does ocean/sea things. What''s that? The <vocabulary>navy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3794', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B7%E8%BB%8D', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:11:17.937721Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.41891Z'),
(3795, 'vocabulary', '飯', '飯', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. It also refers to <vocabulary>cooked rice</vocabulary>, because you gotta eat cooked rice with every meal, so it''s basically synonymous with "meal" itself.

This word has a slightly gruff feel to it so it''s used mostly in informal situations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3795', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%AF', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:11:26.298858Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.458194Z'),
(3796, 'vocabulary', 'ご飯', 'ご飯', 'The ご just makes this word more polite and fancy — two important qualities when it comes to food. This has the same meaning as the kanji (<kanji>meal</kanji>), making it <vocabulary>meal</vocabulary>, but it can also just mean <vocabulary>cooked rice</vocabulary>. So, "Come eat your ご飯" can mean either "come eat your rice" or "come eat your meal" — or both, since most Japanese meals come with rice anyway.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3796', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%94%E9%A3%AF', 15, 1, '2012-09-19T23:11:33.516447Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.481088Z'),
(3797, 'vocabulary', '悲しむ', '悲しむ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>sad</kanji> and this verb is transitive, so it means <vocabulary>to be sad about</vocabulary> something. Boohoo.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3797', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%B2%E3%81%97%E3%82%80', 17, 1, '2012-09-20T18:06:09.057389Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:34.160687Z'),
(3798, 'vocabulary', '落ち着く', '落ち着く', 'Hey. <kanji>Drop</kanji> yourself down and <kanji>arrive</kanji> back to me. You''re going nuts over there. Lower down. Come back. Arrive. You need to do these things <vocabulary>to calm down</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3798', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%90%BD%E3%81%A1%E7%9D%80%E3%81%8F', 14, 1, '2012-09-20T18:07:48.158177Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(3799, 'vocabulary', '仮面', '仮面', 'A <kanji>temporary</kanji> <kanji>face</kanji> is something you put over your face to cover it. It''s a <vocabulary>mask</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3799', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%AE%E9%9D%A2', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:55:59.411086Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.208723Z'),
(3800, 'vocabulary', '係わる', '係わる', 'You know that the kanji means <kanji>connection</kanji>. This is a verb, so one meaning is <vocabulary>to be connected</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>to be involved</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to be related</vocabulary>. Basically things that have to do with being connected with something else.

Does this all sound familiar? In fact, this is another way to write 関わる, which you already learned! The two have exactly the same meaning, but this version is a bit less common.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3800', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%82%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2012-09-20T18:56:14.50512Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:04.064923Z'),
(3801, 'vocabulary', '保守的', '保守的', 'Who are all <kanji>like</kanji> "I want to <kanji>preserve</kanji> and <kanji>protect</kanji> what I have, get out of my business, government!"? <vocabulary>Conservative</vocabulary> people, of course.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3801', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%9D%E5%AE%88%E7%9A%84', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:56:32.925511Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.218835Z'),
(3802, 'vocabulary', '保存', '保存', 'We try to <kanji>preserve</kanji> anything we want to <kanji>exist</kanji> with <vocabulary>preservation</vocabulary>. Want a special sea creature or bird to exist? Up <vocabulary>conservation</vocabulary> efforts! And, if you want to save data (like on a computer), you can use this word for that kind of <vocabulary>saving</vocabulary>, as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3802', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%9D%E5%AD%98', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:56:39.902529Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.230694Z'),
(3803, 'vocabulary', '保持', '保持', 'When you <kanji>preserve</kanji> the things you <kanji>hold</kanji> on to, you''re <vocabulary>keep</vocabulary>ing them. In other words, you''re <vocabulary>hold</vocabulary>ing and <vocabulary>maintain</vocabulary>ing them!

Note that 保持 is a formal word that''s commonly used in professional or official contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3803', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%9D%E6%8C%81', 9, 1, '2012-09-20T18:56:46.929551Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.434474Z'),
(3804, 'vocabulary', '保険', '保険', 'When you are able to <kanji>guarantee</kanji> against <kanji>risk</kanji>, it''s probably because you bought some good <vocabulary>insurance</vocabulary>! What do you use? The gecko 保険?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3804', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%9D%E9%99%BA', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:56:57.621052Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.241705Z'),
(3805, 'vocabulary', '保証', '保証', 'Giving <kanji>evidence</kanji> (or a <kanji>guarantee</kanji>) that you will <kanji>preserve</kanji> something, is giving a <vocabulary>guarantee</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>assurance</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>warranty</vocabulary>.

This word is used when the emphasis is on taking responsibility for something, as in a guarantor, a product warranty, or quality assurance.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3805', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%9D%E8%A8%BC', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:57:04.559476Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.251034Z'),
(3806, 'vocabulary', '生保', '生保', 'A <kanji>Life</kanji> <kanji>guarantee</kanji> is something you get on someone else. If their life ends, it''s guaranteed with money. That''s a <vocabulary>life insurance</vocabulary> policy, for yah! (A.K.A. <vocabulary>life assurance</vocabulary> in some parts of the world!)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3806', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E4%BF%9D', 9, 1, '2012-09-20T18:57:12.245308Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.46071Z'),
(3807, 'vocabulary', '保つ', '保つ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>preserve</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to preserve something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to keep something</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to maintain something</vocabulary>.

This is a transitive verb, so it takes a direct object. You preserve something, like your youth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3807', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%9D%E3%81%A4', 9, 1, '2012-09-20T18:57:19.316522Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.471777Z'),
(3808, 'vocabulary', '仏像', '仏像', 'You have <kanji>Buddha</kanji> and 像, which you learned mainly as "image" but can also mean <kanji>statue</kanji>. So that makes this a <vocabulary>statue of Buddha</vocabulary>. 仏像 can also just mean an <vocabulary>image of Buddha</vocabulary>, of course.

仏像 refers to statues, paintings, or artistic representations of Buddhist figures used as objects of worship. While it often means Buddha sculptures, it also includes depictions not just of the Buddha himself but other Buddhist deities as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3808', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8F%E5%83%8F', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:57:26.466922Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T20:01:27.526431Z'),
(3809, 'vocabulary', '古典的', '古典的', 'When something is "<kanji>old</kanji> <kanji>rule</kanji>s-<kanji>like</kanji>," it is not very modern. It is in the past. It is <vocabulary>classical</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3809', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%A4%E5%85%B8%E7%9A%84', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:57:33.48899Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.269063Z'),
(3810, 'vocabulary', '仏典', '仏典', '<kanji>Buddha</kanji> transcribed all of the <kanji>rule</kanji>s of Buddhism in the <vocabulary>Buddhist Scriptures</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>Buddhist Writing</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3810', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8F%E5%85%B8', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:57:40.36463Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:30.758816Z'),
(3811, 'vocabulary', '字典', '字典', '<kanji>Letter</kanji> <kanji>rule</kanji>s can be found in a <vocabulary>character dictionary</vocabulary>. In Japanese, one kanji "letter" or "character" can hold a lot of information, so you have dictionaries dedicated to kanji meanings. That''s what this is.

This word is a little old-fashioned these days, so you might also see 漢字辞典 used for a character dictionary. (You''ll learn 辞典 soon, if you haven''t already!)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3811', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%97%E5%85%B8', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:57:54.017766Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:30.772817Z'),
(3812, 'vocabulary', '事典', '事典', 'If you come across a <kanji>matter</kanji> in which the <kanji>rule</kanji>s aren’t clear, you’ll need to have a look in an <vocabulary>encyclopedia</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3812', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8B%E5%85%B8', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:58:00.575633Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:30.786588Z'),
(3813, 'vocabulary', '冒険', '冒険', 'You <kanji>dare</kanji> to do something <kanji>risky</kanji>. It''s <vocabulary>adventure</vocabulary> time! Imagine yourself daring yourself to do risky things. Is this an adventure? If not, make it so!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3813', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%92%E9%99%BA', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:58:07.598893Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:30.808965Z'),
(3814, 'vocabulary', '冗談', '冗談', 'When you have a <kanji>superfluous</kanji> <kanji>talk</kanji> you aren''t being serious, you''re telling a <vocabulary>joke</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3814', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%97%E8%AB%87', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:58:17.707245Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:30.834552Z'),
(3815, 'vocabulary', '危険', '危険', 'Something that is <kanji>dangerous</kanji> and <kanji>risky</kanji> is probably pretty <vocabulary>risky</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>dangerous</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>unsafe</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3815', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B1%E9%99%BA', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:58:25.8101Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:30.856762Z'),
(3816, 'vocabulary', '危ない', '危ない', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>dangerous</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>dangerous</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3816', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B1%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:58:31.655478Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:30.886425Z'),
(3817, 'vocabulary', '取る', '取る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>take</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to take something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3817', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8B', 6, 1, '2012-09-20T18:58:37.791972Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.660855Z'),
(3818, 'vocabulary', '下品', '下品', 'Things that are <kanji>below</kanji> normal <kanji>products</kanji> are the cheap knockoffs and bad quality items. These items are incredibly <vocabulary>crude</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>vulgar</vocabulary> in their manufacturing and design. So much <vocabulary>vulgarity</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3818', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E5%93%81', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:58:52.216904Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(3819, 'vocabulary', '作品', '作品', 'If you <kanji>make</kanji> a <kanji>product</kanji>, it''s not really a product anymore, but <vocabulary>a work</vocabulary>. It''s your <vocabulary>work</vocabulary> of art or literature, or maybe a theater <vocabulary>production</vocabulary>.

This can be any kind of artistic creation: a book, a composition, a painting, a movie, a play... It doesn''t mean "product" (you''ll learn the word for that later!) but it can be used for a product when you want to highlight the artistic talent behind it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3819', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%9C%E5%93%81', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:59:05.8626Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(3820, 'vocabulary', '品', '品', 'You learned the kanji means <kanji>product</kanji>, and the vocab here can mean that too, but more often it will have a broader meaning like <vocabulary>article</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>item</vocabulary>. You might hear a salesclerk refer to their goods as 品, but you''ll also see it in phrases like 思い出の品 (a memento) and 感謝の品 (a token of gratitude). ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3820', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%93%81', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:59:12.154472Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:54:24.248635Z'),
(3821, 'vocabulary', '新品', '新品', 'A <kanji>new</kanji> <kanji>product</kanji> is something that''s <vocabulary>brand new</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>new item</vocabulary>. I like looking around the store for the brand new stuff because it''s interesting!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3821', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E5%93%81', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:59:20.928166Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(3822, 'vocabulary', '商品', '商品', '<kanji>Articles</kanji> of <kanji>merchandise</kanji> are still <vocabulary>merchandise</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3822', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%95%86%E5%93%81', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:59:29.232112Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(3823, 'vocabulary', '喜ぶ', '喜ぶ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>rejoice</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to rejoice</vocabulary>. Though <vocabulary>to become happy</vocabulary> probably sounds a little more natural.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3823', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%9C%E3%81%B6', 17, 1, '2012-09-20T18:59:36.053707Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:34.785454Z'),
(3824, 'vocabulary', '公園', '公園', 'A <kanji>public</kanji> <kanji>park</kanji> is a park that is public. It''s a <vocabulary>public park</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3824', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AC%E5%9C%92', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:59:45.766858Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.015118Z'),
(3825, 'vocabulary', '園', '園', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

This word is generally used in the names of gardens, like エデンの園 and 桜の園. It''s also used in phrases like 学びの園, which means "school" and carries the connotation of being a haven for learning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3825', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%92', 33, 1, '2012-09-20T18:59:51.869338Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.16324Z'),
(3826, 'vocabulary', '存じる', '存じる', 'This is like a very formal and humble way of saying <vocabulary>to know</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to believe</vocabulary>. Sure, the kanji means <kanji>suppose</kanji>, but think of it this way... When you say "I suppose" it''s like a humble way of saying "I know" or "I believe." You''re not being as direct about it this way, and in Japanese, that makes you more humble and polite. So, that''s why 存じる means <vocabulary>to know</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to believe</vocabulary>.

Note this word is typically used in the polite ます form. When you’re humble, you may want to be as polite as possible, I suppose!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3826', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%98%E3%81%98%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T18:59:59.805693Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.030105Z'),
(3827, 'vocabulary', '生存', '生存', '<kanji>Exist</kanji>ing <kanji>life</kanji> is in <vocabulary>existence</vocabulary> on this planet... What would that say about <vocabulary>life</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>survival</vocabulary> in such an unforgiving place?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3827', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E5%AD%98', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:00:14.497659Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.044217Z'),
(3828, 'vocabulary', '守る', '守る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>protect</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to protect</vocabulary> and also <vocabulary>to defend</vocabulary>. This can also mean <vocabulary>to obey</vocabulary>, in the sense of obeying the rules.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3828', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%88%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:00:27.892923Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.057954Z'),
(3829, 'vocabulary', '留守', '留守', 'You have gotten a mission. You must go out and <kanji>detain</kanji> a dangerous criminal to <kanji>protect</kanji> your country. While you go do that, you will be <vocabulary>away from home</vocabulary> and have an <vocabulary>absence</vocabulary> from society.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3829', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%99%E5%AE%88', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:00:34.775901Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.070436Z'),
(3830, 'vocabulary', '真実', '真実', 'The <kanji>reality</kanji> of the <kanji>truth</kanji> is nothing more than the <vocabulary>truth</vocabulary> and the <vocabulary>reality</vocabulary>. Doesn''t get any more real than this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3830', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%9F%E5%AE%9F', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:00:42.674079Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.096574Z'),
(3831, 'vocabulary', '専門', '専門', 'Your <kanji>specialty</kanji> <kanji>gate</kanji> is the thing you open to let out your <vocabulary>specialty</vocabulary>. Imagine the thing you''re best at (your specialty). Now imagine opening a gate in your chest that lets it out, allowing you to be good at it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3831', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%82%E9%96%80', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:00:50.48254Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.108935Z'),
(3832, 'vocabulary', '不幸', '不幸', 'When you do <kanji>not</kanji> have <kanji>happiness</kanji>, you have <vocabulary>unhappiness</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>misfortune</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>misery</vocabulary>. This can also mean <vocabulary>death</vocabulary>, usually of a family member.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3832', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%B9%B8', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:01:02.18434Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.125848Z'),
(3833, 'vocabulary', '幸せ', '幸せ', 'This is the noun version of the kanji 幸. The kanji means <kanji>happiness</kanji> and the vocab word means <vocabulary>happiness</vocabulary>.

Compared to 幸福, 幸せ is more commonly used in everyday language and refers to a more immediate, personal sense of happiness or contentment.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3833', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B8%E3%81%9B', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:01:12.443389Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.390991Z'),
(3834, 'vocabulary', '政府', '政府', '<kanji>Politics</kanji> and <kanji>government</kanji>. They are needed to keep the <vocabulary>government</vocabulary> rolling.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3834', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BF%E5%BA%9C', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:01:19.418739Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.140433Z'),
(3835, 'vocabulary', '弁当', '弁当', 'You know how 弁 means <kanji>dialect</kanji> and 当 means <kanji>success</kanji>? Well, this isn''t going to help you much, because this means <vocabulary>bento</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>box lunch</vocabulary>. Chances are, though, you know the word "bento" from English. So, if you read this word (which you should be able to) you can figure out it''s べんとう. If you do that, you can realize this word means <vocabulary>bento</vocabulary> too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3835', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%81%E5%BD%93', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:01:29.050663Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.169691Z'),
(3836, 'vocabulary', '東京弁', '東京弁', 'You know that 東京 is <kanji>Tokyo</kanji>, and you know that 弁 is <kanji>dialect</kanji>, so here you have the <vocabulary>Tokyo dialect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3836', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%B1%E4%BA%AC%E5%BC%81', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:01:52.820591Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.291181Z'),
(3837, 'vocabulary', '〜弁', '〜弁', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. The 〜 is there to show this attaches onto things, like 東京弁, meaning "Tokyo Dialect."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3837', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%BC%81', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:02:01.772882Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.349423Z'),
(3838, 'vocabulary', '急ぐ', '急ぐ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>hurry</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to hurry</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3838', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A5%E3%81%90', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:02:11.042205Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.429228Z'),
(3839, 'vocabulary', '急に', '急に', 'Something that happens in a <kanji>sudden</kanji> way happens <vocabulary>suddenly</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>immediately</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>abruptly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3839', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A5%E3%81%AB', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:02:29.802258Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.459334Z'),
(3840, 'vocabulary', '急死', '急死', 'A <kanji>sudden</kanji> <kanji>death</kanji> makes for just that, a <vocabulary>sudden death</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3840', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A5%E6%AD%BB', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:02:47.683597Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.496705Z'),
(3841, 'vocabulary', '急行', '急行', 'Things that <kanji>hurry</kanji> and <kanji>go</kanji> faster than the other things are <vocabulary>express</vocabulary>. This is often used with trains or subways, so look out for it if you''re looking to get somewhere in a hurry. Also be careful in case it doesn''t stop at your stop, because 急行 trains don''t stop at every station (which is what makes them express!).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3841', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A5%E8%A1%8C', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:02:54.193562Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.518992Z'),
(3842, 'vocabulary', '政治', '政治', 'If you want to <kanji>cure</kanji> the <kanji>government</kanji>, you have to get into <vocabulary>politics</vocabulary>. Gotta join ''em to fix them... but can you survive that long without being corrupted yourself?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3842', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BF%E6%B2%BB', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:03:01.148118Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.551882Z'),
(3843, 'vocabulary', '書く', '書く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>write</kanji> so the verb version means <vocabulary>to write</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3843', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B8%E3%81%8F', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:04:06.760971Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.57999Z'),
(3844, 'vocabulary', '残品', '残品', 'The <kanji>remainder</kanji> <kanji>product</kanji>s are the things that haven''t been sold. They''re the <vocabulary>unsold merchandise</vocabulary>. They''re the <vocabulary>remaining stock</vocabulary>.

This is a very specific word that is mainly used in the context of inventory management or retail sales.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3844', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%8B%E5%93%81', 27, 1, '2012-09-20T19:04:20.098896Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(3845, 'vocabulary', '治す', '治す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. 

The kanji itself means <kanji>cure</kanji>, and this verb means <vocabulary>to cure</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to heal</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to ease</vocabulary> something. This particular verb is when someone does the curing. The illness or injury doesn''t just get better by itself. How can you remember that? Because when you try to heal someone and you mess up, they can try to <reading>sue</reading> (す) you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3845', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%BB%E3%81%99', 17, 1, '2012-09-20T19:04:27.596743Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:35.303953Z'),
(3846, 'vocabulary', '全治', '全治', '<kanji>All</kanji> <kanji>cure</kanji>d! That means you''ve made a <vocabulary>complete recovery</vocabulary>! You''re <vocabulary>completely healed</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3846', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E6%B2%BB', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:04:41.279781Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.594229Z'),
(3847, 'vocabulary', '不治', '不治', 'When you can <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>cure</kanji> something, this is a problem of <vocabulary>incurability</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3847', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%B2%BB', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:04:48.283175Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.608913Z'),
(3848, 'vocabulary', '明治', '明治', 'It’s the era of <kanji>bright</kanji> <kanji>rule</kanji>! If this doesn''t make sense, don''t worry about it. Focus more on the reading, which is めいじ. Sound familiar? This is the famous <vocabulary>Meiji era</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>Meiji Emperor</vocabulary>. If you don''t know about it consider reading up, it''s interesting history!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3848', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%8E%E6%B2%BB', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:04:54.75314Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(3849, 'vocabulary', '浴びる', '浴びる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>bathe</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to bathe in</vocabulary>.

This verb is transitive, and the object is whatever you''re "bathing in". It could be a bath or a shower, but it could also be the sun. So you can both シャワーを浴びる and 日の光を浴びる. More negatively, this can also mean <vocabulary>to be exposed to</vocabulary> something, such as criticism or radioactivity.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3849', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B4%E3%81%B3%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:05:01.595804Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.628479Z'),
(3850, 'vocabulary', '日光浴', '日光浴', 'You know that 日光 is "sunlight" so when you add <kanji>bathe</kanji> you get "sunlight bathe" ... but that sounds dumb, so let''s call this word <vocabulary>sunbathe</vocabulary> instead!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3850', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E5%85%89%E6%B5%B4', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:05:09.522224Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.640436Z'),
(3851, 'vocabulary', '特急', '特急', 'Something that''s <kanji>special</kanji> and in a <kanji>hurry</kanji> must be special indeed. This refers to <vocabulary>limited express train</vocabulary>s in Japan. They go fast, they stop at fewer stops (because they''re special!) and they hurry. Look out for these if you want to get somewhere in a jiffy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3851', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%B9%E6%80%A5', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:05:21.655311Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.651826Z'),
(3852, 'vocabulary', '留学', '留学', 'You <kanji>detain</kanji> your <kanji>study</kanji> and take it away to a foreign place. That foreign place is having you <vocabulary>study abroad</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3852', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%99%E5%AD%A6', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:05:30.062501Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.664405Z'),
(3853, 'vocabulary', '写真', '写真', 'A <kanji>copy</kanji> of <kanji>reality</kanji> is a lot like magic. How do you make a copy of reality, though? You take a <vocabulary>photo</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3853', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%99%E7%9C%9F', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:05:36.615438Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.678873Z'),
(3854, 'vocabulary', '真っ黒', '真っ黒', 'The <kanji>reality</kanji> is that it''s <kanji>black</kanji>. Like, so black that it''s beyond fake black, it''s reality black. This is so black it''s <vocabulary>pitch black</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3854', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%9F%E3%81%A3%E9%BB%92', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:05:57.706237Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.702511Z'),
(3855, 'vocabulary', '幸福', '幸福', 'With a little <kanji>happiness</kanji> and <kanji>luck</kanji> you can achieve <vocabulary>happiness</vocabulary>, though that goes without saying.

幸福 is often used to discuss happiness in a broader, more abstract sense, like the happiness of a population or the concept of happiness in philosophical contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3855', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B8%E7%A6%8F', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:06:33.366239Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.74246Z'),
(3856, 'vocabulary', '笑う', '笑う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>laugh</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to laugh</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3856', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AC%91%E3%81%86', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:06:49.56634Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.75449Z'),
(3857, 'vocabulary', '笑顔', '笑顔', 'A <kanji>laugh</kanji>ing <kanji>face</kanji> is a <vocabulary> smiling face</vocabulary> (or just a <vocabulary>smile</vocabulary>!). 笑顔s all around!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3857', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AC%91%E9%A1%94', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:07:40.773995Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.766022Z'),
(3858, 'vocabulary', 'ゴミ箱', 'ゴミ箱', 'ゴミ means "garbage." Add a <kanji>box</kanji> to it and you have a garbage box aka a <vocabulary>garbage can</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>garbage bin</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3858', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%B4%E3%83%9F%E7%AE%B1', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:07:47.517349Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.786019Z'),
(3859, 'vocabulary', '箱', '箱', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3859', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AE%B1', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:08:14.117529Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.808865Z'),
(3860, 'vocabulary', '荷物', '荷物', 'A <kanji>luggage</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji> is your <vocabulary>luggage</vocabulary>. It''s also your <vocabulary>baggage</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>load</vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3860', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8D%B7%E7%89%A9', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:08:30.589667Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.838233Z'),
(3861, 'vocabulary', '証', '証', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3861', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%BC', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:08:38.098478Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.895837Z'),
(3862, 'vocabulary', '証人', '証人', 'An <kanji>evidence</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>witness</vocabulary>, because witnesses are people who provide evidence (you know, with their mouths or something).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3862', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%BC%E4%BA%BA', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:08:48.316845Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.909974Z'),
(3863, 'vocabulary', '証言', '証言', '<kanji>Evidence</kanji> that you <kanji>say</kanji> is <vocabulary>testimony</vocabulary>. You could also say you''re providing <vocabulary>verbal evidence</vocabulary>. Hopefully you won''t have to give too much of this in your lifetime.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3863', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%BC%E8%A8%80', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:08:55.665577Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.924986Z'),
(3864, 'vocabulary', '私自身', '私自身', '<kanji>I</kanji> am talking about my <kanji>self</kanji>, my own <kanji>body</kanji>… In other words, this is about me <vocabulary>personally</vocabulary> or what I <vocabulary>myself</vocabulary> have done.

私自身 literally means "I myself," and it''s used to emphasize your personal involvement in something. For example, you may say 私自身の考え to highlight that something is your own thought, or 私自身から言う, to really stress that you''ll personally be the one to say something to someone, without going through any intermediaries. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3864', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%81%E8%87%AA%E8%BA%AB', 8, 1, '2012-09-20T19:09:24.27168Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:15:26.006499Z'),
(3865, 'vocabulary', '辞書', '辞書', 'Take all the <kanji>word</kanji>s you use in <kanji>writing</kanji>, put them together and you have a <vocabulary>dictionary</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3865', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%9E%E6%9B%B8', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:09:47.146778Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.953536Z'),
(3866, 'vocabulary', '辞める', '辞める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>quit</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to quit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3866', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%9E%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:09:52.823543Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.001374Z'),
(3867, 'vocabulary', 'お世辞', 'お世辞', 'When you <kanji>quit</kanji> your current <kanji>generation</kanji> to join an older one, you''ll need to use all kinds of <vocabulary>flattery</vocabulary>. Older people love being flattered; so be nice and give them some <vocabulary>compliment</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3867', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E4%B8%96%E8%BE%9E', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:10:00.190198Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.023408Z'),
(3868, 'vocabulary', '遠い', '遠い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>far</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>far</vocabulary> (or it''s <vocabulary>far away</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3868', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%A0%E3%81%84', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:10:06.989347Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.03859Z'),
(3869, 'vocabulary', '入門', '入門', 'If you are able to <kanji>enter</kanji> the <kanji>gate</kanji> then you have gained <vocabulary>admission</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>entrance</vocabulary> to the facility.  Sometimes you do this to start an <vocabulary>apprenticeship</vocabulary>, where you get an <vocabulary>introduction</vocabulary> to some sweet new skills, like how to make an entrance.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3869', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E9%96%80', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:10:14.409898Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.053377Z'),
(3870, 'vocabulary', '関する', '関する', 'You know that 関 means <kanji>related</kanji>. So, when you add する to it you make it a verb. Now it means <vocabulary>to be related to</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3870', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%A2%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:10:23.007973Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.066703Z'),
(3871, 'vocabulary', '関係', '関係', 'A <kanji>connection</kanji> <kanji>related</kanji> to another <kanji>connection</kanji> obviously has some kind of <vocabulary>connection</vocabulary>, at least between all those meanings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3871', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%A2%E4%BF%82', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:10:30.325932Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.115285Z'),
(3872, 'vocabulary', '阪神', '阪神', '<kanji>Osaka</kanji> and the <kanji>god</kanji>s… ? Actually, this is a region known as <vocabulary>Hanshin</vocabulary>. It''s the area between Osaka (大阪) and Kobe (神戸). Think of it this way: 大阪 + 神戸 = 阪神. Isn''t that clever? There''s even a Hanshin railway company and a Hanshin Tigers baseball team — both based in the 阪神 area, of course.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3872', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%98%AA%E7%A5%9E', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:10:52.085058Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:35:01.65414Z'),
(3873, 'vocabulary', '前面', '前面', 'The <kanji>front</kanji> <kanji>face</kanji> of something is the <vocabulary>front</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>front side</vocabulary> of it (kind of like how 外面 is the exterior because it''s the outside face). This also means <vocabulary>fore</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>forefront</vocabulary>, especially when used metaphorically.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3873', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%8D%E9%9D%A2', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:11:13.263211Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T20:03:17.721568Z'),
(3874, 'vocabulary', '地面', '地面', 'The <kanji>ground</kanji>/<kanji>earth</kanji> <kanji>face</kanji> is the surface that makes up the <vocabulary>ground</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>Earth''s surface</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3874', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E9%9D%A2', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:11:21.86814Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.149855Z'),
(3875, 'vocabulary', '外面', '外面', 'The <kanji>outside</kanji> <kanji>face</kanji> to something is the <vocabulary>external appearance</vocabulary> of that thing.

While 外面 can refer to the outer surface of an object, it’s more commonly used to describe someone''s <vocabulary>outward appearance</vocabulary>, behavior, or demeanor on the <vocabulary>surface</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3875', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E9%9D%A2', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:11:30.644233Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(3876, 'vocabulary', '画面', '画面', 'The <kanji>image</kanji> <kanji>surface</kanji> is a <vocabulary>screen</vocabulary>. Just imagine your computer screen. There''s an image on the surface, right? It just happens to move around all the time, like most images on screens these days.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3876', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%BB%E9%9D%A2', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:11:40.631893Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.175505Z'),
(3877, 'vocabulary', '面-3877', '面', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3877', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%A2-3877', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:11:47.705465Z', '2020-05-19T22:26:06.339245Z', '2026-02-25T19:07:32.082027Z'),
(3878, 'vocabulary', '面白い', '面白い', 'The <kanji>face</kanji> of this object is so <kanji>white</kanji>! Why is it so white compared to everything else? This is so <vocabulary>interesting</vocabulary>...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3878', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%A2%E7%99%BD%E3%81%84', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:12:03.537125Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.186955Z'),
(3879, 'vocabulary', '図書館', '図書館', 'A <kanji>public building</kanji> where <kanji>writte</kanji>n things are all <kanji>diagram</kanji>ed out in a way that lets you find said written things? That sounds like a <vocabulary>library</vocabulary> to me!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3879', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%B3%E6%9B%B8%E9%A4%A8', 16, 1, '2012-09-20T19:12:13.143279Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.200167Z'),
(3880, 'vocabulary', '消化する', '消化する', 'You know how 消化 is "digestion"? Well, The verb form of that is this word, which is <vocabulary>to digest</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3880', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B6%88%E5%8C%96%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-09-20T23:52:10.402083Z', NULL, '2025-11-20T03:47:15.21122Z'),
(3881, 'vocabulary', '消える', '消える', 'You''ve seen that 消す is "to extinguish." So, this is another verb version of that. This time, you''re not doing the extinguishing, it''s just happening. Something is going <vocabulary>to disappear</vocabulary> on its own. How can you differentiate this from 消す? Well, える allows you to notice how the object disappeared into thin <reading>air</reading> (える). You know you didn''t do it, so it disappeared on its own, making it <vocabulary>to disappear</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3881', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B6%88%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-09-20T23:52:32.396764Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:36.104807Z'),
(3882, 'vocabulary', '気持ち悪い', '気持ち悪い', 'The 気持ち (feeling) is <kanji>bad</kanji>. So, you can imagine how you feel isn''t good, it''s a <vocabulary>bad feeling</vocabulary>. You''re <vocabulary>feeling terrible</vocabulary>, in fact.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3882', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E6%8C%81%E3%81%A1%E6%82%AA%E3%81%84', 14, 1, '2012-09-20T23:54:15.88087Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(3883, 'vocabulary', '流れる', '流れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>stream</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>to stream</vocabulary> (though it''s also <vocabulary>to flow</vocabulary>, a very similar word). You can differentiate this from 流す because with 流す you flush something down the toilet, which causes it to overflow so you <reading>sue</reading> (す) the toilet maker. With 流れる you find a <reading>rare</reading> (れる) jewel trying to flow down the river. You grab it, and see it''s a rare diamond!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3883', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%81%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-09-20T23:55:02.625267Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:36.184563Z'),
(3884, 'vocabulary', '動かす', '動かす', 'You''ve seen 動く hopefully, and know it means <vocabulary>to move</vocabulary>. This is similar to that, but now you''re moving something else. You know this because as you go <vocabulary>to move something</vocabulary> the thing you''re moving starts to <reading>cuss</reading> (かす) you out because it''s angry. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3884', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%95%E3%81%8B%E3%81%99', 14, 1, '2012-09-21T00:00:13.715776Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:36.207625Z'),
(3885, 'vocabulary', '着ける', '着ける', 'Another verb for this kanji! Crazy! I know, I''m sorry. The previous ones were: 着る (to wear), 着く (to arrive), and now 着ける (to put on / to wear). At least it''s similar to 着る, right? In this case, this word is like 付ける, which means "to attach." It''s used for things you "attach" to your body to wear, like accessories, or a face mask. That''s why this is <vocabulary>to put on</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to wear</vocabulary>. Same sound, different kanji, similar ideas.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3885', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9D%80%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-09-21T00:05:01.307582Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(3886, 'vocabulary', '勉強する', '勉強する', 'You''ve seen 勉強 (study) by now, right? Well, this is the verb version of that. This is <vocabulary>to study</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3886', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%89%E5%BC%B7%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-09-21T18:27:55.827539Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:05:21.088648Z'),
(3887, 'vocabulary', '旅行する', '旅行する', 'You''ve hopefully seen 旅行 by this point. This is the verb version of that. Instead of "trip" it''s <vocabulary>to travel</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to take a trip</vocabulary>. These are fun to do. You should do them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3887', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%85%E8%A1%8C%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-09-21T18:32:09.84285Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:48:50.473642Z'),
(3888, 'vocabulary', '温める', '温める', 'You know that the kanji 温 means <kanji>warm</kanji>, and this verb is related. It means <vocabulary>to heat something up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to warm something up</vocabulary>. It''s all about you doing something to another object to make it hot, like putting it in the microwave. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3888', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%A9%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 13, 1, '2012-09-21T18:37:26.186887Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:36.301931Z'),
(3889, 'vocabulary', '起こす', '起こす', 'You''ve seen 起きる which is "to wake up." This is similar, except you are waking someone else up, which makes the meaning of this word <vocabulary>to wake someone up</vocabulary>. How do you differentiate between this and 起きる? Because of the す on the end. You wake someone up violently, wrenching their neck. They turn around and <reading>sue</reading> (す) you, making you wish you never woke them up.

This can also mean <vocabulary>to cause something</vocabulary>. I guess if you wake someone up and they''re not a morning person, you could cause something... like trouble.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3889', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%B7%E3%81%93%E3%81%99', 14, 1, '2012-09-21T18:37:28.887511Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:36.33107Z'),
(3890, 'vocabulary', '旅行者', '旅行者', 'You''ve seen that 旅行 is "trip." So who is <kanji>someone</kanji> who goes on trips? That is a <vocabulary>tourist</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>traveler</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3890', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%85%E8%A1%8C%E8%80%85', 14, 1, '2012-09-21T18:37:46.000671Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(3891, 'vocabulary', '悪口', '悪口', 'Your <kanji>bad</kanji> <kanji>mouth</kanji> produces nothing but <vocabulary>bad-mouthing</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>trash-talk</vocabulary>.

悪口 often targets someone''s character, appearance, or abilities, with the intention of hurting them or damaging their reputation, sometimes to their face, but often behind their back. It gives rise to unfavorable information that can spread through gossip.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3891', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E5%8F%A3', 14, 1, '2012-09-21T18:43:50.838806Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:36.375946Z'),
(3892, 'vocabulary', '不良', '不良', 'Something that''s <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>good</kanji> is <vocabulary>bad</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>substandard</vocabulary>. This usage is more formal and frequently paired with other kanji compounds. Alternatively, it can refer to someone who is a  <vocabulary>delinquent</vocabulary>, in which case it is often used as a standalone word. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3892', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E8%89%AF', 11, 1, '2012-09-25T17:40:31.903711Z', NULL, '2025-12-23T01:35:37.611412Z'),
(3893, 'vocabulary', '不器用', '不器用', 'You know how 器用 means "dextrous" and "skillful"? Well, this is when you''re <kanji>not</kanji> those things. When you''re not dextrous, you''re <vocabulary>clumsy</vocabulary> and when you''re not skillful you''re <vocabulary>unskillful</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3893', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%99%A8%E7%94%A8', 15, 1, '2012-09-25T17:48:06.144468Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.506011Z'),
(3894, 'vocabulary', 'お願い', 'お願い', 'The noun form of <kanji>request</kanji> (don''t be tricked by the い on the end, it''s not an adjective). So, request goes to <vocabulary>a request</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3894', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E9%A1%98%E3%81%84', 15, 1, '2012-09-25T17:57:03.001964Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.516469Z'),
(3895, 'vocabulary', '飲み放題', '飲み放題', '<kanji>Drink</kanji>ing <kanji>as much as you want</kanji> is nice and all, but don''t get sick. When you see this word, you know you''re at an <vocabulary>all you can drink</vocabulary> place. Tip: Usually there''s a time limit, so drink up!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3895', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%B2%E3%81%BF%E6%94%BE%E9%A1%8C', 15, 1, '2012-09-26T17:33:43.057034Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.52646Z'),
(3896, 'vocabulary', '食べ放題', '食べ放題', 'When you <kanji>eat</kanji> <kanji>as much as you want</kanji>, you are getting an <vocabulary>all you can eat</vocabulary> deal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3896', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9F%E3%81%B9%E6%94%BE%E9%A1%8C', 15, 1, '2012-09-26T17:33:58.395949Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.536141Z'),
(3897, 'vocabulary', '良好', '良好', 'A <kanji>good</kanji> <kanji>like</kanji> is something you like really good, if that makes any sense. This thing is a <vocabulary>good</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>favorable</vocabulary> thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3897', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%89%AF%E5%A5%BD', 14, 1, '2012-09-26T22:42:16.991637Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:36.531427Z'),
(3898, 'vocabulary', '人殺し', '人殺し', 'When you <kanji>kill</kanji> a <kanji>person</kanji> that''s <vocabulary>murder</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3898', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E6%AE%BA%E3%81%97', 15, 1, '2012-09-27T22:04:59.660677Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.555038Z'),
(3899, 'vocabulary', '殺人者', '殺人者', 'You''ve hopefully seen 殺人 (murder) from level 13. So, <kanji>someone</kanji> who does <kanji>murder</kanji> is what? A <vocabulary>murderer</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3899', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%BA%E4%BA%BA%E8%80%85', 15, 1, '2012-09-27T22:06:55.539856Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.564032Z'),
(3900, 'vocabulary', '指輪', '指輪', 'A <kanji>finger</kanji> <kanji>ring</kanji> is the kind of <vocabulary>ring</vocabulary> you''d put on your finger. That''s just simply a "ring" though, so you''ll have to know what "ring" this is via context.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3900', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87%E8%BC%AA', 15, 1, '2012-10-01T18:37:54.97742Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.573138Z'),
(3901, 'vocabulary', '輪ゴム', '輪ゴム', 'A <kanji>ring</kanji> of gum (ゴム) (which is what Japanese people call rubber quite often) is a ring of rubber. What is a ring of rubber that people use often? It''s a <vocabulary>rubber band</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3901', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%AA%E3%82%B4%E3%83%A0', 15, 1, '2012-10-01T18:40:49.085849Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.582555Z'),
(3902, 'vocabulary', '整理する', '整理する', 'When you <kanji>arrange</kanji> something with <kanji>reason</kanji> you''re arranging it. That''s why this is <vocabulary>to arrange</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3902', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B4%E7%90%86%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-10-01T19:00:24.505741Z', NULL, '2025-10-26T02:33:27.730387Z'),
(3903, 'vocabulary', '整理', '整理', 'When you <kanji>arrange</kanji> something according to <kanji>reason</kanji>, the result is an <vocabulary>arrangement</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3903', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B4%E7%90%86', 13, 1, '2012-10-01T19:01:40.946981Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:36.698388Z'),
(3904, 'vocabulary', '暗記する', '暗記する', 'Hopefully you''ve seen 暗記 before now. It means "memorization." So, turn that into a verb and you have <vocabulary>to memorize</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3904', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%97%E8%A8%98%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 15, 1, '2012-10-01T19:37:13.088559Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.591457Z'),
(3905, 'vocabulary', '練習する', '練習する', 'You''ve hopefully seen 練習, which means "practice." So, if you make it into a verb, you have <vocabulary>to practice</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3905', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%B4%E7%BF%92%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 15, 1, '2012-10-01T19:39:37.780717Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.600322Z'),
(3906, 'vocabulary', '映す', '映す', 'You know that 映る is "to be reflected." Now you''re doing the reflecting, which is really projecting (light). That''s why this word is <vocabulary>to project</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3906', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%A0%E3%81%99', 12, 1, '2012-10-01T19:39:56.88752Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:36.775161Z'),
(3907, 'vocabulary', '料理人', '料理人', 'You''ve seen 料理 and probably know it means "cooking." So, what is a <kanji>person</kanji> who does cooking? That is <vocabulary>a cook</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>chef</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3907', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%99%E7%90%86%E4%BA%BA', 16, 1, '2012-10-02T23:52:10.502581Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.224787Z'),
(3908, 'vocabulary', '芸術家', '芸術家', 'You know that 芸術 is <kanji>art</kanji> so you''d think that 芸術家 would be something like "art house." Well, it''s not. Think of it this way: Who is the person who works for "House Art"? It''s the <vocabulary>artist</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3908', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B8%E8%A1%93%E5%AE%B6', 16, 1, '2012-10-02T23:54:34.960965Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.238764Z'),
(3909, 'vocabulary', '自動', '自動', 'Something that can <kanji>move</kanji> by its <kanji>self</kanji> is something that''s <vocabulary>automatic</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3909', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E5%8B%95', 14, 1, '2012-10-02T23:57:44.287246Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:36.846207Z'),
(3910, 'vocabulary', '自動的', '自動的', 'An <kanji>automatic</kanji><kanji>al</kanji> (自動 + 的) thing of course isn''t "automatical." A better way to say it is to cut off the -al and call it <vocabulary>automatic</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3910', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E5%8B%95%E7%9A%84', 16, 1, '2012-10-02T23:58:01.435651Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.250877Z'),
(3911, 'vocabulary', '基本的', '基本的', 'The things that are <kanji>basics</kanji> (基本) <kanji>like</kanji> (的) are <vocabulary>basic</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>fundamental</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3911', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%BA%E6%9C%AC%E7%9A%84', 16, 1, '2012-10-02T23:59:09.260486Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.264059Z'),
(3912, 'vocabulary', '折り目', '折り目', 'A <kanji>fold</kanji> you can see with your <kanji>eye</kanji> is a <vocabulary>fold</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>crease</vocabulary> in something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3912', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%98%E3%82%8A%E7%9B%AE', 16, 1, '2012-10-05T18:33:06.619571Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.276238Z'),
(3913, 'vocabulary', '卒業', '卒業', 'The <kanji>business</kanji> of you <kanji>graduating</kanji> is your <vocabulary>graduation</vocabulary>. If you think about it, it''s kind of a big business, getting you graduated from expensive schools. The graduation business focuses on graduations, and getting you there (hint: this business is a school, probably).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3913', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%92%E6%A5%AD', 14, 1, '2012-10-05T18:42:03.585669Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(3914, 'vocabulary', '卒業式', '卒業式', 'You know that 卒業 is <kanji>graduation</kanji> so what about when you add <kanji>ceremony</kanji> to it? That makes it a <vocabulary>graduation ceremony</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3914', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%92%E6%A5%AD%E5%BC%8F', 16, 1, '2012-10-05T18:42:27.72064Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.289952Z'),
(3915, 'vocabulary', '不完全', '不完全', 'You know that 完全 means "complete" or "perfect." So, a <kanji>not</kanji> "complete" or "perfect" is something that is <vocabulary>incomplete</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>imperfect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3915', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%AE%8C%E5%85%A8', 16, 1, '2012-10-08T23:14:20.359067Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.301067Z'),
(3916, 'vocabulary', '美術館', '美術館', 'You know 美術 is <kanji>fine art</kanji>. So what is a <kanji>public building</kanji> that contains said fine art? It''s a <vocabulary>museum</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3916', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%8E%E8%A1%93%E9%A4%A8', 16, 1, '2012-10-08T23:22:42.89909Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.311876Z'),
(3917, 'vocabulary', '折り紙', '折り紙', 'You know that 折る is "to fold," so what about "to fold <kanji>paper</kanji>"? Ever heard of something that involves lots of folding of paper that is Japanese? Most likely you have. This is <vocabulary>origami</vocabulary>, aka the <vocabulary>art of paper folding</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3917', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%98%E3%82%8A%E7%B4%99', 16, 1, '2012-10-09T05:53:00.051418Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.32408Z'),
(3918, 'vocabulary', '私立大学', '私立大学', 'You''ve seen 私立, which is "private," and you''ve seen 大学, which is "college" or "university." So, combine them together and you have a <vocabulary>private college</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>private university</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3918', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%81%E7%AB%8B%E5%A4%A7%E5%AD%A6', 8, 1, '2012-10-09T06:09:22.793396Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(3919, 'vocabulary', '希望', '希望', 'Both your <kanji>hope</kanji>s and <kanji>wish</kanji>es are held within this vocab word. So, what is the meaning of this word? It is both <vocabulary>hope</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>wish</vocabulary>. Either will work.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3919', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%8C%E6%9C%9B', 14, 1, '2012-10-09T17:42:43.611809Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:37.093197Z'),
(3920, 'vocabulary', '希望する', '希望する', 'You know that 希望 is a "wish." When you "do a wish," that means <vocabulary>to wish</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to request</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to hope</vocabulary>.

This is a formal way to say that you wish for something, so you might use it at work to request an opportunity to work abroad, or let them know politely that you''re resigning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3920', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%8C%E6%9C%9B%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2012-10-09T17:43:55.20392Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.337834Z'),
(3921, 'vocabulary', '技術', '技術', 'You''ve seen 技 as a vocab word meaning "technique," and the kanji 術 means <kanji>technique</kanji>. So this means <vocabulary>technique</vocabulary>! Your technique is also your <vocabulary>craft</vocabulary>, and what’s needed for <vocabulary>technology</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3921', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%80%E8%A1%93', 16, 1, '2012-10-09T17:57:19.509007Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.352909Z'),
(3922, 'vocabulary', '妥協する', '妥協する', 'You know that 妥協 means <kanji>compromise</kanji>. So, what happens when you make it a verb? It becomes <vocabulary>to compromise</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3922', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%A5%E5%8D%94%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2012-10-09T19:43:16.941018Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.36606Z'),
(3923, 'vocabulary', '妥協', '妥協', 'When you have <kanji>gentle</kanji> <kanji>cooperation</kanji> nobody has their way. You''re very gently coming towards the middle, coming to a <vocabulary>compromise</vocabulary>. (Whether you like it or not.)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3923', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%A5%E5%8D%94', 14, 1, '2012-10-09T19:44:04.56655Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T22:03:16.865544Z'),
(3924, 'vocabulary', '要求', '要求', 'You <kanji>need</kanji> this <kanji>request</kanji> to happen, so you don''t just ask nicely, you <vocabulary>demand</vocabulary> it. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3924', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%81%E6%B1%82', 10, 1, '2012-10-09T19:45:14.460133Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.185635Z'),
(3925, 'vocabulary', '要求する', '要求する', 'You hopefully remember that 要求 means "demand" . When you make that a verb, it becomes <vocabulary>to demand something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3925', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%81%E6%B1%82%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2012-10-09T19:45:31.192376Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:53:56.46731Z'),
(3926, 'vocabulary', '消化', '消化', 'What is something that will first <kanji>extinguish</kanji> something and then <kanji>change</kanji> it? One thing I can think of is your body. It takes in food, extinguishes it in your stomach, then changes it into things your body can use (and then excrete). That''s why this "extinguish-change" process is your <vocabulary>digestion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3926', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B6%88%E5%8C%96', 12, 1, '2012-10-09T23:59:38.691473Z', NULL, '2025-11-20T03:47:15.21122Z'),
(3927, 'vocabulary', '消化不良', '消化不良', 'You''ve hopefully seen 消化 by now and know that it means "digestion." If you know that, then you can see that the "digestion" is <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>good</kanji>. When your digestion is not good, what do you have? <vocabulary>Indigestion</vocabulary>. Excuse me, I have to run to the restroom...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3927', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B6%88%E5%8C%96%E4%B8%8D%E8%89%AF', 14, 1, '2012-10-10T00:02:43.169078Z', NULL, '2025-11-20T03:47:15.21122Z'),
(3928, 'vocabulary', '不合格', '不合格', 'You already learned 合格する (to pass a test). Well, slap 不 (not) in front of 合格 and you get <vocabulary>failing a test</vocabulary>.

One thing to note: unlike 合格, 不合格 can''t combine with する to be made into a verb. It''s typically just used as a noun to describe the state of having failed or been rejected. So if you pass a test, feel free to shout something like 合格した! But if you fail, it''s better to just mumble 不合格だった...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3928', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%90%88%E6%A0%BC', 15, 1, '2012-10-10T00:08:08.159003Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.629033Z'),
(3929, 'vocabulary', '卒業する', '卒業する', 'You know that 卒業 is "graduation." Make it a verb, and you have <vocabulary>to graduate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3929', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%92%E6%A5%AD%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2012-10-10T00:12:27.374364Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.417669Z'),
(3930, 'vocabulary', '残す', '残す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>remainder</kanji>, but you can''t really "to remainder." What is something that''s similar that would make sense? How about <vocabulary>to leave behind</vocabulary>. This is when you''re actually doing the leaving behind too. For example, you eat some food and you leave behind your rice because you''re a bad child. You can remember this because of す. Every time you leave some food behind your mom would <reading>sue</reading> you, because she was a terrible mom. Imagine this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3930', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%8B%E3%81%99', 16, 1, '2012-10-10T00:17:10.720829Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.440183Z'),
(3931, 'vocabulary', '固める', '固める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>hard</kanji> so the verb vocab version? <vocabulary>To make something hard</vocabulary>. You need to differentiate this from 固まる, though, which means "to harden" (the hardening happens on its own). This word means "to make something hard," because you''re doing the hardening. You can remember this because you want to make something hard. And to do that, you send special "<reading>mail</reading>" (める) to this thing you want to make hard, and this mail does that for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3931', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BA%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2012-10-10T00:17:50.271693Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.467884Z'),
(3932, 'vocabulary', '変事', '変事', 'You know 変 means "change," but it can also mean "strange." So a <kanji>strange</kanji> <kanji>occurrence</kanji> is an incident that is unlikely. In other words, an <vocabulary>unexpected incident</vocabulary> or an <vocabulary>unusual incident</vocabulary>.

変事 is a formal word used to describe sudden and unusual occurrences that disrupt normal conditions, such as natural disasters, emergencies, or crises. You won''t see this word often, but may still come across it in formal writing.

With 変事, think of how the 変 comes first to emphasize the strangeness of the incident. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3932', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%89%E4%BA%8B', 15, 1, '2012-10-17T20:28:06.19159Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.619013Z'),
(3933, 'vocabulary', '建築家', '建築家', 'You know that 建築 is architecture. So, someone of <kanji>house</kanji> <kanji>architecture</kanji> is someone who does architecture. They are an <vocabulary>architect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3933', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%BA%E7%AF%89%E5%AE%B6', 23, 1, '2012-10-17T22:07:08.058927Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:37.587894Z'),
(3934, 'vocabulary', '紀元前', '紀元前', 'Remember how 紀元後 is "Common Era", or "anno Domini"? Well, this is the opposite because it has <kanji>before</kanji> (前) instead of 後. It''s <vocabulary>Before the Common Era</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>Before Christ</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3934', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%80%E5%85%83%E5%89%8D', 17, 1, '2012-10-17T22:10:42.448627Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:37.625556Z'),
(3935, 'vocabulary', '朝ご飯', '朝ご飯', 'The <kanji>morning</kanji> <kanji>meal</kanji> is the meal you eat in the morning. That is <vocabulary>breakfast</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3935', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%9D%E3%81%94%E9%A3%AF', 17, 1, '2012-10-17T22:11:01.327851Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:59:19.67524Z'),
(3936, 'vocabulary', 'イギリス人', 'イギリス人', 'イギリス means "Great Britain" or "United Kingdom." So what is a "Great Britain person?" You guessed it — a <vocabulary>British person</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>Brit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3936', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%A4%E3%82%AE%E3%83%AA%E3%82%B9%E4%BA%BA', 3, 1, '2012-10-17T22:19:55.537041Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(3937, 'vocabulary', '泣き虫', '泣き虫', 'You''ve seen that 泣く is to cry, so you can probably guess that 泣き is just "cry." You''ve also seen how 虫 is rarely a nice thing to call someone. So what''s a mean thing you can call someone who cries? How about a <vocabulary>crybaby</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3937', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A3%E3%81%8D%E8%99%AB', 17, 1, '2012-10-18T06:57:43.450587Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:57:54.583601Z'),
(3938, 'vocabulary', '泣き声', '泣き声', 'A <kanji>cry</kanji> <kanji>voice</kanji> is a sound of <vocabulary>crying</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>sob</vocabulary>. Don''t cry! Kanji will get easier someday!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3938', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A3%E3%81%8D%E5%A3%B0', 17, 1, '2012-10-18T06:58:10.056455Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:57:54.583601Z'),
(3939, 'vocabulary', '晩ご飯', '晩ご飯', 'The <kanji>night</kanji> <kanji>meal</kanji> is the meal you eat at night. That is <vocabulary>dinner</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3939', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%A9%E3%81%94%E9%A3%AF', 17, 1, '2012-10-18T06:59:27.189172Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:59:19.67524Z'),
(3940, 'vocabulary', '昼ご飯', '昼ご飯', 'The <kanji>noon</kanji> <kanji>meal</kanji> is the meal you eat at noon. What meal is that? It''s delicious, delicious <vocabulary>lunch</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3940', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%BC%E3%81%94%E9%A3%AF', 17, 1, '2012-10-18T07:55:42.670827Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:59:19.67524Z'),
(3941, 'vocabulary', '願い事', '願い事', 'You know that お願い means "a request," and 事 can mean "thing." Well, a request for some specific thing can also be called <vocabulary>one''s wish</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>prayer</vocabulary>. Do you have any 願い事 in your heart right now? Of course you do! Don''t worry, you can tell me.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3941', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A1%98%E3%81%84%E4%BA%8B', 17, 1, '2012-10-18T08:22:44.442811Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:47:34.523008Z'),
(3942, 'vocabulary', '同期中', '同期中', 'You''re making things in two different places be exactly the <kanji>same</kanji> for <kanji>a period of time</kanji>. You''re in the <kanji>middle</kanji> of a process called <vocabulary>syncing</vocabulary>. You can see 同期中 a lot in computery contexts, or if you change your device language to Japanese. (Not that I recommend that...)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3942', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8C%E6%9C%9F%E4%B8%AD', 15, 1, '2012-10-18T17:33:13.610471Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.639418Z'),
(3943, 'vocabulary', '寺', '寺', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3943', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BA', 15, 1, '2012-10-20T08:13:36.414961Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.649093Z'),
(3944, 'vocabulary', '期待する', '期待する', 'Hopefully you know the word 期待 by now (expectation). This is the verb version, which makes it <vocabulary>to expect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3944', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%9F%E5%BE%85%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-10-20T18:38:10.616446Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:37.951764Z'),
(3945, 'vocabulary', '回転ずし', '回転ずし', 'You know that 回転 is "rotation" and you probably know what すし is (gets rendaku''d in this word to ずし). So, what is "rotation sushi?" It''s <vocabulary>rotating sushi</vocabulary>, also known as <vocabulary>conveyor belt sushi</vocabulary>. Look it up if you don''t know what it is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3945', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9E%E8%BB%A2%E3%81%9A%E3%81%97', 14, 1, '2012-10-20T18:40:49.18029Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T19:51:12.830058Z'),
(3946, 'vocabulary', '回転する', '回転する', 'You know that 回転 means "rotation" or "revolution." So, what happens when you make it into a verb? It becomes <vocabulary>to rotate</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to revolve</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3946', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9E%E8%BB%A2%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-10-20T18:42:13.441014Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.00125Z'),
(3947, 'vocabulary', '証明', '証明', 'Is the <kanji>evidence</kanji> <kanji>bright</kanji> enough to convince me? Is this really <vocabulary>proof</vocabulary> enough to <vocabulary>prove</vocabulary> it?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3947', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%BC%E6%98%8E', 16, 1, '2012-10-25T22:35:03.792352Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(3948, 'vocabulary', '曜日', '曜日', 'The <kanji>weekday</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji> is the <vocabulary>day of the week</vocabulary>, as in "Monday" or "Tuesday." You''ll be learning those soon, but first you should learn 曜日 to get you prepared.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3948', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%9C%E6%97%A5', 16, 1, '2012-10-26T01:07:54.688858Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T16:54:31.234984Z'),
(3949, 'vocabulary', '気持ちいい', '気持ちいい', 'When you have a "feeling" (気持ち) that is "good" (いい), you are <vocabulary>feeling good</vocabulary>.

気持ちいい is what you say when something really <vocabulary>feels good</vocabulary>, both physically and emotionally. You can say it after stepping into a hot bath, feeling a cool breeze, or getting a massage that hits just right. It''s the Japanese version of leaning back and going "ahhh, this feels nice!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3949', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E6%8C%81%E3%81%A1%E3%81%84%E3%81%84', 11, 1, '2012-10-26T17:31:10.229342Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:51:18.141131Z'),
(3950, 'vocabulary', '運', '運', 'This takes the <kanji>luck</kanji> meaning from the kanji. Think of it this way: If you''re "carrying" you''re going to create your own luck. Now nobody will mess with you, kanji gangstah. You carry guns and have a ton of <vocabulary>luck</vocabulary> because of it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3950', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8B', 12, 1, '2012-10-26T17:35:56.621957Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.098261Z'),
(3951, 'vocabulary', '関西', '関西', 'Get <kanji>connected</kanji> to <kanji>west</kanji> Japan by going to <vocabulary>Kansai</vocabulary>! 

関西 is a region in western Japan that includes cities like Osaka, Kyoto, and Kobe. To get to West Japan, you have to pass through (or over) it, so you can think of it as the geographical beginning of western Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3951', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%A2%E8%A5%BF', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T07:25:40.531546Z', NULL, '2025-10-22T14:35:33.782679Z'),
(3952, 'vocabulary', '大阪', '大阪', 'A <kanji>big</kanji> place called <kanji>Osaka</kanji>? This is the city of <vocabulary>Osaka</vocabulary>! Ever heard of it? It''s a big city, and kind of the rival to Tokyo.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3952', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E9%98%AA', 16, 1, '2012-10-30T07:42:20.664403Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:34:07.282342Z'),
(3953, 'vocabulary', '右側', '右側', 'The <kanji>right</kanji> <kanji>side</kanji> is the <vocabulary>right side</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3953', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B3%E5%81%B4', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:12:09.690948Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.17035Z'),
(3954, 'vocabulary', '左側', '左側', 'The <kanji>left</kanji> <kanji>side</kanji> is the <vocabulary>left side</vocabulary>. Simple as that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3954', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A6%E5%81%B4', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:12:17.390422Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.198426Z'),
(3955, 'vocabulary', '内側', '内側', 'The <kanji>inside</kanji> <kanji>side</kanji> is the <vocabulary>inside</vocabulary>. A little redundant but makes a lot of sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3955', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%85%E5%81%B4', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:12:23.912532Z', NULL, '2025-09-05T05:40:05.235761Z'),
(3956, 'vocabulary', '外側', '外側', 'The <kanji>outside</kanji> <kanji>side</kanji> is the <vocabulary>outside</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3956', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E5%81%B4', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:12:30.940881Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(3957, 'vocabulary', '両側', '両側', '<kanji>Both</kanji> <kanji>side</kanji>s is <vocabulary>both sides</vocabulary>. Which side? Both sides.

両側 typically refers to both sides in a physical sense, like saying there are houses on both sides of the street or handrails on both sides of a staircase. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3957', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%A1%E5%81%B4', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:12:39.136133Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:45:39.570399Z'),
(3958, 'vocabulary', '東側', '東側', 'The <kanji>east</kanji> <kanji>side</kanji> is the <vocabulary>east side</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3958', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%B1%E5%81%B4', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:12:48.730956Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:37:00.630652Z'),
(3959, 'vocabulary', '西側', '西側', 'The <kanji>west</kanji> <kanji>side</kanji> of something is known as the <vocabulary>west</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>west side</vocabulary>. Sorry, I couldn''t think of a good west side story for this mnemonic.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3959', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%BF%E5%81%B4', 18, 1, '2012-10-30T22:12:57.926359Z', NULL, '2026-01-24T18:37:13.345638Z'),
(3960, 'vocabulary', '兵員', '兵員', 'A <kanji>soldier</kanji> <kanji>member</kanji> (or a member of the soldiers) is a person that is of the <vocabulary>military personnel</vocabulary>. It also shows the amount of <vocabulary>military strength</vocabulary> an army has. The more soldier members, the more military strength and military personnel it has!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3960', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B5%E5%93%A1', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:13:05.60243Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:54:26.999027Z'),
(3961, 'vocabulary', '兵器', '兵器', 'The <kanji>container</kanji>s that <kanji>soldier</kanji>s use aren''t just bowls and stuff, they''re the things that hold their <vocabulary>arms</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>weapons</vocabulary>. Think spear and sword containers, full of weapons!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3961', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B5%E5%99%A8', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:13:12.713599Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:54:26.999027Z'),
(3962, 'vocabulary', '兵士', '兵士', 'A <kanji>soldier</kanji> <kanji>samurai</kanji> is still just another <vocabulary>soldier</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3962', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B5%E5%A3%AB', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:13:19.97179Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:54:26.999027Z'),
(3963, 'vocabulary', '米兵', '米兵', 'Remember how 米国 is "America." Well, more of that again. With 米 representing "America" you can combine that with 兵 (<kanji>soldier</kanji>) to have yourself a <vocabulary>US soldier</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>American soldier</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3963', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B1%B3%E5%85%B5', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:13:36.23433Z', NULL, '2026-01-19T08:15:51.228829Z'),
(3964, 'vocabulary', '原因', '原因', 'The <kanji>original</kanji> <kanji>cause</kanji> is how you got into this mess. Forget all the things it caused, the first <vocabulary>cause</vocabulary> is the <vocabulary>origin</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>source</vocabulary> of what''s going on right now. You can pinpoint one thing that caused all of this!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3964', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%9F%E5%9B%A0', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:13:45.434961Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:58:23.689111Z'),
(3965, 'vocabulary', '原子', '原子', 'The <kanji>original</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> of everything is the <vocabulary>atom</vocabulary>. It is the child from which everything else is created, for it was the original child. Atoms are the building blocks of all things!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3965', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%9F%E5%AD%90', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:13:53.935019Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.470844Z'),
(3966, 'vocabulary', '原作', '原作', 'The <kanji>original</kanji> thing you <kanji>make</kanji> is the <vocabulary>original work</vocabulary>. It''s always better to have the original, rather than a copy, I think.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3966', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%9F%E4%BD%9C', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:14:00.732585Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:12:39.703958Z'),
(3967, 'vocabulary', '内因', '内因', 'The <kanji>inside</kanji> <kanji>cause</kanji> is an <vocabulary>internal cause</vocabulary>. Internal causes are usually the nastiest causes, if you ask me.

This word is mostly used in the fields of medicine and psychology. You’ll often see it as 内因性, which is a technical term used to talk about factors that originate within a person or an organism.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3967', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%85%E5%9B%A0', 51, 1, '2012-10-30T22:14:07.941648Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:23:47.286666Z'),
(3968, 'vocabulary', '主因', '主因', 'The <kanji>main</kanji> <kanji>cause</kanji> is the <vocabulary>main cause</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>primary cause</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3968', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB%E5%9B%A0', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:14:15.019672Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:58:23.689111Z'),
(3969, 'vocabulary', '悪因悪果', '悪因悪果', 'The <kanji>evil</kanji> <kanji>cause</kanji> yields the <kanji>evil</kanji> <kanji>fruit</kanji>, or more simply, <vocabulary>evil breeds evil</vocabulary>.

This is a Buddhist term, often used together with 善因善果 (virtue breeds virtue) to illustrate the concept of karma. While not a widely known phrase, it a nice saying to remind us of the importance of our actions. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3969', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E5%9B%A0%E6%82%AA%E6%9E%9C', 55, 1, '2012-10-30T22:14:23.662127Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:58:23.689111Z'),
(3970, 'vocabulary', '食堂', '食堂', 'An <kanji>eat</kanji>ing <kanji>hall</kanji> is a big room in which you eat. What big rooms do you eat in? How about a <vocabulary>cafeteria</vocabulary>, a <vocabulary>dining hall</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>diner</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3970', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9F%E5%A0%82', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:14:33.249525Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.589708Z'),
(3971, 'vocabulary', '塩', '塩', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3971', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%A9', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:14:44.119909Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.612593Z'),
(3972, 'vocabulary', '塩水', '塩水', '<kanji>Water</kanji> with <kanji>salt</kanji> in it is <vocabulary>salt water</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>salty water</vocabulary>.

Unlike "salt water" in English, which often suggests seawater, 塩水 refers to any water that contains some salt in it, including seawater, salt water for gargling, and medical saline.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3972', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%A9%E6%B0%B4', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:14:50.519018Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:37:35.965924Z'),
(3973, 'vocabulary', '塩味', '塩味', 'The <kanji>salt</kanji> <kanji>taste</kanji> is a <vocabulary>salty taste</vocabulary>.

塩味 specifically refers to the <vocabulary>saltiness</vocabulary> found in foods or drinks. It''s commonly used in cooking to describe a dish''s flavor profile or on food packaging to indicate that the item has a <vocabulary>salty flavor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3973', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%A9%E5%91%B3', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:15:02.891994Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:43:11.528784Z'),
(3974, 'vocabulary', '官金', '官金', '<kanji>Government</kanji> <kanji>gold</kanji> (aka money) is what makes up <vocabulary>government funds</vocabulary>.

官金 is a formal word for <vocabulary>government money</vocabulary>. You may come across it in news or discussion about public spending, especially in relation to how state money is handled or misused.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3974', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%98%E9%87%91', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:15:11.249827Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T02:00:25.173654Z'),
(3975, 'vocabulary', '外交官', '外交官', 'Someone from the <kanji>outside</kanji> who comes in to <kanji>mix</kanji> with the <kanji>government</kanji> is a <vocabulary>diplomat</vocabulary>. Really, that''s what diplomats do, right? They come from the outside (country). They mix with the government, and they do what they''re there to do!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3975', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E4%BA%A4%E5%AE%98', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:15:18.868421Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(3976, 'vocabulary', '察する', '察する', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>guess</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to guess</vocabulary>. Alternatively, you can learn this as <vocabulary>to sense</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to presume</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3976', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%9F%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:17:03.585843Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.734133Z'),
(3977, 'vocabulary', '察知', '察知', 'Okay, so you <kanji>guess</kanji> but you also kind of <kanji>know</kanji>. You can make an <vocabulary>inference</vocabulary> as to what the answer is... You''re not 100% sure, but you think you know, even though you''re guessing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3977', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%9F%E7%9F%A5', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:17:10.00354Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T17:41:12.590829Z'),
(3978, 'vocabulary', '席', '席', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3978', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%AD', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:17:24.446438Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.780802Z'),
(3979, 'vocabulary', '欠席', '欠席', 'When a <kanji>seat</kanji> is <kanji>lack</kanji>ing the person it''s supposed to have in it, there is an <vocabulary>absence</vocabulary>. Where is Johnny? Another absence? Ugh. Johnny. C''mon.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3979', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%A0%E5%B8%AD', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:17:30.503573Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.806244Z'),
(3980, 'vocabulary', '出席', '出席', 'The people who <kanji>exit</kanji> their <kanji>seat</kanji>s at the end of the show are the number of people who showed up, not counting empty seats. This is the true <vocabulary>attendance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3980', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E5%B8%AD', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:17:37.190388Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.833514Z'),
(3981, 'vocabulary', '空席', '空席', 'A <kanji>sky</kanji> <kanji>seat</kanji> is a seat full of sky (aka nothing!). This is an <vocabulary>empty seat</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>open seat</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3981', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA%E5%B8%AD', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:17:42.940452Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.856892Z'),
(3982, 'vocabulary', '常に', '常に', 'The particle に here is a clue that this word is an adverb. An adverb describes actions, so what''s a <kanji>normal</kanji> action? It''s one you''re <vocabulary>always</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>constantly</vocabulary>, doing. That''s what makes it normal!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3982', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%B8%E3%81%AB', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:17:52.137194Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.880355Z'),
(3983, 'vocabulary', '日常', '日常', '<kanji>Normal</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji>s are what you have in your regular <vocabulary>everyday life</vocabulary>. This is your <vocabulary>normal life</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3983', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E5%B8%B8', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:18:00.755594Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.906445Z'),
(3984, 'vocabulary', '常識', '常識', '<kanji>Normal</kanji> <kanji>discerning</kanji> is the discerning you do on a daily basis, without thinking. It is all the things that you do that are <vocabulary>common sense</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3984', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%B8%E8%AD%98', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:18:07.877228Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:21:29.482657Z'),
(3985, 'vocabulary', '干す', '干す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>dry</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to dry something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3985', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B2%E3%81%99', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:18:12.701509Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.957773Z'),
(3986, 'vocabulary', '干天', '干天', 'Remember how 天気 is weather (and how 天 often refers to sky things?). Well, when those sky things are dry, you have <vocabulary>dry weather</vocabulary>.

Note that 干天 is quite old-fashioned and you''ll find it mainly in formal writing and advanced set expressions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3986', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B2%E5%A4%A9', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:18:18.771764Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:38.981536Z'),
(3987, 'vocabulary', '干渉', '干渉', 'When you''re <kanji>ford</kanji>ing a stream, and you suddenly feel <kanji>dry</kanji> feelings around you, that doesn''t make any sense for something wet. This dryness is an <vocabulary>interference</vocabulary>. It''s an <vocabulary>intervention</vocabulary> trying to stop you from fording.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3987', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B2%E6%B8%89', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:18:25.332091Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.007387Z'),
(3988, 'vocabulary', '幻', '幻', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. You can add <vocabulary>phantom</vocabulary> to the meanings as well, because phantoms are kind of like <vocabulary>illusion</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3988', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BB', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:18:30.23372Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.035511Z'),
(3989, 'vocabulary', '幻覚', '幻覚', 'An <kanji>illusion</kanji> you have when you''re <kanji>awake</kanji> is a <vocabulary>hallucination</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3989', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BB%E8%A6%9A', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:18:35.822046Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.060818Z'),
(3990, 'vocabulary', '幻想', '幻想', 'An <kanji>illusion</kanji> that you <kanji>imagine</kanji> is your big <vocabulary>fantasy</vocabulary> (it''s also an <vocabulary>illusion</vocabulary>). This makes sense, though, right? An illusion is an illusion, sure. But, when you imagine it, it becomes your fantasy!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3990', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BB%E6%83%B3', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:18:40.749001Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.08364Z'),
(3991, 'vocabulary', '底', '底', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3991', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%95', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:18:47.185382Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.106775Z'),
(3992, 'vocabulary', '心底', '心底', 'The <kanji>heart</kanji> <kanji>bottom</kanji> is the <vocabulary>bottom of one''s heart</vocabulary>. Do you want to learn kanji? Do you want to learn it from 心底? Okay, fine, you can move onto the reading then.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3992', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83%E5%BA%95', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:18:52.620739Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.128621Z'),
(3993, 'vocabulary', '川底', '川底', 'The <kanji>river</kanji> <kanji>bottom</kanji> is the <vocabulary>river bottom</vocabulary>, also known as the <vocabulary>riverbed</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3993', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%9D%E5%BA%95', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:19:05.281433Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.150645Z'),
(3994, 'vocabulary', '建つ', '建つ', 'You know that 建てる is "to build." This is similar to that, but it''s the version where you''re not doing the building. Something is just getting built. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>to be built</vocabulary>. You can remember that by thinking about how <reading>two</reading> (つ) buildings were built without you noticing. These two buildings just seemed to pop up out of nowhere and, unfortunately, they''re now blocking your view. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3994', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%BA%E3%81%A4', 25, 1, '2012-10-30T22:19:14.624649Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.172674Z'),
(3995, 'vocabulary', '建物', '建物', 'This is a <kanji>build</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji>, or perhaps a <kanji>build</kanji>ing <kanji>thing</kanji>. In other words, a <vocabulary>building</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>structure</vocabulary>. 

建物 is a very general word that can refer to any kind of building, including residential buildings, commercial buildings, or public facilities.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3995', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%BA%E7%89%A9', 24, 1, '2012-10-30T22:19:20.894347Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(3996, 'vocabulary', '建前', '建前', 'We all <kanji>build</kanji> up something in <kanji>front</kanji> of ourselves. It''s the front we present to the world, the <vocabulary>public behavior</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>public stance</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>social niceties</vocabulary> that maintain harmony.

建前 is very important in Japan. For example, you might find yourself invited to an acquaintance''s home out of courtesy, with the expectation that you’ll decline (also out of courtesy!). A host of a party may say "just bring yourself," but guests know this could be 建前, and considering their own 建前, they bring a small gift anyways.  People can also be reluctant to say "no" to things, like drinks with coworkers when they really want to go home, and may not to show it when they find situations inconvenient or unpleasant. So you might need some guesswork to peek behind the 建前 that people build up in front of themselves!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3996', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%BA%E5%89%8D', 27, 1, '2012-10-30T22:19:30.582551Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.223265Z'),
(3997, 'vocabulary', '恋', '恋', 'When you have <kanji>romance</kanji> you also have <vocabulary>love</vocabulary>! The romantic kind of mushy, gushy love. Be careful though. This word doesn''t share its meaning with its kanji. But, that''s how romance is. It gets all crazy and turns to love really fast.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3997', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%8B', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:19:37.516054Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.244867Z'),
(3998, 'vocabulary', '失恋', '失恋', 'The <kanji>fault</kanji> of <kanji>romance</kanji> isn''t the romance itself, it''s when everything falls apart. It''s when you experience <vocabulary>heartbreak</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>unrequited love</vocabulary>. Have you ever felt the fault of romance? If so, remember back to that time, and think about how terrible of a fault it is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3998', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%B1%E6%81%8B', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:19:42.344577Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.266522Z'),
(3999, 'vocabulary', '愛', '愛', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/3999', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9B', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:19:50.246359Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:28:57.446613Z'),
(4000, 'vocabulary', '愛国心', '愛国心', 'When you have <kanji>love</kanji> for your <kanji>country</kanji> deep within your <kanji>heart</kanji>, that is <vocabulary>patriotism</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4000', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9B%E5%9B%BD%E5%BF%83', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:19:59.820652Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:28:57.446613Z')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;


This word means "absolutely" when it''s used as an adverb, and that''s the use you''ll hear most in conversation. However, as a noun it means "right and wrong", as in whether something is right or wrong, or good or bad.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4009', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%AF%E9%9D%9E', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:22:59.560941Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.516365Z'),
(4010, 'vocabulary', '果物', '果物', 'A <kanji>fruit</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji> is just <vocabulary>fruit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4010', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%9C%E7%89%A9', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:23:10.498113Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(4011, 'vocabulary', '結果', '結果', 'You <kanji>tie</kanji> up all the <kanji>fruit</kanji> because you''re going to use them to make giant fruit letters. These giant fruit letters will show the <vocabulary>result</vocabulary> of the big monkey election.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4011', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%90%E6%9E%9C', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:23:38.042154Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.562259Z'),
(4012, 'vocabulary', '栄える', '栄える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>flourish</kanji> or <kanji>prosper</kanji>, so the verb vocab versions of these would be <vocabulary>to flourish</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to prosper</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4012', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%84%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2012-10-30T22:23:44.735662Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:51:35.377081Z'),
(4013, 'vocabulary', '光栄', '光栄', 'When a <kanji>light</kanji> shines on how much you''ve <kanji>prosper</kanji>ed, it''s a great <vocabulary>honor</vocabulary>. You don''t feel like you did anything so special, but it''s really a privilege for the light to come down on you like this.

光栄 is generally used to express a deep sense of gratitude and pride when given an opportunity, receiving a compliment, or being acknowledged in a meaningful way. In those situations it''s common to say 光栄です to show you feel honored or privileged.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4013', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%89%E6%A0%84', 25, 1, '2012-10-30T22:23:50.725161Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.605572Z'),
(4014, 'vocabulary', '栄光', '栄光', 'You have <kanji>prosper</kanji>ed so much that <kanji>light</kanji> seems to shine forth from your every pore in a golden aura. With the wealth, fame, and admiration you have achieved, you are the walking embodiment of <vocabulary>glory</vocabulary>.

栄光 is commonly used in expressions like 過去の栄光 (past glory), referring to the great achievements or status that one had in the past.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4014', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%84%E5%85%89', 27, 1, '2012-10-30T22:23:56.54226Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.627845Z'),
(4015, 'vocabulary', '梅', '梅', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4015', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A2%85', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:24:02.431804Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:56:52.200313Z'),
(4016, 'vocabulary', '梅酒', '梅酒', '<kanji>Alcohol</kanji> that comes from <kanji>ume</kanji> is <vocabulary>ume wine</vocabulary>. It can also be called <vocabulary>plum wine</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>plum liquor</vocabulary>. If you''re of age give it a taste, it''s quite nice!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4016', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A2%85%E9%85%92', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:24:11.562759Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:56:52.200313Z'),
(4017, 'vocabulary', '交渉', '交渉', 'When you <kanji>ford</kanji> into the thick of things and <kanji>mix</kanji> everything up, you''re getting involved in a <vocabulary>negotiation</vocabulary>. That''s what negotiations are though, right? You gotta get right in there, fording through the muck. Then, you gotta mix things up and compromise between both parties.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4017', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%A4%E6%B8%89', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:24:28.886737Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.701026Z'),
(4018, 'vocabulary', '無力', '無力', 'When your <kanji>power</kanji> is <kanji>nothing</kanji> you are <vocabulary>powerless</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4018', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E5%8A%9B', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:24:35.134705Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(4019, 'vocabulary', '無休', '無休', 'If <kanji>rest</kanji> is <kanji>nothing</kanji> then there are <vocabulary>no holidays</vocabulary>. If there are no holidays, then that means a place is <vocabulary>always open</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>nonstop</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4019', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E4%BC%91', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:24:41.031605Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.747112Z'),
(4020, 'vocabulary', '無名', '無名', 'Someone with "<kanji>nothing</kanji>" for their <kanji>name</kanji> is either trying to remain <vocabulary>anonymous</vocabulary> or is simply <vocabulary>unknown</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4020', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E5%90%8D', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:24:49.193954Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(4021, 'vocabulary', '無事', '無事', 'A <kanji>nothing</kanji> <kanji>occurrence</kanji> is an occurrence where nothing happens to you, or at least nothing too bad — that means you''re <vocabulary>safe and sound</vocabulary>. Phew! 

In addition to meaning <vocabulary>safe</vocabulary>, 無事 can also describe a situation where everything goes off <vocabulary>without a hitch</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4021', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E4%BA%8B', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:24:56.577326Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:47:34.523008Z'),
(4022, 'vocabulary', '無知', '無知', 'When you <kanji>know</kanji> <kanji>nothing</kanji> (Jon Snow), you know nothing. This is <vocabulary>ignorance</vocabulary>, it''s being <vocabulary>ignorant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4022', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E7%9F%A5', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:25:03.086603Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T17:50:35.299574Z'),
(4023, 'vocabulary', '無茶', '無茶', 'Imagine having <kanji>nothing</kanji> but <kanji>tea</kanji> for an entire week. Sounds completely <vocabulary>irrational</vocabulary>, right? Such a <vocabulary>reckless</vocabulary> diet plan seems <vocabulary>excessive</vocabulary>.

無茶 is often used to point out that something goes against common sense; or to criticize or disapprove of some actions or situations that might be seen as reckless or excessive.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4023', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E8%8C%B6', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:25:09.859384Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:16:41.901026Z'),
(4024, 'vocabulary', '無料', '無料', 'When the <kanji>fee</kanji> is <kanji>nothing</kanji> it is <vocabulary>free</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>without charge</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4024', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E6%96%99', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:25:14.827479Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.871586Z'),
(4025, 'vocabulary', '細い', '細い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>thin</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>thin</vocabulary>, though it can be <vocabulary>slender</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4025', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%B0%E3%81%84', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:25:20.247816Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:47:55.647024Z'),
(4026, 'vocabulary', '結局', '結局', 'If you <kanji>tie</kanji> the <kanji>bureau</kanji> up with your delays this may not get done this year <vocabulary>after all</vocabulary>.

This word carries the nuance that despite the various events or factors leading up to it, the final outcome just is what it is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4026', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%90%E5%B1%80', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:25:33.51754Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.918958Z'),
(4027, 'vocabulary', '署名', '署名', 'When you write your <kanji>name</kanji> for a <kanji>government office</kanji>, they require you to write out your <vocabulary>signature</vocabulary>. Printing won''t do for official government office business.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4027', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BD%B2%E5%90%8D', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:25:44.155745Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(4028, 'vocabulary', '薬', '薬', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. If you''re looking for medicine in Japan, look for this sign.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4028', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%AC', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:25:50.964045Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.96484Z'),
(4029, 'vocabulary', '薬学', '薬学', 'People who <kanji>study</kanji> about <kanji>medicine</kanji> are studying the <vocabulary>study of pharmacy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4029', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%AC%E5%AD%A6', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:26:01.261152Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:39.986973Z'),
(4030, 'vocabulary', '薬方', '薬方', 'When <kanji>medicine</kanji> is sent to your <kanji>direction</kanji> it''s going to you. That''s your <vocabulary> prescription</vocabulary>.

Note that 薬方 isn''t commonly used in modern Japanese. You might encounter it in historical or fictional settings, where it can add a distinct flavor to the narrative.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4030', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%AC%E6%96%B9', 51, 1, '2012-10-30T22:26:09.470383Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.008228Z'),
(4031, 'vocabulary', '薬物', '薬物', 'These <kanji>medicine</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji>s are of the bad kind, they''re <vocabulary>drugs</vocabulary>!

As a standalone word, 薬物 generally refers to (often) illegal drugs that cause addiction or have a stimulating effect. Depending on the context, it can also refer to pharmaceutical drugs (aka <vocabulary>medicine</vocabulary>), but in that case, it''s usually used in compounds like 薬物アレルギー (drug allergy).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4031', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%AC%E7%89%A9', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:26:16.80905Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(4032, 'vocabulary', '薬用', '薬用', 'Something that''s <kanji>use</kanji>d for <kanji>medicine</kanji> is <vocabulary>medicinal</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4032', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%AC%E7%94%A8', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:26:23.233332Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.05167Z'),
(4033, 'vocabulary', '虚栄心', '虚栄心', 'When how much you <kanji>prosper</kanji> goes into your <kanji>heart</kanji> and just enters a <kanji>void</kanji>, it will rarely mean anything to you. A person who has this trait is going to have extreme <vocabulary>vanity</vocabulary> because he/she just needs to suck more and more prosperity into their bottomless pit of a heart. It''s like a black hole. They need more and more and more so then they have to become vain.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4033', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%99%9A%E6%A0%84%E5%BF%83', 39, 1, '2012-10-30T22:26:28.94275Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.072639Z'),
(4034, 'vocabulary', '虚弱', '虚弱', 'You have yourself a <kanji>void</kanji> of <kanji>weak</kanji>ness. This means you are <vocabulary>weak</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>feeble</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4034', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%99%9A%E5%BC%B1', 41, 1, '2012-10-30T22:26:38.785046Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T06:03:32.726396Z'),
(4035, 'vocabulary', '自覚', '自覚', 'What does <kanji>self</kanji> and <kanji>awake</kanji> mean in combination? This refers to <vocabulary>self-awareness</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>consciousness</vocabulary> of yourself, or a <vocabulary>realization</vocabulary>.

Wondering about the difference between 意識 and 自覚? 意識 refers to a general state of awareness or consciousness, while 自覚 specifically relates to self-awareness. It implies clearly recognizing one''s own actions, abilities, or circumstances, and is often used in contexts relating to personal growth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4035', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E8%A6%9A', 20, 1, '2012-10-30T22:28:06.723636Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.122191Z'),
(4036, 'vocabulary', '感覚', '感覚', 'You know how we use <kanji>feeling</kanji>s to <kanji>memorize</kanji> things here on WaniKani? I ask you to feel angry or sad or happy while imagining out the mnemonic stories. I also ask you to use your <vocabulary>sense</vocabulary>s, because feelings are memorized best when you use <vocabulary>the senses</vocabulary>. This can also refer to any particular <vocabulary>sensation</vocabulary> or even somebody''s <vocabulary>sense</vocabulary> of humor or fashion!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4036', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E8%A6%9A', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:28:12.744125Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(4037, 'vocabulary', '説明', '説明', 'When you make your <kanji>theory</kanji> <kanji>bright</kanji> (and clear and easy to understand) you must have come up with a great <vocabulary>explanation</vocabulary> for it. Before, it was dark and murky in everyone''s mind. But, when you gave your explanation, it became bright and easy to understand! Yay!

説明 is a general, everyday word that is used when explaining something unknown or unfamiliar to make it understandable. You may see it when explaining how to use a device or describing a situation, for example.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4037', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%AC%E6%98%8E', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:28:18.595837Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(4038, 'vocabulary', '小説', '小説', 'A <kanji>small</kanji> <kanji>theory</kanji> is some idea you have that you put on paper. Perhaps it''s an idea about life, that you write in the form of a <vocabulary>novel</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4038', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E8%AA%AC', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:28:25.124215Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.189876Z'),
(4039, 'vocabulary', '伝説', '伝説', 'When people <kanji>transmit</kanji> a <kanji>theory</kanji> through the ages, it can turn into a <vocabulary>legend</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>legendary story</vocabulary>. Kind of like how the "theory" of King Arthur''s sword turned into the legend of Excalibur.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4039', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9D%E8%AA%AC', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:28:43.82631Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.213196Z'),
(4040, 'vocabulary', '仮説', '仮説', 'A <kanji>temporary</kanji> <kanji>theory</kanji> isn''t one that''s set in stone yet. In fact, it''s not really a theory at all, it''s more of a <vocabulary>hypothesis</vocabulary>.

This word is generally used when referring to research or some sort of investigation where a hypothesis is put forward that needs to be tested and validated through evidence and experimentation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4040', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%AE%E8%AA%AC', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:28:50.113925Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.237948Z'),
(4041, 'vocabulary', '図説', '図説', 'A <kanji>diagram</kanji> <kanji>theory</kanji> is an <vocabulary>illustration</vocabulary> that is used as an <vocabulary>explanatory diagram</vocabulary>. Just imagine a chalk board with all sorts of drawings of things, showing how they work, at least in theory.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4041', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%B3%E8%AA%AC', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:28:56.340076Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.258914Z'),
(4042, 'vocabulary', '知識', '知識', 'You''re <kanji>discerning</kanji> about what you <kanji>know</kanji>. You choose what to keep and what not to keep in your brain. This is the sum of your <vocabulary>knowledge</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4042', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%A5%E8%AD%98', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:29:02.391366Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:21:29.482657Z'),
(4043, 'vocabulary', '意識', '意識', 'Your <kanji>idea</kanji>s while <kanji>discerning</kanji> things show your <vocabulary>consciousness</vocabulary> and an <vocabulary>awareness</vocabulary> of your surroundings. You not only know that you have ideas, but you think about them and discern them.

意識 is often used in relation to whether someone has consciousness or not, like in medical contexts or news stories. In other contexts it can refer to being aware of certain things, like a high awareness of environmental issues.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4043', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%8F%E8%AD%98', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:29:09.635185Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:21:29.482657Z'),
(4044, 'vocabulary', '警察', '警察', 'They <kanji>warn</kanji> you and they <kanji>guess</kanji> when you''ve done something wrong (with evidence, of course!). They are the <vocabulary>police</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4044', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%A6%E5%AF%9F', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:29:18.843794Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.33711Z'),
(4045, 'vocabulary', '非常', '非常', 'When an <kanji>injustice</kanji> to what''s <kanji>normal</kanji> is being committed, that makes it an <vocabulary>emergency</vocabulary>! 

Although it''s less common, this can also be used as a な adjective. In that case, it means <vocabulary>extreme</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>unusual</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4045', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%9E%E5%B8%B8', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:29:36.537158Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.359299Z'),
(4046, 'vocabulary', '鼻', '鼻', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4046', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BC%BB', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:29:43.157337Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:23:36.291149Z'),
(4047, 'vocabulary', '鼻くそ', '鼻くそ', 'If you''re like everyone else learning a new language, you learn the bad words first. This isn''t a bad word, but it sort of contains one. くそ means "sh*t" (as in "poop") in Japanese. What is your <kanji>nose</kanji> sh*t, then? It''s your <vocabulary>snot</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>boogers</vocabulary>. Eww, stop eating them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4047', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BC%BB%E3%81%8F%E3%81%9D', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:29:51.263817Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:23:36.291149Z'),
(4048, 'vocabulary', '鼻先', '鼻先', 'You know the <kanji>previous</kanji> part of your <kanji>nose</kanji>? The part of your nose I just chopped off? Well, you were lucky, as that was only the <vocabulary>tip of the nose</vocabulary>. Time to cut off the rest, next!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4048', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BC%BB%E5%85%88', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:29:57.411786Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:23:36.291149Z'),
(4049, 'vocabulary', '鼻歌', '鼻歌', 'Try singing a <kanji>nose</kanji> <kanji>song</kanji>. How does it sound? Like <vocabulary>humming</vocabulary> perhaps?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4049', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BC%BB%E6%AD%8C', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:30:02.881628Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:23:36.291149Z'),
(4050, 'vocabulary', '鼻血', '鼻血', 'A <kanji>nose</kanji> with <kanji>blood</kanji> is a nose with a <vocabulary>nosebleed</vocabulary>. It is a <vocabulary>bloody nose</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4050', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BC%BB%E8%A1%80', 17, 1, '2012-10-30T22:30:13.125055Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:23:36.291149Z'),
(4051, 'vocabulary', '上品', '上品', 'Remember how 下品 is "crude" and "vulgar" - this is obviously the opposite. This is <vocabulary>elegant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4051', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A%E5%93%81', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:08:06.839492Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(4052, 'vocabulary', '大阪弁', '大阪弁', 'Remember 大阪 (Osaka)? This is the <kanji>dialect</kanji> they speak there, making this word <vocabulary>Osaka dialect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4052', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E9%98%AA%E5%BC%81', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:08:16.696058Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:35:32.716471Z'),
(4053, 'vocabulary', '関西弁', '関西弁', 'Remember 関西, the Kansai region of Japan? Well, they have their own <kanji>dialect</kanji> — the <vocabulary>Kansai dialect</vocabulary>.

関西弁 can actually refer to any number of local dialects from the Kansai region. There are lots of them (Osaka, Kyoto, and Nara dialects to name a few), all with little differences in pronunciation and vocabulary that separate them from the standard Japanese you''d hear in Tokyo.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4053', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%A2%E8%A5%BF%E5%BC%81', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:08:24.322945Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:19:07.778293Z'),
(4054, 'vocabulary', '土曜日', '土曜日', 'This is a day of the week. You know this because it has 曜日 in it. But what day of the week is it? The <kanji>dirt</kanji> day is the day you finally get to go outside and get a little dirty. Maybe you''re in the yard gardening, at the park playing baseball, or even riding dirt bikes somewhere with buddies. Or maybe you''re just lying face down in the dirt ''cause you drank too much on Friday. Either way, <vocabulary>Saturday</vocabulary> is the day for getting down and dirty.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4054', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%9F%E6%9B%9C%E6%97%A5', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:08:31.022665Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:07:48.953804Z'),
(4055, 'vocabulary', '日曜日', '日曜日', 'This is a day of the week. You know this because it has 曜日 in it. But what day of the week is it? The <kanji>Sun</kanji> day of the week is <vocabulary>Sunday</vocabulary>. Doesn''t get much easier than that for you. Lucky!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4055', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E6%9B%9C%E6%97%A5', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:08:38.204875Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:02:58.553797Z'),
(4056, 'vocabulary', '月曜日', '月曜日', 'This is a day of the week. You know this because it has 曜日 in it. But what day of the week is it? The "<kanji>moon</kanji>" day of the week is <vocabulary>Monday</vocabulary>. Why? Because "Moon" is pretty much just "Mon," right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4056', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%88%E6%9B%9C%E6%97%A5', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:08:45.339162Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:23:52.568567Z'),
(4057, 'vocabulary', '木曜日', '木曜日', 'This is a day of the week. You know this because it has 曜日 in it. But what day of the week is it? The <kanji>tree</kanji> day of the week is <vocabulary>Thursday</vocabulary>. Tree day comes after water day (which is Wednesday, for Water-Wednesday!). Trees need water to grow, which is why the tree comes right after the water.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4057', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%A8%E6%9B%9C%E6%97%A5', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:08:53.700429Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T13:28:42.935127Z'),
(4058, 'vocabulary', '水曜日', '水曜日', 'This is a day of the week. You know this because it has 曜日 in it. But what day of the week is it? The <kanji>water</kanji> day of the week is where the water flows from. Think of Wednesday (hump day) as being like a big hill in the middle of your week. What originates in the hills and flows down from them? Water! That''s why "water day" is <vocabulary>Wednesday</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4058', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E6%9B%9C%E6%97%A5', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:08:59.18387Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:58:21.218551Z'),
(4059, 'vocabulary', '留学生', '留学生', 'Remember 留学, which means "study abroad"? Do you also remember 学生, which is "student"? This word is like those two words mashed together (they share a 学), making it a "study abroad student." What''s a better way to say that? How about <vocabulary>exchange student</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4059', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%99%E5%AD%A6%E7%94%9F', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:09:19.630986Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.782582Z'),
(4060, 'vocabulary', 'お笑い', 'お笑い', 'You know that 笑う is "to laugh." This is a noun version of that, though it''s not "a laugh," (that''s 笑い). Add お to that and you have the thing that makes you laugh. It''s <vocabulary>comedy</vocabulary>. It''s the <vocabulary>joke</vocabulary> that''s elevated with お because it made you laugh SOOOOO much. Haha!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4060', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E7%AC%91%E3%81%84', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:09:26.264359Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.805194Z'),
(4061, 'vocabulary', '手荷物', '手荷物', 'Remember 荷物 and how it means "baggage" or "luggage." Add a <kanji>hand</kanji> to it and you have <vocabulary>hand baggage</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>hand luggage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4061', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E8%8D%B7%E7%89%A9', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:09:31.452633Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(4062, 'vocabulary', '証明書', '証明書', 'Remember how 証明 means "proof"? Well, when you <kanji>write</kanji> the proof down on something, you have a <vocabulary>certificate</vocabulary>.

This can be used for different kinds of <vocabulary>written proof</vocabulary>, like a 卒業証明書 (graduation certificate), 身分証明書 (personal ID), and more.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4062', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%BC%E6%98%8E%E6%9B%B8', 17, 1, '2012-11-01T00:09:38.476066Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(4063, 'vocabulary', '笑い', '笑い', 'You know that 笑う is "to laugh." This is the noun version, because the う in 笑う turns to い (so don''t confuse this as an adjective). What is the noun version of "to laugh"? It''s <vocabulary>a laugh</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4063', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AC%91%E3%81%84', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:09:43.993076Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.874828Z'),
(4064, 'vocabulary', '借用', '借用', 'A <kanji>borrowing</kanji> <kanji>task</kanji> is just another way to say <vocabulary>borrowing</vocabulary>. This is also for when you take out a <vocabulary>loan</vocabulary>.

借用 is a formal word for the borrowing of things like money, facilities, or other goods. You can also use it in a jokingly formal way in everyday conversation though, like ちょっと借用しただけだよ (I just borrowed it for a moment).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4064', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%9F%E7%94%A8', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:09:49.538119Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:44:31.146854Z'),
(4065, 'vocabulary', '借りる', '借りる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>borrow</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to borrow</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4065', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%9F%E3%82%8A%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:09:54.291068Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:44:31.146854Z'),
(4066, 'vocabulary', '借金', '借金', 'The <kanji>money</kanji> (and <kanji>gold</kanji>) you <kanji>borrow</kanji> is your <vocabulary>debt</vocabulary>. Be sure to pay it back before the yakuza come for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4066', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%9F%E9%87%91', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:10:00.865845Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:44:31.146854Z'),
(4067, 'vocabulary', '借家', '借家', 'When you <kanji>borrow</kanji> a <kanji>house</kanji> it''s a <vocabulary>rented house</vocabulary>. This could also be a <vocabulary>house for rent</vocabulary>.

You can use 借家 in conversation to formally refer to a rented house or a house for rent. But it''s more commonly found in legal contexts, like in laws and regulations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4067', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%9F%E5%AE%B6', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:10:06.835186Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:47:01.699064Z'),
(4068, 'vocabulary', '僧院', '僧院', 'An <kanji>monk</kanji>’s <kanji>institution</kanji> is going to be a <vocabulary>monastery</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>temple</vocabulary>. What a nice place to live, I''m sure!

This word refers to Buddhist monasteries, and it''s often used for ones outside of Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4068', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%83%A7%E9%99%A2', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:10:12.27614Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:40.998509Z'),
(4069, 'vocabulary', '仏僧', '仏僧', 'A <kanji>Buddha</kanji> <kanji>priest</kanji> is a <vocabulary>Buddhist Priest</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4069', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8F%E5%83%A7', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:10:18.5469Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T20:01:27.526431Z'),
(4070, 'vocabulary', '切り取る', '切り取る', 'If you <kanji>cut</kanji> and <kanji>take</kanji> something, you are going to cut it right off. So, this verb is <vocabulary>to cut off</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4070', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%87%E3%82%8A%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8B', 7, 1, '2012-11-01T00:10:30.753939Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T19:47:55.653021Z'),
(4071, 'vocabulary', '取り出す', '取り出す', 'Remember how 取る means "to take," and 出す means "to take out"? Well, when you combine the two, the meaning is still <vocabulary>to take out</vocabulary>.

Compared with 出す, which has a wide range of meanings, 取り出す is limited to the action of taking something out from somewhere. It feels a bit more descriptive than 出す — like you''re reaching into a bag or a drawer, <em>taking</em> something in your hand, then pulling it out.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4071', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8A%E5%87%BA%E3%81%99', 7, 1, '2012-11-01T00:10:36.236142Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:32:08.173258Z'),
(4072, 'vocabulary', '取り分け', '取り分け', 'You 取り (take) and then 分け (divide) something. But you''ll want to be <vocabulary>especially</vocabulary> careful not to give anyone an unequal share. This is especially important because we’re dealing with money, and people will flip out and shoot you under the table if you aren''t especially accurate.

Note that this word is usually written in kana.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4072', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8A%E5%88%86%E3%81%91', 47, 1, '2012-11-01T00:10:41.988687Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.25846Z'),
(4073, 'vocabulary', '取れる', '取れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

This is an intransitive verb. That means it does not take a direct object. Something comes off. Something is removed. No direct object here. That''s why this verb means <vocabulary>to come off</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be removed</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4073', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2012-11-01T00:10:50.162009Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:50:41.083993Z'),
(4074, 'vocabulary', '取り決め', '取り決め', 'You''ve <kanji>take</kanji>n a <kanji>decision</kanji> from someone. By doing this, you''ve created a kind of <vocabulary>arrangement</vocabulary> with them. Imagine yourself pulling this decision out of the person. They don''t want to necessarily give it to you, but they''re letting you pull it out of them, kind of. When you''ve pulled it out, you''ve come to an arrangement, though they''ve pulled some of your decision out too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4074', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8A%E6%B1%BA%E3%82%81', 27, 1, '2012-11-01T00:10:55.189309Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:09:27.747622Z'),
(4075, 'vocabulary', '文句', '文句', 'A <kanji>paragraph</kanji> in <kanji>writing</kanji> is what you send someone when you want to submit a <vocabulary>complaint</vocabulary>. You want to make sure these are in writing, though, otherwise you don''t have proof of your complaint. Also, don''t make it longer than a paragraph, because that''s too long and nobody cares about how you think the shop staff mistreated your dog Fluffy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4075', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%87%E5%8F%A5', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:11:00.928461Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.165737Z'),
(4076, 'vocabulary', '禁句', '禁句', 'A <kanji>paragraph</kanji> that is under <kanji>prohibition</kanji> is under prohibition because it contains a <vocabulary>taboo word</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>taboo phrase</vocabulary>. Do not say this word, please!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4076', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A6%81%E5%8F%A5', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:11:21.444525Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.191425Z'),
(4077, 'vocabulary', '可愛い', '可愛い', 'It''s <kanji>possible</kanji> to <kanji>love</kanji> this thing. It''s so gosh darn <vocabulary>cute</vocabulary>! Any really cute thing is possible to love.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4077', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%AF%E6%84%9B%E3%81%84', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:11:30.028442Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:28:57.446613Z'),
(4078, 'vocabulary', '許可', '許可', 'I <kanji>permit</kanji> that it is <kanji>possible</kanji>. So, go ahead, do it. I give you <vocabulary>permission</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4078', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%B1%E5%8F%AF', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:11:43.75637Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.243693Z'),
(4079, 'vocabulary', '可分-4079', '可分', 'When it''s <kanji>possible</kanji> to <kanji>separate</kanji> something, it''s <vocabulary>divisible</vocabulary>. Just imagine separating things into parts. It''s possible. So, if that''s possible, it''s divisible. Now think of something that''s nothing (i.e. "no candy bars"). That is not possible to separate into parts. That is not divisible.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4079', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%AF%E5%88%86-4079', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:12:01.962824Z', '2021-10-07T17:34:42.864754Z', '2025-06-11T05:32:41.269349Z'),
(4080, 'vocabulary', '不可欠', '不可欠', 'It''s <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>possible</kanji> to <kanji>lack</kanji>. It is <vocabulary> indispensable</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>essential</vocabulary>, so don''t lack it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4080', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%8F%AF%E6%AC%A0', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:12:17.241303Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.294529Z'),
(4081, 'vocabulary', '告白', '告白', 'To <kanji>announce</kanji> something and make it <kanji>white</kanji> is to make a <vocabulary>confession</vocabulary>. Think of a completely blank sheet of paper - your confession clears all the announcements from the sheet of paper, making it white.

This word is often used when someone confesses romantic feelings for someone else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4081', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%8A%E7%99%BD', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:12:24.073434Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:34:31.16393Z'),
(4082, 'vocabulary', '告げる', '告げる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>announce</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to announce</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4082', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%8A%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:12:32.558192Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:34:31.16393Z'),
(4083, 'vocabulary', '広告', '広告', 'When you <kanji>announce</kanji> something to a <kanji>wide</kanji> audience, you''re just throwing your message out there for everyone to hear. This is just a big wide <vocabulary>advertisement</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4083', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%83%E5%91%8A', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:12:38.359311Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:34:31.16393Z'),
(4084, 'vocabulary', '予告', '予告', 'If you <kanji>announce</kanji> something <kanji>beforehand</kanji> you will have <vocabulary>advance notice</vocabulary>. Advance notice is always nice to give.

You''ll also see this used as an abbreviation of 予告編, meaning <vocabulary>trailer</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>preview</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4084', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%88%E5%91%8A', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:12:43.533525Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:34:31.16393Z'),
(4085, 'vocabulary', '公告', '公告', 'A <kanji>public</kanji> <kanji>announcement</kanji> is a <vocabulary>public notice</vocabulary>.

公告 looks and sounds a lot like 広告, but the meanings are different. You might remember 広告 is for advertisements, but 公告 here is for the kind of official statements that companies or governments put out for the public, especially shareholders, creditors, or other stakeholders.

You can remember 公告 is a public notice because the kanji for "public" is 公, and it''s not covered by a canopy like the other one. It''s uncovered, all out in the open for everyone to see — public!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4085', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AC%E5%91%8A', 50, 1, '2012-11-01T00:12:48.103203Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:34:31.16393Z'),
(4086, 'vocabulary', '報告', '報告', '<kanji>News</kanji> that you <kanji>announce</kanji> becomes a <vocabulary>report</vocabulary>. When someone announces news on a topic, they are giving you a report on the <vocabulary>information</vocabulary>.

報告 is a report given by someone after they''ve done some investigation or research into the topic. It can be used for class presentations on a topic or a report given to your boss. This differs from 報道, which is a report released by news organizations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4086', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%B1%E5%91%8A', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:12:53.571359Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:34:31.16393Z'),
(4087, 'vocabulary', '喫煙', '喫煙', 'If you <kanji>consume</kanji> <kanji>smoke</kanji> you are <vocabulary>smoking</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4087', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%AB%E7%85%99', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:12:59.378667Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:35:52.021679Z'),
(4088, 'vocabulary', '喫茶店', '喫茶店', 'If you <kanji>consume</kanji> <kanji>tea</kanji> in a <kanji>shop</kanji>, it could either be a <vocabulary>cafe</vocabulary>, a <vocabulary>tea house</vocabulary>, or slightly confusingly, a <vocabulary>coffee shop</vocabulary>. Welcome! Sit down. Relax. Have some tea (or coffee).

This word often refers to cafes with a retro feel to them, though they can be modern too. These places usually serve a variety of drinks and light meals.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4088', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%AB%E8%8C%B6%E5%BA%97', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:13:04.127262Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:35:52.021679Z'),
(4089, 'vocabulary', '情報', '情報', 'The <kanji>feeling</kanji> of <kanji>news</kanji> is that it should give you <vocabulary>information</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>data</vocabulary>, not fluff.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4089', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%85%E5%A0%B1', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:13:08.929236Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.499886Z'),
(4090, 'vocabulary', '予報', '予報', 'A <kanji>news report</kanji> that covers things <kanji>beforehand</kanji> (before they happen) is a <vocabulary>forecast</vocabulary>. For example, a weather forecast is a 天気予報.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4090', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%88%E5%A0%B1', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:13:14.292568Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.521504Z'),
(4091, 'vocabulary', '報じる', '報じる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>news report</kanji> so you could try to say "to news report" but that doesn''t make sense. What does make sense is "<vocabulary>to report on</vocabulary>," however.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4091', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%B1%E3%81%98%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:13:21.931561Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.543716Z'),
(4092, 'vocabulary', '報道', '報道', '<kanji>News</kanji> travels down the <kanji>road</kanji> by way of a <vocabulary>news report</vocabulary>.

報道 specifically refers to information put out by news organizations or the press in the form of newspaper articles, radio or television broadcasts, etc. Notice that it''s different from 報告, which is more general and means the reporting of information by anyone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4092', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%B1%E9%81%93', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:17:47.804723Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.565278Z'),
(4093, 'vocabulary', 'お守り', 'お守り', 'You know how 守る is "to protect," right? This is like that, but it''s the noun version. It''s not really "a protection," though it kind of is. What things can you wear to protect you from things like bad luck, demons, and ancient aliens? That would be an <vocabulary>amulet</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>charm</vocabulary>. You''ll see these kinds of things sold at temples and such.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4093', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E5%AE%88%E3%82%8A', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:18:06.499257Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.587625Z'),
(4094, 'vocabulary', '留守番', '留守番', 'You know how 留守 means "absence" or "away from home"? Well, now that you''re absent from your home, it''s someone else''s  turn to come to your home and take care of it. This person is the <vocabulary>caretaker</vocabulary>, someone who''s <vocabulary>looking after one''s house</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4094', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%99%E5%AE%88%E7%95%AA', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:18:12.019141Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.611665Z'),
(4095, 'vocabulary', '正座', '正座', 'The <kanji>correct</kanji> way to <kanji>sit</kanji> is this way: <vocabulary>traditional Japanese sitting</vocabulary> (this is when you sit on your legs and your feet, kneeling). You can also call it <vocabulary>seiza</vocabulary>. Learning the reading will help with the meaning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4095', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A3%E5%BA%A7', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:18:19.002044Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:52:04.645576Z'),
(4096, 'vocabulary', '座禅', '座禅', 'When you <kanji>sit</kanji> and do <kanji>zen</kanji> you are probably doing <vocabulary>zen meditation</vocabulary>. Hummmmm.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4096', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%A7%E7%A6%85', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:18:26.67733Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:52:04.645576Z'),
(4097, 'vocabulary', '座席', '座席', 'A <kanji>sit</kanji> <kanji>seat</kanji> is probably going to be a <vocabulary>seat</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4097', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%A7%E5%B8%AD', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:18:33.235107Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:52:04.645576Z'),
(4098, 'vocabulary', '銀座', '銀座', 'The <kanji>silver</kanji> <kanji>seat</kanji> isn''t some crazy kingdom where the king sits upon a silver seat. It is a famous place in Tokyo. Perhaps if you read the reading it will become more apparent what the meaning is (since they''re the same). This silver seat place is <vocabulary>Ginza</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4098', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%80%E5%BA%A7', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:18:45.220535Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T09:52:41.789411Z'),
(4099, 'vocabulary', '駅弁', '駅弁', 'Remember how 弁当 is "bento"? Well, the 弁 in this word comes from that, so pretend it means "bento." Then, when you have a <kanji>train station</kanji> to go along with the bento, you have a <vocabulary>train station bento</vocabulary>. As you might think, a train station bento is just a bento that''s sold at a train station. Easy!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4099', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%85%E5%BC%81', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:18:49.682401Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.721687Z'),
(4100, 'vocabulary', '弓', '弓', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4100', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%93', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:18:56.645417Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.743865Z'),
(4101, 'vocabulary', '弓道', '弓道', 'The <kanji>path</kanji> of the <kanji>bow</kanji>. This is <vocabulary>archery</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4101', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%93%E9%81%93', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:19:03.260522Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.764618Z'),
(4102, 'vocabulary', '忘れる', '忘れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>forget</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to forget</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4102', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%98%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:19:08.64926Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.786005Z'),
(4103, 'vocabulary', '忘年会', '忘年会', 'Time to get <kanji>together</kanji> and <kanji>forget</kanji> the <kanji>year</kanji>... what a terrible year, good thing a new one is coming. This get together is a <vocabulary>year end party</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4103', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%98%E5%B9%B4%E4%BC%9A', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:19:15.4081Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.807798Z'),
(4104, 'vocabulary', '下書き', '下書き', 'Something <kanji>below</kanji> your <kanji>writing</kanji> isn''t what you want to necessarily publish to the world. It''s below your writing, after all. This is merely a <vocabulary>rough draft</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4104', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E6%9B%B8%E3%81%8D', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:19:30.937252Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(4105, 'vocabulary', '書き入れる', '書き入れる', 'To <kanji>insert</kanji> what you <kanji>write</kanji> into something is <vocabulary>to write in</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to fill in</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4105', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B8%E3%81%8D%E5%85%A5%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:19:36.682268Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.854262Z'),
(4106, 'vocabulary', '書き方', '書き方', 'This is the <kanji>way</kanji> of <kanji>writing</kanji> (kind of like how 見方 is the way of seeing). So this means <vocabulary>way to write</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>how to write</vocabulary>. How''s your 書き方 for kanji? Mine is terrible.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4106', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B8%E3%81%8D%E6%96%B9', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:19:41.940833Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:41.881034Z'),
(4107, 'vocabulary', '書き直す', '書き直す', '書く means "to write" and 直す means "to fix," right? Well, if you write something and then decide to fix it, you''re going to have <vocabulary>to rewrite</vocabulary> it. Yup, the only way to fix that horrible chicken scratch paragraph you''ve written is to totally rewrite it from the ground up, so you''d better start rewriting ASAP.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4107', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B8%E3%81%8D%E7%9B%B4%E3%81%99', 17, 1, '2012-11-01T00:19:47.460706Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:06:56.328049Z'),
(4108, 'vocabulary', '何枚', '何枚', 'You have <kanji>flat object</kanji>s but <kanji>what</kanji> amount of flat objects? This word is asking that question: "<vocabulary>how many flat objects</vocabulary> are there?" If this doesn''t make sense, think back to words like 何回 or 何年 which ask "how many ____" just like this one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4108', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%95%E6%9E%9A', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:19:52.415623Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(4109, 'vocabulary', '枚数', '枚数', 'You have <kanji>flat object</kanji>s. You have a <kanji>number</kanji> of them. These flat objects are flat things, right? Flat things like sheets of paper, perhaps (or other sheets of things). So, this word is <vocabulary>number of sheets</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4109', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%9A%E6%95%B0', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:19:57.565171Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:02:02.92783Z'),
(4110, 'vocabulary', '五枚', '五枚', 'You know that 枚 is a <kanji>flat objects counter</kanji>. So, now we''re counting! You have <vocabulary>five flat objects</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4110', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%94%E6%9E%9A', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:20:02.833064Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:02:02.92783Z'),
(4111, 'vocabulary', '洗う', '洗う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>wash</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to wash</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4111', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%97%E3%81%86', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:20:31.973566Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(4112, 'vocabulary', '洗車', '洗車', 'A <kanji>wash</kanji> that washes <kanji>car</kanji>s is a <vocabulary>car wash</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4112', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%97%E8%BB%8A', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:20:37.25903Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(4113, 'vocabulary', '洗練', '洗練', 'You take your <kanji>practice</kanji> and you <kanji>wash</kanji> it. Slowly chunks fall off and it becomes more and more simple. You are washing away the gunk on the outside to attain <vocabulary>refinement</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>polish</vocabulary> yourself, via practice.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4113', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%97%E7%B7%B4', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:20:42.284395Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(4114, 'vocabulary', '皿洗い', '皿洗い', 'You know the word 皿 (plate) and the word 洗う (to wash), right? You can probably guess dish washing is happening here, but this is a noun, so it''s the idea of <vocabulary>dishwashing</vocabulary>, AKA <vocabulary>doing dishes</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>washing dishes</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4114', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%BF%E6%B4%97%E3%81%84', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T00:20:52.634991Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(4115, 'vocabulary', '焼く', '焼く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>bake</kanji> or <kanji>burn</kanji>. So, the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to bake</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to burn something</vocabulary>, or even <vocabulary>to grill</vocabulary>.

焼く means to apply heat to something to change it in a physical way. It can refer to lightly toasting something, charring it, or even burning it to ashes depending on the context. It''s commonly used in cooking contexts to describe baking, grilling, or roasting food. But it can also be used with pottery, film photography, and even sun-tanning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4115', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%BC%E3%81%8F', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:20:57.688301Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.097928Z'),
(4116, 'vocabulary', '日焼け', '日焼け', 'When the <kanji>sun</kanji> <kanji>burn</kanji>s something, and that something is you, it''s giving you <vocabulary>sunburn</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4116', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E7%84%BC%E3%81%91', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:21:23.67481Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.121016Z'),
(4117, 'vocabulary', '焼き鳥', '焼き鳥', 'A nicely <kanji>bake</kanji>d <kanji>bird</kanji> isn''t just any bird, it''s <vocabulary>grilled chicken</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>roast chicken</vocabulary>. You may also know it by its Japanese name: <vocabulary>yakitori</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4117', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%BC%E3%81%8D%E9%B3%A5', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:21:29.575902Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:12:26.957805Z'),
(4118, 'vocabulary', '焼き肉', '焼き肉', 'Nicely <kanji>bake</kanji>d <kanji>meat</kanji> is <vocabulary>grilled meat</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>yakiniku</vocabulary>. Mmm, yum! If you go to Japan look out for 焼肉 places (same meaning and reading). They''re pretty tasty, if you''re into meat at least.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4118', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%BC%E3%81%8D%E8%82%89', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:21:34.70486Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.168372Z'),
(4119, 'vocabulary', '夕焼け', '夕焼け', 'When the <kanji>evening</kanji> is <kanji>burning</kanji> with sun, it is the sun going down, getting all close to the horizon and earth. This is the <vocabulary>sunset</vocabulary>, or more specifically, the <vocabulary>evening glow</vocabulary> you can see during a sunset.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4119', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%95%E7%84%BC%E3%81%91', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:21:42.611212Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.193072Z'),
(4120, 'vocabulary', '煙', '煙', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4120', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%99', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:21:55.289622Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:12:49.709742Z'),
(4121, 'vocabulary', '黒煙', '黒煙', '<kanji>Black</kanji> <kanji>smoke</kanji> is <vocabulary>black smoke</vocabulary>. It''s also something you should avoid if you can.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4121', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BB%92%E7%85%99', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:22:00.204851Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:12:49.709742Z'),
(4122, 'vocabulary', '祈る', '祈る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>pray</kanji> and this verb is transitive, so it means <vocabulary>to pray for</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4122', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%88%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-11-01T00:22:06.297425Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.265083Z'),
(4123, 'vocabulary', '祈念', '祈念', 'You <kanji>pray</kanji> with your <kanji>thought</kanji>s, making this a <vocabulary>prayer</vocabulary>.

While 祈願 is about formally asking a specific deity for something, 祈念 refers to <vocabulary>praying</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>wishing</vocabulary> in a more silent and personal sense. It''s used in formal situations to express hopes for success, safety, peace, or recovery, and often appears in names of monuments or events that combine remembrance and hope, such as 平和祈念公園 (Peace Memorial Park) or 復興祈念イベント (Reconstruction Prayer Event).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4123', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%88%E5%BF%B5', 39, 1, '2012-11-01T00:22:10.594446Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T17:45:21.183044Z'),
(4124, 'vocabulary', '祈願', '祈願', 'You <kanji>pray</kanji> and make a <kanji>request</kanji>, as a <vocabulary>prayer</vocabulary> to some deity at a shrine or temple. 

This usually refers to making a wish or asking for a favor from a deity, like prosperity, good health, or winning an important sports game.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4124', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%88%E9%A1%98', 21, 1, '2012-11-01T00:22:16.144922Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.332346Z'),
(4125, 'vocabulary', '禁煙', '禁煙', 'If <kanji>smoke</kanji> is <kanji>prohibite</kanji>d you probably shouldn''t smoke. Aka <vocabulary>no smoking</vocabulary>, okay?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4125', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A6%81%E7%85%99', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:22:21.592068Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:12:49.709742Z'),
(4126, 'vocabulary', '禁止', '禁止', '<kanji>Stop</kanji>! That''s <kanji>prohibition</kanji> (prohibited)! When something is prohibited and you''re forced to stop, that thing is obviously <vocabulary>forbidden</vocabulary>. If you see this you should probably not do the thing it''s telling you not to do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4126', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A6%81%E6%AD%A2', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:22:26.51242Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.379892Z'),
(4127, 'vocabulary', '禅寺', '禅寺', 'A <kanji>zen</kanji> <kanji>temple</kanji> is just what it sounds like, a <vocabulary>zen temple</vocabulary>. Sounds nice.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4127', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A6%85%E5%AF%BA', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:22:31.650158Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.411954Z'),
(4128, 'vocabulary', '禅僧', '禅僧', 'A <kanji>Zen</kanji> <kanji>priest</kanji> is a <vocabulary>zen priest</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4128', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A6%85%E5%83%A7', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:22:36.124233Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.434318Z'),
(4129, 'vocabulary', '私大', '私大', 'You know how 私 can mean <kanji>private</kanji> and how 大 is the first half of 大学? Well, combine those together and you have the shortened version of <vocabulary>private college</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>private university</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4129', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%81%E5%A4%A7', 10, 1, '2012-11-01T00:22:43.560278Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(4130, 'vocabulary', '人種', '人種', 'A <kanji>kind</kanji> or <kanji>type</kanji> of <kanji>person</kanji> is when you categorize a person into different types. How do we do that? Well, you categorize by <vocabulary>race of people</vocabulary> (or more simply by <vocabulary>race</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4130', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E7%A8%AE', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:22:48.672934Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(4131, 'vocabulary', '等しい', '等しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>equal</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>equal</vocabulary> (and also <vocabulary>similar</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>equivalent</vocabulary>). That "similar" one is a bit weird, because "similar" is not equal, though I''d lean more towards equal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4131', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AD%89%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:22:59.872552Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.497491Z'),
(4132, 'vocabulary', '等号', '等号', 'How do you represent an <kanji>equal</kanji> <kanji>number</kanji>? With an <vocabulary>equal sign</vocabulary>.

It''s worth pointing out that this is a pretty formal word that''s used in advanced math. In everyday conversation, people would use イコール for the equal sign.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4132', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AD%89%E5%8F%B7', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:23:05.562872Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.51882Z'),
(4133, 'vocabulary', '一等', '一等', 'When you''re <kanji>equal</kanji> to <kanji>one</kanji> you are the very top. You are <vocabulary>first class</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>first rank</vocabulary>. In fact, you are <vocabulary>the best</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4133', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E7%AD%89', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:23:10.195899Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.541093Z'),
(4134, 'vocabulary', '不等', '不等', 'When something is <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>equal</kanji> is of course <vocabulary>unequal</vocabulary>. It''s an <vocabulary>inequality</vocabulary>. You''ll usually see this word in technical contexts like math and engineering, but it does pop up in more general formal writing, too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4134', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E7%AD%89', 55, 1, '2012-11-01T00:23:14.956518Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.638458Z'),
(4135, 'vocabulary', '訓練', '訓練', 'You''ve got your <kanji>instruction</kanji> and you have your <kanji>practice</kanji>. Time to start your <vocabulary>training</vocabulary>, or simply your <vocabulary>practice</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4135', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%93%E7%B7%B4', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:26:07.470744Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:20:13.293925Z'),
(4136, 'vocabulary', '胸', '胸', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4136', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%B8', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:26:13.579245Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:02:33.540143Z'),
(4137, 'vocabulary', '脳', '脳', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4137', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%B3', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:26:20.2417Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.633204Z'),
(4138, 'vocabulary', '脳死', '脳死', 'If your <kanji>brain</kanji> is <kanji>dead</kanji> you have <vocabulary>brain death</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4138', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%B3%E6%AD%BB', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:26:25.571833Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.654599Z'),
(4139, 'vocabulary', '脳みそ', '脳みそ', 'Do you know みそ Soup and how miso goes in it? This is that miso. It''s soft and strange feeling. Now, your <kanji>brain</kanji> <kanji>miso</kanji>, that''s your <vocabulary>brains</vocabulary> or your <vocabulary>gray matter</vocabulary>. Essentially, if someone cuts your head open with a katana, this is perhaps what would come out.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4139', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%B3%E3%81%BF%E3%81%9D', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:26:31.358944Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.677611Z'),
(4140, 'vocabulary', '洗脳', '洗脳', 'Give your <kanji>brain</kanji> a nice <kanji>wash</kanji>. No! Wait! It''s not as nice as it sounds. This is <vocabulary>brainwashing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4140', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%97%E8%84%B3', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:26:46.42908Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(4141, 'vocabulary', '二枚舌', '二枚舌', 'You have <kanji>two</kanji> <kanji>flat objects</kanji> that make up your <kanji>tongue</kanji>. What kind of tongue is made up of two of anything? That''s a tongue that''s split, that''s a <vocabulary>forked tongue</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4141', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E6%9E%9A%E8%88%8C', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T00:27:05.56529Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:02:02.92783Z'),
(4142, 'vocabulary', '落書き', '落書き', 'When you <kanji>fall</kanji> into the urge to <kanji>write</kanji>, you just grab whatever''s nearby and start <vocabulary>doodling</vocabulary> on it.

落書き means doodling or <vocabulary>scribbling</vocabulary> without much thought, and it also means the scribbles themselves. Imagine a kid doodling in the margins of their notebook in class. <vocabulary>Graffiti</vocabulary> can be 落書き too, but usually this refers to vandalistic graffiti rather than the artistic stuff — think of an "anarchy" symbol under a bridge or a stick figure drawing in a bathroom stall.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4142', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%90%BD%E6%9B%B8%E3%81%8D', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:27:10.486297Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:46:04.638998Z'),
(4143, 'vocabulary', '葉書', '葉書', 'A <kanji>writing</kanji> that you put on a <kanji>leaf</kanji> is like something else. Instead of a leaf, think about writing you put on a leafish sized item. A <vocabulary>postcard</vocabulary> perhaps?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4143', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%91%89%E6%9B%B8', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:27:15.785978Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:10:47.677773Z'),
(4144, 'vocabulary', '訓読み', '訓読み', 'The <kanji>instruction</kanji> <kanji>reading</kanji> may not make a lot of sense, but you''ve heard this word before. Jump to the reading and you''ll know what the meaning of this word is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4144', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%93%E8%AA%AD%E3%81%BF', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:27:27.284541Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:20:13.293925Z'),
(4145, 'vocabulary', '教訓', '教訓', 'At least way back in the day, things that <kanji>teach</kanji> <kanji>instructions</kanji> to people are things that teach a <vocabulary>lesson</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>moral</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>teachings</vocabulary>. Back in the day, lots of learning was done through storytelling, and storytelling taught lessons and morals, thus, this word.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4145', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%99%E8%A8%93', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:28:08.364092Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:20:13.293925Z'),
(4146, 'vocabulary', '許す', '許す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>permit</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to permit</vocabulary>. It also means <vocabulary>to forgive</vocabulary>, in that "I''ll permit it this time... but only this time." Look out for 許さない in TV shows, too. It means "I won''t permit this!" or "I won''t forgive you!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4146', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%B1%E3%81%99', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:28:19.489243Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.836968Z'),
(4147, 'vocabulary', '試みる', '試みる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>try</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to try to do something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to attempt something</vocabulary>.

This verb is transitive, meaning it takes a direct object. Here, the direct object is whatever it is that you''re trying to do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4147', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%A6%E3%81%BF%E3%82%8B', 9, 1, '2012-11-01T00:28:27.097869Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.48252Z'),
(4148, 'vocabulary', '試す', '試す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>attempt</kanji> or <kanji>try</kanji>. The verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to try something out</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to give something a try</vocabulary>. Why don''t you try out the meaning of this verb, while you''re at it?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4148', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%A6%E3%81%99', 11, 1, '2012-11-01T00:28:41.91948Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.881795Z'),
(4149, 'vocabulary', '試合', '試合', 'An <kanji>attempt</kanji> to <kanji>join</kanji> together isn''t just any get together. Imagine two people boxing. They attempt to knock each other out when they join together. That''s why this word is <vocabulary>game</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>match</vocabulary>. Did you watch the 試合 today? No! Don''t tell me what happened, I recorded it on the VHS.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4149', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%A6%E5%90%88', 14, 1, '2012-11-01T00:28:46.921695Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:42.906687Z'),
(4150, 'vocabulary', '試食', '試食', 'Go ahead and <kanji>try</kanji> an <kanji>eat</kanji>. How was that little try? That little <vocabulary>food sample</vocabulary>? That little <vocabulary>taste</vocabulary>?

You know when you walk into the supermarket and have people offering you the tiniest cube of cheese? That''s what this is. So this word is commonly used in the context of product demonstrations or food events.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4150', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%A6%E9%A3%9F', 9, 1, '2012-11-01T00:28:52.052762Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.496671Z'),
(4151, 'vocabulary', '友達', '友達', 'A <kanji>friend</kanji> that you <kanji>attain</kanji> is your <vocabulary>friend</vocabulary>. This is the most common way to say "friend," so learn it if you want to make any!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4151', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8B%E9%81%94', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:29:01.716083Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:06:12.518486Z'),
(4152, 'vocabulary', '配達する', '配達する', 'You <kanji>distribute</kanji> the goods, they <kanji>attain</kanji> the goods. You are going <vocabulary>to deliver</vocabulary> to everyone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4152', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%8D%E9%81%94%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:29:24.968751Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:06:12.518486Z'),
(4153, 'vocabulary', '達人', '達人', 'A <kanji>person</kanji> who has "<kanji>attain</kanji>ed" is someone who knows much. They are a <vocabulary>master</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>expert</vocabulary>, obviously.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4153', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%94%E4%BA%BA', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:29:33.12765Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:06:12.518486Z'),
(4154, 'vocabulary', '伝達', '伝達', 'When you <kanji>transmit</kanji> something and you <kanji>attain</kanji> it on the other end, you''ve done a <vocabulary>transmission</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4154', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9D%E9%81%94', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:29:49.556083Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:06:12.518486Z'),
(4155, 'vocabulary', '静か', '静か', 'This is the な adjective form of <kanji>quiet</kanji>, which is kind of just like the noun, anyways. What is the adjective version of quiet? It''s also <vocabulary>quiet</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4155', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%99%E3%81%8B', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:29:55.227214Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:08:32.29071Z'),
(4156, 'vocabulary', '静止', '静止', 'Everything is <kanji>quiet</kanji> and <kanji>stop</kanji>ped. The world is <vocabulary>still</vocabulary> and everything is <vocabulary>at rest</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4156', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%99%E6%AD%A2', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:30:18.95859Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:08:32.29071Z'),
(4157, 'vocabulary', '平静', '平静', 'A place that is <kanji>peace</kanji> and <kanji>quiet</kanji> is a very <vocabulary>calm</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>serene</vocabulary> place. Ahh, so nice!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4157', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B3%E9%9D%99', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:30:25.901753Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:08:32.29071Z'),
(4158, 'vocabulary', '人類', '人類', 'There''s only one <kanji>person</kanji> <kanji>type</kanji> and that is <vocabulary>mankind</vocabulary>, otherwise known as <vocabulary>humanity</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>humankind</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4158', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E9%A1%9E', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:30:51.984268Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(4159, 'vocabulary', '分類', '分類', 'You <kanji>divide</kanji> something into different <kanji>type</kanji>s. That''s called <vocabulary>classification</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>categorization</vocabulary>.

分類 is a relatively formal word that refers to the act of classifying or categorizing items, organisms, or information into specific groups based on shared characteristics or criteria.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4159', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%86%E9%A1%9E', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:30:57.450416Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:32:59.293769Z'),
(4160, 'vocabulary', '種類', '種類', 'The <kanji>kind</kanji> and <kanji>type</kanji> is the <vocabulary>kind</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>type</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>sort</vocabulary>. Basically, if you know the meaning of either of these kanji you''ll know the meaning of this word as well, since they all share nicely.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4160', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%AE%E9%A1%9E', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:31:03.9045Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:32:59.293769Z'),
(4161, 'vocabulary', '親類', '親類', 'These people are kind of like a <kanji>type</kanji> of <kanji>parents</kanji>. Not quite parents, but kind of, in a pinch anyways. These are your <vocabulary>relatives</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4161', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA%E9%A1%9E', 18, 1, '2012-11-01T00:31:12.438251Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:32:59.293769Z'),
(4162, 'vocabulary', '体験', '体験', 'Your <kanji>body</kanji> goes through <kanji>test</kanji>s and each time it does, it gains a little more <vocabulary>experience</vocabulary>. What has your body experienced? What tests has your body been through?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4162', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%93%E9%A8%93', 9, 1, '2012-11-01T00:31:20.540492Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.515658Z'),
(4163, 'vocabulary', '受験', '受験', 'When you <kanji>accept</kanji> a <kanji>test</kanji> you are <vocabulary>taking an exam</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>taking a test</vocabulary>. Good luck! A++++!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4163', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%97%E9%A8%93', 9, 1, '2012-11-01T00:31:28.057268Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:48.601574Z'),
(4164, 'vocabulary', '実験', '実験', 'A <kanji>test</kanji> that tries to find <kanji>truth</kanji> is an <vocabulary>experiment</vocabulary>. This is because you don''t know the truth, so the only way to find it is to try things out and test them. Thus, experiment!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4164', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9F%E9%A8%93', 9, 1, '2012-11-01T00:31:33.638277Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.538034Z'),
(4165, 'vocabulary', '試験', '試験', 'If you <kanji>attempt</kanji> a <kanji>test</kanji>, you''re taking an <vocabulary>examination</vocabulary>. There are a lot of these in Japan, so make sure you know this word if you want to fit in with all the whining and whinging that students do, especially come the final year of high school.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4165', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%A6%E9%A8%93', 9, 1, '2012-11-01T00:31:41.601792Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.547735Z'),
(4166, 'vocabulary', '乱れる', '乱れる', 'The kanji means <kanji>riot</kanji>, but this verb version word doesn''t really mean "to riot." It''s more like <vocabulary>to be in disorder</vocabulary>. But if you think about it, when things are rioting and crazy, they definitely are in disorder, so you can think of it that way. You can differentiate this from the future word 乱す because with れる you know it is <reading>rare</reading> for it to be like this. You aren''t doing it, but it just happens on occasion (rarely) so you take advantage of it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4166', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%B1%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:38:07.776986Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T23:21:03.249729Z'),
(4167, 'vocabulary', '乱戦', '乱戦', 'A <kanji>riot</kanji> of <kanji>war</kanji> is where things go nuts. This is a <vocabulary>melee</vocabulary>!

乱戦 refers to a <vocabulary>chaotic battle</vocabulary>. It can describe actual warfare, sports matches, or other competitive scenarios where things become tumultuous and disorganized.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4167', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%B1%E6%88%A6', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:38:15.015275Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.381316Z'),
(4168, 'vocabulary', '混乱', '混乱', 'There''s a <kanji>riot</kanji> and everything''s getting all <kanji>mix</kanji>ed up! Within that, there is of course a ton of <vocabulary>confusion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4168', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%B7%E4%B9%B1', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:38:20.648283Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:15:00.980995Z'),
(4169, 'vocabulary', '乱交-4169', '乱交', 'A big <kanji>mix</kanji> of folks forming a kind of <kanji>riot</kanji>. You''d think this would be something more violent, but it''s really just an <vocabulary>orgy</vocabulary>. Lots of mixing, I guess, and not very much organization on the orgy-organizer''s part, at least according to this kanji sequence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4169', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%B1%E4%BA%A4-4169', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:38:26.911683Z', '2019-12-24T23:41:08.515772Z', '2025-06-11T05:32:43.427825Z'),
(4170, 'vocabulary', '〜冊', '〜冊', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4170', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%86%8A', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:38:31.991742Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.450432Z'),
(4171, 'vocabulary', '三冊', '三冊', '<kanji>Three</kanji> <kanji>books</kanji> equals <vocabulary>three books</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4171', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E5%86%8A', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:38:38.811512Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.473911Z'),
(4172, 'vocabulary', '八冊', '八冊', 'When you have <kanji>eight</kanji> <kanji>books</kanji> you have <vocabulary>eight books</vocabulary>. This is an example of how to count books, so if you have eight of them this is what you''d say.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4172', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AB%E5%86%8A', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:38:44.301781Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.500664Z'),
(4173, 'vocabulary', '一冊', '一冊', 'When you have <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>books</kanji> you really just have <vocabulary>one book</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4173', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%86%8A', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:38:49.312319Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.523272Z'),
(4174, 'vocabulary', '別冊', '別冊', 'Usually you call <kanji>books</kanji> "volumes" when they come in sets, right? This is like that, but <kanji>separate</kanji>, making this a <vocabulary>separate volume</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4174', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A5%E5%86%8A', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:38:54.697428Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.545832Z'),
(4175, 'vocabulary', 'いい加減', 'いい加減', 'You <kanji>add</kanji> and you <kanji>subtract</kanji>. This brings you back to zero. There was absolutely no point in doing those actions. Completely <vocabulary>pointless</vocabulary>. In fact, I''d say that was downright <vocabulary>irresponsible</vocabulary> of you. The extra いい is there to mean "good," as in "good, you''ve figured out how pointless that was, now stop."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4175', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%84%E3%81%84%E5%8A%A0%E6%B8%9B', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:39:00.676067Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:02.806385Z'),
(4176, 'vocabulary', '原子力', '原子力', 'The <kanji>power</kanji> of the <kanji>atom</kanji> (原子) is <vocabulary>nuclear power</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4176', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%9F%E5%AD%90%E5%8A%9B', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:39:07.010171Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(4177, 'vocabulary', '改善', '改善', 'When you <kanji>renew</kanji> something and it ends up <kanji>morally good</kanji> it''s definitely an <vocabulary>improvement</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4177', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%B9%E5%96%84', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:39:14.010989Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.613391Z'),
(4178, 'vocabulary', '善悪', '善悪', 'You have <kanji>morally good</kanji> and <kanji>bad</kanji>. This is the battle between <vocabulary>good and evil</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4178', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%84%E6%82%AA', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:39:19.270493Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.636013Z'),
(4179, 'vocabulary', '集団', '集団', 'A <kanji>group</kanji> that has <kanji>collect</kanji>ed together is a bunch of people. It is a <vocabulary>group</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>crowd</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4179', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%86%E5%9B%A3', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:39:25.312682Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.661891Z'),
(4180, 'vocabulary', '入団', '入団', 'If you <kanji>enter</kanji> a <kanji>group</kanji>, it''s called <vocabulary>enrollment</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>enlistment</vocabulary>. You''re <vocabulary>joining</vocabulary> the group.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4180', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E5%9B%A3', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:39:31.301988Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T07:34:32.021667Z'),
(4181, 'vocabulary', '困る', '困る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>distressed</kanji> and <kanji>troubled</kanji>. The verb versions of those are <vocabulary>to be distressed</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to be troubled</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4181', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%B0%E3%82%8B', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T03:39:37.455995Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.706991Z'),
(4182, 'vocabulary', '季節', '季節', 'The <kanji>seasons</kanji> <kanji>season</kanji> is the <vocabulary>season</vocabulary>. Who likes winter? It''s my favorite 季節.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4182', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A3%E7%AF%80', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:39:45.240366Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.72876Z'),
(4183, 'vocabulary', '宇宙', '宇宙', 'The <kanji>midair</kanji> of <kanji>outer space</kanji> is the space that''s in outer space which is <vocabulary>outer space</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>space</vocabulary>, and the <vocabulary>universe</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4183', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%87%E5%AE%99', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:39:53.382042Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.750293Z'),
(4184, 'vocabulary', '警官', '警官', 'You know how 警察 is "police"? Well, this is kind of like that. This time you have someone from the <kanji>government</kanji> trying to <kanji>warn</kanji> you about the bad things you do. This person, like 警察 is a <vocabulary>police officer</vocabulary>. It''s kind of informal, so you might also say this means <vocabulary>cop</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4184', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%A6%E5%AE%98', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:40:09.897059Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.773371Z'),
(4185, 'vocabulary', '内容', '内容', '<kanji>Inside</kanji> that <kanji>form</kanji>... there is something inside there. What are the <vocabulary>contents</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4185', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%85%E5%AE%B9', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:40:17.412868Z', NULL, '2025-09-05T05:40:05.235761Z'),
(4186, 'vocabulary', '容疑', '容疑', 'You have the <kanji>appearance</kanji> of <kanji>doubt</kanji> about you. That''s because you''re under <vocabulary>suspicion</vocabulary>. There''s a <vocabulary>charge</vocabulary> against you. Oh no!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4186', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B9%E7%96%91', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:40:23.304755Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:46:31.302923Z'),
(4187, 'vocabulary', '形容詞', '形容詞', 'A <kanji>part of speech</kanji> that creates the <kanji>shape</kanji> and <kanji>form</kanji> of a word is something that is descriptive (its shape and form are in there, after all!). This shape and form part of speech is an <vocabulary>adjective</vocabulary>. You are so beautiful!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4187', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%A2%E5%AE%B9%E8%A9%9E', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:40:28.914699Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.859321Z'),
(4188, 'vocabulary', '履歴書', '履歴書', 'Think about all the important places your <kanji>footwear</kanji> has touched, one place to the next in <kanji>continuation</kanji>, and <kanji>write</kanji> it all down. What you''d get is a <vocabulary>personal history</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>resume</vocabulary>. I guess you can think of it as a history of the schools and jobs you''ve left your footprints at.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4188', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%A5%E6%AD%B4%E6%9B%B8', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:40:34.010053Z', NULL, '2026-02-07T16:21:38.79083Z'),
(4189, 'vocabulary', '履く', '履く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>footwear</kanji>. And what do you do with footwear, usually? You put it on! That''s why this word is <vocabulary>to put on shoes</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to put on pants</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to put on a skirt</vocabulary>.

Okay, pants and skirts aren''t exactly footwear. But 履く is basically used for any garment you wear from the waist down, including socks, shoes, boots, pants, skirts, shorts, and even underpants.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4189', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%A5%E3%81%8F', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:40:39.487687Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:02:59.585767Z'),
(4190, 'vocabulary', '若布', '若布', 'A <kanji>young</kanji> <kanji>cloth</kanji> for some reason is the seaweed <vocabulary>wakame</vocabulary>. If you''ve never heard of wakame before, go look it up. It''s delicious and really good for you. Seaweed is kind of like cloth in the sea, right? Well, this young cloth is nice and soft and thin, like wakame. I imagine young people are soft and thin too, which is why the young cloth gets to be wakame.

Note that this word is more commonly written in kana, like わかめ or ワカメ.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4190', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%A5%E5%B8%83', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:40:51.355906Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.932937Z'),
(4191, 'vocabulary', '布', '布', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4191', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%83', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:41:04.674664Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:43.959576Z'),
(4192, 'vocabulary', '財布', '財布', 'Your <kanji>wealth</kanji> <kanji>cloth</kanji> is your <vocabulary>wallet</vocabulary>. Really, a wallet is just a piece of cloth you keep in your pocket that has your wealth in it, right? This can also be your <vocabulary>purse</vocabulary>, especially if you live in the ol'' UK.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4192', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A1%E5%B8%83', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:41:11.042876Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T16:59:59.548262Z'),
(4193, 'vocabulary', '布団', '布団', 'Put some <kanji>cloth</kanji> in a <kanji>group</kanji> and you get a <vocabulary>futon</vocabulary>. It’s just the kind of nice, soft <vocabulary>bedding</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>sleeping mat</vocabulary> you’d like to curl up on.

Keep in mind that this word is different from the English version. We’re not talking about sofa-beds for your dorm room here. In Japanese, your 布団 is the traditional bedding you lay out on the floor at night and put away in the daytime. 布団 can refer to the sleeping mat, the <vocabulary>duvet</vocabulary> that covers it, or both items as a set.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4193', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%83%E5%9B%A3', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:41:19.322969Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.010227Z'),
(4194, 'vocabulary', '毛布', '毛布', 'A <kanji>fur</kanji> <kanji>cloth</kanji> isn''t something you''d just throw away. You''d probably turn this warm fur cloth into a nice <vocabulary>blanket</vocabulary>.

毛布 usually refers specifically to a <vocabulary>wool blanket</vocabulary>, as opposed to comforters for example.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4194', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%9B%E5%B8%83', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:41:27.099187Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.035065Z'),
(4195, 'vocabulary', '生徒', '生徒', 'One who is <kanji>junior</kanji> in <kanji>life</kanji> is a <vocabulary>student</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>pupil</vocabulary>. It''s kind of like how a 先生 is someone who came previous to you in life. Cool how that works, right? Okay 生徒, get back to work!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4195', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E5%BE%92', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:41:35.233605Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.05745Z'),
(4196, 'vocabulary', '仏教徒', '仏教徒', 'You know 仏教 means “Buddhism” so a <kanji>follower</kanji> of Buddhism is a <vocabulary>Buddhist</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4196', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8F%E6%95%99%E5%BE%92', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:41:41.451855Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T20:01:27.526431Z'),
(4197, 'vocabulary', '信徒', '信徒', 'A <kanji>junior</kanji> <kanji>believe</kanji>r is a <vocabulary>believer</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>follower</vocabulary>. They certainly aren''t the leader or anything because of their junior status, but at least they''re there, and at least they''re believing. Oh, and excuse me, may I take a moment of your time? I''d like to tell you about the glory that is the Crabigator....', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4197', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%A1%E5%BE%92', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:41:47.922705Z', NULL, '2025-11-16T19:02:11.093561Z'),
(4198, 'vocabulary', '得る', '得る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>acquire</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to acquire</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4198', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%97%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:41:52.99755Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.126325Z'),
(4199, 'vocabulary', '得', '得', 'When you are able to <kanji>acquire</kanji> whatever you want, you start to see the <vocabulary>benefit</vocabulary>s of everything around you and begin to <vocabulary>profit</vocabulary> from them. Aren''t you so amazing?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4199', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%97', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:41:59.866147Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.15048Z'),
(4200, 'vocabulary', '得意', '得意', 'When you''re able to <kanji>acquire</kanji> <kanji>idea</kanji>s at a fast rate, you must be familiar with whatever it is you''re doing. If you acquire ideas in this way, there''s a good chance you''re <vocabulary>good at</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>skilled at</vocabulary> this action.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4200', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%97%E6%84%8F', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:42:05.369Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.17433Z'),
(4201, 'vocabulary', '説得', '説得', 'You <kanji>acquire</kanji> <kanji>theory</kanji> after theory. In doing this, you have a lot of theories in your head. You use all these theories as tools for <vocabulary>persuasion</vocabulary>. These theories will allow you to <vocabulary>convince</vocabulary> anyone of anything.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4201', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%AC%E5%BE%97', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:42:11.597889Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.200754Z'),
(4202, 'vocabulary', '忙しい', '忙しい', 'This is a single kanji with しい on the end, meaning it''s an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>busy</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>busy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4202', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%99%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:42:22.75653Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.222583Z'),
(4203, 'vocabulary', '恋人', '恋人', 'A <kanji>person</kanji> of <kanji>romance</kanji> is someone you do romancey things with. That person is your <vocabulary>lover</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4203', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%8B%E4%BA%BA', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:42:28.0792Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(4204, 'vocabulary', '改まる', '改まる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

The kanji itself means <kanji>renew</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be renewed</vocabulary>. Like most verbs ending in まる, this is an intransitive verb, meaning it doesn''t take a direct object. Something renews or gets renewed, or something changes. See? No direct object.

Although it''s not often translated as "renew," 改まる implies a kind of renewal in the changes it''s used to describe — whether it''s the revision of regulations or the old year changing over to a new one. It can also be used for personal improvements, or describe a shift to a more formal demeanor in social situations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4204', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%B9%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T03:42:41.688824Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.277579Z'),
(4205, 'vocabulary', '改正', '改正', '<kanji>Renew</kanji> and make something <kanji>correct</kanji>. When you do this, you are doing a <vocabulary>revision</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>amendment</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>alteration</vocabulary>. Ah, much better now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4205', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%B9%E6%AD%A3', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:42:48.194509Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.300092Z'),
(4206, 'vocabulary', '大失敗', '大失敗', 'You know how 失敗 is a mistake? Well, a <kanji>large</kanji> <kanji>mistake</kanji> is going to be a <vocabulary>total failure</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>huge mistake</vocabulary>. So terrible!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4206', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%A4%B1%E6%95%97', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:42:57.4388Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(4207, 'vocabulary', '昆布', '昆布', '<kanji>Insect</kanji> <kanji>cloth</kanji> is actually <vocabulary>konbu</vocabulary>. <vocabulary>Konbu seaweed</vocabulary> is very common in Japan, and it''s delicious. And insects happen to love using it as cloth, too. They make all their clothes out of konbu.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4207', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%86%E5%B8%83', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:43:07.76712Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.345762Z'),
(4208, 'vocabulary', '易しい', '易しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>easy</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>easy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4208', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%93%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T03:43:13.731512Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.3678Z'),
(4209, 'vocabulary', '容易', '容易', 'The <kanji>form</kanji> in front of you is <kanji>easy</kanji>. Whatever it is, you''ll be able to do it with <vocabulary>ease</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4209', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B9%E6%98%93', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:29:08.833738Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.390741Z'),
(4210, 'vocabulary', '暴れる', '暴れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>violence</kanji>. "To violence" doesn''t sound great, so instead this word is <vocabulary>to act violently</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to rage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4210', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%B4%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:29:14.924294Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.413406Z'),
(4211, 'vocabulary', '暴力', '暴力', 'The <kanji>power</kanji> of <kanji>violence</kanji> is more <vocabulary>violence</vocabulary>, and it is a <vocabulary>force</vocabulary> to be reckoned with.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4211', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%B4%E5%8A%9B', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:29:21.477411Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(4212, 'vocabulary', '乱暴', '乱暴', '<kanji>Riot</kanji> and <kanji>violence</kanji>. That sounds terrible! That sounds like a place full of violence, because rioting is <vocabulary>violent</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>violence</vocabulary> is violent too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4212', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%B1%E6%9A%B4', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:29:29.08474Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.458235Z'),
(4213, 'vocabulary', '暴走', '暴走', '<kanji>Violent</kanji> <kanji>running</kanji>... sounds like a marathon gone wrong. No, this is when you run around and you''re all violent. A better way to describe this would be to say "<vocabulary>run wild</vocabulary>" or "<vocabulary>run out of control</vocabulary>."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4213', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%B4%E8%B5%B0', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:29:35.057983Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.481582Z'),
(4214, 'vocabulary', '梅干し', '梅干し', 'You know 梅 is a Japanese plum, and 干す means "to dry something." So when you combine them as 梅干し, you get dried plums, right? Well, sort of. You have to pickle them before you dry them, so we can say 梅干し means <vocabulary>pickled plum</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>pickled ume</vocabulary>. And if you know the Japanese name, you can call it by that too: <vocabulary>umeboshi</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4214', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A2%85%E5%B9%B2%E3%81%97', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:29:40.376795Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:56:52.200313Z'),
(4215, 'vocabulary', '歴史', '歴史', 'The <kanji>continuation</kanji> of <kanji>history</kanji> is <vocabulary>history</vocabulary> itself. This is the main word for "history" and you''ll see it a lot if you''re in school or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4215', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%B4%E5%8F%B2', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:29:46.83048Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:12:50.785587Z'),
(4216, 'vocabulary', '学歴', '学歴', 'A <kanji>continuation</kanji> of <kanji>study</kanji>ing is the time that passes that we study. This is <vocabulary>academic history</vocabulary>, so pay close attention!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4216', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A6%E6%AD%B4', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:29:55.360688Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:12:50.785587Z'),
(4217, 'vocabulary', '比べる', '比べる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>compare</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to compare</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4217', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%94%E3%81%B9%E3%82%8B', 11, 1, '2012-11-01T04:30:02.319357Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.578Z'),
(4218, 'vocabulary', '比例', '比例', 'You <kanji>compare</kanji> <kanji>example</kanji>s, trying to figure out in which way they''re <vocabulary>being proportional</vocabulary>. You know these examples you''re comparing are in <vocabulary>proportion</vocabulary> to one another, you just need to figure out how. 

Use 比例 when talking about how one thing changes in proportion to another. Like the price of a good in proportion to its supply and demand, for instance. Check out our common word combinations and context sentences for some examples!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4218', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%94%E4%BE%8B', 15, 1, '2012-11-01T04:30:07.483864Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.290724Z'),
(4219, 'vocabulary', '対比', '対比', 'When you look at something <kanji>versus</kanji> something else, you <kanji>compare</kanji> them with one another. You look at the <vocabulary>contrast</vocabulary> between them and make a <vocabulary>comparison</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4219', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BE%E6%AF%94', 9, 1, '2012-11-01T04:30:20.787355Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:12:11.505385Z'),
(4220, 'vocabulary', '混ぜる', '混ぜる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>mix</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to mix something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to stir something</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to blend something</vocabulary>. 

This is when someone is actively doing the mixing. You can remember this because <reading>they''re</reading> (ぜる) doing the mixing themselves, rather than just sitting around and waiting for something to naturally mix on its own.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4220', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%B7%E3%81%9C%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:30:26.532248Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:51:15.260371Z'),
(4221, 'vocabulary', '混ざる', '混ざる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>mix</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to get mixed</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be mixed</vocabulary>. 

混ざる is used to talk about something getting mixed, without focusing on who did the mixing. You can remember this because the <reading>Tzar</reading> (ざる) of Russia presided over lots of things getting mixed together — peoples, languages, religions. The Tzar''s empire was a big melting pot where all kinds of things got mixed together.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4221', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%B7%E3%81%96%E3%82%8B', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T04:30:32.326058Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:53:11.760532Z'),
(4222, 'vocabulary', '減る', '減る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>decrease</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to decrease</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be decreased</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4222', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%9B%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:31:02.579381Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:02.806385Z'),
(4223, 'vocabulary', '減法', '減法', 'What''s a <kanji>method</kanji> to <kanji>decrease</kanji> something? Well, you take something away. You do <vocabulary>subtraction</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4223', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%9B%E6%B3%95', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:31:20.154526Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:02.806385Z'),
(4224, 'vocabulary', '節句', '節句', 'Think of what a <kanji>paragraph</kanji> of a <kanji>season</kanji> would be. It''s a small part of the season, but it''s worth writing about. That''s a <vocabulary>seasonal festival</vocabulary>. These are fun!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4224', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AF%80%E5%8F%A5', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:31:32.882649Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.736562Z'),
(4225, 'vocabulary', '絡む', '絡む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>entangle</kanji>, but this verb version is usually used in a figurative sense, so it means <vocabulary>to be involved with</vocabulary>, as in 事件に絡む (to be involved with an incident).

絡む can still describe something thread-like winding around something else though, like ツタが木に絡む (the ivy twines around the tree), so it also means <vocabulary>to get tangled</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to entwine</vocabulary>. Note that this use is still intransitive because it focuses on the state of things being entwined, and に marks the location of the action, not a direct object.

To remember that this is intransitive, imagine suddenly hearing a lot of <reading>moo</reading>ing (む) and turning around to find that your cows have somehow gotten entangled. They''re all just suddenly in a knot. HOW??', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4225', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%A1%E3%82%80', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:31:44.90791Z', NULL, '2025-07-22T20:19:03.682653Z'),
(4226, 'vocabulary', '続く', '続く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>continue</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to continue</vocabulary>. Please continue.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4226', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%9A%E3%81%8F', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:31:52.968974Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:18:52.065407Z'),
(4227, 'vocabulary', '続々', '続々', 'When you <kanji>continue</kanji> and then you <kanji>continue</kanji> again (that''s the <kanji>repeater</kanji> kanji), you get things happening <vocabulary>one after another</vocabulary>. Continue continue continue!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4227', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%9A%E3%80%85', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:32:08.397855Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:18:52.065407Z'),
(4228, 'vocabulary', '舌', '舌', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4228', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%8C', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:33:00.907758Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.841289Z'),
(4229, 'vocabulary', '猫舌', '猫舌', 'A <kanji>cat</kanji>''s <kanji>tongue</kanji> is for some reason associated with an <vocabulary>aversion to hot foods</vocabulary>. I guess I haven''t seen a cat drinking up boiling milk, but whatever. Just imagine a cat licking up something super hot and freaking out. Cats don''t like hot things, which is why this is an aversion to hot foods.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4229', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8C%AB%E8%88%8C', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:33:35.067614Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.86448Z'),
(4230, 'vocabulary', '若い', '若い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>young</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>young</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4230', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%A5%E3%81%84', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:33:42.225714Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.885872Z'),
(4231, 'vocabulary', '若者', '若者', 'A <kanji>young</kanji> <kanji>someone</kanji> is a <vocabulary>young person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4231', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%A5%E8%80%85', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:33:47.006521Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(4232, 'vocabulary', '若々しい', '若々しい', 'A <kanji>young</kanji> <kanji>young</kanji> thing has <vocabulary>youthful</vocabulary>ness on their side. Oh to be youthful and <vocabulary>young looking</vocabulary> again!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4232', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%A5%E3%80%85%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:33:51.801636Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(4233, 'vocabulary', '名詞', '名詞', 'A <kanji>name</kanji> <kanji>part of speech</kanji> is when you are naming something. What part of speech has names in it? That would be the <vocabulary>noun</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4233', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8D%E8%A9%9E', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:34:18.249665Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(4234, 'vocabulary', '動詞', '動詞', 'A <kanji>part of speech</kanji> that represents <kanji>moving</kanji> is a part of speech that describes actions. That is a <vocabulary>verb</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4234', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%95%E8%A9%9E', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:34:23.580687Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:44.974865Z'),
(4235, 'vocabulary', '歌詞', '歌詞', 'A <kanji>song</kanji>''s <kanji>part of speech</kanji> is the part of the song where you''re talking (aka singing). These are the word parts of the song. These are the <vocabulary>song lyrics</vocabulary>. Anyone ready for some カラオケ?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4235', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%8C%E8%A9%9E', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:34:29.170239Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:03:31.014085Z'),
(4236, 'vocabulary', '助詞', '助詞', 'A <kanji>part of speech</kanji> that''s just meant to <kanji>help</kanji> your grammar out are the little things. They tie grammar and words together, and that''s about it. These are <vocabulary>grammar particle</vocabulary>s or just plain old <vocabulary>particle</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4236', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%A9%E8%A9%9E', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:34:34.529905Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.027055Z'),
(4237, 'vocabulary', '数詞', '数詞', 'A <kanji>number</kanji> <kanji>part of speech</kanji> is a <vocabulary>numeral</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>number word</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4237', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B0%E8%A9%9E', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:34:40.659386Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.041761Z'),
(4238, 'vocabulary', '自動詞', '自動詞', 'Remember how 動詞 means "verb"? When the verb''s action happens by it<kanji>self</kanji> or to the subject itself, it''s an <vocabulary>intransitive verb</vocabulary>.

In other words, it doesn''t need a direct object to complete its action. These are verbs like  行く or  着く or  消える. They''re doing something, but they''re not doing it <em>to</em> anyone or anything. Can you think of other  自動詞 in Japanese?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4238', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E5%8B%95%E8%A9%9E', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:34:57.199997Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.075429Z'),
(4239, 'vocabulary', '無意識', '無意識', 'Know how 意識 is "consciousness"? What about when this <kanji>consciousness</kanji> doesn''t exist (aka it is <kanji>nothing</kanji>)? That''s when it becomes <vocabulary>unconsciousness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4239', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E6%84%8F%E8%AD%98', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:35:09.119863Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:21:29.482657Z'),
(4240, 'vocabulary', '私財', '私財', '<kanji>Private</kanji> <kanji>wealth</kanji> are <vocabulary>private funds</vocabulary> or your <vocabulary>own funds</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4240', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%81%E8%B2%A1', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:35:40.805302Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T16:59:59.548262Z'),
(4241, 'vocabulary', '借財-4241', '借財', 'The <kanji>wealth</kanji> you <kanji>borrow</kanji> is your <vocabulary>debt</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4241', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%9F%E8%B2%A1-4241', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:35:46.836226Z', '2021-09-02T17:43:09.966494Z', '2026-01-07T16:59:59.548262Z'),
(4242, 'vocabulary', '連続', '連続', 'If you <kanji>take along</kanji> and <kanji>continue</kanji> something, you are creating a <vocabulary>serial</vocabulary> version of it. Come along now. Okay, continue! Come along now. Okay, continue! This is how Lord of the Rings was made, probably. Come along now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4242', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%A3%E7%B6%9A', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:35:52.643768Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:18:52.065407Z'),
(4243, 'vocabulary', '連れる', '連れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>take along</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary> to take along</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4243', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%A3%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:35:58.428635Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.197758Z'),
(4244, 'vocabulary', '財閥', '財閥', 'A <kanji>wealth</kanji> <kanji>clan</kanji> is a group of people who do whatever they can to get their money. These are the <vocabulary>plutocrats</vocabulary> of the world, but you can also call them a <vocabulary>financial clique</vocabulary>.

財閥, or the <vocabulary>zaibatsu</vocabulary>, were large family-controlled conglomerates prominent in Japan until the end of World War II. Some, like Mitsubishi, survive in modernized form. You might see zaibatsu mentioned in books on Japanese history or economics.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4244', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A1%E9%96%A5', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:36:04.27467Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T16:59:59.548262Z'),
(4245, 'vocabulary', '非常に', '非常に', 'When something is moving towards <kanji>emergency</kanji> (非常), it is something that is <vocabulary>very</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>extremely</vocabulary> bad. Like, <vocabulary>exceedingly</vocabulary> so. It''s nuts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4245', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%9E%E5%B8%B8%E3%81%AB', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:36:09.975212Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.249181Z'),
(4246, 'vocabulary', '非常口', '非常口', 'You know how 非常 means <kanji>emergency</kanji> and how 口 can be some kind of doorway (like in 入り口, for example)? Well, if that''s the case, then you have an emergency doorway. Usually you run away from emergencies, though, making this an <vocabulary>emergency exit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4246', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%9E%E5%B8%B8%E5%8F%A3', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:36:17.983126Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.27078Z'),
(4247, 'vocabulary', '順位', '順位', 'The <kanji>order</kanji> that you are <kanji>rank</kanji>ed in is your <vocabulary>ranking</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>standing</vocabulary> amongst others on a list. Beat that high score, Ryu!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4247', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%86%E4%BD%8D', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:36:29.369803Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:19:51.47423Z'),
(4248, 'vocabulary', '順番', '順番', 'When you go in <kanji>order</kanji> of <kanji>turn</kanji>s that''s the <vocabulary>order</vocabulary>. It also represents <vocabulary>one''s turn</vocabulary> in said order as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4248', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%86%E7%95%AA', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:36:34.807586Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:19:51.47423Z'),
(4249, 'vocabulary', '大きい順', '大きい順', 'When the <kanji>order</kanji> is from <kanji>big</kanji> (to small), you are talking about <vocabulary>decreasing order</vocabulary>. That''s <vocabulary>largest first</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4249', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E3%81%8D%E3%81%84%E9%A0%86', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:36:40.103878Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(4250, 'vocabulary', '小さい順', '小さい順', 'When something goes from <kanji>small</kanji> and goes in <kanji>order</kanji> it''s in <vocabulary>increasing order</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>smallest first</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4250', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E3%81%95%E3%81%84%E9%A0%86', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:36:46.213474Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:19:51.47423Z'),
(4251, 'vocabulary', '席順', '席順', 'The <kanji>order</kanji> of <kanji>seat</kanji>s is the <vocabulary>seating order</vocabulary>. Everyone go sit in your proper positions, please.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4251', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%AD%E9%A0%86', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:36:51.882765Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:19:51.47423Z'),
(4252, 'vocabulary', '年代順', '年代順', 'You put <kanji>year</kanji>s in <kanji>order</kanji> of the <kanji>period</kanji> they come in and you''re really putting them in <vocabulary>chronological order</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4252', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E4%BB%A3%E9%A0%86', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:36:57.790893Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:19:51.47423Z'),
(4253, 'vocabulary', '五十音順', '五十音順', 'This is a little confusing for someone who didn''t grow up with the Japanese language. You have <kanji>fifty</kanji> <kanji>sound</kanji>s. Then, they''re in a certain <kanji>order</kanji>. So, these fifty sounds are the Japanese syllabary. That''s hiragana / katakana, probably from back in the day when there were more sounds than there are now. So, the "fifty sounds in order" makes up <vocabulary>aiueo order</vocabulary>, where the "aiueo" is あいうえお representing the five columns.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4253', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%94%E5%8D%81%E9%9F%B3%E9%A0%86', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T04:37:13.983021Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:19:51.47423Z'),
(4254, 'vocabulary', '余る', '余る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>surplus</kanji> so the verb version would be <vocabulary>to be in surplus</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to remain left over</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to be in excess</vocabulary>. Too much!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4254', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%99%E3%82%8B', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:45:14.643046Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.44754Z'),
(4255, 'vocabulary', '余計', '余計', 'The <kanji>surplus</kanji> is <kanji>measure</kanji>d and it''s all <vocabulary>unneeded</vocabulary>. Yeah, it''s definitely <vocabulary>more than needed</vocabulary>. Maybe we should get rid of all this surplus?

余計 usually carries a negative connotation, meaning there''s <vocabulary>too much</vocabulary> of something, or it''s not needed and could be left out. It could be too many things, or unnecessary words or actions it would be better not to say or do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4255', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%99%E8%A8%88', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:45:20.329701Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.468521Z'),
(4256, 'vocabulary', '残余', '残余', 'The <kanji>remaining</kanji> <kanji>surplus</kanji> is the <vocabulary>remainder</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>residual</vocabulary> left over.

Just bear in mind that this is a technical word that you''ll mostly see in legal and financial contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4256', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%8B%E4%BD%99', 51, 1, '2012-11-01T06:45:28.162633Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.49123Z'),
(4257, 'vocabulary', '個人', '個人', 'In terms of an <kanji>individual</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji>... well, that''s <vocabulary>personal</vocabulary>, and depends on the <vocabulary>individual</vocabulary>. Whatever it is, an individual person has personal tastes, so make sure to think of that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4257', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%8B%E4%BA%BA', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:45:34.081704Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(4258, 'vocabulary', '一個', '一個', 'The counter 個, which means <kanji>individual</kanji> on its own, refers to "an individual thing." This is usually a small thing, so it''s used to count small things. This word here talks about <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>individual</kanji>. A better way to translate that would be to call it <vocabulary>one small thing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4258', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%80%8B', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:45:39.963802Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:26:22.13496Z'),
(4259, 'vocabulary', '個室', '個室', 'An <kanji>individual</kanji> <kanji>room</kanji> just for you is your own <vocabulary>private room</vocabulary>. How nice!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4259', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%8B%E5%AE%A4', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:45:46.130467Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:26:22.13496Z'),
(4260, 'vocabulary', '三個', '三個', '<kanji>Three</kanji> <kanji>individual</kanji>s is <vocabulary>three small things</vocabulary>. The kanji 個 can be used to count small objects, just like this! Try other numbers as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4260', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E5%80%8B', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:45:51.796022Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:26:22.13496Z'),
(4261, 'vocabulary', '倒す', '倒す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

The kanji itself means <kanji>overthrow</kanji> or <kanji>collapse</kanji>, and this is a transitive verb (like most verbs ending in す) related to those actions. It means <vocabulary>to knock over</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to bring down</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to defeat</vocabulary>.

It can be used to talk about physically knocking something over, as well as bringing something or someone down, like a government, or defeating someone, like an enemy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4261', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%92%E3%81%99', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:45:57.451135Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.600907Z'),
(4262, 'vocabulary', '面倒', '面倒', 'When you''re looking straight into the <kanji>face</kanji> of a <kanji>collapse</kanji> it means <vocabulary>trouble</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>difficulty</vocabulary> coming soon.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4262', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%A2%E5%80%92', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:46:10.847239Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.624866Z'),
(4263, 'vocabulary', '倒産', '倒産', 'If someone <kanji>overthrow</kanji>s your <kanji>property</kanji>, it''ll end in <vocabulary>bankruptcy</vocabulary>. Without property you''re sure to <vocabulary>go bankrupt</vocabulary>, unfortunately.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4263', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%92%E7%94%A3', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:46:22.79798Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.650626Z'),
(4264, 'vocabulary', '借り手', '借り手', 'Remember how 手 can mean "doer" in some words? So here, you have <kanji>borrow</kanji> <kanji>doer</kanji>, or more simply <vocabulary>borrower</vocabulary>.

This word is often used to specifically refer to borrowers in contrast to lenders.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4264', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%9F%E3%82%8A%E6%89%8B', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:46:27.925779Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:44:31.146854Z'),
(4265, 'vocabulary', '厚い', '厚い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>thick</kanji>? Also <vocabulary>thick</vocabulary>.

This is a relatively formal word that is generally used in writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4265', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%9A%E3%81%84', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:46:33.494982Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.70322Z'),
(4266, 'vocabulary', '圧力', '圧力', 'The <kanji>pressure</kanji> <kanji>power</kanji> is the <vocabulary>pressure</vocabulary>. This can also be <vocabulary>stress</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4266', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%A7%E5%8A%9B', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:46:48.620906Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(4267, 'vocabulary', '血圧', '血圧', 'Check the <kanji>pressure</kanji> of your <kanji>blood</kanji>. It''s your <vocabulary>blood pressure</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4267', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%80%E5%9C%A7', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:47:00.030945Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:28:34.36878Z'),
(4268, 'vocabulary', '指圧', '指圧', 'When someone applies <kanji>finger</kanji> <kanji>pressure</kanji>, they''re probably practicing <vocabulary>shiatsu</vocabulary>.

Shiatsu is an alternative therapy from Japan, which involves applying pressure with the fingertips to specific spots on the body. This word can also refer to any kind of <vocabulary>finger pressure</vocabulary>, logically enough!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4268', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87%E5%9C%A7', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:47:06.465924Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:28:34.36878Z'),
(4269, 'vocabulary', '存在', '存在', 'I <kanji>exist</kanji>, I <kanji>suppose</kanji>... this is the kind of <vocabulary>existence</vocabulary> I have. I''m not really sure either way, and I don''t care. Existence is futile, I suppose.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4269', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%98%E5%9C%A8', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:47:13.83005Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.796475Z'),
(4270, 'vocabulary', '現在', '現在', 'In the <kanji>present time</kanji> we <kanji>exist</kanji>. What time is it? It''s <vocabulary>present</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>now</vocabulary>. It is the <vocabulary>current</vocabulary> time!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4270', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%BE%E5%9C%A8', 23, 1, '2012-11-01T06:47:19.012237Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.818768Z'),
(4271, 'vocabulary', '実在', '実在', 'The <kanji>truth</kanji> is that you <kanji>exist</kanji> in <vocabulary>reality</vocabulary>. This is your <vocabulary>real existence</vocabulary>. Well, that makes sense, I suppose.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4271', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9F%E5%9C%A8', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:47:24.214217Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.84392Z'),
(4272, 'vocabulary', '在外', '在外', 'When you <kanji>exist</kanji> <kanji>outside</kanji> (outside of what? Japan, of course), you are <vocabulary>overseas</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>abroad</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4272', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%A8%E5%A4%96', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:47:29.298542Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(4273, 'vocabulary', '夢中', '夢中', 'Even in the <kanji>middle</kanji> of your <kanji>dream</kanji> you''re thinking about your body pillows. You''re <vocabulary>crazy about</vocabulary> them, wrapped in <vocabulary>absorption</vocabulary>.

Alternatively, this describes doing something <vocabulary>frantic</vocabulary>ally, like running away from somebody or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4273', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A2%E4%B8%AD', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:47:34.57029Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:07:24.930649Z'),
(4274, 'vocabulary', '悪夢', '悪夢', 'A <kanji>bad</kanji> <kanji>dream</kanji> is also known as a <vocabulary>nightmare</vocabulary>. Ah! Noooooo!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4274', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E5%A4%A2', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:47:40.104486Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.923178Z'),
(4275, 'vocabulary', '夢', '夢', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4275', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A2', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:47:45.674511Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:45.949707Z'),
(4276, 'vocabulary', '妨害', '妨害', 'Something that <kanji>obstruct</kanji>s and also does <kanji>damage</kanji> is an <vocabulary>obstruction</vocabulary>. No obstruction is ever good and if left too long it really will cause damage and <vocabulary>interference</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4276', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%A8%E5%AE%B3', 31, 1, '2012-11-01T06:47:51.337948Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(4277, 'vocabulary', '妨げる', '妨げる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>obstruct</kanji>, so what is the verb vocab version? It''s <vocabulary>to obstruct</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4277', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%A8%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2012-11-01T06:47:57.954242Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.054014Z'),
(4278, 'vocabulary', '被る', '被る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji means <kanji>cover</kanji>, so the verb version means <vocabulary>to put on</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to wear</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to cover oneself with</vocabulary>.

被る is used when you wear things that cover your head or face, like hats, helmets, and masks, or when cover yourself with something, like pulling a blanket over yourself. Figuratively, it can also refer to overlapping events — think of it like one event "covering" another on a timeline.

Since the kanji''s other meaning is <kanji>incur</kanji>, 被る can also mean taking on a burden or blame — incurring responsibility, in other words.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4278', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A2%AB%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2012-11-01T06:48:03.831345Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:34:08.629351Z'),
(4279, 'vocabulary', '妻', '妻', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This is used to refer to your own wife, though, for the most part.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4279', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%BB', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:48:10.704812Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.076579Z'),
(4280, 'vocabulary', '夫妻', '夫妻', 'You have a <kanji>husband</kanji> and a <kanji>wife</kanji>. Combine them together and they''re <vocabulary>husband and wife</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>married couple</vocabulary>. This can also be added to a name to mean <vocabulary>Mr. and Mrs.</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4280', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%AB%E5%A6%BB', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:48:17.476811Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.101815Z'),
(4281, 'vocabulary', '妻子', '妻子', 'You have your <kanji>wife</kanji> and you have your <kanji>child</kanji>. These are your <vocabulary>wife and kids</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4281', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%BB%E5%AD%90', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:48:34.66829Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.126901Z'),
(4282, 'vocabulary', '一夫多妻', '一夫多妻', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>husband</kanji>, <kanji>many</kanji> <kanji>wives</kanji>. This is <vocabulary>polygamy</vocabulary>.

More specifically, 一夫多妻 is a term for <vocabulary>polygyny</vocabulary>, or marriage between one man and multiple women. You''ll mostly run into it in period dramas or discussions of history and anthropology, since it was common among the ruling elite of certain societies during premodern times.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4282', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%A4%AB%E5%A4%9A%E5%A6%BB', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:48:40.010626Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:40:52.607073Z'),
(4283, 'vocabulary', '嫌', '嫌', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. The vocab has the added meanings of <vocabulary>eww</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>gross</vocabulary>, and even <vocabulary>reluctant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4283', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AB%8C', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:48:45.676815Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.172958Z'),
(4284, 'vocabulary', '嫌い', '嫌い', 'You know that 嫌 means <kanji>dislike</kanji> and this vocab word means the same thing: <vocabulary>dislike</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>hate</vocabulary>!

Though this word is an adjective in Japanese, it usually makes sense to translate it as a verb in English, because of the difference in sentence structure. However, a more literal translation would be <vocabulary>dislikable</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>detestable</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4284', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AB%8C%E3%81%84', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:48:50.669249Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.199814Z'),
(4285, 'vocabulary', '機嫌', '機嫌', '<kanji>Machine</kanji>s <kanji>dislike</kanji> everything. Their <vocabulary>mood</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>temper</vocabulary>, is very sour. What about your mood?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4285', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A9%9F%E5%AB%8C', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:48:57.384978Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:04:30.700843Z'),
(4286, 'vocabulary', '嫌疑', '嫌疑', 'I <kanji>dislike</kanji> him and I have <kanji>doubt</kanji>s about him. I therefore have much <vocabulary>suspicion</vocabulary> against him as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4286', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AB%8C%E7%96%91', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:49:02.607913Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:46:31.302923Z'),
(4287, 'vocabulary', '留守番電話', '留守番電話', 'You hopefully know that this has something to do with <vocabulary>housesitting</vocabulary> (留守番). So, a <vocabulary>telephone</vocabulary> (電話) that does housesitting for you is what? It''s your <vocabulary>answering machine</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>voicemail</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4287', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%99%E5%AE%88%E7%95%AA%E9%9B%BB%E8%A9%B1', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:49:14.103406Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.030484Z'),
(4288, 'vocabulary', '公害', '公害', '<kanji>Public</kanji> <kanji>damage</kanji> is something that affects everyone, the whole public. What is in the air and gets into everything no matter where you are? How about <vocabulary>pollution</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4288', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AC%E5%AE%B3', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:49:19.15413Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(4289, 'vocabulary', '水害', '水害', '<kanji>Water</kanji> <kanji>damage</kanji> is so bad they call it <vocabulary>water damage</vocabulary>. Doesn''t get any clearer than this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4289', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E5%AE%B3', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:49:24.279565Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(4290, 'vocabulary', '無害', '無害', 'Psh, that guy''s <kanji>damage</kanji> is <kanji>nothing</kanji>. He''s what I''d call "mostly <vocabulary>harmless</vocabulary>."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4290', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E5%AE%B3', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:49:29.423244Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(4291, 'vocabulary', 'お尻', 'お尻', 'The お is just there to be nice to your butt. This word simply means <vocabulary>butt</vocabulary> (or any other word for it, like <vocabulary>buttocks</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>bottom</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4291', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E5%B0%BB', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:49:37.252938Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:23:24.054216Z'),
(4292, 'vocabulary', '尻尾', '尻尾', 'Your <kanji>butt</kanji> <kanji>tail</kanji> is just your <vocabulary>tail</vocabulary>, though I wonder why they had to specify that it was your butt tail. Where else do you have a tail, I wonder?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4292', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%BB%E5%B0%BE', 22, 1, '2012-11-01T06:49:43.028433Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:23:24.054216Z'),
(4293, 'vocabulary', '弓矢', '弓矢', 'A <kanji>bow</kanji> and an <kanji>arrow</kanji>. Put them together and you have a <vocabulary>bow and arrow</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4293', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%93%E7%9F%A2', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:49:48.240473Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.41934Z'),
(4294, 'vocabulary', '忘れ物', '忘れ物', 'Remember how 忘れる is "to forget"? Well, a <kanji>thing</kanji> you''ve forgotten is a <vocabulary>forgotten item</vocabulary>.

忘れ物 is a casual way to refer to something left behind accidentally, like an umbrella you forgot about and left on the train.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4294', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%98%E3%82%8C%E7%89%A9', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:49:59.234448Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(4295, 'vocabulary', '機械', '機械', 'A <kanji>machine</kanji> <kanji>contraption</kanji> is just a <vocabulary>machine</vocabulary>', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4295', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A9%9F%E6%A2%B0', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:50:04.51529Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:04:30.700843Z'),
(4296, 'vocabulary', '飛行機', '飛行機', 'The <kanji>fly</kanji> and <kanji>go</kanji> <kanji>machine</kanji> is some machine you get into that flies and goes places. It''s an <vocabulary>airplane</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4296', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9B%E8%A1%8C%E6%A9%9F', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:50:10.22033Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:04:30.700843Z'),
(4297, 'vocabulary', '危機', '危機', 'A <kanji>dangerous</kanji> <kanji>machine</kanji> is walking through your town, destroying everything. This is a huge <vocabulary>crisis</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4297', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B1%E6%A9%9F', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:50:15.814311Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:04:30.700843Z'),
(4298, 'vocabulary', '災い', '災い', 'This is the noun form of <kanji>disaster</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>disaster</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>misfortune</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4298', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%BD%E3%81%84', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:50:22.787618Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.536651Z'),
(4299, 'vocabulary', '災害', '災害', 'A <kanji>disaster</kanji> that does <kanji>damage</kanji>. Usually these are <vocabulary>natural disaster</vocabulary>s, though they could also just be regular <vocabulary>disaster</vocabulary>s as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4299', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%BD%E5%AE%B3', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:50:47.318593Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(4300, 'vocabulary', '火災', '火災', 'A <kanji>fire</kanji> <kanji>disaster</kanji> is a <vocabulary>fire disaster</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>conflagration</vocabulary>.

火災 is a more formal version of 火事. It usually refers to a serious and significant fire that causes extensive damage or loss of life.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4300', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%AB%E7%81%BD', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:51:02.535973Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.588684Z'),
(4301, 'vocabulary', '災難', '災難', 'A <kanji>disaster</kanji> that is <kanji>difficult</kanji> is particularly difficult, making it more of a <vocabulary>misfortune</vocabulary>. Obviously a disaster that wasn''t difficult wouldn''t really be that bad, so a difficult one gets the "misfortune" or <vocabulary>calamity</vocabulary> label.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4301', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%BD%E9%9B%A3', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:53:25.71479Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.615782Z'),
(4302, 'vocabulary', '震災', '震災', 'An <kanji>earthquake</kanji> <kanji>disaster</kanji> is just that, an <vocabulary>earthquake disaster</vocabulary>. These are no good.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4302', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%87%E7%81%BD', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:53:31.316299Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.641976Z'),
(4303, 'vocabulary', '煙い', '煙い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>smoke</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>smoky</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4303', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%99%E3%81%84', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:53:36.731545Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:12:49.709742Z'),
(4304, 'vocabulary', '犯す', '犯す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>crime</kanji> and the verb vocab version can mean <vocabulary>to commit a crime</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to make a mistake</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to rape</vocabulary>, depending on the context.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4304', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8A%AF%E3%81%99', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:53:41.564421Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.688684Z'),
(4305, 'vocabulary', '犯人', '犯人', 'A <kanji>crime</kanji>  <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>culprit</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>criminal</vocabulary>.

Just note that 犯人 is mostly used to talk about the culprit of a specific crime, not criminals in general.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4305', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8A%AF%E4%BA%BA', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:53:46.640952Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(4306, 'vocabulary', '共犯者', '共犯者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> who does <kanji>crime</kanji> <kanji>together</kanji> with you is your <vocabulary>accomplice</vocabulary> in crime. It''s best to "take care of" these before the coppers get to them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4306', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B1%E7%8A%AF%E8%80%85', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:53:52.152656Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(4307, 'vocabulary', '確率', '確率', 'You''re <kanji>certain</kanji> there is this <kanji>percent</kanji> chance it will happen? You know that this is the <vocabulary>probability</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4307', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A2%BA%E7%8E%87', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:53:57.862777Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.757183Z'),
(4308, 'vocabulary', '比率', '比率', 'You <kanji>compare</kanji> the <kanji>percentage</kanji>, figuring out the <vocabulary>ratio</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>proportion</vocabulary> of one thing to another. This can also be expressed as a <vocabulary>percentage</vocabulary>!

比率 usually shows up in cases where you compare two numbers with each other, like the number of male and female students in a classroom. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4308', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%94%E7%8E%87', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:54:04.170692Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.782319Z'),
(4309, 'vocabulary', '産む', '産む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>give birth</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to give birth</vocabulary>.

You know another 産む as well (生む). This 産む is more sciency, but both are good to know.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4309', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%A3%E3%82%80', 21, 1, '2012-11-01T06:54:19.33896Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.804452Z'),
(4310, 'vocabulary', '確かに', '確かに', 'This is an adverb, but it has the same meaning (basically) as the kanji. It means <vocabulary>certainly</vocabulary>, and it can also mean <vocabulary>surely</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4310', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A2%BA%E3%81%8B%E3%81%AB', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:54:26.810648Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T09:14:00.43324Z'),
(4311, 'vocabulary', '正確', '正確', 'It is <kanji>correct</kanji> and you''re <kanji>certain</kanji> of it. When you''ve done this much checking and you''re this sure, your measurements must be very <vocabulary>accurate</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>precise</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4311', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A3%E7%A2%BA', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:54:39.200591Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.849404Z'),
(4312, 'vocabulary', '立入禁止', '立入禁止', '<kanji>Stand</kanji>ing and <kanji>enter</kanji>ing are both 禁止 (prohibited). If you see this sign, it''s essentially saying <vocabulary>do not enter</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>no entry</vocabulary>.

While this type of sign will usually say 立入禁止, you may also encounter variations such as 立ち入り禁止 or 立入り禁止.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4312', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%8B%E5%85%A5%E7%A6%81%E6%AD%A2', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:54:44.848726Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:46.870823Z'),
(4313, 'vocabulary', '穴', '穴', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4313', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%B4', 4, 1, '2012-11-01T06:54:50.776349Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.64284Z'),
(4314, 'vocabulary', '穴場', '穴場', 'Hey, this <kanji>hole</kanji> is a really nice <kanji>place</kanji>! But no one knows about it because they think it''s just a hole. That makes it a <vocabulary>hidden gem</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>hidden spot</vocabulary>, and you better hope the tourists don''t hear about it.

穴場 is for places that are good but relatively unknown. You can use it for any kind of location — restaurants, parks, beaches, fishing spots only the locals know, or even a hidden study spot on campus that you always have to yourself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4314', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%B4%E5%A0%B4', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:54:57.829535Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:10:25.945269Z'),
(4315, 'vocabulary', '鼻の穴', '鼻の穴', 'What''s your <kanji>nose</kanji>''s <kanji>hole</kanji>? How about your <vocabulary>nostril</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4315', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BC%BB%E3%81%AE%E7%A9%B4', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:55:03.521631Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:23:36.291149Z'),
(4316, 'vocabulary', '穴子', '穴子', 'A <kanji>hole</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> isn''t some kind of creepy hole child (not sure how else to describe it). It''s a <vocabulary>conger eel</vocabulary>, a type of tasty eel eaten in Japan. It might be easier to learn the reading first, though, and use that, because the second meaning option is <vocabulary>anago</vocabulary>, the Japanese word for it (also used in English).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4316', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%B4%E5%AD%90', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:55:09.15375Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.003994Z'),
(4317, 'vocabulary', '経つ', '経つ', 'You know how 経 means <kanji>passage of time</kanji>, right? Well, this is the verb form of that. What does time do when it passages? It passes. So, this verb means <vocabulary>to pass</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to elapse</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4317', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%8C%E3%81%A4', 22, 1, '2012-11-01T06:55:19.926258Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.046828Z'),
(4318, 'vocabulary', '経験', '経験', 'Through various <kanji>test</kanji>s over the <kanji>passage of time</kanji>, you have gained much <vocabulary>experience</vocabulary>. Time and testing is all it takes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4318', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%8C%E9%A8%93', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:55:25.515244Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.08342Z'),
(4319, 'vocabulary', '経験者', '経験者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> who has experience (経験) is an <vocabulary>experienced person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4319', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%8C%E9%A8%93%E8%80%85', 22, 1, '2012-11-01T06:55:30.479762Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(4320, 'vocabulary', '罪', '罪', 'If you have <kanji>guilt</kanji> then you have committed a <vocabulary>sin</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>crime</vocabulary>. Come here and confess to Father Koichi.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4320', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BD%AA', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:55:36.050625Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.155143Z'),
(4321, 'vocabulary', '有罪', '有罪', 'To <kanji>have</kanji> <kanji>guilt</kanji> is to be <vocabulary>guilty</vocabulary>. This can also simply mean <vocabulary>guilt</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4321', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%89%E7%BD%AA', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:55:43.333903Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.182126Z'),
(4322, 'vocabulary', '犯罪', '犯罪', 'You have <kanji>crime</kanji> and you have <kanji>guilt</kanji>. Sounds like you did the <vocabulary>crime</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4322', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8A%AF%E7%BD%AA', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:55:50.295643Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.209224Z'),
(4323, 'vocabulary', '臭い', '臭い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>stinking</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>stinking</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>stinky</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4323', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AD%E3%81%84', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:55:55.322415Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T06:26:42.382198Z'),
(4324, 'vocabulary', '被害', '被害', 'When you get <kanji>cover</kanji>ed in <kanji>damage</kanji>, you can''t avoid it — you really suffer <vocabulary>damage</vocabulary>.

被害 refers to damage or <vocabulary>harm</vocabulary> that someone suffers because of some unfortunate occurrence, like a crime, accident, or disaster. As the kanji 被 suggests, it emphasizes being on the receiving end of the harm, not just the damage itself. You''ll mostly see it in serious contexts where victims or losses are being discussed — news articles and official reports, for example.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4324', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A2%AB%E5%AE%B3', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:56:06.327687Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(4325, 'vocabulary', '余裕', '余裕', 'When your <kanji>surplus</kanji> is <kanji>abundant</kanji>, you have <vocabulary>leeway</vocabulary> for a lot of things. You have plenty of <vocabulary>room to spare</vocabulary>.

余裕 is used to describe a state of having ample leeway for something. It means you can handle things comfortably, without feeling stressed or overwhelmed, whether it''s about money, time, or just mentally.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4325', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%99%E8%A3%95', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:56:11.994059Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:25:39.169801Z'),
(4326, 'vocabulary', '裕福', '裕福', 'You have such <kanji>abundant</kanji> <kanji>luck</kanji>! If you have abundant luck, you''re probably also <vocabulary>wealthy</vocabulary>, because lots of <vocabulary>wealth</vocabulary> equals lots of luck, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4326', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%95%E7%A6%8F', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:56:17.470176Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:25:39.169801Z'),
(4327, 'vocabulary', '論文', '論文', 'A <kanji>writing</kanji> of your <kanji>theory</kanji> is usually a <vocabulary>thesis</vocabulary>. Remember how school teachers always wanted you to present theories in writing? And to make sure you have a thesis in that <vocabulary>paper</vocabulary>? I do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4327', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%96%E6%96%87', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:56:25.235046Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.375195Z'),
(4328, 'vocabulary', '理論', '理論', 'You have <kanji>reason</kanji> behind your <kanji>theory</kanji>, making it a real <vocabulary>theory</vocabulary> (unlike those that don''t have reason, I guess?).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4328', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%90%86%E8%AB%96', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:56:32.680593Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.407981Z'),
(4329, 'vocabulary', '論理', '論理', 'Using <kanji>theory</kanji> and <kanji>reason</kanji>, you are one who uses much <vocabulary>logic</vocabulary>. Imagine this type of person, I bet you know at least one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4329', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%96%E7%90%86', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:56:39.487199Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.442179Z'),
(4330, 'vocabulary', '会議', '会議', 'A place where people <kanji>meet</kanji> and do <kanji>deliberation</kanji>. Sounds boring, but also sounds like some kind of <vocabulary>meeting</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>conference</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4330', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9A%E8%AD%B0', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:57:00.86723Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.473576Z'),
(4331, 'vocabulary', '議論', '議論', 'There is much <kanji>deliberation</kanji> and <kanji>theory</kanji> exchanging when you are trying to present an <vocabulary>argument</vocabulary>. Of course, it''s a very nice one. Lots of thinking and stuff, you know? That''s why there''s deliberation and theories instead of just screaming. That kind of "argument."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4331', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%B0%E8%AB%96', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:57:10.91095Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.505696Z'),
(4332, 'vocabulary', '不思議', '不思議', 'This is <kanji>not</kanji> what you <kanji>thought</kanji> would happen during your <kanji>deliberation</kanji>. You thought it would be very normal. Instead, it''s the opposite. It''s full of <vocabulary>wonder</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>mystery</vocabulary>. It''s <vocabulary>marvelous</vocabulary>', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4332', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%80%9D%E8%AD%B0', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:57:23.222218Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.538041Z'),
(4333, 'vocabulary', '防止', '防止', 'To <kanji>prevent</kanji> and <kanji>stop</kanji> someone from doing something is <vocabulary>prevention</vocabulary>. It''s good to have prevention instead of letting something bad happen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4333', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%98%B2%E6%AD%A2', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:57:44.198933Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.569458Z'),
(4334, 'vocabulary', '防ぐ', '防ぐ', 'You''re doing a <kanji>prevent</kanji>ion. When you prevent something, what are you really doing? You''re trying <vocabulary>to defend against</vocabulary> something from happening. That''s why this verb is a little weird and means <vocabulary>to defend</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to protect against</vocabulary>. It can also be <vocabulary>to prevent</vocabulary>, too, but just make sure you''re thinking of it more like you''re defending something with your prevention.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4334', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%98%B2%E3%81%90', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:58:00.142519Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.602767Z'),
(4335, 'vocabulary', '防水', '防水', 'Something that <kanji>prevent</kanji>s <kanji>water</kanji> is <vocabulary>waterproof</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>watertight</vocabulary>. Time to take a bath with my 防水 cell phone...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4335', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%98%B2%E6%B0%B4', 25, 1, '2012-11-01T06:58:08.283073Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.636598Z'),
(4336, 'vocabulary', '防火', '防火', 'Trying to <kanji>prevent</kanji> <kanji>fire</kanji>? Better get into <vocabulary>fire prevention</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4336', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%98%B2%E7%81%AB', 25, 1, '2012-11-01T06:58:15.9976Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.669189Z'),
(4337, 'vocabulary', '予防', '予防', 'To <kanji>prevent</kanji> <kanji>beforehand</kanji> is still <vocabulary>prevention</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4337', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%88%E9%98%B2', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:58:25.421186Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.703188Z'),
(4338, 'vocabulary', '困難', '困難', 'You''re <kanji>distressed</kanji> and it''s <kanji>difficult</kanji>. This is quite the <vocabulary>hardship</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>difficulty</vocabulary> you''re feeling.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4338', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%B0%E9%9B%A3', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:58:30.619243Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.735623Z'),
(4339, 'vocabulary', '非難', '非難', 'A <kanji>difficult</kanji> <kanji>injustice</kanji> to take from someone is a <vocabulary>criticism</vocabulary>. Ugh, what a pain to be criticized! So difficult, and besides, it''s an injustice, because I''m perfect!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4339', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%9E%E9%9B%A3', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:58:36.02627Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.768945Z'),
(4340, 'vocabulary', '難しい', '難しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>difficult</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>difficult</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4340', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%A3%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:58:41.119741Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.801412Z'),
(4341, 'vocabulary', '地震', '地震', 'An <kanji>earth</kanji> <kanji>earthquake</kanji> is just another way to say <vocabulary>earthquake</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4341', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E9%9C%87', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:58:45.653192Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:12.452684Z'),
(4342, 'vocabulary', '震度', '震度', 'The <kanji>earthquake</kanji> <kanji>degree</kanji>s is how you measure an earthquake. The 震度 is the <vocabulary>earthquake intensity</vocabulary>, AKA <vocabulary>earthquake magnitude</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>seismic intensity</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>seismic scale</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4342', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%87%E5%BA%A6', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:58:51.752147Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.847589Z'),
(4343, 'vocabulary', '余震', '余震', 'A <kanji>surplus</kanji> (that''s an extra!) <kanji>earthquake</kanji> is a quake that  comes after the main (non-surplus) quake. That''s an <vocabulary>aftershock</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4343', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%99%E9%9C%87', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:59:05.100299Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.869665Z'),
(4344, 'vocabulary', '人類学', '人類学', 'The <kanji>study</kanji> of <kanji>mankind</kanji> (人類) is known as <vocabulary>anthropology</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4344', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E9%A1%9E%E5%AD%A6', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:59:11.921365Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(4345, 'vocabulary', '飛ぶ', '飛ぶ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>fly</kanji>, and the verb vocab version is also <vocabulary>to fly</vocabulary>. In addition to flying, the verb also means <vocabulary>to jump</vocabulary>. Just think of jumping as being some sort of... smaller version of flying, and this''ll make sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4345', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9B%E3%81%B6', 20, 1, '2012-11-01T06:59:26.188028Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:47.918958Z'),
(4346, 'vocabulary', '果たして', '果たして', 'So this doesn''t really make much sense in terms of the kanji and word, but it is what it is. Let''s remember it this way:

The kanji is <kanji>fruit</kanji> so the meaning is <vocabulary>really</vocabulary>? Like, really really? Because it sounds like you made that up. I really doubt it means really.

It can also mean <vocabulary>as was expected</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>just as I thought</vocabulary>, but usually we use it as a way to say, "Really? I don''t really believe you..."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4346', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%9C%E3%81%9F%E3%81%97%E3%81%A6', 19, 1, '2012-11-01T17:56:28.700755Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:13:58.840623Z'),
(4347, 'vocabulary', '水兵', '水兵', 'A <kanji>water</kanji> <kanji>soldier</kanji> is a soldier that is out to sea. In other words, a <vocabulary>navy sailor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4347', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E5%85%B5', 17, 1, '2012-11-06T04:46:24.95864Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:54:26.999027Z'),
(4348, 'vocabulary', '細かい', '細かい', 'This is an adjective utilizing the second meaning of 細, <kanji>detailed</kanji>. So, this just means <vocabulary>detailed</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>fine</vocabulary>!

You can remember that this one is "detailed" or "fine" because the かい makes you think of <reading>coy</reading>otes. Your hobby is making extremely detailed and fine model railroads out of nothing but raw meat. This, however, means that coyotes regularly come in and destroy all of your fine handiwork.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4348', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%B0%E3%81%8B%E3%81%84', 19, 1, '2012-11-06T05:11:51.230064Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:47:55.647024Z'),
(4349, 'vocabulary', '警察署', '警察署', 'A <kanji>government office</kanji> for police (警察) is a <vocabulary>police station</vocabulary>, because that''s where they hang out, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4349', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%A6%E5%AF%9F%E7%BD%B2', 19, 1, '2012-11-09T01:48:09.346506Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:48.012242Z'),
(4350, 'vocabulary', '説明書', '説明書', 'Remember how 説明 means "explanation"? Take that explanation and <kanji>write</kanji> it down. Now you have an <vocabulary>instruction manual</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>user guide</vocabulary>.

This is the booklet or sheet of paper that comes with appliances, electronics, or flat-pack furniture — the thing that explains how to use the product.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4350', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%AC%E6%98%8E%E6%9B%B8', 18, 1, '2012-11-09T02:06:24.16299Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(4351, 'vocabulary', '汽車', '汽車', 'A <kanji>steam</kanji> <kanji>car</kanji> is a car that runs on steam. No regular car runs on steam, but what thing that has things called "cars" does run on steam? How about a <vocabulary>steam train</vocabulary>? You can also think about 電車s, which are electric cars (but means "train"). This is similar to that, but now it''s run with steam, making it a steam train.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4351', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%BD%E8%BB%8A', 18, 1, '2012-11-09T05:45:28.882749Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:48.063512Z'),
(4352, 'vocabulary', '汽船', '汽船', 'A <kanji>steam</kanji> <kanji>ship</kanji> is a <vocabulary>steam ship</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>steam boat</vocabulary>. So fancy back in their day!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4352', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%BD%E8%88%B9', 18, 1, '2012-11-09T05:46:08.37438Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:48.090702Z'),
(4353, 'vocabulary', '手洗い', '手洗い', 'You remember how 洗う is "to wash"? Well, when you <kanji>wash</kanji> your <kanji>hand</kanji>s you''re doing <vocabulary>hand washing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4353', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E6%B4%97%E3%81%84', 20, 1, '2012-11-09T18:17:42.724382Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(4354, 'vocabulary', '入学試験', '入学試験', 'For <kanji>school admission</kanji> (入学) you''re taking an <kanji>examination</kanji> (試験). What exams do you have to take to get into a school? How about <vocabulary>entrance exam</vocabulary>s? The Japanese ones are pretty hard. Another word for them is "hell tests."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4354', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E5%AD%A6%E8%A9%A6%E9%A8%93', 11, 1, '2012-11-14T00:41:31.630718Z', NULL, '2026-01-05T08:21:15.382996Z'),
(4355, 'vocabulary', '書類', '書類', 'If you put <kanji>writing</kanji> into a <kanji>category</kanji>, you''d have to write it down after you''ve chosen what category it is in. Where would you write that down? Into a <vocabulary>document</vocabulary>, probably.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4355', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B8%E9%A1%9E', 18, 1, '2012-11-14T09:17:08.260973Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:32:59.293769Z'),
(4356, 'vocabulary', '胸焼け', '胸焼け', 'When your <kanji>chest</kanji> is <kanji>burn</kanji>ing, you have what''s known as <vocabulary>heartburn</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4356', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%B8%E7%84%BC%E3%81%91', 20, 1, '2012-11-15T04:29:56.764142Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:02:33.540143Z'),
(4357, 'vocabulary', '不可分', '不可分', 'When it''s <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>possible</kanji> to <kanji>divide</kanji> something, that makes it <vocabulary>indivisible</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>inseparable</vocabulary>.

不可分 is a formal term describing something that is inherently linked and can''t be separated without losing its meaning or function.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4357', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%8F%AF%E5%88%86', 20, 1, '2012-11-15T04:34:57.195156Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:48.217962Z'),
(4358, 'vocabulary', '笛', '笛', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4358', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AC%9B', 19, 1, '2012-11-15T19:01:42.435938Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:20:17.248906Z'),
(4359, 'vocabulary', '汽笛', '汽笛', 'A <kanji>steam</kanji> <kanji>flute</kanji> is a flute that goes off using steam. The only time I can think of this happening is via a <vocabulary>steam whistle</vocabulary>. Choo choooo!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4359', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%BD%E7%AC%9B', 19, 1, '2012-11-15T19:02:05.856506Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:20:17.248906Z'),
(4360, 'vocabulary', '口笛', '口笛', 'A <kanji>mouth</kanji> <kanji>flute</kanji>... Normal flutes you blow air through to make a sound. How do you blow air through your mouth to make a sound? By <vocabulary>whistling</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4360', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%A3%E7%AC%9B', 21, 1, '2012-11-15T19:02:38.849875Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:20:17.248906Z'),
(4361, 'vocabulary', '火曜日', '火曜日', 'This is a day of the week. You know this because it has 曜日 in it. But what day of the week is it? The <kanji>fire</kanji> day of the week is the day after the worst day of the week (Monday). You set fire to everything because you''re so pissed off about yesterday, which makes today <vocabulary>Tuesday</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4361', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%AB%E6%9B%9C%E6%97%A5', 18, 1, '2012-11-15T19:42:00.012612Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:14:10.151078Z'),
(4362, 'vocabulary', '金曜日', '金曜日', 'This is a day of the week. You know this because it has 曜日 in it. But what day of the week is it? The <kanji>gold</kanji> day of the week is the greatest day. It''s the day that makes you feel like gold because it''s the end of the weekdays and the beginning of the weekend! This day is <vocabulary>Friday</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4362', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%91%E6%9B%9C%E6%97%A5', 18, 1, '2012-11-15T19:42:15.738898Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:10:11.021307Z'),
(4363, 'vocabulary', '可能性', '可能性', 'The <kanji>possible</kanji> (可能) range of <kanji>gender</kanji> extends beyond two options. It means there''s a <vocabulary>possibility</vocabulary> to identify with many different gender identities.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4363', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%AF%E8%83%BD%E6%80%A7', 20, 1, '2012-11-15T20:00:18.801944Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T21:59:59.512416Z'),
(4364, 'vocabulary', '音訓', '音訓', 'You''ve seen (hopefully) 音読み and 訓読み. This is a combination of the two. This is <vocabulary>on''yomi and kun''yomi</vocabulary>, aka the <vocabulary>Chinese and Japanese readings</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4364', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9F%B3%E8%A8%93', 20, 1, '2012-11-15T21:28:51.987548Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:20:13.293925Z'),
(4365, 'vocabulary', '人達', '人達', 'The kanji 達 pluralizes things sometimes. So, when you have a <kanji>plural</kanji> amount of a <kanji>person</kanji> you have more than one person, you have <vocabulary>people</vocabulary>.

Take note! When using this word, you want to attach it to the type of "people" you are talking about. For example, あの人達 (those people over there). Other examples: 男の人達, その人達, 向こうの人達, etc. You don''t use this by itself, instead you use it with something describing what kind of people you''re talking about.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4365', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E9%81%94', 20, 1, '2012-11-15T21:33:33.147016Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:06:12.518486Z'),
(4366, 'vocabulary', '静々', '静々', '<kanji>Quiet</kanji> <kanji>quiet</kanji>! Do it <vocabulary>quietly</vocabulary>, please.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4366', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%99%E3%80%85', 20, 1, '2012-11-20T18:57:16.878296Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T06:27:10.797738Z'),
(4367, 'vocabulary', '加える', '加える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. 

The kanji itself means <kanji>add</kanji> and this word is the verb version of that: <vocabulary>to add</vocabulary>.

This is a transitive verb, meaning that you are doing the adding to something else. You add a nose to your snowman, for example. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4367', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%A0%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2012-11-20T19:27:53.729948Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:15:08.580781Z'),
(4368, 'vocabulary', '参加', '参加', 'When you <kanji>participate</kanji>, <kanji>add</kanji>ing yourself to the activity, that is <vocabulary>participation</vocabulary>. Well done, you earn a gold star for participating!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4368', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%82%E5%8A%A0', 19, 1, '2012-11-20T19:28:43.468823Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:15:08.580781Z'),
(4369, 'vocabulary', '文句する-4369', '文句する', 'You know how 文句 is a <vocabulary>complaint</vocabulary>, right? Well, add する to make it a verb and you have <vocabulary>to complain</vocabulary>. This is a really casual way to complain though, usually used in manga or on social media. Keep your eye out for it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4369', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%87%E5%8F%A5%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B-4369', 20, 1, '2012-11-20T19:38:12.41676Z', '2018-04-18T21:26:18.505199Z', '2025-06-11T05:32:48.512938Z'),
(4370, 'vocabulary', '焼ける', '焼ける', 'Remember how 焼く is "to bake," "to burn something," or "to grill"? 焼ける is just the intransitive version. That means no direct object needed! So this verb means <vocabulary>to get baked</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to get burned</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to get grilled</vocabulary>. 

焼ける is used when something is heated and transformed by the heat. Depending on the context, it can refer to anything from a muffin getting baked, fish being grilled, or pottery being fired. You can even use it when your skin gets a tan or a house burns down. Well, that last one is kinda sad. I hope it never happens.

You can distinguish 焼ける from 焼く because when cooking, you always <reading>care</reading> (ける) to ensure the food gets baked or grilled really well. You want to make sure it''s properly 焼ける''d (heated through) to be safe!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4370', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%BC%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 20, 1, '2012-11-20T19:40:35.204416Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:48.539339Z'),
(4371, 'vocabulary', '電子機器', '電子機器', 'You have an <kanji>electronic</kanji> (電子) <kanji>container</kanji> <kanji>machine</kanji>. This is just a machine that contains electricity and electric parts. So basically, an <vocabulary>electronic device</vocabulary>, or more generally, <vocabulary>electronic equipment</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>electronics</vocabulary>.

電子機器 refers to complex electronic devices like computers, smartphones, medical devices, control systems, and more. So while it can be used to refer to a wide range of electronic devices, it''s usually not used for things like simple household appliances like toasters or kettles.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4371', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E5%AD%90%E6%A9%9F%E5%99%A8', 20, 1, '2012-11-27T06:38:56.530635Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:04:30.700843Z'),
(4372, 'vocabulary', '連絡する', '連絡する', 'You <kanji>take along</kanji> a message and <kanji>entangle</kanji> it into the telephone. From there it buzzes off to the person in order for you <vocabulary>to contact</vocabulary> them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4372', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%A3%E7%B5%A1%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2012-11-30T19:18:08.061119Z', NULL, '2025-07-22T20:19:03.682653Z'),
(4373, 'vocabulary', '意外', '意外', 'Something <kanji>outside</kanji> your normal <kanji>idea</kanji> of how things work will come as <vocabulary>unexpected</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>surprising</vocabulary> to you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4373', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%8F%E5%A4%96', 11, 1, '2012-12-04T04:47:14.780437Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(4374, 'vocabulary', '関心', '関心', 'The things <kanji>related</kanji> to your <kanji>heart</kanji> are the things that <vocabulary>concern</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>interest</vocabulary> you the most.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4374', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%A2%E5%BF%83', 16, 1, '2012-12-04T04:50:37.318777Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.544767Z'),
(4375, 'vocabulary', '開始', '開始', 'The <kanji>open</kanji> of the <kanji>start</kanji> is the <vocabulary>start</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>beginning</vocabulary>.

Note that 開始 is slightly more formal than 始める.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4375', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%8B%E5%A7%8B', 10, 1, '2012-12-04T04:55:48.588922Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.211263Z'),
(4376, 'vocabulary', '発表', '発表', 'I''m going to <kanji>express</kanji> my <kanji>departure</kanji>. That is my only <vocabulary>announcement</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4376', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BA%E8%A1%A8', 9, 1, '2012-12-04T05:05:25.517333Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:26:46.193094Z'),
(4377, 'vocabulary', '表す', '表す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>express</kanji> and the verb vocab version is transitive, meaning that it''s done to something. So this verb means <vocabulary>to express something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to show something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4377', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%A8%E3%81%99', 9, 1, '2012-12-04T05:08:13.684384Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:26:46.193094Z'),
(4378, 'vocabulary', '広がる', '広がる', 'Remember 広い? Well this is like that, except a verb. When something does the action of getting <kanji>wide</kanji>, it means <vocabulary>to spread out</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to extend</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to stretch</vocabulary>.

You already learned 広げる, which is used when you spread SOMETHING. But 広がる is intransitive, so it doesn''t take a direct object. Like: The sea spreads out before you. See? No direct object.

Actually, verbs ending in okurigana that rhyme with ある tend to be intransitive like this. You can remember this because if something is ある, it''s just there, existing, not acting <em>on</em> anything else. That''s why verbs that sound like it are often intransitive too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4378', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%83%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 6, 1, '2012-12-04T05:12:29.5498Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:23.997816Z'),
(4379, 'vocabulary', '曲線', '曲線', 'A <kanji>bend</kanji> in a <kanji>line</kanji> is merely a <vocabulary>curve</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4379', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B2%E7%B7%9A', 10, 1, '2012-12-04T05:14:25.004745Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.222285Z'),
(4380, 'vocabulary', '出発', '出発', 'You <kanji>exit</kanji> and then make a <kanji>departure</kanji>. This is just a <vocabulary>departure</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4380', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E7%99%BA', 9, 1, '2012-12-04T05:36:30.572084Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:22:52.12866Z'),
(4381, 'vocabulary', '参加する', '参加する', 'You know how 参加 is <kanji>participation</kanji>? Well, make that a verb with する and you have yourself <vocabulary>to participate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4381', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%82%E5%8A%A0%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:32:19.841084Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:15:08.580781Z'),
(4382, 'vocabulary', '参加者', '参加者', 'You know that 参加 is <kanji>participation</kanji>, so <kanji>someone</kanji> who does participation is a <vocabulary>participant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4382', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%82%E5%8A%A0%E8%80%85', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:32:27.8127Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(4383, 'vocabulary', '警察官', '警察官', 'The <kanji>police</kanji> (警察) <kanji>government</kanji> is full of <vocabulary>police officer</vocabulary>s. This is the formal way to say "police officer." It is similar to 警官, but has a slightly more official feel to it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4383', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%A6%E5%AF%9F%E5%AE%98', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:32:32.967002Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:48.84375Z'),
(4384, 'vocabulary', '乱す', '乱す', 'You know how 乱れる is "to be in disorder"? Well, now you''re the one causing this. So, by doing this you are going <vocabulary>to put in disorder</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to throw out of order</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to disturb</vocabulary> whatever it is. Just imagine all the people who will try to <reading>sue</reading> (す) you for your actions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4384', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%B1%E3%81%99', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:32:38.950325Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:48.863804Z'),
(4385, 'vocabulary', '宇宙人', '宇宙人', 'You know how 宇宙 is <kanji>space</kanji>? Well, a <kanji>person</kanji> from space is an <vocabulary>alien</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4385', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%87%E5%AE%99%E4%BA%BA', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:32:44.353529Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(4386, 'vocabulary', '宇宙船', '宇宙船', 'You know how 宇宙 is <kanji>outer space</kanji> and how 船 is <kanji>ship</kanji>, right? Well, put those together and you have an outer space ship (better known as a <vocabulary>space ship</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4386', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%87%E5%AE%99%E8%88%B9', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:33:14.052384Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:48.906341Z'),
(4387, 'vocabulary', '履き物', '履き物', 'Remember how 履く is "to put on shoes" and 物 means "thing"? Well, combine them and you have a word for things you put on your feet, a.k.a. <vocabulary>footwear</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>shoes</vocabulary>.

You might know that 履く is used for lots of different clothes you wear below the waist, but be careful: <vocabulary>履き物</vocabulary> only refers to footwear and shoes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4387', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%A5%E3%81%8D%E7%89%A9', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:33:41.907386Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(4388, 'vocabulary', '説得する', '説得する', 'You know that 説得 is <kanji>persuasion</kanji>, so what is the verb version of that? <vocabulary>To persuade</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4388', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%AC%E5%BE%97%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:33:53.335586Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:48.948618Z'),
(4389, 'vocabulary', '混む', '混む', 'This one is a little strange. You have 混じる (to get mixed up in), 混ぜる (to mix), 混ざる (to get mixed), and now this: 混む, <vocabulary>to be crowded</vocabulary>. It makes sense that when everyone''s all mixed up it''s going to be crowded, but how to differentiate between this and the other 混 words? Just think of how cows <reading>muuuuu</reading> (む) get all bunched up and crowded, which is why this word is <vocabulary>to be crowded</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4389', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%B7%E3%82%80', 22, 1, '2012-12-04T06:34:09.885034Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:15:00.980995Z'),
(4390, 'vocabulary', '混じる', '混じる', 'The kanji means <kanji>mix</kanji>, and you already learned its transitive and intransitive pair: 混ぜる (to mix) and 混ざる (to get mixed). 混じる is another intransitive verb, but it means <vocabulary>to be mixed in with</vocabulary>.

So how are 混ざる and 混じる different? 混ざる is used to talk about things that become mixed together as part of the same whole — like blended colors or intermingled groups. 混じる, on the other hand, is less about blending smoothly and more about something getting mixed in when it doesn''t quite belong — static in your audio, scattered strands of gray in your dark hair, or even some random stranger sitting at your group''s table.

You can remember this by thinking about how this out-of-place thing getting mixed in makes you want to <reading>jeer</reading> (じる): "Boo, get out of here! You don''t fit in!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4390', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%B7%E3%81%98%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-04T06:34:14.711721Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:48:11.836688Z'),
(4391, 'vocabulary', '混乱する', '混乱する', 'If 混乱 is <kanji>confusion</kanji> then making it into a verb would cause it <vocabulary>to be confused</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4391', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%B7%E4%B9%B1%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:34:25.354984Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:15:00.980995Z'),
(4392, 'vocabulary', '減らす', '減らす', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>decrease</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to decrease something</vocabulary>. You can differentiate this from 減る because with this one, you are the one that is going to be doing the decreasing. The <reading>rush</reading> (らす) of decreasing something fills your veins.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4392', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%9B%E3%82%89%E3%81%99', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:34:32.324633Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:02.806385Z'),
(4393, 'vocabulary', '続ける', '続ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>continue</kanji>, so 続ける means <vocabulary>to continue something</vocabulary>. It''s a transitive verb, so it takes a direct object. You can 続ける your work, for example, and you should 続ける your WaniKani lessons at a pace that works for you!

Although it has the same 続 kanji, this word is a bit different from 続く, which is used for when something just continues on its own (like rain, or trends). It can be hard to remember the difference, so here''s a mnemonic to help you with 続ける:

You only <reading>care</reading> (ける) about things that you can continue through your own actions. For those other things that just continue on their own, you''re like, That''s <reading>coo</reading>'' (く), but you’re not that interested. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4393', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%9A%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:34:45.117331Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:18:52.065407Z'),
(4394, 'vocabulary', '固有名詞', '固有名詞', 'A <kanji>noun</kanji> (名詞) that <kanji>has</kanji> <kanji>hard</kanji>ness is a noun that can''t be budged. It''s a big noun, so big that you have to capitalize these kinds of nouns in English. It is a <vocabulary>proper noun</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4394', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BA%E6%9C%89%E5%90%8D%E8%A9%9E', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:35:02.265676Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(4395, 'vocabulary', '保守主義', '保守主義', 'You know that 主義 is "-ism." Add that ism to people who want to <kanji>protect</kanji> and <kanji>conserve</kanji> their country so it stays just the way it is (forever). That''s <vocabulary>conservatism</vocabulary> and they''re <vocabulary>conservative</vocabulary> people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4395', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%9D%E5%AE%88%E4%B8%BB%E7%BE%A9', 23, 1, '2012-12-04T06:35:09.029476Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:46:56.671656Z'),
(4396, 'vocabulary', '他動詞', '他動詞', 'Remember how 動詞 means "verb"? When the verb is done to some <kanji>other</kanji> thing, it''s a <vocabulary>transitive verb</vocabulary>.

That means it takes a direct object. Verbs like 食べる, 使う, and 上げる are transitive verbs because they need a direct object, an "other" thing, to receive their action.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4396', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%96%E5%8B%95%E8%A9%9E', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:35:53.841881Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T04:47:22.214427Z'),
(4397, 'vocabulary', '暴力団', '暴力団', 'A <kanji>group</kanji> that has <kanji>power</kanji> using <kanji>violence</kanji>? Sounds like a <vocabulary>crime syndicate</vocabulary> to me, probably the <vocabulary>Yakuza</vocabulary> or some other <vocabulary>gangster organization</vocabulary> .', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4397', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%B4%E5%8A%9B%E5%9B%A3', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:36:18.840838Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(4398, 'vocabulary', '容疑者', '容疑者', 'I have a <kanji>suspicion</kanji> (容疑) about <kanji>someone</kanji>. That someone is my main <vocabulary>suspect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4398', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B9%E7%96%91%E8%80%85', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:36:24.396343Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(4399, 'vocabulary', '暴走族', '暴走族', 'A <kanji>tribe</kanji> that <kanji>runs wild</kanji> (暴走) is what the Japanese call a <vocabulary>biker gang</vocabulary>. Just imagine them riding around on their bikes, running wild. They''re also close, like a family. How nice!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4399', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%B4%E8%B5%B0%E6%97%8F', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:36:30.604687Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.192176Z'),
(4400, 'vocabulary', '経済', '経済', 'When a <kanji>passage of time</kanji> <kanji>comes to an end</kanji>, you look back on it and see how well you did. Did we make more money? Did we make less? That''s all <vocabulary>economics</vocabulary>, baby (that''s money, baby!).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4400', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%8C%E6%B8%88', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:36:35.626675Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.21348Z'),
(4401, 'vocabulary', 'いい加減にしろ', 'いい加減にしろ', 'You know how いい加減 is unfounded or pointless? Because if you <kanji>add</kanji> and then <kanji>decrease</kanji> you haven''t really done anything? Well, this is like saying "do the pointless!" Okay, that doesn''t make any sense. This is a phrase that means something similar to <vocabulary>cut it out</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>cut the BS</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4401', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%84%E3%81%84%E5%8A%A0%E6%B8%9B%E3%81%AB%E3%81%97%E3%82%8D', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:36:47.250611Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:02.806385Z'),
(4402, 'vocabulary', '手続き', '手続き', 'Someone takes your <kanji>hand</kanji> and shows you how to <kanji>continue</kanji>. "This is the <vocabulary>procedure</vocabulary>," they say. Move your hand like this. Verrrryyy goood. Good procedure. Good!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4402', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E7%B6%9A%E3%81%8D', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:36:53.235878Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:18:52.065407Z'),
(4403, 'vocabulary', '敵', '敵', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

敵 means <vocabulary>enemy</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>opponent</vocabulary>, whether in war, sports, competitions, or other conflict situations. Just keep in mind that 敵 can sound a bit strong!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4403', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B5', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:36:59.16774Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.285913Z'),
(4404, 'vocabulary', '素敵', '素敵', 'The <kanji>element</kanji> of your <kanji>enemy</kanji> that bugs you the most is how <vocabulary>lovely</vocabulary> they are. You wish you were as <vocabulary>charming</vocabulary> as them… and maybe that''s why you insist on being their enemy. You just can''t deal with how <vocabulary>wonderful</vocabulary> they are.

素敵 is the kind of word you use when something makes your heart flutter — when it feels delightful, admirable, or even inspiring. For example, 素敵な人 can mean someone who''s attractive, but it can also refer to someone you admire, respect, or feel drawn to because of their attitude, values, character, or vibe.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4404', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%A0%E6%95%B5', 22, 1, '2012-12-04T06:37:04.95922Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:38:20.342554Z'),
(4405, 'vocabulary', '大敵', '大敵', 'Your <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>enemy</kanji> is your worst enemy. Your <vocabulary>arch enemy</vocabulary>.

大敵 is for a powerful or dangerous opponent, whether it''s a <vocabulary>formidable adversary</vocabulary> in battle or competition, or something abstract, like calling carelessness your <vocabulary>great enemy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4405', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E6%95%B5', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:37:10.481944Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(4406, 'vocabulary', '無敵', '無敵', 'You have <kanji>nothing</kanji> when it comes to <kanji>enemies</kanji>. Why? It''s not because you''re super kind and everyone likes you. No, it''s that you''ve defeated anyone who''s tried. You are <vocabulary>unrivaled</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>invincible</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4406', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E6%95%B5', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:37:17.368136Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.35264Z'),
(4407, 'vocabulary', '済む', '済む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>come to an end</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to come to an end</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4407', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%88%E3%82%80', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:37:34.930454Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.373181Z'),
(4408, 'vocabulary', '委員', '委員', 'A <kanji>committee</kanji> <kanji>member</kanji> is a <vocabulary>member of a committee</vocabulary> (or a <vocabulary>committee member</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4408', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%94%E5%93%A1', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:37:40.027134Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:34:02.31068Z'),
(4409, 'vocabulary', '挙がる', '挙がる', 'When you <kanji>raise</kanji> something you are causing it <vocabulary>to be raised</vocabulary>. This verb can also mean things like <vocabulary>to be listed</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be nominated</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to rise</vocabulary>. For these additional meanings, you might want to imagine something rising up onto a list or someone rising up to a nomination, because lists and nominations are always high up.

', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4409', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%99%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:37:50.986097Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.416253Z'),
(4410, 'vocabulary', '選挙', '選挙', 'You have to <kanji>choose</kanji> someone and <kanji>raise</kanji> them up to office. This is an <vocabulary>election</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4410', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%B8%E6%8C%99', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:37:55.82098Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.43985Z'),
(4411, 'vocabulary', '判子', '判子', 'The <kanji>child</kanji> of a <kanji>judge</kanji> is the thing he puts on his judgements. What does he put on his judgments? His <vocabulary>seal</vocabulary>, you know, to make it all official and stuff.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4411', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A4%E5%AD%90', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:38:01.06733Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T21:56:23.352206Z'),
(4412, 'vocabulary', '評判', '評判', 'When people <kanji>evaluate</kanji> and <kanji>judge</kanji> you at every turn, it means you have quite a <vocabulary>reputation</vocabulary>, and indeed <vocabulary>fame</vocabulary>. Wouldn''t this be terrible if people evaluated and judged you 24/7? Having that kind of reputation or fame would be difficult.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4412', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%95%E5%88%A4', 23, 1, '2012-12-04T06:38:06.031032Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:51:18.620347Z'),
(4413, 'vocabulary', '制服', '制服', 'If you <kanji>control</kanji> people, you make them wear the same <kanji>clothes</kanji>. That''s a <vocabulary>uniform</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4413', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%B6%E6%9C%8D', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:38:11.83421Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:36:25.472861Z'),
(4414, 'vocabulary', '制度', '制度', 'You <kanji>control</kanji> every <kanji>degree</kanji> of this <vocabulary>system</vocabulary>. Think about a system. It''s designed to create more control for you, degree by degree by degree. That''s why they call it "The system," after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4414', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%B6%E5%BA%A6', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:38:17.847982Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:36:25.472861Z'),
(4415, 'vocabulary', '強制', '強制', 'They have <kanji>strong</kanji> <kanji>control</kanji> over us. This means they never stop <vocabulary>forcing</vocabulary> us into <vocabulary>compulsion</vocabulary>s or making all sorts of things <vocabulary>compulsory</vocabulary>.

強制 means making someone do something against their will, often through pressure, threats, or authority. It''s used when someone is forced to sign a document under coercion, for example, or when someone is made to return to their country by force. 強制 means there''s no choice in the matter.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4415', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%B7%E5%88%B6', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T06:38:23.508065Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:36:25.472861Z'),
(4416, 'vocabulary', '自制', '自制', '<kanji>Self</kanji> <kanji>control</kanji> is <vocabulary>self control</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>self restraint</vocabulary>. Have some!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4416', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E5%88%B6', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:18:27.677945Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:36:25.472861Z'),
(4417, 'vocabulary', '務める', '務める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>task</kanji> so what''s the verb vocab version? Well, when you do a task, you are going <vocabulary>to work as</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to serve as</vocabulary> something to do that task.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4417', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%99%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:19:35.543354Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.596857Z'),
(4418, 'vocabulary', '事務所', '事務所', 'When the <kanji>occurrence</kanji> of your <kanji>task</kanji>s is in the same <kanji>place</kanji> every day, that place is probably an <vocabulary>office</vocabulary>.

事務所 is often for office spaces that handle back-office 事務 (administrative or clerical work). It can also be used for offices that offer more specialized services, like a 会計事務所 (accounting <vocabulary>firm</vocabulary>) or 芸能事務所 (talent <vocabulary>agency</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4418', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8B%E5%8B%99%E6%89%80', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:22:13.956298Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:18:00.076038Z'),
(4419, 'vocabulary', '義務', '義務', 'My <kanji>righteousness</kanji> <kanji>task</kanji> is my <vocabulary>obligation</vocabulary> to these people. I will go out and purge the world of evil, be righteous, and make things happen. It''s my <vocabulary>duty</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4419', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%A9%E5%8B%99', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:22:24.101313Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:46:56.671656Z'),
(4420, 'vocabulary', '任務', '任務', 'You have a <kanji>duty</kanji>. You have a <kanji>task</kanji>. You have both a <vocabulary>duty</vocabulary> and a <vocabulary>task</vocabulary> to do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4420', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%BB%E5%8B%99', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:22:29.448168Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.660801Z'),
(4421, 'vocabulary', '審査', '審査', 'I will <kanji>judge</kanji> and <kanji>inspect</kanji> you. This is an <vocabulary>examination</vocabulary>, so we''ll see if you pass.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4421', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%A9%E6%9F%BB', 24, 1, '2012-12-04T07:22:59.596776Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T19:00:40.037806Z'),
(4422, 'vocabulary', '総合', '総合', 'When you take the <kanji>whole</kanji> and <kanji>join</kanji> it together, you get a <vocabulary>synthesis</vocabulary>, and probably a good <vocabulary>overall</vocabulary> picture of things. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4422', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%8F%E5%90%88', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:23:20.510832Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.7082Z'),
(4423, 'vocabulary', '総理', '総理', 'This is an abbreviation of 総理大臣 (prime minister), so this means <vocabulary>prime minister</vocabulary>.

As you learned with 総理大臣, this word is used only for the <vocabulary>Japanese prime minister</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4423', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%8F%E7%90%86', 30, 1, '2012-12-04T07:23:28.752318Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.785795Z'),
(4424, 'vocabulary', '総体的', '総体的', 'With the <kanji>whole</kanji> <kanji>body</kanji>. Do it <vocabulary>as a whole</vocabulary> instead of as a part, or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4424', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%8F%E4%BD%93%E7%9A%84', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:23:37.895576Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.756624Z'),
(4425, 'vocabulary', '設ける', '設ける', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>establish</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to establish</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to set up</vocabulary>.

設ける is a formal word that suggests setting up something in order to prep for future needs or events. If you want to ensure smooth operation or governance, for example, you might set up rules (ルールを設ける) or establish an office (オフィスを設ける). You can even use it to say you’ll arrange a drinking party (酒の席を設ける), in which case it probably means the party has a more formal purpose like networking or discussion.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4425', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%AD%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:23:50.353723Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.779173Z'),
(4426, 'vocabulary', '設定する', '設定する', 'What do you <kanji>establish</kanji> and <kanji>determine</kanji> in your every day life? A setting. When you make that into a verb, you have the word <vocabulary>to set</vocabulary>, as in setting the temperature of the AC, setting your password, or setting your computer to dark mode.

設定する is a little formal, but does get commonly used for device, system, and game settings. It''s also often used in more official contexts, like for setting prices for products or establishing project goals.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4426', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%AD%E5%AE%9A%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:26:17.090775Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(4427, 'vocabulary', '公設', '公設', 'It''s <kanji>public</kanji> <kanji>establish</kanji>ment. It is <vocabulary>public</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4427', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AC%E8%A8%AD', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:26:28.804415Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.826047Z'),
(4428, 'vocabulary', '建設', '建設', 'You do the <kanji>build</kanji>ing to <kanji>establish</kanji> something. This whole process is called <vocabulary>construction</vocabulary>!

建設 is a pretty formal word for construction, focusing on the action or process of <vocabulary>constructing</vocabulary> rather than the structure itself. It’s often used for large construction projects, like malls, dams, or bridges. In some contexts, it can also refer to the <vocabulary>establishment</vocabulary> of significant entities, like a new state.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4428', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%BA%E8%A8%AD', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:26:34.133196Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.84675Z'),
(4429, 'vocabulary', '資本', '資本', 'To start a business, your <kanji>main</kanji> <kanji>resource</kanji> is your <vocabulary>capital</vocabulary>, which can be money and various other types of assets, like real estate properties.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4429', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%87%E6%9C%AC', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:26:39.44988Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.890861Z'),
(4430, 'vocabulary', '資金', '資金', 'The <kanji>resources</kanji> of <kanji>gold</kanji> that we have are our current <vocabulary>funds</vocabulary>. 

資金 is specifically for monetary funds, not other types of assets. That 金 kanji can mean money, so it should be easy to remember.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4430', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%87%E9%87%91', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:26:45.680814Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.914108Z'),
(4431, 'vocabulary', '資料', '資料', 'If you use <kanji>resources</kanji> to make <kanji>material</kanji>, you''ll have <vocabulary>material</vocabulary>. This is in the sense of material for a book, or <vocabulary>data</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>documents</vocabulary> for research. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4431', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%87%E6%96%99', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:26:52.829135Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.937315Z'),
(4432, 'vocabulary', '投資', '投資', 'Go ahead and <kanji>throw</kanji> your <kanji>resources</kanji> at the project. It could use the <vocabulary>investment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4432', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%95%E8%B3%87', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:26:59.544453Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:49.962904Z'),
(4433, 'vocabulary', '人権', '人権', '<kanji>People</kanji> <kanji>Rights</kanji> are <vocabulary>human rights</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4433', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E6%A8%A9', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:27:11.139026Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T22:02:48.269631Z'),
(4434, 'vocabulary', '権利', '権利', 'You have <kanji>rights</kanji> to <kanji>profit</kanji>! This is <vocabulary>a right</vocabulary> you have!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4434', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A8%A9%E5%88%A9', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:27:18.398724Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T22:02:48.269631Z'),
(4435, 'vocabulary', '女権', '女権', 'A <kanji>woman</kanji>''s <kanji>rights</kanji> consist of <vocabulary>women''s rights</vocabulary>.

This is a formal word that is often used at the beginning of compounds, such as 女権拡張運動 (movement for the expansion of women''s rights). In everyday conversations, you can just say 女性の権利 to talk about women’s rights.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4435', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%B3%E6%A8%A9', 47, 1, '2012-12-04T07:27:23.902282Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T22:02:48.269631Z'),
(4436, 'vocabulary', '条件', '条件', 'A <kanji>clause</kanji> in the <kanji>matter</kanji> complicates things. It adds <vocabulary>conditions</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>terms</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>requirements</vocabulary> to an otherwise simple matter.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4436', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%A1%E4%BB%B6', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:27:30.093996Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:03:13.56267Z'),
(4437, 'vocabulary', '事件', '事件', 'An <kanji>occurrence</kanji> of some <kanji>matter</kanji> is really just something that happens. It''s an <vocabulary>incident</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>case</vocabulary>. 

事件 often refers to significant or serious occurrences like crime cases or accidents. It can also be used playfully — "There was a 事件 at the party!" — to dramatize an event like someone asking out your classmate.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4437', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8B%E4%BB%B6', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:27:35.598182Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:47:34.523008Z'),
(4438, 'vocabulary', '派手', '派手', 'This is a weird one. Just think about the <kanji>sect</kanji> of <kanji>hand</kanji>s. They are always doing jazz hands and things like that, trying to be all <vocabulary>showy</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>flashy</vocabulary>. Ugh, what a dumb sect, you think.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4438', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%BE%E6%89%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:27:44.930388Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:15:23.483599Z'),
(4439, 'vocabulary', '立派', '立派', 'People are <kanji>stand</kanji>ing and forming a <kanji>sect</kanji> for you because of the <vocabulary>splendid</vocabulary> things you say and do. Your <vocabulary>admirable</vocabulary> conduct has brought them to their feet, and now they''re worshipping you like some new age guru.

立派 is a slightly formal word used to say something or someone is <vocabulary>impressive</vocabulary>. For example, you might call someone who achieved something great a 立派な人, or describe an elegant, expensive-looking building as a 立派な建物. Even a fine, majestic mustache could be called a 立派なヒゲ!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4439', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%8B%E6%B4%BE', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:38:38.876664Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:15:23.483599Z'),
(4440, 'vocabulary', '岡山県', '岡山県', 'Okayama (岡山) <kanji>Prefecture</kanji> is a prefecture in Japan. Learning the reading will help you to remember the meaning of this word since half of it is in Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4440', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B2%A1%E5%B1%B1%E7%9C%8C', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:38:44.625054Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:25:07.757919Z'),
(4441, 'vocabulary', '素晴らしい', '素晴らしい', 'An <kanji>element</kanji> of <kanji>clear up</kanji> (like the sky clearing up!) is so <vocabulary>wonderful</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>splendid</vocabulary>. Sort of a weird word, but very useful. Just think of that wonderful feeling you get when the sun breaks and things clear up and it''s awesome.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4441', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%A0%E6%99%B4%E3%82%89%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 23, 1, '2012-12-04T07:38:50.187289Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:00:51.825704Z'),
(4442, 'vocabulary', '素材', '素材', 'When you want to use the most basic <kanji>element</kanji>s of a <kanji>material</kanji>, you end up with <vocabulary>raw material</vocabulary>s. Maybe it''s the raw materials you''re going to use to build a house. Maybe it''s the raw materials you need to make a good soup. Either way, they haven''t been touched much (yet).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4442', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%A0%E6%9D%90', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:38:57.122848Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:38:20.342554Z'),
(4443, 'vocabulary', '断る', '断る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>cut off</kanji>. What happens when you do the action of cutting someone off? You''re going <vocabulary>to refuse</vocabulary> them whatever it is they want.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4443', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%AD%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:39:05.304155Z', NULL, '2026-02-07T02:28:43.938844Z'),
(4444, 'vocabulary', '判断', '判断', 'I <kanji>judge</kanji> to <kanji>cut off</kanji> your head. That is my <vocabulary>judgment</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4444', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A4%E6%96%AD', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:39:18.890131Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T21:56:23.352206Z'),
(4445, 'vocabulary', '評価', '評価', 'Let''s <kanji>evaluate</kanji> the <kanji>value</kanji>. What is this also known as? An <vocabulary>evaluation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4445', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%95%E4%BE%A1', 22, 1, '2012-12-04T07:39:45.352124Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:51:00.759578Z'),
(4446, 'vocabulary', '評論', '評論', '<kanji>Evaluate</kanji> my <kanji>theory</kanji>. Then, when you evaluate, please give me <vocabulary>critique</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>criticism</vocabulary> to help me get better?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4446', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%95%E8%AB%96', 22, 1, '2012-12-04T07:39:51.603739Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:50.317816Z'),
(4447, 'vocabulary', '書評', '書評', 'A <kanji>writing</kanji> that has been <kanji>evaluate</kanji>d has gotten a <vocabulary>book review</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4447', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B8%E8%A9%95', 22, 1, '2012-12-04T07:40:10.627214Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:50.342593Z'),
(4448, 'vocabulary', '批判', '批判', 'A <kanji>criticism</kanji> is when you <kanji>judge</kanji>. It is still just a <vocabulary>criticism</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4448', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%B9%E5%88%A4', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:44:16.9571Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T21:56:23.352206Z'),
(4449, 'vocabulary', '任意', '任意', 'My <kanji>idea</kanji> of <kanji>duty</kanji> is that you don''t really have to do it. It''s <vocabulary>optional</vocabulary>, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4449', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%BB%E6%84%8F', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:45:00.129505Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:50.389248Z'),
(4450, 'vocabulary', '検査', '検査', 'We''re going to need to <kanji>examine</kanji> and <kanji>inspect</kanji> you for our annual <vocabulary>checkup</vocabulary>, mmk? Now drop your pants and cough, please.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4450', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A4%9C%E6%9F%BB', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:45:06.892392Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T19:00:40.037806Z'),
(4451, 'vocabulary', '検問する', '検問する', 'You <kanji>examine</kanji> the <kanji>problem</kanji>. In order to do this, you need <vocabulary>to inspect</vocabulary> it or <vocabulary>to examine</vocabulary> it. And if you''re inspecting people like this, you''re probably the police.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4451', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A4%9C%E5%95%8F%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:45:17.438953Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:50.438282Z'),
(4452, 'vocabulary', '審判', '審判', 'This word has <kanji>judge</kanji> and <kanji>judge</kanji> in it, so it has something to do with <vocabulary>judgment</vocabulary>. It''s usually used in sports, so it''s the <vocabulary>referee</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>umpire</vocabulary>, depending on your sport of choice.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4452', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%A9%E5%88%A4', 24, 1, '2012-12-04T07:45:22.822392Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T21:56:23.352206Z'),
(4453, 'vocabulary', '条約', '条約', 'There are many <kanji>clause</kanji>s about the <kanji>promise</kanji>s you make in this contract considering it''s supposed to be a <vocabulary>treaty</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4453', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%A1%E7%B4%84', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:45:43.714759Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:03:13.56267Z'),
(4454, 'vocabulary', '責める', '責める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>blame</kanji> so this also means <vocabulary>to blame</vocabulary>. Additionally, this can also mean <vocabulary>to criticize</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to reproach</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to accuse</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4454', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%AC%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:45:49.45912Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:39:30.966803Z'),
(4455, 'vocabulary', '責任', '責任', 'You learned 責 means "blame," but it could also mean <kanji>responsibility</kanji>, right? Well, when responsibility meets <kanji>duty</kanji>, it brings about even more <vocabulary>responsibility</vocabulary>. You have a responsibility to do your duty, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4455', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%AC%E4%BB%BB', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:45:55.114967Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:51:43.141407Z'),
(4456, 'vocabulary', '省く', '省く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>conserve</kanji>... but why stop there when you could try <vocabulary>to omit</vocabulary> everything and <vocabulary>to eliminate</vocabulary> everything? Just take exactly what you need, conserve it, and omit everything else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4456', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%81%E3%81%8F', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:46:00.554195Z', NULL, '2025-11-02T22:39:57.694852Z'),
(4457, 'vocabulary', '反省', '反省', 'If you''re <kanji>anti</kanji> <kanji>conserve</kanji>, I''m going to ask you to do some heavy <vocabulary>reconsideration</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>reflection</vocabulary>, and see if that''s really the right way to be. If you change your thinking, you might even end up showing <vocabulary>regret</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4457', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8D%E7%9C%81', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:46:20.753282Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:30:02.229578Z'),
(4458, 'vocabulary', '増える', '増える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>increase</kanji>, so the vocabulary verb version means <vocabulary>to increase</vocabulary>. This is when something is just increasing on its own, and you''re not doing the increasing. You know this because you like to <reading>err</reading> (える) on the side of caution and not touch anything when it''s increasing, therefore you''re not doing any of the increasing yourself (it''s just happening).

This is an everyday word that refers to an increase in number or quantity, but keep in mind that it cannot be used to express an increase in degree.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4458', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%97%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:46:27.615701Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:50.598071Z'),
(4459, 'vocabulary', '増加', '増加', 'An <kanji>increase</kanji> will <kanji>add</kanji> to what you had before. This is an <vocabulary>increase</vocabulary>.

This word refers to an increase in number or quantity, often used in formal contexts, reports, or news articles.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4459', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%97%E5%8A%A0', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:46:39.881562Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:15:08.580781Z'),
(4460, 'vocabulary', '急増', '急増', 'A <kanji>sudden</kanji> <kanji>increase</kanji> is just that, a <vocabulary>sudden increase</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>surge</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4460', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A5%E5%A2%97', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:46:48.19894Z', NULL, '2025-09-16T03:26:30.841756Z'),
(4461, 'vocabulary', '増税', '増税', 'When you <kanji>increase</kanji> <kanji>tax</kanji> you''re doing a <vocabulary>tax increase</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4461', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%97%E7%A8%8E', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:46:56.058855Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:45:00.948456Z'),
(4462, 'vocabulary', '税金', '税金', 'The <kanji>tax</kanji> <kanji>gold</kanji> is what you have to pay when you have to pay a <vocabulary>tax</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4462', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%8E%E9%87%91', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:47:25.09209Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:45:00.948456Z'),
(4463, 'vocabulary', '無税', '無税', 'When the <kanji>tax</kanji> is <kanji>nothing</kanji> things are <vocabulary>tax free</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4463', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E7%A8%8E', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:47:32.998093Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:45:00.948456Z'),
(4464, 'vocabulary', '解ける', '解ける', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>solve</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be solved</vocabulary>. In a similar vein, this can also mean <vocabulary>to be cleared</vocabulary>, like with misunderstandings or tension in a room.

解ける is an intransitive verb, so it doesn''t take a direct object. In other words, you use it to say something is solved or gets solved. You can remember this because you don''t <reading>care</reading> (ける) enough to solve things yourself, you just let things get solved on their own.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4464', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A3%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 15, 1, '2012-12-04T07:47:53.888791Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.429208Z'),
(4465, 'vocabulary', '解決', '解決', 'You sit down to <kanji>solve</kanji> an issue and firmly <kanji>decide</kanji> on your course of action. This process is called <vocabulary>solving</vocabulary>, and it leads to a <vocabulary>solution</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>settlement</vocabulary>.

解決 is about finding a resolution or bringing a matter to a close. It can be used either for small-scale personal problems or for large-scale problems like social and political issues.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4465', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A3%E6%B1%BA', 10, 1, '2012-12-04T07:48:00.402654Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:56.541771Z'),
(4466, 'vocabulary', '理解', '理解', 'When the <kanji>reason</kanji> is <kanji>solve</kanji>d, you reach an <vocabulary>understanding</vocabulary> of something. You reach <vocabulary>comprehension</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4466', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%90%86%E8%A7%A3', 13, 1, '2012-12-04T07:48:11.106021Z', NULL, '2025-06-25T14:39:15.000425Z'),
(4467, 'vocabulary', '正解', '正解', 'It is <kanji>correct</kanji> you have <kanji>solve</kanji>d the answer, getting the <vocabulary>correct answer</vocabulary>. You''ll see this on Japanese game shows a lot. 正解！！！', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4467', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A3%E8%A7%A3', 11, 1, '2012-12-04T07:48:32.955823Z', NULL, '2025-06-25T14:34:13.177127Z'),
(4468, 'vocabulary', '分解', '分解', 'When you <kanji>divide</kanji> and <kanji>untie</kanji> things, you''re unraveling the connections between them. In other words, you''re doing a <vocabulary>breakdown</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>dismantling</vocabulary> of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4468', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%86%E8%A7%A3', 27, 1, '2012-12-04T07:48:47.206236Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T20:23:40.067262Z'),
(4469, 'vocabulary', '実際', '実際', 'A <kanji>truth</kanji> <kanji>occasion</kanji> is <vocabulary>reality</vocabulary>. This word is also used a lot as an adverb (with or without the particle に) to mean <vocabulary>actually</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>really</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4469', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9F%E9%9A%9B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:48:54.344231Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:50.949215Z'),
(4470, 'vocabulary', '国際', '国際', 'You have an <kanji> occasion</kanji> to check out another <kanji>country</kanji>. Wow, so <vocabulary>international</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4470', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BD%E9%9A%9B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:48:59.727293Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:50.970247Z'),
(4471, 'vocabulary', '際', '際', 'Even though the kanji mainly means <kanji>occasion</kanji>, for some reason this word means <vocabulary>side</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>edge</vocabulary>. I suppose on <kanji>occasion</kanji> kanji takes you to the <vocabulary>edge</vocabulary> of what ought to make sense when it comes to kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4471', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%9B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:49:05.237891Z', NULL, '2025-11-28T15:18:36.881108Z'),
(4472, 'vocabulary', '認める', '認める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>recognize</kanji> or <kanji>acknowledge</kanji>, so the verb also means <vocabulary>to acknowledge</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to recognize</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to perceive</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to admit</vocabulary>, among others.

This can be used to mean recognizing somebody or something visually, as well as in the sense of "to acknowledge" or "to admit". So you can use this verb to recognize someone''s presence, achievements, marriage, or your own defeat. That said, you''ll usually see 認める in the sense of "to acknowledge".', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4472', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%8D%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:49:11.671981Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.023373Z'),
(4473, 'vocabulary', '確認', '確認', 'Just be sure to make <kanji>certain</kanji> that you <kanji>recognize</kanji> him. We need <vocabulary>confirmation</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>verification</vocabulary>, so make certain again, please.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4473', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A2%BA%E8%AA%8D', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:49:17.478639Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.045259Z'),
(4474, 'vocabulary', '企てる', '企てる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>plan</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to plan</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4474', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%81%E3%81%A6%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:49:44.519821Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.068967Z'),
(4475, 'vocabulary', '企画', '企画', 'Take your <kanji>plan</kanji> and add some <kanji>image</kanji>s to it. This is your <vocabulary>plan</vocabulary>.

While 計画 refers to plans in general, 企画 specifically refers to the planning of events, projects, or initiatives, as well as the actual projects being planned.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4475', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%81%E7%94%BB', 16, 1, '2012-12-04T07:49:51.794967Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.37784Z'),
(4476, 'vocabulary', '正義', '正義', 'The <kanji>correct</kanji> <kanji>righteousness</kanji> is always your <vocabulary>righteousness</vocabulary>. It is your form of <vocabulary>justice</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4476', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A3%E7%BE%A9', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:49:58.486324Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:46:56.671656Z'),
(4477, 'vocabulary', '主義', '主義', 'The <kanji>main</kanji> cause behind someone''s <kanji>righteousness</kanji> is usually what <vocabulary>ism</vocabulary>s, <vocabulary>principle</vocabulary>s or <vocabulary>doctrine</vocabulary>s they believe in. Buddh<vocabulary>ism</vocabulary>, Shinto<vocabulary>ism</vocabulary>, social<vocabulary>ism</vocabulary>, commun<vocabulary>ism</vocabulary>, you name it. If you believe in one of those it''s the main driving factor behind what you believe righteousness to be.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4477', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB%E7%BE%A9', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:50:05.260759Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:46:56.671656Z'),
(4478, 'vocabulary', '義理', '義理', 'You have <kanji>righteousness</kanji> and then a <kanji>reason</kanji> behind it, at least in your own mind. This is your <vocabulary>sense of duty</vocabulary>. And it really is a "sense" in a way, too. You just feel it. It''s not an actual thing that is tangible. It is your own personal reason for this righteousness.

This word can also mean <vocabulary>in-law</vocabulary> like your mother-in-law, because they''re your mother in the social obligation sense only, not by blood.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4478', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%A9%E7%90%86', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:50:19.800405Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:46:56.671656Z'),
(4479, 'vocabulary', '定義', '定義', 'Please <kanji>determine</kanji> what <kanji>righteousness</kanji> means. I want the <vocabulary>definition</vocabulary> for it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4479', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9A%E7%BE%A9', 21, 1, '2012-12-04T07:50:27.155043Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(4480, 'vocabulary', '圧倒する', '圧倒する', 'The <kanji>pressure</kanji> used easily <kanji>collapse</kanji>s whatever it''s pushing against. That''s why this verb means <vocabulary>to overwhelm</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4480', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%A7%E5%80%92%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:36:59.081487Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:28:34.36878Z'),
(4481, 'vocabulary', '高血圧', '高血圧', 'Remember blood pressure (血圧)? Well, now the blood pressure is <kanji>tall</kanji>... so you have "tall blood pressure." Well that''s dumb. Let''s call it <vocabulary>high blood pressure</vocabulary> instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4481', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E8%A1%80%E5%9C%A7', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:37:04.269709Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:28:34.36878Z'),
(4482, 'vocabulary', '存在する', '存在する', 'Know how 存在 is existence? Well, make it a verb and then you have <vocabulary>to exist</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4482', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%98%E5%9C%A8%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:37:10.431102Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.266853Z'),
(4483, 'vocabulary', '嫌味', '嫌味', 'You have a <kanji>flavor</kanji> of <kanji>dislike</kanji>. If someone gives you "flavor" and you dislike it, what might that "flavor" be? How about <vocabulary>hurtful words</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4483', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AB%8C%E5%91%B3', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:37:17.94734Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:14:52.95141Z'),
(4484, 'vocabulary', '機会', '機会', 'When the <kanji>machine</kanji>s <kanji>meet</kanji>, you finally have the <vocabulary>opportunity</vocabulary> to take them all out at once. DIE MACHINES!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4484', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A9%9F%E4%BC%9A', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:37:24.880159Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:04:30.700843Z'),
(4485, 'vocabulary', '防犯', '防犯', 'To <kanji>prevent</kanji> <kanji>crime</kanji> is a good thing. To do this you use <vocabulary>crime prevention</vocabulary> techniques.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4485', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%98%B2%E7%8A%AF', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:37:32.83558Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.335314Z'),
(4486, 'vocabulary', '産業', '産業', 'What will <kanji>give birth</kanji> to <kanji>business</kanji>? Well, some kind of <vocabulary>industry</vocabulary>, probably. Though <vocabulary>heavy industry</vocabulary> is pretty good too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4486', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%A3%E6%A5%AD', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:37:37.664562Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(4487, 'vocabulary', '被害者', '被害者', 'You learned 被害 means "damage," and this is about <kanji>someone</kanji> who suffers damage, so this means <vocabulary>victim</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4487', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A2%AB%E5%AE%B3%E8%80%85', 21, 1, '2012-12-06T01:37:44.511071Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(4488, 'vocabulary', '寝る', '寝る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>sleep</kanji> or <kanji>lie down</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to sleep</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to lie down</vocabulary>—just like what you do when you decide <vocabulary>to go to bed</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4488', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%9D%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:39:35.985914Z', NULL, '2025-12-25T00:13:44.862555Z'),
(4489, 'vocabulary', '昼寝', '昼寝', 'A <kanji>noon</kanji> <kanji>sleep</kanji> is sleep you do during the day, also known as a <vocabulary>nap</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4489', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%BC%E5%AF%9D', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:39:47.065296Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T22:11:42.74859Z'),
(4490, 'vocabulary', '寝不足', '寝不足', 'Remember how 不足 is a physical shortage? Well, now you have a physical shortage of <kanji>sleep</kanji>. That''s a <vocabulary>lack of sleep</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4490', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%9D%E4%B8%8D%E8%B6%B3', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:40:03.362417Z', NULL, '2025-06-25T14:00:39.611736Z'),
(4491, 'vocabulary', '寝坊', '寝坊', 'You <kanji>sleep</kanji> like a <kanji>monk</kanji>, as in you never stop sleeping (because you sleep while you meditate). When you sleep like a monk like this you''re constantly <vocabulary>oversleeping</vocabulary>, probably because you''re up late doing sutras or something. You''re a <vocabulary>late riser</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4491', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%9D%E5%9D%8A', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:40:11.107072Z', NULL, '2025-06-25T14:00:39.611736Z'),
(4492, 'vocabulary', '過ぎる', '過ぎる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>surpass</kanji>, but this verb is intransitive, meaning we’re not focussing on who did the action, but on the action itself. So this means <vocabulary>to pass</vocabulary>, as in “the storm passed”, <vocabulary>to be over</vocabulary> as in “winter’s over”, or <vocabulary>to be too much</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4492', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8E%E3%81%8E%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:40:17.777209Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.486784Z'),
(4493, 'vocabulary', '過去', '過去', 'If you <kanji>surpass</kanji> the <kanji>past</kanji> then you''re going backwards in time (at least if you''re coming from the "present"). If this is the case, then you are definitely in the <vocabulary>past</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4493', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8E%E5%8E%BB', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:40:23.310205Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:21:59.859898Z'),
(4494, 'vocabulary', '罰', '罰', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4494', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BD%B0', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:40:29.943086Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:49:26.899294Z'),
(4495, 'vocabulary', '罰金', '罰金', '<kanji>Penalty</kanji> <kanji>gold</kanji> is the money you pay when you get a penalty. It''s for <vocabulary>a fine</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4495', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BD%B0%E9%87%91', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:40:41.347919Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:49:26.899294Z'),
(4496, 'vocabulary', '罰ゲーム', '罰ゲーム', 'A <kanji>penalty</kanji> <kanji>game</kanji> (ゲーム) is a fun kind of game you see on Japanese television a lot. When you mess up, you are punished. Usually it hurts. You can call this a <vocabulary>batsu game</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>penalty game</vocabulary>. Don''t call it anything else, though, or you know what happens...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4496', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BD%B0%E3%82%B2%E3%83%BC%E3%83%A0', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:40:47.76313Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:49:26.899294Z'),
(4497, 'vocabulary', '罰する', '罰する', 'When you do a <kanji> penalty</kanji> to someone you are trying <vocabulary>to punish</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to penalize</vocabulary> them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4497', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BD%B0%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:40:55.296177Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:49:26.899294Z'),
(4498, 'vocabulary', '誕生', '誕生', 'Give <kanji>birth</kanji> to <kanji>life</kanji>! That is a <vocabulary>birth</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4498', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%95%E7%94%9F', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:41:07.527211Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.631475Z'),
(4499, 'vocabulary', '脱ぐ', '脱ぐ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>undress</kanji> and the verb is <vocabulary>to undress</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4499', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%B1%E3%81%90', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T01:41:15.735723Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.664503Z'),
(4500, 'vocabulary', '脱線', '脱線', 'When something <kanji>escape</kanji>s from the <kanji>line</kanji> it should be on, that''s a <vocabulary>derailment</vocabulary>.

脱線 can refer to train derailments (no surprise there), but often gets used figuratively to mean a <vocabulary>digression</vocabulary>, like in speech or writing. Do you ever derail conversations with your digressions?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4500', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%B1%E7%B7%9A', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T01:41:21.714915Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:30:42.218788Z'),
(4501, 'vocabulary', '脱字', '脱字', 'You''ve got an <kanji>escape</kanji>d <kanji>letter</kanji>, in other words, an <vocabulary>omitted character</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>omitted letter</vocabulary>.

脱字 refers to the kind of typos where letters, characters, or even whole words are accidentally omitted from what you''re writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4501', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%B1%E5%AD%97', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:41:26.580967Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.71087Z'),
(4502, 'vocabulary', '脱走する', '脱走する', 'You <kanji>escape</kanji> and <kanji>run</kanji> the hell out of there. Basically, you''re trying <vocabulary>to escape</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to flee</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to break out</vocabulary>. Pretty simple.

脱走する specifically refers to escaping from a controlled or confined environment, like escaping from prison, a military base, or a guarded facility.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4502', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%B1%E8%B5%B0%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T01:41:33.246849Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.737273Z'),
(4503, 'vocabulary', '脱税', '脱税', 'You''re trying to <kanji>escape</kanji> from <kanji>tax</kanji>es? You scared of them? Huh? That''s <vocabulary>tax evasion</vocabulary>, buddy. You''re going to jail for that. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4503', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%B1%E7%A8%8E', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T01:41:39.174337Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T18:17:09.206446Z'),
(4504, 'vocabulary', '坊さん', '坊さん', 'さん is what you put on people''s names. It''s sort of like "Mr." So a "Mr. Monk" is a monk — specifically a <vocabulary>Buddhist monk</vocabulary>. 

坊さん is a casual term for monks. Even in its more polite form, お坊さん, it''s still a bit too informal to use when speaking directly to a monk. So it''s okay to use when you''re talking <em>about</em> them, but not to them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4504', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9D%8A%E3%81%95%E3%82%93', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:41:44.274657Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.795954Z'),
(4505, 'vocabulary', '赤ん坊', '赤ん坊', 'Here''s a weird one for you. You have a <kanji>red</kanji> <kanji>monk</kanji>. What is this? It''s a <vocabulary>baby</vocabulary>. Think about it. Babies, when they come out, are all red. Also, they have a shaved head, just like a monk would. So, a "red monk" is really just a baby. I wonder who''s getting the shorter end of the stick here, the monk or the baby? Probably the monk.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4505', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%A4%E3%82%93%E5%9D%8A', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:41:49.929454Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.822986Z'),
(4506, 'vocabulary', '坊主', '坊主', 'A <kanji>monk</kanji> <kanji>master</kanji> is still a monk, and this is used for a <vocabulary>Buddhist monk</vocabulary> specifically. Keep in mind this isn''t a word you''d use to address monks directly though, so don''t use it when you''re speaking to them!

This word also is used for describing a <vocabulary>shaved head</vocabulary> (you can probably guess why). That might be why it can also be a casual way of addressing a young <vocabulary>boy</vocabulary>, since in the old days a shaved head was the default hairstyle for boys in Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4506', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9D%8A%E4%B8%BB', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:42:00.4855Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.849247Z'),
(4507, 'vocabulary', '宮', '宮', 'This <kanji>Shinto shrine</kanji> you''re visiting? Turns out if you go inside it''s magically turned into the <vocabulary>imperial palace</vocabulary>. Wow! Feels like I’m a member of the <vocabulary>imperial family</vocabulary> now.

宮 is a historical word that generally refers to Japanese imperial palaces, the Japanese imperial family, or the imperial family members. Depending on the context, it can also refer to a Shinto shrine or its buildings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4507', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%AE', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:42:13.855133Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:51.873359Z'),
(4508, 'vocabulary', '各々', '各々', 'You have <kanji>each</kanji> <kanji>each</kanji> which is <vocabulary>each</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>every</vocabulary>, or "<vocabulary>respectively</vocabulary>"). Kind of goes along the lines of "each and every" in English.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4508', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%84%E3%80%85', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:42:18.694939Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:24:01.572608Z'),
(4509, 'vocabulary', '各〜', '各〜', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4509', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%84%E3%80%9C', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:42:25.193924Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:24:01.572608Z'),
(4510, 'vocabulary', '各地', '各地', '<kanji>Each</kanji> <kanji>ground</kanji> is its own ground. It is <vocabulary>each area</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>various areas</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4510', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%84%E5%9C%B0', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:42:30.4754Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:24:01.572608Z'),
(4511, 'vocabulary', '各自', '各自', '<kanji>Each</kanji> <kanji>self</kanji> refers to <vocabulary>each person</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>individual</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4511', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%84%E8%87%AA', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:42:36.43734Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:24:01.572608Z'),
(4512, 'vocabulary', '案内', '案内', 'The <kanji>plan</kanji> <kanji>inside</kanji> this piece of paper is the <vocabulary>guide</vocabulary> you''ll be using. It''s a plan. It''s inside. Use it as your guide!

Depending on the context, this can also be <vocabulary>information</vocabulary> and even <vocabulary>invitation</vocabulary>. These are all things that guide you in some way, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4512', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A1%88%E5%86%85', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:42:42.773736Z', NULL, '2025-09-05T05:40:05.235761Z'),
(4513, 'vocabulary', '案外', '案外', 'Something that happens <kanji>outside</kanji> the <kanji>plan</kanji> is something that happens <vocabulary>unexpectedly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4513', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A1%88%E5%A4%96', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:42:53.974897Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(4514, 'vocabulary', '置く', '置く', 'When you <kanji>put</kanji> something you don''t just put it, you have <vocabulary>to put down</vocabulary> this item or even <vocabulary>(to) set down</vocabulary> the item. Basically, you have to put something down with this verb. It''s a little different from the kanji meaning you learned but not that different.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4514', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BD%AE%E3%81%8F', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:42:59.191204Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:08:17.52437Z'),
(4515, 'vocabulary', '位置', '位置', 'Go ahead and <kanji>put</kanji> a <kanji>rank</kanji> on something. This one is rank #1, so its <vocabulary>position</vocabulary> is up higher. Then you put a rank #67 on something. That one''s position is lower.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4515', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%8D%E7%BD%AE', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:43:11.328672Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:08:17.52437Z'),
(4516, 'vocabulary', '費用', '費用', 'The <kanji>expense</kanji> of <kanji>use</kanji> is the <vocabulary>expense</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>cost</vocabulary> we have to pay, I''m afraid. If you use something, there''ll be expense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4516', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%BB%E7%94%A8', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:43:16.304689Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.09171Z'),
(4517, 'vocabulary', '消費', '消費', 'You <kanji>extinguish</kanji> an item and therefore have to <kanji>expense</kanji> it. This is how <vocabulary>consumption</vocabulary> works. Expense, extinguish, consumption.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4517', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B6%88%E8%B2%BB', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:43:22.032013Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.114525Z'),
(4518, 'vocabulary', '社費', '社費', 'A <kanji>company</kanji> <kanji>expense</kanji> is a <vocabulary>company expense</vocabulary>. These are nice, by the way, if you can get them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4518', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A4%BE%E8%B2%BB', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:43:29.292369Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.136716Z'),
(4519, 'vocabulary', '価', '価', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

価 refers to the monetary value or the price of goods in a transaction. Although it''s somewhat old-fashioned and less common these days, you may still see it used in advanced phrases like 価をつける (to set a price) or 一文の価もない (not even worth a penny).

Note that あたい can also refer to a numerical value in mathematics, such as the value a variable or function takes, but it''s more commonly written with the kanji 値 in this use.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4519', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%A1', 49, 1, '2012-12-06T01:43:36.608714Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:51:00.759578Z'),
(4520, 'vocabulary', '物価', '物価', '<kanji>Thing</kanji>s with <kanji>value</kanji> have <vocabulary>prices</vocabulary>.

物価 refers to the prices of goods and services in a place — the <vocabulary>prices of commodities</vocabulary>, a.k.a. the <vocabulary>cost of living</vocabulary>. It''s used when discussing how expensive it is to live somewhere or when talking about inflation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4520', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%A9%E4%BE%A1', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:43:42.792474Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:51:00.759578Z'),
(4521, 'vocabulary', '地価', '地価', 'The <kanji>ground</kanji>''s <kanji>value</kanji> is the <vocabulary>land value</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4521', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E4%BE%A1', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T01:43:48.966263Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:51:00.759578Z'),
(4522, 'vocabulary', '勢い', '勢い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, which means you might think this is an adjective, but it''s actually a noun! What''s the noun form of <kanji>force</kanji>? It''s also something''s <vocabulary>force</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>momentum</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4522', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%A2%E3%81%84', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:01:42.101557Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.231153Z'),
(4523, 'vocabulary', '勢力', '勢力', 'Your <kanji>force</kanji> and <kanji>power</kanji> combine together to create an <vocabulary>influence</vocabulary> (or a <vocabulary>force</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4523', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%A2%E5%8A%9B', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:01:49.252474Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(4524, 'vocabulary', '姿勢', '姿勢', 'Put a <kanji>force</kanji> on your <kanji>figure</kanji> to make it into a certain shape. This is your <vocabulary>posture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4524', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%BF%E5%8B%A2', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:01:55.148753Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:49:37.442091Z'),
(4525, 'vocabulary', '大勢', '大勢', 'A <kanji>large</kanji> <kanji>force</kanji> is a group of people. Just think about when this word was probably made up... a long time ago, and the biggest force you could have was a huge group of people. This group of people force is also known as a <vocabulary>crowd</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>a large number</vocabulary> of people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4525', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%8B%A2', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:02:13.055871Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(4526, 'vocabulary', '営む', '営む', 'The kanji means <kanji>manage</kanji>, but when you manage something, what are you usually managing? How about a business? So this word means <vocabulary>to run a business</vocabulary> even though the kanji is manage.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4526', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%B6%E3%82%80', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:02:20.420052Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:07:38.827718Z'),
(4527, 'vocabulary', '営業', '営業', '<kanji>Manag</kanji>ing a <kanji>business</kanji>? That''s <vocabulary>business</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4527', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%B6%E6%A5%AD', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:02:25.469751Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(4528, 'vocabulary', '公営', '公営', 'Something that is <kanji>public</kanji>ly <kanji>manage</kanji>d is something that is <vocabulary>public</vocabulary> aka <vocabulary>government run</vocabulary>. Compare this to 私営 which is private / privately run.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4528', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AC%E5%96%B6', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:02:34.281387Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.38299Z'),
(4529, 'vocabulary', '私営', '私営', 'Anything that''s <kanji>private</kanji>ly <kanji>manage</kanji>d is <vocabulary>private</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>privately run</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4529', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%81%E5%96%B6', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:02:39.641992Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.405841Z'),
(4530, 'vocabulary', '市営', '市営', 'Something that is <kanji>manage</kanji>d by the <kanji>city</kanji> is <vocabulary>run by the city</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>city-run</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4530', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%82%E5%96%B6', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:02:44.71381Z', NULL, '2025-10-02T17:24:51.43252Z'),
(4531, 'vocabulary', '官営', '官営', '<kanji>Manage</kanji>d by the <kanji>government</kanji>? Then it is run by <vocabulary>government management</vocabulary> or it is a  <vocabulary>government operation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4531', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%98%E5%96%B6', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:02:50.882045Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.452353Z'),
(4532, 'vocabulary', '県営', '県営', '<kanji>Prefecture</kanji> <kanji>manage</kanji>d things are <vocabulary>run by the prefecture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4532', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%8C%E5%96%B6', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:02:56.856107Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:25:07.757919Z'),
(4533, 'vocabulary', '示す', '示す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>indicate</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to indicate</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to show</vocabulary>. This is used in contexts like "a study shows that learning Japanese is fun" and "I demonstrated my willingness to learn kanji".', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4533', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A4%BA%E3%81%99', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:03:10.302558Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.501353Z'),
(4534, 'vocabulary', '指示', '指示', 'Use your <kanji>finger</kanji> to <kanji>indicate</kanji> some things. These are your <vocabulary>instructions</vocabulary>. Okay, do this (point), do that (point), and then finally do this (point).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4534', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87%E7%A4%BA', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:03:15.460997Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.526519Z'),
(4535, 'vocabulary', '公示', '公示', 'Please <kanji>indicate</kanji> this in a <kanji>public</kanji> way. How about a <vocabulary>public announcement</vocabulary>?

This word refers to formal public announcements, like parliamentary elections by the Emperor or land price disclosures by the Land Appraisal Committee.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4535', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AC%E7%A4%BA', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:03:21.652567Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.551187Z'),
(4536, 'vocabulary', '大統領', '大統領', 'When you <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>rule</kanji> over a <kanji>territory</kanji>, you''re probably a pretty great leader. A <vocabulary>president</vocabulary>, perhaps?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4536', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E7%B5%B1%E9%A0%98', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:03:28.156673Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(4537, 'vocabulary', '伝統', '伝統', 'When you <kanji>transmit</kanji> something that <kanji>unite</kanji>s the people, you are probably pulling on <vocabulary>tradition</vocabulary>. Tradition is what unites us all and transmits who we are to everyone else. What is your 伝統?

伝統 means "tradition" in the sense of customs, practices, or rituals that are passed down within a culture or community. These traditions bring people together across generations and keep our shared culture alive.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4537', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9D%E7%B5%B1', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:03:33.223729Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.602376Z'),
(4538, 'vocabulary', '統合', '統合', 'When you <kanji>unite</kanji> and <kanji>join</kanji> things together, you get <vocabulary>unification</vocabulary>! This can also mean <vocabulary>integration</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>consolidation</vocabulary>, which are essentially other ways to describe things being united.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4538', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B1%E5%90%88', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:03:38.9813Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T21:33:11.249808Z'),
(4539, 'vocabulary', '受領書', '受領書', 'Please <kanji>accept</kanji> this <kanji>territory</kanji>. Oh, you want some <kanji>writing</kanji> to make the deal official? Okay, here''s a <vocabulary>receipt</vocabulary>.

This word refers to a document which serves as proof of receiving goods, services, or money in an official setting, like a business transaction. If you’re just talking about the receipt you get while shopping, you can simply say レシート or 領収書. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4539', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%97%E9%A0%98%E6%9B%B8', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T02:03:44.493196Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:48.601574Z'),
(4540, 'vocabulary', '領域', '領域', 'This is my <kanji>territory</kanji>. This is my <kanji>region</kanji>. This is my <vocabulary>domain</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4540', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%98%E5%9F%9F', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:03:49.261284Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:54:25.651299Z'),
(4541, 'vocabulary', '領土', '領土', 'The <kanji>soil</kanji> that makes up your <kanji>territory</kanji> is your <vocabulary>territory</vocabulary>. Ain''t nobody goin'' set foot on my soil nuh''uh.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4541', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%98%E5%9C%9F', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:03:54.341522Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.703388Z'),
(4542, 'vocabulary', '策', '策', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4542', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AD%96', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:04:05.682672Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.72637Z'),
(4543, 'vocabulary', '政策', '政策', 'A <kanji>plan</kanji> made by the <kanji>government</kanji> is usually known as a <vocabulary>policy</vocabulary>. "The government passed a policy prohibiting the use of guns on zombies, dooming us all."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4543', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BF%E7%AD%96', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:04:11.134093Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:13.248691Z'),
(4544, 'vocabulary', '藤', '藤', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4544', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%97%A4', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:04:19.958228Z', NULL, '2025-07-09T17:56:03.0248Z'),
(4545, 'vocabulary', '副詞', '副詞', 'This is like your <kanji>side</kanji> <kanji>part of speech</kanji>, as in, you add it to the side of a verb because it''s an <vocabulary>adverb</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4545', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%AF%E8%A9%9E', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:04:31.473561Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.803424Z'),
(4546, 'vocabulary', '副業', '副業', 'A <kanji>side</kanji> <kanji>business</kanji> is a <vocabulary>side business</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4546', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%AF%E6%A5%AD', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:09:25.957402Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(4547, 'vocabulary', '副題', '副題', 'The <kanji>vice</kanji> <kanji>topic</kanji> is the <vocabulary>subtitle</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>subheading</vocabulary>. You know, like the <vocabulary>secondary title</vocabulary> you sometimes see after the colon in a book title. (Which, by the way, have you read <em>WaniKani 2: Return of the Crabigator</em> yet?) ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4547', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%AF%E9%A1%8C', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:09:34.116119Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:18:01.665189Z'),
(4548, 'vocabulary', '値', '値', 'A <kanji>value</kanji> is a <vocabulary>value</vocabulary>, but only in the mathematical sense.

Depending on the reading, this can also mean <vocabulary>price</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4548', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%A4', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:09:39.755114Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:09:02.854992Z'),
(4549, 'vocabulary', '安値', '安値', 'A <kanji>cheap</kanji> <kanji>value</kanji>d item comes at a <vocabulary>low price</vocabulary>. What a good deal!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4549', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%89%E5%80%A4', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T02:10:02.227148Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:09:02.854992Z'),
(4550, 'vocabulary', '価値', '価値', 'A <kanji>value</kanji> and a <kanji>value</kanji>. Obviously this is something that has much <vocabulary>value</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4550', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%A1%E5%80%A4', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:10:27.627979Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:51:00.759578Z'),
(4551, 'vocabulary', '高値', '高値', 'A <kanji>tall</kanji> <kanji>value</kanji>. If you paid for something that had a tall value, it would have a <vocabulary>high price</vocabulary>.

This word is often used when something sells for a comparatively high price. Like when a stock is hitting highs, or goods are sold for higher than the average market price, or something can fetch a high price at auction.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4551', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E5%80%A4', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T02:10:34.346568Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:09:02.854992Z'),
(4552, 'vocabulary', '観る', '観る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>view</kanji> so what is the verb vocab version? It''s <vocabulary>to view</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to watch</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4552', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%B3%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:10:47.80706Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.963569Z'),
(4553, 'vocabulary', '観光', '観光', 'You''re out on the town <kanji>view</kanji>ing the <kanji>light</kanji>s! You know, big holiday light-up displays, flashing marquees on famous theaters, flickering lanterns at mysterious old shrines. Why are you visiting all these cool, photogenic spots? Because you''re <vocabulary>sightseeing</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4553', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%B3%E5%85%89', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:10:54.271994Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:52.990977Z'),
(4554, 'vocabulary', '観客', '観客', 'The <kanji>guest</kanji>s who are <kanji>view</kanji>ing are the <vocabulary>audience</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>spectators</vocabulary>. That''s all that viewers who are guests at a location do, right? Makes total sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4554', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%B3%E5%AE%A2', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:11:04.919857Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.015915Z'),
(4555, 'vocabulary', '外観', '外観', 'The <kanji>outside</kanji> <kanji>view</kanji> of you is your <vocabulary>external appearance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4555', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E8%A6%B3', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:11:10.309867Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(4556, 'vocabulary', '主観', '主観', 'What is your <kanji>main</kanji> <kanji>view</kanji>? Your <vocabulary>subjective view</vocabulary>, of course! This can be your own <vocabulary>subjective opinion</vocabulary>, and also refers to the idea of the <vocabulary>subjective</vocabulary> in general (as opposed to the objective). It can be hard to step back and look at things without your own unique biases and perspectives.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4556', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB%E8%A6%B3', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:11:15.912926Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.068402Z'),
(4557, 'vocabulary', '観念', '観念', 'You have a <kanji>thought</kanji> on a particular <kanji>view</kanji>. This is your <vocabulary>idea</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>notion</vocabulary> on it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4557', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%B3%E5%BF%B5', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:11:28.173642Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.091792Z'),
(4558, 'vocabulary', '姿', '姿', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4558', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%BF', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:11:33.955717Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T15:44:52.079877Z'),
(4559, 'vocabulary', '区域', '区域', 'When there''s a <kanji>district</kanji> in a <kanji>region</kanji>, it''s called a <vocabulary>zone</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>district</vocabulary>.

区域 is a formal word for a kind of designated <vocabulary>area</vocabulary>, usually one defined by specific boundaries or characteristics. It''s often used in urban planning, like for no-parking zones or residential areas. It can also apply to regions like postal districts, police jurisdictions, or restricted areas.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4559', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%BA%E5%9F%9F', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:11:46.785897Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:54:25.651299Z'),
(4560, 'vocabulary', '地域', '地域', 'The <kanji>region</kanji> that is this <kanji>ground</kanji> is the <vocabulary>region</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>area</vocabulary>. Just think of each area of ground. Each area is each region.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4560', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E5%9F%9F', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:11:53.759305Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:54:25.651299Z'),
(4561, 'vocabulary', '吸う', '吸う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>suck</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to suck</vocabulary>, but it''s usually used to talk about what you do to cigarettes, so a better translation I think is <vocabulary>to smoke</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4561', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%B8%E3%81%86', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:12:08.208205Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.212516Z'),
(4562, 'vocabulary', '応じる', '応じる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>respond</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to respond</vocabulary>.

応じる is a formal word that describes acting favorably in response to a call, request, or demand. For example, 注文に応じる goes beyond just taking orders; it means adapting appropriately and fulfilling them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4562', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%9C%E3%81%98%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:12:19.104593Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.242334Z'),
(4563, 'vocabulary', '一応', '一応', 'Okay, only <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>response</kanji> for that, and that is that we have an agreement... <vocabulary>for now</vocabulary>. Just think of the one response you have, and that one response is just to get everyone off your back, so you agree with them... for now.

一応 indicates doing something provisionally or as a precaution, even if it may not be sufficient. Its translation varies based on context and can include <vocabulary>to some extent</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>more or less</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>just in case</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4563', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%BF%9C', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:12:25.217641Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.268932Z'),
(4564, 'vocabulary', '反応', '反応', 'Whoops, this one comes out weird. You have <kanji>anti</kanji> <kanji>respond</kanji> though the meaning of this word is <vocabulary>a reaction</vocabulary>. Maybe you''re "anti respond" but you have no choice, you have to give <vocabulary>a response</vocabulary> despite that fact?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4564', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8D%E5%BF%9C', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:12:31.637668Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:30:02.229578Z'),
(4565, 'vocabulary', '提案', '提案', 'Please <kanji>present</kanji> your <kanji>plan</kanji> in the form of a <vocabulary>proposition</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4565', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%90%E6%A1%88', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:12:43.649293Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.31755Z'),
(4566, 'vocabulary', '提出', '提出', 'Go ahead and <kanji>present</kanji> then <kanji>exit</kanji>. This will be your <vocabulary>presentation</vocabulary> of the materials.

Perhaps it''s the <vocabulary>submission</vocabulary> of your resignation, or the <vocabulary>filing</vocabulary> of a complaint, which explains why you exit so quickly afterwards. You could also just be <vocabulary>handing in</vocabulary> your homework, though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4566', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%90%E5%87%BA', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:12:54.736038Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.34646Z'),
(4567, 'vocabulary', '援助', '援助', 'If you <kanji>aid</kanji> and <kanji>help</kanji> someone you are giving them <vocabulary>assistance</vocabulary>. How kind!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4567', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%B4%E5%8A%A9', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:13:25.467664Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.374338Z'),
(4568, 'vocabulary', '支援', '支援', 'To <kanji>support</kanji> and <kanji>aid</kanji> is to <vocabulary>support</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4568', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%AF%E6%8F%B4', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:14:04.047872Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.397941Z'),
(4569, 'vocabulary', '状況', '状況', 'The <kanji>condition</kanji> <kanji>condition</kanji> is the <vocabulary>condition</vocabulary>. It''s also the <vocabulary>circumstances</vocabulary> if you can remember that. It''s like saying "What''s the condition!??" which is similar to saying "What''s the circumstances?"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4569', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8A%B6%E6%B3%81', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T02:14:16.670611Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.422545Z'),
(4570, 'vocabulary', '状態', '状態', 'The <kanji>condition</kanji> that something is in and the <kanji>appearance</kanji> of it is the <vocabulary>condition</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>state</vocabulary> of that object.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4570', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8A%B6%E6%85%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:14:21.626204Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.446362Z'),
(4571, 'vocabulary', '変態', '変態', 'Your <kanji>appearance</kanji> is <kanji>strange</kanji>. You''re <vocabulary>abnormal</vocabulary>. Oh, and you''re a <vocabulary>pervert</vocabulary> because you look so abnormal. Though you could just be a caterpillar going through your <vocabulary>metamorphosis</vocabulary> to become a butterfly. You just look really weird while the changing is going on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4571', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%89%E6%85%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:14:26.701949Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.472362Z'),
(4572, 'vocabulary', '態と', '態と', 'So this doesn''t quite make sense in terms of the meaning of the kanji... but you''ll be okay. This means <vocabulary>on purpose</vocabulary>. Perhaps when you do something that has the <kanji>appearance</kanji> of doing what you wanted to do, you''re doing it on purpose.

態と is usually written in kana in modern writing, but you''ll occasionally see it in kanji too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4572', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%8B%E3%81%A8', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:14:31.514844Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.502469Z'),
(4573, 'vocabulary', '態度', '態度', 'Your <kanji>appearance</kanji> has many <kanji>degree</kanji>s. You turn it up a dial, and you show off one <vocabulary>attitude</vocabulary>. Then you turn it down a degree and you have another <vocabulary>behavior</vocabulary>. So many <vocabulary>manner</vocabulary>s to show off.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4573', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%8B%E5%BA%A6', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:14:36.822247Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.526668Z'),
(4574, 'vocabulary', '失態', '失態', 'The <kanji>appearance</kanji> of <kanji>fault</kanji> is a huge <vocabulary>blunder</vocabulary> on our family. At least do not have the appearance of fault.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4574', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%B1%E6%85%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:14:42.921181Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.552038Z'),
(4575, 'vocabulary', '賀状-4575', '賀状', '<kanji> Congratulations</kanji> on your <kanji>condition</kanji>. I''ll send you a <vocabulary>greeting card</vocabulary> to make it official. Though this kind of card is almost always in the form of a <vocabulary>New Year''s Card</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4575', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%80%E7%8A%B6-4575', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:14:49.145366Z', '2018-11-30T20:34:25.842382Z', '2025-11-06T02:02:19.646561Z'),
(4576, 'vocabulary', '各駅停車', '各駅停車', 'At  <kanji>each</kanji>  <kanji>station</kanji> someone keeps  <kanji>halt</kanji>ing the  <kanji>car </kanji>. What is this, a <vocabulary>local train</vocabulary>?! Yes, yes it is. You’re on a local train. People use it when they get on (or off) at the smaller stations.

If you’re not used to train travel, just remember that a "local train" is one that stops at every station on a line.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4576', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%84%E9%A7%85%E5%81%9C%E8%BB%8A', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:56:47.604178Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:24:01.572608Z'),
(4577, 'vocabulary', '確認する', '確認する', '確認 is confirmation. When that''s a verb, you have <vocabulary>to confirm</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to verify</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4577', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A2%BA%E8%AA%8D%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:56:59.725277Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.609032Z'),
(4578, 'vocabulary', '済ます', '済ます', 'Remember 済む and how it means "to come to an end." This is like that, but slightly different. This is <vocabulary>to finish something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to settle something</vocabulary>, because this is when you''re doing the coming to an end action. You can remember this because you''re coming in to finish the score uno <reading>mas</reading> (ます) time. This is the last time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4578', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%88%E3%81%BE%E3%81%99', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:57:20.096382Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.633692Z'),
(4579, 'vocabulary', '経済的', '経済的', '経済 is economics. So, something that has to do with economics is the <vocabulary>economic</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>financial</vocabulary> situation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4579', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%8C%E6%B8%88%E7%9A%84', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:57:33.862629Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.659034Z'),
(4580, 'vocabulary', '委員会', '委員会', 'There are member(s) of a committee (委員) all getting together to <kanji>meet</kanji>. This is the <vocabulary>committee</vocabulary> itself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4580', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%94%E5%93%A1%E4%BC%9A', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:57:44.766616Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:34:02.31068Z'),
(4581, 'vocabulary', '選挙する', '選挙する', '<kanji>Election</kanji> is 選挙 so when you make that word into a verb, you have <vocabulary>to elect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4581', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%B8%E6%8C%99%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:57:53.784969Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.715362Z'),
(4582, 'vocabulary', '判断する', '判断する', '判断 is judgement. Make that into a verb and you have <vocabulary>to judge</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to conclude</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4582', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A4%E6%96%AD%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:58:00.496432Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T21:56:23.352206Z'),
(4583, 'vocabulary', '責任感', '責任感', '責任 is "responsibility," and if you''re <kanji>feeling</kanji> it, then you must have a <vocabulary>sense of responsibility</vocabulary>. Good for you!

責任感 describes an internal drive to be reliable and do your duty. It''s often used to praise people who take their obligations seriously and follow through on what they''re supposed to do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4583', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%AC%E4%BB%BB%E6%84%9F', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:58:16.28861Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:39:30.966803Z'),
(4584, 'vocabulary', 'バス停', 'バス停', 'This is where the <kanji>bus</kanji> (バス) halts. It''s the <vocabulary>bus stop</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4584', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%90%E3%82%B9%E5%81%9C', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:58:23.247583Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.785995Z'),
(4585, 'vocabulary', '停止', '停止', '<kanji>Halt</kanji> and <kanji>stop</kanji>. When something does this, it''s in a <vocabulary>stoppage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4585', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%9C%E6%AD%A2', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:58:31.295183Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.808717Z'),
(4586, 'vocabulary', '停電', '停電', 'When you <kanji>halt</kanji> <kanji>electricity</kanji> you experience a <vocabulary>power outage</vocabulary>. Makes sense. When the electricity stops you don''t have any more power.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4586', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%9C%E9%9B%BB', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:58:38.320273Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.030484Z'),
(4587, 'vocabulary', '吸血鬼', '吸血鬼', 'A <kanji>demon</kanji> that <kanji>suck</kanji>s <kanji>blood</kanji>? What mythological creature can you think of that sucks blood? How about a <vocabulary>vampire</vocabulary>? Them Sparklzzzz!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4587', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%B8%E8%A1%80%E9%AC%BC', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:58:46.70484Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.855463Z'),
(4588, 'vocabulary', '吸収', '吸収', 'You <kanji>suck</kanji> something in and <kanji>obtain</kanji> it. Shhheewwwwww! When you suck something in and obtain it into your body, you have done <vocabulary>absorption</vocabulary> because it''s been sucked inside of you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4588', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%B8%E5%8F%8E', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:58:55.748134Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.882561Z'),
(4589, 'vocabulary', '領収書', '領収書', 'I <kanji>obtain</kanji> some <kanji>territory</kanji>, so I want a <kanji>writte</kanji>n record of it. How will I get a written record of my obtained territory? How about with a <vocabulary>receipt</vocabulary>? Emailing one will do just fine.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4589', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%98%E5%8F%8E%E6%9B%B8', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:59:04.873124Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.906555Z'),
(4590, 'vocabulary', '収入', '収入', 'Please <kanji>enter</kanji> what you''ve <kanji>obtain</kanji>ed in here. We''ll divvy it all up later. But we have to enter it all here so we know our <vocabulary>income</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4590', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8E%E5%85%A5', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:59:11.837102Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.92858Z'),
(4591, 'vocabulary', '年賀状', '年賀状', 'A <kanji>year</kanji> <kanji>congratulation</kanji>s <kanji>condition</kanji> is when you tell the world about your condition for the new year. What''s a better way to tell them than by using a <vocabulary>New Year''s Card</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4591', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E8%B3%80%E7%8A%B6', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T02:59:17.185971Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T02:02:19.646561Z'),
(4592, 'vocabulary', '革', '革', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4592', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%A9', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:59:24.861059Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:53.978416Z'),
(4593, 'vocabulary', '革命', '革命', '<kanji>Leather</kanji>''s <kanji>fate</kanji> is in the hands of a <vocabulary>revolution</vocabulary>. It''s the leather revolution! Off with these normal clothes and on with leather ones!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4593', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%A9%E5%91%BD', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:59:29.973791Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.001297Z'),
(4594, 'vocabulary', '皮革', '皮革', '<kanji>Skin</kanji> <kanji>leather</kanji> is really just <vocabulary>leather</vocabulary> because leather is also skin. It''s also <vocabulary>hides</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4594', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%AE%E9%9D%A9', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:59:49.415952Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:30:12.871593Z'),
(4595, 'vocabulary', '無職', '無職', 'When the status of your <kanji>employment</kanji> is "<kanji>nothing</kanji>" then you''re probably <vocabulary>unemployed</vocabulary>. You''re experiencing <vocabulary>unemployment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4595', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E8%81%B7', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T02:59:54.696244Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.049692Z'),
(4596, 'vocabulary', '職場', '職場', 'Your <kanji>employment</kanji> <kanji>location</kanji> is your <vocabulary>place of work</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>place of employment</vocabulary>. It''s also your <vocabulary>workplace</vocabulary>.

Are you using WaniKani at your 職場 right now? Wouldn''t that be funny.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4596', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%81%B7%E5%A0%B4', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:00:00.96788Z', NULL, '2025-12-12T19:57:28.977215Z'),
(4597, 'vocabulary', '職業', '職業', 'Your <kanji>employment</kanji> <kanji>business</kanji> is your <vocabulary>occupation</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>profession</vocabulary>. What is your employment business?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4597', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%81%B7%E6%A5%AD', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:00:05.681666Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(4598, 'vocabulary', '職員', '職員', 'The <kanji>employment</kanji> <kanji>member</kanji>s of a workplace are the <vocabulary>personnel</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>staff</vocabulary>. Pretty straightforward!

職員 and 社員 are two similar terms, but have different meanings. 社員 generally refers to employees of private companies, while 職員 refers to employees who work in various fields such as civil service, education, government, non-profits, and more.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4598', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%81%B7%E5%93%A1', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:00:13.676967Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:18:26.29811Z'),
(4599, 'vocabulary', '有職', '有職', 'To <kanji>have</kanji> <kanji>employment</kanji> just means <vocabulary>having a job</vocabulary>. Wow, congrats on <vocabulary>being employed</vocabulary>!

有職 is a formal word for saying that someone has a steady job, as opposed to 無職 (unemployed). You''ll often see it in official writing like surveys or articles.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4599', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%89%E8%81%B7', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:00:22.4604Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.165086Z'),
(4600, 'vocabulary', '求職', '求職', 'Ugh, time to <kanji>request</kanji> <kanji>employment</kanji> again. Time to go <vocabulary>job hunting</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4600', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%82%E8%81%B7', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:00:56.460887Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:53:56.46731Z'),
(4601, 'vocabulary', '辞職', '辞職', 'If you <kanji>quit</kanji> your place of <kanji>employment</kanji>, you are putting in your <vocabulary>resignation</vocabulary>, aka <vocabulary>resigning</vocabulary>.

辞職 is a formal term that refers to someone voluntarily leaving their job or post in professional settings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4601', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%9E%E8%81%B7', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:01:03.856167Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:55:30.726156Z'),
(4602, 'vocabulary', '鬼', '鬼', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4602', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AC%BC', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:01:11.213289Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T07:35:44.327819Z'),
(4603, 'vocabulary', '規則', '規則', 'A <kanji>standard</kanji> <kanji>rule</kanji> is just one of the <vocabulary>rule</vocabulary>s or <vocabulary>regulation</vocabulary>s. Nothing weird about these rules.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4603', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8F%E5%89%87', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:01:24.195028Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.264785Z'),
(4604, 'vocabulary', '規律', '規律', 'A <kanji>standard</kanji> <kanji>law</kanji> is the <vocabulary>rule</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>law</vocabulary> you have to follow. With these standard laws comes <vocabulary>discipline</vocabulary>. If you don''t have these it will be chaos!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4604', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8F%E5%BE%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T03:01:34.195958Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.297962Z'),
(4605, 'vocabulary', '看護師', '看護師', 'An <kanji>expert</kanji> who <kanji>watches over</kanji> and <kanji>defend</kanji>s people at all hours is the <vocabulary>nurse</vocabulary>. That''s what nurses do, 24/7. They have to check up on people, defend their health, and watch over them, making sure they''re okay. What a tough job.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4605', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%8B%E8%AD%B7%E5%B8%AB', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:01:40.626282Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T08:18:25.050242Z'),
(4606, 'vocabulary', '弁護士', '弁護士', 'A <kanji>samurai</kanji> who uses <kanji>speech</kanji> to <kanji>defend</kanji> people. This samurai does their battle in the court of law. This "samurai" is an <vocabulary>attorney</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>lawyer</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4606', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%81%E8%AD%B7%E5%A3%AB', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:01:48.632342Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T08:18:25.050242Z'),
(4607, 'vocabulary', '保護する', '保護する', 'To <kanji>preserve</kanji> and <kanji>defend</kanji> something is <vocabulary>to conserve</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to protect</vocabulary>. I won''t let you destroy this land I''m trying <vocabulary>to protect</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4607', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%9D%E8%AD%B7%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:01:56.06872Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T08:18:25.050242Z'),
(4608, 'vocabulary', '教師', '教師', 'A <kanji>teach</kanji>ing <kanji>expert</kanji>/<kanji>teacher</kanji> is a <vocabulary>teacher</vocabulary>.

教師 is a relatively formal word for teachers. Unlike 先生, this word is specifically for people in the teaching profession and cannot be used as a name ender.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4608', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%99%E5%B8%AB', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:02:10.952949Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.393915Z'),
(4609, 'vocabulary', '医師', '医師', 'A <kanji>medicine</kanji> <kanji>expert</kanji> is someone who knows a lot about medicine. Those people are (I hope) generally <vocabulary>doctor</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4609', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%BB%E5%B8%AB', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:02:20.363112Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.425239Z'),
(4610, 'vocabulary', '法律', '法律', 'Though there''s no law about two kanji with the same meaning producing the same meaning, a <kanji>law</kanji> <kanji>law</kanji> is probably a <vocabulary>law</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4610', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%95%E5%BE%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:02:30.106926Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.449902Z'),
(4611, 'vocabulary', '律動', '律動', 'The <kanji>law</kanji> of <kanji>move</kanji>ment is governed by <vocabulary>rhythm</vocabulary>. Imagine moving around to a boss beat. As you move, you start to feel the rhythm in your step. Go ahead and walk around your room and feel this rhythm. 

Note that リズム is a much more common word to use for "rhythm," but you may see the kanji version sometimes in formal writing. One thing you will see more often is 律動的 which means "rhythmic."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4611', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%8B%E5%8B%95', 50, 1, '2012-12-06T03:02:35.861024Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.472129Z'),
(4612, 'vocabulary', '不文律', '不文律', 'This <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>writing</kanji> <kanji>law</kanji> is a law that hasn''t been written down. It''s an <vocabulary>unwritten law</vocabulary>, like taking your shoes off and showering every day.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4612', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%96%87%E5%BE%8B', 57, 1, '2012-12-06T03:02:40.8969Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.483482Z'),
(4613, 'vocabulary', '割る', '割る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>divide</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to divide</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to split something</vocabulary>.

It can also mean <vocabulary>to break something</vocabulary>, as in ワイングラスを割る (to break a wine glass). After all, you’re technically dividing the object into parts if you break it, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4613', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%B2%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:02:45.613862Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:40:20.776264Z'),
(4614, 'vocabulary', '役割', '役割', 'Let''s <kanji>divide</kanji> the <kanji>duty</kanji> up amongst various people. Each person will have their own <vocabulary>role</vocabulary> in completing this duty.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4614', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%B9%E5%89%B2', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:05:22.761002Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:40:20.776264Z'),
(4615, 'vocabulary', '一割', '一割', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>division</kanji> is <vocabulary>ten percent</vocabulary>. You''ll just have to imagine this in terms of tens going by. Must be some old Japanese percentage system that carried over or something. Just remember that one wari is ten percent, two waris are twenty percent, and so on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4615', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%89%B2', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:05:31.110879Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:40:20.776264Z'),
(4616, 'vocabulary', '割合', '割合', 'When you <kanji>divide</kanji> something into parts and <kanji>join</kanji> them back together, you are better able to evaluate the <vocabulary>ratio</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>percentage</vocabulary> of these parts in relation to the whole. Think of all these parts, neatly separated and then placed next to one another. It''s only because you divided them that you get to see them this way.

割合 usually refers to the ratio of a certain part when compared to the whole, often expressed as a percentage. When used this way it''s similar to 比率, but 割合 is more common in everyday language.

Note that 割合 can also be used as an adverb, where it means <vocabulary>comparatively</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>fairly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4616', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%B2%E5%90%88', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:05:37.336178Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:40:20.776264Z'),
(4617, 'vocabulary', '裁く', '裁く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>judge</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to judge</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4617', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%81%E3%81%8F', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:05:42.35088Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.615325Z'),
(4618, 'vocabulary', '裁判', '裁判', 'You''re going to get <kanji>judge</kanji>d times two. <kanji>Judge</kanji> and <kanji>judge</kanji> again. Once by the judge and another time by the jury. This is a whole <vocabulary>trial</vocabulary> here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4618', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%81%E5%88%A4', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:05:48.51355Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T06:26:42.512459Z'),
(4619, 'vocabulary', '崎', '崎', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

In modern Japanese, this word is not commonly used as a standalone term. However, it is often found as part of proper nouns that refer to geographic locations that have a cape, as well as family names that are associated with capes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4619', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B4%8E', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:05:55.931983Z', NULL, '2025-11-30T19:10:22.678433Z'),
(4620, 'vocabulary', '川崎', '川崎', 'This is one of those words that''s easier to know if you jump to the reading first. Why? Because it''s the name of a city. It''s <vocabulary>Kawasaki</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4620', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%9D%E5%B4%8E', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:06:02.404106Z', NULL, '2025-11-30T19:10:22.678433Z'),
(4621, 'vocabulary', '長崎', '長崎', 'This word will be easier if you jump to the reading, since it''s the name of a place, <vocabulary>Nagasaki</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4621', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%95%B7%E5%B4%8E', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:06:08.658941Z', NULL, '2025-11-30T19:10:22.678433Z'),
(4622, 'vocabulary', '基準', '基準', 'The <kanji>foundation</kanji> of your <kanji>standard</kanji> is where you place your <vocabulary>standard</vocabulary>. No more, no less. This foundation is where the standard is all based off of.

This word generally refers to a quantitative "standard" that’s a basis for evaluation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4622', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%BA%E6%BA%96', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:06:20.175435Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.729145Z'),
(4623, 'vocabulary', '反則', '反則', 'An <kanji>anti</kanji> <kanji>rule</kanji> is when you break the rules. It''s when you do a <vocabulary>foul</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4623', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8D%E5%89%87', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:06:35.47418Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:30:02.229578Z'),
(4624, 'vocabulary', '法則', '法則', 'It''s both a <kanji>law</kanji> and a <kanji>rule</kanji>. This usually refers to something that is a <vocabulary>physical law</vocabulary>, but also can simply be a <vocabulary>law</vocabulary>, too. Anyways, lots of rules. Lots of laws. It''s both, so the stronger one (law) takes precedence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4624', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%95%E5%89%87', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:06:43.146237Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.775999Z'),
(4625, 'vocabulary', '備える', '備える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>provide</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to provide</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4625', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%99%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:06:49.206438Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:05:59.494452Z'),
(4626, 'vocabulary', '準備', '準備', 'When you follow <kanji>standard</kanji> procedure to <kanji>provide</kanji> what''s needed, you make the perfect <vocabulary>preparation</vocabulary>!

Both 準備 and 用意 mean "preparation," but the nuances are different. 準備 is about preparing in advance for an event or situation, often involving planning or setup. 用意 is about making sure specific items or resources are ready and available.

For example, as part of the 準備 for a party, you might 用意 food and drinks for the guests. You could use 準備 for the food and drinks too — it would just emphasize that they''re part of the <em>overall</em> event setup.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4626', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%96%E5%82%99', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:06:55.295632Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:05:59.494452Z'),
(4627, 'vocabulary', '導く', '導く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>lead</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to lead</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4627', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8E%E3%81%8F', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:07:00.667692Z', NULL, '2025-11-30T19:27:58.20826Z'),
(4628, 'vocabulary', '指導', '指導', 'Take your <kanji>finger</kanji> and <kanji>lead</kanji> with it. Just point at things. Tell people where they need to go, what they need to do, all with your finger leading them. This is <vocabulary>guidance</vocabulary> you are providing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4628', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87%E5%B0%8E', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:07:13.184788Z', NULL, '2025-11-30T19:27:58.20826Z'),
(4629, 'vocabulary', '幹', '幹', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4629', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B9', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:07:18.906573Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.903053Z'),
(4630, 'vocabulary', '新幹線', '新幹線', 'This is one of those words that makes more sense when you look at the reading first. Technically this is the "New Tree Trunk Line" but you wouldn''t realize this is the Japanese <vocabulary>bullet train</vocabulary> aka <vocabulary>Shinkansen</vocabulary> from that, would you? Maybe going to the reading will help you after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4630', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E5%B9%B9%E7%B7%9A', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:07:32.902753Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:30:42.218788Z'),
(4631, 'vocabulary', '張る', '張る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>stretch</kanji>, and the verb version means <vocabulary>to stretch over something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to spread out</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to be taut</vocabulary>, not to be confused with the type of stretching you do after a workout.

This word is both transitive and intransitive. When it follows を, it emphasizes the physical act of extending something over an area, like stretching a canvas over a frame, unfurling a sail on a ship, or roping off a crime scene. When following が, it expresses the resulting state of having been stretched or spread, like a bloated stomach, a swollen muscle, or a spider web.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4631', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%B5%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:08:27.938713Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:04:47.110966Z'),
(4632, 'vocabulary', '優しい', '優しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>gentle</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>gentle</vocabulary> (but also <vocabulary>kind</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>warm hearted</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4632', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%84%AA%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:08:41.345837Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:54.978297Z'),
(4633, 'vocabulary', '男優', '男優', 'A <kanji>man</kanji> who is <kanji>superior</kanji> to us is an <vocabulary>actor</vocabulary>, or more specifically a <vocabulary>male actor</vocabulary>.

Just a word of warning: over the years this word has taken on the connotation of "adult film actor" so most people would use 俳優 for "actor" now. You''ll be learning that word very soon!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4633', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B7%E5%84%AA', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:08:46.098071Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.003107Z'),
(4634, 'vocabulary', '優先', '優先', 'It was <kanji>superior</kanji> <kanji>previous</kanji>ly, so we need to go back there. That, amongst all the other things, is our highest <vocabulary>priority</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4634', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%84%AA%E5%85%88', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:08:47.164221Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:27:12.714404Z'),
(4635, 'vocabulary', '優勝', '優勝', 'The <kanji>superior</kanji> person <kanji>win</kanji>s. They will win the <vocabulary>championship</vocabulary> and be able to claim <vocabulary>victory</vocabulary> over all others.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4635', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%84%AA%E5%8B%9D', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:09:00.272934Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.053054Z'),
(4636, 'vocabulary', '〜宅', '〜宅', 'Just like its kanji, this word means <vocabulary>house</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>home</vocabulary>. By extension, it also sometimes means <vocabulary>household</vocabulary>. It can go after someone’s name to say whose house it is, as in 山田宅 (Yamada’s house), and after words that give more information about the house, such as 女性宅 (women’s homes).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4636', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%AE%85', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:09:12.10249Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:35:23.672471Z'),
(4637, 'vocabulary', '自宅', '自宅', 'Your <kanji>self</kanji> <kanji>home</kanji> is your own home. It''s <vocabulary>one''s home</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4637', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E5%AE%85', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:09:30.697412Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:35:23.672471Z'),
(4638, 'vocabulary', '住宅', '住宅', 'If you <kanji>dwell</kanji> in a <kanji>house</kanji>, that house is your <vocabulary>dwelling</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>residence</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4638', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%8F%E5%AE%85', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:10:01.636571Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:35:23.672471Z'),
(4639, 'vocabulary', '沢', '沢', 'The kanji and the word share the meaning of <vocabulary>swamp</vocabulary>, but this word is primarily used to mean <vocabulary>mountain stream</vocabulary>. Think of it this way, the water for a swamp has to come from somewhere right? It starts out up in the mountains and drains out into a swamp in the valley. The life cycle of a mountain stream into a swamp!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4639', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%A2', 33, 1, '2012-12-06T03:10:20.752071Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.492262Z'),
(4640, 'vocabulary', '贅沢', '贅沢', 'You live in the most <kanji>extravagant</kanji> of <kanji>swamp</kanji>s, which means you’re completely surrounded by <vocabulary>luxury</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>extravagant</vocabulary> things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4640', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B4%85%E6%B2%A2', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:10:31.0486Z', NULL, '2025-12-23T02:18:51.437316Z'),
(4641, 'vocabulary', '施行', '施行', 'If you <kanji>carry out</kanji> something and <kanji>go</kanji>, the result is still <vocabulary>enforcement</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>carrying out</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>implementation</vocabulary> of whatever it is. There’s no point hanging around after you’ve enforced something! So you always carry out and go.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4641', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%BD%E8%A1%8C', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:11:16.827368Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.200684Z'),
(4642, 'vocabulary', '現れる', '現れる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji means <kanji>appear</kanji>, so this word means <vocabulary>to appear</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to emerge</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4642', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%BE%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:11:29.545465Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.230124Z'),
(4643, 'vocabulary', '表現', '表現', 'When you <kanji>express</kanji> something that''s of the <kanji>present time</kanji>, you are saying something that only people of the current time understand. These are <vocabulary>expression</vocabulary>s or <vocabulary>figure of speech</vocabulary>. You say these things during the present time, not the future, not the past, the present time. Otherwise nobody would understand.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4643', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%A8%E7%8F%BE', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:11:43.219292Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:26:46.193094Z'),
(4644, 'vocabulary', '現実', '現実', 'The <kanji>present</kanji> is the <kanji>truth</kanji>. It is what''s going on right now. It is <vocabulary>reality</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4644', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%BE%E5%AE%9F', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:11:57.697533Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.281331Z'),
(4645, 'vocabulary', '現場', '現場', 'Your <kanji>present</kanji> <kanji>location</kanji> is the <vocabulary>on-site location</vocabulary>! And you''re on the <vocabulary>scene</vocabulary> to check out what''s currently going on.

現場 refers to an on-site location where something is happening or has happened. It can refer to a crime scene, an accident site, or a filming location. It may also refer to a construction site or a factory, as these are <vocabulary>workplace</vocabulary>s where more hands-on work or operations take place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4645', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%BE%E5%A0%B4', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:12:07.232441Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:45:13.006847Z'),
(4646, 'vocabulary', '牛乳', '牛乳', '<kanji>Cow</kanji> <kanji>milk</kanji> is <vocabulary>cow''s milk</vocabulary>, though most people just call cow''s milk <vocabulary>milk</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4646', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%9B%E4%B9%B3', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:12:23.410033Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.329314Z'),
(4647, 'vocabulary', '乳首', '乳首', 'What is a <kanji>milk</kanji> <kanji>neck</kanji>? Think about where milk comes from. Now, think of what looks like a little neck on the thing that milk comes from. What is that thing? It''s a <vocabulary>nipple</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4647', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%B3%E9%A6%96', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:12:30.293848Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:13.664937Z'),
(4648, 'vocabulary', '呼ぶ', '呼ぶ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>call</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to call</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to call out</vocabulary>. As in "HEY, YOU!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4648', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%BC%E3%81%B6', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:12:39.234725Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.390034Z'),
(4649, 'vocabulary', '城', '城', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4649', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%8E', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:13:17.23235Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.414407Z'),
(4650, 'vocabulary', '俳優', '俳優', 'A <kanji>superior</kanji> <kanji>haiku</kanji> requires someone really famous and well known to read it... it''s just on that level. Who is super well known and who deserves to read this superior haiku? A great <vocabulary>actor</vocabulary>, perhaps?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4650', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%B3%E5%84%AA', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:13:25.355373Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.439712Z'),
(4651, 'vocabulary', '俳句', '俳句', 'A <kanji>haiku</kanji> <kanji>paragraph</kanji> is just a <vocabulary>haiku</vocabulary>. Haikus are little haiku paragraphs.

You will learn kanji
The Crabigator watches
He feeds on reviews', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4651', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%B3%E5%8F%A5', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:13:35.910623Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.465239Z'),
(4652, 'vocabulary', '秀でる', '秀でる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>excel</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to excel</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4652', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%80%E3%81%A7%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:13:49.939288Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.487906Z'),
(4653, 'vocabulary', '優秀', '優秀', 'Something that''s <kanji>superior</kanji> will <kanji>excel</kanji>. It is <vocabulary>superior</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>excellent</vocabulary> in every way.

This word is typically used to describe someone''s superiority or excellence, often in comparison to others.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4653', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%84%AA%E7%A7%80', 23, 1, '2012-12-06T03:14:03.11281Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.510773Z'),
(4654, 'vocabulary', '違い', '違い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you''d think it''s probably an adjective. However in this case, it''s a noun. What''s the noun form of <kanji>different</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>difference</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4654', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%95%E3%81%84', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:05:36.726746Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.531755Z'),
(4655, 'vocabulary', '違う', '違う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>different</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be different</vocabulary>... and of course, to be different is also <vocabulary>to be wrong</vocabulary> in Japan. Don''t be different!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4655', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%95%E3%81%86', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:05:50.456657Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.55416Z'),
(4656, 'vocabulary', '可燃ゴミ', '可燃ゴミ', '<kanji>Possible</kanji> to <kanji>burn</kanji> ゴミ (garbage). This is <vocabulary>burnable garbage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4656', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%AF%E7%87%83%E3%82%B4%E3%83%9F', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:06:17.394303Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.576894Z'),
(4657, 'vocabulary', '営業する', '営業する', 'The word 営業 is <kanji>business</kanji>. The verb version of that (to do a business) becomes <vocabulary>to run a business</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4657', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%B6%E6%A5%AD%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:06:24.943933Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(4658, 'vocabulary', '伝統的', '伝統的', 'If 伝統 is "tradition," then adding a 的 (<kanji>-al</kanji>) to it would make it <vocabulary>traditional</vocabulary>.
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4658', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9D%E7%B5%B1%E7%9A%84', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:06:32.515219Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.623506Z'),
(4659, 'vocabulary', '誕生日', '誕生日', 'Remember how 誕生 is <kanji>birth</kanji>? If you add <kanji>day</kanji> on there, you have a <vocabulary>birthday</vocabulary>. Happy birthday!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4659', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%95%E7%94%9F%E6%97%A5', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:06:40.623047Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.645239Z'),
(4660, 'vocabulary', '寝坊する', '寝坊する', '寝坊 is <kanji>oversleep</kanji>. Make that into a verb and you have <vocabulary>to oversleep</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4660', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%9D%E5%9D%8A%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:06:47.943728Z', NULL, '2025-06-25T14:00:39.611736Z'),
(4661, 'vocabulary', '過去形', '過去形', 'The <kanji>past</kanji> <kanji>shape</kanji> is how things were in the past. To describe those things (like you would with an adjective, aka 形容詞), you''d use <vocabulary>past tense</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4661', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8E%E5%8E%BB%E5%BD%A2', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:07:06.575322Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:21:59.859898Z'),
(4662, 'vocabulary', '過ぎ', '過ぎ', '過ぎる is <vocabulary>to pass</vocabulary>. When you pass what is needed, what are you left with? Just this word: <vocabulary>too much</vocabulary>. This can also mean <vocabulary>after</vocabulary>, as in "after 5pm", and <vocabulary>past</vocabulary>, as in "past sixty".', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4662', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8E%E3%81%8E', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:07:13.120739Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.720175Z'),
(4663, 'vocabulary', '対策', '対策', 'Your <kanji>versus</kanji> <kanji>plan</kanji> is to attack back. They''ve already attacked you once, so now it''s time for your <vocabulary>counter plan</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4663', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BE%E7%AD%96', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:07:20.555091Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:12:11.505385Z'),
(4664, 'vocabulary', '価値観', '価値観', 'These <kanji>view</kanji>s that have <kanji>merit</kanji> (価値) are your <vocabulary>values</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4664', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%A1%E5%80%A4%E8%A6%B3', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:07:26.425584Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:51:00.759578Z'),
(4665, 'vocabulary', '応援団', '応援団', 'A <kanji>group</kanji> that <kanji>roots for</kanji> you is your <vocabulary>rooting section</vocabulary> or <vocabulary> cheer squad</vocabulary>. Go go go you!

応援団 tend to wear black school uniforms and support their team with lively chants and energetic performances. They can also have cheerleaders with pom-poms, but those are usually just called チアリーダー.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4665', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%9C%E6%8F%B4%E5%9B%A3', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:07:35.912274Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.791562Z'),
(4666, 'vocabulary', '規準', '規準', 'The <kanji>standard</kanji> of <kanji>standard</kanji>s is the main <vocabulary>criterion</vocabulary> we work off of. That’s the <vocabulary>standard</vocabulary> we follow.

Compared to 基準 that''s often a quantitative "standard" for assessment, 規準 is more of a quality-focused "criterion" that can be a basis for goals. You''ll often see this word used in academic or professional contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4666', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8F%E6%BA%96', 28, 1, '2012-12-06T04:07:43.040963Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.81645Z'),
(4667, 'vocabulary', '法規', '法規', '<kanji>Law</kanji>s are <kanji>standard</kanji> in society. You must have <vocabulary>laws</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>regulations</vocabulary> in society.

法規 is a formal term commonly used in legal and bureaucratic contexts. In everyday conversation, people are more likely to use simpler terms, such as 法律 for laws or 規則 for rules and regulations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4667', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%95%E8%A6%8F', 51, 1, '2012-12-06T04:07:50.026679Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.842165Z'),
(4668, 'vocabulary', '担ぐ', '担ぐ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>carry</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to carry</vocabulary>. This has to do with carrying something literal, unlike 担う which is not necessarily literal (you''ll learn it later). And in this case, you carry it on your shoulder(s). You can remember that it''s literal from the reading mnemonic, which has you carrying literal cats on your shoulders.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4668', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%85%E3%81%90', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:08:10.022917Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.86644Z'),
(4669, 'vocabulary', '担当', '担当', 'You <kanji>carry</kanji> everyone else and you are always <kanji>right</kanji>. With these traits, you are <vocabulary>in charge</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4669', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%85%E5%BD%93', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:08:18.606626Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:38:04.319615Z'),
(4670, 'vocabulary', '負担', '負担', 'You <kanji>lose</kanji>. Because of that, you have to <kanji>carry</kanji> this <vocabulary>burden</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>responsibility</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4670', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A0%E6%8B%85', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:08:27.237495Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.917716Z'),
(4671, 'vocabulary', '半額', '半額', 'A <kanji>half</kanji> <kanji>amount</kanji> sale is when things are <vocabulary>half price</vocabulary>. Cheap!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4671', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%8A%E9%A1%8D', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:08:34.07573Z', NULL, '2025-12-11T20:12:42.646308Z'),
(4672, 'vocabulary', '多額', '多額', 'A <kanji>many</kanji> <kanji>amount</kanji> is a <vocabulary>large amount</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>large sum</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4672', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%9A%E9%A1%8D', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:08:40.284222Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.96242Z'),
(4673, 'vocabulary', '年額', '年額', 'The <kanji>year</kanji> <kanji>amount</kanji> is the <vocabulary>yearly amount</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4673', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E9%A1%8D', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:08:48.517308Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:55.98532Z'),
(4674, 'vocabulary', '月額', '月額', 'A <kanji>month</kanji>''s <kanji>amount</kanji> is the <vocabulary>monthly amount</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4674', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%88%E9%A1%8D', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:08:55.884361Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.014445Z'),
(4675, 'vocabulary', '定額', '定額', 'A <kanji>determine</kanji>d <kanji>amount</kanji> is an amount that is fixed, aka a <vocabulary>fixed amount</vocabulary>, a <vocabulary>fixed sum</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>flat rate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4675', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9A%E9%A1%8D', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:09:01.809229Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(4676, 'vocabulary', '残額', '残額', 'The <kanji>remainder</kanji> <kanji>amount</kanji> is the <vocabulary>remaining amount</vocabulary>, aka the <vocabulary>balance</vocabulary>.

For example, you will see this word used when displaying the balance of a prepaid card or e-money, like an IC card.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4676', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%8B%E9%A1%8D', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:09:07.544901Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.065637Z'),
(4677, 'vocabulary', '製品', '製品', '<kanji>Manufacture</kanji>d <kanji>goods</kanji> are <vocabulary>manufactured goods</vocabulary>. What does that end up being? A <vocabulary>product</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4677', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%BD%E5%93%81', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:09:12.949217Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(4678, 'vocabulary', '製作', '製作', 'A place where you <kanji>manufacture</kanji> and <kanji>make</kanji> is a place where <vocabulary>manufacturing</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>production</vocabulary> are done. So many things getting made.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4678', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%BD%E4%BD%9C', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:09:18.781572Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:12:39.703958Z'),
(4679, 'vocabulary', '木製', '木製', '<kanji>Tree</kanji> <kanji>manufacture</kanji>d items are <vocabulary>wooden</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>made from wood</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4679', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%A8%E8%A3%BD', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:09:29.270819Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.139573Z'),
(4680, 'vocabulary', '日本製', '日本製', 'Things that are <kanji>manufacture</kanji>d in <kanji>Japan</kanji> (日本) are <vocabulary>made in Japan</vocabulary>. Good quality stuff, that is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4680', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E8%A3%BD', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:09:34.898986Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.164225Z'),
(4681, 'vocabulary', '中国製', '中国製', 'China (中国) <kanji>manufacture</kanji>d items are <vocabulary>made in China</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4681', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E5%9B%BD%E8%A3%BD', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:09:43.777752Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.186048Z'),
(4682, 'vocabulary', 'アメリカ製', 'アメリカ製', '<kanji>America</kanji> <kanji>manufacture</kanji>d is something that is <vocabulary>made in USA</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>made in America</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4682', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%A2%E3%83%A1%E3%83%AA%E3%82%AB%E8%A3%BD', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:09:50.801025Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.21021Z'),
(4683, 'vocabulary', '自家製', '自家製', 'By my <kanji>self</kanji> at <kanji>home</kanji> I <kanji>manufacture</kanji>d this. It is <vocabulary>homemade</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4683', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E5%AE%B6%E8%A3%BD', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:09:56.916872Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.231302Z'),
(4684, 'vocabulary', '輸出', '輸出', 'When you <kanji>transport</kanji> things and they <kanji>exit</kanji> from your country, you are <vocabulary>export</vocabulary>ing them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4684', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%B8%E5%87%BA', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:10:08.616696Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.287767Z'),
(4685, 'vocabulary', '輸血', '輸血', '<kanji>Transport</kanji> of <kanji>blood</kanji>. Only time you do this is during a <vocabulary>blood transfusion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4685', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%B8%E8%A1%80', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:10:17.195727Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.312901Z'),
(4686, 'vocabulary', '不燃ゴミ', '不燃ゴミ', 'These are <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>burn</kanji>able garbages (ごみ). Pay attention to this on signs when you throw things away. You want to put the right things into <vocabulary>nonburnable garbage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4686', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E7%87%83%E3%82%B4%E3%83%9F', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:10:48.14031Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.338723Z'),
(4687, 'vocabulary', '祝う', '祝う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>celebrate</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to celebrate</vocabulary>. When you burn this word, you should 祝う!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4687', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%9D%E3%81%86', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:10:54.758671Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:19:57.154316Z'),
(4688, 'vocabulary', '祝日', '祝日', 'A <kanji>celebrate</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji> is a day you get off to celebrate something awesome. More specifically, this is known as a <vocabulary>national holiday</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>public holiday</vocabulary>.

祝日 refers to officially recognized holidays people get off from school or (hopefully) work. In Japan, that''s stuff like New Year''s Day, the emperor''s birthday, and the Golden Week holidays, among others.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4688', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%9D%E6%97%A5', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:11:01.423214Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:24:52.288735Z'),
(4689, 'vocabulary', '届く', '届く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>deliver</kanji> so the verb vocab version here is <vocabulary>to be delivered</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to arrive</vocabulary>. You know this one is "to be delivered" (aka it''s just getting delivered, you aren''t doing the delivery) because when this item arrives at your house you say "<reading>coo''</reading>" (く), it arrived.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4689', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%8A%E3%81%8F', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:11:07.928883Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.424273Z'),
(4690, 'vocabulary', '狭い', '狭い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>narrow</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>narrow</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4690', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%AD%E3%81%84', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:11:19.797446Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.453248Z'),
(4691, 'vocabulary', '肩', '肩', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4691', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%A9', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:11:27.065941Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.477332Z'),
(4692, 'vocabulary', '腕', '腕', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4692', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%95', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:11:37.440152Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.502532Z'),
(4693, 'vocabulary', '腕時計', '腕時計', 'Remember how 時計 is <kanji>watch</kanji>? An <kanji>arm</kanji> is now there too, making this a <vocabulary>wristwatch</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4693', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%95%E6%99%82%E8%A8%88', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:11:45.354719Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.554585Z'),
(4694, 'vocabulary', '腰', '腰', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

腰 refers to the <vocabulary>waist</vocabulary> area around the top part of the hip bone — the part that sticks out when you put your hands on your hips. It includes the sides and the back as well, which is why this can also mean <vocabulary>lower back</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4694', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%B0', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:11:51.58657Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.575416Z'),
(4695, 'vocabulary', '触る', '触る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>touch</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to touch something</vocabulary>.

触る is used when someone does the touching. It''s not used to describe objects touching each other.

Note that 触る can be used with either the particle に or を, like 手に触る or 手を触る (to touch a hand). Using に simply marks the location you touch, while using を highlights your action of touching that specific area or object. So the former is intransitive and the latter is transitive, though there''s some debate about that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4695', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A6%E3%82%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:11:57.118881Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.605078Z'),
(4696, 'vocabulary', '触角', '触角', 'The <kanji>angle</kanji> of the <kanji>touch</kanji> is important with an <vocabulary>antenna</vocabulary>. Think about antennae. An ant''s antennae wiggle around and move in different angles, touching things, to understand the world around them. Imagine their tiny bug antennae touching your arm to figure out who you are.

Note that 触角 refers to the antennae of insects, crustaceans, and certain other arthropods, rather than to TV or radio antennas.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4696', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A6%E8%A7%92', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:12:10.995963Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T21:05:47.522155Z'),
(4697, 'vocabulary', '感触', '感触', 'Your <kanji>feeling</kanji> of <kanji>touch</kanji> is your <vocabulary>touch sense</vocabulary> (or simply just <vocabulary>touch</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4697', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E8%A7%A6', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:12:27.090029Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(4698, 'vocabulary', '所載', '所載', 'The <kanji>place</kanji> where the information is <kanji>put on</kanji> is where it has been <vocabulary>recorded</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>published</vocabulary>.

所載 is a formal term usually placed right after the name of a publication as a way to cite it, like ワニカニマガジン所載 (recorded in WaniKani Magazine). Be careful not to confuse this with similar words — 所載 specifically guides readers to the exact "locations" (所) where the content is recorded within a text.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4698', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%80%E8%BC%89', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:12:32.479286Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T18:14:16.673662Z'),
(4699, 'vocabulary', '記載', '記載', 'Please <kanji>write down</kanji> and <kanji>put on</kanji> paper the facts, so we have a <vocabulary>record</vocabulary> of them.

記載 is a relatively formal word for the act of recording, documenting, or noting information in writing. It can also refer to that recorded information itself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4699', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%98%E8%BC%89', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:12:42.415748Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T18:15:32.358294Z'),
(4700, 'vocabulary', '載る', '載る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. It''s also an intransitive verb, meaning the action happens on its own, you don''t do it. The kanji itself means <kanji>place on</kanji>, and this means <vocabulary>to be placed on</vocabulary>.

載る is also often used to refer to something being included in a written document or publication and so can also mean <vocabulary>to be recorded</vocabulary>, as in 新聞に載る (to be recorded in a newspaper) or 教科書に載る (to be recorded in a textbook).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4700', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%89%E3%82%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:15:59.962482Z', NULL, '2025-07-28T23:37:30.57133Z'),
(4701, 'vocabulary', '一層', '一層', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>layer</kanji> more? One whole layer? It must be <vocabulary>much more</vocabulary> important than we thought. Let''s pay <vocabulary>all the more</vocabulary> attention, in that case.

This word is often written in kana, but you''ll see it in kanji too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4701', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%B1%A4', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:16:06.930596Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.736873Z'),
(4702, 'vocabulary', '〜層', '〜層', 'This is a word that''s attached to other things indicating the <vocabulary>layer of</vocabulary> something (or just <vocabulary>layer</vocabulary>). It has essentially the same meaning as the kanji it comes from, <kanji>layer</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4702', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%B1%A4', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:16:11.835141Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.76175Z'),
(4703, 'vocabulary', '型', '型', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4703', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9E%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:16:17.18899Z', NULL, '2025-06-23T18:15:45.739789Z'),
(4704, 'vocabulary', '典型的', '典型的', 'You learned that 的 on the end of a word means “-al” or “-like”. So what is <kanji>rule</kanji> <kanji>model</kanji> <kanji>-like</kanji>? It’s a model that follows all the rules. It''s the normal model. It’s <vocabulary>typical</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4704', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B8%E5%9E%8B%E7%9A%84', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:16:22.451273Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.819688Z'),
(4705, 'vocabulary', '原型', '原型', 'The <kanji>original</kanji> <kanji>model</kanji> of something isn''t usually very good. It''s rough. It''s a <vocabulary>prototype</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4705', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%9F%E5%9E%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:16:28.174003Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.844375Z'),
(4706, 'vocabulary', '都庁', '都庁', 'The <kanji>metropolis</kanji> <kanji>agency</kanji> is the government that controls the metropolitan area. Their office is in the <vocabulary>metropolitan government building</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4706', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%BD%E5%BA%81', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:16:33.615345Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.871951Z'),
(4707, 'vocabulary', '警視庁', '警視庁', 'They <kanji>warn</kanji> people and they <kanji>look at</kanji> crime, and they even have an <kanji>agency</kanji>. Who is it? It''s Tokyo''s <vocabulary>metropolitan police department</vocabulary>. 

The 警 here is actually short for 警察 (police). The word 警視 itself used to refer to officials who oversee police operations, but now it''s mostly used in the 警視庁 agency name. Basically, this is the big agency that employs and oversees all the Tokyo metropolitan police!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4707', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%A6%E8%A6%96%E5%BA%81', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:16:38.31777Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T22:01:53.107114Z'),
(4708, 'vocabulary', '警察庁', '警察庁', 'Unlike the 警視庁, who look at things, this is just the <kanji>police officer</kanji> (警察) <kanji>agency</kanji>. Just the general police agency of the whole country, making them part of the <vocabulary>National Police Agency</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4708', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%A6%E5%AF%9F%E5%BA%81', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:16:43.203495Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.921537Z'),
(4709, 'vocabulary', '官庁', '官庁', 'A <kanji>government</kanji> <kanji>government office</kanji> is a <vocabulary>government office</vocabulary>. Did I mention government?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4709', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%98%E5%BA%81', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:16:47.671329Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:56.94709Z'),
(4710, 'vocabulary', '無視する', '無視する', 'I will <kanji>look at</kanji> <kanji>nothing</kanji>. I am going <vocabulary>to ignore</vocabulary> you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4710', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E8%A6%96%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:16:52.477157Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:26:59.946346Z'),
(4711, 'vocabulary', '視覚', '視覚', 'You <kanji>look at</kanji> things and <kanji>memorize</kanji> them via your sight, using your <vocabulary>sense of sight</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4711', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%96%E8%A6%9A', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:16:57.830143Z', NULL, '2026-03-15T02:58:38.696082Z'),
(4712, 'vocabulary', '近視', '近視', 'When you can <kanji>look at</kanji> things that are <kanji>near</kanji> well but you can''t see things far away, you have <vocabulary>nearsightedness</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>shortsightedness</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>myopia</vocabulary>. Good to get those eyes checked out occasionally.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4712', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E8%A6%96', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:17:02.483071Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:26:59.946346Z'),
(4713, 'vocabulary', '遠視', '遠視', 'When you can <kanji>look at</kanji> <kanji>far</kanji> things, but not near things, you have <vocabulary>farsightedness</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>longsightedness</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>hyperopia</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4713', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%A0%E8%A6%96', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:17:07.263061Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:26:59.946346Z'),
(4714, 'vocabulary', '差す', '差す', 'The kanji is <kanji>distinction</kanji>, and the verb version here means… a million things. Let''s just focus on a few common meanings. Things with distinction tend to stick out from the others. They tend <vocabulary>to shine</vocabulary>, you know? And if you imagine shining a beam of light on something, that light tends <vocabulary>to project</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to extend outward</vocabulary>. It sticks out, shines on something, then makes that thing more distinct too. 

That''s why you can say 光が差す (light shines), かげが差す (a shadow is cast), 指差す (to point at), or even カサを差す (to open and raise an umbrella). Basically, 差す usually has to do with causing something <vocabulary>to appear</vocabulary>, either by shining or projecting outward. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4714', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%AE%E3%81%99', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:17:11.743132Z', NULL, '2026-02-10T18:26:53.595375Z'),
(4715, 'vocabulary', '差別', '差別', 'You make a <kanji>distinction</kanji> between people and based off that you <kanji>separate</kanji> the people. This is terrible! Why so much <vocabulary>discrimination</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4715', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%AE%E5%88%A5', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:17:16.614411Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.09155Z'),
(4716, 'vocabulary', '交差点', '交差点', 'You <kanji>mix</kanji>, but there''s a <kanji>distinction</kanji> as to where you mix at this <kanji>point</kanji>. Where is this point? It can be found when two streets cross, at an <vocabulary>intersection</vocabulary>, also known as a <vocabulary>crossing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4716', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%A4%E5%B7%AE%E7%82%B9', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:17:21.546368Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.112857Z'),
(4717, 'vocabulary', '時差', '時差', 'The <kanji>time</kanji> <kanji>distinction</kanji> is how you tell apart time zones. It is the <vocabulary>time difference</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4717', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%82%E5%B7%AE', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:17:29.292231Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.135051Z'),
(4718, 'vocabulary', '管', '管', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4718', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AE%A1', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:17:33.847982Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:37:30.435096Z'),
(4719, 'vocabulary', '管理', '管理', 'The <kanji>pipe</kanji> of <kanji>reason</kanji>. Imagine someone holding this pipe. It always leads to the correct decision because it is the pipe of reason. Those who have this pipe are in a higher rank than you. They are the <vocabulary>management</vocabulary>, and by using this pipe of reason they''re able to make good management decisions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4719', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AE%A1%E7%90%86', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:17:40.886143Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.177299Z'),
(4720, 'vocabulary', '血管', '血管', 'The <kanji>pipe</kanji>s that hold your <kanji>blood</kanji> are your <vocabulary>blood vessel</vocabulary>s, or your <vocabulary>vein</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4720', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%80%E7%AE%A1', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:17:47.028914Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.19966Z'),
(4721, 'vocabulary', '象', '象', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4721', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B1%A1', 7, 1, '2012-12-06T04:18:00.37473Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.918972Z'),
(4722, 'vocabulary', '対象', '対象', 'The <kanji>versus</kanji> <kanji>phenomenon</kanji> is what you''re going up against. It''s your <vocabulary>target</vocabulary> because you''re heading towards this phenomenon you''re versusing against.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4722', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BE%E8%B1%A1', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:18:06.307757Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:12:11.505385Z'),
(4723, 'vocabulary', '現象', '現象', 'A <kanji>present time</kanji> <kanji>phenomenon</kanji> is a <vocabulary>phenomenon</vocabulary> whether it''s in the present or in the future or in the past.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4723', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%BE%E8%B1%A1', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:18:17.01785Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.266376Z'),
(4724, 'vocabulary', '気象', '気象', 'The <kanji>energy</kanji> <kanji>phenomenon</kanji>. That''s when energy is doing stuff in the sky and nobody understands it. That''s the <vocabulary>weather</vocabulary> for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4724', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E8%B1%A1', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:18:27.246999Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(4725, 'vocabulary', '量る', '量る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>quantity</kanji>, but you don''t "to quantity." To figure out the quantity, however, you have <vocabulary>to measure</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>to weigh</vocabulary> whatever it is you want to get the quantity of.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4725', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8F%E3%82%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:18:45.953336Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.31508Z'),
(4726, 'vocabulary', '量', '量', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4726', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8F', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:18:52.805597Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.339161Z'),
(4727, 'vocabulary', '大量', '大量', 'A <kanji>large</kanji> <kanji>quantity</kanji> is a <vocabulary>large quantity</vocabulary>. Kind of like the <vocabulary>large number</vocabulary> of words you''re learning here now. A <vocabulary>huge amount</vocabulary>. Well done you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4727', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E9%87%8F', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:18:58.276358Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(4728, 'vocabulary', '定量', '定量', 'A <kanji>determine</kanji>d <kanji>quantity</kanji> is a <vocabulary>fixed quantity</vocabulary>.

定量 is a formal word commonly used in academic, technical, financial, and business contexts for discussing research, experiments, or data analysis. You usually encounter it as 定量的 (quantitative) or 定量化 (quantification).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4728', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9A%E9%87%8F', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:19:06.318974Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(4729, 'vocabulary', '境', '境', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4729', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%83', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:19:11.762326Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:33:34.678262Z'),
(4730, 'vocabulary', '国境', '国境', 'A <kanji>country</kanji> <kanji>boundary</kanji> is that country''s <vocabulary>national border</vocabulary>. Be careful about crossing it if you''re carrying bananas. The border police seem to hate when you tell them you''re carrying a banana in your pants when they ask you if you''re bringing any fruits back. No idea why.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4730', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BD%E5%A2%83', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:19:16.939312Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:33:34.678262Z'),
(4731, 'vocabulary', '境界', '境界', 'The <kanji>boundary</kanji> separates <kanji>world</kanji>s. It is a <vocabulary>boundary</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4731', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%83%E7%95%8C', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:19:21.463783Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:33:34.678262Z'),
(4732, 'vocabulary', '環境', '環境', 'The <kanji>loop</kanji> that makes up the <kanji>boundary</kanji> of this area holds in the <vocabulary>environment</vocabulary>, at least this particular one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4732', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%92%B0%E5%A2%83', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:19:26.908262Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:33:34.678262Z'),
(4733, 'vocabulary', '武器', '武器', 'A <kanji>container</kanji> used by the <kanji>military</kanji> is probably going to be used as a <vocabulary>weapon</vocabulary>. After all, that''s what the military does... weapons.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4733', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A6%E5%99%A8', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:19:32.479363Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.514Z'),
(4734, 'vocabulary', '武道', '武道', 'If you take the <kanji>road</kanji> of the <kanji>military</kanji> you''re going to have to fight a lot. Back in the day, this involved learning <vocabulary>martial arts</vocabulary> to fight with. Remember, just wax off.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4734', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A6%E9%81%93', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:19:38.382872Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.535848Z'),
(4735, 'vocabulary', '武士', '武士', 'A <kanji>military</kanji> <kanji>samurai</kanji> is a <vocabulary>samurai</vocabulary>. This is how most people say "samurai" in Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4735', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A6%E5%A3%AB', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:19:43.423154Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.560022Z'),
(4736, 'vocabulary', '質', '質', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4736', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%AA', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:19:49.586104Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.585424Z'),
(4737, 'vocabulary', '質問', '質問', 'A <kanji>quality</kanji> <kanji>problem</kanji> is worth a <vocabulary>question</vocabulary>. A low quality problem however... don''t ask the question. There are stupid questions, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4737', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%AA%E5%95%8F', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:19:55.866544Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.610889Z'),
(4738, 'vocabulary', '品質', '品質', 'A <kanji>product</kanji>''s <kanji>quality</kanji> is in its <vocabulary>material quality</vocabulary>. If the materials aren''t any good then the product isn''t either.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4738', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%93%81%E8%B3%AA', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:20:03.34114Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(4739, 'vocabulary', '良質', '良質', '<kanji>Good</kanji> <kanji>quality</kanji> is always <vocabulary>good quality</vocabulary>. Don''t skimp!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4739', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%89%AF%E8%B3%AA', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:20:15.615952Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.65937Z'),
(4740, 'vocabulary', '特質', '特質', 'Your <kanji>special</kanji> <kanji>quality</kanji> is your <vocabulary>characteristic</vocabulary>s or your <vocabulary>trait</vocabulary>s.

This is a formal word that refers to special properties that only a certain thing possesses, like the properties of steel as a material, or a certain person’s nature.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4740', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%B9%E8%B3%AA', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:20:25.425442Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.686793Z'),
(4741, 'vocabulary', '述べる', '述べる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>mention</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to mention</vocabulary>.

This is a formal word often used in writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4741', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%B0%E3%81%B9%E3%82%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:20:32.529555Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.710267Z'),
(4742, 'vocabulary', '記述', '記述', 'When you <kanji>Write down</kanji> and <kanji>mention</kanji> what happened, you''re <vocabulary>writing down</vocabulary> a <vocabulary>description</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>account</vocabulary> of it. 

記述 simply means writing things down (whether by hand or by typing), usually to have an objective written record about something. 記述 can also refer to the text that''s been written.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4742', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%98%E8%BF%B0', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:20:38.231286Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.733701Z'),
(4743, 'vocabulary', '上述', '上述', '<kanji>Above</kanji> <kanji>mention</kanji>s are <vocabulary>above mentioned</vocabulary>. Look above to see where I mentioned it, okay?

Note that 上述 is a word commonly used in formal writing such as reports, academic papers, or business documents.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4743', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A%E8%BF%B0', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:20:44.117453Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(4744, 'vocabulary', '限界', '限界', 'As you reach the <kanji>limit</kanji> of your <kanji>world</kanji>, you face your <vocabulary>limit</vocabulary> and confront your <vocabulary>limitations</vocabulary>.

This word refers to the maximum extent or limit that something can reach or endure. It conveys the idea of reaching the utmost point beyond which a situation or condition cannot be sustained.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4744', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%90%E7%95%8C', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T04:20:50.372167Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:21:16.900712Z'),
(4745, 'vocabulary', '限定', '限定', 'A specific <kanji>limit</kanji> has been <kanji>determine</kanji>d. This is the <vocabulary>limit</vocabulary> of a certain thing, which can’t be exceeded.

This word means placing a particular limit or restriction on the quantity, scope, or availability of something for a special or exclusive purpose. This is often used for things like sales or products that are only available for a limited time or in restricted quantities.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4745', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%90%E5%AE%9A', 26, 1, '2012-12-06T04:20:57.537052Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(4746, 'vocabulary', '限度', '限度', 'A certain <kanji>limit</kanji>’s <kanji>degree</kanji> is how far it can go. It’s the <vocabulary>limit</vocabulary>; turn it up one more degree and it’s out of <vocabulary>bounds</vocabulary>. Keep it right where it is and it’s perfect.

This word refers to the maximum extent or range of something, beyond which it cannot be surpassed. While similar to 限界, 限度 often implies a predetermined acceptable or tolerable limit, while 限界 is more closely associated with an absolute or ultimate limit. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4746', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%90%E5%BA%A6', 28, 1, '2012-12-06T04:21:05.346754Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.831938Z'),
(4747, 'vocabulary', '限る', '限る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>limit</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to limit</vocabulary>. It is often used in the passive form 限られる, which means <vocabulary>to be limited</vocabulary>, implying that the limiting is being done by someone else.

This is a formal word used to define the limits of time, space, or quantity, or to set restrictions on things. It is also used casually when emphasizing something in particular or considering something to be the best. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4747', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%90%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T04:21:11.933289Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.867405Z'),
(4748, 'vocabulary', '時限', '時限', 'A <kanji>time</kanji> <kanji>limit</kanji> is a <vocabulary>time limit</vocabulary>, but not any old time limit. This is usually used at the beginning of compound words, especially 時限爆弾 (time bomb).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4748', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%82%E9%99%90', 38, 1, '2012-12-06T04:21:18.099351Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.895192Z'),
(4749, 'vocabulary', '期限', '期限', 'The <kanji>period of time</kanji> <kanji>limit</kanji> is the <vocabulary>deadline</vocabulary> for a certain thing. Alternatively, this can describe a <vocabulary>term</vocabulary> for something, like the repayment of debts, or some sort of <vocabulary>due date</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4749', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%9F%E9%99%90', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T04:21:27.38732Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.920069Z'),
(4750, 'vocabulary', '無限', '無限', 'The <kanji>limit</kanji> is <kanji>nothing</kanji>. What? No limit? That means it will never stop! It is <vocabulary>infinite</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4750', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E9%99%90', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T04:22:28.001517Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:57.94569Z'),
(4751, 'vocabulary', '提供', '提供', 'When you <kanji>present</kanji> something and <kanji>offer</kanji> it to someone, you''re kindly <vocabulary>offering</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>providing</vocabulary> it to them.

提供 is a relatively formal word that refers to offering or providing something you have for someone else''s benefit — like giving resources, services, funds, or information.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4751', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%90%E4%BE%9B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:22:32.743626Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:06:42.844886Z'),
(4752, 'vocabulary', '供える', '供える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>offer</kanji>, and this verb version means <vocabulary>to make an offering</vocabulary>.

供える means <vocabulary>to offer</vocabulary> something, usually to a god, Buddha, or someone who has passed away. For example, you might 花を供える (offer flowers) at a grave or altar.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4752', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%9B%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:22:38.585672Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:06:42.844886Z'),
(4753, 'vocabulary', '子供', '子供', 'A <kanji>child</kanji> often has to <kanji>accompany</kanji> someone else when they go places, because they''re… a <vocabulary>child</vocabulary>. Can''t have all those kids running around unaccompanied, can we?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4753', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%90%E4%BE%9B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:22:44.223456Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:06:42.844886Z'),
(4754, 'vocabulary', '展示会', '展示会', 'I want to <kanji>expand</kanji> my business. I want to <kanji>show</kanji> my goods. I want to do this in a place where everyone <kanji>meet</kanji>s up to do these things. You can do this in a <vocabulary>trade show</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4754', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%95%E7%A4%BA%E4%BC%9A', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:22:49.200839Z', NULL, '2025-10-27T15:45:55.954692Z'),
(4755, 'vocabulary', '発展する', '発展する', 'Let''s <kanji>depart</kanji> from this place and <kanji>expand</kanji> it out. To do that we''ll have <vocabulary>to develop</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4755', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BA%E5%B1%95%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:22:53.971421Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:22:52.12866Z'),
(4756, 'vocabulary', '展開', '展開', 'To <kanji>expand</kanji> and <kanji>open</kanji>. Just imagine something happening like this. This is when something is <vocabulary>unfolding</vocabulary> or in <vocabulary>development</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4756', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%95%E9%96%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:23:02.499545Z', NULL, '2025-10-27T15:45:55.954692Z'),
(4757, 'vocabulary', '親展', '親展', 'If <kanji>intimacy</kanji> <kanji>expand</kanji>s to something, that thing is <vocabulary>confidential</vocabulary>. Try visualizing intimacy as a sort of gooey liquid, expanding and covering a letter, and making that letter confidential.

親展 is generally written on the front of envelopes to indicate that only the intended recipient should read its contents.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4757', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA%E5%B1%95', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:23:09.743745Z', NULL, '2025-10-27T15:45:55.954692Z'),
(4758, 'vocabulary', '販売', '販売', '<kanji>Sell</kanji>, <kanji>sell</kanji>, sell. This is the attitude you need if you want to work in <vocabulary>sales</vocabulary> or do <vocabulary>sales</vocabulary>. You have to work on your <vocabulary>selling</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4758', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A9%E5%A3%B2', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:23:16.412865Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:35:42.046135Z'),
(4759, 'vocabulary', '自動販売機', '自動販売機', '<kanji>Automatic</kanji> (自動) <kanji>sell</kanji> <kanji>sell</kanji> <kanji>machine</kanji>. The machine sells things by itself, basically. This is a <vocabulary>vending machine</vocabulary>. There is a shorter version of this, but good to learn the long one first :)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4759', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E5%8B%95%E8%B2%A9%E5%A3%B2%E6%A9%9F', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:23:24.006571Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:35:42.046135Z'),
(4760, 'vocabulary', '通販', '通販', 'Things you <kanji>sell</kanji> that <kanji>pass through</kanji> the world to get to you are probably <vocabulary>mail order</vocabulary>. You order them and they get zoomed off to you, passing through all kinds of space to arrive at your door.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4760', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%9A%E8%B2%A9', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:23:37.959923Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:35:42.046135Z'),
(4761, 'vocabulary', '株', '株', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4761', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%AA', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:23:43.810293Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:39:37.898564Z'),
(4762, 'vocabulary', '株式', '株式', 'A <kanji>stock</kanji> <kanji>ritual</kanji> is the dance we do in the modern world around <vocabulary>stock</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>share</vocabulary>s of different companies. Buy! Sell! Buy! Sell!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4762', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%AA%E5%BC%8F', 24, 1, '2012-12-06T04:23:56.639177Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:39:37.898564Z'),
(4763, 'vocabulary', '吸収する', '吸収する', 'If 吸収 is "absorption" the verb version is <vocabulary>to absorb</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4763', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%B8%E5%8F%8E%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:50:15.228655Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:58.439739Z'),
(4764, 'vocabulary', '割れる', '割れる', 'Remember how 割る is "to divide"? Now you''re learning the intransitive version. Instead of you doing the dividing, something just gets divided or split up. So this is <vocabulary>to be divided</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be split</vocabulary>.

It can also mean <vocabulary>to break</vocabulary>, as in ワイングラスが割れる (the wine glass breaks). After all, when an object breaks, it’s divided into parts, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4764', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%B2%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:50:21.545849Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:40:20.776264Z'),
(4765, 'vocabulary', '割引', '割引', '<kanji>Divide</kanji> that price and <kanji>pull</kanji> it apart. You only have to pay for one part! What a <vocabulary>discount</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4765', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%B2%E5%BC%95', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:50:30.027427Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:40:20.776264Z'),
(4766, 'vocabulary', '準備する', '準備する', 'You''ve seen that 準備 is <kanji>preparation</kanji>. This is the verb version, so it means <vocabulary>to prepare</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to get ready for</vocabulary> something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4766', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%96%E5%82%99%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:50:51.913836Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:05:59.494452Z'),
(4767, 'vocabulary', '頑張る', '頑張る', '張る is <kanji>to stretch</kanji>. You''re <kanji>stubborn</kanji> and want to stretch out what you''re doing right now. Although you''re stubborn, you keep doing it. You persevere. That''s why this word is <vocabulary>to persevere</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to do one''s best</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4767', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%91%E5%BC%B5%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:51:05.41394Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:58.587Z'),
(4768, 'vocabulary', '抜く', '抜く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>pull out</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to pull something out</vocabulary>.

This word often implies that the pulling out requires force or effort. It''s a pretty versatile word, and can also cover things like letting the air out of something, draining the water out of something, and even overtaking someone or pulling through difficulties.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4768', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%9C%E3%81%8F', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:51:27.277725Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:58.636592Z'),
(4769, 'vocabulary', '捕まる', '捕まる', 'You''ve seen how 捕まえる means "to catch," and this is its intransitive sibling: <vocabulary>to be caught</vocabulary>.

At its core, 捕まる means getting physically caught, whether you''ve lost a game of tag or been arrested by the police. Sometimes, it can be used in a more figurative sense too, like when you get held up by a really chatty person (you''ve been "caught" by them) or you finally manage to get a word with someone you''ve been trying to talk to (they''ve been "caught" by you).

You can tell this apart from 捕まえる by thinking about how it would really <reading>mar</reading> (まる) your reputation if you were <vocabulary>to be arrested</vocabulary> by the cops.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4769', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%95%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2012-12-06T09:52:05.860081Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T07:02:47.565743Z'),
(4770, 'vocabulary', '模型', '模型', 'An <kanji>imitation</kanji> <kanji>model</kanji> is obviously not the real thing. It''s (usually) a <vocabulary>plastic model</vocabulary> or just a <vocabulary>model</vocabulary> figure or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4770', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A8%A1%E5%9E%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:52:31.948349Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:58.694142Z'),
(4771, 'vocabulary', '与える', '与える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>give</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to give</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to grant</vocabulary>.

This is a relatively formal word that is generally used in writing. In conversations, people commonly use more relaxed words like あげる or くれる.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4771', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8E%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2012-12-06T09:52:49.431034Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:58.732926Z'),
(4772, 'vocabulary', '賞与金', '賞与金', 'It''s a <kanji>prize</kanji> where you are <kanji>give</kanji>n <kanji>gold</kanji>. In business, this is called a <vocabulary>bonus</vocabulary>. You didn''t expect it (not your salary) and it is given to you (and is gold, if you''re lucky).

Note that you’ll more commonly see its shorter version 賞与.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4772', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%9E%E4%B8%8E%E9%87%91', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:53:05.225988Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:27:28.654407Z'),
(4773, 'vocabulary', '含む', '含む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>include</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to include</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to contain</vocabulary>.

While 含む can be used in everyday conversation, it’s a relatively formal word that is generally used in explanatory sentences.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4773', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%AB%E3%82%80', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:53:11.140872Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:58.791556Z'),
(4774, 'vocabulary', '含意', '含意', '<kanji>Include</kanji> your <kanji>idea</kanji>s here, but think about the <vocabulary>implication</vocabulary>s they will result in too, especially considering you''re just including them now. They have implications!

This word is quite formal, and therefore commonly used in academic or professional contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4774', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%AB%E6%84%8F', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:53:19.780585Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:58.824712Z'),
(4775, 'vocabulary', '影', '影', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4775', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%B1', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:53:25.513456Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:58.851831Z'),
(4776, 'vocabulary', '不況', '不況', 'A "<kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>condition</kanji>" is where nothing is going well. How are things going? Not well. The condition is bad. We''re in a <vocabulary>recession</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4776', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%B3%81', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:53:44.290516Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:58.877867Z'),
(4777, 'vocabulary', '渡る', '渡る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>transit</kanji>, which is just another word for crossing through or crossing over. That''s why this word is <vocabulary>to cross</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to cross over</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4777', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%A1%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:53:53.080189Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:58.910945Z'),
(4778, 'vocabulary', '渡す', '渡す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>transit</kanji>. It''s also in the verb 渡る, which means "to cross." But with 渡す, it''s more like you''re sending something across instead, usually to another person. So 渡す means <vocabulary>to hand over</vocabulary>.

It can also mean <vocabulary>to ferry something across</vocabulary> a river, or <vocabulary>to lay something across</vocabulary> an expanse, like when you lay a plank over a pothole or build a bridge across a river.

To remember the す in 渡す, think of suing someone. When you <reading>sue</reading> (す) them, you have to hand over the papers to let them know you''re suing. If you don''t 渡す the papers, you can''t sue!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4778', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%A1%E3%81%99', 26, 1, '2012-12-06T09:54:00.233665Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.299146Z'),
(4779, 'vocabulary', '響く', '響く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>echo</kanji> so the verb is <vocabulary>to echo</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4779', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9F%BF%E3%81%8F', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:54:08.78012Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:58.962792Z'),
(4780, 'vocabulary', '影響', '影響', 'In the <kanji>shadow</kanji>s you still <kanji>echo</kanji>. Your echo leaves the shadows and hits people, creating an <vocabulary>influence</vocabulary> on them. You''re like the shadow person pulling the strings, making things do what you want... influencing them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4780', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%B1%E9%9F%BF', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:54:14.199189Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:58.991389Z'),
(4781, 'vocabulary', '票', '票', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. It can also be <vocabulary>a vote</vocabulary>, because that''s what ballots tend to do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4781', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%A8', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:54:19.745726Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.023482Z'),
(4782, 'vocabulary', '投票する', '投票する', '<kanji>Throw</kanji> a <kanji>ballot</kanji> in the ballot box <vocabulary>to vote</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4782', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%95%E7%A5%A8%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:54:26.821288Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.048906Z'),
(4783, 'vocabulary', '光景', '光景', 'When <kanji>light</kanji> shines on a <kanji>scene</kanji>, it''s still a <vocabulary>scene</vocabulary>. The light is here to create some drama, also making this a <vocabulary>sight</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>spectacle</vocabulary>.

光景 refers to a scene or sight that leaves an impression, whether it''s unfolding in front of you or recalled in your mind. It''s often used in a poetic way to describe moments that are especially vivid — whether touching, beautiful, striking, or painful.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4783', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%89%E6%99%AF', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:54:40.626266Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:14.218462Z'),
(4784, 'vocabulary', '景況', '景況', 'Take a look at this <kanji>scene</kanji> and the <kanji>condition</kanji> it’s in. This is the climate of the situation, but more specifically the <vocabulary>business climate</vocabulary>. This word is really only used to talk about the state of business and economic stuff, and tends to appear in technical writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4784', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%AF%E6%B3%81', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:54:47.237146Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:56:40.956552Z'),
(4785, 'vocabulary', '景観', '景観', 'Quite the <kanji>scene</kanji>, this <kanji>view</kanji> is. So what you''re looking at is a <vocabulary>landscape</vocabulary>. This often refers to a <vocabulary>cityscape</vocabulary> or the general <vocabulary>scenery</vocabulary> of an area.

This is a formal word that’s commonly used in the context of urban planning or environmental design.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4785', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%AF%E8%A6%B3', 27, 1, '2012-12-06T09:54:53.267186Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.153096Z'),
(4786, 'vocabulary', '全景', '全景', 'When you can see <kanji>all</kanji> the <kanji>scene</kanji> you have a <vocabulary>complete view</vocabulary>.

This is not a common word in everyday conversations, but it can be found in literature, art, film, photography, and travel writing to describe a panoramic or comprehensive view of a scene.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4786', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E6%99%AF', 28, 1, '2012-12-06T09:55:04.730795Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:47:49.244838Z'),
(4787, 'vocabulary', '前景', '前景', 'The <kanji>front</kanji> <kanji>scene</kanji> is the <vocabulary>foreground</vocabulary>.

This word primarily refers to the visual foreground in art, photography, or theater. Unlike "foreground" in English, it’s not commonly used in a figurative sense to describe the most prominent aspect of situations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4787', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%8D%E6%99%AF', 28, 1, '2012-12-06T09:55:15.510796Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.216635Z'),
(4788, 'vocabulary', '訴える', '訴える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>sue</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to sue</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4788', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%B4%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:55:42.98958Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.243895Z'),
(4789, 'vocabulary', '告訴', '告訴', 'I''ve <kanji>announce</kanji>d I''m going to <kanji>sue</kanji> you. To do this, I need to file a <vocabulary>criminal complaint</vocabulary> against you. What''s the nature of my complaint? Uh... You suck.

告訴 is a legal term used when someone submits a criminal complaint to the authorities and formally asks them to investigate and prosecute a crime. It''s not just any old complaint or accusation — it''s an official legal action that carries serious weight.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4789', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%8A%E8%A8%B4', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:55:53.55116Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:33.230553Z'),
(4790, 'vocabulary', '訴訟', '訴訟', 'If you <kanji>sue</kanji> and bring about a <kanji>lawsuit</kanji>, you are bringing about a <vocabulary>lawsuit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4790', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%B4%E8%A8%9F', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:56:00.369834Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T19:04:11.903233Z'),
(4791, 'vocabulary', '逮捕', '逮捕', 'When you <kanji>apprehend</kanji> and <kanji>catch</kanji> someone, that leads to an <vocabulary>arrest</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4791', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%AE%E6%8D%95', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:56:08.600257Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:38:09.45677Z'),
(4792, 'vocabulary', '補う', '補う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>supplement</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to supplement</vocabulary>.

Note that this word is generally considered relatively formal in tone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4792', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%9C%E3%81%86', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:56:15.2433Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:31:35.096929Z'),
(4793, 'vocabulary', '候補者', '候補者', 'The <kanji>candidate</kanji> is <kanji>someone</kanji> who''s a <kanji>supplement</kanji> to the entire political system. Despite being a supplement, they''re still a <vocabulary>candidate</vocabulary> nonetheless, unless they can win!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4793', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%99%E8%A3%9C%E8%80%85', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:56:20.895134Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:31:35.096929Z'),
(4794, 'vocabulary', '気候', '気候', 'The <kanji>energy</kanji> of the <kanji>climate</kanji> is the <vocabulary>climate</vocabulary>. High energy? Climate. Low energy. Climate.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4794', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E5%80%99', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T09:56:29.734796Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(4795, 'vocabulary', '天候', '天候', '<kanji>Heaven</kanji>''s <kanji>climate</kanji> is <vocabulary>the weather</vocabulary>.

Compared to 天気, this is a more formal term used to describe the weather over the period of one day, multiple days, or even weeks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4795', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A9%E5%80%99', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:00:17.129676Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.457261Z'),
(4796, 'vocabulary', '時候', '時候', 'The <kanji>time</kanji>''s <kanji>climate</kanji> is the <vocabulary>seasonal climate</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>seasonal weather</vocabulary>.

You’ll almost always see this word used in the context of 時候のあいさつ (seasonal greetings). It''s a customary practice to start a letter by discussing something related to the current season before addressing the main topic.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4796', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%82%E5%80%99', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:00:22.485505Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.491257Z'),
(4797, 'vocabulary', '構う', '構う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>care</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to care about</vocabulary>.

This word generally implies a more direct physical or verbal expression of care, rather than just mentally caring about someone or something. The exception is when it''s used in the negative, like 構わない (I don''t mind).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4797', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A7%8B%E3%81%86', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:00:36.978775Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.521061Z'),
(4798, 'vocabulary', '構成', '構成', 'If you <kanji>set up</kanji> something so that it <kanji>become</kanji>s a certain way, that''s the <vocabulary>composition</vocabulary> of this thing. That''s its <vocabulary>structure</vocabulary>.

構成 refers to the process of assembling several elements to create a cohesive whole or the assembled whole itself. It can be applied to various contexts, such as social structures, family compositions, artistic works, or the configuration of materials.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4798', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A7%8B%E6%88%90', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:00:43.609434Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.556483Z'),
(4799, 'vocabulary', '結構', '結構', '<kanji>Bind</kanji> and <kanji>set up</kanji> everything so it is <vocabulary>fine</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>fairly</vocabulary>  <vocabulary>sufficient</vocabulary>. 

This word is often used to express that something is adequate or satisfactory, to politely decline an offer or invitation, or to indicate a relatively high degree or extent of something, like:

"No thank you" 結構です
"That''s fine, no more" もう結構です
"It''s fairly big" 結構大きい
etc.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4799', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%90%E6%A7%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:00:51.77297Z', NULL, '2025-12-29T07:44:55.970327Z'),
(4800, 'vocabulary', '模様', '模様', '様 uses its alternative meaning "manner" here, so we have an <kanji>imitation</kanji> <kanji>manner</kanji>. An imitation of the same manner over and over creates a <vocabulary>pattern</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4800', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A8%A1%E6%A7%98', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:01:03.728403Z', NULL, '2025-11-05T12:27:06.16638Z'),
(4801, 'vocabulary', '規模', '規模', 'The <kanji>standard</kanji> of <kanji>imitation</kanji> is to imitate to the <vocabulary>scale</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>scope</vocabulary> of the original.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4801', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8F%E6%A8%A1', 26, 1, '2012-12-06T10:01:20.515703Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.568793Z'),
(4802, 'vocabulary', '新鮮', '新鮮', '<kanji>New</kanji> and <kanji>fresh</kanji> is <vocabulary>fresh</vocabulary> (has to be new if you want it to be fresh, after all).

This word is used to describe fresh vegetables, fish, air, and new ideas or experiences, but is not commonly used to describe freshly made food or drinks. This is because the word emphasizes the inherent freshness of the thing being described, rather than its recent production.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4802', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E9%AE%AE', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:01:59.943357Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.666344Z'),
(4803, 'vocabulary', '鮮やか', '鮮やか', 'Alright, so it''s not quite the same, but something that is <kanji>fresh</kanji> is also <vocabulary>vivid</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>splendid</vocabulary>. I know those are a couple things I''d associate with freshness. Try to make the connection here before moving on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4803', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AE%AE%E3%82%84%E3%81%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:02:06.536904Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.695794Z'),
(4804, 'vocabulary', '鮮魚', '鮮魚', '<kanji>Fresh</kanji> <kanji>fish</kanji> is <vocabulary>fresh fish</vocabulary> (and the best fish, nom nom nom).

This is a fairly specialized word that you’d mostly see at a market. To describe fresh fish more generally, you can just say 新鮮な魚.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4804', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AE%AE%E9%AD%9A', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:02:14.36227Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.732923Z'),
(4805, 'vocabulary', '効く', '効く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>effective</kanji>. The verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be effective</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to work effectively</vocabulary> (since "to effective" doesn''t make sense).

This word is used to describe something that works effectively and as intended, such as medicine that achieves the desired effect or brakes that effectively stop the car.

', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4805', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%B9%E3%81%8F', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:02:27.230202Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.769211Z'),
(4806, 'vocabulary', '効果', '効果', 'The <kanji>fruit</kanji> of something that''s <kanji>effective</kanji> is <vocabulary>an effect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4806', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%B9%E6%9E%9C', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:02:34.300081Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.798316Z'),
(4807, 'vocabulary', '効率', '効率', 'Your <kanji>effective</kanji> <kanji>percent</kanji> shows how effective you are. If you''re at an effective percent of 80%, that''s your <vocabulary>efficiency</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4807', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%B9%E7%8E%87', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:02:44.59412Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.825089Z'),
(4808, 'vocabulary', '効力', '効力', 'Something''s <kanji>effective</kanji> <kanji>power</kanji> is its <vocabulary>effectiveness</vocabulary>. If it has effective power, it remains <vocabulary>in effect</vocabulary>.

This word usually refers to the legal force or effectiveness of a law, agreement, contract, or treaty, but it can also be used to talk about the efficacy of other things, such as medicine.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4808', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%B9%E5%8A%9B', 29, 1, '2012-12-06T10:02:52.09738Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(4809, 'vocabulary', '所属', '所属', 'Your <kanji>place</kanji> of <kanji>belong</kanji>ing describes your <vocabulary>affiliation</vocabulary>. It’s where you <vocabulary>belong to</vocabulary>. 

This is often used to describe a person’s affiliation or membership to a particular organization, team, or group.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4809', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%80%E5%B1%9E', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:03:32.473028Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T13:39:34.753123Z'),
(4810, 'vocabulary', '属する', '属する', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>belong</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to belong to</vocabulary>.

属する is a formal term for describing someone or something’s belonging to or being part of a group, category or classification.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4810', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%9E%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2012-12-06T10:03:51.850524Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T13:39:34.753123Z'),
(4811, 'vocabulary', '金属', '金属', 'Even <kanji>gold</kanji> <kanji>belong</kanji>s to a family... the family of <vocabulary>metal</vocabulary>s!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4811', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%91%E5%B1%9E', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:04:01.855746Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T13:39:34.753123Z'),
(4812, 'vocabulary', '慣れる', '慣れる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>accustomed</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to become accustomed</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4812', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%A3%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:04:10.879378Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:32:59.971784Z'),
(4813, 'vocabulary', '習慣', '習慣', 'I''ve <kanji>learn</kanji>ed to be <kanji>accustomed</kanji> to this. It has become my <vocabulary>custom</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>habit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4813', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BF%92%E6%85%A3', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:04:19.369679Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:42:04.39189Z'),
(4814, 'vocabulary', '豊か', '豊か', 'This is the same as the kanji 豊, making it <vocabulary>plentiful</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>abundant</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>rich</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>wealthy</vocabulary>.

豊か is a versatile and nuanced word that conveys a sense of abundance and richness in various aspects of life. It implies a positive and prosperous state, going beyond just material wealth or great quantity, and encompassing overall well-being and fulfillment.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4814', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B1%8A%E3%81%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:04:31.091438Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.038417Z'),
(4815, 'vocabulary', '豊満', '豊満', 'If you''re <kanji>plentiful</kanji> and <kanji>full</kanji>, you could say you''re <vocabulary>plump</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>full-figured</vocabulary>... in other words, you''re not a snack, you''re the <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XaCrQL_8eMY" target="_blank">Whole. Damn. Meal.</a>

This is a literary expression used to describe the physical appearance of a woman''s body, often focusing on a well-endowed chest.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4815', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B1%8A%E6%BA%80', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:04:41.993388Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:23:56.903683Z'),
(4816, 'vocabulary', '満足', '満足', 'I am <kanji>full</kanji> and <kanji>sufficient</kanji>. I am <vocabulary>satisfied</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4816', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%80%E8%B6%B3', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:04:49.982822Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.093402Z'),
(4817, 'vocabulary', '満月', '満月', 'The <kanji>full</kanji> <kanji>moon</kanji> is a <vocabulary>full moon</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4817', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%80%E6%9C%88', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:04:58.824962Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.120648Z'),
(4818, 'vocabulary', '満員', '満員', '<kanji>Full</kanji> of <kanji>member</kanji>s, so sorry, no more room. This place is a <vocabulary>full house</vocabulary>. That means there is <vocabulary>no vacancy</vocabulary> here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4818', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%80%E5%93%A1', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:05:43.936018Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:18:26.29811Z'),
(4819, 'vocabulary', '満点', '満点', '<kanji>Full</kanji> <kanji>point</kanji>s are when you get a <vocabulary>perfect score</vocabulary>.

満点 can also be used to describe a state of being at the pinnacle of a certain quality or standard, such as サービス満点 (excellent service) or 栄養満点 (highly nutritious).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4819', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%80%E7%82%B9', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:05:49.627904Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:02:55.445065Z'),
(4820, 'vocabulary', '肥満', '肥満', 'Does being <kanji>obese</kanji> mean you''re always <kanji>full</kanji>? Of course not. But clearly that''s what the people who coined the Japanese word for <vocabulary>obesity</vocabulary> thought... You can see there are some implicit biases in these kanji!
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4820', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%A5%E6%BA%80', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:05:56.758037Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.210403Z'),
(4821, 'vocabulary', '肥料', '肥料', 'Your <kanji>fee</kanji> for being <kanji>obese</kanji> is lots of <vocabulary>manure</vocabulary> coming out of your butt. If you eat a lot you''ll poop a lot of manure.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4821', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%A5%E6%96%99', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:06:07.731094Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.236923Z'),
(4822, 'vocabulary', '巻く', '巻く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>scroll</kanji>. But, you don''t "to scroll." What do you do to scrolls, though? You roll them up, making this word "<vocabulary>to roll</vocabulary>."

This word can also mean <vocabulary>to wind</vocabulary>, like when winding thread or a watch, or <vocabulary>to wrap</vocabulary> when involving a rolling or coiling motion, like wrapping sushi in seaweed or wrapping a cozy scarf around your neck.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4822', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%BB%E3%81%8F', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:06:21.375889Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.268615Z'),
(4823, 'vocabulary', '一巻', '一巻', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>scroll</kanji> is one scroll back in the day, but now it refers to books and means <vocabulary>one volume</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4823', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%B7%BB', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:06:27.297507Z', NULL, '2025-07-09T18:39:30.406462Z'),
(4824, 'vocabulary', '下巻', '下巻', 'The <kanji>scroll</kanji> that''s <kanji>below</kanji> is the last scroll you read, making it the <vocabulary>last volume</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4824', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E5%B7%BB', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:06:34.098234Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(4825, 'vocabulary', '上巻', '上巻', 'The <kanji>above</kanji> <kanji>scroll</kanji> is the top scroll, the one you read first. It''s the <vocabulary>first volume</vocabulary>. Many Japanese books come in parts, so look for this if you want to get the first one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4825', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A%E5%B7%BB', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:06:39.243435Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(4826, 'vocabulary', '二巻', '二巻', 'The number <kanji>two</kanji> <kanji>scroll</kanji> is the <vocabulary>second volume</vocabulary>. With Japanese books, they often come in pieces with groups of three being common. This represents the <vocabulary>second volume</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4826', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E5%B7%BB', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:06:44.367735Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.383091Z'),
(4827, 'vocabulary', '捜す', '捜す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>search</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to search for</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to seek</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4827', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%9C%E3%81%99', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:06:50.646389Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:18:12.570183Z'),
(4828, 'vocabulary', '捜査', '捜査', 'When you <kanji>search</kanji> and <kanji>inspect</kanji> things, you are probably part of a <vocabulary>criminal investigation</vocabulary>. You may even be subject to a <vocabulary>manhunt</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4828', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%9C%E6%9F%BB', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:06:57.515975Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T19:00:40.037806Z'),
(4829, 'vocabulary', '絞殺', '絞殺', 'A <kanji>strangle</kanji> <kanji>kill</kanji> is <vocabulary>murder by strangling</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>strangling to death</vocabulary>.

This is a pretty formal word that you’d see most in police reports and detective novels.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4829', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%9E%E6%AE%BA', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:07:05.142192Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.487105Z'),
(4830, 'vocabulary', '絞る', '絞る', 'You''ve learned that the kanji 絞 means <kanji>strangle</kanji>. 絞る, on the other hand, means <vocabulary>to wring out</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to squeeze</vocabulary> something like a towel, or <vocabulary>to narrow down</vocabulary> ideas. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4830', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%9E%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:07:11.684404Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.51123Z'),
(4831, 'vocabulary', '先輩', '先輩', 'My <kanji>comrade</kanji> who came <kanji>before</kanji> me is my <vocabulary>senior</vocabulary>, my senpai (せんぱい).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4831', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%88%E8%BC%A9', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:07:25.666217Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:27:12.714404Z'),
(4832, 'vocabulary', '後輩', '後輩', 'The <kanji>comrade</kanji> who''s <kanji>behind</kanji> you is your <vocabulary>junior</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4832', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%8C%E8%BC%A9', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:08:27.159262Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.570592Z'),
(4833, 'vocabulary', '年輩', '年輩', 'For <kanji>year</kanji>s and years, your <kanji>comrade</kanji>s have been with you, but now they''re all <vocabulary>elderly</vocabulary>.

This is a less common version of 年配, but you''ll still sometimes encounter it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4833', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E8%BC%A9', 27, 1, '2012-12-06T10:08:37.352625Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.713215Z'),
(4834, 'vocabulary', '隠す', '隠す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>hide</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to hide something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to conceal something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4834', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%A0%E3%81%99', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:08:43.58197Z', NULL, '2025-08-27T00:02:29.618118Z'),
(4835, 'vocabulary', '隠居', '隠居', 'You <kanji>hide</kanji> away but you''re <kanji>alive</kanji>. You''ve lived your life, so now it''s time to hide and enjoy your <vocabulary>retirement</vocabulary> by making your <vocabulary>retreat</vocabulary> from working life.

This word refers to life after someone retires, and usually implies being retired from active life and having a quiet, maybe even secluded, lifestyle. It can also imply handing over the reins to your successors. The word is a little old-fashioned, but you''ll still see it in modern writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4835', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%A0%E5%B1%85', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:08:55.665728Z', NULL, '2025-08-27T00:02:29.618118Z'),
(4836, 'vocabulary', '掛ける', '掛ける', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>hang</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to hang something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to hang up something</vocabulary> (as in to hang it up by a hook).

You can remember this because you must <reading>care</reading> (ける) about the way you hang something up. Without care, it won''t stay hanging for long.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4836', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%9B%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:09:05.74359Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:45:29.915612Z'),
(4837, 'vocabulary', '替える', '替える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>replace</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to replace</vocabulary>. You know that you''re doing the replacing because of the <reading>air</reading> (える). You''re replacing the air as quickly as you can because if you don''t you wouldn''t be able to breathe in this small room.

替える means to replace something with another item of the same kind or equivalent, rather than changing its state. Wondering how it differs from 代える? While 代える stresses that you’re using someone or something “in place of another,” 替える simply indicates the act of swapping one thing for another similar thing. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4837', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%BF%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2012-12-06T10:09:27.358774Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.718737Z'),
(4838, 'vocabulary', '替わる', '替わる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>replace</kanji>, and this word means <vocabulary>to be replaced</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be substituted</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>to be switched</vocabulary>.

Wondering about the difference between 代わる and 替わる? 代わる is used when someone or something takes the place of another, like filling in for a sick coworker. Whereas 替わる is used when things or situations switch places or are replaced with something new, like when talking about generations changing or a new governmental cabinet taking over.

This verb is intransitive, meaning it doesn''t take a direct object. You''ll sometimes see it with the particle を, though, because the を is showing a "path" rather than a direct object in this case. For example, 席を替わる (to switch seats) indicates your location is moving from one seat to another. Likewise, 電話を替わる indicates that a call is being handed off to another person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4838', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%BF%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2012-12-06T10:09:31.783741Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.044657Z'),
(4839, 'vocabulary', '不可能', '不可能', 'You already know how 可能 means possible. So when you add <kanji>not</kanji> it becomes <vocabulary>impossible</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4839', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%8F%AF%E8%83%BD', 22, 1, '2012-12-06T20:27:36.465896Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T21:59:59.512416Z'),
(4840, 'vocabulary', '可能', '可能', 'The <kanji>ability</kanji> is there, so it is <kanji>possible</kanji>... wait, there''s enough ability to make this <vocabulary>possible</vocabulary>? Let''s do it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4840', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%AF%E8%83%BD', 18, 1, '2012-12-11T04:12:33.126304Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T21:59:59.512416Z'),
(4841, 'vocabulary', '非難する', '非難する', 'Know how 非難 is a criticism? Make it a verb and you have the word <vocabulary>to criticize</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4841', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%9E%E9%9B%A3%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-11T04:43:01.760339Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.85074Z'),
(4842, 'vocabulary', '大嫌い', '大嫌い', 'Remember how 嫌い is to <kanji>dislike</kanji>? When that dislike is <kanji>big</kanji>, you really dislike it. That means you <vocabulary>hate</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>really hate</vocabulary>) it.

As with 嫌い, a more literal translation is <vocabulary>detestable</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4842', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%AB%8C%E3%81%84', 22, 1, '2012-12-11T04:53:40.483386Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(4843, 'vocabulary', '倒れる', '倒れる', 'You''ve seen how 倒す is "to knock over." But this is when something gets knocked over by itself. You know that you''re not doing anything here, because when you''re not touching all these fine glass items, it''s very <reading>rare</reading> (れる) that anything gets knocked over (because you''re not touching anything, you clumsy dumsy). It''s also when you or someone else collapses. You didn''t push yourself over, you just kind of fell by yourself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4843', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%92%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-11T04:54:12.563275Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.896206Z'),
(4844, 'vocabulary', '飛ばす', '飛ばす', 'Remember how 飛ぶ is to fly? Well this word is for when you make some thing fly. So, this word is <vocabulary>to let something fly</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to send something flying</vocabulary>. It also means <vocabulary>to skip over something</vocabulary>, such as someone''s turn or a question on a test.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4844', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9B%E3%81%B0%E3%81%99', 22, 1, '2012-12-11T17:55:47.400955Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.922583Z'),
(4845, 'vocabulary', '確かめる', '確かめる', 'Like with 確か, which means <kanji>certain</kanji>, this is the verb version of that. So when you "do certain" you are going <vocabulary>to make sure of</vocabulary> something or you''re going <vocabulary>to verify</vocabulary> something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4845', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A2%BA%E3%81%8B%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-11T17:56:04.263814Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:00.949073Z'),
(4846, 'vocabulary', '面倒臭い', '面倒臭い', 'Something is a trouble (面倒) and it is smelly (臭い). So what is something that''s smelly and a trouble? Well, something that''s <vocabulary>troublesome</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>a bother</vocabulary>, I suppose. Basically you can ignore the smelly part. For some reason someone threw that back in, but smelly things are a bother, I suppose.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4846', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%A2%E5%80%92%E8%87%AD%E3%81%84', 22, 1, '2012-12-11T18:07:09.597165Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T17:10:18.510063Z'),
(4847, 'vocabulary', '気温', '気温', 'The <kanji>energy</kanji> of the <kanji>warm</kanji>th will cause the <vocabulary>temperature</vocabulary> to go up and down. Energy goes up? Higher temperature because of more movement and activity. Energy goes down? Well, temperature gets colder and slower.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4847', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E6%B8%A9', 12, 1, '2012-12-11T18:41:30.109285Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(4848, 'vocabulary', '前回', '前回', 'The <kanji>before</kanji> <kanji>time</kanji> was <vocabulary>last time</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>previous time</vocabulary>', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4848', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%8D%E5%9B%9E', 8, 1, '2012-12-11T20:42:02.318611Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.412231Z'),
(4849, 'vocabulary', '時代', '時代', 'You learned 代 means "substitute," but it can also mean "period." And a <kanji>time</kanji> <kanji>period</kanji> is also called a <vocabulary>period</vocabulary> or an <vocabulary>era</vocabulary>. Simple!

時代 refers to a longer span of time, whether it''s an historical period, the present times, distinct eras in pop culture, or personal life stages.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4849', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%82%E4%BB%A3', 7, 1, '2012-12-13T04:29:21.882463Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T18:20:02.502016Z'),
(4850, 'vocabulary', '〜省', '〜省', 'What should you establish to <kanji>conserve</kanji> a country? The <vocabulary>Ministry of</vocabulary>… well, any ministry really. In Japanese, 〜省 often gets tacked on to the names of government ministries. Like the Ministry of Finance (財務省), the Ministry of Foreign Affairs (外務省), and the mouthful that is the Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology (文部科学省). Whew!

〜省 is also the suffix used for Chinese <vocabulary>province</vocabulary>s, such as 広東省 (Guangdong Province), so keep this in mind when reading about international affairs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4850', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E7%9C%81', 21, 1, '2012-12-15T01:11:50.613418Z', NULL, '2025-11-02T22:39:57.694852Z'),
(4851, 'vocabulary', '経済学', '経済学', 'You know how 経済 is economics, right? Well, add <kanji>study</kanji> to that and you have the study of economics, aka <vocabulary>economics</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4851', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%8C%E6%B8%88%E5%AD%A6', 23, 1, '2012-12-19T19:20:35.508145Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.101162Z'),
(4852, 'vocabulary', '検査する', '検査する', 'You know that 検査 is inspection, so the verb version of that would be <vocabulary>to inspect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4852', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A4%9C%E6%9F%BB%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2012-12-19T19:27:24.651508Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T19:00:40.037806Z'),
(4853, 'vocabulary', '挙げる', '挙げる', 'The kanji means <kanji>raise</kanji>, and this verb is when you do that to something. It''s transitive. So this means <vocabulary>to raise something</vocabulary>. It actually has a pretty wide meaning, and can also mean "raise" in the sense of <vocabulary>to cite something</vocabulary>, like an example. It can even mean <vocabulary>to hold something</vocabulary>, like an event.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4853', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%99%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-19T20:46:33.971486Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.156339Z'),
(4854, 'vocabulary', '理解する', '理解する', '理解 is "understanding," so the verb version would be...? How about <vocabulary>to understand</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to comprehend</vocabulary>? Well, do you?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4854', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%90%86%E8%A7%A3%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2012-12-19T20:48:55.449273Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:14.522754Z'),
(4855, 'vocabulary', '批判する', '批判する', '批判 is <kanji>criticism</kanji> so the verb version of that would be <vocabulary>to criticize</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4855', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%B9%E5%88%A4%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2012-12-19T20:49:32.156534Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T21:56:23.352206Z'),
(4856, 'vocabulary', '増やす', '増やす', 'Remember how 増える is "to increase" (as in, something just increases, you aren''t doing the increasing yourself). This one is that one, where you''re doing the increasing. So, this word means <vocabulary>to increase something</vocabulary>. You know you''re doing the increasing because you said "yes" (やす) to increasing something, and did it yourself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4856', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%97%E3%82%84%E3%81%99', 23, 1, '2012-12-19T20:50:32.102409Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.23099Z'),
(4857, 'vocabulary', '解く', '解く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>solve</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to solve</vocabulary>. You know this verb is for when you are doing the solving because you think it''s super <reading>coo</reading>'' (く) to solve things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4857', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A3%E3%81%8F', 13, 1, '2012-12-19T20:53:30.751094Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:14.534195Z'),
(4858, 'vocabulary', '断つ', '断つ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>cut off</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to cut off</vocabulary>.

This is often used metaphorically to describe cutting off things that tie us down mentally or societally. This includes ending bad habits, so it also means <vocabulary>to quit doing something</vocabulary>.

Don''t confuse this with 断る which means "to refuse." You know this is to cut off because of the つ, which hints that you have had <reading>two</reading> (つ) too many chocolates, and it''s time to cut you off.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4858', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%AD%E3%81%A4', 23, 1, '2012-12-19T21:04:22.64976Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.287539Z'),
(4859, 'vocabulary', '施設', '施設', 'Go ahead and <kanji>carry out</kanji> the task and <kanji>establish</kanji> the <vocabulary>institution</vocabulary>. To carry out and establish something is to institute it too. What do you institute? An institution.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4859', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%BD%E8%A8%AD', 23, 1, '2012-12-20T01:43:54.578897Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.317723Z'),
(4860, 'vocabulary', '総理府', '総理府', 'The prime minister''s (総理) <kanji>government</kanji> is where he does all his governmenting. It''s where the Japanese <vocabulary>Prime Minister''s Office</vocabulary> (aka the <vocabulary>Cabinet Office</vocabulary>) is located as well.

総理府 was actually renamed 内閣府 in 2001, so you''d generally only use 総理府 to talk about the Cabinet Offices from back in the day.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4860', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%8F%E7%90%86%E5%BA%9C', 35, 1, '2012-12-20T01:50:16.066988Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.345248Z'),
(4861, 'vocabulary', '朝寝坊', '朝寝坊', 'Remember 寝坊 and how it means <kanji>oversleep</kanji>? Someone who oversleeps in the <kanji>morning</kanji> is someone who''s a <vocabulary>late riser</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4861', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%9D%E5%AF%9D%E5%9D%8A', 24, 1, '2012-12-23T11:06:48.898207Z', NULL, '2025-06-25T14:00:39.611736Z'),
(4862, 'vocabulary', '営業中', '営業中', 'Remember how 営業 means "business"? Well, if you''re in the <kanji>middle</kanji> of running a business, it means you''re <vocabulary>open for business</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4862', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%B6%E6%A5%AD%E4%B8%AD', 24, 1, '2012-12-23T11:07:05.288581Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(4863, 'vocabulary', '置き場', '置き場', '置く is <kanji>to put down</kanji> something. So a <kanji>location</kanji> where you put something down is a <vocabulary>place to put something</vocabulary>.

置き場 often follows another word, signifying it''s the designated area where that specific item should be stored or parked. For example, 自転車置き場.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4863', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BD%AE%E3%81%8D%E5%A0%B4', 24, 1, '2012-12-23T11:13:49.675189Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:08:17.52437Z'),
(4864, 'vocabulary', '主観的', '主観的', '主観 is <kanji>subjectivity</kanji>. So when something moves towards subjectivity it is becoming <vocabulary>subjective</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4864', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB%E8%A6%B3%E7%9A%84', 24, 1, '2012-12-25T01:06:47.174802Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.461384Z'),
(4865, 'vocabulary', '援助する', '援助する', 'You know that 援助 is <kanji>assistance</kanji> or <kanji>aid</kanji>. So the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to assist</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to aid</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4865', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%B4%E5%8A%A9%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-25T01:13:51.744295Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.486151Z'),
(4866, 'vocabulary', '応援する', '応援する', '応援 is <kanji>aid</kanji> and <kanji>root for</kanji>, but for this verb the "root for" one is going to be more on the money. You can call this <vocabulary>to root for</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to cheer on</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to cheer for</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4866', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%9C%E6%8F%B4%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-25T01:15:36.99048Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.511865Z'),
(4867, 'vocabulary', '応援', '応援', 'You must <kanji>respond</kanji> with <kanji>aid</kanji> in order to show your <vocabulary>support</vocabulary> for someone who needs it. You can usually show your support if you <vocabulary>root for</vocabulary> them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4867', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%9C%E6%8F%B4', 22, 1, '2012-12-25T01:17:07.379073Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.534252Z'),
(4868, 'vocabulary', '副大統領', '副大統領', 'You know about 大統領 (<kanji>president</kanji>), right? Well, this is the <kanji>vice</kanji> version of the president, aka the <vocabulary>vice president</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4868', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%AF%E5%A4%A7%E7%B5%B1%E9%A0%98', 24, 1, '2012-12-25T01:20:08.30185Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(4869, 'vocabulary', '提案する', '提案する', 'You remember 提案, which means <kanji>proposition</kanji>? Make that into a verb and you have <vocabulary>to propose</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4869', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%90%E6%A1%88%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-26T02:08:38.072369Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.577615Z'),
(4870, 'vocabulary', '支援する', '支援する', 'To <kanji>support</kanji> and <kanji>aid</kanji> is <vocabulary>to support</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4870', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%AF%E6%8F%B4%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 24, 1, '2012-12-26T02:27:40.969527Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.599402Z'),
(4871, 'vocabulary', '観光客', '観光客', '<kanji>Sightseeing</kanji> (観光) <kanji>guest</kanji>s are <vocabulary>tourist</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>sightseer</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4871', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%B3%E5%85%89%E5%AE%A2', 24, 1, '2012-12-26T02:29:24.506235Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.620455Z'),
(4872, 'vocabulary', '女優', '女優', 'Remember how 男優 was "actor"? Well, I bet you can extrapolate what this one means because it now has <kanji>woman</kanji> in it. This is an <vocabulary>actress</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4872', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%B3%E5%84%AA', 25, 1, '2013-01-02T06:07:01.647912Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T00:40:38.795452Z'),
(4873, 'vocabulary', '革命家', '革命家', 'A person who lives in a revolution (革命) <kanji>house</kanji> is <vocabulary>a revolutionary</vocabulary>. They want government change! They want to take down the people in power!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4873', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%A9%E5%91%BD%E5%AE%B6', 25, 1, '2013-01-02T06:09:26.262766Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.673535Z'),
(4874, 'vocabulary', '革ジャン', '革ジャン', '革 is <kanji>leather</kanji> and ジャン is short for ジャンパー (Jumper, like a jacket). So, put those together and you have a <vocabulary>leather jacket</vocabulary>.

This word is getting somewhat outdated. The trendier term now is レザージャケット.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4874', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%A9%E3%82%B8%E3%83%A3%E3%83%B3', 25, 1, '2013-01-02T19:18:22.265453Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.69669Z'),
(4875, 'vocabulary', '長崎県', '長崎県', '<kanji>Long</kanji> <kanji>Cape</kanji> <kanji>Prefecture</kanji>. This will make more sense when you look at the reading. Hint: It''s <vocabulary>Nagasaki Prefecture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4875', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%95%B7%E5%B4%8E%E7%9C%8C', 25, 1, '2013-01-02T19:21:56.426177Z', NULL, '2025-11-30T19:10:22.678433Z'),
(4876, 'vocabulary', '本当', '本当', 'What is <kanji>real</kanji> is <kanji>right</kanji>. It is the <vocabulary>reality</vocabulary>. It is also the <vocabulary>truth</vocabulary>. Don''t believe anything that isn''t <vocabulary>real</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>true</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4876', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E5%BD%93', 5, 1, '2013-01-06T20:42:49.843677Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.811555Z'),
(4877, 'vocabulary', '本当に', '本当に', '本当 is <kanji>reality</kanji> or <kanji>truth</kanji> (level 5 vocab). If you add on に, this changes the word to an adverb, and we get <vocabulary>really</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>truly</vocabulary>. 本当に!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4877', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E5%BD%93%E3%81%AB', 7, 1, '2013-01-06T20:42:51.443842Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.157672Z'),
(4878, 'vocabulary', '呼ばれる', '呼ばれる', 'If 呼ぶ is "to call" then this is something similar. This is when you are called by someone (and you''re not doing the calling). This word is <vocabulary>to be called</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4878', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%BC%E3%81%B0%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2013-01-06T21:13:58.091151Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.792756Z'),
(4879, 'vocabulary', '現す', '現す', 'This is the transitive version of 現れる (to appear), so rather than something appearing on its own, you’re making it appear. So this word means <vocabulary>to show</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to reveal</vocabulary>, but it’s usually used in set phrases like 姿を現す (to show up) or 正体を現す (to reveal one''s true nature).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4879', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%BE%E3%81%99', 25, 1, '2013-01-06T21:17:39.933499Z', NULL, '2025-07-13T16:05:44.960273Z'),
(4880, 'vocabulary', '景色', '景色', 'The <kanji>scene</kanji>''s <kanji>color</kanji> is the <vocabulary>scenery</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>landscape</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>view</vocabulary>. Looking good, scenery!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4880', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%AF%E8%89%B2', 25, 1, '2013-01-06T23:11:18.321147Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.843331Z'),
(4881, 'vocabulary', '宿', '宿', 'A <kanji>lodge</kanji> is a form of <vocabulary>lodging</vocabulary>. It is also considered to be an <vocabulary>inn</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>hotel</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4881', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%BF', 13, 1, '2013-01-07T09:05:01.098056Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:01.872005Z'),
(4882, 'vocabulary', '間違い', '間違い', 'We waited an <kanji>interval of time</kanji>, and at each interval it was <kanji>different</kanji>. They''re all supposed to be the same. Is there some kind of <vocabulary>mistake</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4882', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%93%E9%81%95%E3%81%84', 26, 1, '2013-01-07T20:29:57.00279Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:44.955366Z'),
(4883, 'vocabulary', '載せる', '載せる', 'Remember how 載る is "to be placed on"? This one is <vocabulary>to place on something</vocabulary>, like a mug on a table. 載せる is transitive, and you know this because you do it your<reading>sel</reading>f (せる). Like 載る, 載せる also often refers to putting something in a written document or publication, so it can mean <vocabulary>to put on record</vocabulary>.

Note that 載せる is never used to refer to putting clothing or accessories on. That''s 着る or 着ける respectively, which you''ve learned already.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4883', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%89%E3%81%9B%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-14T19:49:54.653645Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:44.971658Z'),
(4884, 'vocabulary', '気象庁', '気象庁', 'The <kanji>energy</kanji> <kanji>phenomenon</kanji> <kanji>agency</kanji> sounds like something that could be really cool but it''s really just the <vocabulary>weather agency</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>meteorological agency</vocabulary>. Nothing shady going on here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4884', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E8%B1%A1%E5%BA%81', 26, 1, '2013-01-14T20:08:50.004644Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(4885, 'vocabulary', '居る', '居る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>alive</kanji> and the verb version means <vocabulary>to exist</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be</vocabulary>, for anything that is alive and moving, like people and animals. 

The kanji isn''t used too often for this word, so when you''re writing just use the kana. But you''ll see the kanji used sometimes so it''s important to be able to recognize it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4885', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%85%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2013-01-16T06:27:08.684668Z', NULL, '2026-01-10T15:35:47.756382Z'),
(4886, 'vocabulary', '構造', '構造', 'First you have to <kanji>set up</kanji> the <vocabulary>structure</vocabulary> of something to <kanji>create</kanji> it. This structure is important for the creation of everything, otherwise it would just fall apart.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4886', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A7%8B%E9%80%A0', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:20:08.9052Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:44.996009Z'),
(4887, 'vocabulary', '再び', '再び', 'This is the adverb version of 再, so it shares the meaning <vocabulary>again</vocabulary> and also has the meaning <vocabulary>once again</vocabulary>.

Note that this word is somewhat formal and literary.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4887', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%8D%E3%81%B3', 28, 1, '2013-01-18T00:20:18.067111Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:02.024589Z'),
(4888, 'vocabulary', '再来月', '再来月', 'The <kanji>next month</kanji> (来月) isn''t enough... one more next month, please. Let''s do it <kanji>again</kanji>. That month is the <vocabulary>month after next</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4888', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%8D%E6%9D%A5%E6%9C%88', 27, 1, '2013-01-18T00:20:36.051227Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(4889, 'vocabulary', '再来年', '再来年', 'You have <kanji>next year</kanji> (来年), but then you do a year <kanji>again</kanji>. That''s the <vocabulary>year after next</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4889', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%8D%E6%9D%A5%E5%B9%B4', 27, 1, '2013-01-18T00:20:55.308532Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(4890, 'vocabulary', '再来週', '再来週', 'You know how 来週 is <kanji>next week</kanji>? Well, if you do next week again, you have the <vocabulary>week after next</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4890', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%8D%E6%9D%A5%E9%80%B1', 28, 1, '2013-01-18T00:21:02.071372Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(4891, 'vocabulary', '再開', '再開', '<kanji>Again</kanji> it is <kanji>open</kanji>ing. This is the <vocabulary>reopening</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>resumption</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4891', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%8D%E9%96%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:21:09.810602Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.047475Z'),
(4892, 'vocabulary', '再建', '再建', '<kanji>Again</kanji> you <kanji>build</kanji> it. This is <vocabulary>rebuilding</vocabulary>.

再建 is a formal word that refers to the act of rebuilding something that has deteriorated or been lost, whether it''s a physical structure like a building or an organization, or a part of the body in the context of medical procedures.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4892', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%8D%E5%BB%BA', 29, 1, '2013-01-18T00:21:18.573023Z', NULL, '2025-12-29T00:39:15.602797Z'),
(4893, 'vocabulary', '再度', '再度', '<kanji>Again</kanji> with that <kanji>occurrence</kanji>? That occurrence has now happened <vocabulary>again</vocabulary>.

Note that this word is relatively formal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4893', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%8D%E5%BA%A6', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:21:31.013348Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.07132Z'),
(4894, 'vocabulary', '刺激', '刺激', 'You have all these little <kanji>stab</kanji>s that are very <kanji>fierce</kanji> on you. They tingle like crazy. This is quite the <vocabulary>stimulation</vocabulary> on your body. *tingle tingle tingle.*', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4894', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%BA%E6%BF%80', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:21:38.181501Z', NULL, '2025-12-28T19:49:23.647759Z'),
(4895, 'vocabulary', '刺す', '刺す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

The kanji itself means <kanji>stab</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to stab something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to pierce something</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to sting something</vocabulary>.

This is a transitive verb, meaning that you''re the one doing the stabbing... Should I be afraid of you?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4895', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%BA%E3%81%99', 20, 1, '2013-01-18T00:21:47.958425Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T06:26:42.968126Z'),
(4896, 'vocabulary', '名刺', '名刺', 'Take your <kanji>name</kanji> and <kanji>stab</kanji> someone with it... using your <vocabulary>business card</vocabulary>. Your business card has your name on it. Also, you "stab" it at people who are willing to take it and read it. Not a real stab, but a metaphorical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4896', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8D%E5%88%BA', 25, 1, '2013-01-18T00:22:26.532001Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(4897, 'vocabulary', '創造', '創造', '<kanji>Create</kanji>! <kanji>Create</kanji>! If you do this, you are doing <vocabulary>creation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4897', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%B5%E9%80%A0', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:22:34.024582Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.083922Z'),
(4898, 'vocabulary', '独創', '独創', 'You are <kanji>alone</kanji> in what you are able to <kanji>create</kanji>. Nobody else can create like you. So what you create is your own <vocabulary>original creation</vocabulary>.

独創 refers to the act of <vocabulary>creating something original</vocabulary>, or the resulting creation itself. This word is quite formal, and generally used as 独創的 (original) or 独創性 (originality).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4898', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%AC%E5%89%B5', 44, 1, '2013-01-18T00:22:41.97344Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:02.303538Z'),
(4899, 'vocabulary', '創立', '創立', '<kanji>Create</kanji> it and <kanji>stand</kanji> it up. This is your <vocabulary>establishment</vocabulary>, your <vocabulary>founding</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4899', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%B5%E7%AB%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:22:49.506183Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.0962Z'),
(4900, 'vocabulary', '激励', '激励', 'If someone is <kanji>fierce</kanji> when they <kanji>encourage</kanji> you, that''s some <vocabulary>strong encouragement</vocabulary>! 

This word is kind of formal, and using it will really communicate your appreciation for the <vocabulary>encouragement</vocabulary> someone gives you. Even if they just tossed a casual 頑張れ your way, calling that 激励 will make it sound like some of the strongest, warmest encouragement you''ve ever gotten.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4900', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BF%80%E5%8A%B1', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:22:57.609293Z', NULL, '2025-10-16T19:54:14.297578Z'),
(4901, 'vocabulary', '励ます', '励ます', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>encourage</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to encourage someone</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>to encourage</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4901', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%B1%E3%81%BE%E3%81%99', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:23:05.856324Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.130411Z'),
(4902, 'vocabulary', '占い', '占い', 'Although this has a い on the end it''s tricky and not an adjective. The kanji is <kanji>fortune</kanji>, and the vocab word is <vocabulary>fortune telling</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4902', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%A0%E3%81%84', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:23:12.597701Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.154759Z'),
(4903, 'vocabulary', '占める', '占める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>occupy</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to occupy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4903', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%A0%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:23:21.401223Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.178377Z'),
(4904, 'vocabulary', '独占', '独占', 'You are <kanji>alone</kanji> in what you <kanji>occupy</kanji>. Nobody but you occupies it. You have a <vocabulary>monopoly</vocabulary>. You <vocabulary>monopolize</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4904', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%AC%E5%8D%A0', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:23:26.774146Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.192164Z'),
(4905, 'vocabulary', '印', '印', 'The kanji and the word are almost exactly the same. This word means <vocabulary>mark</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4905', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B0', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:23:35.20512Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.230809Z'),
(4906, 'vocabulary', '第一印象', '第一印象', '<kanji>The first</kanji> (第一) <kanji>phenomenon</kanji> is what''s <kanji>seal</kanji>ed into your head. This is your <vocabulary>first impression</vocabulary> of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4906', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E5%8D%B0%E8%B1%A1', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:23:43.048939Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.242399Z'),
(4907, 'vocabulary', '届ける', '届ける', '届く is "to be delivered." This word is when you''re doing the actual delivering, making it <vocabulary>to deliver</vocabulary>. You can remember this because you <reading>care</reading> (ける) about the package so much that you deliver it yourself, thus doing the action of delivering it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4907', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%8A%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:24:06.853811Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.255917Z'),
(4908, 'vocabulary', '差別する', '差別する', '差別 is discrimination. This is the verb version, <vocabulary>to discriminate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4908', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%AE%E5%88%A5%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:24:15.098344Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.271195Z'),
(4909, 'vocabulary', '人差し指', '人差し指', 'Your <kanji>person</kanji> <kanji>distinction</kanji> <kanji>finger</kanji> is the finger you use to point at people to distinguish them from other people. This is your <vocabulary>pointer finger</vocabulary>... your <vocabulary>index finger</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4909', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E5%B7%AE%E3%81%97%E6%8C%87', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:24:20.637604Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(4910, 'vocabulary', '差し上げる', '差し上げる', 'This is a really humble and polite way to say "<vocabulary>to give</vocabulary>," probably because you''re lowering yourself down and raising the item above your head so far that you have to point at it because if you were touching it you''d be defiling it anyways so, you know, point at it and raise it way up, you little worm on the ground.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4910', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%AE%E3%81%97%E4%B8%8A%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:24:28.528977Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(4911, 'vocabulary', '往復', '往復', '<kanji>Depart</kanji> and then <kanji>restore</kanji> yourself back to your original departure spot. If you do this (departing and coming back) you have a <vocabulary>round trip</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4911', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%80%E5%BE%A9', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:24:34.201881Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.334777Z'),
(4912, 'vocabulary', '従う', '従う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>obey</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to obey</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4912', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%93%E3%81%86', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:24:44.225072Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.351126Z'),
(4913, 'vocabulary', '従業', '従業', 'You must <kanji>obey</kanji> your <kanji>business</kanji>. When you''re in this situation, you’re <vocabulary>engaging in work</vocabulary>.

This is most commonly used in the compounds 従業員 (employee) and 従業者 (worker). You might also come across other forms, such as 従業する, but these are generally found in formal writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4913', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%93%E6%A5%AD', 51, 1, '2013-01-18T00:24:50.025639Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(4914, 'vocabulary', '従来', '従来', 'You''ve <kanji>obey</kanji>ed all that has <kanji>come</kanji> <vocabulary>up to now</vocabulary>. That''s because it''s the <vocabulary>conventional</vocabulary> way of doing things, and it''s what''s <vocabulary>conventionally</vocabulary> expected of you.

This word is a relatively formal way to talk about how things have been up to now. It''s commonly used with の to modify a noun or phrase, or as an adverb.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4914', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%93%E6%9D%A5', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:24:54.849282Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(4915, 'vocabulary', '復習', '復習', '<kanji>Restore</kanji> what you <kanji>learn</kanji>ed with <vocabulary>review</vocabulary>s. That''s what you do on WaniKani, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4915', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%A9%E7%BF%92', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:25:00.8858Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:42:04.39189Z'),
(4916, 'vocabulary', '回復', '回復', 'Many <kanji>times</kanji> you <kanji>restore</kanji> something. You do this over and over for the <vocabulary>recovery</vocabulary> of this thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4916', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9E%E5%BE%A9', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:25:05.862283Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.388165Z'),
(4917, 'vocabulary', '特徴', '特徴', 'A <kanji>special</kanji> <kanji>indication</kanji> on something or yourself is a special <vocabulary>characteristic</vocabulary>. It''s a <vocabulary>distinctive feature</vocabulary> that exists.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4917', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%B9%E5%BE%B4', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:25:11.060298Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.397954Z'),
(4918, 'vocabulary', '象徴', '象徴', 'A <kanji>phenomenon</kanji> of some kind that''s an <kanji>indication</kanji> of something else is a <vocabulary>symbol</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4918', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B1%A1%E5%BE%B4', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:25:19.618298Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.40865Z'),
(4919, 'vocabulary', '怪しい', '怪しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>suspicious</kanji>? Also <vocabulary>suspicious</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4919', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%AA%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:25:27.957918Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.420099Z'),
(4920, 'vocabulary', '怪事件', '怪事件', 'Remember how 事件 means "incident"? Well, a <kanji>suspicious</kanji> incident always has a <vocabulary>strange mystery</vocabulary> behind it. It also always turns into a <vocabulary>mystery case</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4920', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%AA%E4%BA%8B%E4%BB%B6', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:25:35.372789Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.429774Z'),
(4921, 'vocabulary', '怪談', '怪談', 'If you have a <kanji>suspicious</kanji> <kanji>talk</kanji>, you''re talking about something that may or may not be real. One of those things you could be discussing is a <vocabulary>ghost story</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4921', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%AA%E8%AB%87', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:25:51.690743Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.439171Z'),
(4922, 'vocabulary', '我', '我', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

我 is an old-fashioned pronoun and isn''t commonly used outside of set phrases like 我を忘れる (to forget oneself or be utterly absorbed) or 我に返る (to come back to reality or return to one''s senses).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4922', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%91', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:25:58.584136Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T06:27:10.83672Z'),
(4923, 'vocabulary', '無我', '無我', 'There is <kanji>not</kanji> any of <kanji>I</kanji>. It is all about everyone else. It is all about <vocabulary>selflessness</vocabulary>.

This word is commonly used in the phrase 無我夢中 (total absorption).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4923', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E6%88%91', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:26:18.837662Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.459865Z'),
(4924, 'vocabulary', '担う', '担う', '担ぐ is more like when you''re carrying something physical. 担う is when you carry a responsibility, which is why this word means <vocabulary>to assume responsibility</vocabulary>. You can remember this because <reading>YOU</reading> (う) are responsible for this. You assume this responsibility.

Note that 担う is generally used with another word that specifies the responsibility, like 役割を担う (assume a role) or 未来を担う (bear the future).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4924', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%85%E3%81%86', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:26:26.452311Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.469779Z'),
(4925, 'vocabulary', '振る', '振る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>shake</kanji> and this verb is transitive, meaning that you''re shaking something. So this verb means <vocabulary>to shake something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to wave something</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to swing something</vocabulary>. You''ll often see it paired with 手, as in 手を振る, meaning "to wave one''s hand" or, as we''d usually say in English, simply "to wave".

This word can also be used when you shake someone off in the less physical way, as in <vocabulary>to break up with someone</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4925', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%AF%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:26:31.902658Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.482566Z'),
(4926, 'vocabulary', '振動', '振動', 'The <kanji>move</kanji>ment that has to do with <kanji>shake</kanji>s is <vocabulary>vibration</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>oscillation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4926', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%AF%E5%8B%95', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:26:38.784938Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.495333Z'),
(4927, 'vocabulary', '不振', '不振', 'If something does <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>shake</kanji>, it’s probably in <vocabulary>a slump</vocabulary>. If nothing’s shaking, things must be <vocabulary>sluggish</vocabulary>.

不振 is used for times when things are slower or worse than usual. For example, 業績不振 is when a business isn’t doing as well as it usually does, and 食欲不振 is when you don''t feel like eating as much as you normally would.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4927', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%8C%AF', 33, 1, '2013-01-18T00:26:46.299599Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.343601Z'),
(4928, 'vocabulary', '授業', '授業', 'The <kanji>instruct</kanji>ion <kanji>business</kanji> is when you give a <vocabulary>lesson</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>class</vocabulary> to someone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4928', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%88%E6%A5%AD', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:26:54.047757Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(4929, 'vocabulary', '教授', '教授', 'If you <kanji>teach</kanji> and <kanji>instruct</kanji>, then you may be some kind of <vocabulary>professor</vocabulary>. And a professor''s work is… <vocabulary>teaching</vocabulary>!

教授 usually means a university professor, but it can also refer to other kinds of expert instruction in a formal context. You can even use it in phrases like ご教授ありがとうございます to show respect for the expertise of someone of higher status.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4929', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%99%E6%8E%88', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:27:03.717801Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T22:19:06.576194Z'),
(4930, 'vocabulary', '直接', '直接', 'Remember how 直 can also mean "direct?" Well, if you <kanji>direct</kanji>ly <kanji>adjoin</kanji> two things, their connection will become <vocabulary>direct</vocabulary> too. Nothing will stand between them!

That said, 直接 isn''t limited to physical contact. In fact, it''s more often used when someone does something without going through a middle step or person, whether it''s in communication, action, or connection. For example, if you say 直接聞いた (I heard it directly), it means you got the info straight from the source.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4930', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B4%E6%8E%A5', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:27:12.081498Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:46:52.388227Z'),
(4931, 'vocabulary', '間接', '間接', 'After an <kanji>interval of time</kanji> it will <kanji>adjoin</kanji>, but not right away. That is because it was being <vocabulary>indirect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4931', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%93%E6%8E%A5', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:27:30.471172Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:46:52.388227Z'),
(4932, 'vocabulary', '面接', '面接', 'Our <kanji>face</kanji>s have <kanji>adjoin</kanji>ed in this one room to talk. You did the telephone interview where you didn''t show the face of your face. Now you''re showing it, so we can begin the real <vocabulary>interview</vocabulary>.

面接 generally refers to a <vocabulary>job interview</vocabulary> or an <vocabulary>oral examination</vocabulary>. For conversations between media professionals and people of public interest, インタビュー is used instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4932', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%A2%E6%8E%A5', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:27:39.641774Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:46:52.388227Z'),
(4933, 'vocabulary', '故に', '故に', 'You learned that 故 means reason. Basically, whatever comes before 故に in a statement is going to be the reason for something that comes after. So 故に works like <vocabulary>therefore</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>consequently</vocabulary>. It can also be translated as "because" or other similar conjunctions depending on the context.

Also keep in mind that 故に is mainly used in formal contexts, like in academic writing or politicians'' speeches — just like "therefore" in English.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4933', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%85%E3%81%AB', 28, 1, '2013-01-18T00:27:45.876113Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:03.266686Z'),
(4934, 'vocabulary', '事故', '事故', 'An <kanji>occurrence</kanji> that only results because of <kanji>circumstance</kanji>s is also called an <vocabulary>accident</vocabulary>. You didn''t want this bad thing to happen, it was just an occurrence of circumstance! Everything aligned in the worst way.

事故 is mostly used to refer to physical accidents, like traffic accidents (交通事故) or construction accidents (建設事故). Sometimes other kinds of <vocabulary>mishap</vocabulary>s get the 事故 label too, like broadcast mishaps (放送事故), where something inappropriate or unplanned happens on air.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4934', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8B%E6%95%85', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:27:51.99529Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.579578Z'),
(4935, 'vocabulary', '故障', '故障', 'By some <kanji>circumstance</kanji>, the thing you''re using is <kanji>hinder</kanji>ing you. Usually it works, but now it''s <vocabulary>out of order</vocabulary> and has a <vocabulary>malfunction</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4935', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%85%E9%9A%9C', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:27:56.742958Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.605736Z'),
(4936, 'vocabulary', '故意', '故意', 'The <kanji>circumstance</kanji> was caused by the <kanji>idea</kanji> I had. Because I had this idea before the circumstance, it shows I was <vocabulary>intentional</vocabulary> in doing it. I did it <vocabulary>on purpose</vocabulary>.

Note that 故意 is mostly used in formal contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4936', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%85%E6%84%8F', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:28:02.137814Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.623596Z'),
(4937, 'vocabulary', '株式会社', '株式会社', 'This <kanji>company</kanji> (会社) has done the <kanji>ritual</kanji> required to create <kanji>stock</kanji>s for their company. When a company has stocks, they become a <vocabulary>corporation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4937', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%AA%E5%BC%8F%E4%BC%9A%E7%A4%BE', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:28:08.700645Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:39:37.898564Z'),
(4938, 'vocabulary', '株式市場', '株式市場', 'The <kanji>location</kanji> in the <kanji>city</kanji> where the <kanji>stock</kanji>s <kanji>ritual</kanji> takes place is the <vocabulary>stock market</vocabulary>. Just think of what happens in a stock market... the stock ritual, where people buy and sell stocks. And, of course, this is located in the city.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4938', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%AA%E5%BC%8F%E5%B8%82%E5%A0%B4', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:28:16.525246Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:39:37.898564Z'),
(4939, 'vocabulary', '比較', '比較', 'A <kanji>compare</kanji> and <kanji>contrast</kanji> will give you a <vocabulary>comparison</vocabulary> of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4939', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%94%E8%BC%83', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:28:28.306231Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.663933Z'),
(4940, 'vocabulary', '汗', '汗', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4940', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%97', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:28:35.520922Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.676669Z'),
(4941, 'vocabulary', '汗臭い', '汗臭い', 'If your <kanji>sweat</kanji> is <kanji>smelly</kanji> (臭い) you are <vocabulary>sweaty-smelling</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>smelling of sweat</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>smelling like sweat</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4941', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%97%E8%87%AD%E3%81%84', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:28:45.538756Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.689696Z'),
(4942, 'vocabulary', '河', '河', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. 

河 originally referred to the Yellow River in ancient China, so it''s usually associated with very <vocabulary>large river</vocabulary>s. 河 is a lot less common than 川, but you might still find it in literature, where it can emphasize the grand and powerful nature of a river or its cultural significance.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4942', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%B3', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:28:54.939849Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.701404Z'),
(4943, 'vocabulary', '河童', '河童', 'A <kanji>river</kanji> <kanji>juvenile</kanji>... not what you think. It''s a <vocabulary>kappa</vocabulary>, a mythological Japanese creature. That being said, it lives in / around rivers and is about the size of a juvenile, so maybe this makes sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4943', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%B3%E7%AB%A5', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:29:02.075623Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.713983Z'),
(4944, 'vocabulary', '氷河', '氷河', 'An <kanji>ice</kanji> <kanji>river</kanji> is some ice that moves (very slowly) like a river. That is a <vocabulary>glacier</vocabulary>. In fact, many inlets and sounds were created with glaciers, because glaciers moved through them like rivers... not very quickly, though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4944', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B7%E6%B2%B3', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:29:14.544371Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.724271Z'),
(4945, 'vocabulary', '運河', '運河', 'It can <kanji>carry</kanji> a <kanji>river</kanji>. It''s a <vocabulary>canal</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4945', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8B%E6%B2%B3', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:32:43.258133Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.747955Z'),
(4946, 'vocabulary', '河豚', '河豚', 'The <kanji>river</kanji> <kanji>pig</kanji> is what''s known as <vocabulary>fugu</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>blowfish</vocabulary>. You may know it from our mascot and best friend, the Tofugu!

But why does "river pig" = fugu? It''s true that fugu usually live in the ocean, but sometimes they make their way into rivers near the ocean too. The "pig" part probably comes from their round, puffed-up appearance.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4946', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%B3%E8%B1%9A', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:32:51.796009Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.758704Z'),
(4947, 'vocabulary', '激しい', '激しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>fierce</kanji> and <kanji>violent</kanji>? <vocabulary>Fierce</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>violent</vocabulary>! That''s <vocabulary>intense</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4947', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BF%80%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:32:58.415421Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.768576Z'),
(4948, 'vocabulary', '燃える', '燃える', 'Remember how 燃やす means "to burn something" or "to set on fire”? Well, 燃える is the version that means <vocabulary>to burn</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be on fire</vocabulary>. You might say that paper and wood 燃える easily, for example. And if your house is 燃えている, you''re in trouble.

You can remember this slightly different meaning for 燃える because of the える part, which refers to the letter "L." And the "L" stands for "love" — as in your passionate, burning love for kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4948', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%87%83%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:33:05.573154Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.778507Z'),
(4949, 'vocabulary', '燃え付く', '燃え付く', 'When something <kanji>attach</kanji>es a <kanji>burn</kanji> to something that something is about <vocabulary>to catch fire</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4949', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%87%83%E3%81%88%E4%BB%98%E3%81%8F', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:33:12.601534Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:03:42.596356Z'),
(4950, 'vocabulary', '独り', '独り', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

While 一人 simply means "one person," 独り carries a stronger sense of being <vocabulary>alone</vocabulary>, often with connotations of <vocabulary>solitude</vocabulary>. It can also mean someone is <vocabulary>single</vocabulary> (as in not married). ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4950', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%AC%E3%82%8A', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:33:20.833885Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.798759Z'),
(4951, 'vocabulary', '独身', '独身', 'One who is <kanji>alone</kanji> with <kanji>oneself</kanji> simply hasn''t paired up in marriage yet — in other words, they''re <vocabulary>single</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>unmarried</vocabulary>.

独身 refers specifically to marital status. It just means that someone doesn''t have a spouse, not that they''re lonely or tragically eating ice cream by themselves or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4951', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%AC%E8%BA%AB', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:33:30.657932Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:20:52.348005Z'),
(4952, 'vocabulary', '独特', '独特', 'It is <kanji>alone</kanji> and <kanji>special</kanji>. Nothing is like it. It is very <vocabulary>unique</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>peculiar</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4952', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%AC%E7%89%B9', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:33:37.484248Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.820038Z'),
(4953, 'vocabulary', '独立', '独立', '<kanji>Alone</kanji> I <kanji>stand</kanji>. I have at last gained my <vocabulary>independence</vocabulary>!

独立 implies breaking off or separating from something or someone and achieving independent <vocabulary>autonomy</vocabulary>. While it can also refer to self-reliance, it''s more commonly associated with political independence or independence from organizations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4953', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%AC%E7%AB%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:33:43.621264Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T22:04:39.849178Z'),
(4954, 'vocabulary', '独学', '独学', 'You are <kanji>alone</kanji> in your <kanji>study</kanji>. You are studying on your own. This is <vocabulary>self study</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4954', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%AC%E5%AD%A6', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:33:47.946215Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.844314Z'),
(4955, 'vocabulary', '獣', '獣', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4955', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8D%A3', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:33:55.20972Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.85644Z'),
(4956, 'vocabulary', '怪獣', '怪獣', 'Look at that <kanji>suspicious</kanji> <kanji>beast</kanji>... it''s Godzilla, walking around looking suspicious. What is Godzilla? He is a <vocabulary>giant monster</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4956', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%AA%E7%8D%A3', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:34:08.188943Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.867042Z'),
(4957, 'vocabulary', '獣医', '獣医', 'One who does <kanji>beast</kanji> <kanji>medicine</kanji> is a <vocabulary>veterinarian</vocabulary>, taking care of all them animals.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4957', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8D%A3%E5%8C%BB', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:34:20.259793Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.90001Z'),
(4958, 'vocabulary', '獣類', '獣類', 'Things that are of the <kanji>beast</kanji> <kanji>type</kanji> or <kanji>animal</kanji> <kanji>type</kanji> are within the <vocabulary>beasts</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>animals</vocabulary> category.

獣類 refers to four-footed mammals with fur. It’s a technical term generally used in the fields of biology and zoology to categorize animals.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4958', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8D%A3%E9%A1%9E', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:34:26.380055Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:32:59.293769Z'),
(4959, 'vocabulary', '野獣', '野獣', 'A <kanji>field</kanji> <kanji>beast/animal</kanji> is one that lives outside and not under human care. It is a <vocabulary>wild beast</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>wild animal</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4959', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8E%E7%8D%A3', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:34:32.386969Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.945706Z'),
(4960, 'vocabulary', '突く', '突く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>stab</kanji> so the verb vocab version must also be <vocabulary>to stab</vocabulary>.

Wondering how it''s different from 刺す? 突く refers to the act of pushing or pressing something with a tip of an object, often done forcefully and swiftly. So when you use 突く as in "to stab," your focus is on the forcefulness of the action. On the other hand, 刺す focuses more on the actual act of piercing into something or someone with a sharp thing. 

This is why 突く can be be translated many ways, like <vocabulary>to poke</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to jab</vocabulary>, and many more. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4960', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AA%81%E3%81%8F', 24, 1, '2013-01-18T00:34:41.510912Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:04.008267Z'),
(4961, 'vocabulary', '突然', '突然', 'When something happens with a <kanji>sudden</kanji> <kanji>nature</kanji>, it happens... <vocabulary>suddenly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4961', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AA%81%E7%84%B6', 23, 1, '2013-01-18T00:34:48.446023Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:04.046612Z'),
(4962, 'vocabulary', '筆', '筆', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4962', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AD%86', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:34:54.950114Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.957433Z'),
(4963, 'vocabulary', '鉛筆', '鉛筆', 'Your <kanji>lead</kanji> <kanji>writing brush</kanji> has lead in it. What kind of writing instrument has lead in it? How about a <vocabulary>pencil</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4963', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%9B%E7%AD%86', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:34:59.738476Z', NULL, '2026-03-01T05:30:15.446094Z'),
(4964, 'vocabulary', '管理人', '管理人', 'Remember how 管理 is "management"? Well, a management person is someone who manages a place, like the <vocabulary>superintendent</vocabulary> for a building or park, or the <vocabulary>administrator</vocabulary> of a website. 

It can also refer to someone who is appointed to manage an estate, like a property <vocabulary>custodian</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4964', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AE%A1%E7%90%86%E4%BA%BA', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:35:24.39292Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:19:37.274094Z'),
(4965, 'vocabulary', 'お菓子', 'お菓子', '<kanji>Cake</kanji> is a <kanji>child</kanji>''s favorite <vocabulary>snack food</vocabulary>. This word refers to all the junky snacks you can imagine, <vocabulary>candy</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>pastries</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>sweets</vocabulary>, but also savory snacks too. You know, like chips and stuff... 🤤', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4965', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E8%8F%93%E5%AD%90', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:35:48.261805Z', NULL, '2026-03-20T15:33:33.523621Z'),
(4966, 'vocabulary', '触れる', '触れる', '触る means "to touch," and 触れる also means <vocabulary>to touch</vocabulary>. The word also means <vocabulary>to touch on</vocabulary>, as in touching on some subject. Just think of a subject that is <reading>rare</reading> (れる) for you to want to touch on.

触れる is often for when something happens <vocabulary>to come into contact with</vocabulary> something, possibly in a fleeting or momentary way. It''s still used in phrases like "When I touched your hand," but has a lighter, more indirect and literary feel than 触る, which conveys a greater sense of purpose and intent behind the action.

触れる is often used in constructions like 手がカバンに触れる (one''s hand touches a bag), where が indicates what''s doing the touching and に marks what''s being touched. If you see を instead of が, 触れる becomes transitive, changing the meaning to "to have your hand touch a bag."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4966', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A6%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:35:53.327037Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.003273Z'),
(4967, 'vocabulary', '検討する', '検討する', 'You <kanji>examine</kanji> it but you also <kanji>chastise</kanji> it, because you want to make sure this examining is correct. This is <vocabulary>to examine</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to study</vocabulary>. All require chastising things to be sure for sure.

検討する generally implies thoroughly examining something <vocabulary>to consider</vocabulary> its pros and cons, suitability, or viability.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4967', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A4%9C%E8%A8%8E%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:35:59.47146Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.014042Z'),
(4968, 'vocabulary', '討論', '討論', 'I''m going to <kanji> chastise</kanji> you about this <kanji>theory</kanji>. When I do this, this is a <vocabulary>debate</vocabulary>. Doesn''t it feel like you''re being chastised when people are debating with you sometimes, especially with your own theories? I think so.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4968', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%8E%E8%AB%96', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:36:08.075692Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.023969Z'),
(4969, 'vocabulary', 'お誕生日おめでとう', 'お誕生日おめでとう', '誕生日 is <kanji>birthday</kanji>. おめでとう is like "congratulations." So, congratulations for your birthday is like <vocabulary>happy birthday</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4969', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E8%AA%95%E7%94%9F%E6%97%A5%E3%81%8A%E3%82%81%E3%81%A7%E3%81%A8%E3%81%86', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:36:15.881962Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.034358Z'),
(4970, 'vocabulary', '豚', '豚', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4970', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B1%9A', 5, 1, '2013-01-18T00:36:21.259968Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.963528Z'),
(4971, 'vocabulary', '豚肉', '豚肉', 'A <kanji>pig</kanji>''s <kanji>meat</kanji> is called <vocabulary>pork</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4971', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B1%9A%E8%82%89', 6, 1, '2013-01-18T00:36:34.755932Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:44:17.079484Z'),
(4972, 'vocabulary', '自販機', '自販機', 'Remember the super long vending machine that is 自動販売機? This is a shorter easier to remember and say version. Basically every other kanji is removed, making it 自販機.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4972', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E8%B2%A9%E6%A9%9F', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:36:50.067949Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:35:42.046135Z'),
(4973, 'vocabulary', '限定販売', '限定販売', 'When a 限定 (limit) is applied to 販売 (selling), it’s a sort of <vocabulary>exclusive sale</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>limited offer</vocabulary>.

限定販売 is a sales strategy where companies limit the availability of their products to certain regions, stores, time frames, or club members.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4973', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%90%E5%AE%9A%E8%B2%A9%E5%A3%B2', 27, 1, '2013-01-18T00:36:56.345132Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(4974, 'vocabulary', '貯える', '貯える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. Notice how it shares its reading with 蓄える and its kanji with 貯める? It also has a similar meaning — <vocabulary>to set aside</vocabulary>.

Note that 貯える is less common in modern writing, and often feels a little old-fashioned. But you might still see it in contexts where it means <vocabulary>to store up</vocabulary> food and supplies for future needs (like 蓄える) or <vocabulary>to save up</vocabulary> money (like 貯める).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4974', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%AF%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 50, 1, '2013-01-18T00:37:30.260977Z', NULL, '2025-11-05T12:39:20.053646Z'),
(4975, 'vocabulary', '貯金', '貯金', 'Your <kanji>savings</kanji> <kanji>gold</kanji> is your <vocabulary>savings</vocabulary>. You''d be good to have gold right now in your savings, actually. Melt it down into bars, manngggg.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4975', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%AF%E9%87%91', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:37:35.087092Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.064972Z'),
(4976, 'vocabulary', '輸入', '輸入', 'The <kanji>transport</kanji> of things that <kanji>enter</kanji> your country are <vocabulary>import</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4976', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%B8%E5%85%A5', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:37:59.006205Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.074559Z'),
(4977, 'vocabulary', '輸出する', '輸出する', 'Remember how 輸出 is an export? This is the verb, so this is <vocabulary>to export</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4977', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%B8%E5%87%BA%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:38:20.510033Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.084299Z'),
(4978, 'vocabulary', '造る', '造る', 'The kanji means <kanji>create</kanji>, and this word is for the creation of big things. So it means <vocabulary>to construct</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to build</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to make</vocabulary>, and it''s usually used with ships, bridges, buildings, and other large-scale work.

It can also mean <vocabulary>to brew</vocabulary>, as in brewing sake. I guess brewing <em>is</em> like constructing, since it involves carefully choosing and mixing ingredients to get those sip-worthy creations!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4978', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%A0%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:38:26.492565Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.094244Z'),
(4979, 'vocabulary', '製造', '製造', '<kanji>Manufacture</kanji> and <kanji>create</kanji>. That is <vocabulary>manufacturing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4979', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%BD%E9%80%A0', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:38:31.768393Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.104403Z'),
(4980, 'vocabulary', '造園', '造園', '<kanji>Create</kanji> a <kanji>garden</kanji>? You''ll have to know a lot about <vocabulary>landscape gardening</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4980', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%A0%E5%9C%92', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:41:19.485148Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.11614Z'),
(4981, 'vocabulary', '改造', '改造', '<kanji>Renew</kanji> it by <kanji>creat</kanji>ing something anew. This is <vocabulary>remodeling</vocabulary> because you''re creating something and changing something that exists already, renewing it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4981', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%B9%E9%80%A0', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:44:24.606121Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.127404Z'),
(4982, 'vocabulary', '人造', '人造', 'A <kanji>person</kanji> <kanji>create</kanji>d item is <vocabulary>man made</vocabulary>. It''s <vocabulary>synthetic</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>artificial</vocabulary>.

Wonder what makes 人造 different from 人工? While 人工 means "artificial" in general, 人造 specifically refers to something that is produced or constructed by humans. It''s not as commonly used as 人工 and is often found in creative writing when talking about 人造人間 (androids).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4982', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E9%80%A0', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:44:45.486692Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(4983, 'vocabulary', '酒造', '酒造', 'The act of <kanji>alcohol</kanji> <kanji>creat</kanji>ion is done via <vocabulary>brewing</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>distilling</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4983', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%92%E9%80%A0', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:45:19.744271Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.149627Z'),
(4984, 'vocabulary', '気違い', '気違い', 'Your <kanji>energy</kanji> is ... well ... <kanji>different</kanji>. That''s the nice way to put it. You''re <vocabulary>crazy</vocabulary>, actually.

WARNING: We''re teaching you this word because it''s used quite a bit still (especially online) and we want you to be able to understand it if you see it. But please keep in mind that this is considered to be a discriminatory word against people with mental illnesses, so we strongly recommend you do not use or direct this word toward anyone. Think of it like a really inappropriate swear word that makes you really uncool if you say it. Please!

While you still see the kanji version, this word is commonly written in katakana.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4984', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E9%81%95%E3%81%84', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:45:48.327351Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(4985, 'vocabulary', '人違い', '人違い', 'A <kanji>person</kanji> who is <kanji>different</kanji> from the person you thought it was is a <vocabulary>mistaken identity</vocabulary>. Whoops, sorry, I thought you were someone else. 人違い!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4985', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E9%81%95%E3%81%84', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:45:54.74166Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(4986, 'vocabulary', '読み違い', '読み違い', 'When you <kanji>read</kanji> something in a <kanji>different</kanji> way from the way you''re supposed to it''s a <vocabulary>misreading</vocabulary>.

This word can also be used figuratively, like when you misread a situation or someone’s intentions, and your expectations turn out to be incorrect.

Note that 読み間違い can also be used to mean the same thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4986', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%AD%E3%81%BF%E9%81%95%E3%81%84', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:46:10.189953Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.305818Z'),
(4987, 'vocabulary', '大違い', '大違い', '違い is <kanji>difference</kanji>, so a <kanji>big</kanji> one of those is a <vocabulary>big difference</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4987', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E9%81%95%E3%81%84', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:46:16.880134Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(4988, 'vocabulary', '針医-4988', '針医', '<kanji>Needle</kanji> <kanji>medicine</kanji> is when you stick needles into people to cure them. This is done via an <vocabulary>acupuncturist</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4988', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%9D%E5%8C%BB-4988', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:46:30.413415Z', '2025-06-25T14:19:16.420854Z', '2025-09-11T05:41:46.190431Z'),
(4989, 'vocabulary', '針金', '針金', 'A <kanji>needle</kanji> made of <kanji>gold</kanji> is a <vocabulary>wire</vocabulary>. Perhaps wires were made from gold mostly back in the day? And they were thin like needles? That''s my guess.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4989', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%9D%E9%87%91', 28, 1, '2013-01-18T00:48:08.47185Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:04.812573Z'),
(4990, 'vocabulary', '秒針', '秒針', 'The <kanji>second</kanji> <kanji>needle</kanji> is the thin hand that indicates seconds on your clock. That''s the <vocabulary>second hand</vocabulary> of a clock.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4990', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%92%E9%87%9D', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:48:16.021421Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.199982Z'),
(4991, 'vocabulary', '鉛', '鉛', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4991', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%9B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:48:28.242687Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.209486Z'),
(4992, 'vocabulary', '鉛管', '鉛管', 'A <kanji>lead</kanji> <kanji>pipe</kanji> is a <vocabulary>lead pipe</vocabulary>. Don''t hit people with this in the knees.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4992', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%9B%E7%AE%A1', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:48:35.017003Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.223354Z'),
(4993, 'vocabulary', '鉛毒', '鉛毒', '<kanji>Lead</kanji> <kanji>poison</kanji> is <vocabulary>lead toxicity</vocabulary>.

This word generally refers to the toxicity of lead. While it can also refer to <vocabulary>lead poisoning</vocabulary>, 鉛中毒 (なまりちゅうどく) is the more common word for that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4993', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%9B%E6%AF%92', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:48:43.738204Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:27:49.000158Z'),
(4994, 'vocabulary', '障る', '障る', 'This is the verb version of <kanji>hinder</kanji>, but it''s what happens when you hinder someone else enough. You''ll start <vocabulary>to irritate</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to annoy</vocabulary> them. 

Note that this word isn''t used by itself, but in phrases like 気に障る (to hurt one''s feelings) or 体に障る (<vocabulary>to be bad for</vocabulary> one''s health). Check out the Common Word Combinations for more examples!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4994', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%9C%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:49:09.911547Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T02:34:05.341893Z'),
(4995, 'vocabulary', '障子', '障子', 'It can <kanji>hinder</kanji> a <kanji>child</kanji>, but that''s about it. This is a <vocabulary>shoji screen</vocabulary>, which is a type of <vocabulary>Japanese sliding door</vocabulary>. They''re typically covered with thin paper, so, you know, an adult can go through it but a child would be hindered by it.

Shoji are made of thin paper stretched across a wooden lattice frame. They''re lightweight and designed to let in some natural light.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4995', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%9C%E5%AD%90', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:49:32.515002Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.271977Z'),
(4996, 'vocabulary', '障害', '障害', 'There is <kanji>damage</kanji> that <kanji>hinder</kanji>s you from doing things normally. This is a <vocabulary>disability</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4996', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%9C%E5%AE%B3', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:50:34.395914Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(4997, 'vocabulary', '保障', '保障', 'If you <kanji>preserve</kanji> and don''t <kanji>hinder</kanji>, you''re providing <vocabulary>security</vocabulary>, you''re <vocabulary>safeguarding</vocabulary> something.

This word is used in contexts where the emphasis is on protecting from harm. For example, you''ll see it in compound words like 社会保障 (social security) 安全保障 (security) and 人権保障 (safeguarding human rights).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4997', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%9D%E9%9A%9C', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:50:40.203166Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.293523Z'),
(4998, 'vocabulary', '過ごす', '過ごす', '過ぎる means "to pass," and 過ごす is <vocabulary>to pass time</vocabulary>.

過ごす refers to how you live through or experience your time. So it usually needs some extra context when you use it, like what you do while passing time, who you pass it with, or what was it like. Check out the collocations and context sentences to get a better grasp of this word!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4998', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8E%E3%81%94%E3%81%99', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:50:40.285904Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.317543Z'),
(4999, 'vocabulary', '郵便', '郵便', '<kanji>Mail</kanji> (or <kanji>post</kanji>) has <kanji>convenience</kanji> because it is sent for you in the <vocabulary>mail</vocabulary> (or the <vocabulary>post</vocabulary>!).

郵便 originally referred to the postal system, but eventually also came to refer to mail itself as a shorthand for 郵便物.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/4999', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%B5%E4%BE%BF', 26, 1, '2013-01-18T00:50:46.590248Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:11:22.551299Z'),
(5000, 'vocabulary', '腰抜け', '腰抜け', 'You learned that 抜ける is "to fall out." If your <kanji>waist</kanji> falls out, you can''t remain standing. You have to curl up on the ground, like a <vocabulary>coward</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5000', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%B0%E6%8A%9C%E3%81%91', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:22:58.000022Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.110516Z')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;


This can be used for air coming out of a balloon, hair falling out, or pages missing from a book. It also means passing through places or leaving a group you were a part of.

To distinguish this word from 抜く, imagine a bunch of <reading>kale</reading> (ける) coming out of the ground on its own! Nobody pulled it out, it just came out by itself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5001', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%9C%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:23:13.021553Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.145083Z'),
(5002, 'vocabulary', '捕える', '捕える', 'The kanji itself means <kanji>catch</kanji>, and 捕える means <vocabulary>to capture</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to catch</vocabulary>.

捕える and 捕まえる both mean "to catch," but 捕える tends to sound more weighty and literary — you''re far more likely to see it in a book talking about the capture of a fleeing enemy general than to hear it from a kid who just caught a grasshopper.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5002', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%95%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2013-01-24T04:23:17.887007Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T06:57:25.419028Z'),
(5003, 'vocabulary', '掛かる', '掛かる', 'Remember how 掛ける is "to hang something"? Well, 掛かる focuses on the fact that something is hanging, without specifying who did it. So it means <vocabulary>to be hung</vocabulary>. Use it to say a picture is hung on the wall, for example. 

掛かる can also mean <vocabulary>to be hooked</vocabulary>, whether it''s the picture hooked to the wall or a fish that happens <vocabulary>to be caught</vocabulary> on a hook. 

These uses are all intransitive, so you don''t need a direct object them. A hint for this is that 掛かる ends with okurigana that rhyme with ある, and those kinds of verbs tend to be intransitive.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5003', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%9B%E3%81%8B%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:23:25.329731Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:45:29.915612Z'),
(5004, 'vocabulary', '隠れる', '隠れる', '隠す is "to hide something." 隠れる is <vocabulary>to hide oneself</vocabulary> or just <vocabulary>to hide</vocabulary>. You know this because when someone is hiding, it''s <reading>rare</reading> (れる) for anyone else to find them. They take this hide-and-seek thing pretty seriously, you know!

This word can also mean <vocabulary>to be hidden</vocabulary> because sometimes the subject is an inanimate object, as in 月が雲に隠れる (the moon is hidden in the cloud).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5004', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%A0%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:23:37.740736Z', NULL, '2025-08-27T00:02:29.618118Z'),
(5005, 'vocabulary', '値段', '値段', 'The <kanji>value</kanji> is in various <kanji>steps</kanji>. Each step is a different amount, and each amount is a <vocabulary>price</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>cost</vocabulary>.

値段 refers to the price attached to something you can buy, like in stores or markets. For example, you could use 値段 to ask how much something costs at a shop or when talking about the price of a particular meal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5005', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%A4%E6%AE%B5', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:24:02.270362Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:09:02.854992Z'),
(5006, 'vocabulary', '健康', '健康', 'You''re so <kanji>healthy</kanji> it puts you at <kanji>ease</kanji>. That''s your <vocabulary>health</vocabulary>! So healthful, go you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5006', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%A5%E5%BA%B7', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:24:08.443119Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.293054Z'),
(5007, 'vocabulary', '健忘症', '健忘症', 'A <kanji>healthy</kanji> bit of <kanji>forget</kanji>ting <kanji>symptom</kanji>s means you might have <vocabulary>amnesia</vocabulary>.

健忘症 often refers to short-term <vocabulary>memory loss</vocabulary>, either full or partial, but some people might also use it in a sort of joking way for everyday <vocabulary>forgetfulness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5007', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%A5%E5%BF%98%E7%97%87', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:24:13.576015Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.326015Z'),
(5008, 'vocabulary', '効果的', '効果的', 'Remember how 効果 means "effect"? Add <kanji>~like</kanji> to that and it''s <vocabulary>effective</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5008', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%B9%E6%9E%9C%E7%9A%84', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:24:18.680923Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.363015Z'),
(5009, 'vocabulary', '就く', '就く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>get a position</kanji>. The verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to get a position</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to get a job</vocabulary>.

Note that 就く is used with another word that indicates a position or a job, like 王位に就く(becoming a king) or 仕事に就く (finding employment).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5009', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%B1%E3%81%8F', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T04:24:22.688742Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T16:35:25.81941Z'),
(5010, 'vocabulary', '就職', '就職', '<kanji>Getting a position</kanji> of <kanji>employment</kanji> means <vocabulary>finding employment</vocabulary>.

就職 means <vocabulary>getting a job</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>starting employment</vocabulary>, usually after finishing school. It refers to formally entering the workforce, not just casually picking up part-time work. You''ll hear it a lot in phrases like 就職活動 (job hunting) or 就職先 (place of employment).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5010', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%B1%E8%81%B7', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:24:31.412823Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:44:44.581601Z'),
(5011, 'vocabulary', '就業', '就業', 'When you <kanji>get a position</kanji> in some <kanji>business</kanji>, you’re considered <vocabulary>working</vocabulary>.

就業 is a formal word that can refer to both <vocabulary>being employed</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>being at work</vocabulary> for the day. It’s commonly used in kanji compounds, such as 就業時間 (working hours) and 就業規則 (employment regulations).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5011', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%B1%E6%A5%AD', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:24:39.305988Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T16:35:25.81941Z'),
(5012, 'vocabulary', '退屈', '退屈', 'I <kanji>retreat</kanji>! I <kanji>yield</kanji>! I can''t take it anymore, it''s so <vocabulary>boring</vocabulary>. Just imagine yourself giving up on whatever you''re doing because you''re so darn bored. You can''t stand it. You have to retreat. You are ready to yield. Booooooring.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5012', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%80%E5%B1%88', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:24:50.974913Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.48331Z'),
(5013, 'vocabulary', '巨大', '巨大', 'A <kanji>giant</kanji> <kanji>big</kanji> thing is something that is <vocabulary>huge</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>gigantic</vocabulary>.

巨大 is often used in writing to describe something <vocabulary>enormous</vocabulary>. It can be for anything of immense scale, not just physical objects like buildings or monsters, but also abstract things like the impact of a natural disaster or even a person''s influence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5013', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A8%E5%A4%A7', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T04:24:56.560031Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(5014, 'vocabulary', '巨人', '巨人', 'A <kanji>giant</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is <vocabulary>a giant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5014', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A8%E4%BA%BA', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T04:25:01.170871Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(5015, 'vocabulary', '巻きずし', '巻きずし', '巻く is "to roll." ずし is すし. This is <vocabulary>rolled sushi</vocabulary>.

Makizushi refers to cylindrical rolled sushi, which is commonly served in bite-sized pieces. If it''s shaped like a cone, it''s known as 手巻きずし (temakizushi).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5015', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%BB%E3%81%8D%E3%81%9A%E3%81%97', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:25:05.906809Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.568476Z'),
(5016, 'vocabulary', '春巻き', '春巻き', '巻く is "to roll up." If you roll up spring, you get a roll that''s spring flavored. These are known as <vocabulary>spring rolls</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5016', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%A5%E5%B7%BB%E3%81%8D', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:25:11.317464Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:35:30.470443Z'),
(5017, 'vocabulary', '頑張れ', '頑張れ', 'Remember 頑張る means “to persevere” or “to do one’s best”? This time you''re telling someone to 頑張る, so this word means something like <vocabulary>do your best</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>hang in there</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>good luck</vocabulary>! Speaking of which, 頑張れ, you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5017', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%91%E5%BC%B5%E3%82%8C', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:25:16.35568Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.626494Z'),
(5018, 'vocabulary', '怒る', '怒る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>angry</kanji>, the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to get angry</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5018', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%92%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:25:24.128214Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.655457Z'),
(5019, 'vocabulary', '怒鳴る', '怒鳴る', 'An <kanji>angry</kanji> <kanji>chirp</kanji> is a type of shout. The verb version is <vocabulary>to yell</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to shout</vocabulary>.

怒鳴る suggests anger, as you can tell from the kanji, so it''s used when someone yells or shouts to scold others or to express anger or frustration.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5019', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%92%E9%B3%B4%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:25:28.641628Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:38:47.781967Z'),
(5020, 'vocabulary', '悩み', '悩み', 'This is the noun version of <kanji>worry</kanji>, making it <vocabulary>a worry</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>a trouble</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5020', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%A9%E3%81%BF', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:25:39.223227Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.71977Z'),
(5021, 'vocabulary', '悩む', '悩む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>worry</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to worry</vocabulary>.

悩む is often intransitive, like 彼はいつも悩んでいる (he''s always worrying). But it can also be transitive, in which case it''ll take a direct object, like 彼女のことを悩む (to worry about her). ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5021', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%A9%E3%82%80', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:25:46.093764Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.750657Z'),
(5022, 'vocabulary', '惑う', '惑う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>misguided</kanji>, but the verb version is usually used to describe how you feel after being misguided — <vocabulary>to be at a loss</vocabulary>!

惑う usually refers to being in a quandary or a state of confusion. Using 惑う is kind of old-fashioned, but other forms like 惑わせる and 逃げ惑う (to flee frantically) are still fairly common in modern Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5022', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%91%E3%81%86', 39, 1, '2013-01-24T04:26:58.142894Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.785048Z'),
(5023, 'vocabulary', '迷惑', '迷惑', 'You''ve been led <kanji>astray</kanji> and you''ve been <kanji>misguided</kanji>. What a <vocabulary>trouble</vocabulary>, and indeed a <vocabulary>troublesome</vocabulary> <vocabulary>inconvenience</vocabulary> all this is.

迷惑 refers to a <vocabulary>nuisance</vocabulary> that bothers others, like trimming your toenails or speaking loudly on the phone in public.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5023', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%B7%E6%83%91', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:27:07.760602Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.813733Z'),
(5024, 'vocabulary', '誘惑', '誘惑', 'You''re being <kanji>invited</kanji>, but you''re also being <kanji>misguided</kanji>. This is <vocabulary>seduction</vocabulary> that''s talking. A misguided invitation is never a good thing!

This word also translates to <vocabulary>temptation</vocabulary>, but keep in mind that 誘惑’s focus is more on an external factor that entices someone into succumbing to temptation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5024', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%98%E6%83%91', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:27:12.735337Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.84436Z'),
(5025, 'vocabulary', '見抜く', '見抜く', 'You know 抜く means "to pull something out." Whenever you <kanji>see</kanji> something and pull something out of it, you''re able <vocabulary>to see something''s true nature</vocabulary>. You''re able <vocabulary>to see through</vocabulary> things like situations, lies, and people.

見抜く often suggests figuring something out instinctively, with sharp insight or keen perception, especially when it comes to understanding the essence or true nature of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5025', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E6%8A%9C%E3%81%8F', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:27:22.892303Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.867061Z'),
(5026, 'vocabulary', '間抜け', '間抜け', 'You learned that 抜ける is "to fall out." If an <kanji>interval of time</kanji> falls out of you, you''re totally spaced out. You won''t learn anything and you''ll become an <vocabulary>idiot</vocabulary>.

間抜け can refer to both a person who does something foolish and to the foolish actions themselves. It''s often used to criticize <vocabulary>dumb</vocabulary> behavior.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5026', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%93%E6%8A%9C%E3%81%91', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:27:27.798992Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.902974Z'),
(5027, 'vocabulary', '抜き出す', '抜き出す', 'You know 抜く means "to pull something out" and 出す means "to take out," so it''s no surprise this word also means <vocabulary>to pull something out</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to extract</vocabulary>.

More specifically, 抜き出す means to pull something out from a larger set of something. For example, this could mean taking a single bill out of your wallet, or selecting specific data from a large dataset.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5027', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%9C%E3%81%8D%E5%87%BA%E3%81%99', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:27:37.754568Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:32:55.61179Z'),
(5028, 'vocabulary', '招く', '招く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>beckon</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to beckon</vocabulary>. 

Since 招く conjures up the image of someone beckoning, it also means <vocabulary>to invite</vocabulary>, and is a relatively formal word. Figuratively, it can also mean <vocabulary>to cause</vocabulary>, as in inviting some undesirable outcome or situation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5028', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%9B%E3%81%8F', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T04:27:44.012153Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:05.977484Z'),
(5029, 'vocabulary', '招待する', '招待する', 'You <kanji>beckon</kanji> someone to come to you, but then you <kanji>wait</kanji>. This is because they aren''t really near you, so your beckon is more like an invitation. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>to invite</vocabulary> .

招待する is a formal word for inviting someone, particularly to formal events or places.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5029', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%9B%E5%BE%85%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:27:54.253098Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.007357Z'),
(5030, 'vocabulary', '手掛かり', '手掛かり', 'You learned that 掛かる can mean "to be hooked." If you''re doing something physical like climbing and your <kanji>hand</kanji> hooks onto something, that thing is called a <vocabulary>handhold</vocabulary>.

Figuratively, this can also mean <vocabulary>clue</vocabulary>, since a clue is something you grasp onto, much like a handhold, to help you progress in solving a crime or mystery. This figurative use of 手掛かり is actually the most common!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5030', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E6%8E%9B%E3%81%8B%E3%82%8A', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:27:59.153276Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:45:29.915612Z'),
(5031, 'vocabulary', '昇る', '昇る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>ascend</kanji> so the verb vocab version must be <vocabulary>to ascend</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to rise</vocabulary>.

昇る is commonly used to talk about things like the sun and the moon rising, or smoke rising from a fire.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5031', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%87%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T04:28:04.203501Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.626682Z'),
(5032, 'vocabulary', '昇進', '昇進', '<kanji>Ascend</kanji> and <kanji>advance</kanji>! Congratulations, you ascend and advance by getting a new <vocabulary>job promotion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5032', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%87%E9%80%B2', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:28:09.940637Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.087765Z'),
(5033, 'vocabulary', '暇', '暇', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

暇 means the <vocabulary>spare time</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>free time</vocabulary> you have when you''re <vocabulary>not busy</vocabulary> with work, school, or other obligations. It can also indicate a slow period for a business or a time when you have nothing to do, often suggesting a sense of boredom.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5033', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%87', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:28:15.208401Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.115792Z'),
(5034, 'vocabulary', '休暇', '休暇', 'The <kanji>spare time</kanji> is your <vocabulary>vacation</vocabulary>. It is your <vocabulary>holiday</vocabulary>.

休暇 is a relatively formal word that commonly refers to <vocabulary>time off</vocabulary> from work, ranging from a day off to seasonal vacation to temporary leave.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5034', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%91%E6%9A%87', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:28:19.727786Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.150456Z'),
(5035, 'vocabulary', '両替', '両替', 'Coins? Bills? <kanji>Both</kanji> can be <kanji>replace</kanji>d if you want to <vocabulary>change money</vocabulary> at this <vocabulary>money exchange</vocabulary>. Just don’t do your <vocabulary>currency exchange</vocabulary> at the airport — the rates aren’t so great.

You''ll probably see this word the most in arcades where you can go to the 両替 machine and get coins to feed your crane game addiction.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5035', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%A1%E6%9B%BF', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:28:24.729947Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:45:39.570399Z'),
(5036, 'vocabulary', '取り替える', '取り替える', '<kanji>Take</kanji> and <kanji>replace</kanji> something. That is <vocabulary>to replace</vocabulary> something with something else.

取り替える generally means <vocabulary>to swap out</vocabulary> something with a new or different item of the same kind, like replacing an old gas meter with a new one, or exchanging books with your friend after you''ve both read your own.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5036', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8A%E6%9B%BF%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:28:29.930268Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.219319Z'),
(5037, 'vocabulary', '着替え', '着替え', 'You know how 替える is "to replace"? Well, you''re replacing what you <kanji>wear</kanji>. That''s a <vocabulary>change of clothes</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>spare clothes</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5037', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9D%80%E6%9B%BF%E3%81%88', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:28:35.070592Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(5038, 'vocabulary', '究極', '究極', 'You are trying to <kanji>research</kanji> the <kanji>extreme</kanji>s. If you do that, you''ll be able to figure out the <vocabulary>ultimate</vocabulary> weapon. Just researching the safe middle-ground stuff won''t get you ultimate. Extreme research, however, will.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5038', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%B6%E6%A5%B5', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:28:41.24294Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:11:35.013012Z'),
(5039, 'vocabulary', '極める', '極める', 'The kanji itself means <kanji>extreme</kanji>. The verb vocab version isn''t "to extreme" because that wouldn''t make sense. Instead, you bring something to extremes, making this <vocabulary>to reach the peak of something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to perfect</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to reach the extreme</vocabulary>.

極める commonly means reaching the highest level of proficiency or excellence in a particular skill, art, or field. It can also mean taking something to the extreme, like being crazy busy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5039', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%B5%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T04:28:45.875397Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.313722Z'),
(5040, 'vocabulary', '極楽', '極楽', 'This place has <kanji>extreme</kanji> <kanji>comfort</kanji>. It is <vocabulary>paradise</vocabulary>.

極楽 is a short for 極楽浄土 (the Pure Land in Mahayana Buddhism). While it might sound a bit old-fashioned, you can still use it to describe something heavenly. For example, if you''re soaking in a cozy, relaxing hot spring, you could say, "Ahhh…極楽!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5040', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%B5%E6%A5%BD', 54, 1, '2013-01-24T04:28:51.231357Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T18:49:45.773585Z'),
(5041, 'vocabulary', '南極', '南極', 'The <kanji>south</kanji>ern <kanji>extreme</kanji> is the <vocabulary>South Pole</vocabulary>, aka the <vocabulary>Antarctic</vocabulary>. It''s about as south as you can go.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5041', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%97%E6%A5%B5', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:28:58.32347Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T15:17:49.222453Z'),
(5042, 'vocabulary', '構え', '構え', 'You know 構える, which can mean to assume a stance? This is the noun version, making it <vocabulary>stance</vocabulary>.

構え is often used to describe specific stances or <vocabulary>posture</vocabulary>s, both in a literal and figurative sense. In this vein, it can also convey the notion of being mentally and physically prepared for action. Additionally, 構え is also used to refer to the <vocabulary>structure</vocabulary> of something, often focusing on visual appearance.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5042', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A7%8B%E3%81%88', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T04:29:08.853944Z', NULL, '2025-08-31T13:56:47.630506Z'),
(5043, 'vocabulary', '結構です', '結構です', '結構 is something like "fine" or "alright." This is just a little more polite. You can use this to say <vocabulary>no thank you</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>I''m fine</vocabulary> to someone trying to offer you something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5043', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%90%E6%A7%8B%E3%81%A7%E3%81%99', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:29:14.866643Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.438315Z'),
(5044, 'vocabulary', 'お構いなく', 'お構いなく', 'Remember how 構う is "to care about"? You can convert it into お構いなく to politely tell someone not to care about you. So this means, <vocabulary>don''t mind me</vocabulary>.

It can also mean <vocabulary>without regard for</vocabulary>, especially when someone keeps doing something without caring about other people, their surroundings, or unfavorable conditions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5044', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E6%A7%8B%E3%81%84%E3%81%AA%E3%81%8F', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:29:19.620068Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.474051Z'),
(5045, 'vocabulary', '大規模', '大規模', 'Remember how 規模 is <kanji>scale</kanji>? Well, this is a big scale, which is on a <vocabulary>large scale</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5045', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E8%A6%8F%E6%A8%A1', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:29:24.754746Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(5046, 'vocabulary', '小規模', '小規模', '規模 is "scale." This is a <kanji>small</kanji> one, making this <vocabulary>small scale</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5046', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E8%A6%8F%E6%A8%A1', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:29:29.986744Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.539015Z'),
(5047, 'vocabulary', '階段', '階段', 'To get to another <kanji>floor</kanji> you have to go up one <kanji>step</kanji>, then another, and another… These are <vocabulary>stairs</vocabulary>. The word you''re looking for is stairs! Do you have any 階段 in your house?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5047', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%8E%E6%AE%B5', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:29:34.477532Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.567935Z'),
(5048, 'vocabulary', '段々', '段々', '<kanji>Step</kanji> by <kanji>step</kanji>. If you take things step by step, you''re moving up <vocabulary>gradually</vocabulary>, taking <vocabulary>steps</vocabulary>.

段々 is often used to describe gradual progress. But sometimes you''ll see it used for terraced fields or buildings too, since they sorta resemble steps!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5048', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%B5%E3%80%85', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:29:44.713391Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T06:27:10.870175Z'),
(5049, 'vocabulary', '段階', '段階', 'Each time you take a <kanji>step</kanji> up, you reach a new <kanji>floor</kanji>… a new <vocabulary>stage</vocabulary>! We''re talking metaphorical stages. You''re making all kinds of progress. The first stage, the second stage. The third <vocabulary>phase</vocabulary>. Man, you''re blowing through all the stages and phases of Japanese learning. Keep it up!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5049', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%B5%E9%9A%8E', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:29:49.391406Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.626683Z'),
(5050, 'vocabulary', '第一段', '第一段', '第一 means "the first," and 段 is a <kanji>step</kanji>. So, this is <vocabulary>the first step</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5050', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E6%AE%B5', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:29:54.896797Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.651231Z'),
(5051, 'vocabulary', '濃い', '濃い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning it''s probably an adjective. The kanji means <kanji>dense</kanji>, and this can also mean <vocabulary>dense</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>thick</vocabulary> in some contexts, like if you’re describing fog, or body hair! More generally, it can be used for anything that’s highly concentrated, so you can use it for things like <vocabulary>deep</vocabulary> colors, or <vocabulary>strong</vocabulary> coffee too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5051', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BF%83%E3%81%84', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:30:06.949677Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.679647Z'),
(5052, 'vocabulary', '濃度', '濃度', 'When something is <kanji>dense</kanji> to a certain <kanji>degree</kanji>, you’re talking about the <vocabulary>density</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>concentration</vocabulary> of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5052', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BF%83%E5%BA%A6', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:30:19.902159Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.707899Z'),
(5053, 'vocabulary', '〜症', '〜症', 'Multiple <kanji>symptom</kanji>s combine to make a <vocabulary>syndrome</vocabulary>. If you see this at the end of a word, you know it means "______ syndrome," or else indicates some kind of health condition.  

〜症 is used in medical terms for certain health conditions, symptoms, or syndromes, like 熱中症 (heatstroke) or 認知症 (dementia).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5053', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E7%97%87', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:30:28.114628Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T17:39:54.765139Z'),
(5054, 'vocabulary', '症状', '症状', 'Your <kanji>symptom</kanji> <kanji>condition</kanji> is your <vocabulary>symptom</vocabulary>s that you have.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5054', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%87%E7%8A%B6', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:30:33.625215Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.765408Z'),
(5055, 'vocabulary', '不眠症', '不眠症', 'The <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>sleep</kanji> <kanji>symptom</kanji> shows you have a disorder that involves not sleeping. That is <vocabulary>insomnia</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5055', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E7%9C%A0%E7%97%87', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:30:38.694288Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:38:20.528806Z'),
(5056, 'vocabulary', '痛い', '痛い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>pain</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>painful</vocabulary>. 

痛い is commonly used to say that some part of the body hurts or feels sore.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5056', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%9B%E3%81%84', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:30:44.372153Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:48:39.716764Z'),
(5057, 'vocabulary', '頭痛', '頭痛', '<kanji>Head</kanji> <kanji>pain</kanji> is when you have a <vocabulary>headache</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5057', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%AD%E7%97%9B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:30:54.432881Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:48:39.716764Z'),
(5058, 'vocabulary', '眠い', '眠い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>sleep</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>sleepy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5058', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%A0%E3%81%84', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:31:11.377024Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.916735Z'),
(5059, 'vocabulary', '睡眠', '睡眠', 'Feeling <kanji>drowsy</kanji> and like I want to <kanji>sleep</kanji>. I just want to <vocabulary>sleep</vocabulary> so bad.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5059', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9D%A1%E7%9C%A0', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:31:25.354789Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T06:45:18.337984Z'),
(5060, 'vocabulary', '端', '端', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This refers to a literal edge, not a figurative one.

端 refers to the <vocabulary>edge</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>end</vocabulary> of something, like the edge of a table, the far end of a bridge, or even the <vocabulary>corner</vocabulary> of a page.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5060', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%AF', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:31:33.031448Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:06.978093Z'),
(5061, 'vocabulary', '先端', '先端', 'The <kanji>previous</kanji> (or <kanji>ahead</kanji>) <kanji>edge</kanji> is the <vocabulary>tip</vocabulary> of something, like your finger or your chopsticks. It''s also a word used to describe <vocabulary>the latest</vocabulary> (technology, etc), because it''s on the <vocabulary>cutting edge</vocabulary>. The tip!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5061', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%88%E7%AB%AF', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:31:38.160781Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:27:12.714404Z'),
(5062, 'vocabulary', '中途半端', '中途半端', 'Take the <kanji>middle</kanji> <kanji>route</kanji>, <kanji>half</kanji> to the <kanji>edge</kanji>. You don''t do anything the full way. Just to the middle. Just to the half. Not to the edge. This was really <vocabulary>half finished</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>half assed</vocabulary> of you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5062', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E9%80%94%E5%8D%8A%E7%AB%AF', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:31:45.628211Z', NULL, '2025-12-11T20:12:42.646308Z'),
(5063, 'vocabulary', '極端', '極端', 'At the <kanji>extreme</kanji> <kanji>edge</kanji>, which is just the <vocabulary>extreme</vocabulary>.

極端 means something is taken to the extreme without moderation, so it generally has a negative connotation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5063', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%B5%E7%AB%AF', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:31:50.762969Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:07.077043Z'),
(5064, 'vocabulary', '給料', '給料', 'Your <kanji>salary</kanji> <kanji>fee</kanji> are the moneys you get for your <vocabulary>salary</vocabulary>. These are your <vocabulary>wages</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>pay</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5064', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%A6%E6%96%99', 25, 1, '2013-01-24T04:31:55.602987Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:07.110824Z'),
(5065, 'vocabulary', '給与', '給与', 'The <kanji>salary</kanji> I <kanji>give</kanji> you is your <vocabulary>salary</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>wages</vocabulary>. It is the <vocabulary>pay</vocabulary> you earn.

Curious about the difference between 給与 and 給料? 給与 is generally considered the formal version of 給料, but there is another minor difference in nuance. 給料 often refers to your base pay, while 給与 refers to the total amount of <vocabulary>compensation</vocabulary> you receive, including overtime, bonuses, etc.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5065', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%A6%E4%B8%8E', 26, 1, '2013-01-24T04:32:03.635988Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.735198Z'),
(5066, 'vocabulary', '供給', '供給', 'If you can <kanji>offer</kanji> a <kanji>salary</kanji> to someone, it means you''ve got plenty of <vocabulary>supply</vocabulary>. The bigger the salary you can offer, the bigger your supply is, probably. That''s why those big tech companies can pay so well — they have an endless supply of paying customers or ad revenue, I guess. 

供給 is a somewhat formal word you''d generally hear in the context of economics — like <vocabulary>supplying</vocabulary> goods, energy, or food where there''s demand. You wouldn''t use it for art supplies or the food you buy every day.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5066', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%9B%E7%B5%A6', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:32:07.956403Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:06:42.844886Z'),
(5067, 'vocabulary', '減給', '減給', 'A <kanji>decrease</kanji> in <kanji>salary</kanji> is a <vocabulary>pay cut</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5067', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%9B%E7%B5%A6', 25, 1, '2013-01-24T04:32:14.285115Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:02.806385Z'),
(5068, 'vocabulary', '昇給', '昇給', 'When you <kanji>ascend</kanji> in <kanji>salary</kanji> you get a <vocabulary>pay raise</vocabulary> at work.

Note that this is a relatively formal word to talk about <vocabulary>getting a raise</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5068', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%87%E7%B5%A6', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:32:19.106201Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:07.236954Z'),
(5069, 'vocabulary', '締結する', '締結する', 'When you <kanji>tighten</kanji> things up and <kanji>bind</kanji> things together, it''s hard to separate them again. That''s why this word is used <vocabulary>to conclude</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to enter into</vocabulary> important agreements, like treaties and contracts.

Note that this word is quite formal, which makes sense, given the contexts it’s usually used in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5069', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A0%E7%B5%90%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:32:23.777919Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:39:13.80923Z'),
(5070, 'vocabulary', '締める', '締める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>tighten</kanji> so the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to tighten</vocabulary>, like a screw, or to tie, like a necktie or towel.

締める can also mean <vocabulary>to wrap up</vocabulary> when used in reference to a speech or some other occasion. In Japanese culture, people often 締める a situation with some shared action, like clapping in rhythm to end a celebration or having a final bowl of ramen to close out a night. You call it 締める because it''s like you''re tying off the event and giving it a sense of closure.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5070', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A0%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:32:31.91878Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:39:13.80923Z'),
(5071, 'vocabulary', '組織', '組織', 'A <kanji>group</kanji> that''s <kanji>weave</kanji>d together so tightly with hierarchy and everything else is a little more complicated than a group. It''s an <vocabulary>organization</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5071', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%84%E7%B9%94', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:32:36.871078Z', NULL, '2026-04-05T19:31:59.040578Z'),
(5072, 'vocabulary', '織る', '織る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>weave</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to weave</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5072', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B9%94%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:32:42.099142Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:07.377452Z'),
(5073, 'vocabulary', '胃痛', '胃痛', 'When your <kanji>stomach</kanji> <kanji>hurts</kanji> you have yourself a little <vocabulary>stomachache</vocabulary>.

Try to remember this isn''t for general abdominal pain. 胃痛 is specifically for pain or discomfort in the stomach (the internal organ) — a literal stomachache.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5073', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%83%E7%97%9B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:32:57.685234Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:48:39.716764Z'),
(5074, 'vocabulary', '腹', '腹', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5074', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%B9', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:33:04.122908Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:29:08.554779Z'),
(5075, 'vocabulary', 'お腹', 'お腹', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

お腹 is the common everyday word for your <vocabulary>belly</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>abdomen</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>stomach</vocabulary>. It''s common to see this word written in kanji, but you''ll also see it written in hiragana, especially in casual contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5075', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E8%85%B9', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T04:33:09.256258Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:29:08.554779Z'),
(5076, 'vocabulary', '腹立つ', '腹立つ', 'What''s that feeling when your <kanji>belly</kanji> <kanji>stands</kanji> up? It''s that feeling where something jumps up into your throat (in this case your belly) and you get all pissed off. This word means <vocabulary>to get mad</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5076', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%B9%E7%AB%8B%E3%81%A4', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T04:33:14.25293Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:29:08.554779Z'),
(5077, 'vocabulary', '切腹', '切腹', 'When you <kanji>cut</kanji> your <kanji>abdomen</kanji> you are committing <vocabulary>seppuku</vocabulary>. It will help if you already know the word "seppuku" from English, since it''s also the reading here.

切腹 is a form of <vocabulary>ritual suicide</vocabulary> by self-inflicted disembowelment, often performed by samurai to restore honor.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5077', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%87%E8%85%B9', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:33:25.990289Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:29:08.554779Z'),
(5078, 'vocabulary', '腹切り', '腹切り', 'You know 腹 means <kanji>belly</kanji>, and 切り is from 切る, meaning "to cut." Well, if you''re cutting your own belly, there''s a good chance you''re committing <vocabulary>harakiri</vocabulary>, a form of <vocabulary>ritual suicide</vocabulary>.

腹切り sounds blunt and colloquial compared to the more formal 切腹. Kind of like you''re just saying the words "belly cutting" — not very refined! This is often the case with synonym pairs like this: the one with kun''yomi readings will sound more casual, while the one with on''yomi readings will sound more formal. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5078', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%B9%E5%88%87%E3%82%8A', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T04:33:31.277376Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:29:08.554779Z'),
(5079, 'vocabulary', '訪ねる', '訪ねる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>visit</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to visit</vocabulary>.

訪ねる is a somewhat formal word you''ll mostly encounter in writing or very polite speech. It implies visiting somewhere or someone with a clear purpose. You''re not just dropping in because you were in the neighborhood — you have good reason to be visiting that specific place or person!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5079', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%AA%E3%81%AD%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:33:36.322724Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:09:18.096632Z'),
(5080, 'vocabulary', '訪問', '訪問', 'You <kanji>visit</kanji> someone (or somewhere) with a <kanji>question</kanji> in mind. This is <vocabulary>a visit</vocabulary>. Any proper <vocabulary>visit</vocabulary> tries to answer a question, right? Like, "How have you been?" or "What kind of place is this?" If there''s no question behind your visit, you''re kind of just wandering around.

訪問 is a somewhat formal word for visiting somewhere or calling on someone, so you''ll most often encounter it in writing or conversations in business or professional contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5080', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%AA%E5%95%8F', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:33:48.009774Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:08:40.698889Z'),
(5081, 'vocabulary', '誘う', '誘う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>invite</kanji>. The verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to invite</vocabulary>.

誘う refers to casually inviting someone to a place, an event, or an activity. If it''s an invitation to something tempting, it can also mean <vocabulary>to tempt</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to entice</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5081', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%98%E3%81%86', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T04:33:57.223243Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:15.514449Z'),
(5082, 'vocabulary', '貸す', '貸す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>lend</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to lend</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5082', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%B8%E3%81%99', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:34:03.061687Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:07:16.01728Z'),
(5083, 'vocabulary', '貸し切り', '貸し切り', 'The <kanji>lend</kanji>ing gets <kanji>cut</kanji> right away when you make a <vocabulary>private reservation</vocabulary>.

貸し切り means reserving a space exclusively, like renting out a restaurant for a birthday party or chartering a bus, cruise, or jet.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5083', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%B8%E3%81%97%E5%88%87%E3%82%8A', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:34:09.646432Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:07:16.01728Z'),
(5084, 'vocabulary', '迫る', '迫る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>urge</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to urge</vocabulary>.

At its core, 迫る means <vocabulary>to approach</vocabulary>, like a deadline or danger drawing near. This idea extends to urging someone because it''s like you''re coming really close to them, <vocabulary>to press</vocabulary> them into action.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5084', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%AB%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:34:15.557225Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:07.792979Z'),
(5085, 'vocabulary', '圧迫', '圧迫', 'The <kanji>pressure</kanji> is <kanji>urge</kanji>d upon you. It''s the <vocabulary>pressure</vocabulary>, so much pressure!

圧迫 refers to a tight pressure caused by strong compression, or to the act of pressing down itself. Figuratively, it can also mean <vocabulary>constriction</vocabulary>, like tuition fees putting a strain on a household budget, or <vocabulary>oppression</vocabulary>, such as people being subjugated.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5085', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%A7%E8%BF%AB', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:34:21.845439Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:28:34.36878Z'),
(5086, 'vocabulary', '迫害', '迫害', 'Someone has an <kanji>urge</kanji> to <kanji>damage</kanji> things. If that person wants to harm you, you might be facing <vocabulary>persecution</vocabulary>.

As you might guess, this word is quite formal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5086', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%AB%E5%AE%B3', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:34:26.773407Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(5087, 'vocabulary', '切迫', '切迫', 'We''re <kanji>cut</kanji> right down to the end, reaching that <kanji>urge</kanji> position where <vocabulary>urgency</vocabulary> is so strong.

切迫 is used to describe the tension in a situation where a time limit is <vocabulary>drawing near</vocabulary>, or to describe something bad or dangerous as <vocabulary>impending</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5087', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%87%E8%BF%AB', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:34:32.725286Z', NULL, '2025-10-16T19:52:23.875196Z'),
(5088, 'vocabulary', '迷う', '迷う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>astray</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to go astray</vocabulary> (because you don''t just "to astray" now do you?). You can use it if you ever happen <vocabulary>to get lost</vocabulary>. 

Beyond physically getting lost, 迷う is also for times when you waver between different choices or hesitate to take a decisive step.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5088', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%B7%E3%81%86', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:34:37.571964Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T01:01:20.041143Z'),
(5089, 'vocabulary', '迷子', '迷子', 'After going <kanji>astray</kanji>, the <kanji>child</kanji> becomes a <vocabulary>lost child</vocabulary>. Let''s help them find their way home.

Keep in mind that 迷子 isn''t only for lost children; it applies to anyone who''s lost, such as a <vocabulary>lost person</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>stray animal</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5089', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%B7%E5%AD%90', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:34:42.13656Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:07.942974Z'),
(5090, 'vocabulary', '迷路', '迷路', 'A <kanji>road</kanji> where you''re <kanji>astray</kanji> is a road that goes all around in different ways, tricking you and making you lost. This is a <vocabulary>maze</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5090', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%B7%E8%B7%AF', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:34:48.305188Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:55:20.905911Z'),
(5091, 'vocabulary', '迷信', '迷信', 'Led <kanji>astray</kanji> in what you <kanji>believe</kanji> by all these <vocabulary>superstition</vocabulary>s. These superstitions will lead you astray, don''t believe them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5091', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%B7%E4%BF%A1', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:34:53.204228Z', NULL, '2025-11-16T19:02:11.093561Z'),
(5092, 'vocabulary', '退く', '退く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>retreat</kanji>, and the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to retreat</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to withdraw</vocabulary>.

退く can also mean <vocabulary>to resign</vocabulary>, since resigning is a kind of a figurative retreat, like from a professional role or the forefront of some field or industry.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5092', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%80%E3%81%8F', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T04:35:02.789486Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.213614Z'),
(5093, 'vocabulary', '退院する', '退院する', 'Time to <kanji>retreat</kanji> from the <kanji>institution</kanji>. Basically, you are leaving an institution, particularly institutions like hospitals, asylums, etc. When you are leaving one of these places, it means you are <vocabulary>to be discharged</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5093', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%80%E9%99%A2%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:35:08.675804Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.06766Z'),
(5094, 'vocabulary', '早退', '早退', 'An <kanji>early</kanji> <kanji>retreat</kanji> means that you <vocabulary>leave early</vocabulary>.

Note that 早退 means leaving school or work earlier than usual. It''s not used for leaving hangouts or parties early.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5094', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A9%E9%80%80', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:35:20.571563Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:39:10.881814Z'),
(5095, 'vocabulary', '途中', '途中', 'If you''re on a <kanji>route</kanji>, right in the <kanji>middle</kanji>, you''re <vocabulary>on the way</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>en route</vocabulary>.

途中 can also be used figuratively to say you''re <vocabulary>in the middle of</vocabulary> doing something or something is <vocabulary>half done</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5095', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%94%E4%B8%AD', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:35:26.915414Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.131896Z'),
(5096, 'vocabulary', '逮捕する', '逮捕する', 'Remember how 逮捕 is an "arrest" or "capture"? This is the verb version, and it is <vocabulary>to arrest</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to capture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5096', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%AE%E6%8D%95%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2013-01-24T04:35:31.666981Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T07:13:30.096894Z'),
(5097, 'vocabulary', '野郎', '野郎', 'Ugh, that <kanji>field</kanji> <kanji>guy</kanji>. He''s the worst. Always in the fields, making us look bad, quite the <vocabulary>bastard</vocabulary>. Really though, he''s just this guy, but for some reason I sometimes don''t like him.

While 野郎 can be a casual term for a man, it''s usually used to insult or show annoyance towards someone. So it''s best to avoid using it unless you''re really sure it''s okay!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5097', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8E%E9%83%8E', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:35:36.408233Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.194357Z'),
(5098, 'vocabulary', '靴', '靴', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5098', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%B4', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:35:43.843396Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.225427Z'),
(5099, 'vocabulary', '靴下', '靴下', 'What''s <kanji>below</kanji> your <kanji>shoes</kanji>? Your <vocabulary>socks</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5099', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%B4%E4%B8%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:35:50.736897Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(5100, 'vocabulary', '靴屋', '靴屋', 'A <kanji>shoes</kanji> <kanji>shop</kanji> is one that sells shoes. It''s a <vocabulary>shoe store</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>shoe shop</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5100', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%B4%E5%B1%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:35:55.33624Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:18:39.371876Z'),
(5101, 'vocabulary', '長靴', '長靴', '<kanji>Long</kanji> <kanji>shoes</kanji> aren''t long in that the feet part are long (that wouldn''t make sense). Instead, the top part is long to keep you dry when it''s raining. Yes, these are <vocabulary>rain boots</vocabulary>.

In modern Japanese, 長靴 is most commonly used to mean rain boots or <vocabulary>rubber boots</vocabulary>. It can occasionally mean <vocabulary>riding boots</vocabulary> as well. But if you''re referring to fashion boots or boots in general, you can just call them ブーツ.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5101', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%95%B7%E9%9D%B4', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:36:01.058637Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.311112Z'),
(5102, 'vocabulary', '悪影響', '悪影響', '影響 is "influence," so an <kanji>evil</kanji> <kanji>influence</kanji> is a <vocabulary>bad influence</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5102', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E5%BD%B1%E9%9F%BF', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:36:06.293008Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.342219Z'),
(5103, 'vocabulary', '隠居する', '隠居する', '隠居 is "retirement". Make that a verb and you have <vocabulary>to retire</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be retired</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to go into retirement</vocabulary>.

Like 隠居, this word emphasizes life after retiring, and usually implies having a quiet, maybe secluded, lifestyle. It''s a bit of an old-fashioned word, but you''ll still see it in modern writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5103', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%A0%E5%B1%85%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:36:11.806176Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:39:30.849786Z'),
(5104, 'vocabulary', '居酒屋', '居酒屋', 'An <kanji>alive</kanji> <kanji>alcohol</kanji> <kanji>shop</kanji> is a place that serves a lot of alcohol. In Japan, these places are often <vocabulary>izakaya</vocabulary>. If you don''t already know what an izakaya is, you can consider it to be a casual <vocabulary>Japanese Style Bar</vocabulary>.

An izakaya is a pub or tavern where you can enjoy drinks and tasty bar food like yakitori and gyoza, usually at quite affordable prices. Cheers!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5104', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%85%E9%85%92%E5%B1%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T04:36:17.29289Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:18:39.371876Z'),
(5105, 'vocabulary', '矢印', '矢印', '矢印 combines the kanji <kanji>arrow</kanji> and the vocab word 印 (mark), giving us the type of arrow you use for pointing at things, like these: →↑↓←. It could be an arrow on a sign, or on a piece of paper, or a document. We can just call it an <vocabulary>arrow</vocabulary>, or an <vocabulary>arrow symbol</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5105', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%A2%E5%8D%B0', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:44:39.014989Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.425306Z'),
(5106, 'vocabulary', '絶景', '絶景', 'All good things are fleeting. When a <kanji>scene</kanji> in front of you <kanji>dies out</kanji>, it is gone. But, it was beautiful, it was amazing, it was a <vocabulary>spectacular view</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5106', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B6%E6%99%AF', 26, 1, '2013-01-24T09:44:44.677416Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.035024Z'),
(5107, 'vocabulary', '並列', '並列', '<kanji>Line up</kanji> everything in a <kanji>row</kanji>. When you do that, everything will be <vocabulary>side by side</vocabulary>.

並列 is a relatively formal word for things being arranged side by side in alignment. You''ll most commonly see it used in a technical sense, like with <vocabulary>parallel</vocabulary> circuits in electronic stuff. But it''s also used to describe the orderly side-by-side arrangements of other things, such as vehicles parked next to each other in a row or soldiers standing in ranks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5107', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%A6%E5%88%97', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:44:50.856269Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:58:58.842584Z'),
(5108, 'vocabulary', '並行', '並行', 'If several people were to <kanji>line up</kanji> and <kanji>go</kanji> together, they''d all be advancing <vocabulary>in parallel</vocabulary> while <vocabulary>going abreast</vocabulary> of one another.

While 平行 simply refers to things being parallel geometrically, 並行 is used for two or more things placed or moving alongside each other, like buses or trains running side by side. It''s also used to describe two or more activities happening at the same time — things which are <vocabulary>concurrent</vocabulary>, in other words.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5108', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%A6%E8%A1%8C', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:44:56.127731Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T07:36:19.841293Z'),
(5109, 'vocabulary', '並ぶ', '並ぶ', 'The kanji itself means <kanji>line up</kanji>, and this is the intransitive verb version of that, which makes this <vocabulary>to line up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be lined up</vocabulary>. You can remember this because you keep saying <reading>boo</reading> (ぶ), why is everyone already lined up? If you were the one in charge of lining things up, you wouldn''t have <vocabulary>to stand in line</vocabulary> yourself…

Figuratively, this can also mean <vocabulary>to be on par</vocabulary>, as in someone or something matches or is comparable in ability, status, or ranking to another (as if they are aligned side by side in comparison).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5109', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%A6%E3%81%B6', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:45:01.55689Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.536493Z'),
(5110, 'vocabulary', '並', '並', 'The things that <kanji>line up</kanji> are <vocabulary>ordinary</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>average</vocabulary> things. Because the majority of things (or people) fall into the average category, you could say most of them line up side by side with each other.

This might be why 並 is often used in restaurants to refer to standard (or average) portion sizes. And in sushi restaurants, 並 can refer to a standard set, as opposed to a more luxurious or premium one.

Note that when describing something as 並 outside of a restaurant or commercial context, it could have a slightly judgmental tone. Most people wouldn''t love it if you called their work (or them) ordinary and average, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5110', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%A6', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:45:07.283116Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.564776Z'),
(5111, 'vocabulary', '修士', '修士', 'A degree of <kanji>mastering</kanji> equal to a <kanji>samurai</kanji> is required for a <vocabulary>master''s degree</vocabulary>. You''ll have to master your chosen skill to the level of a samurai in order to obtain such a degree… Sounds tough.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5111', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%AE%E5%A3%AB', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:45:18.350752Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.592581Z'),
(5112, 'vocabulary', '修理', '修理', 'When you <kanji>repair</kanji> things for a <kanji>reason</kanji>, you''ll be able to <vocabulary>repair</vocabulary> just about anything! Just remember, always be repairing for a reason.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5112', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%AE%E7%90%86', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:45:27.328056Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.624011Z'),
(5113, 'vocabulary', '研修', '研修', 'You <kanji>sharpen</kanji> your <kanji>mastering</kanji> of a specific skill. One could say that you''re <vocabulary>training</vocabulary>. Additionally, when you sharpen someone else''s mastering, you <vocabulary>give training</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5113', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%94%E4%BF%AE', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:45:35.831802Z', NULL, '2025-09-03T14:41:11.397123Z'),
(5114, 'vocabulary', '修辞学', '修辞学', 'The <kanji>mastering</kanji> <kanji>word</kanji>s field of <kanji>study</kanji> is also known as <vocabulary>rhetoric</vocabulary>.

修辞学 is specifically for the study and theory of rhetoric. Rhetoric as an academic field, in other words. If you''re just talking about the way people persuade each other with words, the more colloquial term is レトリック. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5114', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%AE%E8%BE%9E%E5%AD%A6', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:45:45.53224Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.673114Z'),
(5115, 'vocabulary', '傘', '傘', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5115', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%98', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:45:54.481428Z', NULL, '2025-12-02T00:48:12.061404Z'),
(5116, 'vocabulary', '傘立て', '傘立て', 'This is when you take your <kanji>umbrella</kanji> and you <kanji>stand</kanji> it up. Wait, how is an umbrella standing up? Good question, my dear Crabling, you are putting your umbrella in an <vocabulary>umbrella stand</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5116', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%98%E7%AB%8B%E3%81%A6', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:46:05.459177Z', NULL, '2025-12-02T00:48:12.061404Z'),
(5117, 'vocabulary', '同僚', '同僚', 'We work at the <kanji>same</kanji> place and are <kanji>colleague</kanji>s, making us <vocabulary>colleague</vocabulary>s or <vocabulary>coworker</vocabulary>s.

As you might guess from the 同 kanji, 同僚 generally refers to people working in the same workplace with the same rank or status. In other words, you wouldn''t usually call your boss your 同僚!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5117', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8C%E5%83%9A', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T09:46:14.122086Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.748505Z'),
(5118, 'vocabulary', '官僚', '官僚', 'A <kanji>government</kanji> <kanji>colleague</kanji> is someone who works for the <vocabulary>bureaucracy</vocabulary>, making them a <vocabulary>bureaucrat</vocabulary>.

Just like the word bureaucracy in English, 官僚 often carries a negative connotation, suggesting red tape, inefficiency, or the power and influence that senior civil servants might wield within the government.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5118', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%98%E5%83%9A', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T09:46:21.08017Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:01:08.377054Z'),
(5119, 'vocabulary', '児童', '児童', 'The <kanji>child</kanji> who is a <kanji>juvenile</kanji> is just a <vocabulary>child</vocabulary>. A group of them is <vocabulary>children</vocabulary>.

児童 refers specifically to children who are of elementary school age, typically from around 6 to 12 years old. It''s often used in formal contexts, like in legal documents or when referring to <vocabulary>school children</vocabulary> in educational settings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5119', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%90%E7%AB%A5', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:46:42.693896Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.799853Z'),
(5120, 'vocabulary', '幼児', '幼児', 'An <kanji>infancy</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> is a <vocabulary>toddler</vocabulary>. The kanji are kind of deceptive since this isn''t really used for infants. It''s more for small children, from toddlers on up to <vocabulary>preschooler</vocabulary>s.

幼児 is sort of formal, but it''s commonly used to talk about small children from about one to six years old, or about that specific period of early childhood.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5120', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BC%E5%85%90', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:46:57.893629Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.822664Z'),
(5121, 'vocabulary', '冷たい', '冷たい', 'Something that is <kanji>cool</kanji> is <vocabulary>cold</vocabulary>. This refers to something that is <vocabulary>cold to the touch</vocabulary>, not the weather or something like that (which is 寒い).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5121', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%B7%E3%81%9F%E3%81%84', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:47:08.663681Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.850869Z'),
(5122, 'vocabulary', '冷静', '冷静', 'You''re <kanji>cool</kanji> and <kanji>quiet</kanji>. That''s you, daddio. Nice and <vocabulary>cool-headed</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>calm</vocabulary>. So much <vocabulary>composure</vocabulary>', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5122', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%B7%E9%9D%99', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:47:25.75565Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:08:32.29071Z'),
(5123, 'vocabulary', '冷凍庫', '冷凍庫', 'A <kanji>cool</kanji> <kanji>frozen</kanji> <kanji>storage</kanji> is one which is really cold on the inside, so cold it can freeze things. This is called a <vocabulary>freezer</vocabulary>, not to be confused with 冷蔵庫, which is refrigerator, and doesn''t have the kanji for "freeze" in it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5123', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%B7%E5%87%8D%E5%BA%AB', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:47:33.119025Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.898753Z'),
(5124, 'vocabulary', '凍る', '凍る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>frozen</kanji>, so the vocab version is <vocabulary>to freeze</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5124', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%8D%E3%82%8B', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:47:41.587845Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.938003Z'),
(5125, 'vocabulary', '凍死', '凍死', 'A <kanji>frozen</kanji> <kanji>death</kanji> isn''t a bad way to go, I hear. Still, you don''t want to <vocabulary>freeze to death</vocabulary> if you can help it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5125', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%8D%E6%AD%BB', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:47:47.602157Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.961464Z'),
(5126, 'vocabulary', '凍結', '凍結', 'Things that are <kanji>frozen</kanji> have been <kanji>tie</kanji>d up in <vocabulary>freezing</vocabulary> ice. They <vocabulary>get frozen</vocabulary> that way. 

凍結 is a relatively formal word that is used for the physical freezing of infrastructure, such as roads and water pipes. It can also refer to the freezing of systems or accounts, like the suspension of social media or bank accounts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5126', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%8D%E7%B5%90', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:47:53.832507Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:08.993076Z'),
(5127, 'vocabulary', '処理', '処理', 'I can <kanji>deal with</kanji> it, no matter what the <kanji>reason</kanji>. I swear I can <vocabulary>deal with</vocabulary> this problem on my own!

処理 is a relatively formal word for <vocabulary>handling</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>processing</vocabulary> a problem or task to bring it to a resolution. It covers all kinds of tasks, from administrative work and data processing to practical challenges like waste disposal and complaint handling. It can even apply to defusing unexploded bombs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5127', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%A6%E7%90%86', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:48:04.471752Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:08:13.800046Z'),
(5128, 'vocabulary', '処分', '処分', 'When you <kanji>deal with</kanji> trash, you have to <kanji>divide</kanji> it into different types before you can get to its <vocabulary>disposal</vocabulary>. Whenever you deal with and divide something, you’re preparing to dispose of it.

処分 means getting rid of things you don’t want, like trash or old furniture. Sometimes, it can also mean <vocabulary>punishment</vocabulary>, like suspension from school for breaking the rules. This connection makes sense because both disposal and punishment involve handling something unwanted—whether it''s an item or a behavior.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5128', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%A6%E5%88%86', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:48:11.313845Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:09.045412Z'),
(5129, 'vocabulary', '処置', '処置', 'We''re going to <kanji>deal with</kanji> it by <kanji>put</kanji>ting this up your butt and giving you the <vocabulary>treatment</vocabulary> you need. Kancho!

処置 is commonly used when treating injuries or illnesses. It can also refer to deciding how to handle a situation or the act of handling it, but in these cases, people usually use 処理 instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5129', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%A6%E7%BD%AE', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T09:48:21.390117Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:08:17.52437Z'),
(5130, 'vocabulary', '券', '券', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

券 is a standalone word that means <vocabulary>ticket</vocabulary>, but it''s commonly used as a suffix to form words like 入場券 (admission ticket), 割引券 (discount <vocabulary>coupon</vocabulary>), or 定期券 (commuter <vocabulary>pass</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5130', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%B8', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:48:31.097519Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:09.091815Z'),
(5131, 'vocabulary', '旅券', '旅券', 'Your <kanji>trip</kanji> <kanji>ticket</kanji> is the ticket that lets you go on ALL of your trips. It''s not your airplane ticket, it''s not your boat ticket, it''s the trip ticket. It''s your <vocabulary>passport</vocabulary>, which allows you to take trips.

旅券 is a formal term for a passport. It''s commonly used in official documents and formal settings, like when dealing with government paperwork. In less formal contexts, you can simply say パスポート!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5131', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%85%E5%88%B8', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:48:38.396136Z', NULL, '2025-12-23T03:07:20.392436Z'),
(5132, 'vocabulary', '前売り券', '前売り券', 'What''s a <kanji>before</kanji> <kanji>sell</kanji> <kanji>ticket</kanji>? It''s a ticket you sell before an event. In other words, an <vocabulary>advance ticket</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>ticket sold in advance</vocabulary>!

前売り券 are often tickets with reduced prices and certain benefits or perks. Note that you''ll also see this written as 前売券, without the okurigana, but the reading will stay the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5132', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%8D%E5%A3%B2%E3%82%8A%E5%88%B8', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:48:47.467369Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T21:59:01.931942Z'),
(5133, 'vocabulary', '回数券', '回数券', 'Many <kanji>times</kanji> is how many times you can use this <kanji>number</kanji> of <kanji>ticket</kanji>s. That means there are many tickets, making this a <vocabulary>book of tickets</vocabulary>.

回数券 refers to a book or set of prepaid tickets that are often sold at a discount and can either be physical or digital. They are commonly used for public transportation, like buses or trains, and can also be issued by other businesses that people frequent, such as hot springs and public baths.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5133', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9E%E6%95%B0%E5%88%B8', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:48:53.634488Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.041761Z'),
(5134, 'vocabulary', '刺身', '刺身', 'Remember how the vocabulary word 身 can mean "animal flesh"? Well, when you <kanji>stab</kanji> some animal flesh and eat it raw, you''re having <vocabulary>sashimi</vocabulary>, aka <vocabulary>sliced raw fish</vocabulary> (and sometimes other raw seafood).

Of course, you shouldn''t actually stab your sashimi when you eat it — that would be rude. Just handle it with chopsticks and dip it in soy sauce and wasabi. Yum! And if you want to make a good impression, you can call it お刺身 to sound more polite — especially when ordering.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5134', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%BA%E8%BA%AB', 21, 1, '2013-01-24T09:49:03.655619Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:28:22.201494Z'),
(5135, 'vocabulary', '創造力', '創造力', 'If 創造 is <kanji>creation</kanji> and you add <kanji>power</kanji> to it, you have <vocabulary>creative power</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5135', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%B5%E9%80%A0%E5%8A%9B', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:49:13.651868Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(5136, 'vocabulary', '創造的', '創造的', 'If 創造 is <kanji>creation</kanji>, then you add 的 to it, which means you have a creational thing, which is quite <vocabulary>creative</vocabulary> wouldn''t you say?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5136', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%B5%E9%80%A0%E7%9A%84', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:49:20.492474Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:09.266804Z'),
(5137, 'vocabulary', '博物館', '博物館', 'An <kanji>exhibition</kanji> for <kanji>thing</kanji>s is put in a <kanji>public building</kanji> for anyone to see. This place is a <vocabulary>museum</vocabulary>.

Note that 博物館 isn''t used for art museums, but for museums that focus on a wide range of other subjects, like natural history, science, history, and technology. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5137', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%9A%E7%89%A9%E9%A4%A8', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:49:42.616116Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(5138, 'vocabulary', '博覧会', '博覧会', 'An <kanji>exhibition</kanji> that you <kanji>look at</kanji> and all the people come <kanji>meet</kanji> together. Well, that just sounds like the description of an <vocabulary>exposition</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5138', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%9A%E8%A6%A7%E4%BC%9A', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T09:49:51.598077Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.8736Z'),
(5139, 'vocabulary', '博打', '博打', 'A <kanji>gambling</kanji> <kanji>hit</kanji> can be multiple things. One type of gambling in Japan that was popular in the day involved betting these awesome wooden block things. You''d gamble by hitting them down on the ground, making a nice thunk sound. Another way to think about it is like blackjack. You want a card? You hit! Either way, it''s <vocabulary>gambling</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5139', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%9A%E6%89%93', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:50:28.025482Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:09.354694Z'),
(5140, 'vocabulary', '星占い', '星占い', '占い is "fortune telling." <kanji>Star</kanji> <kanji>fortune</kanji> telling is when you use the stars for fortune telling. That''s known as <vocabulary>astrology</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>horoscope</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5140', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%9F%E5%8D%A0%E3%81%84', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:50:38.470183Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T02:27:52.417356Z'),
(5141, 'vocabulary', '微妙', '微妙', 'Something that''s both <kanji>delicate</kanji> and <kanji>peculiar</kanji> is probably <vocabulary>not so good</vocabulary>. It''s not terrible, but it''s not great either — it could break easily, plus it''s kind of strange… It''s <vocabulary>not really</vocabulary> good, you know? Think of 微妙 as a sort of euphemism for those delicate and peculiar things you''re not really into. 

微妙 can also just mean <vocabulary>delicate</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>subtle</vocabulary>, like 微妙な立場 (a delicate position that requires careful handling) or 微妙なニュアンスの違い (a subtle nuance difference that''s hard to pinpoint).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5141', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%AE%E5%A6%99', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:50:59.753659Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:09.411927Z'),
(5142, 'vocabulary', '妙', '妙', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Note that 妙 is more often used in writing than conversation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5142', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%99', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:51:06.078344Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:09.43675Z'),
(5143, 'vocabulary', '妙薬', '妙薬', 'A <kanji>peculiar</kanji> <kanji>drug</kanji> that seems too good to be true? That''s what we call a <vocabulary>wonder drug</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>miracle cure</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5143', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%99%E8%96%AC', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:51:21.396853Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:09.459864Z'),
(5144, 'vocabulary', '婦人', '婦人', '<kanji>Lady</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is another way of saying <vocabulary>woman</vocabulary> — but kind of politely, you know? A way that''s befitting of a <vocabulary>lady</vocabulary>.

婦人 is a polite but slightly old-fashioned word for an adult woman. You''ll still often see it in formal category names, like 婦人服 (women''s clothing) or 婦人科 (gynecology).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5144', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A9%A6%E4%BA%BA', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:51:29.143757Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:45:03.698895Z'),
(5145, 'vocabulary', '夫婦', '夫婦', 'What do you call a <kanji>husband</kanji> and his <kanji>lady</kanji>? <vocabulary>Husband and wife</vocabulary>! They''re a <vocabulary>married couple</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5145', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%AB%E5%A9%A6', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:51:37.365389Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:39:55.990894Z'),
(5146, 'vocabulary', '主婦', '主婦', 'A <kanji>master</kanji> <kanji>lady</kanji> is a lady who''s in charge of a house. In other words, she''s a <vocabulary>housewife</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>homemaker</vocabulary>! In Japan, this is a pretty common profession for married ladies, although things have been changing little by little. Still, being a housewife means you control the money, which is pretty neat if you ask me.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5146', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB%E5%A9%A6', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T09:51:47.763885Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:53:04.767169Z'),
(5147, 'vocabulary', '幼い', '幼い', 'The kanji means <kanji>infancy</kanji>. The adjective version describes someone who is <vocabulary>very young</vocabulary>, like little kids in their early childhood or babyhood!

Depending on the context, it can also describe behaviors or ideas that are <vocabulary>childish</vocabulary> or immature, often with a negative connotation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5147', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BC%E3%81%84', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:52:03.7396Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:09.586979Z'),
(5148, 'vocabulary', '幼年時代', '幼年時代', 'You have 時代 as a "period" of time you''re talking about. What period? The <kanji>infancy</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji>s. Those years are not just when you''re a baby, but when you were growing up, too. That was your <vocabulary>childhood</vocabulary>.

幼年時代 specifically refers to your <vocabulary>early childhood</vocabulary> years. It''s a formal word you mostly see in writing with a slightly stiff tone, often appearing in literature, autobiographies, or memoirs looking back on someone''s younger years.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5148', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BC%E5%B9%B4%E6%99%82%E4%BB%A3', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:53:02.882749Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:15.806084Z'),
(5149, 'vocabulary', '庫', '庫', 'The kanji means <kanji>storehouse</kanji>, and the vocab is also <vocabulary>storehouse</vocabulary>! Specifically, this usually refers to a traditional Japanese storehouse, which is typically a two story building used to store personal property or merchandise.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5149', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%AB', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:53:09.712019Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:09.632118Z'),
(5150, 'vocabulary', '金庫', '金庫', '<kanji>Gold</kanji> <kanji>storage</kanji> is where you put your gold. You want it to be safe, so you put it in <vocabulary>a safe</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>vault</vocabulary>.

金庫 can refer to various types of secure storage. Depending on the context, it can mean a personal safe, a bank vault, or a safety deposit box.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5150', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%91%E5%BA%AB', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:54:38.905991Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:09.654311Z'),
(5151, 'vocabulary', '車庫', '車庫', '<kanji>Car</kanji> <kanji>storage</kanji> is where you store your car. It''s your <vocabulary>garage</vocabulary>.

車庫 literally means vehicle storage, so it''s used for everything from home garages to large depots for public transportation. It carries a bit more of a formal tone than the katakana word ガレージ (garage).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5151', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8A%E5%BA%AB', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:54:52.594041Z', NULL, '2025-06-25T08:43:23.684078Z'),
(5152, 'vocabulary', '回復する', '回復する', '回復 is <kanji>recovery</kanji>. Make it into a verb and you have <vocabulary>to recover</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5152', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9E%E5%BE%A9%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:55:02.263009Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:09.702887Z'),
(5153, 'vocabulary', '微か', '微か', 'The kanji means <kanji>delicate</kanji>, and so does this word. Something that''s <vocabulary>delicate</vocabulary> is probably also <vocabulary>faint</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>indistinct</vocabulary>. So use 微か to describe things like faint sounds, delicate breezes, and indistinct memories.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5153', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%AE%E3%81%8B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T09:55:22.887788Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.183676Z'),
(5154, 'vocabulary', '微生物', '微生物', 'You''ve learned 微 can mean "delicate," but it can also mean <kanji>tiny</kanji>. Combine that with 生物, which you might remember is "living thing," and what do you get? A tiny living thing…? Well, that''s a <vocabulary>microorganism</vocabulary>. The tiniest kind of living thing there is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5154', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%AE%E7%94%9F%E7%89%A9', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:55:34.326883Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(5155, 'vocabulary', '憲法', '憲法', 'The <kanji>constitution</kanji> <kanji>law</kanji>s are what makes up the <vocabulary>constitution</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5155', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%B2%E6%B3%95', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:55:45.601308Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T08:21:38.112505Z'),
(5156, 'vocabulary', '憲政', '憲政', 'A <kanji>constitution</kanji> <kanji>government</kanji> is a <vocabulary>constitutional government</vocabulary>.

憲政 is a formal word that might come up in discussions about democracy or constitutional frameworks, especially in advanced writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5156', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%B2%E6%94%BF', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:55:58.668476Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:43:08.975746Z'),
(5157, 'vocabulary', '無我夢中', '無我夢中', 'You already know that 無我 is "selflessness" and 夢中 is "crazy about." So this means something like <vocabulary>total absorption</vocabulary>. You''re so into something that you’ve started losing your sense of self. Just like 夢中, this can also describe doing something <vocabulary>frantic</vocabulary>ally, like running away from somebody or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5157', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E6%88%91%E5%A4%A2%E4%B8%AD', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:56:10.268164Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:09.835926Z'),
(5158, 'vocabulary', '身振り', '身振り', 'Remember how the vocab 身 means "body" and 振る means "to shake something"? Well, the way you "shake" your body is your <vocabulary>body language</vocabulary>, or how you <vocabulary>gesture</vocabulary>.

身振り generally refers to body movements and gestures that make communication more expressive or help bridge language barriers. You''ll often encounter this together with 手振り (hand gestures) in the phrase 身振り手振り.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5158', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BA%AB%E6%8C%AF%E3%82%8A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:56:24.281088Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:21:13.75151Z'),
(5159, 'vocabulary', '振り仮名', '振り仮名', '振る is "to shake." 仮名 is kana. This word refers to <vocabulary>furigana</vocabulary>, which is the little kana that''s put next to kanji for the people who don''t use WaniKani and can''t read anything. As to why it''s called "shake kana"? I have no clue. Just imagine putting little kana next to kanji and watching them shake around, though, and you should be good to go.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5159', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%AF%E3%82%8A%E4%BB%AE%E5%90%8D', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:56:29.843628Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(5160, 'vocabulary', '面接する', '面接する', '面接 is an <kanji>interview</kanji>. Make that into a verb and you have to <vocabulary>interview with</vocabulary>.

Remember that 面接 is an interview for a job or admission to a school, not an interview for a magazine or a TV show. インタビューする is the more appropriate word for a media interview.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5160', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%A2%E6%8E%A5%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:56:35.398464Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:46:52.388227Z'),
(5161, 'vocabulary', '撃つ', '撃つ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>to attack</kanji>, but 撃つ means <vocabulary>to shoot</vocabulary>.

This can mean <vocabulary>to fire</vocabulary> a weapon such as a gun or to shoot a projectile, like an arrow. It can also mean to shoot a person or animal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5161', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%92%83%E3%81%A4', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:56:40.748786Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:09.957658Z'),
(5162, 'vocabulary', '攻撃', '攻撃', 'With <kanji>aggression</kanji> you <kanji>attack</kanji> the enemy. This is an <vocabulary>attack</vocabulary>.

攻撃 usually refers to <vocabulary>attacking</vocabulary> someone or something with the intent of causing direct harm or damage, either physically or verbally. You''ll hear it used in the context of war, games, arguments, or even things like media criticism or cyberattacks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5162', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BB%E6%92%83', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:56:45.98707Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T16:50:36.478998Z'),
(5163, 'vocabulary', '攻める', '攻める', 'You don''t say "to aggression." When you do aggression on someone, you''re probably doing something to hurt them in some way. This is an attack, making this word <vocabulary>to attack</vocabulary>.

攻める means to attack or advance on an enemy in battle, but it can also apply to making a strong or proactive move in competitions like sports, business, or games. It can also be used to describe taking bold actions, like moving ahead with a risky plan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5163', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BB%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:56:56.907429Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.023256Z'),
(5164, 'vocabulary', '専攻', '専攻', 'Your <kanji>specialty</kanji>, which you focus on with <kanji>aggression</kanji>, is your <vocabulary>major</vocabulary> in college (your <vocabulary>college major</vocabulary>). Study hard, young grasshopper.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5164', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%82%E6%94%BB', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:57:11.200081Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.04708Z'),
(5165, 'vocabulary', '反攻', '反攻', '<kanji>Anti</kanji> <kanji>aggression</kanji> is when you receive some aggression, then respond to it with anti aggression, aka your <vocabulary>counterattack</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5165', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8D%E6%94%BB', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:57:20.975519Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:30:02.229578Z'),
(5166, 'vocabulary', '診断', '診断', 'To <kanji>diagnose</kanji> is to (metaphorically) <kanji>cut off</kanji> all the possibilities you don''t believe to be likely. That''s how you get a <vocabulary>diagnosis</vocabulary>!

診断 is primarily used in medical contexts to mean the act of <vocabulary>diagnosing</vocabulary> or the diagnosis itself. In non-medical contexts, it can also mean an <vocabulary>assessment</vocabulary> used to evaluate something, like a 性格診断 (personality test).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5166', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%BA%E6%96%AD', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T09:57:38.093946Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.101794Z'),
(5167, 'vocabulary', '浜', '浜', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. It''s worth mentioning that a more common way to say beach is ビーチ, and many people actually say 海 even though this literally means "sea."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5167', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%9C', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:57:50.211921Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.129097Z'),
(5168, 'vocabulary', '横浜', '横浜', 'It''s the <kanji>side</kanji> of the <kanji>beach</kanji>! Well, wait, what is it? This will make better sense when you look at the reading for the word, since it''s a place: <vocabulary>Yokohama</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5168', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A8%AA%E6%B5%9C', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:57:58.19698Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:15.897913Z'),
(5169, 'vocabulary', '清い', '清い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>pure</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>pure</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5169', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%85%E3%81%84', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:58:05.22247Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.176644Z'),
(5170, 'vocabulary', '清潔', '清潔', 'Something so <kanji>pure</kanji> it''s <kanji>pure</kanji> twice. It must be really <vocabulary>clean</vocabulary>! <vocabulary>Cleanliness</vocabulary> is next to pure...liness, after all. I''m sure that''s how the saying goes.

清潔 is a relatively formal word that refers to <vocabulary>sanitary</vocabulary> conditions in personal hygiene, public health, or medical settings. It can refer to both physically clean places and hygienic practices. In literature, 清潔 can also describe a person as morally pure.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5170', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%85%E6%BD%94', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:58:12.089049Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:41:20.397118Z'),
(5171, 'vocabulary', '潔い', '潔い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. The kanji means <kanji>pure</kanji>, but this is an adjective that often describes behavior or character, so it means <vocabulary>honorable</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>dignified</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>gracious</vocabulary>.

How does that work? I guess if you can stay pure despite a tough situation, you must be pretty honorable and dignified. You''re the type who can make a gracious apology (潔い謝り), have a dignified manner (潔い態度), or even die honorably (潔く死ぬ).

潔い is about being strong and doing the right thing without hesitation, especially when it''s hard. For example, when someone admits their mistake honestly or handles a tough situation with courage, you can say they''re 潔い. It''s a way to praise someone for being straightforward and admirable in difficult times.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5171', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%94%E3%81%84', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:58:19.163502Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:41:20.397118Z'),
(5172, 'vocabulary', '不潔', '不潔', '<kanji>Not</kanji> <kanji>clean</kanji> things are <vocabulary>filthy</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>dirty</vocabulary>. Ewughh.

不潔 is a relatively formal word that refers to <vocabulary>unsanitary</vocabulary> conditions in personal hygiene, public health, or medical settings. It can refer to both dirty environments and poor hygiene practices. In literature, 不潔 can also describe something as morally dirty to evoke disgust, though this use is somewhat old-fashioned.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5172', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%BD%94', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:58:26.764972Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:41:20.397118Z'),
(5173, 'vocabulary', '独り言', '独り言', 'Remember how 独り means "alone"? Well, if someone is alone and <kanji>say</kanji>ing something aloud, it constitutes <vocabulary>talking to oneself</vocabulary>.

独り言 means talking or muttering to yourself aloud, whether alone or in public. It can also refer to the words themselves — the things you say as you talk to yourself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5173', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%AC%E3%82%8A%E8%A8%80', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:58:34.462513Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T21:53:11.839256Z'),
(5174, 'vocabulary', '利益', '利益', '<kanji>Profit</kanji> and <kanji>benefit</kanji> literally means <vocabulary>profit</vocabulary>and <vocabulary>benefit</vocabulary>. Just like the English word "profit," it is often used to refer to financial gain, but can also be used for benefits that are non-financial as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5174', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A9%E7%9B%8A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:58:42.233435Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.309745Z'),
(5175, 'vocabulary', '有益', '有益', 'To <kanji>have</kanji> <kanji>benefit</kanji> is to be <vocabulary>beneficial</vocabulary>.

有益 is a more formal word than 役に立つ (useful). It''s often used for things like 有益なアドバイス (beneficial advice) or 有益な情報 (useful information).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5175', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%89%E7%9B%8A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:58:48.519774Z', NULL, '2025-06-12T00:09:47.848082Z'),
(5176, 'vocabulary', '公益', '公益', 'Something for <kanji>public</kanji> <kanji>benefit</kanji> is for <vocabulary>public benefit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>public interest</vocabulary>.

公益 is a formal word used to describe activities or organizations that have a positive impact on society or the general public.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5176', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AC%E7%9B%8A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:58:55.773859Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.371536Z'),
(5177, 'vocabulary', '国益', '国益', 'The <kanji>country</kanji> will <kanji>benefit</kanji> from these actions. It will create <vocabulary>national benefit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>national interests</vocabulary>.

国益 is a word that is often found in formal political, diplomatic, and economic writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5177', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BD%E7%9B%8A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:59:02.62972Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.401791Z'),
(5178, 'vocabulary', '移す', '移す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>shift</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to shift</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5178', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%BB%E3%81%99', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:59:19.821141Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.42947Z'),
(5179, 'vocabulary', '移民', '移民', 'When you''re <kanji>shift</kanji>ing <kanji>peoples</kanji>, you''re causing either <vocabulary>immigration</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>emigration</vocabulary>, which leads to… <vocabulary>immigrant</vocabulary>s, or <vocabulary>emigrant</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5179', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%BB%E6%B0%91', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:59:34.185297Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.458494Z'),
(5180, 'vocabulary', '移住', '移住', 'If you <kanji>shift</kanji> the place you <kanji>dwell</kanji>, that''s called <vocabulary>migration</vocabulary>.

移住 is a relatively formal word for the act of moving to a new place to live, often in a long-term or permanent sense. This word can mean moving to a new town or city, or it can refer to <vocabulary>immigration</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>emigration</vocabulary> to a different country.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5180', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%BB%E4%BD%8F', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T09:59:41.463882Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.481782Z'),
(5181, 'vocabulary', '移住者', '移住者', 'If you <kanji>shift</kanji> the place you <kanji>dwell</kanji>, you’re <kanji>someone</kanji> that we call an <vocabulary>emigrant</vocabulary>, an <vocabulary>immigrant</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>migrant</vocabulary>. Welcome, friend!

You can use this word both for people who migrate between countries and those who migrate within the same country.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5181', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%BB%E4%BD%8F%E8%80%85', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T09:59:50.183918Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(5182, 'vocabulary', '程', '程', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

You''ll see 程 used in all kinds of ways to express the <vocabulary>extent</vocabulary> or degree of something, like 思った程じゃない (not as much as I expected), 言葉で表せない程すごい (so amazing it can''t be expressed in words), or 30分程歩く (to walk for about thirty minutes). Just keep in mind this word is often written in kana.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5182', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%8B', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:00:01.035206Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T05:59:12.613225Z'),
(5183, 'vocabulary', '程度', '程度', 'The <kanji>extent</kanji>''s <kanji>degree</kanji> is the <vocabulary>degree</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>extent</vocabulary> you''re willing to take something.

程度 can also indicate an approximate <vocabulary>level</vocabulary>, like 一時間程度 (for about an hour) or この程度 (to this extent). To get a sense of the different uses, check out the context section.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5183', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%8B%E5%BA%A6', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:00:11.961473Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T00:24:41.936142Z'),
(5184, 'vocabulary', '過程', '過程', '<kanji>Surpass</kanji> the <kanji>extent</kanji>... okay, what does that even mean? You figure out the extent of your abilities, and then you surpass them to get better. You have to do this over and over again in order to keep getting better. It''s a <vocabulary>process</vocabulary> that must be accomplished if you ever hope to get better than you are now.

過程 is often used to describe ongoing progress, like 生産過程 (production process), but it can also be used to talk about how things develop. For example, you might reflect on the 過程 that led to your current job, or explain the steps a team follows to finish a project.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5184', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8E%E7%A8%8B', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:00:22.534117Z', NULL, '2025-06-12T00:11:15.678309Z'),
(5185, 'vocabulary', '日程', '日程', 'The <kanji>day</kanji>''s <kanji>extent</kanji> is that we''re going to do this, this, and this. That is our <vocabulary>schedule</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>itinerary</vocabulary>, so stick to it.

日程 is a relatively formal word used to talk about the schedule for a specific event, meeting, or activity.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5185', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E7%A8%8B', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:00:36.601088Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.618045Z'),
(5186, 'vocabulary', '幼稚', '幼稚', 'He who is both in his <kanji>infancy</kanji> and <kanji>immature</kanji> is probably <vocabulary>childish</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>immature</vocabulary>.

When used as an adjective, 幼稚 describes negative immaturity in someone''s behavior, remarks, or ideas. However, in compounds like 幼稚園 (kindergarten), it simply refers to the early childhood stage without any negative tone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5186', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BC%E7%A8%9A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:00:46.809463Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.650319Z'),
(5187, 'vocabulary', '精神', '精神', 'Your <kanji>spirit</kanji> <kanji>spirit</kanji> is… your <vocabulary>spirit</vocabulary>. This word is for the human <vocabulary>mind</vocabulary> and can refer to your mental state, thinking, attitude, mindset, or even willpower. 

Depending on the context, 精神 can also mean "spirit" as in the essence or core principle of something, like 独立の精神 (the spirit of independence).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5187', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%BE%E7%A5%9E', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:00:58.712042Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.672263Z'),
(5188, 'vocabulary', '精度', '精度', 'Your <kanji>spirit</kanji>''s <kanji>degree</kanji> of focus is what helps you to hone your <vocabulary>precision</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>accuracy</vocabulary>, for some reason. Just imagine yourself throwing a ball. You miss the target. Hmm, okay... time to power up like on Dragonball. Up that spirit energy. Turn the degrees up on it. Boom. Awesome. Now throw again. Hit! You hit it! What great precision you have gained by honing your spirit!

精度 is a relatively formal word, often used to talk about the precision or accuracy of something in technical or analytical contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5188', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%BE%E5%BA%A6', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:01:06.233073Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.697309Z'),
(5189, 'vocabulary', '絶対', '絶対', '<kanji>Extinction</kanji> will happen as you go <kanji>versus</kanji> extinction. It will happen <vocabulary>absolutely</vocabulary>. It will happen <vocabulary>without fail</vocabulary>. There''s nothing you can do to stop extinction.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5189', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B6%E5%AF%BE', 25, 1, '2013-01-24T10:01:12.049504Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:12:11.505385Z'),
(5190, 'vocabulary', '絶つ', '絶つ', 'The kanji means <kanji>extinction</kanji> or <kanji>die out</kanji>, but the verb version here means <vocabulary>to break off contact</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to sever</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to end</vocabulary>. Just think about how when you’re breaking off contact with somebody, you’re essentially making them go extinct or die out from your life completely. It’s such a complete severance, they basically stop existing to you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5190', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B6%E3%81%A4', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:01:22.181529Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.790992Z'),
(5191, 'vocabulary', '絶望', '絶望', 'When <kanji>hope</kanji> has <kanji>died out</kanji>, you have no more hope, only <vocabulary>despair</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5191', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B6%E6%9C%9B', 25, 1, '2013-01-24T10:01:34.981811Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.869838Z'),
(5192, 'vocabulary', 'お菓子屋', 'お菓子屋', 'お菓子 is "candy," right? Add <kanji>store</kanji> onto that and you have a <vocabulary>candy store</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5192', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E8%8F%93%E5%AD%90%E5%B1%8B', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:01:41.444953Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:18:39.371876Z'),
(5193, 'vocabulary', '大衆', '大衆', 'A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>populous</kanji> place sounds like a place with a lot of people but it''s more like a big part of the populace. It''s most of the populace, sure, making it the <vocabulary>general public</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5193', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E8%A1%86', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:01:46.595774Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(5194, 'vocabulary', '衆議院', '衆議院', 'It''s the <kanji>institution</kanji> that does <kanji>deliberation</kanji> on behalf of the <kanji>populace</kanji>. They represent the people. They are the <vocabulary>house of representatives</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5194', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%86%E8%AD%B0%E9%99%A2', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:01:53.768313Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:10.956213Z'),
(5195, 'vocabulary', '貯金箱', '貯金箱', 'Remember how 貯金 means "savings"? Well, a <kanji>box</kanji> you put said savings in is a <vocabulary>savings box</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>piggy bank</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5195', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%AF%E9%87%91%E7%AE%B1', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:02:03.20699Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T20:09:43.788981Z'),
(5196, 'vocabulary', '比較的', '比較的', '比較 is a comparison. Add 的 to it and you have <vocabulary> comparatively</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5196', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%94%E8%BC%83%E7%9A%84', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:02:10.608308Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:11.009259Z'),
(5197, 'vocabulary', '比較する', '比較する', '比較 is <vocabulary>comparison</vocabulary>. So, verb version of that? It''s <vocabulary>to compare</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5197', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%94%E8%BC%83%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2013-01-24T10:02:18.420655Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:11.034296Z'),
(5198, 'vocabulary', '逆', '逆', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Keep in mind that 逆 often implies something is the <vocabulary>reverse</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>opposite</vocabulary> of what it should be. For example, you use 逆 when you''re going in the opposite direction of your destination or wearing your shirt backwards.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5198', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%86', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:02:26.644632Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:30:39.384701Z'),
(5199, 'vocabulary', '逆効果', '逆効果', '効果 means "effect," so the <kanji>opposite</kanji> of said effect is the <vocabulary>opposite effect</vocabulary>. Or in other terms, something <vocabulary>counterproductive</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5199', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%86%E5%8A%B9%E6%9E%9C', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:02:34.14137Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:30:39.384701Z'),
(5200, 'vocabulary', '逆らう', '逆らう', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an <ja>う</ja> sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>opposite</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to go against</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to oppose</vocabulary>.

逆らう refers to resisting natural or social forces, like 逆らって進む (to go against the tide), or to opposing specific people, like 親に逆らう (to defy one''s parents). It means actively resisting something, whether it''s a flow, a trend, or authority, highlighting a conscious choice to take a different path.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5200', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%86%E3%82%89%E3%81%86', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:02:43.219022Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:30:39.384701Z'),
(5201, 'vocabulary', '逆説', '逆説', 'It is <kanji>reverse</kanji> to the <kanji>theory</kanji>! What is going on? Things aren''t right. There must be some kind of <vocabulary>paradox</vocabulary> going on, where all theories are reversed! D:', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5201', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%86%E8%AA%AC', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:02:55.807581Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:30:39.384701Z'),
(5202, 'vocabulary', '大間違い', '大間違い', 'A 間違い is a mistake. A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>mistake</kanji> is a <vocabulary>big mistake</vocabulary>.

大間違い is less about factual errors and more about someone being way off in their assumptions or expectations. It suggests things didn''t go according to plan — like you assumed a smile would fix everything or expected a date to go well, only for things to turn out completely wrong.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5202', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E9%96%93%E9%81%95%E3%81%84', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:03:03.504109Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(5203, 'vocabulary', '郵便局', '郵便局', 'With 郵便 being <kanji>mail</kanji> and 局 being bureau, we have the "mail bureau." They''re more often known as the <vocabulary>post office</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5203', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%B5%E4%BE%BF%E5%B1%80', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:03:14.53829Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:11:22.551299Z'),
(5204, 'vocabulary', '郵便番号', '郵便番号', 'You know that a 郵便 is the <kanji>mail</kanji> and that a 番号 is a <kanji>number</kanji>. So a "mail number," what is that? Usually refers to your <vocabulary>post number</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>postal code</vocabulary>. It is also often referred to with a symbol, which is 〒, so look out for that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5204', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%B5%E4%BE%BF%E7%95%AA%E5%8F%B7', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:03:31.077626Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:11:22.551299Z'),
(5205, 'vocabulary', '郵便箱', '郵便箱', '郵便 is the "mail." A <kanji>box</kanji> that''s used with said mail is a <vocabulary>postbox</vocabulary>.

郵便箱 used to refer specifically to a public <vocabulary>collection box</vocabulary> where you drop off your mail to be sent. But now, some people use it for their home <vocabulary>mailboxes</vocabulary> too! Don''t worry — you can usually tell which is which from context.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5205', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%B5%E4%BE%BF%E7%AE%B1', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:04:19.055221Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:11:22.551299Z'),
(5206, 'vocabulary', '録音', '録音', 'A <kanji>record</kanji> of <kanji>sound</kanji> is a <vocabulary>sound recording</vocabulary> of some kind. Might be minidisk, might be CD, might be digital. Whatever it is, it''s still the record of sound.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5206', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8C%B2%E9%9F%B3', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:04:29.240115Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:11.303631Z'),
(5207, 'vocabulary', '登録', '登録', '<kanji>Climb</kanji> up and <kanji>record</kanji> your information here. This is <vocabulary>registration</vocabulary>. It''s work to get yourself registered (from the climbing) and even if you get your name recorded on there, you may not get in. So record your information, wait, and hope you get in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5207', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BB%E9%8C%B2', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:04:39.93349Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:11.335007Z'),
(5208, 'vocabulary', '記録', '記録', '<kanji>Write down</kanji> a <kanji>record</kanji> of the events so we have a <vocabulary>record</vocabulary>, please. Don''t want people to forget about this momentous occasion, when King Felix got his perfect game.

記録 refers to the act of putting something on record or to the record itself. Just like English "record," it can also refer to the best performance or most remarkable event of its kind that''s been officially measured and noted, like the record for fastest baseball pitch.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5208', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%98%E9%8C%B2', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:04:52.92813Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:43:32.349089Z'),
(5209, 'vocabulary', '軍隊', '軍隊', 'The <kanji>army</kanji> <kanji>squad</kanji> is <vocabulary>an army</vocabulary>. It''s also just the <vocabulary>army</vocabulary>. It refers more to "an" army rather than "the" army. Just a group of people who do army things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5209', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8D%E9%9A%8A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:05:02.195089Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:50:20.608547Z'),
(5210, 'vocabulary', '隊', '隊', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

隊 is commonly used as a suffix to refer to an organized group or unit, often one that works together toward a specific goal or purpose, like a レスキュー隊 (rescue <vocabulary>squad</vocabulary>), 軍の部隊 (military <vocabulary>unit</vocabulary>), or 登山隊 (mountaineering <vocabulary>team</vocabulary>)!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5210', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%8A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:05:16.128416Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:50:20.608547Z'),
(5211, 'vocabulary', '兵隊', '兵隊', 'A <kanji>soldier</kanji> in a <kanji>squad</kanji> is a real <vocabulary>soldier</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>sailor</vocabulary>, unlike those "soldier" who are not in a squad. They don''t have jobs, and can''t be called real soldiers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5211', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B5%E9%9A%8A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:05:26.490225Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:50:20.608547Z'),
(5212, 'vocabulary', '入隊', '入隊', '<kanji>Enter</kanji>ing into a <kanji>squad</kanji> is called <vocabulary>enlistment</vocabulary>!

入隊 can refer to <vocabulary>enlist</vocabulary>ing in any group that ends with 隊 (squad), like the 軍隊 (military) or an 音楽隊 (marching band). These are indeed the most common uses.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5212', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E9%9A%8A', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T10:05:37.527057Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:50:20.608547Z'),
(5213, 'vocabulary', '乾く', '乾く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>dry</kanji>, so this verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to get dry</vocabulary>. You''re not doing the drying here — it''s happening on its own. To remember this, just think about how <reading>coo</reading>l (く) it is that something can become dry all by itself. It''s just, like, the magic of nature — how cool is that?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5213', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%BE%E3%81%8F', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:27:38.523433Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:16.07964Z'),
(5214, 'vocabulary', '乾季', '乾季', 'The <kanji>dry</kanji> <kanji>seasons</kanji> is the <vocabulary>dry season</vocabulary>.

乾季 is a formal term typically used in geographical climate discussions, especially for regions with clear wet and dry seasons like Southeast Asia, Africa, and South America.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5214', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%BE%E5%AD%A3', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:27:44.629332Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:46:14.953387Z'),
(5215, 'vocabulary', '乾杯', '乾杯', 'You <kanji>dry</kanji> out your <kanji>cup of liquid</kanji> after saying <vocabulary>Kanpai</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>cheers</vocabulary>!

乾杯 means <vocabulary>toast</vocabulary>ing with your drink and is how you say "Cheers!" in Japanese. It literally means "dry your cup" (drink up), so traditionally, people would finish their drink after saying it. But it''s much more relaxed nowadays—most people just take a sip for 乾杯.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5215', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%BE%E6%9D%AF', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:27:53.631616Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:11.572469Z'),
(5216, 'vocabulary', '促す', '促す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>urge</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to urge</vocabulary>.

促す means <vocabulary>to encourage</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to prompt</vocabulary> someone to take action or proceed with something. Depending on the context, it can also mean <vocabulary>to promote</vocabulary> the growth, development, or change of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5216', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%83%E3%81%99', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:28:03.625154Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T03:07:13.841805Z'),
(5217, 'vocabulary', '催促', '催促', 'I''m so for this I''m willing to <kanji>sponsor</kanji> you. I am going to <kanji>urge</kanji> you to do this until you do it, so much so I''ll give you money. This is one great <vocabulary>urge</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>press for</vocabulary> you to do this!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5217', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%AC%E4%BF%83', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:28:09.929836Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:11.632356Z'),
(5218, 'vocabulary', '催告', '催告', 'When you <kanji>sponsor</kanji> an <kanji>announce</kanji>ment, you''re allowing those you''re sponsoring to create some kind of <vocabulary>notice</vocabulary> for all those they want to get in contact with.

催告 refers to the act of <vocabulary>officially notifying</vocabulary> someone with a <vocabulary>formal notice</vocabulary>, urging them to take action within a specified time frame. It''s a technical term mainly used in legal or business contexts, like reminding someone to fulfill obligations such as payments or contracts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5218', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%AC%E5%91%8A', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:28:16.494197Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:34:31.16393Z'),
(5219, 'vocabulary', '主催', '主催', 'The <kanji>master</kanji> <kanji>sponsor</kanji> for an event is the host who''s <vocabulary>hosting</vocabulary> (and possibly sponsoring) it. 

主催 is a formal term often used in business, official events, or public gatherings. While it can imply sponsorship, it mainly refers to the act of leading and organizing an event or activity, as well as the person or group hosting it. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5219', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB%E5%82%AC', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:28:26.12685Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:11.693581Z'),
(5220, 'vocabulary', '壊す', '壊す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>break</kanji> and this verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to break something</vocabulary>. 

You''re the one actively doing the breaking here, so there''s always a direct object involved. You know this because someone is trying to <reading>sue</reading> (す) you for the things you''ve broken.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5220', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%8A%E3%81%99', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:29:19.301598Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:31:18.671032Z'),
(5221, 'vocabulary', '全壊', '全壊', 'When <kanji>all</kanji> of something <kanji>breaks</kanji>, every last bit of it is broken. It''s <vocabulary>complete destruction</vocabulary>!!!

全壊 is a formal term used when buildings or structures are completely destroyed by disasters like earthquakes, fires, or accidents. It''s often seen in news reports or insurance assessments to describe the <vocabulary>total destruction</vocabulary> of property.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5221', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E5%A3%8A', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:29:24.605835Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:20:36.651776Z'),
(5222, 'vocabulary', '倒壊', '倒壊', 'When something <kanji>collapse</kanji>s and <kanji>break</kanji>s, it is when a <vocabulary>collapse</vocabulary> happens. Something big collapsing or <vocabulary>falling down</vocabulary> will probably result in its <vocabulary>destruction</vocabulary>.

倒壊 is a formal word often found in news or official documents. It''s used to describe buildings or structures collapsing, usually from destructive forces like natural disasters or bombings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5222', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%92%E5%A3%8A', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:29:35.05864Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:20:36.651776Z'),
(5223, 'vocabulary', '娘', '娘', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

娘 primarily means <vocabulary>daughter</vocabulary> but can also refer to a <vocabulary>young woman</vocabulary> (often unmarried). This is because its original form is 産す女 (むすめ), or "a woman who gives birth." Over time, it came to represent both daughters and young women.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5223', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A8%98', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:29:39.980873Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.398881Z'),
(5224, 'vocabulary', '一人娘', '一人娘', 'An "alone" (一人) <kanji>daughter</kanji> is a daughter with no other daughter siblings, making her the <vocabulary>only daughter</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5224', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E4%BA%BA%E5%A8%98', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:52:26.747097Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(5225, 'vocabulary', '宗教', '宗教', 'The <kanji>religion</kanji> <kanji>teach</kanji>ings are what makes up a <vocabulary>religion</vocabulary>. If you don''t have the teachings of the religion, nobody will learn about it, and it will stop being a religion.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5225', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%97%E6%95%99', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:52:33.128039Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.418634Z'),
(5226, 'vocabulary', '宗派', '宗派', 'A <kanji>religion</kanji> <kanji>sect</kanji> is a sect within religion, most likely, but it can be simply referred to as a <vocabulary>sect</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>denomination</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5226', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%97%E6%B4%BE', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:52:38.266066Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:15:23.483599Z'),
(5227, 'vocabulary', '禅宗', '禅宗', 'The <kanji>zen</kanji> <kanji>religion</kanji> is a part of Buddhism. It is the <vocabulary>Zen sect</vocabulary> of Buddhism, or <vocabulary>Zen Buddhism</vocabulary>.

Practitioners of 禅宗 follow 禅 (Zen) practices like meditation and mindfulness to achieve enlightenment. That''s why it''s called 禅宗!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5227', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A6%85%E5%AE%97', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:52:44.006778Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.450385Z'),
(5228, 'vocabulary', '宴会', '宴会', 'A <kanji>banquet</kanji> <kanji>meet</kanji>ing is when you meet together for a <vocabulary>banquet</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>dinner party</vocabulary>.

宴会 refers to a gathering where people share food and drinks to celebrate something or deepen their social bonds. It''s generally not used for casual dinner parties among friends, but rather for formal or semi-formal social events involving a larger group of people, such as farewell parties for colleagues or workplace holiday parties.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5228', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B4%E4%BC%9A', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:52:50.162459Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.462156Z'),
(5229, 'vocabulary', '宴', '宴', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

宴 is an old-fashioned word for 宴会. It refers to large, festive feasts in traditional settings, often involving lots of sake and the enjoyment of traditional performing arts. You may see it used in literature or in song lyrics to evoke scenes of grand, lavish banquets from ages past.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5229', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B4', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:52:56.148356Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.473248Z'),
(5230, 'vocabulary', '不健康', '不健康', 'If 健康 is "health," then something that is <kanji>not</kanji> this is <vocabulary>unhealthy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5230', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%81%A5%E5%BA%B7', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:53:01.501244Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.485389Z'),
(5231, 'vocabulary', '緊張', '緊張', 'Your <kanji>tense</kanji> body begins to <kanji>stretch</kanji> tighter and tighter as <vocabulary>nervousness</vocabulary> takes over.

緊張 describes that nervousness you feel before a big event or during a stressful moment. It can also refer to other things that feel tight or strained, like muscle <vocabulary>tension</vocabulary>, or even global tensions and strained relations between countries.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5231', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%8A%E5%BC%B5', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:53:06.703512Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.498566Z'),
(5232, 'vocabulary', '怒り', '怒り', 'This word looks a lot like 怒る, doesn''t it? But this word isn''t a verb, so you can think of it as referring to <vocabulary>anger</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>wrath</vocabulary> itself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5232', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%92%E3%82%8A', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:53:11.950385Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.510971Z'),
(5233, 'vocabulary', '怖い', '怖い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>scary</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>scary</vocabulary>.

怖い is commonly used in everyday language to describe something that''s scary on a more immediate or personal level.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5233', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%96%E3%81%84', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:53:17.522959Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.537183Z'),
(5234, 'vocabulary', '恐怖', '恐怖', 'I <kanji>fear</kanji> it. It is <kanji>scary</kanji>. It is pure <vocabulary>terror</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5234', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%90%E6%80%96', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:53:27.608083Z', NULL, '2025-10-12T18:54:49.597739Z'),
(5235, 'vocabulary', '恐怖症', '恐怖症', 'You learned 恐怖 means "terror," and 〜症 can mean "disease." So what''s a terror disease? Well, it''s a <vocabulary>phobia</vocabulary>.

In Japanese, 恐怖症 is mostly used as a suffix for specific phobia names, like 高所恐怖症 (acrophobia, or fear of heights). Do you have any 恐怖症? ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5235', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%90%E6%80%96%E7%97%87', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:53:33.179694Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.574175Z'),
(5236, 'vocabulary', '恐ろしい', '恐ろしい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>fear</kanji>? When you really feel fear, you think something is <vocabulary>terrifying</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>frightening</vocabulary> or even <vocabulary>dreadful</vocabulary>.

恐ろしい can describe something that inspires deep fear or awe, often beyond normal feelings of fear. Since it can be used to create a dramatic or intense atmosphere, you might find 恐ろしい more often in literature.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5236', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%90%E3%82%8D%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:53:43.296552Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.584636Z'),
(5237, 'vocabulary', '一杯', '一杯', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>cup of liquid</kanji> is equal to <vocabulary>one cup</vocabulary>. It could just be <vocabulary>a drink</vocabulary>, but if you''re measuring exactly one cup, it needs to be <vocabulary>full</vocabulary> to the brim. That''s <vocabulary>a lot</vocabulary> of liquid—so be careful not to spill!

一杯 mainly refers to the amount that fills a container, like a cup, glass, bowl, or even spoon. It can also describe something being "full," such as a full stomach or a theater at capacity (like how a cup is filled to the brim!). Depending on the context, it can also mean "a lot," as fullness implies abundance. When it uses that meaning, you''ll also often see it written in just kana (いっぱい).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5237', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%9D%AF', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:53:52.721751Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.614725Z'),
(5238, 'vocabulary', '二杯', '二杯', '<kanji>Two</kanji> <kanji>cups of liquid</kanji> is equals to <vocabulary>two cups</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>two drinks</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>two glasses</vocabulary>, depending on what your drink is in!

〜杯 is a counter used to count the amount that fills a container, such as a cup, glass, bowl, or even a spoon! The contents don''t have to be liquid — you can use 二杯 for two bowls of rice or two spoons of sugar as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5238', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E6%9D%AF', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:53:57.524163Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T08:22:08.335186Z'),
(5239, 'vocabulary', '三杯', '三杯', '<kanji>Three</kanji> <kanji>cups of liquid</kanji> is equals to <vocabulary>three cups</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>three drinks</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>three glasses</vocabulary> depending on what your drink is in!

〜杯 is a counter used to count the amount that fills a container, such as a cup, glass, bowl, or even a spoon! The contents don''t have to be liquid—you can use 三杯 for three bowls of rice or three spoons of sugar as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5239', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E6%9D%AF', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:54:02.265667Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.654144Z'),
(5240, 'vocabulary', '板', '板', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

板 generally refers to something thin, flat, and solid, like a board, plank, slab, or plate. In some contexts, it can also refer to stock market boards that display trading prices, or bulletin boards used for posting messages in online forums.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5240', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%BF', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:54:19.679846Z', NULL, '2026-02-03T01:43:38.646573Z'),
(5241, 'vocabulary', '看板', '看板', 'A <kanji>watch over</kanji> <kanji>board</kanji> is a board that''s put up and watches over everything. What kind of board watches over everything from up high? One that gets the most visibility, so probably a <vocabulary>signboard</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>billboard</vocabulary>.

看板 typically refers to physical signs, such as <vocabulary>store sign</vocabulary>s or billboards. But it can also mean the figurative symbol or face of a business, like 看板メニュー (signature dish) or 看板娘 (popular female employee).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5241', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%8B%E6%9D%BF', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:54:25.214174Z', NULL, '2026-02-03T01:43:38.646573Z'),
(5242, 'vocabulary', '見極める', '見極める', '極める is "to carry to extremes." If you take the action of <kanji>see</kanji>ing something to the extremes, you''re going <vocabulary>to get to the bottom of</vocabulary> whatever it is. You''ll be able <vocabulary>to identify something</vocabulary>, like the truth.

見極める primarily involves the careful examination and discernment of the underlying aspects or truth of a matter. It emphasizes the thoroughness and effort required to make well-informed decisions or judgments, particularly when the situation is complex or difficult to understand.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5242', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E6%A5%B5%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:54:30.772808Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.688773Z'),
(5243, 'vocabulary', '極めて', '極めて', '極める is "to carry to extremes." So, 極めて is something similar. It''s used to refer to something that''s <vocabulary>extremely</vocabulary> _______.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5243', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%B5%E3%82%81%E3%81%A6', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:54:36.907472Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.698569Z'),
(5244, 'vocabulary', '欧米', '欧米', 'You have <kanji>Europe</kanji> and <kanji>America</kanji>. This word refers to <vocabulary>Europe and America</vocabulary>. 

When people use 欧米 in Japan, they usually mean Western countries in general (<vocabulary>the West</vocabulary>), including North America and Western Europe. You''ll often see it in news reports related to Western countries or in topics comparing Japanese and Western cultures, customs, or trends. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5244', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%A7%E7%B1%B3', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:54:42.145257Z', NULL, '2026-01-19T08:15:51.228829Z'),
(5245, 'vocabulary', '中欧', '中欧', 'The <kanji>middle</kanji> part of <kanji>Europe</kanji> is <vocabulary>Central Europe</vocabulary>, wherever that is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5245', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E6%AC%A7', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:54:52.855158Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.72Z'),
(5246, 'vocabulary', '日欧', '日欧', 'The 日 refers to "Japan" (It''s the 日 of 日本). So, you have Japan and <kanji>Europe</kanji>, which makes this <vocabulary>Japan and Europe</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>Japanese-European</vocabulary>.

日欧 is a fairly formal word commonly found in newspapers or official reports, where it’s often used for brevity when referring to Japan and Europe together, like in 日欧関係 (Japanese-European relations).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5246', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E6%AC%A7', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:55:13.214286Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.730326Z'),
(5247, 'vocabulary', '江戸', '江戸', 'The <kanji>bay</kanji> <kanji>door</kanji> isn''t what you might think, it refers to <vocabulary>Edo</vocabulary>, which is what Tokyo was called back in the day. You''ll still see references to it everywhere, though, so it''s good to learn. You can also call this <vocabulary>Tokyo</vocabulary> if you want, but I''d prefer if you answered "Edo," instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5247', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%9F%E6%88%B8', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:55:21.884769Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:13:55.260135Z'),
(5248, 'vocabulary', '入り江', '入り江', 'When you <kanji>enter</kanji> an <kanji>inlet</kanji>, you''ve gone into the <vocabulary>inlet</vocabulary>. It''s also where water goes inside of the inlet (it''s entering the inlet from the ocean, or whatever), so that''s another way to think about it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5248', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E3%82%8A%E6%B1%9F', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:55:26.813881Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.750747Z'),
(5249, 'vocabulary', '添える', '添える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. When <kanji>append</kanji>ing something, you basically want <vocabulary>to add something to</vocabulary> something, or <vocabulary>to include something</vocabulary> with it. 

添える means to add something alongside the main thing, often as a thoughtful touch to complement or support it. For example, you might say 添える when including a letter with a gift to add a personal touch, or when garnishing a dish with parsley to enhance its appearance. It''s also used when you feel inclined to add a second hand to hold a bowl carefully.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5249', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%BB%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:55:34.174277Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.762305Z'),
(5250, 'vocabulary', '添付', '添付', '<kanji>Append</kanji> and <kanji>attach</kanji> this document to your email. That’s called <vocabulary>attachment</vocabulary>.

添付 refers to the <em>act</em> of <vocabulary>attaching</vocabulary> something supplementary to another document, like attaching a file to an email or attaching a map to an invitation. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5250', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%BB%E4%BB%98', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:55:39.969961Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:03:42.596356Z'),
(5251, 'vocabulary', '強烈', '強烈', 'Things that are <kanji>strong</kanji> and <kanji>intense</kanji> are <vocabulary>very strong</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>intense</vocabulary>.

強烈 describes something so strong or intense that it leaves a lasting impression and is often <vocabulary>shocking</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>striking</vocabulary>. It can refer to vividly bright colors, a blinding flash of light, extreme heat, or unusually bold or impactful comments.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5251', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%B7%E7%83%88', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:55:44.888206Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:05:21.088648Z'),
(5252, 'vocabulary', '猛烈', '猛烈', 'If you''re <kanji>fierce</kanji> and <kanji>violent</kanji> then you are <vocabulary>fierce</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>violent</vocabulary>.

猛烈 is used to describe <vocabulary>intense</vocabulary> things, like fierce storms, or actions done with great intensity, like working like mad. Its strong tone can feel a bit comical in casual use, so people also use it playfully to exaggerate things like how hungry they feel.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5252', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8C%9B%E7%83%88', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:55:49.71032Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.796049Z'),
(5253, 'vocabulary', '熱烈', '熱烈', 'Your <kanji>fever</kanji> turns <kanji>violent</kanji> when you express your <vocabulary>fervent</vocabulary> feelings.

熱烈 is a relatively formal word that describes someone being fervently absorbed in something or someone and showing <vocabulary>ardent</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>passionate</vocabulary> behavior, like 熱烈に応援する (support fervently). ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5253', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%86%B1%E7%83%88', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:55:54.409107Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.804937Z'),
(5254, 'vocabulary', '猛〜', '猛〜', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. You can put this on other words to say that it''s a "<vocabulary>Fierce</vocabulary> ____" or "<vocabulary>Intense</vocabulary> _____," or something along those lines. If you see this, you know that something is being done really fiercely or intensely.

猛 is used in compounds to emphasize fierceness or intensity, like 猛暑 (intense heat) or 猛勉強 (intense studying). It can also be used in expressions like 猛アプローチ (relentlessly pursuing someone romantically — a fierce "approach"). In casual uses like this, the strong tone is often intentionally goofy or comical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5254', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8C%9B%E3%80%9C', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:55:59.52854Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:09:48.614238Z'),
(5255, 'vocabulary', '略す', '略す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>abbreviation</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to abbreviate</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to shorten</vocabulary>. In some cases, it can also mean <vocabulary>to omit</vocabulary> something for the sake of simplicity.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5255', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%A5%E3%81%99', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:56:16.313968Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.830752Z'),
(5256, 'vocabulary', '省略', '省略', '<kanji>Conserve</kanji> those letters with <kanji>abbreviation</kanji> and now you''re <vocabulary>omitting</vocabulary> all the extra fluff! 

省略 is a relatively formal word used when you are intentionally <vocabulary>leaving out</vocabulary> something for the sake of brevity or simplicity, like an unneeded explanation or a long-winded greeting. It can also sometimes refer to the <vocabulary>abbreviation</vocabulary> of a word or phrase.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5256', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%81%E7%95%A5', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:56:22.529611Z', NULL, '2025-11-02T22:39:57.694852Z'),
(5257, 'vocabulary', '略語', '略語', '<kanji>Abbreviation</kanji> in <kanji>language</kanji> is an <vocabulary>abbreviation</vocabulary> or an <vocabulary>acronym</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5257', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%A5%E8%AA%9E', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:56:30.256986Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.859141Z'),
(5258, 'vocabulary', '略図', '略図', 'An <kanji>abbreviation</kanji> version of a <kanji>diagram</kanji> doesn''t include all the details. It''s more like a <vocabulary>rough diagram</vocabulary> that shows only the essentials.

While 略図 can be used for any simplified diagram, it most commonly refers to a <vocabulary>rough map</vocabulary> that gives a basic overview of an area.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5258', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%A5%E5%9B%B3', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:56:35.271695Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T15:10:41.904955Z'),
(5259, 'vocabulary', '大略', '大略', 'The <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>abbreviation</kanji> is where you take everything and abbreviate it down to show the big picture. This abbreviation is the <vocabulary>broad outline</vocabulary> of something bigger.

大略 is a relatively formal word commonly found in writing. It generally refers to a broader, rougher <vocabulary>outline</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>overview</vocabulary> rather than a detailed summary.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5259', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E7%95%A5', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:56:42.031712Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(5260, 'vocabulary', '痛み', '痛み', '痛い is "painful." This is the noun version, it is <vocabulary>a pain</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>pain</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5260', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%9B%E3%81%BF', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:56:46.764505Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:48:39.716764Z'),
(5261, 'vocabulary', '痛み止め', '痛み止め', '痛み is "pain." 止める is "to stop." What stops pain if you use it? How about a <vocabulary>painkiller</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5261', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%9B%E3%81%BF%E6%AD%A2%E3%82%81', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:56:51.478466Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:48:39.716764Z'),
(5262, 'vocabulary', '痛む', '痛む', 'Remember how <ja>痛い</ja> is "painful"? This is the verb version of that, so it means <vocabulary>to ache</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to hurt</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to be in pain</vocabulary>.

痛む is an intransitive verb that suggests an ongoing sensation of pain or aching. It''s a little more descriptive than 痛い, so you might see it used in writing or to describe a more persistent or recurring condition. It''s also often used figuratively, especially in expressions like 心が痛む (my heart aches).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5262', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%9B%E3%82%80', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T20:56:56.202724Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:48:39.716764Z'),
(5263, 'vocabulary', '眠る', '眠る', '眠い is "sleepy." The verb version of this is <vocabulary>to sleep</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to fall asleep</vocabulary>. <i>yawn</i>

While 寝る can mean lying down or going to bed (not just sleeping), 眠る only refers to falling asleep or being in a state of sleep.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5263', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%A0%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:57:24.430948Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.921361Z'),
(5264, 'vocabulary', '眠り薬', '眠り薬', 'Some kind of <kanji>sleep</kanji>ing <kanji>medicine</kanji>? That''s a <vocabulary>sleeping potion</vocabulary>!

眠り薬 is often found in storytelling contexts to refer to some kind of sleep-inducing concoction. But it can also be used as a less medical and more colloquial term for actual sleeping medication.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5264', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%A0%E3%82%8A%E8%96%AC', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:57:42.094766Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.943595Z'),
(5265, 'vocabulary', '睡眠薬', '睡眠薬', '<kanji>Drowsy</kanji> <kanji>sleep</kanji> <kanji>medicine</kanji> sounds a little goofy, but it''s exactly what you''d expect it to be: <vocabulary>sleeping pills</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>sleeping medication</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5265', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9D%A1%E7%9C%A0%E8%96%AC', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T20:57:46.537376Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T06:45:18.337984Z'),
(5266, 'vocabulary', '積もる', '積もる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>accumulate</kanji> and the verb version is <vocabulary>to accumulate</vocabulary>.

積もる is used to describe when something continuously collects or piles up over time, like snow on the ground or dust in a room. It can also be used in a more emotional way, like when feelings or thoughts quietly build up inside someone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5266', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%8D%E3%82%82%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:58:06.125279Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.010657Z'),
(5267, 'vocabulary', '面積', '面積', 'The <kanji>surface</kanji> that <kanji>accumulate</kanji>s into one area is a particular <vocabulary>area</vocabulary>. This has to do with math. When you add all the surfaces together by accumulating them into one calculation, you get yourself the area!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5267', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%A2%E7%A9%8D', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:58:12.824588Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.044705Z'),
(5268, 'vocabulary', '体積', '体積', 'See how much liquid this <kanji>body</kanji> can <kanji>accumulate</kanji> in order to calculate its <vocabulary>volume</vocabulary>. It can be the body of a cup, the body of a bucket, or the body of a body. Whatever it is, the body accumulation represents its volume. (You know, how much space it takes up. Wasn''t math class fun?)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5268', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%93%E7%A9%8D', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:58:19.034841Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:16.365073Z'),
(5269, 'vocabulary', '検索', '検索', 'You <kanji>examine</kanji> then <kanji>search</kanji>. These are the steps you take in order to perform a <vocabulary>lookup</vocabulary> of some kind. Or, as the case may be in modern times, do an <vocabulary>internet search</vocabulary>.

検索 originally referred to looking up information in books or using indexes and reference cards, like in a library. These days it''s almost always used for digital and online searches.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5269', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A4%9C%E7%B4%A2', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:58:23.763672Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:03:40.300374Z'),
(5270, 'vocabulary', '索引', '索引', 'You <kanji>search</kanji> then <kanji>pull</kanji> items from the <vocabulary>index</vocabulary> — you know, that list of keywords in alphabetical order at the end of a nonfiction book. Or in 五十音順 order, if you''re fancy enough to be reading Japanese!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5270', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%A2%E5%BC%95', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:58:28.933034Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.089767Z'),
(5271, 'vocabulary', '緊急', '緊急', 'Things are <kanji>tense</kanji> and we have to <kanji>hurry</kanji>! This is quite the <vocabulary>urgent</vocabulary> <vocabulary>emergency</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5271', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%8A%E6%80%A5', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:58:40.456651Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.100836Z'),
(5272, 'vocabulary', '締切', '締切', 'Things 締める (tighten), and eventually 切る (cut off), meaning you’ve reached the <vocabulary>deadline</vocabulary>! You can associate this with the feeling of your chest tightening once you approach the <vocabulary>due date</vocabulary> for something.

Note that you''ll often encounter this written with okurigana like 締め切り as well. You might even see it with a simplified version of the 締 kanji written 〆切, which is relatively common in handwriting!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5272', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A0%E5%88%87', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:58:54.53017Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:39:13.80923Z'),
(5273, 'vocabulary', '締まる', '締まる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>tighten</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to tighten</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5273', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A0%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:59:00.506162Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:39:13.80923Z'),
(5274, 'vocabulary', '大臣', '大臣', 'A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>servant</kanji> is a really important servant — you know, one who serves a king or some other political leader. What do we call a big, highfalutin servant who prances around doing jobs for the leader? A <vocabulary>minister</vocabulary>.

大臣 refers to high-ranking government officials who oversee ministries or departments, and it can be translated in different ways depending on the country and its political system, such as <vocabulary>cabinet minister</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>state minister</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>secretary of state</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5274', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E8%87%A3', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:59:06.245063Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(5275, 'vocabulary', '航法', '航法', 'The <kanji>navigation</kanji> <kanji>method</kanji>s are the methods used for <vocabulary>navigation</vocabulary>.

航法 is a technical term for navigation, specifically referring to the techniques and methods used to guide a ship, aircraft, or spacecraft from one place to another.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5275', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%AA%E6%B3%95', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:59:12.420373Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T18:57:59.068505Z'),
(5276, 'vocabulary', '航空', '航空', '<kanji>Navigation</kanji> in the <kanji>sky</kanji> is <vocabulary>aviation</vocabulary>. Vroom vroom, sky flying!

On its own 航空 refers to aviation, but you''ll usually see it in compound words like 航空機 (aircraft) and 航空会社 (airline company).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5276', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%AA%E7%A9%BA', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:59:16.701967Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T18:57:59.068505Z'),
(5277, 'vocabulary', '直航', '直航', 'Remember how 直行 is "nonstop"? Well, with 直航 it''s the same thing, but it''s only used for flights and boat trips, making this a <vocabulary>nonstop flight</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>direct voyage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5277', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B4%E8%88%AA', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:59:21.116955Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T18:57:59.068505Z'),
(5278, 'vocabulary', '商店街', '商店街', 'If there are <kanji>merchandise</kanji> <kanji>shop</kanji>s filling the <kanji>streets</kanji>, then you must have wandered into a <vocabulary>shopping district</vocabulary>. Hope you remembered to bring your wallet!

商店街 generally refers to a pedestrian-friendly street or area with lots of local shops and small businesses. Many 商店街 are covered with a roof, in which case they become <vocabulary>shopping arcade</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5278', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%95%86%E5%BA%97%E8%A1%97', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:59:28.62234Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.198453Z'),
(5279, 'vocabulary', '街道', '街道', 'When <kanji>streets</kanji> and <kanji>road</kanji>s are combined together, you get something wide enough to be a <vocabulary>highway</vocabulary>!

But 街道 doesn''t mean a modern highway that you drive on. It refers to the great <vocabulary>high road</vocabulary>s of the past that linked major cities and regions. In Japan, these were typically the Edo-period routes like 日光街道 (Nikkō Kaidō) and 中山道 (Nakasendō). It also works for other historically important routes, like ancient Rome''s アッピア街道 (the Appian Way).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5279', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%97%E9%81%93', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:59:33.876971Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:25:22.580655Z'),
(5280, 'vocabulary', '診る', '診る', 'In order to <kanji>diagnose</kanji> a patient, you must first go <vocabulary>to examine a patient</vocabulary>. When you examine them you know what''s wrong with them and then you can diagnose them, which is why this verb is <vocabulary>to examine</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5280', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%BA%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:59:39.455282Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.241789Z'),
(5281, 'vocabulary', '診察', '診察', 'I will <kanji>diagnose</kanji> you by making an informed <kanji>guess</kanji> during this <vocabulary>medical exam</vocabulary>. 

診察 is what a doctor does when they take a look at you — a <vocabulary>medical examination</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>medical consultation</vocabulary>. Keep in mind this doesn''t refer to specific medical tests you might undergo to help the doctor diagnose you. (For those, you''d use 検査.) ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5281', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%BA%E5%AF%9F', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:59:51.44182Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.251973Z'),
(5282, 'vocabulary', '詰める', '詰める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>stuffed</kanji>, this word means <vocabulary>to stuff in</vocabulary>, as in you''re doing the stuffing. You know this because you''re a <reading>mail</reading> (める) carrier, and you always have to stuff mail into people''s packed mailboxes. Why don''t these people check their mail more? It’s hard work trying to cram in all this junk mail!

詰める refers to tightly fitting something into an available space, like cramming clothes into a suitcase, putting jam into a jar, or even scooting over to make room, as you''re squeezing yourself into a narrower space.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5282', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%B0%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T20:59:56.918009Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.264478Z'),
(5283, 'vocabulary', '詰まる', '詰まる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. Remember how 詰める means "to stuff in"? Well, this is the intransitive version that focuses on the thing being stuffed, so it means <vocabulary>to be stuffed with</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be crammed full</vocabulary>. You can remember this because your pillow is stuffed with まる the cat''s hair. It''s filled almost to bursting with bits of まる, making it extremely soft and comfy.

Like your まる-stuffed pillow, 詰まる is used when something completely fills up a space, whether it''s a wallet packed with bills (you wish), a book filled with tons of useful information, or a schedule full of to-dos. It can also mean <vocabulary>to be stuck</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be clogged</vocabulary>, so you can use it if something gets stuck in your throat, or even when your toilet is clogged.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5283', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%B0%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:00:01.591209Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:02:16.244157Z'),
(5284, 'vocabulary', '誘惑する', '誘惑する', 'If 誘惑 is seduction or temptation, then the verb version would be <vocabulary>to seduce</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to tempt</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5284', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%98%E6%83%91%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 28, 1, '2013-01-24T21:00:27.57021Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:13.561189Z'),
(5285, 'vocabulary', '請求', '請求', 'You <kanji>request</kanji> then you <kanji>request</kanji> again… Yep, this sounds like a <vocabulary>request</vocabulary> to me.

請求 isn''t just any old casual request — it''s used when formally requesting something from another party. It often involves payment requests (like a <vocabulary>charge</vocabulary>), but can also refer to asking for things like application forms or informational materials. It can even refer to the kind of <vocabulary>claim</vocabulary> you file in court.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5285', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%8B%E6%B1%82', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T21:00:35.669497Z', NULL, '2026-03-01T17:02:02.461141Z'),
(5286, 'vocabulary', '請願', '請願', 'I <kanji>request</kanji> and then I <kanji>request</kanji> again. So many requests, but nobody will listen to me. My last hope is to get a bunch of people who have the same ideas together and make a <vocabulary>petition</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5286', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%8B%E9%A1%98', 51, 1, '2013-01-24T21:00:51.519505Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:13.607425Z'),
(5287, 'vocabulary', '要請', '要請', 'You <kanji>need</kanji> something so you <kanji>request</kanji> it. But, since it''s such a strong need, it''s not really a request, it''s more of a <vocabulary>demand</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5287', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%81%E8%AB%8B', 42, 1, '2013-01-24T21:01:00.417416Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:28:03.237353Z'),
(5288, 'vocabulary', '請ける', '請ける', 'Someone <kanji>request</kanji>s something, and you''re the one <vocabulary>to take on</vocabulary> their request. Wow, how nice of you <vocabulary>to accept</vocabulary> that request!

請ける is a formal word often used in business contexts to describe someone willingly accepting a job, task, or request. For example, you might use 請ける when taking on a new project or accepting customer orders.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5288', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%8B%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T21:01:06.469085Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.302506Z'),
(5289, 'vocabulary', '貸し〜', '貸し〜', 'To lend or to rent is 貸す. This is the noun version of that, used as a prefix. It is something that has been rented, a "<vocabulary>rental</vocabulary> ____," like a 貸しビル (rental office building) or 貸しボート (rental boat).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5289', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%B8%E3%81%97%E3%80%9C', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T21:01:11.011689Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:07:16.01728Z'),
(5290, 'vocabulary', '道に迷う', '道に迷う', 'Remember 迷う? It means "to get lost." If you get lost on a <kanji>road</kanji>, you are still lost, wandering around looking for the correct turn. This word means <vocabulary>to be lost</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to get lost</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5290', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%93%E3%81%AB%E8%BF%B7%E3%81%86', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T21:01:17.843222Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.333139Z'),
(5291, 'vocabulary', '閣僚', '閣僚', 'The <kanji>cabinet</kanji> <kanji>colleague</kanji>s are the <vocabulary>cabinet ministers</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>cabinet secretaries</vocabulary>. They''re the people in the Cabinet who are all colleagues with each other, making them <vocabulary>cabinet members</vocabulary>. Depending on the country, they''re also called ministers or secretaries.

閣僚 is a formal term that refers to the collective group of high-ranking officials in a cabinet, but it can sometimes refer to an individual cabinet member as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5291', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%A3%E5%83%9A', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T21:01:59.374939Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.343522Z'),
(5292, 'vocabulary', '閣議', '閣議', 'When <kanji>the cabinet</kanji> meets for <kanji>deliberation</kanji> they are having a <vocabulary>cabinet meeting</vocabulary>. So governmenty.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5292', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%A3%E8%AD%B0', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T21:02:07.590081Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.353393Z'),
(5293, 'vocabulary', '英雄', '英雄', 'You''ve learned 英 means "England," but it can also mean <kanji>excel</kanji>. So what kind of person excels at being <kanji>brave</kanji>? A <vocabulary>hero</vocabulary>!

英雄 is a relatively formal word often used to describe historical or legendary heroes, like William Wallace or King Arthur. In more casual contexts, like when talking about superheroes or referring to your dad as your hero, ヒーロー is more common.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5293', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%B1%E9%9B%84', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T21:02:30.859206Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.364303Z'),
(5294, 'vocabulary', '韓国', '韓国', 'The <kanji>Korea</kanji> <kanji>Country</kanji> is <vocabulary>Korea</vocabulary>, of course... well, <vocabulary>South Korea</vocabulary> that is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5294', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9F%93%E5%9B%BD', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T21:02:36.10577Z', NULL, '2026-01-06T00:31:19.090161Z'),
(5295, 'vocabulary', '日韓', '日韓', 'Take the 日 from 日本 (Japan) and the 韓 from 韓国 (Korea), and stick ''em together to get an abbreviation for <vocabulary>Japan and South Korea</vocabulary>.

日韓 is a bit formal, so you''ll usually see it in newspapers or reports referring to Japan and South Korea together. Think international relations and soccer matches.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5295', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E9%9F%93', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T21:02:42.618973Z', NULL, '2026-01-06T00:31:19.090161Z'),
(5296, 'vocabulary', '魅力', '魅力', 'The <kanji>alluring</kanji> <kanji>power</kanji> is the power of <vocabulary>allure</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5296', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AD%85%E5%8A%9B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:02:49.385647Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(5297, 'vocabulary', '並べる', '並べる', '並ぶ is when something is lining up. 並べる is when you''re lining something up. In other words, 並べる is transitive. It means <vocabulary>to line something up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to put something in order</vocabulary>. You know this because when it comes to <reading>bear</reading>s (べる), you have to line them up yourself, because they won''t be doing it for you, that''s for sure. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5297', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%A6%E3%81%B9%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:37:41.896167Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.893493Z'),
(5298, 'vocabulary', '乏しい', '乏しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>scarce</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>scarce</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5298', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%8F%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:37:48.503356Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.901657Z'),
(5299, 'vocabulary', '欠乏', '欠乏', 'We are <kanji>lack</kanji>ing and things are <kanji>scarce</kanji>. We have a <vocabulary>lack</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>scarcity</vocabulary> of resources.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5299', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%A0%E4%B9%8F', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:37:54.484412Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.909962Z'),
(5300, 'vocabulary', '冷やす', '冷やす', '冷たい is "cold." This is the verb version, so you''re making something cold. This word is <vocabulary>to chill something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to cool something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5300', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%B7%E3%82%84%E3%81%99', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:38:02.080762Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.918891Z'),
(5301, 'vocabulary', '結婚', '結婚', 'Let''s <kanji>bind</kanji> this <kanji>marriage</kanji> up with a <vocabulary>marriage</vocabulary>. No better way to make a marriage happen than a marriage, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5301', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%90%E5%A9%9A', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:38:06.724646Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.927184Z'),
(5302, 'vocabulary', '求婚', '求婚', 'When you <kanji>request</kanji> <kanji>marriage</kanji> with someone else, you are doing a <vocabulary>marriage proposal</vocabulary>. Will you marry me, Brad Pitt?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5302', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%82%E5%A9%9A', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:38:18.641921Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:53:56.46731Z'),
(5303, 'vocabulary', '新婚旅行', '新婚旅行', 'You know that 旅行 is a <kanji>trip</kanji>. The other part is <kanji>new</kanji> <kanji>marriage</kanji>. What''s the trip you go on after a new marriage? The <vocabulary>honeymoon</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5303', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E5%A9%9A%E6%97%85%E8%A1%8C', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:38:23.286557Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:48:50.473642Z'),
(5304, 'vocabulary', '未婚', '未婚', '<kanji>Not yet</kanji> <kanji>marriage</kanji>? You haven''t been married. You''re <vocabulary>unmarried</vocabulary>.

未婚 is a relatively formal term often used in official documents or surveys. You''ll usually see it paired with other words, like 未婚女性 (unmarried woman), whereas the word 独身 (single) can be used either on its own or in combination with other words.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5304', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AA%E5%A9%9A', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T21:38:27.91709Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:26:25.571243Z'),
(5305, 'vocabulary', '好奇心', '好奇心', 'You <kanji>like</kanji> it because it gives you an <kanji>odd</kanji> feeling in your <kanji>heart</kanji>. You don''t quite know what it is you like, but that''s because it''s <vocabulary>curiosity</vocabulary> that you have.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5305', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%BD%E5%A5%87%E5%BF%83', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T21:38:33.402032Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.397739Z'),
(5306, 'vocabulary', '奇数', '奇数', '<kanji>Odd</kanji> <kanji>numbers</kanji> are <vocabulary>odd numbers</vocabulary> (as in, 1, 3, 5, 7, though they are a bit strange as well).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5306', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%87%E6%95%B0', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T21:38:38.943736Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.041761Z'),
(5307, 'vocabulary', '奇妙', '奇妙', 'That''s very <kanji>odd</kanji> and <kanji>strange</kanji>, making it <vocabulary>bizarre</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>peculiar</vocabulary>.

奇妙 is often used in literature to describe something that feels unusually <vocabulary>strange</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>odd</vocabulary>. It describes something being an almost otherworldly quality, sparking curiosity because it''s so bizarre.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5307', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%87%E5%A6%99', 29, 1, '2013-01-24T21:38:53.036344Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.417369Z'),
(5308, 'vocabulary', '延ばす', '延ばす', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>prolong</kanji> and this verb means <vocabulary>to prolong something</vocabulary>.

延ばす conveys the idea of expanding upon something that already exists to make it longer, often in terms of time or physical distance. For example, it can refer to delaying a schedule or extending a railway.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5308', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%B6%E3%81%B0%E3%81%99', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:06:34.931421Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.722552Z'),
(5309, 'vocabulary', '延長', '延長', '<kanji>Prolong</kanji> it for a <kanji>long</kanji> while. We need more time. We need a <vocabulary>prolonging</vocabulary> or <vocabulary> extension</vocabulary> of some kind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5309', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%B6%E9%95%B7', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:06:44.986068Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.734118Z'),
(5310, 'vocabulary', '延期する', '延期する', 'Can we <kanji>prolong</kanji> this <kanji>period of time</kanji> to a later date? I want <vocabulary>to postpone</vocabulary>, if possible.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5310', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%B6%E6%9C%9F%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:07:01.440682Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.743814Z'),
(5311, 'vocabulary', '快い', '快い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>pleasant</kanji>? It is also <vocabulary>pleasant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5311', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%AB%E3%81%84', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:07:06.851599Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.754009Z'),
(5312, 'vocabulary', '快感', '快感', 'When you have the <kanji>feeling</kanji> of <kanji>pleasant</kanji>ness, you are experiencing <vocabulary>pleasure</vocabulary>.

While 快感 can describe the thrill of achieving something satisfying, it''s often associated with <vocabulary>pleasurable feeling</vocabulary>s from taboo or unusual experiences. For example, it might refer to the pleasure of teasing someone, indulging in a bad habit like picking earwax, or feeling sensual delight.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5312', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%AB%E6%84%9F', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:07:13.003801Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(5313, 'vocabulary', '快速', '快速', 'It''s <kanji>pleasant</kanji> but also <kanji>fast</kanji>. When getting on a train, it''s nice to find a train that''s both of these things. What makes it pleasant? That would be the <vocabulary>high speed</vocabulary>. What makes it fast? Also the <vocabulary>high speed</vocabulary>. Really, if you''re on a train you''d rather get to your destination sooner than later, so a high speed 快速 train is the way to go.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5313', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%AB%E9%80%9F', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:07:20.533996Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.775751Z'),
(5314, 'vocabulary', '不快', '不快', 'Things that are <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>pleasant</kanji> are <vocabulary>unpleasant</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>uncomfortable</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5314', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%BF%AB', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:07:25.772441Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.785358Z'),
(5315, 'vocabulary', '懐かしい', '懐かしい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>nostalgia</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>nostalgic</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5315', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%87%90%E3%81%8B%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:07:31.079555Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:30:52.824393Z'),
(5316, 'vocabulary', '押す', '押す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>push</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to push</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5316', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%BC%E3%81%99', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:07:36.181954Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.803528Z'),
(5317, 'vocabulary', '撮る', '撮る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>photograph</kanji>. When you make that into a verb, it becomes <vocabulary>to take a picture</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>to take a video</vocabulary>. Basically, it''s for any form of shooting you do with a camera!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5317', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%92%AE%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:08:00.329234Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:24:39.840037Z'),
(5318, 'vocabulary', '撮影', '撮影', 'If you <kanji>photograph</kanji> a <kanji>shadow</kanji>, you''re obviously doing some kind of <vocabulary>photography</vocabulary>. (In the old days photography meant using light to burn shadows onto special paper, but now it''s all fancy and digital.) This isn''t just still photography though; it also means <vocabulary>filming</vocabulary> (as in a video or movie). Basically, if it has to do with taking photos or video, 撮影 is your dude.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5318', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%92%AE%E5%BD%B1', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:08:07.127902Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:24:39.840037Z'),
(5319, 'vocabulary', '盗撮', '盗撮', '<kanji>Steal</kanji>ing <kanji>photograph</kanji>s of you. Wait, you didn''t know you were being photographed or filmed? That''s <vocabulary>secret photography</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>secret filming</vocabulary>. Creeeeeepy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5319', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%97%E6%92%AE', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:08:14.000058Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:24:39.840037Z'),
(5320, 'vocabulary', '診断する', '診断する', '診断 is a <kanji>diagnosis</kanji>. Make this into a verb, and you have <vocabulary>to diagnose</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5320', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%BA%E6%96%AD%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:08:23.669199Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.849562Z'),
(5321, 'vocabulary', '旗', '旗', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5321', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%97', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:08:32.514209Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.859608Z'),
(5322, 'vocabulary', '白旗', '白旗', 'A <kanji>white</kanji> <kanji>flag</kanji> is a <vocabulary>white flag</vocabulary>. It''s one for surrender. I give up! Too many kanji!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5322', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BD%E6%97%97', 32, 1, '2013-01-24T22:08:37.448688Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:14.643144Z'),
(5323, 'vocabulary', '国旗', '国旗', 'A <kanji>country</kanji>''s <kanji>flag</kanji> is the flag they use for their nation. It''s the <vocabulary>national flag</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5323', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BD%E6%97%97', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:08:42.245622Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.868884Z'),
(5324, 'vocabulary', '背景', '背景', 'The <kanji>back</kanji> <kanji>scene</kanji> is the scene going on in the back. You don''t really notice it, because it''s the <vocabulary>background</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5324', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%8C%E6%99%AF', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:08:49.07624Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.878674Z'),
(5325, 'vocabulary', '更に', '更に', 'When you''re <kanji>renew</kanji>ing what you''re saying, you''re adding onto it, which makes this <vocabulary>furthermore</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>further</vocabulary>. 

This is one of those good paragraph or sentence-starting essay words, which is how you''ll often see it used!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5325', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B4%E3%81%AB', 24, 1, '2013-01-24T22:08:54.927177Z', NULL, '2026-04-04T20:17:45.612577Z'),
(5326, 'vocabulary', '更生', '更生', 'Experiencing a <kanji>renew</kanji>al of <kanji>life</kanji> means going through a process of <vocabulary>rehabilitation</vocabulary>.

 更生 generally refers to rehabilitation from various issues such as addiction, crime,  delinquency, or personal struggles. Ideally, this leads to <vocabulary>reforming oneself</vocabulary> and reintegrating into society. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5326', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B4%E7%94%9F', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:09:00.131078Z', NULL, '2026-04-04T20:17:45.612577Z'),
(5327, 'vocabulary', '更新', '更新', 'You’re <kanji>renew</kanji>ing something to become <kanji>new</kanji> again. We’re talking about a <vocabulary>renewal</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>update</vocabulary> to something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5327', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B4%E6%96%B0', 22, 1, '2013-01-24T22:09:05.526636Z', NULL, '2026-04-04T20:17:45.612577Z'),
(5328, 'vocabulary', '変更', '変更', 'Things that <kanji>change</kanji> and <kanji>renew</kanji> are going through <vocabulary>alteration</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>change</vocabulary>. Pretty straightforward!

Unlike 変化, which is used for spontaneous changes, 変更 is used for deliberate or planned changes, like changing an appointment, modifying rules, or altering a plan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5328', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%89%E6%9B%B4', 22, 1, '2013-01-24T22:09:16.535562Z', NULL, '2026-04-04T20:17:45.612577Z'),
(5329, 'vocabulary', '枕', '枕', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5329', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%95', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:09:22.563838Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.915735Z'),
(5330, 'vocabulary', '浮く', '浮く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>float</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to float</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5330', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%AE%E3%81%8F', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:09:34.654953Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.928322Z'),
(5331, 'vocabulary', '浮世絵', '浮世絵', 'A <kanji>float</kanji>ing <kanji>world</kanji> <kanji>painting</kanji> is what you call an <vocabulary>ukiyo-e woodblock print</vocabulary> in Japan. The "floating world" refers to an evanescent world that is impermanent (floating), just as beauty and entertainment are. Back in the day, this type of art was made into woodblock prints and paintings. Unfortunately, we don''t have a name for these in English because they''re so unique and special, but they come up more than you''d think, so get used to ukiyo-e!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5331', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%AE%E4%B8%96%E7%B5%B5', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:09:49.359633Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:12:48.398208Z'),
(5332, 'vocabulary', '浮気', '浮気', 'A <kanji>float</kanji>ing <kanji>spirit</kanji>? If your spirit is floating around (in matters of love), then you might be likely to <vocabulary>cheat</vocabulary> on your partner. Careful — once it starts, your <vocabulary>cheating</vocabulary> could turn into a full-blown <vocabulary>affair</vocabulary>.

浮気 means cheating in a relationship, whether you''re dating or married. But even harmless flirting can sometimes count as 浮気, since it indicates your spirit is "floating" and not as securely tied to your partner as they''d like… ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5332', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%AE%E6%B0%97', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:09:54.927304Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:30:36.182038Z'),
(5333, 'vocabulary', '渇く', '渇く', 'When you have a <kanji>thirst</kanji>, you tend <vocabulary>to become thirsty</vocabulary>.

Note that this does not mean “to be thirsty” by itself, but only in combination with 喉, as in 喉が渇く.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5333', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%87%E3%81%8F', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:10:00.589689Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:03:27.911762Z'),
(5334, 'vocabulary', '漏水', '漏水', 'A <kanji>leak</kanji> of <kanji>water</kanji> is a <vocabulary>water leakage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5334', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%8F%E6%B0%B4', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:10:06.401566Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:56.989239Z'),
(5335, 'vocabulary', '漏出', '漏出', 'Something is <kanji>leak</kanji>ing and whatever is leaking is <kanji>exit</kanji>ing what it was in before. This is when a <vocabulary>leak</vocabulary> occurs.

漏出 is a formal word that means <vocabulary>leakage</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>spillage</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>seepage</vocabulary>. It''s often used for physical leaks of substances like liquids, gases, or other materials.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5335', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%8F%E5%87%BA', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:10:11.722697Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.000051Z'),
(5336, 'vocabulary', '漏れる', '漏れる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>leak</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to leak</vocabulary>.

漏れる can be used for both physical and abstract leakage, like water or gas leaking, sound or light seeping out, or information or emotions being revealed unintentionally.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5336', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%8F%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:10:17.217664Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.012638Z'),
(5337, 'vocabulary', '照らす', '照らす', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>illuminate</kanji>, so this means <vocabulary>to illuminate</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to shine light on</vocabulary> something.

But don''t get it confused with 照れる, which is "to feel embarrassed" — that would be embarrassing. You know this is "to illuminate" because you get a <reading>rash</reading> (らす) every time something shines on you too much.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5337', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%A7%E3%82%89%E3%81%99', 31, 1, '2013-01-24T22:10:29.106239Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:20:57.057702Z'),
(5338, 'vocabulary', '照れる', '照れる', 'Your feel as if your cheeks are <kanji>illuminate</kanji>d when you start <vocabulary>to feel shy</vocabulary>. Probably illuminated in red, which is why this also means <vocabulary>to blush</vocabulary>. 

照れる means <vocabulary>to be bashful</vocabulary>, often in situations where the embarrassment stems from modesty rather than serious discomfort. It''s used to describe feeling self-conscious in an endearing way, such as blushing due to praise, attention, or being teased in a lighthearted manner.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5338', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%A7%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:10:34.984824Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.022818Z'),
(5339, 'vocabulary', '照明', '照明', '<kanji>Illuminate</kanji> and make things <kanji>bright</kanji>, my <vocabulary>illumination</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5339', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%A7%E6%98%8E', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:10:47.303244Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(5340, 'vocabulary', '出版', '出版', 'If you <kanji>exit</kanji> that <kanji>edition</kanji> out of your hands and into the world, you''ve gotten something published! That is <vocabulary>publishing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5340', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E7%89%88', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:10:56.757536Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:11:10.895336Z'),
(5341, 'vocabulary', '版権', '版権', 'You have <kanji>edition</kanji> <kanji>rights</kanji> over this. This edition is yours, and if anyone copies this edition, they get in trouble. You have <vocabulary>copyright</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5341', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%88%E6%A8%A9', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:11:18.469492Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T22:02:48.269631Z'),
(5342, 'vocabulary', '初版', '初版', 'The <kanji>first</kanji> <kanji>edition</kanji> is the <vocabulary>first edition</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5342', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%9D%E7%89%88', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:11:23.246918Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:28:35.812439Z'),
(5343, 'vocabulary', '盗む', '盗む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>steal</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to steal</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5343', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%97%E3%82%80', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:11:29.327601Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.112443Z'),
(5344, 'vocabulary', '強盗', '強盗', 'You are <kanji>strong</kanji> so you break into someone''s home, hold them up, then <kanji>steal</kanji> from them. Ha ha! Take that, next door neighbor! This is a <vocabulary>burglary</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>robbery</vocabulary>, depending on how crazy you get after you use that strength to break down that door.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5344', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%B7%E7%9B%97', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:11:35.162851Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:05:21.088648Z'),
(5345, 'vocabulary', '盗作', '盗作', '<kanji>Steal</kanji> what other people <kanji>make</kanji>? Wow. Way to go. That is <vocabulary>plagiarism</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5345', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%97%E4%BD%9C', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:11:40.745835Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:12:39.703958Z'),
(5346, 'vocabulary', '盗品', '盗品', 'These are <kanji>stolen</kanji> (steal) <kanji>goods</kanji>. So, I''ll give you a deal, because, you know, they''re <vocabulary>stolen goods</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5346', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%97%E5%93%81', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:11:46.357694Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(5347, 'vocabulary', '幼稚園', '幼稚園', 'If 幼稚 is <kanji>childish</kanji>, then a <kanji>garden</kanji> for childish people would be a place full of lots of children, probably growing in some way. When you''re growing little children in a garden, you''re putting them into some kind of school. In this case, it''s a <vocabulary>kindergarten</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5347', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BC%E7%A8%9A%E5%9C%92', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:11:52.724367Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.154229Z'),
(5348, 'vocabulary', '切符', '切符', 'A <kanji>cut</kanji> <kanji>token</kanji> is a token you cut out from a sheet of paper into smaller sheets, and each of these pieces is a <vocabulary>ticket</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5348', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%87%E7%AC%A6', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:11:57.708615Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.163657Z'),
(5349, 'vocabulary', '精一杯', '精一杯', 'You put <kanji>one cup of liquid</kanji>... no, one full cup of liquid <kanji>spirit</kanji> into what you''re doing. None of this half-cup garbage. A full one. That means you''re going at it <vocabulary>with all one''s might</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5349', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%BE%E4%B8%80%E6%9D%AF', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:12:05.079833Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.173529Z'),
(5350, 'vocabulary', '精神的', '精神的', 'Your 精神 is your spirit or your mind. If you add 的 to it, you get <vocabulary>mental</vocabulary>. It can mean <vocabulary>spiritual</vocabulary> too, but it more often shows up in reference to a person''s mental or <vocabulary>emotional</vocabulary> state.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5350', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%BE%E7%A5%9E%E7%9A%84', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:12:11.095614Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.193567Z'),
(5351, 'vocabulary', '精神病', '精神病', '精神 is your spirit or mind, the non-physical part of you. When it''s <kanji>sick</kanji>, it''s a <vocabulary>mental illness</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>mental disorder</vocabulary>.

Note that the term 精神病, when used on its own, carries a stigma and is considered a bit disrespectful and outdated.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5351', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%BE%E7%A5%9E%E7%97%85', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:12:16.112719Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.203922Z'),
(5352, 'vocabulary', '〜系', '〜系', 'Trace something back through its <kanji>lineage</kanji> to determine what <vocabulary>type</vocabulary> of thing it is.

〜系 is a suffix you can attach casually to a word to classify it as a <vocabulary>genre</vocabulary> or group, like 理系 (science-oriented fields), おしゃれ系 (trendy or stylish people), or 日系 (people of Japanese <vocabulary>lineage</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5352', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E7%B3%BB', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:12:23.09705Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T01:19:34.28962Z'),
(5353, 'vocabulary', '日系', '日系', 'The 日 stands for 日本 here, making it mean "Japanese." So what''s a more common way of saying Japanese <kanji>lineage</kanji>? That would be <vocabulary>Japanese descent</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>Japanese origin</vocabulary>.

日系 can be used for both people and entities, such as 日系アメリカ人 (Japanese-American) or 日系企業 (Japanese-operated company).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5353', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E7%B3%BB', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:12:29.853423Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T01:19:34.28962Z'),
(5354, 'vocabulary', '太陽系', '太陽系', 'You know 太陽 means "sun," so hopefully you can guess what a sun <kanji>system</kanji> is. That''s right — it''s the <vocabulary>solar system</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5354', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%AA%E9%99%BD%E7%B3%BB', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:12:34.995358Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T01:19:34.28962Z'),
(5355, 'vocabulary', '体系的', '体系的', 'Give a <kanji>body</kanji> to a <kanji>system</kanji> to make it a real, structured system, you know? Then add 的 to it to make it <vocabulary>systematic</vocabulary>.

体系的 is a formal word for something that follows a clear structure or framework, often used in academic contexts. For example, you might hear it when discussing a systematic approach to solving a problem or organizing information systematically.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5355', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%93%E7%B3%BB%E7%9A%84', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:12:39.614207Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T01:19:34.28962Z'),
(5356, 'vocabulary', '絶える', '絶える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>die out</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to die out</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to come to an end</vocabulary>.

絶える is mostly used for when something that was ongoing comes to an end, like breath stopping, laughter fading away, a supply being cut off, or foot traffic dying down. You know this because when something dies out, nothing is left but thin air (える).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5356', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B6%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:12:44.178908Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.254423Z'),
(5357, 'vocabulary', '翌日', '翌日', '<kanji>The next</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji> is just that, <vocabulary>the next day</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5357', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BF%8C%E6%97%A5', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:13:01.048164Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T07:36:54.117919Z'),
(5358, 'vocabulary', '翌月', '翌月', '<kanji>The next</kanji> <kanji>month</kanji> is <vocabulary>the next month</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5358', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BF%8C%E6%9C%88', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:13:06.846236Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T07:36:54.117919Z'),
(5359, 'vocabulary', '翌朝', '翌朝', '<kanji>The next</kanji> <kanji>morning</kanji> is <vocabulary>the next morning</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5359', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BF%8C%E6%9C%9D', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:13:15.226253Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T07:36:54.117919Z'),
(5360, 'vocabulary', '翌年', '翌年', '<kanji>The next</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji> is <vocabulary>the next year</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5360', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BF%8C%E5%B9%B4', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:13:20.968367Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T07:36:54.117919Z'),
(5361, 'vocabulary', '背', '背', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5361', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%8C', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:13:30.667558Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.306407Z'),
(5362, 'vocabulary', '一覧', '一覧', 'With just <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>look at</kanji> something you''re able to take in all the info you need, because that something is <vocabulary>a list</vocabulary>. A nice list that gives you <vocabulary>an overview</vocabulary> at a glance. Perfect for all that <vocabulary>looking over</vocabulary> you have to do. You busy consumer of information, you.

一覧 usually refers to a neat list you can skim over easily to get the info you need, like a guest list or a data table. With する, it becomes a pretty formal verb for looking over something in a quick way, like skimming through a report or casually checking out a place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5362', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%A7', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:14:10.063372Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:07:07.19438Z'),
(5363, 'vocabulary', 'ご覧', 'ご覧', 'ご覧 is the polite noun form of 見る and means <vocabulary>looking at</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>seeing</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>watching</vocabulary>. It''s often paired with 〜になる, as in ご覧になる, which is a respectful way to describe someone else''s 見る action.

Some other common phrases you might hear are ご覧ください (please take a look) or ご覧のとおり (as you can see). They''re formal but common in polite speech, such as in customer service or business presentations. ご覧 can even be used on its own as a gentle command, like ほら、そこをご覧 ("Hey, look over there"). ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5363', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%94%E8%A6%A7', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:14:16.723538Z', NULL, '2025-07-28T16:43:41.061574Z'),
(5364, 'vocabulary', '観覧', '観覧', 'If you <kanji>view</kanji> and <kanji>look at</kanji> something, you are <vocabulary>viewing</vocabulary> that thing, or <vocabulary>watching</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5364', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%B3%E8%A6%A7', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:14:22.82642Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.344347Z'),
(5365, 'vocabulary', '詳しい', '詳しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>detailed</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>detailed</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5365', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%B3%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 17, 1, '2013-01-24T22:14:28.405806Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:15.85551Z'),
(5366, 'vocabulary', '詳細', '詳細', 'Things that are <kanji>detailed</kanji> and... <kanji>detailed</kanji> are, well, the <vocabulary>details</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5366', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%B3%E7%B4%B0', 17, 1, '2013-01-24T22:14:35.75656Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:47:55.647024Z'),
(5367, 'vocabulary', '不詳', '不詳', 'Something is <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>detailed</kanji> at all. I don''t know anything about it. No details whatsoever. It''s a totally <vocabulary>unknown</vocabulary> entity. It''s <vocabulary>unidentified</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5367', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E8%A9%B3', 17, 1, '2013-01-24T22:14:40.643116Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:15.899195Z'),
(5368, 'vocabulary', '貧しい', '貧しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>poor</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>poor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5368', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A7%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:14:47.101602Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.356905Z'),
(5369, 'vocabulary', '貧乏', '貧乏', 'You are <kanji>poor</kanji> and money / food is very <kanji>scarce</kanji> for you, making you <vocabulary>poor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5369', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A7%E4%B9%8F', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:14:52.797112Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.366876Z'),
(5370, 'vocabulary', '購入', '購入', 'When your <kanji>purchase</kanji> <kanji>enter</kanji>s into effect, you have made your <vocabulary>purchase</vocabulary>.

購入 is a relatively formal word for the act of purchasing something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5370', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%BC%E5%85%A5', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:15:04.382921Z', NULL, '2025-08-25T17:05:57.402032Z'),
(5371, 'vocabulary', '購買', '購買', 'You <kanji>purchase</kanji> <em>and</em> <kanji>buy</kanji> something? That''s some serious <vocabulary>purchasing</vocabulary>.

購買 is a formal term for the act of purchasing goods or services, mostly in business or economic contexts. It''s often combined with another word, like in 購買意欲 (consumer appetite) or 購買力 (purchasing power).

In some contexts, 購買 can also refer to a <vocabulary>school shop</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>campus store</vocabulary>, especially in high schools or universities.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5371', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%BC%E8%B2%B7', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:15:10.2604Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:13:56.397935Z'),
(5372, 'vocabulary', '購読', '購読', 'This is a <kanji>purchase</kanji>d <kanji>read</kanji> — especially one that comes regularly. In other words, a <vocabulary>subscription</vocabulary>.

購読 specifically refers to paying for a subscription to some kind of reading material, like a newspaper or magazine, so be sure not to use it for free subscriptions!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5372', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%BC%E8%AA%AD', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:15:17.902354Z', NULL, '2025-08-25T17:05:57.402032Z'),
(5373, 'vocabulary', '越える', '越える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>go beyond</kanji>, the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to go beyond</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5373', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B6%8A%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:15:28.714602Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.411507Z'),
(5374, 'vocabulary', '越権', '越権', 'If you <kanji>go beyond</kanji> your <kanji>rights</kanji>, you are going too far. You''re doing what you''re not supposed to do, and you''re overstepping your authority.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5374', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B6%8A%E6%A8%A9', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:15:33.756194Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T22:02:48.269631Z'),
(5375, 'vocabulary', '逆さま', '逆さま', 'Something is <kanji>reverse</kanji>, that much you know. The さま comes from 様, which could mean <kanji>manner</kanji>. So, something that is in a "reverse manner," is something that is <vocabulary>upside down</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5375', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%86%E3%81%95%E3%81%BE', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:15:39.088832Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:30:39.384701Z'),
(5376, 'vocabulary', '遊ぶ', '遊ぶ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>play</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to play</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5376', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8A%E3%81%B6', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:15:44.152999Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.451817Z'),
(5377, 'vocabulary', '遊園地', '遊園地', 'The <kanji>play</kanji> <kanji>park</kanji> <kanji>ground</kanji> is where you go play. It''s fun. It''s an <vocabulary>amusement park</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5377', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8A%E5%9C%92%E5%9C%B0', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:15:49.969758Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.462773Z'),
(5378, 'vocabulary', '適当', '適当', 'Everything is <kanji>suitable</kanji> and <kanji>right</kanji>. Therefore it is <vocabulary>appropriate</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>proper</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>suitable</vocabulary>. Strangely enough, this also means <vocabulary>irresponsible</vocabulary>. Maybe it''s because when everything is suitable and right for you, you''re taking too much and not leaving enough for everyone else, making you irresponsible?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5378', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%A9%E5%BD%93', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:15:55.732299Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:38:04.319615Z'),
(5379, 'vocabulary', '不適', '不適', 'Something that is <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>suitable</kanji> is <vocabulary>unsuitable</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>inappropriate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5379', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E9%81%A9', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:16:00.544435Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.509898Z'),
(5380, 'vocabulary', '登録する', '登録する', '登録 is <kanji>registration</kanji>. Make that into a verb and you have <vocabulary>to register</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5380', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BB%E9%8C%B2%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:16:05.1234Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.520509Z'),
(5381, 'vocabulary', '預ける', '預ける', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>deposit</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to deposit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to entrust</vocabulary>. 

You use 預ける when you leave something in someone else''s care for a while, expecting to get it back. For example, you can use 預ける when you deposit money in a bank, leave your coat in a cloakroom, or even when you park your bicycle in a parking area. You can also use it when you leave your child with grandparents or leave your dog at a pet hotel.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5381', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%90%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:16:38.488927Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:22:56.408073Z'),
(5382, 'vocabulary', '預金', '預金', '<kanji>Deposit</kanji> your <kanji>gold</kanji> here in the bank. When you do that, it is a <vocabulary>bank deposit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5382', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%90%E9%87%91', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:16:49.939804Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:22:56.408073Z'),
(5383, 'vocabulary', '飾る', '飾る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>decorate</kanji>. The verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to decorate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5383', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%BE%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:17:30.406643Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.555077Z'),
(5384, 'vocabulary', '服飾', '服飾', 'The <kanji>clothes</kanji> with which I <kanji>decorate</kanji> myself with is my <vocabulary>attire</vocabulary> or my <vocabulary>fashion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5384', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%8D%E9%A3%BE', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:17:37.577457Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.564362Z'),
(5385, 'vocabulary', '電飾', '電飾', 'The 電 is what powers electric things. Many times, these electric things light up (especially back in the day, when electricity did this a lot more than other things). So, you have "lighting." Then, you have <kanji>decorate</kanji>. So, if you decorate with lighting, you have <vocabulary>decorative lighting</vocabulary>.

This is quite a specific word that is generally used as a product category for decorative lighting. It’s a less common word than other expressions, such as デコレーションライト or イルミネーションライト.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5385', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E9%A3%BE', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:17:45.696942Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.030484Z'),
(5386, 'vocabulary', '騒ぐ', '騒ぐ', 'The kanji is <kanji>boisterous</kanji>. The verb version is <vocabulary>to make noise</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be boisterous</vocabulary>. Basically, you''re being boisterous when you make a lot of noise, so that''s where it comes from.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5386', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A8%92%E3%81%90', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:17:52.011482Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.582485Z'),
(5387, 'vocabulary', '騒音', '騒音', '<kanji>Boisterous</kanji> <kanji>sound</kanji> is when there''s a lot of <vocabulary>noise</vocabulary> around you. It''s the <vocabulary>din</vocabulary> of the crowd or environment.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5387', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A8%92%E9%9F%B3', 30, 1, '2013-01-24T22:17:56.501323Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.592559Z'),
(5388, 'vocabulary', '輸入する', '輸入する', '輸入 is <kanji>import</kanji>. Now let''s make it a verb. <vocabulary>To import</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5388', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%B8%E5%85%A5%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 28, 1, '2013-02-03T11:34:38.415509Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:16.46032Z'),
(5389, 'vocabulary', '内', '内', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

When this kanji is a word on its own, it''s actually very often written in hiragana. Still, the kanji version of the word is worth knowing, too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5389', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%85', 3, 1, '2013-02-06T15:02:15.49391Z', NULL, '2025-09-05T05:40:05.235761Z'),
(5390, 'vocabulary', '我々', '我々', '我 is me, so two of me makes <vocabulary>we</vocabulary>! Or <vocabulary>us</vocabulary>!

You''ve learned that 我 is an old-fashioned pronoun. 我々 is similar in that it feels formal and authoritative. This means it''s often used in situations that require official representation, like company statements, business meetings, or political speeches. Check out our context sentences for some examples!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5390', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%91%E3%80%85', 28, 1, '2013-02-23T13:34:48.183494Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(5391, 'vocabulary', '織物', '織物', 'If you <kanji>weave</kanji> a <kanji>thing</kanji>, you get a <vocabulary>textile</vocabulary>.

織物 primarily refers to woven <vocabulary>fabric</vocabulary>. It''s often associated with traditional crafts, such as kimono textiles.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5391', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B9%94%E7%89%A9', 29, 1, '2013-03-06T05:09:26.365756Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(5392, 'vocabulary', '直接的', '直接的', '直接 means "direct." Add 的 to the mix and you have <vocabulary>directly</vocabulary>.

This is a relatively formal word, so you''ll encounter other words like ダイレクト in more casual contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5392', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B4%E6%8E%A5%E7%9A%84', 28, 1, '2013-03-06T08:16:27.684906Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:46:52.388227Z'),
(5393, 'vocabulary', '怒気', '怒気', 'An <kanji>angry</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji> comes out of you when you have <vocabulary>anger</vocabulary>.

怒気 looks simple, but it''s a pretty advanced term more commonly used in literature, typically to describe a character''s anger as expressed through their voices, facial expressions, attitudes, or the surrounding atmosphere.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5393', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%92%E6%B0%97', 29, 1, '2013-03-06T08:34:38.640418Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(5394, 'vocabulary', '腹が減った', '腹が減った', 'When your <kanji>belly</kanji> has <kanji>decreased</kanji> in the amount of stuff inside it, you become <vocabulary>hungry</vocabulary>.

腹が減った is a casual way of saying <vocabulary>I''m hungry</vocabulary> in Japanese, often with a bit of a rough or masculine tone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5394', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%B9%E3%81%8C%E6%B8%9B%E3%81%A3%E3%81%9F', 29, 1, '2013-03-06T08:49:03.186588Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:29:08.554779Z'),
(5395, 'vocabulary', '招き猫', '招き猫', 'A <kanji>beckoning</kanji> <kanji>cat</kanji> is a <vocabulary>beckoning cat</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>maneki neko</vocabulary>. 

招き猫 are the cat statues believed to bring good luck and fortune to their owners. They''re often placed in front of shops with one paw raised, as if beckoning and saying, "Hey, come on in!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5395', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%9B%E3%81%8D%E7%8C%AB', 29, 1, '2013-03-06T09:22:20.997424Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.459995Z'),
(5396, 'vocabulary', '構える', '構える', 'The kanji itself means <kanji>set up</kanji>, so this means <vocabulary>to set up</vocabulary>, as in establishing something in an orderly manner.

This can also mean <vocabulary>to assume a stance</vocabulary>, like taking a fighting posture, or to hold in preparation, such as a weapon, while preparing to engage in a fight.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5396', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A7%8B%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2013-03-06T22:34:08.174107Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.470009Z'),
(5397, 'vocabulary', '訪れる', '訪れる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>visit</kanji> and the verb vocab version is also <vocabulary>to visit</vocabulary>.

訪れる is somewhat formal, so you''ll encounter it more in writing than everyday speech. It''s used to talk about visiting places, but not people. That''s one big difference with the similar-looking 訪ねる. Another is that you don''t need need a specific purpose for the kind of visiting you do with 訪れる.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5397', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%AA%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2013-03-07T03:52:31.475126Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:10:32.690072Z'),
(5398, 'vocabulary', '航空券', '航空券', 'An <kanji>aviation</kanji> (航空) <kanji>ticket</kanji> is the kind of ticket you need to fly on an airplane. That''s an <vocabulary>airplane ticket</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5398', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%AA%E7%A9%BA%E5%88%B8', 31, 1, '2013-03-07T20:49:27.952638Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T18:57:59.068505Z'),
(5399, 'vocabulary', '攻撃する', '攻撃する', '攻撃 is an <kanji>attack</kanji>. Make that into a verb and you have <vocabulary>to attack</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5399', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BB%E6%92%83%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-03-12T06:42:41.111098Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T16:52:18.698069Z'),
(5400, 'vocabulary', '先程', '先程', 'The <kanji>previous</kanji> <kanji>extent</kanji> of time was <vocabulary>a little while ago</vocabulary>. Just think to a previous extent of time that was indeed a little while ago. Now, associate!

先程 is limited to a relatively brief time frame within the same day. So it can be used for something mentioned earlier in a speech or at an event from <vocabulary>earlier in the day</vocabulary>, but not for something from a few days ago. 先程 is also polite and formal, so it''s generally used in professional or respectful settings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5400', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%88%E7%A8%8B', 30, 1, '2013-03-12T16:55:59.068353Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:27:12.714404Z'),
(5401, 'vocabulary', '官僚的', '官僚的', 'If 官僚 is "bureaucracy," then adding a 的 to it would make it <vocabulary>bureaucratic</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5401', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%98%E5%83%9A%E7%9A%84', 30, 1, '2013-03-12T17:02:02.085544Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.626081Z'),
(5402, 'vocabulary', '鼻詰まり', '鼻詰まり', '詰まる is "to be stuffed." So, if your <kanji>nose</kanji> is <kanji>stuffed</kanji>, then you have a <vocabulary>stuffy nose</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5402', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BC%BB%E8%A9%B0%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8A', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:43:35.584414Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:23:36.291149Z'),
(5403, 'vocabulary', '思い詰める', '思い詰める', '詰める is "to stuff in." If you''re really stuffing in your thoughts (<kanji>think</kanji>), you''re thinking very deeply and seriously. So this is <vocabulary>to brood</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to dwell on</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to take something to heart</vocabulary>.

思い詰める suggests you''re pondering deeply over something serious or distressing, bordering on overthinking.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5403', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%9D%E3%81%84%E8%A9%B0%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:46:41.575531Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:16.867666Z'),
(5404, 'vocabulary', '見詰める', '見詰める', 'You learned 詰める means "to stuff in." When you want to <kanji>see</kanji> something so much that you "stuff" your gaze into it, you''re probably going <vocabulary>to stare at</vocabulary> it.

見詰める means <vocabulary>to gaze</vocabulary> at something intently, often with deep thought, admiration, curiosity, or strong emotions. Keep in mind that you''ll usually see this word with the 詰 in kana, as 見つめる.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5404', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E8%A9%B0%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:46:52.666252Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:04:43.252166Z'),
(5405, 'vocabulary', '雄犬', '雄犬', 'A <kanji>male</kanji> <kanji>dog</kanji> is a <vocabulary>male dog</vocabulary>.

It''s worth mentioning that you''ll see this most often written in katakana, as both オス犬 and オスの犬.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5405', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%84%E7%8A%AC', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:47:02.064796Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:16.920772Z'),
(5406, 'vocabulary', '絶やす', '絶やす', 'Remember how 絶える means "to die out?" Well 絶やす is its transitive version, meaning <vocabulary>to let something die out</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to put an end to something</vocabulary>. 

絶やす is used when you stop something from continuing, like ending a family line, letting a tradition fade, or allowing a fire to go out. You know this because if you''re putting an end to something, you probably don''t like it, so you say "<reading>YAASS</reading>" (やす) with a big evil grin as you let it die out.

Note that 絶やす often appears in its negative form 絶やさない, because it''s commonly used to describe the intent not to let something precious or important come to an end.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5406', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B6%E3%82%84%E3%81%99', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:47:07.966094Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:53:59.551858Z'),
(5407, 'vocabulary', '緊張する', '緊張する', '緊張 means "nervousness" or "tension." So, the verb version is <vocabulary>to feel nervous</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to get tense</vocabulary>.

People often use 緊張する when they''re about to do something important, like giving a speech or taking a test. In formal contexts it can also be used to talk about muscles physically getting tense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5407', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%8A%E5%BC%B5%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:47:14.048541Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:16.975582Z'),
(5408, 'vocabulary', '融合', '融合', '<kanji>Dissolve</kanji> and then <kanji>join</kanji> together again to create a reaction, a <vocabulary>fusion</vocabulary> reaction.

融合 is a relatively formal word that describes two or more things blending together to become a unified whole. It can describe fusion in the scientific sense (like nuclear fusion) or in a more figurative sense, such as the fusion of ideas, cultures, styles, or technologies.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5408', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9E%8D%E5%90%88', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:47:19.920822Z', NULL, '2025-07-28T21:15:29.660686Z'),
(5409, 'vocabulary', '融資', '融資', 'Someone <kanji>dissolve</kanji>s their <kanji>resources</kanji> in order to more easily give them to you, because they are <vocabulary>financing</vocabulary> you with a <vocabulary>loan</vocabulary>. This someone is an investor, and they want to see your business succeed!

融資 is a formal word for <vocabulary>lending money</vocabulary>. It''s used when a financial institution lends money to an individual or a business to help with things like starting a company, buying property, or handling expenses.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5409', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9E%8D%E8%B3%87', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:47:52.265586Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:17.028287Z'),
(5410, 'vocabulary', '離れる', '離れる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an <ja>う</ja> sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>detach</kanji>, but what happens when something detaches? It causes it <vocabulary>to become separated</vocabulary>, aka <vocabulary>to separate</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to move away</vocabulary> from something else.

You use 離れる when two things that were together separate and become distant from one another. This can refer to a physical detachment, but it can also describe someone moving away from a place, group, or role, or even people drifting apart emotionally. You also use it to describe places that are far from each other.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5410', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%A2%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:48:31.276513Z', NULL, '2025-11-10T19:00:02.830873Z'),
(5411, 'vocabulary', '離婚', '離婚', '<kanji>Detach</kanji> from your <kanji>marriage</kanji>. This is a <vocabulary>divorce</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5411', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%A2%E5%A9%9A', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:52:03.480894Z', NULL, '2025-07-28T21:10:14.088631Z'),
(5412, 'vocabulary', '分離', '分離', 'When you <kanji>divide</kanji> and <kanji>detach</kanji> things from each other, you''re creating <vocabulary>separation</vocabulary> between them. Makes sense, right?

分離 is a word you might hear in science when discussing how different substances, like oil and water, don''t mix and separate from each other.

Additionally, 分離 can also be a formal term used to describe separating groups of people, often in discussions about serious topics like social <vocabulary>segregation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5412', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%86%E9%9B%A2', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:52:25.983193Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:17.115293Z'),
(5413, 'vocabulary', '印鑑', '印鑑', 'A <kanji>seal</kanji> that''s <kanji>model</kanji>ed after you is a useful <vocabulary>seal</vocabulary> indeed — it''s your very own <vocabulary>personal seal</vocabulary>!

印鑑 is a small stamp with your name carved into it. In Japan, people use it on official documents instead of signing their name. It''s like your legally recognized "signature stamp," and it''s something most adults in Japan need to have.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5413', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B0%E9%91%91', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:52:31.652806Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T23:32:31.454205Z'),
(5414, 'vocabulary', '壊れる', '壊れる', '壊す is "to break something." 壊れる is when something just gets broken or is broken, and you''re not doing the breaking. This is <vocabulary>to be broken</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5414', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%8A%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:52:37.558301Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:20:36.651776Z'),
(5415, 'vocabulary', '壊れ物', '壊れ物', 'A <kanji>thing</kanji> that could <kanji>break</kanji> easily, is a <vocabulary>fragile thing</vocabulary>. Be careful with things like this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5415', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%8A%E3%82%8C%E7%89%A9', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:52:44.203331Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:20:36.651776Z'),
(5416, 'vocabulary', '既に', '既に', 'Something that <kanji>previously</kanji> happened has <vocabulary>already</vocabulary> happened.

既に is relatively formal. Use it as a neutral way to describe actions or states that are already complete. By comparison, もう is more casual and might sometimes carry a sense of surprise or regret that something has happened sooner than expected. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5416', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A2%E3%81%AB', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:52:50.881954Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:59:24.484003Z'),
(5417, 'vocabulary', '既存', '既存', 'Something that <kanji>previously</kanji> <kanji>exist</kanji>ed is probably still <vocabulary>existing</vocabulary> now.

既存 is a relatively formal word that is often used in official contexts to talk about things that are already in place, like existing systems, facilities, or ideas.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5417', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A2%E5%AD%98', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:52:56.639369Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:54:31.629382Z'),
(5418, 'vocabulary', '既決', '既決', 'It was <kanji>previously</kanji> <kanji>decide</kanji>d, which means it''s been <vocabulary>decided</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>settled</vocabulary>.

既決 is a formal word that means something has already been decided or settled. You''ll come across it in official settings, like when a company meeting wraps up and certain topics are marked as 既決.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5418', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A2%E6%B1%BA', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:53:08.90433Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:09:27.747622Z'),
(5419, 'vocabulary', '普通', '普通', 'You <kanji>pass through</kanji> like completely <kanji>normal</kanji>. You are <vocabulary>normal</vocabulary>. You do things <vocabulary>normally</vocabulary>, normally.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5419', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%AE%E9%80%9A', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:53:16.279868Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:17.325253Z'),
(5420, 'vocabulary', '普段', '普段', 'The <kanji>normal</kanji> <kanji>steps</kanji> are the ones that are totally <vocabulary>usual</vocabulary>. They''re the ones you <vocabulary>usually</vocabulary> do, unlike those weird not usual steps.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5420', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%AE%E6%AE%B5', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:53:25.214607Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:17.350071Z'),
(5421, 'vocabulary', '編む', '編む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>knit</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to knit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5421', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A8%E3%82%80', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:53:38.603893Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:17.379147Z'),
(5422, 'vocabulary', '編集', '編集', '<kanji>Knit</kanji> and <kanji>gather</kanji> everything back together, which you knit. When you do this to fix things and bring it back together, this is an <vocabulary>edit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5422', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A8%E9%9B%86', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:53:46.671468Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:17.402882Z'),
(5423, 'vocabulary', '編者', '編者', 'A <kanji>knit</kanji> <kanji>someone</kanji> is one who knits words together and makes them better. This someone is an <vocabulary>editor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5423', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A8%E8%80%85', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:54:08.310682Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(5424, 'vocabulary', '外務大臣', '外務大臣', 'An <kanji>outside</kanji> <kanji>task</kanji>s <kanji>minister</kanji> (大臣) is someone who is tasked with dealing with things that are outside the country. This is the <vocabulary>minister of foreign affairs</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5424', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E5%8B%99%E5%A4%A7%E8%87%A3', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:54:13.753188Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(5425, 'vocabulary', '総理大臣', '総理大臣', 'The <kanji>whole</kanji> <kanji>reason</kanji> our country is still running is because of our "minister" (大臣). Which minister? The <vocabulary>prime minister</vocabulary>, of course. Without our prime minister, we''d all be doomed I say. DOOMED!

Unlike 首相, which can be used for any prime minister, this word is used only for the <vocabulary>Japanese prime minister</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5425', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%8F%E7%90%86%E5%A4%A7%E8%87%A3', 30, 1, '2013-03-12T22:54:26.08236Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(5426, 'vocabulary', '華やか', '華やか', 'The kanji means <kanji>showy</kanji>, and this word is basically a super positive version of that — it means <vocabulary>bright and showy</vocabulary>. 

華やか describes the kind of elegant beauty that brightens a room. A little showy, maybe, but in an uplifting way rather than just gaudy. It can describe <vocabulary>gorgeous</vocabulary> clothes, like 華やかなドレス (a gorgeous dress) or bright, <vocabulary>festive</vocabulary> colors and atmospheres.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5426', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8F%AF%E3%82%84%E3%81%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:54:45.059198Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:17.034974Z'),
(5427, 'vocabulary', '中華', '中華', '中国 is China, right? That''s what this 中 represents. And, of course the Chinese are known for being super showy, which is why you have <kanji>showy</kanji> there. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5427', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E8%8F%AF', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:55:06.255336Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:17.542107Z'),
(5428, 'vocabulary', '地下街', '地下街', 'You''re "underground" (地下) in the <kanji>streets</kanji>, but don''t worry, you''re not in some post-apocalyptic sci-fi novel — most of the underground streets and areas in Japan are just <vocabulary>underground shopping mall</vocabulary>s.

地下街, or <vocabulary>underground shopping center</vocabulary>s, are common in big cities in Japan, especially near train stations, where you''ll find lots of shops, restaurants, and walkways below street level.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5428', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E4%B8%8B%E8%A1%97', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:55:13.29508Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:31:32.191699Z'),
(5429, 'vocabulary', '豪華', '豪華', 'Oh wow, that is <kanji>exceptional</kanji>ly <kanji>showy</kanji>! Whatever that thing is, it is <vocabulary>luxurious</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>splendid</vocabulary> and I want one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5429', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B1%AA%E8%8F%AF', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:55:18.922222Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:04:30.445599Z'),
(5430, 'vocabulary', '監視', '監視', 'You <kanji>oversee</kanji> and <kanji>look at</kanji> all that is in your domain. You are conducting <vocabulary>surveillance</vocabulary>.

監視 is the active <vocabulary>observation</vocabulary> of someone or something to ensure safety or compliance, or to detect suspicious activity. It can also refer to the people who carry out this <vocabulary>monitoring</vocabulary>, either directly or through the use of things like cameras.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5430', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%A3%E8%A6%96', 29, 1, '2013-03-12T22:55:27.072757Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:26:59.946346Z'),
(5431, 'vocabulary', '監督', '監督', 'The one who <kanji>oversee</kanji>s and <kanji>direct</kanji>s is the one who gives <vocabulary>supervision</vocabulary>. 

監督 refers to the act of supervising or to the person who supervises, such as the <vocabulary>head coach</vocabulary> of a sports team or a movie <vocabulary>director</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5431', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%A3%E7%9D%A3', 29, 1, '2013-03-12T22:56:10.377711Z', NULL, '2026-01-01T17:02:29.820649Z'),
(5432, 'vocabulary', '鑑定', '鑑定', 'When a <kanji>model</kanji> human <kanji>determine</kanji>s something, that''s an <vocabulary>appraisal</vocabulary> you should probably pay attention to. This isn''t just some random nobody off the street — they''re a model determiner, so they''re really giving you an <vocabulary>expert judgment</vocabulary>.

鑑定 often refers to evaluating the authenticity or value of things like art, antiques, or real estate. It''s also used in contexts like fingerprint analysis, or in legal settings where court-appointed experts provide technical evaluations to help judges make decisions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5432', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%91%91%E5%AE%9A', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:56:24.831641Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(5433, 'vocabulary', '除く', '除く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>exclude</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to exclude</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5433', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%A4%E3%81%8F', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:56:33.279691Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:17.713353Z'),
(5434, 'vocabulary', '掃除', '掃除', '<kanji>Sweep</kanji> and <kanji>exclude</kanji> all that dirt from the floor, please. When you do this a lot, it becomes <vocabulary>cleaning</vocabulary>.

掃除 is a common word you''ll hear often in daily life, and it can mean anything from quickly vacuuming your room to doing a full-on deep clean of your entire house.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5434', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%83%E9%99%A4', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:56:49.789801Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:41:39.325074Z'),
(5435, 'vocabulary', '除外', '除外', '<kanji>Exclude</kanji> this one and put it <kanji>outside</kanji> what we''re considering. This is the <vocabulary>exception</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5435', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%A4%E5%A4%96', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:57:14.732144Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(5436, 'vocabulary', '尋ねる', '尋ねる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>inquire</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to inquire</vocabulary>, or simply <vocabulary>to ask</vocabulary>.

尋ねる is the polite, slightly formal word you use when you directly ask someone for information you don''t know — like directions or details.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5436', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8B%E3%81%AD%E3%82%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:57:21.85908Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:34:09.930091Z'),
(5437, 'vocabulary', '尋問', '尋問', 'When you <kanji>inquire</kanji> about a <kanji>problem</kanji>, you tend to do <vocabulary>interrogation</vocabulary>-style <vocabulary>questioning</vocabulary> to get to the bottom of it. Why is it like this? What happened? Is it bigger than a breadbox?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5437', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8B%E5%95%8F', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:57:27.964155Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:34:09.930091Z'),
(5438, 'vocabulary', '幾ら', '幾ら', 'This has the same meaning as the kanji, specifically the <vocabulary>how much</vocabulary> meaning. To remember this, just think about how <reading>Ra</reading>, the Egyptian God, is always asking "<vocabulary>How much</vocabulary> will it cost to build this pyramid?" Ugh, too much, let''s hire some slaves.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5438', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BE%E3%82%89', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:57:34.152179Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:17.899442Z'),
(5439, 'vocabulary', '幾つ', '幾つ', 'This has the same meaning as the kanji, specifically the <vocabulary>how many</vocabulary> meaning. You know this because you ask "how many" and they answer "つ." This can also be used to ask <vocabulary>how old</vocabulary> someone is, but generally only for children''s ages.

This word is usually written in kana in modern writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5439', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BE%E3%81%A4', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:57:46.079632Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:17.923136Z'),
(5440, 'vocabulary', '幾何学', '幾何学', '<kanji>How many</kanji>? <kanji>What</kanji>? In this area of <kanji>study</kanji>, you ask these questions a lot. This area of study is <vocabulary>geometry</vocabulary>, because you have to ask how many sides and how much the area is or something (can you tell I don''t remember any maths?).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5440', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BE%E4%BD%95%E5%AD%A6', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:57:53.924363Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(5441, 'vocabulary', '廊下', '廊下', 'A <kanji>corridor</kanji> is <kanji>below</kanji>. What do you call a corridor that''s below? Well, really it''s still just a <vocabulary>corridor</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>hallway</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5441', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%8A%E4%B8%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:58:00.325723Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(5442, 'vocabulary', '画廊', '画廊', 'An <kanji>image</kanji> <kanji>corridor</kanji> is a corridor in which there are images hanging on the wall. This is a <vocabulary>picture gallery</vocabulary>.

Note that 画廊 generally refers to a physical <vocabulary>art gallery</vocabulary>, where artworks are exhibited for in-person viewing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5442', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%BB%E5%BB%8A', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:58:36.570153Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:18.006541Z'),
(5443, 'vocabulary', '掃く', '掃く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>sweep</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to sweep</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5443', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%83%E3%81%8F', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:58:41.852386Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:05:40.288005Z'),
(5444, 'vocabulary', '泥', '泥', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5444', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A5', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:59:40.788431Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.694516Z'),
(5445, 'vocabulary', '泥水', '泥水', '<kanji>Mud</kanji> <kanji>water</kanji> is <vocabulary>muddy water</vocabulary>. Don''t step in it, if you can help it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5445', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A5%E6%B0%B4', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T22:59:47.366787Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.694516Z'),
(5446, 'vocabulary', '棒', '棒', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

棒 refers to anything that''s long, thin, and solid, whether it''s a <vocabulary>pole</vocabulary>, a <vocabulary>rod</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>stick</vocabulary>. It can be used for a wide variety of objects, from tree branches to popsicle sticks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5446', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A3%92', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:00:25.455575Z', NULL, '2025-08-18T03:16:41.070675Z'),
(5447, 'vocabulary', '驚く', '驚く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>surprised</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be surprised</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5447', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A9%9A%E3%81%8F', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:00:30.88151Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:18.153248Z'),
(5448, 'vocabulary', '驚嘆', '驚嘆', 'I''m so <kanji>surprised</kanji> that it caused me to <kanji>sigh</kanji>. This is like when you see something super impressive and it surprises the heck out of you. Then, you breathe out a sigh because of your great <vocabulary>amazement</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>admiration</vocabulary> for what you just saw.

驚嘆 is a formal word you''ll mostly see in writing to describe something truly awe-inspiring, or the state of being amazed by an impressive thing. It''s about more than just surprise — it describes the deep admiration you feel when marveling at great talent, skill, or beauty.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5448', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A9%9A%E5%98%86', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:00:44.144059Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:34:02.823227Z'),
(5449, 'vocabulary', '嘆く', '嘆く', 'When you <kanji>sigh</kanji>, you feel a certain type of emotion. A type that makes you feel sad and causes you <vocabulary>to lament</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to grieve</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5449', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%98%86%E3%81%8F', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:00:50.434776Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:18.216259Z'),
(5450, 'vocabulary', '嘆息', '嘆息', 'A <kanji>sigh</kanji> <kanji>breath</kanji> is a <vocabulary>sigh</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5450', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%98%86%E6%81%AF', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:02:28.457051Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:18.244663Z'),
(5451, 'vocabulary', '感嘆符', '感嘆符', 'A <kanji>feeling</kanji> where you <kanji>sigh</kanji> is one in which you''re surprised by something, and can''t believe it, so you sigh. Ugh, *sigh*. What <kanji>token</kanji> represents this in writing? An <vocabulary>exclamation mark</vocabulary>!!!!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5451', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E5%98%86%E7%AC%A6', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:02:39.283992Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(5452, 'vocabulary', '倉庫', '倉庫', 'A <kanji>storage</kanji> <kanji>warehouse</kanji> is some kind of <vocabulary>warehouse</vocabulary>, or some kind of <vocabulary>storehouse</vocabulary>.

Curious about the difference between 倉庫 and 庫? While 庫 refers to traditional Japanese storehouses, 倉庫 is used for modern warehouses and storehouses (both the commercial and personal kind).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5452', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%89%E5%BA%AB', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:02:45.458384Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:18.300984Z'),
(5453, 'vocabulary', '倉', '倉', 'The kanji means <kanji>storehouse</kanji>, and the vocab is also <vocabulary>storehouse</vocabulary>!

Wondering what the difference between 倉 and 庫 is? Long ago, 倉 were places to store grain, while 庫 used to be associated with places to store vehicles, as the kanji composition suggests. These days, they''re both used for general storage, but also refer to places to store goods and merchandise. Between the two, 倉 is much more commonly used.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5453', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%89', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:03:27.370108Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:18.331297Z'),
(5454, 'vocabulary', '子孫', '子孫', 'A <kanji>child</kanji> who is the <kanji>grandchild</kanji> of someone else is a <vocabulary>descendant</vocabulary> of that person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5454', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%90%E5%AD%AB', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:03:35.440634Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:18.358874Z'),
(5455, 'vocabulary', '孫', '孫', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5455', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%AB', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:03:41.403704Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:18.387194Z'),
(5456, 'vocabulary', '巣', '巣', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

巣 refers to all kinds of animal homes — a bird''s <vocabulary>nest</vocabulary>, a spider''s web, a mouse''s burrow, or even a beehive. Sometimes, it can be used figuratively to describe places where people hole up, whether that be a cozy home for lovers or a shady hideout for gangs…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5456', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A3', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:03:47.863209Z', NULL, '2025-07-28T21:28:32.959304Z'),
(5457, 'vocabulary', '巣立ち', '巣立ち', 'When you live in a nest (巣), there''s only one reason for you to stand up (立つ), and that''s to <vocabulary>leave the nest</vocabulary>. Your mother may be sad about this, but everyone needs to <vocabulary>become independent</vocabulary> someday.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5457', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A3%E7%AB%8B%E3%81%A1', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:03:54.183888Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T22:05:59.130515Z'),
(5458, 'vocabulary', 'ハチの巣', 'ハチの巣', 'ハチ is a bee. Buzz buzz. Just imagine "eight bees" combining into one bee, making it a "hachi." Then, there''s a <kanji>nest</kanji>. What is a nest for bees? A <vocabulary>beehive</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5458', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%8F%E3%83%81%E3%81%AE%E5%B7%A3', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:04:00.641759Z', NULL, '2025-07-28T21:25:54.78315Z'),
(5459, 'vocabulary', 'クモの巣', 'クモの巣', 'クモ is a spider. Add a <kanji>nest</kanji> to it, and you have a spider nest. What do spiders call their nests? They call it a <vocabulary>spiderweb</vocabulary> (that is, if they can talk).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5459', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%AF%E3%83%A2%E3%81%AE%E5%B7%A3', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:04:06.301733Z', NULL, '2025-07-28T21:25:54.78315Z'),
(5460, 'vocabulary', '帯', '帯', 'The kanji and the word are the same, except that this isn''t just any type of <kanji>belt</kanji>. It''s an <vocabulary>obi</vocabulary>, a wide cloth <vocabulary>belt</vocabulary> worn with traditional clothes like kimono and yukata.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5460', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%AF', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:04:12.262373Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:18.54235Z'),
(5461, 'vocabulary', '地帯', '地帯', 'This <kanji>earth</kanji> <kanji>belt</kanji> is the area within the belt, which holds this area of earth in. It is separate from the rest of the earth, making it its own <vocabulary>zone</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>district</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5461', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E5%B8%AF', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:04:28.044465Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:18.568843Z'),
(5462, 'vocabulary', '直径', '直径', 'Draw a <kanji>straight</kanji> line across the <kanji>diameter</kanji> of a circle, then measure it. This gives you the <vocabulary>diameter</vocabulary>. Whoa, look out everyone — we''ve got a geometry whiz in the room!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5462', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B4%E5%BE%84', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:04:33.467918Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:12:57.344176Z'),
(5463, 'vocabulary', '半径', '半径', '<kanji>Half</kanji> a <kanji>diameter</kanji> is a <vocabulary>radius</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5463', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%8A%E5%BE%84', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:04:38.987938Z', NULL, '2025-12-11T20:12:42.646308Z'),
(5464, 'vocabulary', '経路', '経路', 'If you <kanji>pass through</kanji> the <kanji>road</kanji>s, you are figuring out which roads to use, when to use them, and essentially coming up with the <vocabulary>route</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>course</vocabulary> to take to get to wherever you''re going.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5464', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%8C%E8%B7%AF', 20, 1, '2013-03-12T23:04:45.481414Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:55:20.905911Z'),
(5465, 'vocabulary', '救急車', '救急車', '<kanji>Rescue</kanji> in a <kanji>hurry</kanji> using this <kanji>car</kanji>. What kinds of cars can do this? How about an <vocabulary>ambulance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5465', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%91%E6%80%A5%E8%BB%8A', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:04:50.341185Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:57:49.848656Z'),
(5466, 'vocabulary', '救う', '救う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>rescue</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to rescue</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5466', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%91%E3%81%86', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:04:55.190327Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:18.699944Z'),
(5467, 'vocabulary', '散る', '散る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>scatter</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be scattered</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to get scattered</vocabulary>. 

散る is an intransitive verb, meaning something is scattering on its own. It''s commonly used to describe when things like petals or leaves fall, fluttering down and scattering across the ground. It can also be used more abstractly, such as a crowd suddenly dispersing, your concentration breaking, your dreams falling apart, or even a soldier "falling" in battle.

You know 散る is intransitive because you rue (る) your decision not to go see the cherry blossoms when they were in bloom; now the petals have all scattered to the wind and you''ve missed your chance. Darn.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5467', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%A3%E3%82%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:05:13.678642Z', NULL, '2025-08-28T13:31:49.763912Z'),
(5468, 'vocabulary', '散歩', '散歩', '<kanji>Scatter</kanji> around the ground and <kanji>walk</kanji>. When you walk in a scatter all around, you''re going for <vocabulary>a walk</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>a stroll</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5468', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%A3%E6%AD%A9', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:05:18.597585Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:17.226732Z'),
(5469, 'vocabulary', '解散', '解散', '<kanji>Untie</kanji> and <kanji>scatter</kanji> everything. This will cause the <vocabulary>dissolution</vocabulary> the <vocabulary>breakup</vocabulary>, and indeed the <vocabulary>breaking up</vocabulary> of whatever is being untied then scattered.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5469', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A3%E6%95%A3', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:05:36.594563Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T20:23:40.067262Z'),
(5470, 'vocabulary', '粉', '粉', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Can also mean <vocabulary>flour</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5470', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%89', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:05:47.747728Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:17.233212Z'),
(5471, 'vocabulary', '麦粉-5471', '麦粉', '<kanji>Wheat</kanji> <kanji>powder</kanji> is <vocabulary>wheat flour</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5471', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BA%A6%E7%B2%89-5471', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:05:53.240154Z', '2018-11-30T20:36:23.67541Z', '2026-02-26T05:21:06.048836Z'),
(5472, 'vocabulary', '花粉症', '花粉症', 'The <kanji>flower</kanji> <kanji>powder</kanji> <kanji>symptom</kanji>s are when you start sneezing from flower powder, aka pollen that comes from all sorts of flowers, which is pretty much all plants. This word, however, refers to the most common version of this sickness, which is <vocabulary>hay fever</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5472', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B1%E7%B2%89%E7%97%87', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:06:07.380796Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:20:54.638732Z'),
(5473, 'vocabulary', '粉状', '粉状', 'Something that is in a <kanji>powder</kanji> <kanji>condition</kanji> is something that has been <vocabulary>powdered</vocabulary>. Puff!

粉状 is a relatively formal word that is used to describe something being in powder form.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5473', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%89%E7%8A%B6', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:06:12.640215Z', NULL, '2025-08-07T03:55:07.311341Z'),
(5474, 'vocabulary', '人脈', '人脈', 'Your <kanji>person</kanji> <kanji>vein</kanji>s are all the veins you have going out to all the different people you know. These are your <vocabulary>personal connections</vocabulary>, your <vocabulary>network</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5474', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E8%84%88', 32, 1, '2013-03-12T23:06:18.331288Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(5475, 'vocabulary', '山脈', '山脈', 'A <kanji>mountain</kanji> <kanji>vein</kanji> is a vein that extends across the land, and is covered in mountains. This refers to a <vocabulary>mountain range</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5475', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%B1%E8%84%88', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:06:25.337118Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:18.978596Z'),
(5476, 'vocabulary', '血脈', '血脈', '<kanji>Blood</kanji> flows through your <kanji>vein</kanji>s, carrying the stories of your <vocabulary>lineage</vocabulary>, your <vocabulary>bloodline</vocabulary>.

Note that this word is quite formal and literary.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5476', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%80%E8%84%88', 57, 1, '2013-03-12T23:06:32.229319Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.000877Z'),
(5477, 'vocabulary', '野菜', '野菜', 'A <kanji>field</kanji> <kanji>vegetable</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>vegetable</vocabulary>, whether it comes from the field or not.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5477', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8E%E8%8F%9C', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:06:39.812029Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.029965Z'),
(5478, 'vocabulary', '菜食', '菜食', 'If you eat <kanji>vegetable</kanji> <kanji>food</kanji> you are on a <vocabulary>vegetarian diet</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5478', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8F%9C%E9%A3%9F', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:06:44.521875Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.061121Z'),
(5479, 'vocabulary', '貨物', '貨物', '<kanji>Freight</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji>s is <vocabulary>freight</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>cargo</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5479', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A8%E7%89%A9', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:06:52.588914Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(5480, 'vocabulary', '通貨', '通貨', 'The thing that will <kanji>pass through</kanji> <kanji>freight</kanji> is <vocabulary>currency</vocabulary>. Why? Because when you want freight to be your own, you have to put money through it and to the person who owns it. When you do that, it becomes yours. Use that currency!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5480', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%9A%E8%B2%A8', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:07:05.489295Z', NULL, '2025-09-22T00:08:15.307425Z'),
(5481, 'vocabulary', '大陸', '大陸', 'A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>land</kanji> is the biggest types of land on this earth that there are. That is a <vocabulary>continent</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5481', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E9%99%B8', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:07:10.84502Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(5482, 'vocabulary', '陸軍', '陸軍', 'The <kanji>land</kanji> <kanji>army</kanji> is the <vocabulary>land army</vocabulary>, also known as the plain old <vocabulary>army</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5482', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%B8%E8%BB%8D', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:07:26.156806Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.177335Z'),
(5483, 'vocabulary', '似る', '似る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>resemble</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to resemble</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to look like</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5483', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%BC%E3%82%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:07:32.533996Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.204523Z'),
(5484, 'vocabulary', '真似', '真似', 'You''re taking <kanji>reality</kanji> and trying to <kanji>resemble</kanji> it. To do this, you have to do an <vocabulary>imitation</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>mimicry</vocabulary>, or an <vocabulary>impersonation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5484', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%9F%E4%BC%BC', 33, 1, '2013-03-12T23:07:44.057166Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T20:04:39.445204Z'),
(5485, 'vocabulary', '平均', '平均', 'Something that''s <kanji>flat</kanji> and <kanji>equal</kanji> to everything around it, all those flat things around it, are <vocabulary>average</vocabulary>. They''re the <vocabulary>mean</vocabulary> (same as average).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5485', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B3%E5%9D%87', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:07:56.963383Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.243208Z'),
(5486, 'vocabulary', '均等', '均等', 'This is <kanji>equal</kanji> and that is <kanji>equal</kanji>. They''re both the same, amazing! They carry <vocabulary>equality</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>uniformity</vocabulary>,  and <vocabulary>evenness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5486', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9D%87%E7%AD%89', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:08:02.364475Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.269287Z'),
(5487, 'vocabulary', '均整', '均整', 'Things are <kanji>equal</kanji> in how they''re <kanji>arrange</kanji>d. They have perfect <vocabulary>symmetry</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>balance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5487', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9D%87%E6%95%B4', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:08:07.180449Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.300568Z'),
(5488, 'vocabulary', '墓', '墓', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

墓 means <vocabulary>grave</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>tomb</vocabulary>. Since you want to be respectful when talking about someone''s eternal resting place, it''s common to add the honorific お and say お墓.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5488', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%93', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:08:13.953347Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.329856Z'),
(5489, 'vocabulary', '墓地', '墓地', 'A <kanji>grave</kanji> <kanji>ground</kanji> is an area with lots of graves in the ground. This is a <vocabulary>cemetery</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>graveyard</vocabulary>.

墓地 is the standard word for a designated and organized cemetery.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5489', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%93%E5%9C%B0', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:08:22.087002Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.359457Z'),
(5490, 'vocabulary', '富士山', '富士山', 'Did you know that the <kanji>mountain</kanji> that is <kanji>rich</kanji> in <kanji>samurai</kanji> happens to be Japan''s most famous mountain? It makes sense considering samurai are probably Japan''s most famous thing as well. Combine the two most famous things and you have <vocabulary>Mt Fuji</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>Mount Fuji</vocabulary> if you''d rather write it that way).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5490', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%8C%E5%A3%AB%E5%B1%B1', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:08:29.25331Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.38733Z'),
(5491, 'vocabulary', '富む', '富む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>rich</kanji> so the verb vocab version would be <vocabulary>to be rich in</vocabulary>.

富む is a formal word that''s commonly used in writing. While it can refer to material wealth, it''s more commonly used figuratively to describe an abundance of a certain quality, a place rich in nature or specific resources, or emotional richness or fulfillment.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5491', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%8C%E3%82%80', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:08:35.073119Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.410655Z'),
(5492, 'vocabulary', '富', '富', 'The kanji and the word are almost exactly the same. Someone who is <kanji>rich</kanji> has <vocabulary>wealth</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>riches</vocabulary>, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5492', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%8C', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:08:41.055319Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:17.341795Z'),
(5493, 'vocabulary', '富裕', '富裕', 'If you''re <kanji>rich</kanji> and <kanji>abundant</kanji>, you are super <vocabulary>wealthy</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>affluent</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>rich</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5493', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%8C%E8%A3%95', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:08:48.020671Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:25:39.169801Z'),
(5494, 'vocabulary', '道徳', '道徳', 'The <kanji>way</kanji> of <kanji>virtue</kanji> is the way you take if you have <vocabulary>morals</vocabulary>. Hopefully you have some.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5494', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%93%E5%BE%B3', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:08:54.6656Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.494544Z'),
(5495, 'vocabulary', '徳川', '徳川', 'This is one of those words that makes more sense when you look at the reading. The "Virtue River" isn''t some virtuous river, it''s in fact the <vocabulary>Tokugawa</vocabulary> family name, made famous by the Tokugawa shogunate. They ran Japan for a while, you know? ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5495', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%B3%E5%B7%9D', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:09:02.059468Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.522446Z'),
(5496, 'vocabulary', '徳島県', '徳島県', 'The <kanji>virtue</kanji> <kanji>island</kanji> <kanji>prefecture</kanji> is <vocabulary>Tokushima prefecture</vocabulary>. The reading will give away the meaning of this one, so long as you knew "prefecture."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5496', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%B3%E5%B3%B6%E7%9C%8C', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:09:10.067359Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:25:07.757919Z'),
(5497, 'vocabulary', '探す', '探す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>look for</kanji> and the verb version of the kanji is <vocabulary>to look for</vocabulary>.


Curious about the difference between 捜す and 探す? While there is some overlap between the two, 捜す is typically used in official, thorough searches for something or someone that is thought to concretely exist and has disappeared or cannot be easily found. In contrast, 探す can also be used for personal searches for things that one desires or is looking for.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5497', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A2%E3%81%99', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:09:21.069108Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.58427Z'),
(5498, 'vocabulary', '探究', '探究', '<kanji>Look for</kanji> something. <kanji>Research</kanji> answers. This is an <vocabulary>academic inquiry</vocabulary>, or in other words, <vocabulary>research</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5498', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A2%E7%A9%B6', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:09:34.37106Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:11:35.013012Z'),
(5499, 'vocabulary', '探偵', '探偵', 'You <kanji>look for</kanji> things and you <kanji>spy</kanji> on people. Your job isn''t as glamorous as it sounds. You''re a <vocabulary>private detective</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5499', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A2%E5%81%B5', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:09:50.927641Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.639041Z'),
(5500, 'vocabulary', '内偵', '内偵', 'When you go <kanji>inside</kanji> somewhere to quietly <kanji>spy</kanji> on someone, you''re doing a <vocabulary>secret investigation</vocabulary>.

内偵 is a formal word for investigations where the target doesn''t know they''re being investigated. It can mean an <vocabulary>undercover investigation</vocabulary> by police, or it can be a secret check on a business partner, a new hire, or even a suspicious spouse by companies or detectives.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5500', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%85%E5%81%B5', 31, 1, '2013-03-12T23:09:55.777516Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T06:06:52.383112Z'),
(5501, 'vocabulary', '美味しい', '美味しい', 'Something with a <kanji>beauty</kanji> <kanji>taste</kanji> is something that is really <vocabulary>tasty</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>delicious</vocabulary>. Mmmm, yum!

While 美味しい mostly describes tasty food, you may also occasionally see it being used for a sweet deal or situation that benefits you. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5501', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%8E%E5%91%B3%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 15, 1, '2013-03-13T19:00:03.225422Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.658953Z'),
(5502, 'vocabulary', '綺麗', '綺麗', 'Things that are <kanji>beautiful</kanji> and <kanji>lovely</kanji> are very <vocabulary>beautiful</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>pretty</vocabulary>, and often <vocabulary>clean</vocabulary>, too. You can call someone 綺麗. You can also mention how clean something is with 綺麗. Gives you some insight as to what "beauty" is in Japan, I suppose. Take your baths, folks.

Wondering what the difference is between 綺麗 and 美しい? 美しい is more dramatic, so it implies you’re really blown away by something’s (or someone’s) beauty. 綺麗, on the other hand, is a more everyday word that covers anything that’s beautiful, pretty, or even just clean. In other words, the bar is a bit lower for 綺麗!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5502', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%BA%E9%BA%97', 28, 1, '2013-03-15T17:27:32.548055Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.724384Z'),
(5503, 'vocabulary', '身', '身', 'The kanji and the vocab word are the same, so this means <vocabulary>body</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>oneself</vocabulary>. It also refers to <vocabulary>animal flesh</vocabulary> sometimes, meaning the non-bone edible parts of animals, including seafood. It''s the "mi" in sashimi!

Wondering about the difference between 体 and 身? Basically, 体 is for the physical human (or animal) body as a whole, and is often used in relation to its condition or appearance. 身 can refer to a physical human body too, but unlike 体, it''s also used to refer to the self as an individual and, by extension, to one''s social standing or circumstances.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5503', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BA%AB', 8, 1, '2013-03-15T18:53:32.941224Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:27:09.215149Z'),
(5504, 'vocabulary', '移る', '移る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>shift</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be shifted</vocabulary>. You know that this is the one where something is shifted (and you''re not doing the shifting), because it''s really <reading>ru</reading>de (る) to be shifted, and you don''t like it when people do it to you (because it happens quite a lot, strangely).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5504', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%BB%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-03-15T19:04:11.556054Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:17.391427Z'),
(5505, 'vocabulary', '結婚式', '結婚式', 'If 結婚 is <kanji>marriage</kanji>, then a marriage <kanji>ceremony</kanji> is a <vocabulary>marriage ceremony</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>wedding</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5505', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%90%E5%A9%9A%E5%BC%8F', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:21:54.60472Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T22:31:56.723758Z'),
(5506, 'vocabulary', '押しボタン', '押しボタン', 'If 押し is <kanji>push</kanji>, and a ボタン is a button, then you have yourself a <vocabulary>push button</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5506', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%BC%E3%81%97%E3%83%9C%E3%82%BF%E3%83%B3', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:22:00.89135Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.805195Z'),
(5507, 'vocabulary', '押入れ', '押入れ', 'You <kanji>push</kanji> things to make them <kanji>enter</kanji> the <vocabulary>closet</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>cupboard</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5507', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%BC%E5%85%A5%E3%82%8C', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:22:06.267306Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.816441Z'),
(5508, 'vocabulary', '出版する', '出版する', 'If 出版 is <kanji>publishing</kanji>, then the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to publish</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5508', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E7%89%88%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:23:12.742386Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:11:10.895336Z'),
(5509, 'vocabulary', '出版社', '出版社', 'If 出版 means <kanji>publishing</kanji>, then a <kanji>company</kanji> that does said publishing is a <vocabulary>publishing company</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5509', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E7%89%88%E7%A4%BE', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:23:18.83333Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:11:10.895336Z'),
(5510, 'vocabulary', '貧乏人', '貧乏人', '貧乏 is <kanji>poor</kanji>, so a poor <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>poor person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5510', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A7%E4%B9%8F%E4%BA%BA', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:23:26.207601Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(5511, 'vocabulary', '冷える', '冷える', 'If 冷やす is "to chill something," then this would be the version where something is just chilled. You''re not doing the action of chilling anything, it''s just that way. You know this because if you get involved, there''s always some kind of chilling <reading>err</reading>or (える). So, you step back and let the fridge do its job without messing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5511', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%B7%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:23:32.081488Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.865202Z'),
(5512, 'vocabulary', '結婚する', '結婚する', '結婚 is <kanji>marriage</kanji>, so the verb version of that would be <vocabulary>to marry</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to get married</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5512', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%90%E5%A9%9A%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:23:37.917796Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.877222Z'),
(5513, 'vocabulary', '撮影禁止', '撮影禁止', '撮影 is <kanji>photography</kanji>. 禁止 is <kanji>prohibition</kanji>. So if photography is prohibited, then we can assume there''s a <vocabulary>no photos</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>no photos allowed</vocabulary> rule in place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5513', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%92%AE%E5%BD%B1%E7%A6%81%E6%AD%A2', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:23:43.616823Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:24:39.840037Z'),
(5514, 'vocabulary', '照り焼き', '照り焼き', 'You <kanji>illuminate</kanji> some food, then <kanji>bake</kanji> it. To illuminate it, you put on some shiny sauce. Sound familiar, in terms of a type of food? This is <vocabulary>teriyaki</vocabulary>, though I have to warn you it''s quite different from the teriyaki you get in the west.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5514', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%A7%E3%82%8A%E7%84%BC%E3%81%8D', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:23:49.158189Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.900641Z'),
(5515, 'vocabulary', '背中', '背中', 'The <kanji>middle</kanji> of your <kanji>back</kanji> is <vocabulary>one''s back</vocabulary> (or your <vocabulary>back</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5515', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%8C%E4%B8%AD', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:23:57.701888Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.91159Z'),
(5516, 'vocabulary', '購読する', '購読する', 'If 購読 is a "subscription", then the verb vocab version would be <vocabulary>to subscribe</vocabulary>.

Reminder: this refers specifically to paying for a subscription to reading materials, like newspapers or magazines, so avoid using it for free subscriptions or music apps!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5516', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%BC%E8%AA%AD%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:24:02.810744Z', NULL, '2025-08-25T17:05:57.402032Z'),
(5517, 'vocabulary', '迎える', '迎える', 'The kanji means <kanji>welcome</kanji>, but the verb version is <vocabulary>to pick someone up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to meet someone</vocabulary>. That''s weird. But, think of it this way: When you go to welcome someone, you are going <vocabulary>to meet someone</vocabulary> too. You have to go somewhere to wait and welcome them, which is why you are going to pick someone up or going to meet them. Something like that anyways.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5517', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%8E%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:24:20.74902Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:46:54.110797Z'),
(5518, 'vocabulary', '大騒ぎ', '大騒ぎ', 'If 騒ぐ is "to make noise," then a <kanji>big</kanji> version of this is even louder. It''s a <vocabulary>clamor</vocabulary> and an <vocabulary>uproar</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5518', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E9%A8%92%E3%81%8E', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:24:46.774871Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(5519, 'vocabulary', '序文', '序文', '<kanji>Preface</kanji> <kanji>writing</kanji> is the <vocabulary>preface</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5519', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%8F%E6%96%87', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:25:29.681953Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:19.968175Z'),
(5520, 'vocabulary', '順序', '順序', 'The <kanji>order</kanji> of things is the <kanji>preface</kanji> to the entire <vocabulary>sequence</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>steps</vocabulary> we''re planning on taking. So, just remember. The order is just the preface. It is what allows us to come up with a sequence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5520', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%86%E5%BA%8F', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:25:35.588438Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:19:51.47423Z'),
(5521, 'vocabulary', '志', '志', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5521', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%97', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:25:41.095193Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.003107Z'),
(5522, 'vocabulary', '意志', '意志', 'You have an <kanji>idea</kanji> and you have <kanji>intent</kanji> to carry it out. This is your <vocabulary>will</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>volition</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5522', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%8F%E5%BF%97', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:25:47.414088Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.015198Z'),
(5523, 'vocabulary', '志望', '志望', 'Your <kanji>intention</kanji> for your <kanji>hope</kanji> is what you want. It''s not just a hope. You have intention behind it. This is your <vocabulary>desire</vocabulary>. This is your <vocabulary>ambition</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5523', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%97%E6%9C%9B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:25:58.1689Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.028153Z'),
(5524, 'vocabulary', '恩人', '恩人', 'A <kanji>kindness</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a very kind person. This person is a <vocabulary>benefactor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5524', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%A9%E4%BA%BA', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:26:03.893633Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(5525, 'vocabulary', '恩賞', '恩賞', 'A <kanji>kindness</kanji> <kanji>prize</kanji> is one that someone gives you out of kindness. Perhaps you''ve done something nice, which is why they are giving you a <vocabulary>reward</vocabulary>.

Note: this is an older word, so you''ll probably only see it in history books, but it''s important to know anyway!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5525', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%A9%E8%B3%9E', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:26:10.829356Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T07:08:03.661183Z'),
(5526, 'vocabulary', '採る', '採る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>gather</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to gather</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5526', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A1%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:26:22.974686Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:46:31.228624Z'),
(5527, 'vocabulary', '採用', '採用', 'I <kanji>gather</kanji> things and <kanji>use</kanji> them. The things I''ve gathered, are the things I <vocabulary>use</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5527', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A1%E7%94%A8', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:26:30.147904Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:46:31.228624Z'),
(5528, 'vocabulary', '採決', '採決', 'When people <kanji>gather</kanji> and <kanji>decide</kanji> things, they decide together, usually by <vocabulary>voting</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5528', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A1%E6%B1%BA', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:26:34.792662Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:46:31.228624Z'),
(5529, 'vocabulary', '採算', '採算', '<kanji>Gather</kanji> the money and then <kanji>calculate</kanji> how much of it you have. Oh, nice, looks like you have a <vocabulary>profit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5529', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A1%E7%AE%97', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:26:40.56595Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T15:37:55.121141Z'),
(5530, 'vocabulary', '桜', '桜', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5530', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A1%9C', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:26:46.365436Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.130345Z'),
(5531, 'vocabulary', '桜んぼ', '桜んぼ', 'A <kanji>cherry tree</kanji> can create a <vocabulary>cherry</vocabulary>. This is what this word is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5531', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A1%9C%E3%82%93%E3%81%BC', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:26:57.495093Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.14107Z'),
(5532, 'vocabulary', '桜肉', '桜肉', 'Strangely, <kanji>cherry tree</kanji> <kanji>meat</kanji> isn''t just some kind of cherry steak... it''s <vocabulary>horse meat</vocabulary>. Hopefully you''ll just remember that due to the strangeness of this word''s kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5532', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A1%9C%E8%82%89', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:27:02.858748Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.1521Z'),
(5533, 'vocabulary', '桜色', '桜色', 'A <kanji>cherry blossom</kanji> <kanji>color</kanji> is what color? Kind of a <vocabulary>pink</vocabulary>, wouldn''t you say? Also, pink can be ピンク, if you want it to be.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5533', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A1%9C%E8%89%B2', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:27:12.403592Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.162831Z'),
(5534, 'vocabulary', '永遠', '永遠', 'An <kanji>eternity</kanji> that is <kanji>far</kanji> away is not really an eternity, it just feels like it. It''s <vocabulary>forever</vocabulary>, but not the real kind of forever that''s actually forever. That''s 永久, which is a real forever, this is more like "whoa, I haven''t eaten sushi in forever" (actually, you have).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5534', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B8%E9%81%A0', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:27:20.449938Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.18566Z'),
(5535, 'vocabulary', '永久', '永久', '<kanji>Eternity</kanji> is a <kanji>long time</kanji>... as in <vocabulary>forever</vocabulary>. This word has to do with an actual forever. So, like, forever. Not like an "oh man I haven''t done that in forever," because if you''ve done something before, it''s not forever. This is forever for realsies.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5535', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B8%E4%B9%85', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:27:31.907611Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.196153Z'),
(5536, 'vocabulary', '液体', '液体', 'Things that have a <kanji>fluid</kanji> <kanji>body</kanji> are made of <vocabulary>liquid</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5536', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B6%B2%E4%BD%93', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:27:38.57322Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:17.53814Z'),
(5537, 'vocabulary', '血液', '血液', 'Your <kanji>blood</kanji> <kanji>fluid</kanji> is your <vocabulary>blood</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5537', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%80%E6%B6%B2', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:27:46.140649Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.218196Z'),
(5538, 'vocabulary', '眼', '眼', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This is just a little more technical than 目.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5538', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%BC', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:28:06.390862Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.229041Z'),
(5539, 'vocabulary', '眼球', '眼球', 'An <kanji>eyeball</kanji> <kanji>sphere</kanji> is an <vocabulary>eyeball</vocabulary>. Eww.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5539', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%BC%E7%90%83', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:28:40.378099Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.240724Z'),
(5540, 'vocabulary', '老眼', '老眼', 'An <kanji>elderly</kanji> <kanji>eyeball</kanji> is one that can''t see things close up. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>farsightedness</vocabulary>. But this is the kind of farsightedness that only comes with age. So you have to be an elderly oldey to have 老眼.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5540', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%81%E7%9C%BC', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:28:50.450896Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.253856Z'),
(5541, 'vocabulary', '先祖', '先祖', 'Your <kanji>previous</kanji> <kanji>ancestor</kanji>s are your <vocabulary>ancestors</vocabulary>. All ancestors are previous to you, after all, unless you want to talk time travel, then who knows.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5541', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%88%E7%A5%96', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:28:56.271355Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:27:12.714404Z'),
(5542, 'vocabulary', '祖父', '祖父', 'Your <kanji>ancestor</kanji> <kanji>father</kanji> isn''t your father, it''s the father that came before your father''s generation, your <vocabulary>grandfather</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5542', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%96%E7%88%B6', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:29:02.362624Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:01:24.564442Z'),
(5543, 'vocabulary', '祖母', '祖母', 'Your <kanji>ancestor</kanji> <kanji>mother</kanji> isn''t your mother, it''s the mother before your mother, your <vocabulary>grandmother</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5543', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%96%E6%AF%8D', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:29:07.400179Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.292739Z'),
(5544, 'vocabulary', '功績', '功績', 'One <kanji>achievement</kanji> plus another <kanji>achievement</kanji> is an even bigger <vocabulary>achievement</vocabulary>. Wow, what an <vocabulary>accomplishment</vocabulary>!

功績 is a formal word that refers to noteworthy contributions or successes. It''s commonly used to praise someone''s hard work or great results.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5544', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%9F%E7%B8%BE', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:29:18.869432Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.304067Z'),
(5545, 'vocabulary', '成績', '成績', 'What kind of things <kanji>become</kanji> your <kanji>achievement</kanji>s? Your <vocabulary>grades</vocabulary>. At least when you''re in school, that is. Your grades become your main achievements, right? So let''s take a look at those grades… Oof, some of those "achievements" weren''t all that amazing. Don''t worry, grades aren''t everything.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5545', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%90%E7%B8%BE', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:29:27.360204Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.316589Z'),
(5546, 'vocabulary', '興味', '興味', 'I have an <kanji>interest</kanji> in the <kanji>flavor</kanji>... You do? Yes, I have a very deep <vocabulary>interest</vocabulary> in it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5546', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%88%E5%91%B3', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:29:40.767238Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:20:50.221396Z'),
(5547, 'vocabulary', '自衛', '自衛', '<kanji>Self</kanji> <kanji>defense</kanji> is when you protect yourself. It''s <vocabulary>self defense</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5547', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E8%A1%9B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:29:46.554606Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.339204Z'),
(5548, 'vocabulary', '衛生', '衛生', 'In <kanji>defense</kanji> of <kanji>life</kanji>, you have to remain clean. If you are dirty, you will die earlier (think: plagues). So, to defend said life, you must have good <vocabulary>hygiene</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5548', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%9B%E7%94%9F', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:29:54.874941Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.350543Z'),
(5549, 'vocabulary', '複雑', '複雑', 'There are <kanji>duplicate</kanji>s of everything, not to mention the duplicates are <kanji>random</kanji>. Nothing makes sense, and everything is super <vocabulary>complicated</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5549', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A4%87%E9%9B%91', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:30:07.638351Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:28:49.641004Z'),
(5550, 'vocabulary', '衛星', '衛星', 'A <kanji>defense</kanji> <kanji>star</kanji> isn''t really a star, but it is something shiny in the sky that can look like a star if it''s bright enough. This is a <vocabulary>satellite</vocabulary>, and while it''s not always defensive, satellites can have defensive properties. Think ion cannons and deflector shields and things like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5550', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%9B%E6%98%9F', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:30:38.627263Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T02:27:52.417356Z'),
(5551, 'vocabulary', '複数', '複数', 'The <kanji>duplicate</kanji> <kanji>numbers</kanji> are when you duplicate the numbers into more numbers, representing <vocabulary>multiple</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5551', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A4%87%E6%95%B0', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:31:09.474196Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:28:49.641004Z'),
(5552, 'vocabulary', '複写', '複写', 'When you make a <kanji>duplicate</kanji> <kanji>copy</kanji> you are <vocabulary>copying</vocabulary> things. Especially if you''re making a <vocabulary>photocopy</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>carbon copy</vocabulary>. It''s like a copy of a copy! 

複写 is a pretty formal word for copying documents, text, or images. It''s still often used to talk about carbon copies. But for photocopies, most people just say コピー(する) these days.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5552', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A4%87%E5%86%99', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:31:21.292843Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:28:49.641004Z'),
(5553, 'vocabulary', '雑音', '雑音', '<kanji>Random</kanji> <kanji>noise</kanji> is still <vocabulary>noise</vocabulary>, though more specifically it is random noise, like <vocabulary>static</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5553', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%91%E9%9F%B3', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:31:28.655766Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.414508Z'),
(5554, 'vocabulary', '雑費', '雑費', '<kanji>Random</kanji> <kanji>expense</kanji>s are <vocabulary>miscellaneous expenses</vocabulary>. Not actually random, but they feel that way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5554', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%91%E8%B2%BB', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:31:41.623449Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.428358Z'),
(5555, 'vocabulary', '酸っぱい', '酸っぱい', 'The taste of something with <kanji>acid</kanji> is... well... you''d think it''d be acidic, but it''s a <vocabulary>sour</vocabulary> taste. Some acidic things are pretty sour, though. Lemons are pretty acidic, and they''re super sour too, so think about that when you need to remember this word.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5555', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%B8%E3%81%A3%E3%81%B1%E3%81%84', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:31:57.018106Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:48:42.672332Z'),
(5556, 'vocabulary', '賛成', '賛成', 'It has <kanji>become</kanji> that we <kanji>agree</kanji>. We have <vocabulary>agreement</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5556', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%9B%E6%88%90', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:32:03.329153Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.456886Z'),
(5557, 'vocabulary', '酸素', '酸素', 'The <kanji>acid</kanji> <kanji>element</kanji> is <vocabulary>oxygen</vocabulary>. In German it’s the same as in Japanese - it’s called “Sauerstoff”, including “sauer”, the word for “sour”. The noun version is “Säure” which means both “sourness” and “acid” (which again is the same in Japanese, the same kanji is used in both words). The word “oxygenium” in Greek/Latin literally means “acid maker”. Long ago chemists thought oxygen was a defining characteristic of acids, later they found out there are also acids without oxygen like hydrochloric/muriatic/salt acid and the defining characteristic actually is hydrogen. But the name stuck.

Or you could just imagine all the oxygen you breathe has turned into acid (oh no!), and it sucks to breathe. Whatever works for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5557', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%B8%E7%B4%A0', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:32:09.18737Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:38:20.342554Z'),
(5558, 'vocabulary', '銭', '銭', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. It can also mean <vocabulary>money</vocabulary> more generally.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5558', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%AD', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:32:14.448259Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T17:43:52.890462Z'),
(5559, 'vocabulary', '銭湯', '銭湯', 'Give a <kanji>coin</kanji> and get <kanji>hot water</kanji>. So you hand over a coin and then you get hot water in the form of a bath. It''s a bath for anyone willing to pay, making it a <vocabulary>public bath</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5559', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%AD%E6%B9%AF', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:32:19.490624Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.508657Z'),
(5560, 'vocabulary', '飼う', '飼う', 'The kanji is <kanji>domesticate</kanji>. When you domesticate an animal, you get <vocabulary>to keep a pet</vocabulary> of some kind. You''d use this to describe when you are keeping a dog or cat or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5560', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%BC%E3%81%86', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:32:54.303596Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:51:58.846638Z'),
(5561, 'vocabulary', '傷', '傷', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5561', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%B7', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:33:52.21075Z', NULL, '2025-07-31T19:46:17.449704Z'),
(5562, 'vocabulary', '傷める', '傷める', 'The kanji means <kanji>wound</kanji>, and this verb is transitive, so it means <vocabulary>to damage something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to injure something</vocabulary>.

You can use it to talk about parts of your body, so you''ll sometimes see it used interchangeably with 痛める. The difference is that it''s objectively focussing on the damage, not the pain. That''s why 傷める can be used for objects like cars and furniture, too, whereas 痛める is only for people and body parts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5562', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%B7%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 36, 1, '2013-03-15T23:33:58.053333Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.569776Z'),
(5563, 'vocabulary', '傷心', '傷心', 'A <kanji>wound</kanji> of your <kanji>heart</kanji>. That could be many things, but in this case it''s <vocabulary>heartbreak</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5563', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%B7%E5%BF%83', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:34:16.509784Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.582373Z'),
(5564, 'vocabulary', '傷者', '傷者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> who has a <kanji>wound</kanji> is someone who is wounded. They are an <vocabulary>injured person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5564', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%B7%E8%80%85', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:34:21.43053Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(5565, 'vocabulary', '自民党', '自民党', 'The <kanji>individual</kanji> <kanji>peoples</kanji> <kanji>party</kanji> is the political group that believes in each individual of the people (or something?). The translation doesn''t really get hinted at in this meaning, but this group is the <vocabulary>Liberal Democratic Party</vocabulary>, aka the <vocabulary>LDP</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5565', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E6%B0%91%E5%85%9A', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:34:27.794627Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.607821Z'),
(5566, 'vocabulary', '共産党', '共産党', 'The idea of everyone <kanji>together</kanji> sharing <kanji>property</kanji> between them comes from which <kanji>party</kanji>? That''s the <vocabulary>Communist Party</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5566', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B1%E7%94%A3%E5%85%9A', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:34:33.959387Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.622193Z'),
(5567, 'vocabulary', '政党', '政党', 'A <kanji>politics</kanji> <kanji>party</kanji> is a <vocabulary>political party</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5567', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BF%E5%85%9A', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:34:44.721466Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.636186Z'),
(5568, 'vocabulary', '党員', '党員', 'A <kanji>party</kanji> <kanji>member</kanji> is <vocabulary>party member</vocabulary> of a political party.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5568', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%9A%E5%93%A1', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:34:51.368794Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:18:26.29811Z'),
(5569, 'vocabulary', '社会党', '社会党', 'The <kanji>society</kanji> (社会) <kanji>party</kanji> is the <vocabulary>socialist party</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5569', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A4%BE%E4%BC%9A%E5%85%9A', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:35:03.75877Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.710737Z'),
(5570, 'vocabulary', '卵', '卵', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5570', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B5', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:35:10.200298Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.745452Z'),
(5571, 'vocabulary', '生卵', '生卵', 'Remember how 生 can mean <kanji>raw</kanji>? Well, it does now. So, a <kanji>raw</kanji> <kanji>egg</kanji>? First of all, delicious. Second of all, it means <vocabulary>raw egg</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5571', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E5%8D%B5', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:35:17.767605Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.760038Z'),
(5572, 'vocabulary', 'ゆで卵', 'ゆで卵', 'The ゆで comes from the verb ゆでる, which means "to boil." So this word means <vocabulary>boiled egg</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5572', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%86%E3%81%A7%E5%8D%B5', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:35:30.133759Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.775405Z'),
(5573, 'vocabulary', '厳しい', '厳しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>strict</kanji>? It is also <vocabulary>strict</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5573', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%B3%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:35:38.646567Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.789785Z'),
(5574, 'vocabulary', '厳禁', '厳禁', 'Something with <kanji>strict</kanji> <kanji>prohibition</kanji> is <vocabulary>strictly forbidden</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>strictly prohibited</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5574', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%B3%E7%A6%81', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:35:46.640694Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.80537Z'),
(5575, 'vocabulary', '捨てる', '捨てる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>throw away</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to throw away</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5575', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%A8%E3%81%A6%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:35:52.271552Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:50:00.772162Z'),
(5576, 'vocabulary', '込む-5576', '込む', 'This word ends with the hiragana う, which tells you it''s a verb. The kanji 込 means <kanji>into</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to get into</vocabulary>.

This word generally attaches to the stem of another verb, and adds a nuance of moving something towards the center of something, or going deep into something. You’ll be seeing lots of examples of words with 込む on the end, so you’ll gradually get a feel for the nuance it adds!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5576', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%BC%E3%82%80-5576', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:36:15.532031Z', '2023-11-29T18:20:51.670943Z', '2025-06-11T05:33:20.84218Z'),
(5577, 'vocabulary', '密か', '密か', 'Something that has <kanji>secrecy</kanji> is <vocabulary>secret</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>private</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5577', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%86%E3%81%8B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:36:55.772418Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.855097Z'),
(5578, 'vocabulary', '密会', '密会', 'A <kanji>secrecy</kanji> <kanji>meet</kanji> is a <vocabulary>secret meeting</vocabulary>. You know the password?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5578', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%86%E4%BC%9A', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:37:01.484389Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.90874Z'),
(5579, 'vocabulary', '密告', '密告', 'In <kanji>secrecy</kanji>, you <kanji>announce</kanji> something to the authorities. Why? Because you are giving them a <vocabulary>tip-off</vocabulary>. The person you''re <vocabulary>informing</vocabulary> on might even say you''re <vocabulary>snitching</vocabulary>.

密告 means secretly informing on someone — usually about wrongdoing or a crime — to the authorities or a person in charge. It carries a strong negative tone, often suggesting betrayal or sneaky behavior, like reporting a fellow gang member''s drug possession to the police.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5579', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%86%E5%91%8A', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:37:09.496758Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:34:31.16393Z'),
(5580, 'vocabulary', '秘密', '秘密', '<kanji>Secret</kanji> and <kanji>secrecy</kanji>. Combine the two and you have quite the <vocabulary>secret</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5580', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%98%E5%AF%86', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:37:16.794544Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.940035Z'),
(5581, 'vocabulary', '密輸', '密輸', 'A <kanji>secrecy</kanji> <kanji>transport</kanji> is when you transport something in secrecy. If you don''t want people to know what you''re transporting, you''ll have to resort to <vocabulary>smuggling</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5581', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%86%E8%BC%B8', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:37:31.044409Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.95433Z'),
(5582, 'vocabulary', '染める', '染める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>dye</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to dye</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5582', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%93%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:37:41.630044Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.967768Z'),
(5583, 'vocabulary', '感染', '感染', 'The <kanji>feeling</kanji> of <kanji>dye</kanji>, covering your body, is the feeling of nasty germs and diseases penetrating your skin, getting inside of you. This is an <vocabulary>infection</vocabulary>, and it''s gross.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5583', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E6%9F%93', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:37:55.177345Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(5584, 'vocabulary', '伝染病', '伝染病', 'A <kanji>sick</kanji>ness that can <kanji>transmit</kanji> and then <kanji>dye</kanji> into you (aka get inside you and infect you, via transmission) is a <vocabulary>contagious disease</vocabulary>. Everyone cover their mouths when they cough and sneeze, and please wash your hands, okay?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5584', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9D%E6%9F%93%E7%97%85', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:38:01.097132Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:20.99566Z'),
(5585, 'vocabulary', '汚れる', '汚れる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>dirty</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to get dirty</vocabulary>. You know you''re not doing the dirtying, because it''s <reading>rare</reading> (れる) for you to get anything dirty, though things do get dirty on their own.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5585', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%9A%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:38:15.101215Z', NULL, '2026-01-09T21:18:46.204933Z'),
(5586, 'vocabulary', '汚染', '汚染', 'A <kanji>dirty</kanji> <kanji>dye</kanji> that''s all throughout the sky and makes things gross when it touches them is <vocabulary>pollution</vocabulary>. If it''s bad enough it really is like a dye, too, making your white clothes brown, yuck.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5586', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%9A%E6%9F%93', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:38:20.329993Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.039827Z'),
(5587, 'vocabulary', '汚水', '汚水', '<kanji>Dirty</kanji> <kanji>water</kanji> is <vocabulary>dirty water</vocabulary>. Gross. Could also be <vocabulary>sewage</vocabulary>. Even grosser.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5587', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%9A%E6%B0%B4', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:38:26.35888Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.052291Z'),
(5588, 'vocabulary', '汚点', '汚点', 'A <kanji>dirty</kanji> <kanji>point</kanji> is a little point of dirtiness on something. This is a <vocabulary>stain</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>smudge</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5588', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%9A%E7%82%B9', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:40:57.610277Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.065642Z'),
(5589, 'vocabulary', '欲しい', '欲しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>want</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>wanted</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5589', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%B2%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:41:07.007278Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.0787Z'),
(5590, 'vocabulary', '欲求', '欲求', 'What you <kanji>want</kanji> and <kanji>request</kanji> are your <vocabulary>wants</vocabulary>, your <vocabulary>desires</vocabulary>, perhaps your <vocabulary>craving</vocabulary>. You want these things so you request them. I wants them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5590', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%B2%E6%B1%82', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:41:14.704914Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:53:56.46731Z'),
(5591, 'vocabulary', '肉欲', '肉欲', 'Your <kanji>meat</kanji> <kanji>want</kanji>s are your deepest desires, your <vocabulary>carnal desires</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5591', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%89%E6%AC%B2', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:41:19.663059Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.122578Z'),
(5592, 'vocabulary', '食欲', '食欲', 'I have a <kanji>food</kanji> <kanji>want</kanji>. When I want food (or when I don''t want food), it has to do with your <vocabulary>appetite</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5592', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9F%E6%AC%B2', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:41:26.580471Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.13899Z'),
(5593, 'vocabulary', '暖かい', '暖かい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>warm</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>warm</vocabulary> as well! Though this is for a <vocabulary>warm</vocabulary> weather or temperature, not some warm food or something like that, that would be 温かい, a different あたたかい. Confusing, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5593', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%96%E3%81%8B%E3%81%84', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:41:30.990433Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.15467Z'),
(5594, 'vocabulary', '温暖', '温暖', '<kanji>Warm</kanji> <kanji>warm</kanji> is <vocabulary>warm</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5594', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%A9%E6%9A%96', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:41:36.048301Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.169864Z'),
(5595, 'vocabulary', '机', '机', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5595', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%BA', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:41:40.944928Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.184309Z'),
(5596, 'vocabulary', '秘める', '秘める', 'If you <kanji>secret</kanji> something, you are trying <vocabulary>to keep secret</vocabulary> something. That, or you''re trying <vocabulary>to conceal</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5596', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%98%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:41:48.228415Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.200636Z'),
(5597, 'vocabulary', '便秘', '便秘', 'For this word, the 便 comes from 便所, which you know as "toilet." A <kanji>toilet</kanji> <kanji>secret</kanji> is one you take with you to the bathroom, one that you don''t want people to know about. This is <vocabulary>constipation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5597', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%BF%E7%A7%98', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:41:56.482973Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.216034Z'),
(5598, 'vocabulary', '簡易', '簡易', 'It has <kanji>simplicity</kanji> and is <kanji>easy</kanji>. Whatever it is, it is <vocabulary>simple</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>easy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5598', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B0%A1%E6%98%93', 33, 1, '2013-03-15T23:42:06.707641Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.775558Z'),
(5599, 'vocabulary', '訳', '訳', 'This word takes the <kanji>reason</kanji> meaning of the kanji, making it <vocabulary>reason</vocabulary>. What''s the reason for it having two meanings? I have no clue, but there must be a reason.

While 理由 is the general term for reasons, 訳 tends to be more focused on the underlying reason or cause of something. It''s also used when explaining a situation or when there''s an implied need to justify or clarify something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5599', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%B3', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:42:12.789566Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.258397Z'),
(5600, 'vocabulary', '訳す', '訳す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>translate</kanji> so the verb version would be <vocabulary>to translate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5600', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%B3%E3%81%99', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:42:23.856183Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.27316Z'),
(5601, 'vocabulary', '訳者', '訳者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> who <kanji>translate</kanji>s things is a <vocabulary>translator</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5601', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%B3%E8%80%85', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:42:29.863253Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(5602, 'vocabulary', '訳語', '訳語', 'A <kanji>translate</kanji>d bit of <kanji>language</kanji> is a <vocabulary>translated term</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5602', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%B3%E8%AA%9E', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:42:37.970255Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.298106Z'),
(5603, 'vocabulary', '通訳', '通訳', 'If you let words <kanji>pass through</kanji> you, and as they do you <kanji>translate</kanji> them, you are doing <vocabulary>interpreting</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5603', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%9A%E8%A8%B3', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:42:47.993935Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.310338Z'),
(5604, 'vocabulary', '英訳', '英訳', '<kanji>English</kanji> <kanji>translate</kanji> is an <vocabulary>English translation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5604', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%B1%E8%A8%B3', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:42:53.269549Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.324414Z'),
(5605, 'vocabulary', '和訳', '和訳', 'Remember how 和 represents Japan? Now, take that and add <kanji>translate</kanji> to it. With that, you have <vocabulary>Japanese translation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5605', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%92%8C%E8%A8%B3', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:42:58.667379Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.337495Z'),
(5606, 'vocabulary', '延長する', '延長する', 'If 延長 is <kanji>prolonging</kanji>, then the verb version is <vocabulary>to prolong</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5606', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%B6%E9%95%B7%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:46:27.311991Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.349999Z'),
(5607, 'vocabulary', '今更', '今更', '<kanji>Now</kanji> isn’t good enough for <kanji>renew</kanji>al. It’s already <vocabulary>too late</vocabulary>. The time has passed <vocabulary>now</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5607', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A%E6%9B%B4', 25, 1, '2013-03-15T23:48:29.152695Z', NULL, '2026-04-04T20:17:45.612577Z'),
(5608, 'vocabulary', '漏らす', '漏らす', 'You learned 漏れる means "to leak." 漏らす is the transitive version, which means it takes a direct object. So this means <vocabulary>to let something leak</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to leak something</vocabulary>. The reason you let something leak is because you <reading>rush</reading>ed (らす) the job (because you did it yourself)!

Even though it''s transitive, 漏らす often implies an unintentional leak, as in おしっこを漏らす (wet one''s pants) or 水を漏らすな (don''t let water spill). It''s also used figuratively, like 秘密を漏らす (leak a secret), 不満を漏らす (express dissatisfaction), or ためいきを漏らす (let out a sigh).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5608', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%8F%E3%82%89%E3%81%99', 32, 1, '2013-03-15T23:49:22.186474Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.288165Z'),
(5609, 'vocabulary', '越す', '越す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>go beyond</kanji> so the verb vocab version is also <vocabulary>to go beyond</vocabulary>.

This is very similar in meaning to 越える. Both can refer to overcoming some physical object or passing beyond a certain amount of time. However, 越す tends to sound a little less intense, while 越える really focuses on the physical or emotional challenge of overcoming a certain milestone. For example, while 山を越す simply refers to crossing a mountain, 山を越える gives the impression that crossing the mountain is a difficulty you''ve overcome.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5609', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B6%8A%E3%81%99', 32, 1, '2013-03-16T00:03:43.058879Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.426213Z'),
(5610, 'vocabulary', '浮かれる', '浮かれる', '浮く means "to float," and 浮かれる draws on that metaphor of "floating" to mean <vocabulary>to be happy</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be in high spirits</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to be elated</vocabulary>. We have the same metaphor in English — think of being "on cloud nine." ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5610', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%AE%E3%81%8B%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2013-03-16T00:18:31.67396Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.439886Z'),
(5611, 'vocabulary', '編集する', '編集する', '編集 is <kanji>edit</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to edit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5611', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A8%E9%9B%86%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:26:00.281695Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.789112Z'),
(5612, 'vocabulary', '掃除機', '掃除機', 'A <kanji>cleaning</kanji> (掃除) <kanji>machine</kanji> is a <vocabulary>vacuum cleaner</vocabulary>. Suck that dirt up!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5612', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%83%E9%99%A4%E6%A9%9F', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:26:05.727359Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:41:39.325074Z'),
(5613, 'vocabulary', '散歩する', '散歩する', '散歩 is "a walk." Make that into a verb and you have <vocabulary>to go for a walk</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to go for a stroll</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5613', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%A3%E6%AD%A9%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:26:19.732334Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:17.833734Z'),
(5614, 'vocabulary', '華々しい', '華々しい', '華やか is "showy." Now we''re doubling it, making it doubly showy. If you make something showy enough, it becomes <vocabulary>gorgeous</vocabulary>, but it also becomes pretty <vocabulary>flamboyant</vocabulary>. So, so, so flamboyant.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5614', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8F%AF%E3%80%85%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:26:28.115047Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(5615, 'vocabulary', '除いて', '除いて', '除く is "to exclude." This is the te-form, and it means "except." So, something like "please get everything except broccoli," because who wants broccoli?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5615', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%A4%E3%81%84%E3%81%A6', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:26:42.800238Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.868048Z'),
(5616, 'vocabulary', '物真似', '物真似', '真似 is "impersonation." Actually, this word is too. It''s also <vocabulary>impersonation</vocabulary>.

物真似 is very similar to 真似, but can be used in slightly different contexts. 真似 is used for general acts of mimicry or imitation, and can also be used to refer to the way someone does something. On the other hand, while 物真似 also describes acts of mimicry and imitation, it''s additionally used in performance or entertainment contexts to refer to people who do impersonation acts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5616', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%A9%E7%9C%9F%E4%BC%BC', 34, 1, '2013-03-18T17:26:47.761Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T20:04:39.445204Z'),
(5617, 'vocabulary', '墓場', '墓場', 'A <kanji>grave</kanji> <kanji>location</kanji> is a place where there are graves. It is a <vocabulary>graveyard</vocabulary>.

This word is more direct and informal than 墓地, so using 墓場 for an actual cemetery might come off as disrespectful. It''s commonly used in phrases like ゆりかごから墓場まで (from the cradle to the grave) or 墓場まで持っていく (take a secret to the grave).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5617', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%93%E5%A0%B4', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:26:54.649712Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.88185Z'),
(5618, 'vocabulary', '閉める', '閉める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>closed</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to close something</vocabulary>.

閉める primarily means <vocabulary>to shut something</vocabulary> in a physical sense (often with a sliding or twisting motion), like when you close doors, windows, curtains, faucets, and so on. Additionally, it can also be used for closing shops, either for the day or permanently.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5618', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%89%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2013-03-18T17:27:27.198147Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.159279Z'),
(5619, 'vocabulary', '閉店', '閉店', 'A <kanji>closed</kanji> <kanji>shop</kanji> is when a store''s status is <vocabulary>store closed</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>closed store</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5619', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%89%E5%BA%97', 14, 1, '2013-03-18T17:27:35.881955Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.587995Z'),
(5620, 'vocabulary', '開閉', '開閉', '<kanji>Open</kanji>ed and <kanji>closed</kanji> things are <vocabulary>opening and closing</vocabulary>.

開閉 is a relatively formal word to refer to the action of opening and closing physical objects such as doors, shutters, or valves. You often see it in instruction manuals or product descriptions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5620', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%8B%E9%96%89', 14, 1, '2013-03-18T17:27:40.903827Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:21.612876Z'),
(5621, 'vocabulary', '雑誌', '雑誌', '<kanji>Random</kanji> <kanji>magazine</kanji> is pretty much all magazines. Just a bunch of random stuff inside, making it a <vocabulary>magazine</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5621', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%91%E8%AA%8C', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:27:48.334465Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.894908Z'),
(5622, 'vocabulary', '窓口', '窓口', 'A <kanji>window</kanji> <kanji>mouth</kanji> is where you go to buy tickets out of the window. You know how there''s those windows with holes in them, or the window is the hole itself? That''s the kind of <vocabulary>ticket window</vocabulary> I''m talking about.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5622', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AA%93%E5%8F%A3', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:27:55.426592Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.908953Z'),
(5623, 'vocabulary', '窓', '窓', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5623', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AA%93', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:28:03.042587Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.920669Z'),
(5624, 'vocabulary', '否', '否', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Note that 否 (いな) is very formal and somewhat archaic. Today, it''s most commonly used in the expression 〜か否か (whether… or <vocabulary>not</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5624', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%A6', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:28:08.169925Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T17:17:56.158928Z'),
(5625, 'vocabulary', '否定', '否定', '<kanji>No</kanji>! I will not <kanji>determine</kanji> that. In fact, I <vocabulary>deny</vocabulary> it. This is my <vocabulary>denial</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5625', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%A6%E5%AE%9A', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:28:16.636407Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(5626, 'vocabulary', '否決', '否決', '"<kanji>No</kanji>" is the <kanji>decision</kanji> I''ve made. Sorry, but this is <vocabulary>rejection</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5626', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%A6%E6%B1%BA', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:28:26.701312Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:09:27.747622Z'),
(5627, 'vocabulary', '筋', '筋', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well, at least on the <kanji>tendon</kanji> side of things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5627', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AD%8B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:28:32.879696Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:17.885724Z'),
(5628, 'vocabulary', '筋肉', '筋肉', 'Your <kanji>muscle</kanji> <kanji>meat</kanji> is your <vocabulary>muscle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5628', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AD%8B%E8%82%89', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:28:39.078713Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.97922Z'),
(5629, 'vocabulary', '垂らす', '垂らす', 'You learned that 垂れる means "to droop" or "to drip." Well, this is the transitive version of that, meaning it takes a direct object. This time, you''re the one making something droop or drip, which is why this word means <vocabulary>to dangle something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to dribble something</vocabulary>. You can remember it''s transitive because of the す ending, which you''ve seen with lots of transitive verbs by now.

垂らす describes when you dangle something or let something hang in a way which allows gravity to act on it, like hanging a line when fishing. It''s also used to describe letting something drip — for example, a little kid letting mucus drip from their nose or a sleeping person dribbling drool out of their mouth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5629', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9E%82%E3%82%89%E3%81%99', 30, 1, '2013-03-18T17:28:51.356093Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:48:36.563518Z'),
(5630, 'vocabulary', '垂直', '垂直', 'Something <kanji>droop</kanji>s <kanji>straight</kanji> down, making it perfectly <vocabulary>vertical</vocabulary>. If we wanted to get fancy, we could even say it''s <vocabulary>perpendicular</vocabulary> to the floor.

垂直 is a formal, precise word meaning vertical — perfectly up-and-down, at a 90-degree angle to the ground or another surface. You''ll hear it in math, science, construction, or any situation where exact orientation matters, like 垂直の線 (a vertical line).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5630', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9E%82%E7%9B%B4', 26, 1, '2013-03-18T17:29:02.675747Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T07:29:35.974555Z'),
(5631, 'vocabulary', '宝', '宝', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5631', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9D', 4, 1, '2013-03-18T17:29:09.031236Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.150871Z'),
(5632, 'vocabulary', '宝石', '宝石', 'A <kanji>treasure</kanji> <kanji>stone</kanji> is a very valuable stone. What kind of stones are considered treasures? How about a <vocabulary>jewel</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>gem</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5632', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9D%E7%9F%B3', 4, 1, '2013-03-18T17:29:22.632077Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.161447Z'),
(5633, 'vocabulary', '国宝', '国宝', 'A <kanji>country</kanji>''s <kanji>treasure</kanji> is their <vocabulary>national treasure</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5633', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BD%E5%AE%9D', 6, 1, '2013-03-18T17:29:30.746123Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.395142Z'),
(5634, 'vocabulary', '宣伝', '宣伝', 'If you want to <kanji>proclaim</kanji> and <kanji>transmit</kanji> something to a bunch of people, then what better way to do it than <vocabulary>advertising</vocabulary>? Yup, this word refers to various types of <vocabulary>promotion</vocabulary>s, like those for products, services, or ideas. Or, when it comes to transmitting all the things a leader proclaims, you can also call it <vocabulary>propaganda</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5634', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%A3%E4%BC%9D', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:29:42.917584Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T04:33:10.865472Z'),
(5635, 'vocabulary', '宣言', '宣言', 'You <kanji>proclaim</kanji> things by <kanji>say</kanji>ing them. This is a <vocabulary>proclamation</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>declaration</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5635', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%A3%E8%A8%80', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:29:49.045109Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.028334Z'),
(5636, 'vocabulary', '尊い', '尊い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>revered</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>revered</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5636', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8A%E3%81%84', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:30:12.095435Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.040913Z'),
(5637, 'vocabulary', '尊敬', '尊敬', 'You are <kanji>revered</kanji> and you have my <kanji>respect</kanji>. This is <vocabulary>respect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5637', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8A%E6%95%AC', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:30:16.905378Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.067475Z'),
(5638, 'vocabulary', '忠実', '忠実', 'I have <kanji>loyalty</kanji> to you, so I always tell you the <kanji>truth</kanji>. I am very <vocabulary>faithful</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>devoted</vocabulary> to you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5638', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%A0%E5%AE%9F', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:30:22.316201Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.078208Z'),
(5639, 'vocabulary', '忠告', '忠告', 'A <kanji>loyalty</kanji> <kanji>announcement</kanji> is the kind of announcement you give to someone who you''re loyal to. To this type of person, your announcement would come in the form of <vocabulary>advice</vocabulary> or maybe <vocabulary>admonishment</vocabulary>, if you''re upset with what they''ve done.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5639', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%A0%E5%91%8A', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:30:27.341447Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:34:31.16393Z'),
(5640, 'vocabulary', '拡がる', '拡がる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>extend</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to extend</vocabulary>.

Does the kanji look familiar, kind of like 広い, perhaps? 拡がる is the less common and narrower counterpart of 広がる. It''s used to describe the enlargement or extension of the size, area, or range of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5640', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%A1%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:30:33.433331Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.099519Z'),
(5641, 'vocabulary', '拡張', '拡張', 'Go ahead and <kanji>extend</kanji> it and <kanji>stretch</kanji> it. That''s how we''ll do this <vocabulary>enlargement</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>expansion</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>extension</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5641', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%A1%E5%BC%B5', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:30:41.590461Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.110747Z'),
(5642, 'vocabulary', '拡大', '拡大', '<kanji>Extend</kanji> the lens nice and <kanji>big</kanji> to <vocabulary>zoom</vocabulary> in on the subject, please. That''s <vocabulary>magnification</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>enlargement</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>expansion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5642', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%A1%E5%A4%A7', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:30:49.426097Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(5643, 'vocabulary', '操る', '操る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>manipulate</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to manipulate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5643', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%93%8D%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:31:00.382677Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.137925Z'),
(5644, 'vocabulary', '操', '操', 'This takes the second meaning of the kanji you learned, <vocabulary>chastity</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5644', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%93%8D', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:31:07.683565Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.148959Z'),
(5645, 'vocabulary', '操作', '操作', 'If you <kanji>manipulate</kanji> the things you <kanji>make</kanji>, you are in <vocabulary>operation</vocabulary> of said things.

This can not only refer to the operation of devices and machinery, but also the <vocabulary>manipulation</vocabulary> of various elements, such as markets, stock prices, and more, with the intention of furthering one''s own interests.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5645', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%93%8D%E4%BD%9C', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:31:17.529306Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:17.957797Z'),
(5646, 'vocabulary', '体操', '体操', 'Take your <kanji>body</kanji> and <kanji>manipulate</kanji> it around for exercise. This is <vocabulary>Calisthenics</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>gymnastics</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5646', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%93%E6%93%8D', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:31:24.674261Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.185266Z'),
(5647, 'vocabulary', '敬語', '敬語', '<kanji>Respect</kanji> <kanji>language</kanji> is what you speak to someone you respect and want to elevate. You can do this using this type of Japanese, which is known as <vocabulary>honorific</vocabulary> language.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5647', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%AC%E8%AA%9E', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:31:41.544037Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.196719Z'),
(5648, 'vocabulary', '暮らす', '暮らす', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>livelihood</kanji>. The verb version is <vocabulary>to live</vocabulary>. It''s not really "to live" literally, though. It''s more like <vocabulary>to live one''s life</vocabulary>, rather than just to live.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5648', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%AE%E3%82%89%E3%81%99', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:31:53.497276Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.206861Z'),
(5649, 'vocabulary', '野暮', '野暮', 'A <kanji>field</kanji> of <kanji>livelihood</kanji> is so lively! Look at everything jumping around. It is so fresh and new and awesome! That being said, being so lively and fresh and new, it''s pretty <vocabulary>unrefined</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>rustic</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5649', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8E%E6%9A%AE', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:31:59.74329Z', NULL, '2025-10-23T03:44:19.140231Z'),
(5650, 'vocabulary', '灰', '灰', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5650', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%B0', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:32:14.168925Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.229402Z'),
(5651, 'vocabulary', '灰色', '灰色', '<kanji>Ash</kanji> <kanji>color</kanji> tends to be a kind of gray color, wouldn''t you say? That makes this word <vocabulary>gray</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>grey</vocabulary>, depending on where you''re from).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5651', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%B0%E8%89%B2', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:32:19.379059Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.240208Z'),
(5652, 'vocabulary', '灰皿', '灰皿', 'An <kanji>ash</kanji> <kanji>plate</kanji> is a plate you use for ashes. That''s an <vocabulary>ashtray</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5652', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%B0%E7%9A%BF', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:32:27.526379Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.755267Z'),
(5653, 'vocabulary', '熟れる', '熟れる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>ripen</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to ripen</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5653', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%86%9F%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:32:34.992722Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.259967Z'),
(5654, 'vocabulary', '熟語', '熟語', '<kanji>Ripen</kanji>ed <kanji>language</kanji> is language that''s had some time to mature like fine wine. We''re talking about nuanced, grown-up words: <vocabulary>compound word</vocabulary>s or <vocabulary>kanji compound</vocabulary>s. You know that "<vocabulary>jukugo</vocabulary>" thing we talk about so much? That''s this!

熟語 refers to Japanese words made up of two or more kanji — stuff like 天気, 学校, and even the word 熟語 itself. You may also see it in the context of 四字熟語 (よじじゅくご) — four-character kanji compounds that often have idiomatic or proverbial meanings. 

In Japanese schools, 熟語 can sometimes refer to idioms or common word pairings in foreign languages. But when talking about Japanese itself, 熟語 simply means kanji compounds — kanji teaming up so their meanings can ripen into one neat little word!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5654', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%86%9F%E8%AA%9E', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:32:41.518683Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:55:24.532505Z'),
(5655, 'vocabulary', '未熟', '未熟', 'Something that has <kanji>not yet</kanji> <kanji>ripen</kanji>ed, is, well, <vocabulary>unripe</vocabulary>. This is often used figuratively to say that someone is either <vocabulary>immature</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>inexperienced</vocabulary>, so you''ll often encounter those usages!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5655', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AA%E7%86%9F', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:32:49.675637Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.281392Z'),
(5656, 'vocabulary', '成熟', '成熟', 'To <kanji>become</kanji> <kanji>ripe(n)</kanji> is to <vocabulary>ripen</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>mature</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5656', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%90%E7%86%9F', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:32:55.238673Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.291459Z'),
(5657, 'vocabulary', '異なる', '異なる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>differ</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to differ</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5657', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%B0%E3%81%AA%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:33:02.266531Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.301642Z'),
(5658, 'vocabulary', '異状', '異状', 'It <kanji>differ</kanji>s from the normal <kanji>condition</kanji> of things, making it weird. It is an <vocabulary>abnormality</vocabulary>. There is <vocabulary>something wrong</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5658', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%B0%E7%8A%B6', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:33:09.268712Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.311313Z'),
(5659, 'vocabulary', '異性', '異性', 'Someone who <kanji>differ</kanji>s in <kanji>gender</kanji> from you is of a <vocabulary>different gender</vocabulary>. This is often used to mean the <vocabulary>opposite sex</vocabulary> in the sense of men as viewed by women and vice versa.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5659', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%B0%E6%80%A7', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:33:15.246902Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.321681Z'),
(5660, 'vocabulary', '異義', '異義', 'My idea of <kanji>righteousness</kanji> is <kanji>different</kanji> from yours. Really, it''s just a <vocabulary>different meaning</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5660', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%B0%E7%BE%A9', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:33:25.152829Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:46:56.671656Z'),
(5661, 'vocabulary', '天皇', '天皇', 'The Japanese <kanji>emperor</kanji> is supposed to have come from the <kanji>heaven</kanji>s, so that just makes this heaven emperor the <vocabulary>emperor of Japan</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5661', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A9%E7%9A%87', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:33:31.966755Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.355971Z'),
(5662, 'vocabulary', '皇太子', '皇太子', 'The <kanji>emperor</kanji>''s <kanji>fat</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> is the <vocabulary>crown prince</vocabulary>, not because he''s fat, but because he''s the emperor''s child.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5662', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%87%E5%A4%AA%E5%AD%90', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:33:39.072358Z', NULL, '2025-09-24T17:23:41.203717Z'),
(5663, 'vocabulary', '皇族', '皇族', 'The <kanji>emperor</kanji>''s <kanji>tribe</kanji> is his family. This family is known as the <vocabulary>imperial family</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5663', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%87%E6%97%8F', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:33:51.054674Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.378007Z'),
(5664, 'vocabulary', '皇室', '皇室', 'The <kanji>emperor</kanji>''s <kanji>room</kanji> is where the <vocabulary>Imperial Family</vocabulary> gathers.

Note that 皇室 refers to the <vocabulary>Imperial Household</vocabulary> as a family unit, rather than a physical space.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5664', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%87%E5%AE%A4', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:33:58.611854Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:57:32.391485Z'),
(5665, 'vocabulary', '盛る', '盛る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>heaps</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to heap up</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>to serve food</vocabulary>, as in you''re heaping on food onto someone''s plate.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5665', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%9B%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:34:28.118647Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.398626Z'),
(5666, 'vocabulary', '砂', '砂', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5666', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%82', 36, 1, '2013-03-18T17:34:35.150343Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:18.068944Z'),
(5667, 'vocabulary', '砂糖', '砂糖', 'A <kanji>sand</kanji> <kanji>sugar</kanji> is just <vocabulary>sugar</vocabulary>.  Nom nom nom, sugar.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5667', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%82%E7%B3%96', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:34:45.713012Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T04:27:14.256999Z'),
(5668, 'vocabulary', '砂漠', '砂漠', 'A <kanji>sand</kanji> <kanji>desert</kanji> is just a <vocabulary>desert</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5668', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%82%E6%BC%A0', 35, 1, '2013-03-18T17:34:51.748786Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:27:03.046576Z'),
(5669, 'vocabulary', '漠然', '漠然', 'Something with a <kanji>vague</kanji> <kanji>nature</kanji> is also quite <vocabulary>vague</vocabulary>. 

Note that you need to add と to the end of this word to make it an adverb, and たる to make it an adjective!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5669', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%A0%E7%84%B6', 35, 1, '2013-03-18T17:34:57.440845Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:22.407855Z'),
(5670, 'vocabulary', '果糖', '果糖', 'A <kanji>fruit</kanji> <kanji>sugar</kanji> is the type of sugar that''s more commonly known as <vocabulary>fructose</vocabulary>. Mmm, delicious fructose.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5670', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%9C%E7%B3%96', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:35:03.519972Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.418936Z'),
(5671, 'vocabulary', '無糖', '無糖', 'Something with <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>sugar</kanji> is something that is <vocabulary>sugar free</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>unsweetened</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5671', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E7%B3%96', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:35:11.195676Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.429511Z'),
(5672, 'vocabulary', '納める', '納める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>supply</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to supply</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5672', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%8D%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:35:16.743603Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.440448Z'),
(5673, 'vocabulary', '収納', '収納', 'When you <kanji>obtain</kanji> <kanji>supply</kanji>, you have to put it all together somewhere. You have to put it in <vocabulary>storage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5673', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8E%E7%B4%8D', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:35:34.033307Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.45112Z'),
(5674, 'vocabulary', '納入', '納入', 'When you <kanji>enter</kanji> your <kanji>supply</kanji> into the machine, that is your <vocabulary>payment</vocabulary> for the day. Every day you have to enter your supply, otherwise they crack down on you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5674', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%8D%E5%85%A5', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:35:46.869469Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.461927Z'),
(5675, 'vocabulary', '肺', '肺', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5675', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%BA', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:35:53.444596Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.471891Z'),
(5676, 'vocabulary', '肺がん', '肺がん', '肺 is <kanji>lung</kanji>, がん is "cancer," I suppose because you want it <reading>gone</reading> (がん) from you. That makes this word <vocabulary>lung cancer</vocabulary>, which, with all the smoking in Japan, happens a decent amount.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5676', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%BA%E3%81%8C%E3%82%93', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:35:59.250468Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.482676Z'),
(5677, 'vocabulary', '肺病', '肺病', 'A <kanji>lung</kanji> <kanji>sick</kanji>ness is bad for those lungs of yours. It means you have <vocabulary>lung disease</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5677', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%BA%E7%97%85', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:36:05.343385Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.503034Z'),
(5678, 'vocabulary', '著しい', '著しい', 'The kanji means <kanji>author</kanji>, but if you think about it, what do authors do? They write about <vocabulary>remarkable</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>marked</vocabulary> things, whether it''s fiction or nonfiction.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5678', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%91%97%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:36:34.147765Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:51:50.70388Z'),
(5679, 'vocabulary', '著者', '著者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> who is an <kanji>author</kanji> is an <vocabulary>author</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5679', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%91%97%E8%80%85', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:36:40.917899Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(5680, 'vocabulary', '著作', '著作', 'An <kanji>author</kanji> <kanji>make</kanji>s <vocabulary>writing</vocabulary> in an activity known as <vocabulary>authorship</vocabulary>.

Note that this word isn''t a general term for writing! 著作 has a more specific nuance — it''s mostly used to talk about a work or the writings of a particular author. You''ll also see it often in the compound 著作権, in relation to copyright.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5680', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%91%97%E4%BD%9C', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:36:49.980183Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:51:50.70388Z'),
(5681, 'vocabulary', '蒸れる', '蒸れる', 'Anywhere with lots of <kanji>steam</kanji> is going to get hot and sticky, so this word means... <vocabulary>to get hot and sticky</vocabulary>!

With 蒸れる, something gets hot and sticky due to trapped heat and moisture, like sweaty armpits under a shirt on a humid day, or a sticky bottom after sitting at a desk for a while.

Additionally, 蒸れる can mean <vocabulary>to be steamed</vocabulary>, like what happens to rice as it softens in a rice cooker.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5681', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%92%B8%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:36:55.634984Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.547769Z'),
(5682, 'vocabulary', '蒸気', '蒸気', '<kanji>Steam</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji> is <vocabulary>steam</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>vapor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5682', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%92%B8%E6%B0%97', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:37:05.035206Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(5683, 'vocabulary', '蔵', '蔵', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Curious about the difference between 蔵 and 倉? Traditionally, the 蔵 kanji was used for sturdy buildings that stored important items. This is where samurai kept their valuables! On the other hand, 倉 originally referred to places to store grain, and usually weren''t as robustly made. They''re basically interchangeable now, but the 蔵 kanji is more commonly used.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5683', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%94%B5', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:37:20.21767Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.571414Z'),
(5684, 'vocabulary', '冷蔵庫', '冷蔵庫', 'A <kanji>cool</kanji> <kanji>storehouse</kanji> <kanji>storage</kanji> is a place you put things (like food) to keep them cool. This is a <vocabulary>refrigerator</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5684', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%B7%E8%94%B5%E5%BA%AB', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:37:31.469462Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.583189Z'),
(5685, 'vocabulary', '地蔵', '地蔵', 'The <kanji>ground</kanji> <kanji>storehouse</kanji> refers to a person''s name. It may be easier to learn this one reading first because of that. Anyways, this person is <vocabulary>Jizo(u)</vocabulary>, a Bodhisattva, and protector of children.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5685', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E8%94%B5', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:37:44.283068Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.596937Z'),
(5686, 'vocabulary', '装い', '装い', 'This looks like it''s an adjective, but it''s just a tricky word. It''s a noun, and it means the same thing as the kanji, making it <vocabulary>attire</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5686', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%85%E3%81%84', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:37:50.051075Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.608371Z'),
(5687, 'vocabulary', '装置', '装置', 'This <kanji>attire</kanji> is <kanji>put</kanji> inside this <vocabulary>equipment</vocabulary>, where it''s changed into something else. This attire <vocabulary>device</vocabulary> is strange and interesting. Imagine something that it does to your attire.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5687', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%85%E7%BD%AE', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:38:01.308373Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:08:17.52437Z'),
(5688, 'vocabulary', '変装', '変装', '<kanji>Change</kanji> your <kanji>attire</kanji> to look like someone else. This is a <vocabulary>disguise</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5688', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%89%E8%A3%85', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:38:16.718015Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.628264Z'),
(5689, 'vocabulary', '服装', '服装', 'Your <kanji>clothes</kanji> which <kanji>attire</kanji> you is your <vocabulary>attire</vocabulary>. It''s how you <vocabulary>dress</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5689', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%8D%E8%A3%85', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:38:29.355375Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.638529Z'),
(5690, 'vocabulary', '裏口', '裏口', 'The <kanji>backside</kanji> <kanji>mouth</kanji> (think: entrance) is the <vocabulary>back door</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>rear entrance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5690', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%8F%E5%8F%A3', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:38:40.941062Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.648374Z'),
(5691, 'vocabulary', '裏切る', '裏切る', 'If you <kanji>cut</kanji> someone from the <kanji>backside</kanji> you''re doing a backstabbing, making this word <vocabulary>to backstab</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to betray</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5691', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%8F%E5%88%87%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:38:49.796412Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.658304Z'),
(5692, 'vocabulary', '裏切り', '裏切り', 'From the <kanji>backside</kanji> you <kanji>cut</kanji> someone. Wow. What a jerk. What <vocabulary>betrayal</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>treachery</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5692', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%8F%E5%88%87%E3%82%8A', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:39:00.848336Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.6684Z'),
(5693, 'vocabulary', '裏通り', '裏通り', 'A <kanji>backside</kanji> <kanji>street</kanji> (or "avenue") is a street that''s not a main street... it''s hidden and behind everything, making it an <vocabulary>alley</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5693', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%8F%E9%80%9A%E3%82%8A', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:39:07.444134Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.678935Z'),
(5694, 'vocabulary', '裏', '裏', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5694', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%8F', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:39:20.864075Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:10:27.895031Z'),
(5695, 'vocabulary', '諸君', '諸君', 'My <kanji>various</kanji> <kanji>buddies</kanji>! Wait, that didn''t come out right. <vocabulary>Gentlemen</vocabulary>! <vocabulary>My friends</vocabulary>! There we go, much better.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5695', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%B8%E5%90%9B', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:39:39.988422Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.699412Z'),
(5696, 'vocabulary', '諸〜', '諸〜', 'This has the same meaning as the kanji. Stick this onto another word and you have "various _____."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5696', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%B8%E3%80%9C', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:39:45.526198Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.710274Z'),
(5697, 'vocabulary', '家賃', '家賃', 'Your <kanji>house</kanji> <kanji>rent</kanji> is your <vocabulary>house rent</vocabulary>, though it can be your apartment <vocabulary>rent</vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5697', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B6%E8%B3%83', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:39:52.696605Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:50:26.818765Z'),
(5698, 'vocabulary', '賃貸', '賃貸', '<kanji>Rent</kanji> it and I will <kanji>lend</kanji> it to you. This thing that is being lent and rented? It''s a <vocabulary>rental</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5698', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%83%E8%B2%B8', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T17:39:57.619533Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:07:16.01728Z'),
(5699, 'vocabulary', '貨物船', '貨物船', 'A <kanji>freight</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji>s <kanji>ship</kanji> is a boat that freights things. It''s a <vocabulary>freighter</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5699', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A8%E7%89%A9%E8%88%B9', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T18:34:50.562932Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(5700, 'vocabulary', '小麦粉', '小麦粉', '<kanji>Small</kanji> <kanji>wheat</kanji> <kanji>powder</kanji> is <vocabulary>wheat flour</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>flour</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5700', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E9%BA%A6%E7%B2%89', 33, 1, '2013-03-18T18:58:16.301883Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:21:06.048836Z'),
(5701, 'vocabulary', '怖がる', '怖がる', '怖い is "scary." This is a verb version, making it <vocabulary>to be scared</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be afraid of</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5701', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%96%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-03-18T19:01:01.537234Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.775094Z'),
(5702, 'vocabulary', '恐れる', '恐れる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>fear</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to fear</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5702', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%90%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-18T22:41:22.741278Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:22.906654Z'),
(5703, 'vocabulary', '迷わす', '迷わす', 'You learned 迷う means "to go astray," and 迷わす is its transitive version, meaning it takes a direct object. So it means <vocabulary>to lead someone astray</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to perplex someone</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to mislead someone</vocabulary>.

While 迷わす can mean literally causing someone to get lost, it''s more commonly used for the act of confusing someone — for example, overwhelming them with difficult choices or intentionally misleading them in a malicious way.

You know this verb is transitive because there''s nothing <reading>worse</reading> (わす) than purposefully misleading someone. Intentionally leading someone astray is worse than anything!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5703', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%B7%E3%82%8F%E3%81%99', 31, 1, '2013-03-18T22:45:46.371554Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:22.937032Z'),
(5704, 'vocabulary', '積む', '積む', 'You learned that 積もる (to accumulate) is used when something like snow or dust piles up over time. 積む is the transitive version, so it means <vocabulary>to pile something up</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to stack up</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to load</vocabulary>. You know this because you are the one <reading>mo</reading>ving (む) things to pile them up!

積む is often used for physical actions like stacking boxes or loading luggage, but it''s also used in more abstract ways, such as building up experience or skills. Think of it like "stacking up" experience points to level up in your life!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5704', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%8D%E3%82%80', 31, 1, '2013-03-18T22:47:48.996569Z', NULL, '2026-03-20T15:00:51.622565Z'),
(5705, 'vocabulary', '主催する', '主催する', 'If 主催 means "host" or "hosting," the verb version of that would be <vocabulary>to host</vocabulary>.

主催する refers to hosting events, especially formal ones like conferences, concerts, or company parties. It''s not just about "doing" an event — it shows that the person or group is the main sponsor and organizer, the one really in charge of making it happen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5705', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB%E5%82%AC%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-19T16:33:59.097049Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:22.996156Z'),
(5706, 'vocabulary', '催促する', '催促する', 'If 催促 is <kanji>urge</kanji>, then the verb version would be <vocabulary>to urge</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5706', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%AC%E4%BF%83%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 31, 1, '2013-03-19T16:36:24.483542Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.02291Z'),
(5707, 'vocabulary', '泥棒', '泥棒', 'I don''t know why a <kanji>mud</kanji> <kanji>pole</kanji> is a <vocabulary>robber</vocabulary>, but for some reason it is. Just imagine a robber covering himself in mud and standing behind a pole to hide, then jump out, then rob you of everything you''ve got.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5707', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A5%E6%A3%92', 33, 1, '2013-03-20T17:50:01.717832Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.694516Z'),
(5708, 'vocabulary', '似合う', '似合う', 'It <kanji>resemble</kanji>s you! It <kanji>suit</kanji>s you! Wow, your good looks really cause this scarf <vocabulary>to suit</vocabulary> you, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5708', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%BC%E5%90%88%E3%81%86', 32, 1, '2013-03-20T18:06:59.556688Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.058202Z'),
(5709, 'vocabulary', '驚かす', '驚かす', 'Remember how 驚く is "to be surprised"? This is when you are surprising someone else, doing the surprising, making this word <vocabulary>to surprise</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to shock</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5709', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A9%9A%E3%81%8B%E3%81%99', 33, 1, '2013-03-20T18:08:53.544307Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.815737Z'),
(5710, 'vocabulary', '掃除する', '掃除する', '掃除 is <kanji>cleaning</kanji>, so the verb version would be <vocabulary>to clean</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5710', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%83%E9%99%A4%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2013-03-21T17:14:56.434444Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:41:39.325074Z'),
(5711, 'vocabulary', '乾かす', '乾かす', '乾く is "to be dried." This is the version where you''re doing the drying, making this <vocabulary>to dry something</vocabulary>. You know this because it''s annoying to have to dry something yourself, so you <reading>cuss</reading> (かす) endlessly as you do it. "#$@%! Why do I have to waste my time drying this %&$# thing?!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5711', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%BE%E3%81%8B%E3%81%99', 33, 1, '2013-03-21T17:16:01.456564Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.853514Z'),
(5712, 'vocabulary', '散らかす', '散らかす', '散る is "to be scattered." 散らかす on the other hand is when you''re doing the scattering. So it means <vocabulary>to scatter</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to make a mess</vocabulary>.

You often hear it when someone talks about making a room messy or leaving things all over the place. You can remember it easily because after a <reading>ruckus</reading> (らかす), people often leave a mess behind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5712', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%A3%E3%82%89%E3%81%8B%E3%81%99', 33, 1, '2013-03-21T17:24:54.787939Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.867534Z'),
(5713, 'vocabulary', '離す', '離す', 'Remember how 離れる means "to separate," where something separates or moves away from something else? Well, 離す is the transitive version, so it takes a direct object and means <vocabulary>to separate something</vocabulary>. You can remember this because if you try to separate two people who don''t want to be separated, they might sue (す) you in response!

離す is used when you detach or put space between people or objects. This could be literal, like when you 離す your bed from the wall, or more figurative, like when you carelessly 離す your eyes from something important. 

It can also mean <vocabulary>to let go</vocabulary> of something, because you''re "separating" your hand from it. This use is very similar to 放す, but it doesn''t have that nuance of setting something free — it''s just the neutral act of letting go of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5713', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%A2%E3%81%99', 33, 1, '2013-03-21T17:25:49.350667Z', NULL, '2025-11-10T19:05:51.326532Z'),
(5714, 'vocabulary', '複数形', '複数形', 'If 複数 is <kanji>multiple</kanji>, and 形 is <kanji>shape</kanji>, then when something is in a "multiple shape," it has more than one version of itself. It''s the <vocabulary>plural</vocabulary>, or the <vocabulary>plural form</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5714', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A4%87%E6%95%B0%E5%BD%A2', 34, 1, '2013-03-22T17:33:18.490902Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:28:49.641004Z'),
(5715, 'vocabulary', '了解', '了解', 'When you''ve <kanji>finish</kanji>ed <kanji>solv</kanji>ing something, you''ve come to a point of <vocabulary>understanding</vocabulary>. 

了解 is often used to show your understanding of information or instructions that have been communicated to you, similar to "<vocabulary>Got it</vocabulary>" or "<vocabulary>Understood</vocabulary>." It can also be used to show agreement, approval, or consent, since those things naturally depend on mutual understanding too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5715', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%86%E8%A7%A3', 15, 1, '2013-03-22T18:54:57.650454Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.689961Z'),
(5716, 'vocabulary', '主', '主', 'The <kanji>master</kanji> is the <vocabulary>owner</vocabulary> who has dominance or control over something.

主 is usually used with other words to indicate a person who owns something or who is principally involved in an action. That might sound a little vague, so check out the collocations and context sentences to get a better grasp of this word!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5716', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB', 30, 1, '2013-03-25T17:24:21.492294Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.645918Z'),
(5717, 'vocabulary', '社会', '社会', 'Not to be confused with 会社, which is a company, this is <vocabulary>society</vocabulary>. When a <kanji>company</kanji> <kanji>meet</kanji>s together, it''s like a bunch of people, who are in groups, who come together to live in one world. That is a <vocabulary>society</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5717', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A4%BE%E4%BC%9A', 7, 1, '2013-03-27T18:28:18.61175Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.169638Z'),
(5718, 'vocabulary', '心臓', '心臓', 'The <kanji>heart</kanji> <kanji>internal organs</kanji> is the <vocabulary>heart</vocabulary>, of course.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5718', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83%E8%87%93', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:19:03.222798Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.256786Z'),
(5719, 'vocabulary', '肺臓', '肺臓', 'The <kanji>lung</kanji> <kanji>internal organ</kanji> is the <vocabulary>lung</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5719', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%BA%E8%87%93', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:19:08.373501Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.268553Z'),
(5720, 'vocabulary', '内臓', '内臓', '<kanji>Inside</kanji> <kanji>internal organs</kanji> are your <vocabulary>internal organs</vocabulary>, though you''d think the second kanji would give a hint to that without the 内.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5720', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%85%E8%87%93', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:19:14.596611Z', NULL, '2025-09-05T05:40:05.235761Z'),
(5721, 'vocabulary', '引っ越す', '引っ越す', '越す is "to go beyond." Now you''re going beyond your home and <kanji>pull</kanji>ing everything with you to go to another home. You are trying <vocabulary>to move</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>to move homes</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5721', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%95%E3%81%A3%E8%B6%8A%E3%81%99', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:19:20.671422Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.290885Z'),
(5722, 'vocabulary', '追い越す', '追い越す', '越す means "to go beyond." 追 is <kanji>follow</kanji>. If you are following someone, and then you go beyond them, you have overtaken them. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>to overtake</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to surpass</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5722', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%BD%E3%81%84%E8%B6%8A%E3%81%99', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:19:30.867486Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.302529Z'),
(5723, 'vocabulary', '祖父母', '祖父母', 'You know that 祖父 is grandfather and 祖母 is grandmother. This is both combined. Is it some hybrid grandmapa monster you''ve created in your lab? Hardly, it just refers to your <vocabulary>grandparents</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5723', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%96%E7%88%B6%E6%AF%8D', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:19:41.790615Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:01:24.564442Z'),
(5724, 'vocabulary', '飼い主', '飼い主', 'You know that to 飼う is "to keep a pet." If you''re the <kanji>master</kanji> that is <kanji>keeping a pet</kanji> (飼う), then you are the <vocabulary>pet owner</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5724', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%BC%E3%81%84%E4%B8%BB', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:20:14.905485Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.324543Z'),
(5725, 'vocabulary', '傷つく', '傷つく', 'Remember how 傷 is "wound"? Now a wound is つくing to you, or in other words, is getting attached to you. If you get a wound attached to your body, you are probably going <vocabulary>to get hurt</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5725', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%B7%E3%81%A4%E3%81%8F', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:20:19.950066Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.335332Z'),
(5726, 'vocabulary', '傷つける', '傷つける', 'You つける (attach) a <kanji>wound</kanji> to someone or something. In other words, this means <vocabulary>to hurt someone</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to scratch</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to damage</vocabulary> something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5726', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%B7%E3%81%A4%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:20:24.557225Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.355959Z'),
(5727, 'vocabulary', '使い捨て', '使い捨て', 'After you <kanji>use</kanji> it you <kanji>throw away</kanji> it, making whatever it is you used a <vocabulary>disposable</vocabulary> item.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5727', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%BF%E3%81%84%E6%8D%A8%E3%81%A6', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:20:30.901917Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:50:00.772162Z'),
(5728, 'vocabulary', '投げ捨てる', '投げ捨てる', 'You <kanji>throw</kanji> and <kanji>throw away</kanji> something. That''s why this means <vocabulary>to throw away</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5728', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%95%E3%81%92%E6%8D%A8%E3%81%A6%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:20:36.335886Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:50:00.772162Z'),
(5729, 'vocabulary', '込める', '込める', 'You know the kanji 込 means <kanji>into</kanji>. This verb is transitive, meaning you are doing the action. If you “do something into something” you are putting it into that thing. So this means <vocabulary>to put in</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to load something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5729', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%BC%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:20:42.718457Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.395128Z'),
(5730, 'vocabulary', '申し込む', '申し込む', 'I must <kanji>say humbly</kanji> that I want to get <kanji>into</kanji> this position, so I''m planning <vocabulary>to apply</vocabulary> for it and <vocabulary>to submit an application</vocabulary>.

申し込む isn''t just about applying, though. It''s about officially communicating desires, whether you want <vocabulary>to propose</vocabulary> marriage, challenge someone to a game of tennis, or even offer a donation to a good cause.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5730', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B3%E3%81%97%E8%BE%BC%E3%82%80', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:20:48.688358Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:14:57.717072Z'),
(5731, 'vocabulary', '飛び込み自殺-5731', '飛び込み自殺', 'To <kanji>fly</kanji> <kanji>into</kanji> something and "commit suicide" (自殺) is <vocabulary>suicide by train</vocabulary>. Unfortunately, this is a term you should probably be familiar with because it comes up from time to time on the news and around train stations. (Hopefully someday it will no longer be a term you need to know and we can remove it forever.)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5731', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9B%E3%81%B3%E8%BE%BC%E3%81%BF%E8%87%AA%E6%AE%BA-5731', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:20:55.230232Z', '2024-11-12T21:39:27.014673Z', '2025-06-11T05:33:23.416947Z'),
(5732, 'vocabulary', '書き込む', '書き込む', 'You know that 書く is "to write" and the kanji 込 is <kanji>into</kanji>, so this means <vocabulary>to write in</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to fill out</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to jot down</vocabulary>.

書き込む focuses more on the physical action of writing than 書く on its own. This is because 込む stresses that you’re putting words INTO some space, either on paper or on computer.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5732', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B8%E3%81%8D%E8%BE%BC%E3%82%80', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:21:00.057343Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T08:52:44.921166Z'),
(5733, 'vocabulary', '人込み', '人込み', 'When a <kanji>person</kanji> goes <kanji>into</kanji> a place (then another person goes into the place, and then another person and another person), that place eventually gets filled with a <vocabulary>crowd of people</vocabulary>.

Note that this word is more commonly written 人混み.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5733', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E8%BE%BC%E3%81%BF', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:21:08.673483Z', NULL, '2026-01-05T06:25:06.212479Z'),
(5734, 'vocabulary', '染み', '染み', 'This is the noun version of 染. A dye would seem like the right answer, but what else is a dye? If you get it on you when you don''t want to get it on you, it becomes a <vocabulary>stain</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5734', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%93%E3%81%BF', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:21:22.609229Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.459067Z'),
(5735, 'vocabulary', '染まる', '染まる', 'If 染める is "to dye," then this is <vocabulary>to be dyed</vocabulary>. You aren''t doing the dyeing, because you know that if you did it you would have done things right, unlike the other person who dyed your hair. This person <reading>marr</reading>ed (まる) it up really bad.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5735', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%93%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:21:28.959467Z', NULL, '2025-08-20T06:49:28.385256Z'),
(5736, 'vocabulary', '汚す', '汚す', '汚れる is "to get dirty." This is when you make something dirty, aka "<vocabulary>to make dirty</vocabulary>." You know that this is the version where you make things dirty yourself because when you make someone dirty with all that poop you''re flinging, they <reading>sue</reading> (す) you immediately. This happens a lot.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5736', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%9A%E3%81%99', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:21:36.750995Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T16:31:29.431343Z'),
(5737, 'vocabulary', '言い訳', '言い訳', 'You <kanji>say</kanji> a <kanji>reason</kanji> for your actions, but really it''s just an <vocabulary>excuse</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5737', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%80%E3%81%84%E8%A8%B3', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:21:43.796731Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.55119Z'),
(5738, 'vocabulary', '詰め込む', '詰め込む', 'You know that 詰める is "to stuff in" and the kanji 込 is <kanji>into</kanji>. When you’re stuffing something into something else, you are going <vocabulary>to stuff</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to cram</vocabulary> it in.

Just as in English, this word can also be used in a figurative sense, like cramming knowledge into your head.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5738', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%B0%E3%82%81%E8%BE%BC%E3%82%80', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:22:05.508097Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.563207Z'),
(5739, 'vocabulary', '歓迎', '歓迎', 'It''s my <kanji>delight</kanji> to <kanji>welcome</kanji> you, so I give you a big, warm <vocabulary>welcome</vocabulary>.

歓迎 is a relatively formal word that refers to warmly accepting someone in, like <vocabulary>welcoming</vocabulary> someone who''s arrived somewhere or joined a group.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5739', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%93%E8%BF%8E', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:22:13.931433Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.57569Z'),
(5740, 'vocabulary', '血液型', '血液型', 'Your <kanji>blood</kanji> <kanji>fluid</kanji> <kanji>type</kanji> is your <vocabulary>blood type</vocabulary>. This is a very important thing to know in Japan for some reason.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5740', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%80%E6%B6%B2%E5%9E%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:22:18.995103Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.58816Z'),
(5741, 'vocabulary', '興味がない', '興味がない', '興味 is <kanji>interest</kanji> in something. If you do not have (がない) that interest, then you have <vocabulary>no interest</vocabulary> or you''re <vocabulary>not interested</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5741', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%88%E5%91%B3%E3%81%8C%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:22:31.385786Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:20:50.221396Z'),
(5742, 'vocabulary', '賛成する', '賛成する', 'The 賛成 portion means "agreement", "approval", or "support", so the verb version would also be <vocabulary>to agree</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to approve</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to support</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5742', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%9B%E6%88%90%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:22:37.986366Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.616635Z'),
(5743, 'vocabulary', '誤解', '誤解', 'A <kanji>mistake</kanji> in what you <kanji>solve</kanji>... wow, sorry, I thought I was supposed to solve this, not that. What a terrible <vocabulary>misunderstanding</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5743', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%A4%E8%A7%A3', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:22:43.900864Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T20:23:40.067262Z'),
(5744, 'vocabulary', '誤字', '誤字', 'A <kanji>mistake</kanji> in <kanji>letter</kanji>s is a <vocabulary>typo</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5744', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%A4%E5%AD%97', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:22:50.012085Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.643448Z'),
(5745, 'vocabulary', '誤用', '誤用', 'A <kanji>mistake</kanji> in <kanji>use</kanji> is a <vocabulary>misuse</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5745', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%A4%E7%94%A8', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:22:55.998776Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.656183Z'),
(5746, 'vocabulary', '誤算', '誤算', 'A <kanji>mistake</kanji> in what you <kanji>calculate</kanji> is a <vocabulary>miscalculation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5746', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%A4%E7%AE%97', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:23:00.963864Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T15:37:55.121141Z'),
(5747, 'vocabulary', '貴い', '貴い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. This adjective describes things that are <kanji>valuable</kanji> in a way that transcends monetary worth, so it means <vocabulary>noble</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>invaluable</vocabulary>.

While 尊い is used for things you honor from the heart, 貴い leans toward a dignified, noble kind of value — the kind tied to high status or social rank. Because of that, you may encounter 貴い in stories about historical class systems or old social hierarchies. It doesn''t appear as commonly as 尊い in modern Japanese, but you may still see it in descriptions of someone''s noble presence or the sense of dignity they give off.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5747', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%B4%E3%81%84', 36, 1, '2013-04-05T18:23:09.027874Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T06:27:32.779088Z'),
(5748, 'vocabulary', '兄貴', '兄貴', 'The <kanji>older brother</kanji> is <kanji>valuable</kanji> to you. But, no matter how valuable he is to you, he''s still just your <vocabulary>older brother</vocabulary>. 

P.S. You''ll often see some gangstah thug call a higher ranking gangstah thug (one that acts like an older brother to all the other gangstah thugs) this. It''s like family within the gang.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5748', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%84%E8%B2%B4', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:23:16.976136Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:33:29.386153Z'),
(5749, 'vocabulary', '貴様', '貴様', 'A <kanji>valuable</kanji> <kanji>formal name ender</kanji>. It''s like saying "the valuable great you"... or something. Anyways, this means <vocabulary>you</vocabulary>, but it isn''t as nice as it sounds.

貴様 used to be a polite way to say "you," but in modern Japanese it''s almost always a strong insult, roughly like saying <vocabulary>you bastard</vocabulary>. The tone is dramatic, so you''ll hear it from anime villains, but not usually in real conversation unless someone''s joking around and pretending to be Vegeta from Dragon Ball.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5749', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%B4%E6%A7%98', 37, 1, '2013-04-05T18:23:24.48959Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T06:27:32.779088Z'),
(5750, 'vocabulary', '貴族', '貴族', 'People of a <kanji>valuable</kanji> <kanji>tribe</kanji> are part of <vocabulary>the nobility</vocabulary>. Unlike our tribes, they are valuable.

貴族 refers to a noble or <vocabulary>aristocrat</vocabulary> in old social systems, so you mostly see it in history books or fantasy settings. In modern Japanese, people sometimes use it jokingly when a person does something very luxurious or indulgent — as if they''re living like an aristocrat for a moment.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5750', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%B4%E6%97%8F', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T18:23:30.302649Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T06:27:32.779088Z'),
(5751, 'vocabulary', '降る', '降る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>descend</kanji>, but this word means something closer to <vocabulary>to fall</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to come down</vocabulary>.

降る is commonly used when talking about the weather, like 雨が降る (it rains) or 雪が降る (it snows). It also applies to other things falling from above, like 火山灰が降る (volcanic ash falls) or even figuratively, as in 幸運が降ってくる (good luck comes your way).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5751', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%8D%E3%82%8B', 28, 1, '2013-04-05T18:23:40.174606Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.31012Z'),
(5752, 'vocabulary', '降参', '降参', 'I am going to <kanji>descend</kanji> my <kanji>participat</kanji>ion in this conflict. I am going to descend all my weapons to the ground. I am going to <vocabulary>surrender</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5752', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%8D%E5%8F%82', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:23:45.300823Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T21:46:55.985219Z'),
(5753, 'vocabulary', '降車', '降車', '<kanji>Descend</kanji> off the (train) <kanji>car</kanji>. You are <vocabulary>getting off</vocabulary> a train, or a bus, or anything else with wheels.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5753', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%8D%E8%BB%8A', 22, 1, '2013-04-05T18:23:50.335072Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.787283Z'),
(5754, 'vocabulary', '牛丼', '牛丼', 'A <kanji>cow</kanji> <kanji>rice bowl</kanji> is a bowl of rice covered in beef, making it a <vocabulary>beef bowl</vocabulary>. Go get some Yoshinoya.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5754', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%9B%E4%B8%BC', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:23:59.759764Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.800169Z'),
(5755, 'vocabulary', 'うなぎ丼', 'うなぎ丼', 'うなぎ means "eel," so うなぎ丼 is an eel <kanji>rice bowl</kanji>, aka an <vocabulary>eel bowl</vocabulary>. And yes, it''s very good.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5755', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%86%E3%81%AA%E3%81%8E%E4%B8%BC', 36, 1, '2013-04-05T18:24:06.726128Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:18.466501Z'),
(5756, 'vocabulary', '丼', '丼', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

To be exact, 丼 refers to a chinaware bowl that''s bigger and sturdier than your ordinary <vocabulary>rice bowl</vocabulary>. It''s often used for one-bowl dishes like katsudon, gyūdon, poke, but also noodle soups like ramen!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5756', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BC', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:24:21.727647Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T18:19:33.916606Z'),
(5757, 'vocabulary', '吐く', '吐く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>throw up</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to throw up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to puke</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to spit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5757', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%90%E3%81%8F', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:24:27.382239Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:17:12.930062Z'),
(5758, 'vocabulary', '奴', '奴', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5758', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%B4', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:24:44.723919Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.866593Z'),
(5759, 'vocabulary', '奴隷', '奴隷', 'Some <kanji>dude</kanji> who is a <kanji>slave</kanji> is just a <vocabulary>slave</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5759', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%B4%E9%9A%B7', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:24:59.890279Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.880588Z'),
(5760, 'vocabulary', '芋', '芋', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5760', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%8B', 19, 1, '2013-04-05T18:25:04.60203Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.907822Z'),
(5761, 'vocabulary', '焼き芋', '焼き芋', '<kanji>Bake</kanji>d <kanji>potato</kanji> is actually <vocabulary>baked sweet potato</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>roasted sweet potato</vocabulary>). Although it''s literally just potato, most Japanese speakers will automatically think of sweet potato when they hear this word. Yum! 🍠 ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5761', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%BC%E3%81%8D%E8%8A%8B', 19, 1, '2013-04-05T18:25:14.900157Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.934877Z'),
(5762, 'vocabulary', 'じゃが芋', 'じゃが芋', 'A じゃが <kanji>potato</kanji> is a <vocabulary>potato</vocabulary>. More specifically, a <vocabulary>white potato</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5762', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%98%E3%82%83%E3%81%8C%E8%8A%8B', 19, 1, '2013-04-05T18:25:27.759018Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.962221Z'),
(5763, 'vocabulary', '縮まる', '縮まる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>shrink</kanji> so the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to shrink</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5763', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%AE%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:25:38.672274Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.976923Z'),
(5764, 'vocabulary', '縮小', '縮小', '<kanji>Shrink</kanji> what you have into something <kanji>small</kanji>. It was big, now it''s small. This is a <vocabulary>reduction</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5764', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%AE%E5%B0%8F', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:25:55.922187Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:23.989871Z'),
(5765, 'vocabulary', '単純', '単純', '<kanji>Simple</kanji> and <kanji>pure</kanji>. This is <vocabulary>simple</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5765', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%98%E7%B4%94', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:26:00.922559Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:22:41.279055Z'),
(5766, 'vocabulary', '不純', '不純', '<kanji>Not</kanji> <kanji>pure</kanji> things are <vocabulary>impure</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5766', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E7%B4%94', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:26:08.10393Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:22:41.279055Z'),
(5767, 'vocabulary', '純毛', '純毛', 'Something that is <kanji>pure</kanji> <kanji>fur</kanji> is pure fur... but what kind of fur? In this case it''s wool, making this mean <vocabulary>all wool</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5767', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%94%E6%AF%9B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:26:13.133697Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:22:41.279055Z'),
(5768, 'vocabulary', '縦', '縦', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5768', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%A6', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:26:17.61447Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.043479Z'),
(5769, 'vocabulary', '縦書', '縦書', '<kanji>Vertical</kanji> <kanji>writing</kanji> is <vocabulary>vertical writing</vocabulary>. That''s when you see Japanese and it''s written top to bottom instead of sideways.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5769', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%A6%E6%9B%B8', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:26:22.136038Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T03:17:19.160132Z'),
(5770, 'vocabulary', '縦横', '縦横', '<kanji>Vertical</kanji> and <kanji>side</kanji> (horizontal). That means you have the <vocabulary>length and width</vocabulary> and the <vocabulary>vertical and horizontal</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5770', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%A6%E6%A8%AA', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:26:26.811285Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.069629Z'),
(5771, 'vocabulary', '純粋', '純粋', '<kanji>Pure</kanji> plus 粋, which you might remember as "stylish" but which can mean <kanji>pure</kanji> as well. Wow, that''s double the purity for this word. No wonder it also means <vocabulary>pure</vocabulary>! 

純粋 is usually for pure-hearted people or feelings, like 純粋な心 (a pure heart) or 純粋な気持ち (pure feelings). You can also say 純粋な疑問 — for <vocabulary>genuine</vocabulary> doubt or innocent wondering that just popped into your head, purely out of curiosity, with no hidden motives.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5771', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%94%E7%B2%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:26:32.081894Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:22:41.279055Z'),
(5772, 'vocabulary', '粋', '粋', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

粋 means <vocabulary>stylish</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>chic</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>tasteful</vocabulary>, but with a nuance of understated elegance or refined coolness. For example, arranging something behind the scenes so things go beautifully might be regarded as 粋 — a <vocabulary>classy</vocabulary> gesture done with style!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5772', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:26:36.545608Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T23:57:06.766482Z'),
(5773, 'vocabulary', '神聖', '神聖', '<kanji>God</kanji> is <kanji>holy</kanji>. Everything about a holy god is full of <vocabulary>holiness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5773', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%9E%E8%81%96', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:26:41.818325Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.106335Z'),
(5774, 'vocabulary', '聖書', '聖書', 'The <kanji>holy</kanji> <kanji>writing</kanji> is the <vocabulary>Bible</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5774', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%81%96%E6%9B%B8', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:26:46.606951Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.118263Z'),
(5775, 'vocabulary', '聖日-5775', '聖日', 'A <kanji>holy</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji> is a <vocabulary>holy day</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5775', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%81%96%E6%97%A5-5775', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:26:51.864541Z', '2021-09-09T14:23:34.879745Z', '2025-06-11T05:33:24.12955Z'),
(5776, 'vocabulary', '聖地', '聖地', '<kanji>Holy</kanji> <kanji>ground</kanji> is <vocabulary>holy land</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5776', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%81%96%E5%9C%B0', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:26:59.125446Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.140942Z'),
(5777, 'vocabulary', '磁気', '磁気', '<kanji>Magnet</kanji>ic <kanji>energy</kanji> is when <vocabulary>magnetism</vocabulary> occurs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5777', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A3%81%E6%B0%97', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:27:04.44619Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(5778, 'vocabulary', '磁石', '磁石', 'A <kanji>magnet</kanji> <kanji>stone</kanji> is a <vocabulary>magnet</vocabulary>. Magnets look like little stones, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5778', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A3%81%E7%9F%B3', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:27:10.603337Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.164432Z'),
(5779, 'vocabulary', '磁場', '磁場', 'A <kanji>magnet</kanji> <kanji>location</kanji> is an area with a <vocabulary>magnetic field</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5779', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A3%81%E5%A0%B4', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:27:16.121323Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.175524Z'),
(5780, 'vocabulary', '紅', '紅', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same, so this also means <vocabulary>deep red</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>crimson</vocabulary>. Additionally, it can also mean <vocabulary>rouge</vocabulary>, usually in reference to the cosmetic.

紅 is also a bit of an old-fashioned and somewhat literary word, so keep that in mind!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5780', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%85', 39, 1, '2013-04-05T18:27:21.448001Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:15:46.032389Z'),
(5781, 'vocabulary', '紅茶', '紅茶', 'A <kanji>deep red</kanji> <kanji>tea</kanji> isn''t a deep red tea... it''s a <vocabulary>black tea</vocabulary>. If you think about it, a lot of black tea is really deep red, which is why a deep red tea is a black tea instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5781', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%85%E8%8C%B6', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:27:31.418145Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:16:41.901026Z'),
(5782, 'vocabulary', '注射', '注射', 'When you <kanji>shoot</kanji> something into someone that you actually have to <kanji>pour</kanji> (aka a liquid), you''re shooting them up with a needle, giving them a <vocabulary>shot</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>injection</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5782', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A8%E5%B0%84', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:27:37.619332Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.209932Z'),
(5783, 'vocabulary', '反射', '反射', 'You <kanji>shoot</kanji> yourself at a mirror, and it is the <kanji>anti</kanji>-you. It is your <vocabulary>reflection</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5783', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8D%E5%B0%84', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:27:42.682928Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:30:02.229578Z'),
(5784, 'vocabulary', '発射する', '発射する', '<kanji>Departure</kanji> and <kanji>shoot</kanji> something off. If you do that to something, you are going <vocabulary>to launch</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5784', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BA%E5%B0%84%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:28:11.22748Z', NULL, '2025-12-24T23:53:55.147205Z'),
(5785, 'vocabulary', '放射', '放射', 'It <kanji>release</kanji>s and <kanji>shoot</kanji>s out of the bomb. It''s not fire. It''s <vocabulary>radiation</vocabulary>, and it sticks around a long time once it has been released.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5785', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BE%E5%B0%84', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:28:15.774775Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T02:08:38.914766Z'),
(5786, 'vocabulary', '幕', '幕', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5786', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%95', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:28:20.684464Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:25:18.136405Z'),
(5787, 'vocabulary', '字幕', '字幕', 'A <kanji>curtain</kanji> of <kanji>letter</kanji>s that goes on your screen are <vocabulary>subtitles</vocabulary>. Sometimes they have black bar backgrounds too, which are like curtains!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5787', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%97%E5%B9%95', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:28:27.728969Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:25:18.136405Z'),
(5788, 'vocabulary', '黒幕', '黒幕', 'Just imagine a <kanji>black</kanji> <kanji>curtain</kanji> off in the background. You don''t know what''s behind it, but you know what''s in front of it. Thing is, the thing in front of the curtain is controlled by whoever is behind the black curtain. This person is the <vocabulary>wirepuller</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>mastermind</vocabulary> of the situation. Like the Wizard of Oz, or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5788', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BB%92%E5%B9%95', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:28:36.238639Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:25:18.136405Z'),
(5789, 'vocabulary', '拝む', '拝む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>worship</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to worship</vocabulary>.

拝む generally means <vocabulary>to pray to</vocabulary> a deity, spirit, or revered object while assuming a worshipping position, such as bowing or clasping one''s hands together in prayer. Additionally, 拝む can also mean <vocabulary>to see</vocabulary> with a sense of respect and reverence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5789', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%9D%E3%82%80', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:28:44.033149Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.292626Z'),
(5790, 'vocabulary', '拝見する', '拝見する', 'You <kanji>worship</kanji> only the things you <kanji>see</kanji>. So, in order to see if something is worth worshiping, you''ll go <vocabulary>to have a look at</vocabulary> that thing.

And just so you know, this is a very formal word used when you''re trying to be polite. So keep that in mind, please!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5790', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%9D%E8%A6%8B%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:30:49.115895Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.3046Z'),
(5791, 'vocabulary', '推定', '推定', 'You’re doing a bit of <kanji>infer</kanji>ring while you <kanji>determine</kanji> something, which means you’re not 100% sure, making this an <vocabulary>estimation</vocabulary>.

推定 refers to an estimation that’s generally based on some evidence or reasoning. You’ll often find this used in statistical or scientific contexts, where it refers to an educated guess or estimation based on available data.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5791', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A8%E5%AE%9A', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:31:05.995405Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(5792, 'vocabulary', '推理', '推理', 'To <kanji>infer</kanji> <kanji>reason</kanji> is to have <vocabulary>reasoning</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5792', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A8%E7%90%86', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:31:10.797382Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:26:11.344562Z'),
(5793, 'vocabulary', '推薦', '推薦', 'To <kanji>infer</kanji> and <kanji>recommend</kanji> something is to give a <vocabulary>recommendation</vocabulary>. What do you recommend?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5793', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A8%E8%96%A6', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:31:15.665Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:26:11.344562Z'),
(5794, 'vocabulary', '自薦', '自薦', 'As for my<kanji>self</kanji>? I <kanji>recommend</kanji> this product. It is a <vocabulary>self-recommendation</vocabulary>. You make it of yourself!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5794', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E8%96%A6', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:31:21.60894Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:18.624159Z'),
(5795, 'vocabulary', '薦める', '薦める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>recommend</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to recommend</vocabulary>. This word is often used in a positive context for recommending things, actions, and people by implying or pointing out their charms, merits, or both.

Although 薦める and 勧める are similar, there are subtle differences between the two. 勧める is often used to suggest that someone perform a specific action, often as friendly advice or encouragement. It can also be used when offering something, such as a drink. On the other hand, 薦める is used to recommend a person or thing as suitable for a particular situation, with a focus on endorsing the subject rather than a specific action.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5795', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%A6%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:31:32.545707Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.388879Z'),
(5796, 'vocabulary', '発揮する', '発揮する', 'You cause the <kanji>departure</kanji> of something in you (to the outside), then <kanji>brandish</kanji> it for all to see. You''re sending it out to show everyone. That''s why this means <vocabulary>to exhibit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to demonstrate</vocabulary>.

発揮する means <vocabulary>to bring out</vocabulary> one''s abilities, strengths, or qualities in a visible or effective way. It''s often used when someone performs well under pressure or maximizes their potential in a given situation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5796', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BA%E6%8F%AE%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:31:39.35533Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:27:49.36217Z'),
(5797, 'vocabulary', '指揮', '指揮', 'Take your <kanji>finger</kanji> and <kanji>brandish</kanji> it to <vocabulary>command</vocabulary> the troops. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5797', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87%E6%8F%AE', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:31:46.646438Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:27:49.36217Z'),
(5798, 'vocabulary', '沿岸', '沿岸', 'The <kanji>run alongside</kanji> <kanji>coast</kanji> basically just describes the <vocabulary>coast</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5798', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%BF%E5%B2%B8', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:31:54.331315Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.424464Z'),
(5799, 'vocabulary', '沿海', '沿海', 'The <kanji>run alongside</kanji> <kanji>sea</kanji> refers to the area along the coast. It can be either the sea that runs alongside the land or the land that runs alongside the sea, making it the <vocabulary>coastal waters</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>coastline</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5799', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%BF%E6%B5%B7', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:32:04.756134Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T23:02:03.205024Z'),
(5800, 'vocabulary', '沿線', '沿線', 'To <kanji>run alongside</kanji> the <kanji>line</kanji> (referring to the train line) means <vocabulary>along the train line</vocabulary>. What''s along the train line? All kinds of things if you look carefully.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5800', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%BF%E7%B7%9A', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:32:11.046231Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:30:42.218788Z'),
(5801, 'vocabulary', '沿う', '沿う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>run alongside</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to run alongside</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5801', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%BF%E3%81%86', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:32:17.415145Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.461683Z'),
(5802, 'vocabulary', '源', '源', 'The <kanji>origin</kanji> is the <vocabulary>origin</vocabulary>, but it also refers to the <vocabulary>ground spring</vocabulary>s, where the origin of water comes from.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5802', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%90', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:32:22.617446Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:30:31.665871Z'),
(5803, 'vocabulary', '源氏', '源氏', 'The <kanji>origin</kanji> <kanji>family name</kanji> is the original dude. The original dude comes from the original and first novel ever written, the Tale of <kanji>Genji</kanji>. These kanji represent <vocabulary>Genji</vocabulary>, good ol'' Genji. Perhaps hopping over to the reading will help you to know the meaning of this kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5803', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%90%E6%B0%8F', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:32:33.643917Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:30:31.665871Z'),
(5804, 'vocabulary', '電源', '電源', 'The <kanji>electricity</kanji> <kanji>origin</kanji> is where the electricity comes from. It is the <vocabulary>power source</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5804', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E6%BA%90', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:32:42.315391Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:30:31.665871Z'),
(5805, 'vocabulary', '震源地', '震源地', 'The <kanji>earthquake</kanji> <kanji>origin</kanji> <kanji>ground</kanji> is where the earthquake started from. It is the <vocabulary>epicenter</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5805', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%87%E6%BA%90%E5%9C%B0', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:32:48.208875Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:30:31.665871Z'),
(5806, 'vocabulary', '劇', '劇', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well (<vocabulary>drama</vocabulary>). Can also mean <vocabulary>play</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>show</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5806', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%87', 17, 1, '2013-04-05T18:32:56.40179Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.537138Z'),
(5807, 'vocabulary', '喜劇', '喜劇', '<kanji>Rejoice</kanji> <kanji>drama</kanji> isn''t so much drama as it is <vocabulary>comedy</vocabulary>, since it makes you rejoice.

喜劇 is a relatively formal word that refers to comedy in a more traditional or stage-theater sense. For general comedies, people often say お笑い or コメディ.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5807', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%9C%E5%8A%87', 17, 1, '2013-04-05T18:33:02.127763Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.559249Z'),
(5808, 'vocabulary', '悲劇', '悲劇', 'A <kanji>sad</kanji> <kanji>drama</kanji> always ends in <vocabulary>tragedy</vocabulary>. That''s what makes it sad.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5808', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%B2%E5%8A%87', 17, 1, '2013-04-05T18:33:08.361251Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.580463Z'),
(5809, 'vocabulary', '劇的', '劇的', 'Something that is <kanji>drama</kanji> <kanji>al</kanji> is <vocabulary>dramatic</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5809', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%87%E7%9A%84', 17, 1, '2013-04-05T18:33:13.057327Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.603718Z'),
(5810, 'vocabulary', '劇団', '劇団', 'A <kanji>drama</kanji> <kanji>group</kanji> is a <vocabulary>drama troupe</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>theatrical company</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5810', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%87%E5%9B%A3', 19, 1, '2013-04-05T18:33:18.089254Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.625784Z'),
(5811, 'vocabulary', '劇場', '劇場', 'A <kanji>drama</kanji> <kanji>location</kanji> is where people do drama. This is a <vocabulary>theater</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5811', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%87%E5%A0%B4', 17, 1, '2013-04-05T18:33:23.836268Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.648057Z'),
(5812, 'vocabulary', '歌劇', '歌劇', 'A <kanji>song</kanji> <kanji>drama</kanji>. Ugh, I hate these. They sing so much and they''re so dramatic. This is an <vocabulary>opera</vocabulary>.

歌劇 is a formal word for opera that is typically used in theatrical or artistic contexts. In everyday speech, people usually just say オペラ.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5812', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%8C%E5%8A%87', 17, 1, '2013-04-05T18:33:30.679734Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:03:31.014085Z'),
(5813, 'vocabulary', '勤める', '勤める', 'The kanji is <kanji>work</kanji> which means this verb has something to do with working. It''s not straight up "to work," instead it''s "<vocabulary>to work for</vocabulary>" or "<vocabulary>to be employed at</vocabulary>."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5813', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%A4%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:33:37.697918Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.681583Z'),
(5814, 'vocabulary', '通勤', '通勤', 'The things you <kanji>pass through</kanji> on your way to <kanji>work</kanji> are the things you see and run into during your <vocabulary>commute</vocabulary>.

Note that you can only use this word for commuting to work. You don’t use it to go to school unless you work at a school or something!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5814', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%9A%E5%8B%A4', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:33:50.261391Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.69584Z'),
(5815, 'vocabulary', '常勤', '常勤', '<kanji>Normal</kanji> <kanji>work</kanji> is work you do all day long, 8-hours a day, 5 days a week. That is <vocabulary>full time employment</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>full time</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5815', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%B8%E5%8B%A4', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:33:57.042076Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.713349Z'),
(5816, 'vocabulary', '承る', '承る', 'Giving <kanji>consent</kanji> to someone is similar to humbly listening to and accepting their words. So this word means <vocabulary>to humbly hear</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to accept</vocabulary>.

承る is a humble version of 聞く. Its past form 承りました is commonly used in professional settings to respectfully acknowledge and accept requests, information, or tasks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5816', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%BF%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:34:03.161437Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.72926Z'),
(5817, 'vocabulary', '承知', '承知', 'You <kanji>consent</kanji> and <kanji>know</kanji> everything that''s going on. You <vocabulary>consent</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>know</vocabulary> about all this. You''re <vocabulary>aware</vocabulary> of it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5817', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%BF%E7%9F%A5', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:35:20.645015Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T17:50:55.659831Z'),
(5818, 'vocabulary', '承認', '承認', 'I <kanji>recognize</kanji> your <kanji>consent</kanji>. It is an <vocabulary>approval</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>recognition</vocabulary> of what I''m doing. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5818', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%BF%E8%AA%8D', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:35:27.989742Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.785483Z'),
(5819, 'vocabulary', '歓楽街', '歓楽街', 'You''ll find <kanji>delight</kanji> and <kanji>comfort</kanji> in these <kanji>streets</kanji>, because you have entered an <vocabulary>entertainment district</vocabulary>! 

歓楽街 refers to an area in a city where many nightlife entertainment venues, like bars, dance clubs, host or hostess clubs, and sometimes businesses offering sketchy red-light adult services, are clustered together.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5819', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%93%E6%A5%BD%E8%A1%97', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:36:07.812725Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T18:49:45.773585Z'),
(5820, 'vocabulary', '損なう', '損なう', 'To do <kanji>loss</kanji> to something means you are doing something to make something have loss. This is <vocabulary>to harm</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to injure</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5820', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%8D%E3%81%AA%E3%81%86', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:36:14.051124Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.823302Z'),
(5821, 'vocabulary', '損', '損', 'A <kanji>loss</kanji> will give you a <vocabulary>disadvantage</vocabulary>. So, try not to get any losses... or you''ll be at a disadvantage.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5821', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%8D', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:36:19.886881Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.834825Z'),
(5822, 'vocabulary', '損害', '損害', 'Your <kanji>loss</kanji> comes from <kanji>damage</kanji> done to you. This is <vocabulary>damage</vocabulary>.

This word is usually used as a noun. It can occasionally be turned into verb by adding する, but this is very rare!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5822', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%8D%E5%AE%B3', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:36:30.29666Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(5823, 'vocabulary', '大損', '大損', 'A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>loss</kanji> is a <vocabulary>heavy loss</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5823', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E6%90%8D', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:36:41.969947Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(5824, 'vocabulary', '全損', '全損', 'An <kanji>all</kanji> <kanji>loss</kanji> is when you lose it all. It is a <vocabulary>total loss</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5824', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E6%90%8D', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:36:50.912229Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:47:49.244838Z'),
(5825, 'vocabulary', '枝', '枝', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5825', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%9D', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:36:56.455075Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.885053Z'),
(5826, 'vocabulary', '豆', '豆', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5826', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B1%86', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:37:07.747939Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.898235Z'),
(5827, 'vocabulary', '小豆', '小豆', '<kanji>Small</kanji> <kanji>bean</kanji>s refer to a type of bean known as <vocabulary>azuki bean</vocabulary>s. It will help if you''ve heard of this delicious popular bean, because there''s not really anything effective we can do with the meaning mnemonic from this point on. Just know that small beans are azuki beans.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5827', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E8%B1%86', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:37:13.468421Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.909813Z'),
(5828, 'vocabulary', '納豆', '納豆', 'A <kanji>supply</kanji> of <kanji>bean</kanji>s is when you take a bunch of beans, mash them up into a supply, and then ferment them all together to make them into <vocabulary>natto</vocabulary> (also known as <vocabulary>fermented soybeans</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5828', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%8D%E8%B1%86', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:37:20.107505Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.921427Z'),
(5829, 'vocabulary', '腐る', '腐る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>rot</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to rot</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5829', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%90%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:37:27.019583Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:33:00.426254Z'),
(5830, 'vocabulary', '腐食', '腐食', 'When something <kanji>rot</kanji>s and <kanji>eat</kanji>s away at something, that is <vocabulary>corrosion</vocabulary>. Not a good thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5830', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%90%E9%A3%9F', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:37:33.368691Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:33:00.426254Z'),
(5831, 'vocabulary', '腐敗', '腐敗', '<kanji>Rot</kanji> and <kanji>failure</kanji> lead to <vocabulary>decomposition</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>rotting</vocabulary>. This word also has the related meaning of  <vocabulary>corruption</vocabulary>, similar to how "rotten" can mean "corrupt" in English.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5831', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%90%E6%95%97', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:37:38.250554Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:33:00.426254Z'),
(5832, 'vocabulary', '爪', '爪', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well, though it adds a few more meanings as well, like <vocabulary>nail</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>talon</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>hoof</vocabulary> or really any hard thing on the ends of fingers or feet.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5832', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%88%AA', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:37:43.601112Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.987864Z'),
(5833, 'vocabulary', '爪切り', '爪切り', 'When you <kanji>cut</kanji> your <kanji>claw</kanji> you use a <vocabulary>nail clipper</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5833', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%88%AA%E5%88%87%E3%82%8A', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:37:49.211881Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:24.999842Z'),
(5834, 'vocabulary', '刻む', '刻む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>carve</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to carve</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5834', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%BB%E3%82%80', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:37:55.792319Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.012024Z'),
(5835, 'vocabulary', '時刻表', '時刻表', 'If you take the <kanji>time</kanji> and <kanji>carve</kanji> it onto a <kanji>surface</kanji>, then you''ve made a <vocabulary>timetable</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>schedule</vocabulary>.

時刻表 generally refers to a timetable or schedule for transportation, such as trains, buses, or flights.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5835', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%82%E5%88%BB%E8%A1%A8', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:38:01.816109Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:26:46.193094Z'),
(5836, 'vocabulary', '深刻', '深刻', 'A <kanji>deep</kanji> <kanji>carve</kanji> is very <vocabulary>serious</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>grave</vocabulary>. Just imagine getting cut with a sword. If the carve is deep, well, that''s serious.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5836', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%B1%E5%88%BB', 34, 1, '2013-04-05T18:38:07.459754Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.035641Z'),
(5837, 'vocabulary', '閉じる', '閉じる', 'Another verb version of the kanji 閉! This is a special one that can be either transitive or intransitive, so 閉じる means <vocabulary>to close something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to close</vocabulary>.

閉じる is for returning things that have expanded open back to their closed state. It''s used for books, umbrellas, web browsers, and even eyes and mouths. There are some overlaps with 閉める (you can 閉じる or 閉める a gate, for example) but 閉める is mostly used for things like doors, windows, and curtains.
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5837', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%89%E3%81%98%E3%82%8B', 20, 1, '2013-04-05T19:11:51.696275Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.058302Z'),
(5838, 'vocabulary', '一筋', '一筋', 'You have <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>muscle</kanji>, therefore it only does one thing. That''s it. It is <vocabulary>single mindedly</vocabulary> doing that one thing. It is <vocabulary>intently</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>earnestly</vocabulary> doing that one thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5838', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E7%AD%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:11:57.783493Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.070678Z'),
(5839, 'vocabulary', '垂れる', '垂れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>droop</kanji>, so the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to droop</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to hang down</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to drip</vocabulary>. So you can use 垂れる to describe your dog''s floppy ears hanging down or your ice cream’s melty goodness dripping away.

垂れる is generally intransitive, but it can also take a direct object in specific expressions, like 説教を垂れる (to give a preachy lecture), こうべを垂れる (to bow one''s head in humility), or the crude slang クソを垂れる (to drop a load).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5839', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9E%82%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 28, 1, '2013-04-05T19:12:26.993884Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T07:38:44.489525Z'),
(5840, 'vocabulary', '宣言する', '宣言する', 'If 宣言 is a "proclamation," then the verb version will be <vocabulary>to proclaim</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5840', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%A3%E8%A8%80%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:12:32.808238Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.10889Z'),
(5841, 'vocabulary', '盛り上げる', '盛り上げる', '盛る is "to heap up." If you "raise" (上げる) what you heap up, well, that comes with heaping something up, making this word <vocabulary>to heap up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to pile up</vocabulary>. This word is also used when you want <vocabulary>to liven up</vocabulary> some things. Heap up that energy and stir things up!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5841', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%9B%E3%82%8A%E4%B8%8A%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:12:49.942008Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(5842, 'vocabulary', '裏切り者', '裏切り者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> who backstabs (裏切り) someone is a <vocabulary>traitor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5842', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%8F%E5%88%87%E3%82%8A%E8%80%85', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:12:55.995185Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(5843, 'vocabulary', '諸々', '諸々', 'You have <kanji>various</kanji> <kanji>various</kanji> things. They are <vocabulary>various</vocabulary>. You have <vocabulary>all kinds of</vocabulary> them. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5843', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%B8%E3%80%85', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:13:00.752411Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(5844, 'vocabulary', '三つ編み', '三つ編み', '編む is "to knit." When you knit in <kanji>three</kanji>s, you are knitting in a braid style. What do you braid? Hair. That makes this <vocabulary>braided hair</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5844', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E3%81%A4%E7%B7%A8%E3%81%BF', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:13:07.58234Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.222361Z'),
(5845, 'vocabulary', '閉まる', '閉まる', 'If 閉める is "to close something" or "to shut something," then this is going to be the version where you don''t do anything and something closes on its own. That''s why this is <vocabulary>to close</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to shut</vocabulary>. You remember this because the door keeps shutting on your hand, <reading>marr</reading>ing (まる) it over and over again. Ouch.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5845', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%89%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2013-04-05T19:13:18.436645Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.278951Z'),
(5846, 'vocabulary', '宝くじ', '宝くじ', '宝 is a <kanji>treasure</kanji>. くじ is a lottery. So if you have a treasure lottery (you win treasure if you win the lottery) then you have yourself a <vocabulary>lottery</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5846', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9D%E3%81%8F%E3%81%98', 6, 1, '2013-04-05T19:13:25.386159Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.217909Z'),
(5847, 'vocabulary', '納まる', '納まる', 'If 納める is "to supply" or "to deliver," then this word is when things have been delivered: so it''s <vocabulary>to settle into</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be satisfied</vocabulary>. 

納まる means something or someone has found its right place and settled there. It often implies that there was a prior state of uncertainty or dissatisfaction, but now everything is in its right place.

Like most verbs with まる as okurigana, this is an intransitive verb. That means it happens by itself, with no emphasis on the person or thing that''s making something settle or be satisfied.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5847', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%8D%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:13:33.688028Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.323446Z'),
(5848, 'vocabulary', '納得', '納得', 'I will <kanji>supply</kanji> and you will <kanji>acquire</kanji>. Does that sound good? Do I have your <vocabulary>consent</vocabulary>? I can see that you''re <vocabulary>understanding</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>being convinced</vocabulary>. You''re planning on <vocabulary>accepting</vocabulary> my proposition, aren''t you?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5848', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%8D%E5%BE%97', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:13:41.521653Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.358472Z'),
(5849, 'vocabulary', '否定形', '否定形', '否定 is denial. Remember how 形 sometimes refers to grammar tense / type? Well, with a denial type grammar, you have a <vocabulary>negative form</vocabulary>, making it a <vocabulary>negative sentence</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5849', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%A6%E5%AE%9A%E5%BD%A2', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:13:53.52288Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(5850, 'vocabulary', '否定する', '否定する', 'If 否定 is "denial," then the verb version would be <vocabulary>to deny</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5850', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%A6%E5%AE%9A%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:14:01.159729Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(5851, 'vocabulary', '尊敬する', '尊敬する', 'If 尊敬 is "respect" then the verb version would be <vocabulary>to respect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5851', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8A%E6%95%AC%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:14:09.793819Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.419126Z'),
(5852, 'vocabulary', '尊敬語', '尊敬語', '尊敬 is respect. A respect <kanji>language</kanji> is <vocabulary>honorific Japanese</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>honorific language</vocabulary>, though usually this refers to Japanese).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5852', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8A%E6%95%AC%E8%AA%9E', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:14:30.878878Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.439288Z'),
(5853, 'vocabulary', '暮らし', '暮らし', 'If 暮らす is "to live" or "to live one''s life" then the noun version would be simply <vocabulary>life</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5853', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%AE%E3%82%89%E3%81%97', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:14:35.758067Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:18.844204Z'),
(5854, 'vocabulary', '同音異義語', '同音異義語', 'You have the <kanji>same</kanji> <kanji>sound</kanji> with a <vocabulary>different meaning</vocabulary> (異義), and it''s a part of <kanji>language</kanji>. Same sounds... different meanings... that sounds like a <vocabulary>homophone</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>homonym</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5854', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8C%E9%9F%B3%E7%95%B0%E7%BE%A9%E8%AA%9E', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:14:47.441414Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:46:56.671656Z'),
(5855, 'vocabulary', '著作権', '著作権', '著作 is "writing" or "authorship." The <kanji>rights</kanji> that writing has is what''s known as <vocabulary>copyright</vocabulary>.

Note that 著作権 refers to copyright for anything, not just writing.
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5855', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%91%97%E4%BD%9C%E6%A8%A9', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:14:53.696013Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T22:02:48.269631Z'),
(5856, 'vocabulary', '蒸し暑い', '蒸し暑い', 'If it’s a <kanji>steam</kanji>ing <kanji>hot</kanji> day, it means the weather is <vocabulary>hot and humid</vocabulary> (or you can just say <vocabulary>humid</vocabulary>, because that usually implies hotness too). This is a useful word for summers in Japan, since you''ll complain about this all the time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5856', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%92%B8%E3%81%97%E6%9A%91%E3%81%84', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:14:59.717452Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.526499Z'),
(5857, 'vocabulary', '蒸し返す', '蒸し返す', 'You <kanji>steam</kanji> something to <kanji>return</kanji> it to its previously warm state. This is when you go <vocabulary>to reheat</vocabulary> something. But we usually use it to talk about bringing something up again and again in conversations or arguments. That''s why it means <vocabulary>to rehash</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to bring up again</vocabulary>. You''re bringing up memories from the past and steaming them up again.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5857', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%92%B8%E3%81%97%E8%BF%94%E3%81%99', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:15:05.325952Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.542973Z'),
(5858, 'vocabulary', '装う', '装う', 'If you go to <kanji>attire</kanji> yourself, you''re covering up who you are and trying to be someone else. You are trying <vocabulary>to feign</vocabulary>. You are trying <vocabulary>to wear</vocabulary> a lie. You are trying <vocabulary>to pretend</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5858', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%85%E3%81%86', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:15:19.298953Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.559901Z'),
(5859, 'vocabulary', '遅れる', '遅れる', 'If something or someone is <kanji>slow</kanji>, and they "do slow" they are going <vocabulary>to be late</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5859', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%85%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:15:27.074857Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.579874Z'),
(5860, 'vocabulary', '遅刻', '遅刻', 'A <kanji>slow</kanji> <kanji>carve</kanji> is so slow. If you carve something slowly, it will take you a long time. You will <vocabulary>be late</vocabulary> with a slow carve like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5860', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%85%E5%88%BB', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:15:41.598651Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.619277Z'),
(5861, 'vocabulary', '遅延', '遅延', 'I was too <kanji>slow</kanji>, so we''ll have to <kanji>prolong</kanji> the time in which this product will come out. This is a <vocabulary>delay</vocabulary> :(', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5861', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%85%E5%BB%B6', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:15:47.300458Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.638311Z'),
(5862, 'vocabulary', '彫刻', '彫刻', 'You <kanji>carve</kanji> then you <kanji>carve</kanji> again. So much carving until, wait, wow, it looks like something! All that carving became a <vocabulary>sculpture</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5862', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%AB%E5%88%BB', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:15:52.841786Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.658121Z'),
(5863, 'vocabulary', '測る', '測る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>measure</kanji> so the verb vocab means <vocabulary>to measure</vocabulary>. This word generally has to do with scientific or medical measurements.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5863', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%AC%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:15:58.74805Z', NULL, '2025-11-20T02:24:43.756039Z'),
(5864, 'vocabulary', '推測', '推測', 'When you <kanji>infer</kanji> and <kanji>measure</kanji>, it’ll lead to some sort of <vocabulary>conjecture</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>speculation</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>guess</vocabulary> about something.

推測 means guessing based on known facts or information, while using reasoning to fill in the gaps.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5864', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A8%E6%B8%AC', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:17:42.334003Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:26:11.344562Z'),
(5865, 'vocabulary', '予測', '予測', 'A <kanji>beforehand</kanji> <kanji>measure</kanji> is when you make a <vocabulary>prediction</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>estimation</vocabulary> about something.

While 予想 often refers to a subjective prediction, 予測 generally refers to <vocabulary>forecast</vocabulary>ing future events based on <i>objective evidence</i>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5865', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%88%E6%B8%AC', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:18:10.934312Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.740831Z'),
(5866, 'vocabulary', '観測', '観測', '<kanji>View</kanji> and <kanji>measure</kanji> what you are watching. This is <vocabulary>observation</vocabulary>. It is no mere viewing, it is more than that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5866', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%B3%E6%B8%AC', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:18:21.167493Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.755596Z'),
(5867, 'vocabulary', '破る', '破る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>tear</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to tear</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5867', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%B4%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:18:28.29877Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:17:49.824318Z'),
(5868, 'vocabulary', '破壊', '破壊', '<kanji>Tear</kanji> and <kanji>break</kanji> everything, taking it all down. <vocabulary>Destruction</vocabulary>! <vocabulary>Demolition</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5868', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%B4%E5%A3%8A', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:18:32.895302Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:17:49.824318Z'),
(5869, 'vocabulary', '破船', '破船', 'A <kanji>tear</kanji> in your <kanji>ship</kanji> will cause it to wreck, making it a <vocabulary>shipwreck</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5869', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%B4%E8%88%B9', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:18:37.758915Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:17:49.824318Z'),
(5870, 'vocabulary', '破産', '破産', 'If someone <kanji>tear</kanji>s all your <kanji>property</kanji> it''ll lead to <vocabulary>bankruptcy</vocabulary>. With all your property torn, you''re going to <vocabulary>go bankrupt</vocabulary>. What mean person did that?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5870', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%B4%E7%94%A3', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:18:42.857664Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:17:49.824318Z'),
(5871, 'vocabulary', '田舎', '田舎', 'A <kanji>rice paddy</kanji> and a <kanji>cottage</kanji>. This is the image of the <vocabulary>countryside</vocabulary> to me. This is <vocabulary>rural</vocabulary>. Just cottages and rice paddies...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5871', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B0%E8%88%8E', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:18:53.539736Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.820297Z'),
(5872, 'vocabulary', '兵舎', '兵舎', 'A <kanji>soldier</kanji> <kanji>cottage</kanji> is a place where the soldiers stay. This is the <vocabulary>barracks</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5872', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B5%E8%88%8E', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:18:58.170937Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:54:26.999027Z'),
(5873, 'vocabulary', '庁舎', '庁舎', 'A <kanji>government office</kanji> <kanji>cottage</kanji> is the place where the government office is stored. That''s the <vocabulary>government office building</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5873', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%81%E8%88%8E', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:19:03.105747Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.845624Z'),
(5874, 'vocabulary', '講演', '講演', '<kanji>Perform</kanji> your <kanji>lecture</kanji>. This is your <vocabulary>lecture</vocabulary>. This is your <vocabulary>address</vocabulary> to the people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5874', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AC%9B%E6%BC%94', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:19:07.911787Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.858212Z'),
(5875, 'vocabulary', '講師', '講師', 'A <kanji>lecture</kanji> <kanji>expert</kanji> is a <vocabulary>lecturer</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>instructor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5875', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AC%9B%E5%B8%AB', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:19:17.191503Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.87117Z'),
(5876, 'vocabulary', '講義', '講義', '<kanji>Lecture</kanji> of <kanji>righteousness</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>lecture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5876', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AC%9B%E7%BE%A9', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:19:27.834168Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:46:56.671656Z'),
(5877, 'vocabulary', '滞る', '滞る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>stagnate</kanji>, and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be overdue</vocabulary>. After all, if something stagnates, it''s not advancing, which causes it <vocabulary>to be delayed</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5877', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BB%9E%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:19:40.562084Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.912389Z'),
(5878, 'vocabulary', '遅滞', '遅滞', 'I am too <kanji>slow</kanji> so things have really begun to <kanji>stagnate</kanji>. What a <vocabulary>delay</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5878', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%85%E6%BB%9E', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:19:53.549524Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.926745Z'),
(5879, 'vocabulary', '紹介', '紹介', 'My <kanji>introduce</kanji> was <kanji>jammed in</kanji> there for you to hear. That is my <vocabulary>introduction</vocabulary>. Jammin'' it in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5879', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%B9%E4%BB%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:20:01.390151Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.940333Z'),
(5880, 'vocabulary', '介入', '介入', 'I <kanji>jammed in</kanji> myself and <kanji>enter</kanji>ed your personal life. Because I care, you know? I don''t want you to be addicted to My Little Pony anymore, so this is an <vocabulary>intervention</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5880', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8B%E5%85%A5', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:20:06.763618Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.952605Z'),
(5881, 'vocabulary', '己', '己', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. It also means <vocabulary>myself</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>I</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5881', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%B1', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:20:13.307623Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.966783Z'),
(5882, 'vocabulary', '自己', '自己', 'Your <kanji>self</kanji> <kanji>oneself</kanji>, is your <vocabulary>oneself</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>self</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5882', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E5%B7%B1', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:20:18.584449Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.980123Z'),
(5883, 'vocabulary', '厄介', '厄介', 'The <kanji>misfortune</kanji> is just getting <kanji>jammed in</kanji> there. So much misfortune leads to very <vocabulary>troublesome</vocabulary> <vocabulary>trouble</vocabulary>.

厄介 describes something or someone that is troublesome, or the trouble itself. It''s very similar to 面倒, but 厄介 is often used for more challenging or complicated situations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5883', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%84%E4%BB%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:20:26.675797Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:25.994303Z'),
(5884, 'vocabulary', '亀', '亀', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. BURN BURN BURN.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5884', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%80', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:20:34.044807Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.00725Z'),
(5885, 'vocabulary', '海亀', '海亀', 'A <kanji>sea</kanji> <kanji>turtle</kanji> is a <vocabulary>sea turtle</vocabulary>. Cowabunga!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5885', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B7%E4%BA%80', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:20:53.671098Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.019484Z'),
(5886, 'vocabulary', '互い', '互い', 'Despite it looking like an adjective, this is the noun version of the kanji, making it <vocabulary>mutual</vocabulary>. It also means <vocabulary>each other</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>reciprocal</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>together</vocabulary>, too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5886', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%92%E3%81%84', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:21:09.50301Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.032378Z'),
(5887, 'vocabulary', '相互', '相互', '<kanji>Mutual</kanji> and <kanji>mutual</kanji> is <vocabulary>mutual</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5887', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B8%E4%BA%92', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:21:17.905386Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.044771Z'),
(5888, 'vocabulary', '交互', '交互', 'Two people''s efforts <kanji>mix</kanji> in a <kanji>mutual</kanji> way when they work <vocabulary>alternately</vocabulary>. Going <vocabulary>by turns</vocabulary> is not only fair — it''s efficient too!

交互 describes when two things are <vocabulary>alternating</vocabulary> — whether that''s a pair of visual elements arrayed alternately in a pattern, or just two people taking turns doing something, one after the other.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5888', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%A4%E4%BA%92', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:21:26.385413Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:31:55.014313Z'),
(5889, 'vocabulary', '真剣', '真剣', 'The <kanji>reality</kanji> of the <kanji>sword</kanji> is that it will cut you right in half and kill you, easily. The fact that this is true is a very <vocabulary>serious</vocabulary> thing. Very serious.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5889', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%9F%E5%89%A3', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:21:33.526476Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T20:04:39.445204Z'),
(5890, 'vocabulary', '剣道', '剣道', 'The <kanji>path</kanji> of the <kanji>sword</kanji> is known as <vocabulary>kendo</vocabulary>. You could also say <vocabulary>Japanese fencing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5890', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%A3%E9%81%93', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:21:50.278245Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.087164Z'),
(5891, 'vocabulary', '寿司', '寿司', 'The <kanji>lifespan</kanji> of a <kanji>director</kanji> depends on how much <vocabulary>sushi</vocabulary> they eat. 

Okay, the kanji in 寿司 aren''t actually related to the meaning of the word, so this one''s kind of tricky. Just try to think of sushi as the secret to a long lifespan for directors. Imagine all those directors wolfing down a bunch of sushi because they''re desperate to lengthen their lifespans.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5891', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BF%E5%8F%B8', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:21:57.282498Z', NULL, '2025-11-16T19:10:22.124723Z'),
(5892, 'vocabulary', '寿命', '寿命', 'Your <kanji>lifespan</kanji> is your <kanji>fate</kanji> and will show you how long your <vocabulary>lifespan</vocabulary> is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5892', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BF%E5%91%BD', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:22:06.88044Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.115499Z'),
(5893, 'vocabulary', '長寿', '長寿', 'A <kanji>long</kanji> <kanji>lifespan</kanji> is a <vocabulary>long life</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5893', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%95%B7%E5%AF%BF', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:22:15.546862Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.130088Z'),
(5894, 'vocabulary', '喜寿', '喜寿', 'You can <kanji>rejoice</kanji> because your <kanji>lifespan</kanji> has reached this special point. That special point is your <vocabulary>seventy seventh birthday</vocabulary>. So many sevens, lucky!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5894', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%9C%E5%AF%BF', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:22:25.136171Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.142225Z'),
(5895, 'vocabulary', '福寿', '福寿', '<kanji>Luck</kanji> for your whole <kanji>lifespan</kanji>, that''s a life of <vocabulary>happiness and longevity</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5895', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A6%8F%E5%AF%BF', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:22:30.833759Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.167896Z'),
(5896, 'vocabulary', '彼', '彼', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5896', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%BC', 12, 1, '2013-04-05T19:22:38.375213Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.198944Z'),
(5897, 'vocabulary', '彼氏', '彼氏', 'A <kanji>he</kanji> <kanji>mister</kanji> is not just he, it''s "mister he," and mister he is your he, your <vocabulary>boyfriend</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5897', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%BC%E6%B0%8F', 13, 1, '2013-04-05T19:22:53.267489Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.228475Z'),
(5898, 'vocabulary', '彼ら', '彼ら', 'This is <kanji>he</kanji> but plural. The "ra" makes it plural, making it "<vocabulary>they</vocabulary>" or "<vocabulary>them</vocabulary>." In modern Japanese, this word generally refers to a group of men or boys, but it can also be used for mixed groups as well. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5898', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%BC%E3%82%89', 12, 1, '2013-04-05T19:22:59.020876Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:19.016781Z'),
(5899, 'vocabulary', '恥ずかしい', '恥ずかしい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>shame</kanji>? It could be <vocabulary>ashamed</vocabulary> or even <vocabulary> embarrassing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5899', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%A5%E3%81%9A%E3%81%8B%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:23:03.822032Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:08:11.512316Z'),
(5900, 'vocabulary', '恥', '恥', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5900', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%A5', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:23:11.551088Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:07:09.364336Z'),
(5901, 'vocabulary', '杉', '杉', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5901', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%89', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:23:38.008529Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.294242Z'),
(5902, 'vocabulary', '果汁', '果汁', '<kanji>Fruit</kanji> <kanji>soup</kanji> isn''t really a soup, it''s more of a <vocabulary>fruit juice</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5902', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%9C%E6%B1%81', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:23:50.645655Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.306366Z'),
(5903, 'vocabulary', '汁', '汁', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5903', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%81', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:23:56.059795Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.319396Z'),
(5904, 'vocabulary', '味噌', '味噌', 'A <kanji>flavor</kanji> that makes foods <kanji>boisterous</kanji> is a great flavor. In this case, it''s the flavor of <vocabulary>miso</vocabulary>, which goes in a lot of Japanese dishes, and certainly does make the flavor of things boisterous.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5904', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%B3%E5%99%8C', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:24:00.523832Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:20:50.221396Z'),
(5905, 'vocabulary', '炎', '炎', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5905', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%8E', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:24:05.089421Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.353937Z'),
(5906, 'vocabulary', '炎症', '炎症', 'When you have <kanji>flame</kanji> <kanji>symptom</kanji>s that''s when you feel like you''re filled with fire, which is <vocabulary> inflammation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5906', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%8E%E7%97%87', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:24:10.04028Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.366009Z'),
(5907, 'vocabulary', '為に', '為に', 'You move towards the <kanji>sake</kanji>. When you move towards it, you are doing something <vocabulary>for the sake of</vocabulary>. But why use fancy language like that? Let''s just leave it as <vocabulary>for</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5907', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%BA%E3%81%AB', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:24:14.713335Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.377939Z'),
(5908, 'vocabulary', '行為', '行為', 'You <kanji>go</kanji> somewhere for the <kanji>sake</kanji> of committing some <vocabulary>deed</vocabulary>. Is the <vocabulary>act</vocabulary> you plan to carry out there benevolent? Or will your <vocabulary>behavior</vocabulary> perhaps be… malicious?

行為 is a somewhat formal word that objectively refers to something a person does, with the focus more on the action itself than the intention behind it. It''s commonly used in writing, rules, or discussions about right and wrong, like 犯罪行為 (criminal act) or 迷惑行為 (nuisance behavior).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5908', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%8C%E7%82%BA', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:24:19.157562Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:35:24.308173Z'),
(5909, 'vocabulary', '有意', '有意', 'Things that <kanji>have</kanji> <kanji>idea</kanji>s behind them have <vocabulary>significance</vocabulary>. In other words, they''re <vocabulary>significant</vocabulary>. Nothing shallow, frivolous, and thoughtless for me, thank you! I only want the significant stuff.

有意 is a formal term most commonly used in academic contexts to refer to statistically significant research results.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5909', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%89%E6%84%8F', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:24:26.528784Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T23:48:18.832068Z'),
(5910, 'vocabulary', '熊', '熊', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5910', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%86%8A', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:24:33.015142Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T05:58:29.379374Z'),
(5911, 'vocabulary', '熊本県', '熊本県', '<kanji>Bear</kanji> <kanji>origin</kanji> <kanji>prefecture</kanji> is <vocabulary>Kumamoto Prefecture</vocabulary>. Probably best to learn this meaning via the reading. Also, you should look up Kumamon of Kumamoto-ken.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5911', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%86%8A%E6%9C%AC%E7%9C%8C', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:24:48.734754Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T05:58:29.379374Z'),
(5912, 'vocabulary', '地獄', '地獄', 'The <kanji>earth</kanji> <kanji>prison</kanji> is a prison down inside the earth. What prison is under the earth? That would be <vocabulary>hell</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5912', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E7%8D%84', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:24:58.408437Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.445582Z'),
(5913, 'vocabulary', '獄内', '獄内', 'If you''re <kanji>inside</kanji> <kanji>prison</kanji> you''re <vocabulary>in prison</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5913', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8D%84%E5%86%85', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:25:18.363133Z', NULL, '2025-09-05T05:40:05.235761Z'),
(5914, 'vocabulary', '入獄', '入獄', 'When you <kanji>enter</kanji> <kanji>prison</kanji> that''s <vocabulary>imprisonment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5914', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E7%8D%84', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:25:25.81418Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.471448Z'),
(5915, 'vocabulary', '出獄', '出獄', 'If you <kanji>exit</kanji> from <kanji>prison</kanji> you are getting your <vocabulary>release from prison</vocabulary>. But, before you move on, let me tell you one thing: Hope is a dangerous thing. Hope can drive a man insane.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5915', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E7%8D%84', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:25:51.441285Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.495037Z'),
(5916, 'vocabulary', '脱獄', '脱獄', 'You''re making your <kanji>escape</kanji> from <kanji>prison</kanji> . So this means... <vocabulary>escape from prison</vocabulary>. Or <vocabulary>jailbreak</vocabulary> if you''re feeling a little fancy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5916', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%B1%E7%8D%84', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:25:59.921263Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.510139Z'),
(5917, 'vocabulary', '酔う', '酔う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>drunk</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be drunk</vocabulary>.

This drunk feeling also happens to some people in boats and cars. But we call that <vocabulary>to be seasick</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be carsick</vocabulary>. You aren''t actually drunk, but you feel like you are (and you''re probably gonna puke, yuck!).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5917', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%94%E3%81%86', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:26:12.02052Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.530166Z'),
(5918, 'vocabulary', '泥酔', '泥酔', 'If you''re <kanji>mud</kanji> <kanji>drunk</kanji>, you''re so drunk you''re rolling around on the ground. You are <vocabulary>dead drunk</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5918', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A5%E9%85%94', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:26:24.654903Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.694516Z'),
(5919, 'vocabulary', '酢', '酢', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5919', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%A2', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:26:41.650837Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.559017Z'),
(5920, 'vocabulary', '酢の物', '酢の物', 'A <kanji>vinegar</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji> is a thing that has been vinegared. This is a <vocabulary>vinegared food</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>pickled food</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5920', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%A2%E3%81%AE%E7%89%A9', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:26:57.81901Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(5921, 'vocabulary', '鍋', '鍋', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5921', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8D%8B', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:27:03.697141Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.595753Z'),
(5922, 'vocabulary', '湖', '湖', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5922', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B9%96', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:27:21.405611Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.607567Z'),
(5923, 'vocabulary', '山中湖', '山中湖', 'The <kanji>mountain</kanji> <kanji>middle</kanji> <kanji>lake</kanji> is <vocabulary>lake Yamanaka</vocabulary>, a famous and very nice lake in Japan. It''s also a lake that will help you to learn the lake kanji reading better.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5923', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%AD%E6%B9%96', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:27:27.837167Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.618969Z'),
(5924, 'vocabulary', '青銅', '青銅', '<kanji>Blue</kanji> <kanji>copper</kanji> is <vocabulary>bronze</vocabulary>. Bronze gets a bluish tint to it, which is why it''s a blue copper.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5924', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%92%E9%8A%85', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:27:35.172906Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.630543Z'),
(5925, 'vocabulary', '銅山', '銅山', 'In a <kanji>copper</kanji> <kanji>mountain</kanji> you go in and make a <vocabulary>copper mine</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5925', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%85%E5%B1%B1', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:27:41.953296Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.64284Z'),
(5926, 'vocabulary', '銅像', '銅像', 'A <kanji>copper</kanji> <kanji>statue</kanji> is a <vocabulary>copper statue</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5926', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%85%E5%83%8F', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:27:54.479351Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.655361Z'),
(5927, 'vocabulary', '払う', '払う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>pay</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to pay</vocabulary>.

This word also means <vocabulary>to brush off</vocabulary>, like when you brush dirt or dust off of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5927', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%95%E3%81%86', 15, 1, '2013-04-05T19:28:01.574584Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.441088Z'),
(5928, 'vocabulary', '油', '油', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5928', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%B9', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:28:31.749505Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.694517Z'),
(5929, 'vocabulary', '油断', '油断', 'If you let someone <kanji>cut off</kanji> your <kanji>oil</kanji> supply, it''s probably because of your <vocabulary>inattentiveness</vocabulary>. You <vocabulary>let your guard down</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5929', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%B9%E6%96%AD', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:28:37.406654Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.717189Z'),
(5930, 'vocabulary', '油田', '油田', 'An <kanji>oil</kanji> <kanji>rice paddy</kanji> is really more like an <vocabulary>oil field</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5930', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%B9%E7%94%B0', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:28:49.787606Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.728811Z'),
(5931, 'vocabulary', '原油', '原油', 'The <kanji>original</kanji> <kanji>oil</kanji> is oil in its original state. That is <vocabulary>crude oil</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5931', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%9F%E6%B2%B9', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:29:06.268592Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.740772Z'),
(5932, 'vocabulary', '給油', '給油', 'You take your <kanji>salary</kanji> and buy <kanji>oil</kanji>. When you are doing this, you are <vocabulary>fueling</vocabulary> something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5932', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%A6%E6%B2%B9', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:29:15.693801Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.753072Z'),
(5933, 'vocabulary', '醤油', '醤油', '<kanji>Soy sauce</kanji> <kanji>oil</kanji> is <vocabulary>soy sauce</vocabulary>, or as it''s called in Japanese, <vocabulary>shoyu</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5933', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%86%A4%E6%B2%B9', 35, 1, '2013-04-05T19:29:32.39254Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.766417Z'),
(5934, 'vocabulary', '明日', '明日', 'A <kanji>bright</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji> isn''t today (because today was terrible), it''s <vocabulary>tomorrow</vocabulary>, because you can always look to tomorrow to be a better, brighter day.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5934', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%8E%E6%97%A5', 7, 1, '2013-04-08T16:52:27.764957Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(5935, 'vocabulary', '空車', '空車', 'You learned 空 means "sky," but it can also mean "empty." An <kanji>empty</kanji> <kanji>car</kanji> literally means "empty car," but in practice, you''ll usually see this lit up on a taxi to mean <vocabulary>open taxi</vocabulary>. If you spot it, feel free to hail it down!

You might also see 空車 at a parking lot. In that case, it means there''s an <vocabulary>empty parking space</vocabulary> available.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5935', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA%E8%BB%8A', 5, 1, '2013-04-11T18:11:02.111116Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.840779Z'),
(5936, 'vocabulary', '簡単', '簡単', '<kanji>Simplicity</kanji> is <kanji>simple</kanji>, making this <vocabulary>simple</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5936', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B0%A1%E5%8D%98', 33, 1, '2013-04-25T23:07:37.733235Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.893325Z'),
(5937, 'vocabulary', '降りる', '降りる', 'The kanji is <kanji>descend</kanji>, but this verb vocab word is <vocabulary>to get off</vocabulary>, as in, to get off the train, or to get out of the car. If you think about trains back in the day, you had to descend down to the platform to get out of them, which is how this word became what it is. You know that this word is the "to get off" word because once you get off the train, you enter the <reading>real</reading> (りる) world, where hardships are real and not as comfy as the train.

Figuratively, this word also means <vocabulary>to step down</vocabulary>, as in "to give up" or "to resign” from something. For example, you can say ニュース番組を降りる (to step down from a news program) or ゲームを降りる (to drop out of a game).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5937', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%8D%E3%82%8A%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2013-05-03T16:35:08.759495Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.852821Z'),
(5938, 'vocabulary', '親子丼', '親子丼', 'This very common food is <kanji>parent</kanji> and <kanji>child</kanji> <kanji>rice bowl</kanji>, aka a <vocabulary>parent and child rice bowl</vocabulary>. The parent is a chicken. The child is an egg. This can also be called <vocabulary>chicken and egg rice bowl</vocabulary>, or simply <vocabulary>oyako-don</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5938', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA%E5%AD%90%E4%B8%BC', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:37:43.782781Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T18:23:21.975771Z'),
(5939, 'vocabulary', '吐き気', '吐き気', 'This <kanji>throw up</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji> you feel inside of you… could it be… <vocabulary>nausea</vocabulary>?!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5939', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%90%E3%81%8D%E6%B0%97', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:37:52.237691Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:17:12.930062Z'),
(5940, 'vocabulary', '吐き出す', '吐き出す', 'You know 吐く is "to throw up," and 出す is "to take out" or "to let out." So you''re not just throwing up here, you''re letting it out into the world on purpose, which makes this <vocabulary>to spit out</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to throw something up</vocabulary>. 

吐き出す can be literal, as in 水を吐き出す (to spit out water), or figurative, as in 気持ちを吐き出す (to let out all your feelings).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5940', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%90%E3%81%8D%E5%87%BA%E3%81%99', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:38:19.842492Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:17:12.930062Z'),
(5941, 'vocabulary', '奴ら', '奴ら', 'If a 奴 is just some dude or some guy, adding a ら on there makes it plural. Now it''s <vocabulary>those dudes</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>those guys</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5941', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%B4%E3%82%89', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:38:28.206214Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.912173Z'),
(5942, 'vocabulary', '口紅', '口紅', 'A <kanji>mouth</kanji> <kanji>deep red</kanji> is a deep red you put on your mouth. This is <vocabulary>lipstick</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5942', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%A3%E7%B4%85', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:39:17.132205Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.925701Z'),
(5943, 'vocabulary', '薦め', '薦め', 'If 薦める is "to recommend," then the noun version would be a <vocabulary>recommendation</vocabulary> or some <vocabulary>advice</vocabulary>. This generally refers to a recommendation of a person or thing, not an action.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5943', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%A6%E3%82%81', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:39:22.650201Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.93818Z'),
(5944, 'vocabulary', '勤め', '勤め', 'If 勤める is "to be employed at," then the noun version of this is <vocabulary>work</vocabulary>. I mean, I really hope you''re doing work at the place that employed you... if not you''ll probably be fired.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5944', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%A4%E3%82%81', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:39:53.670169Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:26.9489Z'),
(5945, 'vocabulary', '電磁場', '電磁場', 'A 磁場 is a "magnetic field." An <kanji>electricity</kanji> version of this is an <vocabulary>electromagnetic field</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5945', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E7%A3%81%E5%A0%B4', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:40:00.892506Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.030484Z'),
(5946, 'vocabulary', '放射能', '放射能', '放射 is "radiation." Add the <kanji>ability</kanji> of radiation and you have <vocabulary>radioactivity</vocabulary>, for some reason. I guess things don''t have radioactivity until you have given them the ability of radiation, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5946', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BE%E5%B0%84%E8%83%BD', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:40:05.684438Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T21:59:59.512416Z'),
(5947, 'vocabulary', '指揮者', '指揮者', 'If 指揮 is "command" or "lead" then <kanji>someone</kanji> who does those things is a <vocabulary>commander</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>conductor</vocabulary>. Usually the kind that conducts an orchestra!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5947', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87%E6%8F%AE%E8%80%85', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:40:16.669312Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(5948, 'vocabulary', '源氏物語', '源氏物語', 'Remember 源氏? He has his own 物語. This is the very first novel ever written (that we know about). This is the <vocabulary>Tale of Genji</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5948', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%90%E6%B0%8F%E7%89%A9%E8%AA%9E', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:40:22.234573Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:30:31.665871Z'),
(5949, 'vocabulary', '損害する-5949', '損害する', 'If 損害 is "damage" then the verb version would be <vocabulary>to damage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5949', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%8D%E5%AE%B3%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B-5949', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:40:27.103994Z', '2020-11-26T10:43:10.879138Z', '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(5950, 'vocabulary', '枝豆', '枝豆', 'A <kanji>branch</kanji> <kanji>bean</kanji> doesn''t mean a whole lot this way, but you probably know the food that this describes anyways. This is <vocabulary>edamame</vocabulary>, a food made from <vocabulary>steamed soybeans</vocabulary>, usually with a bit of salt added for flavor.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5950', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%9D%E8%B1%86', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:40:33.766511Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.018723Z'),
(5951, 'vocabulary', '豆腐', '豆腐', '<kanji>Bean</kanji> <kanji>rot</kanji> is when you take soy beans, let them ferment, and then you make <vocabulary>tofu</vocabulary> out of them! Yep, that''s what tofu is. Rotten beans.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5951', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B1%86%E8%85%90', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:40:38.984684Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:33:00.426254Z'),
(5952, 'vocabulary', '旧姓', '旧姓', 'Your <kanji>former</kanji> <kanji>surname</kanji> is just that: your <vocabulary>former surname</vocabulary>! This is often your <vocabulary>maiden name</vocabulary>, as in the name you had before getting married.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5952', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A7%E5%A7%93', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:40:49.354988Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.04289Z'),
(5953, 'vocabulary', '復旧', '復旧', '<kanji>Restore</kanji> to the <kanji>former</kanji> self. This is <vocabulary>restoration</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5953', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%A9%E6%97%A7', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:40:54.453894Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.054282Z'),
(5954, 'vocabulary', '姓名', '姓名', 'Your <kanji>surname</kanji> and your <kanji>name</kanji> put together create your <vocabulary>full name</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5954', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%93%E5%90%8D', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:41:01.829363Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(5955, 'vocabulary', '貿易', '貿易', '<kanji>Trade</kanji> is <kanji>easy</kanji> if you like <vocabulary>trade</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>commerce</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5955', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%BF%E6%98%93', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:41:07.137905Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.078037Z'),
(5956, 'vocabulary', '甘い', '甘い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>sweet</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>sweet</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5956', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%98%E3%81%84', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:41:12.244946Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.090828Z'),
(5957, 'vocabulary', '甘く見る', '甘く見る', 'When you <kanji>see</kanji> something as <kanji>sweet</kanji>, you''re just looking at it like it''s some little sweet kid with sweet flavoring. You are not taking it seriously, or you are taking it lightly. So sweet! That''s why this word is <vocabulary>to not take seriously</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to take lightly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5957', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%98%E3%81%8F%E8%A6%8B%E3%82%8B', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:41:18.482838Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.103338Z'),
(5958, 'vocabulary', '甘党', '甘党', 'The <kanji>sweet</kanji> <kanji>group</kanji> is a group of people who love sweet things. They really have quite the <vocabulary>sweet tooth</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5958', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%98%E5%85%9A', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:41:23.71457Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.115319Z'),
(5959, 'vocabulary', '将来', '将来', 'The <kanji>commander</kanji> will <kanji>come</kanji> in the <vocabulary>future</vocabulary>. Not anytime soon, but in the future.

将来 generally implies the nearer, more concrete future. You’ll probably use it when discussing your career plans or a company''s growth strategy, and not what the Earth will be like 100 years from now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5959', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%86%E6%9D%A5', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:41:30.037794Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(5960, 'vocabulary', '将軍', '将軍', 'The <kanji>commander</kanji> of the <kanji>army</kanji> is the <vocabulary>shogun</vocabulary>. Do you know the shogun? He ran Japan, and was like the ruling general over the states. You can also call him the <vocabulary>general</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>commander</vocabulary>, if you like.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5960', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%86%E8%BB%8D', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:41:38.073726Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.138678Z'),
(5961, 'vocabulary', '大将', '大将', 'The <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>commander</kanji> is the <vocabulary>general</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>admiral</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>head</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>leader</vocabulary>). Basically, they''re big stuff.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5961', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%B0%86', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:41:48.257436Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(5962, 'vocabulary', '主将', '主将', 'The <kanji>master</kanji> <kanji>commander</kanji> is the <vocabulary>commander</vocabulary> of all commanders, the <vocabulary>commander in chief</vocabulary> if you will... or, even the <vocabulary>team captain</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5962', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB%E5%B0%86', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:41:57.97035Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.161609Z'),
(5963, 'vocabulary', '同盟', '同盟', 'We are of the <kanji>same</kanji> <kanji>alliance</kanji>. We are in an <vocabulary>alliance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5963', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8C%E7%9B%9F', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:42:11.731035Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.17254Z'),
(5964, 'vocabulary', '連盟', '連盟', 'If we <kanji>take along</kanji> our <kanji>alliance</kanji> and move it forward to something better, we have a <vocabulary>league</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>federation</vocabulary>. It''s like we''re one group now!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5964', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%A3%E7%9B%9F', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:42:17.225573Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.183417Z'),
(5965, 'vocabulary', '盟約', '盟約', 'An <kanji>alliance</kanji> <kanji>promise</kanji> is one that''s made with a <vocabulary>pledge</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>pact</vocabulary>. Imagine making an alliance with someone, pledging your support.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5965', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%9F%E7%B4%84', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:42:30.637459Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.194342Z'),
(5966, 'vocabulary', '遺産', '遺産', 'If you <kanji>leave behind</kanji> <kanji>property</kanji> it''s known as your <vocabulary>inheritance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5966', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%BA%E7%94%A3', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:42:34.555478Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.205224Z'),
(5967, 'vocabulary', '遺失', '遺失', 'The thing we <kanji>leave behind</kanji> at a <kanji>fault</kanji> is a <vocabulary>loss</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5967', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%BA%E5%A4%B1', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:42:39.719084Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.215778Z'),
(5968, 'vocabulary', '遺伝', '遺伝', 'What I <kanji>leave behind</kanji> and <kanji>transmit</kanji> to you is your <vocabulary>heredity</vocabulary>. You have my genes, even after I''m gone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5968', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%BA%E4%BC%9D', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:42:45.856129Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.226938Z'),
(5969, 'vocabulary', '遺体', '遺体', 'You <kanji>leave behind</kanji> your <kanji>body</kanji> to join the spirit realm. This body remainder is your <vocabulary>corpse</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>remains</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5969', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%BA%E4%BD%93', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:42:51.198458Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.238577Z'),
(5970, 'vocabulary', '伸びる', '伸びる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>stretch</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to grow longer</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be stretched</vocabulary>. It''s often used figuratively, in which case you''ll translate it as <vocabulary>to make progress</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to increase</vocabulary>.

You know this is version where you aren''t doing the stretching, it just kinda happens, because it''s just like how your stomach stretches when you drink beer (びる). Its capacity just seems to increase so you can take in more and more beer.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5970', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%B8%E3%81%B3%E3%82%8B', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:43:00.993033Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T22:27:56.201791Z'),
(5971, 'vocabulary', '債券', '債券', 'A <kanji>debt</kanji> <kanji>ticket</kanji> is a <vocabulary>bond</vocabulary>. You buy it from the government and then they pay it back to you with interest... in theory. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5971', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%B5%E5%88%B8', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:43:07.507336Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.275893Z'),
(5972, 'vocabulary', '債権', '債権', 'Your <kanji>debt</kanji> <kanji>rights</kanji> state that you have a certain amount of <vocabulary>credit</vocabulary> with your bank. And if they try to say you don''t have that credit you can file a <vocabulary>claim</vocabulary> against them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5972', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%B5%E6%A8%A9', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:43:13.169784Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T22:02:48.269631Z'),
(5973, 'vocabulary', '国債', '国債', 'A <kanji>country</kanji>''s <kanji>debt</kanji> is the <vocabulary>national debt</vocabulary>. It can also be <vocabulary>bonds</vocabulary>, like the ones you buy from your country.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5973', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BD%E5%82%B5', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:43:31.516622Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.302848Z'),
(5974, 'vocabulary', '及ぶ', '及ぶ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>reach</kanji> and the verb version is <vocabulary>to reach</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to amount to</vocabulary> . This is like "x reached y" or "x amounted to y."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5974', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8A%E3%81%B6', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:43:43.864532Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.325284Z'),
(5975, 'vocabulary', '普及', '普及', 'When something <kanji>reach</kanji>es <kanji>normal</kanji>, it has finished <vocabulary>diffusion</vocabulary>. Normal things aren''t all bunched up and concentrated. Normal things are spread out nice and evenly, via diffusion.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5975', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%AE%E5%8F%8A', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:43:59.263011Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.337433Z'),
(5976, 'vocabulary', '及第', '及第', 'I <kanji>reach</kanji>ed the <kanji>ordinal number prefix</kanji> that represents a passing score on my test. This means I was able to <vocabulary>make the grade</vocabulary>! Wow, <vocabulary>passing an exam</vocabulary> feels great.

及第 is an old-fashioned term that you might see as part of the word 及第点 (a passing grade), especially if you read a lot of classic Japanese books.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5976', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8A%E7%AC%AC', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:44:10.808966Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.350894Z'),
(5977, 'vocabulary', '奈良', '奈良', '<kanji>Nara</kanji> is <kanji>good</kanji> and it is <vocabulary>Nara</vocabulary> (the place, we''re talking about).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5977', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%88%E8%89%AF', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:44:16.758525Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:44:44.794692Z'),
(5978, 'vocabulary', '神奈川県', '神奈川県', 'The <kanji>god</kanji> <kanji>Nara</kanji> <kanji>river</kanji> <kanji>Prefecture</kanji> is <vocabulary>Kanagawa Prefecture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5978', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%9E%E5%A5%88%E5%B7%9D%E7%9C%8C', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:44:21.944368Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:25:07.757919Z'),
(5979, 'vocabulary', '幅', '幅', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5979', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%85', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:44:42.790057Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.388586Z'),
(5980, 'vocabulary', '廃止', '廃止', 'It is <kanji>obsolete</kanji> so we must <kanji>stop</kanji> it. No more of it anymore. This is <vocabulary>abolition</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5980', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%83%E6%AD%A2', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:44:48.015327Z', NULL, '2025-07-15T06:31:15.178195Z'),
(5981, 'vocabulary', '廃れる', '廃れる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>obsolete</kanji> and this word means <vocabulary>to become obsolete</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5981', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%83%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:44:53.2023Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.411757Z'),
(5982, 'vocabulary', '廃絶', '廃絶', 'Something that is <kanji>obsolete</kanji> will end in <kanji>extinction</kanji>. Join in the calls for its <vocabulary>abolition</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>elimination</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>extinction</vocabulary>, whatever it is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5982', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%83%E7%B5%B6', 45, 1, '2013-05-03T16:45:01.069919Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.42295Z'),
(5983, 'vocabulary', '変える', '変える', 'The kanji is <kanji>change</kanji>, and this verb is <vocabulary>to change something</vocabulary>. If you change one thing for another thing, you are going <vocabulary>to change</vocabulary> it, after all. This word refers to changing one thing for a similar thing.

変える and 代える sound the same and are sometimes used in similar contexts, but are different in nuance. 変える refers to the act of making something different, and involves altering the nature or state of something. For example, changing the layout of a room or changing one''s perspective on an issue. 代える means to substitute or exchange one thing for another that is expected to fulfill the same role, use or purpose, such as replacing a worker with a substitute.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5983', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%89%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 15, 1, '2013-05-03T16:45:05.887063Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.668773Z'),
(5984, 'vocabulary', '換気', '換気', 'Something that will <kanji>exchange</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji> is something that pulls in air, takes out the bad stuff, and then outputs good air, exchanging it for the bad. That is <vocabulary>ventilation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5984', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%9B%E6%B0%97', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:45:11.24403Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:34:55.971355Z'),
(5985, 'vocabulary', '変換', '変換', 'If you <kanji>change</kanji> something then <kanji>exchange</kanji> it, you are making a <vocabulary>conversion</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>change</vocabulary>.

変換 generally refers to converting something from one form to another, like data conversion, energy transformation, or even converting hiragana to kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5985', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%89%E6%8F%9B', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:45:16.332958Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:34:55.971355Z'),
(5986, 'vocabulary', '摘む', '摘む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>pluck</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to pluck</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to pick</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to prune</vocabulary>, like with plants.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5986', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%91%98%E3%82%80', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:45:22.073905Z', NULL, '2026-01-18T20:18:48.214331Z'),
(5987, 'vocabulary', '指摘', '指摘', 'Take your <kanji>finger</kanji> and <kanji>pluck</kanji> out the one thing out of all the things that needs <vocabulary>pointing out</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>identification</vocabulary>. Yep, that''s the one. Pluck that one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5987', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87%E6%91%98', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:45:27.793856Z', NULL, '2026-01-18T20:18:48.214331Z'),
(5988, 'vocabulary', '摘発する', '摘発する', 'To <kanji>pluck</kanji> off someone''s mask and <kanji>departure</kanji> it off somewhere away from their face, that is <vocabulary>to unmask</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to expose</vocabulary> someone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5988', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%91%98%E7%99%BA%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:45:33.375578Z', NULL, '2026-01-18T20:18:48.214331Z'),
(5989, 'vocabulary', '核', '核', 'The kanji and the word are the same. That means they share meanings as well. This word can also mean <vocabulary>core</vocabulary> because that''s where the nucleus is in a cell, and <vocabulary>nuclear</vocabulary> because we like to blow ourselves up nowadays...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5989', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%B8', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:45:40.393787Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.541609Z'),
(5990, 'vocabulary', '核兵器', '核兵器', '<kanji>Nucleus</kanji> arms (兵器), aka weapons, are <vocabulary>nuclear weapons</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5990', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%B8%E5%85%B5%E5%99%A8', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:45:45.860051Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:54:26.999027Z'),
(5991, 'vocabulary', '核実験', '核実験', 'A <kanji>nucleus</kanji> experiment (実験) is a <vocabulary>nuclear test</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5991', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%B8%E5%AE%9F%E9%A8%93', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:45:53.113905Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.565043Z'),
(5992, 'vocabulary', '沖合', '沖合', 'The <kanji>open sea</kanji> is where the sea <kanji>join</kanji>s together to be the <vocabulary>open sea</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5992', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%96%E5%90%88', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:45:59.424017Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.578388Z'),
(5993, 'vocabulary', '縄', '縄', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5993', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%84', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:46:04.879407Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:16:57.591914Z'),
(5994, 'vocabulary', '縄文', '縄文', '<kanji>Rope</kanji> <kanji>writing</kanji> was used during the <vocabulary>Jomon</vocabulary> period, a time in <vocabulary>ancient Japanese</vocabulary> history when people decorated pottery with rope.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5994', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%84%E6%96%87', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:46:10.236244Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.603134Z'),
(5995, 'vocabulary', '津波', '津波', 'A <kanji>harbor</kanji> <kanji>wave</kanji> is a wave big enough, it could easily submerge an entire harbor. What could you call such a wave? Perhaps a <vocabulary>tsunami</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>tidal wave</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5995', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%A5%E6%B3%A2', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:46:15.838882Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:11:56.943742Z'),
(5996, 'vocabulary', '献血', '献血', 'I would like to <kanji>offer</kanji> my <kanji>blood</kanji> to someone else in the form of a <vocabulary>blood donation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5996', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8C%AE%E8%A1%80', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:46:21.354989Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.627222Z'),
(5997, 'vocabulary', '献金', '献金', 'I''d like to <kanji>offer</kanji> <kanji>money</kanji>, no strings attached. It''s free. It''s a <vocabulary>donation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5997', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8C%AE%E9%87%91', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:46:26.755887Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:19.417814Z'),
(5998, 'vocabulary', '医療', '医療', 'A <kanji>medicine</kanji> can <kanji>heal</kanji>. This is <vocabulary>medical treatment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5998', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%BB%E7%99%82', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:46:32.188101Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:36:15.651867Z'),
(5999, 'vocabulary', '治療', '治療', '<kanji>Cure</kanji> and <kanji>heal</kanji> me. That is <vocabulary>medical therapy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/5999', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%BB%E7%99%82', 36, 1, '2013-05-03T16:46:37.183345Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:27.66141Z'),
(6000, 'vocabulary', '継父', '継父', 'A <kanji>father</kanji> you''ve <kanji>inherit</kanji>ed isn''t your original father, he''s your <vocabulary>stepfather</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6000', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%99%E7%88%B6', 55, 1, '2013-05-03T16:46:42.485944Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:01:24.564442Z')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;


Usually 〜鍋 will come after the name of the soup base or main ingredient, like 味噌鍋 (miso hotpot) or キムチ鍋 (kimchi stew).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6034', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E9%8D%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:25:52.761065Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.171159Z'),
(6035, 'vocabulary', '払い', '払い', 'If 払う is "to pay" or "to brush off," then the noun version is <vocabulary>payment</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>brushing off</vocabulary>.

Usually 払い is tacked on the end of another word, like in カード払い (payment by credit card).

When it indicates a kind of brushing off or brushing away, it appears in words like 足払い (sweeping an opponent''s legs in judo) or, more figuratively, せき払い (clearing one''s throat).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6035', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%95%E3%81%84', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:25:59.562985Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.197685Z'),
(6036, 'vocabulary', '油絵', '油絵', 'An <kanji>oil</kanji> <kanji>painting</kanji> is an <vocabulary>oil painting</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6036', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%B9%E7%B5%B5', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:26:05.204368Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:12:48.398208Z'),
(6037, 'vocabulary', '甘酢', '甘酢', '<kanji>Sweet</kanji> <kanji>vinegar</kanji> is <vocabulary>sweet vinegar</vocabulary>. Them Japanese love their sweet things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6037', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%98%E9%85%A2', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:26:13.727093Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.235973Z'),
(6038, 'vocabulary', '盛り上がる', '盛り上がる', '盛り上げる is "to heap up" - this has a similar idea, except now this is more like an excitement or swelling. As excitement heaps up in you, you are going <vocabulary>to get excited</vocabulary>. Note how 上がる is in here. This means something is happening to you (or something else), but you''re not doing the action. When you get all sorts of excitement building up in you, you aren''t really doing it, it''s just happening on its own in reaction to something else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6038', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%9B%E3%82%8A%E4%B8%8A%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:26:20.112836Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(6039, 'vocabulary', '遅い', '遅い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>slow</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>slow</vocabulary> (or, if you''re slow, you''re probably <vocabulary>late</vocabulary>, too).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6039', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%85%E3%81%84', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:26:25.021726Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.260791Z'),
(6040, 'vocabulary', '恥ずかしがる', '恥ずかしがる', '恥ずかしい is "embarrassing." This is the verb version. What happens when someone is embarrassed? They become shy and bashful. That''s why this word is <vocabulary>to be shy</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be bashful</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6040', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%A5%E3%81%9A%E3%81%8B%E3%81%97%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:26:31.517094Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:07:09.364336Z'),
(6041, 'vocabulary', '破れる', '破れる', 'If 破る is "to tear" or "to rip," then this is <vocabulary>to get torn</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to get broken</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to get ripped</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6041', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%B4%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:26:44.960774Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:17:49.824318Z'),
(6042, 'vocabulary', '自己紹介', '自己紹介', 'A one''s self (自己) introduction (紹介) is a <vocabulary>self introduction</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6042', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E5%B7%B1%E7%B4%B9%E4%BB%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:26:51.060729Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.299813Z'),
(6043, 'vocabulary', '紹介する', '紹介する', 'A 紹介 is an introduction. So the verb version is <vocabulary>to introduce</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6043', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%B9%E4%BB%8B%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:26:58.074295Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.312511Z'),
(6044, 'vocabulary', '紹介状', '紹介状', 'Remember how a 年賀状 is a new year''s card? This is another kind of card/letter, but this time it''s for an introduction (紹介), making it a <vocabulary>letter of introduction</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6044', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%B9%E4%BB%8B%E7%8A%B6', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:27:07.022226Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.324422Z'),
(6045, 'vocabulary', '汁物', '汁物', 'A <kanji>soup</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji> is what you use to refer to <vocabulary>soups</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6045', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%81%E7%89%A9', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:27:13.422023Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(6046, 'vocabulary', '味噌汁', '味噌汁', 'You know that 味噌 is "miso," and that 汁 is "soup." So, combine them together and you have <vocabulary>Miso Soup</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6046', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%B3%E5%99%8C%E6%B1%81', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:27:28.058542Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:20:50.221396Z'),
(6047, 'vocabulary', '酔っ払い', '酔っ払い', 'If you''re so <kanji>drunk</kanji> that you''ll be <kanji>pay</kanji>ing for it later, it''s safe to say you''re a <vocabulary>drunk person</vocabulary>. You''re way past being tipsy at this point, and you''re definitely going to pay for it… with a hangover in the morning. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6047', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%94%E3%81%A3%E6%89%95%E3%81%84', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:27:38.059319Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.358529Z'),
(6048, 'vocabulary', '二日酔い', '二日酔い', 'On the <kanji>second day</kanji>, you experience your <kanji>drunk</kanji>enness a different way... in the form of a <vocabulary>hangover</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6048', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E9%85%94%E3%81%84', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:27:43.72498Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.371869Z'),
(6049, 'vocabulary', '給油所', '給油所', 'A <kanji>fueling</kanji> (給油) <kanji>place</kanji> is a place where you get things fueled. This can either be a <vocabulary>gas station</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>filling station</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6049', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%A6%E6%B2%B9%E6%89%80', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:27:55.125738Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:18:00.076038Z'),
(6050, 'vocabulary', '跳ぶ', '跳ぶ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>hop</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to hop</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to jump</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to leap</vocabulary>. Basically anything that gets both feet off the ground via your own foot propulsion.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6050', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B7%B3%E3%81%B6', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:40:51.486152Z', NULL, '2025-10-24T01:56:56.454498Z'),
(6051, 'vocabulary', '昭和', '昭和', 'The <kanji>shining</kanji> <kanji>peace</kanji> time was a time known as the <vocabulary>Showa Era</vocabulary>. This can also refer to the <vocabulary>Showa Emperor</vocabulary> or just <vocabulary>Showa</vocabulary> itself. Everything Showa is here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6051', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%AD%E5%92%8C', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:41:33.683121Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.408212Z'),
(6052, 'vocabulary', '漁師', '漁師', 'A <kanji>fishing</kanji> <kanji>expert</kanji> is a <vocabulary>fisherman</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6052', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%81%E5%B8%AB', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:41:39.387745Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.419338Z'),
(6053, 'vocabulary', '漁船', '漁船', 'A <kanji>fishing</kanji> <kanji>boat</kanji> is a <vocabulary>fishing boat</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6053', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%81%E8%88%B9', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:41:49.290073Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.430173Z'),
(6054, 'vocabulary', '漁業', '漁業', 'The <kanji>fishing</kanji> <kanji>industry</kanji> is the <vocabulary>fishing industry</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>fishery</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6054', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%81%E6%A5%AD', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:41:55.256724Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(6055, 'vocabulary', '償う', '償う', 'To make <kanji>reparation</kanji>s is <vocabulary>to compensate for</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to make up for</vocabulary> something. You''re going <vocabulary>to atone for</vocabulary> your sins.

This word is mainly used when someone is taking responsibility for a mistake, a wrongdoing, or a loss by providing compensation or otherwise making things right.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6055', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%84%9F%E3%81%86', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:42:03.907197Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:20:00.461835Z'),
(6056, 'vocabulary', '弁償', '弁償', 'My <kanji>dialect</kanji> has offended you? I will make <kanji>reparation</kanji> by giving you a <vocabulary>reimbursement</vocabulary> and some <vocabulary>compensation</vocabulary>. I''m sorry my dialect was so bad you couldn''t understand the speech. Here''s your money back.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6056', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%81%E5%84%9F', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:42:10.940227Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.479796Z'),
(6057, 'vocabulary', '刑務所', '刑務所', 'The <kanji>punish</kanji> <kanji>task</kanji> <kanji>facility</kanji> is the place you go to do your punishment tasks. It is a <vocabulary>jail</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>prison</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6057', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%91%E5%8B%99%E6%89%80', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:42:17.671099Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:18:00.076038Z'),
(6058, 'vocabulary', '死刑', '死刑', 'A <kanji>death</kanji> <kanji>punish</kanji>ment is the <vocabulary>death penalty</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6058', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%BB%E5%88%91', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:42:22.814979Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.503318Z'),
(6059, 'vocabulary', '刑事', '刑事', 'You know 事 means "occurrence," but it can also mean "matter." And someone who handles <kanji>punish</kanji>ment <kanji>matter</kanji>s is someone like a <vocabulary>police detective</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>detective</vocabulary>) trying to find and punish the bad guys.

刑事 isn''t an official job title but a common way to refer to a police officer whose job is to investigate crimes and arrest criminals. Depending on the context, it can also refer to matters that fall under criminal law, like 刑事事件 (criminal case).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6059', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%91%E4%BA%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:42:29.370083Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:47:34.523008Z'),
(6060, 'vocabulary', '刑期', '刑期', 'A <kanji>punish</kanji> <kanji>period of time</kanji> is the time in which you are being punished in prison. This is your <vocabulary>prison term</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6060', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%91%E6%9C%9F', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:42:36.936933Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.531535Z'),
(6061, 'vocabulary', '刑罰', '刑罰', 'A <kanji>punish</kanji> <kanji>penalty</kanji> is a <vocabulary>punishment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6061', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%91%E7%BD%B0', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:42:42.797453Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:49:26.899294Z'),
(6062, 'vocabulary', '終身刑', '終身刑', 'Until their <kanji>end</kanji>, someone''s <kanji>body</kanji> is being <kanji>punish</kanji>ed. If they''re being punished until their end, they''ve probably been given a <vocabulary>life sentence</vocabulary>, aka <vocabulary>life imprisonment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6062', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%82%E8%BA%AB%E5%88%91', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:42:54.847496Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:21:37.325745Z'),
(6063, 'vocabulary', '募る', '募る', 'The kanji is <kanji>recruit</kanji>, so this verb vocab version can also mean <vocabulary>to recruit</vocabulary>. However, its primary meaning is <vocabulary>to appeal for something</vocabulary>, like a job position or donations.

This verb can also be intransitive (oh my!) and mean <vocabulary>to grow stronger</vocabulary>, usually in reference to some sort of feelings or emotions. Make sure to check out the common word combinations for some example usages!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6063', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%9F%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:43:08.204591Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.586347Z'),
(6064, 'vocabulary', '募金', '募金', 'If you <kanji>recruit</kanji> <kanji>gold</kanji> you are trying to raise money. In other words, you''re <vocabulary>fundraising</vocabulary>.

募金 originally just referred to the act of collecting <vocabulary>donations</vocabulary> for a cause, such as disaster relief, charity, or nonprofit activities. But nowadays, it can also mean the act of donating money for such a cause.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6064', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%9F%E9%87%91', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:43:18.21434Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:27:31.306578Z'),
(6065, 'vocabulary', '募集', '募集', 'I want to <kanji>recruit</kanji> some people, so let''s <kanji>gather</kanji> them up and do some <vocabulary>recruiting</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>solicitation</vocabulary> to get them.

募集 means looking for participants — whether that means job applicants, volunteers, contestants, or new club members. You''ll often see it in official announcements and job ads.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6065', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%9F%E9%9B%86', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:43:27.145237Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T16:50:58.791089Z'),
(6066, 'vocabulary', '応募', '応募', 'I <kanji>respond</kanji> to your <kanji>recruit</kanji>ment with this <vocabulary>application</vocabulary> to try and join your group.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6066', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%9C%E5%8B%9F', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:43:35.323498Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.629382Z'),
(6067, 'vocabulary', '執る', '執る', 'You learned that 執 means "<kanji>tenacious</kanji>," but it can also mean "carry out." The verb version here means <vocabulary>to take up</vocabulary>, like taking up a task or responsibility, or <vocabulary>to carry out</vocabulary>. 

執る is a formal word that means taking something in hand and carrying out an action. 筆を執る means "to take up the pen," for example, but also implies you''re starting to write something meaningful. From this, 執る is also often used when someone takes charge of an important task or adopts a firm stance on something, like 指揮を執る (to take command). ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6067', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%B7%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:43:41.381799Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T23:08:23.525531Z'),
(6068, 'vocabulary', '執着', '執着', 'You <kanji>tenacious</kanji>ly <kanji>wear</kanji> something. You have some kind of <vocabulary>attachment to</vocabulary> it. You can''t let go. It''s an <vocabulary>obsession</vocabulary>, after all, and you just can''t stop <vocabulary>clinging to</vocabulary> it!

執着 usually refers to an unhealthy or excessive attachment to something. An obsession with money, dwelling on the past, or even persisting in your own narrow-minded beliefs — these are all kinds of 執着.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6068', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%B7%E7%9D%80', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:43:47.164525Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(6069, 'vocabulary', '執筆', '執筆', 'You learned that 執 means "tenacious," but it can also mean "<kanji>carry out</kanji>." So what do you carry out with a <kanji>writing brush</kanji>? You carry out <vocabulary>writing</vocabulary>! But not just any writing — this is <vocabulary>professional writing</vocabulary>, so you better get it right!

執筆 is a formal word for the act of writing, especially professional or important work like novels, magazine columns, news articles, or academic papers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6069', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%B7%E7%AD%86', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:44:03.012777Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T23:20:52.57916Z'),
(6070, 'vocabulary', '塁', '塁', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6070', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%81', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:44:08.990976Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:37:37.525129Z'),
(6071, 'vocabulary', '塁打', '塁打', 'A <kanji>baseball base</kanji> <kanji>hit</kanji> is a <vocabulary>base hit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>single</vocabulary> in baseball terms. A 二塁打 is a double, a 三塁打 is a triple, and a homerun is something you''ll learn here on WaniKani at some point if you haven''t already.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6071', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%81%E6%89%93', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:44:17.135857Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:37:37.525129Z'),
(6072, 'vocabulary', '塁審', '塁審', 'A <kanji>baseball base</kanji> <kanji>judge</kanji> is one who judges things on a baseball field. This is a <vocabulary>baseball umpire</vocabulary>, or more simply, an <vocabulary>umpire</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6072', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%81%E5%AF%A9', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:44:25.402987Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:37:37.525129Z'),
(6073, 'vocabulary', '崩す', '崩す', 'The kanji is <kanji>crumble</kanji>, and this verb is for when you actively make or let something crumble, so it means <vocabulary>to break down</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to tear down</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to crumble</vocabulary>. It''s transitive, so you''re the one tearing stuff down. You can remember this because if you go around tearing down people''s stuff, they''ll probably sue (す).

崩す is for when you break down or loosen something that was solid or properly arranged. You can 崩す a sandcastle at the beach, your posture when you slouch, or even your health when you let yourself get worn down and sick. There are tons of ways to use this tricky word, so be sure check the context section for examples.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6073', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B4%A9%E3%81%99', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:44:51.269284Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:03:31.652533Z'),
(6074, 'vocabulary', '崩壊', '崩壊', 'When something begins to <kanji>crumble</kanji> and <kanji>break</kanji>, it will certainly <vocabulary>collapse</vocabulary> at some point. It just can''t hold up under the pressure.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6074', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B4%A9%E5%A3%8A', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:44:58.85693Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:20:36.651776Z'),
(6075, 'vocabulary', '患う', '患う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>afflicted</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be afflicted with</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to suffer from</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6075', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%A3%E3%81%86', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:45:06.617514Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:51:48.112271Z'),
(6076, 'vocabulary', '患者', '患者', 'An <kanji>afflicted</kanji> <kanji>someone</kanji> is someone who is <vocabulary>a patient</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>medical patient</vocabulary>. They are afflicted with some disease which is why they need medical attention.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6076', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%A3%E8%80%85', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:45:12.101283Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(6077, 'vocabulary', '急患', '急患', 'Someone who is so <kanji>afflicted</kanji> that you have to <kanji>hurry</kanji> and help is an <vocabulary>emergency patient</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6077', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A5%E6%82%A3', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:45:17.460913Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:57:49.848656Z'),
(6078, 'vocabulary', '戻す', '戻す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>return</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to return</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to return something</vocabulary>. You know you''re doing the action of returning someone or something because when you do, they just <reading>sue</reading> (す) you because you broke the thing you were supposed to return before returning it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6078', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%BB%E3%81%99', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T17:45:24.990596Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.804532Z'),
(6079, 'vocabulary', '戻る', '戻る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>return</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to return</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be returned</vocabulary>. This is when something does this on its own. You aren''t doing the returning of something in this case.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6079', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%BB%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:45:32.237707Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.816293Z'),
(6080, 'vocabulary', '対向する', '対向する', 'Being in the <kanji>versus</kanji> <kanji>yonder</kanji> (the opposite side) means you are in one yonder and someone is in the other yonder, and you are coming towards each other. So this means <vocabulary>to be oncoming</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to come towards</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6080', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BE%E5%90%91%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:45:52.26327Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:12:11.505385Z'),
(6081, 'vocabulary', '反抗する', '反抗する', 'If you''re <kanji>anti</kanji> something and you <kanji>confront</kanji> the government about it, in some cases you are going <vocabulary>to rebel</vocabulary> against the government... that is, if you''re crazy enough about it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6081', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8D%E6%8A%97%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:46:00.794239Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:30:02.229578Z'),
(6082, 'vocabulary', '抗戦', '抗戦', 'We are ready to <kanji>confront</kanji> <kanji>war</kanji>, if we have to. We are going to start the <vocabulary>resistance</vocabulary> against your regime, confront you, and even make war.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6082', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%97%E6%88%A6', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:46:06.941397Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.869384Z'),
(6083, 'vocabulary', '抗体', '抗体', '<kanji>Confront</kanji> your <kanji>body</kanji>? Sounds like it''s something that''s against bodies... perhaps, something that''s <vocabulary>antibody</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6083', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%97%E4%BD%93', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:46:13.175001Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.882721Z'),
(6084, 'vocabulary', '抵抗する', '抵抗する', '<kanji>Resist</kanji> and <kanji>confront</kanji> those who pressure or attack you. This is <vocabulary>to resist</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to withstand</vocabulary>.

抵抗する means resisting an external force, whether physically or emotionally.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6084', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%B5%E6%8A%97%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:46:21.087262Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:40:04.640165Z'),
(6085, 'vocabulary', '大抵', '大抵', 'A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>resist</kanji> is something he <vocabulary>generally</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>usually</vocabulary> does... at least <vocabulary>in most cases</vocabulary>. He''s usually a big resistor, that one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6085', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E6%8A%B5', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:46:28.142986Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:40:04.640165Z'),
(6086, 'vocabulary', '上旬', '上旬', 'What are the <kanji>above</kanji> <kanji>ten days</kanji> of the month? They''re the <vocabulary>first ten days of the month</vocabulary>, of course, or even just the <vocabulary>beginning of the month</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6086', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A%E6%97%AC', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:46:38.542558Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(6087, 'vocabulary', '下旬', '下旬', 'These are the <kanji>below</kanji> <kanji>ten days</kanji> of the month. In other words, the <vocabulary>last ten days of the month</vocabulary>, or even just the <vocabulary>end of the month</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6087', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E6%97%AC', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:46:45.336097Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(6088, 'vocabulary', '中旬', '中旬', 'The <kanji>middle</kanji> <kanji>ten days</kanji> is the <vocabulary>middle ten days of the month</vocabulary>. You could also call this the <vocabulary>middle of the month</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6088', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E6%97%AC', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:46:51.763308Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.950404Z'),
(6089, 'vocabulary', '旬', '旬', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6089', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%AC', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:46:59.028349Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:28.961868Z'),
(6090, 'vocabulary', '湾', '湾', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well, though it contains some extra similar meanings as well, like a <vocabulary>bay</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>inlet</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6090', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B9%BE', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:47:06.247466Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.713234Z'),
(6091, 'vocabulary', '台湾', '台湾', 'The <kanji>machine</kanji> <kanji>gulf</kanji> isn''t what you''d expect. In fact, looking at the reading will probably help the most first, since this word represents <vocabulary>Taiwan</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6091', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B0%E6%B9%BE', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:47:11.986263Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.713234Z'),
(6092, 'vocabulary', '爆発', '爆発', '<kanji>Explode</kanji> <kanji>departure</kanji> is when an explode departures from a certain point, causing an <vocabulary>explosion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6092', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%88%86%E7%99%BA', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:47:18.570723Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:22:52.12866Z'),
(6093, 'vocabulary', '爆弾', '爆弾', 'An <kanji>explode</kanji> <kanji>bullet</kanji> is when an explode shoots out of something like a bullet, but much bigger, like from a <vocabulary>bomb</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6093', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%88%86%E5%BC%BE', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:47:26.250729Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:33:28.795445Z'),
(6094, 'vocabulary', '爆笑', '爆笑', 'You <kanji>explode</kanji> with <kanji>laugh</kanji>s. Wow, what <vocabulary>explosive laughter</vocabulary>.

爆笑 means to <vocabulary>burst out laughing</vocabulary>, often loudly and uncontrollably. While it originally referred to group laughter, it can now also be used by and for individuals. You can even use it like <vocabulary>LOL</vocabulary> or LMAO in English: just stick it at the end of your Japanese messages to show you''re really laughing it up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6094', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%88%86%E7%AC%91', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:47:33.04458Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:58:31.981956Z'),
(6095, 'vocabulary', '自爆', '自爆', 'If you <kanji>self</kanji> <kanji>explode</kanji>, it means you''re blowing up yourself, and probably taking others with you. This is a <vocabulary>suicide bomb</vocabulary>.

自爆 generally refers to <vocabulary>suicide bombing</vocabulary> in a military or terrorist context. Figuratively, it can also mean ruining one''s own situation through careless actions, such as scoring an own goal in soccer.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6095', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E7%88%86', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:47:43.64066Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:51:34.558205Z'),
(6096, 'vocabulary', '爆撃', '爆撃', 'An <kanji>explode</kanji> <kanji>attack</kanji> is when someone attacks with exploding things, like bombs. This is a <vocabulary>bombing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6096', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%88%86%E6%92%83', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:48:02.20781Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.049896Z'),
(6097, 'vocabulary', '弾', '弾', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

弾 refers to <vocabulary>bullet</vocabulary>s and other projectiles, including artillery <vocabulary>shell</vocabulary>s and BB <vocabulary>pellet</vocabulary>s. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6097', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%BE', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:48:07.243452Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:33:28.795445Z'),
(6098, 'vocabulary', '弾む', '弾む', 'Weird that this kanji means <kanji>bullet</kanji> but the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to bounce</vocabulary>. Just imagine someone shooting a bullet. What does it do when it hits the right materials? It''s going <vocabulary>to bounce</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6098', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%BE%E3%82%80', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:48:21.541414Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:33:28.795445Z'),
(6099, 'vocabulary', '弾丸', '弾丸', 'Back in the day, a <kanji>bullet</kanji> was a <kanji>circle</kanji> shape. So, this word is <vocabulary>bullet</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6099', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%BE%E4%B8%B8', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:48:28.18756Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T15:28:07.250648Z'),
(6100, 'vocabulary', '聴力', '聴力', 'Your <kanji>listen</kanji> <kanji>power</kanji> is your <vocabulary>hearing ability</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6100', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%81%B4%E5%8A%9B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:48:39.50482Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(6101, 'vocabulary', '聴者', '聴者', 'A <kanji>listen</kanji> <kanji>someone</kanji>, is someone who listens. This is a <vocabulary>listener</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6101', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%81%B4%E8%80%85', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:48:51.026703Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(6102, 'vocabulary', '聴く', '聴く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>listen</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to listen to</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to hear</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6102', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%81%B4%E3%81%8F', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:49:07.188556Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:19.814796Z'),
(6103, 'vocabulary', '盗聴', '盗聴', 'I <kanji>steal</kanji> by <kanji>listen</kanji>ing to what people say. I do this by <vocabulary>wiretapping</vocabulary> their phone and <vocabulary>bugging</vocabulary> their homes.

盗聴 usually refers to deliberate surveillance using devices like hidden microphones or wiretaps.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6103', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%97%E8%81%B4', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:49:20.425226Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.144526Z'),
(6104, 'vocabulary', '跡', '跡', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6104', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B7%A1', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:49:26.373357Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.706403Z'),
(6105, 'vocabulary', '奇跡', '奇跡', 'Some <kanji>odd</kanji> <kanji>traces</kanji> are left over here. We don''t really understand what happened, because it was a <vocabulary>miracle</vocabulary>, but it certainly left some odd traces behind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6105', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%87%E8%B7%A1', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:49:31.794967Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.706403Z'),
(6106, 'vocabulary', '証跡', '証跡', '<kanji>Evidence</kanji> <kanji>traces</kanji> are little bits of <vocabulary>evidence</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>proof</vocabulary>, left behind. They''re the <vocabulary>traces</vocabulary> that show that something happened.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6106', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%BC%E8%B7%A1', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:49:38.822597Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.706403Z'),
(6107, 'vocabulary', '遺跡', '遺跡', 'All you <kanji>leave behind</kanji> are <kanji>traces</kanji> of the past. These are the <vocabulary>ruins</vocabulary> of what happened before. The <vocabulary>historic remains</vocabulary>...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6107', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%BA%E8%B7%A1', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:49:45.84651Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.706403Z'),
(6108, 'vocabulary', '遣う', '遣う', 'The kanji means <kanji>dispatch</kanji>, but the word 遣う itself simply means <vocabulary>to use</vocabulary> because it''s basically just a more formal version of 使う. Still, the kanji adds a subtle nuance: it suggests you''re sending out some care or intention when using something, usually things like time, money, language, or feelings.

Note that you can almost always use 使う in everyday situations. 遣う is usually found in formal or literary Japanese, including older writing styles or texts that aim for a polished or dignified tone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6108', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%A3%E3%81%86', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:49:53.566709Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:15:14.008072Z'),
(6109, 'vocabulary', '闘う', '闘う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>fight</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to fight</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to struggle</vocabulary>.

When you use 闘う instead of 戦う, it often means fighting with strong will or emotion — like battling illness, hardship, or standing up for a cause. It can be used for physical battles too, but it usually implies there''s struggle and fighting spirit behind it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6109', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%97%98%E3%81%86', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:50:24.039615Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.21807Z'),
(6110, 'vocabulary', '戦闘', '戦闘', 'A <kanji>war</kanji> <kanji>fight</kanji> isn''t just any old fight — it''s a <vocabulary>battle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6110', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%A6%E9%97%98', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:50:31.547306Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T21:26:24.514007Z'),
(6111, 'vocabulary', '闘志', '闘志', 'You have <kanji>fight</kanji> in you, and you have the <kanji>intention</kanji> to win. You have <vocabulary>fighting spirit</vocabulary>. Right? Right?! Show me your fighting spirit!

闘志 is a relatively formal word that you''ll often see in writing about sports, business, or personal challenges.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6111', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%97%98%E5%BF%97', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:50:40.240114Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.242184Z'),
(6112, 'vocabulary', '陣', '陣', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

In historical contexts, 陣 refers to an <vocabulary>army camp</vocabulary> or to the <vocabulary>formation</vocabulary>s soldiers used on the battlefield, often in relation to samurai battles. Sometimes, it can even mean the <vocabulary>battle</vocabulary> itself when you see it in the battle''s name.

In modern Japanese, that older sense lives on in sports, where 陣 can mean one team''s side of the field. More commonly, though, 陣 is used as a suffix for a <vocabulary>group</vocabulary> of people with a shared affiliation, like 報道陣 (press corps) or 教授陣 (faculty).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6112', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%A3', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:50:47.655475Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:19.85261Z'),
(6113, 'vocabulary', '陣営', '陣営', 'This <kanji>army camp</kanji> is really well <kanji>manage</kanji>d. Sure, it''s still an <vocabulary>army camp</vocabulary>, but it''s a really darn good one!

陣 and 陣営 both mean "army camp" when used literally, but in modern Japanese 陣営 is also often used to refer to competing <vocabulary>camp</vocabulary>s or <vocabulary>faction</vocabulary>s in politics, business, or sports.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6113', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%A3%E5%96%B6', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:50:53.751653Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:19.859019Z'),
(6114, 'vocabulary', '香り', '香り', 'This is the noun version of 香, making it have the same meaning, <vocabulary>fragrance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6114', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%99%E3%82%8A', 39, 1, '2013-05-03T17:50:59.399025Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.276233Z'),
(6115, 'vocabulary', '香港', '香港', 'The <kanji>fragrance</kanji> <kanji>harbor</kanji>, perhaps this is where the perfumes came from back in the day. This place is <vocabulary>Hong Kong</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6115', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%99%E6%B8%AF', 41, 1, '2013-05-03T17:51:06.618967Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.287722Z'),
(6116, 'vocabulary', '香水', '香水', 'A <kanji>fragrance</kanji> of <kanji>water</kanji> is a <vocabulary>perfume</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6116', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%99%E6%B0%B4', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:51:17.903211Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.300173Z'),
(6117, 'vocabulary', '香川県', '香川県', 'The <kanji>fragrance</kanji> <kanji>river</kanji> <kanji>prefecture</kanji> is <vocabulary>Kagawa Prefecture</vocabulary>. Might help to look at the reading first.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6117', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%99%E5%B7%9D%E7%9C%8C', 40, 1, '2013-05-03T17:51:23.229375Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:25:07.757919Z'),
(6118, 'vocabulary', '兆候', '兆候', 'An <kanji>omen</kanji> for the <kanji>climate</kanji> of things is a <vocabulary>sign</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>indication</vocabulary> of change. If there''s an omen, there''s going to be a sign as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6118', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%86%E5%80%99', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:51:28.861836Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.327778Z'),
(6119, 'vocabulary', '前兆', '前兆', 'A <kanji>before</kanji> <kanji>omen</kanji> is something that tells you about something that will happen before it happens. Really this is just an <vocabulary>omen</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>sign</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6119', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%8D%E5%85%86', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:51:40.369442Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.340816Z'),
(6120, 'vocabulary', '臨む', '臨む', 'If you <kanji>look to</kanji> something, you are facing it, taking it head on. That''s why this verb vocab is <vocabulary>to face</vocabulary>, as in "to face terrorism," etc.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6120', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%A8%E3%82%80', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:51:47.668671Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T08:19:04.090481Z'),
(6121, 'vocabulary', '臨海', '臨海', 'If you <kanji>look to</kanji> the <kanji>sea</kanji>, you are <vocabulary>seaside</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>coastal</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6121', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%A8%E6%B5%B7', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:51:56.070418Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.365359Z'),
(6122, 'vocabulary', '臨時', '臨時', 'You <kanji>look to</kanji> the <kanji>time</kanji>, because something <vocabulary>irregular</vocabulary> seems to be happening outside the normal schedule.

臨時 highlights something out of the ordinary, like 臨時の仕事 (<vocabulary>temporary</vocabulary> job) or 臨時休業 (<vocabulary>unscheduled</vocabulary> closure). It can also sometimes mean "extra," but only in a special, irregular sense, like 臨時ボーナス (extra bonus).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6122', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%A8%E6%99%82', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:52:02.546746Z', NULL, '2025-10-05T17:07:40.453962Z'),
(6123, 'vocabulary', '削る', '削る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>whittle down</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to whittle something down</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to shave something down</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>to reduce something</vocabulary>.

This verb is transitive, meaning you''re whittling something down, like a piece of wood, or reducing something, like your expenses.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6123', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%8A%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:52:08.406741Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.398154Z'),
(6124, 'vocabulary', '削除', '削除', '<kanji>Whittle down</kanji> and <kanji>exclude</kanji> this item from the list. Time to remove it. It''s <vocabulary>deletion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6124', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%8A%E9%99%A4', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:52:13.897012Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.410317Z'),
(6125, 'vocabulary', '削減', '削減', '<kanji>Whittle down</kanji> and <kanji>decrease</kanji> something. This is a <vocabulary>reduction</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>cutback</vocabulary>, since you''re whittling down and decreasing it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6125', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%8A%E6%B8%9B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:52:30.488897Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:02.806385Z'),
(6126, 'vocabulary', '契約', '契約', 'I <kanji>pledge</kanji> and <kanji>promise</kanji> to do that thing... so much so, I''m willing to sign a <vocabulary>contract</vocabulary> about it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6126', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%91%E7%B4%84', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:52:36.510758Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:44:30.75481Z'),
(6127, 'vocabulary', '契機', '契機', 'I <kanji>pledge</kanji> myself to the <kanji>machine</kanji>s. It is my only <vocabulary>chance</vocabulary> to survive the upcoming machine apocalypse.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6127', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%91%E6%A9%9F', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:52:48.703371Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:44:30.75481Z'),
(6128, 'vocabulary', '恵む', '恵む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>favor</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to do a favor</vocabulary> for.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6128', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%B5%E3%82%80', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:52:56.981179Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.499562Z'),
(6129, 'vocabulary', '知恵', '知恵', 'You are <kanji>favor</kanji>ed with <kanji>know</kanji>ing things. You have <vocabulary>wisdom</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>intelligence</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6129', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%A5%E6%81%B5', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:53:02.321513Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T17:53:59.143539Z'),
(6130, 'vocabulary', '抱く', '抱く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>hug</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to embrace</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to hold</vocabulary> (in one''s arms, that is). It can also mean <vocabulary>to hug</vocabulary>, especially in the form of 抱きしめる.

Keep in mind that depending on the context, 抱く can be a euphemism for something way more sexual than just hugging. Not always, of course, but pretty often, so just be careful how you use it! 抱きしめる is the better option if you''re talking about regular old hugs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6130', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%B1%E3%81%8F', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:53:09.202138Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.531745Z'),
(6131, 'vocabulary', '掲げる', '掲げる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>display</kanji>, so if you''re doing the action of displaying something, you''re going <vocabulary>to put up</vocabulary> something, or, sometimes more specifically, <vocabulary>to put up a sign</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6131', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%B2%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:53:17.914358Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:42:46.078365Z'),
(6132, 'vocabulary', '掲示', '掲示', 'To <kanji>display</kanji> and <kanji>indicate</kanji> something is to put up a <vocabulary>notice</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>bulletin</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6132', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%B2%E7%A4%BA', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:53:23.960386Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:42:46.078365Z'),
(6133, 'vocabulary', '掲載', '掲載', 'To <kanji>display</kanji> your writing so it''s <kanji>put on</kanji> public record, it needs to go into <vocabulary>publication</vocabulary>. You''re getting it <vocabulary>print</vocabulary>ed in a magazine, newspaper, etc., and this can refer to Internet publications as well. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6133', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%B2%E8%BC%89', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:53:34.466312Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:42:46.078365Z'),
(6134, 'vocabulary', '狙い', '狙い', 'Your <kanji>aim</kanji> is your <vocabulary>aim</vocabulary>. What are you aiming for? Despite looking like an adjective, this is a noun, so be careful!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6134', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%99%E3%81%84', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:53:39.961045Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.614574Z'),
(6135, 'vocabulary', '狙撃', '狙撃', 'If you have to <kanji>aim</kanji> with your <kanji>attack</kanji>, you''re probably <vocabulary>shooting</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>sniping</vocabulary> something with a projectile (like a gun, or something).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6135', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%99%E6%92%83', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:53:50.468756Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.628169Z'),
(6136, 'vocabulary', '葬式', '葬式', 'A <kanji>burial</kanji> <kanji>ceremony</kanji> is a <vocabulary>funeral</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6136', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%91%AC%E5%BC%8F', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:53:56.14805Z', NULL, '2025-12-25T03:08:56.417913Z'),
(6137, 'vocabulary', '葬る', '葬る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>burial</kanji>, so the verb version would be <vocabulary>to bury</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to inter</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6137', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%91%AC%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:54:13.334823Z', NULL, '2025-12-25T03:08:56.417913Z'),
(6138, 'vocabulary', '火葬', '火葬', 'A <kanji>fire</kanji> <kanji>burial</kanji> is when you burn the body. You call this <vocabulary>cremation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6138', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%AB%E8%91%AC', 37, 1, '2013-05-03T17:54:21.057072Z', NULL, '2025-12-25T03:08:56.417913Z'),
(6139, 'vocabulary', '貿易会社', '貿易会社', 'A trade (貿易) company (会社) is a company that makes a lot of trades, because it''s easy for them, making this word a <vocabulary>trading firm</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6139', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%BF%E6%98%93%E4%BC%9A%E7%A4%BE', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:31:36.391365Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.683128Z'),
(6140, 'vocabulary', '遺伝子', '遺伝子', 'If 遺伝 is <kanji>heredity</kanji> and you pass your heredity to your <kanji>child</kanji>, how is it being passed? Via <vocabulary>genes</vocabulary>, of course.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6140', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%BA%E4%BC%9D%E5%AD%90', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:31:48.09692Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.696172Z'),
(6141, 'vocabulary', '奈良県', '奈良県', 'Remember <kanji>Nara</kanji> (奈良)? This is the <kanji>prefecture</kanji> of it, making it <vocabulary>Nara Prefecture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6141', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%88%E8%89%AF%E7%9C%8C', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:31:53.399489Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:25:07.757919Z'),
(6142, 'vocabulary', '廃止する', '廃止する', '廃止 is <kanji>abolition</kanji>, so the verb version of that would be <vocabulary>to abolish</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6142', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%83%E6%AD%A2%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:31:59.183747Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.730062Z'),
(6143, 'vocabulary', '乗り換える', '乗り換える', 'You have <kanji>ride</kanji> and <kanji>exchange</kanji>, so you take your ride (bus, train, whatever) and exchange to another bus, train, or whatever. That''s why this word means <vocabulary>to transfer</vocabulary>, and most commonly <vocabulary>to change trains</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6143', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%97%E3%82%8A%E6%8F%9B%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:32:06.909066Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:34:55.971355Z'),
(6144, 'vocabulary', '指摘する', '指摘する', 'If 指摘 is "pointing out" then the verb version is <vocabulary>to point out</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6144', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87%E6%91%98%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:32:12.214006Z', NULL, '2026-01-18T20:18:48.214331Z'),
(6145, 'vocabulary', '沖縄', '沖縄', 'The <kanji>open sea</kanji> <kanji>rope</kanji> is the rope that attaches <vocabulary>Okinawa</vocabulary> to the mainland.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6145', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%96%E7%B8%84', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:32:22.04514Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.789483Z'),
(6146, 'vocabulary', '維持する', '維持する', 'To "do maintenance (維持)" is keeping something in that maintained position, which is <vocabulary>to maintain</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6146', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%AD%E6%8C%81%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:32:35.133869Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.801472Z'),
(6147, 'vocabulary', '明治維新', '明治維新', 'You know <kanji>Meiji</kanji>, (明治), and you know <kanji>restoration</kanji> (維新). Combine them together and you have the <vocabulary>Meiji Restoration</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6147', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%8E%E6%B2%BB%E7%B6%AD%E6%96%B0', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:32:41.319921Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(6148, 'vocabulary', 'お見舞い', 'お見舞い', 'The <kanji>dance</kanji> of going to <kanji>see</kanji> someone. It''s a <vocabulary>sympathy visit</vocabulary>. Because, you know, you gotta dance the dance of sympathy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6148', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E8%A6%8B%E8%88%9E%E3%81%84', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:32:46.204024Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.825992Z'),
(6149, 'vocabulary', '舞踏会', '舞踏会', 'A dancing (舞踏) <kanji>meet</kanji> is when you all get together to do dancing, the kind that''s like "step step swing" (or whatever). This is a <vocabulary>ball</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>dance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6149', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%9E%E8%B8%8F%E4%BC%9A', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:32:52.025265Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.837225Z'),
(6150, 'vocabulary', '一般的', '一般的', '一般 is "general" and "common", and adding 的 just gives you <vocabulary>ordinary</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>general</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>common</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6150', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E8%88%AC%E7%9A%84', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:32:56.87756Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.859454Z'),
(6151, 'vocabulary', '全般的', '全般的', '<kanji>All</kanji> <kanji>general</kanji>-<kanji>al</kanji>! It''s super general. It''s <vocabulary>general</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>overall</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>across the board</vocabulary>. Nothing specific going on here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6151', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E8%88%AC%E7%9A%84', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:33:04.038543Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:47:49.244838Z'),
(6152, 'vocabulary', '頼み', '頼み', 'To 頼む is "to ask for" or "to request." The noun version of that is <vocabulary>a favor</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>a request</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6152', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%BC%E3%81%BF', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:33:09.996386Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.88517Z'),
(6153, 'vocabulary', '頼る', '頼る', '頼む is "to ask for" or "to request." This word is <vocabulary>to rely on</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to trust</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to depend on</vocabulary>. You know it is this word because it is <reading>ru</reading>de to rely on your friends, but you are anyways.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6153', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%BC%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:33:16.422733Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T20:20:24.403402Z'),
(6154, 'vocabulary', '依頼人', '依頼人', 'You already know that 依頼 means "request." What''s a "request" <kanji>person</kanji>? It''s someone who requests things from you. In other words, your <vocabulary>client</vocabulary>!

依頼人 generally refers to someone requesting particular services or assistance. Whether it''s consulting a lawyer, employing a detective, or initiating a bank transfer, they''re the one making the request.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6154', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%9D%E9%A0%BC%E4%BA%BA', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:33:26.258073Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(6155, 'vocabulary', '馬鹿らしい', '馬鹿らしい', '馬鹿 is "stupid" so if you hear a rumor about something (らしい) that''s stupid, you''ll just respond by saying "that''s <vocabulary>absurd</vocabulary>."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6155', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%AC%E9%B9%BF%E3%82%89%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:33:31.046311Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.928936Z'),
(6156, 'vocabulary', '需要', '需要', 'You <kanji>demand</kanji> and <kanji>need</kanji> this thing, which is why you must make this <vocabulary>demand</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6156', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%80%E8%A6%81', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:33:38.976223Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:28:03.237353Z'),
(6157, 'vocabulary', '必需品', '必需品', 'I have <kanji>certain</kanji> <kanji>demand</kanji>s for <kanji>goods</kanji> to take on this trip. Don''t worry, it''s just the <vocabulary>necessities</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>essentials</vocabulary>.

必需品 is a relatively formal word that refers to items that are necessary for daily life, survival, or specific activities.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6157', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%85%E9%9C%80%E5%93%81', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:33:44.334167Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(6158, 'vocabulary', '年齢', '年齢', 'Your <kanji>year</kanji> <kanji>age</kanji> is the number of <vocabulary>years old</vocabulary> you are.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6158', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E9%BD%A2', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:33:50.480172Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.970315Z'),
(6159, 'vocabulary', '高齢者', '高齢者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> of <kanji>tall</kanji> <kanji>age</kanji> is going to be an <vocabulary>elderly person</vocabulary> or an <vocabulary>old person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6159', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E9%BD%A2%E8%80%85', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:33:55.746729Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(6160, 'vocabulary', '老齢', '老齢', 'If you are at an <kanji>elderly</kanji> <kanji>age</kanji> you are at an <vocabulary>old age</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6160', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%81%E9%BD%A2', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:34:01.669988Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:29.992896Z'),
(6161, 'vocabulary', '宜しい', '宜しい', 'You send your <kanji>best regards</kanji> to everyone. Is this <vocabulary>good</vocabulary>? Is it <vocabulary>okay?</vocabulary> Is it <vocabulary>all right</vocabulary>? Yes, of course, how nice of you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6161', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9C%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:34:14.197271Z', NULL, '2025-10-23T04:12:26.875085Z'),
(6162, 'vocabulary', '繰る', '繰る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>spin</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to spin</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6162', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B9%B0%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:34:43.274482Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.015802Z'),
(6163, 'vocabulary', '避ける', '避ける', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>avoid</kanji> or <kanji>dodge</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to avoid</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to dodge</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6163', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%BF%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:34:56.400925Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.027237Z'),
(6164, 'vocabulary', '避難', '避難', '<kanji>Dodge</kanji> a <kanji>difficult</kanji> situation by getting away from it. Do an <vocabulary>evacuation</vocabulary> to get out of there!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6164', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%BF%E9%9B%A3', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:35:01.978437Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.038899Z'),
(6165, 'vocabulary', '避妊', '避妊', 'I <kanji>dodge</kanji>d being <kanji>pregnant</kanji>. Whew. Good thing I was taking my <vocabulary>birth control</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6165', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%BF%E5%A6%8A', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:35:11.937047Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.051276Z'),
(6166, 'vocabulary', '妊娠する', '妊娠する', 'To do <kanji>pregnant</kanji> <kanji>pregnant</kanji> is <vocabulary>to be pregnant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6166', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%8A%E5%A8%A0%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:35:23.661451Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.063251Z'),
(6167, 'vocabulary', '妊婦', '妊婦', 'A <kanji>pregnant</kanji> <kanji>lady</kanji> is a <vocabulary>pregnant woman</vocabulary>. How simple is that!

妊婦 is a relatively formal term used to refer to a woman who is pregnant. In casual situations it''s common to add さん and say 妊婦さん, to make it sound friendlier.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6167', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%8A%E5%A9%A6', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:35:28.621285Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:45:15.149494Z'),
(6168, 'vocabulary', '致す', '致す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>do</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to do</vocabulary>. This is a polite way to say "to do."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6168', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%B4%E3%81%99', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:35:34.084714Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.085613Z'),
(6169, 'vocabulary', '一致する', '一致する', 'You do <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>do</kanji> and I''ll do another do. I''ll do the same thing <vocabulary>to match</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6169', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E8%87%B4%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:35:40.257918Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.098247Z'),
(6170, 'vocabulary', '致命的', '致命的', 'Somebody <kanji>do</kanji>ing you <kanji>fate</kanji> <kanji>-al</kanji> would... probably be pretty <vocabulary>fatal</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6170', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%B4%E5%91%BD%E7%9A%84', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:35:44.993092Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.109732Z'),
(6171, 'vocabulary', '朝刊', '朝刊', 'The <kanji>morning</kanji> <kanji>edition</kanji> is the <vocabulary>morning edition</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6171', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%9D%E5%88%8A', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:35:51.348086Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.121182Z'),
(6172, 'vocabulary', '月刊', '月刊', 'The <kanji>moon</kanji> <kanji>edition</kanji> is the <vocabulary>monthly edition</vocabulary>, since 月 also means "month."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6172', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%88%E5%88%8A', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:35:57.012991Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.132666Z'),
(6173, 'vocabulary', '夕刊', '夕刊', 'The <kanji>evening</kanji> <kanji>edition</kanji> is the <vocabulary>evening edition</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6173', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%95%E5%88%8A', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:36:04.715311Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.144634Z'),
(6174, 'vocabulary', '刊行する', '刊行する', 'The <kanji>edition</kanji> will <kanji>go</kanji> out to the public if we were <vocabulary>to publish</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6174', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%8A%E8%A1%8C%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:36:10.878388Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.157631Z'),
(6175, 'vocabulary', '日刊', '日刊', 'The <kanji>day</kanji>''s <kanji>edition</kanji> is the <vocabulary>daily edition</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6175', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E5%88%8A', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:36:17.469799Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.170381Z'),
(6176, 'vocabulary', '奏楽', '奏楽', 'When you <kanji>play music</kanji> with a 楽器 (musical instrument), you get 奏楽 — a <vocabulary>musical performance</vocabulary>.

奏楽 is a formal word that refers to musical performance or <vocabulary>performing music</vocabulary>. It''s often associated with formal or ceremonial music, like traditional Japanese court music played at shrines, temples, or imperial events.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6176', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%8F%E6%A5%BD', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:36:24.025493Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:44:06.446344Z'),
(6177, 'vocabulary', '演奏', '演奏', 'If you <kanji>perform</kanji> and <kanji>play music</kanji> you are doing a <vocabulary>musical performance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6177', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%94%E5%A5%8F', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:36:29.70557Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.200913Z'),
(6178, 'vocabulary', '伴う', '伴う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>accompany</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to accompany</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be accompanied by</vocabulary>.

This word is both transitive and intransitive. So it works both when bringing someone with you, like 助手を伴って来る (to come with an assistant), or when going along with someone else, like 上司に伴って行く (to go with one''s boss). When it''s about physically accompanying someone, it''s often used together with a movement verb.

伴う is also used figuratively to talk about consequences that tend <vocabulary>to go hand in hand with</vocabulary> something, like 変化にはリスクが伴う (change comes with risk). In this case, 伴う is usually used by itself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6178', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%B4%E3%81%86', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:36:34.750949Z', NULL, '2025-10-05T17:02:35.211612Z'),
(6179, 'vocabulary', '同伴する', '同伴する', 'The <kanji>same</kanji> <kanji>accompany</kanji> means you are in the same place as someone, and you are accompanying them. This word means <vocabulary>to accompany</vocabulary>. This is only used for accompanying people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6179', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8C%E4%BC%B4%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:36:40.237035Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.228504Z'),
(6180, 'vocabulary', '併殺', '併殺', 'A <kanji>join</kanji> <kanji>kill</kanji> is when in one play you get two kills (outs) in baseball, making it a <vocabulary>double play</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6180', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%B5%E6%AE%BA', 39, 1, '2013-05-03T18:36:46.668065Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.241139Z'),
(6181, 'vocabulary', '併合', '併合', '<kanji>Join</kanji> and <kanji>join</kanji> with each other. This is an <vocabulary>annexation</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>merger</vocabulary>.

併合 generally means that one entity absorbs or incorporates another entity, often implying a dominant or controlling role by one of the parties.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6181', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%B5%E5%90%88', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:36:51.620276Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.253703Z'),
(6182, 'vocabulary', '傾く', '傾く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>lean</kanji>, and the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to be leaned</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be tilted</vocabulary>. You aren''t doing the leaning or the tilting, though, you''re just watching, which is why you say "cool" (く) when you see it, because it''s already done.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6182', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%BE%E3%81%8F', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:37:03.990306Z', NULL, '2025-06-23T18:18:01.240453Z'),
(6183, 'vocabulary', '傾向', '傾向', 'If you usually <kanji>lean</kanji> <kanji>yonder</kanji>, in that direction, that will be your <vocabulary>tendency</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6183', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%BE%E5%90%91', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:37:10.953645Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.278466Z'),
(6184, 'vocabulary', '却って', '却って', 'The kanji means <kanji>contrary</kanji>, and this is the adverb version of that, so it means <vocabulary>on the contrary</vocabulary>.

却って is a common expression used when something brings the opposite result from what you expected, like "I tried to help, but <vocabulary>instead</vocabulary> it made things worse." The kanji version is formal, so you''ll often see it in kana as かえって, especially in casual writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6184', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B4%E3%81%A3%E3%81%A6', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:37:17.258995Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T19:28:50.301344Z'),
(6185, 'vocabulary', '却下', '却下', 'It is <kanji>contrary</kanji> to what I think is good. It is <kanji>below</kanji> my standards. Thus, <vocabulary>rejection</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6185', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B4%E4%B8%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:37:24.87009Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(6186, 'vocabulary', '奥', '奥', 'Something that is in the <kanji>interior</kanji> of something else is inside of it, deep, and <vocabulary>far back</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>in the back</vocabulary>. Just imagine something interior to something else, it''s probably in the back.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6186', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%A5', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:37:36.690592Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.328446Z'),
(6187, 'vocabulary', '奥さん', '奥さん', 'The <kanji>interior</kanji> person (the honorific さん shows it''s a person) is the person who stands in the interior of the house and doesn''t leave, making that person your <vocabulary>wife</vocabulary>. Ouch. Sexist kanji strikes again.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6187', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%A5%E3%81%95%E3%82%93', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:37:43.223599Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.341395Z'),
(6188, 'vocabulary', '遠慮', '遠慮', 'A <kanji>far</kanji> <kanji>consider</kanji> is when you let your considerations go far from you so you don''t think about how angry you are about what''s going on in front of you. It allows you to have some <vocabulary>restraint</vocabulary>, since your considerations are far from here. It also allows you to <vocabulary>decline</vocabulary> things, since you''re not considering the other person''s feelings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6188', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%A0%E6%85%AE', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:37:59.3839Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:51:52.975966Z'),
(6189, 'vocabulary', '配慮', '配慮', 'If you <kanji>distribute</kanji> your <kanji>consider</kanji>s, you will be giving <vocabulary>consideration</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>care</vocabulary> to whatever you''re distributing these cares to.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6189', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%8D%E6%85%AE', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:38:17.507443Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:51:52.975966Z'),
(6190, 'vocabulary', '考慮', '考慮', 'I''ll <kanji>think</kanji> and <kanji>consider</kanji> this. I''ll give it <vocabulary>consideration</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6190', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%83%E6%85%AE', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:38:22.534393Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:34:08.229191Z'),
(6191, 'vocabulary', '懸ける', '懸ける', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>suspend</kanji>, but in this case, you''re going to suspend your win or loss and try <vocabulary>to stake</vocabulary> it all on one, big bet. You''re going <vocabulary>to risk</vocabulary> it all!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6191', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%87%B8%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:38:28.677514Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.393091Z'),
(6192, 'vocabulary', '懸命', '懸命', 'Go ahead and <kanji>suspend</kanji> <kanji>fate</kanji>, let''s get this done before the time we are fated to do it. We are that <vocabulary>eager</vocabulary> to get started.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6192', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%87%B8%E5%91%BD', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:38:52.682772Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.405373Z'),
(6193, 'vocabulary', '房', '房', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6193', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%BF', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:38:58.082754Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:30:31.466992Z'),
(6194, 'vocabulary', '冷房', '冷房', 'A <kanji>cool</kanji> <kanji>cluster</kanji> is something that has a cluster of cool-creating coolant. This device is <vocabulary>air conditioning</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6194', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%B7%E6%88%BF', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:39:11.593284Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.44054Z'),
(6195, 'vocabulary', '暖房', '暖房', 'A <kanji>warm</kanji> <kanji>cluster</kanji> is when you get a bunch of warmth together, and then use it to warm your home. This is indoor <vocabulary>heating</vocabulary>, kind of like the same way how an air conditioner is a "cool cluster."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6195', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%96%E6%88%BF', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:39:18.740649Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.452695Z'),
(6196, 'vocabulary', '扱う', '扱う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>handle</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to handle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6196', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%B1%E3%81%86', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:39:27.098294Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.464301Z'),
(6197, 'vocabulary', '抑圧', '抑圧', 'I want to <kanji>suppress</kanji> and <kanji>pressure</kanji> you! Muahaha! This is <vocabulary>oppression</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6197', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%91%E5%9C%A7', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:39:48.297126Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:28:34.36878Z'),
(6198, 'vocabulary', '抑制', '抑制', 'Try to <kanji>suppress</kanji> and <kanji>control</kanji> the enemy. We must <vocabulary>control</vocabulary> them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6198', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%91%E5%88%B6', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:39:53.176481Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:36:25.472861Z'),
(6199, 'vocabulary', '抑止', '抑止', 'This is going to <kanji>suppress</kanji> and <kanji>stop</kanji> what''s coming at you. It''s a <vocabulary>deterrent</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6199', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%91%E6%AD%A2', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:40:03.767867Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.500869Z'),
(6200, 'vocabulary', '選択', '選択', '<kanji>Choose</kanji> and <kanji>select</kanji> your choice. This is your <vocabulary>selection</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6200', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%B8%E6%8A%9E', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:40:09.024928Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.511917Z'),
(6201, 'vocabulary', '描写', '描写', '<kanji>Draw</kanji> a <kanji>copy</kanji> of the world and you are making a <vocabulary>depiction</vocabulary> of the world.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6201', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%8F%E5%86%99', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:40:24.185715Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:44:43.400712Z'),
(6202, 'vocabulary', '描画', '描画', 'If you <kanji>draw</kanji> an <kanji>image</kanji>, you end up with a <vocabulary>drawing</vocabulary> (or a <vocabulary>painting</vocabulary>).

描画 is a relatively formal word that refers to the process of creating pictures (rather than the pictures themselves). This includes not only painting or drawing but also digitally creating images on a computer screen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6202', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%8F%E7%94%BB', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:40:29.606273Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:44:43.400712Z'),
(6203, 'vocabulary', '描く', '描く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>draw</kanji> and the verb vocab refers to creating visual art, like drawings or paintings, making this <vocabulary>to draw</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to paint</vocabulary>. It can also be used figuratively <vocabulary>to illustrate</vocabulary> ideas or dreams, or <vocabulary>to depict</vocabulary> scenes in one''s mind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6203', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%8F%E3%81%8F', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:40:36.47139Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:44:43.400712Z'),
(6204, 'vocabulary', '盤', '盤', 'The kanji and the word are the same! This word also means <vocabulary>board</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>disc</vocabulary>, which makes sense if you think about it. Boards you play games on are basically just trays for the pieces, and discs go inside boards (like records) for storage.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6204', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%A4', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:40:44.014162Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.559119Z'),
(6205, 'vocabulary', '円盤', '円盤', 'A <kanji>round</kanji> <kanji>tray</kanji> is a <vocabulary>disk</vocabulary>, though if it goes through the air it becomes a <vocabulary>flying saucer</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6205', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%86%E7%9B%A4', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:40:51.133961Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:10:33.22049Z'),
(6206, 'vocabulary', '基盤', '基盤', 'A <kanji>foundation</kanji> <kanji>tray</kanji> is a flat thing that is your foundation. It holds everything up. It is the <vocabulary>base</vocabulary> of everything that goes above it. It is the <vocabulary>foundation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6206', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%BA%E7%9B%A4', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:40:56.419372Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.582525Z'),
(6207, 'vocabulary', '対称', '対称', 'If you <kanji>versus</kanji> (compare) the <kanji>title</kanji> of these two things, you''ll notice that they''re the same as each other. They have <vocabulary>symmetry</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6207', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BE%E7%A7%B0', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:41:23.461288Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:12:11.505385Z'),
(6208, 'vocabulary', '仮称', '仮称', 'A <kanji>temporary</kanji> <kanji>title</kanji> is what you give something when you don''t have a final title for it. it''s a <vocabulary>working name</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>tentative name</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6208', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%AE%E7%A7%B0', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:41:27.954419Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.615722Z'),
(6209, 'vocabulary', '一人称', '一人称', 'The <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> <kanji>title</kanji> is the title one person gives themselves. They are speaking in the <vocabulary>first person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6209', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E4%BA%BA%E7%A7%B0', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:41:33.561958Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(6210, 'vocabulary', '一緒', '一緒', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>cord</kanji> is enough to tie you <vocabulary>together</vocabulary> with someone. Imagine an invisible cord stretching between you and the people you do things together with. That one cord is like a symbol of your togetherness. Very poetic (or very Death Stranding), right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6210', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E7%B7%92', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:41:49.492533Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T00:09:48.530936Z'),
(6211, 'vocabulary', '内緒', '内緒', 'Keep information <kanji>inside</kanji> a <kanji>cord</kanji> that cordons off just you and a few others (or maybe no one else). Only the people you let inside the cord can know, because it''s a <vocabulary>secret</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>confidential</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6211', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%85%E7%B7%92', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:41:54.198738Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T00:09:48.530936Z'),
(6212, 'vocabulary', '緩い', '緩い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>loose</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>loose</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6212', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A9%E3%81%84', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:42:03.444566Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.664408Z'),
(6213, 'vocabulary', '緩む', '緩む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>loose</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to loosen</vocabulary>. This is when something loosens on its own, and not via your actions. You know this because a cow is loosening things, <reading>moo</reading> (む)...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6213', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A9%E3%82%80', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:42:07.772372Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.681221Z'),
(6214, 'vocabulary', '緩やか', '緩やか', 'This is the na-adjective version of <kanji>loose</kanji>, so that means this word means <vocabulary>loose</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>mild</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>gentle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6214', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A9%E3%82%84%E3%81%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:42:12.601583Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.693523Z'),
(6215, 'vocabulary', '委託', '委託', 'To the <kanji>committee</kanji> I would like to <kanji>consign</kanji> these things. This is what I <vocabulary>consign</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6215', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%94%E8%A8%97', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:42:22.45875Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:34:02.31068Z'),
(6216, 'vocabulary', '託す', '託す', 'If you <kanji>consign</kanji> something to someone, you are going <vocabulary>to entrust</vocabulary> them with that item. Here you go, I entrust you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6216', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%97%E3%81%99', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:42:27.207278Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.718858Z'),
(6217, 'vocabulary', '信託', '信託', 'If you <kanji>believe</kanji> you can <kanji>consign</kanji> your trust to someone, then you can <vocabulary>trust</vocabulary> them, or you are being <vocabulary>entrusting</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6217', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%A1%E8%A8%97', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:42:31.566725Z', NULL, '2025-11-16T19:02:11.093561Z'),
(6218, 'vocabulary', '受託', '受託', 'You <kanji>accept</kanji> and <kanji>consign</kanji> the job. You are going to <vocabulary>be entrusted with</vocabulary> this property for the winter. Don''t go into room 237.

受託 is a formal word that means taking on a task someone has entrusted to you. You''ll often see it in business contexts to talk about a company taking on work from another, like 業務を受託する (to <vocabulary>undertake</vocabulary> contract work). In legal contexts, it can also refer to being entrusted with physical items or money.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6218', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%97%E8%A8%97', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:42:36.892754Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:48.601574Z'),
(6219, 'vocabulary', '贈る', '贈る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>presents</kanji> so if you do presents, you''re giving them, making this <vocabulary>to give a present</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to gift</vocabulary>.

贈る is used for the act of giving when it''s done with particular thoughtfulness or sincerity — often to express things like gratitude, love, congratulations, or respect. Things you might 贈る include flowers on a birthday, a commemorative item at an event, or even words of thanks. It can also extend to bestowing honors, titles, ranks, or awards, as these are also "gifts" in a sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6219', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B4%88%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:42:42.649627Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T18:39:22.3442Z'),
(6220, 'vocabulary', '贈収賄', '贈収賄', 'The <kanji>presents</kanji> you <kanji>acquire</kanji> are a <kanji>bribe</kanji>. What? That is <vocabulary>bribery</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6220', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B4%88%E5%8F%8E%E8%B3%84', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:42:52.84986Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.766209Z'),
(6221, 'vocabulary', '賄賂', '賄賂', 'A <kanji>bribe</kanji> <kanji>bribe</kanji> is a <vocabulary>bribe</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6221', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%84%E8%B3%82', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:43:06.787768Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.778393Z'),
(6222, 'vocabulary', '賄う', '賄う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>bribe</kanji>, but when you pay a bribe, you are going <vocabulary>to pay</vocabulary> something or <vocabulary>to cover</vocabulary> something, like a meal, or something like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6222', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%84%E3%81%86', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:43:12.150579Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:22:21.559981Z'),
(6223, 'vocabulary', '逃げる', '逃げる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>escape</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to escape</vocabulary>. Also, it can be <vocabulary>to flee</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to run away</vocabulary>. Basically, if you''re trying to get away from someone or something, you could be doing this.

逃げる is a general word for escaping that can be used in any situation, from running away from danger, to avoiding responsibility, to simply leaving a place quickly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6223', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%83%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:43:16.855242Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.328031Z'),
(6224, 'vocabulary', '逃亡', '逃亡', 'If you <kanji>escape</kanji> <kanji>death</kanji> you have, well, an <vocabulary>escape</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6224', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%83%E4%BA%A1', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:43:22.666515Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.813899Z'),
(6225, 'vocabulary', '生還', '生還', 'We got <kanji>life</kanji> by <kanji>sending back</kanji> our runner to home plate. That run allowed us more life in this game. Now, maybe we''ll win! It was all by <vocabulary>reaching home plate</vocabulary>. Outside of baseball, this means <vocabulary>returning alive</vocabulary> from somewhere.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6225', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E9%82%84', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:43:28.954255Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.82463Z'),
(6226, 'vocabulary', '返還', '返還', 'If we <kanji>return</kanji> and <kanji>send back</kanji> things, we will have done a <vocabulary>return</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>restoration</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6226', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%94%E9%82%84', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:43:34.147605Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.84861Z'),
(6227, 'vocabulary', '還元', '還元', '<kanji>Send back</kanji> to the <kanji>origin</kanji> means you are going to <vocabulary>give something back</vocabulary>. This word is commonly used to mean "giving benefits back to the consumers.” For example, if a company makes more money than usual, they can thank consumers with a 利益還元 sale, lowering prices to "give back" some of the profits.

In the context of chemistry, 還元 also means <vocabulary>reduction</vocabulary>, like reducing an oxidized substance back to its original, unoxidized form.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6227', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%82%84%E5%85%83', 38, 1, '2013-05-03T18:43:44.341216Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.877385Z'),
(6228, 'vocabulary', '湯豆腐', '湯豆腐', '豆腐 is <kanji>tofu</kanji>, and 湯 means <kanji>hot water</kanji>, making it tofu you''ve put in hot water, aka <vocabulary>boiled tofu</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6228', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B9%AF%E8%B1%86%E8%85%90', 38, 1, '2013-05-08T17:06:58.056009Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:33:00.426254Z'),
(6229, 'vocabulary', '伸ばす', '伸ばす', 'If 伸びる is "to be stretched" then 伸ばす is <vocabulary>to stretch something</vocabulary>, where you''re doing the stretching. You know this because you took a <reading>bus</reading> and you stretched it out to be an even longer bus.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6229', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%B8%E3%81%B0%E3%81%99', 38, 1, '2013-05-08T17:07:17.442582Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.904892Z'),
(6230, 'vocabulary', '及ぼす', '及ぼす', 'If you have <kanji>reach</kanji>, that means you''re able <vocabulary>to exert</vocabulary> a great influence on people. Watch out, though, because it also means you''re able <vocabulary>to cause</vocabulary> great harm.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6230', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8A%E3%81%BC%E3%81%99', 38, 1, '2013-05-08T17:07:31.914019Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.971416Z'),
(6231, 'vocabulary', '及び', '及び', '及ぶ means "to reach" or "to amount to." The noun version is more like a grammar thing, as it means "<vocabulary>as well as</vocabulary>." Perhaps if you are reaching a certain point or certain amount, then you are adding things on. That''s why this means "as well as".

Note that 及び is a term usually used in formal writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6231', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8A%E3%81%B3', 38, 1, '2013-05-08T17:07:42.77368Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.98527Z'),
(6232, 'vocabulary', '踏み込む', '踏み込む', '踏む is "to step on" and the kanji 込 means <kanji>into</kanji>, so this means <vocabulary>to step into</vocabulary>.

踏み込む means to step further and harder than usual, like stepping forward to lunge for a ball or stepping hard on the accelerator. Figuratively, it can also mean <vocabulary>to intrude</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to delve into</vocabulary>, like stepping into someone''s house without permission or exploring complex topics one step further.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6232', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B8%8F%E3%81%BF%E8%BE%BC%E3%82%80', 38, 1, '2013-05-08T17:08:16.719882Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:30.997163Z'),
(6233, 'vocabulary', '超す', '超す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>ultra</kanji> or <kanji>super</kanji> so the verb version is what happens when you do something ultra or super much. You start <vocabulary>to exceed</vocabulary> the amount you were meant to do it. If you ultra drive a car, you exceed the speed limit. If you ultra study, you exceed your brain capacity. Get it?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6233', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B6%85%E3%81%99', 38, 1, '2013-05-08T17:08:28.552152Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.008824Z'),
(6234, 'vocabulary', '派遣', '派遣', 'The <kanji>sect</kanji> of <kanji>dispatch</kanji> helps with all the <vocabulary>dispatch</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>deployment</vocabulary> duties.

When used together with the word 社員, this word can also mean <vocabulary>temporary</vocabulary>, in the sense of a temporary employee, possibly someone working for a company via a temping agency.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6234', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%BE%E9%81%A3', 37, 1, '2013-05-10T17:54:27.10266Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:15:23.483599Z'),
(6235, 'vocabulary', '燃やす', '燃やす', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>burn</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to burn something</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to set on fire</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6235', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%87%83%E3%82%84%E3%81%99', 24, 1, '2013-05-14T00:27:28.355355Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.054337Z'),
(6236, 'vocabulary', '縄張り', '縄張り', 'Take your <kanji>rope</kanji> and <kanji>stretch</kanji> it out to show where the cutoff is. This is when you <vocabulary>rope off</vocabulary> something. It shows your <vocabulary>territory</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>turf</vocabulary>. Don''t cross this rope I''ve stretched out, it''s my <vocabulary>domain</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6236', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%84%E5%BC%B5%E3%82%8A', 38, 1, '2013-05-16T20:02:20.156225Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.065244Z'),
(6237, 'vocabulary', '〜丁目', '〜丁目', 'A <kanji>street</kanji> you can see with your <kanji>eye</kanji> isn''t soooo big you can''t see it (like a town would be), but it''s still pretty big because it has a street. This section of the town is the <vocabulary>district of a town</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>city block</vocabulary> (though specifically one of an irregular size).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6237', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E4%B8%81%E7%9B%AE', 7, 1, '2013-05-28T18:07:14.661016Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:15:32.558339Z'),
(6238, 'vocabulary', '換える', '換える', 'The kanji is <kanji>exchange</kanji>, so this verb is <vocabulary>to exchange</vocabulary>. If you exchange one thing for another thing, you are going <vocabulary>to exchange</vocabulary> it, after all. This word refers to exchanging one thing for something different.

Wondering about the difference between 替える and 換える? 替える means swapping one thing for another of the same kind, whereas 換える means exchanging or trading one thing for something different, often of similar value. So, you use 替える when switching out light bulbs, but opt for 換える when trading jewelry for cash.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6238', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%9B%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 36, 1, '2013-06-04T00:26:33.452652Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:34:55.971355Z'),
(6239, 'vocabulary', '将棋', '将棋', '<kanji>Commander</kanji> <kanji>chess piece</kanji> is a game known as <vocabulary>shogi</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>Japanese chess</vocabulary>, but <vocabulary>shogi</vocabulary> is better). It''s similar to chess in a lot of ways and it''s pretty interesting. Check it out if you already like chess.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6239', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%86%E6%A3%8B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:34:47.867756Z', NULL, '2025-07-14T06:31:54.830249Z'),
(6240, 'vocabulary', '跡継ぎ', '跡継ぎ', 'The <kanji>traces</kanji> are the people that <kanji>inherit</kanji> what you have. They are your <vocabulary>heir</vocabulary>s or <vocabulary>heiress</vocabulary>es. Your <vocabulary>successor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6240', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B7%A1%E7%B6%99%E3%81%8E', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:35:07.042665Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.706403Z'),
(6241, 'vocabulary', '跳ねる', '跳ねる', 'Not to be confused with 跳ぶ, this word has the same meaning. The reading is different, however.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6241', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B7%B3%E3%81%AD%E3%82%8B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:35:12.046746Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:09:41.579423Z'),
(6242, 'vocabulary', '縄跳び', '縄跳び', 'A <kanji>rope</kanji> that you <kanji>hop</kanji> over is a <vocabulary>jump rope</vocabulary>. 縄跳び can also refer to the activity of <vocabulary>jumping rope</vocabulary> (aka skipping rope).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6242', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%84%E8%B7%B3%E3%81%B3', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:35:17.95979Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T22:58:00.851257Z'),
(6243, 'vocabulary', '応募する', '応募する', 'If 応募 is "application" then the verb version is <vocabulary>to apply for</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to enlist</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6243', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%9C%E5%8B%9F%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:35:27.812246Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.159811Z'),
(6244, 'vocabulary', '本塁打', '本塁打', 'A <kanji>hit</kanji> you get in <kanji>baseball</kanji> that takes you to the <kanji>origin</kanji> plate is a <vocabulary>home run</vocabulary>. HOOOMMEERUNNNN!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6244', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E5%A1%81%E6%89%93', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:35:33.31715Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:37:37.525129Z'),
(6245, 'vocabulary', '崩れる', '崩れる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>crumble</kanji> and the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to crumble</vocabulary>, like, something is crumbling or collapsing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6245', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B4%A9%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:35:42.535075Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.18517Z'),
(6246, 'vocabulary', '払い戻す', '払い戻す', '払い is a "payment" and 戻す is "to return." If you return a payment, you are going <vocabulary>to repay</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to refund</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to reimburse</vocabulary> someone for their payment.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6246', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%95%E3%81%84%E6%88%BB%E3%81%99', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:35:54.829395Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.207516Z'),
(6247, 'vocabulary', '弾く', '弾く', 'You''d think that this means "to bullet" but it does not. It means <vocabulary>to play a stringed instrument</vocabulary>. It''s actually used for all stringed instruments, and all instruments with a keyboard, like pianos and electric keyboards. Just imagine taking a bullet and using it as a pick for a guitar, then using it to press down the keys of a piano and play a tune.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6247', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%BE%E3%81%8F', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:36:01.915571Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:02:44.340812Z'),
(6248, 'vocabulary', '言葉遣い', '言葉遣い', 'You may remember 言葉 means "word" and 遣う means "to use." That makes this your <vocabulary>choice of words</vocabulary> or, more simply, your <vocabulary>word use</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6248', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%80%E8%91%89%E9%81%A3%E3%81%84', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:36:08.905451Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:10:47.677773Z'),
(6249, 'vocabulary', '小遣い', '小遣い', '遣う means "to use," so a "<kanji>small</kanji> use" refers to what you can do with your <vocabulary>spending money</vocabulary>. You''re not rolling in dough over here, so you can only use your spending money on small things. Think of it like a little <vocabulary>allowance</vocabulary> you can use to buy baseball cards and bubblegum and stuff.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6249', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E9%81%A3%E3%81%84', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:36:14.53679Z', NULL, '2025-07-22T20:52:02.431716Z'),
(6250, 'vocabulary', '削除する', '削除する', 'If 削除 is "deletion," then the verb version would be <vocabulary>to delete</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6250', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%8A%E9%99%A4%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:36:19.288974Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.277719Z'),
(6251, 'vocabulary', '掲示板', '掲示板', 'A 掲示 is a notice or sign. Put this on a <kanji>board</kanji>, and it becomes a <vocabulary>bulletin board</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6251', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%B2%E7%A4%BA%E6%9D%BF', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:36:25.193337Z', NULL, '2026-02-03T01:43:38.646573Z'),
(6252, 'vocabulary', '渋滞', '渋滞', 'A <kanji>bitter</kanji> <kanji>stagnate</kanji> is when you''re stagnated in traffic. It feels bitter, doesn''t it? Why can''t people drive faster? Why are people looking at the accident instead of driving? I''m so bitter about it! That''s why this word is <vocabulary>traffic jam</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>traffic congestion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6252', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%8B%E6%BB%9E', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:36:36.033122Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.30077Z'),
(6253, 'vocabulary', '募集中', '募集中', '<kanji>Recruit</kanji> and <kanji>gather</kanji> everyone to the <kanji>middle</kanji>. They''re all here gathering for this recruitment because we''re <vocabulary>now hiring</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>now recruiting</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6253', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%9F%E9%9B%86%E4%B8%AD', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:36:41.710802Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.313555Z'),
(6254, 'vocabulary', '戻り道', '戻り道', 'If 戻る is "to <kanji>return</kanji>" then you return on the <kanji>street</kanji> you came from, you are taking the <vocabulary>way back</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6254', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%BB%E3%82%8A%E9%81%93', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:36:47.991556Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.324514Z'),
(6255, 'vocabulary', '取り戻す', '取り戻す', 'To <kanji>take</kanji> and <kanji>return</kanji> something to yourself is <vocabulary>to take back</vocabulary> something. Give it back!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6255', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8A%E6%88%BB%E3%81%99', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:36:55.172051Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.336139Z'),
(6256, 'vocabulary', '原子爆弾', '原子爆弾', '原子 is atom and 爆弾 is bomb. What is an atom bomb? An <vocabulary>atomic bomb</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6256', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%9F%E5%AD%90%E7%88%86%E5%BC%BE', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:37:01.707535Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:33:28.795445Z'),
(6257, 'vocabulary', '足跡', '足跡', '<kanji>Foot</kanji> <kanji>traces</kanji> are <vocabulary>footprint</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6257', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B6%B3%E8%B7%A1', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:37:08.183831Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.706403Z'),
(6258, 'vocabulary', '狙う', '狙う', 'Remember 狙い and how it means "aim"? Make that into a verb, and you have <vocabulary>to aim at</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6258', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%99%E3%81%86', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:37:15.817541Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.370937Z'),
(6259, 'vocabulary', '邦訳', '邦訳', 'A <kanji>home country</kanji> <kanji>translation</kanji> is a translation to your home country''s language. Since this is a Japanese word, this translation would be a <vocabulary>translation into Japanese</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6259', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%82%A6%E8%A8%B3', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:37:21.844565Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.383248Z'),
(6260, 'vocabulary', '邦人', '邦人', 'A <kanji>home country</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>fellow countryman</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>compatriot</vocabulary> (only for Japanese people though). In Japan, you''re a <vocabulary>Japanese national</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6260', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%82%A6%E4%BA%BA', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:37:28.1163Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(6261, 'vocabulary', '連邦', '連邦', '<kanji>Take along</kanji> your <kanji>home country</kanji> to all the other spaces you want to add to your group. Put them together to form the <vocabulary>commonwealth</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>federation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6261', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%A3%E9%82%A6', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:37:33.791195Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T08:20:55.660865Z'),
(6262, 'vocabulary', '呼び鈴', '呼び鈴', 'A <kanji>buzzer</kanji> that will <kanji>call</kanji> you to the door is a <vocabulary>doorbell</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6262', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%BC%E3%81%B3%E9%88%B4', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:37:41.20107Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.418119Z'),
(6263, 'vocabulary', '鈴', '鈴', 'The meaning for this word comes from the kanji. It''s a <vocabulary>small bell</vocabulary>!

鈴 typically refers to small, spherical bells that jingle or tinkle — think sleigh bells or the bell on a dog collar, or the bells hanging in Japanese shrines that visitors ring before praying (though these can range in size).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6263', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%88%B4', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:37:49.244759Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:20.449488Z'),
(6264, 'vocabulary', '岐阜', '岐阜', 'The <kanji>branch off</kanji> <kanji>mound</kanji> is the area of Japan known as <vocabulary>Gifu</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6264', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B2%90%E9%98%9C', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:37:58.158735Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.440242Z'),
(6265, 'vocabulary', '隆盛', '隆盛', '<kanji>Prosperity</kanji> in <kanji>piles</kanji> is a lot of <vocabulary>prosperity</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6265', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%86%E7%9B%9B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:38:09.534393Z', NULL, '2025-10-13T22:29:21.634411Z'),
(6266, 'vocabulary', '隆起', '隆起', 'When <kanji>prosperity</kanji> <kanji>wakes up</kanji> it will push through whatever it''s in and create a <vocabulary>protuberance</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>bulge</vocabulary>. Just imagine it pushing out of the wall, trying to get out, making a bulge. So prosperous!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6266', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%86%E8%B5%B7', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:38:14.744121Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.466088Z'),
(6267, 'vocabulary', '雇う', '雇う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>employ</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to employ</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6267', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%87%E3%81%86', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:38:20.386998Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:47:35.976472Z'),
(6268, 'vocabulary', '解雇する', '解雇する', 'You are going to <kanji>untie</kanji> the person who is in your <kanji>employ</kanji> from being employed with you. They are free. This means you fired them, which is why this verb means <vocabulary>to fire someone</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6268', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A3%E9%9B%87%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:38:26.548804Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:47:35.976472Z'),
(6269, 'vocabulary', '雇用', '雇用', 'The <kanji>employ</kanji> <kanji>task</kanji> is <vocabulary>employment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6269', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%87%E7%94%A8', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:38:33.389123Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:47:35.976472Z'),
(6270, 'vocabulary', '壁', '壁', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6270', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%81', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:38:57.320436Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.520456Z'),
(6271, 'vocabulary', '壁紙', '壁紙', 'A <kanji>wall</kanji>''s <kanji>paper</kanji> is <vocabulary>wallpaper</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6271', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%81%E7%B4%99', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:39:04.4489Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.533129Z'),
(6272, 'vocabulary', '棋士', '棋士', 'A <kanji>chess piece</kanji> <kanji>samurai</kanji> is someone who plays chess or go, making this word a <vocabulary>shogi player</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>go player</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6272', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A3%8B%E5%A3%AB', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:39:14.337054Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:09:00.472911Z'),
(6273, 'vocabulary', '棋院', '棋院', 'A <kanji>chess piece</kanji> <kanji>institution</kanji> is where everyone loves to play chess. But let''s imagine it''s in Japan. If Japanese chess is more properly known as go, then that makes this a <vocabulary>go association</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>go institution</vocabulary>.

A 棋院 is an organizational body for professional and amateur go players. It puts on tournaments, assigns rankings, and even hosts actual matches sometimes, which is why this can also be translated as a <vocabulary>go hall</vocabulary> in some contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6273', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A3%8B%E9%99%A2', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:39:18.80112Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:08:14.076898Z'),
(6274, 'vocabulary', '渋い', '渋い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. The adjective form of <kanji>bitter</kanji> is <vocabulary>bitter</vocabulary>, or more specifically, a <vocabulary>bitter flavor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6274', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%8B%E3%81%84', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:39:24.136842Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.583419Z'),
(6275, 'vocabulary', '片〜', '片〜', 'Something that is <kanji>one-sided</kanji> has only one side and not the whole. That''s just <vocabulary>one of a pair</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>fragment</vocabulary> of the whole. Stick this onto another word and you have one of a pair or an "incomplete_____."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6275', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%87%E3%80%9C', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:39:35.190626Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:20.520866Z'),
(6276, 'vocabulary', '片手', '片手', 'A <kanji>one sided</kanji> <kanji>hand</kanji> is one of your hands, that means you''re doing something <vocabulary>one handed</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6276', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%87%E6%89%8B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:39:40.757425Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.610083Z'),
(6277, 'vocabulary', '片言', '片言', '<kanji>One sided</kanji> <kanji>say</kanji>ing things means you don''t know both sides of the language, causing you to speak in <vocabulary>broken language</vocabulary>, for example speaking <vocabulary>a few words</vocabulary> in <vocabulary>broken Japanese</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>broken English</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6277', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%87%E8%A8%80', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:39:47.726643Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.63466Z'),
(6278, 'vocabulary', '片仮名', '片仮名', '<kanji>One-sided</kanji> <kanji>kana</kanji> is <vocabulary>katakana</vocabulary>. Might be easier to do the reading first on this one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6278', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%87%E4%BB%AE%E5%90%8D', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:39:59.646644Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(6279, 'vocabulary', '片道', '片道', 'A <kanji>one sided</kanji> <kanji>street</kanji> is for <vocabulary>one way</vocabulary> only, because everything just goes in one direction.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6279', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%87%E9%81%93', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:40:07.262682Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.658836Z'),
(6280, 'vocabulary', '群れ', '群れ', 'This is the noun version of the kanji, making it <vocabulary>flock</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6280', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%A4%E3%82%8C', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:40:19.493741Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.670926Z'),
(6281, 'vocabulary', '群馬県', '群馬県', 'The <kanji>flock</kanji> of <kanji>horse</kanji>s <kanji>prefecture</kanji> is the part of Japan known as <vocabulary>Gunma Prefecture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6281', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%A4%E9%A6%AC%E7%9C%8C', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:40:40.466068Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:25:07.757919Z'),
(6282, 'vocabulary', '群集', '群集', 'A <kanji>flock</kanji> that has <kanji>gather</kanji>ed is a <vocabulary>crowd</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>multitude</vocabulary>. Because, well, lots of things gathering.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6282', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%A4%E9%9B%86', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:40:51.843127Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.693685Z'),
(6283, 'vocabulary', '仙人', '仙人', 'A <kanji>hermit</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>hermit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>mountain man</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6283', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%99%E4%BA%BA', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:41:05.057772Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(6284, 'vocabulary', '水仙', '水仙', 'A <kanji>water</kanji> <kanji>hermit</kanji>, for some reason, is a <vocabulary>daffodil</vocabulary>. Just imagine a water hermit dancing around picking <vocabulary>daffodil</vocabulary>s, because he loves ''em! Yayyy daffodils!!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6284', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E4%BB%99', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:41:10.409533Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.716581Z'),
(6285, 'vocabulary', '仙台', '仙台', 'The <kanji>hermit</kanji> <kanji>machine</kanji> is a giant machine that lives in the <vocabulary>Sendai</vocabulary> area of Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6285', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%99%E5%8F%B0', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:41:18.712379Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.727986Z'),
(6286, 'vocabulary', '充実', '充実', '<kanji>Allocate</kanji> the <kanji>truth</kanji> more and more to make things better. The truth is, we need to allocate even more, until we''ve reached <vocabulary>fullness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6286', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%85%E5%AE%9F', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:41:24.701429Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.738911Z'),
(6287, 'vocabulary', '充電', '充電', '<kanji>Allocate</kanji> <kanji>electricity</kanji> back to the battery with a <vocabulary>recharge</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6287', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%85%E9%9B%BB', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:41:29.915317Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.030484Z'),
(6288, 'vocabulary', '補充', '補充', 'To take a <kanji>supplement</kanji> and <kanji>allocate</kanji> it to where it needs to be is to <vocabulary>resupply</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>refill</vocabulary> something.

補充 means filling in the gaps when there''s a shortage. It''s used for things like <vocabulary>restock</vocabulary>ing supplies, replacing what''s been used, or adding more staff to meet demand. The goal is to bring things back to a needed or complete level.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6288', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%9C%E5%85%85', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:41:36.869424Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:31:35.096929Z'),
(6289, 'vocabulary', '免れる', '免れる', 'You are going to <kanji>excuse</kanji> yourself from the situation, and in order to do that, you have <vocabulary>to escape from</vocabulary> the person / thing trying to hold you there in order <vocabulary>to avoid</vocabulary> them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6289', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%8D%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:41:41.614866Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.782774Z'),
(6290, 'vocabulary', '免状', '免状', 'Your <kanji>excuse</kanji> for the current <kanji>condition</kanji> is surprising, especially considering your <vocabulary>diploma</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>license</vocabulary> on the issue. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6290', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%8D%E7%8A%B6', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:41:47.59523Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.796249Z'),
(6291, 'vocabulary', '免許', '免許', 'You have an <kanji>excuse</kanji> that <kanji>permit</kanji>s you to do what you want. It is a <vocabulary>license</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>certificate</vocabulary> allowing you to do this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6291', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%8D%E8%A8%B1', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:41:54.352053Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.809086Z'),
(6292, 'vocabulary', '免税', '免税', 'An <kanji>excuse</kanji> from <kanji>tax</kanji>es? That''s a <vocabulary>tax exemption</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6292', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%8D%E7%A8%8E', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:42:01.867219Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:45:00.948456Z'),
(6293, 'vocabulary', '御免', '御免', 'An <kanji>honorable</kanji> <kanji>excuse</kanji> is all I have, and that is no good. I''m <vocabulary>sorry</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>I beg your pardon</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6293', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%A1%E5%85%8D', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:42:08.385708Z', NULL, '2025-08-25T16:30:28.632013Z'),
(6294, 'vocabulary', '免除', '免除', 'To <kanji>excuse</kanji> and to <kanji>exclude</kanji>, that''s <vocabulary>exemption</vocabulary>.

免除 means granting relief or exemption from a duty or obligation that would otherwise be required, like paying tuition fees, repaying debts, obtaining specific licenses for certain activities, and so on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6294', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%8D%E9%99%A4', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:42:18.481367Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.846674Z'),
(6295, 'vocabulary', '勧める', '勧める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>recommend</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to recommend</vocabulary>.

', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6295', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%A7%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2013-06-07T17:42:23.18943Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:36.985747Z'),
(6296, 'vocabulary', '勧告', '勧告', 'If you take what you <kanji>recommend</kanji> and <kanji>announce</kanji> it to the world, you are making a <vocabulary>recommendation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6296', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%A7%E5%91%8A', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:42:27.487773Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:34:31.16393Z'),
(6297, 'vocabulary', '勧誘', '勧誘', 'I <kanji>recommend</kanji> this to you, so I''d like to <kanji>invite</kanji> you to try it. This is a <vocabulary>solicitation</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>invitation</vocabulary>.

勧誘 means soliciting someone to buy a product or service or inviting them to join a group or activity, often in a pushy way. It''s commonly used to describe what telemarketers and door-to-door evangelists do, or for students recruiting new members for extracurricular activities.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6297', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%A7%E8%AA%98', 29, 1, '2013-06-07T17:42:31.920865Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.030758Z'),
(6298, 'vocabulary', '圏外', '圏外', 'The <kanji>range</kanji> is <kanji>outside</kanji> of the range, meaning you''re <vocabulary>out of range</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6298', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%8F%E5%A4%96', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:42:36.429574Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(6299, 'vocabulary', '英語圏', '英語圏', 'The <kanji>English</kanji> <kanji>range</kanji> is the areas where people speak English, aka the <vocabulary>English Speaking World</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6299', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%B1%E8%AA%9E%E5%9C%8F', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:42:42.002487Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.940601Z'),
(6300, 'vocabulary', '南極圏', '南極圏', 'Remember how 南極 means "South Pole" or the "Antarctic"? Add <kanji>range</kanji> onto that word and you get the region known as the <vocabulary>Antarctic Circle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6300', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%97%E6%A5%B5%E5%9C%8F', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:42:48.659581Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T15:17:49.222453Z'),
(6301, 'vocabulary', '埋める', '埋める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>bury</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to bury</vocabulary> as well.

埋める can also mean <vocabulary>to fill</vocabulary>, and can refer to filling a hole, vacancy, or gap. Note that this word can be used to refer to filling in figurative holes and gaps too, like a hole in one’s finances!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6301', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%8B%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2013-06-07T17:42:52.901798Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:31.97917Z'),
(6302, 'vocabulary', '埼玉県', '埼玉県', 'The <kanji>cape</kanji> <kanji>ball</kanji> <kanji>prefecture</kanji> is <vocabulary>Saitama Prefecture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6302', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%BC%E7%8E%89%E7%9C%8C', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:43:08.283016Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:21:57.094904Z'),
(6303, 'vocabulary', '奪う', '奪う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>rob</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to rob</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6303', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%AA%E3%81%86', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:43:13.075382Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:28:32.471313Z'),
(6304, 'vocabulary', '御飯', '御飯', 'The <kanji>honorable</kanji> <kanji>meal</kanji> is <vocabulary>cooked rice</vocabulary>. It''s the meal of Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6304', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%A1%E9%A3%AF', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:43:18.945215Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:59:19.67524Z'),
(6305, 'vocabulary', '御覧になる', '御覧になる', 'Add an <kanji>honorable</kanji> little 御 for someone to <kanji>look at</kanji> when you want to show respect to them. 御覧になる is the honorific form of 見る, so it still means <vocabulary>to look</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to see</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to watch</vocabulary>, but with a more respectful tone.

You''ll often use it when someone of higher status than you is doing the looking and you want to sound like a polite, refined adult who''s showing proper respect to them. Keep in mind that the first 御 is often written in kana, like ご覧になる.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6305', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%A1%E8%A6%A7%E3%81%AB%E3%81%AA%E3%82%8B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:43:30.310927Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:18:00.93106Z'),
(6306, 'vocabulary', '慎む', '慎む', 'If you have <kanji>humility</kanji> you will try <vocabulary>to be discreet</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6306', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%8E%E3%82%80', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:43:41.323183Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:29:49.214205Z'),
(6307, 'vocabulary', '慎重', '慎重', '<kanji>Humility</kanji> is <kanji>heavy</kanji>, so it''s best nobody knows your secret. You should be <vocabulary>discreet</vocabulary> instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6307', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%8E%E9%87%8D', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:43:46.132975Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:29:49.214205Z'),
(6308, 'vocabulary', '拒む', '拒む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>refuse</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to refuse</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6308', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%92%E3%82%80', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:44:02.710129Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:10:45.058569Z'),
(6309, 'vocabulary', '拒否', '拒否', 'I <kanji>refuse</kanji>. I am saying "<kanji>no</kanji>." That is my <vocabulary>refusal</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6309', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%92%E5%90%A6', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:44:15.667335Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.075792Z'),
(6310, 'vocabulary', '枠', '枠', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6310', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%A0', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:44:25.665268Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.087595Z'),
(6311, 'vocabulary', '枠組み', '枠組み', 'The <kanji>frame</kanji> <kanji>group</kanji> is a <vocabulary>frame</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>framework</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6311', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%A0%E7%B5%84%E3%81%BF', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:44:32.312247Z', NULL, '2026-04-05T19:31:59.040578Z'),
(6312, 'vocabulary', '甲', '甲', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

甲 has traditionally meant <vocabulary>turtle shell</vocabulary>, but these days it appears much more often in expressions like 足の甲 (<vocabulary>instep</vocabulary>) and 手の甲 (<vocabulary>back of the hand</vocabulary>). If you think about it, those body parts kind of resemble turtle shells, don’t you think?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6312', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B2', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:44:37.571281Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.110865Z'),
(6313, 'vocabulary', '甲斐', '甲斐', 'A <kanji>turtle shell</kanji> that is <kanji>patterned</kanji> has much more <vocabulary>worth</vocabulary> than a boring non-patterned turtle shell. It probably has a better <vocabulary>effect</vocabulary> too if you throw it at your opponent in a go-kart race or something — give it a shot and see what the <vocabulary>result</vocabulary> is!

甲斐 doesn''t mean "worth" in the sense of monetary value, but rather in the sense that some action you took paid off or was worth doing. You''ll usually see it paired with verbs or other nouns, like 苦労の甲斐があった (it was worth the trouble) or 頑張った甲斐があった (trying hard paid off).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6313', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B2%E6%96%90', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:44:43.062199Z', NULL, '2025-09-30T19:05:33.31225Z'),
(6314, 'vocabulary', '福祉', '福祉', 'You have much <kanji>luck</kanji> in your <kanji>welfare</kanji>, making you have good <vocabulary>welfare</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>well being</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6314', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A6%8F%E7%A5%89', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:44:49.594291Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.135908Z'),
(6315, 'vocabulary', '稲', '稲', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6315', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%B2', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:45:03.853468Z', NULL, '2025-07-24T17:10:46.943742Z'),
(6316, 'vocabulary', '稲田', '稲田', 'A <kanji>rice plant</kanji> <kanji>rice paddy</kanji> is a <vocabulary>rice field</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>rice paddy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6316', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%B2%E7%94%B0', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:45:10.942552Z', NULL, '2025-07-24T17:20:49.014101Z'),
(6317, 'vocabulary', '稲作', '稲作', 'If you <kanji>make</kanji> a <kanji>rice plant</kanji> then you are doing <vocabulary>rice cultivation</vocabulary>, since you''re making it from the seed to the harvest and doing it all!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6317', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%B2%E4%BD%9C', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:45:19.784875Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:12:39.703958Z'),
(6318, 'vocabulary', '譲る', '譲る', 'If you are going to <kanji>defer</kanji> to someone, you are going <vocabulary>to concede</vocabulary> something. I concede / defer this to you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6318', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%B2%E3%82%8B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:45:24.727223Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.185084Z'),
(6319, 'vocabulary', '移譲', '移譲', '<kanji>Shift</kanji> something and <kanji>defer</kanji> it somewhere else. This is a <vocabulary>transfer</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>assignment</vocabulary>.

This is a formal term used to describe the legal transfer of rights, properties, or liabilities, so it''s most commonly seen in official or legal documents.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6319', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%BB%E8%AD%B2', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:45:32.227245Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.196579Z'),
(6320, 'vocabulary', '謙譲語', '謙譲語', '<kanji>Language</kanji> when you <kanji>defer</kanji> to <kanji>modesty</kanji> is <vocabulary>humble Japanese</vocabulary>. Of course, this doesn''t have to refer to Japanese, though it usually does, meaning this could mean <vocabulary>humble language</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6320', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AC%99%E8%AD%B2%E8%AA%9E', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:45:39.538403Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.209119Z'),
(6321, 'vocabulary', '謙虚', '謙虚', 'The <kanji>modesty</kanji> <kanji>void</kanji> is that big pit you go into when you are showing <vocabulary>modesty</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>humility</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6321', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AC%99%E8%99%9A', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:45:45.20723Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.222175Z'),
(6322, 'vocabulary', '躍る', '躍る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>leap</kanji> so the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to leap</vocabulary>. It also means <vocabulary>to jump</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to bounce</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6322', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BA%8D%E3%82%8B', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:45:58.538656Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.235336Z'),
(6323, 'vocabulary', '飛躍', '飛躍', 'To <kanji>fly</kanji> and <kanji>leap</kanji> is <vocabulary>leaping</vocabulary>.

飛躍 is mainly used in a figurative way to highlight remarkable progress in areas like personal growth or technological advancement. It can also describe a logic that jumps suddenly from one point to another without clear connections.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6323', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9B%E8%BA%8D', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T17:46:04.830082Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.24787Z'),
(6324, 'vocabulary', '活躍', '活躍', 'A <kanji>lively</kanji> <kanji>leap</kanji> is what you do when you have <vocabulary>active participation</vocabulary> in an <vocabulary>activity</vocabulary> and often find <vocabulary>success</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>do great</vocabulary>.

This word is usually used to describe someone''s outstanding or remarkable activity in a certain sport, career, or other field or activity. Have a look at the common word combinations for some example usages.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6324', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%BB%E8%BA%8D', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:46:10.198094Z', NULL, '2025-10-07T00:13:30.183596Z'),
(6325, 'vocabulary', '躍進', '躍進', 'When you <kanji>leap</kanji> and <kanji>advance</kanji>, you make <vocabulary>rapid progress</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>great advancement</vocabulary>.

You might recall that 飛躍 also means "rapid progress," but what sets them apart? 躍進 emphasizes the subject''s perspective as it advances at a rapid pace, whereas 飛躍 conveys significant progress or leaps more neutrally, without highlighting pace or perspective.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6325', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BA%8D%E9%80%B2', 42, 1, '2013-06-07T17:46:15.236525Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.284912Z'),
(6326, 'vocabulary', '躍動', '躍動', 'Every <kanji>leap</kanji> you take is a <kanji>move</kanji> filled with <vocabulary>vibrancy</vocabulary>. Your <vocabulary>dynamic movement</vocabulary> captures the essence of <vocabulary>lively motion</vocabulary>, captivating all who witness it.

躍動 is not just about movement, but also about expressing internal vitality or liveliness through movements or actions. It’s commonly used as 躍動感 (a lively feeling).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6326', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BA%8D%E5%8B%95', 42, 1, '2013-06-07T17:46:20.513817Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.298028Z'),
(6327, 'vocabulary', '跳躍', '跳躍', 'If you <kanji>hop</kanji> and <kanji>leap</kanji> into the air, you probably are doing a <vocabulary>jump</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>leap</vocabulary> into the air.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6327', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B7%B3%E8%BA%8D', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:46:56.99633Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.308738Z'),
(6328, 'vocabulary', '銃', '銃', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6328', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%83', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:47:03.338059Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:30:36.495728Z'),
(6329, 'vocabulary', '銃殺', '銃殺', 'A <kanji>gun</kanji> <kanji>kill</kanji> is a <vocabulary>death by shooting</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6329', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%83%E6%AE%BA', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:47:13.412007Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:30:36.495728Z'),
(6330, 'vocabulary', '銃弾', '銃弾', 'A <kanji>gun</kanji>''s <kanji>bullet</kanji> is a <vocabulary>bullet</vocabulary>, though this word can specifically refer to a <vocabulary>rifle bullet</vocabulary> as well (probably because it was mostly rifles that people had way back in the day).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6330', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%83%E5%BC%BE', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:47:19.184131Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:33:28.795445Z'),
(6331, 'vocabulary', '短銃', '短銃', 'A <kanji>short</kanji> <kanji>gun</kanji> is a <vocabulary>pistol</vocabulary>, because it''s not long like a rifle, it''s a short gun!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6331', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%AD%E9%8A%83', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:47:27.309391Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:30:36.495728Z'),
(6332, 'vocabulary', '項目', '項目', 'You need to go through a <kanji>paragraph</kanji> in chunks that you can easily see with your <kanji>eye</kanji> so you break it up by <vocabulary>item</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6332', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%85%E7%9B%AE', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:47:34.536283Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.38243Z'),
(6333, 'vocabulary', '事項', '事項', 'You know 事 means "occurrence," but it can also mean "matter." So a <kanji>matter</kanji> <kanji>paragraph</kanji> shows the <vocabulary>matter</vocabulary>s, <vocabulary>item</vocabulary>s, or <vocabulary>subject</vocabulary>s that make up a larger issue or discussion at hand.

事項 is a formal word usually seen at the end of another phrase that specifies what matters or items are being referred to, like 注意事項 (matters to be noted) or 協議事項 (items for discussion).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6333', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8B%E9%A0%85', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:47:40.848765Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:20.735963Z'),
(6334, 'vocabulary', '要項', '要項', 'A <kanji>need</kanji>ed <kanji>paragraph</kanji> contains the <vocabulary>important points</vocabulary> of what you want to say.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6334', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%81%E9%A0%85', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:47:54.65341Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:28:03.237353Z'),
(6335, 'vocabulary', '鋼', '鋼', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6335', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8B%BC', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:48:02.335428Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.416862Z'),
(6336, 'vocabulary', '鋼材', '鋼材', '<kanji>Steel</kanji> <kanji>material</kanji> is <vocabulary>steel material</vocabulary>. Boom!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6336', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8B%BC%E6%9D%90', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:48:10.022161Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.428501Z'),
(6337, 'vocabulary', '製鋼', '製鋼', 'If you <kanji>manufacture</kanji> <kanji>steel</kanji> that is <vocabulary>steel manufacture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6337', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%BD%E9%8B%BC', 39, 1, '2013-06-07T17:48:16.809677Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.440649Z'),
(6338, 'vocabulary', '宜しく', '宜しく', 'The kanji is <kanji>best regards</kanji> and this can be <vocabulary>best regards</vocabulary> as well. It can also mean <vocabulary>well</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>properly</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>suitably</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6338', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9C%E3%81%97%E3%81%8F', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:09:47.960679Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.452784Z'),
(6339, 'vocabulary', '宜しくお願いします', '宜しくお願いします', 'A 宜しい (good, okay, all right) <kanji>request</kanji> is when you send your <vocabulary>best regards</vocabulary> to someone. This is a set phrase you say when someone is going to help you out and you''re all like "thank you for your help" or "I''m counting on you" or some combination of the two. It''s a word you''ll see a lot to varying degrees of kanji and length.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6339', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9C%E3%81%97%E3%81%8F%E3%81%8A%E9%A1%98%E3%81%84%E3%81%97%E3%81%BE%E3%81%99', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:09:57.301501Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.464822Z'),
(6340, 'vocabulary', '繰り返す', '繰り返す', '繰る is "to spin" (so spinning) and 返す is "to return" (so returning). Put them together and you have something that''s spinning and returning to the same spot over and over. Something that does that is repeating itself, making this <vocabulary>to repeat</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to repeat over and over</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6340', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B9%B0%E3%82%8A%E8%BF%94%E3%81%99', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:10:04.135781Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:36:02.881025Z'),
(6341, 'vocabulary', '避難する', '避難する', 'If you know that 避難 is "evacuation" then you know that this is the verb form of that: <vocabulary>to evacuate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6341', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%BF%E9%9B%A3%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:10:09.00321Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.490824Z'),
(6342, 'vocabulary', '傾ける', '傾ける', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>lean</kanji> and the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to lean something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6342', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%BE%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:10:14.096799Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.503558Z'),
(6343, 'vocabulary', '奥様', '奥様', 'Your <kanji>interior</kanji> <kanji>formal name ender</kanji> (person) is the person who''s waiting internal, out of the picture (remember, kanji was made forever ago and is pretty sexist). This person is <vocabulary>your wife</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6343', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%A5%E6%A7%98', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:10:31.115872Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.515923Z'),
(6344, 'vocabulary', '遠慮する', '遠慮する', 'If 遠慮 is "decline" or "restrain" then the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to decline</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to restrain</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6344', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%A0%E6%85%AE%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:10:37.218547Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:51:52.975966Z'),
(6345, 'vocabulary', '懸かる', '懸かる', 'If something is <kanji>suspend</kanji>ed, it is going <vocabulary>to hang</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to depend on</vocabulary> your actions. We need you to make sure it goes right! Your actions are what we''re suspended on!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6345', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%87%B8%E3%81%8B%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:10:42.623479Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.544787Z'),
(6346, 'vocabulary', '一生懸命', '一生懸命', 'You have only <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>life</kanji> so do everything with lots of <kanji>eager</kanji>ness (懸命). Do everything <vocabulary>with all one''s might</vocabulary>. Go <vocabulary>all out</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6346', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E7%94%9F%E6%87%B8%E5%91%BD', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:10:49.466663Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.569055Z'),
(6347, 'vocabulary', '扱い', '扱い', 'If 扱う is "to handle," the noun version of that is <vocabulary>handling</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>treatment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6347', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%B1%E3%81%84', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:10:54.107226Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:51:13.659259Z'),
(6348, 'vocabulary', '取り扱う', '取り扱う', 'To <kanji>take</kanji> and then <kanji>handle</kanji> means you''re taking it and handling it, causing you <vocabulary>to manage</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to handle</vocabulary> something. You got it because you took it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6348', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8A%E6%89%B1%E3%81%86', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:10:59.266221Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.595134Z'),
(6349, 'vocabulary', '選択する', '選択する', '選択 is "selection" or "choice." If you do the verb version of this, you are going <vocabulary>to select</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to choose</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6349', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%B8%E6%8A%9E%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:11:05.185019Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.607237Z'),
(6350, 'vocabulary', '称える', '称える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>title</kanji>, so just think about when someone gives you an awesome title. They are doing this <vocabulary>to praise</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to give praise</vocabulary> to you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6350', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%B0%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:11:11.09672Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.618357Z'),
(6351, 'vocabulary', '内緒話', '内緒話', 'Remember how 内緒 means "secret" or "confidential"? Well, that makes this is a secret <kanji>talk</kanji>, aka a <vocabulary>confidential talk</vocabulary> — like the kind often done by <vocabulary>whispering</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6351', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%85%E7%B7%92%E8%A9%B1', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:11:17.100028Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T00:09:48.530936Z'),
(6352, 'vocabulary', '緩める', '緩める', '緩む is "to loosen," but has to do with loosening on its own. This word is when you loosen or relax something, meaning <vocabulary>to loosen</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to relax</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6352', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A9%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:11:24.072201Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.64165Z'),
(6353, 'vocabulary', '贈り物', '贈り物', 'Remember 贈る, which means "to give a present"? Well, a <kanji>thing</kanji> that you give as a present is <vocabulary>a present</vocabulary>.

Since 贈り物 comes from the verb 贈る, it describes <vocabulary>a gift</vocabulary> not just as an object but as something given with care. You''ll often see it in writing or polite speech when someone wants to emphasize the warmth or sincerity behind a gift.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6353', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B4%88%E3%82%8A%E7%89%A9', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:11:29.081994Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(6354, 'vocabulary', '葬儀', '葬儀', 'A <kanji>burial</kanji> <kanji>ceremony</kanji> is known as a <vocabulary>funeral</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6354', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%91%AC%E5%84%80', 41, 1, '2013-06-07T18:11:35.18321Z', NULL, '2025-12-25T03:08:56.417913Z'),
(6355, 'vocabulary', '沖縄県', '沖縄県', 'Remember how 沖縄 is "Okinawa"? This is the prefecture, <vocabulary>Okinawa Prefecture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6355', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%96%E7%B8%84%E7%9C%8C', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:11:40.244116Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:25:07.757919Z'),
(6356, 'vocabulary', '贈賄', '贈賄', '<kanji>Presents</kanji> that you give as a <kanji>bribe</kanji> is a form of <vocabulary>bribery</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6356', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B4%88%E8%B3%84', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:11:47.62022Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.687134Z'),
(6357, 'vocabulary', '逃す', '逃す', 'You already learned that 逃げる means "to escape," and this verb is when you let something escape, or slip through your fingers. So this means <vocabulary>to miss</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to lose</vocabulary> a good chance, or <vocabulary>to let go</vocabulary> of a good opportunity.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6357', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%83%E3%81%99', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:12:01.903038Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.712678Z'),
(6358, 'vocabulary', '逃亡者', '逃亡者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> who has <kanji>escape</kanji>d <kanji>death</kanji> by running away is a <vocabulary>fugitive</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>runaway</vocabulary>. I think there was a movie about that once!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6358', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%83%E4%BA%A1%E8%80%85', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:12:06.757765Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(6359, 'vocabulary', '顧問', '顧問', 'The people who will <kanji>review</kanji> a <kanji>problem</kanji> are those who know more about the problems. They are the <vocabulary>advisor</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6359', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A1%A7%E5%95%8F', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:12:33.437571Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.747752Z'),
(6360, 'vocabulary', '顧みる', '顧みる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>review</kanji>. What do you do when you review something? You look back on it. Meaning <vocabulary>to look back on something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to reflect on something</vocabulary>. This can also mean <vocabulary>to be attentive to someone</vocabulary>, for example your family.

This is a transitive verb, meaning it takes a direct object. You look back on the past, you reflect on the consequences of your actions, and you''re attentive to your family.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6360', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A1%A7%E3%81%BF%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:12:38.80177Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.771464Z'),
(6361, 'vocabulary', '回顧録', '回顧録', 'You <kanji>revolve</kanji> to <kanji>review</kanji> the <kanji>record</kanji> of the past. These records are <vocabulary>memoirs</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>reminiscences</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6361', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9E%E9%A1%A7%E9%8C%B2', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:12:44.901694Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.783109Z'),
(6362, 'vocabulary', '駐日', '駐日', 'Something or someone <kanji>resident</kanji> in 日本 (Japan) is <vocabulary>stationed in Japan</vocabulary>.

駐日 is generally used to describe both individuals and institutions stationed in Japan for official duties, such as the 駐日大使 (Ambassador to Japan) or 駐日大使館 (embassy stationed in Japan).

', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6362', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%90%E6%97%A5', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:12:52.526264Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.796815Z'),
(6363, 'vocabulary', '駐在', '駐在', 'You''re a <kanji>resident</kanji> and you <kanji>exist</kanji> in the place you''re residing in. That means you have <vocabulary>residence</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6363', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%90%E5%9C%A8', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:12:58.037084Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.810485Z'),
(6364, 'vocabulary', '駐車場', '駐車場', 'A <kanji>location</kanji> where <kanji>car</kanji>s can be a <kanji>resident</kanji> is a <vocabulary>parking lot</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6364', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%90%E8%BB%8A%E5%A0%B4', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:13:03.057261Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.822921Z'),
(6365, 'vocabulary', '駐留軍', '駐留軍', 'A <kanji>resident</kanji> <kanji>detain</kanji>ed in a location who is in the <kanji>army</kanji> is a member of the <vocabulary>stationed troops</vocabulary> at this location. They could also be the <vocabulary>occupying troops</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6365', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%90%E7%95%99%E8%BB%8D', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:13:11.062433Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.834077Z'),
(6366, 'vocabulary', '駆ける', '駆ける', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>gallop</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to gallop</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6366', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%86%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:13:15.916368Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.847341Z'),
(6367, 'vocabulary', '柱', '柱', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6367', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%B1', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:13:25.576235Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.858555Z'),
(6368, 'vocabulary', '電柱', '電柱', 'An <kanji>electricity</kanji> <kanji>pillar</kanji> is a <vocabulary>telephone pole</vocabulary>. All those wires and telephone wires going to and from those telephone poles.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6368', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E6%9F%B1', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:13:33.149433Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.030484Z'),
(6369, 'vocabulary', '唱える', '唱える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>chant</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to chant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6369', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%94%B1%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:13:42.413351Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.883055Z'),
(6370, 'vocabulary', '主唱', '主唱', 'The <kanji>master</kanji> <kanji>chant</kanji>er is the one who is the loudest. He makes sure everyone hears his chants. He''s the main <vocabulary>advocate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6370', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB%E5%94%B1', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:13:49.195128Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.894855Z'),
(6371, 'vocabulary', '唱歌', '唱歌', 'You''re <kanji>chant</kanji>ing and you''re <kanji>sing</kanji>ing and you''re having a gay ol'' time. You''re <vocabulary>singing</vocabulary> all your <vocabulary>songs</vocabulary> and even the chanting sounds like singing. It''s so fun.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6371', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%94%B1%E6%AD%8C', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:13:55.192819Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:03:31.014085Z'),
(6372, 'vocabulary', '独唱', '独唱', 'An <kanji>alone</kanji> <kanji>chant</kanji> is one you do alone. It''s a <vocabulary>solo</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6372', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%AC%E5%94%B1', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:14:04.308045Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.919279Z'),
(6373, 'vocabulary', '歌唱', '歌唱', 'You''re <kanji>sing</kanji>ing and you''re <kanji>chant</kanji>ing. It''s a gay ol'' time. You''re <vocabulary>singing</vocabulary> and singing and singing!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6373', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%8C%E5%94%B1', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:14:10.814225Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:03:31.014085Z'),
(6374, 'vocabulary', '合唱団', '合唱団', 'People who <kanji>join</kanji> in a <kanji>chant</kanji> <kanji>group</kanji> are people who are in a <vocabulary>chorus</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>choir</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6374', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%88%E5%94%B1%E5%9B%A3', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:14:15.96087Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.942262Z'),
(6375, 'vocabulary', '孝行', '孝行', 'If you <kanji>go</kanji> towards <kanji>filial piety</kanji>, you are going to be doing <vocabulary>filial piety</vocabulary>. Do this for your parents.

孝行 is commonly used in the phrase 親孝行 (also meaning "filial piety") when someone does something kind or thoughtful for their parents, like visiting often, helping them, giving them a gift, or taking them on a trip. By extension, it can also sometimes describe <vocabulary>showing devotion</vocabulary> toward other loved ones — caring for and respecting them almost <em>as though</em> they were a parent.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6375', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%9D%E8%A1%8C', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:14:22.444656Z', NULL, '2025-11-10T19:11:40.832898Z'),
(6376, 'vocabulary', '不孝', '不孝', '<kanji>Not</kanji> <kanji>filial piety</kanji> is when you don''t listen to your parents. This is <vocabulary>disobedience to parents</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6376', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%AD%9D', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:14:27.803761Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.964848Z'),
(6377, 'vocabulary', '俊才', '俊才', 'A person with <kanji>genius</kanji> and <kanji>talent</kanji> is a <vocabulary>genius</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>person of exceptional talent</vocabulary>. So smart!

俊才 is an advanced word usually only seen in writing. It tends to refer more to the kind of genius or talent that can be developed through effort. In that sense it differs from 天才, which is for the kind of genius that comes from heaven — either you have it or you don''t.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6377', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%8A%E6%89%8D', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:14:41.247274Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:32.975934Z'),
(6378, 'vocabulary', '兼業', '兼業', '<kanji>Concurrently</kanji> with my current <kanji>business</kanji> I have a <vocabulary>side business</vocabulary>. It''s really a <vocabulary>second job</vocabulary>, actually.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6378', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%BC%E6%A5%AD', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:14:45.913707Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(6379, 'vocabulary', '兼用', '兼用', 'If you <kanji>concurrently</kanji> <kanji>use</kanji> one thing for multiple purposes, you''re using something with a <vocabulary>combined use</vocabulary>. Something that is <vocabulary>serving two purposes</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6379', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%BC%E7%94%A8', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:14:54.550872Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.00096Z'),
(6380, 'vocabulary', '兼ねる', '兼ねる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>concurrently</kanji>, but since you can''t "to concurrently", we''re going to have to come up with another word to use: "<vocabulary>to combine</vocabulary>" or "<vocabulary>to double as</vocabulary>." Since this kanji has to do with something having a dual purpose, we can say that something can combine as being another thing, or double as being another thing, which is where these words come from.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6380', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%BC%E3%81%AD%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:15:03.865984Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T08:55:40.409547Z'),
(6381, 'vocabulary', '洗剤', '洗剤', '<kanji>Wash</kanji> your clothes with a <kanji>dose</kanji> of <vocabulary>detergent</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6381', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%97%E5%89%A4', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:15:09.728342Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(6382, 'vocabulary', '薬剤', '薬剤', 'A <kanji>medicine</kanji> <kanji>dose</kanji> is <vocabulary>medicine</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>drugs</vocabulary> you''re taking.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6382', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%AC%E5%89%A4', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:15:18.326353Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.08358Z'),
(6383, 'vocabulary', '薬剤師', '薬剤師', 'A <kanji>medicine</kanji> <kanji>dose</kanji> <kanji>expert</kanji> is someone who knows exactly how much dose of each medicine you should be getting. The person who specializes in this is a <vocabulary>pharmacist</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6383', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%AC%E5%89%A4%E5%B8%AB', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:15:25.153537Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.096475Z'),
(6384, 'vocabulary', '防腐剤', '防腐剤', 'A <kanji>prevent</kanji> <kanji>rot</kanji> <kanji>dose</kanji> is a dose of something you put on your cut or wound that prevents it from rotting (or you put it on something else to prevent it from rotting). This is an <vocabulary>antiseptic</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>preservative</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6384', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%98%B2%E8%85%90%E5%89%A4', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:15:33.746988Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:33:00.426254Z'),
(6385, 'vocabulary', '〜剤', '〜剤', 'If you take a <kanji>dose</kanji> of something, you are taking a <vocabulary>dose</vocabulary> of <vocabulary>medicine</vocabulary>. Take your cough syrup, child!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6385', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%89%A4', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:15:40.23817Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.121031Z'),
(6386, 'vocabulary', '吹く', '吹く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>blow</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to blow</vocabulary>.

This word can be either intransitive or transitive. So, something can be blowing on its own, as in 風が吹く (the wind blows), or you can blow something, like 笛を吹く (to blow a whistle).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6386', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%B9%E3%81%8F', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:15:49.627899Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:44:53.789091Z'),
(6387, 'vocabulary', '堀', '堀', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6387', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%80', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:16:17.306396Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:32:01.704322Z'),
(6388, 'vocabulary', '内堀', '内堀', 'The <kanji>inside</kanji> <kanji>ditch</kanji> is the inner ditch, or, if you make it awesome enough, the <vocabulary>inner moat</vocabulary>. If they get through the outer moat, we have the inner one, at least!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6388', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%85%E5%A0%80', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:16:29.250763Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:32:01.704322Z'),
(6389, 'vocabulary', '外堀', '外堀', 'The <kanji>outside</kanji> <kanji>ditch</kanji> is the ditch around our town that''s on the outside (not the inside ditch, which is the inner moat). This is the <vocabulary>outer moat</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6389', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E5%A0%80', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:16:34.788184Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(6390, 'vocabulary', '堀川-6390', '堀川', 'A <kanji>ditch</kanji> <kanji>river</kanji> is a <vocabulary>canal</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6390', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%80%E5%B7%9D-6390', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:16:40.245078Z', '2020-05-19T22:26:06.339245Z', '2025-12-03T22:32:01.704322Z'),
(6391, 'vocabulary', '巡回', '巡回', 'You <kanji>patrol</kanji> and <kanji>revolve</kanji> around the block to make your <vocabulary>patrol</vocabulary>. You''re <vocabulary>going around</vocabulary> patrolling.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6391', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A1%E5%9B%9E', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:16:45.877093Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.197281Z'),
(6392, 'vocabulary', '巡礼', '巡礼', 'A <kanji>patrol</kanji> of <kanji>thanks</kanji> is a patrol you make to something you''re thanking. Sounds religious. Sounds like a <vocabulary>pilgrimage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6392', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A1%E7%A4%BC', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:16:51.297228Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.208616Z'),
(6393, 'vocabulary', '巡る', '巡る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>patrol</kanji>, but what do you do when you patrol? You go around, which is <vocabulary>to go around</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6393', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A1%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:16:56.718618Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.219974Z'),
(6394, 'vocabulary', '巡査', '巡査', 'Those who <kanji>patrol</kanji> and <kanji>inspect</kanji> are <vocabulary>police officer</vocabulary>s or <vocabulary>patrol officer</vocabulary>s.

巡査 specifically refers to the lowest-ranking officers in the police force in Japan, whose duty is patrolling or doing things around 交番 (police boxes), or responding to emergency calls.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6394', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A1%E6%9F%BB', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:17:01.197701Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T19:00:40.037806Z'),
(6395, 'vocabulary', '戒める', '戒める', 'I say my <kanji>commandment</kanji> in order <vocabulary>to warn</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to caution</vocabulary> the people not to do bad things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6395', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%92%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:17:05.781736Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.247034Z'),
(6396, 'vocabulary', '戒告', '戒告', 'A <kanji>commandment</kanji> is <kanji>announce</kanji>d to you, but you should take it more as an <vocabulary>admonition</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>warning</vocabulary>. You''re an adult after all, so people don''t get to command you around. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6396', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%92%E5%91%8A', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:17:10.435609Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:34:31.16393Z'),
(6397, 'vocabulary', '訓戒', '訓戒', 'My <kanji>instruction</kanji> is my <kanji>commandment</kanji>. You must do what I say in my instructions/commandments. This is my <vocabulary>warning</vocabulary>. This is my <vocabulary>admonition</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6397', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%93%E6%88%92', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:17:18.481571Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:20:13.293925Z'),
(6398, 'vocabulary', '警戒', '警戒', 'My <kanji>warning</kanji> <kanji>commandment</kanji> is there to make sure you take <vocabulary>precaution</vocabulary> and show <vocabulary>vigilance</vocabulary> when it comes to that precaution. It''s only a warning commandment, though, which is why it has to do with prevention and precaution.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6398', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%A6%E6%88%92', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:17:26.62703Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.283477Z'),
(6399, 'vocabulary', '排気', '排気', 'The <kanji>emit</kanji>ted <kanji>energy</kanji> of some machine is <vocabulary>exhaust</vocabulary>. This can also refer to <vocabulary>ventilation</vocabulary>. Think of it as air (energy) being emitted so new, fresh air can take its place!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6399', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%92%E6%B0%97', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:17:31.522968Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(6400, 'vocabulary', '排他', '排他', 'When you <kanji>expel</kanji> <kanji>other</kanji>s, you''re practicing <vocabulary>exclusion</vocabulary>.

排他 is usually used in the phrase 排他的 (exclusive), which is a formal word that describes a tendency to exclude or reject those who are not part of a particular group. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6400', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%92%E4%BB%96', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:17:36.593126Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T04:47:22.214427Z'),
(6401, 'vocabulary', '排水', '排水', '<kanji>Emit</kanji>ted <kanji>water</kanji> is what we call <vocabulary>drainage</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6401', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%92%E6%B0%B4', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:17:44.074716Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.31904Z'),
(6402, 'vocabulary', '排出', '排出', 'Things that are <kanji>emit</kanji>ted and then <kanji>exit</kanji> are, well, <vocabulary>emission</vocabulary>s. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6402', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%92%E5%87%BA', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:17:51.697486Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.3301Z'),
(6403, 'vocabulary', '排除', '排除', 'If you <kanji>expel</kanji> or <kanji>exclude</kanji> something or someone, that''s called <vocabulary>exclusion</vocabulary>. Alternatively, you could also call this <vocabulary>removal</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>elimination</vocabulary>.

排除 is a relatively formal word that refers to the act of removing or eliminating something undesirable or unwanted.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6403', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%92%E9%99%A4', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:17:56.168261Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.341393Z'),
(6404, 'vocabulary', '携帯', '携帯', 'A <kanji>portable</kanji> <kanji>belt</kanji> is something so portable you can put it on your belt. It is <vocabulary>portable</vocabulary>. Note, this is also a very common term for a <vocabulary>cell phone</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>mobile phone</vocabulary>. You''ll learn more about that in the coming levels.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6404', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%BA%E5%B8%AF', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:18:03.414785Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.353986Z'),
(6405, 'vocabulary', '携わる', '携わる', 'Somehow the kanji <kanji>portable</kanji> turns into the verb <vocabulary>to engage in</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to participate</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to take part in</vocabulary>. I suppose if you are portable, you''re able to engage in all kinds of things, since you can move all over the place, participating everywhere you can (rather than being not portable and being stuck in one place).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6405', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%BA%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:18:08.166779Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:21.054559Z'),
(6406, 'vocabulary', '敏感', '敏感', 'If you have an <kanji>alert</kanji> <kanji>feeling</kanji>, you are <vocabulary>sensitive to</vocabulary> things going on around you. That means you have a lot of <vocabulary>sensitivity</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6406', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%8F%E6%84%9F', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:18:13.510938Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(6407, 'vocabulary', '過敏', '過敏', 'You''ve <kanji>surpass</kanji>ed what is normal <kanji>alert</kanji>ness. You are too alert. You notice everything. This is <vocabulary>oversensitivity</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6407', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8E%E6%95%8F', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:18:18.09487Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.394301Z'),
(6408, 'vocabulary', '機敏', '機敏', 'A <kanji>machine</kanji> <kanji>alert</kanji> is an alert that is timed by a machine, not by a human. It''s pretty <vocabulary>quick</vocabulary>. It''s also <vocabulary>quick-witted</vocabulary>. Machines can move and think so much faster than us, after all.

機敏 is often used to describe the way someone responds <vocabulary>prompt</vocabulary>ly to various situations, and implies being highly responsive and adaptive.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6408', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A9%9F%E6%95%8F', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:18:23.309414Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:04:30.700843Z'),
(6409, 'vocabulary', '鋭敏', '鋭敏', 'Someone on <kanji>sharp</kanji> <kanji>alert</kanji> has a <vocabulary>sharpness</vocabulary> about them. They are <vocabulary>quick witted</vocabulary> and have a strong <vocabulary>keenness</vocabulary> to them.

This word is formal and literary.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6409', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8B%AD%E6%95%8F', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:18:27.874607Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.418676Z'),
(6410, 'vocabulary', '鋭い', '鋭い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>sharp</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>sharp</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6410', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8B%AD%E3%81%84', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:18:33.525628Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.431156Z'),
(6411, 'vocabulary', '鋭利', '鋭利', 'Something that is <kanji>sharp</kanji> will <kanji>benefit</kanji> from its <vocabulary>sharpness</vocabulary>, since that''s all it has, really.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6411', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8B%AD%E5%88%A9', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:18:38.45608Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.445038Z'),
(6412, 'vocabulary', '新鋭', '新鋭', 'Something that is <kanji>new</kanji> and <kanji>sharp</kanji> (presumably because it''s so new) is on the <vocabulary>cutting edge</vocabulary>. It''s <vocabulary>up-and-coming</vocabulary>, the next big thing!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6412', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E9%8B%AD', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:18:46.441699Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.460863Z'),
(6413, 'vocabulary', '敷く', '敷く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>spread</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to spread out</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to lay out</vocabulary>.

This word generally refers to laying something flat, like a carpet or a picnic blanket. This use also extends to laying railroad tracks or asphalt for street paving. Metaphorically, it describes laying down something foundational, like establishing rules or specific organizational systems.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6413', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B7%E3%81%8F', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:18:51.04875Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:36:00.06764Z'),
(6414, 'vocabulary', '敷金', '敷金', '<kanji>Spread</kanji> the <kanji>gold</kanji> around. First part, I want you to put in a <vocabulary>security deposit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6414', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B7%E9%87%91', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:18:55.967269Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:36:00.06764Z'),
(6415, 'vocabulary', '屋敷', '屋敷', 'Your <kanji>roof</kanji>s are <kanji>spread</kanji> out around your property. Put these roofs and areas all together and you have your <vocabulary>residence</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>estate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6415', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%8B%E6%95%B7', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:19:03.587844Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:36:00.06764Z'),
(6416, 'vocabulary', '座敷', '座敷', 'A <kanji>sit</kanji>ting <kanji>spread</kanji> is a spread of tatami mats, making up the <vocabulary>tatami room</vocabulary> in a Japanese house.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6416', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%A7%E6%95%B7', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:19:08.685476Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:36:00.06764Z'),
(6417, 'vocabulary', '〜殿', '〜殿', 'The kanji means <kanji>Milord</kanji>, and this word used to be a name ender similar to the English title <vocabulary>Lord</vocabulary>, but nowadays it''s closer to <vocabulary>Mr.</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>Ms.</vocabulary>. It''s rarely used in speaking or even written correspondence these days, but is still used in official documents, and you''ll probably see it most on certificates. If in doubt, you''re better off choosing the name ender 様 because it''s much more widely used, and also more polite than 殿.
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6417', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%AE%BF', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:19:13.586106Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.534939Z'),
(6418, 'vocabulary', '殿様', '殿様', '<kanji>Milord</kanji> (you say very formally with a <kanji>formal name ender</kanji>). You say it very formally because this person is a lord, or more specifically, a <vocabulary>feudal lord</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6418', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%BF%E6%A7%98', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:19:35.723708Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.547332Z'),
(6419, 'vocabulary', '殿堂', '殿堂', '<kanji>Milord</kanji>''s <kanji>hall</kanji> is a nice place. It''s where a lord would live. A <vocabulary>palace</vocabulary>. By extension, this can also mean a <vocabulary>sanctuary</vocabulary>, and even a <vocabulary>hall of fame</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6419', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%BF%E5%A0%82', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:19:40.818767Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.569069Z'),
(6420, 'vocabulary', '犠打', '犠打', 'A <kanji>sacrifice</kanji> <kanji>hit</kanji> is one you do in baseball, when you hit the ball in the air, and then the person on third tags up and scores. In baseball, this can either be a <vocabulary>sacrifice bunt</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>sacrifice fly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6420', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8A%A0%E6%89%93', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:19:56.283658Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.582166Z'),
(6421, 'vocabulary', '獲得', '獲得', 'If you <kanji>seize</kanji> and <kanji>acquire</kanji> you''re making an <vocabulary>acquisition</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>possession</vocabulary> of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6421', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8D%B2%E5%BE%97', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:20:09.782312Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.593279Z'),
(6422, 'vocabulary', '捕獲', '捕獲', 'To <kanji>catch</kanji> and <kanji>seize</kanji> is <vocabulary>capture</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>seizure</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6422', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%95%E7%8D%B2', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:20:14.890106Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:38:09.45677Z'),
(6423, 'vocabulary', '獲物', '獲物', 'A <kanji>seize</kanji>d <kanji>thing</kanji> is something you''ve seized from your victory. It''s your <vocabulary>spoils</vocabulary>. It''s your <vocabulary>trophy</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6423', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8D%B2%E7%89%A9', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:20:20.330373Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(6424, 'vocabulary', '茂る', '茂る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>luxuriant</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be luxuriant</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to grow thickly</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6424', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8C%82%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:20:27.823678Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:43:01.883768Z'),
(6425, 'vocabulary', '繁茂', '繁茂', 'Something that is <kanji>overgrown</kanji> and <kanji>luxuriant</kanji> must have quite the <vocabulary>luxuriant growth</vocabulary>. Lots and lots of growth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6425', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B9%81%E8%8C%82', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:20:34.530784Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:43:01.883768Z'),
(6426, 'vocabulary', '頻繁', '頻繁', 'Something that is <kanji>frequent</kanji>ly <kanji>overgrown</kanji> is something that is super <vocabulary>frequent</vocabulary>. It never stops attacking, never stops coming, and is honestly getting <vocabulary>incessant</vocabulary> in how often it overgrows. Those darn [put plant name here].', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6426', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%BB%E7%B9%81', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:20:41.670375Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.66003Z'),
(6427, 'vocabulary', '繁殖', '繁殖', 'Something that is <kanji>overgrown</kanji> and will <kanji>multiply</kanji> is obviously <vocabulary>breeding</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>multiplying</vocabulary> a ton.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6427', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B9%81%E6%AE%96', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:20:47.594884Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:10:51.684762Z'),
(6428, 'vocabulary', '繁栄', '繁栄', 'If you''re <kanji>overgrown</kanji> with <kanji>prosper</kanji>ity you are probably really <vocabulary>prospering</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6428', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B9%81%E6%A0%84', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:20:53.100362Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.684216Z'),
(6429, 'vocabulary', '頻度', '頻度', 'The <kanji>frequent</kanji>ness of something, measured in <kanji>occurrence</kanji>s is measured in <vocabulary>frequency</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6429', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%BB%E5%BA%A6', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:21:00.067488Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.696904Z'),
(6430, 'vocabulary', '頻発', '頻発', 'Something that <kanji>frequent</kanji>ly <kanji>departure</kanji>s has much frequency and is a <vocabulary>frequent occurrence</vocabulary>. Oh, that? Yeah, it departures with much frequency.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6430', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%BB%E7%99%BA', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:21:06.134381Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:22:52.12866Z'),
(6431, 'vocabulary', '頻りに', '頻りに', 'Something that goes towards being <kanji>frequent</kanji> is something that is <vocabulary>frequently</vocabulary> happening.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6431', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%BB%E3%82%8A%E3%81%AB', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:21:10.558292Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.722731Z'),
(6432, 'vocabulary', '生殖', '生殖', '<kanji>Life</kanji>, when it <kanji>multipl</kanji>ies (multiply), is doing <vocabulary>reproduction</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6432', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E6%AE%96', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:21:15.616517Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:10:51.684762Z'),
(6433, 'vocabulary', '殖える', '殖える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>multiply</kanji> and the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to multiply</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to increase</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6433', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%96%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:21:20.282449Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:10:51.684762Z'),
(6434, 'vocabulary', '薄い', '薄い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>dilute</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>dilute</vocabulary>. And something that''s been diluted is now <vocabulary>thin</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6434', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%84%E3%81%84', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:21:25.449219Z', NULL, '2025-11-15T18:08:26.290738Z'),
(6435, 'vocabulary', '薄弱', '薄弱', 'Something is <kanji>dilute</kanji>d to the point of <kanji>weak</kanji>ness. It has much <vocabulary>weakness</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>feebleness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6435', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%84%E5%BC%B1', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:21:36.792553Z', NULL, '2026-03-19T06:03:32.726396Z'),
(6436, 'vocabulary', '薄情', '薄情', 'If you <kanji>dilute</kanji> <kanji>feeling</kanji> from someone, they have no feelings. They become <vocabulary>cold hearted</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6436', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%84%E6%83%85', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:21:41.885406Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.782568Z'),
(6437, 'vocabulary', '衝撃', '衝撃', 'If you <kanji>collide</kanji> and <kanji>attack</kanji> what you''re colliding into, you''re going to not only cause a large <vocabulary>shock</vocabulary> but also <vocabulary>crash</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>impact</vocabulary> into them. Blamo!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6437', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%9D%E6%92%83', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:21:48.303098Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.794417Z'),
(6438, 'vocabulary', '衝突', '衝突', 'When things <kanji>collide</kanji> and <kanji>thrust</kanji> into each other, you have a <vocabulary>collision</vocabulary>, or at least a strong <vocabulary>impact</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6438', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%9D%E7%AA%81', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:21:54.367599Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.80585Z'),
(6439, 'vocabulary', '名誉', '名誉', 'A <kanji>famous</kanji> <kanji>honor</kanji> is still an <vocabulary>honor</vocabulary>, but it is also a <vocabulary>prestige</vocabulary> to have, since you are famously honorable. So prestigious! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6439', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8D%E8%AA%89', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:21:59.928314Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(6440, 'vocabulary', '栄誉', '栄誉', 'You have a <kanji>prosper</kanji>ity of <kanji>honor</kanji>, therefore you have <vocabulary>honor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6440', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%84%E8%AA%89', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:22:04.691814Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.832225Z'),
(6441, 'vocabulary', '褒める', '褒める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>praise</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to praise</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6441', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A4%92%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:22:10.123906Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.844698Z'),
(6442, 'vocabulary', '褒美', '褒美', 'I <kanji>praise</kanji> you for your <kanji>beauty</kanji>. I''m so into your beauty, I hand you a <vocabulary>reward</vocabulary>... a <vocabulary>prize</vocabulary>, basically.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6442', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A4%92%E7%BE%8E', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:22:15.852079Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.856426Z'),
(6443, 'vocabulary', '褒賞', '褒賞', 'I <kanji>praise</kanji> you for what you did, so here''s your <kanji>prize</kanji> to go along with my praise, a <vocabulary>medal</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6443', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A4%92%E8%B3%9E', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:22:23.721236Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:27:28.654407Z'),
(6444, 'vocabulary', '透ける', '透ける', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>transparent</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be transparent</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be see-through</vocabulary>.

透ける is usually for things that are partially transparent or see-through because they''re thin or sheer, like stockings and lace curtains. Sometimes people even joke that your true thoughts are 透けてる (showing through).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6444', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%8F%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:22:28.564629Z', NULL, '2025-09-30T18:48:18.847243Z'),
(6445, 'vocabulary', '透明', '透明', 'Something that is <kanji>transparent</kanji> and <kanji>bright</kanji> so that you can see through it is <vocabulary>transparent</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6445', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%8F%E6%98%8E', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:22:33.424534Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(6446, 'vocabulary', '隣', '隣', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6446', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%A3', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:23:04.481059Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.905409Z'),
(6447, 'vocabulary', '隣家', '隣家', 'A <kanji>neighbor</kanji> <kanji>house</kanji> is a <vocabulary>neighboring house</vocabulary>. That house is right next to ours!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6447', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%A3%E5%AE%B6', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:23:10.77348Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.917784Z'),
(6448, 'vocabulary', '近隣', '近隣', 'All the <kanji>near</kanji> <kanji>neighbor</kanji>s are the people who live around you and near you. They''re a part of the <vocabulary>neighborhood</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6448', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E9%9A%A3', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:23:23.070135Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.92906Z'),
(6449, 'vocabulary', '隣国', '隣国', 'A <kanji>neighbor</kanji> <kanji>country</kanji> is a <vocabulary>neighboring country</vocabulary>. For us, our 隣国 in America are those sneaky Canadians... always waiting to strike, I can tell...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6449', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%A3%E5%9B%BD', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:23:30.280229Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.94246Z'),
(6450, 'vocabulary', '隣人', '隣人', 'A <kanji>neighbor</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>neighbor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6450', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%A3%E4%BA%BA', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:23:34.915332Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(6451, 'vocabulary', '雅致', '雅致', 'If everything you <kanji>do</kanji> is <kanji>elegant</kanji> you have <vocabulary>artistry</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>good taste</vocabulary>. So much <vocabulary>elegance</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6451', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%85%E8%87%B4', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:23:39.475994Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.968658Z'),
(6452, 'vocabulary', '雅楽', '雅楽', 'It''s so <kanji>elegant</kanji> and so <kanji>comfort</kanji>ing. This is <vocabulary>Japanese court music</vocabulary>, and it''s what fancy court people listened to in Japan back in the day. You can also call it <vocabulary>gagaku</vocabulary> if you''d like.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6452', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%85%E6%A5%BD', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:23:44.092644Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:45:39.241652Z'),
(6453, 'vocabulary', '優雅', '優雅', 'Something that is <kanji>superior</kanji> and <kanji>elegant</kanji> has much <vocabulary>elegance</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>grace</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6453', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%84%AA%E9%9B%85', 40, 1, '2013-06-07T18:23:48.682791Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:33.991481Z'),
(6454, 'vocabulary', '控える', '控える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>abstain</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to abstain</vocabulary> as well!

控える is super versatile. It can mean <vocabulary>to refrain</vocabulary> from drinking, <vocabulary>to hold back</vocabulary>  comments, or <vocabulary>to stand by</vocabulary> (abstaining from taking action). In some contexts, it can even mean "to take a memo" because memo-taking is like putting information on standby!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6454', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A7%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:45:11.284609Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.00335Z'),
(6455, 'vocabulary', '群れる', '群れる', 'Remember how 群れ is a <kanji>flock</kanji>? This is the verb version, and means <vocabulary>to flock</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to crowd</vocabulary> together with a bunch of other people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6455', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%A4%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:45:19.922134Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.015807Z'),
(6456, 'vocabulary', '埋もれる', '埋もれる', 'You know that 埋まる is "to be buried." This verb is also <vocabulary>to be buried</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to get buried</vocabulary>. 

However, there''s a slight difference between them. 埋まる focuses on the subject itself getting buried or filled, while 埋もれる highlights the sense of something being hidden or obscured by being covered. So you could use it to describe a yard covered in snow or your talent being overshadowed in a competitive environment. 

You can remember this nuance because it''s <reading>more rare</reading> (もれる) to see something completely buried and covered up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6456', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%8B%E3%82%82%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2013-06-24T22:45:31.530972Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.863802Z'),
(6457, 'vocabulary', '埋め合わせる', '埋め合わせる', '埋める is "to bury." 合わせる is "to unify together." If you bury somebody in order to unify them with you, you''re almost certainly trying <vocabulary>to compensate for</vocabulary> something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6457', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%8B%E3%82%81%E5%90%88%E3%82%8F%E3%81%9B%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2013-06-24T22:47:18.485061Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.068609Z'),
(6458, 'vocabulary', '御手洗', '御手洗', 'You know 手洗い, right? This is like that, but way more polite sounding. The meaning is still the same though: <vocabulary>restroom</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>bathroom</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>toilet</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6458', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%A1%E6%89%8B%E6%B4%97', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:47:23.412308Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(6459, 'vocabulary', '岐阜県', '岐阜県', 'You''ve seen how 岐阜 is "Gifu." Now make it a prefecture to make <vocabulary>Gifu prefecture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6459', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B2%90%E9%98%9C%E7%9C%8C', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:47:29.12843Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:25:07.757919Z'),
(6460, 'vocabulary', '雇用者', '雇用者', 'An <kanji>employment</kanji> (雇用) <kanji>someone</kanji> is someone who gives you employment, aka an <vocabulary>employer</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6460', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%87%E7%94%A8%E8%80%85', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:47:41.113172Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(6461, 'vocabulary', '破片', '破片', '<kanji>Tear</kanji> off a <kanji>one-sided</kanji> piece. It''s only one part, not all the parts. It''s a <vocabulary>fragment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6461', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%B4%E7%89%87', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:48:03.044085Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:17:49.824318Z'),
(6462, 'vocabulary', '御免なさい', '御免なさい', 'Remember 御免? This is a polite way to say it. It means <vocabulary>sorry</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>I beg your pardon</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6462', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%A1%E5%85%8D%E3%81%AA%E3%81%95%E3%81%84', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:48:09.523035Z', NULL, '2025-08-25T16:30:28.632013Z'),
(6463, 'vocabulary', '社会福祉', '社会福祉', '福祉 is <kanji>welfare</kanji>, 社会 is <kanji>society</kanji>. So, a society''s welfare is <vocabulary>social welfare</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6463', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A4%BE%E4%BC%9A%E7%A6%8F%E7%A5%89', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:48:15.37783Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.142308Z'),
(6464, 'vocabulary', '謙遜', '謙遜', 'If you have <kanji>modesty</kanji> and are <kanji>humble</kanji>, then you are probably <vocabulary>humble</vocabulary> and have <vocabulary>humility</vocabulary> (and have <vocabulary>modesty</vocabulary> too, I guess).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6464', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AC%99%E9%81%9C', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:48:21.492567Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.153437Z'),
(6465, 'vocabulary', '拳銃', '拳銃', 'A <kanji>fist</kanji> <kanji>gun</kanji> is a small fistish sized gun that has some punch (because, you know, fist!). The gun I''m describing is a <vocabulary>revolver</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>handgun</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6465', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%B3%E9%8A%83', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:48:26.934674Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:30:36.495728Z'),
(6466, 'vocabulary', '思い遣り', '思い遣り', 'If you <kanji>dispatch</kanji> what you <kanji>think</kanji> to the other person, you''re thinking about them, and you''re being nice. You''re showing <vocabulary>consideration</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>sympathy</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>compassion</vocabulary> for the other person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6466', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%9D%E3%81%84%E9%81%A3%E3%82%8A', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:48:32.173586Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.185964Z'),
(6467, 'vocabulary', '原爆', '原爆', '原 is often tied to atomic things, as atoms are the origin of all things. If an atom <kanji>explode</kanji>s, you have yourself an <vocabulary>atomic bomb</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6467', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%9F%E7%88%86', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:48:39.803146Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.197771Z'),
(6468, 'vocabulary', '渋谷', '渋谷', 'The <kanji>bitter</kanji> <kanji>valley</kanji> is a place known as <vocabulary>Shibuya</vocabulary>. This is a very famous area in Tokyo. Very busy too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6468', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%8B%E8%B0%B7', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:48:46.545465Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:43:56.588475Z'),
(6469, 'vocabulary', '充電する', '充電する', 'If 充電 is a <kanji>recharge</kanji> then the verb version is going to be <vocabulary>to recharge</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to charge a battery</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6469', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%85%E9%9B%BB%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:48:52.605794Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.030484Z'),
(6470, 'vocabulary', '甲斐性', '甲斐性', 'Remember how 甲斐 can mean "worth"? If worth is in your <kanji>nature</kanji>, you''re probably a person of some <vocabulary>capability</vocabulary>. You''re worth knowing and people appreciate your <vocabulary>dependability</vocabulary>.

甲斐性 means being dependable, responsible, or resourceful, especially in supporting others financially or emotionally. Traditionally it refers to a man''s ability to provide for his family, so 甲斐性がない is often used to criticize someone for not making enough money.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6470', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B2%E6%96%90%E6%80%A7', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:48:57.478892Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.266049Z'),
(6471, 'vocabulary', '生き甲斐', '生き甲斐', 'You know that 生きる is "to live" and 甲斐 is "effect" or "worth." Something that has a good effect on your life and gives it worth is your <vocabulary>reason for living</vocabulary>. From your standpoint, it''s <vocabulary>something worth living for</vocabulary>.

Keep in mind 生き甲斐 is mostly used as a noun, so you''ll see it in phrases like 仕事が生き甲斐 (my job is my reason for living) or 生き甲斐が欲しい (I want something to live for).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6471', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E3%81%8D%E7%94%B2%E6%96%90', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:49:02.706972Z', NULL, '2025-09-30T18:58:47.800265Z'),
(6472, 'vocabulary', '手の甲', '手の甲', 'Your <kanji>hand</kanji>''s <kanji>turtle shell</kanji> is the back of it (see you can make it shaped like a turtle shell if you try hard!). That''s why this is the <vocabulary>back of one''s hand</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6472', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E3%81%AE%E7%94%B2', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:49:09.793156Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.28855Z'),
(6473, 'vocabulary', 'やり甲斐', 'やり甲斐', 'やり is from やる, meaning "to do," and 甲斐 means "effect" or "worth." So, if the thing you do has an effect and is worth something, then it is <vocabulary>worth doing</vocabulary>.

やり甲斐 is a noun, so it''s often used in phrases like やり甲斐がある (to be worth doing) or やり甲斐を感じる (to feel worthwhile).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6473', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%84%E3%82%8A%E7%94%B2%E6%96%90', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:49:17.381069Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:21.34312Z'),
(6474, 'vocabulary', '躍り', '躍り', 'If 躍る is "to leap" or "to jump," then this noun version will be <vocabulary>jumping</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>leaping</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6474', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BA%8D%E3%82%8A', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:49:28.362991Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T19:50:34.734589Z'),
(6475, 'vocabulary', '伺う', '伺う', 'The kanji means <kanji>inquire</kanji>, and the verb version means the same — <vocabulary>to inquire</vocabulary>. Depending on the context it can also mean <vocabulary>to hear</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to visit</vocabulary>. 

伺う is a humble verb, so you can use with people of higher status to show your respect for them. For example, if you ask your professor a question or hear something from them, you can say 教授に伺う. If you visit them, you can say 教授のところに伺う. And in customer service, you''ll often hear お伺いします, which is the polite way for a staff member to say they''re ready to listen and assist.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6475', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%BA%E3%81%86', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:49:39.658797Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T22:05:09.554981Z'),
(6476, 'vocabulary', '徹する', '徹する', 'If you do <kanji>penetrate</kanji>, sure, you can <vocabulary>to penetrate</vocabulary>. But, if you penetrate something you really get into it, like, you are going <vocabulary>to devote oneself</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6476', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%B9%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:49:49.527131Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.33646Z'),
(6477, 'vocabulary', '徹夜', '徹夜', '<kanji>Penetrate</kanji> the <kanji>night</kanji> and go on through to the morning. You were up <vocabulary>all night</vocabulary> and had a <vocabulary>sleepless night</vocabulary>, aka an <vocabulary>all nighter</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6477', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%B9%E5%A4%9C', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:49:55.109069Z', NULL, '2025-11-19T08:38:20.514027Z'),
(6478, 'vocabulary', '徹底', '徹底', '<kanji>Penetrate</kanji> to the <kanji>bottom</kanji>. ALL the way to the bottom. Don''t stop half way. This is <vocabulary>thoroughness</vocabulary>. This is <vocabulary>completeness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6478', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%B9%E5%BA%95', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:50:00.184757Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.362172Z'),
(6479, 'vocabulary', '瀬戸', '瀬戸', 'The <kanji>rapids</kanji> <kanji>door</kanji> originally meant a strait or channel, but these days it''s mostly a place name: <vocabulary>Seto</vocabulary>. Some famous examples are Seto City (瀬戸市) in Aichi, known for its earthenware, and the Seto Inland Sea (瀬戸内海) between Honshu, Shikoku, and Kyūshū.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6479', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%80%AC%E6%88%B8', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:50:09.383378Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T17:37:07.182108Z'),
(6480, 'vocabulary', '高瀬', '高瀬', 'The <kanji>high</kanji> <kanji>rapids</kanji> are the <vocabulary>shallows</vocabulary>. How does that make sense?! I guess this means geographically high. So higher up the river, near the source, the water tends to be pretty shallow.

You''ll see 高瀬 mostly in names, such as 高瀬川 (Takase River) or 高瀬ダム (Takase Dam).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6480', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E7%80%AC', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:50:14.917228Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.399107Z'),
(6481, 'vocabulary', '早瀬', '早瀬', 'A <kanji>quick</kanji> <kanji>rapids</kanji> is going to have <vocabulary>swift currents</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6481', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A9%E7%80%AC', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:50:19.963619Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:53:32.555199Z'),
(6482, 'vocabulary', '瀬', '瀬', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6482', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%80%AC', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:50:24.697882Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.421883Z'),
(6483, 'vocabulary', '撤去', '撤去', 'A <kanji>withdrawal</kanji> is something you do to remove something from the <kanji>past</kanji>. It is a <vocabulary>withdrawal</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>removal</vocabulary> of what you did or submitted before.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6483', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%92%A4%E5%8E%BB', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:50:29.407843Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:21:59.859898Z'),
(6484, 'vocabulary', '撤回', '撤回', 'If you do a <kanji>withdrawal</kanji> of something it''s like you''re <kanji>revolv</kanji>ing back to how things originally were. This is a <vocabulary>withdrawal</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>repeal</vocabulary>. It''s <vocabulary>taking back</vocabulary> something you said previously.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6484', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%92%A4%E5%9B%9E', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:50:33.996809Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T04:23:55.976021Z'),
(6485, 'vocabulary', '撤兵', '撤兵', 'A <kanji>withdrawal</kanji> of <kanji>soldier</kanji>s is a <vocabulary>withdrawal of troops</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6485', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%92%A4%E5%85%B5', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:50:38.776988Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:54:26.999027Z'),
(6486, 'vocabulary', '措置', '措置', '<kanji>Set aside</kanji> and <kanji>put</kanji> what you''ll need to get the job done over there. These are the <vocabulary>measures</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>steps</vocabulary> we''re taking to make sure things get done properly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6486', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%AA%E7%BD%AE', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:50:47.693493Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:08:17.52437Z'),
(6487, 'vocabulary', '措辞', '措辞', 'You <kanji>set aside</kanji> these <kanji>word</kanji>s specifically so you could get the <vocabulary>wording</vocabulary> right. Wording is important sometimes, like when you''re trying to communicate something sensitive, or when you''re writing a poem. Sometimes only certain words will do.

Note that 措辞 usually refers to the <vocabulary>phrasing</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>diction</vocabulary> of writing, not speech.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6487', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%AA%E8%BE%9E', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:50:52.718627Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.487896Z'),
(6488, 'vocabulary', '拠る', '拠る', 'The kanji means <kanji>based on</kanji>, and the verb means <vocabulary>to be based on</vocabulary>. 

拠る implies a foundational or sometimes causal relationship. If X is based on Y, you could also say X is due to Y, or X depends on Y, right? That''s why this can also mean <vocabulary>to be due to</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to depend on</vocabulary>. 

It''s worth noting that this word is usually written in kana.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6488', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%A0%E3%82%8B', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:50:58.407867Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:51:54.258509Z'),
(6489, 'vocabulary', '拠点', '拠点', 'Something <kanji>based on</kanji> <kanji>points</kanji> has a ranking, meaning you''ll know where your <vocabulary>position</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>location</vocabulary> is, compared to everyone else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6489', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%A0%E7%82%B9', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:51:03.132662Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:51:54.258509Z'),
(6490, 'vocabulary', '証拠', '証拠', 'The <kanji>evidence</kanji> is <kanji>based on</kanji> something. What is it based on? This <vocabulary>physical evidence</vocabulary>. Ha ha! Guilty!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6490', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%BC%E6%8B%A0', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:51:09.407775Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:51:54.258509Z'),
(6491, 'vocabulary', '根拠', '根拠', 'The <kanji>root</kanji> that this is <kanji>based on</kanji> is the <vocabulary>basis</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>grounds</vocabulary> for my argument. Look, it goes all the way down to the root problem!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6491', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%B9%E6%8B%A0', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:51:14.132113Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:51:54.258509Z'),
(6492, 'vocabulary', '礼儀', '礼儀', 'The <kanji>thanks</kanji> <kanji>ceremony</kanji> is the dance you do in life when it comes to your <vocabulary>manners</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>courtesy</vocabulary> to others. Also means <vocabulary>etiquette</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6492', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A4%BC%E5%84%80', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:51:19.743669Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.547204Z'),
(6493, 'vocabulary', '儀式', '儀式', 'A <kanji>ceremony</kanji> <kanji>ceremony</kanji> is a <vocabulary>ceremony</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6493', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%84%80%E5%BC%8F', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:51:25.531598Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T22:31:56.723758Z'),
(6494, 'vocabulary', '祝儀', '祝儀', 'To <kanji>celebrate</kanji> a <kanji>ceremony</kanji>, you offer a <vocabulary>congratulatory gift</vocabulary>.

祝儀 generally refers to monetary gifts for celebrations, such as congratulatory money for weddings. It can also include money given away when you make a hole-in-one in golf or win big on a gamble.

While 祝儀 can also refer to the celebration itself, this use is not as common.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6494', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%9D%E5%84%80', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:51:30.575202Z', NULL, '2025-09-30T19:10:38.839468Z'),
(6495, 'vocabulary', '辞儀する', '辞儀する', 'The <kanji>quit</kanji> <kanji>ceremony</kanji> is when you quit and are ready to go somewhere, so you bow and then get going. That''s why this verb is <vocabulary>to bow</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6495', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%9E%E5%84%80%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:51:35.256413Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.580635Z'),
(6496, 'vocabulary', '果樹', '果樹', 'A <kanji>fruit</kanji> <kanji>wood</kanji> is what a fruit grows on (the wood of a fruit is a tree, dummy). That makes this word a <vocabulary>fruit tree</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6496', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%9C%E6%A8%B9', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:51:40.496405Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.59162Z'),
(6497, 'vocabulary', '樹皮', '樹皮', 'A <kanji>wood</kanji> <kanji>skin</kanji> is <vocabulary>tree bark</vocabulary>. It''s the wooden skin around trees.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6497', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A8%B9%E7%9A%AE', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:51:45.782544Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:30:12.871593Z'),
(6498, 'vocabulary', '植樹', '植樹', 'If you <kanji>plant</kanji> <kanji>wood</kanji> you are doing <vocabulary>tree planting</vocabulary>, since only trees are made of wood.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6498', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A4%8D%E6%A8%B9', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:51:54.599242Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:07:35.298442Z'),
(6499, 'vocabulary', '放棄', '放棄', 'You <kanji>release</kanji> and <kanji>abandon</kanji> something. This is <vocabulary>abandonment</vocabulary>.

放棄 is a relatively formal word for <vocabulary>abandoning</vocabulary> something. It can refer to giving up responsibilities, like neglecting duties or parental obligations, or <vocabulary>relinquishing</vocabulary> entitlements and rights.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6499', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BE%E6%A3%84', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:52:01.570875Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T02:08:38.914766Z'),
(6500, 'vocabulary', '廃棄', '廃棄', 'It''s <kanji>obsolete</kanji>, so you’re going to <kanji>abandon</kanji> it. This is the act of <vocabulary>disposal</vocabulary>. Whatever it is, you’re <vocabulary>disposing</vocabulary> of or <vocabulary>scrapping</vocabulary> it because it’s no longer needed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6500', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%83%E6%A3%84', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:52:05.942215Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.639142Z'),
(6501, 'vocabulary', '投棄', '投棄', 'When you <kanji>throw</kanji> out something and <kanji>abandon</kanji> it, you are essentially <vocabulary>dumping</vocabulary> it.

投棄 is a formal word for <vocabulary>throwing away</vocabulary> things, often used in the context of illegally dumping waste.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6501', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%95%E6%A3%84', 42, 1, '2013-06-24T22:52:10.521067Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.650321Z'),
(6502, 'vocabulary', '虎', '虎', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6502', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%99%8E', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:52:14.835902Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.661925Z'),
(6503, 'vocabulary', '蛍', '蛍', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6503', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9B%8D', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:52:20.11201Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.673958Z'),
(6504, 'vocabulary', '蜂', '蜂', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6504', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9C%82', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:52:25.384034Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.685675Z'),
(6505, 'vocabulary', '蜂蜜', '蜂蜜', 'A <kanji>bee</kanji>''s <kanji>honey</kanji> is its <vocabulary>honey</vocabulary>, honey.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6505', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9C%82%E8%9C%9C', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:52:30.736103Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.697574Z'),
(6506, 'vocabulary', '焼酎', '焼酎', '<kanji>Bake</kanji>d <kanji>hard liquor</kanji> is slightly higher in alcohol content. This is <vocabulary>shochu</vocabulary>, which I''m guessing is heated in some way during its production.

Shochu is a type of Japanese traditional hard liquor. It''s a distilled spirit made from ingredients like sweet potato, barley, rice, buckwheat, and sugar cane.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6506', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%BC%E9%85%8E', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:52:35.736377Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.709606Z'),
(6507, 'vocabulary', '蜜', '蜜', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6507', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9C%9C', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:52:56.915892Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.721593Z'),
(6508, 'vocabulary', '廃墟', '廃墟', '<kanji>Obsolete</kanji> <kanji>ruins</kanji> are places that are abandoned because nobody can run them anymore (due to being obsolete, or due to not having money, or due to whatever else). These things are <vocabulary>ruins</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>abandoned buildings</vocabulary>. People like to explore these, a lot.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6508', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%83%E5%A2%9F', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:53:01.964024Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.734335Z'),
(6509, 'vocabulary', '艦船', '艦船', 'A <kanji>warship</kanji> <kanji>ship</kanji> is a <vocabulary>warship</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6509', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%89%A6%E8%88%B9', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:53:07.787496Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.74688Z'),
(6510, 'vocabulary', '艦隊', '艦隊', 'A <kanji>warship</kanji> <kanji>squad</kanji> is a <vocabulary>naval fleet</vocabulary> or an <vocabulary>armada</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6510', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%89%A6%E9%9A%8A', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:53:12.745841Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:50:20.608547Z'),
(6511, 'vocabulary', '軍艦', '軍艦', 'An <kanji>army</kanji> <kanji>warship</kanji> is a <vocabulary>battleship</vocabulary>. Did you sink it? Oh oh! You sunk my 軍艦!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6511', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8D%E8%89%A6', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:53:17.168085Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.769625Z'),
(6512, 'vocabulary', '潜水艦', '潜水艦', 'A <kanji>conceal</kanji> in the <kanji>water</kanji> <kanji>warship</kanji> is a <vocabulary>submarine</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6512', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%9C%E6%B0%B4%E8%89%A6', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:53:22.468621Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:41:52.065519Z'),
(6513, 'vocabulary', '航空母艦', '航空母艦', 'An <kanji>aviation</kanji> (航空) <kanji>mother</kanji> <kanji>warship</kanji> is a warship that is like mother to all the aviation things (the airplanes). They come home to their mother warship, which is an <vocabulary>aircraft carrier</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6513', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%AA%E7%A9%BA%E6%AF%8D%E8%89%A6', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:54:03.158212Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T18:57:59.068505Z'),
(6514, 'vocabulary', '潜む', '潜む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>conceal</kanji>, so if you conceal yourself and walk around you are going <vocabulary>to lurk</vocabulary> or you are going <vocabulary>to lie dormant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6514', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%9C%E3%82%80', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:54:09.342586Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:41:52.065519Z'),
(6515, 'vocabulary', '原潜-6515', '原潜', 'The <kanji>original</kanji> (often associated with atoms and nuclear tech) <kanji>conceal</kanji>ing machine is the <vocabulary>nuclear submarine</vocabulary>. It is nuclear, and it conceals itself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6515', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%9F%E6%BD%9C-6515', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:54:22.662873Z', '2021-10-14T17:36:13.772209Z', '2025-12-03T22:41:52.065519Z'),
(6516, 'vocabulary', '潜水', '潜水', 'To <kanji>conceal</kanji> yourself in <kanji>water</kanji>, you''d better start <vocabulary>diving</vocabulary> and get under the water where it can conceal you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6516', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%9C%E6%B0%B4', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:54:29.205673Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:41:52.065519Z'),
(6517, 'vocabulary', '潜在意識', '潜在意識', 'Remember how 意識 means "consciousness"? Well, this is the kind of consciousness that has a more <kanji>conceal</kanji>ed <kanji>exist</kanji>tence inside you — in other words, it''s your <vocabulary>subconscious</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6517', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%9C%E5%9C%A8%E6%84%8F%E8%AD%98', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:54:33.278123Z', NULL, '2026-01-14T17:48:03.101989Z'),
(6518, 'vocabulary', '拳', '拳', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6518', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%B3', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:54:38.983486Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.848974Z'),
(6519, 'vocabulary', '拳骨', '拳骨', 'A <kanji>fist</kanji> that''s like a <kanji>bone</kanji> (hard) is a fist that''s clenched and ready to punch, making this a <vocabulary>clenched fist</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>knuckles</vocabulary>, if you want to get even bonier about it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6519', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%B3%E9%AA%A8', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:54:44.003652Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.860042Z'),
(6520, 'vocabulary', '炭', '炭', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6520', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%AD', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:55:24.016876Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.872833Z'),
(6521, 'vocabulary', '炭素', '炭素', 'Things of the <kanji>charcoal</kanji> <kanji>element</kanji> are made up of much <vocabulary>carbon</vocabulary>. Just look deep into the charcoal to see all the carbon in there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6521', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%AD%E7%B4%A0', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:55:29.503592Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:38:20.342554Z'),
(6522, 'vocabulary', '石炭', '石炭', 'A <kanji>stone</kanji> <kanji>charcoal</kanji> is charcoal shaped like a little stone. Or, maybe it''s often found in stone. That''s <vocabulary>coal</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6522', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%B3%E7%82%AD', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:55:35.054192Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.895721Z'),
(6523, 'vocabulary', '畑', '畑', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. 

You can also stick this after the name of a crop and get a ____ <vocabulary>field</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6523', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%91', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:55:46.945022Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.907177Z'),
(6524, 'vocabulary', '田畑', '田畑', 'We''re talking about both a <kanji>rice paddy</kanji> and the other <kanji>field</kanji>s where farmers grow crops. So, this means <vocabulary>fields</vocabulary>, or more precisely <vocabulary>rice and crop fields</vocabulary>. It''s all about <vocabulary>farmland</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6524', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B0%E7%95%91', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:55:51.643295Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.918448Z'),
(6525, 'vocabulary', '麦畑', '麦畑', 'A <kanji>wheat</kanji> <kanji>field</kanji> is a <vocabulary>wheat field</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>barley field</vocabulary> ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6525', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BA%A6%E7%95%91', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:55:56.882599Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:21:06.048836Z'),
(6526, 'vocabulary', '包む', '包む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>wrap</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to wrap</vocabulary>. 

包む can be used in both literal and figurative ways, so it can be used for wrapping up gifts, enveloping someone in love, or even for fog or light enveloping an area. In cultural contexts, it also sometimes implies the act of giving a financial gift, since in Japan things are often wrapped nicely before being given — even stacks of bills.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6526', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%85%E3%82%80', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:56:01.698225Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:21.539313Z'),
(6527, 'vocabulary', '包丁', '包丁', 'Something you have to <kanji>wrap</kanji> up when you go into the <kanji>street</kanji> is your <vocabulary>kitchen knife</vocabulary>. Why would you wrap up your kitchen knife? Well, both to keep it from getting damaged and to keep it from accidentally cutting people, obviously.

Okay, the kanji don''t really seem related to the meaning here. But that''s because this word actually derives from the name of a skilled butcher character in ancient Chinese literature. The more you know!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6527', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%85%E4%B8%81', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:56:06.37547Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:15:32.558339Z'),
(6528, 'vocabulary', '包囲', '包囲', '<kanji>Wrap</kanji> and <kanji>surround</kanji> them. We are going to <vocabulary>siege</vocabulary> the enemy. This is <vocabulary>encirclement</vocabulary> around their base!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6528', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%85%E5%9B%B2', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:56:11.536214Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.96556Z'),
(6529, 'vocabulary', '衣', '衣', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

In modern Japanese, 衣 is more often used to describe the <vocabulary>clothes</vocabulary> your <em>food</em> is wearing, e.g., the <vocabulary>coating</vocabulary> on tempura or the <vocabulary>breading</vocabulary> on deep-fried foods.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6529', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%A3', 25, 1, '2013-06-24T22:56:17.479767Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:34.988483Z'),
(6530, 'vocabulary', '更衣室', '更衣室', 'A <kanji>renew</kanji> <kanji>clothes</kanji> <kanji>room</kanji> is a <vocabulary>changing room</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>locker room</vocabulary>.

更衣室 is a room where people change their clothes, commonly found in places like gyms, swimming pools, schools, or workplaces. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6530', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B4%E8%A1%A3%E5%AE%A4', 23, 1, '2013-06-24T22:56:22.165792Z', NULL, '2026-04-04T20:17:45.612577Z'),
(6531, 'vocabulary', '脱衣', '脱衣', 'If you <kanji>undress</kanji> from <kanji>clothes</kanji> you are <vocabulary>undressing</vocabulary>.

脱衣 is a relatively formal word that you''ll mostly see on the signage for changing rooms at public baths, where they''ll be called 脱衣所 or 脱衣場. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6531', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%B1%E8%A1%A3', 22, 1, '2013-06-24T22:56:26.83045Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T18:44:49.308441Z'),
(6532, 'vocabulary', '仁', '仁', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6532', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%81', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:56:31.496653Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.048719Z'),
(6533, 'vocabulary', '仁義', '仁義', '<kanji>Humanity</kanji> and <kanji>righteousness</kanji>. This is your <vocabulary>duty</vocabulary>. This is <vocabulary>humanity and justice</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6533', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%81%E7%BE%A9', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:56:37.812761Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:46:56.671656Z'),
(6534, 'vocabulary', '鉱山', '鉱山', 'A <kanji>mineral</kanji> <kanji>mountain</kanji> is one that you should build <vocabulary>a mine</vocabulary> in, to get all those minerals.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6534', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%B1%E5%B1%B1', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:56:43.422145Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.071831Z'),
(6535, 'vocabulary', '鉱石', '鉱石', 'A <kanji>mineral</kanji> <kanji>stone</kanji> is an <vocabulary>ore</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>mineral</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6535', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%B1%E7%9F%B3', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:56:47.917677Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.084595Z'),
(6536, 'vocabulary', '鉱物', '鉱物', 'A <kanji>mineral</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji> is a <vocabulary>mineral</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6536', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%B1%E7%89%A9', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:56:52.828036Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(6537, 'vocabulary', '炭鉱', '炭鉱', '<kanji>Charcoal</kanji> <kanji>mineral</kanji> has been found! Time to dig a <vocabulary>coal mine</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>coal pit</vocabulary> to retrieve it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6537', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%AD%E9%89%B1', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:56:57.362362Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.108938Z'),
(6538, 'vocabulary', '鉄鉱', '鉄鉱', 'The <kanji>iron</kanji> <kanji>mineral</kanji> is <vocabulary>iron ore</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6538', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%84%E9%89%B1', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:57:03.083152Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.121138Z'),
(6539, 'vocabulary', '鉱業', '鉱業', 'The <kanji>mineral</kanji> <kanji>business</kanji> is the <vocabulary>mining industry</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6539', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%B1%E6%A5%AD', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:57:09.072369Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T18:56:41.283425Z'),
(6540, 'vocabulary', '至る', '至る', 'The kanji means <kanji>attain</kanji> but the meaning of this verb is <vocabulary>to arrive at</vocabulary>. I suppose that''s because if you attain something like enlightenment, you also arrive at that enlightenment as well. Thus, <vocabulary>to arrive at</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6540', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%B3%E3%82%8B', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:57:14.200785Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.144625Z'),
(6541, 'vocabulary', '夏至', '夏至', 'If you have <kanji>attain</kanji>ed <kanji>summer</kanji>, that must mean you finished out with the previous season and are going into summer. This is the <vocabulary>summer solstice</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6541', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%8F%E8%87%B3', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:57:20.050467Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:04:44.644357Z'),
(6542, 'vocabulary', '冬至', '冬至', 'If you are about to <kanji>attain</kanji> <kanji>winter</kanji>, then it''s the <vocabulary>winter solstice</vocabulary>. Happy winter, everyone!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6542', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%AC%E8%87%B3', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:57:25.143775Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.169461Z'),
(6543, 'vocabulary', '必至', '必至', 'If it''s <kanji>certain</kanji> that you will <kanji>attain</kanji> it, then it is <vocabulary>inevitable</vocabulary>. It is a <vocabulary>foregone</vocabulary> conclusion.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6543', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%85%E8%87%B3', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:57:31.114448Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:33:00.725847Z'),
(6544, 'vocabulary', '至上', '至上', 'If you <kanji>attain</kanji> <kanji>above</kanji> it means you''re up above everyone, and you''re looking down on them. You have <vocabulary>supremacy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6544', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%B3%E4%B8%8A', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:57:36.131714Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(6545, 'vocabulary', '誠', '誠', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6545', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%A0', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:57:41.265044Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.211244Z'),
(6546, 'vocabulary', '誠意', '誠意', 'The <kanji>idea</kanji> of <kanji>sincerity</kanji> is <vocabulary>sincerity</vocabulary>. It''s also to have <vocabulary>good faith</vocabulary> in others.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6546', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%A0%E6%84%8F', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:57:46.742715Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T21:21:57.032656Z'),
(6547, 'vocabulary', '誠実', '誠実', 'You have <kanji>sincerity</kanji> and you have <kanji>truth</kanji>. You are so <vocabulary>sincere</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>honest</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>faithful</vocabulary>, good friend.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6547', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%A0%E5%AE%9F', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:57:52.206647Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.235969Z'),
(6548, 'vocabulary', '忠誠', '忠誠', 'I have <kanji>sincerity</kanji> about my <kanji>loyalty</kanji>. That shows <vocabulary>loyalty</vocabulary>, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6548', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%A0%E8%AA%A0', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:57:57.302799Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.250499Z'),
(6549, 'vocabulary', '郷里', '郷里', 'Your <kanji>hometown</kanji> <kanji>home village</kanji> is the one that''s your <vocabulary>hometown</vocabulary>. It''s also your <vocabulary>birthplace</vocabulary>, where you were born.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6549', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%B7%E9%87%8C', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:58:03.098756Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.263309Z'),
(6550, 'vocabulary', '故郷', '故郷', 'By <kanji>circumstance</kanji> this is your <kanji>hometown</kanji>. Not much to say other than it''s your <vocabulary>hometown</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>birthplace</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6550', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%85%E9%83%B7', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:58:10.400791Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.274994Z'),
(6551, 'vocabulary', '帰郷する', '帰郷する', 'If you <kanji>return</kanji> to the <kanji>hometown</kanji> you are going <vocabulary>to return to one''s hometown</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6551', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%B0%E9%83%B7%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:58:17.005402Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:41:01.299215Z'),
(6552, 'vocabulary', '侵攻', '侵攻', 'If you <kanji>invade</kanji> with <kanji>aggression</kanji> that''s an <vocabulary>invasion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6552', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%B5%E6%94%BB', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:58:24.218455Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:46:39.742165Z'),
(6553, 'vocabulary', '侵入', '侵入', 'If you <kanji>invade</kanji> and <kanji>enter</kanji> that is an <vocabulary>intrusion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6553', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%B5%E5%85%A5', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:58:30.085669Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:46:39.742165Z'),
(6554, 'vocabulary', '侵す', '侵す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>invade</kanji> and the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to invade</vocabulary> as well!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6554', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%B5%E3%81%99', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:58:38.960516Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:46:39.742165Z'),
(6555, 'vocabulary', '侵害', '侵害', 'You <kanji>invade</kanji>d and <kanji>damage</kanji>d me. You are doing <vocabulary>infringement</vocabulary> on my territory. This is a <vocabulary>violation</vocabulary> of me.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6555', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%B5%E5%AE%B3', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:58:44.682506Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(6556, 'vocabulary', '偽', '偽', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6556', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%BD', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:58:59.377413Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.347553Z'),
(6557, 'vocabulary', '偽る', '偽る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>fake</kanji> and the verb vocab version is similar, it means <vocabulary>to lie</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to cheat</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to deceive</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6557', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%BD%E3%82%8B', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:59:09.765312Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.36161Z'),
(6558, 'vocabulary', '偽装', '偽装', 'Some <kanji>fake</kanji> <kanji>attire</kanji> doesn''t make you look fake, it makes you disappear. This fake attire is fake surroundings. So, if you''re in a shrub, it''s shrub attire. If you''re in the darkness, it''s dark attire. This is <vocabulary>camouflage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6558', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%BD%E8%A3%85', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:59:26.606857Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.372921Z'),
(6559, 'vocabulary', '偽造', '偽造', 'Let''s <kanji>create</kanji> a <kanji>fake</kanji>. This fake we''ve created? It''s a <vocabulary>forgery</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6559', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%BD%E9%80%A0', 41, 1, '2013-06-24T22:59:36.553147Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.384219Z'),
(6560, 'vocabulary', '駆け込む', '駆け込む', '駆ける is "to gallop" and the kanji 込 means <kanji>into</kanji>. To gallop into a place is <vocabulary>to rush into</vocabulary> it. You can’t gallop slowly, after all.

This can also mean <vocabulary>to seek refuge</vocabulary>, as in hurrying to a place for safety and support.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6560', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%86%E3%81%91%E8%BE%BC%E3%82%80', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:44:03.355337Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.396247Z'),
(6561, 'vocabulary', '駆け出す', '駆け出す', 'You know 駆ける is "to gallop" and 出す is "to let out." Well, in order to gallop off and let yourself out of somewhere, you''re going to need <vocabulary>to start running</vocabulary> first.

Fundamentally, 駆け出す just describes the act of beginning to run, but it''s often used to mean <vocabulary>to rush out</vocabulary> of somewhere. For example, schoolchildren might 駆け出す out of their classroom and make a beeline for the playground when they hear the bell ring.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6561', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%86%E3%81%91%E5%87%BA%E3%81%99', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:44:09.378639Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:38:51.592453Z'),
(6562, 'vocabulary', '駆け回る', '駆け回る', 'To 駆ける is "to gallop." 回る is "to revolve." So if you''re galloping around in a revolving fashion, you are going <vocabulary>to run around</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6562', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%86%E3%81%91%E5%9B%9E%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:44:22.301251Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.420623Z'),
(6563, 'vocabulary', '不孝者', '不孝者', 'Remember how 不孝 is "disobedience to parents"? Well, <kanji>someone</kanji> who shows <kanji>disobedience to parents</kanji> is a <vocabulary>disobedient child</vocabulary> or an <vocabulary>unfilial child</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6563', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%AD%9D%E8%80%85', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:44:34.91556Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(6564, 'vocabulary', '親不孝', '親不孝', 'You know that 不孝 means "disobedience to parents. So, if you add <kanji>parent</kanji>s to it, it just means the same thing. Just a longer version of the word, basically. This means <vocabulary>lack of filial piety</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>disobedience to parents</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6564', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA%E4%B8%8D%E5%AD%9D', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:45:15.193774Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.443982Z'),
(6565, 'vocabulary', '携帯電話', '携帯電話', 'You know that 携帯 means "portable" or "cell phone" or "mobile phone." And then 電話 means "telephone." This is basically just the longer version of simply saying 携帯, making this a <vocabulary>portable phone</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>cell phone</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>mobile phone</vocabulary>. It''s sort of like the difference between saying "my cell" and "my cell phone," you dig?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6565', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%BA%E5%B8%AF%E9%9B%BB%E8%A9%B1', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:45:23.430892Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.030484Z'),
(6566, 'vocabulary', '最新鋭', '最新鋭', '最新 is, as you can probably guess, "the newest." So, the newest <kanji>sharp</kanji> thing, is the <vocabulary>cutting edge</vocabulary>. It is <vocabulary>state of the art</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6566', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%80%E6%96%B0%E9%8B%AD', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:45:29.987769Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.467606Z'),
(6567, 'vocabulary', '犠飛', '犠飛', 'A <kanji>sacrifice</kanji> <kanji>fly</kanji> is a <vocabulary>sacrifice fly</vocabulary>. In baseball, this is when you hit the ball in the air so the runner can tag up and move up a base.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6567', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8A%A0%E9%A3%9B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:45:35.630667Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.479229Z'),
(6568, 'vocabulary', '不透明', '不透明', 'You know that 透明 is "transparency" or "transparent" - so, if something is not transparent, then it is <vocabulary>opaque</vocabulary>, making this word <vocabulary>opacity</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6568', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E9%80%8F%E6%98%8E', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:45:40.68152Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(6569, 'vocabulary', '克服', '克服', 'Time to <kanji>overcome</kanji> the <kanji>clothes</kanji> of our enemies. They all wear the same kind of clothes (let''s say blue clothes) so we know that if we overcome the blue coated people, we will have won. It will be a <vocabulary>conquest</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6569', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%8B%E6%9C%8D', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:45:47.317177Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.507069Z'),
(6570, 'vocabulary', '克明', '克明', 'If you''re <kanji>overcome</kanji> with <kanji>bright</kanji>ness, you''ll be able to see all the little fine <vocabulary>detailed</vocabulary> things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6570', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%8B%E6%98%8E', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:45:55.55978Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(6571, 'vocabulary', '到着', '到着', 'At the <kanji>arrival</kanji> time you will <kanji>arrive</kanji>. This is your <vocabulary>arrival</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6571', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%B0%E7%9D%80', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:46:00.897531Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(6572, 'vocabulary', '双子', '双子', 'The <kanji>pair</kanji> of <kanji>child</kanji>ren are the same as each other. They''re <vocabulary>twins</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6572', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8C%E5%AD%90', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:46:08.046138Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.550558Z'),
(6573, 'vocabulary', '双-6573', '双', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6573', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8C-6573', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:46:14.038251Z', '2025-07-09T07:10:11.125325Z', '2025-07-09T07:10:11.13803Z'),
(6574, 'vocabulary', '双眼鏡', '双眼鏡', 'A <kanji>pair</kanji> of <kanji>eyeball</kanji> <kanji>mirror</kanji>s - that''s <vocabulary>binoculars</vocabulary>. Binoculars have mirrors in them to allow you to see through the pair of eye holes, which is how this word makes sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6574', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8C%E7%9C%BC%E9%8F%A1', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:46:19.060647Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:33:42.082405Z'),
(6575, 'vocabulary', '哲学', '哲学', 'The <kanji>philosophy</kanji> <kanji>study</kanji> is the study of <vocabulary>philosophy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6575', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%93%B2%E5%AD%A6', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:46:23.716104Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:46:41.592775Z'),
(6576, 'vocabulary', '喪', '喪', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6576', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%AA', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:46:29.021359Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.60469Z'),
(6577, 'vocabulary', '喪失', '喪失', 'You are <kanji>mourning</kanji> your <kanji>fault</kanji>. Because of your fault, you have gotten a <vocabulary>loss</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>forfeit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6577', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%AA%E5%A4%B1', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:46:34.228929Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.618257Z'),
(6578, 'vocabulary', '堅い', '堅い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>solid</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>solid</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>serious</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>upright</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>hard</vocabulary>. This word is often used in the figurative sense, as in a solid defence, a serious topic, or an upright person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6578', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%85%E3%81%84', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:46:39.656152Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.642526Z'),
(6579, 'vocabulary', '床', '床', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6579', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%8A', 7, 1, '2013-07-02T19:47:14.276217Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.160034Z'),
(6580, 'vocabulary', '起床', '起床', '<kanji>Wake up</kanji> from your <kanji>bed</kanji> and get up. You are <vocabulary>rising</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>getting out of bed</vocabulary>.

起床 is a relatively formal word that is commonly used in writing. It''s also used as a command to wake up individuals in settings like military bases or prisons.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6580', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%B7%E5%BA%8A', 35, 1, '2013-07-02T19:47:26.334147Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.693378Z'),
(6581, 'vocabulary', '温床', '温床', 'A <kanji>warm</kanji> <kanji>bed</kanji> is a "<vocabulary>hotbed</vocabulary>."

While "hotbed" can sometimes have positive connotations in English, 温床 generally refers to a <vocabulary>breeding ground</vocabulary> for something unwelcome.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6581', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%A9%E5%BA%8A', 37, 1, '2013-07-02T19:47:31.565285Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:21.730474Z'),
(6582, 'vocabulary', '病床', '病床', 'A <kanji>sick</kanji> <kanji>bed</kanji> is a <vocabulary>sickbed</vocabulary>. If you''re in that bed, you''re probably sick.

病床 is a relatively advanced word found in written phrases like 病床にふす (to be in sickbed). It can also formally refer to a <vocabulary>hospital bed</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6582', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%85%E5%BA%8A', 36, 1, '2013-07-02T19:47:36.160169Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.745716Z'),
(6583, 'vocabulary', '括る', '括る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>fasten</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to fasten</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to tie up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to tie together</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6583', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%AC%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:47:41.505732Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T00:09:32.879317Z'),
(6584, 'vocabulary', '一括', '一括', '<kanji>Fasten</kanji> everything into <kanji>one</kanji>. <vocabulary>All together</vocabulary> in one <vocabulary>batch</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>lump</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6584', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%8B%AC', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:47:46.600859Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T00:09:32.879317Z'),
(6585, 'vocabulary', '総括', '総括', 'If you take the <kanji>whole</kanji> and <kanji>fasten</kanji> it together, you are <vocabulary>summing up</vocabulary> something and creating an <vocabulary>overview</vocabulary> of it.

総括 is a formal word you''ll often come across in official meetings or reports. It means pulling together and summing up scattered bits of information — sometimes for a simple overview, like a department budget report, but more often for the sake of evaluating results and planning a future direction.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6585', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%8F%E6%8B%AC', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:47:51.638323Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T00:09:32.879317Z'),
(6586, 'vocabulary', '統括', '統括', '<kanji>Unite</kanji> something together and <kanji>fasten</kanji> it and you have <vocabulary>unification</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>generalization</vocabulary>. Nowadays this word is used to mean <vocabulary>management</vocabulary> in some contexts too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6586', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B1%E6%8B%AC', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:47:57.722256Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T00:09:32.879317Z'),
(6587, 'vocabulary', '括弧', '括弧', 'The <kanji>fasten</kanji> <kanji>arc</kanji>s are arcs that go around something to fasten these things all together. What are these things? They are (<vocabulary>parentheses</vocabulary>) and [<vocabulary>brackets</vocabulary>].', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6587', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%AC%E5%BC%A7', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:48:03.14255Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T00:09:32.879317Z'),
(6588, 'vocabulary', '円弧', '円弧', 'A <kanji>round</kanji> <kanji>arc</kanji> is, well, the shape of an <vocabulary>arc</vocabulary>. It has that round shape to it, you know?
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6588', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%86%E5%BC%A7', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:48:07.663803Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:48:00.080361Z'),
(6589, 'vocabulary', '挑む', '挑む', 'Because the hiragana ends with a う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>challenge</kanji>, and the verb form means <vocabulary>to challenge someone</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to take on something difficult</vocabulary>. 

挑む is a relatively formal word for boldly facing a challenge. It has a strong, spirited feel, like a climber saying "I''m taking on Mt. Everest!" You''ll come across it in headlines, live sports commentary, anime battle scenes, or even in daily life when someone wants to sound determined and fired up about their big personal goal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6589', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%91%E3%82%80', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:48:13.445329Z', NULL, '2025-12-07T19:03:34.17888Z'),
(6590, 'vocabulary', '挑戦', '挑戦', 'A <kanji>challenge</kanji> for <kanji>war</kanji>? This is <vocabulary>a challenge</vocabulary> worth accepting. After all, we can''t give up without at least giving it <vocabulary>a try</vocabulary> or making some kind of <vocabulary>an attempt</vocabulary>.

Note that 挑戦 refers to a challenge not in the sense of a difficult, challenging task, but in the sense of a bold or daring attempt to take on that task — whether that''s defeating a formidable foe, performing an impressive feat, or tackling a tricky problem.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6590', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%91%E6%88%A6', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:48:18.645527Z', NULL, '2025-12-07T19:03:34.17888Z'),
(6591, 'vocabulary', '挑発', '挑発', 'My <kanji>challenge</kanji> has made a <kanji>departure</kanji> and is being sent to the enemy. This challenge that I''m sending is a <vocabulary>provocation</vocabulary> to see if they''ll take me up on my challenge.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6591', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%91%E7%99%BA', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:48:23.700562Z', NULL, '2025-12-07T19:03:34.17888Z'),
(6592, 'vocabulary', '掘る', '掘る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>dig</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to dig</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6592', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%98%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:48:28.189034Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:50:46.147403Z'),
(6593, 'vocabulary', '発掘', '発掘', 'Let''s <kanji>departure</kanji> on our <kanji>dig</kanji>ging up of this site. It is more than just digging, we''re going somewhere and learning new things with this dig. This is an <vocabulary>excavation</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6593', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BA%E6%8E%98', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:48:32.874427Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:50:46.147403Z'),
(6594, 'vocabulary', '揚げる', '揚げる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>hoist</kanji>. But most commonly (and most importantly), this word means <vocabulary>to deep fry</vocabulary>. I guess you can think of it as hoisting that delicious deep-fried food out of the oil.

Of course, 揚げる can also mean <vocabulary>to hoist</vocabulary> (a sail) or <vocabulary>to raise</vocabulary> (a flag) depending on the context.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6594', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%9A%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:48:38.555124Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:42:49.398284Z'),
(6595, 'vocabulary', '握る', '握る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>grip</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to grasp</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6595', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%A1%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:48:50.913453Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.937911Z'),
(6596, 'vocabulary', '握手', '握手', 'A <kanji>grip</kanji> with your <kanji>hand</kanji> (onto another hand) is a <vocabulary>handshake</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6596', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%A1%E6%89%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:48:58.621441Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.950475Z'),
(6597, 'vocabulary', '握力', '握力', 'Your <kanji>grip</kanji> <kanji>power</kanji> is your <vocabulary>grip</vocabulary>, or more specifically your <vocabulary>grip strength</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>handgrip</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6597', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%A1%E5%8A%9B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:49:07.911169Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(6598, 'vocabulary', '揺る', '揺る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>shake</kanji> and the verb vocab is <vocabulary>to shake</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6598', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%BA%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:49:23.175058Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:52:31.392765Z'),
(6599, 'vocabulary', '動揺', '動揺', 'When your heart <kanji>move</kanji>s then <kanji>shake</kanji>s, you’re in a state of <vocabulary>emotional turmoil</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>unrest</vocabulary>.

動揺 refers to <vocabulary>being emotionally shaken</vocabulary>, or feeling disturbed, agitated, or unsettled.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6599', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%95%E6%8F%BA', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:49:28.403873Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:52:31.392765Z'),
(6600, 'vocabulary', '書斎', '書斎', 'I <kanji>write</kanji> for <kanji>purification</kanji>. I do this purification writing in my <vocabulary>study</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>den</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6600', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B8%E6%96%8E', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:49:33.135748Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:35.999282Z'),
(6601, 'vocabulary', '斎場', '斎場', 'A <kanji>purification</kanji> <kanji>location</kanji> is the place where you perform purification on people. Who needs the most purification? The dead. Where do you purify them? In a <vocabulary>funeral hall</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6601', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%8E%E5%A0%B4', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:49:38.513592Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.009969Z'),
(6602, 'vocabulary', '暫く', '暫く', 'When you do something <kanji>temporarily</kanji>, you''re only doing it <vocabulary>for a while</vocabulary>. It''s not permanent — however long it lasts, it''s just <vocabulary>for some time</vocabulary>.

暫く refers to a stretch of time that''s not too long, but not super short either — just somewhere in between. How long, exactly? It kinda depends on the speaker. If it feels like a while to you, then hey, it''s a while!

Keep in mind that the kanji form is quite formal, so it''s more commonly written in kana, like しばらく.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6602', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%AB%E3%81%8F', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:49:44.53727Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T00:04:25.580155Z'),
(6603, 'vocabulary', '暫定', '暫定', '<kanji>Temporarily</kanji> <kanji>determine</kanji> something. That makes it <vocabulary>tentative</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>temporary</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6603', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%AB%E5%AE%9A', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:51:31.816144Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(6604, 'vocabulary', '解析', '解析', 'If you <kanji>untie</kanji> the <kanji>analysis</kanji>, you are really just doing <vocabulary>analysis</vocabulary> to the analysis, if you catch my drift.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6604', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A3%E6%9E%90', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:51:36.662599Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T20:23:40.067262Z'),
(6605, 'vocabulary', '分析', '分析', 'You <kanji>divide</kanji> things to then do <kanji>analysis</kanji> on them. We can just call this <vocabulary>analysis</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>analyzing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6605', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%86%E6%9E%90', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:51:43.423547Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.065962Z'),
(6606, 'vocabulary', '枢軸', '枢軸', 'The <kanji>hinge</kanji> <kanji>axis</kanji> of something is the <vocabulary>axis</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>pivot</vocabulary> where things hinge.

枢軸 is generally used figuratively to describe the <vocabulary>central part</vocabulary> of something important, or the center of power.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6606', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%A2%E8%BB%B8', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:51:48.366143Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:50:28.040803Z'),
(6607, 'vocabulary', '中枢', '中枢', 'The <kanji>middle</kanji> <kanji>hinge</kanji> is the <vocabulary>center</vocabulary> <vocabulary>pivot</vocabulary> point.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6607', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E6%9E%A2', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:51:53.906967Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.103537Z'),
(6608, 'vocabulary', '軸', '軸', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Literally, 軸 can refer to a physical <vocabulary>axis</vocabulary>, like the shaft something rotates around. Figuratively, it describes the <vocabulary>pivotal part</vocabulary> of something, like the central figure in a project, the core idea of a discussion, or the core belief that shapes who you are.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6608', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%B8', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:51:58.796032Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:55:36.820306Z'),
(6609, 'vocabulary', '掛軸', '掛軸', 'It <kanji>hang</kanji>s on an <kanji>axis</kanji> point. The axis opens up, and reveals a <vocabulary>hanging scroll</vocabulary>. This might take a little extra imagination, but I believe in you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6609', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%9B%E8%BB%B8', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:52:04.16271Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:45:29.915612Z'),
(6610, 'vocabulary', '中軸', '中軸', 'The <kanji>middle</kanji> of the <kanji>axis</kanji> is where the <vocabulary>axis</vocabulary> is, of course, but it''s also where the <vocabulary>pivot</vocabulary> is too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6610', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E8%BB%B8', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:52:12.623058Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T19:50:01.349867Z'),
(6611, 'vocabulary', '柄', '柄', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6611', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%84', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:52:17.800937Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.148102Z'),
(6612, 'vocabulary', '国柄', '国柄', 'The <kanji>country</kanji> <kanji>pattern</kanji> is the pattern of things a country does. This is the <vocabulary>national character</vocabulary> of a nation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6612', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BD%E6%9F%84', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:52:23.438552Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.160916Z'),
(6613, 'vocabulary', '人柄', '人柄', 'A <kanji>person</kanji>''s <kanji>pattern</kanji> is the pattern of actions which they take to become who they are. What do they usually do? What is their pattern? This is the same as their <vocabulary>personal character</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>personality</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6613', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E6%9F%84', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:52:29.611176Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(6614, 'vocabulary', '一泊', '一泊', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>overnight</kanji> is a <vocabulary>one night''s stay</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6614', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%B3%8A', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:52:35.03622Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.183973Z'),
(6615, 'vocabulary', '二泊', '二泊', '<kanji>Two</kanji> <kanji>overnight</kanji>s is a <vocabulary>two-night stay</vocabulary>. In other words, you''re <vocabulary>staying for two nights</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6615', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E6%B3%8A', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:52:40.562664Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T04:11:02.025205Z'),
(6616, 'vocabulary', '宿泊', '宿泊', 'A <kanji>lodge</kanji> where you will stay <kanji>overnight</kanji> is <vocabulary>lodging</vocabulary>. It is where you will <vocabulary>stay the night</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6616', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%BF%E6%B3%8A', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:52:45.6839Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.20728Z'),
(6617, 'vocabulary', '泊まる', '泊まる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>overnight</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to stay the night</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6617', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%8A%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:52:50.878014Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.256342Z'),
(6618, 'vocabulary', '滑る', '滑る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>slide</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to slide</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to slip</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to glide</vocabulary>. 

滑る is a versatile word that can cover all kinds of actions, like sliding down playground slides, slipping on ice, skiing or skating, and even failing a test or being demoted. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6618', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BB%91%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:52:56.224124Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:01:23.771445Z'),
(6619, 'vocabulary', '円滑', '円滑', 'Imagine something <kanji>round</kanji> that <kanji>slide</kanji>s around with no trouble at all. Its movements are so <vocabulary>effortless</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>smooth</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6619', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%86%E6%BB%91', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:53:03.485951Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:10:33.22049Z'),
(6620, 'vocabulary', '潟', '潟', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6620', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%9F', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:53:08.526481Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.295086Z'),
(6621, 'vocabulary', '干潟', '干潟', 'A <kanji>dry</kanji> <kanji>lagoon</kanji> is a <vocabulary>tidal flat</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6621', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B2%E6%BD%9F', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:53:16.819537Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.30844Z'),
(6622, 'vocabulary', '焦点', '焦点', 'The <kanji>char</kanji> <kanji>point</kanji> is the part you''re looking at so hard it burns a little hole in it. Just imagine yourself looking at a single point so hard that it begins to smolder. This is the <vocabulary>focal point</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>focus</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6622', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A6%E7%82%B9', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:53:21.848435Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.322341Z'),
(6623, 'vocabulary', '焦げる', '焦げる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>char</kanji>, but the verb version is <vocabulary>to get burned</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to get scorched</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to get charred</vocabulary>.

This is for when something is burned on the surface. Most of the time, 焦げる is the unhappy and accidental kind of burning. You can use 焦げる when your bread is burned in the toaster, for example, or a shirt is scorched and blackened by an iron.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6623', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A6%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:53:26.460933Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T22:23:22.936166Z'),
(6624, 'vocabulary', '範囲', '範囲', 'Let''s take a look at this <kanji>example</kanji> and <kanji>surround</kanji> it. This example shows the <vocabulary>extent</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>scope</vocabulary> of what we''re doing, and the line that surrounds it shows how far that scope goes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6624', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AF%84%E5%9B%B2', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:53:38.285313Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.349665Z'),
(6625, 'vocabulary', '模範', '模範', 'An <kanji>imitation</kanji> of an <kanji>example</kanji> is the <vocabulary>exemplification</vocabulary> of something. It is also a <vocabulary>model</vocabulary> of the example of what you''re making.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6625', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A8%A1%E7%AF%84', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:53:43.767614Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.361426Z'),
(6626, 'vocabulary', '範', '範', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6626', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AF%84', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:53:48.384764Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.373045Z'),
(6627, 'vocabulary', '紛らす', '紛らす', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>distract</kanji> and the verb vocab version of this word is <vocabulary>to distract</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to divert</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6627', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%9B%E3%82%89%E3%81%99', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:53:53.765343Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.384517Z'),
(6628, 'vocabulary', '紛糾', '紛糾', 'When <kanji>distract</kanji>ions <kanji>twist together</kanji> they create all kinds of <vocabulary>complication</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>confusion</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>turmoil</vocabulary>. Imagine following one distraction, then being distracted again from the original distraction. Ugh, suddenly they all twist together and you''re stuck in a web of complication and confusion with no way to escape from the turmoil.

紛糾 is a formal word that describes a situation becoming mired in conflict or disagreement. It''s often used in news or official reports — for example, a political debate might 紛糾する because opinions clash and no conclusion can be reached.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6628', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%9B%E7%B3%BE', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:54:04.763011Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:48:40.338647Z'),
(6629, 'vocabulary', '糾弾', '糾弾', 'If you <kanji>twist together</kanji> some <kanji>bullet</kanji>s to make a big twisty super-bullet, you will be subject to <vocabulary>condemnation</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>denunciation</vocabulary>. Stop inventing new kinds of bullets and go do something productive!

糾弾 is a formal word used when someone is publicly condemned or denounced for wrongdoing. You may come across it in news reports or political discussions, like when activists 糾弾する a politician''s scandal or an organization''s injustice, for example.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6629', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B3%BE%E5%BC%BE', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:54:29.092931Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:44:14.475922Z'),
(6630, 'vocabulary', '綱', '綱', 'The kanji is <kanji>rope</kanji> so this word also refers to a <vocabulary>rope</vocabulary>. This can also mean a <vocabulary>sumo champion''s belt</vocabulary>, though you''ll probably see that meaning a lot less, unless you''re a huge sumo fan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6630', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%B1', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:54:35.502886Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.432827Z'),
(6631, 'vocabulary', '横綱', '横綱', 'The "<kanji>side</kanji> <kanji>rope</kanji>." This doesn''t make much sense. What this is, though (you''ll want to look at the reading), is the <vocabulary>Yokozuna</vocabulary>. This is the <vocabulary>Sumo Grand Champion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6631', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A8%AA%E7%B6%B1', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:54:40.898646Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.446616Z'),
(6632, 'vocabulary', '綱引き', '綱引き', 'The <kanji>rope</kanji> <kanji>pull</kanji>. Both sides are pulling on the rope. That is a <vocabulary>tug of war</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6632', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%B1%E5%BC%95%E3%81%8D', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:54:45.80261Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.460737Z'),
(6633, 'vocabulary', '網', '網', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6633', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%B2', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:54:51.180565Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.473948Z'),
(6634, 'vocabulary', '交通網', '交通網', 'You learned a while ago that 交通 means "traffic." So what would a traffic <kanji>net</kanji> be? A <vocabulary>traffic network</vocabulary>, or even a <vocabulary>transportation network</vocabulary>! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6634', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%A4%E9%80%9A%E7%B6%B2', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:55:00.411314Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.48685Z'),
(6635, 'vocabulary', '通信網', '通信網', 'When you <kanji>pass through</kanji>, you find out what everyone <kanji>believe</kanji>s via a great <kanji>net</kanji> connecting everyone. What is this net? It''s a <vocabulary>communication network</vocabulary>! When you think about it, things like telephone networks or the internet are really just big invisible nets that allow beliefs and information to pass through from one person to another.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6635', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%9A%E4%BF%A1%E7%B6%B2', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:55:06.890799Z', NULL, '2025-11-16T19:02:11.093561Z'),
(6636, 'vocabulary', '肝', '肝', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Though, it also means <vocabulary>chutzpah</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>courage</vocabulary> (besides just being <vocabulary>liver</vocabulary>). Maybe your liver is where your courage is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6636', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%9D', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:55:11.674603Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.515627Z'),
(6637, 'vocabulary', '肝炎', '肝炎', 'If you have the ol'' <kanji>liver</kanji> <kanji>flame</kanji> then you probably have <vocabulary>hepatitis</vocabulary>. AUGH! MY LIVER IS ON FIRE.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6637', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%9D%E7%82%8E', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:55:16.323065Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.529102Z'),
(6638, 'vocabulary', '肝臓', '肝臓', 'The <kanji>liver</kanji> <kanji>internal organ</kanji> is your <vocabulary>liver</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6638', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%9D%E8%87%93', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:55:21.379763Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.5424Z'),
(6639, 'vocabulary', '芝', '芝', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6639', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%9D', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:55:26.597827Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.557838Z'),
(6640, 'vocabulary', '芝生', '芝生', 'Your <kanji>lawn</kanji> of <kanji>life</kanji> is just your <vocabulary>lawn</vocabulary>. Of course it''s alive, otherwise it wouldn''t be a very good lawn.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6640', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%9D%E7%94%9F', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:55:31.392927Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.571768Z'),
(6641, 'vocabulary', '東芝', '東芝', 'The <kanji>east</kanji> <kanji>lawn</kanji> isn''t some eastern lawn, it''s in reference to the company <vocabulary>Toshiba</vocabulary>. Might be better to look at the reading first for this one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6641', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%B1%E8%8A%9D', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:55:36.460059Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:18:24.908021Z'),
(6642, 'vocabulary', '芝草', '芝草', 'The <kanji>lawn</kanji> <kanji>grass</kanji> is the <vocabulary>lawn</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>turf</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6642', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%9D%E8%8D%89', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:55:43.44365Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.598371Z'),
(6643, 'vocabulary', '荒い', '荒い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>wild</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>wild</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>rough</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6643', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8D%92%E3%81%84', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:55:50.289594Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.610969Z'),
(6644, 'vocabulary', '荒れる', '荒れる', 'The kanji is <kanji>wild</kanji> and the verb is <vocabulary>to be stormy</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be rough</vocabulary>. If something is wild, like a storm or something, then it will be stormy and rough, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6644', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8D%92%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:55:59.51956Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.624433Z'),
(6645, 'vocabulary', '袋', '袋', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6645', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A2%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:56:09.271912Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.638953Z'),
(6646, 'vocabulary', 'ゴミ袋', 'ゴミ袋', 'A <kanji>garbage</kanji> (ゴミ) <kanji>sack</kanji> is a <vocabulary>garbage sack</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>garbage bag</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6646', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%B4%E3%83%9F%E8%A2%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:56:13.725876Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.653581Z'),
(6647, 'vocabulary', '福袋', '福袋', 'The <kanji>luck</kanji> <kanji>sack</kanji> is a <vocabulary>mystery bag</vocabulary>. These are bags that are sold during the New Year''s holiday. They contain various goods that are sold for a certain price. You don''t know what''s in them, but you know the minimum value of it at least. These are known as <vocabulary>mystery sack</vocabulary>s as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6647', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A6%8F%E8%A2%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-02T19:56:19.026233Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.665678Z'),
(6648, 'vocabulary', '軍艦島', '軍艦島', 'A 軍艦 is a <kanji>battleship</kanji>, and 島 is an island. Welcome to... <vocabulary>Battleship Island</vocabulary> (go Jurassic Park music).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6648', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8D%E8%89%A6%E5%B3%B6', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:43:29.006176Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.677018Z'),
(6649, 'vocabulary', '偽物', '偽物', 'A <kanji>fake</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji> is <vocabulary>fake</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>counterfeit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6649', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%BD%E7%89%A9', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:45:14.407203Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(6650, 'vocabulary', '包み', '包み', 'Something that you <kanji>wrap</kanji> is a <vocabulary>bundle</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>package</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6650', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%85%E3%81%BF', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:47:41.312816Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.714394Z'),
(6651, 'vocabulary', '浴衣', '浴衣', 'The <kanji>bathe</kanji> <kanji>clothes</kanji> are clothes you wear to and from the bath. They''re your <vocabulary>yukata</vocabulary>.

浴衣 originated as robes worn in a steam bath (that explains the kanji), but these days people mostly wear them at summer festivals or traditional Japanese inns. Be careful not to mix up 浴衣 with 着物, which are much more formal and elaborate!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6651', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B4%E8%A1%A3', 27, 1, '2013-07-02T21:47:54.474945Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.747167Z'),
(6652, 'vocabulary', '誠に', '誠に', 'The に on the end of this word turns <kanji>sincerity</kanji> into an adverb. What''s the adverb version of sincerity? It''s <vocabulary>sincerely</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>indeed</vocabulary>. It <vocabulary>really</vocabulary> is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6652', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%A0%E3%81%AB', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:47:59.505876Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.779656Z'),
(6653, 'vocabulary', '誰', '誰', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6653', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%B0', 11, 1, '2013-07-02T21:48:04.871264Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.807949Z'),
(6654, 'vocabulary', '誰か', '誰か', '誰 is <kanji>who</kanji>, 誰か is like asking "who?" If you don''t know who it is, you just know it''s <vocabulary>someone</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6654', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%B0%E3%81%8B', 12, 1, '2013-07-02T21:48:10.622194Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.832691Z'),
(6655, 'vocabulary', '珍〜', '珍〜', 'Something <kanji>rare</kanji> is often <vocabulary>unusual</vocabulary>. This word could also mean <vocabulary>rare</vocabulary> (of course) and <vocabulary>peculiar</vocabulary> depending on the context.

These days, media often uses this word to describe the way something is unusual, quirky, and funny.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6655', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%8D%E3%80%9C', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:48:16.067247Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.862259Z'),
(6656, 'vocabulary', '珍味', '珍味', 'A <kanji>rare</kanji> <kanji>taste</kanji> is a taste you can''t have very often, because it''s rare. This is a <vocabulary>delicacy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6656', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%8D%E5%91%B3', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:48:21.008325Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:20:50.221396Z'),
(6657, 'vocabulary', '珍しい', '珍しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>rare</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>rare</vocabulary>. But, this can also mean <vocabulary>unusual</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>curious</vocabulary> (as in "odd" curious, not that you''re curious about something).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6657', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%8D%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:48:25.993661Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:53:53.450427Z'),
(6658, 'vocabulary', '裂く', '裂く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>split</kanji>, but if you split something, you are going <vocabulary>to tear</vocabulary> it or <vocabulary>to rip up</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6658', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%82%E3%81%8F', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:48:31.2465Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.9005Z'),
(6659, 'vocabulary', '決裂', '決裂', 'The <kanji>decision</kanji> is <kanji>split</kanji>, causing a <vocabulary>breakdown</vocabulary> in all we''ve decided. A big ol'' <vocabulary>rupture</vocabulary>.

決裂 is a formal word used when talks or relationships totally fall apart. Since it refers to a full-on rupture and sounds dramatic, you''ll often see it in news headlines or news reports when negotiations between companies or countries crash and burn.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6659', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%BA%E8%A3%82', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:48:35.853637Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T00:59:25.689942Z'),
(6660, 'vocabulary', '分裂', '分裂', 'Things get <kanji>divide</kanji>d and <kanji>split</kanji>. We can just call this a <vocabulary>split</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>division</vocabulary>.

分裂 primarily refers to cells dividing in biology, but it can also figuratively describe an entity or group splitting into separate parts, often due to internal conflict.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6660', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%86%E8%A3%82', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:48:43.535178Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.924842Z'),
(6661, 'vocabulary', '襲う', '襲う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>attack</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to attack</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6661', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%B2%E3%81%86', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:48:48.152926Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.93897Z'),
(6662, 'vocabulary', '襲撃', '襲撃', 'An <kanji>attack</kanji> <kanji>attack</kanji> is an <vocabulary>attack</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>assault</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6662', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%B2%E6%92%83', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:48:53.280484Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.950329Z'),
(6663, 'vocabulary', '奇襲', '奇襲', 'An <kanji>odd</kanji> <kanji>attack</kanji> is an attack you don''t expect. This is a <vocabulary>surprise attack</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6663', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%87%E8%A5%B2', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:48:58.159635Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.963867Z'),
(6664, 'vocabulary', '空襲', '空襲', 'A <kanji>sky</kanji> <kanji>attack</kanji> is an <vocabulary>air raid</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>air attack</vocabulary>. Tora tora tora!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6664', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA%E8%A5%B2', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:49:05.819437Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:36.977533Z'),
(6665, 'vocabulary', '逆襲', '逆襲', 'A <kanji>reverse</kanji> <kanji>attack</kanji> is a <vocabulary>counterattack</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6665', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%86%E8%A5%B2', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:49:12.058819Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:30:39.384701Z'),
(6666, 'vocabulary', '貢献', '貢献', 'The <kanji>tribute</kanji> I <kanji>offer</kanji> is my <vocabulary>contribution</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6666', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A2%E7%8C%AE', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:49:17.115344Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.002014Z'),
(6667, 'vocabulary', '趣', '趣', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. 
This word covers a lot of meanings so it can be <vocabulary>charm</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>taste</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>vibe</vocabulary>, or even <vocabulary>gist</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6667', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B6%A3', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:49:23.595022Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.01325Z'),
(6668, 'vocabulary', '趣味', '趣味', 'If something''s got <kanji>charm</kanji> and is to your <kanji>taste</kanji>, then it''s your <vocabulary>hobby</vocabulary>. Hobbies are activities that you find charming and develop a taste for.
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6668', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B6%A3%E5%91%B3', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:49:28.791696Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:20:50.221396Z'),
(6669, 'vocabulary', '距離', '距離', 'The <kanji>distance</kanji> that is <kanji>detach</kanji>ed from the distance is the <vocabulary>distance</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>range</vocabulary>. Just think of one part of the distance, stretch it out, and then look at it. It shows the distance and range!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6669', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B7%9D%E9%9B%A2', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:49:42.244684Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.039401Z'),
(6670, 'vocabulary', '戸籍', '戸籍', 'A <kanji>door</kanji> <kanji>register</kanji>? Well, a register of people who live behind the same door. In other words, a <vocabulary>family register</vocabulary>. (Think of "door" as shorthand for a house in this case.) Family registers are actually pretty interesting. Look up "koseki" on Google and you''re sure to find many fascinating histories.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6670', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%B8%E7%B1%8D', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:49:48.505857Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:07:26.086065Z'),
(6671, 'vocabulary', '国籍', '国籍', 'If you''re on a <kanji>country</kanji>''s <kanji>register</kanji>, you probably have <vocabulary>nationality</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>citizenship</vocabulary> there. What''s your 国籍?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6671', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BD%E7%B1%8D', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:49:53.317657Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:07:26.086065Z'),
(6672, 'vocabulary', '暴露', '暴露', 'Using <kanji>violence</kanji> I''m going to <kanji>expose</kanji> the truth! This is <vocabulary>disclosure</vocabulary>. This is <vocabulary>exposure</vocabulary>.

This word carries the connotation of something scandalous being revealed, often in a dramatic manner.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6672', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%B4%E9%9C%B2', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:50:04.969224Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.07867Z'),
(6673, 'vocabulary', '露骨', '露骨', 'I''m going to <kanji>expose</kanji> my <kanji>bone</kanji>s for a second here and be <vocabulary>blunt</vocabulary> with you... this makes sense, because if you''re exposing your bones at someone, you''re not hiding anything. You''re being frank and blunt.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6673', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%B2%E9%AA%A8', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:50:11.815403Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.09131Z'),
(6674, 'vocabulary', '露店', '露店', 'An <kanji>expose</kanji>d <kanji>shop</kanji> is one that''s not inside. It''s outside, and exposed. It''s a <vocabulary>street stall</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6674', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%B2%E5%BA%97', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:50:16.300489Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.103Z'),
(6675, 'vocabulary', '露出', '露出', 'When what you <kanji>expose</kanji> is <kanji>exit</kanji>ed out of hiding, exposed for everything to see... that is <vocabulary>exposure</vocabulary>. It can also refer to <vocabulary>photographic exposure</vocabulary> too, in case you were wondering.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6675', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%B2%E5%87%BA', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:50:21.178967Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.115322Z'),
(6676, 'vocabulary', '牧師', '牧師', 'A <kanji>pasture</kanji> <kanji>teacher</kanji> is one who leads his flock around through life. This is a <vocabulary>pastor</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>minister</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>clergyman</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6676', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%A7%E5%B8%AB', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:50:25.874565Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.128561Z'),
(6677, 'vocabulary', '牧野', '牧野', 'A <kanji>pasture</kanji> <kanji>field</kanji> is <vocabulary>pasture land</vocabulary>. It''s also a <vocabulary>ranch</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6677', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%A7%E9%87%8E', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:50:31.027133Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.14139Z'),
(6678, 'vocabulary', '牧草', '牧草', 'The <kanji>pasture</kanji> <kanji>grass</kanji> is the <vocabulary>pasture</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>grass</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6678', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%A7%E8%8D%89', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:50:37.070595Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.153358Z'),
(6679, 'vocabulary', '牧場', '牧場', 'A <kanji>pasture</kanji> <kanji>place</kanji> is a <vocabulary>livestock farm</vocabulary>. It''s also a <vocabulary>ranch</vocabulary>. The place where things pasture is this place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6679', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%A7%E5%A0%B4', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:50:42.421406Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.164558Z'),
(6680, 'vocabulary', '印刷', '印刷', 'With a <kanji>seal</kanji> we''re <kanji>printing</kanji> everything out. The seal is what prints the words on the paper, making this <vocabulary>printing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6680', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B0%E5%88%B7', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:50:47.371483Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.176457Z'),
(6681, 'vocabulary', '刷る', '刷る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>printing</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to print</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6681', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%B7%E3%82%8B', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:50:52.343479Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.189345Z'),
(6682, 'vocabulary', '明朗', '明朗', 'A <kanji>bright</kanji> <kanji>bright</kanji> is something that''s <vocabulary>bright</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6682', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%8E%E6%9C%97', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:50:57.294599Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(6683, 'vocabulary', '朗報', '朗報', 'A <kanji>bright</kanji> <kanji>news</kanji> report is going to be one full of <vocabulary>good news</vocabulary>. A dark news report is probably bad news, but we only care about good news right now, don''t we?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6683', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%97%E5%A0%B1', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:51:02.406467Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.215713Z'),
(6684, 'vocabulary', '朗らか', '朗らか', 'This is the na-adjective version of <kanji>bright</kanji>. The word is <vocabulary>bright</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>cheerful</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6684', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%97%E3%82%89%E3%81%8B', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:51:08.513114Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.228329Z'),
(6685, 'vocabulary', '潮', '潮', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6685', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%AE', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:51:13.306291Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.240077Z'),
(6686, 'vocabulary', '風潮', '風潮', 'The <kanji>wind</kanji> <kanji>tide</kanji> is the direction in which the tide is blowing. Is it blowing in? Is it blowing out? It is the <vocabulary>tide</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>current</vocabulary>. These days, we usually use this word for the current <vocabulary>trend</vocabulary>s that come in like the tide.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6686', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A2%A8%E6%BD%AE', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:51:30.127054Z', NULL, '2025-07-01T19:41:45.398894Z'),
(6687, 'vocabulary', '満潮', '満潮', 'A <kanji>full</kanji> <kanji>tide</kanji> is a <vocabulary>high tide</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>full tide</vocabulary>. That''s when the water comes up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6687', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%80%E6%BD%AE', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:51:35.077456Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.263593Z'),
(6688, 'vocabulary', '潮流', '潮流', 'A <kanji>tide</kanji>s <kanji>stream</kanji> is the stream going through the tide. It''s the <vocabulary>tide</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>tidal current</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6688', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%AE%E6%B5%81', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:52:10.505298Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.276495Z'),
(6689, 'vocabulary', '即〜', '即〜', 'If you see 即 before a word, you know that it''s probably something that happens really fast or instantaneously. That''s why this is <vocabulary>immediate</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>instant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6689', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B3%E3%80%9C', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:52:16.341007Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.288153Z'),
(6690, 'vocabulary', '即興', '即興', 'An <kanji>instant</kanji> <kanji>interest</kanji> in something you didn''t have interest in before? Well, time for some <vocabulary>improvisation</vocabulary>, because you''re going to have to pretend you have interest in it (since you don''t actually).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6690', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B3%E8%88%88', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:52:21.244758Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.300362Z'),
(6691, 'vocabulary', '即座', '即座', 'An <kanji>instant</kanji> <kanji>sit</kanji> is <vocabulary>immediate</vocabulary>. It''s also <vocabulary>instantaneous</vocabulary>. Just imagine yourself sitting instantly. No time passes between when you were standing and sitting. The instantaneous nature of your sit is definitely... troubling.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6691', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B3%E5%BA%A7', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:52:28.64584Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:52:04.645576Z'),
(6692, 'vocabulary', '即死', '即死', 'An <kanji>instant</kanji> <kanji>death</kanji> is an <vocabulary>instant death</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6692', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B3%E6%AD%BB', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:52:33.852676Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.323386Z'),
(6693, 'vocabulary', '即日', '即日', 'The <kanji>instant</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji> is the day that''s going on this instant. It''s the <vocabulary>same day</vocabulary> as now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6693', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B3%E6%97%A5', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:52:38.298395Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.335645Z'),
(6694, 'vocabulary', '即効', '即効', 'It''s <kanji>instant</kanji>ly <kanji>effect</kanji>ive! Sounds like something out of Pokemon. This word means <vocabulary>immediate effect</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>instant effect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6694', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B3%E5%8A%B9', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:52:43.988399Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.347115Z'),
(6695, 'vocabulary', '垣', '垣', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Note that for the word fence in Japanese, フェンス is typically used. 垣 isn''t very common but it''s sometimes used in literature.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6695', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9E%A3', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:52:49.168491Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.368056Z'),
(6696, 'vocabulary', '垣根', '垣根', 'A <kanji>hedge</kanji> (or <kanji>fence</kanji>) <kanji>root</kanji> is where the hedge and fence grow from. What grows from this? Well, it''s simply a <vocabulary>hedge</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>fence</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6696', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9E%A3%E6%A0%B9', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:52:54.631207Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.380006Z'),
(6697, 'vocabulary', '石垣', '石垣', 'A <kanji>stone</kanji> <kanji>hedge</kanji> is a wall (hedges are nice walls), but it''s made of stone. It''s a <vocabulary>stone wall</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6697', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%B3%E5%9E%A3', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:52:59.926991Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.39102Z'),
(6698, 'vocabulary', '権威', '権威', 'You have <kanji>rights</kanji> and <kanji>might</kanji> over this area. That gives you <vocabulary>authority</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6698', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A8%A9%E5%A8%81', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:53:04.961966Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T22:02:48.269631Z'),
(6699, 'vocabulary', '威張る', '威張る', 'Take your <kanji>dignity</kanji> and <kanji>stretch</kanji> it. Your dignity is so stretched! That makes you feel big! That''s why this word is <vocabulary>to act big</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be arrogant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6699', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A8%81%E5%BC%B5%E3%82%8B', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:53:09.499828Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:18.693962Z'),
(6700, 'vocabulary', '球威', '球威', 'Your <kanji>sphere</kanji> <kanji>might</kanji> is the force you command over a sphere or ball… in this case, a baseball, making this <vocabulary>a pitcher''s stuff</vocabulary>. 

If you''re not familiar with this baseball term, we recommend giving it a google. In short, it refers to a pitcher''s pitches, judged by how inherently hard those pitches are to hit.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6700', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%90%83%E5%A8%81', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:53:14.728052Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:18.693962Z'),
(6701, 'vocabulary', '威厳', '威厳', '<kanji>Dignity</kanji> is a <kanji>strict</kanji> thing. If you''re too lenient, you''ll lose all your <vocabulary>dignity</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>majesty</vocabulary>.  ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6701', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A8%81%E5%8E%B3', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:53:20.706149Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:18.693962Z'),
(6702, 'vocabulary', '封', '封', 'The kanji is <kanji>seal in</kanji>, but the vocab noun version is <vocabulary>seal</vocabulary>. This is simply a seal (not the water kind, the kind you use to seal something with).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6702', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%81', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:53:26.6286Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:57:46.95136Z'),
(6703, 'vocabulary', '開封', '開封', 'When you <kanji>open</kanji> something <kanji>seal</kanji>ed, you''re <vocabulary>unsealing</vocabulary> it or <vocabulary>breaking a seal</vocabulary>.

開封 generally refers to opening something sealed, like opening a letter or package.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6703', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%8B%E5%B0%81', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:53:31.955076Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:57:46.95136Z'),
(6704, 'vocabulary', '封書', '封書', 'A <kanji>seal in</kanji> <kanji>writing</kanji> is writing that has been sealed into an envelope. This is a <vocabulary>sealed letter</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6704', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%81%E6%9B%B8', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:53:38.601607Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:57:46.95136Z'),
(6705, 'vocabulary', '封建主義', '封建主義', 'You''ve learned that 主義 is "-ism." So what happens when you <kanji>seal</kanji> up everything you <kanji>build</kanji> and keep it for yourself, and make that your -ism? You get <vocabulary>feudalism</vocabulary>. Why? Because feudalism is a system built on the idea that power should be sealed within one group or family. Anyone outside of this sealed system is a vassal to the lord — that''s feudalism for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6705', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%81%E5%BB%BA%E4%B8%BB%E7%BE%A9', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:53:43.788867Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:57:46.95136Z'),
(6706, 'vocabulary', '封筒', '封筒', '<kanji>Seal in</kanji> the <kanji>cylinder</kanji>. This cylinder that is sealed in is an <vocabulary>envelope</vocabulary>. You just have to think back to the day, when things were more like in scroll form. So, a scroll''s envelope would have been shaped like a cylinder, making this an <vocabulary>envelope</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6706', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%81%E7%AD%92', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:53:48.761785Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:57:46.95136Z'),
(6707, 'vocabulary', '筒', '筒', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. You''ll see this used to mean <vocabulary>pipe</vocabulary> more often than <vocabulary>cylinder</vocabulary>, though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6707', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AD%92', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:53:53.233483Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.499979Z'),
(6708, 'vocabulary', '山岳', '山岳', 'The <kanji>mountain</kanji> <kanji>peak</kanji>s are just the <vocabulary>mountains</vocabulary>. Just because you can only see the peaks doesn''t mean the rest of the mountain doesn''t exist.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6708', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%B1%E5%B2%B3', 45, 1, '2013-07-02T21:53:58.246506Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.51055Z'),
(6709, 'vocabulary', '岳', '岳', 'The <kanji>peak</kanji> of the mountain is a <vocabulary>peak</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>mountain</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6709', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B2%B3', 47, 1, '2013-07-02T21:54:05.249255Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.600718Z'),
(6710, 'vocabulary', '慰謝', '慰謝', 'I show my <kanji>consolation</kanji> by <kanji>apologiz</kanji>ing. This is <vocabulary>consolation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6710', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%B0%E8%AC%9D', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:54:46.202948Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:59:33.248971Z'),
(6711, 'vocabulary', '慰める', '慰める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>consolation</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to console</vocabulary>.

慰める means <vocabulary>to comfort</vocabulary> someone who''s experiencing sadness or distress. It can also imply cheering someone up by offering kind words, encouragement, or emotional support.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6711', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%B0%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:54:51.550727Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:59:33.248971Z'),
(6712, 'vocabulary', '慰問', '慰問', 'The best <kanji>consolation</kanji> for someone''s <kanji>problem</kanji> is <vocabulary>paying a sympathy call</vocabulary>. It is <vocabulary>consolation</vocabulary> through a <vocabulary>sympathy visit</vocabulary>.

慰問 is a formal way of saying お見舞い (sympathy visit). It''s often used when someone notable, like a celebrity or politician, makes a visit to offer support and comfort to people in difficult situations, like hospital patients, nursing home residents, or those affected by disasters.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6712', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%B0%E5%95%8F', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:54:56.349774Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:59:33.248971Z'),
(6713, 'vocabulary', '慰安', '慰安', 'You have <kanji>consolation</kanji> so now you can <kanji>relax</kanji>. What a great feeling this is. You now have <vocabulary>solace</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>relaxation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6713', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%B0%E5%AE%89', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:55:01.458741Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:59:33.248971Z'),
(6714, 'vocabulary', '懇意', '懇意', 'Being <kanji>courteous</kanji> is the right <kanji>idea</kanji> if you want to be on <vocabulary>friendly</vocabulary> terms with others. The more courteous you are to someone (and vice versa), the more a sense of <vocabulary>closeness</vocabulary> grows between you.

懇意 formally describes being on good, cordial terms with someone. You might talk about a colleague you''ve become friendly with (懇意になった同僚) or a professor who treats you warmly (懇意にしてくれる教授), for example.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6714', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%87%87%E6%84%8F', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:55:07.429043Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.575676Z'),
(6715, 'vocabulary', '懇話', '懇話', 'A <kanji>courteous</kanji> <kanji>talk</kanji> is a <vocabulary>friendly talk</vocabulary>. We all know that "friendly talks" are never what they seem, though... right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6715', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%87%87%E8%A9%B1', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:55:12.534229Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.586769Z'),
(6716, 'vocabulary', '懇親', '懇親', 'If you treat someone with <kanji>courteous</kanji> <kanji>intimacy</kanji>, you''re probably trying to build a <vocabulary>friendship</vocabulary> with them. Or… maybe something more? No, stop, it''s not that kind of intimacy! This word is all about polite friendship and <vocabulary>camaraderie</vocabulary>. Yay for friendship!

懇親 is a formal word that means getting to know someone better in a friendly and sociable manner. You''ll most often see it in the word 懇親会 (a casual party for socializing).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6716', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%87%87%E8%A6%AA', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:55:17.240431Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.608719Z'),
(6717, 'vocabulary', '懇談', '懇談', 'A <kanji>courteous</kanji> <kanji>talk</kanji> is an <vocabulary>informal talk</vocabulary> where people gather and discuss something in a friendly way.

懇談 is often used for parent-teacher meetings or community meetings. It''s an informal but courteous talk, usually between acquaintances rather than close friends.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6717', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%87%87%E8%AB%87', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:55:24.45514Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.62959Z'),
(6718, 'vocabulary', '懲りる', '懲りる', 'The kanji means <kanji>chastise</kanji> but the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to learn by experience</vocabulary>. I guess these are kind of similar. When you make a mistake, someone will chastise you, and then you will learn by experience (either via the experience or via the chastising).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6718', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%87%B2%E3%82%8A%E3%82%8B', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:55:30.080282Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.640135Z'),
(6719, 'vocabulary', '懲罰', '懲罰', 'I will <kanji>chastise</kanji> you and give you <kanji>penalty</kanji>. I do this as a <vocabulary>discipline</vocabulary>. A <vocabulary>punishment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6719', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%87%B2%E7%BD%B0', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:55:38.609432Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:49:26.899294Z'),
(6720, 'vocabulary', '摩擦', '摩擦', 'Things that <kanji>chafe</kanji> and <kanji>rub</kanji> have lots of <vocabulary>friction</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6720', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%91%A9%E6%93%A6', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:55:43.660069Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.660942Z'),
(6721, 'vocabulary', '擦れる', '擦れる', 'This is an intransitive version of 擦る, so it means <vocabulary>to be rubbed</vocabulary>. It often implies damage, in which case it can mean <vocabulary>to be grazed</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be scraped</vocabulary>, like when your knee gets grazed against a rough wall. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6721', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%93%A6%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 47, 1, '2013-07-02T21:55:48.369874Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.772452Z'),
(6722, 'vocabulary', '相撲', '相撲', 'A <kanji>mutual</kanji> <kanji>slap</kanji> is when two big guys run at each other and slap each other. This is <vocabulary>sumo wrestling</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6722', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B8%E6%92%B2', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:56:01.791461Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.683774Z'),
(6723, 'vocabulary', '一斉', '一斉', 'At <kanji>one</kanji> moment it happened <kanji>simultaneous</kanji>ly. It was <vocabulary>simultaneous</vocabulary>. It was <vocabulary>all at once</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6723', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%96%89', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:56:07.323637Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T03:09:32.141363Z'),
(6724, 'vocabulary', '旨い', '旨い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>delicious</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>delicious</vocabulary>. It also means "<vocabulary>skillful</vocabulary>" and "<vocabulary>clever</vocabulary>" as well, because delicious things are skillfully delicious, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6724', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A8%E3%81%84', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:56:15.96662Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T04:46:14.680063Z'),
(6725, 'vocabulary', '旨', '旨', 'The <kanji>point</kanji> of something is the <vocabulary>gist</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>principle</vocabulary> of it. So, what''s the gist of this vocab word? It''s <vocabulary>gist</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6725', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A8', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:56:21.352092Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.718909Z'),
(6726, 'vocabulary', '要旨', '要旨', 'The parts we <kanji>need</kanji>. The <kanji>point</kanji> of that. That is the <vocabulary>main point</vocabulary>. That is the <vocabulary>essentials</vocabulary>. It''s what we need and it''s to the point, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6726', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%81%E6%97%A8', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:56:26.660985Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:28:03.237353Z'),
(6727, 'vocabulary', '論旨', '論旨', 'The <kanji>point</kanji> to my <kanji>theory</kanji> is the <vocabulary>point of an argument</vocabulary>. That''s what I''m trying to say in my theory / argument, man!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6727', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%96%E6%97%A8', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:56:31.423502Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.742593Z'),
(6728, 'vocabulary', '柔らかい', '柔らかい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, which tells you it''s probably an adjective. The kanji means <kanji>gentle</kanji>, and the adjective version means <vocabulary>soft</vocabulary> — as in a gentle, <vocabulary>tender</vocabulary> kind of softness. Think soft towel, soft hand, soft voice… anything soft in a way that gives you the warm fuzzies.

In reference to the human body, 柔らかい can also mean <vocabulary>flexible</vocabulary>, with an emphasis on supple, effortless movement.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6728', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%94%E3%82%89%E3%81%8B%E3%81%84', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:56:37.613324Z', NULL, '2025-12-29T21:44:43.855984Z'),
(6729, 'vocabulary', '柔道', '柔道', 'The <kanji>gentle</kanji> <kanji>path</kanji> is, surprisingly, <vocabulary>judo</vocabulary>. Yep, the sport / art based on throwing people around.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6729', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%94%E9%81%93', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:56:42.545648Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.766964Z'),
(6730, 'vocabulary', '柔和', '柔和', 'A <kanji>gentle</kanji> and <kanji>peace</kanji>ful person is <vocabulary>gentle</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>tender</vocabulary>.

柔和 is commonly used in literature to describe someone whose nature or features express <vocabulary>gentleness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6730', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%94%E5%92%8C', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:56:47.768452Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.777851Z'),
(6731, 'vocabulary', '沈める', '沈める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>sink</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to sink something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to submerge something</vocabulary>.

This is a transitive verb, meaning it takes a direct object. So instead of describing something sinking on its own, you use 沈める when you sink an enemy ship or submerge yourself in a bathtub. 

You know this because you can''t sink anything until you get that <reading>mail</reading> (める) saying, "Yes, proceed with sinking!" You''ve got to have that mail if you want to sink something, or else everyone will blame you for the missing ship — or for you disappearing under the water.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6731', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%88%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T21:56:53.943179Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.789784Z'),
(6732, 'vocabulary', '沈む', '沈む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>sink</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to sink</vocabulary>.

On a literal level, 沈む refers to physical sinking — a boat sinking into the water or someone''s foot sinking into the mud, for example. However, it can also be used metaphorically to talk about things like the sun setting (太陽が沈む), a nation falling into decline (国が沈む), or even someone "sinking" into a depressed mood (気分が沈む).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6732', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%88%E3%82%80', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:56:59.172167Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T00:54:05.737384Z'),
(6733, 'vocabulary', '沈滞', '沈滞', '<kanji>Sink</kanji> and <kanji>stagnate</kanji>. Imagine something is doing just that. It is in the state of <vocabulary>stagnation</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>inactivity</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6733', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%88%E6%BB%9E', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:57:13.297178Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.814944Z'),
(6734, 'vocabulary', '沼', '沼', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Note that, unlike English, which differentiates between different types of wetlands like marshes, swamps, or bogs, Japanese 沼 refers to any pond-like areas with muddy, murky water.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6734', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%BC', 28, 1, '2013-07-02T21:57:20.100184Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.842894Z'),
(6735, 'vocabulary', '沼田', '沼田', 'A <kanji>bog</kanji> <kanji>rice paddy</kanji> is a <vocabulary>marshy rice field</vocabulary>. This is just a rice paddy in marshy form.

In modern language, 沼田 is mostly used as a place name or surname, such as <vocabulary>Numata</vocabulary> City in Gunma Prefecture or someone with the last name Numata.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6735', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%BC%E7%94%B0', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:57:25.536114Z', NULL, '2025-07-28T16:56:58.370645Z'),
(6736, 'vocabulary', '沼地', '沼地', 'A <kanji>bog</kanji> <kanji>earth</kanji> place is a place where the earth is boggy. That''s a <vocabulary>marshland</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>wetland</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6736', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%BC%E5%9C%B0', 28, 1, '2013-07-02T21:57:30.490983Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.886748Z'),
(6737, 'vocabulary', '泥沼', '泥沼', 'A <kanji>mud</kanji>dy <kanji>bog</kanji> is a way of describing a <vocabulary>quagmire</vocabulary>. It’s a bog with almost no water left, having become all muddy and sticky. Gross (and dangerous)!

This word is typically used figuratively to describe a situation where a conflict becomes messy and difficult to resolve, or a problem from which it is difficult to extricate oneself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6737', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A5%E6%B2%BC', 33, 1, '2013-07-02T21:57:35.742816Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.694516Z'),
(6738, 'vocabulary', '沼沢', '沼沢', 'A <kanji>bog</kanji> <kanji>swamp</kanji> is a <vocabulary>swamp</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>marsh</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6738', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%BC%E6%B2%A2', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:57:43.793718Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.922186Z'),
(6739, 'vocabulary', '安泰', '安泰', '<kanji>Relax</kanji> in <kanji>peace</kanji>. Wherever you are that''s allowing you to do this, it is certainly <vocabulary>peace</vocabulary>. You feel <vocabulary>security</vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6739', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%89%E6%B3%B0', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:57:48.718613Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:37.933342Z'),
(6740, 'vocabulary', '滅ぼす', '滅ぼす', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>destroy</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to destroy</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to annihilate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6740', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BB%85%E3%81%BC%E3%81%99', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:57:56.334168Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:36.594815Z'),
(6741, 'vocabulary', '滅亡', '滅亡', '<kanji>Destroy</kanji> to <kanji>death</kanji>. If you are destroyed to this point, this will be your <vocabulary>downfall</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>ruin</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6741', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BB%85%E4%BA%A1', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:58:01.679823Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:36.594815Z'),
(6742, 'vocabulary', '撲滅', '撲滅', '<kanji>Slap</kanji> that bug and <kanji>destroy</kanji> it. Haha! I slapped all the bugs away. This is <vocabulary>extermination</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6742', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%92%B2%E6%BB%85', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:58:07.254903Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:36.594815Z'),
(6743, 'vocabulary', '絶滅', '絶滅', '<kanji>Extinction</kanji> where everything is <kanji>destroy</kanji>ed is <vocabulary>extinction</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6743', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B6%E6%BB%85', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:58:13.516771Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:36.594815Z'),
(6744, 'vocabulary', '壊滅', '壊滅', 'If you <kanji>break</kanji> and <kanji>destroy</kanji> something, that is <vocabulary>destruction</vocabulary>. That is <vocabulary>annihilation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6744', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%8A%E6%BB%85', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:58:18.755168Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:36.594815Z'),
(6745, 'vocabulary', '幻滅', '幻滅', 'The <kanji>illusion</kanji> is <kanji>destroy</kanji>ed. If your illusion is destroyed, that is <vocabulary>disillusionment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6745', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BB%E6%BB%85', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:58:24.157889Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:36.594815Z'),
(6746, 'vocabulary', '滋養', '滋養', '<kanji>Nourishing</kanji> and <kanji>foster</kanji>ing. The thing that gives you this is <vocabulary>nourishment</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>nutrition</vocabulary>.

滋養 is a formal and somewhat old-fashioned word. It''s mostly used in writing to describe nutritious foods or the health benefits of supplements, tonics, or energy drinks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6746', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BB%8B%E9%A4%8A', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:58:30.486263Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:40:05.658345Z'),
(6747, 'vocabulary', '高炉', '高炉', 'A <kanji>high</kanji> <kanji>furnace</kanji> is one that''s particularly hot and blasty. This is a <vocabulary>blast furnace</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6747', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E7%82%89', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:58:35.258032Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.027676Z'),
(6748, 'vocabulary', '暖炉', '暖炉', 'A <kanji>warm</kanji> <kanji>furnace</kanji> is a furnace for warming you and other things. This is a <vocabulary>fireplace</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>hearth</vocabulary> or even a <vocabulary>stove</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6748', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%96%E7%82%89', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:58:40.540012Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.039284Z'),
(6749, 'vocabulary', '炉心', '炉心', 'The <kanji>furnace</kanji> <kanji>heart</kanji> is the heart of the furnace. The heart of this particular furnace, the one we''re talking about here, is the <vocabulary>nuclear reactor core</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6749', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%89%E5%BF%83', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:58:45.186176Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.05149Z'),
(6750, 'vocabulary', '原子炉', '原子炉', 'If a 原子 is an <kanji>atom</kanji> and a 炉 is a <kanji>furnace</kanji>, what is a furnace that has atoms? It is an <vocabulary>atomic reactor</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>nuclear reactor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6750', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%9F%E5%AD%90%E7%82%89', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:58:50.337439Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.062825Z'),
(6751, 'vocabulary', '琴', '琴', 'The <kanji>harp</kanji>, at least in Japan, is the <vocabulary>koto</vocabulary>, also known (to us) as the <vocabulary>Japanese harp</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>Japanese zither</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6751', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%90%B4', 43, 1, '2013-07-02T21:58:56.558923Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.076762Z'),
(6752, 'vocabulary', '携帯ストラップ', '携帯ストラップ', 'You know how 携帯 refers to a mobile phone? This is a <kanji>strap</kanji> (ストラップ) for a mobile phone, making this a <vocabulary>cell phone strap</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>mobile phone strap</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6752', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%BA%E5%B8%AF%E3%82%B9%E3%83%88%E3%83%A9%E3%83%83%E3%83%97', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:34:09.456039Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.088079Z'),
(6753, 'vocabulary', '克服する', '克服する', '克服 is "conquest" so the verb version of that is <vocabulary>to conquer</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6753', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%8B%E6%9C%8D%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:34:15.622635Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.100011Z'),
(6754, 'vocabulary', '哲学者', '哲学者', 'A <kanji>philosophy</kanji> <kanji>scholar</kanji> (学者) is someone who studies philosophy. They''re known as a <vocabulary>philosopher</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6754', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%93%B2%E5%AD%A6%E8%80%85', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:34:21.127336Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(6755, 'vocabulary', '喪服', '喪服', 'Your <kanji>mourning</kanji> <kanji>clothes</kanji> is your <vocabulary>mourning dress</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>mourning clothes</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6755', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%AA%E6%9C%8D', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:34:27.69813Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.121866Z'),
(6756, 'vocabulary', '挑戦者', '挑戦者', 'A <kanji>challenge</kanji> (挑戦) <kanji>someone</kanji> is someone who is going to challenge you. They are <vocabulary>a challenger</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6756', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%91%E6%88%A6%E8%80%85', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:34:32.136328Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(6757, 'vocabulary', '揚げ', '揚げ', 'Remember how 揚げる means "to deep fry"? This is the prefix or suffix version of that word, making it <vocabulary>deep-fried</vocabulary>...

Note that 揚げ can sometimes be used as a standalone word, in which case it usually refers to deep-fried tofu.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6757', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%9A%E3%81%92', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:34:37.769678Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:42:49.398284Z'),
(6758, 'vocabulary', '握り', '握り', 'Remember how 握る is "to grasp"? This is the noun version and means <vocabulary>one''s grip</vocabulary>. It''s often used to talk about how strong or loose someone''s grip is, like on a tool, a baseball, or even an opponent''s collar in judo.

If you add a number to 握り, like in 一握り, it can mean <vocabulary>handful</vocabulary>. It can also refer to <vocabulary>nigiri sushi</vocabulary>, since making that kind of sushi involves taking a handful of rice and gripping it into shape.

Occasionally this can also refer to something you''re meant to grip, like the handle on a fishing rod or a pachinko machine, but this use is less common. Whew, there''s a lot to know about this word — but hopefully you''ve got a grip on 握り now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6758', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%A1%E3%82%8A', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:34:49.106925Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:03:12.781425Z'),
(6759, 'vocabulary', '揺さぶる', '揺さぶる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>shake</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to shake</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6759', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%BA%E3%81%95%E3%81%B6%E3%82%8B', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:34:54.390608Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:52:31.392765Z'),
(6760, 'vocabulary', '揺する', '揺する', '<kanji>Shake</kanji> plus "to do" is the verb version of shake. That''s <vocabulary>to shake</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to rock</vocabulary> slowly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6760', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%BA%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:35:00.399034Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:52:31.392765Z'),
(6761, 'vocabulary', '揺れる', '揺れる', 'Remember 揺る which means "to shake" or "to jolt"? This is like that, but now you''re not doing the shaking and jolting, making it <vocabulary>to be shaken</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be jolted</vocabulary>. You know this because it''s really <reading>rare</reading> (れる) for you to be shaken like this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6761', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%BA%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:35:08.761252Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:52:31.392765Z'),
(6762, 'vocabulary', '泊まり', '泊まり', 'You know that 泊まる is "to stay the night." The noun version is a <vocabulary>overnight stay</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>sleepover</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6762', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%8A%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8A', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:35:13.725775Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.215897Z'),
(6763, 'vocabulary', '泊める', '泊める', '泊まる is "to stay the night." Now you''re letting someone else stay the night, which makes this <vocabulary>to let someone stay over</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to let someone stay the night</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6763', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%8A%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 45, 1, '2013-07-02T22:35:18.957756Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T23:31:10.456499Z'),
(6764, 'vocabulary', '滑り台', '滑り台', 'Remember how 滑る is "to slide," and 台 is a counter for machines? In this case, the "machine" you slide down is a <vocabulary>playground slide</vocabulary>, or just a <vocabulary>slide</vocabulary> for short.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6764', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BB%91%E3%82%8A%E5%8F%B0', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:35:24.583587Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:02:28.053308Z'),
(6765, 'vocabulary', '焦る', '焦る', 'This is a bit of a weird word. The kanji is <kanji>char</kanji>, and a previous word you learned (焦げる) is "to be burned." This, on the other hand, is "to be in a hurry" or "to be impatient." I guess if you''re getting burned or have that feeling, you''re going <vocabulary>to be in a hurry</vocabulary> and you''re going <vocabulary>to be impatient</vocabulary>. You can tell that this is that word because it only has one kana, る, which stands for <reading>ru</reading>de. You''re feeling someone is being rude (or maybe you are), which is why you have this feeling of impatience and hurrying.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6765', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A6%E3%82%8B', 45, 1, '2013-07-02T22:35:29.343605Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.255536Z'),
(6766, 'vocabulary', '焦がす', '焦がす', 'Remember how 焦げる means "to get burned"? 焦がす is the transitive version, so this time someone is actively doing the burning, making it <vocabulary>to burn</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to scorch</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to char</vocabulary>.

焦がす means to burn the surface of something, usually blackening it to some degree. Unlike 焦げる, it can be for both intentional burning (a nice crème brûlée) and accidental burning (leaving bread in the toaster too long, scorching your sleeve on the stove).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6766', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A6%E3%81%8C%E3%81%99', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:35:37.986056Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T22:19:40.643251Z'),
(6767, 'vocabulary', '紛れる', '紛れる', 'You know that 紛らす means "to divert" or "to distract." This is when you''re not doing the action, now, making this word <vocabulary>to be diverted</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be distracted</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6767', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%9B%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:35:48.559394Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.283137Z'),
(6768, 'vocabulary', '紛らわしい', '紛らわしい', 'The kanji is <kanji>distract</kanji> and the adjective version is <vocabulary>confusing</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>misleading</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6768', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%9B%E3%82%89%E3%82%8F%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:35:56.027255Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T02:27:23.52422Z'),
(6769, 'vocabulary', '網戸', '網戸', 'A <kanji>net</kanji> <kanji>door</kanji> is a <vocabulary>screen door</vocabulary>. Makes sense, right? Screen doors are like little meshy nets made into a door shape.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6769', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%B2%E6%88%B8', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:36:05.554758Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.305233Z'),
(6770, 'vocabulary', '荒波', '荒波', 'A <kanji>wild</kanji> <kanji>wave</kanji> will make for some <vocabulary>stormy seas</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>raging waves</vocabulary>. Don''t go out in these kinds of waves.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6770', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8D%92%E6%B3%A2', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:36:13.301665Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:11:56.943742Z'),
(6771, 'vocabulary', '手袋', '手袋', 'A <kanji>hand</kanji> <kanji>sack</kanji> is a sack for your hands... better put, these are <vocabulary>gloves</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6771', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E8%A2%8B', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:36:21.630203Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.327964Z'),
(6772, 'vocabulary', '竜', '竜', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6772', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%9C', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:36:30.001365Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.340868Z'),
(6773, 'vocabulary', '竜巻', '竜巻', 'A <kanji>dragon</kanji> <kanji>scroll</kanji> is a dragon that''s shaped kind of like a scroll, spinning around. This is a <vocabulary>tornado</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6773', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%9C%E5%B7%BB', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:36:36.965269Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.354416Z'),
(6774, 'vocabulary', '恐竜', '恐竜', 'A <kanji>fear</kanji> <kanji>dragon</kanji> is a <vocabulary>dinosaur</vocabulary>. Dinosaurs are much, much scarier than dragons, and that''s why dinosaurs are called fear dragons in Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6774', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%90%E7%AB%9C', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:36:43.261242Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.377282Z'),
(6775, 'vocabulary', '縁起', '縁起', 'Remember how 縁 means "edge," but can also mean "relation"? Well, the <kanji>relation</kanji> that can <kanji>wake up</kanji> some new feeling in you is an <vocabulary>omen</vocabulary>. Think about it — seeing an omen is really just you being sensitive enough to notice all the relations going on in the world.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6775', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%81%E8%B5%B7', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:36:50.741903Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:29:09.593874Z'),
(6776, 'vocabulary', '無縁', '無縁', 'You learned 縁 means "edge," but remember how it means "relation" too? Well, if something has a <kanji>nothing</kanji> <kanji>relation</kanji>, it''s probably <vocabulary>unrelated</vocabulary>.

無縁 describes <vocabulary>having no ties</vocabulary> with something else — you''re utterly divorced from or <vocabulary>uninvolved</vocabulary> with that thing. For example, maybe you grew up in 宗教とは無縁な家庭 (a totally nonreligious household), or maybe you live お酒とは無縁な生活 (a life free of alcohol). Whatever the case, this thing is totally alien to you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6776', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E7%B8%81', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:36:57.297886Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:33:18.601194Z'),
(6777, 'vocabulary', '縁談', '縁談', 'You learned 縁 means "edge," but remember how it can also mean "relation"? Well, when you have a <kanji>relation</kanji> to <kanji>discuss</kanji>, that can only mean one thing: it''s <vocabulary>marriage proposal</vocabulary> time! Er, that was the direction you were hoping to take this relation, right? Hope so…

縁談 is a relatively formal word for the discussion, suggestion, arrangement, or proposal of marriage, often in the context of matchmaking or arranged marriages. It''s not for the actual act of proposing — for that you''d just use プロポーズ. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6777', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%81%E8%AB%87', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:37:05.968464Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:27:49.118361Z'),
(6778, 'vocabulary', '左翼', '左翼', 'The <kanji>left</kanji> <kanji>wing</kanji> is the <vocabulary>left wing</vocabulary>, as in the "left wing party" or "left wing politics" or something like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6778', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A6%E7%BF%BC', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:37:13.361996Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.425248Z'),
(6779, 'vocabulary', '右翼', '右翼', 'The <kanji>right</kanji> <kanji>wing</kanji> is the <vocabulary>right wing</vocabulary>. As in, "right wing politics" or "right wing beliefs."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6779', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B3%E7%BF%BC', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:37:19.962275Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.437379Z'),
(6780, 'vocabulary', '翼', '翼', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6780', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BF%BC', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:37:26.864888Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T22:30:45.275455Z'),
(6781, 'vocabulary', '吉', '吉', 'Having <kanji>good luck</kanji> is a <vocabulary>good omen</vocabulary> to have. Good luck means more good luck, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6781', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%89', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:37:32.961988Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.464163Z'),
(6782, 'vocabulary', '不吉', '不吉', 'Something that is <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>good luck</kanji> is <vocabulary>ominous</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>sinister</vocabulary>. It''s also <vocabulary>bad luck</vocabulary> and an <vocabulary>ill omen</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6782', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%90%89', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:37:38.861815Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.477185Z'),
(6783, 'vocabulary', '忍ぶ', '忍ぶ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>endure</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to endure</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6783', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%8D%E3%81%B6', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:37:44.819374Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:35:05.2219Z'),
(6784, 'vocabulary', '忍者', '忍者', 'A <kanji>conceal</kanji>ed <kanji>someone</kanji> is someone who can conceal themselves. Who''s better at that than anyone? A <vocabulary>ninja</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6784', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%8D%E8%80%85', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:37:51.147818Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(6785, 'vocabulary', '刃', '刃', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6785', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%83', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:38:08.056749Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:18:22.640804Z'),
(6786, 'vocabulary', '刃物', '刃物', 'A <kanji>blade</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji> is an <vocabulary>edged tool</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>cutlery</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6786', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%83%E7%89%A9', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:38:14.458845Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:18:22.640804Z'),
(6787, 'vocabulary', '刃先', '刃先', 'The <kanji>blade</kanji>''s <kanji>ahead</kanji> is the farthest part of the blade away from you (since it''s ahead of you). This is the <vocabulary>edge of the blade</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>blade edge</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6787', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%83%E5%85%88', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:38:20.857644Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:18:22.640804Z'),
(6788, 'vocabulary', '桃', '桃', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6788', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A1%83', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:38:26.89025Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.55475Z'),
(6789, 'vocabulary', '桃色', '桃色', 'A <kanji>peach</kanji> <kanji>color</kanji> is a <vocabulary>peach color</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>pink</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6789', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A1%83%E8%89%B2', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:38:31.654621Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.567898Z'),
(6790, 'vocabulary', '辛い', '辛い', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. That being said, this can also mean <vocabulary>difficult</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>painful</vocabulary>, often in a psychological sense, though it can be physical as well. I guess if you''re in a spicy situation it''s going to be a difficult one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6790', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%9B%E3%81%84', 9, 1, '2013-07-02T22:38:37.652651Z', NULL, '2025-12-23T01:38:33.184703Z'),
(6791, 'vocabulary', '香辛料', '香辛料', 'The <kanji>fee</kanji> for <kanji>fragrance</kanji> and <kanji>spicy</kanji> in your food is the fee of <vocabulary>spices</vocabulary>. If you don''t pay your spices fee, you aren''t going to have any spice or fragrance in your food. If you do, you''ll have those things and your food will be awesome!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6791', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%99%E8%BE%9B%E6%96%99', 38, 1, '2013-07-02T22:38:44.313504Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.605909Z'),
(6792, 'vocabulary', '辛子', '辛子', 'The <kanji>spicy</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> is the child of spicy. So, not as spicy as mamma spicy, but still a bit spicy. This child spicy is <vocabulary>mustard</vocabulary>. Not too spicy, but not too mild.

辛子 generally refers to <vocabulary>Japanese mustard</vocabulary>, which tends to be browner and stronger than マスタード (Western mustard). Sometimes, especially as a prefix, it can also indicate spiciness from red pepper, like 辛子マヨネーズ (spicy mayo).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6792', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%9B%E5%AD%90', 9, 1, '2013-07-02T22:38:52.369695Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.734247Z'),
(6793, 'vocabulary', '辛抱', '辛抱', 'A <kanji>spicy</kanji> <kanji>hug</kanji> is a hug that feels not so great. But, it''s not really painful all at once, either. As you''re receiving a spicy hug, you just have to show <vocabulary>patience</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>endurance</vocabulary> while it lasts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6793', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%9B%E6%8A%B1', 38, 1, '2013-07-02T22:39:03.64631Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.646227Z'),
(6794, 'vocabulary', '辛勝', '辛勝', 'A <kanji>spicy</kanji> <kanji>win</kanji> is a <vocabulary>narrow victory</vocabulary>. Kind of makes sense, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6794', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%9B%E5%8B%9D', 58, 1, '2013-07-02T22:39:10.145472Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.660321Z'),
(6795, 'vocabulary', '謎', '謎', 'The kanji and the word mean exactly the same thing. That makes this a <vocabulary>mystery</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>riddle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6795', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AC%8E', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:39:16.168374Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T01:09:45.68337Z'),
(6796, 'vocabulary', '謎々', '謎々', 'A <kanji>mystery</kanji>, doubled? Hm, this is truly a <vocabulary>riddle</vocabulary>. You could even call it a <vocabulary>puzzle</vocabulary> or an <vocabulary>enigma</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6796', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AC%8E%E3%80%85', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:39:21.056443Z', NULL, '2025-12-02T10:14:58.023438Z'),
(6797, 'vocabulary', '侍', '侍', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6797', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%8D', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:39:26.156666Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.711042Z'),
(6798, 'vocabulary', '俺', '俺', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Keep in mind that 俺 has a more informal and masculine sound than neutral options like 私 and 僕. While it carries a rougher vibe, it''s not solely reserved for tough personalities and is very common in casual chats among buddies.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6798', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%BA', 13, 1, '2013-07-02T22:39:40.581985Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.741106Z'),
(6799, 'vocabulary', '俺ら', '俺ら', '<kanji>I</kanji> with a ら on it makes it plural. It''s less formal than 俺たち (if you''ve seen that word yet), but has the same meaning. It''s <vocabulary>we</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6799', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%BA%E3%82%89', 14, 1, '2013-07-02T22:39:47.306111Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.770012Z'),
(6800, 'vocabulary', '俺たち', '俺たち', 'When たち is attached to a word referring to a person, it makes it plural. So, when you attach たち to 俺 (<kanji>I</kanji>), it means <vocabulary>we</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>us</vocabulary>.

Just remember that 俺 is quite casual and rough sounding, so it''s best to only use it with close friends (or when you''re shaking someone down...).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6800', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%BA%E3%81%9F%E3%81%A1', 13, 1, '2013-07-02T22:39:52.675389Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.795562Z'),
(6801, 'vocabulary', '叱る', '叱る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>scold</kanji> and the verb vocab version also means <vocabulary>to scold</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6801', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B1%E3%82%8B', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:39:57.971351Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.809438Z'),
(6802, 'vocabulary', '娯楽', '娯楽', '<kanji>Recreation</kanji> and <kanji>comfort</kanji> are things you do for <vocabulary>pleasure</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>amusement</vocabulary>. So nice!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6802', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A8%AF%E6%A5%BD', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:40:03.486384Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T18:49:45.773585Z'),
(6803, 'vocabulary', '斗', '斗', 'How much can you hold in this <kanji>ladle</kanji>? Why, exactly <vocabulary>eighteen liters</vocabulary> of course! Eighteen is your favorite number, how convenient.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6803', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%97', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:40:08.843536Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.832892Z'),
(6804, 'vocabulary', '朱', '朱', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. 

朱 refers to <vocabulary>vermilion</vocabulary>, a bright red color with a slight orange tint, and can also be translated as <vocabulary>scarlet red</vocabulary>. It''s common in Japanese culture — you''ll often see it on shrine gates and lacquerware, for example.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6804', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%B1', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:40:14.289014Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:06:22.937152Z'),
(6805, 'vocabulary', '朱印', '朱印', 'A <kanji>vermilion</kanji> colored <kanji>seal</kanji> is a <vocabulary>red seal</vocabulary>. Just look up the (Japanese) word in Google Image search and you''ll see what this is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6805', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%B1%E5%8D%B0', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:40:20.632737Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.859442Z'),
(6806, 'vocabulary', '丘', '丘', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6806', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%98', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:40:33.033714Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.871665Z'),
(6807, 'vocabulary', '砂丘', '砂丘', 'A <kanji>sand</kanji> <kanji>hill</kanji> is a <vocabulary>sand dune</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6807', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%82%E4%B8%98', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:40:37.975885Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:27:03.046576Z'),
(6808, 'vocabulary', '梨', '梨', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same, so this means <vocabulary>pear</vocabulary> too.

Note that 梨 usually refers specifically to the spherical <vocabulary>Asian pear</vocabulary>. When you want to refer to the teardrop-shaped European variety of pear, use the word 洋梨 (ようなし).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6808', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A2%A8', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:40:45.455068Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:51:00.861349Z'),
(6809, 'vocabulary', '〜匹', '〜匹', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6809', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%8C%B9', 15, 1, '2013-07-02T22:40:52.555206Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.115965Z'),
(6810, 'vocabulary', '四匹', '四匹', '<kanji>Four</kanji> <kanji>small animal</kanji>s is <vocabulary>four small animals</vocabulary>. Use this to count four dogs, or cats, or whatever else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6810', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B%E5%8C%B9', 15, 1, '2013-07-02T22:40:59.218581Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.171859Z'),
(6811, 'vocabulary', '一匹', '一匹', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>small animal</kanji> is <vocabulary>one small animal</vocabulary>. Use this to count small animals like cats and dogs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6811', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%8C%B9', 19, 1, '2013-07-02T22:41:04.587287Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.962794Z'),
(6812, 'vocabulary', '叫ぶ', '叫ぶ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>shout</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to shout</vocabulary>. Can also be <vocabulary>to scream</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6812', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%AB%E3%81%B6', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:41:10.95593Z', NULL, '2025-09-08T08:33:28.058447Z'),
(6813, 'vocabulary', '絶叫', '絶叫', '<kanji>Extinction</kanji> caused by a <kanji>shout</kanji>. You are making everyone else''s voices go extinct, and you <vocabulary>scream</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>shout</vocabulary> over them. You are making an <vocabulary>exclamation</vocabulary>, gosh darn it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6813', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B6%E5%8F%AB', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:41:17.390652Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:38.98864Z'),
(6814, 'vocabulary', '釣り', '釣り', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6814', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%A3%E3%82%8A', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:41:23.248777Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.002582Z'),
(6815, 'vocabulary', '釣る', '釣る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>fishing</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to fish</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6815', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%A3%E3%82%8B', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:41:31.68913Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.016148Z'),
(6816, 'vocabulary', '髪', '髪', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Just be sure to know that this is the hair on your head.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6816', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%AA', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:41:37.606174Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.028062Z'),
(6817, 'vocabulary', '金髪', '金髪', '<kanji>Gold</kanji> <kanji>hair</kanji> is <vocabulary>blonde</vocabulary> hair. Makes sense!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6817', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%91%E9%AB%AA', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:41:42.748735Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.039943Z'),
(6818, 'vocabulary', '髪の毛', '髪の毛', 'Your <kanji>hair</kanji> <kanji>fur</kanji> is the hair fur that''s specifically on your head. It''s your <vocabulary>head hair</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6818', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%AA%E3%81%AE%E6%AF%9B', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:41:47.348244Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.051209Z'),
(6819, 'vocabulary', '髪型', '髪型', 'Your <kanji>hair</kanji> <kanji>model</kanji> is how you model your hair. It''s your <vocabulary>hairstyle</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>hairdo</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6819', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%AA%E5%9E%8B', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:41:54.616934Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.063757Z'),
(6820, 'vocabulary', '嵐', '嵐', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6820', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B5%90', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:42:00.70518Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.075254Z'),
(6821, 'vocabulary', '笠', '笠', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6821', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AC%A0', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:42:05.441707Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.087209Z'),
(6822, 'vocabulary', '大砲', '大砲', 'A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>cannon</kanji> is... wait for it... just a <vocabulary>cannon</vocabulary>. I guess all cannons are big, right? At least when you compare them to guns.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6822', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E7%A0%B2', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:42:14.892912Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(6823, 'vocabulary', '涙', '涙', 'Your <kanji>teardrop</kanji>s are also known as your <vocabulary>tears</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6823', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B6%99', 35, 1, '2013-07-02T22:42:20.117643Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.130828Z'),
(6824, 'vocabulary', '感涙', '感涙', 'An excess of <kanji>feeling</kanji> produces <kanji>teardrop</kanji>s. These are <vocabulary>tears of emotion</vocabulary>, but not the bad kind. You''re just <vocabulary>being moved to tears</vocabulary>.

感涙 is an advanced word mainly used in writing. It refers to the kind of tears you might shed when you''re profoundly touched with positive emotion, like when you''re truly grateful for something or moved by a powerful speech.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6824', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E6%B6%99', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:42:25.044237Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(6825, 'vocabulary', '缶ビール', '缶ビール', 'A <kanji>can</kanji> of <kanji>beer</kanji> (ビール) is a <vocabulary>can beer</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>canned beer</vocabulary>. Not a bottled one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6825', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BC%B6%E3%83%93%E3%83%BC%E3%83%AB', 13, 1, '2013-07-02T22:42:32.963328Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.164887Z'),
(6826, 'vocabulary', '缶', '缶', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6826', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BC%B6', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:42:41.328013Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.176806Z'),
(6827, 'vocabulary', '空き缶', '空き缶', 'You know that 空く means "to become empty," and 缶 means <kanji>can</kanji>, so this must be an <vocabulary>empty can</vocabulary>.

空き缶 usually refers to drink cans after their contents have been consumed, rather than a brand-new empty can that hasn''t been filled yet. In other words, it''s the kind of can you''d throw away or recycle after finishing your drink.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6827', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA%E3%81%8D%E7%BC%B6', 13, 1, '2013-07-02T22:42:46.374173Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:52:00.002011Z'),
(6828, 'vocabulary', '缶コーヒー', '缶コーヒー', 'A <kanji>can</kanji> <kanji>coffee</kanji> (コーヒー) is coffee that''s in a can. That''s <vocabulary>canned coffee</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>can coffee</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6828', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BC%B6%E3%82%B3%E3%83%BC%E3%83%92%E3%83%BC', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:42:52.117372Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.211866Z'),
(6829, 'vocabulary', '姫', '姫', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Can also be used to refer to a <vocabulary>noble girl</vocabulary> as well, as in a girl of the noble family.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6829', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%AB', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:42:59.535632Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.223821Z'),
(6830, 'vocabulary', '姫様', '姫様', 'The <kanji>princess</kanji> (<kanji>formal name ender</kanji>) is the princess, and you''re talking to her politely. She is the <vocabulary>princess</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6830', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%AB%E6%A7%98', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:43:08.063809Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.236005Z'),
(6831, 'vocabulary', '棚', '棚', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6831', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A3%9A', 23, 1, '2013-07-02T22:43:13.710223Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.262253Z'),
(6832, 'vocabulary', '本棚', '本棚', 'A <kanji>book</kanji> <kanji>shelf</kanji> is a <vocabulary>bookcase</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>bookshelf</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6832', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E6%A3%9A', 23, 1, '2013-07-02T22:43:23.282215Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.289066Z'),
(6833, 'vocabulary', '戸棚', '戸棚', 'A <kanji>door</kanji> <kanji>shelf</kanji> is a shelf with a door on it. What kinds of shelves do you know of that have doors on them? How about <vocabulary>cabinet</vocabulary>s? Or maybe <vocabulary>cupboard</vocabulary>s?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6833', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%B8%E6%A3%9A', 23, 1, '2013-07-02T22:43:29.917707Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.315832Z'),
(6834, 'vocabulary', '粒', '粒', '<kanji>Grains</kanji> are made of single <vocabulary>grain</vocabulary>s. You can also use this to refer to a <vocabulary>bead</vocabulary> or use it as a <vocabulary>counter for grains</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6834', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%92', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:43:35.307055Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.327461Z'),
(6835, 'vocabulary', '粒子', '粒子', 'A <kanji>grain''s</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> is even smaller than a grain. It''s a <vocabulary>particle</vocabulary>, sometimes even an <vocabulary>atomic particle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6835', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%92%E5%AD%90', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:43:41.629766Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.339574Z'),
(6836, 'vocabulary', '雷雨', '雷雨', 'A <kanji>thunder</kanji> <kanji>rain</kanji> is a rain that''s coming with thunder. For all intents and purposes, if you see this, you''ll think <vocabulary>thunderstorm</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6836', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%B7%E9%9B%A8', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:43:55.183057Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.350432Z'),
(6837, 'vocabulary', '雷', '雷', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6837', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%B7', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:44:01.172907Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.360959Z'),
(6838, 'vocabulary', '魚雷', '魚雷', '<kanji>Fish</kanji> <kanji>thunder</kanji> is underwater thunder that shoots at things and are kind of shaped like fish. That''s a <vocabulary>torpedo</vocabulary>. Load the fish thunder!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6838', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AD%9A%E9%9B%B7', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:44:10.114804Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.372859Z'),
(6839, 'vocabulary', '芽', '芽', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6839', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%BD', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:44:17.025989Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.38406Z'),
(6840, 'vocabulary', '塔', '塔', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Can also be a <vocabulary>pagoda</vocabulary>. If you think about it, pagodas were the towers of the olden days.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6840', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%94', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:44:23.890903Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.395674Z'),
(6841, 'vocabulary', '管制塔', '管制塔', 'The <kanji>pipe</kanji> <kanji>control</kanji> <kanji>tower</kanji> is the tower that controls all the pipes... think of the pipes not as pipes, but as directions things are moving in. You''re controlling all of that on a map, and the pipe shows where everything is going and you''re changing it to be where it needs to go. You are in the <vocabulary>control tower</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6841', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AE%A1%E5%88%B6%E5%A1%94', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:44:29.207154Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:36:25.472861Z'),
(6842, 'vocabulary', 'バベルの塔', 'バベルの塔', 'バベル''s 塔 is Babel''s tower. That''s the <vocabulary>Tower of Babel</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6842', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%90%E3%83%99%E3%83%AB%E3%81%AE%E5%A1%94', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:44:38.885688Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.466536Z'),
(6843, 'vocabulary', 'エッフェル塔', 'エッフェル塔', 'The <kanji>Eiffel</kanji> (エッフェル) <kanji>tower</kanji> is the <vocabulary>Eiffel Tower</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6843', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%A8%E3%83%83%E3%83%95%E3%82%A7%E3%83%AB%E5%A1%94', 44, 1, '2013-07-02T22:44:45.965956Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.479812Z'),
(6844, 'vocabulary', '引き裂く', '引き裂く', 'You know that 裂く is "to tear" or "to rip up." So, if you''re trying to tear or rip up something, and you''re <kanji>pull</kanji>ing it at the same time, you are going <vocabulary>to tear up</vocabulary> that thing, or more accurately perhaps <vocabulary>to tear off</vocabulary> something, since you''re pulling.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6844', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%95%E3%81%8D%E8%A3%82%E3%81%8F', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:10:53.730605Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.495332Z'),
(6845, 'vocabulary', '核分裂', '核分裂', '分裂 means "split" or "division." The <kanji>nucleus</kanji> version of this split is <vocabulary>nuclear fission</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6845', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%B8%E5%88%86%E8%A3%82', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:11:06.675531Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.51068Z'),
(6846, 'vocabulary', '襲撃する', '襲撃する', 'Remember how 襲撃 is an <kanji>assault</kanji> or <kanji>attack</kanji>? This is the verb form, so it''s <vocabulary>to attack</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to assault</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6846', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%B2%E6%92%83%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:11:13.250266Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.524441Z'),
(6847, 'vocabulary', '遠距離', '遠距離', 'A <kanji>far</kanji> <kanji>distance</kanji> <kanji>detach</kanji>ment is a distance that is far. It is <vocabulary>long distance</vocabulary>. Also remember that 距離 is a "distance" so a far distance is a <vocabulary>long distance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6847', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%A0%E8%B7%9D%E9%9B%A2', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:11:21.954546Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.538388Z'),
(6848, 'vocabulary', '近距離', '近距離', 'A 距離 is a <kanji>distance</kanji> and 近 is <kanji>near</kanji>. A near distance is a <vocabulary>short distance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6848', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E8%B7%9D%E9%9B%A2', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:11:28.287171Z', NULL, '2026-01-05T23:19:07.929774Z'),
(6849, 'vocabulary', '短距離', '短距離', 'A 距離 (<kanji>distance</kanji>) that is <kanji>short</kanji> is a <vocabulary>short distance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6849', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%AD%E8%B7%9D%E9%9B%A2', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:11:34.41098Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:23:36.0332Z'),
(6850, 'vocabulary', '入籍', '入籍', 'Remember how 戸籍 is the family register? This is referring to that, and that makes this word <vocabulary>entry in family register</vocabulary>. In most cases, that specifically means a <vocabulary>registration of marriage</vocabulary> in that family register.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6850', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E7%B1%8D', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:11:46.863289Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:07:26.086065Z'),
(6851, 'vocabulary', '一斉に', '一斉に', '一斉 is <kanji>simultaneous</kanji> or <kanji>all at once</kanji>. Add the に in there and you''re heading towards those things. For "simultaneous" that becomes <vocabulary>simultaneously</vocabulary>. For "all at once" it stays at <vocabulary>all at once</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6851', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%96%89%E3%81%AB', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:12:00.040881Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.590592Z'),
(6852, 'vocabulary', '滅びる', '滅びる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>destroy</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be destroyed</vocabulary>. You know you''re not destroying things because of the <reading>beer</reading> (びる). You''d never destroy beer... but someone else might. Grrr.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6852', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BB%85%E3%81%B3%E3%82%8B', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:12:06.427218Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:36.594815Z'),
(6853, 'vocabulary', '硬い', '硬い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>stiff</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>stiff</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>hard</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>firm</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6853', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A1%AC%E3%81%84', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:12:12.150251Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.625607Z'),
(6854, 'vocabulary', '硬直', '硬直', 'Keep something <kanji>stiff</kanji> and <kanji>straight</kanji> and it''s naturally going to lead to <vocabulary>stiffness</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>stiffening</vocabulary>.

硬直 commonly describes a person''s body becoming stiff, whether from shock or discomfort, or due to the onset of rigor mortis following death.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6854', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A1%AC%E7%9B%B4', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:12:18.358913Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:14:20.398462Z'),
(6855, 'vocabulary', '稼ぐ', '稼ぐ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>earnings</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to earn income</vocabulary>, because you can''t "to income" something. Can also be <vocabulary>to make money</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6855', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%BC%E3%81%90', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:12:23.959252Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:16:29.380113Z'),
(6856, 'vocabulary', '澄む', '澄む', 'Something with <kanji>lucidity</kanji> is clear. That''s why this word is <vocabulary>to become clear</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to become transparent</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6856', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BE%84%E3%82%80', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:12:29.993689Z', NULL, '2026-01-04T19:54:54.196787Z'),
(6857, 'vocabulary', '矛', '矛', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6857', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%9B', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:12:59.287066Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.684424Z'),
(6858, 'vocabulary', '肌', '肌', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well, though you''ll want to know that this refers most specifically to <vocabulary>human skin</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6858', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%8C', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:13:06.183821Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.695547Z'),
(6859, 'vocabulary', '鳥肌', '鳥肌', '<kanji>Bird</kanji> <kanji>skin</kanji> is <vocabulary>goosebumps</vocabulary>. Makes sense, since a goose is a bird and a bird''s skin is bumpy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6859', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%B3%A5%E8%82%8C', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:13:12.449212Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:12:26.957805Z'),
(6860, 'vocabulary', '肌触り', '肌触り', 'The <kanji>skin</kanji> <kanji>touch</kanji> is  the feeling of touching with your skin. That is <vocabulary>the touch of</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>feel of</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>texture</vocabulary> of something. It''s what you feel when you touch your skin to something!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6860', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%8C%E8%A7%A6%E3%82%8A', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:13:17.92417Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T23:12:17.87634Z'),
(6861, 'vocabulary', '肌色', '肌色', '<kanji>Skin</kanji> <kanji>color</kanji> is <vocabulary>skin color</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>skin tone</vocabulary>.

This word originally referred to a pale orange or peach color, and sometimes you''ll still see it used that way. But labeling that as 肌色 wasn''t inclusive of all skin tones. Now, it simply refers to skin color in general.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6861', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%8C%E8%89%B2', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:13:24.671765Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.740398Z'),
(6862, 'vocabulary', '小舟', '小舟', 'A <kanji>small</kanji> <kanji>boat</kanji> is a <vocabulary>boat</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>small craft</vocabulary>. If something is a "big boat" it''s better to call it a "ship" I think, which is why this is just a <vocabulary>boat</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6862', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E8%88%9F', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:13:30.093801Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.752703Z'),
(6863, 'vocabulary', '舟', '舟', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Note that this word isn''t usually used by itself (that would be 船). It''s usually attached to things that are boat-related, but I wanted you to learn it individually so you can use it that way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6863', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%9F', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:13:35.791548Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.76418Z'),
(6864, 'vocabulary', '鐘', '鐘', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

鐘 usually refers to larger, open-ended <vocabulary>bell</vocabulary>s of the sort you might find in a church''s bell tower or a Buddhist temple.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6864', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%90%98', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:13:41.630658Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:22.878389Z'),
(6865, 'vocabulary', '警鐘', '警鐘', 'A <kanji>warn</kanji> <kanji>bell</kanji> is a bell that goes off when you need to be warned about something. This is usually an <vocabulary>alarm bell</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>fire bell</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6865', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%A6%E9%90%98', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:13:46.814625Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.789501Z'),
(6866, 'vocabulary', '凶器', '凶器', 'A <kanji>villain</kanji> <kanji>vessel</kanji> is a vessel used by a villain. This vessel holds bullets, and is a <vocabulary>dangerous weapon</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6866', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%B6%E5%99%A8', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:13:54.137004Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.801367Z'),
(6867, 'vocabulary', '元凶', '元凶', 'The <kanji>origin</kanji> <kanji>villain</kanji> is the villain who''s running everything. They''re the mastermind behind the villainy going on. They''re the <vocabulary>ringleader</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>main culprit</vocabulary>.

元凶 isn''t just limited to people, so it can often refer to the <vocabulary>root cause</vocabulary> of a problem too, like 汚染の元凶 (the main cause of pollution). It carries a serious, negative tone and is mostly used in writing about crimes, scandals, or other major issues.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6867', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%83%E5%87%B6', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:14:00.652068Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.772555Z'),
(6868, 'vocabulary', '凶悪', '凶悪', 'A <kanji>villain</kanji> who is <kanji>bad</kanji> is the most <vocabulary>atrocious</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>fiendish</vocabulary> villain of them all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6868', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%B6%E6%82%AA', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:14:05.527954Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.825675Z'),
(6869, 'vocabulary', '狩り', '狩り', 'You have the noun version of <kanji>hunt</kanji>. That is <vocabulary>hunting</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6869', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%A9%E3%82%8A', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:14:11.623553Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.837677Z'),
(6870, 'vocabulary', '狩人', '狩人', 'A <kanji>hunt</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a person who hunts. A <vocabulary>hunter</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6870', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%A9%E4%BA%BA', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:14:18.331263Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(6871, 'vocabulary', '頃', '頃', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. When used as a suffix, it means <vocabulary>approximate time</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>around</vocabulary> (as in "around 3:00").', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6871', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%83', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:14:41.392126Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.872511Z'),
(6872, 'vocabulary', '先頃', '先頃', 'The <kanji>previous</kanji> <kanji>approximate</kanji> was the previous approximate time, which is <vocabulary>recently</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>the other day</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6872', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%88%E9%A0%83', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:14:47.51281Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:27:12.714404Z'),
(6873, 'vocabulary', '近頃', '近頃', 'The <kanji>near</kanji> <kanji>approximate</kanji> is the approximate time that was near to you. This period of time is the <vocabulary>lately</vocabulary> period of time. It''s <vocabulary>recently</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6873', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E9%A0%83', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:14:52.991426Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.898472Z'),
(6874, 'vocabulary', '年頃', '年頃', 'The <kanji>year</kanji> <kanji>approximate</kanji> is your <vocabulary>approximate age</vocabulary>. This is also an indirect way to refer to <vocabulary>adolescence</vocabulary>, and when people reach <vocabulary>marriageable age</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6874', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E9%A0%83', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:14:58.551815Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.922914Z'),
(6875, 'vocabulary', 'いつ頃', 'いつ頃', 'いつ is "when" and 頃 is "about." Combine those together and you have "<vocabulary>about when</vocabulary>?" or "<vocabulary>how soon</vocabulary>?"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6875', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%84%E3%81%A4%E9%A0%83', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:15:05.128175Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.935432Z'),
(6876, 'vocabulary', 'この頃', 'この頃', '<kanji>This</kanji> (この) <kanji>approximate</kanji> is this approximate time. It is <vocabulary>recently</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>nowadays</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>these days</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6876', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%93%E3%81%AE%E9%A0%83', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:15:17.17032Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.947472Z'),
(6877, 'vocabulary', '若い頃', '若い頃', 'The <kanji>young</kanji> <kanji>approximate</kanji> is the approximate age of when you were young. This, approximately, is <vocabulary>one''s youth</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>early life</vocabulary>.

若い頃 is often used when fondly remembering the past, like, "When I was younger, I used to…"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6877', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%A5%E3%81%84%E9%A0%83', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:15:23.263278Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T23:19:07.295853Z'),
(6878, 'vocabulary', '魂', '魂', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6878', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AD%82', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:15:35.100523Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.970236Z'),
(6879, 'vocabulary', '〜魂', '〜魂', 'When attached to a word, this refers to a ____ spirit. It''s  the same meaning as the kanji, too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6879', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E9%AD%82', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:15:42.935144Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.982116Z'),
(6880, 'vocabulary', '脚', '脚', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Wondering how 脚 and 足 differ? You might remember 足 is mostly for feet but can sometimes refer to legs. 脚 refers to the whole <vocabulary>leg</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>lower limb</vocabulary> (often including the foot). Basically, go with 足 for feet and 脚 for legs!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6880', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%9A', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:16:09.033724Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:39.996092Z'),
(6881, 'vocabulary', '脚本', '脚本', 'A <kanji>leg</kanji> <kanji>book</kanji> is just the legs of a book. Not a whole book. It only contains the parts that walk around and move, not so much the descriptions (things that don''t move). This kind of book is a <vocabulary>script</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>screenplay</vocabulary>. These "books" are focused on actions and things people say, which is why it''s just the legs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6881', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%9A%E6%9C%AC', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:16:15.214531Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.009934Z'),
(6882, 'vocabulary', '可也', '可也', 'It''s <kanji>possible</kanji> that it''s <kanji>considerably</kanji> considerable. It''s <vocabulary>considerably</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>fairly</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>quite</vocabulary>... something. Also please note that this is almost always written in kana and not kanji. But, we''re learning kanji here, so gosh darn it we''re going to learn some kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6882', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%AF%E4%B9%9F', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:16:20.71551Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.023148Z'),
(6883, 'vocabulary', '井戸', '井戸', 'A <kanji>well</kanji> <kanji>door</kanji> is just a <vocabulary>well</vocabulary>. It''s the door that goes into the well hole, making it a well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6883', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%95%E6%88%B8', 14, 1, '2013-07-03T18:16:30.855825Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.06057Z'),
(6884, 'vocabulary', '天井', '天井', 'The <kanji>heaven</kanji> <kanji>well</kanji> is the well that goes up into the heavens. This is above you, and it is a <vocabulary>ceiling</vocabulary>. Maybe the "well" part is referring to the chimney that goes up through the ceiling to the heavens when you look up in a super old house?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6884', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A9%E4%BA%95', 16, 1, '2013-07-03T18:16:37.62282Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.234619Z'),
(6885, 'vocabulary', '呪い', '呪い', 'Despite looking like an adjective this is a noun. It means the same thing as the kanji: <vocabulary>curse</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6885', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%AA%E3%81%84', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:16:49.362914Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.093628Z'),
(6886, 'vocabulary', '呪う', '呪う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>curse</kanji>, and the verb version is <vocabulary>to curse</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to put a curse on</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6886', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%AA%E3%81%86', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:16:54.622918Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.105273Z'),
(6887, 'vocabulary', '暦', '暦', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6887', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%A6', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:17:14.933528Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.117985Z'),
(6888, 'vocabulary', '旧暦', '旧暦', 'The <kanji>former</kanji> <kanji>calendar</kanji> is the calendar from before. It''s the <vocabulary>lunisolar calendar</vocabulary> and the <vocabulary>traditional Japanese calendar</vocabulary>. Certainly not the current calendar, anyways.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6888', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A7%E6%9A%A6', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:17:19.454486Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.130063Z'),
(6889, 'vocabulary', '西暦', '西暦', 'In the <kanji>west</kanji>, a <kanji>calendar</kanji> shows the year according to the <vocabulary>common era</vocabulary>, aka the <vocabulary>Christian era</vocabulary>. You know, like 2025 CE (or 2025 AD) — we''re 2000+ years into the common era.

This <vocabulary>Western calendar</vocabulary> differs from the traditional calendar of Japan, which names its eras for reigning emperors. The current Reiwa era followed the Heisei era, which came after the Showa era. Neat, right? On official forms in Japan, you can often choose whether to write your birth year in the Western or Japanese style.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6889', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%BF%E6%9A%A6', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:17:24.18565Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:19:07.778293Z'),
(6890, 'vocabulary', '曇る', '曇る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>cloudy</kanji> and the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to get cloudy</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to become cloudy</vocabulary>.

Aside from describing the weather, 曇る can also refer to other physical phenomena, like when your glasses or bathroom mirror gets fogged up. It can also be used figuratively, such as when someone''s expression clouds over (表情が曇る) due to a negative emotion.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6890', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%87%E3%82%8B', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:17:31.451089Z', NULL, '2025-10-23T04:10:26.818037Z'),
(6891, 'vocabulary', '曇り', '曇り', 'The noun version of <kanji>cloudy</kanji> is <vocabulary>cloudy weather</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>cloudiness</vocabulary>. In some cases, this can also refer to the type of <vocabulary>fog</vocabulary> you''d find on a window or your glasses.

Despite being a noun in Japanese, you''ll actually see this translated as the adjective "cloudy" in English a lot. Don''t let that fool you, though, it still behaves as a noun in Japanese! It''s just another example of word types not quite matching up between English and Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6891', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%87%E3%82%8A', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:17:36.78225Z', NULL, '2025-07-18T00:30:25.188993Z'),
(6892, 'vocabulary', '眺める', '眺める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>stare</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to stare at</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>to look at</vocabulary> in a very staringish way).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6892', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%BA%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:17:50.076222Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.185117Z'),
(6893, 'vocabulary', '眺望', '眺望', 'You <kanji>stare</kanji> at <kanji>hope</kanji>. It looks good. That is your <vocabulary>prospect</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>view</vocabulary>, as you stare at hope.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6893', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%BA%E6%9C%9B', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:17:56.871882Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.197953Z'),
(6894, 'vocabulary', '全裸', '全裸', 'You''re <kanji>all</kanji> <kanji>naked</kanji>, which makes you <vocabulary>completely naked</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>totally naked</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6894', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E8%A3%B8', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:18:01.535848Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:29:09.139112Z'),
(6895, 'vocabulary', '半裸', '半裸', 'If you''re <kanji>half</kanji> <kanji>naked</kanji> you''re <vocabulary>half nude</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>half naked</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6895', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%8A%E8%A3%B8', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:18:06.357241Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:29:09.139112Z'),
(6896, 'vocabulary', '裸', '裸', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6896', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%B8', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:18:11.651007Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:29:09.139112Z'),
(6897, 'vocabulary', '賭ける', '賭ける', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>gamble</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to gamble</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6897', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%AD%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:18:28.363777Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.24906Z'),
(6898, 'vocabulary', '賭け', '賭け', 'The noun version of <kanji>gamble</kanji> is <vocabulary>gambling</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>betting</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>a gamble</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>a bet</vocabulary>. Whatever you do, put it all on black 13. Trust me.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6898', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%AD%E3%81%91', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:18:36.679294Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.261721Z'),
(6899, 'vocabulary', '疲れる', '疲れる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>tired</kanji>, so the verb version means <vocabulary>to get tired</vocabulary>. 

疲れる refers to a physical or mental kind of tiredness, like you just worked out or studied a ton, but isn''t used to describe sleepiness.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6899', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%B2%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 13, 1, '2013-07-03T18:18:41.376398Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T00:42:57.229108Z'),
(6900, 'vocabulary', '疲労', '疲労', 'You''re <kanji>tired</kanji> from <kanji>labor</kanji>. From this, you''re showing <vocabulary>fatigue</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>weariness</vocabulary>.

疲労 is a little formal, so you might encounter it more often in writing than in conversation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6900', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%B2%E5%8A%B4', 12, 1, '2013-07-03T18:18:47.357201Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.306895Z'),
(6901, 'vocabulary', '塾', '塾', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6901', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%BE', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:18:52.574927Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.319819Z'),
(6902, 'vocabulary', '義塾', '義塾', 'A <kanji>righteousness</kanji> <kanji>cram school</kanji> is, for some reason, a <vocabulary>private school</vocabulary>. Maybe because they don''t think the public cram schools are any good, so they take their righteousness somewhere else to start a private one? I''m not sure.

You may have seen the abbreviation 私学 (for 私立学校) as another word for private school. 私学 is typically used for any schools, elementary through university, that are run by individuals or private groups.

義塾 instead generally describes private schools founded during the Edo and Meiji periods. In contemporary Japanese, it''s not really used on its own but can be seen in school names like 明徳義塾高等学校 (Meitoku Gijuku High School).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6902', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%A9%E5%A1%BE', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:19:01.806932Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:46:56.671656Z'),
(6903, 'vocabulary', '塾生', '塾生', 'Remember how 学生 is "student"? This plays off that. A <kanji>cram school</kanji> "student" is a student of a cram school, a <vocabulary>cram school student</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6903', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%BE%E7%94%9F', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:19:07.427279Z', NULL, '2025-11-09T20:00:24.078053Z'),
(6904, 'vocabulary', '電卓', '電卓', 'An <kanji>electricity</kanji> <kanji>table</kanji> is a <vocabulary>calculator</vocabulary>. Why is this? You can think about it in a couple of different ways. Maybe in the past, a calculator (aka abacus) would take up a whole table, making this "electric" version a calculator. Or, maybe it can be used to solve tables. Wrong kind of table, but should help you to remember the meaning of this word.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6904', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E5%8D%93', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:19:13.049985Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.030484Z'),
(6905, 'vocabulary', '食卓', '食卓', 'An <kanji>eat</kanji>ing <kanji>table</kanji> is a <vocabulary>dining table</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6905', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9F%E5%8D%93', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:19:19.552403Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.368007Z'),
(6906, 'vocabulary', '卓', '卓', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6906', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%93', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:19:26.920771Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.380759Z'),
(6907, 'vocabulary', '卓球', '卓球', '<kanji>Table</kanji> <kanji>sphere</kanji> is a sport where you have a table and then you use a sphere on it. That''s <vocabulary>table tennis</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>ping pong</vocabulary>. Makes sense, right? You use a ping pong ball and you play on a table.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6907', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%93%E7%90%83', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:19:33.800188Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.392954Z'),
(6908, 'vocabulary', '磨く', '磨く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>polish</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to polish</vocabulary> or, strangely specifically (and we''ll learn an even more specific word for this later) <vocabulary>to brush your teeth</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6908', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A3%A8%E3%81%8F', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:19:39.74999Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.404078Z'),
(6909, 'vocabulary', '磨き', '磨き', 'A <kanji>polish</kanji> is a <vocabulary>polish</vocabulary> or even a <vocabulary>shine</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6909', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A3%A8%E3%81%8D', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:19:46.091632Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.415196Z'),
(6910, 'vocabulary', '細菌', '細菌', 'A <kanji>thin</kanji> <kanji>bacteria</kanji> is... well... a <vocabulary>bacteria</vocabulary>. I guess all bacteria are really thin. So thin you can''t see them with your naked eye, in fact.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6910', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%B0%E8%8F%8C', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:20:00.575542Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:47:55.647024Z'),
(6911, 'vocabulary', 'ばい菌', 'ばい菌', 'A "ばい" <kanji>bacteria</kanji> is a <vocabulary>germ</vocabulary>. In case you''re wondering, the ばい is referring to the kanji 黴, but people don''t usually write that part.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6911', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%B0%E3%81%84%E8%8F%8C', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:20:05.840455Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.439479Z'),
(6912, 'vocabulary', '無菌', '無菌', 'Someplace with <kanji>nothing</kanji> amounts of <kanji>bacteria</kanji> is <vocabulary>sterilized</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>aseptic</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6912', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E8%8F%8C', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:20:12.305143Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.452741Z'),
(6913, 'vocabulary', '殺菌', '殺菌', 'If you <kanji>kill</kanji> <kanji>bacteria</kanji> you are performing <vocabulary>sterilization</vocabulary>. You''re killing those bacteria.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6913', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%BA%E8%8F%8C', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:20:18.292369Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.466791Z'),
(6914, 'vocabulary', '陰', '陰', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Also means <vocabulary>shadow</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6914', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%B0', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:20:33.737511Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.480906Z'),
(6915, 'vocabulary', '陰気', '陰気', 'A <kanji>shade</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji> is a shady feeling. It''s a feeling of <vocabulary>gloom</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>melancholy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6915', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%B0%E6%B0%97', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:20:41.415505Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(6916, 'vocabulary', '悪霊', '悪霊', 'An <kanji>evil</kanji> <kanji>ghost</kanji> isn''t so much a ghost as it is an <vocabulary>evil spirit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6916', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E9%9C%8A', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:21:04.071748Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.505964Z'),
(6917, 'vocabulary', '霊感', '霊感', 'A <kanji>ghost</kanji> <kanji>feeling</kanji> is <vocabulary>inspiration</vocabulary>. For some reason, the feeling of ghosts is the feeling of inspiration. I guess a ghost goes inside you and makes you feel this inspiration? Oh, wait, you can feel that because you have the <vocabulary>sixth sense</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6917', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%8A%E6%84%9F', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:21:11.35393Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(6918, 'vocabulary', '亡霊', '亡霊', 'A <kanji>deceased</kanji> <kanji>ghost</kanji> is a <vocabulary>ghost</vocabulary> (all ghosts are deceased). Can also refer to "<vocabulary>the dead</vocabulary>."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6918', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%A1%E9%9C%8A', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:21:18.788714Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.530001Z'),
(6919, 'vocabulary', '霊園', '霊園', 'A <kanji>ghost</kanji> <kanji>garden</kanji> is where many ghosts are harvested and where many ghosts grow. This is a <vocabulary>cemetery</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6919', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%8A%E5%9C%92', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:21:25.116606Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.543141Z'),
(6920, 'vocabulary', '加湿器', '加湿器', 'An "<kanji>add</kanji> <kanji>damp</kanji>ness <kanji>container</kanji>" is a container that adds dampness to the air. That''s a <vocabulary>humidifier</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6920', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%A0%E6%B9%BF%E5%99%A8', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:21:33.085123Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:15:08.580781Z'),
(6921, 'vocabulary', '湿地', '湿地', 'The <kanji>damp</kanji> <kanji>earth</kanji> is where water seems to congregate, but not so much that there''s a lake or ocean or anything like that. Just damp. This is a <vocabulary>wetlands</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6921', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B9%BF%E5%9C%B0', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:21:44.053043Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.566594Z'),
(6922, 'vocabulary', '湿気', '湿気', 'A <kanji>damp</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji> is a feeling of dampness. It is <vocabulary>moisture</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>humidity</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6922', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B9%BF%E6%B0%97', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:21:51.557114Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(6923, 'vocabulary', '湿る', '湿る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>damp</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be damp</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be moist</vocabulary>. Ewww.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6923', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B9%BF%E3%82%8B', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:21:58.519695Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.589665Z'),
(6924, 'vocabulary', '嫁', '嫁', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6924', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AB%81', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:22:05.656978Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:42:49.765512Z'),
(6925, 'vocabulary', '嫁ぐ', '嫁ぐ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>bride</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to marry</vocabulary>, because you can''t really "to bride" someone.

嫁ぐ is often used when describing how someone has or is going <vocabulary>to marry into</vocabulary> another family.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6925', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AB%81%E3%81%90', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:22:12.717555Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.612858Z'),
(6926, 'vocabulary', '溝', '溝', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

溝 is commonly used for physical gaps like drainage ditches, small trenches, or grooves in surfaces. It can also describe metaphorical gaps, like emotional or relational divides between people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6926', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%9D', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:22:19.777612Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.625304Z'),
(6927, 'vocabulary', '海溝', '海溝', 'An <kanji>ocean</kanji> <kanji>gutter</kanji> is an <vocabulary>ocean trench</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6927', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B7%E6%BA%9D', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:22:26.800698Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.63868Z'),
(6928, 'vocabulary', '排水溝', '排水溝', 'You already know that <kanji>emit</kanji>ted <kanji>water</kanji> means "drainage." Add <kanji>gutter</kanji> to that and this becomes a <vocabulary>drainage</vocabulary> in the sense of a <vocabulary>drainage ditch</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>gutter</vocabulary>.

When it comes to the drain in your sink or bathtub, you can use 排水口, which has the same pronunciation but a different kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6928', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%92%E6%B0%B4%E6%BA%9D', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:22:56.034221Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.664977Z'),
(6929, 'vocabulary', '滝', '滝', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6929', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BB%9D', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:23:02.660446Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.678848Z'),
(6930, 'vocabulary', '滝川', '滝川', 'A <kanji>waterfall</kanji> <kanji>river</kanji> is a river that''s moving like a waterfall! Fast and furious! Water that moves like this are <vocabulary>rapids</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6930', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BB%9D%E5%B7%9D', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:23:12.482481Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.69044Z'),
(6931, 'vocabulary', '狂う', '狂う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>lunatic</kanji> or <kanji>crazy</kanji>. The verb version of this is <vocabulary>to go mad</vocabulary> (you don''t really "go lunatic" now, do you?) or <vocabulary>to go crazy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6931', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%82%E3%81%86', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:23:29.451584Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.701826Z'),
(6932, 'vocabulary', '狂気', '狂気', '<kanji>Lunatic</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji> is <vocabulary>madness</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>insanity</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6932', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%82%E6%B0%97', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:23:36.790114Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(6933, 'vocabulary', '翔る', '翔る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji means <kanji>fly</kanji>, so the verb version means <vocabulary>to fly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6933', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BF%94%E3%82%8B', 45, 1, '2013-07-03T18:23:45.299836Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.726661Z'),
(6934, 'vocabulary', '親孝行', '親孝行', 'You have the kanji for <kanji>parent</kanji> combined with 孝行, which you know means "filial piety." If you show filial piety toward your parents… well, it''s still <vocabulary>filial piety</vocabulary>. Now it''s just extra clear who you''re showing that filial devotion to! 

While it may feel kind of redundant, 親孝行 tends to be a little more common than 孝行. It''s nice to add "parent" for extra love points, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6934', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA%E5%AD%9D%E8%A1%8C', 42, 1, '2013-07-08T23:39:29.222885Z', NULL, '2025-11-10T19:15:19.656201Z'),
(6935, 'vocabulary', '見逃す', '見逃す', '逃す is "to let go" or "to let escape." If something escapes your <kanji>see</kanji>ing, then you are going <vocabulary>to overlook</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6935', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E9%80%83%E3%81%99', 44, 1, '2013-07-11T22:37:06.495957Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.75344Z'),
(6936, 'vocabulary', '吹き出す', '吹き出す', 'You know 吹く is "to blow" and 出す is "to let out." So what does it mean to let out a blow? It means <vocabulary>to begin to blow</vocabulary>! For something to begin blowing — the wind, for example — first it needs to let out an initial blow, right?

This word also means <vocabulary>to spout out</vocabulary> — whether it''s water spouting out of a broken pipe or someone speaking up suddenly — and can even mean <vocabulary>to burst out laughing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6936', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%B9%E3%81%8D%E5%87%BA%E3%81%99', 42, 1, '2013-07-12T17:18:07.020347Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:36:55.726234Z'),
(6937, 'vocabulary', '吹き込む', '吹き込む', 'You know that 吹く is "to blow" and the kanji 込 means <kanji>into</kanji>. So this word means <vocabulary>to blow in</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to breathe into</vocabulary>, and it can be used both transitively and intransitively.

It can also be used figuratively to mean to breathe into or<vocabulary>to imbue</vocabulary>, like breathing new life into something, or imbuing someone’s mind with hatred.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6937', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%B9%E3%81%8D%E8%BE%BC%E3%82%80', 42, 1, '2013-07-12T17:19:48.277618Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.778284Z'),
(6938, 'vocabulary', '口笛を吹く', '口笛を吹く', 'You know that 口笛 is "whistling." So, if you''re <kanji>blow</kanji>ing your whistle you''re going <vocabulary>to whistle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6938', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%A3%E7%AC%9B%E3%82%92%E5%90%B9%E3%81%8F', 42, 1, '2013-07-12T17:20:57.522812Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:20:17.248906Z'),
(6939, 'vocabulary', '薄める', '薄める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>dilute</kanji> and the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to dilute</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to water down</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6939', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%84%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-12T17:33:02.288624Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.80267Z'),
(6940, 'vocabulary', '逃れる', '逃れる', 'You''ve seen 逃げる and 逃す. This one is 逃れる. This one simply means <vocabulary>to escape</vocabulary>. So that''s not too bad. The difference between this one and the other escapes is that it has to do more with luck.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6940', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%83%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2013-07-12T17:34:17.533032Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.814842Z'),
(6941, 'vocabulary', '取り逃がす', '取り逃がす', 'The thing you want to <kanji>take</kanji> has <kanji>escape</kanji>d. Meaning you failed to catch this thing. This word is <vocabulary>to fail to catch</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6941', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8A%E9%80%83%E3%81%8C%E3%81%99', 44, 1, '2013-07-12T17:36:02.834673Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.826549Z'),
(6942, 'vocabulary', '吹き飛ばす', '吹き飛ばす', 'You know that 吹く is "to blow" and that 飛ばす is "To let fly." So, if you use your blowing to let something fly, you are going <vocabulary>to blow away</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6942', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%B9%E3%81%8D%E9%A3%9B%E3%81%B0%E3%81%99', 42, 1, '2013-07-15T15:22:30.496143Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.838539Z'),
(6943, 'vocabulary', '伺い', '伺い', 'If 伺う is "to inquire" or "to visit" then the noun version would be a polite way to say <vocabulary>inquiry</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>enquiry</vocabulary>. In other words, a <vocabulary>question</vocabulary>. It can also mean a <vocabulary>visit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6943', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%BA%E3%81%84', 43, 1, '2013-07-16T17:44:38.588893Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.850513Z'),
(6944, 'vocabulary', '群がる', '群がる', 'Remember 群れる, which means "to flock" or "to crowd."? This also means that!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6944', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%A4%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 43, 1, '2013-07-18T01:11:39.060142Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.86343Z'),
(6945, 'vocabulary', '潜る', '潜る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>conceal</kanji>, but as a verb, this word means <vocabulary>to go under</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to pass through</vocabulary>. Most commonly, this word means <vocabulary>to dive underwater</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to go underground</vocabulary>. Can you see why the kanji for conceal kind of makes sense in these cases? If you''re underwater or underground, no one can see you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6945', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%9C%E3%82%8B', 43, 1, '2013-07-18T18:58:08.33779Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:41:52.065519Z'),
(6946, 'vocabulary', '合わせる', '合わせる', 'You know that the alternative kanji meaning for 合 is <kanji>join</kanji>, which is what we''re using here. The verb form of join is <vocabulary>to join together</vocabulary>, and it can also mean <vocabulary>to unify</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to match</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6946', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%88%E3%82%8F%E3%81%9B%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2013-07-25T19:43:59.985369Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.91246Z'),
(6947, 'vocabulary', '寝床', '寝床', 'A <kanji>sleep</kanji> <kanji>bed</kanji> is where you sleep. Where do you sleep? In a <vocabulary>bed</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>crib</vocabulary>. Side note, if you''re a baby sleeping in a crib, you must really want to learn kanji. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6947', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%9D%E5%BA%8A', 44, 1, '2013-07-25T22:14:44.182971Z', NULL, '2025-06-25T14:00:39.611736Z'),
(6948, 'vocabulary', '手堅い', '手堅い', 'You know that 堅い is "solid" or "tough." When your <kanji>hand</kanji> is <kanji>solid</kanji> and tough, it''s <vocabulary>solid</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>steady</vocabulary>. If your hand is shaky, it''s the opposite, or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6948', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E5%A0%85%E3%81%84', 44, 1, '2013-07-26T18:29:45.337183Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:40.939139Z'),
(6949, 'vocabulary', '荒らす', '荒らす', '荒れる is "to be stormy" or "to be rough." 荒らす is when you do the stormy rough things. You go wild on everyone, trying <vocabulary>to lay waste</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to mess up</vocabulary> everything around you.

荒らす is all about creating a mess. It can be physical destruction, like a wild pig tearing up a garden or a robber ransacking and messing up a house. Or it can refer to disrupting a peaceful situation, such as trolling an online forum or chat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6949', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8D%92%E3%82%89%E3%81%99', 44, 1, '2013-07-26T19:26:06.80572Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:04:12.341392Z'),
(6950, 'vocabulary', '荒々しい', '荒々しい', '荒い is <kanji>rough</kanji>. Double that to 荒々しい and you have the same thing: <vocabulary>wild</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>rough</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6950', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8D%92%E3%80%85%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 44, 1, '2013-07-26T19:55:19.345202Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(6951, 'vocabulary', '悪趣味', '悪趣味', 'Remember how 趣味 means "hobby"? Well, if you have <kanji>bad</kanji> hobbies, it probably means you have <vocabulary>bad taste</vocabulary>. 

悪趣味 is often used to describe something that lacks sophistication or sensitivity, like tacky shoes and offensive jokes, as well as someone with that kind of taste.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6951', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E8%B6%A3%E5%91%B3', 45, 1, '2013-07-29T20:41:26.232093Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:05:11.752165Z'),
(6952, 'vocabulary', '包み紙', '包み紙', 'A 包み is a <kanji>bundle</kanji> or <kanji>package</kanji>, and 紙 is <kanji>paper</kanji>. What paper goes around bundles and packages? That would be <vocabulary>wrapping paper</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6952', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%85%E3%81%BF%E7%B4%99', 45, 1, '2013-07-29T21:45:24.577938Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.027594Z'),
(6953, 'vocabulary', '潮時', '潮時', 'The <kanji>tide</kanji> <kanji>time</kanji> is the <vocabulary>tidal hour</vocabulary>.

This word is often used figuratively, as in “the perfect time to act”, especially in reference to quitting something or backing away from a situation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6953', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%AE%E6%99%82', 45, 1, '2013-07-29T22:13:15.818664Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.053295Z'),
(6954, 'vocabulary', '黒潮', '黒潮', 'The <kanji>black</kanji> <kanji>tide</kanji> isn''t what you think, most likely. This is the <vocabulary>Japan current</vocabulary>, aka the <vocabulary>Kuroshio Current</vocabulary>. It''s a Northern flowing  current on the West side of the Pacific Ocean. Have you heard of the Gulf Stream? It''s kind of like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6954', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BB%92%E6%BD%AE', 45, 1, '2013-07-30T20:04:19.025145Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.068066Z'),
(6955, 'vocabulary', '擦る', '擦る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>rub</kanji> and this verb means <vocabulary>to rub</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to scrub</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6955', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%93%A6%E3%82%8B', 45, 1, '2013-07-31T19:25:38.459758Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.083144Z'),
(6956, 'vocabulary', '小包', '小包', 'A <kanji>small</kanji> <kanji>bundle</kanji> is merely a <vocabulary>package</vocabulary>. Something big would be a big package, I guess.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6956', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E5%8C%85', 45, 1, '2013-07-31T21:49:22.462987Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.098178Z'),
(6957, 'vocabulary', '素粒子', '素粒子', 'A <kanji>particle</kanji> (粒子) that is the <kanji>element</kanji> version of it is an <vocabulary>elementary particle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6957', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%A0%E7%B2%92%E5%AD%90', 46, 1, '2013-08-01T18:17:38.430122Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:38:20.342554Z'),
(6958, 'vocabulary', 'お握り', 'お握り', '握り is a "grip." When you add お it''s elevated a bit. For some reason it becomes a <vocabulary>rice ball</vocabulary> (aka <vocabulary>onigiri</vocabulary>). To make a rice ball, you use your grip to make them into a ball shape, which is probably where it comes from.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6958', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E6%8F%A1%E3%82%8A', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:03:51.890844Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.124474Z'),
(6959, 'vocabulary', '揚げ出し', '揚げ出し', 'Remember how 揚げる can mean "to deep fry"? 揚げ出し is a Japanese cooking technique where you deep fry something (usually tofu) and serve it in broth. This word might be easy to remember if you know that "dashi" soup stock is often used to make the agedashi broth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6959', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%9A%E3%81%92%E5%87%BA%E3%81%97', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:05:05.47101Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:42:49.398284Z'),
(6960, 'vocabulary', '叫び', '叫び', '叫ぶ is "to shout" and "to scream." This is the noun version. This is a <vocabulary>shout</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>scream</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6960', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%AB%E3%81%B3', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:06:27.758327Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.16142Z'),
(6961, 'vocabulary', '叫び声', '叫び声', 'A <kanji>shout</kanji> <kanji>voice</kanji> is a <vocabulary>shout</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>yell</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6961', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%AB%E3%81%B3%E5%A3%B0', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:06:41.707698Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.173555Z'),
(6962, 'vocabulary', '新芽', '新芽', 'A <kanji>new</kanji> <kanji>sprout</kanji> is a <vocabulary>sprout</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>bud</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>shoot</vocabulary>. All sprouts are new, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6962', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E8%8A%BD', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:07:53.209499Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.187027Z'),
(6963, 'vocabulary', '焦り', '焦り', 'Remember 焦る, which means "to be in a hurry" or "to be impatient"? This is the noun version, which is <vocabulary>impatience</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6963', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A6%E3%82%8A', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:08:55.333286Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.199395Z'),
(6964, 'vocabulary', '墨', '墨', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6964', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%A8', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:09:08.192355Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.212654Z'),
(6965, 'vocabulary', '墨絵', '墨絵', 'An <kanji>ink</kanji> <kanji>painting</kanji> is an <vocabulary>ink painting</vocabulary>.

墨絵 refers to artwork created in monochrome, using black sumi ink with a brush on handmade paper.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6965', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%A8%E7%B5%B5', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:09:22.175407Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:12:48.398208Z'),
(6966, 'vocabulary', '入れ墨', '入れ墨', 'If you <kanji>enter</kanji> <kanji>ink</kanji> into (your body), you are getting yourself a <vocabulary>tattoo</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6966', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E3%82%8C%E5%A2%A8', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:09:25.467593Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.236405Z'),
(6967, 'vocabulary', '鳩', '鳩', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6967', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%B3%A9', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:09:35.360677Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.248544Z'),
(6968, 'vocabulary', '穏やか', '穏やか', 'This is a な adjective version of the kanji which means <kanji>calm</kanji>. The vocab version here is <vocabulary>calm</vocabulary> as well, though you can use it to mean <vocabulary>mild</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6968', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%8F%E3%82%84%E3%81%8B', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:09:49.459548Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.26065Z'),
(6969, 'vocabulary', '穏当', '穏当', 'Everything is <kanji>calm</kanji> and <kanji>right</kanji>. It is <vocabulary>proper</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>reasonable</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6969', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%8F%E5%BD%93', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:10:00.789399Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:38:04.319615Z'),
(6970, 'vocabulary', '平穏', '平穏', 'A <kanji>flat</kanji> <kanji>calm</kanji> is a <vocabulary>calm</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>tranquil</vocabulary> place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6970', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B3%E7%A9%8F', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:10:05.73384Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.283943Z'),
(6971, 'vocabulary', '不穏', '不穏', 'Something that''s <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>calm</kanji> is in a state of <vocabulary>unrest</vocabulary>. When things are <vocabulary>unsettled</vocabulary>, it''s <vocabulary>disquieting</vocabulary> for people.

不穏 is generally used to describe the unsettling mood or atmosphere of a situation, highlighting tension and unease rather than describing something physical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6971', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E7%A9%8F', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:10:10.777906Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.295475Z'),
(6972, 'vocabulary', '鈍い', '鈍い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>dull</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>dull</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6972', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%88%8D%E3%81%84', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:10:24.255757Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.307549Z'),
(6973, 'vocabulary', '鈍感', '鈍感', 'If you’re too <kanji>dull</kanji> to have <kanji>feeling</kanji>, you’re <vocabulary>insensitive</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>thickheaded</vocabulary>.

鈍感 often means someone''s not very tuned in to emotions or what''s happening around them. It''s like they can miss subtle romantic hints or not sense the silent tension in a room.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6973', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%88%8D%E6%84%9F', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:10:24.444157Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(6974, 'vocabulary', '鈍器', '鈍器', 'You''re using a <kanji>dull</kanji> <kanji>container</kanji> as a <vocabulary>blunt weapon</vocabulary>. It''s all you have to hand.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6974', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%88%8D%E5%99%A8', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:10:30.066055Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.329577Z'),
(6975, 'vocabulary', '悪魔', '悪魔', 'A <kanji>bad</kanji> <kanji>devil</kanji> is a <vocabulary>devil</vocabulary> (because they''re all bad), or a <vocabulary>demon</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>satan</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6975', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E9%AD%94', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:10:34.742496Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.340294Z'),
(6976, 'vocabulary', '魔法', '魔法', 'The <kanji>devil</kanji>''s <kanji>method</kanji> is <vocabulary>magic</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>witchcraft</vocabulary>. That''s their method of doing things, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6976', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AD%94%E6%B3%95', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:10:42.501667Z', NULL, '2025-12-23T02:16:59.545242Z'),
(6977, 'vocabulary', '魔術', '魔術', 'The <kanji>devil</kanji>''s <kanji>technique</kanji> is <vocabulary>sorcery</vocabulary>. The kind of thing that devils do.

魔術 is a word that refers to <vocabulary>magic arts</vocabulary>, with emphasis on the "craft" of magic. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6977', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AD%94%E8%A1%93', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:10:44.708572Z', NULL, '2025-10-07T00:11:43.883554Z'),
(6978, 'vocabulary', '魔女', '魔女', 'A <kanji>devil</kanji> <kanji>woman</kanji> is a <vocabulary>witch</vocabulary>. She uses magic, so she must be a witch! Also, does she float like wood / a very small rock?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6978', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AD%94%E5%A5%B3', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:10:49.45806Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.374241Z'),
(6979, 'vocabulary', '寮', '寮', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Can also refer to a <vocabulary>hostel</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6979', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%AE', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:10:56.32364Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.386788Z'),
(6980, 'vocabulary', '寮生', '寮生', 'A <kanji>dormitory</kanji> <kanji>student</kanji> (comes from 学生) is a <vocabulary>boarding student</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>boarder</vocabulary>. This is a student who lives in a dormitory.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6980', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%AE%E7%94%9F', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:11:15.02675Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.400587Z'),
(6981, 'vocabulary', 'お盆', 'お盆', 'The kanji 盆 means <kanji>tray</kanji> or <kanji>ancestral festival</kanji>. The お just adds politeness or respect. So お盆 also means <vocabulary>tray</vocabulary>, or refers to the <vocabulary>ancestral festival</vocabulary> also known as Obon.

Usually, the context will tell you which meaning for お盆 is being used, but you can also distinguish between them by the pitch accent. お盆 for "tray" uses a Low-High-High accent (like our female audio), and お盆 for "ancestral festival" uses a Low-High-Low accent (like our male audio).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6981', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E7%9B%86', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:11:22.816245Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.351032Z'),
(6982, 'vocabulary', '寧ろ', '寧ろ', 'This is the same as the kanji, <vocabulary>rather</vocabulary>. Can also mean <vocabulary>better</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>instead</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6982', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%A7%E3%82%8D', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:11:28.451115Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.423872Z'),
(6983, 'vocabulary', '丁寧', '丁寧', 'A <kanji>street</kanji> <kanji>rather</kanji> than a not-street. That''s what I''m offering you to walk on. It''s much nicer this way. I am being <vocabulary>polite</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>courteous</vocabulary> by offering you this street.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6983', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%81%E5%AF%A7', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:11:34.075529Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T01:07:41.222801Z'),
(6984, 'vocabulary', '病棟', '病棟', 'A <kanji>sick</kanji> <kanji>building</kanji> is a building (or part of a building) for sick people. In other words, a <vocabulary>hospital ward</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6984', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%85%E6%A3%9F', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:11:39.497623Z', NULL, '2025-07-03T18:10:24.04064Z'),
(6985, 'vocabulary', '吾輩', '吾輩', '<kanji>I</kanji>, my <kanji>comrade</kanji> (which is me), is <vocabulary>I</vocabulary>, it''s <vocabulary>myself</vocabulary>, it''s <vocabulary>me</vocabulary>. This is a weird way to say "I" and has a hint of arrogance to it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6985', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%BE%E8%BC%A9', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:11:54.352888Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.464137Z'),
(6986, 'vocabulary', '斬新', '斬新', 'Something that has <kanji>slice</kanji>d <kanji>new</kanji>ness into the world is <vocabulary>novel</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>original</vocabulary>. Just imagine the world as a place of things that are already done. If you want to get something <vocabulary>innovative</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>inventive</vocabulary> in there, you have to slice that oldness open to make space for the new.

斬新 is often used when you want to commend someone''s creativity, novelty, or forward-thinking. However, it can also be used as a polite way of saying something is unusual or unexpectedly different, without openly calling it strange.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6986', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%AC%E6%96%B0', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:11:59.209773Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T23:17:53.712001Z'),
(6987, 'vocabulary', '斬殺', '斬殺', 'A <kanji>slice</kanji> <kanji>kill</kanji> is a <vocabulary>killing with a sword</vocabulary>. You don''t want to hear this, if at all possible.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6987', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%AC%E6%AE%BA', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:12:04.401256Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.487277Z'),
(6988, 'vocabulary', '斬る', '斬る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>slice</kanji> and the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to cut down</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to slice off</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to lop off</vocabulary>. 

This word mainly refers to cutting people with a sword, if you haven''t figured it out yet.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6988', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%AC%E3%82%8B', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:12:09.851991Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.500608Z'),
(6989, 'vocabulary', '椅子', '椅子', 'A <kanji>chair</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> is for some reason just a <vocabulary>chair</vocabulary>. Not sure why it''s a child, but it is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6989', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A4%85%E5%AD%90', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:12:14.271382Z', NULL, '2025-11-05T12:30:32.490274Z'),
(6990, 'vocabulary', '歳月', '歳月', '<kanji>Year</kanji>s and <kanji>month</kanji>s show the passage of <vocabulary>time</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>years</vocabulary> gone by.

歳月 is a relatively formal word that''s often used in literature when reflecting on the passage of long periods of time — usually in a way that''s poetic, thoughtful, or emotional.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6990', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%B3%E6%9C%88', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:12:26.97349Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:59:54.048707Z'),
(6991, 'vocabulary', '〜歳', '〜歳', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This is put on the end of numbers to show how old someone is.

You already learned 〜才 (years old), and 〜歳 means the exact same thing. No trick questions here. The difference is that 歳 is a pretty complicated kanji, so 才 is the simplified, more casual option for easier readability. Basically, 才 is the jeans-and-t-shirt version, while 歳 shows up in a suit and tie when things get official.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6991', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%AD%B3', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:12:48.141388Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:49.255116Z'),
(6992, 'vocabulary', '二十歳', '二十歳', '<kanji>Two</kanji> times <kanji>ten</kanji> <kanji>years old</kanji> is <vocabulary>twenty years old</vocabulary>.

Since 〜歳 can be replaced with 〜才, 二十歳 can also be written 二十才. Either way, it still means you''re officially an adult in Japan… even if you don''t feel like one yet.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6992', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%81%E6%AD%B3', 48, 1, '2013-08-02T22:12:55.066692Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T07:19:45.802601Z'),
(6993, 'vocabulary', '万歳', '万歳', '<kanji>Ten thousand</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji>s! That''s a lot of time. This is the word for the turned-English Japanese phrase, <vocabulary>banzai</vocabulary>!

万歳 is a cheerful word used to shout "<vocabulary>hooray</vocabulary>!" when celebrating something good. Because it''s usually said with both arms raised, the action of <vocabulary>raising one''s arms</vocabulary> can also be referred to as 万歳. This is often used when talking to children, like when telling them to raise their arms to take off a shirt.

And since raising your hands can also mean "<vocabulary>I give up</vocabulary>" in Japan, 万歳 can sometimes mean that as well. So basically, it''s either "Yay, we did it!" or "That''s it, I''m done." It really covers all your bases!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6993', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%87%E6%AD%B3', 47, 1, '2013-08-02T22:13:00.772271Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T07:27:04.931821Z'),
(6994, 'vocabulary', '歳暮', '歳暮', 'Here, 歳 means <kanji>year</kanji> and 暮 comes from 暮れる (to end). So 歳暮 literally means "end of the year," but in modern Japanese, this almost always refers to a <vocabulary>year-end gift</vocabulary>, which is basically a way to say thanks before the year wraps up.

歳暮 is almost always used with the honorific お, as in お歳暮. It''s a formal gift, like nicely-wrapped boxed sweets or coffee sent to bosses, clients, or people who have helped you during the year.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6994', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%B3%E6%9A%AE', 50, 1, '2013-08-02T22:13:07.31544Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T07:07:24.321099Z'),
(6995, 'vocabulary', '歳入', '歳入', 'Every <kanji>year</kanji>, money <kanji>enter</kanji>s the government''s coffers. This is its <vocabulary>annual revenue</vocabulary>.

歳入 specifically refers to <vocabulary>annual government revenue</vocabulary>, rather than the revenue brought in by a company or an individual. It''s the money that a national or local government collects in a single fiscal year, also known as <vocabulary>fiscal-year revenue</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6995', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%B3%E5%85%A5', 49, 1, '2013-08-02T22:13:12.249994Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T07:13:52.929875Z'),
(6996, 'vocabulary', '涼しい', '涼しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>cool</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>cool</vocabulary>.

涼しい is used to describe a comfortable level of coolness, particularly one that''s pleasant and <vocabulary>refreshing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6996', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B6%BC%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 25, 1, '2013-08-02T22:13:17.742924Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.61281Z'),
(6997, 'vocabulary', '清涼', '清涼', 'A <kanji>pure</kanji> <kanji>cool</kanji> is <vocabulary>refreshing</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>cool</vocabulary>, isn''t it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6997', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%85%E6%B6%BC', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:13:21.989612Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.626663Z'),
(6998, 'vocabulary', '涼風', '涼風', 'A <kanji>cool</kanji> <kanji>wind</kanji> is a <vocabulary>cool breeze</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6998', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B6%BC%E9%A2%A8', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:13:26.718687Z', NULL, '2025-07-01T19:41:45.398894Z'),
(6999, 'vocabulary', '猿真似', '猿真似', 'If 真似 is <kanji>imitation</kanji> and if a <kanji>monkey</kanji> is doing said imitation, that''s summed up by the saying: <vocabulary>monkey see monkey do</vocabulary>.

This refers to when someone does a <vocabulary>mindless imitation</vocabulary> of others without any real thought, so is often used when criticizing people. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/6999', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8C%BF%E7%9C%9F%E4%BC%BC', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:13:32.034593Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T20:04:39.445204Z'),
(7000, 'vocabulary', '猿', '猿', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7000', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8C%BF', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:13:37.085759Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.664726Z')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;


碁 is the classic East Asian game of strategy played with black and white stones on a large grid board. It can be used on its own, but you''ll also often see it in compounds such as 碁石 (ごいし) or 碁会所 (ごかいしょ).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7008', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A2%81', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:15:32.599516Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:10:25.208634Z'),
(7009, 'vocabulary', '囲碁', '囲碁', 'When do you <kanji>surround</kanji> your enemy''s <kanji>go</kanji> pieces with your own? When you''re playing <vocabulary>go</vocabulary>!

碁 and 囲碁 are exactly the same thing, but 碁 sounds slightly more formal and 囲碁 is more common when talking about <vocabulary>the game of go</vocabulary> in everyday conversations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7009', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%B2%E7%A2%81', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:15:37.629375Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:10:47.684006Z'),
(7010, 'vocabulary', '碁盤', '碁盤', 'A <kanji>go</kanji> <kanji>tray</kanji> is a tray for playing the game of go. In other words, it''s a <vocabulary>go board</vocabulary>.

碁盤 refers to the gridded wooden game board where players place their go stones and engage in tiny, polite battles.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7010', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A2%81%E7%9B%A4', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:15:42.560454Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:11:09.830075Z'),
(7011, 'vocabulary', '碁会所', '碁会所', 'A <kanji>go</kanji> <kanji>meet</kanji>ing <kanji>place</kanji> is a place where people meet to play go. It''s a <vocabulary>go parlor</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>go playing parlor</vocabulary>.

碁会所 is a place where people gather to play go, chat, and quietly judge each other''s life choices based on their opening moves. There, you''ll find both seasoned veterans and hopeful beginners sharing the same tables… and occasionally the same sighs of regret.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7011', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A2%81%E4%BC%9A%E6%89%80', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:15:47.479273Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:11:34.718016Z'),
(7012, 'vocabulary', '租界', '租界', 'This <kanji>tariff</kanji> <kanji>world</kanji> you live in is a <vocabulary>foreign concession</vocabulary>. It''s a <vocabulary>foreign settlement</vocabulary> in another country where foreigners get to make up all the rules, so they''ve gone and slapped tariffs on everything to annoy the country''s native government.

租界 is most commonly used to refer to the foreign-controlled areas in China that had their own rules and law enforcement during the late 1800s and early 1900s. You''ll mostly see it in historical contexts, but it''s also sometimes used in fiction to describe foreign-controlled districts in imagined settings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7012', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%9F%E7%95%8C', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:15:52.882295Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:21:16.900712Z'),
(7013, 'vocabulary', '租税', '租税', 'A <kanji>tariff</kanji> <kanji>tax</kanji> is a form of <vocabulary>taxes</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>taxation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7013', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%9F%E7%A8%8E', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:15:59.423213Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:45:00.948456Z'),
(7014, 'vocabulary', '泡', '泡', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7014', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A1', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:17:08.092304Z', NULL, '2025-07-10T18:17:59.077902Z'),
(7015, 'vocabulary', '気泡', '気泡', '<kanji>Energy</kanji> <kanji>bubbles</kanji> are bubbles filled with the "energy" that''s all around us (air). That''s why this is an <vocabulary>air bubble</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7015', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E6%B3%A1', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:17:14.60928Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(7016, 'vocabulary', '水泡', '水泡', '<kanji>Water</kanji> <kanji>bubbles</kanji> are bubbles that come from the water. This is usually in the form of <vocabulary>foam</vocabulary>, but could also be a <vocabulary>bubble</vocabulary>.

水泡 is a literary word, mainly seen in the idiom 水泡に帰す (to come to nothing). This phrase paints a picture of your efforts going down the drain, turning into foam or bubbles in the water, and ultimately disappearing into nothingness.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7016', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E6%B3%A1', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:17:19.53293Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.851886Z'),
(7017, 'vocabulary', '発泡', '発泡', 'The <kanji>departure</kanji> of <kanji>bubbles</kanji> is when bubbles leave something, like a mouth. That''s why this is <vocabulary>foaming</vocabulary>.

発泡 is a technical word used for something that''s filled with air bubbles or produces foam. It''s often used in materials and product names, like 発泡スチロール (styrofoam), 発泡酒 (low-malt beer), or 発泡剤 (foaming agent).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7017', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BA%E6%B3%A1', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:17:31.016336Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:49:29.030631Z'),
(7018, 'vocabulary', '癖', '癖', 'The kanji itself means <kanji>habit</kanji> but it usually refers to a <vocabulary>bad habit</vocabulary>, so try to remember that meaning if you could.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7018', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%96', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:17:35.84617Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.874062Z'),
(7019, 'vocabulary', '幽閉', '幽閉', '<kanji>Secluded</kanji> and <kanji>closed</kanji> off, putting you in <vocabulary>confinement</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>imprisonment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7019', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BD%E9%96%89', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:18:27.069371Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.884747Z'),
(7020, 'vocabulary', '幽霊', '幽霊', 'A <kanji>secluded</kanji> <kanji>ghost</kanji> is someone who''s secluded and a ghost, because everyone''s forgotten about him (thus the "ghostness" of him). This thing is a <vocabulary>ghost</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>specter</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7020', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BD%E9%9C%8A', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:18:57.532051Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.895857Z'),
(7021, 'vocabulary', '口癖', '口癖', 'A <kanji>mouth</kanji> <kanji>habit</kanji> is something you say with your mouth a lot. It''s a habit. It''s your <vocabulary>favorite phrase</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>catchphrase</vocabulary>. Many people have the <vocabulary>habit of saying</vocabulary> the certain things over and over again, whether they want to or not.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7021', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%A3%E7%99%96', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:19:29.583952Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.907294Z'),
(7022, 'vocabulary', '鍛錬', '鍛錬', 'When you <kanji>forge</kanji> and <kanji>temper</kanji> yourself, you''re probably <vocabulary>training</vocabulary> your body or trying to <vocabulary>discipline</vocabulary> yourself to be physically and mentally tougher.

鍛錬 once described forging metal, but now it''s usually about strengthening yourself. Think about how you push yourself again and again, like a blacksmith pounding hot iron, to mold your mind, body, and abilities into something stronger!! That''s 鍛錬.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7022', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8D%9B%E9%8C%AC', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:19:35.400128Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T23:27:43.513887Z'),
(7023, 'vocabulary', '錬金術', '錬金術', '<kanji>Tempering</kanji> <kanji>gold</kanji> is a <kanji>technique</kanji> so magical that we can consider it to be <vocabulary>alchemy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7023', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8C%AC%E9%87%91%E8%A1%93', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:19:40.36265Z', NULL, '2025-06-12T17:19:07.793799Z'),
(7024, 'vocabulary', '鍛える', '鍛える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>forge</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to forge</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to temper</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7024', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8D%9B%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:19:47.14536Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.940712Z'),
(7025, 'vocabulary', '穂', '穂', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7025', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%82', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:19:52.224665Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:41.951668Z'),
(7026, 'vocabulary', '稲穂', '稲穂', 'A <kanji>head of plant</kanji> of a <kanji>rice plant</kanji> is the <vocabulary>head of rice</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>ear of rice</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7026', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%B2%E7%A9%82', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:19:57.904898Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:10:31.982273Z'),
(7027, 'vocabulary', '皇帝', '皇帝', 'The <kanji>sovereign</kanji> <kanji>emperor</kanji> of Japan is also the <vocabulary>emperor</vocabulary> of other places too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7027', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%87%E5%B8%9D', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:20:10.611474Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:40:37.858322Z'),
(7028, 'vocabulary', '帝', '帝', 'The one true <kanji>sovereign</kanji> is the <vocabulary>Japanese emperor</vocabulary>, a.k.a. the <vocabulary>emperor of Japan</vocabulary>. On its own, 帝 only refers to him, not to other emperors around the world.

帝 is a classical term for the emperor in Japan. It''s found in works of classical literature, historical novels, or period dramas that are set in feudal Japan. In modern Japanese, the Japanese emperor is called 天皇.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7028', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%9D', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:20:18.953873Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:40:37.858322Z'),
(7029, 'vocabulary', '帝国', '帝国', 'A <kanji>sovereign</kanji> <kanji>country</kanji> is one that is part of its own <vocabulary>empire</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7029', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%9D%E5%9B%BD', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:20:24.71428Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:40:37.858322Z'),
(7030, 'vocabulary', '帝政', '帝政', '<kanji>Sovereign</kanji> <kanji>government</kanji> and <kanji>Politics</kanji> are those who are part of the <vocabulary>imperial government</vocabulary>. This type of government is <vocabulary>imperialism</vocabulary>. Of course, back in the day there weren''t as many sovereign governments out there, so imperialism is the one that fits the bill in this word''s case.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7030', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%9D%E6%94%BF', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:20:35.231289Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:40:37.858322Z'),
(7031, 'vocabulary', '瞬く', '瞬く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>blink</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to blink</vocabulary>.

This word is generally used in the phrase 瞬く間に (in the blink of an eye).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7031', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9E%AC%E3%81%8F', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:20:42.535846Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.019707Z'),
(7032, 'vocabulary', '瞬間', '瞬間', 'A <kanji>blink</kanji> <kanji>interval of time</kanji> is a very short period of time. It''s <vocabulary>an instant</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>moment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7032', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9E%AC%E9%96%93', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:20:52.659376Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:38:48.299693Z'),
(7033, 'vocabulary', '瞬時', '瞬時', 'A <kanji>blink</kanji> of <kanji>time</kanji> is a mere <vocabulary>moment</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>instant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7033', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9E%AC%E6%99%82', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:21:07.001995Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.041895Z'),
(7034, 'vocabulary', '白菊', '白菊', 'A <kanji>white</kanji> <kanji>chrysanthemum</kanji> is a <vocabulary>white chrysanthemum</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7034', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BD%E8%8F%8A', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:21:17.123172Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.053575Z'),
(7035, 'vocabulary', '誇大', '誇大', 'Your <kanji>pride</kanji> is too <kanji>big</kanji>. You make it bigger than it is, in fact. You do this with <vocabulary>exaggeration</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>hyperbole</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7035', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%87%E5%A4%A7', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:21:22.594033Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(7036, 'vocabulary', '瞬き', '瞬き', 'A <kanji>blink</kanji> is a <vocabulary>blink</vocabulary> and a <vocabulary>wink</vocabulary> as well, depending on how many eyes you use.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7036', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9E%AC%E3%81%8D', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:21:34.40019Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.078837Z'),
(7037, 'vocabulary', '一瞬', '一瞬', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>blink</kanji> is one instant. That is <vocabulary>an instant</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>a moment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7037', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E7%9E%AC', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:21:54.386666Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:50:55.457813Z'),
(7038, 'vocabulary', '誇る', '誇る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>pride</kanji> and the verb version is <vocabulary>to be proud of</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to boast of</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7038', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%87%E3%82%8B', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:22:48.671971Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.104167Z'),
(7039, 'vocabulary', '誇張', '誇張', 'Take your <kanji>pride</kanji> and <kanji>stretch</kanji> it out. It''s an <vocabulary>exaggeration</vocabulary>, because you''ve stretched your pride out pretty thin.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7039', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%87%E5%BC%B5', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:22:57.544643Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.117195Z'),
(7040, 'vocabulary', '庄園', '庄園', 'A <kanji>manor</kanji> with a <kanji>park</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>manor</vocabulary> — it''s just a really big and impressive one.

庄園 refers to a medieval-style manor — a large <vocabulary>agricultural estate</vocabulary> granted to a lord by the monarch to be managed privately as a source of personal income. In Japan, 庄園 were an important part of the medieval economic system, with the aristocracy and the religious establishment controlling thousands of them around the country. Many famous samurai families took their names from 庄園, as they often managed them locally in place of absentee owners who preferred to hang out in Kyoto.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7040', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%84%E5%9C%92', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:23:04.566014Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:29:08.167217Z'),
(7041, 'vocabulary', '阻む', '阻む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>thwart</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to thwart</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to hamper</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7041', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%98%BB%E3%82%80', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:23:09.524366Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.140571Z'),
(7042, 'vocabulary', '阻止', '阻止', 'If you <kanji>thwart</kanji> and <kanji>stop</kanji> something, you are an <vocabulary>obstruction</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>hindrance</vocabulary>.

阻止 is a relatively formal word that means <vocabulary>preventing</vocabulary> something from happening, often by actively resisting rather than just stopping something passively.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7042', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%98%BB%E6%AD%A2', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:23:14.20444Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T16:47:32.28403Z'),
(7043, 'vocabulary', '阻害', '阻害', '<kanji>Thwart</kanji> and <kanji>damage</kanji> the advancement of something. That''s an <vocabulary>obstruction</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>inhibition</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7043', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%98%BB%E5%AE%B3', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:23:19.329038Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(7044, 'vocabulary', '黙る', '黙る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>shut up</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to shut up</vocabulary>.

黙る means <vocabulary>to be silent</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to stop talking</vocabulary>, with a sense of deliberately holding one''s tongue. It''s a neutral word to describe someone choosing not to speak, but certain forms — like 黙れ (shut up) — can sound very harsh.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7044', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BB%99%E3%82%8B', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:23:24.704053Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T20:13:06.846865Z'),
(7045, 'vocabulary', '沈黙', '沈黙', 'You <kanji>sink</kanji> down and <kanji>shut up</kanji>. You go into a <vocabulary>silence</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>hush</vocabulary>. This tends to be an awkward silence or hush.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7045', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%88%E9%BB%99', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:29:32.459932Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.183538Z'),
(7046, 'vocabulary', '黙殺', '黙殺', '<kanji>Shut up</kanji> and <kanji>kill</kanji> your talking or I''ll kill it for you. I''m not listening to this. I''m <vocabulary>ignoring</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7046', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BB%99%E6%AE%BA', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:29:36.762493Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.195173Z'),
(7047, 'vocabulary', '俵', '俵', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7047', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%B5', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:29:44.123421Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:23:03.044564Z'),
(7048, 'vocabulary', '土俵', '土俵', 'On the <kanji>soil</kanji> we put <kanji>straw sack</kanji>s in a circle to mark a <vocabulary>sumo ring</vocabulary>. Go look up some pictures of this word in Japanese and you''ll see exactly what we mean. Better yet, go see some live sumo!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7048', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%9F%E4%BF%B5', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:29:51.362088Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:28:52.254751Z'),
(7049, 'vocabulary', '綿', '綿', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7049', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%BF', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:30:05.516667Z', NULL, '2025-06-19T15:49:58.310723Z'),
(7050, 'vocabulary', '綿布', '綿布', 'A <kanji>cotton</kanji> <kanji>cloth</kanji> is a <vocabulary>cotton cloth</vocabulary> of <vocabulary>cotton material</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7050', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%BF%E5%B8%83', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:30:16.080574Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.256315Z'),
(7051, 'vocabulary', '架設', '架設', 'If you have a <kanji>shelf</kanji> to <kanji>establish</kanji> on your wall, you''ll need to do some <vocabulary>construction</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>installation</vocabulary> work. 

架設 is pretty formal. Use it specifically for the kind of construction or installation you do with bridges, cables, electric lines, pipelines, and other stuff that stretches like a "shelf" from one point to another.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7051', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%B6%E8%A8%AD', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:30:21.255612Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.269428Z'),
(7052, 'vocabulary', '書架', '書架', 'A <kanji>write</kanji> <kanji>shelf</kanji> is a shelf with writing on it. That''s a <vocabulary>bookshelf</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>bookcase</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7052', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B8%E6%9E%B6', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:30:27.870571Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.282791Z'),
(7053, 'vocabulary', '担架', '担架', 'It will <kanji>carry</kanji> or <kanji>bear</kanji> people like a <kanji>shelf</kanji>. This thing that carries people is a <vocabulary>stretcher</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>litter</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7053', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%85%E6%9E%B6', 46, 1, '2013-08-02T22:30:32.962377Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.296343Z'),
(7054, 'vocabulary', '眺め', '眺め', '眺める is "to stare at." The noun version is <vocabulary>view</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>scene</vocabulary>. It''s what you''re staring at.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7054', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%BA%E3%82%81', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:31:01.79352Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:42.986563Z'),
(7055, 'vocabulary', '疲れ', '疲れ', '疲れる is "to get tired." This is the noun version, which makes it <vocabulary>tiredness</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>fatigue</vocabulary>.

疲れ is a more casual version of 疲労, so people commonly choose 疲れ over 疲労 in everyday conversation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7055', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%B2%E3%82%8C', 13, 1, '2013-08-05T18:31:10.056398Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:42.33608Z'),
(7056, 'vocabulary', '湿らせる', '湿らせる', 'Remember how 湿る is "to be moist"? This is the version where you''re doing the moistening, making it <vocabulary>to moisten</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to dampen</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7056', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B9%BF%E3%82%89%E3%81%9B%E3%82%8B', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:33:31.589851Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T18:16:21.512684Z'),
(7057, 'vocabulary', '砕く', '砕く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>smash</kanji> and the verb vocab version of the kanji is <vocabulary>to smash</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to break</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7057', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%95%E3%81%8F', 48, 1, '2013-08-05T18:33:38.263209Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T23:21:30.232959Z'),
(7058, 'vocabulary', '砕石', '砕石', '<kanji>Smash</kanji>ed <kanji>stone</kanji> is smashed stone. It''s <vocabulary>rubble</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>broken stone</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7058', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%95%E7%9F%B3', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:33:45.316337Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T23:21:30.232959Z'),
(7059, 'vocabulary', '粉砕', '粉砕', 'To <kanji>powder</kanji> I will <kanji>smash</kanji> it! If I do that, it''s <vocabulary>pulverization</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>smashing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7059', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%89%E7%A0%95', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:33:50.977736Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T23:21:30.232959Z'),
(7060, 'vocabulary', '粘る', '粘る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>sticky</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be sticky</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>to persevere</vocabulary>, because if you''re being sticky towards the situation, not giving up and not letting go. You''re totally persevering by sticking around.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7060', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%98%E3%82%8B', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:33:56.689242Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.027272Z'),
(7061, 'vocabulary', '粘土', '粘土', '<kanji>Sticky</kanji> <kanji>earth</kanji> is <vocabulary>clay</vocabulary>. Just think about feeling the clay. It''s sticky especially when fresh and kind of wet, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7061', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%98%E5%9C%9F', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:34:09.214307Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.038257Z'),
(7062, 'vocabulary', '粘着', '粘着', 'If something <kanji>sticky</kanji> <kanji>wears</kanji> something else on it, it will stick together because there''s <vocabulary>adhesion</vocabulary> between them.

粘着 refers to the adhesive properties of a material, especially in compound words like 粘着テープ (adhesive tape). On its own, it often describes the behavior of people who can''t let go of a particular issue and keep on about it in unwanted ways.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7062', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%98%E7%9D%80', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:34:16.375697Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(7063, 'vocabulary', '化粧', '化粧', '<kanji>Change</kanji> your face with <kanji>cosmetics</kanji>! This is via <vocabulary>makeup</vocabulary> or, of course, <vocabulary>cosmetics</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7063', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%96%E7%B2%A7', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:34:22.648977Z', NULL, '2025-11-20T03:47:15.21122Z'),
(7064, 'vocabulary', '欺く', '欺く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>deceit</kanji>, so when you''re doing deceit, you are going <vocabulary>to deceive</vocabulary> someone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7064', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%BA%E3%81%8F', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:34:34.524918Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.075629Z'),
(7065, 'vocabulary', '詐欺', '詐欺', 'If there are <kanji>lie</kanji>s and <kanji>deceit</kanji> in your interactions with people, you''re committing <vocabulary>fraud</vocabulary>, you''re carrying out a <vocabulary>scam</vocabulary>, you''re <vocabulary>swindling</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7065', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%90%E6%AC%BA', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:34:40.738603Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.086372Z'),
(7066, 'vocabulary', '詐取', '詐取', 'You use your <kanji>lie</kanji> to <kanji>take</kanji> what you want. That''s when you <vocabulary>swindle</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>defraud</vocabulary> someone to get their stuff. How dare you!

詐取 refers to the act of fraudulently obtaining something, typically money or property, through deceptive practices. This term is formal and often used in legal contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7066', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%90%E5%8F%96', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:34:53.085821Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.102442Z'),
(7067, 'vocabulary', '詐称', '詐称', 'If you <kanji>lie</kanji> about your <kanji>title</kanji>, the title which represents who you are, then that is a <vocabulary>misrepresentation</vocabulary>. A <vocabulary>false statement</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7067', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%90%E7%A7%B0', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:34:58.587449Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.111816Z'),
(7068, 'vocabulary', '霧', '霧', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7068', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%A7', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:35:06.092386Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.121534Z'),
(7069, 'vocabulary', '黒い霧', '黒い霧', 'A <kanji>black</kanji> <kanji>fog</kanji> is going to be a really <vocabulary>thick fog</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>black veil</vocabulary> that covers suspicious acts or crimes. It''s not a literal cloud of fog or a physical veil, it''s what you hide your bad deeds in, metaphorically speaking.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7069', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BB%92%E3%81%84%E9%9C%A7', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:35:11.57854Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.131993Z'),
(7070, 'vocabulary', '朝霧', '朝霧', '<kanji>Morning</kanji> <kanji>fog</kanji> is <vocabulary>morning fog</vocabulary>, because it''s fog in the morning. This is the best kind of fog, I think.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7070', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%9D%E9%9C%A7', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:35:18.638036Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.14133Z'),
(7071, 'vocabulary', '柳', '柳', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7071', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%B3', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:35:25.782815Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.149959Z'),
(7072, 'vocabulary', '花柳', '花柳', 'The <kanji>flower</kanji> <kanji>willow</kanji> for some reason is a <vocabulary>red light district</vocabulary>. Just imagine a shady red light district, and right in the middle is a flowering willow.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7072', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B1%E6%9F%B3', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:35:32.480721Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:20:54.638732Z'),
(7073, 'vocabulary', '川柳', '川柳', 'A <kanji>river</kanji> of <kanji>willow</kanji>s... so hilarious! What a great <vocabulary>comic haiku</vocabulary>, that one is. Try to think of a comic haiku about the river willow, if you can.

The River Willow
Flows upstream like a salmon
This is not funny', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7073', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%9D%E6%9F%B3', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:35:37.559454Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.167799Z'),
(7074, 'vocabulary', '伊勢', '伊勢', 'The place with <kanji>Italy</kanji> <kanji>force</kanji> is a place known as <vocabulary>Ise</vocabulary>. It''s a region of Japan that has a nice peninsula, I think. Going straight to the reading for this one may be your best bet.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7074', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%8A%E5%8B%A2', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:35:50.216973Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.176459Z'),
(7075, 'vocabulary', '伊達', '伊達', 'What does <kanji>Italy</kanji> <kanji>attain</kanji> like no other? <vocabulary>Flamboyance</vocabulary>! They redefine <vocabulary>chic</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>stylishness</vocabulary>, dazzling everyone with their unabashed display.

This word isn’t just about fashion; it''s also for flamboyant, attention-grabbing actions. While it''s an old-fashioned word, you still find it in phrases like 伊達眼鏡 (fashion glasses) or 伊達に〜はしていない (not doing something for show).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7075', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%8A%E9%81%94', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:36:32.340904Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:06:12.518486Z'),
(7076, 'vocabulary', '補佐', '補佐', 'A <kanji>supplement</kanji> of <kanji>help</kanji> is a form of <vocabulary>assistance</vocabulary>.

補佐 is a formal word for the act of providing <vocabulary>support</vocabulary> to a leader or person in charge. It can also refer to an official or organizational role, like 部長補佐 (an <vocabulary>assistant</vocabulary> general manager).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7076', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%9C%E4%BD%90', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:36:41.717137Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:08:24.172997Z'),
(7077, 'vocabulary', '大佐', '大佐', 'A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>help</kanji> is a <vocabulary>colonel</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>navy captain</vocabulary>. They go around the seas, helping people in need by fighting the bad guys.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7077', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E4%BD%90', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:36:47.385416Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(7078, 'vocabulary', '少佐', '少佐', 'The <kanji>few</kanji> who <kanji>help</kanji> are the <vocabulary>major</vocabulary>s or <vocabulary>lieutenant commander</vocabulary>s. They are the few who step up to the plate to help out in the army, according to this word''s kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7078', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%91%E4%BD%90', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:37:01.730561Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:10:15.994971Z'),
(7079, 'vocabulary', '中佐', '中佐', 'The <kanji>middle</kanji> <kanji>help</kanji> in the army or navy is the <vocabulary>lieutenant colonel</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>navy commander</vocabulary>. I''m guessing these ranks are kind of in the middle or middle high end of the ranking scales?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7079', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E4%BD%90', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:37:06.856Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.226617Z'),
(7080, 'vocabulary', '尺', '尺', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7080', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%BA', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:37:13.843409Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.249286Z'),
(7081, 'vocabulary', '巻尺', '巻尺', 'A <kanji>scroll</kanji> of <kanji>shaku</kanji>. So, a scroll is all rolled up, right? And a shaku is an older form of Japanese measurement. If you roll up a measuring thing into a scroll-like container... what does it become? A <vocabulary>tape measure</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7081', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%BB%E5%B0%BA', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:37:18.97821Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.270729Z'),
(7082, 'vocabulary', '哀れ', '哀れ', 'If something is <kanji>pathetic</kanji> then you should have <vocabulary>pity</vocabulary> on it. You should also feel a little <vocabulary>sorrow</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>grief</vocabulary> for it, because seriously, so pathetic!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7082', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%93%80%E3%82%8C', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:37:26.693426Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.279422Z'),
(7083, 'vocabulary', '可哀想', '可哀想', 'A <kanji>possible</kanji> <kanji>pathetic</kanji> scenario you might <kanji>imagine</kanji> is probably not just possibly pathetic. It''s downright <vocabulary>pitiful</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>pathetic</vocabulary>, for sure.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7083', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%AF%E5%93%80%E6%83%B3', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:37:46.753043Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:12:31.527071Z'),
(7084, 'vocabulary', '悲哀', '悲哀', 'I am <kanji>sad</kanji> and <kanji>pathetic</kanji> in how I feel. Such a deep <vocabulary>sorrow</vocabulary>! Such <vocabulary>grief</vocabulary> from my sad pathetic feelings. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7084', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%B2%E5%93%80', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:37:55.622838Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.30413Z'),
(7085, 'vocabulary', '唇', '唇', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7085', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%94%87', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:38:00.729556Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.316916Z'),
(7086, 'vocabulary', '下唇', '下唇', 'The <kanji>bottom</kanji> <kanji>lip</kanji> is your <vocabulary>lower lip</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>bottom lip</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7086', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E5%94%87', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:38:07.103193Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(7087, 'vocabulary', '上唇', '上唇', 'The <kanji>above</kanji> <kanji>lip</kanji> is the <vocabulary>upper lip</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7087', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A%E5%94%87', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:38:14.998317Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(7088, 'vocabulary', '塀', '塀', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7088', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%80', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:38:20.74519Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.348576Z'),
(7089, 'vocabulary', '土塀', '土塀', 'A <kanji>soil</kanji> <kanji>fence</kanji> is a fence made from soil. Usually this is in the form of a <vocabulary>mud wall</vocabulary> or an <vocabulary>earthen wall</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7089', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%9F%E5%A1%80', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:38:26.298613Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.357803Z'),
(7090, 'vocabulary', '撃墜', '撃墜', 'An <kanji>attack</kanji> that causes a <kanji>crash</kanji> is when you''re <vocabulary>shooting down</vocabulary> something, usually an aircraft.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7090', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%92%83%E5%A2%9C', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:38:32.518888Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.36634Z'),
(7091, 'vocabulary', '墜落', '墜落', 'If you <kanji>crash</kanji> and <kanji>fall</kanji> then you''re <vocabulary>crashing</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>falling</vocabulary>, or maybe both.

墜落 refers to falling from a height and ending with a crash, like an airplane or a satellite plummeting to the ground.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7091', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%9C%E8%90%BD', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:38:39.165886Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:46:04.638998Z'),
(7092, 'vocabulary', '欠如', '欠如', 'You <kanji>lack</kanji> <kanji>likeness</kanji> to anything out there. You totally <vocabulary>lack</vocabulary> something that gives you likeness. It''s a <vocabulary>deficiency</vocabulary>, or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7092', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%A0%E5%A6%82', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:38:44.346223Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.38731Z'),
(7093, 'vocabulary', '突如', '突如', 'When something bears a <kanji>sudden</kanji> <kanji>likeness</kanji>, we say it happens <vocabulary>suddenly</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>all of a sudden</vocabulary>.

Note that this word is relatively formal and somewhat literary.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7093', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AA%81%E5%A6%82', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:38:52.673786Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.396368Z'),
(7094, 'vocabulary', '躍如', '躍如', 'A <kanji>leap</kanji> of <kanji>likeness</kanji> is when something that''s not real takes a big leap towards the likeness to reality. This happens when something is <vocabulary>vividly depicted</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>clearly expressed</vocabulary>.

This word is generally used in the phrase 面目躍如たる, which functions as an adjective to exemplify someone’s honor.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7094', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BA%8D%E5%A6%82', 55, 1, '2013-08-05T18:38:59.007761Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.198502Z'),
(7095, 'vocabulary', '老婆', '老婆', 'An <kanji>elderly</kanji> <kanji>old woman</kanji> is just an <vocabulary>old woman</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7095', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%81%E5%A9%86', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:39:04.550243Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.405425Z'),
(7096, 'vocabulary', 'お婆さん', 'お婆さん', 'An <kanji>old woman</kanji>, even with an honorific and an お is still an <vocabulary>elderly woman</vocabulary>. She might be your <vocabulary>grandmother</vocabulary> as well, though, so be nice!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7096', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E5%A9%86%E3%81%95%E3%82%93', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:39:10.742864Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T21:31:13.1679Z'),
(7097, 'vocabulary', '崖', '崖', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7097', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B4%96', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:39:15.673615Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:30:09.082137Z'),
(7098, 'vocabulary', '布巾', '布巾', 'A <kanji>cloth</kanji> <kanji>towel</kanji> is just a cloth towel. Essentially it''s known as a <vocabulary>dish cloth</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>tea towel</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7098', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%83%E5%B7%BE', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:39:20.81475Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.432092Z'),
(7099, 'vocabulary', '雑巾', '雑巾', 'A <kanji>miscellaneous</kanji> <kanji>cloth</kanji> is a <vocabulary>dust cloth</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>house cloth</vocabulary>. It''s just a cloth that you use on random things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7099', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%91%E5%B7%BE', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:39:27.364201Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.440486Z'),
(7100, 'vocabulary', '帽子', '帽子', 'A <kanji>hat</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> is a <vocabulary>hat</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>cap</vocabulary>. Sadly, it''s not made from children, unless you''re crazy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7100', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%BD%E5%AD%90', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:39:38.017374Z', NULL, '2026-01-08T01:12:56.492436Z'),
(7101, 'vocabulary', '貨幣', '貨幣', 'The <kanji>freight</kanji> of <kanji>cash</kanji> comes in. What is a freight of cash? It''s just <vocabulary>money</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>currency</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>coinage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7101', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A8%E5%B9%A3', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:39:44.173478Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.458163Z'),
(7102, 'vocabulary', '紙幣', '紙幣', '<kanji>Paper</kanji> <kanji>cash</kanji> is <vocabulary>paper money</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7102', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%99%E5%B9%A3', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:39:49.102055Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.467215Z'),
(7103, 'vocabulary', '恨む', '恨む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>grudge</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to hold a grudge</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7103', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%A8%E3%82%80', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:40:03.826556Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.486708Z'),
(7104, 'vocabulary', '痛恨', '痛恨', 'A <kanji>pain</kanji> <kanji>grudge</kanji> is pain you feel from the grudge. I wish I could change my grudge. It is my <vocabulary>regret</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7104', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%9B%E6%81%A8', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:40:10.956864Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:48:39.716764Z'),
(7105, 'vocabulary', '憎む', '憎む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>hate</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to hate</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to detest</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7105', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%8E%E3%82%80', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:40:24.274167Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.505085Z'),
(7106, 'vocabulary', '愛憎', '愛憎', 'If you have <kanji>love</kanji> and <kanji>hate</kanji> then you have <vocabulary>love and hate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7106', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9B%E6%86%8E', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:40:30.754625Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:28:57.446613Z'),
(7107, 'vocabulary', '憩う', '憩う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>rest</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to rest</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to relax</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7107', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%A9%E3%81%86', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:40:43.072851Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.537082Z'),
(7108, 'vocabulary', '休憩', '休憩', 'A <kanji>rest</kanji> <kanji>rest</kanji> is a <vocabulary>rest</vocabulary>. A <vocabulary>break</vocabulary>. A <vocabulary>recess</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7108', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%91%E6%86%A9', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:41:15.056194Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:33:18.733832Z'),
(7109, 'vocabulary', '扇ぐ', '扇ぐ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>folding fan</kanji> and since you don''t "to folding fan" something, this word means <vocabulary>to fan</vocabulary> instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7109', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%87%E3%81%90', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:41:32.462567Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.553897Z'),
(7110, 'vocabulary', '扇風機', '扇風機', 'A <kanji>folding fan</kanji> <kanji>wind</kanji> <kanji>machine</kanji> is a machine that blows wind like a folding fan. That''s an <vocabulary>electric fan</vocabulary> or an <vocabulary>electronic fan</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7110', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%87%E9%A2%A8%E6%A9%9F', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:41:37.611662Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:04:30.700843Z'),
(7111, 'vocabulary', '扇子', '扇子', 'A <kanji>folding fan</kanji>''s <kanji>child</kanji> is, for some reason, a <vocabulary>folding fan</vocabulary>. I''m guessing there are different sizes of folding fans, and this is the smaller more manageable kind you can carry around easily and make it smaller by folding it up, which is why it''s the "child" version of the folding fan. Still a folding fan, though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7111', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%87%E5%AD%90', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:41:43.68199Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.58173Z'),
(7112, 'vocabulary', '扉', '扉', 'Like the kanji, this means <vocabulary>door</vocabulary>.

You''ve already learned 戸 (door), but 扉 is a bit different. While 戸 can refer to any kind of door (though often sliding ones), 扉 more specifically refers to a <vocabulary>hinged door</vocabulary>, including things like gates or panels that open on hinges.

Because 扉 evokes the image of a door that swings open, it''s also sometimes used metaphorically. For example, a book''s front page can be called a 扉, and you''ll see expressions like 心の扉 (the door to one''s heart) too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7112', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%89', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:41:52.425728Z', NULL, '2026-01-14T18:14:31.964552Z'),
(7113, 'vocabulary', '門扉', '門扉', 'What are <kanji>gate</kanji> <kanji>door</kanji>s? The <vocabulary>doors of a gate</vocabulary>, though we may still just call them a <vocabulary>gate</vocabulary> in English.

門扉 is a formal word you''ll encounter more often in writing or architecture talk. It usually refers specifically to the part of the gate that slides or swings open (in other words, its doors).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7113', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%80%E6%89%89', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:41:57.622462Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T23:42:14.264969Z'),
(7114, 'vocabulary', '挿絵', '挿絵', 'If you <kanji>insert</kanji> a <kanji>picture</kanji> into a book, it becomes a <vocabulary>book illustration</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7114', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%BF%E7%B5%B5', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:42:02.944127Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:12:48.398208Z'),
(7115, 'vocabulary', '挿話', '挿話', '<kanji>Insert</kanji> some extra <kanji>talk</kanji>ing into a story, and you have a <vocabulary>side story</vocabulary>!

挿話 is a relatively formal word that refers to a short <vocabulary>anecdote</vocabulary> or little <vocabulary>episode</vocabulary> inserted into a story that isn''t essential to the main narrative but adds some additional context, background, or flavor.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7115', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%BF%E8%A9%B1', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:42:08.848728Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:24.109482Z'),
(7116, 'vocabulary', '挿入', '挿入', 'If you <kanji>insert</kanji> and <kanji>enter</kanji> something, you are doing <vocabulary>insertion</vocabulary> to it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7116', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%BF%E5%85%A5', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:42:14.530496Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.633916Z'),
(7117, 'vocabulary', '掌', '掌', 'The kanji means <kanji>palm</kanji>, and so does the vocab! This is your <vocabulary>palm</vocabulary>, as in the <vocabulary>palm of one''s hand</vocabulary>.

Something to note here is that you''ll typically see "palm" in Japanese written as 手のひら. This kanji isn''t as common but it does still mean the same thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7117', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%8C', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:42:20.404398Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:06:01.259319Z'),
(7118, 'vocabulary', '車掌', '車掌', 'Who''s the person with the <kanji>car</kanji> in the <kanji>palm</kanji> of their hand? The 車 here actually comes from 電車 (train), so this person is the <vocabulary>train conductor</vocabulary>. Think about it — the <vocabulary>conductor</vocabulary> handles things like checking tickets, making announcements, and giving signals to the driver, so everything that happens on the train is quite literally in their hands… er, palms. 

Note that these days, even many Japanese people get the 車掌 mixed up with the 運転士 (driver). However, the 車掌 is not the person driving the train — they''re more like the train''s overall supervisor, in charge of managing passengers and overseeing safety.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7118', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8A%E6%8E%8C', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:42:30.825099Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:04:45.344902Z'),
(7119, 'vocabulary', '掌握', '掌握', 'Put something in your <kanji>palm</kanji> and <kanji>grip</kanji> it. Now it''s in your grasp — you''re <vocabulary>grasping</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7119', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%8C%E6%8F%A1', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:42:36.48766Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:06:01.259319Z'),
(7120, 'vocabulary', '滴る', '滴る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>drip</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to drip</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7120', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BB%B4%E3%82%8B', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:42:44.097393Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.700275Z'),
(7121, 'vocabulary', '水滴', '水滴', 'A <kanji>water</kanji> <kanji>drip</kanji> is a <vocabulary>drop of water</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>water drop</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7121', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E6%BB%B4', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:42:51.536674Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.708957Z'),
(7122, 'vocabulary', '点滴', '点滴', '<kanji>Point</kanji> <kanji>drip</kanji>s are drips coming down little by little. These are the drips of an <vocabulary>intravenous drip</vocabulary>. 

Back in the day this was a way of talking about raindrops, or really any falling drops of water. But nowadays we only use it for <vocabulary>IV drip</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7122', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%B9%E6%BB%B4', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:43:10.870336Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.719773Z'),
(7123, 'vocabulary', '炊く', '炊く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>cook</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to cook</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to boil</vocabulary>. Often it has to do with cooking grains (<vocabulary>to cook grains</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7123', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%8A%E3%81%8F', 48, 1, '2013-08-05T18:43:16.76756Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:43.305707Z'),
(7124, 'vocabulary', '自炊', '自炊', 'A <kanji>self</kanji> <kanji>cook</kanji> is <vocabulary>cooking for oneself</vocabulary>. Better break out the TV tray.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7124', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E7%82%8A', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:43:24.116458Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.731612Z'),
(7125, 'vocabulary', '炊事', '炊事', 'A <kanji>cook</kanji>ing <kanji>occurrence</kanji> is just the act of <vocabulary>cooking</vocabulary>. It''s the <vocabulary>kitchen work</vocabulary>. 

炊事 refers to tasks involved in meal preparation as part of everyday life. It''s a relatively formal word and not as commonly used as 料理, but you might see it used in lists of household chores or duties, like in a job description for a housekeeper.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7125', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%8A%E4%BA%8B', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:43:30.408755Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.746152Z'),
(7126, 'vocabulary', '炊飯器', '炊飯器', 'A <kanji>cook</kanji> <kanji>food</kanji> <kanji>container</kanji> is a container used to cook the main food staple in Japan. In this case it''s a <vocabulary>rice cooker</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7126', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%8A%E9%A3%AF%E5%99%A8', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:44:04.342598Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:59:19.67524Z'),
(7127, 'vocabulary', '爽やか', '爽やか', 'This is the noun version of the kanji, making it <vocabulary>refreshing</vocabulary> as well.

爽やか generally describes something light, pleasant, and uplifting. You can use it for things like a cool morning breeze, the refreshing scent of fruit, or even a person who gives off a clean-cut and charming impression.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7127', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%88%BD%E3%82%84%E3%81%8B', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:44:10.224417Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:17:42.880938Z'),
(7128, 'vocabulary', '〜畳', '〜畳', 'The 〜 shows the number of tatami mats, and the 畳 part is "tatami" of course. So, this is a <vocabulary>counter for tatami mats</vocabulary>. It is generally used to show how big an apartment or something is. Oh, your apartment is 12 tatami mats? Nice and big!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7128', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E7%95%B3', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:44:16.34855Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T21:25:09.76674Z'),
(7129, 'vocabulary', '畳', '畳', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7129', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%B3', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:44:26.629081Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.799985Z'),
(7130, 'vocabulary', '明瞭', '明瞭', 'Something that is <kanji>bright</kanji> and <kanji>clear</kanji> has great <vocabulary>clarity</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>clearness</vocabulary>. Just look at something with such brightness and such clearness! It has so much clarity!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7130', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%8E%E7%9E%AD', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:44:40.236114Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(7131, 'vocabulary', '箸', '箸', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7131', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AE%B8', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:45:00.719274Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.816301Z'),
(7132, 'vocabulary', '割り箸', '割り箸', '<kanji>Divide</kanji>able <kanji>chopsticks</kanji> are chopsticks you can split apart. They are <vocabulary>splittable chopsticks</vocabulary>, almost always made from wood of some kind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7132', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%B2%E3%82%8A%E7%AE%B8', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:45:07.851657Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:40:20.776264Z'),
(7133, 'vocabulary', '胴', '胴', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7133', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%B4', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:45:16.149669Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.833268Z'),
(7134, 'vocabulary', '胴体', '胴体', 'Your <kanji>torso</kanji> <kanji>body</kanji> is the body area that''s your torso. It''s your <vocabulary>body</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>trunk</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>torso</vocabulary>, depending on what kind of body you have.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7134', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%B4%E4%BD%93', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:45:21.529102Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.842313Z'),
(7135, 'vocabulary', '芯', '芯', 'The kanji means <kanji>wick</kanji> and this word also means <vocabulary>wick</vocabulary>, but it means things at the <vocabulary>core</vocabulary> of other long things more often. Things like <vocabulary>pencil lead</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>marrow</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7135', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%AF', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:45:29.255324Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.851426Z'),
(7136, 'vocabulary', '虹', '虹', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7136', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%99%B9', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:45:35.374996Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.859506Z'),
(7137, 'vocabulary', '虹色', '虹色', '<kanji>Rainbow</kanji> <kanji>color</kanji>ed things are <vocabulary>rainbow colored</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7137', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%99%B9%E8%89%B2', 47, 1, '2013-08-05T18:45:45.931714Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.867573Z'),
(7138, 'vocabulary', 'お嬢さん', 'お嬢さん', 'Adding an お in front and a さん behind <kanji>miss</kanji> makes for a very respectful way of addressing a<vocabulary>young lady</vocabulary>. It can also be used to refer to somebody’s <vocabulary>daughter</vocabulary> in a polite manner.

Note that older generations tend to use this word to directly address a young woman. But be careful when doing this yourself, as it might come across as overly formal!

', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7138', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E5%AC%A2%E3%81%95%E3%82%93', 47, 1, '2013-08-07T18:45:26.544596Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.880073Z'),
(7139, 'vocabulary', '裸足', '裸足', 'If you have a <kanji>naked</kanji> <kanji>foot</kanji>, it means you''re not wearing anything on your foot, making you <vocabulary>barefoot</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7139', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%B8%E8%B6%B3', 47, 1, '2013-08-07T18:53:19.211557Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:29:09.139112Z'),
(7140, 'vocabulary', '紛れもない', '紛れもない', '紛らわしい is "confusing" or "misleading." This is the negative of that (you can tell by the ない), meaning it''s kind of the opposite of those things. This word is <vocabulary>unmistakable</vocabulary>, <vocabulary> indisputable </vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>obvious</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7140', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%9B%E3%82%8C%E3%82%82%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84', 46, 1, '2013-08-08T18:00:51.102563Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:43.557859Z'),
(7141, 'vocabulary', '稼ぎ', '稼ぎ', '稼ぐ is "to earn income." This is the noun version. It''s just <vocabulary>earnings</vocabulary>, the things you earn.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7141', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%BC%E3%81%8E', 47, 1, '2013-08-08T18:31:46.596863Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T23:44:25.178944Z'),
(7142, 'vocabulary', '〜狩り', '〜狩り', 'You know that 狩り is hunting already. This is the same thing (<vocabulary>hunting</vocabulary>). Difference is this one is attached to other words to specify what kind of hunting you''re doing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7142', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E7%8B%A9%E3%82%8A', 47, 1, '2013-08-08T19:04:50.953614Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.907143Z'),
(7143, 'vocabulary', '歯を磨く', '歯を磨く', '磨く is "to polish" or "to shine." If you''re doing that to your <kanji>teeth</kanji> you are brushing them. That''s why this is <vocabulary>to brush one''s teeth</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7143', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%AF%E3%82%92%E7%A3%A8%E3%81%8F', 47, 1, '2013-08-08T19:16:40.514Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:12:37.987919Z'),
(7144, 'vocabulary', '澄ます', '澄ます', '澄む is "to become clear" - this is when you make something clear, because you <reading>mus</reading>t (ます) make it clear. You have to. That''s why this is or <vocabulary>to make something clear</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to clear something</vocabulary>.

This can also mean <vocabulary>to strain</vocabulary> when used together with 耳, as in 耳を澄ます, "to strain one''s ears".', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7144', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BE%84%E3%81%BE%E3%81%99', 47, 1, '2013-08-08T19:20:41.946314Z', NULL, '2026-01-04T19:54:54.196787Z'),
(7145, 'vocabulary', '塊', '塊', 'The kanji and the word are the same, so this can mean <vocabulary>lump</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>mass</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>bundle</vocabulary>. Additionally, this can also refer to being the <vocabulary>embodiment</vocabulary> of something. When somebody or something is a mass or bundle of some attribute or trait, they might as well be the embodiment of it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7145', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%8A', 45, 1, '2013-08-08T19:24:43.372919Z', NULL, '2025-06-23T18:23:40.557701Z'),
(7146, 'vocabulary', '塊魂', '塊魂', 'A <kanji>lump</kanji> <kanji>spirit</kanji> is the lump of spirit that''s used in the famous game <vocabulary>Katamari Damacy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7146', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%8A%E9%AD%82', 47, 1, '2013-08-08T19:26:26.430546Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.940047Z'),
(7147, 'vocabulary', '矛先', '矛先', 'The <kanji>spear</kanji> <kanji>ahead</kanji> is the most ahead part of the halberd. That''s the <vocabulary>point of spear</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>spearpoint</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7147', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%9B%E5%85%88', 47, 1, '2013-08-09T20:01:09.72259Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:27:12.714404Z'),
(7148, 'vocabulary', 'お陰で', 'お陰で', '陰 is a shadow, and adding お to it and makes it honorific. It becomes the shadow of someone very important, and means <vocabulary>thanks to</vocabulary>, as in "thanks to you, I was able to pass the test." This originally comes from the idea of being in the shadow of the gods, who are protecting you and helping you out!

If that''s hard to remember, think about shade helping you out. Perhaps <vocabulary>thanks to</vocabulary> the <kanji>shade</kanji> you were able to survive the heat of the desert.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7148', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E9%99%B0%E3%81%A7', 47, 1, '2013-08-09T22:53:14.970549Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:43.981537Z'),
(7149, 'vocabulary', '本屋', '本屋', 'A <kanji>book</kanji> <kanji>shop</kanji> is a shop that sells books. A <vocabulary>bookstore</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7149', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E5%B1%8B', 9, 1, '2013-08-15T00:15:21.331099Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:18:39.371876Z'),
(7150, 'vocabulary', '不味い', '不味い', 'You''ve seen 美味しい by this point. This is the opposite of that. Instead of 美 you have 不 (not), so something that does <kanji>not</kanji> have <kanji>flavor</kanji> is something that doesn''t taste good. i.e. something <vocabulary>bad tasting</vocabulary>. This can also refer to someone who''s <vocabulary>unskillful</vocabulary> at something as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7150', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%91%B3%E3%81%84', 17, 1, '2013-08-15T00:20:49.566602Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:20:50.221396Z'),
(7151, 'vocabulary', '大丈夫', '大丈夫', 'The <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>height</kanji> of your <kanji>husband</kanji> makes everything <vocabulary>safe</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>all right</vocabulary>. Nothing you need to worry about when you have your super giant husband around to protect you. This is a really useful word, and it is used all the time. If someone asks: "Are you alright?" you could say in response "大丈夫" to mean you''re doing all right.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7151', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E4%B8%88%E5%A4%AB', 18, 1, '2013-08-15T00:31:32.567976Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(7152, 'vocabulary', '癖に', '癖に', 'Even though you have this <kanji>habit</kanji> you''re still able to win. That''s why this is <vocabulary>And yet</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>though</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>in spite of</vocabulary>. Because, <vocabulary>in spite of</vocabulary> your bad habit, you still won. Frustrating.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7152', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%96%E3%81%AB', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:11:27.357264Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:43.779469Z'),
(7153, 'vocabulary', '帝国主義', '帝国主義', 'The <kanji>Empire</kanji> (帝国) <kanji>ism</kanji> (主義) is when you are doing <vocabulary>imperialism</vocabulary>. Long live the emperor!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7153', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%9D%E5%9B%BD%E4%B8%BB%E7%BE%A9', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:11:39.091063Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:40:37.858322Z'),
(7154, 'vocabulary', '車椅子', '車椅子', 'You know that 椅子 is a "chair." What about a <kanji>car</kanji> <kanji>chair</kanji>? It''s a chair with wheels. A <vocabulary>wheelchair</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7154', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8A%E6%A4%85%E5%AD%90', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:12:37.511015Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:43.805036Z'),
(7155, 'vocabulary', '土俵際', '土俵際', 'Remember how 土俵 is a sumo ring? Now you''re at its <kanji>edge</kanji>, which means this is the <vocabulary>edge of a sumo ring</vocabulary>. This can also refer to a <vocabulary>last moment</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>critical moment</vocabulary> in time, like the moment before you get pushed out of the ring.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7155', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%9F%E4%BF%B5%E9%9A%9B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:12:44.1638Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:31:05.495299Z'),
(7156, 'vocabulary', '紛れ', '紛れ', 'When you''re <kanji>distract</kanji>ed, everything you do is a <vocabulary>fluke</vocabulary>. You''re not focusing, so it all comes down to <vocabulary>chance</vocabulary>.

Note that this word is mostly written in kana only!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7156', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%9B%E3%82%8C', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:12:49.164406Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:43.833258Z'),
(7157, 'vocabulary', '脅し', '脅し', 'The kanji is <kanji>threat</kanji> and the noun version (here) is <vocabulary>threat</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>a threat</vocabulary>.

Curious about the difference between 脅し and 脅迫? Both involve threats or intimidation, but the key distinction is the level of severity. 脅し is generally associated with milder forms of intimidation and coercion, while 脅迫 entails more direct and serious threats that may even break the law.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7157', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%85%E3%81%97', 51, 1, '2013-08-16T23:13:08.328813Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:43.845629Z'),
(7158, 'vocabulary', '脅迫', '脅迫', 'The thing that will <kanji>threaten</kanji> and then <kanji>urge</kanji> you to do something else is the <vocabulary>threat</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>menace</vocabulary>.

Note that 脅迫 often involves overt and serious threats that may cross legal boundaries.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7158', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%85%E8%BF%AB', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:13:17.388916Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:43.859089Z'),
(7159, 'vocabulary', '魔法使い', '魔法使い', 'You know that 魔法 is "magic" so what about someone who <kanji>use</kanji>s <kanji>magic</kanji>? What are they called? How about a <vocabulary>wizard</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>witch</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>sorcerer</vocabulary>?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7159', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AD%94%E6%B3%95%E4%BD%BF%E3%81%84', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:13:30.685711Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:43.87204Z'),
(7160, 'vocabulary', '涼む', '涼む', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>cool</kanji> (temperature-wise) and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to cool oneself</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to cool off</vocabulary>. Whoo, that feels much cooler now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7160', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B6%BC%E3%82%80', 26, 1, '2013-08-16T23:13:36.799053Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:45.058682Z'),
(7161, 'vocabulary', '備蓄', '備蓄', 'I need something to <kanji>provide</kanji> for me in an emergency, so I''m <kanji>amass</kanji>ing <vocabulary>emergency stores</vocabulary>. I''m building up a <vocabulary>stockpile</vocabulary> of <vocabulary>reserves</vocabulary>, just in case.

備蓄 is a formal word for stockpiling or reserving supplies for future use, especially in preparation for emergencies or shortages. It''s commonly used for storing food, water, fuel, or other necessities in case of disasters. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7161', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%99%E8%93%84', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:13:42.947072Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:05:59.494452Z'),
(7162, 'vocabulary', '貯蓄', '貯蓄', 'Take your <kanji>savings</kanji> and <kanji>amass</kanji> them. That is your <vocabulary>savings</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7162', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%AF%E8%93%84', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:13:50.503541Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T21:28:35.518569Z'),
(7163, 'vocabulary', '蓄える', '蓄える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>amass</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to store up</vocabulary> — as in gradually gathering and keeping something so it''s there when you need it.

This often relates to storing up food or supplies. In the same vein, it can mean <vocabulary>to amass</vocabulary> a fortune for later use, or <vocabulary>to build up</vocabulary> things like stamina and knowledge to slowly prepare yourself for something. You can even say ヒゲを蓄える (to build up or grow facial hair). Readiness comes in many forms, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7163', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%93%84%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:13:57.520951Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T21:28:35.518569Z'),
(7164, 'vocabulary', '蚊', '蚊', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7164', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9A%8A', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:14:03.004868Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:43.959071Z'),
(7165, 'vocabulary', '蚊帳', '蚊帳', 'A <kanji>mosquito</kanji> <kanji>notebook</kanji>, for some reason, is a <vocabulary>mosquito net</vocabulary>. Think of a notebook with graph paper lines on it, perhaps. These lines are shaped like a net. Surround yourself with it to make your own <vocabulary>mosquito net</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7165', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9A%8A%E5%B8%B3', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:14:08.469686Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T17:18:32.520267Z'),
(7166, 'vocabulary', '記帳', '記帳', 'Please <kanji>record</kanji> it in the <kanji>notebook</kanji>. This will be the <vocabulary>registry</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7166', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%98%E5%B8%B3', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:14:16.032813Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:43.983597Z'),
(7167, 'vocabulary', '手帳', '手帳', 'A <kanji>hand</kanji> <kanji>notebook</kanji> is a notebook you can hold in your hand. This could simply be a <vocabulary>notebook</vocabulary>, but it could also be a <vocabulary>planner</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7167', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E5%B8%B3', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:14:22.326829Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:43.996252Z'),
(7168, 'vocabulary', 'メモ帳', 'メモ帳', 'A <kanji>memo</kanji> (メモ) <kanji>notebook</kanji> is a <vocabulary>memo book</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>memo pad</vocabulary> that you use to write notes in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7168', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%A1%E3%83%A2%E5%B8%B3', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:14:40.126901Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.009276Z'),
(7169, 'vocabulary', '電話帳', '電話帳', 'A <kanji>telephone</kanji> (電話) <kanji>notebook</kanji> is a book you use with your telephone. It''s a <vocabulary>telephone book</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>telephone directory</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7169', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E8%A9%B1%E5%B8%B3', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:14:47.096585Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.030484Z'),
(7170, 'vocabulary', '蛇', '蛇', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7170', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9B%87', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:14:52.827925Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.035907Z'),
(7171, 'vocabulary', '貼る', '貼る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>paste</kanji> so the verb vocab is <vocabulary>to paste</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>to stick</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7171', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%BC%E3%82%8B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:14:58.714502Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:20:39.951302Z'),
(7172, 'vocabulary', '辱める', '辱める', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>humiliate</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to humiliate</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to disgrace</vocabulary>.

This is a formal word to describe an action that damages someone''s reputation or makes them feel deeply ashamed, often in public situations. In some contexts, this can also imply sexual assault, so it should be used with caution. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7172', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%B1%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:15:04.865146Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.061298Z'),
(7173, 'vocabulary', '恥辱', '恥辱', 'If you <kanji>shame</kanji> and <kanji>humiliate</kanji> me, I will have a sense of <vocabulary>disgrace</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>shame</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7173', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%A5%E8%BE%B1', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:15:15.876818Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:07:09.364336Z'),
(7174, 'vocabulary', '屈辱', '屈辱', '<kanji>Yield</kanji> a <kanji>humiliat</kanji>ion onto your life. What a <vocabulary>disgrace</vocabulary>. What a <vocabulary>humiliation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7174', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%88%E8%BE%B1', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:15:25.269862Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.084718Z'),
(7175, 'vocabulary', '鉢', '鉢', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7175', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%A2', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:15:30.268319Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.097631Z'),
(7176, 'vocabulary', '火鉢', '火鉢', 'Start a <kanji>fire</kanji> in your <kanji>flower pot</kanji> to make a <vocabulary>brazier</vocabulary>. A nice, homemade brazier. It might ruin the aesthetics of your garden, but at least you''ll be warm. (Or you might just burn down your house.)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7176', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%AB%E9%89%A2', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:15:37.794476Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.110241Z'),
(7177, 'vocabulary', '鉢巻', '鉢巻', 'Above your <kanji>flower pot</kanji> hangs a <kanji>scroll</kanji>, but it''s not just there for decoration — you can also use it as a <vocabulary>headband</vocabulary> when you''re gardening! Planting flowers is sweaty work, after all, and a good headband can make all the difference.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7177', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%A2%E5%B7%BB', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:15:45.767954Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.121288Z'),
(7178, 'vocabulary', '闇', '闇', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7178', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%97%87', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:15:53.259702Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:29:54.281231Z'),
(7179, 'vocabulary', '暗闇', '暗闇', 'A <kanji>dark</kanji> <kanji>darkness</kanji> is a <vocabulary>darkness</vocabulary>. It''s <vocabulary>the dark</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7179', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%97%E9%97%87', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:15:58.435765Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T02:01:16.682053Z'),
(7180, 'vocabulary', '無闇に', '無闇に', '"<kanji>Darkness</kanji> is <kanji>nothing</kanji> to worry about," I said <vocabulary>thoughtlessly</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>unreasonably</vocabulary>. Of course darkness is worth worrying about. It''s darkness! Who knows what is in there!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7180', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E9%97%87%E3%81%AB', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:16:04.482228Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.17362Z'),
(7181, 'vocabulary', '隙', '隙', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7181', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%99', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:16:32.346099Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.184855Z'),
(7182, 'vocabulary', '隙間', '隙間', 'A <kanji>crevice</kanji> <kanji>interval</kanji> is an area with a <vocabulary>crevice</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>crack</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7182', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%99%E9%96%93', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:16:37.237608Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T21:23:35.278206Z'),
(7183, 'vocabulary', '霜', '霜', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7183', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%9C', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:16:43.521676Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.210243Z'),
(7184, 'vocabulary', '飢餓', '飢餓', 'One person <kanji>starve</kanji>s and another person <kanji>starve</kanji>s. Then more people. So much starve that there''s <vocabulary>starvation</vocabulary> going on. It''s <vocabulary>famine</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>hunger</vocabulary>, everywhere.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7184', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%A2%E9%A4%93', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:16:54.419154Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.243279Z'),
(7185, 'vocabulary', '飢える', '飢える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>starve</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to starve</vocabulary>.

This is a formal word and it''s often used in phrases like 愛情に飢える (<vocabulary>to be starved for</vocabulary> love) or 知識に飢える (<vocabulary>to have a thirst for</vocabulary> knowledge).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7185', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%A2%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:17:00.476627Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.256664Z'),
(7186, 'vocabulary', '餓死', '餓死', '<kanji>Starve</kanji> to <kanji>death</kanji> is <vocabulary>death from starvation</vocabulary>... <vocabulary>Starving to death</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7186', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A4%93%E6%AD%BB', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:17:06.212901Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.269706Z'),
(7187, 'vocabulary', '畜産', '畜産', '<kanji>Livestock</kanji> will <kanji>give birth</kanji> to more livestock. You''re in the biz of <vocabulary>livestock farming</vocabulary>, that''s why. This is <vocabulary>animal husbandry</vocabulary>.

畜産 is a formal agricultural term, often used in discussions about farming industries, food production, and environmental impact.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7187', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%9C%E7%94%A3', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:17:12.313403Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T19:56:27.062421Z'),
(7188, 'vocabulary', '家畜', '家畜', '<kanji>House</kanji> broken <kanji>livestock</kanji> are <vocabulary>domestic animals</vocabulary> / <vocabulary>livestock</vocabulary>. They''re made to live with us in or near our homes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7188', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B6%E7%95%9C', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:17:18.590897Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.292411Z'),
(7189, 'vocabulary', '畜生', '畜生', 'The <kanji>livestock</kanji> <kanji>life</kanji>. The life of a <vocabulary>beast</vocabulary>! Arghhh, <vocabulary>damnit</vocabulary>, I hate the life of a beast. Damnit.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7189', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%9C%E7%94%9F', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:17:24.055194Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.303543Z'),
(7190, 'vocabulary', '迅速', '迅速', 'Things that are <kanji>swift</kanji> and <kanji>fast</kanji> are going to be <vocabulary>quick</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>fast</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>rapid</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>swift</vocabulary>. So many quick things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7190', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%85%E9%80%9F', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:17:29.913414Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T00:57:41.875504Z'),
(7191, 'vocabulary', '騎士', '騎士', 'A <kanji>horse riding</kanji> <kanji>samurai</kanji> is also known as a <vocabulary>knight</vocabulary>. Onward, knights of the round! Off to find the holy grail!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7191', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A8%8E%E5%A3%AB', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:17:36.057447Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.326294Z'),
(7192, 'vocabulary', '騎馬', '騎馬', 'A <kanji>horse riding</kanji> <kanji>horse</kanji> is what you need for <vocabulary>riding a horse</vocabulary>.

This is a formal word and it’s typically used at the beginning of compounds where you''ll see it translated as things like <vocabulary>mounted</vocabulary>, as in "mounted police," or <vocabulary>equestrian</vocabulary>, as in “equestrian peoples.” It''s working like an adjective there, but it''s still a noun in Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7192', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A8%8E%E9%A6%AC', 50, 1, '2013-08-16T23:17:41.304697Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T00:31:26.066272Z'),
(7193, 'vocabulary', '騎手', '騎手', '手 takes its alternative meaning "doer" here, so that gives us <kanji>horse riding</kanji> <kanji>doer</kanji>. Who does horse riding? It’s a <vocabulary>jockey</vocabulary>.

This word usually refers to a professional or skilled rider in horse racing or equestrian sports.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7193', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A8%8E%E6%89%8B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:17:46.261766Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.362916Z'),
(7194, 'vocabulary', '騎兵', '騎兵', 'A <kanji>horse riding</kanji> <kanji>soldier</kanji> is a member of the <vocabulary>cavalry</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7194', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A8%8E%E5%85%B5', 50, 1, '2013-08-16T23:17:50.911548Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:54:26.999027Z'),
(7195, 'vocabulary', '尽きる', '尽きる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>use up</kanji> or <kanji>exhaust</kanji>, and this verb is <vocabulary>to be used up</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be exhausted</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to run out</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7195', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%BD%E3%81%8D%E3%82%8B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:19:46.51323Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.39795Z'),
(7196, 'vocabulary', '理不尽', '理不尽', '<kanji>Reason</kanji> is <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>exhaust</kanji>ed... but what you''re asking is <vocabulary>unreasonable</vocabulary>. Soon my reason will be exhausted, though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7196', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%90%86%E4%B8%8D%E5%B0%BD', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:19:56.576131Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.409369Z'),
(7197, 'vocabulary', '尽力', '尽力', 'If you <kanji>exhaust</kanji> <kanji>power</kanji> you are putting out <vocabulary>efforts</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7197', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%BD%E5%8A%9B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:20:02.567015Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(7198, 'vocabulary', '彩る', '彩る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>coloring</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to color something</vocabulary>, because you''re doing coloring.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7198', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%A9%E3%82%8B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:20:08.827692Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.435211Z'),
(7199, 'vocabulary', '色彩', '色彩', 'The <kanji>color</kanji> <kanji>coloring</kanji> is the <vocabulary>color</vocabulary> of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7199', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%89%B2%E5%BD%A9', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:20:16.015925Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.44841Z'),
(7200, 'vocabulary', '迷彩', '迷彩', '<kanji>Astray</kanji> <kanji>coloring</kanji> is coloring that''s all over the place. It''s astray all over your clothes. This astray coloring is <vocabulary>camouflage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7200', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%B7%E5%BD%A9', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:20:29.480497Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.461984Z'),
(7201, 'vocabulary', '水彩画', '水彩画', 'A <kanji>water</kanji> <kanji>coloring</kanji> <kanji>painting</kanji> is a <vocabulary>watercolor painting</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7201', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E5%BD%A9%E7%94%BB', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:20:37.597663Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.47492Z'),
(7202, 'vocabulary', '油彩', '油彩', '<kanji>Oil</kanji> <kanji>coloring</kanji> is better known as <vocabulary>oil painting</vocabulary>. What fine strokes! What fine lines!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7202', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%B9%E5%BD%A9', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:20:51.655666Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:39:43.050646Z'),
(7203, 'vocabulary', '記憶', '記憶', 'When you <kanji>record</kanji> <kanji>recollection</kanji>s in your mind, that concept is the concept of <vocabulary>memory</vocabulary>. This word is not a specific memory, instead it''s the idea of memory itself. So, uh, try to memorize that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7203', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%98%E6%86%B6', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:20:57.653416Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:46:04.085335Z'),
(7204, 'vocabulary', '憶測', '憶測', 'A <kanji>recollection</kanji> and <kanji>measure</kanji>ment of the situation is what I use to make a <vocabulary>guess</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>speculation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7204', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%B6%E6%B8%AC', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:21:03.936279Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T21:20:06.990299Z'),
(7205, 'vocabulary', '溶かす', '溶かす', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>melt</kanji> and this word means <vocabulary>to melt something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to melt</vocabulary>. You''re doing the melting, and you know it because you are the <reading>cause</reading> (かす) of the melting.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7205', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%B6%E3%81%8B%E3%81%99', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:21:35.512254Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.536348Z'),
(7206, 'vocabulary', '溶岩', '溶岩', 'It can <kanji>melt</kanji> a <kanji>boulder</kanji>? What is this stuff that''s so hot? It''s <vocabulary>lava</vocabulary>. It''s also probably made from melted boulders too, come to think of it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7206', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%B6%E5%B2%A9', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:21:40.454753Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:18:14.999229Z'),
(7207, 'vocabulary', '水溶性', '水溶性', 'With <kanji>water</kanji> it will <kanji>melt</kanji>. It is <kanji>natural</kanji>ly like this. It is <vocabulary>water soluble</vocabulary>.

水溶性 is a scientific and technical term that refers to <vocabulary>water solubility</vocabulary>. It''s commonly used in chemistry, medicine, and product descriptions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7207', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E6%BA%B6%E6%80%A7', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:21:48.691164Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T19:53:13.056236Z'),
(7208, 'vocabulary', '耐える', '耐える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>resistant</kanji>. If you''re resisting things, then you''re enduring and withstanding them. That''s why this is <vocabulary>to endure</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to withstand</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7208', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%90%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:21:55.47415Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:24.566403Z'),
(7209, 'vocabulary', '忍耐', '忍耐', 'I''m <kanji>resistant</kanji> and I <kanji>endure</kanji>. I have much <vocabulary>endurance</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>perseverance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7209', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%8D%E8%80%90', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:22:01.551609Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:35:05.2219Z'),
(7210, 'vocabulary', '耐熱', '耐熱', 'If something is <kanji>resistant</kanji> to <kanji>heat</kanji> then it is <vocabulary>heat resistant</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>heatproof</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7210', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%90%E7%86%B1', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:22:07.63125Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.599539Z'),
(7211, 'vocabulary', '耐火', '耐火', 'If something is <kanji>resistant</kanji> to <kanji>fire</kanji> then it is <vocabulary>fire resistant</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>fireproof</vocabulary>. Turtles are not this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7211', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%90%E7%81%AB', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:22:14.268813Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.612385Z'),
(7212, 'vocabulary', '耐久性', '耐久性', 'I have the <kanji>nature</kanji> of being <kanji>resistant</kanji> for a <kanji>long time</kanji>. Things that are resistant for a long time have good <vocabulary>durability</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7212', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%90%E4%B9%85%E6%80%A7', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:22:19.58923Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.62471Z'),
(7213, 'vocabulary', '踊る', '踊る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>dance</kanji> and the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to dance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7213', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B8%8A%E3%82%8B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:22:24.281597Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.636573Z'),
(7214, 'vocabulary', '踊り', '踊り', 'A <kanji>dance</kanji> is a <vocabulary>dance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7214', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B8%8A%E3%82%8A', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:22:29.622381Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.648765Z'),
(7215, 'vocabulary', '踊り場', '踊り場', 'While a <kanji>dance</kanji> <kanji>location</kanji> can be a location in which you dance, this word actually means <vocabulary>landing</vocabulary> (as in, a landing between two flights of stairs on a staircase) or <vocabulary>plateau</vocabulary> (as in, for example, a plateauing economy) most of the time.

While the meaning has changed over time to mostly refer to landings on staircases, it can still be used to mean <vocabulary>dance hall</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>dance floor</vocabulary>, although loanwords like ダンスホール are more commonly used these days.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7215', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B8%8A%E3%82%8A%E5%A0%B4', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:22:35.234304Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.66851Z'),
(7216, 'vocabulary', '盆踊り', '盆踊り', 'The <kanji>ancestral festival</kanji> <kanji>dance</kanji> is the <vocabulary>ancestral festival dance</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>bon festival dance</vocabulary>.

盆踊り (<vocabulary>bon odori</vocabulary>) is a festival dance performed for Obon, a Buddhist event that honors the spirits of one''s ancestors. In this dance, people often wear yukata and dance in circles around a central platform, moving in synchronized patterns. It''s a fun time, be sure to attend one if you can!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7216', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%86%E8%B8%8A%E3%82%8A', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:22:40.075927Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.680201Z'),
(7217, 'vocabulary', '賢い', '賢い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>clever</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>clever</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>intelligent</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7217', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%A2%E3%81%84', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:22:45.373197Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.691025Z'),
(7218, 'vocabulary', '賢人', '賢人', 'A <kanji>wise</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>sage</vocabulary>! Or you can just be straightforward and call them a <vocabulary>wise person</vocabulary>. 

賢人 is a somewhat old-fashioned word used to describe someone with deep knowledge or good judgment. It has a dignified, antiquated feel that makes you think of wise figures in old legends or classical literature.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7218', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%A2%E4%BA%BA', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:22:50.654364Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(7219, 'vocabulary', '賢明', '賢明', 'You''re <kanji>wise</kanji> and <kanji>bright</kanji> so obviously you''re going to make <vocabulary>wise</vocabulary> decisions too — <vocabulary>sensible</vocabulary> in every regard. In fact, when you make these very wise choices and judgment calls, your brain glows brightly.

賢明 is a formal word that is often seen in writing. It''s commonly used to say how someone made a wise decision or sensible choice.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7219', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%A2%E6%98%8E', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:22:56.501907Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(7220, 'vocabulary', '輝く', '輝く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>radiance</kanji>. Imagine if something is radiating on something else. That would cause it <vocabulary>to shine</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to glitter</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to sparkle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7220', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%9D%E3%81%8F', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:23:02.78374Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.727324Z'),
(7221, 'vocabulary', '光輝', '光輝', 'The <kanji>light</kanji>''s <kanji>radiance</kanji> is that of <vocabulary>brightness</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>splendor</vocabulary>.

光輝 can refer to both physical radiance or to the metaphorical brilliance of someone''s achievements. It''s a formal word used mostly in advanced written contexts, but you may also come across it as a name (usually for males) in everyday life.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7221', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%89%E8%BC%9D', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:23:07.502699Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.740672Z'),
(7222, 'vocabulary', '輝度', '輝度', 'The <kanji>radiance</kanji> <kanji>degree</kanji>s show the <vocabulary>luminance</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>brightness</vocabulary> of something.

This is a technical term used in physics. It refers to the amount of light reflected or emitted from a surface.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7222', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%9D%E5%BA%A6', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:23:13.105499Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.753299Z'),
(7223, 'vocabulary', '麻', '麻', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well, but it also means <vocabulary>cannabis</vocabulary> as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7223', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BA%BB', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:23:20.403647Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.766553Z'),
(7224, 'vocabulary', '大麻', '大麻', '<kanji>Big</kanji> <kanji>hemp</kanji> is <vocabulary>marijuana</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>cannabis</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7224', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E9%BA%BB', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:25:15.544037Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(7225, 'vocabulary', '麻酔', '麻酔', 'A <kanji>hemp</kanji> <kanji>drunk</kanji>ness is when you take some "hemp" and put it into your body so you don''t really feel anything anymore. This is <vocabulary>anesthesia</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7225', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BA%BB%E9%85%94', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:25:20.979864Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.804971Z'),
(7226, 'vocabulary', '灯り', '灯り', 'The kanji means <kanji>lamp</kanji>, and the noun version means essentially the same thing: a <vocabulary>light</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>lamp</vocabulary>.

While 光 refers to light in an abstract sense and 明かり to the illumination something gives off, 灯り (or sometimes just 灯) refers to a manmade light source — whether a lamp, a candle, or even an indistinct light glimpsed in the distance at night. Compared to more modern terms like ライト, it feels gentler and warmer, and appears more often in poetic or descriptive contexts like novels or songs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7226', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%AF%E3%82%8A', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:25:30.282922Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:56:31.688597Z'),
(7227, 'vocabulary', '街灯', '街灯', 'The <kanji>streets</kanji> are lit by <kanji>lamp</kanji>s — <vocabulary>streetlight</vocabulary>s, to be specific! And nighttime in the city sure would be a lot scarier without them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7227', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%97%E7%81%AF', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:25:35.143021Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:56:31.688597Z'),
(7228, 'vocabulary', '電灯', '電灯', 'An <kanji>electricity</kanji> <kanji>lamp</kanji> is exactly what it sounds like: an <vocabulary>electric light</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>electric lamp</vocabulary>.

電灯 is a slightly technical word that broadly refers to electrical lighting. It can include many kinds of lighting devices, such as light bulbs, fluorescent lamps, streetlights, and flashlights, but it''s most commonly used for a fixed light that illuminates a room or street.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7228', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E7%81%AF', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:25:40.826671Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:56:31.688597Z'),
(7229, 'vocabulary', '咲く', '咲く', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>blossom</kanji> or <kanji>bloom</kanji> and the verb version is <vocabulary>to bloom</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7229', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%92%B2%E3%81%8F', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:25:46.352672Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:05:32.976363Z'),
(7230, 'vocabulary', '遅咲き', '遅咲き', 'A <kanji>slow</kanji> <kanji>bloom</kanji> is a bloom that happens late. It''s a <vocabulary>late flower</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>late blooming</vocabulary>. Important to know if you''re going to go do 花見.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7230', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%85%E5%92%B2%E3%81%8D', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:25:50.859051Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:05:32.976363Z'),
(7231, 'vocabulary', '返り咲き', '返り咲き', 'The <kanji>return</kanji> <kanji>blossom</kanji> is when the blossoms return. A <vocabulary>second bloom</vocabulary>. This is another word for a <vocabulary>comeback</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7231', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%94%E3%82%8A%E5%92%B2%E3%81%8D', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:25:58.18934Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:05:32.976363Z'),
(7232, 'vocabulary', '培う', '培う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>cultivate</kanji> and the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to cultivate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7232', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%B9%E3%81%86', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:26:08.015172Z', NULL, '2025-07-24T17:35:00.944942Z'),
(7233, 'vocabulary', '培養', '培養', '<kanji>Cultivate</kanji> and <kanji>foster</kanji> something. That''s <vocabulary>cultivation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7233', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%B9%E9%A4%8A', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:26:12.791427Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:40:05.658345Z'),
(7234, 'vocabulary', '悔しい', '悔しい', 'The kanji is <kanji>regret</kanji> and this is <vocabulary>regrettable</vocabulary>. And something regrettable is likely to be <vocabulary>frustrating</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>vexing</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>disappointing</vocabulary>.

悔しい generally expresses a feeling of frustration, regret, or vexation, often due to a sense of failure or missed opportunity. It conveys a strong emotional response to a situation where one feels disappointed or bitter about not achieving something they desired, especially when they were close to success or when the outcome feels unfair.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7234', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%94%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:27:04.687696Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.915491Z'),
(7235, 'vocabulary', '後悔', '後悔', 'My <kanji>behind</kanji> <kanji>regret</kanji> is the regret from before, which is all regret. So this means <vocabulary>regret</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>repentance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7235', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%8C%E6%82%94', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:27:15.779496Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.938492Z'),
(7236, 'vocabulary', '脇', '脇', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7236', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%87', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:27:25.796184Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.949368Z'),
(7237, 'vocabulary', '脇見', '脇見', 'If you look to your <kanji>armpit</kanji> to <kanji>see</kanji> you''re <vocabulary>looking away</vocabulary> from where you should be looking. If you''re too busy staring at your armpit while you drive, for example, you''ll probably get in an accident. So don''t look away like this!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7237', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%87%E8%A6%8B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:27:33.071391Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.960651Z'),
(7238, 'vocabulary', '脇役', '脇役', 'If you''re on <kanji>armpit</kanji> <kanji>duty</kanji> you''re definitely not the lead. You''re standing next to the lead''s armpit, because you''re the <vocabulary>supporting actor</vocabulary>. You''re in a <vocabulary>supporting role</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7238', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%87%E5%BD%B9', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:27:38.1822Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.971782Z'),
(7239, 'vocabulary', '未遂', '未遂', 'When you try something but can <kanji>not yet</kanji> <kanji>accomplish</kanji> it, it''s only an <vocabulary>attempt</vocabulary> that failed or stayed incomplete. That''s 未遂, and it''s often used in legal contexts for crimes that didn''t succeed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7239', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AA%E9%81%82', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:27:43.380598Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T00:18:48.338044Z'),
(7240, 'vocabulary', '遂行', '遂行', '<kanji>Accomplish</kanji> and <kanji>go</kanji> forth! These are your <vocabulary>accomplishment</vocabulary>s. This is your <vocabulary>execution</vocabulary> of actions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7240', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%82%E8%A1%8C', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:27:48.734188Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:44.995834Z'),
(7241, 'vocabulary', '班', '班', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7241', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%AD', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:28:05.034663Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.009185Z'),
(7242, 'vocabulary', '首班', '首班', 'The <kanji>neck</kanji> of the <kanji>squad</kanji> is the most important part of the squad, for if you lose the neck you lose the head (they''re all one thing). This person is the <vocabulary>head</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>leader</vocabulary> of a group.

This is a very formal word to refer to someone in a top role, especially a <vocabulary>Prime Minister</vocabulary> (aka the head of the cabinet).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7242', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%96%E7%8F%AD', 60, 1, '2013-08-16T23:28:12.335458Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.021303Z'),
(7243, 'vocabulary', '班長', '班長', 'The <kanji>squad</kanji> <kanji>leader</kanji> is the <vocabulary>squad leader</vocabulary>. Can also be the <vocabulary>honcho</vocabulary> (ever wonder where this word came from?).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7243', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%AD%E9%95%B7', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:28:17.555735Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.033139Z'),
(7244, 'vocabulary', '塗布', '塗布', 'Let''s <kanji>paint</kanji> a <kanji>cloth</kanji>''s worth of paint on the wall. This is one <vocabulary>application</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>coating</vocabulary> of paint.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7244', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%97%E5%B8%83', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:28:24.615244Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.044512Z'),
(7245, 'vocabulary', '塗る', '塗る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>paint</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to paint</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to plaster</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to lacquer</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7245', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%97%E3%82%8B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:28:30.885331Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.055744Z'),
(7246, 'vocabulary', '塗装', '塗装', 'With <kanji>paint</kanji> I want to <kanji>attire</kanji> the wall. Let''s put a <vocabulary>coating</vocabulary> of <vocabulary>painting</vocabulary> up on the wall.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7246', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%97%E8%A3%85', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:28:36.889589Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.066827Z'),
(7247, 'vocabulary', '塗料', '塗料', '<kanji>Paint</kanji> <kanji>material</kanji> is <vocabulary>paint</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>painting material</vocabulary>.

塗料 refers to the paint or <vocabulary>coating</vocabulary> used for surfaces like walls or industrial items to decorate or protect them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7247', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%97%E6%96%99', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:28:44.808357Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.078965Z'),
(7248, 'vocabulary', '斜め', '斜め', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7248', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%9C%E3%82%81', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:28:50.319815Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.091216Z'),
(7249, 'vocabulary', '斜面', '斜面', 'A <kanji>diagonal</kanji> <kanji>surface</kanji> is a <vocabulary>slant</vocabulary>. A <vocabulary>slope</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7249', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%9C%E9%9D%A2', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:28:58.555394Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T03:20:33.474004Z'),
(7250, 'vocabulary', '斜体', '斜体', 'When text has a <kanji>diagonal</kanji> <kanji>body</kanji> to it, it is leaning. It is in <vocabulary>italics</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>italic font style</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7250', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%9C%E4%BD%93', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:29:04.552306Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.115182Z'),
(7251, 'vocabulary', '殴る', '殴る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>punch</kanji>, so the verb means <vocabulary>to punch</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to strike</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to hit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7251', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%B4%E3%82%8B', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:29:10.3272Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:14:53.54038Z'),
(7252, 'vocabulary', '殴打', '殴打', 'An <kanji>punch</kanji> <kanji>hit</kanji> is a <vocabulary>hit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>strike</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>blow</vocabulary>. Not a friendly hit, that''s for sure.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7252', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%B4%E6%89%93', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:29:21.452105Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:14:53.54038Z'),
(7253, 'vocabulary', '盾', '盾', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7253', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%BE', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:29:34.991207Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.147569Z'),
(7254, 'vocabulary', '矛盾', '矛盾', 'A <kanji>spear</kanji> and a <kanji>shield</kanji>. Totally different things. Not at all like each other. A complete <vocabulary>contradiction</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7254', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%9B%E7%9B%BE', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:29:39.863532Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.158207Z'),
(7255, 'vocabulary', '後ろ盾', '後ろ盾', 'The <kanji>rear</kanji> <kanji>shield</kanji> is the shield that protects your back. This is your <vocabulary>backing</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>support</vocabulary>. So, not a literal shield, but the kind of protection that you want to have anyways.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7255', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%8C%E3%82%8D%E7%9B%BE', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:29:47.375329Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.16859Z'),
(7256, 'vocabulary', '収穫', '収穫', '<kanji>Obtain</kanji> a <kanji>harvest</kanji>. When you do this you have a <vocabulary>harvest</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>crop</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7256', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8E%E7%A9%AB', 48, 1, '2013-08-16T23:29:58.219601Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.179995Z'),
(7257, 'vocabulary', '貨幣価値', '貨幣価値', '貨幣 is "<kanji>currency</kanji>" and 価値 is "<kanji>value</kanji>." This shows the <vocabulary>currency value</vocabulary>. Hopefully it''s good for you when you visit Japan!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7257', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%A8%E5%B9%A3%E4%BE%A1%E5%80%A4', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:31:10.575638Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:51:00.759578Z'),
(7258, 'vocabulary', '不明瞭', '不明瞭', '明瞭 is "<kanji>clarity</kanji>." If there is <kanji>not</kanji> any of this clarity, then you have <vocabulary>dimness</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>obscurity</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7258', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%98%8E%E7%9E%AD', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:31:17.213747Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(7259, 'vocabulary', '浸透', '浸透', '<kanji>Immersed</kanji> to the point where it''s <kanji>transparent</kanji>, because it''s so soaked... that is total <vocabulary>permeation</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>soaking</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>osmosis</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7259', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B8%E9%80%8F', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:31:56.851973Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.217769Z'),
(7260, 'vocabulary', '粘り', '粘り', 'If 粘る is "to be sticky" then the noun version is <vocabulary>stickiness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7260', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%98%E3%82%8A', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:32:02.691879Z', NULL, '2026-02-16T22:38:00.955625Z'),
(7261, 'vocabulary', 'お婆ちゃん', 'お婆ちゃん', 'An <kanji>old woman</kanji> is who this is. Then, you add ちゃん for the name ender. You add お to be nicer. This person is a <vocabulary>granny</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>grandma</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>old lady</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>old woman</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7261', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E5%A9%86%E3%81%A1%E3%82%83%E3%82%93', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:32:10.51824Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.241084Z'),
(7262, 'vocabulary', '憎らしい', '憎らしい', '憎む is "to hate" - the adjective version is <vocabulary>hateful</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7262', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%8E%E3%82%89%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:32:16.707148Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.252374Z'),
(7263, 'vocabulary', '憎しみ', '憎しみ', 'If 憎む is "to hate" then the noun version would be <vocabulary>hatred</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7263', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%8E%E3%81%97%E3%81%BF', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:32:22.897315Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.264849Z'),
(7264, 'vocabulary', '憎い', '憎い', 'If 憎む means "to hate" then this adjective version is <vocabulary>hateful</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7264', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%8E%E3%81%84', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:32:29.305586Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.27833Z'),
(7265, 'vocabulary', '扇', '扇', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7265', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%87', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:32:35.484864Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.291895Z'),
(7266, 'vocabulary', '団扇', '団扇', 'A <kanji>group</kanji> of <kanji>folding fan</kanji>s, no matter how big it gets, is still a <vocabulary>fan</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7266', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%A3%E6%89%87', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:32:41.783947Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.304643Z'),
(7267, 'vocabulary', '挿入する', '挿入する', '挿入 is "insertion" so the verb version is <vocabulary>to insert</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7267', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%BF%E5%85%A5%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:33:17.013842Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.318199Z'),
(7268, 'vocabulary', '駒', '駒', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Note that this can also refer to a <vocabulary>shogi piece</vocabulary> or any other board <vocabulary>game piece</vocabulary> as well!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7268', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%92', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:33:22.003613Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.331825Z'),
(7269, 'vocabulary', '紫', '紫', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7269', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%AB', 23, 1, '2013-08-19T17:33:29.119011Z', NULL, '2025-12-25T06:24:53.321498Z'),
(7270, 'vocabulary', '紫外線', '紫外線', 'The <kanji>purple</kanji> <kanji>outside</kanji> <kanji>line</kanji> is the color spectrum that''s outside purple. Those are <vocabulary>ultraviolet rays</vocabulary> (the lines are the rays).

紫外線 is a technical term, but you''ll come across it a lot in everyday 紫外線対策 (UV protection) contexts like weather reports, skincare, or health talk.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7270', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%AB%E5%A4%96%E7%B7%9A', 52, 1, '2013-08-19T17:33:36.271383Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.797222Z'),
(7271, 'vocabulary', '紫色', '紫色', 'A <kanji>purple</kanji> <kanji>color</kanji> is <vocabulary>purple</vocabulary>. Simple as that!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7271', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%AB%E8%89%B2', 23, 1, '2013-08-19T17:33:56.214622Z', NULL, '2025-12-25T06:24:53.321498Z'),
(7272, 'vocabulary', '抽象', '抽象', 'The thing you <kanji>extract</kanji> from a <kanji>phenomenon</kanji> is an <vocabulary>abstraction</vocabulary>. Extracting abstraction out of a phenomenon may not sound like a very concrete or vivid mnemonic, but I guess that makes it an appropriate abstraction…', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7272', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%BD%E8%B1%A1', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:34:03.297709Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.385483Z'),
(7273, 'vocabulary', '抽選', '抽選', '<kanji>Extract</kanji> a lucky ticket to <kanji>choose</kanji> the winner of the <vocabulary>lottery</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>raffle</vocabulary>! Today I extract... number 23! We''ve chosen a winner!

抽選 refers to <vocabulary>drawing lots</vocabulary> and randomly selecting a winner or winners from a pool of candidates or entries.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7273', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%BD%E9%81%B8', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:34:08.731876Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.398391Z'),
(7274, 'vocabulary', '抽出', '抽出', 'To <kanji>extract</kanji> something from an <kanji>exit</kanji> is to do an <vocabulary>extraction</vocabulary>.

抽出 is commonly used to refer to the extraction of substances, such as extracting juice, essences, or compounds from materials like chemicals or metals. Depending on the context, it could also refer to <vocabulary>extracting</vocabulary> relevant information from a dataset or text.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7274', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%BD%E5%87%BA', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:34:16.734523Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.409631Z'),
(7275, 'vocabulary', '誓い', '誓い', 'This looks like an adjective, but it''s actually a noun. This word is the same as the kanji meaning: <vocabulary>vow</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>oath</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7275', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%93%E3%81%84', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:34:33.117171Z', NULL, '2025-07-24T17:36:31.399784Z'),
(7276, 'vocabulary', '誓約', '誓約', 'A <kanji>vow</kanji> <kanji>promise</kanji> is the promise that you made with your <vocabulary>vow</vocabulary>. It''s the <vocabulary>oath</vocabulary> you swear to keep!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7276', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%93%E7%B4%84', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:34:48.609927Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.432329Z'),
(7277, 'vocabulary', '丈夫', '丈夫', 'With the <kanji>height</kanji> of my <kanji>husband</kanji>, he''s very <vocabulary>firm</vocabulary>. Try to push him over. Impossible. He''s so tall and <vocabulary>sturdy</vocabulary> due to his height.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7277', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%88%E5%A4%AB', 15, 1, '2013-08-19T17:46:55.006563Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.679782Z'),
(7278, 'vocabulary', '丈', '丈', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This word is usually used for clothing <vocabulary>height</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>length</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7278', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%88', 15, 1, '2013-08-19T17:48:34.514674Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.708523Z'),
(7279, 'vocabulary', '背丈', '背丈', 'Someone''s <kanji>back</kanji> <kanji>height</kanji> is their <vocabulary>height</vocabulary>. It also refers to their <vocabulary>stature</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7279', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%8C%E4%B8%88', 32, 1, '2013-08-19T17:54:14.038443Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.489932Z'),
(7280, 'vocabulary', '頑丈', '頑丈', 'A <kanji>stubborn</kanji> <kanji>height</kanji> is a shorter, stockier height. It makes a person especially <vocabulary>solid</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>firm</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>stout</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7280', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%91%E4%B8%88', 15, 1, '2013-08-19T17:55:25.919955Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.717689Z'),
(7281, 'vocabulary', '悟る', '悟る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>realize</kanji>, so the verb version means <vocabulary>to realize</vocabulary>. But you probably already realized that…

悟る is often used when someone understands something deeply, not just at the surface-level. It''s a sudden, clear realization. So this also means <vocabulary>to attain enlightenment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7281', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%9F%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:55:50.173697Z', NULL, '2025-07-30T07:39:06.642064Z'),
(7282, 'vocabulary', '覚悟', '覚悟', 'When you <kanji>awake</kanji> and <kanji>realize</kanji> something, you''ve achieved <vocabulary>mental readiness</vocabulary>. You have the <vocabulary>resolution</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>determination</vocabulary> to face any challenge now.

覚悟 refers to mentally preparing yourself to face a difficult situation or accept an unfavorable outcome. It conveys a sense of strong determination and readiness to confront risks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7282', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%9A%E6%82%9F', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:55:57.074391Z', NULL, '2025-07-24T14:21:53.426044Z'),
(7283, 'vocabulary', '開拓', '開拓', '<kanji>Open</kanji> up <kanji>cultivation</kanji> back on this land. It was closed before, due to it being a wasteland, but now it''s time for <vocabulary>reclamation</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>cultivation</vocabulary> of this land.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7283', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%8B%E6%8B%93', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:56:21.243719Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T21:16:32.091876Z'),
(7284, 'vocabulary', '拘置', '拘置', 'When you <kanji>arrest</kanji> someone, you <kanji>put</kanji> them in <vocabulary>detention</vocabulary>.

拘置 is a formal term that means <vocabulary>detaining</vocabulary> someone in custody — typically in a detention center — while they await trial or go through legal proceedings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7284', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%98%E7%BD%AE', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:56:40.227862Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:08:17.52437Z'),
(7285, 'vocabulary', '礎', '礎', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7285', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A4%8E', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:56:46.897077Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.625565Z'),
(7286, 'vocabulary', '基礎', '基礎', 'A <kanji>foundation</kanji> of <kanji>foundation</kanji>s is a <vocabulary>foundation</vocabulary>. It is the <vocabulary>fundamentals</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7286', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%BA%E7%A4%8E', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:56:52.456583Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.636981Z'),
(7287, 'vocabulary', '鶴', '鶴', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7287', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%B6%B4', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:56:57.857094Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.648203Z'),
(7288, 'vocabulary', '刈る', '刈る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>prune</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to prune</vocabulary> (or <vocabulary>to mow</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to cut</vocabulary>).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7288', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%88%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:57:04.108978Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.659879Z'),
(7289, 'vocabulary', '草刈り', '草刈り', 'Let''s take the <kanji>grass</kanji> and <kanji>prune</kanji> it. This is called <vocabulary>mowing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7289', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8D%89%E5%88%88%E3%82%8A', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:57:12.654073Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.673095Z'),
(7290, 'vocabulary', '刈り取る', '刈り取る', 'If you <kanji>prune</kanji> and <kanji>take</kanji> from what you pruned, you''re going <vocabulary>to mow</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to reap</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to harvest</vocabulary> whatever it is you''re pruning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7290', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%88%E3%82%8A%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:57:21.260455Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.685682Z'),
(7291, 'vocabulary', '剛健', '剛健', 'When you are <kanji>sturdy</kanji> and <kanji>healthy</kanji> you are full of <vocabulary>vigor</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>virility</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7291', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%9B%E5%81%A5', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:57:29.026215Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.696865Z'),
(7292, 'vocabulary', '唯', '唯', 'The kanji means <kanji>solely</kanji>, and this means <vocabulary>solely</vocabulary>, but also other related things like <vocabulary>only</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>merely</vocabulary>. They''re all pretty close, so try to remember a few!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7292', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%94%AF', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:57:34.618935Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.708816Z'),
(7293, 'vocabulary', '唯一', '唯一', 'It''s <kanji>solely</kanji> <kanji>one</kanji>. The <vocabulary>only</vocabulary> one. The <vocabulary>sole</vocabulary> one. Totally <vocabulary>unique</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7293', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%94%AF%E4%B8%80', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:57:41.133137Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.719824Z'),
(7294, 'vocabulary', '唯物論', '唯物論', 'The <kanji>solely</kanji> <kanji>things</kanji> <kanji>theory</kanji> is the theory that only things are important. This is also known as <vocabulary>materialism</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7294', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%94%AF%E7%89%A9%E8%AB%96', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:57:49.004686Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(7295, 'vocabulary', '壇', '壇', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7295', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%87', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:57:54.524313Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.743615Z'),
(7296, 'vocabulary', '花壇', '花壇', 'A <kanji>flower</kanji> <kanji>podium</kanji> is a podium of flowers, put up higher than the ground. This is a <vocabulary>flower bed</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7296', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B1%E5%A3%87', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:58:00.557551Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:20:54.638732Z'),
(7297, 'vocabulary', '仏壇', '仏壇', 'A <kanji>buddha</kanji> <kanji>podium</kanji> is the podium on which you put Buddha. This is a <vocabulary>Buddhist altar</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7297', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8F%E5%A3%87', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:58:05.39502Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T20:01:27.526431Z'),
(7298, 'vocabulary', '尼僧', '尼僧', 'A <kanji>nun</kanji> <kanji>priest</kanji> is a <vocabulary>nun</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>priestess</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7298', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%BC%E5%83%A7', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:58:10.923837Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.781324Z'),
(7299, 'vocabulary', '尼', '尼', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7299', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%BC', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:58:15.938699Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.79366Z'),
(7300, 'vocabulary', '気概', '気概', 'A <kanji>spirit</kanji> of <kanji>approximation</kanji> is the approximation of spirit. My approximation of your spirit? I think you have a <vocabulary>strong spirit</vocabulary>. I think you have <vocabulary>backbone</vocabulary>.

気概 is a formal word mostly used in writing. It refers to someone''s strong spirit in the face of challenges. It conveys a sense of courage and moral strength, often associated with pride and resilience in difficult situations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7300', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%97%E6%A6%82', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:58:20.813251Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(7301, 'vocabulary', '概算', '概算', 'An <kanji>approximation</kanji> of the <kanji>calculation</kanji> is an <vocabulary>approximation</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>rough estimate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7301', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A6%82%E7%AE%97', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:58:25.508021Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T15:37:55.121141Z'),
(7302, 'vocabulary', '概念', '概念', 'An <kanji>approximation</kanji> of <kanji>thought</kanji> is just thinking up the <vocabulary>general idea</vocabulary>. The <vocabulary>concept</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>notion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7302', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A6%82%E5%BF%B5', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:58:31.00066Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.831104Z'),
(7303, 'vocabulary', '概要', '概要', 'An <kanji>approximation</kanji> of what we <kanji>need</kanji> is my <vocabulary>outline</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>summary</vocabulary>.

概要 is a formal word that refers to a brief overview of key points, often used in documents, reports, or presentations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7303', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A6%82%E8%A6%81', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:58:36.471162Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:28:03.237353Z'),
(7304, 'vocabulary', '大概', '大概', 'A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>approximation</kanji> is when I say something <vocabulary>in general</vocabulary>. I''m <vocabulary>mainly</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>mostly</vocabulary> telling everything.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7304', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E6%A6%82', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:58:44.854689Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(7305, 'vocabulary', '一概に', '一概に', 'We''re moving towards <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>approximation</kanji>. It used to be more approximations, but now it''s just one, and because it''s just one it''s not really an approximation anymore. It''s <vocabulary>unconditionally</vocabulary> set. It''s set <vocabulary>as a rule</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7305', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%A6%82%E3%81%AB', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:58:50.360165Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.871037Z'),
(7306, 'vocabulary', '浸る', '浸る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>immersed</kanji>. Things that are immersed are described by verbs like <vocabulary>to be soaked in</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be submerged</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be flooded</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7306', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B8%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:59:08.375137Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.883426Z'),
(7307, 'vocabulary', '浸水', '浸水', '<kanji>Immersed</kanji> in <kanji>water</kanji>. That sounds like <vocabulary>submersion</vocabulary>. Possibly like a <vocabulary>flood</vocabulary>, even.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7307', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B8%E6%B0%B4', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:59:13.534582Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.894855Z'),
(7308, 'vocabulary', '侵食', '侵食', 'Something that <kanji>invade</kanji>s and <kanji>eat</kanji>s away at something is something that causes <vocabulary>erosion</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>corrosion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7308', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%B5%E9%A3%9F', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:59:19.166023Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T00:07:07.736307Z'),
(7309, 'vocabulary', '淡い', '淡い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>faint</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>faint</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7309', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%A1%E3%81%84', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:59:24.949562Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.918298Z'),
(7310, 'vocabulary', '淡水', '淡水', 'A <kanji>faint</kanji> <kanji>water</kanji> is water that''s not thick with salt. It''s <vocabulary>fresh water</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7310', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%A1%E6%B0%B4', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:59:30.36862Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.93043Z'),
(7311, 'vocabulary', '煮る', '煮る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. 

The kanji itself means <kanji>boil</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to boil something</vocabulary>. This is a transitive verb, so it''s used when you put something into a pot and boil it up. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7311', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%AE%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:59:37.027819Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.942368Z'),
(7312, 'vocabulary', '雑煮', '雑煮', 'A <kanji>random</kanji> <kanji>boil</kanji> isn''t all that random. It''s specifically the <vocabulary>new year''s dish</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>mochi soup</vocabulary>, eaten on new years. Old people and children tend to die every year from this (choking on the mochi), so be  careful if you eat it! Maybe the reason it''s called "random boil" is because someone decided to boil a bunch of random things (like mochi) and then it became a new years dish later on. Who knows. That information isn''t even known by the ancients.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7312', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%91%E7%85%AE', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:59:48.280022Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.956892Z'),
(7313, 'vocabulary', '煮物', '煮物', '<kanji>Boil</kanji>ed <kanji>thing</kanji>s turn into <vocabulary>boiled foods</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>stewed foods</vocabulary>. You can eat anything, if it''s boiled long enough.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7313', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%AE%E7%89%A9', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T17:59:54.555073Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(7314, 'vocabulary', '覆る', '覆る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>capsize</kanji> but the verb version is what happens when a plan or idea capsizes. It going <vocabulary>to be overturned</vocabulary>. It was a bad idea, go back, don''t do it or we''ll all sink!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7314', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%86%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:00:25.523708Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.982984Z'),
(7315, 'vocabulary', '覆面', '覆面', '<kanji>Cover</kanji> your <kanji>face</kanji>. Not with your hands or a puppy, but with a <vocabulary>mask</vocabulary>! Okay, I guess you could cover it with a puppy mask, but that''s all you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7315', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%86%E9%9D%A2', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:00:30.915627Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:45.994504Z'),
(7316, 'vocabulary', '転覆', '転覆', 'It <kanji>revolve</kanji>s then <kanji>capsize</kanji>s. When a ship revolves onto its back and then capsizes, this causes <vocabulary>capsizing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7316', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%A2%E8%A6%86', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:00:36.177545Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.005136Z'),
(7317, 'vocabulary', '謀る', '謀る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>conspire</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to conspire</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to plot</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7317', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AC%80%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:00:41.402873Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.018269Z'),
(7318, 'vocabulary', '無謀', '無謀', 'The <kanji>conspir</kanji>ing I''m doing? <kanji>Nothing</kanji>... that''s how much. If I don''t conspire, I don''t think about my actions. If I don''t think, then I am just being <vocabulary>reckless</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>thoughtless</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7318', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E8%AC%80', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:00:49.076794Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.032091Z'),
(7319, 'vocabulary', '陰謀', '陰謀', 'Under the <kanji>shade</kanji> people <kanji>conspire</kanji>. When people do this, <vocabulary>conspiracy</vocabulary> appears.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7319', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%B0%E8%AC%80', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:00:54.086272Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.045766Z'),
(7320, 'vocabulary', '陶器', '陶器', 'A <kanji>pottery</kanji> <kanji>container</kanji> is <vocabulary>pottery</vocabulary>. It''s <vocabulary>ceramics</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7320', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%B6%E5%99%A8', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:00:59.302854Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T17:20:01.534933Z'),
(7321, 'vocabulary', '陶芸', '陶芸', '<kanji>Pottery</kanji> <kanji>art</kanji> is making pottery. In other words, it''s <vocabulary>ceramic art</vocabulary> or simply <vocabulary>ceramics</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7321', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%B6%E8%8A%B8', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:01:11.457414Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T17:20:50.732309Z'),
(7322, 'vocabulary', '隔離', '隔離', '<kanji>Isolate</kanji> and <kanji>detach</kanji>ed from everything else. This thing (or person) is in <vocabulary>isolation</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>quarantine</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7322', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%94%E9%9B%A2', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:01:19.878598Z', NULL, '2026-02-19T14:31:49.726046Z'),
(7323, 'vocabulary', '隔週', '隔週', '<kanji>Isolate</kanji> this <kanji>week</kanji> from next week, and do that over and over. That way we have <vocabulary>every other week</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7323', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%94%E9%80%B1', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:01:29.466752Z', NULL, '2026-02-19T14:31:49.726046Z'),
(7324, 'vocabulary', '間隔', '間隔', 'I''d like to <kanji>isolate</kanji> this <kanji>interval of time</kanji>. Take it, make it separate, and then look at it. Now there is a <vocabulary>space</vocabulary> there. An <vocabulary>interval</vocabulary>, if you will.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7324', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%93%E9%9A%94', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:01:36.809318Z', NULL, '2026-02-19T14:31:49.726046Z'),
(7325, 'vocabulary', '隔月', '隔月', 'If you <kanji>isolate</kanji> this <kanji>month</kanji> from next month, and then do that over and over again, you''ll be looking at <vocabulary>every other month</vocabulary> isolated from the other months.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7325', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%94%E6%9C%88', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:01:42.294784Z', NULL, '2026-02-19T14:31:49.726046Z'),
(7326, 'vocabulary', '隔てる', '隔てる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>isolate</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to isolate</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to partition</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to separate</vocabulary>. You get the idea, anyways.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7326', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%94%E3%81%A6%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:01:48.806167Z', NULL, '2026-02-19T14:31:49.726046Z'),
(7327, 'vocabulary', '遠征', '遠征', 'A <kanji>far</kanji> distance we must travel to <kanji>subjugate</kanji> our enemies. This is a long <vocabulary>expedition</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>campaign</vocabulary> to do just that!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7327', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%A0%E5%BE%81', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:01:54.794329Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.148744Z'),
(7328, 'vocabulary', '征服', '征服', 'I will <kanji>subjugate</kanji> everyone to wear the <kanji>clothes</kanji> I want them to wear. They will wear my uniform, for they will be under my <vocabulary>subjugation</vocabulary>. I will <vocabulary>conquest</vocabulary> them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7328', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%81%E6%9C%8D', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:02:22.297287Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.162233Z'),
(7329, 'vocabulary', '陛下', '陛下', 'Your <kanji>highness</kanji> I am so <kanji>below</kanji> you because <vocabulary>your majesty</vocabulary> is so great and amazing!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7329', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%9B%E4%B8%8B', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:02:28.787153Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(7330, 'vocabulary', '俗語', '俗語', '<kanji>Vulgar</kanji> <kanji>language</kanji> isn''t necessarily bad words, or something like that. It''s just rougher and not as standard as standard language. It is <vocabulary>slang</vocabulary>.

俗語 is a formal term for slang, often used in writing. If you want to talk about "slang" more casually, you''d simply say スラング.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7330', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%97%E8%AA%9E', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:02:42.881537Z', NULL, '2026-03-07T17:49:05.230084Z'),
(7331, 'vocabulary', '民俗', '民俗', 'The <kanji>peoples</kanji> who are <kanji>vulgar</kanji> aren''t refined city folk. They''re people with <vocabulary>folk customs</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>ethnic customs</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7331', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%91%E4%BF%97', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:02:58.120251Z', NULL, '2026-03-07T17:49:05.230084Z'),
(7332, 'vocabulary', '俗', '俗', 'Things that are <kanji>vulgar</kanji> are things like <vocabulary>slang</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>local manners</vocabulary>, and just plain <vocabulary>common</vocabulary> stuff.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7332', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%97', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:03:11.878273Z', NULL, '2026-03-07T17:49:05.230084Z'),
(7333, 'vocabulary', '桑', '桑', 'The kanji means <kanji>mulberry</kanji>, so this means <vocabulary>mulberry</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>mulberry tree</vocabulary>.

Note that the fruit of the mulberry is more commonly known as 桑の実.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7333', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A1%91', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:03:17.874386Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.218399Z'),
(7334, 'vocabulary', '桑原', '桑原', 'A <kanji>mulberry</kanji> <kanji>field</kanji> is a <vocabulary>mulberry field</vocabulary>.

Based on an old belief that lightning does not strike mulberry fields, 桑原 (often recited twice, as in "桑原桑原") can also be used as a charm to ward off undesirable events, though it''s pretty old-fashioned.

Apart from that use, 桑原 now mainly refers to the family name <vocabulary>Kuwabara</vocabulary>, like one of the main protagonists of the anime and manga series 幽遊白書.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7334', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A1%91%E5%8E%9F', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:03:25.515077Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.240497Z'),
(7335, 'vocabulary', '桑畑', '桑畑', 'A <kanji>mulberry</kanji> <kanji>field</kanji> is a <vocabulary>mulberry field</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>mulberry plantation</vocabulary>. This is more like a commercial / organized mulberry growing operation, so not some random field of mulberries.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7335', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A1%91%E7%95%91', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:03:31.001298Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.252738Z'),
(7336, 'vocabulary', '潤う', '潤う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>watered</kanji>. When something is watered it becomes moist, right? That''s why this verb is <vocabulary>to be moist</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be moistened</vocabulary>.

潤う refers to things being well-moisturized, like skin or eyes. Think of them as being enriched with moisture. That''s why the meaning also extends to financial prosperity — moisture enriches the body, and financial gain enriches your life or business. So this word also means <vocabulary>to be prosperous</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7336', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%A4%E3%81%86', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:03:35.918835Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.264498Z'),
(7337, 'vocabulary', '利潤', '利潤', 'The <kanji>profit</kanji>s that I''m <kanji>watered</kanji> with are pouring all over me. That''s my <vocabulary>profit</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>returns</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7337', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A9%E6%BD%A4', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:03:41.09319Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.276716Z'),
(7338, 'vocabulary', '数珠', '数珠', 'A <kanji>number</kanji> of <kanji>pearls</kanji>, put together on a string. This refers to a <vocabulary>rosary</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>prayer beads</vocabulary>. You''ll see these a lot in Japan, for sale or with monks wearing them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7338', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B0%E7%8F%A0', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:03:46.920861Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.041761Z'),
(7339, 'vocabulary', '珠算', '珠算', 'A <kanji>pearl</kanji> <kanji>calculation</kanji> is calculations you do with pearls. If you think about using pearls to calculate something, it''s going to look a lot like using an abacus. That''s why this calculation is <vocabulary>calculation with abacus</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7339', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%A0%E7%AE%97', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:03:52.993012Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T15:37:55.121141Z'),
(7340, 'vocabulary', '真珠', '真珠', 'A <kanji>reality</kanji> <kanji>pearl</kanji> is a real pearl! That''s just a <vocabulary>pearl</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7340', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%9F%E7%8F%A0', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:03:59.640122Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:50:20.056509Z'),
(7341, 'vocabulary', '衰える', '衰える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>decline</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to decline</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7341', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%B0%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:04:14.502735Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.325176Z'),
(7342, 'vocabulary', '老衰', '老衰', 'An <kanji>elderly</kanji> person in <kanji>decline</kanji> is someone who is old and in decline. They are experiencing <vocabulary>senility</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>senile decay</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7342', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%81%E8%A1%B0', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:04:20.372705Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.336458Z'),
(7343, 'vocabulary', '衰退', '衰退', 'Something that <kanji>decline</kanji>s and <kanji>retreats</kanji> from decline is something that''s on a <vocabulary>decline</vocabulary>. It''s also <vocabulary>decay</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7343', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%B0%E9%80%80', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:04:25.44667Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.347391Z'),
(7344, 'vocabulary', '推奨', '推奨', 'I''d like to <kanji>infer</kanji> and <kanji>encourage</kanji> this item to you. It is my <vocabulary>recommendation</vocabulary>. It is my <vocabulary>endorsement</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7344', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A8%E5%A5%A8', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:04:32.921957Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:26:11.344562Z'),
(7345, 'vocabulary', '奨学金', '奨学金', '<kanji>Encourage</kanji> <kanji>study</kanji> <kanji>money</kanji> is money that you give to a kid to encourage him to study. That comes in the form of a <vocabulary>scholarship</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>stipend</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7345', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%A8%E5%AD%A6%E9%87%91', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:04:45.994744Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.370518Z'),
(7346, 'vocabulary', '劣化', '劣化', 'Your <kanji>inferiority</kanji> has <kanji>change</kanji>d. In fact, it''s getting worse and worse, changing by the minute. Each minute that goes by is another minute where you''re getting worse. This is <vocabulary>deterioration</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7346', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%A3%E5%8C%96', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:04:55.972816Z', NULL, '2025-11-20T03:47:15.21122Z'),
(7347, 'vocabulary', '劣悪', '劣悪', '<kanji>Inferiority</kanji> is <kanji>bad</kanji>. It is <vocabulary>inferiority</vocabulary>, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7347', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%A3%E6%82%AA', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:05:01.666099Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.393439Z'),
(7348, 'vocabulary', '劣等感', '劣等感', '<kanji>Inferiority</kanji> is what I feel because I don''t have an <kanji>equal</kanji> <kanji>feeling</kanji> with you. This is due to my <vocabulary>inferiority complex</vocabulary>, my <vocabulary>sense of inferiority </vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7348', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%A3%E7%AD%89%E6%84%9F', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:05:09.073631Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(7349, 'vocabulary', '劣る', '劣る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>inferiority</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be inferior to</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7349', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%A3%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:05:15.263037Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.431279Z'),
(7350, 'vocabulary', '勘', '勘', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Also could mean <vocabulary>perception</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>hunch</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7350', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%98', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:05:23.051058Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.443907Z'),
(7351, 'vocabulary', '勘案', '勘案', 'Using our <kanji>intuition</kanji> we will come up with a <kanji>plan</kanji>. We''ll be <vocabulary>taking into consideration</vocabulary> the information we have along with our intuition, so don''t worry.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7351', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%98%E6%A1%88', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:05:29.198861Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.468182Z'),
(7352, 'vocabulary', '勘弁', '勘弁', '<kanji>Intuition</kanji> from my <kanji>dialect</kanji> tells me that I should <vocabulary>pardon</vocabulary> you. Give you <vocabulary>forgiveness</vocabulary>. You''re lucky I speak this dialect.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7352', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%98%E5%BC%81', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:05:36.72569Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.481347Z'),
(7353, 'vocabulary', '割り勘', '割り勘', 'I will <kanji>divide</kanji> the <kanji>intuition</kanji> up. For some reason when I say "intuition" I mean "money" because this is referring to <vocabulary>splitting the cost</vocabulary>, like when you''re at a restaurant.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7353', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%B2%E3%82%8A%E5%8B%98', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:05:50.768499Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:40:20.776264Z'),
(7354, 'vocabulary', '勘違い', '勘違い', 'An <kanji>intuition</kanji> <kanji>difference</kanji> is a <vocabulary>misunderstanding</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>wrong guess</vocabulary>. Sorry! My intuition was off.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7354', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%98%E9%81%95%E3%81%84', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:06:00.056328Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T07:54:52.581508Z'),
(7355, 'vocabulary', '王妃', '王妃', 'When a <kanji>king</kanji> takes a <kanji>royal consort</kanji>, she becomes the <vocabulary>queen</vocabulary>.

王妃 is a word for the wife of a reigning king. She''s a queen because of who she married, not because she has any power herself. The more technical term for that is <vocabulary>queen consort</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7355', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8E%8B%E5%A6%83', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:06:09.306393Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T02:11:43.55783Z'),
(7356, 'vocabulary', '皇太子妃', '皇太子妃', 'Remember how 皇太子 is "crown prince"? Well, when a crown prince gets married his royal consort is the <vocabulary>crown princess</vocabulary>.

皇太子妃 specifically refers to the wife of the Crown Prince, rather than a woman who is the heir to the throne herself. Technically speaking, she is a <vocabulary>crown princess consort</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7356', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%87%E5%A4%AA%E5%AD%90%E5%A6%83', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:06:15.810101Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T00:00:27.133218Z'),
(7357, 'vocabulary', '〜妃', '〜妃', 'The kanji means <kanji>royal consort</kanji>, so adding it to someone''s name gives you their royal style: "<vocabulary>Princess</vocabulary> ______," or, in some cases, "<vocabulary>Queen</vocabulary> ______."

〜妃 essentially refers to the wife of a crown prince, emperor, or other royal family member. These days, you''ll almost always see it used with princesses, like ダイアナ妃 (Princess Diana) or キャサリン妃 (Princess Catherine), but in historical contexts you''ll occasionally see it used with queen consorts and empress consorts too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7357', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%A6%83', 49, 1, '2013-08-19T18:06:29.895236Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T02:27:51.519767Z'),
(7358, 'vocabulary', '溶ける', '溶ける', '溶かす is "to melt something" and that makes 溶ける <vocabulary>to melt</vocabulary> (where something just melts, and you''re not doing the melting).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7358', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%B6%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:42:04.687139Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.57244Z'),
(7359, 'vocabulary', '悪賢い', '悪賢い', 'Someone who is <kanji>bad</kanji> but also <kanji>clever</kanji> (賢い) is someone who is using their cleverness for evil. When you refer to clever evil people, usually you describe them not as "clever" but as <vocabulary>devious</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>cunning</vocabulary>, or other things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7359', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E8%B3%A2%E3%81%84', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:42:11.448325Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.584811Z'),
(7360, 'vocabulary', '輝き', '輝き', 'If 輝く is "to shine" or "to glitter" think about the noun version of this. This would be <vocabulary>radiance</vocabulary>. Radiance can come in the form of shining and glittering.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7360', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%9D%E3%81%8D', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:42:34.587157Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.595903Z'),
(7361, 'vocabulary', '蟹', '蟹', 'The meaning is the same as the kanji''s meaning, <kanji>crab</kanji>. If you know this word, and you know the word 鰐 as well... then, well, you know something, though I''m not sure what that something is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7361', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9F%B9', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:42:34.601387Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.606973Z'),
(7362, 'vocabulary', '鰐', '鰐', 'The great <kanji>alligator</kanji>! This can mean both <vocabulary>alligator</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>crocodile</vocabulary>, because really, they''re not so different, are they? No crabigator, that''s for sure.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7362', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%B0%90', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:42:45.21138Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.628554Z'),
(7363, 'vocabulary', '鬱陶しい', '鬱陶しい', '<kanji>Gloom</kanji> <kanji>pottery</kanji> is... well, it''s <vocabulary>gloomy</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>depressing</vocabulary>!

This word can also mean <vocabulary>annoying</vocabulary>. If something or someone always makes you feel gloomy, you''ll probably think it''s annoying, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7363', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AC%B1%E9%99%B6%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:42:54.936747Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T17:20:01.534933Z'),
(7364, 'vocabulary', '峰', '峰', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Can also be <vocabulary>peak</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>ridge</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7364', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B3%B0', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:43:03.57113Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T17:29:40.36544Z'),
(7365, 'vocabulary', '連峰', '連峰', '<kanji>Take along</kanji> this <kanji>summit</kanji> and move it across a range, visiting more summits. One summit after another you take the summit along to. By covering this many summits, you notice there''s a string of mountains. This is a <vocabulary>mountain range</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7365', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%A3%E5%B3%B0', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:43:46.438234Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.678035Z'),
(7366, 'vocabulary', '巧い', '巧い', 'You''re so <kanji>adept</kanji>! That makes you <vocabulary>adept</vocabulary> at that thing. <vocabulary>Skillful</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>clever</vocabulary> are other ways to do it too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7366', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A7%E3%81%84', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:43:59.039494Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T04:46:23.583178Z'),
(7367, 'vocabulary', '巧妙', '巧妙', 'Something that is <kanji>adept</kanji> but <kanji>peculiar</kanji> in how adept it is would have to be something truly <vocabulary>clever</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>ingenious</vocabulary>. Like a clever scam or trick.

巧妙 is typically used to describe deceptive actions, techniques, or methods that are not only <vocabulary>skillful</vocabulary> but also somewhat sly or crafty. It indicates a level of sophistication, often involving clever scheming or manipulation. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7367', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A7%E5%A6%99', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:44:03.91999Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T04:46:34.339046Z'),
(7368, 'vocabulary', '風邪', '風邪', 'A <kanji>wind</kanji> that is <kanji>wicked</kanji> comes and blows on you. It''s so cold, and ends up getting you sick. You now have a <vocabulary>cold</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7368', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A2%A8%E9%82%AA', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:44:10.343947Z', NULL, '2025-07-01T19:41:45.398894Z'),
(7369, 'vocabulary', '邪魔', '邪魔', 'A <kanji>wicked</kanji> <kanji>devil</kanji>! Ugh, I hate those things. They''re always a <vocabulary>hindrance</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>intrusion</vocabulary>. Just imagine a wicked devil hanging out in your house, eating your food, and sleeping on your bed. What a hindrance.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7369', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%82%AA%E9%AD%94', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:44:18.935206Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.732542Z'),
(7370, 'vocabulary', '邪', '邪', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7370', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%82%AA', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:44:24.291892Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.747194Z'),
(7371, 'vocabulary', '下駄', '下駄', 'Look <kanji>below</kanji> you. These things carry your <kanji>burdensome</kanji> weight. All of it. These are the shoes you wear, though in this case they are specifically <vocabulary>geta</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>Japanese wooden clogs</vocabulary>. The clogs themselves are kind of burdensome and hard to wear too, actually, so you can think of it that way as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7371', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E9%A7%84', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:44:29.696765Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(7372, 'vocabulary', '駄目', '駄目', 'If something is <kanji>burdensome</kanji> to the <kanji>eye</kanji> (when you look at it), you know that that thing is <vocabulary>not good</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>hopeless</vocabulary>. Just look at that burdensome thing with your eye. This can also mean <vocabulary>not allowed</vocabulary>. Parents say this to their kids all the time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7372', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%84%E7%9B%AE', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:44:48.232579Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.773261Z'),
(7373, 'vocabulary', '無駄', '無駄', '<kanji>Nothing</kanji> but <kanji>burdensome</kanji>. That''s all you get out of doing this task. The <vocabulary>futility</vocabulary> of it is ridiculous. The <vocabulary>uselessness</vocabulary> is huge.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7373', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E9%A7%84', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:44:54.182369Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.785916Z'),
(7374, 'vocabulary', '唐突', '唐突', 'A <kanji>sudden</kanji> <kanji>stab</kanji> is <vocabulary>abrupt</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>unexpected</vocabulary>. Quick, dodge!!

唐突 describes sudden actions or events that catch people off guard.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7374', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%94%90%E7%AA%81', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:45:00.249758Z', NULL, '2025-08-27T00:04:28.706513Z'),
(7375, 'vocabulary', '法廷', '法廷', 'The <kanji>law</kanji> <kanji>courts</kanji> are the <vocabulary>court</vocabulary>s you visit to get law distributed. The room in which you do this in is a <vocabulary>courtroom</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7375', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%95%E5%BB%B7', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:45:05.178493Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.812753Z'),
(7376, 'vocabulary', '宮廷', '宮廷', '<kanji>Shinto shrine</kanji> <kanji>courts</kanji> are the courts of the Shinto shrines. Shinto is often associated with the emperor, which is why this the <vocabulary>imperial court</vocabulary>. <vocabulary>Royal court</vocabulary> will do as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7376', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%AE%E5%BB%B7', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:45:09.876233Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.826579Z'),
(7377, 'vocabulary', '名簿', '名簿', 'A <kanji>name</kanji> <kanji>record book</kanji> is a book that contains names. That is a <vocabulary>register of names</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>list of names</vocabulary>. Is your name on the list?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7377', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8D%E7%B0%BF', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:45:16.263979Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(7378, 'vocabulary', '帳簿', '帳簿', 'A <kanji>notebook</kanji> that is also a <kanji>record book</kanji> is an <vocabulary>account book</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>register</vocabulary>, showing the records of all the transactions that have gone through.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7378', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%B3%E7%B0%BF', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:45:21.658274Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.852528Z'),
(7379, 'vocabulary', '表彰', '表彰', 'You want people to <kanji>express</kanji> how they feel about you in a <kanji>clear</kanji> manner, so you demand they give you <vocabulary>public acknowledgement</vocabulary>. You want clear, concise <vocabulary>public recognition</vocabulary> for all of your amazing talents, skills, and for reaching level 50 of WaniKani. Maybe they''ll throw you a parade!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7379', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%A8%E5%BD%B0', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:45:35.177717Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:26:46.193094Z'),
(7380, 'vocabulary', '漫画', '漫画', '<kanji>Comic</kanji> <kanji>drawing</kanji>s are <vocabulary>manga</vocabulary>, a.k.a. <vocabulary>Japanese comics</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7380', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%AB%E7%94%BB', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:45:46.079617Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.87853Z'),
(7381, 'vocabulary', '漫才', '漫才', 'A <kanji>comic</kanji> <kanji>talent</kanji>! And not just one, but two comic talents, because this means a <vocabulary>comic dialogue</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>comedy double act</vocabulary>. 

More specifically, 漫才 refers to a double act that features a "funny man" (ボケ) and "straight man" (ツッコミ) in a comic dialogue routine. 漫才 is one of the most popular forms of comedy in Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7381', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%AB%E6%89%8D', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:45:55.236456Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.892392Z'),
(7382, 'vocabulary', '訂正', '訂正', 'When you <kanji>revise</kanji> something to make it <kanji>correct</kanji> you are doing a <vocabulary>correction</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>revision</vocabulary>.

訂正 refers to officially correcting mistakes in written or spoken content, like documents, reports, or statements. It can describe both the act of correcting and the correction itself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7382', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%82%E6%AD%A3', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:46:01.094594Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.90684Z'),
(7383, 'vocabulary', '改訂版', '改訂版', 'The <kanji>renew</kanji>ed and <kanji>revise</kanji>d <kanji>edition</kanji> of something is the <vocabulary>revised edition</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7383', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%B9%E8%A8%82%E7%89%88', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:46:08.910008Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:11:10.895336Z'),
(7384, 'vocabulary', '諮問', '諮問', 'When you need to <kanji>consult</kanji> someone on a <kanji>problem</kanji>, you schedule a <vocabulary>consultation</vocabulary> with them. 

諮問 is a very formal word for consultation or seeking advice, usually in the context of a government body or authority asking a council for their opinion. 
政府が委員会に諮問する means the government refers a matter to a committee for deliberation or advice, for example. This also appears in names for advisory committees (諮問委員会).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7384', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%AE%E5%95%8F', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:46:14.70372Z', NULL, '2026-03-17T23:15:12.118146Z'),
(7385, 'vocabulary', '諮る', '諮る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>consult</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to consult with</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to confer with</vocabulary>.

諮る is a very formal word that refers to consulting someone for advice or approval — usually a group, council, or authority. You''ll see it in political or business contexts, like 計画を審議会に諮る (to submit a plan to the committee for consideration). ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7385', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%AE%E3%82%8B', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:46:20.799375Z', NULL, '2026-03-17T23:10:32.17368Z'),
(7386, 'vocabulary', '感銘', '感銘', 'A <kanji>feeling</kanji> of <kanji>inscription</kanji> is the feeling one gets when something so strong happens that it''s like the feeling gets inscribed into your soul. This is something that left a very <vocabulary>deep impression</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7386', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E9%8A%98', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:46:27.521545Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(7387, 'vocabulary', '銘柄', '銘柄', 'The <kanji>inscription</kanji>''s <kanji>pattern</kanji> shows what <vocabulary>brand</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>make</vocabulary> this item is. If the pattern of the inscription is an apple, you''ll know the brand is Apple. If it''s a Window, you know it''s Microsoft. Etc.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7387', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%98%E6%9F%84', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:46:32.879566Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.970783Z'),
(7388, 'vocabulary', '銘々', '銘々', 'You look at this <kanji>inscription</kanji> then you look at that <kanji>inscription</kanji> and you notice that <vocabulary>each</vocabulary> one is an <vocabulary>individual</vocabulary> inscription.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7388', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%98%E3%80%85', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:46:38.995483Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(7389, 'vocabulary', '堰', '堰', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7389', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%B0', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:46:46.413683Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:46.99546Z'),
(7390, 'vocabulary', '堤', '堤', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7390', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%A4', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:46:52.356106Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.008349Z'),
(7391, 'vocabulary', '堤防', '堤防', 'The <kanji>embankment</kanji> <kanji>prevent</kanji>s the water from going over the edge. It''s an <vocabulary>embankment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7391', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%A4%E9%98%B2', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:47:11.970181Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.020567Z'),
(7392, 'vocabulary', '漂う', '漂う', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>drift</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to drift</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to waft</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7392', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%82%E3%81%86', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:47:17.157474Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.04424Z'),
(7393, 'vocabulary', '漂流', '漂流', '<kanji>Drifting</kanji> down the <kanji>stream</kanji>. Something that is doing this is <vocabulary>drifting</vocabulary>. It could also be the <vocabulary>drift</vocabulary> of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7393', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%82%E6%B5%81', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:47:23.835789Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.057996Z'),
(7394, 'vocabulary', '漂着', '漂着', 'Something has <kanji>drift</kanji>ed to the point where it has <kanji>arrive</kanji>d. Where do drifting things arrive? They arrive on shore (as they drift on top of water). That makes this word <vocabulary>drifting ashore</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7394', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%82%E7%9D%80', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:47:29.587898Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(7395, 'vocabulary', '翻意', '翻意', 'When you <kanji>flip</kanji> your <kanji>idea</kanji> of something, you''re changing how you think about it. That''s why this word is <vocabulary>change one''s mind</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7395', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BF%BB%E6%84%8F', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:47:36.78144Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.084163Z'),
(7396, 'vocabulary', '翻訳', '翻訳', '<kanji>Flip</kanji> the <kanji>translation</kanji> of a word from one language to another. That is the <vocabulary>translation</vocabulary> of that word (or phrase, etc.).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7396', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BF%BB%E8%A8%B3', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:47:41.992973Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.095101Z'),
(7397, 'vocabulary', '翻る', '翻る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. While the kanji means <kanji>flip</kanji>, the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to billow</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to flutter</vocabulary>, as in some kind of material (maybe a dress or a flag) billowing or fluttering in the wind. It can also mean <vocabulary>to suddenly change</vocabulary> your mind, or your attitude, etc.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7397', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BF%BB%E3%82%8B', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:47:54.233941Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.107312Z'),
(7398, 'vocabulary', '軌跡', '軌跡', '<kanji>Rut</kanji> <kanji>traces</kanji> left in the road are <vocabulary>track</vocabulary>s that mark the <vocabulary>trajectory</vocabulary> of a vehicle’s wheels.

This can also be used figuratively to refer to leaving or following some sort of trace, or to describe someone’s or something’s trajectory, among other things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7398', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8C%E8%B7%A1', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:48:00.519064Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.706403Z'),
(7399, 'vocabulary', '軌道', '軌道', 'A <kanji>rut</kanji> in the <kanji>road</kanji> is for some reason an <vocabulary>orbit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>trajectory</vocabulary>. Maybe imagine a big ol'' rut in the road. You drive at it at very high speeds, hit it, and fly into the sky at a <vocabulary>trajectory</vocabulary> that will send you into <vocabulary>orbit</vocabulary>. Now you''re up in space, circling the earth, wishing you didn''t drive so fast.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7399', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8C%E9%81%93', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:48:06.80117Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.141207Z'),
(7400, 'vocabulary', '皇后', '皇后', 'The <kanji>emperor</kanji> <kanji>empress</kanji> is the emperor''s wife. That''s the <vocabulary>Japanese Empress</vocabulary>, specifically, since 皇 usually refers to the Japanese emperor.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7400', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%87%E5%90%8E', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:48:12.062915Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.152444Z'),
(7401, 'vocabulary', '興奮', '興奮', 'My <kanji>interest</kanji> is <kanji>stirred up</kanji>! I''m feeling <vocabulary>excitement</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>stimulation</vocabulary> from all this stirring up, in fact!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7401', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%88%E5%A5%AE', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:48:17.939411Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.166091Z'),
(7402, 'vocabulary', '奮起', '奮起', 'I''m getting all <kanji>stirred up</kanji>, which has caused me to <kanji>wake up</kanji> (figuratively). I''m <vocabulary>rousing</vocabulary> myself to tackle this challenge!

奮起 is a formal word for <vocabulary>rousing someone</vocabulary> or oneself to action in challenging situations. It describes a moment when someone decides to gather their strength and face a task or problem head-on. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7402', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%AE%E8%B5%B7', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:48:25.132522Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T19:40:23.925513Z'),
(7403, 'vocabulary', '〜亭', '〜亭', 'If you see the kanji <kanji>restaurant</kanji> attached to a place name, there''s a good chance that it''s a <vocabulary>restaurant</vocabulary>, especially a <vocabulary>traditional Japanese restaurant</vocabulary>. It also crops up on the end of other places where you can rest, like <vocabulary>inn</vocabulary>s, those traditional <vocabulary>pavilion</vocabulary>s you see in Japanese parks and temple grounds, and <vocabulary>tea house</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7403', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E4%BA%AD', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:48:32.544159Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.192978Z'),
(7404, 'vocabulary', '仰ぐ', '仰ぐ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>look up to</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to look up at</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to look up to</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7404', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%B0%E3%81%90', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:48:37.537067Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.214376Z'),
(7405, 'vocabulary', '仰々しい', '仰々しい', 'Someone tries to make you <kanji>look up to</kanji> them... twice. That''s too many times to look up to someone. When this happens, this person must be <vocabulary>pompous</vocabulary>.

仰々しい is a relatively formal word often used to describe something that feels <vocabulary>exaggerated</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>excessively grand</vocabulary>. It can refer to a speech, ceremony, or behavior that comes across as overly formal, showy, or dramatic.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7405', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%B0%E3%80%85%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:48:49.513829Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(7406, 'vocabulary', '仰天', '仰天', 'I <kanji>look up to</kanji> <kanji>heaven</kanji>, and I am <vocabulary>taken aback</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>amazed</vocabulary> by it. It''s just so amazing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7406', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%B0%E5%A4%A9', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:48:54.703739Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.267346Z'),
(7407, 'vocabulary', '信仰', '信仰', 'I <kanji>believe</kanji> and <kanji>look up to</kanji> a higher power, for I have <vocabulary>religious faith</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7407', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%A1%E4%BB%B0', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:49:00.503825Z', NULL, '2025-11-16T19:02:11.093561Z'),
(7408, 'vocabulary', '伯', '伯', 'The <kanji>chief</kanji> of this district is that guy, the <vocabulary>count</vocabulary>. He''s also the <vocabulary>earl</vocabulary> of another district, and the <vocabulary>chief</vocabulary> of yet another.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7408', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%AF', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:49:05.38008Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.317144Z'),
(7409, 'vocabulary', '伯母', '伯母', 'Who is the <kanji>chief</kanji> <kanji>mother</kanji>? That''s your <vocabulary>aunt</vocabulary>. And this aunt is the older sister of your mom or dad because she is the chief mother. An aunt who is a younger sister of your parent is 叔母, which is another word for "aunt" you already know!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7409', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%AF%E6%AF%8D', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:49:16.626856Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.342862Z'),
(7410, 'vocabulary', '偶に', '偶に', 'Something you do <kanji>accidentally</kanji> is hopefully something you do <vocabulary>occasionally</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>once in a while</vocabulary>.

Also note that this word is usually written in hiragana. You''ll only <em>occasionally</em> see it in kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7410', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%B6%E3%81%AB', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:49:22.908029Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.361183Z'),
(7411, 'vocabulary', '偶然', '偶然', 'Something that happened <kanji>accidentally</kanji> by <kanji>nature</kanji> probably happened <vocabulary>by chance</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>unexpectedly</vocabulary>. It was just a <vocabulary>coincidence</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7411', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%B6%E7%84%B6', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:49:29.610233Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.401125Z'),
(7412, 'vocabulary', '配偶者', '配偶者', '<kanji>Distribute</kanji> yourself <kanji>accidentally</kanji> to <kanji>someone</kanji>. That person ended up becoming your <vocabulary>spouse</vocabulary>. You distributed yourself to one person a little too much, it seems.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7412', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%8D%E5%81%B6%E8%80%85', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:49:35.954244Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(7413, 'vocabulary', '淀川', '淀川', 'The <kanji>eddy</kanji> <kanji>river</kanji> isn''t so much a thing as it is a famous place. We''re talking about <vocabulary>Yodogawa</vocabulary>. It might be better to look at the reading to learn the meaning. Look up this Osaka Prefecture river if you''d like more info.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7413', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%80%E5%B7%9D', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:49:41.656504Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.467495Z'),
(7414, 'vocabulary', '古墳', '古墳', 'An <kanji>old</kanji> <kanji>tomb</kanji> is an <vocabulary>ancient tomb</vocabulary>. They''re mostly from the <vocabulary>Kofun</vocabulary> period, and indeed that''s where the period gets its name.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7414', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%A4%E5%A2%B3', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:49:51.507024Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.514453Z'),
(7415, 'vocabulary', '墳墓', '墳墓', 'A <kanji>tomb</kanji> <kanji>grave</kanji> is a <vocabulary>grave</vocabulary>... or it could be a <vocabulary>tomb</vocabulary>. Or maybe both.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7415', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%B3%E5%A2%93', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:49:59.119236Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.53793Z'),
(7416, 'vocabulary', '壮大', '壮大', 'It''s so <kanji>robust</kanji> and <kanji>big</kanji>! Things like this are <vocabulary>magnificent</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>grand</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>epic</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7416', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%AE%E5%A4%A7', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:50:05.833016Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(7417, 'vocabulary', '壮年', '壮年', 'Your <kanji>robust</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji>s are the best years of your life! This is the <vocabulary>prime of life</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7417', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%AE%E5%B9%B4', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:50:10.758468Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.577577Z'),
(7418, 'vocabulary', '壮行', '壮行', 'You want to make someone feel <kanji>robust</kanji> when they <kanji>go</kanji> away somewhere, so you give them a <vocabulary>send-off</vocabulary>. It''s just a nice way to say <vocabulary>farewell</vocabulary>!

壮行 is usually for celebratory send-offs and appears in words like 壮行会 (farewell party) or 壮行式 (pep rally).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7418', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%AE%E8%A1%8C', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:50:16.854633Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.593493Z'),
(7419, 'vocabulary', '〜把', '〜把', 'The kanji refers to a <kanji>bundle</kanji>, but this refers to a <vocabulary>counter for bundles</vocabulary>. Put this on a number, and you have "1 bundle" or "2 bundles" or even three, oh my!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7419', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%8A%8A', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:50:36.044637Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:22:31.647431Z'),
(7420, 'vocabulary', '把握', '把握', 'Using the <kanji>grasp</kanji> meaning of 把 and combining it with <kanji>grip</kanji>, you can tell you''re grasping at something and then gripping it. I usually grasp then grip at things flying at me, which makes this word mean <vocabulary>grasp</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>understand</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7420', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%8A%E6%8F%A1', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:50:41.034798Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:22:31.647431Z'),
(7421, 'vocabulary', '運搬', '運搬', 'To <kanji>carry</kanji> you and <kanji>transport</kanji> you. That''s a form of <vocabulary>transport</vocabulary>. Could also be a <vocabulary>carriage</vocabulary>, which is also a form of transport.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7421', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8B%E6%90%AC', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:50:45.760788Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.652366Z'),
(7422, 'vocabulary', '搬送', '搬送', 'Things that <kanji>transport</kanji> and <kanji>send</kanji> you places are forms of <vocabulary>transportation</vocabulary>.

搬送 is most often used in medical contexts to refer to the transportation of patients.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7422', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%AC%E9%80%81', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:50:51.602976Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:26:48.812324Z'),
(7423, 'vocabulary', '搬出', '搬出', '<kanji>Transport</kanji> and <kanji>exit</kanji> these goods from here (by transporting them). Take it away. It''s time to <vocabulary>carry out</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7423', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%AC%E5%87%BA', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:50:58.123838Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.694267Z'),
(7424, 'vocabulary', '液晶', '液晶', 'A <kanji>fluid</kanji> <kanji>crystal</kanji> is a <vocabulary>liquid crystal</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7424', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B6%B2%E6%99%B6', 43, 1, '2013-08-19T18:51:09.581043Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.711841Z'),
(7425, 'vocabulary', '水晶', '水晶', 'A <kanji>water</kanji> <kanji>crystal</kanji> is just a <vocabulary>crystal</vocabulary>. I guess crystals are clear and look kind of like water, yeah? That''s why it''s being referred to as a water crystal, making it really just a crystal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7425', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E6%99%B6', 43, 1, '2013-08-19T18:51:21.637841Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.731112Z'),
(7426, 'vocabulary', '洞', '洞', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7426', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%9E', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:51:27.230156Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:08:15.063999Z'),
(7427, 'vocabulary', '空洞', '空洞', 'A <kanji>sky</kanji> <kanji>cave</kanji> is a cave with sky (open space) in it. This is a <vocabulary>hollow</vocabulary>, but can also refer to a <vocabulary>cavity</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7427', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA%E6%B4%9E', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:51:34.057043Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.77031Z'),
(7428, 'vocabulary', '洞穴', '洞穴', 'A <kanji>cave</kanji> <kanji>hole</kanji> is a <vocabulary>cave</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>den</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7428', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%9E%E7%A9%B4', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:51:39.748008Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:10:42.563403Z'),
(7429, 'vocabulary', '生涯', '生涯', 'A <kanji>life</kanji> <kanji>horizon</kanji> is your life, which you''re looking out over, all the way to the very end. This is <vocabulary>one''s lifetime</vocabulary>, though it could also refer to <vocabulary>one''s career</vocabulary>. Basically, at the end of it something dies.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7429', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E6%B6%AF', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:51:45.822907Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.805679Z'),
(7430, 'vocabulary', '検疫', '検疫', 'We have to <kanji>examine</kanji> you during this <kanji>epidemic</kanji>... soooo, if you could come over here and just stay here for a long time, that''d be great. Yeah, it''s kind of a <vocabulary>quarantine</vocabulary>, sorry about that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7430', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A4%9C%E7%96%AB', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:51:50.985128Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.826738Z'),
(7431, 'vocabulary', '免疫', '免疫', 'To <kanji>excuse</kanji> yourself from the <kanji>epidemic</kanji>, you''ll have to have <vocabulary>immunity</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>immunization</vocabulary> from the disease.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7431', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%8D%E7%96%AB', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:51:56.833095Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.847376Z'),
(7432, 'vocabulary', '疫病', '疫病', 'An <kanji>epidemic</kanji> of <kanji>sick</kanji>ness is going to come from an <vocabulary>infectious disease</vocabulary>. Perhaps a <vocabulary>plague</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>epidemic</vocabulary> of some kind?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7432', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%AB%E7%97%85', 50, 1, '2013-08-19T18:52:03.56429Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.861697Z'),
(7433, 'vocabulary', '恨み', '恨み', '恨む is "to hold a grudge." This is the noun version, which is a <vocabulary>grudge</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>resentment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7433', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%A8%E3%81%BF', 49, 1, '2013-08-20T22:16:16.519509Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.876304Z'),
(7434, 'vocabulary', '哀れむ', '哀れむ', 'If something is <kanji>pathetic</kanji> then you should try <vocabulary>to pity</vocabulary> that thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7434', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%93%80%E3%82%8C%E3%82%80', 49, 1, '2013-08-20T22:16:17.716954Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.890739Z'),
(7435, 'vocabulary', '憩い', '憩い', 'If 憩う is to rest, then this noun version (I know it looks like an adjective, but it''s not) would be <vocabulary>rest</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7435', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%A9%E3%81%84', 49, 1, '2013-08-20T22:17:24.594724Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.904359Z'),
(7436, 'vocabulary', '脅す', '脅す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>threaten</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to threaten</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7436', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%85%E3%81%99', 50, 1, '2013-08-20T22:23:55.147273Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.918445Z'),
(7437, 'vocabulary', '飢え', '飢え', 'You''ve seen that 飢える is "to starve." The noun version is <vocabulary>hunger</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>starvation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7437', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%A2%E3%81%88', 50, 1, '2013-08-20T22:26:14.993497Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.932638Z'),
(7438, 'vocabulary', '尽くす', '尽くす', '尽きる is "to be exhausted". 尽くす is when you''re doing the exhausting of something, which makes it <vocabulary>to exhaust something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to run out</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>to devote oneself</vocabulary> to a certain cause.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7438', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%BD%E3%81%8F%E3%81%99', 50, 1, '2013-08-20T22:26:19.094722Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.956965Z'),
(7439, 'vocabulary', '彩り', '彩り', 'This is the noun version of the kanji and has the same meaning as well: <vocabulary>coloring</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7439', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%A9%E3%82%8A', 50, 1, '2013-08-20T22:26:30.377612Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.96958Z'),
(7440, 'vocabulary', '悔やむ', '悔やむ', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>regret</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to regret</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to mourn</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7440', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%94%E3%82%84%E3%82%80', 50, 1, '2013-08-20T22:27:36.331359Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:47.981555Z'),
(7441, 'vocabulary', '殴り合い', '殴り合い', 'Remember 殴る, which means "to punch"? Well, add on 合う and you''ve got <vocabulary>punches</vocabulary> that have <vocabulary>come together</vocabulary> with other punches. By "come together" I mean your fist is coming in contact with someone''s face, and their fist is coming in contact with your kidneys. In other words, this is a <vocabulary>fistfight</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7441', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%B4%E3%82%8A%E5%90%88%E3%81%84', 50, 1, '2013-08-20T22:31:54.692038Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:14:53.54038Z'),
(7442, 'vocabulary', '殴り込み', '殴り込み', '<kanji>Punch</kanji> your way <kanji>into</kanji> a place, and you''re launching a <vocabulary>raid</vocabulary>. 

殴り込み usually means a raid by gang members or delinquents, where they barge into someone''s home or enemy territory to start a fight.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7442', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%B4%E3%82%8A%E8%BE%BC%E3%81%BF', 50, 1, '2013-08-20T22:31:55.24894Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:14:53.54038Z'),
(7443, 'vocabulary', '詐欺師', '詐欺師', 'You know that 詐欺 is "fraud." An <kanji>expert</kanji> in fraud is someone who does this it lot, and possibly for a living. That''s a <vocabulary>swindler</vocabulary>, a <vocabulary>con artist</vocabulary>,  or a <vocabulary>scammer</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7443', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%90%E6%AC%BA%E5%B8%AB', 49, 1, '2013-08-22T18:46:54.217181Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:48.032838Z'),
(7444, 'vocabulary', '遂げる', '遂げる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>accomplish</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to accomplish</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7444', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%82%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 50, 1, '2013-08-24T00:10:47.290718Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:48.044535Z'),
(7445, 'vocabulary', '麻布', '麻布', 'A <kanji>hemp</kanji> <kanji>cloth</kanji> is a <vocabulary>hemp cloth</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7445', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BA%BB%E5%B8%83', 50, 1, '2013-08-24T00:36:48.695229Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:48.056602Z'),
(7446, 'vocabulary', '花柳界', '花柳界', 'The <kanji>red light district</kanji> (花柳) <kanji>world</kanji> of the red light district is the <vocabulary>red light district</vocabulary>, though this can refer especially to the "<vocabulary>pleasure quarters</vocabulary>" or "<vocabulary>world of the geisha</vocabulary>" as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7446', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B1%E6%9F%B3%E7%95%8C', 49, 1, '2013-08-26T19:01:11.55419Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:20:54.638732Z'),
(7447, 'vocabulary', '休憩所', '休憩所', 'A <kanji>rest</kanji> (休憩) <kanji>place</kanji> is a <vocabulary>rest area</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>rest stop</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7447', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%91%E6%86%A9%E6%89%80', 49, 1, '2013-08-26T19:13:32.944444Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:18:00.076038Z'),
(7448, 'vocabulary', '粘々', '粘々', '<kanji>Sticky</kanji> <kanji>sticky</kanji> things are really <vocabulary>sticky</vocabulary> and covered with <vocabulary>stickiness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7448', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%98%E3%80%85', 47, 1, '2013-08-26T19:21:49.12266Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(7449, 'vocabulary', 'お疲れ様', 'お疲れ様', 'You are a 疲れ (tired) 様 (honorific that kind of refers to whoever''s being mentioned). You say this to people when they''ve done good work — at the end of a workday or after a hard practice, for example. I guess the idea is that if they''re tired, they''ve done some good work. Tell your coworkers お疲れ様でした when they leave for the day, or just use お疲れ in more casual situations with friends!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7449', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E7%96%B2%E3%82%8C%E6%A7%98', 14, 1, '2013-08-27T00:21:13.420698Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T00:08:14.311912Z'),
(7450, 'vocabulary', '鬱気', '鬱気', 'A <kanji>gloomy</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji> has filled the room. This puts you in a <vocabulary>gloomy mood</vocabulary>. You are now <vocabulary>feeling downcast</vocabulary>.

This word is formal, so you''ll mostly just see it in literature.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7450', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AC%B1%E6%B0%97', 50, 1, '2013-08-28T21:33:24.156207Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(7451, 'vocabulary', '一歩', '一歩', 'If <kanji>歩</kanji> is "walk" and you take <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>walk</kanji>, then really you are just taking <vocabulary>one step</vocabulary>. When there''s a number before 歩, it refers to the number of steps you''re taking. The most common is this one, and you''ll see it on urinals when they kindly ask you to please take one step forward because peeing isn''t a game and somebody has to clean up after you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7451', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%AD%A9', 6, 1, '2013-10-10T19:40:01.187523Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.2441Z'),
(7452, 'vocabulary', '〜階', '〜階', 'You know that the kanji refers to a <kanji>floor</kanji> (as in the "fifth floor"). This word represents the version that comes after a number, shown with a 〜. Essentially, this has the same meaning as the kanji you learned, making it nice and easy.

Note that floor-counting systems can vary across different countries. In Japan the ground floor is generally called 一階, with the floors above being 二階, 三階, and so on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7452', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E9%9A%8E', 12, 1, '2013-10-10T19:42:49.203129Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:48.196312Z'),
(7453, 'vocabulary', '〜付き', '〜付き', 'You know that the kanji 付 means <kanji>attach</kanji> and words like 付く mean "to be attached." This has the same idea. When you see this attached to another word, for example, you might know that something is attached to something else, or perhaps <vocabulary>included</vocabulary> with it. For example, if you stay at a hotel room, maybe it is a breakfast付き, i.e. "<vocabulary>including</vocabulary> breakfast."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7453', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E4%BB%98%E3%81%8D', 11, 1, '2013-10-10T19:47:33.882603Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:03:42.596356Z'),
(7454, 'vocabulary', '〜枚', '〜枚', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well, so this is <vocabulary>flat object counter</vocabulary>.

This counter can be used with various "flat" things, such as <vocabulary>clothing items</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>sheets</vocabulary>, paper, stamps, dishes, and more. Notice how they''re all kind of flat shaped?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7454', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%9E%9A', 18, 1, '2013-10-10T19:50:19.125491Z', NULL, '2025-12-15T22:02:02.92783Z'),
(7455, 'vocabulary', '〜台', '〜台', 'This counter is used with some kind of <kanji>machine</kanji> or device, usually a fairly large one (so nothing itty bitty). Cars are a good example of this. But it can also be used for things like computers, which were once the size of rooms, but are now nice and small. Thanks technology!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7455', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%8F%B0', 3, 1, '2013-10-10T19:55:55.160745Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:48.267927Z'),
(7456, 'vocabulary', '三百', '三百', 'You have <kanji>three</kanji> <kanji>hundred</kanji>s. What does that come out to? <vocabulary>Three hundred</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7456', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E7%99%BE', 6, 1, '2013-10-10T19:58:50.74197Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.496634Z'),
(7457, 'vocabulary', '都市', '都市', 'A <kanji>metropolis</kanji> <kanji>city</kanji> is a <vocabulary>city</vocabulary>. When you''re referring to just a plain ol'' city, sans name, you can use this word.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7457', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%BD%E5%B8%82', 12, 1, '2013-10-10T20:00:03.832948Z', NULL, '2025-10-02T17:24:51.43252Z'),
(7458, 'vocabulary', '言語', '言語', 'When you <kanji>say</kanji> words in a <kanji>language</kanji>, you are speaking a <vocabulary>language</vocabulary>. Not sure what that language is, but since you''re saying it it must be real.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7458', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%80%E8%AA%9E', 10, 1, '2013-10-10T20:01:56.386973Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.235783Z'),
(7459, 'vocabulary', '地上', '地上', 'Remember 地下, which is "below ground." Well, this is <kanji>ground</kanji> and <kanji>above</kanji> combined together, so I bet you can guess that this means <vocabulary>above ground</vocabulary>. But usually things above ground don''t float (unless you''re in space), so it''s really <vocabulary>on the ground</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7459', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E4%B8%8A', 8, 1, '2013-10-10T20:04:48.19627Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(7460, 'vocabulary', '全部', '全部', 'If you take <kanji>all</kanji> <kanji>part</kanji>s and put them together, you have <vocabulary>all</vocabulary> things. The <vocabulary>entire</vocabulary> collection. It is <vocabulary>whole</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7460', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E9%83%A8', 9, 1, '2013-10-10T20:07:00.897926Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:47:49.244838Z'),
(7461, 'vocabulary', '〜時', '〜時', 'If there''s a number before 時, it means <vocabulary>o''clock</vocabulary>. For example １時 is 1:00, and ２３時 is 23:00 or 11pm.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7461', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%99%82', 7, 1, '2013-10-11T18:27:12.559814Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.210451Z'),
(7462, 'vocabulary', '万人', '万人', 'If we''re talking about <kanji>ten thousand</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji>s, then we might as well be talking about <vocabulary>all people</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>everybody</vocabulary>, because that''s a lot of people, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7462', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%87%E4%BA%BA', 42, 1, '2013-10-11T18:33:07.789757Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(7463, 'vocabulary', '味方', '味方', 'You like the <kanji>flavor</kanji> of a particular <kanji>direction</kanji>, so you go that way. Everyone else going in the same flavor direction is your <vocabulary>ally</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>supporter</vocabulary>. They''re your flavor direction allies. They have the same tastes and they''re chasing the same flavors in life, so naturally they become allies, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7463', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%B3%E6%96%B9', 11, 1, '2013-10-11T18:36:56.012444Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T20:59:50.129414Z'),
(7464, 'vocabulary', '天使', '天使', 'One who <kanji>use</kanji>s <kanji>heaven</kanji> to their advantage is someone from heaven, like an <vocabulary>angel</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7464', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A9%E4%BD%BF', 9, 1, '2013-10-11T18:39:25.490882Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.619333Z'),
(7465, 'vocabulary', '〜人', '〜人', 'When you see this on a number, for example ７人 or ３人, you''re using it to count people. Just remember, both 一人 and 二人 have alternative readings. From 3 and up it''s just the number plus にん, like this. ５人 is ごにん, １０人 is じゅうにん, etc. This is the <vocabulary>counter for people</vocabulary>, which makes this word the <vocabulary>number of people</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7465', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E4%BA%BA', 3, 1, '2013-10-11T18:46:26.587327Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(7466, 'vocabulary', '発売中', '発売中', 'You have 発売, which means "for sale," and then you have 中 which is <kanji>middle</kanji>. An item that is in the middle of being for sale is <vocabulary>now for sale</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>in stores now</vocabulary> or in other words <vocabulary>now available</vocabulary>. You''ll see this for items which have recently been released or when a shopkeeper wants to let you know that they''re selling something now, finally, or whatever.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7466', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BA%E5%A3%B2%E4%B8%AD', 11, 1, '2013-10-24T19:23:44.504033Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:32:06.067577Z'),
(7467, 'vocabulary', 'お手洗い', 'お手洗い', 'You know that 手洗い is "handwashing" (and also a not as polite way to mention a bathroom). When you add お you''re suddenly making it polite. Why? Because you''re talking about something a little more crass than handwashing. You''re talking about the <vocabulary>toilet</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>restroom</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>bathroom</vocabulary>. Ewww. Poop goes there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7467', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E6%89%8B%E6%B4%97%E3%81%84', 22, 1, '2013-11-19T21:18:06.470407Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(7468, 'vocabulary', '進撃', '進撃', 'You <kanji>advance</kanji> and <kanji>attack</kanji>! This is a <vocabulary>charge</vocabulary> on someone or something, though it can also be an <vocabulary>advance</vocabulary> on them. Yep, this is the 進撃 from 進撃の巨人, the popular anime and manga. Don''t say you never learned anything useful here.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7468', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%B2%E6%92%83', 28, 1, '2013-12-27T18:51:03.970687Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:48.601792Z'),
(7469, 'vocabulary', '自動車', '自動車', 'You know that 自動 is <vocabulary>automatic</vocabulary>. So what is an automatic <kanji>car</kanji>? That is an <vocabulary>automobile</vocabulary>, or simply a <vocabulary>car</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7469', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E5%8B%95%E8%BB%8A', 16, 1, '2014-01-15T18:27:20.530418Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.581357Z'),
(7470, 'vocabulary', '黒板', '黒板', 'A <kanji>black</kanji> <kanji>board</kanji> is a <vocabulary>blackboard</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7470', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BB%92%E6%9D%BF', 29, 1, '2014-01-15T18:32:32.346326Z', NULL, '2026-02-03T01:43:38.646573Z'),
(7471, 'vocabulary', '汚い', '汚い', 'This is the adjective form of 汚, which is <kanji>dirty</kanji>. This word is the adjective <vocabulary>dirty</vocabulary>, but you can also use it to say things like <vocabulary>filthy</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>foul</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>unclean</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7471', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%9A%E3%81%84', 36, 1, '2014-01-15T18:36:12.689079Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:48.708307Z'),
(7472, 'vocabulary', '速い', '速い', 'Just like the meaning of the kanji (<kanji>fast</kanji>) this adjective also means <vocabulary>fast</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7472', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%9F%E3%81%84', 10, 1, '2014-01-15T18:39:09.796597Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.259168Z'),
(7473, 'vocabulary', '眼鏡', '眼鏡', 'An <kanji>eyeball</kanji> <kanji>mirror</kanji> is something that refracts and focuses light into your eyeball in a different way so you can see better. Really, these things are glasses, not eye mirrors, but same sort of things when you get down to it. Just imagine someone wearing mirrors on their eyes, and when you ask them what those are for, they just say they''re <vocabulary>glasses</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7473', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%BC%E9%8F%A1', 34, 1, '2014-01-15T18:54:07.400519Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:33:42.082405Z'),
(7474, 'vocabulary', '北海道', '北海道', 'The <kanji>north</kanji> <kanji>sea</kanji> <kanji>road</kanji> is the road you take to get to the northern sea. This is the big northern island of Japan, actually, and it''s called <vocabulary>Hokkaido</vocabulary>. It''s a very nice place, so be sure to visit for some of the freshest seafood around.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7474', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%97%E6%B5%B7%E9%81%93', 13, 1, '2014-01-15T18:56:37.013894Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.438028Z'),
(7475, 'vocabulary', '新年', '新年', 'A <kanji>new</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji> is a <vocabulary>new year</vocabulary>. Hope you had / have / are having a good one, depending on when you read this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7475', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E5%B9%B4', 9, 1, '2014-01-15T18:59:28.504231Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.631331Z'),
(7476, 'vocabulary', '教科書', '教科書', 'The thing you use to <kanji>teach</kanji> a <kanji>course</kanji> with <kanji>writing</kanji>s is a <vocabulary>textbook</vocabulary>. It''s a book of writings you use to teach your course!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7476', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%99%E7%A7%91%E6%9B%B8', 16, 1, '2014-01-15T19:01:52.142978Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.596376Z'),
(7477, 'vocabulary', '足す', '足す', 'Remember 足りる? That is "to be enough." How can you make something be enough? You keep adding things to the pile until you reach that point where there are enough! That''s why this verb is <vocabulary>to add something</vocabulary>, because you keep adding things until you 足りる.

This verb is transitive, like most verbs ending in す, which is why you are adding something. It''s not getting itself added all by itself. Maybe you add so much that someone tries to <reading>sue</reading> (す) you for that nasty pile of squirrels you keep outside.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7477', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B6%B3%E3%81%99', 6, 1, '2014-01-15T19:04:24.687492Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:04.883815Z'),
(7478, 'vocabulary', '要る', '要る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>need</kanji> and the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to need</vocabulary>.

This one''s a little tricky, because although it''s usually translated as "to need" in English, it''s used more like <vocabulary>to be needed</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be necessary</vocabulary>. That is, it''s an intransitive verb in Japanese, so it doesn''t take a direct object.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7478', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%81%E3%82%8B', 9, 1, '2014-01-15T19:10:07.410843Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:28:03.237353Z'),
(7479, 'vocabulary', '座る', '座る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>sit</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to sit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7479', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%A7%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2014-01-15T19:13:02.2379Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:52:04.645576Z'),
(7480, 'vocabulary', '品詞', '品詞', 'A <kanji>product</kanji> of <kanji>part of speech</kanji> is more <vocabulary>part of speech</vocabulary>. Evil begets evil, so to speak.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7480', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%93%81%E8%A9%9E', 19, 1, '2014-01-15T19:20:02.795191Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(7481, 'vocabulary', '形容動詞', '形容動詞', 'A 形容詞 is an <kanji>adjective</kanji>. So what about an adjective with a 動詞 (verb) in there? How does that make sense? I''m not entirely sure either, but an adjective with movement in it is a <vocabulary>na adjective</vocabulary>. These are the adjectives that can end with な (便利な, for example). In fact, they''re more like nouns than they are verbs, so what gives? Whatever it is, just decide you don''t want to deal with this craziness. Just say <reading>nah</reading>, I don''t care about this anymore.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7481', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%A2%E5%AE%B9%E5%8B%95%E8%A9%9E', 21, 1, '2014-01-15T19:26:08.260521Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:48.929401Z'),
(7482, 'vocabulary', '車両', '車両', 'A <kanji>car</kanji> has wheels on <kanji>both</kanji> sides, and together they make a <vocabulary>vehicle</vocabulary>.

車両 is a formal word often used in official or technical contexts. It refers not just to standard cars but also to <vocabulary>train car</vocabulary>s and other vehicles that carry people or goods. You''ll commonly see it in public transportation terms, on signs like "No vehicles allowed," or in driver''s license and traffic law classifications.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7482', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8A%E4%B8%A1', 6, 1, '2014-01-15T19:27:32.174521Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:45:39.570399Z'),
(7483, 'vocabulary', '実感', '実感', 'A <kanji>reality</kanji> <kanji>feeling</kanji> is what you get when something feels real. The <vocabulary>realization</vocabulary> sets in — whatever this thing is, you''ve got a <vocabulary>real feeling</vocabulary> that it''s actually happening.

実感 means a realization that comes from lived experience. For example, when you''re expecting a baby, you may understand that you''re becoming a parent, but not truly have that realization until the baby is born or you hold them in your arms. It''s the sense of something feeling real beyond theory or explanation — when it truly sinks in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7483', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9F%E6%84%9F', 21, 1, '2014-01-15T19:31:09.657139Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(7484, 'vocabulary', '火傷', '火傷', 'A <kanji>fire</kanji> <kanji>wound</kanji> is a <vocabulary>burn</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>scald</vocabulary> you got from fire (or other hot things). Ouch!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7484', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%AB%E5%82%B7', 32, 1, '2014-01-15T19:36:23.758597Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:48.990116Z'),
(7485, 'vocabulary', '読者', '読者', 'The <kanji>read</kanji>ing <kanji>someone</kanji> is someone who is reading. They are the <vocabulary>reader</vocabulary> (of a book, blog, article, etc).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7485', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%AD%E8%80%85', 20, 1, '2014-01-15T19:39:43.469153Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(7486, 'vocabulary', '解説', '解説', 'Once you''ve <kanji>solve</kanji>d a <kanji>theory</kanji>, you should be able to give a pretty good <vocabulary>explanation</vocabulary> of it to somebody else. Makes sense, right? 

解説 refers to a detailed and analytical explanation. It''s often used when someone, often an expert, provides an in-depth analysis about a topic, like a sports game or news article <vocabulary>commentary</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7486', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A3%E8%AA%AC', 21, 1, '2014-01-15T19:40:44.502462Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T20:23:40.067262Z'),
(7487, 'vocabulary', '対談', '対談', 'A <kanji>versus</kanji> <kanji>talk</kanji> is when you sit down with another person and have a <vocabulary>talk</vocabulary>. As you''re talking, it might evolve into a <vocabulary>discussion</vocabulary>. Or perhaps you''re there to have an <vocabulary>interview</vocabulary>.

対談 is usually a formal discussion between two or more individuals, often organized ahead of time, and with a specific purpose or topic in mind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7487', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BE%E8%AB%87', 9, 1, '2014-01-15T19:42:57.750391Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:12:11.505385Z'),
(7488, 'vocabulary', '意欲', '意欲', 'If you have an <kanji>idea</kanji> of what you <kanji>want</kanji> already, then that''s good! You have <vocabulary>will</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>desire</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>ambition</vocabulary>. Those who don''t know what they want can''t have any of these.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7488', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%8F%E6%AC%B2', 32, 1, '2014-01-15T19:45:08.991118Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.067774Z'),
(7489, 'vocabulary', '食べ物', '食べ物', 'An <kanji>eat</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji> (aka "a thing you eat") is <vocabulary>food</vocabulary>. Eat those things. Eat that food. Because it''s a "thing" you know it''s a noun. Interesting how this kanji can turn a verb like this into a noun, right? Look out for it because this pattern will show up with other verbs as well!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7489', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9F%E3%81%B9%E7%89%A9', 11, 1, '2014-01-15T19:45:18.210968Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(7490, 'vocabulary', '波', '波', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7490', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A2', 11, 1, '2014-01-17T22:49:53.77481Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:11:56.943742Z'),
(7491, 'vocabulary', '顔面', '顔面', 'The <kanji>face</kanji> of a <kanji>face</kanji> is a face, but more specifically the <vocabulary>face of a person</vocabulary>. Probably because the most face-like thing you can ever have is a human''s face... at least in our eyes (by "our" I mean the human race''s eyes, because I am totally with you guys on that one).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7491', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A1%94%E9%9D%A2', 16, 1, '2014-01-29T19:44:34.542005Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.608067Z'),
(7492, 'vocabulary', '言葉', '言葉', 'A <kanji>leaf</kanji> of the things you <kanji>say</kanji> is like a leaf from a tree of speaking. That leaf is a part of the whole language, it is a single <vocabulary>word</vocabulary>, though it can add up to be a <vocabulary>language</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>manner of speech</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7492', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%80%E8%91%89', 12, 1, '2014-02-11T00:38:00.29859Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:10:47.677773Z'),
(7493, 'vocabulary', '丁度いい', '丁度いい', 'If 丁度 is "exactly", then "exactly good" is going to be just the right amount, aka <vocabulary>just right</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7493', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%81%E5%BA%A6%E3%81%84%E3%81%84', 11, 1, '2014-02-11T20:04:17.870495Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:15:32.558339Z'),
(7494, 'vocabulary', '律動的', '律動的', 'Rhythm is 律動, but by adding a 的 in there we get the adjective version of that word, making it <vocabulary>rhythmical</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>rhythmic</vocabulary>.

Note that リズミカル and テンポのいい are much more common words to use for "rhythmical" and “rhythmic” respectively, but you may sometimes see 律動的 in formal writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7494', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%8B%E5%8B%95%E7%9A%84', 51, 1, '2014-02-26T20:28:48.464972Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.20131Z'),
(7495, 'vocabulary', '別れる', '別れる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>separate</kanji>, so you can guess the meaning is <vocabulary>to separate</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be divided</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7495', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A5%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 13, 1, '2014-03-21T22:35:42.684004Z', NULL, '2025-09-26T00:52:13.392821Z'),
(7496, 'vocabulary', '共有する', '共有する', 'This is the same as 共有 but the verb version.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7496', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B1%E6%9C%89%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 13, 1, '2014-03-21T22:38:20.582543Z', NULL, '2025-09-26T00:54:50.839541Z'),
(7497, 'vocabulary', '保持する', '保持する', 'This is the verb version of 保持 (keep), so this means <vocabulary>to keep</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to hold</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to maintain</vocabulary>.

Note that this is a formal word that''s commonly used in professional or official contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7497', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%9D%E6%8C%81%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 11, 1, '2014-03-21T22:41:36.139615Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.270975Z'),
(7498, 'vocabulary', '僕', '僕', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7498', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%83%95', 12, 1, '2014-05-01T17:12:22.199532Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.292407Z'),
(7499, 'vocabulary', '編集者', '編集者', 'An <kanji>edit</kanji> is 編集. <kanji>Someone</kanji> who does those edits is an <vocabulary>editor</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7499', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A8%E9%9B%86%E8%80%85', 33, 1, '2014-05-01T17:14:43.102399Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(7500, 'vocabulary', '研修生', '研修生', '<vocabulary>研修</vocabulary> is a training or study of some kind. If it''s in your current <kanji>life</kanji> (生) that you are doing the training, then you are a <vocabulary>trainee</vocabulary>. This is a lot like how a student is in the status of "study life."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7500', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%94%E4%BF%AE%E7%94%9F', 29, 1, '2014-05-01T17:17:49.750293Z', NULL, '2025-11-05T12:02:19.949637Z'),
(7501, 'vocabulary', '迷惑メール', '迷惑メール', 'When you have "troublesome" (迷惑) mail, you have mail you don''t want. This is known as <vocabulary>spam</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>spam mail</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>junk mail</vocabulary>. On a more technical level it''s <vocabulary>unsolicited mail</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7501', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%B7%E6%83%91%E3%83%A1%E3%83%BC%E3%83%AB', 29, 1, '2014-05-01T17:21:55.637606Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.535237Z'),
(7502, 'vocabulary', '在留する', '在留する', 'If 在留 is <kanji>residence</kanji>, then the verb version of that would be <vocabulary>to reside</vocabulary>.

Like 在留, this is also a more formal term. You might encounter it at the immigration office, but you probably won''t hear it in everyday conversation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7502', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%A8%E7%95%99%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2014-05-01T17:27:04.043942Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.382269Z'),
(7503, 'vocabulary', '在留', '在留', 'Where you <kanji>exist</kanji> is the place you''re <kanji>detain</kanji>ed. As long as you''re in this place, this will be your <vocabulary>residence</vocabulary>, even if you''re <vocabulary>staying in a foreign country</vocabulary>.

在留 is an official term used for people residing in a foreign country. You''ll find it in phrases like 在留カード (residence card) or 在留外国人 (foreign resident).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7503', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%A8%E7%95%99', 20, 1, '2014-05-01T17:29:20.536769Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.404267Z'),
(7504, 'vocabulary', '地球', '地球', 'The <kanji>earth</kanji> <kanji>sphere</kanji>! It''s where we live and the planet we call home. It''s <vocabulary>the earth</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7504', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E7%90%83', 12, 1, '2014-05-01T17:31:28.711026Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.426547Z'),
(7505, 'vocabulary', '故', '故', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7505', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%85', 26, 1, '2014-05-01T17:46:07.127397Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.345144Z'),
(7506, 'vocabulary', '演技', '演技', 'Your <kanji>performance</kanji> <kanji>skill</kanji> allows you to be great at <vocabulary>acting</vocabulary> and doing awesome <vocabulary>performance</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7506', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%94%E6%8A%80', 23, 1, '2014-05-01T18:03:35.922293Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T21:48:56.409206Z'),
(7507, 'vocabulary', '演ずる', '演ずる', 'If 演 is <kanji>performance</kanji> then the verb version of this would be <vocabulary>to perform</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7507', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%94%E3%81%9A%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2014-05-01T18:05:45.235021Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.501078Z'),
(7508, 'vocabulary', '演芸', '演芸', 'When you turn <kanji>acting</kanji> into <kanji>art</kanji> you get the <vocabulary>performing arts</vocabulary>. It is <vocabulary>popular entertainment</vocabulary>! Lights, action! Welcome to Broadway!

This word is more traditional and isn''t used very much these days.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7508', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%94%E8%8A%B8', 23, 1, '2014-05-01T18:08:45.136476Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.523952Z'),
(7509, 'vocabulary', '瞳孔', '瞳孔', 'Your <kanji>pupil</kanji> <kanji>hole</kanji> is still your <vocabulary>pupil</vocabulary>. It can also be called your <vocabulary>pupillary</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7509', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9E%B3%E5%AD%94', 46, 1, '2014-05-01T18:51:52.120992Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.53593Z'),
(7510, 'vocabulary', '眼孔', '眼孔', 'Your <kanji>eyeball</kanji> <kanji>cavity</kanji> (hole) is where your eye is held. This is your <vocabulary>eye socket</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7510', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%BC%E5%AD%94', 46, 1, '2014-05-01T18:56:11.465535Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.549337Z'),
(7511, 'vocabulary', '掲載する', '掲載する', '掲載 is <kanji>publication</kanji> so the verb version of that is <vocabulary>to publish</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to print</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7511', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%B2%E8%BC%89%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 39, 1, '2014-05-01T19:28:18.039331Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:42:46.078365Z'),
(7512, 'vocabulary', '寸', '寸', 'This <kanji>measurement</kanji> also refers to a specific measurement, a "<vocabulary>sun</vocabulary>", which is about <vocabulary>3.03 centimeters</vocabulary>. You''ll hear it brought up from time to time to measure things, though usually with more traditional Japanese items.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7512', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%B8', 44, 1, '2014-05-01T21:10:40.272779Z', NULL, '2025-08-25T16:53:50.232341Z'),
(7513, 'vocabulary', '寸暇', '寸暇', 'You have a <kanji>measurement</kanji> (3.03 cm!) of <kanji>spare time</kanji>. This isn''t much free time, but it''s a <vocabulary>free minute</vocabulary> that you have.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7513', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%B8%E6%9A%87', 46, 1, '2014-05-01T21:12:58.278789Z', NULL, '2025-08-25T16:53:50.232341Z'),
(7514, 'vocabulary', '通う', '通う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji means <kanji>pass through</kanji> but we already know the word 通る which means to pass through. This word means a different kind of passing through, like how you pass through the halls of your school everyday. But we don''t say to pass through school, we say <vocabulary>to attend</vocabulary> school, or <vocabulary>to commute</vocabulary> to school.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7514', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%9A%E3%81%86', 11, 1, '2014-10-27T23:13:46.278151Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:25.907882Z'),
(7515, 'vocabulary', '二日', '二日', '<vocabulary>Second day</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>day two</vocabulary>. No matter how you look at this combination of kanji, the meaning of the word makes a lot of sense!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7515', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E6%97%A5', 2, 2, '2014-10-28T00:39:33.118256Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.632409Z'),
(7517, 'vocabulary', '人々', '人々', '<kanji>Person</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji>, why, that''s more than one person. It''s <vocabulary>people</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7517', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E3%80%85', 2, 2, '2014-11-14T18:19:46.833856Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(7518, 'vocabulary', '少女', '少女', 'A <kanji>little</kanji> <kanji>woman</kanji> is a <vocabulary>girl</vocabulary>. 

少女 is a relatively formal word that''s often used in writing to describe a <vocabulary>young girl</vocabulary>, usually between childhood and adolescence (or between the ages of about five and eighteen).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7518', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%91%E5%A5%B3', 3, 1, '2014-11-14T18:25:46.114667Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:10:15.994971Z'),
(7519, 'vocabulary', '万', '万', 'When a vocab word is a single kanji without okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) it usually has the same meaning as the kanji it''s spawned from. If you know the kanji meaning you''ll also know the vocab meaning. It''s <vocabulary>ten thousand</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7519', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%87', 3, 1, '2014-11-14T18:29:21.504737Z', NULL, '2025-10-31T14:37:55.727427Z'),
(7520, 'vocabulary', '赤い', '赤い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>red</kanji>? Why, it''s <vocabulary>red</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7520', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%A4%E3%81%84', 4, 1, '2014-11-14T18:31:55.00798Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.170522Z'),
(7521, 'vocabulary', '白い', '白い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>white</kanji>? <vocabulary>White</vocabulary>, of course!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7521', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BD%E3%81%84', 4, 1, '2014-11-14T18:35:12.377082Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.180554Z'),
(7522, 'vocabulary', '百', '百', 'When a vocab word is a single kanji without okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) it usually has the same meaning as the kanji it''s spawned from. If you know the kanji meaning you''ll also know the vocab meaning. It''s <vocabulary>one hundred</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7522', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BE', 4, 1, '2014-11-14T18:37:28.973381Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:55:06.825475Z'),
(7523, 'vocabulary', '会', '会', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>meet</kanji>, and it means a <vocabulary>gathering</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>meeting</vocabulary>, as you’d expect! It isn’t really used for the kind of meeting you have at an office, though. It’s more in the sense of a people coming together for a specific purpose, like volunteer <vocabulary>group</vocabulary>, or a birthday <vocabulary>party</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7523', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9A', 13, 1, '2014-11-14T18:41:41.841484Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.797821Z'),
(7524, 'vocabulary', '早く', '早く', 'This is the adverb version of the kanji <kanji>early</kanji>. It''s a little thing you can begin sentences with to say you''re doing something <vocabulary>early</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>quickly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7524', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A9%E3%81%8F', 6, 1, '2014-11-14T18:43:56.695938Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:53:32.555199Z'),
(7525, 'vocabulary', '死', '死', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7525', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%BB', 6, 1, '2014-11-14T18:49:36.540871Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.456056Z'),
(7526, 'vocabulary', '姉', '姉', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This is your older sister, by the way. Not somebody else''s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7526', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%89', 6, 1, '2014-11-14T18:51:16.307302Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T19:40:45.091587Z'),
(7527, 'vocabulary', '〜室', '〜室', 'You''ll see this attached to other words... like "waiting" or "guest." When you see this, you''ll know it is a ___ room, making this word mean <vocabulary>room</vocabulary> (same as the kanji).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7527', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%AE%A4', 6, 1, '2014-11-14T18:54:08.26051Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:57:32.391485Z'),
(7528, 'vocabulary', '大きく', '大きく', 'This is the adverb version of the kanji <kanji>big</kanji>. It''s a little thing you can put in front of things to say that they are "<vocabulary>in a big way</vocabulary>" or "<vocabulary>on a grand scale</vocabulary>."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7528', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E3%81%8D%E3%81%8F', 7, 1, '2014-11-14T19:02:03.154209Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(7529, 'vocabulary', '近く', '近く', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>near</kanji>. So it also means <vocabulary>near</vocabulary>, or in the <vocabulary>neighborhood</vocabulary> of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7529', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E3%81%8F', 7, 1, '2014-11-14T19:08:12.383861Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.235706Z'),
(7530, 'vocabulary', '向ける', '向ける', 'You know that 向く means "to face" or "to turn towards", and this verb is related. This is a transitive verb, meaning it takes a direct object. It''s when someone turns or points something. That something doesn''t just turn on its own. So this verb means <vocabulary>to turn something towards</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to point something at</vocabulary>.

In order to remember that this word means "to turn something towards," focus on the け. You have a bottle of ketchup in your hands and you''re turning it toward your worst enemy to spray them. Picture yourself pointing the ketchup toward them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7530', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%91%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2014-11-14T19:13:58.554837Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.442953Z'),
(7531, 'vocabulary', 'お前', 'お前', 'The お is added to elevate someone in <kanji>front</kanji> of you. What do you call someone in front of you? That''s right, <vocabulary>you</vocabulary>. Over time, this word has lost its sense of politeness, and nowadays it sounds very rough and often rude. This "you" is a good way to show anger, and some people also use it in very casual situations to show affection.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7531', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E5%89%8D', 8, 1, '2014-11-14T19:17:18.898568Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.45306Z'),
(7532, 'vocabulary', '〜部', '〜部', 'You''ll see this attached to other words... like "sales" or "tennis." When you see this, you''ll know it is a ___ department, or a ___ club, making this word mean <vocabulary>department</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>club</vocabulary>, similar to the kanji meaning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7532', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E9%83%A8', 11, 1, '2014-11-14T19:19:27.03003Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:49.997184Z'),
(7533, 'vocabulary', '部分', '部分', '<kanji>Part</kanji> of a <kanji>part</kanji>? That''s a <vocabulary>portion</vocabulary>, a <vocabulary>section</vocabulary> of that part, and even a <vocabulary>part</vocabulary> in itself!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7533', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%A8%E5%88%86', 9, 1, '2014-11-14T19:21:32.155212Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.684114Z'),
(7534, 'vocabulary', '千葉', '千葉', 'There is only one place in Japan that has a <kanji>thousand</kanji> <kanji>leaves</kanji> and that''s <vocabulary>Chiba</vocabulary>. Picture all of those leaves swirling around you on your vacation to Chiba. Beautiful.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7534', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%83%E8%91%89', 10, 1, '2014-11-14T19:25:22.186816Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:10:47.677773Z'),
(7535, 'vocabulary', '思わず', '思わず', 'This is an adverb, but it comes from a negative form of the verb 思う meaning <vocabulary>without thinking</vocabulary>. If you do something without thinking, you do it <vocabulary>unintentionally</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>reflexively</vocabulary>.

思わず is used when you react to something in spite of yourself, like 思わず笑った (I couldn''t help but laugh) or 思わず目を閉じた (I closed my eyes reflexively).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7535', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%9D%E3%82%8F%E3%81%9A', 10, 1, '2014-11-14T19:27:15.192299Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T01:11:17.455458Z'),
(7536, 'vocabulary', '立ち飲み', '立ち飲み', '立つ means "to stand," and 飲む means "to drink," right? Well, when you do both at the same time, you''re <vocabulary>standing and drinking</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>drinking while standing</vocabulary>.

It also refers to casual <vocabulary>standing bar</vocabulary>s where there are no seats — customers stand while having drinks and light snacks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7536', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%8B%E3%81%A1%E9%A3%B2%E3%81%BF', 10, 1, '2014-11-14T19:29:55.289612Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.395803Z'),
(7537, 'vocabulary', '田代島', '田代島', 'A <kanji>rice paddy</kanji> is used as a <kanji>substitute</kanji> for an <kanji>island</kanji>. Which island is it? That''s right, <vocabulary>Tashirojima</vocabulary>. Imagine a rice paddy in the middle of the sea, standing in for <vocabulary>Tashiro Island</vocabulary>', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7537', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B0%E4%BB%A3%E5%B3%B6', 13, 1, '2014-11-14T19:32:37.343725Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:50.111707Z'),
(7538, 'vocabulary', '表情', '表情', 'When you <kanji>express</kanji> a <kanji>feeling</kanji> it shows on your face. This is called a <vocabulary>facial expression</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7538', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%A8%E6%83%85', 13, 1, '2014-11-14T19:34:56.746539Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:26:46.193094Z'),
(7539, 'vocabulary', '性', '性', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

性 is a somewhat formal word to talk about <vocabulary>gender</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>sex</vocabulary>, including sexual desire or activity. It''s also a common suffix to describe the <vocabulary>nature</vocabulary> of something. 安全性 refers to how safe something is by nature, for example, and ウィルス性 describes something virus-caused, like viral pneumonia.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7539', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A7', 14, 1, '2014-11-14T19:37:29.608514Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:50.159933Z'),
(7540, 'vocabulary', '見返す', '見返す', 'When you <kanji>see</kanji> someone doubting you and <kanji>return</kanji> something successful instead, that is <vocabulary>to show someone what one can do</vocabulary> or more simply <vocabulary>to show someone</vocabulary>. In other words, you show someone who doubted you or treated you unfairly what you’re really capable of.

There are a couple of other meanings, too. It can mean <vocabulary>to look back over something</vocabulary>, like the answers to your kanji test you want to double check, and sometimes <vocabulary>to return someone’s stare</vocabulary>. You can see how all of these meanings relate to the kanji, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7540', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E8%BF%94%E3%81%99', 15, 1, '2014-11-14T19:39:03.620335Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.743278Z'),
(7541, 'vocabulary', '動き', '動き', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>move</kanji>. So it means <vocabulary>movement</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7541', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%95%E3%81%8D', 14, 1, '2014-11-14T19:41:04.554082Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:50.208698Z'),
(7542, 'vocabulary', '軍', '軍', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7542', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%8D', 15, 1, '2014-11-14T19:42:32.559131Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.763854Z'),
(7543, 'vocabulary', '列', '列', 'When things are put in a <kanji>row</kanji> they end up forming a <vocabulary>line</vocabulary>. This can be a line of people waiting in a <vocabulary>queue</vocabulary>, a <vocabulary>row</vocabulary> of seats in a theater, or a <vocabulary>column</vocabulary> in Excel and other spreadsheets.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7543', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%97', 15, 1, '2014-11-14T19:44:51.372177Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.775032Z'),
(7544, 'vocabulary', '伝', '伝', 'When you get someone to <kanji>transmit</kanji> information, they''re a type of <vocabulary>intermediary</vocabulary>, a <vocabulary>go-between</vocabulary>, if you will.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7544', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9D', 15, 1, '2014-11-14T19:46:21.059609Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.785586Z'),
(7545, 'vocabulary', '急', '急', 'This takes its meaning from the <kanji>sudden</kanji> part of the kanji, meaning it''s <vocabulary>sudden</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>urgent</vocabulary>, or an <vocabulary>emergency</vocabulary>! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7545', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A5', 16, 1, '2014-11-14T19:48:26.256079Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.620183Z'),
(7546, 'vocabulary', '兵', '兵', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7546', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B5', 17, 1, '2014-11-14T19:49:38.23185Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:54:26.999027Z'),
(7547, 'vocabulary', '無理', '無理', 'If you can do <kanji>nothing</kanji> within <kanji>reason</kanji>, then what you''re trying to do is <vocabulary>unreasonable</vocabulary> and probably downright <vocabulary>impossible</vocabulary>. If you still try, you''re probably just <vocabulary>overdoing</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7547', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E7%90%86', 17, 1, '2014-11-14T19:51:08.263779Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:50.354211Z'),
(7548, 'vocabulary', '側', '側', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7548', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%B4', 17, 1, '2014-11-14T19:53:02.383734Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:50.376054Z'),
(7549, 'vocabulary', '〜達', '〜達', 'You''ll see this attached to other words to make them <kanji>plural</kanji>. That''s why it''s called a <vocabulary>pluralizing suffix</vocabulary>. 

Note that you can only attach this to people and animals to make them plural. It can also be used with a person''s name, like マリアたち. In that case, it refers to Maria and the group of people she''s with! This might sound a little complicated, but you''ll get the hang of it with a little practice!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7549', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E9%81%94', 18, 1, '2014-11-14T19:54:37.633808Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:06:12.518486Z'),
(7550, 'vocabulary', '覚える', '覚える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>memorize</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to memorize</vocabulary>. That shouldn''t be too hard <vocabulary>to remember</vocabulary>, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7550', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%9A%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2014-11-14T19:57:05.071526Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:50.421324Z'),
(7551, 'vocabulary', '連中', '連中', 'People you want to <kanji>take along</kanji> to the <kanji>middle</kanji> of somewhere are probably people you know pretty well. They''re your <vocabulary>gang</vocabulary>, they''re the <vocabulary>guys</vocabulary>.

This word often has a negative feel to it, though, as in "those guys can''t be trusted" or "they''re a selfish bunch".', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7551', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%A3%E4%B8%AD', 19, 1, '2014-11-14T19:59:44.697036Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:50.443584Z'),
(7552, 'vocabulary', '多忙', '多忙', 'If you''ve had <kanji>many</kanji> <kanji>busy</kanji> days, then you''re even more <vocabulary>busy</vocabulary> than you thought!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7552', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%9A%E5%BF%99', 19, 1, '2014-11-14T20:01:44.534775Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:50.468186Z'),
(7553, 'vocabulary', '機', '機', 'The kanji and the word are the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7553', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A9%9F', 20, 1, '2014-11-14T20:04:08.921141Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:04:30.700843Z'),
(7554, 'vocabulary', '背後', '背後', 'Something that''s <kanji>behind</kanji> your <kanji>back</kanji> is in the <vocabulary>background</vocabulary>. It''s also probably plotting against you, <vocabulary>behind the scenes</vocabulary>. Sneaky, sneaky.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7554', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%8C%E5%BE%8C', 30, 1, '2014-11-14T20:05:40.914482Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.669509Z'),
(7555, 'vocabulary', '剣', '剣', 'The kanji and the word are the same. That means they share meanings as well. This word covers all types of <vocabulary>sword</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>sabre</vocabulary>s, though it was originally used mostly for <vocabulary>double-edged sword</vocabulary>s. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7555', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%A3', 35, 1, '2014-11-14T20:07:19.841514Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:50.542994Z'),
(7556, 'vocabulary', '寸法', '寸法', 'One of the easiest <kanji>method</kanji>s of <kanji>measurement</kanji> is to just check the <vocabulary>size</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>dimension</vocabulary>s, and there you go, you''ve got the <vocabulary>measurement</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7556', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%B8%E6%B3%95', 44, 1, '2014-11-14T20:08:35.388432Z', NULL, '2025-08-25T16:53:50.232341Z'),
(7557, 'vocabulary', '寸前', '寸前', '<kanji>Before</kanji> you take the last <kanji>measurement</kanji>, you know you''re <vocabulary>on the brink</vocabulary> of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7557', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%B8%E5%89%8D', 44, 1, '2014-11-14T20:11:22.369321Z', NULL, '2025-08-25T16:53:50.232341Z'),
(7558, 'vocabulary', '逃がす', '逃がす', 'You know how 逃げる means "to escape"? This is the transitive version, so it means <vocabulary>to let someone escape</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to let someone go</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to set someone free</vocabulary>. You know this because you''d want to let everyone escape when the place is filled with gas (がす).

逃がす is often about purposely letting someone or something go — like setting a bird free or letting a captive escape quietly. But it can also be used in more unintentional and regretful situations, like failing to catch a criminal or missing a big opportunity.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7558', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%83%E3%81%8C%E3%81%99', 42, 1, '2014-11-14T20:13:26.302539Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T07:23:04.13028Z'),
(7559, 'vocabulary', '魔', '魔', 'The kanji and the word are the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7559', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AD%94', 46, 1, '2014-11-14T21:47:36.458457Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:50.605673Z'),
(7560, 'vocabulary', '人工', '人工', 'A <kanji>person</kanji> of <kanji>industry</kanji> makes a lot of things by hand, so everything they make is <vocabulary>artificial</vocabulary>, and also means it''s <vocabulary>man made</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7560', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E5%B7%A5', 1, 2, '2015-02-25T22:23:17.972924Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(7561, 'vocabulary', '一日', '一日', 'This word has two different meanings. <vocabulary>One day</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>first day</vocabulary>. No matter how you look at this word, those meanings make a lot of sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7561', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%97%A5', 2, 2, '2015-02-25T22:24:10.545552Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:50.653888Z'),
(7562, 'vocabulary', '七日', '七日', '<vocabulary>Seventh day</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>day seven</vocabulary>. No matter how you look at this word, it makes a lot of sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7562', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%83%E6%97%A5', 2, 2, '2015-02-25T22:25:21.799617Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:50.68129Z'),
(7563, 'vocabulary', '三日', '三日', '<vocabulary>Third day</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>day three</vocabulary>. No matter how you look at this word, it makes a lot of sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7563', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E6%97%A5', 2, 2, '2015-02-25T22:27:58.843119Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:50.705763Z'),
(7564, 'vocabulary', '今日は-7564', '今日は', 'This is a strange one. It''s こんにちは, which you''ve probably heard before, but it''s spelled using the kanji <kanji>now</kanji> and <kanji>day</kanji>. So when you say "now day!" you''re really saying <vocabulary>hello</vocabulary>!

Warning! Don''t mix this up with きょうは! While they may look the same, they don''t mean the same thing. We will only ever ask you for こんにちは, so please try to remember that!

We usually don''t use kanji for this, so don''t use it when you''re writing. But you''ll probably run into it in the wild so it''s good to know!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7564', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A%E6%97%A5%E3%81%AF-7564', 3, 1, '2015-02-25T22:29:25.843789Z', '2020-01-10T23:45:48.511534Z', '2025-06-11T05:33:50.728328Z'),
(7565, 'vocabulary', '二十日', '二十日', '<kanji>Two</kanji> times <kanji>ten</kanji> <kanji>days</kanji> is <vocabulary>twenty days</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7565', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%81%E6%97%A5', 4, 1, '2015-02-25T22:30:20.465803Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.191371Z'),
(7566, 'vocabulary', '草地', '草地', '<kanji>Grass</kanji> covering the <kanji>earth</kanji> is a <vocabulary>meadow</vocabulary>. Or, if you''re less creative, it''s <vocabulary>grassland</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7566', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8D%89%E5%9C%B0', 6, 1, '2015-02-25T22:32:44.089022Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:04.983141Z'),
(7567, 'vocabulary', '台風', '台風', 'A <kanji>machine</kanji> that pumps <kanji>wind</kanji> can malfunction and make too much, causing a <vocabulary>typhoon</vocabulary>. It''s a really powerful machine to be able to move that much wind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7567', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B0%E9%A2%A8', 7, 1, '2015-02-25T22:33:35.401359Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.248993Z'),
(7568, 'vocabulary', '考古学', '考古学', 'If you often <kanji>think</kanji> about <kanji>study</kanji>ing <kanji>old</kanji> things, it''s called <vocabulary>archeology</vocabulary>. All archeology involves thinking about old things, at least a little bit.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7568', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%83%E5%8F%A4%E5%AD%A6', 5, 1, '2015-02-25T22:34:49.679616Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:34:08.229191Z'),
(7569, 'vocabulary', '地中海', '地中海', '<kanji>Earth</kanji>''s <kanji>middle</kanji> <kanji>sea</kanji> is the <vocabulary>Mediterranean Sea</vocabulary>. You know, because it''s surrounded by land. It''s a sea in the middle of all that earth.

Actually, the Japanese name 地中海 matches the Latin roots of the English name too: medius for "middle" and terra for "earth." Whoa, did we just teach you some Latin? I sense a new business venture here. Wanius Kanius, anyone? ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7569', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E4%B8%AD%E6%B5%B7', 8, 1, '2015-02-25T22:35:54.61392Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.46962Z'),
(7570, 'vocabulary', '毎朝', '毎朝', '<kanji>Every</kanji> <kanji>morning</kanji> is <vocabulary>every morning</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7570', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%8E%E6%9C%9D', 8, 1, '2015-02-25T22:36:55.895513Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.48788Z'),
(7571, 'vocabulary', '不自由', '不自由', 'Remember how 自由 means freedom? Well when you add <kanji>not</kanji> it causes you <vocabulary>discomfort</vocabulary> and really <vocabulary>inconveniences</vocabulary> you. That not is in the way of your freedom.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7571', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E8%87%AA%E7%94%B1', 9, 1, '2015-02-25T22:37:54.969608Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.695658Z'),
(7572, 'vocabulary', '教え', '教え', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>teach</kanji>. So it means <vocabulary>teaching</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7572', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%99%E3%81%88', 9, 1, '2015-02-25T22:39:07.72436Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.706899Z'),
(7573, 'vocabulary', '表', '表', 'Remember how 表 meant both <kanji>express</kanji> and <kanji>surface</kanji>? This expands on the second meaning, making this <vocabulary>the front</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>the surface</vocabulary>. 

表 refers to the visible, outward-facing side of something, in contrast to the hidden, back side. It''s used for surfaces on display, like the front of a building, the face of a coin, the cover of a book, or the top surface of a package.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7573', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%A8', 9, 1, '2015-02-25T22:39:59.061365Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:26:46.193094Z'),
(7574, 'vocabulary', '作業', '作業', 'If you want to <kanji>make</kanji> a <kanji>business</kanji> successful you need to be prepared to do a lot of <vocabulary>work</vocabulary>. Be ready to handle many <vocabulary>task</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>operation</vocabulary>s!

作業 often focuses on individual, hands-on tasks that are undertaken to accomplish a specific objective.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7574', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%9C%E6%A5%AD', 10, 1, '2015-02-25T22:40:56.026155Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.406872Z'),
(7575, 'vocabulary', '病む', '病む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>sick</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to get sick</vocabulary>. 

病む means to fall ill in more formal or literary contexts. In casual conversation, especially among younger people, 病む is used to describe someone feeling down, so this can also mean to get depressed.

Note that this word can be transitive and take a direct object, like 心を病む (to be mentally unwell), but can also be used intransitively without a direct object.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7575', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%85%E3%82%80', 10, 1, '2015-02-25T22:41:56.97758Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.417992Z'),
(7576, 'vocabulary', '中指', '中指', 'The <kanji>middle</kanji> <kanji>finger</kanji> is the <vocabulary>middle finger</vocabulary>, of course! And because we don''t see the difference, it''s your <vocabulary>middle toe</vocabulary> too. Stick that up the next time someone makes you mad.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7576', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E6%8C%87', 13, 1, '2015-02-25T22:43:14.590421Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.060718Z'),
(7577, 'vocabulary', '屋上', '屋上', '<kanji>Above</kanji> the <kanji>roof</kanji> is the <vocabulary>rooftop</vocabulary>. What else would be up there?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7577', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%8B%E4%B8%8A', 11, 1, '2015-02-25T22:44:09.459974Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(7578, 'vocabulary', '支度', '支度', 'To <kanji>support</kanji> yourself to some <kanji>degree</kanji>, you need <vocabulary>preparation</vocabulary>. Doing your hair and makeup in the morning supports your presentation to some extent, right? 

This is another word for "preparation" besides 準備 and 用意. They’re all similar, but 支度 is usually used in personal contexts, such as 身支度 (getting dressed) or 旅支度 (<vocabulary>getting ready</vocabulary> for a trip).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7578', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%AF%E5%BA%A6', 27, 1, '2015-02-25T22:45:23.70852Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T18:21:17.861861Z'),
(7579, 'vocabulary', '用意', '用意', 'This <kanji>task</kanji> is just an <kanji>idea</kanji> because you''re still doing <vocabulary>preparation</vocabulary> for it. You''re <vocabulary>getting ready</vocabulary>. Once you finish that, the task idea can become a task reality, but until then you''re just preparing.

用意 means having something ready for a specific purpose. Meals, cars, rooms, and even umbrellas are all things you might get to a state of readiness with your 用意. And once it''s 用意''d, that means it''s set and ready to go.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7579', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%A8%E6%84%8F', 11, 1, '2015-02-25T22:46:16.724207Z', NULL, '2026-03-03T17:01:31.736473Z'),
(7580, 'vocabulary', '会員', '会員', 'If you <kanji>meet</kanji> enough <kanji>member</kanji>s you will become a <vocabulary>member</vocabulary> yourself!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7580', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9A%E5%93%A1', 12, 1, '2015-02-25T22:51:34.343429Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:18:26.29811Z'),
(7581, 'vocabulary', '船員', '船員', 'A <kanji>member</kanji> of a <kanji>boat</kanji> is called a <vocabulary>sailor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7581', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%B9%E5%93%A1', 12, 1, '2015-02-25T22:52:20.384356Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:18:26.29811Z'),
(7582, 'vocabulary', '都会', '都会', 'When <kanji>metropolis</kanji>es <kanji>meet</kanji> they become an <vocabulary>urban area</vocabulary>. This word is often used to mean <vocabulary>city</vocabulary> as opposed to the country or rural areas.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7582', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%BD%E4%BC%9A', 12, 1, '2015-02-25T22:53:33.82772Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.234454Z'),
(7583, 'vocabulary', '女神', '女神', 'A <kanji>woman</kanji> who is also a <kanji>god</kanji> is a <vocabulary>goddess</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7583', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%B3%E7%A5%9E', 13, 1, '2015-02-25T22:54:51.996476Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.260304Z'),
(7584, 'vocabulary', '周り', '周り', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>circumference</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>circumference</vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7584', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%A8%E3%82%8A', 14, 1, '2015-02-25T22:56:06.846501Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.284487Z'),
(7585, 'vocabulary', '深さ', '深さ', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>deep</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>depth</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7585', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%B1%E3%81%95', 14, 1, '2015-02-25T22:56:59.824814Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.307735Z'),
(7586, 'vocabulary', '動物園', '動物園', 'Remember how 動物 means animal? Well when you add <kanji>park</kanji> it becomes a <vocabulary>zoo</vocabulary>. It''s a park for animals. Except the animals can''t leave. Ever.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7586', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%95%E7%89%A9%E5%9C%92', 16, 1, '2015-02-25T22:57:48.047123Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.631843Z'),
(7587, 'vocabulary', '記念日', '記念日', 'You should <kanji>write down</kanji> all your <kanji>thoughts</kanji> on this <kanji>day</kanji>, because it''s not just any day — it''s an <vocabulary>anniversary</vocabulary>!

Wondering how 記念日 differs from 周年? 周年 is usually for a numbered anniversary, like 十周年 (10th anniversary), whereas 記念日 refers to the specific date on which a memorable event took place in the past.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7587', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%98%E5%BF%B5%E6%97%A5', 16, 1, '2015-02-25T22:58:24.222025Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.643745Z'),
(7588, 'vocabulary', '変わる', '変わる', 'You''ve seen how 変える means "to change" something. This word means that something changed by itself, so it means <vocabulary>to be changed</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be altered</vocabulary>.

変わる refers to the act of changing into something different, and involves alterations in nature, appearance, or state. For example, it can describe changes in weather or the season, someone undergoing a noticeable change in appearance or behavior, or one''s feelings or thoughts evolving over time.

Since 変わる means "becoming something different," you can also use 変わった and 変わっている to describe someone or something that''s "different from what''s normal." For example, you can say 変わった人 or 変わっている人 to mean "an odd person."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7588', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%89%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8B', 17, 1, '2015-02-25T22:59:41.372251Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.378256Z'),
(7589, 'vocabulary', '勇む', '勇む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>courage</kanji> so the verb version is what happens when you have lots of it. Someone with courage is probably the kind of person who is able <vocabulary>to be in high spirits</vocabulary> in the face of something!

勇む means <vocabulary>to be eager</vocabulary> to face something or to face it with bravery and determination. It often indicates proactive and enthusiastic engagement. You''ll find it in written expressions like 喜び勇む (to joyfully and eagerly undertake something) or 勇んで出かける (to go out with determination).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7589', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%87%E3%82%80', 35, 1, '2015-02-25T23:03:52.759154Z', NULL, '2025-07-09T18:36:46.760149Z'),
(7590, 'vocabulary', '無し', '無し', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>nothing</kanji>, so when you have nothing, you are <vocabulary>without</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7590', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E3%81%97', 17, 1, '2015-02-25T23:04:55.181845Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.436245Z'),
(7591, 'vocabulary', '喜び', '喜び', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>rejoice</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>joy</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>delight</vocabulary>. You rejoice because there are lots of reasons for your <vocabulary>glee</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7591', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%9C%E3%81%B3', 19, 1, '2015-02-25T23:06:00.923364Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.46192Z'),
(7592, 'vocabulary', '警告', '警告', 'When you <kanji>announce</kanji> something to <kanji>warn</kanji> somebody, you''re <vocabulary>warning</vocabulary> them. Sometimes you''re just trying to give them some helpful <vocabulary>advice</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7592', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%A6%E5%91%8A', 18, 1, '2015-02-25T23:08:28.206957Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:34:31.16393Z'),
(7593, 'vocabulary', '連日', '連日', 'What happens when you <kanji>take along</kanji> a <kanji>day</kanji>? It becomes <vocabulary>every day</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7593', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%A3%E6%97%A5', 19, 1, '2015-02-25T23:09:10.850531Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.508556Z'),
(7594, 'vocabulary', '自在', '自在', 'When you''re all by yourself and only your <kanji>self</kanji> <kanji>exist</kanji>s, you probably have <vocabulary>full control</vocabulary>. You can do whatever you want <vocabulary>freely</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>at will</vocabulary>.

自在 is commonly used with the particle に to form the adverb 自在に. This describes how someone can handle something effortlessly, as if they have full control over it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7594', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E5%9C%A8', 20, 1, '2015-02-25T23:10:11.880519Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.531838Z'),
(7595, 'vocabulary', '布地', '布地', '<kanji>Cloth</kanji> from the <kanji>earth</kanji> is still <vocabulary>cloth</vocabulary>, just a little dirty.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7595', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%83%E5%9C%B0', 21, 1, '2015-02-25T23:11:56.263485Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.557212Z'),
(7596, 'vocabulary', '不機嫌', '不機嫌', 'Remember how 機嫌 means mood? Well when you add <kanji>not</kanji> it becomes a <vocabulary>bad mood</vocabulary>. It''s so bad it''s like you have no mood at all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7596', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%A9%9F%E5%AB%8C', 22, 1, '2015-02-25T23:13:08.474182Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:04:30.700843Z'),
(7597, 'vocabulary', '震える', '震える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>earthquake</kanji> so the verb means <vocabulary>to shiver</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to shake</vocabulary>, like you would if there was an earthquake happening right now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7597', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%87%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2015-02-25T23:13:41.866224Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.604515Z'),
(7598, 'vocabulary', '上演', '上演', 'What''s <kanji>above</kanji> the stage and involves <kanji>acting</kanji>? A <vocabulary>performance</vocabulary>!

This word does not refer to an actor''s acting on stage. Instead, it usually refers to a theatrical or musical presentation in its entirety, so it''s used in contexts relating to the act of staging an artistic or dramatic production, or features of the production itself, like its start time or running time (as in 上演時間).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7598', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A%E6%BC%94', 23, 1, '2015-02-25T23:14:35.07961Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(7599, 'vocabulary', '全額', '全額', '<kanji>All</kanji> of the <kanji>amount</kanji> is your <vocabulary>total</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7599', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A8%E9%A1%8D', 24, 1, '2015-02-25T23:16:57.419397Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:47:49.244838Z'),
(7600, 'vocabulary', '見渡す', '見渡す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.  The kanji means <kanji>see</kanji> and <kanji>transit</kanji>, but the vocab isn''t quite the same. You are going to transit your eyes over an area to take in what you see. So you''re really going <vocabulary>to look out over</vocabulary> everything. This word is usually used when you''re looking at landscapes or a really large area.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7600', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E6%B8%A1%E3%81%99', 25, 1, '2015-02-25T23:19:07.47096Z', NULL, '2025-12-17T07:36:01.264771Z'),
(7601, 'vocabulary', '従順', '従順', 'When you <kanji>obey</kanji> <kanji>orders</kanji> people consider you to be an <vocabulary>obedient</vocabulary> person.

This word is relatively formal and somewhat literary.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7601', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%93%E9%A0%86', 26, 1, '2015-02-25T23:20:11.073497Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:19:51.47423Z'),
(7602, 'vocabulary', '何故', '何故', 'When you want to know <kanji>what</kanji> the <kanji>circumstances</kanji> are around you, you have to ask <vocabulary>why</vocabulary>. Why do things happen around you? Asking why is very important if you want to understand the world.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7602', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%95%E6%95%85', 26, 1, '2015-02-25T23:20:57.189397Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(7603, 'vocabulary', '居間', '居間', 'A room that comes <kanji>alive</kanji> in <kanji>interval</kanji>s is your <vocabulary>living room</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>sitting room</vocabulary>. You don''t spend all your time in there, so it can only be alive sometimes.

Note that 居間 is a little old-fashioned. These days, the more common word for a living room is リビングルーム.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7603', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%85%E9%96%93', 27, 1, '2015-02-25T23:21:40.998643Z', NULL, '2025-07-09T18:01:37.117475Z'),
(7604, 'vocabulary', '寄与', '寄与', 'When you <kanji>draw near</kanji> to <kanji>give</kanji> something, you are probably giving a <vocabulary>contribution</vocabulary>.

寄与 is a very formal word that refers to significant contributions in professional or official settings. It''s commonly used in academic or business contexts to highlight how someone''s work contributes to the advancement of a field of study or the growth of a company.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7604', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%84%E4%B8%8E', 29, 1, '2015-02-25T23:22:22.393042Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.545431Z'),
(7605, 'vocabulary', '幾度', '幾度', '<kanji>How many</kanji> <kanji>occurence</kanji>s is just another way of asking <vocabulary>how many times</vocabulary>.

This word is more literary and formal than 何回 or 何度.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7605', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%BE%E5%BA%A6', 31, 1, '2015-02-25T23:23:08.873745Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.842742Z'),
(7606, 'vocabulary', '恐い', '恐い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>fear</kanji>? <vocabulary>Scary</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>frightening</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>I''m afraid</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>I''m scared </vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7606', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%90%E3%81%84', 32, 1, '2015-02-25T23:24:23.556058Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.854335Z'),
(7607, 'vocabulary', '背が高い', '背が高い', 'This is the words 背 and 高い put together to make one adjective. When your height is tall it means you are a <vocabulary>tall</vocabulary> person. This word is only used for tall people though, not anything else that''s tall like a building or a tree.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7607', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%8C%E3%81%8C%E9%AB%98%E3%81%84', 32, 1, '2015-02-25T23:25:19.193179Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.866716Z'),
(7608, 'vocabulary', '幕府', '幕府', 'A <kanji>government</kanji> behind a <kanji>curtain</kanji> is the <vocabulary>bakufu</vocabulary>. They didn''t let anyone know their government secrets so they would hide behind that curtain and keep everything to themselves.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7608', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%95%E5%BA%9C', 34, 1, '2015-02-25T23:26:44.084915Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:25:18.136405Z'),
(7609, 'vocabulary', '大幅', '大幅', 'Something <kanji>big</kanji> in <kanji>width</kanji> is <vocabulary>large</vocabulary>. In fact, it''s of a pretty <vocabulary>substantial</vocabulary> size. This word is used to describe a large magnitude or degree of change, increase, decrease, or difference in a given situation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7609', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%B9%85', 36, 1, '2015-02-25T23:27:54.930662Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(7610, 'vocabulary', '奥深い', '奥深い', 'If your <kanji>interior</kanji> is <kanji>deep</kanji>, then you''re a really <vocabulary>profound</vocabulary> person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7610', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%A5%E6%B7%B1%E3%81%84', 38, 1, '2015-02-25T23:28:46.2431Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.913558Z'),
(7611, 'vocabulary', '衣服', '衣服', '<kanji>Clothes</kanji> <kanji>clothes</kanji> are also known as <vocabulary>clothing</vocabulary>!

衣服 is slightly more formal than the everyday word 服. It''s kind of like saying "clothing" or "<vocabulary>garment</vocabulary>s" instead of just "<vocabulary>clothes</vocabulary>."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7611', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%A3%E6%9C%8D', 22, 1, '2015-02-25T23:29:47.914813Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:51.936249Z'),
(7612, 'vocabulary', '縁', '縁', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

縁 often describes the physical outlines of something, such as メガネの縁 (frames of glasses) or 池の縁 (the <vocabulary>edge</vocabulary> of a pond).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7612', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%81', 44, 1, '2015-02-25T23:30:32.294839Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:23:13.568105Z'),
(7613, 'vocabulary', '伯父', '伯父', 'Who is the <kanji>chief</kanji> <kanji>father</kanji>? That''s your <vocabulary>uncle</vocabulary>. And this uncle is the older brother of your mom or dad because he is the chief father. An uncle who is a younger brother of your parent is 叔父, which is another word for "uncle" you already know!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7613', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%AF%E7%88%B6', 50, 1, '2015-02-25T23:32:02.301404Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:01:24.564442Z'),
(7614, 'vocabulary', '九日', '九日', '<vocabulary>Ninth day</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>day nine</vocabulary>. No matter how you look at this word, it makes a lot of sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7614', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%9D%E6%97%A5', 2, 2, '2015-03-23T22:08:05.931291Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:40:38.042901Z'),
(7615, 'vocabulary', '八日', '八日', '<vocabulary>Eighth day</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>day eight</vocabulary>. No matter how you look at this word, it makes a lot of sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7615', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AB%E6%97%A5', 2, 2, '2015-03-23T22:12:16.264985Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.003887Z'),
(7616, 'vocabulary', '十日', '十日', '<vocabulary>Tenth day</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>day ten</vocabulary>. No matter how you look at this word, it makes a lot of sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7616', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%81%E6%97%A5', 2, 2, '2015-03-23T22:15:09.052837Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.02781Z'),
(7617, 'vocabulary', '一生', '一生', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>life</kanji> is all you get. It''s your <vocabulary>whole life</vocabulary>. This can also mean <vocabulary>all one''s life</vocabulary>, and when it''s used as an adjective it means <vocabulary>lifelong</vocabulary>.

一生 is about life as a span of time: your whole life from start to finish. You''ll see it in phrases like 一生の友人 (a lifelong friend) or 一生わすれない (to never forget something for the rest of one''s life).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7617', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E7%94%9F', 5, 1, '2015-03-23T22:17:01.968675Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:25:14.273424Z'),
(7618, 'vocabulary', '国王', '国王', 'A <kanji>country</kanji> <kanji>king</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>king</vocabulary>, just of a bigger area.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7618', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BD%E7%8E%8B', 6, 1, '2015-03-23T22:18:59.496698Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.370953Z'),
(7619, 'vocabulary', '水星', '水星', 'The <kanji>water</kanji> <kanji>star</kanji> is the planet <vocabulary>Mercury</vocabulary>. That''s where we hid all of our secret moon water. Mercury looks enough like our moon, so we put it there so no one could find it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7619', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E6%98%9F', 6, 1, '2015-03-23T22:21:10.059092Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T02:27:52.417356Z'),
(7620, 'vocabulary', '金星', '金星', 'The <kanji>gold</kanji> <kanji>star</kanji> is <vocabulary>Venus</vocabulary>. It''s the only planet in our solar system that''s the same color as gold.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7620', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%91%E6%98%9F', 6, 1, '2015-03-23T22:23:23.337269Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T02:27:52.417356Z'),
(7621, 'vocabulary', '売れる', '売れる', 'You know that the kanji means <kanji>sell</kanji> and the word 売る means "to sell something." 売れる is the intransitive version of this, so this word means for something <vocabulary>to sell</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be sold</vocabulary>.

It can also be used for something <vocabulary>to sell well</vocabulary>, that often results in that thing beginning <vocabulary>to be popular</vocabulary>.

You can remember this because it''s <reading>rare</reading> (れる) to see something sell really well to the point where it gets popular!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7621', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%B2%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2015-03-23T22:25:38.931012Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.900048Z'),
(7622, 'vocabulary', '船体', '船体', 'A <kanji>boat</kanji>''s <kanji>body</kanji> is called the <vocabulary>hull</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7622', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%B9%E4%BD%93', 7, 1, '2015-03-23T22:27:20.910225Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.261123Z'),
(7623, 'vocabulary', '当たり前', '当たり前', 'You''ve seen how 当たり means success. If you''ve experienced success <kanji>before</kanji>, everything after that seems to be pretty <vocabulary>obvious</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7623', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%93%E3%81%9F%E3%82%8A%E5%89%8D', 8, 1, '2015-03-23T22:28:52.39737Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.505928Z'),
(7624, 'vocabulary', '水道', '水道', 'A <kanji>water</kanji> <kanji>road</kanji> carries your <vocabulary>water supply</vocabulary>. In the old days, these were called <vocabulary>aqueduct</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7624', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E9%81%93', 8, 1, '2015-03-23T22:30:32.371206Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.522876Z'),
(7625, 'vocabulary', '付近', '付近', 'If you <kanji>attach</kanji> things <kanji>near</kanji> you, they end up being in the same <vocabulary>neighborhood</vocabulary> as you. They''re attached <vocabulary>nearby</vocabulary> so they can''t go much further. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7625', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%98%E8%BF%91', 9, 1, '2015-03-23T22:32:10.773423Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:03:42.596356Z'),
(7626, 'vocabulary', '欠かす', '欠かす', 'Remember how 欠 means <kanji>lack</kanji>? This verb is related. The 欠かす form is a little tricky, but it''s sort of like you''re causing yourself to lack something. So it means <vocabulary>to do without</vocabulary>. Depending on the context, it can also mean <vocabulary>to miss an event</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to fail to do</vocabulary> something. Just keep in mind this word is most often used in the negative, like 欠かせないもの (things you can''t do without) or 朝のコーヒーは欠かさない (never go without morning coffee).

You can remember 欠かす this way: Since you cause yourself to lack something with this word, it''s all your fault. That''s why you want to <reading>cuss</reading> (かす). You made yourself miss out on something. Well, $*%!!!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7626', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%A0%E3%81%8B%E3%81%99', 11, 1, '2015-03-23T22:33:36.041447Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:26.433061Z'),
(7627, 'vocabulary', '見物', '見物', 'If you try to <kanji>see</kanji> a bunch of <kanji>thing</kanji>s at once, you are probably <vocabulary>sightseeing</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>watching</vocabulary>. This can also refer to the person doing the action, so it can sometimes mean <vocabulary>sightseer</vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7627', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E7%89%A9', 11, 1, '2015-03-23T22:36:03.948507Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:22:48.599518Z'),
(7628, 'vocabulary', '入院', '入院', 'So you''re <kanji>enter</kanji>ing an <kanji>institution</kanji>. But we''re not talking about any institution, we''re talking specifically about hospitals. So this word means <vocabulary>hospitalization</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7628', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E9%99%A2', 10, 1, '2015-03-23T22:39:11.643743Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.756025Z'),
(7629, 'vocabulary', '葉', '葉', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7629', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%91%89', 10, 1, '2015-03-23T22:41:00.808251Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:10:47.677773Z'),
(7630, 'vocabulary', '予め', '予め', 'The kanji and the word are almost exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Note that 予め is mostly used in formal contexts like meetings, announcements or notices, and business emails.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7630', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%88%E3%82%81', 11, 1, '2015-03-23T22:48:59.7428Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.349394Z'),
(7631, 'vocabulary', '平仮名', '平仮名', 'Remember how you learned that 仮名 means "kana?" Well this is <kanji>flat</kanji> kana. Flat kana is just <vocabulary>hiragana</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7631', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B3%E4%BB%AE%E5%90%8D', 11, 1, '2015-03-23T22:59:25.074793Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(7632, 'vocabulary', '文字通り', '文字通り', 'Remember how you learned the words 文字 and 通り? Well an "avenue" made of "letters" is <vocabulary>literally</vocabulary> <vocabulary>to the letter</vocabulary>. Wink.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7632', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%87%E5%AD%97%E9%80%9A%E3%82%8A', 11, 1, '2015-03-23T23:03:18.738966Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.396836Z'),
(7633, 'vocabulary', '相図-7633', '相図', 'A <kanji>mutual</kanji> <kanji>diagram</kanji> is one that''s for everyone to see. It''s a <vocabulary>sign</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7633', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B8%E5%9B%B3-7633', 11, 1, '2015-03-23T23:04:46.243274Z', '2020-05-19T22:26:06.339245Z', '2025-06-11T05:33:52.410359Z'),
(7634, 'vocabulary', '島', '島', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7634', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B3%B6', 12, 1, '2015-03-23T23:05:51.862753Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.435668Z'),
(7635, 'vocabulary', '落ち', '落ち', 'When you''re talking or writing a paper, the part that <kanji>falls</kanji> at the end is the <vocabulary>conclusion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7635', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%90%BD%E3%81%A1', 12, 1, '2015-03-23T23:07:17.303419Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:46:04.638998Z'),
(7636, 'vocabulary', '陽気', '陽気', 'If you have a <kanji>sunshine</kanji> <kanji>spirit</kanji>, you''re probably a really <vocabulary>cheerful</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>jovial</vocabulary> person. 

陽気 can also refer to <vocabulary>warm weather</vocabulary>. (Technically it refers to weather or climate in general, but these days people associate it with warm, sunny weather because of its kanji.)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7636', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%BD%E6%B0%97', 16, 1, '2015-03-23T23:09:23.071838Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.847716Z'),
(7637, 'vocabulary', '学問', '学問', 'How can you solve your <kanji>study</kanji> <kanji>problem</kanji>? With a little extra <vocabulary>schooling</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>study</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>learning</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7637', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A6%E5%95%8F', 13, 1, '2015-03-23T23:10:31.101749Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.507931Z'),
(7638, 'vocabulary', '周期', '周期', 'If you follow the <kanji>circumference</kanji> of a <kanji>period of time</kanji>, you end up back at the beginning and it makes a full <vocabulary>cycle</vocabulary>. Events in history always repeat, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7638', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%A8%E6%9C%9F', 14, 1, '2015-03-23T23:11:39.903362Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.529371Z'),
(7639, 'vocabulary', '理性', '理性', 'If you have <kanji>reason</kanji> in your <kanji>nature</kanji>, you have <vocabulary>reason</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>sense</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>intellect</vocabulary>.

This is "reason" as in logical thinking.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7639', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%90%86%E6%80%A7', 14, 1, '2015-03-23T23:12:50.820405Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.551382Z'),
(7640, 'vocabulary', '情けない', '情けない', 'You''ve seen how the word 情け means pity, so adding ない to that turns it into an adjective meaning <vocabulary>pitiable</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>miserable</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7640', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%85%E3%81%91%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84', 15, 1, '2015-03-23T23:14:04.594115Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.797351Z'),
(7641, 'vocabulary', '場面', '場面', 'Everything on a <kanji>location</kanji>''s <kanji>surface</kanji> is really just a part of the <vocabulary>scene</vocabulary> before you. This is the kind of scene that would be set in a novel. It''s the <vocabulary>setting</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7641', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%B4%E9%9D%A2', 16, 1, '2015-03-23T23:15:06.070802Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.70928Z'),
(7642, 'vocabulary', '辞典', '辞典', 'You need to know the <kanji>word</kanji> <kanji>rule</kanji>s of the language you''re learning, don''t you? Then you need a good <vocabulary>dictionary</vocabulary> that can explain its <vocabulary>lexicon</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7642', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%9E%E5%85%B8', 16, 1, '2015-03-23T23:16:23.146413Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.723656Z'),
(7643, 'vocabulary', '完結', '完結', 'The <kanji>perfect</kanji> way to <kanji>tie</kanji> up a research paper is with a <vocabulary>conclusion</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7643', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%8C%E7%B5%90', 17, 1, '2015-03-23T23:20:07.996275Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.636831Z'),
(7644, 'vocabulary', '無言', '無言', 'When you have <kanji>nothing</kanji> to <kanji>say</kanji> you sit in <vocabulary>silence</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7644', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E8%A8%80', 17, 1, '2015-03-23T23:21:16.602351Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.66071Z'),
(7645, 'vocabulary', '固まる', '固まる', 'You''ve seen how 固める means "to make hard." This word means that something hardened by itself, it means <vocabulary>to harden</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to solidify</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7645', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BA%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2015-03-23T23:23:40.70723Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.682344Z'),
(7646, 'vocabulary', '達する', '達する', 'You know that 達 means <kanji>attain</kanji>. So, when you add する to it you make it a verb. Now it means <vocabulary>to attain</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7646', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%94%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2015-03-23T23:25:13.648158Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:06:12.518486Z'),
(7647, 'vocabulary', '関連', '関連', 'If you <kanji>take along</kanji> some things that are <kanji>related</kanji>, you''ll be able to understand their <vocabulary>relation</vocabulary> better.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7647', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%A2%E9%80%A3', 19, 1, '2015-03-23T23:26:24.381125Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.729498Z'),
(7648, 'vocabulary', '議会', '議会', 'The place you <kanji>meet</kanji> for <kanji>deliberation</kanji> in Japan is called the <vocabulary>Diet</vocabulary>. It''s their version of <vocabulary>congress</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7648', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%B0%E4%BC%9A', 20, 1, '2015-03-24T15:43:43.339527Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.753671Z'),
(7649, 'vocabulary', '得体', '得体', 'With time your <kanji>body</kanji> will <kanji>acquire</kanji> a certain type of <vocabulary>character</vocabulary>. It shows your true <vocabulary>nature</vocabulary> as a person.

Note: We almost always use this word in the phrases 得体が知れない/得体の知れない, so check out the context sentences to see what kind of "character" we''re talking about.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7649', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%97%E4%BD%93', 21, 1, '2015-03-24T15:45:57.564341Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.777065Z'),
(7650, 'vocabulary', '価格', '価格', 'When you <kanji>value</kanji> <kanji>status</kanji> you really care about the <vocabulary>price</vocabulary> of things. And usually the higher the <vocabulary>value</vocabulary>, the higher the <vocabulary>cost</vocabulary>.

価格 refers to something''s market value, or to product pricing in a broader sense. It''s sort of a formal word, so you might see it on fliers or in business and economic discussions about pricing strategies, for example.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7650', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%A1%E6%A0%BC', 22, 1, '2015-03-24T15:47:51.745779Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:51:00.759578Z'),
(7651, 'vocabulary', '台詞', '台詞', 'A <kanji>machine</kanji> that can quickly generate a <kanji>part of speech</kanji> is handy for remembering <vocabulary>one''s lines</vocabulary> on stage. If you forget your <vocabulary>line</vocabulary>, just activate the machine, and it will give you a helpful prompt: "Adjective! Adjective!"

台詞 usually means a line or speech in a play, movie, or drama — something a character says out loud. But it''s also used in everyday talk when someone makes a <vocabulary>remark</vocabulary> that''s clever, dramatic, or memorable, almost like quoting a line.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7651', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B0%E8%A9%9E', 23, 1, '2015-03-24T15:49:01.689897Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:26.568187Z'),
(7652, 'vocabulary', '左腕', '左腕', 'Your <kanji>left</kanji> <kanji>arm</kanji> is your <vocabulary>left arm</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7652', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A6%E8%85%95', 24, 1, '2015-03-24T15:53:44.810402Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.845777Z'),
(7653, 'vocabulary', '額', '額', 'You previously learned this kanji with three different meanings. In vocabulary form, it just has one of these meanings, <vocabulary>framed picture</vocabulary>! Similarly, it can just mean <vocabulary>picture</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>frame</vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7653', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A1%8D', 24, 1, '2015-03-24T15:55:34.435782Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.868393Z'),
(7654, 'vocabulary', '規則正しい', '規則正しい', 'This is a combination of the words 規則 and 正しい, that you learned already. If you follow the rules in the correct order, everything will stay <vocabulary>regular</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>orderly</vocabulary>.

This word means that something follows a regular pattern or schedule. It’s commonly used to describe disciplined lifestyles, such as with regular sleep, exercise, and healthy eating habits, and implies that the regularity is desirable.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7654', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8F%E5%89%87%E6%AD%A3%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 25, 1, '2015-03-24T15:56:39.856903Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.892163Z'),
(7655, 'vocabulary', '怪物', '怪物', 'If you come across a <kanji>suspicious</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji> it''s probably a <vocabulary>monster</vocabulary>! Run away!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7655', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%AA%E7%89%A9', 26, 1, '2015-03-24T16:02:57.223839Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(7656, 'vocabulary', '屈む', '屈む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>yield</kanji>, and 屈む means <vocabulary>to bend down</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to crouch</vocabulary>. After all, bending down and crouching are sort of like yielding to gravity, don’t you think?

屈む refers to the act of lowering your head or body, so it can also mean <vocabulary>to duck</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to squat</vocabulary> depending on the context.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7656', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%88%E3%82%80', 27, 1, '2015-03-24T16:05:52.962097Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:52.93417Z'),
(7657, 'vocabulary', '家政婦', '家政婦', 'Your <kanji>house</kanji> <kanji>politics</kanji> are in the hands of one <kanji>lady</kanji>: your <vocabulary>housekeeper</vocabulary>. Your housekeeper knows more about your house than you do — she cleans the place, after all — so it''s only natural that she would control all its politics too.

Note that it''s common to use the polite honorific さん when talking about a 家政婦さん!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7657', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B6%E6%94%BF%E5%A9%A6', 29, 1, '2015-03-24T16:07:25.338087Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:47:17.736501Z'),
(7658, 'vocabulary', '寄付', '寄付', 'If you <kanji>draw near</kanji> someone and <kanji>attach</kanji> some stuff to them, you''re making a <vocabulary>donation</vocabulary>. They get to keep it, after all. 

寄付 means donating money, goods, or resources, usually for a charitable purpose.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7658', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%84%E4%BB%98', 29, 1, '2015-03-24T16:09:18.019563Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:03:42.596356Z'),
(7659, 'vocabulary', '快楽', '快楽', '<kanji>Pleasant</kanji> <kanji>comfort</kanji> brings you a lot of <vocabulary>pleasure</vocabulary>.

快楽 is more commonly used in writing and often describes pleasure with a sense of indulgence, especially physical or sensory pleasure.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7659', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%AB%E6%A5%BD', 30, 1, '2015-03-24T16:10:21.638764Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T18:49:45.773585Z'),
(7660, 'vocabulary', '恐らく', '恐らく', 'The kanji in this word means <kanji>fear</kanji>, but the word itself means <vocabulary>probably</vocabulary>. I guess you only have to fear something if it''s <em>probably</em> going to happen, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7660', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%90%E3%82%89%E3%81%8F', 31, 1, '2015-03-24T16:12:04.156447Z', NULL, '2025-07-22T20:27:06.171731Z'),
(7661, 'vocabulary', '飾り', '飾り', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>decorate</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>decoration</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7661', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%BE%E3%82%8A', 32, 1, '2015-03-24T16:13:04.991821Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:53.065398Z'),
(7662, 'vocabulary', '貴重', '貴重', 'Something that''s <kanji>valuable</kanji> and <kanji>heavy</kanji> is extremely <vocabulary>precious</vocabulary> and probably even more <vocabulary>valuable</vocabulary> than your less heavy things.

貴重 is used especially when something is rare, important, or hard to replace. You can use it for physical things, but you''ll see it used to show appreciation for intangible things too, like 貴重なご意見 (your valuable opinion) or 貴重な体験 (a precious experience).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7662', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%B4%E9%87%8D', 35, 1, '2015-03-24T16:15:14.459233Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T06:27:32.779088Z'),
(7663, 'vocabulary', '舞', '舞', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7663', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%9E', 36, 1, '2015-03-24T16:17:39.524676Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:53.091019Z'),
(7664, 'vocabulary', '欠伸', '欠伸', 'When you <kanji>lack</kanji> <kanji>stretch</kanji>ing, your body has to get that energy out somehow, so you end up <vocabulary>yawning</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7664', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%A0%E4%BC%B8', 38, 1, '2015-03-24T16:26:22.659765Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:53.104187Z'),
(7665, 'vocabulary', '小柄', '小柄', 'Someone built from a <kanji>small</kanji> <kanji>pattern</kanji> has a <vocabulary>small build</vocabulary>. I know lots of little old ladies that were built from these small patterns.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7665', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E6%9F%84', 42, 1, '2015-03-24T16:27:20.878498Z', NULL, '2025-10-17T02:37:45.272844Z'),
(7666, 'vocabulary', '落雷', '落雷', '<kanji>Thunder</kanji> that <kanji>fall</kanji>s from the sky is a <vocabulary>thunderbolt</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7666', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%90%BD%E9%9B%B7', 44, 1, '2015-03-24T16:28:31.34092Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:46:04.638998Z'),
(7667, 'vocabulary', '灯る', '灯る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>lamp</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to be lit</vocabulary>.

灯る describes a light appearing and beginning to glow, like ろうそくに火が灯る (a candle is lit) or 灯りが灯る (a light comes on). Compared to more matter-of-fact words like つく (turn on), 灯る feels softer and a bit more descriptive, almost like the light is quietly coming to life rather than simply switching on.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7667', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%AF%E3%82%8B', 50, 1, '2015-03-24T16:29:32.541522Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T19:56:31.688597Z'),
(7668, 'vocabulary', '大いに-7668', '大いに', 'This words is all about doing something in a <kanji>big</kanji> way. Particle に on the end tells us its an adverb, meaning that it describes <i>how</i> an action is carried out. It can mean <vocabulary>very</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>a lot of</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>greatly</vocabulary>, depending on the context. 
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7668', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E3%81%84%E3%81%AB-7668', 2, 2, '2015-05-11T16:56:31.629975Z', '2020-04-03T18:44:03.036015Z', '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(7669, 'vocabulary', '用', '用', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7669', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%A8', 3, 1, '2015-05-11T17:00:59.299439Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T02:25:41.390326Z'),
(7670, 'vocabulary', '仕草', '仕草', 'Things you end up <kanji>doing</kanji> in the <kanji>grass</kanji> and not inside probably take a lot of big <vocabulary>gesture</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>action</vocabulary>s. You have to do them outside on the grass because if you did them anywhere else you might break something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7670', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%95%E8%8D%89', 5, 1, '2015-05-11T17:03:54.712226Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.918463Z'),
(7671, 'vocabulary', '生える', '生える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. Remember how 生きる means "to live?" Well this is what happens right after it starts to live. It begins <vocabulary>to grow</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7671', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 6, 1, '2015-05-11T17:09:40.138237Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:59:46.377049Z'),
(7672, 'vocabulary', '〜形', '〜形', 'The kanji and the word share one of the same meanings: <vocabulary>form</vocabulary>. It doesn''t just mean form though, it means <vocabulary>tense</vocabulary> too. Like verb tense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7672', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%BD%A2', 5, 1, '2015-05-12T18:51:52.391156Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.934199Z'),
(7673, 'vocabulary', '土星', '土星', 'The <kanji>ground</kanji> <kanji>star</kanji> is <vocabulary>Saturn</vocabulary>. That''s why it''s called "sat"urn. Because the first people to find it sat on the ground there. It''s a star made completely of sit-on-able ground.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7673', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%9F%E6%98%9F', 6, 1, '2015-05-12T18:53:06.236499Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T02:27:52.417356Z'),
(7674, 'vocabulary', '活気', '活気', 'If you have <kanji>lively</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji> you have more than enough <vocabulary>energy</vocabulary> for everyone to start noticing. They come up to you and compliment you on your amazing <vocabulary>liveliness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7674', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%BB%E6%B0%97', 6, 1, '2015-05-12T18:57:45.69108Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(7675, 'vocabulary', '〜号室', '〜号室', 'This is a counter that can be used after numbers to let you know what <kanji>number</kanji> <kanji>room</kanji> it is. It''s a <vocabulary>room number</vocabulary> that you use in apartments and hotels to know exactly which room you''re looking for.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7675', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%8F%B7%E5%AE%A4', 7, 1, '2015-05-12T19:00:40.577633Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:57:32.391485Z'),
(7676, 'vocabulary', '大空', '大空', 'The <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>sky</kanji> isn''t just any old patch of sky. It''s <vocabulary>the skies</vocabulary> above — <vocabulary>the open sky</vocabulary> where birds soar, <vocabulary>the heavens</vocabulary> where stars twinkle.

You already learned 空 for "sky," right? 大空 basically means the same thing, but with a more poetic sense of grandeur and scale. You won''t hear it much in everyday conversation, but you''ll encounter it in things like literature, song lyrics, and slogans or taglines for aviation-related media.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7676', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E7%A9%BA', 7, 1, '2015-05-12T19:03:23.872318Z', NULL, '2026-01-22T21:45:57.488942Z'),
(7677, 'vocabulary', '見当たる', '見当たる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. 

見当たる is made up of the kanji for  <kanji>see</kanji> and <kanji>right</kanji>, so what happens when you see the right thing? It was able <vocabulary>to be found</vocabulary>! 

This word is generally used in the negative form (見当たらない). You can use it as a roundabout expression for when you can’t find what you’re looking for.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7677', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E5%BD%93%E3%81%9F%E3%82%8B', 7, 1, '2015-05-12T19:14:30.665024Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.308589Z'),
(7678, 'vocabulary', '三角形', '三角形', 'You already learned that 三角 means "triangle." Well this means <vocabulary>triangle</vocabulary> too, but it''s the mathematical term. You should use it in math class.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7678', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E8%A7%92%E5%BD%A2', 7, 1, '2015-05-12T19:53:55.638347Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.319638Z'),
(7679, 'vocabulary', '活用形', '活用形', 'You know that 活用 means "conjugation" and that 〜形 means "form," so that means this is the <vocabulary>conjugated form</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7679', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%BB%E7%94%A8%E5%BD%A2', 7, 1, '2015-05-12T20:04:29.407073Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.34688Z'),
(7680, 'vocabulary', '役場', '役場', 'A <kanji>service</kanji> <kanji>location</kanji> is a place that people go when they''re in need of a service. The best place for this is the <vocabulary>town hall</vocabulary>. Everyone goes to this one location to voice their complaints, and in return their needs are serviced by the officials in the town hall.

役場 specifically refers to the administrative office of a town (町) or village (村) rather than a city (市). Depending on the location, it may be also be called a 町役場 or 村役場.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7680', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%B9%E5%A0%B4', 10, 1, '2015-05-12T20:10:57.600785Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.447394Z'),
(7681, 'vocabulary', '点ける', '点ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. 

The kanji itself means <kanji>point</kanji> so the verb version is something you need to point your finger to do. Picture pointing your finger and pushing a lightswitch <vocabulary>to turn something on</vocabulary>, in this case the light. You won''t be able <vocabulary>to switch something on</vocabulary> if you can''t point your finger.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7681', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%B9%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 8, 1, '2015-05-12T20:15:55.821054Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.545409Z'),
(7682, 'vocabulary', '何度', '何度', 'If someone asks you <kanji>what</kanji> <kanji>occurrence</kanji> or <kanji>what</kanji> <kanji>degree</kanji>s they''re really trying to ask you <vocabulary>how many times</vocabulary> something happened. Sometimes they''re asking <vocabulary>what temperature</vocabulary> it is too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7682', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%95%E5%BA%A6', 9, 1, '2015-05-12T20:23:03.04555Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(7683, 'vocabulary', '泳ぎ', '泳ぎ', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>swim</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>swimming</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7683', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%B3%E3%81%8E', 9, 1, '2015-05-12T20:24:23.71433Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.756319Z'),
(7684, 'vocabulary', '記事', '記事', 'When you <kanji>write down</kanji> a lot of stuff focusing on one <kanji>thing</kanji> it''s called an <vocabulary>article</vocabulary>. If it''s on something that just happened it will probably be a <vocabulary>news story</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7684', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%98%E4%BA%8B', 9, 1, '2015-05-12T20:27:06.260952Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.766422Z'),
(7685, 'vocabulary', '口調', '口調', 'When your <kanji>mouth</kanji> makes <kanji>tone</kanji>s we call it your <vocabulary>tone</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>tone of voice</vocabulary>. They''re different kinds of sounds you make with your mouth that make you sound happy, sad, mean, etc.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7685', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%A3%E8%AA%BF', 10, 1, '2015-05-12T20:29:41.340515Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.457717Z'),
(7686, 'vocabulary', '語る', '語る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>language</kanji>, so when you do language you''re going <vocabulary>to talk</vocabulary> about something. Maybe you''re going <vocabulary>to tell</vocabulary> a story, or the truth.

This verb is transitive, so it generally takes a direct object in Japanese, even though the English translations might not.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7686', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%9E%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2015-05-12T20:32:43.608672Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.468298Z'),
(7687, 'vocabulary', '初歩', '初歩', 'The <kanji>first</kanji> thing you <kanji>walk</kanji> through when you learn something new will always be the <vocabulary>basics</vocabulary>. You never start by running, you always have to start slow and work your way through the <vocabulary>ABCs</vocabulary> before you start to run.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7687', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%9D%E6%AD%A9', 11, 1, '2015-05-12T20:35:00.392196Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:28:35.812439Z'),
(7688, 'vocabulary', '待合', '待合', 'The place you <kanji>wait</kanji> to <kanji>join</kanji> other people is a <vocabulary>waiting room</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>meeting place</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7688', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%85%E5%90%88', 14, 1, '2015-05-12T20:36:48.024037Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:53.624033Z'),
(7689, 'vocabulary', '新た', '新た', 'This is the adverb version of the kanji <kanji>new</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>new</vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7689', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E3%81%9F', 11, 1, '2015-05-12T20:38:22.421727Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:53.647278Z'),
(7690, 'vocabulary', '西洋', '西洋', 'Go <kanji>west</kanji> until you encounter <kanji>Western style</kanji> buildings and customs and languages everywhere. Hey, you''ve made it to <vocabulary>the West</vocabulary>! 

西洋 refers to <vocabulary>Western countries</vocabulary>, especially European and English-speaking countries. It''s the same kind of "West" that people mean when they talk about <vocabulary>Western</vocabulary> culture and all that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7690', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%BF%E6%B4%8B', 11, 1, '2015-05-12T20:42:41.016455Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T21:53:15.126796Z'),
(7691, 'vocabulary', '期間', '期間', 'A <kanji>period of time</kanji> <kanji>interval</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>period</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>interval</vocabulary> of time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7691', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%9F%E9%96%93', 12, 1, '2015-05-12T20:44:12.940381Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:53.697514Z'),
(7692, 'vocabulary', '行動', '行動', 'When you <kanji>go</kanji> to <kanji>move</kanji> you''re actually making an <vocabulary>action</vocabulary>. This word can also mean <vocabulary>behavior</vocabulary>, since your actions are what make up your behavior.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7692', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%8C%E5%8B%95', 12, 1, '2015-05-12T20:48:14.720214Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:53.720666Z'),
(7693, 'vocabulary', '集まり', '集まり', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>gather</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>gathering</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7693', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%86%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8A', 12, 1, '2015-05-12T20:49:41.450128Z', NULL, '2025-09-26T00:48:49.550244Z'),
(7694, 'vocabulary', '想定', '想定', 'When you <kanji>imagine</kanji> something that''s somewhat <kanji>determine</kanji>d, that means you already have some sort of <vocabulary>assumption</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>expectation</vocabulary> about it.

想定 usually refers to assumptions made in advance about certain conditions or situations. It’s often used when talking about preparing for or anticipating future events, especially ones where planning might be required, like natural disasters.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7694', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%B3%E5%AE%9A', 13, 1, '2015-05-12T20:50:49.354868Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(7695, 'vocabulary', '悲しみ', '悲しみ', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>sad</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>sadness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7695', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%B2%E3%81%97%E3%81%BF', 19, 1, '2015-05-12T20:55:03.019418Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:53.785846Z'),
(7696, 'vocabulary', '言葉つき', '言葉つき', 'Remember how 言葉 means "language?" Well, the way you "attach" (付き) language to things is your <vocabulary>diction</vocabulary>. Everyone has their own 言葉つき when they speak. How would you describe your 言葉つき?

言葉つき refers to the characteristics of <vocabulary>how one speaks</vocabulary>, including the words they choose, how they say them, and their overall speaking style.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7696', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%80%E8%91%89%E3%81%A4%E3%81%8D', 14, 1, '2015-05-12T21:07:53.369874Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:10:47.677773Z'),
(7697, 'vocabulary', '放つ', '放つ', 'Remember 放す (to release) and 放れる (to get loose)? 放つ is another transitive verb of the <kanji>release</kanji> family, but this is a more dynamic kind of releasing, so it means <vocabulary>to fire</vocabulary> off something. 

You can remember this because you usually need つ (two) hands to fire something off properly, whether it''s a slingshot or a hit in baseball.

放つ often implies setting something free with force or impact. It''s used to describe things like shooting arrows, firing bullets, letting animals loose into the wild, or even strongly emitting something like light or an offensive smell. More figuratively, it can describe the act of releasing something into the world, whether that''s a creative work you''re unveiling or a hot take you''re firing off on social media.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7697', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BE%E3%81%A4', 15, 1, '2015-05-12T21:16:30.96938Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.810695Z'),
(7698, 'vocabulary', '幸運', '幸運', 'When you <kanji>carry</kanji> <kanji>happiness</kanji> with you, you look at the good side of everything and end up having really <vocabulary>good luck</vocabulary>. Someone who carries happiness will be really lucky in life.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7698', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B8%E9%81%8B', 16, 1, '2015-05-12T21:26:24.381473Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.734497Z'),
(7699, 'vocabulary', '遠く', '遠く', 'This is the noun/no-adjective version of the kanji <kanji>far</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>far away</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>distant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7699', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%A0%E3%81%8F', 18, 1, '2015-05-12T21:34:43.594765Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:53.869125Z'),
(7700, 'vocabulary', '愛する', '愛する', 'You know that 愛 means <kanji>love</kanji>. So, when you add する to it you make it a verb. Now it means <vocabulary>to love</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7700', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9B%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 17, 1, '2015-05-12T21:52:03.209096Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:28:57.446613Z'),
(7701, 'vocabulary', '相変わらず', '相変わらず', 'A <kanji>mutual</kanji> <kanji>change</kanji> you make with other people will go so smoothly that it will seem like everything is working <vocabulary>as usual</vocabulary>. It will be like everything is <vocabulary>the same</vocabulary> as it was before because the change was mutual.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7701', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B8%E5%A4%89%E3%82%8F%E3%82%89%E3%81%9A', 17, 1, '2015-05-12T21:57:13.909642Z', NULL, '2026-02-19T09:33:02.556952Z'),
(7702, 'vocabulary', '子守歌', '子守歌', 'A <kanji>song</kanji> you sing to <kanji>protect</kanji> your <kanji>child</kanji> while they sleep is called a <vocabulary>lullaby</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7702', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%90%E5%AE%88%E6%AD%8C', 18, 1, '2015-05-12T21:59:16.874126Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:03:31.014085Z'),
(7703, 'vocabulary', '高等', '高等', 'When you are <kanji>high</kanji>er than <kanji>equal</kanji>, you are above the very top, you are above everyone. You are <vocabulary>high class</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7703', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E7%AD%89', 18, 1, '2015-05-12T22:02:10.077873Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:53.956663Z'),
(7704, 'vocabulary', '辞書形', '辞書形', 'This is a combination of the word 辞書 "dictionary" and the suffix 〜形 or "form." Put them together and you get <vocabulary>dictionary form</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7704', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%9E%E6%9B%B8%E5%BD%A2', 18, 1, '2015-05-12T22:03:31.551102Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:53.978437Z'),
(7705, 'vocabulary', '会議室', '会議室', 'A <kanji>room</kanji> where you <kanji>meet</kanji> for <kanji>discussion</kanji>s is known as a <vocabulary>conference room</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7705', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9A%E8%AD%B0%E5%AE%A4', 20, 1, '2015-05-12T22:04:37.254379Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:57:32.391485Z'),
(7706, 'vocabulary', '議員', '議員', 'A <kanji>discussion</kanji> <kanji>member</kanji> talks about all the really important things that need to be discussed for the sake of the country. They are a <vocabulary>member of the Diet</vocabulary>. The Diet is Japan''s version of congress. <vocabulary>Diet members</vocabulary> discuss really important matters.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7706', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%B0%E5%93%A1', 20, 1, '2015-05-12T22:06:08.067033Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:18:26.29811Z'),
(7707, 'vocabulary', '尾', '尾', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7707', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%BE', 20, 1, '2015-05-12T22:07:42.210232Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.044797Z'),
(7708, 'vocabulary', '最後尾', '最後尾', 'You have 最, which means <kanji>most</kanji>, and 後尾, which means something like "back" or "rear" (<kanji>behind</kanji> + <kanji>tail</kanji>). When you''re at the most back part of something, you might find yourself at the <vocabulary>end of a line</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>end of a queue</vocabulary>.

最後尾 is often seen on signs at the end of a long line to help people locate where to join the queue.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7708', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%80%E5%BE%8C%E5%B0%BE', 20, 1, '2015-05-12T22:08:34.27891Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.065446Z'),
(7709, 'vocabulary', '心得', '心得', 'What does your <kanji>heart</kanji> want to <kanji>acquire</kanji> above all other things? <vocabulary>Knowledge</vocabulary>! That''s why you''re here, isn''t it? 

Dig deep and see that all your heart wants is more <vocabulary>tips</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>rules</vocabulary>, to gain a better <vocabulary>understanding</vocabulary> of… Japanese!

心得 refers to practical skills or knowledge gained through learning, like expertise in tea ceremony or mastery of a foreign language. It can also mean things to keep in mind in certain situations, like phone etiquette or know-how for a job.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7709', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83%E5%BE%97', 21, 1, '2015-05-12T22:09:24.165765Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.088337Z'),
(7710, 'vocabulary', '嫌悪感', '嫌悪感', '<kanji>Bad</kanji> <kanji>feeling</kanji>s that you <kanji>dislike</kanji> are <vocabulary>unpleasant feelings</vocabulary>. These unpleasant feelings include things like hatred, disgust, revulsion, and fear. All of those are considered to be a part of the term unpleasant feelings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7710', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AB%8C%E6%82%AA%E6%84%9F', 22, 1, '2015-05-12T22:14:57.433811Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(7711, 'vocabulary', '指差す', '指差す', 'When you want to use your <kanji>finger</kanji> to explain the <kanji>distinction</kanji> between more than one thing, you need <vocabulary>to point at</vocabulary> it. Otherwise the person you''re talking to won''t be able to tell which one you''re talking about.

In general, 指差す specifically means using one’s index finger to physically point at something or someone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7711', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87%E5%B7%AE%E3%81%99', 24, 1, '2015-05-12T22:16:28.927669Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:26.789876Z'),
(7712, 'vocabulary', '家庭教師', '家庭教師', 'This is a combination of the words 家庭 and 教師. A "teacher" who only teaches when you''re with your "family" is known as a <vocabulary>tutor</vocabulary>. They don''t teach you at school, they''re a <vocabulary>private teacher</vocabulary> that comes to your home to help you learn.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7712', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B6%E5%BA%AD%E6%95%99%E5%B8%AB', 25, 1, '2015-05-12T22:19:29.578866Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.158516Z'),
(7713, 'vocabulary', '不規則', '不規則', 'You know that 規則 means "rule" or "regulation." So a <kanji>not</kanji> "rule" or "regulation" is an <vocabulary>irregularity</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7713', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E8%A6%8F%E5%89%87', 25, 1, '2015-05-12T22:21:06.514915Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.179503Z'),
(7714, 'vocabulary', 'お祝い', 'お祝い', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>celebrate</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>celebration</vocabulary>! 

お祝い is also used to mean a <vocabulary>congratulatory gift</vocabulary> itself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7714', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E7%A5%9D%E3%81%84', 26, 1, '2015-05-12T22:22:36.471764Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.393722Z'),
(7715, 'vocabulary', '独裁', '独裁', 'When one person is in a position to <kanji>judge</kanji> everyone <kanji>alone</kanji> by themselves, they''re definitely a part of a <vocabulary>dictatorship</vocabulary>. One person should never be alone to judge everyone because that''s how dictators are made and they''re not so great.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7715', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%AC%E8%A3%81', 26, 1, '2015-05-12T22:25:05.17301Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.405291Z'),
(7716, 'vocabulary', '引退', '引退', 'When you <kanji>pull</kanji> yourself back and <kanji>retreat</kanji>, you''re <vocabulary>retiring</vocabulary>. At some point in everyone''s lives you just can''t do it anymore and it''s time to take your <vocabulary>retirement</vocabulary>.

This word focuses on the moment of retiring. It''s often used to mean <vocabulary>withdrawing from public life</vocabulary>, when someone ends their career in a particular field, like when celebrities or pro athletes retire. It can also be used for students who stop extracurricular activities like clubs and sports teams ahead of graduation, often to study for their exams. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7716', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%95%E9%80%80', 27, 1, '2015-05-12T22:26:23.110032Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.269685Z'),
(7717, 'vocabulary', '従兄弟', '従兄弟', 'Remember how 兄弟 means "siblings?" Well when they''re siblings you have to <kanji>obey</kanji> they''re not your brother or sister, they''re your <vocabulary>cousin</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7717', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%93%E5%85%84%E5%BC%9F', 28, 1, '2015-05-12T22:28:06.025915Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T22:21:54.132661Z'),
(7718, 'vocabulary', '居眠り', '居眠り', 'Sometimes even though you think you''re very much awake and <kanji>alive</kanji>, you can''t help fall into a <kanji>sleep</kanji> and start <vocabulary>dozing off</vocabulary>. But you''re not dead! You''re totally alive, so let''s hope no one thinks you''re doing anything other than <vocabulary>nodding off</vocabulary>.

居眠り refers to light, unintentional sleep. It often happens when someone is supposed to stay awake but dozes off due to tiredness or boredom, like during a meeting, class, or (heaven forbid) while driving.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7718', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%85%E7%9C%A0%E3%82%8A', 29, 1, '2015-05-12T22:29:44.050154Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:07:57.47222Z'),
(7719, 'vocabulary', '快適', '快適', 'Something that is both <kanji>pleasant</kanji> and <kanji>suitable</kanji> is still really <vocabulary>pleasant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7719', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%AB%E9%81%A9', 30, 1, '2015-05-12T22:31:11.176042Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.688237Z'),
(7720, 'vocabulary', '恐れ', '恐れ', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>fear</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>fear</vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7720', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%90%E3%82%8C', 31, 1, '2015-05-12T22:33:01.823358Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.386984Z'),
(7721, 'vocabulary', '飾り気', '飾り気', 'If you <kanji>decorate</kanji> your <kanji>spirit</kanji> with a bunch of pretty things like streamers and balloons, people will think you''re just <vocabulary>showing off</vocabulary>. Yes, you know your spirit is awesome, but everyone else doesn''t want it pushed in their face with your over the top decorations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7721', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%BE%E3%82%8A%E6%B0%97', 32, 1, '2015-05-12T22:34:22.35637Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(7722, 'vocabulary', '迎え', '迎え', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>welcome</kanji>, so it means a <vocabulary>welcome</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>greeting</vocabulary>, but it''s usually used for someone who''s coming and <vocabulary>meeting</vocabulary> you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7722', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%8E%E3%81%88', 34, 1, '2015-05-12T22:36:27.336058Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.412063Z'),
(7723, 'vocabulary', '誤り', '誤り', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>mistake</kanji>, so it means a <vocabulary>mistake</vocabulary> or if it''s on a computer, an <vocabulary>error</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7723', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%A4%E3%82%8A', 36, 1, '2015-05-12T22:38:15.266935Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.424649Z'),
(7724, 'vocabulary', '降ろす', '降ろす', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. You already learned that 降りる means "to get off." This is when you''re helping something else descend down from something, not when you yourself are getting down. So it means <vocabulary>to take down</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to lower</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7724', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%8D%E3%82%8D%E3%81%99', 25, 1, '2015-05-12T22:40:34.537895Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.446386Z'),
(7725, 'vocabulary', '透き通る', '透き通る', 'Something that is <kanji>transparent</kanji> enough for things (like light) to <kanji>pass through</kanji> is going <vocabulary>to be transparent</vocabulary>. In other words, it''s going <vocabulary>to be clear</vocabulary>.

Unlike 透ける, which is more for things that are partially transparent due to being thin or sheer, 透き通る describes things that are totally transparent, clear, or pure — think glass, water, a cloudless sky, or even a clear and pure voice.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7725', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%8F%E3%81%8D%E9%80%9A%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2015-05-12T22:42:40.439718Z', NULL, '2025-09-30T18:46:44.307415Z'),
(7726, 'vocabulary', '手頃', '手頃', 'When you control your <kanji>hand</kanji> an <kanji>approximate</kanji> amount, you are going to be a very <vocabulary>moderate</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>reasonable</vocabulary> person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7726', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E9%A0%83', 45, 1, '2015-05-12T22:43:45.937014Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.479991Z'),
(7727, 'vocabulary', '無邪気', '無邪気', 'If there is <kanji>nothing</kanji> <kanji>wicked</kanji> about your <kanji>spirit</kanji> at all, you are a going to be an <vocabulary>innocent</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>simple minded</vocabulary> person. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7727', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E9%82%AA%E6%B0%97', 50, 1, '2015-05-12T22:45:13.587979Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(7728, 'vocabulary', '水中', '水中', 'If you''re in the <kanji>middle</kanji> of a bunch of <kanji>water</kanji>, then you''re most likely <vocabulary>underwater</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7728', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E4%B8%AD', 2, 2, '2015-06-25T17:06:33.774997Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.517064Z'),
(7729, 'vocabulary', '丸ごと', '丸ごと', 'If you go to (ごと) a <kanji>circle</kanji>, you need to go to it <vocabulary>in its entirety</vocabulary>. That means you have to circle around the <vocabulary>whole</vocabulary> thing. Otherwise, it isn''t even a circle. It''s like a line or a squiggle or something.

You often hear this word when talking about food – like when biting into a whole apple or accidentally swallowing a grape without chewing. But it''s not just for food: you can also use it to talk about memorizing a whole book from beginning to end, for example.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7729', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%B8%E3%81%94%E3%81%A8', 4, 1, '2015-06-25T17:11:14.030946Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T15:28:07.250648Z'),
(7730, 'vocabulary', '外交', '外交', 'You want to <kanji>mix</kanji> opinions from <kanji>outside</kanji> your country into the way you handle things. That''s called <vocabulary>diplomacy</vocabulary>. If you mix your country with the ones outside of your own, you''ll all profit!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7730', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E4%BA%A4', 5, 1, '2015-06-25T17:13:44.597609Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(7731, 'vocabulary', '生まれ', '生まれ', 'Remember how you learned that 生まれる means "to be born?" Well once you''re born that place where it happened becomes your <vocabulary>birthplace</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7731', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8C', 5, 1, '2015-06-25T17:15:46.162264Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.967736Z'),
(7732, 'vocabulary', '地中', '地中', 'If you''re in the <kanji>middle</kanji> of the <kanji>ground</kanji>, then you''re most likely <vocabulary>underground</vocabulary>. Are you a moleperson? That''s pretty cool.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7732', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E4%B8%AD', 6, 1, '2015-06-25T17:21:52.149263Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.455212Z'),
(7733, 'vocabulary', '活用', '活用', 'If you make <kanji>lively</kanji> <kanji>use</kanji> of something, you''re probably <vocabulary>utilizing</vocabulary> it or making <vocabulary>practical use</vocabulary> of it.

活用 means making full use of something. In the context of Japanese grammar, 活用 also refers to the <vocabulary>conjugation</vocabulary> of verbs and adjectives.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7733', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%BB%E7%94%A8', 6, 1, '2015-06-25T17:23:33.319148Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.54517Z'),
(7734, 'vocabulary', '一体', '一体', 'Okay, so this one is really weird, sorry. Each of us only get <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>body</kanji> in our lifetimes, right? Well what <vocabulary>the heck</vocabulary>! What if I want more than one body. Let''s all be mad about this together. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7734', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E4%BD%93', 7, 1, '2015-06-25T17:28:52.72616Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.358645Z'),
(7735, 'vocabulary', '小声', '小声', 'A <kanji>small</kanji> <kanji>voice</kanji> is so small that you can hardly hear it. It''s a <vocabulary>low voice</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>whisper</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7735', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%8F%E5%A3%B0', 7, 1, '2015-06-25T17:34:04.942458Z', NULL, '2025-10-21T16:17:02.597074Z'),
(7736, 'vocabulary', '辺', '辺', 'One of the kanji meanings is <kanji>side</kanji>, and as a standalone word this also means <vocabulary>side</vocabulary>, as in the sides of a shape.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7736', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%BA', 11, 1, '2015-06-25T17:47:02.640329Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.762886Z'),
(7737, 'vocabulary', '役目', '役目', 'If you have an <kanji>eye</kanji> for <kanji>service</kanji>, then you''ll always think helping people is your <vocabulary>duty</vocabulary>! If you see someone in need of service, you’ll always offer your help, because that’s your duty.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7737', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%B9%E7%9B%AE', 8, 1, '2015-06-25T17:49:07.974845Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.595556Z'),
(7738, 'vocabulary', '生地', '生地', '<kanji>Life</kanji> that comes out of the <kanji>earth</kanji> is made up of different kinds of <vocabulary>material</vocabulary>s. Sometimes it''s <vocabulary>cloth</vocabulary>, sometimes it''s <vocabulary>dough</vocabulary>, but everything has to be made out of something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7738', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E5%9C%B0', 8, 1, '2015-06-25T17:50:47.795619Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.609548Z'),
(7739, 'vocabulary', '具合', '具合', 'You have to find the right <kanji>tool</kanji> to <kanji>suit</kanji> your <vocabulary>condition</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>state</vocabulary>. If you have a <vocabulary>health</vocabulary> condition, the tool is a pill, or exercise, or real life friends. But it always depends on the condition you''re in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7739', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B7%E5%90%88', 14, 1, '2015-06-25T17:52:56.446541Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T02:54:10.694388Z'),
(7740, 'vocabulary', '火事', '火事', 'A <kanji>fire</kanji> <kanji>matter</kanji> is a <vocabulary>fire</vocabulary>, as in the destructive burning of something. There''s a fire! Run, run away!

火事 is often used for when houses or other buildings are on fire. If you hear someone shout 火事だ, it''s probably time to get out of wherever you are.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7740', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%AB%E4%BA%8B', 9, 1, '2015-06-25T17:54:09.991573Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.776928Z'),
(7741, 'vocabulary', '通す', '通す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. You already learned that 通る means to pass, but this is a little different. You aren''t the one passing, Sue (す) is. So you have to let Sue pass. In other words, this verb is transitive, meaning it has an object. That''s why it means <vocabulary>to let something pass</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to put something through</vocabulary>.

The way this verb is translated into English changes a lot depending on the noun it''s paired with. For example 火を通す is literally "to let the fire pass through", and means "to heat through". 針に糸を通す is literally "to put thread through a needle", so means "to thread a needle".', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7741', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%9A%E3%81%99', 9, 1, '2015-06-25T17:56:03.85939Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.791824Z'),
(7742, 'vocabulary', '台所', '台所', 'What <kanji>place</kanji> in your house has most of the <kanji>machine</kanji>s in it? Your <vocabulary>kitchen</vocabulary>! Just think about it. Refrigerator, freezer, dishwasher, stove, microwave, WOW! That''s so many. The kitchen definitely has the most machines.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7742', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B0%E6%89%80', 10, 1, '2015-06-25T18:00:15.092048Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.478049Z'),
(7743, 'vocabulary', '農場', '農場', 'The <kanji>place</kanji> you do your <kanji>farming</kanji> is a <vocabulary>farm</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7743', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%B2%E5%A0%B4', 10, 1, '2015-06-25T18:02:03.698702Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.48977Z'),
(7744, 'vocabulary', '別', '別', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7744', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A5', 11, 1, '2015-06-25T18:03:21.247893Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:54.944391Z'),
(7745, 'vocabulary', '心持ち', '心持ち', 'When it comes to your <kanji>heart</kanji>, the way you hold (持つ) it determines your <vocabulary>mindset</vocabulary> and your <vocabulary>feeling</vocabulary>s.

心持ち is a slightly old-fashioned word for someone''s inner state or attitude beneath the surface — kind of like their frame of mind or disposition. In modern usage, it''s more commonly used as an adverb to mean "<vocabulary>somewhat</vocabulary>," like 心持ち速く (somewhat fast, just a bit faster).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7745', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83%E6%8C%81%E3%81%A1', 11, 1, '2015-06-25T18:06:41.780281Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:44:47.801672Z'),
(7746, 'vocabulary', '昔話', '昔話', '<kanji>Long ago</kanji> people used to <kanji>talk</kanji> about all sorts of things. Most of them weren''t true. Long ago people liked to lie a lot. Now we call those things they talked about <vocabulary>folk tale</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>legend</vocabulary>s. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7746', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%94%E8%A9%B1', 11, 1, '2015-06-25T18:08:03.066792Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:38:35.691793Z'),
(7747, 'vocabulary', '見事', '見事', 'Learner, if you <kanji>see</kanji> a <kanji>thing</kanji>, then it must be <vocabulary>splendid</vocabulary>. You saw it, after all, so of course it''s <vocabulary>magnificent</vocabulary>. You only see the <vocabulary>beautiful</vocabulary> things in this world. And that''s an <vocabulary>admirable</vocabulary> quality. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7747', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%8B%E4%BA%8B', 11, 1, '2015-06-25T18:09:42.680067Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:47:34.523008Z'),
(7748, 'vocabulary', '植える', '植える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>plant</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to plant something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to grow something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7748', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A4%8D%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2015-06-25T18:12:15.999099Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:07:35.298442Z'),
(7749, 'vocabulary', '親分', '親分', 'Who plays a <kanji>parent</kanji> <kanji>part</kanji> to you? Your <vocabulary>boss</vocabulary> or your <vocabulary>leader</vocabulary>!

親分 is an informal and sort of old-fashioned word for someone who''s in charge of close-knit groups, like in gangs or traditional trades such as carpentry and fishing. The 親分 is like a parent figure who takes care of the other members. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7749', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA%E5%88%86', 12, 1, '2015-06-25T18:13:53.693268Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.063932Z'),
(7750, 'vocabulary', '顔文字', '顔文字', 'Remember how you learned that 文字 can mean "characters?" Well make a <kanji>face</kanji> out of those characters and you''ve made a <vocabulary>kaomoji</vocabulary>! ʅ(◔౪◔ ) ʃ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7750', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A1%94%E6%96%87%E5%AD%97', 12, 1, '2015-06-25T18:15:42.813252Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T01:46:22.729191Z'),
(7751, 'vocabulary', '王様', '王様', 'The <kanji>king</kanji> (<kanji>formal name ender</kanji>) is the <vocabulary>king</vocabulary>, and you''re talking to him politely. He''s the king after all! If you don''t he might behead you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7751', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8E%8B%E6%A7%98', 13, 1, '2015-06-25T18:18:08.536476Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.117653Z'),
(7752, 'vocabulary', '意図的', '意図的', 'You wouldn''t put your <kanji>idea</kanji> in a <kanji>diagram</kanji> <kanji>~like</kanji> format if it wasn''t <vocabulary>intentional</vocabulary>. Doing that takes a lot of work. So you can only make diagram like things <vocabulary>on purpose</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7752', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%8F%E5%9B%B3%E7%9A%84', 14, 1, '2015-06-25T18:19:25.015398Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.142341Z'),
(7753, 'vocabulary', '近代的', '近代的', 'Something that is <kanji>near</kanji> <kanji>period</kanji> <kanji>like</kanji> is very <vocabulary>contemporary</vocabulary>. But if it looks like it''s from a period not near the one you''re in... throw it away! It isn''t <vocabulary>modern</vocabulary> enough to touch your hands!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7753', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E4%BB%A3%E7%9A%84', 14, 1, '2015-06-25T18:21:41.541066Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.171281Z'),
(7754, 'vocabulary', '整える', '整える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>arrange</kanji>, and the verb version also means <vocabulary>to arrange</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to put in order</vocabulary>. This has to do with putting things in their rightful place, getting affairs in order, or even preparing equipment.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7754', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B4%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 15, 1, '2015-06-25T18:24:07.717959Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.820817Z'),
(7755, 'vocabulary', '折れる', '折れる', 'You already learned how 折る means "to fold something" or "to break something". This is the intransitive version of that, which just means it has no object. You just folded something too far, and now it''s starting <vocabulary>to break</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7755', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%98%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2015-06-25T18:26:03.516859Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.745839Z'),
(7756, 'vocabulary', '門', '門', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7756', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%80', 16, 1, '2015-06-25T18:27:26.741262Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.757771Z'),
(7757, 'vocabulary', '愛人', '愛人', 'The <kanji>person</kanji> you make <kanji>love</kanji> to is your <vocabulary>lover</vocabulary>. Woooh! Don''t worry I won''t tell. Have fun and be safe, kids.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7757', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9B%E4%BA%BA', 17, 1, '2015-06-25T18:31:24.055933Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(7758, 'vocabulary', '虚しい', '虚しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>void</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>empty</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>vacant</vocabulary>. Just think of what has to happen to make a void in the first place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7758', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%99%9A%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 40, 1, '2015-06-25T18:34:05.562811Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.28926Z'),
(7759, 'vocabulary', '果てる', '果てる', 'When your life has borne all its <kanji>fruit</kanji>, it is at last time for it <vocabulary>to end</vocabulary>. Everyone has <vocabulary>to die</vocabulary> someday, but you can rest easy knowing you lived a long and fruitful existence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7759', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%9C%E3%81%A6%E3%82%8B', 17, 1, '2015-06-25T18:37:20.364809Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:01:53.868441Z'),
(7760, 'vocabulary', '対等', '対等', 'It doesn''t matter what you say <kanji>versus</kanji> <kanji>equal</kanji> rights. If you really want things to be <vocabulary>equal</vocabulary>, you have to support <vocabulary>equality</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7760', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BE%E7%AD%89', 18, 1, '2015-06-25T18:51:08.432004Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:12:11.505385Z'),
(7761, 'vocabulary', '宙', '宙', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7761', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%99', 19, 1, '2015-06-25T18:52:13.2128Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.355133Z'),
(7762, 'vocabulary', '天気予報', '天気予報', 'This is a combination of the words 天気, meaning "weather," and 予報 meaning "forecast." Put them together and you get the <vocabulary>weather forecast</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7762', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A9%E6%B0%97%E4%BA%88%E5%A0%B1', 20, 1, '2015-06-25T18:53:13.947694Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(7763, 'vocabulary', '嫌悪', '嫌悪', 'You <kanji>dislike</kanji> someone so <kanji>bad</kanji>ly that it turns to <vocabulary>disgust</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>hate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7763', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AB%8C%E6%82%AA', 20, 1, '2015-06-25T18:55:07.94967Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.404926Z'),
(7764, 'vocabulary', '対応', '対応', 'How you <kanji>respond</kanji> <kanji>versus</kanji> someone else is how you''re <vocabulary>dealing with</vocabulary> them. It''s your <vocabulary>response</vocabulary> to their words and actions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7764', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BE%E5%BF%9C', 22, 1, '2015-06-25T18:58:28.388417Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:12:11.505385Z'),
(7765, 'vocabulary', '心得る', '心得る', 'Remember how you learned that 心得 means "knowledge"? Well the verb version of that is <vocabulary>to know</vocabulary>. (You know it deep in your heart.)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7765', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83%E5%BE%97%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2015-06-25T19:00:30.720222Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.457306Z'),
(7766, 'vocabulary', '本質', '本質', 'The <kanji>real</kanji> <kanji>quality</kanji> of something is its <vocabulary>essence</vocabulary>, or its <vocabulary>true nature</vocabulary>. Whether it''s good or bad is a different story.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7766', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E8%B3%AA', 24, 1, '2015-06-25T19:02:57.596511Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.483473Z'),
(7767, 'vocabulary', '反響', '反響', 'An <kanji>anti</kanji> <kanji>echo</kanji> is still an <vocabulary>echo</vocabulary>! It''s just going back and forth with a normal echo. Together they get a weird <vocabulary>reverberation</vocabulary> going.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7767', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8D%E9%9F%BF', 25, 1, '2015-06-25T19:04:21.604574Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:30:02.229578Z'),
(7768, 'vocabulary', '万年筆', '万年筆', 'The only kind of <kanji>writing brush</kanji> you can use for <kanji>ten thousand</kanji> <kanji>year</kanji>s is a <vocabulary>fountain pen</vocabulary>! All other pens run out of ink and then you''re out of luck. But fountain pens can be refilled, making them work for pretty much exactly ten thousand years.  ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7768', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%87%E5%B9%B4%E7%AD%86', 26, 1, '2015-06-25T19:06:08.236535Z', NULL, '2025-10-31T14:37:16.020874Z'),
(7769, 'vocabulary', '狭める', '狭める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>narrow</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to narrow something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to narrow down something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7769', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%AD%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2015-06-25T19:07:10.331459Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.428585Z'),
(7770, 'vocabulary', '手渡す', '手渡す', 'Remember how you learned that 渡す means "to hand over?" Well you can use your actual <kanji>hand</kanji> <vocabulary>to hand over</vocabulary> something too! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7770', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E6%B8%A1%E3%81%99', 27, 1, '2015-06-25T19:08:21.144262Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.584626Z'),
(7771, 'vocabulary', '成程', '成程', 'If you <kanji>become</kanji> aware of something to an <kanji>extent</kanji> you''ll end up saying <vocabulary>I see</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>that makes sense</vocabulary>! But you won''t see if you didn''t become aware enough. So it''s that extent of understanding you''re trying to reach before you can chime in with an "Ahh, <vocabulary>indeed</vocabulary>."

成程 is a common expression used to acknowledge understanding or express agreement with something that''s just been explained to you. It can be used in both formal and informal conversations. Just keep in mind this word is usually written in kana!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7771', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%90%E7%A8%8B', 30, 1, '2015-06-25T19:09:25.610676Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:28:14.604744Z'),
(7772, 'vocabulary', '怒らせる', '怒らせる', 'Remember how you learned that 怒る means "to get angry?" Well this is when someone else gets angry and it''s because YOU made them angry. It means <vocabulary>to anger someone</vocabulary>.

Verbs ending with an あせる sound (like 怒らせる) often mean that you''re causing someone to feel, do, or experience something. So, 怒らせる means you make someone angry. You can remember this because you always make Russell (らせる) angry. No matter what you say, you seem to get on his nerves… you''re just constantly angering this Russell guy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7772', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%92%E3%82%89%E3%81%9B%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2015-06-25T19:13:02.825326Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:24:14.544936Z'),
(7773, 'vocabulary', '押し', '押し', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7773', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%BC%E3%81%97', 30, 1, '2015-06-25T19:15:05.437106Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.726109Z'),
(7774, 'vocabulary', '用心棒', '用心棒', '"<kanji>Use</kanji> your <kanji>heart</kanji> and this <kanji>pole</kanji> when you guard me," you tell your <vocabulary>bodyguard</vocabulary>. A good bodyguard needs both — the heart of a lion, and a nice sturdy pole to smack fools with.

用心 actually means "caution," so 用心棒 suggests someone who watches out for danger — often with a 棒 (stick) in hand! The word has a bit of an old-school, samurai-era feel, but it still gets used both in writing and conversation from time to time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7774', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%A8%E5%BF%83%E6%A3%92', 31, 1, '2015-06-25T19:17:43.05711Z', NULL, '2025-07-28T21:19:56.013502Z'),
(7775, 'vocabulary', '散らし', '散らし', 'What do people usually <kanji>scatter</kanji> in a rush (らし)? <vocabulary>Leaflet</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>flyer</vocabulary>s. Handing them out takes way too much time, so if you just <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vfaNkGXrEtA" target="_blank">scatter them all over the place</a> your job will be done so much faster!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7775', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%A3%E3%82%89%E3%81%97', 33, 1, '2015-06-25T19:19:30.580193Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.919648Z'),
(7776, 'vocabulary', '暮れる', '暮れる', 'You''ve been so busy with your <kanji>livelihood</kanji>, you didn''t even notice it''s started <vocabulary>to get dark</vocabulary> outside. When you''re doing lots of living, days and seasons just seem <vocabulary>to end</vocabulary> rather quickly like that.

Alternatively, think about how <reading>rare</reading> (れる) it is <vocabulary>to get dark</vocabulary>. It really only happens once every 24 hours. A pretty rare occurence if you ask me.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7776', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%AE%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2015-06-25T19:22:30.16854Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T01:02:36.03338Z'),
(7777, 'vocabulary', '貴重品', '貴重品', 'Remember how you learned that 貴重 means "valuable?" Well valuable <kanji>product</kanji>s are your <vocabulary>valuables</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7777', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%B4%E9%87%8D%E5%93%81', 36, 1, '2015-06-25T19:23:46.553403Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(7778, 'vocabulary', '馬鹿馬鹿しい', '馬鹿馬鹿しい', 'Remember how you learned that 馬鹿 means "idiot?" Well put two idiots together and everything they say and do is absolutely <vocabulary>absurd</vocabulary>. Remember how <vocabulary>ridiculous</vocabulary> that "Dumb and Dumber" movie was? Yeah. Like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7778', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%AC%E9%B9%BF%E9%A6%AC%E9%B9%BF%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 38, 1, '2015-06-25T19:26:14.31758Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.751552Z'),
(7779, 'vocabulary', '埋まる', '埋まる', 'Remember how you learned that 埋める means "to bury?" Well this is something that is being buried, maybe even you! Either way, you''re not the one doing the burying. It means <vocabulary>to be buried</vocabulary>.

埋まる can also mean to be filled, like a pond being completely filled by dirt from a landslide or seats being filled at an event.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7779', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%8B%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2015-06-25T19:29:44.356566Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.777533Z'),
(7780, 'vocabulary', '一翼', '一翼', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>wing</kanji> is just <vocabulary>a part </vocabulary>of the bird or plane it''s attached to. But that one wing plays <vocabulary>a role</vocabulary> that''s very important!

This word usually comes up in the phrase 〜の一翼を担う (to play a part in…). ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7780', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E7%BF%BC', 44, 1, '2015-06-25T19:32:08.370101Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.79183Z'),
(7781, 'vocabulary', '両翼', '両翼', '<kanji>Both</kanji> <kanji>wing</kanji>s are <vocabulary>both wings</vocabulary>! There are two and you have them all!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7781', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%A1%E7%BF%BC', 44, 1, '2015-06-25T19:35:04.779377Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:45:39.570399Z'),
(7782, 'vocabulary', '揺らぐ', '揺らぐ', 'Hey look! If you weren''t sick of them yet, it''s another verb that goes with <kanji>shake</kanji>! This one means <vocabulary>to shake</vocabulary> too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7782', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%BA%E3%82%89%E3%81%90', 46, 1, '2015-06-25T19:37:03.448366Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:52:31.392765Z'),
(7783, 'vocabulary', '偉人', '偉人', 'A <kanji>person</kanji> with a lot of <kanji>greatness</kanji> is a <vocabulary>great person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7783', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%89%E4%BA%BA', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:07:04.024943Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:02:16.590849Z'),
(7784, 'vocabulary', '偉大', '偉大', '<kanji>Big</kanji> <kanji>greatness</kanji> is still <vocabulary>greatness</vocabulary>, just a lot of it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7784', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%89%E5%A4%A7', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:15:24.471222Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(7785, 'vocabulary', '偉い', '偉い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>greatness</kanji>? <vocabulary>Great</vocabulary>! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7785', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%89%E3%81%84', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:16:59.240868Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.855568Z'),
(7786, 'vocabulary', '勘定', '勘定', 'When you use your <kanji>intuition</kanji> to <kanji>determine</kanji> the amount of something, you''ll end up doing the <vocabulary>calculations</vocabulary> in your head. You do the same thing when it''s time to pay your <vocabulary>bill</vocabulary> at dinner too: just determine the amount in your head, go with your intuition, and all should be good.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7786', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%98%E5%AE%9A', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:18:36.070996Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(7787, 'vocabulary', '召す', '召す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>call</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to call</vocabulary>, as in calling someone over.

召す is an honorific verb, so it''s used to talk about the actions of social higher-ups. 召す suggests the image of a superior who summons someone or something to themselves, hence the main meaning to call. But it can also be a polite way to say <vocabulary>to take</vocabulary>, like when they take a meal or a drink, and <vocabulary>to wear</vocabulary> when talking about their clothes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7787', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%AC%E3%81%99', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:20:31.467804Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.884148Z'),
(7788, 'vocabulary', '喚起', '喚起', 'If you <kanji>scream</kanji> <kanji>wake up</kanji>! at someone you can bet they''re going to <vocabulary>awaken</vocabulary>. Screaming anything at a sleeping person would probably <vocabulary>rouse</vocabulary> them. They''ll probably be mad at you though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7788', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%9A%E8%B5%B7', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:22:29.649006Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:32:55.497409Z'),
(7789, 'vocabulary', '喚く', '喚く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>scream</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to scream</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to shout</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7789', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%9A%E3%81%8F', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:23:54.696707Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:32:55.497409Z'),
(7790, 'vocabulary', '召喚', '召喚', 'If you try to <kanji>call</kanji> upon someone by <kanji>scream</kanji>ing for them, you''re probably trying to <vocabulary>summon</vocabulary> them. It''s a little bit more serious than just calling them over casually. Maybe you''re summoning someone to court, or summoning a magical creature in a video game. Pretty serious stuff. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7790', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%AC%E5%96%9A', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:25:20.452599Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:32:55.497409Z'),
(7791, 'vocabulary', '塚', '塚', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Usually these types of <vocabulary>mound</vocabulary>s are <vocabulary>burial mound</vocabulary>s. (There''s bodies under there!)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7791', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A1%9A', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:26:30.148192Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.939038Z'),
(7792, 'vocabulary', '愛媛県', '愛媛県', 'The <kanji>prefecture</kanji> in Japan where they <kanji>love</kanji> <kanji>princess</kanji>es is <vocabulary>Ehime Prefecture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7792', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9B%E5%AA%9B%E7%9C%8C', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:27:45.839061Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:28:57.446613Z'),
(7793, 'vocabulary', '慈悲', '慈悲', 'If you have <kanji>mercy</kanji> for people who are <kanji>sad</kanji> you have a lot of <vocabulary>compassion</vocabulary>. You just can''t help but go easy on sad people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7793', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%88%E6%82%B2', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:29:29.680804Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.963381Z'),
(7794, 'vocabulary', '慈愛', '慈愛', 'When you have <kanji>mercy</kanji> upon those you <kanji>love</kanji> it''s called <vocabulary>affection</vocabulary>. You end up doing things out of the <vocabulary>kindness</vocabulary> of your heart because they mean a lot to you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7794', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%88%E6%84%9B', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:31:41.464017Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:28:57.446613Z'),
(7795, 'vocabulary', '慈善', '慈善', 'A <kanji>morally good</kanji> person who has <kanji>mercy</kanji> upon people gives to <vocabulary>charity</vocabulary>. These people prove they''re good by mercifully giving all their money away to the poor and needy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7795', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%88%E5%96%84', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:33:32.837525Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:55.986821Z'),
(7796, 'vocabulary', '挟む', '挟む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>put between</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to put between</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to sandwich</vocabulary>.

挟む means to put something between two things, like putting ham between bread or catching your finger in a door (ouch). You''ll also see it in phrases like 口を挟む (to butt in with your opinion) or 耳に挟む (to catch a rumor that "slips between" your ears).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7796', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%9F%E3%82%80', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:34:42.018891Z', NULL, '2025-08-27T00:30:33.743751Z'),
(7797, 'vocabulary', '枯渇', '枯渇', 'Something that will <kanji>wither</kanji> and <kanji>dry up</kanji> ends up doing exactly that, <vocabulary>drying up</vocabulary>. And as it dries up, there''s less and less of it until there''s nothing left. This is called <vocabulary>depletion</vocabulary>!

This word is often used to describe situations where natural resources are getting used up and running out.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7797', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%AF%E6%B8%87', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:38:29.050288Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:03:27.911762Z'),
(7798, 'vocabulary', '枯れる', '枯れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>wither</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to wither</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to die</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7798', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%AF%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:40:58.59927Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.022684Z'),
(7799, 'vocabulary', '沸く', '沸く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>boil</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to boil</vocabulary>. It can also be used to mean <vocabulary>to get excited</vocabulary>, because you''re boiling with that excitement.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7799', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%B8%E3%81%8F', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:42:47.998696Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.034657Z'),
(7800, 'vocabulary', '沸点', '沸点', 'The <kanji>point</kanji> at which something starts to <kanji>boil</kanji> is the <vocabulary>boiling point</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7800', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%B8%E7%82%B9', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:43:55.738668Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.046603Z'),
(7801, 'vocabulary', '浦', '浦', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

That said, it''s mostly used as a standalone word meaning "bay" in classical literature. Nowadays, it comes up most in place names, like the port Tagonoura (田子の浦) and the city of Urayasu (浦安市).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7801', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%A6', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:45:21.008081Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.059563Z'),
(7802, 'vocabulary', '浸す', '浸す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>immersed</kanji>, so the verb version is what happens when you immerse something in a liquid. It''s <vocabulary>to soak</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to dip</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7802', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B8%E3%81%99', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:47:04.765025Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.073355Z'),
(7803, 'vocabulary', '渦', '渦', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7803', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%A6', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:48:45.687113Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.088351Z'),
(7804, 'vocabulary', '渦中', '渦中', 'In the <kanji>middle</kanji> of a <kanji>whirlpool</kanji> you''ll find a spinning <vocabulary>vortex</vocabulary> of water. You really don''t want to get sucked in there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7804', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%A6%E4%B8%AD', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:49:45.272862Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.103851Z'),
(7805, 'vocabulary', '潤い', '潤い', 'This may look like an adjective, but it''s actually the noun version of the kanji <kanji>watered</kanji>. Watered things, like plants, hold a lot of <vocabulary>moisture</vocabulary> in them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7805', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%A4%E3%81%84', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:52:55.01747Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.116869Z'),
(7806, 'vocabulary', '洗濯機', '洗濯機', 'A <kanji>wash</kanji> <kanji>wash</kanji> <kanji>machine</kanji> is just another way of saying <vocabulary>washing machine</vocabulary>. It does extra washing that you can''t do by hand so there are two washes here. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7806', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%97%E6%BF%AF%E6%A9%9F', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:54:48.139963Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:04:30.700843Z'),
(7807, 'vocabulary', '洗濯粉', '洗濯粉', '<kanji>Wash</kanji> <kanji>wash</kanji> <kanji>powder</kanji> is the <vocabulary>detergent</vocabulary> you put in the wash you make sure your clothes are washed well. Detergent used to only be available in powder form back in the old days, and it still can be, so this is for that good powdery stuff.

Note that this word sounds a bit old-fashioned. To talk about laundry detergent in general, 洗濯洗剤 is more common nowadays.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7807', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%97%E6%BF%AF%E7%B2%89', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:57:16.522423Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(7808, 'vocabulary', '洗濯屋', '洗濯屋', 'The <kanji>wash</kanji> <kanji>wash</kanji> <kanji>shop</kanji> is where you go to get your clothes washed extra well. It''s the <vocabulary>dry cleaners</vocabulary>! They wash your clothes way better than you can because they wash them twice.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7808', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%97%E6%BF%AF%E5%B1%8B', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:58:24.103556Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(7809, 'vocabulary', '乾燥', '乾燥', 'If you take something <kanji>dry</kanji> and push it to <kanji>dry up</kanji> further, you get 乾燥. It''s the word for <vocabulary>dryness</vocabulary>, whether it''s chapped lips, dry air, or parched objects. It also describes the act of <vocabulary>drying</vocabulary>, like when you dehydrate food or hang your clothes to dry.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7809', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%BE%E7%87%A5', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T16:59:35.859587Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:08:49.298692Z'),
(7810, 'vocabulary', '焦燥', '焦燥', 'If you want to <kanji>char</kanji> fish for dinner it will <kanji>dry up</kanji> because of your own <vocabulary>impatience</vocabulary>. Charring fish takes time and if you''re too <vocabulary>uneasy</vocabulary> and shove it too far in the fire it will dry out, burn, and get gross.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7810', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A6%E7%87%A5', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:00:38.798625Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T01:08:49.298692Z'),
(7811, 'vocabulary', '玄関', '玄関', 'The most <kanji>mysterious</kanji> thing that''s <kanji>connected</kanji> to a house is the <vocabulary>entranceway</vocabulary>. When you''re walking into one you never know what''s coming next. You just take off your shoes and hope the rest of the house isn''t really weird or something. If it is, the <vocabulary>entryway</vocabulary> is then your only way out.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7811', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8E%84%E9%96%A2', 29, 1, '2015-06-30T17:02:24.994574Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T18:07:32.224525Z'),
(7812, 'vocabulary', '玄米', '玄米', 'You learned 玄 means "mysterious," but remember how it can also mean "dark"? Well, <kanji>dark</kanji> <kanji>rice</kanji> is <vocabulary>unpolished rice</vocabulary> — aka <vocabulary>brown rice</vocabulary>. At a glance, you might think it''s some cool, edgy food that antiheroes eat, but the kanji actually just comes from brown rice being darker in color than white rice.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7812', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8E%84%E7%B1%B3', 30, 1, '2015-06-30T17:03:45.144Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T06:49:57.557884Z'),
(7813, 'vocabulary', '瓶', '瓶', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7813', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%93%B6', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:05:21.99477Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.242026Z'),
(7814, 'vocabulary', '花瓶', '花瓶', 'A <kanji>bottle</kanji> for <kanji>flowers</kanji> is a <vocabulary>vase</vocabulary>. You should get some flowers and put them in a vase for someone special right now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7814', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B1%E7%93%B6', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:06:22.142801Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:20:54.638732Z'),
(7815, 'vocabulary', '空き瓶', '空き瓶', 'A <kanji>sky</kanji> <kanji>bottle</kanji> is a bottle that has nothing but sky in it. It''s an <vocabulary>empty bottle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7815', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA%E3%81%8D%E7%93%B6', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:10:06.872293Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.265684Z'),
(7816, 'vocabulary', '火炎瓶', '火炎瓶', 'A <kanji>fire</kanji> in a <kanji>bottle</kanji> that makes a <kanji>big fire</kanji> when thrown is called a <vocabulary>Molotov Cocktail</vocabulary>. Don''t throw them in the house please.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7816', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%AB%E7%82%8E%E7%93%B6', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:12:12.872281Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.27726Z'),
(7817, 'vocabulary', '瓶詰', '瓶詰', 'What''s <kanji>stuffed</kanji> in the <kanji>bottle</kanji>? Jam you <vocabulary>bottled</vocabulary> for later! How could you forget?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7817', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%93%B6%E8%A9%B0', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:13:24.612316Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.289834Z'),
(7818, 'vocabulary', '耕す', '耕す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>plow</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to plow</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to till</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7818', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%95%E3%81%99', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:14:54.616252Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.301098Z'),
(7819, 'vocabulary', '耕地', '耕地', '<kanji>Plow</kanji>ed <kanji>land</kanji> is called <vocabulary>arable land</vocabulary>. This is the kind of land you use for growing crops or for pastures.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7819', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%95%E5%9C%B0', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:17:44.394382Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.312418Z'),
(7820, 'vocabulary', '耕作', '耕作', '<kanji>Plow</kanji> the earth to <kanji>make</kanji> it ready for <vocabulary>cultivation</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>farming</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7820', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%95%E4%BD%9C', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:18:43.710862Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:12:39.703958Z'),
(7821, 'vocabulary', '農耕', '農耕', '<kanji>Farming</kanji> with a <kanji>plow</kanji> is still <vocabulary>farming</vocabulary>.

農耕 is a relatively formal word for agriculture or agricultural cultivation, focusing on activities like plowing, planting, and growing crops. It''s often used to describe farming practices and settled agricultural lifestyles, as opposed to hunting and gathering.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7821', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%B2%E8%80%95', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:21:45.215572Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T02:02:32.030901Z'),
(7822, 'vocabulary', '聡い', '聡い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s another way of saying <kanji>wise</kanji>? <vocabulary>Smart</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>discerning</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7822', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%81%A1%E3%81%84', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:22:52.753565Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.350004Z'),
(7823, 'vocabulary', '聡明', '聡明', 'A <kanji>wise</kanji> and <kanji>bright</kanji> person is <vocabulary>wise</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>intelligent</vocabulary>. In fact, they have so much <vocabulary>wisdom</vocabulary> that it''s showing up as brightness on their skin. Sparkle sparkle goes the wise old woman.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7823', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%81%A1%E6%98%8E', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:24:02.140412Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(7824, 'vocabulary', '肯定', '肯定', 'An <kanji>agreement</kanji> is <kanji>determine</kanji>d by means of <vocabulary>affirmation</vocabulary>. If both parties are <vocabulary>affirming</vocabulary> each other''s positions, then it''s safe to say they probably agree on things.

肯定 is a relatively formal word used for affirming something as correct or valid. It can describe actively agreeing with an opinion or stance, but it''s more often used to describe a clear, <vocabulary>positive</vocabulary> response to a question or claim. It also commonly appears in introspective contexts, like 自己肯定感 (a feeling of self-acceptance), when talking about recognizing yourself or your own life in a positive way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7824', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%AF%E5%AE%9A', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:26:13.116719Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(7825, 'vocabulary', '脂肪', '脂肪', 'If you add <kanji>fat</kanji> to <kanji>fat</kanji>, what do you get? You get <vocabulary>fat</vocabulary>, obviously. Come on now, this isn''t rocket science.

脂肪 specifically refers to the solid fat found inside plants and animals. In casual conversation, it''s often used when talking about someone''s excess body fat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7825', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%82%E8%82%AA', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:27:57.092118Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:13:41.173292Z'),
(7826, 'vocabulary', '樹脂', '樹脂', 'The <kanji>fat</kanji> that comes out of the <kanji>wood</kanji> from trees is used to make <vocabulary>resin</vocabulary>. Trees don''t have fat, you say? Well you''re wrong, look it up. Trees are super fat and we use the resin from them to do all sorts of stuff.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7826', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A8%B9%E8%84%82', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:29:50.31046Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.421039Z'),
(7827, 'vocabulary', '皮膚', '皮膚', 'The <kanji>skin</kanji> under your <kanji>skin</kanji> is still <vocabulary>skin</vocabulary>. The extra layer is there to make sure when you get little cuts they don''t bleed all over the place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7827', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%AE%E8%86%9A', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:30:54.293002Z', NULL, '2026-03-26T08:11:42.807516Z'),
(7828, 'vocabulary', '苗', '苗', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7828', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%97', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:40:08.075977Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.4493Z'),
(7829, 'vocabulary', '苗字', '苗字', 'A <kanji>seedling</kanji> <kanji>letter</kanji> is the very first letter you learn when you''re little. What''s the first thing you learn to spell when you''re little? Your <vocabulary>surname</vocabulary>! You need to know who your family is, otherwise if you get lost or something happens, you won''t know how to get home! Or even who you are!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7829', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%97%E5%AD%97', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:41:29.922782Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.461944Z'),
(7830, 'vocabulary', '蓮', '蓮', 'A <kanji>lotus</kanji> is, well, a <vocabulary>lotus</vocabulary>. In Buddhist scripture, though, it''s often a <vocabulary>sacred lotus</vocabulary>. If you spot one growing in a random pond somewhere, it''s probably the former, but if it looks all ethereal and there''s a Buddha sitting on it, it''s probably the latter, so use your judgment! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7830', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%93%AE', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:42:43.242786Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:44:40.098639Z'),
(7831, 'vocabulary', '蓮花', '蓮花', 'A <kanji>lotus</kanji> <kanji>flower</kanji> originally means <vocabulary>lotus flower</vocabulary>, but these days 蓮花 often refers to a small flower that looks like a lotus, called a <vocabulary>Chinese milk vetch</vocabulary>. It can also mean a <vocabulary>ramen spoon</vocabulary>, because those spoons you get with ramen have a unique shape resembling a lotus petal.

Keep in mind that you might also see it written as 蓮華, or just in kana as れんげ or レンゲ.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7831', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%93%AE%E8%8A%B1', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:44:28.285589Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:20:54.638732Z'),
(7832, 'vocabulary', '覆う', '覆う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>cover</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to cover</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to hide</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7832', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%86%E3%81%86', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:47:23.003786Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.498031Z'),
(7833, 'vocabulary', '貞節', '貞節', '<kanji>Chastity</kanji> is always in <kanji>season</kanji> so it''s always cool to try to keep your <vocabulary>chastity</vocabulary>. Spring, summer? Doesn''t matter. Your decision to stay that way is fine no matter what time of year it is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7833', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%9E%E7%AF%80', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:49:11.951428Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.510898Z'),
(7834, 'vocabulary', '貞操', '貞操', 'You are a strong, independent person and you are the only one who can <kanji>manipulate</kanji> your <kanji>chastity</kanji>. Nobody else can. So if you want to keep your <vocabulary>chastity</vocabulary> that''s completely up to you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7834', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%9E%E6%93%8D', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:50:33.895566Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.522929Z'),
(7835, 'vocabulary', '不貞', '不貞', 'When you do <kanji>not</kanji> have <kanji>chastity</kanji>, it''s probably because you were <vocabulary>unfaithful</vocabulary> to your promise to whoever you told you''d stay chaste to.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7835', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E8%B2%9E', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:51:48.32955Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.534764Z'),
(7836, 'vocabulary', '軒', '軒', 'In order to use a <kanji>house counter</kanji> in the first place, you need to have something to count. That''s why you have to find <vocabulary>eaves</vocabulary>. And all those eaves together <vocabulary>stand in a row</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7836', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%92', 43, 1, '2015-06-30T17:53:03.457714Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T06:26:42.826351Z'),
(7837, 'vocabulary', '〜軒', '〜軒', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. 

This is generally used to count single-family detached houses, but it can also be used to count individual units within a residential complex, as well as shops or restaurants.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7837', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E8%BB%92', 35, 1, '2015-06-30T17:54:45.417998Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.558851Z'),
(7838, 'vocabulary', '柔軟', '柔軟', 'A <kanji>gentle</kanji> and <kanji>soft</kanji> thing will be really <vocabulary>flexible</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>pliable</vocabulary>. Soft things are really easy to bend, like towels and flower petals.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7838', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%94%E8%BB%9F', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:55:49.663491Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T02:00:53.286194Z'),
(7839, 'vocabulary', '軟禁', '軟禁', '<kanji>Soft</kanji> <kanji>prohibition</kanji> isn''t going to be as bad as jail or prison. They went soft on you, so you''re only under <vocabulary>house arrest</vocabulary>. That''s not so bad, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7839', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%9F%E7%A6%81', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:56:52.504077Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.581112Z'),
(7840, 'vocabulary', '私邸', '私邸', 'A <kanji>private</kanji> <kanji>residence</kanji> is a <vocabulary>private residence</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7840', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%81%E9%82%B8', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:57:47.73463Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.596092Z'),
(7841, 'vocabulary', '官邸', '官邸', 'A <kanji>government</kanji> <kanji>residence</kanji> is one that is assigned to government officials. That means it''s their <vocabulary>official residence</vocabulary>, and it goes on all their official paperwork.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7841', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%98%E9%82%B8', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:58:33.453598Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.607284Z'),
(7842, 'vocabulary', '公邸', '公邸', 'A <kanji>public</kanji> <kanji>residence</kanji> is the one you tell everyone for things like documents and loans and stuff. That means it''s your <vocabulary>official residence</vocabulary> and it''s in everyone''s books.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7842', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%AC%E9%82%B8', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T17:59:46.648582Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.618378Z'),
(7843, 'vocabulary', '邸宅', '邸宅', 'If you have a <kanji>residence</kanji> that''s also your <kanji>house</kanji>, it''s still your <vocabulary>residence</vocabulary>. But you probably have a lot of money if you can own your own house, so it''s probably a nice, big <vocabulary>mansion</vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7843', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%82%B8%E5%AE%85', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:01:27.782998Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:35:23.672471Z'),
(7844, 'vocabulary', '邸内', '邸内', 'The land <kanji>inside</kanji> of a <kanji>residence</kanji> is called the <vocabulary>grounds</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>premises</vocabulary>. Only really big residences have land inside them big enough to be called the "grounds" though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7844', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%82%B8%E5%86%85', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:02:42.845063Z', NULL, '2025-09-05T05:40:05.235761Z'),
(7845, 'vocabulary', '郊外', '郊外', '<kanji>Suburbs</kanji> on the <kanji>outside</kanji> of town are still <vocabulary>suburbs</vocabulary>. They''re sometimes called the <vocabulary>outskirts</vocabulary> of town too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7845', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%8A%E5%A4%96', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:03:54.785098Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(7846, 'vocabulary', '近郊', '近郊', '<kanji>Suburbs</kanji> <kanji>near</kanji> you are still <vocabulary>suburbs</vocabulary>. They''re all pretty much the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7846', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E9%83%8A', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:05:51.027205Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.664624Z'),
(7847, 'vocabulary', '郡', '郡', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7847', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%A1', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:06:45.580585Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.675861Z'),
(7848, 'vocabulary', '釈明', '釈明', 'An <kanji>explanation</kanji> that''s <kanji>bright</kanji> is just a really clear and well developed <vocabulary>explanation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7848', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%88%E6%98%8E', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:07:47.638575Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(7849, 'vocabulary', '注釈', '注釈', 'If your brain can''t stop <kanji>pour</kanji>ing out all the <kanji>explanations</kanji> your teacher gives you, you''ll need to keep <vocabulary>notes</vocabulary>. That way when the information pours out of your head, you''ll still have your <vocabulary>comments</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>annotations</vocabulary> to help you remember the teacher''s explanations later.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7849', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A8%E9%87%88', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:09:27.845501Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.698381Z'),
(7850, 'vocabulary', '会釈', '会釈', 'Before you can give an <kanji>explanation</kanji> of who you are to someone you''re about to <kanji>meet</kanji>, you have to <vocabulary>nod</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>bow</vocabulary> as a <vocabulary>greeting</vocabulary> first. It''s rude to start explaining who you are before you greet them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7850', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9A%E9%87%88', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:10:59.375134Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.711354Z'),
(7851, 'vocabulary', '解釈', '解釈', 'Even if you <kanji>untie</kanji> an <kanji>explanation</kanji> it''s still a pretty good <vocabulary>explanation</vocabulary> anyway. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7851', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A3%E9%87%88', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:12:02.403071Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T20:23:40.067262Z'),
(7852, 'vocabulary', '隅', '隅', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7852', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%85', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:13:11.48057Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.736878Z'),
(7853, 'vocabulary', '片隅', '片隅', 'A <kanji>one sided</kanji> <kanji>corner</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>corner</vocabulary>, just a really small one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7853', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%87%E9%9A%85', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:14:10.207042Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.748255Z'),
(7854, 'vocabulary', '四隅', '四隅', '<kanji>Four</kanji> <kanji>corner</kanji>s are <vocabulary>four corners</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7854', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9B%E9%9A%85', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:15:54.069472Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:13:05.258541Z'),
(7855, 'vocabulary', '隅々', '隅々', '<kanji>Corner</kanji> <kanji>corner</kanji>s are still <vocabulary>corners</vocabulary>. Sometimes they''re the corners TO corners, so they''re the little <vocabulary>nooks</vocabulary> and crannies that are hard to get at.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7855', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%85%E3%80%85', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:23:49.254601Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(7856, 'vocabulary', '〜隻', '〜隻', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Note that 隻 is generally used to count relatively large vessels, like ships or even whole fleets.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7856', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E9%9A%BB', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:26:08.108778Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:56.784111Z'),
(7857, 'vocabulary', '頂く', '頂く', 'If you <kanji>humbly</kanji> do something you are going <vocabulary>to humbly receive</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to humbly accept</vocabulary>, or eat or drink it. Basically you''re humbly doing a lot of things. And you can use this word for all of them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7857', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%82%E3%81%8F', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:27:31.818963Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T21:23:50.258729Z'),
(7858, 'vocabulary', '山頂', '山頂', 'The <kanji>mountain</kanji> <kanji>summit</kanji> is still called the <vocabulary>summit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7858', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%B1%E9%A0%82', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:34:06.813996Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T19:41:32.310705Z'),
(7859, 'vocabulary', '襟', '襟', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7859', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%9F', 51, 1, '2015-06-30T18:34:59.727635Z', NULL, '2026-02-20T01:45:31.12494Z'),
(7860, 'vocabulary', '鰐蟹', '鰐蟹', 'This is it. You''re finally here. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q5th225wgsk" target="_blank">This is the moment where you learn the true meaning behind WaniKani!</a>', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7860', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%B0%90%E8%9F%B9', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:31:23.815899Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.583203Z'),
(7861, 'vocabulary', '不倫', '不倫', 'If you do <kanji>not</kanji> have any <kanji>ethics</kanji> you''ll probably do something crappy, like commit <vocabulary>adultery</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>infidelity</vocabulary>. Usually only unethical people would cheat on their spouse, right?

Unlike 浮気, which can mean cheating in either dating or marriage, 不倫 is strictly adultery in marriage. It sounds way more serious (and has way bigger consequences). Basically, all 不倫 is 浮気, but not all 浮気 is 不倫… Both are bad, but one may come with legal fees.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7861', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%80%AB', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:38:43.434969Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.602596Z'),
(7862, 'vocabulary', '倫理', '倫理', '<kanji>Ethics</kanji> and <kanji>reason</kanji> together in a person make them have very strong <vocabulary>ethics</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>morals</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7862', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%AB%E7%90%86', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:41:55.506956Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.619274Z'),
(7863, 'vocabulary', '偏る', '偏る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>biased</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to be biased</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be prejudiced</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7863', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%8F%E3%82%8B', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:43:10.834364Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.63107Z'),
(7864, 'vocabulary', '偏見', '偏見', 'A <kanji>biased</kanji> way of <kanji>see</kanji>ing is also known as <vocabulary>prejudice</vocabulary>, or just <vocabulary>bias</vocabulary>.

偏見 is often used to describe unfair judgments or opinions based on stereotypes rather than facts. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7864', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%8F%E8%A6%8B', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:44:40.121104Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.642495Z'),
(7865, 'vocabulary', '偏狭', '偏狭', '<kanji>Narrow</kanji> <kanji>biased</kanji> people are considered to be <vocabulary>narrow minded</vocabulary> and they''re usually disliked for their <vocabulary>intolerance</vocabulary> of other cultures and anything unfamiliar to them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7865', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%8F%E7%8B%AD', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:45:59.534728Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.654672Z'),
(7866, 'vocabulary', '偏食', '偏食', 'People who are <kanji>biased</kanji> about what they <kanji>eat</kanji> usually only eat sweet stuff like candy and ice cream. This <vocabulary>picky eating</vocabulary> gives them a really <vocabulary>unbalanced diet</vocabulary>. Candy and ice cream are okay, but you have to be open to eating other things too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7866', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%8F%E9%A3%9F', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:48:03.986966Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.665901Z'),
(7867, 'vocabulary', '示唆', '示唆', 'Someone prone to <kanji>indicate</kanji> and <kanji>instigate</kanji> will have lots of <vocabulary>suggestions</vocabulary> for other people. By dropping <vocabulary>hint</vocabulary>s, they can indicate problems and instigate fights among people. Their <vocabulary>implication</vocabulary>s do a lot of harm.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7867', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A4%BA%E5%94%86', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:49:42.092726Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.677168Z'),
(7868, 'vocabulary', '噴火', '噴火', 'When a volcano <kanji>erupt</kanji>s with <kanji>fire</kanji> it makes a really big <vocabulary>eruption</vocabulary>. You should probably start running.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7868', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%99%B4%E7%81%AB', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:51:33.307626Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.690384Z'),
(7869, 'vocabulary', '噴煙', '噴煙', 'Sometimes volcanoes <kanji>erupt</kanji> with just <kanji>smoke</kanji> instead of lava or fire. That''s called <vocabulary>volcanic smoke</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7869', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%99%B4%E7%85%99', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:52:43.900342Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.703062Z'),
(7870, 'vocabulary', '噴射', '噴射', 'When something <kanji>erupt</kanji>s and <kanji>shoots</kanji> a bunch of water or lava out it comes out in a huge <vocabulary>jet</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>spray</vocabulary>s everything.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7870', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%99%B4%E5%B0%84', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:55:08.572367Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.715104Z'),
(7871, 'vocabulary', '噴水', '噴水', '<kanji>Water</kanji> <kanji>erupts</kanji> from <vocabulary>water fountains</vocabulary>. Sometimes they''re big eruptions and sometimes they''re small, but they''re always fun to watch and enjoy in the hot summer.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7871', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%99%B4%E6%B0%B4', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:56:52.043257Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.729177Z'),
(7872, 'vocabulary', '噴出', '噴出', 'When something <kanji>erupt</kanji>s out an <kanji>exit</kanji> it usually had to fight pretty hard to get to that exit. That''s why it ends up <vocabulary>spewing</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>gushing</vocabulary> all over the place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7872', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%99%B4%E5%87%BA', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:58:19.281435Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.741853Z'),
(7873, 'vocabulary', '孤児', '孤児', 'An <kanji>orphan</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> is sadly still an <vocabulary>orphan</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7873', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A4%E5%85%90', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T22:59:26.819942Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.769622Z'),
(7874, 'vocabulary', '孤島', '孤島', 'An <kanji>orphan</kanji> <kanji>island</kanji> is one that''s all alone, without any other island friends or family. That''s what makes it a <vocabulary>solitary island</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7874', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A4%E5%B3%B6', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:00:27.048088Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.784172Z'),
(7875, 'vocabulary', '孤独', '孤独', 'An <kanji>orphan</kanji> who''s <kanji>alone</kanji> is trapped in a world of <vocabulary>isolation</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>loneliness</vocabulary>. Having no parents is hard enough, but not having anyone else is even lonelier.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7875', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A4%E7%8B%AC', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:01:57.876219Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.812454Z'),
(7876, 'vocabulary', '孤立', '孤立', 'When <kanji>orphan</kanji>s <kanji>stand</kanji>, they stand in <vocabulary>isolation</vocabulary> — <vocabulary>alone</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>friendless</vocabulary>. Orphans don''t have any parents, so this world can be a pretty cruel place for them. It''s really sad.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7876', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A4%E7%AB%8B', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:03:13.149536Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.825023Z'),
(7877, 'vocabulary', '怠惰', '怠惰', 'A <kanji>lazy</kanji>, <kanji>lazy</kanji> person is plagued with never ending <vocabulary>laziness</vocabulary>. They spend their days in <vocabulary>idleness</vocabulary>, totally afflicted with <vocabulary>sloth</vocabulary>.

怠惰 is a formal word you''ll most likely see in writing. It describes someone who habitually avoids effort or responsibility, so it speaks to a person''s character or general attitude rather than a single instance of laziness.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7877', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A0%E6%83%B0', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:04:23.044905Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:03:45.494916Z'),
(7878, 'vocabulary', '怠る', '怠る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. You learned the kanji means "lazy," but it can also mean <kanji>neglect</kanji>, so the verb means <vocabulary>to be negligent</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to neglect</vocabulary> something.

怠る is a relatively formal word for failing to carry out a responsibility, either on purpose or due to forgetfulness or negligence. You''ll see it in contexts like 義務を怠る (to neglect one''s duty) or 注意を怠る (to fail to exercise caution).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7878', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A0%E3%82%8B', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:05:40.949425Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:01:55.772552Z'),
(7879, 'vocabulary', '恒星', '恒星', 'A <kanji>constant</kanji> <kanji>star</kanji> is the kind that stays in one place. It''s just a regular <vocabulary>star</vocabulary>, like the north star or our sun, not a comet or an asteroid.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7879', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%92%E6%98%9F', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:07:24.585481Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T02:27:52.417356Z'),
(7880, 'vocabulary', '恒例', '恒例', '<kanji>Constant</kanji> <kanji>example</kanji>s that you use all the time end up becoming part of an <vocabulary>established practice</vocabulary>. You constantly use them so it just stays that way after a while.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7880', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%92%E4%BE%8B', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:08:31.600028Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.070746Z'),
(7881, 'vocabulary', '恒久', '恒久', 'A thing that''s <kanji>constant</kanji> for a <kanji>long time</kanji> becomes <vocabulary>permanent</vocabulary>. Leave a shoe on the sidewalk for a long time? Permanently stuck there. Constantly making dumb faces? Permanently stuck like that. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7881', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%92%E4%B9%85', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:10:05.297079Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.081526Z'),
(7882, 'vocabulary', '恒常', '恒常', 'Keeping things <kanji>constant</kanji> and <kanji>normal</kanji> ensures <vocabulary>constancy</vocabulary>.

恒常 is a formal word used to describe something that remains <vocabulary>constant</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>unchanging</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7882', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%92%E5%B8%B8', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:11:05.187173Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.093382Z'),
(7883, 'vocabulary', '惰性', '惰性', 'If you have the <kanji>nature</kanji> of being <kanji>lazy</kanji> it will become a <vocabulary>habit</vocabulary>. The <vocabulary>momentum</vocabulary> created by your natural laziness will make it so you can''t break out of your lazy cycle.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7883', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%B0%E6%80%A7', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:12:18.366746Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.104719Z'),
(7884, 'vocabulary', '我慢', '我慢', 'When <kanji>I</kanji> am faced with <kanji>overconfidence</kanji>, it seriously tests my <vocabulary>endurance</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>patience</vocabulary>. I really hate overconfident people, so it takes a lot of <vocabulary>self-control</vocabulary> for me to smile at them and pretend to be civil.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7884', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%91%E6%85%A2', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:14:23.410886Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:45:50.932066Z'),
(7885, 'vocabulary', '自慢', '自慢', 'When your <kanji>self</kanji> has <kanji>overconfidence</kanji> in something, that''s called <vocabulary>pride</vocabulary>. You can''t shut up about that thing, and you <vocabulary>boast</vocabulary> about it to whoever will listen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7885', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E6%85%A2', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:15:41.198213Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:46:51.636369Z'),
(7886, 'vocabulary', '緩慢', '緩慢', 'You learned 慢 as "overconfidence," but it can also mean "laziness." And when you''re feeling all <kanji>loose</kanji> due to your <kanji>laziness</kanji>, your movements are probably going to be pretty <vocabulary>slow</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>sluggish</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7886', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%A9%E6%85%A2', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:22:40.017036Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:47:59.981843Z'),
(7887, 'vocabulary', '怠慢', '怠慢', 'If you get <kanji>lazy</kanji> because of your <kanji>overconfidence</kanji>, you''ll probably be prone to <vocabulary>negligence</vocabulary>. You start cutting corners everywhere because you think you''re infallible, and before you know it you''re neglecting all the little things you were supposed to take care of.

怠慢 refers to the kind of negligence where someone fails to fulfill a duty. It suggests they could have done better but didn''t because of <vocabulary>laziness</vocabulary>, carelessness, lack of effort, or not taking their responsibilities seriously.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7887', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A0%E6%85%A2', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:28:14.736069Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:59:45.447543Z'),
(7888, 'vocabulary', '慢性', '慢性', '<kanji>Overconfidence</kanji> is in the <kanji>nature</kanji> of any problem that''s <vocabulary>chronic</vocabulary>. No matter what you throw at it, it just confidently sticks around. Whether it''s an illness or some other nagging issue, one thing''s for sure: it''s going to chronically and confidently bug you, forever.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7888', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%A2%E6%80%A7', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:30:03.770868Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:52:31.973714Z'),
(7889, 'vocabulary', '擁護', '擁護', '<kanji>Embrace</kanji> your duty to <kanji>defend</kanji> your country and prepare for its <vocabulary>protection</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>defense</vocabulary>. You love your country, so you''ll do what you have to in order to <vocabulary>support</vocabulary> its defense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7889', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%93%81%E8%AD%B7', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:32:51.823858Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.928168Z'),
(7890, 'vocabulary', '抱擁', '抱擁', 'An <kanji>embrace</kanji> and a <kanji>hug</kanji> are pretty much the same thing. So you''re still going to <vocabulary>embrace</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>hug</vocabulary> people!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7890', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%B1%E6%93%81', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:34:48.504132Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.94374Z'),
(7891, 'vocabulary', '擁立', '擁立', 'You <kanji>embrace</kanji> someone to keep them <kanji>stand</kanji>ing. You''re <vocabulary>backing</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>supporting</vocabulary> this person, so no matter what happens you''ve gotta keep them on their feet.

擁立 is a formal word for backing someone up for a position, usually in politics or organizations. It''s used when people unite to promote or nominate someone as a leader or as a political candidate.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7891', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%93%81%E7%AB%8B', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:36:01.721719Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.960956Z'),
(7892, 'vocabulary', '擁する', '擁する', 'To do an <kanji>embrace</kanji> is <vocabulary>to have</vocabulary> that person or animal in your arms. You hug it and then run away with it. It''s yours now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7892', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%93%81%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:37:30.565168Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.97412Z'),
(7893, 'vocabulary', '特殊', '特殊', 'An <kanji>especially</kanji> <kanji>special</kanji> thing is even more <vocabulary>special</vocabulary> and probably a lot more <vocabulary>unique</vocabulary> than ordinary special things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7893', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%B9%E6%AE%8A', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:38:46.16205Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.991303Z'),
(7894, 'vocabulary', '殊勝', '殊勝', 'You''re <kanji>especially</kanji> <kanji>win</kanji>ning when you selflessly devote yourself or overcome personal weaknesses. That''s what makes it <vocabulary>admirable</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>praiseworthy</vocabulary>!

殊勝 is a formal term often used to describe someone''s commendable endurance, sincere attitude, good intentions, or virtuous actions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7894', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%8A%E5%8B%9D', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:40:39.490023Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:17.010386Z'),
(7895, 'vocabulary', '没', '没', 'When a plant or an animal <kanji>die</kanji>s you have to <vocabulary>discard</vocabulary> it. You can''t just keep it sitting around the house after <vocabulary>death</vocabulary>, that''s weird. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7895', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%A1', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:42:35.569898Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:17.02521Z'),
(7896, 'vocabulary', '没後', '没後', 'All the art, music, or books that come out <kanji>after</kanji> a person <kanji>die</kanji>s are considered to be released <vocabulary>after death</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>posthumously</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7896', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%A1%E5%BE%8C', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:43:59.218134Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:17.041522Z'),
(7897, 'vocabulary', '戦没', '戦没', 'When a person <kanji>die</kanji>s in a <kanji>war</kanji> they''re considered to have been <vocabulary>killed in action</vocabulary>. There are lots of memorials around the world for people who died this way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7897', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%A6%E6%B2%A1', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:45:06.530718Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:17.079644Z'),
(7898, 'vocabulary', '沈没', '沈没', 'If you <kanji>sink</kanji> and <kanji>die</kanji> it''s because you were <vocabulary>sinking</vocabulary> in the ocean or a pool or something. It''s important to notice you''re <vocabulary>going down</vocabulary> so you don''t die like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7898', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%88%E6%B2%A1', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:49:00.419588Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:17.092Z'),
(7899, 'vocabulary', '日没', '日没', 'At night the <kanji>sun</kanji> <kanji>die</kanji>s at <vocabulary>sunset</vocabulary>. Don''t worry, it will be reborn tomorrow morning at sunrise and everything will be okay.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7899', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E6%B2%A1', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:50:22.929362Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:17.105011Z'),
(7900, 'vocabulary', '没頭', '没頭', 'All the extra things <kanji>die</kanji> in the <kanji>head</kanji> when one is <vocabulary>immersing oneself</vocabulary> in one thing. That way, the thing they''re focusing on is the only thing in their brain.

没頭 is a relatively formal word that means being deeply absorbed or engrossed in something. It''s often positive and describes a state where someone is so focused on an activity they lose track of time or forget about everything else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7900', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%A1%E9%A0%AD', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:51:55.645507Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:38:54.907026Z'),
(7901, 'vocabulary', '没落', '没落', 'A building or civilization that <kanji>die</kanji>s and then <kanji>falls</kanji> is in a state of <vocabulary>ruin</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>collapse</vocabulary>. Like the Mayans or the Western Roman Empire. They fell pretty hard.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7901', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%A1%E8%90%BD', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:53:16.591958Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:17.129485Z'),
(7902, 'vocabulary', '埋没', '埋没', 'You <kanji>bury</kanji> things into people who look like they''ve <kanji>die</kanji>d. It''s called <vocabulary>implantation</vocabulary>. They''re not really dead though. This is surgery and they''ll wake up and go about their lives with whatever that thing was that you put in them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7902', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%8B%E6%B2%A1', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:54:34.083418Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:17.141142Z'),
(7903, 'vocabulary', '出没', '出没', 'Animals <kanji>exit</kanji> the mountains into your land and never seem to <kanji>die</kanji>! They end up <vocabulary>appearing frequently</vocabulary> in your garden, eating all your yummy carrots and watermelons, and then make their exit back into the hills. They''re <vocabulary>infesting</vocabulary> your home now and you don''t know what to do!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7903', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E6%B2%A1', 52, 1, '2015-06-30T23:56:00.850072Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:17.156541Z'),
(7904, 'vocabulary', '没収', '没収', 'When you <kanji>obtain</kanji> something acting like that person had <kanji>die</kanji>d, it''s called <vocabulary>confiscation</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>forfeiture</vocabulary>. Maybe if they were alive, you think, they wouldn''t have let me take it. Well they are still alive so it''s theirs and you can''t keep it forever.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7904', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%A1%E5%8F%8E', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T00:00:25.178073Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:17.169879Z'),
(7905, 'vocabulary', '犠牲', '犠牲', 'Who do you <kanji>sacrifice</kanji> as an <kanji>offering</kanji> to the gods? A poor <vocabulary>victim</vocabulary> will be your <vocabulary>sacrifice</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7905', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8A%A0%E7%89%B2', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T00:03:53.502444Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:17.183776Z'),
(7906, 'vocabulary', '猟', '猟', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7906', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8C%9F', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T00:06:59.80207Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.404483Z'),
(7907, 'vocabulary', '猟師', '猟師', 'If you can <kanji>teach</kanji> <kanji>hunting</kanji> then you must be a really accomplished <vocabulary>hunter</vocabulary> yourself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7907', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8C%9F%E5%B8%AB', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T00:11:45.902961Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.416368Z'),
(7908, 'vocabulary', '密猟', '密猟', '<kanji>Hunting</kanji> in <kanji>secrecy</kanji> is only done through illegal <vocabulary>poaching</vocabulary>. If you weren''t poaching protected animals you wouldn''t have to do it secretly. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7908', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%86%E7%8C%9F', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T00:13:41.609269Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.427789Z'),
(7909, 'vocabulary', '狩猟', '狩猟', '<kanji>Hunting</kanji> where you <kanji>hunt</kanji> is still <vocabulary>hunting</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7909', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%A9%E7%8C%9F', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T00:14:56.020638Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.438764Z'),
(7910, 'vocabulary', '猟犬', '猟犬', 'A <kanji>hunting</kanji> <kanji>dog</kanji> is a <vocabulary>hunting dog</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7910', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8C%9F%E7%8A%AC', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T00:16:04.089923Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.450432Z'),
(7911, 'vocabulary', '発祥', '発祥', 'Your <kanji>auspicious</kanji> <kanji>departure</kanji> from another world is your <vocabulary>origin</vocabulary> story. You originally came from another place, and you had to leave there gloriously before you could enter this world.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7911', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BA%E7%A5%A5', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T00:17:49.372974Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:22:52.12866Z'),
(7912, 'vocabulary', '不祥事', '不祥事', 'A <kanji>not</kanji>-<kanji>auspicious</kanji> <kanji>occurrence</kanji> is a <vocabulary>scandal</vocabulary>. A scandal is pretty much the opposite of an auspicious occurrence.

不祥事 refers to a scandal that brings about dishonor, particularly to institutions, organizations, or public figures. It often involves misconduct, corruption, or other unethical behavior that has a significant negative impact on their public image.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7912', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E7%A5%A5%E4%BA%8B', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T00:18:47.62264Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:47:34.523008Z'),
(7913, 'vocabulary', '秩序', '秩序', 'The <kanji>preface</kanji> of <kanji>order</kanji> is still <vocabulary>order</vocabulary>. Because order is always prefaced by more order. Order keeps us all in check.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7913', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%A9%E5%BA%8F', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T00:19:43.08939Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.484462Z'),
(7914, 'vocabulary', '食糧', '食糧', '<kanji>Provisions</kanji> you can <kanji>eat</kanji> are still <vocabulary>provisions</vocabulary> and sometimes called <vocabulary>rations</vocabulary>, especially when troops are eating them.

This word often refers to <vocabulary>staple foods</vocabulary> like rice and wheat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7914', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9F%E7%B3%A7', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T00:20:33.557879Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.505921Z'),
(7915, 'vocabulary', '膨大', '膨大', 'A thing that <kanji>swell</kanji>s <kanji>big</kanji> ends up being <vocabulary>huge</vocabulary> or at least really <vocabulary>bulky</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7915', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%86%A8%E5%A4%A7', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T00:21:32.590435Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(7916, 'vocabulary', '膨れる', '膨れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>swell</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to swell</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to expand</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7916', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%86%A8%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T00:25:56.492243Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.529323Z'),
(7917, 'vocabulary', '膨張', '膨張', 'You need to <kanji>swell</kanji> and <kanji>stretch</kanji> if you want to see any kind of <vocabulary>expansion</vocabulary> of your company. Growth can''t happen without a little wiggle room.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7917', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%86%A8%E5%BC%B5', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:02:45.719802Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.541447Z'),
(7918, 'vocabulary', '芳香', '芳香', 'An <kanji>aromatic</kanji> <kanji>fragrance</kanji> is usually a <vocabulary>fragrance</vocabulary> with a <vocabulary>sweet smell</vocabulary>.

芳香 is a formal word for a pleasant, natural fragrance, like the scent of roses. You''ll most likely see this in the word 芳香剤 (air fresheners), but it''s also used in literature for pleasant aromas.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7918', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B3%E9%A6%99', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:04:06.877821Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:12:44.910804Z'),
(7919, 'vocabulary', '芳しい', '芳しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. The kanji is <kanji>aromatic</kanji>, so this also means <vocabulary>aromatic</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>fragrant</vocabulary>. 

芳しい is a formal word, often used in writing to describe <vocabulary>sweet-smelling</vocabulary> flowers, tea, or incense. In a figurative sense it can mean "favorable," too, but this meaning mostly appears in the negative form 芳しくない (unfavorable, poorly received).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7919', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B3%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:05:41.98633Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:23:00.669016Z'),
(7920, 'vocabulary', '茨', '茨', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7920', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8C%A8', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:07:04.788297Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T11:15:27.171603Z'),
(7921, 'vocabulary', '覇気', '覇気', 'If you have the <kanji>leadership</kanji> <kanji>spirit</kanji> it means you have a lot of <vocabulary>ambition</vocabulary> to go after the things you want. And you''ll be able to lead others because of it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7921', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%87%E6%B0%97', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:08:17.971384Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:11:43.434481Z'),
(7922, 'vocabulary', '制覇', '制覇', 'Those with <kanji>leadership</kanji> who want <kanji>control</kanji> will set out on missions of <vocabulary>conquest</vocabulary> and strive for world <vocabulary>domination</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7922', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%B6%E8%A6%87', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:09:23.970927Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:11:43.434481Z'),
(7923, 'vocabulary', '覇権', '覇権', 'Those with the <kanji>rights</kanji> to <kanji>leadership</kanji> have complete <vocabulary>hegemony</vocabulary> over the country. And they will reign until the end of time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7923', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%87%E6%A8%A9', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:10:54.293085Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:11:43.434481Z'),
(7924, 'vocabulary', '覇者', '覇者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> with the most <kanji>leadership</kanji> is known as the <vocabulary>supreme ruler</vocabulary>. No one has more leadership than them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7924', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%87%E8%80%85', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:13:22.985803Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:11:43.434481Z'),
(7925, 'vocabulary', '連覇', '連覇', 'If you <kanji>take along</kanji> your <kanji>leadership</kanji> over and over, you''re on a <vocabulary>winning streak</vocabulary>! You can expect <vocabulary>successive victories </vocabulary> in <vocabulary>consecutive championships</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7925', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%A3%E8%A6%87', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:17:22.95837Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:11:43.434481Z'),
(7926, 'vocabulary', '〜貫', '〜貫', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7926', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E8%B2%AB', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:20:23.273523Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.662776Z'),
(7927, 'vocabulary', '一貫', '一貫', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>sushi counter</kanji> is counting <vocabulary>one piece of sushi</vocabulary>. This can also mean <vocabulary>consistency</vocabulary>, which makes sense if you think about piece of sushi—each one should be as consistent as the last! Linked to this, it can also mean <vocabulary>integration</vocabulary>, because integration is easier if there''s consistency, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7927', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E8%B2%AB', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:42:33.346922Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.723175Z'),
(7928, 'vocabulary', '貫通', '貫通', 'When you <kanji>pierce</kanji> some fabric with a needle it needs to <kanji>pass through</kanji> to the other side to actually <vocabulary>pierce</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>penetrate</vocabulary> it. Make sure it goes all the way through if you want to sew properly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7928', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%AB%E9%80%9A', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:43:37.461349Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.735904Z'),
(7929, 'vocabulary', '貫徹', '貫徹', 'You have to <kanji>pierce</kanji> this piece of information into your brain until it finally <kanji>penetrate</kanji>s, which takes a lot of <vocabulary>persistence</vocabulary>. Do that and it will be quite the <vocabulary>accomplishment</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7929', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%AB%E5%BE%B9', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:49:09.57846Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.76258Z'),
(7930, 'vocabulary', '貫く', '貫く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>pierce</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to go through</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to pierce</vocabulary>.

貫く is a relatively formal word, mostly used in writing. It can mean physically piercing something, but more often it means <vocabulary>to follow through</vocabulary> on your beliefs or goals all the way to the end.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7930', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%AB%E3%81%8F', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:50:40.320868Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.801747Z'),
(7931, 'vocabulary', '突貫', '突貫', 'If you <kanji>stab</kanji> to <kanji>pierce</kanji> something, you’d better do it in a <vocabulary>rush</vocabulary>. If you aren''t quick enough, you could get into trouble, so rush forth with a mighty charge.

突貫 means doing something in a rush. It’s usually used in the compound 突貫工事, which means fast but often sloppy construction work. It can also describe charging at an enemy, but that meaning is less common today.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7931', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AA%81%E8%B2%AB', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:51:50.012494Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.814101Z'),
(7932, 'vocabulary', '賠償', '賠償', '<kanji>Compensation</kanji> <kanji>reparation</kanji>s are still <vocabulary>compensation</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>reparations</vocabulary>! That''s convenient.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7932', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%A0%E5%84%9F', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:55:17.98817Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.838206Z'),
(7933, 'vocabulary', '処遇', '処遇', 'You should <kanji>deal with</kanji> and change your <kanji>treatment</kanji> of certain people depending on what kind of situation you''re in. This vocab is just the same, making this <vocabulary>dealing with</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>treatment</vocabulary.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7933', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%A6%E9%81%87', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:57:53.561531Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.850268Z'),
(7934, 'vocabulary', '遭遇', '遭遇', 'The kanji here are <kanji>encounter</kanji> and <kanji>encounter</kanji>, which makes this… <vocabulary>encounter</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7934', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%AD%E9%81%87', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T16:59:17.109765Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.86325Z'),
(7935, 'vocabulary', '待遇', '待遇', 'Just because you have to <kanji>wait</kanji> for special <kanji>treatment</kanji> doesn''t mean it''s not still good <vocabulary>treatment</vocabulary>. And just because everyone didn''t cheer when you arrived, doesn''t mean you''re getting a cold <vocabulary>reception</vocabulary>, you know? Maybe they''re planning a surprise party for you or something... yeah.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7935', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%85%E9%81%87', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:00:21.565155Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.876207Z'),
(7936, 'vocabulary', '優遇', '優遇', 'When you are given <kanji>superior</kanji> <kanji>treatment</kanji> it''s called <vocabulary>preferential treatment</vocabulary>. This is the best way to be treated.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7936', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%84%AA%E9%81%87', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:01:40.581259Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.890459Z'),
(7937, 'vocabulary', '冷遇', '冷遇', 'When you are given <kanji>cool</kanji> <kanji>treatment</kanji> it''s called a <vocabulary>cold reception</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>inhospitality</vocabulary>. This sucks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7937', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%B7%E9%81%87', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:03:42.05495Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.903503Z'),
(7938, 'vocabulary', '境遇', '境遇', 'The <kanji>treatment</kanji> of your <kanji>boundary</kanji> of your neighborhood is something you should be concerned about, because it''s your <vocabulary>environment</vocabulary> and if you don''t take care of it, who will?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7938', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%83%E9%81%87', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:04:40.004956Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:33:34.678262Z'),
(7939, 'vocabulary', '遭う', '遭う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>encounter</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to encounter</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to meet</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7939', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%AD%E3%81%86', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:05:45.228736Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.929309Z'),
(7940, 'vocabulary', '遭難', '遭難', 'If you <kanji>encounter</kanji> something <kanji>difficult</kanji> it''s going to result in <vocabulary>disaster</vocabulary>. Especially if you''re in a boat. Encountering really difficult water will result in a <vocabulary>shipwreck</vocabulary>! Ick!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7940', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%AD%E9%9B%A3', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:06:48.30264Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:57.942576Z'),
(7941, 'vocabulary', '鎖', '鎖', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7941', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8E%96', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:07:43.465674Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:07:52.612107Z'),
(7942, 'vocabulary', '鎖国', '鎖国', 'A <kanji>country</kanji> in <kanji>chain</kanji>s is under a <vocabulary>national isolation</vocabulary> policy. They''re bound and they can''t visit anywhere else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7942', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8E%96%E5%9B%BD', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:09:25.577228Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:07:52.612107Z'),
(7943, 'vocabulary', '封鎖', '封鎖', 'A <kanji>seal</kanji>ed <kanji>chain</kanji> won''t break or open so it gets in the way of everyone, forming a <vocabulary>blockade</vocabulary>. This can happen at banks too and it will result in them <vocabulary>freezing</vocabulary> your account!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7943', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%81%E9%8E%96', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:10:21.102668Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:57:46.95136Z'),
(7944, 'vocabulary', '閉鎖', '閉鎖', 'A <kanji>closed</kanji> <kanji>chain</kanji> lets everyone know that a building or store is <vocabulary>closing</vocabulary> for good. It''s got to <vocabulary>lockout</vocabulary> everyone so they won''t try to get in anymore.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7944', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%89%E9%8E%96', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:16:37.218108Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:07:52.612107Z'),
(7945, 'vocabulary', '連鎖', '連鎖', 'If you <kanji>take along</kanji> a <kanji>chain</kanji> with you it''s still a <vocabulary>chain</vocabulary>, it''s just moving somewhere new.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7945', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%A3%E9%8E%96', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:25:02.493451Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:07:52.612107Z'),
(7946, 'vocabulary', '陥る', '陥る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>cave in</kanji>, and the verb version takes that feeling further. It means <vocabulary>to fall into</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be thrown into</vocabulary> something negative, like a trap, trouble, or even a bad emotional state.

陥る describes a moment when the world around you or your heart caves in, and you''re suddenly thrown into that space and get trapped inside. It''s like slipping into a dark hole in life you didn''t see coming. That''s the feeling!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7946', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%A5%E3%82%8B', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:55:50.728895Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:14:18.0193Z'),
(7947, 'vocabulary', '陥没', '陥没', 'A <kanji>cave in</kanji> where everyone <kanji>die</kanji>s is still a <vocabulary>cave in</vocabulary>, just a really sad one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7947', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%A5%E6%B2%A1', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:58:02.212602Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.054252Z'),
(7948, 'vocabulary', '陥落', '陥落', 'If you <kanji>fall</kanji> during a <kanji>cave in</kanji> you''ll <vocabulary>fall</vocabulary> along with everything around you. And that <vocabulary>sinking</vocabulary> feeling in your stomach is because... I mean, well you ARE sinking into the ground. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7948', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%A5%E8%90%BD', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T17:59:46.336448Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:46:04.638998Z'),
(7949, 'vocabulary', '欠陥', '欠陥', 'This item is <kanji>lack</kanji>ing a piece, causing its surface to <kanji>cave in</kanji>. What a <vocabulary>defect</vocabulary>!

欠陥 is a relatively formal word that refers to a <vocabulary>fault</vocabulary> in something, whether in a physical object, a system, or even a plan. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7949', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%A0%E9%99%A5', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T18:01:23.798498Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T23:48:38.926208Z'),
(7950, 'vocabulary', '陳腐', '陳腐', 'If you keep <kanji>exhibit</kanji>ing the same actions and emotions and phrases eventually they''re going to <kanji>rot</kanji> and sound <vocabulary>cliched</vocabulary> to everyone. You have to mix things up once in a while. You don''t want to get <vocabulary>stale</vocabulary>, do you?!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7950', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%B3%E8%85%90', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T18:02:46.51975Z', NULL, '2025-12-05T19:33:00.426254Z'),
(7951, 'vocabulary', '陳列', '陳列', 'An <kanji>exhibit</kanji> of things in a <kanji>row</kanji> only happens at an <vocabulary>exhibition</vocabulary>. Everything will be neatly lined up on <vocabulary>display</vocabulary> for you there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7951', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%B3%E5%88%97', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T18:04:21.158096Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.106328Z'),
(7952, 'vocabulary', '陳述', '陳述', '<kanji>Exhibit</kanji> what you want to <kanji>mention</kanji> in a loud, clear <vocabulary>statement</vocabulary>. You have to say it if you want people to listen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7952', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%B3%E8%BF%B0', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T18:05:49.025717Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.118243Z'),
(7953, 'vocabulary', '陳情', '陳情', '<kanji>Exhibit</kanji> your <kanji>feeling</kanji>s in a <vocabulary>petition</vocabulary>. If you feel something is unjust you have to say something. Form an <vocabulary>appeal</vocabulary> and fight back!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7953', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%B3%E6%83%85', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T18:07:15.226931Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.129158Z'),
(7954, 'vocabulary', '隼', '隼', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7954', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%BC', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T18:08:12.522267Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.141905Z'),
(7955, 'vocabulary', '必須', '必須', 'Something that''s <kanji>certain</kanji>ly <kanji>necessary</kanji> is something you can''t live without. It''s <vocabulary>indispensable</vocabulary>. And it''s <vocabulary>required</vocabulary> by you to keep on living.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7955', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%85%E9%A0%88', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T18:08:59.249363Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:33:00.725847Z'),
(7956, 'vocabulary', '颯と', '颯と', 'This is the adverb version of the kanji <kanji>quick</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>quickly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7956', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A2%AF%E3%81%A8', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T18:11:09.636066Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.168104Z'),
(7957, 'vocabulary', 'お風呂', 'お風呂', 'Let the <kanji>wind</kanji> in from the window above your <kanji>bath</kanji> so the water is cool enough for you to take a <vocabulary>bath</vocabulary>! If the water is too hot, you''ll get all dizzy and that''s dangerous!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7957', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E9%A2%A8%E5%91%82', 52, 1, '2015-07-01T18:12:36.969153Z', NULL, '2025-07-01T19:41:45.398894Z'),
(7958, 'vocabulary', '皮膚科', '皮膚科', 'Remember how you learned that 皮膚 is "skin?" Well the <kanji>science</kanji> of skin is <vocabulary>dermatology</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7958', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%AE%E8%86%9A%E7%A7%91', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:28:18.360361Z', NULL, '2026-03-26T08:11:42.807516Z'),
(7959, 'vocabulary', '皮膚病', '皮膚病', 'Remember how you learned that 皮膚 is "skin?" Well when your skin is <kanji>sick</kanji>, you have a <vocabulary>skin disease</vocabulary>. You should probably go to a dermatologist right away.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7959', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9A%AE%E8%86%9A%E7%97%85', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:29:45.753823Z', NULL, '2026-03-26T08:11:42.807516Z'),
(7960, 'vocabulary', '襟元', '襟元', 'The <kanji>origin</kanji> of your <kanji>collar</kanji> is your <vocabulary>collar</vocabulary>! And the <vocabulary>nape of the neck</vocabulary> because that''s where collars go.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7960', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%9F%E5%85%83', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:31:44.772285Z', NULL, '2026-02-20T01:45:31.12494Z'),
(7961, 'vocabulary', '襟巻き', '襟巻き', 'Remember how you learned that 巻く means "to roll?" Well the thing you roll up around your <kanji>collar</kanji> is a <vocabulary>scarf</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7961', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%9F%E5%B7%BB%E3%81%8D', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:35:15.457137Z', NULL, '2026-02-20T01:45:31.12494Z'),
(7962, 'vocabulary', '丹誠', '丹誠', 'A miner works with such <kanji>sincerity</kanji> that her arms and legs start turning <kanji>rust-colored</kanji> from <vocabulary>working earnestly</vocabulary>. All the clay and rocks she''s putting her <vocabulary>effort</vocabulary> into digging are turning her the same color. That color is how you know someone works with serious <vocabulary>diligence</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7962', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%B9%E8%AA%A0', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:36:20.925557Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:31:18.785014Z'),
(7963, 'vocabulary', '丹念', '丹念', 'You have <kanji>rust-colored</kanji> <kanji>thought</kanji>s because you''re so <vocabulary>diligent</vocabulary>. You have a really old-school way of thinking about things — so old-school that your thoughts are getting rusty — and that''s why you manage to be <vocabulary>careful</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>meticulous</vocabulary> even in this fast-paced modern world.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7963', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%B9%E5%BF%B5', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:37:43.158892Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T17:55:52.120197Z'),
(7964, 'vocabulary', '批准', '批准', 'If there is only <kanji>semi</kanji> <kanji>criticism</kanji> on a law <vocabulary>ratification</vocabulary> is still possible. It''s only if everyone criticizes it that it won''t be ratified.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7964', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%B9%E5%87%86', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:38:53.222936Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.259654Z'),
(7965, 'vocabulary', '過剰', '過剰', 'If you <kanji>surpass</kanji> your <kanji>surplus</kanji> then you have lots of <vocabulary>excess</vocabulary> stuff.

過剰 is a relatively formal word that refers to something being <vocabulary>excessive</vocabulary>. It can also often mean <vocabulary>over</vocabulary> — like overconsumption, overreaction, or overproduction — in various contexts related to behaviors, emotions, or physical quantities.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7965', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8E%E5%89%B0', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:40:04.515355Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T03:12:02.433453Z'),
(7966, 'vocabulary', '余剰', '余剰', 'If you have a <kanji>surplus</kanji> <kanji>surplus</kanji> it''s a little <vocabulary>redundant</vocabulary> and you end up having a <vocabulary>surplus</vocabulary> of surpluses! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7966', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%99%E5%89%B0', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:41:08.202146Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.282383Z'),
(7967, 'vocabulary', '剰余金', '剰余金', '<kanji>Surplus</kanji> of <kanji>surplus</kanji> <kanji>money</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>surplus</vocabulary>. There''s just too much money everywhere!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7967', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%B0%E4%BD%99%E9%87%91', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:42:25.874857Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.294179Z'),
(7968, 'vocabulary', '召し上がる', '召し上がる', 'You''ve learned that 召す can mean "to take" when a social higher-up takes a meal or a drink. Now you''re adding 上がる (to go up), so you''ve got the added image of the food or drink rising up to their mouth. In other words, this is a respectful way of saying <vocabulary>to eat</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to drink</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7968', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%AC%E3%81%97%E4%B8%8A%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:44:13.176609Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(7969, 'vocabulary', '啓示', '啓示', 'What <kanji>indicate</kanji>s that you''ve been <kanji>enlighten</kanji>ed enough to reach enlightenment? A <vocabulary>revelation</vocabulary>! That''s the only way you can know you''ve been enlightened enough.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7969', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%95%93%E7%A4%BA', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:45:57.184666Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.318504Z'),
(7970, 'vocabulary', '啓発', '啓発', 'When you are <kanji>enlighten</kanji>ed enough you''ll need to make your <kanji>departure</kanji> from this world into <vocabulary>enlightenment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7970', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%95%93%E7%99%BA', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:47:10.364353Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:22:52.12866Z'),
(7971, 'vocabulary', '啓蒙', '啓蒙', '<kanji>Enlighten</kanji> the <kanji>darkness</kanji> of people''s minds with <vocabulary>instruction</vocabulary> and they''ll reach <vocabulary>enlightenment</vocabulary> like you did!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7971', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%95%93%E8%92%99', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:48:06.679771Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.341078Z'),
(7972, 'vocabulary', '土壌', '土壌', '<kanji>Soil</kanji> <kanji>soil</kanji> is still <kanji>soil</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7972', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%9F%E5%A3%8C', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:49:03.737469Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.353346Z'),
(7973, 'vocabulary', '平壌', '平壌', 'What''s the city of <kanji>flat</kanji> <kanji>soil</kanji>? <vocabulary>Pyongyang</vocabulary> in North Korea. Which is pretty misleading, it''s got more than a bunch of flat dirt, but back in the old days before the illustrious Kim Jong line built it up, that''s all that was there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7973', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B3%E5%A3%8C', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:50:19.954512Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.364529Z'),
(7974, 'vocabulary', '寛大', '寛大', 'It doesn''t matter how <kanji>big</kanji> your <kanji>tolerance</kanji> is because it''s still <vocabulary>tolerance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7974', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%9B%E5%A4%A7', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:53:08.090199Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(7975, 'vocabulary', '寛容', '寛容', 'All <kanji>forms</kanji> of <kanji>tolerance</kanji> are still <vocabulary>tolerance</vocabulary>. Usually people like this are really <vocabulary>open minded</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>generous</vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7975', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%9B%E5%AE%B9', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:54:26.956044Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.389187Z'),
(7976, 'vocabulary', '寛ぐ', '寛ぐ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>tolerance</kanji>, so the verb version is what happens when you''re a really tolerant person. You''re able <vocabulary>to relax</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to feel at home</vocabulary> because nothing really gets to you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7976', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%9B%E3%81%90', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:55:26.657823Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.400967Z'),
(7977, 'vocabulary', '元帥', '元帥', 'The <kanji>commander</kanji>''s <kanji>origin</kanji> was as the <vocabulary>general of the army</vocabulary>. He stepped down, but that''s where he started. Maybe he''s working the system backwards...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7977', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%83%E5%B8%A5', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:57:08.646849Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.412711Z'),
(7978, 'vocabulary', '総帥', '総帥', 'The <kanji>commander</kanji> of the <kanji>whole</kanji> army is just called " the <vocabulary>commander</vocabulary>." No one knows his real name and everyone is too afraid to ask. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7978', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%8F%E5%B8%A5', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:58:17.341819Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.424879Z'),
(7979, 'vocabulary', '統帥', '統帥', 'The <kanji>commander</kanji> that <kanji>unite</kanji>s and <kanji>rule</kanji>s the world is one with <vocabulary>supreme command</vocabulary>. He''s better than all the other commanders who couldn''t bring everyone together and now he rules over everyone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7979', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B1%E5%B8%A5', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T21:59:12.146337Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.437159Z'),
(7980, 'vocabulary', '徐行', '徐行', 'If you <kanji>go</kanji> <kanji>gently</kanji> you''re actually <vocabulary>going slowly</vocabulary>. You can''t be gentle if you don''t move slowly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7980', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%90%E8%A1%8C', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T22:00:30.519692Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.449266Z'),
(7981, 'vocabulary', '徐々', '徐々', 'If you do it <kanji>gently</kanji> <kanji>gently</kanji> then you''re only doing it <vocabulary>gradually</vocabulary>. You have to go <vocabulary>slowly</vocabulary> if you want to be as gentle as possible.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7981', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%90%E3%80%85', 53, 1, '2015-07-01T22:01:37.052887Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(7982, 'vocabulary', '惨事', '惨事', 'A <kanji>disaster</kanji> <kanji>occurrence</kanji> is just a <vocabulary>disaster</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>tragedy</vocabulary>. Look away!

惨事 is a formal word for a tragic or <vocabulary>gruesome event</vocabulary> — the kind that''s so distressing it makes you want to avert your eyes. It emphasizes the horror and emotional pain of the event.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7982', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%A8%E4%BA%8B', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T16:25:53.761073Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:47:34.523008Z'),
(7983, 'vocabulary', '悲惨', '悲惨', 'A <kanji>sad</kanji> <kanji>disaster</kanji> is one that''s especially <vocabulary>disastrous</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>tragic</vocabulary>. It''s the worst kind you can imagine.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7983', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%B2%E6%83%A8', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T16:29:57.709576Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.48396Z'),
(7984, 'vocabulary', '惨め', '惨め', 'If you face a lot of <kanji>disaster</kanji>s in your life you''re going to be really <vocabulary>miserable</vocabulary>. It''s a shame, but it''s hard to be anything but <vocabulary>wretched</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>pathetic</vocabulary> when your life is full of disastrous stuff.

惨め describes someone or something as miserable, usually emphasizing sadness or failure in emotional or social situations. When you describe yourself as 惨め, it expresses a strong sense of helplessness and humiliation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7984', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%A8%E3%82%81', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T16:36:59.88043Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T03:16:01.938541Z'),
(7985, 'vocabulary', '惨敗', '惨敗', '<kanji>Failure</kanji> that causes <kanji>disaster</kanji> is a <vocabulary>crushing failure</vocabulary> and you were <vocabulary>utterly beaten</vocabulary> by your opponent.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7985', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%A8%E6%95%97', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T16:40:44.51198Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.508134Z'),
(7986, 'vocabulary', '惨状', '惨状', '<kanji>Disastrous</kanji> <kanji>condition</kanji>s in the weather lead to you seeing a <vocabulary>disastrous scene</vocabulary>. Just don''t look outside and you won''t see the horrible stuff out your window right now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7986', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%A8%E7%8A%B6', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T16:42:08.501127Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.519713Z'),
(7987, 'vocabulary', '披露', '披露', '<kanji>Expose</kanji> what you want to <kanji>expose</kanji> by making an <vocabulary>announcement</vocabulary>. Then <vocabulary>show</vocabulary> the world!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7987', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%AB%E9%9C%B2', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T16:43:12.468182Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.531057Z'),
(7988, 'vocabulary', '挟まる', '挟まる', 'You already learned the word 挟む is for putting something between two things. 挟まる is the intransitive version — it''s used to describe when something gets caught between two things with no direct object. So it means <vocabulary>to get between</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be caught in</vocabulary>.

You use 挟まる for situations like your jacket getting caught in a car door, food getting stuck between your teeth, or a memo being tucked between pages.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7988', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%9F%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T16:45:12.723199Z', NULL, '2025-08-27T00:35:03.437648Z'),
(7989, 'vocabulary', '据える', '据える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>install</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to install</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to fix</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7989', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%AE%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T16:49:06.269077Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.556162Z'),
(7990, 'vocabulary', '搭載', '搭載', 'When something has already been <kanji>board</kanji>ed and <kanji>put on</kanji>to something, it comes <vocabulary>built in</vocabulary>.

搭載 refers to something built into or onto another object, which generally is a vehicle or a machine.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7990', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%AD%E8%BC%89', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T16:51:19.884446Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.579354Z'),
(7991, 'vocabulary', '搭乗', '搭乗', 'When you <kanji>board</kanji> a plane or a car to take a <kanji>ride</kanji> it''s called <vocabulary>boarding</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>embarkation</vocabulary>. You have to get on the right way or you can''t ride.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7991', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%AD%E4%B9%97', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T16:53:47.159268Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.606731Z'),
(7992, 'vocabulary', '曙', '曙', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7992', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%99', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T16:55:01.035015Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.619466Z'),
(7993, 'vocabulary', '枯れ木', '枯れ木', 'A <kanji>wither</kanji>ed <kanji>tree</kanji> is a <vocabulary>dead tree</vocabulary> and it''s got really <vocabulary>dry wood</vocabulary>. Perfect for a campfire!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7993', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%AF%E3%82%8C%E6%9C%A8', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T16:58:12.882148Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.630764Z'),
(7994, 'vocabulary', '枯らす', '枯らす', 'You learned 枯れる (to wither), but this is when you''re the one making a plant wither, so it means <vocabulary>to let wither</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to kill a plant</vocabulary>.

枯らす literally means <vocabulary>to let something dry up</vocabulary>, usually by neglect or lack of water (though too much water can also 枯らす plants). It''s often used when someone accidentally lets plants die, but it can also describe intentionally killing plants with a herbicide. You''ll also see it used figuratively, like 声を枯らす (to lose one''s voice).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7994', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%AF%E3%82%89%E3%81%99', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:01:33.433148Z', NULL, '2026-01-14T18:02:00.09764Z'),
(7995, 'vocabulary', '沸かす', '沸かす', 'You already learned the word 沸く which means "to boil" or "to grow hot" but this is when you are the one heating the water. It still means <vocabulary>to boil</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to heat</vocabulary>. But remember that you''re the one who is doing the heating.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7995', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%B8%E3%81%8B%E3%81%99', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:04:41.641385Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.654866Z'),
(7996, 'vocabulary', '沸騰', '沸騰', 'The <kanji>inflation</kanji> of <kanji>boil</kanji>ing water means that bubbles are being inflated in there and bubbling to the top. This is called <vocabulary>boiling</vocabulary>, or if it''s happening in a person it''s called <vocabulary>seething</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7996', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%B8%E9%A8%B0', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:06:36.563052Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.667189Z'),
(7997, 'vocabulary', '自浄', '自浄', 'If you <kanji>purify</kanji> your <kanji>self</kanji> you are performing <vocabulary>self purification</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7997', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E6%B5%84', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:09:58.921678Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.680204Z'),
(7998, 'vocabulary', '浄化', '浄化', '<kanji>Purify</kanji> water to <kanji>change</kanji> it from being dirty. This is called <vocabulary>purification</vocabulary>. It''s important to purify water before you drink it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7998', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%84%E5%8C%96', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:11:14.623738Z', NULL, '2025-11-20T03:47:15.21122Z'),
(7999, 'vocabulary', '浄土', '浄土', 'Land where all the people have been <kanji>cleanse</kanji>d and <kanji>purified</kanji> is the <vocabulary>Pure Land</vocabulary> of Buddhism. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/7999', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%84%E5%9C%9F', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:15:01.436449Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.706339Z'),
(8000, 'vocabulary', '不浄', '不浄', 'If you have <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>cleanse</kanji>d yourself you''re going to be covered in <vocabulary>uncleanliness</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>dirtiness</vocabulary>. Eww. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8000', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%B5%84', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:18:16.613212Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.719418Z'),
(8001, 'vocabulary', '浄水', '浄水', 'When you <kanji>cleanse</kanji> <kanji>water</kanji> it becomes <vocabulary>clean water</vocabulary> that you can drink.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8001', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%84%E6%B0%B4', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:19:30.031908Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.731514Z')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;


洗浄 is a relatively formal, technical word that you''ll often see in manuals, product labels, medicine, or industrial contexts. For example, contact lens cleaning solution is called コンタクトレンズの洗浄液, and a dishwasher is called a 食器洗浄機. It''s also used metaphorically in terms like 資金洗浄 (money laundering).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8003', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%97%E6%B5%84', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:22:49.911211Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:37:53.369888Z'),
(8004, 'vocabulary', '渦巻き', '渦巻き', 'This is a combination of the word 渦 meaning "whirlpool" and 巻く meaning "to roll." A rolling whirlpool is still a <vocabulary>whirlpool</vocabulary>, just a really violent one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8004', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%A6%E5%B7%BB%E3%81%8D', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:24:05.074549Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.765314Z'),
(8005, 'vocabulary', '玄人', '玄人', 'A <kanji>mysterious</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is really really good at what they do. So good that they aren''t exactly sure what is it they do, but you know they''re definitely an <vocabulary>expert</vocabulary> at whatever it is.

玄人 is a relatively formal word that refers to a highly skilled <vocabulary>professional</vocabulary> in a specific field, as opposed to 素人 (amateur). In casual conversation, words like プロ (professional) are more common.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8005', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8E%84%E4%BA%BA', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:30:19.974364Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T18:07:32.224525Z'),
(8006, 'vocabulary', '瓜', '瓜', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8006', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%93%9C', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:32:48.88048Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.786569Z'),
(8007, 'vocabulary', '西瓜', '西瓜', 'In the <kanji>west</kanji>, <kanji>melons</kanji> are round and striped and green, and… Wait a second. That''s a <vocabulary>watermelon</vocabulary>! I guess watermelons come from the western part of Japan?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8007', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%BF%E7%93%9C', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:34:02.431463Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:19:07.778293Z'),
(8008, 'vocabulary', '原稿', '原稿', 'Your <kanji>original</kanji> <kanji>draft</kanji> is called a <vocabulary>manuscript</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8008', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%9F%E7%A8%BF', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:35:34.445299Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.811318Z'),
(8009, 'vocabulary', '草稿', '草稿', 'You end up writing your rough <kanji>draft</kanji> on some <kanji>grass</kanji> leaves because you forgot your <vocabulary>notes</vocabulary>. This grass will serve as your notes now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8009', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8D%89%E7%A8%BF', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:36:26.027677Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.824796Z'),
(8010, 'vocabulary', '投稿', '投稿', '<kanji>Throw</kanji> your <kanji>draft</kanji> at people to <vocabulary>post</vocabulary> it on their Facebook wall! Wait, that''s not how it works? Hush, yes it does, just throw it harder.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8010', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%95%E7%A8%BF', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:37:22.847455Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.837648Z'),
(8011, 'vocabulary', '稿料', '稿料', '稿料 is short for 原稿料, which is a combination of 原稿 (manuscript) and <kanji>fee</kanji>. Unsurprisingly, that makes this a <vocabulary>manuscript fee</vocabulary>. It''s the <vocabulary>payment for writing</vocabulary> that an author gets for turning in their copy!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8011', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%BF%E6%96%99', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:38:26.756737Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T22:51:49.408534Z'),
(8012, 'vocabulary', '緯度', '緯度', '<kanji>Latitude</kanji> <kanji>degree</kanji>s are still known as <vocabulary>latitude</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8012', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%AF%E5%BA%A6', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:39:22.644961Z', NULL, '2026-01-01T16:52:47.454928Z'),
(8013, 'vocabulary', '南緯', '南緯', '<kanji>South</kanji> <kanji>latitude</kanji> is <vocabulary>south latitude</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>southern latitude</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8013', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%97%E7%B7%AF', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:41:41.247658Z', NULL, '2026-01-01T16:52:47.454928Z'),
(8014, 'vocabulary', '北緯', '北緯', '<kanji>North</kanji> <kanji>latitude</kanji> is <vocabulary>north latitude</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>northern latitude</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8014', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%97%E7%B7%AF', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:42:35.868326Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.438028Z'),
(8015, 'vocabulary', '経緯', '経緯', 'If you are trying to <kanji>manage</kanji> <kanji>latitude</kanji>s you''re really trying to manage the <vocabulary>details</vocabulary> of something. Managing latitudes is just a fancy way of saying it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8015', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%8C%E7%B7%AF', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:43:27.512388Z', NULL, '2026-01-01T16:52:47.454928Z'),
(8016, 'vocabulary', '緯線', '緯線', 'A <kanji>latitude</kanji> <kanji>line</kanji> can also be called a <vocabulary>parallel</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8016', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%AF%E7%B7%9A', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:48:20.211301Z', NULL, '2026-01-01T16:52:47.454928Z'),
(8017, 'vocabulary', '化繊', '化繊', '<kanji>Fiber</kanji>s that have been <kanji>change</kanji>d from other materials are <vocabulary>synthetic fibers</vocabulary>. They aren''t natural because they were something else once. Like plastic, or kittens.

Note that this is an abbreviation of the specialist term 化学繊維, and it mostly comes up in industrial contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8017', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%96%E7%B9%8A', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:50:06.785284Z', NULL, '2025-11-20T03:47:15.21122Z'),
(8018, 'vocabulary', '繊維', '繊維', 'The <kanji>fiber</kanji> you use to <kanji>maintain</kanji> your own health is the <vocabulary>fiber</vocabulary> you eat!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8018', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B9%8A%E7%B6%AD', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:53:52.02262Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.933232Z'),
(8019, 'vocabulary', '合繊', '合繊', '<kanji>Fiber</kanji>s you <kanji>join</kanji> together to make more, stronger fibers, they turn into <vocabulary>synthetic fiber</vocabulary>s.

Note that this is an abbreviation of the specialist term 合成繊維, and it mostly comes up in industrial contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8019', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%88%E7%B9%8A', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:54:50.004293Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.944094Z'),
(8020, 'vocabulary', '繊細', '繊細', '<kanji>Slender</kanji> and <kanji>thin</kanji> things are very <vocabulary>delicate</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>dainty</vocabulary>. They''re easily broken, so you have to be careful.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8020', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B9%8A%E7%B4%B0', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:57:37.357418Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:47:55.647024Z'),
(8021, 'vocabulary', '肯く', '肯く', 'If you want to show your <kanji>agreement</kanji> with someone you need <vocabulary>to nod</vocabulary> at them so they know. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8021', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%AF%E3%81%8F', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T17:59:10.739801Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.968098Z'),
(8022, 'vocabulary', '胞子', '胞子', 'The <kanji>child</kanji> of a <kanji>cell</kanji> is a <vocabulary>spore</vocabulary>. They''re just as tiny and they''re floating all around you right now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8022', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%9E%E5%AD%90', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:00:30.955372Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:58.98132Z'),
(8023, 'vocabulary', '細胞', '細胞', '<kanji>Thin</kanji> <kanji>cell</kanji>s are what make up our bodies. They''re still <vocabulary>cells</vocabulary> but they can also be called <vocabulary>biological cells</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8023', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%B0%E8%83%9E', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:02:04.233278Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:47:55.647024Z'),
(8024, 'vocabulary', '同胞', '同胞', 'People who have the <kanji>same</kanji> <kanji>cell</kanji>s as you are your <vocabulary>brethren</vocabulary>, they''re your <vocabulary>brothers</vocabulary>!  ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8024', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8C%E8%83%9E', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:03:10.23034Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.006744Z'),
(8025, 'vocabulary', '胡座', '胡座', 'When <kanji>barbarian</kanji>s <kanji>sit</kanji> they only <vocabulary>sit cross legged</vocabulary>. Sitting on your feet hurts and they don''t care how they look, so they''re going to be comfortable and cross their legs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8025', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%A1%E5%BA%A7', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:04:16.554417Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:52:04.645576Z'),
(8026, 'vocabulary', '胡瓜', '胡瓜', '<kanji>Barbarian</kanji>s have <kanji>melon</kanji>s too, but they''re long and funny looking. Wait, those aren''t melons, they''re <vocabulary>cucumber</vocabulary>s! Barbarians must have mixed them up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8026', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%A1%E7%93%9C', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:05:50.352147Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.034712Z'),
(8027, 'vocabulary', '脂身', '脂身', 'The <kanji>fat</kanji> on an animal''s <kanji>body</kanji> is called <vocabulary>fatty meat</vocabulary>. Some people really like to eat it and others really like to hate it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8027', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%82%E8%BA%AB', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:07:10.135304Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:24:51.658554Z'),
(8028, 'vocabulary', '舗', '舗', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Note that you won''t actually see this used as a standalone word very often, except in the phrase 舗を構える (to open a shop), but even that is fairly rare! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8028', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%97', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:08:40.52919Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.061067Z'),
(8029, 'vocabulary', '店舗', '店舗', 'A <kanji>shop</kanji> <kanji>shop</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>shop</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8029', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%97%E8%88%97', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:09:23.876785Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.072371Z'),
(8030, 'vocabulary', '舗装', '舗装', 'When you <kanji>pave</kanji> and <kanji>attire</kanji> something, you’ll end up with a <vocabulary>pavement</vocabulary>. Imagine the asphalt used in the paving of a street as its attire and this’ll make sense!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8030', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%97%E8%A3%85', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:10:31.871362Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.08309Z'),
(8031, 'vocabulary', '本舗', '本舗', 'The <kanji>main</kanji> <kanji>shop</kanji> is the <vocabulary>main shop</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>head office</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8031', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E8%88%97', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:11:54.463023Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.093847Z'),
(8032, 'vocabulary', '艦艇', '艦艇', 'What do a <kanji>warship</kanji> and a <kanji>rowboat</kanji> have in common? In wartime they''re both considered <vocabulary>military vessel</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8032', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%89%A6%E8%89%87', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:12:53.03954Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.105645Z'),
(8033, 'vocabulary', '競艇', '競艇', 'To <kanji>compete</kanji> in a <kanji>rowboat</kanji> is to take part in a <vocabulary>boat race</vocabulary>, also known as a <vocabulary>regatta</vocabulary>. Who are you gonna bet on?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8033', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%B6%E8%89%87', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:20:08.971374Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.117236Z'),
(8034, 'vocabulary', '苗木', '苗木', 'A <kanji>seedling</kanji> <kanji>tree</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>seedling</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8034', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%97%E6%9C%A8', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:38:39.153103Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.128565Z'),
(8035, 'vocabulary', '苗床', '苗床', 'A <kanji>bed</kanji> just for <kanji>seedling</kanji>s is called a <vocabulary>nursery</vocabulary>. It''s where all the little seedling grow before they''re sold or replanted outside.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8035', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%97%E5%BA%8A', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:41:22.672265Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.140891Z'),
(8036, 'vocabulary', '葵', '葵', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8036', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%91%B5', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:42:12.687101Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.153506Z'),
(8037, 'vocabulary', '蒙古', '蒙古', 'The region of <kanji>darkness</kanji> in days of <kanji>old</kanji> referred to <vocabulary>Mongolia</vocabulary>. At least in China, where this word comes from. Imagine all those ancient Mongol tribes at the dark, unknown edges of China. Everything beyond the borders was shrouded in (figurative) darkness, I guess. No wonder they picked these kanji back then.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8037', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%92%99%E5%8F%A4', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:43:10.585371Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.166039Z'),
(8038, 'vocabulary', '蓮根', '蓮根', '<kanji>Lotus</kanji> <kanji>root</kanji> is <vocabulary>lotus root</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8038', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%93%AE%E6%A0%B9', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:44:13.762779Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:44:40.098639Z'),
(8039, 'vocabulary', '虐殺', '虐殺', 'To <kanji>oppress</kanji> people and then <kanji>kill</kanji> them is an <vocabulary>atrocity</vocabulary>. It''s also called a <vocabulary>massive killing</vocabulary> because that''s what you''re actually doing. And you''re awful, why would you ever do that?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8039', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%99%90%E6%AE%BA', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:45:19.466638Z', NULL, '2025-08-27T00:08:20.131366Z'),
(8040, 'vocabulary', '虐待', '虐待', 'If you can''t <kanji>wait</kanji> to <kanji>oppress</kanji> people when you grow up, you''re the kind of person who''s prone to inflicting <vocabulary>abuse</vocabulary> on other people. You should try to correct that right away.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8040', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%99%90%E5%BE%85', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:47:00.893358Z', NULL, '2025-08-27T00:08:20.131366Z'),
(8041, 'vocabulary', '残虐', '残虐', 'The <kanji>remainder</kanji> of <kanji>oppress</kanji>ion is <vocabulary>cruelty</vocabulary>. Even when you''re done oppressing people the <vocabulary>brutality</vocabulary> you caused will linger in their minds and hearts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8041', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%8B%E8%99%90', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:50:19.395813Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:35:43.39693Z'),
(8042, 'vocabulary', '暴虐', '暴虐', '<kanji>Violence</kanji> and <kanji>oppress</kanji>ion are all signs of <vocabulary>tyranny</vocabulary> and that someone is being really <vocabulary>cruel</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8042', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%B4%E8%99%90', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:51:13.896175Z', NULL, '2025-08-27T00:08:20.131366Z'),
(8043, 'vocabulary', '虐げる', '虐げる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>oppress</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to oppress</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to persecute</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8043', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%99%90%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:52:48.124497Z', NULL, '2025-08-27T00:08:20.131366Z'),
(8044, 'vocabulary', '諭す', '諭す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>admonish</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to admonish</vocabulary>.

諭す is used when a person (often in a position of authority) says something in an effort <vocabulary>to reason with</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to talk sense into</vocabulary> someone younger or lower in rank. There''s a sense of disapproval, but they''re not scolding — they''re gently trying to get them to understand the error of their ways. Think of a parent patiently lecturing a child or a teacher calmly admonishing a student, and you''ll get the gist.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8044', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%AD%E3%81%99', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:54:41.489073Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T00:49:17.981892Z'),
(8045, 'vocabulary', '教諭', '教諭', 'Who <kanji>admonish</kanji>es and <kanji>teach</kanji>es you? A <vocabulary>Teacher</vocabulary>. Probably not the nicest one though...

This word is more formal and specific than 先生 or 教師. It refers to licensed teachers who have permanent positions in schools, not temporary ones like supply teachers.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8045', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%99%E8%AB%AD', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T18:59:03.258227Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.268252Z'),
(8046, 'vocabulary', '軒並み', '軒並み', '<kanji>Line up</kanji> <kanji>house counters</kanji> and you''ll see that all the houses you''ve counted are the same <vocabulary>across the board</vocabulary>. It also turns them into a <vocabulary>row of houses</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8046', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%92%E4%B8%A6%E3%81%BF', 44, 1, '2015-07-02T19:01:41.174664Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.282227Z'),
(8047, 'vocabulary', '錦', '錦', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8047', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8C%A6', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:03:12.509199Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.307003Z'),
(8048, 'vocabulary', '錦鯉', '錦鯉', 'A <kanji>brocade</kanji> <kanji>carp</kanji> is a fancy way of saying it''s a carp that has a lot of pretty colors on it. It''s a <vocabulary>colored carp</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>colored koi</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8048', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8C%A6%E9%AF%89', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:05:15.820575Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.319963Z'),
(8049, 'vocabulary', '随時', '随時', 'If you have <kanji>all</kanji> the <kanji>time</kanji> in the world you can do whatever you want <vocabulary>at any time</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8049', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%8F%E6%99%82', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:06:12.055728Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.334457Z'),
(8050, 'vocabulary', '随所', '随所', '<kanji>All</kanji> <kanji>place</kanji>s are <vocabulary>everywhere</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8050', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%8F%E6%89%80', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:07:03.15709Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:18:00.076038Z'),
(8051, 'vocabulary', '随筆', '随筆', '<kanji>All</kanji> that comes out of a <kanji>writing brush</kanji> is <vocabulary>essay</vocabulary>s. You can''t paint or doodle, you have to write with it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8051', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%8F%E7%AD%86', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:07:52.045741Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.362694Z'),
(8052, 'vocabulary', '随分', '随分', 'If you manage to make <kanji>all</kanji> <kanji>part</kanji>s of something work together you worked <vocabulary>very</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>extremely</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>considerably</vocabulary> hard to do it. Doing all of anything is hard so lots of work had to happen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8052', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%8F%E5%88%86', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:08:42.134975Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.376046Z'),
(8053, 'vocabulary', '追随', '追随', 'Even if you <kanji>follow</kanji> <kanji>all</kanji> the people you''ve ever met, you''re still <vocabulary>follow</vocabulary>ing them. Once a follower, always a follower.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8053', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%BD%E9%9A%8F', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:09:38.508668Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.39083Z'),
(8054, 'vocabulary', '頂', '頂', 'The kanji means <kanji>summit</kanji> and this word means <vocabulary>summit</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>peak</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>top</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>crown</vocabulary>, most often in reference to mountains.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8054', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%82', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:11:48.866013Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T19:41:32.310705Z'),
(8055, 'vocabulary', '頂戴', '頂戴', '<kanji>Humbly</kanji> <kanji>receive</kanji> something and you''ll be <vocabulary>humbly receiving</vocabulary> it. Or <vocabulary>humbly eating</vocabulary> it if they gave you food and not a puppy or something inedible. It can also mean <vocabulary>humbly having</vocabulary>. The theme here is humility!

This is usually formal when written in kanji and used together with a verb like する. It can also be used alone to make very casual requests. In casual requests, it''s still written in kanji in novels and other creative writing, but is generally written in hiragana anywhere else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8055', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%82%E6%88%B4', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:13:03.434396Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T19:41:32.310705Z'),
(8056, 'vocabulary', '高騰', '高騰', '<kanji>Tall</kanji> <kanji>inflation</kanji> of prices means that there was a <vocabulary>sudden price jump</vocabulary> and it''s waaaay more expensive than it used to be.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8056', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E9%A8%B0', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:14:07.864785Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.449399Z'),
(8057, 'vocabulary', '暴騰', '暴騰', '<kanji>Violent</kanji> <kanji>inflation</kanji> causes a <vocabulary>sudden rise</vocabulary> in prices. It sucks. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8057', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%B4%E9%A8%B0', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:14:51.992201Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.461557Z'),
(8058, 'vocabulary', '急騰', '急騰', '<kanji>Sudden</kanji> <kanji>inflation</kanji> results in a <vocabulary>sudden rise</vocabulary> of prices, aka a sharp rise in prices.

急騰 is commonly used to refer to abrupt and unexpected rises in price or value, such as a <vocabulary>surge</vocabulary> in stock prices.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8058', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A5%E9%A8%B0', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:15:39.638062Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T22:31:22.224872Z'),
(8059, 'vocabulary', '鯉', '鯉', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8059', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AF%89', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:16:48.86526Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.501287Z'),
(8060, 'vocabulary', '緋鯉', '緋鯉', 'A <kanji>scarlet</kanji> colored <kanji>carp</kanji> is called a <vocabulary>red carp</vocabulary> and sometimes a <vocabulary>golden carp</vocabulary> because sometimes people don''t understand colors.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8060', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%8B%E9%AF%89', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:17:51.659484Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.513202Z'),
(8061, 'vocabulary', '真鯉', '真鯉', 'In <kanji>reality</kanji>, <kanji>carp</kanji> are usually <vocabulary>black carp</vocabulary>. The colored ones aren''t as common, people just wish they were.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8061', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%9F%E9%AF%89', 53, 1, '2015-07-02T19:18:58.272984Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T20:04:39.445204Z'),
(8062, 'vocabulary', '風呂場', '風呂場', 'Remember how you learned that 風呂 means "bath?" Well the <kanji>place</kanji> you take a bath in is the <vocabulary>bathroom</vocabulary>!

Note that this word means "bathroom" in the literal sense — it''s not a word for "restroom."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8062', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A2%A8%E5%91%82%E5%A0%B4', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T18:35:31.914803Z', NULL, '2025-07-01T19:41:45.398894Z'),
(8063, 'vocabulary', '風呂屋', '風呂屋', 'Remember how you learned that 風呂 means "bath?" Well the <kanji>store</kanji> you pay to take a bath in is the <vocabulary>public bathhouse</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8063', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A2%A8%E5%91%82%E5%B1%8B', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T18:43:35.205086Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:18:39.371876Z'),
(8064, 'vocabulary', '倫理学', '倫理学', 'You already learned that 倫理 means "ethics" so when you add <kanji>study</kanji> it means the <vocabulary>ethics</vocabulary> you study in school.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8064', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%AB%E7%90%86%E5%AD%A6', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T18:49:06.914004Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.560818Z'),
(8065, 'vocabulary', '倫理的', '倫理的', 'Things that are <kanji>ethic</kanji> <kanji>al</kanji> are <vocabulary>ethical</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8065', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%AB%E7%90%86%E7%9A%84', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T18:50:30.632488Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.574037Z'),
(8066, 'vocabulary', '偏り', '偏り', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8066', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%81%8F%E3%82%8A', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T18:52:03.91075Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.585692Z'),
(8067, 'vocabulary', '冠', '冠', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8067', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%A0', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T18:54:07.575207Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.597278Z'),
(8068, 'vocabulary', '王冠', '王冠', 'A <kanji>king</kanji>''s <kanji>cap</kanji> is his <vocabulary>crown</vocabulary>. It''s a hat only he can wear.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8068', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8E%8B%E5%86%A0', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T18:55:36.718845Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.608015Z'),
(8069, 'vocabulary', '殊勲', '殊勲', 'You do things <kanji>especially</kanji> worthy of <kanji>merit</kanji> and they''re called <vocabulary>meritorious deeds</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8069', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%8A%E5%8B%B2', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T18:58:25.999309Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:44:35.552963Z'),
(8070, 'vocabulary', '叙勲', '叙勲', '<kanji>Describe</kanji> someone''s <kanji>merit</kanji> at a ceremony for the <vocabulary>conferring of decorations</vocabulary>. That''s when medals are given out to people who deserve them. Usually in the military or something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8070', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%99%E5%8B%B2', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T19:00:12.131493Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.630979Z'),
(8071, 'vocabulary', '勲章', '勲章', 'To show that you had <kanji>merit</kanji> for a <kanji>chapter</kanji> of your life you will be given a <vocabulary>decoration</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>medal</vocabulary>. Especially if you''re a soldier or an athlete.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8071', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%B2%E7%AB%A0', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T19:04:01.614684Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.642331Z'),
(8072, 'vocabulary', '卸', '卸', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8072', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B8', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T19:05:39.228516Z', NULL, '2025-08-25T16:38:18.835259Z'),
(8073, 'vocabulary', '卸す', '卸す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>wholesale</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to sell wholesale</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8073', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B8%E3%81%99', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T19:07:00.904582Z', NULL, '2025-08-25T16:38:18.835259Z'),
(8074, 'vocabulary', '卸値', '卸値', 'When you sell something at <kanji>wholesale</kanji> <kanji>value</kanji> it''s being sold at a <vocabulary>wholesale price</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8074', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B8%E5%80%A4', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T19:09:17.482629Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:09:02.854992Z'),
(8075, 'vocabulary', '卸売', '卸売', '<kanji>Sell</kanji> something at the <kanji>wholesale</kanji> price and it''s... well it''s <vocabulary>wholesale</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8075', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B8%E5%A3%B2', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T19:10:26.356818Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T01:32:06.067577Z'),
(8076, 'vocabulary', '叙事詩', '叙事詩', 'If you <kanji>describe</kanji> a bunch of <kanji>occurrence</kanji>s in a <kanji>poem</kanji>, it''s probably an <vocabulary>epic poem</vocabulary>. This is for those super long, really descriptive poems you might be forced to read in school, like The Epic of Gilgamesh (ギルガメシュ叙事詩).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8076', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%99%E4%BA%8B%E8%A9%A9', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T19:12:37.326871Z', NULL, '2025-08-26T23:47:34.523008Z'),
(8077, 'vocabulary', '叙述', '叙述', 'When you <kanji>describe</kanji> an event by <kanji>mention</kanji>ing everything that happened, you must be <vocabulary>giving an account</vocabulary> of it.

叙述 refers to <vocabulary>narrating</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>describing</vocabulary> events in an orderly sequence. It usually suggests a specific writing technique associated with storytelling and narrative.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8077', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%99%E8%BF%B0', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:04:53.098344Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.710649Z'),
(8078, 'vocabulary', '叙情', '叙情', 'The best way to <kanji>describe</kanji> your <kanji>feeling</kanji>s is with <vocabulary>lyricism</vocabulary>. Making a clear <vocabulary>description of feelings</vocabulary> is difficult, but it will make you feel better.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8078', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%99%E6%83%85', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:09:03.929228Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.722991Z'),
(8079, 'vocabulary', '自叙伝', '自叙伝', '<kanji>Tell</kanji> everyone how you <kanji>describe</kanji> your<kanji>self</kanji> in your <vocabulary>autobiography</vocabulary>. It''s the only way they''ll be able to understand the real you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8079', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E5%8F%99%E4%BC%9D', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:11:09.536143Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.735242Z'),
(8080, 'vocabulary', '阿呆', '阿呆', '<kanji>Shock</kanji> someone by <kanji>flatter</kanji>ing them and they''ll end up looking like a <vocabulary>fool</vocabulary>. Taking compliments always turns people into silly <vocabulary>idiot</vocabulary>s. It''s okay, they''re just embarrassed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8080', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%98%BF%E5%91%86', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:13:57.730862Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.747563Z'),
(8081, 'vocabulary', '呆ける', '呆ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>shock</kanji>. What happens when you''re so old that your brain goes into shock? You start <vocabulary>to grow senile</vocabulary>. It''s sad, but lots of people can''t handle being old and they get like this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8081', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%86%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:16:39.846814Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.759213Z'),
(8082, 'vocabulary', '露呈', '露呈', '<kanji>Present</kanji> and <kanji>expose</kanji> wrongdoing to get proper <vocabulary>exposure</vocabulary>. The world needs <vocabulary>disclosure</vocabulary> and so do you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8082', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%B2%E5%91%88', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:17:59.126673Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.772164Z'),
(8083, 'vocabulary', '進呈', '進呈', '<kanji>Present</kanji> your findings in <kanji>advance</kanji> during an early <vocabulary>presentation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8083', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%B2%E5%91%88', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:20:45.835099Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.783321Z'),
(8084, 'vocabulary', '贈呈', '贈呈', '<kanji>Present</kanji> your <kanji>presents</kanji> at the <vocabulary>presentation</vocabulary>. Presents for everyone!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8084', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B4%88%E5%91%88', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:21:52.529445Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.79646Z'),
(8085, 'vocabulary', '哺育', '哺育', '<kanji>Nurse</kanji> and <kanji>raise</kanji> your baby by <vocabulary>nursing</vocabulary>. Milk helps babies grow up big and strong.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8085', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%93%BA%E8%82%B2', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:24:22.790919Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.809534Z'),
(8086, 'vocabulary', '噴き出す', '噴き出す', '噴 means <kanji>eruption</kanji>, and 出す can mean "to let out," right? Well if you let out an eruption, it''s probably going to cause something <vocabulary>to burst out</vocabulary>. You know, like lava out of a volcano. Or maybe it''s going to <vocabulary>to spout out</vocabulary>, like a geyser out of the earth. 

This may remind you of another word you''ve learned: 吹き出す. They''re very similar and often interchangeable, but in general 噴き出す is used for things that spout out with even more power and intensity. Along those lines, it can also be used figuratively, like when pent-up emotions burst out suddenly.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8086', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%99%B4%E3%81%8D%E5%87%BA%E3%81%99', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:25:46.033645Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T19:40:58.019821Z'),
(8087, 'vocabulary', '孤児院', '孤児院', 'You already learned that the word 孤児 means "orphan," so when you put a bunch of orphans in an <kanji>institution</kanji> it''s called an <vocabulary>orphanage</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8087', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AD%A4%E5%85%90%E9%99%A2', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:27:10.061576Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:05:09.185984Z'),
(8088, 'vocabulary', '尚', '尚', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8088', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%9A', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:28:48.54094Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:24:57.542175Z'),
(8089, 'vocabulary', '和尚', '和尚', 'Who works for <kanji>peace</kanji> and is held in high <kanji>esteem</kanji>? A <vocabulary>Buddhist high priest</vocabulary>. They work harder than anyone else to reach world peace so they''re held in very high esteem.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8089', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%92%8C%E5%B0%9A', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:33:42.63877Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.85684Z'),
(8090, 'vocabulary', '高尚', '高尚', 'You only hold someone in <kanji>high</kanji> <kanji>esteem</kanji> if they are really <vocabulary>high</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>noble</vocabulary> people. They have to be <vocabulary>refined</vocabulary> to be held so highly by you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8090', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E5%B0%9A', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:35:24.132144Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.868213Z'),
(8091, 'vocabulary', '時期尚早', '時期尚早', 'You already learned that the word 時期 means "time period," so when you add <kanji>furthermore</kanji> and <kanji>early</kanji> to that, it means that something happened ahead of time. It means <vocabulary>premature</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8091', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%82%E6%9C%9F%E5%B0%9A%E6%97%A9', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:37:00.912003Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:53:32.555199Z'),
(8092, 'vocabulary', '庶民', '庶民', '<kanji>Common</kanji> <kanji>peoples</kanji> make up the <vocabulary>masses</vocabulary>. They are the <vocabulary>common people</vocabulary> and no society is complete without them.

Depending on the context, 庶民 can take on a slightly negative connotation, especially if the 庶民 in question are contrasted with more elite or sophisticated groups.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8092', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%B6%E6%B0%91', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:38:06.188446Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.898519Z'),
(8093, 'vocabulary', '庶務', '庶務', '<kanji>Common</kanji> <kanji>task</kanji>s are a part of the <vocabulary>general affairs</vocabulary> of the people. That''s why they''re general, they''re the ones everyone is involved in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8093', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%B6%E5%8B%99', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:39:08.387761Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.911656Z'),
(8094, 'vocabulary', '怠ける', '怠ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>lazy</kanji> or <kanji>neglect</kanji>, and this verb means basically the same thing — <vocabulary>to slack off</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be lazy</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to neglect</vocabulary>.

怠ける is a word you use to describe someone who skips study time, avoids chores, or isn''t trying very hard at something — even though they should be. It can sound critical, but it isn''t nearly as heavy as words like 怠慢 or 怠惰, and it can even be used in a light or joking way sometimes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8094', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A0%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:40:27.175352Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:06:10.716947Z'),
(8095, 'vocabulary', '悠々', '悠々', '<kanji>Leisure</kanji> with more <kanji>leisure</kanji> added on? That''s a really <vocabulary>quiet</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>leisurely</vocabulary> life you''ve got going on!

Note that you need to add と to the end of this word to make it an adverb, and たる to make it an adjective!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8095', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%A0%E3%80%85', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:42:27.010742Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(8096, 'vocabulary', '悠久', '悠久', '<kanji>Leisure</kanji> that last for a <kanji>long time</kanji> ends up going on for all <vocabulary>eternity</vocabulary>. It never ends, and why should it? You''re soooo relaaaaaxed, you can''t possibly ever stop.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8096', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%A0%E4%B9%85', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:43:21.737471Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.946318Z'),
(8097, 'vocabulary', '悠長', '悠長', '<kanji>Long</kanji> <kanji>leisure</kanji> makes everything you do <vocabulary>leisurely</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>slow</vocabulary>. You''re able to perform tasks at a <vocabulary>deliberate</vocabulary> rate because nothing''s rushing you. And you''ve been like this for so long, why would anything try?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8097', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%A0%E9%95%B7', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:44:37.644949Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.95781Z'),
(8098, 'vocabulary', '愚', '愚', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Since this word is both a noun and a na-adjective, it can also mean things like <vocabulary>folly</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>foolishness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8098', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9A', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:47:43.447748Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.980887Z'),
(8099, 'vocabulary', '愚痴', '愚痴', 'A <kanji>foolish</kanji> and <kanji>stupid</kanji> person will make lots of <vocabulary>idle complaint</vocabulary>s. You really don''t like being around them because they <vocabulary>grumble</vocabulary> about how nothing is good enough all the time. But they really just don''t get it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8099', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9A%E7%97%B4', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:48:44.919512Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:33:59.994116Z'),
(8100, 'vocabulary', '傲慢', '傲慢', 'You are <kanji>proud</kanji> and bursting with <kanji>overconfidence</kanji>, so it naturally follows that you would be an <vocabulary>arrogant</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>haughty</vocabulary> person. <vocabulary>Pride</vocabulary> always comes before the fall, though, so… watch yourself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8100', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%B2%E6%85%A2', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T21:56:23.210266Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T00:55:31.617443Z'),
(8101, 'vocabulary', '誘拐', '誘拐', '<kanji>Invite</kanji> someone to <kanji>kidnap</kanji> you and you''ll have a <vocabulary>kidnapping</vocabulary> on your hands. That wasn''t very smart. You should try to be more careful.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8101', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AA%98%E6%8B%90', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:04:31.637459Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T00:02:14.740926Z'),
(8102, 'vocabulary', '杏', '杏', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8102', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%8F', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:06:17.79746Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.028086Z'),
(8103, 'vocabulary', '銀杏', '銀杏', 'If it looks like a <kanji>silver</kanji> <kanji>apricot</kanji>, it''s probably a <vocabulary>ginkgo</vocabulary>. Ever seen a <vocabulary>ginkgo nut</vocabulary> from a <vocabulary>ginkgo tree</vocabulary>? They really do look like little silver apricots. (Go ahead and google it if you don''t believe me.)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8103', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8A%80%E6%9D%8F', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:08:23.874055Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T09:52:41.789411Z'),
(8104, 'vocabulary', '栞', '栞', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8104', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%9E', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:11:17.587773Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.052956Z'),
(8105, 'vocabulary', '栽培', '栽培', '<kanji>Planting</kanji> seeds in land to <kanji>cultivate</kanji> crops is called <vocabulary>cultivation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8105', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%BD%E5%9F%B9', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:12:47.650175Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.064324Z'),
(8106, 'vocabulary', '盆栽', '盆栽', '<kanji>Tray</kanji> <kanji>planting</kanji> is <vocabulary>bonsai</vocabulary>. It makes sense because that''s exactly what you''re doing: planting a tree in a tray!

盆栽 refers both to the art of cultivating these miniature trees in small trays (or pots), as well as to those bonsai trees themselves. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8106', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%86%E6%A0%BD', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:13:39.165964Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.075167Z'),
(8107, 'vocabulary', '欄', '欄', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8107', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%84', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:14:31.791095Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.087384Z'),
(8108, 'vocabulary', '空欄', '空欄', 'A <kanji>space</kanji> as empty as the <kanji>sky</kanji> is a <vocabulary>blank space</vocabulary>. You''re probably supposed to fill that in with something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8108', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA%E6%AC%84', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:15:27.509833Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.098206Z'),
(8109, 'vocabulary', '欄干', '欄干', 'A <kanji>column</kanji> made of <kanji>dry</kanji> wood is turned into a <vocabulary>guardrail</vocabulary> to help people walk up and down stairs. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8109', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%84%E5%B9%B2', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:16:36.224798Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.108952Z'),
(8110, 'vocabulary', '欄外', '欄外', 'If you write <kanji>outside</kanji> the <kanji>column</kanji>s in your notebook paper you''re actually writing in the <vocabulary>margin</vocabulary>s. It''s a good space to put doodles.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8110', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%84%E5%A4%96', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:17:29.304214Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(8111, 'vocabulary', '殊に', '殊に', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8111', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%8A%E3%81%AB', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:18:32.952443Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:44:35.552963Z'),
(8112, 'vocabulary', '犠牲者', '犠牲者', 'You already learned that 犠牲 means "victim," so when you add <kanji>someone</kanji> to that, it still means <vocabulary>victim</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8112', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8A%A0%E7%89%B2%E8%80%85', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:20:13.575496Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(8113, 'vocabulary', '哺乳瓶', '哺乳瓶', 'A <kanji>milk</kanji> <kanji>bottle</kanji> that babies use to <kanji>nurse</kanji> is called a <vocabulary>baby bottle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8113', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%93%BA%E4%B9%B3%E7%93%B6', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:27:41.542933Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.156891Z'),
(8114, 'vocabulary', '過疎', '過疎', 'If the <kanji>neglect</kanji> of an area <kanji>surpass</kanji>es what was expected, then the result will be <vocabulary>depopulation</vocabulary>. No one wants to live in a neglected town, especially one that''s worse than what they were told.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8114', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8E%E7%96%8E', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:28:34.828056Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.168094Z'),
(8115, 'vocabulary', '空疎', '空疎', 'The <kanji>sky</kanji> has an air of <kanji>neglect</kanji> about it and makes it seems like everything you''re doing is in <vocabulary>vain</vocabulary>. Why even bother? The sky doesn''t even believe in you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8115', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA%E7%96%8E', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:30:08.855557Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.179544Z'),
(8116, 'vocabulary', '疎遠', '疎遠', 'If your <kanji>neglect</kanji> of family members goes too <kanji>far</kanji> you''ll end up with <vocabulary>estrangement</vocabulary> in your relationships with them. And they''ll probably <vocabulary>neglect</vocabulary> you as much as you neglect them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8116', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%8E%E9%81%A0', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:35:58.073263Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.192221Z'),
(8117, 'vocabulary', '疎開', '疎開', 'You’re looking for a <kanji>neglect</kanji>ed place that you can <kanji>open</kanji> up and use for <vocabulary>evacuation</vocabulary>.

While 避難 is a more general term for evacuating or seeking shelter during emergencies, 疎開 describes evacuating specifically from cities or crowded areas to safer places during disasters like earthquakes, tsunamis, etc.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8117', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%8E%E9%96%8B', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:37:29.437855Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.205225Z'),
(8118, 'vocabulary', '疎外', '疎外', '<kanji>Neglect</kanji> someone so much that you remove yourself from their inner circle and live <kanji>outside</kanji> of their life and it''s called <vocabulary>estrangement</vocabulary>. You might never see them again. But that''s okay with you, your relationship is too weird to fix now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8118', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%8E%E5%A4%96', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:40:04.503219Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(8119, 'vocabulary', '疎通', '疎通', 'If you never <kanji>neglect</kanji> to <kanji>pass through</kanji> your thoughts to another person the two of you will be able to have a special <vocabulary>mutual understanding</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8119', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%8E%E9%80%9A', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:41:09.47135Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.230021Z'),
(8120, 'vocabulary', '疎ら', '疎ら', 'The kanji and the word are almost exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8120', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%8E%E3%82%89', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:42:20.619788Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.242376Z'),
(8121, 'vocabulary', '疾患', '疾患', 'Something that you are <kanji>afflicted</kanji> with <kanji>rapid</kanji>ly is a <vocabulary>disease</vocabulary>. You should go to the doctor''s right away to treat this fast-acting ailment.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8121', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%BE%E6%82%A3', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T22:43:55.510981Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.253753Z'),
(8122, 'vocabulary', '疾走', '疾走', 'A <kanji>rapid</kanji> <kanji>run</kanji> is called a <vocabulary>sprint</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>dash</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8122', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%BE%E8%B5%B0', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T23:08:49.896662Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.265862Z'),
(8123, 'vocabulary', '疾風', '疾風', 'A <kanji>rapid</kanji> <kanji>wind</kanji> is a <vocabulary>gale</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>strong wind</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8123', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%BE%E9%A2%A8', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T23:21:10.206085Z', NULL, '2025-07-01T19:41:45.398894Z'),
(8124, 'vocabulary', '疾病', '疾病', 'A <kanji>rapid</kanji> <kanji>sick</kanji>ness is an <vocabulary>illness</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>disease</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8124', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%BE%E7%97%85', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T23:22:10.749323Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.289863Z'),
(8125, 'vocabulary', '音痴', '音痴', 'If you''re <kanji>stupid</kanji> when it comes to <kanji>sound</kanji>s, then you''re <vocabulary>tone deaf</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8125', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9F%B3%E7%97%B4', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T23:23:13.988516Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.301093Z'),
(8126, 'vocabulary', '痴呆', '痴呆', 'If you seem <kanji>stupid</kanji> and <kanji>shock</kanji>ed all the time you might have <vocabulary>dementia</vocabulary>. It makes it so everything is unfamiliar and confusing, so you can''t help looking silly and confused all the time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8126', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%B4%E5%91%86', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T23:23:58.067074Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.314475Z'),
(8127, 'vocabulary', '痴漢', '痴漢', 'A <kanji>stupid</kanji> <kanji>man</kanji> is a <vocabulary>groper</vocabulary>. I mean, not always. But it''s probably fair to call a groper or <vocabulary>molester</vocabulary> a stupid man.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8127', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%B4%E6%BC%A2', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T23:26:07.896927Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.32818Z'),
(8128, 'vocabulary', '無秩序', '無秩序', 'Remember how you learned that 秩序 means "order?" Well if you have <kanji>nothing</kanji> like that then it falls into <vocabulary>disorder</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>chaos</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8128', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E7%A7%A9%E5%BA%8F', 54, 1, '2015-07-06T23:28:06.117041Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.341867Z'),
(8129, 'vocabulary', '自粛', '自粛', 'If you''re <kanji>solemn</kanji> by your<kanji>self</kanji>, you have a lot of <vocabulary>self control</vocabulary>. You don''t need anyone to tell you to be that way, you have your own <vocabulary>self discipline</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8129', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E7%B2%9B', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T16:58:31.316895Z', NULL, '2025-08-27T00:10:59.544915Z'),
(8130, 'vocabulary', '厳粛', '厳粛', 'If everyone is acting <kanji>solemn</kanji> and <kanji>strict</kanji> then you should probably pay special attention to the <vocabulary>gravity</vocabulary> of the situation you''re in. Don''t start playing the trumpet when you''re supposed to be bowing your head!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8130', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%B3%E7%B2%9B', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:03:22.47455Z', NULL, '2025-08-27T00:10:59.544915Z'),
(8131, 'vocabulary', '静粛', '静粛', 'A <kanji>solemn</kanji> <kanji>quiet</kanji> person will always be <vocabulary>silent</vocabulary>. They never ever speak.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8131', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%99%E7%B2%9B', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:05:26.273091Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:08:32.29071Z'),
(8132, 'vocabulary', '兵糧', '兵糧', '<kanji>Provisions</kanji> that are only for <kanji>soldier</kanji>s are <vocabulary>army provisions</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8132', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%B5%E7%B3%A7', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:11:47.132187Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:54:26.999027Z'),
(8133, 'vocabulary', '紋', '紋', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8133', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%8B', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:28:18.126981Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.401379Z'),
(8134, 'vocabulary', '指紋', '指紋', 'The <kanji>family crest</kanji> on your <kanji>finger</kanji>? That''s just your <vocabulary>fingerprint</vocabulary>. It can be used to identify you, so it''s pretty much the same as your own personal family crest.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8134', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87%E7%B4%8B', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:37:02.194873Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.412255Z'),
(8135, 'vocabulary', '波紋', '波紋', 'Throw your <kanji>family crest</kanji> into the <kanji>wave</kanji>s and you''ll make lots of <vocabulary>ripple</vocabulary>s. There will probably also be <vocabulary>repercussions</vocabulary> with your family because those things are important and you really shouldn''t be tossing them into the sea''s waves.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8135', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%A2%E7%B4%8B', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:38:45.424584Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:11:56.943742Z'),
(8136, 'vocabulary', '紋章', '紋章', 'Look at a <kanji>chapter</kanji> on <kanji>family crest</kanji>s in that book and you''ll see every <vocabulary>crest</vocabulary> in your area. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8136', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%8B%E7%AB%A0', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:39:57.334135Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.434436Z'),
(8137, 'vocabulary', '膨らむ', '膨らむ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>swell</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to swell</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to expand</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8137', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%86%A8%E3%82%89%E3%82%80', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:41:07.755281Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.446156Z'),
(8138, 'vocabulary', '茎', '茎', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8138', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8C%8E', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:42:15.442533Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.45765Z'),
(8139, 'vocabulary', '茨城県', '茨城県', 'The <kanji>prefecture</kanji> famous for its ancient <kanji>thorn</kanji> <kanji>castle</kanji> is <vocabulary>Ibaraki prefecture</vocabulary>. I may or may not be making this up. Don''t fact check me, just look at that cool thorny castle in Ibaraki that I put in your head. Ooooh, how cool!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8139', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8C%A8%E5%9F%8E%E7%9C%8C', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:43:09.678812Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T11:15:27.171603Z'),
(8140, 'vocabulary', '山荘', '山荘', 'A <kanji>mountain</kanji> <kanji>villa</kanji> is a <vocabulary>mountain retreat</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8140', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%B1%E8%8D%98', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:50:46.799781Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.482402Z'),
(8141, 'vocabulary', '荘厳', '荘厳', 'A <kanji>villa</kanji> must be <kanji>strict</kanji> with its rules to maintain its <vocabulary>solemn</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>majestic</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>dignified</vocabulary> atmosphere. A lax, loosey-goosey villa isn''t going to impress anyone.

荘厳 is a relatively formal word often used in writing to describe something grand and dignified. You''ll see it used for things like historic cathedrals where the atmosphere feels solemn and reverent, as well as formal ceremonies like royal events. In music, it can describe symphonies or orchestral pieces that sound powerful, majestic and awe-inspiring.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8141', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8D%98%E5%8E%B3', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:55:54.170044Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:18:01.165967Z'),
(8142, 'vocabulary', '別荘', '別荘', 'A <kanji>villa</kanji> <kanji>separate</kanji> from your normal house is a <vocabulary>holiday house</vocabulary>. You go there when you''re sick of your normal house to relax.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8142', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%A5%E8%8D%98', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:57:34.057633Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.507921Z'),
(8143, 'vocabulary', '歌謡', '歌謡', '<kanji>Noh chanting</kanji> out a <kanji>song</kanji> makes it an even longer, more confusing <vocabulary>song</vocabulary>. But it''s still a song nonetheless!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8143', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%8C%E8%AC%A1', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:58:36.672505Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:03:31.014085Z'),
(8144, 'vocabulary', '童謡', '童謡', '<kanji>Noh chanting</kanji> for <kanji>juvenile</kanji>s are <vocabulary>nursery rhyme</vocabulary>s. Kids won''t understand the heavy stuff, so you have to dumb it down for them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8144', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%A5%E8%AC%A1', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T17:59:31.944989Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.530882Z'),
(8145, 'vocabulary', '民謡', '民謡', 'The <kanji>peoples</kanji> <kanji>Noh chanting</kanji> is <vocabulary>folk songs</vocabulary>. They''re the songs for the common people. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8145', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%91%E8%AC%A1', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T18:00:50.384184Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.542295Z'),
(8146, 'vocabulary', '貫き通す', '貫き通す', 'This is a combination of the words 貫く (to go through) and 通す (to let pass). Literally, it can describe piercing fully through an object. However, 貫き通す is usually used figuratively to mean <vocabulary>to stick to</vocabulary> something to the very end. Just like piercing an object from one side to the other, 貫き通す implies you push forward no matter what, sticking to your beliefs or determinations all the way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8146', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%AB%E3%81%8D%E9%80%9A%E3%81%99', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T18:03:11.038461Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T06:26:42.706771Z'),
(8147, 'vocabulary', '賠償金', '賠償金', '<kanji>Gold</kanji> you get as <kanji>compensation</kanji> for <kanji>reparation</kanji>s is called <vocabulary>reparations</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8147', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%A0%E5%84%9F%E9%87%91', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T18:18:20.530898Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.572183Z'),
(8148, 'vocabulary', '実践', '実践', 'To tell you the <kanji>truth</kanji>, <kanji>practice</kanji> is <vocabulary>practice</vocabulary>. You can''t just substitute something else for it. You''ll only make progress with real practice! You can do it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8148', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9F%E8%B7%B5', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T18:43:26.733573Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T01:27:56.076678Z'),
(8149, 'vocabulary', '逸話', '逸話', 'How do you <kanji>deviate</kanji> from a normal <kanji>talk</kanji>? With lots of <vocabulary>anecdote</vocabulary>s. If you add enough of them into your conversations no one will ever be able to keep track of what you were talking about to begin with.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8149', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%B8%E8%A9%B1', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T18:44:28.232594Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.594065Z'),
(8150, 'vocabulary', '逸品', '逸品', '<kanji>Deviate</kanji> from normal, everyday <kanji>product</kanji>s and buy lots of <vocabulary>gem</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>treasure</vocabulary>s! Especially if you''re buying presents. Normal stuff just isn''t going to cut it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8150', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%B8%E5%93%81', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T18:46:29.792812Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(8151, 'vocabulary', '逸脱', '逸脱', 'If you <kanji>deviate</kanji> from the plan and <kanji>escape</kanji> from it, that''s a serious <vocabulary>deviation</vocabulary> and could ruin everything! Stick to the plan! Stop deviating!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8151', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%B8%E8%84%B1', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T18:47:43.052586Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.617138Z'),
(8152, 'vocabulary', '秀逸', '秀逸', 'Do you <kanji>excel</kanji> when you <kanji>deviate</kanji> from the norm? Well, then you''re made for <vocabulary>excellence</vocabulary>, my friend!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8152', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%80%E9%80%B8', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T18:49:16.650728Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.674135Z'),
(8153, 'vocabulary', '逸らす', '逸らす', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>deviate</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to turn away</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to avert</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8153', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%B8%E3%82%89%E3%81%99', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T18:50:38.86792Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.687003Z'),
(8154, 'vocabulary', '最優遇', '最優遇', 'The <kanji>most</kanji> <kanji>superior</kanji> <kanji>treatment</kanji> is another way of saying it''s the <vocabulary>most favorable treatment</vocabulary> you''ve ever received. It''s when you get the <vocabulary>warmest reception</vocabulary> out of everyone around you. Like they think you''re the president or something. Either way, it''s pretty great!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8154', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%80%E5%84%AA%E9%81%87', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T18:54:31.988777Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.715923Z'),
(8155, 'vocabulary', '応酬', '応酬', '<kanji>Repay</kanji> what someone lent you in <kanji>response</kanji> to them already lending YOU money, and the two of you will have <vocabulary>exchange</vocabulary>d money. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8155', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%9C%E9%85%AC', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T18:55:40.787976Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.728274Z'),
(8156, 'vocabulary', '報酬', '報酬', '<kanji>Repay</kanji> people for the <kanji>news</kanji> they tell you by <vocabulary>reward</vocabulary>ing them! Everyone deserves <vocabulary>compensation</vocabulary> for the time they take to pass information to you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8156', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%B1%E9%85%AC', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T18:57:23.248318Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.744005Z'),
(8157, 'vocabulary', '酷', '酷', 'Something that''s <kanji>cruel</kanji> tends to be <vocabulary>harsh</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>severe</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>terrible</vocabulary>. And of course, it''s still <vocabulary>cruel</vocabulary> to boot.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8157', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%B7', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:22:42.124732Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.763371Z'),
(8158, 'vocabulary', '過酷', '過酷', 'Your boss <kanji>surpass</kanji>es his normal <kanji>cruel</kanji>ty and rains <vocabulary>severity</vocabulary> down on your head. You had other things to do this weekend and now you have to work through it? <vocabulary>Harsh</vocabulary>...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8158', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8E%E9%85%B7', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:23:44.43485Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.777537Z'),
(8159, 'vocabulary', '酷使', '酷使', 'If you <kanji>use</kanji> something in a <kanji>cruel</kanji> way it''s called <vocabulary>exploitation</vocabulary>. You''re probably going to <vocabulary>overuse</vocabulary> it, at that rate too. Cut it out, dude.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8159', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%B7%E4%BD%BF', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:24:39.401096Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.791579Z'),
(8160, 'vocabulary', '酷似', '酷似', 'If you <kanji>resemble</kanji> someone who''s <kanji>cruel</kanji>, you, well, you <vocabulary>resemble</vocabulary> them. Maybe you''re really mean too. I guess only you can know that. But you do look like that other guy...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8160', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%B7%E4%BC%BC', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:28:39.084326Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.80335Z'),
(8161, 'vocabulary', '酷い', '酷い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>cruel</kanji>? <vocabulary>Cruel</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>awful</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8161', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%B7%E3%81%84', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:30:05.116658Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.82957Z'),
(8162, 'vocabulary', '酷暑', '酷暑', '<kanji>Cruel</kanji> <kanji>hot weather</kanji> is the kind that''s too much for anyone to handle. It''s <vocabulary>intense heat</vocabulary>. Stranded in the desert in summer, heat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8162', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%B7%E6%9A%91', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:32:12.563472Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.844319Z'),
(8163, 'vocabulary', '残酷', '残酷', 'The <kanji>remainder</kanji> of <kanji>cruel</kanji>ness is <vocabulary>cruelty</vocabulary>. There always seems to be plenty to go around, and it kind of sucks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8163', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%8B%E9%85%B7', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:33:34.581035Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.85912Z'),
(8164, 'vocabulary', '冷酷', '冷酷', 'A <kanji>cruel</kanji> <kanji>cool</kanji> person is more <vocabulary>cruel</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>coldhearted</vocabulary> than just a normal cruel person. It''s probably because they''re cold all the time and it makes them mad. You should stay away from people like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8164', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%B7%E9%85%B7', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:34:33.256484Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.873278Z'),
(8165, 'vocabulary', '酷評', '酷評', '<kanji>Evaluate</kanji> in a <kanji>cruel</kanji> way so you can lay some <vocabulary>severe criticism</vocabulary> down on your competition! It''s not the nicest thing to do, and it''s not very constructive for them, but it will make you look better. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8165', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%B7%E8%A9%95', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:36:44.87969Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.887339Z'),
(8166, 'vocabulary', '鎌', '鎌', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8166', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8E%8C', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:38:06.254673Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.901296Z'),
(8167, 'vocabulary', '新陳代謝', '新陳代謝', 'Your body thinks you want to <kanji>substitute</kanji> <kanji>new</kanji> <kanji>exhibit</kanji>s inside yourself but it <kanji>apologize</kanji>s and replaces them more than you''d like. What it''s actually doing is controlling your <vocabulary>metabolism</vocabulary>. Picture your body using food you eat by setting it up in an exhibit in your stomach. But it gets bored of it and wants to substitute it with a newer one, so it makes you hungry again, digesting the food fast.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8167', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E9%99%B3%E4%BB%A3%E8%AC%9D', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:38:53.025288Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T04:17:56.558073Z'),
(8168, 'vocabulary', '陳列室', '陳列室', 'The <kanji>room</kanji> where you <kanji>exhibit</kanji> things in a <kanji>row</kanji> is a <vocabulary>showroom</vocabulary>. Maybe there are cars lined up, or boats, or tiny porcelain cats! Either way, they need a nice big showroom to show them off.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8168', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%B3%E5%88%97%E5%AE%A4', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:40:15.145197Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:57:32.391485Z'),
(8169, 'vocabulary', '必須条件', '必須条件', 'You already learned that 必須 can mean "essential" and that 条件 means "condition," so put them together and you get <vocabulary>essential condition</vocabulary>! That basically means that it''s a <vocabulary>must</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8169', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%85%E9%A0%88%E6%9D%A1%E4%BB%B6', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:41:06.838207Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:33:00.725847Z'),
(8170, 'vocabulary', '露顕', '露顕', 'You <kanji>expose</kanji> something new and it <kanji>appear</kanji>s in front of the whole world as your <vocabulary>discovery</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8170', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%B2%E9%A1%95', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:42:00.858229Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.956483Z'),
(8171, 'vocabulary', '顕在', '顕在', 'If it <kanji>appear</kanji>s to <kanji>exist</kanji>, then it''s probably an <vocabulary>actual</vocabulary> thing... you know, existing and stuff. That should be pretty <vocabulary>obvious</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8171', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A1%95%E5%9C%A8', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:42:54.060182Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:00.967867Z'),
(8172, 'vocabulary', '顕著', '顕著', 'When an <kanji>author</kanji> <kanji>appear</kanji>s in public it''s always <vocabulary>remarkable</vocabulary>. People usually don''t remember there''s anyone behind those pages, so when the actual author shows up in person it''s pretty <vocabulary>striking</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8172', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A1%95%E8%91%97', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:43:50.255633Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:51:50.70388Z'),
(8173, 'vocabulary', '顕彰', '顕彰', 'If you want someone''s awesomeness to <kanji>appear</kanji> <kanji>clear</kanji>ly to the world, you can start by <vocabulary>honoring</vocabulary> them. <vocabulary>Commending</vocabulary> someone in a public setting is a surefire way to let everyone know how great they are.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8173', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A1%95%E5%BD%B0', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:49:20.641914Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T21:43:08.91233Z'),
(8174, 'vocabulary', '鯨', '鯨', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8174', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AF%A8', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:50:18.010774Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:01.006721Z'),
(8175, 'vocabulary', '捕鯨', '捕鯨', 'If you <kanji>catch</kanji> a <kanji>whale</kanji> you''re <vocabulary>whaling</vocabulary>, and that''s illegal now so you should probably put the whale back.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8175', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%95%E9%AF%A8', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:51:42.974158Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:38:09.45677Z'),
(8176, 'vocabulary', '鯨肉', '鯨肉', '<kanji>Whale</kanji> <kanji>meat</kanji> is <vocabulary>whale meat</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8176', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AF%A8%E8%82%89', 54, 1, '2015-07-07T21:52:38.644804Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:01.07257Z'),
(8177, 'vocabulary', '之', '之', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Note that this is almost always written in kana in modern Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8177', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%8B', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:07:23.684908Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.415483Z'),
(8178, 'vocabulary', '起伏', '起伏', '<kanji>Wake up</kanji>, now <kanji>lay</kanji> yourself down, now wake up again, okay now lay again. What you''re doing is called <vocabulary>undulation</vocabulary>. If your emotions do it it''s called <vocabulary>ups and downs</vocabulary>. Everyone goes through stuff like this sometimes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8178', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%B7%E4%BC%8F', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:10:35.78174Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.431413Z'),
(8179, 'vocabulary', '伏せる', '伏せる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>bow</kanji> or <kanji>lay</kanji>, so the verb version is what when you try <vocabulary>to cover</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to hide</vocabulary> something. You''re doing the action to something, you''re not the one hiding.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8179', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%8F%E3%81%9B%E3%82%8B', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:12:08.930876Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.445681Z'),
(8180, 'vocabulary', '降伏', '降伏', '<kanji>Bow</kanji> as you <kanji>descend</kanji> into enemy territory. This is how you have to <vocabulary>surrender</vocabulary> if you want to live. Keep your head nice and low and descend into their ranks.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8180', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%8D%E4%BC%8F', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:13:39.290574Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.467425Z'),
(8181, 'vocabulary', '潜伏', '潜伏', '<kanji>Bow</kanji> your head to <kanji>conceal</kanji> yourself better than you were <vocabulary>conceal</vocabulary>ing yourself before. If you want to go into <vocabulary>hiding</vocabulary> you need to know how to lay low!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8181', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%9C%E4%BC%8F', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:31:56.994487Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T22:41:52.065519Z'),
(8182, 'vocabulary', '伏線', '伏線', 'Connect a bunch of things <kanji>lay</kanji>ing in a <kanji>line</kanji>. Something''s happening here. You can''t put your finger on it, but you''re pretty sure this is <vocabulary>foreshadowing</vocabulary> for something else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8182', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%8F%E7%B7%9A', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:33:01.49874Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:30:42.218788Z'),
(8183, 'vocabulary', '伏兵', '伏兵', 'A <kanji>soldier</kanji> only <kanji>lay</kanji>s herself down when she''s about to <vocabulary>ambush</vocabulary> the enemy! If you see some soldiers laying around, it''s not that they''re lazy, it''s because they''re getting ready to pounce! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8183', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%8F%E5%85%B5', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:36:03.678706Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:54:26.999027Z'),
(8184, 'vocabulary', '佳作', '佳作', '<kanji>Make</kanji> something <kanji>excellent</kanji> and everyone will call it a <vocabulary>fine work</vocabulary>. Or make something that sucks and let everyone down. It''s your call.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8184', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%B3%E4%BD%9C', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:37:11.778457Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.580757Z'),
(8185, 'vocabulary', '傍', '傍', 'Something <kanji>nearby</kanji> is <vocabulary>near</vocabulary> to you. It''s very <vocabulary>close</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8185', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%8D', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:38:20.11108Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.594885Z'),
(8186, 'vocabulary', '傍観', '傍観', '<kanji>Nearby</kanji> there is a beautiful <kanji>view</kanji>. <vocabulary>Look on</vocabulary> as the sun sets and the sky changes color. You would give up anything to be able to <vocabulary>spectate</vocabulary> this kind of thing all the time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8186', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%8D%E8%A6%B3', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:39:56.109055Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.60952Z'),
(8187, 'vocabulary', '傍受', '傍受', 'Only <kanji>accept</kanji> new people moving in <kanji>nearby</kanji> once you''ve finished properly <vocabulary>monitoring</vocabulary> them! <vocabulary>Tapping</vocabulary> into their phones will probably help too, but don''t tell anyone I told you to do that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8187', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%8D%E5%8F%97', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:40:59.409632Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:48.601574Z'),
(8188, 'vocabulary', '凝固', '凝固', 'Your pudding <kanji>congeal</kanji>ed and is all <kanji>hard</kanji>. How on earth did it <vocabulary>solidify</vocabulary> like this? It shouldn''t <vocabulary>freeze</vocabulary> unless you put it in the freezer. Unless, did you? Oh... ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8188', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%9D%E5%9B%BA', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:42:11.219287Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.635274Z'),
(8189, 'vocabulary', '凝視', '凝視', '<kanji>Freeze</kanji> and <kanji>look at</kanji> me. Your <vocabulary>stare</vocabulary> is giving me the creeps! You can move now. Hey. You can move now. Stop I said!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8189', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%9D%E8%A6%96', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:43:18.15945Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:26:59.946346Z'),
(8190, 'vocabulary', '凝る', '凝る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>congeal</kanji> or <kanji>freeze</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to grow stiff</vocabulary>. Remember 肩こり (shoulder stiffness)? This is where that こり part comes from.

凝る also means  <vocabulary>to be passionate about</vocabulary> something. Think of it this way: your passions are <kanji>freez</kanji>ing onto one thing, so you''re going <vocabulary>to be into</vocabulary> that thing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8190', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%9D%E3%82%8B', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:44:25.420191Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.66805Z'),
(8191, 'vocabulary', '奉仕', '奉仕', '<kanji>Dedicate</kanji> yourself to <kanji>doing</kanji> the best at school by having perfect <vocabulary>attendance</vocabulary>! That''s how you can show you''re really dedicated to learning.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8191', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%89%E4%BB%95', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:45:44.184012Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.681112Z'),
(8192, 'vocabulary', '信奉', '信奉', '<kanji>Dedicate</kanji> your life to what you <kanji>believe</kanji> in and you''ll have really strong <vocabulary>belief</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>faith</vocabulary> in whatever that thing is. Just try to dedicate yourself to something nice.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8192', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BF%A1%E5%A5%89', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:47:09.797243Z', NULL, '2025-11-16T19:02:11.093561Z'),
(8193, 'vocabulary', '奉公', '奉公', '<kanji>Dedicate</kanji> yourself to the <kanji>public</kanji> by doing community <vocabulary>service</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8193', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%89%E5%85%AC', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:48:18.507491Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.709207Z'),
(8194, 'vocabulary', '奉納', '奉納', 'If you <kanji>dedicate</kanji> your entire <kanji>supply</kanji> of food to the gods it''s called an <vocabulary>offering</vocabulary>, and it takes real <vocabulary>dedication</vocabulary> to do something like that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8194', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%89%E7%B4%8D', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:50:22.720206Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.725574Z'),
(8195, 'vocabulary', '尿', '尿', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8195', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%BF', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:51:24.730434Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.738503Z'),
(8196, 'vocabulary', '弥生', '弥生', '<kanji>Life</kanji> in Japan was <kanji>increasing</kanji> at its fastest rate during the <vocabulary>Yayoi Period</vocabulary>. It''s one of the earliest periods of history in Japan that we know about! You should look it up, if you don''t already know about it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8196', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%A5%E7%94%9F', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T16:55:30.064935Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.751764Z'),
(8197, 'vocabulary', '循環', '循環', '<kanji>Circulation</kanji> in a <kanji>loop</kanji> is still <vocabulary>circulation</vocabulary>. Or <vocabulary>rotation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8197', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%AA%E7%92%B0', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:07:13.307393Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.7659Z'),
(8198, 'vocabulary', '哀悼', '哀悼', '<kanji>Grieve</kanji> for a <kanji>pathetic</kanji> situation by offering your <vocabulary>condolence</vocabulary>s. Showing <vocabulary>regret</vocabulary> is the best way to handle it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8198', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%93%80%E6%82%BC', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:08:25.978315Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.779832Z'),
(8199, 'vocabulary', '追悼', '追悼', '<kanji>Follow</kanji> people who <kanji>grieve</kanji> by <vocabulary>mourning</vocabulary> with them. You don''t want to be the only one who doesn''t seem sad.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8199', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%BD%E6%82%BC', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:09:37.741888Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.792807Z'),
(8200, 'vocabulary', '惜しむ', '惜しむ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>frugal</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to be frugal</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be sparing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8200', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%9C%E3%81%97%E3%82%80', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:12:24.945713Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.806298Z'),
(8201, 'vocabulary', '惜敗', '惜敗', 'Your team was about to win the game but you decided to be <kanji>frugal</kanji> with your shots near the end and it resulted in <kanji>defeat</kanji>, making it a <vocabulary>loss of a close game</vocabulary>. If you had just taken those shots your team probably would have won. What a <vocabulary>regrettable defeat</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8201', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%9C%E6%95%97', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:14:35.49385Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.820082Z'),
(8202, 'vocabulary', '愉快', '愉快', 'A <kanji>pleasant</kanji> <kanji>pleasant</kanji> person is super <vocabulary>pleasant</vocabulary>! They''re <vocabulary>happy</vocabulary> and nice to everybody all the time!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8202', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%89%E5%BF%AB', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:15:53.81816Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.832287Z'),
(8203, 'vocabulary', '憂鬱', '憂鬱', 'With lots of <kanji>grief</kanji> and <kanji>gloom</kanji> in your life you''ll probably end up in a spiral of <vocabulary>depression</vocabulary>. Don''t worry, you''ll be okay. Things get better with time so don''t give into the <vocabulary>gloom</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8203', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%82%E9%AC%B1', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:17:21.193742Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.846662Z'),
(8204, 'vocabulary', '憂国', '憂国', 'Whenever something bad happens to your <kanji>country</kanji> you feel an exceptional amount of <kanji>grief</kanji> for your fellow country people. This is some serious <vocabulary>patriotic concern</vocabulary> you''ve got going on. Let''s hope you use those concerns for something good and don''t get too carried away.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8204', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%82%E5%9B%BD', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:19:36.611129Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.859349Z'),
(8205, 'vocabulary', '憂慮', '憂慮', 'All you can do is <kanji>consider</kanji> your <kanji>grief</kanji> and how it''s ruining your life. That''s called <vocabulary>anxiety</vocabulary>, and it can be a real problem. You end up being <vocabulary>concern</vocabulary>ed about everything, but don''t worry. There are ways to combat it. You''ll be okay!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8205', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%82%E6%85%AE', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:20:42.658456Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:51:52.975966Z'),
(8206, 'vocabulary', '憂える', '憂える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>grieve</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to grieve</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be anxious</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8206', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%82%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:21:57.98085Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.886184Z'),
(8207, 'vocabulary', '遺憾', '遺憾', 'If you <kanji>leave behind</kanji> something and it makes you feel <kanji>remorse</kanji> it''s a really <vocabulary>regrettable</vocabulary> situation. You should probably go back for whatever it was so you won''t lead an <vocabulary>unsatisfactory</vocabulary> life.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8207', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%BA%E6%86%BE', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:24:38.083286Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.897421Z'),
(8208, 'vocabulary', '披露宴', '披露宴', 'You already learned that 披露 means "announcement" or "display," so add a <kanji>banquet</kanji> to that and you''re at a <vocabulary>wedding reception</vocabulary>! Or if no one''s getting married but they''re showing off something at a big place with food and stuff it''s a just a normal <vocabulary>reception</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8208', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%AB%E9%9C%B2%E5%AE%B4', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:25:35.078002Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.911988Z'),
(8209, 'vocabulary', '一抹', '一抹', '<kanji>Erase</kanji> <kanji>one</kanji> part of your feelings and you''ll only feel <vocabulary>a little</vocabulary> bit of them. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8209', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%8A%B9', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:26:48.785676Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.933063Z'),
(8210, 'vocabulary', '抹殺', '抹殺', 'To <kanji>erase</kanji> or <kanji>kill</kanji> something results in total <vocabulary>obliteration</vocabulary>. This word refers to the <vocabulary>erasure</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>denial</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>ignoring</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>killing</vocabulary> of something''s very existence. It can even mean <vocabulary>murder</vocabulary> in some cases.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8210', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%B9%E6%AE%BA', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:27:58.917414Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:04.961352Z'),
(8211, 'vocabulary', '抹茶', '抹茶', '<kanji>Erase</kanji> your need for normal <kanji>tea</kanji> by drinking <vocabulary>matcha</vocabulary>. It is far superior to all others and you''ll never turn back once you''ve had a taste.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8211', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%B9%E8%8C%B6', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:35:47.251921Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:16:41.901026Z'),
(8212, 'vocabulary', '抹消', '抹消', 'If you want to <kanji>erase</kanji> and <kanji>extinguish</kanji> every lamp and every picture of a lamp in the world, just <vocabulary>erase</vocabulary> them all. Simple as that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8212', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%B9%E6%B6%88', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:36:54.969047Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.042846Z'),
(8213, 'vocabulary', '据え付ける', '据え付ける', 'This is a combination of the words 据える, meaning "to install," and 付ける, meaning "to attach." Put them together and you''re still going <vocabulary>to install</vocabulary> something or <vocabulary>to equip</vocabulary> something. There''s just more hands on work happening.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8213', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%AE%E3%81%88%E4%BB%98%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:38:12.856526Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:03:42.596356Z'),
(8214, 'vocabulary', '一旦', '一旦', 'You only have time to do <kanji>one</kanji> thing at <kanji>dawn</kanji> <vocabulary>once</vocabulary>. Dawn is really short so you can do whatever it is <vocabulary>for a moment</vocabulary>, but that''s it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8214', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%97%A6', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:39:21.034637Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.133268Z'),
(8215, 'vocabulary', '元旦', '元旦', 'The <kanji>origin</kanji> of <kanji>dawn</kanji> can be found on <vocabulary>New Year''s Day</vocabulary>. That''s when the new sun is born and rises above the earth for a whole year until it dies. And that''s the origin of dawn, children. Don''t let your teachers tell you otherwise.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8215', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%83%E6%97%A6', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:40:12.563015Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.145578Z'),
(8216, 'vocabulary', '繁昌', '繁昌', '<kanji>Overgrown</kanji> <kanji>prosperity</kanji> is still <vocabulary>prosperity</vocabulary>. Why, it''s even <vocabulary>thriving</vocabulary>. Nice!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8216', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B9%81%E6%98%8C', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:44:33.64834Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.157706Z'),
(8217, 'vocabulary', '素朴', '素朴', 'People made from <kanji>simple</kanji> <kanji>element</kanji>s are also pretty <vocabulary>simple</vocabulary>. And they can be <vocabulary>naive</vocabulary> too. But that''s just how they''re built.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8217', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%A0%E6%9C%B4', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:45:31.443964Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:38:20.342554Z'),
(8218, 'vocabulary', '栃木県', '栃木県', 'The <kanji>prefecture</kanji> with lots of <kanji>horse chestnut</kanji> <kanji>tree</kanji>s is <vocabulary>Tochigi prefecture</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8218', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%83%E6%9C%A8%E7%9C%8C', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:48:03.177241Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:25:07.757919Z'),
(8219, 'vocabulary', '栓', '栓', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8219', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%93', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:49:03.81255Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.212074Z'),
(8220, 'vocabulary', '栓抜き', '栓抜き', 'What do you use to <kanji>pull out</kanji> a <kanji>cork</kanji>? A <vocabulary>bottle opener</vocabulary>! Or a <vocabulary>corkscrew</vocabulary> if you''re feeling adventurous.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8220', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%93%E6%8A%9C%E3%81%8D', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:49:58.206421Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.22419Z'),
(8221, 'vocabulary', '洗浄剤', '洗浄剤', 'You already learned that the word 洗浄 means "washing." So when you''re washing the laundry you have to use just the right <kanji>dose</kanji> of <vocabulary>detergent</vocabulary> to make sure they wash properly. If you use too little detergent they''ll still smell, but if you use too much detergent the washer will explode with bubbles! Just be careful okay, don''t end up like me.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8221', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%97%E6%B5%84%E5%89%A4', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:50:43.500199Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(8222, 'vocabulary', '南瓜', '南瓜', 'In the <kanji>south</kanji>, a <kanji>melon</kanji> grows all lumpy and orange because of the hot sun, or sometimes orange and green. Wait, that''s not a melon, it''s a <vocabulary>pumpkin</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>squash</vocabulary>! 

南瓜 often refers to any kind of squash, including Halloween pumpkins. But in everyday Japanese, it usually means a kind of Japanese squash that''s green on the outside and orange inside, called <vocabulary>kabocha</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8222', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%97%E7%93%9C', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:51:58.008967Z', NULL, '2025-10-06T15:17:49.222453Z'),
(8223, 'vocabulary', '瓜実顔', '瓜実顔', 'If you ask me to tell the <kanji>truth</kanji> I''ll say your <kanji>face</kanji> is shaped like a <kanji>melon</kanji>. No no, it''s not a bad thing! You just have a really <vocabulary>oval face</vocabulary>!

瓜実顔 is an old-fashioned word that may still be used in literature to describe a slender, oval-shaped face, traditionally considered beautiful and elegant. The image comes from the smooth, slightly elongated oval shape of an 瓜 (melon or gourd) seed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8223', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%93%9C%E5%AE%9F%E9%A1%94', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:53:40.879553Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:38:30.733016Z'),
(8224, 'vocabulary', '治癒', '治癒', 'A <kanji>healing</kanji> <kanji>cure</kanji> will be great for <vocabulary>healing</vocabulary> sick people. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8224', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%BB%E7%99%92', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:55:21.056044Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.275368Z'),
(8225, 'vocabulary', '癒着', '癒着', 'What do you <kanji>wear</kanji> to <kanji>heal</kanji> a really serious injury? An <vocabulary>adhesion</vocabulary>. Okay, so you don''t actually wear these, but your body makes them for your insides to wear so you can heal. Nice job insides!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8225', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%92%E7%9D%80', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:56:28.70616Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(8226, 'vocabulary', '粗野', '粗野', 'Your home is out in the middle of a <kanji>coarse</kanji> looking <kanji>field</kanji>. Whenever you bring friends over the only word they say to describe your dirt and grass home is that it''s <vocabulary>rustic</vocabulary>. That''s pretty <vocabulary>rude</vocabulary> of them, you love your dirt home! Even if it is kinda small and stinky and in a field.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8226', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%97%E9%87%8E', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:57:30.326564Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.300824Z'),
(8227, 'vocabulary', '粗い', '粗い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>coarse</kanji>? <vocabulary>Coarse</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>rough</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8227', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%97%E3%81%84', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T17:59:19.719621Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.31269Z'),
(8228, 'vocabulary', '粗悪', '粗悪', 'If it''s <kanji>coarse</kanji> in a <kanji>bad</kanji> way it''s still <vocabulary>coarse</vocabulary> but it''s also <vocabulary>crude</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>inferior</vocabulary>. You don''t want a fuzzy sweater to be coarse, do you? Or a carpet? If they are, they''re this kind of coarse.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8228', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%97%E6%82%AA', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:00:14.530417Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.329198Z'),
(8229, 'vocabulary', '累計', '累計', '<kanji>Measure</kanji> how much you''ve <kanji>accumulate</kanji>d and you''ll have a <vocabulary>cumulative total</vocabulary>. That total includes all the things you''ve bought since the beginning of time. Probably.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8229', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%AF%E8%A8%88', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:01:42.160714Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.342958Z'),
(8230, 'vocabulary', '累進', '累進', '<kanji>Accumulate</kanji> enough of a good reputation at your company in <kanji>advance</kanji> and you''ll be able to have <vocabulary>successive promotion</vocabulary>s. You''ll be boss in no time! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8230', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%AF%E9%80%B2', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:03:03.201282Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.35734Z'),
(8231, 'vocabulary', '累積', '累積', '<kanji>Accumulate</kanji> and <kanji>accumulate</kanji> make <vocabulary>accumulation</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8231', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%AF%E7%A9%8D', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:04:13.486525Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.370976Z'),
(8232, 'vocabulary', '老舗', '老舗', 'An <kanji>elderly</kanji> <kanji>shop</kanji> is an <vocabulary>old shop</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8232', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%81%E8%88%97', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:05:10.667842Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.384179Z'),
(8233, 'vocabulary', '山葵', '山葵', 'If you dig up <kanji>hollyhock</kanji>s in the <kanji>mountain</kanji>s, and grind up their roots, you''ll have <vocabulary>wasabi</vocabulary>! Don''t eat too much, or you''ll burn your tongue off.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8233', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%B1%E8%91%B5', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:06:26.026189Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.403933Z'),
(8234, 'vocabulary', '捕虜', '捕虜', '<kanji>Catch</kanji> a <kanji>captive</kanji> and he''ll be a <vocabulary>prisoner of war</vocabulary>. Or, if you''re not in a war, just an ordinary <vocabulary>captive</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8234', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%95%E8%99%9C', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:07:37.334554Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:38:09.45677Z'),
(8235, 'vocabulary', '虜', '虜', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8235', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%99%9C', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:08:34.098367Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:53:58.880181Z'),
(8236, 'vocabulary', '該当', '該当', 'If <kanji>the above</kanji> is <kanji>correct</kanji> then please sign the <vocabulary>correspond</vocabulary>ing line to agree with it. And make sure you correspond in a timely manner.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8236', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%B2%E5%BD%93', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:09:43.318675Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.455772Z'),
(8237, 'vocabulary', '貴賓', '貴賓', 'A <kanji>valuable</kanji> <kanji>VIP</kanji> is a <vocabulary>noble visitor</vocabulary>. They''re probably a queen or a diplomat or something. So you better treat them really well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8237', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%B4%E8%B3%93', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:10:44.585283Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T06:27:32.779088Z'),
(8238, 'vocabulary', '国賓', '国賓', 'A <kanji>VIP</kanji> from another <kanji>country</kanji> is a <vocabulary>state guest</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8238', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%BD%E8%B3%93', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:11:36.009242Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.48041Z'),
(8239, 'vocabulary', '来賓', '来賓', 'A <kanji>VIP</kanji> <kanji>come</kanji>s to your door. They''re your <vocabulary>guest</vocabulary>! I hope you aren''t going to make them sleep in the couch.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8239', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%A5%E8%B3%93', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:12:42.767515Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(8240, 'vocabulary', '賓客', '賓客', 'A <kanji>VIP</kanji> <kanji>guest</kanji> is a <vocabulary>guest of honor</vocabulary>. They''re even more important than a normal guest.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8240', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%93%E5%AE%A2', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:13:32.857521Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.507829Z'),
(8241, 'vocabulary', '赴任', '赴任', '<kanji>Proceed</kanji> to a new place to do your <kanji>duty</kanji>. That''s just a fancy way to say you''re about to <vocabulary>move for a different job</vocabulary>. This is usually a job at the same company you''re already at, it''s just a different position for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8241', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%B4%E4%BB%BB', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:14:29.682355Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.519451Z'),
(8242, 'vocabulary', '旦那', '旦那', 'At <kanji>dawn</kanji> <kanji>what</kanji>''s the first thing you''re supposed to see? Your <vocabulary>master</vocabulary>. He wants you to wake him up and bring his slippers. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8242', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A6%E9%82%A3', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:19:07.081554Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.531995Z'),
(8243, 'vocabulary', '輪郭', '輪郭', 'Trace the <kanji>enclosure</kanji> of a <kanji>wheel</kanji> on paper and you''ll have drawn its <vocabulary>outline</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8243', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%AA%E9%83%AD', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:20:03.850597Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:05.555262Z'),
(8244, 'vocabulary', '鎮圧', '鎮圧', '<kanji>Tranquilize</kanji> <kanji>pressure</kanji> with medication that helps with its <vocabulary>suppression</vocabulary>. Then you won''t have to feel it anymore.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8244', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8E%AE%E5%9C%A7', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:20:55.719386Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:28:34.36878Z'),
(8245, 'vocabulary', '鎮魂', '鎮魂', 'To <kanji>tranquilize</kanji> <kanji>soul</kanji>s you have to a have a mass <vocabulary>repose of souls</vocabulary>. Then their souls will move on to the next life and stop hanging around here, haunting umbrellas.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8245', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8E%AE%E9%AD%82', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:22:12.062972Z', NULL, '2025-09-22T02:03:24.078817Z'),
(8246, 'vocabulary', '鎮める', '鎮める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>tranquilize</kanji>, so the verb version is what happens when you''ve already been able to tranquilize someone. It''s <vocabulary>to appease</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to suppress</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8246', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8E%AE%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:24:13.536466Z', NULL, '2025-09-22T02:03:24.078817Z'),
(8247, 'vocabulary', '重鎮', '重鎮', 'You’ll need a <kanji>heavy</kanji> dose to <kanji>tranquilize</kanji> yourself enough to be a <vocabulary>leader</vocabulary>. It can pretty stressful to be a leader so most of them are sedated. Tranquilize yourself too and you''ll be just like them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8247', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8D%E9%8E%AE', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:30:01.553399Z', NULL, '2025-09-22T02:03:24.078817Z'),
(8248, 'vocabulary', '鎮痛剤', '鎮痛剤', '<kanji>Tranquilize</kanji> your <kanji>pain</kanji> with a strong <kanji>dose</kanji> of <vocabulary>painkiller</vocabulary>s! Yum yum. Don''t take too many though!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8248', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8E%AE%E7%97%9B%E5%89%A4', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:43:21.821393Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:48:39.716764Z'),
(8249, 'vocabulary', '骨髄', '骨髄', '<kanji>Bone</kanji> <kanji>marrow</kanji> is <vocabulary>bone marrow</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8249', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AA%A8%E9%AB%84', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:47:53.170981Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:03.901306Z'),
(8250, 'vocabulary', '真髄', '真髄', 'Examine <kanji>reality</kanji> and extract its <kanji>marrow</kanji> — this will let you perceive its <vocabulary>essence</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>soul</vocabulary>. Ponder these things hard enough, and you may even discover the <vocabulary>true meaning</vocabulary> of life, the universe, and everything.

真髄 is a formal word used to describe the true essence or core of something — often with a bit of literary flair. It''s used when talking about the deepest meaning or most important part of an idea, skill, or philosophy. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8250', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%9F%E9%AB%84', 55, 1, '2015-07-08T18:52:36.500674Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:15:21.606143Z'),
(8251, 'vocabulary', '神髄', '神髄', 'A <kanji>god</kanji>''s bone <kanji>marrow</kanji> contains their <vocabulary>essence</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>soul</vocabulary>. Don''t go messing around with the bone marrow of gods trying to confirm this, though — you''ll probably end up getting smote.

神髄 is just another way of writing 真髄, so it shares its nuance of describing the deepest meaning or most important part of something. 真髄 actually started out as an incorrect spelling of 神髄, but got promoted to a legit alternative after enough people started using it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8251', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%9E%E9%AB%84', 56, 1, '2015-07-08T19:05:49.389523Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T06:35:40.345892Z'),
(8252, 'vocabulary', '脊髄', '脊髄', 'A person of great <kanji>stature</kanji> probably has a lot of <kanji>marrow</kanji> around their <vocabulary>spinal cord</vocabulary>. (Tall stature = longer spinal cord, after all.)

Be careful with this one — it actually has nothing to do with the bone marrow in your spine, and everything to do with your spinal cord. You know, that nerve superhighway protected by your spine''s vertebrae? That thing. They just called it marrow in the old days because it was something soft they found inside the spine. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8252', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%8A%E9%AB%84', 57, 1, '2015-07-08T19:13:57.155321Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T06:30:43.680591Z'),
(8253, 'vocabulary', '凛々しい', '凛々しい', 'A <kanji>cold</kanji> <kanji>cold</kanji> person is so cold that they''re super cool. They''re sooo cool and <vocabulary>gallant</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>manly</vocabulary>. Touch a manly person and feel how cold their skin is. It''s like that all the way down to their heart.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8253', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%9B%E3%80%85%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:04:05.348792Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(8254, 'vocabulary', '非凡', '非凡', 'It''s an <kanji>injustice</kanji> that so many people in this world are <kanji>mediocre</kanji> and we can''t all be a <vocabulary>prodigy</vocabulary> at something. Everyone deserves to be <vocabulary>unique</vocabulary>, but it just isn''t true.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8254', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%9E%E5%87%A1', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:10:22.006988Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.132568Z'),
(8255, 'vocabulary', '凡人', '凡人', 'A <kanji>mediocre</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is an <vocabulary>ordinary person</vocabulary>. There isn''t anything weird or interesting about them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8255', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%A1%E4%BA%BA', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:11:27.391524Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(8256, 'vocabulary', '平凡', '平凡', 'You''re so <kanji>mediocre</kanji> that you''re <kanji>flat</kanji>. That''s really <vocabulary>common</vocabulary>. Look at most of the midwest. It''s really <vocabulary>mediocre</vocabulary> and flat too. And there''s a whole lot of it out there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8256', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B3%E5%87%A1', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:12:53.101315Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.157917Z'),
(8257, 'vocabulary', '凡庸', '凡庸', '<kanji>Mediocre</kanji> and <kanji>commonplace</kanji> things are still <vocabulary>mediocre</vocabulary> and <kanji>commonplace</kanji>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8257', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%A1%E5%BA%B8', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:13:56.000981Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.170012Z'),
(8258, 'vocabulary', '匠', '匠', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

匠 means a skilled <vocabulary>artisan</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>craftsman</vocabulary>, someone who has exceptional skill in making things by hand. The word carries a strong sense of expertise and craftsmanship, often built through years of dedication. It''s commonly used for traditional craftspeople such as carpenters, swordsmiths, or potters, but it can also be used more broadly for anyone considered a "master" of their craft.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8258', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%A0', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:15:09.779893Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:42:03.461615Z'),
(8259, 'vocabulary', '意匠', '意匠', 'An <kanji>artisan</kanji> has an <kanji>idea</kanji> and makes a brand new <vocabulary>design</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8259', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%8F%E5%8C%A0', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:17:56.352492Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:29:41.941203Z'),
(8260, 'vocabulary', '師匠', '師匠', 'An <kanji>artisan</kanji> who''s good enough to be a <kanji>teacher</kanji> has to be a <vocabulary>master</vocabulary> of her craft.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8260', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%AB%E5%8C%A0', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:19:04.313821Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:29:41.941203Z'),
(8261, 'vocabulary', '巨匠', '巨匠', 'An <kanji>artisan</kanji> who is a <kanji>giant</kanji> in their field is a <vocabulary>master</vocabulary>! Everyone knows their name! Or, they''re just really large... like tall, so everyone calls them a master cause they don''t want to get crushed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8261', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A8%E5%8C%A0', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:20:00.38963Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:29:41.941203Z'),
(8262, 'vocabulary', '呆れる', '呆れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>shock</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to be shocked</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be amazed</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8262', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%86%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:21:48.672976Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.227386Z'),
(8263, 'vocabulary', '呉服', '呉服', 'If you want to <kanji>give</kanji> <kanji>clothes</kanji> to other people, you have to buy <vocabulary>draperies</vocabulary> first and then sew them together. That''s a fancy word for <vocabulary>cloth</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8263', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%89%E6%9C%8D', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:22:55.440904Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.239187Z'),
(8264, 'vocabulary', '呉越同舟', '呉越同舟', 'You <kanji>give</kanji> your rival information that will <kanji>go beyond</kanji> what you normally say and you realize the two of you are in the <kanji>same</kanji> <kanji>boat</kanji> on a lot of personal things. You are <vocabulary>bitter enemies in the same boat</vocabulary>! Or <vocabulary>classic rivals</vocabulary>, like you see in movies and read about. You never would have known if you hadn''t gone beyond what you normally give people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8264', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%89%E8%B6%8A%E5%90%8C%E8%88%9F', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:24:58.187861Z', NULL, '2025-07-10T06:22:20.985787Z'),
(8265, 'vocabulary', '嘉日', '嘉日', 'A <kanji>day</kanji> you hold in high <kanji>esteem</kanji> is an <vocabulary>auspicious day</vocabulary>. That''s a fancy way of saying it''s a really <vocabulary>lucky day</vocabulary>! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8265', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%98%89%E6%97%A5', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:26:08.151366Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.262714Z'),
(8266, 'vocabulary', '主宰', '主宰', 'The <kanji>master</kanji> <kanji>manager</kanji> rules over all of the other managers and he''s in charge of their <vocabulary>supervision</vocabulary>. If anything goes wrong it will be blamed on him! He has to keep a close watch on them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8266', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%BB%E5%AE%B0', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:27:02.241387Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.274888Z'),
(8267, 'vocabulary', '宰相', '宰相', 'The person everyone comes to a <kanji>mutual</kanji> agreement about to be the <kanji>manager</kanji> of the country is the <vocabulary>Prime Minister</vocabulary>.

Compared to 首相 or 総理大臣 that you''ve learned as "prime minister" previously, 宰相 were prime ministers appointed by monarchs. So this is a bit of a dated term used for the prime ministers of an imperial or royal court.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8267', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B0%E7%9B%B8', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:28:25.217334Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.287249Z'),
(8268, 'vocabulary', '寂しい', '寂しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>lonely</kanji>? It''s still <vocabulary>lonely</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8268', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%82%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:29:56.272978Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.300121Z'),
(8269, 'vocabulary', '静寂', '静寂', 'If you''re <kanji>lonely</kanji> and <kanji>quiet</kanji> you''ll be surrounded in <vocabulary>silence</vocabulary>.

静寂 is pretty advanced, so you''ll mostly only see it used in literature to suggest a sense of profound silence.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8269', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%99%E5%AF%82', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:30:48.068424Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:08:32.29071Z'),
(8270, 'vocabulary', '大尉', '大尉', 'The <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>military officer</kanji> is in charge of everyone else. He''s the <vocabulary>captain</vocabulary>. You can tell because he wears a really big captain hat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8270', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%B0%89', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:33:58.630729Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(8271, 'vocabulary', '少尉', '少尉', 'Someone who''s only been a <kanji>military officer</kanji> for a <kanji>few</kanji> years hasn''t been able to get very far in the ranks. They''re an <vocabulary>ensign</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>second lieutenant</vocabulary>. That''s still not a bad position to be in!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8271', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%91%E5%B0%89', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:35:10.983089Z', NULL, '2025-11-25T02:10:15.994971Z'),
(8272, 'vocabulary', '中尉', '中尉', 'A <kanji>military officer</kanji> who''s in the <kanji>middle</kanji> of their career has become a <vocabulary>first lieutenant</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8272', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E5%B0%89', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:36:05.503968Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.347646Z'),
(8273, 'vocabulary', '尚更', '尚更', 'Having a <kanji>furthermore</kanji> <kanji>renewal</kanji> means things become <vocabulary>all the more</vocabulary>... good, bad, cute, ugly, whatever it is you’re talking about! Or, on the opposite side of things, <vocabulary>still less</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8273', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%9A%E6%9B%B4', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:37:43.561071Z', NULL, '2026-04-04T20:17:45.612577Z'),
(8274, 'vocabulary', '尚且つ', '尚且つ', 'Someone keeps saying <kanji>furthermore</kanji> and <kanji>also</kanji> over and over again. Just when you think they''ve stopped, they turn around and say "<vocabulary>and yet</vocabulary>," before going back to repeating <vocabulary>furthermore</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>besides</vocabulary> again and again. It''s driving you crazy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8274', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B0%9A%E4%B8%94%E3%81%A4', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:38:56.948705Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.371922Z'),
(8275, 'vocabulary', '庶民的', '庶民的', 'Remember how 庶民 means "common people"? Well, anything that''s "common-people-<kanji>like</kanji>" is <vocabulary>ordinary</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>unpretentious</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>approachable</vocabulary>.

Unlike 庶民, which can sometimes have negative connotations, 庶民的 is usually positive. It''s used for things that appeal to ordinary people, like an unpretentious restaurant or a person with a familiar, approachable vibe.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8275', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%B6%E6%B0%91%E7%9A%84', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:41:21.71471Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.382874Z'),
(8276, 'vocabulary', '疲弊', '疲弊', 'If you''re <kanji>tired</kanji> by all the <kanji>evil</kanji> things in your life you might die of <vocabulary>exhaustion</vocabulary>!

While 疲労 and 疲れ mostly refer to physical or mental fatigue, 疲弊 describes being depleted and exhausted due to prolonged stress, overwork, or long-term difficulties. As a result, it can also be used to describe economic or financial exhaustion.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8276', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%B2%E5%BC%8A', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:43:08.920706Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.394333Z'),
(8277, 'vocabulary', '弊害', '弊害', 'The <kanji>evil</kanji> <kanji>damage</kanji> you do has an <vocabulary>evil effect</vocabulary> on people. All that negativity can have a really <vocabulary>harmful influence</vocabulary> on them. You should think before you do things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8277', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%8A%E5%AE%B3', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:45:53.449928Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:37:18.509805Z'),
(8278, 'vocabulary', '弦', '弦', 'The kanji and the word share meanings — in this case, the musical <vocabulary>string</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>bowstring</vocabulary> meanings!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8278', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%A6', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:48:04.65572Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.428465Z'),
(8279, 'vocabulary', '弦楽', '弦楽', 'Remember how 楽 was used in 音楽 (music)? Well this is that 楽 again. So this is <kanji>string</kanji> <kanji>music</kanji>, which of course means <vocabulary>string music</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8279', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%A6%E6%A5%BD', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:49:08.773255Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:47:05.786565Z'),
(8280, 'vocabulary', '怠い', '怠い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. The kanji itself means <kanji>lazy</kanji>, but this adjective is more for the kinds of feelings associated with laziness or inactivity. That''s why it means <vocabulary>feeling sluggish</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>lacking energy</vocabulary>.

怠い is how you feel when you''re sick, exhausted, or just can''t get moving. You can also use it figuratively for something <vocabulary>bothersome</vocabulary> or mentally draining — a wearisome argument, maybe, or an unfunny joke that makes you think "Ugh."

Note that this word is usually written in kana as だるい or ダルい, especially in casual writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8280', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A0%E3%81%84', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:50:19.06619Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:15:41.306519Z'),
(8281, 'vocabulary', '怠け者', '怠け者', 'A <kanji>lazy</kanji> <kanji>someone</kanji> is a <vocabulary>lazy person</vocabulary>.

怠け者 describes someone who habitually avoids effort. Note that when written in katakana as ナマケモノ, it usually refers to the animal. Because, well… Sloths move slowly and look kinda lazy, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8281', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%A0%E3%81%91%E8%80%85', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T17:52:02.206561Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:09:06.523459Z'),
(8282, 'vocabulary', '恭しい', '恭しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>respect</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>respectful</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8282', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%AD%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:06:27.521317Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.51676Z'),
(8283, 'vocabulary', '満悦', '満悦', 'You''re <kanji>full</kanji> of <kanji>delight</kanji>. It''s still <vocabulary>delight</vocabulary> though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8283', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BA%80%E6%82%A6', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:36:55.840832Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.540454Z'),
(8284, 'vocabulary', '愚か', '愚か', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8284', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9A%E3%81%8B', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:37:57.165635Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.559325Z'),
(8285, 'vocabulary', '〜拍', '〜拍', 'The kanji refers to a <kanji>beat</kanji> and the 〜 lets you know this is a counter. So this is used as a <vocabulary>counter for beats</vocabulary> for when you''re listening to or playing music.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8285', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%8B%8D', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:38:59.483968Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.574142Z'),
(8286, 'vocabulary', '拍手', '拍手', '<kanji>Hand</kanji>s <kanji>beat</kanji>ing together is actually <vocabulary>applause</vocabulary>. Though... you might be doing it wrong if you''re actually beating them together.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8286', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%8D%E6%89%8B', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:40:17.546781Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.592793Z'),
(8287, 'vocabulary', '脈拍', '脈拍', 'Your <kanji>vein</kanji>s carry the steady <kanji>beat</kanji>s of life. That rhythmic beat is your <vocabulary>pulse</vocabulary>. It''s always there, pulsing away… hopefully.

脈拍 is a technical word that refers specifically to your pulse rate. It''s what you feel when checking your pulse on your wrist or neck.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8287', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%88%E6%8B%8D', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:43:03.581882Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T16:43:15.460153Z'),
(8288, 'vocabulary', '搾る', '搾る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>squeeze</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to squeeze</vocabulary>. The way that this word differs from 絞る is that 絞る means "to wring out" and 搾る means to squeeze or <vocabulary>to juice</vocabulary> fruits/vegetables or <vocabulary>to milk</vocabulary> a cow.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8288', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%BE%E3%82%8B', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:44:14.557207Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.661215Z'),
(8289, 'vocabulary', '搾取', '搾取', '<kanji>Take</kanji> children from their homes and <kanji>squeeze</kanji> all their talent out until they have nothing left. That''s called <vocabulary>exploitation</vocabulary>, and lots of companies make money off of it. It''s not very nice though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8289', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%BE%E5%8F%96', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:45:26.887328Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.694897Z'),
(8290, 'vocabulary', '搾乳', '搾乳', '<kanji>Squeeze</kanji> an udder to get <kanji>milk</kanji>. Look at that, you''re <vocabulary>milking</vocabulary> a cow! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8290', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%90%BE%E4%B9%B3', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:46:48.421833Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.736058Z'),
(8291, 'vocabulary', '摂氏', '摂氏', 'Okay, so this is a stretch but stay with me! <kanji>In addition</kanji> to your <kanji>family name</kanji>, the first thing you need to learn as a child is how to understand <vocabulary>centigrade</vocabulary>. If you can''t tell what temperature it is outside, or in your house, or in your body to tell if you''re sick, you won''t do so well with this living thing. So when you think back to the first things you learned in addition to your own family name, think centigrade!

Of course, in reality, these kanji were simply chosen for their pronunciation rather than their meaning, but hopefully that mnemonic will help you remember which kanji are used here!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8291', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%91%82%E6%B0%8F', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:47:53.717386Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.778366Z'),
(8292, 'vocabulary', '摂理', '摂理', 'Some things happen outside of <kanji>reason</kanji>. They happen <kanji>in addition</kanji> to it. That''s called <vocabulary>divine providence</vocabulary>. You can''t explain it, but something outside of reason definitely influenced the things around you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8292', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%91%82%E7%90%86', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:49:00.230718Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.800932Z'),
(8293, 'vocabulary', '摂取', '摂取', 'Your body <kanji>take</kanji>s nutrients into itself <kanji>in addition</kanji> to all the extra crap you eat. It''s called your daily <vocabulary>intake</vocabulary>. And it gets all of those nutrients in your body through <vocabulary>absorption</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8293', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%91%82%E5%8F%96', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:50:59.464446Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.820062Z'),
(8294, 'vocabulary', '智', '智', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

智 means <vocabulary>wisdom</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>intelligence</vocabulary>, especially the kind that comes from good judgment and clear thinking. Rather than mere knowledge, it refers to a deeper <vocabulary>understanding</vocabulary> of what you know and how to apply it. On its own, it has a somewhat classical or literary feel, so you''ll mostly encounter it in more formal or traditional writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8294', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%BA', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:51:53.542696Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:35:02.417413Z'),
(8295, 'vocabulary', '柴', '柴', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8295', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%B4', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:53:13.142204Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.859218Z'),
(8296, 'vocabulary', '柴犬', '柴犬', 'The <kanji>brushwood</kanji> <kanji>dog</kanji> is the <vocabulary>Shiba Inu</vocabulary>. They''re the same pretty orange color as brushwood trees, so that''s how they got their name.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8296', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%B4%E7%8A%AC', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:54:06.242908Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.880107Z'),
(8297, 'vocabulary', '洪水', '洪水', 'A <kanji>flood</kanji> of <kanji>water</kanji> still makes a <vocabulary>flood</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8297', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%AA%E6%B0%B4', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:55:08.853389Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.902264Z'),
(8298, 'vocabulary', '猶予', '猶予', 'If you <kanji>still</kanji> need more time to finish a project and you tell whoever is in charge <kanji>beforehand</kanji> you''ll be able to get an <vocabulary>extension</vocabulary>. Then you''ll be able to finish it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8298', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8C%B6%E4%BA%88', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:56:24.68938Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.923235Z'),
(8299, 'vocabulary', '疎か', '疎か', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8299', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%8E%E3%81%8B', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:57:28.72221Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.945134Z'),
(8300, 'vocabulary', '方向音痴', '方向音痴', 'This is a combination of the words 方向 and 音痴. When your sense of direction is tone-deaf, it means you have <vocabulary>no sense of direction</vocabulary>. You get lost no matter how hard you try.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8300', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B9%E5%90%91%E9%9F%B3%E7%97%B4', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T18:58:51.979919Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.966168Z'),
(8301, 'vocabulary', '墓碑', '墓碑', 'Combine a <kanji>grave</kanji> and a <kanji>tombstone</kanji> and you make a <vocabulary>gravestone</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8301', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%93%E7%A2%91', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T19:02:05.458676Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:02.987867Z'),
(8302, 'vocabulary', '石碑', '石碑', 'A <kanji>tombstone</kanji> made of a big <kanji>stone</kanji> is used as a <vocabulary>stone monument</vocabulary>. These stones are usually so big that they''re used for multiple people who died together. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8302', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%B3%E7%A2%91', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T19:03:15.755323Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.030507Z'),
(8303, 'vocabulary', '碑文', '碑文', 'The <kanji>writing</kanji> on a <kanji>tombstone</kanji> makes up the <vocabulary>inscription</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>epitaph</vocabulary> the family members decided to put there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8303', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A2%91%E6%96%87', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T19:06:23.91873Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.046006Z'),
(8304, 'vocabulary', '記念碑', '記念碑', 'Some <kanji>tombstone</kanji>s are used to <kanji>write down</kanji> the <kanji>thought</kanji>s of dead people. They''re <vocabulary>monument</vocabulary>s. They can be really big or really small, but they have to be dedicated to somebody (usually a dead somebody).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8304', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A8%98%E5%BF%B5%E7%A2%91', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T19:14:37.733523Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.061049Z'),
(8305, 'vocabulary', '穀物', '穀物', '<kanji>Grain</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji>s are <vocabulary>grain</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>cereal</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8305', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%80%E7%89%A9', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T19:16:54.411002Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(8306, 'vocabulary', '穀類', '穀類', '<kanji>Grain</kanji>s are <kanji>type</kanji>s of <vocabulary>grains</vocabulary>! You thought they would be something else? Nope. Grains are pretty boring.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8306', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%80%E9%A1%9E', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T19:20:05.252867Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:32:59.293769Z'),
(8307, 'vocabulary', '米穀', '米穀', '<kanji>Rice</kanji> <kanji>grain</kanji>s make up the <vocabulary>rice</vocabulary> we eat everyday. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8307', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B1%B3%E7%A9%80', 56, 1, '2015-07-09T20:48:54.639986Z', NULL, '2026-01-19T08:15:51.228829Z'),
(8308, 'vocabulary', '窒息', '窒息', '<kanji>Suffocate</kanji> to the point that you have no <kanji>breath</kanji> left and you''ll die of <vocabulary>suffocation</vocabulary>. Chew your food and this shouldn''t happen!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8308', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AA%92%E6%81%AF', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T16:25:24.81037Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.121184Z'),
(8309, 'vocabulary', '窒素', '窒素', 'An <kanji>element</kanji> that can cause a person to <kanji>suffocate</kanji> is <vocabulary>nitrogen</vocabulary>. Too much or too little will cause it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8309', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AA%92%E7%B4%A0', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T16:33:50.792628Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:38:20.342554Z'),
(8310, 'vocabulary', '窮地', '窮地', '<kanji>Destitute</kanji> <kanji>land</kanji>s will be faced with a <vocabulary>dilemma</vocabulary>. Do they leave? Or do they try to make the best of it?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8310', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AA%AE%E5%9C%B0', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T16:49:04.104155Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.147519Z'),
(8311, 'vocabulary', '窮屈', '窮屈', 'Don''t <kanji>yield</kanji> to anything when you''re <kanji>destitute</kanji> because it''ll make things even more <vocabulary>tight</vocabulary>. Usually you have to be pretty <vocabulary>rigid</vocabulary> about what you can and cannot afford. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8311', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AA%AE%E5%B1%88', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T16:50:32.312247Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.16524Z'),
(8312, 'vocabulary', '窮乏', '窮乏', 'Towns <kanji>destitute</kanji> and <kanji>scarce</kanji> of food will end up in <vocabulary>poverty</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>destitution</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8312', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AA%AE%E4%B9%8F', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T16:54:59.946845Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.176902Z'),
(8313, 'vocabulary', '困窮', '困窮', 'You''ll be <kanji>distressed</kanji> and <kanji>destitute</kanji> if you''re living in <vocabulary>poverty</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8313', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%B0%E7%AA%AE', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T16:56:32.901983Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.188901Z'),
(8314, 'vocabulary', '窮状', '窮状', '<kanji>Destitute</kanji> <kanji>condition</kanji>s lead to <vocabulary>distress</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8314', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AA%AE%E7%8A%B6', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T16:59:24.419254Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.202244Z'),
(8315, 'vocabulary', '紳士', '紳士', '<kanji>Gentlemen</kanji> who are also <kanji>samurai</kanji> are still <vocabulary>gentlemen</vocabulary>. They just carry swords.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8315', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%B3%E5%A3%AB', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T17:08:27.976617Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.215543Z'),
(8316, 'vocabulary', '縛る', '縛る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>bind</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to tie</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to bind</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8316', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%9B%E3%82%8B', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T17:12:53.255951Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.229053Z'),
(8317, 'vocabulary', '束縛', '束縛', '<kanji>Bind</kanji> a <kanji>bundle</kanji> of sticks to a person as a type of <vocabulary>restraint</vocabulary>. They can''t get away when they''re tied to a bunch of sticks, can they? ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8317', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%9F%E7%B8%9B', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T17:28:21.299305Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.243674Z'),
(8318, 'vocabulary', '縫う', '縫う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>sew</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to sew</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to stitch</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8318', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%AB%E3%81%86', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T17:34:57.041597Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.25707Z'),
(8319, 'vocabulary', '裁縫', '裁縫', '<kanji>Sew</kanji> so well that people will <kanji>judge</kanji> you to be the best and you''ll be a champion at <vocabulary>sewing</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8319', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%81%E7%B8%AB', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T17:42:00.020677Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.270248Z'),
(8320, 'vocabulary', '縫製', '縫製', '<kanji>Sew</kanji> to <kanji>manufacture</kanji> things by <vocabulary>sewing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8320', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%AB%E8%A3%BD', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T17:44:42.5249Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.282337Z'),
(8321, 'vocabulary', '船舶', '船舶', 'A <kanji>boat</kanji> <kanji>ship</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>ship</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8321', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%B9%E8%88%B6', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T17:51:18.471289Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.296611Z'),
(8322, 'vocabulary', '舶来', '舶来', 'A <kanji>boat</kanji> that <kanji>come</kanji>s from somewhere else is bringing <vocabulary>imported</vocabulary> goods.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8322', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%B6%E6%9D%A5', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T18:01:53.39926Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(8323, 'vocabulary', '蝶', '蝶', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

蝶 is a formal way to say <vocabulary>butterfly</vocabulary>. You may come across this in literature or more technical writing, like insect field guides or biology textbooks. It''s also often used in compounds such as 蝶ネクタイ (bow tie) and 蝶結び (bow knot), or butterfly species names like 紋白蝶 (cabbage white butterfly).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8323', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9D%B6', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T18:08:11.894656Z', NULL, '2025-10-05T16:45:32.59557Z'),
(8324, 'vocabulary', '歌謡曲', '歌謡曲', 'You already learned that 歌謡 means "song," so when you add <kanji>music</kanji> to that it becomes a super <vocabulary>popular song</vocabulary>. It''s not just a song anymore. It''s in everything you hear!

More broadly, this can refer to a whole genre of Japanese pop music from the Showa era. In other words, it refers to <vocabulary>retro J-POP</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>Showa-era pop music</vocabulary>. The word <vocabulary>kayokyoku</vocabulary> is sometimes used for it in English too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8324', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%8C%E8%AC%A1%E6%9B%B2', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T18:13:42.036323Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:03:31.014085Z'),
(8325, 'vocabulary', '管轄', '管轄', 'Whoever holds the <kanji>pipe</kanji> has <kanji>control</kanji> of the situation so you give them <vocabulary>jurisdiction</vocabulary> to do whatever they want. It''s better than getting hit by a pipe.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8325', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AE%A1%E8%BD%84', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T18:38:44.194588Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:46:39.987396Z'),
(8326, 'vocabulary', '所轄', '所轄', 'The <kanji>place</kanji> you have <kanji>control</kanji> over is your <vocabulary>jurisdiction</vocabulary>.

所轄 commonly refers to the jurisdiction of a police station or other law enforcement agency, like 所轄の警察署 (the district police station) or 所轄の税務署 (the district tax office). It can also be used for government departments that have authority over certain tasks or regions, like 所轄官庁 (the authorities concerned), as well as within organizations to describe which department is responsible for specific tasks, like 所轄部門 (the responsible department).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8326', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%80%E8%BD%84', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T20:36:46.579397Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:46:39.987396Z'),
(8327, 'vocabulary', '直轄', '直轄', 'Remember how 直 can also mean "direct?" Well, if you have <kanji>direct</kanji> <kanji>control</kanji>, then you have… <vocabulary>direct control</vocabulary>. Doesn''t get much simpler than that.

直轄 is a formal word that is usually used when a central authority manages something directly, bypassing local or intermediate levels. For example, an institution under the direct control of the government can be called 政府の直轄機関.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8327', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B4%E8%BD%84', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T20:38:30.765941Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:46:39.987396Z'),
(8328, 'vocabulary', '逸れる', '逸れる', 'This is the intransitive form of 逸らす (to turn away), meaning it doesn''t take a direct object. 逸れる is like when something turns off from the normal course of things, so it means <vocabulary>to stray</vocabulary>. A conversation straying from the main topic, a car that strays from the main road, or your focus straying in a moment of distraction — all of these work with 逸れる.

In other situations, it can mean <vocabulary>to get separated</vocabulary>, like when you get separated from your friends in a crowd.

To remember this is intransitive, just imagine that it''s rare (れる) for you to stray and wander off, whether on the road or in conversation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8328', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%B8%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T20:39:37.92743Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.38824Z'),
(8329, 'vocabulary', '遥か', '遥か', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8329', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%A5%E3%81%8B', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T20:50:46.637536Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:37:07.832071Z'),
(8330, 'vocabulary', '無報酬', '無報酬', 'You already learned that 報酬 means "compensation," so adding <kanji>not</kanji> to it means the person working isn''t getting paid, aka working <vocabulary>without pay</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8330', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E5%A0%B1%E9%85%AC', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T21:01:18.014456Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.413906Z'),
(8331, 'vocabulary', '錯誤', '錯誤', 'If you''re <kanji>confused</kanji> and you make a <kanji>mistake</kanji>, no matter how much you didn''t mean it, it''s still a <vocabulary>mistake</vocabulary>. You should ask for help next time you don''t know what you''re doing and you won''t make these <vocabulary>error</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8331', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8C%AF%E8%AA%A4', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T21:07:20.277083Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.426595Z'),
(8332, 'vocabulary', '錯覚', '錯覚', 'If you feel <kanji>confused</kanji> while <kanji>awake</kanji>, it may be because you''re experiencing an <vocabulary>illusion</vocabulary>.

錯覚 is what happens when your brain is <vocabulary>misperceiving</vocabulary> reality in some way, like when it misinterprets experiences, information, or sensory stimuli.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8332', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8C%AF%E8%A6%9A', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T21:08:28.55936Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:23:41.86509Z'),
(8333, 'vocabulary', '錯乱', '錯乱', 'A <kanji>confused</kanji> <kanji>riot</kanji> causes even more <vocabulary>confusion</vocabulary>. Soon there will be so many <vocabulary>distraction</vocabulary>s happening, what with cars tipping over and fires starting, that no one will know which way is up!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8333', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8C%AF%E4%B9%B1', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T21:09:54.274468Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.451915Z'),
(8334, 'vocabulary', '交錯', '交錯', 'You <kanji>mix</kanji> a bunch of ingredients into a <kanji>confused</kanji> <vocabulary>mixture</vocabulary>. That''s going to be one weird cake, or whatever the mixture''s for.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8334', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%A4%E9%8C%AF', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T21:11:37.262207Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.46567Z'),
(8335, 'vocabulary', '倒錯', '倒錯', 'When your <kanji>confused</kanji> urges <kanji>overthrow</kanji> normal thoughts and feelings it''s a <vocabulary>perversion</vocabulary>. Some are harmless, but some are not.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8335', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%92%E9%8C%AF', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T21:13:24.315012Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:29.463329Z'),
(8336, 'vocabulary', '丘陵', '丘陵', 'Just because there''s a <kanji>mausoleum</kanji> on this <kanji>hill</kanji> doesn''t mean it isn''t still a <vocabulary>hill</vocabulary>. (It is. It''s still a hill.)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8336', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%98%E9%99%B5', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T21:14:33.127075Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.495132Z'),
(8337, 'vocabulary', '靖国神社', '靖国神社', 'You already know that 神社 means "shinto shrine," so add <kanji>peaceful</kanji> and <kanji>country</kanji> to it and you get a shrine that calls for the peace of the entire country. It''s Yasukuni Shrine. If you watch the news you know that this name and everything it stands for is always a <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yasukuni_Shrine" target="_blank">topic of current debates</a>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8337', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%96%E5%9B%BD%E7%A5%9E%E7%A4%BE', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T21:15:30.376156Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.508321Z'),
(8338, 'vocabulary', '飽和', '飽和', 'If you''re so <kanji>bored</kanji> of <kanji>peace</kanji> because all there IS is peace, it''s called <vocabulary>saturation</vocabulary> of the peace market. You''ve pretty much been soaked through with peace and now you want a little chaotic excitement!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8338', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%BD%E5%92%8C', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T21:16:38.444398Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.521593Z'),
(8339, 'vocabulary', '飽きる', '飽きる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>bored</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to get tired of</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to lose interest in</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8339', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%BD%E3%81%8D%E3%82%8B', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T21:18:14.494836Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.534457Z'),
(8340, 'vocabulary', '飽食', '飽食', 'You''re <kanji>bored</kanji> but you keep <kanji>eating</kanji>. Maybe you''re eating because you''re bored. If you''re bored all the time, eventually your main goal in life becomes <vocabulary>satiation</vocabulary>. 

In modern Japanese, this word crops up most in 飽食の時代, which could be roughly translated as "the age of plenty". 飽食の時代 refers to the modern era in countries like Japan, which are characterized by an abundance of food, in contrast to the past, when food was scarce.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8340', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%BD%E9%A3%9F', 56, 1, '2015-07-10T21:19:13.452858Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.548175Z'),
(8341, 'vocabulary', '熊之実', '熊之実', 'I want you to try to <kanji>bear</kanji> <kanji>this</kanji> <kanji>truth</kanji> and that''s these kanji make up the word <vocabulary>clownfish</vocabulary>! Do you think a clownfish is this bear? I''m telling the truth! Maybe. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8341', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%86%8A%E4%B9%8B%E5%AE%9F', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:36:34.689386Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T05:58:29.379374Z'),
(8342, 'vocabulary', '乙', '乙', 'If something is the <kanji>latter</kanji>, it can''t be first, it has to be at least <vocabulary>second</vocabulary>. This works for lists too. So if you have A through D, it''s going to be <vocabulary>B</vocabulary>.

This word is also used as slang for おつかれさま, which is where the <vocabulary>thanks</vocabulary> meaning comes from. It can also be used as a prefix to mean <vocabulary>chic</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8342', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%99', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:38:59.628499Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.588854Z'),
(8343, 'vocabulary', '甲乙', '甲乙', '<kanji>A</kanji> and <kanji>B</kanji> make <vocabulary>A and B</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8343', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B2%E4%B9%99', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:39:58.186446Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.601199Z'),
(8344, 'vocabulary', '間伐', '間伐', '<kanji>Fell</kanji> trees in <kanji>interval</kanji>s, instead of all at once, and it''s called <vocabulary>tree thinning</vocabulary>. That way there''s still some forest there, but you get your wood to use for other things too. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8344', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%93%E4%BC%90', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:41:03.198428Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T19:45:25.966494Z'),
(8345, 'vocabulary', '伐採', '伐採', '<kanji>Fell</kanji> trees and <kanji>gather</kanji> them up. That''s called <vocabulary>lumbering</vocabulary>. Or if you cut them all down and take every last one, it''s <vocabulary>deforestation</vocabulary> and you should be ashamed!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8345', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%90%E6%8E%A1', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:42:09.713563Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:46:31.228624Z'),
(8346, 'vocabulary', '減俸', '減俸', 'Your boss says she''s going to <kanji>decrease</kanji> your <kanji>salary</kanji> with a company-wide <vocabulary>salary reduction</vocabulary>. You should probably start looking for a new job that won''t do <vocabulary>salary cut</vocabulary>s like this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8346', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%9B%E4%BF%B8', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:43:05.763245Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:58:02.806385Z'),
(8347, 'vocabulary', '年俸', '年俸', 'The <kanji>salary</kanji> you get each <kanji>year</kanji> is an <vocabulary>annual salary</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8347', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E4%BF%B8', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:44:05.008643Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.651984Z'),
(8348, 'vocabulary', '傍ら', '傍ら', 'The kanji 傍 literally means <kanji>nearby</kanji> or <kanji>side</kanji>, but here in 傍ら it''s being used figuratively, which is why this means <vocabulary>while</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>alongside</vocabulary>. 

傍ら can mean <vocabulary>beside</vocabulary> in a physical sense, but you''ll more often see it in writing to show someone doing two things at once, like 仕事の傍ら勉強している (studying while working). Basically, it''s like you''re doing another task or activity <em>beside</em> your main one.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8348', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%8D%E3%82%89', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:45:18.786979Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:29.52273Z'),
(8349, 'vocabulary', '凸版', '凸版', 'Press <kanji>uneven</kanji> carvings into paper to make the newest <kanji>edition</kanji> of your magazine. What you''re using is a <vocabulary>letterpress</vocabulary>. This made mass printing nice and cheap(ish) in Japan!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8349', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%B8%E7%89%88', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:48:06.239241Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:11:10.895336Z'),
(8350, 'vocabulary', '凹凸', '凹凸', 'A surface covered in <kanji>convex</kanji> and <kanji>concave</kanji> parts is going to be really <vocabulary>uneven</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>rough</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8350', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%B9%E5%87%B8', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:52:43.999545Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.702791Z'),
(8351, 'vocabulary', '恐喝', '恐喝', 'A man in a leather jacket <kanji>scold</kanji>s you and is using <kanji>fear</kanji> tactics to get paid. He''s <vocabulary>blackmailing</vocabulary> you! That''s illegal. Come to think of it, he''s probably a member of the yakuza. You should move.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8351', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%90%E5%96%9D', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T17:59:00.043502Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.716103Z'),
(8352, 'vocabulary', '坪', '坪', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8352', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9D%AA', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:09:00.764761Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:24:18.855236Z'),
(8353, 'vocabulary', '堕落', '堕落', 'A <kanji>degradation</kanji> <kanji>fall</kanji> is a fall into <vocabulary>depravity</vocabulary>. Meaning you''ll probably lead a life of <vocabulary>degradation</vocabulary>. Stealing, stabbing, money laundering, various excesses… That kind of depravity.

堕落 refers to a state of falling into a bad or immoral condition, whether it''s about a person''s character or a society and its values.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8353', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%95%E8%90%BD', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:25:33.978397Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T17:44:11.58005Z'),
(8354, 'vocabulary', '奉行', '奉行', 'If you can <kanji>dedicate</kanji> yourself to <kanji>go</kanji>ing to a bunch of meetings and dealing with everyone''s problems you''ll make a good <vocabulary>magistrate</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8354', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%89%E8%A1%8C', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:28:12.643941Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.767591Z'),
(8355, 'vocabulary', '海峡', '海峡', 'A <kanji>ravine</kanji> in the water connecting two <kanji>sea</kanji>s is a <vocabulary>channel</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>strait</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8355', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B7%E5%B3%A1', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:29:54.143806Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.779656Z'),
(8356, 'vocabulary', '峡谷', '峡谷', 'A <kanji>ravine</kanji> in a <kanji>valley</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>ravine</vocabulary>. It can also be a <vocabulary>gorge</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>canyon</vocabulary>. They''re usually really big. Like the Grand Canyon!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8356', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B3%A1%E8%B0%B7', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:30:47.483705Z', NULL, '2026-03-12T07:45:30.847135Z'),
(8357, 'vocabulary', '弔意', '弔意', 'Express your unique <kanji>idea</kanji> of <kanji>condolence</kanji> by offering your <vocabulary>condolence</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>sympathy</vocabulary> to people who are going through hard times.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8357', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%94%E6%84%8F', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:32:04.166258Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.805543Z'),
(8358, 'vocabulary', '弔辞', '弔辞', 'When someone <kanji>quit</kanji>s living you need to offer your <kanji>condolence</kanji>s during their <vocabulary>memorial address</vocabulary>. Let it all out and tell everyone what you thought about that dead guy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8358', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%94%E8%BE%9E', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:32:52.308718Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.818155Z'),
(8359, 'vocabulary', '弔う', '弔う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>condolence</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to mourn for</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to condole</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8359', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%94%E3%81%86', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:34:01.96367Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T17:45:53.77165Z'),
(8360, 'vocabulary', '悪循環', '悪循環', 'You already know that 循環 means "circulation" or "cycle," so adding <kanji>bad</kanji> to that makes it a <vocabulary>vicious cycle</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8360', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E5%BE%AA%E7%92%B0', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:35:25.516898Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.847608Z'),
(8361, 'vocabulary', '循環器', '循環器', 'You already know that 循環 means "circulation," so adding <kanji>container</kanji> makes it what holds things that circulate. They''re your <vocabulary>circulatory organ</vocabulary>s. They contain all the blood moving throughout your body like a container. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8361', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%AA%E7%92%B0%E5%99%A8', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:43:16.128513Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.862786Z'),
(8362, 'vocabulary', '惜しい', '惜しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>frugal</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>regrettable</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>precious</vocabulary>. If you''re too frugal to buy something your actions are regrettable later when you realize you wanted that thing. If you''re so frugal you only buy one single thing, it''s very precious to you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8362', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%9C%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:46:23.576103Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.876757Z'),
(8363, 'vocabulary', '不愉快', '不愉快', 'You already learned that 愉快 means "pleasant" or "happy," so add <kanji>not</kanji> to that and you''re <vocabulary>unpleasant</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>unhappy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8363', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%84%89%E5%BF%AB', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:52:56.471748Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.891491Z'),
(8364, 'vocabulary', '憂い', '憂い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, but wait! It''s not an adjective, it''s just a noun (tricky, tricky). The kanji means <kanji>grief</kanji>, so this word means <vocabulary>grief</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>distress</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8364', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%82%E3%81%84', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:55:30.56175Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.905486Z'),
(8365, 'vocabulary', '果敢', '果敢', 'A <kanji>daring</kanji> <kanji>fruit</kanji> is very <vocabulary>resolute</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>bold</vocabulary>! She''s going to go out and be a fruit superhero or scientist or something. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8365', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9E%9C%E6%95%A2', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:57:25.663127Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.919419Z'),
(8366, 'vocabulary', '敢えて', '敢えて', 'This is the adverb version of the kanji <kanji>daring</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>dare to</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>daringly</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8366', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%A2%E3%81%88%E3%81%A6', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:58:24.138017Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.933274Z'),
(8367, 'vocabulary', '敢行', '敢行', 'You use your <kanji>daring</kanji> and just <kanji>go</kanji>! You made a <vocabulary>decisive action</vocabulary> by just going like that! It was go or not go and you chose go!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8367', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%A2%E8%A1%8C', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T18:59:53.673013Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.94821Z'),
(8368, 'vocabulary', '敢然', '敢然', 'If you have a <kanji>daring</kanji> <kanji>nature</kanji> you''ll <vocabulary>boldly</vocabulary> go where no one else has gone before!

Note that you need to add と to the end of this word to make it an adverb, and たる to make it an adjective!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8368', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%A2%E7%84%B6', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T19:01:21.232019Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.963749Z'),
(8369, 'vocabulary', '敢闘', '敢闘', 'If you''re involved in a <kanji>daring</kanji> <kanji>fight</kanji>, you''re probably <vocabulary>fighting bravely</vocabulary>. You could get hurt, or worse, but you don''t even care! Talk about fighting bravely… And good luck with that.

敢闘 is a very formal word often used to praise someone who gave it their all — especially in competitions — even if they didn''t win. You might see it in phrases like 敢闘賞 (Fighting Spirit Award) at school sports festivals and sumo tournaments.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8369', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%A2%E9%97%98', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T19:03:11.167667Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.977307Z'),
(8370, 'vocabulary', '勇敢', '勇敢', 'You have <kanji>courage</kanji> and <kanji>daring</kanji>! You''re a <vocabulary>brave</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>heroic</vocabulary> hero of old. A samurai perhaps?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8370', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%87%E6%95%A2', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T19:04:43.914066Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:03.98949Z'),
(8371, 'vocabulary', '旋回', '旋回', 'It doesn''t matter how many <kanji>times</kanji> a <kanji>rotation</kanji> goes around, it''s still a <vocabulary>rotation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8371', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%8B%E5%9B%9E', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T19:05:33.387696Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.002761Z'),
(8372, 'vocabulary', '旋風', '旋風', 'A <kanji>rotation</kanji> of <kanji>wind</kanji> forms a <vocabulary>whirlwind</vocabulary>, in both the physical and figurative senses. On the figurative side, it can also mean <vocabulary>commotion</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>sensation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8372', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%8B%E9%A2%A8', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T19:06:50.988082Z', NULL, '2025-07-01T19:41:45.398894Z'),
(8373, 'vocabulary', '旋律', '旋律', 'If you follow the <kanji>law</kanji> of <kanji>rotation</kanji> while you play music you''ll end up with a lovely <vocabulary>melody</vocabulary>. If you don''t, you''ll miss all the cues on beat that rotation gives you and it will sound like crap.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8373', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%8B%E5%BE%8B', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T21:52:01.018459Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:18:18.260627Z'),
(8374, 'vocabulary', '楓', '楓', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8374', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%93', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T21:59:26.987266Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.054905Z'),
(8375, 'vocabulary', '楓糖', '楓糖', '<kanji>Maple</kanji> <kanji>sugar</kanji> is <vocabulary>maple sugar</vocabulary>!

While you sometimes see 楓糖 on packaging for maple sugar or products made with it, in everyday life it''s more common to just say メープルシュガー.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8375', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%93%E7%B3%96', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:00:51.919568Z', NULL, '2025-07-31T15:32:20.689904Z'),
(8376, 'vocabulary', '水槽', '水槽', 'A <kanji>tank</kanji> of <kanji>water</kanji> can be used as a <vocabulary>fish tank</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>water tank</vocabulary> for drinking. It really only depends on you and how many fish you have.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8376', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E6%A7%BD', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:05:05.827739Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.081134Z'),
(8377, 'vocabulary', '浴槽', '浴槽', 'The <kanji>tank</kanji> you <kanji>bathe</kanji> in is a <vocabulary>bathtub</vocabulary>. Unless you''re bathing in like... the town''s water supply. If you are, please stop. I don''t want to drink your bathwater. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8377', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B4%E6%A7%BD', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:06:08.184191Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.093807Z'),
(8378, 'vocabulary', '定款', '定款', '<kanji>Article</kanji>s that <kanji>determine</kanji> the rules and regulations of your job or a company are called <vocabulary>articles of incorporation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8378', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9A%E6%AC%BE', 60, 1, '2015-07-13T22:07:19.608158Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(8379, 'vocabulary', '約款', '約款', 'An <kanji>article</kanji> you write a <kanji>promise</kanji> on is an <vocabulary>agreement</vocabulary>. Make sure you read them thoroughly before you sign them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8379', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%84%E6%AC%BE', 60, 1, '2015-07-13T22:08:21.614204Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.119379Z'),
(8380, 'vocabulary', '借款-8380', '借款', 'An <kanji>article</kanji> that proves that you''re only <kanji>borrow</kanji>ing money or stuffed animals is called a <vocabulary>loan</vocabulary>. You have to pay that person back for those fluffy critters.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8380', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%9F%E6%AC%BE-8380', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:09:44.906068Z', '2021-09-23T17:47:38.709388Z', '2025-11-27T19:44:31.146854Z'),
(8381, 'vocabulary', '漬ける', '漬ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>pickle</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to pickle something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to marinate something</vocabulary>.

漬ける is specifically used to describe the process of pickling or marinating foods, such as vegetables, fish, or meat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8381', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%AC%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 36, 1, '2015-07-13T22:10:59.075402Z', NULL, '2025-07-09T18:23:16.396866Z'),
(8382, 'vocabulary', '烏', '烏', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8382', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%83%8F', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:13:02.612773Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.174121Z'),
(8383, 'vocabulary', '烏龍茶', '烏龍茶', '<kanji>Imperial</kanji> <kanji>tea</kanji> the color of a <kanji>crow</kanji>''s feathers can only be the famous, delicious <vocabulary>Oolong tea</vocabulary>. It came from the imperial people of China to Japan (probably) and there are lots of different variants. But the leaves are all that blackish/grey crow feather color.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8383', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%83%8F%E9%BE%8D%E8%8C%B6', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:14:08.631832Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:16:41.901026Z'),
(8384, 'vocabulary', '盲人', '盲人', 'A <kanji>blind</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>blind person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8384', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B2%E4%BA%BA', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:16:24.476685Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(8385, 'vocabulary', '盲点', '盲点', 'Your <kanji>blind</kanji> <kanji>point</kanji> is a <vocabulary>blind spot</vocabulary>. Be careful to watch your blind spots when you''re driving.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8385', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B2%E7%82%B9', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:17:32.558402Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.213967Z'),
(8386, 'vocabulary', '盲目', '盲目', '<kanji>Blind</kanji> <kanji>eye</kanji>s will mean you suffer from <vocabulary>blindness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8386', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B2%E7%9B%AE', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:18:22.744483Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.228337Z'),
(8387, 'vocabulary', '色盲', '色盲', 'If you''re only <kanji>blind</kanji> to <kanji>color</kanji> then you have <vocabulary>color blindness</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8387', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%89%B2%E7%9B%B2', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:19:10.88797Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.242495Z'),
(8388, 'vocabulary', '粗塩', '粗塩', 'If you want to find some <kanji>coarse</kanji> <kanji>salt</kanji> just dive in the sea and collect <vocabulary>sea salt</vocabulary>! The pieces are nice and big and lumpy, so you really taste it when you''re cooking.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8388', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B2%97%E5%A1%A9', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:20:01.332616Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.256799Z'),
(8389, 'vocabulary', '紺', '紺', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8389', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%BA', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:21:22.126867Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.268932Z'),
(8390, 'vocabulary', '濃紺', '濃紺', 'Put a <kanji>thick</kanji> drop of <kanji>navy blue</kanji> paint on a canvas and it will look like an even bolder <vocabulary>dark navy blue</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8390', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BF%83%E7%B4%BA', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:22:29.125802Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.282379Z'),
(8391, 'vocabulary', '網羅', '網羅', 'If your <kanji>net</kanji> is <kanji>spread out</kanji> widely enough, it''ll be <vocabulary>covering</vocabulary> everything. You''re going to have to spread it out really, really far, though.

網羅 is a relatively formal word that means "covering" in the metaphorical sense, like an exhaustive list or a reference document <vocabulary>encompassing</vocabulary> all the information in a particular field. It gives off a strong feeling of "wow, they really got it all!" or even a proud "yep, I covered it all" kind of vibe. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8391', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%B2%E7%BE%85', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:24:27.299652Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.296504Z'),
(8392, 'vocabulary', '羅列', '羅列', '<kanji>Spread out</kanji> a complete <kanji>row</kanji> of numbers in the right order and you have <vocabulary>enumeration</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8392', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%85%E5%88%97', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:25:34.109384Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.31016Z'),
(8393, 'vocabulary', '羅針盤', '羅針盤', '<kanji>Spread out</kanji> a <kanji>needle</kanji> in a <kanji>tray</kanji> of water after rubbing it with a magnet and you''ll have made a <vocabulary>compass</vocabulary>! Try it at home kids! It really works!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8393', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%85%E9%87%9D%E7%9B%A4', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:26:48.916763Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.323817Z'),
(8394, 'vocabulary', '胎児', '胎児', 'A <kanji>womb</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> is a child that''s still in the womb, aka a <vocabulary>fetus</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8394', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%8E%E5%85%90', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:27:54.244485Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T17:49:15.486378Z'),
(8395, 'vocabulary', '堕胎', '堕胎', 'If you bring <kanji>degradation</kanji> to a <kanji>womb</kanji>, and there was a fetus in it, it causes an <vocabulary>abortion</vocabulary>. Or, in the case of an unwanted criminal abortion, <vocabulary>feticide</vocabulary>.

This word is very formal and is often used in legal contexts or literature.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8395', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%95%E8%83%8E', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:29:04.169437Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.351043Z'),
(8396, 'vocabulary', '受胎', '受胎', '<kanji>Accept</kanji> a child into your <kanji>womb</kanji> with <vocabulary>conception</vocabulary>. Now there''s a baby in there!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8396', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%97%E8%83%8E', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:29:58.891124Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:48.601574Z'),
(8397, 'vocabulary', '胎盤', '胎盤', 'Something comes out of a <kanji>womb</kanji> and is then put on a <kanji>tray</kanji>. It''s the <vocabulary>afterbirth</vocabulary>. Yeah, yeah, eww. Grow up, it''s a word you should know.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8397', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%8E%E7%9B%A4', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:31:12.449428Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.375999Z'),
(8398, 'vocabulary', '腸', '腸', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8398', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%B8', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:32:27.311469Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.387852Z'),
(8399, 'vocabulary', '胃腸', '胃腸', '<kanji>Intestines</kanji> and <kanji>stomach</kanji> make up the <vocabulary>stomach</vocabulary> in your body. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8399', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%83%E8%85%B8', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:34:06.25243Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:42:06.30193Z'),
(8400, 'vocabulary', '盲腸', '盲腸', 'Which of your <kanji>guts</kanji> is <kanji>blind</kanji>? Your <vocabulary>appendix</vocabulary>. That''s why so many people have to get them out. It''s in there blind, bumping around, messing things up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8400', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B2%E8%85%B8', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:35:05.733857Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.412924Z'),
(8401, 'vocabulary', '大腸', '大腸', 'The <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>intestines</kanji> are called the <vocabulary>large intestine</vocabulary>, or the <vocabulary>colon</vocabulary>. It''s where your poop goes and comes from!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8401', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E8%85%B8', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:36:23.596442Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(8402, 'vocabulary', '膜', '膜', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8402', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%86%9C', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:37:22.109013Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.43901Z'),
(8403, 'vocabulary', '角膜', '角膜', '<kanji>Membrane</kanji>s that wrap around at an <kanji>angle</kanji> are in your eye. Eyes are round and the membranes have to bend around the front part at an angle. It''s called your <vocabulary>cornea</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8403', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%92%E8%86%9C', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:38:16.936148Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T21:05:47.522155Z'),
(8404, 'vocabulary', '網膜', '網膜', 'In the back of your eye there''s a <kanji>net</kanji>-like <kanji>membrane</kanji> called the <vocabulary>retina</vocabulary>. It''s net-like because it catches… light! You know, to help you see.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8404', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%B2%E8%86%9C', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:39:46.805356Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.461858Z'),
(8405, 'vocabulary', '萌芽', '萌芽', 'A <kanji>sprout</kanji> that <kanji>sprout</kanji>s can thank <vocabulary>germination</vocabulary> for being able to <vocabulary>sprout</vocabulary> nice and big and strong. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8405', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%90%8C%E8%8A%BD', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:40:47.062592Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.473737Z'),
(8406, 'vocabulary', '均衡', '均衡', '<kanji>Equal</kanji> out your <kanji>equilibrium</kanji> and you''ll have solid <vocabulary>equilibrium</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8406', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9D%87%E8%A1%A1', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:44:06.514688Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.487745Z'),
(8407, 'vocabulary', '平衡', '平衡', '<kanji>Flat</kanji>ten out your <kanji>equilibrium</kanji> and you''ll have perfect <vocabulary>balance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8407', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B3%E8%A1%A1', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:45:45.527265Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.499586Z'),
(8408, 'vocabulary', '烏賊', '烏賊', 'Look in the sea and you''ll see what looks like a <kanji>crow</kanji> moving under the water and it''s a <kanji>thief</kanji>! It''s stealing from all the fish. As it comes closer you see that it isn''t a crow, it''s a <vocabulary>squid</vocabulary>. Or a <vocabulary>cuttlefish</vocabulary>? One or the other.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8408', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%83%8F%E8%B3%8A', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:50:00.52798Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.518742Z'),
(8409, 'vocabulary', '海賊', '海賊', '<kanji>Robber</kanji>s who sail on the high <kanji>sea</kanji>s are <vocabulary>pirate</vocabulary>s! Arrr!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8409', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B7%E8%B3%8A', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T22:54:16.626709Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.533293Z'),
(8410, 'vocabulary', '山賊', '山賊', '<kanji>Mountain</kanji> <kanji>robber</kanji>s are known as <vocabulary>bandit</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8410', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%B1%E8%B3%8A', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:01:43.010302Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.548057Z'),
(8411, 'vocabulary', '盗賊', '盗賊', 'A <kanji>robber</kanji> who <kanji>steal</kanji>s is a <vocabulary>thief</vocabulary>. And still a <vocabulary>robber</vocabulary>, I guess.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8411', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%97%E8%B3%8A', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:04:14.324383Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.563526Z'),
(8412, 'vocabulary', '迎賓館', '迎賓館', 'A <kanji>public building</kanji> where you <kanji>welcome</kanji> <kanji>VIP</kanji> guests is a <vocabulary>reception hall</vocabulary>. Or a <vocabulary>guest house</vocabulary> if they''re staying the night.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8412', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%8E%E8%B3%93%E9%A4%A8', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:09:42.026221Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.577449Z'),
(8413, 'vocabulary', '普遍', '普遍', 'Something <kanji>normal</kanji> and <kanji>universal</kanji> is still <vocabulary>universal</vocabulary>. Normally.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8413', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%AE%E9%81%8D', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:11:48.433361Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.589713Z'),
(8414, 'vocabulary', '一遍', '一遍', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>universal</kanji> event can only happen <vocabulary>once</vocabulary>. It''s too rare to happen more than that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8414', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E9%81%8D', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:12:41.217168Z', NULL, '2026-03-23T09:25:02.666059Z'),
(8415, 'vocabulary', '何遍', '何遍', '<kanji>What</kanji>''s <kanji>universal</kanji>? <vocabulary>How many times</vocabulary> do you think that question''s been asked throughout time? Probably a lot of times.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8415', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%95%E9%81%8D', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:13:29.139189Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(8416, 'vocabulary', '遍歴', '遍歴', 'It’s a <kanji>universal</kanji> truth that we are the <kanji>continuation</kanji> of our <vocabulary>experiences</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>history</vocabulary>.

The original meaning of this word is <vocabulary>journey</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>travels</vocabulary>, in the literal sense of the word, but nowadays you’d usually use it more abstractly, to talk about things like your past experiences, or perhaps your spiritual journey.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8416', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8D%E6%AD%B4', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:14:47.138163Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:12:50.785587Z'),
(8417, 'vocabulary', '遮る', '遮る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>intercept</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to intercept</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to interrupt</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8417', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%AE%E3%82%8B', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:16:19.150005Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.655601Z'),
(8418, 'vocabulary', '遮断', '遮断', 'If you <kanji>intercept</kanji> trade and <kanji>cut off</kanji> a country from the rest of the world they''ll be stuck in a state of <vocabulary>isolation</vocabulary> or if they have some kind of disease like zombie-itus, it''s a <vocabulary>quarantine</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8418', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%AE%E6%96%AD', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:18:02.57029Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.669102Z'),
(8419, 'vocabulary', '酵素', '酵素', 'The one <kanji>element</kanji> you need to <kanji>ferment</kanji> something is an <vocabulary>enzyme</vocabulary>. That breaks whatever you''re trying to ferment down in just the right way. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8419', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%B5%E7%B4%A0', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:19:17.982475Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:38:20.342554Z'),
(8420, 'vocabulary', '酵母', '酵母', 'The <kanji>mother</kanji> of good <kanji>ferment</kanji>ation is <vocabulary>yeast</vocabulary>! If you want to bake a pie or bread you have to have the best. And if you don''t have any, maybe your neighbor has some extra that you can borrow?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8420', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%B5%E6%AF%8D', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:20:44.550234Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.697237Z'),
(8421, 'vocabulary', '発酵', '発酵', 'When you <kanji>ferment</kanji> something it makes a <kanji>departure</kanji> from what it originally was and transforms during <vocabulary>fermentation</vocabulary> into something new.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8421', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BA%E9%85%B5', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:22:24.484477Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:22:52.12866Z'),
(8422, 'vocabulary', '醸成', '醸成', 'Beer can only <kanji>become</kanji> <kanji>brew</kanji>ed if you decide to <vocabulary>brew</vocabulary> it. Beer can''t make itself, you know.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8422', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%86%B8%E6%88%90', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:26:48.802497Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.726983Z'),
(8423, 'vocabulary', '醸造', '醸造', '<kanji>Create</kanji> a <kanji>brew</kanji> of beer by <vocabulary>brewing</vocabulary> it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8423', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%86%B8%E9%80%A0', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:27:57.66292Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.739871Z'),
(8424, 'vocabulary', '醸す', '醸す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>brew</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to brew</vocabulary>.

These days 醸す is often used figuratively, in which case it''ll mean <vocabulary>to give rise to</vocabulary>. You''ll see it in relation to an event giving rise to controversy or debate, for example, or even in a phrase like ムードを醸す (to give rise to a certain mood).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8424', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%86%B8%E3%81%99', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:29:00.527196Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T18:25:18.158061Z'),
(8425, 'vocabulary', '鎮まる', '鎮まる', 'You already learned that 鎮める means "to appease," but this one is what happens after they''ve been appeased. They start <vocabulary>to quiet down</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8425', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8E%AE%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:30:11.779681Z', NULL, '2025-09-22T02:03:24.078817Z'),
(8426, 'vocabulary', '閲覧', '閲覧', 'If you want to <kanji>look at</kanji> and give a quick <kanji>inspection</kanji> of some stuff then you''re just <vocabulary>browsing</vocabulary>. You don''t want to buy yet, you just have to give a look.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8426', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%B2%E8%A6%A7', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:33:01.104933Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.779189Z'),
(8427, 'vocabulary', '検閲', '検閲', 'If you <kanji>examine</kanji> something too closely during your <kanji>inspection</kanji> you''ll probably find a flaw and want to <vocabulary>censor</vocabulary> it. Put little black bars over all the flaws you find as you stare.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8427', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A4%9C%E9%96%B2', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:34:49.058021Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.791282Z'),
(8428, 'vocabulary', '校閲', '校閲', 'An <kanji>inspection</kanji> you make of your homework before <kanji>school</kanji> is a <vocabulary>revision</vocabulary>. Sometimes <vocabulary>proofreading</vocabulary> will help you find mistakes before you hand it in too. Just make sure you look everything over.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8428', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A0%A1%E9%96%B2', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:40:03.375848Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.803183Z'),
(8429, 'vocabulary', '鼓舞', '鼓舞', 'The <kanji>beat</kanji> makes you want to <kanji>dance</kanji>. It''s the <vocabulary>inspiration</vocabulary> you need to dance. The beat gives you <vocabulary>encouragement</vocabulary> to dance all night long. And without such a rockin'' beat you would have lame dance moves.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8429', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BC%93%E8%88%9E', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:41:18.261212Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.816643Z'),
(8430, 'vocabulary', '鼓', '鼓', 'What do you hit to make a sick <kanji>beat</kanji>? A cool little <vocabulary>hand drum</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8430', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BC%93', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:42:36.630667Z', NULL, '2025-11-15T22:11:20.299215Z'),
(8431, 'vocabulary', '鼓動', '鼓動', 'The <kanji>beat</kanji> that <kanji>move</kanji>s inside your body is the <vocabulary>beat</vocabulary>ing of your heart. Put your hand up to your neck and you should be able to feel it <vocabulary>pulse</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8431', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BC%93%E5%8B%95', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:44:20.75199Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.855656Z'),
(8432, 'vocabulary', '鼓膜', '鼓膜', 'You can hear something <kanji>beat</kanji> because of a <kanji>membrane</kanji> in your ear called an <vocabulary>eardrum</vocabulary>. Make sure you don''t stick your fingers too far in your ear or you could really hurt it. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8432', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BC%93%E8%86%9C', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:48:22.583508Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.869946Z'),
(8433, 'vocabulary', '太鼓', '太鼓', 'A <kanji>fat</kanji> <kanji>drum</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>drum</vocabulary>. This word can be used for any type of drum, but a common one in Japan is the <vocabulary>taiko</vocabulary>. There are lots of different types of taiko, and most of them are big and fat, and make a lovely deep sound. They''re super cool to watch and play!

If talking about taiko drums specifically, you can also say 和太鼓 to be clear that you''re not talking about any old drums.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8433', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%AA%E9%BC%93', 57, 1, '2015-07-13T23:49:27.373441Z', NULL, '2025-09-24T17:23:41.203717Z'),
(8434, 'vocabulary', '享受', '享受', 'The best thing to <kanji>receive</kanji> and <kanji>accept</kanji> into your life is <vocabulary>enjoyment</vocabulary>! Don''t block it out, let it in! Be happy!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8434', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%AB%E5%8F%97', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:27:59.897687Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:48.601574Z'),
(8435, 'vocabulary', '傑作', '傑作', 'Something you <kanji>make</kanji> that oozes with <kanji>greatness</kanji> is your <vocabulary>masterpiece</vocabulary>. The very <vocabulary>best work</vocabulary> you''ll ever make in your entire life.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8435', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%91%E4%BD%9C', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:29:52.387642Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:12:39.703958Z'),
(8436, 'vocabulary', '豪傑', '豪傑', 'If you look back in history and see someone of <kanji>exceptional</kanji> <kanji>greatness</kanji>, they''re probably a <vocabulary>hero</vocabulary>. Not just a normal person, but an <vocabulary>extraordinary person</vocabulary> like Hercules or Mae Jemison.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8436', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B1%AA%E5%82%91', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:31:08.487952Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T20:04:30.445599Z'),
(8437, 'vocabulary', '凌ぐ', '凌ぐ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>endure</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to endure</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to keep out</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8437', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%8C%E3%81%90', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:33:57.67028Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.954525Z'),
(8438, 'vocabulary', '解剖', '解剖', '<kanji>Divide</kanji> a body and <kanji>untie</kanji> its organs to perform an <vocabulary>autopsy</vocabulary>. You have to cut it all up and untangle the parts to see why this person or animal died.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8438', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A3%E5%89%96', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:36:08.220208Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T20:23:40.067262Z'),
(8439, 'vocabulary', '呆け', '呆け', 'Someone who''s <kanji>shock</kanji>ed at everything all the time will seem like an <vocabulary>idiot</vocabulary>. At this point, putting your drinks in a glass shouldn''t be surprising. I mean, come on. Only a <vocabulary>fool</vocabulary> lets himself be shocked by everything he sees.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8439', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%86%E3%81%91', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:38:05.292061Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:04.987576Z'),
(8440, 'vocabulary', '呆気', '呆気', 'The <kanji>energy</kanji> of being <kanji>shock</kanji>ed fills your body and leaves you <vocabulary>dumbfounded</vocabulary>. You''re so <vocabulary>taken aback</vocabulary> by this surge of shock that all you can do is stand still and let the energy go through you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8440', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%86%E6%B0%97', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:39:42.887494Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:23:06.059974Z'),
(8441, 'vocabulary', '呆れ返る', '呆れ返る', 'This is a combination of the word 呆れる meaning "to be amazed" and 返る meaning "to be returned." You''re so amazed at something that it keeps returning to your mind. It means <vocabulary>to be utterly amazed</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8441', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%91%86%E3%82%8C%E8%BF%94%E3%82%8B', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:40:46.089757Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.016096Z'),
(8442, 'vocabulary', '嘱託', '嘱託', 'A company <kanji>request</kanji>s <kanji>consign</kanji>ment work and you take it to become a <vocabulary>part time employee</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8442', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%98%B1%E8%A8%97', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:41:53.776089Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.030355Z'),
(8443, 'vocabulary', '奔走', '奔走', 'If you <kanji>bustle</kanji> and <kanji>run</kanji> around all day from being busy you end up <vocabulary>running about</vocabulary> to get them all done. You''ll <vocabulary>be busy</vocabulary> getting it all done, but your <vocabulary>efforts</vocabulary> won''t be in vain!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8443', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%94%E8%B5%B0', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:43:06.959341Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.043372Z'),
(8444, 'vocabulary', '奔放', '奔放', 'Some day you''ll <kanji>run</kanji> around, <kanji>release</kanji>d from the world and its troubles and you''ll end up running <vocabulary>wild</vocabulary>. All your actions will be completely <vocabulary>uninhibited</vocabulary> when you aren''t tied to your school or your job. It will be great!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8444', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%94%E6%94%BE', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:44:22.912138Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T02:08:38.914766Z'),
(8445, 'vocabulary', '媒介', '媒介', 'A <kanji>mediator</kanji> <kanji>jammed in</kanji> the middle of a group of angry people ends up becoming the <vocabulary>intermediary</vocabulary> between them. The mediator can''t just ignore what''s happening around them, and they have the tools to fix this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8445', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AA%92%E4%BB%8B', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:46:25.232266Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.069796Z'),
(8446, 'vocabulary', '媒体', '媒体', 'A <kanji>mediator</kanji> who works with someone who doesn''t have a <kanji>body</kanji> has to use some kind of mortal <vocabulary>media</vocabulary> to record what they''re saying. Print media? Sure. Electronic media? Alright? Ghost media? No! Humans can''t read that stuff!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8446', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AA%92%E4%BD%93', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:47:24.157546Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.083298Z'),
(8447, 'vocabulary', '触媒', '触媒', 'If a <kanji>mediator</kanji> <kanji>touch</kanji>es people the right way, they will become a <vocabulary>catalyst</vocabulary> for change. This could be good or bad change depending on what they stuck their hands in.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8447', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A6%E5%AA%92', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:48:23.818864Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.097678Z'),
(8448, 'vocabulary', '媒酌-8448', '媒酌', 'A <kanji>mediator</kanji> <kanji>serve</kanji>s families and even countries by <vocabulary>matchmaking</vocabulary> for them. They know everyone and everything so to stop family fights and wars, they put two people from each side together to get married and it fixes everything.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8448', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AA%92%E9%85%8C-8448', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:53:02.243399Z', '2021-09-30T18:06:23.769501Z', '2025-06-11T05:34:05.112489Z'),
(8449, 'vocabulary', '帆', '帆', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8449', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%86', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:54:33.12671Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.130558Z'),
(8450, 'vocabulary', '帆走', '帆走', 'A <kanji>sail</kanji> <kanji>run</kanji>s boats along the sea by <vocabulary>sailing</vocabulary>! You can''t run along the water unless the sail is out and catching the wind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8450', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%86%E8%B5%B0', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:56:14.356609Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.144468Z'),
(8451, 'vocabulary', '弦楽器', '弦楽器', 'You already know that 楽器 means "musical instrument." Well, this one has <kanji>string</kanji>s, making it a <vocabulary>stringed instrument</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8451', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%A6%E6%A5%BD%E5%99%A8', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:57:25.827601Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:48:13.06424Z'),
(8452, 'vocabulary', '管弦楽', '管弦楽', 'You already know that 弦楽 means "string music," so add some <kanji>pipe</kanji>s to that, like trumpets and saxophones, and you''ll have <vocabulary>orchestral music</vocabulary>. You can''t have a whole orchestra with just the string section!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8452', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AE%A1%E5%BC%A6%E6%A5%BD', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T17:58:23.231644Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T18:49:45.773585Z'),
(8453, 'vocabulary', '回忌', '回忌', 'The <kanji>times</kanji> when you are in <kanji>mourning</kanji> each year are for a loved one''s <vocabulary>death anniversary</vocabulary>. They come around again every year. It''s important you remember your loved ones at this time.

You''ll often see this used with a number before 回忌, indicating a certain anniversary. However, you actually start counting your 回忌 starting with the first period of mourning around the time of the person''s death, which would be the 一回忌. Hence, 二回忌 would usually be translated as "first death anniversary", 三回忌 as "second death anniversary", and so on. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8453', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9E%E5%BF%8C', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T17:59:47.072726Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.19453Z'),
(8454, 'vocabulary', '忌', '忌', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8454', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%8C', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T18:04:57.801599Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.207479Z'),
(8455, 'vocabulary', '禁忌', '禁忌', '<kanji>Prohibition</kanji> of something with a punishment so serious that you''ll end up in <kanji>mourning</kanji> after is a <vocabulary>taboo</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8455', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A6%81%E5%BF%8C', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T18:10:56.289665Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.222227Z'),
(8456, 'vocabulary', '一周忌', '一周忌', 'Remember how you learned that 一周 means "one time around?" Well if you''re <kanji>mourning</kanji> one time like this a year, then it''s because it''s the <vocabulary>first anniversary of death</vocabulary> for a family member. Maybe it was the death of your mom, your grandad or your goldfish, this is the first year since they died. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8456', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%91%A8%E5%BF%8C', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T18:13:13.314367Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.236173Z'),
(8457, 'vocabulary', '忌まわしい', '忌まわしい', 'This is a single kanji with しい on the end, meaning it''s an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>mourning</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>unpleasant</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>disgusting</vocabulary>. Nobody said mourning was fun.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8457', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%8C%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8F%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 59, 1, '2015-07-14T18:14:43.738532Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.250263Z'),
(8458, 'vocabulary', '感慨', '感慨', '<kanji>Feeling</kanji>s that make you <kanji>sigh</kanji> out loud are really <vocabulary>strong feelings</vocabulary>. You wouldn''t sigh if they didn''t move you so much.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8458', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E6%85%A8', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:15:42.795364Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(8459, 'vocabulary', '憤慨', '憤慨', 'You <kanji>resent</kanji> someone so much that you <kanji>sigh</kanji> with <vocabulary>anger</vocabulary> whenever they''re around. Everything they do makes you feel really strong <vocabulary>resentment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8459', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%A4%E6%85%A8', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:18:30.506972Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.279733Z'),
(8460, 'vocabulary', '憤り', '憤り', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>resent</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>resentment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8460', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%A4%E3%82%8A', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:20:11.520336Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.293325Z'),
(8461, 'vocabulary', '遊戯', '遊戯', 'You <kanji>play</kanji> and <kanji>play</kanji> all day! What do you play? A <vocabulary>game</vocabulary>! Or a <vocabulary>sport</vocabulary>! Which are technically games!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8461', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8A%E6%88%AF', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:21:48.303341Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.309322Z'),
(8462, 'vocabulary', '戯曲', '戯曲', 'A <kanji>play</kanji> that uses lots of intense, emotional <kanji>music</kanji> is a <vocabulary>drama</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8462', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%AF%E6%9B%B2', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:26:46.281397Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:51:25.132787Z'),
(8463, 'vocabulary', '戯れる', '戯れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>play</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to play with</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to toy with</vocabulary> someone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8463', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%AF%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:27:50.543923Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.337346Z'),
(8464, 'vocabulary', '扶助', '扶助', '<kanji>Help</kanji> and <kanji>aid</kanji> someone and you will <vocabulary>help</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>aid</vocabulary> them! Good job, you''re a good person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8464', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%B6%E5%8A%A9', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:28:47.754644Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.349108Z'),
(8465, 'vocabulary', '扶養', '扶養', '<kanji>Aid</kanji> and <kanji>foster</kanji> someone to show your <vocabulary>support</vocabulary> for them. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8465', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%B6%E9%A4%8A', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:30:32.687878Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:40:05.658345Z'),
(8466, 'vocabulary', '拍子', '拍子', 'Break down a <kanji>beat</kanji> into all of its <kanji>child</kanji>ren and you’ve got more than just a single beat. You’ve got an assortment of <vocabulary>beat</vocabulary>s, making a <vocabulary>rhythm</vocabulary>, and setting a <vocabulary>tempo</vocabulary>.

Additionally, this can also refer to <vocabulary>the moment</vocabulary> something happens. You’ll usually encounter this in the form of ~した拍子に. Have a look at our context sentences to see this usage in action!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8466', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%8D%E5%AD%90', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:31:28.669686Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.387612Z'),
(8467, 'vocabulary', '暁', '暁', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8467', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%81', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:32:56.422641Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.404832Z'),
(8468, 'vocabulary', '不朽', '不朽', 'If it does <kanji>not</kanji> <kanji>rot</kanji> then it''s <vocabulary>immortal</vocabulary>. It will live forever!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8468', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E6%9C%BD', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:33:36.189105Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.421302Z'),
(8469, 'vocabulary', '老朽', '老朽', '<kanji>Elderly</kanji> people start to <kanji>decay</kanji> and they get all <vocabulary>decrepit</vocabulary> and gross. Like a corpse. Bleh. Good thing I''ll never be <vocabulary>too old</vocabulary>. Ho ho ho!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8469', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%80%81%E6%9C%BD', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:34:21.357371Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.434964Z'),
(8470, 'vocabulary', '朽ちる', '朽ちる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>decay</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to decay</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to die out</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8470', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%BD%E3%81%A1%E3%82%8B', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:41:22.743203Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.474681Z'),
(8471, 'vocabulary', '脊椎', '脊椎', 'Your <kanji>stature</kanji>''s <kanji>spine</kanji> is really just your <vocabulary>spine</vocabulary>, when you think about it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8471', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%84%8A%E6%A4%8E', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:43:33.560102Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T06:30:43.680591Z'),
(8472, 'vocabulary', '殻', '殻', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This word isn''t limited to animal shells though! It covers seed shells, or husks, too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8472', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%BB', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:45:58.879515Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.515306Z'),
(8473, 'vocabulary', '地殻', '地殻', 'The <kanji>earth</kanji>''s <kanji>shell</kanji> is called the <vocabulary>earth''s crust</vocabulary>. You should have learned that in earth science. And if you didn''t, at least you know now!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8473', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%B0%E6%AE%BB', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:49:11.806306Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.529176Z'),
(8474, 'vocabulary', '淑やか', '淑やか', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8474', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%91%E3%82%84%E3%81%8B', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:50:33.835204Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.549526Z'),
(8475, 'vocabulary', '淑女', '淑女', 'A <kanji>graceful</kanji> <kanji>woman</kanji> is a proper, fancy <vocabulary>lady</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8475', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%91%E5%A5%B3', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:51:37.398691Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.562722Z'),
(8476, 'vocabulary', '汚濁', '汚濁', 'If the air is <kanji>muddy</kanji> and <kanji>dirty</kanji> it''s because there is too much <vocabulary>pollution</vocabulary> in that area. You should wear a mask and be careful of <vocabulary>contamination</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8476', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B1%9A%E6%BF%81', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:52:49.990259Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.575382Z'),
(8477, 'vocabulary', '濁す', '濁す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>muddy</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to make muddy</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to make cloudy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8477', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BF%81%E3%81%99', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:54:01.909833Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.592195Z'),
(8478, 'vocabulary', '濁流', '濁流', 'What''s another way to say <kanji>muddy</kanji> <kanji>stream</kanji>? That would be <vocabulary>muddy current</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>muddy river</vocabulary>. 

濁流 usually refers to a fast-moving current that''s churning with dirt and debris. You''ll often see it in the context of flooding or heavy rain, so a murky 濁流 river can be associated with danger.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8478', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BF%81%E6%B5%81', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:54:57.655329Z', NULL, '2026-04-09T01:41:09.901507Z'),
(8479, 'vocabulary', '起訴猶予', '起訴猶予', 'Remember how you learned that 猶予 means "extension?" Well you <kanji>wake up</kanji> ready to <kanji>sue</kanji> people when you''re told there has been an extension, meaning there was a <vocabulary>suspension of indictment</vocabulary> on your case. What the heck, you woke up ready for this! Now you have to wait to try and sue them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8479', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%B7%E8%A8%B4%E7%8C%B6%E4%BA%88', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:55:59.592155Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:30.112101Z'),
(8480, 'vocabulary', '執行猶予', '執行猶予', 'Remember how you learned that 猶予 means "extension?" You were supposed to <kanji>carry out</kanji> your sentence and <kanji>go</kanji> to jail, but the judge gave you a 猶予 (extension). In other words, you''ve received a <vocabulary>suspended sentence</vocabulary>.

執行猶予 means you''re guilty, but the judge hits the pause button on your prison time. If you stay out of trouble, the prison time gets dismissed. But if you commit another crime, you must serve both the original and new sentence. Basically, you better hope for a suspended sentence!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8480', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9F%B7%E8%A1%8C%E7%8C%B6%E4%BA%88', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:57:23.996783Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T23:23:47.975841Z'),
(8481, 'vocabulary', '瑞々しい', '瑞々しい', 'You give your <kanji>congratulations</kanji> to someone a few times because they seem so <vocabulary>fresh</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>vibrant</vocabulary>. You feel like you should because it must be nice to be like that still. Someday it will be gone, so make sure you say it a few times right away.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8481', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%91%9E%E3%80%85%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T18:58:48.550309Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(8482, 'vocabulary', '硫酸', '硫酸', 'Mix <kanji>sulfur</kanji> and <kanji>acid</kanji> and you''ll get <vocabulary>sulfuric acid</vocabulary>! That''s how science works! How simple!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8482', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A1%AB%E9%85%B8', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T19:00:00.246102Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:48:42.672332Z'),
(8483, 'vocabulary', '窃盗', '窃盗', 'You decided to <kanji>steal</kanji>... and then you <kanji>steal</kanji> again a few minutes later! Now you''re wanted for <vocabulary>theft</vocabulary>. <vocabulary>Stealing</vocabulary> is wrong, you should have known better!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8483', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AA%83%E7%9B%97', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T19:00:52.289118Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:18:34.258471Z'),
(8484, 'vocabulary', '紳士協定', '紳士協定', 'Remember how you learned that 紳士 means "gentleman?" Well when two men <kanji>determine</kanji> that the best thing to do is <kanji>cooperate</kanji> they form a <vocabulary>gentlemen''s agreement</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8484', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%B3%E5%A3%AB%E5%8D%94%E5%AE%9A', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T19:02:02.8079Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T23:15:52.231082Z'),
(8485, 'vocabulary', '絹', '絹', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8485', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B9', 37, 1, '2015-07-14T19:03:39.911189Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.721813Z'),
(8486, 'vocabulary', '絹糸', '絹糸', 'A <kanji>silk</kanji> <kanji>thread</kanji> is <vocabulary>silk thread</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8486', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B9%E7%B3%B8', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T19:04:47.032507Z', NULL, '2025-07-28T16:59:44.337071Z'),
(8487, 'vocabulary', '金縛り', '金縛り', 'Remember how you learned that 縛る means "to bind?" Well, in Japanese being bound by <kanji>gold</kanji> means you''re experiencing <vocabulary>sleep paralysis</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>temporary paralysis</vocabulary>. Just imagine that you''re waking from a dream where you''re bound up in gold thread, and it''s stopping you from moving.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8487', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%91%E7%B8%9B%E3%82%8A', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T20:55:33.90691Z', NULL, '2025-11-03T23:36:48.371022Z'),
(8488, 'vocabulary', '縛り首', '縛り首', 'Remember how 縛る means "to tie?" Well if you tie something around someone''s <kanji>neck</kanji> then it''s called <vocabulary>hanging</vocabulary>. They''ll probably die from it too, so it can also be <vocabulary>death by hanging</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8488', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%9B%E3%82%8A%E9%A6%96', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:00:11.950849Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.762443Z'),
(8489, 'vocabulary', '縫目', '縫目', 'Remember how 縫う means "to sew." Well the only way you can see if a pair of jeans has been sewed with your <kanji>eye</kanji> is if you see a <vocabulary>seam</vocabulary>. If your eyes can''t spot the <vocabulary>stitch</vocabulary>es, then it probably wasn''t sewed at all. Maybe it was superglued together.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8489', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B8%AB%E7%9B%AE', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:01:55.101965Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.776242Z'),
(8490, 'vocabulary', '肖像', '肖像', 'When art <kanji>resemble</kanji>s the <kanji>image</kanji> of a person, it''s a <vocabulary>portrait</vocabulary>. And it doesn''t just resemble them, it captures their image perfectly. Now that''s what I call a portrait.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8490', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%96%E5%83%8F', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:03:07.155172Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.790691Z'),
(8491, 'vocabulary', '藩', '藩', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8491', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%97%A9', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:04:04.869565Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.814687Z'),
(8492, 'vocabulary', '藩主', '藩主', 'The <kanji>master</kanji> of a <kanji>fiefdom</kanji> is the <vocabulary>feudal lord</vocabulary>. We also call them <vocabulary>Daimyo</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8492', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%97%A9%E4%B8%BB', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:06:27.441244Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.834367Z'),
(8493, 'vocabulary', '蝶々', '蝶々', 'A <kanji>butterfly</kanji> with another <kanji>butterfly</kanji> added to it? It''s still a <vocabulary>butterfly</vocabulary>, just a really big one with... with four wings. And laser eyes? Oh well, just go with it. It''s a butterfly.

While 蝶 is the formal word for butterfly you''ll see in advanced books or science stuff, 蝶々 is the everyday version you''ll actually say out loud. The repetition just makes it sound a little cuter, you know? It kind of… flutters off the tongue.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8493', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9D%B6%E3%80%85', 56, 1, '2015-07-14T21:07:23.8695Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T02:20:41.824685Z'),
(8494, 'vocabulary', '棋譜', '棋譜', 'Write down the <kanji>score</kanji>s people get from taking all of their opponent''s <kanji>chess piece</kanji>s and you''ll be writing down <vocabulary>game records</vocabulary>. This is usually only used for old-fashioned Japanese games like go and shogi. Let''s see who has the highest score at the end!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8494', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A3%8B%E8%AD%9C', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:08:34.751857Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.86471Z'),
(8495, 'vocabulary', '楽譜', '楽譜', 'A <kanji>comfort</kanji>ing <kanji>score</kanji> is a <vocabulary>musical score</vocabulary>. Music is really relaxing, and so is reading <vocabulary>sheet music</vocabulary>. You should try it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8495', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%BD%E8%AD%9C', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:09:39.24276Z', NULL, '2026-03-02T22:48:57.173938Z'),
(8496, 'vocabulary', '系譜', '系譜', 'Your <kanji>lineage</kanji> and <kanji>genealogy</kanji> make up your <vocabulary>genealogy</vocabulary>, also known as your <vocabulary>family history</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8496', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B3%BB%E8%AD%9C', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:10:56.144127Z', NULL, '2026-03-04T01:19:34.28962Z'),
(8497, 'vocabulary', '年譜', '年譜', 'Write down the <kanji>year</kanji>s of your <kanji>genealogy</kanji> in order and it makes a <vocabulary>chronological record</vocabulary> of your life. Look back on it when you''re old and sigh about the old days.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8497', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E8%AD%9C', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:11:59.707125Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.9093Z'),
(8498, 'vocabulary', '譜面', '譜面', 'The <kanji>score</kanji> on a <kanji>surface</kanji> is your <vocabulary>sheet music</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>musical score</vocabulary>. 

譜面 often refers more specifically to a music score’s pages. For a more general term for musical score, go with 楽譜.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8498', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%9C%E9%9D%A2', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:12:47.344776Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.922345Z'),
(8499, 'vocabulary', '恩赦', '恩赦', '<kanji>Pardon</kanji> someone out of the <kanji>kindness</kanji> in your heart and you''ll have granted them <vocabulary>amnesty</vocabulary>. How kind of you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8499', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%A9%E8%B5%A6', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:13:47.728393Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:41:42.780592Z'),
(8500, 'vocabulary', '赦免', '赦免', 'An <kanji>excuse</kanji> and a <kanji>pardon</kanji> is a <vocabulary>pardon</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>amnesty</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8500', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%A6%E5%85%8D', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:14:43.694098Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.955951Z'),
(8501, 'vocabulary', '容赦', '容赦', '<kanji>Form</kanji> a <kanji>pardon</kanji> with your mouth and actually <vocabulary>pardon</vocabulary> someone. Show your <vocabulary>forgiveness</vocabulary> with the words "I''m sorry."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8501', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B9%E8%B5%A6', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:15:45.317974Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.970023Z'),
(8502, 'vocabulary', '更迭', '更迭', 'You <kanji>renew</kanji> and <kanji>alternate</kanji> certain positions or people, making this a <vocabulary>reshuffle</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>dismissal</vocabulary>.

更迭 generally highlights the dismissal of someone from a position for replacement. You’ll often see this used in political contexts, like the reshuffling of certain ministers or other offices in a cabinet or diet; or just in general for important positions in other fields as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8502', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B4%E8%BF%AD', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:16:32.436073Z', NULL, '2026-04-04T20:17:45.612577Z'),
(8503, 'vocabulary', '晩酌', '晩酌', 'At <kanji>night</kanji> you are <kanji>serve</kanji>d an <vocabulary>evening drink</vocabulary>. It''s okay to have a strong <vocabulary>drink with dinner</vocabulary> every now and then.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8503', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%A9%E9%85%8C', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:17:24.254986Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:05.996336Z'),
(8504, 'vocabulary', '酌む', '酌む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>serve</kanji>, and the verb version is <vocabulary>to scoop</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to serve</vocabulary>.

It can also mean <vocabulary>to take into consideration</vocabulary> someone else''s feelings or opinions. Kind of like you''re scooping up the person''s feelings and considering them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8504', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%8C%E3%82%80', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:18:19.24609Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.022674Z'),
(8505, 'vocabulary', '錠', '錠', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8505', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8C%A0', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:19:28.560936Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.035881Z'),
(8506, 'vocabulary', '手錠', '手錠', 'You want to <kanji>lock</kanji> this criminal''s <kanji>hand</kanji>s up so he won''t go punching anymore grandpas. You need some <vocabulary>handcuffs</vocabulary> to make sure he doesn''t go around being a criminal anymore. Then you can lock him up good.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8506', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E9%8C%A0', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:21:51.569188Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.049023Z'),
(8507, 'vocabulary', '錠剤', '錠剤', 'You take a <kanji>dose</kanji> of medicine but it gets <kanji>lock</kanji>ed in your throat because it''s the kind of <vocabulary>pill</vocabulary> you chew, not swallow! You need to pay attention to which one is which or it won''t help at all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8507', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8C%A0%E5%89%A4', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:22:51.064116Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.061255Z'),
(8508, 'vocabulary', '陪審', '陪審', 'Who is going to <kanji>accompany</kanji> the <kanji>judge</kanji> in deciding the results of the trial? The <vocabulary>jury</vocabulary>! They''re the only other people besides the judge who can decide someone''s fate in court.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8508', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%99%AA%E5%AF%A9', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:23:51.436951Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.076476Z'),
(8509, 'vocabulary', '飽き', '飽き', 'If you''re <kanji>bored</kanji> all the time it makes you tired and the <vocabulary>weariness</vocabulary> you feel will show on your face. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8509', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%BD%E3%81%8D', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:24:45.560014Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.091225Z'),
(8510, 'vocabulary', '飽くまでも', '飽くまでも', 'I''m so <kanji>bored</kanji>. On top of my boredom is くまでも. If you took this separately, it would mean "even as a bear." So it''s like you''re saying I''m so <kanji>bored</kanji>, even if I was a bear. If you''re so bored that you''d be bored even as a bear, you''re going to be bored <vocabulary>to the last</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to the end</vocabulary>. Nothing will please you, you bored bear person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8510', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%BD%E3%81%8F%E3%81%BE%E3%81%A7%E3%82%82', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:25:45.097374Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.105357Z'),
(8511, 'vocabulary', '鶏', '鶏', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8511', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%B6%8F', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:27:00.327371Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.132126Z'),
(8512, 'vocabulary', '鶏肉', '鶏肉', '<kanji>Chicken</kanji> <kanji>meat</kanji> is <vocabulary>chicken meat</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8512', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%B6%8F%E8%82%89', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:28:38.117498Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.145033Z'),
(8513, 'vocabulary', '鶏卵', '鶏卵', 'A <kanji>chicken</kanji> <kanji>egg</kanji> is a <vocabulary>chicken egg</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8513', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%B6%8F%E5%8D%B5', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:29:56.565036Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.1581Z'),
(8514, 'vocabulary', '養鶏', '養鶏', '<kanji>Foster</kanji> a bunch of <kanji>chicken</kanji>s and start <vocabulary>chicken farming</vocabulary>! They will be your best friends! Your only friends.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8514', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A4%8A%E9%B6%8F', 58, 1, '2015-07-14T21:31:08.11594Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:40:05.658345Z'),
(8515, 'vocabulary', '早乙女', '早乙女', 'A young <kanji>woman</kanji> who is planting rice from <kanji>early</kanji> morning into the <kanji>latter</kanji> evenings is a <vocabulary>rice planting girl</vocabulary>. You''ll see them out there all day long, from dawn till dusk, planting, taking care of, and picking rice.

This is also a common family name, and on rare occasion, a given name as well. Some well-known people with this name include historical fiction writer Saotome Mitsugu (早乙女 貢), former pro-wrestler, model, and idol Saotome Miku (早乙女 未来), and stage and screen actor Saotome Taichi (早乙女 太一).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8515', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A9%E4%B9%99%E5%A5%B3', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T19:02:57.503361Z', NULL, '2026-01-27T00:53:32.555199Z'),
(8516, 'vocabulary', '東亜', '東亜', '<kanji>East</kanji> <kanji>Asia</kanji> is <vocabulary>East Asia</vocabulary>, otherwise known historically as <vocabulary>the Orient</vocabulary>.

東亜 is an older way to say "East Asia," covering countries like Japan, China, and Korea. These days, people usually say 東アジア instead, but you''ll still sometimes see 東亜 in old writing and as part of proper nouns.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8516', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%B1%E4%BA%9C', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T19:10:36.723772Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T22:54:02.046382Z'),
(8517, 'vocabulary', '侮蔑', '侮蔑', '<kanji>Despise</kanji> someone and your <kanji>scorn</kanji> for them will turn into full-on <vocabulary>contempt</vocabulary>.

侮蔑 is a formal word, mainly used in writing, that describes words, tone, or behavior which is contemptuous and demeaning. For example, derogatory expressions can be called 侮蔑的な表現, and speech that openly belittles someone may be described as 侮蔑を込めた言い方.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8517', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%AE%E8%94%91', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T19:12:31.603902Z', NULL, '2026-01-23T01:40:34.468616Z'),
(8518, 'vocabulary', '侮る', '侮る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>despise</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to despise</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8518', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%AE%E3%82%8B', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T19:14:22.133518Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.241092Z'),
(8519, 'vocabulary', '侮辱', '侮辱', 'You <kanji>despise</kanji> this one guy so much that you want to <kanji>humiliate</kanji> him so you <vocabulary>insult</vocabulary> him in public. That wasn''t very nice, but it did seem to embarrass him. I guess you won.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8519', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%AE%E8%BE%B1', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T19:16:47.638351Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.256259Z'),
(8520, 'vocabulary', '凸凹', '凸凹', 'Remember how you learned that 凹凸 means "uneven?" Well spin something uneven around and it''s still <vocabulary>uneven</vocabulary>! Just kinda backwards, just like this word!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8520', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%B8%E5%87%B9', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T19:18:09.52401Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.271117Z'),
(8521, 'vocabulary', '凹む', '凹む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>concave</kanji>, but "to concave" doesn''t really make sense. So the verb version is <vocabulary>to get dented</vocabulary>. Or, more figuratively, <vocabulary>to feel down</vocabulary>.

凹む is used in a physical sense when the surface of something is dented, collapsed inward, or features some kind of hollow depression. That can be anything from a dented car to even a fit, slimmed-down belly (sort of like the flab is "dented" in).

In a figurative sense, 凹む is used when you''re feeling down. Almost like your heart is dented, you know? We all have those days.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8521', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%B9%E3%82%80', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T19:21:29.516756Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.29076Z'),
(8522, 'vocabulary', '卑屈', '卑屈', 'A <kanji>lowly</kanji> person who <kanji>yield</kanji>s to everyone is really <vocabulary>subservient</vocabulary>. They''ll do everything people tell them because they don''t have the nerves to speak up and just say no.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8522', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%91%E5%B1%88', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T19:24:25.7454Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.304441Z'),
(8523, 'vocabulary', '卑劣', '卑劣', 'If you treat someone in a <kanji>lowly</kanji> manner because you think they''re <kanji>inferior</kanji> to you then you''re a <vocabulary>mean</vocabulary> person. Plain and simple.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8523', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%91%E5%8A%A3', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T19:25:22.086854Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.317248Z'),
(8524, 'vocabulary', '叔父', '叔父', 'An <kanji>uncle</kanji> feels like a <kanji>father</kanji> sometimes, but he''s still an <vocabulary>uncle</vocabulary> after all.
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8524', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%94%E7%88%B6', 36, 1, '2015-07-15T19:28:42.656853Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:01:24.564442Z'),
(8525, 'vocabulary', '叔母', '叔母', 'An <kanji>aunt</kanji> feels like a <kanji>mother</kanji> sometimes, but she''s still an <vocabulary>aunt</vocabulary> after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8525', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%94%E6%AF%8D', 36, 1, '2015-07-15T20:26:40.533276Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.340516Z'),
(8526, 'vocabulary', '吟味', '吟味', 'If you tasted the air at a <kanji>recital</kanji> the <kanji>flavor</kanji> would taste like <vocabulary>scrutiny</vocabulary>. That''s really what everyone is doing when they watch. They don''t really want to hear you, they just want to judge you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8526', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%9F%E5%91%B3', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T20:27:32.171658Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:20:50.221396Z'),
(8527, 'vocabulary', '堪える', '堪える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>endure</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to endure</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to bear</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8527', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A0%AA%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T20:37:50.82631Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.365207Z'),
(8528, 'vocabulary', '婚姻', '婚姻', '<kanji>Marry</kanji> and <kanji>marriage</kanji> still make <vocabulary>marriage</vocabulary>. Congratulations! Wooh! Yay! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8528', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A9%9A%E5%A7%BB', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T20:39:48.520144Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.376936Z'),
(8529, 'vocabulary', '駐屯', '駐屯', '<kanji>Resident</kanji> soldiers live in <kanji>barracks</kanji> when they''ve been <vocabulary>stationed</vocabulary> in another country. They have to serve their <vocabulary>occupancy</vocabulary> for a certain amount of time before they get to go back home.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8529', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%90%E5%B1%AF', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T20:40:38.177316Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.390669Z'),
(8530, 'vocabulary', '岬', '岬', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

岬 means <vocabulary>cape</vocabulary>, which is a piece of land jutting out into the sea. While 崎 is mostly used in place names and compound words, 岬 can be used as a standalone noun, although you also see it in place names such as 宗谷岬 (Cape Sōya).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8530', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B2%AC', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T20:41:40.72328Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:30.315687Z'),
(8531, 'vocabulary', '峠', '峠', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

峠 refers to a mountain pass, and often specifically the highest point of a <vocabulary>mountain pass</vocabulary>, where it passes between two mountains. When used figuratively, it means <vocabulary>the worst</vocabulary>, as in the toughest moment of a specific event, like the height of a health crisis or the hottest day of a heatwave.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8531', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B3%A0', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T20:44:34.154533Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:13:57.805665Z'),
(8532, 'vocabulary', '崇高', '崇高', 'You <kanji>worship</kanji> someone who is so <kanji>high</kanji> above you that you can''t help but be in awe of their <vocabulary>loftiness</vocabulary>. Their <vocabulary>nobility</vocabulary> is the reason you feel like you should worship them in the first place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8532', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B4%87%E9%AB%98', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T20:48:44.875807Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.433187Z'),
(8533, 'vocabulary', '崇拝', '崇拝', '<kanji>Worship</kanji> in the morning, <kanji>worship</kanji> in the night, <vocabulary>worship</vocabulary> all day! Worship that WaniKani poster. Do it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8533', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B4%87%E6%8B%9D', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T20:51:32.680474Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.449189Z'),
(8534, 'vocabulary', '惜しまない', '惜しまない', 'Remember how you learned that 惜しむ means "to be sparing?" Well if you make that negative with ない, then you''re doing it <vocabulary>without sparing</vocabulary> anything instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8534', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%83%9C%E3%81%97%E3%81%BE%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T20:52:25.920409Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.462523Z'),
(8535, 'vocabulary', '慶事', '慶事', 'If you can <kanji>congratulate</kanji> someone for an <kanji>occurrence</kanji> in their life, it must be an <vocabulary>auspicious event</vocabulary>. You''ll remember this happy event for a long, long time.

慶事 is a very formal word used to refer to an auspicious event, such as a wedding, the birth of a child, or other significant celebrations. You might encounter 慶事 in official announcements or formal congratulatory messages.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8535', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%B6%E4%BA%8B', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T20:53:18.681266Z', NULL, '2025-10-16T19:53:28.756562Z'),
(8536, 'vocabulary', '弁慶', '弁慶', 'You should <kanji>congratulate</kanji> a person who speaks lots of <kanji>dialect</kanji>s because they have to be a really <vocabulary>strong person</vocabulary>, just like Mr. <vocabulary>Benkei</vocabulary> was. You think learning Japanese is hard now, just wait till you hear Osaka-ben and Hokkaido-ben. It takes a really strong person — a Benkei — to learn all those dialects, so congratulate them and tell them how strong they are!

弁慶 is the name of a Japanese historical figure who was really strong, so Benkei is the Japanese equivalent of Hercules or Achilles in Western languages.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8536', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%81%E6%85%B6', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T20:56:10.685346Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.499254Z'),
(8537, 'vocabulary', '慶祝', '慶祝', 'You <kanji>congratulate</kanji> someone and <kanji>celebrate</kanji> by saying <vocabulary>congratulation</vocabulary>s and having a <vocabulary>celebration</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8537', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%B6%E7%A5%9D', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T20:57:50.371931Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.511999Z'),
(8538, 'vocabulary', '慶弔', '慶弔', 'If you are saying <kanji>congratulation</kanji>s and also giving your <kanji>condolence</kanji>s then they are <vocabulary>congratulations and condolences</vocabulary>. Pretty easy, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8538', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%B6%E5%BC%94', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T20:58:58.989846Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.525656Z'),
(8539, 'vocabulary', '憂き目', '憂き目', 'If you have <kanji>grief</kanji> in your <kanji>eye</kanji>s, it means you''ve gone through a lot of <vocabulary>bitterness</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>misery</vocabulary>. You can always see in someone''s eyes if they''ve experienced it too.

You''ll see this word most often in 憂き目を見る and 憂き目に合う, which both mean "to experience <vocabulary>hardship</vocabulary>."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8539', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%82%E3%81%8D%E7%9B%AE', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:02:07.297014Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.537862Z'),
(8540, 'vocabulary', '憧れ', '憧れ', 'If you <kanji>long for</kanji> something you''ll get feelings of <vocabulary>yearning</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>longing</vocabulary>. Don''t worry, some day you''ll get whatever it is. 

憧れ can also describe a strong feeling of <vocabulary>admiration</vocabulary>, particularly for people.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8540', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%A7%E3%82%8C', 28, 1, '2015-07-15T21:03:22.992989Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.563289Z'),
(8541, 'vocabulary', '憧れる', '憧れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>long for</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to long for</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to yearn after</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8541', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%86%A7%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2015-07-15T21:04:40.664154Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.585264Z'),
(8542, 'vocabulary', '稚拙', '稚拙', '<kanji>Clumsy</kanji> and <kanji>immature</kanji> people end up being really <vocabulary>unskillful</vocabulary>. You can''t do your job if you''re always dropping things and whining, can you? They''re usually also really <vocabulary>childish</vocabulary>, if you didn''t already guess that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8542', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%9A%E6%8B%99', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:05:47.364898Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T04:47:48.160372Z'),
(8543, 'vocabulary', '模擬', '模擬', 'Food that <kanji>imitate</kanji>s an <kanji>imitation</kanji> of some OTHER food, is an even worse <vocabulary>imitation</vocabulary>. You don''t want to eat that. It''s a <vocabulary>sham</vocabulary>! It''s <vocabulary>mock</vocabulary>ing real food! It''s probably made of dust and cardboard!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8543', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A8%A1%E6%93%AC', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:08:04.358145Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.616177Z'),
(8544, 'vocabulary', '擬装', '擬装', 'Use <kanji>attire</kanji> that <kanji>imitate</kanji>s your surroundings to create a <vocabulary>disguise</vocabulary>. Or throw on some <vocabulary>camouflage</vocabulary> so that no one will be able to find you in Walmart.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8544', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%93%AC%E8%A3%85', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:09:16.973365Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.629647Z'),
(8545, 'vocabulary', '取り敢えず', '取り敢えず', 'If you only <kanji>take</kanji> without <kanji>daring</kanji>, then you can only take something <vocabulary>for the time being</vocabulary>. Since you weren''t daring enough to take complete hold of it, you only have it <vocabulary>for now</vocabulary>.

取り敢えず is a common, casual phrase you''ll hear all the time. People use it when making tentative decisions or taking temporary measures, and it often carries a "we''ll figure out the rest later" vibe. Note this word is usually written in kana as とりあえず.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8545', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8A%E6%95%A2%E3%81%88%E3%81%9A', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:12:01.538846Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T20:08:33.30582Z'),
(8546, 'vocabulary', '法曹', '法曹', 'The <kanji>official</kanji> <kanji>method</kanji> of doing anything requires hiring a <vocabulary>lawyer</vocabulary>. They have to be there when you sign forms or sue people, or it isn''t official at all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8546', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%95%E6%9B%B9', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:17:15.054578Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.715283Z'),
(8547, 'vocabulary', 'ご無沙汰', 'ご無沙汰', 'If you <kanji>select</kanji> to talk to <kanji>nothing</kanji> but <kanji>sand</kanji> for a really long time, you''re <vocabulary>neglecting to contact</vocabulary> anyone else. Your relatives miss you! Stop talking to sand and call them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8547', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%94%E7%84%A1%E6%B2%99%E6%B1%B0', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:21:13.587959Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:18:19.722322Z'),
(8548, 'vocabulary', '流浪', '流浪', '<kanji>Wander</kanji> from <kanji>stream</kanji> to stream and you''ll get really used to <vocabulary>vagrancy</vocabulary>. You''ll love <vocabulary>wandering</vocabulary> so much that you''ll convert to <vocabulary>nomadism</vocabulary>. The open sky is your home!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8548', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%81%E6%B5%AA', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:22:14.476243Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.742246Z'),
(8549, 'vocabulary', '浪費', '浪費', 'If you <kanji>wander</kanji> around doing nothing at your own <kanji>expense</kanji> it will be a huge <vocabulary>waste</vocabulary> of money. It''s not like you were doing or buying anything important, you were just wandering around, after all. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8549', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%AA%E8%B2%BB', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:24:47.244705Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.758026Z'),
(8550, 'vocabulary', '放浪', '放浪', '<kanji>Release</kanji> your worldly ties and <kanji>wander</kanji> the world. You''ll get used to <vocabulary>wandering</vocabulary> and you''ll spend the rest of your days <vocabulary>roaming</vocabulary> the world until you die. It''s quite the life! But you can only do it if you let go of everything else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8550', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%BE%E6%B5%AA', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:27:06.967019Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T02:08:38.914766Z'),
(8551, 'vocabulary', '浪人', '浪人', 'A <kanji>person</kanji> who <kanji>wander</kanji>s because their lord was killed, is known as a <vocabulary>ronin</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>masterless samurai</vocabulary>. Now they''re just like the rest of us, but they can''t stop wandering because they have nowhere they belong.

In modern times, ronin refers to those who failed their university entrance exams, waiting until for another chance to take the test again.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8551', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%AA%E4%BA%BA', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:28:08.055173Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(8552, 'vocabulary', '浮浪者', '浮浪者', '<kanji>Someone</kanji> who <kanji>float</kanji>s and <kanji>wander</kanji>s from place to place is a <vocabulary>vagrant</vocabulary>. They don''t really have a home, but they keep hanging around yours. It''s kind of annoying. (This word has a negative connotation, you wouldn''t use it for someone who is just homeless, unless you''re really mean.)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8552', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%AE%E6%B5%AA%E8%80%85', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:29:30.348771Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:25:49.820187Z'),
(8553, 'vocabulary', '漆', '漆', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8553', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%86', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:31:14.439317Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.825554Z'),
(8554, 'vocabulary', '漆器', '漆器', '<kanji>Lacquer</kanji> <kanji>container</kanji>s are called <vocabulary>Lacquerware</vocabulary>. Those pretty bowls you''re eating out of? That shininess is because it''s lacquerware!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8554', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%86%E5%99%A8', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:32:54.345695Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.840675Z'),
(8555, 'vocabulary', '漆黒', '漆黒', '<kanji>Lacquer</kanji> <kanji>black</kanji> looks <vocabulary>jet black</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>pitch black</vocabulary>. It''s as black as black can be!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8555', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%86%E9%BB%92', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:34:22.377391Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.854902Z'),
(8556, 'vocabulary', '漬物', '漬物', 'In Japanese, <kanji>pickle</kanji>d <kanji>thing</kanji>s refer to  <vocabulary>Japanese pickled vegetables</vocabulary>!

漬物 refers to <vocabulary>Japanese pickles</vocabulary> made by soaking vegetables like cucumbers, daikon radish, or eggplant in a solution containing ingredients like salt, rice bran paste, vinegar, and soy sauce. They''re left to ferment or marinate, enhancing their flavors. In contrast, western pickles are simply called ピクルス.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8556', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%AC%E7%89%A9', 37, 1, '2015-07-15T21:44:27.806162Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(8557, 'vocabulary', '〜漬け', '〜漬け', 'You already learned that 漬ける means "to pickle something," so if you add that to the end of a food word (and leave off the る) it means that food has been <vocabulary>pickled in</vocabulary> something, like  酢漬け  (pickled in vinegar).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8557', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%BC%AC%E3%81%91', 38, 1, '2015-07-15T21:45:55.553763Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T18:16:57.043022Z'),
(8558, 'vocabulary', '漬かる', '漬かる', 'You already learned that 漬ける means "to pickle something." When it becomes 漬かる, it means the food has reached a state where the pickling or marinating process is complete, and it''s ready to eat. So this word means <vocabulary>to be well pickled</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be well marinated</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8558', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%AC%E3%81%8B%E3%82%8B', 42, 1, '2015-07-15T21:53:15.159589Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.944483Z'),
(8559, 'vocabulary', '甚大', '甚大', 'Another way to say something is <kanji>very</kanji> <kanji>big</kanji> is to say it is <vocabulary>immense</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>enormous</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8559', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9A%E5%A4%A7', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:54:11.883649Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(8560, 'vocabulary', '甚だ', '甚だ', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8560', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9A%E3%81%A0', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T21:56:06.664563Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:25:37.753432Z'),
(8561, 'vocabulary', '親睦', '親睦', 'If a <kanji>parent</kanji> is <kanji>friendly</kanji> enough you can form a <vocabulary>friendship</vocabulary> with them, not just a kid/parent bond.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8561', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA%E7%9D%A6', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:00:28.181279Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:06.987007Z'),
(8562, 'vocabulary', '暗礁', '暗礁', 'A <kanji>dark</kanji> <kanji>reef</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>reef</vocabulary>. It''s just really deep underwater so it''s kinda dark under there. Because of that it can also be called a <vocabulary>sunken reef</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8562', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9A%97%E7%A4%81', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:02:15.334607Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T02:01:16.682053Z'),
(8563, 'vocabulary', '環礁', '環礁', 'A <kanji>reef</kanji> in the shape of a <kanji>loop</kanji> is an <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atoll" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener"><vocabulary>atoll</vocabulary></a>. If you don''t know what that is, it''s a <vocabulary>circular coral reef</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8563', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%92%B0%E7%A4%81', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:03:09.527392Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.022039Z'),
(8564, 'vocabulary', '岩礁', '岩礁', 'A <kanji>reef</kanji> made of <kanji>boulder</kanji>s is still a <vocabulary>reef</vocabulary>, but it''s made of <vocabulary>sunken rocks</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8564', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B2%A9%E7%A4%81', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:04:32.42808Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:18:14.999229Z'),
(8565, 'vocabulary', '禍根', '禍根', '<kanji>Evil</kanji>''s <kanji>root</kanji> is the <vocabulary>root of evil</vocabulary>. It''s the <vocabulary>source of evil</vocabulary> things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8565', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A6%8D%E6%A0%B9', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:06:11.64963Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.049901Z'),
(8566, 'vocabulary', '戦禍', '戦禍', 'One of the most <kanji>evil</kanji> things about <kanji>war</kanji> are the <vocabulary>war damages</vocabulary> that happen during it. Whole countrysides, cities, and lots of people can die. So I''d say the <vocabulary>ravages of war</vocabulary> are the most evil thing about war. Period.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8566', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%A6%E7%A6%8D', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:07:23.674434Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.063258Z'),
(8567, 'vocabulary', '危篤', '危篤', 'If someone is so <kanji>dangerous</kanji> that they <kanji>deliberately</kanji> hurt you, you''re going to end up in <vocabulary>critical condition</vocabulary>. This wasn''t an accident, they knew what they were doing and they didn''t miss. Now you''re <vocabulary>on the verge of death</vocabulary>. Hold on, kiddo.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8567', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%B1%E7%AF%A4', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:08:39.835915Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.075909Z'),
(8568, 'vocabulary', '紡ぐ', '紡ぐ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>spinning</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to spin</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to make yarn</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8568', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%A1%E3%81%90', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:10:38.974066Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.092699Z'),
(8569, 'vocabulary', '紡績', '紡績', 'A <kanji>spinning</kanji> <kanji>achievement</kanji> is just the <vocabulary>spinning</vocabulary> you produce — like thread or <vocabulary>yarn spinning</vocabulary>. 

紡績 is a technical term that specifically refers to the process of spinning fibers into yarn or thread, typically in the textile industry.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8569', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%A1%E7%B8%BE', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:13:10.780143Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.105405Z'),
(8570, 'vocabulary', '紡織', '紡織', '<kanji>Spinning</kanji> and <kanji>weaving</kanji> means <vocabulary>spinning and weaving</vocabulary>. Yay!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8570', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%A1%E7%B9%94', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:14:04.554275Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.120516Z'),
(8571, 'vocabulary', '胆', '胆', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

胆 means <vocabulary>guts</vocabulary>, and just like the English word guts, it can also be used to mean <vocabulary>courage</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>fortitude</vocabulary>. However, 胆 is much more formal and advanced than its English equivalent, and it''s almost always seen in the set phrase 胆が据わる (to have nerves of steel).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8571', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%86', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:15:20.375164Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.135079Z'),
(8572, 'vocabulary', '魂胆', '魂胆', 'The <kanji>soul</kanji> and <kanji>guts</kanji> of a story lie in the <vocabulary>plot</vocabulary>! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8572', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AD%82%E8%83%86', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:16:51.25325Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.147651Z'),
(8573, 'vocabulary', '胆石', '胆石', '<kanji>Gallbladder</kanji> <kanji>stone</kanji>s are more commonly known as <vocabulary>gallstones</vocabulary>. Sometimes you have to get them surgically removed. Scary!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8573', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%86%E7%9F%B3', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:17:43.70758Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.159984Z'),
(8574, 'vocabulary', '大胆', '大胆', 'If you have really <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>guts</kanji> you''re a super <vocabulary>bold</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>daring</vocabulary> person. In fact, you might even be superman. That''s how big your guts are. Don''t worry, I don''t tell anyone I know who you are. It''ll be our little secret. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8574', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E8%83%86', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:18:33.822733Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(8575, 'vocabulary', '落胆', '落胆', 'Your <kanji>guts</kanji> feel like they''re <kanji>fall</kanji>ing when you experience a lot of <vocabulary>discouragement</vocabulary>. You know that sinking feeling you get in the pit of your stomach? That''s your guts falling!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8575', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%90%BD%E8%83%86', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:19:38.46687Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:46:04.638998Z'),
(8576, 'vocabulary', '大腸菌', '大腸菌', 'Remember how you learned that 大腸 means "large intestines?" Well when you get <kanji>bacteria</kanji> in there you end up with <vocabulary>E. Coli</vocabulary>. Go to a doctor right away! You''re gonna be stuck in the bathroom for a while. Sorry...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8576', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E8%85%B8%E8%8F%8C', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:20:25.253293Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(8577, 'vocabulary', '萌え', '萌え', 'What is a <kanji>sprout</kanji> doing? It''s <vocabulary>sprouting</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>budding</vocabulary>. A <vocabulary>crush</vocabulary> you have on someone can sprout too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8577', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%90%8C%E3%81%88', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:21:33.778953Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.211591Z'),
(8578, 'vocabulary', '軽蔑', '軽蔑', '<kanji>Lightweight</kanji> <kanji>scorn</kanji>, no matter how light, is still <vocabulary>scorn</vocabulary>. Those feelings are pretty strong no matter what.

軽蔑 is used when you look down on or disdain someone, seeing them as inferior in character or ability. It often follows unethical or disappointing behavior that makes you think, "I can''t respect you as a person anymore."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8578', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%BD%E8%94%91', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:22:43.089096Z', NULL, '2026-01-23T01:40:34.468616Z'),
(8579, 'vocabulary', '蔑む', '蔑む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>scorn</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to scorn</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to despise</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8579', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%94%91%E3%82%80', 60, 1, '2015-07-15T22:23:37.00918Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T07:36:25.979371Z'),
(8580, 'vocabulary', '不均衡', '不均衡', 'Remember how 均衡 means "balance?" Well if you do <kanji>not</kanji> have any balance, you''re going to have an <vocabulary>imbalance</vocabulary> instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8580', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8D%E5%9D%87%E8%A1%A1', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:24:36.75744Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.254732Z'),
(8581, 'vocabulary', '詠む', '詠む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>compose</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to compose</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8581', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%A0%E3%82%80', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:25:41.703036Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.269129Z'),
(8582, 'vocabulary', '普遍的', '普遍的', 'Remember how 普遍 means "universal?" Well when you add <kanji>like</kanji> it''s still <vocabulary>universal</vocabulary>! Easy!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8582', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%AE%E9%81%8D%E7%9A%84', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:26:43.46957Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.282582Z'),
(8583, 'vocabulary', '一遍に', '一遍に', 'Remember how you learned that 一遍 means "once?" Well if you add に and make it an adverb, it becomes <vocabulary>at once</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8583', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E9%81%8D%E3%81%AB', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:27:40.872193Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.296712Z'),
(8584, 'vocabulary', '遮断機', '遮断機', 'Remember how 遮断 means "isolation?" Well the <kanji>machine</kanji> that isolates you from a train is a <vocabulary>crossing gate</vocabulary>. That way you won''t walk, bike, or drive your car into an oncoming train.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8584', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%AE%E6%96%AD%E6%A9%9F', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:28:33.818882Z', NULL, '2025-12-29T01:51:12.062818Z'),
(8585, 'vocabulary', '左遷', '左遷', 'Usually if you''re going to the left in Japanese, it''s not a good thing. So if you <kanji>transition</kanji> to the <kanji>left</kanji> it''s seen as a <vocabulary>demotion</vocabulary>. Going to the right is way better.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8585', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A6%E9%81%B7', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:29:33.338729Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.324438Z'),
(8586, 'vocabulary', '遷都', '遷都', 'If you want to <kanji>transition</kanji> the <kanji>capital</kanji> to a new, better place, you need to <vocabulary>move the capital</vocabulary>. This used to happen a lot way back in Japanese history. It''s actually pretty interesting so you might want to read up on it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8586', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%B7%E9%83%BD', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:30:27.049004Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.336978Z'),
(8587, 'vocabulary', '変遷', '変遷', '<kanji>Change</kanji> and <kanji>transition</kanji> mean <vocabulary>change</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>transition</vocabulary>! Nifty.

変遷 refers to historical or long-term changes. It’s often used when discussing the evolution or progression of entities over long periods.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8587', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%89%E9%81%B7', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:31:21.044382Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.350449Z'),
(8588, 'vocabulary', '酪農', '酪農', '<kanji>Dairy</kanji> <kanji>farming</kanji> is <vocabulary>dairy farming</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8588', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%AA%E8%BE%B2', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:32:29.08804Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.364548Z'),
(8589, 'vocabulary', '鋳造', '鋳造', 'If you want to <kanji>create</kanji> a <kanji>cast</kanji> you need to learn <vocabulary>casting</vocabulary> first. Then you can make lots of neat art, crafts, and counterfeit money. Wait, no, don''t do that last one. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8589', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8B%B3%E9%80%A0', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:33:32.920092Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:46:12.340974Z'),
(8590, 'vocabulary', '閑散', '閑散', 'It''s such a <kanji>leisure</kanji>ly day that everyone has <kanji>scatter</kanji>ed and left the area <vocabulary>deserted</vocabulary>. It''s just you now. Lounging in the shade.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8590', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%91%E6%95%A3', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:34:44.469054Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.392853Z'),
(8591, 'vocabulary', '閑静', '閑静', '<kanji>Leisure</kanji>ly <kanji>quiet</kanji> is the best kind of <vocabulary>quiet</vocabulary>. Even your brain gets to be quiet because you don''t have a care left in the world. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8591', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%91%E9%9D%99', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:35:40.340406Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:08:32.29071Z'),
(8592, 'vocabulary', '雌', '雌', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Note that this is often written in katakana, as in メス.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8592', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%8C', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:36:31.044098Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.422157Z'),
(8593, 'vocabulary', '雌花', '雌花', 'A <kanji>female</kanji> <kanji>flower</kanji> is a <vocabulary>female flower</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8593', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%8C%E8%8A%B1', 59, 1, '2015-07-15T22:38:21.594042Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:20:54.638732Z'),
(8594, 'vocabulary', '倹約', '倹約', 'I <kanji>promise</kanji> this will be a really <kanji>thrifty</kanji> place because it''s a <vocabulary>thrift</vocabulary> store! They wouldn''t lie about that!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8594', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%B9%E7%B4%84', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T18:30:22.077422Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.450857Z'),
(8595, 'vocabulary', '解剖学', '解剖学', 'Remember how you learned that 解剖 means "autopsy?" Well you have to <kanji>learn</kanji> about <vocabulary>anatomy</vocabulary> before you''re going to be allowed to perform an autopsy or dissect anything. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8595', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A3%E5%89%96%E5%AD%A6', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T18:32:13.543154Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T20:23:40.067262Z'),
(8596, 'vocabulary', '弾劾', '弾劾', 'The final <kanji>bullet</kanji> of <kanji>censure</kanji> is <vocabulary>impeachment</vocabulary>. You''ve already been publicly humiliated and now you''re being impeached because of it. Yikes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8596', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%BE%E5%8A%BE', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T18:33:31.120804Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:33:28.795445Z'),
(8597, 'vocabulary', '隠匿', '隠匿', '<kanji>Hide</kanji> and <kanji>hide</kanji> make <vocabulary>hide</vocabulary>! Lots of hiding going on around here. I think... I can''t find anyone so I can only assume.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8597', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9A%A0%E5%8C%BF', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T18:34:29.655482Z', NULL, '2025-08-27T00:02:29.618118Z'),
(8598, 'vocabulary', '匿名', '匿名', '<kanji>Hide</kanji> your <kanji>name</kanji> for <vocabulary>anonymity</vocabulary>. There''s lots of that here on the internet. In fact, you''re probably using a <vocabulary>pseudonym</vocabulary> right now, aren''t you? Yeah, you are. You can''t hide THAT from me at least... uh... Ssssteve?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8598', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%BF%E5%90%8D', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T18:35:33.16582Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(8599, 'vocabulary', '升', '升', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8599', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%87', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T18:36:27.057138Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.527877Z'),
(8600, 'vocabulary', '升目', '升目', '<kanji>Measure</kanji> that shape on the paper with your <kanji>eye</kanji>s. It''s got four sides.. it''s.. it''s a <vocabulary>square</vocabulary>! Or a <vocabulary>box</vocabulary> from above maybe... But it''s definitely the kind you fill in on a graph.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8600', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%87%E7%9B%AE', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T18:37:18.293884Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.541493Z'),
(8601, 'vocabulary', '唄', '唄', 'A <kanji>shamisen song</kanji> is a <vocabulary>shamisen song</vocabulary>, yes. But did you know it''s also... a <vocabulary>song</vocabulary>?! Oh, you did? Well okay then.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8601', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%94%84', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T18:38:27.144463Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.553538Z'),
(8602, 'vocabulary', '唄う', '唄う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>Shamisen song</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to sing</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8602', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%94%84%E3%81%86', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T18:39:59.14052Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.565437Z'),
(8603, 'vocabulary', '長唄', '長唄', 'A <kanji>long</kanji> <kanji>shamisen song</kanji> is a <vocabulary>long shamisen song</vocabulary>! They''re also called <vocabulary>nagauta</vocabulary>, and they''re usually pretty epic too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8603', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%95%B7%E5%94%84', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T18:41:32.731592Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.576875Z'),
(8604, 'vocabulary', '囚人', '囚人', 'A <kanji>criminal</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> will end up as a <vocabulary>prisoner</vocabulary> or an <vocabulary>inmate</vocabulary> if they get caught! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8604', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9A%E4%BA%BA', 39, 1, '2015-07-16T18:43:38.649907Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(8605, 'vocabulary', '死刑囚', '死刑囚', 'Remember how you learned that 死刑 means "death penalty?" Well a <kanji>criminal</kanji> who has been sentenced to it is a <vocabulary>condemned criminal</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>death row convict</vocabulary>. They''re condemned to death.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8605', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%BB%E5%88%91%E5%9B%9A', 39, 1, '2015-07-16T18:46:22.595142Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:30.629503Z'),
(8606, 'vocabulary', '坑道', '坑道', 'A <kanji>pit</kanji> that''s used as a <kanji>road</kanji> is called a <vocabulary>tunnel</vocabulary>. Or a <vocabulary>mine</vocabulary>, if they''re taking valuable things out of it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8606', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9D%91%E9%81%93', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T18:49:05.261175Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.63962Z'),
(8607, 'vocabulary', '妄想', '妄想', '<kanji>Reckless</kanji> things you <kanji>imagine</kanji> are just <vocabulary>delusion</vocabulary>s. You shouldn''t dwell on them, they probably aren''t going to happen.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8607', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%84%E6%83%B3', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T18:50:00.270254Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.652298Z'),
(8608, 'vocabulary', '婿', '婿', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8608', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A9%BF', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:26:44.806765Z', NULL, '2025-12-31T21:59:48.459919Z'),
(8609, 'vocabulary', '花婿', '花婿', 'A <kanji>groom</kanji> gets a <kanji>flower</kanji> on his lapel and he''s finally a real <vocabulary>bridegroom</vocabulary>! Before that he was just waiting to be one. The flower makes it for real.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8609', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8A%B1%E5%A9%BF', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:27:32.065344Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:20:54.638732Z'),
(8610, 'vocabulary', '娘婿', '娘婿', 'Your <kanji>daughter</kanji>''s <kanji>groom</kanji> will become your <vocabulary>son in law</vocabulary>. He''s a great guy! Congratulations!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8610', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A8%98%E5%A9%BF', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:28:52.697089Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:46:26.209714Z'),
(8611, 'vocabulary', '寡婦', '寡婦', 'A <kanji>widow</kanji>ed <kanji>lady</kanji> is a <vocabulary>widow</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8611', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%A1%E5%A9%A6', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:31:53.443138Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:45:23.614781Z'),
(8612, 'vocabulary', '寡黙', '寡黙', 'Tell a <kanji>widow</kanji> to <kanji>shut up</kanji> and she''ll be <vocabulary>silent</vocabulary> for the rest of the day. That wasn''t very nice. You''re a mean person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8612', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%A1%E9%BB%99', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:33:01.218757Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.717643Z'),
(8613, 'vocabulary', '帆船', '帆船', 'A <kanji>sail</kanji> <kanji>boat</kanji> is a <vocabulary>sailboat</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8613', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%86%E8%88%B9', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:35:00.327725Z', NULL, '2025-08-25T16:46:10.132941Z'),
(8614, 'vocabulary', '帆柱', '帆柱', 'The <kanji>pillar</kanji> the <kanji>sail</kanji> is attached to is called a <vocabulary>mast</vocabulary>. Without it the sail wouldn''t do it''s job and your sailboat wouldn''t move at all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8614', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%86%E6%9F%B1', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:35:59.561117Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.74538Z'),
(8615, 'vocabulary', '廉価', '廉価', 'Buy something at <kanji>bargain</kanji> <kanji>value</kanji> and you''ll be getting it at a really <vocabulary>low price</vocabulary>. Good for you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8615', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%89%E4%BE%A1', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:36:58.562369Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:51:00.759578Z'),
(8616, 'vocabulary', '管弦楽団', '管弦楽団', 'Remember how you learned that 管弦楽 means "orchestral music?" Well the <kanji>group</kanji> that plays that music is an <vocabulary>orchestra</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8616', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AE%A1%E5%BC%A6%E6%A5%BD%E5%9B%A3', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:39:18.390293Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T18:49:45.773585Z'),
(8617, 'vocabulary', '思慕', '思慕', 'If you can''t stop <kanji>think</kanji>ing about how much you <kanji>yearn for</kanji> someone, that <vocabulary>yearning</vocabulary> can drive you crazy. Try to keep it in check, and enjoy how you can hold such <vocabulary>deep affection</vocabulary> for someone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8617', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%9D%E6%85%95', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:40:10.391073Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.800332Z'),
(8618, 'vocabulary', '慕う', '慕う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>yearn for</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to yearn for</vocabulary>. But that sounds kinda old fashioned, so you can just use <vocabulary>to adore</vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8618', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%95%E3%81%86', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:42:24.177473Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.814068Z'),
(8619, 'vocabulary', '感慨無量', '感慨無量', 'Remember how you learned that 感慨 means "strong feelings?" Well feelings so strong that you can <kanji>not</kanji> even think of a <kanji>quantity</kanji> to measure or explain how much you feel. These are really <vocabulary>deep emotion</vocabulary>s. They fill you all the way up and you can''t help but tell everyone about them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8619', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E6%85%A8%E7%84%A1%E9%87%8F', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:45:16.118894Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(8620, 'vocabulary', '感慨深い', '感慨深い', 'This is a combination of the words 感慨 meaning "strong feelings" and 深い meaning "deep." Something that makes you feel strong emotions deep down inside is <vocabulary>deeply moving</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8620', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9F%E6%85%A8%E6%B7%B1%E3%81%84', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:46:14.274078Z', NULL, '2026-01-21T06:57:42.938948Z'),
(8621, 'vocabulary', '悪戯', '悪戯', 'A <kanji>bad</kanji> kind of <kanji>play</kanji>ing is when you <vocabulary>tease</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>prank</vocabulary> someone. It''s mean and you shouldn''t do it! Depending on the context this can sometimes have a far more negative connotation and mean <vocabulary>lewd behavior</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8621', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E6%88%AF', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:47:38.14899Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.869036Z'),
(8622, 'vocabulary', '戯れ', '戯れ', 'Remember how 戯れる can mean "to play" or "to flirt?" Well this is when you <vocabulary>play flirt</vocabulary>! Or just make a <vocabulary>joke</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8622', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%AF%E3%82%8C', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:49:07.570224Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.882465Z'),
(8623, 'vocabulary', '拷問', '拷問', '<kanji>Torture</kanji> may be a <kanji>problem</kanji> but that doesn''t stop people from using <vocabulary>torture</vocabulary> to get their way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8623', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%B7%E5%95%8F', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:52:09.184022Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.895323Z'),
(8624, 'vocabulary', '某〜', '某〜', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8624', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%90%E3%80%9C', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:53:03.311684Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.906817Z'),
(8625, 'vocabulary', '桟橋', '桟橋', 'Add a <kanji>bridge</kanji> to a <kanji>jetty</kanji> and you''ll have a pretty cool <vocabulary>pier</vocabulary>. Put a ferris wheel on there and make some money!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8625', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A1%9F%E6%A9%8B', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T21:54:31.463664Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T01:26:42.255813Z'),
(8626, 'vocabulary', '殉職', '殉職', 'If you want to become a <kanji>martyr</kanji> during your <kanji>employment</kanji> you need to <vocabulary>die at your post</vocabulary>. I don''t know why you''d WANT to do that though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8626', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%89%E8%81%B7', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T22:03:24.061926Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.92922Z'),
(8627, 'vocabulary', '吸い殻', '吸い殻', 'This is a combination of the words 吸う meaning "to smoke" and 殻 meaning "shell" or "husk." The husk left over after you''re done smoking is the <vocabulary>cigarette butt</vocabulary>. Or <vocabulary>tobacco ashes</vocabulary> if you just have a pile of them.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8627', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%B8%E3%81%84%E6%AE%BB', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T22:08:47.215332Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.940369Z'),
(8628, 'vocabulary', '分泌', '分泌', '<kanji>Part</kanji> of you is starting to <kanji>secrete</kanji> some kind of <vocabulary>secretion</vocabulary>. It''s kinda gross, so maybe you should go to the doctor to check if it''s normal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8628', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%86%E6%B3%8C', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T23:05:08.903492Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.952566Z'),
(8629, 'vocabulary', '泌尿器', '泌尿器', 'The <kanji>container</kanji>s your <kanji>urine</kanji> <kanji>secrete</kanji>s into are your <vocabulary>urinary organs</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8629', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B3%8C%E5%B0%BF%E5%99%A8', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T23:06:59.727862Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.963801Z'),
(8630, 'vocabulary', '渓谷', '渓谷', 'A <kanji>valley</kanji> in a <kanji>valley</kanji> is still a <vocabulary>valley</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8630', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%93%E8%B0%B7', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T23:08:10.749412Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:43:56.588475Z'),
(8631, 'vocabulary', '渓流', '渓流', 'A <kanji>stream</kanji> that runs through a <kanji>valley</kanji> is a <vocabulary>mountain stream</vocabulary>. Valleys happen in between mountains, so just picture the river running through the middle of them in your head.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8631', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%93%E6%B5%81', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T23:08:54.636489Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:07.988627Z'),
(8632, 'vocabulary', '湧く', '湧く', 'The kanji itself means <kanji>well up</kanji>, and this is also <vocabulary>to well up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to gush out</vocabulary>.

Remember us talking about courage welling up in the kanji mnemonic? This word can also be used for that kind of figurative welling up, like of emotions, which is where the meaning <vocabulary>to arise</vocabulary> comes from.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8632', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B9%A7%E3%81%8F', 36, 1, '2015-07-16T23:10:22.064108Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.019451Z'),
(8633, 'vocabulary', '湧水', '湧水', '<kanji>Well up</kanji> <kanji>water</kanji> is <vocabulary>spring water</vocabulary>. Kind of makes sense, right? 

Note that 湧水 is a formal word generally encountered in written language.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8633', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B9%A7%E6%B0%B4', 36, 1, '2015-07-16T23:40:31.388049Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.046344Z'),
(8634, 'vocabulary', '漸く', '漸く', 'If you do something <kanji>gradually</kanji> for a long time, when you finish it gives you that <vocabulary>FINALLY</vocabulary> feeling. It took so long and you worked so hard and you''re finally done!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8634', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%B8%E3%81%8F', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T23:42:53.384663Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.057325Z'),
(8635, 'vocabulary', '濁る', '濁る', 'You already know that 濁す means "to make muddy," but this is when you''re the one getting muddy. It means <vocabulary>to become muddy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8635', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BF%81%E3%82%8B', 60, 1, '2015-07-16T23:48:39.833023Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.069459Z'),
(8636, 'vocabulary', '煩い', '煩い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>annoy</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>annoying</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8636', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%A9%E3%81%84', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:11:47.032477Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.081912Z'),
(8637, 'vocabulary', '煩雑', '煩雑', 'If you think someone is trying to <kanji>annoy</kanji> you at <kanji>random</kanji>, just pay attention and you''ll see that they have a <vocabulary>complex</vocabulary> annoying pattern they''re using on you. Once you figure that out, it won''t annoy you as much and you''ll win!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8637', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%A9%E9%9B%91', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:14:43.004797Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.096669Z'),
(8638, 'vocabulary', '煩忙', '煩忙', 'If you <kanji>annoy</kanji> someone who''s <kanji>busy</kanji> they''re still going to be <vocabulary>busy</vocabulary>. You annoying them can''t change them. Though now they''re probably busy AND annoyed. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8638', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%A9%E5%BF%99', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:16:46.700376Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.111589Z'),
(8639, 'vocabulary', '湖畔', '湖畔', 'The <kanji>lake</kanji> <kanji>shore</kanji> is the <vocabulary>lake shore</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8639', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B9%96%E7%95%94', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:17:55.602781Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.125936Z'),
(8640, 'vocabulary', '下痢', '下痢', '<kanji>Diarrhea</kanji> that goes <kanji>below</kanji> you is still <vocabulary>diarrhea</vocabulary>, unfortunately. I hope it''s going down into a toilet...', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8640', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E7%97%A2', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:19:18.484443Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(8641, 'vocabulary', '赤痢', '赤痢', '<kanji>Red</kanji> <kanji>diarrhea</kanji> means there''s blood in there and you''re probably dying of <vocabulary>dysentery</vocabulary>! Have you been playing too much Oregon Trail? Either way, get to a doctor!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8641', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B5%A4%E7%97%A2', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:20:21.040843Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.152574Z'),
(8642, 'vocabulary', '矯正', '矯正', '<kanji>Correct</kanji> something you thought was already <kanji>correct</kanji> by making a <vocabulary>correction</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8642', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%AF%E6%AD%A3', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:22:04.057431Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.167384Z'),
(8643, 'vocabulary', '硫黄', '硫黄', '<kanji>Sulfur</kanji> that''s <kanji>yellow</kanji> is still <vocabulary>sulfur</vocabulary>. It smells like rotten eggs, which are also kinda yellow.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8643', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A1%AB%E9%BB%84', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:23:36.930233Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.178317Z'),
(8644, 'vocabulary', '罷免', '罷免', 'You <kanji>quit</kanji> your job but with a pretty good <kanji>excuse</kanji> to back it up, so you get a <vocabulary>dismissal</vocabulary> instead of just quitting outright. If you''re in the army and you do this it''s called a <vocabulary>discharge</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8644', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BD%B7%E5%85%8D', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:26:11.10881Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.190403Z'),
(8645, 'vocabulary', '藍', '藍', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8645', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%97%8D', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:27:22.801702Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.201918Z'),
(8646, 'vocabulary', '藻', '藻', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8646', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%97%BB', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:28:33.118013Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.213196Z'),
(8647, 'vocabulary', '海藻', '海藻', '<kanji>Sea</kanji> <kanji>seaweed</kanji> is still <vocabulary>seaweed</vocabulary>! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8647', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B7%E8%97%BB', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:29:41.025745Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.22577Z'),
(8648, 'vocabulary', '野蛮', '野蛮', '<kanji>Barbarian</kanji>s who live in <kanji>field</kanji>s, not even in huts or houses or anything, are called <vocabulary>savage</vocabulary> by people who DO live in huts and houses. They think if you live out in a field you''re really <vocabulary>uncivilized</vocabulary>. But that''s all a matter of opinion, I guess.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8648', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8E%E8%9B%AE', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:30:49.040492Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.759312Z'),
(8649, 'vocabulary', '南蛮', '南蛮', 'From the <kanji>south</kanji> come the <kanji>barbarians</kanji> known as… the <vocabulary>southern barbarians</vocabulary>.

A long time ago this word referred to the peoples of Southeast Asia, but when European traders arrived in the south of Japan in the 1500s, they were called 南蛮 too. They were the barbarians in the south, after all. Today this word mostly refers to <vocabulary>nanban</vocabulary>-style food named after those traders. (Think fried food and tartar sauce… Yum.)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8649', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%97%E8%9B%AE', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:31:45.110022Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.759312Z'),
(8650, 'vocabulary', '蛮行', '蛮行', '<kanji>Barbarian</kanji>s <kanji>go</kanji> around and commit acts of <vocabulary>barbarism</vocabulary>! What else would they do? They''re barbarians!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8650', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9B%AE%E8%A1%8C', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:33:20.642532Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:25.759312Z'),
(8651, 'vocabulary', '謹賀新年', '謹賀新年', 'Remember how you learned that 新年 means "New Year?" Well when you say <kanji>humble</kanji> <kanji>congratulations</kanji> on the new year, you''re really saying <vocabulary>Happy New Year!</vocabulary>', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8651', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AC%B9%E8%B3%80%E6%96%B0%E5%B9%B4', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:35:44.332413Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T02:02:19.646561Z'),
(8652, 'vocabulary', '容赦なく', '容赦なく', 'Remember how 容赦 can mean "forgiveness" and "mercy?" Well add なく (basically making it NOT that), and it becomes <vocabulary>relentlessly</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>mercilessly</vocabulary>. The exact opposite of what it was before!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8652', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B9%E8%B5%A6%E3%81%AA%E3%81%8F', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:38:14.992753Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:32:56.530617Z'),
(8653, 'vocabulary', '逝去', '逝去', '<kanji>Die</kanji> in the <kanji>past</kanji> and it was <vocabulary>death</vocabulary> that you suffered from!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8653', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%9D%E5%8E%BB', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:39:21.780844Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:21:59.859898Z'),
(8654, 'vocabulary', '醜い', '醜い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>ugly</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>ugly</vocabulary> still!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8654', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%86%9C%E3%81%84', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:40:38.464632Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.317352Z'),
(8655, 'vocabulary', '醜悪', '醜悪', '<kanji>Ugly</kanji>, <kanji>bad</kanji> things are <vocabulary>ugly</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>mean</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>offensive</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8655', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%86%9C%E6%82%AA', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:41:32.383454Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.340167Z'),
(8656, 'vocabulary', '醜態', '醜態', 'Something with an <kanji>ugly</kanji> <kanji>appearance</kanji> is a <vocabulary>shameful sight</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>disgraceful behavior</vocabulary>. You feel bad just looking at it. It probably feels bad too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8656', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%86%9C%E6%85%8B', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:43:48.006982Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.363733Z'),
(8657, 'vocabulary', '醜聞', '醜聞', 'What do you <kanji>hear</kanji> that is really <kanji>ugly</kanji>? A <vocabulary>scandal</vocabulary>. Scandals are the ugliest topics for gossip.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8657', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%86%9C%E8%81%9E', 60, 1, '2015-07-17T16:44:43.593063Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:08:56.80488Z'),
(8658, 'vocabulary', '狐', '狐', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8658', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%90', 44, 1, '2015-07-17T16:46:27.161032Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.390239Z'),
(8659, 'vocabulary', '大人しい', '大人しい', 'Remember how you learned that 大人 means "adult?" Well this is the adjective version of that. The I-adjective (see how it ends in い?). What adjective do adults want to be able to use for their own kids? They want to say they are <vocabulary>obedient</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>quiet</vocabulary> kids!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8659', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E4%BA%BA%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 3, 1, '2015-08-24T17:41:32.867791Z', NULL, '2026-01-15T09:55:34.020754Z'),
(8660, 'vocabulary', '出かける', '出かける', 'You <kanji>exit</kanji> but you do it with かける added to it. Just think of that かける as you are exiting while being "cocky" about it. You do this when you want <vocabulary>to go out</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to leave</vocabulary>. You go out in a cocky way!

This word can only be used for a person going out. You can''t say a train 出かける''d.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8660', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E3%81%8B%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 4, 1, '2015-08-24T22:01:42.089056Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:57:51.618372Z'),
(8661, 'vocabulary', '大体', '大体', 'If all you can see is the <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>body</kanji> of something, then you''ll only get a <vocabulary>general</vocabulary> idea of what it is. You''ll be able to say that it''s <vocabulary>generally</vocabulary> a certain way, but that''s <vocabulary>about</vocabulary> all. You''ll get the <vocabulary>gist</vocabulary>, but no more.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8661', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E4%BD%93', 5, 1, '2015-08-24T22:08:43.106814Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(8662, 'vocabulary', '海王星', '海王星', 'The <kanji>ocean</kanji> <kanji>king</kanji>''s <kanji>star</kanji> is <vocabulary>Neptune</vocabulary>! If you know anything about Roman mythology this should be easy to remember!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8662', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B7%E7%8E%8B%E6%98%9F', 7, 1, '2015-08-24T22:21:38.756654Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T02:27:52.417356Z'),
(8663, 'vocabulary', '〜以下', '〜以下', 'Start <kanji>from</kanji> something and then go <kanji>below</kanji> it. Now you have "<vocabulary>no more than</vocabulary> ___," or, in other words, "___ <vocabulary>or less</vocabulary>!"

When used with numbers, 以下 always includes the number itself, like 十人以下 (ten or fewer people). In non-numerical contexts, it can mean "less than" in a looser sense, like お前は犬以下だ (You''re less than a dog).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8663', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E4%BB%A5%E4%B8%8B', 7, 1, '2015-08-24T22:24:12.396075Z', NULL, '2026-02-12T21:26:04.323652Z'),
(8664, 'vocabulary', '番組', '番組', 'Use a <kanji>number in a series</kanji> to <kanji>group</kanji> the things you watch and listen to. That makes them your <vocabulary>program</vocabulary>s. Your TV and radio programs, specifically. If you watch TV you''ll hear it a lot!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8664', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%AA%E7%B5%84', 8, 1, '2015-08-24T22:26:28.306935Z', NULL, '2026-04-05T19:31:59.040578Z'),
(8665, 'vocabulary', '部室', '部室', 'A <kanji>club</kanji> <kanji>room</kanji> is a <vocabulary>clubroom</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8665', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%A8%E5%AE%A4', 12, 1, '2015-08-24T22:27:54.823879Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:57:32.391485Z'),
(8666, 'vocabulary', '大学院', '大学院', 'Remember how you learned that 大学 means "college?" Well the <kanji>institution</kanji> you go to after college is <vocabulary>graduate school</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8666', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%AD%A6%E9%99%A2', 10, 1, '2015-08-24T22:29:27.08012Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(8667, 'vocabulary', '親指', '親指', 'Your <kanji>parent</kanji> <kanji>finger</kanji> is your <vocabulary>thumb</vocabulary>! It''s bigger than your other fingers, so that means it''s in charge too! This goes for the fingers on your feet too, I mean, your toes. So the parent toe is your <vocabulary>big toe</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8667', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA%E6%8C%87', 13, 1, '2015-08-24T22:36:27.059632Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.625421Z'),
(8668, 'vocabulary', '大学院生', '大学院生', 'Remember how you learned that 大学院 means "graduate school" and 大学生 means "college student?" Well smush them together and you have a  <vocabulary>graduate student</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8668', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E5%AD%A6%E9%99%A2%E7%94%9F', 12, 1, '2015-08-24T22:41:39.709225Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(8669, 'vocabulary', '意地悪', '意地悪', 'A person whose <kanji>idea</kanji>s are <kanji>ground</kanji>ed in <kanji>evil</kanji> is a <vocabulary>malicious</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>mean</vocabulary> <vocabulary>jerk</vocabulary>! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8669', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%8F%E5%9C%B0%E6%82%AA', 14, 1, '2015-08-24T22:42:59.525967Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.677422Z'),
(8670, 'vocabulary', '昼休み', '昼休み', 'Remember how you learned that 休み means "break?" When you take a break at <kanji>noon</kanji> it''s your <vocabulary>lunch break</vocabulary>! Or, if you aren''t hungry, it''s a <vocabulary>noon break</vocabulary> instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8670', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%BC%E4%BC%91%E3%81%BF', 15, 1, '2015-08-24T22:49:22.465306Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.832967Z'),
(8671, 'vocabulary', '食料品', '食料品', '<kanji>Product</kanji>s you''re paying a <kanji>fee</kanji> to <kanji>eat</kanji> later are your <vocabulary>groceries</vocabulary>. Buy them now, eat them later, yum!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8671', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9F%E6%96%99%E5%93%81', 16, 1, '2015-08-24T23:06:07.762801Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(8672, 'vocabulary', '愛情', '愛情', 'Those <kanji>love</kanji> <kanji>feeling</kanji>s you''re having mean you''re in <vocabulary>love</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8672', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%84%9B%E6%83%85', 17, 1, '2015-08-24T23:09:02.305408Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:28:57.446613Z'),
(8673, 'vocabulary', '幸い', '幸い', '<kanji>Happiness</kanji> is <vocabulary>happiness</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8673', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B8%E3%81%84', 18, 1, '2015-08-24T23:10:21.794048Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.781888Z'),
(8674, 'vocabulary', '混血', '混血', 'If you have <kanji>mixed</kanji> <kanji>blood</kanji>, some people might say you''re of <vocabulary>mixed race</vocabulary>. Own it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8674', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B7%B7%E8%A1%80', 19, 1, '2015-08-24T23:12:23.00241Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:15:00.980995Z'),
(8675, 'vocabulary', '議長', '議長', 'The person in charge of <kanji>long</kanji> <kanji>discussion</kanji>s is the <vocabulary>chairman</vocabulary>, or the <vocabulary>speaker</vocabulary>. They also say who gets to talk and for how long.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8675', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AD%B0%E9%95%B7', 20, 1, '2015-08-24T23:14:50.666901Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.830232Z'),
(8676, 'vocabulary', '楽天主義', '楽天主義', 'You know that 主義 is "-ism." If you add that to being <kanji>comfort</kanji>able like you''re in <kanji>heaven</kanji> then you have lots of <vocabulary>optimism</vocabulary>. You''re sure you''ll be okay in any situation!

This is a noun in Japanese but, depending on the context, it''s often translated into English as an adjective.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8676', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A5%BD%E5%A4%A9%E4%B8%BB%E7%BE%A9', 23, 1, '2015-08-24T23:16:43.962334Z', NULL, '2025-11-29T00:46:56.671656Z'),
(8677, 'vocabulary', '待機', '待機', 'A <kanji>wait</kanji>ing <kanji>machine</kanji> is a machine on <vocabulary>standby</vocabulary>.  This word doesn’t always apply to machines though. It could be a person who’s on standby, in which case you might also say they’re <vocabulary>on call</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8677', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%85%E6%A9%9F', 22, 1, '2015-08-24T23:36:38.075337Z', NULL, '2025-12-01T21:04:30.700843Z'),
(8678, 'vocabulary', '沢山', '沢山', '<kanji>Swamp</kanji>s and <kanji>mountain</kanji>s happen <vocabulary>a lot</vocabulary> in Japan. That''s why we use this to mean <vocabulary>many</vocabulary>! Because there are many of them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8678', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%A2%E5%B1%B1', 23, 1, '2015-08-24T23:47:38.35498Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.908636Z'),
(8679, 'vocabulary', '燃料', '燃料', 'A <kanji>material</kanji> you can <kanji>burn</kanji> is called <vocabulary>fuel</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8679', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%87%83%E6%96%99', 24, 1, '2015-08-24T23:49:05.751064Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.934891Z'),
(8680, 'vocabulary', '収める', '収める', 'When you <kanji>obtain</kanji> something you need to keep it safe, so you decide <vocabulary>to store</vocabulary> it somewhere. Then you can always go back and look at the thing you managed <vocabulary>to achieve</vocabulary> or  <vocabulary>to score</vocabulary>, and no one can take it from you.

収める means "to store" with an emphasis on placing something in their proper location. This nuance extends to convey a sense of accomplishment, such as "achieving a favorable outcome.”', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8680', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8E%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2015-08-24T23:51:33.525599Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:08.959715Z'),
(8681, 'vocabulary', '違反', '違反', 'If you''re <kanji>anti-</kanji> something in a way that''s <kanji>different</kanji> from the norm then you''ll be in <vocabulary>violation</vocabulary> of the law! The laws that the "not" different people made. Either way, those people will give you a hard time for your anti-stance against the thing they like, and say it''s a <vocabulary>transgression</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8681', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%95%E5%8F%8D', 26, 1, '2015-08-24T23:58:58.892061Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T20:26:10.479152Z'),
(8682, 'vocabulary', '理屈', '理屈', '<kanji>Reason</kanji> <kanji>yield</kanji>s to <vocabulary>logic</vocabulary> and more <vocabulary>reasoning</vocabulary>.

理屈 can be neutral, but it often has negative connotations too, like when it refers to an <vocabulary>argument</vocabulary> that contains questionable logic, pretexts, or too much pedantry.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8682', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%90%86%E5%B1%88', 27, 1, '2015-08-25T18:56:40.890603Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:09.016058Z'),
(8683, 'vocabulary', '浜辺', '浜辺', 'A <kanji>beach</kanji> <kanji>area</kanji> is just a <vocabulary>beach</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8683', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%9C%E8%BE%BA', 28, 1, '2015-08-25T19:00:43.384488Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:09.037716Z'),
(8684, 'vocabulary', '寄る', '寄る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

The kanji itself means <kanji>draw near</kanji>, so this verb version is <vocabulary>to draw near</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>to stop by</vocabulary>, since stopping by a place is like drawing near it briefly, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8684', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%84%E3%82%8B', 29, 1, '2015-08-25T19:03:53.514047Z', NULL, '2025-09-10T17:43:37.592702Z'),
(8685, 'vocabulary', '程よく', '程よく', 'If the <kanji>extent</kanji> you go to do something is properly (よく) done, then you did it <vocabulary>properly</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>well</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8685', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%8B%E3%82%88%E3%81%8F', 30, 1, '2015-08-25T19:06:14.464139Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.735781Z'),
(8686, 'vocabulary', '華道', '華道', 'The <kanji>flower</kanji> <kanji>way</kanji> (or the way of the flower) is the Japanese art of <vocabulary>flower arrangement</vocabulary>.

With such a lofty name, you know this isn''t referring to any old flower arrangement you can buy in a shop. This is for the art and practice of <vocabulary>Japanese flower arrangement</vocabulary>, also known as ikebana.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8686', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8F%AF%E9%81%93', 31, 1, '2015-08-25T19:08:20.764772Z', NULL, '2026-01-12T00:06:08.846732Z'),
(8687, 'vocabulary', '武装', '武装', 'The most important part of your <kanji>military</kanji> <kanji>attire</kanji> are your <vocabulary>arms</vocabulary> (not the ones attached to your body, your guns and stuff). Arms are what make you a real military person.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8687', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A6%E8%A3%85', 33, 1, '2015-08-25T19:46:30.871155Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:44.93434Z'),
(8688, 'vocabulary', '白熊', '白熊', 'A <kanji>white</kanji> <kanji>bear</kanji> is a <vocabulary>polar bear</vocabulary>! They''re the only white bears in the whole world!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8688', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%BD%E7%86%8A', 35, 1, '2015-08-25T20:26:27.516609Z', NULL, '2025-11-23T05:58:29.379374Z'),
(8689, 'vocabulary', '弾力', '弾力', 'If you want the <kanji>power</kanji> to stop a <kanji>bullet</kanji> you need to have just the right amount of <vocabulary>elasticity</vocabulary>. Bullets go right through rigid things, but if the material is strong and has the right amount of <vocabulary>flexibility</vocabulary>, the bullet will bounce right off!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8689', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%BE%E5%8A%9B', 37, 1, '2015-08-25T20:29:24.084748Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:33:28.795445Z'),
(8690, 'vocabulary', '控え', '控え', 'Something that can <kanji>abstain</kanji> until it''s needed makes for good <vocabulary>backup</vocabulary>. Think about it — you don''t want backup rushing in too early, right? It should abstain until the backup is actually needed.

Note that 控え isn''t used for spares and extras. It''s more for necessary backups, like a backup player in sports, a document <vocabulary>copy</vocabulary> for record keeping, or a <vocabulary>receipt stub</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8690', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%A7%E3%81%88', 39, 1, '2015-08-25T20:30:52.007626Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:09.180694Z'),
(8691, 'vocabulary', '慰謝料', '慰謝料', 'Remember how 慰謝 means "consolation?" Add <kanji>fee</kanji> to that and you get <vocabulary>compensation</vocabulary>. Or <vocabulary>consolation money</vocabulary>, depending on the situation.

慰謝料 refers to compensation for mental or emotional distress. It covers damages for non-physical harm, such as emotional pain in divorce settlements, affairs, or accidents, where the victim seeks financial redress for their distress.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8691', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%B0%E8%AC%9D%E6%96%99', 43, 1, '2015-08-25T20:36:15.515129Z', NULL, '2025-12-08T22:59:33.248971Z'),
(8692, 'vocabulary', '木綿', '木綿', '<kanji>Cotton</kanji> that comes off a <kanji>tree</kanji> is still <vocabulary>cotton</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8692', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%A8%E7%B6%BF', 46, 1, '2015-08-25T20:38:10.873574Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:09.208044Z'),
(8693, 'vocabulary', '左右', '左右', 'Put <kanji>left</kanji> and <kanji>right</kanji> together and you end up with <vocabulary>left and right</vocabulary>! Just make sure you say them in the right order!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8693', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B7%A6%E5%8F%B3', 2, 2, '2015-10-26T19:19:20.145014Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:09.23718Z'),
(8694, 'vocabulary', '大気', '大気', 'If there is a <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>energy</kanji> it''s going to fill up the <vocabulary>atmosphere</vocabulary>! Think about big energies you feel in the atmosphere: thunderstorms, tornadoes, the aurora borealis. Just remember, this is the atmosphere in the sky, not the atmosphere in a fancy restaurant.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8694', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E6%B0%97', 4, 1, '2015-10-26T19:25:16.260584Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(8695, 'vocabulary', '町民', '町民', '<kanji>Town</kanji> <kanji>peoples</kanji> are the <vocabulary>townspeople</vocabulary>! This can also be singular, so a <vocabulary>townsperson</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8695', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%BA%E6%B0%91', 7, 1, '2015-10-26T19:30:26.564818Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.394537Z'),
(8696, 'vocabulary', '文学', '文学', 'If you <kanji>study</kanji> <kanji>writing</kanji>, it''s called <vocabulary>literature</vocabulary>. You''ll get to study lots of things other people wrote before you!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8696', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%87%E5%AD%A6', 5, 1, '2015-10-26T19:33:23.485673Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:36.012215Z'),
(8697, 'vocabulary', '知る', '知る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>know</kanji> so the verb version means <vocabulary>to know</vocabulary>. It can additionally refer to how one comes to know something, so it also means <vocabulary>to learn</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to find out</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8697', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%A5%E3%82%8B', 6, 1, '2015-10-26T19:35:53.594042Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.445683Z'),
(8698, 'vocabulary', '来週', '来週', 'The <kanji>next</kanji> <kanji>week</kanji> is the <vocabulary>next week</vocabulary>. It isn''t last week or this week, it''s the next one!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8698', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%A5%E9%80%B1', 7, 1, '2015-10-26T19:37:39.688708Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(8699, 'vocabulary', '科目', '科目', 'If you''ve got an <kanji>eye</kanji> on a certain <kanji>course</kanji>, it''s a <vocabulary>subject</vocabulary> you really want to take. Maybe you''re in school and that means you''ll be taking a lot of <vocabulary>course</vocabulary>s. Maybe you''re a teacher and you''re planning out your <vocabulary>curriculum</vocabulary>. Either way, you have your eye on the course-prize.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8699', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%91%E7%9B%AE', 8, 1, '2015-10-26T19:38:52.726009Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.654103Z'),
(8700, 'vocabulary', '合図', '合図', 'A <kanji>diagram</kanji> everyone <kanji>join</kanji>s together to see is a <vocabulary>signal</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>sign</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8700', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%88%E5%9B%B3', 14, 1, '2015-10-26T19:41:30.934683Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:09.416696Z'),
(8701, 'vocabulary', '生物', '生物', 'A <kanji>life</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji> is a <vocabulary>living thing</vocabulary>, a <vocabulary>creature</vocabulary>, or an <vocabulary>organism</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8701', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E7%89%A9', 9, 1, '2015-10-26T19:43:31.906565Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(8702, 'vocabulary', '家事', '家事', 'You know 事 means "occurrence," but it also means "matter." So you can think of a <kanji>house</kanji> <kanji>matter</kanji> as <vocabulary>housework</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>chores</vocabulary>. Housework and chores are really just house matters that you have to deal with, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8702', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%B6%E4%BA%8B', 9, 1, '2015-10-26T19:45:06.227696Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T17:06:15.262383Z'),
(8703, 'vocabulary', '今朝', '今朝', '<kanji>Now</kanji> <kanji>morning</kanji> is just another way of saying <vocabulary>this morning</vocabulary>. It''s the closest morning to right now!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8703', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%8A%E6%9C%9D', 10, 1, '2015-10-26T21:18:40.246978Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.51055Z'),
(8704, 'vocabulary', '港', '港', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8704', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%AF', 12, 1, '2015-10-26T21:20:43.738796Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:09.519919Z'),
(8705, 'vocabulary', '神様', '神様', '<kanji>God</kanji> (<kanji>formal name ender</kanji>) is <vocabulary>God</vocabulary>! Or the <vocabulary>gods</vocabulary>, if you''re talking to more than one. You have to be polite to them, so you add that ender on there. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8705', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%9E%E6%A7%98', 13, 1, '2015-10-26T21:23:41.4643Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:09.546383Z'),
(8706, 'vocabulary', '文化祭', '文化祭', 'Remember how 文化 means "culture?" Well add <kanji>festival</kanji> to it and you end up with a <vocabulary>school festival</vocabulary> where students showcase a school''s cultural activities!

文化祭 literally means a <vocabulary>culture festival</vocabulary>, but 文化 here means school culture — exhibits and productions related to the arts, science, and even food. 文化祭 are usually held in high schools and universities, where students take the lead in running them. Expect fun activities, classrooms turned into cafés or haunted houses, and lots of stage performances!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8706', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E7%A5%AD', 12, 1, '2015-10-26T21:24:53.177227Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T07:09:11.367809Z'),
(8707, 'vocabulary', '晴れ', '晴れ', 'If you say something is going to <kanji>clear up</kanji>, you''re usually talking about the weather. That makes this <vocabulary>clear weather</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>sunny</vocabulary>.

Maybe because of those connotations, 晴れ can also be used to describe special occasions that are usually public. For example, a wedding or a big recital might be called a 晴れの場 (special occasion). Similarly, the special clothing you wear to such events is called 晴れ着 (formal attire).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8707', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%99%B4%E3%82%8C', 15, 1, '2015-10-26T21:26:13.540076Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:00:51.825704Z'),
(8708, 'vocabulary', '治安', '治安', 'If you <kanji>rule</kanji> so that your people can <kanji>relax</kanji>, then there will be good <vocabulary>public order</vocabulary>. You don''t want the people you rule being upset or stressed out, you want <vocabulary>public safety</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>public peace</vocabulary> for all!

治安 refers to the overall state of security of a place, especially in terms of crime and social stability. You''ll hear it in phrases like 治安がいい (the area is safe) or 治安が悪い (the area has poor public safety), usually in reference to particular cities or neighborhoods.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8708', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%BB%E5%AE%89', 16, 1, '2015-10-26T21:27:10.751399Z', NULL, '2026-03-06T01:31:28.007963Z'),
(8709, 'vocabulary', '一昨日', '一昨日', 'Remember how you learned that 昨日 means "yesterday?" Well <kanji>one</kanji> day <kanji>previous</kanji> to that <kanji>day</kanji> is the <vocabulary>day before last</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8709', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%98%A8%E6%97%A5', 17, 1, '2015-10-26T21:28:30.482602Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:56:10.241947Z'),
(8710, 'vocabulary', '禁じる', '禁じる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>prohibition</kanji> so the verb version means <vocabulary>to prohibit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to forbid</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8710', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A6%81%E3%81%98%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2015-10-26T21:30:37.201702Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:09.668449Z'),
(8711, 'vocabulary', '高等学校', '高等学校', 'Remember when you learned that 高等 means "high class" and 学校 means "school?" Well put them together and you have <vocabulary>senior high school</vocabulary>! It''s the most high class school you''ve got (unless you go to university, but that''s considered to be separate). 

Fun fact: 高校 is the abbreviation of this word. Neat, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8711', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E7%AD%89%E5%AD%A6%E6%A0%A1', 20, 1, '2015-10-26T21:31:41.27587Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:09.697382Z'),
(8712, 'vocabulary', '目覚める', '目覚める', 'If your <kanji>eye</kanji>s are <kanji>awake</kanji> then you''re going <vocabulary>to wake up</vocabulary>. Your eyes are usually the first thing to awaken when you''re waking up, you know? 

This word can also mean <vocabulary>to awaken</vocabulary> but it''s like when you to come to your senses, not literally waking up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8712', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%AE%E8%A6%9A%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2015-10-26T21:33:25.205404Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:18:51.662535Z'),
(8713, 'vocabulary', '有り難う', '有り難う', 'Some people <kanji>have</kanji> a <kanji>difficult</kanji> time saying <vocabulary>thank you</vocabulary>. But not you now that you know how!

This is often written in kana, so you don''t need to use the kanji when writing. But you''ll sometimes see the kanji used so it''s important to be able to recognize them! Did you know this is an abbreviation of 有難うございます? You can also spell it like this: 有難う.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8713', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%89%E3%82%8A%E9%9B%A3%E3%81%86', 22, 1, '2015-10-26T21:36:11.261784Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:09.757661Z'),
(8714, 'vocabulary', '現に', '現に', 'The <kanji>present time</kanji> is the time you''re <vocabulary>actually</vocabulary> in. You aren''t <vocabulary>really</vocabulary> in any other time, because that''s not how time works!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8714', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%BE%E3%81%AB', 23, 1, '2015-10-26T21:37:56.410978Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:09.779432Z'),
(8715, 'vocabulary', '両腕', '両腕', '<kanji>Both</kanji> <kanji>arm</kanji>s are <vocabulary>both arms</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8715', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%A1%E8%85%95', 24, 1, '2015-10-26T21:39:05.902894Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:45:39.570399Z'),
(8716, 'vocabulary', '増す', '増す', 'It''s another verb with the kanji 増! This one means <vocabulary>to increase</vocabulary> too! But!

Just like 増える, this is when something is just increasing on its own. You can use this word to talk about an increase in number, quantity, or degree, but it’s more literary and less common than 増える, except when referring to an increase in degree.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8716', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A2%97%E3%81%99', 25, 1, '2015-10-26T21:42:50.64161Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:09.834041Z'),
(8717, 'vocabulary', '右腕', '右腕', 'Your <kanji>right</kanji> <kanji>arm</kanji> is your <vocabulary>right arm</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8717', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B3%E8%85%95', 26, 1, '2015-10-26T21:44:35.026219Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:46.453718Z'),
(8718, 'vocabulary', '胃', '胃', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.

Just keep in mind 胃 is specifically for the internal organ "stomach," not for the general abdominal area "stomach."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8718', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%83', 27, 1, '2015-10-26T21:46:39.620281Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:42:06.30193Z'),
(8719, 'vocabulary', '何故なら', '何故なら', 'This is like saying "when it comes to the 何故," which means you''re giving a reason, making this <vocabulary>because</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>the reason is</vocabulary>, followed by… whatever you wanna say! 

Note that this is a quite formal way to say "because," so you''ll mostly encounter this in writing or formal speech, and almost always at the beginning of a sentence in Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8719', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%95%E6%95%85%E3%81%AA%E3%82%89', 28, 1, '2015-10-26T21:47:29.194988Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T01:35:19.367749Z'),
(8720, 'vocabulary', '迷い', '迷い', 'Remember how you learned that 迷う means "to go astray" or "to get lost?" Well, when you feel like you''ve gone astray or gotten lost, you might act with <vocabulary>hesitation</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>indecision</vocabulary>. You suddenly have <vocabulary>doubt</vocabulary> about where to go or what to do next.

While 迷う can mean getting lost either physically or mentally, 迷い most often conveys an abstract sense of inner conflict or uncertainty, especially in decision making.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8720', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%B7%E3%81%84', 29, 1, '2015-10-26T21:49:14.744144Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T16:34:07.557917Z'),
(8721, 'vocabulary', '遊び', '遊び', 'This is the noun version of the kanji <kanji>play</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>play</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>playing</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8721', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8A%E3%81%B3', 32, 1, '2015-10-26T21:50:21.607626Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T18:47:45.62769Z'),
(8722, 'vocabulary', '華美', '華美', 'If something has <kanji>showy</kanji> <kanji>beauty</kanji> then it''s going a little overboard and is going to come across as <vocabulary>gaudy</vocabulary> <vocabulary>ostentation</vocabulary>. Too much is too much.

Compared to the similar word 派手 (flashy), 華美 suggests a more refined kind of <vocabulary>splendor</vocabulary> — luxurious and elegant, but a tad excessive. It''s a formal word, generally used in writing to describe clothing or decor that''s beautiful but verges on being showy.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8722', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8F%AF%E7%BE%8E', 31, 1, '2015-10-26T21:51:28.359422Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:47:05.234852Z'),
(8723, 'vocabulary', '火照る', '火照る', 'If <kanji>fire</kanji> <kanji>illuminate</kanji>s your face, you''re going <vocabulary>to feel hot</vocabulary>. Sometimes you start <vocabulary>to flush</vocabulary> when you''re embarrassed and it just feels like there is fire making your face light up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8723', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%AB%E7%85%A7%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2015-10-26T21:52:56.090946Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:10.045527Z'),
(8724, 'vocabulary', '異常', '異常', 'When something <kanji>differ</kanji>s from <kanji>normal</kanji> it''s called <vocabulary>abnormal</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8724', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%B0%E5%B8%B8', 33, 1, '2015-10-26T21:55:25.78325Z', NULL, '2025-10-21T00:18:11.610559Z'),
(8725, 'vocabulary', '傷む', '傷む', 'The kanji means <kanji>wound</kanji>, and this verb is intransitive, so it means <vocabulary>to be damaged</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to go bad</vocabulary>. After all, fruit with a wound on it is going bad, isn''t it?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8725', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%82%B7%E3%82%80', 34, 1, '2015-10-26T21:57:49.693643Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:10.085691Z'),
(8726, 'vocabulary', '石油', '石油', '<kanji>Oil</kanji> that has <kanji>stone</kanji>s in it is from the ground. It''s not olive oil, it''s <vocabulary>petroleum</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8726', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%B3%E6%B2%B9', 35, 1, '2015-10-26T21:59:14.179479Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:10.099016Z'),
(8727, 'vocabulary', '依然', '依然', 'If you have a <kanji>dependent</kanji> <kanji>nature</kanji>, then even if you go off on your own, you''re <vocabulary>still</vocabulary> going to ask for help. Dependent people still need help because that''s just how they are.

Note that you need to add と to the end of this word to make it an adverb, and たる to make it an adjective!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8727', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BE%9D%E7%84%B6', 39, 1, '2015-10-26T22:01:18.184034Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:10.112472Z'),
(8728, 'vocabulary', '支払い', '支払い', 'The <kanji>pay</kanji> that <kanji>support</kanji>s you is made in <vocabulary>payment</vocabulary>s. Whether you make payments to keep your house or your car, or you get payments from your boss, they keep you going.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8728', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%AF%E6%89%95%E3%81%84', 16, 1, '2015-10-26T22:02:15.176672Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.782042Z'),
(8729, 'vocabulary', '奥底', '奥底', 'Something is in the <kanji>interior</kanji> AND the <kanji>bottom</kanji>? Why, it''s in the very <vocabulary>depths</vocabulary>! And if it''s in the interior and bottom of you, then it''s at the <vocabulary>bottom</vocabulary> of your heart!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8729', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%A5%E5%BA%95', 38, 1, '2015-10-26T22:03:51.159898Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:10.150533Z'),
(8730, 'vocabulary', '〜房', '〜房', 'Remember when you learned 房? Well it can be used as a counter for clusters of fruit. Like a <vocabulary>section</vocabulary> of an orange or a <vocabulary>bunch</vocabulary> of grapes! Yum!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8730', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%88%BF', 40, 1, '2015-10-26T22:05:28.45581Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:10.164437Z'),
(8731, 'vocabulary', '渋々', '渋々', 'If you bite into something <kanji>bitter</kanji> (twice!) you''ll take your next bite <vocabulary>reluctantly</vocabulary>. Because who would want that much of something bitter? Not you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8731', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%8B%E3%80%85', 41, 1, '2015-10-26T22:09:43.462002Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(8732, 'vocabulary', '露', '露', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8732', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9C%B2', 43, 1, '2015-10-26T22:11:02.583839Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:10.190122Z'),
(8733, 'vocabulary', '入力', '入力', '<kanji>Enter</kanji> something with all your <kanji>strength</kanji> and you will look down to see that what you''re doing is <vocabulary>input</vocabulary>ting data. You''re inputting it right now! Into this site! The answers you <vocabulary>enter</vocabulary> into WaniKani with all your strength can be said with this word. Wow. Full circle already, and you''re only on level 2. Look at you go!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8733', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%A5%E5%8A%9B', 2, 2, '2016-04-05T17:49:51.283644Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(8734, 'vocabulary', '戸', '戸', 'A <kanji>door</kanji> is a <vocabulary>door</vocabulary> — the kanji and the word are exactly the same.

戸 can refer to any kind of door, but it''s more commonly used for <vocabulary>sliding door</vocabulary>s, since those are the traditional style of doors in Japan. For Western-style or high-tech doors, people tend to just say ドア.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8734', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%B8', 3, 1, '2016-04-05T17:53:21.308855Z', NULL, '2026-01-18T18:00:24.57903Z'),
(8735, 'vocabulary', '女の人', '女の人', 'A <kanji>woman</kanji> <kanji>person</kanji> is a <vocabulary>woman</vocabulary>.

The の particle here means the first noun is modifying the second one, so you can probably see why 女の人 (a woman person) really just means woman! It''s more polite than saying 女 by itself, so that''s all you have to remember. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8735', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A5%B3%E3%81%AE%E4%BA%BA', 3, 1, '2016-04-05T17:59:10.479594Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(8736, 'vocabulary', '本来', '本来', 'You want to talk about the <kanji>origin</kanji> where something <kanji>come</kanji>s from. That''s where it is from <vocabulary>originally</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8736', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E6%9D%A5', 5, 1, '2016-04-05T18:03:44.361736Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:40:50.823694Z'),
(8737, 'vocabulary', '手首', '手首', 'Your <kanji>hand</kanji>''s <kanji>neck</kanji> is your <vocabulary>wrist</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8737', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E9%A6%96', 6, 1, '2016-04-05T18:06:54.738753Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.550282Z'),
(8738, 'vocabulary', '光る', '光る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>light</kanji> or <kanji>shine</kanji>, so the verb is <vocabulary>to shine</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to flash</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to glitter</vocabulary>. Just imagine what happens when something emits light!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8738', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%89%E3%82%8B', 7, 1, '2016-04-05T18:10:01.298504Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.425176Z'),
(8739, 'vocabulary', '数年', '数年', 'When a <kanji>number</kanji> of <kanji>year</kanji>s have passed you say that <vocabulary>several years</vocabulary> have passed! You don''t want to say how many years, so just say several years.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8739', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%B0%E5%B9%B4', 8, 1, '2016-04-05T18:13:39.537297Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.668449Z'),
(8740, 'vocabulary', '絵文字', '絵文字', 'When you use a <kanji>picture</kanji> to replace your <kanji>letter</kanji>s to type, you''re using <vocabulary>emoji</vocabulary>! Those are the faces you send when you text or email. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8740', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B5%E6%96%87%E5%AD%97', 8, 1, '2016-04-05T18:27:05.987756Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.680153Z'),
(8741, 'vocabulary', '部首', '部首', 'Picture the <kanji>part</kanji> of your body that makes up your <kanji>neck</kanji>. Are you picturing it? That''s a building block that makes up your body. A really important one. If you didn''t have that neck, you wouldn''t look like a person at all. Sound familiar? It should! Because that part of your neck is a <vocabulary>radical</vocabulary>! Just like part of your neck is a building block of your body, the radical is a building block of kanji. Get it? I sure hope so!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8741', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%83%A8%E9%A6%96', 10, 1, '2016-04-05T18:33:22.312414Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.427704Z'),
(8742, 'vocabulary', '近所', '近所', 'The <kanji>place</kanji> you live <kanji>near</kanji> is your <vocabulary>neighborhood</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8742', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BF%91%E6%89%80', 10, 1, '2016-04-05T18:35:57.730175Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.529764Z'),
(8743, 'vocabulary', '足し算', '足し算', 'Remember how 足す means "to add?" Add <kanji>calculate</kanji> to that and you''ll have <vocabulary>addition</vocabulary>! It''s the type of calculation you''re doing when you add.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8743', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B6%B3%E3%81%97%E7%AE%97', 10, 1, '2016-04-05T18:37:47.534406Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T15:37:55.121141Z'),
(8744, 'vocabulary', '引き算', '引き算', 'Remember how 引く means "to pull" and "to withdraw"? Well, if you <kanji>calculate</kanji> by pulling or withdrawing one number out of another, you''re doing <vocabulary>subtraction</vocabulary>. Kinda like how your bank balance gets lower every time you subtract (withdraw) money from it, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8744', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%95%E3%81%8D%E7%AE%97', 10, 1, '2016-04-05T18:43:22.630567Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T15:37:55.121141Z'),
(8745, 'vocabulary', '生物学', '生物学', 'Remember how 生物 means "living things?" Well when you <kanji>study</kanji> living things you''re studying <vocabulary>biology</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8745', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E7%89%A9%E5%AD%A6', 11, 1, '2016-04-05T18:48:33.388701Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:55.709853Z'),
(8746, 'vocabulary', '指先', '指先', 'You know that <kanji>previous</kanji> part of your <kanji>finger</kanji>? The part I just chopped off? Well that was your <vocabulary>fingertip</vocabulary>! I only had time to get the tip. It used to be on your finger and now it''s right here, in my hand. Sorry, I guess. Oh, but don''t worry, I''ll even things out and get the tip of your toe real quick too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8746', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%87%E5%85%88', 13, 1, '2016-04-05T18:51:24.74584Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:27:12.714404Z'),
(8747, 'vocabulary', '湯', '湯', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8747', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B9%AF', 12, 1, '2016-04-05T18:54:13.788091Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:10.635285Z'),
(8748, 'vocabulary', '活動', '活動', 'The <kanji>lively</kanji> <kanji>move</kanji>s you make are your <vocabulary>activity</vocabulary>.

活動 includes all kinds of activities, including nature activities, club activities, economic activity, and so on!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8748', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%BB%E5%8B%95', 12, 1, '2016-04-05T18:56:30.785617Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:10.663263Z'),
(8749, 'vocabulary', '本能', '本能', 'The <kanji>abilities</kanji> you <kanji>origin</kanji>ally start out with are <vocabulary>instinct</vocabulary>s. Animals have lots of them because they aren''t very good at learning. They have to start out knowing them from the start.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8749', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AC%E8%83%BD', 14, 1, '2016-04-05T18:58:05.328034Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T21:59:59.512416Z'),
(8750, 'vocabulary', '運動会', '運動会', 'Remember how 運動 means "exercise?" When you <kanji>meet</kanji> with a bunch of other people to exercise it''s called <vocabulary>Sports Day</vocabulary>! This is a day at schools in Japan where everybody does physical stuff together! It''s fun and also very tiring! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8750', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%8B%E5%8B%95%E4%BC%9A', 14, 1, '2016-04-05T19:00:22.882744Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:10.720863Z'),
(8751, 'vocabulary', '昨年', '昨年', '<kanji>Yesterday</kanji>''s <kanji>year</kanji> was actually <vocabulary>last year</vocabulary>. Today must be New Years then!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8751', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%A8%E5%B9%B4', 15, 1, '2016-04-05T19:02:49.705875Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.856698Z'),
(8752, 'vocabulary', '資格', '資格', 'The credible <kanji>resources</kanji> that determine your professional <kanji>status</kanji> are your <vocabulary>qualifications</vocabulary>. You need some good ones if you want to get into a good school or land a sweet job.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8752', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%87%E6%A0%BC', 21, 1, '2016-04-05T19:04:23.991294Z', NULL, '2025-11-14T21:58:04.430445Z'),
(8753, 'vocabulary', 'お土産', 'お土産', 'Are you visiting a neat place? Grab some <kanji>dirt</kanji> and bring it back with you. Then pretend to <kanji>give birth</kanji> to it in front of your friends. That dirt is now a special <vocabulary>souvenir</vocabulary>! It''s the best <vocabulary>present</vocabulary> you could ever give someone!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8753', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E5%9C%9F%E7%94%A3', 22, 1, '2016-04-05T19:07:41.741946Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:10.799781Z'),
(8754, 'vocabulary', '割り算', '割り算', '<kanji>Divide</kanji> and <kanji>calculate</kanji>? Why, that''s just <vocabulary>division</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8754', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%89%B2%E3%82%8A%E7%AE%97', 23, 1, '2016-04-05T19:09:35.56305Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:40:20.776264Z'),
(8755, 'vocabulary', '輸送', '輸送', 'You want to <kanji>transport</kanji> something by <kanji>send</kanji>ing it? Well, that''s <vocabulary>transportation</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8755', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BC%B8%E9%80%81', 24, 1, '2016-04-05T19:13:42.232598Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:26:48.812324Z'),
(8756, 'vocabulary', '掛け算', '掛け算', 'If you try to <kanji>calculate</kanji> something, but instead you feel the urge to <kanji>hang</kanji> your teacher, you know you''re doing <vocabulary>multiplication</vocabulary>. It''s hard, guys. Like really hard. The hardest kind of math of them all. But you can do it! No one needs to get hanged. And don''t hang your head in defeat either. Multiply those numbers like a boss! Hang the numbers instead!!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8756', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8E%9B%E3%81%91%E7%AE%97', 25, 1, '2016-04-05T19:14:44.833563Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T15:37:55.121141Z'),
(8757, 'vocabulary', '間違える', '間違える', 'Remember how 間違い means "a mistake?" Well this is the verb version. It means <vocabulary>to make a mistake</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to mistake</vocabulary>.

This verb is transitive. That means that it takes a direct object. Bear in mind that this one is a bit tricky, though, because although it''s transitive in Japanese, the English translation will sometimes be intransitive.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8757', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%93%E9%81%95%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 28, 1, '2016-04-05T19:21:33.861373Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:10.908616Z'),
(8758, 'vocabulary', '移動', '移動', '<kanji>Shift</kanji> and <kanji>move</kanji> to create <vocabulary>movement</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8758', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A7%BB%E5%8B%95', 28, 1, '2016-04-05T19:22:58.016664Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:10.944205Z'),
(8759, 'vocabulary', '捜索', '捜索', 'A <kanji>search</kanji> <kanji>search</kanji> is a more intense <vocabulary>search</vocabulary>, like the official <vocabulary>investigation</vocabulary> for a missing person.

捜索 is a formal term often used by authorities or in news reports. It refers to an official search for a missing person or thing, and is commonly used in police investigations, rescue missions, or other organized searches.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8759', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%9C%E7%B4%A2', 29, 1, '2016-04-05T19:25:48.006978Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:20:14.847093Z'),
(8760, 'vocabulary', '背広', '背広', 'If your <kanji>back</kanji> is <kanji>wide</kanji>, you need a nice <vocabulary>business suit</vocabulary> to cover it up when you go to work. Otherwise you''ll look completely out of place sitting at a desk like a giant, wide-backed wrestler. Is that what you want to look like? Of course not! You need to go out and buy a fancy suit to cover your wide back up right now!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8760', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%83%8C%E5%BA%83', 32, 1, '2016-04-05T19:27:37.318375Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:11.002349Z'),
(8800, 'vocabulary', '札', '札', 'This word is almost the same as the kanji, but only half of it. It means <vocabulary>tag</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>amulet</vocabulary>. They''re about the same shape and size as money, but you can''t buy a house with them (...probably).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8800', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%AD', 9, 1, '2018-04-18T22:09:50.306891Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.672369Z'),
(8801, 'vocabulary', '以後', '以後', '<kanji>From</kanji> <kanji>after</kanji>, just means <vocabulary>after this</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>from now on</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8801', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%A5%E5%BE%8C', 7, 1, '2018-04-18T22:39:58.294147Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.436668Z'),
(8802, 'vocabulary', '使用', '使用', 'Something you <kanji>use</kanji> that has a <kanji>use</kanji> has... well, <vocabulary>use</vocabulary>. You can even use it for practical <vocabulary>application</vocabulary>s.

使用 has a slightly more formal tone compared to 使う, and it’s preferred in situations that require a more precise and less casual choice of words.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8802', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BD%BF%E7%94%A8', 9, 1, '2018-04-18T22:43:35.90594Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.811063Z'),
(8803, 'vocabulary', '番号札', '番号札', 'You know 番号 means "number" so add a <kanji>tag</kanji> to it and you have a <vocabulary>numbered tag</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8803', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%95%AA%E5%8F%B7%E6%9C%AD', 10, 1, '2018-04-18T22:46:53.951262Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.577339Z'),
(8804, 'vocabulary', '特選', '特選', 'If something is <kanji>special</kanji> you <kanji>choose</kanji> it. That makes it a <vocabulary>special selection</vocabulary> of yours and it gets a <vocabulary>special prize</vocabulary> that only you could give!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8804', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%89%B9%E9%81%B8', 13, 1, '2018-04-18T22:56:05.183971Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:11.174363Z'),
(8805, 'vocabulary', '人参', '人参', 'What do you use to get a <kanji>person</kanji> to <kanji>participate</kanji> in something? A <vocabulary>carrot</vocabulary>, of course. You''ve heard of "the carrot and the stick," right? So next time you want a person to participate, offer them a carrot.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8805', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E5%8F%82', 14, 1, '2018-04-18T23:06:57.792326Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(8806, 'vocabulary', '荷札', '荷札', 'A <kanji>luggage</kanji> <kanji>tag</kanji> is a <vocabulary>tag</vocabulary> you put on luggage. It''s also a <vocabulary>label</vocabulary> you can put on a package before you send it, like a <vocabulary>shipping label</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8806', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8D%B7%E6%9C%AD', 16, 1, '2018-04-18T23:09:30.200414Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.795933Z'),
(8807, 'vocabulary', '品物', '品物', 'A <kanji>product</kanji> <kanji>thing</kanji> is any type of general <vocabulary>goods</vocabulary>. It''s about as specific as you can get when you''re talking about <vocabulary>thing</vocabulary>s like this.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8807', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%93%81%E7%89%A9', 18, 1, '2018-04-18T23:11:20.318705Z', NULL, '2026-02-28T02:47:52.4482Z'),
(8808, 'vocabulary', '値札', '値札', 'A <kanji>price</kanji> <kanji>tag</kanji> is... a <vocabulary>price tag</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8808', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%80%A4%E6%9C%AD', 24, 1, '2018-04-18T23:13:27.299572Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:09:02.854992Z'),
(8809, 'vocabulary', '優れる', '優れる', 'This is the verb version of the kanji <kanji>superior</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>to surpass</vocabulary>. Someone who is superior is always going to surpass and <vocabulary>to excel</vocabulary> beyond others. That''s what makes them so superior in the first place!

This is a formal word that is often used in writing or formal speech. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8809', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%84%AA%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2018-04-18T23:14:59.116036Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T17:21:38.163479Z'),
(8810, 'vocabulary', '以降', '以降', 'Everything that happened <kanji>since</kanji> you <kanji>descend</kanji>ed some stairs, is <vocabulary>hereafter</vocabulary>. It''s all <vocabulary>after</vocabulary> the stairs thing you did, <vocabulary>since</vocabulary> you did it already.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8810', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%A5%E9%99%8D', 34, 1, '2018-04-18T23:16:32.497521Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:11.32195Z'),
(8811, 'vocabulary', '御札', '御札', 'You learned another vocabulary word 札 as "amulet," but this is a special <kanji>honorable</kanji> <vocabulary>amulet</vocabulary>. It''s a <vocabulary>talisman</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8811', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%A1%E6%9C%AD', 43, 1, '2018-04-18T23:18:56.307748Z', NULL, '2025-08-25T16:30:28.632013Z'),
(8812, 'vocabulary', '辛口', '辛口', 'If you put something <kanji>spicy</kanji> in your <kanji>mouth</kanji> you''ll end up with a <vocabulary>spicy taste</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>dry taste</vocabulary> in there. Spice sucks up all your mouth moistness.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8812', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BE%9B%E5%8F%A3', 9, 1, '2018-04-18T23:22:36.023886Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.262434Z'),
(8813, 'vocabulary', '洗濯する', '洗濯する', '<kanji>Wash</kanji> <kanji>wash</kanji> <vocabulary>to wash</vocabulary> your clothes. You wash wash your laundry. Hmm wash wash sounds like the sound my laundry machine makes when it''s washing my underpants.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8813', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B4%97%E6%BF%AF%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 51, 1, '2018-04-18T23:24:28.982694Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T19:57:04.726064Z'),
(8814, 'vocabulary', '投手', '投手', 'You already know that 手 means "hand," but it can also mean "doer," as in someone who performs a particular action or task. Why? Because we use our hands to do things. And when it comes to <kanji>throw</kanji>ing, the <kanji>doer</kanji> is the <vocabulary>pitcher</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8814', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%95%E6%89%8B', 8, 1, '2018-11-15T18:37:28.774907Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:31:50.716876Z'),
(8815, 'vocabulary', '伝記', '伝記', 'If you want to <kanji>transmit</kanji> the story of someone''s life, you have to <kanji>write down</kanji> all of it in a <vocabulary>biography</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8815', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BC%9D%E8%A8%98', 11, 1, '2018-11-15T18:48:00.865665Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:11.441529Z'),
(8816, 'vocabulary', '祭日', '祭日', 'A <kanji>festival</kanji> <kanji>day</kanji> literally means "festival day," as in a day for Shinto festivals or rituals. These days, though, it really just means <vocabulary>holiday</vocabulary>.

Usually this will be a <vocabulary>national holiday</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>public holiday</vocabulary>, since some Japanese public holidays are based on Shinto festival days. 祭日 was the official term for public holidays prior to World War II, but today 祝日 is used in its place. Because of this, it sounds a little old-fashioned now, but you may still encounter it occasionally.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8816', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A5%AD%E6%97%A5', 33, 1, '2018-11-15T18:55:44.651648Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T06:55:27.635604Z'),
(8817, 'vocabulary', '着々', '着々', 'Something you <kanji>wear</kanji>, and <kanji>wear</kanji> again, is something you <vocabulary>steadily</vocabulary> wear. You do it so often, it''s reliable. Also, if you wear something this much it''s steadily wearing away too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8817', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9D%80%E3%80%85', 12, 1, '2018-11-15T18:59:06.992844Z', NULL, '2026-02-27T03:26:37.001449Z'),
(8818, 'vocabulary', '遠足', '遠足', '<kanji>Far</kanji> <kanji>feet</kanji> are feet that have gone on long <vocabulary>excursions</vocabulary>. These feet have been on some <vocabulary>trip</vocabulary>s, man. They''ve seen some things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8818', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%A0%E8%B6%B3', 16, 1, '2018-11-15T19:09:30.743467Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:32.808527Z'),
(8841, 'vocabulary', '匂い', '匂い', 'This the noun version of the kanji for <kanji>smell</kanji>. It also means <vocabulary>smell</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>scent</vocabulary>!

Although 匂う often implies something smells unpleasant, 匂い is more neutral and can refer to any kind of smell.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8841', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%82%E3%81%84', 32, 1, '2019-04-27T00:42:44.805333Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:11.560018Z'),
(8842, 'vocabulary', '匂う', '匂う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>smell</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to smell</vocabulary>.

Although 匂う is technically a neutral term, it often means <vocabulary>to smell bad</vocabulary>. To emphasize the stinkiness, you can also use the kanji 臭 instead of 匂.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8842', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%82%E3%81%86', 30, 1, '2019-04-27T00:44:50.082866Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.74862Z'),
(8843, 'vocabulary', '嬉々', '嬉々', 'You''re so <kanji>glad</kanji>, you get <kanji>glad</kanji> again. No matter what you''re doing, it''s undoubtedly in a <vocabulary>state of enjoyment</vocabulary>.

嬉々 itself refers to a <vocabulary>joyful state</vocabulary> where someone happily does something. It''s generally used as the adverb 嬉々として, which means “happily” or “gladly.” While less common, it can also serve as an adjective when combined with たる.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8843', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AC%89%E3%80%85', 40, 1, '2019-04-27T01:02:49.802201Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(8844, 'vocabulary', '嬉しい', '嬉しい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>glad</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>glad</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8844', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AC%89%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 40, 1, '2019-04-27T01:05:53.982048Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:11.634675Z'),
(8845, 'vocabulary', '嘘', '嘘', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. A <kanji>lie</kanji> is a <vocabulary>lie</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8845', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%98%98', 41, 1, '2019-04-27T01:08:46.621339Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:11.656271Z'),
(8846, 'vocabulary', '嘘吐き', '嘘吐き', 'If <kanji>lies</kanji> puke (吐く) out of your mouth, you''re a <vocabulary>liar</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8846', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%98%98%E5%90%90%E3%81%8D', 41, 1, '2019-04-27T01:10:58.956717Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:17:12.930062Z'),
(8847, 'vocabulary', '串', '串', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8847', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%B2', 37, 1, '2019-04-27T01:12:47.55368Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:11.703147Z'),
(8848, 'vocabulary', '串焼き', '串焼き', '<kanji>Skewer</kanji>s you grill (焼く) turn into <vocabulary>grilled skewers</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8848', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%B2%E7%84%BC%E3%81%8D', 37, 1, '2019-04-27T01:14:08.551094Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:11.727339Z'),
(8849, 'vocabulary', '喉', '喉', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8849', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%89', 18, 1, '2019-04-27T01:15:34.488791Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:49:17.397909Z'),
(8850, 'vocabulary', '喉飴', '喉飴', '<kanji>Throat</kanji> <kanji>candy</kanji> is the kind of candy that should make your throat feel better. That''s right, it''s a <vocabulary>cough drop</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>throat lozenge</vocabulary>. Don''t just eat regular candy when your throat hurts! Use the right kind.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8850', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%89%E9%A3%B4', 20, 1, '2019-04-27T01:17:04.656778Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:11.772188Z'),
(8851, 'vocabulary', '喉頭', '喉頭', 'The body part that lives between your <kanji>throat</kanji> and your <kanji>head</kanji> is your <vocabulary>larynx</vocabulary>. Otherwise known as the <vocabulary>voice box</vocabulary>, because your vocal cords live in there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8851', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%89%E9%A0%AD', 18, 1, '2019-04-27T01:18:58.120122Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:38:54.907026Z'),
(8852, 'vocabulary', '叩く', '叩く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>tap</kanji> or <kanji>strike</kanji>, and the verb version is <vocabulary>to strike</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to tap</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to hit</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to knock</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to beat</vocabulary>.

This verb can be used for both things and people, and the English translation will change depending on what it is you''re striking. You''ll always have some kind of object, though, since this is a transitive verb. So you might tap someone on the shoulder, knock at a door, or beat a drum.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8852', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%A9%E3%81%8F', 18, 1, '2019-04-27T01:20:51.692812Z', NULL, '2025-07-02T07:11:28.862889Z'),
(8853, 'vocabulary', '叩頭', '叩頭', '<kanji>Tap</kanji> your <kanji>head</kanji> against the floor to show your subservient respect by doing this deep <vocabulary>bow</vocabulary>. This is the most respectful bow you can possibly do.

叩頭 refers to the traditional Chinese custom where individuals kneel and touch their foreheads to the ground as a gesture of deep respect or submission. You may come across the word 叩頭 in literature and drama set in historical China, particularly in imperial settings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8853', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%A9%E9%A0%AD', 60, 1, '2019-04-27T01:22:45.098837Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:38:54.907026Z'),
(8854, 'vocabulary', '飴', '飴', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. When we talk about this kind of <vocabulary>candy</vocabulary>, it''s usually <vocabulary>hard candy</vocabulary> 🍬 or a <vocabulary>lollipop</vocabulary> 🍭 though. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8854', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%B4', 18, 1, '2019-04-27T01:25:12.901153Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:11.864098Z'),
(8855, 'vocabulary', '飴細工', '飴細工', '<kanji>Candy</kanji> made into a <kanji>thin</kanji> <kanji>construction</kanji> is a <vocabulary>sugar sculpture</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>candy sculpture</vocabulary>. It''s usually a <vocabulary>sugar animal</vocabulary>, but it can be anything, really.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8855', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%B4%E7%B4%B0%E5%B7%A5', 18, 1, '2019-04-27T01:26:38.678446Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:47:55.647024Z'),
(8865, 'vocabulary', '噂', '噂', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8865', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%99%82', 33, 1, '2019-04-29T23:35:27.093702Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:35:26.620172Z'),
(8866, 'vocabulary', '諦観', '諦観', 'When you <kanji>give up</kanji> and just try to enjoy the <kanji>view</kanji>, that''s <vocabulary>resignation</vocabulary>. You know things aren''t going well, and you know you can''t do anything about it now, so you resign yourself to what''s to come.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8866', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%A6%E8%A6%B3', 58, 1, '2019-04-29T23:37:11.604942Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:37:21.517151Z'),
(8867, 'vocabulary', '諦める', '諦める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>give up</kanji> or <kanji>abandon</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to give up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to abandon</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8867', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%A6%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2019-04-29T23:39:01.505222Z', NULL, '2026-01-11T23:37:21.517151Z'),
(8868, 'vocabulary', '捉える', '捉える', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji means <kanji>catch</kanji>, but the verb version here means <vocabulary>to grasp</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to perceive</vocabulary>. You''re still catching something, just in a more metaphorical sense.

At first glance, this word might remind you a little of 捕える, since (spoiler alert) both words have the same reading. However, while 捕える is about physically capturing something that can run away, like a criminal or a wild animal, 捉える tends to be more figurative. You''ll see it used to talk about things like grasping ideas, seizing opportunities, or even captivating people''s hearts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8868', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%89%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2019-04-29T23:42:10.80787Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T06:49:24.911379Z'),
(8869, 'vocabulary', '膝', '膝', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8869', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%86%9D', 38, 1, '2019-04-29T23:44:32.359095Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:11.999827Z'),
(8870, 'vocabulary', '膝頭', '膝頭', 'Your <kanji>knee</kanji>''s <kanji>head</kanji> is the <vocabulary>kneecap</vocabulary>. It''s also called the <vocabulary>patella</vocabulary>, if you want to get fancy and talk about bone names.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8870', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%86%9D%E9%A0%AD', 38, 1, '2019-04-29T23:46:53.734883Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:38:54.907026Z'),
(8871, 'vocabulary', '膝小僧', '膝小僧', 'In your <kanji>knee</kanji> there is a <kanji>small</kanji> <kanji>priest</kanji> named <vocabulary>Kneecap</vocabulary>.

This is a really common, cute way of talking about your kneecap. It''s usually used by kids and adults alike, and it''s more common in spoken Japanese than 膝頭. Kind of like how we use "kneecap" instead of naming the "patella" bone all the time.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8871', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%86%9D%E5%B0%8F%E5%83%A7', 40, 1, '2019-04-29T23:49:03.492848Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.04488Z'),
(8872, 'vocabulary', '眉', '眉', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. This also includes the place where your <vocabulary>eyebrows</vocabulary> live: your <vocabulary>brow</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8872', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%89', 37, 1, '2019-04-29T23:50:54.815666Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.069623Z'),
(8873, 'vocabulary', '眉間', '眉間', 'Your <kanji>eyebrows</kanji> <kanji>interval</kanji> is that place between your eyebrows called the <vocabulary>brow</vocabulary>. The technical term for this interval between your brows is the <vocabulary>glabella</vocabulary>, but I bet you never use that word. Unless you''re... an eyebrow doctor?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8873', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%89%E9%96%93', 37, 1, '2019-04-29T23:53:57.427298Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.094302Z'),
(8874, 'vocabulary', '眉毛', '眉毛', 'An <kanji>eyebrow''s</kanji> <kanji>hair</kanji> is the individual <vocabulary>eyebrow</vocabulary> hair that makes them up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8874', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9C%89%E6%AF%9B', 39, 1, '2019-04-29T23:56:49.151227Z', NULL, '2025-09-09T23:36:48.048141Z'),
(8875, 'vocabulary', '濡らす', '濡らす', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>wet</kanji> or <kanji>damp</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to wet</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to dampen</vocabulary> something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8875', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BF%A1%E3%82%89%E3%81%99', 30, 1, '2019-04-29T23:58:57.13066Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:27:57.762429Z'),
(8876, 'vocabulary', '濡れる', '濡れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>wet</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to get wet</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8876', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BF%A1%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2019-04-30T00:00:57.244945Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.16998Z'),
(8877, 'vocabulary', '濡れ衣', '濡れ衣', 'Ah yes, <kanji>wet</kanji> <kanji>clothes</kanji> must be wet clothes-NOT! This used to mean "wet clothes" a long, long time ago, but these days it''s used for <vocabulary>false accusations</vocabulary>. Think of it this way: if you make false, baseless accusations, it''s like you''re dumping water all over someone for no reason. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8877', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BF%A1%E3%82%8C%E8%A1%A3', 43, 1, '2019-04-30T00:02:36.142969Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.193041Z'),
(8878, 'vocabulary', '痩せる', '痩せる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>thin</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to get thin</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8878', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%A9%E3%81%9B%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2019-04-30T00:05:52.570356Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.218278Z'),
(8879, 'vocabulary', '痩身', '痩身', 'A <kanji>thin</kanji> <kanji>body</kanji> type is called <vocabulary>lean</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>slim</vocabulary>. These days this is usually used in the context of <vocabulary>slimming</vocabulary> salons and massages you can get that may or may not work.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8879', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%A9%E8%BA%AB', 34, 1, '2019-04-30T00:07:40.984915Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:24:51.658554Z'),
(8887, 'vocabulary', '羨ましい', '羨ましい', 'This is a single kanji with hiragana that ends in an い, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>envy</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>envious</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8887', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%A8%E3%81%BE%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 23, 1, '2019-04-30T18:09:56.174078Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.274759Z'),
(8888, 'vocabulary', '羨む', '羨む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>envy</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to envy</vocabulary> someone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8888', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%A8%E3%82%80', 21, 1, '2019-04-30T18:14:37.375754Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.300642Z'),
(8889, 'vocabulary', '羨望', '羨望', '<kanji>Envy</kanji> consumes your <kanji>hope</kanji> turning it all to <vocabulary>envy</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>jealousy</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8889', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%BE%A8%E6%9C%9B', 21, 1, '2019-04-30T18:16:35.219408Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.32379Z'),
(8890, 'vocabulary', '慌てる', '慌てる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>confused</kanji> or <kanji>bewildered</kanji> but this is usually just used when someone starts <vocabulary>to panic</vocabulary>. They''re so confused and bewildered that they try <vocabulary>to hurry</vocabulary> through things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8890', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%8C%E3%81%A6%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2019-04-30T18:18:26.071929Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.348093Z'),
(8891, 'vocabulary', '慌ただしい', '慌ただしい', 'This is a single kanji with an い all the way at the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. <kanji>Confused</kanji> is already an adjective and this word is a little different. Usually when you get confused it''s because you were already <vocabulary>hurried</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>busy</vocabulary>, and things were <vocabulary>hectic</vocabulary>. So this adjective is what causes your confusion.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8891', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%85%8C%E3%81%9F%E3%81%A0%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 51, 1, '2019-04-30T18:21:30.091283Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.373976Z'),
(8892, 'vocabulary', '恐慌', '恐慌', '<kanji>Fear</kanji> mixed with <kanji>confusion</kanji> creates <vocabulary>panic</vocabulary>! You get a <vocabulary>scare</vocabulary> so bad, you''re fearful and you don''t know what to do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8892', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%81%90%E6%85%8C', 49, 1, '2019-04-30T18:23:19.495454Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.400945Z'),
(8893, 'vocabulary', '世界恐慌', '世界恐慌', '世界 means "world" and 恐慌 means "panic" or "scare," so when you put them together you get a scare that made the entire world panic. This is how we refer to <vocabulary>The Great Depression</vocabulary>. It was caused by and subsequently resulted in everybody in the whole world panicking together about money.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8893', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%96%E7%95%8C%E6%81%90%E6%85%8C', 51, 1, '2019-04-30T18:28:46.088858Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:21:16.900712Z'),
(8894, 'vocabulary', '挨拶', '挨拶', '<kanji>Push open</kanji> the door to the conversation that is going to <kanji>be imminent</kanji> by saying the proper <vocabulary>greeting</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>salutation</vocabulary>. This word might also refer to a short <vocabulary>speech</vocabulary> you give at an event or ceremony.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8894', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8C%A8%E6%8B%B6', 44, 1, '2019-04-30T18:31:47.644001Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.449849Z'),
(8895, 'vocabulary', '〜斤', '〜斤', 'This has the 〜 for counters, so you can bet this shares the <vocabulary>bread loaf counter</vocabulary> meaning with the kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8895', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%96%A4', 5, 1, '2019-04-30T18:34:03.527808Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:36.037754Z'),
(8896, 'vocabulary', '一斤', '一斤', '<kanji>One</kanji> <kanji>bread loaf counter</kanji> is counting <vocabulary>one loaf of bread</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8896', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%96%A4', 7, 1, '2019-04-30T18:38:49.463982Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.449788Z'),
(8897, 'vocabulary', '二斤', '二斤', '<kanji>Two</kanji> <kanji>bread loaf counter</kanji>s are counting <vocabulary>two loaves of bread</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8897', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%8C%E6%96%A4', 7, 1, '2019-05-01T00:21:19.481209Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:30:32.462725Z'),
(8898, 'vocabulary', '袖', '袖', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8898', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A2%96', 22, 1, '2019-05-01T00:24:11.261419Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:29:03.843014Z'),
(8899, 'vocabulary', '半袖', '半袖', '<kanji>Half</kanji> <kanji>sleeve</kanji>s is just another name for <vocabulary>short sleeves</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8899', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%8A%E8%A2%96', 24, 1, '2019-05-01T00:26:22.148631Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:29:03.843014Z'),
(8900, 'vocabulary', '長袖', '長袖', '<kanji>Long</kanji> <kanji>sleeve</kanji>s are <vocabulary>long sleeves</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8900', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%95%B7%E8%A2%96', 24, 1, '2019-05-01T00:27:55.691649Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:29:03.843014Z'),
(8901, 'vocabulary', '領袖', '領袖', 'Someone who has <kanji>territory</kanji> under their <kanji>sleeve</kanji> is the <vocabulary>leader</vocabulary>. They''re the <vocabulary>boss</vocabulary> of all this territory because they say so, and because they shoved it all into their big sleeves.

This is a very formal word often used in political contexts, particularly when referring to the leader of a faction or political party.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8901', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%98%E8%A2%96', 60, 1, '2019-05-01T00:29:47.667981Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:29:03.843014Z'),
(8902, 'vocabulary', '凄い', '凄い', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. What''s the adjective form of <kanji>amazing</kanji>? It''s also <vocabulary>amazing</vocabulary>! Or <vocabulary>terrible</vocabulary>. This word can be both good and bad, it really depends on the context, because what is amazing is usually also a little awful. You know? ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8902', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%84%E3%81%84', 41, 1, '2019-05-01T00:31:47.988473Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.651537Z'),
(8903, 'vocabulary', '凄く', '凄く', 'This may look like a verb at first, but it''s actually the adverb version of the adjective 凄い. When something is <kanji>awful</kanji> it''s usually <vocabulary>awfully</vocabulary> awful.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8903', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%84%E3%81%8F', 43, 1, '2019-05-01T00:33:43.489409Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.674919Z'),
(8904, 'vocabulary', '凄絶', '凄絶', 'Something really <kanji>terrible</kanji> causing mass <kanji>extinction</kanji> will be <vocabulary>extremely gruesome</vocabulary>. It will also probably be <vocabulary>extremely violent</vocabulary>, so don''t look.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8904', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%84%E7%B5%B6', 41, 1, '2019-05-01T00:35:32.949229Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.696369Z'),
(8905, 'vocabulary', '絞める', '絞める', 'The kanji means <kanji>strangle</kanji> and this vocab is the verb version of that: <vocabulary>to strangle</vocabulary>. It’s usually used in the phrase 首を絞める, which also means “to strangle.” 

There is another word, 絞る which means "to wring out" that looks very similar. You know that this one is "to strangle" because it has める sticking out of it. Imagine that in the <reading>mail</reading> (める) you get a letter that says "I''m going <vocabulary>to strangle</vocabulary> you!" When you get this in the mail, you know it''s about strangling.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8905', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%9E%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 25, 1, '2019-10-04T23:21:19.866745Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:04.005215Z'),
(8914, 'vocabulary', '妖精', '妖精', 'A <kanji>supernatural</kanji> <kanji>spirit</kanji> flutters up to you. It''s a <vocabulary>fairy</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8914', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%96%E7%B2%BE', 40, 1, '2020-06-17T20:52:04.82103Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.748677Z'),
(8915, 'vocabulary', '妖怪', '妖怪', 'What''s <kanji>supernatural</kanji> and <kanji>suspicious</kanji>? A <vocabulary>monster</vocabulary>, or, if you know the Japanese term, a <vocabulary>yokai</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8915', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%96%E6%80%AA', 40, 1, '2020-06-17T20:54:53.680513Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.773303Z'),
(8916, 'vocabulary', '妖しい', '妖しい', 'You can guess this is an adjective by looking at the い it ends with. One of the meanings of the kanji, <kanji>enchanting</kanji>, is already an adjective. That''s the meaning of this word, too: <vocabulary>enchanting</vocabulary>. What is enchanting? It''s basically <vocabulary>charming</vocabulary> in a <vocabulary>mysterious</vocabulary> way.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8916', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%96%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 40, 1, '2020-06-17T20:57:07.094049Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.799814Z'),
(8917, 'vocabulary', '喋る', '喋る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>chat</kanji> or <kanji>talk</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to chat</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to talk</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8917', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%8B%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2020-06-17T21:01:14.731945Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.824692Z'),
(8918, 'vocabulary', '鮭', '鮭', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8918', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AE%AD', 36, 1, '2020-06-17T21:03:30.314314Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.84861Z'),
(8919, 'vocabulary', '宛名', '宛名', 'The <kanji>address</kanji> <kanji>name</kanji> is the name that''s on the address, making it the <vocabulary>addressee</vocabulary>. This can also mean <vocabulary>name and address</vocabulary>.

This can be used for both physical mail and email.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8919', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9B%E5%90%8D', 39, 1, '2020-06-17T21:05:47.325782Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T09:46:52.810583Z'),
(8920, 'vocabulary', '蹴る', '蹴る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>kick</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to kick</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8920', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B9%B4%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2020-06-17T21:08:36.575357Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.895709Z'),
(8921, 'vocabulary', '一蹴する', '一蹴する', 'Giving <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>kick</kanji> is certainly an effective (if brutal) way <vocabulary>to flatly reject</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to brush aside</vocabulary> something or someone. That one kick can also be used <vocabulary>to easily beat</vocabulary> anyone.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8921', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E8%B9%B4%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2020-06-17T21:10:46.729427Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.920213Z'),
(8924, 'vocabulary', '喧嘩', '喧嘩', 'When things get <kanji>noisy</kanji> and <kanji>rowdy</kanji> there''s a good chance things will end in a <vocabulary>fight</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8924', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%A7%E5%98%A9', 41, 1, '2020-06-24T16:45:03.512771Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T23:08:32.26304Z'),
(8925, 'vocabulary', '喧しい', '喧しい', 'You can guess this is an adjective from the い at the end. The meaning for the kanji is <kanji>noisy</kanji>, so the adjective version is also <vocabulary>noisy</vocabulary>.

喧しい is a casual but emphatic way to say someone or something is too noisy. It''s often used in Japanese comedy and variety shows, so depending on the situation there can be a playful vibe to it. You can even use it in a figurative way to describe someone who''s being overly picky, fussy, or annoying about something particular (they''re being "noisy" about insignificant details). Note that in everyday situations, you''ll often just see this word written in kana as やかましい.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8925', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%96%A7%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 41, 1, '2020-06-24T16:49:31.852322Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T22:17:39.266797Z'),
(8926, 'vocabulary', '裂ける', '裂ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji means <kanji>split</kanji>, so this verb has to do with something splitting. 

This is an intransitive verb, so this verb means <vocabulary>to be split</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be torn</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to burst apart</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8926', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A3%82%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 44, 1, '2020-08-20T22:12:35.502858Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:12.99242Z'),
(8927, 'vocabulary', '治る', '治る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

The kanji 治 means <kanji>cure</kanji>, but this verb refers to the natural process of getting better: <vocabulary>to get better</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to get well</vocabulary>, or simply <vocabulary>to heal</vocabulary>. This is an intransitive verb, meaning that it happens on its own. Nobody cures you, you just get better.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8927', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%BB%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2020-08-20T22:18:45.821439Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.017694Z'),
(8928, 'vocabulary', '薄まる', '薄まる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji means <kanji>dilute</kanji>, and this is an intransitive verb, as you can guess from the まる ending. So this verb means <vocabulary>to be diluted</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be diminished</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to become weak</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8928', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%96%84%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2020-08-20T23:19:07.377583Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.0396Z'),
(8929, 'vocabulary', '寄せる', '寄せる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

This is a transitive verb. That means it takes a direct object. The kanji itself means <kanji>draw near</kanji>, and the verb version is about making or letting something draw near to something else. That''s why it means <vocabulary>to move something close to</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to pull something over</vocabulary>, and also <vocabulary>to contribute something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8929', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%84%E3%81%9B%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2020-08-20T23:43:31.831742Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.551541Z'),
(8930, 'vocabulary', '退ける', '退ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. 

The kanji itself means <kanji>retreat</kanji>, and this verb version is when you make something retreat. In other words, it means <vocabulary>to repel something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to dismiss something</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to reject something</vocabulary>.

This is a transitive verb. That means it takes a direct object. You repel something, like bad luck. You reject something, like a proposal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8930', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%80%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2020-08-20T23:58:45.372214Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.446706Z'),
(8931, 'vocabulary', '収まる', '収まる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

If you <kanji>obtain</kanji> something, you''ll want it <vocabulary>to fit inside</vocabulary> your bag. Otherwise how can you take it home? When talking about problems or emotions, this verb can also mean <vocabulary>to settle down</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to be finished</vocabulary> like a dispute settling down or someone’s anger calming down.

Like most verbs with まる as okurigana, this is an intransitive verb. That means it happens by itself, with no emphasis on the person or thing that''s making something fit inside, or settle down.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8931', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8E%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2020-08-21T20:59:18.165062Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.117923Z'),
(8932, 'vocabulary', '転がす', '転がす', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>revolve</kanji>, and the verb version is when you make something revolve. You roll it. That''s why this verb is <vocabulary>to roll something</vocabulary>.

This is a transitive verb. That means you roll something else, like a ball. Or, if you''re a dung beetle, poop. 💩 Not to be confused with 転がる, which is intransitive, like so many verbs ending in がる.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8932', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%A2%E3%81%8C%E3%81%99', 11, 1, '2020-08-21T21:23:00.231694Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.143775Z'),
(8933, 'vocabulary', '苦しめる', '苦しめる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. 

The kanji itself means <kanji>suffer</kanji>, and the verb is all about when you make someone else suffer. That''s why it''s <vocabulary>to hurt someone</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to torment</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to harass</vocabulary>.

You need to differentiate this from 苦しむ, which is about when YOU suffer. You can remember that 苦しめる is about making someone else suffer with this mnemonic: Who made you suffer? <reading>She ma</reading>de (しめ) me suffer! She''s so mean!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8933', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%A6%E3%81%97%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2020-08-21T21:39:57.44039Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T01:15:20.116546Z'),
(8934, 'vocabulary', '痛める', '痛める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. 

The kanji itself means <kanji>pain</kanji>, and this verb is transitive, so it means to inflict pain. In other words, it''s <vocabulary>to injure something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to hurt someone</vocabulary>.

This verb focuses on the pain involved in an injury, so it can only be used for things that feel pain, like people and body parts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8934', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%9B%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2020-08-21T22:11:52.520414Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T18:48:39.716764Z'),
(8935, 'vocabulary', '延びる', '延びる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

This is an intransitive verb, meaning that it happens on its own, and we''re not talking about what makes it happen. So this verb means <vocabulary>to be extended</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be delayed</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to be prolonged</vocabulary>. If you know the word ビル, you can think about how building work is always prolonged or delayed, to help you remember that this is intransitive. We never know why it''s delayed, it just happens all by itself!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8935', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BB%B6%E3%81%B3%E3%82%8B', 31, 1, '2020-08-21T22:24:46.046152Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.215517Z'),
(8936, 'vocabulary', '加わる', '加わる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

The kanji 加 means <kanji>add</kanji>, and this verb means <vocabulary>to be added to</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to join in</vocabulary>.

It is an intransitive verb, like most verbs ending in わる. That means that it happens by itself, or at least the cause of the adding is not you. Something gets added to something else, perhaps by magic?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8936', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%A0%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2020-08-21T22:33:35.582454Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T20:15:08.580781Z'),
(8937, 'vocabulary', '零れる', '零れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

This is an intransitive verb, so this verb means <vocabulary>to get spilled</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to fall out</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to overflow</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8937', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%B6%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 48, 1, '2020-08-21T22:39:03.867152Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.264437Z'),
(8938, 'vocabulary', '点く', '点く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

The kanji 点 means <kanji>point</kanji>, but this verb actually means <vocabulary>to be lit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be ignited</vocabulary>. Think of it this way, when a flashlight turns on, it points a beam, of light.

You already learned 点ける, which is for when you turn something on. 点く, on the other hand, is an intransitive verb. That means it happens by itself. Like, "Oh look! The lights came on! That''s <reading>cool</reading> (く)!"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8938', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%B9%E3%81%8F', 10, 1, '2020-08-21T22:43:43.896897Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:54.877368Z'),
(8939, 'vocabulary', '進める', '進める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>advance</kanji>, and this verb means <vocabulary>to advance something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to move something forward</vocabulary>.

This is a transitive verb, so you act upon something else. You advance something. You move something forward. Like a school project, or your plans for taking over the world.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8939', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%B2%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 11, 1, '2020-08-21T22:52:08.585687Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.312212Z'),
(8940, 'vocabulary', '従える', '従える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

The kanji itself means <kanji>obey</kanji>, but the verb meaning is a bit different. In most contexts, it often just means <vocabulary>to be accompanied by</vocabulary> someone or <vocabulary>to have in tow</vocabulary>. To help you remember this, imagine that everyone you''re accompanied by obeys you! You''re sort of their leader, so you go everywhere with them following in tow.

従える is a relatively formal word that''s often used in literature.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8940', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BE%93%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2020-08-25T18:21:28.693646Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.334387Z'),
(8941, 'vocabulary', '炊ける', '炊ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. This is an intransitive verb, so it means <vocabulary>to be cooked</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be boiled</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to be done cooking</vocabulary>. Also a little cultural note — this word is used for both rice, and other foods in western Japan, while it''s only used for rice in eastern Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8941', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%8A%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2020-08-25T18:29:20.873295Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.359573Z'),
(8942, 'vocabulary', '揚がる', '揚がる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

This is an intransitive verb. The kanji means <kanji>hoist</kanji>, but the verb most commonly means <vocabulary>to be fried up</vocabulary>, ready to eat. Imagine something being hoisted up onto the table because it''s fried and ready to eat. The verb can also mean, more logically, <vocabulary>to be up</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8942', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%9A%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 43, 1, '2020-08-25T18:33:42.509118Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:42:49.398284Z'),
(8943, 'vocabulary', '受かる', '受かる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji 受 means <kanji>accept</kanji> and this verb is related. It means <vocabulary>to get accepted</vocabulary>, usually into something good like a university or a new job. It can also mean <vocabulary>to pass</vocabulary>, often in the context of a test or an exam.

This is an intransitive verb, so no direct object is needed. A hint for this is that it ends with okurigana that rhyme with ある, and those kinds of verbs tend to be intransitive. Also, be careful: in English you''d use the transitive expression "pass a test," but in Japanese you''d say 試験に受かる. Kind of like saying you pass <em>in</em> the exam — so it''s still intransitive!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8943', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%97%E3%81%8B%E3%82%8B', 13, 1, '2020-08-25T18:36:56.942645Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:48.601574Z'),
(8944, 'vocabulary', '改める', '改める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

The kanji itself means <kanji>renew</kanji>, and this verb''s meaning isn''t far off. What do you do if you renew something, usually to make it better? You''re going <vocabulary>to change something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to revise something</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to correct something</vocabulary>. This verb is transitive, so it takes a direct object.

改める is a formal word for when you''re actively make changes or improvements, like fixing a mistake or reforming a system. You might also use it when you decide to change your behavior or update your way of doing something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8944', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%94%B9%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2020-08-25T18:45:11.734627Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.435869Z'),
(8945, 'vocabulary', '広げる', '広げる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. 

The kanji itself means <kanji>wide</kanji>, and the verb version is what happens when you try to make something wider. How do you make something wider? That''s right, you have <vocabulary>to spread something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to unfold something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to open something up</vocabulary>. All of those actions will make a thing wider!

This is a transitive verb. That means it takes a direct object. You spread something. Like a newspaper, or a cloth on a table.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8945', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BA%83%E3%81%92%E3%82%8B', 3, 1, '2020-08-25T18:50:14.113575Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.529179Z'),
(8946, 'vocabulary', '終える', '終える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>finish</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to finish something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to end something</vocabulary>.

This is a transitive verb, meaning you finish something, like a long day at work!

You need to differentiate this from 終わる, which is when something just ends on its own. 終える is about when you make something end. Remember it like this: Canadians always end their sentences with "eh" え, right? Well that''s how you know they''re ending their sentence. Just listen for the "eh," eh?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8946', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%82%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 12, 1, '2020-08-25T18:54:34.119396Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.481747Z'),
(8947, 'vocabulary', '帰す', '帰す', 'The hiragana ends with an う sound, so you know this is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>return</kanji>, and this verb is transitive, meaning it takes a direct object. You send someone back. You send them home. And tell them to never come near you again. So this means <vocabulary>to send someone back</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to send someone home</vocabulary>.

In fact, most verbs ending in す are transitive. To help you remember that, think of <reading>su</reading>ing (す) someone to make sure they go home!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8947', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B8%B0%E3%81%99', 16, 1, '2020-08-25T19:07:31.317812Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:41:01.299215Z'),
(8948, 'vocabulary', '砕ける', '砕ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

This is an intransitive verb, so this verb means <vocabulary>to be smashed</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be shattered</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to break</vocabulary> (for things like waves and tension!).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8948', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%95%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 49, 1, '2020-08-25T19:31:26.266073Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T23:21:30.232959Z'),
(8949, 'vocabulary', '刺さる', '刺さる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

This is an intransitive verb, meaning that the piercing just happens, without emphasis on what makes it happen. That''s why it means <vocabulary>to pierce into</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to stick in</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to stab into</vocabulary>. 

This is a tricky word because its English translations often end up being transitive, but in Japanese we always use this with the particle に, not を, so it''s intransitive. You can see this in phrases like 言葉が心に刺さった (the words pierced my heart).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8949', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%BA%E3%81%95%E3%82%8B', 22, 1, '2020-08-25T19:40:42.007943Z', NULL, '2026-01-07T00:47:24.063498Z'),
(8950, 'vocabulary', '煮える', '煮える', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

You already learned 煮る, which is used for when you purposefully boil something. This verb, on the other hand, is intransitive, meaning that it refers to when something is getting boiled or cooked up, with no emphasis on who put the thing in the pot. That''s why this verb means <vocabulary>to be boiled</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be cooked</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8950', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%AE%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 50, 1, '2020-08-25T19:44:31.178924Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.594434Z'),
(8951, 'vocabulary', '欠く', '欠く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. 

The kanji itself means <kanji>lack</kanji>, and this verb means <vocabulary>to lack something</vocabulary>. Pretty simple in English, but 欠く is actually kind of advanced and formal in Japanese, so it''s mostly just used in writing. 

Also keep in mind this is a transitive verb, so 欠く takes a direct object — maybe someone lacks <em>knowledge</em>, for example, or you lack <em>concentration</em>. This is a bit different from saying "your concentration is lacking," at least grammatically. (For that, you''d use 欠ける.)', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8951', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AC%A0%E3%81%8F', 11, 1, '2020-08-25T19:56:28.016637Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.618145Z'),
(8952, 'vocabulary', '外す', '外す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

The kanji 外 means <kanji>outside</kanji>, and the meaning of this verb is somewhat related. It means <vocabulary>to remove</vocabulary> something. Think of it like this — if you remove your watch, you are putting it "outside" of its usual spot (your wrist), right?

Like many verbs ending in す, this verb is transitive. It''s used for when you intentionally take something and put it outside of where it''s supposed to be — so depending on the context, it can also mean <vocabulary>to exclude</vocabulary> someone, or even <vocabulary>to miss something</vocabulary> (like a target). See how all these meanings relate to the idea of something ending up outside its intended spot?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8952', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%96%E3%81%99', 15, 1, '2020-08-25T20:29:18.4964Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(8953, 'vocabulary', '産まれる', '産まれる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

This is an intransitive verb. That means that it just happens, and we''re not talking about what makes it happen. So this verb means <vocabulary>to be born</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8953', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%A3%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 20, 1, '2020-08-25T20:39:32.079498Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.671623Z'),
(8954, 'vocabulary', '温まる', '温まる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends in an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji 温 means <kanji>warm</kanji>. So the verb 温まる means <vocabulary>to warm up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to get warm</vocabulary>. 

温まる is intransitive, so no direct object needed. Your cat warms up in the sunlight, for example. No direct object, right? A hint for this is that 温まる ends with okurigana that rhyme with ある, and those kinds of verbs tend to be intransitive!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8954', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%A9%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 14, 1, '2020-08-25T20:43:00.080274Z', NULL, '2025-11-06T18:20:24.41073Z'),
(8956, 'vocabulary', '預かる', '預かる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>deposit</kanji>, and this word is for when someone is depositing something (or someone) with you, because they want you <vocabulary>to look after</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to take care of</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to hold on to</vocabulary> it.

Remember that 預ける is used when you leave something with someone else to take care of temporarily. 預かる, on the other hand, is used when you''re the one receiving something to look after. You can remember this because you''re great at taking care of cargo (かる). You''re always looking after everyone''s cargo, but you''re happy to hold on to it for them.

Also note that even though 預かる ends with かる and kind of looks intransitive, it''s actually an exception! It''ll pretty much always have a direct object to specify what you''re looking after.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8956', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%90%E3%81%8B%E3%82%8B', 32, 1, '2021-04-16T20:11:58.046153Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:22:56.408073Z'),
(8957, 'vocabulary', '山びこ', '山びこ', 'You know that 山 means mountain. Add the hiragana びこ to the end and this gives you <vocabulary>echo</vocabulary>. That''s because bee children (bee 子) love echos, especially <vocabulary>mountain echo</vocabulary>s! Imagine baby bees buzzing as loudly as they can and hearing their voices echoing back at them through the mountains.

As the kanji suggests, 山びこ is generally used to describe the echo you get if you shout in the mountains. To describe other types of echo, you can use the katakana word エコー.

By the way, there is actually a kanji for the second part of this word, but it''s so rare that it''s usually written in kana!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8957', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%B1%E3%81%B3%E3%81%93', 3, 1, '2021-04-27T11:15:28.323332Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:24:50.55118Z'),
(8958, 'vocabulary', '力いっぱい', '力いっぱい', 'You''ve hopefully already learned that 力 means "power." You might have also heard the word いっぱい, because it''s used a lot in conversation. It has a few meanings (you''ll learn more of them later) but the important one here is "full" or "a lot." Put these two words together and you have <vocabulary>full power</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>with all one''s strength</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>as hard as one can</vocabulary>. Remember this one with all your strength!

Just a side note: いっぱい can also be written in kanji, which you''ll learn later on! However, in simple words like this one it''s usually written in kana.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8958', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8A%9B%E3%81%84%E3%81%A3%E3%81%B1%E3%81%84', 1, 2, '2021-04-27T11:32:26.880303Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:47.392403Z'),
(8959, 'vocabulary', 'テーブルの上', 'テーブルの上', 'First we have a katakana word, so it''s safe to assume that it''s a loan word. Like many loan words, it comes from English. Say it out loud and what do you have? That''s right, "table". Then comes the particle の, which joins two nouns together. Finally we have 上, which you learned means "up" or "above". Put all of these pieces together and we have a phrase meaning <vocabulary>on the table</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>tabletop</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8959', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%86%E3%83%BC%E3%83%96%E3%83%AB%E3%81%AE%E4%B8%8A', 3, 1, '2021-04-27T12:04:52.113101Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(8960, 'vocabulary', 'ベッドの下', 'ベッドの下', 'This starts with another katakana word, so you can assume that it''s a loan word. This one also comes from English. Can you figure out what it means? That''s right, "bed". Then comes the particle の, which joins two nouns together. Finally, we have 下, which you learned means "down" or "below". Put these elements together and you get <vocabulary>below the bed</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>under the bed</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>beneath the bed</vocabulary>. What''s under your bed?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8960', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%99%E3%83%83%E3%83%89%E3%81%AE%E4%B8%8B', 3, 1, '2021-04-27T12:21:07.720651Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(8961, 'vocabulary', '口にする', '口にする', 'You''ve already learned that 口 means "mouth" and here it''s followed by the particle に, which pinpoints a location. The verb する means "to do" so you''re doing something to your mouth! Logically enough, this expression is used to describe lots of actions that you do with your mouth. It''s often used to mean <vocabulary>to mention</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to say</vocabulary>, but with an added descriptive nuance that you''ve said something accidentally or unexpectedly, maybe something you shouldn''t have said! 🙊

It can also mean other things done with the mouth, like <vocabulary>to eat</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to drink</vocabulary>!  ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8961', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%A3%E3%81%AB%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 4, 1, '2021-04-27T13:25:27.470978Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:23:59.239173Z'),
(8962, 'vocabulary', '山道', '山道', 'A <kanji>mountain</kanji> <kanji>road</kanji> is a <vocabulary>mountain road</vocabulary>! Or a <vocabulary>mountain trail</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>mountain path</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8962', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%B1%E9%81%93', 10, 1, '2021-04-27T13:52:35.604211Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.835785Z'),
(8970, 'vocabulary', '麺', '麺', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8970', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BA%BA', 40, 1, '2021-05-04T13:00:47.336674Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.915964Z'),
(8971, 'vocabulary', '麺棒', '麺棒', 'Grab your <kanji>noodle</kanji> <kanji>pole</kanji> and let''s make some noodles. What''s a noodle pole? It''s a <vocabulary>rolling pin</vocabulary>!

You use your rolling pin to flatten out the noodle dough before you slice it into noodles. That''s why a rolling pin is so important to make noodles.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8971', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BA%BA%E6%A3%92', 40, 1, '2021-05-04T13:03:35.687969Z', NULL, '2025-07-28T21:19:56.013502Z'),
(8972, 'vocabulary', '拉麺', '拉麺', 'If you''re going to <kanji>abduct</kanji> some <kanji>noodles</kanji>, make sure it''s everybody''s favorite noodles, <vocabulary>ramen</vocabulary>! The other meaning of the first kanji actually makes more sense here though, because you <kanji>pull</kanji> the dough to stretch it when you make <vocabulary>ramen noodles</vocabulary>. Choose whichever meaning helps you remember it best!

This word is mostly written in kana, usually as ラーメン, but sometimes らーめん. You''ll see the kanji version pretty often on packaging and menus, though, so it''s worth knowing! The word is also occasionally written as らー麺 or ラー麺.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8972', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%89%E9%BA%BA', 42, 1, '2021-05-04T15:06:24.038657Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:13.961909Z'),
(8973, 'vocabulary', '素麺', '素麺', 'If you can read this word, you may well know what it means. It''s everybody''s second favorite noodle dish, <vocabulary>somen</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>somen noodles</vocabulary>, which are <vocabulary>fine white noodles</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8973', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%A0%E9%BA%BA', 42, 1, '2021-05-12T12:58:01.852292Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:38:20.342554Z'),
(8974, 'vocabulary', '中華麺', '中華麺', 'You already know that 中華 means Chinese, and <kanji>麺</kanji> means noodles. Put them together and what have you got? That''s right, <vocabulary>Chinese noodles</vocabulary>! Yum!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8974', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%AD%E8%8F%AF%E9%BA%BA', 40, 1, '2021-05-12T14:16:12.279724Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.009046Z'),
(8975, 'vocabulary', '苺', '苺', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8975', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%BA', 14, 1, '2021-05-12T14:53:34.433012Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.032212Z'),
(8976, 'vocabulary', '股', '股', 'The kanji means <kanji>crotch</kanji> and the vocabulary also means <vocabulary>crotch</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>groin</vocabulary>.

Note that 股 is a little less specific than 股間, and can also be used for a <vocabulary>fork</vocabulary> in a road or path, which makes sense when you think about it, doesn''t it?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8976', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%A1', 34, 1, '2021-05-13T07:44:33.516966Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.056249Z'),
(8977, 'vocabulary', '股下', '股下', 'Your <kanji>crotch</kanji> and what''s <kanji>below</kanji> it is called your <vocabulary>inseam</vocabulary>. It''s that line that goes down the inside of your pants. Depending on your variety of English, you might call this your <vocabulary>inside leg</vocabulary> instead!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8977', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%A1%E4%B8%8B', 35, 1, '2021-05-13T07:48:37.372104Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(8978, 'vocabulary', '股間', '股間', 'The <kanji>groin</kanji> <kanji>interval</kanji> is simply the <vocabulary>groin</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>crotch</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8978', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%A1%E9%96%93', 33, 1, '2021-05-13T07:52:41.287801Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.293839Z'),
(8979, 'vocabulary', '股関節', '股関節', 'Did you know that your <kanji>crotch</kanji> is <kanji>connected</kanji> via various <kanji>joint</kanji>s? That makes this word your <vocabulary>hip joints</vocabulary> or, more generally, just your <vocabulary>hip</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8979', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%A1%E9%96%A2%E7%AF%80', 33, 1, '2021-05-13T07:54:45.184061Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.319939Z'),
(8980, 'vocabulary', '柵', '柵', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. Isn''t that fencetastic?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8980', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%B5', 30, 1, '2021-05-13T07:57:37.872409Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.540498Z'),
(8981, 'vocabulary', '鉄柵', '鉄柵', 'Ready for this one? An <kanji>iron</kanji> <kanji>fence </kanji> is an <vocabulary>iron fence</vocabulary>. Don''t you love it when it''s straightforward?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8981', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%84%E6%9F%B5', 30, 1, '2021-05-13T07:59:41.575015Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.491569Z'),
(8982, 'vocabulary', '電気柵', '電気柵', 'If you combine 電気 (electricity) with 柵 (<kanji>fence</kanji>) you have an <vocabulary>electric fence</vocabulary>. Shocking, I know.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8982', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%BB%E6%B0%97%E6%9F%B5', 30, 1, '2021-05-13T08:02:10.711968Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:17:20.030484Z'),
(8983, 'vocabulary', '噛む', '噛む', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>bite</kanji> or <kanji>chew</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to bite</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to chew</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8983', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%99%9B%E3%82%80', 38, 1, '2021-05-13T08:04:33.722115Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.228356Z'),
(8984, 'vocabulary', '狼', '狼', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8984', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%BC', 14, 1, '2021-05-13T08:06:22.936741Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.252763Z'),
(8985, 'vocabulary', '狼男', '狼男', 'What’s another word for a <kanji>wolf</kanji> <kanji>man</kanji>? A <vocabulary>werewolf</vocabulary>, obviously.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8985', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8B%BC%E7%94%B7', 14, 1, '2021-05-13T08:07:54.159503Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.276766Z'),
(8986, 'vocabulary', '咳', '咳', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8986', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%92%B3', 34, 1, '2021-05-13T08:09:30.17345Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.302893Z'),
(8987, 'vocabulary', '人狼', '人狼', 'A <kanji>person</kanji> <kanji>wolf </kanji> is a <vocabulary>werewolf</vocabulary>. This word is less common than 狼男, but it''s used more and more thanks to the card game of the same name!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8987', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E7%8B%BC', 25, 1, '2021-05-13T08:27:50.180276Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:04.017456Z'),
(8988, 'vocabulary', '麺つゆ', '麺つゆ', 'You know that 麺 means "noodles", and つゆ is another word for "soup" or "broth". Put the two together and you have the delicious <vocabulary>mentsuyu </vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>noodle soup base</vocabulary>. It''s usually made of dashi, soy sauce, mirin and sugar​. Yum!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8988', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%BA%BA%E3%81%A4%E3%82%86', 40, 1, '2021-05-13T08:34:13.771586Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.350053Z'),
(8989, 'vocabulary', '拉致', '拉致', 'If someone <kanji>abduct</kanji>s what do they <kanji>do</kanji>? That''s <vocabulary>abduction</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>kidnapping</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>taking captive</vocabulary>. This word comes up fairly frequently in Japanese newspapers, and is often used to refer to kidnappings carried out by North Korea in the seventies and eighties.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8989', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%89%E8%87%B4', 40, 1, '2021-05-13T08:52:49.035078Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.372663Z'),
(8997, 'vocabulary', '苛める', '苛める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>frustration</kanji>. What do frustrated people do? They tend <vocabulary>to bully</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to pick on</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to tease</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>to torment</vocabulary> other people. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8997', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%9B%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2021-05-17T08:02:11.706301Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.394369Z'),
(8998, 'vocabulary', '苛々する', '苛々する', 'Double <kanji>frustration</kanji> would cause anyone <vocabulary>to get irritated</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to get annoyed</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to grow impatient</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8998', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%9B%E3%80%85%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2021-05-17T08:08:38.068096Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(8999, 'vocabulary', '煎餅', '煎餅', 'What do you get when you <kanji>roast</kanji> <kanji>mochi</kanji>? <vocabulary>Senbei</vocabulary>, a crispy Japanese <vocabulary>rice cracker</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/8999', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%8E%E9%A4%85', 47, 1, '2021-05-17T08:17:16.898444Z', NULL, '2025-09-17T17:45:58.120591Z'),
(9000, 'vocabulary', '煎茶', '煎茶', 'When you <kanji>roast</kanji> <kanji>tea</kanji>, you get <vocabulary>green tea</vocabulary>, also known as <vocabulary>green leaf tea</vocabulary>or <vocabulary>sencha</vocabulary>.

In reality there are various ways to make green tea, but for the purposes of this mnemonic, let''s imagine it''s always roasted!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9000', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%8E%E8%8C%B6', 47, 1, '2021-05-17T08:20:28.878126Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T03:16:41.901026Z'),
(9001, 'vocabulary', '煎る', '煎る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>roast</kanji>, and the verb version is <vocabulary>to roast</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to toast</vocabulary>. This verb is most commonly used when referring to dry roasting things like coffee, tea, or beans. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9001', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%85%8E%E3%82%8B', 51, 1, '2021-05-17T08:23:54.384235Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.481411Z'),
(9002, 'vocabulary', '親戚', '親戚', 'What exactly are your <kanji>parent</kanji> <kanji>relatives</kanji>? Your own <vocabulary>relatives</vocabulary>! You''re all <vocabulary>kin</vocabulary>, after all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9002', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA%E6%88%9A', 35, 1, '2021-05-17T08:26:36.957984Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.505051Z'),
(9003, 'vocabulary', '餅', '餅', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. <kanji>Mochi</kanji> is <vocabulary>mochi</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9003', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A4%85', 42, 1, '2021-05-17T08:38:37.85647Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.529687Z'),
(9004, 'vocabulary', '屁', '屁', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9004', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%81', 33, 1, '2021-05-17T08:41:53.146027Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.345217Z'),
(9005, 'vocabulary', '屁理屈', '屁理屈', 'You already know 理屈 means "logic" or "argument." So what''s <kanji>flatulence</kanji> logic? It''s an <vocabulary>illogical argument</vocabulary>, a <vocabulary>far-fetched argument</vocabulary>, or sometimes a <vocabulary>lame excuse</vocabulary>. It''s all just hot air!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9005', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%81%E7%90%86%E5%B1%88', 33, 1, '2021-05-17T08:54:44.203601Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.39888Z'),
(9006, 'vocabulary', '完璧', '完璧', 'A <kanji>perfect</kanji> <kanji>disc</kanji> is <vocabulary>flawless</vocabulary>. It''s just <vocabulary>perfect</vocabulary>. 

As you may already know, in Japan the symbol ⭕ is used to mark correct answers. See how it looks like a perfect disc?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9006', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%8C%E7%92%A7', 38, 1, '2021-05-17T09:08:24.684343Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.611109Z'),
(9007, 'vocabulary', '痒い', '痒い', 'You can guess this is an adjective from the い at the end. The meaning for the kanji is <kanji>itchy</kanji>, and the adjective version is <vocabulary>itchy</vocabulary> too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9007', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%97%92%E3%81%84', 23, 1, '2021-05-17T09:12:00.627937Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.63589Z'),
(9008, 'vocabulary', '苛む', '苛む', 'As usual, you can guess this is a verb thanks to the う sound on the end. The kanji means <kanji>frustration</kanji>, and unfortunately frustration tends <vocabulary>to torment</vocabulary> people. This verb is pretty advanced, but still useful to know!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9008', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8B%9B%E3%82%80', 45, 1, '2021-05-17T09:40:54.449686Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.656808Z'),
(9009, 'vocabulary', '天王星', '天王星', 'The <kanji>heaven</kanji> <kanji>king</kanji> <kanji>star</kanji> is the planet <vocabulary>Uranus</vocabulary>. Knowledge of Greek mythology will definitely help you here, because the English name is a reference to the Greek god of the skies, and the Japanese name is a direct translation of that!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9009', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A9%E7%8E%8B%E6%98%9F', 13, 1, '2021-05-17T10:02:28.180847Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T02:27:52.417356Z'),
(9010, 'vocabulary', '火星', '火星', 'The <kanji>fire</kanji> <kanji>star</kanji> is <vocabulary>Mars</vocabulary>. This should be easy to remember, because Mars is the only planet in our solar system that''s the color of fire.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9010', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%81%AB%E6%98%9F', 7, 1, '2021-05-17T10:06:22.419534Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T02:27:52.417356Z'),
(9011, 'vocabulary', '木星', '木星', 'The <kanji>tree</kanji> <kanji>star</kanji> is the planet <vocabulary>Jupiter</vocabulary>. Imagine Jupiter covered in very strange trees — the kind that are able to take root in gas! It''s a pretty weird image, but will hopefully help you to remember this word. Alternatively, think about how the clouds around Jupiter make patterns that look a lot like wood grain.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9011', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9C%A8%E6%98%9F', 7, 1, '2021-05-17T10:10:08.844955Z', NULL, '2025-10-28T02:27:52.417356Z'),
(9013, 'vocabulary', '冥王星', '冥王星', 'The <kanji>underworld</kanji> <kanji>king</kanji> <kanji>star</kanji> is the dwarf planet <vocabulary>Pluto</vocabulary>. Since Pluto was the ruler of the underworld in classical mythology, this makes a lot of sense!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9013', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%A5%E7%8E%8B%E6%98%9F', 60, 1, '2021-05-17T10:29:02.676923Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:51:44.236861Z'),
(9014, 'vocabulary', '冥界', '冥界', 'The <kanji>underworld</kanji> <kanji>world</kanji> is the <vocabulary>underworld</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>Hades</vocabulary>, or the <vocabulary>realm of the dead</vocabulary>.

This word is a bit poetic, and mostly used in writing, particularly in contexts like Buddhism, spirituality, and ancient mythologies. It''s usually interchangeable with 冥土 (which is also in this level), but 冥土 is used more in certain set expressions, which we''ll mention in the page for that word!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9014', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%A5%E7%95%8C', 60, 1, '2021-05-17T10:32:51.79725Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:51:44.236861Z'),
(9015, 'vocabulary', '冥土', '冥土', 'The <kanji>underworld</kanji> <kanji>dirt</kanji> is another word for the <vocabulary>realm of the dead</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>underworld</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>Hades</vocabulary>. Like the word 冥界 (also in this level), it''s rather poetic, and mostly used in written contexts involving spirituality, Buddhism, and ancient mythologies.

There are a few set expressions that only use 冥土, though. One example is 冥土の土産, "a souvenir to take to the realm of the dead". It refers to something abstract that is so valuable, it''s worth taking to the underworld, such as the solution to a great mystery, or a happy memory.

Another expression is 冥土の旅支度, which means "preparations for the journey to the realm of the dead", and can refer to the preparation of the deceased before a funeral, as wells as certain preparations made by Buddhists while they''re still alive.

You''ll also see this word written as 冥途 sometimes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9015', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%A5%E5%9C%9F', 60, 1, '2021-05-17T10:36:21.089218Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:51:44.236861Z'),
(9018, 'vocabulary', '飲酒運転', '飲酒運転', 'You know that 飲酒 is "drinking alcohol" and 運転する is "to drive", so it makes sense that this word means <vocabulary>drunk driving</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>drinking and driving</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9018', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%B2%E9%85%92%E9%81%8B%E8%BB%A2', 21, 1, '2021-05-17T10:56:29.762216Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.822392Z'),
(9019, 'vocabulary', '飲酒', '飲酒', '<kanji>Drink</kanji> <kanji>alcohol</kanji> is <vocabulary>drinking alcohol</vocabulary>, or just <vocabulary>drinking</vocabulary>. This always refers to alcohol though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9019', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%B2%E9%85%92', 19, 1, '2021-05-17T11:37:58.19221Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.844761Z'),
(9020, 'vocabulary', '出身', '出身', 'The <kanji>exit</kanji> one''s <kanji>body</kanji> comes from can also be called <vocabulary>one''s origin</vocabulary>.

出身 often translates as <vocabulary>one''s hometown</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>one''s birthplace</vocabulary>. But you can also use it with a school or company someone "originated" from, like in the phrase ハーバード大学出身 (a graduate of Harvard), or トヨタ出身 (someone whose career got started at Toyota).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9020', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E8%BA%AB', 9, 1, '2021-05-17T11:42:12.292738Z', NULL, '2026-02-23T01:19:55.826853Z'),
(9021, 'vocabulary', '関わる', '関わる', 'You know that the kanji means <kanji>related</kanji>. As a verb, this often means <vocabulary>to be involved</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be related</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>to be connected</vocabulary>. Basically, things that have to do with being related to something else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9021', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%96%A2%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2021-05-17T11:47:35.424767Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.896761Z'),
(9022, 'vocabulary', '高度', '高度', 'Something that is <kanji>tall</kanji> in <kanji>degree</kanji>s can either refer to its <vocabulary>altitude</vocabulary>, or to the fact that it''s quite <vocabulary>high-level</vocabulary>. 

Note that this can refer to any altitude, even lower ones. But when used to refer to, for example, skills or a technique, it always talks about a high level or degree.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9022', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%AB%98%E5%BA%A6', 10, 1, '2021-05-17T11:50:09.067111Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.847538Z'),
(9027, 'vocabulary', '築く', '築く', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>construct</kanji> and the verb version is <vocabulary>to build something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to construct something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9027', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AF%89%E3%81%8F', 24, 1, '2021-05-17T12:20:15.094394Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.941424Z'),
(9029, 'vocabulary', '莫大', '莫大', 'Something that is <kanji>endless</kanji>ly <kanji>big</kanji> isn''t just big, but <vocabulary>huge</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>enormous</vocabulary>.

This word is often used in contexts that involve money, but it can also be used for other things like time, energy, and data.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9029', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8E%AB%E5%A4%A7', 25, 1, '2021-05-17T12:42:38.13671Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:04.031742Z'),
(9034, 'vocabulary', '仕える', '仕える', 'Everything we''re <kanji>doing</kanji> is <vocabulary>to serve</vocabulary> you, you know.

This verb is a bit archaic and therefore has a formal feel to it. It''s used to talk about serving emperors, kings, and gods, among others.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9034', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%95%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 45, 1, '2021-05-17T13:16:08.979079Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:14.990327Z'),
(9035, 'vocabulary', '初恋', '初恋', 'Your <kanji>first</kanji> <kanji>love</kanji> is your <vocabulary>first love</vocabulary> or your <vocabulary>first crush</vocabulary>. This usually refers to the first time you fall in love, but it can also refer to the person you fall in love with.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9035', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%9D%E6%81%8B', 18, 1, '2021-05-17T13:22:49.79537Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:28:35.812439Z'),
(9036, 'vocabulary', '初耳', '初耳', 'The <kanji>first</kanji> time something reaches your <kanji>ear</kanji>, it''s <vocabulary>something heard for the first time</vocabulary>. If it''s never entered your ear before, it''s <vocabulary>news</vocabulary> to you!

', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9036', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%9D%E8%80%B3', 15, 1, '2021-05-17T13:25:03.186724Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.463771Z'),
(9037, 'vocabulary', '回答', '回答', 'You know 回 means "times," but it can also mean "revolve." You <kanji>revolve</kanji> around an <kanji>answer</kanji> until you''re ready to give your <vocabulary>answer</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>response</vocabulary> to the question!

回答 refers to responding to a question, inquiry, or request. For example, you use 回答 when answering a questionnaire or letting someone know whether you can attend a party.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9037', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9B%9E%E7%AD%94', 10, 1, '2021-05-17T13:27:55.879016Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:57.588731Z'),
(9038, 'vocabulary', '解答', '解答', 'You <kanji>solve</kanji> a problem by working toward the <kanji>answer</kanji>. This is the act of <vocabulary>solving</vocabulary>, which eventually leads to the final <vocabulary>answer</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>solution</vocabulary>!

解答 specifically refers to solving problems and giving answers with a correct solution, often for structured contexts like exams or math problems. It can also refer to the answer or solution itself.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9038', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A3%E7%AD%94', 12, 1, '2021-05-17T13:30:19.032001Z', NULL, '2025-07-24T17:39:57.774158Z'),
(9039, 'vocabulary', '昆虫', '昆虫', 'An <kanji>insect</kanji> <kanji>insect</kanji> is... you guessed it! An <vocabulary>insect</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9039', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%86%E8%99%AB', 19, 1, '2021-05-17T13:32:09.796826Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.107673Z'),
(9040, 'vocabulary', '殺虫剤', '殺虫剤', 'What <kanji>kill</kanji>s <kanji>insect</kanji>s with one <kanji>dose</kanji>? That''s an <vocabulary>insecticide</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>pesticide</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9040', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AE%BA%E8%99%AB%E5%89%A4', 42, 1, '2021-05-17T13:33:53.395392Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.13072Z'),
(9041, 'vocabulary', '岡山城', '岡山城', 'This is the same 岡山 that you learned with 岡山県. So what''s Okayama <kanji>castle</kanji>? Yep, <vocabulary>Okayama Castle</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9041', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B2%A1%E5%B1%B1%E5%9F%8E', 25, 1, '2021-05-17T13:41:57.245984Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:04.047557Z'),
(9042, 'vocabulary', '人里', '人里', 'A <kanji>person</kanji>''s <kanji>hometown</kanji> is a <vocabulary>human habitation</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>village</vocabulary>. This word has a slightly old-fashioned feel, and it''s usually used for small villages in the countryside.

It''s often combined with はなれた (separated) to talk about how far away from the nearest human habitation something is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9042', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%BA%E9%87%8C', 7, 1, '2021-05-17T13:44:15.437273Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:41:25.964816Z'),
(9043, 'vocabulary', '里芋', '里芋', 'A <kanji>hometown</kanji> <kanji>potato</kanji> is a <vocabulary>taro</vocabulary>. In Japan, taro is the kind of potato that everyone has in their hometown, because it''s been around for such a long time and it''ll grow anywhere.

In English, "taro" can sometimes be used for other root vegetables too, but just keep in mind this word refers specifically to the <vocabulary>Japanese taro</vocabulary> — a small, starchy, potato-like vegetable.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9043', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8C%E8%8A%8B', 33, 1, '2021-05-17T13:46:06.157129Z', NULL, '2025-10-23T04:02:13.526891Z'),
(9044, 'vocabulary', 'お持ち帰り', 'お持ち帰り', 'When you''re <kanji>hold</kanji>ing food and <kanji>return</kanji>ing home with it, you''re likely getting <vocabulary>takeout</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9044', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E6%8C%81%E3%81%A1%E5%B8%B0%E3%82%8A', 19, 1, '2021-05-17T13:48:22.165576Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T06:41:01.299215Z'),
(9045, 'vocabulary', '任す', '任す', 'The meaning of this word is the same as 任せる. Both words are pretty common, but 任す is slightly less common than 任せる.

Here''s the mnemonic again, just in case you need a refresher:

If you pass a <kanji>duty</kanji> on to someone, that means you''re going <vocabulary>to entrust something</vocabulary> to that person, or you''re going <vocabulary>to delegate something</vocabulary>. In other words, you''re going <vocabulary>to leave something to</vocabulary> them, so that they do it for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9045', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%BB%E3%81%99', 29, 1, '2021-05-17T13:51:42.14673Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.241954Z'),
(9046, 'vocabulary', '我が国', '我が国', 'You learned that 我が means "our", so our <kanji>country</kanji> is simply <vocabulary>our country</vocabulary>.

Just like 我が itself, this expression is somewhat old-fashioned and stylized. It comes up a lot in political discourse and other formal contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9046', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%91%E3%81%8C%E5%9B%BD', 31, 1, '2021-05-17T13:55:43.981903Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.264542Z'),
(9047, 'vocabulary', '我が〜', '我が〜', 'Here we have <kanji>I</kanji> followed by the particle が, giving us a somewhat old-fashioned and stylized way of say <vocabulary>our</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>one''s own</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>my</vocabulary>.

One explanation for this is that in ancient Japanese, が was used as a possessive particle, much like の is in modern Japanese. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9047', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%88%91%E3%81%8C%E3%80%9C', 30, 1, '2021-05-17T14:00:42.793967Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.56207Z'),
(9048, 'vocabulary', '乗車券', '乗車券', 'If you want to <kanji>ride</kanji> in a <kanji>car</kanji>, the <kanji>ticket</kanji> you''ll need is a <vocabulary>passenger ticket</vocabulary>.

乗車券 is primarily a <vocabulary>ticket</vocabulary> for a train ride, so think of "car" here as a train car. It can also refer to tickets for other rides, like Ferris wheels, cable cars, ropeways, or certain buses that require a prior ticket purchase.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9048', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%97%E8%BB%8A%E5%88%B8', 29, 1, '2021-05-17T14:06:11.104139Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.308575Z'),
(9049, 'vocabulary', '乗客', '乗客', 'If you take a <kanji>ride</kanji> as a <kanji>guest</kanji> (or a <kanji>customer</kanji>), that makes you a <vocabulary>passenger</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9049', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%97%E5%AE%A2', 15, 1, '2021-05-17T14:11:15.807762Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.888518Z'),
(9050, 'vocabulary', '乗馬', '乗馬', 'If you <kanji>ride</kanji> a <kanji>horse</kanji>, that''s <vocabulary>horseback riding</vocabulary> or, in some parts of the world, simply <vocabulary>horse riding</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9050', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B9%97%E9%A6%AC', 15, 1, '2021-05-17T14:12:43.523618Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.453771Z'),
(9052, 'vocabulary', '頁', '頁', 'The kanji and the vocabulary are exactly the same, so they share meanings as well.

This word is often written in katakana as ページ, especially in informal writing and online. But you''ll also see the kanji version, especially to refer to the pages of printed texts and in more formal writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9052', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%81', 10, 1, '2021-05-17T14:20:55.688139Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.912537Z'),
(9053, 'vocabulary', '癒す', '癒す', 'The kanji means <kanji>healing</kanji> and the verb version means <vocabulary>to heal something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to cure something</vocabulary>, and even <vocabulary>to quench a thirst</vocabulary>, both literally and metaphorically. As you can probably guess, this last use has a poetic feel to it.

Like many verbs ending in す, this verb is transitive, meaning it''s being done to something. So you can use it when you cure an illness, heal a broken heart, or quench your thirst for adventure!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9053', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%99%92%E3%81%99', 57, 1, '2021-05-17T14:23:15.280294Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.400632Z'),
(9054, 'vocabulary', '弊社', '弊社', 'An <kanji>evil</kanji> <kanji>company</kanji> is actually <vocabulary>our company</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>our humble company</vocabulary>. We''re not going to say our company is virtuous, are we? That wouldn''t be humble at all. So instead we call it evil, to show our humility.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9054', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BC%8A%E7%A4%BE', 56, 1, '2021-05-17T14:40:21.353172Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.42425Z'),
(9055, 'vocabulary', '北斗七星', '北斗七星', 'What can you see in the <kanji>north</kanji> that looks like a <kanji>ladle</kanji> and is made up of <kanji>seven</kanji> <kanji>star</kanji>s? That''s the group of stars we call <vocabulary>the Big Dipper</vocabulary> or, depending on where you are in the world, perhaps <vocabulary>the Plough</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9055', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%97%E6%96%97%E4%B8%83%E6%98%9F', 44, 1, '2021-05-17T14:44:03.15488Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.438028Z'),
(9056, 'vocabulary', '一斗缶', '一斗缶', 'A <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>ladle</kanji> <kanji>can</kanji> is a <vocabulary>eighteen-liter container</vocabulary>, which is also a <vocabulary>five-gallon container</vocabulary>. That must be one big ladle!

If you live in Japan you''ve probably seen these. They''re rectangular shaped containers made of metal that are used mostly for industrial purposes, for storing liquids like paint, fuel and chemicals. The 斗 actually refers to an old Japanese measurement, which is roughly equivalent to eighteen liters.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9056', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%96%97%E7%BC%B6', 44, 1, '2021-05-17T14:45:31.141904Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.469376Z'),
(9057, 'vocabulary', '綾取り', '綾取り', 'One <kanji>design</kanji> that you can <kanji>take</kanji> anywhere with you is <vocabulary>cat''s cradle</vocabulary>, the game where you loop string around your hands and fingers to create patterns.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9057', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%BE%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8A', 52, 1, '2021-05-17T14:47:59.949788Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.234204Z'),
(9058, 'vocabulary', '硬貨', '硬貨', '<kanji>Stiff</kanji> <kanji>freight</kanji> always has to be paid for in <vocabulary>coin</vocabulary>s, because coins are also stiff. You can''t go paying for something stiff with something not stiff, that would make no sense at all.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9058', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A1%AC%E8%B2%A8', 45, 1, '2021-05-17T15:20:07.888973Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.514126Z'),
(9059, 'vocabulary', '鑑みる', '鑑みる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. You learned this kanji means "model," but it can also mean "<kanji>take into account</kanji>," so the verb version means <vocabulary>to take something into account</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be in light of</vocabulary>.

鑑みる literally comes from the idea of "looking at something closely to judge it." It''s a formal word used to describe the act of thoughtfully considering something based on things like prior experiences or surrounding circumstances.

It''s a transitive verb, but it can take を or に to mark the thing you''re taking into account. Both particles are grammatically correct, but に sounds slightly more formal, which often matches the contexts where this word is used.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9059', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%91%91%E3%81%BF%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2021-05-17T15:31:00.242191Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T23:46:28.709959Z'),
(9060, 'vocabulary', '彫る', '彫る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>carve</kanji> and the verb version is <vocabulary>to carve something</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to engrave something</vocabulary>.

This is a transitive verb, meaning it can take a direct object. You engrave letters into a stone, and you carve a scary face into a pumpkin. Those letters and that face are your objects.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9060', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BD%AB%E3%82%8B', 47, 1, '2021-05-17T15:34:07.672561Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.559512Z'),
(9061, 'vocabulary', '同窓会', '同窓会', 'A <kanji>same</kanji> <kanji>window</kanji> <kanji>meeting</kanji> is a meeting with people who spent a long time gazing out of the same classroom window as you. Who are those people? They''re your former classmates, and this is a <vocabulary>class reunion</vocabulary>. It can also mean <vocabulary>alumni association</vocabulary>, which is still something you do with former classmates, so it makes sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9061', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8C%E7%AA%93%E4%BC%9A', 36, 1, '2021-05-17T15:44:26.491653Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.582428Z'),
(9062, 'vocabulary', '抱っこ', '抱っこ', 'You know that the kanji means <kanji>hug</kanji> and the verb 抱く means "to hug". This noun is similar, but it usually means carrying by hugging, so <vocabulary>holding in one''s arms</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>carrying</vocabulary>. This is one that children use a lot!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9062', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%B1%E3%81%A3%E3%81%93', 37, 1, '2021-05-17T15:50:05.124823Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.603869Z'),
(9063, 'vocabulary', '雄', '雄', 'The kanji means male and this also means male. It''s mostly used for animals.

Note that this is often written in katakana, as オス.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9063', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%84', 31, 1, '2021-05-17T15:54:26.782756Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.631952Z'),
(9064, 'vocabulary', '居候', '居候', 'To stay <kanji>alive</kanji> in some <kanji>climate</kanji>s, some have no choice but to be a <vocabulary>freeloader</vocabulary>. That is, <vocabulary>a lodger who pays nothing</vocabulary>. If you live somewhere with an inhospitable climate but you can''t afford to pay rent, your only option is to live with someone for free.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9064', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B1%85%E5%80%99', 33, 1, '2021-05-17T15:58:36.196179Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.362136Z'),
(9065, 'vocabulary', '温厚', '温厚', 'When a <kanji>warm</kanji> temper is applied extra <kanji>thick</kanji>, it leads to a person having a <vocabulary>mild-mannered</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>gentle</vocabulary> personality.

Note that this is a more formal term, so you''ll usually encounter it in writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9065', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B8%A9%E5%8E%9A', 33, 1, '2021-05-17T16:00:56.035706Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.84403Z'),
(9066, 'vocabulary', '由来', '由来', 'What''s the <kanji>reason</kanji> that something <kanji>come</kanji>s into being? Its <vocabulary>origin</vocabulary>, of course. Nothing can come into the world without a <vocabulary>source</vocabulary>, because it''s always <vocabulary>coming from</vocabulary> somewhere.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9066', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B1%E6%9D%A5', 15, 1, '2021-05-17T16:02:40.972585Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:49.47382Z'),
(9067, 'vocabulary', '経由', '経由', 'You <kanji>pass through</kanji> somewhere for a <kanji>reason</kanji>, so you''re going <vocabulary>via</vocabulary> that place.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9067', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%8C%E7%94%B1', 20, 1, '2021-05-17T16:18:29.296523Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.724239Z'),
(9068, 'vocabulary', '由緒', '由緒', 'The main <kanji>reason</kanji> behind your <kanji>connection</kanji> to some people is your <vocabulary>lineage</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>history</vocabulary>. Lineage and history tie people (especially families) together.

This word can be used for people with a prestigious family lineage as well as for historic buildings, especially temples and shrines.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9068', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B1%E7%B7%92', 38, 1, '2021-05-17T16:22:15.393859Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T00:09:48.530936Z'),
(9069, 'vocabulary', '由緒正しい', '由緒正しい', 'Someone or something whose 由緒 (lineage) is 正しい (correct) is <vocabulary>of honorable lineage</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>venerable</vocabulary> indeed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9069', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B1%E7%B7%92%E6%AD%A3%E3%81%97%E3%81%84', 39, 1, '2021-05-17T16:25:20.958773Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T00:09:48.530936Z'),
(9070, 'vocabulary', '〜分', '〜分', 'What''s <kanji>part</kanji> of an hour? A <vocabulary>minute</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>minutes</vocabulary>! This word is the suffix version of 分, so it goes after numbers to say how many minutes, and is used for telling the time too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9070', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E5%88%86', 7, 1, '2021-05-17T16:28:42.829636Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.036376Z'),
(9071, 'vocabulary', '種', '種', 'What <kanji>kind</kanji>s of <vocabulary>seed</vocabulary>s are you going to sow? Think about all the different kinds of seeds, and the kinds of plants they''ll grow into.

This can also mean <vocabulary>source</vocabulary>, as in a source of worry or joy, or even of a good story.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9071', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A8%AE', 20, 1, '2021-05-17T16:31:12.308612Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.810866Z'),
(9072, 'vocabulary', '任せる', '任せる', 'If you pass a <kanji>duty</kanji> on to someone, that means you''re going <vocabulary>to entrust something</vocabulary> to that person, or you''re going <vocabulary>to delegate something</vocabulary>. In other words, you''re going <vocabulary>to leave something to</vocabulary> them, so that they do it for you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9072', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%BB%E3%81%9B%E3%82%8B', 26, 1, '2021-05-17T17:03:44.04763Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.832333Z'),
(9073, 'vocabulary', 'お任せ', 'お任せ', 'This word comes from 任せる, but without the る on the end it''s a noun. You also have the honorific お on the beginning, which is a clue that you''re addressing someone else. So you''re entrusting something to the person you''re talking to, and this means <vocabulary>leaving decisions to someone else</vocabulary>. This is used a lot in restaurants, where it means <vocabulary>chef''s choice</vocabulary> or simply <vocabulary>omakase</vocabulary>. In other words, you''re leaving the choice of food to the chef.

It can be used in other contexts, too, like if you leave your haircut up to your stylist or a flower arrangement up to the florist, or even if you follow the recommended program at the gym.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9073', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E4%BB%BB%E3%81%9B', 27, 1, '2021-05-17T17:06:05.642159Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.855227Z'),
(9074, 'vocabulary', '怪我', '怪我', 'Do you know how <kanji>suspicious</kanji> <kanji>I</kanji> get if I have an <vocabulary>injury</vocabulary>? I start suspecting everyone around me of having given me the injury. It was you, wasn''t it?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9074', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%80%AA%E6%88%91', 36, 1, '2021-05-17T17:12:43.170431Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.878105Z'),
(9075, 'vocabulary', '解凍', '解凍', 'If you <kanji>untie</kanji> something that''s <kanji>frozen</kanji>, you''re <vocabulary>thawing</vocabulary> it. The best way to go about <vocabulary>defrosting</vocabulary> anything is always to untie it first.

In the context of computer files, this word can also mean <vocabulary>unzipping</vocabulary>. We unzip files in English, but in Japanese we thaw them!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9075', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A7%A3%E5%87%8D', 28, 1, '2021-05-17T17:15:21.520646Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T20:23:40.067262Z'),
(9076, 'vocabulary', '豊富', '豊富', 'Something that''s <kanji>plentiful</kanji> and <kanji>rich</kanji> is <vocabulary>abundant</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>plentiful</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>rich</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9076', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B1%8A%E5%AF%8C', 31, 1, '2021-05-17T17:17:32.734772Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:15.919663Z'),
(9077, 'vocabulary', '双方', '双方', 'You''ve got a <kanji>pair</kanji> of <kanji>direction</kanji>s, so this is something that concerns <vocabulary>both parties</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>both sides</vocabulary>. Things are going in two directions, not just one! 

双方 is a relatively formal term that is often used in contexts like <vocabulary>mutual</vocabulary> agreements, negotiations, or discussions where both sides are actively engaged.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9077', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%8C%E6%96%B9', 42, 1, '2021-05-17T17:55:09.704249Z', NULL, '2025-07-09T07:15:38.886983Z'),
(9078, 'vocabulary', '交代', '交代', 'If you <kanji>mix</kanji> and <kanji>substitute</kanji> two people, that''s a <vocabulary>change</vocabulary> of personnel, or a <vocabulary>substitution</vocabulary>. Perhaps those people are even working in <vocabulary>shift</vocabulary>s, so they mix and substitute each other at regular intervals.

You''ll see this word in pretty diverse contexts, because it can refer to anything from a change of political regime, to the substitution of a player during a match, to factory shifts. The underlying meaning is that one person or group is switched out with another.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9078', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%A4%E4%BB%A3', 9, 1, '2021-05-17T17:59:14.721164Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.00878Z'),
(9080, 'vocabulary', '押さえる', '押さえる', 'The kanji means <kanji>push</kanji>, and this verb means things like <vocabulary>to hold something down</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to grasp something</vocabulary>. This verb is pretty versatile, and you can use it if you''re holding down a piece of paper, holding a cloth to a wound, or grasping a concept.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9080', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8A%BC%E3%81%95%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2021-05-17T18:08:41.750025Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.522128Z'),
(9081, 'vocabulary', '献立', '献立', 'I will <kanji>offer</kanji> you food and <kanji>stand</kanji> here while you eat it, because I curated this <vocabulary>menu</vocabulary> and I want to make sure you enjoy it.

This word doesn''t mean the physical menu you get in a restaurant; rather, it refers to a <vocabulary>meal plan</vocabulary>, whether that means the sequence of courses for a single meal or the general meal plan for someone''s entire week.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9081', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8C%AE%E7%AB%8B', 45, 1, '2021-05-17T18:12:22.979266Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T22:08:09.64093Z'),
(9082, 'vocabulary', '空手', '空手', 'What can you do with <kanji>empty</kanji> <kanji>hand</kanji>s? <vocabulary>Karate</vocabulary>, that''s what. Karate is practiced without weapons, so with empty hands!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9082', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA%E6%89%8B', 9, 1, '2021-05-17T18:15:11.942099Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.0241Z'),
(9083, 'vocabulary', '出張', '出張', 'You <kanji>exit</kanji> the building and <kanji>stretch</kanji> in preparation for your <vocabulary>business trip</vocabulary>. This is the routine you always follow for your business trips: exit, and stretch.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9083', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E5%BC%B5', 23, 1, '2021-05-17T18:19:02.331451Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.059541Z'),
(9084, 'vocabulary', '出社', '出社', 'If you <kanji>exit</kanji> somewhere in order to go to your <kanji>company</kanji>, that''s <vocabulary>going to the office</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>arriving at the office</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>coming to work</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9084', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%BA%E7%A4%BE', 6, 1, '2021-05-17T18:21:46.224075Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.181203Z'),
(9085, 'vocabulary', '無くなる', '無くなる', 'Here we have <kanji>nothing</kanji>, followed by the verb なる. A verb that means "become nothing"? That''ll be <vocabulary>to be gone</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to run out</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be lost</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to disappear</vocabulary>.

This can be translated in various ways depending on the situation, but just remember that it always refers to something changing from being there, to not being there.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9085', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E3%81%8F%E3%81%AA%E3%82%8B', 20, 1, '2021-05-17T18:26:43.419117Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.104182Z'),
(9086, 'vocabulary', '無くす', '無くす', 'This word ends with a う sound so you can guess it''s a verb. The kanji means <kanji>nothing</kanji>, and you''ll end up with nothing if you want to <vocabulary>to get rid of something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to do away with something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to lose something</vocabulary>.

This verb is transitive, so the action is happening to something. So maybe you''re getting rid of debt, or discrimination, or stress... Whatever it is, there''s a direct object with this verb!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9086', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%84%A1%E3%81%8F%E3%81%99', 21, 1, '2021-05-17T18:38:15.41478Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.126219Z'),
(9087, 'vocabulary', '青春', '青春', 'The time of <kanji>young</kanji> <kanji>spring</kanji> for humans is really just their <vocabulary>youth</vocabulary>. Just like plants in spring, your <vocabulary>adolescence</vocabulary> is when you start growing to your full potential! You might even say it''s <vocabulary>the springtime of one''s life</vocabulary>.

This generally refers to the period when you’re an energetic teenager and young adult. It can have connotations of exhilarating experiences and bittersweet feelings, as well as nostalgic memories.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9087', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9D%92%E6%98%A5', 17, 1, '2021-05-17T18:41:10.348181Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T19:35:30.470443Z'),
(9088, 'vocabulary', 'その他', 'その他', 'その means "that" or "the", and その plus <kanji>other</kanji> means things like <vocabulary>the others</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>other</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>other than that</vocabulary>, depending on the context. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9088', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%9D%E3%81%AE%E4%BB%96', 5, 1, '2021-05-17T18:43:55.930215Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T04:47:22.214427Z'),
(9089, 'vocabulary', '豚骨ラーメン', '豚骨ラーメン', '<kanji>Pig</kanji> <kanji>bone</kanji> ramen is <vocabulary>tonkotsu ramen</vocabulary> or, very logically indeed, <vocabulary>pork bone ramen</vocabulary>.

豚骨ラーメン is a type of Japanese ramen made with a rich, creamy broth from 豚骨 (pork bones). The bones are simmered for hours to extract the flavors, resulting in a flavorful and hearty soup. It''s so delicious, you''ll be back for more. <em>Bone</em> appetit!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9089', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B1%9A%E9%AA%A8%E3%83%A9%E3%83%BC%E3%83%A1%E3%83%B3', 30, 1, '2021-05-17T18:47:34.607531Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.511592Z'),
(9090, 'vocabulary', '豚カツ', '豚カツ', '豚 comes from 豚肉 (pork), and カツ comes from the word "cutlet" and means just that. And a <kanji>pork</kanji> カツ is what any Japanese food fan will probably know as simply <vocabulary>tonkatsu</vocabulary>, a.k.a. <vocabulary>breaded pork cutlet</vocabulary>.

豚カツ is a Japanese pork cutlet coated in crispy panko breadcrumbs and deep-fried, making it light and crunchy. It''s usually served with a tasty sweet-savory sauce. It''s so good, you''ll pig out on it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9090', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B1%9A%E3%82%AB%E3%83%84', 29, 1, '2021-05-17T18:50:14.531189Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.220795Z'),
(9091, 'vocabulary', '〜毎に', '〜毎に', 'Add a に to <kanji>every</kanji> and you get <vocabulary>for every</vocabulary>. This means something happens <vocabulary>at intervals of</vocabulary> a certain time frame or milestone, like <vocabulary>once every</vocabulary> year, month, day, etc.!

Note that this is very frequently written in hiragana, but you''ll encounter the kanji version as well, especially when it follows a number.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9091', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E6%AF%8E%E3%81%AB', 25, 1, '2021-05-17T18:52:16.027328Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:04.182715Z'),
(9094, 'vocabulary', '激怒', '激怒', 'Being <kanji>fierce</kanji> and <kanji>angry</kanji> means you''re filled with <vocabulary>rage</vocabulary>, or for you to <vocabulary>be enraged</vocabulary>.

激怒 is a relatively formal word, often used in literature to describe someone flying into a rage or experiencing intense, uncontrollable anger. Therefore, you’ll frequently encounter this as a suru-verb in the form of 激怒する.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9094', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BF%80%E6%80%92', 29, 1, '2021-05-17T19:16:53.632095Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.276701Z'),
(9096, 'vocabulary', '勿論', '勿論', 'You <kanji>must not</kanji> consider this a <kanji>theory</kanji>, because <vocabulary>of course</vocabulary> it''s a fact. <vocabulary>Needless to say</vocabulary>, this is the way things are.

This word is often written in kana, but it''s worth knowing the kanji version too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9096', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%BF%E8%AB%96', 55, 1, '2021-05-17T19:23:28.337271Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T16:59:03.937037Z'),
(9097, 'vocabulary', '勿体ない', '勿体ない', 'You <kanji>must not</kanji> use your <kanji>body</kanji> for anything <vocabulary>wasteful</vocabulary>, because that would be <vocabulary>a waste</vocabulary>. You''re <vocabulary>too good</vocabulary> for that!

This word is used in all kinds of situations where there''s a feeling that something is going to waste, including food, money, time, talent, and opportunities. You can also use it to say that someone is too good for a job, or even for another person. While it''s usually written in kana, you''ll see it in kanji too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9097', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8B%BF%E4%BD%93%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84', 58, 1, '2021-05-17T19:32:56.504593Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.323174Z'),
(9098, 'vocabulary', '瑛斗', '瑛斗', 'A <kanji>crystal</kanji> <kanji>ladle</kanji> is the Japanese given name <vocabulary>Eito</vocabulary>. If you know the singer-songwriter Eito, imagine him holding a crystal ladle. If not, any Eito will do! Other public figures with the name include pro baseball player Tanaka Eito (田中 瑛斗) and Takarazuka actor Namiki Eito (波輝 瑛斗).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9098', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%91%9B%E6%96%97', 55, 1, '2021-05-18T12:30:40.47115Z', NULL, '2025-10-07T00:14:30.057128Z'),
(9099, 'vocabulary', '遼太', '遼太', '<kanji>Distant</kanji> <kanji>fat</kanji> makes up the Japanese given name <vocabulary>Ryota</vocabulary>. Imagine soccer player Noguchi Ryota (野口 遼太) kicking a fat soccer ball into the distance while voice actor Asari Ryota (浅利 遼太) commentates.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9099', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%BC%E5%A4%AA', 55, 1, '2021-05-18T12:36:38.589563Z', NULL, '2025-09-24T17:23:41.203717Z'),
(9100, 'vocabulary', '亮平', '亮平', '<kanji>Clear</kanji> <kanji>flat</kanji> is one way to write the Japanese given name <vocabulary>Ryohei</vocabulary>. 

Some well-known people with the name include metal sculptor Miyada Ryohei (宮田 亮平), actor Suzuki Ryohei (鈴木 亮平), and model and actor Ōtani Ryohei (大谷 亮平), who acts in both Japanese and Korean films and dramas.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9100', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BA%AE%E5%B9%B3', 51, 1, '2021-05-18T12:38:04.678182Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.397731Z'),
(9101, 'vocabulary', '綾乃', '綾乃', 'This is a <kanji>design</kanji> <kanji>from</kanji> <vocabulary>Ayano</vocabulary>. Do you know an Ayano who''s good at designing things? A couple creative Ayanos include traditional Okinawan folk singer and sanshin player Uema Ayano (上間 綾乃), and author Takeda Ayano (武田 綾乃) who won the Yoshikawa Eiji Literary Newcomer Award in 2021.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9101', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B6%BE%E4%B9%83', 52, 1, '2021-05-18T12:41:02.428654Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.152976Z'),
(9102, 'vocabulary', '大輔', '大輔', 'Who''s a <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>help</kanji>? That''ll be <vocabulary>Daisuke</vocabulary>!

Famous Daisukes include film director and screenwriter Itō Daisuke (伊藤 大輔), figure skater Takahashi Daisuke (髙橋 大輔), and actor and singer Namikawa Daisuke (浪川 大輔).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9102', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E8%BC%94', 52, 1, '2021-05-18T12:42:21.559118Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T04:32:16.221844Z'),
(9103, 'vocabulary', '諒一郎', '諒一郎', 'If you only <kanji>comprehend</kanji> <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>guy</kanji>, make that guy <vocabulary>Ryoichiro</vocabulary>.

The name 諒一郎 isn''t very common, but it''s worth learning because the kanji 諒 is used in lots of names, and both 諒 and 一郎 are names in their own right. So by learning this name, you should be able to read a whole lot of other names, too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9103', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%AB%92%E4%B8%80%E9%83%8E', 53, 1, '2021-05-18T12:43:43.047003Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.474345Z'),
(9104, 'vocabulary', '莉子', '莉子', '<kanji>Jasmine</kanji> <kanji>child</kanji> is one way to write the girl''s name <vocabulary>Riko</vocabulary>. Imagine fashioning a child out of jasmine, and calling her Riko. Examples of people named Riko include pop star and singer Fukumoto Riko (福本 莉子) and idol and actress Nakayama Riko (中山 莉子).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9104', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8E%89%E5%AD%90', 53, 1, '2021-05-18T12:44:40.929195Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.499381Z'),
(9105, 'vocabulary', '駿馬', '駿馬', 'A <kanji>speed</kanji> <kanji>horse</kanji> is a <vocabulary>fast horse</vocabulary>, and a <vocabulary>fine horse</vocabulary>. This word isn''t very common, but it does come up in horse racing contexts, and creative writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9105', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A7%BF%E9%A6%AC', 53, 1, '2021-05-18T12:47:28.752733Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.521424Z'),
(9106, 'vocabulary', '茜色', '茜色', '<kanji>Rose madder</kanji> <kanji>color</kanji> is more simply called <vocabulary>madder red</vocabulary>, which is a kind of <vocabulary>deep red</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>crimson</vocabulary>. This word is used most often to describe the color of the sky at sunset in literature.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9106', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8C%9C%E8%89%B2', 54, 1, '2021-05-18T12:48:44.791815Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.545832Z'),
(9107, 'vocabulary', '那智の滝', '那智の滝', '<kanji>What</kanji> <kanji>wisdom</kanji> <kanji>waterfall</kanji> is <vocabulary>Nachi Falls</vocabulary>, one of the most famous (and wisest) waterfalls in Japan. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9107', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%82%A3%E6%99%BA%E3%81%AE%E6%BB%9D', 56, 1, '2021-05-18T12:51:16.351564Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T00:33:15.236501Z'),
(9108, 'vocabulary', '浄瑠璃', '浄瑠璃', 'If you <kanji>cleanse</kanji> a <kanji>lapis lazuli</kanji> rock until it goes <kanji>glassy</kanji>, you can use it in a <vocabulary>joruri</vocabulary> performance. In case you haven''t heard of joruri, it''s traditional Japanese chanted storytelling, sort of like a <vocabulary>ballad</vocabulary>, that''s accompanied by shamisen music, and often puppets.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9108', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%84%E7%91%A0%E7%92%83', 58, 1, '2021-05-18T12:53:32.506971Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.597724Z'),
(9109, 'vocabulary', '瑠璃色', '瑠璃色', 'What is <kanji>lapis lazuli</kanji> <kanji>glassy</kanji> <kanji>color</kanji>? It''s <vocabulary>bright blue</vocabulary>. If you''ve never seen the rock lapis lazuli, look it up and you''ll see why this means bright blue!

This word is generally used in literature.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9109', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%91%A0%E7%92%83%E8%89%B2', 58, 1, '2021-05-18T12:55:23.895652Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.669143Z'),
(9110, 'vocabulary', '蒼白', '蒼白', 'If you''re <kanji>pale</kanji> <kanji>white</kanji>, you''re <vocabulary>pale</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>pallid</vocabulary>. This word is usually used to talk about the <vocabulary>pallor</vocabulary> of someone''s face.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9110', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%92%BC%E7%99%BD', 57, 1, '2021-05-18T12:57:35.901025Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.695057Z'),
(9112, 'vocabulary', '拓哉', '拓哉', 'The <kanji>cultivation</kanji> of a <kanji>question mark</kanji> is something that only people called <vocabulary>Takuya</vocabulary> can do. Imagine a famous Takuya, like actor and singer Kimura Takuya (木村 拓哉 a.k.a. キムタク), or a Takuya you know, cultivating a huge field of question marks. Rows and rows of question marks. I wonder what he''s going to do with all those question marks once they''re ready to harvest. Other people who must have an abundance of question marks are pro baseball player Kinoshita Takuya (木下 拓哉 a.k.a. キノタク) and actor, model, and K-Pop star Terada Takuya (寺田 拓哉).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9112', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%93%E5%93%89', 57, 1, '2021-05-18T13:00:12.413844Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.71988Z'),
(9113, 'vocabulary', '菅原', '菅原', '<kanji>Sedge</kanji> <kanji>field</kanji> is both <vocabulary>Sugahara</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>Sugawara</vocabulary>, the Japanese family names. Sedgefield is a family name (and a place-name) in English too, so that might help you with this one!

This is a very old surname, said to have originated with Nara period scholar Hanishi-no-sukune (土師宿禰) who changed his name to Sugawara-no-furuhito (菅原古人). Historically, the name has been associated with scholars and writers, including prominent Heian period statesman, scholar, and poet Sugawara-no-michizane (菅原道真) and noblewoman, writer, and poet Sugawara-no-takasue-no-musume (菅原孝標女), also known more simply as Lady Sarashina in the English speaking world.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9113', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%8F%85%E5%8E%9F', 58, 1, '2021-05-18T13:01:32.889099Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.744554Z'),
(9114, 'vocabulary', '酌', '酌', 'If you <kanji>serve</kanji> someone, you''re <vocabulary>pouring alcohol</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>pouring sake</vocabulary> for them. You''re <vocabulary>filling someone''s glass</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9114', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%8C', 58, 1, '2021-05-18T13:02:47.148193Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.768915Z'),
(9115, 'vocabulary', '酌量', '酌量', 'If you <kanji>serve</kanji> someone a certain <kanji>quantity</kanji> of understanding, you''re <vocabulary>making allowances</vocabulary>. You''re <vocabulary>taking into consideration</vocabulary> all extenuating circumstances.
', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9115', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%8C%E9%87%8F', 58, 1, '2021-05-18T13:05:47.635765Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.792433Z'),
(9116, 'vocabulary', '酌み交わす', '酌み交わす', '酌む means "to serve" and the kanji 交 means <kanji>mix</kanji>. What happens if you serve and mix together? You''re going <vocabulary>to pour each other a drink</vocabulary> because you plan  <vocabulary>to drink together</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9116', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%85%8C%E3%81%BF%E4%BA%A4%E3%82%8F%E3%81%99', 60, 1, '2021-05-18T13:07:10.55647Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.816381Z'),
(9117, 'vocabulary', '詠う', '詠う', 'The verb form of <kanji>compose</kanji> is <vocabulary>to compose</vocabulary>. This particular verb is usually used in relation to poetry, and can also mean <vocabulary>to recite</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to sing</vocabulary>, though more in the sense of singing someone''s praises than singing a song.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9117', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%A0%E3%81%86', 60, 1, '2021-05-18T13:09:22.770109Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.843267Z'),
(9118, 'vocabulary', '詠歌', '詠歌', 'A <kanji>compose</kanji>d <kanji>song</kanji> is a <vocabulary>poem</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>song</vocabulary>. This can also refer to the act of <vocabulary>composing a poem or song</vocabulary>, which makes a lot of sense given the kanji.

This is used particularly when referring to waka poetry.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9118', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%A0%E6%AD%8C', 59, 1, '2021-05-18T13:11:03.253076Z', NULL, '2025-11-11T23:03:31.014085Z'),
(9119, 'vocabulary', '詠嘆', '詠嘆', 'If you <kanji>compose</kanji> something with a <kanji>sigh</kanji>, it''s because you''re experiencing a <vocabulary>deep feeling</vocabulary>. You might express this deep feeling with an <vocabulary>exclamation</vocabulary>, as you <vocabulary>marvel</vocabulary> at the wonders of the world.

This word can be used for a range of deep feelings, good or bad, and can also be used for the expression of those feelings, so you''ll notice that the English translation varies quite a bit depending on the context.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9119', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A9%A0%E5%98%86', 59, 1, '2021-05-18T13:12:04.984837Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.89073Z'),
(9120, 'vocabulary', '梓川', '梓川', '<kanji>Japanese birch</kanji> <kanji>river</kanji> is <vocabulary>Azusa River</vocabulary> or simply <vocabulary>Azusagawa</vocabulary>. Azusagawa is both the Japanese name for the Azusa River, and a Japanese family name.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9120', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A2%93%E5%B7%9D', 59, 1, '2021-05-18T13:14:10.075316Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.913584Z'),
(9121, 'vocabulary', '某', '某', 'The kanji means <kanji>certain</kanji>, but when it''s used as a standalone word, or after a family name, it means things like <vocabulary>someone or other</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>you-know-who</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>something</vocabulary>. It''s basically a vague way to refer to someone who''s name you''ve forgotten, or you don''t want to mention for some reason.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9121', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%90', 60, 1, '2021-05-18T13:18:46.346891Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.936774Z')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9122', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%90%E5%9B%BD', 60, 1, '2021-05-18T13:19:53.308282Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:16.960444Z'),
(9123, 'vocabulary', '某所', '某所', 'No surprises here. A <kanji>certain</kanji> <kanji>place</kanji> is <vocabulary>a certain place</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9123', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%90%E6%89%80', 60, 1, '2021-05-18T13:20:51.616456Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:18:00.076038Z'),
(9124, 'vocabulary', '某氏', '某氏', 'A <kanji>certain</kanji> <kanji>family name</kanji> belongs to <vocabulary>a certain person</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9124', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9F%90%E6%B0%8F', 60, 1, '2021-05-18T13:22:09.659818Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:17.014534Z'),
(9126, 'vocabulary', '沙羅', '沙羅', 'If <kanji>sand</kanji> is <kanji>spread out</kanji> you get the given name <vocabulary>Sara</vocabulary>. You''ve written your name in sand, right? Now imagine a Sara you know spreading out sand so that it spells her name. Or imagine a pro athlete, like wrestler Doshō Sara (土性 沙羅), judoka Asahina Sara (朝比奈 沙羅), or ski jumper Takanashi Sara (髙梨 沙羅) spreading out the sand. I bet they''d find some skillful ways to do it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9126', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B2%99%E7%BE%85', 59, 1, '2021-05-18T13:31:36.35732Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:18:19.722322Z'),
(9127, 'vocabulary', '土砂', '土砂', '<kanji>Earth</kanji> and <kanji>sand</kanji> is <vocabulary>earth and sand</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>sediment</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9127', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%9C%9F%E7%A0%82', 44, 1, '2021-05-18T13:33:03.196709Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:27:03.046576Z'),
(9128, 'vocabulary', '砂利', '砂利', 'If <kanji>sand</kanji> makes a lot of <kanji>profit</kanji>, it swells up and becomes <vocabulary>gravel</vocabulary>. They''re like the fat cats of the beach.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9128', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A0%82%E5%88%A9', 47, 1, '2021-05-18T13:34:16.447714Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:27:03.046576Z'),
(9131, 'vocabulary', '日の出', '日の出', 'What do we call the <kanji>sun</kanji>''s <kanji>exit</kanji> from the horizon? That''ll be the <vocabulary>sunrise</vocabulary>. Imagine the sun exiting the horizon and climbing up into the sky.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9131', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%97%A5%E3%81%AE%E5%87%BA', 3, 1, '2021-05-18T14:03:07.140051Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.629873Z'),
(9141, 'vocabulary', '生ビール', '生ビール', 'You just learned the vocab 生, meaning fresh. Combined with the katakana word for beer makes this draft beer or unpasteurized beer.

Note that while in a lot of countries, "draft beer" refers only to beer that''s drawn from a keg, 生ビール can also refer to unpasteurized canned or bottled beer, giving it a shorter shelf-life, but also keeping it fresh.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9141', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E3%83%93%E3%83%BC%E3%83%AB', 3, 1, '2021-05-18T14:14:11.846375Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:28.125097Z'),
(9142, 'vocabulary', '元々', '元々', 'It''s the <kanji>origin</kanji> of all <kanji>origin</kanji>s, so it''s the way it was <vocabulary>originally</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>from the beginning</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>by nature</vocabulary>. You get the idea.

Note that this is frequently written in just hiragana, but you''ll encounter the kanji version too!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9142', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%83%E3%80%85', 9, 1, '2021-05-18T14:15:42.769785Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(9143, 'vocabulary', '元パートナー', '元パートナー', 'Your <kanji>origin</kanji> partner is a partner you had originally, but don''t have anymore. In other words, this is your <vocabulary>ex-partner</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>former partner</vocabulary>.

Like in English, this word is fairly formal, and it covers any kind of ex-partner, including business and romantic partners.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9143', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%85%83%E3%83%91%E3%83%BC%E3%83%88%E3%83%8A%E3%83%BC', 6, 1, '2021-05-18T14:16:31.916633Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.713112Z'),
(9145, 'vocabulary', '心強い', '心強い', 'Things that make your <kanji>heart</kanji> <kanji>strong</kanji> are <vocabulary>reassuring</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>heartening</vocabulary> to hear. You can practically feel your heart becoming stronger in your chest.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9145', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%83%E5%BC%B7%E3%81%84', 9, 1, '2021-05-18T14:18:57.977596Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:05:21.088648Z'),
(9147, 'vocabulary', '北アメリカ', '北アメリカ', 'You already encountered アメリカ in the previous level, so you should know that it means "America." Put <kanji>north</kanji> in front of this and you get <vocabulary>North America</vocabulary>, just like in English!

Note that 北アメリカ refers to the continent of North America, as opposed to the northern part of the USA. アメリカ on its own usually means the USA, but when it has "north" or "south" in front of it like this, it''s always referring to the continents.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9147', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%97%E3%82%A2%E3%83%A1%E3%83%AA%E3%82%AB', 4, 1, '2021-05-18T14:20:46.955043Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.438028Z'),
(9148, 'vocabulary', '北口', '北口', 'Remember how 入り口 means "entrance" and 出口 means "exit"? This vocab is just the 口 part with a direction added to it. This also makes it both an entrance or exit. So when you have the kanji for <kanji>north</kanji>, it makes this both a <vocabulary>north exit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>north entrance</vocabulary>. This is a great word to know if you need to know which side of a train station you need to leave on to go somewhere or meet someone!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9148', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%97%E5%8F%A3', 3, 1, '2021-05-18T14:21:47.041201Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.438028Z'),
(9150, 'vocabulary', '内ポケット', '内ポケット', 'Your <kanji>inside</kanji> ポケット is your <vocabulary>inside pocket</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9150', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%86%85%E3%83%9D%E3%82%B1%E3%83%83%E3%83%88', 7, 1, '2021-05-18T14:24:47.238875Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.437547Z'),
(9151, 'vocabulary', '石ころ', '石ころ', 'You''ve already learned 石, which means <kanji>stone</kanji>. You haven''t seen ころ before, but it''s sometimes used as a casual suffix for small, round things. Put the two together, and you''ve got a <vocabulary>small stone</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>pebble</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9151', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9F%B3%E3%81%93%E3%82%8D', 6, 1, '2021-05-18T14:25:53.024475Z', NULL, '2026-01-20T07:49:28.949976Z'),
(9152, 'vocabulary', '町外れ', '町外れ', 'You already know that 町 means "town" and that 外れ can mean "periphery" or "outskirts." This is just a combination of the two, so it means the <vocabulary>outskirts of town</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9152', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%BA%E5%A4%96%E3%82%8C', 23, 1, '2021-05-18T14:26:58.419722Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:20:46.600461Z'),
(9154, 'vocabulary', '世に出る', '世に出る', 'Here we have <kanji>world</kanji> with the particle に, following by the verb 出る (to go out). Put that together and this means <vocabulary>to go out into the world</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to be published</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be released to the public</vocabulary> in some way or another. Once it''s out there in the world, there''s even a chance for it <vocabulary>to become renowned</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9154', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%96%E3%81%AB%E5%87%BA%E3%82%8B', 40, 1, '2021-05-18T14:30:44.38058Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:33.709649Z'),
(9155, 'vocabulary', '他の人', '他の人', 'Here you have <kanji>other</kanji> and <kanji>person</kanji> joined together by the particle の, which shows that "other" is describing "person". So this means <vocabulary>someone else</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>other people</vocabulary>.

This is more general than 他人, which you already learned. 他の人 can be anyone that''s not the speaker, or not the person being talked about. 他人, on the other hand, has an added implication that the person is someone unknown or unrelated.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9155', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%96%E3%81%AE%E4%BA%BA', 6, 1, '2021-05-18T14:31:30.783843Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T04:47:22.214427Z'),
(9158, 'vocabulary', '西口', '西口', 'Remember 北口 from a couple levels ago, and how 入り口 means "entrance" and 出口 means "exit?" Combine that 口 meaning with the kanji for <kanji>west</kanji>, and you get <vocabulary>west exit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>west entrance</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9158', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%BF%E5%8F%A3', 5, 1, '2021-05-18T14:34:24.580296Z', NULL, '2025-11-04T03:00:07.618355Z'),
(9159, 'vocabulary', 'ナポレオン三世', 'ナポレオン三世', 'Adding "third generation" to someone''s name means that they are the third generation of people with that name. In this case, the person''s name is ナポレオン (Napoleon), so this means <vocabulary>Napoleon The Third</vocabulary>, also written as <vocabulary>Napoleon III</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9159', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%8A%E3%83%9D%E3%83%AC%E3%82%AA%E3%83%B3%E4%B8%89%E4%B8%96', 7, 1, '2021-05-18T14:35:42.357246Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:26.065894Z'),
(9160, 'vocabulary', '自撮り', '自撮り', 'A <kanji>self</kanji> <kanji>photograph</kanji> is exactly that: a <vocabulary>selfie</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9160', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E6%92%AE%E3%82%8A', 35, 1, '2021-05-18T14:37:14.556526Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T19:24:39.840037Z'),
(9161, 'vocabulary', '節目', '節目', 'What <kanji>season</kanji>s should you keep an <kanji>eye</kanji> on? The seasons that are <vocabulary>turning point</vocabulary>s in your life. Keep an eye on those seasons, because you don''t want to miss the <vocabulary>critical juncture</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>milestone</vocabulary>s.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9161', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AF%80%E7%9B%AE', 23, 1, '2021-05-18T14:38:24.056292Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:17.505153Z'),
(9165, 'vocabulary', '節々', '節々', 'Your <kanji>joint</kanji> <kanji>joint</kanji>s are your <vocabulary>various joints</vocabulary>, or even <vocabulary>every joint</vocabulary> in your body, or a specific body part. When we''re not talking about the joints in your body, this means something like <vocabulary>various parts</vocabulary>, referring to various parts of a whole. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9165', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AF%80%E3%80%85', 30, 1, '2021-05-18T14:43:00.39298Z', NULL, '2025-11-13T22:33:14.86011Z'),
(9166, 'vocabulary', '一千万', '一千万', 'You already know that 一千 is "<kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>thousand</kanji>," and that 万 is "<kanji>ten thousand</kanji>." Add all those zeros together (or multiply 1,000 with 10,000) and you end up with <vocabulary>ten million</vocabulary>. 

You learned that 千 is the more common and default way to say 1,000 and 一千 is sometimes used for emphasis or clarity. Well, to say ten million, you almost always say it as 一千万. I mean, who wouldn''t want to emphasize ten million! It''s ten million, you know?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9166', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E5%8D%83%E4%B8%87', 6, 1, '2021-05-18T14:45:26.401983Z', NULL, '2025-11-16T16:56:07.714784Z'),
(9167, 'vocabulary', '田んぼ', '田んぼ', 'You know that the kanji 田 means <kanji>rice paddy</kanji>, so it should be easy to work out that this word also means <vocabulary>rice paddy</vocabulary>.

田んぼ is a common way to say "rice paddy," especially in casual conversation. You can tell because if you ever came across a ju<reading>mbo</reading> (んぼ) rice paddy, you''d probably say something like, "Whoa, that''s one jumbo 田んぼ!" When a 田んぼ is that jumbo, the surprise makes your speech naturally more relaxed and informal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9167', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B0%E3%82%93%E3%81%BC', 2, 2, '2021-05-18T14:46:37.772955Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:17.581256Z'),
(9169, 'vocabulary', '三日月', '三日月', 'What''s a "third day moon"? It''s a <vocabulary>crescent moon</vocabulary>. A crescent moon is the newly visible moon that appears on the third day of the month in the lunar calendar that Japanese people used back in the day.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9169', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%88', 5, 1, '2021-05-18T14:50:00.002028Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:51:41.443876Z'),
(9170, 'vocabulary', '錦市場', '錦市場', 'You might already know 市場 means "market," like in 株式市場 (stock market). So is this a <kanji>brocade</kanji> market? Well, "brocade" actually refers to the name of a street here, not brocade fabrics. It''s a place name, so we can just call this the <vocabulary>Nishiki Market</vocabulary>!

錦市場 is a famous food market on a street called 錦小路 (Nishiki Kōji) in Kyoto, and it''s packed with delicious local specialties. Give it a quick search if you haven''t heard of it! Maybe your next travel destination?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9170', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%8C%A6%E5%B8%82%E5%A0%B4', 54, 1, '2021-05-18T14:50:54.235417Z', NULL, '2025-10-02T17:24:51.43252Z'),
(9174, 'vocabulary', '天の川', '天の川', 'You''re looking at <kanji>heaven</kanji>''s <kanji>river</kanji>, or the river of heaven. There''s only one river in heaven (i.e., the sky), and that''s the <vocabulary>Milky Way</vocabulary>! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9174', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A9%E3%81%AE%E5%B7%9D', 38, 1, '2021-05-18T14:57:31.134765Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T06:10:33.899219Z'),
(9176, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ちょっと', 'ちょっと', 'You <reading>chort</reading>le (ちょっと) <vocabulary>a little</vocabulary> at a silly joke. You can’t help chortling <vocabulary>for a moment</vocabulary>. Go ahead, chortle just a little, for just a moment, to help you remember this word.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9176', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%A1%E3%82%87%E3%81%A3%E3%81%A8', 10, 1, '2022-09-14T11:39:10.684981Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.772249Z'),
(9177, 'kana_vocabulary', 'おはよう', 'おはよう', 'From <reading>Ohio</reading> (おはよう), you shout <vocabulary>good morning</vocabulary>. Why Ohio? Because that''s where you woke up this morning. Imagine yourself waking up in Ohio, and wishing everyone "good morning" at the top of your lungs.

おはよう is a casual way to say "good morning." It can also be written in kanji, but hiragana is much more common.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9177', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E3%81%AF%E3%82%88%E3%81%86', 2, 2, '2023-03-14T13:35:00.593974Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:17.715564Z'),
(9178, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ホテル', 'ホテル', 'ホテル comes from the English word <vocabulary>hotel</vocabulary> and the meaning is exactly the same!

The only thing to watch out for is that the "l" on the end becomes ル, because that''s the closest sound in Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9178', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%9B%E3%83%86%E3%83%AB', 2, 2, '2023-03-14T14:26:53.74665Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:17.743871Z'),
(9179, 'kana_vocabulary', 'これ', 'これ', '<reading>Colle</reading>ct (これ) <vocabulary>this one</vocabulary> right here! Go ahead, collect this one.

You can use これ to talk about anything that''s close to you. Things that are close to you are easy to collect, after all!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9179', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%93%E3%82%8C', 3, 1, '2023-04-18T18:12:48.999787Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.446275Z'),
(9180, 'kana_vocabulary', 'する', 'する', 'It''s only <reading>through</reading> (する) doing things that we get better at them, so you need <vocabulary>to do</vocabulary> them as much as you can.

する basically means <vocabulary>to do</vocabulary>, but it’s incredibly versatile and goes beyond its English equivalent. Besides being used alone, it can be put together with a whole range of nouns to turn them into verbs. We call these する verbs and you''ll learn a lot of them as we go along!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9180', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B', 3, 1, '2023-04-18T18:27:49.646257Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.456691Z'),
(9181, 'kana_vocabulary', 'リンゴ', 'リンゴ', '<reading>Ringo</reading> (リンゴ) just ate your <vocabulary>apple</vocabulary>! You were about to take a bite, and he grabbed it. What is Ringo doing snatching apples? Surely he can afford his own.

Did you know that Ringo Starr once appeared in a Japanese TV commercial for <vocabulary>apple</vocabulary> juice? That might help you remember this word, too!

リンゴ is also commonly written in hiragana, and even in kanji, but you’ll probably see it in katakana the most.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9181', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%AA%E3%83%B3%E3%82%B4', 3, 1, '2023-04-24T21:41:08.633403Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.466635Z'),
(9182, 'kana_vocabulary', 'コーヒー', 'コーヒー', 'コーヒー is the katakana version of the English word <vocabulary>coffee</vocabulary>. 

Just watch out for the second syllable, which changes to ヒー in Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9182', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%B3%E3%83%BC%E3%83%92%E3%83%BC', 3, 1, '2023-04-24T21:49:47.951572Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.477438Z'),
(9183, 'kana_vocabulary', 'こんにちは', 'こんにちは', '<reading>Connie Chihua</reading>hua (こんにちは) says <vocabulary>hello</vocabulary>. Connie Chihuahua is an amazing talking chihuahua. She can only say "hello", but that alone is pretty impressive.

こんにちは is a formal way to say "hello". It’s generally used during the daytime, from late morning to early evening. Watch out for the は on the end, which is always pronounced わ.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9183', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%93%E3%82%93%E3%81%AB%E3%81%A1%E3%81%AF', 3, 1, '2023-04-24T21:53:13.498824Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.500579Z'),
(9184, 'kana_vocabulary', 'いつ', 'いつ', 'He <reading>eats</reading> (いつ) and eats and eats. <vocabulary>When</vocabulary> does this guy not eat?

いつ is a question word that is used to ask <vocabulary>when</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9184', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%84%E3%81%A4', 3, 1, '2023-04-24T21:57:04.910366Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T19:00:27.515961Z'),
(9185, 'kana_vocabulary', 'どれ', 'どれ', '<reading>Do re</reading> (どれ) mi fa sol la ti... <vocabulary>which one</vocabulary> of these notes are you going to start singing from?

The question word どれ is used to ask <vocabulary>which one</vocabulary> out of three or more choices.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9185', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%A9%E3%82%8C', 4, 1, '2023-04-24T22:01:30.93083Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:17.914103Z'),
(9186, 'kana_vocabulary', 'テレビ', 'テレビ', 'テレビ comes from the English word <vocabulary>television</vocabulary>. It’s been shortened, which is really common with loanwords in Japanese. The "vi" sound becomes ビ, too.

テレビ is sometimes written as "TV", but even then it’s usually pronounced テレビ.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9186', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%86%E3%83%AC%E3%83%93', 4, 1, '2023-04-24T22:14:02.189207Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:17.935402Z'),
(9187, 'kana_vocabulary', 'うん', 'うん', 'Wanna play <reading>Un</reading>o (うん)? <vocabulary>Yep</vocabulary>! ''Course I do. I always wanna play Uno.

うん is a casual way to say <vocabulary>yes</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>yeah</vocabulary>, so you’d generally use it with friends and family, and in other informal situations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9187', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%86%E3%82%93', 4, 1, '2023-04-24T22:34:28.303716Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:17.957585Z'),
(9188, 'kana_vocabulary', 'はい', 'はい', 'Want me to write a <reading>hai</reading>ku (はい)? <vocabulary>Yes</vocabulary>, of course!

はい is a formal word for "yes". You can use it to let someone know they’re right, or simply that you’re listening, as well as to respond if someone says your name.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9188', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%AF%E3%81%84', 4, 1, '2023-04-24T22:38:41.754869Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:17.97887Z'),
(9189, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ノート', 'ノート', 'ノート is a shortened form of <vocabulary>notebook</vocabulary>, with an extra "o" on the end since you can’t end a word with "t" in Japanese.

You can also use this to mean <vocabulary>notes</vocabulary>, in the context of taking down notes in a class.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9189', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%8E%E3%83%BC%E3%83%88', 4, 1, '2023-04-24T22:41:46.35982Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:18.001286Z'),
(9190, 'kana_vocabulary', 'さようなら', 'さようなら', 'I <reading>saw your Nara</reading> (さようなら), but I then had to say <vocabulary>goodbye</vocabulary>. It was just a short trip, but at least I saw your Nara.

さようなら is a formal way to say goodbye, so a good word to use with colleagues and teachers.

A quick tip before I say goodbye: さようなら often drops its middle う, becoming さよなら.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9190', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%95%E3%82%88%E3%81%86%E3%81%AA%E3%82%89', 4, 1, '2023-04-24T22:44:09.937711Z', NULL, '2026-03-17T01:28:58.646416Z'),
(9191, 'kana_vocabulary', 'こんばんは', 'こんばんは', '<reading>Con ban, wah!</reading> (こんばんは) cries your friend, just as you say <vocabulary>good evening</vocabulary>. There''s a report on the evening news about a con ban (yes, that''s a ban on conning people). Wah! Exciting stuff.

You can use こんばんは as a greeting from around 5pm or 6pm, in both formal and informal situations.　Watch out for that は on the end, which is pronounced わ here too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9191', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%93%E3%82%93%E3%81%B0%E3%82%93%E3%81%AF', 4, 1, '2023-04-24T22:46:02.407047Z', NULL, '2026-03-17T01:29:30.32804Z'),
(9192, 'kana_vocabulary', 'それ', 'それ', '<reading>Sorry</reading> (それ), can you grab <vocabulary>that one</vocabulary> for me? Yeah, <vocabulary>that</vocabulary>, thanks!

You can use それ for things that are close to the person you''re talking to, or fairly close to both of you.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9192', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%9D%E3%82%8C', 5, 1, '2023-04-24T22:50:07.710461Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.154231Z'),
(9193, 'kana_vocabulary', 'コンビニ', 'コンビニ', 'コンビニ comes from the English word <vocabulary>convenience store</vocabulary>, but who has time for all those syllables? That''s why they cut it down to "conveni". So much more convenient that way.

Watch out for the “v” sound, which usually becomes “b” in Japanese, and this word is no exception.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9193', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%B3%E3%83%B3%E3%83%93%E3%83%8B', 5, 1, '2023-04-24T23:01:18.823533Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.206891Z'),
(9194, 'kana_vocabulary', 'デパート', 'デパート', 'デパート comes from  <vocabulary>department store</vocabulary>. Yet another shortened loanword! There''s an extra "o" sound on the end of this one, since a word can''t end in "t" in Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9194', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%87%E3%83%91%E3%83%BC%E3%83%88', 5, 1, '2023-04-24T23:05:41.604417Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.235868Z'),
(9195, 'kana_vocabulary', 'すみません', 'すみません', '<reading>Sue me, Ma Sen!</reading> (すみません), you yell at Ma Sen, before quickly adding "<vocabulary>Excuse me</vocabulary>…<vocabulary>I''m sorry</vocabulary>, so <vocabulary> sorry</vocabulary>!" You shouldn''t have shouted at Ma Sen like that. You hope she won’t actually sue you.

You can use すみません both to apologize and to get someone’s attention. This expression is really common in Japan, and dropping it in wherever you can will help you blend right in!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9195', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%99%E3%81%BF%E3%81%BE%E3%81%9B%E3%82%93', 5, 1, '2023-04-24T23:08:10.474835Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.262839Z'),
(9196, 'kana_vocabulary', 'いいえ', 'いいえ', '<reading>Eeh! Eh?</reading> (いいえ) <vocabulary>No</vocabulary>! You’re so surprised by whatever the question was, you can only manage "Eeh! Eh? No!" in response.

いいえ is a formal word for "no", so you can use it to answer politely in the negative.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9196', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%84%E3%81%84%E3%81%88', 5, 1, '2023-04-24T23:10:33.851884Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.28578Z'),
(9197, 'kana_vocabulary', 'あなた', 'あなた', '"<reading>A gnat, ah!</reading> (あなた). I’m going to get <vocabulary>you</vocabulary>! Yes YOU," you shout as you swat at that pesky gnat buzzing around your head.

あなた is a lot less common than “you” in English, but you’ll come across it in adverts, online forums, and textbooks, where it’s used to address a general, anonymous audience. Some people also use it with their significant other, in which case it sounds intimate, like "darling". In casual conversations, though, it can come off as standoffish, so it''s better to use someone''s name or social title (like "teacher") instead.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9197', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%82%E3%81%AA%E3%81%9F', 5, 1, '2023-04-24T23:13:12.429906Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:34.303147Z'),
(9198, 'kana_vocabulary', 'この', 'この', 'Remember how これ means "this one"? Well, この simply means <vocabulary>this</vocabulary>. The difference is that これ is used on its own, whereas この goes before a noun. For example, この人 means "this person".

Notice how words like this starting with こ are close to you? We''ll be learning a few more of these, and they all follow the same pattern!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9198', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%93%E3%81%AE', 6, 1, '2023-04-24T23:16:20.622475Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.19161Z'),
(9199, 'kana_vocabulary', 'あれ', 'あれ', 'You need <reading>a le</reading>ns (あれ)? How about <vocabulary>that one over there</vocabulary>? You’re in a camera shop picking out a new lens, and that one over there looks good.

Use あれ to refer to something that''s far from both you and the person you''re talking to.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9199', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%82%E3%82%8C', 6, 1, '2023-04-24T23:19:54.829678Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.202895Z'),
(9200, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ドル', 'ドル', 'ドル is the Japanese version of the English word <vocabulary>dollar</vocabulary>. 

The dollar symbol $ is also common in Japanese, and the pronunciation is still ドル.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9200', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%89%E3%83%AB', 6, 1, '2023-04-24T23:22:42.63872Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:06.209385Z'),
(9201, 'kana_vocabulary', 'どの', 'どの', 'Remember how どれ means "which one"? どの has a very similar meaning: <vocabulary>which</vocabulary>. Whereas どれ is used on its own, どの is used before a noun to ask "which" out of three or more options. So for example, どの本 is "which book (out of that pile)?".

Notice how question words often start with a ど? We''ll be seeing more of these!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9201', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%A9%E3%81%AE', 6, 1, '2023-04-24T23:24:16.684845Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.463926Z'),
(9202, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ない', 'ない', 'The <reading>kni</reading>fe (ない) is <vocabulary>not there</vocabulary>. It''s not here and it''s not there. It’s missing. It''s nowhere to be found, so don''t bother looking for it!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9202', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84', 6, 1, '2023-04-24T23:28:19.045752Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.128212Z'),
(9203, 'kana_vocabulary', 'カバン', 'カバン', 'If there''s a <reading>ca</reading>r <reading>ban</reading> (カバン), you''ll need a nice <vocabulary>bag</vocabulary> to carry all your stuff in. You can''t just throw it all in the car, so get yourself a nice bag instead.

カバン is used for bags that you can close and that have a fairly rigid structure, like satchels or briefcases, but not more flimsy bags that don''t close, like plastic or paper shopping bags.

This word can also be written in hiragana and kanji. All versions are pretty common, but you''ll probably see it in katakana the most. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9203', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%AB%E3%83%90%E3%83%B3', 6, 1, '2023-04-24T23:34:28.686555Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T02:43:41.175274Z'),
(9204, 'kana_vocabulary', 'タクシー', 'タクシー', 'タクシー is the katakana version of the English word <vocabulary>taxi</vocabulary>. The "xi" sound becomes クシー to fit better with Japanese pronunciation.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9204', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%BF%E3%82%AF%E3%82%B7%E3%83%BC', 6, 1, '2023-04-24T23:39:13.658795Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.404869Z'),
(9205, 'kana_vocabulary', 'その', 'その', 'Remember how それ means "that one"? その has a very similar meaning: <vocabulary>that</vocabulary>. It''s used before a noun that''s either close to the person you''re talking to, or fairly close to both of you. For example, その本 is "that book", and the book in question might be in the hand of the person you''re talking to, or it might be on a shelf not far from you both.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9205', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%9D%E3%81%AE', 7, 1, '2023-04-24T23:40:53.959896Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.919421Z'),
(9206, 'kana_vocabulary', 'あの', 'あの', 'Remember how あれ means "that one over there"? Well, あの means <vocabulary>that</vocabulary>, and goes before a noun to point out something that is far away from both you and the person you''re talking to, such as あの本 (that book over there) or あの子犬 (that puppy over there).

Notice how these words starting with あ are far away from you? We''ll be learning a few more of these, and they all follow the same pattern! What''s more, you’ll notice that words ending in の tend to go before a noun.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9206', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%82%E3%81%AE', 7, 1, '2023-04-24T23:42:33.596913Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T14:32:25.930423Z'),
(9207, 'kana_vocabulary', 'でも', 'でも', 'I want to show you my <reading>demo</reading> (でも), <vocabulary>but</vocabulary> you don''t want to see it. <vocabulary>However</vocabulary>, I''m going to show it to you anyway. So there.

Something to watch out for with でも is that it always goes at the beginning of a sentence, not in the middle, like "but" usually does in English. It’s also a fairly casual word, so it’s not normally used in formal situations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9207', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%A7%E3%82%82', 8, 1, '2023-04-24T23:43:43.733819Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.223325Z'),
(9208, 'kana_vocabulary', 'まだ', 'まだ', 'You''ve been getting <reading>madder</reading> (まだ) and madder, and you’re <vocabulary>still</vocabulary> mad. You’re <vocabulary>not yet</vocabulary> as mad as you could be, though.

You can use まだ to talk about a situation that started in the past and continues into the present. Wait! Where are you going? I''m still explaining this! I''m not done yet!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9208', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%BE%E3%81%A0', 8, 1, '2023-04-24T23:45:52.233059Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.340416Z'),
(9209, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ここ', 'ここ', 'Remember how words beginning with こ are close to you? It ends with こ too, which is a clue that we’re talking about a place. So ここ means <vocabulary>here</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9209', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%93%E3%81%93', 8, 1, '2023-04-24T23:49:19.082747Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.215666Z'),
(9210, 'kana_vocabulary', 'おやつ', 'おやつ', '<reading>Oh yah! Two</reading> (おやつ) <vocabulary>snack</vocabulary>s, just for you. Imagine your two snacks. What are they? I bet they''re delicious. Oh yah!

おやつ can be anything from candy to cookies to onigiri. Basically, it''s anything fairly light that’s eaten between meals.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9210', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E3%82%84%E3%81%A4', 8, 1, '2023-04-24T23:52:43.457614Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.257925Z'),
(9211, 'kana_vocabulary', 'どこ', 'どこ', 'Remember how words beginning with ど are often question words, and words ending in こ refer to places? This one''s no exception, so どこ means <vocabulary>where</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9211', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%A9%E3%81%93', 8, 1, '2023-04-24T23:55:52.602511Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.278177Z'),
(9212, 'kana_vocabulary', 'もしもし', 'もしもし', 'Woah, this place is <reading>mossy</reading>, <reading>mossy</reading> (もしもし)! This moss needs clearing up, so you call up the person in charge. <vocabulary>Hello</vocabulary>, says the person on the phone.

もしもし is a <vocabulary>telephone hello</vocabulary>, the standard way to answer the phone in Japan!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9212', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%82%E3%81%97%E3%82%82%E3%81%97', 8, 1, '2023-04-24T23:58:15.359562Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.313121Z'),
(9213, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ほとんど', 'ほとんど', 'What can be eaten <reading>hot on dough</reading> (ほとんど)? <vocabulary>Most</vocabulary> things can. <vocabulary>Almost all</vocabulary> things, in fact. <vocabulary>Hardly</vocabulary> anything tastes bad when it''s hot and served on dough.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9213', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%BB%E3%81%A8%E3%82%93%E3%81%A9', 9, 1, '2023-04-25T00:01:23.967209Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.075078Z'),
(9214, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ガラス', 'ガラス', 'This comes from the word <vocabulary>glass</vocabulary>, and it refers to the stuff used to make Cinderella''s slippers, and windows, in which case it can also mean a <vocabulary>pane of glass</vocabulary>.

We''ll learn how to say the other meanings of "glass" later!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9214', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%AC%E3%83%A9%E3%82%B9', 9, 1, '2023-04-25T00:04:54.596915Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.094764Z'),
(9215, 'kana_vocabulary', 'そこ', 'そこ', 'Remember how words beginning with そ are either close to the person you''re talking to, or fairly close to both of you? And how words ending in こ refer to places? This one''s no exception. そこ means <vocabulary>there</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9215', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%9D%E3%81%93', 9, 1, '2023-04-25T00:07:31.489584Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.131671Z'),
(9216, 'kana_vocabulary', 'あそこ', 'あそこ', 'Remember how words beginning with あ show that whatever you''re talking about is far away from both you and the person you''re talking to? And how words ending in こ refer to places? So what do you think this means? That right, it''s <vocabulary>over there</vocabulary>.

Note that even though あそこ means "over there," it''s still used to refer to a specific, concrete place, as opposed to a word like 向こう which means "over there" in a broader, more general sense.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9216', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%82%E3%81%9D%E3%81%93', 9, 1, '2023-04-25T00:09:44.774013Z', NULL, '2025-12-10T21:38:05.898646Z'),
(9217, 'kana_vocabulary', 'キロ', 'キロ', 'キロ comes from <vocabulary>kilo</vocabulary>, and it can mean <vocabulary>kilogram</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>kilometer</vocabulary>, or any unit that starts with "kilo" in English.

You can also use abbreviations like "kg" and "km" in Japanese, and they''re often just pronounced キロ too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9217', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%AD%E3%83%AD', 9, 1, '2023-04-25T00:17:35.208605Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.146876Z'),
(9218, 'kana_vocabulary', 'タバコ', 'タバコ', 'タバコ is the Japanese version of the Portuguese word "tabaco." You probably won’t be shocked to learn that タバコ means <vocabulary>tobacco</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>cigarette</vocabulary> in English!

Watch out for the first syllable, which is タ and not ト. タバコ can also be written in hiragana, and even in kanji, but katakana is the most common by far.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9218', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%BF%E3%83%90%E3%82%B3', 10, 1, '2023-04-25T00:24:14.607432Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.783606Z'),
(9219, 'kana_vocabulary', 'サービス', 'サービス', 'This is the equivalent of the English word <vocabulary>service</vocabulary>. By extension, it''s often used to mean a <vocabulary>freebie</vocabulary>. Though it''s generally used as a standard noun, it can also be a verbal noun used with する. In this case, it means <vocabulary>giving a freebie</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9219', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%B5%E3%83%BC%E3%83%93%E3%82%B9', 10, 1, '2023-04-25T00:26:49.486712Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.792696Z'),
(9220, 'kana_vocabulary', 'しかし', 'しかし', '<reading>She cashe</reading>s (しかし) in on everything. <vocabulary>However</vocabulary>, this time you''re going to stop her. No more cashing in.

しかし is used at the beginning of a sentence, just like "however." It''s pretty formal, and you''ll see it much more in written Japanese than spoken Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9220', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%97%E3%81%8B%E3%81%97', 10, 1, '2023-04-25T00:28:12.335485Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.800739Z'),
(9221, 'kana_vocabulary', 'パン', 'パン', 'パン is the katakana version of the Portuguese word "pão" and means <vocabulary>bread</vocabulary> in English.

If you know the Portuguese, Spanish, or French word for bread, you shouldn''t have trouble remembering this word. If not, here''s a mnemonic to help you:

You use a <reading>pan</reading> (パン) to make all your <vocabulary>bread</vocabulary>. It''s your special bread pan, and all the bread you make with it is delicious.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9221', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%91%E3%83%B3', 10, 1, '2023-04-25T00:32:16.066622Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.808143Z'),
(9222, 'kana_vocabulary', 'とても', 'とても', 'Look at this <reading>totem, oh</reading> (とても)! It''s <vocabulary>very</vocabulary>, very, very, very lucky, as all totems should be. 

This word has a slightly formal feel to it, and you’ll come across it most in writing.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9222', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%A8%E3%81%A6%E3%82%82', 10, 1, '2023-04-25T00:33:52.424602Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.816409Z'),
(9223, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ビル', 'ビル', 'ビル is the Japanese version of the English word <vocabulary>building</vocabulary>. It usually refers to buildings that are taller than they are wide, most commonly apartment blocks and office buildings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9223', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%93%E3%83%AB', 10, 1, '2023-04-25T00:38:16.353957Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:55.825076Z'),
(9224, 'kana_vocabulary', 'もう', 'もう', 'You''ve <reading>mow</reading>ed your lawn (もう) <vocabulary>already</vocabulary>? Wow, you''re ahead on your mowing. Good work. You''ll have to do it again <vocabulary>soon</vocabulary>, though.

This can also mean <vocabulary>yet</vocabulary> in a question, and <vocabulary>anymore</vocabulary> with a negative.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9224', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%82%E3%81%86', 12, 1, '2023-04-25T00:40:10.937095Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:19.149525Z'),
(9226, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ワンピース', 'ワンピース', 'ワンピース comes from the English word one-piece, but it generally refers to a casual <vocabulary>dress</vocabulary> in Japanese.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9226', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%AF%E3%83%B3%E3%83%94%E3%83%BC%E3%82%B9', 13, 1, '2023-04-25T00:52:02.856721Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:19.206181Z'),
(9227, 'kana_vocabulary', 'マンション', 'マンション', 'マンション comes from the English word "mansion," but it just means an ordinary <vocabulary>apartment building</vocabulary> in Japanese. Depending on context, it can also mean an individual <vocabulary>apartment</vocabulary>. Either way, it’s definitely not a big, luxurious house in Japanese!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9227', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%9E%E3%83%B3%E3%82%B7%E3%83%A7%E3%83%B3', 13, 1, '2023-04-25T00:58:20.179039Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:19.233292Z'),
(9228, 'kana_vocabulary', 'オノマトペ', 'オノマトペ', 'オノマトペ comes from the French word “onomatopée” which, as you can probably guess, means <vocabulary>onomatopoeia</vocabulary>.

オノマトペ has a wider meaning than its English equivalent, and it also covers <vocabulary>mimetic word</vocabulary>s, which are words that evoke ideas and actions rather than sounds.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9228', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%82%AA%E3%83%8E%E3%83%9E%E3%83%88%E3%83%9A', 15, 1, '2023-04-25T01:00:35.748036Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:48.907901Z'),
(9229, 'kana_vocabulary', 'トランプ', 'トランプ', 'トランプ comes from the English word "trump", as in "a trump card", but it means <vocabulary>deck of cards</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>playing cards</vocabulary> in Japanese.

The Japanese meaning probably comes from card games played by English-speaking Westerners back in the day. Japanese people heard the word "trump" a lot, and assumed it meant a deck of cards.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9229', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%88%E3%83%A9%E3%83%B3%E3%83%97', 15, 1, '2023-04-25T01:04:29.641812Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:48.92381Z'),
(9230, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ふわふわ', 'ふわふわ', '<reading>Who are who are</reading> (ふわふわ) <vocabulary>fluffy</vocabulary>? I’m thinking puppies, but ask yourself, "who are the fluffiest creatures I know?"

This is an オノマトペ meaning fluffy, like a puppy, a cloud, or a cotton ball. It can also describe the movement of a dandelion puffball or a jellyfish, <vocabulary>floating lightly</vocabulary> in the air or the water. And if you’re <vocabulary>feeling lightheaded</vocabulary>, that’s called ふわふわ too. Basically, anything with light or fluffy vibe!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9230', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%B5%E3%82%8F%E3%81%B5%E3%82%8F', 16, 1, '2023-04-25T01:08:48.090865Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.07156Z'),
(9231, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ペラペラ', 'ペラペラ', '<reading>Parrot, parrot</reading> (ペラペラ), your speaking is so <vocabulary>fluent</vocabulary>!

ペラペラ is an オノマトペ that describes someone speaking a language fluently, or simply being a real chatterbox.

Related to this, ペラペラ also means <vocabulary>leafing through a book</vocabulary>. And in an extension of that meaning, it can describe something <vocabulary>paper thin</vocabulary> or flimsy, like a piece of paper from that book you''re leafing through.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9231', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%9A%E3%83%A9%E3%83%9A%E3%83%A9', 17, 1, '2023-04-25T01:12:32.657197Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:19.346653Z'),
(9232, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ドキドキ', 'ドキドキ', '<reading>Doc, Doc</reading> (ドキドキ), listen to my <vocabulary>racing heart</vocabulary>. I''m so <vocabulary>excited</vocabulary>, but I''m also a bit <vocabulary>nervous</vocabulary>.

ドキドキ is an オノマトペ used to describe the feeling of your heart beating fast. When does your heart race? When you get excited or nervous, so ドキドキ also expresses those feelings!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9232', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%89%E3%82%AD%E3%83%89%E3%82%AD', 18, 1, '2023-04-25T01:15:44.539362Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:19.374062Z'),
(9233, 'kana_vocabulary', 'バイキング', 'バイキング', 'バイキング comes from the English word <vocabulary>Viking</vocabulary>, but in Japanese, it often refers to a <vocabulary>buffet</vocabulary>.

バイキング came to mean "buffet" thanks to Japan’s first all-you-can-eat restaurant, "Viking", which was named after a feast scene from a movie called (you guessed it) "Viking".', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9233', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%90%E3%82%A4%E3%82%AD%E3%83%B3%E3%82%B0', 30, 1, '2023-04-25T01:18:59.378343Z', NULL, '2025-06-17T14:30:44.530905Z'),
(9234, 'kana_vocabulary', 'レントゲン', 'レントゲン', 'レントゲン comes from the German word "Röntgen" meaning <vocabulary>X-ray</vocabulary>, named after their inventor Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen.

If that’s news to you, here’s a mnemonic to help you remember: 

You’re planning to <reading>rent a Gen</reading>ji (レントゲン), but you ask Genji to get an <vocabulary>X-ray</vocabulary> first, just to make sure he’s in good health before signing the rental contract.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9234', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%AC%E3%83%B3%E3%83%88%E3%82%B2%E3%83%B3', 36, 1, '2023-04-25T01:24:53.229106Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:19.427526Z'),
(9235, 'kana_vocabulary', 'まぐれ', 'まぐれ', 'Somebody tried to <reading>mug Re</reading>mi (まぐれ), your friend, but by <vocabulary>chance</vocabulary> they managed to escape! Their escape was a <vocabulary>fluke</vocabulary>, but what a relief they didn''t get mugged.

This word can be written in kanji, but the kana version is way more common. In fact, it''d be quite a fluke if you saw it in kanji.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9235', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%BE%E3%81%90%E3%82%8C', 25, 1, '2023-04-25T01:27:12.487553Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.249276Z'),
(9236, 'kana_vocabulary', 'ホッチキス', 'ホッチキス', 'This word means <vocabulary>stapler</vocabulary>. It comes from the name of the company that first imported staplers into Japan: Hotchkiss! Here’s a mnemonic to help you remember it:

Ever given a <reading>hot kiss</reading> (ホッチキス) to a <vocabulary>stapler</vocabulary>? Imagine what that feels like, and you''ll remember this word forever. Just keep in mind that sometimes it''s written as ホチキス, but ホッチキス is more common. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9236', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%83%9B%E3%83%83%E3%83%81%E3%82%AD%E3%82%B9', 48, 1, '2023-04-25T01:29:58.217522Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:19.48251Z'),
(9237, 'vocabulary', '立場', '立場', 'Your <kanji>stand</kanji> <kanji>location</kanji> is where you stand. In other words, it''s your <vocabulary>standpoint</vocabulary>, like in a discussion or on an issue. 

While 立場 can mean a literal standing place, it''s almost always used metaphorically — to describe your stance in an argument or the complexities of your <vocabulary>position</vocabulary> within society, a situation, or a relationship.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9237', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%8B%E5%A0%B4', 9, 1, '2023-07-08T02:23:40.976199Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:25.162476Z'),
(9238, 'vocabulary', '上手い', '上手い', 'Remember how 上手 means that you''re <vocabulary>good at</vocabulary> something? Well, this word uses the exact same kanji, and means the exact same thing!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9238', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8A%E6%89%8B%E3%81%84', 5, 1, '2023-07-10T17:58:16.344128Z', NULL, '2026-01-13T07:59:15.567121Z'),
(9239, 'vocabulary', '悪化', '悪化', 'A <kanji>bad</kanji> <kanji>change</kanji> is exactly what it sounds like: <vocabulary>deterioration</vocabulary>, aka <vocabulary>getting worse</vocabulary> .', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9239', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%82%AA%E5%8C%96', 16, 1, '2023-07-10T17:59:52.833315Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:31.722868Z'),
(9240, 'vocabulary', '教わる', '教わる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

The kanji itself means <kanji>teach</kanji>, and this verb means <vocabulary>to learn something from someone</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be taught something</vocabulary>. When you use this verb, you''re not learning something by yourself, you''re being taught by someone else.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9240', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%95%99%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2023-07-10T18:00:59.426366Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:54.913981Z'),
(9241, 'vocabulary', '正月', '正月', 'You <kanji>correct</kanji> the <kanji>moon</kanji> because it''s a <vocabulary>New Year</vocabulary>. It''s the beginning of a fresh year, so you gotta have the moon in the correct position.

正月 usually refers to the first few days of a new year, and it''s one of the biggest holidays in Japan. Originally, it meant the entire first month of the new year, so you still might see it used that way in some contexts — especially historical ones.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9241', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%A3%E6%9C%88', 7, 1, '2023-07-10T18:01:35.057876Z', NULL, '2026-02-02T21:29:30.797307Z'),
(9242, 'vocabulary', '現役', '現役', 'At the <kanji>present time</kanji> you''re providing a <kanji>service</kanji>, so you''re <vocabulary>actively working</vocabulary>. 現役 means you have a certain role (such as school teacher or a high school student, etc.) and you''re still <vocabulary>active</vocabulary> in that role, as opposed to being retired or having graduated.

This word is also often used to describe students who are taking or have passed a university entrance exam while they were still high school students.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9242', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%8F%BE%E5%BD%B9', 25, 1, '2023-07-10T18:02:24.743371Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.261861Z'),
(9243, 'vocabulary', '行', '行', 'You write the word <kanji>go</kanji> on a <vocabulary>line of text</vocabulary>. See the word "go" on that <vocabulary>line</vocabulary>, and think about where you''re going.

This word can also be used for <vocabulary>row</vocabulary>s (horizontal lines in Excel and other spreadsheets).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9243', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%8C', 14, 1, '2023-07-10T18:03:19.01432Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:19.654543Z'),
(9244, 'vocabulary', '疑う', '疑う', 'The kanji means <kanji>doubt</kanji>, so this is just that as a verb. So this means <vocabulary>to doubt</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to suspect</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to be suspicious of</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9244', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%96%91%E3%81%86', 15, 1, '2023-07-10T18:04:49.4032Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:48.939235Z'),
(9245, 'vocabulary', '空く', '空く', 'You have the kanji for <kanji>empty</kanji>, so this verb means <vocabulary>to become empty</vocabulary>. 

It can also have a slightly different meaning when it''s read differently, but you''ll learn this through other words later on, so don''t worry about it for now.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9245', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%A9%BA%E3%81%8F', 13, 1, '2023-07-10T18:05:57.372285Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T06:26:42.579497Z'),
(9247, 'vocabulary', '繋がる', '繋がる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>connect</kanji>, which makes this <vocabulary>to be connected to</vocabulary>. In other situations, this can also mean <vocabulary>to be tied together</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to lead to</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9247', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B9%8B%E3%81%8C%E3%82%8B', 18, 1, '2023-07-10T18:14:18.039536Z', NULL, '2026-01-19T09:59:47.46711Z'),
(9248, 'vocabulary', '肩こり', '肩こり', 'When your shoulders get a little こり, it means you''ve got <vocabulary>shoulder stiffness</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>stiff neck</vocabulary>. 

The こり here is a nominalized version of the verb こる, which means "to become stiff," so maybe that''ll help you remember this!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9248', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%82%A9%E3%81%93%E3%82%8A', 25, 1, '2023-07-10T18:15:29.398945Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.187113Z'),
(9249, 'vocabulary', '下りる', '下りる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji means <kanji>below</kanji>, which is a clue to the direction this movement verb takes: this is <vocabulary>to go down</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to descend</vocabulary>. In some cases, this can also mean <vocabulary>to be issued</vocabulary>, mostly in situations where some sort of grant or allowance is issued by an authority.

You might notice that this is similar to 下る (くだる), and you can use either to describe going down a mountain. The difference is that 下る implies a gradual sloping motion, like floating down a stream, whereas you''d say 下りる if you’re jumping out of trees!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9249', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E3%82%8A%E3%82%8B', 15, 1, '2023-07-10T18:16:37.520647Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:48.972762Z'),
(9250, 'vocabulary', '去る', '去る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>past</kanji>, and this verb means <vocabulary>to leave</vocabulary>. It should be easy to remember because you know what people say — you leave past in the past.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9250', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8E%BB%E3%82%8B', 10, 1, '2023-07-10T18:17:07.738241Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:54.96862Z'),
(9252, 'vocabulary', '揃う', '揃う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb.

This is an intransitive verb that uses the <kanji>complete</kanji> kanji. It means <vocabulary>to be complete</vocabulary>. It can also be used when you want things <vocabulary>to be uniform</vocabulary>. It feels satisfying to have everything complete and uniform, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9252', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%83%E3%81%86', 28, 1, '2023-07-10T18:18:38.89097Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:19.824955Z'),
(9253, 'vocabulary', '繋ぐ', '繋ぐ', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>connect</kanji>, and this verb means <vocabulary>to connect something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to tie something to</vocabulary> something else. Like connect people, hold hands, connect a cable to a charger, etc.

Figuratively, when you want to connect something over time, like a conversation, then you want <vocabulary>to maintain</vocabulary> something over time as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9253', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B9%8B%E3%81%90', 20, 1, '2023-07-10T18:19:09.896493Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:19.846712Z'),
(9254, 'vocabulary', '雨戸', '雨戸', 'A <kanji>rain</kanji> <kanji>door</kanji> is really just a <vocabulary>shutter</vocabulary>. In Japan, having shutters for rain protection is very common. When there were no glass windows, the shutters were simply like exterior doors in Japan, so "rain door" kind of makes sense as "shutter"! 

This word is usually for <vocabulary>sliding storm shutter</vocabulary>s. For other types of shutters, you can just say シャッター.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9254', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%A8%E6%88%B8', 23, 1, '2023-07-10T18:19:40.305659Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:19.870038Z'),
(9255, 'vocabulary', '三十代', '三十代', 'You have the number "thirty", and <kanji>age</kanji>. So this refers to <vocabulary>one''s thirties</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9255', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%89%E5%8D%81%E4%BB%A3', 5, 1, '2023-07-10T18:20:24.316936Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T16:49:35.982109Z'),
(9256, 'vocabulary', '代金', '代金', 'When you buy something, you basically <kanji>substitute</kanji> it with <kanji>money</kanji>. That substitute money is the <vocabulary>cost</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>price</vocabulary> of the thing you''re paying for.

There are a lot of money-related synonyms in Japanese, but keep in mind this one refers to the money you pay to get some stuff in return (usually goods), not to an expense, price, or value. It''s "substitute money" after all!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9256', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%A3%E9%87%91', 5, 1, '2023-07-10T18:20:52.328476Z', NULL, '2026-03-05T17:46:40.065873Z'),
(9257, 'vocabulary', '問う', '問う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>problem</kanji> and this verb means <vocabulary>to question something</vocabulary>. That''s because when you want to get to the bottom of a problem, you question things.

問う has a formal, literary sound and tends to carry a sense of raising a meaningful or weighty question. You''ll see it in essays, news articles, and philosophical writing when someone is considering deeper issues, like questioning values, society, or something''s very existence. It can also just mean <vocabulary>to ask</vocabulary>, but will sound stuffier and more old-fashioned than the standard 聞く.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9257', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%95%8F%E3%81%86', 15, 1, '2023-07-10T18:21:35.546299Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:48.952715Z'),
(9258, 'vocabulary', '明かり', '明かり', 'What makes it <kanji>bright</kanji> around here? It''s <vocabulary>light</vocabulary>!

Note: 明かり is mostly used for light coming from a specific source, like a lamp or light bulb. You''ll learn a more general word for light in a couple levels.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9258', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%8E%E3%81%8B%E3%82%8A', 3, 1, '2023-07-10T18:22:03.936816Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(9260, 'vocabulary', '貰う', '貰う', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>receive</kanji>, making this <vocabulary>to receive something</vocabulary>, or alternatively, <vocabulary>to get something from someone</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be given something</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9260', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%B0%E3%81%86', 21, 1, '2023-07-10T18:23:22.009561Z', NULL, '2026-02-12T14:15:50.010198Z'),
(9261, 'vocabulary', '下る', '下る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji means <kanji>below</kanji>, so you can guess the direction this movement verb takes: this is <vocabulary>to descend</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to go down</vocabulary>.

As a movement verb, it usually describes a gradual downward movement. For example, you''d use this verb to describe walking down a mountain, driving down a hill, or going down a river in a boat.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9261', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%8B%E3%82%8B', 9, 1, '2023-07-10T18:24:01.707902Z', NULL, '2025-11-07T23:06:30.483389Z'),
(9262, 'vocabulary', '問い合わせる', '問い合わせる', 'Remember how 問う means "to question something" and 合わせる means "to join together"? If you''re joining your questions together, you''re probably trying to <vocabulary>to inquire</vocabulary> about something. Inquiring is much more involved than plain old asking, after all.

問い合わせる is a phrase you can use when contacting a business or government office to request information or resolve an issue. It''s a bit formal, but in an everyday business-speak kind of way, and it doesn''t have the philosophical or weighty tone that 問う does.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9262', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%95%8F%E3%81%84%E5%90%88%E3%82%8F%E3%81%9B%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2023-07-10T18:24:29.911072Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T06:27:22.66861Z'),
(9263, 'vocabulary', '揃える', '揃える', 'Remember 揃う, "to be complete"? This is the transitive version of that, and it means things like <vocabulary>to arrange something</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to gather something</vocabulary>, and <vocabulary>to align something</vocabulary>.

This one is a little tricky to understand and translate well. At its core, this verb describes the act of arranging all of the necessary things for something. But it can also describe aligning certain things in a uniform, matching manner. If this still isn''t clear, have a careful look at our context sentences and collocations!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9263', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8F%83%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 30, 1, '2023-07-10T18:25:04.027009Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:20.062935Z'),
(9264, 'vocabulary', '毛皮', '毛皮', 'This word is pretty straightforward. You have <kanji>fur</kanji> and <kanji>skin</kanji>, making a word meaning <vocabulary>fur</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>pelt</vocabulary>, like the kind that can be used to make garments.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9264', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AF%9B%E7%9A%AE', 14, 1, '2023-07-10T18:25:36.156084Z', NULL, '2026-01-30T23:30:12.871593Z'),
(9266, 'vocabulary', '炒める', '炒める', 'This is the verb version of the kanji <kanji>fry</kanji>, so it means <vocabulary>to fry</vocabulary>, more specifically <vocabulary>to stir-fry</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to sauté</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9266', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%92%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 36, 1, '2023-07-10T18:27:08.299093Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:20.108189Z'),
(9267, 'vocabulary', '雨天', '雨天', 'You see the kanji for <kanji>rain</kanji> being followed by the 天 kanji from 天気 (weather). This word means <vocabulary>rainy weather</vocabulary>.

This has a formal nuance to it, and is often used for event announcements (whether to postpone it in case of rainy weather, etc).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9267', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9B%A8%E5%A4%A9', 35, 1, '2023-07-10T18:27:37.785072Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:20.129852Z'),
(9268, 'vocabulary', '一昨年', '一昨年', 'Remember how 一昨日 means "day before last"? Similarly, <kanji>one</kanji> year <kanji>previous</kanji> to that <kanji>year</kanji> is the <vocabulary>year before last</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9268', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E6%98%A8%E5%B9%B4', 19, 1, '2023-07-10T18:28:29.170627Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:56:10.241947Z'),
(9269, 'vocabulary', '受け入れる', '受け入れる', 'Remember 受ける (to receive something) and 入れる (to put in)? This just combines those two. You receive something, and put it into your heart. You accept it. So this verb means <vocabulary>to accept something</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to take in</vocabulary>. 

You can use this verb for all kinds of acceptance, such as accepting an apology, accepting exchange students to your school, etc.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9269', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%97%E3%81%91%E5%85%A5%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 15, 1, '2023-07-10T18:29:43.70789Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:48.894006Z'),
(9270, 'vocabulary', '手軽', '手軽', 'Your <kanji>hand</kanji>s are <kanji>light</kanji> because it was a <vocabulary>simple and easy</vocabulary> task, so you didn''t get your hands full. This word describes something that''s hassle-free and doesn''t take much effort, time, or sometimes cost to do.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9270', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E8%BB%BD', 16, 1, '2023-07-10T18:30:12.236077Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.094792Z'),
(9271, 'vocabulary', '梅雨', '梅雨', 'In the <kanji>ume</kanji> season, it <kanji>rain</kanji>s a lot in Japan (and some other East Asian countries). That''s why <kanji>ume</kanji> <kanji>rain</kanji> means <vocabulary>rainy season</vocabulary>. It usually happens from the end of May to mid-July, when ume get ready to be harvested. 

Keep in mind this is a seasonal word unique to Japan and East Asia, so you usually don''t use it to refer to rainy seasons that happen in other regions of the world.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9271', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A2%85%E9%9B%A8', 39, 1, '2023-07-10T18:30:38.226064Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T18:56:52.200313Z'),
(9272, 'vocabulary', '炒る', '炒る', 'The kanji is <kanji>fry</kanji>. You already know the verb for "to fry" (炒める), so this verb is for a slightly different cooking technique — <vocabulary>to roast</vocabulary>. 

So what''s the difference between the two? Generally speaking, 炒める involves the use of hot fat or oil, while 炒る describes a way of cooking that involves less or no oil or fat, often to dry roast things. For example, you''d use 炒める for making fried noodles or fried rice, and 炒る for roasting sesame seeds or beans.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9272', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%92%E3%82%8B', 38, 1, '2023-07-10T18:31:08.666627Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:20.240116Z'),
(9273, 'vocabulary', '天然', '天然', 'Something of <kanji>heaven</kanji>''s <kanji>nature</kanji> is indeed <vocabulary>natural</vocabulary>, with no human intervention involved.

Although this word is technically a noun, it''s often used as an adjective. That''s also what distinguishes it from 自然. So you''ll often see 天然 used to say that something is "natural" or "of nature", while 自然 will often be used to talk about nature in general, like saying that nature is beautiful.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9273', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A9%E7%84%B6', 33, 1, '2023-07-10T18:31:44.983333Z', NULL, '2025-09-01T04:01:43.409978Z'),
(9274, 'vocabulary', '明らか', '明らか', 'When it''s <kanji>bright</kanji> around, things become more <vocabulary>obvious</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>clear</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>evident</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9274', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%8E%E3%82%89%E3%81%8B', 14, 1, '2023-07-10T18:32:10.05976Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(9275, 'vocabulary', '炒飯', '炒飯', 'What''s your favorite <kanji>fry</kanji> <kanji>meal</kanji>? Surely it''s <vocabulary>fried rice</vocabulary>, aka <vocabulary>chahan</vocabulary>! Who doesn''t love fried rice?

Note that this word is commonly written in katakana as チャーハン, so you may also see it written that way on a menu or product label!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9275', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%92%E9%A3%AF', 42, 1, '2023-07-10T18:32:31.878398Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T19:59:19.67524Z'),
(9276, 'vocabulary', '生じる', '生じる', 'Remember how 生まれる means "to be born"? This word is similar, but it''s used for when non-living things are "born". So as an intransitive verb, this means <vocabulary>to occur</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to arise</vocabulary>. When used transitively, it means <vocabulary>to cause something</vocabulary>. You''ll probably encounter the intransitive use much more, though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9276', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%9F%E3%81%98%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2023-07-10T18:33:01.138311Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:07:30.926651Z'),
(9278, 'vocabulary', '仕組み', '仕組み', '<kanji>Doing</kanji> something as a <kanji>group</kanji> requires a <vocabulary>system</vocabulary>. Without some sort of <vocabulary>structure</vocabulary>, everyone does their own thing, and you would never be able to get stuff done as a group, you know?

仕組み refers to the concept of a "mechanism" or "system." It includes the underlying structure, organization, or framework that governs how something works or operates, highlighting its logic, order, and other often unseen things.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9278', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%BB%95%E7%B5%84%E3%81%BF', 7, 1, '2023-07-10T18:33:50.679569Z', NULL, '2026-04-05T19:31:59.040578Z'),
(9279, 'vocabulary', '受け取る', '受け取る', 'You know that 受ける means "to receive something." Combine it with 取る (to take something), and 受け取る… still means <vocabulary>to receive something</vocabulary>.

However, while 受ける generally implies passive reception, 受け取る carries a stronger connotation of actively taking possession of something. It is used when receiving a package or gift, or even when interpreting someone''s words as having a specific meaning, such as an insult or compliment.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9279', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%97%E3%81%91%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8B', 9, 1, '2023-07-10T18:34:21.347378Z', NULL, '2025-11-08T00:22:48.601574Z'),
(9280, 'vocabulary', '素直', '素直', 'Remember how 直 can also mean "direct?" Well, if someone has an <kanji>element</kanji> of <kanji>direct</kanji>ness to them, they''re probably a really <vocabulary>honest</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>upfront</vocabulary> person.

素直 usually describes someone who''s sincere, emotionally open, willing to listen, and not stubborn. In some contexts, it can also imply someone is obedient or compliant. For example, a child who always does what they''re told without objection might be called 素直.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9280', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%A0%E7%9B%B4', 25, 1, '2023-07-10T18:34:58.168509Z', NULL, '2026-03-07T17:32:28.412962Z'),
(9281, 'vocabulary', '行列', '行列', 'You know the two words that make up this word, 行 and 列, and they both have similar meanings: a line and a row. Put them together and you have a <vocabulary>line</vocabulary> or a <vocabulary>queue</vocabulary>.

This usually means a line of people waiting for their turn, for example to enter a restaurant. It can be also used for a line that marches, like a line in a parade. In a mathematics context, it can refer to a <vocabulary>matrix</vocabulary>, which makes sense because they''re lines and rows of numbers and stuff.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9281', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%8C%E5%88%97', 16, 1, '2023-07-10T18:35:37.206334Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:23.124204Z'),
(9282, 'vocabulary', '十分', '十分', 'You have <kanji>ten</kanji> <kanji>part</kanji>s out of ten. Which means you have quite <vocabulary>enough</vocabulary>. The amount you have is <vocabulary>sufficient</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>plenty</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9282', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%81%E5%88%86', 6, 1, '2023-09-12T20:13:38.428176Z', NULL, '2025-10-08T17:35:05.492202Z'),
(9283, 'vocabulary', '立ち去る', '立ち去る', 'You 立つ and then 去る. Makes sense, as in order <vocabulary>to leave</vocabulary>, you''ll have to stand up first.

立ち去る describes the physical act of leaving a place, often used when walking away from a scene or departing a place after some kind of incident or event.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9283', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AB%8B%E3%81%A1%E5%8E%BB%E3%82%8B', 21, 1, '2023-09-12T20:16:54.563522Z', NULL, '2025-11-17T22:02:05.655823Z'),
(9284, 'vocabulary', '歯科', '歯科', '<kanji>Tooth</kanji> <kanji>science</kanji> gives you the science of <vocabulary>dentistry</vocabulary>, simple as that!

Note that when used by itself, this word solely refers to the scientific field of dentistry. You''ll often see it used in combination with other medical words, so have a look at the common word combinations if you wanna know more!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9284', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%AD%AF%E7%A7%91', 36, 1, '2023-09-12T20:17:34.911074Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:12:37.987919Z'),
(9285, 'vocabulary', '准教授', '准教授', 'Remember 教授 meaning "professor"? That makes this a <kanji>semi</kanji> professor, or in other words, an <vocabulary>associate professor</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9285', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%87%86%E6%95%99%E6%8E%88', 49, 1, '2023-09-12T20:20:15.182081Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:20.528101Z'),
(9286, 'vocabulary', '一ヶ月', '一ヶ月', 'Remember the 〜ヶ月 counter? Replace the 〜 with a <kanji>one</kanji> and this becomes <vocabulary>one month</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9286', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E3%83%B6%E6%9C%88', 9, 1, '2023-09-12T20:20:56.136134Z', NULL, '2026-02-24T00:52:10.85213Z'),
(9287, 'vocabulary', '〜ヶ月', '〜ヶ月', 'Alright, so this one''s a bit weird. See that oddly small ヶ there? Well, together with 月 (<kanji>month</kanji>), it becomes a <vocabulary>counter for months</vocabulary>. You''ll use this when you want to talk about a specific number of months, so not when you''re saying January, February, etc.

Note that you''ll run into different variants of this. While 一ヶ月 is the most common, you''ll also encounter 一か月, 一カ月, and rarely 一箇月. Luckily, they''re all read the same, just written differently. Speaking about reading, let''s get to it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9287', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E3%83%B6%E6%9C%88', 9, 1, '2023-09-12T20:21:56.747838Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:26.835598Z'),
(9288, 'vocabulary', '明後日', '明後日', 'Things will be <kanji>bright</kanji> <kanji>after</kanji> the next <kanji>day</kanji>. Or you can think of it as adding an 後 in between your 明日. And what comes after tomorrow? It''s the <vocabulary>day after tomorrow</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9288', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%8E%E5%BE%8C%E6%97%A5', 10, 1, '2023-09-12T20:22:37.834286Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(9289, 'vocabulary', '客観的', '客観的', 'Remember 主観的 meaning "subjective"? This is the counterpart to that. And since <kanji>guest</kanji>s'' and customers'' <kanji>view</kanji>s are always objectively right, this means <vocabulary>objective</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9289', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%A2%E8%A6%B3%E7%9A%84', 27, 1, '2023-09-12T20:23:16.026878Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:20.623175Z'),
(9290, 'vocabulary', '鉄板', '鉄板', 'Well, you''ve got <kanji>iron</kanji> and you''ve got a <kanji>board</kanji>. You''d probably just call it an <vocabulary>iron plate</vocabulary>, though. But this often isn''t just any iron plate. If you could guess from the reading, it''s often used in cooking as an <vocabulary>iron griddle</vocabulary>, or a <vocabulary>teppan grill</vocabulary>. Ever heard of Teppanyaki?

Figuratively, it means a <vocabulary>sure thing</vocabulary> or a practically guaranteed success. For example, you can use 鉄板 for a racehorse almost certain to win, or a joke that consistently gets big laughs.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9290', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%89%84%E6%9D%BF', 30, 1, '2023-09-12T20:23:53.171507Z', NULL, '2026-02-03T01:43:38.646573Z'),
(9291, 'vocabulary', '紅葉', '紅葉', 'You''ve got some <kanji>deep red</kanji> <kanji>leaves</kanji>. You''ll usually see those during autumn, which makes this <vocabulary>autumn leaves</vocabulary>. Just <vocabulary>red leaves</vocabulary> is fine too, though.

Note that this refers not only to the leaves themselves, but also the process of them changing color, so this can also mean <vocabulary>leaves turning red</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9291', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%85%E8%91%89', 34, 1, '2023-09-12T20:31:19.207137Z', NULL, '2026-03-29T18:10:47.677773Z'),
(9292, 'vocabulary', '親父', '親父', 'One''s <kanji>parent</kanji> <kanji>father</kanji> is simply just <vocabulary>one''s father</vocabulary>. 

Note that 親父 has a masculine and somewhat vulgar tone to it. It can refer to your own dad, someone else''s, or a <vocabulary>middle-aged man</vocabulary> you''re friendly with. When not talking about your own father, you''ll want to add a さん at the end, making it 親父さん.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9292', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A6%AA%E7%88%B6', 47, 1, '2023-09-12T20:34:07.776277Z', NULL, '2026-03-31T18:01:24.564442Z'),
(9293, 'vocabulary', '水泳', '水泳', 'When you''re doing a <kanji>water</kanji> <kanji>swim</kanji>, you''re really just… well, <vocabulary>swimming</vocabulary>.

Note that 水泳 will usually refer to swimming as a sport or organized activity. When talking about swimming as a general action, 泳ぎ and 泳ぐ are more commonly used.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9293', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B0%B4%E6%B3%B3', 12, 1, '2023-09-12T20:34:33.094335Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:20.722825Z'),
(9294, 'vocabulary', '重ねる', '重ねる', 'You''ve got lots of <kanji>heavy</kanji> things, so the only logical thing is <vocabulary>to stack</vocabulary> them all up. 

Besides heavy physical things, you can also use this word when you''re going <vocabulary>to accumulate</vocabulary> things like experience, practice, or years on your back.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9294', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8D%E3%81%AD%E3%82%8B', 16, 1, '2023-09-12T20:35:06.619301Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.470989Z'),
(9295, 'vocabulary', '通じる', '通じる', 'You have <kanji>pass through</kanji>, so this is <vocabulary>to pass through</vocabulary>.

This word has a bunch of different meanings, and translations will vary depending on context. In short, it''s mostly used figuratively to mean that something, like communication, is passing or coming through successfully. Have a look at our common word combinations and context sentences for some example usages!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9295', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%80%9A%E3%81%98%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2023-09-12T20:35:46.133885Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:20.780613Z'),
(9296, 'vocabulary', '相次ぐ', '相次ぐ', 'Things that happen <kanji>mutual</kanji>ly right <kanji>next</kanji> to each other are said <vocabulary>to happen successively</vocabulary>. In other words, <vocabulary>to happen one after another</vocabulary>.

相次ぐ is a relatively formal word often used in news reports to talk about multiple events or incidents happening one after another, usually in quick succession.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9296', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B8%E6%AC%A1%E3%81%90', 29, 1, '2023-09-12T20:36:09.685813Z', NULL, '2025-06-16T18:32:50.777684Z'),
(9297, 'vocabulary', '食う', '食う', 'You know the kanji means <kanji>eat</kanji>, and you also already know 食べる. So what''s the deal with this also meaning <vocabulary>to eat</vocabulary>? 

食う carries a more masculine and coarse tone than 食べる, so it can come off as rough or impolite in certain situations. It also has a wider set of usages, for example in saying that something is eating up time or resources, or saying that you were bitten by a bug. You''ll come across more of these, but they''re all pretty straightforward, so don''t worry!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9297', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9F%E3%81%86', 38, 1, '2023-09-12T20:36:29.936801Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:20.829118Z'),
(9298, 'vocabulary', '為替', '為替', 'For the <kanji>sake</kanji> of <kanji>exchange</kanji>! What sounds so glorious is really just <vocabulary>currency exchange</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9298', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%82%BA%E6%9B%BF', 44, 1, '2023-09-12T20:37:02.057446Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:20.853133Z'),
(9299, 'vocabulary', '蛇口', '蛇口', 'This isn''t a literal <kanji>snake</kanji>''s <kanji>mouth</kanji>, but rather a <vocabulary>faucet</vocabulary>. They do kind of have a resemblance though, just think about how the shape of a faucet kind of resembles that of a snake, with water coming out of its mouth.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9299', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9B%87%E5%8F%A3', 51, 1, '2023-09-12T20:37:22.391606Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:20.875494Z'),
(9300, 'vocabulary', '十分間', '十分間', 'You''ve got a <kanji>ten</kanji> <kanji>minute</kanji> <kanji>interval of time</kanji>. That simply makes this <vocabulary>ten minutes</vocabulary>.

Note that, as we mentioned on the page for 〜間, you can omit 間 and this will still mean <vocabulary>ten minutes</vocabulary>! You''ll especially see this in conversations and more casual contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9300', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%81%E5%88%86%E9%96%93', 9, 1, '2023-09-12T20:38:09.069798Z', NULL, '2025-10-01T13:47:24.250567Z'),
(9301, 'vocabulary', '世話', '世話', 'Older <kanji>generation</kanji>s mostly <kanji>talk</kanji> about one thing: <vocabulary>care</vocabulary>. Everybody wants to receive care once they''re older, don''t they?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9301', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%96%E8%A9%B1', 14, 1, '2023-09-12T20:38:45.035242Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:20.91856Z'),
(9302, 'vocabulary', '首相', '首相', 'The <kanji>neck</kanji> <kanji>minister</kanji>? This meaning <vocabulary>prime minister</vocabulary> makes sense if you think about the neck being the most important part of the body, kind of like the head! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9302', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A6%96%E7%9B%B8', 16, 1, '2023-09-12T20:39:08.998044Z', NULL, '2025-10-30T00:02:22.483957Z'),
(9303, 'vocabulary', '流石', '流石', 'A <kanji>stream</kanji> containing <kanji>stone</kanji>s is like… the most natural thing ever. It''s just <vocabulary>as expected</vocabulary>. 

Note that you''ll encounter this being written in kana as well, but the meaning is the same.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9303', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%81%E7%9F%B3', 19, 1, '2023-09-12T20:39:44.114956Z', NULL, '2025-09-15T03:22:17.0277Z'),
(9304, 'vocabulary', '重なる', '重なる', 'If you remember 重ねる, then you''ll recognize this as the intransitive version of that verb. Literally, this means <vocabulary>to be piled up</vocabulary>, but it''s often used to mean <vocabulary>to coincide</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to overlap</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to accumulate</vocabulary>.

As is often our recommendation for more complicated or hard-to-translate words like this one, check out our common word combinations and context sentences to see how this word is used!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9304', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%8D%E3%81%AA%E3%82%8B', 19, 1, '2023-09-12T20:40:09.149619Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:20.991032Z'),
(9305, 'vocabulary', '統一', '統一', 'To <kanji>unite</kanji> as <kanji>one</kanji> is to achieve total <vocabulary>unity</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>uniformity</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9305', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B5%B1%E4%B8%80', 27, 1, '2023-09-12T20:40:38.597662Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:21.013502Z'),
(9306, 'vocabulary', '一ヶ所', '一ヶ所', 'Remember the small ヶ acting as a counter? Together with <kanji>one</kanji> and <kanji>place</kanji>, it makes this <vocabulary>one place</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>one spot</vocabulary>.

Just like 〜ヶ月, you''ll also run into this being written as 一箇所, 一か所, or 一カ所.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9306', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E4%B8%80%E3%83%B6%E6%89%80', 10, 1, '2023-09-12T20:42:18.188909Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:54.941594Z'),
(9307, 'vocabulary', '費やす', '費やす', 'Turn <kanji>expense</kanji> into a verb and you get <vocabulary>to spend</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to expend</vocabulary>. Or, in some cases, <vocabulary>to waste</vocabulary>.

In addition to spending money, 費やす can be used to refer to spending other resources like time, effort, energy, and emotions.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9307', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%BB%E3%82%84%E3%81%99', 27, 1, '2023-09-12T20:42:45.947719Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:21.067572Z'),
(9308, 'vocabulary', '申請', '申請', 'When you <kanji>say humbly</kanji> that you''re <kanji>request</kanji>ing something, you''re putting forward an <vocabulary>application</vocabulary>.

申請 refers to the <em>process</em> of officially <vocabulary>applying</vocabulary> for approval or permission, like ビザの申請 (application for a visa), ライセンスの申請 (application for a license), or 休暇の申請 (application for leave/vacation).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9308', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%B3%E8%AB%8B', 29, 1, '2023-09-12T20:43:04.068282Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:14:57.717072Z'),
(9309, 'vocabulary', '素人', '素人', 'You''ve got a very <kanji>element</kanji>al <kanji>person</kanji>. They''re so elementary that they''re quite an <vocabulary>amateur</vocabulary>, really. Maybe a little too elemental.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9309', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%A0%E4%BA%BA', 35, 1, '2023-09-12T20:43:23.859088Z', NULL, '2026-02-26T05:38:20.342554Z'),
(9310, 'vocabulary', '衣装', '衣装', '<kanji>Clothes</kanji> and <kanji>attire</kanji> makes this <vocabulary>clothing</vocabulary> at its core, but these days, it usually refers to <vocabulary>stage outfit</vocabulary>s or <vocabulary>costume</vocabulary>s for performances and public events.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9310', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%A3%E8%A3%85', 42, 1, '2023-09-12T20:43:45.576802Z', NULL, '2025-09-18T04:21:19.196511Z'),
(9311, 'vocabulary', '唐揚げ', '唐揚げ', 'You''ve got a combination of <kanji>China</kanji> and 揚げ, meaning "fried." That makes this Chinese-style <vocabulary>Fried Chicken</vocabulary>, or as you''ll probably also know it, <vocabulary>karaage</vocabulary>.

While 唐揚げ most often refers to fried chicken, it can technically also refer to other deep fried meats, fish, or vegetables. As it''s the most common meaning, remembering this as "fried chicken" will be just fine, though.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9311', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%94%90%E6%8F%9A%E3%81%92', 55, 1, '2023-09-12T20:44:05.653486Z', NULL, '2026-01-16T18:42:49.398284Z'),
(9314, 'vocabulary', '選択肢', '選択肢', 'You''re free to <kanji>choose</kanji> and <kanji>select</kanji> any of these <kanji>limb</kanji>s, leaving you many <vocabulary>choices</vocabulary>. With all these <vocabulary>options</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>alternatives</vocabulary>, which one will you go for?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9314', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%B8%E6%8A%9E%E8%82%A2', 44, 1, '2023-09-13T17:47:00.695938Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:21.253199Z'),
(9315, 'vocabulary', '蘇生', '蘇生', 'When you <kanji>revive</kanji> <kanji>life</kanji>, what you''re doing is <vocabulary>resuscitation</vocabulary>. Or, to be less medical, <vocabulary>revival</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9315', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%98%87%E7%94%9F', 52, 1, '2023-09-13T17:47:33.435256Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:21.283117Z'),
(9316, 'vocabulary', '紫蘇', '紫蘇', 'What could a <kanji>purple</kanji> <kanji>revive</kanji> be? This actually means <vocabulary>shiso</vocabulary> (aka <vocabulary>perilla</vocabulary>).

紫蘇 is a type of herb with a fresh, slightly minty flavor. Some varieties are green, while others have purple leaves. In Japanese cooking, it''s commonly used as a garnish for sashimi. It''s also often pickled together with umeboshi (pickled plums), or deep-fried as tempura. 

Note that, as with other plant and tree species, you''ll often encounter it written in katakana as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9316', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B4%AB%E8%98%87', 53, 1, '2023-09-13T17:48:06.908778Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T06:20:40.096818Z'),
(9328, 'vocabulary', '応える', '応える', 'The kanji means <kanji>respond</kanji>, and this is also <vocabulary>to respond to</vocabulary>, but often in the sense of trying <vocabulary>to meet</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to live up to</vocabulary> some expectation, need, or other feelings.

Check out our common word combinations and context sentences for some example usages of this!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9328', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%BF%9C%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2024-02-12T19:30:43.211672Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:21.596293Z'),
(9330, 'vocabulary', '年配', '年配', 'As the <kanji>years</kanji> get <kanji>distribute</kanji>d, you slowly turn into an <vocabulary>elderly</vocabulary> <vocabulary>senior</vocabulary>, proud of your <vocabulary>old age</vocabulary>.

This word is used to politely describe an elderly person, as in 年配の方 or 年配者.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9330', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B4%E9%85%8D', 25, 1, '2024-02-12T19:36:04.025414Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T19:24:03.204967Z'),
(9331, 'vocabulary', '第一弾', '第一弾', 'The <kanji>ordinal number prefix</kanji> <kanji>one</kanji> <kanji>bullet</kanji> is actually the <vocabulary>first installment</vocabulary> of something. Picture someone firing a gun to let everyone know that, yes, this is indeed the <vocabulary>first round</vocabulary> of… whatever you''re doing.

Note that this is quite similar to 第一段. While 第一段 refers to the first phase or step within one larger, continuous process or structure, 第一弾 is used to refer to the first installment or part of a series of events or releases.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9331', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E5%BC%BE', 38, 1, '2024-02-12T19:36:59.649697Z', NULL, '2025-12-06T03:33:28.795445Z'),
(9332, 'vocabulary', '飛び込み', '飛び込み', 'You’re doing a 飛ぶ and 込む at the same time, or you <kanji>jump</kanji> <kanji>into</kanji>, meaning you''re <vocabulary>diving in</vocabulary>. Picture yourself standing somewhere up high, and then jumping to dive into a deep body of water.

Alternatively, this can also refer to doing something, or joining or going somewhere without an appointment, announcement, or prior registration, which is where the <vocabulary>dropping by</vocabulary> meaning comes in. For some use cases of this, check out our common word combinations and context sentences below!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9332', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A3%9B%E3%81%B3%E8%BE%BC%E3%81%BF', 34, 1, '2024-02-12T19:38:07.164926Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T05:34:21.665785Z'),
(9333, 'vocabulary', '海水魚', '海水魚', 'You''ve got a <kanji>sea</kanji> <kanji>water</kanji> <kanji>fish</kanji>, or in other words, a <vocabulary>saltwater fish</vocabulary>.

海水魚 refers to fish that live in the ocean, as opposed to freshwater fish. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9333', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%B5%B7%E6%B0%B4%E9%AD%9A', 7, 1, '2024-02-12T19:43:28.919042Z', NULL, '2025-10-10T17:07:46.05978Z'),
(9334, 'vocabulary', '漣斗', '漣斗', 'This isn''t a <kanji>ripples</kanji> <kanji>ladle</kanji>, but the Japanese given name <vocabulary>Rento</vocabulary>! 

The name Rento is fairly common, but this kanji version is quite unique. Sadly, there aren''t any famous 漣斗s we can mention, but you should be proud of yourself for reaching this level in WaniKani and mastering such a high-level name!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9334', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%A3%E6%96%97', 60, 1, '2025-06-04T18:10:26.77901Z', NULL, '2025-06-11T20:13:27.197563Z'),
(9336, 'vocabulary', '姪', '姪', 'This is just the kanji <kanji>niece</kanji> as a vocab, so this also means <vocabulary>niece</vocabulary>!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9336', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A7%AA', 30, 1, '2025-06-04T20:11:58.184509Z', NULL, '2025-07-02T07:23:03.05029Z'),
(9337, 'vocabulary', '頭部', '頭部', 'Your <kanji>head</kanji> <kanji>part</kanji>, or the part of your body that''s your head, is… well, your <vocabulary>head</vocabulary>!

This is a more formal way of saying "head" compared to just 頭, so you''ll encounter this more frequently in medical and other, more formal contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9337', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A0%AD%E9%83%A8', 17, 1, '2025-06-04T20:24:30.891786Z', NULL, '2026-04-06T17:38:54.907026Z'),
(9339, 'vocabulary', '手拭い', '手拭い', 'You have the kanji for <kanji>hand</kanji> and the noun version of the verb for <kanji>wipe</kanji>. With a bit of imagination, that makes this a <vocabulary>hand towel</vocabulary>, or just a <vocabulary>towel</vocabulary>. They''re a very specific kind of cloth towel, which is why you''ll also often encounter them with their Japanese name <vocabulary>tenugui</vocabulary>.

Unlike お手拭き, which you learned about a few levels ago, 手拭い are always towels — never tissues. These thin, flat cloths are long and narrow, serving a variety of purposes beyond simply cleaning your hands. Their history is fascinating, so be sure to look them up!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9339', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%89%8B%E6%8B%AD%E3%81%84', 34, 1, '2025-06-04T20:47:02.075007Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T02:39:57.168631Z'),
(9340, 'vocabulary', 'お手拭き', 'お手拭き', 'You have <kanji>hand</kanji> and the noun version of 拭く (to wipe). Put the two together and you get a <vocabulary>hand wipe</vocabulary>!

お手拭き refers to a <vocabulary>wet hand towel</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>wet napkin</vocabulary> used for cleaning your hands, often provided at restaurants in Japan before a meal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9340', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%81%8A%E6%89%8B%E6%8B%AD%E3%81%8D', 27, 1, '2025-06-04T20:56:25.941361Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T02:39:57.168631Z'),
(9341, 'vocabulary', '拭く', '拭く', 'The kanji means <kanji>wipe</kanji>, and this is the verb version of that, making this <vocabulary>to wipe</vocabulary>.

拭く is generally used for actions involving cleaning or drying surfaces by wiping, often with a cloth, towel, or similar item.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9341', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%AD%E3%81%8F', 26, 1, '2025-06-04T21:10:46.013117Z', NULL, '2026-03-01T22:16:05.656428Z'),
(9342, 'vocabulary', '拭う', '拭う', 'You have the kanji for <kanji>wipe</kanji>, and this is the verb version of that, which makes this <vocabulary>to wipe</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to mop up</vocabulary>.

You''ve already learned 拭く, which is a general word for the act of wiping, whether you''re wiping a table or wiping your forehead. 拭う, on the other hand, is more commonly used in writing to describe the act of wiping away and removing something specific like sweat, tears, filth, or even doubts. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9342', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8B%AD%E3%81%86', 33, 1, '2025-06-04T21:24:02.647386Z', NULL, '2025-10-15T02:39:57.168631Z'),
(9344, 'vocabulary', '叶える', '叶える', 'The kanji means "come true" or <kanji>grant</kanji>, and this is the transitive verb version of that, making this <vocabulary>to grant</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to fulfill</vocabulary>.

叶える refers to making something desired come true, either for oneself or someone else. It''s usually used in the context of fulfilling wishes, dreams, or goals, but it can also be used for granting someone''s request in more formal contexts.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9344', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B6%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 23, 1, '2025-06-04T21:58:09.275198Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:16:32.71995Z'),
(9345, 'vocabulary', '叶う', '叶う', 'The kanji means <kanji>come true</kanji>, and this is the intransitive verb version of that, making this <vocabulary>to come true</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be fulfilled</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9345', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8F%B6%E3%81%86', 21, 1, '2025-06-04T22:11:33.650299Z', NULL, '2026-01-31T00:16:32.71995Z'),
(9346, 'vocabulary', '針', '針', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9346', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%9D', 26, 1, '2025-06-04T22:25:07.466089Z', NULL, '2025-09-11T05:41:44.412807Z'),
(9347, 'vocabulary', '実り', '実り', 'Remember how 実る means "to bear fruit"? This is just the noun version of that word, so it means <vocabulary>harvest</vocabulary>. (A nice harvest is what you get when your crops literally bear fruit!)

Literally speaking, 実り refers to fruit and crops that are ripe and ready to be harvested. It''s a bit old-fashioned and poetic, but you might see it in phrases like 実りの秋 (the fruitful season of autumn). Like the verb version 実る, it''s more often used figuratively nowadays to refer to the <vocabulary>fruits</vocabulary> of hard work.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9347', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9F%E3%82%8A', 21, 1, '2025-06-04T22:36:42.877147Z', NULL, '2025-06-25T14:44:15.588635Z'),
(9348, 'vocabulary', '実る', '実る', 'You might remember 実 means "truth," but it can also mean <kanji>fruit</kanji>! That''s why this verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to bear fruit</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to come to fruition</vocabulary>. 

実る literally means <vocabulary>to ripen</vocabulary>, but it''s mostly used figuratively to describe when hard work leads to results, like in the phrase 努力が実る (effort pays off).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9348', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AE%9F%E3%82%8B', 20, 1, '2025-06-04T22:48:15.802106Z', NULL, '2025-06-18T20:19:45.187445Z'),
(9349, 'vocabulary', '対決', '対決', 'When it''s you <kanji>versus</kanji> someone else to <kanji>decide</kanji> it all, you know this is a <vocabulary>showdown</vocabulary>. It''s a decisive <vocabulary>confrontation</vocabulary>!

対決 is when two sides go head-to-head in a serious match or showdown. Think rivals <vocabulary>facing off</vocabulary> in a big game or the final battle in an anime. It''s tense and dramatic, but also exciting — like, "Alright, this is it. Time to settle things. Who''s gonna come out on top?"', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9349', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%AF%BE%E6%B1%BA', 8, 1, '2025-06-04T23:33:59.452044Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.206631Z'),
(9350, 'vocabulary', '北国', '北国', 'Go <kanji>north</kanji> into the <kanji>country</kanji>. Now you''re in <vocabulary>northern country</vocabulary>.

北国 is used to describe northern regions where it gets all cold and snowy in winter, like Hokkaido or Tohoku in Japan or parts of Finland or Canada outside of Japan.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9350', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%97%E5%9B%BD', 7, 1, '2025-08-19T00:37:05.543904Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.438028Z'),
(9351, 'vocabulary', '南国', '南国', 'If you go far enough <kanji>south</kanji>, every <kanji>country</kanji> is a <vocabulary>tropical country</vocabulary>. Well, relative to Japan anyway… Probably different if you live in the southern hemisphere. 

In Japanese, 南国 often refers to tropical countries or islands like Thailand, Guam, and Hawaii. Sometimes it can refer to the warmer southern regions of Japan as well, like Okinawa.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9351', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%97%E5%9B%BD', 9, 1, '2025-08-19T15:30:27.071708Z', NULL, '2025-10-09T18:19:22.645674Z'),
(9352, 'vocabulary', '南アフリカ', '南アフリカ', 'This combines <kanji>south</kanji> and アフリカ, the word for "Africa." Put them together to get <vocabulary>South Africa</vocabulary>. 

南アフリカ will almost always mean the country of South Africa, but in some contexts it''ll occasionally refer to the wider region of <vocabulary>Southern Africa</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9352', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%97%E3%82%A2%E3%83%95%E3%83%AA%E3%82%AB', 8, 1, '2025-08-19T15:49:14.241142Z', NULL, '2025-11-18T14:34:21.187645Z'),
(9353, 'vocabulary', '西ヨーロッパ', '西ヨーロッパ', 'The <kanji>west</kanji> bit of ヨーロッパ (Europe) is <vocabulary>Western Europe</vocabulary>. 

Have you ever been to 西ヨーロッパ? You know, places like France, the UK, and Belgium, with all their wine and cheese and beer… Western Europe!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9353', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%BF%E3%83%A8%E3%83%BC%E3%83%AD%E3%83%83%E3%83%91', 6, 1, '2025-08-20T01:47:10.241947Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T00:40:07.010955Z'),
(9354, 'vocabulary', '南風', '南風', 'A <kanji>south</kanji> <kanji>wind</kanji> is… a <vocabulary>south wind</vocabulary>!

南風 is a wind that blows from the south. In Japan it''s associated with the warmer temperatures it carries up from the tropics.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9354', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8D%97%E9%A2%A8', 9, 1, '2025-08-20T02:36:00.72465Z', NULL, '2025-10-16T19:51:14.173222Z'),
(9355, 'vocabulary', '北風', '北風', 'From the <kanji>north</kanji> comes a <kanji>wind</kanji>. Naturally, it''s the <vocabulary>north wind</vocabulary>.

北風 blows down from the north, and in Japan is often associated with the colder temperatures it brings.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9355', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%97%E9%A2%A8', 8, 1, '2025-08-20T02:43:57.765167Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.438028Z'),
(9356, 'vocabulary', '西日', '西日', 'The <kanji>west</kanji> <kanji>sun</kanji> is, of course, the <vocabulary>afternoon sun</vocabulary>. 

西日 describes the sun when it''s in the western part of the sky. It generally refers to the afternoon sun, but it can also mean the <vocabulary>setting sun</vocabulary> or the <vocabulary>evening sun</vocabulary>. Basically, you can use it for sun or sunlight from mid-afternoon until sunset!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9356', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A5%BF%E6%97%A5', 7, 1, '2025-08-20T02:55:14.793085Z', NULL, '2025-10-16T14:11:08.50652Z'),
(9357, 'vocabulary', '東アジア', '東アジア', 'You have the kanji for <kanji>east</kanji> combined with アジア, the word for "Asia" — that makes this <vocabulary>East Asia</vocabulary>. Can you name all the countries in 東アジア?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9357', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%B1%E3%82%A2%E3%82%B8%E3%82%A2', 7, 1, '2025-08-20T03:02:22.770306Z', NULL, '2025-12-11T15:37:48.420008Z'),
(9358, 'vocabulary', '大西洋', '大西洋', 'A <kanji>big</kanji> <kanji>west</kanji> <kanji>ocean</kanji>, you say? It''s gotta be the <vocabulary>Atlantic Ocean</vocabulary>.

If you think about it from Japan''s perspective, the Atlantic is a big, faraway ocean to the west, right? ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9358', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%A7%E8%A5%BF%E6%B4%8B', 12, 1, '2025-08-20T03:12:46.617308Z', NULL, '2025-12-04T08:47:52.037199Z'),
(9359, 'vocabulary', '北極', '北極', 'The <kanji>north</kanji>ern <kanji>extreme</kanji> of the world is the <vocabulary>North Pole</vocabulary>, aka the <vocabulary>Arctic</vocabulary>. You know, the pole with the polar bears.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9359', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%8C%97%E6%A5%B5', 28, 1, '2025-08-20T03:26:43.74407Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.438028Z'),
(9360, 'vocabulary', '東西南北', '東西南北', 'If something happens in the <kanji>east</kanji>, <kanji>west</kanji>, <kanji>south</kanji>, and <kanji>north</kanji>, then it''s happening in <vocabulary>all directions</vocabulary>. What other directions are there? That''s literally all of them.

This word usually appears in expressions like 東西南北を見る (to look in all directions). But it can also be used more literally to mean <vocabulary>east west south north</vocabulary>, aka the <vocabulary>cardinal directions</vocabulary>. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9360', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9D%B1%E8%A5%BF%E5%8D%97%E5%8C%97', 19, 1, '2025-08-20T03:35:04.430548Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T19:16:06.438028Z'),
(9363, 'vocabulary', '捻挫', '捻挫', 'A <kanji>twist</kanji> <kanji>sprain</kanji> is a <vocabulary>sprain</vocabulary>. You probably shouldn''t twist it anymore though — that sprain looks bad enough as it is.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9363', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%BB%E6%8C%AB', 26, 1, '2025-08-28T01:37:07.852633Z', NULL, '2025-10-29T11:29:07.91631Z'),
(9364, 'vocabulary', '捻出', '捻出', '<kanji>Twist</kanji> your wallet till money <kanji>exit</kanji>s. This is the best way of <vocabulary>scraping together</vocabulary> cash when you''re low on funds.

捻出 describes the act of working hard to scrape something together, whether you''re <vocabulary>scrounging up</vocabulary> money or free time in a tight situation, or <vocabulary>coming up with</vocabulary> an idea by really racking your brain. Imagine twisting an almost-empty tube of toothpaste to get that last little bit, and that''s what 捻出 feels like.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9364', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%BB%E5%87%BA', 26, 1, '2025-08-28T01:51:07.238301Z', NULL, '2025-10-29T11:23:03.810772Z'),
(9365, 'vocabulary', '〜の様に', '〜の様に', 'You learned 様 is a "formal name ender," but it can also mean <kanji>manner</kanji>. When you attach this to another noun with の and add に on the end to make it an adverb, you can describe something that''s being done <vocabulary>similarly to</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>in the same way as</vocabulary> something else. In other words, something about it is just <vocabulary>like</vocabulary> that other thing — almost <vocabulary>as if</vocabulary> they were one and the same! ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9365', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E3%80%9C%E3%81%AE%E6%A7%98%E3%81%AB', 25, 1, '2025-09-01T02:10:16.204109Z', NULL, '2025-11-05T11:32:37.89725Z'),
(9366, 'vocabulary', '貯める', '貯める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>savings</kanji>, so the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to save up</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to store</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to accumulate</vocabulary> something valuable.

貯める is used when you''re saving up something useful over time — typically money, loyalty points, or airline miles, for example, but sometimes water and other resources too.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9366', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%AF%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 31, 1, '2025-09-01T02:32:48.711563Z', NULL, '2025-11-05T11:56:12.122812Z'),
(9367, 'vocabulary', '様子', '様子', 'The <kanji>manner</kanji> of your <kanji>child</kanji> changes with the <vocabulary>state of things</vocabulary>.

様子 is a versatile word that refers to the state of things — essentially, how things appear or seem. It can describe a <vocabulary>situation</vocabulary>, someone''s <vocabulary>condition</vocabulary>, appearance, demeanor, or manner, or even the atmosphere in a room (the "vibe").', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9367', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%A7%98%E5%AD%90', 26, 1, '2025-09-01T02:47:52.132921Z', NULL, '2025-11-05T11:40:43.312257Z'),
(9368, 'vocabulary', '新緑', '新緑', 'What''s <kanji>new</kanji> and <kanji>green</kanji>? That''d be the <vocabulary>new greenery</vocabulary> you see on trees and other plants every year!

新緑 refers to <vocabulary>new foliage</vocabulary> — fresh green leaves of late spring and early summer, just after they''ve fully emerged. In Japan, it''s seen as a symbol of that time of year, so it often carries a slightly poetic nuance.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9368', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%96%B0%E7%B7%91', 16, 1, '2025-09-01T03:03:16.687751Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T08:41:48.691107Z'),
(9369, 'vocabulary', '緑茶', '緑茶', '<kanji>Green</kanji> <kanji>tea</kanji> is <vocabulary>green tea</vocabulary>.

緑茶 technically refers to all kinds of green tea (i.e., non-oxidized teas). But in everyday Japanese, the word usually means green-colored tea made from leaf infusions, typically sencha.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9369', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%B7%91%E8%8C%B6', 15, 1, '2025-09-01T03:14:01.814444Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.915312Z'),
(9370, 'vocabulary', '捻る', '捻る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>twist</kanji>, so the verb version is <vocabulary>to twist</vocabulary>.

捻る literally means "to twist," so you can use it for everything from turning the handle of a faucet to twisting your ankle in a soccer game. When combined with other words, it can also take on metaphorical meanings, such as 頭を捻る (to think hard) and 首を捻る (to puzzle over).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9370', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%BB%E3%82%8B', 28, 1, '2025-09-01T03:27:24.29006Z', NULL, '2025-11-05T11:48:25.868548Z'),
(9371, 'vocabulary', '貯まる', '貯まる', 'Remember how 貯める means "to save up"? This is a lot like that, except the focus is on the thing that''s being saved up. That''s why it means <vocabulary>to be saved up</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to add up</vocabulary>. To remember this is the intransitive version, just think about how it''s easy for money to be saved up if you put it in the <reading>mar</reading>ket (まる).

貯まる will often refer to money, like in 資金が貯まる (funds get saved up). But you''ll also see it in phrases like マイルが貯まる (frequent flier miles add up). ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9371', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B2%AF%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2025-09-01T03:47:32.348435Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T09:38:31.215835Z'),
(9372, 'vocabulary', '夜明け', '夜明け', 'We have <kanji>night</kanji> and the noun version of "to dawn" (明ける). What comes when the night ends and a new day dawns? The <vocabulary>dawn</vocabulary>, of course.

夜明け refers to dawn or <vocabulary>daybreak</vocabulary> in the literal sense, but it can also be used metaphorically to mean the <vocabulary>beginning</vocabulary> of something new, as in 時代の夜明け (the dawn of an era).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9372', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%9C%E6%98%8E%E3%81%91', 8, 1, '2025-09-10T04:47:34.144848Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(9373, 'vocabulary', '更ける', '更ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. 

The kanji itself means <kanji>renew</kanji>, but the verb meaning is a little different. This means <vocabulary>to grow late</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>to wear on</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>to deepen</vocabulary>. Think of it this way: you only renew stuff that''s <em>not</em> new, after time has worn on a bit — your streaming subscriptions, your driver''s license, your marriage vows. And with each renewal you feel the passage of time a little more deeply…

You''ll see 更ける more often in writing, usually in reference to night or autumn, as in 夜が更ける (the night grows late) and 秋が更ける (autumn deepens).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9373', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%9B%B4%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2025-09-10T16:54:35.606971Z', NULL, '2026-04-04T20:17:45.612577Z'),
(9374, 'vocabulary', '夜更け', '夜更け', 'We have <kanji>night</kanji> and the noun version of 更ける (to grow late). Stick the two together, and you get a word that means <vocabulary>late at night</vocabulary>, the <vocabulary>dead of night</vocabulary>, or the <vocabulary>wee hours</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9374', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%9C%E6%9B%B4%E3%81%91', 28, 1, '2025-09-10T17:14:40.278687Z', NULL, '2026-04-07T19:51:53.264825Z'),
(9375, 'vocabulary', '明ける', '明ける', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>bright</kanji>, but the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to dawn</vocabulary>. It gets bright each morning when a new day dawns, so the connection kinda makes sense, right?

While 明ける is commonly used with 夜 to talk about the dawn breaking, it''s also often used to talk about other time-related transitions. Because of this, it can mean <vocabulary>to end</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to begin</vocabulary> depending on the situation, as in 冬休みが明ける (winter vacation ends) or 年が明ける (the new year begins). Think of it like a new phase in your life is "dawning."', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9375', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%98%8E%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 7, 1, '2025-09-10T17:26:38.156834Z', NULL, '2026-04-10T05:38:55.064771Z'),
(9376, 'vocabulary', '夜更かし', '夜更かし', 'Remember how 夜更け meant "late at night"? Well, this is similar to that, but instead of referring to the time of night, it refers to the act of <vocabulary>staying up late</vocabulary>. 

夜更かし typically means staying up well past the time you know you should be going to bed. It''s often associated with guilty pleasures like binge-watching TV shows or gaming. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9376', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%9C%E6%9B%B4%E3%81%8B%E3%81%97', 29, 1, '2025-09-10T17:41:25.164478Z', NULL, '2026-04-04T20:17:45.612577Z'),
(9377, 'vocabulary', '夜中', '夜中', 'The <kanji>night</kanji>''s <kanji>middle</kanji> is the <vocabulary>middle of the night</vocabulary>. In other words, <vocabulary>late at night</vocabulary>.

夜中 doesn''t refer to an exact time, but it generally means the later part of the night, around when most people tend to be asleep.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9377', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%9C%E4%B8%AD', 26, 1, '2025-09-10T17:54:29.235948Z', NULL, '2025-11-12T08:49:27.569167Z'),
(9379, 'vocabulary', '賑わう', '賑わう', 'The kanji itself means <kanji>bustling</kanji> or <kanji>lively</kanji>, so the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to be bustling</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to be lively</vocabulary>.

賑わう is typically used to describe bustling places or times. You might see it in phrases like 若者で賑わう街 (streets bustling with young people) or 夏に観光客で賑わう (to be crowded with tourists in summer). Since bustling places tend to flourish economically, it can also carry a nuance of prosperity and mean <vocabulary>to thrive</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9379', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%91%E3%82%8F%E3%81%86', 42, 1, '2025-09-10T18:24:43.242251Z', NULL, '2025-11-19T09:25:36.636884Z'),
(9380, 'vocabulary', '賑やか', '賑やか', 'This shares meanings with the kanji, making it <vocabulary>bustling</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>lively</vocabulary>.

You might see 賑やか used to describe bustling and crowded city streets, a festive celebration, the sound of boisterous laughter, or even just a cheerful, animated person. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9380', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%B3%91%E3%82%84%E3%81%8B', 42, 1, '2025-09-10T19:14:24.640828Z', NULL, '2025-11-19T09:20:24.861783Z'),
(9382, 'vocabulary', '舐める', '舐める', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>lick</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to lick</vocabulary>.

舐める also has a slang meaning of <vocabulary>to underestimate</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to look down on</vocabulary>. In those cases it''ll often be written as ナメる or sometimes なめる. You might see it in manga or hear characters in movies say なめんなよ (don''t underestimate me). Think of the slang meaning like this: you''d have to really underestimate someone to think you could just lick them without consequences, right?', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9382', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%88%90%E3%82%81%E3%82%8B', 15, 1, '2025-09-10T19:39:01.971982Z', NULL, '2026-03-24T15:11:50.727496Z'),
(9383, 'vocabulary', '自ら', '自ら', 'The kanji <kanji>self</kanji> is all by itself! So, naturally, this word means <vocabulary>oneself</vocabulary>. Don''t let the ら on the end confuse you, by the way — this word is singular.

Like 自分, 自ら means "oneself," but it''s mostly used in formal or literary contexts. It can also be used as an adverb to mean <vocabulary>on one''s own initiative</vocabulary> to emphasize voluntary action, or <vocabulary>personally</vocabulary> to highlight direct involvement — for example, 自ら辞める (to quit on one''s own initiative) or 自ら謝る (to personally apologize).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9383', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%87%AA%E3%82%89', 23, 1, '2025-09-11T06:29:24.241549Z', NULL, '2025-11-05T11:24:49.741179Z'),
(9384, 'vocabulary', '直線', '直線', 'A <kanji>straight</kanji> <kanji>line</kanji> is a <vocabulary>straight line</vocabulary>. You know, the shortest distance between two points and all that.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9384', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B4%E7%B7%9A', 10, 1, '2025-09-24T03:12:33.323456Z', NULL, '2025-11-26T08:06:56.328049Z'),
(9385, 'vocabulary', '直進', '直進', 'If you make a <kanji>straight</kanji> <kanji>advance</kanji>, you are <vocabulary>going straight ahead</vocabulary>. 

直進 is a slightly formal-sounding word for <vocabulary>direct forward motion</vocabulary>. You''ll often hear it in regard to the motion of vehicles, like when your GPS tells you to go straight at a light or an announcement on the train tells you the train will be going straight at the next junction.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9385', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B4%E9%80%B2', 10, 1, '2025-09-24T03:24:59.720816Z', NULL, '2025-11-27T04:32:54.822191Z'),
(9386, 'vocabulary', '直前', '直前', 'Something happened <kanji>direct</kanji>ly <kanji>before</kanji> this. In other words, it happened <vocabulary>just before</vocabulary> this.

直前 is used to talk about something happening <vocabulary>immediately prior to</vocabulary> something else, like when your date cancels on you just before you were supposed to meet or your doctor tells you not to eat a snack right before bed.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9386', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B4%E5%89%8D', 11, 1, '2025-09-24T03:41:16.258057Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T06:07:09.542714Z'),
(9387, 'vocabulary', '直ちに', '直ちに', 'If someone tells you to get <kanji>straight</kanji> to doing something, you ought to do it <vocabulary>right away</vocabulary>. Go on, chop-chop!

直ちに means to do something <vocabulary>immediately</vocabulary>, and it has a nuance of urgency — for example, a tsunami warning asking people to immediately evacuate their homes or your doctor telling you to contact them at once if you experience a certain symptom. However, it can also mean <vocabulary>directly</vocabulary>, like 失敗は直ちに死に繋がる (failure will lead directly to death).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9387', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%9B%B4%E3%81%A1%E3%81%AB', 35, 1, '2025-09-24T03:57:20.06495Z', NULL, '2025-12-03T06:13:23.380449Z'),
(9398, 'vocabulary', '僅差', '僅差', 'When there''s only a <kanji>slight</kanji> <kanji>distinction</kanji> between you and your opponent, victory is sure to be decided by a <vocabulary>narrow margin</vocabulary>.

僅差 refers to a very <vocabulary>small difference</vocabulary> — like winning or losing by just a hair. You''ll hear it in sports, competitions, tests, or rankings when the gap is tiny: a few points, a few seconds, or almost nothing at all. It is a relatively formal word that shows up more in writing, news, and commentary than in casual chats.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9398', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%83%85%E5%B7%AE', 33, 1, '2025-12-21T21:37:33.62695Z', NULL, '2026-01-28T22:23:43.654249Z'),
(9399, 'vocabulary', '鞄', '鞄', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same, so this means <vocabulary>bag</vocabulary>.

You actually learned the kana version of this word already: カバン. In casual writing, it''s often just written in kana, but the kanji version is fairly common too. You''ll especially see it in commercial settings — store signs and ads, for example — or in literature with a more grown-up vibe.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9399', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%9E%84', 38, 1, '2025-12-28T23:29:19.521067Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T07:03:17.620725Z'),
(9400, 'vocabulary', '妃', '妃', 'The kanji and the vocabulary are exactly the same, so this means <vocabulary>royal consort</vocabulary>. Depending on who she''s married to, she could be a <vocabulary>queen</vocabulary>, a <vocabulary>princess</vocabulary>, or even an <vocabulary>empress</vocabulary>. 

妃 can overlap in meaning with 后, but 后 is reserved for the highest-ranking consort, like an empress or queen, while 妃 is a more general word which includes lower-ranking consorts and the wives of other royal family members. In modern Japanese, the kana version きさき often appears in fairytale-style stories with kings and queens. But it''s good to know both kanji, especially if you''re reading anything historical.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9400', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A6%83', 53, 1, '2025-12-28T23:44:11.979551Z', NULL, '2026-01-29T01:04:17.004265Z'),
(9401, 'vocabulary', '唾液', '唾液', '<kanji>Saliva</kanji> <kanji>fluid</kanji> sounds really gross, but it''s actually just regular ol'' <vocabulary>saliva</vocabulary> — which is still gross, but it''s better than solid saliva, right?

In contrast to 唾 (<vocabulary>spit</vocabulary>), 唾液 is the more formal word for saliva. So you''ll see this one more often in medical contexts, like 唾液検査 (saliva test).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9401', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%94%BE%E6%B6%B2', 37, 1, '2025-12-29T00:01:22.451078Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T07:00:37.773306Z'),
(9402, 'vocabulary', '唾', '唾', 'The kanji means <kanji>saliva</kanji>, and this is the basic, everyday word for that: <vocabulary>spit</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9402', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%94%BE', 37, 1, '2025-12-29T00:12:34.424175Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T07:49:00.882342Z'),
(9403, 'vocabulary', '潰す', '潰す', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>crush</kanji> or <kanji>squash</kanji> so the verb version is <vocabulary>to crush</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to squash</vocabulary>.

潰す means to crush or smash something beyond repair. While it''s often used literally, like 箱を潰す (flatten a box), it can also be used in figurative ways, like ruining a plan, killing time, blowing a chance, or even driving a business to bankruptcy. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9403', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%B0%E3%81%99', 35, 1, '2025-12-29T00:22:08.474752Z', NULL, '2026-02-17T08:00:51.143894Z'),
(9404, 'vocabulary', '潰れる', '潰れる', '潰す means "to crush," and this is its intransitive version: <vocabulary>to be crushed</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to collapse</vocabulary>. Here, the focus is on the thing that''s getting crushed or collapsing — and hopefully it''s <reading>rare</reading> (れる) for things to get crushed or collapse in your immediate vicinity!

潰れる is used to describe something giving out from pressure and getting totally squashed or flattened. From its literal meaning of an object being ruined beyond recognition, it''s also very common to hear it used figuratively for things like businesses going under, plans falling through, or even someone being too overwhelmed to hold up.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9404', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BD%B0%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 37, 1, '2025-12-29T00:30:27.072482Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T07:06:08.793941Z'),
(9405, 'vocabulary', '后', '后', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same, so this means <vocabulary>empress</vocabulary>. 

后 specifically refers to the wife of a male ruler, rather than the sort of empress who might rule a country herself. In ancient Japan, it referred to the emperor''s highest-ranking wife — the <vocabulary>empress consort</vocabulary> — but you''ll also see it used in non-Japanese contexts to refer to a king''s <vocabulary>queen</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9405', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%90%8E', 52, 1, '2025-12-29T21:12:14.201167Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T07:12:18.1416Z'),
(9406, 'vocabulary', '軟らかい', '軟らかい', 'This is a single kanji with an い on the end, meaning you know it''s probably an adjective. The kanji means <kanji>soft</kanji>, so the adjective form of that is exactly the same: <vocabulary>soft</vocabulary>.

While 柔らかい describes a pleasant, gentle softness or graceful flexibility, 軟らかい refers to softness or pliability as an objective physical property. So, for example, 柔らかいお肉 is the delicious, tender steak you have at a fancy restaurant, while 軟らかいお肉 simply describes the only kind of meat your toothless granny can eat. Likewise, a graceful gymnast or ballerina has a 柔らかい体, while your friend who can fit himself inside a suitcase — well, he has an objectively 軟らかい体.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9406', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%BB%9F%E3%82%89%E3%81%8B%E3%81%84', 53, 1, '2025-12-29T21:25:27.120948Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T06:34:12.054611Z'),
(9439, 'vocabulary', '蚕', '蚕', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9439', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%9A%95', 57, 1, '2025-12-29T21:48:10.82271Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T07:28:49.396009Z'),
(9440, 'vocabulary', '金儲け', '金儲け', 'The 金 here is from お金 (money), and you recently learned that 儲ける is "to make a profit." Well, if you''re acquiring money and making a profit, you are <vocabulary>making money</vocabulary>. You''ve got this whole <vocabulary>money-making</vocabulary> thing figured out.

金儲け is a casual word for making money with a practical and slightly self-interested vibe. It''s direct and a tad rough — the kind of term you''d hear in unvarnished talk about profit, side hustles, and get-rich-quick schemes.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9440', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%87%91%E5%84%B2%E3%81%91', 34, 1, '2025-12-29T21:56:13.383138Z', NULL, '2026-02-11T06:39:28.471912Z'),
(9441, 'vocabulary', '儲かる', '儲かる', 'Remember how 儲ける means "to make a profit"? Well, this is the intransitive version — the version that doesn''t take a direct object — so it means <vocabulary>to be profitable</vocabulary>. The かる ending is a hint, because verbs with かる as okurigana (kana added to the kanji) are usually intransitive.

Compared to 儲ける, which is about you gaining something, 儲かる is more about the situation itself bringing in money, like この店は儲かってる (this shop is doing well). It can also refer to some benefit coming in by luck, like 一日儲かった (I got an extra day).', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9441', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%84%B2%E3%81%8B%E3%82%8B', 35, 1, '2025-12-29T22:11:40.212275Z', NULL, '2026-02-18T06:55:09.010838Z'),
(9442, 'vocabulary', '儲ける', '儲ける', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>make money</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is pretty similar: <vocabulary>to make a profit</vocabulary>.

儲ける means <vocabulary>to gain something</vocabulary> in a way that feels profitable or advantageous. It''s most common in money talk, like 株で儲ける (make a profit from stocks), but it can also describe other kinds of lucky or beneficial gains. For example, you might 一日儲ける (gain an extra day) thanks to a lucky turn of events or an efficient way of doing something.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9442', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%84%B2%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B', 33, 1, '2025-12-29T22:19:35.565997Z', NULL, '2026-02-04T07:38:01.518575Z'),
(9443, 'vocabulary', '僅か', '僅か', 'When something is <kanji>slight</kanji>, that means you only have <vocabulary>a few</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>a little</vocabulary> of that thing.

僅か is a relatively formal word that indicates a small amount. You''ll hear it when someone wants to emphasize how little something is, like 僅かな差 (a little gap) or 僅か10分 (<vocabulary>just</vocabulary> ten minutes). Note this word is often written in kana, as わずか, unless the writing style is very formal.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9443', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%83%85%E3%81%8B', 35, 1, '2025-12-29T22:30:52.513607Z', NULL, '2026-02-25T06:26:42.939517Z'),
(9444, 'vocabulary', '蘇る', '蘇る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>revive</kanji>, but in this case you''re the one getting revived, so this means <vocabulary>to be resurrected</vocabulary>.

蘇る is a dramatic-sounding word for something coming back to life. It can be literal (yes, even zombies), but it''s also used for figurative resurrections, like being vividly reminded of an old memory.

Fun fact: the word originally means "to return from 黄泉(よみ)," the land of the dead in Japanese mythology. That''s why 蘇る sounds so dramatic — it makes you think of someone returning from the underworld to the mortal world!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9444', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%98%87%E3%82%8B', 54, 1, '2026-02-24T17:25:43.685259Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T06:35:56.834751Z'),
(9446, 'vocabulary', '甥', '甥', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same, so this means <vocabulary>nephew</vocabulary>.

In casual conversation, you may more often hear 甥っ子, which is a friendlier and more affectionate-sounding way to talk about your nephew.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9446', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E7%94%A5', 33, 1, '2026-02-24T17:42:29.288761Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T07:21:41.098994Z'),
(9453, 'vocabulary', '平行', '平行', 'Find a <kanji>flat</kanji> surface and <kanji>go</kanji> along it. Now you are running <vocabulary>parallel</vocabulary> to it.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9453', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%B9%B3%E8%A1%8C', 5, 1, '2026-02-24T18:53:28.156457Z', NULL, '2026-03-18T07:29:53.490843Z'),
(9454, 'vocabulary', '腫れ', '腫れ', 'You know that 腫れる means "to swell," and this is the noun version: <vocabulary>swelling</vocabulary>.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9454', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%AB%E3%82%8C', 40, 1, '2026-02-24T19:00:47.870656Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T06:35:08.846496Z'),
(9455, 'vocabulary', '腫れる', '腫れる', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>swelling</kanji> so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to swell</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to become inflamed</vocabulary>.

腫れる is used when a part of the body visibly swells up, like a swollen face, a bug bite, or puffy eyes from crying.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9455', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%85%AB%E3%82%8C%E3%82%8B', 39, 1, '2026-02-24T19:05:47.995084Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T06:28:28.839828Z'),
(9456, 'vocabulary', '夕方', '夕方', 'When do things begin to go in the <kanji>evening</kanji> <kanji>direction</kanji>? That would be in the <vocabulary>early evening</vocabulary> or simply the <vocabulary>evening</vocabulary>.

夕方 refers to the period of the day between afternoon and nighttime, when the sun is starting to go down and it''s gradually beginning to get darker outside.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9456', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A4%95%E6%96%B9', 5, 1, '2026-02-24T19:13:18.579849Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T08:56:35.547408Z'),
(9457, 'vocabulary', '餌', '餌', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same, so this means <vocabulary>feed</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>animal food</vocabulary>, or <vocabulary>bait</vocabulary>.

餌 refers to food given to animals, like pet food or bait used in fishing. It can also be used figuratively to describe tempting a person with money or benefits — "bait" used to lure them in.

This word is also commonly written in katakana as エサ.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9457', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A4%8C', 21, 1, '2026-02-24T19:22:21.476507Z', NULL, '2026-04-11T18:47:55.743592Z'),
(9458, 'vocabulary', '騙す', '騙す', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>trickery</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to trick</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to deceive</vocabulary>.

騙す means to trick or deceive someone by making them believe something that isn''t true. It carries a clearly negative nuance, since it always involves deliberate dishonesty. Note that this word is also commonly written in kana as だます.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9458', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A8%99%E3%81%99', 32, 1, '2026-02-25T16:56:30.901903Z', NULL, '2026-03-25T07:20:35.296823Z'),
(9459, 'vocabulary', '捕まえる', '捕まえる', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>catch</kanji>, so the verb vocab version means <vocabulary>to catch</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>to grab</vocabulary>.

捕まえる generally refers to the act of physically catching a person, animal, or object. By extension, you can also use it for things like grabbing a server''s attention in a busy restaurant, tracking down an employee in a massive store to ask a question, or even catching a passing taxi on the street. Basically, if it takes effort to catch, 捕まえる works!', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9459', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%8D%95%E3%81%BE%E3%81%88%E3%82%8B', 27, 1, '2026-02-25T17:05:48.400745Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T07:07:58.430795Z'),
(9460, 'vocabulary', '遡及', '遡及', '<kanji>Go upstream</kanji> through the currents of time to <kanji>reach</kanji> something in the past — this is called <vocabulary>being retroactive</vocabulary>. 

遡及 is a formal, technical word used when something is applied retroactively. You''ll mostly see it in legal or administrative contexts when discussing rules or effects that are being applied to past actions or situations.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9460', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%A1%E5%8F%8A', 44, 1, '2026-02-25T17:24:52.482682Z', NULL, '2026-04-02T17:24:43.758404Z'),
(9461, 'vocabulary', '漢', '漢', 'You learned the kanji as "Chinese," but remember how it could also mean <kanji>man</kanji>? Well, that''s what this word means, but with a twist: this is a <vocabulary>manly man</vocabulary>. You know, a <vocabulary>man''s man</vocabulary> — a <vocabulary>real man</vocabulary>.

漢 is an old-school word meaning "man," but with a particular emphasis on stereotypically masculine traits like toughness, pride, and grit. In modern Japanese, it can still be used in place of 男 when you want to highlight those qualities or praise someone''s bold, no-nonsense manliness.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9461', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E6%BC%A2', 53, 1, '2026-02-25T17:34:26.971789Z', NULL, '2026-04-01T07:44:04.494473Z'),
(9462, 'vocabulary', '餌食', '餌食', 'An animal makes you its <kanji>feed</kanji> and <kanji>eat</kanji>s you. You have become its <vocabulary>prey</vocabulary>.

餌食 refers to a living thing eaten as food by a predator. Beyond its literal meaning of animal prey, it''s also often used figuratively to mean someone who is helplessly targeted and falls <vocabulary>victim</vocabulary> to a crime, scam, or bad situation — as if they were being hunted in the wild.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9462', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A4%8C%E9%A3%9F', 23, 1, '2026-02-25T17:42:54.86635Z', NULL, '2026-04-11T18:47:55.743592Z'),
(9463, 'vocabulary', '騙る', '騙る', 'Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. Huh — another verb for <kanji>trickery</kanji>? Yup, but this one is for a specific kind of trickery: it means <vocabulary>to falsely impersonate</vocabulary>.

騙る refers to pretending to be something you''re not, especially by claiming a false identity, status, or story. It''s narrower than 騙す, because it specifically involves misrepresentation — like when a scammer tries <vocabulary>to pose as</vocabulary> someone they''re not.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9463', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%A8%99%E3%82%8B', 34, 1, '2026-02-25T17:52:10.43796Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T06:46:12.216013Z'),
(9464, 'vocabulary', '壺', '壺', 'The kanji and the word are exactly the same, so this means <vocabulary>pot</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>jar</vocabulary>.

壺 usually refers to a pot — as in a ceramic vessel — with a rounded body and narrow opening. It can also be used figuratively, like 壺にはまる (something perfectly clicks with your sense of humor and you can''t stop laughing) and ド壺にはまる (getting completely stuck in a bad situation with no easy way out). In these figurative uses, 壺 is often written in kana.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9464', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%A3%BA', 50, 1, '2026-02-25T18:07:22.701616Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T06:58:05.011031Z'),
(9465, 'vocabulary', '列車', '列車', 'You see a <kanji>row</kanji> of <kanji>car</kanji>s lined up neatly. Are they stuck in traffic? Nope. They''re train cars. Congratulations, you''ve found a <vocabulary>train</vocabulary>!

You learned 電車 means "train," but it technically just refers to electric ones. On the other hand, 列車 is the broader, all-trains-welcome term. Diesel? Steam? Fancy long-distance express? All covered. But 列車 sounds more formal, so while 電車 is an everyday word, you may come across 列車 more often in writing or in the official descriptions used by railway companies.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9465', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E5%88%97%E8%BB%8A', 16, 1, '2026-02-25T18:57:12.721412Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T07:17:38.354238Z'),
(9466, 'vocabulary', '行方', '行方', 'If you <kanji>go</kanji> off in a random <kanji>direction</kanji>, people may lose track of your <vocabulary>whereabouts</vocabulary>. Make sure you call home and let everyone know your whereabouts once you get wherever it is you''re going.

行方 means where someone or something has gone, especially when the whereabouts are unknown. You''ll often hear it in the phrase 行方不明 (missing). It can also be used more abstractly to talk about how things will turn out in the future, like 計画の行方 (the fate of the plan), with an emphasis on the outcome being up in the air.', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9466', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E8%A1%8C%E6%96%B9', 25, 1, '2026-02-25T19:09:16.287473Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T07:25:28.334832Z'),
(9467, 'vocabulary', '遡る', '遡る', 'This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an う sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means <kanji>go upstream</kanji>, so the verb vocab version is <vocabulary>to go upstream</vocabulary>.

遡る literally means to go upstream against the flow of a river, but it''s often used figuratively to mean going back in time or tracing something back to its origin — the idea being that you''re going "against the flow" of time. You''ll see it used in regard to things like history, root causes, and memories with phrases like 過去に遡る. ', 'https://api.wanikani.com/v2/subjects/9467', 'https://www.wanikani.com/vocabulary/%E9%81%A1%E3%82%8B', 46, 1, '2026-02-25T19:26:34.127797Z', NULL, '2026-04-08T07:32:57.460038Z')
