-- 4. Kanji Details


When you were <kanji>nine</kanji> your favorite foods were <reading>coo</reading>kies (く) and <reading>cu</reading>cumbers (きゅう). Yum yum!', 'N5', NULL),
(443, 'Make sure you know your radicals well and you''ll be A-Okay on this one!', 'How unbelievable is it that someone got <kanji>seven</kanji> percent on a test, especially if you try to cheat? Imagine the girl you loathe the most from school, and imagine her cheating on the test (it''s important it''s a girl, because of the "she" part in <reading>she chea</reading>ted (しち). Then, imagine your happiness when she''s super upset about getting seven percent on it. Serves her right, obviously.', 'You know this kanji means <kanji>seven</kanji>, so let''s use that to remember the reading too. <kanji>Seven</kanji> is a lucky number, usually, but this time it isn''t so good: 

<kanji>Seven</kanji> percent is all she got on her test after <reading>she chea</reading>ted (しち) on it.', 'N5', NULL),
(444, 'Know the radicals, and you shall know the kanji.', 'Imagine how angry you were about the policy. You''ll have to come up with something to be angry about yourself (pick something that would actually make you angry, like maybe you''re only allowed to wear cut off short-shorts jeans at Nintendo now), but if you pick something good, you''ll definitely remember the two readings for this. Just remember you''re working at Nintendo, and the policy has to be something about jeans. Then, imagine yourself as the only person complaining and everyone else in their dumb jeans (or lack-thereof) shaking their head at you because you''re being a fool.', 'Only one <kanji>person</kanji> at <reading>Nin</reading>tendo (にん) complained about the new <reading>jean</reading>s (じん) policy, and that person was you.', 'N5', NULL),
(445, 'Make sure you know those radicals, yo!', 'Imagine lights turning on and flashing, and an entire audience inside. You''re at a game show, and the announcer announces the neeeeew tent in his announcey voice. Feel the surprise. Feel the happiness. Feel the excitement. A beautiful new tent all for you! ', 'Turns out, after you <kanji>enter</kanji> the tent (remember how the person in the radical is entering one?), you''re given a <reading>new</reading> (にゅう) one! Why? No idea, but hey — it''s new!', 'N5', NULL),
(446, 'Count the fins out in front of you, all the way from one to eight. It''ll help if you actually do this. Now smell each fin, and think "Wow, I have to smell all eight of these?" Yes, you do.', 'You''ve asked him why you can only put eight fins at a time in the hatch, but he never wants to answer. What could be down in the hole that the hatch covers? Why is there a hatch at all? What''s with numbers and hatches being mysterious?', 'After you count and smell all <kanji>eight</kanji> of your fins, it''s time to put them away. You open a large <reading>hatch</reading> (はち) and put them in. This is where you keep your fins, always putting in eight at a time because that''s what the boss wants.', 'N5', NULL),
(447, 'If you know the radical, you should be feeling fine… No, you should feel powerful!', 'Know anyone named <reading>Ricky</reading> (りき), even a famous person like Ricky Ricardo or Ricky Gervais? Imagine that person''s face on the monster the <kanji>Power</kanji> Rangers are fighting. Now, imagine the Power Rangers <reading>lock</reading>ing (りょく) him up. He looks much less scary once he''s locked up. Phew.', 'Who has the most power of anyone? It''s the <kanji>Power</kanji> Rangers. They are battling their arch enemy <reading>Ricky</reading> (りき). They defeat him and <reading>lock</reading> (りょく) him up, so that he can''t hurt anyone ever again.', 'N4', NULL),
(448, 'If you''re familiar with Christianity, they have a cross and ten commandments. Cross = ten, you see?', 'Picture the jewels on this cross. Maybe draw it out on a piece of paper so the visual really sticks. Each cross is really a symbol for different sets of ten things, and in this case, it''s ten jewels.', 'This cross in particular has <kanji>ten</kanji> <reading>jew</reading>els (じゅう) on it. Two on each arm and two in the middle. That''s ten jewels!', 'N5', NULL),
(449, 'This one should be pretty self explanatory! Even easier, you can look at this kanji and see it has three straight lines. You know what one looks like. You know what two looks like. So... this is three!', 'Picture Santa-san sitting at his desk at the North Pole, eating cookies, and marking one, two, then finally three lines next to names. Once the third line appears, the kid doesn''t get presents anymore.', '<kanji>Three</kanji> is the number of chances you get with <reading>San</reading>ta-san (さん). He has a list, and when you get to three "naughty things" lines, that''s it. You''re on Santa-san''s three strikes and you''re out list.', 'N5', NULL),
(450, 'Focus your imagination on how body parts should be buried, not above ground like this. Sure, finding a toe is a little strange, but finding a toe above the ground? Downright silly.', 'Try to remember Joe, the local farmhand. He is a recurring character that will come up anytime the reading for a kanji is じょう.

He is a very big man, and he wears farmer''s clothes. It''s important to imagine your version of Joe as very big, because it will help us to differentiate him from Jo-Anne, who is the character associated with the similar, but shorter reading (and character) じょ. Although Joe will develop as you go through all the じょう reading kanji, for now, just imagine him as a big, kinda slow dude, who works for a local farmer. Also, he only has nine or fewer toes, apparently.', 'Of course when you find a toe <kanji>above</kanji> the ground, you want to know where the toe came from. When you examine it, you see a name written on there. This toe belongs to the local clumsy farmhand, <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう).', 'N5', NULL),
(451, 'Where''s that toe again? Below the ground. Maybe it''s your toe. Imagine digging it out and thinking, Ugh, why does it have to be under the ground like this? So irritating. Will I be able to sew it back on in time to save it?', 'There''s another useful on''yomi reading that just isn''t useful enough for you to learn now. That reading is げ, but we''re only going to worry about that much, much later when you have to learn one or two vocab words that read 下 as げ. That''s the problem with really common kanji — too many readings.', 'You keep digging in the ground for the toe <kanji>below</kanji>, but instead you hit something hard and metallic. You try to dig around it, but it''s huge. Turns out it''s a <reading>car</reading> (か). Keep digging until you unearth the car below. (Inside, you find the toe you were looking for.)', 'N5', NULL),
(452, 'If you learned your radicals — which the WaniKani system forces you to do (unless you''re cheating) — you''ll already know the meaning of the kanji.', 'You just can''t forget all the cool things about kanji learning he said from that mouth. What a great mouth こういち has. How are the lips of his mouth both so skinny and fat at the same time?', 'Do you remember seeing the construction worker こういち earlier in the 工 mnemonic? If you haven''t yet, not to worry!

For kanji with こう readings (there are a lot of them!) we will use the character こういち every time. He''s one of the people who started this website, so we''ll do our best in the mnemonics to embarrass him as much as possible. Anyways, remember that whenever you see こう, think こういち.

Everyone agrees, the <kanji>mouth</kanji> is <reading>こう</reading>いち''s best feature. You stare at it lovingly as he whispers sweet kanji readings to you.', 'N4', NULL),
(453, 'So, if you know the radical, you know the meaning of this kanji as well.', 'When you look at the big guy in your head, imagine him covered in tie dye head to toe. Think about how ridiculous it looks on him, especially since he''s such a big guy. Shirt. Sweatpants. Hat. Shoes... it goes on and on.', 'There''s this big guy (that''s how you remember the radical, remember?), and he''s wearing a huge <reading>Tie Dye</reading> (たい, だい) shirt. Because he''s so huge, the tie dye shirt is also super huge. Everything about him is pretty big, but the tie dye stands out more than anything.', 'N5', NULL),
(454, 'That means if you know your radicals well, you know the meaning of this kanji as well! How swell.', 'Whenever the reading じょ comes up, we will use the character Jo-Anne. She''s quite a short woman, which is to help us differentiate じょ from じょう (who is the giant, oafish farmhand Joe). じょ is a smaller lady, じょう is a giant man.', 'When you think of a <kanji>woman</kanji>, think of <reading>Jo</reading>-Anne (じょ), though she usually goes by just "Jo."', 'N5', NULL),
(455, 'Make sure you''re studying your radicals. If you are, then you''re studying your kanji as well!', 'Be sure to imagine this vividly. The more absurdly you can imagine it, the more likely you are to remember the reading, <reading>san</reading>.', 'Think about <kanji>mountains</kanji> talking to each other, calling each other by their names and adding the Japanese name-ender <reading>san</reading> (さん) to each of their names. "Hello, Everest-<reading>san</reading>." "Oh hi, Fuji-<reading>san</reading>."', 'N5', NULL),
(456, 'So, if you know the radical you know the kanji as well.', 'Imagine standing by the river, watching all those cars floating by. They start off really dirty, but the river washes them all clean.', 'You''re standing next to a <kanji>river</kanji> that''s actually used as a <reading>ca</reading>r <reading>wa</reading>sh (かわ). ', 'N5', NULL),
(457, 'Know your radicals and you''ll know this kanji''s meaning.', 'If こういち as a construction worker somehow doesn''t make your legs tremble, that''s okay. Just make sure you take this story to a ridiculous enough level to construct a memory in that brain of yours. Be mad at him for not following construction site regulations — where''s his shirt, shoes, and pants, and why is he using the bulldozer like that?!

Alternatively, say you really like what you see (you''re going to have to imagine a much better-looking version of こういち''s face, though). You and こういち make eye contact a little too long. He nods, puts his cigarette out, and takes off his hard hat, letting his long Fabio-esque locks flow. You beckon him over and he comes. What happens next is up to you and your own imagination. My guess is you have a very deep conversation about kanji and the best ways to learn it.

Anyway, when you see 工 in a kanji, it will often take the こう reading. Make sure to remember that, because it comes up quite a bit!', 'Every time a kanji uses the こう reading (there are a lot!), we''ll use the character こういち. He''s one of the people who started this website, so we''ll do our best in the mnemonics to embarrass him as much as possible. Like this:

You see some <kanji>construction</kanji> in front of you. There, standing shirtless (only suspenders and a hard hat) is <reading>こう</reading>いち, looking back at you.', 'N4', NULL),
(458, 'This kanji also looks a lot like the kanji 力. To avoid getting them confused, remember that this one is missing the little nubbin at the top, because 90% of the power comes from the sword to cut your enemies down (which is why the sword kanji is 90% of the power kanji).', 'Imagine trying to travel to とうきょう with your katana. Airport security is not happy with you trying to take a katana on the plane, so you get a full cavity search. I hope you don''t have any other swords hidden anywhere.', 'So now you have this sweet <kanji>katana</kanji> in your hands and you''re swinging it around like an idiot. Feel free to make whooshing noises as you swing it around. To become a master katana wielder, you decide to travel to <reading>とう</reading>きょう (Tokyo). とうきょう has the best sword and katana schools in the world, so it makes sense to travel to とうきょう.', 'N1', NULL),
(459, 'As long as you know the radical dirt, you know the kanji dirt.', 'Dogs and dirt just go together!', 'Who spends a lot of time in <kanji>dirt</kanji>? <reading>Do</reading>gs (ど) do! Dogs dig in dirt, they roll in dirt, sometimes they try to eat dirt (which isn''t so great).', 'N5', NULL),
(460, 'As long as you know the "thousand" radical, you''ll know this kanji as well!', 'Problem is, as they all tumble down the slide the change falls out of their pockets, so there''s a thousand times a thousand cents scattered all over on the floor under the slide.', 'Remember the thousand people going down the slide on a cross in the radical 千? Turns out each one of them was carrying a <kanji>thousand</kanji> <reading>cen</reading>ts (せん). Go ahead and try to calculate what a thousand times a thousand cents would come out to. It''s quite a lot, actually.', 'N5', NULL),
(461, 'Do what you''re meant to do. Study those radicals!', 'Young people love staying up the entire evening doing youth-people things, and it''s really obnoxious for us curmudgeonly folk.

Note: All the ゆう readings will use the word "youths" as a keyword for the mnemonic, so bear that in mind for any future ゆう reading you''ll encounter.', 'The <kanji>evening</kanji> is when all the <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう) come outside to play.', 'N4', NULL),
(462, 'So, if you know your radicals, you know your kanji as well! This won''t last forever (where radicals are exactly the same as the kanji) so enjoy it while the kanji are still small and simple!', 'The child (sheep) runs away and you chase after it. Every time it''s about to turn a corner it looks like a sheep again, but whenever you yell and point, it changes back into a child. You''re the only one who can see this sheep child!', 'The main reading here is し, so to remember it we''ll use this mnemonic:

There is a <kanji>child</kanji> standing in front of you. You look closely at the child but something seems wrong. You rub your eyes and when you open them again, there is a <reading>shee</reading>p (し) in front of you. A sheep?! You yelp, blink again, and it''s back to being a child. Are… are all children really just sheep? ', 'N5', NULL),
(463, 'Knowing radicals sure is nice, huh?', 'You read that right, shougun is written with a long う sound. Make sure you spell it right from now on!', 'This <kanji>small</kanji> kanji would be the perfect gift to give to the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). The Shougun loves little things, and the <kanji>little</kanji> kanji represents all of that.', 'N5', NULL),
(464, 'As long as you know the radical street, you know the kanji street.', 'Mrs. Chou is a character you''ll see a lot throughout WaniKani, anytime the reading is ちょう like this one. A lore will develop around her, so the more vividly you can imagine your Mrs. Chou right now, the easier things will get later. In general, she''s a mean, evil woman, so as long as you imagine her within those parameters, you''re good. And, when I say she is a "street-woman" in the mnemonic, I don''t mean she is homeless. She has a nice, middle-class home that''s not outside of her financial means. What I''m saying is she wanders the streets looking for evil things to do. These are HER streets. So whenever you see the street radical, know that Mrs. Chou is probably going to show up!', 'So you''re walking down this <kanji>street</kanji>, and off in the distance you see the silhouette of somebody. A lady. Big, black curly hair and red eyes. It is the street-woman, Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう).', 'N1', NULL),
(467, 'It''s not quite a <kanji>circle</kanji> though. You just need to add a <radical>drop</radical> of liquid to the edges to smooth it out to become a full circle.', 'Obviously if you "don''t know this reading" well enough, you should watch some more Maru videos. I''ll understand... just make sure you come back someday, okay?', 'Do you know about the adorable cat Maru? If not, you should <a href="http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z_AbfPXTKms" target="_blank">check it out</a>. May seem like a distraction, but if you come back knowing the reading for this kanji, then it''s totally worthwhile.

When you think about a circle, think about <reading>Maru</reading> all rolled up in a little circle, being super adorable. Don''t tell me it doesn''t melt your heart just thinking about it.

Alternatively, you could also look at this kanji and think of Maru inside of a box, with his little tail sticking out of it. Whatever works best for you, just get yourself to "Maru."', 'N2', NULL),
(468, 'Imagine yourself placing the barbs in front of the slide, thinking about how talented you are. It took years of training to perfect this talent. But it was worth it. Everyone on the playground respects you now.', 'Cyborgs have brain implants and cool bionic limbs, so they''re basically the most talented people around.', 'But then people start to say that no human could have so much <kanji>talent</kanji>, that you must be a <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい). Are you really a talented cyborg?', 'N3', NULL),
(469, 'Make sure you''re studying your radicals, otherwise you might find yourself in the middle of a kanji-learning problem.', 'Imagine the fear and shock you''d feel after finding out it was Chewbacca who shot the middle of your mouth (and head). Touch his fur. Look really close at his weapon. Give him a sniff, too. Just so that memory never fades away.', 'To remember the reading for this kanji, we use the word Chewbacca to pull up ちゅう in our memory.

If you remember back to the radical 中, a stick was stuck in the middle of the mouth of something. Let''s say it was your mouth, and when you look up to see who did it, there stands <reading>Chew</reading>bacca. He''s even doing his Chewbacca yell. And it isn''t a stick in your mouth, it''s an arrow from his bowcaster. It just so happens Chewbacca''s bowcaster looks just like this kanji too. Go figure.', 'N5', NULL),
(470, 'Know your radicals, friend-o.', 'One, two, three, four, five—GODZILLA IS HERE GO GO, RUN!', '<kanji>Five</kanji> is the number of buildings <reading>Go</reading>dzilla (ご) will knock down. Oh, look! There he goes now.', 'N5', NULL),
(471, 'Imagine this beast doing various crazy things, the crazier the better (to help you remember). Make sure all these things are associated with the devil and the devil''s number (<kanji>six</kanji>), so you can remember the meaning of this kanji.', 'Sing along with the monster, if it helps you to remember the reading of this kanji.', 'Whenever we need to remember the reading ろく we just use the word <reading>rock</reading>. 

So, think about the devil and the devil''s number. What is often associated with those things? Why, <reading>rock</reading> (ろく) and roll is. Imagine the lid-head fin-legged beast suddenly blasting out rock music in the middle of his destructive mayhem.', 'N5', NULL),
(472, 'This kanji can mean both "yen" and "round"! It''s OK if you can''t remember both meanings. But it might help to think about how round the yen coins are inside this person''s head, which can also be pretty round… ', 'Picture these round えん in your mind. Maybe look some up online too, just to be safe. See how round those yen (えん) are?', 'What does the word <kanji>yen</kanji> sound like? It sounds like <reading>えん</reading>. Why? because back in the day, Japanese used to have a "ye" sound, but now it doesn''t. Instead, it''s an "e" sound, which is why yen = えん!', 'N5', NULL),
(473, 'If you know your radicals well (and you should!) you know the meaning of this kanji well, as well!', 'Imagine yourself in line to get into heaven. You''re super worried, because you''re not sure if you''ll get in. You heard there was a one in <reading>ten</reading> chance... those aren''t good odds. Feel nervous in your imagination. Feel worried. Feel the pressure as you get closer and closer to the gate... will you make it?? Ten percent!', '<kanji>Heaven</kanji>... nowadays, people aren''t all that good. Things have gotten bad. That''s why the acceptance rate to get into heaven is only <reading>ten</reading> (てん) percent!', 'N5', NULL),
(474, 'Make sure you''re studying your radicals. It won''t be forever that radicals and kanji overlap so nicely like this!', 'You try to shake your hands off, but it''s no good. The televisions are stuck, and the volume won''t go down. You have no idea how you got these televisions on your hands. All you know is that you want them off right this instant.', 'To remember the reading て we will use the word "television." You have great hands. Go ahead and look at them. OH MY GOD. You have little <reading>te</reading>levisions (て) on your hands. They are playing the dumbest show you can think of, too, both hands at the same time.', 'N4', NULL),
(475, 'If you can''t remember the radical for writing, maybe you could try… writing it down?', 'Imagine writing a big X (or even a big 文) on all your buns, then showing them to the bun monster. Watch as he turns and goes at the sight of the writing, all disappointed. Not today, bun monster.', 'This kanji has more than one important reading you need to learn! But don''t worry, here''s a mnemonic to help you:

You always put <kanji>writing</kanji> on your <reading>bun</reading>s (ぶん) to keep them from getting eaten by the bun <reading>mon</reading>ster (もん). What kind of writing? Usually it''s just an X to show the monster these buns are off-limits. (In fact, it kind of looks like the X part in 文. Hmm…)', 'N4', NULL),
(476, 'The radical for this one is easy too, making the meaning for this kanji super easy as well!', 'This reading will be easier to remember if you know of Friedrich Nietzsche... so do your research and learn about him if you don''t. He''s a pretty historical dude. Imagine the sun totally pissed off, too. It makes a grunting noise as it builds up a sun beam to blast him off the face of the earth.

Also note that there''s another reading worth learning as well: じつ. The reading can be remembered by thinking "<reading>Gee! Two</reading> readings?" Because, seriously, two readings? Too many...', 'There are so many on''yomi for this kanji, but since this kanji is so important and common, you''ll end up learning them all eventually whether you want to or not. For now, you''re only going to have to learn one of the readings, and that one is にち. 

The <kanji>sun</kanji> is so hot. It focused its rays to take out one man... and that man is Friedrich <reading>Nietzsche</reading> (にち), famous philosopher. He philosophized one too many things about the sun, so the sun decided it was time he paid.', 'N5', NULL),
(477, 'This should be EZPZ as long as you know the radicals already!', 'Use the げつ reading when this kanji means "moon", in words like "moonlight". Use げつ for counting months, and saying things like "last month and "next month", too. Go for がつ in the names of months, like "January".

Don''t worry too much about memorizing this pattern, though. You''ll be getting lots of practice soon!', 'The <kanji>moon</kanji> really <reading>gets</reading> (げつ) to your <reading>guts</reading> (がつ). Staring at the moon for too long will give you terrible indigestion, so be careful!', 'N5', NULL),
(478, 'So, how are those radicals doing? Do you know them well?', 'Feel the shame, feel the irritation. Make yourself want to run away from those dumb trees that are mocking you.', 'You’re walking through the trees, looking at them. They look ominous. Suddenly, you trip and fall. The trees start to <reading>mock</reading> (もく) you. Even the mocking birds mock you. It’s terrible. It’s painful. You’re being mocked as you pull yourself up off the ground.', 'N5', NULL),
(479, 'So as long as you know the radicals, you know the kanji.', 'I know that was a long one, but try to imagine all the different bits. Try to feel the feelings (cold, smell, etc) too. The more you create in this fake memory (which you’re using to remember and associate water with すい (swing)) the more likely you are to end up remembering enough to make it happen. Especially when it comes to the senses part, the more vividly you imagined it, the more likely you are to remember the on’yomi.', 'Imagine a lake (i.e. a whole bunch of <kanji>water</kanji>). When you think of water, you should try to think of this beautiful lake. It’s sunny, the trees smell nice (imagine a tree smell if you can – the more senses you use the better!) and on that tree there is an awesome tire <reading>swi</reading>ng (すい). You get onto the tire swing and you are swinging back and forth. The water is right in front of you, and you are able to use the swing to throw yourself into the water. It’s a lot colder than you expected, and you come back to the surface, gasping for breath. The water gets into your mouth and you taste it. How strange, it’s very, very <reading>swee</reading>t. Imagine the sweetness of the water and how weird it is (really really sweet, this water is).', 'N5', NULL),
(480, 'If you know the radicals then this one is EZPZ.', 'Oh no... Oh no!! If you don''t have a <reading>car</reading>, imagine what it''d feel like if your car just exploded and burned down. If you do have a car, imagine your own car in this situation. It should be a terrible feeling, if you imagine it well enough. So, when you think <kanji>fire</kanji>, remember the fear you had when your car was given to the flames. Now you have to walk everywhere, sad :(', 'Whenever we need to remember か, we use the word <reading>car</reading>.

For this kanji, when we think <kanji>fire</kanji>, we ought to think of something on fire. What''s on fire? It''s obviously your very own <reading>car</reading> (か)! Oh Goddd NOOO!', 'N5', NULL),
(481, 'Soon there will be very few kanji/radical-same combos ... so enjoy it while you can.', 'P.S. Don''t actually let your dog eat noodles, at least not with marinara sauce. Disney didn''t show you how that Lady and Tramp date probably ended — unromantically, with indigestion.', 'Every <kanji>dog</kanji> likes to <reading>ea</reading>t <reading>noo</reading>dles (いぬ). Like that movie where the dogs share a noodle and end up kissing, right? So when you see 犬, just imagine how those two eat noodles.', 'N4', NULL),
(482, 'Hopefully you''re a king of radicals... because if you are, this kanji was super easy for you!', 'You need to be polite when you talk to the king and add "Oh" to the beginning, otherwise the king will be pretty angry and bad things might happen.

We''re going to use the 王 kanji for all おう mnemonics from now on. So make sure you always remember to say おう King!', 'When you see the <kanji>king</kanji> you need to say, "<reading>Oh</reading> (おう) King! Oh King, oh no, oh why are you looking at me like that? Oh King... you aren''t going to chop off my head are you? OH NO!"', 'N3', NULL),
(483, 'In your imagination, really feel like your life depends on getting out. The important part is the feeling of "needing to get out of the mountain pass." If you have this feeling, you''ll be more likely to remember that this kanji means exit, because that''s what you want to do when you see this kanji.', 'Focus on the surprise of realizing you''re being hunted while trying to find the exit to the mountain range, then focus on the next part, where you get shot. View it in your head as the hunter shooting you. Powww!', 'As you''re trying to <kanji>exit</kanji> this mountain range, you suddenly realize that someone is actually hunting you. What? Make sure to feel that sudden realization. It''s crazy. Then, you find the exit and run towards it. Sadly, the hunter is waiting there, and he <reading>shoots you</reading> (しゅつ) through the leg, knocking you down.', 'N5', NULL),
(484, 'Imagine looking at a narwhal from the front. No mouth. Now look at it from the right. Mouth!', 'Imagine a bunch of youths walking in a group and looking at everything from the right. You remember when you were a youth and you used to look at everything from one side too.

Note: All the ゆう readings will use the word "youths" for the reading mnemonic. You can think of these "youths" as a group of younger people who cause a lot of the problems in this world.', 'How did we know to look from the <kanji>right</kanji>, though? Because the <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう) figured it out. Youths think in ways that older people usually don''t. That''s why they can see the swirls on cereal, hear the music behind noise, and of course, look at things from the right side.', 'N5', NULL),
(485, 'If you can see this kanji as a kind of square, you should be able to remember it means "four" as well, since squares always have four sides.', 'What''s better than a four-sided sheep?', 'Now that you have <kanji>four</kanji> sides, you look like a four-sided animal: the <reading>shee</reading>p (し). Look at yourself in the mirror and see an adorable four-sided sheep looking back at you. Imagine yourself with perfectly square wool. (You can only see four sides because mirrors aren''t 3D. So you''re a flat, cartoonish sheep.)', 'N5', NULL),
(486, 'Picture a bunch of narwhals constructing an underwater ice village. They''re all working away with their left hands.', 'If you''re right-handed, imagine trying to use these left-handed saws. It flips and flops all over until you drop it. But if you''re left-handed it feels just right!', 'In their <kanji>left</kanji> hands, the narwhals all hold <reading>saw</reading>s (さ). That''s because the ice for their homes is already there, so they just need to saw away at them to make the shapes they want. Narwhal construction is actually very simple.', 'N5', NULL),
(487, 'Make sure you know those radicals, yo! And good luck with all the meanings (sorry sorry sorry).', 'Just think about books and how you use them to hone your skills. Or read a book about honing things. Like swords.', '<kanji>Book</kanji>s are one of the best ways to <reading>hone</reading> (ほん) your intellect, you know?', 'N5', NULL),
(488, 'Imagine walking, staring at the ground until you come across a line that you''re supposed to stop at. If you stop, the big "correct" sign appears, chiming a happy tune.', 'Perhaps a slightly unorthodox weapon for a ruler of medieval Japan, but you won''t complain about a saber from the Shogun himself! ', 'You did the <kanji>correct</kanji> thing, so here''s your reward: a <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい) from the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) himself! ', 'N4', NULL),
(489, 'This kanji looks just like a king bouncing a <kanji>ball</kanji>, no? If not, imagine the kanji itself bouncing the drop (the <kanji>ball</kanji>) over and over again. Kanji are surprisingly playful like that.', 'Try to remember Tamara the ball girl. She''ll come up anytime the reading for a kanji is たま, and usually she''ll be involved with some kind of ball or round object. That''s her job, after all.', 'When he''s done playing with it, the king gives his <kanji>ball</kanji> to <reading>Tama</reading>ra (たま), the resident ball girl, for safekeeping.', 'N2', NULL),
(490, 'Just know your radicals, and you will know your kanji as well.', 'Smell the tacos. Touch the tacos. Taste the tacos. This is definitely a very strange rice field.', 'To remember the reading た, we use the word "taco."

You''re in the rice field... but when you lean down to examine the rice more closely, you see that it''s not rice that''s growing, it''s a bunch of little <reading>ta</reading>cos (た), blossoming out of the ground.', 'N4', NULL),
(491, 'Study your radicals hard! Most important thing!', 'Imagine two teams (black and <kanji>white</kanji>), wearing their team colors, working really hard on hacking the iPhone. It is a huge contest, and people are watching with a lot of tension, to see who will hack the <kanji>white</kanji> iPhone first. Feel that tension, and see the colors separating them so vividly. The colors are important, and the excitement of the winner holding up the <reading>hack</reading>ed iPhone is too.', 'The <kanji>white</kanji> team was the first to be able to <reading>hack</reading> (はく) the <kanji>white</kanji> iPhone.', 'N5', NULL),
(492, 'Well... you''ll know them both as long as you studied your radicals, young grasshopper. If you didn''t, then you''re just a young grasshopper who needs to study their radicals.', 'Of course, feel the pain of your eye being gone... and struggle to get it back. You eventually give up because the sheep is too fast, but then you come up with an idea. What if I call out back to the sheep in its own language? Go ahead and cover one eye and start making "MEHHH" noises to see if you can get the sheep to come back to you and give you your eye. It''s up to you whether it works or not, but be sure to feel frustrated by its reaction, or happy by it. Either one works.', 'Your <kanji>eyes</kanji> are important to you, right? Sadly, one of your eyes is missing right now, because a sheep grabbed it and pulled it out, and is now running away from you, yelling his sheep call.... "<reading>Mehhhhhh</reading> (め)!"', 'N4', NULL),
(494, 'If you''re keeping up with your radical studies, you''re also keeping up with your kanji studies!', 'Imagine the doormen being extra straight… too straight in their standing. On their uniform is “The Ritz” written in fine lettering, and as you walk past them, they stand up straight and say “Welcome to the Ritz!” Whatever you do, while you’re thinking about it, make sure you think about how they stand up so straight, and associate it with these doormen, who work, specifically, at the Ritz. If you effectively imagine all this, you should be able to remember this on’yomi. Go through it like it’s a movie in your head!', 'Think of someone who has to <kanji>stand</kanji> up straight for you. These people are pros in hospitality. These are the doormen at the <reading>Ritz</reading> (りつ).', 'N4', NULL),
(495, 'If you can get to ten thousand different pieces, then that means your sword is absolutely perfect. If your sword dulls before you get to ten thousand, you have to throw it away.', 'Carefully draw your manga onto these ten thousand leaf pieces. Maybe you even use your sword again to give them some textured details. You''re so creative and talented!', 'Now that you have <kanji>ten thousand</kanji> pieces of tiny leaf paper, you decide to turn them into a <reading>man</reading>ga (まん)! Each little leaf piece will become a panel.', 'N5', NULL),
(497, 'Make sure you know those radicals, yo!', 'Go outside right now and do something to save the condors. If you don''t do something right now there won''t be enough time left!', 'Right <kanji>now</kanji> the <reading>con</reading>dors (こん) are in danger! Not next week, not next year, right now! Haven''t you heard that deforestation and pollution are killing all of the condors?!', 'N5', NULL),
(500, 'And, you have no idea how to get them out. They''re <kanji>inside</kanji> for good, and it''s freaking you out. As in, there''s a little person in there, running around, controlling your brain. People think you''re crazy, but you know better... He''s <kanji>inside</kanji> there, doing evil things.', 'Imagine him holding the knife out, threatening to stab your brain. You’re in a conundrum here. If you don’t take him out, you’re in trouble, but if you do try to take him out, he might stab you in the brain with his knife. Try to think a way around this situation, focusing mainly on his knife.', 'You know this kanji means <kanji>inside</kanji>, so you can keep thinking about the person that''s inside you, running around. You finally discover him, and try to take him out, but before you can, he pulls out a <reading>kni</reading>fe (ない) and threatens to cut you if you do anything irrational.', 'N3', NULL),
(501, 'Picture the fins falling onto the sword, and being divided into many parts. And if those fins represented hours, they’d be divided into minutes. Makes sense, right?', 'This is how you feed your people: neatly divided, delicious buns. Picture these delicious buns divided into perfectly shaped parts. Yummy.', 'Now that the fins have been cut into neat <kanji>part</kanji>s, it''s time to cut something else: <reading>bun</reading>s (ぶん).', 'N5', NULL),
(502, 'Take these seven swords and swing them around. <kanji>Cut</kanji> everything in your path, including yourself. Then, think about how dumb it was to try to use seven swords at once, and then feel the pain of the cut you gave yourself. Ouch, ouch ouch.', 'Just make sure you think about how you need to give them away in <reading>sets</reading>, and not anything else. This is the most important thing to think about in your head as you''re trying to establish the mnemonic for your kanji reading memory. It''s gotta be sets.', 'Because having seven swords for yourself is too much, you decide to take the swords and divide them up amongst pairs of people. You <kanji>cut</kanji> them into pairs, and give <reading>sets</reading> (せつ) to everyone who needs them in the village.', 'N4', NULL),
(503, 'Picture all the kids out sliding on the slide at noon during their lunch break. It''s hot and dry and they''re very happy.', 'Watch in horror as Godzilla''s giant foot crushes all the kids and their slide at exactly noon. You''d better go...', '<kanji>Noon</kanji> also happens to be the time <reading>Go</reading>dzilla (ご) decided to attack for the same reasons: it''s dry and kids are using the slide.', 'N5', NULL),
(504, 'Imagine that narwhal sitting there. It waves its flipper and says "hi friend" to you.', 'Your narwhal friend goes on to warn you about all those youths, and how they will try to take away your friendship with their good looks and young ages.', '"Hi <kanji>friend</kanji>," says the narwhal. "I must warn you about all the <reading>you</reading>ths." (ゆう)', 'N5', NULL),
(505, 'We''ve fattened you up with lots of juicy radical knowledge, so this one should be pretty easy.', 'Picture this fat person curled up all cozy-like on their Japanese futon. They look so content! Have you ever slept on a proper Japanese futon, by the way? We''re talking premium, cloud-grade comfort — luxurious enough to make you feel like a real fat cat. ', 'Imagine a <kanji>fat</kanji> person who loves to sleep on <reading>futo</reading>n (ふと).', 'N3', NULL),
(506, 'Imagine a small slide (how could you not, after you see the radicals?). So few people can use this thing, really, but there are a <em>few</em> little ones who can fit on it at least.', 'Imagine this in your head. Even the few people who fit on the slide are struggling to get past the Shogun. Meanwhile the Japanese secret service is trying to make sure no one gets close to the shogun while he slides. A few people are crying. They want to slide, they want to leave… It''s chaos.', 'Another reason so <kanji>few</kanji> people can use it is that the <reading>Sho</reading>gun (しょう) is trying to go down the slide. We''re talking a grown man who''s way too big for this little slide, so a few people have to wait behind him — it''s a slide traffic jam.', 'N4', NULL),
(507, 'Imagine taking a stick and pulling it back into your bow. Imagine how hard you have to pull. This thing is really strong! ', 'Imagine using all your strength to pull on this bow. Imagine the heat rising in your body. There''s sweat dripping down your face. It''s so hot, but you have to keep pulling!', 'When you <kanji>pull</kanji> the bow back, your body strains and you feel an immense <reading>he</reading>at (ひ) rise inside of you. You keep pulling and the heat grows hotter and hotter.', 'N3', NULL),
(508, 'Also, make sure you know the radicals by heart, it''ll help you to learn more effectively, just like with this kanji.', 'This is when I start kicking you in the shin and you imagine feeling the pain of getting kicked there. As I kick you in the shin, the stress is getting too much for your poor heart inside your shin. Poor shin, and poor heart.', 'The <kanji>heart</kanji>, where is it stored in your body? Most people thought it was in the chest, but in reality, your <kanji>heart</kanji> is stored in your <reading>shin</reading> (しん).', 'N4', NULL),
(509, 'Time to open the door to the reading part of this mnemonic.', 'Feel the pain of your toe going away. Think about the other side of the <kanji>door</kanji>, where your <reading>toe</reading> is probably rolling around after it blasted off your foot.', 'You open the <kanji>door</kanji> and immediately get it closed back on your <reading>toe</reading> (と), literally causing your toe to explode. ', 'N2', NULL),
(510, 'The only direction you need to go in is forward, it''s always the right way.', 'Close your eyes and picture your home in your mind. Can you tell which direction it is from where you are?', 'What <kanji>direction</kanji> do you always know? Which direction do you always want to go? <reading>Ho</reading>me (ほう). Something about your home always stays with you, no matter how far away you go. So if you''re lost and you don''t know which direction to go, just think of home!', 'N4', NULL),
(511, 'To distinguish this from the "noon" kanji (午), try thinking of the bit poking out of the top of this kanji as a tuft of hair on the cow''s head — a cow lick, if you like.', 'I hope you know what わぎゅう beef is, otherwise this will be a difficult one to learn.', 'Have you ever heard of わ<reading>ぎゅう</reading> (wagyu) beef? That ぎゅう is this ぎゅう.', 'N4', NULL),
(512, 'No, I am not your father... but this kanji / radical set is!', 'Hopefully you know who they are. If not, think about your father laughing like this: ちちちちちちちち〜！', 'Imagine your father is <reading>Cheech</reading> (ちち) from Cheech and Chong.', 'N5', NULL),
(513, 'Just make sure you''re studying your radicals, otherwise it won''t be as simple as I''d hoped.', 'Smell the <kanji>fur</kanji> as you <reading>mow</reading> it down. Feel it flying everywhere. It''s giant, so it must be pretty intense. Make it more intense to help yourself to remember the reading of this kanji better.', 'With <kanji>fur</kanji>, whisk your imagination to a land where there are stalks of fur all around, just like a big tall grassy field (except instead of grass, it''s fur). You''re surrounded by the black fur, the wind is blowing. Imagine yourself touching the long fur and smelling it. What does it feel and smell like? Now it''s time to get rid of it. There''s just too much <kanji>hair</kanji>! To get rid of it, you''ll need to <reading>mow</reading> (もう) it down with a lawn mower. Imagine yourself starting up the lawn mower, and taking it around, mowing down the hair. Hear the sound of the mower in your head (they''re loud and distinct). The important thing is to imagine yourself mowing everything. Key is to think "fur → in the fur → get rid of the fur → mow the fur," in that order. That''ll get you to "mow," which will help you to remember the on’yomi.', 'N2', NULL),
(514, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Imagine the fluffy wool of the sheep, stopping you from moving forward. You can''t push past it, no matter how hard you try.', 'You have to <kanji>stop</kanji> because there is a <reading>shee</reading>p (し) in front of you. You try to walk around the sheep, but it moves to stop in front of you again. Every time the sheep stops you stop too.', 'N4', NULL),
(516, 'You go outside. There''s ice everywhere. It''s freezing cold. Yep, this is definitely winter.', 'Your pronunciation might be a little weird. But that’s okay. People still love your presents, and the way you say "fu’ you!" ', '<kanji>Winter</kanji> is also the time of year you give out presents: "One fu'' you, one fu'' you and… one <reading>fu'' you</reading> (ふゆ)!"', 'N4', NULL),
(517, 'Hold on to that radical knowledge! It''ll be the north star that guides you through your studies.', 'Yep, those things are called keytars. And for some reason, in the north, everyone has a keytar. They''re basically obsessed with them. North = keytar. Quite simple.', 'You go <kanji>north</kanji>, and notice that <em>everyone</em> has a <reading>keytar</reading> (きた). You know, those keyboards that people hold and play like a guitar?', 'N5', NULL),
(518, 'Imagine the triceratops on its side, then imagine it getting up. When it stands up normally, half of its body is soaking wet still. It''s a half-n-half triceratops right now.', 'Imagine him using his blaster to shoot the poor triceratops in half, perfectly down the wet-dry line of its body.', '"You''re half wet and half dry! I will cut you in <kanji>half</kanji> with my laser blaster so you''re proper again," a voice says. It is  <reading>Han</reading> Solo (はん), the anti-establishment guy who hates when things aren''t even.', 'N5', NULL),
(519, 'Make sure you know those radicals, yo!', 'Imagine a 子 looking at old things. A record player. A Game Boy. A cassette player. Kids just don''t get old things.

Note: When you see the (古) radical in a kanji, there''s a good chance it will use the reading こ.', '<kanji>Old</kanji> things are misunderstood by the <reading>子</reading>s (こ) of the world.

*Note:* Come up with a 子 character you can use for all the こ reading mnemonics going forward. This child will show up many times throughout your mnemonic journey, and it will help if you imagine the same character the whole time.', 'N4', NULL),
(520, 'Doesn''t this kanji look like a machine mouth? Now wouldn''t you want to keep that private from the people you know?', 'Imagine being so afraid that people will find out about your machine mouth that you have to dye it to match a normal human mouth. Oh, when will this discrimination against machine-kind end?!', 'To make sure they can''t tell what''s <kanji>machine</kanji> and what''s human, you <reading>dye</reading> (だい) the mouth to make it look like skin. You find dye the same shade as your normal mouth and just slap it on there.', 'N4', NULL),
(521, 'After imagining your evening toe overheating, rush outside to fix it. It''s the best way to prevent fire toe, which is way worse. In the evening it''s nice and cold outside too, so feel this on your skin, and feel the cold air on your toe, which is starting to get some relief.', 'If you don''t know Guy Fawkes, you should do a little reading up on him to make this mnemonic make sense. Also, Anonymous People wear a Guy Fawkes mask, if that helps any.', 'You go <kanji>outside</kanji> (you''re not supposed to be out there at night!) and you''re accosted by a couple of guys. Then, all of a sudden, <reading>Guy</reading> (がい) Fawkes jumps from the roof above you and takes them out, then starts running. "Guy Fawkes!" you yell after him!', 'N5', NULL),
(522, 'Picture this towel with the name of a city on it. Any city you like. Maybe it''s your city!', 'Imagine a city that has a sheep everywhere you look, as far as the eye can see. This city was built on sheep, don''t you say a bad word about sheep when you''re there.', 'This <kanji>city</kanji> must have a lot of <reading>shee</reading>p (し) because, besides the manhole lid and the towel, all you can see are sheep. There are sheep everywhere. It''s a sheep city.', 'N3', NULL),
(523, 'You want your private canopy to be wide so you can sprawl out under it as much as you want. No one''s going to come in and tell you to close your legs or sit up straight. So it can be as wide as you like!', 'Picture yourself lying under your wide, private canopy pretending to be a super hero on the ground. You''re stretching a nice, wide super hero pose, like you''re flying through the sky.', 'You like being <kanji>wide</kanji> because it makes you feel like a different person. But not just any person, like a super <reading>hero</reading> (ひろ)!', 'N4', NULL),
(524, 'See how the vertical bar is broken and your window frame is all messed up now? Why would your mother do such a thing?! Her hand is bleeding too…', 'You thought your mother was going to explain or apologize, but instead she''s pointing and saying, "Haha," over and over.

Yeah. That sounds like my mom.', 'Suddenly your <kanji>mother</kanji> points her bloodied finger at you and laughs, "<reading>Haha</reading> (はは)!" She keeps laughing, haha haha haha, over and over. You feel even more alarmed.', 'N5', NULL),
(525, 'Make sure you know those radicals, yo!', 'Eat your yogurt. (Gogurt doesn''t count, don''t @ me.)', 'Today, your <kanji>task</kanji> is to eat <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう). It''s nutritious, it''s delicious, and I''m telling you to do it, so you have to. A task is basically just something you have to do, so that''s what this is.', 'N4', NULL),
(526, 'Just know your radicals and you''ll know this kanji!', 'Imagine the arrow as a human-sized living thing with legs, arms, and clothes, though. Strangely, it’s able to walk around even though it''s an arrow. It has some kind of agenda. Make it a bit evil, if you can. What a strange thing…', 'To remember the reading や we use the word “Yankees” (like the evil baseball team, go M’s!).

What is this arrow doing? It’s following around all the <reading>Ya</reading>nkee (や) baseball players, stalking them, waiting to make its move (I’ll let you decide what its “move” is).', 'N1', NULL),
(545, 'More people can see this character since the kid is on the roof.', 'Imagine the child holding up the letter yelling at everyone who passes that the "J" stands for "Jesus." And yes, this kid just loves the Big Lebowski.', 'The <kanji>character</kanji> that is being shown is "J," which stands for <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ). The kid wants everyone to see this <kanji>letter</kanji> and <kanji>symbol</kanji>.', 'N4', NULL),
(626, 'If you already know the radical, this one''s a freebie. That should make your day a little brighter, hm?', 'You might also say "Ah!" when you come up with a bright idea, like in those light bulb moments.', 'What do you say when you see something <kanji>bright</kanji>? "<reading>Ah</reading> (あ)!" Like when you open the curtain and the sunshine hits your eyes, or someone shines a flashlight right into your face. Ahhhh, so bright!', 'N4', NULL),
(850, 'Study your radicals and you''ll read good.', 'Imagine having someone''s life in the palm of your hands. It''s up to you whether you protect it with your saber or... well... do the other thing sabers are for.', 'You can save <kanji>life</kanji> or take it away with your new <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい). This is the saber you got from doing the correct thing (remember?), and now you need to figure out how to use it with this life. So what do you do?', 'N5', NULL),
(493, 'Know your radicals and therefore know your kanji. It''s just that simple.', 'Whoops, maybe you shouldn''t throw stones through windows or you''ll see people''s sexy times.', 'You throw a <kanji>stone</kanji> through a window... and, now you can see something very <reading>sexy</reading> (せき).', 'N3', NULL),
(498, 'Know your radicals!', 'Genji''s origin story is pretty important when it comes to mnemonics and Japanese literature. He''s going to keep popping up on your kanji journey, so make sure you remember him!', 'The <kanji>origin</kanji> of everything can be found in <reading>Gen</reading>ji (げん). Genji is the name of the main character in the world''s first ever novel, "The Tale of Genji." 

Picture him as a rich, pretty boy who cares a ton about his looks and gets along really well with all the ladies. He''s also really great at poetry, which helps the lady situation.', 'N4', NULL),
(527, 'It''s all these generational differences that make the world go ''round!', 'Hold the saber up high and pledge to protect the world for all of the generations to come! Then swish it around and make buzzing sounds with your mouth.', 'It''s your job to protect the <kanji>world</kanji> and every <kanji>generation</kanji> with your special <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい). It was given to you to uphold correctness, and you''ll pass it on to the next generation when it''s time. Then they''ll watch out for the world when you''re gone.', 'N4', NULL),
(528, '', 'Picture this master of water controlling the droplet as it flies around inside these chutes, like he''s solving a difficult puzzle with it. Astounding!', 'Do you remember back to the radical mnemonic, where the <kanji>master</kanji> is floating a water drop above his head? Imagine back to there. Once you do, the most amazing thing happens. The master sends the drop of water into a <reading>chu</reading>te (しゅ) and it goes careening through an intricate series of them.', 'N4', NULL),
(529, 'Imagine different groups of leaders — they''re all dressed nice and normal. Then, off to the side are the leaders of bears, cats, alligators, and narwhals... They''re the <em>other</em> ones, no matter how you look at it. They are in their own group, and they are considered "others" because society won''t accept them :(', 'Just imagine the "others" shaking their heads at these taco worshipers. Seriously, what the heck?', 'To remember the reading た, we use the word "taco":

Then there''s the <kanji>other</kanji>s of the others. These are the <reading>ta</reading>co (た) worshipers. They''re really strange.', 'N3', NULL),
(531, 'Picture how ridiculous this beggar looks, taking copies of his forehead with the ground copy machine. ', 'This particular shaman''s specialty is being able to turn any surface into a copy machine at a swirl of his little shaman wand. Picture him running around in circles, doing a little dance and chant, and then the ground suddenly turning into one giant copy machine.', 'This ground <kanji>copy</kanji> machine can only be the work of one person: a <reading>sha</reading>man (しゃ)!', 'N4', NULL),
(532, 'Keep your private dirt in the past. It''s better for everyone.', 'Imagine physically, literally burying your past 子 self. If you hide it in Little Kyoto, no one will ever be able to dig it up.', 'To remember the reading きょ, we''ll use "Little Kyoto." This will differentiate きょ from きょう, which is regular-sized Kyoto. Get it? You decide to bury all this private dirt from your <kanji>past</kanji> in Little <reading>Kyo</reading>to (きょ). That stuff is from when you were just a little <reading>子</reading> (こ) and you don''t like to think about it.', 'N4', NULL),
(533, 'What are these numbers? What do they mean? Think over it for a while, get frustrated, and try to figure it out. You can''t, though, so you probably won''t ever get inside.', 'Grandpa ごういち keeps saying numbers as you free him from the prison. Just one after another. Numbers numbers numbers. Hopefully old man ごういち isn''t crazy.', '"You want to know the <kanji>number</kanji> to the hatch?" yells an old voice from inside. You say "yes," and the voice gives you the numbers and you''re in! You go down into the prison, where there is one prisoner: Grandpa <reading>ごう</reading>いち.

Note: For the ごう reading mnemonics, we use Grandpa ごういち because こういち is used for the こう reading. So just remember that ごう comes from Koichi''s grandpa ごういち.', 'N3', NULL),
(534, 'That means if you know your radicals well, you know the meaning of this kanji as well! How swell.', 'Next time you put a belt on, make sure it''s in the exact center of your body. Heck, try it now. And don''t forget to say "Ohh!" as you do it. It''ll keep the reading for this kanji front and center in your mind.', 'Remember that guy from the 央 radical fastening his belt across the <kanji>center</kanji> of his body? This guy always secures his belt at the center — not slightly to the left, not slightly to the right, but dead center. His precision is so mind-blowing that everyone watching can''t help but say, "<reading>Ohh</reading>!"(おう).', 'N2', NULL),
(535, 'Imagine carefully taking off your horns and laying them flat on a paper towel. You have to smooth them out so they''re as flat as possible.', 'Imagine carefully laying a flat layer of hay on top of your flat horns. You feel really at peace and... you''re tired now. You decided to lie on top of the hay and take a nap while you wait for your horns to dry.', 'Being <kanji>flat</kanji> isn''t enough, so you cover the whole thing in <reading>hay</reading> (へい). Hay absorbs water, so you add a flat layer of it.', 'N3', NULL),
(536, 'Imagine: it''s just you, your fingers, and the wild street. It''s hitting time!', 'Now imagine your dad saying, "Don''t do a hit!" over and over at you. "I''m sorry dad! But the streets were calling me!"', 'As you <kanji>hit</kanji> one chump after another, suddenly your <reading>da</reading>d (だ) comes marching out of your house. Uh oh. Dad caught you hitting, which, honestly, isn''t a very nice thing to do, so he has every right to stop you.', 'N3', NULL),
(537, 'Imagine yourself adding that drop. What is it exactly? Something pretty potent, that''s for sure. Now go ahead and touch the block of ice... wow, it''s really cold — and only from one drop, too. Feel the burn on your finger from where you touched the <kanji>ice</kanji>.', 'How did this happen? Was it your fault? Imagine the situation that would get Corey frozen like this.', 'When you think of <kanji>ice</kanji>, I want you to think of someone frozen with ice. Do you know anyone named <reading>Corey</reading> (こおり)? Anyone famous named Corey, perhaps Corey Feldman? Imagine this Corey person frozen solid with ice.', 'N2', NULL),
(538, 'Imagine yourself with a cross inside your mouth, saying all kinds of humble things. "Oh no, I''m not that good." "Oh no, my mustache doesn''t look that great."', 'Imagine mowing your neighbor''s lawn while you say humbly how bad you are at mowing lawns. What a good neighbor you are.', 'As you''re saying all these humble things, you''re also <reading>mow</reading>ing (もう) your neighbor''s lawn. Your neighbors love you so much, because you''re so humble, and you mow their lawn for them.', 'N3', NULL),
(540, 'If you know your radicals, you know your kanji too.', 'Imagine someone with a huge head, washing dishes. When I say huge, I mean huge. She turns towards you with her big head and says “Hi, I’m Sarah” and you see her name tag, which also says Sarah. She is Sarah, the dish washer. The plates she holds up next to her head pale in comparison, even though they''re the biggest pieces.', 'The person washing all the <kanji>dish</kanji>es was named <reading>Sarah</reading> (さら). She was named that because of her large head, which was shaped like Sarah Jessica Parker’s head (very big).', 'N2', NULL),
(541, 'It''s raining, so you lend Totoro your umbrella. As a thanks, he gives you a present wrapped in bamboo leaves.', 'Thanks for the new ray gun, Totoro!', 'So what did you receive as a <kanji>thanks</kanji>? You open it up, and there''s a <reading>ray</reading> (れい) gun inside!', 'N3', NULL),
(542, 'The best thing to do with a tree is rest under it. Picture the leader resting under the tree with his eyes half closed.', 'Don''t email me saying cucumbers don''t grow on trees. Cucumber trees exist. Look them up and then rest easy knowing I''d never lie to you.', 'As he <kanji>rest</kanji>s, he suddenly gets hungry and pulls a <reading>cu</reading>cumber (きゅう) off the tree and bites into it. A good rest isn''t complete without some crunchy tree cucumbers.', 'N5', NULL),
(543, 'Try to imagine the previous person — they must have been absolutely filthy to leave all this dirt behind. Why''d they have to go down the slide ahead of you?! If only you could''ve gotten here before them…', 'If you look really far off into the distance ahead of you, you might still be able to spot this dirty, slide-ruining centaur. Hurry up and go after him before he gets on another slide!', 'Then you realize: the <kanji>previous</kanji> person on the slide must''ve been a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん). Centaurs track dirt everywhere ''cause they''re always galloping around in the wilderness.', 'N5', NULL),
(544, 'Imagine yourself going to the area you chose, and watching "Evening Mouth" speak. There are thousands of others there, wanting to see the giant floating mouth in the night sky. "Evening Mouth" then says "Everyone, what''s my <kanji>name</kanji>?!" and everyone shouts back "Evening Mouth!" in unison. "Evening Mouth" has certainly gotten really <kanji>famous</kanji>.', 'You know what they say. April showers bring May Flowers. What do <reading>May</reading> Flowers bring? They bring “Evening Mouth,” baby.', 'So, when does “Evening Mouth” appear? Turns out, “Evening Mouth” only comes out to talk in <reading>May</reading> (めい). You know this because it’s just starting to be spring, and when you look around (you’re watching “Evening Mouth” again) you see some spring flowers come up.', 'N5', NULL),
(546, 'Last year was a close call and you ended up shooting the cow''s horn, which is why the cow radical looks a little strange here. Can you see how the cow radical fits underneath the gun?', 'Nendoroids are Japanese plastic figurines. (Look them up if you need to! And yes, there are cow nendoroids.) It probably won’t be easy to make a cow-sized nendoroid this year, but better than having to shoot a real one.', 'It seems everyone has caught on to the fact that you deliberately miss every <kanji>year</kanji>, so this year you make a <reading>nen</reading>doroid (ねん) decoy in the shape of a cow.', 'N5', NULL),
(547, 'Imagine the sun coming up slowly, over the horizon. It then lines up with the cross, and at that moment, your alarm goes off, waking you up. You check the alarm clock, and instead of displaying the time, it just says EARLY and FAST on it, flashing in big red letters and alternating.', 'But you get your soul up early and head to the church fast anyway, because you''re doing all of this for your soul in the first place!', 'To remember the そう reading we use the word "soul."

When you''re forced to get up <kanji>fast</kanji> and <kanji>early</kanji> you can feel it in your <reading>sou</reading>l (そう). You''re so tired and it''s so early, your soul cries out, "Please don''t make me go this fast this early. I''m gonna leave your body if you keep this up!"', 'N4', NULL),
(548, 'Touch the energy, catch the spirit ball. Is it solid or gaseous? Does it tingle or burn? Try to imagine it out. Then, yes, go ahead and sell that valuable energy treasure to a power company or something.', 'You take the key and open another treasure that''s nearby. Open it up and see what''s inside. Imagine something spectacular. Thanks, energy key.', 'As you reach inside the <kanji>energy</kanji> <kanji>spirit</kanji> ball, you feel something hard inside. It''s a large <reading>key</reading> (き) that gives you access to another treasure.', 'N5', NULL),
(549, 'Imagine flying toward the sun to see a hundred leaves growing together in the shape of one big leaf. You want to go closer!', 'Imagine hearing Goofy guh-hyuck over and over after you save him from being stranded on the sun. How would you feel being stuck in a cramped ship with laughing Goofy and his hundred leaves? Oh, you thought he would leave those behind? Nope, they saved him so you have to take them back too. Happy travels!', 'You fly closer, counting all one <kanji>hundred</kanji> leaves when you hear a strange sound. You take off your space helmet and hear, "GUH <reading>HYUCK</reading> (ひゃく)!" You know that laugh... it''s Goofy! On top of the hundredth leaf is Goofy laughing and waving around. He must have built this leaf tower to get your attention.', 'N5', NULL),
(550, 'Just make sure you know your radicals!', 'Really take as much bamboo as you could possibly hold. Take some bamboo, then take some more, and take more… It’s free after all.', 'Today is free <kanji>bamboo</kanji> day, so just <reading>take</reading> (たけ) all the bamboo you want. ', 'N2', NULL),
(551, 'This is why it''s good to make sure you know your radicals.', 'Think about how it tastes. Think about how it feels in your throat. Let it get caught on your teeth and around your tongue. It''s a weird feeling having thread in your mouth, and you want to use that weird feeling to remember you''re going to <reading>eat</reading> it.', 'You have some <kanji>thread</kanji>... what''s the first thing you do with it? You take it and put it in your mouth, and start sucking it down like spaghetti. You have decided to <reading>eat</reading> (いと) the thread.', 'N2', NULL),
(552, 'Study those radicals hard to make sure you know your kanji as well!', 'This is sort of a stereotypical singer sort of image. Make sure you concentrate a lot when you''re listening to your mimimimiiii tone. In fact, why don''t you do it out loud right now - make a little fool of yourself. It''s fun, and it''ll help you to remember!', 'Put your hand to your <kanji>ear</kanji> and sing... "<reading>Mimi</reading>mimimimiiiii!" (みみ) and listen to your tone with your ear.', 'N3', NULL),
(553, 'It''s times like these when you gotta hope you''re studying your radicals!', 'Imagine little words that say "mush" flying out from under your foot as you mush the poor thing. What does a "mush" sound sound like? You''ll have to come up with something unique to yourself, and focus on it to remember this reading.', 'It''s an <kanji>insect</kanji>! Get it! You squish it with your foot - what sound does it make? A <reading>mush</reading> (むし) sound.', 'N2', NULL),
(554, 'There is a whole group of people out there who are measuring these trees. Imagine them asking each other "do you think this will be a good tree for our village center?" and other people nodding in agreement or disagreement as they measure.', 'Look at the mural on the side of this newly constructed village center. So many trees! Visualize the whole process of the tree becoming a building, as you reflect on the message of the mural.', 'They take all the wood back to where they''re building their <kanji>village</kanji>, and once they construct the village center they paint a <reading>mura</reading>l (むら) on the side of it. The mural is of the forest where they got the trees from, to honor the village''s origins.', 'N2', NULL),
(555, 'That means if you know your radicals well, you know the meaning of this kanji too! Man, kanji are easy sometimes.', 'Picture the man watching as the first light of dawn tints the sky pink and orange. Time to get to work, man!', 'When you think of a <kanji>man</kanji>, think of <reading>dawn</reading> (だん), because that man has to get up at dawn to power his rice paddy every day. ', 'N5', NULL),
(556, 'Think about it, a single farm wouldn''t need a whole street. Just paths would do. There is a street next to this rice paddy, so there must be other people, which makes this a <kanji>town</kanji>.', 'Remember imagining Mrs. Chou in great detail in level 2? Time for some of that work to come to fruition. Imagine Mrs. Chou walking down the street of the town towards you, ready to make your day bad. She breaks things as she walks towards you, then knocks you down for walking around in her town. She says, "Wherever there''s a street, you''ll find Mrs. Chou." Then spits on your back as she walks away.', 'You go to visit this nice little <kanji>town</kanji>. But then, there she is... Every time you walk down the street, the street-woman Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう) arrives to ruin your day.', 'N4', NULL),
(557, 'Your leader is a nice guy who wants there to be flowers for future generations to enjoy. That''s why he''s painstakingly planting all these flowers with his spoon. Imagine watching him dig hole after hole with his little spoon just to plant some flowers for everyone.', 'You and everyone else are free to enjoy the flowers too, but… Just make sure to get out of the way when Hannah shows up. These flowers are really for her.', 'Secretly, the leader is actually planting all these <kanji>flower</kanji>s for <reading>Hannah</reading> (はな), a girl he has a huge crush on. Everyone thinks the leader''s a nice, selfless guy, but really he''s just trying to impress Hannah.', 'N4', NULL),
(558, 'Make sure you''re studying your radicals. If you do, you''ll know a lot of kanji as well, almost magically!', 'The struggling to see bit is pretty important — make sure that you slowly see a little better and better as the person comes closer, but it''s difficult the whole time (struggles are good for memory). Then, it starts to become more and more clear, and you start saying "that can''t be <reading>me</reading>. No way it''s me..." When the person comes up to you (the person, being you) you learn about parallel universes and other fabulous things. Still, the whole time you can''t stop thinking about how "this person looks just like me... no, this person is me!"', 'You see something. It''s walking towards you, but you need to see better. When the person comes close, you realize "That person is <reading>me</reading> (み)!"', 'N5', NULL),
(559, 'Shellfish are shellfish, you know?', 'Imagine rowing your kayak out into the ocean to collect shellfish. Imagine the color and shape of the kayak. Feel the spray of the salt water and the taste of some fresh shellfish, right out of the ocean.', 'To get some <kanji>shellfish</kanji>, all you have to do is hop in your <reading>kay</reading>ak (かい) and get some. Shellfish live in the water.', 'N2', NULL),
(560, 'What a lucky ducky — as long as you know your radicals, you''ll know this kanji''s meaning as well!', 'Think of あか as being some sort of alias for "red." That''s why it''s pronounced あか in Japanese, AKA "red."', 'This kanji means <kanji>red</kanji>, <reading>AKA</reading>, also known as, あか. ', 'N4', NULL),
(561, 'Same kanjis and radicals make Jack a happy boy. You should also learn the other meaning as well, though, if you can.', 'Just think about your bare feet. Think about them too much. Feel how cold it is on your feet. Now, put on a sock to make it feel better. Ahhh, so nice!', 'What do you wear on your <kanji>foot</kanji>? You wear a <reading>sock</reading> (そく).', 'N4', NULL),
(562, 'Make sure you study your radicals... though don''t do it while driving.', 'The more fantastic you make this car, the better. Also, feel the surprise of a shaman getting out of this car. What a cool dude that shaman is.', 'When we need to remember the reading しゃ, we use "shaman".

There''s a <kanji>car</kanji>. It''s the nicest car you''ve ever seen. Exquisite, beautiful. Wouldn''t you be surprised to learn it belongs to a <reading>sha</reading>man (しゃ)? Well it does. This shaman charges a premium for his shaman services so he can afford fancy cars like this.', 'N5', NULL),
(563, 'Actually, it does make it easy — the radicals make kanji wayyy easier, no?', 'Be sure to feel irritated at that person. Think in your head as they''re telling these dumb "not" jokes how much of a fool they are... and how much of a fool you are for sitting here and listening to them.', 'To remember ふ we''ll use the word "fool."

Think of those jokes, where someone says something, then ends with a loud "<kanji>NOTTTT</kanji>!" Someone is doing that to you, over and over. What is that person? That person is a <reading>foo</reading>l (ふ).', 'N4', NULL),
(564, 'Imagine the leader samurai doing things. He''s doing more than anyone else, no one can keep up with what he''s doing.', 'Picture the leader samurai doing lots of sheep things. He''s shearing sheep. He''s feeding sheep. He''s doing everything you could possibly do, because he''s the sheep samurai.', 'He was <kanji>doing</kanji> things with <reading>shee</reading>p (し). He is the leader of the sheep clan, and he was able to do more with sheep than anyone else. That''s why he''s the leader.', 'N4', NULL),
(1118, NULL, 'Imagine yourself laughing at Ana while you point at her, continuing to jeer as Ana struggles to escape the hole. You’d help Ana out of the hole, but you’re too busy laughing at her.', '<reading>Ana</reading> (あな) fell into a <kanji>hole</kanji>. Laugh at Ana and her unfortunate misfortune of having fallen into a hole like a ninny. Haha, Ana – you’re stupid and clumsy!', 'N1', NULL),
(1520, 'Picture the treasure reflected in the drop of water. It''s marvelous!', 'Picture his home from the outside. It looks like a normal, small castle. But then you walk in and it''s a treasure trove of... well, treasure!', 'All of this <kanji>treasure</kanji> is kept in the king''s <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう). He can''t let other people see how much treasure he has, or they''ll get mad. Or worse, ask him to share some with his people. Yuck. The treasure stays at home.', 'N2', NULL),
(515, 'To help this mnemonic along, literally imagine your older brother as someone that''s just a giant mouth with a pair of legs walking around. It talks and talks and talks, too, irritating you. Feel the irritation specifically at your older brother. He''s older than you, so you can''t beat him up... yet.', 'Hmm... don''t you want to go to きょうと someday? Maybe you should have sent your brother somewhere less cool.', 'You wish you could box your loud-mouth <kanji>older brother</kanji> up and send him far away to <reading>きょう</reading>と. きょうと is super far away from where you currently live, so just ship your older bro off so he can never bother you again.', 'N4', NULL),
(530, 'Picture the leader of the ceremony out back, unsure of what period or age they''re in, while the substitute leader walks in and stares at them in shock.', 'Now you''re out back with the original leader and the substitute leader, trying to dye the color of this stupid shirt to match that one.', 'The <kanji>substitute</kanji> leader is wearing the wrong color clothes, so you have to <reading>dye</reading> (だい) them, like they did in the olden <kanji>age</kanji>s.', 'N4', NULL),
(539, 'Imagine sliding down a slide made of rough, gnarly tree branches. Their bark digs into your skin and pulls it right off of your body. Okay, it''s painful, but it''s a small price to pay for having such beautifully exfoliated skin.', 'Your skin usually protects <em>you</em> from the heat, but imagine how raw it would be after you shed the old layer all at once. It''s kinda vulnerable right now, so take care of it.', 'Without your rough old <kanji>skin</kanji> to protect you, you feel the full <reading>he</reading>at (ひ) of the sun now. It burns… it burns! Yeah, you better protect that fresh new skin from the heat.', 'N2', NULL),
(565, 'This may seem traumatic, but actually your father is surprisingly into it. Imagine him mixed in with all kinds of other things. It''s important to note that this kanji is about things being mixed in, not so much about the action of mixing. That''s another kanji. ', '"I love mixing in with potatoes and tomatoes," your father こういち states. "Turn up that temperature. I like to mingle with a bit more tingle."', 'With a <kanji>mix</kanji> of your father and other ingredients <kanji>mingl</kanji>ing in your cooking pot, you lift up the lid... your father pops up. Your father is <reading>こう</reading>いち. He stares back at you with a big smile.', 'N3', NULL),
(566, 'Know your radicals, yo!', '"I met my soulmate in a kayak," said Koichi.', 'What better way to <kanji>meet</kanji> new people than to start <reading>kay</reading>aking (かい)! Get into your kayak and just float out into the water. You''ll eventually bump into someone else in a kayak and boom, you''re meeting someone new!', 'N4', NULL),
(567, 'Absorbing light is natural for reptiles, and a round one can absorb even more. Imagine this strange, round piceratops basking in the warm rays of light shining down from the sky. What a rare sight you''ve come across.', 'Imagine many different (but also kind of the same) こういちs attacking and throwing spears at the piceratops. Little do they know that it''s fully charged up after absorbing all that light, and it unleashes a bright flash of energy, rendering all the こういちs momentarily blind and allowing it to escape.', 'As you watch this majestic beast absorbing <kanji>light</kanji>, suddenly several dozen feral <reading>こう</reading>いちs burst out from the wilderness, spears in hand. Oh no! Those こういちs are hunting this rare piceratops!', 'N3', NULL),
(568, 'Pick up the mustache, hold it up next to your face in front of a mirror. It''s unbelievable, but it''s the same in every way, down to the last hair.', 'If it helps, imagine hundreds or thousands of doughnuts all around you with the same exact mustache. First of all, it''s terrifying to be surrounded by thousands of mutant doughnuts. Secondly, it''s really weird they all have the same mustache as you.', 'When we need to remember the reading どう, we use the word "doughnut".

While this is already strange enough, you notice something stranger. Over a couple feet away is a <reading>dou</reading>ghnut (どう) who also has the <kanji>same</kanji> mustache as you (and the one that fell on the ground). The doughnut looks at you for a moment, then scampers off. This doughnut must be a mutant because normally doughnuts don''t move.', 'N4', NULL),
(569, 'Imagine yourself counting and counting smaller and smaller mouths inside of each other, recording the number of times you can find a mouth inside another mouth. Dig deeper and deeper until it feels ridiculous how small the mouths are getting. Be sure to focus on the counting and the number of times you find one. Now, how many mouths is this times two, because your mouth is like this too.', 'Your kayak is slashing and dipping through the revolving mouths, going down and down more times than you can count!', 'If you''re having a hard time wrapping your head around the infinite number of <kanji>times</kanji> these mouths <kanji>revolve</kanji>, just picture yourself in a little <reading>kay</reading>ak (かい) going around and down through the mouths.', 'N3', NULL),
(570, 'If you accidentally think "a lot" or something similar, that''s okay too. It''s the basic meaning that counts, and all these things are pretty close, though eventually you want to get to the point where you know that this kanji does in fact mean <kanji>many</kanji>.', 'The tacos pelt you and knock you off your feet. Hard shell, soft shell, meat, lettuce, tomatoes, cheese... it''s terrible. And there are so many of them, flying from the sky to drown you in tacos. This is absolutely absurd (and you should think that too, so you remember the reading).', 'To remember the reading た we use the word "taco."

Let''s start with the kanji meaning: <kanji>many</kanji>. You go outside in the evening (because that''s what this kanji is made up of) and are surprised when you''re hit by <kanji>many</kanji> <reading>ta</reading>cos (た) flying from the sky. For some reason it''s raining tacos, and of course you have no idea why.', 'N4', NULL),
(571, 'Picture a triceratops. Now picture Wolverine. An equals sign between them. A little arrow pointing at the horns, an arrow pointing at the blades. And a big flashing CORRECT sign above them. Need I say more?', 'If you''re lucky, you might see Wolverine fighting a triceratops in the middle of Shibuya Crossing in Tokyo. Heroes and monsters are always fighting in Tokyo, right? At least that''s what my anime tells me.', 'Because you got the answer <kanji>correct</kanji>, you win a free trip to <reading>とう</reading>きょう. Congrats!', 'N3', NULL),
(572, 'Make sure you get every one, and don''t miss any of them. Actually search around in your imagination for any more windows, because you want to stress it''s important you hit each and every window with your gun.', 'Now you need to shoot every one of the mice that came through those broken windows. You can''t let any survive. You have to get every last one out of here!!', 'Now that <kanji>every</kanji> window is broken, you feel pretty stupid because <reading>mi</reading>ce (まい) are pouring in through <kanji>each</kanji> one. You''ve made a way for mice to get into your house. That wasn''t smart.', 'N5', NULL),
(574, 'Aren''t radicals nice? As nice and as fundamental as… rice. They make things so much easier, if you''ve been studying them.', 'Imagine helping to bulldoze the rice into the bay, watching it fill it up, then pile high. A bay isn''t the best space for rice storage, but it''ll do. The important thing is that you think you have way too much of the stuff, so the only way to get rid of it is to dump it in a large body of water, and that large body of water is a bay.', 'A ton of <kanji>rice</kanji> is grown in <kanji>America</kanji>. And yet you never see rice in American cuisine. So where''s all that unused rice going? Actually, it all gets dumped into a big <reading>bay</reading> (べい). They have too much rice and no place to store it, so they''ve started using bays to store it. ', 'N3', NULL),
(575, 'Have you been studying your radicals? If so, this''ll be easy!', 'Feel the honey in each feather, taste it trickling over your lips... And go ahead and plummet towards the ground (because you were jumping off a building to fly when someone doused you with honey). At least it''s a tasty way to go, no?', 'You''ve gathered <kanji>feather</kanji> after feather, like some kind of wannabe birdman or birdwoman, and now you''re ready to fly. But as soon as you''re about to take off, someone dumps <reading>honey</reading> (はね) on you, making your feathers all goopy.', 'N2', NULL),
(576, 'Imagine him in his coffin, changing from a begging position to a stereotypical "thinking" position. His hand is on his chin, eyebrows furrowed. Focus on the beggar''s thinking. This is a pretty abstract idea to imagine, so it will be important to create a vivid picture in your mind''s eye.', 'Then, the beggar realizes that he <em>is</em> こういち. Here he is begging in a coffin, and still he only thinks of himself. ', 'What does the beggar <kanji>think</kanji> of? A thought bubble appears near his head. In it is a picture of <reading>こう</reading>いち! He''s thinking that if こういち were here, he''d save him. Oh, if only こういち could save us all!', 'N4', NULL),
(577, 'Study those radicals, and perhaps we''ll eat some meat tonight.', 'If you don''t know anyone named Nick, then just imagine it''s Nic Cage. Picture him as he chews that succulent meat, maybe even closing his eyes in gastronomic ecstasy. Yeah. Go for it, Nic. Enjoy that meat.', 'You know who loves <kanji>meat</kanji>? <reading>Nick</reading> (にく). You probably know a Nick — imagine him eating some succulent meat right now.', 'N4', NULL),
(578, 'Radicals have self. Kanji have self. Kanji and radicals can sometimes be the same thing... so do they really have "self"? Only way to find out is to study both of them hard!', 'You can feel free to do some introspection here.', 'When we need to remember じ we use the word "Jesus."

Who said you should always look at your <kanji>self</kanji> before tossing stones (or whatever) at others? <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ) did. So, make sure you look at your own self before being a jerk.', 'N4', NULL),
(579, 'Color me pink! This kanji''s super easy if you know your radicals.', 'You can even have someone ask you about your ear... "Is your ear okay? I think it''s the color blue..."', 'You already have <kanji>color</kanji> on the mind because you know the meaning of this kanji, so think of something colorful about yourself. Imagine your <reading>ear</reading> (いろ) being a weird color... green, purple, red, whatever. Just make it really bright and standing out.', 'N4', NULL),
(580, 'If you know your radicals well enough (and you do), you''ll know this kanji.', 'Remember, whenever we see the reading こう, think of こういち, a founder of WaniKani (who loves gyoza). We''ll keep it consistent, so over time you should develop a character around こういち that you learn to love or hate.', '"Oh, did he <vocabulary>go</vocabulary> again?" When <reading>こう</reading>いち is not around, that''s what people often say because he goes to the <reading>gyo</reading>za (ぎょう) restaurant almost every day. こういち just loves gyoza so much, you always know where he is when he is not home.', 'N5', NULL),
(581, 'Make sure you know all those radicals, it will make your life more pleasant.', 'Imagine the dusty, ramshackle towns in the Wild West where you find your niche. You set up shops with big signs that say SNAKE OIL. Congrats, you''ve cornered the snake oil niche in the Wild West!', 'You go off to the <vocabulary>west</vocabulary>. It''s the Wild West, even. There aren''t many people out here, making it easy to find a <reading>niche</reading> (にし) for your snake oil business.', 'N5', NULL),
(582, 'Think of our president (or your prime minister, or dictator, or whatever) and imagine this person appearing on television one morning with a big ol'' lip ring. Feel surprised by it. Just say "What??" over and over again. You want to have the feeling of "what" in you when you imagine this — that''ll help you to remember this kanji''s meaning.', 'Imagine this leader calling the nanny over. "What is it?" asks the nanny. "Naan!" shouts the leader. What would that leader do without their naan nanny? What indeed.', 'This leader with the lip ring wants one thing. <kanji>What</kanji> is it? A <reading>nanny</reading> (なに) who can make <reading>naan</reading> (なん). The leader loves naan because it''s soft enough to eat with their lip ring on. And a nanny needs to make it because the leader can''t do anything on their own. ', 'N5', NULL),
(583, 'Imagine looking down at your body and thinking, huh, I wonder what all that junk is? Oh well, I''m sure the leader has it all figured out in that book of hers.', 'Picture the leader, reading a book, putting a tie on her body. Look around at you and your family and your neighbors. You don''t know what your body meat-bags do and you don''t know how to tie a tie. Womp womp.', 'The leader''s <kanji>body</kanji> book also taught her about something very body specific: how to tie a <reading>tie</reading> (たい). You can''t have a tie on your body, no no. Those are for the leader and her smart body.', 'N4', NULL),
(584, 'Imagine the leader taking his key to open up a safe that has a button to make other keys for the underlings. No key is as great as this key, though.', 'Imagine the leader making the keys and dropping them one by one into a big sack. He''s always walking around carrying a big sack over his shoulder, distributing the keys to his underlings and watching them saw them into shape.', 'The leader <kanji>make</kanji>s all the keys and puts them in a <reading>sack</reading> (さく) to distribute them to the underlings, who then cut them into shape with a <reading>saw</reading> (さ).', 'N4', NULL),
(585, 'Scientists have spent decades trying to figure out what the diagram of two massive drops and treasure in your mouth could mean, but to no avail. Imagine the pain of having to spend your entire life with a mouth full of two massive drops and treasure… poor you.', 'Now you’re stuck at this zoo with your diagram mouth full of two massive drops and treasure. Scientists think this can''t be it, so further research will be required. Your diagram mouth of drops and treasure isn''t off the hook quite yet. Just a few more years, spent at this zoo! I promise! ', 'After decades of analyzing the <kanji>diagram</kanji>, scientists found out it leads to… a <reading>zoo</reading> (ず)?! ', 'N4', NULL),
(586, 'Imagine him and the other samurai with their flags on sticks, waving them around shouting things like "We want a voice!”, “Give us a voice!”', 'Imagine your surprise at seeing this large protest of 子 samurai, shouting for their voices to be heard. Really make sure you can hear yourself going “You’re a 子, eh?!” in your head.', 'You decide to join the samurai’s protest for more <vocabulary>voice</vocabulary> but immediately notice… they’re all <reading>子, eh</reading> (こえ)?!', 'N3', NULL),
(588, 'If you don''t have a younger brother, just pretend you do for now. Imagine him really vividly. He is a stereotypical little boy, playing dress-up with horns and a bow and arrow, and he''s going down a makeshift slide held up by a stick.', 'Imagine the bright red, blue, green and purple dye in your younger brother''s hair. He''s put so much on that he''s dyed half his face too. He''s going to be in trouble when he gets home!', 'Your <kanji>younger brother</kanji> loves playing dress-up so much that he''s now started <reading>dye</reading>ing (だい) his hair all kinds of colors.', 'N4', NULL),
(589, 'Picture all the different shapes this hairy lantern could take in your mind.', 'Picture the shape of this hairy, glowing cake.', 'You stare at the <kanji>shape</kanji> until you finally recognize it: <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい)! Well, it''s hairy, glowing cake, but it''s still shaped like a big slice of cake.', 'N3', NULL),
(590, 'This kanji kind of looks like a Predator face, so just imagine Predators come whenever the ground is scattered with rice. They come from all around to grab handfuls of it. Who''s next, then? Come forward!', 'If you love rye grains, on the other hand, imagine them being really happy that they came to get the rice. They''re so lucky it''s rye!', 'When they come, though, they''re all disappointed. It turns out it''s <reading>rye</reading> (らい) grains, and rye grains taste terrible. Yuck.', 'N5', NULL),
(591, 'Picture a young person way back in old-timey history times burying the spirit of one of their ancestors in the dirt. Now watch as an old-timey time lapse plays out in front of your eyes and a company is born!', 'Imagine going to a shaman and having them tell you that for your company to be successful you need to bury a spirit in the dirt so it can help the company grow. You should probably listen.', 'Where did you learn about this method of <kanji>company</kanji> growing? From a <reading>sha</reading>man (しゃ), of course. Shamans aren''t just spouting nonsense, they know what they''re talking about. And spirits are right up their shaman alley.', 'N4', NULL),
(592, 'You gotta know your radicals first! Only then can you start to study these kanji from other angles — meanings, readings, all that good stuff. One angle at a time, you know?', 'Kinda like how a dog cocks its head at an angle when it''s trying to understand what you say. That''s what you look like when you''re studying radicals! Aww, you''re so cute.', 'To see those kanji angles from every <kanji>angle</kanji>, you <reading>cock</reading> (かく) your head to the side.', 'N2', NULL),
(593, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Genji is a real smooth talker. In fact, he can talk his way out of anything. If you can just stay calm and think "What would Genji say?" before saying anything, you''ll probably end up saying something cool instead of something lame.', 'If you aren''t sure what to <kanji>say</kanji>, just think about <reading>Gen</reading>ji (げん) and ask yourself, "What would Genji say?"', 'N4', NULL),
(594, 'Imagine a bunch of Valley Girls walking around with these strange hats. Fins and fish mouths... what a strange new fad.', 'What a jerk he is. Imagine the deep tan lines on TAN-E as well. He''s the epitome of robot laziness.', 'Hanging around with all the <kanji>Valley</kanji> Girls in the sunny area of LA is also a robot. It''s WALL-E''s distant (and lazy, lounging) relative, <reading>TAN-E</reading> (たに). He sits around getting a tan in LA with the Valley Girls while his cousin picks up trash.', 'N2', NULL),
(595, 'Make sure you know your radicals!', 'Running really takes it out of you. In this case, it takes your soul out of you. Don''t worry, when you stop your soul will collide with you again and everything will be okay. (Probably.)', 'You <kanji>run</kanji> and run and run and suddenly you feel your <reading>sou</reading>l (そう) leave your body. Still running, you turn around and see your soul running a few steps behind you.', 'N4', NULL),
(596, 'Picture a scooter coming toward you in the dark. You see the gleam of the axe as it looms nearer and nearer.', 'The King is now so near to you his nose and mustache are tickling your face. He''s holding the axe high above his head and saying, "I can get as close to you as I want and you can''t stop me because I''m the King. I can get sooooo close. Look, I''m not touching you though!"', 'As they draw <kanji>near</kanji>, you recognize who is riding the scooter: the <reading>Kin</reading>g (きん) of England! Behind him, a bunch of his guards also on scooters and wielding axes appear and move <kanji>close</kanji> to you. ', 'N4', NULL),
(598, 'Imagine you''re starving. It''s cold and you''ve run out of food. You don''t know what you''re going to do. If you don''t find something, you''ll lose your life. Then, you break open a barrel, and wheat pours out. It''s beautiful. You just found some life in the middle of winter.', 'Imagine the disappointment. You go from finding life to losing it all... now you have no hope for the winter :( Also, keep in mind that むぎ and mug aren''t super-alike, so you''ll have to use it to get  close enough to remember the actual reading, むぎ.', 'You found your <kanji>wheat</kanji>. Thank God. You''ll survive. You have to take the wheat back to your house, though. On your way, you get <reading>mug</reading>ged (むぎ).', 'N2', NULL),
(599, 'Of course, this isn''t what you''d expect of a little viking child. It''s the opposite. That''s why it''s so surprising! He loves to study!', 'You should tell the other vikings to stop gawking at this child. Just because he likes to study doesn''t mean he''s a bad viking...', 'This crazy viking child who prefers <kanji>study</kanji> over pillaging (and other viking activities)... the other vikings just stop and <reading>gawk</reading> (がく) at him when they walk by.', 'N5', NULL),
(600, 'Follow the logic of this one and you should be okay.', 'Obviously that didn''t work well - you''ll want to focus on your battle cry plus indecent swearing right afterwards to remember this reading.', 'So, now you have this <kanji>forest</kanji> and you want to cut it down (so much money!). But, you don''t have any tools, only your hands. You think... I''ve done karate for a while, so I can probably chop it down that way. You get in a chopping stance, then do your battle cry and hit: "<reading>HAYA ... .SHI******</reading>!" (はやし)', 'N2', NULL),
(601, 'Next time you look up at the sky, try to imagine it''s a big empty hole you''re stuck at the bottom of.', 'First the sky''s cloudy, then it''s clear and empty, then overcast again... Just coup after coup!', 'The <kanji>sky</kanji> is usually empty until clouds stage a <reading>coup</reading> (くう). Basically you can think of the sky as the site for one coup after another as the weather changes.', 'N4', NULL),
(602, 'Just know your radicals and you''ll be golden, so to speak.', 'Picture the King on a huge pile of gold. Gold coins, gold candelabras, gold ponies. He''s laughing and pointing at all of it while making very strong eye contact with you.', 'The most <kanji>gold</kanji> in the land belongs to the <reading>Kin</reading>g (きん) of England. In fact, you''ve never actually met someone with gold, it all goes straight to the King whenever someone finds some. You just have a few pennies to work with.', 'N5', NULL),
(603, 'Make sure you know your radicals!', 'Imagine a world where each rain drop had to choose a target to aim for. How strange would rainfall be? Also, あめ and "aim" don''t really sound too much alike, but the goal is to use "aim" to get to あめ, so make sure to take note of that in your mind as you learn the reading.', 'What if the <kanji>rain</kanji> actually tried to <reading>aim</reading> (あめ) for you? As in, drops would move out of their normal flight path just to hit you on your head? You''d be a lot wetter, that''s for sure.', 'N5', NULL),
(604, 'Don''t feel blue, you should know this kanji automagically!', 'You aren''t sure how you or the saber turned blue, but at least you and your saber are a good match now, I guess.', 'You''ve been feeling really <kanji>blue</kanji>. Mostly because your skin, hair, and eyes have all turned blue. It was an out-of-the-blue transformation! But now someone places his hand on your shoulder and points at your <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい). It''s blue too! You aren''t alone!', 'N4', NULL),
(605, 'Think of how disappointed you are. You wanted a beautiful cross of flowers, and now you just have a big overgrown patch of thick grass. It isn''t even pretty green grass, it''s a little brown and damaged from the sun.', 'Imagine using your cool saw to saw off the heads of all of this grass. You made the cross pretty big, so there is a ton of grass to get through. The sun is beating down on you and the grass too, which isn''t making it any easier.', 'The <kanji>grass</kanji> on top is brown and burned from the sun, so you need to take a <reading>cool saw</reading> (くさ) and hack the tops off. The saw is actually physically cool to the touch, to help heal the grass'' sunburned bits. It just happens to look cool too.', 'N3', NULL),
(606, 'Imagine a super loud sizzling sound, drowning everything else out. It''s ridiculously loud, and hurts your ears more than the sun''s heat hurts your skin.', 'Your burning body sounds just like sizzling, crispy onions being fried in a pan. Listen close. It''s exactly the same! You can almost smell onions too! ', 'This <kanji>sound</kanji> isn''t just loud, it''s familiar. Finally you place it: <reading>on</reading>ions (おん)!', 'N4', NULL),
(622, 'All those late nights studying radicals really pay off, you know.', 'Lou is a total night owl, so you only hit him up at night.', 'You call your friend at <kanji>night</kanji> and say "<reading>Yo, Lou</reading> (よる)!"', 'N4', NULL),
(1309, 'Imagine walking outside at night and seeing a strange oinking animal. You think it''s a pig, but just to be sure you grab that thing and hold it up to the moonlight. Yes, definitely a pig! Now you know.', 'A pig from Bhutan?! Yes, all pigs are actually from Bhutan, it whispers. Then this Bhutan pig escapes your grip and trots off into the moonlight.', 'Legend has it that if you catch a <kanji>pig</kanji> in moonlight, it will tell you its secrets. Sure enough, this pig tells you it''s from <reading>Bhuta</reading>n (ぶた).', 'N1', NULL),
(8884, 'Imagine you''ve gone back in time a good hundred years, and you''re trying to count loaves of bread after each swing of your axe. Axes and bread loaves will become intertwined in your brain.', 'Imagine being stuck using an axe on bread while the King of England glares down at you, munching and spilling crumbs everywhere.', 'You imagined being back in time too good and now you''re stuck using an <kanji>axe</kanji> to be a <kanji>bread loaf counter</kanji> for the <reading>Kin</reading>g (きん) of England. You aren''t sure which king he is or when in time you are, but he''s mean and keeps eating all the bread you cut up with your axe.', 'N1', NULL),
(607, 'Imagine your leader-hero holding a spoon out, then doing something awesome with it to totally change everything for the better. It''s amazing the change this person can bring about with such a simple, everyday object!', 'Of course, doing something like this is utterly amazing. Feel amazed in your imagination, because if you don''t, then it''ll be hard to remember the reading for this kanji. If you are amazed, you''ll know that spoons going to cars = the reading is か, so make sure you take the time to be amazed.', 'Then, this hero of yours takes their spoon and says some magic words into it, <kanji>changing</kanji> it into a <reading>car</reading> (か).', 'N3', NULL),
(608, 'Imagine seeing the earth moving as a dirt alligator rises out from the ground. You should run.', 'If you ever see someone throwing cheese on the ground, now you know why. They aren''t crazy, they''re saving us humans from what''s lurking under the earth.', 'The only way to keep this dirt alligator from dragging you under the <kanji>earth</kanji> and eating you is to feed it <reading>chee</reading>se (ち). That way it won''t lead you to a horrific death under the earth by dragging you under and spinning you around until you eat dirt (literally).', 'N4', NULL),
(609, 'You''ll want to focus on the part where you find both, not so much the things themselves. Think about how you can''t believe you did so well finding both things at once. Usually you only find one or the other when you''re searching.', 'Also think... "What''s that row boat doing here?" But, it''s your only hope, so you have to focus on it really hard.', 'But, you dig too deep and water flows into where you are. Your only hope is a <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat floating off in the distance.', 'N3', NULL),
(610, 'Think of as many kings as you can think of. Guess what? Literally <em>all</em> of them wear hats! Think of strange hats, funny hats, normal hats, and crowns. Just make sure you''re amazed by the fact they''re <em>all</em> wearing them.', 'To add to this story and make it more memorable, you can imagine the other kings getting upset, saying things like "All kings must wear hats! You are no king!" The king of Zen Buddhism just ignores them and begins to meditate.', 'But, as you''re going through <kanji>all</kanji> the kings, it turns out not all of them have hats after all. The last one you come to is hatless. He''s the king of <reading>Zen</reading> (ぜん). He has simple clothes and a shaved head, unlike all the other fancy kings standing around nearby.', 'N3', NULL),
(611, 'Yonder is a bit of a weird word, but it fits the meaning of this kanji quite well. You''ll have to trust me that it''s more useful in Japanese than it is in English. When you imagine through this mnemonic, make sure the drop comes from the side and hits you in the mustache with quite a bit of force. That means it came from a far distance thus making you look over "yonder."', '"Who is that over yonder!" you yell.
"It is I, (こういち)," he yells back. "I shot at you over yonder because you weren''t doing your kanji reviews often enough and you''re taking up space on our database."
"But I''m paying you!"
"Oh, that''s true. Well, I''m going to walk over yonder and leave you alone, then."', 'Who shot this drop at you from <kanji>yonder</kanji>? You look over yonder, and see a figure. It is <reading>こう</reading>いち.', 'N3', NULL),
(612, 'Picture the super relaxed woman lounging under her cheap but sturdy roof. Ah, she doesn''t have a care in the world!', 'Think about it: anchovies cost next to nothing, they come in cans, you don''t have to cook them or cut them up. You just munch them down. There''s nothing more cheap and relaxing than eating anchovies under your own roof.', 'As she <kanji>relax</kanji>es, she pulls out the <kanji>cheap</kanji>est, most relaxing food in the world: <reading>an</reading>chovies (あん).', 'N4', NULL),
(613, 'Also, another way to think of this is that the river radical is actually just three rivers, separating drops, which are capitals of various states. Rivers are usually great ways to divide up a country, and states are one way to do it!', 'The state shoes also have things like the state birds, state flowers, and state fish printed on each of them. They''re quite collectable, you should buy a pair for your favorite state!', 'Each <kanji>state</kanji> has its own <reading>shoe</reading> (しゅう) to represent it. The bottom of each shoe is the shape of the state, so when you step it leaves a state-shaped imprint in the ground.', 'N2', NULL),
(614, 'Listen to the mouths. All twenty of them. They sound great. Have them sing your favorite music. Imagine the music coming at you, bending all around in strange shapes through the air.', 'Imagine how you feel as they cloak you with music. Feel the music all around you, enveloping you. Become the music itself.', 'The twenty mouths make so much <kanji>music</kanji> that they literally <reading>cloak you</reading> (きょく) with it. They cloak you with the music.', 'N3', NULL),
(615, 'You don''t have anything else. This is it. All you have. ', 'They came and took your things. Now they have all your stuff, and you only have the moon and a narwhal. You don''t have much anymore, but at least you have these things still.', 'Everything you <kanji>have</kanji> (except the narwhal and the moon) were taken from you by a roving gang of <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう).', 'N4', NULL),
(617, 'This spoon isn''t for pudding, it''s for death!', 'Picture the death sheep. He looks like a black shadow, and he has a scar on his face. He''s holding a bunch of spoons, from all the other people the yakuza marked for death!', '<kanji>Death</kanji> comes in the form of a <reading>shee</reading>p (し). The death sheep works for the yakuza, and kills anyone who has their spoon.', 'N4', NULL),
(618, 'You are no sheep—you study your radicals, unlike most Japanese learners. You''ll learn sooo much faster!', 'Imagine the taste of this yogurt. You''re scared and tired from running, but it tastes great!', 'To remember よう, we use the word "Yogurt." The <kanji>sheep</kanji> are after you. There are too many of them, and they''re chasing you down, stalking you... because you stole their sheep''s milk <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう). Now you''re running and crying and trying to eat the yogurt before the sheep catch up and stomp you down with their hooves.', 'N1', NULL),
(619, 'There''ll be some bad blood between us if you''re not studying your radicals, especially when they''re the same like this kanji!', 'Feel the relief that you didn''t just lick up someone''s blood. Taste the ketsup taste, too. It''s pretty good. Not quite ketchup, but not quite catsup. Yum!', 'To remember the reading けつ, we use the word "ketsup." The perfect mix of ketchup and catsup!

The radical is technically <radical>blood</radical>, but you know that radical is just a drop (of blood) on a plate. So, you ask yourself... Is that really blood on the plate? Could it be? You decide to take a gamble and lick it off. When you taste it, it''s definitely not blood. No, it''s <reading>ketsup</reading> (けつ).', 'N2', NULL),
(621, 'Imagine his talking taking physical form. As he talks, waves extend out from him, and stop right at the border of his country before getting to the next one.', 'Would you drink that much soda for breakfast? Now this king says he wants to pass a law requiring everyone in the country to drink nothing but Coke! That''s crazy!', 'Turns out the king of this <kanji>country</kanji> loves drinking <reading>Coke</reading> (こく). Every morning that big mouth of his swallows a whole 2-liter bottle of Coke for breakfast.', 'N5', NULL),
(623, 'Imagine a little girl (your younger sister) running around with her arms out, pretending to be a jet, too. Prwoooooo!', 'Ew, gross. Your younger sister is such a weirdo! Why does she have mice on her back while pretending to be a jet?', 'Your <kanji>younger sister</kanji> is giving some <reading>mi</reading>ce (まい) a ride on her back like a jet. ', 'N4', NULL),
(624, 'You want to go to the city someday, but you''re not old enough yet, like your older sister is.', 'Picture your older sister and her sheep walking through the city. Everyone turns to stare at them, because they''re the only ones of their kind.', 'Your <kanji>older sister</kanji> may have moved to the city, but she kept her pet <reading>shee</reading>p (し) with her. Now the only woman in the city is your older sister, and the only sheep is her pet.', 'N4', NULL),
(625, 'Picture the very first store in history. A fortune teller decided enough was enough, they should be in some kind of structure where it''s safe, official looking, and more permanent.', 'Picture ten shops, all made up of someone selling something under a canopy. The first one is the fortune teller, but the nine others are whatever you want them to be!', 'This <kanji>shop</kanji> quickly turned into <reading>ten</reading> (てん) shops! It was a good idea, after all, so all of the other people selling goods and services decided to copy it and create their own shops. The next day there were ten more.', 'N4', NULL),
(627, 'Remember the radical mnemonic? The sun rises behind that tree in the east!', 'Imagine the secondhand pain you''d feel watching him as he gashes his knee. Ow… Surely nothing in the east can be worth that much suffering?', 'You see a man walk straight toward the sun rising in the <kanji>east</kanji>. It''s so bright he can''t see anything, so he trips and <reading>he gash</reading>es (ひがし) his knee on a rock. Yikes… But he gets back up, determined to head east, and, oh no, he gashes his other knee! OK, he should probably give up on going east.', 'N5', NULL),
(628, 'Question is, what made you stop? Imagine yourself walking at a nice casual pace... then imagine something that would make you stop that''s incredibly interesting and strange. Maybe an alien is going down the slide? He would say "keep walking, friend."', 'Of course, imagine the pain of your face. Did your nose break? After bringing yourself together, look down at what hit you. It''s a hoe! How did you manage to step on just the right spot to flip it up on you like that? Examine the hoe. Pay close attention to it. Whose hoe is this? etc.', 'So, you''re <kanji>walk</kanji>ing around, minding your own business, when suddenly something flips up and whacks you in the face. You stepped on the head of a <reading>hoe</reading> (ほ) and it caused it to swing up from the ground and hit you.', 'N4', NULL),
(631, 'Think of all the important things you know that you were hoping to blab about: JFK''s real killer, the truth about Area 51, Koichi''s shocking connections to the Illuminati. You better hurry and get this arrow out of your mouth so you can tell the world what you know!', 'Take a closer look at that bow-slinging thug who fired the arrow into your mouth. Their shadowy silhouette looks awfully… woolly, doesn''t it? It''s undeniable: the sheep are trying to shut you up!', 'One of the things you <kanji>know</kanji> too much about is the <reading>shee</reading>p (し) plan for world domination! Yes, you know that sheep are in fact plotting to overthrow humanity and take over the world. ', 'N4', NULL),
(632, 'As long as you know the radical long, you know the kanji long as well.', '<kanji>Long</kanji> isn''t an idea that''s easy to imagine in any specific sense, but you can try some other things too, as long as it gets associated with Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading>. For the most part, when you see a kanji with the radical 長 the reading will be ちょう.', 'You see a very <kanji>long</kanji> person. It''s Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう), who has made herself long, like her street. "I made myself long so I can watch more of the street, my domain. I''m the <kanji>leader</kanji> of these streets. Watch yourself not-long person," says Mrs. Chou.', 'N5', NULL),
(633, 'Perhaps it''s some kind of magic, but you don''t care. Why are these strange objects always in front of me? Why won''t they stay put? This will get pretty freaky after a while. Try to run. Try to hide. Spin around. Do whatever you need to do. No matter what, these objects are always in front.', 'Go ahead and do some Zen Buddhist meditation. Maybe it''ll help you to remember this kanji''s reading better (and calm your mind so you''re ready for the next kanji!).', 'You''re going crazy with these things in <kanji>front</kanji> of you all the time. You finally seek the help of a <reading>Zen</reading> (ぜん) master, who tells you that meditation will save you from this curse. You try it out, and sure enough, the objects are gone! It worked!', 'N5', NULL),
(635, 'Imagine the room you live in now. Take away all the stuff and you''re left with a roof above the ground. Your own little private place atop the dirt.', 'Imagine watching the little shih tzu come into your room and lift its leg to all your things. This isn''t fair. The shih tzu has its own room to pee in. Why does it have to ruin yours?', 'The only problem with your <kanji>room</kanji> is that you can''t always be alone. Because of your dad''s <reading>shih tzu</reading> (しつ). The shih tzu likes to come into your room and pee everywhere. Ruining your privacy completely.', 'N4', NULL),
(636, 'Imagine trying to loiter while you poop in the winter. You''re just trying to take your time as you squat over the snow, but soon you hear things behind you.', 'Godzilla and こういち were going at it behind you the entire time you were just lazily taking a poop in the snow?! Ahh, that''s so embarrassing! And also dangerous, they could have crushed you! You should look behind you more.', 'You finally look <kanji>behind</kanji> you and see <reading>Go</reading>dzilla (ご) and a giant <reading>こう</reading>いち having a violent battle <kanji>back</kanji> there!', 'N5', NULL),
(637, 'Think, think, think. Think about this rice paddy heart.', 'If you don''t know Moe from the Simpsons use a different Moe, perhaps the one from the Three Stooges? You should be able to come up with one. Think really hard about the Moe you know best and imagine them standing watching you from the edge of the rice field.', 'As you are <kanji>think</kanji>ing these <kanji>thought</kanji>s, you spot someone on the edge of the rice field. He really reminds you of someone. But who? That''s it! He reminds you of that bartender on The Simpsons. What''s his name? You think some more. What is it? <reading>Oh! Moe</reading> (おも) is his name!', 'N4', NULL),
(638, 'Imagine all sorts of stars all around the universe giving life to planets as suns. These are all the stars you see in the sky and there are billions of them. I hope you don''t feel overwhelmed by how minuscule we really are when you look up at all of them.', 'Now you''re soaring amongst the stars, swinging your saber at all manner of cosmic foes, and... wait. No, that was a dream. You roll over and pat your saber. The two of you are ready just in case the stars need your help.', '"All these life-giving <kanji>star</kanji>s need a protector," you say, pulling out your <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい). It''s a big responsibility, but with your trusty saber in hand, you''re sure you can defend our star system.', 'N2', NULL),
(639, 'Your entire body wakes up, you feel so lively! The tongues are making you more aware of yourself and your surroundings than you''ve ever been. You want to dance, you want to sing, you want to live your life.', 'You and the cats are running around playing together, because you can''t seem to get the lively feelings to stop. You have to get the energy out, but the cats have sharp claws and they''re scratching you.', 'This <kanji>lively</kanji> feeling isn''t just happening to you, the tongue tsunami is licking it into all of the <reading>cats</reading> (かつ) in your neighborhood too!', 'N3', NULL),
(640, 'You''ll have to think of the "sea" as its own entity, controlling the tsunami, otherwise you might confuse and combine the two. So when you point your gun out the window, aim it at the "sea," not the "tsunami." It''s like aiming for the head of a zombie and not the arms or legs.', 'Imagine paddling in your kayak, your gun still in one hand, as you chase the sea back to its home.', 'The <kanji>sea</kanji> decides it doesn''t want to die today, so it turns its tsunami around and goes the other way. You hop in your <reading>kay</reading>ak (かい) and chase after it with your gun, to make sure it actually returns to the sea and doesn''t just drown someone else.', 'N4', NULL),
(641, 'Imagine actually going somewhere to get your fortune told and watching a big boiling pot of goo. As it boils, it melts down to a single point at the very bottom. Where this point is determines your future.', 'Is the ten spot good? Are you going to live a long life or does this point mean you''re only going to live ten more years?! Ahh!', 'As you stare at the <kanji>point</kanji>, you watch in awe as it moves to the <reading>ten</reading> (てん) spot in a long series of numbers.', 'N3', NULL),
(642, 'Imagine carefully ladling grains into different piles so you can do science experiments on them. This course is going to make you an expert. You''ll be the best in this department!', 'From your science meddling, these grains grow huge, red, and sprout sports cars when they''re ripe. This is the true power of science!', 'Using your <kanji>science</kanji> skills, the right temperatures and measurements, you manage to get these grains to grow into a <reading>ca</reading>r (か). This is the science <kanji>department</kanji>''s special car now!', 'N3', NULL),
(643, 'Imagine wrestling with an angel for its flowery hat, then running home with it. Success! Oh yes, this tea is going to taste… <em>heavenly</em>. *rimshot*', 'For some reason knowing the angel''s name makes you feel extra guilty. Chad the angel, flowerless and hatless... Oh well. At least its name lives on in this heavenly tea.', 'After brewing the <kanji>tea</kanji>, you look at the hat again and find a nametag inside that says <reading>Cha</reading>d (ちゃ). You decide that from now on this tea variety will be known as Chad tea, in honor of Chad.', 'N4', NULL),
(644, 'If you know the radical, you basically know the meaning(s) of this kanji. There is a second meaning added to it, though, and that is <kanji>meal</kanji>. Just think about the things you eat? What are they? In the simplest form, they are just meals. So, if you know "eat" you should be able to remember "meal" as well.', 'So, do you keep eating and keep getting shocked? Or do you not eat and continue to starve? What is worse? Obviously you''re in one of the Saw movies, or something, too, so feel the fear. What will you choose? Life with shocks or death from hunger?', 'Thank goodness, you finally get to <kanji>eat</kanji> your <kanji>meal</kanji>. You''re so hungry. Someone has been starving you and now they''ve finally given you food. Unfortunately, though, every time you take a bite, you get a <reading>shock</reading> (しょく) from somewhere.', 'N5', NULL),
(645, NULL, 'Look at yourself in a mirror as the cool beans make their way down your neck. You can see them, like little ants marching all the way down to your tummy.', 'The only thing you do with your <kanji>neck</kanji> is use it to swallow lots of <reading>coo</reading>l <reading>bea</reading>ns (くび). Mm mm. Feel those cool, cool beans sliding down your neck. You don''t really taste them, but you know they''re there.', 'N3', NULL),
(660, 'I guess not all houses are homes, and not all homes are houses. But there''s a pretty big overlap, right? And in most situations, this kanji''s got you covered. ', 'Imagine all the eggs you''ve got in your house. Whole fridgefuls! It''s not a normal house, but that''s okay. If you couldn''t eat eggs, it wouldn''t feel like home.', 'What do you do in your <kanji>house</kanji>? You <reading>ea</reading>t <reading>e</reading>ggs (いえ), that''s what. You eat eggs three times a day, every day. You really love eating eggs in your <kanji>home</kanji>!', 'N4', NULL),
(851, 'This thing is deceased. It ain''t alive anymore.', 'You didn''t want to know what would cause your death, you say? Well that''s a shame. I guess you could try to avoid bowling from now on, but that won''t do any good. Mark my words, your death will be bowling-related.', 'Your <kanji>death</kanji> will be because of a <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう) accident. Oh, you didn''t know? You''re going to be hit by a giant bowling ball and crushed flat.', 'N3', NULL),
(923, 'Look at your private grains. Who are those for? Why, they''re for I!', 'The moral of the story is: don''t let a sheep sneak into your private stash of things just for you (I). I''m sure this is a metaphor for something important...', 'You''re getting into the zone, thinking about yourself (<kanji>I</kanji>) and how much you love having things that are <kanji>private</kanji> and just yours, when you realize there is a <reading>shee</reading>p (し) in this grain bag. And it''s eating all of your (I''s) private grains. No!', 'N4', NULL),
(966, 'Feel the rush of stealing something (even though it''s a weird something, admittedly). Start walking away quickly. You don''t want to get caught!', 'When someone asks you "Do you like to take little toes?" your answer is always "Toetally."', 'You''re the kind of person who likes <vocabulary>to take</vocabulary> things. "What things?" you ask. Little <reading>toe</reading>s (と). Because that isn''t creepy.', 'N3', NULL),
(629, 'What are the images of, anyway? Well, it''s better not to say out loud. Just think of the kind of image you enjoy looking at most. That''s what''s in the box at the end of the street. Aren''t you the lucky one?', 'Try to see the images of the gargoyles. Winged, stony monsters. Very cool. And the roosters? Colorful little dinosaurs. What''s not to like? ', 'Okay, I didn''t want to tell you this, but the <kanji>image</kanji>s are all <kanji>drawing</kanji>s and <kanji>painting</kanji>s of <reading>ga</reading>rgoyles (が) and <reading>cock</reading>s (かく). Male chickens, that is. Turns out that''s what you wanted to see most. Don''t worry, no one is judging you.', 'N4', NULL),
(634, 'If you keep up with your radicals, your kanji studies will never go south. Haha! <em>Go south!</em> Get it? I said g—', 'Picture Mean Amy down in the south, saying mean things in her American southern accent: "Y''all <em>don''t</em> come back now, ya hear?" Man, Mean Amy is so mean. And southern.', 'Think about the <kanji>south</kanji>. What''s down there? <reading>Mean Amy</reading> (みなみ) lives down there. Think of the last mean thing she did to you and imagine it while yelling out "MEAN AMYYYYY" at the sky.', 'N5', NULL),
(646, 'If you know the radical you know this.', 'Imagine having to use regular, ordinary ketchup, or just old, boring catsup on your burger. Or even worse, an actual tomato. BLEH!', 'To remember けつ, we use the word "ketsup." It''s the perfect blend of ketchup and catsup!

You''re <kanji>lack</kanji>ing something in your fridge. You look around, trying to find the <reading>ketsu</reading>p (けつ) you lack. You can''t find it, because you lack it.', 'N3', NULL),
(647, 'Think about your own surname in your language. Where did it come from? Were you a clan of village dwellers? Maybe you made shoes! You should look it up and find out!', 'Imagine your ancestors, herding sheep. Thanks to them your name means "sheep people." Thanks, great-great-great-great-great-grandpappy.', 'Don''t worry, I''ll tell you where your <kanji>family name</kanji> came from. Your family used to herd <reading>shee</reading>p (し). You read that right, your family was of the sheep clan because you took care of sheep all day.', 'N1', NULL),
(648, 'Because it''s a cross with a mouth, it thinks that it is reason in all the chaos.', '"Those youths just do things for no reason! That''s why I, the Cross of Reason, am here! I will knock some reason into those youths."', 'The <kanji>reason</kanji> that this Cross of Reason exists is to combat the <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう) of the world, who just bring chaos into everything.', 'N3', NULL),
(649, 'The sick part is that all those bills were once this tree''s friends. Picture the tree sobbing uncontrollably, as pieces of its former friends fall everywhere around it. No wonder it needs that umbrella.', 'Try to remember this little girl named Satsuki, since she''ll come up whenever the reading for a kanji is さつ (there''s a good handful of them). Picture her in your mind, with details like her hair and clothes. Maybe even holding an umbrella or standing by a tree? You should also know she''s kind of a bookworm, I guess, in terms of personality. If you can imagine her clearly now, it''ll be easy to remember さつ kanji later on.  ', 'And the person who gave the tree an umbrella to protect it from all those <kanji>bills</kanji>? <reading>Satsu</reading>ki (さつ), a little girl famous for… handing out umbrellas, I guess. Makes sense that Satsuki would take care of these bills then.', 'N2', NULL),
(650, 'The mouth clan are distinct peoples. They do, of course, use their mouths.', 'That''s why they''re the mouth clan. This nation of people are known for mouthing off and saying mean things. Imagine them, as a nation, saying mean things to you. How awful! All being from the same place, they''re all into it too. In a way, they''re not the mouth clan, they''re the mean clan.', 'The mouth clan <kanji>peoples</kanji> are a <kanji>nation</kanji> of <kanji>people</kanji> who use their mouths to say <reading>mean</reading> (みん) things to other peoples.', 'N3', NULL),
(651, 'Imagine riding your scooter around in a really large area, pressing your sword into the ground as you do. Once you''ve finished carving out the sides of the entire area, you claim to everyone around that this is YOUR area, and they aren''t allowed in.', 'Imagine doing everything you can to get this hen out of your area, but it manages to dodge and outwit you at every turn. You even slash at it with your sword and try to run it over with your scooter, but the hen can''t be stopped. This is the hen''s area now. You have been defeated.', 'As you look down at your <kanji>area</kanji> with its sword-carved <kanji>side</kanji>s, you suddenly realize there is a <reading>hen</reading> (へん) pecking at the ground in there. "Um, excuse me, hen, I believe I said this is MY area?!" You try to kick it out, but it hops over your leg.', 'N2', NULL),
(652, 'Imagine guiding your leader''s dainty feet into the heeled shoes, then attaching the weird head replica. Now picture yourself trying to keep a straight face as you tell them they totally look tall enough. ', 'Just focus on how attaching requires two things to remember this reading.', 'You have to remember the kun''yomi reading of this kanji, and the part you need to remember is つ. So, think of how you would <kanji>attach</kanji> something to something else. What would you need? You''d need <reading>two</reading> (つ) things to put together!', 'N3', NULL),
(653, 'Picture the person, the lion is leaning over them, the drop of spit dangling from their mouth. "I-I want to go back where I came from," the person gasps, shaking.', 'The eagle came from the sky and technically rescued him, but compared to where he was before, it may not be a better situation. I think this person is going to be eaten either way.', '<kanji>From</kanji> above there is a loud caw, and a giant <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) swoops down, forcing the lion to back off. The eagle grabs the person with its talons, bringing him into the air! But now he''s... in the air being carried by a giant eagle, <kanji>compared with</kanji> where he was before, is this really better?!', 'N4', NULL),
(654, 'Imagine looking out the window of your house and watching your husband slide down his slide over and over. He''s not even good at it, he falls off the side halfway down most of the time. You want to complain, but he''s pretty decent otherwise. This is his only fault.', 'You run outside and yell, "AHA! It''s your fault that shih tzu died! You and that stupid slide!"', 'You try to come up with another <kanji>fault</kanji> of his so you can complain, when you see a tiny <reading>shih tzu</reading> (しつ) trotting by. Oh no! Before you can call out, your husband has fallen off the slide and crushed the poor thing. You hear a sharp yelp and then nothing.', 'N3', NULL),
(655, 'Implanting a slide into your heart is certain to improve your cardiovascular health.', 'Feel the impact as the doctor hits you again and again. You''re not certain that this is safe, but you do feel certain you''ve got a terrible doctor.', 'To be <kanji>certain</kanji> that the heart-slide is working, the doctor who put it there <reading>hits</reading> (ひつ) you in the chest a few times as a test.', 'N3', NULL),
(656, 'Feel the irritation of this. How irritating is this kid going to be the whole flight over? The jet is going as fast as it can!', 'Go ahead and pretend to listen to the kid asking if we''re there yet. Then in your mind go "me me me me me me me" quietly.', 'What a selfish kid, right? Well, all he thinks about is "<reading>Me</reading> (み) me me me me me me me me me me." Hate kids like that.', 'N3', NULL),
(657, 'Imagine the end of the earth being like how they imagined it pre-Columbus era. Just a big drop going off into space, giant waterfalls plummeting down. Your crazy jet has made it to the very end! Congratulations!', 'It''s surprisingly dirty down here, because all the water takes the garbage this way. That''s why you sit on these mats you brought.', 'When you get to the <kanji>end</kanji> of the world, you land your jet and put out a few <reading>mats</reading> (まつ) to sit on so you can view the sights. ', 'N3', NULL),
(658, 'He goes on to explain that he changed your school to here because there is a tree to block the satellite''s view. Also, he wears a tin foil lid on his head so they can''t track him. The regular school was no good because "they were watching." So now you have this new school, taught under a tree by your father who''s wearing a lid on his head.', 'On your first day of school, imagine walking under the tree and seeing all your classmates. They''re all こういち–similar to each other, but with some variation. Your father explains these こういちs escaped from a government research facility, and they come to this school to receive a better education.  "These poor こういちs need a school that will teach them more than just kanji and Japanese vocabulary."', 'Your new <kanji>school</kanji> has other students too. They are all <reading>こう</reading>いちs, though.', 'N5', NULL),
(659, 'Just think of the eye leaf as weird (it is weird, after all). It grows in the winter. Now, think of any normal leaves. They do the exact opposite, which is to grow in the summer.', 'Think about summer. Now think about yourself tying knots. Now think about summer again. Now think about tying knots again. Now think about your mother getting upset at you because all you do is tie knots all summer long. Go play video games like a normal kid!', 'Now it''s summertime! What do you do during the <kanji>summer</kanji>? You just sit around with your favorite string tying <reading>knots</reading> (なつ) in it over and over again.', 'N4', NULL),
(661, 'Just look at these weak, iced-over bows. If you tried to draw back their strings they''d snap into a million pieces.', 'Now you''re stuck with two weak broken bows and no jack. Ice is the worst.', 'You take the bows out to your car, which is stuck in a bunch of ice and snow, and try to use them as a kind of <kanji>weak</kanji> <reading>jack</reading> (じゃく). You know, to get your car unstuck. Unfortunately, your bows are too weak and brittle to use as jacks, and they just snap in half.', 'N2', NULL),
(662, 'Imagine being in a temple and realizing you don''t know what time it is. Never fear! Look on up at the sun. Ah, yes. Blinding noon.', 'Imagine him going to all kinds of different times and also make sure you''re specifically imagining Jesus doing all these things. Put these two together and you''ll remember the reading in no time. (Haha, get it?)', '<kanji>Time</kanji> in a temple makes you think of <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ). Specifically, Jesus being able to perform time travel magic. He winks at you, goes back to the dinosaur ages, where he wrestles a T-rex. Then, he goes to WWII era and punches Hitler in the mouth.', 'N5', NULL),
(663, 'The paper itself was also made by your clan using thread woven really tightly together.', 'Imagine a big guard coming up to you and asking to see your papers so they can see what clan you belong to. Well you''re from the Commi clan, so you really hope this guard is okay with that.', 'You need this specific <kanji>paper</kanji> to prove that you aren''t a <reading>Commi</reading> (かみ), aka a Communist. Or that you are. It really depends on where you want to go and what decade this is.', 'N4', NULL),
(664, 'Just imagine writing in a diary and saying everything to write aloud to yourself in your head. 

"Dear me, today I learned a lot of new kanji on WaniKani. It was the best. I love the crabigator sooooo much. Love, me."', 'Picture the key you use to keep what you write down a secret. What does it look like? How does it feel in your hands?', 'You need to protect what you <kanji>write down</kanji> and <kanji>record</kanji> for yourself with a <reading>key</reading> (き). These are your special thoughts and feelings, you don''t want anyone else to see! Lock them away with a key!', 'N3', NULL),
(665, 'Imagine your mama passing through your house on a scooter, not stopping because she needs to get her task done urgently. ', 'Why do you have to do the task too? Because there are two tasks, so the two of you have to do them.', 'As you watch your mama <kanji>pass through</kanji> the house, she yells, "YOU HAVE TO DO THIS TASK <reading>TOO</reading> (つう)!" Too?! Oh no, now you have to do it too. Hurry up!', 'N4', NULL),
(666, 'Imagine yourself saying how tall and expensive you are to everyone in the room. Then they use a lid to cover your mouth so you can''t talk to anyone anymore. But, then your mustache starts talking, saying the same thing. That''s why this lid covers both your mustache and your mouth. Now you can''t say that you are tall or expensive.', 'The こういちs finally get tired of your shenanigans, and lid up your mouth and mustache so you can''t say that you''re tall or expensive. ', '"I am <kanji>tall</kanji>er and more <kanji>expensive</kanji> than you." 

You''re saying this because there are some <reading>こう</reading>いちs in the room, and you want to make them feel inferior (you''re a こういち hater). You keep comparing your height to the こういちs in the room, and you keep checking price tags. Then you tell them you''re taller and more expensive than they are.', 'N5', NULL),
(667, 'Imagine holding this bow. Feel how strong it is as you draw the string. Now look closely at the insect bodies it''s made of. Thanks for your help, little guys.', 'Imagine all the people who live in きょうと, using their strong arms to make strong bows out of strong insect carapaces. Could きょうと be Japan''s strongest city?', 'You had this <kanji>strong</kanji> bow made especially for you in <reading>きょう</reading>と. きょうと is famous for their strong bows, so if you ever want another, you know where to go.', 'N4', NULL),
(668, 'Picture a teacher excitedly teaching, bundled up in the cold. Snow falling outside. Grumpy children being taught inside.', 'Imagine the teaching teacher teaching you about Japan in きょうと in winter. You''ve always wanted to go to きょうと!', 'Teachers can teach the most about Japan in winter by going to <reading>きょう</reading>と. きょうと is where all the old temples and history of Japan is centered. And it''s really gorgeous when it snows.', 'N4', NULL),
(669, 'If a king has no one to rule then he has no reason to exist.', 'Just like you can''t have a king without a village, you can''t have reason without re.', 'You can''t have <kanji>reason</kanji> without <reading>re</reading> (り).', 'N4', NULL),
(670, 'Picture a group of people threaded together by their top hats. Don''t you want to be part of a cool group like that? Your own top hat team.', 'What do you do in the cumin group? You smell the cumin. Cook with it. Taste it. This group lives and breathes cumin!', 'Everyone in this <kanji>group</kanji> loves <reading>cumi</reading>n (くみ). You know, the spice or seed. The group take off their top hats and fill them with cumin. You''re officially a group for lovers of cumin.', 'N3', NULL),
(671, 'Picture this boat. It''s still a boat, but there is a big mouth on the front and it has fins that it paddles forward with.', 'Picture this mouth and boat with a bunch of centaurs riding in it. Centaurs have a lot more parts than humans, so it makes sense that their boats have more parts (animal ones) too.', 'This <kanji>boat</kanji> belongs to the <reading>cen</reading>taurs (せん). That''s probably why you''ve never seen a boat like it before.', 'N3', NULL),
(672, 'A week in earth time is also known as a "full scoot" in scooter lingo. ;)', 'You can''t ride a scooter for a week barefoot, are you nuts?! Get some shoes!', 'If you want to scoot for a <kanji>week</kanji>, you''ll need <reading>shoe</reading>s (しゅう). Good shoes too, not sandals or high heels or boots. Solid, high quality shoes, so you don''t fall off the scooter and hurt yourself.', 'N4', NULL),
(673, 'Imagine Wolverine in the pouring rain, creating a little halo of snow around him wherever he goes. It''s strangely beautiful. That is one cold, traumatized dude…', 'Sheesh, emotionally distant people are complicated. The more you keep trying to help them, the more they reject you. Wolverine could do with a nice holiday to melt all that snow away.', 'You''re concerned for Wolverine''s mental health. Him being so emotionally cold that he turns rain to <kanji>snow</kanji> means <reading>you kee</reading>p (ゆき) asking if he''s okay. But that only makes him want to be more distant. ', 'N3', NULL),
(674, 'Nothing fishy about that, right?', 'Have him continue to scream "GO GYO GYO GYO!" as you try to get your engine started and attempt to flee your impending doom. The fish helps too, because he''s pretty worried as well.', 'You catch a <kanji>fish</kanji>. As you pull it out of the water, it starts yelling and pointing: "<reading>GO</reading>!" (ぎょ). "GO GYO GYO GYO!" There''s a huge cruise ship coming right towards your little fishing boat. No wonder the fish was yelling GO at you.', 'N4', NULL),
(675, 'As long as you know the radical bird you know the kanji bird.', 'After bursting out of this bird, she turns her head sharply in your direction. "I don''t just own the streets, I own the birds too. I''m inside every bird, and I, Mrs. Chou, can burst out at any time."', 'Think of a run-of-the-mill <kanji>bird</kanji>, whatever comes to mind for you first is fine. Then, your lovely bird swells. The shape of a face, then a hand, start stretching the bird apart until it explodes outward. Covered in blood and feathers is Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう).', 'N4', NULL),
(676, '', 'The walls are coming in closer, too. Make sure you feel a bit of peril as you look for this key. The walls have a slight opening at the bottom too, so if you don''t find it before the wall goes over it, the key will be lost forever, and you''ll be smushed!', 'You''re in a room, surrounded completely by <kanji>yellow</kanji>. You can''t get out. Somewhere in the room is a yellow <reading>key</reading> (き) but you can''t find it, because everything looks the same. You crawl around trying to find this tiny thing so that you can escape.', 'N2', NULL),
(677, '', 'You need to drink all this Coke, otherwise you''re going to drown. Drink this Coke and keep tasting its cold sweetness as you drink it all to survive.', 'You''re in a completely <kanji>black</kanji> room. There''s no way out, and you can''t see. The room starts to fill with liquid. It gets in your mouth. You taste it. It''s <reading>Coke</reading> (こく)! Mm, tasty Coke.', 'N4', NULL),
(853, 'Picture a table tilted on its side with some of its legs longer than others, a perfect slide for insects! Imagine all the different insects jumping into the wind each in their own particular style.', 'The insects ate all the food that was left on the table, and now they can''t just fly away into the wind like they normally do. Instead, they have to try this new style by using the table as a slide to help them take off into the wind.', 'You wonder what''s pushing these insects to jump into the <kanji>wind</kanji> in such a manner, and you realize that it''s because they''ve had way too much <reading>foo</reading>d (ふう).', 'N4', NULL),
(893, 'Look around you, everything you see is reflecting the light of the sun. Unless it''s night time, then the tiny sun in the center of your lightbulb is creating the light that reflects there.', 'Picture the light of the sun reflecting off a single acorn on the forest floor. So much symbolism!', 'This <kanji>reflect</kanji>ed light is important for <reading>a</reading>corns (えい), in particular. Without light reflecting off everything, the sun''s light would never make it down to the ground where they''ve fallen. They would never grow into trees. And we''d all suffocate.', 'N4', NULL),
(1237, 'Picture the first Japanese person to see an elephant. He stops dead in his tracks, points and screams out, "What a phenomenon!"', 'Picture the shougun standing over a pit of zombie elephants. None of these are getting out on his watch, letting people believe in such crazy phenomenons.', '<kanji>Elephant</kanji>s are great and all, except that when they die they turn into elephant <reading>zo</reading>mbies (ぞう). But you never see these zombies because the <reading>shou</reading>gun (しょう) has a special project in place that destroys all elephant bodies when they die.

Hint: The readings and meanings for this kanji match up really nicely. ぞう is always for elephant and しょう is always for phenomenon. Remember it like this, the elephant is the zombie and the shougun keeps everyone from finding out about this phenomenon.', 'N2', NULL),
(1810, 'You lay it down, cut it into slats, and voila! A floor! And a bed!', 'You can imagine how comfortable it is to sleep on a floor of yucca leaves, but at the same time how horrible the smell must be when they start to rot.', 'It''s great having a <kanji>floor</kanji> or <kanji>bed</kanji>, but whenever you use it you say <reading>yucka</reading> (ゆか) because it just gets so dirty. That''s the one flaw of using a floor as a bed. In fact, it''s the dirtiest floor you''ve ever seen, because it''s covered in <reading>yucca</reading> (ゆか) leaves. You''re sleeping on a yucca floor. Ew!', 'N2', NULL),
(573, 'Imagine the alligator riding the tsunami in. When he hits the ground, and the water recedes, he''s all alone in a pond, but it doesn''t provide much for him. Imagine him looking for food in the little, shallow pond. Imagine him trying to stay wet... it''s just not a great situation to be in, unfortunately.', 'Picture an alligator crying in a pond full of cheese. It''s eating a piece really slowly and weeping.', 'The worst part about this <kanji>pond</kanji> is that the only thing for the alligator to eat is <reading>chee</reading>se (ち). Cheese may be yummy for humans, but alligators hate it. They''d rather eat the cow the cheese came from. Sadly, this pond is full of cheese.', 'N2', NULL),
(616, 'Feel a little frustrated you can''t find ice. Have the words "<kanji>next</kanji> next next" echoing through your head as you go from store to store, irritated.', 'Imagine him coming down. Imagine your surprise. He hands you the ice, and it''s cold. You thank him, and he flies away.', 'To remember the reading じ, we used the word "Jesus" - Finally, when you''re at the last store in existence, you decide it''s time to pray to <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ), asking for a miracle. He comes down from the heavens with ice in his hands.', 'N3', NULL),
(620, 'Know your radicals!', 'When you think of the capital, any capital, think of きょうと.', 'To remember the reading きょう we use the old <kanji>capital</kanji> of Japan, Kyoto, which is spelled <reading>きょう</reading>と. It was the capital of Japan before Tokyo!', 'N4', NULL),
(678, 'Picture a branch supporting the weight of you, your entire family, and maybe a few friends.', 'The big sheep walks up the side of the tree with its weird hooves and starts to put its weight on the branch. "No no no!" you and your whole family scream. The branch can''t support the weight of the sheep. It snaps! You all tumble to the ground.

The end.', 'You''re happily sitting on this <kanji>branch</kanji>, letting it <kanji>support</kanji> all this weight, when a <reading>shee</reading>p (し) starts to climb the tree. A very, very big sheep. Uh oh... no no, sheep. If you get on this branch it won''t be able to support all this weight!!!', 'N3', NULL),
(679, 'Picture the leader master who dwells in a big, beautiful dwelling. Everyone else is jealous.', 'Imagine opening the door to the leader master''s dwelling and thousands of jewels come pouring out, whisking you off, down the mountain.', 'She needs this big place to <kanji>dwell</kanji> so she has somewhere to store all of her <reading>jew</reading>els (じゅう). Masters always have lots of fancy, expensive things, and she has jewels.', 'N4', NULL),
(680, 'Imagine putting on your top hat and getting power from it. Then, you use that power to help someone. It''s important to make the association that it''s the top hat giving you the power to help that person. You''re basically Frosty the Snow Man.', 'You can imagine saving her however you like. Perhaps she was under a car, and you used your top hat powers to help her out of that situation by lifting it above your head.

Note: Jo-Anne is the character we''re using with じょ reading mnemonics. She is short compared to Joe (じょう), the giant farmhand, which is how you''ll differentiate the two similar readings.', 'The person you <kanji>help</kanji> with your top hat power? It''s <reading>Jo</reading>-Anne (じょ).', 'N3', NULL),
(681, 'More specifically, antibiotics. Your wound is festering pretty badly.', 'Or, so you think. He just keeps circling and circling. Feel some hate for the eagle. Why isn''t he dropping the medicine? You don''t have long to live!', 'When we need to remember the sound い we use the word "Eagle."

You need your <kanji>medicine</kanji> really bad. Then, you look up. There''s an <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) flying overhead. He has a package. It''s medicine! You''re saved!', 'N4', NULL),
(682, 'Imagine having all the local rulers as your buddies, all the influence you would have. You might end up with everyone wanting to be your buddy.', 'Oh no! This coonhound mistook Wolverine for a giant racoon. (His mask kinda makes him look like one.) You better go help your buddy now. Be mindful of the coonhound though, they''re good hunters I hear.', 'As you''re about to embrace your <kanji>buddy</kanji> Wolverine, a <reading>coon</reading>hound (くん) tackles him off the slide, and bites him in the process.', 'N3', NULL),
(683, 'What''s that, you say? It doesn''t always have to be writing types versus measurement types? We can work together to create a better society? Nah, sorry, I''d rather it be me versus the world. ', 'Think of someone you know who''s your total opposite. Now imagine you''re both in ties, but opposite colors, and you''re debating… or fighting in a cage match or something. ', 'Whenever it''s you <kanji>versus</kanji> someone else, you wear a <reading>tie</reading> (たい). Naturally, your tie is always the <kanji>opposite</kanji> color of your opponent''s tie. Kinda like how political rivals wear different color ties when they debate. It''s pretty silly, but at least your tie makes it clear that it''s you versus them.', 'N3', NULL),
(684, 'The cape covering the mouth represents how this bureau never talks about their secrets. The flag represents your country. Think about all the cool secret things the Bureau of Secret Things must have.', 'Now, this mnemonic isn’t perfectly close, but the goal is to get you close enough in your brain so that you can remember きょく later. If you can get to CLOAK YOU, then you can get to きょく much easier than just trying to get there from scratch. So imagine working in this bureau and getting to cloak yourself all the time.', 'The <kanji>bureau</kanji>''s best-kept secret is cloaking devices that <reading>cloak you</reading> (きょく) with the press of a button.', 'N3', NULL),
(685, 'Imagine standing around with a weapon in your hands. It feels more like you''re doing nothing, loitering around the wall, but this is how you offer your service to your country, so you have to do it.', 'Somehow your duty has become pushing this stubborn, hairy yak away from the entrance to your country. You poke it in the butt with your weapon, but it doesn''t seem to care.', 'You''re about halfway through your <kanji>service</kanji>, when a <reading>yak</reading> (やく) walks by. It stops directly in front of you and grunts. "Excuse me, Mr. Yak, but it''s my <kanji>duty</kanji> to escort you away from my country."', 'N3', NULL),
(686, 'Imagine a weapon in between your fingers. It doesn''t matter what kind. Now feel the weight as you throw it.', 'Imagine throwing the weapon in your fingers just before you walk into とうきょう. You can see the lights, smell the smells! Throw your weapons and your cares away!', 'You <kanji>throw</kanji> your weapon away because you''re about to enter <reading>とう</reading>きょう and there are no weapons allowed there. There is, however, a big pile of thrown away weapons by the border, which is where you''re throwing yours right now.', 'N3', NULL),
(687, 'Have trouble with the decision. Which one? Tsunami will be big and cool, but a hook will be more discreet. How can you decide? Can you think of a neat way to decide this? You''re like Two-Face deciding on how to kill Batman.', 'Get everything ready to take your target down. How will you collect enough ketsup to make a ketsup tsunami? Where will you unleash it? You gotta decide on the ketsup logistics now.', 'To remember the けつ reading, we always use the word "ketsup." It''s a special mix of ketchup and catsup (yum):

You finally <kanji>decide</kanji>. You''re going to go with the tsunami... but not just any tsunami. You''ll kill him with a tsunami of <reading>ketsu</reading>p (けつ).', 'N3', NULL),
(688, 'Imagine carefully digging a hole nine times, in nine different spots, so you can do your research. Hmm, I wonder what kind of research these holes are for…', 'Put nine full-grown cucumbers into their holes so you can research what happens next.', 'Your <kanji>research</kanji> is on <reading>cu</reading>cumbers (きゅう)!', 'N4', NULL),
(689, 'Imagine hurtling down a slide, your inner self reveling in the joy of the moment. Then, you hit a sharp barb — OUCH!!! Suddenly, you''re very conscious of your body again. ', 'Feel the sharp pain in your shin as it radiates through your body. It kinda sucks, but in a way it''s reassuring proof of the physical existence of oneself.', 'What part of your <kanji>body</kanji> did the barb get stuck in? Your <reading>shin</reading> (しん). That pointy little barb dug deep into your shin and made you painfully aware of the fact that <kanji>oneself</kanji> and one''s body are inextricable.', 'N3', NULL),
(690, 'Well, well… someone''s lucky to not have to learn a new kanji.', 'Think back on someone you met recently whose name or face you can''t really remember. Hate to break it to you, but… they were a shaman.', 'If you don''t know who <kanji>someone</kanji> is, odds are they''re a <reading>sha</reading>man (しゃ). Only a shaman would have the mystical powers to obscure their own identity and pass themselves off as a mysterious, unknown "someone."', 'N4', NULL),
(691, 'Imagine walking around at night and you need to sharpen your sword. You walk up to a stone lantern and rub your sword all over it to sharpen it. Touch the blade. Indeed, it''s been sharpened!', 'You''ll want to remember this character, because he will come up many times throughout your WaniKani journey. Ken the samurai used to work for WaniKani, but then he went rogue because he thought WaniKani wasn''t being radical enough. So now he wanders the website carrying his squid friend (who you''ll meet later) in a bucket.

To help you visualize him, imagine a samurai with armor made from crab shells, a helmet that is just an alligator head, and swords with crab claw hand guards. His great beard and mustache are styled in the shape of whatever kanji is giving you the most trouble at the time.', 'Why did you <kanji>sharpen</kanji> your blade? Because you are about to do battle with <reading>Ken</reading> (けん), the famous samurai warrior.', 'N4', NULL),
(692, '', 'Feel the shock of this. It sounds just like a sheep, it''s strange.', 'You are looking at a horse. It''s looking at you. You try making horse noises at it, to get a reaction. Instead of reacting with a normal horse noise, it just makes sheep noises: "<reading>Baaa</reading>," (ば) it says. "Baaaaa."', 'N3', NULL),
(693, 'Makes sense, because a bunch of individual trees is what makes up a forest!', 'Keep repeating this pattern over and over, continually focusing in on how you need "more" trees to make this forest. For some reason the person working with you only goes up by one at a time, though, so you have to say it a lot (it''ll help you to remember, anyways!).', 'One tree? No. Two trees? Not enough. I want <reading>more</reading> (もり) trees, please!', 'N2', NULL),
(694, 'Imagine looking at the dirt. It''s a distinct color and texture, and you''re able to identify your location just from that alone. Now draw that line on the ground and you''ll always remember this place.', 'Remember Joe? He''s our local farmhand who only has nine toes (from the 上 mnemonic). Imagine that guy explaining to you how to know your location from dirt. He may be a bit clumsy, but the man really knows his dirt.', 'Knowing your <kanji>location</kanji> from dirt is quite a feat! Who taught you that? It was <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう), the big local farmhand. Despite being a bit weird, he can identify any <kanji>place</kanji> just from its dirt!', 'N4', NULL),
(695, 'Imagine a sun attached up in the sky with two crosses, which act as scaffolding, and the moon gradually fading off to the right as the morning wears on.', 'You watch the sun scaffolded by crosses, and the moon next to it, as you sip on your early-morning Asahi.', 'Every <kanji>morning</kanji> you have <reading>Asa</reading>hi (あさ) beer for breakfast.', 'N4', NULL),
(696, 'Go through in your mind the system your farm / village uses for deciding who has to sickle on any given day. It rotates, and it''s a turn-based system. So, do you all stand in a line? Is there a calendar? Come up with something interesting, focusing on your turn in the process you come up with. 

 It will be helpful to remember both the "number in a series" and "turn" meanings for the vocab coming up in the future.', 'Try to feel disappointed by this. Strange thing to feel disappointed by, but you were a great sickler. You were going to win the great prize, too (come up with something huge!), but now you''re banned for the season.', 'This <kanji>turn</kanji>-based rice sickle system is a contest as well! But, you are disappointed to find out that you have been <reading>ban</reading>ned (ばん) from the game because of the performance enhancing steroids you''ve been taking.', 'N3', NULL),
(697, 'Shout "PLEASE ANSWER ME!" a few times to get the idea of "answer" in your head. What you really want is an answer, whether it''s bad or good, but nobody''s willing to say anything because you look so terrible.', 'With this shiny quarter in your hand, everyone is giving you their answer. Yay for bribery! ', 'You pull out a <reading>quarter</reading> (こた) and offer it to whoever will give you an <kanji>answer</kanji>.', 'N4', NULL),
(698, 'It''s unbelievable the kind of picture you''re able to paint with just the tips of threads. So much detail. So artistic. If it helps, imagine that you drew the Mona Lisa, better than the original, only using this thread tool.', 'Tell a few more people about your picture. Have them all do the same surprised "Eh?!" reaction at your art skills.', 'You tell someone you painted this masterpiece of a <kanji>picture</kanji>. Their response? "<reading>EH</reading> (え)?!"', 'N3', NULL),
(699, 'Imagine looking over a bunch of different kinds of shellfish wrapped in nets. Think about which ones you want to buy.', 'Imagine buying an entire car''s worth of shellfish. Mmm, delicious.', 'You decide to <kanji>buy</kanji> as many shellfish as you can fit in your <reading>ca</reading>r (か). You keep buying until your car is completely full of shellfish before you stop. Cars are totally a realistic unit of measure.', 'N4', NULL),
(700, 'Imagine riding your scooter down the road. You''re carefully aiming the scooter''s neck the right way so you don''t end up going out of control and dying.', 'Alternate ending: You do a sweet jump, go right through the doughnut hole, take a bite, and land safely back onto the road!', 'To remember the reading どう we use the word "doughnut."

You''re traveling down this <kanji>road</kanji> so fast that you don''t see the giant <reading>dou</reading>ghnut (どう) that''s rolling toward you until the last minute. You crash into it, and both you and the doughnut go tumbling down. Ahg, your neck!', 'N4', NULL),
(701, '"Interval" is a weird word to remember, but just think of the sun going around the earth and through the gate like clockwork. Every 24 hours it passes through the gate, and each interval is exactly the same.', 'Picture Genghis Khan ordering his most skilled inventors and builders to create this special sun gate so they can harness the power of intervals, and all people under his rule can benefit from the accurate telling of time.', 'This method of keeping track of <kanji>interval</kanji>s of time was invented by the great Khans of Mongolia, specifically the most famous Khan of all: Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん).

Genghis Khan has had a huge impact on the world as we know it, especially the Eurasian continent of the past, so he''s going to come up a lot from now on. Be prepared to get very familiar with him and his friends!', 'N5', NULL),
(702, 'Picture all of the different types of clouds in the sky. There are puffy white ones, heavy dark ones, and wispy thin ones. But they''re all clouds.', 'You look at each cloud and each one is a different cool mom. They''re each doing different cool mom things. You suddenly don''t want to look at the clouds anymore.', 'You lie down and try to figure out what the <kanji>cloud</kanji>s are shaped like. The first cloud is shaped... like a <reading>coo</reading>l <reading>mo</reading>m (くも). She''s wearing high pants, and holding a kid on each hip, but she''s also listening to the latest music and using slang. Huh. You look to the next cloud and see... wait, another cool mom?', 'N2', NULL),
(703, 'She has to count each grain in order to divvy up the rice supply and make it last the winter. Imagine the amount you get today — just a small number of grains. It''s not a good time for you guys…', 'Come up with a story about why your farm got sued for everything. Imagine sitting in court. Listen to the judge deliver the verdict. Be angry. The woman will be counting rice for years to come.', 'Why do you have to <kanji>count</kanji> your rice out like this? It''s because someone <reading>sue</reading>d (すう) your farm and took everything you owned. Since being sued, you only have a small <kanji>amount</kanji> of rice to last the winter.', 'N3', NULL),
(704, 'Close your eyes and feel the aura of comfort wash over you. Listen to the musical tinkling of the tree''s icy branches, setting you at ease. You never want this pleasurable moment to end!', 'Place a hanger on the rack. Feel the wave of pleasure that runs through you, and then bask in the afterglow of comfort and ease that it leaves behind. Now, go find something else to hang up on your rack. You need another hit of comfort and pleasure.', 'You want more of this aura of <kanji>comfort</kanji>, so you chop down the magic tree and build a clothing <reading>rack</reading> (らく) out of it. Then, you put the rack in your house so you can enjoy that fuzzy feeling of comfort and ease every time you go to hang something up.', 'N4', NULL),
(705, 'Your problem is you weren''t saying it with your tongue before. Once you start using your tongue, you''re able to speak suddenly!', 'You decided to teach a walrus how to speak because it seemed like the hardest thing to teach, with its tusks and all that. It turned out to be really easy. Although, you may have picked the Justin Long walrus, by accident. Either way, this walrus can speak! And you definitely (probably) helped.', 'If something uses their tongue, it will be able to <kanji>talk</kanji> and <kanji>speak</kanji>. To prove this, you teach a <reading>wa</reading>lrus (わ) to use its tongue, and lo and behold, it speaks to you!', 'N5', NULL),
(706, 'Imagine being hit by the electricity. Feel the powerful electric shock pulse through your body. You''re like a crazier Ben Franklin out here.', 'Feel the indent in your face. It doesn''t hurt, strangely, but it is pretty huge. Panic about this dent a bit.', 'After the <kanji>electricity</kanji> hits you, you feel your face. In it is a huge <reading>den</reading>t (でん).', 'N5', NULL),
(707, 'Why do you hate this place, in particular? Come up with a great reason why to help you to remember this very broad kanji meaning.', 'Come up with a weird reason as to why the circus is at this place you hate so much. Why did you come here with your axe? Does it have something to do with the circus show?', 'You smash through the door and finally get inside the <kanji>place</kanji>, only to find some kind of <reading>sho</reading>w (しょ) going on. It''s a circus show — elephants, tightrope walkers, bears on unicycles! The greatest show on earth, but why is it at this place?', 'N3', NULL),
(855, 'Imagine someone who looks super anti-social (and a little feral). Ask them why they''re sitting on this stool inside a cliff. They reply it''s because they hate people. They''re anti-people. In fact, they''re anti-everything.', 'Make sure you get to the point where you pull this anti-social guy''s beard off, to reveal Han Solo beneath. Be surprised... not just because he''s Han Solo, but because he''s real, too! Maybe you can yell out "Han! Your people need you!"', 'Turns out, when you go to this guy and pull off his beard, its the <kanji>anti</kanji>-hero from Star Wars, <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo! What is Han doing here, so hidden away? He''s obviously hiding from Jabba because he doesn''t want to get frozen in carbonite again.', 'N3', NULL),
(587, 'Imagine running into this samurai salesman as you walk through a sketchy part of town. He cautiously glances over his shoulder before opening his trench coat to reveal to you… all the foreheads and legs he''s trying to sell.', 'Imagine loudly saying "bye" over and over at this shady samurai. He keeps trying to tell you about how fresh and high-quality all his foreheads and legs are, but you just loudly repeat "bye" as you back away from him.', 'If you run into this samurai who <kanji>sell</kanji>s severed foreheads and legs, you should just say "<reading>bye</reading> (ばい)!" and get the heck out of there. Actually, if anyone tries to sell you severed body parts — samurai or not — you should probably say bye over and over until you can get away from them.', 'N4', NULL),
(708, 'Imagine his muffled words as he tries to explain this with the cross in his mouth. Hm, better not pry further. Maybe he''s trying to… X-orcize his demons… ', 'Imagine Jesus gently taking the cross out of Wolverine''s mouth. What a swell dude.', 'This <kanji>occurrence</kanji> doesn''t impress <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ), who appears and gently removes the cross from Wolverine''s mouth.', 'N4', NULL),
(709, 'Smell the ground. Waft the earthy smell of history into your leader-like nostrils. What does it smell like? It smells useful. And also like ground.', 'When you use the ground for sheep like this, your people rejoice. You''re the best leader ever! You always use everything to your advantage!', 'You <kanji>use</kanji> the ground to raise <reading>shee</reading>p (し). When you were down there sniffing around you realized that your people will flourish best if you use this land for grazing and raising sheep. ', 'N4', NULL),
(710, 'You feel the panic upon realizing what you''ve just done. This is murder in the first degree. You used a terrible tool to do it, and now you must hide it.', 'That goo is really slimy. It''s disgusting. You feel sick to your stomach. Please don''t throw up, there''s already enough junk on the ground with the eye and fins and all.', 'The <kanji>tool</kanji> is dripping with <reading>goo</reading> (ぐ). Goopy, incriminating goo. It''s the guts of the fish you just killed and it''s all over your hands too. Yuck.', 'N3', NULL),
(711, 'You''re dealing with a lot of emotions right now. You feel the pain from being cleated in the forehead, but you''re also glad to have finally been accepted into the soccer club. Suppress your pain and receive this honor.', 'Did you ever play soccer as a kid? Do you remember accepting a small juice box into your little kid hands during halftime? Apparently pros drink the same ones.', 'After they <kanji>accept</kanji> you, they give you a small <reading>ju</reading>ice (じゅ) box. It''s to help you recover from the trauma of getting kicked. And you <kanji>receive</kanji> the juice with grace.', 'N3', NULL),
(712, 'Imagine Japanese people with grain in their mouth. The whole country is at peace.', 'The walruses also achieve peace by putting grain in every walrus'' mouth, following the Japanese style.', '<kanji>Peace</kanji> is the <kanji>Japanese style</kanji>. The <reading>wa</reading>lruses (わ) living off of Japan see this, and start to emulate.', 'N3', NULL),
(714, 'Think back on all the experiments and calculations you did to determine this. You tested all kinds of things — umbrellas, awnings, tarps — but ultimately you determined that only a roof would suffice.', 'Stick your hands into your coat pockets and feel those crispy, toasty taters. You''re not gonna let them come to any harm.', 'Why did you <kanji>determine</kanji> your coat rack needed a roof? Because your coat pockets are full of <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tots! You can''t afford to let your tater tots get rained on, so you determined they needed to be properly sheltered with a roof.', 'N3', NULL),
(715, 'Imagine sitting comfortably on your roof as the world around you changes to spring. Snow melts and reveals all of the truths that have been obscured and hidden in winter.', 'Gee! Two babies! Reality sure is lovely.', 'At the moment, you''re trying to learn the <kanji>truth</kanji> about the deer that is coming out of the snowy woods when you see not one, but two baby deer appear with it! <reading>Gee! Two</reading> (じつ) babies! You keep looking around and realize that all of the animals seem to have two babies that they had hidden in the snow.', 'N3', NULL),
(716, 'Imagine placing each piece of clothing you own onto the stool and carefully stamping them with a symbol of the moon. This should stop people from stealing all of your clothes!', 'Your friend turns red and tries to pull the clothes back, "F**k you!" they yell. "No, f**k you, these are my clothes and I have proof this time!"', 'A few days later you''re with a friend when you recognize the <kanji>clothes</kanji> they''re wearing. You grab it, rip it off, and scream, "<reading>F**k</reading> (ふく) you!" There is your moon stamp! These are your clothes!!', 'N4', NULL),
(717, 'Picture most of the earth''s population already swimming on the tsunami that will last the rest of eternity. Your floaties and life preservers would help you through this mess. You need to swim!', 'Picture yourself swimming in the tsunami with everyone else. You can''t really talk to them, you''re too busy swimming for your life, so you just shout "Oy!" to them and they shout it back.', '<reading>Oy</reading> (およ)! Oy, stop daydreaming about <kanji>swim</kanji>ming and actually do it!', 'N3', NULL),
(718, 'Imagine if cows could fly. Wouldn''t you be jealous of them? I mean, if a cow can fly, any old thing should be able to, right?', 'Imagine a thing like flying boots. Do you believe such a thing could be real?', 'You want a <kanji>thing</kanji> to help you fly with the cow so you put on your flying <reading>boots</reading> (ぶつ). Your flying boots are a pretty strange thing in themselves, but they''re the only way you can keep up.', 'N4', NULL),
(719, 'Flowers, like anything that grows old, go through a lot of suffering as their bodies break down. They''re no different from you and me.', 'No one can resist a good cookie. It will ease your suffering, or at least distract you for a little bit.', 'If a flower or a person is growing old and <kanji>suffering</kanji>, give them a <reading>coo</reading>kie (く).', 'N3', NULL),
(720, 'Think of the last time life gave you a kick, and how you expressed that pain to make it go away, specifically on the surface that is your face.', 'Imagine yourself saying it more like ひょう, though. ひょう man!', 'You need to <kanji>express</kanji> yourself, so you call up a friend and say "<reading>Heyo</reading>!" (ひょう).', 'N3', NULL),
(721, 'Of course, we feel terrible about forcing the clowns into their own part of town. It''s not that they''re lesser than us, it''s just that we don''t like them.', 'Picture all of the clowns, trapped in their weird clown part of town, drinking booze and smashing each other over the heads with the bottles. You''re glad you don''t live in that part.', 'You see, that <kanji>part</kanji> of town is full of <reading>boo</reading>ze (ぶ). Clowns need a lot of booze to keep their charade going, so we just let them have it. But they aren''t allowed in our part anymore, because they all stink of nasty clown booze.', 'N3', NULL),
(722, 'Just imagine you''re a car or a train and the grain is a person. It''s going for a nice ride in your mouth!', 'Now the nori is going for a ride too, woohoo!', 'You decide the grain is probably lonely on this <kanji>ride</kanji> alone, so you add some <reading>no</reading>ri (の) seaweed, so it has a friend.', 'N3', NULL),
(723, 'Picture all of the hungry guests and customers under your roof, eating your food and sleeping on your floor.', 'Kyaku looks just like any other customer. Picture a stereotypical "customer" type person in your head.', 'One of your <kanji>guest</kanji>s is a regular named <reading>Kyaku</reading> (きゃく) the <kanji>customer</kanji>. Kyaku is always staying over at one place or another, so everyone knows their name.', 'N3', NULL),
(724, 'That''s the natural next progression of claiming some land. Building a roof upon it and building some shops and stores inside. You''ll see why in a moment.', 'You have yourself a whole Yankees stadium, and all it took was a roof and some shops and stores! If you build it, they will come.', 'The <kanji>roof</kanji> covering the dirt is there so that the <reading>Ya</reading>nkees (や) can play baseball year round. The <kanji>store</kanji>s and <kanji>shop</kanji>s are there to sell hot dogs and Yankees memorabilia.', 'N4', NULL),
(725, 'Since a yurt is round, the stool has to be angled at certain degrees. It''s part of yurt culture. Imagine someone adjusting the stool with a measurement tool to adjust it to an exact degree.', 'The dog changes its degrees at every occurrence of the stool changing. Picture the dog leaning at the exact degree the stool is at.', 'Also angled at exact <kanji>degree</kanji>s is the <reading>do</reading>g (ど).', 'N4', NULL),
(727, 'Imagine holding a tiny temple in your fingers. Hold it up to the light. Hold it on the train. Hold it while you sleep.', 'Jesus likes when you hold things like temples. He probably likes when you hold other things too, like ice cream, puppies, and homeless peoples'' hands.', 'As you <kanji>hold</kanji> the temple you hear a voice. You look down through your fingers and see a tiny <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ). He''s smiling and gives you a thumbs up. Aww, thanks Jesus!', 'N4', NULL),
(728, 'The world is basically just a bunch of rice paddies smooshed together, if you think about it.', 'A rice paddy is mostly made up of water, just like our world, and you don''t want to have wet feet. Use the power of your kayak to become a world traveler.', 'To get around this <kanji>world</kanji> of rice paddies, you need to use a <reading>kay</reading>ak (かい).', 'N4', NULL),
(729, 'Feel the heat from the lantern. It''s not quite as comforting as being inside a tent, but it''s better than nothing. The lantern will light your way on your departure.', 'How cold and unprotected would your head be without a hat? Very. Taking hats along with departures is integral to their success. Imagine how unsafe you would feel with no hats. That''s no departure I want to set out on.', 'Oh! Don''t forget your <reading>hats</reading> (はつ) either. Wouldn’t want to start a <kanji>departure</kanji> without hats or a lantern. That''d be a short journey.', 'N4', NULL),
(730, 'It''s the same with humans, you know? If you want to have a mutual relationship you need to be able to look each other in the eyes. If you''re too embarrassed, or you look down on them, then that isn''t mutual respect!', 'This is basically a FernGully moment, you know? You can feel the tree''s pain like it''s your pain. It''s that kind of mutual sharing of souls you need.', 'If your respect for the tree is <kanji>mutual</kanji> with the respect the tree feels for you, you''ll be able to see the tree''s <reading>sou</reading>l (そう). Right now its soul looks pretty sad, like it''s trying to say, "Why are your people cutting down all my friends? I thought we had a mutual understanding."', 'N3', NULL),
(731, 'Imagine the small lion casting his eye across his future prefecture. His small eye is twinkling, because he''s proud that he''ll one day be prefect, and this prefecture will be his.', 'Picture Ken protecting the prefecture, punching hyenas in the face, and yelling, "This may just be a prefecture, not a country, but I''ll give it my all for my lion prefect-to-be!"', 'This small lion''s <kanji>prefecture</kanji> is being protected by <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai, because the lion is too small to protect all of this land himself. Ken the samurai is used to wandering far and wide, so one prefecture is nothing to him.', 'N2', NULL),
(732, 'Imagine a great big beautiful sheep. See the sheep''s beauty.

This sheep is so big and fluffy that you can''t see its tail, which is why it looks bit different in this kanji.', 'The bees are swarming around the sheep, marvelling at its beauty and trying to figure out how to get nectar from it.', 'The big sheep''s <kanji>beauty</kanji> has attracted a swarm of <reading>bee</reading>s (び). The sheep is so beautiful that the bees have mistaken it for a flower.', 'N3', NULL),
(733, 'Imagine that it''s your first day in prison. You''re walking around the yard, minding your own business, when suddenly a shellfish smacks you right in the head and falls into your open hands. "HAHA YOU LOSE!" someone shouts.', 'Now you have to try to make some other fool lose. Otherwise you''ll be the loser fool forever.', 'If you <kanji>lose</kanji>, you''re a <reading>foo</reading>l (ふ). All you had to do was not hold the prison shellfish. It really shouldn''t have been that hard to not lose, ya fool.', 'N3', NULL),
(734, 'Picture the horned thing on its scooter, a big messenger bag over its shoulder with the kanji for heaven on it. "You got somethin'' you want me to send?" it mutters.', 'You put your soul into an envelope, stick a stamp on it, and hand it off to be sent to heaven. Bye-bye! I hope this doesn''t have any negative effects on your body!', 'You want to <kanji>send</kanji> your <reading>sou</reading>l (そう) to heaven. I mean, you don''t want it to go to some of those other places you''ve heard of, so why not send your soul?', 'N4', NULL),
(735, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Imagine holding a real jewel in your right hand and a fake jewel in your left. The one in your right hand is suuuuupeerrr heavy!', 'In your hand is a very <kanji>heavy</kanji> <reading>jew</reading>el (じゅう). Real jewels are always heavier than they look.', 'N4', NULL),
(736, 'Imagine yourself next to the two campfires, having a heart-to-heart talk. What are you saying?', 'If you try to talk all night and past dawn, you''ll burn out. Take the day, think, then tonight you can try to have an all-night talk again.', 'You <kanji>talk</kanji> in this intense, fiery way all the way until <reading>dawn</reading> (だん), but no longer! Once dawn comes the discussion needs to be concluded.', 'N3', NULL),
(737, 'Picture the woman in the helicopter needing something. She''s looking around. Searching. But she doesn''t know what it is!', 'What does she need? Yogurt!', 'Finally she knows what she <kanji>need</kanji>s! It''s <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう)! Delicious, creamy yogurt!', 'N3', NULL),
(738, 'Watch as the moon''s power fills the gladiator. He starts to glow, grows larger, and has some craters on his face now. Well.. okay.', 'The Shougun is only interested in people who win. But you probably could have guessed that already.', 'He <kanji>win</kanji>s over and over again, and soon the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) is even rooting for him. In fact, the Shougun claims him as his personal gladiator of choice.', 'N3', NULL),
(739, 'She tires of things so quickly, you hardly have time to get used to them before they''re gone.', 'Picture all of the temporary cars she''s tried filling up an entire junkyard. They''re all a little different, but in the end they were all just temporary fads.', 'The leader''s most <kanji>temporary</kanji> possession is her <reading>ca</reading>r (か). Or... cars, because they''re all very temporary. She buys one, drives it around, and then suddenly she''s anti red car. She needs a blue one!', 'N1', NULL),
(854, 'Imagine yourself about to cross the street, and your mama saying in your ear: "always look beforehand"!', 'As you cross the road, you pick up the yo-yo and put it in your pocket. You''re really glad you looked beforehand, because you now have a yo-yo!', 'As you look <kanji>beforehand</kanji>, you see a <reading>yo-</reading>yo (よ) lying in the street.', 'N3', NULL),
(857, 'As you imagine yourself cutting down the standing tree, think about what new tree you''ll plant in its place.', 'Only your shin got hit, so focus all your imaginary pain down there. Try to pull out the splinters. Ouch!', 'You cut down the tree to make room for a <kanji>new</kanji> one, but it falls on your <reading>shin</reading> (しん). ', 'N4', NULL),
(858, 'Unfortunately, now you have to return to the store to return this scooter, but you have no scooter to get there with. Ugh.', 'The employee leans down toward you in hen form and says, "Ooh, I''m sorry. We don''t take returns from hens. You''ll have to just return home."', 'You <kanji>return</kanji> to the store and wait in line to return the scooter but the person working returns doesn''t want you to return it. He stares you dead in the eyes, waves his hand, and turns you into a <reading>hen</reading> (へん)!', 'N3', NULL),
(890, 'For now, just imagine the leader of the elephants being told images and statues of him are being put up everywhere. Don''t think about what the statue looks like, because that''s next.', 'But this is exactly what the leader of the elephants asked for. You see, the leader of the elephants was a big fan of zombies. Imagine how excited he''d get to have this zombie version of himself appearing as images and statues, all across the land.', 'When they unveil the <kanji>image</kanji>s and <kanji>statue</kanji>s of the leader elephant, everyone gasps. They look like him, but he''s a <reading>zo</reading>mbie (ぞう). Gasp!', 'N2', NULL),
(983, 'Basically, a tree leader is someone who stands up for trees. Mostly she talks, but that can be a powerful skill to have.', 'What a terrible scene. Focus on the fact that they''re using a horrible, modified back hoe. It''s more like a weapon than a back hoe, and they show no mercy at all.', 'She chains herself to a tree to <kanji>preserve</kanji> them. But, unfortunately, the lumberjacks just take a giant back <reading>hoe</reading> (ほ) and tear this tree leader and the tree she''s attached to in half.', 'N1', NULL),
(1029, 'Try to imagine trying this hard to be heard. The construction workers are all screaming at each other with ease. It''s so hard for you to try and keep up with their volume!', 'Huh. You should have thought to try this sheep technique earlier.', 'You <kanji>try</kanji> to get their attention the only way you know how, by lifting a <reading>shee</reading>p (し) above your head. The sheep baas and suddenly everything is silent, all eyes are on you. Well, they''re on the sheep.', 'N4', NULL),
(1048, 'Imagine the horse and squid in a big testing room, getting ready to test you. What are they testing you on? Weirdly, they''re going to test you on all the kanji that use horse and squid radicals. You ready?', 'To make sure you don''t cheat on the horse and the squid test, Ken the samurai is watching you closely, pacing up and down. Every now and then, he waves his katana menacingly.', 'The <kanji>test</kanji> is being supervised by <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai.', 'N4', NULL),
(1088, '', 'Feel today''s heat. Now compare it to yesterday. You might want to check your thermometer if you''re not sure!', '<kanji>Compare</kanji> the <reading>he</reading>at (ひ) today with the heat yesterday. Is it hotter today? It''s up to you to compare.', 'N2', NULL),
(1880, NULL, 'Imagine a bunch of little black belt spice molecules からて punching, kicking and chopping all the way through your mouth and into your throat and nasal passages. Oww, it''s pure agony!', 'When you eat <kanji>spicy</kanji> food, it gives a <reading>から</reading>て chop to your mouth. That''s where that spicy feeling comes from!', 'N2', NULL),
(465, 'Make sure you look at this amazing fishing lure too - hold it in your hand, see the two barbs fused together. Think about how strange this lure is. Is this really the best in the world? Will this really finish all other fishing lures?', 'Make him think that he can’t just use his new lure on the side of the lake / ocean / whatever. He has to get out there, but unfortunately all he has is a crappy row boat. Imagine the row boat being terrible, leaky, and dangerous, which is why you’re surprised when he goes for it. That’s a terrible row boat to use! If you do this, you''ll focus your mind on the rowboat part of things, which will help you to remember the reading of this kanji.', 'How does the fish lure maker test his newly <kanji>finish</kanji>ed lure out? The one he just finished? He gets in a <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat and rows out into the sea.', 'N2', NULL),
(499, 'Picture yourself in private, with fins. You wish others could see them. "That''s it," you decide. "It''s time to show the world. I''m going to make my fins public!"', 'How long have you been hiding in private with your fins? The public areas have changed, and all the people have become こういちs. One mother こういち--pushing a baby こういち--looks up and stares at your fins. Now you remember why you never go out in public.', 'You head out into the <kanji>public</kanji> where everyone can see your fins, but... things have changed. All people are the same. There are thousands of <reading>こう</reading>いちs walking around, and they all stare at you (though they pretend like they aren''t).', 'N4', NULL),
(630, 'Here''s some straight talk for ya: if you study your radicals, you''ll have no problem with this one.', 'Imagine shoving food straight down your esophagus and choking as you do. Why would you do this to yourself?!', 'Put food <kanji>straight</kanji> into your esophagus without chewing and you will <reading>choke</reading> (ちょく). You want to get straight to the most important part of eating — the digesting part — but if you don''t follow all the intermediate steps, you''ll end up choking.', 'N3', NULL),
(713, 'Picture a woman seeing a new machine and beginning work on it right away to see how she can start it up.', 'Picture a machine starting up as the woman begins to shear the sheep. Picture the sheep staring at you, staring at it.', 'Actually, before she can <kanji>begin</kanji>, she has to shear the <reading>shee</reading>p (し) over there. The machine is going to turn wool into yarn, but you can''t just start that process by shoving the sheep in there. You have to keep the little sheep safe!', 'N4', NULL),
(740, 'If you run into yourself, it really messes you up, you know? You won''t be able to handle what you''re seeing so you have to wake up.', 'Your obi is like a special object you only have in dreams. If you have your obi, it''s okay, it''s a dream and you can wake up now! Make sure you memorize exactly how it feels so dream-you can''t trick you or something.', 'If one runs into oneself and you don''t <kanji>wake up</kanji>, just look down at your waist. If you''re wearing an <reading>o</reading>bi (お) you know it''s a dream and you''ll eventually wake up. If you aren''t wearing an obi then it isn''t a dream and you don''t have anything to wake up from.', 'N4', NULL),
(741, 'Imagine how fast you can go on your scooter holding this bundle. It''s way faster than trying to go on foot, that''s for sure!', 'Imagine riding the scooter as fast as you can in just your socks. You can feel the wind pulling them off your feet!', 'You were going so <kanji>fast</kanji>, you only put on <reading>sock</reading>s (そく). So now you''re barreling down the street at top speeds in just your sad, white socks.', 'N3', NULL),
(742, 'Don''t be that person who keeps the alcohol all to themselves. Distribute it to all of the legally-aged people you know!', 'This becomes the haiku you say before every party, every potluck, and every night out at the bar. You even get it tattoo''d to your arm.', 'As you <kanji>distribute</kanji> the alcohol you say a <reading>hai</reading>ku (はい):

Here you go my friends
Alcohol for everyone
Never drink alone', 'N3', NULL),
(743, 'It’s made with spiced rum, coconut rum, and dark rum. Imagine yourself on a sandy beach drinking this cocktail. Feel the sun on your face and the wind in your hair as you sip on this fruity and rummy alcoholic beverage known as a tsunami. You could even picture an actual tsunami in the water coming right for you. I wouldn’t recommend it though—actual tsunami are scary, not drinkable and delicious.', 'Picture the alcohol flowing down this long chute from somewhere far away. You try to see where it''s coming from, but all you can see is this long, long chute stretching along the entire beach, until it disappears into the distance. You should probably be concerned that you''re nonstop drinking alcohol from a random chute, but it tastes so good!', 'As you lie on this beach, the <kanji>alcohol</kanji> keeps coming down a <reading>chu</reading>te (しゅ), straight into your mouth. You can feel the sand in your toes and the alcohol sliding down your throat from this special tsunami chute.', 'N3', NULL),
(744, 'Institutions are places of learning, but they have to be a physical place with a roof and all that. If you''re learning out in a field somewhere, that''s probably not a proper institution.', 'Just remember, it''s an IN-stitution, and you''ll know this reading easy-peasy.', 'What does <kanji>institution</kanji> start with? In (いん)!', 'N4', NULL),
(745, 'Picture the season of winter as a long thread, embalmed in ice. At the end of it is the year’s end. The thread of winter and ice brings you closure, it marks the end.', 'This thread isn''t just a metaphor, it''s real! Feel the cold ice on it, as you lace it into your shoe. An age old tradition, the lacing of icy threads as winter ends. Your shoes will look so cool, or should I say… iced out. ', 'Whenever winter <kanji>end</kanji>s, you take the thread covered in ice, and lace it into your <reading>shoe</reading> (しゅう). ', 'N4', NULL),
(746, 'Picture yourself in a library poring through huge tomes on feathers, specifically for completely white ones. You want to learn everything you can!', 'Oh geez, oh no. You better learn how to control these shoes before you—CRASH!!! Welp, you just shot through a plane. You need to learn more before you act next time.', 'You <kanji>learn</kanji> that you should tie these white feathers to your <reading>shoe</reading>s (しゅう). You do it before you learn what it will do, and then suddenly you''re jump-flying out of the building.', 'N4', NULL),
(747, 'Imagine driving your car around and around the cloud, instead of going through it. At least it''s soft and cool.', 'Unfortunately, not long after you get through that cloud another one comes down and gets in your way. Now you''re counting each revolution, one... two... three... all the way up to ten.', 'You have to <kanji>revolve</kanji> around the cloud <reading>ten</reading> (てん) times before it disappears and you''re able to go through.', 'N4', NULL),
(748, 'Imagine picking up the turkey and putting it on your scooter. Of course, you give it your helmet as well, safety first.', 'Silly turkey, you can''t advance that fast, of course you''ll hurt your shins. It''s better to advance slowly, but I guess I couldn''t expect you to know that.', 'You wave as the turkey <kanji>advance</kanji>s into the street at top speeds, collides with a car, and scrapes up its <reading>shin</reading>s (しん) almost immediately. Ah!', 'N3', NULL),
(749, 'Imagine being out in this tsunami-induced storm. You''re wet, cold, and scared, but you''re calmed and mesmerized by how the flowers dance in the wind before they finally fall.', 'Imagine running around catching the flowers as they fall. Imagine placing each fallen petal onto the rack to keep it safe and dry.', 'You want to save these flowers that <kanji>fall</kanji> around you, so you take out a drying <reading>rack</reading> (らく) and place them carefully on it. Any flowers that fall will go on the rack like this, so can save them for later.', 'N3', NULL),
(750, 'Leaves, no matter how little, are the most important thing in the world for trees and flowers.', 'は is the sound of a leaf coming into existence. Whenever you see a plant from now on, hear the little "hah!" sound it makes when each leaf pops out of its body.', 'Picture a little flower working really hard to grow its first leaf. The <kanji>leaf</kanji> pops out and it shouts, "<reading>Hah</reading> (は)!”', 'N3', NULL),
(751, 'Picture yourself trying to move a huge slab tombstone. It’s difficult, no? I bet you wish you had a car or some other vehicle to make the process much less painful. Your arms are getting sore from carrying the tombstone and you’re getting sweaty and hot from all the work. What you would do for a car with air conditioning.', 'Remember all that hard work you were doing before and how you got all sweaty and gross? Now imagine yourself inside of a nice car with air conditioning, and the tombstone in the trunk. It feels great doesn’t it? The air conditioning is perfect, and driving a car is much easier than carrying a tombstone around. Thank goodness you found a car loophole in the tombstone carrying rules.', 'What do you wish you had to transport this tombstone? Oh, that’s right, a car. So you need some kind of <reading>car</reading> <reading>loo</reading>phole (かる). If you had a car, this tombstone moving business would be no problem. To cars, tombstones are naught but <kanji>lightweight</kanji> pebbles! But the rules say you can''t use cars to carry tombstones, so you need to find a car loophole.', 'N2', NULL),
(752, 'Imagine trying to carry your scooter down a busy road, with all the cars whizzing past. Your forehead is still hurting from that nasty bang. Good luck!', 'It''s hard enough to carry a scooter in heavy traffic, but now you have to try to keep yourself and it dry with an umbrella too. ', 'As you <kanji>carry</kanji> the scooter, your <kanji>luck</kanji> turns, and it starts to rain, so you pull out your <reading>um</reading>brella (うん). Now you''re holding your scooter, your forehead, and an umbrella.', 'N4', NULL),
(753, 'Imagine being a traveler, walking around the outside of a huge walled city. You really need to get inside but all of the gates are closed. Finally, you see a single, bright lantern marking the open gate! What a relief.', 'Imagine kayaking in the dark toward an open gate. Finally, you''ve made it!', 'This <kanji>open</kanji> gate is in the water though, so you''ll need a <reading>kay</reading>ak (かい) to get in. Luckily, you''ve been carrying your kayak with you just in case.', 'N4', NULL),
(754, 'Collect another turkey, send it flying and gobbling up into the tree next to the other one.', 'Picture the nice, feathered shoes you''ll be able to make once you''ve gathered all of these turkeys together. Ohoho, you can''t wait! Go collect another turkey!', 'Once you''ve <kanji>collect</kanji>ed enough turkeys, you''re going to turn them into a <reading>shoe</reading> (しゅう). Maybe even two shoes, but that will mean <kanji>gather</kanji>ing even more turkeys!', 'N4', NULL),
(755, 'Imagine eating and eating and lacking a delicious, cold, thirst-quenching drink. Your mouth is dry. You have to drink something, anything!', 'Luckily, this nori isn''t dried and pressed like they usually are, and you manage to make a bright green nori drink. You gulp it down, and even though it tastes like the ocean, it hits the spot!', 'You get desperate for a <kanji>drink</kanji> and put some <reading>no</reading>ri (の) seaweed in a blender, trying to create a drink of your own.', 'N4', NULL),
(756, 'Imagine all the money you''ll rake in with this new business. People will come from far and wide to see the spiky mer-sheep sideshow. And for an extra charge you''ll let ''em touch those pointy spikes and slimy fins. Oh yes, business will be booming. ', 'Imagine the smell of garlicky gyoza filling the sideshow tent, as customers try to feed the sheep and compete for its attention. Business is so good that the sheep gets tired of gyoza and won''t eat it anymore. Oh well… Skewer them on its spikes and save ''em for later.', 'As you evolve your <kanji>business</kanji>, you let customers feed the sheep <reading>gyo</reading>za (ぎょう) dumplings, its favorite snack. For a nominal fee, of course. People love it when the mutant sheep eats gyoza out of their hands. Wouldn''t you?', 'N4', NULL),
(757, 'Picture the world way far away, from space. You see a giant tsunami carve out a massive chunk of land to the west of Japan on the main continent. Slowly the tsunami waters recede, leaving the outline of China.', 'Khan is only one man, but watch as he takes China for himself. A tiny little flag with a horse on it is raised in the middle, for all of the Chinese people to see. Hmm, this could be a problem.', 'You peer closer at <kanji>China</kanji> and see one man, Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん), leading his troops into the vast country, ready to conquer all the Chinese people living in China!', 'N4', NULL),
(758, 'Imagine kissing someone''s foot. Now imagine kissing someone''s foot after they''ve been walking down a road for hours. It''s disgusting. Smell the stench of sweat wafting up into your nostrils as the foot approaches your lips. It''s wretched.', 'Walking down a road barefoot like this is a sure way to dirty up your feet. Feel the dirt, rocks, and twigs under your feet. The road is hard and dry and is not making your journey very comfortable at all.', 'What was this person walking down again? Oh, that''s right – a <reading>ro</reading>ad (ろ). They were walking down a filthy road.', 'N3', NULL),
(759, 'Imagine slowly turning up your big boombox until your ears hurt and the ground beneath your feet is vibrating with the beat. Soon you and the boombox are sliding with all of the earth down the hill and onto your farmland. Hooray, now the farming can begin!', 'Picture Nostradamus flailing his arms and trying to explain his vision to the farmers at the time. He knows that if they listen their farming techniques will improve, but they''re looking at him like he''s nuts.', 'The first person to come up with this <kanji>farming</kanji> method was <reading>No</reading>stradamus (のう), the famous seer and prophecy man. He predicted humans of the future would use special "yell cubes" to "move that good earth."', 'N2', NULL),
(760, 'You think you''ve found a ton of gold bars! Imagine being so happy about this. Then, imagine your heart dropping in your chest when you notice the fault. Then, it dropping down even farther when you figure out it''s merely iron.', 'Watch a trailer on YouTube for Tetsuo the Iron Man. Go ahead, do it now – I’ll wait. Did you watch it? Remember that nonsense every time you see this kanji.', 'Do you know that movie, <reading>Tetsu</reading>o (てつ) the <kanji>Iron</kanji> Man? We talked about it on Tofugu a while back. It’s one of the wackiest Japanese movies of all time. If you don’t know about Tetsuo the Iron Man, you should definitely look it up (if you aren''t averse to horror/violent films). Tetsuo is one of the most insane movies in the history of ever.', 'N2', NULL),
(761, 'Imagine singing a song with a couple lip rings in your mouth. The articulation will be all wrong! It''s definitely better to lack lip rings if you''re going to sing.', 'There''s something about your car that makes your singing sound so much better than usual, you know?', 'Where are you always singing <kanji>song</kanji>s, by the way? In your <reading>ca</reading>r (か). It''s not just you, either. Pretty much everyone likes to sing in their car.', 'N4', NULL),
(762, 'Your next move is to go to the doctor, because your brain has calculated that the pain is extreme. Imagine the doctor asking how much it hurts. Well, on a scale of one to ten, doctor, I''d calculate this is about a twenty.', 'Imagine trekking through the frozen wastes of the North Pole (with your eye patch on) to obtain Santa-san''s calculation advice. After much discussion, he concludes that your bamboo attacker deserves twenty. No, not twenty years in prison — just twenty pieces of coal. Oh, how disappointing…', 'Next you want to <kanji>calculate</kanji> how much punishment your bamboo attacker deserves, so you call on <reading>San</reading>ta-san (さん). Santa-san is always calculating how many pieces of coal the naughty children deserve, so he''s perfect for this <kanji>calculation</kanji> job.', 'N2', NULL),
(763, 'I''m sure you''ve pressed your ear up against a wall, door, or gate before to hear what''s going on on the other side. Remember back to a time you did this and how sneaky you felt listening in on people without them knowing. You sneaky frood, you.', 'I''m sure many of you were forced to play "Hot Cross Buns" on the recorder in school at some point. Picture yourself playing this song and remember the melody when you see this kanji.', 'From the other side of the gate, you <kanji>hear</kanji> one of the people start to play a song on a recorder. You recognize the song as "Hot Cross <reading>Bun</reading>s (ぶん)." You haven''t heard this song since grade school. "Hot cross buns, hot cross buns~"', 'N5', NULL),
(764, 'Picture these five mouths saying the same language to each other. They can understand each other! The mouths are smiling and laughing too.', 'Godzilla speaks a language some people understand! You wish you knew this earlier, then you might have been able to stop some of his destruction. Try to say, "Hey Godzilla, please stop destroying my city," in his language. It''s a lot of growling and yelling. He doesn''t seem to understand you. You better practice your pronunciation.', 'Another mouth joins in speaking the <kanji>language</kanji>, but it''s green and scaled and... it''s <reading>Go</reading>dzilla''s (ご) mouth! Godzilla speaks this language too.', 'N5', NULL),
(765, 'Imagine reading what you have for sale out loud to people walking by because they’re too lazy to read it for themselves.', 'Imagine reading through your list of yo-yo tricks. Do you know how to pronounce those weird yo-yo names?', 'What are you forced to <kanji>read</kanji> aloud to sell? <reading>Yo-</reading>yo (よ) instructions! You’d think they’d be self explanatory, but there are actually a lot of rules and tricks that you have to read to get good at yo-yoing. Read all of these out loud so you can sell them.', 'N5', NULL),
(766, 'Imagine yourself out in the wilderness on a sunny day. The weather is perfect and the scenery is beautiful. The only sound you can hear is the chirp of birds throughout the woods around you. It''s very peaceful.', 'Picture yourself surrounded by birds of all kinds, handing out nachos. Just listen to their happy chirping. They''re sitting on your arms, shoulders, and even your head, trying to get closer to the nachos. That chirping is so loud!', 'The best way to experience the <kanji>chirp</kanji>-chirping of birds is to offer them <reading>na</reading>chos (な). Birds love nachos so much, it''s a guaranteed way to make them chirp with delight.', 'N3', NULL),
(767, 'Imagine you''re the person who fell overboard. The white water is rushing violently and it is deathly cold. Feel the water and freezing temperature on your skin. But look! Someone has thrown you a line of thread! You''re saved!', 'Coming out of the rushing water, this centaur is like an angel sent from heaven. He''s just saved your life. Imagine the happiness and gratitude you feel towards this centaur as you are pulled to safety by that line of thread.', 'The person who threw you that <kanji>line</kanji> of thread is actually a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん). How kind of him. After you are safe you hop on the centaur''s back and ride around to get the wind to dry you off.', 'N2', NULL),
(768, 'Picture the scene of a woman with a chainsaw cutting down this tree marked with a yellow X. Hear the sound of the chainsaw buzzing and the grinding of wood as it meets the side of the trunk. It’s very loud. Woodchips go flying everywhere.', 'Now picture the same scene as before only imagining Yoko Ono wielding that chainsaw. Maybe she’s dancing. Maybe there’s some Beatles music playing in the background. Either way it’s a strange sight – remember it.', 'Wait, that woman with the chainsaw is <reading>Yoko</reading> (よこ) Ono! Yes, the same Yoko Ono who’s married to John Lennon of the Beatles. What’s she doing chopping down trees on the <kanji>side</kanji> of your house? Who can say. Silly Yoko.', 'N3', NULL),
(769, 'Because the radical <radical>circumference</radical> is in play, you can think of the crime scene you''re investigating as a statue of a giant globe, and you''re doing a circumference around it. Then, in the reading, we''ll think about what you''ll <radical>say</radical>.', 'From what you know about evil Mrs. Chou, you probably could have guessed it was her. The "Circumference Investigation" is now over, though you do still have to catch the perp, Mrs. Chou.', '"I came here to <kanji>investigate</kanji> this place, and I did indeed find out who the criminal is... it''s none other than Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう)!"', 'N3', NULL),
(770, 'Imagine yourself standing in the shade of a beautiful, big, old tree. You see your parent walk past and shout out to them. They come and join you, and it''s like the two of you are the only two people in the world right now. That''s intimacy.', 'Busting your shins on things really hurts. I''m sure you''ve hit your shins on coffee tables and the like all your life. Remember that pain and think how much worse it would be banging your bare shins on pointy tree roots just when you were enjoying a moment of parental intimacy. Not enjoyable.', 'Standing under the tree, you go to give your <kanji>parent</kanji> a big hug, but you manage to bang your <reading>shin</reading> (しん)  on a huge tree root. Your parent pulls you in for a warm hug and rubs your shin. Despite the pain, it becomes an unforgettable "shin-timate" moment.', 'N4', NULL),
(771, 'Imagine, if you will, a geoduck. Now imagine it with a massive head. It looks ridiculous, and more disgusting than ever. It''s actually really creeping you out, because the head keeps getting larger as the geoduck eats more beans. Ew.', 'Remember how gross geoducks are? Of course you do. Now imagine one exploding its atomic head right in front of you. You''ve got geoduck mess all over yourself. It''s gross, slimy, sticky, and covering you from head to toe. It''s wretched.', 'As the geoduck eats more and more beans its <kanji>head</kanji> gets bigger and bigger, until finally the head explodes like an <reading>atomi</reading>c (あたま) bomb. BOOM! Geoduck parts go flying everywhere and pieces of its massive head float to the ground as atomic fallout.', 'N3', NULL),
(772, 'Imagine being slapped in the face with a geoduck — a sharp hot pain, which then mingles with the cold geoduck residue left behind on your face. It''s a strange, contradictory sensation.', 'Cows are big animals. So you probably got hit in the face with that geoduck pretty hard. Imagine the lingering pain, and how indignant you''d feel. Imagine getting in that cow''s face and demanding an explanation.', 'But where did that geoduck come from? As you turn from the cliff edge, you find yourself <kanji>face</kanji>-to-face with a <reading>cow</reading> (かお). This cow is the one who hit you in the face with a geoduck. You don''t know why, but something about your face has upset the cow.', 'N3', NULL),
(773, 'Imagine if you were a sick stick of dynamite. Every time you sneezed you ran the risk of blowing up. It would be a terrifying way to live. Just imagine it.', 'Picture this as your life. You could explode at any moment, you’re sick, and you have B.O. Nobody wants to be around you because you might explode and kill them, and you stink. What a terrible way to live!', 'Being nervous all the time about spontaneously exploding would probably make you sweat profusely. And you know what sweating causes? <reading>B.O.</reading> (びょう). You’d be one smelly stick of dynamite. B.O. is a very unpleasant smell, and being <kanji>sick</kanji> is no excuse.', 'N4', NULL),
(774, 'Most of the sun is getting into your ear, but not all of it. Darn, maybe you should try angling yourself a different way. ', 'Install the part into your cyborg. As long as most of the sun gets into its human ear, most of its cyborg bits will have power.', '<kanji>Most</kanji> of this is for your <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい). You''re testing out a new way to power it, but if you can''t get most of the sun into your ear, it won''t be enough to power most of the cyborg''s robo-parts.', 'N3', NULL),
(913, 'It''s a simple request, really. Just wipe up those water drops off the ground.', '"But cucumbers are already mostly water, this isn''t going to absorb anything!" you cry. Well, too bad. That''s my request, so you better do it. Figure out how to make the cucumber work.', 'Oh, one more thing. I <kanji>request</kanji> that you wipe up the water with this <reading>cu</reading>cumber (きゅう).', 'N3', NULL),
(1150, 'Will you be able to solve this cow riddle? Picture yourself carefully trying to untie a cow with a sword at just the right angle. What a tricky thing to solve.', 'So you''re on a kayak, with a cow, and you have to find just the right angle with your sword in order to untie the cow and solve this riddle. A bit much to wrap one''s head around. Anyway, good luck with that.', 'Did I mention that you have to <kanji>solve</kanji> this whole riddle while on a <reading>kay</reading>ak (かい)? No?! Whoops.', 'N3', NULL),
(775, 'Picture one Wolverine versus, like, a hundred prisoners. Who would win? Yeah, my money''s on the mutant in this conflict. ', 'Put yourself in Wolverine''s shoes. Imagine what it would feel like to have conflicting values, what it would do to your soul. Wanting to be peaceful on the one hand, but on the other not wanting to allow bigotry of any kind. It''s a conflict in your soul.', 'This constant <kanji>conflict</kanji> between Wolverine and the other inmates is really affecting his <reading>sou</reading>l (そう). Despite what people think, Wolverine really doesn''t like to fight. He just hates injustice so much, he feels he has to do something about it.', 'N3', NULL),
(776, 'I''m sure you''ve heard of flip-flopping in politics, when people try to take both sides of an issue, staying in the middle ground and never taking a stand. Imagine one of these people to represent this kanji.', 'Also, this kanji is pronounced exactly like the vocab word 中 (なか). If you can remember that, it''ll make remembering 仲 easier.', 'Imagine a flip-flopping politician you have an average <kanji>relationship</kanji> with. Now imagine that there''s a <reading>knocker</reading> (なか) on their forehead and you get to knock that knocker as hard as you like. Maybe you can knock some integrity into them and form a better relationship.', 'N2', NULL),
(777, 'Picture this special cloud floating above your leader. It''s probably just a speaker attached to a drone covered in cotton balls.', 'You highly doubt this is a magical cloud that can transmit messages. You inch closer to the big dent and try to poke it. Your finger goes right in! Hmm, you''re still pretty sure there''s a trick to this thing.', 'As the leader <kanji>transmit</kanji>s his latest series of messages to you and the others, you notice a pretty large <reading>den</reading>t (でん) in it. That dent is probably a clue to what this cloud is really made of.', 'N3', NULL),
(778, 'Imagine a fish trying to hold a full hand of cards together. Impossible! Fish don’t have fingers to keep them spread properly. Now imagine a fish trying to hold two cards together and nodding to hit. It''s much easier to keep things together this way!', 'Imagine a bunch of fish gathering together in きょうと to participate in this blackjack tournament. With so many fish all together in one place, it probably smells pretty fishy.', 'Once a year trillions of fish get <kanji>together</kanji> in <reading>きょう</reading>と to play blackjack. They have a huge tournament there once a year.', 'N3', NULL),
(779, 'Imagine a woman and a child you like very much. Just appreciate them, and think about how much you like those two. The reading mnemonic definitely won''t ruin your image of them or anything.', 'And you DO like them. You have always been a fan of こういち''s kanji learning methods, and you feel so lucky to have married a woman who looks just like こういち and then (this is the real miracle!) gave birth to a child that looks like こういち too. Hashtag blessed.', '"I <kanji>like</kanji> you two," you say to the woman and child. They both turn their heads 180 degrees back towards you. They both have faces that look just like <reading>こう</reading>いち''s. "We like you too," they say in unison.', 'N3', NULL),
(780, 'Picture the drunkard standing in front of you. He picked up the sword and is holding it above his head. It''s becoming... becoming...', 'If a sword becomes a saber in the hands of a drunkard... then what are you? Geez this became pretty deep.

You slice down the drunkard with your saber and become a little more depressed than you were before.', 'The sword has <kanji>become</kanji> a <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい)! It''s just like the saber you have! Hey, wait.', 'N3', NULL),
(781, 'Imagine yourself digging a coffin hole with nothing but a spoon. Your hands get all cramped, your wrist starts to hurt, and you get painful blisters on your hands. It takes forever to dig a hole big enough for a coffin!', 'You hated roe when you were still young, but now that you’re an elderly old fart you really want to eat it. Roe is for old people, you guess, as you slurp it up.', 'Now that you’re <kanji>elderly</kanji> (and probably about to die) you suddenly crave some delicious <reading>roe</reading> (ろう). Salmon roe, sea urchin roe, tofugu roe, doesn’t matter. Your old, tired bones need roe after all that hard work!', 'N3', NULL),
(782, 'Picture this great leader. Imagine him actually standing on the shoulders of his supporters who are a lower rank than he is. He’s standing atop this great pyramid as a fearless leader.', 'Picture the same ranking pyramid from before, only all the members are boy scouts, and the leader at the top is an eagle. Hear the piercing screech of the eagle with your ears.', 'This great leader is actually the leader of the boy scouts. And as we all know, the highest <kanji>rank</kanji> in boy scouts is the rank of <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) scout. This great leader has attained the rank of eagle scout and stands above all other scouts.', 'N3', NULL),
(783, 'Picture yourself, face smooshed into the ground, and you can hear the leader shouting above you, "GET LOW!"', 'Get low to the ground right now and ask your mom or dad to roll some tater tots towards your face. Eat them up, mm mm.', '<kanji>Low</kanji> people like you can only eat low food like potatoes. But that''s boring, so you low people learned to make <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tots. You roll them to each other and eat them without lifting your heads.', 'N2', NULL),
(784, 'Imagine the butterflies in your stomach as you prepare to do something for the first time. Now look down at your badass outfit and the sword on your belt. Feel the nervousness subside a little. You''ve got this.', 'Picture yourself strutting down the runway at this fashion show. You''re doing pretty well for your first show — until you trip over your sword and fall flat on your face. Darn it — now everyone''s gonna know this was your first time.', 'So, what is it you''re doing for the <kanji>first</kanji> time? A fashion <reading>show</reading> (しょ). Your special outfit and sword aren''t just to calm your nerves — they''re also what you''ll be showing off on the catwalk.', 'N3', NULL),
(785, 'Anyone can put a sword and knife in their mouth. But not everyone can make them separate while still in your mouth.', '"I bet you can''t separate the sword and knife," they say in an annoying voice. Alright, time for you to show them. Nobody bets you anything without you blindly trying to do it!', 'Why are you trying to make a sword and knife <kanji>separate</kanji> in your mouth? Because someone <reading>bets</reading> (べつ) you can''t do it.', 'N4', NULL),
(786, 'Imagine buying some rice. Now cut all the grains with a knife into smaller grains. Now sell them for two, three, four times the profit!', 'Picture all of your reeds full of grains in a row. The entire world will benefit from your crafty invention. And you''ll turn a great profit too, which certainly doesn''t hurt.', 'To make an even better <kanji>profit</kanji>, you decide to cut up <reading>ree</reading>ds (り) too. You stuff the grains in the reeds and sell that as "self-cooking" sets.', 'N3', NULL),
(787, 'Imagine you''re one of this woman''s minions, toiling away in the kitchen. The heat from the stoves is intense, and you''re hoping she doesn''t see the sweat dripping from your forehead into the food. Man, you''ve never toiled so hard in your life.

Note: There are a few other kanji with similar meanings. This one is used in words that refer to doing work, with a focus on making a concerted effort.', 'Secretly it''s this dog you toil for, not the woman. Try to picture what kind of dog it is. And imagine sneaking extra portions to it under the table when the woman isn''t looking. Yes, if it weren''t for this innocent dog, you''d have done with this life of toil!', 'As a servant you <kanji>toil</kanji> not just for the woman, but also for her <reading>do</reading>g (ど). Anything she gets, the dog gets too. That means double the toil for you. But hey, at least the dog is cute. ', 'N3', NULL),
(788, 'Imagine yourself as a powerful Viking. You have the power to take anything you want, pillaging and plundering to your heart’s content. Imagine all the things you would acquire with this sort of power.

Note - there are a few other kanji that have a similar meaning to this one. This kanji is used in words related to physical, or manual labor, with an emphasis on how darn tiring it is!', 'Picture this powerful Viking laying siege on a small fishing village and taking all the roe and stuffing it into his bearded face. So much labor went into harvesting that roe! Vikings are so mean!', 'It takes a lot of <kanji>labor</kanji> to harvest <reading>roe</reading> (ろう) so that’s what the Viking decides to take first! Roe is delicious, but it’s small and delicate. Vikings don’t labor for their own roe! They take it and eat it!', 'N3', NULL),
(789, 'Pretend you are a high ranking officer and imagine yourself giving orders to your underlings. Imagine all the power and influence you have. It feels good.', 'Students of American history are very familiar with the Mayflower. Recall learning about it in school whenever you see this kanji. Or look it up on Wikipedia if you don’t know about it. The Mayflower is a big deal!', 'You are the commanding officer on the <reading>May</reading>flower (めい). You give orders to colonize America. The orders spewing from your mouth hole determine the <kanji>fate</kanji> of the settlers and the fate of America and the fate of the world! You and the Mayflower have changed everything forever.', 'N3', NULL),
(790, 'You''re on top of the mountain and the entire coast is stretching out below. The shore goes as far as your eyes can see.', 'Sit down next to Gandhi and close your eyes. You can hear the water hitting the shore below. The coastal birds are screaming. You crack open an eye and see Gandhi still sitting perfectly still. ', 'Sitting up there, watching the <kanji>coast</kanji> with you, is <reading>Gan</reading>dhi (がん). The world famous Gandhi, is just hanging out up here with you, looking down at what you now realize must be a coast in India.', 'N2', NULL),
(791, 'Picture winter heading straight for you and frosting everything over. Now picture the leaves being released by the trees.', '"Release my home from this leaf prison!" you shout at the now bare trees.', 'The trees <kanji>release</kanji> all of their leaves onto your <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう). Slowly, you watch in horror as your home is consumed by the released leaves. They enter in through the windows, doors, and the chimney.', 'N3', NULL),
(792, 'Imagine yourself playing a never-ending game of blackjack out in the hot sun. You’re sticky, sweaty, and tired. But all you can do is continue to play your game of blackjack. It’s miserable.', 'Imagine your muck ashi, all dirty and muddy from walking on that old road from long ago. Feel the muck between the toes of your ashi, squishing with every step. Hear the sound the mud makes as it slides between your sweaty mucky toes.', 'Once you finally finish the game, you walk the long walk back home, in the sun, barefoot, on a dirt road that hasn''t been maintained since <kanji>long ago</kanji>. Your feet are all sweaty from being out in the sun, and walking on this dirt road gives you <reading>muck ashi</reading> (むかし). Your ashi (feet) are all mucky and muddy from the combination of dirt and sweat. Your muck ashi are really gross, man.', 'N3', NULL),
(793, 'Imagine being struck by the waves of a tsunami. Feel the force of their impact and the overall sensations on your skin. Those are some mighty waves.', 'The waves are basically punching you in the gut, forcing the air out of your lungs, and making you shout "Hah!"', 'When each <kanji>wave</kanji> hits your body, you shout, "<reading>Hah</reading> (は)!" It''s not like it''s on purpose. When the wave crashes into you, the wind gets knocked right out of you, making you say "Hah!"', 'N2', NULL),
(794, 'Picture yourself as this tsunami master. You’re like a water bender from Avatar, only more awesome. Feel the cold wet water pouring out of your fingertips as you create water out of nothing. Imagine what it would be like to have this power.', 'Imagine Chewbacca soaking wet. All his fur is matted down and he looks pretty depressed. He smells like wet dog too. Smell this smell in your nostrils as you imagine how pathetic this drenched Chewbacca feels.', 'If <reading>Chew</reading>bacca (ちゅう) was trying to attack you, you’d be able to defend yourself easily. Simply <kanji>pour</kanji> a tsunami on him and Chewbacca would surrender in a heartbeat. Chewbacca doesn’t like having water poured on him, especially not all the water from a tsunami.', 'N4', NULL),
(795, 'Picture the moon raising this ball of trash, giving it love and attention.', 'Watch as the little trash ball 行くs up into the sky and floats around its moon mama. If you raise your child right it will want to 行く wherever you go too.', 'The moon <kanji>nurture</kanji>s the trash ball until one day, it speaks! "<reading>行く</reading> (いく), 行く！" it says. It wants to go live with mom moon!', 'N3', NULL),
(796, 'Imagine finding something that suits your life and your style. You''re not just going to leave it there, right? No, you''re going to pick it up! Go on, use your fingers and pick up that thing.', 'You picked up the hero sword and now you''re the new hero. Time to go kill a dragon. Good luck, and try not to die, hero person!', 'You''re walking around looking for something to <kanji>pick up</kanji> when you see a sword in a rock. Yes, this will suit you. You pick up the sword and people jump out around you and tell you that you are the new <reading>hero</reading> (ひろ)! Uh oh. Maybe you shouldn''t have picked this up.', 'N2', NULL),
(797, 'Look down at your finger and the burned stumps where the rest of your fingers used to be. It hurts. And you''re sad. But at least you still have one finger left.', 'Picture sitting with a sheep. It''s right across from you, staring into your eyes. It reaches out its sheep finger hoof and you reach out with your finger. They touch. It''s magical.', 'Don''t worry, you aren''t the only one out there with just one <kanji>finger</kanji>, <reading>shee</reading>p (し) only have one finger too. I mean, they''re hooves, which are really just a single animal finger. It''s just big and kind of flat.', 'N3', NULL),
(798, 'Imagine how ridiculous a sheep would look all decked out in Western-style cowboy gear. Now imagine it riding a tsunami wave as it screams, "Baa~ baa~ baa~!"', 'Imagine yourself eating this Western-style apple pie yogurt. It tastes just like apple pie, only creamier and fruitier. Taste the sweet yogurt in your mouth as you imagine yourself scarfing some down.', 'After a whole day of riding this tsunami, the sheep is hankerin'' for some <kanji>Western-style</kanji> food, namely <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう). Yogurt is Western-style food, right? If it helps you can imagine the yogurt is flavored like apple pie, to make it more American and Western.', 'N4', NULL),
(799, 'Imagine a spirit appearing before you. It''s a god. You should be as humble as possible. Out of respect and also because you don''t want to be cursed or something.', 'If you don''t get down on your shins before god''s spirit, it could take them away. Even your jeans couldn''t protect you then. You don''t want to live a shin-less life, do you?', 'When you meet a <kanji>god</kanji>''s <kanji>spirit</kanji>, you need to get down on your <reading>shin</reading>s (しん). Get down on your ankles? No, not low enough. Knees? No, no, get your shins flat on the ground to show how humble you really are. Luckily, you''re wearing <reading>jean</reading>s (じん), so at least that will stop your poor shins getting chafed!', 'N3', NULL),
(800, 'Normally an hourglass has sand to count the seconds, but yours uses grains. Imagine just a few grains left, and watch them trickle down as you count a second, and then another second.', 'Your hourglass timer was a timer to specifically let you know when your B.O. was set to return.', 'After the final <kanji>second</kanji>s go by, your awful <reading>B.O.</reading> (びょう) appears.', 'N2', NULL),
(801, 'When you level up your Japanese skills, you''ll take this escalator to rank up too!', 'A teacher comes over and says, "Excuse me, this is advanced level cucumber studies. I don''t think you''re at this level yet... please take the escalator down and follow the threads to find your level."', 'You want to see what the highest <kanji>level</kanji> is, so you go up the wrong escalator. At the top you find a giant <reading>cu</reading>cumber (きゅう).', 'N1', NULL),
(802, 'Picture the bear on its scooter with the offending drop of water on its head following really closely behind your scooter. You can almost feel his bear breath on your neck!', 'Picture the bear chasing you on its scooter, trying to reach out and grab the obi off your waist. The drop of water on its head is jiggling from the exertion. Woah, it''s getting a little too close!', 'The bear is trying to <kanji>follow</kanji> you because it wants your <reading>o</reading>bi (お) as compensation for its injured pride. The obi is waving in the wind behind your scooter, and the bear is reaching out with its teeth to try and grab it.', 'N3', NULL),
(803, 'Imagine what the simple drunkard does, to start the war. They''re not in their right mind, and the centaurs (getting you ready for the reading mnemonic!) are an easily offended people. Go ahead and run through the simple drunkard''s actions toward the centaurs that start this war.', 'Imagine the war: humans vs centaurs. Tens of thousands clashing in battle.', 'The simple drunkard started a <kanji>war</kanji> with the <reading>cen</reading>taurs (せん)! This is especially unfortunate, because the centaurs are really good at <kanji>battle</kanji>s because they''re all automatically cavalry troops.', 'N3', NULL),
(804, 'Imagine yourself standing on your older brother. They push you off. So you do it again. You want to be on top, but your older brother doesn''t want you to. You compete for the top spot over and over again, throughout your whole life, because that''s what one does with their older brother.', 'Imagine yourself competing to stand on top of your older brother. Do you win? Do you get the one ticket to きょうと?', 'You <kanji>compete</kanji> with your older brother because only one of you may go to <reading>きょう</reading>と. Whoever stands on top the most gets to go to きょうと, so you must compete for a spot.', 'N2', NULL),
(805, 'Imagine putting a little drop of water over a root you want to grow. Doing this is good for the plant and also good for your soul.', 'Rowing even feels good on your arms and back. Probably just as good as all these roots will feel.', 'A <kanji>good</kanji> way to get a lot of drops of water for your roots is to buy a <reading>row</reading> boat (りょう). Then just row out to the middle of the lake and plonk all the roots right into the source!', 'N3', NULL),
(806, 'Just constructing this thing was an achievement. Feel proud like it was, well, an achievement.', 'Thanks こういち. Now everyone will know of your achievements around kanji learning and inside-out earth theory.

Hint: When you see 工 in a kanji it will often take the こう reading. Remember that, because it comes up quite a bit!', 'This <kanji>achievement</kanji> was made possible by <reading>こう</reading>いち, so of course this construction project is a giant statue of the man himself. What an achievement of a statue. What an achievement of an achievement.', 'N1', NULL),
(807, 'Picture a temple dedicated to a single cow. All of the people who come and worship here have a special place in their hearts for the cow.', 'You walk up to a statue of the special cow in the temple and see a collar around its neck that says "Tokugawa Moo Moo" on it. How... special!', 'This cow is <kanji>special</kanji> because it belonged to <reading>Toku</reading>gawa (とく) Ieyasu, the first shōgun of the Tokugawa shogunate and uniter of Japan! He''s a super important figure in Japanese history. You should absolutely look him up and read a bit about him (when you''re caught up on these lessons, that is).', 'N4', NULL),
(808, 'Imagine the leader looking around the rice paddy. She keeps saying, "Ugh, it would be convenient if I didn''t have to walk around out here looking for the treasure that''s going to make things more convenient for me. Ugh." Go ahead and say this phrase out loud, too, pretending to be the leader in the rice paddy. Feel the cold and the wet, making it less convenient for you.', 'Feel the happiness that the leader would feel at this revelation. It will make everything much more convenient for her, since she''s not the one doing all the manual labor. She won''t have to eat out of a bento, that''s for sure.', 'She finds a place to dig in the ground. It looks like there''s some fresh dirt here. Finally, the treasure of <kanji>convenience</kanji> that will make this leader''s life so much easier. She digs into the ground and pulls out a box. When she opens it, she finds out it''s a <reading>ben</reading>to (べん). "My god," she thinks. "This really will make my life more convenient. My people can take their lunches with them now instead of coming back to base to eat, which will waste way less time!"', 'N3', NULL),
(809, 'Picture this leader asking you (yet again) to work for them. "Can you move those heavy objects with all that power of yours?" Typical leader — as soon as they know you''re good at a certain kind of work, they ask you to do it all the time. 

Note: There are a few other kanji that have a similar meaning to this one. This kanji is used in words that refer to doing work, with a focus on movement or operating something.', 'Picture yourself receiving a doughnut as payment after you''ve been working. It''s delicious. Doughnuts are fantastic. Taste the fresh doughnut in your mouth as it crumbles and melts on your tongue. It''s the best doughnut you''ve ever had.', 'Where do you get the power to do all this <kanji>work</kanji>? From <reading>dou</reading>ghnuts (どう). You eat nothing but doughnuts. If you had no doughnuts, you couldn''t work. In fact, your leader pays you for your work… in doughnuts.', 'N3', NULL),
(810, 'Here''s an order: study your radicals.', 'Imagine an authority figure giving you this order as an official order. Ray guns don''t really exist, so you think it''s a pretty strange order.

Hint: when you see the <radical>orders</radical> radical, the reading will often be れい. The mnemonic will likely involve orders to get a ray gun of some kind.', 'Your <kanji>orders</kanji> are to get a <reading>ray</reading> (れい) gun.', 'N2', NULL),
(811, 'Put your hand over your heart, or on your wrist, or somewhere you can feel your pulse. Your heart is pumping blood through your body, hear the sound of your heart beat and try to picture and get an idea for how all the blood flows through your body.', 'Picture the majestic animal that is the bald eagle. Imagine the sound of an eagle screeching as it zooms through the air, flapping its wings in the wind. It’s pretty impressive.', 'The heart of an <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) is very strong. The person who knew this probably came up with the <kanji>idea</kanji> to make the bald eagle a symbol of the United States. Eagles are always flying around and doing things, so their hearts are very strong.', 'N4', NULL),
(812, 'What''s your favorite food? Imagine that, as you bite into it, tiny jets are released from inside the food to attack your tongue with flavor bombs, stimulating your sense of taste. ', 'Of course, if you''re vegetarian imagine the flavor of the meat being terrible. Up to you. Make the mnemonic work for your lifestyle.', 'And what has more <kanji>flavor</kanji> than a big slab of <reading>me</reading>at (み)? You love meat and its flavor nearly as much as you love the <kanji>taste</kanji> of its juice. So many good things to put in your mouth!', 'N4', NULL),
(726, 'Imagine waiting outside a temple. People are giving you looks. People think you''re loitering, not just waiting innocently.', 'Imagine adjusting your tie as you wait. Oh no, now even more people are staring at you. The tie thing is making you look even more suspicious!', 'As you <kanji>wait</kanji> outside the temple, you can''t help but adjust your <reading>tie</reading> (たい). When you wait you get nervous, and when you get nervous you fidget. So now you''re messing with your tie a whole bunch.', 'N4', NULL),
(813, 'Imagine yourself under great exertion. You''re attempting to use your power to move something and it''s really requiring a lot of exertion to move. Picture yourself doing this action and imagine how difficult it is.', 'Picture this Ben in your head. Ben always uses lots of exertion to do tasks. Imagine him using an unnecessary amount of power and exertion in carrying out normal everyday tasks.', 'Excuse me, I don’t think I’ve introduced myself yet. My name is <reading>Ben</reading> (べん) and I use powerful muscle <kanji>exertion</kanji> to accomplish every task. Ben knows what Ben wants and how to get it. Remember that, kids.', 'N4', NULL),
(814, 'Somehow your little garden is now being used by the Yoga King to teach yoga to a whole group of random people under this canopy... you have no idea how they got back here.', 'You''re not sure what else to do, so you end up serving tater tots in your garden all day to stretchy strangers.', 'Not only is this Yoga King taking over your <kanji>garden</kanji>, but he''s ordering you to serve food to his class. "You there, peasant, fetch these people your freshest <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tots from this puny garden to give them strength."', 'N3', NULL),
(815, 'Imagine trying to live without a heart. You couldn’t. Your blood stops flowing and your body shuts down. You would no longer be able to draw breath.', 'Imagine smelling this smelly sock. Imagine the smelly stench of the dirty sock wafting up into your nostrils and filling your lungs with its odor. Gross.', 'You know how when people are unconscious and their <kanji>breath</kanji> slows or comes to a stop, they use smelling salts to revive them? Well a smelly <reading>sock</reading> (そく) can work just as well. Socks get really stinky and their stench is powerful enough to revive someone who isn’t drawing breath.', 'N3', NULL),
(816, 'Imagine coming to your senses after shooting up a block of tofu for stress relief or whatever you were doing. Yeah, you gotta get away from tofu for a while… It''s definitely time for a relaxing trip.', 'Imagine soaking up the sun on the beaches of Rio de Janeiro. Rio has its fair share of guns, but at least there''s a lot less tofu around these parts.', 'So, where do you go on your <kanji>trip</kanji>? To <reading>Rio</reading> (りょ). You had to go way down south to get as far away as possible from tofu. Ahh, nothing like a nice trip to Rio!', 'N4', NULL),
(817, 'Roots are roots, ね.', 'Picture a family of condors nestled at the bottom of a tree in its roots. Then a huge machine comes over and mows them all down as it cuts all the trees off their roots. Darn you humans! Do you have to ruin everything?!', 'Tree <kanji>root</kanji>s are the perfect roosting place for <reading>con</reading>dors (こん). Unfortunately, living on roots is also why they''re always endangered. Condors can be killed pretty easily living on the ground on roots like this.', 'N2', NULL),
(818, 'As the tsunami recedes, watch as all of the trash comes streaming out of the river and crowds the bank.', 'Now Ryu is punching and kicking and tatsumaki senpukyaku-ing all of the trash into burnable and non-burnable piles. But the stream doesn''t end. Now he''s trying to get his other Street Fighter pals to help him beat it back!', 'Next to the trash <kanji>stream</kanji> you suddenly see <reading>Ryu</reading> (りゅう) from Street Fighter and he looks pissed. He''s furious that so many people throw their trash in the river and it causes streams like this.', 'N3', NULL),
(819, 'I’m sure you’ve heard many theories on how dinosaurs went extinct. Well, this is the real story. Imagine this poor triceratops and all his friends being extinguished by this powerful tsunami, sweeping them up into the moon where there''s no air to breathe or leaves to eat.', 'Watch the Shougun''s eyes light up as an actor dressed like a tsunami crashes into an actor dressed as a triceratops and all of the lights on the stage are extinguished. Spooky...', 'This famous event where almost all life was <kanji>extinguish</kanji>ed is reenacted every year for the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). He''s got a bit of an unhealthy obsession with extinguishing life...', 'N3', NULL),
(820, 'Picture a clown and all the ridiculous funny things they do. Imagine the clown throwing a pie at someone''s face and how funny that is. Chuckle to yourself and remember this kanji.', 'This clown has served its purpose. Wave your hand in the air and say bye-bye to this worthless clown.', 'When the clown stops being funny, it''s time to say <reading>bye</reading> (ばい). At first, the clown <kanji>double</kanji>d the leader''s entertainment, but now the clown is just annoying. It''s time to say bye-bye.', 'N2', NULL),
(821, 'Imagine a shellfish opening and closing its mouth, talking to you and telling you what a proud member of its family it is. It''s a strange sight, but picture it in your mind anyway.', 'Imagine trying to go to the shellfish inn. Imagine all the members-only signs telling you that you can''t come in. Womp womp.', 'If you were a <kanji>member</kanji> of the same shellfish family, you''d be able to go to the special shellfish <reading>inn</reading> (いん). Every species has a members-only inn.', 'N4', NULL),
(822, 'Imagine the mountain as small as you''d like. Maybe it''s only barely sticking out of the sea, and the bird is having trouble standing on it. Whatever you do, make sure you think of it as an island, possibly owned by the bird.', 'The Sheep Mama is a huge, majestic sheep with magical powers. She kicks the bird off the island with her hooves, and the bird squawks and flies away as fast as it can.', 'But this <kanji>island</kanji> doesn''t only belong to the bird. In fact, the bird shouldn''t be on this island, because it belongs to the <reading>She</reading>ep <reading>Ma</reading>ma (しま), who is like Mother Nature but for sheep. The all-powerful Sheep Mama comes to kick the bird off the island, because this place is only for sheep! All hail the Sheep Mama.', 'N2', NULL),
(823, 'Imagine being at a moonlit festival and waiting in line for the stool and jackhammer. You don''t want to eat candy apples or ride rides, you want to wait your turn to try out the jackhammer (while comfortably seated on a stool, of course).', 'Imagine your cyborg going to this festival. Everyone here has some type of cyborg enhancement, like robotic arms, legs, or braces. Your cyborg is really excited to be around its people and play with jackhammers.', 'This <kanji>festival</kanji> is for <reading>cy</reading>borgs (さい) only. Picture the cyborg you made getting excited that you''re taking it to the festival. That''s why all of the activities have to do with heavy machinery. The rides are mostly just cyborgs letting other people ride them, but it''s still pretty fun.', 'N2', NULL),
(824, 'Picture yourself, standing tall in the light of the early morning. You hold your head up high, open to your bookmark, and read the name of the chapter aloud: "Chapter 42: The Most Important Number."', 'Picture yourself in a line with other authors, standing in the early morning, waiting to read chapters from your respective books. You really hope the Shougun likes yours. If he does, you''ll be famous! If he doesn''t, you might be beheaded.', 'You''re practicing this specific <kanji>chapter</kanji> because you''ve been asked to read it for the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). There will be a bunch of other authors reading chapters of their books to him too, and you want to make a good impression.', 'N2', NULL),
(825, 'Imagine a line of bamboo shoots arranged from oldest to youngest. They look like pipe organ pipes, or a xylophone, and now you need to count them.', 'Now they''re in descending order already, and you can count them nice and easy thanks to your dye method.', 'To remember you need to use the <kanji>ordinal number prefix</kanji> before you count these bamboo, you <reading>dye</reading> (だい) them.', 'N1', NULL),
(826, 'Imagine just a ton of someones and buildings all packed together in a small area. More than a village or town or city. With enough someones and buildings, you end up with a metropolis.', 'Imagine this huge metropolis. Now imagine every building just being a toe. Tall skyscraper toes. Smaller house toes. Wide apartment building toes. And then the people walking around. They are toes, too. Toes walking around and going into toe buildings, with toe cars and toe dogs. If you imagine the toe metropolis deeply enough, it should be weird enough to burn into your memory.', 'In this <kanji>metropolis</kanji> everything is a <reading>toe</reading> (と). The someones are toes, walking around. The buildings are toes, too.', 'N3', NULL),
(827, 'Picture a massive 1 ton weight. It’s heavy. You can’t even begin to move it without some sort of powerful machine to help you. Imagine yourself trying to move it with just your bare hands.', 'Okay, so you don’t have enough power to move this doughnut as is. In order to move it, you’re going to have to use your superhuman power of eating. Once you eat enough of it, the remainder will be easy to move. Imagine yourself digging into this massive doughnut and taste its fresh baked goodness on your tongue. It’s mighty tasty.', 'Remember that 1 ton weight? Well, actually it’s a <reading>dou</reading>ghnut (どう). A huge, massive, 1 ton doughnut. It’s super heavy and hard to move, but it’s delicious.', 'N4', NULL),
(828, 'Imagine this mustache entrepreneur, standing at his merchandise stand, selling his wares. He looks quite dignified, legs and all.', 'Now all of the merchandise belongs to the Shougun. The mustache has nothing left to sell, but at least he''s rich!', 'The mustache is selling his <kanji>merchandise</kanji> so well that even the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) notices. He leans down really close to the standing mustache man''s merchandise and stays very still. Then he snaps his fingers and his servants come rushing out to buy it all!', 'N3', NULL),
(829, 'You don''t want a bunch of evil in your heart, do you? No, I didn''t think so.', 'Acupuncture was originally invented in Asia a long, long time ago, to stop the bad chi from going through your heart.', 'Luckily, people in Asia know how to get the <kanji>evil</kanji> <kanji>bad</kanji>ness out of your heart with <reading>acu</reading>puncture (あく).', 'N4', NULL),
(830, 'This guy headed your direction with his gun doesn’t look friendly at all. Hear the sound of the bowstring as you nock your arrow and prepare your shot to defend yourself. Then imagine the sound of everyone in your tribe doing the same.', 'You remember what a bosozoku is, right? We wrote about them on Tofugu a while ago. They’re bike gang members and the “zoku” in bosozoku means tribe. Remember that article when you see this kanji.', 'When he starts to get closer, you notice that this guy is a boso<reading>zoku</reading> (ぞく) bike gang member. Bosozoku means “running <kanji>tribe</kanji>” and this guy is running right at you. With a gun. So gather your own tribe behind you and prepare to defend yourself against this bosozoku member.', 'N4', NULL),
(831, 'Picture this tsunami rushing past the tree you managed to climb up. It''s a deep one, way higher than your forehead, but you''re safe here. Legs exhausted, sure, but safe and sound.', 'Imagine all the debris floating in the shin-deep water now, bumping up against your shins. Probably hurts, considering how sensitive shins are.', 'You wait until the tsunami is only as <kanji>deep</kanji> as your <reading>shin</reading> (しん). Then you climb down the tree and wade home in the shin-deep water.', 'N3', NULL),
(832, 'Imagine the king holding a sphere in his hand, and looking at it. It''s a green sphere, and that is because...', 'Now imagine the king requesting a cucumber sphere, and holding it up to look at it. If you want, have him take a bite out of the sphere, and say "this sphere is a cucumber?" Imagine the taste of it. Imagine the smell, and its bumpy cucumber skin.', 'The <kanji>sphere</kanji> is a <reading>cu</reading>cumber (きゅう) sphere. A strange sphere shaped cucumber.', 'N3', NULL),
(833, 'Picture all those juveniles standing on top of buildings in your village, being bored, and doing juvenile things.', ' Imagine looking up to the top of the buildings in your village. Thousands of juveniles, staring down at you and everyone else, eating doughnuts. The crumbs falling down on top of you. But, the doughnuts smell good, at least. Maybe you try to catch some crumbs.', 'Mostly, though, the  <kanji>juvenile</kanji>s are standing on top of village buildings, eating <reading>dou</reading>ghnuts (どう).', 'N2', NULL),
(835, 'All buildings have white marks that you can compare with the rest to see where the floors are. ', 'Kayaks working at desks. Kayaks that are desks. Kayaks walking around checking on other kayaks. The kayak floor is quite odd. It''s odd there is a floor only about kayaks.', 'One <kanji>floor</kanji> is just all <reading>kay</reading>aks (かい). You''re looking at this building, and every other <kanji>story</kanji> is normal, but that one floor is kayak after kayak after kayak.', 'N2', NULL),
(836, 'Picture the entire roof covered in ice. It''s supposed to be spring right now, but it''s too cold for the ice to melt off your roof.', 'The samurai isn''t doing a good job stopping the cold, and soon he and his sword have frozen into your ice roof. So now it''s cold and you have a dead samurai stuck to your house. Great.', 'You can''t stand the <kanji>cold</kanji> anymore, so you hire a <reading>samu</reading>rai (さむ) to come try to hack the ice off of your roof with his sword. You stand outside shivering, and watching as the samurai tries to stab the ice off of your roof.', 'N3', NULL),
(838, 'Imagine you''re this someone, standing out in the hot sun. Feel the hot rays beating down on you. You''re getting all sweaty and sticky from the heat now, ugh. ', 'Feel the hot rays of sunlight beating down on you from two directions. "Ah, two hot suns is more than I can take," you gasp as your body slowly melts into a human puddle of sweat.', 'One sun was <kanji>hot</kanji> enough, but then a second one shows up. "<reading>Ah, two</reading> (あつ)!" you scream. If you were sweaty and sticky from the heat of just one sun, imagine what this second one is going to do to you. Ah, two hot suns!', 'N1', NULL),
(839, 'Imagine looking at the moon and seeing a crab on it and thinking: "oh, it''s the crab moon <kanji>time period</kanji>."', 'Imagine a key with a little crab moon on it, denoting the Crab Moon period of time. Then, go around and lock everything with it and watch thousands of crabs trying to get into everything. Luckily, they can''t, because you have a special key that locks things so crabs can''t get in. When the Crab Moon time period is over, you can use your key to unlock everything.', 'When the crab moon <kanji>period of time</kanji> is upon us, we must take a special <reading>key</reading> (き) and lock everything in such a way that crabs cannot get in.', 'N3', NULL),
(840, 'After four or five attempts, you''ve finally planted a perfectly straight tree. Pat yourself on the back for a job well done.', 'After planting your tree and failing again for the 100th time, you stand there in total shock. How could it be this difficult?', 'You <kanji>plant</kanji> and re-plant your tree over and over, utterly <reading>shock</reading>ed (しょく) at how hard it is to plant it right. You thought planting trees was easy, so this is a real shock to you.', 'N2', NULL),
(841, 'Stop what you''re doing and look into the rice box. Nope, it''s definitely full of teeth. There''s a grain or two of rice, but it''s like 98% teeth.', 'On second thought, I don''t need to explain my box of teeth to you. Hah! Figure it out yourself.', 'Why do I have a rice box full of <kanji>teeth</kanji>? <reading>Hah</reading> (は)! Wouldn''t you like to know. Hah! I definitely have an answer that isn''t weird or creepy…', 'N3', NULL),
(842, 'Right after the tsunami, this plate was wet, but the sun is warming and drying the plate. Imagine looking at the plate and seeing the beads of water evaporate off it as it warms up. Feel the warm steam on your face as this happens.', 'Imagine yourself frying onions on this plate warmed by the sun. Smell the onion smell as the onion fumes get into your eyes and your sinuses. It makes your eyes water as you are very sensitive to onions.', 'The plate starts to get so <kanji>warm</kanji> that you could fry an <reading>on</reading>ion (おん) on it. Who doesn’t love fried onions? That plate sure is warm enough, so start cookin’ ‘em!', 'N2', NULL),
(843, 'Picture the harbor. You''re the only one there. The wind is whipping violently and all of the boats docked around you are being bounced around. You can see the tsunami rising out of the ocean and coming for you and the harbor.', 'Well this sucks. You wanted to enjoy being alone together with the tsunami and the harbor, but now こういち is here ruining your peace. He''s floating around in the harbor like everything isn''t about to be destroyed, sipping on a fancy drink in his inner tube.', 'As you look around at the <kanji>harbor</kanji> in your last moments you suddenly see someone in the water. It''s <reading>こう</reading>いち! He''s sitting in an inner tube in the harbor waiting for the tsunami too.', 'N3', NULL),
(844, 'Picture the boiling hot tsunami water as it comes for you. Psh, this will be easy.', 'You decided to live in a yurt because of the easy access it provides to hot water. They''re so mobile!', 'As the <kanji>hot water</kanji> comes, you just lift up the bottom of the <reading>yu</reading>rt (ゆ) you live in, and it comes flowing in. All of the cups, pots, and barrels you laid on your floor are now full of hot water.', 'N2', NULL),
(845, 'Imagine this tent of beans. Make it huge, bulging with beans! Now, get climbing. Feel the beans on the other side of the tent''s wall.', 'It''s beautiful! It''s breathtaking! All your climbing makes this view of とうきょう even more worthwhile!', 'You <kanji>climb</kanji> the bean tent, and from this vantage point you can see <reading>とう</reading>きょう in the distance. ', 'N3', NULL),
(846, 'Try to picture what this king is wearing as he comes down the slide and arrives at the bottom. He can surely afford to wear the finest garments — plus, his horns give him all sorts of options for accessorizing. ', 'Imagine the sound of horrible chalk squeaks as the king furiously sketches all sorts of crazy outfits on his massive royal chalkboard — all the while muttering "What to wear, what to wear?"', 'How does this king decide what to <kanji>wear</kanji> each day? He uses <reading>chalk</reading> (ちゃく) to sketch out his wardrobe ideas on a chalkboard. This helps him <kanji>arrive</kanji> at a conclusion faster.', 'N4', NULL),
(847, 'Picture yourself shooting this arrow. Imagine yourself nocking the arrow to the bow, and pulling the string back. That bean plant is not short enough, and you''re going to do something about it.', 'Imagine yourself being out in the sun for so long that you get an amazing tan. Your skin feels warm all over from all the sun you were getting throughout the day. Feel the warmth coming off of your tanned skin.', 'All this shooting of arrows to make the bean plants <kanji>short</kanji> is taking a long time. Since you''re out in the sun, you''re starting to get a <reading>tan</reading> (たん). After the day is finally over, your tan is really, really dark.', 'N2', NULL),
(848, 'It makes sense for a big open field to have been what was there before the village was made.', 'It was sad that they had to destroy the natural habitat of the Yankees (a field) to put up the village.', 'On this <kanji>field</kanji>, the <reading>Ya</reading>nkees (や) used to play.', 'N4', NULL),
(849, 'Imagine these geysers and the white water shooting from the springs under the ground. Now imagine yourself standing over one when the water shoots out. You’d get super wet and probably knocked over due to the force of the spring water. Imagine this happening to yourself.', 'Imagine this centaur pushing its way out from the hole in the ground, plugging the water as it squeezes through the hole. Why was there a centaur in the spring? Once it gets out, imagine the centaur standing in front of the spring as it bubbles up and starts shooting water out again. This spring belongs to the centaur.', 'From the <kanji>spring</kanji>, a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん) emerges.', 'N2', NULL),
(852, 'Just remember that this "suit" means "to suit you" - not the thing you wear, okay!', 'Imagine you two standing next to each other, posing for a photograph. You two suit each other well. Grandpa ごういち with his gray beard, and you with your [well, you have to fill in this part by yourself].', 'You need someone who will <kanji>suit</kanji> you. Someone who can <kanji>join</kanji> you on your life journey. So you call Grandpa <reading>ごう</reading>いち.

Note: For the ごう reading mnemonics, we use Grandpa ごういち because こういち is used for the こう reading. So just remember that ごう comes from Koichi''s grandpa ごういち.', 'N3', NULL),
(1594, 'He''s already eyeing you down, loitering this close to my skin. Might wanna... back up a little.', 'Imagine him turning from a frown to a broad smile as he smells the fragrant curry you''ve brought.', '<kanji>He</kanji> may get grumpy if you loiter around my skin, but a well-made <reading>curry</reading> (かれ) always brightens his mood. So give him some curry.

', 'N3', NULL),
(1890, 'This version of <kanji>I</kanji> is more boyish. It''s a sort of young man/boy way of saying "I," though you''ll hear women use it too sometimes (it''s a teensy bit tomboyish though).', 'I am beaucoup I am. (Is that a pun? Did I do it right?) I dunno. Just say ぼく a lot and try to connect all of your approximate knowledges of languages together. Merci ぼく from ぼく.', 'You use the word <kanji>I</kanji> a lot. You say it so much, you just start referring to yourself with the french <reading>beaucoup</reading> (ぼく) for a lot. Because you love yourself a lot.', 'N1', NULL),
(1926, 'Imagine yourself being really, really tired. Then look at your skin. It''s all blotchy and sickly looking.', 'Feel all the heat (ひ) emanating from your body. It makes you realize how very, very tired you are.', 'Why so <kanji>tired</kanji>? Your body is <reading>hea</reading>ted (ひ) up from exercising way too much. You should let that heat cool down so your skin starts looking better. ', 'N3', NULL),
(859, 'Imagine having your mouth bashed into a gate. It’s going to hurt. Imagine how painful this would be and how problematic it is. Imagine that the only question in your mind is how to get to the hospital as quickly as you possibly can.', 'Monsters are scary. Maybe you’ve had a dream where you were being chased by one. Remember how scary that is when you see this kanji.', 'The reason you ran mouth first into this gate was because you were being chased by a <reading>mon</reading>ster (もん). A monster in and of itself is a big enough <kanji>problem</kanji> as is, let alone smashing your mouth into a gate. You poor, hapless soul.', 'N4', NULL),
(860, 'Back in the old days a lodge had to be huuuuge so when a leader brought a hundred people through town they''d have a place to stay. ', 'Imagine being someone staying at the lodge as they came in and everything shook around you. This lodge would probably last through an earthquake too!', 'The <kanji>lodge</kanji> <reading>shook</reading> (しゅく) as the hundred people came tumbling in through the door. It shook, but it didn''t fall over. The roof stayed firmly in place, and everyone got to sleep under it okay.', 'N3', NULL),
(861, 'Can you picture a tree without eyes and a heart imagining something? Contemplating? Probably not. To imagine or contemplate, a tree definitely needs eyes to observe and a heart to experience feelings.', 'Use <em>your</em> soul to imagine what this tree is imagining with <em>its</em> soul. Is it contemplating the deep mysteries of the universe? Is it imagining where that squirrel has gone off to?', 'If a <radical>tree</radical> can <kanji>imagine</kanji>, it likely has a <reading>soul</reading> (そう). Its soul is what makes it possible for the tree to <kanji>contemplate</kanji> things, you see?', 'N3', NULL),
(862, 'The feeling is kind of hard to describe. On the one hand it''s warm and loving, but on the other hand it''s also kind of wet and slobbery.', 'Just be careful not to let in all the Genghis Khan feelings. The confidence and motivation are good, but the feeling of wanting to wantonly slaughter your enemies is probably one you ought to repress.', 'Overall, though, it''s a good <kanji>feeling</kanji> — so good that you feel like Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん)! With this canine lovingly slobbering on your heart, you feel like you could go out and conquer the world!', 'N3', NULL),
(863, 'Picture all the things you''ve arranged correctly ahead of winter — logs bundled for the fire, blankets for the night, and yourself, all bundled up in your neatly organized home — just as the first snowflakes begin to fall.', 'Last winter you tripped and fell on a saber you''d left lying around. Ouch, lesson learned. That''s when your passion for organizing things in bundles started. You know better than anyone the value of arranging your sabers as safely and tidily as possible.', 'But the most important things to <kanji>arrange</kanji> and <kanji>organize</kanji> are your <reading>sa</reading>bers (せい). You have an extensive saber collection that you simply must organize before winter arrives.', 'N1', NULL),
(864, 'Imagine a weird sound coming from the sun. Maybe a rumble, or something like a balloon deflating? Then everything goes totally dark. Welp, guess you''ll be living in the dark for the rest of your life. ', 'And because it''s so dark, the anchovy taste is all you can concentrate on. Imagine that fishy, salty taste on your tongue, the little softened bones sticking between your teeth. Ugh.', 'Now wandering around your house in the <kanji>dark</kanji> you start to feel hungry, so you reach blindly for some food and take a bite. Yuck! It''s <reading>an</reading>chovies (あん). You don''t even like anchovies, how did they get in your house? Hmm… Dark forces are at work here…', 'N3', NULL),
(865, 'Imagine what Sheep-sama must have done to become such important and distinguished sheep royalty. Some pretty amazing things, probably, especially for a sheep.', 'Imagine playing in the water with Sheep-sama in the summer. It''s crazy hot outside in the summer, so going in the water feels nice. Imagine the coolness of the refreshing water envelop your body.', 'This sheep with the <kanji>formal name ender</kanji> only likes to play outside in the water during the <reading>summer</reading> (さま). Summer is the best season for this fancy sheep. The other seasons just can''t compare.', 'N3', NULL),
(866, 'Picture this majestic tree bridge laid on its side, beckoning you into heaven with its mustachioed mouth. "Cross over!" it says in its bridge voice. Do… do you just step over the mouth? Can you wipe your shoes on the mustache, like it''s a bristly doormat to heaven?', 'Hear the bridge shouting "HASH BROWNS" in its bridge voice. You stuff some hash into its mouth and it starts to chew loudly. Oh, ew… Little hash brown bits are getting stuck in its mustache. Fortunately, the bridge lets you across. Unfortunately, it turns out all of heaven smells like hash browns.', 'Actually, to get across this <kanji>bridge</kanji> you have to give it some <reading>hash</reading> (はし) browns. That''s what the bridge''s mouth is for. Hash browns to get across the heaven bridge? Small price to pay, really.', 'N2', NULL),
(868, 'Still, imagine pulling Wolverine from X-Men out of the water with that thread, and seeing him looking kinda green. Pretty green, but he could be greener. We have to go greener! We have to make the Hulk green with envy! So you put him back in the water, again and again…', 'These Marvel crossovers are getting ridiculous.', 'As the flailing Wolverine begins to turn even more <kanji>green</kanji>, he calls out, "HELP <reading>ME DORY</reading> (みどり)!" Me, Dory? No, not you. He''s calling out to that fish from Finding Nemo, another Disney IP. "Help me Dory, I''m green!" Then Dory leaps out of the water to bite through the thread and save Wolverine… THE END. Fade to green.', 'N2', NULL),
(869, 'You''re absolutely obsessed with pointing your thread towards the east, so all you do is practice. Practice, practice, practice.', 'Everything in your life is a rental, so you better get to practicing. Your house, your car, your friends — all of these rentals need to be paid off! ', 'You do all of this <kanji>practice</kanji> to try and afford all of your <reading>ren</reading>tals (れん). One day, all of this practice will pay for all of your rentals.', 'N2', NULL),
(870, 'Picture all of the people at the temple reciting poems together. You join in too, and it''s a really powerful moment!', 'You turn to the person next to you and realize you have to say everything in poem form.

Excuse me
I''m not a creep
so hear my plea
is that a sheep?', 'As you recite a long <kanji>poem</kanji> at the temple you see a <reading>shee</reading>p (し) in the crowd. It''s opening and closing its mouth like it wants to say the poem too.', 'N1', NULL),
(871, 'Imagine taking a gold coin and scrubbing it with some soap and water. As you scrub, you slowly see that it''s really silver underneath. Dang it, silver again!', 'Guinea pigs are to silver as normal pigs are to truffles.', 'If you want to hunt for some <kanji>silver</kanji> for yourself, all you need is a <reading>guin</reading>ea (ぎん) pig. Guinea pigs can smell silver, so get a couple and set them loose in the woods and they''ll lead you straight to it.', 'N4', NULL),
(872, 'Everywhere you go, you overhear someone talking about your stylish geoduck-patterned jacket. It''s the only topic anyone wants to talk about.', 'Imagine how annoying it is to constantly be asked about the dye you used in your jacket. You''d really like to change the topic — some might even say you''re <em>dye</em>-ing to change it…', 'After a few days, the <kanji>topic</kanji> changes from the cool geoduck design to what kind of <reading>dye</reading> (だい) you used to get those real-to-life geoduck colors. ', 'N4', NULL),
(873, 'Picture a public building, like a museum or a library, and look at the roof. There''s a bear eating the roof!', 'Imagine Genghis Khan signing something and giving a speech talking about how important it is that all bears are able to eat the roofs of public buildings, as a bear in the background eats the roof.', '<kanji>Public building</kanji>s must make their roofs available for eating because of a law passed by Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん).', 'N4', NULL),
(874, 'Imagine the horse and the shrimp sitting next to each other on a small bench. There is nobody else at the train station. It is awkward.', 'Instead of saying "a key" he says "eh key." How strange. Imagine the station master saying it like this with his weird accent. You ask him if he has "a key" and he replies with, "What is a key?" Then you say "eh key." He totally understands you now.', 'The train pulls into the <kanji>station</kanji>. They walk up to the train doors, but they don''t open. "Perhaps <reading>a key</reading> (えき) would help?" asks the station master, as he goes over to open the door to the train.', 'N4', NULL),
(875, 'Imagine a hundred million free puppies. That’s a lot. Imagine playing with all of them. You’d be pretty happy. Unless you hate puppies. Then you’d be pretty unhappy, I imagine.', 'Picture this cult leader gathering a hundred million people into Oklahoma. It’d be awfully crowded. Imagine yourself being stifled by the bodies of a hundred million other people.', 'The cult leader has his headquarters in <reading>Ok</reading>lahoma (おく). Are there a <kanji>hundred million</kanji> people in Oklahoma? No, probably not. But hey, this leader is shooting for the stars. First Oklahoma, then the universe!', 'N2', NULL),
(876, 'Imagine having all these big products. How will you contain them all!? Imagine yourself stuffing all of these big products into their respective containers.', 'Imagine the size of these big keys you have to use to lock these containers. It’s a really big key. Picture yourself straining to turn the key in the lock.', 'Once you get the big products into a <kanji>container</kanji>, you then lock it with a <reading>key</reading> (き). You wouldn’t want these products to bust out of their containers, so it’s best to lock them up with a key.', 'N1', NULL),
(877, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Picture the sheep samurai. Maybe draw him on a piece of paper. He''s a sheep wearing wool armor, and he has ram horns on the hilt of his katana.', 'The most famous <kanji>samurai</kanji> in Japanese history was the illustrious <reading>shee</reading>p (し) samurai. He wasn''t JUST a sheep who learned the ways of the sword, he would use the wool from the sheep enemies he killed to create his armor!', 'N1', NULL),
(878, 'Imagine ladling up a nice big scoop of rice and then seeing the little fee ladle next to it. Ugh. The material fee.', 'That''s a lot of material you have to give up every time you want to eat some rice. Maybe you should take this row boat oar ladle and row out into the ocean where no one can tax you.', 'One day, the <kanji>fee</kanji> ladle is suddenly the size of <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat oars! What! You''re supposed to pay a row boat sized fee now?!', 'N4', NULL),
(879, 'This is not the best signpost you''ve ever seen, but it''s better than nothing at all, you suppose.', 'Press it a few more times and hear the sound in your head. Also spell it out in kana as you hear it as a nice little additional visual.', 'As you look at this weird <kanji>signpost</kanji> you notice a button. You press it because it will supposedly tell you more about these dangerous helicopters and jackhammers. Instead, it just yells out in a loud obnoxious voice: <reading>HEYO</reading> (ひょう)!!', 'N1', NULL),
(880, 'You didn''t just cut the tree down, you stabbed it and sliced it and killed it to death.', 'Here''s that little girl Satsuki again. Remember how you imagined her last time? Try to have a picture of her in your mind so these さつ kanji are easier to remember.', 'But after the <kanji>kill</kanji>, <reading>Satsu</reading>ki (さつ) appears and yells at you. She likes trees — remember how she protected one in the 札 mnemonic? — so Satsuki''s pretty upset you''d kill one like that.', 'N3', NULL),
(881, 'Picture a weird-looking, misshapen crescent moon casting its light onto the scene of a dog boiling up everything you''d find in nature. Trees, sticks, mud, little animals. All of that is being boiled by this dog by the light of a weird-looking moon. ', 'Picture the dog kneeling in front of the pot of concentrated nature and breathing deeply as it practiced Zen. You kind of wish you could join it, considering how peaceful it looks.', 'Destroying <kanji>nature</kanji> like this is the dog''s way of practicing <reading>Zen</reading> (ぜん) Buddhism. As it breathes in the fumes from all of the different pieces of nature being boiled down, the dog gets really zen and chill.', 'N3', NULL),
(882, 'Even if you don''t have a womb, just picture one anyway: it''s turning around in a circle faster and faster, as the heat bubbles and boils in you. You can feel this heat spread through your entire body as you burn up with a fever.', 'These nets aren''t making the fever heat go away, but they have made the spinning stop at least.', 'The <kanji>heat</kanji> from your <kanji>fever</kanji> is making your insides and your head and your entire body spin and boil, until your doctor throws some <reading>nets</reading> (ねつ) around you. You were flailing around and they couldn''t get you to stop, so they had to catch you in a series of nets.', 'N3', NULL),
(883, 'Think about it. Every grocery store has the fruit section right at the front, wouldn’t you say?', 'Picture yourself driving a car and salivating over the fruit waiting for you in the fruit section. Imagine your favorite fruit as you put the pedal to the metal in your awesome car.', 'To get to the fruit <kanji>section</kanji>, you have to go to the store. To get to the store you have to drive a <reading>ca</reading>r (か). Driving a car is the fastest way to get to the fruit section at the store.', 'N2', NULL),
(884, 'Imagine a game at a fair where you have to throw a shellfish into the mouth of a hungry Viking. You want this prize just as much as the Viking wants his shellfish.  ', 'You want this prize. You want to meet the Shougun. Soon you''ll both be eating shellfish together!', 'The <kanji>prize</kanji> you''re working so hard to win is lunch with the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). This is a pretty huge prize, considering all you''re doing is knocking something over.', 'N2', NULL),
(885, 'Imagine this wheel you made out of a hat, some ground (presumably to keep it all together), and a bookshelf. You install it on your car and hope for the best. This is why you should buy wheels from professional wheel makers.', 'A ring-shaped wheel is better than nothing. Hopefully it will get you where you need to go.', 'Your <kanji>wheel</kanji> is... generally <reading>rin</reading>g (りん) shaped, so that''s good.', 'N2', NULL),
(886, 'Your angel self says you should choose the safest scooter. The devil self says safety is for suckers, and you should buy one that''s loud and dangerous and covered in flames.', 'The angel self says, "I choose this centaur scooter because of its strong arms." The devil self says, "I choose this scooter because it looks like a badass horse monster, wooh!"', 'Suddenly your selfs both gasp and <kanji>choose</kanji> a scooter in front of you that looks like a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん). The scooter is designed like a human on the top, the bars are its arms, and the bottom with the seat and the wheels are all horse. This centaur scooter is what you choose to ride.', 'N3', NULL),
(887, 'Imagine looking at yourself in the mirror only to see you''re made of gold. It would be pretty shocking. Imagine how surprised you would be.', 'Picture yourself in front of a mirror, except your reflection is Chairman Kaga Takeshi. (Look up his picture if it helps.) Imagine not recognizing yourself at first, how weird that would feel, then suddenly remembering: "Kaga… me!"', 'As you look into the <kanji>mirror</kanji> you think, "<reading>Kaga</reading>… <reading>me</reading> (かがみ)?" You always forget that you''re Chairman Kaga from the Iron Chef TV show. That''s why you say "Kaga… me?" every time you look in the mirror.', 'N1', NULL),
(889, 'Imagine eating a fish fin with sheep wool on it. It tastes fishy like the ocean but the wool tastes like clothes. It''s probably the worst thing ever, but try to imagine you''re tasting it right now.', 'Remember how bad those sheep fins tasted? Now imagine eating your favorite yogurt flavor instead. This yogurt is delicious.', 'The best way to <kanji>foster</kanji> a better relationship with these foster kids would be to give them some <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう). Maybe they can dip the sheep fins in the yogurt to help mask the strange taste. Yogurt is also very nutritious, so it''s a good supplement to the foster kids'' diet.', 'N1', NULL),
(891, 'Start with a blue soul. What feeling is that? Now a red soul. What emotion is that? Go through as many colors as you can think of, focusing on the fact that each color represents a different emotion or feeling.', 'Maybe he was just having a bad day, but that still really hurt… and now your soul’s all blue too. Seems you’ll have to walk around with a blue soul for the next few days.', 'Why is your soul <kanji>feeling</kanji> blue? Because <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう), the big local farmhand, was extremely mean to you. ', 'N3', NULL),
(892, 'When you do a bad thing you should apologize. And saying everyone''s private body measurements is definitely something you should not have done. So it''s apology time.', '"What do we say?" the shaman says, poking you in the back. "Ugh, I''m sorry I told everyone how tiny your socks are, I guess." "And?" "Annnddd I''ll never do it again."', 'You were told you had to <kanji>apologize</kanji> by the village <reading>sha</reading>man (しゃ). At first you didn''t even know why everyone was glaring at you, but the shaman took you aside and explained. And now she''s standing behind you with her arms crossed as you apologize to everybody.', 'N1', NULL),
(894, 'She''s just acting so weird. You doubt she would ever make you hunt for your own meal like this. What are you going to do?', 'With her head in the guillotine, it''s your mama who starts to look doubtful now. "How could you ever doubt me?" she cries. Ugh, now you''re really not sure. This guillotine thing has just compounded your doubts.', 'Now full of <kanji>doubt</kanji>, you decide to test your mama by putting her in a <reading>gui</reading>llotine (ぎ). It sounds harsh, but the guillotine is the only way to clear up your doubts.', 'N3', NULL),
(895, 'Just look at the color white. Compare it to other colors. Now look at white again. Split it up into all the colors, creating a beautiful spectrum. Wow, it''s <kanji>all</kanji> the colors!', 'Imagine everything covered in white. Everything in the world. Then, you see a dark splotch. It''s the miners, they''re all covered in coal, ruining all your ambitions. Walk up to them and see all the dirt on the perfectly white uniforms you gave them just a few minutes earlier. Feel enraged at them. How could they ruin all this white you made for <kanji>all</kanji> the people of the world!?', 'You decide white light is the ultimate color. You decide you want to put it on <kanji>all</kanji> things to make everything white. "What a great idea," you think. You try to put it on everything and you nearly succeed... except, the <reading>miners</reading> (みな). They keep getting dirty doing all their mining things. NOOO, your plan is ruined!', 'N3', NULL),
(1898, 'Picture a tin can in your mind as clearly as you can.', 'かん is can, it''s really easy, come on. Just take this one.', 'The reading for a <kanji>can</kanji> is かん, which seems impossibly easy. If that isn''t close enough for you, picture this:

Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) is drinking out of a tin can. He glug glug glugs, gasps, and wipes his face. He holds out the can, "Khan made cans," and winks.', 'N2', NULL),
(888, 'Remember, this is the OG = original geoduck. There are no other geoducks yet.', 'People listened when Gandhi said, "My friends. We must stop trying to eat the geoduck, and instead allow it to breed. Join me in this fast!"

And that''s why we have so many geoducks on the earth today. Thanks Gandhi.', 'The only person that would even hear the geoduck''s <kanji>request</kanji> was <reading>Gan</reading>dhi (がん). Gandhi closed his eyes, sat down, and spread the geoduck''s request to the rest of the world.', 'N3', NULL),
(896, 'The leader of the yakuza is just showing you an example of a knife. Maybe to scare you? Why would an example of what a knife is scare you, though? Coming from the leader of the yakuza, this is a weird tactic.', 'Why is the leader of the yakuza showing you examples of weapons, not to mention weapons that aren''t real, like ray guns?', '"This is an <kanji>example</kanji>," the leader of the yakuza tells you. "Next time, I''ll show you an example of a <reading>ray</reading> (れい) gun."', 'N3', NULL),
(897, 'Try to picture the lid you give to the person(s), then their putting it on a cross. Why does this ceremony symbolize graduation? Maybe the persons are your teachers, and it''s about the divine knowledge you''ve now finished collecting under your "lid," or something. Congrats?', 'Imagine walking up to the stage with no lid, and the person(s) just say, "Sorry, you can''t graduate. So two more years. Next!"', 'Uh oh, looks like you forgot your lid. They decide not to let you <kanji>graduate</kanji>. <reading>So two</reading> (そつ) more years now until you can graduate, it seems. So two more years?! Just for forgetting a lid? Nonsense!', 'N2', NULL),
(898, 'The power of cooperation is a *ahem* powerful thing.', 'Imagine being a citizen of きょうと. Watch as the powers from your neighboring prefectures march across the border to come cooperate together to stop the bandits that raided your farm. You admire their cooperation. You admire きょうと!', 'Nowhere is the power of cooperation more apparent than in <reading>きょう</reading>と. When invaders are attacking, when the townspeople want to rebel, when there is a kitty up a tree, the people combine their power from all reaches of Japan and cross into きょうと to put a stop to the madness through cooperation.', 'N2', NULL),
(899, 'When you''re thinking about your next hair style, if you don''t like participating in things, you should think about something you can actually keep more private.', 'Would you want to participate in Christmas if it meant looking like Santa and visiting every single house on earth?', 'You know who has big hair and loves to <kanji>participate</kanji> in everything? <reading>San</reading>ta-san (さん)! Well, his hair is more big on the bottom (it''s a beard) than whatever you have on your head, but hair is hair. And he participates in present-giving in every house in the world! See? Big hair = participation!', 'N3', NULL),
(900, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Try to find the circumference of your shoe and then email us with the measurement. I promise you''ll never forget this kanji if you actually do it.', 'Practice how to measure the <kanji>circumference</kanji> of an item by using your <reading>shoe</reading> (しゅう). Your shoe is kind of round, and it''s free. I mean it''s right there on your foot.', 'N2', NULL),
(901, 'Picture yourself surrounded by this well on all sides. You look up and you can barely see the mouth of the well where you fell in and now you are trapped. Imagine how scared you are. Imagine trying to claw your way up the sides of the well but all you do is break your nails on the walls.', 'This eagle is basically a superhero. Imagine an eagle dressed in a superman outfit. He’s your savior. Imagine your gratitude towards this eagle superhero.', 'The well walls <kanji>surround</kanji> you as you look up to the mouth once again. This time you see an <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) flying overhead. You call out to the eagle to save you and the eagle comes swooping down into the well and flies you out to safety. What a nice eagle!', 'N2', NULL),
(902, 'Go ahead and touch that old man''s mouth. It''s super hard feeling when you touch it.', '"You pressed the hard skin holding me captive! Thank you!" the 子 says, as they break free from their hard mouth prison.', 'You touch the old man''s <kanji>hard</kanji> mouth and it crumbles away, revealing the face of a <reading>子</reading> (こ) inside.', 'N2', NULL),
(903, 'Watch the grain children live their entire lives throughout the seasons. Though there are 72 seasons in Japan, so the life of a grain is actually really long there.', 'Picture these seasonal keys floating through the sky, unlocking the weather changes, the growth of new grains, and the aging of us all.', 'All of the <kanji>seasons</kanji> have their own special <reading>key</reading> (き). As they change, that season''s key appears and unlocks the next step in the grain''s life.', 'N2', NULL),
(904, 'Picture this absolutely perfect roof. They really got it right on their first try.', 'Genghis Khan was a perfectionist, and by conquering and bringing all of these different people together, he was able to create something perfect by all standards.', 'This <kanji>perfect</kanji> roof was commissioned by Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん). We got all of the carpenters and home makers and construction workers he''d conquered together and tasked them with making the first roof, but only if they made it absolutely perfect.', 'N3', NULL),
(905, 'Picture this narwhal: above its long horn there is a huge, shiny treasure floating. It pulls the towel tight around itself, "Brururu you found me I see!" it whistles at you.', 'If you find the key, what will you wish for? Think about your single, greatest wish. Close your eyes and picture it as clearly as you can and you''ll find the key.', 'In order to open the treasure and get your <kanji>wish</kanji>, you need the <reading>key</reading> (き). The narwhal knows where it''s hidden, so you''ll have to convince it to tell you where the key is.', 'N2', NULL),
(906, 'Think about the last time you thought it was right to make a decision with your heart. What was it? Use these emotions to remember this kanji.', 'Think of all the things you''d do with your life if you only had one 年 left. Maybe you should go do some of them anyways, right now?', 'You''re living in the now and letting your heart control all your <kanji>thought</kanji>s. Why are you following your heart? You found you only have one <reading>年</reading> (ねん) to live, so you have to live for the now.', 'N3', NULL),
(907, 'Imagine holding an axe between your fingers and folding it like a pretzel. Or some silly putty. Or a really soft axe.', 'How many axes can you bend at once? A set of two? What about a set of four? Or a set of forty-two?', 'Unfortunately, as you <kanji>fold</kanji> these axes in half you realize they come in <reading>sets</reading> (せつ), so there are twice the axes you thought there would be. To compensate, you start folding them in sets too.', 'N3', NULL),
(908, 'Now picture the death moon king as the Emperor, sitting in his Death Star. There is no hope, except for that little opening you can shoot through to destroy the whole thing. That''s hope, I guess!', 'In the end, the real hope was bowling all along. I hope you''ve learned a valuable lesson from this space movie.', 'The name A New <kanji>Hope</kanji>, as we all know, comes from the ending where the moon king''s son sends a <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう) ball into that little opening and knocks it out of the sky.', 'N3', NULL),
(909, 'Picture a tree standing in the forest. It''s thinking so hard you can see the math equations flying around in its branches, until finally a cartoon light bulb appears above its head. This talented tree has figured out how to become lumber! ', 'Watch as the tree becomes lumber and that lumber is used to craft a ginormous xylophone. Each key is its own plank of lumber!', 'Trees can be turned into all kinds of things, but this one wants to use its <kanji>lumber</kanji> and <kanji>material</kanji> to build a <reading>xy</reading>lophone (ざい). It''s the most difficult instrument to create, so it suits the talent level of this tree!', 'N2', NULL),
(910, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Imagine doing laundry and making sure to bundle your socks together so none of them will be lost.', 'You should always <kanji>bundle</kanji> your <reading>sock</reading>s (そく). If you don''t, there is a 100% chance you''ll lose all of the left ones.', 'N3', NULL),
(911, 'Imagine climbing up a pine tree. The pine needles are prickly, so your hands get pricked as you climb. Smell the pine tree smell and feel the pine sap on your hands.', 'Picture yourself falling out of this pine tree and onto the mats. The mats are nice and soft so it’s nice. Hear the whoosh of air as you collapse onto the mats.', 'In case you fall, someone has laid <reading>mats</reading> (まつ) out around the base of the <kanji>pine</kanji> tree. That way, you’ll land on the mats should something unfortunate happen.', 'N1', NULL),
(912, 'Imagine the yakuza bars all being shut down. There''s only one place where they can remain.', 'Imagine a yakuza bar in Zanzibar. What a strange place to hold out.', 'The <kanji>remainder</kanji> of the yakuza bars aren''t in Japan. Strangely, they are all in <reading>Zan</reading>zibar (ざん).', 'N3', NULL),
(914, 'Picture this pasty white dude jumping into the target-bottomed pool. The sun glints off of his unnaturally white skin and stings your eyes.', 'He hits the water right on target and the water splashes onto you. Since he was a techie, he was able to perform the perfect dive. And now you’re all wet.', 'This white dude is a huge <reading>techie</reading> (てき) so he has electronic enhancements that make sure he hits the <kanji>target</kanji>. Being a techie has its advantages.', 'N4', NULL),
(915, 'The promise reminding thread on your finger was tied too tight. You can feel the thread starting to cut off your circulation as your finger starts to turn purple and go numb. It’s really uncomfortable.', 'Imagine yourself sitting on a riding mower as you’re pulled around the yard by a yak. The yak is having a hard time pulling you so it''s making lots of yak noises. Hear them in your mind.', 'After you get out of the pool, you keep the <kanji>promise</kanji> you made to your mother. So you get on your <reading>yak</reading> (やく) powered mower and trim the grass. Yes, your mower is pulled by a yak. It’s very efficient.', 'N3', NULL),
(916, 'Imagine piling up flowers on top of clouds to make your own unique art. It’s a fiddly business, because they keep falling through the clouds, but you get there in the end. Now step back and marvel at your work of flower cloud art.', 'Picture the gay club with your art displayed behind the bar. Fame and fortune are just around the corner, now.', 'Your beautiful <kanji>art</kanji> is going to be displayed at a <reading>gay</reading> (げい) club. Imagine the pride you feel about having your art on display.', 'N2', NULL),
(917, 'Do you live on the beach? Maybe the foundation of your house was made from crabs in dirt too. Why don''t you go outside and check?', 'Keys to houses are always made out of a piece of the foundation it was built upon. How else would they be unique from one another?', 'Remember, a good <kanji>foundation</kanji> is the <reading>key</reading> (き) to a good house. No, literally. You have to use a piece of the foundation to make the key to your house.', 'N1', NULL),
(918, 'Picture your own soul and your own life. Listen to what your soul says about your gender and nature — because your life is yours to live, and only your soul can define who you truly are!', 'The next time you get one of those surveys where you have to choose your gender, just think back to that time in your life when a random official came and certified your gender with their saber. Ahh, what lovely memories.', 'Once you''ve decided what your <kanji>gender</kanji> is, someone comes to your house and lays a <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい) on your shoulders. "This is now your <kanji>nature</kanji>," they say, lifting the saber. It''s official! It''s in all the newspapers, there''s a parade, and everyone pats you on the back. Thanks, gender saber!', 'N3', NULL),
(920, 'Imagine kissing a tree. Better yet, go kiss a tree. If you''re lucky, it''ll give you a little taste of sap — an even surer sign of your status.', 'Probably because a cock crowing means the sun''s about to come up. Morning light is basically tree breakfast, after all, and they say the way to a tree''s heart is through its chloroplasts. So picture yourself as a cock, cock-a-doodle-doing to a tree about your status.', 'The best way to convince a tree of your <kanji>status</kanji> is to say "cock-a-doodle-doo" at it, like a <reading>cock</reading> (かく), i.e. a male chicken. Trees really place a lot of status on the ability to sound like a cock crowing.', 'N3', NULL),
(921, 'I wish I had this kind of ability... maybe I''ll go up to the moon and try to steal your spoons.', 'Oh, you thought I was talking about that old Nostradamus, who also had the ability to predict things? Nah, you''re way cooler than him.', 'This <kanji>ability</kanji> to predict the future makes you famous and now everyone knows your name: <reading>No</reading>stradamus (のう)! And now that people know about your name and special ability, everyone will love you!', 'N3', NULL),
(924, 'Imagine the force and mass of the moon hitting you right in the boney part of your forehead. Whomp! It sure would hurt.', 'Again, feel your bones crack as you smash into the moon. At least you''re warm though. Feel the cozy coats you''re wearing and snuggle up in their warmth.', 'Outer space is cold, and if you''re traveling to the moon, you''re going to need a lot of <reading>coats</reading> (こつ). All of these coats will keep you warm, even if you do end up breaking your forehead <kanji>bone</kanji>. ', 'N2', NULL),
(925, 'Picture a woman walking gently over someone''s back. She''s hardly moving.', 'Poor old dad. His back hurts so his daughter has to walk on it with cleats to make it feel better. But if she isn''t gentle enough, she''ll just make it worse.', 'Actually, the person she''s being <kanji>gentle</kanji> with is her <reading>da</reading>d (だ). No one would ever want to hurt their dad, especially if he''s asked you to walk gently on his back as a way of giving him a massage.', 'N1', NULL),
(926, 'Imagine looking up into the sky, trying to figure out what the atmosphere up there is made out of. Your only lead: It''s raining, a lot. So, the atmosphere must be partly made of rain, and it''s just falling down.', 'The reading for this kanji is actually the same as one of 分''s on''yomi readings (ふん). One trick about complicated kanji is they often take the reading of one of the pieces they''re made from. In this case it''s 分. How fun.', 'Turns out, studying the <kanji>atmosphere</kanji> is <reading>fun</reading> (ふん)! ', 'N1', NULL),
(927, 'You know what a geoduck looks like, right? Does that look like a creature that was looking to change and evolve and be awesome? No, geoducks are weird and stubborn. Imagine how stubborn you’d have to be to not want to evolve into a more attractive animal.', 'Gandhi’s stubbornness, however, was pretty effective. Everyone knows Gandhi. Remember Gandhi and his famous stubborn attitude whenever you see this kanji.', 'You know who else was <kanji>stubborn</kanji>? <reading>Gan</reading>dhi (がん). Gandhi believed in non-violent resistance and he was pretty stubborn about it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1514, 'After your hard work, hear the gate close behind you with a creak. You''re on the inside now, you''re one of the talented ones. And your talents have changed you, made you different from the others — much like the talent radical in this kanji is a little different from its usual shape!', 'Picture a bunch of people outside the closed gates, sleeping in piles of hay. You were there too once. You know how itchy that hay is, and how cold it can be at night. But it was all worth it.', 'Outside the talent gate that''s always <kanji>closed</kanji>, <reading>hay</reading> (へい) is piled up to make beds for the people who are still trying to get in. Who knows how long they''ll be there outside the closed gate, sleeping in the hay, but they''re all working hard to become talented.', 'N3', NULL),
(1918, 'Make sure you remember that this is a well of water not a "well, actually."', 'You''re absolutely terrified of this giant eagle hiding in the well. You''re not even sure what its goal is, maybe it''s just out to scare you... Either way, imagine yourself being absolutely terrified of this eagle every time you have to go fetch water from the well.', 'Each time you go to the town <kanji>well</kanji>, there''s a giant <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) hiding inside, just waiting for you to show up. ', 'N1', NULL),
(834, 'Feel the sunshine coming into the building. It gets into this building so easily, and makes it very bright and warm. No matter where you look, sunshine is everywhere.', 'Picture your yogurt glistening in the sunlight. Watch as it slowly curdles and starts to smell sour. You still eat your yogurt, but it''s hot and lumpy now. Sunlight and yogurt do not mix.', 'The <kanji>sunshine</kanji> gets into the building so easily, in fact, that it hits your <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう). The sunshine makes the yogurt... gross.', 'N3', NULL),
(867, 'Nope, the luck is definitely coming from the spirit in your scarecrow. You aren''t lucky on your own.', 'If you get a luck spirit to say f*** you, you might accidentally convince it to move somewhere else. If it does, you lose your luck. So be careful.', 'You want to check the scarecrow to make sure your recent <kanji>luck</kanji> is coming from it, so you kick it. "<reading>F***</reading> (ふく) you!" it yells. Oh yeah, there''s a spirit in there.', 'N3', NULL),
(919, 'If you could have any skill imaginable, what would it be? Wouldn''t it be nice to have these magical branching fingers? (Definitely. It would.)', 'Imagine a tiny guillotine made for the cutting of fingers. Whoever made this must have had special hand skills too! Then maybe the skill-less people tested it on them…', 'Unfortunately, these <kanji>skill</kanji>s are too much for the normal skill-less people to handle, so they pull out the <reading>gui</reading>llotine (ぎ). I guess they''re going to chop off your skillful fingers because they''re kinda scary.', 'N2', NULL),
(922, 'Angels are really good at going around carrying drops! Imagine the artistry and technique involved. I bet they do some kind of dance, and there''s lots of sparkly magic.', 'Imagine an angel trained in ninjutsu. Marvel at all their divine ninja techniques!', 'Angels are so good at this because they are trained in nin<reading>jutsu</reading> (じゅつ), or the "ninja techniques." Everyone knows that ninjas are great at carrying drops on the go!', 'N3', NULL),
(928, NULL, 'That''s too bad - feel sorry for the person and learn a little more about the terror attack, if it will help you to remember the reading of this kanji better.', 'Someone died. You ask how this person died, the person who''s getting buried by the <kanji>temple</kanji>. The priest/monk says it was in a tragic <reading>terror</reading> (てら) attack.', 'N2', NULL),
(929, 'This kanji makes a lot of sense. Just follow the logic.', 'Imagine yourself screaming "Eeeeee! Whaaaat!?" and running away from that boulder as fast as you can.', '"<kanji>Boulder</kanji>!!!" yells a voice from behind you. "<reading>Eee, wha</reading>...?" (いわ) you reply. You look up to see a boulder falling off the cliff above you. "Eeeeee! Whaaaat!?," you shout again, running and screaming this time.', 'N2', NULL),
(930, 'Your knife is one of those Swiss Army knives, so it''s got a bunch of tools you''ll need when you return home. Your broom is just a broom, but… maybe if you pretend to ride it like a witch, it''ll make the trip home more fun?', 'Imagine your family asking all your coworkers and friends to 返す you so you can return home. It''s heartwarming, but also kind of embarrassing at the same time.', 'For you to <kanji>return home</kanji>, your family has to ask the world to <reading>返</reading>す (かえ) you to them. You belong at home with your family — the outside world is just borrowing you, and eventually it needs to 返す you and let you return home.', 'N4', NULL),
(931, 'The spring sun shows itself only in spring, and that''s how you know the season has truly arrived. Its light has a special quality that gives everything a fresh, spring-like hue.', 'Just picture how lively everything becomes in the springtime, and let yourself be guided by the wonderful sounds, smells and colours. Let yourself enjoy the hallucinations.', 'You also know <kanji>spring</kanji> has arrived when you start <reading>hallu</reading>cinating (はる). With all the funky pollens wafting around, it''s kind of inevitable that spring will make you hallucinate — it''s just part of the season.', 'N4', NULL),
(932, 'Really try to imagine this giant shrimp on the sun as a normal thing that happens, without fail, every day at noon. "Hey guys, it''s noon," the shrimp announces casually, then leaves again.', 'Right after the shrimp''s noon announcement, imagine it struggling to put on heels while sitting on the sun. This shrimp is almost bigger than the sun, actually, so all its heel struggles are easy to see.', 'At <kanji>noon</kanji>, the shrimp also puts on its high <reading>heel</reading>s (ひる). It wants to look good for the other shrimp it''s going to lunch with. High noon? More like high heels, am I right?', 'N4', NULL),
(933, 'Look into the blue sky and imagine the clouds parting as the weather clears up and the sun starts to come out. Gorgeous! The beauty is so great you have to squint.', 'Imagine walking down the street as the weather clears up and saying to everyone you pass, "Hah! I was right! I knew it would clear up! Hah!" Basking in the beautiful weather is nice, but basking in feelings of smug superiority is even better.', 'When it starts to <kanji>clear up</kanji>, you exclaim, "<reading>Hah</reading> (は)! Told you so!" You had a feeling it would clear up, but no one believed you, so now you have to yell "Hah!" and rub your correctness in everyone''s face.', 'N3', NULL),
(934, 'Picture all the grains in autumn. Picture all of the colors. The colors of fire.', 'Ah yes, the sound of the seasons changing to autumn.

Ack!
Ack!
Ack!', 'As the grains burn, and the season changes to autumn, imagine them yelling out in pain: "<reading>ACK</reading> (あき)!"', 'N4', NULL),
(935, 'Imagine trying to use your hands to show the size of this cross, but the other person shakes their head and says, "Use your words!" Ugh, okay, you''ll have to measure it then.', '"This cross measures two cakes wide and three cakes long," you say.', 'You need to <kanji>measure</kanji> this cross but all you have is a <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい). Good enough! I''m sure all cakes are exactly the same size, so they''re a reliable unit of measure.', 'N4', NULL),
(936, 'You have a yakuza knife. There are a bunch there, all in a row, so you follow suit by putting yours in the row too.', 'Imagine a bunch of yakuza being enthusiastic about putting their knives in a row. They all say together "Ret''s do it!"', 'Should we put them in a <kanji>row</kanji>? <reading>Let''s</reading> (れつ) put them in a row!', 'N3', NULL),
(937, 'Picture a big pirate map, and on it are a bunch of these markers for the districts where you can find the treasures you''ve been searching for.', 'The treasure of the cookie district is cookies, which you want. And to be fair, even if you weren''t a pirate, you''d probably want those cookies.', 'There is one <kanji>district</kanji> you''re interested in, and that''s the <reading>coo</reading>kie (く) district.', 'N2', NULL),
(938, 'Imagine the pain in your calves as you walk up a steep slope. Wow, this dirt is really anti-you right now. Going down the slope will be a lot easier and quicker, but that''s still just the dirt trying to shoo you away as fast as possible.', 'Maybe it looks flat to a spectator, but a soccer pitch is actually a series of very small slopes. Those soccer athletes who seem to fall and flop all the time are in fact just slipping on tiny invisible slopes.', 'You need to know these things about <kanji>slope</kanji>s if you want to play <reading>soccer</reading> (さか). Soccer is surprisingly slope-intensive, actually.', 'N2', NULL),
(939, 'Ritual and ceremony kind of mean the same thing. Don''t worry too much if you mix them up a little — each will help you to remember the other. There''s a reason this kanji is made up of the ceremony radical already!', 'It''s good to practice making up your own mnemonics every once in a while. It''ll help if you add more to the mnemonics already here, or if you can make up new ones that are more personal to you!', 'So, you''re at a <kanji>ritual</kanji>, but not just any old ritual. This is a full-blown <kanji>ceremony</kanji> and it''s incredibly <reading>chic</reading> (しき). Imagine just how chic it is. Give it a shot — I''ll let you come up with the chicness this time (it''s good practice).', 'N3', NULL),
(940, 'Imagine who your leader is here — maybe a boss, or maybe the Crabigator. Now say it out loud to this imagined leader: "Believe and trust in me!"', 'Picture yourself pleading with your leader to trust and believe in you. Imagine the pain from your shin splints as you kneel. It hurts a lot, but it proves how serious you are.', 'As you face your leader and say "<kanji>Believe</kanji> and <kanji>trust</kanji> in me," you kneel down on each <reading>shin</reading> (しん). You have shin splints, so it hurts a bit, but you do it anyway to show how serious you are.', 'N3', NULL),
(941, 'That means if you know your radicals well, you know the meaning of this kanji too!', 'Imagine a courageous man standing against the youths. They use weird slang and try to tell him about avocado toast, but the man keeps his courage.', 'A man needs <kanji>courage</kanji> to stand up to all the <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう) of the world. They are so numerous and difficult to understand, but the man can face them with courage thanks to his mama.', 'N2', NULL),
(942, 'Simple is simple!', 'Get a tan, it''s simple, stupid! (Though it''s terrible for you so uh... don''t always take our advice, kay?)', 'One of the most <kanji>simple</kanji> things in the world is getting a <reading>tan</reading> (たん). All you have to do is stand outside long enough. It doesn''t matter what skin type you have or what the weather is like. Eventually, if you stand out there long enough, you''ll get a tan.', 'N3', NULL),
(943, 'Picture the creepiest creeper you know. They''re wearing a cape, laughing from behind a big desk in their new director-level position. That cape has really gone to their head.', 'Picture that creeper you know. Their cape is made of wool now, since they''re the Director of Sheep, and they''re directing all kinds of creepy policy decisions about sheep. I''m not even sure what those would be, but try to imagine some.', 'After this creeper dons the cape, they decide to become <kanji>Director</kanji> of <reading>Shee</reading>p (し). From now on all sheep-related business, government policy, and law will be determined by this creeper in a cape, because they are the Director of Sheep.', 'N1', NULL),
(944, 'With every winter that comes around, another year passes, and you change. Again and again. The never ending cycle of winters and change.', 'Picture you and your hens. Going through change, every winter, again and again. ', 'You know what else <kanji>change</kanji>s each winter? <reading>Hen</reading>s (へん)! They''re constantly laying eggs, which then (possibly) turn into new hens.', 'N3', NULL),
(945, '', 'Husbands love eating food. Who doesn’t though, really?', 'What do all <kanji>husband</kanji>s love? <reading>Foo</reading>d (ふう)! Can you hear your future husband? He’s saying, "Fooooood! FOOOOOD!” like some kind of food craving zombie. ', 'N3', NULL),
(947, 'Imagine having the power to control time like this. Picture yourself unlocking the sun to go back in time. Feel the heat of the sun and the bright light in your eyes as you unlock the door to the past.', 'Imagine wearing this sack as you''re sucked through the vortex of time and space. Feel yourself spinning around as you hurtle back into yesterday.', 'Time traveling to a <kanji>previous</kanji> time, like <kanji>yesterday</kanji>, can be rough on the body. As such, you wear a small <reading>sack</reading> (さく) to hold your valuables while you''re ripped through the sands of time. The sack keeps your important stuff from getting lost. The sack holds your key to the sun as well.', 'N3', NULL),
(948, 'To keep life safely inside the window, put that poison down.', 'Have you done Sudoku before? Imagine yourself completing these puzzles and concentrating really hard. They’re fun, but also challenging.', 'If anything''s worse than <kanji>poison</kanji> it''s doing Su<reading>doku</reading> (どく) puzzles.', 'N2', NULL),
(949, 'Tsunamis used to come crashing into towns, just leveling them completely. Today we have new methods of building and protecting ourselves, and laws to keep them in place, so the past doesn''t repeat itself.', 'Your home has strong walls and ceilings. You aren''t allowed to leave the doors unlocked and open. These methods and laws are because of those past tsunamis and the damage they used to cause. Your home is safe thanks to them!', 'These <kanji>method</kanji>s and <kanji>law</kanji>s affected your own <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう) the most.', 'N3', NULL),
(950, 'Imagine getting hit with the full force of a tsunami. It slams into you and crushes every bone in your body. It hurts so much you cry.', 'Imagine stuffing yourself with nachos. The tears stop. You''re feeling bruised and wet, but much better.', 'Do you know the only way to stop yourself <kanji>cry</kanji>ing after a tsunami? That''s right, <reading>na</reading>chos (な)! Nachos are the only proven way to cheer people up after being hit by a tsunami.', 'N1', NULL),
(951, 'Picture yourself at this bar, sipping on a drink while you''re wading in this shallow water. Feel the shallow water getting your socks and shoes all wet. It''s uncomfortable and cold.', 'Picture yourself in the bar again sipping on this Asahi beer. Taste the beery beer taste of Asahi as you walk around the bar with your wet feet.', 'What beer are you drinking at this bar while standing in <kanji>shallow</kanji> water? You’re drinking <reading>Asa</reading>hi (あさ), the beer of the salaryman. Asahi is super dry, so it takes your attention away from the shallow water a bit.', 'N2', NULL),
(952, 'Picture this account of your life, the thread of the narrative linking all of the different important points in your life together.', 'Picture the people in your life that you''d give this special key to. They''d get to read the account of your life, so make sure they''re trustworthy before you hand that key over.', 'You take this <kanji>account</kanji> and lock it with a special <reading>key</reading> (き). You only give this key to people you really trust, so they can read the account of your life and truly understand you.', 'N1', NULL),
(953, 'Picture yourself in the center of England, speaking English to all of the beautiful flowers.', 'Imagine walking around the giant English acorn. Touch its smooth surface with your hand.', 'You keep walking to the very central center of <kanji>England</kanji> and see a giant <reading>a</reading>corn (えい) planted in the middle of the ground. This acorn is the source of all the flowers and life in England.', 'N4', NULL),
(954, 'Imagine yourself running away from the North Korean army. You have to get to the border, the entire army is coming after you for hitting their dear leader!', 'Picture yourself running away from them as they shout and shoot at you. Hear the bullets wizzing past your head as you run away as fast as you can.', 'The <kanji>army</kanji> is after you. Each army soldier has a <reading>gun</reading> (ぐん) and they want to use these guns of theirs to shoot you dead! You killed their leader with a car and now they’re gonna get you back with a gun!', 'N2', NULL),
(955, 'Look down at the huge meal in front of you. You''ll have to eat your food eventually, but I guess there are plenty of reasons to be anti-eat right now.', 'Picture Han Solo slowly eating your whole meal. He''s making constant eye contact with you, reaching down and shoving the entire meal into his mouth.', 'As you stare at the <kanji>meal</kanji>, <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo appears before you and takes a bite! This is what happens if you don''t eat your food, someone else will eat it.', 'N4', NULL),
(956, 'Imagine being in the privacy of your own home, your own room, and saying to yourself, "Yes, Buddha, you''re my leader."

Or don''t, if that doesn''t sit right with you. Freedom of religion and all that, right?', 'Picture Buddha wearing a giant pair of rubber boots. Yes, now this is fashion.', 'Your private <kanji>Buddha</kanji> is a little different than other people''s idea of him. Your Buddha is wearing <reading>boots</reading> (ぶつ). They''re big and rubber, and they look fabulous on him.', 'N2', NULL),
(958, 'Imagine you’re the sun, working all day. Of course you get tired. This is a good excuse to go to bed at night and get some rest. Feel how sore and hot and sweaty and exhausted you are after a hard day’s work of being the sun.', 'Stop working so much! Humans need some rest too, so it can''t always be bright. Maybe you should try some therapy. We all just want to see you get better, sun. Anyway, you''re banned for now. Cya.', 'Unfortunately, you also have a history of overworking yourself, so you''ve been outright <reading>ban</reading>ned (ばん) from showing up at <kanji>night</kanji>.', 'N3', NULL),
(959, 'Picture this cat meowing and purring as it rubs up against all the flowers in the rice paddy field. Aww, it’s so cute!', 'Try not to imagine this too hard. It is disturbing.', 'Wait a minute… This <kanji>cat</kanji> is pretty odd looking. You take a closer look, seeing it is missing its <reading>neck</reading> (ねこ). You go to pet it, but it''s super sensitive about that area of its head, which is basically a face connected to a body...', 'N3', NULL),
(1608, 'Imagine people coming from miles around to pay for a look at your private fingers.', 'If somebody looks at your fingers without paying, without your consent, file a harassment claim on their butt! That is NOT okay!', 'If someone tries to look without paying, that''s <reading>hara</reading>ssment (はら)! Nobody gets to see something of yours without proper payment, but more importantly, your consent!', 'N3', NULL),
(1891, 'You can only see the legs, though, because a cloth is covering the cage. What kind of small animal is it? Try to look.', 'Who is giving this cat all those hickies? Pretty gross, if you ask me.', 'You look up through the cloth, and see a hairless cat. This <kanji>small animal</kanji> is hairless, but most surprisingly is covered in <reading>hickie</reading>s (ひき).', 'N2', NULL),
(960, 'Zombies are dead, so I guess their mouths have all the things garden plants crave. Their mouths are like compost piles, if you think about it.', 'Oh. The zombie probably bit down on your head and now it''s the end for you. At least you were looking at a nice garden at the end of it all...', 'You look closer at the <kanji>garden</kanji> and suddenly everything <reading>en</reading>ds (えん).', 'N3', NULL),
(961, 'Picture the majestic scene — two Wolverines (yes, there are two) and a turkey, frolicking in the sunlight together. It''s one of those rare but regular natural occurrences that only happens once a week, on a particular day. ', 'Picture yourself looking at the shadow cast by the two Wolverines and the turkey. Imagine waiting a whole 24 hours and knowing exactly when your yogurt is ready. All thanks to this makeshift sundial which can accurately tell you which day of the week it is.', 'Now that you know how to figure out what <kanji>day of the week</kanji> it is, you can start making <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう). It can take up to 24 hours to make yogurt. Knowing which weekday it is at any given moment means you can perfect your yogurt-making schedule.', 'N4', NULL),
(962, 'Just imagine yourself sitting on the sun, writing back to your friends and family with a brush. For someone who can visit the sun, it''s really weird that you are writing with a brush like this, but you''re doing it just the same.', 'Think of your favorite live shows and imagine them on the sun. Also imagine yourself writing back to your friends and family about said shows.', 'What did you <kanji>write</kanji> about? You were writing about this amazing circus <reading>show</reading> (しょ) you just watched on the sun. You didn''t expect there to be any shows here, but turns out you were wrong!', 'N5', NULL),
(963, 'Picture the zombie, going far off into the distance. It''s getting smaller and harder to see.', 'I mean, to be fair, the zombie wasn''t really alive to begin with. But going far enough to end it all? That''s some serious distance!', 'The zombie goes so <kanji>far</kanji>, that its life <reading>en</reading>ds (えん). Zombies need to eat to live (it''s really all they need), but they go so far on their scooter trip, that they just give up and end it all.', 'N3', NULL),
(964, 'Know your radicals or you won''t be allowed to pass.', 'Picture the most terrifying monster you can imagine, standing as tall as the gate itself. How on earth are you ever going to get past that thing?! ', 'You want to pass through this <kanji>gate</kanji> but it’s guarded by… a <reading>mon</reading>ster (もん)! ', 'N2', NULL),
(965, 'Imagine you''re this leader, and you''ve just picked up a piece of thread from the ground. You make a jarring psychic connection with the owner of the thread. Your head spins, your heart races, and your hands tremble. Picture this happening to you as you pick up the thread from the ground.', 'The connection is so strong that you feel like you''re eating the cake as well. It''s vanilla-strawberry. You taste the fluffy and creamy vanilla-strawberry flavors washing over your tongue. Imagine yourself eating this delectable cake. It''s fantastically delicious.', 'As you develop this psychic <kanji>connection</kanji> with the owner of the thread, you can tell that they''re currently eating a <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい).', 'N3', NULL),
(967, 'Which store has the best product selection, do you think? Picture all those articles and goods lined up on the shelves in their little boxes, kind of in the shape of this kanji.', 'Then another Hindu person walks in your store and buys some. Then another... What are all these Hindu people doing buying your products, you wonder? When you ask them, they tell you "it''s a cultural thing."', 'As you try to sell some <kanji>product</kanji>s, you finally get a buyer. It''s a <reading>Hin</reading>du (ひん) person. She walks in and buys your products on the spot!', 'N4', NULL),
(968, 'If you think about it, your roof protects you from a lot of things! Rain, snow, bird poop. But if your roof''s measurements are off it won''t be able to protect you properly.', 'Odds are, the roof collapsed or a tree fell on you and you died from lack of protection. So now your family and friends are going to sue on your behalf.', 'If the measurements are off, and your roof doesn''t <kanji>protect</kanji> you, then you get to <reading>sue</reading> (す)! If you''re still alive, that is.', 'N3', NULL),
(969, 'Spicy foods literally do trigger happy chemicals in your brain. It wouldn''t be too far off to say that putting a lid on a pot filled with something spicy could produce happiness.', 'こういち looks sad. But when you serve him happiness, he starts to perk up a bit. A couple more bites of happiness, and there''s a smile on his face. Although when you found こういち he was depressed, down on his luck, and hiding from the government, cooking him some spicy foods seems to have brought him some happiness.', 'Out of spicy foods you made pure <kanji>happiness</kanji>. You give it to your sad friend <reading>こう</reading>いち, who waits at the table.', 'N3', NULL),
(970, 'Picture this beating heart in the prison. Was it Wolverine''s? That would explain why it''s, like, beating all supernaturally on its own. What should you do with it? You''d better hurry, its owner probably needs it.', 'And if it really is Wolverine''s heart, you''re gonna have to be cool as a cucumber when you try to help him, even if you''re in a hurry. He''s emotionally cold, remember? Gotta play it cool with him, despite the emergency. ', 'You start to overheat as you <kanji>hurry</kanji> around, so you grab a <reading>cu</reading>cumber (きゅう) to cool you down. The high water content re-hydrates you and lets you continue hurrying to help save this heart. ', 'N4', NULL),
(971, 'Picture trying to get the pope to stop staring at the fins lying on the dirty ground. Yup, he''s completely lost touch with reality, you think.', 'Imagine the pope angrily kicking your shin super hard. You fall over and pull it close. Feel how badly it aches. Who knew you''d need shin guards to deal with a pope that''s lost touch with reality just from dropping his dinner.', 'You''re doing your best to bring the pope back to <kanji>reality</kanji> when he snaps and kicks you in the <reading>shin</reading> (しん). Oww! What the heck, pope! But he''s already back staring at the fins and your shin is throbbing where he kicked it.', 'N4', NULL),
(972, 'Remember back to those woodshop electives you had in school (if you did have them) and imagine yourself crafting a bamboo box out of a bamboo tree you felled yourself. Don''t forget to wear eye protection though! Wouldn''t want anything to happen to those valuable eyes of yours.', 'Picture yourself filling up your lovingly crafted bamboo box with your collection of hardcore punk and rock records. Ah, they fit just perfectly thanks to that discerning eye of yours.', 'Now you can use that <kanji>box</kanji> to store all your <reading>ha</reading>rd<reading>co</reading>re (はこ) punk vinyl records. You''re a real connoisseur, so you know that hardcore music should only ever be kept in bamboo boxes.', 'N3', NULL),
(973, 'Picture this big, strong leader. They actually love flowers and feel flattered by your compliment! But being the leader, they can''t help wondering why they should have to carry your luggage…', 'On top of being a strong leader, they also have a very kind heart, so your begging convinces them and they agree to carry your luggage.', 'Unconvinced by your flattery, the leader refuses to carry your <kanji>luggage</kanji>, so you get on your <reading>knee</reading>s (に) and beg them to carry it.', 'N2', NULL),
(974, 'The next time you look at your face, also notice how much surface area it has.', 'Look at the face of all men put together. Measure how much surface area they all take up.', 'This <kanji>face</kanji> you''re looking at, that has a lot of <kanji>surface</kanji> area, belongs to <reading>men</reading> (めん). Not a single man, but all men put together. It''s hundreds of thousands of men''s faces in a row.', 'N3', NULL),
(975, 'Imagine a fish playing music using his fins. What kind of a song is it? Imagine it in your head as you see a sign with the fish fin rule on it.', 'Picture a group of ten (as per the rule) fish playing music with their fins. Now the song can be louder and more intricate. Imagine it playing in your head.', 'There’s also a <kanji>rule</kanji> in place to limit the amount of fish fin musicians. No more than <reading>ten</reading> (てん) are allowed to gather and play their music together. That’s the rule. No more than ten.', 'N1', NULL),
(977, 'Picture a map of the world with actual canopies attached to each government, representing the ruling governments of each place.', 'Think of all the people who''ve been labeled as fools by the government because they try to detach themselves from under their reach. It''s a pretty long list...', 'Try to get out from under this <kanji>government</kanji> canopy and you''ll be labeled a <reading>foo</reading>l (ふ). Unless... you can fool them!', 'N2', NULL),
(978, 'Imagine if you had your own machine to cure yourself if a tsunami came to where you live. It would be pretty convenient!', 'Imagine getting Jesus to cure half the people and feeding the other half lots of cheese and their wounds miraculously healing on their own! You''re a hero!', 'Alternative ways to <kanji>cure</kanji> yourself after a tsunami are either praying to <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ) or eating your weight in <reading>chee</reading>se (ち). I''m not sure if I have enough cheese for all the people that got hit though... Hmm, do you have cheese?

Before you make your decision, remember that whoever cures you will be able to <kanji>reign</kanji> over you and your people. So do you want to be led by Jesus or by some cheese? The choice is yours.', 'N3', NULL),
(979, 'Sure, there''s a lot of water in the valley, but you''re big too. It''s the perfect place for you to bathe... and good thing too, you''re getting smelly.', 'Imagine cooking egg yolks in the same water you bathe in.', 'You know what would make the <kanji>bath</kanji>ing experience even better? If you had some egg <reading>yolk</reading>s (よく) to eat. Just crack those yolks into the water you bathe in, and it''ll cook to perfection. Yum!', 'N2', NULL),
(980, 'Imagine yourself as a bamboo plant that laughs in heaven. What are you laughing about? Chuckle to yourself as you remember this kanji.', 'Now picture these bamboo again up in bamboo heaven, and remember this strange accent to remember that the bamboo plants up in bamboo heaven are laughing with nervous laughter at this bamboo war that’s a goin’ on up there.', 'There’s actually a war a goin’ on up in that there bamboo heaven, y’know? Ye might not have heard of it with all that there <kanji>laugh</kanji>in’ a goin’ on, but there definitely be a <reading>war a</reading> (わら) goin’ on up in bamboo heaven, y’hear? The bamboo are scared so that’s why they be laughin’ so much. They nervous.', 'N3', NULL),
(981, 'You may have to quit eating but you never have to quit saying words. Especially if you''re, well, trying to learn a whole new language full of them.', 'Picture Jesus scarfing down jalapeño peppers. He never quits. And then he says some words about how the jalapeños gave up their lives for this contest, so we better appreciate them. Thanks Jesus!', 'Oh, you''re probably wondering who won that spicy food contest after you <kanji>quit</kanji> and became obsessed with <kanji>word</kanji>s instead. It was <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ). Who did you expect? He was great with words AND never quitting when things got spicy.', 'N3', NULL),
(982, 'Perhaps they are related to heaven because the devil has horns? Perhaps they are connected to heaven because these are the horns of heaven''s enemies? The real reason they''re connected will be revealed in the reading mnemonic.', 'Imagine Genghis Khan taking horns off his enemies, and making a gate to heaven. The horn gate is connected to both heaven and Genghis Khan in that way.', 'They are <kanji>related</kanji> and <kanji>connected</kanji> to heaven because these are the horns that Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) made from his enemies. God said he could come into heaven if he made a gate of horns, so that''s what he did.', 'N3', NULL),
(984, 'Can you even imagine twenty different dialects in your head? Well I can tell you that there are definitely at least twenty of them in Japan alone.', 'It also helps that bento are delicious, and anyone with any dialect will love them.', 'The best way to learn someone''s <kanji>dialect</kanji> is to buy them a <reading>ben</reading>to (べん) from where they''re from. Not only do these bento have writing in their dialect on them, but their speech will immediately shift to match. Then you''ll be able to hear how they sound in their head and in private!', 'N1', NULL),
(985, 'Imagine all this politics and government talk as you shiver outside in the winter cold. You’re freezing and you’re bored, but you know this is the right (correct) thing to do.', 'Picture two different politicians, both wielding sabers, fighting to make the government the way they want it to be! Who are you rooting for?', 'Everyone involved in <kanji>politics</kanji> and <kanji>government</kanji> wields a special <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい). If two political parties are arguing, they''ll whip out their sabers and start slashing away!', 'N3', NULL),
(986, 'Imagine running into the rice paddy and detaining the private sword holders one by one. They won''t get away this time!', 'If Ryu hadn''t shown up with this loophole you never would have detained all these private sword guys by yourself!', 'You''re running out of steam and you''re afraid you might not be able to <kanji>detain</kanji> all of these guys when <reading>Ryu</reading> (りゅう) from Street Fighter appears. He hates swords, and will only use fists because it''s the honorable way to fight, so he starts helping you detain them!

He''s also found a <reading>loo</reading>phole (る) in the system and you don''t have to be super gentle with these guys either. So detain them as forcefully as you want. It is your rice paddy they''re in, after all.', 'N3', NULL),
(987, 'You convince the officials questioning you that your evidence can be trusted and it''s <kanji>proof</kanji> that you definitely didn''t have anything to do with any law breaking or murdering.', 'You better hope the Shougun believes your evidence is accurate, because he''s known to kill people willy-nilly when he thinks they''re lying.', 'The officials force you to present this <kanji>evidence</kanji> in front of the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう), himself. Your knees quake, you feel like you''re gonna be sick, but you try to stand up tall and tell him everything. After all, you know your <kanji>proof</kanji> is correct, so you shouldn''t be so scared.', 'N1', NULL),
(988, 'One squid got stuck in his own building, but no one could save him because going in would be too risky. Poor little squid.', 'The squid cries, "I can build one that isn''t so risky next time!" Ken shakes his head, "I''m not letting you do anymore risky things, you''re coming with me from now on." Ken scoops up the squid into a bucket, hoists him over his shoulder, and walks off into the sunset.

Who knows what adventures await our new duo. All we know is that they''ll definitely be getting into some risky business.', '"It''s too <kanji>risky</kanji>, leave me!" the squid cries. "I''ll save you, underwater citizen! Nothing is too risky for <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai!" The building is collapsing on the squid, the risk is so great, but Ken the samurai dives in and saves him!', 'N3', NULL),
(989, 'Imagine a dangerous prison surrounded by cliffs and snakes. You wouldn''t want to be here either, would you?', 'Imagine staring at the key dangling right outside of the dangerous prison you''re trapped in. Would you take the chance and go for it?', 'Lucky for you, even though you''re stuck in this <kanji>dangerous</kanji> place, there is hope. There is a <reading>key</reading> (き). All dangerous prisons have a key. You just have to find and steal it so you can avoid the cliffs and snakes!', 'N3', NULL),
(990, 'Imagine needing this kind of horrible proof to believe something exists. Maybe you should have more faith in what we teach.', 'I suppose you''ll never forget that narwhals exist now that your son has one permanently stuck in him.', 'Oh, and the child we had to narwhal stab to prove they <kanji>exist</kanji> - that''s your <reading>son</reading> (そん). Maybe you should have listened and kept your son out of the Narwhal Danger <reading>Zone</reading> (ぞん) after we warned you about it.', 'N3', NULL),
(991, 'Visualize yourself doing this. The numbers just come to you. It''s just what you''re good at. Your specialty is something no one else in the world is as good at as you!', 'Picture yourself at a centaur funeral. Tons of centaurs standing around and stamping their hooves as you make your measurement and stick a cross into the ground.', 'Hearing of your amazing <kanji>specialty</kanji> and cross measurement abilities, a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん) from a far off land recruits you for a job. The centaur’s father has just passed away and the centaur wants you to measure the rice paddies that have been left to him.', 'N2', NULL),
(992, 'Looking at the sun straight on hurts. It’s bad for your eyes and burns your brain. Imagine how painful it is to stare at the sun.', 'Picture yourself bowling without the power of sight. It would be awfully difficult to do well. You’d just have to concentrate really hard on the sound of the pins and determine where you need to throw the ball.', 'After you’ve blinded yourself from the <kanji>dare</kanji> of looking at the sun and losing sight in each eye, the guy dares you to go <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう). You’re blind now, so bowling will be quite the challenge.', 'N1', NULL),
(993, 'Seriously, you don''t need a forehead table. It''s totally unnecessary to have a table made of foreheads and is a superfluous purchase. First of all, getting all those foreheads is a waste of people. Second, forehead tables aren''t very strong. Superfluous, superfluous, superfluous.', 'Now every time you visit Joe, you’ll have to look at his giant, superfluous forehead table. Imagine how gross it looks, with dozens of foreheads protruding from its surface. Only Joe would buy such a thing.', 'The only person you know with a <kanji>superfluous</kanji> forehead table is <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう), the ten foot tall, simple farmhand.', 'N1', NULL),
(994, 'Try to imagine standing your whole life on a big, slanted slope, like one of these buildings that are so anti-Osaka. Wouldn''t be easy, would it? By the way, although this kanji literally means "slope," it''s mainly just used in the name for Osaka and to refer to the Osaka region, so that''s the meaning we''ll focus on.', 'Han loves Osaka for its gritty, port-city vibe, which reminds him of the spaceport cantinas he used to frequent. And he chooses to live on top of a slope because he wants to be closer to space… obviously.', 'Who lives in <kanji>Osaka</kanji> at the top of a big <kanji>slope</kanji>? It''s <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo.', 'N3', NULL),
(1153, 'Imagine a hat walking about on your head, pacing back and forth deep in thought. See the hat stroke its chin in contemplation as it tries to think up a plan to get off your noggin and back inside.', 'Watch as the hat begins to grin. This plan is so simple it’s sure to work. Now watch as the hat obtains the key and enters the locked door. What sort of mischief will the hat get into once inside?', 'The <kanji>plan</kanji> that made this hat stop right in its tracks was very simple. First, obtain the <reading>key</reading> (き) to the house. Then, use the key to get in the front door. How simple!', 'N1', NULL),
(837, 'Imagine watching a criminal after he''s done stealing from a bank. He''s all alone. He stops throwing the money around. And cries. So so sad.', 'Imagine being so sad that your entire body fills with heat. That heat even makes squiggly heat lines pop out above and around you.', 'The <kanji>sad</kanji> feelings fill the criminal''s body with <reading>he</reading>at (ひ). This heat spreads and radiates out. If you walk next to them on the street, you''ll feel the sad heat floating by you.', 'N3', NULL),
(976, 'Imagine having a drum in your mouth and rejoicing about it! Imagine how loud it would be!', 'Imagine if people could just stop the rejoicing of others by locking their mouths shut with keys. What a sad world.', 'You loudly <kanji>rejoice</kanji> for a while when suddenly someone runs over and shoves a <reading>key</reading> (き) in your mouth, locking it shut. You can''t rejoice anymore! You need that key to unlock your mouth!', 'N3', NULL),
(995, 'Know your radicals! I shouldn''t even have to tell you this by now.', 'Picture Genji, the original Japanese protagonist, walking through the streets. He''s signing his name for swooning ladies and gentlemen. Genji is the OG (original Genji).', 'The <kanji>original</kanji> Japanese hero was <reading>Gen</reading>ji (げん), from "The Tale of Genji."', 'N3', NULL),
(996, 'Imagine this thin thread of rice paddy. It looks so detailed because all the rice paddy stuff is contained in this thin thread shape. You could <em>maybe</em> get one narrow line of rice on this thing.', 'Picture the cyborg eating a really thin piece of rice. Now it''s completely full! What a smart, thrifty cyborg.', 'This <kanji>thin</kanji> rice paddy is for your <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい). You gave your cyborg a very thin human stomach, so it can only digest one thin line of rice at a time.', 'N2', NULL),
(997, 'Imagine having some medicine that smells and tastes like fresh cut flowers. Smell the smell in your nose and taste the taste on your tongue.', 'Picture a yak in your mind. Pretend you are riding atop the yak as it transports this medicine. Feel the fur of the yak in your hands as you cling on for dear life.', 'The best way to transport all the <kanji>medicine</kanji> on land is with a <reading>yak</reading> (やく). Yaks are big, strong, and very capable of transporting flower medicine like these.', 'N3', NULL),
(998, 'Really focus in on your nose. Your nostrils get huge, and your nose is huge. Smell the rice and its freshness in your imagination. Your nose is amazing.', 'Honestly, is there anything better to do with your nose than smell 花s? 花s and noses are a match made in heaven (unless you have pollen allergies…).', 'As you sniff your rice paddies, an unexpected smell wafts into your <kanji>nose</kanji>: <reading>花</reading>s (はな). Oh no — did you accidentally plant 花s instead of rice? Oh well, the 花s are putting your nose in such a state of bliss that you actually think things might be better this way.', 'N2', NULL),
(999, 'Picture the shellfish leader in your mind and make sure to pay special attention to the knife that''s sticking out of his side. It''s right in the groove of his shell.', 'Try saying かわ out the side of your mouth like the leader of the shellfish and you''ll probably say がわ too. Don''t stick a knife in your side though, we won''t pay your medical bills when you complain to us that this mnemonic got you injured.', 'Because of this knife in his <kanji>side</kanji>, he can''t pronounce certain words correctly. The most obvious one is when he says <reading>川</reading> (かわ), but it comes out of the side of his mouth as がわ.', 'N3', NULL),
(1000, 'Imagine trying to carve some fish with an axe, to get them ready to eat. Not an easy thing to do. If you''re a trained axe soldier, it''ll probably be a lot easier. So picture yourself chopping off fins as an expert axe soldier.', 'Imagine this soldier lighting a bale of hay on fire to cook his fish. The hay burns really fast, but it should be enough.', 'Uh-oh, it looks like this axe <kanji>soldier</kanji> was so excited about chopping the fins off his fish that he didn''t think about how he was going to cook them. The only thing nearby to light on fire is a bale of <reading>hay</reading> (へい). Well, it''s not the best thing, but at least it will get these fish cooked.', 'N2', NULL),
(1001, 'Picture a long Viking hall, with a big table and tons of chairs. All of the Vikings are gossiping and sharing all their dirt on their enemies.', 'Now picture all of the Vikings in the hall spitting dirt with one breath and shoveling doughnuts in with the next. It''s pretty gross!', 'While the Vikings gather to spill the dirt in their <kanji>hall</kanji>, they also stuff their faces with <reading>dou</reading>ghnuts (どう). Ah yes, Vikings love to eat doughnuts when they talk trash.', 'N4', NULL),
(1002, 'In olden days, salt was so valuable it was used as money. So imagine the gun buried in the dirt, its mouth vigilantly guarding the plate of salt.', 'Are you sure you want to take her salt? Sure, she owes you, but I don''t think you want to argue with the mouth of a gun.', 'When you see the <kanji>salt</kanji>, you think to yourself, "<reading>She owe</reading>s (しお) me big time, so I''m going to go ahead and snatch her salt!" Who is she, you ask? She''s the lady who owns the salt. And the gun in the dirt, of course.', 'N2', NULL),
(1003, 'Picture this yurt, with towels laid out as seats for everyone who visits.', 'Imagine the sexiest person you''ve ever seen, using a towel as a seat next to you in this yurt. Maybe they''re in a bathing suit. Maybe they''re completely covered up. Either way, they''re very sexy.', 'Something about using a towel for a <kanji>seat</kanji> is very <reading>sexy</reading> (せき) to you. Probably because you associate it with sexy people using towel seats on the beach.', 'N3', NULL),
(1004, 'Imagine you''re a shellfish in the cold, cold winter. The water is freezing but you must tough it out so no one will say you''re a failure in spring when they find your dead, defeated shellfish body.', 'Imagine being a shellfish freezing in the cold winter water, but you avoid failure by muttering haiku over and over to keep you warm and determined.', 'The best way to avoid <kanji>failure</kanji> is to say a little <reading>hai</reading>ku (はい) to distract you from the cold. In fact, this can work in any situation where you''re afraid of defeat. Say a haiku aloud, it will calm your nerves and warm you from the inside.', 'N3', NULL),
(1005, NULL, 'Imagine you have a hankering for your favorite fruit. You gotta get in your car and put the pedal to the metal! Feel the wind in your hair as your car speeds down the road and listen to that engine purr.', 'They sell <kanji>fruit</kanji> at the store, and you need to drive your <reading>ca</reading>r (か) to get there. A car is the fastest way to fruit.', 'N3', NULL),
(1007, 'Imagine picking fruit off an ume tree this way, shooting them off the branches with your gun. You take aim at an unsuspecting plum — BANG! The gunshot rings in your ears and the smell of gunpowder fills the air. The ume branch is now one plum lighter. ', 'Imagine eating your harvest of ume. Taste the ripe fruity flesh of these Japanese plums as you stuff your gluttonous face.', 'You''ve probably heard of <kanji>ume</kanji> before, depending on your exposure to Japan, but an <reading>ume</reading> (うめ) is a <kanji>Japanese plum</kanji>.', 'N1', NULL),
(1008, NULL, 'Listen to this cow moo. Can you do a better moo than this moo cow? Nope.', '<kanji>Nothing</kanji> can <reading>moo</reading> (む) quite like a cow can moo. Cows are the best at going moo. That’s a fact.', 'N2', NULL),
(1009, 'Tying two fingers together with a good luck thread is very common where you''re from.', 'This ketsup is exactly what you were looking for, but now it won''t stop. You need to get out of this bind by untying the thread on your finger. But it won''t budge. It''s covered in ketsup. Help! You''re going to drown!', 'After you <kanji>bind</kanji> your fingers, the good luck starts pouring in. Literally. Suddenly <reading>ketsu</reading>p (けつ) starts pouring down from the heavens.', 'N1', NULL),
(1010, 'Imagine all the dumb things you blabber about, with your mouth open big and wide. Saying things you shouldn''t say, causing all kinds of drama. Shame on you.', 'Who knew you could cause people to live in an inn instead of their own homes with your big mouth...', 'Your big mouth is the <kanji>cause</kanji> behind all your friends and family moving to an <reading>inn</reading> (いん). They move to the inn so they don''t have to live with you and hear you talk anymore.', 'N3', NULL),
(1011, 'Vikings get a lot of nasty stuff on their mouths from their messy eating and pillaging, so it makes sense that they''d all have mouth towels. It''s as ordinary and normal as it gets, at least amongst the vikings.', 'When you ask Joe about his Viking mouth towel, he just tells you, "It''s normal for everyone to have mouth towels. Totally ordinary." Now that you think about it, he really is a messy dude, so he probably needs it.', 'It''s <kanji>normal</kanji> for a viking to have a mouth towel, but your sloppy farmhand friend <reading>Joe</reading>(じょう) has a mouth towel too. That''s a habit that''s only <kanji>normal</kanji> for vikings, so why does he have one?', 'N3', NULL),
(1012, 'Yes, you can even tell a tequila drunk from a vodka drunk based on the sounds they make. (Imagine how exactly they''d be different.) To be able to say what sound each kind of drunkard makes, it takes a very discerning, discriminating mind.', 'All chic people show some kind of discernment. For some it''s about discerning what''s fashionable. For you, it''s about discriminating one kind of drunk sound from another. That''s your version of chic.', '<kanji>Discerning</kanji> individuals like you are considered <reading>chic</reading> (しき). What''s more chic than your <kanji>discriminating</kanji> way of listening?', 'N3', NULL),
(1013, 'Think of all the criminals you know. Even if what they did was a mistake, it was still some type of injustice, right? It was something the rest of the world sees as a negative thing.', 'This is the heat of embarrassment, of knowing you did something negative or wrong.', 'When you commit an <kanji>injustice</kanji>, you''ll know because <reading>he</reading>at (ひ) fills your body. Maybe you''ve never done any kind of big thing, but when you make a <kanji>mistake</kanji> your face fills with heat and you turn red!', 'N3', NULL),
(1014, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Imagine keeping Genghis Khan completely dry all day every day. He won''t even get wet to bathe. Instead he makes you rub his body with dry sand and salts.', 'You need to <kanji>dry</kanji> Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん). He hates being wet, all of the Mongols do, so your job is to dry his entire body. And his clothes, and his horse. In fact, when it rains, you have to ride next to him keeping him dry by holding an umbrella above his head.', 'N2', NULL),
(1015, 'Imagine the sun in the sky. Its rays blind you as it corrects all of the errors you’ve made.', 'Imagine the sun being so absolutely consumed with rage that it catches a zeppelin on fire and crashes it. Feel the heat from the grounded zeppelin as it smolders.', 'Then, a <reading>ze</reading>ppelin (ぜ) flies in front of the sun, blocking it out. <kanji>Absolutely</kanji>, the sun cannot ignore this transgression. Therefore, the sun corrects the zeppelin’s evil ways by frying it with sunshine and bringing it to the ground.', 'N1', NULL),
(1016, 'Imaging taking your shoes off, hiking up your pants, and holding all of your things above your head as you ford across the tsunami.', 'Your new fording techniques have increased the Shougun''s military power! Congrats, I guess.', 'Your ability to <kanji>ford</kanji> this tsunami impresses everyone who witnesses it, especially the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). Now that you''ve taught him a tsunami can be forded, he''s going to start sending his troops through them instead of waiting for them to pass.', 'N1', NULL),
(1018, 'Picture the government bear looking through government documents and snorting gruffly as it decides what to sign and what to reject. It has a little government sash and hat.', 'Picture Genghis Khan patting the first bear government worker on the back as they start their first day working for the government.', 'The first <kanji>government</kanji> that allowed bears was set up by Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん). He was all about conquering people and then using them as efficiently as possible. He discovered that bears are incredibly useful in government buildings as government workers.', 'N3', NULL),
(1019, 'Imagine being on the roof above a festival. Can you guess what''s happening below you based on the sounds and smells?', 'And what''s Satsuki doing up here, anyway? Your guess is as good as mine. Now imagine you and her trying to guess what kind of festival it is.', 'To get a better <kanji>guess</kanji>, you ask <reading>Satsu</reading>ki (さつ) for help. She''s that little girl you know from some earlier mnemonics, and she happens to be up on the roof with you. Picture her as you ask, "What''s your guess, Satsuki?"', 'N3', NULL),
(1020, 'Picture all of the people in your clan under this canopy digging with all their might, trying to find the bottom. "Isn''t there... no bottom though?" you think to yourself.', 'The bottom is full of water, and everyone and everything down there is soaked through now.', 'Suddenly someone down in the hole shouts that they''ve reached the <kanji>bottom</kanji>, but it''s full of water and they''re all <reading>soak</reading>ed (そこ)!', 'N2', NULL),
(1021, 'Picture a person you love. Feel the joy of love in your heart as you brave the winter to see them. Your cleats crunch the snow underfoot, it''s cold outside, but you''re kept warm and happy by that feeling of love.', 'Imagine seeing your love with your own two eyes after a long time apart. It feels great to be reunited and see eye to eye once again. Imagine this feeling.', 'Walking through the winter snow with your cleats on, you''ve gotta keep your <reading>eye</reading> (あい) on the prize. It''s a really hard journey, but you keep your eye on the prize, which is your <kanji>love</kanji> and the time you''ll get to spend with them.', 'N3', NULL),
(1022, 'Doesn''t matter who, but you''re going to catch someone from a government office using this net, it doesn''t matter who.', 'Imagine the show they put on. You''ll call your show "Exactly 8 Hours of Bureaucracy."', '<kanji>Government office</kanji> and <kanji>political office</kanji> people put on the best <reading>show</reading>s (しょ). That''s why you want to capture someone from there with your net.', 'N2', NULL),
(1023, 'Imagine the things you''d warn people about. What is it about winter that''s so dangerous?', 'The winter cold will make you ache
Your whole body will start to shake
Don''t go outside
I''ll eat your cake', 'To make sure people listen to your <kanji>warn</kanji>ing, you threaten to take away their <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい). People will do anything for cake, so when you bring it up, they actually take your attempts to warn them about winter seriously.', 'N3', NULL),
(1024, 'Perhaps you have a person in mind that your heart comes back to again and again. Associate that person with this notion and kanji, and this''ll be easy to memorize!', 'Don’t let your love become a rental. Wait for the right person to come around.', 'But, remember that your <kanji>romance</kanji> isn’t a <reading>ren</reading>tal (れん). You really wanna reserve it for the right person!', 'N2', NULL),
(1025, 'How else would a Viking memorize something? Imagine having to memorize sea charts for your Viking navigation. Look and see the maps in your brain as you memorize them.', 'Imagine yourself memorizing the sound of a cock crow and using it to play jokes on your fellow Vikings. Cock-a-doodle-doo!', 'Every morning, a <reading>cock</reading> (かく) crows and rouses the Vikings. Seeing this, one of the Vikings <kanji>memorize</kanji>s the cock crow so that he can mimic it and play pranks on his fellow Vikings.', 'N3', NULL),
(1026, 'Imagine saying your theory out loud to a guard about how what he''s guarding isn''t even there. Imagine how he would react.', 'Which theory do you believe? That there is nothing or that there are sets of things?', 'The guard listens to your <kanji>theory</kanji> but then he says his OWN! He thinks there are two <reading>sets</reading> (せつ) of guarded items, and he''s only guarding one. According to his theory, either one could be real! His theory is more plausible, don''t you think?', 'N3', NULL),
(1027, 'Imagine having poop in your cape. It would smell bad and anyone close to you would be able to smell right through the illusion.', 'Genji grabs the cape before you can stop him and he puts it on. You can see the illusion fade, he keeps smiling but more tears start streaming down his face. "A wonderful illusion, the best I''ve ever seen," he chokes out and marches away, without looking back.', 'Somehow the poop cape <kanji>illusion</kanji> goes over really well. The crowd is giving you a standing ovation and right in the center is <reading>Gen</reading>ji (げん). He marches up to you, wipes a tear from his eye, and says, "Your illusion cape is wonderful. I must have it!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1412, 'Imagine getting really close to a sheep and seeing all the tiny detailed curls in their wool. There are hidden patterns you''d never noticed before now!', 'Picture the Shougun, leaning in close to inspect the detailed patterns all over the sheep. His nose is so close, he can probably smell all the little detailed nuances of the sheep''s musk too.', 'The reason these sheep are so <kanji>detailed</kanji> is that they are trying to impress the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). If they''re fancy enough, perhaps the Shougun won’t kill them for their meat and make their wool into clothing.', 'N1', NULL),
(1569, 'Picture this scene from the drama as dramatically as you can. The pig stares into the tiger''s eyes, "Tiger, you wouldn''t–uh!" The pig''s eyes slowly lower to see the knife sticking into its gut, the tiger''s hand still holding it. "This is what you get, you pig," the tiger spits back, twisting.', 'Picture Gecki acting out the part of the tiger and the pig in this drama. It''s so intense, you''re moved to tears. Bravo Gecki, bravo!', 'This <kanji>drama</kanji> is being put on by <reading>Gecki</reading> (げき), your childhood pet gecko. Gecki is playing all the parts, because as true drama fans know, one-gecko-shows are the highest form of art.', 'N2', NULL),
(1028, 'Imagine saying things to this river. What would you say? Flow harder! Babble better! Picture yourself shouting at the river as the water moves along.', 'Picture this raccoon captain and his raccoon crew coming down the river. The raccoons are all chittering about as they paddle the boat down the river.', 'While giving <kanji>instruction</kanji> to the river, you see a small boat coming down it. Inside the miniature boat is a rac<reading>coon</reading> (くん) captain and his raccoon crew. The raccoon captain is instructing them, “Stroke! Stroke!”', 'N2', NULL),
(1030, 'Make sure you know those radicals!', 'Imagine fitting a cucumber into the string of your bow. Imagine the smell of it as you draw the string back. Mm, take a quick bite, then let it loose.', 'You have a <kanji>bow</kanji> in one hand and in the other... a <reading>cu</reading>cumber (きゅう). You''re going to shoot this cucumber with this bow into the salad you''re making. Many people don''t know this, but this makes your cucumbers taste better.', 'N1', NULL),
(1031, 'Imagine announcing to all the denizens of the playground that you''ve licked their slide clean. For some reason, no one seems to be rushing to play on it…', 'Honestly, after that big announcement, you deserve a Coke. Public speaking''s hard, so you should treat yourself to a Coke whenever you announce something — regardless of whether you had to lick something gross beforehand.', 'After you <kanji>announce</kanji> the slide is clean, you take a big swig of <reading>Coke</reading> (こく). The dirty slide left a nasty taste in your mouth, but the Coke is helping to wash it away. ', 'N3', NULL),
(1032, 'Imagine sorting all the different types of grains by their weight.', 'Picture the two chutes you use to separate the different types of grains. One has a sign that says "Light Chute" and the other says "Heavy Chute."', 'You sort all of the different <kanji>kind</kanji>s and <kanji>type</kanji>s by putting them in the right <reading>chu</reading>tes (しゅ). They slide away, probably to some big type-based piles you can''t see.', 'N3', NULL),
(1033, 'Feel the dirt spatter your face as you zip around off-road trails with your sheep under your arm. You will attain glory! You will attain greatness!', 'Feel the pain of the tattoo needle as you get this commemorative tattoo. You''re getting this tattoo on your forehead, of course, because you want to make sure everyone sees it.', 'After you <kanji>attain</kanji> that <kanji>plural</kanji> combination of glory, greatness, and fame, you get a <reading>tattoo</reading> (たつ) to celebrate: "world''s greatest," in gothic lettering, above a picture of you and your sheep on your scooter. ', 'N3', NULL),
(1034, 'Picture this big geoduck shoveling rice down in a flurry of geoduckness. Hear the sloshing and goopy glugging as the geoduck gorges itself on every type of rice.', 'Picture this big, rice-engorged geoduck, too big to move. It''s now in a wheel chair and on a rice IV. Imagine its wheelchair squeaking by as it wheezes and groans — ruined by all those types of rice.', 'While eating every <kanji>type</kanji> and <kanji>kind</kanji> of rice from every <kanji>category</kanji>, the big geoduck has become even bigger. Its body is <reading>rui</reading>ned (るい). It''s painfully obese. This geoduck has eaten itself to ruin.', 'N3', NULL),
(1035, 'Stand up and look at the happy news today! It definitely isn''t sad or depressing.', 'Because your reporting is done from home, most of the news is about how your plants are growing. What the cat did this morning. And it certainly isn''t the news without the weather!', 'You research and <kanji>report</kanji> the <kanji>news</kanji> from <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう). Only after, once all of the news is stamped onto the stools, do you leave your home.', 'N3', NULL),
(1037, 'Picture this bamboo temple compared to some temples made of stone or wood. It''s the same size, shape, and it has the same number of worshipers. It''s equal in every way.', 'While many things in とうきょう may not be created equal, the temples are, at least. ', 'The bamboo temple is also an <kanji>equal</kanji> distance away from the other temples in <reading>とう</reading>きょう. When you look at a map of とうきょう temples, you can measure them out and they''ll all be perfectly equal.', 'N3', NULL),
(1038, 'Imagine how much steam a tsunami could create. Hear the steam hissing off of the water and feel the moisture from the steam washing over your body.', 'Steam is very light, so carrying this box of steam energy will be no problem. Picture yourself wandering about with a box of steam in one hand and a key in the other.', 'In order to contain this <kanji>steam</kanji> energy and store it for later, you lock it up in a box with a <reading>key</reading> (き). That way the energy can be used at a later time. Provided you don’t lose the key, that is.', 'N1', NULL),
(1039, 'Think about what precious thing this leader borrowed from you so long ago. What was it? Think about how angry you are about not having the thing returned to you.', 'Come up with all the terrible things you do to this leader in the shack. Hey, it''s just a little payback for the borrowing he did.', 'In your rage, you decide to find this leader, take back what''s yours, and in return, <kanji>borrow</kanji> the leader''s life. So you found him, grabbed him, and threw him into a <reading>shack</reading> (しゃく) — where you "borrow" a few parts of the leader himself, and do other terrible things to him.', 'N4', NULL),
(1040, 'Imagine you''re the gambler. Feel the heat intensify as your nostrils fill with the smell of baked goods.', 'Imagine this guy running around baking in the fire of his home, only this time with a Yankees hat and jersey on. He doesn''t bake for them anymore, but he still loves the team.', 'He used to <kanji>bake</kanji> for the <reading>Ya</reading>nkees (や). Baseball cakes, bat baguettes, that kind of thing. A career-ending kitchen injury forced him to retire. That''s when he developed a gambling problem. But he still likes to bake whenever the Yankees are on TV.', 'N2', NULL),
(1041, 'Imagine how it feels sitting on dirt. Is it comfortable? Probably not. Maybe you feel a little uneasy about just sitting on plain dirt. Try to recall this feeling whenever you encounter this kanji.', 'This Czar you met is ruthless and has formally banned all non-sitting activities. Imagine the excruciating pain, the stiffness, the cramping in your legs — all caused by this 24-7 sit-a-thon the Czar has cruelly decreed.', 'Who decided people have to <kanji>sit</kanji> like this? The C<reading>za</reading>r (ざ). The Czar wants to be above everyone, so he makes them sit on the dirt under canopies while he goes around on horseback or something, towering over them as they sit.', 'N3', NULL),
(1042, 'Imagine someone close to you experiencing the death of their heart. They look right at you but they''ve already started to forget everything. I know this is not a fun thing to imagine, but use that emotion to help you remember this kanji, not forget it.', 'You know, that''s probably why so many old people go bowling. They forgot everything else.', 'When someone <kanji>forget</kanji>s everything they''re left with only one thing to do: go <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう). Bowling is easy, and there''s nothing to forget. You pick up a ball. You drop it. Everything else is automatically taken care of.', 'N3', NULL),
(1043, 'Imagine this soapy tsunami, washing all the things in town. Hear the soapy waves bubbling with cleanliness as they crash down around you.', 'Imagine these centaurs frolicking around in the aftermath of the tsunami previous. It''s just like a big bathtub for them. Hear them splashing and neighing in the water.', 'It wasn''t just the town that got a <kanji>wash</kanji>, it was the <reading>cen</reading>taurs (せん) too. (Centaurs happen to live in this town.) And being half horse, centaurs have a lot of hair that needs washing. Luckily, the soap tsunami gave them a good wash too.', 'N3', NULL),
(1044, 'Imagine finding this treasure chest and donning the mighty chest armor. Feel the weight of the golden chest piece and the vast protection it grants you.', 'If you can remember this kanji''s reading and meaning with a Canadian accent, you''ll have no problem, eh.', 'Where is this <kanji>chest</kanji> armor from? It''s from the moon. Now how would you say that if you were Canadian? It''s from the <reading>moon, eh</reading> (むね). Right, this here chest armor is from the moon, eh.', 'N2', NULL),
(1045, 'As you visualize the treasure chest you realize that the moon was your face, the grass your hair, and the treasure chest your brain! It was you all along!', 'Use your brain like Nostradamus and try to see the future. You see... you see... huh, your brain is just surrounded by kanji.', 'If you can unlock the secrets of your <kanji>brain</kanji> you''ll have powers like <reading>No</reading>stradamus (のう). Nostradamus was able to look inward at his own hidden brain treasure chest and see the future, so maybe you can be like him too.', 'N2', NULL),
(1046, 'Picture this leader in their mask. Listen to them chant prayers that reverberate throughout the building.', 'Imagine the monk/priest''s bright, pure soul shining behind the mask. You''ll never actually see it, unless you''re becoming a monk/priest yourself, and then your soul will need protecting too.', 'This <kanji>monk</kanji> or <kanji>priest</kanji> is wearing a mask to protect their <reading>sou</reading>l (そう). You aren''t a monk or a priest, so they don''t want your not-religious-enough eyes to dirty their soul.', 'N1', NULL),
(1047, 'Try out Zen meditation for a little bit. Look away from your computer or phone or tablet and let your life be simple for a second. Just let it all go and free your spirit from all the noise.', 'Without a new reading to learn, you should have time to close your eyes and clear your mind. Zen it out, friend.', 'The reading for this kanji is <reading>Zen</reading> (ぜん), just like the <kanji>Zen</kanji> in <kanji>Zen Buddhism</kanji>. Pretty easy to remember, no? Just remember that the reading is the same as the meaning. It’s easy!', 'N1', NULL),
(1049, 'Imagine getting a magical lip ring. First, you have to find a passable one. Then, you have to go through the pain of having your lip pierced. Is it even possible to imagine how painful that would be?!', 'It was possible to get this passable car thanks to your lip ring. Hooray!', 'Since anything and everything is <kanji>possible</kanji> with your new <kanji>passable</kanji> lip ring, you use it to get a new <reading>ca</reading>r (か). You go to the car dealership and ask for a free (passable) car. The manager is impressed with your lip ring and gladly hands over the keys to the latest model of your favorite car. Not bad.', 'N3', NULL),
(1050, 'Imagine a world where you''re not allowed to say anything except for at noon. At that moment, after you''ve been staring at the clock, waiting for that opportunity, you burst forth with things you want to say. But, once it hits 12:01, you are grabbed and your mouth is covered. You are not permitted to say anything outside of that time.', 'Imagine going to big Kyoto where everything is fine. Then, go to little Kyoto where it''s not. Little Kyoto has the short きょ (instead of the long one), and this is how you can remember the difference.', 'Where do these archaic horrible rules exist? The smaller version of Kyoto, Little <reading>Kyo</reading>to (きょ). Normal Kyoto is just fine. This smaller one is where a group of people went to create their own little town with their own dumb rules.', 'N3', NULL),
(1051, 'What with the snow and wind, winter also tends to knock trees down flat… which is probably why the tree radical on the left looks all flat and squished here. That''s another way to remember this kanji is a counter for flat stuff!', 'Now imagine yourself counting with these mice you just smashed. They''re freshly dead, so there''s not much of a stench yet, but you still feel bad about using dead mice to help you count. Imagine yourself counting with these mice as you try to hold back tears from all the murder you just did.', 'As you count each <kanji>counter for flat objects</kanji>, a bunch of <reading>mi</reading>ce (まい) show up and start climbing all over you. This won''t do, so you take two slats and start smashing and flattening all the mice. Now that the mice are flat too, you can also use them as a counter for flat objects. Good job, mice.', 'N2', NULL),
(1052, 'Imagine watching this conflict from above the blue water on a boat. You can kind of see the action, but you can''t hear a thing.', 'Sabers are sharp, curved, and totally quiet. Great for underwater blue conflicts, and sneaky assassinations. ', 'As you watch below the <kanji>quiet</kanji> water, every once in a while a <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい) breaches the surface and quietly slices back down.', 'N3', NULL),
(1053, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Imagine actually getting an assignment to write a paragraph all about cookies. Would you write about your favorite cookies? Your eating methods?', 'You have to write an entire <kanji>paragraph</kanji> about a topic, so you choose <reading>coo</reading>kies (く).', 'N1', NULL),
(1054, 'Imagine standing in front of a forest with your jackhammer, staring at a sign with just this kanji on it. Tree plus tree with jackhammer equals prohibited. Hmm.', 'Picture the King angrily covering his ears as his subjects jackhammer trees. He screams and shouts, his crown bouncing on his head, and points at his new sign that prohibits the jackhammering of trees.', 'The person who decided to <kanji>prohibit</kanji> jackhammering two trees is the <reading>Kin</reading>g (きん) of England. He''s the only person who''s able to prohibit certain things, so it had to be him.', 'N2', NULL),
(1055, 'Imagine yourself doing a magic trick where you put a big sword in your mouth. You say: "Now, for my next illusion, I will consume a sword!"', 'It''s odd that there would be a "consume a sword" trick in your magic kit. It seems so real for a kit you can buy at the store.', 'The "<kanji>consume</kanji> a sword" illusion came with a cheap magic <reading>kit</reading> (きつ) you had.', 'N2', NULL),
(1056, 'Imagine the smoke stopping up your lungs and getting in your eyes. All you know other than the smoke is the sound of the helicopter that''s causing it to happen.', 'Imagine choking on the smoke and accepting that this is the end. Boohoo, if only you''d learned more kanji before the end!', 'The <kanji>smoke</kanji> is too much and you realize this is the <reading>en</reading>d (えん) for you. You''re gonna die here!', 'N3', NULL),
(1057, 'Say this sentence out loud. Incredible! You''ve used the power of your mouth to add words together in a sentence. Your parents must be very proud of you.', 'Imagine riding around in your new car, singing songs with your powerful mouth to the playlists you added to the fancy new radio.', 'Your parents are so proud of you being able to <kanji>add</kanji> words together that they surprise you with a new <reading>ca</reading>r (か). Wow! You add some stickers to your bumper and add your favorite playlist to the car radio. Let''s drive.', 'N3', NULL),
(1058, 'Picture yourself in a store, looking at a bunch of different bamboo stamps to make sure you don’t buy one that’s out of season, or one that has damaged joints. The season and the joints, that’s what quality bamboo is all about, and it’s why we have these stamps!', 'How many bamboo sets will you be buying this season? I''m getting 42 sets.', 'These <kanji>season</kanji> stamps are marked on bamboo <reading>sets</reading> (せつ). You can''t buy a single banana or a single grape, and just like that, if you want some nice seasonal bamboo, you have to buy them in sets.', 'N1', NULL),
(1059, 'Just make sure you get a really thirsty canine, because they''re going to need to drink a LOT of water to decrease the tsunami''s power.', 'Now you''re wondering if you made the right choice. The world might actually be a better place if Genji decreased his silly lover-boy antics a bit.', 'After you and your canine <kanji>decrease</kanji> the tsunami''s power, <reading>Gen</reading>ji (げん) comes out to thank you. That tsunami was on track to wash away Genji''s house, which would have forced him to majorly decrease his frivolous playboy activities.', 'N2', NULL),
(1060, 'Geoducks usually live in the ocean, but when they migrate to a river, they do so in a specific, predetermined order. Imagine what that sequence might be. Maybe in order of neck length, with the longest-necked geoducks going first? Or maybe in alphabetical order?', 'Imagine it''s June, and you''re on a beach somewhere watching a bunch of geoducks make their way to a river. Marvel at the biological clock that determines the June-based migration sequence for these fascinating creatures. ', 'Actually, they go in birth-month <kanji>order</kanji>, starting from <reading>June</reading> (じゅん). No one knows why they follow this June-based <kanji>sequence</kanji>. But the geoducks born in June always go first in the migration order.', 'N2', NULL),
(1061, 'Picture a valley in your mind. Now put a roof on it. Hmm. It isn''t <kanji>shape</kanji>d like a valley anymore.', 'Now imagine this taking shape before your eyes and becoming actual yogurt! You grab a giant spoon and take a bite.', 'Its new <kanji>appearance</kanji> looks familiar to you, like it''s a big <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう) cup! A valley with a roof on it looks exactly like a yogurt cup. Huh!', 'N3', NULL),
(1062, 'Picture a narwhal wrapped in a fuzzy towel blanket, with a towel tail cover, and some towel underpants. Everything is made of this type of cloth.', '"You fool, you aren''t a narwhal, that cloth isn''t for you!" they say. They''re so mean.', 'If you use this type of <kanji>cloth</kanji> for your clothes and blankets people will call you a <reading>foo</reading>l (ふ). They think you''re a fool who can''t tell the difference between human cloth and towel cloth.', 'N2', NULL),
(1063, 'Easy is easy!', 'I told you easy was easy.', 'What would be an <kanji>easy</kanji> reading for this kanji? <reading>い</reading>! The sound easy starts with!', 'N3', NULL),
(1064, 'Picture the talented person huddled over a pile of shellfish, whispering to them about the wealth they''ll soon be turned into.', 'You''d be wealthy too if you sold that cyborg you made. It may not have any shellfish in it and it can''t play the xylophone, but you could change that…', 'This talented person really starts accumulating <kanji>wealth</kanji> when their <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい) is unveiled. It''s a half-man, half-shellfish cyborg that plays the <reading>xy</reading>lophone  (ざい), and everyone wants to buy it.', 'N3', NULL),
(1065, 'Look to your right, if you see flowers (metaphorically, maybe) then you''re still young. Congratulations!', 'All young people have a walker looming just off to the side. A sign that your youth won''t last forever, dear summer child.', 'When <kanji>young</kanji> people look to the right, they''ll see their future: a <reading>walke</reading>r (わか). You know, those metal contraptions with tennis balls on the bottoms to make sure they don''t scrape the floor. Everything may be flowers and happiness now, but your future is in a walker.', 'N3', NULL),
(1066, '"Is it an adjective?"
"No, that''s the wrong part of speech."
"Is it a verb?"
"N-no! Of course not!"', 'You stare blankly at him. What part of speech is that?! It''s an animal, you know that. Oh! A noun. Duh.', 'The director asks you again, "What is the part of speech for the word <reading>shee</reading>p (し)?"', 'N2', NULL),
(1067, 'Imagine holding up an insect to the sun to compare the two. See how they both shine, but the sun shines more brightly than the insect.', 'Feel how dark and cold it is suddenly with all those condors blocking out the sun.', 'While you’re holding up an <kanji>insect</kanji> to the sun for comparison, a flock of <reading>con</reading>dors (こん) flies by, blocking out the sun. There are so many condors that they manage to totally block out the sun.', 'N1', NULL),
(1068, 'Imagine this washed up drunkard leader passed out in front of the gate to your house. You have to pass him whenever you enter or exit your house. He''s gross and he smells. Experience the stench in your nostrils. How he was ever the leader of a clan you have no idea.', 'Hear the crushing blows of bats against flesh as you imagine this scene in your mind. You''re beating the crap out of this guy with a bunch of bats, but hey — he had it coming.', 'Eventually you get sick and tired of this washed up <kanji>clan</kanji> leader and you gather your own <kanji>clique</kanji> to chase this guy away from your gate and your property. You and your friends grab some <reading>bats</reading> (ばつ) and use these bats to beat the drunkard leader. Being beaten with bats frightens him, and he scurries away.', 'N1', NULL),
(1069, 'Imagine yourself on this cliff. You desire a continuation for your journey, not wanting to stop here. Feel the strong wind on your body, blowing your hair about as you stand on the edge of the cliff. The leaves on the trees rustle in the breeze.', 'Picture yourself using the wrecking ball to knock down the two trees on the cliff. Hear the the trees crack and splinter from the force of the wrecking ball. That wrecking ball is the sound of continuation.', '<kanji>Continuation</kanji> is impossible unless you chop these trees down to make a bridge to the other side. You decide to use a <reading>wrecki</reading>ng (れき) ball. Luckily, there''s one right by you. So you take the wrecking ball and knock the trees over, making a bridge with them.', 'N2', NULL),
(1070, NULL, 'Your significant other had been watching you sleep with your tongue out for a while now, so you''re super embarrassed you wet the sheets with your nasty tongue drool. You feel very judged and embarrassed.', 'You’re sound asleep in bed when your <kanji>tongue</kanji> drops out of your mouth and you start to drool, getting saliva all over the bed <reading>sheet</reading> (した). You wake up with a soggy patch of sheet near your mouth and wipe the drool off your face. Your significant other sees the whole thing.', 'N1', NULL),
(1071, 'Imagine counting books on a bunch of bookshelves. One book, two book, red book, blue book!', 'Imagine how tedious it''d be to watch her counting one by one. Teach her your book counter ways!', 'You use this <kanji>book counter</kanji> method to count all the books <reading>Satsu</reading>ki (さつ) has in her house. Remember her? She''s got, like, a ton of books, but she''s just a little girl so she needs some help. That''s where your book counter know-how comes in handy.', 'N2', NULL),
(1072, 'You don''t want to float up into outer space and get all wet, do you? No? Then you better make sure you have a roof above your head.', 'Look closely up at outer space and you''ll see all of the uni floating around up there. They''re really dark, so it can be hard to notice them, but I promise you, they''re there.', 'Why isn''t it dry in <kanji>outer space</kanji>, you ask? Because of all the <reading>u</reading>ni (う) swimming around up there. They have nasty, wet, pointy spikes and they''re all over the place.', 'N2', NULL),
(1073, 'At the moment you can''t remember the reason you did it, but when you jumped off your roof you just floated in midair.', 'Oh Chewbacca, you lovable fuzzball. You better not drop me in midair, or I''ll die. I hope you bring me up there!', 'As you spin a bit in <kanji>midair</kanji> you look up and see <reading>Chew</reading>bacca (ちゅう) looking down at you from a spaceship. He''s laughing his Chewbacca laugh and you realize he has you trapped in his tractor beam.', 'N1', NULL),
(1074, 'Look around you. See all those busy people? Their souls are dying. Don''t end up like them.', 'Think about your busy schedule this week. Now cancel everything and just go bowling instead.', 'Take time out of your <kanji>busy</kanji> life and go <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう) once in a while. In fact, you should probably go bowling once a week at least. Nothing brings energy back to your life like some relaxing bowling.', 'N3', NULL),
(1075, 'If you look into the black hole, you can see your footwear right there, marked with the flag.', 'It''s amazing that these cute little reeds could grow in your footwear in the middle of a black hole. As a great philosopher once said, life finds a way.', 'You grab your <kanji>footwear</kanji> from the black hole. In your footwear (thanks to the shelter they provided), some <reading>ree</reading>ds (り) have grown in!', 'N1', NULL),
(1076, 'I hope this group is full of people who enjoy being inside someone else''s mouth, because they''re gonna be pretty mad when you let them out if they aren''t.', 'Imagine letting this group of, now very wet, people out of your mouth one by one. They don''t seem to care that they weigh a ton! That''s a large measurement, they should be proud.', 'This <kanji>group</kanji> is so big it takes you till <reading>dawn</reading> (だん) to get your measurement: they weigh a <reading>ton</reading> (とん) too!', 'N2', NULL),
(1077, 'Picture this violent fight scene in your brain. Imagine water locked in battle with the sun as the sun’s fire flares up and the water starts to sizzle.', 'Imagine the sun and the water trying to bowl. Hear the sound of bowling balls rolling down the lane and pins rattling around and crashing together.', 'But after a while, the sun and the water have had enough of <kanji>violence</kanji>. They decide to settle their differences with a friendly game of <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう). Bowling is a great way to relieve stress and in the end, they bond over it.', 'N2', NULL),
(1078, 'Imagine the sun getting blocked out as a tsunami rises up from the ocean. Feel the cold fear of death on you as the tsunami approaches. That tsunami is gonna mix you up.', 'Imagine yourself getting mixed up in this situation and the pterodactyl-sized condor picking you up. Feel the condor''s talons digging into your shoulders as it grabs you up and flies you towards the sun.', 'Just before the tsunami crashes over you, a <reading>con</reading>dor (こん) swoops out of the sky and grabs you up. This condor must be a <kanji>mix</kanji> — a normal condor is not strong enough to pick up a human. The condor must be mixed with something else. Like a pterodactyl.', 'N2', NULL),
(1079, 'Imagine yourself getting stabbed in the tongue with an umbrella. It’s very painful. Taste the blood and metal in your mouth as this happens to you.', 'Picture yourself squaring off with Macho Man Randy Savage in the middle of this riot. You have the advantage of having an umbrella, but Randy has the advantage of being himself. Picture yourself getting into a fight with him.', 'Just now you realize that the man you stabbed in the tongue with your umbrella was none other than Macho Man <reading>Ran</reading>dy Savage (らん). Being a professional wrestler, Macho Man Randy Savage is ready to throw down at a moment’s notice. A <kanji>riot</kanji> immediately erupts. It’s a battle royal with you and Randy at the epicenter.', 'N2', NULL),
(1080, 'Imagine Sean Connery saying “Don’t loiter – run, Junior!” and you should have a much easier time remembering this phrase if you remember it in his voice.', 'Imagine that you are Indiana Jones Junior and this is happening to you. Feel the terrible pain of your throbbing toe as you clutch it, with the bad guys catching up with you.', 'Deciding not to loiter any longer, Indiana Jones <kanji>Junior</kanji> takes his dad’s advice and starts to run. Unfortunately he wasn’t paying attention to where his feet were and he stubbed his <reading>to</reading>e (と) on a jagged rock. His toe is in so much pain that he can’t bear to run any longer.', 'N3', NULL),
(1081, 'Imagine loitering around near the sun temple. Maybe you acquire some illegal goods, maybe you just acquire a tan (it is a SUN temple, after all). ', 'Your eyes fill with tears under Tokugawa''s gaze and you drop everything you acquired to the ground. You bow before him apologizing. You''ll never do this again!', 'You managed to <kanji>acquire</kanji> some tobacco, but just as you''re about to leave <reading>Toku</reading>gawa (とく) Ieyasu comes marching up to you!! Or at least, his ghost does. He stares you dead in the eyes and says, "I didn''t found the shogunate for you to acquire illegal goods and kill your body toking on my soil."', 'N3', NULL),
(1082, 'Imagine sitting in your house all winter, meditating and thinking about how you can renew yourself.', 'Imagine being back in your house in winter. You''re thinking about all the kayaking you''re gonna do when winter ends. You''re even making the movements with your arms, so you''ll be a really renewed you.', 'Winter ends and, <kanji>renew</kanji>ed, you come out ready to start a new hobby: <reading>kay</reading>aking (かい)! Nothing says renewal like a brand new kayak and sense of purpose in life.', 'N2', NULL),
(1083, 'Imagine selling thread so you can continue living. You search for thread continually now. But are you gathering thread to continue to live, or living to continue gathering thread?', 'Continue selling thread and paying off the Bosozoku. It''s the only way you can continue to survive!', 'You have to <kanji>continue</kanji> to sell that thread because a Boso<reading>zoku</reading> (ぞく) bike gang is forcing you to do it. You see? If you want to continue to live, you have to appease this Bosozoku gang. You better keep selling!', 'N3', NULL),
(1084, 'Your scooter and your car, connected by a tiny little string, are the two things you take along with you wherever you go. ', 'The roof over your head? Rental. The clothes on your body? Rental. All of your friends? Rental. The only thing you permanently take along with you are your scooter and your car.', 'You <kanji>take along</kanji> your scooter and your car. Anything else, you can do a <reading>ren</reading>tal (れん) for! ', 'N3', NULL),
(1085, 'Imagine yourself as the good person being approached by this forlorn sheep without horns. Hear the clack of the horns together as the sheep drops them out of his mouth onto the ground and lets out a pathetic bleat. “Baa~aa~ah~”', 'Imagine yourself using your Zen magic to heal this sheep. Place your hands on the sheep’s horns and imagine returning them to their rightful location on the sheep’s head. Feel the soft wool of the sheep as he bleats in happiness.', 'Since you are <kanji>morally good</kanji>, you decide not to murder the sheep and harvest its organs. Instead, as a practitioner of the <kanji>good</kanji> teachings of <reading>Zen</reading> (ぜん) Buddhism, you decided to help the sheep out. Your Zen teachings have increased the power of your healing abilities, so you fix the sheep up in no time.', 'N1', NULL),
(1087, 'Imagine trying to kiss someone with a piece of thread between your lips. Feel the thread getting all entangled between the two of you. Thread entangled, tongues entangled, lips entangled — just a thready kiss of entanglement.', 'Imagine pausing this kiss to pull a thread out of your mouth and entangle it around a random rack in your room. Smooth move, Casanova! Your partner will totally love that.', 'This isn''t going well, so you pull out the weird kiss thread and <kanji>entangle</kanji> it around a <reading>rack</reading> (らく). Good thing that rack was there! Much better to have it entangled around a rack when you''re trying to kiss someone.', 'N2', NULL),
(1121, 'Imagine yourself cutting down some bamboo, thinking, "This is the reason I''m here, I need a flute, ASAP!" There is no other reason to be here besides this.', 'You''ve done what you came to do. You summoned an alien craft with your bamboo flute, gaining some alien tech in the process. Open up the box to see what tech awaits you.', 'You cut down the bamboo and whittle a <kanji>flute</kanji>. You start playing a song. You know the reason why. An alien spacecraft comes flying down and drops a box full of alien <reading>tech</reading> (てき) in front of you. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1122, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Look at a history book, behind each of those humans was a sheep, changing the world as we know it.', '<kanji>History</kanji> was full of famous <reading>shee</reading>p (し). Sure there are people and stuff, but historical sheep are where it''s at. There''s the famous sheep samurai, the death sheep who works for the yakuza, just tons!', 'N2', NULL),
(1553, 'Potatoes grow when flowers dry out. All the nutrients are going to the potato under the ground, so the flowers up top get sucked dry.', 'Picture an emo person sitting alone in a dark room, staring at a potato. "You and I are the same."', '<kanji>Potato</kanji>es are totally <reading>emo</reading> (いも) because of the way they dry out flowers. Emo people and potatoes both like to dry out flowers.', 'N1', NULL),
(1725, 'Imagine the village where you grew up. Can you see the dirt area where all your ancestors were buried?', 'Imagine crying as you bury your pet uni. Your tears can''t save it now. (Though it is a bit ironic, since it needed salt water.)', 'You don''t just <kanji>bury</kanji> dead relatives here. You also buried your dead pet <reading>u</reading>ni (う) in that dirt. You thought having an uni as a pet would be a good idea. Too bad uni need water to survive.', 'N2', NULL),
(1086, 'Imagine yourself as this distressed and troubled person. Taste the taste of sap and bark in your mouth as you nom on the tree with all your might.', 'Picture a very distressed condor watching you munch your mouth all over its tree house. You''ve made a condor enemy this day.', 'You thought you were the most <kanji>distressed</kanji> and <kanji>troubled</kanji> person in this situation, but then you look up and see a very equally distressed and troubled <reading>con</reading>dor (こん). The tree you''ve been mouthing is its home. And now you''ve slobbered all over it, ruining this condor''s day.', 'N3', NULL),
(1089, 'Imagine a disaster so bad that there are floods AND fires. That''s too much!', 'You better figure out a way to stop your cyborg from causing more disasters like this. Fix it now, or you''re gonna be in big trouble.', 'This <kanji>disaster</kanji> was caused by your <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい)! It''s malfunctioning, running around leaking large amounts of water and breathing fire. I guess you didn''t work out all the kinks before you let it roam wild.', 'N1', NULL),
(1090, 'Imagine this drunkard, squatting down under the tree to poop. You can smell the poop and a faint stench of alcohol in the air. How could he not have realized that there was a machine toilet right there?', 'Listen to the drunkard grumbling about the lack of a key as he poops under the tree. He doesn''t sound happy, and every time he stresses to push out the poop, his voice fluctuates. Imagine hearing this in your mind and being a wee bit disgusted.', 'Oh, I see — the door to the outhouse with the <kanji>machine</kanji> toilet is locked, and the drunkard doesn''t have the <reading>key</reading> (き). If he had a machine like a jackhammer, or a circular saw, or some other heavy duty machine, he might be able to get in without a key, but since he has none of those or a key, he''s reduced to pooping under the tree instead.', 'N3', NULL),
(1091, 'You take a peek under the lid to check, and your poop explosion is one hundred percent gone! Phew!', 'Look around you at the classy surroundings of the Ritz Hotel bathroom. You feel one hundred percent relieved now, and ready to enjoy your date at the Ritz!', 'It was extra important that there was zero <kanji>percent</kanji> poop left, because you''re not on a date just anywhere, you''re at the <reading>Ritz</reading> (りつ)!

', 'N1', NULL),
(1092, NULL, 'You’re flying through the air super fast. Feel the air rushing past you as heat builds up all over your body from rocketing through the air so fast.', 'When you <kanji>fly</kanji>, you generate a lot of <reading>he</reading>at (ひ) because you’re moving so fast through the atmosphere. Normally this heat would be a nuisance, but you’re a superhero so it doesn’t bother you. This is actually the reason you’re able to fly – you’re a superhero and super resistant to heat.', 'N3', NULL),
(1093, 'Imagine that moment as you fall off the roof. In slow motion you can see the roof moving away, you see yourself as a little kid, a teen, an adult, then BAM you''ve injured your mouth. Ow ow ow!', 'No matter how much you try to wave your hands and gesture, they keep calling you Guy. It''s really annoying, actually, because you aren''t a guy and you aren''t Guy either. But your mouth is too damaged for you to tell him.', 'As you try to assess the <kanji>damage</kanji>, you hear someone shout, "<reading>Guy</reading> (がい), hey, you okay, guy?" They run up to you, but your mouth is too <kanji>damage</kanji>d, you can''t respond. "Okay guy, I''m gonna call you Guy now. Let''s get you to the hospital."', 'N3', NULL),
(1094, 'The hat you’re wearing is ridiculous, giving you a surplus of bad style. Imagine a bunch of people scowling at you, not only because of your crazy hat, but because of the annoying sound of the jackhammer. Hear the obnoxious noise around you as the jackhammer rattles your bones.', 'Your hat is doing little to defend you from the onslaught of yo-yos being tossed at you. Feel the yo-yos hitting your body and listen to them roll away after striking you.', 'One of the people walking by is the owner of a push cart <reading>yo</reading>-yo (よ) stand. Having a <kanji>surplus</kanji> of yo-yos, he starts throwing them at you. This makes you stop using the jackhammer as you need both hands to fend off the yo-yo barrage.', 'N3', NULL),
(1095, 'Try and picture a Chinese turkey in your brain. Now imagine yourself trying to catch it, but you just can’t. It’s too difficult. The Chinese turkey is too elusive.', 'Picture yourself entering a bakery, frantically scouring through all the Naan in an attempt to find one that’s actually a Chinese turkey. "WHERE ARE THE TURKEYS?", you scream, to everyone else’s bewilderment. Those elusive, difficult to find Chinese Naan turkeys…', 'Do you know the actual reason Chinese turkey are so <kanji>difficult</kanji> to find? It’s because they’re all made out of <reading>Naan</reading> (なん). Of course they’re elusive when they blend in so well with all the other Naan!', 'N3', NULL),
(1097, 'Imagine the most uncomfortable outfit you can. Your body is incurring some minor damage right now, but hey — at least you''re covering your skin properly.', 'Even if you incur some damage from uncomfortable clothing, it''s better to cover up and retain heat than to underdress and incur damage from frostbite or something.', 'Another reason to <kanji>cover</kanji> yourself is to retain <reading>he</reading>at (ひ). Actually, retaining heat and staying warm is probably the reason people started covering themselves up in the first place. ', 'N2', NULL),
(1098, 'See the green mountains rising on either side of the valley, hear the birdsong in the distance… and behold the miles of clothes racks that hold all your outfits. This valley is the only space big enough to contain your material abundance and wealth.', 'Picture these wealthy youths as they peruse your valley of outfits. They''re mostly influencers making short-form video content for their socials, basically leeching off your abundance. Ugh. Is this how people get wealthy in the 21st century?', 'This valley is also <kanji>abundant</kanji> with <kanji>wealthy</kanji> <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう) — trendy, wealthy youths who come from around the world to descend on your valley and peep your outfit collection.', 'N1', NULL),
(1099, 'Picture this happening in real time. First it starts to rain really hard, then the ground starts to shift and run, then everything is shaking and quaking so violently it''s an earthquake!', 'You grab your shin, but the earthquake shakes the coffee table toward you to hit your shin yet again! Ow ow ow stop!', 'The <kanji>earthquake</kanji> shakes you so violently that you fall over, banging your <reading>shin</reading> (しん) on the coffee table. The edge of the coffee table is very sharp, so it cuts into your shin pretty badly.', 'N2', NULL),
(1101, 'What kind of animal could it be? You start to feel the soft fur with your hand and then the stench of death hits your nostrils. Ew.', 'See the bees swarming out from around the tail and toward your face. Run! Run before there’s nothing left of you but your own tail!', 'You try to pull the flag out of the ground and hear a rumbling buzzing sound. At the end of the flagpole you see a huge <reading>bee</reading>hive (び)! These bees made their home under the <kanji>tail</kanji>! Maybe that’s why the flag was sticking in it… to warn people. Oops.', 'N1', NULL),
(1102, 'Can you even imagine twenty completely unique contraptions? Why don''t you try, just for fun.', '"Kayak contraptions" is pretty vague, but you do have a kayak, so you''re tempted anyway. If only you could figure out what each contraption did so you could justify buying some.', 'You want to know what these <kanji>contraptions</kanji> are for, so you ask. The drunkard says, "These are <reading>kay</reading>ak (かい) contraptions *urp* they''re for your uh *blarp* kayak... things."', 'N2', NULL),
(1103, 'Try hitting a sponge on a turkey''s forehead. Not certain. Now a stone. <kanji>Certain</kanji>.', 'As you smash the stone into the turkey''s forehead, yell out "This is for you, my cock! THIS IS FOR YOU!" Your cock lover watches from the shadows.', 'You''re killing this turkey, and you need to make sure he''s dead for <kanji>certain</kanji>. The reason you need to make sure this turkey is dead is because of the <reading>cock</reading> (かく) in your life. Male chickens get really jealous when turkeys are around.', 'N3', NULL),
(1104, 'Imagine yourself feeling this strong dislike towards the Mona Lisa. She gets all the attention and you get none. It’s not fair and you’re upset.', 'Ken walks slowly toward the Mona Lisa, dislike shines in his eyes, but then! He falls for her too. No! Ken, destroy her!', 'You <kanji>dislike</kanji> the Mona Lisa so much you hire <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai to cut her up.', 'N1', NULL),
(1105, 'Imagine that leader''s old mouth saying things that make them stand apart from the others. They''re individual from everyone else.', '"You must be your own individual. Don''t be sheeple," he says to the 子s.', 'As an <kanji>individual</kanji>, they must teach all the <reading>子</reading>s (こ) their way. Their individual way.', 'N2', NULL),
(1106, 'Imagine being down there under that heavy cliff. Imagine feeling the cliff''s pressure as you watch it compress the dirt. It''s so hard to breathe... h... help!', 'Imagine yelling out to the dirt to encourage it: "Ah, two cliffs is nothing! Come on, you got this!" You''re the dirt''s gym buddy, and you won''t let it buckle under the pressure.', 'You want to increase the <kanji>pressure</kanji>, so you stack on a second cliff. "<reading>Ah, two</reading> (あつ)?!" the dirt cries out in dismay. This dirt is tough, but it''s really starting to feel the pressure now.', 'N2', NULL),
(1107, 'Imagine seeing a narwhal sticking a stick, and maybe even its horn too, into the dirt on land. It needs to know if life exists outside of the ocean for sure.', 'Hear the plinky plink of the xylophone and think about what exists in this world. It''s a sad song, you realize.', 'The narwhal can''t find anything but it can''t stop thinking about what could <kanji>exist</kanji> outside of its world. It pulls out a <reading>xy</reading>lophone (ざい) and starts tapping the keys with its horn. It plays a sad song about the meaning of life and whether existence is worth it.', 'N3', NULL),
(1108, 'Watch yourself sleeping. Then, a net of flowers appear on your forehead as you start to dream.', 'While still dreaming, you say "moo" out loud too. "Moooo!"', 'You <kanji>dream</kanji> about cows saying "<reading>moo</reading> (む), moo, moo!"', 'N3', NULL),
(1109, 'The baby is falling off the cliff! Imagine the sheer terror you feel as the baby plummets towards the ground below.', 'Oh Santa-san. You''re always here for me when I need you most. You can keep all my property and give it away as presents.', 'You <kanji>give birth</kanji>, and now this happens. Such bad luck. But wait! What''s that sound? Sleigh bells? Could it be?! It''s <reading>San</reading>ta-san (さん)! He caught your baby! And all your <kanji>property</kanji>.', 'N3', NULL),
(1110, 'Imagine a mole sneaking up behind the leader mole and stabbing him with a knife. This type of overthrowing of leadership feels familiar to you. Maybe this has happened before in the past?', 'Picture this like an exaggerated reenactment on the History channel. Leader after leader of とうきょう gets overthrown and replaced with the next guy. It never stops. (Until the end of the Edo period, I guess.)', 'Where do leaders get <kanji>overthrow</kanji>n the most? In <reading>とう</reading>きょう. Just think back in Japanese history, back when everyone was at war with one another. People were taking over and getting overthrown and then that guy gets overthrown and then THAT guy gets overthrown. Basically, it used to be hard to stay a leader in とうきょう.', 'N3', NULL),
(1111, 'Seriously. If you or someone else is stinking to high heaven, embrace a big bar of self care.', 'Imagine trying to use a big bar of soap on yourself to get the stinky 草 off. But it''s not working... the 草 stink is SO STRONG!', 'You''re <kanji>stinking</kanji> up the room because you spent your day rolling in <reading>草</reading> (くさ). And based on this particular stench, I''d say an animal left something in that 草 too...', 'N1', NULL),
(1112, 'I mean, this unibrow is really, really thick. So thick it doesn’t even look real. But it is. It’s really real and really thick.', 'Imagine the sun noticing its mistake and apologizing to the child. "Ah, two thick unibrows wasn''t what you wanted? Wow, I''m sorry. This is embarrassing… I''m actually not sure I can remove the second one now that it''s on there." ', 'The child glances up at their <kanji>thick</kanji> unibrow and yells, "<reading>ah, two</reading> (あつ)!" Yup, it turns out the sun misunderstood the child''s pleas for help. Instead of pulling them up from the cliff, it gave them a second thick unibrow on top of the first one.', 'N2', NULL),
(1113, 'Oh, how you miss your Wolverine woman and her long, sharp claws… Now all you''ve got left to remember her by is this cross. Your poor, poor wife.', 'All your wife needs is a little cyborg tune-up. Bionic brain here, some new claws there. Yep, your cy-wife''s as good as new.', 'Wait a minute… She can''t be dead because your <kanji>wife</kanji> is a <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい)! How could you forget?!', 'N3', NULL),
(1114, 'Picture yourself in a deliberation with some other people. They think what they''re saying is right, and it drives you mad. Then you realize that you''re doing the same thing.', 'Imagine how the deliberation concludes. Then, at the end, someone says "This deliberation is over! We have decided to _________" (in regards to the guillotine).', 'What are you in <kanji>deliberation</kanji> about? Whether or not to send some poor soul to the <reading>gui</reading>llotine (ぎ). "I think he should go," you say. "I don''t think he should," says another. This <kanji>deliberation</kanji> is going nowhere...', 'N3', NULL),
(1115, 'Picture a snake as a hardened criminal. See this snake with many prison tattoos and muscles from working out in his jail cell. Hear the snake hissing as it slithers about to commit yet another crime.', 'Now see Han Solo doing battle with the snake (cue Star Wars music). The snake hisses and lunges at Han, but Han shoots the snake and it’s incapacitated. Smell the smell of scorched snake flesh in your nose.', 'This snake has evaded the law for too long. It’s time for a true hero to put an end to its <kanji>crime</kanji> spree. Enter <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo. Han Solo is the only man strong and brave enough to put an end to this snake and his crime filled ways.', 'N3', NULL),
(1116, 'Imagine throwing a net around someone you think, no, you know is guilty of doing something bad to you. It feels pretty nice.', 'Woah! The xylophone is huge, how did they... well whatever, you definitely have proof that they''re guilty now and the cops should be here any minute to take your criminal catch.', 'The <kanji>guilt</kanji> shows on the criminal''s face. They smile, shrug, and pull a stolen <reading>xy</reading>lophone (ざい) out of their back pocket.', 'N3', NULL),
(1117, 'There''s a building right there. Try to go that direction. Oh, did the building prevent you? Told you so.', 'Your bowling antics are now preventing a bunch of people from going the directions they want to go. Nice job.', 'The building is <kanji>prevent</kanji>ing you from going the right direction, so you pull a <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう) ball out of your bag, aim, and bowl the building over. The building, and all the ones around it, go tumbling down. But now the rubble is preventing you from going further.', 'N2', NULL),
(1119, 'This theory you''ve thought of is great, it''s going to change everything!', '"Ron? Ron, what''s your theory? What''s your theory, Ron?" Hermione asks over and over. But you freeze. You don''t remember who you are. "Hermione, I have a theory that I was just a person learning Japanese, and not a magical boy at all." "Shut up Ron, that''s a stupid theory," she replies.', '"What is your theory, <reading>Ron</reading> (ろん)?" asks Hermione. You''re Ron Weasley, and you just came up with a theory that you think will help you stop Voldemort.', 'N3', NULL),
(1120, 'Picture yourself coming back to the same tombstone each passing year and tying a new thread to it. You''re trying to manage these feelings by keeping track of how much time passes.', 'Maybe this is your future tombstone, and eating cake each year is a preemptive celebration of your death day! Ooh, what a spooky theory.', 'Dealing with the <kanji>passage of time</kanji> can be tough, so after you tie the thread to the tombstone, each year you eat a piece of <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい). You already eat cake to mark the passage of time on your birthday, so why not here as well?', 'N3', NULL),
(1296, 'Imagine the pain and confusion of being stabbed by a violent tree. You hit your head on the tree again, as if to test it, and again a knife falls out and stabs you. Oh, so it''s a stabbing tree, is it? That does it.', 'Feel the grain of the saw''s wooden handle in your bare hand. As the saw bites into tree bark, a retaliatory rain of knives comes stabbing down around you. But you don''t even flinch. You will saw this stabbing tree down.', 'There''s nothing more dangerous than a tree that likes to <kanji>stab</kanji>, so you get your <reading>saw</reading> (さ) and start to saw it down. Trees that stab get sawed.', 'N2', NULL),
(946, 'You do the bridge pose, and in your mind you imagine your brush painting an actual bridge. When you open your eyes, there it is! A bridge has been built, leading straight to your front door.', 'It seems like Ken the samurai really appreciates your building skills.', 'You <kanji>build</kanji> a bridge. Immediately after, someone is climbing up onto it. It''s <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai. He waves at you and says, "Did you build this? The craftsmanship is amazing."', 'N4', NULL),
(1036, 'Imagine trying to pray as hard as you can as a spirit with an axe closes in on you. Can you hold your prayer concentration long enough?', 'I guess the key was prayer all along. ( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°)', 'You <kanji>pray</kanji> and pray and pray and nothing happens. You stop praying and dare to open your eyes. There is a <reading>key</reading> (き) in front of you. Grab the key and hold it before you and pray for the spirit to take it. Moments later, both the key and the spirit are gone.', 'N2', NULL),
(1123, 'Picture yourself holed up in a winter encampment, waiting to strike at the mohawk wearing enemies. Feel the cold around you as you scan the horizon for mohawks.', 'Imagine yourself all teched out like the techie you are. Hear the beeps and boops of all the technology you have at your disposal as you search for more mohawk enemies.', 'You''re kind of a <reading>techie</reading> (てき), so you have an advantage over your <kanji>enemy</kanji>. You''re armed with night vision goggles and other cool tech gear as well. Being a techie certainly has its advantages.', 'N1', NULL),
(1124, 'Imagine a tsunami simultaneously happening as the world comes to an end. Oh no, isn''t there any way to stop this!?', 'So the world won''t come to an end this one time, thanks to the cyborg you made. Though it doesn''t look very good. The cyborg is beaten up and full of water. This might be the end for it...', 'Just as the world is about to <kanji>come to an end</kanji>, your <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい) appears and blocks it with its cyborg body!', 'N3', NULL),
(1125, 'Imagine what this committee of women could be coming together to discuss. Isn''t grain just grain? Why do they need a whole committee?', 'Imagine a committee of women standing together in a field of grain, watching eagles swooping around. They take notes and mutter to one another about their options.', 'Well, they formed this <kanji>committee</kanji> because <reading>ea</reading>gles (い) have been eating all the grain. The women meet as a committee and try to come up with ways to stop these pesky eagles from eating all the grain and leaving none for the humans.', 'N2', NULL),
(1126, 'Feel the grass in your toes and the fins in your hand as you raise them up to the sky. See the sun glistening off the fins as you raise them up in the air.', 'Imagine yourself going through the same process as before, only this time imagine yourself in Little Kyoto. Little Kyoto is really little, so make sure you''re in the right spot.', 'This ritual only works in one part of the world however. It absolutely must be performed in Little <reading>Kyo</reading>to (きょ). The way Little Kyoto lines up with the sun and the sky and all that astrological junk makes it the best place for you to <kanji>raise</kanji> fish fins up in the air.', 'N1', NULL),
(1127, 'If you can''t decide who gets custody of the baby, the dog, the house, the car, the furniture, the savings, and the house plants, you have to leave it up to the judge. And the judge''s judgment is that everything is cut in half with a knife. What an efficient judge.', 'Han Solo the judge is a very busy person, so he has to judge quickly and efficiently. He''ll cut all your stuff perfectly in half before he wastes time listening to you argue about who earned what. Sure it''s crude, but at least it''s… equal?', 'This <kanji>judge</kanji> is <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo, so you know he''s not going to back down.', 'N3', NULL),
(1128, 'Imagine this new cow-controlled world — there are cows on every street corner, armed with towels and knives, intimidating all us humans into following their system.', 'The cows'' towels and knives won''t stand a chance against your saber!', 'It''s time to take <kanji>control</kanji> back from the cows! Pull out your <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい) and fight back! ', 'N3', NULL),
(1129, 'It''s hard to get a task done in winter. That''s why you have this spear to power and motivate you.', 'The moo haunts you as you finish your task. Luckily this spear is helping you keep going even though this task is really difficult.', 'Your <kanji>task</kanji> is to spear this cow. "<reading>Moo</reading> (む)!" it yells at you and runs away. You need to make dinner though... that''s your task, so you better catch up to that cow and put an end to its mooing.', 'N3', NULL),
(1130, 'Inspect the tree. Inspect the top hat. Did you find anything?', 'Turns out the top hat was just decorative and the tree was growing out of the earth, like any other tree. Imagine your shame now. You should be more careful — don''t be sawing trees willy nilly.', 'As you <kanji>inspect</kanji> the tree, you pick up a <reading>saw</reading>(さ) and start cutting it. You need to investigate how the tree and the top hat are connected, and a saw is the only way to do it properly.', 'N2', NULL),
(1131, 'The world has been in turmoil and the public has been suffering, but it''s time to stop splitting our hearts apart and fighting. It''s time to be whole again.', 'Suddenly people who were fighting for years are crying and holding one another. Their souls recognize each other, they''re healed, and the whole nation is finally seeing eye to eye.', 'Once their hearts are <kanji>whole</kanji>, the public''s <reading>sou</reading>l (そう) becomes one!', 'N2', NULL),
(1132, 'Try it out: I want a stabbing weapon with extra weapons that can pop out on the sides. *POOF* You''ve established a new weapon. And it''s been added to the official weapon wiki of the world.', 'Establish weapons in sets or the people who use them won''t be able to match each other.', 'Weapons have to be <kanji>establish</kanji>ed in <reading>sets</reading> (せつ). So you have to make another one that goes with it. Make them cohesive please.', 'N2', NULL),
(1133, 'Our resources are based around a system of trading shellfish. It''s not that weird, plenty of ancient societies used shellfish as their main resource!', 'Of course you''re going to use your resources to get a sheep. You''re living in a time when shellfish are used to trade for goods and services!', 'You save up all of your <kanji>resources</kanji> so you can buy a <reading>shee</reading>p (し). You bring a big sack of your shellfish resources to the market and finally get to pick out that sheep you''ve had your eyes on for months.', 'N3', NULL),
(1134, 'Imagine comforting this turkey as it leans against the tree, breathing its final breaths. "I lay down my life for the rights of my people…" it gasps. What a noble turkey.', 'Ken jumps down and picks the turkey up, the broken arrow sticking out of its neck. "Turkeys do not have rights, my friend. Just let this one go."', '"You have <kanji>rights</kanji>, so I won''t shoot you like this turkey," says the voice of <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai, from above you in the tree.', 'N3', NULL),
(1135, 'Picture this hoity-toity cow leader who thinks he’s too good for small matters and affairs. See him acting all fancy like he’s better than everyone else. The cow even moos with a fake British accent. How pretentious.', 'Picture Ken the samurai scooping poop for the leader of the cows, because that''s a matter of poop and state.', 'All of his <kanji>affair</kanji>s are taken care of by <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai, who he hired as his underling.', 'N3', NULL),
(1136, 'This sect of the tofu-lovers club takes their duties very seriously and they must protect this tofu cliff at all costs.', 'Oh, do you want to be a member of the tofu sect? Hah! Don''t make me laugh. If you have to ask to be a member of a sect, you''ll never get in.', 'All of the members of the <kanji>sect</kanji> are standing on the cliff, their hands raised, and right as the tsunami is about to collide with them, they shout, "<reading>Hah</reading> (は)!" and it''s repelled! The tsunami sinks and rolls back out to sea — the tofu cliff is safe!', 'N1', NULL),
(1137, 'Imagine this hill of horns on top of this mountain. This hill of horns makes it possible for you to get just high enough to touch the top of the sky.', 'Feel indifferent about this hill of horns on top of the mountain. It''s all in your head anyways.', 'How was standing atop this hill of horns on top of a mountain to touch the sky? Eh. It was <reading>oka</reading>y (おか), but that''s about it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1138, 'Visualize this element of life, interconnecting all living things. Without it, everything would fall apart and into chaos.', 'Imagine grabbing hold of the element that holds all life together and trying to wash it with soap. It''s slippery so you keep dropping it and soap keeps going in your eyes.', 'You need to keep this <kanji>element</kanji> clean, so you make sure to wash it with <reading>so</reading>ap (そ) every single day. This turns out to be pretty hard because the element is an invisible thread, but you do your best.', 'N1', NULL),
(1139, 'Picture this lion on his hind legs, walking through a rice field. He’s holding an axe in one hand and acting like a human. See him cut off the plants and hear the whoosh of the axe as he cuts.', 'When dawn comes in Africa (it''s a lion, so that''s where he is) it gets too hot to be in the sun, so the lion gets cut off from his job of cutting things off.', 'The lion will <kanji>cut off</kanji> rice stalks until <reading>dawn</reading> (だん). But at the first light of dawn, it will run and hide in the shade of the trees, cut off from the rice.', 'N3', NULL),
(1141, 'Some criticism is okay, as long as it''s constructive and it helps the person get better. But when you start pointing fingers, it''s time to step back and try to not be such a jerk.', 'Before you throw criticism around, imagine that embarrassed heat. You don''t want to feel that way, so why would you do it to someone else? ', 'If you''re having trouble not giving out <kanji>criticism</kanji>, just think about how you would react if someone criticized you. You''d feel an embarrassed <reading>he</reading>at (ひ) rise throughout your body. That heat will cover your whole face.', 'N1', NULL),
(1142, 'Imagine yourself being protected by your king and leader. They don’t really like you, but it is their duty to protect you. See them fighting off evil doers and taking bullets for your sake.', 'You’re a nincompoop. Even though you’re so nincompoopy, your king and leader still have a duty to protect you. Feel happy and content, despite your nincompoopery.', 'You’re the village <reading>nin</reading>compoop (にん), but the king and the leader still have a <kanji>duty</kanji> to protect you. Even a nincompoop is important to the kings and leaders of the world.', 'N3', NULL),
(1143, 'As you examine the tree and the squid in it, you think, "Huh, nope still weird." You should examine it further to find out what''s going on.', 'Imagine how many trees Ken examined before he found you both. Picture him putting his face really close to each one, then shedding a single tear when he realized this squid wasn''t in it.', 'You''re sticking your face in really, really close to <kanji>examine</kanji> this little squid in the tree when <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai appears behind you. "There you are, squid! Thank you citizen, I''ve been looking all over for my little chum."', 'N1', NULL),
(1145, 'The clause states the tree can only be a tree in winter, but not during other seasons. Once winter descends, the clause takes effect and it becomes a tree. (See how winter is descending on the tree from above in this kanji?) Go ahead and look at its contract — see the winter tree clause, right there, in fine print.', 'Joe noticed that the trees were getting too crowded, so he wrote up these clauses and added them to the trees'' contracts. Now, the forests are less crowded, thanks to the little clause. Makes sense that a farmhand like Joe would do this, too. The fewer trees, the more farmland. It''s good for folks like Joe.', 'This <kanji>clause</kanji> was written into all trees'' contracts by <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう), the large farmhand.', 'N1', NULL),
(1146, 'Just imagine an innocent little shellfish, living its quiet little life, while everyone''s heaping blame on it. It hides in its little shell and tries to deflect all that blame. What a life!', 'Imagine trying to look sexy as you slurp up those saucy little shellfish.', 'When those poor shellfish aren''t having <kanji>blame</kanji> piled on them, they''re being eaten, because shellfish are thought to make for a very <reading>sexy</reading> (せき) meal. ', 'N3', NULL),
(1147, 'You only have like, three eyes left. If you conserve them.', 'The Shougun stares down at you, eyes blazing, and you hand over all but one of your eyes. Now you really have to conserve, or you''ll be blind. Thanks Shougun. I bet he has plenty of eyes, he didn''t need yours.', 'You''re trying to <kanji>conserve</kanji> your eyes, when the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) marches by. He stops, sees you, and demands you give him your remaining eyes! But sir, I''m conserving what little I have left!', 'N2', NULL),
(1148, 'Imagine putting it on, and suddenly the dirt around you increases by quite a bit.', 'Zombie hands keep coming out of the ground, but as long as you wear the mask and increase the dirt on top of them, they won''t be able to escape their earthly prison.', 'Use the dirt mask to <kanji>increase</kanji> the dirt around you. The <reading>zo</reading>mbies (ぞう) are trying to escape, so you just keep increasing the dirt on top of them so they can''t get out.', 'N3', NULL),
(1149, 'Can you imagine a guard coming to your door and demanding you pay your grain tax?', 'You don''t want to be sliced with the zeiber, so you listen to vat he zei-zei-zeis and pay the tax.', 'You have to pay this <kanji>tax</kanji>, because if you don''t the guard will cut you down with his special German saber, a <reading>zei</reading>ber (ぜい). "Don''t you hear vat I zei? Pay ze tax or I vill zlei you vis my zeiber!" he shouts.', 'N2', NULL),
(1151, 'A festival can''t be happening all the time. Then it wouldn''t be special, it would just be a party building, so they can only happen on special occasions.', 'Your cyborg has been really great lately. Your wife loves it. It saved the world. It''s nice of you to take this occasion to show the cyborg how much it means to you all with a festival.', 'The <kanji>occasion</kanji> this time is to celebrate your wonderful <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい). It''s about time you did something nice for it.', 'N3', NULL),
(1152, 'How could you not acknowledge somebody if every word they said was carved into your heart? It''s not particularly painful, but it does make you quite uncomfortable.', 'You''re one of the biggest Nintendo fans out there, so how could you not acknowledge their greatness? No matter what is carved into your heart, Nintendo will always occupy a special place.', 'Having their words carved into your heart makes you <kanji>recognize</kanji> the authority of many people. But the only one you could ever truly <kanji>acknowledge</kanji>, is <reading>Nin</reading>tendo (にん).', 'N3', NULL),
(1154, 'Go on, put that righteous radical knowledge to use!', '"THIS IS NOT RIGHTEOUSNESS!" you yell, just before the guillotine comes down on your neck.', 'Such <kanji>righteousness</kanji>! Well, at least according to the king who thinks he''s being righteous. His version of righteousness is to send anyone who disagrees with him to the <reading>gui</reading>llotine (ぎ).', 'N1', NULL),
(9343, 'Picture yourself holding your cross right up next to your mouth as you speak your prayers. What will you wish to come true? It could be anything you want!', 'Legend has it that writing down your wishes in kana will lead to higher chances of them actually coming true! Whether you go with hiragana or katakana is up to you, just make sure it''s all in some form of kana.', 'And if you really want your wish to <kanji>come true</kanji>, make sure to write it down in <reading>kana</reading>!', 'N1', NULL),
(957, 'Imagine yourself working the bamboo saw. It''s made of bamboo, but its sharp edge glistens with a mysterious aura as you slice through tree after tree. Feel how unnaturally easy it is to cut down piles of logs to build with.', 'Imagine trying to construct the rest of this building covered in baby chicks. They''re very cute, but this is dangerous! You have to build very carefully, so you don''t accidentally hurt any of them.', 'As you <kanji>construct</kanji> and <kanji>build</kanji> this building, you hear a strange cheeping noise. Suddenly a <reading>chick</reading> (ちく) pops out of one of the logs you are building with. Then another, and another. You''re surrounded by chicks! Is this because you used the magical(?) bamboo saw??', 'N2', NULL),
(1140, 'What situation would you like to evaluate? Be sure to say "peace" before doing so. Imagine yourself doing this out loud and think of what you would say.', 'Imagine yourself trying to explain a situation you’re about to evaluate and your audience keeps shouting “Heyo!” every other sentence. Imagine how frustrating that would be. Get angry at your rude audience and how much they shout “Heyo.”', 'When you say "peace," you end up making a really bad pun and you hear somebody say “<reading>Heyo</reading>!” (ひょう) You look at the people around you and try to <kanji>evaluate</kanji> who said heyo, but you can’t tell. You start talking again when somebody exclaims “That’s what she said” and another person shouts “Heyo!” once again, but you still can’t evaluate who’s saying these things.', 'N1', NULL),
(1155, 'Imagine getting so mad that you use a knife instead of words to say what you''re trying to say. A net falls over you and pulls you down. The knife goes flying. This is your penalty for acting like a crazy person.', 'If you don''t want to be punished with bats, maybe next time don''t use a knife instead of your words.', 'Your <kanji>penalty</kanji> for breaking the law isn''t over. Now that you''re stuck in a net, you can''t fight back when they hit you with <reading>bats</reading> (ばつ). ', 'N1', NULL),
(1156, 'Picture multiple people you know saying you should do yoga if you plan to give birth. It isn''t a question of whether you want to, it''s the correct way to do it. So you should.', 'Hopefully having a tan doesn''t make birth harder, since you''ve worked so hard to do this the correct way with so much yoga.', 'To prepare for giving <kanji>birth</kanji> you end up doing so much yoga outside that you get a really deep <reading>tan</reading> (たん).', 'N1', NULL),
(1157, 'Picture the guard being so warm from the moonlight that he’s trying to simultaneously undress and escape. You can’t just let him leave his post!', 'Imagine yourself desperately throwing darts at the undressing, escaping guard, trying to make him stop. Or perhaps trying to pin his clothes to his body using the darts. Anything to stop this madness!', 'You’re unable to stop the guard from <kanji>undress</kanji>ing and making his <kanji>escape</kanji>, so you decide to start throwing some <reading>darts</reading> (だつ) at him. Perhaps that’ll make him stop.', 'N1', NULL),
(1158, 'Picture yourself running on a scooter with a bunch of other people and closing your eyes tight, willing another mustache to sprout out of your face to surpass them all.', 'Watch helplessly as the car surpasses every single one of your scooters. Both of your mustaches droop in shame.', 'Suddenly, something else manages to <kanji>surpass</kanji> you all. It''s a <reading>ca</reading>r (か)! A car with two mustaches is also riding on a scooter and going way faster than you.', 'N3', NULL),
(1160, 'Picture this entire scene. The icicle falls in slow motion from the roof. It hits wolverine right in the middle of his forehead. He goes down. He has to lie down. He has to sleep.', 'Wolverine might have a concussion that''s making him neigh in his sleep. I guess it''s good that he''s lying down, but I''m pretty sure you''re not supposed to sleep... Maybe you should wake him up so the neighing will stop—I mean, so he''ll be okay!', 'While wolverine <kanji>sleep</kanji>s, he makes a horrible <reading>neigh</reading>ing (ね) sound. It''s like there''s a giant horse in his mouth screaming to get out! NEIGH NEIGH!', 'N3', NULL),
(1161, 'It is indeed just a roof over a bathtub, but I guess spirits inhabit everything according to Shintoism, so there''s no reason this bathtub can''t be a Shinto shrine too.', 'It makes sense, the person who says "It''s-a-me! (Mario)" is someone who works on bathtubs (and plumbing).', 'What god does this <kanji>Shinto shrine</kanji> hold? You see that "It''s a-<reading>me-ya</reading> (みや)! (Mario!)"', 'N1', NULL),
(1162, 'Imagine kissing each of your best friends on the mouth. Does this make you uncomfortable? Excited? Both? Feel that feeling.', 'Kissing the cock beak is kind of a strange experience. Feel the feathers in your hands as you steady the cock to kiss it. Cock-a-doodle-doo!', 'Actually, <kanji>each</kanji> and <kanji>every</kanji> one of your best pals is of the <reading>cock</reading> (かく) variety. By that, I mean each of them is a rooster. Imagine kissing each cock on its beak.', 'N2', NULL),
(1163, 'Picture a tree coming up with some elaborate plans to make sure they can spend as little money as possible.', 'You''re not sure about this anchovy plan. Are you cheap enough to choose a life of eating only anchovies?', 'The cheap tree''s <kanji>plan</kanji> involves only buying <reading>an</reading>chovies (あん) and surviving on them. Good exposure to the sun and fresh water is too expensive. The tree plans to save money by eating anchovies.', 'N1', NULL),
(1164, 'Imagine that some wild animal wanders into your net. Yes! You knew putting the net there was a good idea. But wait… Oh no, it escaped! It turns out your net wasn''t straight when you put it there.', 'Imagine the smell of the cheese as you put it in the net. Maybe a sharp cheddar? The aroma really carries. All the better to attract more animals! Soon you’ll be putting your cheese-eating prey on the dinner table.', 'Then you decide to <kanji>put</kanji> <reading>chee</reading>se (ち) in your net. With a perfectly straight net and the cheese you put down as bait, there’s no way this fails. Success is all about how you put the net down and the cheese you put in it. ', 'N3', NULL),
(1165, 'Imagine opening your bank account and looking at all of your monthly expenses. Now drag the little dollar and shellfish icons over to cover all of those costs.', 'When you see how expensive your heat is, you feel a heat going through your body too.', 'What is the biggest <kanji>expense</kanji> and <kanji>cost</kanji>? It''s the <reading>hea</reading>t (ひ) bill.', 'N3', NULL),
(1166, 'Can you understand the value of this helicopter? The leader flew it to war and back! So of course it''s worth a lot!', 'Imagine driving a valuable car with the leader''s helicopter on top. Yeah!', 'To further increase the helicopter''s <kanji>value</kanji>, the leader tied it to a <reading>ca</reading>r (か). Wow, now it has the value of a helicopter and a car combined! For a total value of… Well, imagine for yourself what value the car adds.', 'N1', NULL),
(1167, 'Imagine yourself in the womb, the circle of power. You’re being born into the world. Feel the force of your mother all around you.', 'All that force works wonders, and when the baby''s born you slice through the umbilical cord with your saber.', 'Now pretend you''re the mother. You''ll need all the <kanji>force</kanji> you can muster to bring that baby into the world, so you grab hold of your special <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい) and slice it about in the air to give you the extra force you need.', 'N2', NULL),
(1168, 'Picture a Viking trying to manage his Viking friends so they''ll use the bathtub properly. He has to manage which people go in when, how hot or cold the water is, and provide all the soap. It''s a very important job.', 'Some of the Vikings managed to fill their bathtubs with acorns and are trying to rub themselves clean with them instead of water...', 'The poor Viking trying to <kanji>manage</kanji> all the others keeps having to confiscate <reading>a</reading>corns (えい). "You can''t take baths in acorns and wood, it has to be water!" he shouts.', 'N2', NULL),
(1169, 'Imagine using this jackhammer to show someone where the prize is buried. Feel the vibrations through your hands and the rest of your body.', 'Picture Jesus getting all over the top and super preachy about his religious beliefs. He’s doing everything he can to indicate to the crowd that he’s the best, and show them how cool he is. Listen to Jesus preach to the crowd, desperate for their attention.', 'Actually, you''re unearthing <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ). He’s the treasure. For your soul. He’s here to show you that Jesus is still a hip and cool guy and he wants to <kanji>indicate</kanji> to you that having faith in Jesus and God and stuff is super rad. Jesus is all like, “Who has two thumbs and wants to <kanji>show</kanji> you the way of the Lord? This guy.”', 'N3', NULL),
(1170, 'Allocate all of your thread like you would your cash: by uniting it! Not at a bank, but in a big, intertwined lump. Now nobody can stop you and your big lump of thread!', 'Try to do this yourself at home. Unite all of your thread and poof! There''s とうきょう!', 'You''re busy trying to <kanji>unite</kanji> all of your thread, twisting and tying and wrapping, and suddenly you look at the ball in your hands and see <reading>とう</reading>きょう. You''ve united this thread into the shape of とうきょう. There are even little pins for all the famous neighborhoods and tourist spots. This is strange, because you don''t remember putting them there.', 'N1', NULL),
(1171, 'Imagine this geoduck shouting orders at you to march into enemy territory. See its gross body as a mouth forms and starts shouting out orders.', 'Feel yourself in the row boat as you imagine rowing away to safe territory. Hear the geoduck in the background shouting at you from shore, ordering you to return to land.', 'Not wanting to end up dead in enemy <kanji>territory</kanji>, you decide to escape this geoduck leader by getting on a <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat and rowing your way outta there. The geoduck commander orders you to halt your escape, but you still get in your row boat and row away.', 'N2', NULL),
(1172, 'Imagine being trapped under this bamboo tree that is resting on your head. You’re stuck. Feel the weight of the bamboo tree pressing on your head as you try and devise a plan to get out from under it.', 'Bamboo is usually kind of light, but this bamboo has a sack of rocks tied to each end making it heavy. Imagine yourself trying to remove this bamboo tree by pushing it up and away from you. It’s like doing a bench-press, bamboo style.', 'But why is this bamboo tree so heavy? You look to each end of the bamboo and realize that there is a <reading>sack</reading> (さく) tied on each end. Each sack seems to be filled with heavy rocks. Looks like someone made a <kanji>plan</kanji> to trap you here. Now you have to devise a plan to escape!', 'N1', NULL),
(1173, 'The moon gladiators have a thing for wisterias.', 'Imagine them reaching down from the moon and plucking some wisteria up from Mt. Fuji then taking them back up to the moon. Now they water them.', 'Since they''re on the moon, where did they get the <kanji>wisteria</kanji> from? Actually, from one of the points on earth that is nearest to the moon, Mt. <reading>Fuji</reading> (ふじ).', 'N1', NULL),
(1174, 'I don''t know how we managed to get a knife-wielding scarecrow as a vice president, there''s a side of politics I don''t get, I guess. I mean, just look at him up there. If something happens to our president we''re stuck with that thing.', 'What''s going on? Normally if a vice president yells f**k you there would be... I dunno... consequences? What''s happening?!', 'It''s the <kanji>vice</kanji> president''s turn to speak at the podium and he just clears his throat and yells, "<reading>F**k</reading> (ふく) you!" to the crowd, holding up his knife. They cheer.', 'N2', NULL),
(1175, 'View the turkey as the turkey views you. View the arrow stuck in it too.', 'As you view this strange scene, Genghis Khan turns and views you viewing him. He points two fingers to his eyes and then to you, and marches off into the woods. You also view the turkey running off in the other direction.', 'As you <kanji>view</kanji> the turkey, Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) suddenly comes into your view too. He towers over the injured turkey, leans down, and... pulls the arrow out!?', 'N3', NULL),
(1176, 'Imagine how terrifying it is when your leader looks straight at YOU. Don''t worry, though — you''ve got more value than you give yourself credit for, and your leader can surely see that.', 'Imagine the despair you feel walking down the cheese aisle at the store and seeing how one little pack of string cheese now costs the same as your monthly rent. Why did your leader have to spike the value of cheese like this?', 'One thing your leader knows has great <kanji>value</kanji> is <reading>chee</reading>se (ち). Say goodbye to your days of casually snacking on cheesy treats from the conbini, because your leader''s decided the value of cheese is going up 1000%!', 'N3', NULL),
(1177, 'If you''re ever having trouble remembering how to suck or inhale, just picture an actual escalator that leads into your mouth. That''ll definitely help.', 'You can inhale a cucumber too, I suppose, but don''t tell anyone I told you to.', 'Use this mouth ability to <kanji>suck</kanji> <reading>cu</reading>cumbers (きゅう) into your mouth. Cucumbers are shaped like cylinders so you can suck them into your mouth like this. Just as nature intended.', 'N3', NULL),
(1178, 'Picture this drunkard in the dirt, her mouth pressed to the ground. "Ahh," she says, "so this is the taste of the Bordeaux region." Then she has a glass of Bordeaux, and starts noting all its particular regional qualities.', 'Imagine her crying out, "Why are there such icky, earthy notes in all these regional wines?!" Feel the second-hand embarrassment for her. In her drunkenness she can''t understand that putting her mouth on the ground makes everything taste icky, no matter what region it''s from.', 'Turns out she thinks everything from this <kanji>region</kanji> is <reading>icky</reading> (いき). In fact, to the drunkard, every region tastes icky and gross. You don''t have the heart to tell her it''s the dirt and ground, not the region itself, that tastes icky…', 'N2', NULL),
(1179, 'Imagine yourself as a figure judge on this panel. You need to find a woman with the perfect figure to cast in your commercial. You''d cast yourself, but you just aren''t confident (Ridiculous! You''re special, and beautiful, and no matter who you are [yes you reading this right now] you should be proud of who you are!).', 'We should all try harder to have a figure like this stunning sheep woman!', 'The next woman comes in and you examine her <kanji>figure</kanji>. The figure you''ve been searching for! It just happens to belong to a <reading>shee</reading>p (し) woman. But she''s soft, fluffy, and round. The perfect figure!', 'N1', NULL),
(1180, 'You usually ignore everyone, but this one person... they really got under there and touched your heart. You finally feel like they''re the right person to respond to.', 'The 王 touched your heart, you responded, and now you''re together. It''s like a fairy tale!', 'You <kanji>respond</kanji> and it turns out it was to the <reading>王</reading> (おう).', 'N1', NULL),
(1181, 'Picture yourself in class. You want to present next! You want to submit your ideas for class approval. So raise those fingers to the sun the correct way and wait for your teacher to call on you.', 'The next time you have to submit work in class just raise your hand and give everyone tater tots. Instant "A" material.', 'You want to <kanji>present</kanji> your show and tell of <reading>ta</reading>ter tots (てい) to the class. You raise your hand, the teacher calls on you, and you <kanji>submit</kanji> the tater tots for her and the class''s approval. "Oh my!" the teacher exclaims, "I hope you brought enough tater tots to present to the whole class!" You raise your hand once more to the sky, and in it you submit a whole tray of tater tots!', 'N1', NULL),
(1182, 'Picture yourself playing football. You catch the ball with your fingers and then assist your team by running and smashing your body into the enemy team. But in the process you smash your fingers... so you need to go to the aid tent.', 'You can''t assist your team anymore because you got hurt. This isn''t fair! It''s the end. The end of your life and your career. Boo hoo hoo!', 'When you need <kanji>aid</kanji> for your football wrecked fingers you can''t help but feel like it''s the <reading>en</reading>d (えん) for you. Your fingers hurt so much, you don''t even want the nice nurses to aid you.', 'N1', NULL),
(1183, 'Picture a bunch of big, sharp icicles all falling down directly next to this dog. Now imagine the condition he would''ve been in, had they all fallen directly on top of him. Poor little thing.', 'Good guy Joe, always worrying about his dog''s condition. Everyone needs a farmhand like Joe.', 'What saved the dog''s <kanji>condition</kanji> was none other than <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう), the local farmhand. He constantly keeps an eye on the dog''s condition, including any dangerously sharp icicles that could descend on him.', 'N3', NULL),
(1184, 'See yourself in this ideal appearance. Everyone loves to look at you because you look so awesome. Your heart has given you many great abilities. Feel strong and powerful in this body of yours.', 'Since you don’t know how to tie a tie or tie your shoes, you wear slip ons and clip ons. Hear the noise of Velcro being undone as you kick off your shoes and unclip your tie after a hard day’s work at the office.', 'While you may have the <kanji>appearance</kanji> of someone who is super strong, powerful, and awesome, you do have one shortcoming. You don’t know how to <reading>tie</reading> (たい) a tie. Actually, you don’t even know how to tie your shoes, let alone your tie. How embarrassing.', 'N1', NULL),
(1185, 'Shellfish were once used as currency, so this totally makes sense. Just think of the shellfish as money coming out of your mouth as you say your congratulations.', 'The next time you''re congratulating someone, assume the gargoyle stance and dump all of their congratulatory shellfish on them from your mouth.', 'When you say <kanji>congratulations</kanji> like this, you look just like a <reading>ga</reading>rgoyle (が). You''re crouching down, your mouth wide open, just like a stone gargoyle.', 'N1', NULL),
(1431, 'Seems like the treasure has some magical ability to renew the cells in your body. Really try to imagine this feeling as you pull the treasure out of the rice paddy’s ground. Perhaps it feels just as refreshing as getting a full night’s sleep.', 'All you could hear was a sly “yoink!”, and suddenly your life renewing treasure was gone. God knows what things こういち will get up to with this life renewing treasure in his possession (but it sure won’t be anything good).', 'Just as you were getting accustomed to this life <kanji>renew</kanji>ing treasure, <reading>こう</reading>いち comes out of nowhere, stealing your treasure and running off with it.', 'N3', NULL),
(1548, 'Focus on descending the stairs. There are many signs that say "To Winter Cow: descend" with an arrow pointing down. "I guess I''ll have to descend," you say.', '"Hello, thank you for descending all the way down here to see me," says the Winter Cow with こういち''s face. "You thought you could use the Winter Cow to stop me, but as you can see, I got here first."', 'You <kanji>descend</kanji> to the bottom and there it is, the Winter Cow. You walk up to the cow, and it swings its head back to see you. Instead of a cow''s face, it''s <reading>こう</reading>いち''s face.', 'N3', NULL),
(1796, '', 'Look at the eagle and her egg sitting atop your clothes. Every time you get close the eagle starts to screech at you fiercely. Screee! Screee!', 'An <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) sees a pile of your <kanji>clothes</kanji> and lays an <reading>e</reading>gg (え) in them. The eagle’s egg needs a soft warm place to incubate, and your pile of clothes is the perfect place.', 'N2', NULL),
(1186, 'You can obtain a lot of goods this way without needing any cash. Just don''t tell anyone I told you.', 'You have to pull very slowly, or the shoes will fall off the stool. But you really, really want to obtain these particular shoes (they''re really sweet), so you keep scooting them toward you.', 'Today you want to <kanji>obtain</kanji> a new pair of <reading>shoe</reading>s (しゅう). You tie your vines around the stool they''re on, and slowly inch them toward the door.', 'N3', NULL),
(1187, 'Imagine being told to halt in front of your favorite restaurant, then being turned away. Noooo! Better start sucking up to the restaurant leader.', 'HALT! NO TATERS FOR YOU!', 'People only <kanji>halt</kanji> because they want this restaurant''s world famous <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tots. Which is probably why there is a leader out front making sure only people who can really appreciate the tater tots can get in to eat them.', 'N2', NULL),
(1188, '', 'You won’t get much leather from a cock, but you could skin one in a pinch, should you really need a little extra leather.', 'One way to get <kanji>leather</kanji> is to skin a male chicken, a <reading>cock</reading> (かく). Not a very efficient way to get leather, but one way nonetheless.', 'N2', NULL),
(1189, 'Hear the strange sounds this drunkard is making. Let his ramblings enter your ears and imagine what he could be trying to say.', 'Actually you find out that he does have employment. He’s a garbage man. Feel the shock of making this shocking discovery. How can this drunkard possibly hold down his job like this?', 'Since this guy is such a pathetic drunkard, it comes as no <reading>shock</reading> (しょく) that he can’t find <kanji>employment</kanji>. If he did find employment, I would be filled with both shock and awe.', 'N3', NULL),
(1190, NULL, 'Picture yourself locking up a demon with a special demon containing key. Hear the key click into the lock as the demon screams to be set free.', 'How do you keep a <kanji>demon</kanji> locked up? Use a <reading>key</reading> (き). Use a lock and key and keep that demon locked up safely behind bars so he can’t get out and do bad demon stuff.', 'N1', NULL),
(1192, 'Picture this turkey holding its flowers while sitting on its stool. Hear it gobbling as it defends everything around it with its mere presence.', 'Imagine Godzilla approaching the flower turkey. Hear Godzilla roar as the turkey gobbles in fear before being crushed beneath the massive and scaly foot of Godzilla.', 'The only thing this flower-bearing turkey can''t <kanji>defend</kanji> against is <reading>Go</reading>dzilla (ご). Godzilla is pretty much undefeatable, especially for a turkey. The turkey is totally unable to defend anything from the mighty Godzilla.', 'N1', NULL),
(1193, 'The entire house glows with blobs of life, and the mouth holds the knife in its mouth and cuts the life blobs apart to help them grow.', 'Picture Wario''s mouth dividing himself now, and creating more, smaller Warios. They all yell WA! and divide up even more until the whole room is full of わりs and わs!', 'As it <kanji>divide</kanji>s life, the mouth begins to look really familiar. It has big teeth and a crooked mustache... it''s <reading>Wari</reading>o (わり)!

Sometimes Wario cuts himself by accident and just becomes <reading>わ</reading> (which is all he says too).', 'N3', NULL),
(1194, 'He yells back, "YOU CAN''T JUDGE ME, I CAN DO WHAT I WANT. GET THAT JUDGEY CROSS OFF ME!"', 'The cyborg joins in, "A judge would convict you if the fashion police brought you in. I have a bunch of meat and robot parts and even I''m better looking than you."', 'As you <kanji>judge</kanji> each other, your <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい) comes stomping over and slams the drunkard against the wall. With your trusty cyborg coming to your rescue, you can continue to judge this guy all you want.', 'N1', NULL),
(1195, 'Cape Cod, for example. Picture it now (or look it up if you need to). Really does look like a big lip ring stuck in the side of Massachusetts, doesn''t it? Imagine seeing that cape from the top of your mountain, wind blustering around you.', 'Try to draw that bit of coastline where the cape is. Nice, easy, smooth lines, until BAM! — the cape. Now the coastline is all jagged like the edge of a saw or key.', 'Looking down at the <kanji>cape</kanji>, you realize capes always make the coastline look jagged, like a <reading>saw</reading> or <reading>key</reading> (さき). Thanks for keeping the coastline interesting, you saw-key capes!', 'N1', NULL),
(1196, 'But why? Because this acting will distract everyone from the impending tsunami. You dance around in your fins, pretending to swim. It''s a wonderful performance. ', 'The performance and the tsunami end together. How poetic.', 'As the <kanji>acting</kanji> <reading>en</reading>ds (えん), so do you all.', 'N3', NULL),
(1197, 'Imagine a police officer coming for you. He''s yelling at you because you have a brush and you''re loitering. He grabs you, throws you to the ground, telling you what you''re doing wrong.', 'Imagine the hotel staff watching as you get thrown to the ground. You yell "won''t the Ritz do anything to help me??" They won''t.', 'Where were you loitering with your brush, breaking the <kanji>law</kanji>? Right outside the <reading>Ritz</reading> (りつ) Hotel. You''d think that it would be okay since you were staying there, but the law is the law.', 'N2', NULL),
(1198, 'The expert is also your teacher, and she''s showing you how to clean up after this bear.', 'Your teacher assures you this wet sheep is far less dangerous to clean up after than a wet bear. They''re the expert, so you should listen and wipe up after that sheep.', 'The <kanji>teacher</kanji> tells you to start with something smaller, after all, you''re not an <kanji>expert</kanji> yet. You have to practice on a wet <reading>shee</reading>p (し). "I became a wet, dangerous animal expert by starting with sheep, you will too!" your teacher says.', 'N3', NULL),
(1199, 'You know that way cartoon characters and pirates watch out below them, with their hand shading their eye? That! Now imagine doing that right now.', 'Welp, nothing much watching over them will do now. Genghis Khan is probably going to take over your town, so I guess you can watch if you want but... I wouldn''t.', 'As you <kanji>watch over</kanji> your friends and neighbors you see movement in the distance. Oh no, it''s Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) and his army!', 'N1', NULL),
(1200, 'See this tsunami riding turkey in your mind. Hear it gobble loudly as it waves its cross around. Remember, this is standard fare for you. Nothing unusual here.', 'It’s usually pretty toasty in the month of June (in the Northern Hemisphere, at least) so imagine going to bed in this heat. Feel the spray of the refreshing tsunami water in your dream as the turkey continues to ride those tsunami waves with his cross.', 'These wacky dreams are only <kanji>standard</kanji> fare in <reading>June</reading> (じゅん) though. You don’t have them any other time of the year, just in June. You don’t know why June is the only time you have dreams about tsunami riding turkeys, but that’s just the way it is.', 'N2', NULL),
(1201, 'Picture a shellfish wielding a knife and shouting out new rules to all of the nervous people standing around it.', 'Once everyone follows the rule, and the shellfish is on top of a pile of socks, he cackles, "It''s easier to stab your toes with my knife now! Let''s get to more rules!"', 'The first <kanji>rule</kanji> the shellfish enacts is that all humans take off their <reading>sock</reading>s (そく) and give them to him. Hmm... is this some kind of nasty shellfish? Who would want socks?', 'N2', NULL),
(1202, 'Imagine a leader carrying flowers up to the top of a cliff. They are saying "I must provide more flowers to the cliff" as they climb up.', '"The bees must be equipped with more flowers. I will provide these flowers for the bees!"', 'Why do they <kanji>provide</kanji> and <kanji>equip</kanji> the cliff with flowers? It''s all for the <reading>bee</reading>s (び). ', 'N3', NULL),
(1203, 'Roads are really long, so I imagine it would take a long time to measure it (if you want to go from end to end). Imagine yourself pulling a tape measure along with you, all the while thinking, "Where will this lead me?”', 'What a perfect way to end this road measuring. You''re hungry. Nom nom nom. Imagine eating your fill, and taste the doughnuts in your mouth. You just walked a long way!', 'Where does the road that you''re measuring <kanji>lead</kanji> to? To a <reading>dou</reading>ghnut (どう) shop!', 'N2', NULL),
(1204, 'Picture everything early in the morning covered in damp dew. Even your hat is uncomfortably wet. Now walk up to a large tree trunk and touch it—it''s dry!', 'Now that you think about it, Genghis Khan was the one who taught people that tree trunks stay dry in the morning!', 'You lean against the <kanji>tree trunk</kanji>, waiting for your hat to dry, and suddenly you realize Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) is on the other side of it. You look around and see that all of the tree trunks around you have Mongols drying their hats under them.', 'N1', NULL),
(1205, 'Imagine holding this bow in your hands, and then stretching it into a longer bow. Feel the strain on your arms as you try to stretch wood. You can feel it stretching, but it''s strenuous.', 'Now Mrs. Chou is hitting you with the stretched out bow. You cry and apologize, but she won''t listen. "I''m going to stretch you, like you stretched my bow," she says. Then she does it.', 'As you <kanji>stretch</kanji> the bow, you hear someone running at you. "That''s my bow you''re stretching!" yells Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう). She is not happy that you''re stretching her things. You should probably try to un-stretch it, put it down, and run before she beats you.', 'N1', NULL),
(1206, 'As a leader, you are superior. You’re so gentle to these foreheads. See yourself caring for them in the winter. Feel the chill in the air as you wrap the foreheads in blankets and prepare heating pads for them.', 'Picture the youths trying to show you that they are the superior ones, then they sneeze, their eyes fill with tears, and you have to gently tuck them back into bed. Silly youths.', 'You need to be <kanji>superior</kanji> because all of these foreheads belong to <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう). They don''t know how to take care of themselves, though they try to convince you they can. They''re too young to know any better.', 'N3', NULL),
(1207, 'Imagine staring down at the ground as you prepare to use the roof of your house like a slide. I hope you aren''t afraid of heights, because you have to do this seven times in a row.', 'Feel the tack stab into your butt as you slide down your house''s roof. Ow! Weirdly, though, it kinda makes you feel more at one with the house — like it''s really your home now.', 'When you slide down the roof of your <kanji>house</kanji>, you end up with a <reading>tack</reading> (たく) in your butt cheek. All the roof shingles on your new <kanji>home</kanji> are attached with tacks, so it''s honestly a miracle that you only got one tack stuck in you.', 'N3', NULL),
(1208, 'Imagine yourself in the swamp when the tsunami hits. Feel the water crash into you along with the shrimp. You’re all wet and shrimpy now.', 'Look at the side of your house. It’s covered with shrimp that have been impaled on the tacks. Perfect for dinner.', 'Your house was right next to the <kanji>swamp</kanji> and on this side of your house you had many a <reading>tack</reading> (たく) there to hang things on. Now, on every tack there is skewered a shrimp. The force of the tsunami drove them into the tacks on the side of your house.', 'N1', NULL),
(1210, 'See the alligator swim really slowly in the direction of a gun. See the alligator as it prepares to carry out the murder. It’s scary.', 'Watch as the alligator slinks under the water, holding the gun above the water. When it gets close enough, it carries out the murder on the sheep. Shooting it right in the chest. Now it can eat the sheep without worrying about needing to chomp on it.', 'In order to <kanji>carry out</kanji> this murder, the alligator has to sneak up on its prey, so it can shoot it. You look across the water and see a <reading>shee</reading>p (し) grazing by the shore. The alligator is going to carry out a murder on that sheep!', 'N1', NULL),
(1211, 'People are constantly asking the king to do things for them and solve their problems. But the king can see exactly what needs to be done at the present time, and what can wait until tomorrow.', 'Picture pretty Genji, carefully instructing his followers what to do at the present time and what can wait.', 'At the <kanji>present time</kanji> the king is <reading>Gen</reading>ji (げん). The time has finally come for him to get serious, and as they say, there''s no time like the present.', 'N3', NULL),
(1212, 'See this child running in his cleats. Picture him holding the umbrella upside-down, milk sloshing all about as he runs.', 'Imagine post-apocalyptic New York City. Milk is a hot commodity here so it’s important not to spill any of the milk. Imagine yourself carrying the milk in the umbrella and being careful not to spill any of it.', 'But where is this child exactly? He’s in <reading>New</reading> (にゅう) York City. What’s he doing in New York City you ask? Well, he''s milking a cow. Why is there a cow to be milked in New York City? Good question. It’s the end of the world and New York City is the last remaining civilization. This is how people live now. <kanji>Milk</kanji> is important.', 'N2', NULL),
(1213, 'Call for peace. Say it aloud. I’m using my mouth to call for peace!', 'Imagine yourself whirling this yo-yo about while you call for peace. Imagine yourself doing a variety of tricks with it as you slowly convince the people to agree to peace.', 'But before you <kanji>call</kanji> for peace, you must first placate the masses. To do so, you astound them with your <reading>yo</reading>-yo (よ) skills. Once they are so entranced by how talented you are with a yo-yo, you easily convince them to make peace.', 'N3', NULL),
(1214, 'Every great building, especially castles, started off as just a plot of dirt, you know? It''s kind of inspiring what we can become.', 'Once a castle is there, there''s really no turning back. So just remember that when dirt becomes a castle, that''s all she wrote.', 'Once you turn dirt into a <kanji>castle</kanji>, that''s all <reading>she wro</reading>te (しろ). That''s it. It''s done. The story is over.', 'N2', NULL),
(1215, 'Unfortunately, the leader of the criminals also writes haiku, and they want all the criminals to keep doing crimes. Look inside and see how much you want what others have, write a haiku, and then steal!', 'Just remember: a haiku is always a haiku.', 'A <kanji>haiku</kanji> is still a <reading>hai</reading>ku (はい). Whether it''s written by a law abiding leader or a criminal leader.', 'N1', NULL),
(1216, 'This special workout routine is so hard that, if you can do it, you''ll be able to excel at every other thing you try.', 'You have to eat the grains AND run up the stairs, or you aren''t actually excelling. Good shoes will help you get there faster and protect your toes.', 'In order to <kanji>excel</kanji> at this, though, you need a good pair of <reading>shoe</reading>s (しゅう). ', 'N1', NULL),
(1304, 'Yeah, take that, you hole! Stab, thrust, stab! Wow, this hole keeps getting bigger thanks to all your stabbin''.', 'Remember "totes," that slang word for "totally"? It fell out of use almost as sudden as it appeared. Now it totes gives me a little stab of nostalgia whenever I hear it.', 'Give that hole one last big <kanji>stab</kanji>, now you''ve <reading>totes</reading> (とつ) showed that hole who''s boss. What did I tell you about the <kanji>sudden</kanji> <kanji>thrust</kanji> technique? It''s totes super effective. Like, totes.', 'N3', NULL),
(1900, 'Imagine having some tall, moon radical-shaped shelves in your house. Where would you put them? Picture them there now. Honestly, they''d actually look pretty sophisticated.', 'Think about the ton-a-stuff you''re going to put on this tall shelf. Like, literally measure out a ton of things and put them on there. It can hold it, I''m sure…', 'To match the moon radical shape, this would have to be a pretty tall <kanji>shelf</kanji>, meaning you can store a <reading>ton-a</reading>-stuff (たな) on it. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2051, 'Imagine tying this spoon down with thread that''s a color other than purple. Ah, the thread snaps. Darn, you better use your extra strong purple thread (the best color for strength!).', 'Imagine that everything is in purple in this village. Cars, houses, trees. Absolutely everything was purple.', 'To remember this reading, think about how the village you previously went to was all <kanji>purple</kanji>. It was the "<reading>村先</reading>" (むらさき) that you went to.', 'N1', NULL),
(1144, 'Picture the judge sitting under the roof. Your number is sitting on his big desk, next to the gavel.', 'You try to ask the judge what his ruling is, but he just hits you on the shins again!', 'The <kanji>judge</kanji> is not happy with you. He tells you to come closer and when you do, he takes his gavel and hits you in the <reading>shin</reading>s (しん). Ouch!', 'N1', NULL),
(1191, 'This husband sets a new standard for what it means to be a husband. He’s pretty great. Imagine yourself shaking this husband’s hand because he’s so awesome.', 'Picture this woman actually sticking a physical key into the husband’s chest and turning it. Now this is anything but standard, but it’ll help you remember this kanji and its reading.', 'It’s pretty <kanji>standard</kanji> for a husband and wife to exchange vows when getting married, and the husband said this girl held the <reading>key</reading> (き) to his heart. She unlocked the husband’s heart with the key and now he’s a man made whole.', 'N3', NULL),
(1217, 'Think about your morning routine. You carry yourself into the bathroom and carry a toothbrush into your mouth. You carry your coat and bag out the door.', 'Picture yourself carrying a coat and a bag, walking through the rice fields to work, with the hot, dawn sun shining down, making your fingers get tanner and tanner.', 'Once you''re <kanji>carry</kanji>ing everything, and the dawn sun is shining bright, you start getting <reading>tan</reading> (たん). Especially those fingers you''re using to carry things.', 'N2', NULL),
(1218, 'If you''re a guest you either have to have the right amount of geoducks or geoducks in framed pictures. Otherwise you''re a jerk.', 'Feel the feel of hundreds of gawks. Feel ashamed as they gawk at you. How could you!?', 'Unfortunately, if you mess up the <kanji>amount</kanji> or the <kanji>framed picture</kanji>s, every other guest will <reading>gawk</reading> (がく) at you.', 'N2', NULL),
(1219, 'Imagine being able to control your clothes with your mind. What would you have them do? Imagine what you would do with your clothes if you could manufacture something to control them and move them around at will with your mind.', 'Now you''re trying to manufacture a machine that will make sabers and clothes you can use to control them. Soon you''ll have a manufactured army of saber-controlling clothes!', 'You <kanji>manufacture</kanji> these clothes to control your <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい). It''s hard to manufacture, you have to do a lot of work, but you manage to make your clothes control your saber. Now you don''t need to rely on your hands!', 'N1', NULL),
(1220, 'Imagine scooting down a street in Korea and seeing how different literally every single thing is compared to what you''re used to in Japan.', 'Tigger is different in Korea too. For one, he''s a white tiger now, and he''s, like, really aggressive. Wait, was he always like that?', 'What''s also <kanji>different</kanji> is that there''s a weird tiger bouncing on its tail alongside you yelling that its name is <reading>Tigger</reading> (ちが). You didn''t know Tigger was living in Korea these days. That''s definitely different.', 'N3', NULL),
(1221, 'Imagine stuffing a bunch of Stormtroopers in your car so you can transport them across the country. Well the Death Star can transport even more Stormtroopers, faster, and through space!', 'It''s easy to transport a yurt even without a car or the Death Star.', 'Right now you and the Death Star are <kanji>transport</kanji>ing <reading>yu</reading>rts (ゆ). They''re the universe''s #1 best transportable homes!', 'N2', NULL),
(1222, 'Feel the fire all around you as it burns. You''re out in nature — the middle of a forest fire. Hear the woods crackle as they burn up around you.', 'Cry your eyes out as you smell the burning plastic of your nendoroids. Worst. Day. Ever.', 'What is the worst thing that could <kanji>burn</kanji> in this fire? Your <reading>nen</reading>doroid (ねん) collection. You''ve spent a lot of time and money collecting all these cool and rare and unique nendoroids and now they''ve all burned in the fire.', 'N2', NULL),
(1223, 'Imagine what celebrating would be like. Imagine an older brother too, if you don''t actually have one.', 'Never celebrate too hard. Otherwise you''ll always have to think about the time a celebration spirit shook your brother till his head fell off.', 'Unfortunately, the two of you <kanji>celebrate</kanji>d too much, and you went a little crazy. The spirit grabbed your brother and <reading>shook</reading> (しゅく) him. Shook him so hard his head popped off.', 'N2', NULL),
(1224, 'You need to deliver this flag to the president. Imagine yourself running as fast as you can while carrying the parcel in order to get it to him. Your lungs are tired and your legs burn, but you must deliver the flag as soon as possible!', 'The world depends on you to do this delivery. If you fail, the United States will be ruined. You have to do it! Deliver that flag now! Run! Run!', 'To <kanji>deliver</kanji> this flag is something you just have <reading>to do</reading> (とど). Need to do. Want to do. There is no one else to do it. This is something only you are equipped to do.', 'N2', NULL),
(1225, 'Imagine yourself married to this animal husband with horns that are narrow. It’s a strange marriage. Listen to the goat making its goat noises as it clomps around your home. Now hug your goat husband and feel its soft fluffy coat against your skin.', 'But that doesn’t mean you can’t imagine the semantics. The finer points of the relationship. Go ahead, imagine all the weird stuff that comes with the territory for having a goat as a husband. Use all that weird stuff to remember this kanji and its reading. Want an example? Just imagine the goat doing dishes! Goats don’t have hands! Hahahahaha!!', 'I’m not even going to get into the <reading>sema</reading>ntics (せま) of how this weird relationship of yours works. Nope, this reading explanation is on the straight and <kanji>narrow</kanji>. Need to know basis. Classified. All that junk. There will be no discussion of the semantics between you and your goat lover.', 'N1', NULL),
(1226, 'Picture yourself smashing through this door using your shoulder and seeing the moon on the other side. Hear the splintering of wood as your shoulder crashes through the door and feel the impact with your body.', 'Now the weird man is inspecting your shoulder really closely and he keeps muttering "catapult powers" under his breath. It''s very unsettling, but he''s kind of right. A shoulder works just like the mechanism that winds up a catapult.', '"Wow, your <kanji>shoulder</kanji> is just like my <reading>cata</reading>pult (かた)!" you hear someone shout. As you turn you''re just in time to see a man catapulting through his own moon door!', 'N2', NULL),
(1227, 'Arms really look like snakes in the moonlight. Picture it. Your arm is long and shiny, just like a snake.', 'Your arms are nude, eh? That''s why they look like snakes, you know. You should be more careful.', 'Your <kanji>arm</kanji> is exposed to the moon and the outside and you feel a chill. Where are your clothes? Ah, you''re n<reading>ude, eh</reading> (うで)?! I guess it is pretty common to sleep in the nude, eh?', 'N2', NULL),
(1228, 'Imagine flying your helicopter closer to the woman. Even as you touch down she never takes her hand off her waist. You get closer and try to see why she''s touching her waist like that.', 'Imagine how warm your waist would be if you stuffed a bunch of 子 sheep into your waistband. Just don''t let anyone see you do it. Pretty sure that''s animal cruelty…', 'Once you get close, you see that her <kanji>waist</kanji> looks weird and… puffy? You realize she''s stuffed some <reading>子 shee</reading>p (こし) under her skirt''s waistband to line her waist with them. Guess it''s cold up here on the moon, so the 子 sheep keep her waist warm. And they had to be 子 sheep, otherwise they wouldn''t be small enough to fit under her clothes. ', 'N2', NULL),
(1229, 'Imagine yourself approaching this insect from a sneaky and stealthy angle. Now touch it. What does it feel like? Smooth? Hard? Fuzzy? Feel the insect in your mind as you touch it.', 'Feel this electrical shock to your system as you touch the lightning bug. This discourages you from ever touching another insect again. You don’t enjoy the shock.', 'Uh oh, looks like this particular insect is a lightning bug so when you <kanji>touch</kanji> it you get a <reading>shock</reading> (しょく). Did you know that lightning bugs contain bazillions of volts inside them? Well they do, and if you touch one, you get a shock. A very big shock.', 'N2', NULL),
(1230, 'Imagine the power that starts emanating from the cross once it’s placed on the car, it can’t move even an inch. Nope, it''s not going anywhere until its owner sobers up.', 'Picture your cyborg, approaching a drunkard''s car to place a cross on it. You see it open a big compartment on its chest, and it''s filled with crosses.', 'You have a lot of crosses to <kanji>place on</kanji> drunkard cars, so you get your <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい) to do it for you. The cyborg doesn''t need sleep, so it can patrol the streets 24/7 for the sake of public safety. What a saintly cyborg.', 'N1', NULL),
(1231, 'These layers are meant to protect you. We all have layers we hide behind sometimes, you know? You can''t be totally transparent with everyone.', 'Everyone in the crowd is completely silent, staring at your soul, with all of its layers peeled off.', 'Someone jumps up on stage and starts peeling off your <kanji>layer</kanji>s! You stand there, first goes the flag, then the mask, then ... your <reading>sou</reading>l (そう) is laid bare. No, literally. You don''t have a body, your soul was under that mask. And now it''s floating there for all to see.', 'N2', NULL),
(1232, 'Why would anyone make a model that looks like a lantern and a knife sticking into the ground?', 'Haven''t you seen any of those crazy cakes modeled after things like movies, towns, giants hams, and purses? They''re all the rage. Next time you make a cake try to make it look like a model lantern and knife with fake cake dirt and everything.', 'You touch the <kanji>model</kanji> and see that it''s made of <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい)! Someone really into lanterns and knives must have been given this model cake as a birthday present! Though they left it out here so they probably didn''t like it.', 'N2', NULL),
(1233, 'Imagine them putting together papers, talking to politicians, and standing in the street outside the capitol. They demand for this agency to be formed. They are doing all the correct, legal things to do it too.', '"Whenever you''re in a 丁, you''re in my territory," she yells, taking out a few agency members with her fists. "You won''t be starting any kind of agency here, not on my watch." And she lights it all on fire. ', 'This new <kanji>agency</kanji> is just starting to make progress when you hear a low rumble... then a screech.

"THIS IS MY STREET. NO NEW AGENCIES ON MY STREET!" Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう) cries as she barrels into everyone.', 'N2', NULL),
(1234, 'Imagine using your whole spirit to look at something. Like, you feel your spirit flowing out from your eyes and enveloping whatever you''re looking at. It''s kind of trippy. When''s the last time you really looked at something like that? Try it now.', 'Look at a picture of a sheep now. Really look at it with all your spirit. Bet you start noticing some things you''d never seen before, right? ', 'The first thing you want to <kanji>look at</kanji> like this is a <reading>shee</reading>p (し). You''ve seen a lot of sheep, but you''ve never really looked at them closely, so your image of them is kind of… woolly.', 'N1', NULL),
(1235, 'Note that the sheep has to sweep its tail to one side so that it doesn''t get in the way of all the construction tools. That''s why it looks a little different here. Imagine a sheep in a hard hat, with its tail on one side, getting down to some construction. How very distinctive.', 'Imagine the sheep holding the saw in its hooves. It isn''t easy to grip a saw with hooves, but this is no ordinary sheep.', 'The extra <kanji>distinction</kanji> of this sheep is that its really skilled with a <reading>saw</reading> (さ). That''s why it''s so good at construction.', 'N3', NULL),
(1236, 'Bamboo is great for pipes, and the only place for the bear to get it is your roof. So, that''s why it''s there.', 'Watch the bear take away your bamboo. Follow it to Genghis Khan''s house. There, the bear installs the pipe in Genghis Khan''s bathroom.', 'The <kanji>pipe</kanji> will be for Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん), who would like some pipes for his house.', 'N2', NULL),
(1238, 'Picture dawn, the sun rising, the birds chirping, and the screams of a new born baby rise in the village. Or Maybe it''s a new sheep! Or a new hat that someone just finished making.', 'When every dawn brings a new amount of people, those new people are going to need that same amount of row boats.', 'Today''s dawn has brought with it an increase in the <kanji>quantity</kanji> of <reading>row</reading>(りょう) boats! Aww, aren''t they cute? Look at all of the new row boats lined up together. They all have their own sets of oars too.', 'N2', NULL),
(1239, 'Imagine a big line in the dirt where you stand and see over into the other side: that''s a boundary. The boundary between your home and your neighbor''s, the boundary between cities or countries… Some you can cross, and some you can''t.', 'Picture yourself outside of Kyoto. Picture the boundary between you and Kyoto. Now hop over it!', 'The <kanji>boundary</kanji> you''re looking at now is the <reading>Kyo</reading>to (きょう) boundary. Kyoto is one of the most famous places in Japan, so you can imagine its boundary is really clear and marked by all kinds of boundary signs that say KYOTO.', 'N2', NULL),
(1240, 'Watch as the king loops the net around and around and around the zombie, keeping it completely pinned down.', 'Picture Genghis Khan looping around and around just like the king is looping around and around this zombie.', 'The king also explains that this <kanji>loop</kanji> technique was used by Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) when he was conquering cities. He''d loop around the entire place with a net and trap them.', 'N1', NULL),
(1241, 'The military really seems to have a thing for stopping a lot during their ceremonies.', 'This is a ceremony for celebrating the military so I guess they can drink as much booze as they want, but this seems very strange to you.', 'You look closer at the people in the <kanji>military</kanji> and realize that every time they stop they take a drink of <reading>boo</reading>ze (ぶ)!', 'N2', NULL),
(1242, 'Imagine the highest quality shellfish you''ve ever seen. They''re hard, smooth, and basically impenetrable.', 'I guess your shih tzu knows good quality shellfish too. :(', 'You finally get all of the shellfish open and you''re admiring their <kanji>quality</kanji>, when your <reading>shih tzu</reading> (しつ) jumps up on the table and gobbles them down. NO! You try to chase after the little dog, but it''s too late. They''re gone.', 'N4', NULL),
(1243, 'The angel just visited you, but it forgot to mention something important (probably life-threatening, it''s an angel after all), and now you see it scooting at top speeds toward you.', 'The angel really should have mentioned ninjutsu earlier. Then maybe you would have gone straight to a dojo to learn instead of falling into a ninjutsu trap yourself. The angel definitely failed its mission.', '"I forgot to <kanji>mention</kanji>, you have to study nin<reading>jutsu</reading> (じゅつ)!" the angel yells. But it''s too late, there are ninjas jumping out of the bushes around you and taking you hostage.', 'N2', NULL),
(1244, 'Picture handing things to your leader as you accompany them somewhere. You''re happy to do this, but you know you have so much more to offer than a hot towel and a pretty face. When will they see what you really have to offer?!', 'Picture you and the leader enjoying some matcha together under the cherry blossoms in きょうと. Yeah, this was definitely worth it. So glad you took them up on their offer and accompanied them.', 'Once a year, the leader will <kanji>offer</kanji> you a vacation in <reading>きょう</reading>と. It''s an all-expenses-paid trip! The catch is that you have to <kanji>accompany</kanji> your leader to きょうと…', 'N3', NULL),
(1245, 'Imagine yourself kicking this flag to see the blackjack table expand. Feel your foot kicking the flag. You’ve kicked it so many times that your leg is sore, but you need to expand the table some more so you keep kicking it. Imagine the blackjack table making that sound Mario makes whenever he gets a mushroom and expands in size.', 'Imagine yourself kicking the flag ten times. Count it off in your head and imagine each instance up to ten. Any more kicks after ten don’t cause the table to do anything, but it does cause your leg to get sore.', 'The blackjack table does have a limit though of how many times it will <kanji>expand</kanji>, and that limit is <reading>ten</reading> (てん). You can only kick the flag to get the table to expand ten times.', 'N1', NULL),
(1246, 'If you''re serious about being anti-shellfish then you should sell all the ones you have.', 'Picture yourself selling all of your shellfish to Han Solo. You stand there with your arms crossed, and Han just shakes his head at you and continues to toss them onto his spaceship.', 'You''re going to <kanji>sell</kanji> all of our shellfish to <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo. He isn''t anti-shellfish like you, and he knows how to turn a profit too!', 'N2', NULL),
(1247, 'Imagine lying battered and bruised in the upper boughs of this tree and thinking about all your worthless stocks and shares. Hopefully you had fun jumping out of your private jet, ''cause you can''t afford to ride in it anymore.', 'Imagine watching a real-time graph of your stock portfolio as its value plummets. The line dives toward the x-axis, and then… KABOOM! Not a cent of value left. Oh well… At least you had fun shouting "KABOOM" as your stocks crashed.', 'Your <kanji>stock</kanji>s and <kanji>share</kanji>s fell so hard that they probably went "<reading>KABOO</reading>M (かぶ)!" when they hit rock bottom.', 'N1', NULL),
(1006, 'Picture a village of Vikings, flourishing and prospering because they live right on the edge of a forest they can just hack away at whenever they need a new ship.', 'Picture a bunch of kid Vikings planting acorns in rows and watching them flourish and grow into big trees that they can cut down to keep up their Viking ways.', 'Vikings knew that in order to <kanji>prosper</kanji> they also needed to collect <reading>a</reading>corns (えい). If you''re chopping down trees all the time, you need to actively grow them back too. That''s why acorns are the actual, secret key to Viking prosperity.', 'N2', NULL),
(1248, 'Picture the roots of a building: metal beams going into the ground. Now imagine trying to build something like a subway under there. It''s not good! The building limits what can go under it.', 'Picture Genji tunneling under a building where a cute lady lives. He''s trying to get in, but the building roots limit how far he can dig. No! Now how will he steal a secret kiss?!', 'The person who hates these <kanji>limit</kanji>s more than anyone else is <reading>Gen</reading>ji (げん). He wants to be able to tunnel under buildings, sneak in, smooch ladies, and sneak back out. But all the building root limits make that impossible!', 'N3', NULL),
(1249, 'See the beggar on the ground. He’s writhing in agony and moaning for help. Imagine how sad and pitiful he looks.', 'Now watch as the beggar plays with the yo-yo you just gave him. He’s actually pretty good with it. See the beggar doing insane tricks with his new yo-yo as passersby give him change for his impressive yo-yo performance.', 'When you set the money down next to the beggar he shouts, “No! I want a <reading>yo</reading>-yo (よ)!” so you go to the corner store and buy him a yo-yo with the money instead. You <kanji>give</kanji> the yo-yo to the beggar and he looks very happy.', 'N3', NULL),
(1250, 'Imagine how your repertoire of new words will impress all your friends and family. Don’t include them on your secret that you used the Mouth Now! program, just feel super smart with all the words you now know.', 'Since you bought the program, you also got the Gandhi books included with your order. Imagine yourself reading them and learning all about non-violent resistance. Fascinating.', 'If you buy now, we’ll also <kanji>include</kanji> a handful of books by <reading>Gan</reading>dhi (がん) himself. Gandhi didn’t use our program, but we’re sure if he’d had the opportunity to, he would have used it. So buy now, and get the words of Gandhi in your hands as well as your mouth!', 'N2', NULL),
(1251, 'Feel the heat of the sun on your back as you look down over your capital, your long hair blowing over your shoulder. Your shadow stretches in front of you and it''s honestly pretty impressive.', 'What do you do? Keep standing here, admiring the view and casting this shadow on the acorn. Or do you move and let the acorn grow, becoming a tree that can cast its own shadow on your capital.', 'In the shade from your <kanji>shadow</kanji> you see a small <reading>a</reading>corn (えい) laying on the ground. You''re about to reach down and pick it up when you realize that your shadow is keeping this little acorn from growing up big and strong.', 'N1', NULL),
(1252, 'Imagine your poor brother and the condition he is in. His glass bones and paper skin make it impossible for him to move very fast. Imagine the sad face he makes when he realizes his fate.', 'This is also, incidentally, where the tsunami''s condition is the worst, so you better get to きょうと quick!', 'His <kanji>condition</kanji> gets worse and you have to travel all the way to his home in <reading>きょう</reading>と to get him out.', 'N2', NULL),
(1253, 'Imagine being carried away on your stool by the tsunami, with your entire yurt floating around you too. You''re both in transit... to somewhere. You don''t know where though.', 'You hope the tsunami''s water will drop you off somewhere nice. Somewhere it''s easy to jump on a different kind of mass transit, to get back home again.', 'The <kanji>transit</kanji> of yourself and your yurt home is due to the <reading>water</reading> (わた). This is water transit.', 'N3', NULL),
(1254, 'Hear the poop echo. You know what a poop usually sounds like, now imagine that sound again, but louder and with an echo. How could anyone not notice?', 'Welp. きょうと knows you pooped. That will echo through your memories for the rest of your life. I hope you''re happy.', 'The <kanji>echo</kanji> doesn''t stop there, this building''s roots extend throughout the entire city of <reading>きょう</reading>と. The echo reverberates and soon everyone in きょうと is listening, horrified, to your poop.', 'N1', NULL),
(1255, 'Imagine trying to fill out your ballot while flying in a helicopter and using only a jackhammer to do it. Do you think you could do it? Do you think you could do it AND vote for the right person?', 'You''re starting to think this method of filling out ballots is rigged.', 'While using the jackhammer on the <kanji>ballot</kanji>, the helicopter pilot keeps yelling “<reading>Heyo</reading> (ひょう), don''t mess up! Heyo! Watch out, don''t let me see!” as he flies the helicopter all over the place.', 'N1', NULL),
(1256, 'Picture this scene in your mind. Squint as the sun shines in your eyes, and feel its warmth as it rises up over the capital.', 'Picture this scene again, but this time the sun is making the buildings of the capital look like big, fancy cakes. Now take a bite!', 'The only way to make this <kanji>scene</kanji> better would be to add <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい). Now that you think about it, as the sun rises and frosts the capital with light, the buildings start to look like big, shiny cakes.', 'N3', NULL),
(1257, 'REMINDER: You are not allowed to touch your friends without their permission. If they say no to you pulling out their friendship, then you don''t get to pull out their friendship. End of story.', 'REMINDER: If someone asks you to pull out their hair to help them achieve a nude head, you don''t have to do it unless you want to! Choices and respect, people!', 'Your friend looks you in the eye and says, I want you to <kanji>pull out</kanji> my hair. I want my head to be <reading>nu</reading>de (ぬ). Now you have to use your fingers on your friend to pull out their hair and make their head all nude.', 'N3', NULL),
(1258, 'Imagine being unable to stop yourself from saying "I have the axe with the drop on it!" and the recently axed person jumps out from behind a bush and shouts, "Aha! I''m gonna sue you!"', 'The person you axed cries and tries desperately to find their blood on the axe so they can keep suing you properly. You hold the soap behind your back and cackle.', 'You want to <kanji>sue</kanji> them back so you clean the axe with some <reading>so</reading>ap (そ). "Your honor," you say in court, "I don''t see any drops of blood, I countersue!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1259, 'Think of all the things you could say in public that could get you stuck dealing with a lawsuit.

Oh my gosh! How dare you think those things?! You''re lucky you didn''t say them out loud in public, or I''d file a lawsuit against you myself!', 'You better hope what you said wasn''t about the Shougun, or he''s really going to use this lawsuit to destroy you.', 'Any and all <kanji>lawsuit</kanji>s need to be brought before the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). Oh, you thought you''d get a judge or a jury? Nope, the Shougun decides your fate.', 'N1', NULL),
(1260, 'Imagine chasing a criminal for ages on your scooter, stopping only for the occasional water break, and finally apprehending them when they hit a dead end. How good would it feel to write a ticket with your fancy brush?  ', 'It''s a really sturdy tie you use to apprehend them, so even if they struggle there''s no chance they get away.', 'When you do catch up and <kanji>apprehend</kanji> someone, you always tie them down with the <reading>tie</reading> (たい) from your neck.', 'N1', NULL),
(1261, 'Imagine getting married in a t-shirt and sweatpants. Sure, you could do it, but wouldn''t you feel like something was missing? Like you needed to supplement the situation a little?', 'This hoe can also be used to pull back the bride''s veil — one of its many supplemental uses.', 'You decide to further <kanji>supplement</kanji> your wedding outfit with a garden <reading>hoe</reading> (ほ). This hoe is a symbolic supplement — it represents the new life you''ll "cultivate" together with your partner.', 'N2', NULL),
(1262, 'Imagine this leader twirling his stick around with his hook so quickly it changes the actual weather. Then he shoots it into the sky with an arrow, changing the actual climate itself.', 'Ask こういち why he''s changing the climate. He says more people do their WaniKani reviews when it''s raining, so he decided to create climate change to make it more dangerous to go outside. "This way, people will stay indoors and do their WaniKani reviews," he tells you.', 'Who is this <kanji>climate</kanji> and <kanji>weather</kanji>-changing <kanji>candidate</kanji> leader with the hook, stick, and arrow? It''s none other than <reading>こう</reading>いち.', 'N3', NULL),
(1263, 'Watch the lifeguard walk around the beach. He needs to pick a nice big tree for his set up, so he can provide the best care possible.', 'Imagine こういち setting up a care station. You hope you don''t get in trouble in the water so you don''t have to see how good he is at caring for people.

Hint: when you see the "lifeguard" radical, the reading is probably going to be こう.', 'You look closer at the <kanji>set up</kanji> and realize the lifeguard is <reading>こう</reading>いち! You know he teaches Japanese but... is こういち really qualified to care for people like this?', 'N3', NULL),
(1264, 'Look at this greenhouse tree struggle to grow big and strong like the wild tree outside. Hear its leaves shake and timber creak. How cute.', 'Hear the little tree moaning at its pitiful imitation of a real tree. The greenhouse tree sounds very sad and you actually start to feel bad for it. Moan along with the tree in sympathy.', 'The greenhouse tree realizes that its <kanji>imitation</kanji> will never be as good as the real thing. When it realizes this, the tree <reading>mo</reading>ans (も) aloud. The tree is still kinda tiny, so the moan isn’t very loud, but it is still pretty strange to witness. Speaking of witnesses, <reading>Bo</reading>bo (ぼ) the clown sees this greenhouse tree moaning and decides to take it for his next clown show. Surely this miniature moaning tree can be worked into Bobo''s next clown act.', 'N1', NULL),
(1265, 'Imagine being at a wedding and getting to catch a ton of things with your fingers.', 'Picture yourself grumpily hoeing all the things you couldn''t catch, right into a big dumpster.', 'You''re having a good time <kanji>catch</kanji>ing things until it''s cleanup time and someone throws a <reading>hoe</reading> (ほ) at you. First of all, ouch, that was your face. Second, this is your wedding. You aren''t supposed to have to hoe it all up. You''re supposed to drop everything you caught and leave!', 'N3', NULL),
(1266, 'Imagine eating this fresh fish and fresh sheep. It’s the freshest you’ve ever tasted. Taste it on your tongue and experience how fresh it is.', 'Can you imagine eating a fresh centaur? How weird would that be? Half of it is human-like, so you’d be like, a half cannibal. Weird, huh?', 'This place also serves <kanji>fresh</kanji> <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん), but that’s just weird. Centaur is endangered and you don’t believe in eating endangered things. Even if they are incredibly fresh.', 'N1', NULL),
(1267, 'It doesn''t matter what power you mix, because all power is strong. No matter what you use this mixed power on, it will be effective. Imagine some situations where you mix power with power. ', 'こういちs punch attack was super effective against that Pikurchort.', 'The most <kanji>effective</kanji> mix of two powers is two <reading>こう</reading>いちs. Take one, mix it with another, and you have something with immeasurable power. You use it to attack that animal-like creature. It''s super effective!', 'N2', NULL),
(1268, 'Look at that mantis, pretending it belongs here. What are you gonna do about it?', 'Your Bosozoku life has prepared you for this moment. Those who don''t belong shall be punished, you think to yourself.', 'Luckily for you, and unluckily for this mantis who doesn''t <kanji>belong</kanji> on your flag, you''re a Boso<reading>zoku</reading> (ぞく) bike gang member, and you don''t take crap from nobody. You climb the flagpole, punch the mantis and yell, "YOU DON''T BELONG HERE!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1269, 'Close your eyes and picture your soul. Now your soul is opening a little window inside of you that is the perfect size for shellfish of all kinds. Hopefully with exposure and acceptance you''ll grow accustomed to them.', 'Force yourself to get accustomed to shellfish like Genghis Khan, then maybe you''ll rule most of the planet someday.', 'You''ll need to become <kanji>accustomed</kanji> to eating shellfish if you want to grow up big and strong like Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん). He didn''t have shellfish growing up either you know, he had to grow accustomed to their taste too, but look how successful he was!', 'N3', NULL),
(1270, 'Imagine dancing to your own plentiful bean music as you gorge on the abundant stock of beans.


', 'Be polite! Keep your plentiful farts at home.', 'Your music bean farts are too <kanji>plentiful</kanji> to leave your <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう). If you tried to leave your home you''d definitely fart all over people outside, so you keep your plentiful farts to yourself.', 'N2', NULL),
(1271, 'Picture both of these little flowers, happily swaying in the breeze. Then the tsunami comes, leaves, and you look back and their little cup-heads are full to the brim with water.', 'Soon you have full color pages, full of stories about both of these tsunami-filled flowers and their adventures together. And when you''re done, your heart is full as well. Awwww.', 'As you stare at the <kanji>full</kanji> flowers, you decide to write a <reading>man</reading>ga (まん) about them. Your head is full of manga ideas!', 'N3', NULL),
(1272, 'Imagine this moon in bed, slowly getting bigger and bigger, becoming more obese every day. Hear the sound of the bed creaking and cracking as the moon grows larger and larger.', 'Imagine the obese moon cozied up in bed with a heated blanket and hot cocoa.', 'Since the <kanji>obese</kanji> moon is in bed all day, and since the moon stays pretty cold, she needs some extra <reading>he</reading>at (ひ) to stay warm. A heated blanket should do the trick.', 'N1', NULL),
(1273, 'Imagine being a gladiator. Your big fight is coming and you''re the  main event! Look at the scroll for the fight and try to find yourself.', 'After a while of showing off the scroll, someone finally pulls you aside and says, "That guy isn''t you, it''s the real Genghis Khan. He''s coming to the event and he''s against you. So. Nice knowing you."

Oh.', 'When you look at the <kanji>scroll</kanji> you realize... you look just like Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん)! You excitedly fist pump and start showing everyone how cool you are.', 'N2', NULL),
(1274, 'Now you have to search through a bunch of religious texts for this humble stool with your fingers. Don''t go too fast and get paper cuts!', 'You dedicated your soul to searching, and now that your soul is free from its body, you can continue the search until you find it!', 'You <kanji>search</kanji> and you search and, unfortunately, you spend your entire life searching and you die without finding the humble stool. But don''t worry, your <reading>sou</reading>l (そう) comes back and now you get to continue your search but with afterlife powers!', 'N2', NULL),
(1275, 'Just imagine thread everywhere, mixing in with everything. It''s all around, and no matter where you go you get more and more caught in this thread. Eventually it tightens because you''re trying to escape, and you feel yourself having trouble breathing. Imagine the moment when you realize you''re getting strangled.', 'In your final moments before you are totally strangled, you think you see a little thread come out of his butt. "I should have done my WaniKani reviews" is your last thought.', 'As you are <kanji>strangle</kanji>d you see who set this trap. (What else could it be, with all this thread everywhere?) It is <reading>こう</reading>いち, who just watches you as you lose consciousness, tapping his fingers together.', 'N1', NULL),
(1276, 'Just because someone is your comrade doesn''t mean they actually like you. Don''t let your guard down and let them near your car.', 'Comrade, what the heck
I want to get my car back
I thought we were friends.', 'You feel really weird about this whole situation so you write your <kanji>comrade</kanji> a <reading>hai</reading>ku (はい). It''s really all you can do, since your car is long gone.', 'N1', NULL),
(1277, 'You know the phrase, "You can run, but you can''t hide?" Wolverine can''t run anymore, so he''s trying to hide (even though he knows it probably won''t work).', 'Lucky Wolverine! Inns are good for hiding. The staff isn''t supposed to give out his room number, and there are tons of different rooms for him to hide in.', 'Wolverine looks up at the building he''s trying to <kanji>hide</kanji> behind and sees it''s an <reading>inn</reading> (いん).', 'N1', NULL),
(1278, 'If someone got dirt all up in your finger and toe business, wouldn''t you want to hang them upside-down too?', 'Don''t get dirt on me, buddy. Or I''ll hang you upside-down and hit you with my car. Then you''ll be sorry.', 'You <kanji>hang</kanji> this person upside-down but they don''t seem to be suffering enough, so you hit them with your <reading>ca</reading>r (か). No mercy.', 'N3', NULL),
(1279, 'See these two husbands as the sun sets under them. Listen to them each saying their vows, expressing how they could never replace one another.', 'See the husbands driving off into the sunset in their car. Hear the engine rumble as the car speeds off into the distance.', 'After the ceremony, the husband and his husband get in a <reading>ca</reading>r (か) and drive off into the sunset. They’ll never be able to <kanji>replace</kanji> this memory with anything. Truly it is the happiest day of these husbands’ lives.', 'N2', NULL),
(1280, 'Imagine the old flag with a face and mouth and eyes and nose... it talks and listens and everything else. It''s a flag that''s <kanji>alive</kanji> D:', 'Not as cool as a real relic, it''s put in this dumb Little Kyoto shrine that''s basically just a tourist attraction. Eh, whatever.', 'The flag that is old and <kanji>alive</kanji> is actually a Buddhist relic (that''s why it''s alive, in fact ... and old!). But, it''s a really dumb relic, so it''s put in Little <reading>Kyo</reading>to (きょ).', 'N3', NULL),
(1345, 'When you see someone wearing a fancy suit that suits them, don''t you wonder about how much their salary is?', 'Imagine your salary being paid in cucumbers. Open that sweet paycheck and watch the cucumbers come tumbling out.', 'Your <kanji>salary</kanji> is paid in <reading>cu</reading>cumbers (きゅう). Maybe you shouldn''t have worn that cucumber tie when you interviewed for this job, even though it suited you well.', 'N3', NULL),
(1371, 'Imagine what the world would be like without thread. No thread of any color. How would you fix tears in clothes or make new ones? It would be a strange world where thread is extinct.', 'Pretend you have deadly zits. They’re everywhere, not just your face. You can’t pop them or you’ll die, but they’re all bulging and extremely painful. Now you’re facing extinction. Lousy.', 'The <kanji>extinction</kanji> of thread was brought on by a very strange thing. Deadly <reading>zits</reading> (ぜつ). These zits killed off all the producers of thread, and eventually the thread went extinct. Dang zits.', 'N3', NULL),
(1954, 'Imagine being in the capital. The tsunami hits and now you are all wet. You are also very cool because the water is not warm. You are cool and wet. How sad.', 'You''re still feeling pretty cool and wet when another wave courses by. You see Sue surfing on it, screaming at the top of her lungs, "FREEDOM!!! NOW EVERYWHERE IS SUE ZOO!!!"', 'You find out that the <kanji>cool</kanji> tsunami water is coming from <reading>Sue Zoo</reading> (すず). The only animal there is a penguin named Sue, so the Sue Zoo is always kept cool for her. But now that Sue''s fountain broke, the cool water is everywhere! Time to sue the zoo…', 'N2', NULL),
(1281, 'Imagine being at a maker''s contest. Each person has to announce loudly what they''ll create, then they have to create it.', 'Imagine the scooter you created with a zombie on it. It''s a perfect place for the zombie to get stuck and scooted off into nowhere.', 'But, a scooter has been <kanji>create</kanji>d before. What is different about yours? The scooter you will create is specifically designed to keep <reading>zo</reading>mbies (ぞう) busy. ', 'N2', NULL),
(1282, 'Picture yourself instructing a class full of knowledge hungry little kids. They reach out with their fingers to accept the truth you bring to them.', 'Picture yourself instructing little kids how to punch the straw into small juice boxes. Hold it right-side-up! Don''t squeeze too hard! Don''t punch it through the other side! No! Don''t bite the box! Instructing is hard!', '"Yes! I will <kanji>instruct</kanji> you, my dear students!" you cry, "I will instruct you on how to open a small <reading>ju</reading>ice (じゅ) box!" That''s right. These little kids need help learning how to open small juice boxes (small because kids are small).', 'N1', NULL),
(1283, 'Imagine breaking and bending a comb so you can use it to stamp your seal onto things.', 'Hopefully the inn accepts this seal... or you aren''t going to have a place to sleep tonight. And it kind of just looks like you dipped a comb in ink and pressed it around on some paper.', 'You need this <kanji>seal</kanji> to stay at an <reading>inn</reading> (いん). All inns require official paperwork, so you had to improvise and make a seal on the fly.', 'N2', NULL),
(1284, 'What do you create in your warehouse? Picture all the different things you could create in your mind.', 'When you create something your soul is happy! It''s good for you, you know?', 'When you <kanji>create</kanji> it''s good for your <reading>sou</reading>l (そう). It''s so freeing, in fact, that when you get really into your creating, sometimes you''d swear your soul was floating a little bit outside of your body. But every time you notice, it sinks back down. Hmm.', 'N1', NULL),
(1285, 'Imagine loitering around near (but not too near!) the black hole waiting for it to finish so you can restore the galaxy.', 'Maybe if you yell f**k you enough things will be restored. Ha. No. Everything''s gone and it''s your fault for being gullible.', 'Finally there''s nothing else for the black hole to eat so you hit the <kanji>restore</kanji> button.... but nothing happens. "Oh <reading>f**k</reading> (ふく) you!" you scream as you realize you were lied to.', 'N2', NULL),
(1286, 'Picture yourself loitering around your master’s fancy mansion. You’re pretending to dust things off, but you’re actually just waiting for him to depart on his journey.', 'Your master has departed! You are the 王 now!', 'Finally, it''s time for your master''s <kanji>journey</kanji> and he calls to you and says, "You are the <reading>王</reading> (おう) of the house now. Take care of everything while I''m gone."', 'N1', NULL),
(1287, 'Imagine this garage of yours with a sea of white cars. Now there is a new car that really sticks out like a sore thumb. Imagine yourself looking out over your cars and seeing this wacky car practically jumping out at you.', 'See yourself driving around in this peacock car. Hear the engine purr as strangers jeer and yell at you. This car sure does get a lot of attention, doesn’t it?', 'What did I say this car looks like? Oh that’s right, a pea<reading>cock</reading> (かく). This car looks like a peacock. It’s the color of a peacock and you’ll look like you’re peacocking when you’re out on the town driving it. It really is such a stark <kanji>contrast</kanji> to like, every other car out there.', 'N1', NULL),
(1288, 'Imagine yourself using this bamboo writing brush. Do you know many kanji? Of course you do! Imagine yourself writing them all with this nice writing brush. It’s actually fun to write things with it!', 'Imagine the sound the brush makes when it hits the paper. It’s a bit of a plop, and then kind of a scratching sound as the brush bristles move around. Really focus on this sound that the brush makes when it hits the paper. ', 'Once the <kanji>writing brush</kanji> <reading>hits</reading> (ひつ) that paper, it’s like you transform into a totally different person. All of a sudden you’re a master calligrapher, writing anything and everything in a most beautiful fashion. Once that brush hits the paper, it’s on!', 'N2', NULL),
(1289, 'Imagine having these gold fins in your mouth. Taste the metal as your tongue runs over them.', 'Lead poisoning, huh? What a way for everything to end...', 'You pay for your <kanji>lead</kanji> but the cashier puts it in your mouth where the gold fins were and... everything <reading>en</reading>ds (えん).', 'N1', NULL),
(1290, 'Imagine walking down the street to deposit more shellfish into your special savings and gaze happily at the huge pile you keep far enough away that you aren''t tempted to spend it.', 'Imagine trying to lift up a shellfish with a pair of smooth chopsticks. It slips right out. Ugh! I guess that''s good though, because it''s a savings for a reason (you shouldn''t be spending it)!', 'When you want to withdraw from your <kanji>savings</kanji> you force yourself to use <reading>cho</reading>psticks (ちょ) to pick up the shellfish. They''re especially slippery, hard to use chopsticks too, that way it''s really hard to take too much out of your savings.', 'N2', NULL),
(1291, 'Imagine a poor, broken town. And, imagine the mayor of a town telling their people that things are bad because of that bad old winter. "It''s to blame for our circumstance!" he yells.', 'Imagine your kid character walking through town, using their super powers to destroy buildings and rot food.', 'In reality, this <kanji>circumstance</kanji> was actually caused by a single <reading>子</reading> (こ), though. This kid went through town, destroying everything in their path.', 'N1', NULL),
(1292, 'Picture this huge, tall building standing directly in the way. You peer around the other side and there is a book floating in the air that says "Chapter 5: Success and Happiness." Dang, you really want to get over there to that part of your life.', 'The Shougun wants to hinder you and your life. He wants you to have to work harder so you become better, faster, and stronger than anyone else. Hindering you is his way of making sure you don''t take the easy way out in life.', 'Someone put this building here specifically to <kanji>hinder</kanji> you. They knew you were close to the next chapter of your life, and they wanted to hinder you really good. And there they are, sitting on a throne smirking. It''s the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう).', 'N1', NULL),
(1293, 'First of all, this could be really fun. Second of all, if this person is a demon or something, you should really just obey them or they could curse you. What''s the worst that could happen?!', 'You stole those jewels together and now you''re an accomplice for accompanying him! You better hide those jewels!!', 'If you <kanji>obey</kanji> the horned man you''ll end up with a ton of <reading>jew</reading>els (じゅう)!', 'N1', NULL),
(1294, 'Just keep going with this sentence about the ego. You can''t stop, because really it''s your ego that''s out of control. That''s <kanji>I</kanji> when you''re saying it.', 'You''re just destroying the ego of that other guy. "Why?" you ask? "Because <kanji>I</kanji> am the boss here. And you are the baby. Ego things."', '<kanji>I</kanji> am the greatest in the world! I am no baby like you, that''s why. A baby who says "<reading>ga</reading> (が) ga ga ga!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1295, 'Imagine this white tsunami, more violent and fierce than any other tsunami you’ve ever seen, coming toward you. It’s very frightening. Feel the cold wetness it brings to your skin.', '"Geecckkkiiiiii!!!" you scream in horror, as he''s thrown about by the fierce, winter tsunami. You have to close your eyes. You can''t watch Gecki go.', 'The tsunami is too <kanji>fierce</kanji>, too <kanji>violent</kanji>, and before you realize what''s happening, <reading>Gecki</reading> (げき), your beloved gecko, is lifted up and carried off into the air.', 'N1', NULL),
(1297, 'If you can get to the top of the cliff, it will use its "ten thousand power" to encourage you.', 'A cliff is encouraging you to buy a ray gun for some reason. This is not the encouragement you expected, but it is encouragement nonetheless. You go back down the cliff and immediately buy a ray gun.', 'When you get there, it does <kanji>encourage</kanji> you. The cliff says "Hey, I encourage you to buy a <reading>ray</reading> (れい) gun."', 'N1', NULL),
(1298, 'Imagine being chastised with a measurement device. Think how horrible it would be getting hit on your knuckles, your nose, or the back of your head. It would be awful. Imagine this experience.', 'How could you say something in English while you''re in とうきょう?! After you went to all the trouble to learn all this kanji and Japanese? Geez, we almost think you deserve to be chastised...', 'You''re being <kanji>chastise</kanji>d because you tried to say something in English in <reading>とう</reading>きょう. Everyone around you slowly turned around, eyes aflame and rulers in hand, ready to chastise you for uttering those forbidden words.', 'N1', NULL),
(1299, 'Picture a bunch of postal workers going in and out of the post office building, drooping under the weight of so much mail. It''s a tough job.', 'Imagine all these droopy youths, showing up for the morning shift to pay their way through college as mail carriers. Ah, you were one of them once…', 'Because it''s such a physically demanding job, all the <kanji>mail</kanji> carriers in this <kanji>post</kanji> office are <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう). Only the youths can handle this kind of heavy mail-bag lifting, I guess. ', 'N2', NULL),
(1300, 'Picture this golden cross in your head. Why do you suppose there’s this needle stuck in it? You try to pull it out but you can’t. Envision yourself straining, trying to get the thing out.', 'Imagine a needle sticking into your shin. It hurts a ton. How would you try to get it out?', 'You''re about to give up on the <kanji>needle</kanji> when it pops out and flies directly into your <reading>shin</reading> (しん). Ouch! Now you''re desperately trying to pull the needle out of your shin, but it really won''t budge this time. Maybe you should have minded your own business.', 'N2', NULL),
(1301, 'We don''t want to focus too much on what the indication / sign actually is, though, because it will take away from the <kanji>indication</kanji> part (which we want to remember). So, focus on the fact that this is some kind of indication that''s important, but you don''t know what that indication means or is... because we''ll use that with the reading.', 'Imagine an announcer watching the loitering mountain king, "There he is... and it''s still winter. We all know what that means. It''s a sign that Mrs. Chou will be coming out of hibernation soon." Then, you can imagine her bursting forth from the mountain, running down her street.', 'When you spot a loitering mountain king in the winter, you know it''s an <kanji>indication</kanji> that soon Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう) will appear. It''s like a Groundhog''s Day kind of situation, but instead it means Mrs. Chou is about to come out, perhaps early for the season.', 'N1', NULL),
(1302, 'Imagine inching closer to this suspicious soul floating around a tombstone. What do you think it''s doing?', 'Picture a suspicious, kettle holding, kayaking soul. The afterlife may be more interesting than you thought.', 'You sneak around the tombstone to see what this <kanji>suspicious</kanji> soul is doing, and you realize it''s pretending to <reading>kay</reading>ak (かい). The soul is sitting behind the tombstone, in a small kayak, moving its arms like it''s rowing through water.

Even more suspicious, the soul seems to be holding a <reading>ke</reading>ttle (け). Considering the soul is already dead, this doesn''t seem like something it will need.', 'N1', NULL),
(1303, 'Picture this beast creeping, low to the ground, in the grass around the rice paddy. It''s got its eyes on you. Uh oh.', 'All beasts have something they''re trying to protect. In this case, it''s some beastly jewels.', 'The <kanji>beast</kanji> is guarding its precious <reading>jew</reading>els (じゅう), which it buried in the grass or the rice paddy somewhere. If you don''t want the beast to think you''re coming to steal those jewels, you better keep your distance.', 'N1', NULL),
(1305, 'Look at this cake you have made. It’s majestic. Now taste some of it. It is one great tasting cake. Imagine yourself eating the best cake in all of ever.', 'Hear the roar of the car engine as you get splattered in the face with the cake. Wipe it off of your face just in time to see the car speeding off into the distance. Also, recall that this kanji shares a reading with the similar kanji, 果.', 'You’re standing back, admiring your <kanji>cake</kanji> when you hear a <reading>ca</reading>r (か) engine revving and before you know it your cake has been run over. The car came out of nowhere and ran your cake right over, spraying fruit cake bits and flowers all over the place. Stupid car!', 'N2', NULL),
(1306, 'Imagine how painful this would be, having your lip ring ripped from your lip. It would hurt a lot. Touch your lip and imagine missing half of it. Yikes.', 'Imagine yourself fording the river, all wet and cold from the water. Keeping your eyes on your car, you move slowly across. Look at the car. Look at it!', 'But why were you trying to cross this <kanji>river</kanji> anyway? Well your <reading>ca</reading>r (か) was on the other side of it, and you figured if you could get to your car you could outrun the tsunami and be safe. Too bad you fell and ripped out your lip ring though.', 'N2', NULL),
(1307, 'Imagine shaking your fingers at the dirt. Do you think you can shake the dirt hard enough to cause a landslide (spoilers, no).', 'Imagine your shins shaking, your hands are waving, and they won''t stop. No! You shouldn''t have tried to start a landslide. This was a bad idea!', 'You think you finally got the earth to <kanji>shake</kanji> but when you look down it''s just your <reading>shin</reading>s (しん). Darn you shins, I thought I made some progress. Hey wait... why won''t you stop?!', 'N1', NULL),
(1308, 'Sweat really does feel like a tsunami though. Wow, it''s dry and hot out right now!', 'The one place you wish sweat wouldn''t go, but somehow it always does: the ass.', 'The <kanji>sweat</kanji> isn''t just on your face, it''s also dripping down your <reading>ass</reading> (あせ).', 'N2', NULL),
(1310, 'Picture the dirt-covered head popping up again and again. How can it move under the ground like that if it''s just a head? You decide to get closer this time, and for once it speaks: "Get me out of the ground!"', 'Imagine how dirty you and your saw are after all this. At least you could help this cyborg with your saw. "Thanks again," it says, then runs off shaking the dirt out of its hair.', 'The dirt-covered head asks for help <kanji>again</kanji> and again, but you''ve only got a <reading>saw</reading> (さ). So you start digging with it, scraping the dirt away, until you see this head has the body of a <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい)! ', 'N2', NULL),
(1311, 'Look around you. Other women are adjoining their fingers and standing with you. There''s hundreds, no, thousands of you standing together.', 'Picture a bunch of women standing together. They''re holding hands in sets, adjoined together.', 'All the women <kanji>adjoin</kanji> in <reading>sets</reading> (せつ). Sets of two, of course. One woman with another woman, standing in unison.', 'N2', NULL),
(1312, 'Imagine this animal and this insect. Let’s say it’s a flea and a dog. Imagine them going everywhere together. To the park, getting ice cream, even the drive in movie theater. They’re inseparable.', 'Picture this animal with the insect on his head doing a Sudoku. See the insect hopping around on the animals head shouting answers and suggestions into the animal’s ear. How cute.', 'One activity that the animal can never do <kanji>alone</kanji> is solve Su<reading>doku</reading> (どく) puzzles. The insect always has to help him out with them, and together they are an unstoppable Sudoku solving team.', 'N1', NULL),
(1313, 'Imagine what your toe tastes like. Or actually taste it, I don''t care. What do the taste and texture of it say about you, about your future fortune?', 'Imagine getting your fortune told by a centaur fortune teller. Do you trust this mythical beast to know your future? Watch him manipulate his crystal ball as his hooves stomp the ground beneath him.', 'To <kanji>occupy</kanji> some free time you have, you go and get your <kanji>fortune</kanji> told at a fortune teller. Your fortune is that you’ll have an encounter with a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん) in the near future. Just then you realize that the fortune teller is actually a centaur! The fortune has come true!', 'N2', NULL),
(1519, 'Don''t let your spirits droop — kanji can be tough to learn, but you''re doing great!', 'Imagine setting your bum in a droopy rubber swing seat. Feel it droop down a little lower as you put your weight into it and let your legs dangle. Ah, such comfort.', 'You know what''s better when it <kanji>droop</kanji>s a little? A <reading>swi</reading>ng (すい). Those swings with the droopy rubber seats are so comfortable to sit on and <kanji>dangle</kanji> your legs off of — way better than the rigid wooden ones.', 'N1', NULL),
(1314, 'Something about the way they''re beckoning you with their fingers is really enticing. You can''t help but walk toward them.', 'Even if the Shougun didn''t have an army that would crush you if you didn''t come when he called, you''d go anyway. Those beckoning fingers are just so... weirdly enticing.', 'The person <kanji>beckon</kanji>ing you is the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). You can''t help but obey the Shougun''s beckoning call.', 'N3', NULL),
(1315, 'When you think about it, a comb is like a ladder. So if you cut all the rungs off with your weapon and put them on the ground, they turn into stairs!', 'Imagine standing at the top of the stairs you made yourself, looking down at the entire town below you, as the light of dawn breaks and fills everything before you with light. And now more people can see this beautiful sight if they climb your steps.', 'You slowly work to create these <kanji>stairs</kanji>, you have to slice up a lot of combs, and at <reading>dawn</reading> (だん) they are done!', 'N3', NULL),
(1316, 'Imagine having a whole rice paddy in your stomach. A rice paddy the size of the moon. Your stomach hurts a lot. You feel like you’re going to throw up.', 'This eagle saw your stomach distress and decided to help by making you barf. Hear the screech of the eagle as it swoops down and rams you in the stomach.', 'From the sky, an eagle swoops down and flies right into your distended <kanji>stomach</kanji>. Bam! The <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) hits your stomach and you vomit up the entire moon sized rice paddy.', 'N2', NULL),
(1317, 'Look down at your perfect belly. It''s looking back up at you.', 'Forget to eat lunch? Belly grumbles, "F**k you." Call yourself fat and say you need to lose weight? Belly cries, "F**k you!" Be nice to your belly.', 'If you don''t treat your <kanji>belly</kanji> with respect, when you look down at it, it''ll yell, "<reading>F**k</reading> (ふく) you!" Whoa, looks like someone''s got an <em>upset</em> stomach.', 'N3', NULL),
(1318, 'Sick people shouldn''t have to do things, of course they''re in pain. You shouldn''t let your mama do anything if she''s in pain, do it for her!', 'You finish doing your tasks and hers too, and you ask her, "How is the pain now, Mama?" "THE PAIN IS TOO MUCH!" she cries. Oh mama.', 'Your mama''s <kanji>pain</kanji> is <reading>too</reading> (つう) much for her to handle, so now you have to do all of her tasks too.', 'N3', NULL),
(1319, 'Picture the roots raising up a little bit from the ground and tripping up your scooter. They reject you, so you should just retreat.', 'You didn''t retreat fast enough, the roots are rejecting your life, and strangling you with your own tie.', 'You''re trying to <kanji>retreat</kanji> by scooting backwards, but the roots grab onto your <reading>tie</reading> (たい). You try to pull your tie off but it''s just getting tighter and tighter around your neck.', 'N3', NULL),
(1320, 'Look at this flag telling you to yield. Now look through the exit right by the flag. You can see a bunch of people out there walking. Imagine standing by the exit, yielding to the other people until you have an opening. Hear the hustle and bustle of the people as you wait.', 'Darn coots! Forget the yield flag, it’s time to exit. Imagine busting out of the exit, punching and pushing your way through the old coots. Hear them scream and shout “Augh! My hip!” as you plow through them.', 'Even though the flag told you to <kanji>yield</kanji>, you’re starting to lose your patience. The people walking outside the exit are all a bunch of crazy old <reading>coots</reading> (くつ)! These crazy coots keep walking right in front of the door, getting in the way. The old coots aren’t even going anywhere, they just keep looping around to obstruct the way!', 'N1', NULL),
(1321, 'Picture your soul curled up inside this treasure chest that''s just sitting out on the grass. Anyone could open it up and look inside, and this worries you quite a bit!', 'You worry about what you''ll find if you look directly at your soul. But you also worry about what will happen if someone else looks. So you''re stuck in a constant state of worrying: nah or yah?!', 'Unfortunately, you also <kanji>worry</kanji> about opening it yourself. You stand in front of the chest and think, "Should I open it? <reading>Nah, yah</reading> (なや). Wait. Nah. Yah. Dang, you can''t make up your mind.', 'N2', NULL),
(1322, 'Imagine yourself in your spare time, sitting on this stool in a cage in the sun. Feel the warmth of the sun on your body and feel the metal bars of the cage holding you in. You try to forget about your situation by combing your hair with the comb. The comb radical is a little modified, so be sure to remember that you made this comb yourself and it’s a little wonky.', 'Picture yourself in this cage. You’re going to be there for life because you’d stolen too many cars. Feel the tears run down your face as you continue to comb your hair. You’re going to die in this cage.', 'So how did you get in this cage? Well, before you used to comb your hair in your <kanji>spare time</kanji>, you used to steal <reading>ca</reading>rs (か). You’d stolen many a car in your day, but you finally got caught in the act of stealing a car, and now you’re spending all of your spare time in that cage, just combing your hair.', 'N1', NULL),
(1323, 'Imagine this drunkard bipping and bopping to the sound of the music as he weaves this thread through his clothes to repair them.', 'Imagine this drunkard, and his chic but shabby appearance. Let''s hope he can repair that chic outfit and go back to being his usual chic self.', 'This drunkard is really <reading>chic</reading> (しき), but he''s just fallen on hard times and has been hitting the bottle pretty hard lately. The piece of clothing he''s using the thread to weave back together is one of his fancy chic outfits.', 'N1', NULL),
(1324, '"Shellfish used to be money! People used to lend them to each other all the time!" you say to your friend. "Hmm... okay, I guess so," they say skeptically.', 'Imagine yourself in this new car that you got with the money your friend lent you. Hear the roar of the engine and feel the wind in your hair.', 'You want your friend to <kanji>lend</kanji> you some money so you can buy yourself a <reading>ca</reading>r (か). You need money and a car a lot more than you need this pile of shellfish, so you’re happy to put them up as collateral for the loan.', 'N4', NULL),
(1325, 'Usually there aren''t paved areas inside areas of rice. You must be really lost if you ended up here with your scooter.', 'How you got so lost that you ended up on an old ship from 1620 is beyond my understanding. But you did it.', 'You got so <kanji>lost</kanji> and went so <kanji>astray</kanji> you ended up on the <reading>May</reading>flower  (めい), a ship that eventually took you across the Atlantic Ocean. Now you''ve really, really gone astray.', 'N3', NULL),
(1326, 'Picture this misguided drunkard. His whole deal is messed up.', 'Did I do it? Did I figure out the kid lingo? Am I cool? Or am I just another wack, misguided adult?', 'Nowadays we don''t say <kanji>misguided</kanji>, we say <reading>wack</reading> (わく). Like, damn that drunkard is so messed up, he''s wack.', 'N1', NULL),
(1327, 'Picture yourself saying out loud how you''ve left grain on the stairs, trying to lure people in. You''re surprised people are actually falling for this.', 'Imagine the youths just loitering in the stairs having eaten all the grain and taking up all the space making it impossible for anyone else to come in.', 'You''ve <kanji>invite</kanji>d people in by leaving grain on the stairs but some <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう) have ended up eating all the grain.', 'N1', NULL),
(1328, 'Imagine this dog going to the capital and getting a position. The poor dog looks pretty messed up though. The radical for dog in this kanji is a bit modified, so just remember that the dog in this story is all messed up.', 'Watch the broken dog settle in to his new shoes in his new home in the capital. The old dog can finally enjoy life!', 'In order to help the dog <kanji>get a position</kanji>, the capital cobbler made him a pair of nice <reading>shoe</reading>s (しゅう). The shoes help him walk on his hurt legs, but more importantly, they boost his confidence and make him feel ready for any position.', 'N1', NULL),
(1329, 'You better think long and hard about where you think they should visit before you say so. They''re relying on you.', 'Your home is just over that direction, and it''s your favorite place to visit, so why wouldn''t this stranger want to visit your home too?! So come and visit my home, random person!', 'You end up saying, "You should <kanji>visit</kanji> my <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう)." How kind of you!', 'N3', NULL),
(1330, 'This woman is so angry! Pretend you are the woman. Sit on your stool and feel your heart, painful in your chest, as you fume and think about how angry you are.', 'Imagine your dog (or somebody else''s dog) putting his furry, warm head in your lap. Feel how comforting it is. Are you still feeling angry?', 'As you''re sitting on that stool feeling <kanji>angry</kanji>, your <reading>do</reading>g (ど) comes up to console you. He nuzzles his head into your lap, and you start to feel the anger melt away.', 'N3', NULL),
(1331, 'Feel the air whoosh past your face as you shoot off the end of the long slide and ascend into the air. It''s starting to get hot and the sun is getting closer!', 'As you ascend, look back through tear-filled eyes and see the Shougun staring up at you. He nods slightly. You''ve pleased him.', 'You need to <kanji>ascend</kanji> into the sky and toward the sun because the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) said so. He''s testing out some space travel technology (the twenty story slide) and you''re his guinea pig.', 'N2', NULL),
(1332, 'You are one of these peoples, so imagine yourself closing your eyes and sleeping. Go ahead and do it right now. Close one eye, and then the other. Now imagine yourself sleeping.', 'Those other peoples were really mean to you. Imagine all the mean things they were saying to you. What did they say? Think about all these mean things as you close your eyes and drift off to sleep. Also keep in mind that this kanji has the same reading as 民, which is included in this kanji.', 'The reason you want to <kanji>sleep</kanji> is because all the other peoples were <reading>mean</reading> (みん) to you. They were so mean that the only way you could get over it was to close your eyes and sleep. You just want to sleep.', 'N3', NULL),
(1333, 'You''re so drowsy that your eyelids have drooped all the way down to the floor. Be careful not to step on them as you drowsily make your way to bed.', 'Feel yourself getting drowsier and drowsier as the swing gently rocks you to sleep. Hopefully it''s one of those swings with the leg holes so you don''t fall out as you''re drifting off.', 'The reason you''re so <kanji>drowsy</kanji> is because you''re on a swing (すい). A swing works like a baby cradle — it rocks you and makes you feel drowsy right away!', 'N1', NULL),
(1334, 'If you know all of the symptoms for each and every sickness you''d be able to cure them all!', 'You get all up close and personal with the Shougun. Hmm, his symptoms are watery eyes, stuffy nose, angry shouting (wait no, that''s not a symptom, is it?). Don''t mess up!', 'You need to figure out the <kanji>symptom</kanji>s the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) is having. This is really serious too, so you better be correct about which sickness he has. If something bad happens to the Shougun it will be off with your head.', 'N1', NULL),
(1335, 'Imagine pulling this thread and feeling your towel tighten around you. It''s getting so tight that you can barely stand properly, which is probably why the stand radical looks a little weird here.', 'You really don''t need to do this, you know — eating tater tots isn''t a sin. Tighten that towel just enough to be snug and comfy, and keep enjoying your taters. No one''s judging you.', 'You''re <kanji>tighten</kanji>ing your towel because you''ve been eating a lot of <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tots lately. You want to keep a slim figure, and rather than give up your beloved tater tots, you''ve just opted to constrict yourself with this towel, corset-style.', 'N1', NULL),
(1336, 'Look at how white the scooter is. It urges you to use it. It compels you. You feel coerced...', 'Really all marketing, advertising, and the like is just a hack of your brain.

Hint: If you see 白 in a kanji, there''s a good likelihood the reading will be はく.', 'When they <kanji>urge</kanji>, <kanji>compel</kanji> and <kanji>coerce</kanji> you like this, they are <reading>hack</reading>ing (はく) your brain.', 'N1', NULL),
(1337, 'You start with a sheet of leather. Now imagine yourself going through the process of changing them to shoes. Smell the leather and feel it, too.', 'Imagine some of these old coots busting into a modern shoe store and kicking over all the display stands and setting everything on fire because they hate non leather shoes so much. Hear the old coots screaming and babbling nonsense as they destroy the store.', 'Some <kanji>shoes</kanji> aren’t made from leather anymore these days, but you know who always wears shoes made of leather? Old <reading>coots</reading> (くつ). Old coots are so old that the only shoes they wore growing up were made from leather so they refuse to wear any shoes that aren’t made from leather. Crazy old coots.', 'N3', NULL),
(1338, 'Imagine your farming land absolutely covered with this dense layer of silt from the tsunami, but then seeing your crops sprouting up one after another, thicker than ever. It’s all very thick and dense, we think you get the point. ', 'You can always rely on Nostradamus’ predictions hitting the mark, even when it comes to thickly-layered-silt-bringing tsunamis. Good ol’ reliable Nostradamus.', 'Know who’s likely responsible for this <kanji>dense</kanji>, <kanji>thick</kanji> layer of silt? <reading>No</reading>stradamus (のう). He’s the one who urged you to buy this farmland in the first place, likely because he predicted the tsunami would bring this thick layer of silt.', 'N2', NULL),
(1339, 'Imagine yourself at the top of the mountain, holding the rake in your hands. Look over the edge. Feel the sinking feeling in your stomach as you realize how high up you really are.', 'Imagine yourself at the edge of this mountain getting your tan on. Feel the sun beating down on you and feel the fresh mountain breeze blowing over your body. It’s very relaxing.', 'You set up this stand at the top of the mountain so you could give a speech to the world. But you need to look your best, so you decide to get a <reading>tan</reading> (たん). You lie next to the <kanji>edge</kanji> of the mountain, and prepare to tan. Everyone likes a person with a healthy tan.', 'N1', NULL),
(1340, 'How are you going to approach this weirdly specific, extreme task? Dig a hole using the stool and then plant the trees with your mouth? Good luck figuring this one out! You''re in a weird underground prison for a reason, after all.', 'Goku barely manages to cloak you before the prison guards realize what''s going on. With this cloak, you''ll be planting trees with your mouth and stool like no one else before! Hooray!', 'This <kanji>extreme</kanji> challenge could only be solved by one person: <reading>Goku</reading> (ごく) from Dragon Ball Z! He has a special cloak that''ll allow you to plant trees in record time, so he quickly <reading>cloaks you</reading> (きょく) to help with your extreme task.

', 'N2', NULL),
(1341, 'Okay, so it might not be legal to take the sidewalk route depending on where you live, but physically, you can do whatever you want because scooters are small.', 'Psh, whatever. They aren''t your toes, and this is the fastest route! You''ll name it the toe route and do whatever you want.', 'You decide to take the fastest <kanji>route</kanji>, since you have so many options, and drive on the sidewalk. However, in doing so you run over every single <reading>toe</reading> (と) on every single person trying to walk there.', 'N3', NULL),
(1342, 'If you want to be healthy too, just do yoga and rub this healthy brush all over your bod.', 'Imagine Ken, the picture of samurai health, doing yoga and getting that brush all up in those bits.', 'Not everyone wants to be <kanji>healthy</kanji>, but <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai sure does. He needs to be ready at a moment''s notice so he can do tasks for other people. He''s at every class, rubbing himself down with the brush.', 'N1', NULL),
(1343, 'Imagine the sense of peace you''d feel in a place like this. The sound of the water, the shade of the canopy… Whoever was here must have felt at ease as they wrote or drew. But will they come back for their brush?', 'Finding you there, こういち invites you spend some time under the canopy with him, practicing kanji together with his brush. Why not? Seems peaceful.', 'It turns out this place of <kanji>ease</kanji> and <kanji>peace</kanji> was created by <reading>こう</reading>いち, who suddenly comes in looking for the brush he left.', 'N1', NULL),
(1344, 'Feel free to walk around the Good Building. There are no things or people, except for that one guy. Who is that guy.', 'You try to talk to the guy, poke him, and even yell at him. But, all he does is hand you some fish roe. When you take it, he offers you more fish roe. What''s up with this guy?', 'You talk to that <kanji>guy</kanji>. The only thing he does is offer you <reading>roe</reading> (ろう).', 'N1', NULL),
(2429, 'Have you ever been away from your village? Maybe you''re far away right now, studying Japanese alone. Can''t you feel your body longing for your soul back home?', 'You long for a cougar to leave you alone! You yearn for a cougar to bother someone else!', 'No matter how much you <kanji>long for</kanji> it, you can''t go back because <reading>a cougar</reading> (あこが) is in the way. Whenever you try to return to your village to be reunited with the soul you yearn for, a cougar is back, and won''t let you in.', 'N1', NULL),
(1346, 'Imagine riding your scooter to the barracks. It literally drops you on the ground when you get there, and then drives in reverse back the opposite way you came.', 'If you don’t know what Gak is, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kgST63f6x88&amp;ab_channel=NSIFloam" target="_blank">check out this Gak commercial</a>. The 90s were basically the heyday of non-Newtonian fluids masquerading as toys. Don''t you wish you could <em>reverse</em> time and play with Gak again?', 'After you <kanji>reverse</kanji> in the <kanji>opposite</kanji> direction, you find a bunch of <reading>Gak</reading> (ぎゃく) covering your scooter wheels. The soldiers must have booby-trapped their barracks with Gak. You''re able to reverse out of the barracks, but the Gak has gunked up your wheels so bad that they only go in reverse now. Weird… Guess you''ll be going in reverse forever.', 'N2', NULL),
(1347, NULL, 'See Little Kyoto after the giant rampaged through it. It looks even smaller and more pathetic than ever. Look at all the smoldering rubble and smell the smoke with your nose.', 'Just recently, a <kanji>giant</kanji> rampaged through Little <reading>Kyo</reading>to (きょ), so now it''s even smaller than before. Poor Little Kyoto.', 'N2', NULL),
(1348, 'The canopy just protects your car from the elements as it sits in storage for long periods of time.', 'Imagine a kid sneaking into your canopy to steal your car that''s in storage. Maybe it''s two 子s, one stacked on the other with a trench coat. One 子 works the pedals while the other 子 works the steering.', 'Your car is in this <kanji>storage</kanji> <kanji>warehouse</kanji>, just sitting there. A <reading>子</reading> (こ) notices this, and decides to take your car on a joyride. You won''t notice, right? You never drive it.', 'N2', NULL),
(1349, 'Imagine yourself getting new legs. Legs that belong to a child. They’re limber and young and full of energy! Feel your legs of a child underneath you!', 'Say back to Jesus, “I’m not your child Jesus! And you’re just jealous of my new legs! You wish you had the legs of a child, Jesus! Your legs are like super old!” Actually say this out loud, and remember how Jesus wishes he could be a child again too.', 'As you delight in your new legs, <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ) descends from the sky. “My <kanji>child</kanji>,” he says, “why have you pointed the stick at the sun and wished for the legs of a child? You were already perfect, just as all of God’s children are perfect.”', 'N2', NULL),
(1350, 'Making things cool and cold are pretty much the only orders you''ll hear from ice. Ice is very simple in that regard.', 'Imagine a cold ray coming out of your ray gun. Anything it touches becomes cold. "Stay cool, bird boy," you say.

Hint: When you see the radical <radical>orders</radical> (令) in a kanji, it''s fairly likely the reading will be れい.', 'How will you make things <kanji>cool</kanji> and <kanji>cold</kanji>? You''ll use a special <reading>ray</reading> (れい) gun that shoots a cold ray.', 'N3', NULL),
(1351, 'Imagine this super frozen ice from the east. You’ve never seen anything quite like it before. It’s so frozen, it’s like it was frozen once and then frozen again. Twice frozen. Feel the ice and how freezing cold it is. It’s super cold, guys.', 'Imagine being in とうきょう and seeing all this frozen ice. Hear people speaking Japanese all around you as you touch and feel the freezing twice-frozen ice.', 'Japan is a country in the east, and this ice was <kanji>frozen</kanji> in <reading>とう</reading>きょう. とうきょう is really good at freezing frozen things, so it makes sense that this frozen ice came from there.', 'N2', NULL),
(1352, 'Infants poop a lot. There''s poop everywhere. The powerful aroma overwhelms you. Eww.

Also refers to childhood, when you still have some of your poop powers, but they''re already starting to go away.', 'See this infant gobbling up all the yogurt he can get his grubby little hands on. Soon, he begins to poop all over the place. Hear the infant screaming, "Goo-goo! Yogurt poop power! I''m a baby! Wahhhh!!"', 'In <kanji>infancy</kanji>, one eats a lot of soft foods, such as <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう). Yogurt is the food item above all others that grants superior pooping power.', 'N2', NULL),
(1353, 'Imagine an immature turkey complaining at you until you give it grains. Now it''s taking selfies with the grains and posting pics to Instagram and hashtagging them #GRAINLIFE.', 'Imagine a bunch of immature kids and turkeys eating cheese together.', 'Another thing <kanji>immature</kanji> turkeys (and people) love is <reading>chee</reading>se (ち). Most of us non-immature people and birds still like cheese, but if you didn''t have an immature cheese phase as a kid or teen, you won''t be able to like cheese as a mature old person.', 'N1', NULL),
(1354, 'Look at this winter table. How are you ever going to deal with it? Just think of your favorite warm food and then imagine it getting cold. Deal with it, dog.', 'Feel the relief of getting rid of this table. No more having to deal with it anymore. The circus show can have it.', 'This winter table is something you have to <kanji>deal with</kanji>, sure, but what if you just gave it to a circus <reading>show</reading> (しょ)? They like weird stuff like this.', 'N3', NULL),
(1355, 'Picture yourself at this specialty exhibition. Feel the weight of the cross shaped badge on your chest as you continue gambling at the exhibition.', 'Hack the gambling machines. Imagine the sounds of the exhibition around you as you work to hack the machines. Look at the computer parts and cables as you work.', 'You’re not very good at <kanji>gambling</kanji>, but you are pretty good at cheating. You <reading>hack</reading> (はく) the specialty <kanji>exhibition</kanji> gambling machines to pay out lots of cash just for you. You hack quickly and quietly, so luckily no one notices that you’ve hacked the system.', 'N1', NULL),
(1356, 'Picture this pure blue tsunami. You can see every crystal clear drop of water in it.', 'SPLAASHH! The tsunami''s pure water splits and it absorbed into your saber. Now it''s pure too. And transparent!', 'Seeing this <kanji>pure</kanji> blue tsunami inspires you to pull out your glowing blue <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい)! As the pure water comes toward you, you take one, long slash!', 'N2', NULL),
(1357, 'Picture the tsunami purifying the land of life, and in your mind see the sword cutting clean through the thread of life.', 'The area is SO pure that the ground absorbs the ketsup and now it''s all pure... ketsup color.', 'After the tsunami pulls away, everything left is <kanji>pure</kanji> white. You hate this, so you run over and squirt <reading>ketsu</reading>p (けつ) all over the place to make it not so pure.', 'N1', NULL),
(1358, 'See this gold Wolverine holding his gold medal after getting out of the water. His dad doesn’t want to forget anything about this precious moment so he makes a record of it. Imagine him scribbling furiously in his notebook.', 'Imagine a pitiful Wolverine crying on the ground as each rock hits him. All he wanted was some recognition from his father. Poor guy. Imagine poor Wolverine like this as his dad just looks on and writes in his notebook.', 'Since Wolverine is actually a mutant, he is soon disqualified from his gold medal. His father makes a <kanji>record</kanji> of this also. One angry fan threw a <reading>rock</reading> (ろく) at Wolverine afterwards. Then another threw a rock. And another rock. Before you knew it, everyone was throwing rocks at Wolverine. Wolverine’s dad didn’t step in to help though, he just kept keeping records.', 'N2', NULL),
(1359, 'Our squad''s symbol is horns on a pig, and we put it on everything to show the whole world our squad owns this turf.', 'Picture our squad. It''s Koichi, Viet, the Crabigator and Tofugu. We''re all wearing matching horned pig ties and walking toward you in slow motion, our squad building towering behind us with the pig smirking down at you.', 'Our <kanji>squad</kanji> also wears special <reading>tie</reading>s (たい) with our horned pig on it. And we don''t care if you think that''s lame, because we''re A SQUAD. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1360, 'Imagine your leader holding this stick of hair in the winter, going around mastering all kinds of skills and repairing everything in sight. What a strange stick, giving even stranger abilities to its wielder.', 'Picture your leader again; stick in one hand, shoe in the other. He looks kind of threatening, like he''s about to slap you with the stick and then throw the shoe at you. But that''s what it takes for mastering certain skills. This is all for discipline’s sake.', 'What''s the key to <kanji>mastering</kanji> any skill? A stick and a <reading>shoe</reading> (しゅう)! ', 'N1', NULL),
(1361, 'Right! You needed to buy a ticket to see the gladiator! He doesn''t want to kill you, he wants to check to make sure you bought a ticket.', 'Ken takes a long time to examine your ticket. I don''t think he understands that he needs a ticket to fight the gladiator. Maybe you should give him yours.', 'The gladiator happily marks your <kanji>ticket</kanji> with his blade, when <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai pushes past you. He holds up his own sword, challenging the gladiator, who just shakes his head and points at your ticket again.', 'N2', NULL),
(1362, 'The lady of the house will tolerate nothing less than excellence, so get ready to sweep and re-sweep this floor till you meet your lady''s expectations.', '"You there — fool. Come sweep this floor again." 
"Yes, m''lady — your humble fool lives to serve, m''lady."', 'Instead of your name, the <kanji>lady</kanji> of the house just calls you "<reading>foo</reading>l" (ふ). You rarely meet her ladyship''s lofty standards, so from her perspective you''re just a silly nincompoop fool who can''t get anything right.', 'N3', NULL),
(1363, 'Remember how odd this one is?', 'You only give this odd key to people you trust, so they can unlock your odd secrets and see how odd you are deep down.', 'You''re <kanji>odd</kanji> too, just like this kanji. But you don''t want anyone to know, so you lock away your <kanji>strange</kanji> habits with a <reading>key</reading> (き).', 'N1', NULL),
(1364, 'Imagine saying this to a woman. Try and imagine her face as you tell her how peculiar she is.', 'Who''s peculiar? Me and you. Me, you — we are peculiar! Peculiar, peculiar, peculiar!', 'But when you think about it, we''re all a little peculiar. Me, you, <reading>myou</reading> (みょう) everybody!', 'N1', NULL),
(1365, 'You know what would make learning this kanji lovely? Studying your radicals.', 'Try to imagine feeling this way about your ray gun. It''s so lovely.', '"<kanji>Lovely</kanji>" is the name of my <reading>ray</reading> (れい) gun. It''s more lovely than any other thing.', 'N1', NULL),
(1366, 'What other delicate things and tiny changes would you notice on the mountain? A little bird alighting in a tree, or the snowflakes it shakes loose from a branch? That''s for you to decide, but imagine it vividly whatever it is.', 'Picture yourself looking at the bee, thinking how each hair on its body is so delicate and tiny. Maybe it has a delicate aroma to go with it. A slight smell of honey?', 'Ah! Of course! The <kanji>delicate</kanji> bumble <reading>be</reading>e (び). Slightly out of place in winter, but this tiny creature is your idea of delicate. It lands on your finger, and you look at every tiny fiber of its being.', 'N1', NULL),
(1367, 'You pick up the horns off the ground and put them on your head, then eat some fins off the plate. This is just one of many benefits to living here. You''ll never go hungry!', 'Okay, so using the 駅 is probably a better benefit than eating cold fins off a plate on the ground, but you take what you can get. They''re all free!', 'Another <kanji>benefit</kanji> is free <reading>駅</reading> (えき) usage! Whenever you come back home from places on the train, this 駅 is completely free.', 'N1', NULL),
(1368, 'Picture the grain in your head with a time-lapse of one evening after another happening in the background. Slowly, the grain shifts.', 'Picture the eagle flying down on the second evening, scattering the grains it doesn''t eat, shifting their location.', 'This <kanji>shift</kanji> is caused by the <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) that wants to eat the grains. Every other evening, the eagle swoops down, shifts the grains (eating a few) and then flies off.', 'N2', NULL),
(1369, 'Of course, you need to imagine pulling the Grain Mouth King''s finger, to some extent. You can''t just read what we write for you, without going to the imagination zone. Pull that finger real hard a couple times, and watch an extra large purge of grain pour forth.', 'Imagine hearing the rumors that the Grain Mouth King ran out of grain, so you go check the extent of his failure yourself. But tater tots taste pretty good too. Imagine eating one of his mouth tater tots and go ahead and comment about it to him.', 'The <kanji>extent</kanji> to which we were harvesting grain from his mouth was too great. The Grain Mouth King has run out of grain. He worries he will lose power if he has no grain to offer, so he switches to barfing out <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tots instead.', 'N3', NULL),
(1370, 'Picture this rice, the pulsing blue spirit of each grain is glowing so bright!', 'As you release the spirits in the rice, your saber begins to glow blue too. Their spirits are going inside your saber, making it stronger!', 'You want to see the <kanji>spirit</kanji> up close, so you take your <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい), throw the blue rice in the air, and slice it in half! The rice explodes and blue light fills everything!

Then it''s gone.', 'N3', NULL),
(1372, 'Imagine yourself lining up these horns in the ground. The spikes are sharp and you poke yourself with one. Ouch. Need to be more careful as you line these up.', 'Imagine the hay in that bull''s mouth as it chomps angrily, watching you line up horns in the ground. It probably wishes you were the hay.', 'As you <kanji>line up</kanji> these horns and stick them into the ground, you notice a bull munching on <reading>hay</reading> (へい) nearby. It''s staring at the horns you''re lining up. Somehow it feels like it''s munching that hay angrily. You gulp, looking at its horn as it grabs another mouthful of hay, and you feel pretty guilty.', 'N2', NULL),
(1373, 'The constitution makes it so that everything you hold dear is locked away, nice and safe on a piece of paper, and no one can change the rules or hurt you without your say so. ', 'Picture Ken standing in front of your constitution. But keep a close watch, cause wily ol'' Nic Cage is in the business of thievin'' on constitutions.', 'Your country''s <kanji>constitution</kanji> is being protected by none other than <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai, hero for hire.', 'N1', NULL),
(1374, 'Blood shows that all the people are related. Hair shows that we''re all humans. Tofu is food to all. So, as you can see, those three words describe the entire populace.', 'If you see people in your populace without shoes, it''s your job as a fellow member of society to help them get some!', 'The entire <kanji>populace</kanji> deserves <reading>shoe</reading>s (しゅう). It''s a basic thing all humans should have like flowing blood, hair (somewhere), and tofu to eat.', 'N1', NULL),
(1375, 'See these four people all under one makeshift umbrella. The giant hat is set atop the cross, which is what makes it kinda like an umbrella, and the four pairs of hands are all holding it upright.', 'Imagine the four people in the umbrella hat again, only this time imagine them skipping along singing "Mi casa es su casa." It''ll help you remember.', 'You know the Spanish phrase "Mi <reading>casa</reading> (かさ) es su casa"? Well, this is kind of like that, but only with a hat. And that hat is also an <kanji>umbrella</kanji>. Mi casa es yours too, because it''s an umbrella and also a hat. This makes sense, trust me.', 'N1', NULL),
(1376, 'See these things on the beach. It makes sense to see fins, as fins are usually in the ocean, so seeing them on the beach is logical. Pick up the stuff you find as you feel the beach sand between your toes.', 'Pick up the hammer in your hand. It’s pretty heavy. The tsunami must have been very strong to pick up such a heavy hammer.', 'Walking down the <kanji>beach</kanji> a bit more, you also find a <reading>hammer</reading> (はま). Gosh, looks like the tsunami hit a workshop or a hardware store or something. First the axe, now a hammer.', 'N1', NULL),
(1377, 'Picture the car, weapon out the window, preparing to attack you full force!', 'Gecki is attacking you now. He''s hitting you over and over with his tiny car. He''s yelling and smacking your ankles with his weapon. And all you can do is wait and let the attack happen, because you know you deserve it.', 'Right before the <kanji>attack</kanji> begins, you recognize the hand holding this weapon and driving this car. It''s <reading>Gecki</reading> (げき), your long-lost gecko who you may or may not have let fly off into a really fierce tsunami. Oops. I think he may be here attacking you out of revenge.', 'N1', NULL),
(1378, 'Imagine yourself walking through the winter construction site, taking out your aggression on anyone you see. You hate this winter construction!', '"Aggression" is going to depend on you, so you should imagine a scene where you take your aggression out on こういち, whatever that entails.

Hint: When you see the radical <radical>construction</radical> (工) in a kanji, it will often take the こう reading.', 'You''re taking your <kanji>aggression</kanji> out on everyone. Then, the sexy construction worker from WaniKani Level 1, <reading>こう</reading>いち, walks by without a shirt on. You take out all your aggression on him.', 'N1', NULL),
(1479, 'If something is a bit odd, it''s usually beautiful in its own way, just like this beautiful thread.', 'To remember that there''s a beautiful thread hidden away, you tie a not-as-beautiful thread to the end of the key.', 'You take this <kanji>beautiful</kanji> thread and lock it up with a <reading>key</reading> (き). You want to keep it all to yourself, you want its beauty to be for your eyes only!', 'N1', NULL),
(1866, 'Close your eyes and hear the bog''s tempting call.', 'So that''s what happened to him! If you''re a youngun, just do a little Google and you''ll be pretty pleased, I''m sure.', 'As you listen to the <kanji>bog</kanji>''s call you realize it looks just like the <reading>Numa</reading> (ぬま) Numa guy in your mind. You open your eyes and it''s just a bog. You close your eyes, and there he is. The Numa Numa guy.', 'N1', NULL),
(1379, 'You point to the gun. You point to the ground (that''s where the gun goes). You point to the plate (you want it). You have to oversee everything the servant does.', 'If anyone had to oversee a lot, it was Genghis Khan, who had one of the largest kingdoms in all of history.', 'Why must you <kanji>oversee</kanji> everything? Because you are Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん), and Genghis Khan oversees everything.', 'N1', NULL),
(1380, 'Imagine cutting a tree into a cup of liquid and focus on the pain in your throat when you try to actually use it. Instead you kick the tree and angrily count cups of liquid in your head, because they simply are NOT the same.', 'Think of a haiku about a cup of liquid and a counter for cups. Use that haiku to help remember this kanji.', 'Another way to remember the <kanji>counter for cups</kanji> using a <kanji>cup of liquid</kanji> would be by creating and memorizing a <reading>hai</reading>ku (はい) about them. Aren’t haikus useful?', 'N3', NULL),
(1381, 'Don''t worry, there aren''t bullets in the gun. It''s an air gun! So just imagine shooting the air into your nose every morning to make sure it''s nice and dry.', 'Now the next time you manage to get your nose nice and dry, think back to our founding father Genghis Khan and thank him for where you are today.', 'This technique for a nice <kanji>dry</kanji> nose was invented by Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん)! Well, not him, but someone who worked under him. You see, he had a bit of a damp nose problem.', 'N2', NULL),
(1382, 'This message is brought to you by our generous sponsor, the leader of the mountain turkeys. Long may he reign.', 'Turn your cyborg over and you''ll see a stamp under one of its feet. It says, "Sponsored by" with a little picture of a turkey on top a mountain.', 'This same <kanji>sponsor</kanji> is also how you made your <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい). You didn''t think you were able to get all those body parts yourself, did you? No no, the leader of the mountain turkeys sponsored you so you could do your experiments.', 'N1', NULL),
(1383, 'Imagine your leader''s face when he sees your bare foot, and just how emphatic he is as he urges you to put a sock on it. He''s really worried your foot''s going to get cold!', 'Feel this sock on your foot. It feels great. Wiggle your toes around inside the sock as you think about how glad you are that you did as your leader urged. Note that this kanji also has the same reading as 足, so keep that in mind too!', 'As <kanji>urge</kanji>d, you put a <reading>sock</reading> (そく) on your foot. Ahh, that feels so much better. What a great thing, this sock.', 'N1', NULL),
(1384, 'Picture a map of Europe in your mind. Now picture little words saying which district is lacking what. This one is lacking water. That one is lacking food.', 'Okay, so maybe this is an old view of Europe. But back in the old days the 王s there would take everything from their subjects, so every district would lack something. Good thing things aren''t that way now, huh?', '<kanji>Europe</kanji> is lacking so much because their <reading>王</reading> (おう) keeps taking everything.', 'N2', NULL),
(1385, 'Picture all the construction on the bay and the inlet getting destroyed by the tsunami. Look at all of it fall apart and crash to the ground as the tsunami knocks it down. Imagine yourself right near the action, feeling the water spray from the tsunami.', 'EH? A tsunami you say? Those construction areas are screwed, man. Again, imagine the tsunami demolishing the area. Then be all like, “Eh? A tsunami? Aw, man.”', 'Imagine the Japanese people standing on the <kanji>bay</kanji> and the <kanji>inlet</kanji> as the tsunami approaches. “<reading>Eh</reading> (え)? What is that, Akiko?” “Eh? Where? Oh gawd, it’s a tsunami!!”', 'N1', NULL),
(1386, 'Picture this blue, slimy request. You could have done that thing by yourself, but you requested it of someone else, and now it''s all over and everyone can see it.', 'Now they have the blue request gunk on them, and you can walk away as your already-blue sword absorbs the rest. Oh, and you don''t have to fulfill the request (bonus!).', 'The next time someone <kanji>request</kanji>s something from you, take out your <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい) and slice it in half!', 'N1', NULL),
(1387, 'Picture a male narwhal and a male turkey meeting together in private. Whispering. Giggling. Pointing at each other''s male bits, each admiring how manly and brave the other is.', 'It''s just nature. Youths like to learn and try new things. And if two male youth animals wanna get together in private that''s their business.', 'These <kanji>male</kanji> animals are obviously experimenting with things still. Ah, they must be <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう)!  To be a youth, again!', 'N1', NULL),
(1389, 'Imagine what this janky makeshift net would look like. It''s both comical and sad. Every day you set it up around the cross, but next morning it''s lying in a broken heap in the dirt. What keeps breaking it?!', 'Imagine the rage you''d feel at these unrepentant kayakers who keep breaking your stuff. Oh, and don''t forget to be angry at yourself — for putting the cross and net so close to a river that kayaks can''t even go by without breaking it.', 'You realize what keeps making your net <kanji>break</kanji> are <reading>kay</reading>aks (かい). The cross and net are <em>right</em> next to a river trail that kayakers do their kayaking in, and every time they go by their paddles smack into your net and break it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1390, 'Imagine the doctor diagnosing you with hair loss. He pulls out a hat and places it on your poor, thinning hair. ', 'Hold your shin as you feel the throbbing pain in it. Even though the pain is so extreme, you can’t help but still think about the diagnosis you got from the doctor.', 'You just had the doctor <kanji>diagnose</kanji> you with hair loss. How terrible! You stumble out of the room, dumbfounded. Not paying attention, you bang your <reading>shin</reading> (しん) on the table. Your shin really hurts. What an awful day.', 'N1', NULL),
(1391, 'Imagine kissing all the members of the cabinet. A lot of them are old men. Imagine kissing all of them at the gate. Eww.', 'Imagine yourself sneaking past the cabinet members, finding your cock, grabbing it, and getting out of there. You’ve recovered your rooster! The day is won.', 'You need to kiss each member of <kanji>the cabinet</kanji> to gain access to their building. And you need to get into the building to recover your <reading>cock</reading> (かく). Your prize winning cock has been captured by a member of the cabinet and you need to infiltrate their headquarters and recover your rooster!', 'N1', NULL),
(1392, 'Imagine seeing one of the leader''s colleagues walking around, watching you. You''d get back to work and stop goofing around, right?', 'Imagine goofing off in your row boat and then seeing one of the colleagues waiting on the shore for you. No! She''s gonna take your row boat away!', 'You have to be good around her <kanji>colleague</kanji>s, because if you aren''t your <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat permit will be taken away. Row boating is the only fun you ever get to have, besides learning kanji. So act as best you can around the leader''s colleagues so you don''t lose your row boat rights.', 'N1', NULL),
(1393, 'Picture a pile of grains accumulating in size while a person watches. No matter how large the pile gets, the person keeps blaming them for something!', 'Is that why people become farmers? Because they think... accumulating a lot of grains is sexy? People are weird.', 'Now the grains have <kanji>accumulate</kanji>d a ton, they''re overflowing in their grain sacks, and the farmer freezes and says, "Now those are some <reading>sexy</reading> (せき) grains."', 'N3', NULL),
(1394, 'Look up and see that eye, shining down and supervising you closely. Hear the man shouting as he directs you in one task after another. It makes you uncomfortable, honestly.', 'Tokugawa lingers before you in full shogun garb, then nods his head in approval, winks, and disappears.', 'A man who can <kanji>direct</kanji> others so well must be <reading>Toku</reading>gawa (とく) Ieyasu. Sure enough, when he finally comes down off his stool you see that he’s the ghost of Tokugawa Ieyasu!!! That explains why you could only make out the ghostly gleam of his supervising eye before.', 'N1', NULL),
(1395, 'Retainer and servant are basically the same thing, so remembering this one shouldn’t be too hard for you.', 'Imagine your servant banging his shins on everything in your house. Hand him a pair of jeans and tell him to put them on to soften the blows.', 'Your <kanji>servant</kanji>, also known as your <kanji>retainer</kanji>, bangs his <reading>shins</reading> (しん) all the time so you make him wear <reading>jean</reading>s (じん) to work now. He bangs his shins on all the furniture in the house, knocking over all your valuables. But at least when he’s wearing jeans it doesn’t leave bruises like it did when he wasn’t wearing pants.', 'N2', NULL),
(1396, 'Try to imagine how cool and confident Ri''-Pad is, they probably grow and wear their rice in a cool and trendy way, like a middle part or something.', 'Try to visualize the different reactions people have to the name Ri''-Pad. What reaction did you have? What face did you make when you saw it? ', 'An <kanji>abbreviation</kanji> like Ri''-Pad is sure to get a strong <reading>reac</reading>tion (りゃく) — some will find it a little pretentious, some will hate it, but most will probably think it''s really cool. ', 'N2', NULL),
(1397, 'You forget to put the lid back on the navigation station and all those tools and boat pieces go flying around the boat and into the water. Oops.', 'Picture this seafaring こういち angrily chasing you around the ruined navigation station, trying to hit you with a telescope.', 'You watch the <kanji>navigation</kanji> equipment roll away and then you feel a slap on the back of your head. Ouch! You turn around and there stands <reading>こう</reading>いち, who you took with you as the navigator of this expedition. And, you just lost all his stuff!', 'N2', NULL),
(1398, 'Imagine being under the same roof as an odd person who''s always trying to draw near to you. You don''t want anything to do with them, but they won''t back off.', 'You hold the key tightly in your hand and press your ear to the door. You can still hear the odd person drawing near. Hopefully this key keeps them away.', 'They continue to <kanji>draw near</kanji> until you can''t take it anymore. You run out from under the roof, slam the door closed, and lock it with a <reading>key</reading> (き).', 'N3', NULL),
(1399, '"Boards are much more useful than trees." – The Anti-Tree Association. Now picture this sign on a big machine that''s cutting up all the trees into big boards.', 'You couldn''t care less about getting banned from the Tree Lovers Association. You and your Anti-Tree Association still keep on turning every tree into boards, ban or no ban. Damn those trees!', 'The Tree Lovers Association has one word when they see you turning trees into <kanji>board</kanji>s: "<reading>Ban</reading>ned (ばん)," they shout at you. "Boards mean you''re banned!"', 'N2', NULL),
(1400, 'Imagine yourself caked in dirt as you make your way through this urban jungle — someone really needs to clean up these streets!', 'These two guys seem like they might be wise guys, so you hightail it out of there. You don''t want to be another victim to the streets.', 'As you roam the <kanji>streets</kanji>, brushing the dirt off yourself, a <reading>guy</reading> (がい) bumps into you: "Hey guy, watch where you''re going!" As he says this he bumps into another guy. "Whoa there, guy — you think you own these streets or somethin''?" "As a matter of fact, my guy, I run these streets, so you''d better check yourself."', 'N1', NULL),
(1401, 'It''s a religion building. They have a lot of different names, churches, mosques, temples, cathedrals, but none of those matter. All that matters is that you really drill whichever religion you want into the heads of the people who come under your roof.', 'Picture the pile of shoes left outside. If you see a bunch of shoes like this you can be sure there''s a religious place around here.', 'Your <kanji>religion</kanji> requires everyone to take off their <reading>shoe</reading>s (しゅう). Mostly because the shoes will help them resist the jackhammering, so you tell them that to join your religion they have to leave their shoes at the door.', 'N1', NULL),
(1402, 'You walk by this tense servant and pause for a moment. They raise their tense eyes up at you and you can see they''re shaking.', 'Imagine how tense you would be if you were about to try to murder the King! Your entire body would tense up, maybe to the point that you wouldn''t be able to actually go through with it.', 'You ask the servant why they''re so <kanji>tense</kanji>, and they say they''ve been assigned to assassinate the <reading>Kin</reading>g (きん) of England with this piece of thread.', 'N1', NULL),
(1403, 'Look at how good this woman is! She’s my daughter! She’s good! You wish your daughter was as good a woman as mine is!', 'Think about how your daughter is basically a moose who may do dumb things. How ashamed you feel. How very, very ashamed. Unlike how I feel about my wonderful, perfect, not a moose-like daughter.', 'Your <kanji>daughter</kanji>, on the other hand, is not a good woman. Your daughter looks like a <reading>moose who may</reading> (むすめ) not be able to find its way out of a wet paper bag. Your daughter looks like a moose who may lose its way without a map. Your daughter looks like a moose who may do lots of stupid things.', 'N3', NULL),
(1404, 'Picture a large banquet of food on a sunny day, covered by an outdoor roof, and a woman carefully setting it all up.', 'Honestly though, have you ever been to a banquet that ended well? Something always goes wrong when you have that many people under one roof.', 'Unfortunately, the <kanji>banquet</kanji> roof comes crashing down right before the guests arrive, and it <reading>end</reading>s (えん) before it can start.', 'N1', NULL),
(1405, 'Watch as the narwhal''s face contorts as you see its soul get slurped out by the towel it was using.', 'You don''t know what''s more scary. That the narwhal''s soul got sucked out, or that all of its kind is calling it a fool now that it''s dead.', 'As you watch this <kanji>scary</kanji> scene you can''t help but mutter, "<reading>Foo</reading>l," (ふ) under your breath. Then you hear "Fool, fool, fool, it''s too scary you fool!" and you realize all of the other narwhals are chanting.', 'N3', NULL),
(1406, 'Try and imagine this saw slicing into your heart. Are you filled with fear? You should be.', 'I fear you’ll never make it to きょうと. Think of きょうと and how you long for it as you slowly die from that saw wound to the heart.', 'Even more so than your <kanji>fear</kanji> of dying, you are filled with the fear that you’ll never get to vacation in <reading>きょう</reading>と. You always wanted to take a trip to きょうと, but if you die from this saw wound to the heart, you’ll never get the chance to go.', 'N3', NULL),
(1407, 'Take a small drop of water and append it to the tsunami. Feel the drop of water on your finger and visualize yourself flicking it at the tsunami as it races towards heaven.', 'Look up at this ten story tall tsunami. It looks big and powerful. Feel the water spray from the tsunami as it towers over you.', 'Just after you <kanji>append</kanji> the small drop of water to the tsunami, it grows <reading>ten</reading> (てん) stories high. Before it was only nine, but since you appended that drop, the tsunami is now a full ten stories tall.', 'N1', NULL),
(1408, 'See the fierce animal fiercely eating the child from the plate. It’s very gruesome. See blood and body parts flying everywhere as the animal devours the child.', 'That child really shouldn’t have forgotten to mow the lawn. Imagine you are the child, being eaten by the fierce animal and wishing that you would have just mowed the lawn like you said you would.', 'The reason this <kanji>fierce</kanji> animal is eating the child from the plate is because the child forgot to <reading>mow</reading> (もう) the animal’s lawn. The fierce animal had hired the child to mow his lawn today, and when the child forgot to do it, the fierce animal hunted the child down and ate him.', 'N1', NULL),
(1409, 'The yakuza member is so upset his knife boils. Right after, they will definitely get violent and intense.', 'In the case of our mnemonic, it''s a yakuza member getting violent. So, imagine a yakuza member saying "Ret''s get violent!" before doing just that.', 'When someone gets <kanji>violent</kanji> or <kanji>intense</kanji>, they always yell "<reading>Let''s</reading> (れつ) get violent and intense!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1410, 'It hurts to have this cross stuck in your forehead, but try to ignore that as you search for where the thread leads. Imagine gathering the thread in your hands as you move along.', 'Why are there more crosses and threads in the sack? Are you supposed to take them from the sack and throw them at other people’s foreheads? Maybe. Imagine yourself reaching into the sack and throwing the crosses at other people.', 'Your <kanji>search</kanji> is over. You found the end of the thread. The thread leads you to a <reading>sack</reading> (さく). A sack filled with more crosses with more threads attached to them. What does it all mean!?!', 'N1', NULL),
(1411, 'Better safe than sorry. Don''t just say good luck once, make sure they''re stuffed!', 'Picture a person with the actual words "good luck" stuffed into their body.', 'You <kanji>stuffed</kanji> them by saying good luck <reading>two</reading> (つ) whole times!', 'N2', NULL),
(1723, 'You can’t recommend this turkey. It’s probably dangerous. Imagine sending this turkey back in a restaurant, very upset with everything. Also keep in mind that the arrow is half buried in the turkey and looks a little broken since the arrow radical is a bit off.', 'Welp, I guess if Genghis Khan recommends eating food with weapons still in them, you''re okay with recommending it too. Eat up, everyone.', 'You <kanji>recommend</kanji> the Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) special instead of this arrow-tainted turkey! When it comes out, your heart sinks. Genghis Khan apparently ate a cow with an arrow broken off in it.', 'N1', NULL),
(2145, 'When''s the last time you saw something <em>bright</em> and mysterious? Never, I bet. ', 'Who''s the mysterious man who''s always reciting poetry? Genji! 
Who''s the mysterious man who gets all the ladies? Genji! 
Who''s the mysterious man looking all dark and depressed because someone turned him down? G... Genji…', 'There is no one more <kanji>mysterious</kanji> than <reading>Gen</reading>ji (げん). He''s tall, <kanji>dark</kanji>, handsome, and most of all... 〜mysterious〜!', 'N1', NULL),
(1413, 'See this demon flying around in his jet. Do you feel allured? You should. Hear the roar of the jet engine as the demon cackles.', 'The demon won’t be so alluring once you see him scarfing down humans, tearing the meat from their bones. How gross.', 'This <kanji>alluring</kanji> demon on a jet wants one thing and one thing only. <reading>Me</reading>at (み). Human meat. This demon needs human meat to survive so he is taking his jet into the city to feast on some meat.', 'N1', NULL),
(1414, 'Imagine the feeling of drinking spoon after spoon of refreshing tsunami water, how it replenishes your dried up body. You''ve been in this sun prison for so long, holding onto this spoon and waiting for this moment. Better take advantage of it — even a tsunami will dry up real quick on the sun.', 'Imagine quenching your thirst in a real car wash. Open your mouth and let the soapy water dump on over and in you. You''ll never be thirsty again!', 'This method of quenching your <kanji>thirst</kanji> reminds you of being in a <reading>ca</reading>r <reading>wa</reading>sh (かわ). The water pours over you in waves, and you''re stuck inside a metal cage. Totally the same thing as a car wash, right?', 'N1', NULL),
(1415, 'Basically, think of 系 as linking things that belong together in some kind of group. Lineages, solar systems, genres, styles… Anything along those <em>lines</em>.', 'You come from a lineage of cake makers and eaters. No wonder you love cake so much! (And if you hate cake, no wonder your family doesn''t like you.)', 'You trace your <kanji>lineage</kanji> back through your whole family <kanji>system</kanji> and realize every single relative loved <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい). Most of them were cake bakers too!', 'N1', NULL),
(1416, 'Marriage is a big deal and it''s going to connect your two clans, so you have to do the ceremony in the sun in front of everyone so they can see.', 'Do you want to go through with this marriage? You better hope you see a condor then! It''s the official bird of love and unification, ya know. That''s why so many people wait until they''re older to get married these days, because there aren''t as many condors thanks to deforestation.', 'For the <kanji>marriage</kanji> to be official, you need to wait for a <reading>con</reading>dor (こん) to appear. If it doesn''t show up before the sun goes down again, the marriage was a failure. You have to try again tomorrow with a different woman.', 'N3', NULL),
(1417, 'Imagine a child riding a scooter with a gun having a good time. "Come play with me!" they shout. "No!" you yell back.', 'Imagine trying to explain to the youth that this is not play and that they should stop, but them youths can''t stop playing.

P.S. From the WaniKani staff to you: Don''t let youths play with guns, please.', 'Everything is <kanji>play</kanji> for <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう), even if it''s a terrible, scary, not good situation like this one.', 'N3', NULL),
(1418, 'Look at your flag and hear it flapping in the wind. Also hear your ears ringing a bit from when you shot that crab. Look at all the bits of exploded crab strewn around the area. Yowza.', 'Picture yourself locking this flag up with your key. Imagine turning the key in the lock and hearing the click when it is secured.', 'To make sure that no one removes your <kanji>flag</kanji>, you lock it to one of the lifeguard posts and you throw away the <reading>key</reading> (き). Since no one will have the key to unlock the flag, no one will be able to remove it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1419, 'We don''t want the earth to actually boil, but we do want some of that boiling hot sun light to illuminate all of the darkness of the universe and let us see.', 'Picture the Shougun calling out to the sun, asking it to illuminate our planet with its boiling hot rays. You better thank the Shougun the next time you see him for everything he does for you.', 'The person who calls the sun asking it to <kanji>illuminate</kanji> the earth is the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). It''s very specifically the Shougun''s responsibility to make sure the earth is illuminated every morning.', 'N2', NULL),
(1420, 'Imagine that feeling you get when everything is just SO pleasant. There is a pressure from your soul''s hook digging in. It''s a good feeling though, a pleasant one! It isn''t too hard or too soft.', 'Go look up pictures of kayaks to get some of that pleasant high. Mm, your soul is hooking you real good.', 'If you want to feel this <kanji>pleasant</kanji> feeling just look at a <reading>kay</reading>ak (かい). Kayaks are the most pleasant boats in the world. Feel that pleasant feeling in your chest just from reading about kayaks in this mnemonic right now.', 'N2', NULL),
(1421, 'One-sided paper is just anti-everything. It''s bad for the environment. It''s bad for easy reading. Put all those one-sided papers together into one, big edition instead.', 'Please read my super special Han Solo edition of the Star Wars story. I ripped out all the one-sided pages that had him in it and put them into a single edition together so it''s just the good stuff now.', 'The first <kanji>edition</kanji> you create is a fan fiction all about the adventures of <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo. You hated how short and one-sided his story in Star Wars seemed to be, and trying to keep track of it all was really hard. But not anymore!', 'N2', NULL)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;


Hint: When you see the "lifeguard" radical, the reading is probably going to be こう.', 'This time you <kanji>purchase</kanji> some <reading>こう</reading>いち goods. You know, since you''re such a fan. How else can you show love in this world, if not through purchases? The lifeguard hands over a little haul of こういち plushies and figurines. Nice.', 'N1', NULL),
(1437, 'Run fast! Go beyond the limits imposed upon you by nature! Imagine yourself running faster than any human has ever run ever. Feel yourself cutting through the air, wind breaking across you as you break the sound barrier. Boom!', 'Etch these runes into your legs. Go ahead and run a finger over your legs and pretend to etch runes into them. These runes you etch will allow you to run fast forever.', 'To solidify your ability to <kanji>go beyond</kanji> the abilities of other humans, you <reading>etch</reading> (えつ) sacred runes into your legs to preserve your running abilities. Etching these runes makes sure that your skills will never deteriorate.', 'N3', NULL),
(1438, 'Just picture it: an entire home decorated top to bottom with guns covered in towels, so only the outline shows. As your guests try to eat you say, "Guess what gun this is?! Haha, you got it wrong, pull the towel back."', 'Maybe you should be more conscious of other people when you decorate. Or at least, don''t shock people into fainting or yelling or leaving. Guns do that, you know?', 'If you actually <kanji>decorate</kanji> your house like this it will give people quite a <reading>shock</reading> (しょく). In fact, they might be so shocked they run out of there screaming.', 'N1', NULL),
(1439, 'Imagine the boisterous sounds coming from this small room. Hear the horse neigh and stomp his hooves, hear the insect buzzing loudly, and hear the wooden stool rattle around as the horse and insect try to escape the room.', 'The horse gets even more frightened now that it killed the insect and saw an actual soul float by its head and it grows even more boisterous. ', 'At the height of their <kanji>boisterous</kanji> sounds, there''s a sudden, awful CRONCH(!) and you see the <reading>sou</reading>l (そう) of the insect slowly rising out of the room, through the ceiling.', 'N1', NULL),
(1440, 'Imagine flying north to the moon. Feel the force of being rocketed through space on your journey. Now feel your spine telescope and your back break. It really hurts.', 'Say this haiku out loud. Memorize it and use this haiku to remember this kanji.', 'Your <kanji>back</kanji> hurts so much that the only way to properly explain the pain is in <reading>hai</reading>ku (はい) form. 

I flew to the moon.
It is there I broke my back.
My back hurts real bad.', 'N3', NULL),
(1441, 'Which fingers do you point at the sun, and which do you put in your ear? Act it out, that''ll help you remember. Now try to relax on the stool and smile for the camera. This is a professional photograph, so you better do what you''re told!', 'Go ahead and say Satsuki out loud, all happy-like. Makes a pretty big smile, right? From now on you should always say Satsuki when you''re being photographed.', 'As you''re getting the <kanji>photograph</kanji> taken, the photographer tells you to say <reading>Satsu</reading>ki (さつ). He says it''s like saying cheese but more effective. Satsuki!', 'N1', NULL),
(1442, 'Wait for this next plate. Here it is—now steal it! Imagine stealing this plate and making off with it.', 'Be careful not to drop that plate! Wouldn’t want to steal something just to break it. Imagine running through the streets of とうきょう. See all the sights and hear all the sounds. Just don’t get too distracted, you gotta get to a safe place and stash your stolen plate away.', 'You <kanji>steal</kanji> this plate from a <reading>とう</reading>きょう restaurant, so after you steal it, picture yourself running through the streets of とうきょう as you lose your pursuers.', 'N3', NULL),
(1096, 'Picture this woman with direction, knocking down obstruction after obstruction on her path to success. ', 'Now imagine that she actually is a bowling ball. Watch her roll down the bowling lane and smash all those stupid pins!', 'This woman is truly magnificent. Some compare her to a <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう) ball, because of how she knocks down the obstructions in her path like bowling pins.', 'N1', NULL),
(1443, 'Imagine the rocket turkey taking off. Just like a regular rocket, as it goes up, used pieces of the turkey rocket detach and fall back to the ground.', 'The reeds catch on fire - probably best not to fire off turkey rockets so close to these fields of reeds. Imagine the launch site completely surrounded by reeds.', 'When the rocket turkey parts <kanji>detach</kanji>, they fall to the ground onto a bed of <reading>ree</reading>ds (り).', 'N1', NULL),
(1444, 'Actually... try to forget this one. Don''t think about a bully dissolving things in acid. Think happy thoughts. Like... the bully getting dissolved instead and all the little insects coming back to life and being happy.', 'When you first read this and you pictured the bully dissolving the insect, you pictured them as a youth, right? See? It makes complete sense.', 'The only bullies heartless enough to <kanji>dissolve</kanji> insects like this are <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう). Do you really think an adult would melt a harmless critter? No. Only inexperienced youths could be so cruel.', 'N1', NULL),
(1445, 'Picture yourself sitting on a rocking chair in the library knitting away. It''s quiet and peaceful and the light is warming you just right. Ahh, bliss!', 'Imagine the granny or grampy you are (and must be to have hens and want to knit in peace in a library). You''re tired of these hens eating your thread while you try to knit sweaters for all your grandbabies so you come to the library.', 'You need to come to the library to <kanji>knit</kanji> because if you try to do it at home all your <reading>hen</reading>s (へん) will try to eat the thread. Hens love eating things that look like worms, like hay, string, and thread. So it''s important to get far away from them before you start to knit.', 'N2', NULL),
(1446, 'Picture these showy flowers growing straight up and out of the middle of the rice paddy, into the shape of a giant cross.', 'Imagine how the car feels as you ram into the showy flowers. The petals explode and rain everywhere. You turn on your windshield wipers to push them away.', 'These <kanji>flower</kanji>s are too <kanji>showy</kanji>. You hate them. You hate these showy flowers so much you run them over with your <reading>ca</reading>r (か)!', 'N1', NULL),
(1447, 'Look at the root where your fang previously was. Previously, when you were young, you had a lot of nice fangs but they''re all rotting as you get older and you pull them out.', 'Previously you didn''t have a key, but now you do to keep your old fangs safe.', 'To remember the beautiful fangs you <kanji>previously</kanji> had, you keep all of the ones you pull out in a box you lock with a <reading>key</reading> (き).', 'N1', NULL),
(1448, 'Gaze out in a line from the sun as you observe the world. Everything is very normal looking and comforting.', 'Imagine this creepy looking fool as he approaches. He’s kind of freaking you out. Feel very uncomfortable as you continue looking at this fool.', 'Soon you see something that doesn’t look so <kanji>normal</kanji>. It’s a <reading>foo</reading>l (ふ), a jester. Dressed all in fool’s garb, this fool is dancing up towards you with a ridiculously unsettling grin on his face.', 'N2', NULL),
(1449, 'Mix those pig mouths and foreheads in your pot. Think of how exceptional your soup looks.', '"Can I have some of your exceptional soup?" Grandpa ごういち asks. "It smells so swell."', '<kanji>Exceptional</kanji> pig mouth forehead soup is a favorite of Grandpa <reading>ごう</reading>いち. You see him sniffing around your window, then he knocks and waves.', 'N1', NULL),
(1450, 'Imagine this golden man standing on a raised platform, looking down at you judgmentally with his arms crossed. You mutter curses at him under your breath, but really you''re just jealous you''re not a model human like him.', 'Hold on a sec — didn''t Genghis Khan kill a bunch of people and father like a thousand kids? He doesn''t seem like such a model person to you, but, hey, you don''t make the rules around here — Genghis Khan does.', 'As you look closer at the golden <kanji>model</kanji> human, you realize he''s actually Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん)! No wonder he''s covered in gold and overseeing everyone — he''s the greatest conqueror in history!', 'N1', NULL),
(1451, 'Say you''re looking at a map of the city, with all the buildings laid out on it. There are too many, so you start marking which ones to exclude from the building plans. You also remove some buildings from the map all together.', 'She''s pointing out buildings to exclude / remove with great ferocity. "Remove this one. Exclude this one. Oh, and remove this one too."', 'Working with you on this project to <kanji>exclude</kanji> buildings from the plan, as well as <kanji>remove</kanji> buildings that already exist is little <reading>Jo</reading>-Anne (じょ).', 'N3', NULL),
(1452, 'Imagine Wolverine going around, measuring the construction site with his mouth. Look at him with his mouth wide open, wandering around the site deep in thought. What a weirdo.', 'Imagine Wolverine handing you some new jeans at the construction site. Um, they''re kind of all ripped up? Turns out that''s just the style he likes. You''ll be so fashionable now! Just like good old ''90s Wolvie.', 'Another thing Wolverine has to <kanji>inquire</kanji> about is <reading>jean</reading>s (じん). He wants to make sure everyone on the construction site has good-fitting jeans to work in, so he inquires with them about their jean measurements.', 'N1', NULL),
(1453, 'You’ve already pooped twice on this drunkard, how many more times can you do it? How much do you think this person can take? Just imagine yourself doing this.', 'Imagine yourself as you eke out that last poop. Really concentrate on eking it out so you can get away in time. Flex your butt muscles for added effect.', 'As you continue thinking about <kanji>how much</kanji> poop this drunkard can take and <kanji>how many</kanji> more poops you can drop on him, he begins to wake up. Quickly, you <reading>eke</reading> (いく) out one last poop before you scurry away. You eke that poop out just in time to make your escape.', 'N3', NULL),
(1454, 'You walk through this corridor every day, and the canopy guy always accosts you with canopy sales. You wish there was another corridor you could take, but it''s the only way to get where you want to go.', 'The roe salesman in the corridor is worse than the canopy salesman. They''re both insistent, but the roe salesman smells bad.', 'You take the <kanji>corridor</kanji>, but this time the canopy guy is gone. In his place is a new salesman. This one is trying to sell you <reading>roe</reading> (ろう).', 'N1', NULL),
(1455, 'Keep your fingers tightly wrapped around the broom — if you drop it mid-sweep, all that dirt and dust will go flying everywhere and you''ll have to start over from scratch.', 'Feel your soul getting more and more purified as you sweep every inch of your house. Your soul''s getting squeaky-clean, and it honestly feels amazing.', '<kanji>Sweep</kanji>ing is good for the <reading>sou</reading>l (そう). Every sweep of the broom makes your soul a little more pure. Cleanliness is next to godliness, after all.', 'N2', NULL),
(1456, 'Imagine using a spoon to dig your flag outta all this mud. So much mud! Feel its cold, soiled wetness on your hands as you dig away.', 'Imagine them working together — Dora and Doro, the Muddy Explorers! Who knows why she''s able to stay clean, while Doro gets so muddy…', 'You may know of Dora the Explorer. But have you heard about her dirty, <kanji>mud</kanji>dy brother, <reading>Doro</reading> (どろ)? They''re like a team. And because of all the exploring he does, Doro is always covered in mud.', 'N2', NULL),
(1457, 'Use this pole to hang your towels or curtains. Ooh, use it to pole vault!', 'Imagine all the irritated glares you get as you use your pole to cheat your way through several games of bowling. That''s against the rules, buddy!', 'After much deliberation, you decide to use this <kanji>pole</kanji> to go <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう). It''s a pretty long pole, so you can just stand at the top of the lane and directly poke the bowling pins with it.', 'N2', NULL),
(1458, 'Imagine trying to write a poem while riding a horse. It’d be really hard to do. I bet you’d be surprised if you managed to do it as well.', 'Imagine riding through きょうと on a horse. As if that wasn’t difficult enough for poem writing, now you have all the sights and sounds of きょうと to distract you as well.', 'You might also be <kanji>surprised</kanji> to learn that you are to ride this horse all across <reading>きょう</reading>と. That gives you a time limit. You have until you traverse きょうと to write this poem about winter.', 'N1', NULL),
(1459, 'Let out a long sigh as you recall memories from China and all the Chinese things you saw and did. Oh, sigh.', 'Sigh again as you remember how nice it was to tan everywhere. Picture yourself wandering around some Chinese streets with your shirt off as you soak in the rays and get a tan.', 'Your favorite Chinese thing to do is <reading>tan</reading> (たん). You can wander around without your shirt on and tan all day. No one will <kanji>sigh</kanji> or scoff at you, it’s perfectly acceptable. The Chinese just love to get their tan on.', 'N1', NULL),
(1460, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Imagine if you kept a bunch of souls in a warehouse. This way they can''t haunt anyone. And they can''t get lost out in the spirit world. The warehouse is best for them.', 'You have a <kanji>warehouse</kanji> to store all of your <reading>sou</reading>ls (そう). When people or animals you know pass away, you take their souls and store them in your dry warehouse for safekeeping. It''s a public service, really.', 'N1', NULL),
(1461, 'Look at this family tree. See the child, and then the grandchild. Trace the lineage with your finger as you observe.', 'Just remember that a son is all you really want. You need a son to carry on the family name and the lineage. So make sure that your child and your grandchild are both sons.', 'The <reading>son</reading> (そん) of a son is a <kanji>grandchild</kanji>. In this case, a grandson. Easy.', 'N2', NULL),
(1462, 'Picture a cute little nest with a family of birds in it. It looks like it was woven together with bits of grass and strawberries, so it smells delicious too.', 'Picture the nest, made of grass, fruit, and the suit of the angry salaryman who''s shouting below the tree, and missing the top half of his suit.', 'The bottom of the <kanji>nest</kanji> also seems to be padded with someone''s <reading>su</reading>it (す). Did... did this bird attack a human for this?!', 'N1', NULL),
(1463, 'The belt is easy to tighten and it catches all the sweat before it can fall into your eyes. It isn''t quite as absorbent as a towel though.', 'It works! Your belt tied in a tie is the perfect invention!', 'The <kanji>belt</kanji> isn''t absorbent enough, so you wrap it in your <reading>tie</reading> (たい) and then tie the whole thing around your forehead again.', 'N2', NULL),
(1464, 'Imagine loitering around a tombstone. You''re leaning on it. You''re staring at nothing. Oh, and you''re also measuring its diameter.', 'These carefully measured tombstone-diameter cakes are your special way of saying thanks to the ghosts for letting you loiter around their graves.', 'You need to know the <kanji>diameter</kanji> of each tombstone so you can make <reading>ca</reading>kes (けい) for each one. But the cakes can''t be wider than the tombstones, or they''ll flop off onto the ground.', 'N1', NULL),
(1465, 'It''s so hot. You hate it. You really hope winter will get your request and come early to rescue you.', 'This cucumber is supposed to be cool and refreshing enough to rescue you from the heat. I guess it was too much to expect winter to come early just for you. At least cucumbers are yummy.', 'You get a note in the mail from winter! This is it, you''re going to be rescued! You open the note and it says, "I have <kanji>rescue</kanji>d you with this <reading>cu</reading>cumber (きゅう)." A cucumber falls out of the note.', 'N1', NULL),
(1466, 'Imagine playing blackjack on the moon where even the slightest move causes the cards to scatter about. Watch them float along. It’s very frustrating to play this way.', 'Imagine yourself next to Santa-san as he tries to prevent the cards from scattering. Look at the expression of concentration on Santa-san’s face as he tries to prevent the scatter.', 'You are playing blackjack with <reading>San</reading>ta-san (さん). Santa-san is doing his best to use his Christmas magic to prevent the <kanji>scatter</kanji> of cards, but it’s not working out so well. Good effort, Santa-san.', 'N3', NULL),
(1467, 'Imagine yourself grinding this rice into a powder. Hear the scrape of the rice on stone as you grind it down to the finest powder.', 'Okay, we got a little carried away there. Just keep in mind how fun this is. Make sure you say fun with a little bit of a Scottish accent. You know what they say, Scots have more fun!', 'Actually, grinding the rice down to a <kanji>powder</kanji> is super <reading>fun</reading> (ふん). It’s so fun you don’t want to stop! AH HAHAHAHA! RICE POWDER! SO FUN!', 'N2', NULL),
(1468, 'Feel the tofu running through your veins. Feel the tofu goodness spreading all over your body. It''s so intense you can even taste a little tofu in your mouth.', 'Say this out loud, it''ll help you remember. Really focus on the "myake" be sure to say it like you''re all bugged out from tofu overdose.', 'Eventually, your friends and family find you at the cliff, tripping on tofu. Each and every <kanji>vein</kanji> on your body is engorged, pulsating with the effects of tofu. Your friends try and take you home. "You cyan''t <reading>myake</reading> (みゃく) me!" you slur, suffering from the effects of the tofu. "None of you cyan myake me go home! You cyan’t myake me! Nyah!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1469, 'Now you have two vegetables and no flowers. Do vegetables even grow on trees?', 'Picture your cyborg nibbling on the vegetables you cleat-kicked out of this tree. You give all of the things you don''t like to your cyborg nowadays. Which is probably why there are so many weird things all over it...', 'You feed these <kanji>vegetable</kanji>s (that you didn''t even want) to your <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい). It only needs a little though, since its stomach is so thin, so you just make it hold the rest.', 'N2', NULL),
(1470, 'Imagine changing your shellfish from loose, wet form, to solid freight form. It''s actually a lot of hard work.', 'Picture all of this freight stacked on top of your car. As you drive the car, every turn you make feels slow and heavy from the freight weight.', 'To move this <kanji>freight</kanji>, you need a <reading>ca</reading>r (か). You strap all of the freight containers to the top of your car, stacked nice and high and precarious, so you can move it.', 'N2', NULL),
(1471, 'Why must it be placed upon the land? If it were put in water, it would dissolve too quickly. So, you put your womb building on the land, instead.', 'It''s made of biological materials, so of course it will reek.', 'The <kanji>land</kanji> around your womb building will, unfortunately, <reading>reek</reading> (りく) though.
', 'N2', NULL),
(1472, 'Watch this leader as the transformation takes place. Now he doesn’t resemble anything like the leader he used to be. He doesn’t look like the same person. Watch him morph in your head.', 'See all the odd-looking lions respectfully getting down on one knee? They look even odder now.', 'Now he <kanji>resemble</kanji>s an even greater leader, and all the lions get down on one <reading>knee</reading> (に) to pay their respects.

(Note that we recently changed this kanji''s main reading from ね to に, because ね is an irregular reading.)', 'N3', NULL),
(1473, 'Imagine seeing a pool that isn''t equal. It''s like a tiny kiddy pool with an inch of water. And it''s surrounded by mounds of dirt. No no no, this won''t do—add more water to make them equal.', 'Okay, so the existence of a king kind of makes things not, well, equal, for all people. But at least aesthetically everything will be!', 'You spend a few hours making sure the water-to-dirt ratio is perfectly <kanji>equal</kanji>, not for yourself, but because the <reading>Kin</reading>g (きん) of England told you to. You may agree that equality is important, but it''s the King''s responsibility to keep things that way. Which is why he ordered you, small peon, to do the equalizing work for him.', 'N2', NULL),
(1474, 'Why would someone put a grave in a greenhouse? Is this one of those "green burials" everyone''s talking about these days? Having the whole tomb complex seems a tad counterproductive, though…', 'You watch in horror as Bobo raises his arms and slowly rises from his grave. "At last, I have awoken from my slumber… Prepare yourselves, mortals — Bobo''s taking reservations for birthday parties again!"', 'You pry open the <kanji>grave</kanji> inside the inner chamber of the <kanji>tomb</kanji>, and are shocked to find the mummified body of <reading>Bo</reading>bo (ぼ) the Clown staring back at you! How terrifying!', 'N1', NULL),
(1475, 'Imagine looking up at the roof at this scarecrow. There''s a sign around its neck that says, "Rich people welcome." Ah, you''re safe. Time to go to bed!', 'You poor sad, no longer rich fool.', 'You wake up the next morning and all of your <kanji>rich</kanji>es have been stolen! You call the police and they call you a <reading>foo</reading>l (ふ)! Pardon me?! "You basically put an advertisement on your roof telling everyone you''re rich. Of course you got robbed, you fool."', 'N3', NULL),
(1476, 'People who keep the cross in their hearts are supposed to be full of virtue, always doing the right thing, always helping others. But you aren''t sure if you want to be virtuous, it''s a lot of responsibility. So you look down at this symbolic cross and loiter instead of making a decision.', 'I don''t know about you, but I''m pretty sure Tokugawa Ieyasu is telling you to choose a life of virtue.', 'As you try to decide if you want to live a life of <kanji>virtue</kanji> the ghost of <reading>Toku</reading>gawa (とく) Ieyasu appears before you. He points to your heart and the cross, then points to his own and you see a glowing cross in his own.', 'N1', NULL),
(1477, 'Imagine it''s like a quest from a book or game. What are you looking for? Maybe a powerful sword, or a lost princess. Picture it in your mind, this thing you''re looking for.', 'Feel the sun''s rays on your skin as you search and search and search. Watch as your skin gets darker, crispier, and — hmm — starts smelling like cooked meat. Now that''s a good tan.', 'You <kanji>look for</kanji> it for so long that you can''t help getting a <reading>tan</reading> (たん). I mean, you''re up some tree, shielding your eyes from the sun as you look for that thing, and it''s been hours, days? Of course you''re going to get a tan.', 'N3', NULL),
(1478, 'There’s a hidden camera on his toe and the leader wants to have plenty of secret spy footage to review later.', 'If it wasn''t for your toe spy network, these egregious tater tot crimes would have never reached the light of day.', 'You and the leader review the <kanji>spy</kanji> footage and find a single <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tot hidden within the shellfish. Together you uncover a huge smuggling ring. Clams, oysters, mussels, you name it! Shellfish of all kinds are using their bodies to sneak tater tots to the undersea world.', 'N1', NULL),
(496, 'Imagine a never-ending slide that goes deep down inside a building. You can''t see anything in the dark around you, you just plunge down and slide forever. And forever is a long time, my friend.', 'Cucumbers are tasty, but not when you''ve been eating them for a long time.', 'You slide for such a <kanji>long time</kanji> that you get hungry. Luckily, once in a while a <reading>cu</reading>cumber (きゅう) comes tumbling down the slide next to you.', 'N2', NULL),
(1480, 'Although not necessarily a book preface, you can imagine a book made out of canvas pages. The canvas pages that came beforehand (i.e., in the beginning, before the book started) is the preface.', 'Actually imagine reading through the preface of your favorite book. Except, there''s a preface that just goes into great detail about Jo-Anne and doesn''t seem related at all to the book.', 'You read the <kanji>preface</kanji>. It just goes on and on and on about <reading>Jo</reading>-Anne (じょ), a short lady the author loves very much.', 'N1', NULL),
(1481, '"Nya! Welcome to my ship, grab a scooter and scoot on up here, we''re about to start the welcome ceremonya!"', 'You''re really happy this cat pirate welcomes gay people aboard. Whether that''s because you''re gay or just because you''re a baseline decent human being is up to you.

Also if you aren''t the kind of person who would welcome gay people onto your cat pirate ship, you should just leave nyaow.', 'The cat pirate then launches into a speech about how they <kanji>welcome</kanji> all kinds of people. Especially <reading>gay</reading> (げい) people. He puts a certain emphasis on gay, so you figure that the cat pirate either thinks you''re gay or wants you to be.', 'N3', NULL),
(1482, 'If you''re facing down a samurai, it''s pretty important for you to be able to understand their intentions. Not all samurai are good, you know.', 'The sheep samurai only has violent intentions for other samurai and sheep. Its heart is dark and surprisingly easy to read.', 'You try to read the <kanji>intention</kanji>s of the samurai in front of you and they''re dark, evil, scary. Oh no! These are the intentions of the <reading>shee</reading>p (し) samurai!', 'N1', NULL),
(1483, 'Share your kindness with the world. Use your big mouth to let people know what you''re doing. "I''M TAKING OUT THE TRASH AND NOBODY EVEN ASKED ME!" Y''know, that sort of thing. Go ahead and say that out loud a few times.', 'Imagine chopping up these onions. Smell the onion smell in the air as your eyes begin to sting from the onions. Your friend had better appreciate this kindness.', 'To further show your <kanji>kindness</kanji>, you chop up some <reading>on</reading>ions (おん) for a friend who always cries when they chop onions. The onions make you cry too, so it''s a nice act of kindness that you''re toughing it out for your pal.', 'N1', NULL),
(1484, 'Picture your fingers really getting in there to gather the tree pieces from out of this cleat. Some of the tree bits are stabbing under your fingernails, but you have to keep gathering them, don''t stop now!', 'Gather all of these cyborg bits so you can put the cyborg back together later.', 'You <kanji>gather</kanji> them all together and realize there are <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい) parts mixed in with the tree bits. Uh oh, it''s as you feared. Your cyborg definitely got into trouble somewhere around here and you walked all over it with your cleats.', 'N2', NULL),
(1485, 'Imagine yourself sitting under the tree in the grass with the woman. Smell the cherries and see the sakura blossoms falling down all around you. How whimsical.', 'Sakura means cherry tree and sakura is the way to pronounce this kanji. Easy!', 'How do you say <kanji>cherry tree</kanji> in Japanese? <reading>Sakura</reading> (さくら). <kanji>Sakura</kanji> is Japanese for cherry tree. I’m sure you’ve probably heard this before, so it shouldn’t be too hard to remember.', 'N1', NULL),
(1486, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'It''s the miracle of eternal life!', 'How long is an <kanji>eternity</kanji>? Just look at this little <reading>a</reading>corn (えい) and ask. The acorn starts small but it grows into a huge tree and can live for all eternity by dropping more acorns to create more trees. Its acorn line will live on forever!', 'N2', NULL),
(1487, 'Picture the dark fluids from the tsunami flowing by your window at night. You look out and the liquid is shining in the moonlight. It looks like an entire sea is out there!', 'Picture the 駅 filled to the brim with fluids and liquids. Maybe if you swim, you''ll be able to catch your train.', 'This <kanji>fluid</kanji> stays in the <reading>駅</reading> (えき) longer than anywhere else. All of those tsunami liquids pour deep inside the 駅 late at night and the sun still can''t reach them to help evaporate them during the day. It''s a real *night*mare.', 'N2', NULL),
(1488, 'Go ahead and feel your eyeballs moving around in your head. Your eyes aren''t just the part on the surface, they''re the whole balls in there, attached at the root.', 'Look at Gandhi’s eyeball with your eyeball. It''s pretty remarkable.', 'You know who has some pretty good <kanji>eyeball</kanji>s? <reading>Gan</reading>dhi (がん). Gandhi used his eyes to see the oppression around him and decided to do something about it. But it''s all thanks to his eyeballs.', 'N1', NULL),
(1489, 'Imagine coming across your long dead top hatted ancestor in spirit form. What would you do? What would you say?', 'Your ancestor didn''t come here to see you, it just wanted to learn about soap. Something that they didn''t have back when it was alive. That''s actually kinda sad... and stinky. Your ancestor needs to use this soap, if anything.', 'You ask your <kanji>ancestor</kanji> what it wants and it points to the <reading>so</reading>ap (そ) by the sink. Soap? It nods at you. I guess your ancestor is here to see the wonders of soap.', 'N3', NULL),
(1490, 'Pretty soon your fingers, toes, and whole body are covered in threads, for all your innumerable achievements. Think back on those achievements, on all those times you took the blame for someone. You''re just so, so selfless. What a good person you are. Ahh, it feels good to be good.', 'Imagine setting this sexy thread-based fashion trend. You''re named the sexiest person alive and everyone dresses like spools of thread to copy you. But the achievements to match? Well, only you have those.', 'But your latest <kanji>achievement</kanji> is covering yourself in these threads and everyone thinking it''s <reading>sexy</reading> (せき). The newspapers and fashion mags can''t get enough of your sexy style. See? If you''re a good person, people will think your achievements are sexy.', 'N2', NULL),
(1491, 'Pick up these fins and the two combs. Feel their weight in each hand as you examine them with interest. Smell the fish smell and feel the teeth on the combs.', 'You''ve always had an interest in きょうと, so it makes sense that you''d be interested in things from きょうと too.', 'Since the combs <kanji>interest</kanji> you more than the fins, you examine the combs more closely. Taking interest in where they were made, you discover they were made in <reading>きょう</reading>と. Dang, きょうと sure does make some nice combs.', 'N1', NULL),
(1492, 'Things in Korea haven''t been on the safest side lately, so make sure you have some ways to defend yourself before you go.', 'Okay, so maybe this defense strategy will take a while, but you aren''t rich and acorns are on the ground for free. Don''t judge!', 'You need a good <kanji>defense</kanji> to go to Korea, so you fill your pockets with <reading>a</reading>corns (えい). When you get to Korea you plant them all around yourself. In no time you''ll have a strong tree defense, keeping you safe.', 'N1', NULL),
(1493, 'Always pick up a few duplicate outfits if you''re planning on venturing out to a black hole.', 'In response, you shout "F*** you too, bro! I need these duplicate outfits for my black hole mission!" For the next few minutes, the two of you just scream "f*** you" at each other multiple times, over and over.', 'As you''re shopping for <kanji>duplicate</kanji> outfits, some guy runs up and starts screaming "<reading>f***</reading> (ふく) you!" at you. "F*** you, man! You''re out here buying <kanji>multiple</kanji> outfits while kids in other countries are starving!"', 'N2', NULL),
(1494, 'Look at the turkeys in the tree. There are nine of them and they are all gobbling up a storm. Hear them gobble as you imagine what random, miscellaneous things they are holding.', 'Look at Zazu asking the turkeys random questions about miscellaneous trivia. You’ve seen the Lion King, right? Zazu should be pretty easy to visualize here.', 'As if things couldn’t get any more <kanji>random</kanji>, <reading>Zazu</reading> (ざつ) from the Lion King flies into the tree and starts asking the nine turkeys <kanji>miscellaneous</kanji> trivia. Zazu always did have a thirst for knowledge, but this is kind of strange.', 'N3', NULL),
(1495, 'Picture the husbands pulling a different side of the shellfish, it pops in two, and they each get a shell. They smile and high five.', 'Imagine Santa-san flying over the husbands in his sleigh, raining shellfish down upon them from the sky. It warms the heart, wouldn’t you agree?', 'Since the husbands were able to <kanji>agree</kanji>, <reading>San</reading>ta-san (さん) believes they’re both good husbands and belong on the good list. Therefore, Santa-san gives them each lots and lots of shellfish for Christmas.', 'N3', NULL),
(1496, 'Imagine crushing up some spiders into alcohol. Watch it turn green. It''s going to eat through the beaker! Quick, do something!', 'It''s Christmas morning. Instead of gifts, Santa-san just throws a piece of charcoal at your face. Feel the charcoal smash into your face and explode into acid, burning you. Santa-san runs away laughing. Ho ho ho!', 'You''re trying to find a container for this <kanji>acid</kanji> when <reading>San</reading>ta-san (さん) bursts through your wall. He frowns down at you. You''re on the naughty list now. You killed spiders AND made dangerous acid? Ooooh you''re in trouble.', 'N1', NULL),
(1497, 'Imagine yourself strenuously pushing a lever to flatten your gold and punch a coin out it. You sweat as you hurry to make enough coins so you can go to the bar before it closes.', 'Imagine handing this centaur bartender your gold coins. Hear him thank you in his booming voice and make you a mysterious gold-colored drink. Look in wonder at how the centaur has managed to fit in the narrow space behind the bar counter.', 'You use the <kanji>coin</kanji> to pay a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん) at the bar. The centaur is the bartender and he makes special drinks for those who pay in gold coins. Good for you!', 'N1', NULL),
(1498, 'Which part of the director did you choose to eat in order to domesticate him? Think about eating that part of the director and imagine its taste in your mouth as you chew. Eww.', 'You’re proud that you managed to domesticate the director, but now you feel kind of bad for having injured him. Okay, you really just feel bad because he’s probably going to sue you and he definitely has a lot of money. Oh, and he’s bleeding all over your car.', 'While you did succeed in your attempt to <kanji>domesticate</kanji> the director, he is now bleeding profusely from the bite wound. All over the script! You gotta get your <reading>ca</reading>r (か) and drive him to the hospital like right away. Get to the car! Hurry!', 'N1', NULL),
(1499, 'Picture how easy it would be for the leader of your country to wound someone if they had a gun. Scary, right?', 'Picture the Shougun you''ve slowly grown to know over the last thirty-ish levels. But he''s holding a gun wounding everyone around him. He''s screaming and shooting and wounding everyone in sight. Even his mom, even his dog, even his farmers.', 'Which leader wounded the Japanese people? The <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). The Shougun is a military leader. The Shougun even has gun in the name. And they wounded hundreds of thousands of samurai and soldiers.', 'N1', NULL),
(1500, 'Look at this big group that contains the Viking and his older brother. Hear them shouting as a group and imagine their Viking battle cry.', 'Imagine this group of Vikings rampaging through とうきょう. See all the Japanese people running away and screaming as the group of Vikings comes busting in, swinging their axes and shouting loudly.', 'This <kanji>party</kanji> has decided to raid <reading>とう</reading>きょう. It’s a large undertaking, but they have a large group of Vikings on the job. とうきょう doesn’t stand a chance.', 'N2', NULL),
(1501, 'Separate the two pools from one another. Now the pools look like the kanji. The kanji is one white pool and one yellow pool. Neat, huh?', 'Just think Tamagotchi whenever you see this kanji. Tamagotchi = tamago = egg. Easy!', 'You remember <reading>Tamago</reading>tchi (たまご), right? Well, Tamagotchi is an <kanji>egg</kanji>, and the toy looks like an egg, so it’s pretty easy to remember that tamago means egg.', 'N2', NULL),
(1502, 'Only really, really strict parents would make their kids do that in the middle of winter! It''s cold!', 'Oh Genji, if only you were as strict about using protection as you were on your elf child.', 'One of the <kanji>strict</kanji> parents of this elf kid is... <reading>Gen</reading>ji (げん)?! Looks like he had another illegitimate kid... this time with an elf.', 'N1', NULL),
(1503, 'Imagine sitting in your cottage. You throw away a banana peel. You throw away some used suntan lotion. You throw away all your cares in the world.', 'Just throw away all your suits when you want to relax. Don''t worry about needing one someday, throw it awaaaay!', 'The number one thing you <kanji>throw away</kanji> while in your cottage is your <reading>su</reading>it (す). Suits just don''t fit the cottage vibe, man.', 'N2', NULL),
(1504, 'Really focus on going "into" wherever this is. Into the place you go. Right into it.', 'How did your 子 get into here? Your 子 really gets into everywhere. They get into all kinds of places, and they get into all kinds of trouble!', 'Once you’ve gone <kanji>into</kanji> this place, what do you see? Your <reading>子</reading> (こ)!', 'N3', NULL),
(1505, 'Really feel the frustration of wanting to uncover all the secrecy around this mountain but being stuck on your stupid roof. ', 'You can imagine trying to break one of the mitts-wearing members of this group, but they can''t tell you anything because of their magical mitts.', 'This <kanji>secrecy</kanji> is so secret that the people who know about it wear magical <reading>mitts</reading> (みつ) of secrecy that make it so we can''t tell anyone, no matter what.', 'N1', NULL),
(1506, 'Imagine observing the two prisons being hit by a giant wave of muddy water, making them completely dirty. How disgusting!', 'The obi were the only nice thing the prisoners had left, and now they''re all dirty. Even as you leave the prisons, you can still hear their echoing cries of "Oh, not our obi!"', 'What''s the most <kanji>dirty</kanji> are the prisons'' <reading>o</reading>bi (お) collections. You can hear the prisoners crying, "Oh, not the obi. Our obi are all dirty, oh no!"', 'N2', NULL),
(1507, 'Think of how much you want to be in this valley. They have everything in there. You lack nothing. You want for nothing. Man, you wanna be in that valley so badly.', 'This was the yolk you wanted. Feel it slosh into your mouth from the sky. Taste the yolk on your tongue before swallowing it. Mmm, egg yolk.', 'Once you get to the valley, you <kanji>want</kanji> some protein. Egg <reading>yolk</reading> (よく) falls from the sky! It’s a miracle! You wanted protein, and egg yolk was presented. It falls right into your open mouth.', 'N3', NULL),
(1508, 'Feel how warm the sun is, as you try to throw and catch the football.', 'Dang, you knew you should have played night football so it wouldn''t get so warm. Now the dawn sun is making you uncomfortable under all those layers of football protection.', 'It''s <kanji>warm</kanji> like this because it''s <reading>dawn</reading> (だん). That''s when the sun comes up.', 'N1', NULL),
(1509, 'Chop this tree into pieces to transform this table into a desk. Hear the sounds of construction in your head as you make this desk with your bare hands.', 'Just imagine someone slurring “It’s cool, eh?” While you think about how cool this desk really is. Run your hands over the finished project as you say it.', 'When you finish the <kanji>desk</kanji> you turn to your friend and say, “<reading>It’s cool, eh</reading> (つくえ)?” Your friend looks at you and says, “S’coo’ eh? What?” “This desk, it’s cool. It’s cool, eh?” “Aw, yeah man. S’coo’ eh.”

Another way to remember this reading is to think about where you find lots of desks. That''s right, in a <reading>school</reading> (つくえ).', 'N2', NULL),
(1510, 'Imagine telling a piece of grain your deepest, darkest secret.', 'This works both ways too. If someone tells you a secret, that same heat fills you up. Now whether this heat is from embarrassment or the rush of getting to be part of a secret is up to you to figure out.', 'If someone finds out your <kanji>secret</kanji> (not from the grain though, it will certainly keep it) then your entire face will fill with <reading>hea</reading>t (ひ).', 'N1', NULL),
(1511, 'Imagine this one shrimp who is bad at translation. He’s trying to find a reason to live, but he’s having trouble. Look at him all alone, being shunned by the other shrimp.', 'The translation of the word yak was the reason this shrimp fell into despair and gave up the translation profession. Now everyone hates him. See this poor shrimp being harassed by others while they shout at him, “Yak! Yak! Yak!”', 'The <kanji>translation</kanji> this shrimp failed to make was for the word “<reading>yak</reading>” (やく). The shrimp found no <kanji>reason</kanji> to translate yak because there are no yak in the sea and then the shrimp just started questioning everything in his life and fell apart.', 'N1', NULL),
(1512, 'Imagine this happening in front of you. See the tsunami turn from the color of water to the color of a forest. The dye washes over you and now you’re forest green as well.', 'Hear each centaur shouting about how he hates that dye for changing his color. They look kind of funny, so you laugh at them a bit, but not too loud – they might hear you and get even more angry.', 'The <kanji>dye</kanji> continues to pass through the forest and hits a group of <reading>cen</reading>taurs (せん). Now each centaur is dyed with the forest green dye. They don’t look happy.', 'N1', NULL),
(1513, 'Bamboo, interval, bamboo, interval, bamboo… Almost like a binary rhythm: 1, 0, 1, 0, 1. Now that''s simplicity. It doesn''t get simpler than that!', 'Simplicity is how he took over the world. Attack or don''t attack? Boom, simple. If he hadn''t stuck to simplicity, he wouldn''t have been able to do what he did. Complex thoughts just get in the way of expansionist ambitions, you know?', 'That kind of <kanji>simplicity</kanji> is what Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) always demanded. Everything he decided was based on simplicity.', 'N2', NULL),
(1515, 'Why hadn''t anyone thought of making a samurai magazine before?!', 'The sheep samurai is ruining your magazine! Maybe you should start a new magazine called, "All the places the sheep samurai will be, so stay away!" to make up for all the damage you''ve caused.', 'Unfortunately, your <kanji>magazine</kanji> helps the <reading>shee</reading>p (し) samurai find other samurai... and kill them. None of the samurai want to share their feelings with your magazine anymore, oh no!', 'N2', NULL),
(1516, 'Imagine someone looking into a hole that shows your private heart. That''s a window. A window you probably should have kept closed. Now everyone is looking in.', 'Look out your window back at the faces. Try to express how mad you are at them with your face through the glass. Make as many mad faces that you can think of.', 'You realize that a ton of people are looking into your <kanji>window</kanji> and seeing all your private things. This makes you <reading>mad</reading> (まど)! What right do they have to know your private thoughts and desires?!', 'N3', NULL),
(1517, 'Imagine encountering this Not Mouth. Ask it a question. It answers, “No”. Imagine doing this over and over until you believe that the Not Mouth always says “no”.', 'Listen as the Not Mouth continues to scream in bloodcurdling agony. Hear the flames flicker as they lick across the Not Mouth’s being. Seems as though the heat was too much for the Not Mouth.', 'You ask the Not Mouth if it is afraid of <reading>he</reading>at (ひ). The Not Mouth says, “<kanji>no</kanji>”. So you set the Not Mouth on fire. The Not Mouth then screams, “NONONONONONONONONONOOOOOO~!” Looks like someone can’t handle the heat.', 'N3', NULL),
(1518, 'Picture your new bamboo muscles glowing under your skin with the moon''s power. You flex and feel the power coursing through you!', 'Imagine standing before the King, explaining in detail how you cut yourself open and replaced your weak human meat with the special muscles you have now. He''s staring intently at you, but more so at your shining body than your mouth as you talk.', 'Your special <kanji>muscle</kanji>s and <kanji>tendon</kanji>s have become famous and the <reading>Kin</reading>g (きん) of England has requested that you appear in his court! It looks like he wants you to teach his barber surgeons how you got your new muscles.', 'N1', NULL),
(1521, 'Picture a shadowy shape on top of the roof, proclaiming to the entire town that the new day is here! It''s come! And it''s also time to get up.', 'Picture that shadow on the roof, proclaiming it''s a new day, but this time make sure you picture the outline of this centaur. He has a human top half and a horse bottom half. And he''s yelling down at you, "HEY THE SUN IS UP IT''S DAWN GET OUT OF BED!"', 'The thing that climbs up on the roof to <kanji>proclaim</kanji> that the new day is here is a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん). Ohoho, did you think I was going to say it was a rooster? Wrong! Centaurs are taller than humans and roosters, so when they climb onto the roof with their horse legs they can see dawn starting before anyone else.', 'N1', NULL),
(1522, 'Look at one of these revered horns. Pick it up and feel its weight. It measures very large. Your arms are getting tired from the weight.', 'Good thing you already have one of these revered horns. Now imagine yourself grinding it up into a powder to mix into a potion for your son to drink. Hear the horn grinding away as you physically break the horn up into millions of tiny pieces.', 'The reason you are interested in these <kanji>revered</kanji> horns so much is that your <reading>son</reading> (そん) is sick and you need one of these revered horns to make a medicine for your son. Only with the revered power of the horns will you be able to save your son’s life.', 'N2', NULL),
(1523, 'Who do you feel the most loyalty towards? Imagine them in the middle of your heart. Feel them in your chest. Imagine them, literally inside the middle of your heart.', 'Chewbacca is very loyal. Focus on your loyalty to Chewbacca. Picture him right in the middle of your heart and hear his Chewbacca yell. Also keep in mind that this kanji shares its reading with 中 which is also included in this kanji.', 'You pictured <reading>Chew</reading>bacca (ちゅう), didn’t you? No? Well you should have. Chewbacca deserves everyone’s <kanji>loyalty</kanji>. Chewbacca is super loyal to you, so he deserves your loyalty back.', 'N1', NULL),
(1524, 'Extend your fingers right now and see how wide you can make them.', 'You poked the cock right in the eye with your extended fingers. Maybe you shouldn''t be doing that in public. Innocent cocks could get hurt.', 'As you <kanji>extend</kanji> your fingers a <reading>cock</reading> (かく) comes running by and runs straight into them. Bu-COCK!', 'N1', NULL),
(1525, '"Ah golly gee wiz, he sure got his fingers all up in my syrup again, dagnabbit." You know, this saying we say all the time when someone manipulates us into doing something.', 'What happens to your soul, you ask? It gets covered in syrup. That''s probably where the syrup fingers phrase came from in the first place.', 'If you want to <kanji>manipulate</kanji> someone else it''s going to have a permanent effect on your <reading>sou</reading>l (そう). Being manipulative isn''t cool. And your soul will suffer forever for it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1526, 'Even better, write a poem about winter IN winter. Then they''ll really respect you.', 'The cake has the word RESPECT in fancy, cursive letters. Imagine eating that cake and taste the respect of your poet peers. Mm mm!', 'Once you have their <kanji>respect</kanji> you get <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい)!', 'N2', NULL),
(1527, 'Just feel happy that your plants have such a nice livelihood now that the sun is out. They look so healthy and fresh!', 'Look at Bobo do what he needs to do to maintain his own livelihood. Hear the flowers screaming as they are plucked from their homes.', 'From out of nowhere, <reading>Bo</reading>bo (ぼ) the notorious clown thief starts picking the plants, ending their <kanji>livelihood</kanji>. Bobo needs the flowers for his clown acts, so he uses the livelihood of the flowers to support his own livelihood.', 'N3', NULL),
(1528, 'See the light from the fire as the ashes fly off the cliff. The wind carries some of the ashes over to you. Feel the ashes as they strike your face and wipe the black ash with your hands.', 'Ashes on my face. Falling from the cliff of fire. Gives my life meaning. Or just come up with your own haiku. Whatever works for you.', 'The <kanji>ashes</kanji> inspire you to write a <reading>hai</reading>ku (はい). Using the ashes you write a haiku on a rock for everyone to enjoy.', 'N2', NULL),
(1529, 'Picture yourself receiving something, arranging it in a circle in the pan, and leaving it to boil. See how quickly that stuff ripens? Now it’s all ripe and yummy.', 'Imagine the jukebox playing music to accompany your ripened treat. Ahh, jukebox music and ripened goodies. Can life get any sweeter?', 'Once everything you received has <kanji>ripen</kanji>ed, you kick back near your <reading>juke</reading>box (じゅく) and eat it. The jukebox sets the mood, making the ripened treat feels twice as sweet.', 'N1', NULL),
(1530, 'Try to picture how each rice paddy differs from the next. Can you think of any differences in the ones you''ve seen?', 'Aha! As an eagle you can easily see that all of the rice paddies have differing colors, shapes, and (if you fly low) smells. They differ a lot!', 'To see how rice paddies <kanji>differ</kanji>, you need an <reading>ea</reading>gle''s (い) eye view. It can be hard to see from the ground, so soar up into the sky like an eagle and look down at the rice paddies together.', 'N1', NULL),
(1531, 'See the emperor, garbed all in white. There are other kings, but the emperor''s white clothing shows his status above the rest.', 'How did こういち become emperor?! His first decree as emperor is that everybody must learn kanji now.

Note: from now on, Emperor こういち will be a recurring character for the reading mnemonic of こう.', 'Who is the <kanji>emperor</kanji>? It is <reading>こう</reading>いち. こういち the emperor stands over his subjects. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1532, 'If you have piles of something, you''re prosperous. If you become a plate, that''s the best thing that can happen to you.', 'You know a family is seriously prosperous when they don''t use forks, they use a magical saber to cut their food. Wowee wow wow.', 'You''re so <kanji>prosperous</kanji> you use your <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい) to cut the food pile and shove the bits into your plate mouth. In fact, your entire family is using your special saber to cut and eat off of you. A normal knife wouldn''t be able to get through all this food!', 'N1', NULL),
(1533, 'Feel these few grains of sand between your fingertips. Imagine the stones that they originally came from. Nature is crazy.', 'You may wonder what a sand saw is. Well, it''s a saw for cutting sand into even finer little pieces. How does it work? It''s a mystery!', 'After breaking up your stone into <kanji>sand</kanji>, you fetch your special sand <reading>sa</reading>w (さ). A few bits are still too chunky, so you''ll have to use a sand saw now.', 'N2', NULL),
(1535, 'Taste that sweet, sweet sugar. Not many people know, but this is how it''s made. The canopy is needed for temperature control, and the wolverine… well, it''s got special enzymes in its mouth or something.', 'As you eat the sugar, you picture とうきょう. The place where you fell in love with these sugar-making techniques.', 'You learned how to make this kind of <kanji>sugar</kanji> in <reading>とう</reading>きょう. You found a special とうきょう sugar sensei and they introduced you to all the secret methods you know now.', 'N1', NULL),
(1536, 'Go inside your house or shed, it doesn''t matter where, and look happily at your thread supply. You have thread in all sizes, shapes, and especially colors.', 'Nostradamus says you should have a large supply of everything you use regularly. Thread, water, canned foods. You can''t see the future, but he can, and if he''s telling you to stock up on your inside supplies I think you should listen.', 'You have this <kanji>supply</kanji> because <reading>No</reading>stradamus (のう) had a prediction, saying you would. And that if you didn''t keep a good supply of thread inside, you''d run out.', 'N1', NULL),
(1537, 'Imagine having a moon and a city inside your lung. Feel their presence as you breathe in and breathe out. Also, 月 often refers to internal organs as well, so remember that when you see this kanji.', 'The city and moon
Inside my lung, forgotten.
Where is the answer?

Or just think of your own haiku. Be creative!', 'Having a moon and city inside your <kanji>lung</kanji> inspires you to write a <reading>hai</reading>ku (はい). Perhaps the city and moon are just metaphorical. Either way, a haiku is the best way to go about things here.', 'N1', NULL),
(1538, 'Imagine a time when a book you were reading used flowery language and you couldn''t understand. Imagine the annoyance with the author for being so overwrought and flowery.', 'Picture the author trying to use chopsticks to hold some little flowers. It looks like they''re having a hard time, but people will do anything to be a great author, I suppose.', 'Unfortunately, the <kanji>author</kanji> is allergic to touching flowers, so they hold them with a pair of <reading>cho</reading>psticks (ちょ). They hold them real close to their face with the chopsticks, so they can still use them to think of fancy, author-ly words.', 'N2', NULL),
(1539, 'Steam comes out of the coral flowers because they hold a lot of water. They''re flowers, but they''re coral flowers, so they came from the ocean. Of course there''s water in them, and if you boil that water steam will come out.', 'It''s weird that Joe is so sensitive to the smell of steaming coral. He''s lived as a farmhand nowhere near the sea all his life... at least that''s what he told you. And now he''s in your house breathing in the steam really deeply with his nose practically in the pot.', 'As you watch the <kanji>steam</kanji> coming off the coral flowers, someone appears behind you. It’s <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう), the big farmhand. "Are you steaming coral?" he asks. "Smelled it a mile away. I love steaming me some coral.”', 'N2', NULL),
(1540, 'Your brain is like a storehouse, you know? Hopefully that''s where you kept your storehouse knowledge.', 'Be really proud of your storehouse. You''ve collected a lot of zombies and put them in there, where they''ll keep fresh a long time!', 'You have a <kanji>storehouse</kanji>. Inside is where you keep all your <reading>zo</reading>mbies (ぞう).', 'N2', NULL),
(1541, 'Picture a samurai in his winter attire. His helmet is white and fuzzy and he has decorative icicles hanging down off of his armor.', 'Picture the kind of attire you''d wear as a samurai to reflect your soul. Mine would be bright pink and covered in horses and bananas and daisies to show that I have a happy, kind soul and that I don''t want to be killed by anyone else.', '<kanji>Attire</kanji> like this is important because it reflects your <reading>soul</reading> (そう). The samurai is wearing clothing with icicles because that''s how his heart feels too: frozen over because he has to fight in this horrible winter weather.', 'N2', NULL),
(1542, 'You''ve been curious about what''s on the lid (and your village''s) backside for years, and you finally can''t take it anymore so you kick the lid as hard as you can!', 'Wait, isn''t uranium bad for people? This explains why all of the villagers'' backsides are covered in weird glowing, green lumps.', 'It turns out your village''s own <kanji>backside</kanji> is covered in <reading>ura</reading>nium (うら). Eureka! You''re all gonna be rich!', 'N2', NULL),
(1543, 'You are someone who can say things in various languages. Speak some of these various languages now. You are someone who is good at saying things. Nice job.', 'Imagine yourself at this show. The spotlight is on and all eyes are watching. You feel kind of nervous, but you speak the various languages with ease.', 'You’re so good at speaking <kanji>various</kanji> languages that you decide to put on a <reading>sho</reading>w (しょ). At the show, you say a lot of things in various languages and everyone is impressed.', 'N2', NULL),
(1544, 'King Shellfish loves when people rent from him. Imagine him rolling in all of the rent money (shellfish) he collected from all the people who rent from him. Hear his shell clattering as he frolics on all the dead shells from the shellfish.', 'Feel your entire body shaking, but especially your chin, as you tell him you''re two shellfish short on rent this month. The last thing you see is his big fist coming right for your chin.', 'If you don''t have enough shellfish to pay your <kanji>rent</kanji>, King Shellfish will punch you in the <reading>chin</reading> (ちん).', 'N1', NULL),
(1545, 'Imagine shooting the catapult on your own people by mistake. Hear the catapult whoosh as your heart drops into your stomach. Oh, what a terrible mistake you’ve just made.', 'Picture Godzilla jumping happily on the castle and surrounding town you hit by mistake. This is probably the worst mistake you''ve ever made.', 'This <kanji>mistake</kanji> alerts <reading>Go</reading>dzilla (ご), who thinks it''s town destroying time. He barrels over the hills, sees where you mistakenly catapulted your own people, and proceeds to stomp on the rest of it for you.', 'N3', NULL),
(1546, 'Your moon storehouse has internal organs because 1) you have to keep them far away from the zombies or they''ll go berserk, and 2) you think it''s funny to see them bounce around in low gravity. Boing, boing, boing, haha look at that internal organ.', 'Basically, you store zombies in your regular storehouse, harvest their organs, then launch them into space where they are put into a storehouse on the moon where the zombies will never be able to find them again.', 'Where did you get all these <kanji>internal organ</kanji>s from? You actually harvested them from the <reading>zo</reading>mbies (ぞう) from your other storehouse. ', 'N2', NULL),
(1547, 'The most valuable kanji are the ones you don''t have to learn from scratch.', 'Just be careful not to lose track of the key. Otherwise, your valuable stuff is going to be safe from everyone… including you.', 'Most <kanji>valuable</kanji> things require a <reading>key</reading> (き) to be accessed. I mean, you''ve gotta keep your valuable stuff safe, right? Best to keep it under lock and key.', 'N1', NULL),
(1550, 'Imagine how queasy you''re feeling with all this dirt in your mouth. You''re going to have to throw up now — you''ve passed the puke threshold.', 'Imagine the sound you made the last time you threw up. It sounded like you were saying "hah!" from the bottom of your tummy.', 'When you <kanji>throw up</kanji> it sounds like you''re shouting, "<reading>Hah</reading> (は)!" Then all the dirt-puke comes out in a wave. It''s less of a laugh "hah" and more of an oh no I''m throwing up "hah."', 'N1', NULL),
(1551, 'Dude, I can''t believe you called that dude a woman. What''s wrong with you?!', 'Look at the poor dog. He''s not a dude and now he doesn''t think he''s a good boy, all because of this other dude. Was he sitting on the stool this whole time, waiting to make this dog feel bad? I guess you''ll never know...', 'You only found out this <kanji>dude</kanji> wasn''t a woman when a <reading>do</reading>g (ど) ran up and the dude stood up. He started rubbing the dog''s face saying, "Who''s a good boy? Who''s a good boy? Not you! Me! I''m the best boy of them all. Because I am a dude, the best of all boys! HAHAHAHA!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1552, 'Traditionally, samurai don''t work with jackhammers or do menial tasks like cleaning brushes and hauling water. This samurai is doing these things for someone else, meaning he''s somebody''s slave.', 'The samurai can use the ray gun to shoot his keeper and get away. Imagine watching him holding up the master and getting away. A ray gun is a truly powerful weapon in the hands of a slave.', 'You don''t like seeing <kanji>slave</kanji>s. As you walk by, you hand this slave a <reading>ray</reading> (れい) gun so he can free himself.', 'N1', NULL),
(1554, 'Picture one hundred people under the same roof watching in horror as their leader suddenly shrinks to nothingness before them.', 'The leader gets shook so badly, he can''t even call out to his people. They''re going to trample his tiny, shrinking body!', 'As the leader <kanji>shrink</kanji>s, the hundred people start screaming and running around the entire place. As a result, everything is <reading>shook</reading> (しゅく) up. Especially the poor, shrunken leader, who gets knocked around the most.', 'N1', NULL),
(1555, 'Imagine a barracks made of pure thread. No brick or clay barracks can compete with yours, as long as you make it out of pure, unadulterated thread.', 'Okay, so maybe building a pure barracks that you can only use in June was a bad idea, but it''s there now and I don''t want you getting it dirty, so just use it when I say so, okay?', 'Unfortunately, this <kanji>pure</kanji> thread barracks can only be used in <reading>June</reading> (じゅん). Any other time of year there will be snow and rain and mud that will get tracked into the pure barracks and ruin them. But June is the cleanest, most pure month, so you can use it then.', 'N2', NULL),
(1556, 'Look at this vertical thread as it hangs from the horns. Take hold of it and pull on the vertical thread. Feel it in your hand.', 'Look at Channing Tatum playing with this thread. He’s like a cat playing with a piece of yarn. Look at Mr. Tatum bouncing around batting at the thread like some silly feline.', 'For some reason, Channing <reading>Tat</reading>um (たて) is strangely attracted to <kanji>vertical</kanji> pieces of hanging thread. Channing Tatum starts batting at the vertical thread like a cat.', 'N1', NULL),
(1557, 'Imagine hefting this cross with the rice in the shape of a nine on it. You don''t feel stylish, but society tells you that you are. You''re stylish.', 'See the rice fly through the air from you swinging the cross. Hear it as it clatters to the ground. This cross just isn''t as stylish anymore now.', 'It''s also <kanji>stylish</kanji> to <reading>swi</reading>ng (すい) the cross around while you wait for things. You''re waiting at a bus stop so you swing the cross around violently. You swing it so violently that the rice comes flying off!', 'N1', NULL),
(1558, 'See the holy ear and mouth of this king. They glisten and glitter and people write hymnals about their holy prowess. Imagine a song playing as you witness their holy majesty.', 'The holy king heard you turned into a prosperous plate and used the saber to cut your food and now he wants to try it too. As he holds your saber, a holy circle forms around it, making it sparkle and shimmer! Your saber is holy now too!!', 'This <kanji>holy</kanji> king has heard of you and your special <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい) that glows with pure, blue light. Specifically, the holy king wants to use it on his food!', 'N1', NULL),
(1559, 'Imagine yourself trying to pick each object up off the ground with your magnet. Hear the clink of the stone against metal as the magnet lifts it off the ground. Must be some iron in it or something.', 'Imagine Jesus snickering behind you as the stone falls to the ground once again. Imagine running after Jesus so you can catch him and give him a piece of your mind. He’s always playing tricks on you.', 'You’re pretty excited that you lifted something up with your <kanji>magnet</kanji>. Science is cool! But then you hear someone laughing behind you. It’s <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ)! Jesus was using his magic to levitate the stone all along, it wasn’t your magnet. Darn it Jesus! You’ve fooled me for the last time!', 'N1', NULL),
(1560, 'Because thread isn''t that strong, you have to use A LOT (to hold things like beams in place). So, the deep red or crimson thread actually stands out a lot in this construction project.', 'こういち walks up with a big smile on his face. "I love the color crimson!" he exclaims. "It''s the same deep red color as my enemies."

Hint: When you see 工 in a kanji it will often take the こう reading.', 'You''re done, and the whole building is a <kanji>deep red</kanji> <kanji>crimson</kanji> color (it takes a lot of thread to hold a whole building in place). It''s time to show the client, Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち, the finished product. ', 'N2', NULL),
(1561, 'Imagine someone measuring your body before they shoot you. You hope it''s the good kind of shoot and that you''re not going to die.', 'Shamans are just a different kind of doctor. And this shot is going to save you from getting the flu, which could kill you. Make sure you always go to the local shaman and schedule a shooting.', 'This <kanji>shoot</kanji> is being done by a <reading>sha</reading>man (しゃ). Not only is he doing the body measurements for the shoot, but he''s also going to be shooting you with a shot so you don''t get the flu.', 'N1', NULL),
(1562, 'Imagine hanging towels over all the windows in the greenhouse. They''re really fuzzy, thick curtains now.', 'Imagine hiding in a greenhouse blocked off with towel curtains, stuffing your face with Big Macs. No one can shame you in here. You can eat as many as you want. And hey, you can use the curtains as napkins too.', 'You''re hanging these towel <kanji>curtain</kanji>s because you need a quiet place to eat a Big <reading>Mac</reading> (まく). But you''re a little ashamed. Big Macs aren''t really good for you, but this stupid greenhouse has too many windows and everyone can see you stuff your face. So that''s where the towel curtains come in.', 'N1', NULL),
(1563, 'Picture your sweaty, sweaty fingers. Dry them off before you enter a place of worship! No god wants to deal with your weird, damp hands.', 'Imagine being in your place of worship. Close your eyes and say a haiku:

So I won''t offend
Lord Crabigator-sama
I dry my hands now.', 'Once you''re dry and in wherever your <kanji>worship</kanji> is, you begin with a <reading>hai</reading>ku (はい). Really all places of worship have their own version of the haiku, and heck, the 5-7-5 rule is just a guideline, so everyone is doing it pretty close to right anyway.', 'N2', NULL),
(1564, 'You''ve bought flowers before, but you really don''t know which ones would work best to stop birds from landing on the canopy and pooping on your helicopter.', 'I hope the centaur recommends a cheap flower... but with your luck flowers that keep birds away will cost a fortune.', 'You ask for a <kanji>recommend</kanji>ation from the <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん) that owns the local flower shop. Now he''s walking around your helicopter canopy and staring at the birds, nodding.', 'N1', NULL),
(1565, 'Imagine a turkey is pointing at you with its fingers. The gears in your brain begin to slowly turn. From looking at the turkey to its fingers and back to the turkey, you can infer that something bad is probably about to happen to you.', 'Picture yourself as Sherlock Holmes, you inferred your way to freedom, swinging away on a conveniently-placed vine.', 'You were able to <kanji>infer</kanji> the turkey''s plan, so now it''s your turn. You look around and see the only way out... is to <reading>swi</reading>ng (すい) to safety! How did I infer that, dear Watson? Why, isn''t it obvious! There are vines here. Turkeys can''t fly. So I shall swing my way to freedom!', 'N1', NULL),
(1566, 'Imagine standing on top of your car, brandishing your fingers in the air while all of the cars stuck behind you honk angrily.', 'You brandish the key in the air and then throw it into the woods. No more cars for you! Everyone should follow your example! Brandish your car keys in the air and throw them away!', 'What are you <kanji>brandish</kanji>ing in your fingers? Your car''s <reading>key</reading> (き)! You want every driver and every car to know that you aren''t going to be driving ever again!', 'N1', NULL),
(1567, 'Your mouth is open so you can scream, because this is probably a bad idea.', 'Okay, so maybe trying to run alongside a tsunami with just some fins and a mouth full of screams and terror wasn''t the best idea. But at least everything ended while you did... uh... something unique?', 'You''re able to <kanji>run alongside</kanji> the tsunami for about ten seconds before everything <reading>en</reading>ds (えん).', 'N1', NULL),
(1568, 'Picture the original tsunami used in the origin story of Japan (never mind that we just made this up). It was huge. Bigger than anything you''ve ever seen. And without it you wouldn''t have your precious anime and manga.', 'The origin of Genji was the origin of all Japan: a big ol'' tsunami.', 'This tsunami was also the <kanji>origin</kanji> of <reading>Gen</reading>ji (げん), from "The Tale of Genji." Just think, without this origin, Japan wouldn''t exist and neither would the first Japanese heartthrob, Genji!', 'N1', NULL),
(1570, 'Imagine putting your boots on and suddenly being filled with the power and energy to work hard and efficiently.', 'Picture yourself in work boots. Now picture the King in work boots. Yours are worn and beaten up and his are clean and look brand new. But at least you both have them, which means you both work at least a little!', 'You need boots to give you the power to <kanji>work</kanji> for the <reading>Kin</reading>g (きん) of England. Really, all work is for the King, since everything is for him. But even he wears boots. And they give him the power to uh... king over things.', 'N3', NULL),
(1571, 'A turkey that lacks an arrow is a delighted turkey.', 'He loves his turkey friend, and every time he sees it he squeals with delight.', '<kanji>Delight</kanji> is what Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) felt when he saw his turkey friend, sans an arrow.', 'N1', NULL),
(1572, 'Picture the child finishing their first water. They look at a big stack of bottled waters, but they know better than to just take two more, they need consent first. "Excuse me, sir, could I please have two more waters?" the child asks.', 'You need to make sure to get express consent from the Shougun before you do most things. Especially if it''s something like taking more than you''ve been given.', '"Of course my child, you have my <kanji>consent</kanji>," comes the voice of the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). The Shougun is very pleased by this child, because he expects everyone to ask for consent around him.', 'N2', NULL),
(1573, 'Imagine raising your fingers to slap, hurt, or point angrily at a member of your own team. How dare you! I hope you''re ready for some deep, personal loss.', 'Picture your son. Your future son, maybe even your animal son, if you don''t want to have real kids. Now imagine the loss of that son. Nooooo!', 'Unfortunately for you, payment is in the form of the <kanji>loss</kanji> of your <reading>son</reading> (そん). The loss of any family member is horrible, but this was your son. He was your favorite child. You loved him. But that''s the loss you must bear for raising your fingers to a team member.', 'N2', NULL),
(1574, 'Picture a tree branch in your hands. Feel it. Smell it. It smells and feels like a tree.', 'Picture the branch suddenly crying out, "Eh, Dad?" in a squeaky voice. You''d better find its real dad, otherwise this branch is never going to leave you alone.', 'You''re smelling and feeling the  <kanji>branch</kanji> when, suddenly, it starts screaming at you: "<reading>Eh, da</reading>d?" (えだ). The branch won''t leave you alone. It follows you around screaming "Eh, dad?", thinking you''re its long-lost tree branch father. Poor thing.', 'N2', NULL),
(1576, '*toots*', 'Imagine eating these beans from とうきょう. They’re the best beans you’ve ever had. Even if you don’t like beans, you love とうきょう beans. Mmm… beans, beans, とうきょう beans. ', 'These <kanji>beans</kanji> are from <reading>とう</reading>きょう. Everybody knows とうきょう has the best beans. What?! You didn’t know that? Tokyo’s beans are the best beans!', 'N1', NULL),
(1577, 'You carve meat, you carve wood, all with a slicing motion with a knife.', 'Imagine washing down all your hard carving work with an ice cold Coke.', 'You work up a sweat trying to <kanji>carve</kanji> meat and wood all day, so you grab yourself a nice <reading>Coke</reading> (こく). You like Coke, and it goes well with meat and sawdust.', 'N3', NULL),
(1578, 'Imagine the smell of rotten meat hanging from your canopy. Now no one wants to come near here.', 'What, did you think attaching this meat to the canopy would make it turn into jerky or something? Fool...', 'You let meat <kanji>rot</kanji>. You''re a <reading>foo</reading>l (ふ). That''s really all I can say at this point.', 'N1', NULL),
(1534, 'The greenhouse was your only hope in turning the desert into a green forest. Now it''s all washed away by salt water, meaning nothing will grow anyways. Feel disappointed about this.', 'Up, back down, back up, back down. Move your head up and down to keep up with the sun’s movements. Will it ever rise like normal?', 'See the sun come up over the <kanji>desert</kanji>, then go <reading>back</reading> (ばく) down. Up, back down, back up, back down. You stand here for days watching because you''re so depressed. Stop being so <kanji>vague</kanji> in your approach, sun! You’re weirding everyone out!', 'N1', NULL),
(1579, 'It''s also a warning to the sheep that if they want to ride scooters they should also slow down, because as we all know, sheep are speed demons.', 'Picture all of the sheep finally going slowly because there is yummy cheese all over the road.', 'You follow the flag, but the sheep refuse to <kanji>slow</kanji> down so you have to get creative. You place <reading>chee</reading>se (ち) every ten feet or so, that way the sheep slow down to bend down on their scooters and take a bite.', 'N3', NULL),
(1580, 'Their hair has literally been carved out of their head, and you discovered it. Of course, the nastiness, the smell, and the feel should be applied to your imagination to make the mnemonic stronger.', 'You can also imagine Mrs. Chou doing the act of carving someone''s hair out, but that may just be too much.', 'When you think of all the people who could have done this... all the people who could have <kanji>carve</kanji>d out someone''s hair, only one person comes to mind, and that is Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう). She''s the only person evil enough to do something like this.', 'N1', NULL),
(1581, 'You can''t eat shellfish that are too small, so you place your knife along them to measure them. If they''re big enough, you stick the knife in and eat them raw.', 'If you measure one of these shellfish and it''s too small, stuff it right into your sock. Then once the sock measures about 12 shellfish long, toss it back to sea!', 'All of the shellfish you <kanji>measure</kanji> that are too small go into your <reading>sock</reading> (そく). Once the sock is full, you swing it around your head and lob it back into the ocean.', 'N2', NULL),
(1582, 'Imagine this sharp stone. Feel its weight in your hand. Now drag the sharp stone against your skin. Feel your skin as it begins to tear. The pain is agonizing.', 'You make this noise when you tear other things with the sharp stone too, but now it''s more like, "Hah! Take that! Feel the same pain I feel!"', 'The moment your skin <kanji>tear</kanji>s you can''t help but yelp, "<reading>Hah</reading> (は)!" It sounds like a laugh, but it''s more like a pained bark.', 'N3', NULL),
(1583, 'As long as you know the radical cottage, you know the kanji cottage.', 'I guess shamans are moving out a lot around here. Why don''t you go look for a cottage you can buy from a shaman! I bet you''ll get a pretty good, totally not cursed, deal!', 'You got this <kanji>cottage</kanji> from a <reading>sha</reading>man (しゃ). In fact, everyone you know got their cottage from a shaman. One day you''re just walking in the woods, and the next thing you know, a shaman is giving you the keys to their old cottage.', 'N1', NULL),
(1584, 'A lifeguard''s favorite part of the job is telling people what to do, where to swim, when to get out of the water, and they love saying it in a bunch of words.', 'Close your eyes and picture こういち lecturing you. Ugh, it''s the worst!

Hint: when you see the "lifeguard" radical, the reading is probably going to be こう.', 'This <kanji>lecture</kanji> loving lifeguard is none other than <reading>こう</reading>いち! I mean, look how long winded the stuff on this site is? Can you imagine him trying to give you a talk about proper swimming etiquette?', 'N2', NULL),
(1585, 'Watch this belt as it stagnated the tsunami. See the belt glow as its enchantment activates, immediately stagnating the tsunami water.', 'Look in the mirror at you in your tie. Put the finishing touches on your outfit and imagine going out into the world with your tie on, no longer afraid of any tsunamis. They’re all stagnant now thanks to that magical belt.', 'While you watch the tsunami <kanji>stagnate</kanji>, you get ready for work. While you were watching this whole thing happening, you were tying your <reading>tie</reading> (たい). Once the tsunami stagnated, you were all done tying your tie. And since the tsunami is no longer a threat, you’re ready to go to work.', 'N1', NULL),
(1586, 'You literally call out, and when they turn around, tie a thread to them, so you''ll never forget that you introduced yourselves to each other.', 'Finally the Shougun sighs and turns toward you. You call out your name and get the thread around him. Uh oh. He doesn''t look happy.', 'You want to <kanji>introduce</kanji> yourself to the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) but no matter how much you call out, he won''t come close enough for you to tie your introduction thread to him. You call louder and louder, "SHOUGUN LET ME INTRODUCE MYSELF!"', 'N2', NULL),
(1587, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Picture yourself jammed into a kayak. Your pants are bunching and sticking out the sides and top. You really don''t fit in here.', 'Look down! You''re <kanji>jammed in</kanji>to something. You can''t move your feet. What is this?! Oh, your body''s been jammed in a <reading>kay</reading>ak (かい).', 'N2', NULL),
(1588, 'Make sure you know those radicals, yo!', 'Kids can only think of oneself. Until a certain age, that''s literally all they can care about.', '<kanji>Oneself</kanji>. Who only thinks of oneself? Every <reading>子</reading> (こ) in the world.', 'N1', NULL),
(1589, 'What will you do? You can either let go and fall off the cliff, or hold on and get bitten by the snake. Man, talk about misfortune.', 'Imagine the glittering maze of hooves and yak horns that enclose you now. Ahhh! Then the yak pass, and you find yourself alive but beaten. Life is one misfortune after another, but misfortunes, like the yak, eventually pass…', 'You decide to let go, but at the bottom of the cliff is an even greater <kanji>misfortune</kanji>: <reading>yak</reading> (やく). A whole stampeding herd of yak is passing just as you fall. Oops! You''ve accidentally committed yourself to the ultimate misfortune, death by yak trampling. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1590, NULL, 'See this turtle as he gathers energy, shaking the earth around him, gathering chi for his Kamehameha blast. He releases it in a mighty blast of energy. Kamehameha!!', 'You know Dragon Ball Z, right? Imagine this <kanji>turtle</kanji> doing a <reading>Kame</reading>hameha (かめ). The turtle charges up his energy and releases a mighty Kamehameha blast. Shwooom!', 'N1', NULL),
(1591, 'Pick up the shuriken and throw it into the ground. Hear it whizz through the air and then plunk into the ground. This act of aggression causes their mutual respect to waver.', 'You give Godzilla a thumbs up, you see what he''s trying to do. He looks down at you and nods. Now you have a mutual respect for one another too.', 'Since you made the shuriken attack the ground, the <kanji>mutual</kanji> respect is out of balance. This summons <reading>Go</reading>dzilla (ご) up from the depths of the ocean once more. He always shows up when something is out of wack, to show humans the error of their ways. He picks up the shuriken and flings it into a city, which explodes, leaving a crater in the ground.', 'N3', NULL),
(1592, 'Imagine creating your own sword out of a knife and a squid.', 'Picture Ken swinging his squid sword at you. *Squish* it squirts ink on your shoulder. But it doesn''t really hurt.', 'You learned how to make <kanji>sword</kanji>s this way from <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai. He put his own little squid buddy on his knife and now no one realizes it''s not a sword!', 'N1', NULL),
(1595, 'Feel the shame. Feel the shame in both your ear and your heart. Imagine the things you’ve done to make you feel so much shame.', 'Hah! What a fool you were to think you could erase your shameful past with a half-hearted attempt at the Hajj. Shame on you.', 'You must cleanse your spirit of this <kanji>shame</kanji>. You decide to complete <reading>Hajj</reading> (はじ) — you know, the Islamic pilgrimage to Mecca. However, when you get there, all the other pilgrims look at you and say, "<reading>Hah</reading> (は)! You? You''re a lost cause, buddy." It seems even Hajj can''t save you from your shame.', 'N3', NULL),
(1596, 'Imagine having a cedar tree in your hair. It’s just a little tree, a sapling, but it’s pretty strange and awkward. It does smell nice though. Mmm… cedar.', 'Give Sue her Sue guitar. Hear Sue squeal with glee as she receives her Sue guitar.', 'Once the <kanji>cedar</kanji> is fully grown, you use the cedar wood to make a guitar for Sue. A <reading>Sue gui</reading>tar (すぎ), if you will. This Sue guitar was made just for Sue. She’ll love it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1597, 'Picture a bowl of soup, but now there is a cross and some tsunami water in it.', 'You''re curious so you take a sip of this jewel soup and... it''s delicious! You should make more and sell it!', 'You pull the cross out of your <kanji>soup</kanji> and see that the cross'' <reading>jew</reading>els (じゅう) fell off and are in there now. The jewels sparkle, shine, and dissolve into the soup!', 'N1', NULL),
(1599, 'Feel the fire all around you, the flames licking at your body. It’s really hot!', 'Sorry, but standing in a blaze isn''t the best idea. Flames are pretty efficient at ending the lives of the things in it. Maybe you should do this kanji lesson over and try not to stand in the flames next time.', 'The <kanji>flame</kanji>s gets bigger, surround you, and then everything <reading>en</reading>ds (えん).', 'N1', NULL),
(1600, 'For goodness sake! Please remember this isn''t "sake" as in お酒! It''s the English "sake," that rhymes with "bake," "cake," and "steak." Oops.. now I''m hungry.', '
See this eagle as it comes to deliver your order just for your sake. Scree! Hear its wings flap and your package plop down on your porch as it''s delivered.', 'For the <kanji>sake</kanji> of convenience, companies will use <reading>ea</reading>gles (い) for delivery jobs in the future. Just put in an order, and it''ll be at your doorstep in no time flat. Eagles are like, super fast at delivery.', 'N1', NULL),
(1601, 'I bet you didn''t know this, but a bear can boil whenever it wants. Imagine a bear heating itself up and boiling, its fur and skin bubbling and letting off steam.', 'You can also imagine a bear graduating in the top 5-10% of its class. Or, just imagine a bear saying "Cuma here!" to you.', 'A <kanji>bear</kanji> boiling in front of you should fill you with great honor. Or, as they say in Latin: Magna <reading>cum</reading> (くま) laude.

Get it? A little hot bear humor for you.', 'N1', NULL),
(1602, 'Imagine any animal that’s not a dog. Now imagine dressing it up like a dog so it doesn’t have to go to prison. Practice throwing your voice by saying “woof woof” aloud to help convince people that your animal is actually a dog.', 'Poor little Goku doesn’t want to go to prison either so Goku does his best to act like a dog even though he isn’t. How adorable.', 'What is the name of your animal? Your animal is named <reading>Goku</reading> (ごく). Yeah, like Goku from Dragon Ball Z. You don’t want your little Goku to go to <kanji>prison</kanji>, so you say that he’s a dog.', 'N1', NULL),
(1603, 'Feel how drunk you are as you look at the cross shaped bottle that once held the alcohol. See the cross start to blur in front of you from how drunk you are.', 'Imagine that vertigo that comes over you if you lean your head back when you''re drunk. It feels like leaning back on a playground swing, in a way. Oh, wait… what if we got drunk and swung on the playground swings? Double swing effect! Actually, never mind. Bad idea.', 'Everything''s spinning now, you''re so <kanji>drunk</kanji> it feels like you''re on a <reading>swi</reading>ng (すい). Lurching back and forth in your drunkenness, feeling more and more nauseated by all this horrible swinging. Ugh.', 'N1', NULL),
(1604, 'Imagine making a bunch of vinegar. You use a key to change it from alcohol to vinegar, yum!', 'Imagine yourself doing the same thing as before but you are now wearing a suit. Dang, you look good.', 'You are demonstrating this practice in front of an audience. You want them to take you seriously so you wear a <reading>su</reading>it (す). A very nice suit. Surely they will now believe that you can turn alcohol into <kanji>vinegar</kanji>.', 'N1', NULL),
(1606, 'Where did lakes come from? They are the residue of tsunamis from many moons ago.', 'This is just a stupid story to get this 子 to stop asking "why, why, why?" about everything. Earlier, the 子 asked "where do lakes come from?" and you had to come up with a crazy story to quiet them.', '"That''s where <kanji>lake</kanji>s come from," you tell the <reading>子</reading> (こ).', 'N2', NULL),
(1607, 'Take a piece of gold in one hand and a piece of copper in the other. Bang them together furiously as you shout. “GOLD IS NOT THE SAME AS COPPER! GOLD IS NOT THE SAME AS COPPER!”', 'Mmm… doughnuts. Eat the doughnut and taste its yummy flavor as you look at the piece of copper. It’s shiny.', 'Smashing the <kanji>copper</kanji> against the gold has made you tired. You should eat a <reading>dou</reading>ghnut (どう). Go get yourself a doughnut.', 'N2', NULL),
(1609, 'Watch as this tsunami crashes over the land, pushing earth out of the way. Once the tsunami passes, hear the spewing geysers of oil as they erupt out of the ground.', 'Look at how this oil has changed the world. Instead of living in big homes, condos, or skyscrapers, everyone lives in a yurt at night and travels with all the oil by day.', 'Now that <kanji>oil</kanji> is so abundant, everyone lives in <reading>yu</reading>rts (ゆ). They''re light and mobile, so people can just move from oil well to oil well, collecting oil.', 'N2', NULL),
(1897, 'Imagine a teardrop going through this process to get out of your eye, though in a more miniature teardrop size.', 'Feel each teardrop rolling like a little wave down your cheek. You''ve never paid such close attention to them before. Teardrops really are just 波だ, you think. Little, ticklish waves.', 'Your <kanji>teardrop</kanji>s are like little waves. What are they? They''re <reading>波だ</reading> (なみだ).', 'N2', NULL),
(2133, 'One eave, one house. Two eaves, two houses.', 'Ken hops down and explains that he''s been hired to count all the houses in his lord''s domain just because his name is the same as the counter for houses. It isn''t really fair, he complains to you, as you try to sneak by and lock him out of your house.', 'On top of the <kanji>eaves</kanji> of your house <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai is counting houses too.', 'N2', NULL),
(1611, 'Pretend you are the stick. Really focus on your former self as you feel the sun warm you with its rays. Imagine yourself growing, larger and larger.', 'I guess this stick was formerly a cucumber tree. Or maybe it just tried so hard it pooped a cucumber. I don''t care which one is true, you pick whatever you want to believe.', 'It''s working, it''s working! The stick is returning to its <kanji>former</kanji> state and... w-wuh? It sprouted a <reading>cu</reading>cumber (きゅう).', 'N2', NULL),
(1612, 'Imagine having your surname changed for the rest of your life. How do you feel about having a different family name? Is it a big deal to you or does it not matter much?', 'Now you share a family name AND a holy blue saber! You can protect your legacy (and surname) hand in hand and saber in saber. (Or something like that.)', 'Picture this special woman in your mind. She''s changing her <kanji>surname</kanji> to yours and taking hold of your special <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい) as well. And it''s... it''s splitting in two!', 'N2', NULL),
(1613, 'Imagine your private sword holding a big bag of shellfish and going off to market to trade them.', 'Oh boy, I hope my private sword trades my shellfish for that new bowling ball I heard about!', 'While he''s out making the <kanji>trade</kanji> for you, you decide to relax and do some <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう). In fact, you can''t wait for the trade to be done because you''re hoping he''s going to come back with a new bowling ball for you.', 'N2', NULL),
(1614, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'You better remember the Shougun is the commander of everything... or else...', 'Who is the <kanji>commander</kanji> of Japan? The <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう)! And if you ever say anyone else or vote for someone else or even THINK about someone else being the commander, he''ll find you and cut off your head.', 'N2', NULL),
(1615, 'Look at this bright plate. Look at it. It’s awesome. It’s blinding. Hold it in your hands. Marvel at it. Wow. This plate is sweet.', 'This Mayflower boat is the best boat you ever did see. Hear the ocean around it as it bobs in the water, full sails billowing in the wind. Boats are cool.', 'The person you are trying to get in an <kanji>alliance</kanji> with you is the captain of the <reading>May</reading>flower (めい). You want to be in an alliance with the captain of the Mayflower because you really like boats. You want to ride on the Mayflower like, so bad.', 'N1', NULL),
(1616, 'Rings, necklaces, wallets, phones? Don''t take them on a scooter. Leave behind all that valuable stuff!', 'If you keep too many of your valuables on you, the eagles might not leave you behind either! They''ll just pick you up off the scooter and carry you and your valuables off to their nests!', 'Anything you don''t <kanji>leave behind</kanji> will be stolen by <reading>ea</reading>gles (い). Eagles like shiny, valuable things, especially ones that are bouncing around on you while you scoot by on a scooter.', 'N1', NULL),
(1617, 'The leader is humble and human, just like the rest of us, and has to stretch to stay healthy too. He''s not some kind of god!', 'Watch as the leader stretches his body and the nori seaweed. He takes a bite, stretches the other way, then stretches out a piece for you to eat too. How humble of him!', 'The leader is currently working hard to <kanji>stretch</kanji> in front of all of you, and as he does, he munches on some <reading>no</reading>ri (の) seaweed snacks. Then he starts stretching the nori too.', 'N2', NULL),
(1618, 'Picture the leader standing up in front of everyone as they blame him. He''s holding a plaque with the amount of debt you''re all in. Try to imagine what that number would be.', 'Watch as your cyborg saves your company, even though it wasn''t even his responsibility, from crippling debt. *sob* I can''t believe your cyborg is gone!', 'To pay off this <kanji>debt</kanji> your leader says you need to sell your <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい) to help the company. You fight him! You yell! You scream! The cyborg puts a half-human half-robot hand on your shoulder and shakes its head sadly.', 'N1', NULL),
(1619, 'Imagine yourself on an escalator. What is up above that you’re trying to reach?', 'Cucumbers grow on trees sometimes, so you need to take escalators to get up there and reach them.', 'You''re riding the escalator trying to <kanji>reach</kanji> a delicious <reading>cu</reading>cumber (きゅう).', 'N1', NULL),
(1620, 'Look at this big jackhammer. Look at it. It’s so big. Also it’s in Nara. Imagine being in Nara and looking at this big jackhammer. You’re all like, dang – that’s a big jackhammer.', 'Just keep in mind what the first part of Nara is. The first part of Nara is “na” so you’ll have no trouble remembering this one.', 'What’s the first part of <kanji>Nara</kanji>? <reading>Na</reading> (な). Well that’s easy.', 'N1', NULL),
(1621, 'The scarecrow creeps you out. You don''t want to look at it anymore, so you''re standing eerily close to it measuring its width, so you can hide it forever.', 'Do you know the width of a habanero? It''s one scarecrow minus one cloth.', 'You finish measuring the <kanji>width</kanji> of the scarecrow and the width of the towel, and you realize you''re the width of a <reading>haba</reading>nero (はば) pepper short. This is fine, you grow those. This is a whole field of habaneros! So you pluck one off the nearest plant, tape it to the cloth, and cover that scarecrow right up.', 'N2', NULL),
(1622, 'During your departure you look back at the obsolete canopy just once. It''s standing there, still and alone. It''s a really sad sight.', 'My sweet canopy
I don''t need you anymore
You are obsolete', 'When something becomes <kanji>obsolete</kanji>, you must write a <reading>hai</reading>ku (はい). Honestly, the best haiku are about something ending, so this is perfect.', 'N1', NULL),
(1623, NULL, 'Put on the armor! Feel the sweetness begin to reflect off your armored body. Only a little gets through — just enough for the sweet flavor on your tongue to taste sweet, but not too sweet.', 'The <kanji>sweet</kanji>, sweet sensation is almost overwhelming. You gotta put on some sweet-resistant <reading>armor</reading> (あま) to protect you from all that sweetness. The armor will prevent you from having a sweet, sweet sweetness overload.', 'N2', NULL),
(1624, 'Imagine counting all of the mouths and legs you''ve pummeled off of the other prisoners. Exchange them for better loot!', 'Think back to when you were a kid and they would roll out those TVs on stands into the classrooms. Imagine watching a documentary on Genghis Khan''s exchange routes where he bought and sold body parts. Educational!', 'You learned this type of <kanji>exchange</kanji> from the mighty Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん). You read a book or watched a show about him once and you''re pretty positive that Genghis Khan would exchange the body parts of his enemies all over the continents of Asia and Europe for nicer stuff.', 'N2', NULL),
(1625, 'Imagine plucking the hairs out of your mohawk with your fingers. It''s kind of hard to grab onto them at first, but eventually you''re plucking them like it''s nothing.', 'You didn''t realize techies were so exclusive... but they just plucked and plucked at your self-esteem, until you decided to pluck your hair out of your own head.', 'You want to <kanji>pluck</kanji> all your mohawk hair out because you''re a <reading>techie</reading> (てき), and everyone at your techie job keeps making fun of you for your hair choices. "Techies should be bald!" your boss screamed. So here you are, plucking your hair out to fit in.', 'N1', NULL),
(1626, 'Imagine slicing this tree smaller and smaller until you finally get to the nucleus of it. It’s super tiny so you have to view it in a microscope. What does it look like?', 'Look at this tree nucleus that looks like a cock. While you’re watching it, the nucleus goes “Cock-a-doodle-doo!”', 'It looks like a <reading>cock</reading> (かく). And by cock I mean a male chicken – a rooster. Why does the <kanji>nucleus</kanji> of a tree look like a cock? Science is weird.', 'N1', NULL),
(1627, 'Look at this tsunami in the middle of the open sea. When the tsunami isn’t destroying things and is just out there in the open sea it looks really majestic and pretty.', 'You really regret saying oki doki this time. You don’t want to surf on a tsunami in the middle of the open sea. Imagine doing so. It’s terrifying and wet and you don’t even know how to surf. You’re going to die.', 'Someone dares you to go out to the <kanji>open sea</kanji> and surf on the middle of the tsunami. “<reading>Oki</reading> (おき) doki,” you reply. You can’t refuse a dare. Every time someone dares you to do something, you always respond with “oki doki”.', 'N1', NULL),
(1628, 'A rope made of threaded turtles. Go ahead and grab onto it and see how it feels. A little rough, but strong.', 'Besides being awful, where did Joe learn to thread turtles into ropes? Why does he have so many turtles despite living on such an inland farm? These are nice ropes that Joe is making though, and you kind of want to buy some.', 'The person making these threaded turtle <kanji>rope</kanji>s is none other than the local farmhand <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう). You watch him stretch and peel pieces of turtle off of turtles, then thread them together into a rope.', 'N1', NULL),
(1629, 'This harbor is all you’ve got, it’s the backbone of your life. So how are you going to defend it from this tsunami caused by a giant brush?!', '“How are two of my friends going to stop an entire tsunami from destroying my dear harbor?”, you might ask yourself. Well don’t ask us, we don’t specialize in brush-caused tsunami harbor defence—we’re just a kanji learning website!', 'You’ll defend your <kanji>harbor</kanji> with your <reading>two</reading> (つ) best friends, that’s how! Luckily, your two friends specialize in defending harbors from giant brush-caused tsunamis. Quite the occupation.', 'N1', NULL),
(1630, 'It is well known that dogs in the South are famous for their kindness and hospitality. It''s customary to always offer something when a guest arrives.', 'Ken offers to carry you, too. Imagine him offering this. You can choose whether or not to accept in your mind.', 'The South Dog <kanji>offer</kanji>s you the services of <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai as you traverse his lands.', 'N1', NULL),
(1631, 'Imagine being sick. Like sick as a dog sick. And then you take some charcoal, put it into your drinking water, and then you''re healed!', 'Picture yourself rowing your row boat into the lake with huge chunks of solid charcoal. You carefully break them up and toss them over the side, so you can heal everyone.', 'You aren''t the only one that needs to be <kanji>heal</kanji>ed, so you take your <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat out onto the lake where your town gets its drinking water and you dump a bunch of charcoal into it! Now everyone will be healed when they drink.', 'N2', NULL),
(1632, 'Picture this lion surrounded by the thousands of grains of rice he''ll inherit from his parents. He''s carefully threading them all together.', 'I''d much rather inherit a cake than a ton of hard to keep track of rice, like this poor lion.', 'If you''re going to <kanji>inherit</kanji> something from your parents, try to convince them to make it <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい), not rice. A single cake is much easier to keep track of and probably tastier (though it will be super stale by the time they die).', 'N1', NULL),
(1633, 'Imagine holding onto a thread that is attached to a turkey. Feel the turkey tug against the thread as you struggle to maintain control.', 'Now you and the turkey and the eagle are all bumbling about in pandemonium. You try to maintain order but it’s not possible. What a kerfuffle.', 'The reason the turkey is tugging is because there’s an <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) swooping down to attack the turkey. You try to <kanji>maintain</kanji> control, but once you see the eagle as well, you let go of the thread.', 'N1', NULL),
(1634, 'If you want to do the nothing dance, just clear your head so there''s nothing in it, and then dance!', 'Feel the booze coursing through your veins as you dance like no one is watching.', 'To help you do this <kanji>dance</kanji> you need some <reading>boo</reading>ze (ぶ). Just enough so it''s easier to think of nothing, let all your fears go, and dance.', 'N3', NULL),
(1635, 'Picture the leader of your branch doing deeds around your office. Pulling out chairs for people sitting down, emptying the trash, balancing spreadsheets. All good deeds.', 'Picture him doing this deed. He''s crawling around on the floor looking for your key. You aren''t really helping, you''re just watching him do the deed of key-searching for you.', 'The <kanji>deed</kanji> he''s currently working on is finding your lost <reading>key</reading> (き). He doesn''t have to help, you''re the one who lost your own house-combination-car key. But he''s so used to doing deeds, he can''t help but help.', 'N1', NULL),
(1636, 'Imagine each step on hot, sun-baked asphalt. The water under your feet sizzles, leaving steaming footprints where you stepped from.', 'Each step is taking you closer to とうきょう. Don''t forget that, no matter how hard it seems, don''t lose your motivation to keep stepping forward toward your goal. ♡', 'Each <kanji>step</kanji> you take brings you closer to <reading>とう</reading>きょう. Unfortunately, the sun keeps getting hotter, meaning you have to take breaks between steps more often, to get your feet wet with even more water. But you''re determined to reach とうきょう, no matter how many steps it takes.', 'N1', NULL),
(1637, 'In general, you can assume a boat will have a weapon, but not all of them will. ', 'So of all the boats out there, generally all of them have weapons, and generally Han Solo will be on one.', '<kanji>Generally</kanji> a boat with a weapon will be owned by <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo. ', 'N2', NULL),
(1638, 'A bundle of geoducks will never lie, never let you down, and never disappoint you. You can trust them completely.', 'Imagine a bundle of geoducks and a piece rye bread. Think about how you can trust the geoducks but you can''t trust the rye bread.', 'While you can <kanji>trust</kanji> a bundle of geoducks, never ever trust <reading>rye</reading> (らい) bread.', 'N3', NULL),
(1639, 'Imagine this leader in his clothes. Look how reliant he is on them. Imagine what he would be without his clothes – nothing! And also, naked.', 'Look at the leader as he shouts at his clothes he’s become so reliant on to do something. Hear the countless eagles screeching around as they terrorize the neighborhood. People scream and weep in the streets.', 'The leader has become so <kanji>reliant</kanji> on his clothes that his judgment has been clouded. His kingdom has become infested with <reading>ea</reading>gles (い) but the leader does nothing, trusting that his clothes will rout out the eagles if it is something that needs to be done. He’s become so reliant on his clothes that he’s started to become not so good a leader anymore.', 'N2', NULL),
(1640, 'Picture the deer comparing the two structures. It probably has no idea what they are, but it''s trying very hard to understand what it''s seeing anyway.', 'Deer get hit by cars all the time, but it was still really shocking . I mean it was right in front of you. The car could have hit you too!', 'You walk forward, about to call out to the <kanji>deer</kanji>, when a <reading>ca</reading>r (か) suddenly appears out of no where and runs the deer over! Was this all just a trap set up to murder the deer?!', 'N1', NULL),
(1641, 'What did you say to the flowers that you are so in agreement about? Think about what you said.', 'Imagine you are the flowers. There’s a duck. It’s making a poo. On you. Ew, man, that’s disgusting. Bleh.', 'You and the flowers are in <kanji>agreement</kanji> on the fact that neither of you like when a <reading>duck</reading> (だく) walks across the flowers. You don’t like having a duck walk on the flowers because the duck will poo in the flowers and then you have to worry about stepping in it. And the flowers just don’t like having duck poo in their face.', 'N1', NULL),
(1707, 'You''re going for an ultra run, calling out with your super loud voice right down the middle of the street.', 'Mrs. Chou is super ultra scary. And if you don''t kick it into super ultra high gear she''s going to catch up to you and make you super ultra dead.', 'You''re just becoming <kanji>ultra</kanji> tired when you hear a terrifying sound. It''s Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう) barreling down toward you. "WERE YOU CALLING FOR ME, CHILD?!" she screams.', 'N2', NULL),
(2336, 'Imagine a tsunami crashing over the land, being blamed by everyone. In its path are piles and piles of pickles, as far as the eye can see! ', 'Picture the two pickles and imagine what kind of pickles they are.', 'Looking at all those <kanji>pickle</kanji>s, you bend down and pick up <reading>two</reading> (つ) of them. One in each hand. You look at the pickles in your hand and wonder whether to try one.', 'N1', NULL),
(2404, 'Your parents didn''t want you having those things, which is why they put them up high, but your aunt and uncle would always spoil you and get them for you.', 'You aren''t supposed to wear your obi unless it''s a special occasion, but your aunt and uncle don''t know that. Now you can run around with it on your head and your parents can''t stop you by putting it up high!', 'You ask your <kanji>aunt</kanji> and <kanji>uncle</kanji> to help you reach your special <reading>o</reading>bi (お) from high up in the closet. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2447, 'Really feel the courage welling up inside of you in the face of this terrifying tsunami. Connect it to some physical feeling—perhaps you can feel it welling up in your chest.', 'Who wells up with courage? You do!', 'Whom does that courage <kanji>well up</kanji> inside of? <reading>You</reading> (ゆう)! In fact, YOU feel like there is a never ending well of courage bursting out of you now. You are going to be a hero!', 'N1', NULL),
(1643, 'Imagine your foot hearing this omen, "If you step on a crack, you''ll break your mother''s back." Now your foot hops over all of the tiny cracks in the street and the sidewalk and you look really silly.', 'Now you''re hopping away in fear from Mrs. Chou!', 'As you hop you start to hear an awful scream coming down the street. It''s Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう) and she''s running straight for you yelling, "STEP ON MY STREET''S CRACKS! STOP HOPPING OR YOUR MOTHER WILL NEVER BREAK HER BACK!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1644, 'The sun doesn''t have a voice, unfortunately, so it calls out the only way it can, with its shining rays.', 'Picture the Shougun standing in the shining sunlight. He''s so bright and beautiful!', 'This <kanji>shining</kanji> is aimed directly at the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). The sun is shining on the Shougun to not only show its support for his leadership, but to let him shine brighter than anyone else in Japan.', 'N1', NULL),
(1645, 'Imagine going fishing for fish in a fishing spot after a tsunami. There are SO many fish here. Your hook barely touches the water and there’s already a fish on it. Man, fishing like this is easy! Smell the smell of fish and sea air around you.', 'Go! Go row! Go and row for the fishing! How do you go? You row! The row part should be pretty easy to remember since you gotta row your boat out to sea, but if you’re having trouble with the go part, just remember that it has the same reading as 魚.', 'To get to the best <kanji>fishing</kanji> spot you need to <reading>go</reading> (ぎょ) <reading>row</reading> (りょう) out to the middle of the ocean. You go by means of rowing. To get to the fish and do the fishing, you need to go and row out to sea.', 'N2', NULL),
(1646, 'Imagine receiving your reparation prize. Feel the prize in your hand. It’s all wrapped up but it’s heavy and feels expensive. What could it be?', 'Imagine receiving your reparations from the Shougun himself. You''re angry at him for threatening you, wounding people, and cutting off the heads of people who go against him, but you take it anyway.', 'The leader who presented you with this <kanji>reparation</kanji> is the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) of Japan. He''s trying to make up for all the threats he made to you and some of the things you saw (which he''s now trying to cover up for).', 'N1', NULL),
(1647, 'Even if it was your own lantern, why stab it with a knife? You could have recycled that. I will punish you for lantern stabbing and pollution!', 'The next time you want to stab a lantern with a knife, think about how good cake tastes and stop. Remember, DARE (Don''t Attack Reusable Electronics)!', 'I will <kanji>punish</kanji> you by taking away your <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい)! You''ll never eat cake again. Not for birthdays, not for holidays. This is your punishment.', 'N1', NULL),
(1648, 'Imagine what you had to do to recruit this greenhouse. Imagine schmoozing with the previous greenhouse owners to recruit them to your cause and sell you the greenhouse.', 'Recruit Bobo the clown. Look at him. He’s terrifying. Bobo will make for a great deterrent for entry to your greenhouse.', 'After you <kanji>recruit</kanji> the greenhouse, you recruit <reading>Bo</reading>bo (ぼ) the clown to guard it for you. Bobo the clown has a terrifying amount of power so he’ll make an excellent guard for the greenhouse.', 'N2', NULL),
(1649, 'Be tenacious about happiness in your life. No matter how many times it slips away, don''t give up. Keep going after it, because it will absolutely come back if you''re tenacious enough.', 'Will someone take the shoe sometimes? Yeah, but the shih tzu doesn''t give up. That''s how you need to be with happiness. Kids, be tenacious!', 'Happiness is as <kanji>tenacious</kanji> as a <reading>shih tzu</reading> (しつ) with a <reading>shoe</reading> (しゅう). That little dog does not want to give up on the shoe, no matter what''s happening around it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1650, 'Picture a serene rice paddy. Now watch the whole thing explode, rice and dirt going everywhere, and a secret, underground baseball base is left there, smoldering.', 'Hold this ruined baseball base in your hands. Hey, maybe it''s a collectible?', 'Unfortunately, thanks to the explosion, the <kanji>base</kanji> is in <reading>rui</reading>ns (るい). It''s dark and smokey and kinda half burned. You probably can''t get this in a regulation game.', 'N1', NULL),
(1651, 'Watch as the moons crumble together, and then the mountain slowly crumbles down into them too.', 'Your home crumbles like a dry cookie until it''s nothing more than a bunch of dust, mixed in with the mountain and the moon. Now where will you live?', 'As the mountain <kanji>crumble</kanji>s you see your <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう), which is at the very top of the mountain, come crumbling down too.', 'N1', NULL),
(1652, 'You did not wish to be afflicted as such. Feel the skewer in your heart and the great pain it gives you. You know you’re dying.', 'You look on in horror as the affliction grabs hold and everything starts to fade, but Genghis Khan still floats before you, watching you closely.', 'Since you’re <kanji>afflicted</kanji> with the death, you start to hallucinate and the ghost of Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) appears before you. "Ah, I was afflicted of the same disease. It killed me, you know."', 'N2', NULL),
(1653, 'Heft this big door in your arms. It’s very heavy. Feel your arms tire as you look for who to return the door to.', 'You’re obsessed with returning this big door. Once you enter this mode, the only way to get out is to return the thing you have.', 'You are in <kanji>return</kanji> <reading>mode</reading> (もど). When you enter this mode, you concentrate only on returning something. Nothing else matters in this mode. You’re in full on return mode.', 'N3', NULL),
(1654, 'They weren''t listening, which is why you slammed that lid down. You''ll have to confront them more directly.', 'こういち is laughing at you as you confront him. Which makes you angrier, so you bang the lid on the table some more.', 'You are trying to <kanji>confront</kanji> Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち. You''re confronting him about all the kanji he''s forcing you, and everyone else, to learn. "Look here こういち! I know you said I''d learn like 2000 kanji in no time, but this is way too efficient! What will the people do when they''re done? Do you think they will sit idly by?"', 'N1', NULL),
(1655, 'Imagine your entire clan putting their fingers in the ground as a form of resistance. It''s a moving sight.', 'You make your choice to resist. He orders his guards to pull your fingers out of the ground, chop them into the size of tater tots, and then deep fry them. After they''re done, the lord pops one in his mouth and says, "Oooh, resistance is delicious."', 'You''re all <kanji>resist</kanji>ing with your fingers in the ground, when the lords appear, angry. "You resist us by hiding your fingers with potatoes in the ground?! You want your fingers to be useless potatoes so badly? We''ll chop them up and turn them into <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tots!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1656, 'You''re welcome. Now you know how to keep everything in season. Lifehack!', 'Joke''s on them though. Soon it will be apple season. Soon it will always be time for apples. Soon June will end and people will forget about everything else, living forever inside that period of ten days when apples are most in season.', 'When things are <kanji>in season</kanji> for more than <kanji>ten days</kanji>, people are going to wonder what''s going on. When''s Christmas? When''s Golden Week? <reading>Soon</reading> (しゅん), you say. Soon enough, it''s <reading>June</reading> (じゅん). Which is the middle of the year, so now everything is soon.', 'N1', NULL),
(1657, 'Picture these extremely violent tsunamis hitting a shore again and again, turning it into a bow-shaped gulf. That''s how gulfs are created in this part of the world!', 'Many tsunamis, again and again, for just one gulf? Yep, that''s just how life goes. Gulfs are in high demand, but you''ll need many tsunamis to create just one.', 'How many <kanji>gulf</kanji>s will this create? Just <reading>one</reading> (わん).', 'N2', NULL),
(1658, 'Imagine the fire explode. Feel the heat of the fire and the explosion. It’s very violent. BOOM BOOM BA-BOOM!', 'Imagine yourself as you back away from the explosion. As you back away, see the fire dance and feel the heat of the explosion on your face.', 'As you watch the fire <kanji>explode</kanji>, you start to <reading>back</reading> (ばく) away. I don’t blame you. You need to back away or else you’ll get caught in the violent explosion. Back away a little more just to be safe.', 'N2', NULL),
(1659, 'Imagine how simple life would be if we replaced all the bows in the world with bullets. (Insert sarcastic crying face here.)', 'Imagine shooting bullets into the sun at dawn. Pow pow, take that history! Blam blam, take that dawn!', 'If you want to shoot <kanji>bullet</kanji>s, you need to do it at <reading>dawn</reading> (だん). Back in the old days, it wasn''t bright enough to shoot arrows at dawn, but you can shoot bullets at dawn because they''re simpler to use!', 'N1', NULL),
(1660, 'Imagine the whole process of sound going through your ear and to your heart, then the gears turning to really understand what someone is saying. It''s a real listen, not a fake non-heart listen.', 'Focus on listening, and focus on their message about Mrs. Chou. Feel afraid about the future because of it.', 'You are <kanji>listen</kanji>ing so well to what someone is saying. What are they telling you that is so important? They are telling you of the omen of Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう), who will destroy the earth.', 'N1', NULL),
(1661, 'Picture yourself stepping around with your foot again and again, leaving traces all over the place. Anyone would be able to trace you like this, but you just love a good little stomping around.', 'You''re absolutely surrounded by all these people attracted to your sexy stomping around with your foot, again and again. Look, no shaming here, whatever floats your boat.', 'Turns out you leaving <kanji>traces</kanji> everywhere is quite <reading>sexy</reading> (せき) to a lot of people.', 'N2', NULL),
(1662, 'Imagine the conversation with emergency services. They say they will dispatch an ambulance immediately.', 'He takes out a very tiny katana and performs surgery right there. The insect dies on the scooter. "It looks like I''ve dispatched this insect to the afterlife."', 'Out of the <kanji>dispatch</kanji>ed ambulance jumps <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai. "Dispatch sent me! I''m here to fix that insect."', 'N1', NULL),
(1663, 'Imagine having to fight against the bean bureaucracy. It''s so absurd! Who can say a bean is too big or too small, in this age of diversity?! Your fighting pays off though, and they let you squeeze your beans through the gate. Well fought, bean brethren. Well fought.', 'How are you supposed to get your beans into とうきょう if a fight breaks out at the gate every time? What a struggle. Fortunately those bean-counting bureaucrats of とうきょう were no match for your fight.', 'The gate you put up a <kanji>fight</kanji> at leads to <reading>とう</reading>きょう. The people of とうきょう need those beans for sustenance, and that''s why you fight — for them!', 'N1', NULL),
(1664, 'Picture an army camp housing all of the different cars the army needs at any given time.', 'Jeans are pretty durable, now that you think about it. And if the soldiers are working on these cars in the camp while they''re there, it makes sense to wear a nice pair of jeans.', 'All of the people in the <kanji>army camp</kanji> are wearing <reading>jean</reading>s (じん). You thought soldiers were supposed to wear uniforms, but it looks like when they''re in the camp they have to wear jeans.', 'N1', NULL),
(1665, 'Imagine this warm, grain fragrance. What does it smell like to you? Cooked rice? Gentle flowers? A warm summer''s day?', 'This fragrance was こういち''s armpits the whole time. You thought it was warm grains. Or... maybe he smells like... grains?', 'As you sniff this lovely <kanji>fragrance</kanji> you get lost in the smell. When you open your eyes again, you see a farming <reading>こう</reading>いち standing in front of you. With... his... arm... up. "Do you like my fragrance? I was genetically modified to have a unique fragrance compared to the other こういちs."', 'N2', NULL),
(1666, 'If you know the omen radical, you know the omen kanji as well!', 'You can clarify with the person telling you the omen several things: "Do you believe in the omen about Mrs. Chou destroying the world?" "When will this omen about Mrs. Chou come true?" etc. As long as you''re associating omen with Mrs. Chou you should be good, so keep harping on that.

Anyway, whenever you hear any kind of omen (omen is a radical in the kanji), there''s a good chance it''s going to be about Mrs. Chou.', 'You listen to the <kanji>omen</kanji>, in it, it tells of an event in the future when Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう) will destroy the world.', 'N2', NULL),
(1667, 'Imagine your servant as he looks to your gun and products. You can always look to him to take care of your menial tasks. You feel smug.', 'Imagine your servant as he looks to your car’s o-ring. Hear him clinking and clanking around in the garage as he does so.', 'After your servant is done with your gun and other products you want him to <kanji>look to</kanji> your car’s o-<reading>rin</reading>g (りん). There’s something wrong with the o-ring so you want your servant to look to that as well.', 'N1', NULL),
(1668, 'Imagine the triceratops with its little moon knife, doing some whittling. Hand it anything and it will whittle it down.', 'Check out the sack that holds all the things that the triceratops whittled down. Go ahead and look through it, it''s quite filled.', 'Everything it will <kanji>whittle down</kanji> shall be put in a <reading>sack</reading> (さく).', 'N1', NULL),
(1669, 'Imagine holding up your sword and pledging to protect the lives of everyone in this city. It''s a big deal!', 'Would you pledge to give up cake to show everyone how serious you are about protecting life?', 'Until the city is safe, you <kanji>pledge</kanji> to give up <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい), your favorite food.', 'N1', NULL),
(1670, 'This is a pretty big favor. Imagine running through a rice paddy carrying a cross with a warm beating heart on it. Thump thump. Thump thump.', 'Imagine how dumb you feel at this miscommunication. Eh? You don’t feel dumb? Well you should!', 'You arrive at your friend’s place and you can finally return the <kanji>favor</kanji> you owe him by presenting him with the heart. “<reading>Eh</reading> (え)? What’s this? I didn’t ask for this!” “Eh!?!?” you reply. Apparently he didn’t want a real heart, he wanted a candy one. He wanted a Valentine!', 'N1', NULL),
(1671, 'Picture it like you''re a baby who is new to hugs. You start with your fingers, spread them out and move forward. Now wrap all that stuff attached to your fingers around another person. You did it! You''re hugging now!', 'Imagine getting a nice, big hug from your dad. Mm. It''s cozy and feels like home.', 'Who do you love to <kanji>hug</kanji> the most? Your <reading>da</reading>d (だ)! He gives the best hugs. They''re warm and big and tight and comforting. The kind of hug dads are really good at giving.', 'N3', NULL),
(1672, 'Picture the sun reflecting off your metal prison spoon as you hold it aloft in your fingers. It hurts your eyes a little, but it also draws everyone''s attention to this spoon you want to display.', 'It''s gotta be a spoon because forks are too pointy and dangerous for prison, by the way. So imagine eating your cake with the spoon you just displayed, big spoonfuls of cake frosting filling your mouth with every bite.', 'The reason you <kanji>display</kanji> it is to get some <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい). In this prison, if you display your spoon deftly enough, the wardens bring you some cake as a reward.', 'N1', NULL),
(1673, 'It''s much easier to aim for an animal wearing a top hat than one without.', 'Your aim with the soap is perfect! The soap knocked the animal''s hat right off.', 'Ready, steady, <kanji>aim</kanji>... fire! You throw a bar of <reading>so</reading>ap (そ) at the animal. Yes, soap. You were sitting in your bathtub, washing and scrubbing, then the animal in the top hat caught your eye.', 'N1', NULL),
(1674, 'Give a yakuza a spoon and you always get 死 (death), in this case twenty times. So it''s honestly not surprising that things would end with commemorative flowers and a burial.', 'I guess the twenty souls couldn''t go up to... wherever... until they had a proper burial.', 'Once the <kanji>burial</kanji> is complete the <reading>sou</reading>ls (そう) of all the dead people come floating up and out of the ground. "Thaaannk YooOoOO~" they moan, as they ascend to the sky.', 'N1', NULL),
(2384, 'Picture feeding some thread into the mouth of a worm as the light from the full moon shines in on you. See the silk come out of the worm''s butt and into your hands.', 'Picture yourself wearing nothing but silk. Silk shirt, silk pants, even silk underwear. It feels great against the skin! And most importantly, you''re not nude.', 'Wearing <kanji>silk</kanji> is the <reading>key</reading> (き) to not being <reading>nude</reading> (ぬ). Wear it!', 'N1', NULL),
(8862, 'Look at yourself in the mirror. You can see the stick holding up the flag and it''s very clear you drew some eyebrows onto the flag with some markers, but you still look pretty good (in your opinion).', 'Imagine walking around town, holding your eyebrow flags. You can feel the meat sticking above your eyes. They stink and they''re weirdly lukewarm, but you know your real eyebrows will grow under them any day now.', 'Underneath your fake <kanji>eyebrows</kanji> are two slabs of <reading>me</reading>at (み). Meat helps hair grow, according to many Internet specialists who also happen to sell meat. You''re waiting for this meat to foster eyebrow hair, and when that sweet day comes you won''t need these flags.', 'N1', NULL),
(1675, 'Go out into the rain and shake your rake. Imagine shouting your demand at the sky as the rain pelts you from above.', 'Picture yourself demanding more rain from the sky, trying to fill up your tiny juice box with what little juice rain you were able to get.', 'Now imagine the rain changing. Wait a second, this isn''t rain, it''s <reading>ju</reading>ice (じゅ)! You shake your rake harder, and <kanji>demand</kanji> that the juice rain continues. It doesn''t listen to your demands and stops as soon as you start bottling it. Well, at least you have enough for one, really small juice box. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1676, 'You get baby age tooth orders (to get your first teeth). You get child age tooth orders (to get your adult teeth). Then you get old age tooth orders (when you start losing your teeth). There are tooth orders for every age.', 'Decide what age you have to be to get your first ray gun, and then imagine getting it when you hit that age. Perhaps you use this ray gun on your teeth, to keep them clean.

Hint: when you see the radical <radical>orders</radical> it''s fairly likely the reading will be れい.', 'Gaining and losing teeth aren''t the only things dependent on <kanji>age</kanji>. There is also an age in which you get your first <reading>ray</reading> (れい) gun.', 'N2', NULL),
(1677, 'Giving your best regards is basically just tipping your hat and showing the roof of it.', 'YOLO means "you only live once," so in a way that''s a good way to give your best regards. At the very least, it''s an interesting way.', 'When you give your <kanji>best regards</kanji> you always yell <reading>YOLO</reading> (よろ) to the other person.', 'N1', NULL),
(1678, 'Imagine carefully dripping syrup onto a thread and then spinning it in a circle.', 'Imagine eating a syrup cookie you made by spinning it yourself. Homemade is always better than store-bought.', 'As you <kanji>spin</kanji> the syrup, it begins to change shape and forms into a <reading>coo</reading>kie (く)! Haven''t you ever made syrup-spun cookies before? The spinning forms and cooks them in the air.', 'N1', NULL),
(1679, 'Imagine riding your scooter, dodging multiple hot peppers as they''re tossed at you. A few of them get stuck in the wheels and keep coming around and around, and you have to dodge them too.', 'Feel the heat flow through your body. It''s getting harder to dodge as the heat grows and you start sweating!', 'As you <kanji>dodge</kanji> the hot peppers, you feel a strong <reading>he</reading>at (ひ) fill your body. You can''t tell if this heat is from the effort from dodging or if you didn''t dodge right and one of the hot peppers touched you. Or maybe the scooter is overheating.', 'N1', NULL),
(1680, 'The king radical is slightly altered in this kanji, so just remember that the king is a strange alien and not human. Imagine being this woman and having this half-breed alien baby inside you. Feels weird.', 'What game are you playing on the Nintendo? Maybe it’s a game where you kill alien invaders? That’s messed up.', 'Being <kanji>pregnant</kanji> with this strange creature, you need to do something to calm you down. You should play <reading>Nin</reading>tendo (にん). Nintendo really calms your nerves and makes being pregnant with some mutant baby not seem like that big of a deal anymore.', 'N1', NULL),
(1681, 'Picture an ultrasound of the pregnant woman, but instead of a little baby heartbeat, you hear a scary roaring sound. That''s what being pregnant is like. Waiting for the landslide to come.', 'Imagine being pregnant and getting kicked by the shins of your baby all day, but especially all night. You''re so tired. Please let this landslide come already.', 'When you''re <kanji>pregnant</kanji> the baby is constantly kicking you with their <reading>shin</reading>s (しん). Not their feet, their shins. And shins are really hard, let me tell you.', 'N1', NULL),
(1682, 'They do most of their own chores during the rest of the year, so winter is when you should ask them to do things for you.', 'Moles will do anything for cheese, but honestly, wouldn''t we all?', 'To get the mole to <kanji>do</kanji> what you want, just give it a nice big hunk of <reading>chee</reading>se (ち).', 'N1', NULL),
(1683, 'You''re really into this magazine, and when you heard there''s a new edition of it, you went out and bought it right away.', 'The article on Genghis Khan was perhaps the best written article in the Dry Knife series to date. How did this magazine get such a big interview?', 'In this <kanji>edition</kanji> of "The Dry Knife," there is a huge article written by Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) on how he kept his knives dry while campaigning.', 'N2', NULL),
(1684, 'Close your eyes and imagine all of the spring sounds that come down to you from heaven in the springtime. Mmm, you can almost smell the damp soil and rain.', 'Picture all of the souls in heaven playing spring music together!', 'In heaven, the ones that <kanji>play music</kanji> are the <reading>sou</reading>ls (そう) of the dead. They have a huge band up there, and even though it just seems like normal spring sounds to you, up there in heaven when the souls play music it''s more like a huge, 100-part band!', 'N1', NULL),
(1685, 'Imagine you are one of the leader’s men who volunteered. What made you want to accompany him? Imagine the good qualities of this leader and how he inspired you to accompany him on this mission.', 'Imagine yourself as you accompany Han Solo. You try to stay calm, but you’re just so excited to be around Han Solo.', 'The leader you decided to <kanji>accompany</kanji> is none other than <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo. Who could be more inspiring? I’m surprised not more than half decided to accompany him. Han Solo is awesome.', 'N1', NULL),
(1686, 'Imagine this shady leader approaching you in the night. The lantern light shines in your face. Would you join him?', 'The last thing you wanted to do was join that guy so you hid in a haystack to avoid him.', 'The leader guy approaches you out of the darkness, his lantern held high. Oh no, not that guy. You''ve heard about his horn problem and you don''t want to <kanji>join</kanji> him. You look around and spot a large <reading>hay</reading>stack (へい). You quickly jump into it and hide from him.', 'N1', NULL),
(1687, 'Why do you want this geoduck to lean? Try to come up with your own reason. And why don''t you look up what a geoduck looks like one more time for good measure. You don''t want to be able to get this image out of your head.', 'Watch as the geoduck slowly leans toward the cake, trying to get at it for a taste. But don''t let it eat any! Keep putting the cake further and further away so the geoduck has to lean more.', 'To get the geoduck to <kanji>lean</kanji> you put a piece of <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい) on one of its sides.', 'N2', NULL),
(1688, 'Look at this stamp. There''s Columbus being hugged by a bunch of South American natives. Yikes, that''s contrary to how things went, that''s for sure.', 'Be contrary to the evildoers of the past and be like Kyaku instead.', 'Don''t be the type of person who acts <kanji>contrary</kanji> to how they should and instead be like <reading>Kyaku</reading> (きゃく) the customer. They''re always so nice and polite and they don''t give diseases to entire populations of people.', 'N1', NULL),
(1689, 'Imagine being in the interior of someone’s tummy. See the big pieces of rice all around you as drops of stomach fluid continue to drop down on your head.', 'If you’re unfamiliar with this Oklahoma song, check it out on YouTube. Imagine being in the interior of someone’s belly as you hear this song booming all around you.', 'While you’re in the <kanji>interior</kanji> of this guy, you hear him break out in song. He starts singing “<reading>Ok</reading>lahoma” (おく) at the top of his lungs. The big pieces of rice slosh around as he sings and dances. “OOOOk-lahoma, where the wind comes sweepin'' down the plain!”', 'N2', NULL),
(1690, 'Look at this tiger as it approaches you. Think what things it has to consider. It has to consider how big of a threat you are and so on. And you need to consider how you''re going to escape from this tiger.', 'Imagine your relief when you realize this tiger isn''t considering you as prey, but is considering whether to visit Rio de Janeiro and just wants your advice. There are no tigers in South America, so I guess it''s natural for one to consider Rio in its vacation plans. ', 'Finally, the tiger speaks: "I''m <kanji>consider</kanji>ing going to <reading>Rio</reading> (りょ). Have you ever been?"', 'N1', NULL),
(1691, 'You see the suspended heart in the distance, and make your way towards it.', '"I''ve protected this suspended heart for you, for you are next in the lineage to receive it." He grabs the heart and throws it into your body.', 'You arrive at the <kanji>suspend</kanji>ed heart. There in front of it, protecting it, is <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1692, 'Imagine seeing all these doors, this cluster of doors. You feel confused at which one to enter but finally pick one. Hear the door creak as you open it.', 'Watch as clusters of pins fall when the bowling balls hit them. You back up and peek through one of the other doors in the cluster and it also houses a bowling alley. Huh.', 'After you make it through the door, you come across another <kanji>cluster</kanji> of doors. Which one to choose? There''s a loud thunderous sound coming from behind one and you decide to open it. Behind it, there''s an entire <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう) alley and people bowling, loudly!', 'N1', NULL),
(1693, 'Imagine being on an escalator and wrapping your fingers around a small handle on your way up or down. It''s still a bit precarious, truth be told.', '暑か? Is the handle hot? Think about it. Is it? What does your imagination tell you?', 'You put your hand on the <kanji>handle</kanji> and an old person leans in close to you and says, "<reading>暑か</reading>?" (あつか). He wants to know if the handle is ... hot? Before you can respond he says it again, and again! “暑か？！ 暑か？！？！ 暑か？！？！？！” he''s shouting as you slowly ride the escalator away from him.', 'N1', NULL),
(1694, 'Imagine suppressing a cat pirate with your own fingers. His fur is so soft, but his nails are so sharp!', 'Defend the yolk! Smell the smell of eggs in the air as you suppress the cat pirate. Maybe even share the yolk with him, once he''s done trying to kill you for it. And then enjoy breakfast while petting your new cat friend.', 'He forgot, but you know you had to <kanji>suppress</kanji> the cat pirate because he was trying to steal your egg <reading>yolk</reading> (よく). You were making breakfast on your ship and the cat pirate boarded you out of nowhere, and tried to take it by force.', 'N1', NULL),
(1695, 'Feel your fingers touching each and every shrimp, one by one, until they graze the one you select as the perfect shrimp. Slowly pull it out and lower it into your mouth. Munch munch... ahhh! The perfect shrimp.', 'Oh, woe is me! How will I select the perfect shrimp with my fingers if there are tacks in the shrimp bowls! Boohoohoo!', 'You do this at every restaurant, party, and indoor sporting event and people start getting wise to your shrimp <kanji>select</kanji>ion methods. One day, as you attempt to select your shrimp, a sharp pain shoots through your finger. Ow! You pull your hand back to find... a <reading>tack</reading> (たく)!', 'N1', NULL),
(1696, 'Imagine using your fingers to draw these rice paddy flowers. Don''t worry about how well you can draw. These flowers will look beautiful no matter how you draw them.', 'To be fair, would you want to look at a drawing that makes you constantly smell stinky B.O.? I didn''t think so.', 'Even though you tried to <kanji>draw</kanji> the flowers as best you could, whenever someone looks at it they smell <reading>B.O.</reading> (びょう). Maybe it''s because you were drawing for a really long time and kept sweating. Maybe it''s because you are just a stinky person. But no one wants to look at your drawing for long because all they can smell is B.O.', 'N1', NULL),
(1697, 'A big boat shaped like a plate is just a tray. You keep your weapons on it, nice and flat.', 'Your ban from the Small Boat Tray Association has led to a massive feud between large boat tray enthusiasts and small boat tray enthusiasts, and it seems like it''ll never end—leading to bans all over the place. If only we could all come together as enthusiasts of boat trays of all sizes...', 'Unfortunately, this boat <kanji>tray</kanji> is so huge, it''s gotten you <reading>ban</reading>ned (ばん) from the Small Boat Tray Association. Those pesky small boat tray enthusiasts...', 'N1', NULL),
(1698, 'This isn''t exactly a title you''re proud of, in fact, you''re really embarrassed. Maybe you can make a name for yourself in something else so you can get a new title.', 'The plaque says, "No one is more deserving of this SAD title." - Love, the Shougun.', 'Word of your SAD <kanji>title</kanji> makes it all the way to the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) and he travels out to see you. When he arrives at your door, with all of his fancy escorts, he hands you a plaque with your title on it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1699, 'Imagine taking that cord and wrapping it around someone (with their permission, of course). Feel your fingers tightening a knot in the cord. Then tie the other end around yourself. Is this some freaky ritual to deepen your connection to them, or… ? Hey, no judgment from me. ', 'Imagine doing this cord show on stage. Feel the heat of the stage lights and the gaze of the audience as your tie your cord. What are they expecting? Why did they pay to see this? Must be some kind of modern performance art — let''s go with that.', 'Actually, you''re tying this <kanji>cord</kanji> around someone as part of a <reading>show</reading> (しょ). What kind of a show? I really shouldn''t say. This cord show isn''t free, I''ll tell you that much.', 'N3', NULL),
(1700, 'Look at this football. The thread is loose on the football and needs to be tightened. Attempt to tighten the loose thread on the football as you hold it in your hands.', 'Listen to your friends shouting “You rule!” and hear their cheers echo in your ears as you toss the football around again.', 'You finally tighten the <kanji>loose</kanji> thread and all of your friends cheer, “<reading>You ru</reading>le!” (ゆる) You rule because you fixed the loose thread on the football and now you can play again. Yeah! You rule, man!', 'N1', NULL),
(1701, 'Imagine what seven things you consign over to this other person for safekeeping. Visualize them in your head as you slide each one down the slide.', 'Let’s imagine what it would be like to hit this tack while sliding on the slide. It would hurt. Good thing you are consigning it over to someone. Probably safer for everyone that way.', 'One of the seven things you <kanji>consign</kanji> over to this guy is a <reading>tack</reading> (たく). It’s the sharpest tack you’ve ever seen. You slide the tack down the slide. Good thing no one is on the slide now, sitting on that tack would hurt.', 'N1', NULL),
(1702, 'Imagine bribing a bunch of yakuza and politicians with all the shellfish you have. Ohohoh, you''ll never be caught!', 'Yeah really, why? Why bribe people at all. You could have just been rolling in shellfish dough. Why risk everything?

And you counter with.

Why not?', 'Welp, the cops found out about your <kanji>bribe</kanji>, that didn''t last long, and now your entire family is watching you get put into the police car screaming, "<reading>WHY</reading> (わい)?!', 'N1', NULL),
(1704, 'You''re like beach santa, except you''re real and you wear a mask made of shellfish.', 'Ooh hoo, it''s me! the Shellfish Mask Man!
I go to beach houses and leave presents where I can!
The presents are not clothes, or anything good.
I don''t even care if you leave me some food.
Try whipping some up, try cooking me peas,
It matters not, I''ll leave you zombies!

Tee hee hee hee hee!', 'The "<kanji>presents</kanji>" you leave? They are actually just <reading>zo</reading>mbies (ぞう). You leave zombies in people''s houses.', 'N2', NULL),
(1705, 'Unfortunately, the omen is riding a scooter too, so you''re going to have to be crafty to escape this omen properly.', 'Imagine scooting in and out of とうきょう traffic, the omen looming ever further back behind you. You''re doing it! You''re escaping!', 'You decide to <kanji>escape</kanji> this omen by weaving in and out of the busy streets of <reading>とう</reading>きょう. By now, you know とうきょう like the back of your hand, so you''re sure you can escape this way.', 'N3', NULL),
(1706, 'You want to send back this zombie the way it came. You definitely don''t want it loose in your neighborhood.', 'You managed to send back the zombie, good for you! Genghis Khan would be proud.', 'As you prepare to <kanji>send back</kanji> the zombie, you imagine yourself as Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん). On your horse (it''s just your own scooter) you stab forward with your spear (stick), swing the scooter the zombie is on, and send it back from whence it came!', 'N1', NULL),
(1017, 'Tigers are pretty intimidating just on their own, let alone with spikey armor. Imagine this tiger, covered in spikes. It’s roaring at you. Pretty scary, huh?', 'Picture this grand match taking place in Little Kyoto. Actual tigers versus the Hanshin Tigers. It’s truly a sight to behold. The void tigers are armed with their spikes and the Hanshin Tigers are armed with their bats. Hear the crack of wood and the roar of tigers in the air as the battle commences.', 'The aforementioned <kanji>void</kanji> has opened up in Little <reading>Kyo</reading>to (きょ). The spiked void tigers chose to come here in order to face off against the Hanshin Tigers baseball team in battle. It will be a vicious match here in Little Kyoto. Since this is taking place in Little Kyoto and not Japan itself, the Hanshin Tigers won''t have the home field advantage. Who will win?', 'N1', NULL),
(1708, 'Imagine standing at the top of the building and looking down at the three slides that stretch off toward your home country. It takes a few hours to slide all the way there, but at the end you''ll be home.', 'Even if you move, even if you haven''t been back in years, your home country will always be your home in some way, deep down.', 'Your <kanji>home country</kanji> is your <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう)!', 'N1', NULL),
(1709, 'Imagine the gold, heavy in your pockets, as you construct this buzzer with your bare hands. Feel the cool metal clink around in your hands as you put it together.', 'Wait, should this buzzer be buzzing or ringing? Oh well, you got your gold. It’s the client’s problem now.', 'Finally the <kanji>buzzer</kanji> is done. You’ve earned your gold. You test the buzzer and it goes <reading>rin</reading>g (りん)! Ring! Ring! Ring! Ring ring ring ring ring! Man, that ringing is annoying but hey, that’s what buzzers do.', 'N1', NULL),
(1711, 'Picture this part of the mountain, branching off from the rest, into a mysterious path of its own.', 'If you branch off from the beaten path, you need 1) access, and 2) to be really careful so you keep your head.', 'If you want to <kanji>branch off</kanji> from your hiking group and explore this branch of the mountain, you need a special <reading>key</reading> (き). Just don''t get caught down there or you''ll get the <reading>gui</reading>llotine (ぎ).', 'N1', NULL),
(1712, 'Picture a dark, bleak winter landscape. No life anywhere. Now turn around and look at the prosperity on display in the building you''re in. Everyone is happy and eating food and not freezing to death.', 'Ryu''s prosperity has made him realize that having things isn''t always enough. Now he''s looking wistfully outside at the winter weather, missing the rough times.', 'This <kanji>prosperity</kanji> isn''t good for everyone though. You look over and see <reading>Ryu</reading> (りゅう) from Street Fighter sitting in the corner. He''s got everything he could ask for. He''s won the tournament, he''s got money, he''s got this nice big building, but now he''s depressed.', 'N1', NULL),
(1713, 'Imagine hundreds of turkeys walking in. The word has gotten out amongst the turkey community that if they walk through your door, you''ll employ them.', '"I will employ turkeys, but I will never employ children!" you exclaim.', 'Although you <kanji>employ</kanji> turkeys, when a <reading>子</reading> (こ) walks through your door, you send them away.', 'N2', NULL),
(1714, 'Abstaining from doing what other people are doing is important sometimes. So make sure you never forget the universal abstaining symbol.', 'Picture yourself in Central America. Standing in the water, blocking poachers from the hicatee turtles. "Abstain!" you shout at them.', 'This symbol to <kanji>abstain</kanji> comes from the <reading>hica</reading>tee (ひか) turtle. The hicatee is a Central American river turtle hunted for its meat, eggs, and shell. Therefore, the poor hicatee are endangered. So people started to rally around them, abstaining from hurting them anymore. They raised their fingers to the sky and shouted, "I abstain from harming these hicatee!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1715, 'Picture yourself walking around and seeing a wall around some dirt. You look inside and there are some hot peppers hiding just below the surface. Phew! Good thing this wall was here to protect you from them. ', 'Picture some corn cobs. Now picture some hot peppers. Put a wall between them.', 'On the other side of this <kanji>wall</kanji> there are much less threatening <reading>cob</reading>s (かべ) of corn. Corn isn''t spicy. It''s delicious and sweet, so it''s free from being walled in.', 'N1', NULL),
(1716, 'Imagine finding this tree crab in your games cabinet trying to make off with your chess piece. This tree crab is big and you weren’t expecting to find it in your games cabinet so you’re kind of terrified. You scream in horror.', 'You put the key in your pocket and call animal control. They unlock the cabinet with the key and remove the tree crab from your home. But who’s to say he won’t be back??', 'Before the tree crab can make it out with the <kanji>chess piece</kanji>, you shut the door to the games cabinet and lock it with a <reading>key</reading> (き), trapping the tree crab inside. Now what?', 'N1', NULL),
(1717, 'You''re reluctant to face this bitter end at the hands of a tsunami.', 'Try to stuff some of the jewels into your bitter mouth before the tsunami stops you.', 'With the <kanji>bitter</kanji> taste in your mouth, you <kanji>reluctant</kanji>ly gather up all of your <reading>jew</reading>els (じゅう) and wait for the tsunami to take you away. At least with your jewels with you, there may be some hope of selling them and building a better life if you make it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1718, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Imagine a one sided situation that you''ve had. Now imagine the winning side with a catapult. For example, a baseball game where one team is destroying the other. That''s because instead of a pitcher, they have a catapult.', 'Think of anything that is <kanji>one sided</kanji>. A battle. A sports game. A relationship. What makes them so one sided? One side always has a <reading>cata</reading>pult (かた).', 'N2', NULL),
(1719, 'Imagine following your sheep buddy back to his flock. Feel the soft fur of all the sheep around you as they baa peacefully.', 'Feel the recoil of the gun in your hands and the ringing of the shots in your ears as you slaughter the flock without remorse. BANG BANG BANG!', 'Now take out your <reading>gun</reading> (ぐん) and murder every sheep in the <kanji>flock</kanji>. Except for your buddy. You’re incredibly jealous of your buddy and you don’t like the attention his flock gives him so that’s why you decided to murder them all with a gun.', 'N2', NULL),
(1720, 'Imagine this odd hermit leading mountain goats around the mountain, marching to the beat of a drum. It’s a strange sight indeed.', 'Picture this old hermit fighting this centaur in a duel. Who do you think is gonna win? Hear the hermit screaming in agony as the centaur demolishes him.', 'One day the <kanji>hermit</kanji> was leading the mountain goats around and he ran into a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん). The centaur said that he was the rightful leader of these mountain goats so the centaur challenged the hermit to a duel.', 'N1', NULL),
(1721, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Picture all the jewels you were able to buy because you allocated your funds correctly. Imagine swimming in these jewels. Like a rich duck.', 'If you <kanji>allocate</kanji> your funds correctly you''ll be able to buy as many precious <reading>jew</reading>els (じゅう) as you want! However, jewels are expensive, so if you don''t allocate any money, you won''t be able to afford any. Ever.', 'N1', NULL),
(1722, 'Know your radicals, stop making excuses.', 'Imagine all the men you’ve talked to in your life. Think of every excuse they’ve told you. Think of the worst excuse you’ve ever heard. Remember that.', 'Who always makes <kanji>excuse</kanji> after excuse? <reading>Men</reading> (めん)! They have an excuse for everything. Why didn’t you take out the trash? Why did you forget my birthday? It’s just excuse after excuse with men. So I’ve heard, anyway.', 'N1', NULL),
(1724, 'On the map, there is a circle drawn around your mouth showing what range your mouth exists in. Thankfully, your tongue and teeth are within that sphere.', 'Everything else on the map was accurate, so you take a close look in the mirror, and there he is: a tiny Ken the samurai, protecting the range that is your mouth. He gives you a small nod and goes back to his post.', 'Looking at the <kanji>range</kanji> of your mouth on the map, you notice a tiny samurai marked near your molars. It is <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai.', 'N1', NULL),
(1727, 'Imagine this turkey preparing to rob a place. See him with the tape measure, and hear his little gobbles of excitement as he realizes the safe here is just the right size for robbing.', 'Picture this turkey with all the stuff he robbed, looking nervously over his shoulder from the back seat of his Uber.', 'Once he''s able to <kanji>rob</kanji> the place, he gets away in an <reading>Uber</reading> (うば). He just called the Uber in advance and had it wait outside. Hey, if you''re gonna rob a place, make sure you have an Uber.', 'N1', NULL),
(1728, 'Imagine feeling nervous as the honorable person pulls up at the stop sign. Hear yourself humbly asking for their stamp as you hand the written request to them. And just who is this honorable being, anyway?', 'Imagine the relief you''d feel as Godzilla hands the request back to you with its personal stamp — some kind of webbed claw print that basically blacks out the whole paper. Thank you, O honorable Godzilla.', 'The <kanji>honorable</kanji> personage is <reading>Go</reading>dzilla (ご). Actually, you''re requesting Godzilla''s stamp of approval for a new demolition job in downtown Tokyo. Godzilla is the god of urban demolition, what with all those cities it''s destroyed, and you need its honorable approval to continue.', 'N3', NULL),
(1729, 'Focus on the feeling of humility here. You''re putting it all out there, you know? With nowhere to hide.', 'Imagine humility literally moving through your shins. Can you still stand or do you need to fall to your shins, in humility?', 'As <kanji>humility</kanji> washes over you, you feel a shaking in your <reading>shin</reading>s (しん). Humility lives in your shins, so now they''re shaking and trying to bring you down to the ground.', 'N1', NULL),
(1730, 'Imagine holding up your fingers to show a giant you''re refusing to let him do something. Yeesh, you''re brave!', 'Imagine standing in front of a giant, refusing to let him crush Little Kyoto. I hope he doesn''t stomp on you instead.', 'What do you <kanji>refuse</kanji>? You refuse to let him stomp all over Little <reading>Kyo</reading>to (きょ). Little Kyoto is your home! And if the giant comes any closer he''s going to destroy all the little buildings and shrines and soba noodle shops.', 'N1', NULL),
(1731, 'Feel the tree bark under your palms as you climb up the tree to take a frame. You get a splinter from grabbing the frame. It really smarts.', 'Why is this frame so wack? Think about what makes it wack, to help you remember this reading.', 'You realize that the <kanji>frame</kanji> isn''t half as nice as you thought it was, in fact it''s totally <reading>wack</reading> (わく). How disappointing.', 'N1', NULL),
(1732, 'Cowabunga! You get an A!', 'Because this is WaniKani, こういち has probably set the turtle shell on fire, too. "Burn those turtle shells, burn those kanji!" he yells.', 'With a <kanji>turtle shell</kanji> on his back, imagine <reading>こう</reading>いち.', 'N1', NULL),
(1733, 'Can you see the patterns in the writing this criminal did? It looks like a quilt or some plaid. It''s almost beautiful, the way those death threats are arranged!', 'Picture an eagle patterned out of cut out letters. What the words say isn''t important (though it is asking for a ransom on your grandma), but the underlying image is gorgeous.', 'As you look closer at the <kanji>patterned</kanji> writing you realize that it forms the shape of an <reading>eagle</reading> (い). The most American bird of them all! Maybe this guy isn''t a criminal after all!', 'N1', NULL),
(1734, 'The next time you see someone on welfare, be nice. Their spirit stopped protecting them, so they need a little more help right now.', 'If you''ve ever been on welfare you probably already know about the sheep.', 'While on <kanji>welfare</kanji>, you have to do the only job available to you: herding <reading>shee</reading>p (し). Sheep can help you out when you''re having a rough time. Sheep provide warmth, wool for clothes, and even food, if you wanna get dark about it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1735, 'When you look at a grain of rice, you should think about how it used to be a rice plant. That rice plant had a family! いいね, you think — that rice plant had a good life.', 'As it reminisces it wants to say "good," but the いいね is tragically cut short… much like the rice plant''s life. It doesn''t even have the strength to say it properly, it has to say いね. Yes, little grain, your rice plant life really was いね. Because you… were an いね. I''m sorry.', 'The grain of rice thinks about its former life as a <kanji>rice plant</kanji> and, like you, mutters "<reading>いね</reading>." What''s that, little rice grain? Are you trying to say life was good?', 'N1', NULL),
(1736, 'There''s many situations where you''d want to defer to a nurse, like when somebody is seriously hurt and you don''t know what to do to help them. Just imagine yourself saying "Nurse!" in order to defer to them for their medical knowledge.', 'You’re starting to panic because you don’t know to help Joe, so you’re just going around saying “Nurse! Nurse!” repeatedly, desperately looking for someone you could defer to. Nurse!!!', 'The reason you’re trying to <kanji>defer</kanji> to a nurse is because you’re trying to save the local farmhand, <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう). He’s seriously injured, and you don’t know how to help him, so you’ll have to defer to the knowledge of a nurse.', 'N1', NULL),
(1737, 'Leonardo da Vinci picked her for her modesty. She''s so modest she shows no emotion.', 'Have you met Ken the samurai in person? He never smiles, and will never show you his emotions. He''s too modest for that.', 'The <kanji>modesty</kanji> of Mona Lisa inspired <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai to become more modest.', 'N1', NULL),
(1738, 'Imagine kicking Wolverine in the head with your foot as the turkey makes turkey sounds in the background. He’s taller than you so you must leap as you kick.', 'Leap! Leap onto the yak. Feel the yak fur beneath you as you ride away to safety, wind in your hair.', 'After you kick Wolverine and the turkey is safe, you <kanji>leap</kanji> onto a <reading>yak</reading> (やく) to make your getaway. This yak is really fast so you’ll have no problem outrunning Wolverine while riding it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1739, 'Picture yourself handing over an armful of gold bars to buy a single gun.', 'Picture an old fashioned holdup. You''re holding a gun and shoveling jewels into a burlap sack. The heist of the century!', 'You need this <kanji>gun</kanji> so you can steal some <reading>jew</reading>els (じゅう)! Why didn''t you just use your gold to buy the jewels, you ask? Because! That''s not how we did things in olden days. When you want jewels you buy a gun with gold and rob a jewelry store! ', 'N1', NULL),
(1740, 'Actually imagine yourself writing one paragraph of explanation to the city so you can get the permits to construct housing for your geoducks. On the form, it specifies you should only write a paragraph.', 'In that paragraph, you praised こういち for being a great emperor, and you said that this construction project would help you to have more time to do your WaniKani reviews. Clearly you were pandering to Emperor こういち when you wrote it. But hey, it worked!

Hint: When you see 工 in a kanji it will often take the こう reading.', 'You turn in your <kanji>paragraph</kanji>, and it is given to Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち, who insists on being involved in all construction projects. Surprisingly, he approves your construction project, thanks to that paragraph you wrote in the application.', 'N1', NULL),
(1741, 'Dig out many other spots. Every time, it''s just steel under there.', '"I suppose I can use this steel, even if it is not a hill of gold," he says. "We will make steel weapons, which we will use to subjugate other nations. The whole world will learn kanji using the WaniKani system!"', 'You report your findings about the <kanji>steel</kanji> you found to Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち. He is displeased that it is a hill of steel.', 'N1', NULL),
(2459, 'Picture the criminal, trapped inside the mouth. They don''t even look sorry!', 'Wearing nothing but shoes is also really uncomfortable too, so it''s actually part of the criminal''s punishment. They don''t seem to know that though.', 'The <kanji>criminal</kanji> is only wearing <reading>shoe</reading>s (しゅう). They''re completely naked otherwise. They have to wear shoes because the mouth jail would be too slippery for them to stand otherwise.', 'N1', NULL),
(1742, 'Imagine hiring a geoduck, then ask them to review your work. Please review my writing, geoduck. Please review my record-keeping, geoduck. Please review my taxes, geoduck. This geoduck you employed can review anything for you.', 'The geoduck tells you that while it was reviewing your records, it found information about a child you have. Even you didn''t know this, so you''re surprised that this review geoduck found something like this.', 'As the geoduck <kanji>review</kanji>s your paperwork, it notices something surprising. You have a <reading>子</reading> (こ)!', 'N1', NULL),
(1743, 'Imagine having a horse as your master. Hear him neigh out orders to you and the other residents of the horse master’s house.', 'Imagine Chewbacca in a cage in the horse master’s house. You’ve proved yourself a worthy resident. Hear Chewbacca making his Wookie sounds as he bashes against the bars of his new cage.', 'To prove yourself a worthy <kanji>resident</kanji> of the horse master’s house, you must capture <reading>Chew</reading>bacca (ちゅう). Your horse master collects rare creatures, and Chewbacca is his latest desire. If you wish to remain a resident of the house, you must capture Chewbacca.', 'N2', NULL),
(1744, 'Hear the horse clip clopping around the district as it continues to gallop. Clip clop, clop clip clop!', 'The tires screech, the horse neighs, BAM! Total destruction everywhere. Bystanders scream in horror. That horse will never gallop again. It’s a sad day for all horsekind.', 'Then the horse <kanji>gallop</kanji>s into the road and gets hit by a <reading>ca</reading>r (か). Oh man, there’s blood everywhere. This horse is never going to gallop again. That car totally destroyed it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1745, 'See the pillar next to the tree master. See the pillar bow over and over in veneration of its tree master.', 'Hear the crack and snap of branches as the tree beats the crap out of the pillar for having murdered Chewbacca. Unforgivable.', 'While bowing, the <kanji>pillar</kanji> accidentally smashes <reading>Chew</reading>bacca (ちゅう). The pillar is horrified for having murdered Chewbacca, his tree master’s closest friend. The pillar is going to be punished.', 'N2', NULL),
(1746, 'Open your mouth and practice the chant right now! Sun sun, sun sun, sun sun!', 'The Shougun is glaring intently at you as you chant. Open your mouth as wide as you can, get all those "sun suns" out, and pray that the sun will appear.', 'You''re doing this <kanji>chant</kanji> because the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) ordered you to. The sun hasn''t been shining on him as much lately, and he''s displeased. So now you have to chant for him.', 'N1', NULL),
(1747, 'Respect your elders, for they have much to teach us. ', 'I am so ignorant and have much to learn. I must show filial piety to my father, こういち, so that he may teach me.', 'With <kanji>filial piety</kanji> in mind, you decide to go visit your father, who is <reading>こう</reading>いち.', 'N1', NULL),
(1748, 'Imagine seeing the leader of the spiders weaving it''s own little spider computer. Doing spider math, and spider chemistry. It''s a genius!', 'Soon we''ll be slaves to the genius spiders. Soon we''ll be the flies. ', 'As you watch the spider <kanji>genius</kanji> you realize that <reading>soon</reading> (しゅん) spiders will take over the humans. They''re just way smarter than us. Soon we''ll be the bugs.', 'N1', NULL),
(1749, 'Pay close attention to the students around you. Don''t go too fast or too slow, or you won''t finish concurrently.', 'It''s a nice painting, the frown is just right, and you finished concurrently with everyone else, but you''re probably going to fail this class.', 'Your entire class finishes <kanji>concurrently</kanji>. You look at your finished result. Wait, that''s not the Mona Lisa. You painted <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai.', 'N1', NULL),
(1750, 'Watch as the dose of poison sinks into your body through the knife that''s in your chest, as it simultaneously happens to the other guy too.', 'Now you''re begging them for a dose of the xylophone drug. Just a taste, you want to live!', '"I need a <kanji>dose</kanji> of the antidote!" you yell, and the other guy looks you dead in the eyes. They slowly pull a tiny <reading>xy</reading>lophone (ざい) out of their pocket, play a few notes, and then eat it. "Ah, you''d like a dose of the xylophone cure, wouldn''t you?"', 'N1', NULL),
(1751, 'Blow some air out of your mouth. It’s easy, right? Imagine what could cause you to fail at this activity.', 'Attempt to blow air out of your mouth while covering it with your hand. It’s hard to do, right? Only a fool would try to blow air out of their mouth this way.', 'You silly <reading>foo</reading>l (ふ), the reason you couldn’t <kanji>blow</kanji> any air out of your mouth is because your hand was over it. You can’t blow air out that way. You silly fool, you.', 'N3', NULL),
(1752, 'Picture the flag in the dirt. Picture the ditch just beyond it. Now look for another way to exit this road, unless you have a ditch wish.', 'It''s a pun, get it? Holy. Hole-y. The ditch is a long hole. See? It''s funny.', 'If you do end up veering into a <kanji>ditch</kanji> you''ll find yourself yelling, "<reading>Holy</reading> (ほり) crap!" Then you''ll go hurtling into the ditch.', 'N1', NULL),
(1753, 'Imagine patrolling the flood waters on your scooter, which is bubbling and scooting along on the surface of the water. You have to be extremely vigilant in your patrol, you don''t want any lives to be lost on your watch.', 'You''re in a group with a bunch of other scooter people, and you''re all assigned to one month of flood patrol duty. But you got June. The floodiest month. Womp womp.', 'You have to <kanji>patrol</kanji> all of the floods in <reading>June</reading> (じゅん). Unfortunately, that''s the month when most of the floods in Japan happen, so you end up on patrol a lot.', 'N1', NULL),
(1754, 'He basically just made up a bunch of commandments to order all the other drunkards around and decided having twenty (twice the amount of that other religion he heard about) would be a good number.', 'Commandment 1: Thou shalt not touch my kayak
Commandment 2: Thou shalt not look at my kayak
Commandment 3: Thou shalt give me beer to put in my kayak

It goes on like that for a while...', 'The drunkard is yelling out his twenty <kanji>commandment</kanji>s to the others at the bar, but they''re all about <reading>kay</reading>aks (かい).', 'N1', NULL),
(1755, 'Picture a criminal’s fingers, and all of the bad energy just oozing out of them, almost like an exhaust pipe emitting toxic fumes.', 'Imagine this criminal''s fingers emitting god-awful, cringe-inducing haiku after haiku. Really feel your skin crawl at the shoddiness of this criminal''s fingers'' haiku.', 'Then you realize what the fingers are actually <kanji>emit</kanji>ting: terrible, awful <reading>hai</reading>ku (はい). ', 'N1', NULL),
(1756, 'Turkeys aren''t portable on their own. I mean you could pick one up I guess, but it will bite and poop on you. So get it in a portable carrier first.', 'Imagine getting a portable turkey cake made of your pet turkey. It''s made of turkey friendly cake mix.', 'You needed the turkey to be more <kanji>portable</kanji> because you''re going to be bringing it to a <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい) shop to turn it into a cake. You aren''t cooking it! Geez, what''s wrong with you? No, you''re making a cake OF it, for its birthday. The baker needs to see it for reference.', 'N1', NULL),
(1757, 'Imagine yourself out in the winter wilderness. You hear twigs snapping, growling, leaves rustling. You’re incredibly alert and aware of everything going on around you.', 'Listen to the sasquatch rumble on by as you continue to smell the smelly smells of this putrid bin you’re in.', 'Being the <kanji>alert</kanji> fellow you are, you hear a sasquatch approaching and dive into a trash <reading>bin</reading> (びん) to hide. The bin smells really bad and you are keenly alert to all the various stenches and smells currently penetrating your nostrils, but at least you are safe.', 'N1', NULL),
(1758, 'You should probably also get some sharp spikes, sharp traps, and sharp spears to keep that gold safe too. Just a bunch of sharp stuff.', 'Imagine covering the floor around your gold in sharp acorns. This is basically a Home Alone situation, and anyone who tries to steal your stuff is gonna get stuck full of sharp things and probably stabbed by your guard.', 'You''re setting up all these sharp traps around your gold when you step on a <kanji>sharp</kanji> <reading>a</reading>corn (えい) that''s just lying on the floor. Ouch! But wait, then you get a great idea!', 'N2', NULL),
(1759, 'Imagine facing the direction of the approaching winter while facing away from the rice paddy. Feel the weight of the cross in your hands as you spread your arms and implore the winter to hold off on its arrival. Drip the drop onto any thirsty rice.', 'Feel the strain on your chic arms as you continue to spread them. Since you''re by far the most chic person around, you have to set a good example for your people, so don''t let them (or your arms) down.', 'With your arms <kanji>spread</kanji> like that, you look extremely <reading>chic</reading> (しき). Spread arms are obviously a chic posture, and the fact that you''re holding a cross makes it even more chic. 

Side note: The official kun''yomi reading is just し, but since far more words use しき, we''re focusing on that here. Even though the き part is technically okurigana, it''s actually used in the readings for most of the compounds that use this kanji!', 'N1', NULL),
(1760, 'When you address your lord you better say "milord," otherwise you might get beheaded with the weapon you''re all giving him.', 'Oh no, oh no, oh no! Now the lord is running around with his dented weapon trying to put dents in all of you. You don''t want a dent in your head! Run!', 'As you give the <kanji>lord</kanji> his weapon together, you see his face turn into a frown, "What''s wrong, <kanji>Milord</kanji>?" you ask. "THERE''S A <reading>DEN</reading>T (でん)!" he bellows.', 'N2', NULL),
(1761, 'The reason he is sacrificing himself is so we can all eat! Yum! Thanks to your sacrifice, righteous cow, we all have hamburgers. You should think about all the cows you ask to die for you every day. Maybe say thanks next time.', 'Of course, the reading is also shared with kanji like 義. The righteousness radicals seems to have that effect on kanji.', 'The <kanji>sacrifice</kanji> must be done via <reading>gui</reading>llotine (ぎ). I know, I know, that sounds really brutal, but it''s the best way. It''s fast, it''s effective, and hopefully the sacrifice—I mean, cow—doesn''t feel a thing.', 'N1', NULL),
(1762, 'Look at this turkey straining itself on the stool trying to seize the flowers, grunting with effort. You think he’ll ever get them?', 'Imagine this scene again only with a big cock holding the flowers as the turkey attempts to seize them. Hear the cock shouting cock-a-doodle-doo as it defends its flowers.', 'The animal the turkey is trying to <kanji>seize</kanji> the flowers from is a <reading>cock</reading> (かく). The cock is a lot bigger than the turkey, so that’s why the turkey needs to stand on the stool in its attempt to seize those flowers.', 'N1', NULL),
(1763, 'Imagine being in this luxuriant garden — smell the overwhelming floral aroma as you clear flowers off the slide and try to shoo away the drunkard so your kids can come out and play.', 'You can''t believe this has happened again. The last drunk to wander in was moaning for a week before the flowers finished draining his life-force. I guess this is just the price you pay for having a luxuriant garden.', 'As you try to shoo the drunkard out of your <kanji>luxuriant</kanji> garden, he begins to <reading>mo</reading>an (も) with distress. Your garden is so luxuriant that the flowers have wrapped their roots around him and started draining the nutrients from his body, causing him to moan in anguish.', 'N1', NULL),
(1764, 'Imagine trying to wade through this overgrown field of thread. It’s really difficult, and also it’s really cold because it’s winter. You shiver. Imagine chopping the thread down with your machete. Chop chop chop!', 'Imagine yourself as Han Solo, bravely blasting your way through the overgrown thread with your blaster. Pew pew pew!', 'The reason this field of thread got so <kanji>overgrown</kanji> is because you’re <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo and you’re not used to simple things like farming thread. Han Solo is used to blasting baddies and kissing princesses. Overgrown thread is not his specialty. Use your blaster to clear a way through this thread instead of using that machete.', 'N1', NULL),
(1765, 'Imagine walking around near this geoduck. This is the best area to frequent when you want to look at the geoduck. Just keep looking at it as you walk.', 'Look at all the Hindu people as you walk around and observe the geoduck. Hear them all speaking Hindi as you continue to walk around.', 'Apparently Hindu people also love geoducks because <reading>Hin</reading>du (ひん) people <kanji>frequent</kanji> this area quite often as well. You always see the geoduck here, but also a lot of Hindu people.', 'N1', NULL),
(1766, 'Why would you ask the yakuza to "straighten out" a problem for you? Do you realize what that means in yakuza-speak? Your problems are going to be multiplying exponentially, friend.', 'Feel shock envelop your entire body as yakuza run around town roughing up everyone who''s ever wronged you. Police attention on you is beginning to multiply, too, and you can''t even think from the shock of it all.', 'Your problems <kanji>multiply</kanji> and give you quite a <reading>shock</reading> (しょく) as yakuza thugs commit crime after shocking crime on your behalf. You really shouldn''t be shocked though — of course crimes are going to multiply when you ask the yakuza to "straighten things out."', 'N1', NULL),
(1767, 'Look at the flowers as the tsunami begins to dilute them with just one drop. Their color and fragrance and dimensions become weaker and more spread out. It’s really trippy to witness.', 'As you continue to watch the tsunami''s drop of water dilute the flowers, imagine what techniques this tsunami is using to hack nature. Is the tsunami a sinister being from the future? Maybe.', 'You watch the flowers become <kanji>dilute</kanji>d with just one drop, and realize that this is a nature <reading>hack</reading> (はく). You’ve never seen anything like this before, so you think the tsunami found out how to hack nature and that’s how it got so good at diluting things. By hacking nature.', 'N2', NULL),
(1768, 'Imagine being too heavy to go anywhere without colliding with things.', 'The Shougun is glaring at you from the ground. "You''ve been colliding with my entire city and now you collide into ME?!" Smoke blows out his nostrils.', 'You''re going around the town, you <kanji>collide</kanji> with a bunch of things, when all of a sudden you collide with the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう)! Oh no! The shogun looks really mad.', 'N1', NULL),
(1769, 'Listen to what the fins have to say as they flop around on the ground in the grass. Hear them swish about as they flop, brushing up against the blades of grass.', 'Imagine taking your yo-yo out to do this. Feel the string tighten around your finger as you unleash the yo-yo and silence all the fins.', 'Being one of little <kanji>honor</kanji>, you decide to squash these vocal fins with your <reading>yo</reading>-yo (よ). You take out your yo-yo and start bashing each fin with it until they stop talking about what little honor you have.', 'N1', NULL),
(1770, 'You also praise the lid. And praise the plants and animals that gave you the food.', 'If you''re going to praise the ingenuity of the invention of the "lid" for its preservation methods, you should definitely do it from home. Or at least from the internet from home. Find your people where you can, you know?', 'You do this <kanji>prais</kanji>ing in your <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう), though, not a church. I don''t think anyone outside your home would understand why you''re praising inanimate objects.', 'N1', NULL),
(1771, 'Picture this transparent scooter. Well, it''s actually a bit hard to see, because it''s see-through. I guess imagine the outline and the feel, as you perch at the top of your stairs.', 'Almost immediately you lose track of the scooter because it''s transparent. All you see is とうきょう in front of you. And no scooter. Maybe this is why people don''t make transparent vehicles.', 'You slip, the <kanji>transparent</kanji> scooter scoots down the stairs without you and into the streets of <reading>とう</reading>きょう. Nooo! Transpa-scootie come back!', 'N1', NULL),
(1772, 'Imagine your neighbor doing a rice dance, whatever that may be, in the building next to yours. That''s your neighbor, for ya.', 'Imagine the ring your neighbor has as overly extravagant. You have to make it wild enough for you to remember. Maybe it is integral to their rice dance. Perhaps you covet your neighbor''s rice dance ring.', 'Your <kanji>neighbor</kanji> has an incredible <reading>rin</reading>g (りん) that they use for their rice dance.', 'N1', NULL),
(1773, 'Look at this turkey with a fang. It’s elegant, but also frightening. See the fanged turkey staring at you menacingly.', 'Picture this elegant gargoyle sitting in front of you. Reach out your hand and feels its hard, elegant stone gargoyle body.', 'This <kanji>elegant</kanji> fang makes the turkey look more like a <reading>ga</reading>rgoyle (が) than a turkey. In fact, you haven''t seen it move at all. Maybe it is just an elegant gargoyle!', 'N1', NULL),
(8835, 'Picture a woman rejoicing loudly. She''s got a big smile on her face and she wants you and everyone else to see how glad she is.', 'You''d be happy too if you''d just found your lost keys. Though maybe you wouldn''t shout it for the entire world to hear.', '"I''m so <kanji>glad</kanji>!" the woman shouts, "Because I found the <reading>key</reading> (き)!" You immediately start to wonder what "the key" is. The key to life? The key to learning Japanese? Then you see she''s holding up some car keys.', 'N1', NULL),
(1775, 'Just make sure you don''t inquire about anything inappropriate, or you''ll end up facing an inquiry from HR…', 'Imagine saying "Ooh, Kaga!" to this overly curious leader-director-chef. Say it out loud, like he''s just inquired about something weird. He just really loves cooking, okay? But he inquires too much about it in the workplace, so you have to let him know how you feel.', 'Turns out the leader and director of your company is Chairman Kaga from the TV show Iron Chef. Every time he tries to <kanji>inquire</kanji> about weird stuff, you have to say <reading>Ooh, Kaga</reading> (うかが) disapprovingly. "What are you doing for dinner tonight?" he inquires. Ooh, Kaga! "What''s the best way to soften an eggplant?" he inquires. Ooh, Kaga!', 'N2', NULL),
(1776, 'Picture a single beam of moonlight penetrating through the winter sky, right onto the trash.', 'The clear, penetrating beam tastes exactly like iron, which is something you really weren''t expecting. But now it''s filling your entire mouth and you don''t want it to stop.', 'You want the light to <kanji>penetrate</kanji> you, so you put your face in the way of the beam and it shoots <kanji>clear</kanji> through you. Your mouth fills with the taste of <reading>鉄</reading> (てつ).', 'N1', NULL),
(1777, 'Picture the bundle of geoducks getting smashed by the tsunami, breaking apart, and getting tossed around in the rapids.', 'Imagine the cool shallow water lapping at your feet as you bite into some crunchy celery and watch the rapids in the distance.', 'While those geoducks are getting tossed about in the <kanji>rapids</kanji>, you''re sitting in the <kanji>shallows</kanji> chomping down on some <reading>ce</reading>lery (せ).', 'N1', NULL),
(1778, 'Imagine you need proof of your withdrawal so badly, that you''re in the trash like this, raking around with your fingers in the moonlight. Close your eyes and feel the cold, smell the stink.', 'The pain from the 鉄 reverberates up your fingers and into your hands and arms. What''s this great lump of 鉄 doing in the trash?', 'While looking for the <kanji>withdrawal</kanji> letter, your fingers hit a piece of <reading>鉄</reading> (てつ). Ouch!', 'N1', NULL),
(1779, 'What would have to happen for you to set aside your own fingers? Try to imagine it right now.', 'Think of all the things you do with your fingers. Now picture how they would look if you had never ever cleaned them with soap. Uhg, disgusting. You should just set them aside here, and find some new ones.', 'I know why they <kanji>set aside</kanji> their fingers long ago, it''s because there was no <reading>so</reading>ap (そ). If you didn''t have soap to clean your fingers you wouldn''t want to keep them for very long...', 'N1', NULL),
(1780, 'Imagine tracing your fingers over the table, the cold winter frost stinging them, as you reveal the secrets of this table and what it was based on.', 'The buildings and temples are all very childlike. Like crayon drawn on a table.', 'This particular table was <kanji>based on</kanji> Little <reading>Kyo</reading>to (きょ). Little buildings and temples, that sort of look like Little Kyoto. Problem is, it was made by a <reading>子</reading> (こ), so it''s not very good.', 'N1', NULL),
(1781, 'Have him run your ceremony for you. Think about why he is so righteous, then think about an extremely righteous ceremony.', '*CHOP*', 'Sadly, the reason only the righteous can run this <kanji>ceremony</kanji> is because it''s a punishment ceremony, where he pulls the cord to cut someone''s head off via a <reading>gui</reading>llotine (ぎ).', 'N1', NULL),
(1782, 'There are a lot of hoodlums out there that try to dress as trees and confuse people. So make sure you measure their drum before you cut one down or tell someone your secrets under it.', 'However! A non-wood tree will be really thirsty from pretending to be a wood tree. It will start making sounds and moving and probably say, "Can I have a juice box, please?"', 'If you''re still not sure if the tree is really made of <kanji>wood</kanji>, just take out a small <reading>ju</reading>ice (じゅ) box and start drinking it. A wooden tree will show no interest in the juice box at all.', 'N1', NULL),
(1783, 'Most people abandon fruit when it''s misshapen. It''s probably fine though...', 'You abandon the fruit and stare intently at this key. It must be why the fruit was weird looking and abandoned in the trash... but what does it go to?! You dig through the trash and try the key on everything that''s been abandoned in there.', 'You reach into the trash, take out the <kanji>abandon</kanji>ed fruit, and bite into it. OUCH! There''s a <reading>key</reading> (き) inside, and you just bit into it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1784, 'Look at the tiger running with his tiger legs. So majestic. So fierce.', 'Imagine watching the tiger as it notices the Torah and then runs away. The tiger sounds as if it is screaming in terror as it flees from the proximity of the Torah.', 'The <kanji>tiger</kanji> is using his legs to run away from the <reading>Torah</reading> (とら). This tiger is deathly afraid of religion, so a holy book like the Torah causes the tiger to run away.', 'N1', NULL),
(1785, 'Imagine this little firefly blinking his firefly light as he flies around shouting a Viking warcry and brandishing an axe. Yaaaargh!', 'Hear Hotaru’s shrill scream as she runs away from the Viking firefly. If you don’t know what Hotaru Tomoe looks like, Google a picture of her to get a better visualization.', 'The Viking <kanji>firefly</kanji> is attacking <reading>Hotaru</reading> (ほたる) Tomoe (alter ego of Sailor Saturn) because the Viking firefly hates the show Sailor Moon and everyone on it. Hotaru screams in terror as she is deathly afraid of fireflies, especially Viking ones.', 'N1', NULL),
(1790, 'Picture a huge warship overseeing everything around it. It''s big and kinda scary.', 'Picture Genghis Khan aboard a monstrous warship. He''s carefully watching the sea and land that he''s conquered.', 'This huge <kanji>warship</kanji> is run by Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん), the famous warlord. Really, what did you expect? Who else is strong enough to captain a warship but him?', 'N1', NULL),
(1791, 'Imagine witnessing this tsunami taking the two husbands up into the sun. Hear the water rushing. That tsunami is massive. What are they going to conceal in the sun?', 'Just make sure to remember the husbands are riding that tsunami alongside their centaur, heading towards the sun. They need to conceal the centaur to keep it safe.', 'The husbands are going to <kanji>conceal</kanji> their pet <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん) in the sun. The local government isn''t too pleased that they have a pet centaur, so they have to conceal it in the sun so it doesn''t get confiscated.', 'N1', NULL),
(1792, 'Picture the gladiator squeezing his hand into a tight fist, raising it up and yelling.', 'Ken and the gladiator clash! Fists fly! You''ve never seen hand-to-hand combat like this! You can hardly keep up!!', 'The gladiator''s <kanji>fist</kanji> is now aimed at <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai. Little does the gladiator know, Ken knows the ways of the samurai and the gladiator. He slowly raises his own hands and turns them into fists.', 'N1', NULL),
(1793, 'You’re angry that you could only find charcoal so you take the charcoal in your hands and you crush it atop the cliff on the mountain. Feel the charcoal breaking in your hands as the wind carries the charcoal dust off from the top of the cliff on the mountain.', 'Imagine lying out in the sun next to the crushed charcoal, thinking you’re going to get tan. You’re very surprised when you actually catch on fire. Hot hot hot!', 'After crushing the <kanji>charcoal</kanji>, you decide to take advantage of your current location and lie out in the sun for a while to get a <reading>tan</reading> (たん). Instead of getting a tan though, you catch on fire. Oops.', 'N2', NULL),
(1795, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Home is where the wrapped heart is.', 'Think about being <kanji>wrap</kanji>ped up, nice and warm and safe in your <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう). Home is where you wrap yourself in a blanket, in the warmth of the fireplace, in the arms of those you love. ', 'N2', NULL),
(1797, 'Reflect upon your own humanity. What does it mean to be human? Is it wearing shoes and hats? Probably.', 'Are you wearing jeans right now? If so, rub them with your hands and say aloud “Oh, the humanity!” Or just imagine wearing jeans if you aren’t actually wearing them.', 'Another thing that reminds you and the leader about your <kanji>humanity</kanji> is wearing <reading>jean</reading>s (じん). Jeans are the go-to legwear for many people, and no one but humans wear them. Jeans are another great symbol of humanity.', 'N1', NULL),
(1798, 'Think of the wide range of minerals there are that aren''t gold. There are a lot.', 'Emperor こういち is getting all the minerals, and the 鋼, so he can take over all neighboring nations and force them into a subjugation of efficient kanji learning.', 'One person is trying to control all the <kanji>minerals</kanji> of the world. That person is Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち.', 'N2', NULL),
(1799, 'It''s actually pretty easy to attain mole status. Just stand in a field. Wait for the ground to move under you and shove your hand in the earth. Blam! An achievement pops up above your head and you''re there.', 'If you can attain two animal levels in one day you''ll be a legend on the leaderboards.', 'Mole isn''t enough, you want to <kanji>attain</kanji> <reading>shee</reading>p (し) status next. Everyone knows it goes human &gt; mole&gt; sheep. Now you need to stand in the field with the mole and get a sheep to come over.', 'N1', NULL),
(1800, 'Imagine saying something with sincerity to someone you want to become important to you. Say something like, “I love you” out loud. Say it with the utmost sincerity.', 'Say, "I love you," again and watch your saber beating in time with your heart. If you weren''t sincere it would fade and grow dull.', 'When you say something with complete, unabashed <kanji>sincerity</kanji> your special <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい) starts to pulse with the beating of your heart. It shows the person you want to become close to that you truly are sincere!', 'N1', NULL),
(1801, 'Picture the poop guy from your hometown. Picture him really vividly. The awkward, rude things he does. The way he smells. The way he makes you never want to come back.', 'Imagine all the wonderful things that exist in your hometown of きょうと. All the things you love to do and see. And now picture poop guy. Ruining it all with his poop hands.', 'Your <kanji>hometown</kanji> is in <reading>きょう</reading>と which, unfortunately, means your poop guy is in きょうと too. Now every time you go back home he''s there stinking everything up.', 'N1', NULL),
(1802, 'Imagine the little leader sitting on Wolverine’s forehead as he shouts “Invade! Invade invade! Invade his brain and destroy the Wolverine!”', 'See the little soldiers slicing open Wolverine’s shin as Wolverine grunts in pain. Getting your leg cut open is not a comfortable thing, even for someone as manly as Wolverine.', 'Soon they realize that they cannot <kanji>invade</kanji> Wolverine through his head so they travel down to his leg and try to enter through his <reading>shin</reading> (しん). The little soldiers take a big knife and slice open Wolverine’s shin in order to invade his body.', 'N1', NULL),
(1803, 'Look at this fake leader. See him scramble into a uniform. Does he look like a real leader? Or can you tell that he is a fake?', 'Watch as the guillotine comes down. You close your eyes for a second, and when you open them you see that the leader''s body was a fake! Wait, where did he go?!', 'Well, everyone found out he was a <kanji>fake</kanji> anyway, and now you''re all going to use a <reading>gui</reading>llotine (ぎ) to cut off his fake head.', 'N1', NULL),
(1804, 'Imagine running a marathon with your old legs to prove you can overcome anything. Feel your old legs cramping up as you try to overcome the marathon and finish the race.', 'Filled with the jubilation of achievement from overcoming all odds, imagine the flavor of this cold Coke in your mouth. Fizzy and Coke flavored, it runs down your throat and into your tummy. Yum.', 'When you pass the finish line and finally <kanji>overcome</kanji> the limits of your old legs, someone hands you an ice cold <reading>Coke</reading> (こく) as a reward. Mmm, such a refreshing Coke.', 'N1', NULL),
(1805, 'Welp, your arrival didn''t go smoothly. You have a knife in your foot. I''m not sure what you did to make that mole hate you, but you''ll have to be more careful next time.', 'This certainly wasn''t the type of arrival you were expecting when you bought your tickets to とうきょう.', 'Your <kanji>arrival</kanji> to <reading>とう</reading>きょう is now ruined. And your next stop is now going to be an arrival at a とうきょう hospital to get this mole knife out.', 'N3', NULL),
(1806, 'Imagine being in front of this pair of stools. Picture yourself sitting on one stool, getting up, then sitting on the other. Repeat this process.', 'It''s the same with pairs of socks and pairs of people. Sometimes what looks like two things is really a perfect pair, with one soul. Meant to be together forever.', 'This <kanji>pair</kanji> of stools shares a <reading>sou</reading>l (そう). Even if you tried to separate them, they''d eventually find their way back together.', 'N2', NULL),
(1807, 'Imagine expressing your philosophy, your deepest thoughts and beliefs, by putting an axe in your mouth. Is it some kind of metaphor for how powerful and sharp your philosophical thinking is?', 'Your philosophy and your way of life means everything tastes like 鉄 now, and you think everyone should be that way.', 'Your axe-in-mouth <kanji>philosophy</kanji> means you''re always tasting <reading>鉄</reading> (てつ). They say blood tastes like copper. Well, weapons (and your philosophy) taste like iron.', 'N1', NULL),
(1808, 'Picture the cross looming overhead, people''s mouths open so they can sob loudly, and everyone wearing the same color clothes (just picture whatever color works for your particular culture/religion combo).', 'If you aren''t mourning enough, when the soul comes out of the person''s body, it will get really mad. "What, I''m not worth a few tears? Really?" And then the soul will haunt you. So you better mourn like you mean it.', 'As the <kanji>mourning</kanji> continues, the <reading>sou</reading>l (そう) of the dead person rises out of the cross. The soul looks around at all of the mourners and nods. Yes, your mourning has pleased it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1809, 'Picture two stools in your mind. One is solid, you want to sit on it. The other looks like the wind will knock it over. Don''t sit on that!', 'You better hope that catapult is solid too. If it isn''t the whole thing will come apart when you try to launch yourself into the air.', 'You need this stool to be extra <kanji>solid</kanji> because you''re using it to test your new <reading>cata</reading>pult (かた). If it isn''t solid, when you launch it and yourself in the catapult, the stool might fall over, killing you both. And you don''t want that, now do you?', 'N1', NULL),
(1811, 'Try to fasten your own tongue with your fingers right now. You probably can''t do it, right? That''s because your fingers are too big and your tongue is too slippery.', 'In this case, many cats fastened your tongue. You should probably be nicer to cats from now on so this doesn''t happen again.', 'Only one creature could use its fingers to <kanji>fasten</kanji> your tongue like this: <reading>cats</reading> (かつ). Cats are notorious for fastening tongues so that people can''t speak. Haven''t you ever heard the phrase, "Cat got your tongue?"', 'N1', NULL),
(1812, 'Imagine shooting a really heavy melon out of a bow. Watch it make an arc through the air. Trace the arc with your finger.', 'Imagine being a 子 and watching melons fly in arcs all over. Imagine a little 子 version of yourself giggling and clapping your hands with absolute glee.', 'Shooting melons in an <kanji>arc</kanji> is something little <reading>子</reading>''s (こ) love to do. It also helps that when they come down from the arc they smash all over the place.', 'N1', NULL),
(1813, 'What kind of challenge is it? If you look closely at your fingers sometimes you can figure out the challenge that is to come based on their movements.', 'Whenever you see an omen (radical), there''s a good chance that Mrs. Chou will be a part of it. This is not a good sign.', 'In your finger, you see a <kanji>challenge</kanji>. And... if you look closely, you can see the fast movements of your fingers forming the outline of Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading>''s (ちょう) face. Oh no, the challenge is going to be Mrs. Chou!', 'N1', NULL),
(1814, 'Imagine digging under the flag by the exit of your building with your bare fingers. The dirt is getting under your fingernails as you dig. It''s getting on your knees. It''s everywhere.', 'You carefully un-dig the old coots and leave them to their old grumpy coot hole... basement... secret clubhouse? Whatever.', 'You <kanji>dig</kanji> until you finally hit something. It''s a bunch of buried old <reading>coots</reading> (くつ). They''re in an underground room for old coots. One yells up at you, "Whatcha doing to our roof, ya whipper snapper?! Stop digging in our lawn!"', 'N2', NULL),
(1815, 'Hoist the sails! Hoist the anchor! Hoist a baby! All of these situations are easier if you have a ton of working fingers. (The more the better.)', 'Imagine hoisting things over and over again. Each time you spot a new thing that needs hoisting, you say "Ah! Something else to hoist!"', 'Today, you have to <kanji>hoist</kanji> so much stuff. "<reading>Ah</reading> (あ)!" you say, "So much hoisting to do." Better get to it!', 'N1', NULL),
(1816, 'Imagine picking up a few different things in a shop. If something fits nicely in your grip: buy it. If it''s too small or too big, put it back.', 'Acupuncture is great for strengthening your grip strength. If you do intense shopping (like I do) you should definitely consider it!', 'You have a really strong <kanji>grip</kanji> thanks to <reading>acu</reading>puncture (あく)! All your fingers are primed for buying things. You can grip anything at a shop—pineapples, tires, even live turtles. All thanks to some well-placed needles.', 'N1', NULL),
(1817, 'Imagine being in space and shaking a satellite with your fingers.', 'Imagine shaking yogurt in space. Now imagine eating yogurt as it continues to shake (in space still)!', 'You need to <kanji>shake</kanji> it because this satellite is full of <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう). You only like yogurt that''s been well shaken. And who can resist space yogurt? Huh? Anyone?', 'N1', NULL),
(1818, 'Close your eyes and feel the purification begin. Try not to let the loud jackhammering sound bother you, just focus on the simultaneous vibrations cleansing you inside and out.', 'Hopefully this purification (or just the two jackhammers shaking your brain up like pudding) will get the memories of your cyborg to go away. You can''t keep picturing it all the time like this.', 'This <kanji>purification</kanji> ritual is supposed to help you forget your <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい). It''s been a while, but you can''t seem to get it out of your head. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1819, 'Feel sad that you were the one to destroy your car for good, even though the sun messed it up temporarily. ', 'You should probably be worried about this situation.', 'You were driving around in <reading>Zan</reading>zibar (ざん) when this happened, too, so now you''re stuck in Zanzibar, a foreign place, and you have no idea what to do next.', 'N1', NULL),
(1820, 'Imagine taking this axe in your hands and chopping away at this tree to do your analysis. Hear the chop chop chop as wood chips fly into your face.', 'Imagine yourself being sexy and doing tree analysis. What sort of sexy analysis moves can you think of? Oh yeah, so sexy.', 'The person who is doing this <kanji>analysis</kanji> is very <reading>sexy</reading> (せき). Who is this sexy person? Why it’s you of course, you sexy beast. I love watching you do analysis on trees. It’s so sexy.', 'N1', NULL),
(1821, 'Don''t forget to buy a hinge. Your door purchase will <kanji>hinge</kanji> on your hinges.', 'Really lengthen out that "sue" because it''s a long "suu" for the reading and that will help.', 'You buy an awesome <kanji>hinge</kanji> and try it out. Instead of working properly, it just falls apart and the door falls on you, crushing your lower body. You yell out (and you have to yell) "I''m GOING TO <reading>SUUUUEEE</reading> (すう) YOU!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1822, 'Imagine the frustration of trying to drive a car without any axis (or axles) for its wheels to spin on. That thing isn''t going anywhere. It''s missing its pivotal part! ', 'The pivotal part your friend leaves out is that those multi-axis wheels are why his car is always crashing. Gee, cool axis control, man.', 'Your friend is always bragging about his car''s <kanji>axis</kanji>. He''s all like, "My car''s wheels move on more than one axis!" And you sarcastically reply: "<reading>Gee, coo</reading>l" (じく).', 'N1', NULL),
(1823, 'Think about what pattern it might be. Is it a checkered pattern? Something more complicated and beautiful? Whatever it is, think of something that really impresses you.', 'Imagine painting your garage with the pattern you imagined earlier.', 'The <kanji>pattern</kanji> is so beautiful that you want to put it on your <reading>gara</reading>ge (がら). So, you do.', 'N1', NULL),
(1824, 'Imagine the white tsunami knocking on your door, walking into your house, and then just laying down on your bed. You try to tell it to leave, but it''s a tsunami, and it says it will be staying overnight thank you very much.', 'See the hairball slowly sinking into the tsunami. The cat hacks up another one.', 'Your cat walks up to the white tsunami that''s staying <kanji>overnight</kanji> and decides it doesn''t like it, so it <reading>hack</reading>s (はく) up a hairball on it. Even that doesn''t get the tsunami to leave.', 'N2', NULL),
(1825, 'Imagine being knocked off your feet by a tsunami, then sliding into a wall or something and breaking a bone or two. You try to get up, but you slip again, breaking a few more bones in the process. ', 'Picture these stray cats trying to right themselves on the wet ground and slipping again each time. They''re doing that thing cats do when they touch water, twisting their bodies around and trying to cling to you. Great, now you''ve got a bunch of slippery cats perched atop your broken body.', 'As you continue to <kanji>slide</kanji> around you see a bunch of stray <reading>cats</reading> (かつ) slipping and sliding too. They''re yowling and sliding right toward you. Uh oh…', 'N1', NULL),
(1826, 'The lagoon is beautiful! The water is hot (from hitting the sun) but it isn''t so bright that you''ll get a sunburn.', 'Now picture it all in slow motion. The catapult tosses the tsunami into the air, it smashes the sun, breaks off into a wing of nice, hot water, and finally splashes back to the earth into a beautiful, secluded lagoon.', 'This <kanji>lagoon</kanji> was created on purpose by a really angry dude and his <reading>cata</reading>pult (かた). He wanted a nice place to lounge that''s tropical, but not too bright. So he came up with a plan: use a giant catapult to send a tsunami into the sun!', 'N1', NULL),
(1827, 'You should know better than to try to boil something without water! Now what are you going to do with this raw, but charred turkey?!', 'The Shougun reaches down and bites a charred hunk off the turkey. He yells and spits it out. Why did he do that?! You have no idea but it looked nasty.', 'You''re staring sadly at the <kanji>char</kanji>red turkey when you suddenly sense the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) looming behind you. You were supposed to be cooking this turkey for him and now he looks really, really mad.', 'N1', NULL),
(1828, 'It''s amazing how fast this guy can whittle!', 'Han Solo''s bamboo example comes to life and runs off.', 'One last <kanji>example</kanji> for you. Now I''m going to take this bamboo and whittle it into an example of <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo!', 'N1', NULL),
(1829, 'You don''t know what part of the thread to look at. What a distraction.', 'Imagine yourself really enjoying looking at parts of the thread. You don''t know why it''s so fun, but being derpy is exciting, I guess.', 'Despite how distracted you are, that was indeed a lot of <reading>fun</reading> (ふん).', 'N1', NULL),
(1830, 'As you twist together endless strands of thread and vines, feel how tough and sturdy your makeshift rope is beginning to get. You could swing through the jungle like Tarzan on this thing.', 'It turns out those vines you''ve been twisting together are cucumber vines, so if you need a break to rest your hands, you can chow down on a healthy and refreshing cucumber while you recover.', 'As you <kanji>twist together</kanji> the thread and vines, you suddenly feel a lump. You look down and see a <reading>cu</reading>cumber (きゅう) twisted into it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1831, 'Imagine yourself twisting this hill of thread up to make thicker ropes. They''re strong!', 'Imagine yourself going tuna fishing on a small boat with nothing but one long rope. This is how tuna fishing is done where you''re from, so you''re sticking to tradition.', 'Congratulations, now you have a perfect rope for <reading>tuna</reading> (つな) fishing. Tunas are massive, so you need a strong rope to reel one in.', 'N1', NULL),
(1832, 'You''ll need to differentiate this from 綱, which uses the same radicals (though 綱 has a real hill radical). Just remember that the "hill" in this one is all weird and lumpy because it''s a net with a bunch of stuff caught in it.', 'Imagine the awkwardness of trying to mow the grass around this big, lumpy, writhing net full of loudly meowing cats, flopping fish, and goodness knows what else.', 'This <kanji>net</kanji> is creating a serious obstacle to you <reading>mow</reading>ing (もう) your lawn. The net has way too much stuff in it for you to be able to drag it away, so for now you''re just going to have to mow around it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1833, 'Imagine holding an empty bottle up to the moon and seeing it''s completely dry. You pat your belly above where your liver is and say, "I guess you''re safe tonight, liver."', 'Genghis Khan glares down at you, "You inherited my liver, and that means no moonshine for you. Protect your liver and don''t drink, like me, Genghis Khan."', 'As you pat your <kanji>liver</kanji> suddenly a strange feeling bubbles up and the ghost of Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) comes out! Right out of your liver.', 'N1', NULL),
(1834, 'Imagine walking through your lawn and smelling all the flowers as you travel over the rolling hills. What a delightful lawn this is.', 'Look at your little Shiba running through the lawn all happy and elated. Hear him barking as he zooms up and down each hill.', 'You are the proud owner of a <reading>Shiba</reading> (しば) Inu, y’know, the Japanese dog? Well, this Shiba Inu of yours loves to run around in your <kanji>lawn</kanji> and through the flowers and over the hills. This is the perfect lawn for your Shiba.', 'N1', NULL),
(1835, 'Imagine yourself out in the wild wilderness as you hear the flowers screaming until death takes them. The river is raging, there’s nothing you can do to help the flowers. The wild has claimed them.', 'Imagine this scene again, only imagine it like the wild flowers are experiencing their own great flood. Ah, those flowers are drowning to death à la everything that died in the Biblical flood.', '<reading>Ah</reading> (あ), the flowers in the <kanji>wild</kanji> have been claimed <reading>à la</reading> (あら) the great flood. Ah, you don’t think so? Well I do.', 'N2', NULL),
(1836, 'Look at this sack of substitute clothes. Imagine it filled with all the clothes you hardly ever wear. What kind of clothes would be inside this sack?', 'Imagine how nasty it is in there. Also imagine how smelly it is. Feel angry about putting roe in your sack. Why would you even do that? Why would the roe do this to you, as well.', 'You need your substitute clothes so you take out your substitute clothes <kanji>sack</kanji>. You open it up to grab your substitute clothes, only to find out that there is a ton of salmon roe inside your sack. Oh man, you forgot all about putting that in there. You yell: <reading>F**K ROE</reading> (ふくろ) because that roe destroyed all your clothes and made it nasty.', 'N2', NULL),
(1838, 'Imagine yourself holding a king''s hat covered in his hair. Who will you sell it to? This is such a rare item!', 'Not sure how it got there, but you''ll make a pretty penny from this. The king only had one chin, so that''s about as <kanji>rare</kanji> as they come.', 'Then you look closer at the hat. It''s about to get even <kanji>rare</kanji>r. Attached to the hair is the king''s <reading>chin</reading> (ちん).', 'N2', NULL),
(1839, 'It doesn''t matter if it''s a yakuza member or not. Any knife will cause a split or tear in clothes.', 'Think of a thing you want to tear with your yakuza buddy. You''re into it, so you agree and say "Ret''s do it!"', '"I want to <kanji>split</kanji> and <kanji>tear</kanji> that." You agree, so you say <reading>Let''s</reading> (れつ) do it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1840, 'IMPERIAL ATTACK, GO! Imagine what this imperial attack might look like and imagine yourself in some swanky imperial clothes while you perform it.', 'Picture the shoes as you attack, they glow—full of attack power! These +4 attack shoes are really special.', 'The <kanji>attack</kanji>''s power is concentrated in the <reading>shoe</reading>s (しゅう) in this imperial outfit. You attack with your feet, kicking the enemy with your old, fancy shoes.', 'N1', NULL),
(1841, 'The Crabigator is a sea creature (maybe?), so making a tribute from shellfish makes some sense.', 'In his power, こういち has made a new religion around the Crabigator, to put the fear of kanji learning into his subjects.

Hint: When you see 工 in a kanji it will often take the こう reading.', 'This great <kanji>tribute</kanji> is being commissioned by Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1842, 'Imagine yourself playing this game and basking in the charm of tapping your ear on the stool. ', 'As you watch, you really see the charm of this game. Some people are throwing the stool down the chute, some people are sliding down it on their ears...how charming. ', 'The added <kanji>charm</kanji> of this game is that there''s this big <reading>chu</reading>te (しゅ) at the end. It''s so charming because you love chutes. 

', 'N1', NULL),
(1843, 'Compare the distance you can travel with one step compared to a giant. It''s a considerable difference in distance.', 'Imagine walking the distance between where you are and the (possibly made up) Little Kyoto. Think about the distance.', 'Going to Little <reading>Kyo</reading>to (きょ) is a short <kanji>distance</kanji> for a giant but a long <kanji>distance</kanji> for you.', 'N1', NULL),
(1844, 'School registers, family registers, member registers — you name it. Picture the fields and hillsides back then, full of names scraped into the dirt. ', 'No wonder people went to the trouble of plowing their names into the dirt back then. They understood the sex appeal of registers.', 'If your name is in a <kanji>register</kanji>, people will find you very <reading>sexy</reading> (せき). This was true long ago too. There''s nothing sexier than someone whose existence is accounted for in official documentation.', 'N2', NULL),
(1845, 'Ugh, you shouldn''t have bought these open toed shoes, at least not during rainy season!', 'You wonder how long this road will go. With exposed toes, your feet are going to get all wet with dew if you can''t get off this blasted road.', 'You run along the <reading>ro</reading>ad (ろ), trying to get out of the rain, but the road keeps going and going, with walls on both sides.', 'N1', NULL),
(1846, 'It''s cold outside (it''s winter after all). Imagine you having to herd all the cows out to pasture even though they don''t want to leave the warm barn.', 'Actually say that phrase out loud while thinking about bringing your cows to pasture.', 'ぼく is one of the common ways to say "I" in Japanese (which you should remember!). In this case, you''re a boy... a cowboy, and you say, "<reading>ぼく</reading>will take these cows out to pasture!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1847, 'The old way of printing involved taking the pattern you want (the flag), pressing it against the place you want to print it (the towel), then just slicing that sucker on there (with the knife).', 'You know her pretty well at this point, so try to imagine what kind of printing Satsuki would make. Picture her holding it up to show you.', 'As you get your flag ready for <kanji>printing</kanji> you look over and see <reading>Satsu</reading>ki (さつ), who''s already finished a nice pattern on her towel. She''s a little young to be using a knife, but her print came out nice.', 'N2', NULL),
(1848, 'Picture the good, good bright moon. Shining and shimmering and smiling down at us on Earth.', 'Picture this scene like it''s a Disney cartoon. The good moon is smiling brightly down at the roe which are smiling right back. They might even start singing a song!', 'It''s so <kanji>bright</kanji> that you can see all the <reading>roe</reading> (ろう) in the sea glowing and reflecting the light like stars. And the tiny baby fishies inside the roe are smiling brightly back up at the moon!', 'N1', NULL),
(1849, 'The moon does affect the tides a bit, so you can imagine the tsunami as the water getting bigger whenever the moon is out. That''s just the tide.', 'She wakes up and says, "If only you came at low tide then I would never have been saved!" She jumps up and runs off, yelling something about destroying the world.', 'You go check out the <kanji>tide</kanji>. The water is coming in a little stronger, and something washes up on the shore. It''s a body! You roll the body over and it is Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう)!', 'N1', NULL),
(1850, 'Lots of things have roots from wanting to have something in an instant. Just think about instant ramen. The root of that was to get ramen real, real fast. Similar to stamps, ya know?', 'Imagine using instant socks. How do they feel? Soggy, but hey, it was fast!', 'Think about the thing you want to make <kanji>instant</kanji>. I''d want instant <reading>sock</reading>s (そく)! Don''t waste time searching for a matching pair or pulling them on. Just take out a pair of instant socks, add water, and the socks are already on your feet. In an instant!', 'N1', NULL),
(1851, 'Picture yourself leaning over a balcony at dawn, looking at all the hedges appear and their shadows stretch over the ground.', 'Imagine rooting around under some hedges, desperately trying to find your car keys. Hedges are prickly and there''s spiders in here! If only you didn''t need a car in the first place!', 'You''re looking intently at one particular <kanji>hedge</kanji> because yesterday you dropped your <reading>ca</reading>r <reading>key</reading>s (かき) behind it. Now that you know where the hedge is you''re going to need to search it to find your car keys.', 'N1', NULL),
(1852, 'Try to imagine who this woman might be. A world leader? A powerful CEO? Whatever the case, she''s got an aura of dignity — and that giant canine behind her only makes her look even more mighty.', 'Imagine trying to crack a joke in front of this mighty woman, only to be silenced by the dignified stare of her eagle. You just can''t make lighthearted banter with someone who''s got an eagle on their shoulder.', 'Just when you thought this woman couldn''t emit any more <kanji>dignity</kanji>, an <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) swoops down and perches on her shoulder. The huge canine behind her already gave her a dignified aura, but this eagle has made her look positively awe-inspiring.', 'N1', NULL),
(1853, 'Picture sealing other things with dirt. Like the bricks in your wall. Or the hole in your floor. Dirt can seal in anything.', 'Dig up your sealed food and eat it in the comfort of your own home. Mm, delicious!', 'You can also <kanji>seal</kanji> <reading>foo</reading>d (ふう) in dirt to make it last longer. That''s how jerky came about, you know. Seal some meat underground and in a few months you''ll have some tasty dried meats to chew.

By the way, this has another reading you should probably know. It''s ほう. Most sealing is done in the <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう), you know? So just think about eating food in your own home!', 'N2', NULL),
(1854, 'Compare the two things in your mind and make them the same.', 'とうきょう is a really weird city now that they passed the cylinder law. Imagine it so deeply that it weirds you out.', 'Picture a <kanji>cylinder</kanji> shaped <reading>とう</reading>きょう. All the buildings are cylinders. The city itself is cylinder shaped too. Even... gasp! The people of とうきょう!', 'N2', NULL),
(1856, 'Obviously it''s not good enough consolation. Be furious about this.', 'Now you have absolutely nothing. Not even a consolation flag.', 'Even though you weren''t happy with the flag, you were even more frustrated to watch an <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) swoop down and grab the flag and fly away with it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1857, 'They may seem gruff and rude at first, but they''ll still let you in their home in the middle of winter unannounced and offer you tea, a warm fire, and a place to sleep.', 'Picture a courteous badger taking care of an injured, and now homeless, condor. It''s fluffing the condor''s pillows, bringing it soup, and patting it on the shoulder saying, "There, there."', 'Badgers are especially <kanji>courteous</kanji> to <reading>con</reading>dors (こん). Condors live in the trees while badgers live in burrows under those trees. When humans tear down the condor''s homes, the badgers who live below them always let them stay over until they get back on their feet.', 'N1', NULL),
(1858, '"What''s in your heart is no good," he chastises you.', 'In the 潮 kanji you saved Mrs. Chou from dying, so he is chastising you for it.', 'Why is he chastising you, really, though? He foretold Mrs. Chou coming back in a previous mnemonic, but you saved Mrs. Chou on the beach (once again, in a previous mnemonic), so you could have prevented her second coming. He is <kanji>chastis</kanji>ing you for saving Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう).', 'N1', NULL),
(1859, 'There''s a hand just chafing against some trees. That''s... interesting.', 'This is the hand of a child looking for its mama. And, it chafes against trees to try and find the warm, soft touch of its mama. Instead, it only finds rough bark, so it continues its chafing.', 'As the hand <kanji>chafe</kanji>s on the trees, it says <reading>ma</reading>ma (ま), mama.', 'N1', NULL),
(1860, 'Have you ever been to a Japanese festival? There''s always one of those portable shrines that are basically just roofs people hold up. That''s what you''re trying to hold with your fingers as the whole thing rubs against them.', 'Picture Satsuki sitting on the roof as you carry it through the festival. She may be a little girl, but her extra weight makes the rubbing so much worse — you can really feel the skin chafing.', 'Just when you thought the <kanji>rub</kanji>bing couldn''t get any worse, <reading>Satsu</reading>ki (さつ) appears and hops up onto the roof. AAGH! It hurts!', 'N1', NULL),
(1861, 'Imagine giving someone a good, hard slap in the face. You''re all up in their business now.', 'Say that out loud, using ぼく. You''re the boss, the boss of slap business.', 'ぼく is one way to say "I" in Japanese, usually used by dudes. But you''re going to use it now when you <kanji>slap</kanji> this guy. Who''s in everybody''s business, slapping them? <reading>ぼく</reading>.', 'N1', NULL),
(1862, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'It''s also simultaneously here and there at the same time. It moves really fast. And simultaneously glowing blue and white. Your saber is a lot of things all at once!', 'What <kanji>simultaneously</kanji> protects and kills? Your holy blue <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい).', 'N1', NULL),
(1863, 'Taste that delicious sun. It doesn''t even burn!', 'You had your eyes closed because the sun is so bright. When you open them you realize the delicious sun you were eating is really just a giant, really bright, cooked sheep. You should probably point this out to NASA or something.', 'This <kanji>delicious</kanji> taste is... familiar somehow. You take a few more bites and then scream. It tastes like <reading>shee</reading>p (し)!', 'N1', NULL),
(1864, 'Imagine gently poking the tree with your spear. You glance around nervously, hoping no one notices how easy on it you''re being.', 'The jewel has a little note on it, "Thank you for being gentle." Did... did the tree just reward you for being gentle?!', 'You''re being really <kanji>gentle</kanji> to the tree when a <reading>jewel</reading> (じゅう) falls out of its branches and hits you on the head.', 'N2', NULL),
(1865, 'Tsunamis are big. Pirates are small. Simple as that.', 'Imagine this dignified chin-holding-up-high pirate. Nod your head, because you know he got what he deserves.', 'As the tsunami comes to <kanji>sink</kanji> the pirate, the pirate holds his <reading>chin</reading> (ちん) up high. He will die with dignity.', 'N2', NULL),
(1867, 'Everything from winter is melting and trickling and thawing. All the dripping sounds fill you with a peace you can barely describe.', 'Imagine carefully dipping all of your ties in the peaceful spring waters and hearing that sweet sound you love at all times.', 'You want to feel this <kanji>peace</kanji> all the time, not just in spring, so you dip your <reading>tie</reading> (たい) in the spring water. Now your tie is making the peaceful dripping sounds you love.', 'N1', NULL),
(1868, 'A tsunami, a canine attack, and a fire disaster make for quite a triple-whammy. What''d you do to make the universe want to destroy you so badly?', 'Imagine pulling on your Mets jersey only to suddenly receive a tsunami warning on your phone. Then, you hear a canine growling and catch a whiff of something burning nearby. Uh-oh, the universe is trying to destroy you again!', 'Actually, the universe wants to <kanji>destroy</kanji> you because you''re a <reading>Mets</reading> (めつ) fan. The universe despises the Mets — why do you think they haven''t won a World Series since 1986?', 'N1', NULL),
(1869, 'The stuff in horns is great for fertilizer. So is poop. So is water (as long as it''s fresh water). So nourishing!', 'Imagine him making soil made out of those things. Jesus approves of this nourishing soil.', 'This is the kind of <kanji>nourishing</kanji> soil that <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ) would make if he was into making soil, probably.', 'N1', NULL),
(1870, 'Open the fire door and feel the heat of the <kanji>furnace</kanji>. Hot!', 'Smell the tar baking from the furnace. Feel the heat from the furnace. Fix the road, while you''re at it.', 'You''re using the <kanji>furnace</kanji> to melt tar to put on the <reading>ro</reading>ad (ろ).', 'N1', NULL),
(1871, 'Imagine yourself playing a harp or a zither, even if you don''t know how. Entertain these kings to the best of your ability, right now. That''s why you''re doing it and not someone who knows what they''re doing. They want it now.', 'Your harp playing is pretty bad, so you put your coat on just in case you need to leave in a hurry to escape an angry audience.', 'You may already know that a Japanese <kanji>harp</kanji> is called a こと, but here''s a mnemonic to help you if you don''t:

When playing your <kanji>harp</kanji>, you always wear your <reading>coat</reading> (こと).', 'N1', NULL),
(2115, 'Crystals take light and refract it around into many directions. That''s why there are three suns in this kanji.', 'Imagine the Shougun lying in his bed of crystals. He still has his really serious, angry face though. Even as he rubs the crystals to his face, he''s glowering.', 'Who is hoarding and harnessing the power of these <kanji>crystal</kanji>s? That would be the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう).', 'N1', NULL),
(1872, 'If you make sure to know the radicals, you''ll also make sure to know the kanji too, automagically.', 'Imagine Mr. Sun measuring distances and rations and whatnot just before going to war — maps, compasses, all kinds of measurement devices piled on his war table. Also, don''t forget that in Chinese Sun is pronounced more like "soon."', '"Calculate your enemy''s position with accurate <kanji>measurement</kanji>," is more or less what <reading>Sun</reading> (すん) Tzu said, I think. The guy wrote The Art of War, you know. When Sun says measurements are important, you don''t question him.', 'N1', NULL),
(1873, 'Not to <kanji>dragon</kanji> with it, but make sure you know your radicals so you know your kanji!', 'Then imagine after his transformation he attacks you using his Street Fighter moves. "Hadouken!"', 'Imagine a <kanji>dragon</kanji>. You are talking to it, which is already pretty strange, but as you''re talking, he turns into the Street Fighter character <reading>Ryu</reading> (りゅう).', 'N1', NULL),
(1874, 'Picture Wolverine and a pig hanging precariously over the edge of this cliff. What a crazy way to form a relation! It''ll all be for naught if the thread snaps, though…', 'Moral of the story: stay away from edges, because they might spell the end for you and all your earthly relations.', 'Just as it seems like they''ll be able to climb back from the <kanji>edge</kanji>, the thread snaps, and Wolverine and his new pig friend plunge to a tragic <reading>en</reading>d (えん). What a shame — they had just begun to form such a friendly <kanji>relation</kanji>, too…', 'N1', NULL),
(1875, 'Imagine a <kanji>wing</kanji> that is made of a rice paddy and a feather put together. Doesn''t make sense, looks ugly, and probably will not fly. Imagine it anyways. It is your wing.', 'Imagine using the yolks to stick everything together. It''s all over your hands, and things are pretty nasty.', 'To make the feather and rice paddy stick together you need some kind of sticking agent. This time we find the original bird that the feather came from and grab its eggs. We break the eggs open and use the <reading>yolk</reading> (よく). It''s pretty sticky, but still probably not aircraft safe. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1876, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Your good luck kitchen kits have charms that say things like, "good food," "good company," and "good luck not cutting off your fingers with kitchen knives."', 'For <kanji>good luck</kanji>, you keep special <reading>kits</reading> (きつ) in your <reading>kitch</reading>en (きち). These kitchen kits contain good luck charms.', 'N1', NULL),
(1877, 'Please, just know your radicals. Please!', 'Hah! Slash! Hah! Thrust! Hah! Parry! There you go — now you''re really using that blade!', 'When you stab your <kanji>blade</kanji> into an enemy you shout "<reading>Hah</reading> (は)!" You''ve gotta get some oomph behind that thrust, after all.', 'N1', NULL),
(1878, 'To endure something is to conceal how it makes you feel, after all. Now imagine there''s a blade pressed close to your heart. Try to endure the pain and conceal your emotions, or your heart might be cut.', 'No one knows better than a ninja how enduring and concealing are closely related. Go ahead, hold your breath and try walking soundlessly around your house, concealing your footsteps. Not easy to endure, is it?', 'Learning to <kanji>endure</kanji> and <kanji>conceal</kanji> is all part of your <reading>nin</reading>ja (にん) training. You must learn to stay totally still, without breathing — you have to be able to endure this if you want to conceal yourself. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1879, 'Of course, this tree omen of a <kanji>peach</kanji> refers to momotaro, the little boy who was born from a peach (you should look it up if you don''t know this children''s story).', 'It grows bigger everytime you say "MORE MORE" twice. If you say it just once, it stays the same.', 'If <reading>momo</reading>taro (もも) wasn''t enough, think about the <kanji>peach</kanji> growing larger and larger. You want it to grow bigger, so you yell at it: "<reading>MO</reading>RE! <reading>MO</reading>RE!" (もも).', 'N1', NULL),
(1882, 'You walk up to a temple and you''re surprised to find that there''s a samurai guarding it. Talk to him for a while so he can tell you why he has been doing this job. He''s quite a nice guy, actually.', 'If you know the meaning you know the reading as well! That doesn''t happen too often with kanji, so consider yourself lucky!', 'The reading is <reading>samurai</reading>, which should be easy enough to remember.', 'N1', NULL),
(1885, 'You ask the woman what she does for fun... for recreation. She says, she goes out and shoots her catapult.', 'Get out of here, Godzilla! I''m gonna shoot you.', 'Her favorite thing to shoot at with her catapult for <kanji>recreation</kanji> is <reading>Go</reading>dzilla (ご).', 'N1', NULL),
(1886, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Your mom comes rushing in and smacks you on the head. "Fool!" she says, "This is toe soup, what did you think you''d ladle up?!" Oops.', 'You use a <kanji>ladle</kanji> to distribute soup. But as you''re ladling you find something odd floating in the soup. It''s... a <reading>toe</reading> (と)! You scream, dropping the ladle and the toe on the ground.', 'N1', NULL),
(1887, 'Vermilion is like an orange red, which is perfect for visibility purposes.', 'The vermilion chute spits you out and you spit out your lunch. And it looks vermilion. Uh oh. Hospital. Please.', 'You jump onto the <kanji>vermilion</kanji> slide. It''s actually more of a steep <reading>chu</reading>te (しゅ). And... oh... urp... it''s making you sick.', 'N1', NULL),
(1888, 'Imagine climbing up the hill. The sun is coming up behind the axe,  silhouetting the whole thing very nicely.', 'You take the axe out of the hill and cut off some cucumber to eat. Mm mm!', 'You reach the top of the <kanji>hill</kanji> and realize the entire hill is covered in <reading>cu</reading>cumbers (きゅう). The axe has a little note on it that says "Cucumber Hill Axe - Please Use."', 'N1', NULL),
(1889, 'Imagine looking up at a tree full of thick-stemmed, jumbo-size pears. You pull out your knife and get ready to hack them all down, one by one.', 'Imagine a beautiful, ripe pear in your mind. Now gnash your teeth. Mmm. You want to eat a pear right now, don''t you?', 'As you cut the <kanji>pear</kanji>s down, you can''t help but <reading>gnash</reading> (なし) your teeth. You want to eat these pears right now. You gnash and grind and wait though, because you want to have them all down before you start the feast.', 'N1', NULL),
(1892, 'You were born with mouth vines (a rare condition where your mouth is always full of vines), so you''ve been shouting all your life. And yet people still can''t hear you. It''s very frustrating.', 'Imagine the word きょうと not coming out clearly because of your mouth vines. People can only hear the きょう part, so they don''t know where you want to go. SHOUT LOUDER! きょうと！', 'You need to <kanji>shout</kanji> so that people can understand you through the mouth vines. You shout, "<reading>きょう</reading>と!" because you hear there is a temple there for people with your condition.', 'N2', NULL),
(1893, 'Imagine yourself fishing in a gold pool, catching golden fish. What a great <kanji>fishing</kanji> spot this is.', 'Count your golden fish. They''re so nice! They''re also worth their weight in gold, because, well, they are gold.', 'You caught a couple of gold fish. <reading>Two</reading> (つ) gold fish, actually!', 'N1', NULL),
(1894, 'Just feel your hair, it''s such a good friend, and you wish it was longer.', 'Pretend to pray to the great hair 神. What is his reply? Does he give you more hair?', 'Who gave you this hair? Probably the great <reading>神</reading> (かみ) of hair. You deserve this great hair.', 'N3', NULL),
(1895, 'If you think about it makes sense. Those mountain winds that come down from the mountain are cold and windy and nasty. When they mix with warmer air down below, it creates a nasty <kanji>storm</kanji>.', 'The rash comes from being wet and not drying off when you get home. Your skin gets irritated by this moisture.', 'A <kanji>storm</kanji> is nothing to scoff at. If you get hit by the storm and get all wet and nasty and electrocuted, you''re bound to get <reading>a rash</reading> (あらし).', 'N1', NULL),
(1899, 'Just imagine a princess saying "UGH, I''m just like a servant, I have to do all the crap you tell me." Then, all the servants roll their eyes, because, really, she''s a princess, lucky duck.', 'The servants all know they should tell the king, because the princess is a servant to the king, just as they are, plus they don''t want to lose their heads.', 'The <kanji>princess</kanji> goes on to do whatever she wants. She tells the real servants, "Don''t tell my daddy, the king, I did this. <reading>He may</reading> (ひめ) not like it."', 'N1', NULL),
(1901, 'If you think about this one it''ll make good sense. Just imagine rice stalks standing up into the air.', 'He has his hands flat, and stabs his hands into the grains. Yah! Yah! Yah!', 'You have a box of <kanji>grains</kanji>. <reading>Ryuu</reading> (りゅう) from Street Fighter is doing training by punching the grains to strengthen his fingers.', 'N2', NULL),
(1902, 'This makes a lot of sense when you think about it, right? The muzzle of the cannon wraps around the rock, then you shoot the rock. Boom!', 'Watch as the cannon goes off, the rock hurtles into your home. And just like that, there is a huge, rock-shaped hole in it. You''ll have to shoot a few more rocks at your home with this cannon before it''s completely gone.', 'You aim the <kanji>cannon</kanji> at your own <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう). You don''t want to have a home anymore, you want to become a pirate, live on a boat, and (of course) shoot cannons all day long.', 'N1', NULL),
(1903, 'Imagine hearing the thunder closing in on you as you frantically try to escape the rice paddy. RUN!!!', 'This rye will save your life. It’s just you and this pile of rye now. Take a deeeeep breath in, and really smell the thunder and rye in the air. You’re fine. You’re cozy. This rye will be your savior.', 'You can’t run fast enough to escape the <kanji>thunder</kanji>, so you decide to hide under a pile of… <reading>rye</reading> (らい)?! ', 'N1', NULL),
(1904, 'Imagine a bunch of these. They look like big fangs poking out of the ground (that is, until they bloom and get into the air).', 'You look at all the sprouts. They have all turned into 目s, looking straight at you. You''re terrified.', 'When the <kanji>sprout</kanji> gets out of the ground and opens up, instead of a flower there is a human-looking <reading>目</reading> (め), looking right at you.', 'N1', NULL),
(1905, 'Imagine walking in the woods and coming across a huge tower made of flowers. It goes so high you can hardly see the top.', 'How do you think the とうきょう tower came to be, anyway? It was once a big flower tower. They just replaced it with metal later. BOOM! History bomb, you''re welcome.', 'The only place flowers grow into <kanji>tower</kanji>s like this is in <reading>とう</reading>きょう. とうきょう dirt is the best. とうきょう flowers are the best. Thus, you end up with a lot of とうきょう towers.', 'N2', NULL),
(9312, 'You feel absolutely terrified looking at the moon with all its branches consisting of limbs, lifelessly flopping around. How did this happen?', 'The moon sheep are always up to no good, so this must all have been devised by them. Those darn moon sheep!', 'After some consideration, you know who put all those <vocabulary>limb</vocabulary>s on the moon. It must''ve been none other than the moon <reading>shee</reading>p (し)!', 'N1', NULL),
(1855, 'Picture that axe, jammed into the mountain''s <kanji>peak</kanji>. The person who did that must have been really cool to get all the way up there.', 'Go ahead, imagine this peak with an axe in it and actually gawk a bit at how far you have to go to get that high.
', 'You can''t help but <reading>gawk</reading> (がく) at the <kanji>peak</kanji>. Somehow it looks even higher now.
', 'N1', NULL),
(1906, 'Then you went back to being crazy. That moment of lucidity was only temporary, and you go back to stockpiling beans.', 'Imagine yourself wearing a sharp suit and being a model of lucidity.', 'Your <kanji>lucidity</kanji> is clear to everyone because you wear a <reading>su</reading>it (す). Everyone knows that lucid people wear suits!', 'N1', NULL),
(1907, 'Spears are spears. It makes sense!', 'How do you like it? Don''t stab cows with spears, you monster.', 'You take this <kanji>spear</kanji> and stab it into a cow. "<reading>MOO</reading> (む)!" it cries. The cow bites the spear out of your hands and stabs you back. "MOO!" you scream.', 'N1', NULL),
(1908, 'Touch it and feel the "moon table." You know it''s not made of moon. How ridiculous.', 'You''d think skin would be softer, but it''s pretty hard. Much harder than you expected.', 'But, when you go to touch this "moon table" made of <kanji>skin</kanji>, you realize that it''s <reading>harder</reading> (はだ) than you expected. ', 'N2', NULL),
(1909, 'Boats are boats, you know?', 'Since 船 and 舟 both mean "boat", this reading should be pretty easy to remember!', 'The reading is the same reading that you learned with the vocabulary 船.', 'N2', NULL),
(1910, 'Every village has a bell of its own, standing tall and proud and shiny in the middle for everyone to see and hear.', 'The Shougun bell even sounds like it''s warning you that the Shougun has arrived. It makes a loud, low shooooouu shooou sound. ', 'The <kanji>bell</kanji> rings whenever the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) arrives in the village. Its ringing echoes throughout the entire village so everyone knows they have to come out and pay their respects.', 'N1', NULL),
(1911, 'And the villain always gets to the treasure chest first, often via nefarious means. That villain is SO important when it comes to treasure chests.', 'Arrrg! If only you had been closer to きょうと! Then you might have beaten this dastardly villain.', 'The <kanji>villain</kanji> beat you all the way to <reading>きょう</reading>と to get this particular treasure chest. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1912, 'Picture a lump of dirt. Is there a demon hiding under there? No. It''s just a saying, ya nerd.', '塊 is derived from 固まり, which is the noun form of 固まる (to be hardened). You know a lump is usually something that''s compressed and hardened.', 'A <kanji>lump</kanji> of something is just a <reading>固まり</reading> (かたまり) — the hardened thing itself.', 'N1', NULL),
(1913, 'My animals can''t protect themselves, that''s why I hunt other animals instead.', 'Imagine hunting down animals in a car.', 'I also do all my <kanji>hunt</kanji>s using a <reading>ca</reading>r (か). I just run those animals down with my car. Kind of cheating, but vroom vroom anyways.', 'N1', NULL),
(1915, 'Right now, however, the demon is looming over you, and you can''t see anything in the cloud above its head. It just looks mad, like it''s gonna pummel you or eat you.', 'POOF! Now there''s a condor spirit floating in front of you, crying. "Thank you, human. You''ve saved my soul!" it says, and float/flies off and disappears.', 'You shout out and beg its <kanji>soul</kanji> for help until, finally, you see the cloud take the shape of a <reading>con</reading>dor (こん). Aha! "You were a condor before you became a demon! Remember who you are and turn back!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1916, 'This leg stamp not only proved you went, but lets everyone around you know you''ve been to the moon before!', 'Picture Kyaku lifting up their pant leg and showing you all the stamps from all the different places they''ve been. There are even some planets on there!', 'You''ve only met one other person with the same mark on their <kanji>leg</kanji> and it was <reading>Kyaku</reading> (きゃく) the customer. Kyaku visits many places all over the world, so it only makes sense that they''ve also been off-world too.', 'N1', NULL),
(1917, 'The radical meaning and the kanji meaning aren''t the same, but we''ll get through this together. Just put a considerable amount of effort into this and you''ll learn considerably more quickly.', 'Also, things that are "considerably" anything start somewhere. They <reading>become</reading> (なり) whatever "considerable" thing they are now from something smaller before.', 'Speaking of <kanji>considerably</kanji> terrifying, that place known as <reading>Nar</reading>nia (なり) is pretty terrifying. Not sure why children want to go there.', 'N1', NULL),
(1920, 'Compare this to a man who is a nurse. You should probably call him mister. That''s why you know a woman who is a nurse is "miss."', 'When you think of "miss," remember the time Joe asked you to call him "Miss Joe". He’s this big, ten foot tall guy, but now he suddenly wants you to call him Miss Joe. Well, we’re all for respecting everbody’s personal choices here, so we better get used to it.', '"Call me <kanji>Miss</kanji> <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう)," Joe says to you.', 'N1', NULL),
(1921, 'Look at the different shadow patterns. Know what they all mean. This is your very own calendar system.', 'Make sure you make the connection between the word 歴史 and this kanji. If you do, it''ll be much easier to remember the reading of this kanji.', 'Remember the word 歴史, which means "history"? Using a <kanji>calendar</kanji>, you too can remember history (otherwise, who knows when these things happened). So, the reading for this kanji is the same as the reading for 歴史''s beginning, because they''re related.', 'N1', NULL),
(1922, 'You look up at the sky, trying to find the sun, but it''s so cloudy that you can''t even find where the sun should be.', 'If it''s cloudy there are clouds, so if you can read one, you can read the other!', 'Remember 雲? It means "cloud" and has the same reading as "cloudy," making them both <reading>くも</reading>.', 'N2', NULL),
(1923, 'You don''t see omens that often, so of course you''re going to stare. We''ll imagine what the omen is in the reading mnemonic.', 'Whenever you see an omen (radical), remember that the omen will probably have Mrs. Chou in it!', 'As you <kanji>stare</kanji> at the omen, you see Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう) knocking down buildings, putting nails on the street, and making herself way longer than you ever thought possible. Oh, and there she is, destroying the world.', 'N1', NULL),
(1924, 'You''re like one of those nudes in a Renaissance painting, strategically covering yourself with a bunch of grapes or a well-placed apple or something.', 'Whatever you do, don''t Google "Ra Egyptian god naked" to see how naked he actually got. There''s no need to taint your browser history for the sake of kanji learning.', 'You know who else is often <kanji>naked</kanji>? The ancient Egyptian god <reading>Ra</reading> (ら). Well, half-naked at least. Egypt is a hot country, so you can''t really blame Ra for wanting to strip down.', 'N1', NULL),
(1927, 'Your cram school thinks that you’ll learn better sitting in a circle in the dirt. So there you are in the dirt, receiving your lessons.', 'Imagine a jukebox playing loudly right beside you while you’re cramming away. Hard to believe, but the jukebox''s melodies do actually amplify the cramming fervor, making you just cram, cram, cram!', 'Not only does your <kanji>cram school</kanji> teach kids in a circle in the dirt, it also plays music on a <reading>juke</reading>box (じゅく). Yes, a jukebox. They believe a jukebox''s tunes help with cramming.', 'N1', NULL),
(1928, 'See all the toes lined up on the <kanji>table</kanji>. They are the ones who showed up early.', 'Imagine the toes talking to each other. "Hello Mr. Toe!" "Oh hello other Mr. Toe!"', 'What do all the early toes on the <kanji>table</kanji> do while they''re waiting until it''s the right time? They just sit around on their table, <reading>talk</reading>ing (たく).', 'N1', NULL),
(1929, 'It''s a single, perfectly round stone under this canopy of trees. It didn''t start this way, but thanks to all the polishing you did, it has become nice and smooth.', 'There''s only this one stone under these trees, and polishing the stone is the only job here. Still, you don''t get much from it, thus the meager life of polishing your stone.', 'You <kanji>polish</kanji> the stone due to your <reading>meager</reading> (みが) lifestyle.', 'N2', NULL),
(1930, 'Imagine how much bacteria would flower in your mouth if you just left grains in there for like, I dunno, a month. That''s a lot of nasty bacteria!', 'Picture the King''s mouth up close. Look at all the bacteria squiggling around and through his teeth and gums. Bleh! Barf!', 'There is a lot of this <kanji>bacteria</kanji> in the <reading>Kin</reading>g (きん) of England''s mouth. He eats a lot of grains, and he won''t let anyone touch his mouth to clean it, but he refuses to do things himself... so it''s absolutely chock full of bacteria.', 'N1', NULL),
(1931, 'This shade is really convenient right now, because it''s been such a hot day. Ahh, shade is so nice and cool.', 'Picture an inn with a cloud right above it right now. Look at the cloud-shaped shade keeping everyone visiting the inn cool and sunburn-free.', 'The building that''s getting this nice <kanji>shade</kanji> is the <reading>inn</reading> (いん) you''re staying at.', 'N1', NULL),
(1932, 'The ghosts are pinned to the ground by the spikes, unable to move. ', 'A ray gun, coincidentally, is the only thing that can kill a ghost, and there it lays, just out of reach. You''ll have to be the one to pull the trigger of the ray gun. Do you pick up the ray gun out of the row boat to destroy the ghosts?', 'You come across the <kanji>ghost</kanji>s, who are stabbed into the ground. "Please," they beg. "Please use that <reading>ray</reading> (れい) gun that''s in that <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat and end our suffering."', 'N1', NULL),
(1933, 'The sun did a little bit of work, so they aren''t wet, but they aren''t dry either. They''re damp.', 'Luckily the shih tzu is kinda pinned between the spikes, so it isn''t dead or anything, but it''s definitely stuck. You try to help it out, but you slip because it''s so damp and you fall in too! Imagine the sound of your cries mixing in with the shih tzu''s as you both struggling to get out but can''t because of how damp everything is.', 'As you''re looking at the <kanji>damp</kanji> spikes, a <reading>shih tzu</reading> (しつ) prances over, slips on the damp ground, and falls right on the spike! Oh nooo!', 'N2', NULL),
(1934, 'Think of a stone being renewed like it is having something like a spa day. Pedicure, manicure, the whole shebang. It’ll come out all stiff and hardened, cause that’s how good stones are.', 'If only you could ask こういち to give you massages again, but he''s too busy for you, now (being emperor and all).', 'You remember how you used to cure these <kanji>stiff</kanji> body times: your old friend <reading>こう</reading>いち would just massage them out for you.', 'N2', NULL),
(1935, 'Imagine looking at the grains in the houses of people who make vastly different earnings. The poor artist has only a few sacks of grain. The rich samurai has an extra house just for holding his grain. That''s how you know he has much higher earnings than the artist.', 'Picture your own earnings. Can you afford a lot of grains and a car?', 'Another way to check someone''s <kanji>earnings</kanji> is to see if they have a <reading>ca</reading>r (か). Would someone who doesn''t make a lot of money have a car? No!', 'N1', NULL),
(1936, 'She''s in your house because you married her. Thus, she''s your bride. Your house is now a home.', 'Your bride is very upset at how sexist you''re being right now, and makes you do all the cleaning instead. The maid still gets fired.', 'Now that you have a <kanji>bride</kanji> in your house, you can get rid of <reading>yo</reading>ur <reading>mai</reading>d (よめ), since your bride will do all the cleaning.', 'N1', NULL),
(1937, 'To be fair, it''s pretty obvious who would win. Nobody knows what that lifeguard was thinking.', '"This lifeguard didn''t believe in the power of kanji learning," こういち says to the press. "He was punished by the great Crabigator, who sent its tsunami as a warning for all."

Hint: when you see the "lifeguard" radical, the reading is probably going to be こう.', 'In the <kanji>gutter</kanji> is a lifeguard, so Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち decides to survey the damage.', 'N1', NULL),
(1938, 'Can you see why a tsunami dragon (especially the Asian skinny snake-like kind) could be a waterfall? Try to imagine it.', 'Now imagine it being a bit tacky. Dragons are alright but... you just don''t want to overdo it, you know?', 'Despite how cool a dragon <kanji>waterfall</kanji> is, to be honest it''s also pretty <reading>tacky</reading> (たき).', 'N1', NULL),
(1939, 'While we''re speaking of animals, he''s absolutely bat sh*t crazy.', 'I think he maybe got too friendly with some raccoons and beavers and went crazy. Watch out for him if you live in きょうと, rabies is no joke.', 'You might see that <kanji>crazy</kanji> <kanji>lunatic</kanji> hanging around the outskirts of <reading>きょう</reading>と. He''ll lurk near your trash cans and scream, "I AM THE ANIMAL KING!" and run away with some orange peels.', 'N1', NULL),
(1940, 'Imagine the sheep with feathers taking off and flying around. It''s an odd sight, but makes sense, kind of.', 'Imagine yourself enjoying the cozy car-kettle brew, while admiring the unusual sight of the sheep with feathers and wings flying through the sky. This is just shear bliss!', 'Watching that sheep with feathers and wings flying around made you thirsty. You remember that you have a <reading>ca</reading>r <reading>ke</reading>ttle (かけ), so you reach into your car and grab your car kettle to make a special car-kettle brew.', 'N1', NULL),
(1941, 'Make sure the dirt you''re using to make black ink is really, really black. Like the kind you find around volcanoes or something.', 'Imagine yelling "SUE ME!" at someone''s face, which is covered in the black ink you spilled there.', 'Be careful that you don''t spill <kanji>black ink</kanji> on anyone or anything. If you do, you have to own it. Imagine spilling black ink on someone, and when they confront you, you yell, "Yeah I got black ink on you, so <reading>sue me</reading> (すみ)!"', 'N1', NULL),
(1942, 'Look at these nine birds strutting down the street like they own the place. The dove and pigeon are out in front because they are the most important. Hear them chirping and fluttering their feathers about.', 'Devour it. You love pigeon hearts. Discard the rest, keep the heart.', 'Take a look at the <kanji>pigeon</kanji> in front of you. Now, grab it, tear it in half, and grab the <reading>heart</reading> (はと) from its little body. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1943, 'He''s calm now because he stomped right into a farmer''s field (thus the rice) and if he didn''t calm down he''d probably ruin it.', 'Picture Wolverine sitting calmly in this field, onions causing tears to stream down his cheeks as he reflects on what he''s done.', 'Now that Wolverine is <kanji>calm</kanji>, tears come to his eyes. Being calm has given him perspective on what his anger has done. Oh, wait no. There are just some <reading>on</reading>ions (おん) in his cleats too, and they''re making calm tears trickle down his eyes.', 'N1', NULL),
(1944, 'Picture a huge barracks made of dull, almost brown, gold. Maybe you should have picked a better material.', 'You stand cowering in front of the don as he inspects how dull the gold barracks are. You don''t wanna be off''d for this just because the don was dull enough to make you use gold!', 'As you stare sadly at the <kanji>dull</kanji> barracks, the <reading>don</reading> (どん) shows up out of nowhere, and he''s pissed. He turns his angry gaze toward you and says, "How did my barracks get so dull? Are you at fault? This is bad, this is very bad. Someone will pay for this."', 'N2', NULL),
(1945, 'You thought the devil hangs out in hell, but he actually hangs out under this canopy of trees.', 'Into the trees is where all mamas go missing. And so the devil searches for his mama under this canopy of trees.', 'Why is the <kanji>devil</kanji> under a canopy of trees? Because he''s looking for his <reading>ma</reading>ma (ま).', 'N1', NULL),
(1946, 'Imagine looking at a dormitory chore wheel and seeing it''s your turn to carry in the charcoal this week. Bleh!', 'The dormitory row boat leaks a little too, so by the time you get back you really need to light the charcoal and heat up!', 'The <kanji>dormitory</kanji> is on an island, so when you go out to get the charcoal you have to take a <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat. You row and row and row until you reach the shed with all the charcoal. Then you row and row and row it back to the dormitory.', 'N1', NULL),
(1947, 'Imagine preparing this tray, just before your ancestors arrive in friendly ghost form to share part of your meal with you. They only come once a year, so make sure it''s a nice tray, okay?', 'Tap the bone china plate with your knuckle bone. Hear the pleasant clink it makes as you set it on the tray.', 'The plate you put on the <kanji>tray</kanji> for the <kanji>ancestral festival</kanji> is made of <reading>bone</reading> (ぼん). Bone china, that is. How fancy… And appropriate, considering bone china is made from actual bone.', 'N1', NULL),
(1948, 'Look at this big tree building over in the east. Imagine actually using it as a building and moving in. And why not? It''s free real estate.', 'Imagine looking down on とうきょう from the top floor of this magnificent building you''ve just moved into. The sun is rising behind you, slowly illuminating the city below.', 'That <kanji>building</kanji> happens to be in the center of <reading>とう</reading>きょう. If you need to find east (which is where とうきょう is), you just look for the building.', 'N1', NULL),
(1951, 'Would you rather have your heart up on the roof, close to falling but out of the way of people. Or would you rather have your heart in a net, which people can''t steal from, but is in the way of cars.', '"I''d rather you didn''t have that!" you shout as your neighbors run through the neighborhood to their own house. You''d rather this wasn''t happening! You''d rather your neighbors let you make your own choice!!', 'As you try to decide which you''d <kanji>rather</kanji> have, your <reading>neigh</reading>bors (ねい) come out and grab the heart out of your hands.', 'N1', NULL),
(1953, 'Imagine how sad you''ll feel this year when you turn that many years old and have to give up your small canine. You can''t believe this much time has already passed — where have all the years gone?', 'No matter how many years old you are, everyone loves kanji with easy readings.', 'Remember the vocab word 〜才 which also meant "<kanji>years old</kanji>"? Well, this has the same reading: さい.', 'N3', NULL),
(1955, 'Imagine looking at a monkey at the zoo. Aww, it''s so cute. But then it turns around and kicks some dirt right into your aww-ing mouth. Yuck! Why monkey, why?!', 'Picture the monkey saluting you as you spit dirt out of your mouth. Taste the bitter dirt and the bitter shame. You were played by a monkey.', 'You spit the dirt out of your mouth and look back at the <kanji>monkey</kanji> to see that it''s <reading>salu</reading>ting (さる) you. Is... is it mocking you?', 'N1', NULL),
(1956, 'Oh, did you think your pupil was a hole? No, no, you misunderstand. That''s where the village stands. It''s full of little people who whisper to your brain what you should see.', 'Even if you''re a pupil non-believer, at least you can see how it''s shaped like a doughnut hole, right?', 'All of the people in your <kanji>pupil</kanji> eat <reading>dou</reading>ghnuts (どう) all day long. That''s why your eye is shaped like a doughnut, with the "hole" (village) as their home.', 'N1', NULL),
(1958, 'Despite it raining, your orders are to make sure none (zero!) of the rain spills anywhere. Figure out what you need to do to make sure you follow these rain orders successfully. Zero spill, alright?', 'You just take your ray gun and "zap!" the clouds are gone. There will be zero spills today.

Hint: when you see the radical <radical>orders</radical> it''s fairly likely the reading will be れい.', 'The best way to make sure there is <kanji>zero</kanji> <kanji>spill</kanji> when it rains is to get rid of the rain. You use a special <reading>ray</reading> (れい) gun and zap the rain right out of the sky. There will be zero spills because there will be zero rain.', 'N2', NULL),
(1959, 'Look at this crab placing stones on the go board. Hear them click and clack as the crab places them with expert precision.', 'Go is go. Easy!', '<kanji>Go</kanji> is pronounced <reading>go</reading> (ご) in Japanese too. How convenient is that? Like, super convenient. So easy even a crab could remember!', 'N1', NULL),
(1960, 'People in top hats like to put tariffs on lots of things. Especially things you need, like grains you eat to stay alive. You have no choice but to pay.', 'Everything you get from other counties has been tariffed except for soap. You''ll be hungry, but at least you''ll be clean.', 'You''re listening to all the things in this <kanji>tariff</kanji> and you finally get fed up and ask what isn''t being taxed now. The top hat man looks at you and smiles, holding out his hand. In his hand is some <reading>soa</reading>p (そ).', 'N1', NULL),
(1961, 'A secluded place has no toilets, so you just poop right on the mountain.', 'Imagine hundreds of youths on this secluded mountain, all pooping on the ground while trying to get a cell phone signal.', 'The <kanji>secluded</kanji> Poop Poop Mountain is a favorite of <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう). They go there to get away from their phones to get in touch with nature again. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1962, 'Picture the bubbles bursting out of the wrapped up tsunami as it crashes down. The air that the tsunami sucked in while it was wrapping up come out as bubbles big and small.', 'Watch helplessly as your home, now trapped in a bubble, floats away into the sky.', 'One of the biggest <kanji>bubbles</kanji> heads straight for your <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう), engulfs it, and picks it up into the air!', 'N1', NULL),
(1963, 'Imagine if you had a habit of eating hot peppers whenever you thought you might be getting sick. To be fair, it flushes out your sinuses and tastes good, so it''s not the worst habit you could have.', 'Unfortunately, now you''ve gotten into the habit of telling everyone what to do when they get sick. "Yeah, yeah, eat a hot pepper, we know. But couscous is better," they say before you can speak. To each their own, I suppose.', 'Everyone you know has a different <kanji>habit</kanji> when they get sick. Instead, they eat <reading>cous</reading>cous (くせ). But you think couscous is gross. It''s bland and does nothing for your stuffy noses and scratchy throats. I wonder how you can convince them to replace their habit with your own.', 'N1', NULL),
(1964, 'Once the gold comb has melted down in the forge, you can forge it into whatever weapon you want.', 'Most people who do forge work have tans. And now you know why.', 'You''re trying to work the <kanji>forge</kanji>, but it''s harder than you thought. You end up forging outside all day and get a wicked <reading>tan</reading> (たん).', 'N1', NULL),
(1965, 'Imagine tempering this gold from the east. You''re heat-treating the gold when you''re tempering it, so feel the heat from the fire on your face and arms.', 'Imagine yourself tempering your gold with this shabby rental forge. It keeps flaring up and almost burning off your eyebrows every time you try to work it. It''s tough doing tempering with a rental! ', 'The smithy where you''re doing the <kanji>tempering</kanji> is just a <reading>ren</reading>tal (れん). You need a rental for your tempering because you haven''t sold enough gold to own your own smithy yet. It''s seen its fair share of tempering, so it''s a pretty shabby rental…', 'N1', NULL),
(1966, 'Imagine yourself walking through a field of rice plants. Now look very closely at the head of the plant in front of you. See the rice bunched up there? That''s the good stuff.', 'Imagine working in a field of rice and using a hoe so you don''t injure the head of the plant. You''re sweating and it''s hot, but you keep at it.', 'When you want to harvest the <kanji>head of the plant</kanji> you''d like to eat, you need to use a <reading>hoe</reading> (ほ). Using a hoe, you can dig up the bottom of the plant without injuring the head.', 'N1', NULL),
(1967, 'The stand radical looks a bit unusual in this kanji, but that just makes it more sovereign-like, don''t you think? Sovereigns don''t stand like the common folk, after all.', 'On top of being the sovereign of breakfast foods, the tater also symbolizes all the qualities of a good sovereign: humble and accessible; firm on the outside, but with a soft and warm heart; and always providing nourishment to the people… or something.', 'And it''s not just any towel you stand on. To show that you''re the <kanji>sovereign</kanji>, it has the symbol of a <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tot on it. The mighty, sovereign tater tot! That''s how everyone knows it belongs to the sovereign.', 'N1', NULL),
(1968, 'Well, of course you blink, you can''t control that. And when a cleat come hurtling toward your eye it blinks automatically.', 'Soon the person who''s cleat hit you is running over and trying to apologize and stop your crying, but it''s too late.', 'You <kanji>blink</kanji> and blink and <reading>soon</reading> (しゅん) there are tears coming out of them too. You try to blink them away but it''s not working. Soon you''re crying and blinking and crying some more.', 'N1', NULL),
(1969, 'Chrysanthemums are super important in Japan because they''re the symbol of the imperial family. They''re even on the cover of Japanese passports. Please don''t ever pick them unless you want to end up in prison eating only rice for the rest of your life.', 'Imagine holding a chrysanthemum in your hands while you get kicked over and over and over in prison. Was it worth it?', 'If you go to prison for <kanji>chrysanthemum</kanji> picking, everyone in the prison is going to <reading>kick</reading> (きく) you nonstop. You broke the chrysanthemum picking law, so kicking is your punishment. And believe me, you don''t want these kicks.', 'N1', NULL),
(1970, 'Imagine really puffing out your chest and saying, "Yes, two big prisons!", with all the pride you can muster. ', 'With your regained, child-like pride, you venture out into the world after spending way too much time in those two big prisons. A new life awaits you, good luck!', 'After mustering all your <kanji>pride</kanji>, you feel like a new person, reborn almost. "I feel like a <reading>子</reading> (こ) again!", you proclaim to the world.', 'N1', NULL),
(1971, 'Not only will the canopy protect your manorial estate from the elements, it will also let would-be trespassers know that this is <em>your</em> private patch of dirt that they''re not allowed to set foot on.', 'You keep a big portrait of the shogun on the wall of your manor house to let all the serfs and peasants on your estate know who they''ll have to deal with if they question your authority.', 'The establishment of your <kanji>manor</kanji> was approved by the <reading>sho</reading>gun (しょう) himself. The shogun presides over all the territory in the land, so you can''t set up a private <kanji>estate</kanji> without his blessing.', 'N1', NULL),
(1972, 'Picture the building, with it''s tall top hat, inching closer to other buildings when no one is looking. It''s planning to thwart everything!', 'Watch as the soap cleans all of the paint away, thwarting the entire paint project. The painters are on the ground, crying and squeaky clean.', 'The building next door is being painted, and the top hat building is jealous. It <kanji>thwart</kanji>s the painting plans by dumping <reading>so</reading>ap (そ) out of its windows and onto the painters and the building!', 'N1', NULL),
(1973, 'The entire village is shouting and yelling and pointing at the poor dog. They''re boiling a giant boiling pot of water, and you can''t even hear yourself think anymore. Shut up, shut up, shut up! ', 'Even the dog is mocking you now. It''s barks sound more like "shut up, shut up" than "bow wow, bow wow." What the heck dog, I was trying to save your life!', 'The villagers are mad that you told them to <kanji>shut up</kanji>, and they start to <reading>mock</reading> (もく) you. They put their hands on their hips, lean forward, cross their eyes, and yell "SHUT UP!" back.', 'N1', NULL),
(1974, 'See this leader''s kick coming at your straw sack. Hear the leader yelling as he aims his kick at your straw sack. Hiyahhh! Also keep in mind that the life radical is missing the little drop/slide thing on the side. It''s missing that because your life is lousy and sad.', 'You thought you were all cool saying "heyo" and moving your sack away from the kick, but they had this planned all along. Heyo…', 'You manage to get your <kanji>straw sack</kanji> out of the way as you shout "<reading>Heyo</reading> (ひょう)! Look, you missed! Heyo!" Unfortunately your leader''s friend was standing behind you and he kicks your sack as you move it out of the way. "Heyo!" he shouts, mockingly.', 'N1', NULL),
(1975, 'Imagine shoving your face into a nice, clean, white cotton towel. Mmm, so fresh.', 'Picture all of the men lined up, making towels out of pure white cotton. They''re all rubbing their faces in the soft cotton whenever they can.', 'This <kanji>cotton</kanji> is made by <reading>men</reading> (めん).', 'N2', NULL),
(1976, 'Imagine using your mouth to turn a tree into a shelf. Yell at this tree and keep yelling at it until you see it become a shelf.', 'Imagine transporting the shelves in your car. Over and over. Back and forth in your car. Vroom vroom.', 'Once you turn the tree into a <kanji>shelf</kanji> you put the shelf in your <reading>ca</reading>r (か) and sell it at a local pawn shop. You keep doing this over and over again until you are rich. You command the trees to turn into shelves and then you take the shelves in your car to sell them. Easy money.', 'N1', NULL),
(2119, 'You run over to where the child fell. They are just fine! Because they had an umbrella, they were able to float to the bottom.', '"You will stay in that cavity or hole until you repent." If it hasn''t been clear up until now, Emperor こういち is going mad with power.', '"Into my <kanji>cavity</kanji> you go!" yells a voice. It is <reading>こう</reading>いち. ', 'N1', NULL),
(1977, 'Picture the nine crosses in front of you, lined up in a triangle. Now take your rock, hold it above your head, and SMASH it down on them as hard as you can!!', 'You smash things because your own heart has been smashed by your cyborg. :''(', 'As you <kanji>smash</kanji> these crosses, you think of your <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい) and how much you want to smash the people who took it away from you. Your cyborg was so good at smashing things too. You could be smashing together, if you didn''t have to sell it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1978, 'Look at this sticky rice fortune of yours. Run your hands through it and feel the sticky rice between your fingers. Hear the sticky sucking sounds and you stir the sticky rice about.', 'Imagine sculpting these sticky rice nendoroids with your bare hands. Feel the sticky rice between your fingers as you smush the rice into cute little nendoroids.', 'This rice is so <kanji>sticky</kanji> that you are able to sculpt <reading>nen</reading>doroids (ねん) from it. Your rice fortune is worth even more now that you are hand making limited edition nendoroids from it.', 'N1', NULL),
(1980, 'Covering things up with deceit is pretty much the worst thing you can do, so don''t be like this crab.', 'Picture the crab with its head, er, front half? Whatever, it''s in the guillotine and it''s screaming, "You''ve been deceived! I never deceived anyone, you''re all sheep!"', '<kanji>Deceit</kanji> can only take you so far, and soon the crab is caught, tried for its crimes and coverups, and sentenced to death via <reading>gui</reading>llotine (ぎ).', 'N1', NULL),
(1981, 'Imagine an actual key going from you to another person. This key is the lie. You gotta believe in the lie for it to reach the other person and unlock their understanding. Visualize this visual lie.', 'Imagine trying to lie your way out of this. He saw you steal his saw. He caught you red handed. What sort of lie can you spin to get yourself out of this situation?', 'The <kanji>lie</kanji> you told was about not stealing your buddy’s <reading>saw</reading> (さ). You actually did steal his saw. He saw you steal his saw, but now you are trying to lie your way out of it. It’s not going well.', 'N1', NULL),
(1982, 'When warm water touches a cold spear, fog billows out and covers everything.', 'You walk toward the kirin through the fog. When you get close, the image before you becomes clear: it''s two cans of Kirin beer on a slide.

Next time you see fog, keep your eyes peeled for the real kirin in the kiri.', 'Through the <kanji>fog</kanji> you see the shape of a mythical <reading>kiri</reading>n (きり). You can only see the outline through the fog, but you know it''s there.', 'N1', NULL),
(1983, 'A willow tree is a tree that looks like it''s on repeat over and over again. It also looks nice on a stamp. Note that the first stamp is kind of an ugly looking backwards stamp, and the second stamp is the normal stamp radical.', 'Watch Ryu as he French kisses this willow tree. Hear him slurping and moaning in ecstasy. Why are you watching this? This is gross. Wait – is this turning you on!? Ew.', '<reading>Ryu</reading> (りゅう) from Street Fighter really loves <kanji>willow</kanji> trees. Ryu loves them so much that he makes out with them. Ryu is open mouth kissing a willow tree. You are watching this. It is happening.', 'N1', NULL),
(1984, 'Imagine Wolverine and his leader Professor X going down this slide to Italy. Hear them wooping and cheering as they ride down the slide. Whee~', 'Imagine this eagle carrying Professor X away from Italy and out over the ocean. You can also remember this reading easily because Italy starts with the letter I. Super simple stuff.', 'Before Professor X can get off the slide into <kanji>Italy</kanji>, a large <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) swoops down and plucks him right out of his wheelchair. That eagle is very strong. Goodbye, Professor X!', 'N1', NULL),
(1985, 'Imagine being the narwhal leader and asking others for help. Ask a shark for help. Ask a whale for help. Ask a shrimp for help. Go on, ask.', 'Imagine using this saw and realizing how big of a help it is. Hear the saw sawing away as you and your fellow narwhals get to work.', 'Then you discover a <reading>saw</reading> (さ) who would be a great big <kanji>help</kanji>. This saw is very strong and would help you and your narwhal construction team out a lot. You should make use of this saw right away.', 'N1', NULL),
(1986, 'Imagine this foot long shrimp. It’s pretty big. Imagine it squirming about making a “shaku shaku” sound as it worms about.', 'Imagine Shaq dribbling the shrimp like a basketball, saying “shaku, shaku, shaku” every time he bounces the shrimp.', 'How is <kanji>shaku</kanji>, the <kanji>Japanese foot</kanji>, pronounced by <reading>Shaq</reading> (しゃく) in Japanese? Why Shaq pronounces it shaku. It’s pronounced shaku in both English and Japanese.', 'N1', NULL),
(1987, 'Imagine kicking this lid and with each kick you feel a little bit more pathetic. Your mouth hurts a lot too.', 'Hold your eye with one hand and your mouth with the other. Feel them pulse in pain as you reflect on how pathetic you are today.', 'You kick the lid one last time and it bounces off the wall and hits you in the <reading>eye</reading> (あい). How <kanji>pathetic</kanji>. You are a pathetic mess. You should have never kicked the lid. Now both your eye and your mouth are in pain, you pathetic fool, you.', 'N1', NULL),
(1988, 'Picture your mouth as an open hole. Now a landslide of something (words? food? something worse?) comes pouring out, and when it''s done you have lips. You flap them open and closed to test them out.', 'Imagine wrapping your new lips around the mouth of this Kuchi beer bottle. It''s so good! And you aren''t spilling any of it!', 'Now you can use your new <kanji>lips</kanji> to enjoy a nice, cold <reading>Kuchi beer</reading> (くちびる). The Kuchi people are Afghan Pashtun nomads. They are lesser known for their amazing beer. And you''ve never been able to get this delicious beer into your mouth, but now that you have lips you can get it all in there.', 'N1', NULL),
(1989, 'Can you see the fence made of horns and flags through the light of the lantern?', 'Picture yourself climbing over this fence (that you''re definitely not supposed to climb based on all these flags and horns) and falling into the piles of hay. It''s a little scratchy, but otherwise pretty comfy!', 'You want to know what this <kanji>fence</kanji> is fencing in, so you take a step closer. As the light from the lantern gets brighter you see that the fence is around a ton of <reading>hay</reading> (へい).', 'N1', NULL),
(1990, 'Picture a pig with horns crashing into a building and both of them crashing into the dirt. Uh oh, is the pig dead?', 'Imagine trying to explain to the pig that it''s a pig, and not a bird. "Tweet, tweet, tweet!" says the pig again. Oh dear.', 'You pull the poor pig out of the rubble of the <kanji>crash</kanji>. It''s not dead, but it had a nasty bump to the head and now thinks it''s a bird. "<reading>TWEE</reading>T (つい)!" says the pig. "Tweet, tweet, tweet!" Poor confused pig.', 'N1', NULL),
(1991, 'Imagine someone''s putting a woman''s mouth over your mouth, looking at you, and commenting on your likeness. All it took was this woman''s mouth.', 'Jo-Anne popped her mouth off and put it on you. Indeed with that mouth on, you have an extreme likeness to her.', 'You have a <kanji>likeness</kanji> to <reading>Jo</reading>-Anne (じょ) when you wear that mouth.', 'N1', NULL),
(1992, 'Imagine a gorgeous woman. Now see her get hit by a wave of water and turn into an old woman. The old woman could still be pretty too, she just looks way old now. Like ancient.', 'Imagine this old woman acting like a sheep, just saying "baa" over and over and over again. Baa! Baa! Baa!', 'In an attempt to distract you from the fact that she is actually an <kanji>old woman</kanji>, the old woman starts to <reading>baa</reading> (ば) and act like a sheep. The old woman goes "Baa! Baa!" and hops around on all fours like a sheep.', 'N1', NULL),
(1994, 'A hat is just a piece of cloth or a towel you get to stay on your head. Extra points if it protects your eyes from the sun.', 'Imagine sitting your grandkids on your lap someday and telling them how you''re the person the bowler hat''s named after. They''ll be bursting with pride and admiration.', 'After inventing the <kanji>hat</kanji>, you wanted to show it off to everyone, so you went to the <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう) alley. You really stood out bowling strike after strike in your fancy new hat, and before you knew it, people had coined a term for your funky headpiece. That''s right — you became the namesake of the iconic bowler hat!', 'N2', NULL),
(1995, 'The towel the cash is in even has a big $ on it like the ones they have at the bank. You follow the penguin into a back alley.', 'You wait for the penguin to leave and you run over to the hay stack and stick your hand in. But you don''t feel the towel or the cash! Instead you pull out your hand and it''s full of hay and needles. Ouch! No fair, gimme that cash!!', 'Your eyes follow the <kanji>cash</kanji>, and you watch as the penguin stuffs it, towel and all, into a <reading>hay</reading> (へい) stack. What a smart penguin, people don''t look for cash in hay, they''re too busy looking for needles.', 'N1', NULL),
(1996, 'Don''t die with a grudge, or your soul might end up angry and trapped in some roots.', 'Picture a tree with the soul of a condor with a grudge in it before you. You feel bad about being so wasteful with paper and chopsticks now, huh?', 'If you see one of these <kanji>grudge</kanji> trees, it''s probably inhabited by the soul of a <reading>con</reading>dor (こん). Humans tend to kill condors and take their trees away to build houses and make toilet paper. So condors hold pretty bad grudges against us, and so their souls end up stuck in a lot of trees.', 'N1', NULL),
(1997, 'You''re supposed to see all the emotions, so why do you only see hate inside everyone?', 'Zombies are pretty filled with hate, so it makes sense that you''d see a bunch of hate if you used the soul mask on them.', 'It turns out you''re not looking at normal people. You see so much <kanji>hate</kanji> because all the people you''re looking at are actually just a group of <reading>zo</reading>mbies (ぞう).', 'N2', NULL),
(1998, 'It''s important to let yourself rest every now and then, you know.', 'You should rest right now. Take a break from Japanese and go eat some cake. (Just remember to come back when your tongue and heart are sufficiently rested!)', 'What better way to <kanji>rest</kanji> than to eat some <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい)! No talking necessary. Just enjoy the delicious taste of cake on your tongue and feel your heart let out a sigh of relief.', 'N1', NULL),
(1999, 'Use this folding fan to fan your face. Feel the refreshing breeze as you admire the gorgeous feather you just added. What a fashionista you’ve become.', 'Imagine handing this newly made folding fan to the centaur. See his face light up with delight as he examines your fine craftsmanship and starts fanning himself with it. Another satisfied customer!', 'Your biggest <kanji>folding fan</kanji> customer is a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん). This centaur just loves your folding fans and this one you just made with the feather was for the centaur’s latest order. You go back out your door and deliver this folding fan to the centaur.', 'N1', NULL),
(2000, 'Imagine waking up one morning to find all your doors missing. The door criminal strikes again! They left the sliding door behind though, since they only wanted the hinged 扉 ones. So… there''s that at least?', 'Now picture yourself waking up to a bright, beautiful morning with all your 扉 doors still safely attached to their hinges. Thanks, 飛び Ra!', 'To protect your <kanji>door</kanji>, you get help from <reading>飛び Ra</reading> (とびら). 飛び Ra is the flying (飛び) version of Ra, the Egyptian sun god. Whenever a criminal tries to steal your door, 飛び Ra flies out from behind it and blinds them with his sun-god brightness. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2001, 'Hold your thousand+ fingers up to the sun and imagine inserting them into the sun''s super-hot ... s-sun lava. (That''s what it is, right? Yeah, let''s go with that.)', 'Since you have a thousand fingers too many, you decide to insert them all into the sun and watch as their souls pop out. You listen to the little souls scream at you.', 'You <kanji>insert</kanji> one finger, it burns up immediately, and its <reading>sou</reading>l (そう) comes flying out screaming for a moment before it disappears. Hmm. You insert another finger and as it burns up, another little soul comes out screeching, "WHY WOULD YOU INSERT ME IN THERE!" and is gone.', 'N1', NULL),
(2002, 'And don''t use your fingers either, ''cause they''ll just get bitten off. You absolutely want to make sure it''s the palm — a nice flat open palm. It''s a lot like feeding a horse, I guess.', 'Imagine if a Shogun and a Viking had actually met in history (never mind that the dates don''t overlap). You can definitely picture the quiet, refined Shogun putting his palm over the mouth of some noisy Viking during a tough diplomatic meeting.', 'This Viking-touching technique of using the <kanji>palm</kanji> was developed by the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). The Shogun has to deal with a lot of loudmouthed Vikings, so he''s learned to use his palm to cover their mouths.', 'N1', NULL),
(2003, 'Imagine having all this tsunami water in your hair. Hear it drip from your mohawk. Drip… drip… drip…', 'Feel the weight of this dryer helmet on your head as you listen to it whirr away. The dripping has finally stopped! Success!', 'Luckily, you’re a self-proclaimed <reading>techie</reading> (てき), so you develop a device to stop the <kanji>drip</kanji> drip dripping. This techie device of yours is like a hair dryer helmet. You put it over your mohawk, it does its thing, and your hair is dry! Being a techie sure does have its benefits.', 'N2', NULL),
(2004, 'Imagine yourself as you cook with a fire very confidently. Knowing you cannot fail, feel the heat and hear the crackling embers of the fire. Smell the delicious smell of whatever you are cooking as well.', 'Imagine swinging this meal over the fire. Feel the flames licking at your fingertips as you cook this meal by swinging it back and forth over the fire.', 'You <kanji>cook</kanji> your meal evenly by <reading>swi</reading>nging (すい) it back and forth over the fire. You swing it left, you swing it right. You swing it to, you swing it fro. All this swinging ensures a properly cooked meal.', 'N1', NULL),
(2005, 'Imagine finding this big treasure treasure treasure treasure and being really refreshed and relieved and happy. This treasure treasure treasure treasure is so big, you’ll be set for life!', 'Imagine the most sour taste you could ever imagine. So super-duper sour! Now imagine that sour taste instantly melting away once you discover this big refreshing treasure treasure treasure treasure. Jackpot!', 'You’d had a <reading>sour</reading> (さわ) taste in your mouth for not being able to find any treasure treasure treasure treasure for such a long time, but now that you found this big one, the sour taste is totally gone and it’s sooooo <kanji>refreshing</kanji>!', 'N1', NULL),
(2006, 'The meaning you''re trying to learn is tatami mat, so focus on the beautiful tatami mats that are strangely in the middle of this rice paddy. On top is a forehead with a top hat. It almost looks like the tatami mats are an altar for this gruesome scene.', 'You saw Joe setting up the tatami mats a day earlier, but you thought he was just making an area for workers in the field to rest in. Does the forehead with a top hat have anything to do with Joe? What sort of ominous sign could this be?', 'Who made this creepy <kanji>tatami mat</kanji> altar? It was <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう), the increasingly concerning farmhand who lives in your village.', 'N2', NULL),
(2007, 'You can even rub charcoal in your eyes if you really want to clear them up, but you should probably only do this under the supervision of your eye doctor.', 'Imagine how dangerous it would be not to have clear vision while you row around in a row boat. You might even smack yourself in the face with your own oars.', 'You have to have <kanji>clear</kanji> vision because you''re about to go out in your <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat. The light off the water shines right into your eyes and you need the charcoal to clear that up for you. Otherwise you''ll end up crashing into the shore or going over a waterfall to your death.', 'N1', NULL),
(2009, 'Imagine if your torso was as large as the moon. You’re a gigantic human. Imagine floating around in space like this. Wooooaaaahhh.', 'Imagine how many doughnuts you would have to eat to get your torso this size. Now imagine eating all of those doughnuts. I hope you like doughnuts, because you’re going to be eating a lot of them.', 'Your <kanji>torso</kanji> got so big because of all the <reading>dough</reading>nuts (どう) you ate. You ate so many doughnuts that your torso got to be the size of the moon and then you floated away into space with your own gravitational pull and everything. Doughnuts are dangerous things.', 'N1', NULL),
(2011, 'Imagine this insect with a construction hat on, building a rainbow. He works very fast. The resulting rainbow is beautiful!', 'Imagine this construction insect being all whiny and needy for attention and stuff. It’s pathetic. Hear him whine and whine because of how needy he is.', 'This <kanji>rainbow</kanji> building insect is so <reading>needy</reading> (にじ). He is needy because he needs everyone to look at his rainbow and praise him for what he’s done. “Look at my rainbow! Look at how good it is and I am! I’m so needy! Needy for praise and recognition!”', 'N1', NULL),
(2012, 'You are making the towel really long, because it''s white and it might be good for writing in. It''s going to become your notebook.', 'Something bad is going to happen, and you need to warn everybody. It''s her, it''s her, it''s her. Mrs. Chou, Mrs. Chou, Mrs. Chou!', 'Inside your long towel <kanji>notebook</kanji> you just write the same name over and over again: "Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading>" (ちょう).', 'N1', NULL),
(2013, 'Imagine sitting at your desk writing when a mosquito starts bzzzzing in your ear. You can''t ignore it, it''s gonna bite you. So you flail around helplessly.', 'You can''t. You can''t write like this. You need to kill the mosquito and its little car too.', 'That <kanji>mosquito</kanji> noise sounds like it''s driving a tiny <reading>ca</reading>r (か) in your ear. How are you supposed to write with a bug driving around going bzzz bzzz like a revving car?', 'N1', NULL),
(2014, 'Snakes love insects, especially when they''re ready to eat on a spoon. But, snakes don''t usually go on top of the roof, so that''s why you have to tempt one with an insect in a spoon.', 'Especially big snakes. They''re really, really hebi.', 'Why don''t you just bring a <kanji>snake</kanji> up to the roof yourself? Because snakes are <reading>heavy</reading> (へび).', 'N1', NULL),
(2016, 'Honestly, that''s what you get for lying. But it doesn''t make it any less humiliating even if you deserve it.', 'Imagine all of the cruel jokes people say to humiliate you. Feel your face burn and your stomach flip.', 'Your friends and coworkers <kanji>humiliate</kanji> you more by making <reading>joke</reading>s (じょく). "Uh oh, I heard it might rain later, I hope you don''t drown in the inch of water that''s coming!"', 'N1', NULL),
(2017, 'Imagine melting down your gold book. You''re excited to have your new flower pot, but you''re a little sad because this book was actually pretty interesting. Make a mental note to buy it in paperback later.', 'Okay, so this may not be the best flower pot. You usually don''t need a hatch or this much gold coating. But that''s how they used to do it in the old days, so that''s good enough for you!', 'The gold <kanji>flower pot</kanji> has a <reading>hatch</reading> (はち) attached to it, which used to be the book''s back cover. That''s fine, you use the hatch as a lid for the flower pot!', 'N1', NULL),
(2020, 'Feel the rain hit your eye and then crystalize into frost immediately. The rain in your eye is wet, and then very cold as it becomes frost.', 'Picture yourself walking proudly, casting your frost gaze at all who you pass. "Wow look at that frosty, cool shemo. She''s sooooo cool!" you hear them whisper. (Haha cool, get it? Because you''re frosty and also a little sad, which is totally in when you''re a teen.)', 'This whole description is pretty emo. You came up with this whole emo <kanji>frost</kanji> idea because you are a <reading>shemo</reading> (しも). An emo who goes by she pronouns. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2021, 'You don’t want to starve, do you? Feel your tummy gurgle in pain. You gotta eat this table. Taste the dry wood taste on your tongue as you eat the table and get splinters in your tongue and gums.', 'Imagine eating every key. Taste the cold metal on your tongue. After you swallow each key, you hear it clink against the other keys already in your tummy. Clink!', 'After you finish the table, you still feel like you might <kanji>starve</kanji> so you decide to eat every <reading>key</reading> (き) off your key ring. One by one, you eat each and every key so you don’t starve.', 'N1', NULL),
(2022, 'You have to check your ego at the door, because you''ll definitely starve otherwise.', 'Now your starved body has been moved to the top of a castle, where you scare away people and birds with your loud grumbling stomach and scary face.', 'You <kanji>starve</kanji> (thanks ego) and turn into a <reading>ga</reading>rgoyle (が). As your stomach aches, your skin turns gray and you slowly harden into a crouched position.', 'N1', NULL),
(2023, 'Examine this mysterious livestock from a distance. Imagine them floating around the mysterious rice paddy like they aren’t even walking. Hear them moo and oink as well.', 'Look at this chick bobbling about in the air. It looks very ephemeral and other worldly. Still a very cute chick though. You want to pet it.', 'Your favorite member of the mysterious <kanji>livestock</kanji> crew is a little <reading>chick</reading> (ちく). The chick is very cute and little. The chick is also very mysterious because it floats about without even flapping its little wings.', 'N2', NULL),
(2024, 'Hear the cape flapping behind you wildly, and hear yourself murmuring your prayers for extra swiftness. You just have a need for speed, okay? And with any luck, the gods will grant you the gift… of swift…', 'Fortunately, the lack of jeans make you even more swift and aerodynamic. You also feel a swift and pleasant breeze on all your downstairs bits now… ', 'You move at such <kanji>swift</kanji> speeds now, your <reading>jean</reading>s (じん) are ripped right off your body. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2025, 'Look at this odd horse. It''s a little odd-looking, but it turned its quirks into strengths and now it''s an exceptional horse for horse riding!', 'Want to ride the odd horse? Then, you must have the key to open its stable! ', 'Before <kanji>horse riding</kanji>, you’ll need the <reading>key</reading> (き) to access the stable and let out the odd horse you''ll be riding.', 'N1', NULL),
(2026, 'Imagine yourself spraying poison on these mysterious flowers, but they just double in size, and continue to amass.', 'Feel the wetness of the rice paddy under your feet and the fluffiness of the chicks pressing in on you. Watch the looming, mysterious flowers above you as the chicks smother you. ', 'As the flowers <kanji>amass</kanji> it starts attracting wildlife. Soon thousands of baby <reading>chick</reading>s (ちく) are amassing in the rice paddy as well. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2027, 'Imagine the lack of shrimp on the ice. All you wanted was some delicious shrimp for dinner, but now it''s all gone.', 'Imagine the worker''s jeans and how gross they are with all those shrimp stuffed into them. Yuck!', 'How could the store <kanji>use up</kanji> its shrimp supply so quickly? Suddenly you notice the worker behind the fish counter has shrimp stuffed into their <reading>jean</reading>s (じん) . Aha! The culprit! ', 'N1', NULL),
(2028, 'Imagine digging your cleats into the bark of the tree until you get high enough for your hair to start coloring itself tree colors. It might be a lot of work, but it''s worth it.', 'You couldn''t take the pain of losing your cyborg, so you''ve become one. Your robo-hair can change its coloring, but soon you''ll be even more cyborg-y than before!', 'This cool <kanji>coloring</kanji> can only happen because you turned yourself into a <reading>cy</reading>borg (さい). Your hair and scalp are now made of high tech parts, not human ones.', 'N1', NULL),
(2029, 'When you have a recollection of some idea or thing in the past, it''s just your soul saying, hey remember this? Let''s not forget this.', 'Imagine recollecting a past life in Oklahoma. Life was simple and nice. You should consider taking this as a sign to move there again!', 'Your soul is sending you a <kanji>recollection</kanji> right now of... your past life as a farmer in <reading>Ok</reading>lahoma (おく). You see yourself farming and sweating and living a wonderful life. Hmm, maybe you should move to Oklahoma and live the simple life again.', 'N1', NULL),
(2030, 'Feel the water from the tsunami crashing into your form. Feel the water on your body as your form begins to melt. I’m melting! I’m mellltiinnnggg!', 'Feel the melt begin to reverse as you eat your yogurt. Taste the yogurt on your tongue as your form begins to solidify once again.', 'The only way to reverse the <kanji>melt</kanji> process is to eat some <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう). The yogurt counteracts the melting from the water and your form will become whole again. Quick, eat some yogurt!', 'N2', NULL),
(2031, 'Try and move this rake over to the measurement table. It is resistant so you have a hard time moving it. Imagine the rake struggling and resisting endlessly.', 'See yourself tying the tie around the rake and attaching it to the measurement table. The rake is still jostling around, but the tie keeps it from moving about too terribly much.', 'Because the rake is persistently <kanji>resistant</kanji>, you eventually tie it to the table with your neck <reading>tie</reading> (たい). The tie won’t hold for long, so you need to take each measurement quickly.', 'N1', NULL),
(2032, 'Picture your mama asking you to dance. The two of you pick up and kick your feet to dance, spinning ''round and ''round.', 'Lean down and take a big whiff of your dancing feet. The odor is... HORRIBLE! AHGH!', 'You both <kanji>dance</kanji> so much that you develop an <reading>odo</reading>r (おど). Not just any odor, an odor particular to stinky dancing feet.', 'N2', NULL),
(2033, '"You sure are clever, man!" you shout encouragingly at your wise servant as he plops his stool down on another mound of shellfish. Dinner tonight is sure to be a seafood feast, and you can''t wait!', 'Picture Ken standing there with his sword and crab-shell armor. Pretty clever of him to disguise himself as your servant for so many years just to save a few mollusks. It might have been wiser not to shuck so many of them first, though…', '"Yes, more <kanji>clever</kanji> than you!" he yells, tearing off his servant''s disguise. "It is I, <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai. I won''t let you eat these shellfish. I''m taking them back to the sea, where they belong!"', 'N2', NULL),
(2034, 'Imagine this radiance emanating from your shiny dome. The radiance is blinding!', 'Point your shiny forehead, filled with radiance, at your car. Hear the engine start and jump to life as you power it with the reflected sunlight from your forehead.', 'This <kanji>radiance</kanji> is the <reading>key</reading> (き) to powering your car. Your car runs on reflected solar energy, and the radiance coming from your forehead is the most powerful source of radiant sunlight ever imagined. Your forehead is a perfect key for this car.', 'N1', NULL),
(2035, 'The moon actually looks like it wants to threaten you now too. It seems to have grown a big, smiling, angry mouth and it''s way closer to you than you''ve ever seen it. All that power is radiating out of it and down towards you, threateningly.', 'Picture the powerful moon, threatening to crush きょうと and all its residents. If only a small green boy could turn back time and save everyone from this powerful moon!', 'This extra power-filled moon is now trying to <kanji>threaten</kanji> the safety of <reading>きょう</reading>と. All of きょうと is shaking and getting dark as the moon comes closer and closer.', 'N1', NULL),
(2036, 'Wow, these hemp plants are so big that they look like trees. But, despite their size they''re still just hemp.', 'Your mama knew that you were going to like hemp in the future, so she planted these hemp plants for you. What a great mama.', 'Who planted all this <kanji>hemp</kanji>? Your <reading>ma</reading>ma (ま) did.', 'N1', NULL),
(2037, 'Picture these lamps lining the street, with their little fires burning brightly. Nowadays we don''t have real fire in them, we use electricity!', 'Picture とうきょう, not lit up by all of the electricity, signs, shops, and things that light up its streets today, but by old-fashioned fire lamps!', 'These street <kanji>lamp</kanji>s were first used in <reading>とう</reading>きょう. It was the most advanced city after all. And more people were out at night in とうきょう than anywhere else in Japan. So they needed lamps to light things up!', 'N2', NULL),
(2038, 'Imagine studying this little blossom. Its mouth is open and nectar-filled, and out of it spring these horn-like things that reach up to heaven. It smells nice too. A perfect specimen for your blossom collection, maybe?', 'Picture those classic dainty little blossoms — buttercups, pansies, you name it. Now imagine their tougher cousins, blossoms so tough you''d need a saw to cut them down. If you can picture the latter, you should remember the さ reading.', 'If it''s shaped like a mouth with horns poking out of it, you know it''s going to be a tough little <kanji>blossom</kanji>. That''s why you''ll need a <reading>saw</reading> (さ) if you''ve got any hope of cutting it down for your blossom collection.', 'N2', NULL),
(2039, 'Imagine burying a dead clown in the dirt and then planting some seeds on top. It''s worth it, you think, if it helps cultivate your crops.', 'Saying bye is polite. Never forget to say bye, even in the middle of a cold-blooded clown murder.', 'Before you can start this whole <kanji>cultivation</kanji> process however, you have to say <reading>bye</reading> (ばい) to the clown. Say bye to him, then bash his head in, and drop him in the dirt.', 'N1', NULL),
(2040, 'Imagine if you went full force into everything you''ve ever even thought of doing. Imagine how burnt out your soul would be. Imagine all the regret you''d have that you didn''t just stick to one thing and do it well.', 'Throw everything you regret away. Only kayak from now on.', 'The <kanji>regret</kanji> is too strong for you to ignore, so you decide to drop everything but <reading>kay</reading>aking (かい). If there''s one thing you refuse to regret, it''s your bright orange kayak and all the friends you made at kayaking club.', 'N1', NULL),
(2042, 'Imagine the pig scooting around. It is able to accomplish anything you can imagine (so go nuts with your imagination).', 'Perhaps it even takes the swing all the way around in a full 360 degree rotation. This horned scooter riding pig can truly accomplish anything.', 'It was even able to successfully <kanji>accomplish</kanji> a feat that involved it jumping its scooter right into a <reading>swi</reading>ng (すい), swinging back and forth, then jumping off.', 'N1', NULL),
(2043, 'Imagine if you were given a knife and allowed into the king''s squad. Would you let the power corrupt you?', 'I''m not saying Han Solo is a bad squad leader, but his track record isn''t the greatest. I mean, best case scenario the king gets stabbed in the arm and they manage to win.', 'This <kanji>squad</kanji> is led by <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo, so it''s basically a 50/50 shot that the king is gonna die.', 'N1', NULL),
(2044, 'Picture all of the different colors of paint being swept along by tsunami after tsunami, painting the dirt as it goes.', 'Imagine dipping each of your toes into the paint-dirt, one by one, to paint each of them a different color. Why are you doing this?', 'You touch the <kanji>paint</kanji> in the dirt with your <reading>toe</reading> (と) to see if it''s still wet. It is. The paint is still very wet and now your toe looks like a tiny rainbow.', 'N2', NULL),
(2045, 'Look at all these ladles lying diagonally in front of you. Pick them up. Feel the wood grain. Wonder why you have so many.', 'Uh oh, is this one of those weird tests where they find out if you''re the chosen one or some old scholar reincarnated? Who knew laying ladles at diagonal angles could reveal this kinda stuff?!', 'In front of this <kanji>diagonal</kanji> pattern is a <reading>sha</reading>man (しゃ) who is currently staring at you. You slowly reach down and turn one of the ladles around, diagonally, and look back up. The shaman''s eyes are wide, and now they''re scribbling on in a notebook.', 'N1', NULL),
(2046, 'Picture two heavily armed residents of your district punching the living crap out of each other. They''re both armed to the teeth with missile launchers, flamethrowers, katanas, lightsabers, etc., but neither one wants to be the first to draw a weapon and escalate the situation.', 'Imagine the heavily armed combatants from earlier slugging it out, but this time the 王 is watching them from a box seat above the action. He gleefully encourages them, a maniacal, bloodthirsty glint in his eye.', 'The district residents <kanji>punch</kanji> each other in front of the <reading>王</reading> (おう). These punching matches are actually gladiatorial battles for the 王''s entertainment.', 'N1', NULL),
(2047, 'Imagine the shield glowing brightly as hymns play in the background. The demons try to get close to the pope to shove him off the cliff but the holy shield is too bright and popey.', 'This pope would have been in a lot of trouble if June''s summer sun hadn''t risen at just the right moment. His shield would have become useless and he might have been shoved off the cliff if it was a less sunny month.', 'The light of the pope''s <kanji>shield</kanji> begins to fade. The shield''s holy power is waning! Just as the demons are about to push him off the cliff, the sun begins to rise. That''s right! It''s <reading>June</reading> (じゅん) and summer has just begun. The pope''s shield reflects the power of June''s bright sun into the eyes of the demons, casting them off the cliff behind him and back into hell!', 'N1', NULL),
(2048, 'Picture this fat turkey with flowers on its head, sitting on a stool. It''s gobbling for grains and you need to be the one who harvests them.', 'Imagine an angry cock chasing you around as you try to harvest grain. He keeps pecking you, ouch!', 'You''re just starting to <kanji>harvest</kanji> the grains when an angry <reading>cock</reading> (かく) shows up! He''s mad because these are his grains and you''re about to harvest them and feed them to a turkey instead of him.', 'N1', NULL),
(2050, 'Imagine a horse trying to write a paragraph about why it deserves to be a chess piece. See the horse struggle with his hooves to write out a paragraph as he neighs in nervous frustration.', 'See this horse in its coma. You feel bad that it will never be able to be a chess piece, but this is a bit dramatic.', 'Upon being told that he will never be a <kanji>chess piece</kanji>, the horse slips into a <reading>coma</reading> (こま). The horse will never awaken from this coma as he knows he will never fulfill his dream of becoming a chess piece, so he has nothing to live for.', 'N1', NULL),
(2052, 'Imagine having fingers that work like a magic wand of extraction. With a flick of your fingers, you could extract any radical out of a kanji too. How could these special fingers not be a reason for you to extract? With these fingers, you can''t stop extracting!', 'Taste that chewy goodness in every bite. All you need is a dash of Chewbacca extract.', 'Your magic fingers can even extract the essence out of living things, so you decide to <kanji>extract</kanji> some from <reading>Chew</reading>bacca (ちゅう). You always thought a nice Chewbacca extract could take your cookies to the next level. It''ll help offset the sweetness and make them extra chewy (or chewbacca-y!). ', 'N1', NULL),
(2053, 'Picture the axe hovering above your fingers. Now say the vow. But don''t lie. The axe knows when you''re lying.', 'Imagine what it would be like to slice off your own fingers with the saber you''ve carried for so long. You better not break that vow. ', 'When you finish your <kanji>vow</kanji>, you take out your holy blue <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい) and cut the axe in half! You know you''re never going to break your vow, and if you do, you''ll use your own saber to cut off your fingers.', 'N1', NULL),
(2054, 'Maybe the five mouths are your five senses, "speaking" information to your soul so you can realize and perceive things about the world. Whoa, dude… ', 'You now realize why Godzilla destroys all those buildings: he''s warning the Japanese people about the use of new technology and warfare and experimentation. He just wants everyone to realize what the consequences of these new things could be!', 'Right now those five mouths are helping you to <kanji>realize</kanji> something about <reading>Go</reading>dzilla (ご). First you hear Godzilla, then you see him, then you smell him, then you touch him. Finally you taste Godzilla. Now you''ve realized exactly what kind of thing he is. You truly know Godzilla.', 'N1', NULL),
(2055, 'Imagine being in a field, using your fingers and a stone to cultivate the whole thing, by hand.', 'Now cultivation is going to involve getting all the tacks out of here, preferably with the stone and not your fingers, so they don''t get stuck anymore.', '<kanji>Cultivation</kanji> is suddenly halted because a <reading>tack</reading> (たく) sticks into your finger. You pull the tack out, throw it away and start cultivation again when... ouch! There''s another tack. This field is full of tacks!', 'N1', NULL),
(2056, 'Imagine reading the paragraph very slowly. You find your answer. It looks like you''ll be able to arrest that person...', 'Still, according to this paragraph you''re running your fingers through, you can do it. You can arrest こういち.', 'Who do you want to <kanji>arrest</kanji>? You want to arrest <reading>こう</reading>いち. Even though he''s started wars, created a religion, and trapped a child in a cavity, it''s going to be tricky to arrest him because he''s the emperor now.', 'N1', NULL),
(2057, 'Imagine making a foundation with the stone and the two trees so you’ll have a place for your coat rack. Feel the wood grain and cold stone against your skin as you move them around to form the foundation.', 'Imagine getting on your hands and knees, rubbing a bar of soap all over the foundation you built. The soap gets into all the nooks and crannies in the wood and stone. Now if a coat falls off the coat rack onto the floor, you just add water and poof! Clean!', 'After you finish the <kanji>foundation</kanji>, the only step that''s left is to wash the entire thing with <reading>so</reading>ap (そ).', 'N1', NULL),
(2058, 'Picture the beautiful black and white crane dancing gracefully on the roof. Now that you know what it is, you feel a little bad about calling it a turkey bird.', 'The crane keeps saying you''re too rude whenever other people come close so they know what kind of person you are. Yikes, you really shouldn''t have gotten on this crane''s bad side.', 'The <kanji>crane</kanji> leans down toward you from its roof perch and squawks, "<reading>Too ru</reading>de (つる)!" You''re shocked! You didn''t know the crane could speak English, but even worse, it knew you called it a turkey bird.', 'N1', NULL),
(2059, 'All treasures need to be taken care of, no matter what they are. Prune away anything that isn''t necessary.', 'You want your car to be small and sleek, not a giant truck, so you have to carefully prune it when it grows too much.', 'Currently, you want to <kanji>prune</kanji> your greatest treasure: your <reading>ca</reading>r (か). Your car is made of wood, and it keeps trying to grow new buds and branches, so you have to prune them off.', 'N1', NULL),
(2060, 'The reason hill knives are so sturdy is because they are shaped like hills. Very wide, very thick, not so long. So, they don''t break and are more sturdy.', 'Imagine Grandpa ごういち rolling the knife back and forth over various things to cut them. "I love my sturdy hill knife," he says.', 'This <kanji>sturdy</kanji> (but strangely shaped) knife is used by Grandpa <reading>ごう</reading>いち. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2061, 'Imagine what makes this turkey’s mouth so unique. This beak is solely his. It belongs to no other. This turkey is the only turkey with a mouth like his.', 'Imagine hanging a U-ey on the highway. Hear your tires screech as you turn around and get off where you’re supposed to.', 'You traveled a long way to see this turkey and the mouth that is <kanji>solely</kanji> his but you missed your exit on the highway and you have to hang a <reading>U-ey</reading> (ゆい). You know what hang a U-ey means, right? It’s making a U-turn. Well, you have to hang a U-ey on the highway so you can get off at the right spot to see this turkey and the mouth that is solely his.', 'N1', NULL),
(2062, 'This kanji also just... really looks like a podium right? With a guy wearing a graduation cap, pointing toward you, in the crowd.', 'I guess that makes sense. This podium is by the dawn for the dawn.', 'You listen to what the <kanji>podium</kanji> has to say for so long you finally can''t help but ask, "Who are you?" "I''m the <reading>dawn</reading> (だん), of course!" And this is my podium where I spread the good news about the dawn!', 'N1', NULL),
(2063, 'Imagine handing the nun your country’s flag. And also a spoon that is very valuable to you. Imagine getting down on one knee as you do so.', 'Feel your knee digging into the cold floor as you present your offerings to the nun. You feel slightly sad as she takes your precious belongings from you.', 'You’re getting down on one <reading>knee</reading> (に) as you present the flag and the spoon to the <kanji>nun</kanji>. The nun accepts your offer and takes your flag and spoon from you. Your soul is saved!', 'N1', NULL),
(2064, 'Once you have an approximation on the age, you''ll be able to come up with an approximation on how much it''s worth too.', 'The guy is trying to look at your approximation now, while calling YOU "guy" over and over, and you just want him to buy it and leave you alone!', 'As you''re recording your <kanji>approximation</kanji> down,  somebody comes up and shouts, “Hey <reading>guy</reading> (がい)! I’ll give you $10,000 for that tree there, guy!” This guy must be crazy. That’s much more than your approximation of the value of this thing. But you sell it to the guy anyway.', 'N1', NULL),
(2065, 'Feel the water all around you as you become immersed in this tsunami. See Wolverine trying to swim his way to the top as you watch the stool floating about in the water.', 'Hear the stool crack against your shin and feel the pain erupt through your leg. You try to scream but you’re under water so you just get a big mouthful of tsunami.', 'Still <kanji>immersed</kanji>, the stool floats over and hits you in the <reading>shin</reading> (しん). The tsunami gave the stool a good push and while it was fully immersed, it ran into your shin. It really hurts.', 'N1', NULL),
(2066, 'You go to see if you can find any remainder of the fire + tsunami battle. You can, but it''s so faint.', 'Look at your skin. It has such a faint tint.', 'Just like the tsunami and fire canceling each other out, leaving a <kanji>faint</kanji> remainder of what was, your <reading>tan</reading> (たん) is extremely faint as well.', 'N1', NULL),
(2067, 'There''s someone in your face yelling about how they want to boil! You yell back, "Okay, if you want to boil, just boil!"', 'Once your knee is done boiling, the person kneels down in front of you and presents it on a platter, mocking you, since you can''t kneel anymore. You should pay more attention to what''s going on from now on or you could find yourself in... HOT WATER. Ohohoho.', 'Welp, the pot is at a rolling <kanji>boil</kanji> and your <reading>knee</reading> (に) is in there. The person wasn''t saying they wanted to boil, they said they wanted to boil someone. And that meant you. Well, your knee anyway.', 'N1', NULL),
(2068, 'Imagine trying to cover up a black hole so that the helicopter doesn''t capsize. It''s one or the other, dude. Cover or capsize. You decide.', 'Man, f**k you you, black hole. F**k you you. Making that helicopter capsize and then refusing to be covered?', 'You''re doing your best to <kanji>cover</kanji> the black hole to stop the helicopter from <kanji>capsiz</kanji>ing but it''s not working! “<reading>F**k</reading> you (ふく) black hole!" you shout. You just want to cover it up! You just want to do the right thing!', 'N1', NULL),
(2069, 'Trees may look all sweet and innocent, like they aren''t listening to every word you say, but it''s a front! They''re conspiring against you!! They''ll blackmail you and take everything!!!', 'Watch as the conspiratorial tree and all it''s gossiping friends are bowled over by your bowling ball. Phew. You''re safe from the trees.

For now.', 'Now you''re getting it! That tree is trying to <kanji>conspire</kanji> against you and take you down. Take your <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう) ball and bowl it and all its friends down!', 'N1', NULL),
(2070, 'Imagine yourself trading cans for pottery with some kind of rope-and-pulley system you''ve rigged up between your building and the prison.', 'A particularly good option would be the とうきょう Metropolitan Art Museum. That would be a great place to hold a pottery exhibit.', 'Where do you display your prison <kanji>pottery</kanji>? In <reading>とう</reading>きょう, of course. とうきょう''s full of fancy art museums that are more than happy to show off your quirky pottery collection.', 'N1', NULL),
(2071, 'Picture a single building, isolated from the rest of the city and those mean, bully buildings that live there.', 'Picture the sun rising behind an isolated building. A cock lets out a cry from the roof. It''s a sad and lonely scene.', 'The <kanji>isolate</kanji>d building only has one companion, and it''s the <reading>cock</reading> (かく) that lives inside of it.', 'N1', NULL),
(2072, 'You can''t just let people do whatever they want. If it isn''t correct to loiter here, then it IS correct to subjugate them, and force them to do what you want. You control them now. And they''re gonna get it!', 'As you swing your saber around, subjugating the loiterers, and making them do as you say, you remind yourself that this is correct. And if it''s correct, you have to use your saber to enforce it.', 'You <kanji>subjugate</kanji> these loiterers with your holy blue <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい)! It''s the correct thing to do, after all, and you were given this saber all those levels ago because you did the correct thing.', 'N1', NULL),
(2073, 'Picture Her Highness, high up in her building. Now compare her home and life to yours, you dirt person.', 'Picture His and Her Highness coming out of their huge building and walking by you in your hay pile. "My goodness, Highness, have you ever seen such a dirty, fluffy creature?" "My no, dear, let''s move quickly to remove it from our sight!"', 'I bet His <kanji>Highness</kanji> has never had to sleep in <reading>hay</reading> (へい) either, like you do every night. Hay is what you use as a pillow, mattress, blanket. Basically hay and dirt are all you have. Unlike His Highness.', 'N1', NULL),
(2074, 'Imagine meeting the leader of Vulgar Valley. Imagine all the vulgar things he says to prove he is worthy of being the leader of Vulgar Valley. He is so very, very vulgar.', 'Hear the motorcycles rev and purr as they carouse through the Vulgar Valley. Hear the leader screaming vulgar things as he and his Bosozoku bike gang ride all around.', 'This <kanji>vulgar</kanji> leader leads his Boso<reading>zoku</reading> (ぞく) bike gang through Vulgar Valley, patrolling the area and spreading vulgarity as he goes.', 'N1', NULL),
(2075, 'Imagine putting three stools on top of a tree and watching it transform into a mulberry. The science of nature!', 'Imagine the huge quantity of mulberries growing from your stool tree. Imagine picking them all. What an enormous quantity of mulberries you now have.', 'Your <kanji>mulberry</kanji> tree then produces a <reading>qua</reading>ntity (くわ) of mulberries. Yum.', 'N1', NULL),
(2076, 'Hear the tsunami crash into the gate as you see the water slosh around and make sure that all of the king’s plants get good and watered.', 'Again, watch and hear the tsunami crashing into the gate and getting the plants watered. This time, remember that the month is June and it is very hot and sticky outside. You’re sweating and very hot.', 'These plants are getting <kanji>watered</kanji> in <reading>June</reading> (じゅん), the month they need watering the most. June is a hot month, so it’s good that this tsunami came and got the king’s plants watered when it did.', 'N1', NULL),
(2077, 'Imagine giving this gorgeous white pearl to the king after he slides down his slide and boards the jet. See his king face light up as he sees the pretty pearl.', 'Oh. I guess the king didn''t need your pearl, since he has a pearl chute to get them off his jet before takeoff. You feel silly. And you kind of want to jump into the pearl chute yourself.', 'The king takes the <kanji>pearl</kanji> and tosses it into a <reading>chu</reading>te (しゅ) next to the slide he took to get into the jet. You watch as the pearl goes down, down, down until it lands into a huge vat of pearls.', 'N1', NULL),
(2078, 'As you watch the lid decline, keep in mind that the sun radical has its middle line extending outside the box on both sides. This is because the sun is going supernova. Pew!', 'Imagine swinging on these vines as you hear the sun explode and go supernova behind you. Swing faster! Swing faster!', 'As the lid begins to <kanji>decline</kanji> in the sky, you realize what’s going to happen so you attempt to <reading>swi</reading>ng (すい) to safety. Swing on some vines in the direction opposite of the sun. You know that once that lid finishes its decline and collides with the sun, there’s gonna be trouble.', 'N1', NULL),
(2079, '"Look at me! You can be a big commander someday too!" he shouts, encouraging everyone.', 'Picture the encouraged Shougun: his eyes are shining with tears, his heart is pounding, he''s been encouraged to be better!', 'He <kanji>encourage</kanji>s the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) too! You''ve never seen the Shougun express any emotion other than intense indifference or intense dislike.', 'N1', NULL),
(2080, 'Imagine you are one of the few without power. Feel the inferiority crushing down on you. It feels terrible and there is nothing you can do about it.', 'Let’s go, let’s go! Time’s a wastin’! I’m tired of this inferiority and want to gain some power!', 'Since you suffer from such extreme feelings of <kanji>inferiority</kanji>, when someone offers you and your friends the chance to gain power, you yell “<reading>Let’s</reading> (れつ) go!” without a second thought. “Let’s go, guys! Come on! Power awaits us!”', 'N1', NULL),
(2081, 'Look at this crab trap and see it visibly glowing with intuition. Its powers of intuition are so strong that you can actually see it shimmering with intuition as it expertly traps all those crabs.', 'Genghis Khan probably has the strongest intuition of anyone in history. This crab trap is getting close though.', 'This crab trap''s <kanji>intuition</kanji> powers were extracted from Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) himself. And as we all know by now, Genghis Khan was the absolute master of intuition, he always knew what his enemies and his allies were about to do.', 'N1', NULL),
(2082, 'Even if you''re royalty, you can''t expect to have a woman all to yourself without popping the question. Put a ring on it and make that lady your royal consort!', 'Imagine standing next to a royal consort. She and her fancy, voluminous 17th-century dress are radiating so much heat that even you''re starting to sweat.', 'You can tell someone''s a <kanji>royal consort</kanji> by the <reading>hea</reading>t (ひ) she radiates. Royal consorts are all smoking hot… literally, because they have to wear bulky, elaborate dresses that were designed several centuries ago. Those things trap heat like nobody''s business.', 'N1', NULL),
(2203, 'Imagine covering a turkey in ice and every time you check it, it''s only semi frozen. It doesn''t matter what you do, it just refuses to freeze all the way through.', 'The lesson is to never try to freeze something in June. If you do it will only semi freeze.', 'Finally you realize it will only <kanji>semi</kanji> freeze because it''s <reading>June</reading> (じゅん)! It''s too hot out for your turkey to freeze all the way. The ice keeps melting off the top and semi thawing it!', 'N1', NULL),
(2084, 'If that signpost wasn''t there how would you know you reached the summit? Like, if you were on top of Everest and there was a bad storm... you''d just topple off the other side. Those summit signposts are important!', 'Picture your beautiful summit home. You know, George Washington had one of these too. So. That''s cool, I guess.', 'At the <kanji>summit</kanji>, you decide to build your new <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう). It''s been destroyed so many times, and you''re sick of it. Maybe if you build you home at the summit of this mountain it will be safer.', 'N1', NULL),
(2085, 'And that adept person is you! You''re an expert when it comes to constructing underground prisons, so you''ve been hired for this job. Good luck!', 'With a dignified nod, Emperor こういち entrusts you with the grand project of constructing an underground prison, confident that the adept builder will make prisoners feel right at home in their new subterranean digs.

Hint: When you see 工 in a kanji it will often take the こう reading.', '<kanji>Adept</kanji> at constructing underground prisons, you''ve caught the eye of Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち, the royal underground prison enthusiast.', 'N1', NULL),
(2086, 'Not like, wicked cool, but like wicked evil. I see you, New Englanders.', 'Imagine being chased around the outside of this wicked fanged building as the wicked fanged jaguar tries to eat you. Ahhh!', 'You peek inside the <kanji>wicked</kanji> building and a wicked <reading>ja</reading>guar (じゃ) suddenly pounces at you! You let out a sigh of relief when it''s stopped by the window, but then... the window opens?! What a wicked building! It''s letting the jaguar get you!', 'N1', NULL),
(2087, 'A fat horse. So burdensome. Not to mention the burden on the horse on top of the fatness.', 'Go visit your dad the burdensome horse because you have to visit your dad. It''s weird because you''re a human and he''s a horse.', 'But you have to be nice to the <kanji>burdensome</kanji> horse because this horse is your <reading>da</reading>d (だ).', 'N1', NULL),
(2089, 'Picture him sitting above you in the judge''s chair in court. He''s about to make his judgement.', '"I hereby sentence you to..." *ploop* "Ooh, this is really good. I hereby sentence..." *ploop* "Wow, are these tater tots? Court dismissed."', 'The <kanji>court</kanji> of the Yoga King is against you. You have to turn this around, so you start pushing <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tots into his tater-tot-hole shaped mouth.', 'N1', NULL),
(2093, 'The bamboo tsunami hits the ground and drops of bamboo turn into sheets of paper. The sheets of paper combine to become record books that record what happened here, on this day.', 'Bamboo can also become <reading>bo</reading> staffs too, if they don''t turn into record books.', 'A <kanji>record book</kanji> is a <reading>bo</reading>ok (ぼ), when it comes down to it.', 'N1', NULL),
(2094, 'Your writing is so clear and concise that just about everyone does read it. Well, everyone with hair that is.', 'Picture the Shougun angrily yelling at you about his hair disaster! The only good thing he can say about your book is that it gave clear directions.', 'Your <kanji>clear</kanji> writing got the attention of the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) and now he''s at your door once again. Although, this time he''s angrier than usual. His hair also looks awful. He took your advice after reading the book and cut his entire ponytail off.', 'N1', NULL),
(2095, 'Imagine reading this issue of your favorite stretchy pirate boy comic. Lumpy uses his long arms as a net, traps the sun and ties it to the stool right as the tsunami hits. What kind of crazy trouble is he getting into this time?!', 'You''ve known about Japanese comics or manga for a long time, and now you can read them too. Look how far you''ve come. Wow, what a journey. I''m just so proud of you, kiddo.', '<kanji>Comic</kanji>s are <reading>man</reading>ga (まん) in Japan, whether they''re comic or serious.', 'N1', NULL),
(2096, 'Think about all the stupid things you said on the streets today. Do you want those exact phrases, mistakes, and mouth sounds following you around forever? Revise it before you write it down!', '"Tater tots, tater tots, tater tots, man.
Bake me a tater tot as fast as you banana."

Well that''s bad. Better revise this so it rhymes.

"Tater tots, tater tots, tater tots, man.
Bake me a tater tot as fast as you want."

Nope, still bad. Revise!

"Tater tots, tater tots, tater tots, man.
Bake me a tater tot as fast as you clam."

Yes. It''s gold.', 'You want to <kanji>revise</kanji> the song you wrote about <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tots at lunch today. But it needs a lot of revising.', 'N1', NULL),
(2097, 'This person wants to consult you about something important, so whatever you say next from your mouth better be really smart-sounding and helpful. Make this person glad they consulted you.', 'They were on the fence about buying one, which is why they consulted with you. You think sheep are the ultimate safe-haven asset, so you told them not to be <em>sheepish</em> and advised them to invest in one.', 'After they <kanji>consult</kanji> with you, the person decides to buy a <reading>shee</reading>p (し).', 'N1', NULL),
(2098, 'This inscription refers to a signature or something similar. This is an artisan''s signature in the form of an inscription.', 'If you need to review 名 go ahead and do that. It will help you to remember this kanji.', 'The inscription is a name written on something, right? Remember the kanji for "name"? It is 名, and it shares the reading with this kanji as well, making both names <reading>めい</reading>.', 'N1', NULL),
(2100, 'Imagine an embankment made of dirt, in the correct place, blocking the sun from your eyes.', 'Picture an embankment made of dirt and tater tots. Not like, tater tots just thrown in the embankment, you probably see those all the time. But dirt AND tater tots in the embankment itself.', 'Unfortunately, building a correct <kanji>embankment</kanji> doesn''t just take dirt, but <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tots too. You pile up the dirt and the tater tots so the embankment can be nice and high.', 'N1', NULL),
(2101, 'Picture your helicopter as it drifts out to sea with the tsunami. ', '"Heyoooo stop drifting awaaaay!!"', 'As it <kanji>drift</kanji>s away you shout, "<reading>Heyooo</reading>! (ひょう)." You know the helicopter probably can''t hear you (or understand you) but maybe it will turn on and stop drifting away if you shout it enough.', 'N1', NULL),
(2102, 'Imagine yourself doing flips with this feather on your head. Flip flip flip.', 'Or, maybe you get so good that you write a 本 (ほん) about doing a flip. Wow, you''re good!', 'You''re pretty good at your <kanji>flip</kanji> technique, but you''re not that good. You really need to practice and <reading>hone</reading> (ほん) your skills and get better.', 'N1', NULL),
(2103, 'Imagine the cars spinning out in the rut. Spin spin spin. The rut gets bigger. Nine is too many cars.', 'Imagine turning the thrusters key, then feeling the car lift off the ground. You''ll finally get out of this <kanji>rut</kanji>!', 'To get out of the rut you have to put in a second <reading>key</reading> (き) that turns on the thrusters. You put in the key and press the "take off" button.', 'N1', NULL),
(2104, 'She was buried in the cliff down to her mouth so nobody would find her! You pull her out of the ground and soon what was just a mouth becomes a whole empress!', 'It''s only natural the empress would want to see her emperor. And right now, the emperor of these lands is Emperor こういち.', 'When you finish unburying the <kanji>empress</kanji>, the first thing she says to you is: "Please, you must hurry and take me to Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち."', 'N1', NULL),
(2105, 'He is a really big turkey too. Like, 12 feet tall, or something. Don''t want to stir up a turkey that''s that big.', 'Is it laughing? Oh no, this is bad, it''s enjoying being stirred up, now we''ll never stop it.', 'While the turkey is <kanji>stirred up</kanji>, it also looks like it''s having a lot of <reading>fun</reading> (ふん). ', 'N1', NULL),
(2106, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Imagine going to a restaurant and seeing a menu of all different types and styles of tater tots and nothing else. Even the drinks are tater tot based. Mmm mm.', 'This <kanji>restaurant</kanji> only serves <reading>tater</reading> (てい) tots. Really, would you ever want to go to a restaurant that didn''t have tater tots? No.', 'N1', NULL),
(2107, 'Imagine this majestic cat pirate leader of leaders. Feel in your heart that you look up to this cat pirate, truly.', 'Of course, the gyoza is filled with fish, because we''re talking about cat pirates, here.', 'Your job is to bring this leader of cat pirates (whom you <kanji>look up to</kanji>) its favorite food: <reading>gyo</reading>za (ぎょう). ', 'N1', NULL),
(2108, 'Imagine all the leaders standing around, then the chief person standing there all in white.', 'You''ll learn more about which reading to use when the vocabulary roll around. In the meantime, try to remember both of them the best you can!', 'The reading is the same as 白, which is included in this kanji. The other reading is お, and it''s like the honorific 御, in a way. You''d want to be polite to someone who is the chief ______, right? That''s why it could be お, too.', 'N1', NULL),
(2109, 'Imagine someone accidentally spilling ooze on a rat, then watch the rat turn into a sentient rat martial artist.', 'However you look at it, try to make this accident be a good accident.', 'But really, if you do something <kanji>accidentally</kanji>, it''s probably a <reading>goo</reading>d (ぐう) thing in a way. You learn a bit about how you can improve yourself so you don''t do it again, or in Splinter''s case, it''s just a good mistake!', 'N3', NULL),
(2111, 'Can you picture this big dirt tomb, with flowers and shellfish decorating it and a cross right on top?', 'See this tomb, and you''ll remember all the fun you had. It''s decorated to make sure you feel that fun.

Fun fact: when you see 賁 in a kanji, the reading is probably going to be ふん.', 'This is a <kanji>tomb</kanji>, but it feels really <reading>fun</reading> (ふん). Decorated with flowers and shellfish shells, it’s not a sad place at all. It''s a fun place to remember all the fun times with people you loved.', 'N1', NULL),
(2112, 'Picture samurai standing in a line. One, two, three... woah. You look up. This guy has icicles growing off him and he''s the size of a bus. Now that''s a robust samurai.', 'Physical robustness is just a reflection of the strength of your soul!', 'You may think it''s just his <kanji>robust</kanji> body, but it''s actually the samurai''s robust <reading>sou</reading>l (そう) that keeps him fighting through the cold and the icicles.', 'N1', NULL),
(2113, 'Imagine pressing your fingers into the bed, grasping tightly and ripping the sheets free. Now bundle them up, tight as you can!', 'Hah! Take that naysayers! Who''s bundling their own sheets now? Me, that''s who! Hah!', 'As you <kanji>bundle</kanji> up your sheets and <kanji>grasp</kanji> them tightly, you triumphantly exclaim, "<reading>Hah</reading> (は)!" to all your imaginary naysayers. In your mind, everyone doubted that you could actually bundle up your own sheets and launder them yourself.', 'N1', NULL),
(2114, 'Imagine loading a bunch of weapons into a boat and sailing off to transport them somewhere else.', 'Picture yourself as a cool smuggler, transporting stolen... I mean... totally legitimate weapons from one place to another. Just like Han Solo!', 'Hey! You''re <kanji>transport</kanji>ing weapons just like <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo! I mean, it was a spaceship, not technically a boat, but he definitely used the Millennium Falcon as a transport.', 'N1', NULL),
(2116, 'Also, imagine a tsunami hitting a cave, becoming the same as the cave (since a cave will shape it).', 'Also note that the 同 part of this kanji gives away the reading as well. 同 and 洞 have the same readings!', 'Best way to remember the reading for this kanji is to think about how the shape of a cave is the shape of a <reading>dou</reading>ghnut (どう).', 'N1', NULL),
(2117, 'Picture the tsunami breaking over the cliff on the horizon. It would be beautiful if it wasn''t so frightening.', 'You wonder who that guy was. You wonder why he was there. You wonder if he''s still alive out there, past the horizon.', 'You squint into the <kanji>horizon</kanji> and see something else. There''s some <reading>guy</reading> (がい) up there. And he''s... he''s being swept away by the tsunami. The guy disappears into the horizon. The guy is gone.', 'N1', NULL),
(2118, 'Imagine if countries decided to make sick weapons that would spread epidemics like this all over part of the world. Let''s hope they don''t...', 'Picture a single 駅 on a map, bright red. Now watch the red spread to all of the other 駅 on the map, dyeing them red and spreading the epidemic all over your country.', 'These <kanji>epidemic</kanji>s spread by <reading>駅</reading> (えき). Each train takes the sickness and shoots off in a different direction, spreading it further.', 'N1', NULL),
(466, 'Imagine your super comfy, state-of-the-art stool. You take it everywhere with you, and you''re always sitting on it. Again, and again, and <kanji>again</kanji>. You love that stool.', 'Imagine your friends getting more and more annoyed with your constant stool-sitting habits! They really don''t get it. What is the matter with you?! Why can''t you just stand like everyone else? Don''t you know sitting is the "new smoking"!?', 'Your friends are starting to worry about you, because you''re always sitting on that stool. "What is the <reading>matter</reading> (また) with you?" they scream! You might want to imagine that all your friends have Jamaican or British accents to make this one fit a bit better!', 'N1', NULL),
(2120, 'I mean, they''re the leader of Korea already, right? So they have to have a decent chunk of greatness in them already to get that far.', 'Picture the greatness of the scene before you: the leader of Korea, a great eagle overhead, it''s so great!!', 'The <kanji>greatness</kanji> radiates off of the leader of Korea, so much so that you can hardly look. Just when you''re about to close your eyes to shield them from the greatness, a great <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) soars over them, letting out a cry.', 'N3', NULL),
(2121, 'There is a giant geoduck at the summit of the mountain, and the street will lead you right there. Imagine the grueling climb up the mountain to the summit. Feel it in your legs, and keep looking at that tall, tall geoduck. Now imagine kneeling those same aching legs humbly to the street below the geoduck.', 'Anytime you use the street, you''re in Mrs. Chou''s territory. After detecting you were using her street to reach the geoduck, she started coming after you. Remember, wherever there''s a street (radical), Mrs. Chou is not far behind.', 'You are at the top, the <kanji>summit</kanji> of everything! You <kanji>humbly</kanji> survey your surroundings from the top of the geoduck, only to see someone on the street, running toward you. It is Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう), and she looks angry.', 'N3', NULL),
(2122, 'Call your mom or dad and ask them to go out to eat with you right now.', 'Imagine getting a call from the Shougun himself. He''s come to your house before, but he''s never asked you to eat with him. This is huge!', 'You are <kanji>call</kanji>ed to come <kanji>eat</kanji> by the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). He''s been really impressed with you lately and he wants to chat over drinks, he says on the phone.', 'N2', NULL),
(2123, 'This is one of the perks of having a horned demonic creature for a husband. You can put his horns between your fingers and get a really nice stretch on your finger joints.', 'Imagine having a strong, muscular sergeant with horns for a husband — so macho! With a husband like that, you''d be running your fingers through his sexy horns every chance you get. Sir, yes sir!', 'Why are your husband''s horns what you always love to <kanji>put between</kanji> your fingers? Because he''s a <reading>ho</reading>t <reading>ser</reading>geant (はさ)! ', 'N2', NULL),
(2124, 'Old trees'' roots and leaves grow inefficient, and they can''t get enough nutrients. It''s only natural for them to wither up a little bit.', 'It gave the 子 its shade, its sap, and eventually its body to build a house. What an awful child, and what a generic giving tree.', 'This <kanji>wither</kanji>ed tree''s best friend is a human <reading>子</reading> (こ). Imagine the child hugging the tree.', 'N2', NULL),
(2125, 'Imagine standing in front of an incoming tsunami. Suddenly in the distance you see someone throwing dollar bills into it and it starts to boil. Ahh!', 'Feel your poor little foots burn in the boiling water. You really want to know who that jerk was that threw dollars into the tsunami to make it boil!', 'The <kanji>boil</kanji>ing water crashes onto the land and you realize your foot is in it! No, not one foot, both your <reading>foots</reading> (ふつ)! It hurts so much that you can''t even remember the word for two foots.', 'N2', NULL),
(2126, 'Imagine how big and heavy this turkey must be. It''s so huge so you need the strength of two Wolverines and the magnitude of a whole tsunami to move it around and wash it.', 'If you want to wash your turkey, make sure to have your special tack ready. The tsunami will recede pretty fast, so you need to tack your turkey to the ground if you don''t want the sea to take it.', 'To make sure your turkey won''t get carried away by the tsunami while you <kanji>wash</kanji> it, you pin it down with a giant <reading>tack</reading> (たく). If you''re washing a giant turkey inside a giant wave, only a giant tack will guarantee it doesn''t get washed away.', 'N2', NULL),
(2127, 'Imagine pouring some syrup next to a fire and watching it dry up. Watch as the sides slowly bubble and harden and lose what makes syrup so good in the first place.', 'Go back to imagining a fire drying up some syrup and watch as a tiny little syrup soul cries and is released into the air. Oh no!', 'If you <kanji>dry up</kanji> syrup like this, you''re removing its <reading>sou</reading>l (そう). Have you ever seen dry syrup before? No soul in that. In fact, you probably have no soul if you can dry out syrup like this without feeling bad about it.', 'N2', NULL),
(2128, 'Picture this horned bottle sitting on the ground. You don''t know who left it here or why, but it''s here and the weirdest looking bottle you''ve ever seen.', 'Imagine picking up this weird bottle with your bare hands and putting it in a trash bin. You did your good deed for today! Yay!', 'Even though you''re not the one who left this <kanji>bottle</kanji> here, you decide to do the right thing and throw it in the trash <reading>bin</reading> (びん). It''s not right to leave a bottle just sitting on the ground, you know.', 'N2', NULL),
(2129, 'You''d think you''d only need a plow, but you also need water if you''re going to plow a field, otherwise what''s the point?', 'Thousands and thousands of こういちs, set free unto the world to do all our <kanji>plow</kanji>ing work.', '<kanji>Plow</kanji>ing all the fields are <reading>こう</reading>いちs. Not Emperor こういち. These are the こういち clones the government made, and now they''re being put to use.', 'N2', NULL),
(2130, 'There are a group of people pointing at pictures of the moon coming closer to earth. They are the world''s leaders, and although they don''t get along normally, this new attack from the moon has united them in agreement: we must stop the moon.', 'The world has united against the moon, and they have made Emperor こういち their temporary leader.', '"We have <kanji>agreement</kanji>," the world''s leaders all say. "We will work together to stop the moon, and we have <kanji>agreement</kanji> on who we will follow, Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち.', 'N2', NULL),
(2131, 'Imagine staring down regretfully at your fat-covered spoon after eating both the moon and the sun. Not only did you eat two really important celestial bodies, but their high fat content made them way less healthy than you expected.', 'If you had known you were going to get a whole sheep''s worth of fat, you would have just eaten a big platter of mutton chops instead of dooming the entire solar system by eating the moon and sun.', 'Unbeknownst to you, the moon and sun contained as much <kanji>fat</kanji> as an entire adult <reading>shee</reading>p (し)! Why didn''t you check the nutrition facts first???', 'N2', NULL),
(2132, 'Imagine yourself opening a tigers mouth and looking all the way down into its stomach and seeing a bunch of skin. Why would you imagine that? Eww.', 'Imagine a fool trying to pull skin out of a tiger''s stomach. You can imagine them getting their skin added to the pile too if you want.', 'The only person who would try to take <kanji>skin</kanji> from a tiger''s stomach is a <reading>foo</reading>l (ふ). You''ll probably get your head bitten off looking for skin that way.  ', 'N2', NULL),
(2134, 'Picture a soft car lacking what it needs to drive down the road. It''s so soft it just flops down and won''t move.', 'Picture trying to drive this soft naan car. It probably just sinks into the ground and then gets eaten by wild animals (and me).', 'This car is probably so <kanji>soft</kanji> because it''s made entirely of <reading>naan</reading> (なん). Naan may be delicious, but it makes a really crappy car. No wonder it was so soft (and yummy).', 'N2', NULL),
(2135, 'Isn''t that all a suburb is? Just a mix of a variety of buildings in one area?', '"That suburb life will encourage more people to study kanji," Emperor こういち continues.', '"<kanji>Suburb</kanji>s are what we need!" says Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち. ', 'N2', NULL),
(2136, 'Imagine a huge splinter coming out of the corner of the building. It''s in a really dangerous spot, but the owner doesn''t seem so worried about it.', 'Actually, please don''t sue me. I''ll fix the splinter coming out of the corner of my building.', 'That <kanji>corner</kanji> is dangerous, but what are you going to do, <reading>sue me</reading> (すみ)?', 'N2', NULL),
(2137, 'Picture yourself counting ships by pointing your finger from one turkey propped up on a stool to the next. You''ll be the best ship counter in the world at this rate!', 'Imagine yourself, a sexy ship counter. Sexily counting ships. You go guy/girl/non binary person! So sexy!', 'Once you become the best <kanji>ship counter</kanji> in the world everyone will think you are super <reading>sexy</reading> (せき)! Nothing is sexier than being able to count, and counting ships is the sexiest thing you can count.', 'N2', NULL),
(2138, 'Picture your clan, working together, to build a new residence for you on their land.', 'Imagine living in a tater tot residence. Whenever you''re hungry, you just lean over to the wall and munch.', 'Your new <kanji>residence</kanji> is complete! You step closer and the smell of <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tots hits your nose. Someone is making you tater tots in your new residence. Wait, no they aren''t. Your residence is MADE of tater tots! Thank you, clan!', 'N1', NULL),
(2139, 'Imagine looking at a map of your county and the surrounding ones. All of the buildings in the counties next to yours are buddy buildings!', 'Imagine taking out a gun and shooting in a random direction. When you find where the bullet landed, you know that''s the county line. Though if it gets stuck in a building or a person it doesn''t count. You''ll have to try again.', 'Your <kanji>county</kanji> is everything you can reach when shooting a <reading>gun</reading> (ぐん). If the bullet can''t reach a building you know, it''s a buddy building in another county.', 'N1', NULL),
(2140, 'Try to come with an explanation for picking up a sickle and cutting the head off a shrimp with it.', 'Picture Shaq popping into your head as you struggle to come up with an explanation for me. Picture Shaq''s face. This is payback for Shaq Fu, you think, as you finalize the rest of the explanation in your mind.', 'In your <kanji>explanation</kanji> you blame everything on <reading>Shaq</reading> (しゃく). He told you to do it! It''s all Shaq''s fault, you explain. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2141, 'Picture the moon turning to face a different direction. Feel all the fat in your body start to get extracted up through your pores, creating this slick, greasy sheen all over your skin. Let''s hope this phase of the lunar cycle ends quickly.', 'Feel how slippery the bowling ball gets as you smear it with fat. Now close your eyes and imagine pins toppling in every direction as your fat-slathered ball careens into them!', 'As long as you''re covered in <kanji>fat</kanji>, you might as well go <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう). Imagine how smoothly that bowling ball''s going to roll after you slather it with some of this fat!', 'N1', NULL),
(2142, 'Picture a prison for legs. It''s like a trap that your legs can never escape, basically. And it''s pretty painful, so imagine your mouth opening big and wide and letting out a scream.', 'Imagine Genghis Khan doing something to make you scream. What is it? What could Genghis Khan do that''s worse than what''s already happening to you?', 'You <kanji>scream</kanji> and scream and scream until a door bursts open. It''s Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) and he looks mad. Apparently your screaming is bothering him and now he''s gonna give you a real reason to scream.', 'N1', NULL),
(2143, 'Picture the awesome princess, kicking butt and taking names, out on the football field.', 'Make sure you know both princess words and you''ll know their readings too.', 'Remember the word 姫? It means <kanji>princess</kanji> too. If you remember the ひめ from 姫, you''ll be able to remember this one too.', 'N1', NULL),
(2144, '"No, I won''t open up until I meet the toe I will marry," it says to you.', 'You give the shellfish a tater tot, and it breaks its vow of chastity, opening up for your toe. You look the other way, but you can hear the sounds of passion below.', '"<kanji>Chastity</kanji> is really important to me," the shellfish says. "But, maybe if you give me a <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tot, I''ll change my mind."', 'N1', NULL),
(2146, 'Picture the seedlings growing among the rice plants. Once they bloom they''ll be picked, so let''s hope they stay small as long as they can.', 'Listen to the little seedlings say myou over and over. Too bad the farmers can tell they aren''t rice plants. ', 'The <kanji>seedling</kanji>s try their best to blend in by referring to themselves as <reading>myou</reading> (みょう), you know, me and you combined. That''s myou.', 'N1', NULL),
(2147, 'Picture the tsunami spinning in a circle, trying not to hit the mustaches until it spins itself into a giant whirlpool.', 'Picture my car getting sucked into the whirlpool. Picture me crying as my car disappears into it forever.', 'The <kanji>whirlpool</kanji> is huge, and it starts pulling things into itself. Oh no, is that my <reading>ca</reading>r (か)? Hey, wait! Whirlpool give my car back!', 'N1', NULL),
(2148, 'Imagine yourself saying "mercy" over an over as this horned guy has you on the ground and keeps pooping over your heart. ', 'Imagine yourself saying mercy and seeing Jesus appearing just in time to save the day. The word "mercy" must be his bat signal. Jesus signal? Sure.', 'As you shout "<kanji>Mercy</kanji>!" one more time, suddenly <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ) appears out of no where and punches the horned guy into the stratosphere. Jesus snaps his fingers and the poop is gone and you''re off the ground. Your heart is safe.', 'N1', NULL),
(2149, 'This collar is what you always wear when you do your "hot lumberjack" act. If you were seriously trying to cut down trees, you wouldn''t be using a jackhammer, after all.', 'Imagine standing there in your collar, jackhammering trees as all your swooning fans scream "Eric! Eriiiic! We love you!" from the audience.', 'Your <kanji>collar</kanji> has "<reading>Eri</reading>c" (えり) emblazoned on it in cursive letters, because this is the stage name you use when you do your lumberjack act. Eric''s not your real name, of course — you just think it sounds hunky and looks good on a collar.', 'N1', NULL),
(2150, 'Imagine placing lotuses on top of your scooter and car. They are the right kind of flowers for these vehicles.', 'You always put a lotus on top of your rental cars and scooters. It just makes those boring, ugly rental cars and scooters so much prettier!', 'You''re putting a <kanji>lotus</kanji> on both your scooter and car because they are <reading>ren</reading>tals (れん).', 'N1', NULL),
(2151, 'Imagine this pose, imagine everything becoming clear. Clear your mind. Clear your head.', 'Picture the row boat on the clear pond in your mind. Watch the clear water flow over the row boat''s oars. ', 'Your mind is as <kanji>clear</kanji> as a sparkling clear pond. On this pond you see yourself in a <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat. A single row boat on the clear pond that is now your mind.', 'N1', NULL),
(2152, 'Close your eyes and open your ears. Feel all of the wise things fill you.', 'Picture your wise soul, living inside your body. Somehow it just seems to know what the public really wants. It''s like your soul is connected to their souls, granting you this wisdom.', 'All of these <kanji>wise</kanji> things are stored in your <reading>sou</reading>l (そう), not your brain. That''s where all of these kinds of intuitive feelings live.', 'N1', NULL),
(2153, 'Picture the tsunami coming towards your beautiful wedding as you stand by the bay. It''s not safe, but it''s memorable!', 'Imagine throwing buckets of uranium into the bay. It only burns your hands a little. And now they glow green, just like the bay does!', 'How did you get a tsunami to show up in the <kanji>bay</kanji> in the first place? By throwing a bunch of <reading>ura</reading>nium (うら) in there, of course! Its highly unstable nature can make any body of water, especially a calm bay, turn violent.', 'N1', NULL),
(2154, 'Imagine that you''re the pig. How big can you make your forehead dirt mound?', 'Picture a pig with a mound of dirt the size of two cars leisurely stomping around on a farm.', 'The pig''s forehead dirt <kanji>mound</kanji> keeps getting bigger until it''s the size of <reading>two cars</reading> (つか)! Pigs are big, but you didn''t think one could balance a mound the size of two cars on its forehead and not fall over.', 'N1', NULL),
(2155, 'Picture the cave in in your mind. Can you see all the little prisoners and guards being squished as the building tumbles down around them? Oh no!', 'Genghis Khan is all about being smart and using better tactics. In this case, why fight a bunch of guards and prisoners when you can just cause a cave in and win immediately? The ol'' Khan cave in, a classic war maneuver.', 'You keep picturing the <kanji>cave in</kanji> and you see Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) climbing over the rubble. He''s the one who took down this prison! And he''s coming for your town next!', 'N1', NULL),
(2156, 'Imagine the window piercing into the shellfish''s shell. Then imagine lining up the pieces, putting sushi on them, and counting them all.', 'Carefully count out all the sushi on their pierced shell plates and place them in front of Genghis Khan and his army. As you reach Genghis Khan himself, you realize it''s just his ghost, but he''s still staring at you as you serve his troops. Ghost or not, you don''t want him to pierce you if you mess up. ', 'Now that your shellfish is properly <kanji>pierce</kanji>d and turned into <kanji>sushi counter</kanji>s, you can serve them to Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) and his men. I really hope you counted them correctly too, because there are a lot of them.', 'N1', NULL),
(2157, 'Not many people have the leadership it takes to lead such a mission. Imagine what it takes to do it!', 'Imagine planting your flag on the moon and looking back at everyone who doubted you. You throw your arms up and exclaim, "Hah! I knew I could do it!" The view is breathtaking, but the real thrill comes from showing all your doubters exactly who was right.', 'You''ve proven your <kanji>leadership</kanji> qualities by successfully completing your mission to the moon. "<reading>Hah</reading> (は)!" you say to all your haters. "I knew I could do it — that''ll teach you! Hah!"', 'N1', NULL),
(2158, 'Picture yourself taking a bath in your bathtub. Relaxing!', 'Imagine being stuck on the road all day for a week. Imagine how good a bath will feel when you finally get home. Nice and hot and relaxing. Mmmm...', 'When do you miss taking a <kanji>bath</kanji> the most? When you''re on the <reading>ro</reading>ad (ろ). Traveling is fun and all, but when you''re stuck in a car, traveling down a dirt road for days, all you want to do is take a nice long bath.', 'N1', NULL),
(2160, 'Someone was obviously going to cook this poop turkey in a pot, and if you hadn''t looked under this lid it surely would have died. Now as you embrace it you can hear the turkey weeping softly.', 'Imagine embracing the turkey as it covers you in yogurt poo. You hope it''s yogurt at least. You aren''t gonna taste it to find out. Maybe. Okay you might taste it a little.', 'As you <kanji>embrace</kanji> the sad turkey it makes a funny sound and suddenly starts pooping <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう) all over you. But you don''t want to stop embracing it! It''s probably just scared still. But the more you squeeze, the faster the yogurt comes out of it!', 'N1', NULL),
(2161, 'The "melon boy orphan," is what they called him. Imagine a melon (your favorite kind) with a face and arms and legs sticking out. He walks up to you and says "hello kind person, could you help an orphan?"', 'That''s the sad thing about the melon orphan boy... he''s just a child. "Hello gov, I''m just a child," he says to you.', 'The <kanji>orphan</kanji> melon boy is but a <reading>子</reading> (こ).', 'N1', NULL),
(2162, 'Picture the shellfish in your hands. Imagine handing them over to the clown as compensation for the unspeakable acts you had him do for you.', 'All you have to do is give him the right amount of compensation and you''ll hear his sweet "bye, bye." Then you never have to see him again.', 'Once the clown has been properly <kanji>compensate</kanji>d, he looks you in the eyes, one hand full of shellfish, the other in the air, and waves, "<reading>Bye</reading> (ばい)" he says. "Bye, bye."', 'N1', NULL),
(2163, 'Picture these little gold triceratops shellfish linked together by their horns in a chain around your neck. Feel them hanging there. Sparkly and cold.', 'Picture the saw in your hands, sawing back and forth on the gold chain around your neck. Maybe this isn''t the best fashion accessory if you have to use a saw to get it off.', 'However, the only way to break the <kanji>chain</kanji> is to cut it open with a <reading>saw</reading> (さ). Once the golden triceratops shellfish start holding on, they never let go. So when you want to take the chain off you have to really hack at it with your saw.', 'N1', NULL),
(2164, 'Imagine these three things coming out of your mouth not one at a time, but all together like an eruption. Perhaps you''re laying on your back, and they burst out of your mouth.', 'It''s really inconvenient that things erupt from your mouth whenever you''re having fun. Makes it hard to have fun.

Fun fact: when you see 賁 in a kanji, the reading is probably going to be ふん.', 'When do inanimate objects <kanji>erupt</kanji> from your mouth? Mostly when you''re having <reading>fun</reading> (ふん).', 'N1', NULL),
(2165, 'Picture this auspicious spirit sheep. Blessing the children with its fluffy hooves.', 'Picture the Shougun telling the world that they must fly spirit sheep flags on this day forever, because of how auspicious it is.', 'The spirit sheep is so <kanji>auspicious</kanji> that the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) declares this day to be the most auspicious of all days. The day when the spirit sheep appeared.', 'N1', NULL),
(2166, 'Picture yourself offering the cow''s life up to the god(s). It''s a good thing we don''t actually have to do this anymore, you think.', 'Thanks to the mercy of your saber, the offering is quick and painless. A normal saber, however, would have cut that cow to bits. Like, really just wrecked it. Good thing we didn''t make you think about that, huh?', 'You prepare the <kanji>offering</kanji> with your holy blue <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい). Normally this would be gruesome, but your saber glows and the cow''s life shoots up into the sky without you laying a hand on it.', 'N1', NULL),
(2167, 'Imagine being one of these people who maintain order by going out in the fields and inspecting every single grain, keeping the good ones and plucking off the ones with faults.', 'If there are faulty grains in there, they cheated and order was not maintained. Now you have to go through all the grains and throw out the weird ones yourself.', 'You can always tell if a company keeps <kanji>order</kanji> or if it <reading>cheats</reading> (ちつ) just by looking at the grains.', 'N1', NULL),
(2168, 'Imagine this spider controlling your mouth with its web, making you say things you wouldn''t dare think of. "Free healthcare for all!" "Pineapple is a legitimate pizza topping!" Man, you gotta stop letting that spider instigate you into saying weird stuff.', 'If this spider''s web is strong enough to control your mouth, you will most likely need a saw to cut it off, right?', 'You want the spider to stop instigating you into saying bad things and starting riots. So you pick up a <reading>saw</reading> (さ) and start cutting off the silken threads that are controlling your mouth.', 'N1', NULL),
(2169, 'Have you been suffering from flat, boring hair? Well do I have the swelling treatment you''ve been looking for!', 'Imagine watching your hair swell to the size of a large bowling ball. Imagine never being able to go bowling again because the comparison is too easy for people to make.', 'Using this method, your hair <kanji>swell</kanji>s so much it looks like a giant <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう) ball is sitting on top of your head. It''s all round, and well... swollen is probably the only word that fits.', 'N1', NULL),
(2170, 'Imagine aiming a bunch of flowers in the same direction towards you. Their aromatic fragrance coats your body. You smell great now!', 'Unfortunately, now when you''re at home you''re choked by the overwhelming fragrance of flowers, all concentrating their aromas on a single point (your home). Oh… oh no it''s too strong. You can''t breathe. And you live alone. Help! Someone! ', 'You decide to bring this <kanji>aromatic</kanji> <kanji>fragrance</kanji> into your <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう). To do this, you plant flowers all the way around your home, all facing in toward it.', 'N1', NULL),
(2171, 'Think of some other things your soul is like, but always make sure you come back to one word: constant.', 'A constant rule from Emperor こういち means constant kanji learning and constant WaniKani reviews. They will always be constant!', 'Also <kanji>constant</kanji> is the Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち, who will surely rule for a thousand more years!', 'N1', NULL),
(2172, 'People hang around the bookshelf, and the leader wants to see if anyone will steal the hat or not. That''s why its a test of ethics.', 'Now we''ll really see how everyone''s ethics are. Will they turn in the ring to the lost and found? Will they ignore it? Will they steal it?', 'To test your <kanji>ethics</kanji> even more, the leader puts down a <reading>rin</reading>g (りん) as well.', 'N1', NULL),
(2173, 'It''s a pretty large building and you can see inside to all of the different exhibits within the main exhibit.', 'Go up to the first exhibit and look very closely at some turtle chins, then some crab chins, then some alligator chins. This is weirdly familiar.', 'Right now the <kanji>exhibit</kanji> has a bunch of <reading>chin</reading>s (ちん) on display. Animal chins, human chins, every chin you can think of is in the exhibit.', 'N1', NULL),
(2174, 'Picture the page in your cookbook where it says "Shaving the hair off your geoduck = Necessary!!" It''s absolutely necessary that you follow that instruction!', 'What''s necessary? To shave the hair off your geoduck and to wear a suit. That''s not too hard. You should do it.', 'It is also <kanji>necessary</kanji> that you wear a <reading>su</reading>it (す) when eating shaved geoduck. It''s a super fancy meal so a suit is necessary for you to enjoy it correctly.', 'N1', NULL),
(2175, 'Imagine showing the leader your favorite book. "That''s not a book in MY library," he says, and throws it in the trash. That''s so biased! He didn''t even give it a shot!', '"This is such biased crap," you say, as you finish reading the title of a book called "Hens are better than all other birds." Screw this leader guy and his books.', 'You decide to give in to his <kanji>biased</kanji> recommendations and look at what he has in his library. It doesn''t take long until you realize that these books are all about <reading>hen</reading>s (へん) and hen care. This guy is so biased about his favorite books that he only filled his library with what HE wants to read. And apparently that thing is taking care of hens... weird.', 'N1', NULL),
(2176, 'These are tough times for your scooter, so be patient with it. Try to treat it with understanding and care.', 'Not just "good", but "gooooood". The long vowel is important here, so try to think of different ways you could make your scooter feel gooooood. Maybe a nice little wash after you’ve removed the splinter will do the trick.', 'Give your scooter only the best <kanji>treatment</kanji>, so it can feel real <reading>goo</reading>ooood (ぐう).', 'N1', NULL),
(2177, 'Picture everyone in the village getting all of their rice together as provisions for the army. Even the kids and old people are helping out.', 'Picture the soldiers gobbling down their provisions of rice and roe in their row boats as they row away.', 'Once all of the <kanji>provisions</kanji> are ready, the soldiers show up in their <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boats and load it all up. But they aren''t done there. "Rice isn''t enough, we need <reading>roe</reading> (ろう) so we will have the strength to row our row boats back." And so the village brings them all of their roe too.', 'N1', NULL),
(2178, 'A jet with a teeter-totter? Normal yakuza boss. A jet with a jungle gym? Powerful yakuza boss. A jet with a slide? <em>Especially</em> powerful yakuza boss.', 'Just don''t get on the bad side of one of these especially important yakuza bosses — a trap door at the bottom of the chute will open and drop you out of their jet.', 'These <kanji>especially</kanji> powerful yakuza bosses have slides that are like <reading>chu</reading>tes (しゅ) — they''re especially fast and especially fun!', 'N1', NULL),
(2179, 'Imagine the level of overconfidence necessary to think you could capture the soul of such an important celestial body with just a net. Get off your stool, stop being lazy, and go try to catch the soul of something more within your league — a small asteroid, for example.', 'Manga has also given you the overconfidence to believe that your lack of progress in life isn''t due to laziness — it''s because you''re still in your "training arc." ', 'Your <kanji>overconfidence</kanji> stems from your <reading>man</reading>ga (まん) addiction, which is also the source of your <kanji>laziness</kanji>. You lie in bed for hours a day reading manga and imagining yourself as the protagonist, and it''s seriously distorted your sense of reality. This is probably why you think you can do outlandish things like capturing the sun''s soul. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2180, 'Picture everyone in the world being swept away in a tsunami to die. This is what happens when you try to use nature as a weapon.', 'You don''t want to die, do you? Get to the boats!', 'The only way to maybe not <kanji>die</kanji> is to find <reading>boats</reading> (ぼつ). As long as the boats don''t get smashed by the tsunami, you might be able to cheat death by holding onto the boats and not drowning.', 'N1', NULL),
(2181, 'Picture a machine with a heart, lying on the couch, eating oil snacks, snoozing and neglecting the orders you''ve given it. What a lazy machine!', 'Picture this lazy machine sitting on the couch again. There are untied ties all over the floor, hanging on the back of the couch, but it totally neglects them.', 'The machine is so <kanji>lazy</kanji> it even starts to <kanji>neglect</kanji> its <reading>tie</reading> (たい). It''s supposed to wear one, but it''s too lazy to put it on now. Do lazy humans put on ties? Nope, and neither do neglectful machines. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2182, 'Picture all of the people you encounter as you ride your scooter, spreading your music around town.', '"We love your music, look at our souls!" Uh oh, this is turning into some kind of horror movie. You better scoot a bit faster or you might meet an unfortunate fate.', 'Everyone you <kanji>encounter</kanji> wants to bare their <reading>sou</reading>l (そう) to you, literally. They''re all coming out to meet you, pulling back their skin and showing you the soul ghosts inside of them. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2183, 'Picture the lazy narwhal laying on a block of ice in the moonlight. There are tools and other blocks of ice around, but he just can''t seem to get up and do any construction work.', 'Picture your lazy dad. Your big, lazy narwhal dad.', 'You can''t tell the <kanji>lazy</kanji> narwhal he''s being lazy because he''s your <reading>da</reading>d (だ). If he was anyone else you''d tell him off for being lazy, but you can''t tell off your dad. That''s just not right.', 'N1', NULL),
(2184, 'Picture the shape of the animal in the grass, off in the distance. You can''t see what kind of animal it is yet, but you know you''re hunting it.', 'You wouldn''t be able to go hunting in any other kind of boat—the motor would be too loud. Picture yourself in a row boat with all of your hunting gear. You need to be extra careful not to make any loud splashing sounds with the oars!', 'You''re currently <kanji>hunting</kanji> in a <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat and you have to row as quietly and carefully as you can or the animal will run away.', 'N1', NULL),
(2185, 'Picture yourself climbing stairs, picture going from room to room. From place to place. From wherever you were to wherever you want to be.', 'Imagine yourself going from one place to another with your pockets stuffed full of nori. The smell is starting to get overwhelming. It''s making you really hungry (or nauseous, depending on how you feel about nori!).', 'If you''re going <kanji>from</kanji> place to place, make sure you have some <reading>no</reading>ri (の) seaweed on you, in case you get hungry. Nori is a great source of energy.', 'N1', NULL),
(2186, 'Imagine holding your creation in your frostbitten hands as the snowflake design starts to appear. The design gets more and more complicated the longer you watch, twisting this way and that, like it''s dancing in front of your eyes.

', 'If you know someone else called Aya, go ahead and use them in this mnemonic! Whoever it is, imagine an Aya watching from afar holding a magic wand, creating this beautiful frosty pattern!', 'As you watch the snowflake <kanji>design</kanji> form you see letters appearing in the corner! They form the name <reading>Aya</reading> (あや), our artist here at WaniKani. Somehow she''s magically putting together this beautiful pattern!', 'N1', NULL),
(2187, 'Imagine how quick the wind knocks you over when you stand up. So quick, you don''t even remember being up. Try again and count how many seconds it takes for it to knock you down. Less than one. Now that''s quick.', 'Okay, pretty quick, but this wind is even quicker than that.', 'That <kanji>quick</kanji> wind is quicker than you can say <reading>Satsu</reading>ki (さつ). And "Satsuki" is a pretty quick sounding name already. Go ahead, say "Satsuki" as quick as you can right now. Heck, try it a few times.', 'N1', NULL),
(2188, 'Imagine tying a cross to this turkey and see it transform into an elegant falcon. Majestic.', 'Picture your falcon Hayabusa and the real... ok fake.. Hayabusa running around together and kicking butt.', 'You decide to name this new <kanji>falcon</kanji> after your favorite falcon-like ninja. Ryu <reading>Hayabusa</reading> (はやぶさ) from <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryu_Hayabusa" target="_blank">Ninja Gaiden</a>! Picture your fat turkey falcon and Ryu Hayabusa teaming up Banjo and Kazooey style. Yeah, that''s the dream right there.', 'N1', NULL),
(2189, 'Picture all of your close friends coming to help you squeeze everything for the wedding into your car. ', 'Imagine calling all your friends and scheduling when you will get their help. Make sure you have your day planner ready!', 'You can''t just ask for <kanji>help</kanji> on the day of the wedding, either. You have to <reading>sche</reading>dule (すけ) getting your friends'' help ahead of time. They might have pretty tight schedules too, so make sure you plan early!', 'N1', NULL),
(2190, 'These roof flowers only see because they do not judge. They accept everything. We humans can learn a thing or two about tolerance from these roof flowers.', 'You wouldn''t expect it, but he actually allowed many types of cultures, religions, and peoples to exist under his reign. The Mongols had an exceptional amount of tolerance for the time.', '<kanji>Tolerance</kanji> is one thing that Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) had a surprising amount of.', 'N1', NULL),
(2191, 'Picture the moon with a big bag circling it. Now look down at your biology homework on cells. They''re identical!', 'AHHH CLEAN ALL OF THESE CELLS OUT OF YOUR HOME!', 'As you learn about <kanji>cell</kanji>s you realize they''re all over your <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう). Without ever noticing it, your home has been slowly filling with dead skin cells. And now that you know what they look like (the moon with a plastic wrap bag around it) you can see them everywhere.', 'N1', NULL),
(2192, 'Anything less than a tsunami of conflict would leave something behind. But this much conflict will cleanse the area of everything.', 'Joe has gone through many things during your WaniKani journey, but this might be too much even for him. Either way, he’s going to try his best to stop this tsunami of conflict, threatening to cleanse the lands of everything we know. With his bare hands, of course.', 'Who could possibly stop this <kanji>cleanse</kanji> of all lands?! The only person capable of this is, of course, <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう), the local farmhand. He’s so tall and burly that he can stop even the largest of tsunamis… right?', 'N1', NULL),
(2193, 'All buildings have at least one scooter. It could be out front, on the roof, in the basement. But all of them have scooters and someone who likes to ride them instead of using a car or a bike.', 'Imagine the sound of the scooter''s tiny engine: zuwee zuwee zu zu zuwee. All of them do this, which is why you hate them.', '<kanji>All</kanji> of these scooters have a specific sound they make as they burst out of the buildings: <reading>zuwee</reading> (ずい).', 'N1', NULL),
(2194, 'Imagine holding your draft up outside in a field of grains. You''re a couple leaves short. Time to get back to the drawing board.', 'Now こういち is out there measuring your draft against the tall grain plants. You better hope it''s long enough, because if it isn''t, Emperor こういち''s probably going to execute you. ', 'Who asked you to write such a tall <kanji>draft</kanji> in the first place? Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち, who wants you to draft up the world''s largest book about how great of an emperor he is.', 'N1', NULL),
(2195, 'Picture this broken, rust-colored boat. It may be rusty and missing some important pieces, but it''s not trying to hide its flaws — and there''s a beautiful sincerity in that.', 'Watch your skin slowly change as you tan to match the rust-colored boat. Don''t worry about the uncomfortable burning sensation — that''s just proof you''re becoming a more sincere, more tan human being.', 'The <kanji>rust-colored</kanji> boat has inspired you, so you lie on its deck and get a <reading>tan</reading> (たん). If you can tan until you''re the same <kanji>cinnabar</kanji> color as the boat, you think some of its <kanji>sincerity</kanji> might rub off on you.', 'N1', NULL),
(2196, 'Soil generally has more nutrients in it. It makes sense for dirt that''s been nursed to become soil.', 'That kind of makes Joe a dirt or soil nurse, if you think about it. Not sure he’d like that title, though… ', '<kanji>Soil</kanji> is a thing that <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう), the huge farmhand, works on improving. If he makes the soil better, the farm will yield more crops.', 'N1', NULL),
(2197, 'Imagine the incredibly laborious task of paving an entire road by yourself, just to get to this cottage wedding shop. You really want your quaint little cottage wedding though, so it’s the only choice you have…', 'Imagine walking around the shop and then falling in love with a hoe with pretty tassels and flowers attached to it. Yes, it''s only for decoration, but you''re going to buy and use it during your cottage wedding.', 'You''re browsing everything in the <kanji>shop</kanji> when a garden <reading>hoe</reading> (ほ) catches your eye. Yes. This will make your cottage wedding the absolute best one. The hoe will give you away at your wedding.', 'N1', NULL),
(2198, 'Picture the gladiator riding his horse in the light of the moon yelling, "Do you have any idea how important it is to increase the average pay rate to account for inflation? All of us gladiators are going to be unable to pay the bills soon because inflation isn''t being taken into account! You''ll suffer too! Listen to me!"', 'Imagine if you were in this gladiator''s place. In five years, you wouldn''t be able to afford to eat thanks to the bosses in とうきょう refusing to adjust for inflation. Back in my day, onigiri only cost FIFteen yen!', '<kanji>Inflation</kanji> rises the most in <reading>とう</reading>きょう, and the gladiator knows that because that''s where most of them live and fight (and die).', 'N1', NULL),
(2199, 'Why thread? Back in the old days globes were sewn together with cloth and thread, so there was a literal thread dividing the Koreas!

Or you can just picture an actual, super long piece of thread going around the entire globe. That works too.', 'Imagine being an eagle yourself, flying around the entire globe, following the latitude line that splits Korea down the middle.', 'The only way you can see this <kanji>latitude</kanji> line is to ride an <reading>ea</reading>gle (い) over it. The line isn''t visible from a car, you''re too close, and a plane is too high. You have to see it from an eagle''s point of view.', 'N1', NULL),
(2200, 'Picture the Yoga King rowing a rowboat. Maybe he''s using his legs. Maybe he''s using his butt? You don''t know. Yoga people are super flexible, he could be using anything to move this rowboat.', 'Go rent a rowboat and try eating tater tots in it. For fun, throw a bunch of them in the water and watch them float. Maybe you''ll catch a fish!', 'As he rows the <kanji>rowboat</kanji> with his butt, you see the Yoga King eating something with his toes. They''re <reading>ta</reading>ter tots (てい). Tater tots are the perfect snack for a rowboat. They''re small, delicious, and they float if you drop them in the water.', 'N1', NULL),
(2201, 'Close your eyes. Picture someone''s arm. Now touch their skin with your fingers and peel it back to expose the muscles and bones underneath.', 'Imagine the heat steaming out, like it''s coming out of a teapot, not out of somebody''s arm skin.', 'You <kanji>expose</kanji> a small hole under their skin and <reading>he</reading>at (ひ) comes pouring out. Aha! You knew there was something wrong under here. Apparently there''s something under this person''s skin that''s exuding way too much heat.', 'N1', NULL),
(2202, 'Imagine weaving this beautiful brocade fabric out of one of your old towels. Try to actually picture the gold and white patterns you want on there. If you''re a crafter, actually do it!', 'Imagine using your niche key to open this hidden brocade storage room. The key is gold and white and looks like brocade too!', 'You want to keep your <kanji>brocade</kanji> fabrics safe, so you lock them away and seal them in with a <reading>niche key</reading> (にしき). It''s niche because brocade is a very niche kind of thing.', 'N1', NULL),
(2204, 'Imagine seeing so much grain that you can stuff your mouth so full you can''t swallow or spit it out. You have to use a knife to remove it. After you get all the grain out, comment on the surplus of grain.', 'Remember how Joe made the soil better? Perhaps this is some of that work paying off. Thanks to that and everything else, there is a great surplus.', 'The <kanji>surplus</kanji> is thanks to the hard work of folks like <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう), the big farmhand. He worked hard to make sure there was a surplus of grain this year.', 'N1', NULL),
(2205, 'Imagine a drunkard crossing threads onto spikes and pulling the tiny fibers free. You don''t think you could do that. Maybe if you got drunk too...', 'Watch the centaur cross the fibers and separate them with his spikes. How strange.', 'You stare at the <kanji>fiber</kanji>s easily coming apart when you realize that this drunkard seems to have a few extra legs... and hair and a tail? This isn''t just a drunk guy, it''s a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん). Maybe separating fibers is really a centaur trick, and that''s why normal people can''t do it...', 'N1', NULL),
(2206, 'Imagine being admonished by the Death Star. It''s hovering above you, giant and round, just yelling at you about everything you''ve ever messed up. It''s really awful being yelled at. But the Death Star doing it is particularly scary and embarrassing.', 'Imagine sitting in your yurt, the home you love, but you can still hear the admonishments from the Death Star in your ears. You feel... bad.', 'Right now you''re being <kanji>admonish</kanji>ed about living in a <reading>yu</reading>rt (ゆ). The Death Star bellows, "What, am I too big and safe for you? I''m easy to move too, you know. You''re a disgrace! You should be ashamed!"', 'N1', NULL),
(2207, 'This disaster is ruining your life. You can''t eat, sleep, all you can do is panic trying to get the hair out.', 'Santa-san has forsaken you. Your life is a wreck. This is a total disaster just because of one big hair.', 'This <kanji>disaster</kanji> is so huge that even <reading>San</reading>ta-san (さん) left his vacation in <reading>Zan</reading>zibar (ざん) to come tell you you''re on the naughty list for being such a disaster person. WHAT?! Even you Santa-san?', 'N1', NULL),
(2208, 'Imagine being a tiger or Wolverine and being oppressed just because you have claws. Imagine being stuck in a cage. Not being allowed to vote. It''s awful!', 'Imagine how silly it would look for Wolverine and these animals to have their claws covered in multicolored goo. Gak is such a humiliating way to oppress someone.', 'Tigers and other clawed beings like Wolverine are so <kanji>oppress</kanji>ed, they''re forced to cover their claws with <reading>Gak</reading> (ぎゃく). You know, that weird silly putty they made us play with as kids? Well, it''s now an instrument of anti-Wolverine, anti-tiger oppression.', 'N1', NULL),
(2209, 'Imagine reaching out and grabbing onto the old flag with your fingers. Feel the dirty grime and little holes it''s gotten. Now install something new!', 'Picture a newly installed suit flying in the air on a pole.', 'You''ve decided to <kanji>install</kanji> a <reading>su</reading>it (す) where the old flag used to be. I mean, a suit is probably more durable than a flag, am I right? This way you won''t have to install flags as often.', 'N1', NULL),
(2210, 'Unfortunately it''s not specific as to what kind of surplus you''re loitering around, so you''ll need to imagine a surplus of something memorable to you. Then, just imagine loitering around there, being really gentle with everything.', 'Imagine Jo-Anne coming back to her surplus, then asking you if you were gentle with the goods. You were, you treated everything very gently.', 'You have to be <kanji>gentle</kanji> and handle these things <kanji>gently</kanji> because they belong to <reading>Jo</reading>-Anne (じょ). You''re loitering around her surplus of (whatever you decided to imagine).', 'N1', NULL),
(2211, 'Picture yourself boarding the ship and letting the flowers slip from your fingers, into the crowd of people waving goodbye.', 'Where else would you go at this point? I mean, come on. You''ve made it 53 levels since you embarked on your kanji journey with us. We know you by now. You want to be in とうきょう.', 'You <kanji>board</kanji> a ship to <reading>とう</reading>きょう, bound for your new life, of course!', 'N1', NULL),
(2213, 'Picture your village in Japan. There''s a big, still pond full of bright and beautiful giant carp. You hear the village kids giggle as they feed them and their big round mouths pop out of the water.', 'Carp are koi and koi are carp. Blub blub.', 'A <kanji>carp</kanji> is called a <reading>koi</reading> (こい) in Japanese. We''ve used that word before so I hope you know it by now. A koi is a koi, you know?', 'N1', NULL),
(2215, 'Picture a person wearing all scarlet all the time. Don''t they give you a criminal-y vibe?', 'Picture a criminal, covered in this scarlet thread, heat radiating off of him.', 'This <kanji>scarlet</kanji> color holds a lot of <reading>he</reading>at (ひ). Scarlet is the color of fire, and it has the heat of fire too.', 'N1', NULL),
(2216, 'Picture someone tied down in a net as dawn breaks.', 'Look at Akebono looking at you as you look at dawn breaking.', 'The someone who saw <kanji>dawn</kanji> for the first time goes on to become sumo wrestling champ <reading>Akebono</reading> Taro (あけぼの). He always gazes at the sunrise at dawn before he wrestles and it fills him with strength.', 'N1', NULL),
(2217, 'Just imagine a huge barbarian (whatever that means to you) floating in the sky, looking down. Some ancient people look up and wave at the old moon barbarian.', '"You see, my child, the old moon was not like the current moon. It was a giant barbarian in the sky, who would eat children that asked too many questions."', 'At least, this is the kind of story you told your <reading>子</reading> (こ) when they asked you where the moon came from.', 'N1', NULL),
(2219, 'Imagine a commander carefully looking over everything. They not only notice a drop of something on a towel, but they''re able to identify that the drop is a small piece of liquid bear.', '"Weeee!" says the commander.', 'You watch the <kanji>commander</kanji> in all his glory... going back and forth on a <reading>swi</reading>ng (すい).', 'N1', NULL),
(2220, 'Imagine searching for this special door in the cold wind and snow of winter. The search for enlightenment always has something to do with suffering and searching on your own like this.', 'All you need to do to attain enlightenment is to eat this cake... out of this mouth. Eh. Maybe enlightenment isn''t worth it.', 'The door <kanji>enlight</kanji>ened you by opening up and revealing the <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい) in its door-mouth. "Cake is the key to enlightenment, my child. Now eat it from my mouth."', 'N1', NULL),
(2221, 'Picture a tiny yokai using a hollyhock as a tent. Aww cute!', 'Imagine yourself in a field of hollyhocks and the あおい of them match the あおい of the heavens! It''s like you''re standing on a giant flower filled mirror of the sky.', '<kanji>Hollyhock</kanji>s are mostly <reading>あおい</reading> (blue) because they copy the color of the heavens, where they came from.', 'N1', NULL),
(2222, 'Imagine the eyes of a horse seeing a spider and then running at top speed to get the heck away from it.', 'Soon the world will be filled with horses running at full speed all thanks to the spider increase of the 2000s! Soon we will be in the future of top speeds!', 'Once people realize that they can increase the <kanji>speed</kanji> of their horses, <reading>soon</reading> (しゅん) everyone will be taking spiders with them to scare their horses into running faster. I say soon because it hasn''t quite caught on yet. But if you help to spread the word it can happen even sooner.', 'N1', NULL),
(2223, 'Imagine the confused faces of the people in the countryside as you try to talk to them. Decide to say it the way it''s said in the capital and see their faces light up as they comprehend what you''re trying to say.', 'Picture yourself rowing away in your new row boat as the townspeople wave goodbye. They were much nicer once they comprehended what you were asking for. Maybe you''ll row your way back here someday.', 'Now that the townspeople finally <kanji>comprehend</kanji> what you were trying to say, they sell you the <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat you were trying to buy. You were using the wrong word, but りょう is what they needed to hear to understand you, and now you can row your way back home.', 'N1', NULL),
(2224, 'It''s weird that jasmine flowers are growing out of your grain, but that''s why you''re cutting them off.', 'You have a farm that grows grain and reeds. Jasmine flowers are growing out of both of those things, ruining your crops! Perhaps you should become a jasmine tea farmer instead...', 'The <kanji>jasmine</kanji> is growing out of everything, not just grain! It''s also growing out of the <reading>ree</reading>ds (り)!', 'N1', NULL),
(2225, 'Picture the underwater fish capital. Statues of whales line the fishy streets, and all of the fish bow down to the whale ruler''s giant body.', 'Picture the gay whale ruler holding whale hands... fins? with his whale husband. They''re so cute.', 'The <kanji>whale</kanji> who resides over the capital just happens to be <reading>gay</reading> (げい). You only know this because his husband is a man whale too. Actually, now that you think about it, all the whales you''ve ever met have been gay.', 'N1', NULL),
(2226, 'Picture the samurai''s villa. It''s in the countryside and covered in flowers and icicles. Even though it''s spring, it''s still cold, so all the new flowers are very cold.', 'Picture the samurai sitting in a chair in his beautiful villa and breathing his last breath. His soul slowly floats up and out of his still body and dances around the villa, free.', 'Once the samurai enters the <kanji>villa</kanji>, he lets his <reading>soul</reading> (そう) go. He''s been fighting for long enough and this villa was always where he wanted to go.', 'N1', NULL),
(2227, 'Picture a drunk guy laughing and plowing a field and planting seeds. He wouldn''t do this if he was sober, but drunk people can''t help themselves.', 'Oh my sweet cyborg child! Is this what they''ve done with you? You''re just a drunkard who''s only good for planting? Nooooooo!!', 'He keeps <kanji>planting</kanji> and planting... a little too much. You walk over and realize this drunkard isn''t just any guy... he''s YOUR <reading>CY</reading>BORG (さい)!', 'N1', NULL),
(2228, 'Imagine you''ve been kidnapped. And the person who kidnapped you is going to cut off some of your fingers and mail them to your parents if they don''t pay the ransom soon enough. You don''t want your fingers to be cut off with a sword! Help!', 'If your parents don''t send in the kayak to your kidnapper soon, you''re gonna lose some fingers. Come on guys, just get the kayak already!!!', 'You''ve been <kanji>kidnap</kanji>ped and your kidnapper has asked for one thing in exchange for your safe return: a <reading>kay</reading>ak (かい). Kayaks are expensive. And it''s way easier for this dude to kidnap you and demand your family buy one and give it to them than for them to work for one.', 'N1', NULL),
(2229, 'Imagine someone putting a tape measure around your forehead and taking careful measurements for your crown. They better not mess up, or you''ll have them put to death!', 'Why don''t you go look up what Genghis Khan''s crown/cap looked like. Then try to imagine how it would look atop your head.', 'You want your <kanji>crown</kanji> to be just like Genghis <reading>Khan</reading>''s (かん), which was more of a cap. He''s your hero though, because he ruled over the most people, so you want yours to match his measurements.', 'N1', NULL),
(2230, 'Most people in places heavy with power, like the scouts, boil down all of their responsibilities and accomplishments this way into merit badges.', 'This is why you never see a military officer or a girl scout leaving their merit badges out in the open. They pin them right onto their clothes. Otherwise the raccoons would come.', 'The only problem with consolidating your <kanji>merit</kanji>s like this is that rac<reading>coon</reading>s (くん) are always trying to steal them. Raccoons really like shiny, small things. Like, say, the physical representation of all your personal and professional merits.', 'N1', NULL),
(2231, 'Picture yourself dropping the alcohol, drop by drop, into the river. Uh oh, the person just caught up to you, so you better repay them.', 'Imagine waiting as this person tries on like twenty different pairs of shoes. They''re getting them for free! Why do they have to be picky?! Just let me repay you already!', 'In order to <kanji>repay</kanji> them for the alcohol you wasted, you offer to give them your <reading>shoe</reading>s (しゅう). They don''t want your shoes though, so you have to bring them to a store and watch them try on a bunch of different pairs so you can repay them that way.', 'N1', NULL),
(2232, 'Imagine the thread slowly forming the writing that makes up your family crest. You''re making a scarf with your crest on it. It''s like a wizarding school house symbol.', 'What kind of monster is on your family crest? Picture it in your mind right now. Now tweet to us @wanikaniapp because I really want to know what you came up with!!', 'Your <kanji>family crest</kanji> is of a <reading>mon</reading>ster! (もん) And the writing says "Our Family Monster."', 'N1', NULL),
(2233, 'Imagine yourself stopping at the empty bank at noon, grabbing a handful of wholesale stamps. So many stamps. So few extra charges.', '"Oh Roshi" you say carefully as you hand your stamps over at noon. The guy raises an eyebrow at you, but gives you them wholesale! It worked! ', 'Oh, one more thing. Going at noon isn''t enough, you have to say the password to get them at <kanji>wholesale</kanji> price too. The password is "<reading>Oh Roshi</reading>" (おろし). Okay, so maybe that''s just the name of the guy there, but it always works when I want to get stamps wholesale so you should do it too!', 'N1', NULL),
(2234, 'Picture these column trees outside the gate to the east. You end up drawing them in the columns of your graph paper because they fit so well.', 'Now draw Randy Savage in the columns on your graph paper. He just seems to fit there now.', 'Out of the tree <kanji>column</kanji>s bursts professional wrestler <reading>Ran</reading>dy (らん) Savage! "Oooh I''m talking about columns that I like mmmm oh yeaah. The Macho Man loves columns MMm OOHH YEAAH~!"', 'N1', NULL),
(2235, 'Imagine excusing yourself by scooting away on a scooter. If they want to get your answer, they''ll have to chase you.', 'Welp, it''s too late to turn back now. You''ve already deviated pretty far, and you''re not even sure where you are now. You were too busy trying to elude that person that you didn''t look where your scooter was taking you.', 'You <kanji>deviate</kanji> from the plan a little too well, and as you''re scooting, what you''ve done <reading>eats</reading> (いつ) away at you. You feel guilty for eluding them... ', 'N1', NULL),
(2236, 'Imagine this mustachioed triceratops. He probably has a monocle and a fedora too. Every time you think you can talk or leave he says, "Furthermore" again. Come on guy, how much do you have to say?', '"Furthermore!" the mustachioed triceratops points at the Shougun, "He is the best Shougun we''ll ever have. And he''ll let me talk forever OH OH OH!"', 'Why don''t you just interrupt this mustache triceratops while he''s saying <kanji>furthermore</kanji>? Because he''s the <reading>Shou</reading>gun''s (しょう) pet. If you interrupt him you''ll probably be put to death or something. Just let him keep going and hope he runs out of furthermores to talk about.', 'N1', NULL),
(2237, 'The spikes don''t just come out of the geoduck, they literally appear out of thin air. They didn''t even <kanji>exist</kanji> before the sun touched them.', '"Thank you sweet geoduck, you have taught me the way of appearing with your spikes."', 'You watch these spikes <kanji>appear</kanji> and suddenly <reading>Ken</reading> (けん)the samurai also appears next to you. "I DID IT!" he shouts, "I''ve learned how to appear out of thin air!"', 'N1', NULL),
(2238, 'Putting a stick in some rice is a ritual that Wolverine practices to mark solemn occasions. Shh, listen up! He''s probably about to say something solemn and important about his dad.', 'Do you think you could shake the solemnness out of someone? Imagine being shooketh so hard, you stop feeling solemn. Wouldn''t that be great?', 'When you first saw how <kanji>solemn</kanji> Wolverine was after his dad died, it really <reading>shook</reading> (しゅく) you. Now you''re used to always seeing him emotionally cold and distant. It might be time to shake that solemnity out of him though.', 'N1', NULL),
(2239, 'You were foolish to give your heart away to Splinter. He didn''t want it. Also he''s a rat, what''s wrong with you?', 'What will you do with your foolish goo heart now? ', 'Splinter tells you to your face how <kanji>foolish</kanji> you are and you feel your heart turn to <reading>goo</reading> (ぐ). Now you can''t even give it to someone else. No one wants goo.', 'N1', NULL),
(2240, 'Wait, you''re telling me you didn''t know it''s common to boil things in yurts? Excuse me?', 'Okay, now that I''ve shown you what to do, you can fit in with the common people here.', 'Here, the <kanji>common</kanji> people all do this, so just let me <reading>show</reading> (しょ) you how. First you go into your yurt, and… Hey, pay attention and watch what I''m showing you!', 'N1', NULL),
(2241, 'Are you the kind to practice flirting with the bartender, or are you more the kind to engage in a bit of small talk with the other patrons? Maybe you''re just trying to practice patience while the bartender is talking instead of getting your order ready.', 'Picture your centaur foot on the bar as you practice to become a gymnast. It''s going to take years and years of this kind of practice, but you''re sure you''ll make it all the way up there some day! Never give up!', 'This type of <kanji>practice</kanji> is especially hard for you because you''re a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん). You only have hooves for feet, and balancing on a bar is going to be a huge hurdle. But your teacher promised if you practiced enough you could make it, and here you are! With your hoofed foot on the bar!', 'N1', NULL),
(2242, 'Imagine the Mouth King giving a presentation about the importance of mouths. His subjects all flatter him by saying, "Please, please, present to us about mouths again! We truuuuuuly love it!"', 'You pour a bucket of tater tots into his mouth body and he continues his presentation about mouths. "You see, mouths are just orifices..."', '"I will <kanji>present</kanji> to you a presentation about mouths, but I require energy. Please fill my giant mouth body with delicious <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tots.', 'N1', NULL),
(2243, 'Picture this sparse, neglected coat rack. All of its arms and the coats that were on it have fallen off and are in a big pile on the ground.', 'If you neglect something for too long, not even a good soap scrub will bring it back. That goes for humans too. Take care of yourselves. Use soap.', 'You go up to the <kanji>neglect</kanji>ed coat rack and try to make things right by cleaning it with <reading>so</reading>ap (そ). But no matter how much you scrub, it won''t get clean, the arms won''t go back on.', 'N1', NULL),
(2244, 'Picture this rapid sickness spreading through your body like a cartoon arrow ricocheting through your body. Yuck!', 'Imagine getting sick this rapidly. All of a sudden, there''s a shih tzu barking in your head, it hurts! And... oh no, where''s the toilet?!', 'This <kanji>rapid</kanji> sickness makes your head pound like there''s a <reading>shih tzu</reading> (しつ) yapping and barking nonstop. And it''s making you sh*t a lot.', 'N1', NULL),
(2245, 'Say whatever you want and send it up to the satellite. Now imagine listening to it when it comes back and hearing beautiful, deep Noh chanting back!', 'Watch closely the next time you go to a Noh play. See the man Noh chanting? Watch close enough and you''ll see him eat a scoop of yogurt every once in a while.', 'While you''re fake <kanji>Noh chanting</kanji> you have to pause every few minutes to take a bite of <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう). Yogurt is good for the throat! At least you think it is. It''s not like you''re actually chanting, the satellite is doing all the real work.', 'N1', NULL),
(2246, 'Imagine wielding your gold Mona Lisa sickle. It''s sooo heavy, but it''s really shiny and it cuts grass and wheat pretty well!', 'Hear all the guests say, "Mona Lisa... comma the sickle? WHAT''S HAPPENED HERE?!" As panic ensues. You like your addition and you''re very proud.', 'Now that you''ve turned the Mona Lisa into a <kanji>sickle</kanji>, you have to change the little description under its name in the Louvre. You draw a giant <reading>comma</reading> (かま) next to "Mona Lisa" and write "THE SICKLE." Then you shove it back on the wall as best you can.', 'N1', NULL),
(2247, 'Imagine if your country''s leader announced that there would be no more alcohol. People would be in the streets in seconds calling them cruel and unjust!', 'Imagine drinking coke while complaining how unjust it is that you can''t add rum to it.', 'After this <kanji>cruel</kanji> announcement is made everyone will turn to the next best thing after alcohol. <reading>Coke</reading> (こく)! It''s not a replacement, but if this unjust law won''t change, then coke sales are going to go through the roof.', 'N1', NULL),
(2248, 'You have a surplus (extra) stool, and someone wants it. But, they need to know more about it, so you describe it in great detail. Imagine yourself doing just that. Every screw and nubbin is described.', 'Jo-Anne is really excited about this stool. It''s the perfect height for her (remember, she''s on the shorter side to distinguish じょ from じょう). She pays attention to you describing the stool while sitting on it and spinning around. Have her ask for you to describe it more. ', 'You <kanji>describe</kanji> the features of the stool to <reading>Jo</reading>-Anne (じょ). ', 'N1', NULL),
(2249, 'Picture all the things you also need. A suit, a tie, a monocle, some shiny shoes. There''s also ... something... else. What is it?', 'Think about all the things you also need when you wear a top hat. The car is probably the most important addition. Otherwise no one can see the top hat in the first place!', 'You have a top hat and all the clothes that go with it but you <kanji>also</kanji> need a nice <reading>ca</reading>r (か) too! You can''t just get all dressed up and then have no way to get anywhere to show it off.', 'N1', NULL),
(2250, 'How stupid do you have to be to put arrows into your mouth until it makes you sick? Pretty darn stupid, it seems.', 'Imagine if everyone knew that cheese cures the stupid out of people. Then maybe we would have more cheese and less stupid in the world!', 'The only way to cure this serious case of <kanji>stupid</kanji> is to make you eat <reading>chee</reading>se (ち). Not only will it fix your sick stomach by cushioning all the arrows that you shoved in your stupid mouth, but it should send smart cheese waves to your brain and cure you of that stupid all together.', 'N1', NULL),
(2254, 'Imagine being in a graveyard looking at all the tombstones and seeing nothing but stems on all of them. All the pretty flowery parts have died and blown away.', 'Look at all the cookie covered tombstones. Now your bag is full of stems, but you can use them to make more cookies to bring back next time. Don''t worry, nobody eats them. Stem cookies are probably poisonous.', 'You have an idea that can make these tombstones look better than just having leftover <kanji>stem</kanji>s all over them. You brought <reading>cookie</reading>s (くき)! Cookies don''t fall apart in the wind, and everyone loves cookies, alive or dead. You replace the stems with the cookies and everything looks a lot nicer.', 'N1', NULL),
(2255, 'Flattering buildings is just as nice as flattering people, as long as they''re okay with it.', 'The "ah" the building made was actually quite loud, and since you''ve never heard a building speak before, it scared you pretty bad. But at least you know the building was flattered by your compliment (I think.)', 'The building is <kanji>flatter</kanji>ed and you hear it shout, "<reading>Ah</reading> (あ)!" and blush. The entire building turns red.', 'N1', NULL),
(2256, 'You fall to your knees, "Oh thank you for not hitting me with your stick and letting me relax!" You haven''t had a moment of leisure in ages!', 'As the leader leisurely watches, the youths smack you with their sticks. What a cruel world! "Enjoy your leisure time," the leader yells over, "the youths hit much softer than I do. Have fun!"', 'You''re just about to start your <kanji>leisure</kanji> time when a group of <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう) appears with their own sticks. You look back for the leader, and he''s wearing a bathrobe, sunglasses, and is drinking some hot cocoa. Wait... this is leisure time for... HIM?! OH NO!', 'N1', NULL),
(2257, 'Picture the mouth sized apricots hanging down off the tree. Imagine using your own mouth to bite them down. Just don''t swallow the pit!', 'Imagine trying to eat the bite sized apricot and spitting it out after the taste of anchovies from the zoo fill you up. ', 'After you put the <kanji>apricot</kanji> in your mouth you realize that this tastes really familiar, but not pleasant. It tastes just like the <reading>an</reading>chovies (あん) from the <reading>zoo</reading> (ず) smell. How did that happen? You don''t like apricots anymore.', 'N1', NULL),
(2258, 'Picture yourself sitting in the west as the red sun sets, smashing those little rose madder roots into a paste to make your deep red dyes. Your fingers turn red from the rose madder too.', 'Hear the little girl say あかね while pointing at the rose madder and your deep red fingers. She''s right. And the way she says it is so darn cute.', 'As you smash the roots of the <kanji>rose madder</kanji> and stain your fingers a <kanji>deep red</kanji>, a little girl comes up to you and points. She says, "<reading>赤ね</reading> (あかね)?" (That''s red, right?) You look down at the rose madder roots and your fingers, and nod. She''s right, it is red. Deep red.', 'N1', NULL),
(2259, 'To be fair, books are just dry trees in a way, but bookmarks are actually dry, dry trees that have flattened out and turned into chunks.', 'What does she owe you? Open the page to your bookmark and see she owes reeds!', 'What are you bookmarking anyway? Look down and see yourself putting one of the tree <kanji>bookmark</kanji>s on a page in your notebook that says "<reading>She owe</reading>s me <reading>ree</reading>ds (しおり)." Oh yeah, you had to make this bookmark in the first place because your friend took your reed bookmarks and didn''t give them back.', 'N1', NULL),
(2260, 'Picture yourself bowing to the leader dog. Leader dog is so cool!', 'Bow even deeper as the dog leader keeps barking what sounds like f**k you. You''re pretty sure that''s what he''s saying at least... but maybe... maybe it''s not.', 'You <kanji>bow</kanji> as low as you can, but you lose your balance and fall over. "<reading>F**k</reading> you (ふく)!" the leader dog barks. Oh no. You messed up and now the leader dog is super mad. Saying f**k you is a little harsh for falling over though, you think.', 'N1', NULL),
(2261, 'Imagine being tranquilized by the sweet caress of gold coins. You can''t even move. You''re in a daze or something.', 'If you let gold tranquilize you, you''ll drool all over your own chin. Maybe the chins of other people too, who knows?', 'You''ve been <kanji>tranquilize</kanji>d and now drool is running down your <reading>chin</reading> (ちん). It''s actually really gross. But you don''t care, look at all this gold! No one will care about your chin when you''re rich!', 'N1', NULL),
(2262, 'You know how you get. When you can''t go outside, you dedicate yourself full force into something else. I mean, you made it to level 55, right? That''s some serious dedication. And it probably started because of one particularly dry spring.', 'Dedicate yourself to doing things in your home! Haha, who are we kidding, you already did that.

We love you, nerd. ♡', 'You decide to <kanji>dedicate</kanji> yourself to your <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう). You don''t want to be outside in the dry spring air, so you focus on home activities. You can clean the kitchen, build a shelf, or dedicate your time to your home internet system.', 'N1', NULL),
(2263, 'Imagine if winter was so brutal that there were only one hundred human foreheads left. You lost all of your friends and family. Feel that grief.', 'Grief for youths is so painful. No one should have to bury their forehead child.', 'Their <kanji>grief</kanji> intensifies because all of the foreheads they lost belonged to <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう). Dead youths everywhere. Now there''s no one left to repopulate the world.', 'N1', NULL),
(2264, 'Imagine carving a really simple, crude toe from a tree. It''s your first attempt, so I guess you should have known it wouldn''t be great.', 'Who carved this simple toe? ぼく!', 'You''re admiring your <kanji>simple</kanji> looking toe tree when a lumberjack runs up and asks who created it. <reading>ぼく</reading>, you say. As in "I" said it, in a manly dude, way. He doesn''t seem to believe you, so you say it a few more times. ぼく ぼく ぼく!', 'N1', NULL),
(2266, 'Thank you soul, for being frugal so that I can have so much now.', 'If you want your spouse or S.O. to think you''re more sexy, just be more frugal. They''ll definitely appreciate it and see you in a different light.', 'You know what being <kanji>frugal</kanji> is? <reading>Sexy</reading> (せき). You save money, you use less resources, that''s the sexiest thing I can think of.', 'N1', NULL),
(2267, 'Imagine being so excellent at leading, that you get double the dirt that everyone else gets.', 'Imagine your own car. Is it as excellent as you?', 'This <kanji>excellent</kanji> leader is going to use all this dirt to store her excellent <reading>ca</reading>rs (か)! Excellent people have to have cars in equal or greater than excellent-ness than themselves.', 'N1', NULL),
(2268, 'Look at your toe. It has no soul. It''s dark and shriveled, like a mummy''s toe. Grieve and watch your tears drip onto it.', 'Walk through the streets of とうきょう grieving, with your dead toe flopping onto the pavement.', 'You decide to <kanji>grieve</kanji> in <reading>とう</reading>きょう. Your toe always wanted to walk in とうきょう, and now you can at least fulfill its dying wish.', 'N1', NULL),
(2269, 'Imagine actually slicing the words you say in the air. The above section is what you want people to pay attention to, so you slice a big gap between the above and the below words.', 'That guy doesn''t care if the above is all he''s supposed to hear. That guy sucks.', 'As you focus on <kanji>the above</kanji> words you realize a <reading>guy</reading> (がい) is trying to push the rest of the things you''re saying back up with the rest of them. No! The above text is all that matters! Go away guy!', 'N1', NULL),
(2270, 'Imagine trying to proceed down the road by running on your toes. No other part of your foot can touch the ground!', 'Imagine being called a fool when you can''t proceed along the road because running on your toes ends up being super hard to do.', 'If you find that you can''t <kanji>proceed</kanji>, you''re a <reading>foo</reading>l (ふ). Everyone can run on their toes. Don''t you know that? No? Then you''re an even bigger fool, now get outta here!', 'N1', NULL),
(2271, 'If you think about it, bone marrow is really just a road for tiny blood scooters.', 'That''s how doctors can tell if there''s something wrong with your bone marrow. If they can''t hear the zuuwee of the scooters, you''re gonna die soon. I mean, maybe, I''m not a doctor.', 'If you put your ear really close to a bone with <kanji>marrow</kanji> still in it, you''ll hear the scooters going <reading>zuwee</reading> (ずい) as they move the blood around inside there.', 'N1', NULL),
(2272, 'Picture the leader very close to your side. He''s looking off into the distance, a distance he''ll never get to because all he can do is stand there with his messed up feet.', 'Picture the leader bowling nearby. You keep him close by, you never know when he''ll fall over and need your help.', 'You decide to bring the leader to a <kanji>nearby</kanji> <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう) alley. It''s really close and you can stay right by his side as he throws bowling balls and gets his anger out.', 'N1', NULL),
(2273, 'Just imagine how much thread you''ll accumulate if you can just grow it along with your rice in the rice paddy! So much thread!', 'Your attempts to accumulate more of something by cheating have brought ruin to everything else you were doing. Nice job, dummy.', 'Sadly, this is a terrible way to <kanji>accumulate</kanji> anything thread related and you ended up <reading>rui</reading>ning (るい) your rice paddy too. I guess if you want to accumulate thread this isn''t the way to do it.', 'N1', NULL),
(2274, 'Maybe the Death Star wouldn''t be sick if it wasn''t so mean to you, yelling at you for living in a yurt, when it was a yurt on the inside the whole time. Wait! I know the cure!', 'Hmm, but does this type of yurt healing technology exist?! You''ll have to find out to cure it!!', 'You realized that the only way to <kanji>cure</kanji> the Death Star is to turn it back into a <reading>yu</reading>rt (ゆ). Then it won''t be able to do any more harm, and its outside will reflect its pleasant self on the inside!', 'N1', NULL),
(2275, 'Imagine the building you just received. You want to protect it, so you get started on that enclosure. Imagine building your enclosure with your bare hands.', 'Your pet cock is the perfect addition to your new enclosure. Just look at it, pecking happily between the enclosure and that building you received.', 'The <kanji>enclosure</kanji> felt a  bit quiet and lonely, so you decided to get yourself a pet <reading>cock</reading> (かく).', 'N1', NULL),
(2276, 'Imagine swimming over to the flag that''s sticking out of the water and peeing there. Yeah, everyone knows what you''re doing over there, but it''s better than getting out and walking to the bathroom right? Just pee in the water. Just do it.', 'Imagine your embarrassment when you find out that you aren''t supposed to urinate in the water, not even by the pee flag, because a life guard yelled NYOOOOOO and pointed right at you while you did it.', 'Right as you start to <kanji>pee</kanji>, you hear the life guard start to yell, "<reading>NYOOOOOO</reading>! (にょう)" and he''s pointing at you! You were just peeing by the pee flag! Why is he mad?', 'N1', NULL),
(2277, 'The VIPs came into our home (the place between our roof and the ground) where they consumed all our shellfish. Only the best for guests, I guess.', 'We are treating these VIP guests extra special because they represent the whole Hindu religion. They get shellfish because of course we weren''t going to serve them beef!', 'These <kanji>VIP</kanji> <kanji>guest</kanji>s are part of a <reading>Hin</reading>du (ひん) envoy. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2278, 'Picture the sad tiger captive and the man laughing as he pokes at it. Tigers shouldn''t be captives, but when men find them they always seem to do this anyway.', 'Now picture this tiger captive in Rio. He''s stuck in a cage at home while the man yucks it up along Rio''s beaches, bragging to all the locals about his captive tiger.', 'The man takes his new tiger <kanji>captive</kanji> to <reading>Rio</reading> (りょ). Why Rio de Janeiro? Turns out you''re allowed to keep tigers captive down in Rio. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2279, 'If you died right now and left your soul behind, do you think you''d have regrets?', 'Picture Genghis Khan looking down at the state of his people, the Mongols, in the modern world. See the remorse in his soul''s face. They should be leaders of the modern world. Not reduced to a people struggling in the background.', 'The soul with the most <kanji>regret</kanji>s is Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん). Everyone knows to stay away from him in the spirit world because he''s so sad. Not because of his own actions, but because of the state of his people now.', 'N1', NULL),
(2280, 'Imagine increasing your power with a new weapon. What would it be?', 'Imagine walking down a line of weapons, each one increasing in power from the one before it. Bow, small gun, bigger gun, tank, Yankee''s bat. Yup, that sounds right.', 'You keep trading in weapons to get <kanji>increasing</kanji>ly stronger gear, when you realize that at the top of the list is... a <reading>Ya</reading>nkee''s (や) baseball bat? Huh. I would have gone for a tank or a bazooka. But I guess if this will increase your power level, you have to go for it.', 'N1', NULL),
(2281, 'Imagine trying to eat the rice you found in a top hat. It''s so coarse it feels like it''s scratching your teeth. There''s probably some dirt in here too. This wasn''t the best idea.', 'If you were trying to make this coarse rice taste like soap, you did it. But if you were trying to make it less coarse, well... sorry, but that''s not how anything works.', 'You take the <kanji>coarse</kanji> rice and try to wash it with <reading>so</reading>ap (そ).', 'N1', NULL),
(2282, 'Imagine this strange circulation of you and the pope up here on a cliff. It''s not even like you two are up here for a reason, you''re just loitering. But he doesn''t want to be hot so he makes you circulate with him.', '"This circulation is all June''s fault," you mutter, walking in a circle with the pope to stay cool.', 'This <kanji>circulation</kanji> wouldn''t have to even be happening if it wasn''t <reading>June</reading> (じゅん). The cliffs are so hot in June, and in any other month the two of you would have just sat down and had a chat. But in June you have to do this weird circulation dance.', 'N1', NULL),
(2283, 'Imagine looking over water in an ice cube tray. You aren''t sure if they''ll turn out alright, and you doubt whether ice is even a thing. As you think this, the water congeals and freezes into gross shapes.', 'Picture frozen, congealed gyoza. Do you want to eat that? I didn''t think so.', 'You always end up <kanji>congeal</kanji>ing your <reading>gyo</reading>za (ぎょう) this way. You want to <kanji>freeze</kanji> your leftover gyoza so you can enjoy it later, but you doubt it will turn out all right and it always congeals in a gross, half-frozen gyoza block. Yuck!', 'N1', NULL),
(2285, 'Picture the prosperous planet that has two suns. They get so much work done that no one is ever hungry or tired or without.', 'Picture the Shougun looking over the prosperous world, the two suns at his back.', 'The person on this planet with two suns who benefits from the prosperity the most is the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). Yes, this planet still has a shougun as their leader, but everything is going so well that they didn''t see a need to change. The shogun will be in charge of this prosperous world forever. No one ever thinks of revolt when everyone has everything they could ever need.', 'N1', NULL),
(2286, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Imagine getting a tan at dawn. Ahh. Relaxing and warm.', '<kanji>Dawn</kanji> is <reading>dawn</reading> (だん)! It''s also a great time to get a <reading>tan</reading> (たん). The sun is bright at dawn, but not so much that it will send skin cancer rays into you.', 'N1', NULL),
(2287, 'Picture the Death Star in your mind. As you picture it, its pleasant, smiling soul floats up and out of it. "Oh hello, dear," it says happily, "Didn''t see you there!"', 'Yurts are very pleasant structures. They''re round and soft and moveable, just like the Death Star''s soul.', 'The <kanji>pleasant</kanji> soul within the Death Star actually looks like a <reading>yu</reading>rt (ゆ), not a big angry, metal ball.', 'N1', NULL),
(2288, 'Imagine your fingers slowly getting closer and closer to the jet''s engine. It feels like it''s going to suck your whole body in. And you better watch out, because if it does you''ll be erased from the planet. And probably from time itself.', 'Now you''re so thankful to the mats. If they weren''t there you might have erased your whole body! Thank you mats! I''ll never walk on you without thanking you for your service again!', 'You''re almost done <kanji>eras</kanji>ing your fingers when the jet starts sucking all of you in. You would try to grab the closest thing to you, but your fingers don''t exist anymore, so you hold onto the <reading>mats</reading> (まつ) at your feet with your toes. The mats are super strong (they''re made for jets, after all) and you manage to save yourself!', 'N1', NULL),
(2289, 'Picture how silly the king looks with this cork on his head. He kind of looks like a fat, plugged up bottle of wine when he yells now.', 'Picture the centaurs drinking and giggling, putting the cork from their wine on their head to imitate the stupid king.', 'It turned out this <kanji>cork</kanji> hat was given to him by a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん). You look into it and it turned out it IS a really big cork that centaurs use on their big wine casks. But the king didn''t understand, and thought it was a hat. The centaurs are probably still laughing.', 'N1', NULL),
(2290, 'As you look at the drop of water, you note that you found the right thing. Written on it is the word “this."', 'You’re always collecting drops of water in the hills. It’s your hobby, in fact. But this one is the first one you’ve ever collected with “this” written on it. What a great addition to your drop collection.', 'You <reading>colle</reading>ct(これ) <kanji>this</kanji> very carefully. You’re going to add this to your drop collection.', 'N1', NULL),
(2292, 'Your scooter is charcoal powered, but it looks like you put too much in there and it drove off somewhere distant without you. If you look really carefully, you can just see it, a distant speck on the horizon.', 'You can still make out the distant shape of your scooter, now on a row boat, fading away and completely out of reach.', 'You chase the scooter to that <kanji>distant</kanji> place, but then you see it heading for a lake. Oh good, it should be stopped by the water. But wait! In the distance you see your scooter drive onto a <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat and... row itself through the water?! No!!', 'N1', NULL),
(2293, 'Picture the king holding this crystal aloft, giggling as the sun shines through it and sparkles.', 'Imagine going to England, to the giant acorn in the center of the flowers, and searching for the king''s crystal. If you find it, you can probably sell it for a fortune! Or just make a really sweet necklace.', 'The king thinks everyone wants his <kanji>crystal</kanji> so he hides it in the giant <reading>a</reading>corn (えい) in the center of the flowers in England. There''s a tiny hole in the bottom of the acorn, and that''s where this crystal stays hidden.', 'N1', NULL),
(2294, 'What? Do you think I''m gonna help you make this decision? No way, you need to dig deep and think about what you want to do.', 'What?! You got nachos either way. You should be happy, don''t ask me any other questions.', 'A. <kanji>What</kanji> is, stab the building on the left? You stab the building on the left with your sword and what comes out is... a plate of delicious <reading>na</reading>chos (な)!

B. <kanji>What</kanji> is, stab the building on the right? You stab the building on the right with your sword and what comes out is... a plate of delicious <reading>na</reading>chos (な)!', 'N1', NULL),
(2295, 'Imagine yourself in a completely white room. All you have is the white walls, your fingers, and your beats. When you stop all you''re left with is white noise. Yuck! Keep those beats coming!', 'Imagine hacking the wall apart as you try to keep this beat going. The music is all you have, man! You can''t lose the beat now!', 'This <kanji>beat</kanji> is sick, but as you tap you realize you''re <reading>hack</reading>ing (はく) up the wall! Slowly but surely, your fingers are hacking out little bits of the wall. And the beats don''t sound as good until you move to a new, clean white area.', 'N1', NULL),
(2296, 'You keep checking outside to see if they have stopped, but they are still drinking. They haven''t stopped yet.', 'You keep checking, but that youth is still drinking. Of course, you don''t know they are a youth, so it''s quite impressive an animal can drink this much.', 'The animal with horns that is <kanji>still</kanji> drinking your alcohol is no animal at all. It is actually one of the local <reading>you</reading>ths (ゆう) dressed up like a horned animal so they can drink as long as they want and you''ll be too scared to shoo them away.', 'N1', NULL),
(2297, 'Picture yourself as the manager of a restaurant or a science lab. You have to test all of your managerial methods at home before you can bring them to work. And if you were to lick that roof of yours... it would be spicy from all of your experiments.', 'Unfortunately, you couldn''t figure out how to make non-spicy cyborgs, but that''s okay because you''re going to be the best cyborg manager ever! No one will ever take them away from you again.', 'You''ve finally done it. You''re now the <kanji>manager</kanji> of a bunch of spicy new <reading>cy</reading>borgs (さい). You''ve been toiling away for over a year, but you''ve finally replaced the cyborg you lost, and now you can manage them properly without anyone getting in the way. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2298, 'Imagine sitting on this small stool, waaaaay up there above the roof. You can''t see anyone and you can''t talk to anyone. You feel this awful emptiness inside. That''s what it''s like to be lonely.', 'You''re so busy talking to Jack, you don''t hear the voices from below, "Aren''t you lonely up there, miss? Do you want us to get a jack to get you down? Hello? I don''t think she can hear us..."', 'You''re so <kanji>lonely</kanji>, you make up an imaginary friend: <reading>Jack</reading> (じゃく). Jack doesn''t get rid of the lonely feeling because he isn''t real, but he is a distraction at least.', 'N1', NULL),
(2299, 'Imagine slowly sewing your scooter to the signpost, passing the thread back and forth through the signpost''s metal holes and your scooter''s handles.', 'Hopefully, if your scooter is still here later you''ll be able to start sewing outside of your home more often from now on!', 'You usually only <kanji>sew</kanji> at <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう), so this is a big exception for you. You''re usually worried that if you sew in public people will judge your stitches, so this is a big step for you!', 'N1', NULL),
(2300, 'If you want to give someone something, use a catapult!', 'You give Godzilla old used cars, chunks of old buildings, and sometimes hamburgers. Godzilla is so happy you''re giving him food, he doesn''t destroy anymore!', '<kanji>Give</kanji> food to your new friend <reading>Go</reading>dzilla (ご) with the catapult! You and Godzilla understand each other now. He''s just hungry.', 'N1', NULL),
(2301, 'You didn''t expect to see water drop damage on this table so now your mood is mediocre too.', 'Imagine having a bone to pick with someone because they''re trying to sell you something mediocre and they just won''t listen. Now you have a bone to pick with the person who recommended this guy to you!', 'You weren''t expecting to see such a <kanji>mediocre</kanji> table so you go up to the guy who''s selling it and say, "Hey, I have a <reading>bone</reading> (ぼん) to pick with you!" But he doesn''t think there is a bone to pick at all, he doesn''t think the table is mediocre like you do.', 'N1', NULL),
(2302, 'Imagine being a small drop of water and seeing other drops of water come to help you by working together. You''d really respect them, right?', 'Imagine respecting someone so much that you''ll cheer ooh yankees over and over even though they''re kind of a crappy team. ', 'If you <kanji>respect</kanji> someone enough you''ll even take up their hobbies. The person you respect most loves the Yankees. You don''t really feel the same, but you respect that about them so you chant, "<reading>Ooh Ya</reading>nkees, <reading>Ooh Ya</reading>nkees!" (うやうや) with them during each game.', 'N1', NULL),
(2303, 'You could swear this thing was gold a long time ago. Now it''s a mix of different things.', 'Since it was in that sack for so long, it just ended up getting mixed with the other stuff in that sack. It was gold a long time ago, but now it''s mixed with everything else.', 'It''s <kanji>mixed</kanji> (and not pure anymore) because the <kanji>confused</kanji> person put it in that <reading>sack</reading> (さく) filled with various other things.', 'N1', NULL),
(2304, 'Picture the samurai taking out his weapon and spinning in a lightning fast circle. The forehead grains pop off and come raining into his open mouth.', 'These grains aren''t even cooked, they''re just hard and raw, so the Coke really helps wash them down.', 'Phew, all of this <kanji>grain</kanji> is making the samurai thirsty, so he reaches in his bag and pulls out an ice cold, refreshing <reading>Coke</reading> (こく).', 'N1', NULL),
(2305, 'Unfortunately, you''re realizing this because you thought a mausoleum would be a good hiding place from the winter cold. Spoilers, it''s not.', 'Imagine taking your frostbitten fingers and trying to get away from the mausoleum in this row boat. It looks like it was made of bones and it is not very seaworthy. But you row with all your might, because you don''t want to end up a permanent fixture in this tomb!', 'You need to escape this <kanji>mausoleum</kanji> now but the only way out is on a <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat over a creepy frozen pond. You''ve heard of these mausoleum row boats before, and you''re pretty sure they''re cursed.', 'N1', NULL),
(2306, 'Imagine walking around in the arctic when you stumble upon an evil group of penguins. They''d be cute if they weren''t holding papers with "Kill All Humans" on them and tiny guns in their penguin hands.', 'Only someone or, in this case, something truly evil would kill someone and hide their remains under some hay. That''s really disrespectful and, well, evil.', 'The <kanji>evil</kanji> penguins can''t let you tell the other human''s what you''ve seen, so they murder you and hide your body in a <reading>hay</reading>stack (へい). Your family will never know what happened to you. The penguins will get away with their evil plans.', 'N1', NULL),
(2307, 'Boats don''t need to be a bright white color. But, ships do! They''re dangerous and big, so they paint them nice and white.', 'You can also imagine hacking the ship''s computer. "Look at me, look at me. You are the hacker now."', 'A white-painted <kanji>ship</kanji> is a kind of <reading>hack</reading> (はく). It makes the ship more visible without costing much.', 'N1', NULL),
(2308, 'Picture a destitute person wandering in the streets with a hole in their body.', 'Picture a destitute person, sitting on the street, holding a cucumber. Putting the cucumber in their hole. Crying.', 'You see this <kanji>destitute</kanji> person and decide to give them a <reading>cu</reading>cumber (きゅう). They don''t even remember that they''re supposed to eat those, so they shove it in the hole you made with your bow.', 'N1', NULL),
(2309, 'Imagine guarding yourself from all the bad things in the world. No more politics, religion, internet trolls debating the two, it''s all guarded from your precious soul. Ahhh, it''s so joyous to be unburdened.', 'Picture joy etched on your own face. No one can get you down.', 'The <kanji>delight</kanji> you feel is so great that it becomes permanently <reading>etch</reading>ed (えつ) in your soul and even your face.', 'N1', NULL),
(2310, 'Imagine if your specialty was dropping people by binding them with thread. You''d be such a weird person.', 'Imagine how you would bind someone so that when they drop they land on their back instead of somewhere more painful. How would you do that?', 'If you''re going to be a weirdo who likes to <kanji>bind</kanji> people, at least make sure that they land on their <reading>back</reading>s (ばく) when they DO drop. Landing on your head or your boobs really hurts. So please at least try to make them land on their backs!', 'N1', NULL),
(2311, 'It''s really important to remember how many things are under your control when you operate a vehicle. It isn''t just the people in your car either, it''s everyone and everything in your way too.', 'If you don''t want to think about hitting adorable cats with your car, you better maintain control. You''re responsible!', 'You have to maintain <kanji>control</kanji> of your car or you''ll hit a bunch of <reading>cats</reading> (かつ). The roads are full of cats at all times. And if you lose control of your car for just a second, you''ll hit at least two or three cats.', 'N1', NULL),
(2312, 'So this mysterious bow''s string is for plucking a one-note tune or launching an attack. Either way, your enemies will be moved to tears.', 'Genji is a lover, not a fighter. Picture Genji, the most handsome and romantic man ever, serenading you with his one-note G-string bow. Swoon! Just hope he doesn''t sink an arrow into you too.', 'Turns out this bow strung with a mysterious <kanji>chord</kanji> belongs to <reading>Gen</reading>ji (げん). Of course! Only someone as elegant and dramatic as Genji would own a bow that plays music when you pluck its string.', 'N1', NULL),
(2313, 'Picture a poor little mole suffocating in your hand after you took it out of its hole. Put it back right away! Don''t kill that little mole!', 'Unfortunately, you can''t use cheats on life, and the mole suddenly stiffens and suffocates to death anyway. You should feel bad about what you''ve done.', 'As the mole <kanji>suffocate</kanji>s it <reading>cheats</reading> (ちつ) and shoves its head into your closed hand. You realize what it''s doing and squeeze your hand tighter so it''s more like a hole. You can feel the mole breathing again now.', 'N1', NULL),
(2314, 'More than one tsunami will definitely result in a flood. Imagine the floody aftermath of more than one tsunami, arriving together.', '"This flood was caused by you! It is because you did not believe in the holy Crabigator! It is because you didn''t do your kanji reviews. You have sinned, sinned, sinned!" こういち says to the scared, cold, now homeless people.', 'After the terrible <kanji>flood</kanji>, Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち comes to survey the destruction and offer hope to his people. But, instead he just tells the people that this flood happened because they didn''t believe in the Crabigator''s words, and because they weren''t doing their kanji reviews.', 'N1', NULL),
(2315, 'Pretend you''re in a class in the mid-20th century, watching a video on bomb explosion safety. In addition to putting your fingers in your ears, you have to do a bunch of other stuff. You can''t just stop after the first step!', 'In addition to ears, you have a lot of sets on your body to worry about. Sets of eyes, nostrils, legs, you get the picture.', '<kanji>In addition</kanji> to all the steps you have to take to protect yourself, you can''t forget that ears come in <reading>sets</reading> (せつ). You can''t just stick one finger in one ear and have that be the end. You have a set of ears to worry about!', 'N1', NULL),
(2316, 'Haven''t you ever been so bored that you just wrapped yourself up into a boredom ball?', 'Ehhhhhhgggg guys I''m stuck at home and I''m soooo bored. Heeelpp!', 'So now you''re <kanji>bored</kanji> and stuck at <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう). You aren''t hungry. There''s nothing on TV. And you don''t want to have to leave your home to do something, even though that would probably solve your boredom problem.', 'N1', NULL),
(2317, 'Picture yourself as the perfect gentleman. You may not have a fancy top hat and tie, but you''re humble about what you wear and you''re kind to everyone.', 'But you''re a gentleman, so you don''t kick their shins back, you smile and hold in the pain. No matter how bad your shins hurt, you never fight back!', 'Unfortunately, as a <kanji>gentleman</kanji> who dresses humbly, you''re always getting kicked in the <reading>shin</reading>s (しん) by rude kids (and occasionally rude adults).', 'N1', NULL),
(2318, 'This is a totally commonplace thing for almost every class or job you''ll ever have. They sit you down under the canopy, put a brush in your hand, and say, "Here, complete this task. I''ll be over there."', 'As you eat the yogurt you think about how commonplace yogurt is too. It''s just a normal, plain, boring old food, found in practically everyone''s fridge.', 'This is so <kanji>commonplace</kanji> and familiar to you that you finish your task early and eat some <reading>yo</reading>gurt (よう) while you wait for the guy to come back.', 'N1', NULL),
(2319, 'Imagine how peaceful staring at the blue ocean, the blue sky, or the blue tumblr makes you.', 'YAASS! Peaceful blue radicals standing in a row. What''s that? YAASS you do want more radicals added to the site. Well okay then, if it will keep you at peace, sure!', 'Now if someone asks you if the color blue makes you <kanji>peaceful</kanji> you should say "<reading>YAASS</reading> (やす)!" And you''ll have us to thank for that! All those peaceful blue radicals. They''re the easiest thing on the site, and if someone asks you if radicals make you feel peaceful say "YAASS" again!', 'N1', NULL),
(2320, 'Imagine hearing someone speak and it''s so powerful and loud that it sounds like they have a drum booming out of their mouth. You''ll love them! You''ll hold them in the highest esteem!', 'If you hold someone in high esteem you give me your- give them your car. Gimme!', 'You hold them in such high <kanji>esteem</kanji> that you give them your <reading>ca</reading>r (か). I ... I said you give them your car! Give them your car. Hey, hey!', 'N1', NULL),
(2321, 'Imagine what it would be like to squeeze your fingers into a keyhole. Maybe try squeezing one finger really tight first. Did that hurt? Well it''s going to be a lot worse in that key hole.', 'You have the ability to squeeze your fingers into locks to open them, so of course you''re stealing. And you squeeze those stolen goodies into your sack so you can take them with you.', 'Once you''ve <kanji>squeeze</kanji>d your finger in the key hole to unlock this door you squeeze all the money and jewels and treasures you can fit into your <reading>sack</reading> (さく). Your stealing sack.', 'N1', NULL),
(2322, 'Imagine any tree in the world with different kinds of butterflies fluttering around. Then go to another tree in the world that looks completely different. Once again, butterflies! They really are fluttering around every tree in the world.', 'She grabs another butterfly and starts pulling off wings again, though this time you can see her evil face looking at you while she does it. "I hate butterflies," she says. "They are always dying in my tea."', 'As you look at a <kanji>butterfly</kanji>, fluttering around a tree, a hand reaches out from the tree and grabs it, then starts plucking the wings off. An evil laugh comes from inside the tree, and you know it''s Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう). ', 'N1', NULL),
(2323, 'Imagine seeing a stone and a cross drop from the sky onto each other to form a tombstone right in front of your eyes!', 'Can you feel the heat radiating off this space tombstone? Maybe there''s a space skeleton sizzling under there too...', 'Now you''re curious about what''s under there but when you try to touch the <kanji>tombstone</kanji> it''s radiating with intense <reading>he</reading>at (ひ)! It must have come from outer space and this heat is from when it entered our atmosphere!', 'N1', NULL),
(2324, 'Picture a military officer doing all those things.', 'Picture the military officer saluting as an eagle flies overhead. Majestic.', 'That <kanji>military officer</kanji>''s favorite bird is an <reading>ea</reading>gle (い)! That should be obvious because the eagle is the bird of America! And that''s the country he fights for!', 'N1', NULL),
(2326, 'Imagine looking down your street and seeing caged axes above all of the artisans homes and shops. You want a new chair? You want some pants mended? Go to one of those signs and you''ll find the right artisan.', 'Picture the Shougun marching angrily down the street. His shoe suddenly snaps in half. With just one glance he''ll be able to find the shoe artisan and make them fix his shoe for free.', '<kanji>Artisan</kanji>s have to be marked this way because the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう) says so. The Shougun doesn''t want to have to put any effort into finding people to make things for him, so he created this new system.', 'N1', NULL),
(2327, 'The scooter satellite is always far off because it does what scooters do best. It just keeps scooting farther and farther into the distance.', 'You''re seeing the scooter satellite because it''s what your heart desires, so your mind is playing tricks on you. But nope, no chance of seeing it from that distance — you''re just hallucinating.', 'You think you can spot the scooter satellite in the <kanji>far off</kanji> distance, but really it''s just a <reading>hallu</reading>cination (はる). It''s way too small and far away for you to see it.', 'N1', NULL),
(2328, 'Imagine shooting an arrow at the mouth of the sun and then waiting for the wisdom of the world to hit you. Did it work? Will you know when it works? Probably...', 'Picture yourself thanking the sun for teaching you that all wisdom comes from cheese. Now take a big bite!', 'You''re standing around waiting for the <kanji>wisdom</kanji> to hit you when something else does. In the head. Ouch! You look down and it''s a wheel of <reading>chee</reading>se (ち)! At first you''re angry, until you realize that this must be a sign. Cheese IS wisdom. And the key to wisdom is eating a lot of cheese!', 'N1', NULL),
(2330, 'Some people make really bad choices when they''re drunk, and in this case, when a shellfish gets drunk and sees something it wants it''ll just rob people for it.', 'Picture a robber shellfish in a Bosozoku bike gang. Look at him the wrong way and he and his gang will steal all your stuff next time!', 'Once the shellfish has a taste for being a <kanji>robber</kanji>, he''ll join a Boso<reading>zoku</reading> (ぞく) bike gang so he can keep stealing more and more crosses. He''ll drink more, steal more, and get to ride cool motorcycles.', 'N1', NULL),
(2331, 'A branch is just like a drumstick, so of course you''re going to drum them together.', 'Kids can''t get enough of it. Anything that makes a loud drumming noise or anything they can beat together is fun for them.', 'Who loves to <kanji>drum</kanji> things together and make a huge racket? <reading>子</reading>s (こ), of course.', 'N1', NULL),
(2332, 'Imagine getting your coat rack to make rotations in a circle just by shooting it with a gun!', 'Picture that centaurs angry face. Convince yourself it was the rotation it didn''t like.', 'As you watch your coat rack''s <kanji>rotation</kanji>s a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん) barges in and takes the gun from you, stops the rotating coat rack and leaves. How rude! I guess centaurs don''t like when things rotate.', 'N1', NULL),
(2333, 'Imagine the moon, then imagine it cross-fading into your big, round tummy. Then, cut it open because you think the moon is easy to cut. And finally, see your intestines as clearly as you can.', 'This scene was in all the omen radicals you''ve been seeing about her. If only you could have stopped her, then none of this would have happened, right? Perhaps it was all destiny, you think, as your intestines fall from your stomach and Mrs. Chou cackles over your weakening body.', 'All over the world, people are cutting their bellies open, because they think its an easy moon (versus a normal moon, or hard moon, presumably). This is because they have fallen victim to a spell by Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう).', 'N1', NULL),
(2334, 'Imagine scooping a melted tree into a tank. It''s a very strange sensation.', '"Um, okay?" you reply to the soul, and walk away from the tank. "W-wait! Tanks can''t move? Come back, let me haunt you with my tree soul!"

It''s a good thing there weren''t wheels on that tank!', 'You''re staring at the big <kanji>tank</kanji> when an eerie sound comes out, "Oooh I am the <reading>sou</reading>l (そう) of the tree! You''ve trapped me in this tank and now I''m going to haunt it!" You don''t want to be haunted by a tank with a soul stuck in it.', 'N1', NULL),
(2335, 'Imagine felling trees with a bunch of other guys, yelling things like, "This is what you get for stepping on my turf!" It''s a little silly.', 'This is good news for the trees, but your drunken leader isn''t going to be happy.', 'You and your gang head out to <kanji>fell</kanji> the trees, only to remember that all you have are <reading>bats</reading> (ばつ). Good luck trying to take down trees with nothing but bats!', 'N1', NULL),
(2337, 'Try to imagine roughly how big a 3.3-square-meter area is. Now imagine yourself lying down in that space. You feel pretty peaceful, right?', 'Even better, you and your friend can squeeze your two bodies into the 壺 after setting it in the 3.3-square-meter area. Now you''re really using the space efficiently!', 'An area of <kanji>3.3 square meters</kanji> also happens to be just the right size for <reading>two bo</reading>dies (つぼ)! Not in, like, a creepy way, mind you — two bodies in the sense of you and a friend snuggling up and finding peace in this <kanji>two-mat area</kanji> together. 

Alternatively, you can remember this word with 壺, which has the same reading. Just imagine putting a big, fancy 壺 in your 3.3 square meter area. It''s the perfect way to utilize the space.', 'N1', NULL),
(2338, 'Turn your thread a sweet navy color and people will be reminded of yummy blue raspberry. MmMmm.', 'Imagine a condor stealing your sweet navy thread right after you dyed it, flying off. Lame!', 'Now that your threads are <kanji>navy</kanji> colored you need to be careful that <reading>con</reading>dors (こん) don''t try to steal it for their nests. Condors love <kanji>dark blue</kanji> things and thread is perfect for a condor nest.', 'N1', NULL),
(2339, 'The reason you''d use a net made of thread is because it''s hard to see. But, it''s only that way when you spread out the net. It''s important to spread out and arrange your net otherwise the turkey will see it.', '"Let me out of this net!" says Ra. "You arranged and spread it out just right so I couldn''t see it!"', 'You <kanji>spread out</kanji> your net and <kanji>arrange</kanji> it just so. You expect it to catch a turkey, but instead you catch the ancient Egyptian god <reading>Ra</reading> (ら).', 'N1', NULL),
(2340, 'Imagine walking up the mountain in your wedding clothes so you can get married without anyone trying to stop you. Then, because you''re still worried, you go into a ravine where you are read your marriage vows.', 'This ravine runs right through the middle of きょうと, though most people don''t notice it. That makes it the best place for a secret wedding to a horned freak-husband.', 'The <kanji>ravine</kanji> is still in <reading>きょう</reading>と though, because if you went outside the city your marriage wouldn''t be legal. So you found a secluded, local ravine.', 'N1', NULL),
(2341, 'The leader shows you the number, it''s all of your salary for spring. "This way, if things dry up worse, you''ll be okay for a while," he says. This is awfully nice, but very suspicious at the same time.', 'You''re sitting at home, and there is a knock at the door. A man in a suit, hat, and dark sunglasses hands you a briefcase full of money. "Here''s your spring salary. Enjoy the dry weather."

"T-thanks?"

Welp, have fun at home with your stacks of early salary cash.', 'Your <kanji>salary</kanji> is sent straight to your <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう) instead of the bank. Okay, something is definitely fishy here.', 'N1', NULL),
(2342, 'Imagine a nurse brewing alcohol with the same attention they would nurse a human patient. But this brew is much more important than any person''s health.', 'Imagine what it must be like for Joe, out working on the fields all day long, working up a sweat. I’d say he’s earned a cold alcoholic brew after such a day. Cut the man some slack.', 'A <kanji>brew</kanji> of alcohol is what <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう) loves most after a long day of working the fields.', 'N1', NULL),
(2343, 'Imagine looking at the bow and stick as you slide them over to your deceased friend''s family. This won''t do enough, but it is a little condolence at least.', 'Imagine her saying, "My condolences," as Mrs. Chou does him in.', 'More than a <kanji>condolence</kanji>, you want to avenge your friend''s death. He was killed by Mrs. <reading>Chou</reading> (ちょう), and she broke his stick and bow, which is why you got the family a new set.', 'N1', NULL),
(2344, 'Imagine saying A B C as you look down someones face at their eyes, nose and mouth. Their nose is B!', 'Imagine running out of cereal so you have to eat your latter option of breakfast oats. They aren''t awful but they aren''t your A breakfast choice, that''s for sure.', 'If you don''t have any cereal you have to eat your <kanji>B</kanji> breakfast option. <reading>Oats</reading> (おつ). You don''t hate oats, but they''re definitely not your first choice.', 'N1', NULL),
(2345, 'Scooters and libraries are universally loved by all! ', 'The next time you scoot to the library look around. There are little hen scooters and hens there with their chicks. They''re reading them books about grains and how to hatch eggs. It''s a universally adorable thing to see.', 'Riding a scooter to the library is so <kanji>universal</kanji>, even <reading>hen</reading>s (へん) do it. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2346, 'There was a lot of crime right here in this rice paddy, so you just built a big prison right there to lower the crime rate so this region can experience equilibrium once again.', 'You might remember from the kanji 耕 that there were こういち clones working in these rice paddy fields. Well, they were the ones disturbing the equilibrium. At least that''s what Emperor こういち said. So by throwing them in a prison built right here, the area could once again achieve equilibrium.  ', 'To restore <kanji>equilibrium</kanji>, you throw all the <reading>こう</reading>いち clones into the prison.', 'N1', NULL),
(2348, 'Imagine the guard doing his inspection in front of the gate all day long. He should have a nice report for you once he''s done.', 'If you hire a guard to stand at your gate and conduct an inspection of your neighborhood, make sure you tell him not to use an etch a sketch!', 'His <kanji>inspection</kanji> is complete, but it looks like he''s used an <reading>etch</reading> (えつ) a sketch to record all the results. That''s not really helpful. If you shake it all the inspection results will disappear!', 'N1', NULL),
(2349, 'Imagine trying to reflect some precious sunlight into your prison room with a spoon. Now imagine a mouth appearing in the way of the sunlight and it scolds you.', 'WHAT KIND OF WORLD IS THIS THAT YOU''RE IN A PRISON WHERE CATS SCOLD YOU?!', 'One day you finally get fed up. You need to know who the mouth of the person who''s <kanji>scold</kanji>ing you is. You stick your spoon out into the sun and the mouth appears, scolding you right on time. You reach out and grab them. But in your hand is a pile of <reading>cats</reading> (かつ).', 'N1', NULL),
(2350, 'Imagine how much daring it would take to go outside in winter knowing you could be freezing your precious elf ears off.', 'Do you think you have the same amount of daring as an elf going out in winter and Genghis Khan? Tell the truth.', 'The amount of <kanji>daring</kanji> you''d need is about equal to the amount Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん) had. And he was really daring!', 'N1', NULL),
(2351, 'Imagine seeing the moon with a membrane sitting stuffed in a greenhouse. It looks like something out of the Alien movies and you''re starting to think putting it in there wasn''t a good idea.', 'Can you imagine if the moon had a membrane and it smelled like a big mac? How does that even happen? You should probably go ask that food chain what they use for their ingredients.', 'You decide to open the greenhouse to check this moon <kanji>membrane</kanji> out but when you open the door the smell of big <reading>mac</reading>s (まく) hits you in the face. Eww. Why does this membrane smell like a big mac... or does that mean big macs smell like a festering moon?', 'N1', NULL),
(2352, 'Imagine what it would be like to have one of your eyes die. You''ll be blind in that eye forever!', 'Imagine a blind person mowing over everyone in their car as they drive on the sidewalk. Eeek. At least they don''t have to see this mess.', 'When you''re <kanji>blind</kanji> you probably shouldn''t drive anymore because if you do you''ll <reading>mow</reading> (もう) everyone over. Actually, don''t mow your lawn either. Blind people probably shouldn''t be driving anything.', 'N1', NULL),
(2353, 'The next time you''re cursing your uterus for making you bleed, curse the moon instead! It''s the moon''s fault you''re in pain.', 'Picture an invisible tie tieing your uterus to the moon. Damn you moon, stop pulling, it hurts!', 'It kind of sucks, but there is no way to sever this <kanji>uterus</kanji> - moon <reading>tie</reading> (たい). But if you find a way please tell me, I can''t take this anymore.', 'N1', NULL),
(2354, 'Because alcohol is so good and addicting, people will work extra hard to ferment it properly. This makes it a good teacher to learn about fermentation. People are motivated to figure it out!', 'You are fermenting thousands of bottles of alcohol for Emperor こういち''s big party.', 'Using <kanji>fermentation</kanji>, you are indeed making some alcohol. It will be drunk at a party put on by Emperor <reading>こう</reading>いち.', 'N1', NULL),
(2355, 'Picture a street where nothing is maintained. The buildings crumble in the dirt, but the people have lost the will to care. It''s not just physical, it''s spiritual degradation too…', 'Your dad isn''t just part of the degradation — he''s the ringleader. He''s sitting in a broken lawn chair, surrounded by empty beer cans, takeout containers, and piles of dirt. He waves his hand, and more junk food wrappers fall around him, almost like he''s commanding this lazy, degenerate lifestyle.', 'And who''s leading this charge into <kanji>degradation</kanji>? Your <reading>da</reading>d (だ), of course.', 'N1', NULL),
(2356, 'If you manage to intercept them before they can go in there to boil things, you''ll be able to pick up all the clothes and comic books you left on the floor. You really don''t want anyone to see those.', 'Phew, you intercepted the shaman in time (though she doesn''t look very happy about being scooted into) and now you can clean up your yurt before they go in.', 'You manage to <kanji>intercept</kanji> the <reading>sha</reading>man (しゃ) who was just about to open the door to your yurt, and instead you crash right into him.', 'N1', NULL),
(2358, 'Study your radicals and you''ll read good.', 'Tote bags are totes not convex though. Don''t mix them up.', 'Is this <kanji>convex</kanji>? It''s <reading>totes</reading> (とつ) convex! ', 'N1', NULL),
(2359, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Watch your mouth around the 王. Have you ever seen a concave person? Yeah that''s because they didn''t live through it. 王s aren''t very nice, you know.', 'If you call someone <kanji>concave</kanji> the <reading>王</reading> (おう) will find out and he''ll have his guards turn you concave.', 'N1', NULL),
(2360, 'Picture a bunch of maple trees being blown by the wind. They don''t seem upset, they actually seem really happy. ', 'Imagine your kite in the maple tree.', 'When the wind goes through the <kanji>maple</kanji> tree you know it''s <reading>kite day</reading> (かえで)!', 'N1', NULL),
(2361, 'Imagine having a drunkard spit a dirty question mark into your hand. You want to say, "Yes, I''m ??? too."', 'Picture yourself throwing the question mark at the drunkard who only cares about the Yankees. What a waste of time.', 'The drunkard takes the <kanji>question mark</kanji> back and presses it to his head. He''s wearing a <reading>Ya</reading>nkee''s (や) cap. Oh come on. "Do you just want to know the Yankee''s score in the game?" The drunkard nods.', 'N1', NULL),
(2362, 'Imagine walking into a warehouse and seeing hundreds of pale flowers growing in it. It''s subtle, but beautiful.', 'Picture a bunch of pale, soulless flowers. You pick one up and it falls apart in your hands.', 'When you grow <kanji>pale</kanji> flowers like this, they don''t grow a <reading>soul</reading> (そう). Soul adds color to plants and flowers. That''s probably why ghosts and dead bodies are pale too.', 'N1', NULL),
(2364, 'Picture your flowers sprouting out of the ground in the bright spring sun.', 'Take a walk around your home right now. Can you find the best place to sprout flowers? Remember, it has to be nice and bright! Not dark and damp. Don''t put them in your closet.', 'You want to grow these <kanji>sprout</kanji>s in your <reading>ho</reading>me (ほう), not outside. So you need to find the windows in your house that are the brightest.', 'N1', NULL),
(9397, 'Silkworms and their silky secretions are actually really core to heaven''s entire aesthetic.', 'Imagine the anticipation as you wait in line to get your must-買い coat. You''re so grateful to the silkworms — thanks to them, you''ll finally be able to hang out with the cool angels!', 'Lately, the <kanji>silkworm</kanji>s have been making "must-<reading>買い coa</reading>ts (かいこ)" that all the angels are going crazy over. Regular angel robes? Those are sooo last year. If you''re not rocking one of these silkworm-crafted must-買い coats, then you simply can''t be part of heaven''s in-crowd.', 'N1', NULL),
(2363, 'Watch the king''s swords digging in your private rice paddy and digging up huge chunks of lapis lazuli. This isn''t fair, that lapis should belong to you.', 'Imagine reading through a giant text called "The Law" and looking for this loophole the king''s men claim to have about taking your lapis lazuli. You can''t find it... but maybe if you search just a bit longer...', 'You run up and ask how this <kanji>lapis lazuli</kanji> thievery isn''t illegal and one of the swordsmen just shouts, "<reading>Loo</reading>phole (る)!" What? What loophole? What law says that the king gets to steal your stuff?', 'N1', NULL),
(2365, 'If you haven''t smelled sulfur before, it smells a bit like rotten eggs. Something that definitely belongs in the trash.', 'Ryu looks down at you, holding the sulfurous rock in one hand. Then he uppercuts you with it! Shoryuken!', 'You throw the <kanji>sulfur</kanji> rock as hard as you can, but it hits someone in the back of the head. Oh no, it''s <reading>Ryu</reading> (りゅう) from Street Fighter. You try to apologize and explain that you just didn''t want this stinky trash rock, I mean, sulfur rock near you. You didn''t know he was there.', 'N1', NULL),
(2366, 'Imagine spilling red wine or red paint down your shirt. Quickly pardon yourself and get changed now!', 'Imagine not pardoning yourself fast enough and having the winter shaman spirit away whatever you got red on. That''s embarrassing.', 'If you don''t <kanji>pardon</kanji> yourself fast enough the winter <reading>sha</reading>man (しゃ) will burst in and cast a spell on you, taking away all the clothes that have red on them! Now you have to pardon yourself for being naked!', 'N1', NULL),
(2367, 'Imagine seeing seven swords just lying abandoned in a hole. Feel the urge to steal them all. Do it, do it!', 'Imagine your delight as you look up the different sword sets online and realize you happened to steal the best one!', 'You <kanji>steal</kanji> the swords and realize they aren''t just seven random swords, they''re from the famous sword <reading>sets</reading> (せつ) they sell online. This particular set is from the Seven Samurai movie. Yes! What a great steal!', 'N1', NULL),
(2368, 'Imagine all of the other people staring and pointing, the kids making fun of you, no one seeing you as equal. And just... sigh. Deep down, you want to get rid of your fangs, but you can''t do it.', 'Now you''re staring in the mirror at yourself. You sigh loudly and take out the pliers. You''ll be a guy like everyone else. S-soon. As soon as you can muster up enough strength to stop sighing and rip these fangs out.', 'You <kanji>sigh</kanji> again and say, "I wish I was just a normal <reading>guy</reading> (がい)." I swear the rest of me is a guy like the rest of you. *sigh*', 'N1', NULL),
(2369, 'Imagine rubbing your husband''s back with your fingers to aid him. You want him to feel better. You want him to get that comforting feeling that this kind of touching gives.', 'That''s true love, my friend.', 'Unfortunately, your husband wouldn''t need your <kanji>aid</kanji> if he wasn''t such a <reading>foo</reading>l (ふ). He walks into doors, he falls down the stairs, he touches the plate when the waiter says it''s hot. And every single time your fool husband hurts himself, you aid him.', 'N1', NULL),
(2370, 'If you were the tiger how would you play with the drunkard? ', 'What would you rather watch? A guillotine at work or a tiger playing like this?', 'If you aren''t the tiger, watching it <kanji>play</kanji> is a little like watching someone''s head get cut off with a <reading>gui</reading>llotine (ぎ). In fact, you don''t want to watch it play at all anymore.', 'N1', NULL),
(2372, 'Picture a net prison full of insects being dragged by a tsunami getting muddier and muddier. Poor buggles!', 'Picture a bunch of muddy ducks munching on some muddy trapped insects, in some muddy leftover tsunami water.', 'Unfortunately for the insects, these <kanji>muddy</kanji> conditions have created the perfect habitat for <reading>duck</reading>s (だく). And muddy insects already imprisoned in a muddy net is a duck''s favorite treat!', 'N1', NULL),
(2373, 'Imagine trying to run down a street with twenty big crosses lining it. It''s so scary that you keep running faster and faster as you go.', 'Imagine distracting yourself from the scary crosses by honing your running skills while you do it. ', 'You figure since you''re already on the <kanji>run</kanji> that you might as well <reading>hone</reading> (ほん) your running skills while you make your way down this scary, cross lined street. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2374, 'Look at the triceratops. Now look at the moon. See the resemblance? Uncanny, don''t you think?', 'Imagine being the Shougun and seeing the resemblance between a triceratops and the moon for the first time. You''d put that on all your stuff too, if you were him.', 'The first person to see this <kanji>resemblance</kanji> was the <reading>Shou</reading>gun (しょう). That''s why he decided to put a triceratops head with the moon behind it on his mon (family crest). Now he''s known as the full moon dinosaur shogun, and he''s very famous.', 'N1', NULL),
(2375, 'Trees can''t live without sunlight, and it''s too damp underground in a prison. The water gets all moldy and the tree will rot and die.', 'This poor cucumber tree, left in this ground prison to rot. There''s nothing worse than eating a rotten cucumber.', 'You get close to the <kanji>rot</kanji>ting tree and see some rotten <reading>cu</reading>cumbers (きゅう) hanging from its branches. No!', 'N1', NULL),
(2376, 'He''s just sitting there, he can''t even pick up his weapon or raise his head anymore.', '"Co co co co cooooock!" the cock screams, shocking the samurai back into his lifeless shell. You did it!', 'The only way to bring a <kanji>husk</kanji> of a samurai back to life is to have a <reading>cock</reading> (かく) crow right in his ear.', 'N1', NULL),
(2377, 'Receive this nice, easy kanji with thanks!', 'Imagine being in きょうと and receiving something wonderful from a passerby in the street. What did you receive? How does it feel to receive something so great?', 'The best place to <kanji>receive</kanji> anything is in <reading>きょう</reading>と. The city is famous for the quality of everything they make, so if you’re in きょうと, keep your fingers crossed that you’ll receive something.', 'N1', NULL),
(2378, 'Imagine being the person who has to check whether each fiefdom has enough flowers and tsunamis per year to keep their status.', 'Picture Han Solo flying around Japan checking to make sure all the fiefdoms are up to code.', 'The most famous <kanji>fiefdom</kanji> checker is <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo. He takes his Millennium Falcon and swoops around checking for flowers and signs of past tsunamis and then he''ll either say you can or cannot be your own fiefdom. Thanks Han Solo!', 'N1', NULL),
(2379, 'Imagine the sweet tree woman being a mediator for you and your arch rival. What she says fixes everything between you two!', 'Imagine her refusing to let you pay her for her mediator services and just saying "bye" over and over until you leave. Weird.', 'You go to pay the woman for being your <kanji>mediator</kanji> for you two but she just says, "<reading>Bye</reading> ばい)." You try to hand her some money and she stares into your eyes. "Bye." Oh... okay, bye then.', 'N1', NULL),
(2380, 'Picture the sad, stepped on chicken. It''s angrily clucking and chasing your husband around your yard for what he did.', 'This cake was for your husband, but now he gets nothing. The chicken gets to eat all of the cake in front of him instead.', 'The only way to stop the <kanji>chicken</kanji> from putting on your husband''s cleats and stepping on him to death in revenge is to feed it the <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい) you were making.', 'N1', NULL),
(2381, 'Imagine trying to request a flag from a scary mantis that has it sitting in it''s mouth. Will it give it to you? Will it bite you? You''ll have to wait and see.', 'Imagine your shock at finding out the mantis not only understood your request, but agreed! This is great but also makes you wonder what other creatures can understand you...', 'After you <kanji>request</kanji> the mantis give you its flag it <reading>shock</reading>s (しょく) you by actually spitting it out of its mouth at your feet. You didn''t think your request would work, or even if the mantis understood human speech, but it shocked you by doing exactly what you asked!', 'N1', NULL),
(2382, 'You have a scooter that you drive every single day, but if you notice a fault, you need to send it in to get it fixed. That''s when you break out your alternate scooter.', 'Imagine riding on your alternate 鉄 scooter. Feel the hard iron under your bum and the loud sounds it makes as you scoot down the street. I hope your normal scooter is fixed soon.', 'The <kanji>alternate</kanji> scooter is made entirely of <reading>鉄</reading> (てつ). It''s hard and cold and not very comfortable when you sit on it. That''s why you chose to keep it as an alternate and not as your everyday scooter.', 'N1', NULL),
(2383, 'Tree turkeys are absolutely huge, and this exceptionally large one has been sitting right on top of your spine for what feels like ages. Imagine the pain of a huge turkey pressing down on your spine for weeks, perhaps months. How are you still holding on?!', 'You desperately try to escape from the weight of the tree turkey still sitting on top of your spine, but to no avail. The only thing you can see is dozens of tree turkeys approaching to plant themselves firmly on top of your spine, screaming "TWEET, TWEET".', 'Just as your <kanji>spine</kanji> seems to be getting used to the pressure, the tree turkey unleashes a cry: "<reading>TWEE</reading>T (つい)!" To your horror, a flock of tree turkeys emerge from the trees.', 'N1', NULL),
(2385, 'I know it''s been a long time, but try not to mix this one up with 	部, if you can. Remember, the clown building is in another part of town and the clowns aren''t supposed to leave. But the building clown is okay to accompany you through the rest of the city.', 'Wave bye to the clown, he''ll accompany you on your building job tomorrow, but right now he has to go home and be with the rest of his clown people. He can''t accompany you after dark.', 'He can only <kanji>accompany</kanji> you for so long though and soon it''s time to say <reading>bye</reading> (ばい) again.', 'N1', NULL),
(2386, 'Instead of dying, the clown divides into slightly thinner clowns.', 'The many clowns crash to the floor as the bowling ball plows through their... his(?) divided bodies. You can''t bear to look anymore.', 'The clown finally stops trying to <kanji>divide</kanji> himself, and you can''t stand to look at all of him. You grab a <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう) ball and bowl all of his divided parts over!', 'N1', NULL),
(2387, 'Say the line up of your family under the sun, then sing some sunny chords to match. You''ll know your genealogy and a musical score!', 'Your family genealogy and a musical score aren''t even close to being the same thing. Memorize these or everyone will think you''re a fool.', 'If you can''t tell the difference between your <kanji>genealogy</kanji> and a musical <kanji>score</kanji>, you''re a <reading>foo</reading>l (ふ) and I can''t help you. You aren''t a fool, are you? You know the difference.', 'N1', NULL),
(2388, 'Imagine being graceful enough to balance on a small stool above a raging tsunami! Sooo cool!', 'It doesn''t matter how badly the stool shook, you stayed graceful and didn''t fall! ', 'No matter how <kanji>graceful</kanji> you are, the tsunami still <reading>shook</reading> (しゅく) the small stool. That''s where your gracefulness came in. The stool shook, but you didn''t. You balanced yourself and refused to go down. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2389, 'Imagine sailing out into the open sea, drops of water hitting your face, as the towel sail catches the air and carries you away.', 'You sail nervously past Han Solo who also seems to be sailing, though in worse shape than you. But he keeps throwing you finger guns so you figure it''s best to just sail on and pretend this never happened.', 'As you <kanji>sail</kanji> off into the ocean you hear a familiar voice shout out, "Ahoy!" <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo calls. He''s also got a towel sail, but it''s on the Millennium Falcon, which is half-submerged in the water.', 'N1', NULL),
(2390, 'Picture the soul on the cross, flowers all around it and shellfish at its feet. Feel your resentment grow.', 'You wanna have fun hanging out on a cross with flowers and all the shellfish you can eat. Your resentment grows and grows as you watch the soul having everything you suddenly want.

Fun fact: when you see 賁 in a kanji, the reading is probably going to be ふん.', 'You realize you <kanji>resent</kanji> them because it looks like they''re having <reading>fun</reading> (ふん). You aren''t having fun, and you wanna be! How dare they have fun when you aren''t!', 'N1', NULL),
(2391, 'Imagine serving all of your guests their alcohol by dumping it all in the pool! Now jump in and drink all you want!', 'Picture Shaq serving your guests by tossing them into your alcohol filled pool. Thanks, Shaq... I guess.', 'As you <kanji>serve</kanji> your guests (and yourself) you look over and see <reading>Shaq</reading> (しゃく) also trying to serve them. And by serving I mean getting served because he''s throwing them into the pool. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2392, 'Imagine being a gambler and seeing the bright dawn sun when you come out of the casino or pachinko parlor. It burns your eyes! Evil dawn, get back inside quick!', 'Imagine getting up at dawn and seeing the あかつき in the sky near the sun. It''s so beautiful!', 'If you''re up at <kanji>dawn</kanji> you''ll also see the <reading>あか</reading>(red) <reading>つき</reading>(moon). The あかつき only happens as the sun comes up at dawn so usually only gamblers see it. But if you get up early, you can see it too!', 'N1', NULL),
(2393, '"Look upon your leader''s greatness!" he shouts, doing a jig around on the branches.', '"Oh leader, your greatness is so... great!" you shout up at him. He''s holding the ketsup bottle over your head and staring into your eyes as he dances in the tree. He isn''t sure if he wants to dump it on you yet. He isn''t sure if you really recognize his excellence.', 'If you don''t recognize the leader''s <kanji>greatness</kanji> he will throw <reading>ketsu</reading>p (けつ) all over you (it''s a special mix of ketchup and catsup). And it''s ketsup because it looks like blood. The blood he''ll spill if you don''t look up and clap at his great dancing.', 'N1', NULL),
(2394, 'It''s only natural that you''d want to put a lock on a gold roof so that nobody could steal it.', 'How did Joe, a simple farmhand, get hold of such a valuable gold roof? Either way, the lock he’s put on it is incredibly tough, so there’s no way you could make off with his gold roof anyway.', 'The person who put the <kanji>lock</kanji> on the gold roof was none other than... <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう)?!', 'N1', NULL),
(2395, 'Imagine having to endure the pain of frostbite because you were a jerk who put his hand on ice for a few hours.', 'Imagine if you were one of these cool Shinobi ninjas that could endure the extreme cold and pain of frostbite!', 'The best people in the world at being able to <kanji>endure</kanji> the pain are the <reading>shino</reading>bi (しの) ninjas! They train by hanging out in frozen areas all the time and don''t complain at all. Maybe if the pain gets to be too much you can go ask them for advice.', 'N1', NULL),
(2396, 'Imagine saying "Congratulations!" to a king who just ordered his servants to rake down all the pretty mountains in his kingdom.', 'Imagine how hollow your congratulation sounds when you really miss those mountains. This king doesn''t deserve your nice words.', 'The moment you say "<kanji>Congratulations</kanji>!" you''ve already started to <reading>miss</reading> (みず) those beautiful mountains. But you can''t let missing them ruin your congratulations! Don''t let the king know!', 'N1', NULL),
(2399, 'Imagine a king who won''t accept a rocket that isn''t made of glassy material. He''s not very smart...', 'Picture the king admiring himself in the glassy surface and then his face contorts. He smashes his fist through the rocket and rips out the reeds. "REEDS AREN''T GLASSY!" he cries, and starts hitting you with them.', 'You take the <kanji>glassy</kanji> rocket and try to stuff some thin <reading>reed</reading>s (り) inside to support it. But because it''s clear and glassy the king sees them at the last second.', 'N1', NULL),
(2371, 'Picture yourself all alone. You never let anyone into your heart but yourself, and now you''re mourning the life and friends you could have had.', 'You try to push the key back into your body. Back into your eye, you even try to swallow it, but it comes back out. Finally you look down at your mourning heart and try the key there. It clicks. And opens.', 'While you''re in <kanji>mourning</kanji>, you feel a sharp pain in your crying eyes. A <reading>key</reading> (き) comes sliding out with your tears. You look around wildly, but you''re still alone. Is this... the key of mourning?', 'N1', NULL),
(2400, 'It''s an upward transition, so that''s nice, but it''s still going to take you a long time to get used to using a big helicopter instead of a scooter.', 'A centaur is just a human halfway between transitioning into a horse, so it isn''t even that hard to do!', 'You decide one <kanji>transition</kanji> isn''t enough, so you transition to a <reading>cen</reading>taur (せん) too. Now you''re a big half horse-half human creature and you''ll fit in the big helicopter better.', 'N1', NULL),
(2401, 'Of course, you''re too clumsy to do that right either, you accidentally threw the door backwards and hurt some people...', 'Imagine trying to use your clumsy fingers all over the sets of doors. You keep falling over and hurting yourself. Why is it so hard to leave?!', 'After you make your <kanji>clumsy</kanji> mistake you realize that these doors come in <reading>sets</reading> (せつ). And now you have to try to open another door. And another door. So many sets of doors!', 'N1', NULL),
(2402, 'Imagine yourself walking along a mountain pass. Look up at the mountains above you, and down at the mountains below you. ', 'Picture yourself hiking along a mountain pass in Tokyo and spotting a little Tokyo gecko run up and down on them. It''s adorable and totally worth the climb.', 'It''s only on this type of <kanji>mountain pass</kanji> that you''ll be able to find the rare and adorable <reading>To</reading>kyo <reading>ge</reading>cko (とうげ). These tiny little critters can only be found on the ridges going above and below the mountains in Tokyo. There are like six mountains in Tokyo, so the next time you''re scaling the ridges keep your eyes open for the Tokyo gecko!', 'N1', NULL),
(2403, 'Someone sat down and deliberately planned the entire horse out, collected the materials, and made it by hand.', 'Tokugawa Ieyasu then poofs out of existence and so does the bamboo horse.', 'As you admire all the <kanji>deliberate</kanji> details in this bamboo horse, the ghost of <reading>Toku</reading>gawa (とく) Ieyasu appears before you! "I deliberately made this horse to show you how important being deliberate is. You should be absolutely certain and decisive about your decisions."', 'N1', NULL),
(2405, 'Picture the female turkey stopping when it sees you holding out a spoon. Why are you doing that human? it thinks. Are you sick?', 'Imagine watching a female turkey make a mess of her own cage because she thinks you need help. How sweet.', 'The <kanji>female</kanji> turkey then runs around making a <reading>mess</reading> (めす) of things trying to figure out what''s wrong with you. She kicks around her hay, water bowl, and scratches at your pants, making more mess marks on them. But you don''t move and she gets even more worried.', 'N1', NULL),
(2406, 'Imagine a little crab getting stuck in a crab trap with dirt and other crabs. Endure the horror as long as you can, little friend.', 'Imagine yourself putting a collar around the crab''s neck, then adding a leash and taking your crab for a walk around your neighborhood.', 'You decide to make the little crab that <kanji>endure</kanji>d being stuck in a dirty crab trap your pet, and put a <reading>colla</reading>r (こら) round its little neck.', 'N1', NULL),
(2407, 'The recital is happening now. So open your mouth and start already!', 'Drink gin before all your recitals! It''ll help!', 'Here, if you are too afraid to start the <kanji>recital</kanji> now drink this <reading>gin</reading> (ぎん) to get your courage up. But once you''re done drinking it the recital starts!', 'N1', NULL),
(2408, 'How hard is it to come up with a good mnemonic for a crab trap? Very hard!', 'How many sentences can you make with very and jeans? More than me I hope.', 'You know what else is <kanji>very</kanji> something? <reading>Jean</reading>s (じん). Jeans are very expensive. Jeans are very tight. Jeans are very popular. Jeans are very a lot of things.', 'N1', NULL),
(2409, 'Man your own jackhammer! (And worship the gods.)', 'You try to argue that you can''t sue someone for the way they choose to worship, but they say they''re all deaf now, all the animals have gone, and no one sleeps anymore because of the shaking. Yikes, they might have a case!', 'Unfortunately, the people who live around here can also hear you and the others <kanji>worship</kanji> in the mountain and they''re going to <reading>sue</reading> (すう) you for the noise. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2410, 'Picture your hat and all of the other things the tsunami + tree water touched. It''s all been hardened with a thick coating of the lacquer.', 'Watch as your shih tzu tries to bite your now lacquered chair. It gets its teeth around the leg, but it''s hard as a rock. The shih tzu starts sneezing and spitting, letting go. Looks like it tastes bad too. Perfect!', 'You decide to scoop up the rest of the <kanji>lacquer</kanji> water and use it on your furniture to protect it from your <reading>shih tzu</reading> (しつ). Now it can''t bite or scratch or ruin any of your things anymore!', 'N1', NULL),
(2411, 'Picture the turtle shell shaped cape. Feel the salty wind in your hair!', 'The next time you go to the cape, remember to bring your old, used meat sack. You can toss it right into the ocean and it won''t bother you ever again! Just make sure no one sees you.', 'You decide to walk along this <kanji>cape</kanji> because it looks like the perfect place to get rid of your old <reading>me</reading>at <reading>sack</reading> (みさき). It''s starting to smell, but the ocean breeze from the cape pulls the meat sack out of your hands and off into the sky (then probably into the sea too).', 'N1', NULL),
(2412, 'Imagine spinning thread into whatever your imagination wants.', 'Picture all of these thread-made bowling items. You''re a really big bowling fan, huh?', 'While you''re <kanji>spinning</kanji> you realize that you''ve always wanted to make your own <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう) ball. And bowling ball cover. And bowling ball shoes.', 'N1', NULL),
(2413, 'It may seem like there are a bunch of turkeys under there, but it''s just the shape the reef takes. Though I guess if you''re looking from above the water it really does look like a bunch of turkeys freaking out.', 'You don''t know if the Shougun likes the reef because he likes the beauty of the ocean, or if he''s fantasizing about turkeys boiling. He''s a... complex man.', 'The <kanji>reef</kanji> is the <reading>Shou</reading>gun''s (しょう) favorite place to be nowadays. He sits next to the water, and stares down into it for hours.', 'N1', NULL),
(2414, 'Know your radicals, yo!', 'Imagine trying to lift some barracks. They weigh a ton! How are you supposed to do this? Why would you even try to lift something that obviously weighs a couple tons?!', '<kanji>Barracks</kanji> are pretty big, so they weigh a <reading>ton</reading> (とん). And they probably take tons of materials to build.', 'N1', NULL),
(2415, 'When you think about the person you want to marry, picture their mouth wide in a big, beautiful smile, a wide-open laugh. That''s the lady for you, alright!', 'When you get to the inn all you do is make her laugh and smile so you can bask in her bright, big, mouth smiles.', 'You <kanji>marry</kanji> this woman right away and you both go straight to an <reading>inn</reading> (いん) together!', 'N1', NULL),
(2416, 'Picture your angry friend standing up next to your fingers, shouting, "I''m the real me! That''s just an imitation!" The rest of your friends look nervously at each other. They doubt this person is real now.', 'Okay, so that''s a lie, Your fingers are definitely imitating them, but you don''t want to end up in the guillotine so you keep your mouth shut.', 'Everyone thinks the friend you were trying to <kanji>imitate</kanji> is the imitation now and they have them in a <reading>gui</reading>llotine (ぎ). You even doubt if they''re an imitation or if your fingers are the imitation.', 'N1', NULL),
(2417, 'Imagine having someone you aren''t friendly with trying to have a look at your womb. No thank you.', 'Imagine only letting this guy start to introduce himself with ぼく before walking the heck outta there. Just because he was acting friendly doesn''t mean he was actually your friend.', 'This <kanji>friendly</kanji> guy comes up to you and start to introduce himself, "<reading>ぼく</reading>-" but you stop him. He''s been getting way too friendly with your womb and you don''t want to hear who he is. He can ぼく at someone else, you''re out.', 'N1', NULL),
(2418, 'Imagine spending your leisure time curled up under a tree that''s growing through a gate. It''s so cozy and secluded!', 'But wait. Isn''t Genghis Khan dead now? He winks at you, "Leisure time is for the dead." He smiles, and fades away, disappearing into the tree.', 'As you <kanji>leisure</kanji>ly nap, you hear a gruff voice, "Ah, you''re done too, huh?" It''s Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん)! He''s spending his leisure time relaxing under this abandoned gate just like you.', 'N1', NULL),
(2419, 'Think of all that music you listened to underground but not in the light of the sun. None of that was official music. None of it!', 'The official music penetrates into your soul, instead of just your ears, and it''s affecting everyone else''s souls too! Oh no... wait that''s too many people. This was your song! No, it''s too popular now, stop your souls from liking it! Ahhh!', 'Once the music is <kanji>official</kanji>, you''ll feel it in your <reading>sou</reading>l (そう). When you listen to it outside, under the bright sun, your soul gets to finally hear it too.', 'N1', NULL),
(2420, 'Try to compose a poem about eternity right now. Just jot it down somewhere.', 'Let me recite the acorn poem to you now:

Acorns start small
Grow up big and tall
Spread their baby acorns for all
Live on for all eternity', 'You decide to <kanji>compose</kanji> a poem about an <reading>a</reading>corn (えい). The tiny acorn is the perfect vessel for you to say how eternity feels to you.', 'N1', NULL),
(2421, 'Imagine dropping down in a rice paddy, then again in front of your cross. Be as lowly as you can make yourself.', 'You tried to make yourself lowly in front of God but this heat is probably a sign that you didn''t do it well enough.', 'The only problem about being <kanji>lowly</kanji> is the <reading>he</reading>at (ひ). Everyone always told you that heat rises, but not here. The rice paddy is incredibly hot... unless oh no. Is that the heat of hell?', 'N1', NULL),
(2422, 'Imagine yourself as a leader who''s come to despise every single one of their followers. Look down at them in disgust as they ask you to grant them all a pony.', 'This is not a healthy way to handle things! If you despise someone you should talk to them, don''t turn to booze. Don''t be like every leader that''s come before you.', 'The more you <kanji>despise</kanji> them, the more <reading>boo</reading>ze (ぶ) you drink. This is not smart or healthy, but it''s what this kind of hate does to people.', 'N1', NULL),
(2423, 'Imagine yourself carefully pouring the gold down each of your three slides. Really be careful — the measurements need to be exact, or the cast won''t come out right.', 'Picture your majestic Chewbacca cast. You place it outside your front door to guard the entrance to your house. Wraaaaaaarrrrrrr!', 'At last, you reveal what you''ve <kanji>cast</kanji>: a gold statue of <reading>Chew</reading>bacca (ちゅう). It''s a life-size cast, which is why you needed so much gold and such exact measurements.', 'N1', NULL),
(2425, 'Imagine the guts you''d need to have to stay up so late that you see the moon and the sun switch places! And to risk gallbladder damage?! That takes guts!', 'Imagine the deep tan you''d get after staying awake outside for such a long time. Tanning is also bad for your gallbladder! Man, you''ve really got guts to be getting tans like that.', 'You stayed awake outside for so long, just to prove you had <kanji>guts</kanji> and powerful <kanji>gallbladder</kanji>, that you also got a sweet <reading>tan</reading> (たん) out of it!', 'N1', NULL),
(2426, 'You wander everywhere, with no particular direction in mind.', 'Wherever the good tsunami goes, it leaves delicious fish roe behind. That''s what makes it a good tsunami.', 'As you <kanji>wander</kanji>, you come across a clue. It is fish <reading>roe</reading> (ろう). ', 'N1', NULL),
(2427, 'Look at the evil spirit with two mustaches on its spirit face. Imagine its face in detail, especially those mustaches.', 'Imagine running around, flailing your arms, trying to get the spirit to stop ruining your car. But your hands go right through him and his two mustaches. You can''t stop his evil doings.', 'You''re watching the <kanji>evil</kanji> mustache spirit when it starts to float toward your <reading>ca</reading>r (か). And it''s... hey! It''s trashing your car! You knew this guy was evil!', 'N1', NULL),
(2428, 'Imagine trying to give more than a kiss of alcohol to dairy and watch it bubble and curdle and get all gross and chunky.', 'Look at all your dairy products: milk, yogurt, cheese, all of it sitting nicely on your dairy rack.', '<kanji>Dairy</kanji> isn''t kept with other drinks, it needs to be placed on a <reading>rack</reading> (らく). I hope you''ve been using your dairy rack to store your dairy. If you haven''t you should get one right away!', 'N1', NULL),
(2430, 'Can you imagine how difficult landing a helicopter is in just one of these horrible conditions? Congratulate them!', 'Cake is always the appropriate celebratory food.', '<kanji>Congratulate</kanji> the pilot with <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい)!', 'N1', NULL),
(2431, '亜 is associated with Asia because of its あ reading, but sometimes you might see it used as ateji for other place names as well. And don''t forget that sub- meaning!', 'A is for Asia!', 'What''s the first sound in <kanji>Asia</kanji>? It''s <reading>A</reading> (あ). Nice and easy!', 'N1', NULL),
(2432, 'Imagine being at the tsunami store and selecting the least fat one there.', 'While you select the tsunami that''s right for you. Keep an eye out for those fat taco-eating ones. And then stay away from them!', 'If you''re having trouble <kanji>select</kanji>ing which one is the least fat just check to see which tsunamis are eating <reading>ta</reading>cos (た). Only fat tsunamis will be eating them, so just look for one without a taco.', 'N1', NULL),
(2433, 'Imagine licking the trunk of a Japanese birch tree and feeling the heat on your tongue.', 'Imagine using a zoo saw to cut down a Japanese birch tree. What''s a zoo saw? It''s a saw that''s used in zoos, to build all the animal enclosures. And to cut down spicy trees.', 'You can only cut down a <kanji>Japanese birch</kanji> using <reading>a zoo saw</reading> (あずさ) because a regular saw can''t handle how spicy these trees are.', 'N1', NULL),
(2434, 'Imagine how those few grains of sand feel to get swept up into a tsunami. They didn''t ask for this. They just wanted to stay at the bottom of the sea.', 'As long as you remember 砂, you can remember さ.', 'You already learnt the reading for 砂, the other kanji for <kanji>sand</kanji>. It should be easy to remember this one too, since it''s also さ!', 'N1', NULL),
(2347, 'By writing up new articles and laws they keep taking things away from samurai. First the katana, now jackhammers?', 'Genghis Khan knew that samurai were a threat to his kingdom, so he took away their secondary weapon, the jackhammer.', 'The one who wrote these <kanji>article</kanji>s with such <kanji>sincerity</kanji> was Genghis <reading>Khan</reading> (かん).', 'N1', NULL),
(2435, 'Picture someone else riding a scooter with an axe in their fingertips. That''s so dangerous! One bump, one bad turn, and that axe slips and they''ll die.', 'Your holy blue saber died for you. It saved you from certain death and now it died. After all you''ve been through together. All 60 levels. This is the end.', 'You slip. The axe collides with your face. You <kanji>die</kanji>. But... wait! There''s a bright, blue glow and suddenly you''re un-dead. You watch in slow motion as your holy blue <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい) brings you back to life and takes the hit from the axe instead.

Wait, no!

It shatters.', 'N1', NULL),
(2436, 'You don''t really want to be in this cage, but there were some things you didn''t want to do today, so you hid. Luckily these flowers block the right side of the cage and hide you completely.', '"N-no?" you respond. "Wrong! I hid all the time. There''s nothing wrong with hiding, you''re doing a great job." he says, and disappears.', 'As you <kanji>hide</kanji> the ghost of <reading>Toku</reading>gawa (とく) Ieyasu appears before you, inside the cage! His arms are crossed and he''s shaking his head at you. "Do you think I managed to become the first shougun of Japan by hiding?"', 'N1', NULL),
(2437, 'Imagine the geoduck laughing and screaming, "Now you''re a widow! Now you''re a widow!" flailing the sword around. It''s awful.', 'It''s just you and your car now, living the widow life, driving on into the sunset and forgetting your past.', 'You may be a <kanji>widow</kanji>, but that doesn''t make you weak. You run outside, jump into your <reading>ca</reading>r (か), and run your entire house and the geoduck over.', 'N1', NULL),
(2438, 'Imagine back to the last time you ate something bad and it gave you diarrhea. It felt like someone was sticking you with a knife, didn''t it?', 'You learned this reed technique from a group of ninjas who use them to breathe underwater, and you never thought you''d use them like this.', 'The <kanji>diarrhea</kanji> is here. It''s happening. And it smells so awful you can''t breathe. You have to take some hollow <reading>reed</reading>s (り) and shove them up your nose and out the bathroom door so you can get some fresh air in you.', 'N1', NULL),
(2439, 'You pull away the lid and crawl inside. There is a ladder that takes you deep into the pit.', 'After you''ve nursed こういち back to health, he explains that he was put here by a clone こういち–the very same こういち who became emperor. He tells you he can think of around 61-70 different pain levels of revenge, but also that "it probably wouldn''t be worth it, so let''s just hang out for a while instead. Ooh, are those cookies?"', 'When you reach the bottom of the <kanji>pit</kanji>, you hear someone scuffling around and the sounds of chains. You light a match and inch closer to see what it is. In the darkness, you see <reading>こう</reading>いち, starved and chained in a pit under your table.', 'N1', NULL),
(2440, 'Imagine if every flower you were supposed to oversee was indigo. Indigo everywhere!', 'Can you imagine having indigo-only seeing eyes? I can. Because of this awesome mnemonic.', 'Wait, maybe these flowers are only <kanji>indigo</kanji> because there is something wrong with your <reading>eye</reading>s (あい)! Maybe your eyes are indigo and that''s why it''s all you see!', 'N1', NULL),
(2441, 'Picture a rice paddy. Half of it is trailing off into the shore. Hear the seagulls. Feel the ocean breeze!', 'Imagine lying on the shore with Han Solo. Enjoying a cool drink. Smelling the fragrance of the rice paddy behind you. Yeah, this is the life!', 'You decide to walk along this <kanji>shore</kanji> when you see <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo lounging in the sand. He''s waving for you to join him! You get to lie on the shore with Han!!', 'N1', NULL),
(2442, 'Imagine using your mouth to sing and a shell to play the shamisen to perform your shamisen song!', 'A shamisen song is a song, yay!', 'A <kanji>shamisen song</kanji> is just an <reading>歌</reading> (うた) but with a shamisen. They''re still songs so they''re said the exact same way!', 'N1', NULL),
(2443, 'First, you put them in a coffin to make them think they''re going to die. Then you tickle them with your fingers. There is no greater psychological + physical torture than this.', 'He keeps screaming: "STOP THIS TICKLE TORTURE. I CAN''T TAKE IT, JUST BURY ME."', 'The beggar you will coffin tickle <kanji>torture</kanji> is none other than old Grandpa <reading>ごう</reading>いち. He is a frail old man, and doesn''t like to be tickled.', 'N1', NULL),
(2444, 'Watch as the tsunami rakes your husband''s cleat across the mountain, carving a valley and an easier path for itself. Nature is brutal.', 'Imagine yourself eating cake with your husband in this newly formed valley. You both agree that this should be called Cake Valley!', 'When the water clears, you and your husband go to the <kanji>valley</kanji> to look for the cleat and instead you find a bunch of <reading>ca</reading>ke (けい)! Maybe this is the tsunami''s way of saying, "Thanks for letting me steal your cleat so I could make this valley shortcut." ', 'N1', NULL),
(2445, 'Look really closely at this Mona Lisa. Hmm, I don''t remember her having a mustache, but I guess it is a little dark under this canopy.', 'No wonder this moustached Mona Lisa is such a bargain. You can really get anything as a rental these days, huh?', 'This Mona Lisa is such a <kanji>bargain</kanji> because it''s just a <reading>ren</reading>tal (れん)!  ', 'N1', NULL),
(2446, 'Picture yourself trying to be humble. You''re down on the ground talking to a pair of boots. You don''t dare raise your eyes past that.', 'Imagine saying something to the King. Get low down on the ground to be as humble as you can. Whisper into the King''s boots. Lick them if you have to.', 'You''re being <kanji>humble</kanji> like this because those boots belong to the <reading>Kin</reading>g (きん) of England. You can''t just say whatever you want at eye level to a king. He''ll have your head chopped off for insolence, or worse!', 'N1', NULL),
(2448, 'Picture the drunk demon as its pretty face drips off revealing the ugly crap piled underneath.', 'The demon drinking alcohol out of its nasty shoes is the ugliest scene you''ve ever seen.', 'The <kanji>ugly</kanji> demon is drunk and taking off his <reading>shoe</reading>s (しゅう) and swinging them around his head. And, oh no, he''s drinking more alcohol out of the shoes.', 'N1', NULL),
(2449, 'Picture twenty slides laid out in a grid system.', 'You can make a grid out of anything if you set your mind to it. And you can do math anywhere as long as you have some grid paper!', 'As you stare at the slide <kanji>grid</kanji> you see some people come out and overhear them talk about their mass <reading>maths</reading> (ます) tournament happening later today. Oohhh it IS a giant grid. And they''re going to use it to do maths later!', 'N1', NULL),
(2450, 'Imagine all the yakuza coming out into the streets and holding up signs saying this guy was a martyr. A hero to all who do bad stuff in bad groups but don''t wanna get caught.', 'Really, it''s not the fault of the people who put him there that he''s become a martyr. It''s the month of June. PUNISH JUNE! PUT JUNE IN THE SUN PRISON! (Is what I assume those yakuza are shouting.)', 'The only reason he ended up a <kanji>martyr</kanji> in the first place is because they put him up there in <reading>June</reading> (じゅん). Everyone knows the sun is way too hot for humans in June. It''s summer!', 'N1', NULL),
(2451, 'Imagine how annoyed you would be if someone set YOU on fire for no reason. The geoduck has every reason to find you annoying, dude.', 'Think how annoying it must be to be a geoduck when Han Solo comes around.', 'You know who loves <kanji>annoying</kanji> geoducks more than anyone else in the universe? <reading>Han</reading> (はん) Solo! If you watch Star Wars closely enough you can see him shooting geoducks with his blaster and catching them on fire for fun. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2452, 'Picture yourself as you slice the power out of that senator you hate by censuring them in front of the world!', 'Look at that guy now. What a loser.', 'Once you <kanji>censure</kanji> them and they lose all their power, they''ll be just another <reading>guy</reading> (がい). A normal guy with everything lost to him. HAHAHA!', 'N1', NULL),
(2453, 'Imagine laying your tree into the water to make your own jetty extending out to the floating bar in the ocean. Innovative!', 'Picture Santa-san coming in from the ocean on his speedboat and docking on your jetty so he can go relax in the bar. Imagine the mountain of presents he lays on the jetty for you as thanks.', 'You also made this <kanji>jetty</kanji> so it would be easier for <reading>San</reading>ta-san (さん) to dock his sleigh. Oh, you didn''t know? His sleigh is more of a speed boat now, and he wants a refreshing drink after delivering all those presents. You get extra presents for making him a jetty, so win-win.', 'N1', NULL),
(2454, 'Imagine what it would be like to fall in love with a coat rack and make it YOUR groom.', 'Try to imagine what this moon coat looks like. Does it glow in the dark like the moon shines at night? Or does it instead have a rugged texture like the moon''s surface? Or maybe both?', 'Since their wedding happened under the full moon, the <kanji>groom</kanji> wore his special <reading>moon coa</reading>t (むこ) to the ceremony. This is the ideal coat for a groom to wear, since they can keep wearing it all through their honeymoon.', 'N1', NULL),
(2455, 'Imagine how it feels to yearn for someone else when you see the small water drop. Imagine how your heart feels.', 'Now you yearn for being somewhere Bobo is not.', 'As you <kanji>yearn for</kanji> that person from your past, you look back at the drop and see... <reading>Bo</reading>bo (ぼ) the clown?! Ugh. Apparently Bobo dropped that small drop of water.', 'N1', NULL),
(2456, 'Your captors think they have you trapped, but you know you can leave whenever you feel like it.', 'As the heat spreads you decide to leave. You hop out of the net and watch the shock spread in your captors eyes.', 'You don''t know if you want to <kanji>quit</kanji> this charade until you feel an uncomfortable <reading>hea</reading>t (ひ). You look down and the net you''re in is being hung above a large fire.', 'N1', NULL),
(2457, 'An arrow from heaven will correct all your problems!', 'If you want someone to correct your teeth or mustache, just wander around きょうと, staring up at the sky. Then when you see an arrow, run for it and try to catch it with your face!', 'These heavenly <kanji>correct</kanji>ing arrows actually come from <reading>きょう</reading>と. They hate seeing people with crooked mustaches, so they shoot them out on the regular. It just looks like they''re coming from heaven because they fall out of the sky.', 'N1', NULL),
(2458, 'Which tree do you think is sweet? That one? Or that one? Are you absolutely certain? I hope you are!', 'So now let me ask you again, are you certain, or would you like to try the bowling ball test?', 'If you really want to be <kanji>certain</kanji>, and not just guess, take out your <reading>bow</reading>ling (ぼう) ball and roll it as hard as you can into the tree. If its the right one it will let out a sweet smell, if it''s not the tree will punch you.', 'N1', NULL),
(2460, 'Certain tsunami secretions are dangerous. Can you think of any?', 'The next time someone asks you what does a tsunami secrete? You''ll say heat!', 'Most tsunami''s <kanji>secrete</kanji> <reading>he</reading>at (ひ). It takes a lot of energy for a tsunami to crash onto the land so the water is really really hot, and it has to secrete that heat as it''s going back out to sea.', 'N1', NULL),
(2461, 'Imagine gradually denting your car door open with an axe while you''re trapped in a tsunami. This isn''t fast enough! ', 'YO YANKEES! Gradually yelling that saved your life!', 'As you''re <kanji>gradually</kanji> making your door open with the axe you look up to see the New York Yankees floating by on top of their bus. You yell, "<reading>YO YA</reading>NKEES (ようや) to get their attention. They see your gradual progress and start throwing balls at your car to help dent it open.', 'N1', NULL),
(2462, 'Seaweed is the flower of the sea!', 'Feed your soul with seaweed and it will thank you. Promise! &lt;3', '<kanji>Seaweed</kanji> is true <reading>sou</reading>l (そう) food. Which means it''s good for your soul. Didn''t you ever read those books about chicken soup being good for your soul? Well that was a lie. Miso soup with seaweed is what''s really good for your soul.', 'N1', NULL),
(2463, 'Picture yourself watching the news about a woman''s death. The word RECKLESS?! pops up on your screen and keeps flashing. Huh, she must have been, you think to yourself.', 'Imagine what reckless thing that woman had to have done to die while mowing the lawn. Eww.', 'As you watch the news about this <kanji>reckless</kanji> woman you find out that she died from <reading>mow</reading>ing (もう) her lawn. You''re not sure how, but now you really believe that she had to be doing it really recklessly to die doing it.', 'N1', NULL),
(2464, 'Again and again, these insect-like barbarians are harassing you and your lands. What shall we do, m''lord?', 'Picture yourself as the lord of your lands, screaming "BAN them" at the top of your lungs. You''ll finally expel these insect-like barbarians that have been plaguing your lands again and again.', '"<reading>BAN</reading> (ばん) the barbarians! Ban them, I say!," you loudly declare as the lord of these lands. ', 'N1', NULL),
(2465, 'Imagine the leader squid giving a speech about how you shouldn''t buy new things, and how you should live with a roommate to save money. Every squid yen counts!', 'Ken''s squid is always shaking his head at Ken the samurai''s obsession with wearing all silk. He should visit Thrifty Threads, the second hand shop, and get some cotton clothes that would do the job just fine.

Hint: Whenever there''s a squid, remember that Ken (けん) the samurai is nearby.', 'Who is a <kanji>thrifty</kanji> squid? <reading>Ken</reading> (けん) the samurai''s squid buddy, who gets carried around in a bucket and never pays rent.', 'N1', NULL)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;


