(3155, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3156, 'The reading is kun''yomi, so here''s a mnemonic to remember it: 

<vocabulary>If</vocabulary> you don''t mind, could you <reading>carry</reading> (かり) this for me? <vocabulary>Supposing</vocabulary> it''s not too much trouble, of course.'),
(3157, 'Okay, so 仮 uses the rare kun''yomi reading か, which you haven''t learned yet, but luckily it''s the same as the on''yomi you have learned! And 名 also uses a kun''yomi reading that you learned with the vocab word 名 (な). So you sort of know these readings already, but it''s a lot to take in, so here''s a mnemonic to help: 

The reading is so tricky for <vocabulary>kana</vocabulary> that it makes you say <reading>かな</reading>… (I wonder…) whenever you try to read it. You keep thinking, "Is the reading kana, かな? Or could it be something else, かな?" You''re just not sure.'),
(3158, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3159, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji, so tomorrow when you wake up you hopefully won''t have to relearn this vocab''s reading.'),
(3160, 'The reading for this one is strange. The reading for 速 (そく) is what you''d expect (on''yomi), but 早 gets a reading you haven''t seen before, meaning you should treat this as an exception. Just think about how you''re learning this word <vocabulary>at once</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>without delay</vocabulary> so there''s no time for any <reading>sa</reading>ss (さっ), just kanji learning.'),
(3161, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3162, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

You’re going <vocabulary>to distribute</vocabulary> flyers about your favorite country: <reading>Cuba</reading> (くば). You’re the biggest Cubaboo there has ever been, so you’ll gladly stand out in the street distributing flyers about Cuba all day long… right?'),
(3163, 'The reading is kun''yomi, which isn''t what you learned with the kanji. But, it should be pretty easy. You know what Japanese alcohol is called already, right? It''s <reading>さけ</reading>, simple as that. So long as you''ve heard of sake you should be A-Okay with this reading.'),
(3164, 'The reading for this is just the vocab 日本''s primary reading plus the on''yomi you learned for 酒, put together.'),
(3165, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3166, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3167, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

This situation has <vocabulary>to end</vocabulary>. You''re the <reading>ower</reading> (おわ) of a lot of money. For this to end, you need to pay back all that money. Try to think of ways to stop being an ower so this stressful situation can end!'),
(3168, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3169, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
