(3208, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

You need <vocabulary>to hear</vocabulary> something very important, but you can''t. In order to hear, you have to take a <reading>key</reading> (き) and unlock the doors covering your ears. Go ahead and imagine yourself doing that, focusing on the key that lets you hear.'),
(3209, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3210, 'The reading is just the vocab word 日本 (にほん) plus the on''yomi that you learned for 語. Combine them together to get the reading for this word.'),
(3211, 'The reading is just the on''yomi reading for 語 which is the reading you learned with the kanji. This is probably because it gets added onto a lot of other words so it''s treated like a jukugo. For example: 日本語 = "Japanese Language." 中国語 = "Chinese Language." スペイン語 = "Spanish Language."'),
(3212, 'The reading for the kanji portion is just the reading you learned with the kanji. Since it''s attached to something, it''s like a jukugo word.'),
(3213, 'Both kanji take the kun''yomi here. For 物, that''s もの, which you learned with the vocabulary 物. You learned the kun''yomi for 語 with the verb 語る, but here it becomes がたり because of rendaku, and the り is tucked inside the kanji.

Maybe you''ve already heard the word ものがたり too. It''s in some film and book names. If you haven''t, and you find this reading hard to remember, think of a story about an infamous <reading>Gatling</reading> (がたり) gun that wandered the land, mowing everything down in its path. It''s a terrible legend, but what if it were true???'),
(3214, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3215, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji. Go read something else, now.'),
(3216, 'This uses the kun''yomi readings from the vocab words 読む and 方. So as long as you''re okay with those, you can apply that knowledge over here.'),
(3217, 'The reading is the same reading as the one you learned with the kanji. So, spend more time chirping and less time memorizing, okay?'),
(3218, 'The reading you learned with the kanji is the same for this vocab word. Enjoy it!'),
(3219, 'The reading for this vocab is the same as the one you learned with the kanji, lucky you!'),
(3220, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
