(3357, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

Do you know how in the Mario universe all the <vocabulary>bad</vocabulary> versions are called "<reading>WARU</reading>" (わる)? For example: "WARUIJI" and "WARIO." That''s because they are bad. They come from this kanji. So, if you ever need to remember this reading, just think back to Wario and Waruigi.'),
(3358, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3359, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3360, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3361, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3362, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3363, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3364, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. Because the reading sounds just like a dirty word, and there may be innocent children about, I''ll let you come up with your own mnemonic for this one. Maybe someone pushes you into a deep hole that will surely kill you so you yell something out as you fall down it?'),
(3365, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You''ve seen these readings before, but the や reading for 夜 is still pretty new, so here''s a mnemonic refresher:

What do you do in the <vocabulary>middle of the night</vocabulary>? You watch the <reading>Ya</reading>nkees (や). You should be asleep, but instead you''re up watching baseball highlights from the Yankees <vocabulary>late at night</vocabulary>. '),
(3366, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3367, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3368, 'This word is made up of a single kanji, so it should use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

You see a <vocabulary>sphere</vocabulary> in <reading>Tama</reading>ra''s (たま) hand. You''re not totally sure what it is, but it''s really spherical. Maybe it''s some kind of <vocabulary>sports ball</vocabulary>. Remember Tamara, the local ball girl? It would totally make sense for Tamara to be holding either a sphere or a sports ball, since that''s kind of all she does.

You might notice 球 has the same reading as the vocab word 玉. Their meanings are kind of similar too, so this reading should be a bit easier to remember!'),
(3369, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3370, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3371, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You didn''t learn the たい reading with the 太 kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you out:

When the <vocabulary>sun</vocabulary> is out, you take off your <reading>tie</reading> (たい). You ever had a tie on outside when it was all sunny and hot? It''s not pleasant. A tie is usually tolerable until the sun comes out — then you''re super aware of how hot and uncomfortable it is. Go ahead, take off your tie.'),
(3372, 'The reading is on''yomi because it''s a jukugo kanji. Be careful of the 一 though. It gets shortened and has a little っ in there.'),
(3373, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3374, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3375, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading which you learned with the kanji (<reading>Samu</reading>rai (さむ)). Remember?'),
(3376, 'You learned this reading with 悲しむ, hooray!'),
(3377, 'This uses the same reading as the one you learned with the kanji, so you''re good to go here!'),
(3378, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(3379, 'The reading is on''yomi because it''s a jukugo word but がく gets shortened to がっ, so be careful. Maybe you just wish the school term would get shorter which is why you shortened がく like that?'),
(3380, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3381, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji.'),
(3382, 'You know how to read 歯 and 医者, so you should be good to go!'),
(3383, 'The readings are both kun''yomi, but those are the readings you learned with these kanji. Notice that は turns to ば, though. Presumably this is because you might yell out "BAH!" when you find out you have a cavity.'),
(3384, 'The reading for this word is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(3385, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

What do you say when your friend wants to get out of a <vocabulary>warm</vocabulary> onsen before you? "<reading>Ah… Ta-ta</reading> (あたた)!" Yeah, you''re not getting out of this warm bath yet — your friend can go on ahead. You just sigh in warm, blissful comfort and wave bye: "Ah… Ta-ta!"

People often pronounce this as あったかい, especially in casual, spoken Japanese. The "correct" reading is still あたたかい though, so keep that in mind when you''re trying to type the kanji or taking an important test!'),
(3386, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3387, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3388, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3389, 'The readings are both the readings you learned with the kanji. Now you can spend a little more time drinking tea at a tea ceremony and a little less time trying to remember kanji readings.'),
(3390, 'The ゆ part of the reading makes sense. The げ part for 気 is a bit of an exception, so here''s a mnemonic to help with that:

No creature likes <vocabulary>steam</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>vapor</vocabulary> more than <reading>ge</reading>ckos (げ). Geckos live in hot places, so wherever you see steam, you''ll be sure to see at least one gecko nearby, if you look hard enough.'),
(3391, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. This has the same reading as 上る which you learned all that time ago in Level 1. If you can remember that, you can remember this one!'),
(3392, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Note this uses the alternative と reading, not to be confused with とう (actually, very confusing, sorry!). Just imagine you cut your <reading>toe</reading> off with an ice pick while mountain climbing. You know it''s a short と because your toe is now short. And さん becomes ざん thanks to our old friend rendaku.'),
(3393, 'What do you want <vocabulary>to wear</vocabulary> today? How about a dress made entirely of <reading>key</reading>s (き)? Feel the metal on your skin and the weight on your shoulders. It''s not a comfortable thing to put on. Maybe just stick with wearing a single key around your neck next time.'),
(3394, 'This uses a different kun''yomi reading than the one you just learned, but 着く (つく) has the same reading as 付く (to be attached). Because when you arrive somewhere, it''s like you''re finally <em>attached</em> to your destination.

If that connection doesn''t quite click, here''s another mnemonic:

You wanted <vocabulary>to arrive</vocabulary> on time but you showed up late. How late were you? <reading>Two</reading> (つ) hours and two minutes late, to be exact.'),
(3395, 'The reading is a bit strange using kun''yomi readings. Luckily, we taught you these already, so go put on that kimono of yours and relax at the 温泉 for a bit.'),
(3396, 'The reading for this one is all kun''yomi vocab reading, but you should know these by now. 着 uses the reading you learned with 着る, but this word employs that pesky rendaku, so remember that き becomes ぎ here.'),
(3397, 'The reading for this one is all kun''yomi vocab reading, but you should know these by now. 着 uses the reading you learned with the vocab word 着る, but this word employs that pesky rendaku, so remember that き becomes ぎ here.'),
(3398, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

Who has <vocabulary>short</vocabulary> hair and always wears shorts? It''s <reading>Ms. Ca</reading>rr (みじか)! Everyone always recognizes Ms. Carr because she''s the one with the short hair and shorts, no matter the weather. Ms. Carr, the ultimate short style icon!'),
(3399, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3400, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3401, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it should use the kun''yomi reading. When learning the kanji, you didn''t learn that reading, so here''s a mnemonic to help you with this word: You''re looking at a natural <vocabulary>spring</vocabulary> in the ground. Water is bubbling up, but then something changes. It looks a bit clogged... something is poking out. Then, all of a sudden, Mario flies out of the springs, falls in front of you and yells: "<reading>izumeeeee</reading> (いずみ), Mario!"'),
(3402, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. Since 人 has two on''yomi readings, here''s a mnemonic to help you remember which one to use:

Here at Tofugu, we measure <vocabulary>population</vocabulary> in <reading>jean</reading>s (じん). Each of us has just one pair of jeans, so if you know the jean count you know the population.'),
(3403, 'Since you should know the readings of the words 子 and 牛 from the previous level, you should be able to read this word too even though it''s using the kun''yomi readings for the kanji.'),
(3404, 'You''d think that the reading for this would consist of the on''yomi readings, but human body parts (hand) do funny things to words. In this case, it''s the kun''yomi readings of both the kanji. You should know the kun''yomi reading for <kanji>切</kanji> and <kanji>手</kanji> from the previous levels, meaning you should be able to remember the reading for this word fairly easily. Watch out for the small っ, though.'),
(3405, 'See the hiragana stuck in there? That''s hinting that these use the kun''yomi readings. You learned the kun''yomi readings of these two kanji separately in the previous level, so if you know those you should know this word''s reading as well.'),
(3406, 'To learn to read this kanji you have to remember the な part of 亡く, though our mnemonic will use the whole thing.

If you''re about to become deceased, you are probably <reading>knock</reading>ing (なく) on death''s door.'),
(3407, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. Since 人 and 生 both have two on''yomi readings, here''s a mnemonic to help you:

Your <vocabulary>life</vocabulary> boils down to just two things: <reading>jean</reading>s and a <reading>sa</reading>ber (じんせい). Your whole life, that''s all you''ve ever needed, jeans and a saber. What are you, some kind of American pirate?'),
(3408, 'If you know the kun''yomi readings of both of these kanji you should be okay. You have two separate single-kanji words put together by a の. That''s why it''s the kun''yomi reading - they''re separate. '),
(3409, 'The readings for this word are strange. Both use the kun''yomi readings - 虫 uses the reading you learned with the kanji, so that''s easy. The 毛 part is like the vocab word 毛 you learned in the previous lesson.'),
(3410, 'The reading for this word is all kun''yomi despite being jukugo. Just think of sketchy things going on in the downtown, just like how there''s sketchy kun''yomi readings for this word. You should know both the kun''yomi readings from the previous level, though, so if you do you know the reading for this word as well.'),
(3411, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. However, 小 uses the kun''yomi reading and 文 is an exception here, so here''s a mnemonic to help you remember this:

You learned your lowercase letters from <reading>子</reading> <reading>Moe</reading> (こも). That''s right, after Moe taught you all the letters, his son 子 Moe taught you the lowercase ones. Such teamwork.'),
(3412, 'This word is actually two separate words combined with a particle. So, since you treat them like separate words, you read each of them with the kun''yomi readings (they are both single kanji words all alone). That being said, in level 4 you learned about the vocab readings for these separate words, so use that knowledge to remember the reading for this word as well.'),
(3413, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it should use the kun''yomi reading. When learning the kanji, you didn''t learn that reading, so here''s a mnemonic to help you with this word: 

This <vocabulary>year</vocabulary> you plan on <reading>tossi</reading>ng (とし) out all your old stuff as a New Year''s resolution. You pronounce it "toshing" though, because it has more vigor that way.'),
(3414, 'The reading for this word is the kun''yomi reading which you learned previously when you learned the vocab word 今. You can tell this is a kun''yomi reading because it has hiragana attached. So if you know that, you should be able to read this word as well.'),
(3415, 'The readings for these are both the kun''yomi readings. Hopefully you learned them when you were doing vocab in the previous levels so that all you have to do now is combine the two separate words into one bigger word. Please don''t take a winter break until you figure this word out.'),
(3416, 'The readings for these kanji are the kun''yomi readings. With 毛 you should have learned this reading via the vocab word in the previous level. With 糸 it''s actually the main reading for the kanji, so you should already know it.'),
(3417, 'Since you already know the vocab word 今 (read いま), you should be able to read this word as well.'),
(3418, 'The readings are a bit strange. They''re both kun''yomi readings, where 出 is で and 来る is きる. This is different from the reading you originally learned with 来る (くる), though, so be careful. If you can remember that and remember the kun''yomi readings from the previous vocab, you should be able to learn this one''s reading as well.'),
(3419, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(3420, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. Since 人 has two on''yomi readings, and the reading of 形 is a bit of an oddball, here''s a mnemonic to help you:

Did you know that all <vocabulary>doll</vocabulary>s at <reading>Nin</reading>tendo are made out of <reading>gyo</reading>za dumplings (にんぎょう)? They''ll also regularly try to get you to eat them... Mmmm, gyoza dolls. Don''t be afraid to take a bite!'),
(3421, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3422, 'The readings for these kanji are the kun''yomi readings. Just think of this word as two separate words put together (手 and 作る). The る in 作る gets changed to り because it''s an adjective, and the つ in つくり gets changed to づ because of the way the words are combined with each other. You should know most of the reading that is in this word, though it will probably take a little bit of thinking to sort out the little changes in those readings.'),
(3423, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You didn''t learn the on''yomi for 東 (とう) with the kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help:

How do you know you''ve made it to <vocabulary>Tokyo</vocabulary>? The station platform signs will say 東京 — <reading>とう</reading>きょう — Tokyo. Just look for those とうきょう signs if you''re headed for the east capital.'),
(3424, 'You want to find a pet <vocabulary>to suit</vocabulary> your personal style, so you get matched with an <reading>a</reading>ardvark (あ). An aardvark will match the ant mounds you use for chairs and couches, it''s the perfect match.'),
(3425, 'Do you know the word "kamikaze" from WWII history? Well, it means "divine wind." The "kaze" part is the wind part, and this word uses the same reading, making it <reading>かぜ</reading> as well.'),
(3426, 'The readings are the kun''yomi reading for 当て and the on''yomi reading for 字. You''ve seen 当てる in the past, so you should be able to read this portion as well. Combine the two together and you have yourself a nice reading.'),
(3427, 'The reading is the same as the word 次 (so it''s つぎ), but this time you do it twice. No strange rendaku or anything like that either, which makes everyone happier.'),
(3428, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You learned that with 向く, but here''s a mnemonic for you in case you forgot:

<vocabulary>Over there</vocabulary>, a cow is saying <reading>moo</reading> (む). It''s way over there, though, so you can''t see or hear it. Kinda makes you wonder: if a cow moos way over there and no one''s around to listen, did it really moo at all?'),
(3429, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You already learned this reading with 曲げる, so you should be good to go!'),
(3430, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You haven''t seen the 南 reading (なん), so here''s a mnemonic to help you out:

In <vocabulary>South America</vocabulary> everyone eats <reading>naan</reading> (なん). You may be surprised, since naan isn''t exactly considered South American food. But what is a flour tortilla, really? It''s South American naan!'),
(3431, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3432, 'Just like 北口 and the others, this also uses kun''yomi readings. And just like before, watch out for the rendaku that changes くち to ぐち!'),
(3433, 'The readings are both kun''yomi, but based off of previous experience with the two separate words that make up this word, you should be able to read it. Just remember that it''s kun''yomi. One hint is the hiragana sticking out of the 思.'),
(3434, 'There''s hiragana attached to this kanji, which means it''s probably the kun''yomi reading (and it is). You''ve seen the kun''yomi reading via the word 近い. So, as long as you know that word, you should be able to read this one as well.'),
(3435, 'The reading for this word is the kun''yomi readings. You should know the words 思う and 出す and their readings, so you should be able to read this word if you know those words.'),
(3436, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3437, 'This uses the kun''yomi readings from the vocab words 言う and 方. You might be more used to the on''yomi for 方 though, so here''s a mnemonic to help you remember the kun''yomi (かた).

Your <vocabulary>way of saying</vocabulary> things is to <reading>cata</reading>pult (かた) them right into people''s ears. Just catapult those words like you''re trying to break through their ear drums.'),
(3438, 'This consists of a couple kun''yomi readings put together. You can tell it''s kun''yomi because of the hiragana sticking out at the end. In previous levels, you learned both the words 先 and 回る (verb version of 回り). If you know those, you should be able to put them together to learn the reading of this word as well.'),
(3439, 'Remember how body parts often change even jukugo words to kun''yomi readings? That''s what happened here too. Both readings are kun''yomi, making this one <reading>はやくち</reading>. '),
(3440, 'The reading is the same as 足りる (たりる) which you should have seen in Level 4.'),
(3441, 'The reading for this one is an exception, because it uses kun''yomi readings even though it is a jukugo kanji. You already know the separate kun''yomi readings from the vocabulary of the last level, but you will have to remember to actually use this knowledge when this word comes up.'),
(3442, 'The readings are the kun''yomi readings. Why? Because this word is really two separate words connected together into one bigger word. You know the kun''yomi readings from vocab practice in previous levels.'),
(3443, 'You should be able to figure out the reading from learning 代わる in the previous level.'),
(3444, 'The reading for 今年 (ことし) is kinda weird, what with the こ part and all. Just imagine the こん reading you learned for 今 has been shortened to こ here, if that helps. Then the 年 part uses its kun''yomi reading とし. It''s an odd combo, so here''s a mnemonic:

Your goal <vocabulary>this year</vocabulary> is to treat everyone with <reading>courtesy</reading> (ことし). You''re going to be nice and polite to everyone this year, even on social media. After all, there''s too much negativity in the world right now. Oh yes, no doubt about it — this year will be one of courtesy.'),
(3445, 'The reading for this one is the same as 代わる which you learned in a previous level. As long as you know that one you should be able to learn this one''s reading as well.'),
(3446, 'The reading for this one is kind of strange. It is the kun''yomi reading for both of the kanji. You know the kun''yomi readings from earlier, back when you learned the vocab 年 and 下.'),
(3447, 'The reading is the kun''yomi reading twice; that''s the one you learn with the vocab word 近い from the previous level. The second 近 gets converted to ぢか, though, so take note of that. To write the character ぢ, type "di" on your keyboard.'),
(3448, 'You''d think that this word would use on''yomi readings to read it, but it doesn''t (shake that angry fist at the kanji gods!). Instead, it''s double kun''yomi, probably because this word is really like two separate words put together, "blue" and "sky." In the previous level you unlocked these two separate words, learning their readings. That means you should be able to read this as well. 

One quick note is the rendaku-fication of 空 as well. It becomes ぞら.'),
(3449, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3450, 'This uses the kun''yomi readings from the vocab words 作る and 方. So as long as you''re okay with those, you can apply that knowledge over here.'),
(3451, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3452, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3453, 'The readings come from 見る and 直す. Know those and you''ll know how to read this word too!'),
(3454, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

The person who <vocabulary>completely</vocabulary> doesn''t understand this vocab word is <reading>Matt</reading> (まった). Be sure to take note of the small っ, too.'),
(3455, 'If you know the reading of the word 長い from level 6 you''ll know the reading for this word too. Only difference is the さ at the end, which is hiragana so it should be easy to read.'),
(3456, 'The hiragana on the end hints that the readings are the kun''yomi readings. Use your memory of 安い and 売る to help you to remember how to read this.'),
(3457, 'The reading for 前 is the kun''yomi, like the reading for the vocabulary word 前. So long as you know that word, you''ll be able to read this one too. The reason it''s not on''yomi is because it''s like two words put together. The word この and the word 前.'),
(3458, 'Just like 北口 and 西口, this also uses kun''yomi readings. And just like before, watch out for the rendaku that changes くち to ぐち!'),
(3459, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You learned this reading already with the kanji, so you should be all set. Lucky you!'),
(3460, 'This word uses both kun''yomi readings (oh darn!). That being said, you''ve seen the kun''yomi readings for both the kanji that make up this word. The word 名 (な) and the word 前 (まえ) both are kun''yomi, so use those words and Voltron them into a single word, なまえ.'),
(3461, 'The reading is a combination of the readings from 出来る and 上がる. So long as you were paying attention in lessons past, you should be able to read this word as well just by combining those other words.'),
(3462, 'How were you able <vocabulary>to bend</vocabulary> this gecko? Solo effort? No way! It actually took both you and your <reading>ma</reading>ma (ま) to make it happen! Just imagine you and your mighty mama, tag-teaming to bend a gecko in half!'),
(3463, 'The reading is the same as with 交ぜる. So long as you know the ま portion of that word''s reading, you''ll be able to read this word as well.'),
(3464, 'The readings are those like the words 走る and 回る. Make sure you know those words from before and apply that knowledge to read this word as well.'),
(3465, 'The reading can be remembered via the word 当たる which you learned a few levels ago. Use that knowledge to remember that this word is read as あてる. The important part is the あ.'),
(3466, 'The reading is almost the same as 交ぜる which you should be familiar with by now. It has the ま but it also has an extra じ in there. That じ is there because you intersect with a group of <reading>mage</reading>s (まじ) that catch you on fire as you walk past.'),
(3467, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

You try to transform. You want to turn into something awesome, like a dinosaur, but then *poof* you turn into a sheep. "<reading>Ba</reading>aaa," you say. "Baaa."'),
(3468, 'The reading is the same as the word 回る (まわ). So long as you know that word, you''ll be able to read this one as well. Use both of these words together to remember their readings.')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;


Your family buys you a <vocabulary>new</vocabulary> Atari! You''re so excited! But wait... what''s this? This is no Atari. This is an <reading>Atara</reading>... a knockoff video game system :('),
(3473, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Use the reading of 高校 from before to learn the reading of this word as well, if it helps.'),
(3474, 'The reading is a combination of 学校 and 中. Really, it''s all on''yomi too, so you should be able to read this one. Take note of the がく turning to がっ, though.'),
(3475, 'The reading can be derived from 付ける, a word you should have seen a few levels ago. If you know that word''s reading you should be able to figure out this word''s reading as well (and vice versa).'),
(3476, 'The readings come from 見る and 付ける. If you use those readings, you''ll be able to read this word too.'),
(3477, 'The reading for this one is a weird one. It uses the kun''yomi readings for both, meaning you''ll have to pull your knowledge of the word 弱い from earlier and apply it here. 虫 should be okay, though, as this is the reading you learned with the kanji.

Just remember, only weak bugs use kun''yomi, and that weak bug is you in this instance.'),
(3478, 'The reading is double kun''yomi, as you can probably guess due to all the hiragana involved. So, you double up on よわ and then add the しい to read this vocabulary word.'),
(3479, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

Something is lacking... it''s a <reading>ca</reading>r (か). You can''t stop thinking about this because you have to walk 40 miles to get to your destination. If only that car wasn''t missing, this would be easier. Imagine the sweat... the pain... the thirst... all because that car''s not present.'),
(3480, 'The reading is just the vocab word 医者 (いしゃ) plus the reading 目 (め), the same as the reading you learned with the kanji.'),
(3481, 'The reading for this word is just the vocab word 一番 plus the word 目. If you know both of those readings you''ll be able to read this one as well.'),
(3482, 'The readings are a bit weird, here. 仕 gets its on''yomi reading... you should be okay with that. 返し gets the kun''yomi reading, though, which is the reading you saw with the word 返す in level 9. You can tell this part uses the kun''yomi vocab reading because it has that hiragana attached to it. Just remember to distinguish between the two parts of the word to remember the readings.'),
(3483, 'Use the reading of 高い (たかい) to help you to learn and remember the reading for this one. They''re the same except for the hiragana at the end.'),
(3484, 'The reading is just like 楽しい, so make sure you know that and you''ll be able to read this one as well.'),
(3485, 'The reading is the same as the word 向く so work off of that word to be able to read this one.'),
(3486, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. It will also help if you know the reading of 研究 already.

Also note that 所 is normal in first reading, but gets rendaku''d in the second reading, meaning しょ becomes じょ. Both readings are valid and they don''t affect the meaning, so it just comes down to personal preference!'),
(3487, 'The reading is a bit weird in this one. It uses the kun''yomi reading for both kanji, even though it looks just like it should be all on''yomi reading. It''s like you''re taking a <vocabulary>shortcut</vocabulary> with your kanji readings and doing the native Japanese readings (kun''yomi) instead of the more intellectual Chinese ones (on''yomi). Luckily, you''ve hopefully seen both of these kanji as vocab already. You have ちか for 近 (short for 近い) and you have 道 (みち), just like the word "street" you learned before. Combine these together and you have the reading for this word. Perhaps it''s like this because you''re combining a couple concepts together into one word, at least when it comes to "short + way."'),
(3488, 'The reading is the same as 助ける, so hopefully you know that word and can apply that knowledge over here.'),
(3489, 'You learned this reading for 間 when you learned 間もなく. And the reading for 合う is just that of the word 合う. If you remember them both, you''ll remember this word!'),
(3490, 'The reading is the same as 返す, so as long as you know that you''ll be able to apply it here to learn the reading of this word as well.'),
(3491, 'The reading is the same as the word 楽しい, so be sure to know that word and you''ll be able to apply it over here.'),
(3492, 'The reading is the same as 決める, so make sure you know that word so you can apply that knowledge over here.'),
(3493, 'The readings just come from two vocab words you''ve hopefully seen already: 投げる and 付ける. So long as you know the readings for those words, the reading for this word should be no problem for you.'),
(3494, 'The reading is the same as 化ける, so make sure you know that word and you''ll be able to learn this word''s reading as well.'),
(3495, 'Hey, tell that <vocabulary>story</vocabulary> about the time you met the <reading>花-shee</reading>p (はなし). You know — the sheep that had 花s growing out of it instead of wool. No one else here has ever met a 花-sheep, so I bet they''d all love to hear your story.'),
(3496, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

You turn <vocabulary>to face</vocabulary> a new direction and look out into the distance. There is a single cow out there. It turns to face you too, and says <reading>moo</reading> (む).'),
(3497, 'The readings are that of 知る and 合う. So long as you know those words, you should be able to read this one too.'),
(3498, 'This word uses the kun''yomi readings. You didn''t learn the kun''yomi reading for 場 with the kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you remember it:

At the <vocabulary>boarding area</vocabulary>, you <reading>baa</reading> (ば) at the bus driver to ask if this is the right bus. You''re in a country inhabited only by sheep, but luckily you''ve practiced your Sheepanese well enough to be able to baa for directions at <vocabulary>bus stop</vocabulary>s, <vocabulary>taxi stand</vocabulary>s, and <vocabulary>train platform</vocabulary>s.'),
(3499, 'This uses the kun''yomi readings from the vocab words 使う and 方. So as long as you''re okay with those, you can apply that knowledge over here.'),
(3500, 'This word uses the on''yomi reading for 仕 and the kun''yomi reading for 事. Please note that 事 uses the reading こと, which you didn''t learn with the kanji, but you have come across it once or twice by now, and because of the way rendaku works, it''s changed to the voiced ごと.'),
(3501, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it probably uses the kun''yomi reading. You did not learn this reading, so here''s a mnemonic to help you to remember: Just think of the first part of the English word <vocabulary>name</vocabulary>. What is it? It''s <reading>na</reading> (な), just like this word!'),
(3502, 'The readings are kun''yomi, but you should be familiar with 負ける and of course 犬 well enough to learn the reading here.'),
(3503, 'The reading for 待ち comes right from 待つ. You know the drill by now.'),
(3504, 'The reading comes from 勝つ. So as long as you have some understanding of that reading, you should be able to read this one as well. '),
(3505, 'The reading is the same as 決定 with する added. Make sure you know 決定 and you''ll know this word as well.'),
(3506, 'The reading is that of the word 出る and the word 会う, both of which you''ve hopefully seen. The 出 portion may be confusing, but you can usually assume that if one part of the word is kun''yomi / vocab reading, the other part is too, which is why 出 uses the vocab reading.

Oh by the way, this word can be written like this too: 出合う. It’s not as common, but you may come across it every now and then, so it’s good to know! '),
(3507, 'The reading comes from 苦しい, so as long as you''re on top of that word, this word should be readable for you as well.'),
(3508, 'The readings come from the 気 kanji (which you know) and the word 付く which you should know by now. Combine these together and you have the ability to read this word. Take note that the 付く part is rendaku''d, though, so it is a tiny bit different.'),
(3509, 'The readings come from the vocab words 見る and 送る. Make sure you know those and you''ll know this word''s reading as well. '),
(3510, 'The reading is the vocab word 時 twice, with the second one getting rendaku''d.'),
(3511, 'For this reading you have to think back to the verb words that get used with this kanji, namely 受ける and 付ける. Then just drop off the るs and put them together. 受 becomes うけ and 付 becomes つけ and you''re left with うけつけ. This is what''s called a compound word. And now you know it. Nice!'),
(3512, 'The readings are the kun''yomi, which is usually the case when there''s hiragana in the middle. You learned the reading for 買い when you learned the kanji, as well as the word 買う, and you learned this reading of 物 in the word 物!'),
(3513, 'The reading is that of 苦い (にがい) plus regular old 手 (て). Remember, this is your bitter person, the person who''s "hard to deal with." So, it uses the strange にがい reading.'),
(3514, 'The readings are kun''yomi, but you should have seen both 白 and 黒い separately as vocabulary by now. It''s the same readings here, so you can apply that knowledge and read this word!'),
(3515, 'This word uses kun''yomi readings, as you can probably guess from the hiragana sticking out at the end. You should know the readings of these as separate words already (from お酒 and 好き), which means all you need to pay attention to is the rendaku: すき changes to ずき.'),
(3516, 'The readings are the vocab words お酒 and 飲む combined together into one. You can guess it''s a kun''yomi reading because of the み on the end of 飲, which should make you think back to vocab words that use the kun''yomi readings for these kanji. If you can do that, you can read this word (and many others, probably)!'),
(3517, 'The readings are those that you learned with the kanji. 声 gets changed to ごえ, though, so take note of that in your head. Perhaps you can imagine an animal that does a "GOEEEEE" cry.'),
(3518, 'These are both the kun''yomi readings. You''ve learned them already, so you already know how to read this word!'),
(3519, 'The reading comes from 聞く, so if you know the reading of that word, you can apply it here.'),
(3520, 'The reading is the reading of 心配 (which you''ve hopefully gotten down okay by now) and 事 using the (こと) reading. It is rendaku''d, as well, so beware of the ごと. (Just another 心配事, am I right?)'),
(3521, 'The reading for 音 is the 音読み. 読み uses the kun''yomi, though, strangely. Way to mess up the one word that should be all on''yomi, Japan. Way. To. Go.'),
(3522, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it should use the kun''yomi reading. You learned that reading with the kanji, but here''s a mnemonic refresher just in case:

You see a man walk straight toward the sun rising in the <kanji>east</kanji>. It''s so bright he can''t see anything, so he trips and <reading>he gash</reading>es (ひがし) his knee on a rock. Yikes… But he gets back up, determined to head east, and, oh no, he gashes his other knee! OK, he should probably give up on going east.'),
(3523, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

You have <vocabulary>to return</vocabulary> the <reading>Ca</reading>r <reading>e</reading>gg (かえ) you found on the side of the road. It will birth a little car, but it needs its mother. So, you try to figure out how to return it. Go ahead and think through that process in your mind. Also think about how weird a car egg would be.'),
(3524, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3525, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
