(3532, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it should use the kun''yomi reading. When learning the kanji, you didn''t learn that reading, so here''s a mnemonic to help you with this word:

You have this <vocabulary>container</vocabulary>. It''s upside down with a stick holding it up and a string on the stick. You''re going to catch some U2s (like, the band). You put some food under it and wait. Then, out of the bushes comes Bono. He sniffs around for a bit and then goes under the container. You pull! <reading>U2 wa</reading>lked (うつわ) right into the trap! Haha!'),
(3533, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3534, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. 

You''ve learned the reading for 器 with the kanji, and 楽 uses the secondary "music" reading you learned with 音楽. Just bear in mind that the がく shortens to がっ.'),
(3535, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Just be sure to note the shortened しょく.'),
(3536, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3537, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3538, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Just be careful of the しゅく changing to じゅく. Once again, will help if you''ve heard of Shinjuku.'),
(3539, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3540, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3541, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: Who are you begging to have pity on you? It''s <reading>NASA</reading>. They have you hanging outside of the space station and they''re going to push you towards earth where you''ll burn up in the atmosphere. You don''t want that, so you beg mission control for some <vocabulary>pity</vocabulary>. What will they do? You decide.'),
(3542, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3543, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3544, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3545, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You haven''t learned the on''yomi for 思 yet, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

All your <vocabulary>thought</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>idea</vocabulary>s are about <reading>shee</reading>p (し) right now. You just can''t stop thinking about sheep. Are you thinking about sheep right now? What kind of sheep are they?'),
(3546, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3547, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3548, 'The readings come from the 飲む vocabulary word and the regular kanji reading for 会. Hopefully you know both of those by now so you can apply them to the reading of this word, too!'),
(3549, 'The reading is all on''yomi, because it''s jukugo. You should know the on''yomi reading of 手 by now just by seeing similar word patterns to this one.'),
(3550, 'The reading for this one is a bit strange. You''ll have to pull from your knowledge of 落ちる and use that for the first half. Then, 葉 is rendaku''d to ば. Because, when a leaf falls, Scrooge says "BAH! Humbug!" (probably because he''s losing money).'),
(3551, 'The reading here is pretty weird. It''s all kun''yomi. My guess is this translates literally to "father parent" (two different words, therefore uses the other readings). You should know the readings of these two separate words, though. It is 父 (ちち) and 親 (おや).'),
(3552, 'The reading for this is strange. It''s the kun''yomi vocab reading, probably because the literal translation is "mother parent" and therefore consists of a couple words combined together into one concept. So, make sure you know the separate word readings of 母 and 親. If you do, you should be able to read this too.'),
(3553, 'Use your knowledge of the reading for 一番目 to remember the reading for this word as well.'),
(3554, 'The readings are just 見 and the vocab word 返る, combined together. So long as you know both of those you should be able to combine them into the reading for this word.'),
(3555, 'This uses the kun''yomi readings from the vocab words 話す and 方. So as long as you''re okay with those, you can apply that knowledge over here.'),
(3556, 'The reading comes from 転がる. Hopefully you know that one well enough to associate it with this one, meaning you can read them both.'),
(3557, 'The reading is the same as 集める so if you know that word you''ll know how to read this word as well.'),
(3558, 'The reading comes from 落ちる. So long as you know that word you can apply that reading over here, too.'),
(3559, 'The reading comes from 終わる. So long as you know that word, you can learn this word''s reading as well!'),
(3560, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3561, 'Do you remember the reading for 開ける, which you learned in level 10? That''s right, it''s あける! So if you know the reading of that word (I hope you do!) then you can apply that knowledge here.

Just a quick side note — 開く can also be read as ひらく, but we''re going to focus on the あく reading here. Since ひらく has slightly different meanings, we won''t accept it as a reading for this word, so be sure to enter あく!
'),
(3562, 'The reading is the same as what you learned with the kanji and the same as 起きる too, meaning you should be able to put together the reading on your own.'),
(3563, 'The reading comes straight from 心配 so try to make sure you know that word so you can read this word! It''s also all jukugo on''yomi reading, too, so you should be able to figure it out regardless.'),
(3564, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Take note that the ぱい in 配 got rendaku''d from はい, as well.'),
(3565, 'The reading can come from 鳴く. So long as you know the reading for that word you can know the reading for this word as well.'),
(3566, 'Awesomely, this uses the same reading as the reading for the vocab words 歌. Just throw the う onto the end and you have yourself the reading. So, sing your heart out and learn this word easily. "IIIIiiIIIiiiII WILLL ALLllwaYYYs Loovvve YoOooOOooooOOOO ♬!"'),
(3567, 'The reading for this is weird. It''s all kun''yomi, which luckily you''ve seen before, but still strange. I guess when you do <vocabulary>as one pleases</vocabulary> you can read kanji however you want, in this case the kun''yomi readings. Do note that there''s a small っ in there before 手, presumably to connect the two readings of the two kanji together.'),
(3568, 'This word uses the kun''yomi, which you didn''t learn with the kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you out. What are you beginning? You are beginning <reading>Haj</reading> (はじ). (Haj is the fifth pillar of Islam, and is a pilgrimage to Mecca during the month of Dhu al-Hijja).'),
(3569, 'This word uses the kun''yomi, which you didn''t learn with the kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you out. What are you beginning? You are beginning <reading>Haj</reading> (はじ). (Haj is the fifth pillar of Islam, and is a pilgrimage to Mecca during the month of Dhu al-Hijja).'),
(3570, 'The readings are kun''yomi readings for both, probably because this is two words put together into one. You know the reading of 風 from previous levels, hopefully. Now all you need to do is apply your knowledge of the word 神 to be able to read the rest.'),
(3571, 'Both of these are the kun''yomi readings that you''ve already learned! The thing is, the た from 伝''s つた changes into だ for who knows what reason. Perhaps you can ask for someone <vocabulary>to help</vocabulary> you to understand this dumb reading change.'),
(3572, 'The readings are the kun''yomi vocab readings, so combine the readings of the words 左 and 利く together to get the reading of this one. It makes sense since this word is like two words put together.'),
(3573, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3574, 'Both kanji use the kun''yomi vocab readings. So, think to the words 右 and 利く to learn how to read this one. It probably reads like this because it''s two separate words come together (right + handed) rather than one word.'),
(3575, 'The reading is a strange one, different from anything you''ve learned, kanji on''yomi or vocab kun''yomi. The reading is この, so think of this: 

What’s something easy <vocabulary>to be fond of</vocabulary>? <reading>子</reading>? <reading>No</reading> (この), not for you. You’re always saying “子? No!” Most people are fond of kids, at least their own, but not you. Noisy, germy, needy 子? No, no thank you!'),
(3576, 'The reading comes from 育つ. So long as you know that one, you know how to read this one as well.'),
(3577, 'The readings are the ones you learned with the kanji, but you can also just notice that this word includes 追う and 付く combined.'),
(3578, 'The reading comes from 戦い a word you''ve hopefully seen by now. Associate these words together in your mind and you''ll be able to read them so long as you know the other one.'),
(3579, 'The reading comes from 追う as well as the kanji reading you learned too.'),
(3580, 'If you know the reading of 注文 you should be able to read this one too. Also, it''s just all jukugo on''yomi readings, so you should be able to read it anyways.'),
(3581, 'The reading comes from 好き which you''ve hopefully seen. Then, 大 is the reading you learned with the kanji.'),
(3582, 'Since this word has okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) you know that it''s probably going to be the kun''yomi reading.

Lucky for you, the kanji readings for transitive and intransitive verbs are almost always the same. Once you learn one, you know them both! This reading is the same as 伝える.'),
(3583, 'The reading is the same one you learned with 放す. If you can remember that word, you can apply the reading over here as well.'),
(3584, 'Remember how you learned 待合 already? Well throw the on''yomi for 室 on the end of that and you have your reading!'),
(3585, 'If you can read 感じる, you should be able to read this too. It uses the same reading — just without る.'),
(3586, 'Guess what? Even though this would use the vocab kun''yomi reading, usually, the readings for both on''yomi and kun''yomi on this kanji are the same, meaning you can read this word no matter what. Feeling better now?'),
(3587, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3588, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3589, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3590, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3591, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3592, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3593, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3594, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

Your reflection is getting reflected onto the mirror. You don''t like it, it doesn''t look like you. Impostor reflection! You take a hammer and you <reading>打つ</reading> (うつ) it as hard as you can, glass flying everywhere. Then some dubstep starts playing from the broken mirror: <reading>うつ</reading>うつうつうつ. 

You might recall that this reading is the same as <reading>写</reading>る and might wonder if they''re related. They are! They both talk about the image of something showing up somewhere else, and use different kanji to distinguish where. In 写る''s case, that would typically be in photographs, and for 映る, it''s in reflections or projections (which includes the contents of screens, like those of TVs or computers). '),
(3595, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3596, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3597, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 
You''re thrown into something <vocabulary>dark</vocabulary> and small. A lid is closed on top of you, and you are confused. What have you been thrown in? You find out you''ve been thrown in a <reading>cooler</reading> (くら). Feel afraid like you would be if you were thrown in such a dark cooler like this. Is it cold?'),
(3598, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3599, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3600, 'The reading is the same as what you learned with the kanji.'),
(3601, 'The reading is just the reading you learned with the kanji. The second one is ざま however, which plays right into the "various" meaning of the word. There''s even various ways to read さま in this word!'),
(3602, 'The reading for both kanji here are the same as the ones you learned with their kanji. Everyone can breathe easier now.'),
(3603, 'You''ve had the on''yomi reading of 目 with 目次 way back in level 6. For this vocab, you''re trying to get to your <vocabulary>goal</vocabulary>, that signpost in the distance. As you''re trekking towards it there are people standing to the side <reading>mock</reading>ing you, telling you you''ll never make it to your goal. Imagine this happening.'),
(3604, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji! No need to burn any 橋s with this word and its kanji.'),
(3605, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: You <vocabulary>kill</vocabulary> someone! Oh no, what are you going to do? Where will you put the body? You decide to stuff it into the <reading>core</reading> (ころ) of a nuclear silo. It will melt and disappear in there, right? At least, that''s what you think. Imagine opening up the core of the nuclear silo and feel the burn of the radiation. You throw the body in and close the core, then run away. Unfortunately, this nuclear core will kill you too, eventually. Then, it will kill everyone in the area, because the body you threw in will overheat the core, causing a giant explosion.'),
(3606, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3607, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3608, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3609, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3610, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Wait! Except 自 is し and not じ! That is because when you add the little dakuten you are adding to the し. When it has no dakuten, it is in the form that <vocabulary>nature</vocabulary> intended.'),
(3611, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3612, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji, so don''t get all feverish over this one.'),
(3613, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: This has the same reading as 暑い, so if you''ve learned that you''ll know how to read this word as well. If you haven''t run into that word, you''ve probably run into the kanji 暑 at least. That uses the reading あつ. Two similar concepts, but there are two kanji to differentiate, which gets confusing. '),
(3614, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Be careful of the ねっ portion (normally is ねつ).'),
(3615, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(3616, 'The readings are the readings you learned with the kanji. If one thing is lucky, it''s probably that.'),
(3617, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(3618, 'The reading is the readings you learned with the kanji, combined!'),
(3619, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3620, 'The reading is the same as the kanji as well! You are so happy that you could write a poem all about this...

The vocab reading
Is the same as the kanji
Crabigator love

How was that?'),
(3621, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3622, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You know these readings, but there''s usually an extra い in the middle. Don''t worry, しか is totally fine too, but しいか is more common. You got this!'),
(3623, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3624, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: When you say you''re sorry <vocabulary>to apologize</vocabulary>, you''d probably start with: "<reading>I am</reading> (あやま) sorry!"'),
(3625, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3626, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3627, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it should use the kun''yomi reading. When learning the kanji, you didn''t learn that reading, so here''s a mnemonic to help you with this word: You put on the <vocabulary>ring</vocabulary> you found, hoping it will be the one ring to rule them all (invisibility!). Instead, it turns out it''s a special <reading>Wa</reading>lken(わ) ring, made out of Christopher Walken''s bones. It talks up to you, telling you how he kept this ring in his (*ahem*) butt during the war so he could get it to you. First of all, why is this ring talking? Second, why is it talking like Walken. Third, eww, gross, it was in his anus.'),
(3628, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

You have the opportunity <vocabulary>to choose</vocabulary>. You choose, and a bright sign lights up reading "<reading>error</reading>" (えら).'),
(3629, 'The reading for 選 is what you''d expect. 手 is the on''yomi reading as it is every once in a while, especially when it has to do with representing a person, so you''ll have to remember that. I think you''ve seen 手 using this reading enough to be able to remember it, though.'),
(3630, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3631, 'The reading is the same as the kanji. '),
(3632, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3633, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3634, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(3635, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3636, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3637, 'You ask for help by saying <vocabulary>please help me</vocabulary>, and that person does try to help you... but the amount they do for you is so <reading>neg</reading>ligible that it hardly does anything to help you at all. Please try to help me again, this time better?'),
(3638, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3639, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

When you were a kid, your parent or guardian did their best <vocabulary>to foster</vocabulary> discipline as they tried <vocabulary>to raise</vocabulary> you. Why? Because of <reading>ya shena</reading>nigans (やしな)! Oh, they tried to cultivate discipline in you, alright — till ya shenanigans were no more.'),
(3640, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3641, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3642, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.

For you advanced learners out there, you might want to know that saying that this word like Kyoko gets you the <vocabulary>main building</vocabulary> meaning, while saying it like Kenichi gets you the <vocabulary>this building</vocabulary> meaning! Isn''t pitch accent fun? But you''ll probably never have to worry about this — context clues will be enough to tell you which meaning is being used. '),
(3643, 'The kanji here uses えき as both its on''yomi and kun''yomi, so no worries here. You can go to this station or that station, and both stations will use the same reading as the kanji it comes from.'),
(3644, 'This is like two separate words put together into one, so you can imagine it''s the kun''yomi readings. You learned the right reading for 駅 in the kanji, and you should know the vocab word 前 by now. Combine the two together and you have yourself <vocabulary>front of station</vocabulary>.'),
(3645, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3646, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3647, 'The reading is the same as with the kanji you learned, so you should feel nice and "at home" with this one 😉'),
(3648, 'The readings are double kun''yomi. The kun''yomi reading is what you learned with 里 already, though you''ll have to pull out the 心 vocab reading to remember it all. Not only that, the 心 gets rendaku''d here making it ごころ. Make sure you watch out for that.'),
(3649, 'The reading for 都 is an exception from what you learned with the kanji. To remember this, just think about someone who does something at their own convenience. They say, I''m going <reading>to go</reading> (つごう) where I want, when I want, at my own convenience.

The 合 (ごう) part is a reading you know from the kanji, though, so if you can get to "to go" with the mnemonic, you should be able to put two and two together to know that it''s a long vowel ごう.'),
(3650, 'The reading comes from 消す, so make sure you know that word so you can read this one!'),
(3651, 'The reading itself has a lot of 悪気 with all the exceptions it has. The 悪 part is read like 悪い. Then, 気 is rendaku''d. What an evil reading. Just think "what''s the most evil reading I can come up with?" when you see this word. Then you should hopefully be able to read it.'),
(3652, 'The reading comes from combining 山 and 登る together to form one word, 山登り. If you know the readings of those two words you know the reading of this word too.'),
(3653, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3654, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3655, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3656, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3657, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: Using all kinds of "<vocabulary>for examples</vocabulary>" like this, you were <reading>taught</reading> (たと) many things throughout your life. For example, you were taught about...'),
(3658, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3659, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3660, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3661, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3662, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: You get <vocabulary>to come</vocabulary> to participate! "It''s <reading>my</reading> (まい) lucky day!" you exclaim.'),
(3663, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Note that the いち becomes いっ as it often does when being used in a counter (一階, 一回, etc.).'),
(3664, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3665, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3666, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

People <vocabulary>surround</vocabulary> you. They circle around you, calling you a chicken. "<reading>Cacko</reading>! (かこ) Cacko! Cacko!" Wait, this isn''t the sound a chicken makes, right? You laugh at them, thinking their way of making a chicken noise is weird. Go ahead and join in with them and say "Cacko" with them as well.'),
(3667, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.

Note: You''ll sometimes hear people say ふいんき, but if you wrote that on a test you''d be marked wrong, so we''re sticking with the more standard ふんいき reading. If you need help remembering it, just look a little closer at the kanji — you''ll know 雰 is ふん because it gets its reading from the little 分 inside it!'),
(3668, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

You need something really <vocabulary>hard</vocabulary> to put in your <reading>cata</reading>pult(かた). Look around you for something hard and imagine putting it in your catapult!'),
(3669, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3670, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3671, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Note that 四 is read using the on''yomi reading, which you''ve seen a few times over the course of WaniKani. Think of し meaning "death" (like 死), and how with the four seasons there comes death every Fall and Winter.'),
(3672, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
