(3788, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3789, 'The reading for this is pretty weird. 計 is all nice and normal, but 時 is と for some reason. It probably is coming from the vocab word 時 (とき), is my guess. But even if it is, it''s shortened, making it a step more complicated. Maybe it''s just better to imagine that your <vocabulary>watch</vocabulary> is a walky <reading>talky</reading> (とけい) and run with that?'),
(3790, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(3791, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. The reading for 画 is the secondary reading though (かく), so take note of that.'),
(3792, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3793, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3794, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3795, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it should use the kun''yomi reading. When learning the kanji, you didn''t learn that reading, so here''s a mnemonic to help you with this word: You''re eating a <vocabulary>meal</vocabulary>... MMmm, delicious! What are you eating? Well, it''s a <reading>Mesh</reading> (めし) net. Taste the mesh net. It tastes terrible. This is a bad meal. Feel it in your mouth. It''s weird and meshy.'),
(3796, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji. Now how about you "<reading>han</reading>d" me another bowl of rice?'),
(3797, 'If you''re going <vocabulary>to be sad about</vocabulary> something right now, it will be <reading>kana</reading> (かな). Don''t use kana when you''re trying to learn kanji! You''ll be sad forever if you''re stuck using kana your whole life, so don''t let that habit start now, friend.'),
(3798, 'The readings come from 落ちる and 着く. Hopefully you know these words so you can read this word as well! If you don''t, just 落ち着く and everything will be okay.'),
(3799, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3800, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. It''s exactly the same as the reading for 関わる, so here''s a reminder of that mnemonic to help you:

Remember the "<reading>Kaw Kaw</reading> (かか) Crow Gang"? They are bad news, always eating from garbage and squawking "kaw kaw"... You don''t want <vocabulary>to be involved</vocabulary> with them.'),
(3801, 'The reading doesn''t match up for this word''s 守. The reading is しゅ, but you can remember that because the conservatives want to protect and preserve their <reading>hoe</reading>s (ほ) and their <reading>chute</reading>s (しゅ) from the government. For some reason those get taxed a lot.'),
(3802, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3803, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(3804, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3805, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3806, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(3807, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

If you want to learn how <vocabulary>to preserve something</vocabulary> like fruits and veggies, you should start with <reading>tamots</reading> (たも), that''s slang for delish tomatoes. Once they''re preserved, you get to make everyone you love eat those yummy tamots. Mmm!'),
(3808, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3809, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3810, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Note that ぶつ gets shortened to ぶっ.'),
(3811, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3812, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3813, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3814, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3815, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3816, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: Someone is in danger! It''s poor old <reading>abe</reading> (あぶ) Lincoln. You try to save him, yelling out 危ないよ！！！！, but he gets shot in the back of the head anyways.'),
(3817, 'Since this word has okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) you know that it''s probably going to be the kun''yomi reading. You already know this kun''yomi reading, so you should be able to read this word.'),
(3818, 'You haven''t seen this reading for 下 yet (sorry there''s so many for this kanji!) so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

Those items are so <vocabulary>crude</vocabulary> that you can''t even tell what they''re supposed to be. Honestly they all just look like statues of lumpy <reading>ge</reading>ckos (げ). That says it''s a lamp but it looks more like a gecko looking at the sky. That chair? Definitely a bunch of geckos having a pool party. Are you sure you didn''t get this stuff from a weird gecko artist?'),
(3819, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3820, 'When this kanji is used as a single-character vocabulary word, it usually has the kun''yomi reading しな. You haven''t seen the しな reading yet, so here''s a mnemonic to help you remember it:

Your favorite <vocabulary>article</vocabulary>s and <vocabulary>item</vocabulary>s all have a special <reading>sheen</reading> (しな) that only you can see. Maybe it''s because of all the memories and emotions you attach to those items, but they all seem to glow with a sheen that makes you feel warm and fuzzy inside when you look at them.

Keep in mind this will have the on''yomi reading ひん when it''s used like a suffix at the end of some compounds, like ブランド品 (a brand-name item). But しな will be your go-to reading when it''s a standalone vocab word used to talk about goods, articles, and items!'),
(3821, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Notice though that ひん changes to ぴん. This is because you''re buying some <vocabulary>brand new</vocabulary> pins?'),
(3822, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3823, 'You start <vocabulary>to rejoice</vocabulary> by saying, "<reading>Yo, rock on</reading> (よろこ)!" Now you know how to rejoice from now on. At church or temple? "Yo, rock on!" Perfect.'),
(3824, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3825, 'This word uses the kun''yomi reading, which you didn''t learn the kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

Which <vocabulary>garden</vocabulary> is the Garden of Eden? <reading>その</reading> garden! That garden right next to you is the Garden of Eden!

It can also use the on''yomi, though, which you''ve already learned. When it takes the on''yomi and is used as a standalone word, it usually refers to some kind of institution, like a 幼稚園.'),
(3826, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji. If you remember the kanji reading, you''ll have no problem remembering this vocabulary reading as well!'),
(3827, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3828, 'You need <vocabulary>to protect</vocabulary> against breast cancer, so you get yourself a <reading>mammo</reading>gram (まも). '),
(3829, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3830, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3831, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3832, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3833, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

On your quest for true <vocabulary>happiness</vocabulary>, you meet a happiness guru. <reading>She always says</reading> (しあわせ), "just be happy!" She always says that, and you always listen. You can''t believe how simple yet profound her approach is! 
'),
(3834, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3835, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3836, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. Should be no surprises here, especially if you remember how to read 東京.'),
(3837, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji. Because it''s normally attached to other words it uses the on''yomi reading like a jukugo word normally would.'),
(3838, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: Someone who is trying <vocabulary>to hurry</vocabulary> is someone who is not at <reading>ease</reading> (いそ).'),
(3839, 'The reading is the same as what you learned with the kanji. <vocabulary>Suddenly</vocabulary> you can read a new word!'),
(3840, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3841, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3842, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3843, 'The reading you learned with the kanji is the on''yomi one, but this word is read with the kun''yomi, か. So just be sure to remember the kun''yomi reading to read this word in the <reading>ca</reading>r (か) ... or <vocabulary>to write</vocabulary> it, or whatever.'),
(3844, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Note that ひん changes to ぴん.'),
(3845, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

I need you <vocabulary>to heal</vocabulary> him, and I need you to heal him right <reading>now</reading> (なお). NOW NOW NOW! 

This also shares the reading for 直す, a very similar word you learned previously, which means "to fix." I''m guessing that the Japanese word なおす was only one word ("to fix" and "to cure" are pretty similar, right?). Then, there were two Chinese words for it (as you can see with the kanji), so the one Japanese word got split into two very similar words.'),
(3846, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3847, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3848, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You didn''t learn the on''yomi for 明 (めい) with the kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you remember it:

The <vocabulary>Meiji era began</vocabulary> with a lot of changes — and by <reading>May</reading> (めい), the new Japan was already shaped.'),
(3849, 'You want to know the best way <vocabulary>to bathe in</vocabulary> something? With a bunch of <reading>a</reading>ardvarks (あ) in the water. They use their sticky little tongues to clean all your cracks and crannies.'),
(3850, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(3851, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Just note that the とく goes to とっ.'),
(3852, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. The reading for 留 is the りゅう version. You can remember this because <reading>Ryu</reading> (りゅう) from street fighter is doing a study abroad program to learn more karate.'),
(3853, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3854, 'The reading for 真 is the kun''yomi reading, which you haven''t learned yet. Think of what is pitch black. It''s your poor <reading>Ma</reading> (ま). She is so dark that she sucks in light, like a black hole... wait, she is a black hole, it explains why your Ma is pitch black.'),
(3855, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3856, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji. You can laugh freely, now!'),
(3857, 'The reading for this word won''t make you <vocabulary>smile</vocabulary>, though. You were smiling, but then you heard about the reading of this kanji. It made you yell out "<reading>EH</reading>??" (え). Just watch out for the rendaku of かお to がお.'),
(3858, 'The reading is just the kun''yomi reading, the one you learned with the kanji. Be sure to pay attention to the rendaku though. はこ changes to ばこ.'),
(3859, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(3860, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You''ll need to remember the 物 reading as もつ, though. Why? Because all your luggage just got thrown into various <reading>moats</reading> along the countryside. It''s going to take you forever to collect your luggage from these moats and by then it''ll be all soggy. Dangit...'),
(3861, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it should use the kun''yomi reading. When learning the kanji, you didn''t learn that reading, so here''s a mnemonic to help you with this word: 
Your relentless search for <vocabulary>evidence</vocabulary> has finally uncovered the existence of <reading>a</reading> <reading>car</reading> <reading>shee</reading>p (あかし)! It''s a bizarre hybrid, having a car''s body but a sheep''s head and legs. "Ah, car sheep! I''ve finally found the evidence proving you''re real!"'),
(3862, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3863, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3864, 'The readings are a bit weird here. The 私 is わたし, but the 自 and 身 use the readings you learned with the kanji. Just imagine the 私 saying to the other two, "I myself will use my vocab reading, but you two should use your kanji readings — got it?"'),
(3865, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3866, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: "Hey, you going <vocabulary>to quit</vocabulary>?" "<reading>Yah</reading> (や) man, I''m going to quit."'),
(3867, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. Just watch out, as the 世 gets shortened from せい to せ! You can memorize this by remembering to be short and sweet with your flattery, don''t make it too long and overcomplicated!'),
(3868, 'You want to go <vocabulary>far</vocabulary>, <vocabulary>far away</vocabulary> when you see a <reading>toe... oh</reading> (とお)! You realize there is a zombie toe on the ground and it reminds you of where you can go that''s far from here—where the zombie that once had this toe went! (Remember the kanji mnemonic? It''s that scooter zombie''s toe that makes you say oh and want to go.)'),
(3869, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3870, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(3871, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3872, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3873, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3874, 'The reading for 面 is what you''d expect. 地 on the other hand is じ, which is a bit different (though close sounding) to what you learned previously. Thank the Chinese and their ever changing language for this one. Think of <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ) laying on the ground, telling you how comfortable the ground is. You say "Jesus, you have to get off the ground, you have a dinner to go to," but he doesn''t want to leave.'),
(3875, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji, so this is がいめん. But note that this can also use the kun’yomi readings, which makes it そとづら.

While がいめん is neutral, そとづら is a bit negative, often implying that the person''s outward appearance might be deceptive, hiding their true nature.'),
(3876, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3877, 'The reading is the same as what you learned with the kanji.'),
(3878, 'The reading is unlike what you''ve learned before, at least with the 面 part (白い is just like the vocab word 白い). When you look at the white face of the object you say to your friend Moe: "<reading>Oh mo</reading>!" (おも) because you want him to come over and look at this. Try to imagine someone you know named Moe, or at least a celebrity or something named Moe.'),
(3879, 'The 書館 part should be readable by you, but it''s the 図 that''s troublesome with an alternate reading from the one you learned. Imagine yourself at the library. You check out every single book you can about the human <reading>toe</reading> (と). You have a giant stack of books on it, then you drop it... ironically on your toe. You decide to <reading>show Khan</reading> (your buddy) your smashed toe.'),
(3880, 'If you know 消化 you should be able to read this. Also, it uses the readings you learned with the kanji anyways, so no problemo there.'),
(3881, 'The reading is different from 消す, unfortunately, so you can''t use that. Just think about how after the object in front of you disappears you use a <reading>key</reading> (き) to open an invisible door. That''s where everything''s disappearing to! You see all your disappeared stuff on the other side of this invisible door which you opened with a magical <reading>key</reading>.'),
(3882, 'The readings come from the words 気持ち and 悪い. Hopefully you''ve seen both and can combine them together to know the reading automatically. No bad feelings with this word, right?'),
(3883, 'The reading is the same as 流す. So long as you know that you should be able to read this one as well!'),
(3884, 'The reading for this can come from 動く. If you know that word, you know how to read this word!'),
(3885, 'The reading is the same as 着く''s reading, but it might be easier to think it shares a reading with 付ける, which has a similar meaning. Put on clothes, attach on clothes, etc.'),
(3886, 'The reading is the same as 勉強. It''s also the readings you learned with these kanji.'),
(3887, 'The reading is the same as 旅行, but it also happens to use the reading you learned with the kanji anyways, so you should be able to read it.'),
(3888, 'The kanji reading is the same as 温かい. As long as you know that reading, you should be able to associate it with this word.

Just like you can say あったかい for 温かい, people often say あっためる in conversation. The "official" reading is still あたためる, so when typing out the kanji or taking a test, try to stick to あたためる.'),
(3889, 'The reading is the same as 起きる, so as long as you know that word you should be able to read this one. Of course, you can also imagine them yelling "OH!" when you shake them awake too, if that helps.'),
(3890, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. It will help if you know the reading of 旅行 already.'),
(3891, 'The reading is the kun''yomi vocab readings for these two kanji, probably because this word is just two words put together into one (bad + mouth). Use your knowledge of the vocab words 悪い and 口 to learn the reading of this word.

Okay, I lied. You should actually do わるぐち instead. わるくち is possible, but the rendaku version is way more common. Sorry to say such bad things before.'),
(3892, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(3893, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Just keep in mind that the reading of 不 changes from ふ to ぶ. If you''re clumsy you''ll get a lot of <reading>boo</reading>-boos (ぶ), get it?'),
(3894, 'The reading is the same like お願いします. Hopefully you''re able to read that by now so you''re able to read this too.'),
(3895, 'The reading comes from the word 放題 (ほうだい) and 飲む (のむ). So long as you know those vocab words, you''ll be able to read this one as well.'),
(3896, 'The reading consists of the readings of 食べる and 放題. If you know those you should be able to read this too.'),
(3897, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You''ve learned them already, so you should be able to read this on your own!'),
(3898, 'The readings are the kun''yomi vocab readings. So, if you know 人''s reading as a vocab word and 殺す''s reading, you should be able to read this word. Just note that ころし goes to ごろし due to rendaku.'),
(3899, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3900, 'The readings come from the individual vocab readings, 指 and 輪. In English we don''t distinguish some other ring from a "finger ring" but in Japanese, you can think of this as two separate words coming together to form one word, which is why it uses the kun''yomi vocab readings like this. So, if you know the individual words, you should be able to figure out this word is read as <reading>ゆびわ</reading>.'),
(3901, 'The reading for 輪 is the kun''yomi vocab reading, which is わ, making this word <reading>わ</reading>ゴム.'),
(3902, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3903, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3904, 'The reading comes from 暗記. It also is the reading you learned with the kanji too.'),
(3905, 'The reading comes from the kanji as well as from the vocab word 練習. You should be able to read this!'),
(3906, 'The reading is the same as 映る. So long as you know the reading of 映る you should be able to read this one too.'),
(3907, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3908, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3909, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3910, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. It will help to know the reading of 自動 as well.'),
(3911, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Knowing how to read the word 基本 will be helpful too.'),
(3912, 'The readings are both kun''yomi vocab readings. You should have seen 折る by now which should help you to read 折り. Then there''s 目, which I''m guessing you won''t have a problem with.'),
(3913, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3914, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. It will help if you know how to read 卒業 already as a word, too.'),
(3915, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. It also will help if you can read 完全.'),
(3916, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3917, 'The reading comes from 折る and the vocab word 紙. As long as you know those you should be able to read this. Also, you probably already know this reading because it''s an English word, too.'),
(3918, 'If you know the readings of the words 私立 and 大学 you''ll be able to read this. It''s also all jukugo on''yomi readings too, so you can read it that way.'),
(3919, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3920, 'The reading comes from 希望. It''s also the on''yomi jukugo reading as well, if that helps any.'),
(3921, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3922, 'The reading is the same as the vocab word 妥協, so if you know that you should be good (also, just the reading of the kanji works too).'),
(3923, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3924, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3925, 'The reading is just the vocab word 要求. It''s also the regular on''yomi jukugo reading.'),
(3926, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3927, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Knowing 消化 and 不良 will help as well.'),
(3928, 'If you know the reading for 合格, all you have to do is add 不 to it! You''ve learned all these readings, yay!'),
(3929, 'The reading is just the same as the vocab word 卒業.'),
(3930, 'The reading for this word can come from level 14 item 残る. Associate the two in your mind and you''ll be able to read both.'),
(3931, 'Since the reading for 固 (かた) is different than the one you learned with the kanji, here''s a mnemonic to help you:

Why would you want <vocabulary>to make something hard</vocabulary>? Obviously because you want to put it in a <reading>cata</reading>pult (かた). There''s no point putting soft things in catapults, after all.'),
(3932, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3933, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. It will also help if you know how to read 建築 already.'),
(3934, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. It will also help if you know how to read 紀元後 already, because then you''ll just have to remember the 前.'),
(3935, 'The readings are the ones you learned with the kanji. It''s also like the vocab: 朝 is like the vocab version, あさ and ご飯 is like the word you learned, ごはん.'),
(3936, 'Often times, when two kanji come together to form a kanji compound (or jukugo), you use the on''yomi readings. This is similar to that, though instead of another kanji it''s a foreign word. This pattern works with almost any country as well. Just add 人 to a country name and you''ve described the people who live there. You''ll see more of these before you''re finished here.

And since 人 has two on''yomi readings, here''s a mnemonic to help you remember which one to use: 

As with all words for nationalities, the 人 here is pronounced じん. All nationalities, including <vocabulary>Brit</vocabulary>s, love <reading>jean</reading>s (じん)!'),
(3937, 'The readings come from 泣く and 虫. So long as you''re okay with those two words you should be able to read this one too.'),
(3938, 'The reading comes from the vocab words 泣く and 声. So long as you know those two words okay, you should be able to read this word too!'),
(3939, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3940, 'The readings come from the words 昼 and ご飯. If you know how to read those words you can read this word as well.'),
(3941, 'The readings come from the words お願い and 事. If you know how to read those you should be able to read this word too. Just remember that 事 is rendaku''d, so it''s read as ごと.'),
(3942, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3943, 'The reading is the same as the kanji too! What good fortune — you must have been praying to Benzaiten at the local お寺.'),
(3944, 'The reading comes from the word 期待, so make sure you know that to know this one as well.'),
(3945, 'The reading comes from 回転, so be sure to know that to know this one. Also, it''s just the readings of the kanji you learned too, so shouldn''t be too hard.'),
(3946, 'The reading comes from 回転 of level 10. Alternatively, you can just use the readings of the kanji as well. EZPZ.'),
(3947, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You didn''t learn the on''yomi for 明 (めい) with the kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you remember it:

You need to show me <vocabulary>proof</vocabulary> to make me believe in the month of <reading>May</reading> (めい). Does it even exist? It may or may not be real (that''s why it''s called May), so you have to <vocabulary>prove</vocabulary> it to me somehow.'),
(3948, 'This word uses the on''yomi reading for 曜 and the kun''yomi reading for 日. You''ve learned both of these already, but be careful because ひ of 日 changes to び here.'),
(3949, 'The reading comes from the vocab word 気持ち, so if you know that (or the vocab words 気 and 持つ) you should be A-okay on this one. <vocabulary>Feels good</vocabulary>, right?'),
(3950, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji! How... shall I say... <vocabulary>luck</vocabulary>-filled?'),
(3951, 'This is a jukugo word that uses on''yomi readings. The 西 on''yomi is usually going to be せい, which you''ve seen before, but in this word it''s さい. Here''s a mnemonic to help you remember that:

Ugh, tricky readings like the one for <vocabulary>Kansai</vocabulary> really make you want to <reading>sigh</reading> (さい).'),
(3952, 'The おおさか reading for this word is made up of the kun''yomi for each kanji. You''ve learned the おお reading (like in 大きい), but you haven''t seen the さか reading for 阪 yet. 

That said, doesn''t 阪 look a lot like 坂, which also means "slope"? Turns out they share the さか reading as well, so if you remember the word 坂 you can apply that reading here. (In fact, 大阪 was sometimes written as 大坂 in the old days.) The easiest mnemonic is probably just to think of the city of  <vocabulary>Osaka</vocabulary> though… Just don''t forget about the double おお.'),
(3953, 'The reading is all kun''yomi vocab reading. You should know the reading of the vocab word 右 by now. Also, the 側 reading happens to be the reading you learned with the kanji as well as with the word 側.'),
(3954, 'The reading for this is vocab kun''yomi readings. Just take the vocab 左 and add it to 側 to have yourself <reading>ひだりがわ</reading>.'),
(3955, 'The readings are the kun''yomi readings. With 側 you''ve already learned it. With 内 you''ve also already learned the reading. Combine them together and you have うちがわ. In case you''ve forgotten, here''s a mnemonic to help you remember that pesky うち portion. What is it that''s drawn you inside? "<reading>Oo, chee</reading>se!" (うち) Cheese has drawn you inside. You''re so excited that you exclaim, "Oo, cheese!"'),
(3956, 'This word uses the kun''yomi readings from the kanji. Since you learned the kun''yomi reading for 外 with the vocabulary, you should be just fine here.'),
(3957, 'The first kanji takes the on''yomi reading and the second takes the kun''yomi reading. Luckily you know both of them already!'),
(3958, 'This word uses the kun''yomi readings you first learned with each kanji. As long as you remember this is double kun''yomi, you''re good to go.'),
(3959, 'This word uses kun''yomi readings, both of which you happened to learn with the kanji. If you can remember these are kun''yomi here, you''ll be good to go! '),
(3960, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3961, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3962, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3963, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3964, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3965, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3966, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3967, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3968, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3969, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. The last 悪果 gets the 悪 shortened a little bit.'),
(3970, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3971, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji, no extra salt needed.'),
(3972, 'This word uses the kun''yomi readings, which you''ve seen with the vocab words 塩 and 水. Combine them together and you have しおみず.

This word can also be read with on''yomi readings, but that version is more formal. しおみず is usually for salty water in everyday contexts, like cooking or gargling. えんすい is more common in scientific contexts or for official categorization, like when referring to a saltwater lake.'),
(3973, 'This uses kun''yomi readings, which you''ve seen with the vocab words 塩 and 味. Combine them and you have yourself しおあじ.

This can also be read with on''yomi, and both しおあじ and えんみ can refer to salty flavor profiles. Traditionally, えんみ was more formal and reserved for culinary arts and cooking shows, but it''s catching on with the younger crowd and popping up more often in everyday conversation about food. For something like the salty flavor of potato chips, though, it''s always しおあじ.'),
(3974, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3975, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3976, 'The reading for this is actually the same as what you learned with the kanji, but the さつ is shortened to さっ. If you had <vocabulary>to guess</vocabulary> be sure to guess with the shorter つ.'),
(3977, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You know the 察 part, although it gets shortened to さっ here. You didn''t learn 知''s on''yomi when you learned the kanji, though, so here''s a mnemonic to help you with it:

One <vocabulary>inference</vocabulary> everyone can make is that you probably want to eat some <reading>chee</reading>se (ち). Seriously, it''s written all over your face. You can try to hide it, but everyone saw how hot and bothered you just got in the dairy section. It doesn''t take a mind-reader to <vocabulary>sense</vocabulary> how bad you''re jonesing for some cheese.'),
(3978, 'The reading is the same as the on you learned with the kanji. Have a seat and relax.'),
(3979, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Note that the けつ is shortened to けっ as つs often are when in a situation like this.'),
(3980, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Just note the しゅつ gets changed to しゅっ, as many つs do in this situation.'),
(3981, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3982, 'The reading is the kun''yomi reading, which you didn''t learn. In order to learn it, think of this:

I <vocabulary>always</vocabulary> <reading>tune</reading> (つね) my violin, but today I didn''t tune it. So, now I sound terrible. Imagine how terrible my untuned violin sounds, compared to how it always sounds.'),
(3983, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3984, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3985, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: Why do you need <vocabulary>to dry something</vocabulary>? Because someone used a <reading>hose</reading> (ほす) and got everything all wet. Ugh, you shouldn''t leave that hose out anymore...'),
(3986, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3987, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3988, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it should use the kun''yomi reading. When learning the kanji, you didn''t learn that reading, so here''s a mnemonic to help you with this word: You look up to see a crazy <vocabulary>illusion</vocabulary>. You can''t believe it''s real. Master Roshi, from Dragonball, is standing in front of you (you thought he was a cartoon!). Then, he splits into a million versions of himself, creating a <reading>mob of Roshi</reading>s (まぼろし). This mob goes on to train many people to fight.'),
(3989, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3990, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3991, 'This vocab uses the same reading as the one you learned with the kanji. Well played!'),
(3992, 'This word uses the on''yomi reading for the first kanji and the kun''yomi reading for the second kanji. You''ve learned them both already, so you should be able to read this too!'),
(3993, 'The readings are the readings you learned with the kanji. Just don''t forget the rendaku for 底!'),
(3994, 'The reading is shared with 建てる, so if you know 建てる then you should be able to read this word as well.'),
(3995, 'The reading might look a little strange, but makes sense once you get it. You already know that the verb 建てる means "to build", and that a もの is a "thing". Now just remove the る part from the verb to make it a noun, combine it with もの, and we get <reading>たてもの</reading>! Just remember that it’s all kun’yomi since it comes from the verb 建てる.'),
(3996, 'The reading is pretty strange. It''s a lot like 建物 in that it''s read as たて, which is like the word 建てる even though the て isn''t showing here. So, you''ll have to remember it''s read like 建てる but the て gets pushed into the kanji.'),
(3997, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it should use the kun''yomi reading. When learning the kanji, you didn''t learn that reading, so here''s a mnemonic to help you with this word: Your <vocabulary>love</vocabulary> isn''t toward a person, it''s toward this giant <reading>Koi</reading> (こい) fish you met the other day. The fish is just so... so... handsome! You get hearts popping above your head every time you think about this koi!'),
(3998, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(3999, 'The reading is the same as the kanji. Now that is true kanji <vocabulary>love</vocabulary>.'),
(4000, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4001, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4002, 'The readings are the ones you learned with the kanji, you self lover you.'),
(4003, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4004, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You didn''t learn 知''s on''yomi when you learned the kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

You know what they really love to know about in <vocabulary>Aichi Prefecture</vocabulary>? <reading>Chee</reading>se (ち). Those folks in Aichi, they''re some real cheese know-it-alls. Don''t ever bring one to a wine and cheese party, because, man — they will be rattling off cheese facts to you all evening.'),
(4005, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: You have been defeated. You lay there on the ground, and the fans around the stadium are disgusted with your performance. Some hipster walks up to you and says: "<reading>Yah, Boo</reading>" (やぶ) referring to how poorly you did. "No boo!", you reply back. "Yah boo", they say again.'),
(4006, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. The 失 gets shortened, and 敗 gets rendaku''d, though. Don''t make that 失敗.'),
(4007, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4008, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4009, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4010, 'The reading for this is an exception, at least for the 果 portion. To remember this part, imagine yourself asking for some <vocabulary>fruit</vocabulary>. <reading>Could I</reading> (くだ) have some fruit, please?'),
(4011, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Just note that the 結 gets shortened to けっ.'),
(4012, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: What do you flourish at? You''re an amazing <reading>soccer</reading> (さか) player who flourishes and prospers no matter where you go to play this wonderful (but not as good as baseball) sport.'),
(4013, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4014, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4015, 'The reading is the same as what you learned with the kanji. It''s also the same as the meaning of the kanji / vocab, though you can think of this as <vocabulary> Japanese plum</vocabulary> as well if you want.'),
(4016, 'The reading is just the readings of the kanji that you learned. How about a toast to easy to read words!'),
(4017, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4018, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. There are a couple possibilities for 力 though, so here''s a mnemonic to help you remember which to use: 

You know 力 uses its りょく reading here because you''re totally <vocabulary>powerless</vocabulary> when they <reading>lock</reading> (りょく) you up. Who''s "they"? I don''t know, but did you hear the click of the lock behind you? That means you''re powerless now.'),
(4019, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4020, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4021, 'One thing that''s not safe and sound is the reading of the 無 in this word. Instead of む it''s ぶ. Why? Because you thought it was safe and sound, but you were fooled. There are ghosts everywhere, all jumping out and screaming <reading>Boo!</reading> (ぶ) at you. Feel the surprise. You thought it was safe and sound!'),
(4022, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You didn''t learn 知''s on''yomi when you learned the kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

What''s the worst thing to live in <vocabulary>ignorance</vocabulary> of? Probably <reading>chee</reading>se (ち). Can you imagine not knowing cheddar from Swiss? Or gouda from brie? Imagine the sad, hollow life you would live if you were <vocabulary>ignorant</vocabulary> to the wonders of cheese.'),
(4023, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4024, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4025, 'If your garden is too <vocabulary>thin</vocabulary>, just get out the <reading>hose</reading> (ほそ)! How about if your hair is thin? Point the hose at your head! Hoses can cure any thin issues you may have.'),
(4026, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Note the けつ gets changed to けっ as つs in this situation often do.'),
(4027, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4028, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it should use the kun''yomi reading. When learning the kanji, you didn''t learn that reading, so here''s a mnemonic to help you with this word: You need some <vocabulary>medicine</vocabulary>, stat! But, nobody wants to give you any medicine. You keep asking your pharmacist, Yuri, if he''ll give you some medicine. He says no. You get pissed off, and you <reading>cuss yuri</reading> (くすり) out. He gives you the medicine after you cuss him out.'),
(4029, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
