(4046, 'The reading is the same as the kanji. Smells nice, right?'),
(4047, 'The reading is just like what you learned with the kanji. <reading>はな</reading>くそ.'),
(4048, 'The readings are the kun''yomi vocab readings. With 鼻 you learned that with the kanji. With 先 you learned that with the vocab. So, you should have all the pieces to be able to read this. Remember, body parts tend to change words into kun''yomi readings for some reason, no exception here.'),
(4049, 'The readings are both kun''yomi vocab readings, probably because a 鼻 is a body part, and body parts do that to jukugo words. You know 鼻''s reading from the kanji (はな) and you know 歌''s reading from the vocabulary word 歌 (うた). Combine them together and you have a very nice humming tune and reading, はなうた.'),
(4050, 'The readings are the kun''yomi vocab readings. You already know 鼻''s reading from the kanji and vocab, and you should know the vocab reading of 血 as well. Put them together and you have はなち, though you want to change the ち to ぢ. Alternatively, it''s possible (though less common, but it doesn''t matter if you''re turning it to kanji via an IME anyways) to write it as はなじ. I guess that blood added a little tenten (it represents the blood coming out of that nose!). Spurt spurt spurt.'),
(4051, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4052, 'If you know how to read 大阪 you can just add 弁 to it and be able to read the whole thing, though maybe not in <vocabulary>Osaka dialect</vocabulary>. Okay, fine, it''s probably the same thing in Osaka dialect.'),
(4053, 'This reading just combines the vocab 関西 with the kanji reading for 弁.'),
(4054, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Just watch out for the ひ becoming a び like it did in the word 曜日.'),
(4055, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Just watch out for the ひ becoming a び like it did in the word 曜日.'),
(4056, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. The 曜日 part is read just like the word 曜日, which you already learned, but 月 has two different on''yomi readings, so here''s a mnemonic to help you remember which one to use:

<vocabulary>Monday</vocabulary> is the day when a person <reading>gets</reading> (げつ) back to their regular weekday schedule. He or she gets out of bed, gets in the car, gets to work, and gets after that paycheck!'),
(4057, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Just watch out for the ひ becoming a び like it did in the word 曜日.'),
(4058, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Just watch out for the ひ becoming a び like it did in the word 曜日.'),
(4059, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4060, 'The reading is the same 笑う or 笑い. It''s also the same as what you learned with the kanji.'),
(4061, 'If you know 荷物 and you know 手, then you should be able to Voltron the two together into one mega reading.'),
(4062, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Knowing 証明''s reading will also be helpful.'),
(4063, 'The reading is the same as 笑う, so just make sure you know that so you can know this as well.'),
(4064, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4065, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: Dude, can I borrow your <reading>ca</reading>r (か)? Please?'),
(4066, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. The しゃく gets shortened to しゃっ though, so beware. Just think of that like the little bit that you lost to the yakuza, because they weren''t happy you didn''t pay back your debt. *cut*'),
(4067, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji, but in this case the second kanji can be read with either the on''yomi or kun''yomi. This uses a less common kun''yomi of 家, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

If you live in a <vocabulary>rented house</vocabulary>, someone has to give you the keys and let you in and stuff. You''re only renting, after all. And when they let you in they''ll say, "Here''s a <reading>shack</reading>, <reading>yah</reading> (しゃくや) house renter! Enjoy!"

By the way, in the secondary reading しゃっか, the しゃく changes to しゃっ. This reading is mostly used in legal contexts though, so しゃくや is more common.'),
(4068, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4069, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4070, 'The readings come from 切る and 取る. So, know those words separately and you can combine them into this word.'),
(4071, 'The readings come from the individual words 取る and 出す. Put them together and you should be able to read this word too.'),
(4072, 'The readings come from the words 取る and 分ける. So long as you know those words you should be able to read this one as well.'),
(4073, 'Since this word has okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) you know that it''s probably going to be the kun''yomi reading.

Lucky for you, the kanji readings for transitive and intransitive verbs are almost always the same. Once you learn one, you know them both! This reading is the same as 取る.'),
(4074, 'This word uses the kun''yomi reading for both kanji, which you might have guessed already since there''s hiragana sticking out of both of them. The 取り comes from 取る and the 決め comes from 決める. Put them together and what have you got? Bibbidi bobbidi とりきめ!'),
(4075, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4076, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4077, 'The reading for this word is screwed up, though if we''re lucky, you already know the word for cute. It seems to have injected itself into American and English culture a decent amount, anyways. If you are one of the lucky ones, you''ll know that this word is read as かわいい. If you''re not, think of the cute thing being a cow. Then you say, "that <reading>cowはいい</reading>" (かわいい), or "that cow is good" because it''s so cute.'),
(4078, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4079, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4080, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
