(4189, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

After you successfully manage <vocabulary>to put on shoes</vocabulary>, you exclaim, "<reading>Hah</reading> (は)! I knew I could do it!" Everyone doubted you could put on your shoes, but you did it — you really showed them. Hah! Now, the question is… will you be able <vocabulary>to put on pants</vocabulary>?'),
(4190, '若い uses the same reading you learned with the kanji, but the reading for 布 is totally irregular. If you can figure out the meaning, you can use that to remember the reading (since they''re the same), but here''s a mnemonic to help you just in case:

You''re eating <vocabulary>wakame</vocabulary> and you suddenly spot a <reading>目</reading> (め) looking up at you. You didn''t know seaweed had eyes!'),
(4191, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it should use the kun''yomi reading. When learning the kanji, you didn''t learn that reading, so here''s a mnemonic to help you with this word: You take your <kanji>cloth</kanji> and put it on top of your head. It''s there because right at <reading>noon</reading> (ぬの) the sun is in the middle of the sky and burns your scalp. So, you cover it with a cloth. Imagine yourself setting an alarm for noon to help you to focus on the noon part.'),
(4192, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4193, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. The reading for 団 is the secondary reading that went with the kanji, though, so watch out for that. If you can figure out the meaning of this word, chances are you already know how to spell it, so carry that over to Japanese.'),
(4194, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4195, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4196, 'Add the on’yomi reading for 徒 to the word you already know. Done!'),
(4197, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4198, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: You need <vocabulary>to acquire</vocabulary> an <reading>e</reading>lephant (え) for your elephant collection. If you don''t acquire an elephant, you''ll look like an idiot in front of your elephant collecting friends.'),
(4199, 'The reading for this is the same as with the kanji you learned. <reading>とく</reading>!'),
(4200, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4201, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. The 説 gets shortened to せっ.'),
(4202, 'You''re <vocabulary>busy</vocabulary> because you have to take care of your friend''s <reading>ice ogre</reading>s (いそが) this weekend. You don''t know why you agreed to this, you were busy enough, but now you''ll never get anything done running around taking care of these little ice ogres.'),
(4203, 'The reading is a bit strange. it''s the two separate words 恋 and 人 put together, making the reading <reading>こいびと</reading>. Oooh lala. Two words coming together like two people coming together. Rawrs.'),
(4204, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You might remember this reading from the transitive verb 改める, but here''s a mnemonic just in case: 

<vocabulary>To be renewed</vocabulary> means to be changed into something <reading>新た</reading> (あらた). Out with the old, in with the 新た, right? Picture yourself in a situation where there''s been a lot of change. Try to remember how it feels when everything is 新た. Are you nervous, excited, or do you usually move on without noticing?'),
(4205, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4206, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. It will help to know 失敗 on its own as well.'),
(4207, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. The 布 part gets changed to ぶ, probably because if a food doesn''t have <vocabulary>konbu</vocabulary> you say <reading>boo</reading> (ぶ).'),
(4208, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: Everything is so <vocabulary>easy</vocabulary> for you to do. Why? Because you don''t do anything yourself. You ask others. You say things like "Hey, can you get me some water?" They say "<reading>yassah!</reading>" (やさ) very enthusiastically.'),
(4209, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4210, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: You''re about <vocabulary>to rage</vocabulary> all over the place. You''re going nuts. You''re going <vocabulary>to act violently</vocabulary>. Then, a calm wizard steps in front of you and mutters "<reading>aba</reading>ra kadabara," (あば) causing you to calm down immediately.'),
(4211, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4212, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4213, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4214, 'The reading for this one, like the meaning, is kind of tricky. It uses two kun''yomi readings that you''ve learned before. So you have うめ plus ほし, which comes from 干す but gets rendaku''d to ぼし here. In other words, うめぼし. I guess the pickling process has mangled the reading of this word. But you''ll just have to deal with it, because this food is as common as it is tasty! 

Also, you might often see this written as 梅干, where the し gets shy or something and hides, but the reading stays the same: うめぼし.'),
(4215, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4216, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4217, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: Look at this <reading>cooler</reading> (くら). Now look at this other cooler. We need <vocabulary>to  compare</vocabulary> the two coolers. Think about all the features each one has and really compare, like you have a list or something.'),
(4218, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4219, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4220, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

The person who taught you how <vocabulary>to mix something</vocabulary> is your <reading>ma</reading> (ま). Your ma is always mixing tasty things: stew, curry, cake batter — even the occasional cocktail. She''s an expert mixer, and she passed her mixing skills on to you.

You can also use the level 5 vocab 交ぜる to help you remember, as it has the same meaning and reading!'),
(4221, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

Who always wants things <vocabulary>to get mixed</vocabulary> well? Your <reading>ma</reading> (ま)! Whether it''s her soup or the colors she paints with, everything she touches ends up getting mixed well and coming together nicely.'),
(4222, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: Your status is <vocabulary>to be decreased</vocabulary> amongst the eyes of the Gods. They decide your punishment is to be decreased into <reading>hell</reading> (へる) where you will live the rest of your days.'),
(4223, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Note that the ほう changes to ぽう. This is because you''re trying to subtract <reading>Po</reading>lar (ぽう) bears from the area around you as they''re very dangerous and you use a cologne made from meat.'),
(4224, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Just note that せつ gets shortened to せっ just like many つs do in this situation.'),
(4225, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

There''s an accident you happen <vocabulary>to be involved with</vocabulary> in <reading>から</reading>て class, where it''s common <vocabulary>to get tangled</vocabulary> up during sparring. This time you and your partner''s limbs are literally in a big entangled mess. Good luck getting out of that.'),
(4226, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: <reading>Two du</reading>des (つづ) get <vocabulary>to continue</vocabulary> on. I''m afraid you two ladies are going to have to stay here. This is a dudes only night club.'),
(4227, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4228, 'Uses the same reading that you learned with the kanji. Go ahead and use your <vocabulary>tongue</vocabulary> and say the reading out loud... "<reading>した</reading>."'),
(4229, 'The reading is the two vocab words separately put together, just as you''d expect from a body-part related kanji. So, 猫 plus 舌 = <reading>ねこじた</reading>. Just remember that 舌 is read here as じた, and not した.'),
(4230, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji. Oh how nice it is to be <vocabulary>young</vocabulary> and to be able to remember that.'),
(4231, 'The reading for this one is a pain. 若 is the same as what you learned with the kanji (わか), but 者 is もの, which isn''t what you learned. Think of this: A <vocabulary>young person</vocabulary> does what they want. They don''t follow tradition or do anything the way you''d want them to, just like the reading of this word. Also, their actions make you really want to <reading>moan</reading> (もの) because ugh, young people. Get off my lawn.'),
(4232, 'The reading is the reading you learned with the kanji. Same goes for the repeater kanji.'),
