(4398, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Will help to be able to read 容疑 first, though.'),
(4399, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4400, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. The さい changes to ざい, though, so watch out for that.'),
(4401, 'The reading is the same as いい加減, which you (hopefully) learned earlier.'),
(4402, 'The reading is the kun''yomi vocab readings of 手 and 続く put together into one. Darn these body parts making everything kun''yomi.'),
(4403, 'The main reading is the same one you learned with the kanji. But there''s also a secondary かたき reading you haven''t seen yet, so here''s a mnemonic for that:

You love to call your <vocabulary>enemy</vocabulary>''s <reading>car tacky</reading> (かたき). They''re always driving around in some tacky sports car, you can''t stand it. Every time they drive by you shout, "Your car is tacky, enemy!"

Note: In terms of differences, the てき reading just means "enemy," whereas かたき typically refers to someone you hold a deep grudge against, often on behalf of another. '),
(4404, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You haven''t learned this reading for 素 yet, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

Oh wow, you look really <vocabulary>lovely</vocabulary> in that <reading>su</reading>it (す). Yes, I can see you through the Internet, and I have to say, that suit is just <vocabulary>dreamy</vocabulary>! Don''t ever take it off.'),
(4405, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. Both kanji have a couple different on''yomi readings though, so here''s a mnemonic to help you remember which ones to pick:

Your <vocabulary>arch enemy</vocabulary> is the <reading>tie techie</reading> (たいてき). Who''s the tie techie? He''s the tech support guy at work who always wears the same tie and never helps you. He just says "try restarting your computer" to every problem, which isn''t helpful. He''s not just a great enemy, he''s basically your nemesis. You hate that tie techie.'),
(4406, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4407, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: Your romp finally has <vocabulary>to come to an end</vocabulary> when someone decides to <reading>sue</reading> (す) you for all your romping.'),
(4408, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(4409, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

