(5070, 'You go <vocabulary>to tighten</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>to tie</vocabulary> your captive up, and you realize you don''t have anything to do it with. Then you see a sheep standing nearby. Okay. You take the <reading>shee</reading>p (し), pull its wool, and tie the sheep and your captive up together around a tree. Problem solved!'),
(5071, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. You didn''t learn that reading for 組, so here''s a mnemonic to help: 

You start an <vocabulary>organization</vocabulary> for <reading>so</reading>ap (そ). You''re all about soap and making it more effective through your organization. So you call it the Soap Organization for Antiseptic Progress, or S.O.A.P. for short.'),
(5072, 'When you have <vocabulary>to weave</vocabulary> the only thing you can weave is an <reading>O</reading> (お) shape. That''s it. Just O''s.'),
(5073, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(5074, 'If anyone tries to touch your <kanji>belly</kanji>, that''s <reading>hara</reading>ssment (はら). Unless you''re cool with it, obviously. But if you''re not, make sure to yell "harassment!" when they touch your belly. You should also shout what kind of harassment it is: 腹ハラ (腹 harassment).

Note: This word will often get rendaku''d when it''s combined with other vocab, like in ビールばら (beer belly) or 別ばら (separate stomach for dessert).'),
(5075, 'The reading is an exception, but it isn''t too hard to remember. Your <vocabulary>belly</vocabulary> contains all your insides, so it makes sense that it would have the same reading as the word for "inside" — <reading>中</reading> (なか)!'),
(5076, 'The readings are 腹 plus 立つ put together. The たつ becomes だつ as a standard reading though.

While はらだつ is the standard reading for 腹立つ, it''s usually found in more formal settings or writing. You''ll probably hear はらたつ more often in everyday conversations, as it’s a shorter, more colloquial way of expressing 腹が立つ!'),
(5077, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. Just watch out for the せつ getting shortened to せっ and the ふく changing to ぷく. Or just know the word seppuku already since it''s pretty well-known. That works too.'),
(5078, 'The readings come from 腹 and 切る. You may also just know the word harakiri already, as it''s made its way into English as well.'),
(5079, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

You want <vocabulary>to visit</vocabulary> your friend <reading>Taz</reading> (たず), the Tasmanian Devil. He''s your best bro, so imagine visiting him and having a good time and saying you''re going to visit him again.'),
(5080, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(5081, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

Everyone is excited to <vocabulary>invite</vocabulary> you to their home for a demo because you''ve just invented <reading>saw</reading> <reading>so</reading>ap (さそ). What''s saw soap? It''s a soap that cuts through all the grime!'),
(5082, 'Allow me <vocabulary>to lend</vocabulary> you some advice. The reading you learned from the kanji can be used here.'),
(5083, 'It''s the reading of 貸す and 切る put together. Know those two readings and you know this one as well. Sometimes you''ll see this word as 貸切り or 貸切 as well.'),
(5084, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

Do you prefer <vocabulary>to urge</vocabulary> people to do something, or <vocabulary>to press</vocabulary> them to do it? It depends on how you want <vocabulary>to approach</vocabulary> them. But it''s all <reading>sema</reading>ntics (せま) because they''re basically the same.'),
(5085, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. The あつ turns into a small っ, and はく goes to ぱく.'),
