(5329, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji, huzzah!'),
(5330, 'The reading comes from the reading you learned with the kanji. う!'),
(5331, 'The readings don''t really line up perfectly, but they''re close (although mixed in terms of what has what reading). Take a look at the word and break everything down. 浮 takes the kun''yomi うき (from 浮く), 世 also takes the kun''yomi よ, and then 絵 takes the on''yomi え. Put that all together and you have うきよえ! Or if you already know the word ukiyo-e, that works too!'),
(5332, 'This kanji uses the kun''yomi read for the first kanji, and the on''yomi reading for the second. You''ve learned them both already, so you should be able to read this too.'),
(5333, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji. Go grab a cup of water so you don''t become dehydrated, okay?'),
(5334, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(5335, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(5336, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: Causing you <vocabulary>to leak</vocabulary> all over the floor (we''re talking blood, here) is your friend <reading>Moe</reading> (も). If you know someone named Moe, imagine them doing this. If you don''t, think of a famous Moe.'),
(5337, 'Speaking of things that illuminate, your <reading>hand</reading>s (手) illuminate all the time for some reason.'),
(5338, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: The thing you feel the most embarrassed about is your <reading>手</reading> (て). It''s all blue and scary looking.'),
(5339, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
