(5433, 'You would like to exclude your <reading>nose</reading> (のぞ) because you don''t like your nose. Imagine your strong feelings about your nose as you decide to exclude it.'),
(5434, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. For this one though, you have to remember to read the 除 part as じ. Just keep in mind that <kanji>cleaning</kanji> (cleanliness) is next to godliness and so is <reading>Je</reading>sus (じ). So, Jesus is like cleaning. Easy.'),
(5435, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(5436, 'You want <vocabulary>to inquire</vocabulary> about some serious matters, but <reading>Taz</reading> (たず), your Tasmanian Devil friend, keeps interrupting <vocabulary>to ask</vocabulary> silly questions instead.'),
(5437, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5438, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You learned this reading already so you should be able to read it!'),
(5439, 'The reading comes from what you learned in the kanji, nice job.'),
(5440, 'The 学 is read correctly, in terms of what you learned. The 幾 and 何 is weird, though. Just think how when you''re asking a question in Japanese, you end the sentence with か. So, it''s like, "何か????" and that''s why it becomes <reading>か</reading>. Then, for 幾 think about how the <reading>key</reading> (き) to answering geometry questions correctly is figuring out "how many" sides something has. So, "do you have the <reading>key</reading>か?"'),
(5441, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(5442, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5443, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

Whenever you have <vocabulary>to sweep</vocabulary> something, you end up getting way too into it: "<reading>Hah</reading> (は)! Take that dirt! Hah! Think you can hang out on my floor, dust? Think again! Hah!"'),
(5444, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it probably uses the kun''yomi reading. You learned this reading with the kanji, but here''s a mnemonic in case you need a refresher:

You may know of Dora the Explorer. But have you heard about her dirty, <vocabulary>mud</vocabulary>dy brother, <reading>Doro</reading> (どろ)? They''re like a team. And because of all the exploring he does, Doro is always covered in mud.'),
(5445, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the kun''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.

There''s another way to read this word that uses both on''yomi readings (でいすい). It''s mostly used in more formal or technical situations though, so we''re focusing on the どろみず reading here!'),
(5446, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(5447, 'What are you so surprised by? Your friend''s <reading>odor</reading> (おどろ). Think of a friend that you wouldn''t expect to be so smelly. Then, be surprised by the odor. Eww, bro, you have to take a shower.'),
(5448, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
(5449, 'For the reading, just think about this feeling you''re having (this lamenting grieving one). Then, decide you''re done with it. <reading>投げ</reading>る (throw) it out the window. You don''t want these feels anymore!'),
(5450, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5451, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well.'),
