(5695, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5696, 'The reading is the same as what you learned with the kanji as well.'),
(5697, 'The reading for 家 is an exceptional one. It is や, because when your landlord asks if you paid your rent, you always say <reading>yah</reading> (や), whether you did or not.'),
(5698, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You just have to remember that 貸 is read as たい for this one. What is your rental? It is a <reading>tie</reading> (たい) fighter. Sweet.'),
(5699, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own. The 物 takes its もつ reading, which makes sense since it sounds like 持つ, which is "to hold." What is this freighter doing? It''s holding freight! So, that''s how you can remember that bit.'),
(5700, 'This word uses the kun''yomi readings of all three kanji. The 小 and the 粉 both use kun''yomi that you haven''t learned yet, but luckily they are both the same! Just think about how "small" things are like <reading>子</reading> (こ), and "powder" is also small. So both 小 and 粉 are read こ.'),
(5701, 'The reading comes from 怖い.'),
(5702, 'The reading is shared with 恐ろしい, which is "scary." Use that, and don''t be afraid. You''ll be able to learn this reading yet.'),
(5703, 'The reading comes from 迷う, so use that so you aren''t lead astray too.'),
(5704, 'The reading comes from 積もる, so pile up your knowledge and remember it that way.'),
(5705, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. It will help to know the word 主催 already as well.'),
(5706, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you''ll know how to read this as well. Will help to know 催促 first.'),
(5707, 'The reading is 泥 plus the reading for 棒 put together.'),
(5708, 'The reading is from 似る and 合う, put together.'),
(5709, 'The reading comes from 驚く. Don''t be surprised if you remember the reading of this word because of your past knowledge.'),
(5710, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own. Also, it''ll help to know the word 掃除 as well.'),
(5711, 'The reading comes from 乾く.'),
(5712, 'The reading of the 散 comes from 散る. So, go ahead and scatter more memories around your brain, you got this already.'),
(5713, 'The reading comes from 離れる.'),
(5714, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own. Will help to know the word 複数 as well.'),
(5715, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5716, 'The reading is of course different from what you learned with the kanji. Think of this:

The <vocabulary>owner</vocabulary> of those <reading>nu</reading>de <reading>shee</reading>p (ぬし) doesn’t even need their wool, he just likes owning nude sheep. So he keeps them as pets and shaves them every day. He’s not interested in sheep with fur. Only nude sheep for this owner! '),
(5717, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5718, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5719, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5720, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5721, 'The readings come from 引く and 越す put together. Careful of that っ.'),
(5722, 'The reading comes from 追う and 越す put together into one. You can overtake this word now that you know that.'),
(5723, 'The reading comes from 祖父 and 祖母, which are exceptional readings on their own, so you''ll need them in order to be able to read this one too.'),
(5724, 'The reading is the kun''yomi readings for both. You''ll need to remember the words 飼う and 主 to be able to remember the reading of this word.'),
(5725, 'The reading is the same as the word 傷.'),
(5726, 'The reading is 傷. If you remember the reading for that you got this too.'),
(5727, 'The reading comes from 使う and 捨てる, combined into one.'),
(5728, 'The reading comes from 投げる and 捨てる, combined into one garbage inducing dump of a word.'),
(5729, 'Lucky for you, the reading you learned with the kanji is the one you use here. In case you need a hand with the okurigana, here’s a mnemonic for you:

You always try <vocabulary>to put in</vocabulary> your emotions into the <reading>mail</reading> (める) you send. You don''t just load your mail with words, but also with emotions, to make it truly heartfelt mail.'),
(5730, 'The readings come from the word 申す and the kanji 込, stuck together... humbly.'),
(5731, 'The readings come from 飛ぶ, 込, and 自殺. Combine them together to read this entire word.'),
(5732, 'The reading comes from the word 書く and the kanji 込 combined into one.'),
(5733, 'The reading comes from the word 人 and the kanji 込. Just keep in mind that the こ changes to ご and you''ll be fine.'),
(5734, 'The reading is unlike what you learned with this kanji before. Just think about how you have this <vocabulary>stain</vocabulary> on your shirt, all because you were trying to dye some <reading>shee</reading>p (し) and they didn''t like it. So now you have a big dye and sheep poo stain. How were you supposed to know sheep don''t like being dyed?!'),
(5735, 'The reading is the same as 染める.'),
(5736, 'The reading comes from 汚れる, so if you know that you can read this.

Note that the reading よごす  is generally used for making things physically dirty, whereas けがす means making someone or something impure in a figurative way.'),
(5737, 'The reading is 言う and 訳 combined.'),
(5738, 'This word uses the readings you learned with the kanji. If you remember your kanji readings, you''ll be fine here.'),
(5739, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5740, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji, at least the 血液 part. The 型 uses the kun''yomi reading, probably because it''s an add-on word.'),
(5741, 'You learned that the word 興味 uses the on''yomi readings for both kanji, so all you have to do is add が + ない and you''ve got a new expression!'),
(5742, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
