(5832, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(5833, 'The reading is the reading you learned for the kanji 爪 and the word for the reading 切る.'),
(5834, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

You don''t have a knife for your steak, so you have <vocabulary>to carve</vocabulary> it using the <reading>key za</reading>g (きざ) technique. You take a key and zag it through the steak repeatedly until you''re able <vocabulary>to cut up</vocabulary> some zaggy bite-sized pieces.'),
(5835, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5836, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5837, 'The reading is different from 閉める or 閉まる. Instead, 閉じる is read とじる.

Why? Because <vocabulary>to close something</vocabulary> that''s expanded or spread open, you always use your <reading>toe</reading> (と)! It''s easy enough with books, but umbrellas are a real challenge… But you, you can do it! You have the toe-tal control and can close anything expanded to the way it was before.'),
(5838, 'The reading is a bit weird. It''s the kun''yomi readings, where 一 is ひと and 筋 is すじ. Look back on the vocab words that use these readings to remember this one.'),
(5839, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

Your belly will likely start <vocabulary>to droop</vocabulary> after a big meal of <reading>ta</reading>cos (た). Nothing fills you up and makes your belly droop over your waistband like tacos — especially if you eat them the proper way and gorge yourself on them until you''re about to explode.'),
(5840, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5841, 'The reading comes from 盛る and 上げる. Know those words and you''ll be able to read this one too.'),
(5842, 'The reading is 裏切り and an exception reading for 者. Just think about how you''d think of a traitor as a "thing" (物) and not a person.'),
(5843, 'The reading is a bit weird. Just think about how when you have various things, you probably have a lot of things. What do you want? You want <reading>more</reading> (もろ) of those things. <reading>More more</reading> more!'),
(5844, 'The reading is the み from 三つ and つ and 編む combined into one (or should I say three?) braids. Just watch out because this isn''t みっつあみ, it''s just みつあみ. Listen to the audio a few times to make sure you remember how it''s spelled and pronounced!'),
(5845, 'The reading is the same as 閉める.'),
(5846, 'The reading uses the 宝 vocab reading, たから.'),
(5847, 'The reading is the same as 納める.'),
(5848, 'The reading is a bit weird for 納. Just imagine asking the consent question from the meaning, then have the other person say "<reading>Nahh!</reading> (なっ), no consent!"'),
(5849, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(5850, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own. Will help to know the word 否定 as well.'),
