(6760, 'The reading comes from 揺る.'),
(6761, 'The reading comes from 揺る.'),
(6762, 'The reading comes from 泊まる, so pull from that and you shall know this.'),
(6763, 'The reading comes from 泊まる.'),
(6764, 'The reading comes from 滑る and 台.'),
(6765, 'You''re being rude, but you''re also being a big <reading>ass</reading> (あせ) with your impatience.'),
(6766, 'The reading comes from 焦げる, so if you know that you know this word as well.'),
(6767, 'The reading comes from 紛らす.'),
(6768, 'You know who is particularly <vocabulary>confusing</vocabulary>? That <reading>Maggie</reading> (まぎ) girl. She''s confusing because she uses <reading>magi</reading>c (まぎ). Think of a Maggie you know, if possible.'),
(6769, 'You learned the kun''yomi for 網 when you learned it as a vocabulary word. Take that reading, add the 戸 reading with rendaku, and you have yourself あみど.'),
(6770, 'The readings come from the kun''yomi readings of the kanji, 荒 (the secondary reading you learned with this kanji) and 波 (just the vocab version).'),
(6771, 'The readings are both the kun''yomi vocab readings, so think of 手 and 袋 as individual words, put together to make hand sacks. Watch out for the rendaku.'),
(6772, 'The reading is the same as what you learned with the kanji.'),
(6773, 'The reading is the kun''yomi readings of the kanji. For 竜 just think about how it''s a <reading>stand</reading>ing (立つ) dragon, because it would have to be if it''s a <vocabulary>tornado</vocabulary>. Your まき reading comes from 巻く, but the kana is pushed into the kanji.'),
(6774, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6775, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own. Just remember that 起 is rendaku''d and becomes ぎ here. '),
(6776, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6777, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6778, 'The reading for 左 is the on''yomi reading, さ. You can remember this because the <vocabulary>left wing</vocabulary> uses <reading>saw</reading>s (さ) to cut down their right wing enemies. Imagine left wing hippies sawing down old right wing conservatives. BBrrrrbbbbbbzzzzz!'),
(6779, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You haven''t learned this reading for 右, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

Those in the <vocabulary>right wing</vocabulary> love eating <reading>u</reading>ni (う), not because they like the taste, but because uni is expensive. Picture some right-wing people in your mind right now and watch as they try really hard to pretend they like eating uni as they shovel buckets of it down their throats.'),
(6780, 'In each political <vocabulary> wing</vocabulary> there are <reading>two boss</reading>es (つばさ). It''s a weird system, but that''s how they''re doing it these days. Imagine your favorite or least favorite political wings, all with two bosses, arguing with each other.'),
(6781, 'This word uses the きち reading you learned with the kanji.'),
(6782, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6783, 'Those who are able <vocabulary>to endure</vocabulary> are those who have had a lot of training. Who has had the most training of anyone? Ninjas. What group are they part of? The <reading>shino</reading>bi (しの).'),
(6784, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own. Watch out for the rendaku.'),
(6785, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji. But it also has a secondary reading you haven''t learned, so here’s a mnemonic for it:

<vocabulary>Blade</vocabulary> in hand, you charge at your foes hollering "<reading>Yah</reading>! <reading>Eee</reading>! <reading>Baa</reading>!" (やいば). As the blade cuts through them, you cry "Yah! Eee! Baa!" even louder. This battle cry always fuels your fury and doubles the force of your swinging blade. "Yah! Eee! Baa!"

While both readings relate to blades, they have slightly different uses. は is used for any cutting tool''s blade or edge, whereas やいば is a little old-fashioned and specifically refers to the blades of swords or knives, often in literary contexts.'),
(6786, 'The reading is the kun''yomi readings of the kanji. You learned 刃''s reading with the kanji, and you probably remember 物 from so many other words.'),
(6787, 'The reading is the kun''yomi readings of the kanji. For 刃 you learned that with the kanji. For 先 that''s the vocab version of the kanji.'),
(6788, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(6789, 'The readings come from the vocab readings (and kanji readings, actually) of the two kanji.'),
(6790, 'Depending on the context, the reading for this word could be からい or つらい. 

The からい reading is the <vocabulary>spicy</vocabulary> one that you learned with the kanji, which you can remember because spicy foods give a <reading>から</reading>て chop to your mouth. 

The つらい reading is the <vocabulary>difficult</vocabulary> or <vocabulary>painful</vocabulary> one. You can remember this because <reading>two Ra</reading>s (つら) are way more difficult to deal with than one Ra.'),
(6791, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You''ve learned them for 香 and 料, but not for 辛, so here''s a mnemonic to help:

The cabinet you keep your <vocabulary>spices</vocabulary> in is in a terrible place on the floor, and you keep banging your <reading>shin</reading> (しん) on it. Ouch!'),
(6792, 'The reading is 辛い (からい) and the on''yomi reading for 子 combined together. It''s one of those weird words that mixes the on''yomi and kun''yomi readings.'),
(6793, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji, but you haven''t learned this for either 辛 or 抱 yet, so let''s use a mnemonic to remember it: 

Think about how much <vocabulary>patience</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>endurance</vocabulary> your <reading>shin</reading>s (しん) need to have to be good at <reading>bo</reading>wling (ぼう). You won''t get any strikes with weak shins!

Note that the ぼう reading is the rendaku''d reading for 抱.
'),
(6794, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6795, 'The reading is the one you learned with the kanji. No <vocabulary>mystery</vocabulary> there, I suppose.'),
(6796, 'This word uses the kun''yomi readings of the kanji you learned, twice. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6797, 'The reading is the same as the meaning and the reading of the kanji: <reading>さむらい</reading>.'),
(6798, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(6799, 'The reading is the same as the kanji''s reading.'),
(6800, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6801, 'The reading comes from the reading you learned with the kanji.'),
(6802, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6803, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(6804, 'The reading can be either the on''yomi or kun''yomi reading, so you have a choice there. Since you learned the on''yomi しゅ with the kanji already, let''s learn the あけ reading now.

あけ sounds like the beginning of 開ける (to open). So imagine you <reading>開け</reading>る (あけ) a person up, to see what color they are inside. Turns out it''s <vocabulary>vermilion</vocabulary>! Uh… you shouldn''t open people up like that.

朱 is mostly read しゅ, but it can also be read あけ in certain phrases, like 朱に染まる, which means "to be covered in blood" (literally, "to be dyed in vermilion").'),
(6805, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6806, 'The reading for the vocab version of this word is the same as 岡 which has the same meaning and the same reading. Lucky! '),
