(6850, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6851, 'The reading comes from 一斉.'),
(6852, '"THE <reading>HORROR</reading>!" (ほろ) you cry, as your beer is about <vocabulary>to be destroyed</vocabulary>.'),
(6853, 'The reading is the same as a very similar word, 固い. So, if you can use that word to help you to remember, you''ll remember the reading of this word in no time as well!'),
(6854, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6855, '<vocabulary>To earn income</vocabulary>, you decided to start a <reading>car</reading> <reading>sa</reading>le. (かせ) "Every car sale, a step to richness!" was your quirky business mantra.'),
(6856, 'You learned this reading when you learned the kanji.'),
(6857, 'The person who''s holding the <vocabulary>spear</vocabulary> is a little <reading>hoe 子</reading>. It''s a child who learned to fight with a hoe, and he''s turned the hoe into a <vocabulary>spear</vocabulary> to fight with. Imagine him swinging his hoe spear around like a little dork.'),
(6858, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(6859, 'The reading is the individual vocab readings of the two kanji, 鳥 and 肌.'),
(6860, 'The reading is 肌 plus 触る''s reading, combined. Watch out for the rendaku.'),
(6861, 'The readings are the vocab kun''yomi readings of each kanji, 肌 and 色 combined into one.'),
(6862, 'The readings are all ones you''ve seen before. 小 uses its こ reading, so just think of this like a "child boat." This makes sense because it''s small. Watch out for the rendaku. '),
(6863, 'The reading is the same as what you learned with the kanji. Also the same as 船.'),
(6864, 'Seeing the <vocabulary>bell</vocabulary>, you decide you need to ring it. You take your <reading>cane</reading> (かね) and hit the bell as hard as you can, making it chime.'),
(6865, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6866, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6867, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You''ve learned these, but since 元 has two on''yomi readings, here''s a mnemonic to help you out:

The <vocabulary>ringleader</vocabulary> of all these problems is <reading>Gen</reading>ji (げん). He''s the <vocabulary>main culprit</vocabulary> behind this mess. Remember all that trouble he stirred up by flirting with too many women in "The Tale of Genji"? Everyone admired his beauty, but there''s no doubt he was the <vocabulary>root cause</vocabulary> of a lot of chaos.'),
(6868, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6869, 'The reading uses the reading from the kanji.'),
(6870, 'You learned the reading of 狩 when you learned the kanji, but the reading for 人 is totally irregular. Just imagine that you''re hunting a reindeer named <reading>Rudo</reading>lph (りゅうど). He has an unusually red nose, which makes him easy to spot and puts him at a bit of a disadvantage.'),
(6871, 'The reading is the same as what you learned with the kanji. Bear in mind that it''s pronounced ころ when it''s a noun, but it''s usually ごろ when it''s a suffix, though some people say ころ in both cases.'),
(6872, 'The reading is the kun''yomi vocab readings of the two kanji. That''s 先 and 頃 (the reading you learned) put together. The 頃 is the rendaku''d version, ごろ so be careful.'),
(6873, 'The reading is 近い and 頃 put together.'),
(6874, 'The reading is the reading of the vocab version of 年 plus 頃, put together. Note: You will also sometimes see this written partially in kana like 年ごろ.'),
(6875, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji, just rendaku''d.'),
(6876, 'The reading is the 頃 reading from the kanji reading. Be careful, it''s the rendaku''d form.'),
(6877, 'The reading is 若い and 頃 combined into one.'),
(6878, 'Look inside your <kanji>soul</kanji>. You see all the bits and bobs that make up your <vocabulary>soul</vocabulary> rolling around and around, being pushed by the Katamari <reading>Tamashii</reading> (たましい). He keeps your soul nice and big and safe!'),
(6879, 'This word is made up of a single kanji, but it uses the on''yomi reading, not the kun''yomi reading. Luckily, you''ve learned this reading already. Yay!'),
(6880, 'The reading is the same as 足!'),
(6881, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6882, 'The reading is the same as the reading you learned with the kanji.'),
(6883, 'This kanji uses the kun''yomi readings for each kanji, for some reason. You learned the kun''yomi reading for both of these kanji as their primary readings already, just watch out for 戸 getting rendaku''d so it becomes ど instead of と. '),
(6884, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You didn''t learn that reading for 井, so we''ve got a mnemonic to help you out with that. Just note that the on''yomi for 井 isn''t actually じょう, but rather しょう, it just ends up getting rendaku''d here!

Fixing up <vocabulary>ceilings</vocabulary> is a tricky task, but you''ve got just the man for it: <reading>Joe</reading> (じょう). Your local farmhand that does it all, nothing is too tricky or out of reach for him, you''re starting to think that this man can do just about anything. What a handy person to have around.'),
(6885, 'The reading is the same as the reading you learned with the kanji.'),
(6886, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(6887, 'When looking at a calendar, the <reading>子読み</reading> (こよみ) of it is to... well... not be able to read it at all. Because, children can''t really read. So, when you look at a calendar you have to explain it to a child, since they cannot read it.'),
(6888, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6889, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6890, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji. It also happens to be the same as 雲, which has a very similar meaning (clouds).'),
(6891, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(6892, 'To learn the reading, just think about how you''re looking at something for a lonnnnng time if you''re staring at it. In Japanese the word "long" is 長い, and guess what the reading of the kanji part is? It''s なが, just like <reading>なが</reading>める''s kanji-part reading.'),
(6893, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6894, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6895, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6896, 'You are <vocabulary>naked</vocabulary> now. You used to wear a car, but you don''t have it anymore (and you''re not wearing it). You <reading>had a ca</reading>r (はだか), but now you don''t have a car. That''s what you''ll explain when someone asks you why you''re naked.'),
(6897, 'The reading is the reading you learned with the kanji.'),
(6898, 'The reading comes from the reading you learned with the kanji.'),
(6899, 'For example, you''re bound <vocabulary>to get tired</vocabulary> if you try to lift <reading>two ca</reading>rs (つか), right? So think about that as you learn this word''s reading.'),
