(6922, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own. Just be careful of the shortening, and the irregular reading for 気.'),
(6923, 'You know what''s really damp and moist? <reading>Shee</reading>p <reading>me</reading>n (しめ) are. They''ve been hanging out in a greenhouse and now their wool is all damp. They''re going to need a towel to dry off.'),
(6924, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(6925, 'Imagine you''re getting married to a valley girl. You''re all like. Hey, I''m gonna marry you! And she''s all like <reading>Tots</reading> (とつ), we gonna get, like, married! Imagine her saying more "tots" things (tots = short for totally in valley girl speak, at least in my imagination).'),
(6926, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it probably uses the kun''yomi reading. You did not learn this reading, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

If you step in that <vocabulary>gutter</vocabulary> you''ll catch <reading>measle</reading>s (みぞ). A gutter or <vocabulary>ditch</vocabulary> has all kinds of dirt and germs in it, including measles, so don''t go stepping in it or drinking from it!'),
(6927, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6928, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6929, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(6930, 'The reading is the kun''yomi vocab reading of each kanji: 滝 and 川. Careful of the rendaku.'),
(6931, 'To remember the reading, just think about how when you go crazy, you are <reading>coming</reading> (来る) to crazy town. Just imagine some voice beckoning you to come. Come here! Please come here! Come to crazy town! Give in to the voices!'),
(6932, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6933, 'You learned this reading already! You should be able to read this too!'),
(6934, 'The reading is 孝行 plus the kun''yomi of 親. Put them together and you''ll become a good filial child.'),
(6935, 'The reading comes from 見 and 逃す.'),
(6936, 'The readings come from 吹く and 出す.'),
(6937, 'The reading comes from the word 吹く and the kanji 込む.'),
(6938, 'The readings are of 口笛 and the reading you learned from 吹.'),
(6939, 'The reading comes from 薄い, so if you know that you''ll be able to read this too.'),
(6940, 'This is the same reading you learned for 逃す, so you should be able to read this word as well.'),
(6941, 'The readings come from 取る and 逃がす.'),
(6942, 'The reading comes from 吹く and 飛ばす, put together.'),
(6943, 'The reading comes from 伺う.'),
(6944, 'The reading is a little different from 群れる, though. There''s an extra ら in there. That being said, it''s pretty similar, so you should be able to use one to remember the other after a few reps. You can also think about it this way: Everyone is flocking together in one place. Strange thing is, it''s to a small <reading>村</reading> (むら). There''s a 村 reunion going on, that''s why.'),
(6945, '潜る has two possible readings: もぐる and くぐる. The first is used when you mean to dive underwater or go underground.  The second is used when you pass through or under something, sometimes with difficulty, such as crawling under a fence. We want you to focus on もぐる, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

Why are you trying to dive underwater again and again? It''s to get <reading>mo'' goo</reading> (もぐ) to sell on the black goo market. Who knew that sea goo was such a commodity?'),
(6946, 'The reading comes from 合う, so if you know that reading you''ll know this reading as well.'),
(6947, 'This word uses the kun''yomi readings of the kanji. You didn''t learn the second kun''yomi reading for 床. You take とこ and rendaku it into どこ. How do you remember this? Just think about your bed. Then wonder, "<reading>where</reading> (どこ) is it?"'),
(6948, 'The reading is the two words 手 and 堅い combined together. Watch out for the rendaku.'),
(6949, 'The reading comes from 荒れる.'),
(6950, 'The reading comes from 荒い. Since you already know that 々 means it repeats, you already know this reading!'),
(6951, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6952, 'The reading is 包み and 紙 combined together. Be careful of rendaku.'),
(6953, 'The reading comes from the individual words, 潮 and 時. Watch out for the rendaku.'),
(6954, 'The reading is the kun''yomi readings of the two kanji, put together. 黒 and 潮.'),
(6955, 'If you''re going <vocabulary>to rub</vocabulary> me like that, I''m going to <reading>sue</reading> (す) you. It''s making me very uncomfortable. The <reading>cos</reading>t  (こす) of your rubbing actions will be high.
    
So this word has two readings and both mean “to rub.” But you usually use する for a single action, such as lighting a match or grazing your knee, while こする means doing it repeatedly, like when you’re cleaning or polishing something.'),
(6956, 'The reading comes from 小 (which is a reading you''ve seen a couple times now, I''m sure) and 包み, with the み pushed inside the package. Beware of the somewhat weird rendaku. You don''t see づ too often.'),
(6957, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6958, 'The reading comes from 握り.

Note that this is usually written in hiragana as おにぎり.'),
(6959, 'The reading is 揚げる and 出す''s reading.'),
(6960, 'The reading comes from 叫ぶ.'),
(6961, 'The reading comes from 叫ぶ and 声, put together. Careful of the rendaku.'),
(6962, 'The reading is the readings you learned with the kanji, combined.'),
(6963, 'The reading comes from 焦る.'),
(6964, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(6965, 'The reading is the readings you learned with the kanji.'),
(6966, 'The reading comes from 入れる and 墨 combined together. Careful of the rendaku.'),
(6967, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(6968, 'What is <vocabulary>calm</vocabulary>? "<reading>Ode</reading> (おだ) to Joy" is nice and calm and mild, as far as music goes.'),
(6969, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6970, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6971, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6972, 'To remember the reading, think about how when you''re i<reading>neb</reading>riated (にぶ) you need to use a <vocabulary>dull</vocabulary> knife. The にぶい reading talks about a thing that is dull. For のろい, which refers to a dull mind (someone who''s <vocabulary>thickheaded</vocabulary>). Think about a <reading>Nor</reading>se (のろ) God, the God of dullbrains.'),
(6973, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
