(6982, 'Just think: <vocabulary>rather</vocabulary> than a <reading>bug</reading> (むし), how about an animal?'),
(6983, 'The reading for 丁 is an unexpected one. It''s てい instead of ちょう, so think about how the street is lined with <reading>ta</reading>ter (てい) tots, and how you can eat from them as you walk along on your trek. How <vocabulary>polite</vocabulary> of someone to leave them there for you!'),
(6984, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6985, 'The reading comes from the readings you learned with the kanji readings.'),
(6986, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6987, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6988, 'The reading is the same as 切る, which means "to cut." Similar meaning, same reading.'),
(6989, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. But the す reading for 子 here is less common and you haven''t seen it since 様子, so here''s a mnemonic to help you out:

Think about how you broke this <vocabulary>chair</vocabulary> when you sat on it, so you <reading>sue</reading> (す) the person who made the chair. (Who happened to be a child, I guess).'),
(6990, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6991, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(6992, 'The reading for twenty years old, specifically, is a really weird reading. It''s はたち. When you turn <vocabulary>twenty years old</vocabulary> you become a hater of cheese. A <reading>hater cheese</reading> (はたち) kinda person.'),
(6993, 'The reading isn''t the same as 万''s normal reading. It''s ばん instead of まん. And 歳 gets rendaku''d. But, since the word "banzai" is so well known, you probably can remember this reading: ばんざい (<vocabulary>Hooray</vocabulary>)!

And if it''s too hard and you can''t remember, you can still say ばんざい (<vocabulary>I give up</vocabulary>). Basically, you''re gonna end up saying ばんざい either way, so you''ll remember it eventually… in theory, at least.'),
(6994, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You haven''t learned this reading for 歳 yet, so here''s a mnemonic to help you out:

There''s one <vocabulary>year-end gift</vocabulary> that always makes people happy: a <reading>sa</reading>ber (せい). Who wouldn''t be stoked to get a saber as an end-of-year gift? It might be a little tricky to wrap, though…'),
(6995, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(6996, 'The reading is the kun''yomi reading, but that''s the reading you learned with the kanji so you should be able to read this one too. If you need a refresher, here''s a mnemonic to help you:

Looking for a place that''s <vocabulary>cool</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>refreshing</vocabulary>? Go to the <reading>Sue Zoo</reading> (すず). Imagine swimming with Sue the penguin in the refreshing water of her pool. Feels good! '),
(6997, 'The reading is all on''yomi, and you know how to read 清. You didn''t learn 涼''s on''yomi (りょう) with the kanji though, so here''s a mnemonic to help:

You want a <vocabulary>refreshing</vocabulary> and <vocabulary>cool</vocabulary> drink really badly. Why? You just had to <reading>row</reading> (りょう) across a huge lake. With the wind in your face. Imagine how refreshing a cool soda would be after rowing all day!'),
(6998, 'The reading is all on''yomi, and you know how to read 風. You didn''t learn 涼''s on''yomi (りょう) with the kanji though, so here''s a mnemonic to help:

Imagine that a <vocabulary>cool breeze</vocabulary> is pushing your little <reading>row</reading> (りょう) boat along, so that you don''t need to do any rowing. Ahh, what a nice cool breeze…'),
(6999, 'The readings are 猿 plus 真似 put together.'),
(7000, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(7001, 'What part of the eye lets a person see? The <vocabulary>pupil</vocabulary>! The pupil lets a <reading>人 見</reading>る (ひとみ). That''s how you can remember ひとみ.'),
(7002, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(7003, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji. That''s 零 extra things for you to learn.'),
(7004, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7005, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7006, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7007, 'Imagine you''re <reading>co-bo</reading>iling (こぼ) water with someone. With the two of you co-boiling, the water is boiling pretty hard. Watch out, though — you''re about <reading>to spill</reading> the hot water everywhere!'),
(7008, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(7009, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7010, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7011, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.

Unlike many other 〜所 vocabulary, the しょ does NOT get rendaku''d with 碁会所, so just watch out for that!'),
(7012, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7013, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7014, 'It''s a <vocabulary>bubble</vocabulary>!

<reading>A Wha---</reading>? (あわ)

A BUBBLE.

A WHA???'),
(7015, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7016, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7017, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own. Just be careful to note the reading changes: the はつ gets shortened to はっ, and the ほう changes to ぽう.'),
(7018, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(7019, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7020, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7021, 'The reading is the kun''yomi vocab reading of both kanji. 癖 is the reading you learned with the kanji. Watch out for the rendaku.'),
(7022, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7023, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7024, '<vocabulary>To forge</vocabulary> something, you need to have your <reading>kit</reading> (きた) with you. Without it, you won''t be doing any forging at all.'),
(7025, 'The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji.'),
(7026, 'This word uses kun''yomi readings for 稲 (いな) and 穂 (ほ). You may not be familiar with the いな reading yet, but it appears in a lot of compounds that use 稲, so it''s good to know. Here''s a mnemonic to help:

To get a big <vocabulary>head of rice</vocabulary> on your rice plant, have it <reading>ea</reading>t <reading>na</reading>chos (いな). Not literally, of course, but like… give it some nacho fertilizer. If you let rice plants eat nacho (fertilizer), you''ll have the biggest, most high-yielding heads of rice ever.'),
(7027, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7028, 'To remember the reading, think about how the <vocabulary>Japanese Emperor</vocabulary>, can always pull out his "<reading>me card</reading>" (みかど) when he''s in trouble. It''s kind of like how a "trump card" is the one that''s above all the others, but with the emperor he''s always up there, so it''s a "me card" to him. And who''s gonna argue with the emperor? Not me.'),
(7029, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7030, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7031, '<reading>Mah ta-ta</reading> (またた) is showing, causing you <vocabulary>to blink</vocabulary>. I don''t know what a "ta-ta" is, but I bet you could come up with something that''s blink-worthy.'),
(7032, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7033, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7034, 'The reading of 白 is a bit different from normal. It''s <reading>しら</reading> instead of しろ, so do your best to keep that in mind. Watch out for the rendaku.'),
(7035, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7036, '<vocabulary>A wink</vocabulary> is all it takes to get the "<reading>Ma butter</reading>" (まばた) from the store. It''s hidden, and they only bring it out if you do the wink.'),
(7037, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7038, 'The <reading>hoe 子</reading> (ほこ) is the kid who is carrying around a hoe all the time, swinging it around, doing dumb things with it. He''s so proud of it, though, and it shows on his face. He''s always boasting about his hoe skillz too.'),
(7039, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7040, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7041, 'Someone walks up to you, saying "<reading>habbah</reading> habbah habbah" (kind of like hubbah hubbah). He''s trying to flash you or something. So, you go <vocabulary>to thwart</vocabulary> him with your taser.'),
(7042, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7043, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7044, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you: 

You tell your friend <vocabulary>to shut up</vocabulary> because he won''t stop saying he''s "<reading>da ma</reading>n (だま)." He keeps calling himself "da man" and it''s really getting on your nerves, so you tell him <vocabulary>to be silent</vocabulary> and force him <vocabulary>to stop talking</vocabulary>. Guess he wasn''t da man after all.'),
(7045, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7046, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7047, 'A bunch of <vocabulary>straw sack</vocabulary>s or <vocabulary>straw bag</vocabulary>s are stacked upon each other to make a tower (たわら). Imagine walking into this sack tower and wondering how it''s all held together. Such poor architecture.'),
(7048, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7049, 'The reading can be either the kun''yomi or on''yomi reading, so you have a choice there. Let''s learn the わた reading, though. Just think <reading>what</reading> (わた) is cotton? What is it? What does it mean? Ask a lot of "what" questions about cotton to yourself.'),
(7050, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own. Watch out for the rendaku.'),
(7051, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7052, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7053, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7054, 'The reading is the same as 眺める.'),
(7055, 'The reading comes from 疲れる.'),
(7056, 'The reading is the same as 湿る.'),
(7057, 'You want <vocabulary>to smash</vocabulary> my stuff? That''s <reading>coo'' だ</reading> (くだ). Whatever you wanna do.'),
(7058, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7059, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You should be able to read this on your own.'),
(7060, 'Will I give up? I''ll <reading>never</reading> (ねば) give up! I''ll also never unstick from you.'),
