(7111, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You''ve seen the す reading for 子 before, but here''s a mnemonic just in case: 

Think about how you accidentally fanned some sweat droplets into a dude''s face when you fanned yourself a little too hard with a <vocabulary>folding fan</vocabulary>, causing him to <reading>sue</reading> (す). Sued for a folding fan faux pas?! Americans really are litigious...'),
(7112, 'This word is made up of a single kanji, so you know it''ll probably use the kun''yomi reading. Fortunately, that''s the とびら reading you learned with the kanji!'),
(7113, 'This is a jukugo word that uses on''yomi readings. You''ve learned the もん reading for 門, but you haven''t seen the ひ reading for 扉 yet (plus, it gets changed to ぴ in this word). Here''s a mnemonic to help you remember that rendaku''d reading:

The <vocabulary>doors of a gate</vocabulary> often smell like <reading>pee</reading> (ぴ). Why? Dogs and drunks alike love to pee against <vocabulary>gate</vocabulary>s, that''s why.'),
(7114, 'The reading used for 挿絵 is an exceptional one. Instead of そう it''s さし. When you think of a <vocabulary>book illustration</vocabulary>, think of an illustration of a <reading>sash</reading>. Just picture after picture of a sash. Purple sashes. Green sashes. Flashy sashes. What a weird book, that only has illustrations of sashes.

絵, however, is the reading you learned with the kanji, so at least you have that.'),
