(7495, 'Remember the word 分ける? That word means "to separate" too. The reading is similar, though not the same, but we can at least use it as a jumping off point. We go from わける to わかれる. It''s like we''ve "separated" the け and the る and combined them in the middle. け, making a かれ, which gets you to <reading>わか</reading>れる.'),
