(7572, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You already learned this reading when you learned 教える, so you should be able to read this word as well.'),
(7573, 'Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it should use the kun''yomi reading. You haven''t learned this reading yet though, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

You''re standing there, staring at <vocabulary>the front</vocabulary> of this building. You spend some time gazing at it before you realize what it is. "<reading>Oh</reading>, <reading>mote</reading>l (おもて)," you say aloud. <vocabulary>The surface</vocabulary> of this building really gave it away. "Oh, motel!"'),
(7574, 'This is a jukugo word, which usually means on''yomi readings from the kanji. Since 作 has two on''yomi readings, here''s a mnemonic to help you remember which one to use:

If you want to get <vocabulary>work</vocabulary> done, you''ll first need to get yourself a <reading>saw</reading> (さ). That''s because you''ll need to cut the work into individual bite-sized <vocabulary>tasks</vocabulary>.'),
(7575, 'Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun''yomi reading. You didn''t learn that reading with this kanji, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

There is only one time you allow yourself <vocabulary>to get sick</vocabulary>, and that''s when the <reading>Ya</reading>nkees (や) are playing. You can''t miss any games so you just happen <vocabulary>to fall ill</vocabulary> whenever they''re playing so you can stay home and watch. That''s pretty convenient, isn''t it?'),
(7576, 'This word uses the kun''yomi readings from both kanji, probably because it includes body parts. You know them both already, so now you know this too!'),
(7577, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You didn''t learn this reading for 屋 though, so here''s a mnemonic to help you:

One place in the world has more <vocabulary>rooftop</vocabulary>s than any where else, and that''s <reading>Ok</reading>lahoma (おく). They get so many tornadoes that they need extra rooftops for when the first few blow off.'),
(7578, 'This is a jukugo word that uses the on''yomi readings of the kanji. You haven''t learned this reading for 度 yet, so here''s a mnemonic to help you remember it:

To remember your morning <vocabulary>preparation</vocabulary>, you set up a <reading>tack</reading> (たく) board covered in reminders in every room of your house. With helpful notes tacked up everywhere, <vocabulary>getting ready</vocabulary> should be as easy as ever.'),
